(window.AURA_PART = window.AURA_PART || {})["docs"] = {"bodies": {"person:aaref-hilaly": "\n# Aaref Hilaly\n\n## One-line take\nHe is BCV's senior Bay Area AI partner and its most-used \"partner of record\" on AI deals. He led Contextual AI's research-founder seed in days (2023) and Decagon's Series B (2024), but on the frontier names (Cognition, Sunday Robotics, Chai Discovery, Simile) he comes in as a non-lead at a growth or late-early round.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he is a two-time founder (one flop, one $410M exit) and an ex-Sequoia investor, so he sells hands-on company building. His own bio says he spends an afternoon at Decagon \"every couple of weeks\" on recruiting, org design and strategy ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/)). He also gets in through long, informal relationships: he backed Decagon co-founder Ashwin Sreenivas's prior company Helia and advised Decagon informally for about a year before BCV led its Series B ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-backed-decagon-ai/)).\n- **What constrains him:** he is selective by design, and says BCV partners with \"a small number of new companies each year\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/)). His 2024 to 2026 frontier exposure is mostly as a follower in rounds led by Founders Fund, Coatue, Index and Greenoaks (see Portfolio).\n- **His view of the market:** he thinks first-generation GenAI categories are \"over-funded and intensely competitive\", so winners must out-execute on product and go-to-market ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/)).\n- **What he'd never do (inferred, not stated):** chase a thin \"AI wrapper\". His posts keep asking for proprietary data, full-workflow capture and real reasoning, as with EvenUp and Actively ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/what-every-ai-company-can-learn-from-evenup/), [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/actively-ai-is-launching-a-new-era-of-gtm-superintelligence/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Decagon came from a prior bet, not a pitch.** He wrote: \"I backed Ashwin’s prior company, Helia (acquired by Scale)\", and through Ashwin he met Jesse Zhang. BCV had been \"informally advising Decagon over the past year or so\" before leading the $65M Series B ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-backed-decagon-ai/), 2024-10-15; [round](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-b)).\n- **Contextual AI was a days-long decision on researchers.** BCV met Douwe Kiela and Amanpreet Singh in early 2023, after noticing their RAG research \"on the desks of CTOs building on LLMs\". Then: \"Within days, we’d agreed to partner and lead their company’s seed round.\" That was a $20M seed, with Lightspeed and Greycroft following ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/), 2023-06-07; co-written with Rak Garg).\n  - The same post lists GPU access as a core founder skill: \"the ability to secure graphics processing units (GPUs)\". It also says the Bain platform helps here, and BCV Labs advertises it as \"an accelerant in securing in-demand compute clusters\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-labs-is-an-ai-incubator-and-technical-community-in-silicon-valley/)).\n- **He reversed on agents within about 15 months.** In the July 2023 thesis he co-wrote with Rak Garg, production agents were \"not suitable for much more than enthralling Twitter threads\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/opportunities-in-ai-creating-abundant-intelligence/)). By October 2024 he titled the Decagon post \"Agents are Real and They’re Spectacular\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-backed-decagon-ai/)).\n- **The 2023 thesis bet against obvious AI apps.** It said AI is \"more akin to the microprocessor than the iPhone\". It also predicted value in obvious categories would accrue to incumbents ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/opportunities-in-ai-creating-abundant-intelligence/)). Yet his later deals (Decagon in support, EvenUp in legal, Charta in medical coding) sit in exactly the categories it called obvious. His bio now says those categories are won on execution, not idea novelty ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/)).\n- **He incubates at seed through BCV Labs.** BCV \"invited them to incubate Echelon with us at BCV Labs\" and backed the ex-Moveworks founders \"from the very beginning\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-new-echelon-of-it-implementations-and-ai-powered-services/), 2025-10). Contextual AI also worked out of BCV Labs ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/)).\n- **He tracks founders for years before investing.**\n  - Charta Health: BCV first met the founders \"at pre-seed in 2023\", and the CMS records a 2025 seed ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/charting-a-new-era-how-charta-health-is-transforming-medical-chart-review/), [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%20%26%26%20references%28%228ff36264-b79e-456f-99fa-9e36758f7b37%22%29%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C_createdAt%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Ename%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Eslug.current%7D)).\n  - Actively AI: BCV met the founders \"during their Stanford days\", stayed in touch, then heard praise from portfolio revenue leaders at Justworks and Attentive ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/actively-ai-is-launching-a-new-era-of-gtm-superintelligence/)).\n- **Physical-AI thesis (Sunday Robotics, co-written with Amanda Huang):** \"the first company that learns fastest from real homes will define the category.\" He stresses US-only data collection, \"Importantly, these are all U.S. households\", which goes against offshore data labour ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/robots-finally-at-home/)).\n- **He states his own record.** Of about 20 early-stage companies he has worked closely with, \"two have gone public, several had happy acquisitions and a few were total disasters\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/)).\n- **He is BCV's in-house voice on talent.** He alone wrote Rak Garg's promotion post (partner after \"just about three years\") and co-wrote the post welcoming Slater Stich and Saanya Ojha as partners with Enrique Salem ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rak-garg-promoted-to-partner/), [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-slater-stich-and-saanya-ojha-our-newest-partners/)). That suggests a hand in BCV's partner-track decisions (inferred).\n- **Timing pattern from the CMS:** BCV's portfolio-record creation dates line up with announcement days. Cognition is 2025-09-08, the day of the >$400M round. Simile is 2026-02-13, a day after its Series A. Sunday Robotics is 2026-03-12, its Series B day. Chai Discovery is 2026-07-14, its Series C day ([CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%20%26%26%20references%28%228ff36264-b79e-456f-99fa-9e36758f7b37%22%29%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C_createdAt%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Ename%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Eslug.current%7D)). In each case BCV was a new, non-lead investor.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:**\n  - Contextual AI: a BCV-led $20M seed into ex-FAIR and Hugging Face researchers, decided within days and incubated at BCV Labs. That is the pattern THESIS.md likes.\n  - He can write large checks (multi-stage firm, growth rounds in Cognition and Chai Discovery).\n  - He is on Sunday Robotics, Simile (Stanford generative-agents research team) and Chai Discovery (AI-bio models).\n- **Against:**\n  - On the frontier names he is not first in. Cognition was led by Founders Fund, with Lux and 8VC earlier. Sunday's Series B was led by Coatue, with Benchmark and Conviction also named. Simile's A was led by Index, and Chai's C came after OpenAI and Thrive at seed ([Chai news](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news)).\n  - His own leads are in applied-AI workflow automation (Decagon, Echelon, Charta, Actively), not labs or chips.\n- **Use him as:** a co-investor for AI-native automation (Serval-like) deals and a growth-round follow-on source, plus a read on what BCV Labs is incubating.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** Contextual AI, Echelon AI (an AI-native IT services automation company, very Serval-adjacent), Decagon, Sunday Robotics and Cognition.\n\n## Background\n- Originally from Pakistan and grew up in England. Neither parent went to college, and he attended school on scholarships ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/)). The schools he attended are not named in sources found.\n- Moved to Silicon Valley after graduate school \"with pretty much just two suitcases\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/)).\n- Founded CenterRun (datacenter automation), which was acquired by Sun Microsystems in 2003 ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/welcoming-our-newest-partner-aaref-hilaly/)). He says it \"couldn’t find product-market fit\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/)).\n- Co-founder and CEO of Clearwell (e-discovery). It grew to over $100M ARR in about four years and was sold to Symantec for $410M ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/welcoming-our-newest-partner-aaref-hilaly/)). The sale year is not in that source.\n- Sequoia Capital, seven years as an investor ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/welcoming-our-newest-partner-aaref-hilaly/)), described as \"partner\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/)). Worked with Guardant Health, Lightstep, MobileIron, Skyhigh Networks and ThousandEyes. Exact Sequoia start and end years were not found.\n- Co-invested alongside BCV on Clari, which led BCV to recruit him ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/welcoming-our-newest-partner-aaref-hilaly/)).\n- Joined BCV as Partner, announced 2020-08-06, with a seed and Series A mandate in West Coast apps and infrastructure ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/welcoming-our-newest-partner-aaref-hilaly/)).\n- **Promotion story:** a lateral senior hire with no junior-to-partner climb. He has held the Partner title at BCV since 2020; no later title change was found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/ | Long first-person bio Q&A, the richest primary source |\n| Substack / blog | https://baincapitalventures.com/insights/ | 25 BCV posts reference him, 2020-10 to 2026-10 (CMS) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/aaref | Handle from BCV CMS; fetch blocked (HTTP 402), so followers unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/aarefhilaly/ | From BCV CMS; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Hosts BCV's video interviews (Cognition, 2026-10) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-10 — BCV video — \"Cognition on Building the AI Software Engineer…\" — https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/video-cognition-on-building-the-ai-software-engineer-and-taking-users-from-survival-mode-to-creative-mode/ — he interviews Scott Wu. The summary notes that over 95% of Devin's codebase is written by Devin, and that 19 of the first 27 Cognition employees were founders.\n- Other podcasts: nothing found. The web-search budget was exhausted this session, so external podcasts were not searched.\n\n## How they think\n- **Venture is post-investment work.** He says \"the joy of venture is working with founders after we invest\". He focuses on non-obvious things a company must do now so it is positioned in 6 to 12 months ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/)).\n- **Multi-stage at seed.** He raised seed from a multi-stage firm for both his companies and \"it really helped\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/)). That is his pitch for BCV at seed.\n- **GenAI scale and opportunity.** He calls GenAI a once-in-a-generation shift \"of a similar scale to the Internet\". He hopes for GenAI-native apps that come from voice and vibe coding ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/)).\n- **Durability.** AI revenue comes easily, but durability needs full-workflow capture and proprietary data, with EvenUp as the example ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/what-every-ai-company-can-learn-from-evenup/), 2025-10).\n- **Services into software.** He is drawn to \"mundane decisions\" automation, which converts services spend into software. Examples are IT consulting (Echelon), legal (EvenUp) and medical coding (Charta) ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/opportunities-in-ai-creating-abundant-intelligence/), [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-new-echelon-of-it-implementations-and-ai-powered-services/)).\n- **What he looks for in founders.** He wants a \"clear, compelling narrative\" and pairings like \"expansive vision combined with maniacal execution\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/), [Decagon](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-b)).\n- **Calls he got right or wrong:**\n  - Right: Decagon, where BCV led the B and Decagon later raised much larger rounds, though those rounds were not checked this session.\n  - Wrong, then reversed: his 2023 agent skepticism.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [His BCV bio Q&A](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/). Founder history, his record, how hands-on he is, and his GenAI view.\n2. [Agents are Real and They’re Spectacular: Why We Backed Decagon AI](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-backed-decagon-ai/), 2024-10-15. The intro path and the year of informal advising.\n3. [Bringing Context to Enterprise AI](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/), 2023-06-07. A seed led within days on research founders.\n4. [Abundant Intelligence](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/opportunities-in-ai-creating-abundant-intelligence/), 2023-07-13. The 2023 map, including the agent skepticism.\n5. [Robots, Finally at Home](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/robots-finally-at-home/), 2026-03. The physical-AI data-flywheel thesis.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. The X fetch was blocked. Co-authors on BCV posts are Rak Garg (most frequent), Abby Meyers, Alysaa Co, Ryan Kim, Amanda Huang, Ajay Agarwal, Matt Harris and Enrique Salem ([CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%20%26%26%20references%28%228ff36264-b79e-456f-99fa-9e36758f7b37%22%29%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C_createdAt%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Ename%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Eslug.current%7D)).\n\n## Portfolio\nThese are BCV CMS records where he is the partner of record (21 records), plus round details from company sources. Stage labels are BCV's.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Contextual AI | Seed $20M | 2023-06-07 | yes (BCV led) | Lightspeed, Greycroft, Elad Gil, Lip-Bu Tan, Sarah Guo, Amjad Masad and others; BCV co-partner Rak Garg | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/). CMS marks it \"Acquired\"; the acquirer was not found |\n| Decagon | Series B $65M | 2024-10-15 | yes | A*, Accel, BOND, ACME, Elad Gil | [src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-b), [post](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-backed-decagon-ai/) |\n| Cognition | >$400M at $10.2B post | 2025-09-08 | no (Founders Fund led) | Lux, 8VC, Neo, Elad Gil, Definition, Swish (existing); Hanabi, D1 (new) | [src](https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents) |\n| Sunday Robotics | Series B $165M at $1.15B | 2026-03-12 | no (Coatue led) | Fidelity, Tiger Global, Benchmark, Conviction, Xtal | [src](https://www.sunday.ai/blog/series-b). Co-written with Amanda Huang |\n| Simile | Series A $100M | 2026-02-12 | no (Index led) | not named | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/life-the-universe-and-simile-leading-similes-series-a/), [BCV](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-human-layer-of-ai-why-we-re-continuing-to-invest-in-simile/) |\n| Simile | Series B >$200M at $2B | 2026-07-30 | no (Greenoaks and Index co-led) | Hanabi, A*, Factory, CVS Health Ventures, Definition | [src](https://www.simile.com/blog/series-b). BCV co-partners Alysaa Co and Ryan Kim |\n| Chai Discovery | Series C $400M at $3.8B (inferred round) | 2026-07-14 | no | Index, Kleiner Perkins, Sequoia, Dimension; BCV not named | [src](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/series-c). CMS: Growth, 2026, created 2026-07-14, with Abby Meyers |\n| Krea | Series B | 2025-04-07 | not stated | a16z (A), Pebblebed and Abstract (seed), HF0, A*, Gradient | [src](https://www.krea.ai/blog/new-krea). $83M total raised; co-partner Rak Garg |\n| Echelon AI | Seed (incubated at BCV Labs) | 2025-10 | unknown | not found | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-new-echelon-of-it-implementations-and-ai-powered-services/). Co-partner Rak Garg |\n| Charta Health | Seed (CMS); met at pre-seed 2023 | 2025 | unknown | not found | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/charting-a-new-era-how-charta-health-is-transforming-medical-chart-review/). Co-partner Alysaa Co |\n| Actively AI | Early (CMS) | 2024 (CMS); post 2025-04-02 | unknown | not found | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/actively-ai-is-launching-a-new-era-of-gtm-superintelligence/). Co-partner Ajay Agarwal |\n| Index (AI product-feedback tool) | Seed (CMS) | 2025 | unknown | not found | CMS; co-partner Rak Garg |\n| Norm Ai | Early (CMS) | 2024 | unknown | not found | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/norm-ai-is-using-ai-to-clean-up-the-sludge-of-regulatory-compliance/). With Matt Harris and Rak Garg |\n| EvenUp | Growth (CMS) | 2023 | unknown | not found | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/leveling-the-legal-playing-field-through-ai-why-we-invested-in-evenup/). With Abby Meyers |\n| Cleanlab | Seed (CMS; acquired) | 2022 | unknown | not found | CMS |\n| Tecton (acquired by Databricks) | Growth (CMS) | 2022 | unknown | not found | CMS |\n| Magical | Seed (CMS) | 2020 | unknown | not found | CMS; with Kevin Zhang |\n| Cube.js, Momento, Felt, ReCharge, Lithos Carbon | Early, Seed, Early, Growth, Seed (CMS) | 2020–2022 | unknown | not found | CMS; non-AI or infrastructure |\n| Sequoia-era: Guardant Health, Lightstep, MobileIron, Skyhigh, ThousandEyes | unknown | pre-2020 | unknown | n/a | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/welcoming-our-newest-partner-aaref-hilaly/) |\n\nCMS source for all rows: [BCV CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%20%26%26%20references%28%228ff36264-b79e-456f-99fa-9e36758f7b37%22%29%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C_createdAt%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Ename%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Eslug.current%7D). Timing per AI deal (first in or not) is in `data/rows/aaref-hilaly-deals.csv`.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Decagon:**\n  1. He backed Ashwin Sreenivas's prior company Helia, which was acquired by Scale.\n  2. Ashwin introduced him to Jesse Zhang.\n  3. BCV advised informally for about a year.\n  4. BCV led the $65M Series B on 2024-10-15.\n\n  Today he spends an afternoon at the company every couple of weeks ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-backed-decagon-ai/), [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/)). BCV was not first in: the release lists A*, Accel and Elad Gil among the backers ([src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-b)).\n- **Contextual AI:** BCV spotted Kiela's RAG papers in CTOs' hands, met the founders early in 2023, and agreed to lead within days. The founders worked out of BCV Labs ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/)). BCV was first institutional lead.\n- **Echelon AI:** the founders came from Moveworks, where BCV partner Enrique Salem led the Series A. BCV invited them to incubate at BCV Labs, where they spent weeks with CIOs and SIs before the seed ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-new-echelon-of-it-implementations-and-ai-powered-services/)). BCV was first in.\n- **Charta Health:** BCV met the ex-Rockset founders at pre-seed in 2023. The founders cold-outbounded practices to win six-figure contracts ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/charting-a-new-era-how-charta-health-is-transforming-medical-chart-review/)).\n- **Cognition, Sunday, Simile, Chai:** BCV entered as a new, non-lead investor in a priced growth or late-early round (see Portfolio). The intro paths are not sourced.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Rak Garg (BCV) | Most frequent co-author and co-partner (Contextual, Krea, Echelon, Index, Norm); wrote Rak's promotion post | 2023 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rak-garg-promoted-to-partner/) |\n| Abby Meyers (BCV) | Co-partner on EvenUp and Chai Discovery | 2024 | CMS |\n| Alysaa Co, Ryan Kim (BCV) | Co-partners on Simile; Alysaa on Charta | 2025 | CMS |\n| Amanda Huang (BCV) | Co-author of the Sunday Robotics post | 2026-03 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/robots-finally-at-home/) |\n| Ajay Agarwal (BCV) | Co-partner on Actively AI | 2024 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/actively-ai-is-launching-a-new-era-of-gtm-superintelligence/) |\n| Enrique Salem (BCV) | Co-wrote the Slater Stich and Saanya Ojha partner-hire post | 2023-01 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-slater-stich-and-saanya-ojha-our-newest-partners/) |\n| Ashwin Sreenivas, Jesse Zhang (Decagon) | Backed Ashwin's Helia, then led Decagon's Series B | before 2024 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-backed-decagon-ai/) |\n| Douwe Kiela, Amanpreet Singh (Contextual AI) | Led their seed | 2023 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/) |\n| Scott Wu (Cognition) | Interviewed him on BCV video | 2026-10 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/video-cognition-on-building-the-ai-software-engineer-and-taking-users-from-survival-mode-to-creative-mode/) |\n| Andy Byrne, Venkat Rangan (Clari) | Co-investor and board-side relationship that led to his BCV hire | pre-2020 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/welcoming-our-newest-partner-aaref-hilaly/) |\n| Sequoia Capital | Investor, about 7 years | pre-2020 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/welcoming-our-newest-partner-aaref-hilaly/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact Sequoia years, and whether he held the title \"partner\" for the whole seven years.\n- Who acquired Contextual AI (CMS marks it acquired), and at what price.\n- BCV's check sizes in Cognition, Sunday Robotics and Chai Discovery, and the intro paths into each.\n- Whether BCV led Krea's Series B. The Krea post credits BCV for the B without naming a lead.\n- What X and podcasts show. The search budget was exhausted, so external interviews were not searched.\n\n## Sources\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/team/aaref-hilaly/ (bio Q&A; CMS: https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_id%3D%3D%228ff36264-b79e-456f-99fa-9e36758f7b37%22%5D%5B0%5D%7Babout%2Cshort_bio%2C_createdAt%7D)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/welcoming-our-newest-partner-aaref-hilaly/ (2020-08-06)\n- BCV CMS portfolio query (see Portfolio)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-backed-decagon-ai/ ; https://decagon.ai/blog/series-b\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/ ; https://contextual.ai/company\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/opportunities-in-ai-creating-abundant-intelligence/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-labs-is-an-ai-incubator-and-technical-community-in-silicon-valley/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-new-echelon-of-it-implementations-and-ai-powered-services/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/charting-a-new-era-how-charta-health-is-transforming-medical-chart-review/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/actively-ai-is-launching-a-new-era-of-gtm-superintelligence/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/what-every-ai-company-can-learn-from-evenup/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/robots-finally-at-home/ ; https://www.sunday.ai/blog/series-b\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-human-layer-of-ai-why-we-re-continuing-to-invest-in-simile/ ; https://www.simile.com/blog/series-b ; https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/life-the-universe-and-simile-leading-similes-series-a/\n- https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/video-cognition-on-building-the-ai-software-engineer-and-taking-users-from-survival-mode-to-creative-mode/\n- https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news ; https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/series-c\n- https://www.krea.ai/blog/new-krea\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rak-garg-promoted-to-partner/ ; https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-slater-stich-and-saanya-ojha-our-newest-partners/\n", "person:aaron-rosenberg": "\n# Aaron Rosenberg\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the investor in Europe closest to the DeepMind alumni pipeline. He ran strategy in Hassabis's office, and Radical uses him to back London research spinouts at seed and Series A, increasingly as lead with a board seat [src](https://radical.vc/team/aaron-rosenberg/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He spent about six years inside DeepMind from 2017, in the Office of the CEO. He helped build the executive team, grew the company from 500 to 1,500 people, and helped incubate and spin out Isomorphic Labs [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-launches-in-london/). As a result, DeepMind researchers who are about to leave already know and trust him. Sifted's October 2026 podcast billed him as \"The man DeepMind researchers call before they quit\" [src](https://sifted.eu/articles/aaron-rosenberg-radical-ventures-podcast/). Five of his six named Radical deals have ex-DeepMind founders: Orbital, Latent Labs, Inherent, Mantic and Discovery Loop (Vinyals and others). Slingshot is the exception. He also helped incubate Isomorphic Labs while at DeepMind.\n- **What constrains him:** He is not a researcher. He is an operator and strategist with an MBA (he is still finishing a CS master's at Penn) [src](https://radical.vc/team/aaron-rosenberg/). His edge is relationships and company building, not technical diligence. He usually enters at Series A or as a co-lead next to a London or US firm (Sofinnova, Forerunner, Index, Khosla). His first sole-lead seed with a board seat came only in September 2026 (Mantic). He is not first money on most of his deals: Orbital had a Fly/Compound seed, Slingshot had an a16z Series A, and Latent Labs had an earlier seed.\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** back AI companies that will not discuss risk. He co-wrote Radical's RAIS responsible-AI framework and said founders who \"welcome the discussion are all the more attractive to us\" [src](https://thelogic.co/news/as-ai-funding-booms-radical-ventures-launches-new-tool-to-help-investors-gauge-risks/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He has run the spinout play himself, not only funded it:** he helped \"incubate and spin out Isomorphic Labs\" while inside DeepMind [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-launches-in-london/).\n- **His likely path into Radical:** he was Deputy Chief of Staff to Dominic Barton at McKinsey, and Barton is now a Radical partner [src](https://radical.vc/team/aaron-rosenberg/). No source states the intro, so this is inference.\n- **A frontier lab invests alongside him:** Thinking Machines is a co-investor in the $25M Mantic seed he led [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/).\n- **Sifted's headline is a journalist's framing, not his words:** \"The man DeepMind researchers call before they quit\" [src](https://sifted.eu/articles/aaron-rosenberg-radical-ventures-podcast/) (2026-10-22; only the headline and description were seen, so confidence is low).\n- **His NeoLabs essay with Kotite says compute access, not capital, now limits these companies:** \"the real constraint is no longer capital\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/) (2026-06-21).\n- **The same essay names DeepMind as the main talent source for neolabs:** \"DeepMind stands out as the dominant source by a wide margin.\" This is his own book's thesis in data form [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/).\n- **He frames neolabs as a power-law portfolio, which is close to your lens:** \"the one that does may justify the entire portfolio\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/).\n- **He describes cap tables as concentrated:** \"The cap table is concentrated.\" In context, it is mostly NVIDIA, a few lead VCs and strategics, which makes allocation scarce for small checks [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/).\n- **His view on Inherent favours small, directed models over scale:** \"training a compact layer of scientific intelligence, rather than scaling a single, monolithic model\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/teaching-ai-to-ask-the-right-questions/) (2026-08-17).\n- **He uses responsible-AI diligence as a selection filter, not compliance:** \"Those that welcome the discussion are all the more attractive to us.\" [src](https://thelogic.co/news/as-ai-funding-booms-radical-ventures-launches-new-tool-to-help-investors-gauge-risks/) (2023-10-31).\n- **His stated reason for joining Radical:** the opportunity to commercialise AI research is \"far surpassing the capacity of any one organization.\" In effect, he left DeepMind to fund the people leaving DeepMind [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-launches-in-london/) (2023-04-03).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He covers frontier-lab and neolab seeds out of London. Discovery Loop (Jeff Dean, Ghemawat, Le, Vinyals), Inherent ($50M seed) and Mantic ($25M seed) are the large-seed new-lab rounds where a $20M check could sit. Because he co-leads rather than solo-leads, there is often room for a third large check (Discovery Loop had Khosla, Lightspeed, Kleiner, Doerr and Alphabet). He is the best person to ask \"who is about to leave DeepMind?\" in Europe. He is less useful for chips or compute (that is David Katz at Radical) or for US AI-native automation.\n\n## Background\n- Harvard College (economics) and Harvard Business School (MBA); finishing a Master's in Computer and Information Technology at the University of Pennsylvania [src](https://radical.vc/team/aaron-rosenberg/)\n- McKinsey & Company, management consultant, including Deputy Chief of Staff to Global Managing Director Dominic Barton (dates not found). Barton later became a Radical partner [src](https://radical.vc/team/aaron-rosenberg/)\n- DeepMind, Head of Strategy & Operations in the Office of the CEO with Demis Hassabis, from 2017. He helped assemble the executive team, scale the company from 500 to 1,500 people, open international offices, set up ethics governance, and incubate and spin out Isomorphic Labs [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-launches-in-london/)\n- Radical Ventures, Partner, opening the London office, announced 2023-04-03 [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-launches-in-london/)\n- Pronouns: sources use \"he\" [src](https://sifted.eu/articles/aaron-rosenberg-radical-ventures-podcast/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/aaron-rosenberg/ | Team page (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://radical.vc/ai-perspectives/ | Bylines on Radical: about 7 posts, 2023–2026 |\n| X | not found | The team page links only the firm account @radicalvcfund; no personal handle found |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/aaronrosenberg12/ | Linked from the team page; follower count not checked |\n| GitHub | not found | RAIS framework published on Radical's GitHub |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Co-hosted one Radical Talks episode with Molly Welch (see below) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-11 — Seedcamp \"This Much I Know\" (host Carlos Espinal) — \"The Impact of AI Innovations and Why We Need to Prioritise Responsible AI\" — https://seedcamp.com/podcasts/the-impact-of-ai-innovations-and-why-we-need-to-prioritise-responsible-ai/ — DeepMind lessons and the RAIS framework (show notes only; no transcript read)\n- Date not found — Radical Talks, with Molly Welch, guest Ross Taylor (General Reasoning) on reinforcement learning and reasoning — Radical Talks feed (from search snippet; not opened)\n- HumanX Europe, Amsterdam — listed as a speaker for \"What's Holding Europe Back?\" — https://humanx.co/europe/speakers/aaron-rosenberg — sources disagree on the year (search snippet says September 2026, the page says the event runs 20–22 September 2027), so whether it has happened is unconfirmed\n- 2026-10-22 — Sifted podcast — \"Meet Aaron Rosenberg: The man DeepMind researchers call before they quit\" — https://sifted.eu/articles/aaron-rosenberg-radical-ventures-podcast/ — page returned 403; only the headline and description were seen\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** Neolabs are a real asset class: 40+ have raised about $40B since SSI, and DeepMind is the main source of their talent. Compute, not capital, is what limits them. Returns follow a power law, with bull, base (acquihire) and bear outcomes [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/). AI for science is a theme: protein design (Latent Labs), materials (Orbital), automated research (Inherent, Discovery Loop) and forecasting (Mantic).\n- **Contrarian view:** Small specialised scientific models plus agents can beat giant monolithic models on discovery tasks (Inherent's Faraday post).\n- **Calls:** Orbital (Series A lead, Feb 2024) re-raised a $50M B led by Plural, with Radical returning [src](https://fortune.com/2026/05/28/exclusive-orbital-industries-raises-50-million-series-b-funding-round-ai-to-discover-exotic-new-materials/). No losses are public.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"The Rise of NeoLabs\" (2026-06-21, with Kotite): his full framework for frontier-lab investing — https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/\n2. \"Radical Ventures Launches in London\" (2023-04-03): why he left DeepMind — https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-launches-in-london/\n3. \"The Era of Superhuman Forecasting\" (2026-09-28): his first board-seat seed lead — https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/\n4. \"Teaching AI to Ask the Right Questions\" (2026-08-17): the Inherent thesis — https://radical.vc/articles/teaching-ai-to-ask-the-right-questions/\n5. \"Underwriting Responsible AI\" (2023-10, with Leah Morris): the RAIS framework — https://radical.vc/underwriting-responsible-ai-venture-capital-needs-a-framework-for-ai-investing/\n\n## Social engagement\nNo personal X account found. He is low-profile: he writes through Radical Reads and speaks at Seedcamp, HumanX and on Sifted. His co-authors are Rich Kotite (twice) and Jordan Jacobs (Discovery Loop).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Orbital Materials (now Orbital Industries) | Series A ($16M) | 2024-02-21 | yes | Toyota Ventures; Fly Ventures; Compound (seed leads) | [src](https://startupintros.com/news/2024-02-01-orbital-materials-series-a); Rosenberg quoted in the release; board per TheOrg (aggregator) [src](https://theorg.com/org/orbital-materials/org-chart/aaron-rosenberg) |\n| Latent Labs | Series A ($40M of $50M total) | 2025-02-13 | co-led | Sofinnova (co-lead); 8VC; Kindred; Pillar; Flying Fish; Isomer; Jeff Dean; Aidan Gomez | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/02/13/latent-labs-raises-50m-develop-new-proteins-ai/), [byline](https://radical.vc/articles/launching-latent-labs/) |\n| Slingshot AI | Series A (A-2; about $53M per secondary source; $93M total) | 2025-07-22 | co-led | Forerunner (co-lead); a16z, Felicis, Menlo (prior) | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/building-the-foundation-model-for-mental-health/), [src](https://bhbusiness.com/2025/07/22/with-93m-raised-slingshot-slingshot-ai-debuts-ai-powered-therapy-service/) |\n| Inherent | Seed ($50M) | 2026-05-30 | co-led | Index (co-lead); NVentures; Ex/Ante; Metaplanet; Macroscopic; Mythos | [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/inherent-ai-50-million-seed-deepmind-faraday-science); Rosenberg wrote Radical's post. His personal lead role is not stated |\n| Discovery Loop | Seed (size undisclosed) | 2026-08-05 | co-led | Khosla (co-lead); Lightspeed; Kleiner Perkins; Doerr; Alphabet | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/our-investment-in-discovery-loop/) (bylined Jacobs and Rosenberg) |\n| Mantic | Seed ($25M) | 2026-09-28 | yes | Balderton; Thinking Machines; DRW | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/); he joined the board, Kotite is board observer |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Orbital:** Founded in 2022 by ex-DeepMind researcher Jonathan Godwin. Fly and Compound led the seed. Radical led the $16M A about 18 months after founding, which was Rosenberg's first named London lead. Radical returned in Plural's $50M B (May 2026), a double-down [src](https://fortune.com/2026/05/28/exclusive-orbital-industries-raises-50-million-series-b-funding-round-ai-to-discover-exotic-new-materials/). Not first in.\n- **Latent Labs:** Founder Simon Kohl led DeepMind's protein design team and contributed to AlphaFold, so he and Rosenberg overlapped at DeepMind. The intro path is not stated. The $50M disclosed at launch included an earlier seed; Radical co-led the $40M A [src](https://radical.vc/articles/launching-latent-labs/).\n- **Slingshot:** Radical came in at the A-2 after a16z's A, as a later entrant co-leading with Forerunner. It is a US company, so his remit goes beyond Europe.\n- **Inherent:** Founders Tantum Collins, Edward Hughes and Louis Kirsch are from DeepMind (cooperative AI and meta-learning), and Kaloyan Aleksiev is from Reka, a Radical portfolio company. Radical co-led the stealth-exit seed with Index. That makes Radical first institutional money, alongside Index [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/inherent-ai-50-million-seed-deepmind-faraday-science).\n- **Discovery Loop:** Jacobs and Rosenberg co-bylined the post. The founders include Oriol Vinyals of DeepMind, so his DeepMind ties likely mattered, though this is inference. The founders said they chose Radical and Khosla to lead (see the firm file).\n- **Mantic:** Toby Shevlane led frontier-model evaluations at Google DeepMind, and Ben Day comes from Foresight Data Machines. Radical led the round and Rosenberg took the board seat. Thinking Machines joining as an investor is unusual.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Partner, leads London/Europe | 2023-04-03 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-launches-in-london/) |\n| Demis Hassabis | Worked for him in the Office of the CEO, DeepMind | 2017 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-launches-in-london/) |\n| Dominic Barton | Former Deputy Chief of Staff at McKinsey; Barton is now a Radical partner | not found | [src](https://radical.vc/team/aaron-rosenberg/) |\n| Rich Kotite | Co-author (NeoLabs, Mantic); co-board on Mantic | 2026 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/) |\n| Jordan Jacobs | Co-lead on Discovery Loop | 2026-08 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/our-investment-in-discovery-loop/) |\n| Isomorphic Labs | Helped incubate and spin out while at DeepMind | before 2023 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-launches-in-london/) |\n| Mantic | Board director | 2026-09 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/) |\n| Orbital Materials | Board member (aggregator) | 2024 | [src](https://theorg.com/org/orbital-materials/org-chart/aaron-rosenberg) |\n| Leah Morris | Co-author of the RAIS framework | 2023-10 | [src](https://thelogic.co/news/as-ai-funding-booms-radical-ventures-launches-new-tool-to-help-investors-gauge-risks/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What he said in the Sifted podcast (2026-10-22): the page returned 403. Get the transcript for intro paths and which DeepMind departures he has funded.\n- Was Radical in Latent Labs' seed, or only the A? The portfolio page shows 2024.\n- Does he hold board seats at Latent Labs, Inherent or Orbital? Orbital is aggregator-only.\n- Is the September 2025 London breakfast of 15 DeepMind staffers (press, no investor named) connected to him?\n- Exact months at McKinsey and DeepMind (LinkedIn not read).\n- Ross Taylor / General Reasoning: is Radical an investor?\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/aaron-rosenberg/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-launches-in-london/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/teaching-ai-to-ask-the-right-questions/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/our-investment-in-discovery-loop/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/launching-latent-labs/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/building-the-foundation-model-for-mental-health/\n- https://thelogic.co/news/as-ai-funding-booms-radical-ventures-launches-new-tool-to-help-investors-gauge-risks/\n- https://sifted.eu/articles/aaron-rosenberg-radical-ventures-podcast/ (headline only)\n- https://seedcamp.com/podcasts/the-impact-of-ai-innovations-and-why-we-need-to-prioritise-responsible-ai/\n- https://humanx.co/europe/speakers/aaron-rosenberg\n- https://startupintros.com/news/2024-02-01-orbital-materials-series-a (aggregator)\n- https://fortune.com/2026/05/28/exclusive-orbital-industries-raises-50-million-series-b-funding-round-ai-to-discover-exotic-new-materials/\n- https://siliconangle.com/2025/02/13/latent-labs-raises-50m-develop-new-proteins-ai/\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/inherent-ai-50-million-seed-deepmind-faraday-science\n- https://bhbusiness.com/2025/07/22/with-93m-raised-slingshot-slingshot-ai-debuts-ai-powered-therapy-service/\n- https://theorg.com/org/orbital-materials/org-chart/aaron-rosenberg (aggregator)\n", "person:abby-meyers": "\n# Abby Meyers\n\n## One-line take\nShe went from Associate to Partner at BCV in about 34 months. Every deal she is named on is a growth-stage entry. The strongest AI-compute deal is Lumilens, where BCV co-led the $700M+ Series C. BCV's own CMS does **not** name her on Periodic Labs, Black Forest Labs or Sunday Robotics.\n\n## Crux\n- **Correction to the owner's premise.** BCV's portfolio CMS lists one partner of record for each of these deals ([CMS query](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%20in%20%5B%22periodic-labs%22%2C%22black-forest-labs%22%2C%22sunday-robotics%22%5D%5D%7Btitle%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3E%7Bfirstname%2Clast_name%7D%7D)):\n  - Periodic Labs: Slater Stich only\n  - Black Forest Labs: Slater Stich only\n  - Sunday Robotics: Aaref Hilaly only\n\n  She does not appear on any of the three. None of the 22 CMS documents that reference her mention them either ([CMS query](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5Breferences(%22H2BFRYjmvpoUBspHJiuckd%22)%5D%7B_type%2Ctitle%7D)). Amanda Huang's file shows the same pattern: BCV's prose credits a junior person, while the CMS names Stich.\n- **Why she wins.** She builds her own conviction through primary research instead of following consensus. BCV's promotion post gives three examples: A/B tests on Apollo's data quality, embedding in creator communities on Discord and Reddit for Whop, and the \"horizontal platform\" read on MaintainX ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/)). In her Legora diligence she interviewed Harvey's own customers ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/investing-in-legora-ai-and-second-mover-advantage/)).\n- **What constrains her.** She works only at growth stage. All 11 portfolio records that name her carry stage \"Growth\" ([CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26references(%22H2BFRYjmvpoUBspHJiuckd%22)%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Ename%7D)). She is never first in. On almost every deal she is paired with Merritt Hummer, so her independent lead authority is unclear.\n- **What she would never do (by her own account).** Chase a \"buzzy category\" she doesn't believe in during a market stampede ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Hiring story.** BCV met her while she was an Associate Consultant at Bain & Company and hired her straight after a reference call: \"She was hired as soon as we hung up the phone.\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/), 2025-08-07)\n- **Track record before partner.** She was \"instrumental in deploying over $190 million across six breakout companies\", five of which became unicorns ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/)). That is about $30M per company on average (my arithmetic).\n- **Original-research diligence.**\n  - Apollo: \"she ran large-scale A/B experiments to compare its data quality\" against incumbents.\n  - Whop: \"she embedded herself directly in Discord and Reddit communities\".\n\n  ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/))\n- **Legora diligence intel** (co-authored with Merritt Hummer):\n  - From interviews with Harvey's customers: \"nearly every Harvey customer we spoke with said they would consider alternatives\".\n  - Legora \"started 2025 at roughly 8% of Harvey’s scale\" and ended the year around 40%.\n  - Their thesis: \"AI weakens the foundations of the first mover playbook\".\n\n  ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/investing-in-legora-ai-and-second-mover-advantage/), CMS created 2026-03-10)\n- **Pylon.** Her highlight of 2025 was Pylon, \"as we co-led their Series B round\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/), 2025-12-18). This is a co-lead she names herself, and the CMS lists her as partner.\n- **Hardest call (2025).** \"knowing when to tune out the hype and trust my own instincts\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/))\n- **2025 surprise.** Capital flowing into \"legacy, unsexy industries (including logistics, lending, car washes, home healthcare)\", which she credits to voice AI ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/))\n- **MaintainX re-lead** (co-authored with Hummer). \"This is our second consecutive lead investment in the company.\" BCV led the Series C in fall 2023, co-led the $150M Series D with Bessemer, and Autodesk then agreed to acquire MaintainX for ~$3.6B ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-re-doubling-down-on-maintainx/), [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-autodesk-and-maintainx-belong-together/)).\n- **Rillet sourcing through BCV's portfolio** (co-authored with Hummer). When BCV offered intros, \"we were surprised to find how many companies were already customers\". BCV joined the $100M Series C ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rillet-closes-the-books-on-legacy-erp/), CMS created 2026-08-18).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **Against:** by THESIS.md's rubric she is growth-only, which would normally mean low. She never competes at your entry point, and none of her deals is a seed or a new lab.\n- **For, on compute:**\n  - Lumilens (photonic interconnects for AI compute): BCV co-led the $700M+ Series C at $5.51B on 2026-08-06, alongside Atreides, Meritech, Seligman and Spark ([src](https://lumilens.com/news-insights/lumilens-emerges-with-900m-in-funding)).\n  - Etched: BCV is a growth investor, with the round unconfirmed ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/abby-meyers/)).\n\n  Both sit in your AI-chips and compute lane. That makes her a likely later-stage buyer and price reference for compute companies you back early.\n- **For, on talent-tracking:** she is a fast-promotion node of the kind you asked to track.\n- **Not on the deals you named.** The deals you credited to her (Periodic Labs, Black Forest Labs, Sunday Robotics) belong to Slater Stich and Aaref Hilaly in BCV's records. For frontier-lab access at BCV, look at Stich, not Meyers.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Concord, Massachusetts. Calls herself a reader first, with a historical bent ([team CMS record](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22team%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22abby-meyers%22%5D%7Babout%7D)).\n- Associate Consultant at Bain & Company before BCV ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/)).\n- Joined BCV as an Associate in New York, focused on growth-stage fintech and application software. The hire post is dated 2022-10-25 ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-our-newest-bcv-hires/)).\n- Named Partner on 2025-08-07 ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/)). That is about 34 months from associate to partner. No principal or VP step is published.\n- Education: nothing found.\n- The firm's posts use she/her ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://baincapitalventures.com/team/abby-meyers/ | BCV team page; 6 highlighted 2026 growth deals (as of 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | BCV insights co-bylines | ~15 posts, mostly co-authored with Merritt Hummer |\n| X | nothing found | Team page links only @BainCapVC |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/abby-meyers-9899b2138/ | Linked from team page; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The web-search budget ran out during this pass, so podcasts and YouTube were not searched. Open item.\n\n## How they think\n- **Second mover beats first mover in AI apps** when end users choose the product and switching costs are still low (Legora vs Harvey) ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/investing-in-legora-ai-and-second-mover-advantage/)).\n- **AI revenue is easy; defensibility comes from capturing the whole workflow and owning proprietary data** (EvenUp, co-authored with Aaref Hilaly) ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/what-every-ai-company-can-learn-from-evenup/)).\n- **AI-in-the-physical-economy:** MaintainX, Atoms, legacy industries. She says this \"has never felt like a consensus idea\" until 2025 ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/)).\n- **2026 prediction:** incumbents fight back by building or buying. MaintainX going to Autodesk is one example ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/)).\n- **What she looks for:** founders creating a category \"that doesn’t exist yet\", with \"reality distortion\" ([team CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22team%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22abby-meyers%22%5D%7Babout%7D)).\n- **Calls so far:**\n  - MaintainX: a ~$3.6B exit to Autodesk after BCV led the Series C and co-led the Series D. A clear win.\n  - Legora: BCV entered around 2026-03. Legora later raised a $550M Series E at $8.8B ([src](https://legora.com/newsroom)). Marked up, but BCV's part in that round is unconfirmed.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Investing in Legora: AI & Second Mover Advantage](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/investing-in-legora-ai-and-second-mover-advantage/): her clearest framework, with diligence detail.\n2. [VC Insights 2025](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/): her only first-person Q&A, covering her hardest call, the Pylon co-lead and her prediction.\n3. [Introducing Abby Meyers, Our Newest Partner](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/): her research methods and her $190M deployment record.\n4. [Why Autodesk and MaintainX Belong Together](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-autodesk-and-maintainx-belong-together/): the full arc of the Series C lead, the Series D re-lead and the exit.\n5. [What Every AI Company Can Learn From EvenUp](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/what-every-ai-company-can-learn-from-evenup/): her view on defensibility.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No personal X account is listed, and search was unavailable this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\nPartner of record comes from BCV's portfolio CMS, which names her on 11 records, all at stage \"Growth\" ([CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26references(%22H2BFRYjmvpoUBspHJiuckd%22)%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Ename%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3E%7Bfirstname%2Clast_name%7D%7D)).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Lumilens (AI photonic interconnects) | Series C, $700M+ at $5.51B | 2026-08-06 | Co-lead | Atreides, Meritech, Seligman, Spark (co-leads); Addition, Alkeon, Mayfield, Qualcomm Ventures, Peak XV, Redpoint. BCV partners: Hummer and Meyers. Mayfield (seed) and Spark (Series B, early 2025) were earlier | [src](https://lumilens.com/news-insights/lumilens-emerges-with-900m-in-funding) |\n| Etched (inference ASICs / clusters) | Growth; round unconfirmed | 2026 (CMS) | No | Company site lists a \"BCV\" logo alongside Sequoia, a16z, Jane Street (which led $700M at $21B), Stripes and others. BCV partners: Hummer and Meyers | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/abby-meyers/), [src](https://www.etched.com/) |\n| Chai Discovery (AI molecular design) | Series C, $400M at $3.8B | 2026-07-14 | No (new investor) | Index (lead) with Kleiner Perkins, Sequoia and Dimension. BCV partners: Hilaly and Meyers | [src](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design) |\n| Atoms (physical automation) | Growth; round unknown | 2026 (CMS) | Unknown | BCV partners: Hummer and Meyers. Atoms' site lists CloudKitchens, Pronto AI and Otter under its umbrella | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/abby-meyers/), [src](https://atoms.co/) |\n| Rillet (AI-native ERP) | Series C, $100M | 2026 (memo; CMS created 2026-08-18) | Unknown | BCV partners: Hummer and Meyers | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rillet-closes-the-books-on-legacy-erp/) |\n| Legora (legal AI) | BCV entry; round unconfirmed | ~2026-03 | Unknown | BCV partners: Hummer and Meyers | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/investing-in-legora-ai-and-second-mover-advantage/) |\n| Pylon (AI B2B support) | Series B | 2025 | Co-lead | BCV partners: Hummer and Meyers | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/) |\n| EvenUp (AI for personal-injury law) | Growth entry | 2023 (CMS); follow-on 2024 | Unknown | BCV partners: Hilaly and Meyers | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/leveling-the-legal-playing-field-through-ai-why-we-invested-in-evenup/) |\n| MaintainX | Series C (led, fall 2023); Series D $150M (co-led with Bessemer, 2025-07) | 2023 / 2025 | Yes | Bessemer. Autodesk agreed to acquire for ~$3.6B (2026) | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-re-doubling-down-on-maintainx/), [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-autodesk-and-maintainx-belong-together/) |\n| Apollo.io | Growth | 2023 (CMS) | Unknown | BCV partners: Hummer and Meyers | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/how-apollo-io-is-helping-every-company-reach-its-full-revenue-potential/) |\n| Whop | Growth | 2024 (CMS) | Unknown | BCV partners: Hummer and Meyers | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/) |\n| ~~Periodic Labs / Black Forest Labs / Sunday Robotics~~ | — | — | — | **Not hers.** The CMS names Slater Stich (Periodic Labs: Seed 2025; Black Forest Labs: Growth 2025) and Aaref Hilaly (Sunday Robotics: Early 2026) | [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%20in%20%5B%22periodic-labs%22%2C%22black-forest-labs%22%2C%22sunday-robotics%22%5D%5D%7Btitle%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3E%7Bfirstname%2Clast_name%7D%7D) |\n\n**Timing.** She was never first in.\n- Lumilens: Mayfield led the seed, Spark led the Series B in early 2025, and BCV came in at the Series C ([src](https://lumilens.com/news-insights/lumilens-emerges-with-900m-in-funding)).\n- Chai Discovery: BCV was a new investor in the Series C, after Thrive, OpenAI and Menlo ([src](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design)).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **MaintainX.** Her research reframed MaintainX from maintenance software into a horizontal platform for physical operations ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/)). BCV led the Series C in fall 2023, co-led the $150M Series D with Bessemer, and Autodesk then agreed to buy the company for ~$3.6B ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-autodesk-and-maintainx-belong-together/)).\n- **Legora.** Diligence was built on interviews with Harvey's customers. Most said they would consider alternatives, and many were already trialling Legora ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/investing-in-legora-ai-and-second-mover-advantage/)).\n- **Rillet.** BCV offered portfolio intros and found many of its companies were already Rillet customers. That fed into the decision to join the $100M Series C ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rillet-closes-the-books-on-legacy-erp/)).\n- **Lumilens and Etched.** No deal story found. The intro paths are unknown.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Merritt Hummer (BCV) | Co-partner on 9 of her 11 CMS deals; co-author on most of her memos | 2023 | [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26references(%22H2BFRYjmvpoUBspHJiuckd%22)%5D%7Btitle%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3E%7Bfirstname%2Clast_name%7D%7D) |\n| Aaref Hilaly (BCV) | Co-partner on EvenUp and Chai Discovery | 2023 | same |\n| Slater Stich (BCV) | Actual partner of record on Periodic Labs and Black Forest Labs | 2025 | [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%20in%20%5B%22periodic-labs%22%2C%22black-forest-labs%22%5D%5D%7Btitle%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3E%7Bfirstname%2Clast_name%7D%7D) |\n| Rak Garg, Saanya Ojha (BCV) | Co-panelists on VC Insights 2025 | 2025 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/) |\n| Enrique Salem, Saanya Ojha (BCV) | Co-authors on the post-quantum security piece | 2023 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/security-in-a-post-quantum-world/) |\n| Nicole Falasco (BCV) | Co-author on the AI and Office of the CFO survey | 2025 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/ai-and-the-office-of-the-cfo-in-2025/) |\n| Max Junestrand (Legora) | Founder she backed | 2026 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/investing-in-legora-ai-and-second-mover-advantage/) |\n| Nic Kopp (Rillet) | Founder she backed | 2026 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rillet-closes-the-books-on-legacy-erp/) |\n| Chris Turlica (MaintainX) | Founder she backed | 2023 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-autodesk-and-maintainx-belong-together/) |\n| Bain & Company | Former employer (Associate Consultant) | pre-2022 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Owner's deal list.** Where did the Periodic Labs, Black Forest Labs and Sunday Robotics attribution come from? Possibly LinkedIn or a mix-up with Amanda Huang or Slater Stich. Check her LinkedIn.\n- **Etched.** Which round did BCV join, and when? The \"BCV\" logo is not spelled out on Etched's site.\n- **Atoms.** Which round did BCV join, and who led it?\n- **Legora.** What was BCV's entry round, and did it take part in the 2026-10 Series E?\n- **Lumilens.** Who sourced the deal (Hummer or Meyers), and what was BCV's check size?\n- **Podcasts.** Any podcast or conference appearances? Search budget ran out this pass.\n- **Title history.** Any intermediate title between Associate and Partner (LinkedIn)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/team/abby-meyers/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/ (2025-08-07)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-our-newest-bcv-hires/ (publishDate 2022-10-25)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/ (2025-12-18)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/investing-in-legora-ai-and-second-mover-advantage/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rillet-closes-the-books-on-legacy-erp/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-re-doubling-down-on-maintainx/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-autodesk-and-maintainx-belong-together/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/what-every-ai-company-can-learn-from-evenup/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-future-of-b2b-support-is-conversational-and-agentic/\n- BCV Sanity CMS (public API): https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production (team id H2BFRYjmvpoUBspHJiuckd)\n- https://lumilens.com/news-insights/lumilens-emerges-with-900m-in-funding (2026-08-06)\n- https://www.etched.com/\n- https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design (2026-07-14)\n- https://legora.com/newsroom\n- https://atoms.co/\n", "person:abhi-srinivas": "\n# Abhi Srinivas\n\n## One-line take\nThe Coatue junior with the most frontier-AI deal bylines: they're named on the posts for both 2026 Anthropic rounds Coatue led or co-led, plus Databricks, Instinct, Decagon and Legion. That makes them the working-level way into the biggest growth checks in AI, but they never come in first.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win.** Srinivas is the VP on Coatue's AI deal team under GP Lucas Swisher and co-founder Thomas Laffont. Coatue's edge here is re-underwriting proven winners at scale and moving early in the growth phase, using proprietary data science. Examples include tracking Claude commits on public GitHub and running a survey of about 500 CIOs.\n- **What constrains them.** Every deal they're named on is growth or late stage. Even the one Series A (Legion) came after Accel and Picture Capital were already in. Coatue was never first in on any of their deals.\n- **What they'd never do.** Lead a pre-product seed into an unproven lab. Their Instinct entry shows the pattern: the fourth priced round, about five months after a seed at a $50M valuation, at a reported $10B.\n- **How they got here.** They came up through Insight Partners' growth model, not through operating or research, so their relationships run to growth-stage CEOs and the senior Coatue partners, not to researchers.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Earliest Coatue byline and the only thesis piece is a robotics memo.** Co-written with Sri Viswanath and Vibhor Khanna (2024-07-17), it argues \"We believe robotics cannot have a ChatGPT Moment\", meaning adoption will come gradually rather than in one breakout moment. It names Skild and Physical Intelligence as companies in the software layer. The piece asks founders to email a dedicated address, robotics@coatue.com. ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/robotics-wont-have-a-chatgpt-moment))\n- **The software-over-hardware view in the same memo.** The authors \"believe software will drive differentiated value in the ecosystem atop hardware\". ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/robotics-wont-have-a-chatgpt-moment), 2024-07-17)\n- **Anthropic was underwritten partly on Coatue's own token use and GitHub tracking.** The Series G post opens by describing Coatue \"burning through Opus tokens\" while drafting it. It cites \"With 35 data scientists and engineers\" in-house and a tracked chart of Claude commits on public GitHub repos. ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-partnership-with-anthropic-enterprise-intelligence-has-liftoff), 2026-02-12)\n- **The Anthropic escalation ladder.** Coatue was a participant in Series F (2025-09-02, [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-series-f-at-usd183b-post-money-valuation)). It was a named lead with GIC on Series G (2026-02-12, [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-30-billion-series-g-funding-380-billion-post-money-valuation)) and a co-lead on Series H (2026-05-28, [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h)). Srinivas is bylined on G and H. No Coatue post naming them on F was found. The May post says \"We're proud to say we're tripling down — and co-leading this round.\" ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-continued-partnership-with-anthropic))\n- **A hidden Coatue to Claude Code link.** The May 2026 post calls \"Boris, a Coatue alum and founder of Claude Code\" a reference point for Anthropic's pace. ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-continued-partnership-with-anthropic)) The post gives a first name only, so the lead is logged as needs-id.\n- **Their Anthropic thesis line.** \"every enterprise workflow ultimately reduces to code\" ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-continued-partnership-with-anthropic), 2026-05-28). The February post compresses it to \"Everything Is Code.\" ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-partnership-with-anthropic-enterprise-intelligence-has-liftoff))\n- **Databricks pitched as \"a research lab\".** The post credits Databricks with compressing builds that took 3-4 years down to 6-9 months, and says \"we couldn't be more excited to lead this round\". Coatue first invested in 2019. ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/our-partnership-with-databricks), 2026-07-20)\n- **Instinct was bought on retention and founder pedigree.** The founder, Noah, was lead author of the Reflexion paper and previously at Sierra. The post's line is \"It's the first personal agent people actually keep using.\" Coatue says it co-led the Series C. ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/partnering-with-instinct), 2026-09-28)\n- **Decagon was underwritten with a CIO survey.** The post says \"In a survey of ~500 CIOs, we discovered that customer service\" is the top AI function. ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/partnering-with-decagon-a-new-customer-experience), 2026-01-28) The memo is written in the first person, but with three bylined authors the \"I\" can't be attributed to Srinivas.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The arguments run both ways:\n- **For.** You asked to map principals and VPs at funds that can write $10M+ or growth checks. Srinivas is the clearest such junior at Coatue on frontier AI: Anthropic G and H, Databricks, Instinct, Decagon. A VP who sits in Coatue's Anthropic process is a useful relationship if you want growth-round follow-on capital or a read on how crossover funds price labs.\n- **Against.** THESIS.md scores \"mostly growth or late stage\" as low. None of their deals is a first-in seed. Legion, the only Series A, already had Accel and Picture Capital. Coatue won't be a co-investor at your ~$20M-into-a-$600M-seed entry point.\n- **Deals that matter to you.** Anthropic (lab; Coatue went participant to lead to co-lead), Instinct (agent company from a Reflexion author; Coatue entered at $10B), and the 2024 robotics memo (Skild, Physical Intelligence).\n- **Fast-promotion screen.** Not met. They're still a VP about 5 years after starting at Insight in 2021, with no partner promotion found.\n\n## Background\n- **Education.** BA in Statistics and Data Science & Economics, Yale University ([src](https://newyorktech.com/people/abhi-srinivas), aggregator).\n- **2020.** Goldman Sachs summer analyst ([src](https://newyorktech.com/people/abhi-srinivas), aggregator).\n- **2021-2024.** Associate at Insight Partners ([src](https://newyorktech.com/people/abhi-srinivas), aggregator). No Insight-authored posts by them were found on insightpartners.com.\n- **2024-present.** Vice President at Coatue ([src](https://newyorktech.com/people/abhi-srinivas), aggregator). The first Coatue byline is 2024-07-17 ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/robotics-wont-have-a-chatgpt-moment)), which fits a mid-2024 move. No Coatue hire announcement was found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | Coatue has no public team page |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.coatue.com/blog | 7 bylined Coatue posts, 2024-07 to 2026-09 |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/abhisrinivas | per newyorktech.com (aggregator), which lists 2,222 followers as of 2026-11-10 |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. No podcast, X post or talk turned up. The web-search budget ran out during this run, so the search was limited to Coatue's sitemap and direct fetches.\n\n## How they think\n- **Recurring idea.** Coding is the wedge to the whole enterprise (\"Everything Is Code\"). They apply it to Anthropic, and in spirit to Databricks (AI Gateway, agentic system of record) and Instinct (agents that use the same tools people do).\n- **Contrarian view.** Robotics will diffuse gradually, with no single \"ChatGPT moment\", and value will accrue to software atop hardware (2024 memo).\n- **Underwriting style.** Data first: GitHub commit tracking, run-rate comparisons with hyperscalers, CIO surveys. They pick the category leader after the category is proven.\n- **Calls.**\n  - Anthropic G at $380B was marked up to $965B post at H three months later ([src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h)), a strong early mark.\n  - Decagon D at $4.5B was triple the prior valuation in under six months ([src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-d-announcement)), which shows Coatue paid up.\n  - No misses on record.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Robotics won't have a ChatGPT Moment (2024-07-17), their only thesis piece ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/robotics-wont-have-a-chatgpt-moment)).\n2. Our Partnership with Anthropic: Enterprise Intelligence Has Liftoff (2026-02-12), on data-driven lab underwriting ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-partnership-with-anthropic-enterprise-intelligence-has-liftoff)).\n3. Our Continued Partnership with Anthropic (2026-05-28), on the re-underwrite logic and the Coatue alum behind Claude Code ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-continued-partnership-with-anthropic)).\n4. Partnering with Instinct (2026-09-28), on how they judge an agent company ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/partnering-with-instinct)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. They have no public X presence.\n\n## Portfolio\nCoatue entered every company below at a later round than the first investors.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Anthropic | Series G ($30B, $380B post) | 2026-02-12 | Yes, Coatue named lead with GIC | Co-leads D. E. Shaw Ventures, Dragoneer, Founders Fund, ICONIQ, MGX. Coatue byline: Laffont, Swisher, Lai, Srinivas | [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-30-billion-series-g-funding-380-billion-post-money-valuation), [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-partnership-with-anthropic-enterprise-intelligence-has-liftoff) |\n| Anthropic | Series H ($65B, $965B post) | 2026-05-28 | Co-lead (lead tier: Altimeter, Dragoneer, Greenoaks, Sequoia) | Co-leads Capital Group, D1, GIC, ICONIQ, XN. Coatue byline adds Pranav Anand | [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h), [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-continued-partnership-with-anthropic) |\n| Databricks | Undisclosed round (size not confirmed here) | 2026-07-20 | Yes, per Coatue post | Byline: Laffont, Swisher, Lai, Srinivas, Sun. Coatue first in 2019 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/our-partnership-with-databricks) |\n| Instinct | Series C (reported $1B at $10B, aggregator) | 2026-09-28 | Co-lead per Coatue post. multiples.vc lists no lead | Sequoia, Benchmark per multiples.vc. Byline: Laffont, Swisher, Srinivas | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/partnering-with-instinct), [src](https://multiples.vc/private-comps/instinct) |\n| Decagon | Series D ($250M, $4.5B) | 2026-01-27/28 | Co-lead with Index Ventures (both new investors) | a16z, A*, Accel, BCV, Elad Gil, Forerunner, Ribbit and others continuing. Byline: Swisher, David Schneider, Srinivas | [src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-d-announcement), [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/partnering-with-decagon-a-new-customer-experience) |\n| Legion Security | Series A (amount undisclosed) | 2025-07-30 | Yes | Accel, Picture Capital. Byline: Sri Viswanath, Nina Gerson, Srinivas | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Anthropic.** Coatue participated in Series F (2025-09-02, [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-series-f-at-usd183b-post-money-valuation)). It then led G with GIC (2026-02-12) and co-led H (2026-05-28). The H post notes \"the last round was only three months ago.\" It credits Claude Code's founder, whom it calls \"a Coatue alum\", for the pace framing. Srinivas's personal role beyond co-authoring is not stated. Coatue was not first: Anthropic had raised for years before Coatue's Series F entry.\n- **Instinct.**\n\n  | Round | Date | Terms | Lead(s) | Source |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|\n  | Seed | Apr 2026 | ~$50M valuation | Conviction, with Greenoaks | aggregator |\n  | Series A | early Aug 2026 | $75M at $500M | Kleiner | aggregator |\n  | Series B | Aug 2026 | $250M at $2.5B | Benchmark and Index | aggregator |\n  | Series C | 2026-09-28 | reported $1B at $10B | Coatue, co-lead | Coatue post |\n\n  All aggregator rows are from [multiples.vc](https://multiples.vc/private-comps/instinct). Coatue entered about 5 months after the seed, at roughly 200x the seed valuation (arithmetic on aggregator figures). Coatue says the founder started Instinct \"less than a year ago\" ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/partnering-with-instinct)).\n- **Decagon.**\n  - Earlier rounds: a16z led the seed, with A* in at seed, and Accel led the A ([src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/), per the repo row for kevin-hartz).\n  - Coatue and Index came in as new investors at the D, at triple the prior valuation in under six months ([src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-d-announcement)).\n  - Nina Gerson, who later moved to Index, had worked on Decagon at Coatue (see people/nina-gerson.md).\n- **Databricks.** This is a continuation position from 2019 (\"nearly a decade of partnership\"). Coatue led the 2026 round ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/our-partnership-with-databricks)).\n- **Legion.** Coatue led the Series A alongside existing backers Accel and Picture Capital. The intro path was not found ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lucas Swisher (Coatue GP) | Senior co-author on Anthropic G/H, Databricks, Instinct, Decagon | 2026 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/partnering-with-instinct) |\n| Thomas Laffont (Coatue co-founder) | Senior co-author on Anthropic G/H, Databricks, Instinct | 2026 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/our-partnership-with-databricks) |\n| Sri Viswanath (ex-Coatue GP) | Senior co-author on robotics memo (2024) and Legion A (2025) | 2024 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/robotics-wont-have-a-chatgpt-moment) |\n| Vibhor Khanna (Coatue) | Co-author on robotics memo | 2024 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/robotics-wont-have-a-chatgpt-moment) |\n| Nina Gerson (now Index) | Co-author on Legion A | 2025 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai) |\n| David Schneider (Coatue) | Co-author on Decagon D | 2026 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/partnering-with-decagon-a-new-customer-experience) |\n| Jade Lai (Coatue) | Co-author on Anthropic G/H, Databricks | 2026 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-continued-partnership-with-anthropic) |\n| Pranav Anand (Coatue) | Co-author on Anthropic H | 2026 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-continued-partnership-with-anthropic) |\n| Philena Sun (Coatue) | Co-author on Databricks | 2026 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/our-partnership-with-databricks) |\n| Insight Partners | Former employer (associate) | 2021-2024 | [src](https://newyorktech.com/people/abhi-srinivas) (aggregator) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Location conflict.** newyorktech.com says San Francisco. The task brief said New York, and the repo's roster-coatue lead row says NY. Neither is confirmed by Coatue.\n- **Insight Partners deals.** Which deals did they work on at Insight in 2021-24? Nothing was found.\n- **Promotion timing.** Will they make partner, and when? Coatue does not publish promotions on its blog.\n- **Robotics follow-through.** Did Coatue invest in Skild or Physical Intelligence after the 2024 memo, and was Srinivas on it?\n- **Boris.** Identify \"Boris\", the Coatue alum and Claude Code founder, and whether Srinivas knows him from Coatue.\n- **Instinct round size and lead.** The Coatue post and multiples.vc disagree on whether Coatue co-led.\n- **Databricks round.** The size and terms of the 2026-07 round were not confirmed from a primary source here.\n- **Search coverage.** The web-search budget was exhausted during this run. X, podcasts and LinkedIn posts were not searched.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/robotics-wont-have-a-chatgpt-moment\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-partnership-with-anthropic-enterprise-intelligence-has-liftoff\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-continued-partnership-with-anthropic\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/our-partnership-with-databricks\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/partnering-with-instinct\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/partnering-with-decagon-a-new-customer-experience\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-series-f-at-usd183b-post-money-valuation\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-30-billion-series-g-funding-380-billion-post-money-valuation\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h\n- https://decagon.ai/blog/series-d-announcement\n- https://multiples.vc/private-comps/instinct (aggregator)\n- https://newyorktech.com/people/abhi-srinivas (aggregator)\n- https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/ (secondary, via repo)\n", "person:abhishek-malani": "\n# Abhishek Malani\n\n## One-line take\nMalani is the growth-team half of Sequoia's late, large AI bets: Etched Series C, Instinct, Parallel and Waymo. Every one is co-signed with a GP, and none was a first check, which makes Malani a signal of where Sequoia's growth money is headed, not a seed-stage co-investor.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** Malani sits on the Sequoia growth team, which writes the large, priced rounds that come after AI companies have proof. Etched is the clearest case: a $300M Series C at a $10B pre-money valuation that Sequoia led [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/). Malani brings a Silver Lake training in deal structure and business models [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/).\n- **What constrains them:** Malani never appears alone. Every company on the Sequoia page lists a more senior partner beside them: Sonya Huang (Etched), Pat Grady (Instinct), Andrew Reed (Parallel, which took the board seat), Konstantine Buhler (Waymo), Alfred Lin (Kalshi), and David Cahn, Lauren Reeder and Brian Halligan (Juicebox) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/etched/) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/instinct/) [src](https://parallel.ai/blog/series-b) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/waymo/) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/kalshi/) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/juicebox/). Malani is also not among the seven GPs left after Sequoia's November 2026 restructuring (per `people/alfred-lin.md`, citing [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)).\n- **What they'd never do (inferred):** Malani is not on record writing a pre-product or pre-revenue check. Their earliest AI deal is Juicebox's Series A, and Juicebox was already at $10M ARR when Sequoia led it [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/why-were-partnering-with-juicebox-the-recruiting-platform-founders-are-obsessed-with/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Etched memo admits Sequoia came in once the inference thesis was consensus.** The post Malani co-wrote with Huang says that today \"building an inference-specific system is consensus\" and calls the bet \"deeply contrarian\" only in 2022. Sequoia arrived about 40 months after Primary's March 2023 seed [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/) (seed date per `companies/etched.md`).\n- **The Etched round terms come from Sequoia's own post:** \"leading their $300 Million Series C at a $10 Billion pre-money valuation\", alongside Jane Street, a16z, Diffusion and SK Hynix [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/).\n- **What they underwrote at Etched was the team, not the chip.** The post says Sequoia is backing \"the machine that builds the machine\", which means the team's ability to ship one generation after another, not Gen 1 alone [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/).\n- **Office visits were part of the diligence.** The memo opens a paragraph with \"Walking into the Etched office for the first time\", then cites the team's San Jose rack lab and Taiwan office [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/).\n- **Macro view on compute:** \"The intelligence race is fundamentally compute constrained.\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/)\n- **Juicebox was sourced through founder word of mouth and Sequoia's own use of the product.** The memo says \"an early-stage founder told us he was 'obsessed'\", and that Sequoia's own recruiting team already used Juicebox [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/why-were-partnering-with-juicebox-the-recruiting-platform-founders-are-obsessed-with/).\n- **Juicebox was capital-efficient before Sequoia came in:** \"With just four people, the team bootstrapped to $1M in ARR.\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/why-were-partnering-with-juicebox-the-recruiting-platform-founders-are-obsessed-with/)\n- **Self-described motive:** Malani left Silver Lake because \"I wanted to play offense: to meet founders earlier\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/). In practice, \"earlier\" still means growth rounds.\n- **What they screen for:** \"companies that are navigating an inflection point\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/).\n- **On Instinct, Sequoia entered at roughly 200x the seed price.** An aggregator lists the seed (Apr 2026, Conviction, ~$50M valuation) and a Sep 2026 $1B round at $10B from Sequoia, Benchmark and Coatue, with \"no lead investor\" identified. This is secondary and unconfirmed by Sequoia or Instinct [src](https://multiples.vc/private-comps/instinct). Sequoia's company page pairs Malani with Pat Grady [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/instinct/).\n- **Title change:** Sequoia's structured page data now calls Malani \"a partner on the Sequoia Capital growth team\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/). The repo's earlier Sequoia roster pass recorded \"Investor (Core)\" (`firms/sequoia-capital.md`). No dated promotion announcement was found.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** Malani is a growth-stage partner. Every AI deal they're named on is Series A or later (Juicebox) or a late growth round (Etched C at $10B pre, Instinct at ~$10B, Parallel B at $2B, Waymo at $126B post) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/) [src](https://waymo.com/blog/2026/02/waymo-raises-usd16-billion-investment-round). Under THESIS.md, \"mostly growth or late stage\" means low.\n\nWhy to track them anyway: they are a likely **downstream buyer** for the owner's seed positions in AI chips and agents. Etched is the template: an early inference-chip bet that Sequoia growth priced once it had silicon. If the owner holds an AI-semis or agent company that is heading toward a $1B+ round, Malani (with Huang or Grady) is a realistic person to warm up early.\n\nThe deals that matter to the owner: **Etched** (AI chips, high), **Instinct** (consumer AI agent, medium), **Parallel Web Systems** (web search for agents, medium) and **Juicebox** (AI-native recruiting automation, medium).\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Connecticut. Their high school didn't offer economics or computer science, so they taught themselves during summers in India, where they enrolled in school alongside cousins [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/).\n- Studied economics and computer science, with a thesis on demand modeling for NYC's bike-sharing system [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/). Sequoia's page metadata lists Harvard University as their alma mater (`alumniOf`) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/). Graduation year not found.\n- Investor at Silver Lake across enterprise software, fintech, fitness and media; dates not found [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/).\n- Joined Sequoia's growth team; join date not found. Now titled Partner [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/).\n- Same school as the Etched founders: the Huang and Malani memo notes that Gavin, Chris and Rob were \"in their dorm rooms at Harvard\" in 2022 [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/). Whether the shared Harvard background played any part in the deal is not established.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/ | Sequoia bio; email listed on page |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/abhishekm1636 | Linked from Sequoia page; returned HTTP 402 on fetch (2026-11-10); follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/abhishek-malani/ | Linked from Sequoia page; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. The web-search budget for this run ran out before podcasts could be searched; only direct fetches of Sequoia, Etched, Parallel, Waymo and Instinct pages were possible.\n\n## How they think\n- **Inflection-point investor:** looks for the moment when \"market timing, product strength and team ambition\" line up [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/).\n- **Teams that repeat over chips that work once:** the Etched memo values the team's ability to ship generation after generation, and argues that winning in hardware means keeping pace with models while pushing the hardware frontier [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/).\n- **Compute is the binding constraint on AI progress** [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/).\n- **Usage pull and capital efficiency:** at Juicebox the case rested on viral recruiter adoption, Sequoia's own use, and a four-person team bootstrapping to $1M ARR [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/why-were-partnering-with-juicebox-the-recruiting-platform-founders-are-obsessed-with/).\n- **Founder trait:** \"intensity with humility\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/).\n- **Calls right or wrong:** too early to judge. Etched went from Sequoia's $10.3B to $21B within a month (per `companies/etched.md`, [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Partnering with Etched: Building the Inference Machine](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/) (with Sonya Huang, 2026-07-23): their thesis on AI chips and the round terms.\n2. [Partnering with Juicebox](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/why-were-partnering-with-juicebox-the-recruiting-platform-founders-are-obsessed-with/) (with Cahn, Reeder and Halligan, 2025-09-25): how a usage-led AI automation deal got sourced.\n3. [Sequoia bio](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/): the move from Silver Lake and what they look for.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed. The X profile could not be fetched (HTTP 402), and the search budget was exhausted.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Etched (AI inference chips) | Series C, $300M at $10B pre ($10.3B reported) | 2026-07-23 | Yes, Sequoia led. Co-partner Sonya Huang. Who led within Sequoia not stated | a16z, Jane Street, Diffusion, SK Hynix (also Argo and Primary per Etched) | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/etched/) |\n| Etched | $700M growth round at $21B | 2026-08-18 | No, Jane Street led. Sequoia participated; Malani's personal role in this round not stated | Kleiner, a16z, Thiel, Tiger, BCV and others | [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one) (per `companies/etched.md`) |\n| Instinct (consumer AI agent, Noah Shinn) | Reported Series C, $1B at $10B (aggregator) | 2026-09 (aggregator); Sequoia lists partnered 2026 | Not identified (\"no lead investor\" per aggregator). Co-partner Pat Grady | Benchmark, Coatue | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/instinct/) [src](https://multiples.vc/private-comps/instinct) |\n| Parallel Web Systems (web search for AI agents) | Series B, $100M at $2B | 2026-04-29 | Sequoia led. Board seat went to Andrew Reed. Malani co-listed with Reed and Sonali Singh | Existing: Kleiner, Index, Khosla, First Round, Spark, Terrain | [src](https://parallel.ai/blog/series-b) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/parallel-web-systems/) |\n| Juicebox (AI recruiting) | Series A (amount not disclosed) | 2025-09-25 | Sequoia led. Co-signed with David Cahn, Lauren Reeder and Brian Halligan | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/why-were-partnering-with-juicebox-the-recruiting-platform-founders-are-obsessed-with/) |\n| Waymo | $16B at $126B post | 2026-02-02 | Sequoia co-led with Dragoneer and DST Global. Co-partner Konstantine Buhler | a16z, Mubadala, Bessemer, Silver Lake, Tiger, T. Rowe, CapitalG, Fidelity, GV, Kleiner, Temasek and others | [src](https://waymo.com/blog/2026/02/waymo-raises-usd16-billion-investment-round) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/waymo/) |\n| Kalshi (prediction markets; not AI) | Sequoia first partnered \"Early\", 2020; the round Malani works on is not stated | 2020 (Sequoia) | unknown. Co-partner Alfred Lin | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/kalshi/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Etched: a late entry, then a doubling.** Primary's Brian Schechter led the seed in March 2023, Stripes priced the $5B Series B in December 2025, and Sequoia (Huang and Malani) led the C in July 2026 (timeline per `companies/etched.md`). Sequoia was not first: it arrived about 40 months after the seed and seven months after Stripes. One month later Jane Street led a $700M round at $21B and Sequoia participated [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one). The memo describes in-person office visits and cites the company's TSMC tape-out and a cluster bring-up in 40 days [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/).\n- **Stanley Druckenmiller and Etched (the user's question).** Etched lists Druckenmiller as an angel, and TechCrunch names him among backers of the earlier unannounced financings. That is a direct personal holding, not exposure through Sequoia (per `companies/etched.md`, citing [etched.com](https://www.etched.com/) and [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/)). Nothing links Druckenmiller to Malani.\n- **Instinct.** The seed was Conviction and Greenoaks (Apr 2026). A Kleiner-led Series A ($75M at $500M) followed; it appears only in the aggregator's text, not its funding table. Index and Benchmark co-led the B in Aug 2026 ($250M at $2.5B) [src](https://siliconangle.com/2026/08/27/consumer-focused-ai-assistant-startup-instinct-reportedly-raising-250m/). Sequoia (Grady and Malani) shows up only at the reported $10B round, about 5 months after the seed [src](https://multiples.vc/private-comps/instinct). Sequoia was among the last to enter.\n- **Parallel.** Sequoia came in at the B, five months after the A, with Reed taking the board seat [src](https://parallel.ai/blog/series-b).\n- **Juicebox.** The deal was sourced through founder word of mouth plus Sequoia's internal recruiting team using the product. Sequoia led the A at $10M ARR and 2,500 customers [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/why-were-partnering-with-juicebox-the-recruiting-platform-founders-are-obsessed-with/).\n- **Waymo and Silver Lake overlap.** Malani's former firm, Silver Lake, was a participant in the same $16B Waymo round that Sequoia co-led [src](https://waymo.com/blog/2026/02/waymo-raises-usd16-billion-investment-round). It's a coincidence of growth syndicates; no evidence connects Malani to Silver Lake's allocation.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sequoia Capital | Partner, growth team | join date not found | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/) |\n| Silver Lake | Former investor | dates not found | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/) |\n| Sonya Huang | Co-partner and co-author, Etched | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/etched/) |\n| Pat Grady | Co-partner, Instinct | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/instinct/) |\n| Andrew Reed | Co-partner, Parallel (Reed holds the board seat) | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/parallel-web-systems/) |\n| Sonali Singh | Co-partner, Parallel | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/parallel-web-systems/) |\n| Konstantine Buhler | Co-partner, Waymo | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/waymo/) |\n| Alfred Lin | Co-partner, Kalshi | listed 2026; Sequoia since 2020 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/kalshi/) |\n| David Cahn, Lauren Reeder, Brian Halligan | Co-authors, Juicebox | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/why-were-partnering-with-juicebox-the-recruiting-platform-founders-are-obsessed-with/) |\n| Etched founders (Gavin Uberti, Rob Wachen, Chris Zhu) | Portfolio | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/etched/) |\n| Noah Shinn | Portfolio founder (Instinct) | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/instinct/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which round of Instinct did Sequoia enter, and did it lead? Sequoia and Instinct have published nothing; only an aggregator reports the $1B/$10B round. Is the founder Noah Shinn the same person as the Reflexion-paper author?\n- Is Malani on any Kalshi round (2024–2025 growth rounds), or are they just listed as a support partner?\n- When did Malani join Sequoia, and when were they made Partner? The firm file had \"Investor (Core)\" earlier, so this may be a recent promotion, which is the owner's alpha segment.\n- Do they hold board or observer seats anywhere?\n- Podcasts and X posts: not searched (search budget exhausted). Worth a rerun.\n- Is Druckenmiller in Etched's equity directly (angel, per Etched) and also through Koko Xs's Nova? See `firms/nova-global.md`.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/abhishek-malani/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/why-were-partnering-with-juicebox-the-recruiting-platform-founders-are-obsessed-with/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/etched/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/instinct/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/parallel-web-systems/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/juicebox/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/waymo/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/kalshi/\n- https://parallel.ai/blog/series-b\n- https://waymo.com/blog/2026/02/waymo-raises-usd16-billion-investment-round\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one\n- https://siliconangle.com/2026/08/27/consumer-focused-ai-assistant-startup-instinct-reportedly-raising-250m/\n- https://multiples.vc/private-comps/instinct (aggregator)\n- Repo: companies/etched.md, firms/sequoia-capital.md, people/alfred-lin.md\n", "person:adam-dangelo": "\n# Adam D'Angelo\n\n## One-line take\nThe OpenAI director whose personal cheques map the data-and-compute supply chain around the labs (Scale, Mercor, Protege, DatologyAI, Cerebras, Lambda), and who is also an LP in Neo [src](https://neo.substack.com/p/neo-has-raised-235m-in-new-funds).\n\n## Crux\nFounders want him for what the name signals to labs and to other angels, not for the cheque size; an OpenAI director and model-platform operator (Poe) on the cap table is a credential for a company that sells to labs. He follows a lead; no round is recorded as led or priced by him. The constraint is conflict and attention: he runs Quora, sits on OpenAI's board, and says nothing in public about his investing, so access is by relationship only. He does not run a fund or take outside money (aggregator profiles describe a single-family office; no Form D or ADV was found).\n\n## Differential insight\n- He is listed first among the individual backers of Neo's 2023 funds, so he is an LP in the firm that seeds the same new-grad founders he later backs directly [src](https://neo.substack.com/p/neo-has-raised-235m-in-new-funds).\n- On the model layer: \"In order to train these frontier models, you need billions of dollars of capital\" [src](https://a16z.com/adam-dangelo-ai-masses/).\n- \"I'm personally the most excited just about scale.\" [src](https://a16z.com/adam-dangelo-ai-masses/)\n- On where startups get room: \"it's a real problem for the incumbent, because they can't compromise their brand\" [src](https://a16z.com/adam-dangelo-ai-masses/).\n- Advice to founders: \"I think experimentation is really the way to go to generate ideas\" [src](https://a16z.com/adam-dangelo-ai-masses/).\n- Shizuku AI (Feb 2026): press describes his cheque beside a16z and DeNA as personal [src](https://thebridge.jp/en/2026/02/ai-virtual-youtuber-shizuku-developer-shizuku-ai-secures-seed-round-funding-led-by-a16z).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. He is not a lead and not a fund, but his book is a clean signal for AI data and compute vendors at seed and Series A, and he is close to the talent network (Neo) that produces new-lab founders. Deals that matter here: Cerebras, Lambda, Mercor, Protege, DatologyAI, Mechanize.\n\n## Background\n- Facebook CTO, 2006 to 2008; co-founded Quora in 2009; OpenAI board since 2018 (dates per Wikipedia, secondary) [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adam_D%27Angelo).\n- Early investor in and advisor to Instagram before the 2012 acquisition; early investor and director at Asana [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adam_D%27Angelo).\n- Says he was excited about AI early: \"I was actually very excited about AI early on in my career.\" [src](https://a16z.com/adam-dangelo-ai-masses/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | none found | |\n| Substack / blog | Quora answers and Poe posts (not read) | |\n| X | https://x.com/adamdangelo | not read this pass; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\nHow founders reach him: no stated process. The visible routes are shared leads (Index, a16z, Khosla, FirstMark) and the Neo network.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-03 — a16z AI Revolution — Bringing AI to the Masses — [link](https://a16z.com/adam-dangelo-ai-masses/) — capital intensity of frontier models; scale optimism; mined\n- 2024-06 — Lightspeed GenSF — highlights talk — [link](https://lsvp.com/event/highlights-from-gensf-featuring-adam-dangelo/) — Poe origin; not mined\n- 2025-04 — Tetragrammaton with Rick Rubin — [link](https://podwise.ai/episodes/3768906) — not mined\n- 2023-11 — The Information profile during the OpenAI board crisis — [link](https://feature.theinformation.com/articles/the-quiet-silicon-valley-insider-complicating-sam-altmans-return) — paywalled, not read\n\nNothing found where he talks about angel investing itself.\n\n## How they think\n- Frontier models are a scale game for a few; everyone else competes on product [src](https://a16z.com/adam-dangelo-ai-masses/).\n- Model makers will mostly distribute through APIs and aggregators such as Poe [src](https://a16z.com/adam-dangelo-ai-masses/).\n- Publicly stated passes or regrets: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n- The a16z conversation, the only long first-person source read [src](https://a16z.com/adam-dangelo-ai-masses/).\n- Partovi's 2023 fund post, for the Neo LP link [src](https://neo.substack.com/p/neo-has-raised-235m-in-new-funds).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed (X not read this pass).\n\n## Portfolio\nAI cheques with a source. \"Early\" marks pre-seed or seed entry. Cheque sizes are not disclosed for any of them.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Lead / co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Vicarious (early) | Seed (launch funding) | 2011-02 | no | not stated | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2012/08/21/vicarious-good-ventures-funding) |\n| Rosebud AI (early) | YC-era angel | 2019 | no | Karpathy; Sutskever; Jeff Dean | [src](https://rosebud.ai/company) |\n| Scale AI | Series C ($100M) | 2019-08 | no | Founders Fund led | [src](https://scale.com/blog/series-c) |\n| Turing | Series B | 2020-11 | no | aggregator only | [src](https://www.employbl.com/companies/Turing.com/funding-rounds) |\n| Cerebras | early angel, round not disclosed | not stated | no | - | [src](https://www.cerebras.ai/company) |\n| Lambda | Series B ($44M) | 2023-03 | no | - | [src](https://lambda.com/blog/lambda-raises-44m-to-build-worlds-best-cloud-for-training-ai) |\n| DatologyAI (early) | Seed ($11.65M) | 2023-09 (announced 2024-02) | no | Amplify led; Jeff Dean; Yann LeCun | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2024/02/22/datologyai-raises-11-65m-automate-data-curation-efficient-ai-training) |\n| Protege (early) | Seed ($10M) | 2024-09 | no | - | [src](https://withprotege.substack.com/p/protege-raises-10-million-and-launches) |\n| Mercor | Series A ($30M) | 2024-09 | no | Benchmark led | [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/mercor-raises-30m-series-a-at-a-250m-valuation-to-create-jobs-with-ai-302252449.html) |\n| Basis | Series A ($34M), then Series B | 2024-12; 2026-02 | no | Khosla led the A | [src](https://www.getbasis.ai/blogs/basis-raises-a-34m-series-a-led-by-khosla-ventures) |\n| Town (early) | Seed | 2025-03 | no | - | [src](https://fintech.global/2025/03/17/town-secures-18m-to-revolutionise-tax-support-for-small-businesses/) |\n| Convex | Series A follow-on ($24M) | 2025-11 | no | Neo re-upped | [src](https://news.convex.dev/convex-raises-24m/) |\n| Shizuku AI (early) | Seed (~$15M) | 2026-02 | no | a16z led; DeNA | [src](https://thebridge.jp/en/2026/02/ai-virtual-youtuber-shizuku-developer-shizuku-ai-secures-seed-round-funding-led-by-a16z) |\n| Simile | Series A ($100M) | 2026-02 | no | Index led; Karpathy; Fei-Fei Li; Rauch | [src](https://www.simile.com/blog/the-simulation-company) |\n| Mechanize | $9.1M at $500M post | 2026-04 | no | angel-heavy | [src](https://www.mechanize.work/mechanize-raises-9-1m) |\n| Daytona | Series B ($60M) | 2026-10 | no | FirstMark led; Pomel; Collison; Lutke | [src](https://www.daytona.io/dotfiles/daytona-raises-60m-series-b-to-build-the-agent-cloud) |\n\nFund interest: LP in Neo's 2023 funds [src](https://neo.substack.com/p/neo-has-raised-235m-in-new-funds).\nAggregator-only, not verified: Helm.ai, PlanetScale, 8090, Wealthfront, Pilot; CB Insights counts 35 investments in total, Seedtable 24 [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/adam-dangelo). Non-AI: Instagram, Asana, a $20M personal cheque into Quora's Series B (search summary, not fetched).\n\n**Hit pattern:** of six seed-stage AI cheques, DatologyAI and Protege are data vendors to labs and Town and Shizuku are applications; the cheques that became hot (Scale, Mercor, Cerebras, Lambda, Basis) were mostly Series A or later entries behind a strong lead.\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing first-hand found for any deal. No founder quote about why they took his money was found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Neo (Ali Partovi) | LP; co-investor in Convex | 2023 | [src](https://neo.substack.com/p/neo-has-raised-235m-in-new-funds) |\n| OpenAI | board director | 2018 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adam_D%27Angelo) |\n| Index Ventures | follows Index-led rounds (Simile; co-investor in Scale C) | 2019 | [src](https://www.simile.com/blog/the-simulation-company) |\n| Andrej Karpathy; Guillermo Rauch | repeat co-angels (Rosebud, Simile) | 2019 | [src](https://www.simile.com/blog/the-simulation-company) |\n| Olivier Pomel; Patrick Collison; Tobias Lutke | co-angels in Daytona | 2026 | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/adam-dangelo) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Cheque sizes and vehicle name (no Form D or ADV found; searched EDGAR by name only indirectly).\n- Whether OpenAI's conflict policy limits what he can back; nothing public found.\n- His X posts and the paywalled Information profile were not read.\n- The 15 to 20 aggregator-listed investments with no primary source.\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/adam-dangelo-ai-masses/\n- https://neo.substack.com/p/neo-has-raised-235m-in-new-funds\n- https://thebridge.jp/en/2026/02/ai-virtual-youtuber-shizuku-developer-shizuku-ai-secures-seed-round-funding-led-by-a16z\n- https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/adam-dangelo (aggregator)\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adam_D%27Angelo (secondary)\n- company announcements linked in the portfolio table\n", "person:adil-bhatia": "\n# Adil Bhatia\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the most compute- and frontier-heavy junior on Redpoint's growth team. He co-signed the memo for the one deal here that Redpoint led (K2 Space's $250M Series C), and Redpoint's own company records credit him on only two deals: Lumilens (GPU optical interconnect) and Mind Robotics. He enters at Series A and later, and he is never first.\n\n## Crux\n- **Three tiers of deal credit.** Redpoint's CMS records disagree with his profile page ([person doc](https://22xmfoma.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_id%3D%3D%2219405783-b544-4d5c-9695-3ea8f5f6d713%22%5D%5B0%5D), [company docs](https://22xmfoma.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22company%22%26%26slug.current%20in%20%5B%22lumilens%22%2C%22mind-robotics%22%2C%22irregular%22%2C%22inferact-vllm%22%2C%22k2-space%22%2C%22physical-intelligence%22%5D%5D%7Btitle%2CinvestmentDate%2C%22people%22%3Aperson%5B%5D-%3Ename%7D)).\n  - **Named on the company record:** Lumilens (with Sai Senthilkumar, Scott Raney and Tanya Jomaa) and Mind Robotics (with Jacob Effron and Scott Raney). Only these two.\n  - **Named author on the memo:** K2 Space Series C, co-signed with Elliot Geidt, Senthilkumar and Jomaa ([memo](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/high-power-satellites-without-cost-constraints-our-investment%20-%20-in-k2-space/), 2025-12-11). The K2 company record lists only Geidt and Senthilkumar.\n  - **Claimed on his own profile but not on the company record:**\n    - Irregular: record names Senthilkumar and Raney.\n    - Inferact (vLLM): record names Satish Dharmaraj and Renee Shah.\n    - Physical Intelligence: record names nobody.\n    - Parafin, which BI credits to him: record names Elliot Geidt only ([BI](https://www.businessinsider.com/venture-capital-industry-rising-stars-list-2026-1)).\n  - **Reading:** he works as the diligence engine under Senthilkumar, Raney and Geidt, not as the partner of record. This matches the pattern already seen in the Abby Meyers and Amanda Huang files at BCV.\n- **Why he wins.** He sits on a growth team that will lead $250M rounds (K2) and co-lead frontier-lab rounds (Irregular, with Sequoia). His book is the one most aligned with compute and the frontier in Redpoint growth: GPU interconnect, the vLLM inference company, a frontier-model security lab, and robotics foundation models.\n- **What constrains him.**\n  - He is a Principal on a team led by managing directors, and every deal carries a more senior co-signer.\n  - Redpoint's entry points are Series A at the earliest. Even Inferact's \"seed\" was a $150M round that a16z and Lightspeed led ([src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference)).\n- **Title conflict to note.**\n  - BI (2026-02-03): he \"joined Redpoint Ventures as vice president of growth in 2024\".\n  - Redpoint's CMS now shows `position: Principal`.\n  - I can't tell whether that is a re-titling, a promotion or a press error. fast_promotion stays unknown.\n- **What he would never do.** Nothing sourced.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The K2 memo is his clearest published voice.** He co-signed it, and it carries the line \"Redpoint is leading K2 Space's $250M Series C\" ([memo](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/high-power-satellites-without-cost-constraints-our-investment%20-%20-in-k2-space/), 2025-12-11). Its framing is contrarian: \"The greatest advances in the history of space exploration were born of unorthodoxy.\" The thesis is to break the cost curve with large, high-power satellites for higher orbits, rather than follow the small-sat, low-orbit orthodoxy.\n- **ICONIQ follow-on.** ICONIQ Growth, his previous firm, co-led K2's next round (Series D, 2026-07-30) ([ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-next-space-economy-our-partnership-with-k2-space)), about 7.5 months after the Redpoint-led Series C he co-signed. No source says he brokered it. The sequence matters because it shows him as a possible bridge between Redpoint and ICONIQ.\n- **How he uses AI to diligence upside.** He told BI he uses ChatGPT's deep research and thinking modes to debate the possibility of \"a company becoming bigger than we would have originally imagined\" ([BI](https://www.businessinsider.com/venture-capital-industry-rising-stars-list-2026-1), 2026-02-03). BI paraphrases his advice as: VCs should build with AI themselves through side projects or internal tools.\n- **Space as a deliberate push.** BI reports he said Redpoint has been \"spending more time in the space ecosystem\" (BI's paraphrase). This is the same quarter Redpoint led K2.\n- **Co-author of Redpoint's annual market updates.**\n  - He co-wrote them with Logan Bartlett and Lydia Day: March 2025 (AI, private, public, and exits and liquidity) and the 2026 Market Update ([CMS](https://22xmfoma.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5Breferences(%2219405783-b544-4d5c-9695-3ea8f5f6d713%22)%5D%7Btitle%2CpublishedAt%2C%22authors%22%3Aauthor%5B%5D-%3Ename%7D)).\n  - The AI update hedges: \"this opportunity may resemble the internet era more than the cloud computing wave\", with a Cisco-style \"air pocket\" risk ([src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-market-update-march-2025/), 2025-03-17).\n  - This is a co-authored house view, not his alone. It does tell you he is the growth-side analyst on Bartlett's public-markets lens.\n- **Mind Robotics timing.**\n  - a16z posted its Mind Robotics investment on 2026-03-11 ([a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mind-robotics/)), and Accel calls the round a \"$500 million Series A\" ([Accel](https://www.accel.com/news/mind-robotics-bringing-ai-to-the-physical-world)).\n  - Redpoint's record carries `investmentDate: 2026-05-13`, about two months later.\n  - This may be a second close or an extension. That is unconfirmed, because a post date is not a round date.\n- **Lumilens correction.**\n  - Redpoint did **not** lead. The co-leads were Atreides, BCV, Meritech, Seligman and Spark, and Redpoint is a participant ([Lumilens](https://lumilens.com/news-insights/lumilens-emerges-with-900m-in-funding), 2026-08-06).\n  - Redpoint's record carries `investmentDate: 2026-08-05` and his name.\n  - This is his most THESIS-relevant credit: optical scale-up interconnect for GPU clusters.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:**\n  - Every company in his book is in your lane: Lumilens (AI compute interconnect), Inferact (vLLM inference), Irregular (frontier AI security lab), Physical Intelligence and Mind Robotics (physical-AI foundation models).\n  - He is a junior at a fund that writes $250M leads, which is exactly the principal type you asked to track.\n  - He is likely to be a growth buyer, and a price reference, for compute companies you back at seed.\n- **Against:**\n  - He never enters where you do. Of his credited deals, the earliest is Inferact's $150M launch round, where Redpoint was a participant, and that is a profile-page claim only.\n  - He is never first in. He is rarely the partner of record.\n- **Deals that matter to you:**\n  - Lumilens Series C (2026): Redpoint participant, he is named.\n  - Irregular Series A (2025): Redpoint co-led with Sequoia, profile-page claim.\n  - Inferact launch round (2026): Redpoint participant, profile-page claim.\n\n## Background\n- Harvard graduate ([BI](https://www.businessinsider.com/venture-capital-industry-rising-stars-list-2026-1)). Degree and year: nothing found.\n- Investor at ICONIQ Growth, \"focused on infrastructure software\" ([Redpoint bio](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/adil-bhatia/)). Dates: nothing found.\n- Joined Redpoint in 2024 ([Redpoint bio](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/adil-bhatia/)). Redpoint's CMS person record was created 2024-07-26, which suggests a mid-2024 start (inferred, [CMS](https://22xmfoma.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_id%3D%3D%2219405783-b544-4d5c-9695-3ea8f5f6d713%22%5D%7B_createdAt%2Cposition%2Cdepartment%7D)). Joining title per BI: vice president of growth.\n- Current title on Redpoint's site: Principal, Growth ([src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/adil-bhatia/)).\n- Named to Business Insider's \"37 rising stars of the venture capital industry\" (2026-02-03) ([src](https://www.businessinsider.com/venture-capital-industry-rising-stars-list-2026-1)).\n- Pronouns: BI uses he/his.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/adil-bhatia/ | Redpoint bio; 6 companies listed (as of 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | co-authored posts on redpoint.com only |\n| X | nothing found | Redpoint CMS lists only LinkedIn; search budget exhausted before an X check |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/adil-bhatia-3a7b21139/ | from Redpoint CMS `socialLinkedin` |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. The search budget was exhausted, so no podcast sweep was possible.\n- 2026-02 — Business Insider rising-stars list — https://www.businessinsider.com/venture-capital-industry-rising-stars-list-2026-1 — Talked up K2 and building with AI. This is a print interview, not a podcast.\n\n## How they think\n- **Underwrite the upside, not just the base case.** He uses LLM deep-research to argue for a bigger outcome ([BI](https://www.businessinsider.com/venture-capital-industry-rising-stars-list-2026-1)).\n- **Unorthodox hardware beats incremental hardware.** The K2 memo celebrates \"unorthodoxy\" and breaking cost curves ([memo](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/high-power-satellites-without-cost-constraints-our-investment%20-%20-in-k2-space/)).\n- **House view, co-authored.**\n  - AI is the next platform shift, but timing may be internet-like with air pockets.\n  - Category winners can be founded years after the shift begins: Google 5 years after Mosaic, Snowflake 6 years after AWS ([AI update](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-market-update-march-2025/)).\n- **Calls right or wrong.** Too early to judge.\n  - K2: ICONIQ co-led the next round about 7.5 months later.\n  - Physical Intelligence: Redpoint entered at the Series A, and the company was later in talks at roughly $20B (see `data/rows/avenir-deals.csv`, Yahoo Finance 2026-10-28).\n\n## Read these first\n1. K2 Space Series C memo (2025-12-11). His only signed deal memo found; shows the \"unorthodoxy\" and cost-curve lens. https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/high-power-satellites-without-cost-constraints-our-investment%20-%20-in-k2-space/\n2. Business Insider rising stars (2026-02-03). His only first-person quotes found. https://www.businessinsider.com/venture-capital-industry-rising-stars-list-2026-1\n3. AI Market Update, March 2025 (co-authored with Bartlett and Day). Redpoint growth's macro frame for AI. https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-market-update-march-2025/\n4. 2026 Market Update (co-authored). https://www.redpoint.com/reports/2026-market-update\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X handle in Redpoint's records, and search was unavailable to check further.\n\n## Portfolio\n\"Credit\" column:\n- **CMS** = named in the company record's `person` field.\n- **memo** = named author of the firm's investment post.\n- **profile** = listed only on his own profile page.\n- **press** = per Business Insider.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Credit | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Lumilens | Series C ($700M+, $5.51B) | 2026-08-05/06 | No. Redpoint participant | Co-leads Atreides, BCV, Meritech, Seligman, Spark; also Mayfield, Qualcomm Ventures, Peak XV, Addition, Alkeon | CMS (with Senthilkumar, Raney, Jomaa) | [Lumilens](https://lumilens.com/news-insights/lumilens-emerges-with-900m-in-funding), [Redpoint](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/lumilens) |\n| Mind Robotics | Series A ($500M per Accel) | Redpoint investmentDate 2026-05-13 | Unknown | a16z (posted 2026-03-11), Accel | CMS (with Effron, Raney) | [Redpoint](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/mind-robotics), [Accel](https://www.accel.com/news/mind-robotics-bringing-ai-to-the-physical-world) |\n| K2 Space | Series C ($250M) | 2025-12-11 | Yes. Redpoint led | ICONIQ Growth co-led the next round (Series D, 2026-07-30) | memo (with Geidt, Senthilkumar, Jomaa) | [memo](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/high-power-satellites-without-cost-constraints-our-investment%20-%20-in-k2-space/) |\n| Irregular (ex-Pattern Labs) | Series A ($80M, ~$450M) | 2025-09-17 | Co-led with Sequoia | Sequoia (co-lead); Assaf Rappaport | profile; press (BI) | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/17/irregular-raises-80-million-to-secure-frontier-ai-models/), [Redpoint](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/irregular) |\n| Inferact (vLLM) | Seed/launch round ($150M, $800M) | 2026-01-22 | No. a16z and Lightspeed led | Altimeter, Sequoia, ZhenFund, Databricks Ventures, others | profile | [Inferact](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference), [Redpoint](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/inferact-vllm) |\n| Physical Intelligence | Series A ($400M); Redpoint also in Series B ($600M, 2025-11-20) | 2024 (A) | No | Series A: Bezos, Thrive, Lux, OpenAI, Bond | profile; press (BI) | [Redpoint](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/physical-intelligence), [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=676543) |\n| Parafin (not AI) | Series C | 2024-12-17 | Unknown | n/a | press (BI); CMS names Elliot Geidt only | [BI](https://www.businessinsider.com/venture-capital-industry-rising-stars-list-2026-1), [Redpoint CMS](https://22xmfoma.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22company%22%26%26title%20match%20%22Parafin%22%5D%7Btitle%2CinvestmentDate%2C%22people%22%3Aperson%5B%5D-%3Ename%7D) |\n\n**Timing: was Redpoint first?** No, on every deal.\n\n| Company | Who was there first | Redpoint entry |\n|---|---|---|\n| Lumilens | Mayfield led the seed; Spark led the Series B | Series C |\n| Physical Intelligence | Thrive led the March 2024 seed | Series A |\n| Inferact | Sequoia partnered in 2025 (Lauren Reeder file); a16z and Lightspeed led the round | Participant in the same round |\n| Irregular | Sequoia co-led the same round | Co-lead in the same round |\n| K2 Space | Company had earlier rounds (backers not checked here) | Series C, as lead |\n| Mind Robotics | a16z and Accel posts predate Redpoint's investment date | Same Series A, later date |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **K2 Space.** Per the memo, \"when we first met them\" the team was struck by Karan Kunjur's \"executional acuity\" and Neel Kunjur's aerospace depth. No intro path is disclosed ([memo](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/high-power-satellites-without-cost-constraints-our-investment%20-%20-in-k2-space/)).\n- **Other deals.** Nothing found on first contact or intro paths.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ICONIQ Growth | former employer (infrastructure software investor) | before 2024 | [Redpoint bio](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/adil-bhatia/) |\n| Sai Senthilkumar (Redpoint Partner, growth) | co-signer: Lumilens record, K2 memo | 2025 | [CMS](https://22xmfoma.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22company%22%26%26%226382ba63-c88b-4532-8bdc-ad4e40fe3b79%22%20in%20person%5B%5D._ref%5D%7Btitle%2C%22people%22%3Aperson%5B%5D-%3Ename%7D) |\n| Scott Raney (Redpoint MD, growth) | co-signer: Lumilens, Mind Robotics records | 2026 | same CMS |\n| Jacob Effron (Redpoint MD, growth) | co-signer: Mind Robotics record | 2026 | [Redpoint](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/mind-robotics) |\n| Elliot Geidt (Redpoint MD, growth) | co-author: K2 memo | 2025 | [memo](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/high-power-satellites-without-cost-constraints-our-investment%20-%20-in-k2-space/) |\n| Tanya Jomaa (Redpoint Associate, growth) | co-signer: Lumilens record, K2 memo | 2025 | same |\n| Logan Bartlett, Lydia Day (Redpoint) | co-authors of market updates | 2025-03 | [AI update](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-market-update-march-2025/) |\n| Karan and Neel Kunjur (K2 Space) | founders backed (memo) | 2025-12 | [memo](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/high-power-satellites-without-cost-constraints-our-investment%20-%20-in-k2-space/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Was he promoted from VP to Principal, or is \"VP\" a BI error? Check his LinkedIn and Redpoint announcements. Search was unavailable this run.\n- Dates of his ICONIQ tenure, and which ICONIQ deals he worked on. In particular, did he work on any K2 relationship there before ICONIQ's 2026 co-lead?\n- Did he source Lumilens? He is the only non-MD name besides Jomaa on the record.\n- What explains Mind Robotics' Redpoint investmentDate (2026-05-13) versus a16z's post (2026-03-11): extension or second close?\n- Does he have an X handle or any podcast appearances? Not checked, because the search budget ran out.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/adil-bhatia/\n- Redpoint Sanity CMS (project 22xmfoma): person doc, company docs, written docs (queries linked inline)\n- https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/high-power-satellites-without-cost-constraints-our-investment%20-%20-in-k2-space/\n- https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-market-update-march-2025/\n- https://www.redpoint.com/reports/2026-market-update\n- https://www.businessinsider.com/venture-capital-industry-rising-stars-list-2026-1\n- https://lumilens.com/news-insights/lumilens-emerges-with-900m-in-funding\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/17/irregular-raises-80-million-to-secure-frontier-ai-models/\n- https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mind-robotics/\n- https://www.accel.com/news/mind-robotics-bringing-ai-to-the-physical-world\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-next-space-economy-our-partnership-with-k2-space\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=676543\n", "person:aditya-naganath": "\n# Aditya Naganath\n\n## One-line take\nA Kleiner partner whose agent-infrastructure Substack theses come before his deals. He has twice taken the Series A behind a Sequoia seed (Traversal 2025, Sail 2026), so he is the Kleiner entry point for agent-ops and inference companies once someone else has done the first check.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He is a former Palantir technical lead and Google PM ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/)) who writes publicly about where agents break (reliability, context, durable execution), then backs companies built on those failure points.\n- **His pattern:** He follows a Sequoia seed with a Kleiner A in the same announced package.\n  - Traversal: combined seed + Series A, \"led by Sequoia and Kleiner Perkins\", 2025-06-20 ([src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement)).\n  - Sail: Sequoia seed, Kleiner Series A, $80M combined, 2026-06-24 ([src](https://sailresearch.com/blog/sail-raises-80m)).\n- **Constraint 1, timing:** In the two deals with clear evidence, he is not the first institutional check. He is the second-in A lead.\n- **Constraint 2, his book is mostly the agent and application layer, not compute:** Glean, Profound, Maxima, Wisdom AI, Traversal ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/)). Only Sail and Together.ai are compute-adjacent, so the task's \"infra and compute specialist\" framing is only half right.\n- **What he'd likely never do:** No source shows a lab, pre-training or chip bet, or a mega-seed. This is an inference from his portfolio, not something he has said.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **His agent-reliability thesis came before Sail and after Traversal.** In July 2025 he wrote \"Reliability is the name of the game for agents\" ([src](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/infrastructure-for-agents), 2025-07-15). He argued it won't be solved purely at the model layer.\n  - That post came about 4 weeks after the Traversal announcement (2025-06-20). It looks like a write-up of a thesis he had already acted on.\n  - Sail (inference for long-horizon agents) followed 11 months later.\n- **He thinks trajectory data is the key asset:** \"The most valuable piece of data today is trajectory data\" ([src](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/infrastructure-for-agents)). In the same post he proposes \"simulation harnesses for agents\", which is how he points at RL environments and evals.\n- **The Palantir ontology shapes his app thesis.** \"Metadata ends up being the biggest source of unlock in today's systems\" ([src](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/context-engineering-is-all-you-need), 2025-10-16).\n  - The post cites Palantir's \"ontology\" by name, and he was a Palantir tech lead.\n  - Wisdom AI (an AI data analyst) and Maxima (accounting) fit this context-engineering thesis. No source ties the post to either deal.\n- **In 2024 he predicted pricing for \"AI coworkers\":** \"five to six figures annually per coworker sold\" ([src](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/ai-copilots-to-coworkers), 2024-03-13).\n  - The same post names Cognition, Factory, Magic and Detail as examples. None of them is on his Kleiner page.\n  - He also wrote \"The simulator is key\", which is the same simulation idea that came back in 2025.\n- **Sail's main credential is a Together kernels hire:** \"Neil was the first kernels hire at Together AI\" ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/sail-the-inference-platform-for-long-horizon-agents/), 2026-06-25).\n  - Naganath has held Together.ai since 2023 ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/)).\n  - Inference, not confirmed: the Together relationship probably helped him get into Sail. No source says Together made the intro.\n- **His inference thesis:** \"inference is fragmenting into platforms optimized for the shape of a specific workload\" ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/sail-the-inference-platform-for-long-horizon-agents/)).\n- **Recurring draft readers:**\n  - Dan Robinson and Alex Mckenzie are thanked on both his 2023 and 2024 Substack posts ([src](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/safeguarding-enterprise-adoption), [src](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/ai-copilots-to-coworkers)).\n  - Divya Gupta, Varadh Jain and Emily Hanks appear on the 2024 post.\n  - This is his informal thinking circle and is not in any press.\n- **Board-seat caveat on Profound:** Kleiner led Profound's $20M Series A (2025-06-18), but Ilya Fushman took the board seat ([src](https://www.tryprofound.com/blog/series-a)). Naganath's page lists Profound, but no source says he led that deal.\n- **Bun caveat:** Bun's founder thanks \"Bucky, Leigh Marie, and Shrav Mehta\" for the Kleiner-led $7M seed ([src](https://bun.com/blog/bun-joins-anthropic), 2025-12-02). Naganath is not named. The 2022 listing on his page may be inherited. That is unconfirmed.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, with caveats.**\n- **Why high:** By `THESIS.md`'s definition, he backs AI-native automation and AI compute at seed and Series A.\n  - Traversal is an autonomous AI SRE, the same agent-ops category as Serval. He co-led its seed + A, then Kleiner co-led the $120M Series B at a $1B valuation ([src](https://traversal.com/blog/traversal-series-b-120-million-ai-sre), 2026-11-03).\n  - Sail is inference infrastructure built by a former Together kernels engineer, with Tri Dao and Lip-Bu Tan as angels ([src](https://sailresearch.com/blog/sail-raises-80m)).\n  - Maxima (agentic accounting, $41M seed + A, 2025-11-18, [src](https://www.maxima.ai/newsroom)) is also AI-native automation.\n- **Caveats:**\n  - He shows no labs, pre-training or AI-chip deals, and no $100M+ seeds.\n  - He tends to take the A after a Sequoia seed rather than lead first money.\n- **How he's useful to me:** As a co-investor or follow-on signal on agent-ops and inference companies, and as a source of Series A appetite for companies I seed. He is not someone I'd sit next to in a lab mega-seed. He is not an LP prospect.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** Sail, Traversal, Together.ai, Maxima.\n\n## Background\n| Period | Move | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Not dated | B.S. Computer Science, Columbia, magna cum laude | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/) |\n| Not dated | First job: software engineering intern at Twitter; earned a patent there for an analytics product he co-created | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/) |\n| Not dated | Technical lead, Palantir Technologies | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/) |\n| Not dated | Software engineer at Nextdoor as a Kleiner Perkins Fellow | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/) |\n| Not dated | MBA, Stanford | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/) |\n| Not dated | PM at Google, growth for the \"next billion users\" team | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/) |\n| By 2019 | Kleiner Perkins investor; earliest company on his page is Glean (2019) | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/) |\n| Now | Partner, focused on AI, enterprise apps, infrastructure and security | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/) |\n\n- He is from Mumbai and speaks English, Hindi and Tamil ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/)).\n- No source dates his move from Google to Kleiner.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/ | Kleiner bio and portfolio list |\n| Substack / blog | https://adityanaganath.substack.com/ | \"Aditya's Newsletter\". 5 posts from 2023-06 to 2025-10; roughly 2–3 a year (as of 2026-11-10) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/anaganath | Linked from the Kleiner page. The fetch returned 402, so posts and followers are unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/aditya-naganath/ | His \"Early Innings of Agentic Products\" post started here ([src](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/the-early-innings-of-agentic-products)) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found this pass because WebSearch was unavailable (the turn's budget was exhausted). This is the main gap.\n\n## How they think\n**Main theses:**\n- Agent reliability is an infrastructure problem: simulation as CI, \"context lakes\", and authentication for agents ([src](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/infrastructure-for-agents)).\n- The winning AI apps are the ones that master context engineering and data modeling ([src](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/context-engineering-is-all-you-need)).\n- Agents need durable execution and semantic caching, and pricing should reflect the value of the outcome ([src](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/the-early-innings-of-agentic-products)).\n- Inference is fragmenting by workload, and the value goes to whichever layer routes each workload ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/sail-the-inference-platform-for-long-horizon-agents/)).\n\n**Contrarian view:** Reliability is \"unlikely to be solved purely at the model layer\" ([src](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/infrastructure-for-agents)). This is a bet against \"the next model fixes it.\"\n\n**Calls:**\n- His 2024 call that copilots would become \"coworkers\" (paired with simulators) came before the 2025 agent wave and before his Traversal and Maxima deals.\n- Traversal reaching a $1B valuation by 2026-11 ([src](https://traversal.com/blog/traversal-series-b-120-million-ai-sre)) is the clearest win so far.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Infrastructure for Agents](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/infrastructure-for-agents) (2025-07): his core reliability, simulation and trajectory-data thesis.\n2. [Sail: The Inference Platform for Long-Horizon Agents](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/sail-the-inference-platform-for-long-horizon-agents/) (2026-06): his compute thesis and how he judges founders.\n3. [Context Engineering is All You Need](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/context-engineering-is-all-you-need) (2025-10): his application-layer thesis, traced back to the Palantir ontology.\n4. [AI Copilots to Coworkers](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/ai-copilots-to-coworkers) (2024-03): an early call on agents, the simulator idea, and pricing.\n5. [The Early Innings of Agentic Products](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/the-early-innings-of-agentic-products) (2025-05): durable execution and pricing by units of work.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X returned 402 and search was unavailable. From Substack acknowledgments, his draft readers are Dan Robinson, Alex Mckenzie, Divya Gupta, Varadh Jain, Emily Hanks, Ethan Chan, Amin Ahmad, Nicolos Ouporov and Alex George ([src](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/ai-copilots-to-coworkers), [src](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/safeguarding-enterprise-adoption)).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Sail Research | Series A (part of $80M seed + A) | 2026-06-24 | Yes, Kleiner led the A; Naganath wrote the post | Sequoia (seed lead), A*, Redpoint, Theory, Vine, CRV; angels Tri Dao, Lip-Bu Tan, John Hennessy | [src](https://sailresearch.com/blog/sail-raises-80m), [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/sail-the-inference-platform-for-long-horizon-agents/) |\n| Traversal | Seed + Series A ($48M combined) | 2025-06-20 | Co-led (Sequoia and Kleiner); Kleiner partners named: Mamoon Hamid, Naganath | NFDG, Hanabi | [src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement) |\n| Traversal | Series B ($120M, $1B valuation) | 2026-11-03 | Kleiner co-led with Redpoint and Khosla; partner not named | Sequoia, Battery | [src](https://traversal.com/blog/traversal-series-b-120-million-ai-sre) |\n| Maxima | Seed + Series A ($41M) | 2025-11-18 | Unclear; Kleiner backing appears only in a Reuters URL slug | Not found | [src](https://www.maxima.ai/newsroom) |\n| Profound | Series A ($20M) | 2025-06-18 | Kleiner led; board seat went to Ilya Fushman | Khosla, NVentures, Saga, SPC, SV Angel | [src](https://www.tryprofound.com/blog/series-a) |\n| Wisdom AI | Not found (page says 2025) | 2025 | Unclear | Not found | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/) |\n| Together.ai | Not found (page says 2023) | 2023 | Unclear | Not found | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/) |\n| Bun (Oven) | $7M seed | 2022 | Kleiner led; founder's thank-you does not name Naganath | Khosla led the later $19M Series A; acquired by Anthropic 2025-12 | [src](https://bun.com/blog/bun-joins-anthropic) |\n| Glean | Not found (page says 2019) | 2019 | Unclear | Not found | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/) |\n| Materialize | Not found (page says 2020) | 2020 | Unclear | Not found | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n**Sail (2026)**\n- Kleiner's post says conviction came from references and direct time with the founders. Neil Movva (CEO) was \"the first kernels hire at Together AI\". Movva calls his co-founder Samir Menon the strongest programmer he met at Stanford ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/sail-the-inference-platform-for-long-horizon-agents/)).\n- Sequoia led the seed with A*, and Kleiner then led the A. Both were announced together on 2026-06-24 ([src](https://sailresearch.com/blog/sail-raises-80m)). The gap between the two rounds is not public.\n- First contact and intro path: not found. Kleiner's Together holding (since 2023) is the plausible bridge, but no source confirms it.\n\n**Traversal (2025)**\n- The founders are Anish Agarwal (CEO), Raj Agrawal, Raaz Dwivedi and Ahmed Lone. They describe themselves as Columbia and Cornell professors, an MIT-trained researcher and a Citadel Securities trader ([src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement)).\n- Sequoia (Bogomil Balkansky, Charlie Curnin) and Kleiner (Mamoon Hamid, Naganath) co-led the combined seed + A. The page does not say who led which round.\n- Kleiner then co-led the $120M Series B in 2026-11, when it \"doubled down\" ([src](https://traversal.com/blog/traversal-series-b-120-million-ai-sre)).\n- Naganath and Agarwal are both Columbia-linked (Naganath studied there, and the founder list includes a Columbia professor). This is a plausible but unsourced connection.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Kleiner Perkins | Partner (previously a KP Fellow at Nextdoor) | By 2019 | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/) |\n| Mamoon Hamid | Co-named Kleiner partner on Traversal | 2025-06 | [src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement) |\n| Sequoia (Bogomil Balkansky, Charlie Curnin) | Co-lead on Traversal; Sequoia seeded Sail ahead of his A | 2025-06 | [src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement), [src](https://sailresearch.com/blog/sail-raises-80m) |\n| Neil Movva, Samir Menon | Founders he backed (Sail) | 2026-06 | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/sail-the-inference-platform-for-long-horizon-agents/) |\n| Anish Agarwal and Traversal co-founders | Founders he backed | 2025-06 | [src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement) |\n| Ilya Fushman | Kleiner colleague; Profound board seat (Naganath also lists Profound) | 2025-06 | [src](https://www.tryprofound.com/blog/series-a) |\n| Dan Robinson, Alex Mckenzie | Repeat draft readers of his Substack (2023 and 2024) | 2023 | [src](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/safeguarding-enterprise-adoption), [src](https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/ai-copilots-to-coworkers) |\n| Redpoint, Khosla | Co-lead on Traversal B (Kleiner); Redpoint also in the Sail A | 2026 | [src](https://traversal.com/blog/traversal-series-b-120-million-ai-sre), [src](https://sailresearch.com/blog/sail-raises-80m) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who sourced Sail? Was it an intro through Together, Tri Dao or Sequoia?\n- How far apart were the Sequoia seed and the Kleiner A for Sail and for Traversal?\n- Who led Maxima's round? The only signal is the Reuters slug ([url](https://www.reuters.com/business/ai-accounting-startup-maxima-raises-41-million-kleiner-perkins-backed-round-2025-11-18/)), which I could not fetch.\n- Wisdom AI's round, date and lead are unsourced.\n- Which Kleiner round in Together.ai (2023) is his?\n- Did he inherit Bun and Glean from departed partners? Bun's thank-you names \"Bucky\".\n- His X activity, any podcasts, and his check size.\n- `people/charlie-curnin.md` does not mention Traversal yet. That would close the Sequoia side of this pairing.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/aditya-naganath/\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/sail-the-inference-platform-for-long-horizon-agents/\n- https://sailresearch.com/blog/sail-raises-80m\n- https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement\n- https://traversal.com/blog/traversal-series-b-120-million-ai-sre\n- https://www.tryprofound.com/blog/series-a\n- https://www.tryprofound.com/blog/profound-raises-96m-series-c\n- https://bun.com/blog/bun-joins-anthropic\n- https://www.maxima.ai/newsroom\n- https://adityanaganath.substack.com/archive\n- https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/infrastructure-for-agents\n- https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/context-engineering-is-all-you-need\n- https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/the-early-innings-of-agentic-products\n- https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/ai-copilots-to-coworkers\n- https://adityanaganath.substack.com/p/safeguarding-enterprise-adoption\n- Not fetched (WebSearch budget exhausted; Reuters blocked; X returned 402): podcasts, X posts, Wisdom AI funding.\n", "person:aftab-mathur": "\n# Aftab Mathur\n\n## One-line take\nA long-serving Temasek Managing Director (at the firm since July 2014, per an aggregator). He has led Temasek's deals in frontier tech such as Zipline, Improbable and Team8, and sits on boards like Perfect Day and Soul Machines. He has almost no public voice, and **nothing ties him to Thrive Capital, OpenAI or Anthropic** (see Open questions).\n\n## Crux\n*(Added 2026-11-09.)* Mathur sits on both sides of Temasek's venture machine. In 2025 he described himself as running the \"innovation investments group and the emerging technologies investments group\", which back VC funds and also invest directly [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html). The edge is scale and patience: Temasek gets Series A/B deal flow and \"break out\" signals from the tier-1 VC funds it backs, then comes in directly at Series C/D with large cheques and can hold past IPO. The binding constraint is size. He says there is a minimum cheque, below which a deal is \"harder for us\", and he looks for companies that \"require a lot of capital\". So he would not write small early cheques himself; those go through funds. He also says Temasek should not be \"the only investor\" in early-stage technologies, and frames it as crowding in private capital [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html).\n\n## Differential insight\n*All from his oral evidence to the UK House of Lords Science and Technology Committee, 2025-06-10 (remote from Singapore). This is a primary transcript that press covered only in part [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html) (API copy: [src](https://committees-api.parliament.uk/api/OralEvidence/16060/Document/Html)); partial press write-up [src](https://sciencebusiness.net/news/international-news/uk-looks-abroad-improve-its-innovation-economy).*\n- **Stage split, as a rule:** Series A/B is reached through VC funds, and Temasek invests directly only later: \"that is when we will come in directly and write those larger cheques\" (Q130). [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html)\n- **Minimum cheque as a decision gate:** \"we need to be able to write a minimum cheque size\" (Q131). He did not state the number. [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html)\n- **He prefers capital-hungry companies:** \"we look for opportunities where the companies require a lot of capital\" (Q131). The context was AI, where he noted heavy demand for the same companies. [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html)\n- **LP behaviour, concentration in a few trusted GPs:** of the tier-1 managers, \"we tend to concentrate our capital with them\" (Q132). [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html)\n- **Fund-portfolio companies as a direct pipeline:** VC funds tell Temasek which companies \"are starting to break out\", but direct deals get independent diligence (Q131). [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html)\n- **Seeding local managers:** in Singapore, \"we have seeded and anchored about five or six VC funds\" (Q130). Later in the same session he said \"seven or eight funds\" (Q139), so the count is inconsistent. In the UK he cited \"over eight or nine VC funds\". [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html)\n- **Contrarian view on non-US VCs:** US VCs over-fund breakout companies (\"we’re helping you go from A to C\"). Other markets give \"just a little less\" for discipline, and he sees that as the gap (Q132). [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html)\n- **AI thesis (mid-2025):** after LLM labs (\"the open AIs, with Mistral and others\"), the next wave is vertical AI-native companies that \"go from zero to a hundred million in 12 or 18 months\" (Q134). [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html)\n- **Fund accountability, enforced with milestones:** for funds and startups Temasek capitalised, \"This is what we need to see by year 3, year 5 or year 7\" (Q139). He also admitted that \"some of it has worked and, to be honest, some has not\". [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html)\n- **Carve-out capital:** some capital is set aside for national-strategic, capex-heavy bets that may not meet return targets, as long as returns are still met on a blended basis (Q133). [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html)\n- **A UK political relationship:** Lord Ranger of Northwood opened with \"Aftab, it is good to see you\" and referred to prior private discussions (Q134). [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low as a direct co-investor or peer. Medium as a door to a large LP.** He invests directly at Series C/D and later, with a minimum cheque, which is far from my seed and new-lab focus. Temasek does appear in huge AI rounds (Anthropic Series G/H, River AI; see Portfolio), but no source ties him personally to them. As an LP gatekeeper he matters more. His group is \"one of the largest investors globally into funds\", but it concentrates in proven tier-1 GPs and anchors new managers mainly in Singapore. An emerging manager like me fits that pattern only weakly unless I can show a track record or a Singapore or ASEAN angle [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html). The deals of his that matter most to me are Zipline and Improbable (frontier-ish deep tech) and his venture debt platforms (InnoVen, EvolutionX), not AI labs.\n\n## Background\nThe Perfect Day board bio uses \"he/him\" [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf). The PDF is undated; its metadata shows it was created in 2022 and modified in 2023.\n- **Education:** BA in Economics, Yale University [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf). No graduation year was found.\n- **Merrill Lynch and Nomura, New York:** began his career as a banker [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf). No dates found.\n- **Matcorp:** founded a specialty chemicals startup [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf). No dates found.\n- **Warburg Pincus:** private equity investor [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf). No dates found.\n- **Reliance Industries (RIL) corporate venture platform:** venture capitalist and founding team member [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf). No dates found.\n- **Temasek, 2014–present:** Managing Director, focused on early and mid-stage global technology investments [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf). MarketScreener gives a start date of 2014-07-01 at \"Temasek Capital Pte Ltd.\" [src](https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/AFTAB-MATHUR-A1TSLC/). TheOrg lists him as Managing Director at Temasek, based in Singapore [src](https://theorg.com/org/temasek/org-chart/aftab-mathur). A search snippet of TheOrg gave a longer title, \"MD, Investment, Innovation & MD, Emerging Technologies\" (unverified, not seen on the fetched page) [src](https://theorg.com/org/temasek/org-chart/aftab-mathur).\n- **Own description (2025-06-10):** in his words he had been \"at Temasek for 11 years\", leading the innovation investments group and the emerging technologies investments group. He described three pillars: direct startup investing, VC and growth fund commitments, and building platforms such as venture debt, growth debt and venture/growth secondaries [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html). His transcript title was \"Managing Director, Investment (Innovation) and Emerging Technologies\". *(Added 2026-11-09.)*\n- **EvolutionX Debt Capital (2021):** at launch of the US$500M DBS–Temasek growth-debt platform in August 2021, he was named joint interim CEO alongside Amit Sinha of DBS, with the title \"Director of Investment (Innovation)\". I saw this in a search result for e27; the page returned 403, so it is unverified [src](https://e27.co/temasek-dbs-team-up-to-launch-growth-debt-financing-platform-for-asias-tech-startups-20210802/). *(Added 2026-11-09.)*\n- **2025-11-28:** he met Thai foreign-ministry adviser Chulamanee Chartsuwan at Temasek in Singapore, titled \"Managing Director for Innovation and Emerging Technologies\" [src](https://indiplomacy.com/2025/12/02/temasek-and-thailand-discuss-strengthening-aseans-startup-ecosystem/). *(Added 2026-11-09.)*\n- **Current status (as of 2026-11-09):** I found no evidence that he has left Temasek. Universal Asset Owners still lists him as Managing Director at Temasek Holdings, last checked 2026-06-09 [src](https://www.universalassetowners.com/registry/person/aftab-mathur/). I found no 2025–2026 news naming him.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found / not verified | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| TheOrg | https://theorg.com/org/temasek/org-chart/aftab-mathur | Org-chart listing, checked 2026-11-09 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. *(2026-11-09 update below.)* I still found no podcasts, Substack or YouTube talks.\n- 2025-06 — UK House of Lords Science and Technology Committee — \"Financing and scaling UK science and technology\", oral evidence on 2025-06-10 (Q129–Q139) — https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html — his most detailed public account of Temasek's split between funds and direct investing, its minimum cheque, and its AI thesis (see Differential insight).\n- 2026-02 (unverified) — Web Summit Qatar — he is listed as an attendee [src](https://qatar.websummit.com/attendees/qat26/70ba3d53-86d5-4867-91ae-0e985890d353/aftab-mathur/). A search snippet described a panel on AI and the global South with Dana Settle (Greycroft), but I could not open the source. A Fast Company Middle East X post quotes him on sovereigns as the \"initial catalyst\" in emerging markets; it returned 402, so I could not verify it [src](https://x.com/fastcompanyme/status/2019296108057620604).\n\n## How they think\nNothing found in his own words. His portfolio leans toward frontier and deep tech: drone logistics (Zipline), simulation software (Improbable), cybersecurity (Team8), digital humans (Soul Machines), Li-Fi (pureLiFi) and precision fermentation (Perfect Day) [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf). That pattern is my own inference, not a thesis he has stated.\n\n*2026-11-09 update: his own words now exist.* See the 2025-06-10 House of Lords evidence [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html). The recurring ideas are:\n- **\"Catalytic capital\":** he says \"I talk about catalytic capital a lot\". Temasek wants to be first in and then crowd in private money, not be the only funder.\n- **Exit flywheels arrive sector by sector:** fintech in Europe and e-commerce in India were first, and recycled liquidity builds each ecosystem (he cites the \"PayPal mafia\").\n- **Vertical AI-native companies are the next capital wave after LLM labs:** coding, healthcare, HR and customer-service agents.\n- **Temasek's US-vs-rest view:** US VCs fund breakout companies generously, while other markets under-fund them for \"discipline\".\n\n## Read these first\n*(2026-11-09.)* Start with the House of Lords oral evidence of 2025-06-10, Q129–Q139. It is the only long-form source in his own words [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html). Before that update I had found nothing by him. The Perfect Day board bio is the most complete primary description of him [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public social accounts were identified.\n\n## Portfolio\nThe Perfect Day bio says he is \"responsible for leading\" Temasek's investments in Zipline, Improbable and Team8. It also lists board seats at Perfect Day, Soul Machines, Eigen Technologies, PureLiFi and Innoven Capital, and board observer roles at Fabric, Bolt Threads, WithinVR and Airmap. He was formerly a board observer at Impossible Foods and Helix RE [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf). The bio gives no rounds or dates.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Zipline | unknown | unknown | Led Temasek's investment (personal role) | unknown | [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf) |\n| Improbable | unknown | unknown | Led Temasek's investment (personal role) | unknown | [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf) |\n| Team8 | unknown | unknown | Led Temasek's investment (personal role) | unknown | [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf) |\n| Soul Machines | Series B ($40M) | 2020-01 | Temasek led; his personal role is unverified beyond his board seat | unknown | [src](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2020/01/10/soul-machines-sources-40m/?format=md) |\n\n**Temasek AI bets: none name him.**\n- **Anthropic:** Temasek invested in Series G (Feb 2026) and Series H (May 2026, $965B post-money) [src](https://vulcanpost.com/911732/temasek-gic-anthropic/). No Temasek individual is named.\n- **OpenAI:** the FT reported preliminary talks on 2024-03-05 [src](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2024/03/05/singapore039s-temasek-in-talks-to-invest-in-openai-ft-reports). In 2026, Temasek said its AI investments over the past year included OpenAI and Anthropic stakes [src](https://www.malaymail.com/news/money/2026/07/08/singapores-temasek-to-triple-ai-investments-by-2031-targets-15pc-exposure/226880). No executives are named.\n- **River AI:** Temasek was an additional investor in the $1.1B round led by General Catalyst and AMP PBC (2026-08) [src](https://www.lw.com/en/news/2026/08/latham-advises-amd-in-river-ai-funding). No Temasek individual is named.\n\nThese are firm-level deals. None is attributed to Mathur, so they are not in his deals rows.\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Temasek | Managing Director | 2014-07 | [src](https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/AFTAB-MATHUR-A1TSLC/) |\n| Perfect Day | Board director | unknown | [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf) |\n| Soul Machines | Board director | unknown | [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf) |\n| Eigen Technologies | Board director | unknown | [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf) |\n| pureLiFi | Board director | unknown | [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf) |\n| InnoVen Capital | Board director (MarketScreener lists this as former) | unknown | [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf) |\n| Fabric, Bolt Threads, WithinVR, Airmap | Board observer | unknown | [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf) |\n| Impossible Foods, Helix RE | Former board observer | unknown | [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf) |\n| Golden Gate Ventures | Advisor (unverified, aggregator only) | unknown | [src](https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/AFTAB-MATHUR-A1TSLC/) |\n| Sterling Biotech Ltd. | Director (unverified, aggregator only) | 2025-06-25 | [src](https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/AFTAB-MATHUR-A1TSLC/) |\n| Reliance Industries venture platform | Founding team member, VC | unknown | [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf) |\n| Warburg Pincus | PE investor (former) | unknown | [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf) |\n| Merrill Lynch, Nomura | Banker (former) | unknown | [src](https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf) |\n| Lord Ranger of Northwood (UK House of Lords) | Prior private discussions on investment landscape, per Lord Ranger | before 2025-06 | [src](https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html) |\n| EvolutionX Debt Capital / Amit Sinha (DBS) | Joint interim CEO at launch (unverified, page 403) | 2021-08 | [src](https://e27.co/temasek-dbs-team-up-to-launch-growth-debt-financing-platform-for-asias-tech-startups-20210802/) |\n| Adrian Chung (Temasek, Institutional Relations) | Colleague at the Thai ministry meeting | 2025-11 | [src](https://indiplomacy.com/2025/12/02/temasek-and-thailand-discuss-strengthening-aseans-startup-ecosystem/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Thrive Capital advisory council: not supported.** Searches on his name with \"Thrive Capital\" returned nothing linking him to Thrive (searched 2026-11-09). I also found no public Thrive \"advisory council\" with Temasek members.\n- **Personal OpenAI or Anthropic investment: not supported.** Temasek as a firm holds Anthropic and OpenAI stakes, but no source names Mathur on either deal, or on River AI.\n- **Sterling Biotech directorship (2025-06-25, MarketScreener only):** unconfirmed. It may be linked to Perfect Day, but I found no source to verify that.\n- **Board seats are possibly stale:** the board list comes from a bio last modified in 2023. One search snippet claimed Soul Machines entered receivership in 2026, but I did not verify it.\n- **Missing details:** rounds and dates for Zipline, Improbable and Team8. TheOrg's longer title is also unconfirmed, and so is whether he still covers emerging tech.\n\n- *(2026-11-09)* What is the actual minimum direct cheque? He said only \"at least X amount\". Which tier-1 VC funds does his group back? Which UK funds are the \"eight or nine\"? Is the Web Summit Qatar 2026 panel real, and what did he say there? The Fast Company ME post could not be opened.\n\n## Sources\n- https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html (House of Lords oral evidence, 2025-06-10; API copy https://committees-api.parliament.uk/api/OralEvidence/16060/Document/Html)\n- https://sciencebusiness.net/news/international-news/uk-looks-abroad-improve-its-innovation-economy (2025-07-03)\n- https://indiplomacy.com/2025/12/02/temasek-and-thailand-discuss-strengthening-aseans-startup-ecosystem/\n- https://e27.co/temasek-dbs-team-up-to-launch-growth-debt-financing-platform-for-asias-tech-startups-20210802/ (search snippet only; 403)\n- https://qatar.websummit.com/attendees/qat26/70ba3d53-86d5-4867-91ae-0e985890d353/aftab-mathur/\n- https://x.com/fastcompanyme/status/2019296108057620604 (not opened; 402)\n- https://perfectday.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/PD-Bio-Mathur.pdf\n- https://theorg.com/org/temasek/org-chart/aftab-mathur\n- https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/AFTAB-MATHUR-A1TSLC/\n- https://www.universalassetowners.com/registry/person/aftab-mathur/\n- https://globalventuring.com/blog/2020/01/10/soul-machines-sources-40m/?format=md\n- https://vulcanpost.com/911732/temasek-gic-anthropic/\n- https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2024/03/05/singapore039s-temasek-in-talks-to-invest-in-openai-ft-reports\n- https://www.malaymail.com/news/money/2026/07/08/singapores-temasek-to-triple-ai-investments-by-2031-targets-15pc-exposure/226880\n- https://www.lw.com/en/news/2026/08/latham-advises-amd-in-river-ai-funding\n", "person:aidan-madigan-curtis": "\n# Aidan Madigan-Curtis\n\n> Research note (2026-11-09): WebSearch was unavailable for this run (shared search budget used up), so this profile is built only from fetches of URLs already known to the repo or linked from them. Coverage of X, LinkedIn, other podcasts and board seats is incomplete. Pronouns: she/her, as used by Eclipse's announcement post ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/welcome-aidan-madigan-curtis-and-jay-knafel)).\n\n## One-line take\nAn operator-turned-partner (Apple ops, Samsara employee #27) who co-formed Bedrock Robotics with its founders before it was incorporated. Bedrock went from formation in 2024 to a $4.1B Series C in October 2026, so she is Eclipse's clearest example of company creation in physical AI ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins.** She has scaled hardware manufacturing (Apple Watch) and sensor-data businesses for physical industries (Samsara) herself. Founders building heavy-industry autonomy want that experience before they incorporate. With Bedrock, Eclipse scoped the problem and go-to-market alongside the founders for months, so Eclipse was in before any cap table existed ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock)).\n- **Her stated rule** is that a fund pays back its LPs through outliers (\"not to hit a double or a triple\"), and she backs category winners in physical sectors because their moats are large ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n- **Constraint.** Her lane is the physical economy: construction, energy, logistics and manufacturing. Eclipse says explicitly that it does not invest in consumer or knowledge-work software ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/Made-in-Eclipse)). She talks about AI compute as an energy and grid problem, not as a model-lab bet.\n- **What she'd never do.** No direct source says. By inference from the firm's focus, she would not back pure-software or foundation-model labs.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Bedrock was co-formed before incorporation.** She and Ryan Gibson \"worked with\" Boris Sofman and Kevin Peterson for months before Bedrock was incorporated, helping choose the problem and shape go-to-market. Sofman connected with her directly, while Peterson's link to Eclipse ran through Seth Winterroth. The firm's quote: \"spent months with Boris and Kevin identifying the right problem\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock), 2026-04-30).\n- **Payoff rule for LPs.** She says the job is outliers: \"not to hit a double or a triple\" ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis), 2026-04-28).\n- **Core thesis:** \"those best companies will be created in the physical space\" ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n- **Why physical moats matter:** \"innovating in physical technology are just so large\" ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n- **Board composition.** She wants board members and investors \"who aren't gonna have their stomach turn at the first factory failure\" ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n- **AI compute as an energy problem.** She says AI demand \"radically outstrips our energy generation and transmission capabilities\". She also points to multi-year transformer lead times and to solar plus batteries co-located with data centers ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n- **Model efficiency.** She uses Claude daily, but argues \"we have to find a better way to do AI\" because models are not energy-efficient ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n- **Contrarian bets.** Good bets are found \"where you sound like a lunatic\" ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n- **Why she joined Eclipse.** She chose Eclipse over continuing as an operator: \"I just had fallen in love with the Eclipse team\" ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n- **Pony AI mention (ambiguous).** She says \"I had a company called Pony AI in Shenzhen\". The transcript does not make her role clear, so this is not recorded as a deal ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n\nAll of the above are mirrored in `data/rows/aidan-madigan-curtis-insights.csv`.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Two earlier lead rows disagree on her fit (eclipse-leads: medium, eclipse-ventures-leads: high). I'm settling on medium for these reasons:\n- **For:** Bedrock Robotics is AI-native automation of heavy machinery. She was in before incorporation, and it compounded to a $4.1B valuation in about two years ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/)). That matches the \"AI-native automation\" part of the thesis. Her views on energy and data centers are adjacent to the compute thesis. Her company-creation motion is a useful co-investor pattern for large formation-stage rounds.\n- **Against:** She does not back pre-training labs or AI chips. Eclipse's one frontier-compute win, Cerebras, was originated by Pierre Lamond and Lior Susan, not by her ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)). Her portfolio is physical industry.\n- **Deals that matter:** Bedrock Robotics. Vention and Fox Robotics are possibles, but only an aggregator places her on those boards.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** BA from Harvard and MBA from Stanford, per an aggregator ([src](https://startupintros.com/people/aidan-madigan-curtis)). She also names both schools on the Crux podcast ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n- **Bridgewater Associates:** investment analyst; dates not found ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/welcome-aidan-madigan-curtis-and-jay-knafel)).\n- **Apple:** Global Operations. She helped launch the first Apple Watch and the iPhone 6S and led Apple's most scaled manufacturing division. Dates not found ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/welcome-aidan-madigan-curtis-and-jay-knafel)).\n- **Samsara:** about five years, joining as employee #27. Her last role was VP/GM of Connected Sites ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/welcome-aidan-madigan-curtis-and-jay-knafel)). An aggregator says she managed 100+ people ([src](https://startupintros.com/people/aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n- **Eclipse:** Partner, announced 2021-08-04 alongside Jay Knafel ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/welcome-aidan-madigan-curtis-and-jay-knafel)). This resolves the earlier \"join date not found\".\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://eclipse.capital/team/ | Team page lists names and titles only; the individual page did not render a bio (2026-11-09) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Eclipse blog posts are credited to \"Eclipse\", not to her |\n| X | not found | Not searchable this run |\n| LinkedIn | not found | Not searchable this run |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only (see below) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-04 — Crux, Critical Capital (host Alfred Johnson) — \"Physical AI\" with Aidan Madigan-Curtis — https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis — Topics: outlier returns, building in the physical world, the US–China manufacturing gap, AI's energy demand versus grid build-out, solar plus batteries for data centers, and her path from Apple to Samsara to Eclipse.\n- A Sourcery podcast episode was surfaced earlier by the Eclipse firm research ([src](https://podbay.fm/p/sourcery/e/1789992954)). The page returned 403, so neither the guest nor the content is confirmed.\n\n## How they think\n- **Outliers only.** Venture pays back through grand slams, not doubles ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n- **Physical moats.** The best companies will be built in physical industries, because iterating on hardware creates moats software can't copy ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n- **Reindustrialisation.** She sees the pendulum swinging toward deglobalisation, and argues the US benefited from Chinese manufacturing labour while China kept compounding factory automation ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n- **AI's bottleneck is energy.** Demand outruns generation and transmission, with multi-year lead times on transformers. She favours solar plus batteries with smart controls co-located at data centers ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n- **Calls so far:** Samsara as an operator (it later went public); Bedrock Robotics as an investor (formed 2024, $4.1B by 2026-10).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Crux Critical Capital episode (2026-04-28): her own framing of the thesis, the energy views and her career path. https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis\n2. Eclipse, \"Breaking ground with Bedrock\" (2026-04-30): how the pre-incorporation co-build worked. https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock\n3. Eclipse, welcome post (2021-08-04): her background. https://eclipse.capital/blog/welcome-aidan-madigan-curtis-and-jay-knafel\n4. Nothing of her own writing found.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. Searching X and LinkedIn was not possible this run.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bedrock Robotics | Formation (pre-incorporation co-build, Eclipse Venture Equity) | 2024 | co-formed with Ryan Gibson | none (pre-cap-table) | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock) |\n| Bedrock Robotics | Launch round $80M | 2025-07-16 | lead not stated | Eclipse; 8VC | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/16/ex-waymo-engineers-launch-bedrock-robotics-with-80m-to-automate-construction/) |\n| Bedrock Robotics | $270M at ~$1.75B | 2026-02 | lead not named | not named | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock) |\n| Bedrock Robotics | Series C $600M at $4.1B | 2026-10-26 | no (CapitalG and Emergence led) | USIT, Bessemer, Temasek (new); 8VC, Georgian, Valor, Xora, Eclipse, Perry Creek, Nvidia (existing) | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/) |\n| Vention | board seat (aggregator only) | not found | not found | not found | [src](https://startupintros.com/people/aidan-madigan-curtis) |\n| Fox Robotics | board seat (aggregator only) | not found | not found | not found | [src](https://startupintros.com/people/aidan-madigan-curtis) |\n\nThe aggregator says she holds 8 board seats but names only these two.\n\n## Deal stories\n**Bedrock Robotics (autonomous heavy machinery, starting with construction)**\n- **Origin.** Boris Sofman connected with Madigan-Curtis. Kevin Peterson had worked with Seth Winterroth before. She and Ryan Gibson then spent months with the two before incorporation, choosing the problem, shaping go-to-market and speeding up execution. Eclipse credits her Apple and Samsara industrial operations experience; Gibson had co-founded a construction-tech company ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock)).\n- **Founding team.** Boris Sofman (CEO; ex-Waymo trucks, co-founder of Anki), Kevin Peterson (CTO, ex-Waymo), Ajay Gummalla (ex-Waymo) and Tom Eliaz (ex-Segment and Twilio) ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/16/ex-waymo-engineers-launch-bedrock-robotics-with-80m-to-automate-construction/)).\n- **Timing: who was in first, and who followed.**\n  - Eclipse was in first, at formation in 2024, before incorporation.\n  - Prototype within six months ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock)).\n  - 8VC is the only other investor named in the $80M launch on 2025-07-16, roughly a year after formation ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/16/ex-waymo-engineers-launch-bedrock-robotics-with-80m-to-automate-construction/)).\n  - $270M at ~$1.75B in 2026-02 ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock)).\n  - Series C on 2026-10-26: $600M at $4.1B, led by CapitalG and Emergence. USIT, Bessemer and Temasek were new. Georgian, Valor, Xora, Perry Creek and Nvidia were existing investors by then, but the round each entered is not sourced ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/)).\n  - The three rounds add up to about $950M, consistent with TechCrunch's \"nearly $1 billion\" raised since 2024.\n  - Eclipse kept participating through the Series C.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Eclipse | Partner | 2021-08-04 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/welcome-aidan-madigan-curtis-and-jay-knafel) |\n| Boris Sofman | Co-formed Bedrock; Sofman connected with her directly | 2024 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock) |\n| Kevin Peterson | Co-formed Bedrock (his intro came via Seth Winterroth) | 2024 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock) |\n| Ryan Gibson | Eclipse co-builder on Bedrock | 2024 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock) |\n| Seth Winterroth | Eclipse partner; prior relationship with Peterson | 2024 (Bedrock) | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock) |\n| Jay Knafel | Announced as an Eclipse partner on the same day | 2021-08-04 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/welcome-aidan-madigan-curtis-and-jay-knafel) |\n| Samsara | Employee #27, later VP/GM Connected Sites (~5 years) | before 2021 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/welcome-aidan-madigan-curtis-and-jay-knafel) |\n| Apple | Global Operations (Apple Watch, iPhone 6S) | before Samsara | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/welcome-aidan-madigan-curtis-and-jay-knafel) |\n| Bridgewater Associates | Investment analyst | before Apple | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/welcome-aidan-madigan-curtis-and-jay-knafel) |\n| Vention; Fox Robotics | Board member (aggregator only, low confidence) | not found | [src](https://startupintros.com/people/aidan-madigan-curtis) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which other boards does she sit on? The aggregator claims 8 but names only Vention and Fox Robotics. When did Eclipse enter each?\n- What was her role with Pony AI (\"I had a company called Pony AI in Shenzhen\")? Was she an operator, an investor, or just speaking loosely?\n- Who led Bedrock's $80M launch and its $270M round? When did Georgian, Valor, Xora, Perry Creek and Nvidia come in?\n- Does she have an X or LinkedIn presence, and what does she post? Is the Sourcery podcast episode hers?\n- How much does Eclipse put into a formation-stage co-build, and what ownership does it take?\n- Is she working on other Venture Equity formations, beyond Bedrock?\n\n## Sources\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/welcome-aidan-madigan-curtis-and-jay-knafel\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock\n- https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/16/ex-waymo-engineers-launch-bedrock-robotics-with-80m-to-automate-construction/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/\n- https://bedrockrobotics.com/news\n- https://startupintros.com/people/aidan-madigan-curtis (aggregator; secondary; contradicts itself on location)\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/Made-in-Eclipse (firm scope)\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era (Cerebras origin, not her deal)\n- https://eclipse.capital/team/\n", "person:albert-lee": "\n# Albert Lee\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (strategics sweep 2b). No earlier profile existed. Rows: `data/rows/albert-lee-{deals,rels,insights,leads}.csv`. The deals file is deliberately header-only: no source names him personally on any deal.*\n\n**Identity check (common name).** Reuters ties the OpenAI hire to \"senior director of corporate development at Google\" who \"is also on the advisory board of Alphabet's independent growth fund, CapitalG\" ([The Star/Reuters 2025-12-15](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/16/openai-hires-veteran-google-executive-as-corporate-development-vp)). CapitalG's own bio of its advisory-board member Albert Lee gives the same title, Google since 2011 ([CapitalG 2025 board](https://capitalg.com/insights/introducing-capitalgs-2025-advisory-board/), [archived CapitalG profile 2025-04-29](https://web.archive.org/web/20250429231932/https://capitalg.com/team/albert-lee)). Both sources describe the same person. He is not Albert Wang (Qualcomm Ventures) or Albert Kim (Samsung) in this repo, and not the guitarist or the Under Armour executive who show up in search.\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the seat that prices OpenAI's balance-sheet stakes and acquisitions. For the owner he is an exit counterparty and a later-round strategic, not a seed co-investor.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** about 15 years running Google's infrastructure M&A (Cloud, YouTube and later DeepMind) and a direct line to the CFO. OpenAI's stated reason for hiring him was a senior leader \"empowered to move quickly\" with visibility across the company ([Reuters via The Star](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/16/openai-hires-veteran-google-executive-as-corporate-development-vp)).\n- **Constraint:** he does not own the most visible deals. Fidji Simo wrote the TBPN memo ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/03/chasing-vibes-openai-ma-strategy-gets-more-confusing-with-tbpn-.html)). Deals that touch Altman (Red Queen Bio, Merge Labs) need sign-off from the compliance officer and unconflicted board members (see [firms/openai-startup-fund.md](../firms/openai-startup-fund.md)). The seed fund is a separate legal entity run by Ian Hathaway.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred from his Google record and the in-tenure deals):** write a small speculative venture check for return alone. His Google remit was matching acquisitions and strategic investments to business priorities, \"to accelerate Google's businesses\" ([archived CapitalG](https://web.archive.org/web/20250429231932/https://capitalg.com/team/albert-lee)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Most of the headline deals predate him.** He started on 2025-12-16 (\"Lee will start his role ... on Tuesday\", [Reuters](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/16/openai-hires-veteran-google-executive-as-corporate-development-vp)). io (2025-05), CoreWeave (2025-03), Statsig (2025-09), the AMD warrant (2025-10), Red Queen Bio's seed (2025-11-13) and Neptune (2025-12-04) were all done before that date. Only Merge Labs (2026-01-15), Torch (2026-01), Promptfoo (2026-03), Astral (2026-03-19) and TBPN (2026-04-02) fall in his tenure. Even for those, CNBC only frames his hire as \"a sign that the company was on the hunt for more targets\" ([CNBC 2026-04-03](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/03/chasing-vibes-openai-ma-strategy-gets-more-confusing-with-tbpn-.html)). **No source names him on any deal.**\n- **His track record grew between two versions of his bio.** In April 2025, CapitalG said \"70+ deals (M&A & strategic investments) with $20B+ deal value\", covering Cloud and YouTube ([archived CapitalG](https://web.archive.org/web/20250429231932/https://capitalg.com/team/albert-lee)). In December 2025, Reuters, citing his LinkedIn, said \"over 60 transactions spanning acquisitions and strategic investments\" worth more than $50B, covering Cloud and DeepMind ([Reuters via The Star](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/16/openai-hires-veteran-google-executive-as-corporate-development-vp)). The jump in value fits Wiz ($32B, announced 2025-03), which CNBC credits him with working on ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/15/openai-poaches-google-executive-to-lead-corporate-development.html)). The move from YouTube to DeepMind suggests his remit shifted during 2025. **Unconfirmed:** I could not see LinkedIn, and the deal count going down (70 to 60) is not explained.\n- **His Google deals were developer and enterprise infrastructure:** \"including the acquisitions of Looker, Apigee, Mandiant, Firebase, and Nest\" ([archived CapitalG](https://web.archive.org/web/20250429231932/https://capitalg.com/team/albert-lee)). The in-tenure OpenAI buys follow the same pattern: Astral is developer tooling for Codex, and Promptfoo is AI security and evals ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/03/19/openai-to-acquire-developer-tooling-startup-astral.html)).\n- **He is not listed on CapitalG's 2026 advisory board** (page published 2026-03-03). He was on the 2025 board (published 2025-03-17) ([2025](https://capitalg.com/insights/introducing-capitalgs-2025-advisory-board/), [2026](https://capitalg.com/insights/Introducing-the-CapitalG-2026-Advisory-Board)). He was not on the 2022, 2023 or 2024 boards either (checked each page).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He does not back new labs or write $20M seed checks. He matters at two points. (1) **Exits:** he is the buyer's seat for AI infrastructure and tooling companies (Astral, Promptfoo, Neptune-type deals). (2) **Strategic rounds:** OpenAI corporate wrote the largest check in Merge Labs' $250M seed and led Red Queen Bio's seed. Both are close to Altman and predate or sit beside his remit. For a new lab, OpenAI corporate is a competitor, not a backer.\n\n## Background\n- Undergraduate degrees in computer science and business administration from UC Berkeley; MBA from Wharton ([archived CapitalG](https://web.archive.org/web/20250429231932/https://capitalg.com/team/albert-lee)). Graduation years not given.\n- In bio order: investment banking at Lehman Brothers, private equity at Francisco Partners, then corporate development at Oracle and Cisco. Dates and order not given beyond \"prior to Google\" ([same](https://web.archive.org/web/20250429231932/https://capitalg.com/team/albert-lee)).\n- Google, 2011 to 2025-12. Senior Director, Corporate Development. He led M&A and strategic investment for Google Cloud and YouTube and oversaw the Strategy & Scouting team (CapitalG, 2025). By December 2025 his remit was Cloud and DeepMind (Reuters). He worked on Wiz ($32B) ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/15/openai-poaches-google-executive-to-lead-corporate-development.html)).\n- CapitalG advisory board, 2025 ([CapitalG](https://capitalg.com/insights/introducing-capitalgs-2025-advisory-board/)).\n- OpenAI, VP of Corporate Development, from 2025-12-16. Reports to CFO Sarah Friar. The Information broke the move on 2025-12-15 ([MarketScreener/Reuters](https://www.marketscreener.com/news/openai-hires-veteran-google-executive-as-corporate-development-vp-ce7d50d9dc8df422), [The Information](https://www.theinformation.com/briefings/exclusive-openai-hires-executive-google-lead-m)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | No personal site found. The CapitalG profile (capitalg.com/team/albert-lee) now redirects to the generic team page; archived copy [here](https://web.archive.org/web/20250429231932/https://capitalg.com/team/albert-lee) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No verified handle tied to him in any source |\n| LinkedIn | unconfirmed | Reuters and Gizmodo cite \"his LinkedIn profile\" but give no URL; I did not guess one |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\nPhoto: none. The archived CapitalG profile loads its headshot by JavaScript, so the raw HTML has an `<img alt=\"Albert Lee\">` with no `src`, and the live page no longer exists. OpenAI has no leadership page that shows him. No photo row was written.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Searches for his name with Google corp dev, Looker, Mandiant, Apigee, \"speaker\", \"panel\" and \"interview\" returned no appearances (2026-11-10).\n\n## How they think\n- The only thinking on record is his CapitalG bio: he \"partners with Google's senior leaders to understand business priorities\" and \"maintains a pulse on the external ecosystem\" to choose acquisitions ([archived CapitalG](https://web.archive.org/web/20250429231932/https://capitalg.com/team/albert-lee)). That is buy-side corp dev driven by product priorities, not venture.\n- Contrarian views: nothing found.\n- Calls right or wrong: Wiz ($32B) and Mandiant are the marquee Google deals credited to him. Their outcomes after close are not assessed here.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Reuters via The Star, 2025-12-15](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/16/openai-hires-veteran-google-executive-as-corporate-development-vp): the hire, the reporting line, the LinkedIn deal figures and the CapitalG tie.\n2. [Archived CapitalG profile, 2025-04](https://web.archive.org/web/20250429231932/https://capitalg.com/team/albert-lee): the full career path, education and the Google deal list.\n3. [CNBC on TBPN, 2026-04-03](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/03/chasing-vibes-openai-ma-strategy-gets-more-confusing-with-tbpn-.html): OpenAI's in-tenure M&A pattern and who wrote the memos.\n4. [firms/openai-startup-fund.md](../firms/openai-startup-fund.md): how his corporate pool differs from Hathaway's fund.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public posting presence.\n\n## Portfolio\nNo deal names him personally. The table lists OpenAI corporate deals by timing against his 2025-12-16 start, so it is attribution by role only. The deal rows sit under `openai` in `data/rows/openai-startup-fund-deals.csv`.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **In tenure (role-attributed only)** | | | | | |\n| Merge Labs | Seed ($250M at $850M); OpenAI wrote the largest check | 2026-01-15 | lead not stated | Bain Capital; Interface Fund; Fifty Years; Gabe Newell | [TechCrunch](https://www.techcrunch.com/2026/01/15/openai-invests-in-sam-altmans-brain-computer-interface-startup-merge-labs/) |\n| Torch Health | Acquisition (~$100M equity, reported) | 2026-01-12 | n/a | — | [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=727478) |\n| Promptfoo | Acquisition (undisclosed) | 2026-03-09 | n/a | — | [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/03/chasing-vibes-openai-ma-strategy-gets-more-confusing-with-tbpn-.html) |\n| Astral | Acquisition (undisclosed) | 2026-03-19 | n/a | — | [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/03/19/openai-to-acquire-developer-tooling-startup-astral.html) |\n| TBPN | Acquisition (undisclosed; memo by Fidji Simo) | 2026-04-02 | n/a | — | [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/03/chasing-vibes-openai-ma-strategy-gets-more-confusing-with-tbpn-.html) |\n| **Before he joined (not his)** | | | | | |\n| CoreWeave | Strategic ($350M shares) | 2025-03-10 | — | — | [Reuters via Yahoo](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/exclusive-coreweave-strikes-12-billion-182359450.html) |\n| io Products | Acquisition (~$6.4B-$6.5B) | 2025-05-21 | n/a | — | [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/03/chasing-vibes-openai-ma-strategy-gets-more-confusing-with-tbpn-.html) |\n| Statsig | Acquisition ($1.1B) | 2025-09 | n/a | — | [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/15/openai-poaches-google-executive-to-lead-corporate-development.html) |\n| AMD | Strategic (warrant for up to 160M shares) | 2025-10-06 | — | — | [Tom's Hardware](https://www.tomshardware.com/pc-components/gpus/openai-signs-6gw-amd-gpu-deal) |\n| Red Queen Bio | Seed ($15M, lead) | 2025-11-13 | yes | Cerberus Ventures; Fifty Years; Halcyon Futures | [Reuters via The Star](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/11/14/openai-backs-startup-aiming-to-block-ai-enabled-bioweapons) |\n| Neptune.ai | Acquisition (all-stock) | 2025-12-04 | n/a | — | [The Star/Reuters](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/04/openai-agrees-to-acquire-ai-startup-neptune-to-boost-model-training-capabilities) |\n\nAt Google (bio-listed, his role on each not specified): Looker, Apigee, Mandiant, Firebase, Nest ([archived CapitalG](https://web.archive.org/web/20250429231932/https://capitalg.com/team/albert-lee)). Wiz ($32B, announced 2025-03) is credited to him by press ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/15/openai-poaches-google-executive-to-lead-corporate-development.html)).\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. No source describes how any OpenAI deal was sourced or negotiated with his involvement. What can be said about sourcing comes from his Google role description: the Strategy & Scouting team plus partnering with business-unit leaders on their priorities. At OpenAI, product leaders (Simo for TBPN; the Codex team for Astral) appear to initiate deals, with corp dev executing. That last point is an inference, not sourced.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | VP Corporate Development (current) | 2025-12-16 | [Reuters](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/16/openai-hires-veteran-google-executive-as-corporate-development-vp) |\n| Sarah Friar | Reports to (CFO) | 2025-12 | [Reuters](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/16/openai-hires-veteran-google-executive-as-corporate-development-vp) |\n| Torben Severson | Peer: OpenAI VP of global business development (ex-Amazon), hired 2025-11 | 2025-11 | [Reuters](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/16/openai-hires-veteran-google-executive-as-corporate-development-vp) |\n| Ian Hathaway | Counterpart: runs the separate OpenAI Startup Fund | 2025-12 | [firms/openai-startup-fund.md](../firms/openai-startup-fund.md) |\n| Google | Senior Director, Corp Dev (former) | 2011 to 2025-12 | [archived CapitalG](https://web.archive.org/web/20250429231932/https://capitalg.com/team/albert-lee) |\n| CapitalG | Advisory board (not on 2026 board) | 2025-03 | [CapitalG](https://capitalg.com/insights/introducing-capitalgs-2025-advisory-board/) |\n| Cisco, Oracle, Francisco Partners, Lehman Brothers | Former employers, before 2011 | undated | [archived CapitalG](https://web.archive.org/web/20250429231932/https://capitalg.com/team/albert-lee) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which in-tenure deals he personally ran, and whether he approves the Startup Fund's Fund II deals or sits apart from them (Fund II's sole LP is reportedly OpenAI).\n- What the 70+/$20B (CapitalG) vs 60+/$50B (LinkedIn via Reuters) figures each count. LinkedIn was not accessible.\n- OpenAI M&A from 2026-05 to 2026-11 was not swept here. Check for later deals that name him.\n- Who replaced him at Google Cloud/DeepMind corp dev.\n- Dates for his roles at Lehman, Francisco Partners, Oracle and Cisco.\n\n## Sources\n- [Reuters via The Star, 2025-12-15](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/16/openai-hires-veteran-google-executive-as-corporate-development-vp)\n- [MarketScreener/Reuters](https://www.marketscreener.com/news/openai-hires-veteran-google-executive-as-corporate-development-vp-ce7d50d9dc8df422)\n- [CNBC 2025-12-15](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/15/openai-poaches-google-executive-to-lead-corporate-development.html)\n- [Gizmodo 2025-12-16](https://gizmodo.com/is-openai-entering-its-acquisition-era-2000699888)\n- [The Information briefing](https://www.theinformation.com/briefings/exclusive-openai-hires-executive-google-lead-m) (paywalled; headline only)\n- [CapitalG 2025 advisory board](https://capitalg.com/insights/introducing-capitalgs-2025-advisory-board/), [CapitalG 2026 advisory board](https://capitalg.com/insights/Introducing-the-CapitalG-2026-Advisory-Board), [archived CapitalG profile](https://web.archive.org/web/20250429231932/https://capitalg.com/team/albert-lee)\n- [CNBC TBPN 2026-04-03](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/03/chasing-vibes-openai-ma-strategy-gets-more-confusing-with-tbpn-.html), [CNBC Astral 2026-03-19](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/03/19/openai-to-acquire-developer-tooling-startup-astral.html)\n", "person:alex-clavel": "\n# Alex Clavel\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the person who signs off on Vision Fund 2's AI stakes, but every source describes a manager and fixer of positions SoftBank already holds, so he matters to me as the approver behind a later-round price-setter, not as someone who finds companies.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** trust. He spent 19 years as a Morgan Stanley telecom banker, advised SoftBank on Sprint, joined in 2015 and then cleaned up the hard positions (WeWork, OneWeb, the T-Mobile stake, the Arm IPO). Rajeev Misra says he \"groomed Clavel as his successor and recommended him to Son\" ([Reuters via Yahoo, 2024-01-11](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html)). His old manager Paul Taubman says he is someone \"never trying to take your job\" (same source).\n- **What constrains him:** Masayoshi Son decides the large bets and the parent's Investment Committee has no Vision Fund investor on it (see [firms/softbank-group.md](../firms/softbank-group.md)). Clavel's fund is now mostly one position: OpenAI, marked at $79.6B at March 2026 ([FY2025 message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/svf)). In September 2025 nearly 20% of his staff were cut so the rest could work on Son's projects ([Reuters, syndicated](https://id.tradingview.com/news/reuters.com,2025:newsml_L6N3V50WU:0-softbank-vision-fund-to-lay-off-20-of-employees-in-shift-to-bold-ai-bets-source-and-memo-say)).\n- **What he would not do:** source a seed round. Reuters wrote that after taking over Marcelo Claure's portfolio in 2022 he \"would still stick with managing investments rather than making new ones\". The only seed-stage move he describes is SoftBank founding its own subsidiary (Stack AV).\n\n## What he signs, sources and prices\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Which pools | Vision Fund 1 through SB Investment Advisers (harvest: exits and distributions). Vision Fund 2 and the LatAm funds through SB Global Advisers (parent money plus Son's co-investment; no outside LPs). In 2024 Reuters also gave him oversight of about $35B on the parent's balance sheet; whether that still holds after Rene Haas took SoftBank Group International on 2026-04-21 is unconfirmed | [Reuters](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html); [SBG 2026-04-21](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260421) |\n| What he signs | The Vision Funds' annual message (FY2024 and FY2025) and internal memos (the 2026 CFO departure). Son put him above the Vision Fund 2 executive committee in July 2023 so that new investments are decided faster. The current investment committee membership is not disclosed | [FY2025 message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/svf); [Reuters via Zawya, 2026-06-16](https://www.zawya.com/en/business/investment/softbank-vision-fund-cfo-is-leaving-company-memo-shows-kv4l71pd) |\n| What he sources | Nothing found. Reuters: \"Even though he was not sourcing deals\", other partners used him for advice. No Vision Fund portfolio page lists him on a deal team ([sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md](../sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md)) | [Reuters](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html) |\n| What he prices | The fund leads or co-leads priced rounds two or more rounds after seed. The one pricing call tied to his tenure is a pass: Vision Fund 2 skipped OpenAI and Anthropic \"due to concerns over frothy valuations\" (reported 2024-01), then first bought OpenAI in September 2024. Reuters does not say the pass was his personal call | [Reuters](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html); [FY2024 message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2025/message/svf) |\n| Check size | No band published. He says \"big and small\". Sourced range of rounds under him: $65M (Tractable) to $30B (OpenAI 2026) | [GCV Powerlist 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-alex-clavel/?format=md) |\n| Strategic tie | He presents OpenAI as a stake plus a Japan joint venture in which SoftBank sells OpenAI to Japanese companies, and says the fund benefits from \"the SBG platform\" (Arm, Ampere, ABB robotics, DigitalBridge). No contract terms are quoted by him | [GCV 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-alex-clavel/?format=md); [FY2025 message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/svf) |\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He was Misra's choice, and became co-CEO in July 2023.** Misra \"groomed Clavel as his successor and recommended him to Son\" ([Reuters](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html)). GCV dates the title: \"promoted to co-CEO of SoftBank Investment Advisers last July\" ([GCV Powerlist 2024, published 2024-06-24](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-alex-clavel-rajeev-misra/?format=md)). That is the same month Son put him above the executive committee, and it settles a date the firm page listed as open.\n- **The pass on OpenAI and Anthropic happened on his watch.** Reuters reported in January 2024 that the fund had passed \"due to concerns over frothy valuations in the sector\". Eight months later: \"in September 2024, we made our first investment in OpenAI\" ([FY2024 message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2025/message/svf)). The reversal came with Son's turn to OpenAI, not from a change in the fund's own process that any source describes.\n- **His version of a seed is a subsidiary.** If the fund cannot find deals, \"the firm will seed its own, as it did with trucking venture Stack AV\", built with Bryan Salesky. In his words: \"When there isn't a market, then we create our own magic\" ([Reuters](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html)).\n- **Where he says the fund looks.** Large language models \"such as OpenAI\", industries AI can disrupt, and \"software companies that use AI in different ways to build a moat\" ([GCV 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-alex-clavel/?format=md)). New labs at seed and chips are absent from his list; chips sit with the parent (Arm, Ampere).\n- **The venture pace fell as OpenAI grew.** \"We made 27 new investments in AI companies during fiscal 2024.\" ([FY2024 message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2025/message/svf)). The FY2025 message names four new companies (Cast AI, Sierra, Emergent, Classiq) against \"over $37 billion\" invested, nearly all OpenAI ([FY2025 message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/svf)). GCV then counts \"more than a dozen deals so far this year\" by June 2026 ([GCV Powerlist 2026](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-alex-clavel-softbank-investment-advisers/?format=md)).\n- **His team now serves Son's projects.** After the September 2025 cuts, \"remaining staff will dedicate more resources to Son’s ambitious AI initiatives\" such as Stargate ([Reuters, syndicated](https://id.tradingview.com/news/reuters.com,2025:newsml_L6N3V50WU:0-softbank-vision-fund-to-lay-off-20-of-employees-in-shift-to-bold-ai-bets-source-and-memo-say)).\n- **His pre-fund book was the parent's balance sheet.** In 2022 he ran \"investments outside of the Vision Funds, including T-Mobile, Deutsche Telecom, WeWork and OneWeb\" and sat on the boards of Arm China, Boston Dynamics, InMobi, SB Energy, Goggo and Levere Holdings ([SBG 2022-06-22](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20220622)). Those are operating companies and a SPAC, not venture boards.\n- **How he describes his own temperament.** From banking: \"You didn't want to surprise your boss, even on the upside.\" And on the house style: \"We show up with the calculator, but we’re also there with our convictions\" ([Vision Fund team page Q&A, about 2022](https://visionfund.com/team/alex-clavel)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** He is not a co-investor for a $20M check into a frontier-AI seed, and not an LP. He runs a fund that enters at Series B or later and sets or matches the price there (Wayve Series C and D, Emergent Series B, Cast AI Series C), and whose balance is dominated by OpenAI.\n- **What he is for me:** the approver behind a later-round price-setter. If one of my seed companies reaches a $70M-plus priced round, a Vision Fund 2 term sheet would go through his committee.\n- **Who to know instead:** the managing partners whose names appear on the AI deal pages. Brett Rochkind (Perplexity, Glean, Runway, Sierra), Sumer Juneja (Emergent, Cast AI, poolside), Kentaro Matsui (Wayve; he also sits in the parent's New Business Office) and Vikas Parekh (OpenAI, SambaNova) ([sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md](../sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md)). For anything above a few billion dollars the decision is Son's.\n- **How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** no public channel was found. He has no X account that I could verify, no podcast appearances, and the Vision Fund site offers only a general contact route. The sourced warm paths are former colleagues: Rajeev Misra (One Investment Management), Paul Taubman (PJT Partners), Michel Combes and Marcelo Claure. He gives short interviews to Global Corporate Venturing each year and spoke to Reuters once.\n\n## Background\n- Princeton University, degree in East Asian Studies. Speaks Mandarin, French and conversational Japanese ([team page](https://visionfund.com/team/alex-clavel)). Aged 49 in January 2024 ([Reuters](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html)).\n- **About 1996 to 2015: Morgan Stanley**, technology, media and telecom M&A in New York, Hong Kong, Shanghai and Tokyo. \"19 years\" per SoftBank; in Hong Kong by 1996 per Reuters. In Tokyo he helped win SoftBank as a client and advised on the Sprint acquisition.\n- **2015: joined SoftBank Group in Tokyo**, then the Silicon Valley office, then New York from 2018 ([SBG 2022-06-22](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20220622)).\n- **About 2020 to 2022: Managing Partner, SoftBank Group International**, working with Michel Combes, who called him \"my partner for the past two years\" (same release).\n- **2022-06-30: CEO of SoftBank Group International.** 2022-08-09: joined the WeWork board as SoftBank's designee ([WeWork](https://www.wework.com/newsroom/wework-announces-new-softbank-appointment-to-its-board-of-directors)).\n- **2022-10: Vision Fund 2 executive committee**, overseeing US and Latin America investing ([Bloomberg Línea](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/english/softbank-appoints-committee-to-oversee-its-vision-funds-including-those-in-latam/); [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/softbank-shakes-up-vc-structure/?format=md)).\n- **2023-07: co-CEO of SB Investment Advisers** and placed above the executive committee ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-alex-clavel-rajeev-misra/?format=md); Reuters).\n- **2024-11: sole CEO** of SBIA and SBGA when Misra left ([GCV 2024-11-12](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/asia/misra-softbank-vision-fund-departure/?format=md)).\n- **2026-04-21: Rene Haas named CEO of SoftBank Group International** ([SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260421)). The release does not mention Clavel; when he left that role is unconfirmed.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [visionfund.com/team/alex-clavel](https://visionfund.com/team/alex-clavel) | Bio and a short Q&A; fetched 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | [Vision Funds annual message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/svf) | Once a year in the SoftBank Group Report; the FY2024 and FY2025 messages are his |\n| X | none found | Not verified; no account located |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | Not fetched; no URL recorded |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- No podcast appearance found.\n- 2024-01 — Reuters profile interview — [syndicated copy](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html) — the \"create our own magic\" line and the Stack AV example.\n- 2024-06, 2025-06, 2026-06 — Global Corporate Venturing Powerlist entries with short quotes — [2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-alex-clavel-rajeev-misra/?format=md), [2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-alex-clavel/?format=md), [2026](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-alex-clavel-softbank-investment-advisers/?format=md) — 2025 is the most useful: where the fund looks and the DoorDash exit numbers.\n- 2026-06 — Forbes Iconoclast Summit, New York — unconfirmed. A search snippet says he named four themes (AI models, AI chips, physical AI, AI infrastructure); the page behind it could not be fetched and no Forbes link was found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Stated strategy:** cover every layer of AI, sell late-stage holdings while markets are good, back the leader in each category ([FY2025 message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/svf)).\n- **Discipline as the selling point:** the FY2024 message stresses runway and cash flow across the portfolio and distributions to LPs ($11.4B that year) ([FY2024 message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2025/message/svf)). He counts the DoorDash exit as the best asset: \"almost 12 times money-on-money return\" ([GCV 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-alex-clavel/?format=md)).\n- **Build when you cannot buy:** subsidiaries, joint ventures or lending when deal flow is thin (Reuters).\n- **Call that went wrong:** the fund passed on OpenAI and Anthropic on price by January 2024 and then bought OpenAI from September 2024.\n- **Call that went right:** the turnarounds and exits (Arm IPO at $4.87B, the $7.6B T-Mobile gain, DoorDash, Wiz).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Reuters profile, 2024-01-11](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html): the only long piece; 12 colleagues interviewed.\n2. [FY2025 Vision Funds message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/svf): the current strategy in his words.\n3. [FY2024 Vision Funds message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2025/message/svf): 27 AI investments, the first OpenAI check, the exits.\n4. [GCV Powerlist 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-alex-clavel/?format=md): where the fund looks next.\n5. [SoftBank release, 2022-06-22](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20220622): what he managed before the Vision Funds.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No verified X or other public account.\n\n## Portfolio\n*\"Named how\" matters here: he is not listed on any deal team. Rows marked \"fund\" are Vision Fund 2 investments made while he approved; the partner of record is someone else.*\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Named how | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Tractable (AI) | Series E ($65M) | 2023-07-18 | Fund led | Fund; Reuters cites it as a small check \"under Clavel\" | [Reuters](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html) |\n| Cato Networks | Growth (round above $200M) | 2023 (unconfirmed) | Not stated | Fund; same Reuters line | [GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-alex-clavel-rajeev-misra/?format=md) |\n| Stack AV (AI) | Seed (SoftBank-created subsidiary) | Not in source | Yes | Personal: his own example in the Reuters interview; parent side | [Reuters](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html) |\n| OpenAI (AI) | Growth (first check 2024-09; $40B round 2025; $30B 2026) | 2024-09 onward | Yes from 2025 | Fund; he calls it \"Our most exciting new investment\". Terms were delegated to Son and Goto, not to him | [GCV 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-alex-clavel/?format=md); [firms/softbank-group.md](../firms/softbank-group.md) |\n| Sierra, Cast AI, Emergent, Classiq (AI) | Strategic; Series C; Series B; Series C extension | 2025-04 to 2026-01 | Co-led Cast AI and Emergent | Fund; he names them as new investments in the FY2025 message | [FY2025 message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/svf) |\n| Wayve (AI) | Series C ($1.05B); Series D ($1.2B) | 2024-05; 2026-02 | Lead; co-lead | Fund; GCV lists it among his unit's deals. Kentaro Matsui is the quoted partner | [GCV 2026](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-alex-clavel-softbank-investment-advisers/?format=md) |\n| WeWork | Debt (restructuring) | Board from 2022-08-09 | No | Personal: board designee; worked on the turnaround | [WeWork](https://www.wework.com/newsroom/wework-announces-new-softbank-appointment-to-its-board-of-directors) |\n| OneWeb | Strategic (turnaround; Eutelsat combination) | Not in source | No | Personal: managed the position | [Reuters](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html) |\n| Arm | IPO ($4.87B) | 2023-09 | Seller | Personal: \"With Clavel's help\" | [Reuters](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html) |\n| T-Mobile | Secondary ($7.6B gain on the stake) | 2023 | Seller | Personal: managed the stake | [Reuters](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **How SoftBank hired him.** He ran Morgan Stanley's Tokyo coverage, took Japanese lessons in the office in the mornings, and advised SoftBank on Sprint. SoftBank hired him in 2015 (Reuters).\n- **Stack AV.** His example of SoftBank creating its own deal: a subsidiary built with Bryan Salesky when the market was not producing enough companies to back. No amount or date is in the source.\n- **OpenAI.** He is the public voice for the position but not the negotiator. The parent signed the 2025 deal, moved the right to invest into Vision Fund 2, and delegated the 2026 terms to CFO Yoshimitsu Goto ([firms/softbank-group.md](../firms/softbank-group.md)). His fund holds and finances the shares, including the August 2026 loan against them.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Masayoshi Son | Reports to him; Son made him CEO of SBGI (2022) and put him above the executive committee (2023-07) | 2015 | [SBG 2022](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20220622) |\n| Rajeev Misra | Predecessor and sponsor; co-CEOs 2023-07 to 2024-11 | 2015 | [Reuters](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html) |\n| Michel Combes | Worked under him at SBGI about 2020 to 2022; succeeded him there and on the WeWork board | ~2020 | [SBG 2022](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20220622) |\n| Marcelo Claure | Took over much of his portfolio in 2022 | 2022 | Reuters |\n| Greg Moon | Fellow member of the 2022 executive committee | 2022-10 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/softbank-shakes-up-vc-structure/?format=md) |\n| Navneet Govil | CFO; Clavel wrote the 2026 memo announcing his exit | 2022-10 | [Reuters via Zawya](https://www.zawya.com/en/business/investment/softbank-vision-fund-cfo-is-leaving-company-memo-shows-kv4l71pd) |\n| Paul Taubman (PJT Partners) | Former manager at Morgan Stanley | 1990s | Reuters |\n| Bryan Salesky | Co-built Stack AV | not dated | Reuters |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who sits on the Vision Fund investment committee today, and what size of check needs Son.\n- Whether he still oversees parent-side holdings now that Rene Haas runs SoftBank Group International, and when he left that CEO role.\n- Whether the pass on OpenAI and Anthropic was his decision, the committee's or Son's.\n- His WeWork board end date (2024 from an aggregator only) and any current board seats.\n- The Forbes Iconoclast Summit remarks (June 2026): no primary link found.\n- The Reuters pieces were read through syndicated copies. EDGAR full-text search was blocked, so no SEC filing naming him was checked.\n\n## Sources\n- Reuters profile, 2024-01-11, via Yahoo: https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html\n- SoftBank Group Report 2026, Vision Funds message: https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/svf\n- SoftBank Group Report 2025, Vision Funds message: https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2025/message/svf\n- SoftBank release, 2022-06-22 (CEO of SBGI): https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20220622\n- SoftBank release, 2026-04-21 (Haas to SBGI): https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260421\n- Vision Fund team page: https://visionfund.com/team/alex-clavel\n- GCV Powerlist 2024: https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-alex-clavel-rajeev-misra/?format=md\n- GCV Powerlist 2025: https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-alex-clavel/?format=md\n- GCV Powerlist 2026: https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-alex-clavel-softbank-investment-advisers/?format=md\n- GCV, 2022-10-21 (executive committee): https://globalventuring.com/corporate/softbank-shakes-up-vc-structure/?format=md\n- GCV, 2024-11-12 (Misra exit): https://globalventuring.com/corporate/asia/misra-softbank-vision-fund-departure/?format=md\n- Bloomberg Línea, 2022-10-20: https://www.bloomberglinea.com/english/softbank-appoints-committee-to-oversee-its-vision-funds-including-those-in-latam/\n- Reuters, 2025-09 (layoffs), syndicated: https://id.tradingview.com/news/reuters.com,2025:newsml_L6N3V50WU:0-softbank-vision-fund-to-lay-off-20-of-employees-in-shift-to-bold-ai-bets-source-and-memo-say\n- Reuters, 2026-06-16 (Govil memo), via Zawya: https://www.zawya.com/en/business/investment/softbank-vision-fund-cfo-is-leaving-company-memo-shows-kv4l71pd\n- WeWork release, 2022-08-11 (board): https://www.wework.com/newsroom/wework-announces-new-softbank-appointment-to-its-board-of-directors\n", "person:alex-davidov": "\n# Alex Davidov\n\n## One-line take\nThe other half of Abstract's two-person founding team: an ex-Bridgewater trader whom Ramtin Naimi calls his skeptical counterweight. He co-signs every Abstract fund vehicle but owns under 5% of the management company and has almost no deal publicly credited to him ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/), [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/285846/PDF/285846.pdf)).\n\n## Crux\nDavidov wins as the systematizer and skeptic inside a firm whose public face, and nearly all its deal credit, belongs to Naimi. Naimi sources and builds relationships; Davidov was brought in to make the process repeatable ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). The constraint is structural. Form ADV lists Naimi at 75%+ of AVF Management LLC and Davidov in the \"less than 5%\" band, though Davidov is recorded as a control person ([src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/285846/PDF/285846.pdf)). He is a co-signatory, not a co-owner, so his personal brand and deal attribution stay thin. What he would never do, inferred from Naimi's framing: be the one pushing a consensus-chasing deal. Naimi casts him as the default skeptic ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Owns under 5% of the management company despite the founding-GP title.** Form ADV Schedule A (annual amendment, 2026-03-31) lists \"DAVIDOV, ALEXANDER\" as PARTNER with ownership code \"NA - less than 5%\" and control person = Y. Naimi (legal name Kanan Naimi-Akbar) is MANAGER since 02/2016 with code E (75%+) ([src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/285846/PDF/285846.pdf)). This is the management company only; his carry share is not disclosed.\n- **Join date is June 2018 per the regulator, not 2017.** Schedule A gives \"PARTNER 06/2018\" ([src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/285846/PDF/285846.pdf)). An aggregator independently has Abstract from 2018-04-30 and Core to 2018-05-31 ([src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/ALEX-DAVIDOV-A2H8E7/)). Colossus calls him Naimi's \"first hire in 2017\" ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). Possible reconciliation, unconfirmed: recruited in 2017, formal partner in mid-2018 ahead of Fund I's first close (AVF I first sale 2018-09-26 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1754983/000175498318000001/primary_doc.xml)). The firm file currently says 2017.\n- **Co-signs all seven Abstract fund vehicles.** He appears as \"Manager of its General Partner\" on AVF I (Form D 2018-10-09: $27.97M sold to 23 investors, first sale 2018-09-26) ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1754983/000175498318000001/primary_doc.xml)), and in the same role on AVF II (2020-09-28), AVGF I (2021-10-21), AVF III and AVGF II (2022-08-31). On AV Seed IV and AV Venture III (2024-11-15) he is \"Managing Member of the General Partner\", while Naimi is listed as \"Trustee of the Managing Member\" ([src](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Alexander%20Davidov%22)). Gross asset values per the 2026 ADV: AVF I $287M, AVF II $258M, AVF III $230M, AVGF I $259M, AVGF II $262M, AV Seed IV $200M, AV Venture III $304M ([src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/285846/PDF/285846.pdf)).\n- **Only portfolio board seat found: Status AI (WishRoll Inc.).** WishRoll Inc.'s 2025-05-16 Form D ($17,113,000 sold, first sale 2025-05-02, 24 investors) lists \"Alex Davidov\" as Director alongside the founders ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2068507/000123191925000052/primary_doc.xml)). WishRoll is the App Store seller of the Status app ([src](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/status-sims-but-social-media/id6596771144)), and Status names Abstract as an investor ([src](https://www.statusai.com/press)). The round was announced on 2026-05-19 as $17M across seed and Series A ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/19/gamified-social-media-network-status-announces-17m-funding-to-help-usher-in-new-era-of-social-networking/)), about a year after the filing. On the next Form D (2026-08-19: $10.4M sold, first sale 2026-08-11) Naimi is the Director and Davidov is not listed ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2068507/000123191926000945/primary_doc.xml)). The filings do not say why.\n- **The skeptic role.** Naimi: \"Alex and I are polar opposites\"; he calls Davidov \"kind of a default skeptic\" ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- **A two-person team for six years.** \"For six years, it was just Naimi and Davidov on the investment team\" ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). Kwon (2023) and Kozyrev (2024) joined later.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Abstract now writes $10M+ checks and makes concentrated frontier bets (about 15% of its 2023 fund into xAI's formation round, per people/ramtin-naimi.md). Davidov co-controls every fund vehicle, so he is a real decision-maker on any large Abstract seed. But I found no frontier-AI deal credited to him personally. His one confirmed board seat is Status AI, an AI consumer role-play app, which sits outside my frontier-lab or infra focus. He is most useful as the skeptic to convince inside an Abstract IC, not as an independent sourcing channel.\n\n## Background\n- Columbia: BS in Computer Science and an MBA, per the firm bio ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)). Graduation years not found.\n- Co-founded Intellitoys, an interactive toy for kids ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)); an aggregator dates the end to 2008 ([src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/ALEX-DAVIDOV-A2H8E7/)).\n- Co-founded SkillSlate, a labor marketplace later acquired by TaskRabbit ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)); an aggregator gives an end date of 2010 ([src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/ALEX-DAVIDOV-A2H8E7/)). The acquisition date was not confirmed in a primary source.\n- Bridgewater Associates: led a team in the trading strategy group ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)) for four years ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). Dates not found.\n- Founder Collective: invested in tech companies there ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)). Dates and title not found; the current Founder Collective team page does not mention him (checked 2026-11-10).\n- Core Innovation Capital: invested there ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)); Principal until 2018-05 per an aggregator ([src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/ALEX-DAVIDOV-A2H8E7/)). Naimi also worked at Core under Arjan Schütte before founding Abstract ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- Abstract: Partner from 06/2018 per Form ADV ([src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/285846/PDF/285846.pdf)); now General Partner ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)).\n- Form ADV lists an individual CRD number for him (5200102), which implies a past securities registration. A BrokerCheck/IAPD lookup returned no public record (checked 2026-11-10).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.abstract.com/people/ | Firm bio and headshot (verified 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | No personal writing found (2026-11-10) |\n| X | nothing found | No handle linked from the firm page; not guessed |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | Not linked from the firm page; not verified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. Web search was rate-limited during this run, so this is not a confirmed absence. He is quoted only through Naimi in Colossus (2025-07) ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n\n## How they think\n- Methodical, trading-derived approach to venture shared with Naimi ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- Default skeptic, the counterweight to Naimi's optimism ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- Calls he got right or wrong: nothing attributable found.\n\n## Read these first\n- Colossus, \"Ramtin Naimi: The Man with the Hot Hand\" (2025-07): the only narrative account of his role ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- Form ADV for AVF Management LLC, Schedule A: the ownership split ([src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/285846/PDF/285846.pdf)).\n- Abstract team page bio ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public social presence was identified (2026-11-10).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Status AI (WishRoll Inc.) | Series A (seed + A reported together as $17M; Form D $17.1M sold) | Form D first sale 2025-05-02; announced 2026-05-19 | Unknown. A secondary source in the repo says Abstract co-led with USV and GC (unverified) | General Catalyst, Union Square Ventures, Y Combinator, LightShed | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2068507/000123191925000052/primary_doc.xml), [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/19/gamified-social-media-network-status-announces-17m-funding-to-help-usher-in-new-era-of-social-networking/) |\n\nI found no other deal naming him personally. EDGAR full-text search for \"Alexander Davidov\" and \"Alex Davidov\" returns only the seven Abstract fund Form Ds and the WishRoll Form D (checked 2026-11-10).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Status AI:** Davidov held the board seat at the time of the May 2025 financing. By the August 2026 financing, Naimi held it instead. The intro path and the reason for the handoff were not found. The mapping of the 2025 Form D to the announced seed and Series A is unconfirmed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Abstract | Founding GP; Partner per ADV; control person; <5% of the management company | 06/2018 (ADV) / 2017 (Colossus) | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/285846/PDF/285846.pdf), [Colossus](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Ramtin Naimi | Co-founder; co-manager of every fund GP | 2017–2018 | [EDGAR](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Alexander%20Davidov%22) |\n| Core Innovation Capital | Former investor (Principal per aggregator), until 2018-05 | — | [firm bio](https://www.abstract.com/people/), [aggregator](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/ALEX-DAVIDOV-A2H8E7/) |\n| Founder Collective | Former investor | dates unknown | [firm bio](https://www.abstract.com/people/) |\n| Bridgewater Associates | Former team lead, trading strategy group (four years) | dates unknown | [firm bio](https://www.abstract.com/people/), [Colossus](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Status AI / WishRoll Inc. | Board director | 2025-05 (Form D); not listed on the 2026-08 Form D | [Form D 2025](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2068507/000123191925000052/primary_doc.xml), [Form D 2026](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2068507/000123191926000945/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which Abstract deals does he personally lead or sit on? Core-era and Abstract board seats beyond Status were not found.\n- What is his carry share? The ADV covers only the management company.\n- Why did the Status board seat pass to Naimi in 2026?\n- Exact Bridgewater and Founder Collective dates, and the SkillSlate–TaskRabbit deal date.\n- Did his stake change when Naimi bought back 100% of the management company by end-2024 (per Colossus)? The ADV still lists him under 5%.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.abstract.com/people/\n- https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/285846/PDF/285846.pdf\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1754983/000175498318000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Alexander%20Davidov%22\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2068507/000123191925000052/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2068507/000123191926000945/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.statusai.com/press\n- https://apps.apple.com/us/app/status-sims-but-social-media/id6596771144\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/19/gamified-social-media-network-status-announces-17m-funding-to-help-usher-in-new-era-of-social-networking/\n- https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/ALEX-DAVIDOV-A2H8E7/ (aggregator)\n", "person:alex-davies": "\n# Alex Davies\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. WebSearch budget was exhausted for this run, so this rests on direct fetches of jumptrading.com, its press releases, the Positron Series B release and Verdict's Series C report. Background gaps are unsearched, not confirmed absent.*\n\n## One-line take\nThe only Jump Trading person on the record for a balance-sheet AI deal. As CTO he is the technical buyer whose benchmarks turned Jump from a Positron customer into a co-lead of its $230M Series B ([src](https://qatar.websummit.com/wp-media/2026/02/Positron-__-Series-B-Press-Release.docx.pdf)).\n\n## Crux\nDavies is a buyer of compute, not a venture investor. Jump's AI-hardware conviction comes from running a chip on its own inference workloads. He cites about 3x lower end-to-end latency than an H100 system, plus an air-cooled footprint and a \"supply chain we can plan around\" ([src](https://qatar.websummit.com/wp-media/2026/02/Positron-__-Series-B-Press-Release.docx.pdf)). The constraint is that this is Jump's own money (proprietary balance sheet, no outside LPs per firms/jump-trading.md). It goes where Jump's trading infrastructure needs it. That rules out a seed in a frontier lab with nothing to deploy. Who approves balance-sheet deals is **unconfirmed**: no source says Davies signs, prices or sources them. He is the stated voice of the rationale, not a confirmed decision-maker.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He hedges Positron against his main vendor.** Six weeks after the Positron release, which pitched Asimov as beating Nvidia Rubin on memory, Davies announced Jump as one of the first financial firms to deploy NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 (2026-03-17). He said: \"Infrastructure is not an afterthought, it is a strategic capability.\" The release cites 15+ years of Nvidia collaboration ([src](https://www.jumptrading.com/signals/jump-trading-becomes-one-of-first-firms-to-deploy-nvidia-vera-rubin-platform)). The Positron stake is an option on a second supplier, not a replacement for Nvidia.\n- **No visible re-up.** Verdict's report on Positron's $875M Series C (2026-09-10) names 23 investors, including rival HFT Hudson River Trading as a strategic. It lists Jump only as a customer: \"Partners such as Parasail, Jump Trading, and i3d.net are among its customers\" ([src](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/)). Jump may have taken pro-rata unannounced; **unconfirmed**. Either way, the customer relationship outlasted the headline investment.\n- **Customer-first path.** Positron's CEO Mitesh Agrawal: \"Jump Trading came to Positron as a customer,\" then Jump stepped up after seeing the Asimov/Titan roadmap ([src](https://qatar.websummit.com/wp-media/2026/02/Positron-__-Series-B-Press-Release.docx.pdf)).\n- **He fronts Jump's academic pipeline.** Davies is the Jump voice on four funded PhD scholarships at UCL's Gatsby Computational Neuroscience Unit (2026-03-16) ([src](https://www.jumptrading.com/signals/jump-trading-partners-with-ucls-gatsby-computational-neuroscience-unit)). That is a plausible London link; his office location is **unconfirmed**.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a co-investor in the VC sense and will not lead a frontier-lab seed. But he is the clearest named diligence gate for Jump's balance sheet in **AI chips and inference infrastructure**: Positron Series B, and plausibly Etched (Jump named as investor, person unknown). For a $20M frontier-AI seed, he matters in two ways. He is a reference customer and technical validator for an inference-hardware or kernel company, and he is the route to Jump as a later-stage co-lead once the company has something Jump can run. He is not on Flapping Airplanes, Etched or Greptile by any source checked.\n\n## Background\n- Chief Technology Officer, Jump Trading: named so in the Positron release (2026-02-04), the UCL Gatsby release (2026-03-16) and the Vera Rubin release (2026-03-17). The jumptrading.com/technology page, whose first archived copy is 2026-04-17, carries his quote bylined \"CTO\" ([src](https://www.jumptrading.com/technology)).\n- Start date, prior employers and education: **not found** (web search unavailable this run; jumptrading.com has no leadership page).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.jumptrading.com/technology | Quote bylined \"Alex Davies, CTO\"; no bio or photo (2026-11-10) |\n| Vendor pages | https://www.redpanda.com/authors/alex-davies | Redpanda post 2023-08-22 and fireside chat 2025-02-19; BrightTalk VAST webcast https://brighttalk.com/webcast/19331/532097 (added 2026-11-11) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Not guessed |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | Not verified this run |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\nHow to reach him: through Jump's technology organization as a vendor. Sell or pilot inference hardware or software against Jump's workloads, as Positron did. A softer route is via UCL Gatsby researchers on the Jump scholarship programme (inferred).\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found (search unavailable; not a confirmed absence).\n\n## How they think\n- Bottlenecks are \"memory and power—not theoretical compute\" ([src](https://qatar.websummit.com/wp-media/2026/02/Positron-__-Series-B-Press-Release.docx.pdf)).\n- Infrastructure is a strategic capability. He says Jump designs its \"AI trading factory as an enduring platform rather than as a series of upgrades\" ([src](https://www.jumptrading.com/signals/jump-trading-becomes-one-of-first-firms-to-deploy-nvidia-vera-rubin-platform)).\n- Speed of engineering: \"You can design something on Monday and see it in production by Friday\" ([src](https://www.jumptrading.com/technology)).\n\n## Read these first\n- Positron Series B release, \"Jump Trading Leads After Deploying Atlas\" section ([src](https://qatar.websummit.com/wp-media/2026/02/Positron-__-Series-B-Press-Release.docx.pdf)).\n- Jump's Vera Rubin deployment release ([src](https://www.jumptrading.com/signals/jump-trading-becomes-one-of-first-firms-to-deploy-nvidia-vera-rubin-platform)).\n- jumptrading.com/technology ([src](https://www.jumptrading.com/technology)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Positron | Series B ($230M at >$1B post) | 2026-02-04 | Co-led (Jump Trading, with ARENA Private Wealth and Unless); Jump's first round in, after being a customer | QIA, Arm, Helena, Valor, Atreides, DFJ Growth, Resilience Reserve, Flume, 1517 | [src](https://qatar.websummit.com/wp-media/2026/02/Positron-__-Series-B-Press-Release.docx.pdf) |\n\nJump was not in Positron's seed or Series A investor lists in data/rounds.csv. It is not named in the Series C ([src](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/)).\n\n## Deal stories\n**Positron (2026-02-04).** Jump deployed Positron's Atlas inference system and tested it against an H100-based system on its own inference workloads. Davies reports about 3x lower end-to-end latency. As Jump saw the Asimov silicon and Titan system roadmap, it \"chose to step up as a co-lead investor,\" per CEO Mitesh Agrawal ([src](https://qatar.websummit.com/wp-media/2026/02/Positron-__-Series-B-Press-Release.docx.pdf)). Davies: \"We invested because Positron combines traction today with a roadmap...\" No board seat is reported. By the Series C (2026-09-10), Jump appears only as a customer ([src](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jump Trading | Chief Technology Officer | by 2026-02-04 | [src](https://qatar.websummit.com/wp-media/2026/02/Positron-__-Series-B-Press-Release.docx.pdf) |\n| Positron / Mitesh Agrawal | Customer, then co-lead investor | 2026-02-04 | [src](https://qatar.websummit.com/wp-media/2026/02/Positron-__-Series-B-Press-Release.docx.pdf) |\n| NVIDIA | Vendor; Vera Rubin NVL72 early adopter | 2026-03-17 | [src](https://www.jumptrading.com/signals/jump-trading-becomes-one-of-first-firms-to-deploy-nvidia-vera-rubin-platform) |\n| UCL Gatsby Computational Neuroscience Unit | Jump scholarship sponsor (Davies quoted) | 2026-03-16 | [src](https://www.jumptrading.com/signals/jump-trading-partners-with-ucls-gatsby-computational-neuroscience-unit) |\n| Ari Schottenstein (ARENA Private Wealth) | Fellow co-lead on Positron B | 2026-02-04 | [src](https://qatar.websummit.com/wp-media/2026/02/Positron-__-Series-B-Press-Release.docx.pdf) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Does Davies approve balance-sheet investments, or does President/CIO Dave Olsen or the co-founders? (unconfirmed; see firms/jump-trading.md)\n- Did Jump take pro-rata in the Positron Series C unannounced?\n- Was Davies involved in Etched, Flapping Airplanes or Greptile, where Jump is named but no person is?\n- Start date, prior employers, education, office location.\n\n## Update 2026-11-11 (people pass)\n\n**Tenure is longer than the Positron deal suggests.** A Redpanda author page titles him \"CTO, Jump Trading\" on a post dated 2023-08-22, so he held the CTO seat at least two and a half years before Positron ([src](https://www.redpanda.com/authors/alex-davies)). An undated BrightTalk webcast bills him \"Technology Lead @ Jump Trading,\" talking about VAST storage under Jump's HPC cloud ([src](https://brighttalk.com/webcast/19331/532097)). The CTO before him, Steve Hunt, held the role for about a decade before leaving for Kraken and then DRW ([src](https://www.efinancialcareers.com/news/jump-trading-cto-that-left-for-crypto-joins-rival-high-frequency-trading-firm), 2024-08-22). Handover date and Davies' start at Jump: **unconfirmed**. How he got the seat: internal promotion from a technology-lead role looks likely, but no source states it.\n\n**The pilot behind the $230M was small.** EE Times reports Positron's CEO calling the first Jump deployment \"really a small test deployment.\" Jump evaluated its workloads remotely within about a day, an on-prem install came up in weeks, and Jump has unusual access to the \"lower levels of the software stack\" ([src](https://www.eetimes.com/positron-230-million-funding-led-by-financial-trading-firms/), 2026-02-04). So the co-lead rests on a pilot plus roadmap access, not a fleet purchase.\n\n**Still testing nine months later.** A Reuters-based Investing.com report (2026-10-23): \"Jump Trading is backing Positron and testing its product\" ([src](https://www.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/highfrequency-traders-race-to-build-ai-computing-power-93CH-4968386)). The same piece says Jump tests Etched chips and holds Etched equity, and that Jump invested in two AI labs, Flapping Airplanes and Sooth Labs. **Davies is not named** on Etched, Flapping Airplanes or Sooth; the person behind those is still unknown. The chip deals fit his test-then-invest pattern; the lab deals do not, which suggests a second decision-maker (Dave Olsen is the likeliest, unconfirmed).\n\n**What he personally sources, signs or prices:** still not stated by any source. He is on record as the evaluator and spokesman for Positron only. Deals per person: Positron Series B (co-led, 2026-02-04; Jump's first round in, no re-up visible in the Series C).\n\n**Reach:** as before, as a vendor to Jump's technology organization. He also appears as a reference customer for infrastructure vendors (Redpanda fireside chat 2025-02-19, VAST webcast), so vendor-hosted events are a practical place to meet him.\n\n**Photo:** headshot hosted on Redpanda's author page (loads, checked 2026-11-11); Jump publishes none.\n\nChecked without result this pass: Reuters article itself (only the Investing.com rewrite found), Jump's own leadership pages (none exist), conference speaker lists (FIX, TradeTech: Olsen, not Davies). Education and pre-Jump employers: not found.\n\n## Sources\n- https://qatar.websummit.com/wp-media/2026/02/Positron-__-Series-B-Press-Release.docx.pdf\n- https://www.jumptrading.com/technology\n- https://www.jumptrading.com/signals/jump-trading-becomes-one-of-first-firms-to-deploy-nvidia-vera-rubin-platform\n- https://www.jumptrading.com/signals/jump-trading-partners-with-ucls-gatsby-computational-neuroscience-unit\n- https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/\n- https://www.eetimes.com/positron-230-million-funding-led-by-financial-trading-firms/\n- https://www.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/highfrequency-traders-race-to-build-ai-computing-power-93CH-4968386\n- https://www.redpanda.com/authors/alex-davies\n- https://brighttalk.com/webcast/19331/532097\n- https://www.efinancialcareers.com/news/jump-trading-cto-that-left-for-crypto-joins-rival-high-frequency-trading-firm\n", "person:alexander-schmitt": "\n# Alexander Schmitt\n\n## One-line take\nA former founder from the TUM/CDTM network, now a Lightspeed partner in Germany. He sources Munich founders years before they raise and then pre-empts: Langfuse seed, Paid's $21M seed, a Stacks Series A less than 12 months after its seed, and RobCo's B and C after he passed on its A.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** early founder relationships out of TUM/CDTM. He met the Langfuse founders about five years before leading their seed, and RobCo's founder about a decade before the C. He backs that with Lightspeed's balance sheet, so he can lead a $21M seed or a EUR100M Series B.\n- **How he decides:** memos open with market \"inevitable truths\". His objections are about business model (can RaaS scale?), not technology. He waits until the doubt is resolved and then pre-empts.\n- **Constraints:** he is Europe-based in a US-run firm. Stacks' founder met 10+ partners worldwide before the A, so his deals need global partnership buy-in. He says founders who \"seed-strap\" don't fit the venture model.\n- **What he'd never do:** back a business that plans to stop raising after the seed, or accept a durable but slow 40%-margin company without a path to accelerate.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Admitted pass on RobCo's Series A.** He called it \"an expensive mistake\" on camera. The memo's two concerns were RaaS scaling and differentiation beyond modular hardware. Quote: \"we initially passed on your series A\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZWmfVPhk9bI) (22:10).\n- **Pass, then pre-empt.** \"why we ultimately doubled down and preempted your series B two years ago\". He then led again in the $100M C [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZWmfVPhk9bI) (22:52).\n- **Memo template.** \"The first part of our investment memo is always the inevitable truths.\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZWmfVPhk9bI) (2:36). On the Investment Memo show he reads the actual IC memo back to the founder: \"That's actually the original memo that we presented to our partnership\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=chF2QPCmbaQ) (2:08).\n- **Long courtship.** On Stacks: \"our first day of having a walk in rainy gray Berlin\". He also took the founder on Lightspeed's kite trip before the A [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ndJO9PhZ2Tw) (11:49).\n- **Pre-emptive A.** \"You wouldn't have needed the money right now.\" Stacks raised its A less than 12 months after the seed [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ndJO9PhZ2Tw) (29:34).\n- **Diligence by calling customers.** He spoke to \"potential customers in the industry while getting to know you better\" and found that some couldn't close month-end [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ndJO9PhZ2Tw) (6:48).\n- **CDTM network.** \"I first met Marc and Max 5 years back in Munich's founders scene\", at CDTM. He then led the $4M Langfuse seed with La Famiglia and YC [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/building-with-langfuse-observability-analytics-for-llm-applications/).\n- **Pricing is the top founder question.** He hears it \"almost three times a day\" from AI-app founders. That pain explains the Paid bet [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3XaKIq_P8ic) (14:38).\n- **Wants growth beyond the margin box.** Against Medina's \"live in the 40% margin box\" he says: \"once you found that box, you can hopefully accelerate even beyond\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3XaKIq_P8ic) (21:38).\n- **Seed-strapping doesn't fit.** \"It's not aligned with the venture capital utility function, candidly speaking\" (London Tech Week, June 2025) [src](https://www.uktech.news/ai/ltw-25-how-agentic-ai-can-radically-change-startup-funding-20250611).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He leads seeds and early As in AI-native automation and AI infrastructure out of Germany: Langfuse (LLM observability seed, 2023), Paid (agent billing, $21M seed, 2025), Stacks (AI accounting close, Series A 2026), Manex AI (factory agents seed, 2025) and Condukt (compliance agents seed, 2025). These are Serval-like automation companies. He is a good co-investor or referral node for German and CDTM-network technical founders. He is not a frontier-lab or chips investor. His defence deals (Helsing, Orqa, Hypersonica) and RobCo growth rounds are outside your core.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in rural Germany. Studied computer science and finance at TUM, CDTM and MIT [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/alexander-schmitt/).\n- Worked in finance, then co-founded MergerSpot (Munich; ML for M&A target profiles) with Julian Wolz and Ingo Mayer. A 2019 TUM job posting names him as co-founder [src](https://www.fa.mgt.tum.de/fileadmin/w00chf/controlling/Lehre/Postings/07.05.19_MergerSpot_BusinessDevelopment.pdf). CEO 2017-2020 per aggregator profiles (theorg/MarketScreener, secondary). The Lightspeed bio says he built and sold a business.\n- Investor at Cherry Ventures (Berlin seed fund) before Lightspeed; 2020 to Sep 2022 per aggregator, dates unconfirmed [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/alexander-schmitt/).\n- Joined Lightspeed's European team as a partner in 2022. Leads the Germany business [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/alexander-schmitt/).\n- Investor in the CDTM alumni venture fund [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/wie-der-cdtm-alumni-venture-fund-das-europ%C3%A4ische-startup/id1511786820?i=1000693455469).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://lsvp.com/team-member/alexander-schmitt/ | Lightspeed bio and stories list (2026-11-11) |\n| Substack / blog | https://lsvp.com/stories/ | Bylined investment memos (Langfuse, Manex AI, Paid, Condukt; co-bylines on RobCo, Helsing); no personal blog found |\n| X | nothing found | No handle located |\n| LinkedIn | not used | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://www.youtube.com/@LightspeedVP | Hosts Lightspeed's \"The Investment Memo\" on YouTube for his European deals (RobCo, Stacks, Hypersonica); 2024 co-host of Startup Insider \"Investments & Exits\" (German) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-03 - FinanceFWD #178 (German) - AI and fintech business models; Lightspeed's Europe push - [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/financefwd-178-mit-lightspeed-partner-alex-schmitt/id1413783570?i=1000605260370) - not mined (no transcript)\n- 2024-06 to 2024-11 - Startup Insider \"Investments & Exits\" with Eva Spannagl (German; Nubank/Monzo, Groq/Black Forest Labs, Bits & Pretzels, Klarna/Volta) - [example](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/groq-black-forest-labs-investments-exits-mit-eva-spannagl/id1511786820?i=1000665205989) - not mined\n- 2025-01 - Startup Insider, \"Was macht Europa zum globalen Tech-Hotspot?\" - [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/was-macht-europa-zum-globalen-tech-hotspot-insights/id1511786820?i=1000682381267)\n- 2025-02 - Startup Insider, CDTM Venture Fund with Leon Szeli - [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/wie-der-cdtm-alumni-venture-fund-das-europ%C3%A4ische-startup/id1511786820?i=1000693455469)\n- 2025-06 - London Tech Week panel: seed-strapping, and an AI-app funding bubble with \"a lot of money burned\" - [link](https://www.uktech.news/ai/ltw-25-how-agentic-ai-can-radically-change-startup-funding-20250611)\n- 2025-11 - Slush 2025, interviews Manny Medina (Paid): pricing agents, margins, repeat founders - [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3XaKIq_P8ic)\n- 2026-01 - The Investment Memo: RobCo (Roman Hölzl): admits the Series A pass - [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZWmfVPhk9bI)\n- 2026-02 - Startup Insider: \"9 Mrd. Kapital, 165 AI-Investments, RobCo-Verdopplung\" - [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/9-mrd-kapital-165-ai-investments-robco-verdopplung/id1511786820?i=1000749534778)\n- 2026-02 - The Investment Memo: Stacks (Albert Malikov): pre-emptive A, Berlin walk origin - [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ndJO9PhZ2Tw)\n- 2026-11 - The Investment Memo: Hypersonica (Philipp Kerth): reads the IC memo; EUR100M B co-lead - [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=chF2QPCmbaQ)\n\n## How they think\n- **Market first.** Memos open with \"inevitable truths\": labour shortages and reshoring for RobCo and Manex, Europe's deterrence gap for Helsing and Hypersonica, ERP fragmentation for Stacks.\n- **Capital efficiency as a signal.** Points he highlights: Hypersonica's Mach 6 flight on under 25M raised; RobCo's negative working capital; Stacks raising its A with cash still in the bank.\n- **Contrarian views.** Seed-strapping is good for founders but not for venture. He sees an AI-app funding bubble with \"a lot of money burned\" [src](https://www.uktech.news/ai/ltw-25-how-agentic-ai-can-radically-change-startup-funding-20250611). He says playbooks no longer apply in the agentic era [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3XaKIq_P8ic). He argues Europe can win in hardware and defence because manufacturing talent is \"mass available\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=chF2QPCmbaQ).\n- **Calls he got wrong, then right.** Passed on RobCo's A, then pre-empted the B and led the C [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZWmfVPhk9bI).\n\n## Read these first\n1. The Investment Memo, RobCo (2026-01): the pass, the two memo concerns, the pre-empt. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZWmfVPhk9bI\n2. The Investment Memo, Stacks (2026-02): how he courts and diligences a seed-stage founder into an A. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ndJO9PhZ2Tw\n3. Langfuse seed memo (2023-11): his earliest solo memo and the CDTM origin story. https://lsvp.com/stories/building-with-langfuse-observability-analytics-for-llm-applications/\n4. Slush 2025 with Manny Medina: his views on AI-app pricing and margins. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3XaKIq_P8ic\n5. UKTN London Tech Week 2025 write-up: seed-strapping and the bubble. https://www.uktech.news/ai/ltw-25-how-agentic-ai-can-radically-change-startup-funding-20250611\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found on X. His public voice is Lightspeed's YouTube show, German startup podcasts (Startup Insider, Finance Forward) and conference stages (Slush, London Tech Week, Bits & Pretzels). He promotes his own portfolio founders on stage (Medina, Hölzl, Malikov, Kerth).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Langfuse | Seed ($4M) | 2023-11 | yes | La Famiglia; Y Combinator | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/building-with-langfuse-observability-analytics-for-llm-applications/) |\n| RobCo | Series B ($42.5M) | 2024-02 | yes (pre-empted) | | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/joining-forces-with-robco-for-an-autonomous-and-automated-future/) |\n| RobCo | Series C ($100M) | 2026-01 | yes (\"leading again\") | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZWmfVPhk9bI) |\n| Helsing | Series C (EUR450M) | 2024-07 | no (new investor) | | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/partnering-with-helsing-europes-leader-in-ai-enabled-defense/) |\n| Orqa | Seed (EUR5.8M) | 2024-12 | unknown | | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/orqa/) |\n| Manex AI | Seed (EUR8M) | 2025-06 | unknown | BlueYard; Capmont; CDTM Venture Fund | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/partnering-with-manex-ai-to-build-the-control-layer-for-autonomous-manufacturing/) |\n| Paid | Seed ($21M) | 2025-09 | yes | | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/the-ai-agent-economy-has-a-19-trillion-problem-our-investment-in-paid/) |\n| Condukt | Seed ($10M) | 2025-11 | co (with MMC) | MMC Ventures | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/building-the-data-foundation-for-agentic-compliance-partnering-with-condukt/) |\n| Stacks AI | Series A | 2026-02 | yes (Lightspeed board seat) | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ndJO9PhZ2Tw) |\n| Hypersonica | Series B (EUR100M) | 2026-11 | co-lead | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=chF2QPCmbaQ) |\n| Multiplier | not stated | | | | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/alexander-schmitt/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **RobCo.** Met Roman Hölzl at TUM about a decade ago. Passed on the Series A, citing RaaS scalability and differentiation beyond hardware. Pre-empted and led the $42.5M B in Feb 2024 at under 100 lifetime robot deployments. Led again in the $100M C in Jan 2026, by which time RobCo was deploying 300+ robots a year [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZWmfVPhk9bI).\n- **Stacks.** First met Albert Malikov on a walk in Berlin; brought him on Lightspeed's kite trip. Called potential customers during diligence. Malikov met 10+ Lightspeed partners. Led the Series A less than 12 months after the seed, while Stacks still had seed cash [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ndJO9PhZ2Tw).\n- **Langfuse.** Knew Marc Klingen and Max Deichmann from CDTM since about 2018. They refined the idea at YC, and he led the $4M seed in Nov 2023 [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/building-with-langfuse-observability-analytics-for-llm-applications/).\n- **Paid.** Bet on repeat founder Manny Medina (Outreach) and led the $21M seed. On stage he connects it to founders' pricing pain [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/the-ai-agent-economy-has-a-19-trillion-problem-our-investment-in-paid/), [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3XaKIq_P8ic).\n- **Hypersonica.** Co-led the EUR100M Series B after the company reached a Mach 6 test flight on under 25M raised [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=chF2QPCmbaQ).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lightspeed Venture Partners | Partner, Europe | 2022 | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/alexander-schmitt/) |\n| Cherry Ventures | Former investor | ~2020-2022 (aggregator) | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/alexander-schmitt/) |\n| MergerSpot | Co-founder/CEO | 2017-2020 (aggregator) | [src](https://www.fa.mgt.tum.de/fileadmin/w00chf/controlling/Lehre/Postings/07.05.19_MergerSpot_BusinessDevelopment.pdf) |\n| Raviraj Jain | Co-author on RobCo and Helsing memos | 2024 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/joining-forces-with-robco-for-an-autonomous-and-automated-future/) |\n| Antoine Moyroud | Co-author on the 2023 LLM event write-up; co-partner on Orqa | 2023 | [src](https://lsvp.com/event/taking-llms-from-proof-of-concept-to-production/) |\n| Roman Hölzl (RobCo) | Met at TUM; lead investor | ~2016 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZWmfVPhk9bI) |\n| Marc Klingen, Max Deichmann (Langfuse) | Met at CDTM; seed lead | ~2018 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/building-with-langfuse-observability-analytics-for-llm-applications/) |\n| Albert Malikov (Stacks) | Courted for ~1.5-2 years; Series A lead, board | ~2024 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ndJO9PhZ2Tw) |\n| Manny Medina (Paid) | Seed lead; Slush interviewer | 2025 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3XaKIq_P8ic) |\n| Eva Spannagl | Startup Insider co-host | 2024 | [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/nubank-monzo-investments-exits-mit-eva-spannagl-alexander/id1511786820?i=1000657740558) |\n| CDTM Venture Fund | Investor (alumnus) | 2025 | [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/wie-der-cdtm-alumni-venture-fund-das-europ%C3%A4ische-startup/id1511786820?i=1000693455469) |\n\n## Open questions\n- The German podcast archive (FinanceFWD 2023, Startup Insider 2024-2026) has no transcripts. The Feb 2026 episode reportedly covers Lightspeed's $9B raise and his bar for founders; worth a German transcription.\n- Exact Cherry Ventures dates and deals, and whether he sourced anything there that Lightspeed later backed.\n- Hypersonica co-lead: the caption reads \"co-led by Lightspeed NGC\", likely \"and GC\". General Catalyst lists Hypersonica in its portfolio (Robin Dechant roster row), but this is unconfirmed.\n- What Multiplier is in his portfolio, and its round.\n- Whether he has an X account.\n\n## Sources\n- https://lsvp.com/team-member/alexander-schmitt/\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZWmfVPhk9bI\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ndJO9PhZ2Tw\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=chF2QPCmbaQ\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3XaKIq_P8ic\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/building-with-langfuse-observability-analytics-for-llm-applications/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/joining-forces-with-robco-for-an-autonomous-and-automated-future/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/partnering-with-helsing-europes-leader-in-ai-enabled-defense/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/partnering-with-manex-ai-to-build-the-control-layer-for-autonomous-manufacturing/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/the-ai-agent-economy-has-a-19-trillion-problem-our-investment-in-paid/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/building-the-data-foundation-for-agentic-compliance-partnering-with-condukt/\n- https://lsvp.com/event/taking-llms-from-proof-of-concept-to-production/\n- https://www.uktech.news/ai/ltw-25-how-agentic-ai-can-radically-change-startup-funding-20250611\n- https://www.fa.mgt.tum.de/fileadmin/w00chf/controlling/Lehre/Postings/07.05.19_MergerSpot_BusinessDevelopment.pdf\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/financefwd-178-mit-lightspeed-partner-alex-schmitt/id1413783570?i=1000605260370\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/9-mrd-kapital-165-ai-investments-robco-verdopplung/id1511786820?i=1000749534778\n- https://lsvp.com/company/orqa/\n", "person:alexander-tamas": "\n# Alexander Tamas\n\n*Profile written 2026-11-10. Web search was unavailable for this run (shared budget used up), so everything below comes from direct fetches of SEC filings, the UK Companies House register and press pages already cited in `firms/vy-capital.md`.*\n\n## One-line take\nThe owner-operator of Vy Capital: he personally controls the firm's UK arm and co-controlled its SPAC sponsor, but no public source names him on any specific Vy AI deal. His partner John Hering is the visible face of the Musk relationship.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** pattern recognition from DST's 2009-12 run (Facebook, Airbnb, Spotify, Twitter, Alibaba, per Vy's own bio [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm)), joined to a model of very few, very large positions held \"for decades\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm). Vy says it \"led fundraising rounds in the vast majority\" of its deals and \"avoid[s] competitive processes\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm).\n- **What constrains him:** the book is about 99% SpaceX [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1628622/000119312526354400/57250.xml). Since July 2025 Vy takes no outside money [src](https://www.forbes.com.tr/para-yatirim/elon-musk-destekcisi-vy-capital-buyuk-kazanclarin-ardindan-dis-yatirimcilara-kapilarini-kapatti). He is close to invisible: no podcasts, no posts, and from 2017 onward the Vy Form Ds list Cayman nominee directors, not him (the 2014 Fund I filing is the only one naming him) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1619788/000161978814000001/primary_doc.xml), [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2060739/000206073925000002/primary_doc.xml).\n- **What he'd never do:** run a competitive auction, or force a deal. Vy returned the $575M SPAC trust in 2022 rather than do a weak merger [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465922102994/tm2226464d1_ex99-2.htm).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He alone controls Vy's UK entity.** VY Capital UK Ltd (incorporated 2022-07-04; SIC \"venture and development capital\") lists Tamas as its only person with significant control: 75%+ of shares and votes and the \"Right to appoint or remove directors\" [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14214424/persons-with-significant-control). The directors are COO Daniel Schwarz and **Christopher Edmund Huey** (appointed 2023-04-27), a name in no Vy press or SEC source found [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14214424/officers). Share capital rose to GBP 2.25M in Dec 2023 [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14214424/filing-history).\n- **Residence is Italy, not Dubai, on the register.** The PSC entry gives country of residence Italy, with a change of details dated 2023-01-01 [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14214424/filing-history). Press still calls Vy Dubai-based [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/vy-capital-spacex-stake-europe). The identity match is by name, German nationality and birth month (September 1977), which fits his stated age of 43 in the Oct 2020 prospectus [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm). Which country he actually works from now is unconfirmed.\n- **Hard DST dates.** He was a designated member of Digital Sky Finance LLP (DST's London LLP) from 2008-08-27 to 2010-10-26. On the day he resigned, a company he directed, Albo Digital Limited, became a member, and DST partner John Lindfors took over as its director [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/OC339660/officers), [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/07262381/officers). That reads as a restructuring of his DST partnership interest in Oct 2010, while press says he stayed at DST until Jan 2013 [src](https://fortune.com/2013/01/31/exclusive-yuri-milner-loses-top-dealmaker). That interpretation is an inference.\n- **Synaptic was Vy's own diligence engine.** The prospectus says Vy \"incubated and commercialized\" Synaptic, a data platform using over 5 billion data points a month to assess companies \"from the outside in\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm). Tamas is named as Synaptic's founder in the same filing. So the screening is quantitative and done before any meeting.\n- **He co-controlled the SPAC sponsor 50/50 with Hering.** \"Our sponsor is controlled by Mr. Tamas and Mr. Hering\" (shared voting and investment discretion over 97.6% of founder shares) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm).\n- **Why Hering exists in the firm.** cc.cz reports Tamas wanted a partner with a \"dravým duchem\" (predatory or driven spirit) to build a giant firm, and met Hering in 2015 [src](https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/). The prospectus calls Hering a founding partner, so the 2015 date conflicts.\n- **Free-speech view.** In a 2019 Berggruen Institute conversation he said it is misguided that \"social media platforms should govern what we can and cannot see\" (via Bloomberg) [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/musk-twitter-bid-leans-financier-005421082.html). That lines up with Vy's $700M Twitter commitment three years later.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He does not do seeds and takes no outside LPs, so he is neither an LP for me nor a seed co-lead. But Vy led the Cerebras Series C (2017-01) and the Neuralink Series C (2021-07), and was in both 2024 xAI rounds, so it will write very large, early-for-growth checks into frontier hardware and labs and syndicate single-asset vehicles of $200M to $700M. For a lab I seed, he is a plausible Series B/C lead. He also funds AI-safety research (Oxford FHI fellowship), which may matter for a safety-minded lab. Deals that matter: Cerebras C, xAI B/C, Neuralink C.\n\n## Background\n- Born in Romania [src](https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/); German citizen; age 43 in Oct 2020 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm). Education not found.\n- Founding member of Arma Partners (tech corporate-finance adviser), dates not given [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/musk-twitter-bid-leans-financier-005421082.html).\n- Goldman Sachs tech M&A, London, 2006-08 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm).\n- DST Partner from 2008 (LLP member from 2008-08-27 [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/vvP_Q0JqrEMCaikd1OmP3gpn4aM/appointments)); Mail.ru Managing Director, took it public in 2010 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm). Left DST in Jan 2013 [src](https://fortune.com/2013/01/31/exclusive-yuri-milner-loses-top-dealmaker).\n- **How he got the seat:** he did not inherit one. He co-founded Vy in 2013 with Mateusz Szeszkowski [src](https://fortune.com/2013/01/31/exclusive-yuri-milner-loses-top-dealmaker) and raised VY Capital Holdings (later Fund I; first sale 2014-09-05), on whose board he sat [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1619788/000161978814000001/primary_doc.xml). The DST record was the credential.\n- Appointed by Dubai's ruler to a digital-economy advisory council (date not given) [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/musk-twitter-bid-leans-financier-005421082.html).\n- Philanthropy: Alexander Tamas Fellowship at Oxford's Future of Humanity Institute (FHI closed in 2024), a neuroscience institute at Imperial College, and a UCSF/CZ Biohub biosafety initiative with Hering [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.vycapital.com/ | Firm site; one-line tagline, no team page or contact (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | unconfirmed | Bloomberg (2022) describes a dormant account from March 2009 with no posts; handle not verified, so not listed [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/musk-twitter-bid-leans-financier-005421082.html) |\n| LinkedIn | unconfirmed | A profile exists per Bloomberg; URL not verified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| Register | https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14214424/persons-with-significant-control | UK PSC entry (verified 2026-11-10) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2019 — Berggruen Institute — conversation (link not found; quoted via Bloomberg) — argued platforms should not decide what users see [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/musk-twitter-bid-leans-financier-005421082.html)\n- No podcasts or conference talks found (web search unavailable this run).\n\n## How they think\n- Few positions, held for decades; lead the round, avoid auctions [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm).\n- Data first: outside-in screening via Synaptic before meeting founders [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm).\n- Embedded with portfolio companies: Vy staff hold portfolio \"email addresses, company ID badges\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm).\n- Call on record (paraphrase from the 2026 investor letter): SpaceX could pass $10T in five to seven years [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/vy-capital-spacex-stake-europe).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Vy Global Growth 424B4 (2020): the only long self-description of Vy and Tamas [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm).\n2. VYGG letter returning the trust (Sept 2022): what he won't do [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465922102994/tm2226464d1_ex99-2.htm).\n3. Bloomberg profile (June 2022): the only reported portrait [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/musk-twitter-bid-leans-financier-005421082.html).\n4. Fortune (Jan 2013): his DST exit [src](https://fortune.com/2013/01/31/exclusive-yuri-milner-loses-top-dealmaker).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found beyond Bloomberg's note that his dormant X account liked a 2022 Musk post about authenticating real humans [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/musk-twitter-bid-leans-financier-005421082.html).\n\n## Portfolio\nPersonal naming is thin. Only the rows marked \"named\" tie a source to Tamas himself; the rest are firm-level deals during his tenure. DST-era and personal rows are already in `data/rows/vy-capital-people-deals.csv` and not repeated here.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Facebook, Airbnb, Spotify, Twitter, JD.com, Alibaba, Xiaomi, Zalando (at DST; named, firm-said) | early primary (rounds not given; Facebook 2009) | 2009-12 | \"leading\" per Vy's bio | DST | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm) |\n| Mail.ru (at DST; named) | IPO | 2010 | n/a | | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm) |\n| SpaceX (personal; named) | undisclosed | not given | no | | [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/musk-twitter-bid-leans-financier-005421082.html) |\n| Neuralink (personal; named) | undisclosed | not given | no | | [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/musk-twitter-bid-leans-financier-005421082.html) |\n| Cerebras (firm only) | Series C, first Vy entry; re-upped in D (2018-11) and F (2021-11) | 2017-01 | yes (Vy) | Benchmark, Foundation, Eclipse came earlier | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebras) |\n| Neuralink (firm only) | Series C ($205M) | 2021-07-29 | yes (Vy) | GV, DFJ Growth, Valor, Craft, Founders Fund, Gigafund | [src](https://www.teslarati.com/neuralink-205-million-investment-round) |\n| xAI (firm only) | Series B; re-up in C | 2024-05-26; 2024-12-23 | no | Valor, a16z, Sequoia, Fidelity and others | [src](https://x.ai/news/series-b), [src](https://x.ai/news/series-c) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **SpaceX:** the 2016 entry is credited to Hering (with Antonio Gracias making the introduction), not Tamas [src](https://www.chaincatcher.com/en/article/2289437), [src](https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/).\n- **Cerebras C (2017):** no source says who at Vy sourced or priced it. Given that Vy's investment team was tiny and Tamas ran the flagship Fund I that holds the stake [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026025762/exhibit43-sx1.htm), he is the likeliest signer. That is an inference.\n- **Twitter (2022):** $700M commitment by the firm [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1494730/000110465922056055/tm2214608-1_sc13da.htm); Tamas declined to comment to Bloomberg [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/musk-twitter-bid-leans-financier-005421082.html).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| John Hering | co-founder; shared control of the SPAC sponsor | 2013 or 2015 (conflict) | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm) |\n| Christopher Huey | director of VY Capital UK Ltd, which Tamas controls | 2023-04-27 | [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14214424/officers) |\n| Daniel Schwarz | director of VY Capital UK Ltd; Vy COO | 2022-07-04 | [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14214424/officers) |\n| John Lindfors | succeeded him at DST's London LLP via Albo Digital Ltd | 2010-10-26 | [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/07262381/officers) |\n| Yuri Milner | DST boss; warm send-off in 2013 | 2008 | [src](https://fortune.com/2013/01/31/exclusive-yuri-milner-loses-top-dealmaker) |\n| Mateusz Szeszkowski | Vy co-founder | 2013 | [src](https://fortune.com/2013/01/31/exclusive-yuri-milner-loses-top-dealmaker) |\n| Javier Olivan, Hugo Barra, Steve Huffman, Sujay Jaswa, Justin Kan, Julie Herendeen | VYGG board he chaired | 2020 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm) |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** there is no public inbox. The realistic paths are (1) John Hering (Bay Area, Boring board), (2) the people who sat on his SPAC board (Sujay Jaswa, Hugo Barra, Steve Huffman, Javier Olivan), each of whom he picked, (3) repeat co-investors Valor/Antonio Gracias and Sequoia/Shaun Maguire, and (4) DST alumni (Yuri Milner, John Lindfors). An AI-safety angle (his FHI fellowship) is a plausible door for a safety-oriented lab; that is an inference.\n\n## Open questions\n- Who is Christopher Huey, and what does VY Capital UK do (accounts PDF not read)?\n- Does Tamas personally sign Vy's AI deals (Cerebras, xAI), or does Hering?\n- Where is he based now: Italy (register) or Dubai (press)?\n- What is Synaptic today?\n\n## Sources\n- VYGG 424B4: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm\n- VY Capital UK Ltd PSC, officers, filing history: https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14214424/persons-with-significant-control ; https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14214424/officers ; https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14214424/filing-history\n- Digital Sky Finance LLP officers: https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/OC339660/officers\n- Albo Digital Ltd officers: https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/07262381/officers\n- Bloomberg via Yahoo 2022-06-09: https://finance.yahoo.com/news/musk-twitter-bid-leans-financier-005421082.html\n- Fortune 2013-01-31: https://fortune.com/2013/01/31/exclusive-yuri-milner-loses-top-dealmaker\n- cc.cz 2026-09-15: https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/\n- ChainCatcher (FT) 2026-09-13: https://www.chaincatcher.com/en/article/2289437\n- Forbes Turkey 2025-07-18: https://www.forbes.com.tr/para-yatirim/elon-musk-destekcisi-vy-capital-buyuk-kazanclarin-ardindan-dis-yatirimcilara-kapilarini-kapatti\n- TNW: https://thenextweb.com/news/vy-capital-spacex-stake-europe\n- Teslarati (Neuralink C): https://www.teslarati.com/neuralink-205-million-investment-round\n- Form Ds: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1619788/000161978814000001/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2060739/000206073925000002/primary_doc.xml\n", "person:alexander-will": "\n# Alexander Will\n\n## One-line take\nAlexander Will is a Calm founding-team operator, an LSE-educated German who co-founded a London startup at 22, and since June 2026 the sole GP of a new, not-yet-funded Antimatter Fund I. Worth knowing as a consumer-operator angel network. He is not a $10M+ check writer.\n\n## Crux\nHis edge is operator credibility and a network from scaling Calm (business development, international, partnerships). The Calm board included Insight and Lightspeed partners [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1721834/000172183420000001/primary_doc.xml). His constraint is size: one person, a first fund, $0 sold at filing, and a $100K minimum LP check [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2139194/000213919426000001/primary_doc.xml). He cannot lead or anchor big seed rounds, and nothing public shows any AI focus.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He alone runs Antimatter.** The Form D names him as the only related person: \"Managing Member of the General Partner of the Issuer\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2139194/000213919426000001/primary_doc.xml). There are no partners and no institutional sponsor.\n- **The fund had raised nothing when it filed.** Offering \"Indefinite\", $0 sold, first sale yet to occur, $100,000 minimum, and a PMB mailbox at 455 Market St [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2139194/000213919426000001/primary_doc.xml). No D/A followed [src](https://data.sec.gov/submissions/CIK0002139194.json).\n- **His full name ties the career together.** The Form D gives \"John Theodor Ronald\" as his middle names. Companies House lists \"Alexander John Theodor WILL\", born November 1983, German, as director of Spoonfed Media Ltd (London, 2006), Listora Ltd (2013) and Ping Pong Fight Club Ltd (2014), with a Los Angeles address by 2013 [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/EGgR0C614YaU8_6qr_aKI1v77eM/appointments). TechCrunch in 2009 called the Spoonfed founders \"a couple of 25-year olds, graduates of the London School of Economics\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/2009/01/16/get-spoonfed-local-events-rated-by-the-crowd/). That matches the Calm Alex Will, who was 35 in August 2019 [src](https://www.business-punk.com/calm-alex-will/) and is listed as an LSE alum on Crunchbase (aggregator, https://www.crunchbase.com/person/alexander-will).\n- **At Calm** Business Punk (2019) described him as Chief Strategy Officer, board member and part of the founding team, covering business development, international, partnerships and communications. On mindfulness's image, he said: \"Dieses esoterische Image wollten wir von Anfang an nicht bedienen.\" [src](https://www.business-punk.com/calm-alex-will/). Calm's 2020 Form D lists \"Alexander Will\" as Executive Officer and Director [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1721834/000172183420000001/primary_doc.xml).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nLow. He is a first-time solo GP with an unknown fund size, a $100K LP minimum and no known AI deals. At most he is a small co-investor or a source of consumer and operator deals, never a competitor for $20M seed leads. The one thing worth watching is whether Antimatter turns out to be AI-focused once it announces; the name suggests a deep-tech or physics brand, but nothing confirms that.\n\n## Background\n- LSE graduate (with Henry Erskine Crum) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2009/01/16/get-spoonfed-local-events-rated-by-the-crowd/).\n- 2006-04: director and company secretary of Spoonfed Media Ltd, London; resigned as secretary 2008-12 [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/EGgR0C614YaU8_6qr_aKI1v77eM/appointments). Spoonfed was an events guide whose chairman was Kevin Eyres, then MD of LinkedIn Europe [src](https://techcrunch.com/2009/01/16/get-spoonfed-local-events-rated-by-the-crowd/).\n- 2013-11 and 2014-05: director of Listora Ltd and Ping Pong Fight Club Ltd, with a Los Angeles address [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/EGgR0C614YaU8_6qr_aKI1v77eM/appointments).\n- Calm: founding team, CSO and board member by 2019 [src](https://www.business-punk.com/calm-alex-will/); Executive Officer and Director on the 2020 Form D [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1721834/000172183420000001/primary_doc.xml). Crunchbase calls him President (aggregator). Start and end dates are **unconfirmed**.\n- 2026-06: Managing Member of Antimatter GP I LLC [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2139194/000213919426000001/primary_doc.xml).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | none found | antimattercapital.io and antimatter.ventures are not tied to him |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | not confirmed | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked (login wall) | |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2019-08 — Business Punk (German) — Calm interview — https://www.business-punk.com/calm-alex-will/ — on sleep as a universal theme and the anti-esoteric positioning.\n\n## How they think\nOnly Calm-era material exists: build mindfulness as a mainstream consumer product rather than an esoteric one [src](https://www.business-punk.com/calm-alex-will/). There is no investing thesis on record.\n\n## Read these first\n- Business Punk interview (2019): https://www.business-punk.com/calm-alex-will/\n- Antimatter Fund I Form D (2026): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2139194/000213919426000001/primary_doc.xml\n\n## Social engagement\nnothing found\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Pachama | Series B ($55M) | 2022-05-05 | no | Future Positive, Breakthrough Energy Ventures, Lowercarbon | [aggregator](https://startupintros.com/news/pachama-series-b-2022-05) (lists \"Alex Will\"; same person inferred) |\n| Khatabook | Series B ($60M) | 2020-05-19 | no | B Capital, Sequoia India, Tencent | [aggregator](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/alexander-will) only |\n\n## Deal stories\nnothing found\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| antimatter-fund | at: Managing Member of GP | 2026-06 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2139194/000213919426000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| calm | at: CSO and board member (identity probable) | unconfirmed | [src](https://www.business-punk.com/calm-alex-will/) |\n| spoonfed | at: Co-founder and director | 2006-04 | [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/EGgR0C614YaU8_6qr_aKI1v77eM/appointments) |\n| henry-erskine-crum | Spoonfed co-founder | 2006 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2009/01/16/get-spoonfed-local-events-rated-by-the-crowd/) |\n| Deven Parekh (Insight), Nicole Quinn (Lightspeed) | fellow Calm directors on the 2020 Form D | 2020 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1721834/000172183420000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Is the Calm Alex Will definitely the Antimatter GP?** Probable: same name, San Francisco, LSE, and the Calm Will was 35 in 2019, matching a Companies House birth date of November 1983. No single document names both, so this is **unconfirmed**. LinkedIn or a fund announcement would settle it.\n- Fund size, first close, LPs, thesis and first investments: all unknown. Check EDGAR for a D/A.\n- When he left Calm, if he has.\n- Whether he is the \"Alex Will\" angel on Pachama and the \"Alexander Will\" angel on Khatabook (aggregator only).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2139194/000213919426000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://data.sec.gov/submissions/CIK0002139194.json\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/EGgR0C614YaU8_6qr_aKI1v77eM/appointments\n- https://techcrunch.com/2009/01/16/get-spoonfed-local-events-rated-by-the-crowd/\n- https://www.business-punk.com/calm-alex-will/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1721834/000172183420000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://startupintros.com/news/pachama-series-b-2022-05 (aggregator)\n- https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/alexander-will (aggregator)\n", "person:alexandre-momeni": "\n# Alexandre Momeni\n\n## One-line take\nGC's European-rooted frontier partner who sits on the firm's bets in Mistral, Black Forest Labs and Chai, and who also writes the small first cheques: GC led Stilla's $5M pre-seed, and a Meta acquisition followed eight months later.\n\n## Crux\nMomeni is the GC name that turns up both on Europe's frontier labs (Mistral, Black Forest Labs) and on Paris/Stockholm pre-seeds and seeds (Stilla, Tsuga). Their advantage is GC's balance sheet. GC can write a small first cheque, then return as a co-lead or with a growth cheque (Mistral: inception, then A, then co-led the €600M B). They are usually one of several GC names on a deal, never the sole public face. Partners like Hemant Taneja, Jeannette zu Fürstenberg and Elena Viboch are quoted or take the board seats, so Momeni's personal decision rights are unclear. They don't appear as lead on any frontier-lab round. In the labs GC is a follower (a16z led BFL's A) or the inside investor that doubles down.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Title confirmation comes from a portfolio company, not GC.** GC's own card gives no title. Tsuga's Series A release quotes them as \"Partner of General Catalyst\" [src](https://www.tsuga.com/resources/blog/pr/tsuga-raises-35-million-series-a) (2026-06-23). This is the only primary-source title found.\n- **Stilla: GC-led pre-seed, then acquired by Meta within months.** Stilla announced a \"$5M in Pre-Seed funding\" round \"led by General Catalyst\" [src](https://stilla.ai/blog/announcing-our-seed-funding) (2026-01-20). On 2026-09-09 the company said it is joining Meta [src](https://stilla.ai/blog/stilla-is-joining-meta). GC's Stilla page lists only zu Fürstenberg and Momeni, backed since 2026 [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/stilla). The angel list is a roll-call of European AI founders (ElevenLabs, Lovable, Legora, Synthesia, Sana, Hugging Face). That says something about the network this pre-seed came through.\n- **Tsuga: seed co-investor first, then a passive A.** The CEO says Singular and GC \"backed our $10 million seed round in December 2025\" [src](https://www.tsuga.com/resources/blog/we-raised-35-million-here-is-why-and-what-it-means-). Singular, not GC, led the $35M A. Momeni's thesis quote: \"a step-function change only possible because of their innovation\" [src](https://www.tsuga.com/resources/blog/pr/tsuga-raises-35-million-series-a).\n- **Black Forest Labs: GC was a follower, and the A was hidden.** BFL only disclosed its Series A in December 2025, as \"a previously unannounced Series A led by Andreessen Horowitz\", with GC as a participant [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier). GC is not named in the $300M B.\n- **Mistral: inside investor that tripled down.** Momeni co-authored GC's Series B post, which says GC partnered \"at their inception a year ago\" and co-led the €600M B [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/tripling-down-on-mistral-ai) (2024-06-11). GC is absent from seed press lists, so the \"inception\" claim is GC's own.\n- **Hippocratic AI was co-created by GC.** The Series C release quotes Taneja on GC \"which co-created Hippocratic AI\" [src](http://hippocraticai.com/hippocratic-ai-announces-series-c-funding-126-million). Momeni is named with Taneja on GC's page (backed since 2023) [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/hippocratic-ai). Tsuga lists Hippocratic as a customer [src](https://www.tsuga.com/), a small sign of cross-portfolio selling inside the GC network.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** They are on Mistral (seed-era entry, then co-lead of the B), Black Forest Labs (Series A participant) and Chai Discovery (GC co-led the $130M B). Those are frontier-lab and frontier-science deals, the core of my thesis. They also write pre-seed and seed cheques in AI-native automation (Stilla, an AI teammate for companies, close to the Serval pattern) and AI infra (Tsuga). GC can do $10M+ and growth cheques, which fits the \"principal/partner at a growth-capable fund\" segment I'm watching. Caveat: on the big lab rounds they look like the second or third GC name, not the decision-maker. I'd use them for European lab and science-team deal flow and co-invest access, not as a lead-picker.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up as an Iranian immigrant (their GC bio) [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/alexandre-momeni)\n- Goldman Sachs trading floor, dates not found [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/alexandre-momeni)\n- AI research at Stanford, dates not found [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/alexandre-momeni)\n- Built products at Nabla (Paris health-AI company), dates not found [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/alexandre-momeni)\n- Education: London School of Economics, École polytechnique, HEC Paris [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/alexandre-momeni)\n- General Catalyst: join date unconfirmed. Aggregators conflict (2021 vs \"Partner since 2022-03\"), and no source URL was recovered. Title \"Partner\" confirmed by Tsuga 2026-06-23 [src](https://www.tsuga.com/resources/blog/pr/tsuga-raises-35-million-series-a). TheOrg (aggregator) also lists Partner and prior companies Nabla, Goldman Sachs and Stanford [src](https://theorg.com/org/general-catalyst/org-chart/alexandre-momeni)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/alexandre-momeni | First-person bio; focus \"frontier intelligence, robotics, and science\" |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://twitter.com/AlexandreMomeni | Linked from GC bio; page not fetchable (402), follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/alexandre-momeni/ | Linked from GC bio; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Speaker listings at Slush and RAISE 2025 were noted in the GC roster pass, but no page or transcript was retrieved this session. The search budget ran out. Nothing verbatim found.\n\n## How they think\n- Their stated focus is \"frontier intelligence, robotics, and science\" [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/alexandre-momeni). The portfolio matches: labs (Mistral, BFL), AI-for-science (Chai), healthcare AI (Hippocratic, Aidoc, Phare, Sword), AI infra (Tsuga) and agents (Stilla).\n- The Tsuga quote shows a full-stack thesis: winners in AI infra rebuild \"from the storage layer to the business model\" [src](https://www.tsuga.com/resources/blog/pr/tsuga-raises-35-million-series-a).\n- Healthcare AI runs through the career: Nabla before GC, then Hippocratic, Phare and Aidoc at GC (inference from bio plus portfolio).\n- No contrarian views or admitted mistakes found in primary sources.\n\n## Read these first\n- GC bio (first person, the most direct statement of focus) — https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/alexandre-momeni\n- \"Tripling down on Mistral AI\" (co-author, 2024-06-11) — https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/tripling-down-on-mistral-ai\n- Tsuga Series A release (their only quote found) — https://www.tsuga.com/resources/blog/pr/tsuga-raises-35-million-series-a\n- Stilla pre-seed post (GC-led, with a list of European AI founder angels) — https://stilla.ai/blog/announcing-our-seed-funding\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing retrieved. X returned 402 and the search budget was exhausted.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mistral AI | Inception/seed-era entry (GC claim) | 2023 | no | Lightspeed led the seed | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/tripling-down-on-mistral-ai) |\n| Mistral AI | Series A €385M | 2023-12 | no (a16z led) | a16z, Lightspeed | [src](https://www.lw.com/en/news/2023/12/Latham%20Advises%20a16z%20on%20Mistral%20AI%20Series%20A%20Funding%20Round) |\n| Mistral AI | Series B €600M | 2024-06-11 | co-lead (GC with DST) | DST Global, a16z, Lightspeed, Nvidia | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/tripling-down-on-mistral-ai) |\n| Mistral AI | Series C €1.7B / Series D €3B | 2025-09-09 / 2026-09-08 | no (existing) | ASML led C; Samsung in D | [src](https://mistral.ai/fr/news/mistral-ai-raises-1-7-b-to-accelerate-technological-progress-with-ai) |\n| Black Forest Labs | Series A (undisclosed size) | disclosed 2025-12-01 | no (a16z led) | BroadLight, Creandum, Earlybird, Northzone, Nvidia | [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier) |\n| Chai Discovery | Series B $130M at $1.3B | 2025-12-15 | co-lead (GC with Oak HC/FT) | Thrive, OpenAI, Dimension, Menlo | [src](https://www.oakhcft.com/news/chai-discovery-announces-130-million-series-b-to-transform-molecular-discovery) |\n| Chai Discovery | Series C $400M at $3.8B | 2026-07-14 | no (Index led) | Index, Kleiner, Sequoia | [src](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design) |\n| Hippocratic AI | $53M round (GC + a16z) | ~2024-04 | co-investor | a16z | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/09/hippocratic-ai-raises-141m-for-creating-patient-facing-ai-agents/) |\n| Hippocratic AI | Series C $126M at $3.5B | 2025-11-03 | no (Avenir led) | CapitalG, a16z, Kleiner | [src](http://hippocraticai.com/hippocratic-ai-announces-series-c-funding-126-million) |\n| Stilla | Pre-seed $5M | 2026-01-20 | yes (GC) | European AI founder angels | [src](https://stilla.ai/blog/announcing-our-seed-funding) |\n| Tsuga | Seed $10M | 2025-12 | lead not stated | Singular | [src](https://www.tsuga.com/resources/blog/we-raised-35-million-here-is-why-and-what-it-means-) |\n| Tsuga | Series A $35M | 2026-06-23 | no (Singular led) | DST Global Partners, Quantumlight, Picus, Databricks Ventures | [src](https://www.tsuga.com/resources/blog/pr/tsuga-raises-35-million-series-a) |\n| Phare Health | not stated | backed since 2023 | unknown | GC: Chris Bischoff | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/phare-health) |\n| AIRE, Aidoc, Sword | not stated | — | unknown | — | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/alexandre-momeni) |\n\nPersonal attribution: GC portfolio pages list Momeni on Mistral (with Taneja, zu Fürstenberg, Clark, Kauffman), Chai (with Viboch, Neupane, Pai), Hippocratic (with Taneja), Stilla (with zu Fürstenberg) and Tsuga (with Quentin Clark). No source says they personally led any of these rounds.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Mistral:** GC says it partnered at inception (June 2023 seed, led by Lightspeed). It then participated in a16z's A and co-led the B with DST six months later, as a returning investor. Momeni is one of four bylined authors of the B post. Who sourced the deal is not stated. A Paris/Polytechnique network link to the founders is plausible but unverified (inference).\n- **Stilla:** GC led the $5M pre-seed in January 2026. The company launched about eight months before joining Meta (announced September 2026). Deal terms not disclosed. This is GC's fastest visible exit in Momeni's book.\n- **Tsuga:** Paris observability company with Datadog roots, founded 2024. GC and Singular backed the $10M seed in December 2025, and Singular led the A six months later.\n- **Chai:** GC was not in the seed (Thrive/OpenAI/Dimension) or the A (Menlo). It entered as a co-lead of the B, about 15 months after the seed. Taneja took the board seat and Viboch was quoted.\n- **BFL:** a16z led the A, which BFL kept quiet until its December 2025 B. GC was a non-lead participant.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hemant Taneja | GC CEO; co-credited on Mistral and Hippocratic | 2023 | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/hippocratic-ai |\n| Jeannette zu Fürstenberg | GC; co-credited on Mistral and Stilla | 2023 | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/stilla |\n| Quentin Clark | GC; co-credited on Mistral and Tsuga | 2023 | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/tsuga |\n| Christopher Kauffman | GC; co-credited on Mistral | 2023 | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/mistral-ai |\n| Elena Viboch | GC MD; co-credited on Chai | 2025 | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/chai-discovery |\n| Chris Bischoff | GC; co-credited on Phare Health | 2023 | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/phare-health |\n| Singular | Co-investor in Tsuga seed; led Tsuga A | 2025-12 | https://www.tsuga.com/resources/blog/we-raised-35-million-here-is-why-and-what-it-means- |\n| Gabriel-James Safar | Tsuga co-founder/CEO (portfolio founder) | 2025 | https://www.tsuga.com/resources/blog/pr/tsuga-raises-35-million-series-a |\n| Siavash Ghorbani, Kaj Drobin | Stilla founders (portfolio founders) | 2026 | https://stilla.ai/press |\n| Nabla | Former employer | — | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/alexandre-momeni |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact GC join date and promotion date to Partner. Aggregators conflict, so check LinkedIn directly.\n- Did Momeni personally source Stilla and Tsuga, the clearest cases where GC led or co-signed early?\n- Which \"AIRE\" is in the bio (needs-id)? What are the round and date?\n- Any podcast, X thread or talk transcript (Slush, RAISE 2025)? The search budget was exhausted this session.\n- Role on BFL: is Momeni the BFL relationship owner at GC? The bio says they work with BFL's founders.\n- Check sizes are not disclosed anywhere.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/alexandre-momeni\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/mistral-ai\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/chai-discovery\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/stilla\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/tsuga\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/hippocratic-ai\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/phare-health\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/tripling-down-on-mistral-ai\n- https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier\n- https://www.oakhcft.com/news/chai-discovery-announces-130-million-series-b-to-transform-molecular-discovery\n- https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design\n- http://hippocraticai.com/hippocratic-ai-announces-series-c-funding-126-million\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/09/hippocratic-ai-raises-141m-for-creating-patient-facing-ai-agents/\n- https://stilla.ai/blog/announcing-our-seed-funding\n- https://stilla.ai/blog/stilla-is-joining-meta\n- https://stilla.ai/press\n- https://www.tsuga.com/resources/blog/pr/tsuga-raises-35-million-series-a\n- https://www.tsuga.com/resources/blog/we-raised-35-million-here-is-why-and-what-it-means-\n- https://www.tsuga.com/about-us\n- https://theorg.com/org/general-catalyst/org-chart/alexandre-momeni (aggregator)\n- https://www.lw.com/en/news/2023/12/Latham%20Advises%20a16z%20on%20Mistral%20AI%20Series%20A%20Funding%20Round\n- https://mistral.ai/fr/news/mistral-ai-raises-1-7-b-to-accelerate-technological-progress-with-ai\n", "person:alexandre-perrin": "\n# Alexandre Perrin\n\n## One-line take\nThe person who, by his own account, actually wrote XTX's early Anthropic, Wayve and Terray tickets while running the unit alone; he has since left AI for a quantum-only Series A/B fund, so he is a source on XTX more than a source of AI money.\n\n## Crux\n- At XTX (2021-07 to 2023-06) he was a one-person investing team on the balance sheet of a single-owner trading firm: he sourced and diligenced, but sign-off sat with XTX leadership (the 2020 investment committee was the two co-CEOs plus internal experts) [src](https://qz.com/1923323/xtx-makes-venture-capital-investments-in-machine-learning-firms/). He self-reports deploying about £40M across 30+ ML startups and leading about half of them [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014).\n- Today he is Principal (not a partner) at Firgun Ventures, a London quantum-only Series A/B fund targeting $250M with a $70M first close anchored by the Qatar Investment Authority [src](https://thequantuminsider.com/2025/11/24/firgun-ventures-launches-250-million-vc-fund-to-invest-in-quantum-announces-first-70-million-close/). His bio there lists \"Ex-Head of Ventures\" at XTX Markets [src](https://www.firgun.vc/team).\n- At XTX he says he \"Led initial investments\" in these companies, meaning he ran XTX's side of the deal; the band was late seed to Series A across Europe, the US and Israel. Only IOMED is described as a round XTX co-led (with Philips). The follow-ons were led by others: TraceAir by PeakSpan (he claims about 4x ROI in a year), Logically by Vitruvian, Turbine by Merck VC [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014).\n- What constrains him: Firgun's mandate is \"Quantum Only\" [src](https://www.firgun.vc/portfolio), so he will not lead a frontier-AI seed from there. The two founders (Kris Naudts, Zeynep Koruturk) are the decision-makers; his own sign-off authority there is unconfirmed.\n\n## Differential insight\n- He ran XTX's arm \"as the sole full-time investor\" (self-reported), so the Anthropic stake behind FN London's \"striking gold with Anthropic\" headline was a deal sourced by a team of one, not a scaled CVC [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) [src](https://www.xtxmarkets.com/news/).\n- Timing correction: Anthropic's Series A closed 2021-05-28, before his 2021-07 start, so if his \"initial investment\" claim is right, XTX entered at the Series B (2022-04-29), the Series C (2023-05-23) or via secondary. Not at the Series A. The round is still unconfirmed (repo rounds rows; [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014)).\n- Return claim: he puts XTX's Anthropic position at \"~15× MOIC to date\" [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014). Self-reported, undated, and the cheque size is not given.\n- Realised exit: \"the exit of DiA Imaging (AI ultrasound analysis) to Philips\" [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014). Sale date not verified (Philips' page returned 404).\n- Scale: \"Deployed ~£40M in 30+ machine learning startups\" and \"Lead investor in ~50% of deals\"; observer on 12 boards [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014). XTX's own UK ventures vehicle held only £57.8m of investments at end-2025 [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/11074405/filing-history), so his roughly £40M was most of the book that exists today.\n- Side vehicles while at XTX: co-founded Cleverton Ventures (2020-11 to 2023-04), an investment club for family offices and HNWIs to \"co-invest €500K-€1M tickets in late Seed and Series A startups\" deal by deal, with him sourcing and running diligence. It overlapped his whole XTX tenure; he also says he co-founded a deep-tech fund, Scalebridge Capital, and built the Young VCs Dinners community (1,000+ members) [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014). All self-reported; Scalebridge's site would not load (TLS error, 2026-11-10).\n- A two-year gap: no role is listed between leaving XTX (2023-06) and joining Firgun (2025-07), apart from the Scalebridge co-founding, which is undated [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. He matters for one reason: he holds the internal history of XTX's entry into Anthropic, Wayve and Terray, and XTX is still writing large AI cheques (Flapping Airplanes seed and A, Gimlet B, Tenstorrent D/E). As a capital source for a $20M frontier-AI seed he is low fit today: Firgun is quantum-only, $70M first close, and he is a principal. Useful as (1) a reference call on how XTX's IC decides, (2) a quantum x AI co-investor in hardware-adjacent rounds, (3) a connector through Young VCs Dinners.\n\n## Background\n- HEC (Master in Strategic Management), Imperial College London MSc Finance 2012-13, CFA Level 2 (2015) [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014)\n- Analyst, growth PE, Keensight Capital, Paris, 2015-01 to 2016-02 [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014)\n- Analyst, LBO, Terra Firma Capital Partners, London, 2016-02 to 2017-03 [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014)\n- Venture and PE Investment Associate, Virgin Management (Branson family office), 2017-05 to 2019-01 [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014)\n- Partner and co-founder, Cleverton Ventures (investment club), 2020-11 to 2023-04 [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014)\n- Head of Ventures, XTX Ventures, 2021-07 to 2023-06; took over after Ekaterina Holt left in 2021 [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) [src](https://fxnewsgroup.com/forex-news/institutional/exclusive-xtx-markets-names-gabriele-papievyte-to-head-ventures-unit/). Successor Gabriele Papievyte joined 2023-08, two months after he left.\n- Principal, Firgun Ventures, London, since 2025-07 [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) [src](https://www.firgun.vc/team)\n- How he got the XTX seat: not found. The CV lists no role between 2019-01 and 2020-11.\n- Languages: French and English native, German and Spanish fluent (self-reported).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.firgun.vc/team | Firm team page, \"Principal\"; verified 2026-11-10. Inbound route is the firm contact form with a Startup/LP/VC dropdown; no partner email published ([src](https://www.firgun.vc/contact)) |\n| Substack / blog | https://firgun-ventures.beehiiv.com/ | Firm newsletter, not personal; no Perrin byline checked |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/alexandre-perrin-8/ | Linked from the Firgun team page (not fetched) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Firgun's \"Time to Talk Quantum\" episodes list other guests, not Perrin [src](https://www.firgun.vc/podcast) |\n\nAlso: HEC alumni CV [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014), which links a personal advisory site (matterhorn-advisory.com; returned 404 on 2026-11-10).\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was rate-limited this pass; only direct page fetches were possible.\n\n## How they think\n- His own CV uses \"he\" (third person) [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014).\n- Nothing first-hand found (no posts, talks or interviews). The CV frames him as a \"deep tech and AI\" investor; the XTX choices he lists lean to applied ML in health (Terray, BIOS, BlueSkeye, IOMED, Voiceitt) plus two large bets (Anthropic, Wayve).\n\n## Read these first\n- His HEC alumni CV, the only first-person source [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014)\n- Firgun's fund-launch coverage, for what his current shop can and can't do [src](https://thequantuminsider.com/2025/11/24/firgun-ventures-launches-250-million-vc-fund-to-invest-in-quantum-announces-first-70-million-close/)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nAll XTX rows are self-reported from his CV; only Terray's round is confirmed by a separate source placing XTX in it.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Anthropic | Undisclosed; Series B (2022-04) or C (2023-05) by timing, unconfirmed | 2021-07 to 2023-06 | no (unconfirmed) | | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n| Wayve | Undisclosed; possibly Series B ($200M, 2022-01-18), unconfirmed | 2021-07 to 2023-06 | unconfirmed | | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n| Terray Therapeutics | Series A ($60M) | 2022-02-15 | no | | [src](https://www.biospace.com/terray-therapeutics-now-has-80-million-to-fund-ai-driven-drug-discovery-efforts) |\n| BIOS Health | Undisclosed (initial) | 2021-07 to 2023-06 | unconfirmed | | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n| BlueSkeye AI | Undisclosed (initial) | 2021-07 to 2023-06 | unconfirmed | | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n| SWAPP | Undisclosed (initial) | 2021-07 to 2023-06 | unconfirmed | | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n| IOMED | Undisclosed (initial) | 2021-07 to 2023-06 | co-led with Philips (self-reported) | | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n| Voiceitt | Undisclosed (initial) | 2021-07 to 2023-06 | unconfirmed | | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n| TraceAir | Undisclosed (follow-on) | 2021-07 to 2023-06 | no | | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n| Logically | Undisclosed (follow-on) | 2021-07 to 2023-06 | no | | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n| Turbine | Undisclosed (follow-on) | 2021-07 to 2023-06 | no | | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n| DiA Imaging | Exit (sale to Philips); entry predates him | unconfirmed | no | | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n\nFirgun's 2026 portfolio (Photonic, Quantum Elements, Quantum Motion, Oxford Quantum Circuits, Universal Quantum, Silicon Quantum Computing, ZuriQ) is listed on the firm site [src](https://www.firgun.vc/portfolio), but no source names Perrin on any of those deals.\n\n## Deal stories\n- Anthropic: nothing beyond his own claim and FN London's headline (paywalled; fetch blocked) [src](https://www.xtxmarkets.com/news/). Who introduced XTX, round and cheque size are all unknown.\n- Turbine: a follow-on in his time; his successor Papievyte later took the Turbine board seat [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| XTX Ventures | Head of Ventures (former) | 2021-07 to 2023-06 | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n| Firgun Ventures | Principal | 2025-07 | [src](https://www.firgun.vc/team) |\n| Kris Naudts, Zeynep Koruturk | His founding partners at Firgun | 2025-07 | [src](https://www.firgun.vc/team) |\n| Cleverton Ventures | Partner and co-founder (former) | 2020-11 to 2023-04 | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n| Scalebridge Capital | Co-founder (undated, self-reported) | | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n| Virgin Management | Investment Associate (former) | 2017-05 to 2019-01 | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n| Ekaterina Holt / Gabriele Papievyte | Predecessor / successor as XTX ventures head | 2021 / 2023-08 | [src](https://fxnewsgroup.com/forex-news/institutional/exclusive-xtx-markets-names-gabriele-papievyte-to-head-ventures-unit/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which Anthropic round XTX entered, the cheque size, and whether XTX Ventures or another XTX entity holds it.\n- Whether Wayve was the 2022-01 Series B.\n- What he did 2019-01 to 2020-11 and 2023-06 to 2025-07; what Scalebridge Capital has done (site down).\n- Whether he is on any Firgun deal by name, and his carry or sign-off at Firgun.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014\n- https://www.firgun.vc/team\n- https://www.firgun.vc/portfolio\n- https://www.firgun.vc/podcast\n- https://thequantuminsider.com/2025/11/24/firgun-ventures-launches-250-million-vc-fund-to-invest-in-quantum-announces-first-70-million-close/\n- https://quantumcomputingreport.com/firgun-ventures-closes-70-million-first-round-to-focus-on-early-growth-stage-quantum-investment/\n- https://qz.com/1923323/xtx-makes-venture-capital-investments-in-machine-learning-firms/\n- https://fxnewsgroup.com/forex-news/institutional/exclusive-xtx-markets-names-gabriele-papievyte-to-head-ventures-unit/\n- https://www.xtxmarkets.com/news/\n- https://www.biospace.com/terray-therapeutics-now-has-80-million-to-fund-ai-driven-drug-discovery-efforts\n", "person:alfred-lin": "\n# Alfred Lin\n\n## One-line take\nSequoia's co-steward is personally the partner of record on two of the frontier-lab seeds that define the owner's lens, Physical Intelligence (2024) and Ineffable Intelligence ($1.1B, 2026), and also signed Anthropic's Series H, so they are the single person at Sequoia who can say yes to both a mega-seed and a mega-growth round.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** Lin combines operator credibility (CFO/COO at Zappos, CFO at LinkExchange) with Sequoia's top-down authority. Lin is now co-steward, so a founder pitching Lin gets the firm's decision-maker in the room. That showed when Lin and Sonya Huang flew to London to win Ineffable ([src](https://thenextweb.com/news/ineffable-intelligence-david-silver-sequoia-nvidia-5-billion)). On the lab mega-seed Lin pairs with the AI specialist Sonya Huang ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-era-of-experience)). On AI-services seeds (Crosby, Finch, Rowspace) Lin is the only partner listed ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/crosby/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/finch/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/rowspace/)).\n- **What constrains them:** Lin's attention is spread across 30+ current board/portfolio seats (Airbnb, DoorDash, Faire, Kalshi, Whatnot, Citadel Securities and more) ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/alfred-lin/)), and they now co-run a firm that just cut back to seven GPs and shut Arc, Scouts and London ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)). Lin's AI seeds are few and huge, or are AI-native services (law, legal aid, finance data) sourced through Sequoia's own portfolio alumni.\n- **What they'd never do:** No evidence of Lin taking an AI-chip or compute bet (no Etched, fal or Ricursive credit on the Lin page; those sit with Huang and others). Lin is not first-check money: on Physical Intelligence, Thrive led and Sequoia followed ([src](https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Ineffable was framed as a contrarian anti-pre-training bet, signed by Lin.** The Huang/Lin post says \"the bet is genuinely contrarian.\" It stresses \"no pre-training, no human data to shortcut the system\" ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-era-of-experience), 2026-04-27). The co-steward put their name on a lab that rejects the dominant LLM recipe.\n- **Sequoia was the original sole lead and later shared it.** In February 2026 TNW reported Sequoia was \"leading\" a $1B round at ~$4B pre-money, with Nvidia, Google and Microsoft in talks ([src](https://thenextweb.com/news/david-silver-is-chasing-superhuman-intelligence-with-a-1bn-seed), 2026-02-19). At close (2026-04-27) Lightspeed was co-lead and the price had moved to $5.1B ([src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/27/deepmind-ineffable-intelligence-record-seed-funding-nvidia-google.html)). So Lin's term sheet set the anchor, and a second lead and a higher price followed within ~10 weeks.\n- **In-person close.** Lin and Huang \"flown to London personally to meet Silver and secure the deal\" ([src](https://thenextweb.com/news/ineffable-intelligence-david-silver-sequoia-nvidia-5-billion)). Huang's podcast outreach to Silver dates to 2024 ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/david-silver-and-lasse-espeholt-of-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-age-of-experience)). The relationship was Huang's, and Lin's role was to bring the firm's top authority to the close (inference from the pairing; no source states the division of labor).\n- **Lin's AI-services seeds are sourced through Sequoia alumni networks, not labs.** Crosby: Sequoia knew John Sarihan from Ramp, and Ryan Daniels had worked with Sequoia's GC at Cooley ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-crosby-a-law-firm-at-the-speed-of-ai/), 2025-06-17). Finch: founders built marketplaces alongside \"DoorDash friends of Sequoia\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-finch-ai-powered-justice/), 2025-04-16). This is the DoorDash board seat working as a talent pipeline.\n- **Lin publicly backs the \"AI-native services firm\" thesis.** In the Crosby post Lin calls AI \"a competitive accelerant\" for a law firm that runs \"at the speed of software\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-crosby-a-law-firm-at-the-speed-of-ai/)). In the Finch post: \"AI automation is disrupting the world of law.\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-finch-ai-powered-justice/)).\n- **Anthropic thesis is enterprise process capture.** Lin's Series H quote: \"Claude is learning how businesses actually operate\" ([src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h), 2026-05-28).\n- **A known miss turned into a win: DoorDash.** Lin passed on DoorDash's seed as too crowded. A dinner hosted by Aileen Lee later changed Lin's mind, and Sequoia did the Series A ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/18/alfred-lin-sequoia-partner-ipo-airbnb-doordash/)). Lin: \"There was something I just couldn't get out of my head.\" This is a sign that Lin re-engages after a pass.\n- **Decision heuristics (own words).** \"I try to be not only fast, but deliberate about Type 1 decisions.\" and \"Making mistakes isn't the sin.\" ([src](https://articles.sequoiacap.com/2018-08-01-alfred-lin), 2018-08-01).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Lin is personally named on two lab seeds squarely in THESIS.md: Physical Intelligence (robotics foundation model, $70M seed, 2024) and Ineffable Intelligence (RL superintelligence lab, $1.1B seed, 2026). Both are the \"very large seed for a new lab\" shape the owner writes $20M checks into. Lin also leads AI-native automation seeds (Crosby, Finch, Rowspace), which matches the owner's Serval-type interest. Caveats: Lin has no AI-chip/compute deal credit, and as co-steward Lin is hard to reach and unlikely to co-invest with a solo-sized check except on a lab mega-seed where Sequoia wants friendly syndicate partners. The best route in on labs is Sonya Huang, the specialist Lin co-signed Ineffable with.\n\n## Background\n- Born in Taiwan, moved to the US at age six ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/18/alfred-lin-sequoia-partner-ipo-airbnb-doordash/)).\n- Harvard; met Tony Hsieh at a Harvard dorm party ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/18/alfred-lin-sequoia-partner-ipo-airbnb-doordash/)).\n- Co-founded Venture Frogs (incubator/seed fund); worked at Tellme Networks ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/alfred-lin/)).\n- CFO of LinkExchange (founded 1996, Sequoia-backed 1997, sold to Microsoft 1998) ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/18/alfred-lin-sequoia-partner-ipo-airbnb-doordash/)).\n- CFO (later COO per firm bio) of Zappos, sold to Amazon in 2009 for $1.2B ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/18/alfred-lin-sequoia-partner-ipo-airbnb-doordash/)).\n- Sequoia partner since 2010; 2021 Forbes Midas List #1 ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/18/alfred-lin-sequoia-partner-ipo-airbnb-doordash/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/alfred-lin/)).\n- Co-steward with Pat Grady after Roelof Botha was voted out \"last fall\" (c. fall 2025). Named one of seven owner-GPs in the 2026-11-04 memo ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/alfred-lin/ | Firm page; email Early-AL@sequoiacap.com listed (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | \"Outlier's Path\" (listed on firm page, no link) | nothing found |\n| X | nothing found (not linked from firm page) | |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Sequoia \"Crucible Moments\" (DoorDash episode) | https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/crucible-moments-doordash |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-01-16 — The Knowledge Project #212 — \"Alfred Lin: How to Think (and Work) Like a World Class Investor\" — https://fs.blog/knowledge-project-podcast/alfred-lin/ — has an AI segment at 01:35:24; transcript paywalled, nothing quoted here.\n- 2021-01-26 — 20VC — Interview with Alfred Lin — https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/alfred-lin/ — input vs output metrics; founders should be \"customer-obsessed and competitor aware.\"\n- Undated — Startup Grind 1:1 with Sequoia's Alfred Lin — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PDAcJ-ih4wA — not reviewed.\n- Undated — Stanford GSB, \"Culture with Brian Chesky and Alfred Lin\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RfWgVWGEuGE — not reviewed.\n\n## How they think\n- Founder-market fit plus \"would I work for this founder\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/alfred-lin/)).\n- Founders need \"both fire and ice\" ([src](https://articles.sequoiacap.com/2018-08-01-alfred-lin)).\n- Input vs output metrics; sceptical of topline GMV ([src](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/alfred-lin/)).\n- AI view on the record: contrarian RL-from-experience labs (Ineffable), AI-native services firms (Crosby, Finch), enterprise context capture (Anthropic). See the sources above.\n- Right: Airbnb board, DoorDash Series A after passing on the seed ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/18/alfred-lin-sequoia-partner-ipo-airbnb-doordash/)). Wrong: the DoorDash seed pass, admitted by Lin.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Ineffable partnership post (Huang and Lin, 2026-04-27): the contrarian anti-pre-training thesis ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-era-of-experience)).\n2. Crosby partnership post (2025-06-17): the AI-native law-firm thesis and how the founders were known ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-crosby-a-law-firm-at-the-speed-of-ai/)).\n3. Finch partnership post (2025-04-16): the AI legal-services thesis and the DoorDash-network sourcing ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-finch-ai-powered-justice/)).\n4. Fortune profile (2021-05-18): operator background and the DoorDash pass-then-invest story ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/18/alfred-lin-sequoia-partner-ipo-airbnb-doordash/)).\n5. Seven Questions (2018): decision heuristics ([src](https://articles.sequoiacap.com/2018-08-01-alfred-lin)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: no X or LinkedIn account was located or linked from the firm page, and Fortune describes Lin as keeping a low profile ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/18/alfred-lin-sequoia-partner-ipo-airbnb-doordash/)). Public output runs through Sequoia partnership posts and podcasts.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI-relevant deals only. Full list on the firm page ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/alfred-lin/)).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ineffable Intelligence | Seed, $1.1B at $5.1B post (first round; company founded late 2025) | 2026-04-27 | Co-lead (Sequoia with Lightspeed). Sequoia was reported sole lead in Feb 2026. Sequoia pairing: Lin + Sonya Huang | Lightspeed (Mhatre, Jain, Moyroud); Index (Rimer, Stevenson); Nvidia; Google; DST Global; UK Sovereign AI Fund | [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/27/deepmind-ineffable-intelligence-record-seed-funding-nvidia-google.html) [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-era-of-experience) |\n| Anthropic | Series H, $65B at $965B post | 2026-05-28 | Lead (one of four: Altimeter, Dragoneer, Greenoaks, Sequoia); Lin quoted | Capital Group, Coatue, D1, GIC, ICONIQ, XN (co-leads) | [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h) |\n| Anthropic | Series G, $30B at $380B post | 2026-02-12 | No (participant); partner not named | GIC, Coatue, D. E. Shaw, Dragoneer, Founders Fund, ICONIQ, MGX | [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-30-billion-series-g-funding-380-billion-post-money-valuation) |\n| Physical Intelligence | Seed, $70M | 2024-03-12 | No (Thrive led). Lin is Sequoia's partner of record (\"Partnered 2024\") | Thrive (lead), Khosla, Lux, OpenAI Startup Fund | [src](https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/) [src](https://sequoiacap.com/companies/physical-intelligence/) |\n| Crosby | Seed, $5.8M (partnered 2024; announced 2025) | 2025-06-17 | Yes (Sequoia \"proud to lead\"; BCV also claims lead). Lin sole Sequoia partner listed; an earlier repo row records a Josephine Chen co-byline, not confirmed in this pass | Bain Capital Ventures | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-crosby-a-law-firm-at-the-speed-of-ai/) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/crosby/) |\n| Finch | Seed | 2025-04-16 | Yes (Sequoia-led; Lin byline) | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-finch-ai-powered-justice/) |\n| Rowspace | Pre-Seed/Seed (partnered 2025) | 2025 | unknown | unknown | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/rowspace/) |\n| Fireworks AI | \"Early\" (partnered 2022). Sequoia later led the $52M Series B (2024-07-11), partner not named | 2022 / 2024-07-11 | Series B: Sequoia led | Benchmark, Databricks Ventures (prior); Nvidia, AMD, MongoDB (B) | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/alfred-lin/) [src](https://siliconangle.com/2024/07/11/fireworks-ai-raises-52m-led-sequoia-522b-valuation) |\n| OpenAI | Growth (partnered 2021); exact transaction not identified | 2021 | No | Tiger Global (2021 tender, per repo row) | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/companies/openai/) |\n| Firetiger | Pre-Seed/Seed (partnered 2025; since acquired) | 2025 | unknown | unknown | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/alfred-lin/) |\n| Otter | Early (partnered 2021) | 2021 | unknown | unknown | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/alfred-lin/) |\n| Bunkerhill Health | Pre-Seed/Seed (partnered 2023) | 2023 | unknown | unknown | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/alfred-lin/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Ineffable Intelligence.** Silver founded the lab in late 2025 ([src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/27/deepmind-ineffable-intelligence-record-seed-funding-nvidia-google.html)). By 2026-02-19 Sequoia was reported leading a $1B seed at ~$4B pre ([src](https://thenextweb.com/news/david-silver-is-chasing-superhuman-intelligence-with-a-1bn-seed)). Lin and Huang flew to London to close ([src](https://thenextweb.com/news/ineffable-intelligence-david-silver-sequoia-nvidia-5-billion)). It closed 2026-04-27 at $1.1B / $5.1B with Lightspeed as co-lead and Index alongside. Index says it had known Silver \"by reputation for years\" ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-superlearner-investing-in-david-silver-and-ineffable-intelligence/)). Timing: Sequoia was the first named lead, so it was effectively first money. Huang's 2024 podcast invitation to Silver was the earliest Sequoia touchpoint found ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/david-silver-and-lasse-espeholt-of-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-age-of-experience)).\n- **Physical Intelligence.** Sequoia came in at the $70M seed alongside Thrive (lead), Khosla, Lux and OpenAI. All five were in the first round, so no one was earlier ([src](https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/)). Sequoia is not named in the Series A ($400M, 2024-11) investor lists. The company still thanks Sequoia ([src](https://www.pi.website/)). How Lin specifically got the allocation: nothing found.\n- **Crosby.** Sequoia knew co-founder Sarihan from Ramp, a Sequoia portfolio company. Co-founder Daniels had worked with Sequoia's GC at Cooley ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-crosby-a-law-firm-at-the-speed-of-ai/)). The company page says partnered 2024, ahead of the 2025-06 announcement ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/crosby/)).\n- **Anthropic.** Sequoia went from participant in Series G (2026-02) to one of four leads on Series H (2026-05), with Lin as quoted partner ([src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h)). That came after Sequoia had been an OpenAI investor since 2021.\n- **DoorDash (non-AI, but shows behaviour).** Lin passed on the seed, then was won over at an Aileen Lee dinner and did the A ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/18/alfred-lin-sequoia-partner-ipo-airbnb-doordash/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sequoia Capital | GP; co-steward; one of 7 owner-GPs | 2010 (partner); c. 2025 (co-steward) | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/) |\n| Pat Grady | Co-steward of Sequoia | c. 2025 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/) |\n| Sonya Huang | Co-author and co-partner on Ineffable | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/ineffable-intelligence/) |\n| David Silver (Ineffable) | Portfolio founder; seed co-lead | 2026 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-era-of-experience) |\n| Tony Hsieh | Co-founder/operating partner (LinkExchange, Zappos, Venture Frogs) | c. 1990s | [src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/18/alfred-lin-sequoia-partner-ipo-airbnb-doordash/) |\n| Brian Chesky (Airbnb) | Board member; Chesky asked for Lin on the board | 2012 | [src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/18/alfred-lin-sequoia-partner-ipo-airbnb-doordash/) |\n| Tony Xu (DoorDash) | Board member | 2014 | [src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/18/alfred-lin-sequoia-partner-ipo-airbnb-doordash/) |\n| Aileen Lee (Cowboy Ventures) | Hosted the dinner that brought Lin back to DoorDash | c. 2014 | [src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/18/alfred-lin-sequoia-partner-ipo-airbnb-doordash/) |\n| Lightspeed (Mhatre, Jain, Moyroud) | Ineffable seed co-lead | 2026 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/) |\n| Bain Capital Ventures | Crosby seed co-lead | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-crosby-a-law-firm-at-the-speed-of-ai/) |\n| Thrive Capital | PI seed lead (Sequoia co-investor) | 2024 | [src](https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who at Sequoia sourced Physical Intelligence: Lin, or another partner with Lin taking the seat? Did Lin follow on in later PI rounds?\n- What did Lin say in the Knowledge Project AI segment (01:35:24)? The transcript is paywalled.\n- Was Lin the partner on the Fireworks Series B lead (2024)? How did Sequoia first enter in 2022?\n- Is Josephine Chen a co-author on the Crosby post (earlier repo row says so; this pass's fetch named only Lin)?\n- Rowspace and Firetiger: round size, co-investors, and who Firetiger was acquired by.\n- Was the move from sole lead (Feb) to co-lead with Lightspeed (Apr) on Ineffable Sequoia's choice or Silver's?\n- Does the 2026-11 restructuring (closing London) change how Lin will source European labs after Ineffable?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/alfred-lin/\n- https://sequoiacap.com/companies/physical-intelligence/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/ineffable-intelligence/\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-era-of-experience\n- https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/david-silver-and-lasse-espeholt-of-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-age-of-experience\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/ineffable-intelligence-david-silver-sequoia-nvidia-5-billion\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/david-silver-is-chasing-superhuman-intelligence-with-a-1bn-seed\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/27/deepmind-ineffable-intelligence-record-seed-funding-nvidia-google.html\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-superlearner-investing-in-david-silver-and-ineffable-intelligence/\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-30-billion-series-g-funding-380-billion-post-money-valuation\n- https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/\n- https://www.pi.website/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-crosby-a-law-firm-at-the-speed-of-ai/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/crosby/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-finch-ai-powered-justice/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/finch/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/rowspace/\n- https://siliconangle.com/2024/07/11/fireworks-ai-raises-52m-led-sequoia-522b-valuation\n- https://sequoiacap.com/companies/openai/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/\n- https://fortune.com/2021/05/18/alfred-lin-sequoia-partner-ipo-airbnb-doordash/\n- https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/alfred-lin/\n- https://articles.sequoiacap.com/2018-08-01-alfred-lin\n- https://fs.blog/knowledge-project-podcast/alfred-lin/\n", "person:ali-ghodsi": "\n# Ali Ghodsi\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (strategics-2 sweep). Method follows [firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md): separate the pools, say who signs, say how the strategic tie attaches. Companion firm page: [firms/databricks-ventures.md](../firms/databricks-ventures.md).*\n\n## One-line take\nGhodsi is the single decision gate on three separate pools of Databricks-related money: the Databricks Ventures (DV) checks, Databricks' own AI acquisitions and model partnerships, and a small personal angel book in Berkeley-orbit AI-infra seeds. The last pool is the one the owner can actually sit beside.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he runs a company valued at $190B (Coatue-led $5B round, 2026-08-13, [letsdatascience](https://letsdatascience.com/news/databricks-closes-5-billion-funding-at-190-billion-2cbf4001)) whose balance sheet he can point at any AI asset. That makes him the buyer of last resort for data and AI-infra founders (MosaicML, Tabular, Neon) and a kingmaker for open-source inference (Inferact, RadixArk via DV). \"Databricks CEO Ali Ghodsi signs off on every investment\" ([Fortune 2024-05-21](https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund)).\n- **Founder-led dealmaking:** he negotiates big deals himself. He texted Naveen Rao \"mind doing a Zoom?\" and signed the MosaicML term sheet on 2023-05-29, about two months after they first met at Cerebral Valley on 2023-03-30 ([Fortune 2023-07-21](https://fortune.com/2023/07/21/inside-how-biggest-ai-deal-came-together-ceos)).\n- **His view of models:** he calls LLMs a commodity (\"AI today, like large language models are actually a commodity\", [TBPN transcript, 2025-12-16](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-12-16/databricks-hits-48b-revenue-run-rate-with-55-growth-as-ali-ghodsi-argues-llms-are-already-commoditized/transcript)) and calls data \"the data ingredient of AI\" ([Fortune 2024-06-05](https://www.fortune.com/2024/06/05/databricks-ceo-ali-ghosdi-on-snowflake-rivalry-and-the-why-behind-databricks-latest-billion-dollar-deal)). So Databricks partners with frontier labs commercially (Anthropic, OpenAI) and does not buy equity in them. It pays up for the data, inference and tooling layers around the models.\n- **Constraint:** DV is a follower (\"We don't lead rounds\", Ferguson, [GCV 2026](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/information-technology/databricks-ventures-building-ecosystem/?format=md)). Databricks itself is still private and is fighting \"a war for AI talent right now, especially with OpenAI, Anthropic\" ([Fortune via Yahoo, 2024-12-17](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/databricks-ceo-ali-ghodsi-raising-202932676.html)). That fight makes acquihires (MosaicML) more attractive to him than minority stakes.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** price a frontier-lab round as a corporate. No Databricks equity in Anthropic, OpenAI or any other frontier lab was found. The only lab-scale seed Databricks joined is Unconventional AI, which was founded by its own departing head of AI.\n\n## The pools he controls or signs\n| Pool | Whose money | What he does | Size and stage | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| (1) Databricks Ventures: Lakehouse Fund (2021), Databricks AI Fund (2024), AI Accelerator (2025) | Databricks balance sheet, single-LP, evergreen | Final sign-off on every check. Andrew Ferguson (VP) runs it, Phil Shin does the deals, and Philip Kirk (SVP corp dev and ventures, since April 2026) sits above them | Small follow-on tickets, Series A and later, with exceptions at seed (Inferact, RadixArk) | [Fortune 2024](https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund), [firm page](../firms/databricks-ventures.md) |\n| (2) Databricks corporate M&A and direct strategic stakes | Databricks balance sheet, funded by mega-rounds: $10B Series J (Dec 2024), $4B Series L at $134B (2025-12-16), $5B at $190B (2026-08-13) | Signs term sheets personally (MosaicML). Announced the Unconventional AI stake | MosaicML $1.3B (2023), Tabular reportedly nearly $2B for about $1M ARR (2024), Neon about $1B (2025), Tecton (2025) | [Fortune 2023](https://fortune.com/2023/07/21/inside-how-biggest-ai-deal-came-together-ceos), [TechCrunch 2024-08-14](https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/14/databricks-reportedly-paid-2-billion-in-tabular-acquisition), [CNBC 2025-12-16](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/16/databricks-funding-valuation.html) |\n| (3) Model-provider commercial deals (not equity) | Databricks P&L | Face of the deals | Anthropic: five years, $100M joint go-to-market (2025-03-26). OpenAI: expected to generate more than $100M in revenue (2025-09-25) | [the-decoder](https://the-decoder.com/anthropic-and-databricks-close-100-million-dollar-deal-for-ai-agents/), [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=712882) |\n| (4) Personal angel checks | His own money | Named participant, never lead | Seeds and one Series A. Amounts not disclosed | see Portfolio |\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He makes big deals alone, by text.** \"mind doing a Zoom?\" was his text to Rao. Rao said the founder-led approach won him over, noting \"Ghodsi was super involved directly with many things\" ([Fortune 2023-07-21](https://fortune.com/2023/07/21/inside-how-biggest-ai-deal-came-together-ceos)).\n- **He buys, runs, then funds the spin-out.** Databricks bought Nervana founder Naveen Rao's MosaicML (2023), made him head of AI, and when Rao left in September 2025 to build AI hardware, Databricks invested in the new company: \"Databricks is also investing in Rao's startup\" ([ARN 2025-09-14](https://www.arnnet.com.au/?p=4059073), [InfoWorld 2025-09-13](https://infoworld.com/article/4056636/databricks-at-a-crossroads-can-its-ai-strategy-prevail-without-naveen-rao.html); first reported by [Bloomberg 2025-09-12](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-09-12/databricks-ai-chief-to-exit-launch-a-new-computer-startup), paywalled, not read). That became Unconventional AI's $475M seed at $4.5B (2025-12). This settles the firm page's \"unconfirmed\" flag on Databricks being in that round, at least as a stated intent. A reported Ghodsi LinkedIn line saying the talks began six months earlier came only from a search summary, **unconfirmed**.\n- **Models are a commodity, so he backs the layer around them.** \"large language models are actually a commodity\" ([TBPN transcript 2025-12-16](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-12-16/databricks-hits-48b-revenue-run-rate-with-55-growth-as-ali-ghodsi-argues-llms-are-already-commoditized/transcript)). This lines up with what the parent funds: inference engines (Inferact/vLLM, RadixArk/SGLang), not labs.\n- **He was skeptical of his own first lead investor.** Of Ben Horowitz: \"we don't want that guy\". On the pitch: \"I think our first pitch was really, really bad.\" The founders asked for about $200K. a16z gave $14M and asked for exclusivity, after Berkeley's Scott Shenker vouched for Matei Zaharia ([Fortune 2023-03-02](https://fortune.com/2023/03/02/databricks-ben-horowitz-data-cloud-startup/)). He shows up in an a16z-led seed again in 2026 (Runta).\n- **He was cautious on the AI capex cycle while raising $4B.** He said he was \"a little bit worried that we're ahead of ourselves\" ([CNBC 2025-12-16](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/16/databricks-funding-valuation.html)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, through the personal pool and the inference-layer pattern. Medium as a corporate.**\n- **Co-investor:** he angels into the same AI-infra seeds the owner targets: Sycamore $65M seed (Coatue and Lightspeed led), SkyPilot $20M+ seed (Lux led), Runta $20M seed (a16z/Casado led) and Factory $5M seed (Sequoia and Lux led). His name on a cap table works as a Databricks-distribution signal more than as capital.\n- **Price-setter:** no. Neither DV nor Ghodsi leads frontier rounds. Databricks joined Unconventional AI's seed on terms set by a16z and Lightspeed.\n- **Acquirer:** yes, and the main reason to know him. For a data, inference or tooling seed the owner backs, Databricks is a likely buyer and pays far above revenue (Tabular, about $1M ARR, reportedly nearly $2B).\n- **LP:** no. DV has no outside LPs, and no personal LP commitments were found.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** Unconventional AI (seed, $475M), Inferact (seed, $150M, via DV), RadixArk (seed, $100M, via DV), Sycamore and SkyPilot (personal angel).\n\n## Background\n- Born 1978 in Iran and raised in Sweden. MSc and MBA, Mid-Sweden University (2002). PhD in distributed computing, KTH (2006). Assistant professor at KTH (2008–2009). Visiting scholar at UC Berkeley (2009), where he worked in AMPLab on Spark and Mesos. Now an adjunct professor at Berkeley ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Ghodsi)).\n- Co-founded Peerialism AB in Stockholm ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Ghodsi)).\n- Co-founded Databricks in 2013 and led engineering. Became CEO in 2016 ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Ghodsi)).\n- Launched DV's Lakehouse Fund in December 2021, backing companies \"building the future of data, analytics, and AI, especially those within the open source community\" ([GCV 2021](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/databricks-sets-up-venture-fund/?format=md)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.databricks.com/blog/author/ali-ghodsi | Databricks blog author page (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/alighodsi | Unverified: returns 200 on 2026-11-10, but X serves 200 for any handle behind its login wall; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/alighodsi | Unverified (999 bot block, 2026-11-10). Main channel for announcements (Rao exit post, 2025-09-13, per [InfoWorld](https://infoworld.com/article/4056636/databricks-at-a-crossroads-can-its-ai-strategy-prevail-without-naveen-rao.html)); URL not loaded (login wall) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Frequent guest: TBPN, BG2, Cerebral Valley (Newcomer), Bloomberg Technology |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023 — Newcomer / Cerebral Valley — \"Databricks CEO Ali Ghodsi & MosaicML Founder Naveen Rao Speak at Cerebral Valley\" — https://podcast.newcomer.co/episode/databricks-ceo-ali-ghodsi-mosaicml-founder-naveen-rao-speak-at-cerebral-valley — the story of the MosaicML deal from both sides (not transcribed here)\n- 2025-12-16 — TBPN — Databricks $4.8B run rate — [transcript](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-12-16/databricks-hits-48b-revenue-run-rate-with-55-growth-as-ali-ghodsi-argues-llms-are-already-commoditized/transcript) — LLMs are already a commodity; Chinese open models (Qwen) are pushing prices down\n- 2026-06 — Data + AI Summit and Bloomberg Technology — said AGI is already here by the 2009 AMPLab definition (aggregator coverage only, [cryptobriefing](https://cryptobriefing.com/databricks-ceo-ghodsi-says-agi-is-here/); **not verified against video**)\n- BG2 guest interview (Databricks and Glean) — https://metacast.app/podcast/bg2pod-with-brad-gerstner-and-bill-gurley/EvPdpvGM/ai-enterprise-databricks-and-glean-bg2-guest-interview/clVFFBLP — not reviewed\n\n## How they think\n- Data, not models, is the moat: \"It's the data ingredient of AI\" ([Fortune 2024-06-05](https://www.fortune.com/2024/06/05/databricks-ceo-ali-ghosdi-on-snowflake-rivalry-and-the-why-behind-databricks-latest-billion-dollar-deal)).\n- On the pace of change, he says people overestimate disruption in the short term and underestimate it in the long run (same source).\n- Contrarian: AGI is \"already here\", and the bottleneck is context and adoption, not intelligence (aggregator, see above).\n- Calls: he bet on open table formats (Tabular/Iceberg) and open models (DBRX, 2024) and then moved to multi-model partnerships with Anthropic and OpenAI in 2025, consistent with treating models as a commodity.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Fortune 2023-07-21: inside the MosaicML deal](https://fortune.com/2023/07/21/inside-how-biggest-ai-deal-came-together-ceos) — how he buys.\n2. [Fortune 2024-05-21: DV's second fund](https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund) — the approval gate.\n3. [TBPN 2025-12-16 transcript](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-12-16/databricks-hits-48b-revenue-run-rate-with-55-growth-as-ali-ghodsi-argues-llms-are-already-commoditized/transcript) — why he won't buy labs.\n4. [Fortune 2023-03-02: Ben Horowitz and the Series A](https://fortune.com/2023/03/02/databricks-ben-horowitz-data-cloud-startup/) — his origin with a16z.\n\n## Social engagement\nLinkedIn-first, for company announcements and Snowflake rivalry posts. TechCrunch reported employees were asked to like or share \"their CEO's LinkedIn posts dunking on Snowflake\" ([TechCrunch 2024-08-14](https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/14/databricks-reportedly-paid-2-billion-in-tabular-acquisition)). X activity not checked.\n\n## Portfolio\nPersonal angel checks (his own money):\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Factory | Seed ($5M) | 2023-11-02 | no | Sequoia, Lux (co-leads) | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2023/11/02/factory-wants-to-use-ai-to-automate-the-software-dev-lifecycle/) |\n| Brain Co | Series A ($30M) | 2025-09-10 | no | Gil Capital, Affinity Partners (reported leads) | [CB Insights (aggregator, unconfirmed in primary)](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/ali-ghodsi) |\n| Sycamore | Seed ($65M) | 2026-03-30 | no | Coatue, Lightspeed (leads); 8VC; Dell Technologies Capital | [Sycamore](https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed) |\n| Runta | Seed ($20M) | 2026-07-16 | no | a16z (Martin Casado lead); Jeff Dean; Fei-Fei Li; Thomas Wolf; Ram Shriram | [Runta](https://runta.com/blog/runta-the-execution-layer-for-agents/) |\n| SkyPilot | Seed ($20M+) | 2026-07-21 | no | Lux (lead); Amplify; Coatue; Jeff Dean; Guillermo Rauch; Amjad Masad; Clem Delangue | [SkyPilot](https://skypilot.ai/blog/skypilot-the-company) |\n\nCorporate deals he approved or announced (Databricks money, not his; rows sit under `databricks-ventures` and `databricks`):\n| Company | Round / deal | Date | Relation | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| MosaicML | Acquisition ($1.3B) | term sheet 2023-05-29 | target; Rao became Databricks head of AI | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2023/07/21/inside-how-biggest-ai-deal-came-together-ceos) |\n| Mistral AI | Series A participation plus partnership | 2024-03 | supplier | [firm page](../firms/databricks-ventures.md) |\n| Einblick | Acquisition (terms undisclosed) | 2024-01-30 | target; co-founder Tim Kraska is his Berkeley research peer | [Fortune](https://www.fortune.com/2024/01/30/databricks-acquiring-ai-startup-einblick-ceo-ali-ghodsi-embraces-asymmetric-strategy) |\n| Tabular | Acquisition (reportedly nearly $2B) | 2024-06 | target | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/14/databricks-reportedly-paid-2-billion-in-tabular-acquisition) |\n| Anthropic | Commercial: five years, $100M | 2025-03-26 | supplier (no equity found) | [the-decoder](https://the-decoder.com/anthropic-and-databricks-close-100-million-dollar-deal-for-ai-agents/) |\n| OpenAI | Commercial: more than $100M | 2025-09-25 | supplier (no equity found) | [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=712882) |\n| Unconventional AI | Seed ($475M at $4.5B) | 2025-12 | spin-out of his own head of AI | [ARN](https://www.arnnet.com.au/?p=4059073) |\n| Inferact (vLLM) | Seed ($150M) via DV | 2026-01-22 | supplier; co-founder Ion Stoica | [Inferact](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **MosaicML (2023):** Ghodsi and Rao met on 2023-03-30 at the Cerebral Valley VIP dinner. Ghodsi texted to start talks in May, and they spoke on May 10. Rao said Databricks was the only buyer that made sense and pushed on price. Ghodsi signed the term sheet on May 29, and the $1.3B deal was announced in June ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2023/07/21/inside-how-biggest-ai-deal-came-together-ceos)).\n- **Unconventional AI (2025):** Rao left Databricks in September 2025 and moved to an advisory role. Databricks said at once that it would invest. The company raised a $475M seed at $4.5B in December, with a16z and Lightspeed leading ([ARN](https://www.arnnet.com.au/?p=4059073), [TechCrunch 2025-10-03](https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/03/sources-naveen-raos-new-ai-hardware-startup-targets-5b-valuation-with-backing-from-a16z)).\n- **Tabular (2024):** a bidding war with Snowflake, as Fortune's and CNBC's sources reported. Bloomberg reported a price of nearly $2B for a company with about $1M ARR ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/14/databricks-reportedly-paid-2-billion-in-tabular-acquisition)).\n\n### Update 2026-11-10 (second pass, strategics-2b): how M&A and DV decisions run through him\n- **He holds a personal veto on acquisitions.** On the a16z podcast with Ben Horowitz (2025-10-15) he described blocking a deal for a successful company more than once, because he judged its staff would dilute Databricks' talent: \"I always vetoed it whenever it came up\" ([third-party transcript](https://rosetta.to/u/a16z/ben-horowitz-and-ali-ghodsi-how-to-run-a-100-billion-business), [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CWAMH9xMJfg), ~00:42; target not named). His order of checks: can the founders build with us and share the vision, then product integration, then financials last. He says buying revenue fails because the acquired leaders leave (same source).\n- **He sources deals himself.** \"We're talking to people all the time\"; and \"the price has to be right\" and \"The team has to be amazing, and there has to be the same vision\" ([Fortune Term Sheet 2024-01-30](https://www.fortune.com/2024/01/30/databricks-acquiring-ai-startup-einblick-ceo-ali-ghodsi-embraces-asymmetric-strategy)). Staying private is part of the plan: \"we can stay private because we're very well-funded\" (same source).\n- **Einblick (acquired, announced 2024-01-30, terms undisclosed)** came from his own past. Co-founder Tim Kraska (now an MIT professor) \"and Ghodsi met when they both were students at Berkeley\", did research together and kept in touch ([Fortune](https://www.fortune.com/2024/01/30/databricks-acquiring-ai-startup-einblick-ceo-ali-ghodsi-embraces-asymmetric-strategy)). Einblick had raised a $6M seed in 2020 (same source). This is the best-sourced case of the Berkeley network acting as his deal channel, alongside SkyPilot (angel) and Inferact (DV).\n- **2026 M&A pace.** \"We do a lot of M&A\" ([TechCrunch 2026-08-13](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/13/databricks-wanted-to-raise-1b-investors-wanted-15b-it-settled-on-5b-at-a-190b-valuation/)), said while raising $5B (Coatue lead), which he had meant to keep to about $1B. Deals in 2026: Quotient AI and SiftD.ai (March), Panther (June), Electric/PGlite (August) and Row Zero (2026-09-24), and he said \"We intend to do many more acquisitions like this in the future\" ([SaaSRise, secondary](https://www.saasrise.com/deals/databricks-buys-row-zero-and-is-scouting-for-more-startups-to-acquire-0cf62ecf-edbc-447c-934d-7ef5822b8bd5)). No source names which of these he or Kirk originated.\n- **The \"approves by email\" framing needs care.** Fortune (2024-05-21) says he \"signs off on every investment\". The 2026 GCV piece describes approvals flowing through an ad hoc committee on an email thread with \"fewer ultimate deciders\". It says only that \"Founder Ali Ghodsi, active in the startup ecosystem, remains engaged\", and it does not name him as the person who approves on that thread ([GCV 2026-03-09](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/information-technology/databricks-ventures-building-ecosystem/?format=md)). Whether he still signs every DV check in 2026, or only the large or seed-stage exceptions, is **unconfirmed**.\n- **Personal angel book re-checked.** The verified set is unchanged: Factory, Sycamore, Runta and SkyPilot. **Brain Co** is weaker than before. Calcalist ([2025-09-10](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/bjcdwwkslg)) names the round's angels as Armstrong, Collison, Hoffman and Arora, not Ghodsi, so only aggregators (CB Insights, Startup Intros) list him. One aggregator ([siliconvalleyinvestclub](https://siliconvalleyinvestclub.com/investors/ali-ghodsi/)) also lists **Replit** and **dbt Labs**. These were not added as deals. Databricks Ventures is in Replit's cap table, and the same page duplicates Factory and shows company-level rounds, so it probably mixes up corporate and personal money. Evalyze's \"$50K-$1M\" cheque range is aggregator-only.\n- **Firm membership rows added:** Peerialism (co-founder, years not stated), KTH (assistant professor 2008-2009) and UC Berkeley (visiting scholar 2009, then adjunct) ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Ghodsi)).\n- **Where to find them, checked 2026-11-10:** the Databricks author page returns 200. x.com/alighodsi returns 200, but X serves 200 for any handle behind its login wall, so the handle is still not verified as his. LinkedIn returns 999 (bot block), so it is unverified. The headshot `aliG.jpg` returns 200 image/jpeg.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Tim Kraska (MIT; Einblick co-founder) | Berkeley research peer; Databricks bought Einblick | Berkeley era; deal 2024-01-30 | [Fortune](https://www.fortune.com/2024/01/30/databricks-acquiring-ai-startup-einblick-ceo-ali-ghodsi-embraces-asymmetric-strategy) |\n| Databricks Ventures | Final approver of every investment | 2021 | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund) |\n| Andrew Ferguson, Phil Shin, Philip Kirk | Run DV and corp dev under him | 2020 / 2022 / 2026 | [firm page](../firms/databricks-ventures.md) |\n| Ben Horowitz (a16z) | Series A backer ($14M, 2013) and board member | 2013 | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2023/03/02/databricks-ben-horowitz-data-cloud-startup/) |\n| Naveen Rao | Acquired MosaicML; Databricks invested in Unconventional AI | 2023 | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2023/07/21/inside-how-biggest-ai-deal-came-together-ceos), [ARN](https://www.arnnet.com.au/?p=4059073) |\n| Ion Stoica, Matei Zaharia, Andy Konwinski | Databricks co-founders (Stoica co-founded Inferact; Konwinski runs Laude Ventures) | 2013 | [Wikipedia: Databricks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Databricks) |\n| Martin Casado (a16z) | Co-investor; Casado led the Runta seed Ghodsi joined | 2026 | [Runta](https://runta.com/blog/runta-the-execution-layer-for-agents/) |\n| Eryk Dobrushkin (Index) | Former chief of staff | n/a | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/team/eryk-dobrushkin/) |\n| Sujay Jaswa (WndrCo) | Close friend; passed on Databricks three times | n/a | [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY) |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** not through a DV inbound form. The working routes are:\n- The Berkeley lab network: Stoica, Zaharia, Shenker. SkyPilot's CEO came out of the lab that incubated Databricks.\n- a16z: Horowitz is on his board, and Casado brought him into Runta.\n- His former chief of staff Eryk Dobrushkin, now at Index.\n- For deal flow, Ferguson and Kirk at DV, who prepare what he signs.\n\n## Open questions\n- Personal check sizes. Whether Brain Co is real (aggregator only).\n- The Unconventional AI check size, and whether it came from DV or directly from Databricks.\n- The reporting line from Kirk and Ferguson to Ghodsi, possibly through the CFO.\n- Any personal LP commitments to venture funds (none found).\n- The exact wording of his LinkedIn post on Rao's exit.\n- The verbatim AGI quote. Video not checked.\n- (2026-11-10) Which company he vetoed on the a16z podcast. Whether he personally signs every DV check in 2026 (GCV no longer says so). Who originated the 2026 acquisitions (him vs Kirk). Replit and dbt Labs as personal angel deals (aggregator only, likely DV). The Brain Co angel check (contradicted by Calcalist's angel list).\n\n## Sources\n- https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund\n- https://fortune.com/2023/07/21/inside-how-biggest-ai-deal-came-together-ceos\n- https://fortune.com/2023/03/02/databricks-ben-horowitz-data-cloud-startup/\n- https://www.fortune.com/2024/06/05/databricks-ceo-ali-ghosdi-on-snowflake-rivalry-and-the-why-behind-databricks-latest-billion-dollar-deal\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/news/databricks-ceo-ali-ghodsi-raising-202932676.html\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/16/databricks-funding-valuation.html\n- https://letsdatascience.com/news/databricks-closes-5-billion-funding-at-190-billion-2cbf4001\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/14/databricks-reportedly-paid-2-billion-in-tabular-acquisition\n- https://www.arnnet.com.au/?p=4059073\n- https://infoworld.com/article/4056636/databricks-at-a-crossroads-can-its-ai-strategy-prevail-without-naveen-rao.html\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/03/sources-naveen-raos-new-ai-hardware-startup-targets-5b-valuation-with-backing-from-a16z\n- https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-12-16/databricks-hits-48b-revenue-run-rate-with-55-growth-as-ali-ghodsi-argues-llms-are-already-commoditized/transcript\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/databricks-sets-up-venture-fund/?format=md\n- https://the-decoder.com/anthropic-and-databricks-close-100-million-dollar-deal-for-ai-agents/\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=712882\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/11/02/factory-wants-to-use-ai-to-automate-the-software-dev-lifecycle/\n- https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed\n- https://runta.com/blog/runta-the-execution-layer-for-agents/\n- https://skypilot.ai/blog/skypilot-the-company\n- https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/ali-ghodsi (aggregator)\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Ghodsi\n- https://www.fortune.com/2024/01/30/databricks-acquiring-ai-startup-einblick-ceo-ali-ghodsi-embraces-asymmetric-strategy\n- https://rosetta.to/u/a16z/ben-horowitz-and-ali-ghodsi-how-to-run-a-100-billion-business (third-party transcript of https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CWAMH9xMJfg)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/13/databricks-wanted-to-raise-1b-investors-wanted-15b-it-settled-on-5b-at-a-190b-valuation/\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/information-technology/databricks-ventures-building-ecosystem/?format=md\n- https://www.saasrise.com/deals/databricks-buys-row-zero-and-is-scouting-for-more-startups-to-acquire-0cf62ecf-edbc-447c-934d-7ef5822b8bd5 (secondary)\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/bjcdwwkslg\n- https://siliconvalleyinvestclub.com/investors/ali-ghodsi/ (aggregator)\n", "person:ali-partovi": "\n# Ali Partovi\n\n## One-line take\nHe finds the best CS undergraduates years before they start companies and funds whatever they start, which is how one person with a $150M fund held Cursor from day one [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting).\n\n## Crux\nHe wins on time, not price: a relationship that starts at 19 beats any term sheet at 23. His edge is the Neo Scholars list and the tests he sets personally. The constraint is that it all runs through him: no second GP of record, a thin partner bench, and small ownership by design [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2045021/000204502125000001/primary_doc.xml) [src](https://neo.com/team). He would not pass on a person because the idea looks bad; he says that is exactly the mistake he made on PayPal and Google [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting).\n\n## Differential insight\n- Cursor: \"As soon as they incorporated, I wanted to make sure we could get in.\" The founders had been Scholars since sophomore year [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting).\n- The first pitch was \"autocomplete for CAD, a co-pilot for hardware design\", and \"Sequoia and Founders Fund both saw the pitch\" [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting).\n- Kalshi: \"when Kalshi was fundraising their seed round, which we led\" [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting).\n- Returns, in his words: funds one and two \"are both on track to be 10x or more\"; for the 2021 fund, \"The headline number would've been $1.2 billion\" [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting).\n- \"Last year, every one of OpenAI's new grad hires was a Neo scholar\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/27/from-coding-tests-to-billion-dollar-startups-ali-partovis-eight-year-experiment-is-paying-off).\n- He put more of his own money into the 2025 fund than into the first three combined, and kept outside capital growth small on purpose [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/27/from-coding-tests-to-billion-dollar-startups-ali-partovis-eight-year-experiment-is-paying-off).\n- Students get \"a $40K grant plus $10K invested in our fund\", so Scholars hold a piece of Neo's portfolio [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. Seed and pre-seed, AI-native companies and new-lab founders (Cursor, Chai Discovery, Applied Compute, Cognition, Adaption Labs, Engram, Etched). He does not write $20M cheques; he is the person who knows the founders three years before a $600M seed exists. Worth knowing as a source, and Connor Ling is the junior to track.\n\n## Background\n- Born in Iran; Harvard graduate [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/27/from-coding-tests-to-billion-dollar-startups-ali-partovis-eight-year-experiment-is-paying-off).\n- Early member of LinkExchange's founding team; co-founded iLike (sold to MySpace, 2009); co-founded Code.org with his twin Hadi [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/27/from-coding-tests-to-billion-dollar-startups-ali-partovis-eight-year-experiment-is-paying-off).\n- Angel, with Hadi, in Facebook, Airbnb and Dropbox [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/27/from-coding-tests-to-billion-dollar-startups-ali-partovis-eight-year-experiment-is-paying-off).\n- Founded Neo in 2017; first fund $82.4M closed 2018 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1726614/000172661418000002/primary_doc.xml).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://neo.com/ | \"The best way to reach us is a warm intro\"; hello@neo.com [src](https://neo.com/team) |\n| Substack / blog | https://neo.substack.com/ | fund and partner announcements, a few a year |\n| X | https://x.com/apartovi | handle per third-party index; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | none found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | neo.com links a show called \"Confession with Ali Partovi\"; not located |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-05 — The Peel with Turner Novak — Cursor + Kalshi Seed Investor on Spotting Outlier Talent — [link](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting) — Cursor origin, returns, Residency terms; mined\n- date not found — 20VC — How I Lost a $125M Deal with Yahoo by Being Too Honest — [link](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/ali-partovi) — not mined\n- date not found — TechCrunch podcast — indicators he uses to spot future tech billionaires — [link](https://techcrunch.com/podcast/ali-partovi-is-using-these-indicators-to-spot-future-tech-billionaires) — not mined\n- 2023 — GeekWire Podcast — OpenAI, Microsoft and the AI opportunity for startups — [link](https://www.geekwire.com/2023/geekwire-podcast-openai-microsoft-and-the-ai-opportunity-for-startups-with-neo-ceo-ali-partovi/) — not mined\n\n## How they think\n- People over idea, learned from misses: on Google, \"Why make an 11th search engine when there are already 10 highly competitive ones?\" [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting)\n- Tests for technical ability, entrepreneurial bent and \"magnetism\" (would his smartest friends join them) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/27/from-coding-tests-to-billion-dollar-startups-ali-partovis-eight-year-experiment-is-paying-off).\n- Price is not the lever: \"We take the risk up front, so this is extremely favorable to startups\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/19/ali-partovis-neo-looks-to-upend-the-accelerator-model-with-low-dilution-terms/).\n- On AI and work: \"a lot of coding as a job is being done by AI\" [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting).\n- Admitted misses: PayPal, Google (passed at three people); lost a $125M Yahoo deal \"by being too honest\" [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting).\n\n## Read these first\n- The Peel transcript: the only place he walks through Cursor, Kalshi and fund marks [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting).\n- \"Neo has raised $235M in new funds\": the LP list [src](https://neo.substack.com/p/neo-has-raised-235m-in-new-funds).\n- TechCrunch 2025-04-27: how he met Truell, and the Fund IV close [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/27/from-coding-tests-to-billion-dollar-startups-ali-partovis-eight-year-experiment-is-paying-off).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed this pass (X not read). Amplified by Cendana's Michael Kim and Kelli Fontaine, who cite Neo as their example of a talent-network manager [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cendanas-michael-kim-on-characteristics).\n\n## Portfolio\nSee [firms/neo.md](../firms/neo.md) for the full table. Deals where he is personally named:\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cursor (Anysphere) | Seed, first money | 2022 (inferred) | not stated | not stated | [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting) |\n| Kalshi (non-AI) | Seed | not stated | yes | not stated | [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting) |\n| Luminous Computing | Seed ($9M) | 2019-06 | co-led | Bill Gates; Gigafund | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=1836583) |\n| Tutor Intelligence | Seed SAFEs, then Series A | 2021-2025 | seed lead | USV (A lead) | [src](https://tutorintelligence.com/blog/tutor-series-a-letter) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Cursor:** met Truell in 2019 through a fellow student; set a handwritten coding test he finished in 15 minutes; mentored the team as Scholars; invested at incorporation on a hardware-CAD pitch; by 2025 the stake alone had more than doubled the 2021 fund [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/27/from-coding-tests-to-billion-dollar-startups-ali-partovis-eight-year-experiment-is-paying-off) [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting).\n- **Kalshi:** backed two new-grad founders he rated (\"these two are brilliant\") and led the seed [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hadi Partovi | twin; co-angel; LP in Neo | - | [src](https://neo.substack.com/p/neo-has-raised-235m-in-new-funds) |\n| Reid Hoffman | one of Neo's first LPs | 2017 | [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting) |\n| Sequoia Capital; Cendana; Horsley Bridge; K5 Global | institutional LPs | 2023 | [src](https://neo.substack.com/p/neo-has-raised-235m-in-new-funds) |\n| Adam D'Angelo | LP in Neo; co-investor in Convex | 2023 | [src](https://neo.substack.com/p/neo-has-raised-235m-in-new-funds) |\n| Michael Truell | Scholar since 2019; Cursor CEO | 2019 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/27/from-coding-tests-to-billion-dollar-startups-ali-partovis-eight-year-experiment-is-paying-off) |\n| Russell Kaplan | first Scholar class; Cognition president; mentor | 2017 | [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting) |\n| Fuzzy Khosrowshahi | uncle; Notion CTO; mentor | - | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/19/ali-partovis-neo-looks-to-upend-the-accelerator-model-with-low-dilution-terms/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Neo's ownership in Cursor and the size of that first cheque.\n- Whether he would co-invest alongside a large seed lead in a Scholar-founded lab.\n- A checked headshot URL (neo.com images load lazily and could not be captured).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.thespl.it/p/cursor-kalshi-seed-investor-on-spotting\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/27/from-coding-tests-to-billion-dollar-startups-ali-partovis-eight-year-experiment-is-paying-off\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/19/ali-partovis-neo-looks-to-upend-the-accelerator-model-with-low-dilution-terms/\n- https://neo.substack.com/p/neo-has-raised-235m-in-new-funds\n- https://neo.com/ ; https://neo.com/team\n- SEC Form D filings for Neo Fund 1.0 through 4.0 (linked in firms/neo.md)\n", "person:ali-tamaseb": "\n# Ali Tamaseb\n\n## One-line take\nHe's a former founder who became a DCVC GP and wrote *Super Founders*. His AI book is app-layer (deepfake detection, a participant check in Odyssey's seed), so he's worth tracking as a DCVC route to founders, not as a frontier-lab investor.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** DCVC sells his edge as a method, not a sector. Its 2023 promotion post says he has \"a celebrated data-driven methodology\" for finding super founders. He uses it across fintech, healthcare, materials and mining ([src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/dcvc-promotes-four-partners-to-general-partner)).\n- **How he deploys:** He leads Series A rounds of about $15–30M (Reality Defender in 2023, ElectronX in 2025), takes the board seat and then re-ups. In hot AI rounds he takes participant slots (Odyssey seed, led by GV).\n- **Constraints:** DCVC's deep-tech mandate, and a book that is mostly outside AI (energy-market infrastructure, hospital data).\n- **What he'd likely never do, by inference:** discriminate against solo or non-technical founders, or require first-mover advantage. His own data says neither matters ([src](https://www.hachettebookgroup.com/titles/ali-tamaseb/super-founders/9781541768413/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Reality Defender's Series A was bigger than announced.** The $15M Series A that DCVC led was announced 2023-10-17. The Form D shows \"totalAmountSold 19424966\" of a $20.08M offering, with first sale 2023-09-29 and Tamaseb listed as Director ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1993926/000199392623000002/primary_doc.xml)). The 2024 extension Form D shows $17.5M sold of $24.54M offered, first sale 2024-08-19. It also adds a new director, Alexander Ross ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1993926/000199392624000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **A quiet non-AI board seat.** He has been a director of Medical Informatics Corp (Sickbay) since at least the May 2022 Form D. He is still listed as \"Ali Tamaseb Director\" on the January 2025 Form D ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1764954/000205086825000001/primary_doc.xml); [2022](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1764954/000176495422000002/primary_doc.xml)). DCVC's first check there was in 2017 ([src](https://www.dcvc.com/companies/medical-informatics)). Neither his DCVC bio nor the firm's portfolio posts tie him to it.\n- **He enters, then leads the next round.** At ElectronX, DCVC backed the 2024 $15M seed. Seventeen months later it led the $30M Series A. In his words, it was \"doubling down on ElectronX, which we first invested in last year\" ([src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/a-new-precision-power-market)).\n- **The ElectronX thesis is AI as demand, not AI as product.** He cites data-center grid load, and \"the runaway success of prediction markets like Polymarket and Kalshi\" ([src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/a-new-precision-power-market)).\n- **He is not the price-setter in hot AI rounds.** At Odyssey he was \"proud to be participating in the company's $9 million seed round\" ([src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/odyssey-is-delivering-powerful-new-3d-generative-ai-tools-to-hollywood)). GV led, and Air Street had been in since late 2023. DCVC is not named in the $18M Series A or the $310M Series B in the repo's sources (data/rounds.csv).\n- **The book's anti-consensus findings,** from the publisher's summary of his dataset:\n  - \"Solo founders and non-technical CEOs had no disadvantage\"\n  - \"being first to market with an idea does not actually matter\"\n  - fewer than 15% of unicorns went through an accelerator ([src](https://www.hachettebookgroup.com/titles/ali-tamaseb/super-founders/9781541768413/))\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** His only AI-native deals are Reality Defender (AI-content detection, app layer) and a participant seed check in Odyssey. Odyssey is a world-model lab that later raised $310M at $1.45B, but DCVC doesn't show as a follow-on investor. He has no pre-training labs, chips or compute deals. Other DCVC partners own those (Matt Ocko on Mythic and MosaicML, Jason Pontin on Callosum; see firms/dcvc.md).\n\nHe matters to you in two ways:\n- **Co-investor.** He leads $15–30M Series A rounds and stays on boards.\n- **Founder-quality signal.** His Super Founders dataset and the Superfounders Club network may surface founders before they have a lab.\n\nHe is not the DCVC partner to pitch for a frontier-lab seed.\n\n## Background\n- **Before DCVC:** startup founder and CEO who grew the company \"to millions in revenue.\" The DCVC bio doesn't name the company, and the name is unconfirmed ([src](https://www.dcvc.com/team/ali-tamaseb)).\n- **Education:** Imperial College President's Medal for Outstanding Achievement; British Council British Alumni Award, 2018 ([src](https://www.hachettebookgroup.com/titles/ali-tamaseb/super-founders/9781541768413/)). The DCVC bio also cites medals in national and international physics and programming Olympiads. His degree and years are not stated on either page.\n- **At DCVC:** earliest dated byline is 2020-03-26, the Deep Tech RFS co-written with Matt Ocko ([src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/solving-the-worlds-hardest-problems-with-deep-tech-request-for-startups)). He was a Partner until his promotion to General Partner on 2023-07-21 ([src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/dcvc-promotes-four-partners-to-general-partner)). His exact join date is unconfirmed.\n- **Author:** *Super Founders* (PublicAffairs, on sale 2021-05-18) ([src](https://www.hachettebookgroup.com/titles/ali-tamaseb/super-founders/9781541768413/)).\n- **Recognition:** named one of Business Insider's top 100 early-stage investors (per the publisher bio; the year and the original BI list are unconfirmed).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.dcvc.com/team/ali-tamaseb | DCVC bio, checked 2026-11-10 (HTTP 200). alitamaseb.com returned 405/436 to direct fetch; not verified |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights | His bylined DCVC posts, roughly 2–3 a year (2023–2025). Medium returned 403 |\n| X | https://twitter.com/alitamaseb | Linked from the DCVC bio; follower count not checked (X not fetchable) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/alitamaseb/ | Linked from the DCVC bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Superfounders Club (https://superfounders.club) returned an empty page on 2026-11-10 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNo transcripts were checked; web search was rate-limited this run. The publisher says he has appeared on the BBC and at TED. Episode-level appearances are unconfirmed.\n\n## How they think\n- **Founder over market:** pattern-matches founders with his unicorn dataset, then picks sectors opportunistically.\n- **Contrarian views (book):** no penalty for solo or non-technical founders, first-mover advantage is irrelevant, and accelerators are rare among unicorns.\n- **Detection and infrastructure plays:** backs picks-and-shovels businesses that benefit from AI rather than models themselves (deepfake detection, power hedging for data-center load).\n- **Diligence:** DCVC ran its own technical tests on Reality Defender before leading. Its post says Reality Defender \"has the best technology we've seen for screening digital content\" ([src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/the-next-cybersecurity-battleground)).\n- **Calls so far:** Odyssey's later $1.45B valuation was a good call, but DCVC was a small participant. Reality Defender and ElectronX outcomes are not yet known.\n\n## Read these first\n- [The next cybersecurity battleground (2023)](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/the-next-cybersecurity-battleground): his memo for leading the Reality Defender Series A.\n- [A new precision power market (2025)](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/a-new-precision-power-market): leading the ElectronX Series A, with AI as a driver of grid demand.\n- [Deep Tech Request for Startups (2020)](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/solving-the-worlds-hardest-problems-with-deep-tech-request-for-startups): a public, YC-style RFS co-written with Ocko.\n- [Super Founders (publisher page)](https://www.hachettebookgroup.com/titles/ali-tamaseb/super-founders/9781541768413/): his dataset and headline findings.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked: X and LinkedIn were not fetchable this run. On DCVC's site he runs Q&As with portfolio CEOs, for example with Ben Colman on [FINRA](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/reality-defender-answers-finra-call) and on [Reality Defender's public API](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/reality-defender-democratizes-deepfake-detection-with-public-api-launch) in 2025.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Reality Defender | Series A ($15M announced; Form D $19.42M sold) | 2023-10-17 (first sale 2023-09-29) | Yes, board | not named in post | [post](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/the-next-cybersecurity-battleground), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1993926/000199392623000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Reality Defender | Series A extension ($33M total A) | 2024-10-22 | No (re-up) | Illuminate Financial (lead), Booz Allen Ventures, IBM Ventures, Accenture, YC | [src](https://www.dcvc.com/press-releases/reality-defender-expands-series-a-to-33-million-to-enhance-ai-detection-capabilities) |\n| Odyssey AI | Seed ($9M) | 2024-07-09 | No | GV (lead), Air Street | [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/odyssey-is-delivering-powerful-new-3d-generative-ai-tools-to-hollywood) |\n| ElectronX | Seed ($15M) | 2024-06-12 | Unconfirmed | not named | [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/electronx-seed) |\n| ElectronX | Series A ($30M) | 2025-11-14 | Yes | not named | [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/a-new-precision-power-market) |\n| Medical Informatics (Sickbay) | Undisclosed (Form D $15.1M sold) | 2022-05-11 | Unconfirmed; director | not stated | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1764954/000176495422000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n**Timing:**\n- **First in:** he was first institutional in Reality Defender as far as the sources show. DCVC's first investment there was 2023, but YC and earlier investors predate it, so this is unconfirmed.\n- **Not first in:** Odyssey (Air Street was earlier).\n- **Doubled down:** Reality Defender (2024 extension) and ElectronX (seed, then led the Series A).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Reality Defender:** DCVC led the $15M Series A in October 2023 after running its own accuracy tests, and Tamaseb joined the board. A year later Illuminate Financial led an extension that took the Series A to $33M, and DCVC re-upped. The intro path is unconfirmed.\n- **ElectronX:** DCVC backed the $15M seed in June 2024, which the Wall Street Journal announced. The company got its CFTC approvals in August 2025, and in November 2025 DCVC led the $30M Series A. The company has now raised more than $55M ([src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/a-new-precision-power-market)).\n- **Odyssey:** DCVC took a participant slot in GV's $9M seed in July 2024. No follow-on is visible.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DCVC | Partner, then GP from 2023-07-21 | by 2020-03 | [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/dcvc-promotes-four-partners-to-general-partner) |\n| Matt Ocko | Co-author of DCVC's 2020 Deep Tech RFS | 2020 | [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/solving-the-worlds-hardest-problems-with-deep-tech-request-for-startups) |\n| Alan Cohen, James Hardiman, Jason Pontin | Promoted to GP in the same 2023 class | 2023 | [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/dcvc-promotes-four-partners-to-general-partner) |\n| Ben Colman (Reality Defender) | Board member and lead investor | 2023 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1993926/000199392623000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Medical Informatics (Emma Fauss, Craig Rusin) | Board director | by 2022 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1764954/000176495422000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Superfounders Club | Runs it | unconfirmed | [src](https://www.dcvc.com/team/ali-tamaseb) |\n\nLP links: none found. Searched EDGAR full text for \"Tamaseb\", which returned only the four Form Ds above, so no LP rows were written.\n\n## Open questions\n- What did he found before DCVC, and when did he join DCVC?\n- Who are the Superfounders Club members, and does the club feed DCVC deal flow?\n- Did DCVC lead the ElectronX seed?\n- Which of his \"billion-dollar companies\" (crypto, fintech, healthcare, energy, per his DCVC bio) are the ones he means?\n- Is Alexander Ross from Illuminate Financial?\n- What has he said on podcasts about the method? There are likely many 2021 book-tour appearances; no transcript was checked.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.dcvc.com/team/ali-tamaseb\n- https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/dcvc-promotes-four-partners-to-general-partner\n- https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/the-next-cybersecurity-battleground\n- https://www.dcvc.com/press-releases/reality-defender-expands-series-a-to-33-million-to-enhance-ai-detection-capabilities\n- https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/reality-defender-answers-finra-call\n- https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/reality-defender-democratizes-deepfake-detection-with-public-api-launch\n- https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/odyssey-is-delivering-powerful-new-3d-generative-ai-tools-to-hollywood\n- https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/electronx-seed\n- https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/a-new-precision-power-market\n- https://www.dcvc.com/companies/electronx\n- https://www.dcvc.com/companies/medical-informatics\n- https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/solving-the-worlds-hardest-problems-with-deep-tech-request-for-startups\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1993926/000199392623000002/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1993926/000199392624000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1764954/000176495422000002/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1764954/000205086825000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.hachettebookgroup.com/titles/ali-tamaseb/super-founders/9781541768413/\n- data/rounds.csv (Odyssey round lists; Fenwick and vcaonline sources)\n", "person:aliisa-rosenthal": "\n# Aliisa Rosenthal\n\n## One-line take\nOpenAI's first commercial hire, now Acrew's first new GP since 2019, and within seven months a director of Core Automation: the person through whom a fintech-and-security fund reaches OpenAI-alumni companies.\n\n## Crux\nShe wins on a network nobody else at the firm has (OpenAI's early commercial and research staff) and on a rare skill founders want, having built enterprise sales from two reps to hundreds. She is constrained by being new to investing and by a firm whose control and funds are built for fintech and security. Her stated focus is applications, so the lab board seat is the exception to watch.\n\n## Differential insight\n- She was recruited: \"I wasn’t initially looking to join a VC fund,\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/21/openais-former-sales-leader-joins-vc-firm-acrew-openai-taught-her-where-startups-can-build-a-moat/)). Lauren Kolodny pitched her; Peter Deng (ex-OpenAI, Felicis) helped her decide.\n- Focus: \"Where I’m really excited to invest is on the application layer.\" (same source).\n- Moat thesis: \"who owns and manages this context layer will become a large advantage\" (same source).\n- She is listed as a director on Core Automation's Form D filed 2026-07-30 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2148145/000214814526000001/primary_doc.xml)); four weeks later Acrew filed a $15M vehicle named \"Acrew CA, L.P.\" ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2148122/000123191926001069/primary_doc.xml)). The link between the two is an inference.\n- The firm calls her \"the first commercial hire at OpenAI\" and its first GP added since founding ([src](https://perspectives.acrewcapital.com/p/welcoming-acrews-newest-general-partner)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh as a person to know: a new GP at a fund that can write $10M-plus, with a board seat at a new lab and first call on OpenAI alumni spinouts. Her own thesis is application-layer, so overlap on labs will be occasional.\n\n## Background\n- OpenAI: head of sales from June 2022 (Fortune, via [src](https://fortune.com/2026/01/22/openais-former-head-of-sales-is-entering-vc-she-still-calls-herself-an-agi-sherpa/)); left about eight months before joining Acrew; led the ChatGPT Enterprise launch ([src](https://www.acrewcapital.com/team-members/aliisa-rosenthal)).\n- Earlier senior operating roles at Mixpanel and WalkMe (same bio; dates not given).\n- Joined Acrew as General Partner on 2026-01-21 after about a decade of knowing the firm ([src](https://perspectives.acrewcapital.com/p/welcoming-acrews-newest-general-partner)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.acrewcapital.com/team-members/aliisa-rosenthal | Firm bio |\n| Substack / blog | https://perspectives.acrewcapital.com | Co-author of the Wispr post |\n| X | https://x.com/aliisarosenthal | Not read |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/aliisa-rosenthal/ | |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-01 — TechCrunch interview — [link](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/21/openais-former-sales-leader-joins-vc-firm-acrew-openai-taught-her-where-startups-can-build-a-moat/) — context layer as moat; sourcing from OpenAI alumni.\n- 2026-01 — Fortune interview — [link](https://fortune.com/2026/01/22/openais-former-head-of-sales-is-entering-vc-she-still-calls-herself-an-agi-sherpa/) — not read in full.\n- No podcast found.\n\n## How they think\n- Durable applications built on many models beat single-model wrappers.\n- Persistent context (\"Context is dynamic. It’s adaptable. It’s scalable.\") is the defensible layer (TechCrunch).\n- Cheaper, lighter models are an opening for startups: \"cheaper models that are lighter weight and innovate on inference costs\" (TechCrunch).\n- Enterprises still underestimate what AI can do: \"There’s a really large gap that I am very optimistic can be filled.\" (TechCrunch).\n\n## Read these first\n- The TechCrunch interview: her thesis in her own words.\n- Acrew's welcome post: how the partners describe her role.\n- \"Why We Backed Wispr\": her first co-signed investment post.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed; X was not read.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Core Automation | Board director on the Series A-stage Form D; probable $15M Acrew SPV | 2026-07 | no | 51 investors, none named | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2148145/000214814526000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Wispr Flow | Series B ($280M at $2B) | 2026-08-17 | no | Menlo Ventures (lead); Notable; Forerunner; Goodwater; Peak XV | [src](https://perspectives.acrewcapital.com/p/why-we-backed-wispr-the-interface) |\n\nHumans& (seed, 2026-01-20) closed the day before she was announced; no source ties her to it.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Core Automation.** Founded by ex-OpenAI researchers (Jerry Tworek is the executive officer on the Form D). She appears as a director; whether as Acrew's designee or an independent is unconfirmed. The later \"Acrew CA\" vehicle suggests the firm raised a dedicated $15M for it.\n- **Wispr.** First author on the firm's post for the Menlo-led Series B; the post does not say how Acrew met the company or whether it holds a seat.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lauren Kolodny | Recruited her to Acrew; co-author on Wispr | 2026 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/21/openais-former-sales-leader-joins-vc-firm-acrew-openai-taught-her-where-startups-can-build-a-moat/) |\n| Peter Deng (Felicis) | Ex-OpenAI colleague who advised on the move | 2026 | same |\n| Core Automation founders | Board director | 2026-07 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2148145/000214814526000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| OpenAI | Head of sales, first commercial hire | 2022-06 | [src](https://perspectives.acrewcapital.com/p/welcoming-acrews-newest-general-partner) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is her Core Automation seat Acrew's or personal, and what did Acrew pay?\n- Which other OpenAI-alumni companies has she backed since January 2026?\n- Did she invest as an angel while at OpenAI?\n\n## Sources\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/21/openais-former-sales-leader-joins-vc-firm-acrew-openai-taught-her-where-startups-can-build-a-moat/\n- https://perspectives.acrewcapital.com/p/welcoming-acrews-newest-general-partner\n- https://www.acrewcapital.com/team-members/aliisa-rosenthal\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2148145/000214814526000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2148122/000123191926001069/primary_doc.xml\n- https://perspectives.acrewcapital.com/p/why-we-backed-wispr-the-interface\n- https://fortune.com/2026/01/22/openais-former-head-of-sales-is-entering-vc-she-still-calls-herself-an-agi-sherpa/\n", "person:alison-davis-riddell": "\n# Alison Davis Riddell\n\n## One-line take\nShe runs Positive Sum as an operator: she has been President and a control person of the adviser since 2024-09. The only round she is publicly named on is an IoT hardware deal (Blues, Series A1, 2023). No source puts her on Etched or any other AI-chip or frontier-AI deal, so she is not the second signer on the compute bets. Treat her as the firm's operating partner and, inference only, a likely gatekeeper for fund mechanics, not as an AI picker ([Form ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/324219/PDF/324219.pdf), [PRNewswire 2023-01-05](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/blues-wireless-raises-32m-to-accelerate-enterprise-adoption-of-cellular-iot-301714865.html)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Correction to the repo seed.** The \"ex-GP of Susa Ventures\" line is not supported. It comes from an AI-written aggregator page that also makes up Google and McKinsey roles ([startupintros, aggregator](https://startupintros.com/people/alison-davis-riddell)). TheOrg's own timeline doesn't say Susa ([TheOrg, aggregator](https://theorg.com/org/positive-sum/org-chart/alison-davis-riddell)). Primary sources show an operator path instead. She was COO of balena, an IoT container-platform company, and left \"to follow her passion for economics and become a partner in a venture capitalist investment fund\" ([balena blog 2023-03-21](https://blog.balena.io/co-ceo-announcement/)).\n- **Why she matters.** Positive Sum Advisors LLC's Form ADV (annual amendment, 2026-03-27) lists four people on Schedule A:\n  - O'Shaughnessy: CEO and member, owner code E (75%+), control person, since 2020-06.\n  - Samuel Blake Cates: Managing Director and member, owner code B (10–25%), not a control person, since 2020-11.\n  - Riddell: **President since 2024-09, ownership \"NA\", control person \"Y\"**.\n  - Robert Joseph Parker: COO since 2022-04, ownership NA, control person Y.\n  \n  So she has control authority but no equity in the management company ([Form ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/324219/PDF/324219.pdf)).\n- **Constraint.** She is not named on any of the firm's eight Form Ds, including Fund III (2024-12), filed three months after she became President. Fund IV and the Conviction Fund (2026-08) list only O'Shaughnessy as Executive Officer, with Parker as signatory. Fund-raising and deal authority read as O'Shaughnessy's; hers reads as operating authority ([Fund III](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2048473/000172348924000003/primary_doc.xml), [Fund IV](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149092/000123191926000870/primary_doc.xml), [Conviction](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149093/000123191926000906/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **What she'd never do (inference, low confidence).** No public voice: no podcast, X or writing was found. She is a behind-the-scenes partner who doesn't use the media machine that drives Positive Sum's sourcing.\n\n## Differential insight\n- She has been **President and a control person of the adviser since 2024-09 without an ownership stake**. Sam Cates, missing from the team page, still owns 10–25%. Quote: \"RIDDELL, ALISON, DAVIS\" listed as \"PRESIDENT\" \"09/2024\" ([Form ADV 2026-03-27](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/324219/PDF/324219.pdf)).\n- **Why she left operating:** \"took an opportunity to follow her passion for economics\" ([balena blog 2023-03-21](https://blog.balena.io/co-ceo-announcement/)). Balena left the COO seat empty after she went.\n- **How she underwrote her one named deal:** sector pattern-matching from operating, \"Having worked directly in the IoT space for six years\" ([PRNewswire 2023-01-05](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/blues-wireless-raises-32m-to-accelerate-enterprise-adoption-of-cellular-iot-301714865.html)).\n- **Founder-life's-work framing matches the firm's tagline:** \"Blues is clearly the culmination of Ray's life's work\" (same source). Positive Sum's site says \"We back founders pursuing their life's work\" ([site](https://www.positivesum.com)).\n- **The \"ex-Susa GP\" claim is an aggregator error.** The source line is \"she was a General Partner at Susa Ventures\", which conflicts with balena's and TheOrg's timelines ([startupintros, aggregator](https://startupintros.com/people/alison-davis-riddell)).\n- **Firm-level marks seen from her seat (inference).** The ADV reports gross asset values of $381.3M for Fund II-QP (Form D sold $118.5M at 2022 filing) and $267.5M for Fund III (sold $184.75M at 2024-12 filing). That implies a large mark-up on Fund II, consistent with Etched's re-pricing, though the ADV names no holdings ([Form ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/324219/PDF/324219.pdf), [Fund II Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1927997/000192799722000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **Resolves a firm-file needs-id.** The \"Joe Parker\" on the team page is Robert **Joseph** Parker, the COO since 2022-04 who signs the 2026 Form Ds ([Form ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/324219/PDF/324219.pdf)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium**, and only through the firm. Positive Sum is a high-fit firm: Etched Series A co-lead, Cognition, Physical Intelligence, Flapping Airplanes ([firms/positive-sum.md](../firms/positive-sum.md)). Riddell personally has **no sourced AI-chip, compute or frontier-AI deal**. Her named deal is Blues (cellular IoT hardware, Series A1 lead, 2023-01). That is hardware-adjacent but not AI.\n- **Etched role: none found.** Every Etched source names O'Shaughnessy: Primary's A post, EP.356, EP.480, TechCrunch 2026-06-30 and etched.com/progress. I checked each for her name with no hits.\n- **Timing:** she was a partner (2022-08, aggregator) when Etched's Series A was co-led (2024-06-25), and became President three months later. Positive Sum was not first into Etched (Primary seeded it in 2023-03), and nothing shows her in the A, the Stripes B, the Sequoia C or the $21B Jane Street round.\n- **For the owner's purposes:** useful as a contact for Positive Sum's fund mechanics, such as Conviction Fund allocation and SPVs like \"EAI Holdings\", where her President role likely matters (inference). Not useful as a frontier-AI signal.\n\n## Background\n- B.A. in Economics and Political Science, University of Washington (aggregator: [TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/positive-sum/org-chart/alison-davis-riddell), [NFX Signal](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/alison-davis-riddell)).\n- 2011-07 to 2014-08: Investment Analyst, University of Washington Investment Management Company (UW's endowment office), which is LP-side experience (aggregator: TheOrg).\n- 2014-10 to 2016-06: Chief of Staff to the CEO, Impossible Foods (aggregator: TheOrg). A GeekWire 2022 list reportedly says the same (seen in search only; page returned 403).\n- 2016-08 to ~2022: balena (formerly resin.io). Roles were Director of Product Marketing and Strategy, then Director of Product and Commercial Strategy, then COO and Board Member (aggregator: TheOrg). balena's own blog confirms the COO role and her departure to VC ([balena 2023-03-21](https://blog.balena.io/co-ceo-announcement/)).\n- 2022-08: Partner, Positive Sum (aggregator: TheOrg). She was quoted as \"Partner at Positive Sum\" by 2023-01-05 ([PRNewswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/blues-wireless-raises-32m-to-accelerate-enterprise-adoption-of-cellular-iot-301714865.html)).\n- 2024-09: President, Positive Sum Advisors LLC (primary: [Form ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/324219/PDF/324219.pdf)). Individual CRD 8244475 (same source).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [positivesum.com/team](https://www.positivesum.com/team) | listed second, after O'Shaughnessy; link only, no bio or photo (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | the team page links her LinkedIn, not X; NFX lists no X |\n| LinkedIn | [adavis17](https://www.linkedin.com/in/adavis17/) | linked from firm team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Undated: a search snippet lists an episode \"080: Resin.io with Alison Davis and Ronald McCollam\" tied to a Frontside page ([frontside.com/people/joe-lasala](https://frontside.com/people/joe-lasala)). It couldn't be confirmed: the page held no matching text and the guessed episode URLs returned 404.\n- No Invest Like the Best appearance found. Her name doesn't appear in EP.356, EP.455 or EP.480 transcripts.\n\n## How they think\n- Operator pattern-matching: she backs the sector she ran, IoT, and cites six years \"directly in the IoT space\" (Blues quote above).\n- Founder-centric \"life's work\" framing, the same as the firm's tagline.\n- Calls right or wrong: Blues (2023 A1 lead) has no public later round or outcome in the sources found. Too early to judge.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Form ADV, Positive Sum Advisors LLC (2026-03-27)](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/324219/PDF/324219.pdf): who controls and owns the adviser, and fund GAVs.\n- [balena, \"Recent organisational changes\" (2023-03-21)](https://blog.balena.io/co-ceo-announcement/): the only primary account of why she moved to VC.\n- [Blues Series A1 release (2023-01-05)](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/blues-wireless-raises-32m-to-accelerate-enterprise-adoption-of-cellular-iot-301714865.html): her only on-record investment rationale.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X account is linked from the firm or aggregators, and no posts were located.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Blues (Blues Wireless) | Series A1, $32M | 2023-01-05 | yes (Positive Sum led; she is the quoted partner) | new: Four Rivers, Northgate, Qualcomm; returning: Sequoia, Cascade, Lachy Groom, XYZ | [PRNewswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/blues-wireless-raises-32m-to-accelerate-enterprise-adoption-of-cellular-iot-301714865.html), [IoT Now](https://www.iot-now.com/2023/01/12/126796-blues-wireless-raises-32m-to-accelerate-enterprise-adoption-of-cellular-iot/) |\n| Etched, Rogo, Cognition and other firm deals | — | — | not attributed to her in any source | — | [firms/positive-sum.md](../firms/positive-sum.md) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Blues:** Positive Sum was **not first in**. Sequoia, Cascade, Lachy Groom and XYZ were \"previous backers\", and Blues had raised a $22M Series A in 2021 ([GeekWire 2021, search snippet](https://www.geekwire.com/2021/ray-ozzies-telecom-startup-blues-wireless-raises-22m-bill-gates-others/)). Positive Sum then led the A1. The founder, Ray Ozzie, was quoted alongside her. No source gives an intro path, but her balena IoT background is the obvious bridge (inference). Price was not disclosed.\n- **Etched:** no story involving her was found (see Fit).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Positive Sum Advisors LLC | President, control person, no ownership | 2024-09 | [Form ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/324219/PDF/324219.pdf) |\n| Patrick O'Shaughnessy | CEO and majority owner of the adviser; her principal | 2022 | [Form ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/324219/PDF/324219.pdf) |\n| Sam (Samuel Blake) Cates | Managing Director, 10–25% owner; not on the team page | 2020-11 (his date) | [Form ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/324219/PDF/324219.pdf) |\n| Robert Joseph (Joe) Parker | COO, fellow control person | 2022-04 (his date) | [Form ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/324219/PDF/324219.pdf) |\n| balena | former COO and Board Member | 2016-08 to ~2022 | [balena blog](https://blog.balena.io/co-ceo-announcement/), TheOrg (aggregator) |\n| Alexandros Marinos | balena founder and ex-CEO, her former boss | 2016 | [balena blog](https://blog.balena.io/co-ceo-announcement/) |\n| Ray Ozzie / Blues | led the A1; named partner | 2023-01 | [PRNewswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/blues-wireless-raises-32m-to-accelerate-enterprise-adoption-of-cellular-iot-301714865.html) |\n| UW Investment Management Company | former investment analyst | 2011–2014 | TheOrg (aggregator) |\n| Impossible Foods | former Chief of Staff to CEO | 2014–2016 | TheOrg (aggregator) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Does she sit on any board for Positive Sum (Blues or others)? Not found.\n- What role does she have in the 2026 SPVs (\"EAI Holdings\", \"SR Holdings\") and the $100M Conviction Fund? Her President title suggests she does allocation and operations (inference).\n- What is Sam Cates's real role? He is a 10–25% owner and Managing Director but is off the team page. He may be the true \"second partner\" economically.\n- Confirm the Frontside \"Resin.io with Alison Davis\" episode.\n- Is she on any Positive Sum AI deal (Modal, Physical Intelligence, Flapping Airplanes)? Check company announcements and Form D related persons.\n\n## Sources\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/324219/PDF/324219.pdf (Form ADV, Positive Sum Advisors LLC, CRD 324219, annual amendment 2026-03-27; primary)\n- https://blog.balena.io/co-ceo-announcement/ (2023-03-21; primary)\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/blues-wireless-raises-32m-to-accelerate-enterprise-adoption-of-cellular-iot-301714865.html (2023-01-05; primary press release)\n- https://www.iot-now.com/2023/01/12/126796-blues-wireless-raises-32m-to-accelerate-enterprise-adoption-of-cellular-iot/\n- https://www.positivesum.com/team , https://www.positivesum.com\n- SEC Form Ds: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2048473/000172348924000003/primary_doc.xml , https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149092/000123191926000870/primary_doc.xml , https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149093/000123191926000906/primary_doc.xml , https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1927997/000192799722000001/primary_doc.xml\n- Checked for her name, with none found: https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one , https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware , https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond , https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/ , ILTB EP.356, EP.455 and EP.480 on podscripts.co\n- Aggregators (secondary): https://theorg.com/org/positive-sum/org-chart/alison-davis-riddell , https://signal.nfx.com/investors/alison-davis-riddell , https://startupintros.com/people/alison-davis-riddell (contains the unsupported Susa claim)\n", "person:amanda-huang": "\n# Amanda Huang\n\n## One-line take\nShe is BCV's main link into the Bay Area ML research community. Since 2024 she has co-run the Cerebras x BCV \"Cafe Compute\" series and small research dinners. BCV credits her with Periodic Labs and Black Forest Labs, but BCV's own portfolio database names Slater Stich as partner on both. The only deal it attributes to her is a 2026 applied-AI seed (Osmo).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** she builds the rooms researchers turn up to. That includes 92 hosted Luma events, a late-night co-working series co-branded with Cerebras, an ICML lunch and a \"Women in Post-Training\" dinner with a Google DeepMind researcher ([src](https://luma.com/user/amandamhuang)). That gives BCV warm access to lab talent before people spin out.\n- **What constrains her:** she is a Principal, not the partner of record. BCV's CMS puts Slater Stich alone on Periodic Labs, Black Forest Labs and Polars ([src](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22periodic-labs%22%5D)). Her stated mandate is developer tools ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/amanda-huang/)).\n- **Timing:** in every lab deal tied to her, BCV followed someone else in. It was not first in.\n- **What she'd never do:** nothing sourced. There is no first-person material from her (her X and LinkedIn were blocked to fetch).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Cafe Compute began as a Cerebras fellowship funnel, not a meetup.** The first edition (2024-11-20) says it is \"brought to you by the Cerebras x BCV fellows program.\" It links to cerebras.ai/fellows, which \"incubates researchers, engineers, and students\" ([src](https://luma.com/agp2c7gw)). So BCV has had a structured pipeline into Cerebras-backed researchers since late 2024.\n- **The sponsor list tracks the network.** Cerebras is the only sponsor present in every edition from 2024 to 2026. Others joined and dropped as the series moved:\n  - 2025-07: Pylon ([src](https://luma.com/jh51she4)).\n  - NYC 2025-09: Haize Labs, SF Compute, Structify, Exa AI, Flora AI ([src](https://luma.com/79m3ny1l)).\n  - 2025-10: Cognition, LMArena ([src](https://luma.com/am0i94on)).\n  - NeurIPS 2025-12: OpenAI, Mercor, SF Compute ([src](https://luma.com/vqfwj0wx)).\n  - 2026-01: Reducto, Parallel ([src](https://luma.com/i70zibrg)).\n  - Boston 2026-02: \"brought to you by Cerebras and CSAIL Alliances\", with BCV not on the sponsor line ([src](https://luma.com/ea880drw)).\n  - 2026-04: Cafe Compute at HumanX ran on the BCV Labs calendar, hosted by BCV Events and Cerebras, without her listed ([src](https://luma.com/cchumanx26)).\n\n  The brand appears to be outgrowing her.\n- **The research roundtable format is a paper read with the author.** The first roundtable (2024-11-21, 16 guests) was on OpenAI's MLE-Bench with Neil Chowdhury, now at Transluce. The format was to \"skim the paper in silence,\" then do Q&A with the author ([src](https://luma.com/7bz2soa9)). Only Roundtable #1 is public, on the BCV Labs calendar ([src](https://luma.com/bcvlabs)). The firm post's plural \"Roundtables\" could not be confirmed.\n- **Small rooms aimed at pre-training and post-training talent.** She co-hosted the 2026-03-09 dinner with Auriel Wright of Google DeepMind for \"women in post training (and pre-training) across various labs\" (6 guests) ([src](https://luma.com/h2a1i71s)). She also ran an 11-guest ICML lunch in Seoul on 2026-07-10, aimed at \"data-efficient RL at scale\" and AI drug discovery researchers ([src](https://luma.com/hjzjnl1q)).\n- **Prose credit and data credit disagree.** The firm post says \"She played a key role in our investments in Periodic Labs, Black Forest Labs\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-amanda-huang-our-newest-principal/)). That sentence is Slater Stich's, not hers. The CMS partner field names only Slater Stich on all three. The only portfolio record that lists her is Osmo (Seed, 2026, with Christina Melas-Kyriazi; record created 2026-08-05) ([src](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22osmo%22%5D)).\n- **Polars predates her.** BCV's CMS records Polars with year_of_investment 2023 (record created 2024-08-22). Her team record was created 2024-08-21 and her Luma account on 2024-04-23 ([src](https://luma.com/user/amandamhuang)). Her \"key role\" was likely in a later round or follow-on, which is unconfirmed.\n- **Structify, again with no CMS credit.** BCV led Structify's seed ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/structify-makes-building-custom-datasets-easy-with-ai/)). The CMS credits Slater Stich and Rak Garg. She hosted a \"BCV x Structify RevOps Learning Dinner\" with co-founder Ronak Gandhi (NYC, 2025-11-06) ([src](https://luma.com/n4o17ua6)), and Structify sponsored Cafe Compute NYC.\n- **Pairs with Ryan Kim on interface and agent bets.** They co-hosted \"Intent: Proactive & Predictive Interfaces\" (2026-05-22) on the ThirdLayer calendar ([src](https://luma.com/vs9pfati)). Speakers came from Simile, ThirdLayer, Interaction and Tomo, and Tomo is a 2026 BCV seed under Ryan Kim and Christina Melas-Kyriazi.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:** her network is exactly the frontier-research talent pool THESIS.md cares about: lab researchers, post-training and pre-training people, Cerebras-adjacent builders, and NeurIPS and ICML crowds. She is a good early-signal source on who is about to leave a lab.\n- **Against:** she is not first in on labs. BCV joined Periodic Labs after a16z and others ([src](https://periodic.com/)) and Black Forest Labs at the 2025-12-01 Series B, not in the a16z-led Series A, as an \"In Collaboration with\" investor ([src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier)). She doesn't control the check, and her stated mandate is developer tools.\n- **Use her as:** a co-investor or scout relationship and a source of researcher intros, not as a lead partner on a $600M lab seed.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** Periodic Labs, Black Forest Labs, Sunday Robotics (co-linked on the post), and the Cerebras relationship.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up between Los Angeles and Taipei. B.S. in Computer Science and Economics from Claremont McKenna College ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-amanda-huang-our-newest-principal/)).\n- Before BCV: backend engineering and developer experience, including Adobe Creative Cloud's developer ecosystem, plus economics research. No dates were found ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-amanda-huang-our-newest-principal/)).\n- Joined BCV by around 2024. BCV's CMS team record was created 2024-08-21, and her first BCV-hosted event was 2024-09-07 ([src](https://luma.com/ldcowje5)). Her exact start date and pre-Principal title were not found.\n- Promoted to Principal on 2026-02-03 ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-amanda-huang-our-newest-principal/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://baincapitalventures.com/team/amanda-huang/ | Firm bio |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | amandamhuang.substack.com returns 404 (2026-11-10) |\n| X | https://x.com/amandamhuang | Handle from BCV and Luma; fetch blocked (HTTP 402), so follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/amanda-m-huang/ | Fetch blocked (HTTP 999) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | github.com/amandamhuang returns 404 |\n| Luma | https://luma.com/user/amandamhuang | 92 hosted and 210 attended as of 2026-11-10; joined 2024-04-23. This is the best primary source on her |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The web-search budget was exhausted this session, and X and LinkedIn could not be fetched. Hosted events are covered under Differential insight.\n\n## How they think\n- From her BCV bio Q&A: \"I look for exceptional founders who want to make developers' lives easier\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/amanda-huang/)). In the same Q&A, she says infrastructure innovation reduces \"overhead and complexity for developers.\"\n- From her event copy (she is listed as host): cross-domain research talk is \"more valuable than ever\" because progress is so fast ([src](https://luma.com/hjzjnl1q)).\n- Sunday Robotics post (2026-03-12; she and Aaref Hilaly are linked on the page, and authorship is not stated): \"The first company that learns fastest from real homes will define the category.\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/robots-finally-at-home/)). That is a data-flywheel view of physical AI.\n- Contrarian views and calls: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Meet Amanda Huang, our newest Principal](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-amanda-huang-our-newest-principal/), 2026-02-03. BCV's own framing of her role (written by Slater Stich).\n2. [Robots, Finally at Home](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/robots-finally-at-home/), 2026-03-12. The Sunday Robotics thesis that she is linked on.\n3. [BCV AI Research Roundtable #1](https://luma.com/7bz2soa9), 2024-11. The roundtable format.\n4. [Cafe Compute, first edition](https://luma.com/agp2c7gw), 2024-11. Shows the Cerebras x BCV fellows origin.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found on X (fetch blocked). On Luma, her recurring co-hosts are:\n- Sarah Chieng and Cerebras.\n- Slater Stich, Daniel Kim, Zhenwei Gao and Diyi Zhu.\n- Mathurah Ravigulan, with design engineering events and \"Women Who Lift SF.\"\n- Ryan Kim and Auriel Wright.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Osmo (AI-native storytelling studio) | Seed | 2026 (CMS record 2026-08-05) | unknown | not found | [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22osmo%22%5D), [team page](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/amanda-huang/). Her only CMS-attributed deal (with Christina Melas-Kyriazi) |\n| Periodic Labs | Seed (BCV CMS) | 2025 (CMS record 2025-09-30) | no | a16z, Felicis, DST, NVentures, Accel, Jeff Bezos, Elad Gil, Eric Schmidt, Jeff Dean | [firm post](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-amanda-huang-our-newest-principal/), [periodic.com](https://periodic.com/). \"Key role\" per firm post; CMS partner is Slater Stich |\n| Black Forest Labs | Series B ($300M, $3.25B post) | 2025-12-01 | no (AMP and Salesforce Ventures led) | Temasek, Air Street, Lux, General Catalyst, NVIDIA, SV Angel and others | [release](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier). \"Key role\" per firm post; CMS stage is Growth, partner Slater Stich |\n| Polars | unknown (CMS year 2023) | 2023 per CMS | unknown | not found | [firm post](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-amanda-huang-our-newest-principal/). Year predates her CMS record, so her role is unclear |\n| Sunday Robotics | Early | 2026 | unknown | not found | [post](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/robots-finally-at-home/). She is linked on the post; CMS partner is Aaref Hilaly |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Periodic Labs:** BCV's CMS record was created 2025-09-30, the day Periodic publicly launched (inferred from the CMS timestamp). How BCV got in, and whether her research network was the path, is not sourced. BCV now lists Periodic first among its AI names in her event copy ([src](https://luma.com/hjzjnl1q)).\n- **Black Forest Labs:** BCV is listed in the Series B \"In Collaboration with\" group. It is not among the Series A investors (a16z led, with BroadLight, Creandum, Earlybird, GC, Northzone and NVIDIA) ([src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier)). It was a late, growth-style entry.\n- **Sunday Robotics:** the founders, Tony Zhao and Cheng Chi, met on X after noticing each other's papers ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/robots-finally-at-home/)). BCV's own entry path is not stated.\n- **Other deals:** nothing found on intro paths for any deal.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sarah Chieng (Cerebras) | Co-host of every Cafe Compute edition she hosted | 2024-11 | [src](https://luma.com/agp2c7gw) |\n| Cerebras | Cerebras x BCV fellows program; Cafe Compute co-sponsor | 2024-11 | [src](https://luma.com/agp2c7gw) |\n| Slater Stich (BCV) | Wrote her promotion post; CMS partner on her credited deals; Cafe Compute co-host | 2024-11 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-amanda-huang-our-newest-principal/), [src](https://luma.com/agp2c7gw) |\n| Christina Melas-Kyriazi (BCV) | Co-partner on Osmo | 2026 | [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22osmo%22%5D) |\n| Ryan Kim (BCV) | Co-host of the Intent panel | 2026-05 | [src](https://luma.com/vs9pfati) |\n| Aaref Hilaly (BCV) | Linked together on the Sunday Robotics post | 2026-03 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/robots-finally-at-home/) |\n| Auriel Wright (Google DeepMind) | Co-host of the Women in Post-Training dinner | 2026-03 | [src](https://luma.com/h2a1i71s) |\n| Neil Chowdhury (Transluce) | Paper author at Roundtable #1 | 2024-11 | [src](https://luma.com/7bz2soa9) |\n| Ronak Gandhi (Structify) | Co-hosted a BCV x Structify dinner | 2025-11 | [src](https://luma.com/n4o17ua6) |\n| Daniel Kim | Cafe Compute and NeurIPS run club co-host | 2024-11 | [src](https://luma.com/a73zusbg) |\n| Zhenwei Gao, Diyi Zhu | Cafe Compute co-hosts | 2025-10 / 2026-01 | [src](https://luma.com/am0i94on), [src](https://luma.com/i70zibrg) |\n| Mathurah Ravigulan | Co-host of Women Who Lift SF and design engineering club events | 2024-09 | [src](https://luma.com/v60bdkaq) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What was her actual role on Periodic Labs? Did she source the founders (Liam Fedus, Ekin Dogus Cubuk) through her research network?\n- What was the Polars \"key role\"? A 2025 Series A follow-on?\n- What is the Cerebras x BCV fellows program? Does BCV get first look at fellows' companies? How many fellows have raised money?\n- Were there Research Roundtables after #1? 61 of her 92 hosted events are not publicly listed, so later ones may be private.\n- She has no publicly listed hosted Luma events after 2026-07-23 (only 31 of 92 hosted events are public). Is she shifting from community to deal work after the promotion?\n- Her X posts: re-try with an authenticated tool.\n\n## Sources\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-amanda-huang-our-newest-principal/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/team/amanda-huang/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/robots-finally-at-home/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/structify-makes-building-custom-datasets-easy-with-ai/\n- BCV public Sanity CMS: https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production (queries for team slug amanda-huang and the portfolio slugs periodic-labs, black-forest-labs, polars, osmo, structify, sunday-robotics, tomo)\n- https://periodic.com/\n- https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier\n- https://osmo.inc/\n- https://luma.com/user/amandamhuang and api.lu.ma user profile events-hosting (user usr-lTpF5UDnGcqQ9Oz)\n- Luma events: agp2c7gw, 7bz2soa9, h2a1i71s, hjzjnl1q, jh51she4, kfifqpiy, 4ao3kqg7, i70zibrg, 79m3ny1l, vqfwj0wx, ea880drw, vs9pfati, am0i94on, a73zusbg, n4o17ua6, cchumanx26 (all at https://luma.com/<id>)\n- Limits: the web-search budget was exhausted this session; X (402) and LinkedIn (999) were blocked.\n", "person:amin-mirzadegan": "\n# Amin Mirzadegan\n\n*Updated 2026-11-10: web search was again unavailable (budget exhausted), so this run used direct fetches only. It adds the Greylock partners on Netic, confirms the Series A date from Netic's own press page, adds two new direct quotes from him, and confirms no Netic round in 2026 is listed. Two runs in a row have found no podcast, Substack, X account or talk by him.*\n\n## One-line take\nA Founders Fund partner, previously at General Atlantic, who is, per this repo's Founders Fund review (`firms/founders-fund.md`), the only non-GP there with a primary-source deal attribution. Bloomberg names him as \"the partner who led the deals\" in Netic, an AI-native automation company for home services, which Founders Fund backed at seed, Series A and Series B in a row [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm).\n\n## Crux\nMirzadegan's visible edge is conviction that compounds. He got Founders Fund into Netic as a follower in Greylock's seed, then led the next two rounds. That is the same seed-to-B \"three in a row\" pattern the firm has used only for Cognition and Anduril [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm). His stated thesis is that AI for \"Main Street\" small businesses (plumbers, roofers, pest control) is a large, under-served market, not only AI for enterprises [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm). The sharper version, in his own words: the hard part is distribution, not the model. He says reaching a customer in Gary, Indiana is harder than reaching one in Sunnyvale, and that Silicon Valley has historically neglected these businesses [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm). So he backs a founder who can sell to non-tech, non-coastal buyers, and once he sees traction he keeps reloading. His background in growth equity (General Atlantic) and his bio's late-stage fintech names (Stripe, Ramp) suggest he is comfortable pricing up a breakout round after round [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/amin-mirzadegan/). That last point is my inference, not something he has said. Constraints: he works inside a firm whose money now sits mostly in growth vehicles, and I found no public writing, podcast or X activity from him. So what he would never do is unknown from primary sources. He did not lead Netic's first round. Greylock led it.\n\n## Differential insight\n*Web search budget was exhausted for this run. Only WebFetch on known URLs was available, so this section is thin. No podcast, Substack, X or talk by him was found.*\n- **He personally led all three Founders Fund rounds in Netic.** Bloomberg describes him as \"the partner who led the deals\" (2025-11-13). Founders Fund participated in the Greylock-led seed, then led Series A and the $23M Series B at a $450M valuation [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm).\n- **Three consecutive early rounds is rare at Founders Fund.** Bloomberg paraphrases him (not a direct quote): \"Backing three early funding rounds in a row is something Founders Fund rarely does\". The article cites Cognition AI and Anduril as the prior cases (2025-11-13) [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm). This puts Netic in the firm's top conviction tier, and it is his call.\n- **His thesis is SMB AI, not enterprise AI:** \"The AI opportunity for serving Main Street companies, we feel, is enormous.\" (2025-11-13) [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm)\n- **Timing gap (original note):** Built In reported Netic's $20M raise from Greylock and Founders Fund on 2025-06-04, citing the WSJ [src](https://builtin.com/articles/netic-raises-20m-funding-20250604). The Series B followed about five months later (2025-11-13) [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm). If the June round is the Series A, which I infer but have not confirmed, he led two rounds within about five months.\n- **Distribution is the moat (new 2026-11-10).** He says reaching \"a customer in Gary, Indiana, is probably a lot harder\" than reaching one in Sunnyvale (2025-11-13) [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm). He also says such businesses \"don't get a lot of love or attention from Silicon Valley\" [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm). Read together, his bet is that go-to-market into non-tech SMBs is the hard, defensible part.\n- **The Greylock seed partners are named (new 2026-11-10).** Greylock lists Asheem Chandna and Sophia Luo (both Partners) on Netic, first in at seed [src](https://greylock.com/portfolio/netic/), [src](https://greylock.com/team/asheem-chandna/), [src](https://greylock.com/team/sophia-luo/). Luo is ex-Scale AI and ex-Character AI [src](https://greylock.com/team/sophia-luo/). Tokmak is also ex-Scale AI [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm). A Scale AI network link at seed is plausible but unconfirmed, and I found nothing on how Mirzadegan was brought in.\n- **Series A date confirmed by the company.** Netic's press page lists \"Netic raises $20M from Founders Fund and Greylock\" on 2025-06-02 (WSJ) [src](https://www.netic.ai/press). Bloomberg says Founders Fund led the A [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm). So the A was very likely the June 2025 $20M round, and the A-to-B gap was about five months and eleven days.\n- **No 2026 round is listed.** As of 2026-11-10, Netic's press page shows no round after the Series B [src](https://www.netic.ai/press).\n- **The founder chose to keep rounds small.** Tokmak said Netic kept its rounds at a sustainable size despite soaring AI valuations [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm). So Mirzadegan led a $23M B at $450M rather than a mega-round. That matters for how he prices.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Netic is exactly the AI-native automation category in `THESIS.md` (the Serval type): an AI platform that runs calls and operations for home-services businesses [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm). He backed it from seed and then led the A and the B. He is a partner, not a GP, doing a hot AI deal before he is famous, which fits the \"rising\" category. Caveats: I found no labs, pre-training or AI-chip deals attributed to him, and his other named companies (Stripe, Ramp, Ohalo) are fintech and ag-bio, mostly later stage. As a co-investor, he is the person to call at Founders Fund for vertical or SMB AI automation, not for a $600M lab seed. For that, John Luttig is the likelier contact (see `firms/founders-fund.md`).\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** BA in Economics, Yale [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/amin-mirzadegan/). No year found.\n- **General Atlantic:** investor before Founders Fund [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/amin-mirzadegan/). Dates not found.\n- **Founders Fund:** Partner. His team profile was posted 2021-09-27, which I take as his approximate start, though that is unconfirmed [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/amin-mirzadegan/). The bio says he has worked on investments \"across stages\", including Ramp, Stripe, Ohalo and Netic.\n- Pronouns: the Founders Fund bio and Bloomberg both use \"he\" [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/amin-mirzadegan/), [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://foundersfund.com/team/amin-mirzadegan/ | Firm bio, checked 2026-11-09 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | The `twitter` field in the FF team API record is empty (https://foundersfund.com/wp-json/wp/v2/team?per_page=100, checked 2026-11-09) |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The web search budget was exhausted, so this is not a full negative. On 2026-11-10, a search of the Founders Fund site's WordPress API for \"Mirzadegan\" returned only his team page. He has no FF blog post or Anatomy of Next episode [src](https://foundersfund.com/wp-json/wp/v2/search?search=Mirzadegan).\n\n## How they think\n- **SMB AI is a large market:** see the \"Main Street\" quote above [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm).\n- **Distribution beats tech access:** he says \"Main Street\" buyers are harder to reach than Bay Area ones and are underserved by Silicon Valley [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm).\n- **Concentrate on breakouts across rounds:** Bloomberg, paraphrasing him, frames three straight early rounds as a rare, deliberate signal [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm).\n- I found no contrarian views or calls, right or wrong, in his own words.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Bloomberg via Insurance Journal, 2025-11-13. This is the only source with his own words and the deal attribution [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm).\n2. His Founders Fund bio, for background and named portfolio [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/amin-mirzadegan/).\n3. `firms/founders-fund.md` in this repo, for firm context on Cognition and Anduril and the serial-lead pattern.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Netic | Seed | unknown (before 2025-06) | No. FF participated; Greylock led | Greylock | [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm) |\n| Netic | Series A (very likely the $20M Netic lists on 2025-06-02) | 2025-06-02 (inferred; company press page) | Yes (FF led) | Greylock, Hanabi Capital, Day One Ventures, SV Angel, angels | [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm), [src](https://builtin.com/articles/netic-raises-20m-funding-20250604) |\n| Netic | Series B, $23M at $450M valuation | 2025-11-13 | Yes (FF led) | not named in the article | [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm) |\n| Ramp | unknown | unknown | unknown (\"worked on\") | unknown | [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/amin-mirzadegan/) |\n| Stripe | unknown | unknown | unknown (\"worked on\") | unknown | [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/amin-mirzadegan/) |\n| Ohalo | unknown | unknown | unknown (\"worked on\") | unknown | [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/amin-mirzadegan/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n**Netic.** Founder Melisa Tokmak (ex-Facebook and Scale AI, Stanford) started Netic after her own HVAC problems in Marin County. Customers include roofers, plumbers and pest-control firms [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm). Greylock was first in: \"Its seed round was led by Greylock Partners with Founders Fund participating.\" Greylock's partners on the deal are Asheem Chandna and Sophia Luo [src](https://greylock.com/portfolio/netic/). The A ($20M, 2025-06-02 per Netic's press page) came next [src](https://www.netic.ai/press). Founders Fund came in alongside, then led the A and the B. Mirzadegan is named as the lead partner across all three [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm). Netic had 22 employees at the Series B [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm). Netic's site links to Forbes' 2026 Next Billion-Dollar Startups list (the page returned 403, so this is unverified) [src](https://www.netic.ai/). How Mirzadegan met Tokmak: not found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Founders Fund | Partner | 2021 (profile post date) | [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/amin-mirzadegan/) |\n| General Atlantic | Former investor | unknown | [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/amin-mirzadegan/) |\n| Netic / Melisa Tokmak | Lead partner on FF's seed, A and B | 2025 or earlier | [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm) |\n| Greylock | Seed lead in Netic; co-investor in A | 2025 or earlier | [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm) |\n| Asheem Chandna (Greylock Partner) | Co-investor in Netic (Greylock seed lead) | before 2025-06 | [src](https://greylock.com/portfolio/netic/) |\n| Sophia Luo (Greylock Partner, ex-Scale AI) | Co-investor in Netic (Greylock seed lead) | before 2025-06 | [src](https://greylock.com/portfolio/netic/) |\n| Hanabi Capital, Day One Ventures, SV Angel | Netic Series A co-investors | 2025 | [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Seed date and size for Netic. Answered: Greylock's partners are Chandna and Luo. Still open: which of them led, and did Luo's and Tokmak's Scale AI overlap drive the seed?\n- Founders Fund's check in each round. The A is now company-dated (2025-06-02) but not labelled \"Series A\" by Netic itself.\n- His role in Ramp, Stripe and Ohalo: was he the lead partner, and at which rounds?\n- His X handle, any podcasts, and other AI-automation deals since 2025-11.\n- Did Netic raise again in 2026? Its press page shows nothing as of 2026-11-10.\n- Any AI-automation deals he led after Netic. Needs web search, which was unavailable in both runs.\n- General Atlantic tenure and focus.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm (Bloomberg, Paayal Zaveri, 2025-11-13)\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-11-13/founders-fund-backs-startup-bringing-ai-to-plumbers-and-roofers (403, not read)\n- https://foundersfund.com/team/amin-mirzadegan/\n- https://foundersfund.com/wp-json/wp/v2/team?per_page=100 (team API; post date 2021-09-27, empty twitter field)\n- https://builtin.com/articles/netic-raises-20m-funding-20250604 (2025-06-04, citing WSJ)\n- https://www.netic.ai/\n- https://www.netic.ai/press (checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://greylock.com/portfolio/netic/ (checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://greylock.com/team/asheem-chandna/\n- https://greylock.com/team/sophia-luo/\n- https://foundersfund.com/wp-json/wp/v2/search?search=Mirzadegan (checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.forbes.com/sites/richardnieva/2026/07/28/next-billion-dollar-startups-2026/ (403, not read)\n", "person:amir-frenkel": "\n# Amir Frenkel\n\n## One-line take\nEclipse's first Chief AI Officer, an engineer who spent nearly a decade at Meta. Within five months of joining, Eclipse had built Noetive with them and led its $41M seed. They are the AI technical gate at a hardware-heavy firm the owner already rates (Cerebras) [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one), [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win.** Frenkel brings deep AI and machine-perception engineering from a big lab to a firm that is mostly hardware and industrial specialists. They also have unusual access: they say they have known the Eclipse team \"for nearly a decade\" [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one). Eclipse uses them both to check AI claims in diligence and to build companies, and Noetive was built with them from scratch [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy).\n- **What constrains them.** They are not a check-writer. The deal posts are signed by partners (Ryan Gibson and Lior Susan on Noetive), so allocation sits with Susan and the partners [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy). Their remit is AI applied to Eclipse's physical-economy thesis (manufacturing, logistics, construction), not frontier labs or pre-training.\n- **What they'd likely never do.** Back pure software or consumer AI with no physical-industry wedge. This is inferred from Eclipse's focus, not stated by Frenkel.\n\n## Differential insight\nCaveat: no podcast, Substack, X post or talk by Frenkel was found. The web-search budget for this run was exhausted, and LinkedIn, Bloomberg and WSJ all blocked fetching. Every item below comes from Eclipse's own blog. The quotes are primary and verbatim, but at best moderately non-obvious.\n\n- **Noetive was built inside the firm, not sourced.** Eclipse writes that it built the company \"from the ground up with Eclipse Chief AI Officer Amir Frenkel\" [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy). In effect, the CAIO role works as an in-house venture-creation seat.\n- **The thesis came from Frenkel's own experience.** \"Amir saw the chance to build the next company from inside that experience.\" The \"experience\" is Eclipse's history in physical-industry AI [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy).\n- **Pedigree.** \"Amir joined Eclipse earlier this year after nearly a decade at Meta\" [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy). The Bloomberg headline slug also says the hire came \"from Meta\" [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2026-04-28/vc-firm-eclipse-hires-first-chief-ai-officer-from-meta) (paywalled and not read).\n- **A long relationship before the hire.** \"I've known the Eclipse team for nearly a decade.\" [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one)\n- **The job, in their words.** \"I'll work directly with founders on AI strategy, product direction\" [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one). They also do diligence on AI-driven companies with the investment team and are meant to help make Eclipse an AI-native firm [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one).\n- **How they see themselves.** \"I am a builder at heart\" [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one).\n- **Timing.** There is no first-in/follower gap to report. Eclipse's involvement predates the company, since it was built with Frenkel. Noetive came out of stealth with the full seed syndicate announced at once and Eclipse as lead [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy). No founding date is given. The time from Frenkel's start (2026-04-28) to the seed announcement (2026-09-16) is about 4.5 months.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Frenkel is not an allocator, so they will not show up next to a ~$20M seed check as a lead. Two things still make them worth tracking:\n1. **Noetive** is the closest thing at Eclipse to a Serval-style AI-native automation company. It runs autonomous operations (\"dynamic production planning and manifest consolidation\") for manufacturing and logistics, rather than IT [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy). It is industrial, not horizontal. This matches how `people/lior-susan.md` grades it.\n2. They are the **technical AI diligence gate** at Eclipse, a firm THESIS.md names as \"my type\" (Cerebras). If Eclipse forms more AI companies in-house or co-invests in AI-heavy physical-economy seeds, Frenkel is likely the person who checks the AI.\nIt falls short of high because they don't touch frontier labs, pre-training or chips, and no sourced check size or allocation authority exists.\n\n## Background\n- Engineer \"since the 1990s\" [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one).\n- Worked on architectures, systems and products across consumer electronics, distributed systems, AI and machine perception. Products they name are smartphones, smart speakers (Amazon Echo) and AR/VR. These are products, not confirmed employers [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one).\n- Has focused on core AI and machine-perception technology for more than a decade [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one).\n- Nearly a decade at Meta before Eclipse. Role title and dates are not sourced [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy).\n- Became Eclipse's first Chief AI Officer on 2026-04-28 [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one).\n- Education: nothing found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://eclipse.capital/team/amir-frenkel | Team page; the fetch returned only the directory listing (2026-11-09) |\n| Substack / blog | https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one | One bylined Eclipse post (2026-04-28) |\n| X | nothing found | Only the firm's @EclipseVentures is linked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/amir-frenkel-b04b46/ | Linked from the day-one post; fetch blocked, so not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. Search budget was exhausted this run, so this is not a confirmed absence.\n\n## How they think\n- AI is the biggest platform shift they have seen: \"For the first time, intelligence itself can be deployed at scale\" [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one).\n- Their framing is applied and builder-first, about AI deployed into real-world operations. Noetive's internal motto is \"Solve, productize, platformize.\" [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy)\n- Contrarian views and calls they got right or wrong: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Day One: Why I'm Joining Eclipse](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one) (2026-04-28): their only bylined piece, with their background and remit.\n2. [Operational intelligence for the physical economy](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy) (2026-09-16, Gibson and Susan): Noetive, built with Frenkel.\n3. Bloomberg newsletter on the hire (paywalled, not read): https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2026-04-28/vc-firm-eclipse-hires-first-chief-ai-officer-from-meta\n4. WSJ Pro on Noetive's raise (paywalled, not read): https://www.wsj.com/pro/venture-capital/noetive-raises-41-million-to-create-a-chatgpt-moment-for-physical-industries-51645088\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No personal X account was identified.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Noetive | Seed, $41M (emerged from stealth) | 2026-09-16 | Eclipse led; company built with Frenkel | Craft Ventures, The Westly Group, Swish Ventures, Factory, Incite Ventures, Gigascale Capital, Operator Partners, Liquid 2 Ventures, angels incl. Zach Frenkel | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Noetive.** Eclipse says it has focused on AI in physical industries since it began. Frenkel joined, saw a chance to build \"from inside that experience\", and Eclipse built the company with them from the ground up. The team includes AI researchers and engineers from Meta, Google and Amazon, plus operators who ran plants for Fortune 500 companies. Its initial design partners are in food manufacturing and logistics. The company came out of stealth on 2026-09-16 with a $41M seed led by Eclipse [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy). The post positions Noetive next to Eclipse companies Bedrock Robotics and Mytra. No CEO is named in the sources read.\n- Other deals: nothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Eclipse | Chief AI Officer (first) | 2026-04-28 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one) |\n| Eclipse team | Known \"for nearly a decade\" before joining | ~2016 (approximate, per their own words) | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one) |\n| Meta | Prior employer, nearly a decade | ~2016–2026 (approximate) | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy) |\n| Noetive | Company Eclipse built with Frenkel; exact title not sourced | 2026 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy) |\n| Lior Susan (Eclipse) | Colleague; co-authored the Noetive post | 2026 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy) |\n| Ryan Gibson (Eclipse) | Colleague; co-authored the Noetive post | 2026 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who is Noetive's CEO? Is Frenkel a co-founder, executive, board member or shareholder? WSJ Pro likely says but was not readable.\n- Noetive's valuation and Eclipse's check size within the $41M.\n- Zach Frenkel, an angel in the Noetive seed, shares the surname. Any relationship is unverified.\n- Frenkel's Meta title and team (Reality Labs? FAIR? perception?), and their employers before Meta.\n- Does Frenkel do diligence on Eclipse's other AI deals (for example Parallax), and do they have any allocation role?\n- Any podcasts, talks or X activity. Re-run web search when budget allows.\n- Watch out: noetive.com is an unrelated strategy consultancy with the same name, not this company.\n\n## Sources\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy\n- https://eclipse.capital/team (directory listing \"Amir Frenkel, Chief AI Officer\")\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2026-04-28/vc-firm-eclipse-hires-first-chief-ai-officer-from-meta (headline/slug only)\n- https://www.wsj.com/pro/venture-capital/noetive-raises-41-million-to-create-a-chatgpt-moment-for-physical-industries-51645088 (not readable)\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/amir-frenkel-b04b46/ (not readable)\n", "person:amjad-masad": "\n# Amjad Masad\n\n## One-line take\nReplit's CEO writes small, frequent angel cheques into developer and AI-infrastructure founders; he was in Perplexity's 2022 seed and shows up in Etched, LiveKit, Resolve AI, Basis and Chroma. He is a signal and a distribution partner, not an allocator.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why founders want him:** Replit is a distribution channel and a customer for developer and AI-infra tools, and he is a loud public voice with a large technical following. A cheque from him is a product endorsement from someone who ships to tens of millions of builders.\n- **Vehicle and size:** personal cheques. His own investor profile on NFX Signal lists a $5K-$50K range and a $25K sweet spot ([src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/amjad-masad)). No fund, Form D or adviser registration found. Aggregator profiles that say $100K-$500K give no source.\n- **Constraint:** tiny tickets and a full-time CEO job, so he rarely leads and does not negotiate. The one round he led publicly was Synthesis ($12M with Balaji Srinivasan, 2022).\n- **Controversy:** a January 2026 profile reports some investors distanced themselves from him over his Gaza posts ([src](https://sfstandard.com/2026/01/07/called-terrorist-sympathizer-now-ai-company-valued-3b/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- Cheque size is small by his own filing: \"$25K\" sweet spot on Signal ([src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/amjad-masad)). Mirrored in insights.\n- He invests in complements to Replit: \"Synthesis is the complement of Replit.\" ([src](https://amasad.me/synthesis)). Read the AI list the same way: Chroma, Contextual, Argmax, SkyPilot, LiveKit, Raindrop and Patronus are all things Replit-built apps and agents consume.\n- \"I'm probably the only contrarian in Silicon Valley\" ([src](https://sfstandard.com/2026/01/07/called-terrorist-sympathizer-now-ai-company-valued-3b/)).\n- I found no podcast or post where he explains how he picks angel deals. That is a gap, not evidence that he has said nothing.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. Not a capital partner at my size, but a strong early-signal angel for AI infrastructure and agents, and an AI chips believer (Etched Series A and C, Extropic). Deals that matter to me: Etched, Perplexity seed, Essential AI seed, Resolve AI seed, Basis.\n\n## Background\n- Born in Amman, Jordan; computer science at Princess Sumaya University for Technology (2010) ([src](https://sfstandard.com/2026/01/07/called-terrorist-sympathizer-now-ai-company-valued-3b/)).\n- Founding engineer at Codecademy; software engineer at Facebook.\n- Co-founded Replit with Haya Odeh in 2016; YC 2018; a16z-backed; $250M at $3B in September 2025.\n- Mentored by Paul Graham; close to Marc Andreessen.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://amasad.me/ | essays; no portfolio page |\n| Substack / blog | https://amasad.me/rss | |\n| X | https://twitter.com/amasad | very active; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/amasad | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-07 — a16z (Erik Torenberg) — Going Direct, Building Replit, and the Future of Software — [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/amjad-masad-on-going-direct-building-replit-and-the-future-of-software/) — video only, not transcribed.\n- n.d. — Founders in Arms — How AI will change programming — [link](https://foundersinarms.substack.com/p/how-ai-will-change-programming-with) — transcript exists, not mined.\n- Joe Rogan Experience and Lex Fridman appearances are mentioned in profiles; episodes not located.\n\n## How they think\n- Software creation is being opened to non-programmers; agents are the product (Replit Agent, 2024).\n- Backs tools his own platform would use, and founders who \"go direct\" in public.\n- Contrarian by self-description; publicly fights with other investors (Josh Wolfe, Keith Rabois, per SF Standard).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Investing in Synthesis](https://amasad.me/synthesis) (2022): the only investment memo he has published.\n- [SF Standard profile](https://sfstandard.com/2026/01/07/called-terrorist-sympathizer-now-ai-company-valued-3b/) (2026-01): background and relationships.\n- [How to Keep Winning](https://amasad.me/keep-winning): not mined.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed in detail. SF Standard lists public clashes with Elon Musk, Josh Wolfe and Keith Rabois.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI cheques. \"Ledger\" = already in data/rounds.csv before this pass; lead names on ledger rows are the ledger's. Stage flag marks pre-seed and seed entries. Cheque sizes are never disclosed per deal.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Seed-stage? | Led? | Lead / co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Perplexity | Seed | 2022-09 | yes | No | Elad Gil, Nat Friedman (co-leads); Karpathy, Abbeel, LeCun | ledger ([src](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/announcing-our-series-a-funding-round-and-mobile-app-launch)) |\n| Chroma | Seed $18M | 2023-04 | yes | No | Quiet Capital (Astasia Myers) | ledger |\n| Essential AI | Seed $8.3M | 2023-05 | yes | No | Thrive Capital | ledger |\n| Contextual AI | Seed $20M | 2023-06 | yes | No | Bain Capital Ventures | ledger |\n| Patronus AI | Seed $3M | 2023-09 | yes | No | Lightspeed | ledger |\n| LiveKit | Series A $22.5M | 2024-06 | no | No | Altimeter | ledger |\n| Etched | Series A $120M; Series C | 2024-06; 2026-07 | no | No | Primary, Positive Sum; later a16z, Thiel, Karpathy | ledger |\n| Resolve AI | Seed | 2024-10 | yes | No | Greylock | ledger |\n| Argmax | Seed $8M | 2024-11 | yes | No | Salesforce Ventures | ledger |\n| Roboflow | Series B | 2024-11 | no | No | GV | ledger |\n| Basis | Series A $34M; Series B | 2024-12; 2026-02 | no | No | Khosla | ledger |\n| Extropic | undisclosed | n/a | n/a | No | n/a | ledger |\n| Raindrop | Seed $15M | 2025-12 | yes | No | Lightspeed | ledger |\n| SkyPilot | Seed $20M+ | 2026-07 | yes | No | Lux (lead); Amplify, Coatue, Jeff Dean | ledger |\n| Phonic | Series A | 2026-10 | no | No | First Round Capital | ledger |\n| **Wabi** | Pre-seed $20M | 2025-11-05 | yes | No | Naval Ravikant, Garry Tan, Justin Kan, Sarah Guo | new ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/05/replika-founder-raises-20m-pre-seed-for-wabi-the-youtube-of-apps)) |\n| **Granola** | Series B $43M | 2025-05-14 | no | No | n/a | new, aggregator ([src](https://shizune.co/investors/profile/amjad-masad)) |\n| **Alta** | Seed $11M | 2025-06-16 | yes | No | n/a | new, aggregator (same) |\n| **Julius AI** | Seed $10M | 2025-07-28 | yes | No | n/a | new, aggregator (same) |\n| **1001 AI** | Pre-seed $9M | 2025-10-21 | yes | No | n/a | new, aggregator (same) |\n| **QuiverAI** | Seed | 2026-02-25 | yes | No | n/a | new, aggregator ([src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/amjad-masad)) |\n| **Avec** | Seed $8.4M | 2026-04 | yes | No | n/a | new, aggregator ([src](https://buttondown.com/AngelRegistry/archive/angel-registry-update-april-9-2026-mobile-format/)) |\n\nListed by aggregators with no round I could pin down, so not written as rows: Runway, Synthesia, XBOW ([Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/investors/amjad-masad/)); Mastra ([xraise](https://xraise.ai/blog/amjad-masad-angel-investor/)). Non-AI: Synthesis (co-led $12M, 2022), Warp (payroll; Series A 2025, B 2026), Tsuga, Sundial. Databases count 52 (CB Insights) to 71 (PitchBook) investments in total; Signal shows 66-70.\n\n**Hit pattern:** of roughly 22 identified AI cheques, 14 were at pre-seed or seed. The ones that became hot: Perplexity (seed), Etched (Series A, now $10.3B), LiveKit, Basis ($1.15B), Resolve AI, Wabi. No public statement of a pass or regret found.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Synthesis (2022):** visited the Ad Astra school, heard progress from the CEO, and led the $12M round with Balaji Srinivasan: \"what Josh created blew me away.\" ([src](https://amasad.me/synthesis)).\n- **Perplexity seed (2022):** named with Karpathy, Abbeel, LeCun and Vaswani in the seed co-led by Elad Gil and Nat Friedman. How he met Aravind Srinivas is not documented in what I read.\n- No founder quote on why they took his money was found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Replit | Co-founder, CEO | 2016 | [src](https://sfstandard.com/2026/01/07/called-terrorist-sympathizer-now-ai-company-valued-3b/) |\n| Balaji Srinivasan | Co-led Synthesis | 2022 | [src](https://amasad.me/synthesis) |\n| Elad Gil, Nat Friedman | Leads he followed (Perplexity seed) | 2022 | ledger |\n| Andrej Karpathy | Repeated co-angel (Perplexity seed, Etched Series C) | 2022 | ledger |\n| Naval Ravikant, Garry Tan, Sarah Guo | Co-angels in Wabi | 2025 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/05/replika-founder-raises-20m-pre-seed-for-wabi-the-youtube-of-apps) |\n| Paul Graham | Mentor | n/a | SF Standard |\n| Marc Andreessen / a16z | Replit backer; personal rapport | n/a | SF Standard |\n\n## Open questions\n- His own words on angel investing: check X (@amasad) and the Founders in Arms transcript.\n- Which round he entered Basis and Extropic.\n- Whether Replit itself (not Masad) invests; no corporate venture arm found.\n- Full Signal list (66-70 deals) sits behind a login.\n\n## Sources\n- https://signal.nfx.com/investors/amjad-masad\n- https://amasad.me/ ; https://amasad.me/synthesis\n- https://sfstandard.com/2026/01/07/called-terrorist-sympathizer-now-ai-company-valued-3b/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/05/replika-founder-raises-20m-pre-seed-for-wabi-the-youtube-of-apps\n- Aggregators (secondary): https://shizune.co/investors/profile/amjad-masad ; https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/amjad-masad ; https://dealroom.co/investors/amjad-masad/ ; https://buttondown.com/AngelRegistry/archive/angel-registry-update-april-9-2026-mobile-format/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/amjad-masad-on-going-direct-building-replit-and-the-future-of-software/\n", "person:anders-ranum": "\n# Anders Ranum\n\n## One-line take\nA fifteen-year Sapphire partner who buys into AI infrastructure once it is the category standard (LangChain, Baseten) and who made one early move in 2026: Reactor's Series A extension alongside NVIDIA.\n\n## Crux\nHe wins by offering enterprise go-to-market reach from a firm that grew out of SAP, to companies that already have the developer mindshare. He is constrained by stage and price: Sapphire leads Series C and D rounds (Tractian) and joins Series B rounds led by others (LangChain). He would not fund a pre-product lab; his own test is \"evidence of monetization\" [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/ai-ma-ipo-valuations-b2b-ranum-sapphire-ventures/). Reactor is the exception to watch.\n\n## Differential insight\n- On the valuation gap between public software and private AI: \"Those two things can't both be right indefinitely\" [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/ai-ma-ipo-valuations-b2b-ranum-sapphire-ventures/).\n- On robotics: \"the gradient approach beats the all-or-nothing bet for near-term value creation\" [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/ai-ma-ipo-valuations-b2b-ranum-sapphire-ventures/).\n- On Reactor: \"the founder-market fit was immediately obvious\" [src](https://sapphireventures.com/blog/sapphire-ventures-backs-reactor-real-time-world-models/).\n- On what makes applied AI defensible: \"no website shows the vibration signature of a gearbox three weeks before it seizes.\" [src](https://sapphireventures.com/blog/sapphire-ventures-deepens-tractian-partnership-series-d/).\n- He rarely writes alone: LangChain with Rajeev Dham, Reactor with Jai Das, Tractian with Demi Obayomi.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nLow. Growth-stage enterprise investor. Relevant only as a follow-on buyer for AI infrastructure companies after Series A, and for the Reactor cheque.\n\n## Background\nBorn in Trondheim, Norway; bachelor's and MBA from the University of South Dakota; 12 years at SAP in product management and strategy, including work on the Business Objects and Sybase acquisitions; joined Sapphire Ventures in 2012; more than two dozen investments since [src](https://sapphireventures.com/team-member/anders-ranum/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://sapphireventures.com/team-member/anders-ranum/ | firm bio |\n| Substack / blog | https://sapphireventures.com/blog/ | co-authors investment posts, a few a year |\n| X | https://twitter.com/aranum | linked from bio; not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/andersranum/ | linked from bio |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-07 — Crunchbase News (email interview) — Welcome To The 'Show Me' Era — [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/ai-ma-ipo-valuations-b2b-ranum-sapphire-ventures/) — valuation gap, M&A, robotics\n- No podcast appearance found.\n\n## How they think\n- AI plus SaaS, not AI versus SaaS: \"I don't think the narrative is AI vs. SaaS.\" [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/ai-ma-ipo-valuations-b2b-ranum-sapphire-ventures/).\n- Software M&A is repricing, not frozen; expects IPOs to wait for 2027 for most companies [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/ai-ma-ipo-valuations-b2b-ranum-sapphire-ventures/).\n- Near-term robotics value is in constrained industrial settings, with hardware mainly as a way to collect data [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/ai-ma-ipo-valuations-b2b-ranum-sapphire-ventures/).\n\n## Read these first\n- Crunchbase News interview, 2026-07-13 [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/ai-ma-ipo-valuations-b2b-ranum-sapphire-ventures/).\n- Reactor investment post, 2026-10-05 [src](https://sapphireventures.com/blog/sapphire-ventures-backs-reactor-real-time-world-models/).\n- Tractian Series D post, 2026-10-22 [src](https://sapphireventures.com/blog/sapphire-ventures-deepens-tractian-partnership-series-d/).\n- LangChain Series B post, 2025-10-21 [src](https://sapphireventures.com/blog/langchain-series-b-agentic-ai-infrastructure/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI-relevant first; full list from his bio [src](https://sapphireventures.com/team-member/anders-ranum/).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Reactor | Series A extension | 2026-10-05 | no | NVIDIA | [src](https://sapphireventures.com/blog/sapphire-ventures-backs-reactor-real-time-world-models/) |\n| LangChain | Series B ($125M) | 2025-10-21 | no | | [src](https://sapphireventures.com/blog/langchain-series-b-agentic-ai-infrastructure/) |\n| Tractian | Series C | 2024 | yes | | [src](https://sapphireventures.com/blog/sapphire-ventures-deepens-tractian-partnership-series-d/) |\n| Tractian | Series D ($130M) | 2026-10-22 | yes | | [src](https://sapphireventures.com/blog/sapphire-ventures-deepens-tractian-partnership-series-d/) |\n| Baseten | round not found | | | | [src](https://sapphireventures.com/team-member/anders-ranum/) |\n| Alation | Series C ($50M) | 2019-01-17 | yes | Salesforce Ventures | ledger |\n| WorkOS, Kong, InfluxData, JupiterOne, Exabeam, Feedzai | on bio; rounds not researched | | | | [src](https://sapphireventures.com/team-member/anders-ranum/) |\n| Exits: Auth0 (Okta, 2021), Portworx (Pure Storage, 2020), Contentful (Salesforce, 2026), Dremio (SAP, 2026), Cyphort (Juniper, 2017), OpsRamp | | | | | [src](https://sapphireventures.com/team-member/anders-ranum/) |\n\nNone of these is a pre-seed or seed cheque.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Reactor**: Sapphire joined with NVIDIA as the company entered its next stage; the post credits the founders (ex-Apple Vision Pro; CEO Alberto Taiuti co-founded Luma AI) and names Overworld as first paying customer [src](https://sapphireventures.com/blog/sapphire-ventures-backs-reactor-real-time-world-models/). How they met is not stated.\n- **Tractian**: Sapphire led the Series C in 2024 and then led the $130M Series D in October 2026 [src](https://sapphireventures.com/blog/sapphire-ventures-deepens-tractian-partnership-series-d/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sapphire Ventures | Partner | 2012 | [src](https://sapphireventures.com/team-member/anders-ranum/) |\n| SAP | former employer (12 years) | to 2012 | [src](https://sapphireventures.com/team-member/anders-ranum/) |\n| Jai Das | co-investor on Reactor | 2026-10 | [src](https://sapphireventures.com/blog/sapphire-ventures-backs-reactor-real-time-world-models/) |\n| Rajeev Dham | co-investor on LangChain | 2025-10 | [src](https://sapphireventures.com/blog/langchain-series-b-agentic-ai-infrastructure/) |\n| Demi Obayomi | principal on Tractian | 2026-10 | [src](https://sapphireventures.com/blog/sapphire-ventures-deepens-tractian-partnership-series-d/) |\n| NVIDIA | co-investor in Reactor | 2026-10 | [src](https://sapphireventures.com/blog/sapphire-ventures-backs-reactor-real-time-world-models/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Why the owner picked him: the Reactor cheque is the only early-stage AI link found.\n- Which Baseten round Sapphire joined and whether he holds any AI board seat.\n- Date he was made partner.\n\n## Sources\nhttps://sapphireventures.com/team-member/anders-ranum/ ; https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/ai-ma-ipo-valuations-b2b-ranum-sapphire-ventures/ ; https://sapphireventures.com/blog/sapphire-ventures-backs-reactor-real-time-world-models/ ; https://sapphireventures.com/blog/sapphire-ventures-deepens-tractian-partnership-series-d/ ; https://sapphireventures.com/blog/langchain-series-b-agentic-ai-infrastructure/\n", "person:andrea-wang": "\n# Andrea Wang\n\n*Created 2026-11-10 (nea-and-names sweep). Builds on the SV Angel firm file and the earlier rows in `data/relationships.csv`. Sources: SV Angel team page (live and Wayback captures), General Catalyst team page and stories (Wayback), her own Substack, and secondary aggregators labelled as such.*\n\n## One-line take\nShe is a product-growth operator (Lime, Amplitude) who spent two years doing seed B2B and applied-AI deals at General Catalyst. Pylon's GC-led seed is her one deal with a byline. She is now SV Angel's newest General Partner, which makes her a relationship node into Stanford and operator-founder networks, not a frontier-lab check writer [src](https://svangel.com/about/team) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-pylon).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** operator empathy and go-to-market depth. She built Amplitude's product-growth team from Series D through the direct listing, and was an early Lime employee from seed to Series D [src](https://svangel.com/about/team). Her GC writing is a three-part product-led-growth (PLG) series for early founders, so her pitch to founders is help with 0-to-1 GTM [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/sales-led-vs-product-led-growth). She looks for founders who are former operators with first-hand pain: \"companies born out of former operators who experienced pain\" [src](https://andreawang.substack.com/p/why-i-angel-invest).\n- **What constrains her:** SV Angel's seed vehicle is small (SV Angel X sold $40M to 29 investors) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2039333/000203933325000001/primary_doc.xml), and the firm co-invests rather than leads (see `firms/sv-angel.md`). At GC she was part of a team led by senior partners (Niko Bonatsos co-bylined Pylon). No deal is publicly attributed to her at SV Angel after about 19 months there.\n- **What she'd never do (inference):** lead a research-lab or hardware round. Her stated theses are applied AI, departmental software, AR/VR, marketplaces and e-commerce [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20241213045819/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/andrea-wang). Nothing she has written touches pre-training, chips or compute.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **She was an angel before she was a VC, with her own published thesis.** In May 2022, still at Amplitude, she wrote that she \"started angel investing in pre-seed and seed stage consumer and enterprise startups\" about a year earlier [src](https://andreawang.substack.com/p/why-i-angel-invest). So her angel book dates from about 2021. Its companies are unknown.\n- **Her stated sourcing filter comes from art history, not finance.** She says she studied Vermeer for his \"discovering beauty and potential in ordinary people\" and links that to spotting founders early [src](https://andreawang.substack.com/p/why-i-angel-invest).\n- **East-to-west pattern matching.** She grew up in Shanghai. She says she thinks about \"how consumer trends in the east might manifest in the west\", and that this is why she joined Lime [src](https://andreawang.substack.com/p/why-i-angel-invest).\n- **She has run a China-leaders talent network for about ten years.** She co-founded Lizard Institute, \"a cohort-based summer incubator\" for future leaders of China, and a bilingual e-magazine with chapters at four universities [src](https://andreawang.substack.com/p/why-i-angel-invest). This is a sourcing channel that is not on any firm page.\n- **Pylon timing.** GC led Pylon's seed in 2023 (\"led their seed round last year\", written 2024-08-28). The post is bylined Niko Bonatsos, Andrea Wang and Tamanna Khemani [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-pylon). a16z then led a $17M Series A in August 2024, about a year after GC's seed [src](https://finsmes.com/2024/08/pylon-raises-17m-in-series-a-funding.html).\n- **SV Angel join window.** She is absent from the SV Angel team-page capture of 2025-02-14 and present, already titled General Partner, on 2025-04-25 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250425222104/https://svangel.com/about/team). Her GC page was still live on 2025-03-29 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250329203327/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/andrea-wang). No announcement was found, so the join date rests on Wayback captures.\n- **Mercor seed (aggregator, unconfirmed).** Signal/NFX's scraped experience list for her includes \"Seed Investor Mercor\" next to \"Investor and Board Observer Pylon\" [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/andrea-wang_1). GC led Mercor's $3.6M seed, and the repo's GC row has no confirmed partner. Her GC team page never listed Mercor as a featured investment. If true, she was on the GC seed team for a company that became a $10B lab supplier.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium, leaning low.** She is a real seed investor with a GP title at a firm whose portfolio wall includes every frontier lab, so she is useful as a relationship node. Her own book is B2B SaaS and applied AI (Pylon customer support, Amigo clinical agents, Aaru simulation), not pre-training, labs or chips. Her only possible frontier-adjacent deal is Mercor, and her role there is unconfirmed. The Flapping Airplanes link in the brief could not be confirmed (see Open questions). Raise this to medium-high only if an SV Angel lab allocation turns out to run through her.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Shanghai, came to the US for college [src](https://andreawang.substack.com/p/why-i-angel-invest).\n- Stanford: BA Economics with an Art History minor; MS Management Science and Engineering. Studied art history at Stanford and Oxford (Stanford in Oxford) for about 2.5 years [src](https://svangel.com/about/team) [src](https://andreawang.substack.com/p/why-i-angel-invest).\n- Investment banking at Bank of America Merrill Lynch, then management consulting at Kearney (dates not found) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20241213045819/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/andrea-wang).\n- Lime: early employee, pre-launch, seed to Series D. Worked across sales, marketing, strategy, product and business operations; helped craft the Series B story and secured the first city contracts (dates not found) [src](https://andreawang.substack.com/p/why-i-angel-invest).\n- Amplitude: Growth Product Lead / Principal PM, Growth. Built the product-growth team from Series D through the direct listing; still there in May 2022 [src](https://andreawang.substack.com/p/why-i-angel-invest) [src](https://amplitude.com/blog/author/andrea-wang).\n- Angel investor from about 2021 [src](https://andreawang.substack.com/p/why-i-angel-invest).\n- General Catalyst, SF, seed investing team, 2023-2025. Her team page was live by 2023-05-30 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230530052908/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/andrea-wang), and SV Angel says she \"led seed enterprise investing in the SF Bay Area\" there [src](https://svangel.com/about/team). Her GC title is unconfirmed: GC's page said \"Investment Team\", and aggregators say Partner or Principal.\n- SV Angel, General Partner, early 2025 to present [src](https://svangel.com/about/team).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://svangel.com/about/team | Firm bio, checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://andreawang.substack.com | One post (\"Why I angel invest\", 2022-05-16); dormant as of 2026-11-10 |\n| X | https://twitter.com/AndreaShuyuWang | Linked from her Substack and GC page; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/andreashuyuwang/ | Linked from the SV Angel team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Date not found — Pocus AMA — \"Growth Product Management: Scaling B2B in PLG with Andrea Wang\" — https://pocus.com/ama/growth-product-management-scaling-b2b-in-plg-with-andrea-wang — from her Amplitude era; not mined.\n- Date not found — ProductLed Summit speaker page — https://pls5.productled.com/speakers/andrea-wang — not mined.\n- Date not found — \"Unlocking Revenue: Andrea Wang (Amplitude)\" event — https://lu.ma/nuys6061 — not mined.\n- No investing-era podcast found (checked web search for her name with General Catalyst or SV Angel plus podcast or interview).\n\n## How they think\n- 0-to-1 is her favourite stage: \"Early stage (0-1) is my favorite stage of investing\" [src](https://andreawang.substack.com/p/why-i-angel-invest).\n- Product-minded, customer-obsessed operator-founders. At GC, \"ambitious, product-minded founders who are former operators\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20241213045819/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/andrea-wang).\n- In GTM tooling she backs AI that \"re-imagine[s] workflows\" used across several teams (the Pylon thesis) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-pylon).\n- PLG vs sales-led: a recurring topic. \"One of my favorite topics to discuss with founders is 0-1 go-to-market\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/sales-led-vs-product-led-growth).\n- Calls right or wrong: Pylon went from GC seed (2023) to a16z A (2024) to a reported ~$31M B (2025-08) [src](https://startupintros.com/news/pylon-series-b-2025-08) (aggregator).\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Why I angel invest\" (2022) — her thesis, sourcing style and background in her own words. https://andreawang.substack.com/p/why-i-angel-invest\n2. \"Our Investment in Pylon\" (GC, 2024-08-28) — how she writes a seed memo. https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-pylon\n3. \"Sales-Led vs. Product-Led Growth\" (GC, 2024-07-23) — her GTM advice to founders. https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/sales-led-vs-product-led-growth\n4. \"Experimenting with Product-Led Growth\" (GC, 2024-06-25). https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/experimenting-with-product-led-growth\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked: X and LinkedIn need login to read. The only public cadence found is the Substack, with one post in 2022.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Pylon | Seed (GC led; ~$3.2M per aggregators) | 2023 | yes (GC) | not stated in GC post | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-pylon) — co-bylined; Signal also lists her as board observer (aggregator) |\n| Amigo AI | Seed ($6.3M; GC and GSV Ventures co-led) | 2024-11 | yes (GC co-lead) | GSV Ventures, Comma Capital, CohoVC | Featured on her GC team page [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20241213045819/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/andrea-wang); round via [src](https://alleywatch.com/2024/11/the-alleywatch-startup-daily-funding-report-11-21-2024/). Her personal role is from the featured list only |\n| Aaru | Seed (undisclosed) | 2024 | unknown | A*, Abstract, Z Fellows (reported) | Featured on her GC page by 2025-03 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250329203327/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/andrea-wang), but GC's company page names David Fialkow and Marc Bhargava [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/aaru). Not written as a deal row |\n| Podqi | undisclosed (GC \"backed since 2025\") | 2025 | unknown | | Featured on her GC page [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250329203327/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/andrea-wang); GC company page names Fialkow and Bhargava [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/podqi). Not written as a deal row |\n| Mercor | Seed ($3.6M, GC led) | 2023/2024 (sources differ) | GC led | | Aggregator only: Signal/NFX experience list [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/andrea-wang_1). Unconfirmed; not written as a deal row |\n| Bandana | Seed ($4M) | 2023-09 | unknown | | Aggregator search snippet (Signal/NFX); not verified. Not written as a deal row |\n| SV Angel deals (2025-) | — | — | — | — | none attributed to her found |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Pylon:** YC W23 founders (Marty Kausas, Advith Chelikani, Robert Eng). GC led the seed in 2023 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-pylon). A secondary profile says Kausas closed GC \"at a sushi dinner\" in a six-day raise during the SVB collapse [src](https://yespress.io/marty-kausas). That is unverified, and it does not name her. The intro path is not found.\n- **Amigo AI:** a clinical AI-agent company; GC and GSV Ventures co-led a $6.3M seed (Nov 2024). Madrona led an $11M Series A (2026-03-10) [src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260310898882/en). Her personal role is not confirmed beyond the featured list on her page.\n- Others: nothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| SV Angel | at: General Partner | 2025 | [src](https://svangel.com/about/team) |\n| General Catalyst | at: seed investing team (title unconfirmed) | 2023–2025 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230530052908/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/andrea-wang) |\n| Amplitude | at: Growth Product Lead | to ~2023 | [src](https://andreawang.substack.com/p/why-i-angel-invest) |\n| Lime | at: early employee | seed to Series D | [src](https://svangel.com/about/team) |\n| Niko Bonatsos | co-authored the Pylon investment post at GC | 2024 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-pylon) |\n| Tamanna Khemani | co-authored the Pylon investment post at GC | 2024 | same |\n| Mike Sho Liu | fellow SV Angel GP, also Stanford MS&E | 2025 | [src](https://svangel.com/about/team) |\n| Lizard Institute | co-founder (incubator for future China leaders) | ~2012 | [src](https://andreawang.substack.com/p/why-i-angel-invest) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Flapping Airplanes: unconfirmed.** The brief calls her a possible SV Angel sponsor. The only basis is an earlier repo row saying she commented on the Series A LinkedIn post, and that comment was not visible when the post was fetched (`firms/sv-angel.md`). SV Angel is not named in the Series A coverage or on SV Angel's portfolio page. I found no source tying her to the company and wrote no row for it.\n- Which SV Angel deals she has sponsored since spring 2025.\n- Her title at GC: the team page said only \"Investment Team\"; aggregators conflict (Partner, Principal).\n- Her role in GC's Mercor seed: Signal lists it, but GC never featured it on her page.\n- Her angel portfolio from 2021-2023 (Signal lists \"Howcast\" and \"Gentench\" angel roles; not verified).\n\n## Sources\n- https://svangel.com/about/team\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20250214181112/https://svangel.com/about/team (absent) and https://web.archive.org/web/20250425222104/https://svangel.com/about/team (present)\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20230530052908/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/andrea-wang\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20241213045819/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/andrea-wang\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20250329203327/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/andrea-wang\n- https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-pylon\n- https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/sales-led-vs-product-led-growth\n- https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/experimenting-with-product-led-growth\n- https://andreawang.substack.com/p/why-i-angel-invest\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2039333/000203933325000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/aaru ; https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/podqi\n- https://signal.nfx.com/investors/andrea-wang_1 (aggregator)\n- https://finsmes.com/2024/08/pylon-raises-17m-in-series-a-funding.html\n- https://alleywatch.com/2024/11/the-alleywatch-startup-daily-funding-report-11-21-2024/\n- https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260310898882/en\n- https://yespress.io/marty-kausas (secondary)\n", "person:andrei-brasoveanu": "\n# Andrei Brasoveanu\n\n## One-line take\nAccel London's security and devtools partner, now writing very large AI-native security seeds into repeat Israeli founders (Oak $60M at inception) and pre-empting follow-ons (Vega A then B five months later).\n\n## Crux\nHe is a former Wall Street quant who runs a homework-first, founder-first process: Accel builds its own view of team, market and customers before meeting the full team, so it is not relying on the founder's story ([src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctech/articles/0,7340,L-3868011,00.html)). He wins with repeat founders and long courtship. He backed Shai Morag a second time at inception and met Lightdash more than a year before the seed ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/building-the-ai-native-identity-operating-system-why-we-re-backing-oak), [src](https://www.accel.com/news/the-bi-platform-for-the-modern-data-stack-our-seed-investment-in-lightdash)). Accel's balance sheet lets him lead again fast, as with Vega's B ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/deepening-our-commitment-to-vega)). His limits: his work is enterprise security, devtools and web3, not model labs or chips, and Accel paced itself during downturns (\"we aren't very aggressive\", 2020).\n\n## Differential insight\n- Diligence rule: \"We don't want to be dependent on what the founders tell us about their company.\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctech/articles/0,7340,L-3868011,00.html) (2020-11-02)\n- Says Accel likes underdog founders in unusual geographies (Australia, New Zealand, Romania): \"We love to back the underdog, we also like to be proactive\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctech/articles/0,7340,L-3868011,00.html)\n- In Zoom diligence he meets every team member because \"sometimes you find that not everyone contributes equally.\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctech/articles/0,7340,L-3868011,00.html)\n- How Vega's Series B started: he raised the idea of going faster with CEO Shay Sandler on a Central Park run up Harlem Hill. \"Shay and I will often get out for runs in Central Park.\" [src](https://www.accel.com/news/deepening-our-commitment-to-vega). Accel then led the $120M B, about five months after leading the $65M seed/A ([src](https://www.newswire.com/news/vega-raises-120m-series-b-to-redefine-the-security-detection-and-response)).\n- Repeat-founder bet: on backing Shai Morag again at Oak, \"Backing him a second time was one of the easiest decisions we've made.\" Oak's $60M inception seed was co-led with Greylock and CRV [src](https://www.accel.com/news/building-the-ai-native-identity-operating-system-why-we-re-backing-oak) (2026-07-15)\n- Admits the Oasis non-human-identity thesis was contrarian: \"I remember how contrarian that conviction felt at the time\". Accel is also Cyera's largest backer, so it sat on both sides of the Cyera-Oasis combination [src](https://www.accel.com/news/cyera-oasis-securing-the-ai-native-enterprise) (2026-07-28)\n- Sourcing through open source: \"We first met the Checkly team via the open-source community\". Accel then led a $2.25M seed in April 2020 [src](https://www.accel.com/news/enabling-engineers-to-detect-and-resolve-issues-10x-faster-our-investment-in-checkly)\n- Admitted failure: his first venture, a three-person quant commodities fund, did not work out. \"we failed to build the next Renaissance Technologies\" [src](https://www.accel.com/team/andrei-brasoveanu)\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. He is a strong early-stage partner who can lead $10M+ rounds, but his book is enterprise security, devtools, open source and web3, not labs, pre-training or chips. The deals that matter to me are the big AI-native seeds: Oak ($60M seed at inception, 2026), Vega (seed/A, then a $120M B), Polar ($10M seed for AI-native developers) and Legion (autonomous SOC seed). He is relevant as a co-lead who joins large seeds alongside US firms (Greylock, CRV) and as the route into Accel's Israeli security network (Cyberstarts co-invests).\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Romania. Studied mathematics at Princeton, then an MBA at Harvard ([src](https://podm8.com/episodes/venture-europe/andrei-brasoveanu-prepared-mind-DWEvJDFnRr); [src](https://www.accel.com/team/andrei-brasoveanu)). He said his trip to the US was \"the first time that I had gotten on a plane\" ([src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctech/articles/0,7340,L-3868011,00.html)).\n- Helped build an algorithmic commodity-futures trading startup in the old NYMEX building. He then worked at KCG/Virtu (US equities HFT) and with Foundation Capital ([src](https://www.accel.com/team/andrei-brasoveanu)). One podcast describes about a decade as a Wall Street quant ([src](https://ridingunicorns.buzzsprout.com/1620178/episodes/17526098-pattern-recognition-and-backing-europe-s-next-unicorns-with-andrei-brasoveanu-partner-accel)).\n- Joined Accel London in 2013 according to the 2019 partner announcement. The team bio says 2014, and the sources disagree. He was promoted to Partner in February 2019, together with Seth Pierrepont ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/announcing-our-two-newest-london-partners-seth-pierrepont-andrei-brasoveanu)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.accel.com/team/andrei-brasoveanu | Accel team page with Q&A |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.accel.com/news | Has written or co-written about 37 Accel \"our investment\" posts, 2018-2026 |\n| X | https://twitter.com/algovc | @algovc; could not load timeline (2026-11-11) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/andreibrasoveanu/ | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2019-06 — SaaStr Europa (Paris) — The Secret to SMB Sales, interviewing Doctolib's Agnes Bazin — https://www.saastr.com/the-secret-to-smb-sales-2/ — said Accel led Doctolib's $20M Series B in 2015\n- 2019-12 — TechCrunch Disrupt Berlin 2019 — listed speaker — https://techcrunch.com/events/disrupt-berlin-2019/speakers/\n- 2021-07-30 — Tech.eu Podcast E239 — Accel and its European strategy — https://podcast.tech.eu/episodes/accel-and-its-european-strategy-with-andrei-brasoveanu — covers deal flow and Accel's \"fintech anti-portfolio\" (not transcribed yet)\n- 2022-05-31 — Venture Europe — Prepared mind — https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/ventureeurope/episodes/Andrei-Brasoveanu---Prepared-mind-e1p50ln — Sky Mavis, Sorare and Nansen (not transcribed yet)\n- 2022-10 — TechCrunch Chain Reaction — Web2 venture firms fight to win in web3 — https://chain-reaction.simplecast.com/episodes/web2-venture-firms-fight-to-win-in-web3-w-andrei-brasoveanu-M2Zp61Du — competing with crypto-native VCs (audio not reached)\n- 2023 — Paris Blockchain Week Summit — speaker — https://www.parisblockchainweek.com/speakers/brasoveanu\n- 2024-08-05 — Technovation with Peter High, ep 895 — https://www.metisstrategy.com/interview/andrei-brasoveanu/ — security in the AI era, Europe and Israel\n- 2025-07-30 — Riding Unicorns S9E1 — https://ridingunicorns.buzzsprout.com/1620178/episodes/17526098-pattern-recognition-and-backing-europe-s-next-unicorns-with-andrei-brasoveanu-partner-accel — the episode description mentions a €100k pricing bet at Celonis and persuading an ex-Shopify executive to raise (from the description only; not transcribed)\n\n## How they think\n- Three founder tests (2019): relentless drive, founder-opportunity fit, and the ability to hire the best people at every level ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/announcing-our-two-newest-london-partners-seth-pierrepont-andrei-brasoveanu)).\n- Open source as Europe's equaliser. In 2020 he argued it removes the need for big field-sales teams and predicted OSS \"is increasingly likely to become a dominant product approach & business model\" ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/the-rise-of-open-source-software-in-europe)).\n- Rates Israel's technical teams above Europe's, saying they are \"at a technical level that is second to none\" ([src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctech/articles/0,7340,L-3868011,00.html)).\n- Calls that worked: the agent and non-human identity thesis (Oasis A, now combining with Cyera), Humio (sold to CrowdStrike), Ermetic (sold to Tenable), Tessian (sold to Proofpoint). Wrong calls: none admitted in sources read, beyond the failed quant fund.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Deepening Our Commitment to Vega](https://www.accel.com/news/deepening-our-commitment-to-vega): how he pre-empts a follow-on.\n- [Why We're Backing Oak](https://www.accel.com/news/building-the-ai-native-identity-operating-system-why-we-re-backing-oak): the repeat-founder, big-seed playbook.\n- [Cyera + Oasis](https://www.accel.com/news/cyera-oasis-securing-the-ai-native-enterprise): the contrarian identity thesis and Accel on both sides of the deal.\n- [Calcalist interview (2020)](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctech/articles/0,7340,L-3868011,00.html): his diligence and geography rules in his own words.\n- [The Rise of Open Source Software in Europe](https://www.accel.com/news/the-rise-of-open-source-software-in-europe): his core thesis on European devtools.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. The X account @algovc exists, but the timeline could not be loaded on 2026-11-11.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Celonis | Series A; Series B ($1B val) | 2016; 2018-06 | yes (A) | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/backing-a-category-creator-our-partnership-with-celonis) |\n| Tessian | Seed | 2017 | | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/congratulations-tessian-and-proofpoint) |\n| Monzo | £85M round | 2018-10 | co-led | General Catalyst | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/backing-a-banking-revolutionary-our-investment-in-monzo) |\n| Humio | Series A | 2019-01 | yes | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/seeing-through-the-cloud-humios-series-a-round) |\n| Rasa | Series A | 2019-04 | | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/enterprise-automation-and-conversational-ai-our-investment-in-rasa) |\n| Personio | (round not stated) | 2020-01 | | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-personio-the-new-os-for-smbs) |\n| Checkly | Seed ($2.25M) | 2020-04 | yes | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/enabling-engineers-to-detect-and-resolve-issues-10x-faster-our-investment-in-checkly) |\n| Ermetic | Series A | 2020-07 | yes | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/congratulations-ermetic-and-tenable) |\n| Raycast | (seed) | 2020-10 | | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/giving-developers-superpowers-our-investment-in-raycast) |\n| Sorare | Series A | 2021-02 | | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/building-the-global-digital-football-club-our-investment-in-sorare) |\n| Genesis | (round not stated) | 2021-03 | | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/changing-how-we-build-software-introducing-genesis) |\n| Tenderly | Series A | 2021-07 | yes | Point Nine; angels Dessaigne, Novakovic, Rauch | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/building-the-go-to-platform-for-blockchain-development-our-investment-in-tenderly) |\n| Sky Mavis | (round not stated) | 2021-10 | | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/enabling-the-blockchain-game-revolution-our-investment-in-sky-mavis) |\n| Conduktor | Series A | 2021-11 | | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/developing-and-managing-kafka-with-confidence-our-investment-in-conduktor) |\n| Nansen | (round not stated) | 2021-12 | | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/surfacing-the-signal-on-the-blockchain-our-investment-in-nansen) |\n| Lightdash | Seed | 2022-10 | yes | angels Kramer, Bjork, Handy, Podjarny | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/the-bi-platform-for-the-modern-data-stack-our-seed-investment-in-lightdash) |\n| Viso | Seed | 2023-10 | | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/making-computer-vision-a-reality-our-investment-in-viso) |\n| Oasis Security | Series A | 2024-01 | yes | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/solving-the-non-human-identity-security-gap-our-investment-in-oasis) |\n| Filigran | Series A | 2024-02 | yes | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/democratizing-threat-intelligence-our-investment-in-filigran) |\n| Lightrun | Series B ($70M) | 2025-04 | co-led | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/shaping-the-future-of-autonomous-application-remediation-our-investment-in-lightrun) |\n| Polar | Seed ($10M) | 2025-06 | yes | angels | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-polar-the-future-of-global-payments-for-ai-native-developers) |\n| Legion | Seed | 2025-07 | | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-legion-transforming-cybersecurity-operations) |\n| Vega | Seed/Series A ($65M combined) | 2025-09 | yes | Cyberstarts, Redpoint, CRV | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/vega-the-future-beyond-siem) |\n| Polars | Series A | 2025-09 | | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/polars-transforming-data-processing-on-modern-hardware) |\n| Vega | Series B ($120M) | 2026-02 | yes | Cyberstarts, Redpoint, CRV | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/deepening-our-commitment-to-vega) |\n| Oak | Seed ($60M, at inception) | 2026-07 | co-led | Greylock, CRV | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/building-the-ai-native-identity-operating-system-why-we-re-backing-oak) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Vega B (2026):** He was running with CEO Shay Sandler up Harlem Hill. He told Sandler that Vega was doing well but could accelerate, and offered Accel's resources. Accel then led the B ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/deepening-our-commitment-to-vega)).\n- **Oak (2026):** Shai Morag is a repeat founder. Accel led Ermetic's A in July 2020 after first meeting the founders in Tel Aviv at the start of Covid. Tenable bought Ermetic in 2023, and Accel backed Oak at inception ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/congratulations-ermetic-and-tenable); [src](https://www.accel.com/news/building-the-ai-native-identity-operating-system-why-we-re-backing-oak)).\n- **Checkly (2020):** He found the company through its open-source community just after founding and led the $2.25M seed. Balderton led the later $20M B ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/enabling-engineers-to-detect-and-resolve-issues-10x-faster-our-investment-in-checkly)).\n- **Monzo (2018):** Accel co-led with General Catalyst, backing Tom Blomfield, whom Accel had first worked with at GoCardless ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/backing-a-banking-revolutionary-our-investment-in-monzo)).\n- **Legion (2025):** He first met CEO Ely Abramovitch in Tel Aviv in June 2023, about a year before the company was founded ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-legion-transforming-cybersecurity-operations)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Accel | Partner (London) | 2013 (bio: 2014); Partner Feb 2019 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/announcing-our-two-newest-london-partners-seth-pierrepont-andrei-brasoveanu) |\n| Foundation Capital | prior role | before Accel | [src](https://www.accel.com/team/andrei-brasoveanu) |\n| KCG/Virtu | HFT, prior role | before Accel | [src](https://www.accel.com/team/andrei-brasoveanu) |\n| Shai Morag | backed twice (Ermetic, Oak) | 2020 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/building-the-ai-native-identity-operating-system-why-we-re-backing-oak) |\n| Rich Kotite | co-author (Filigran, Polar) | 2024 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/democratizing-threat-intelligence-our-investment-in-filigran) |\n| Dana Eliaz | co-author (Legion, Vega) | 2025 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/vega-the-future-beyond-siem) |\n| Candice du Fretay | co-author (Sorare, Conduktor, Lightdash) | 2021 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/the-bi-platform-for-the-modern-data-stack-our-seed-investment-in-lightdash) |\n| Harry Nelis | co-author (Celonis, Personio, Instana) | 2018 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/backing-a-category-creator-our-partnership-with-celonis) |\n| Seth Pierrepont | promoted to Partner together | 2019 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/announcing-our-two-newest-london-partners-seth-pierrepont-andrei-brasoveanu) |\n| Greylock, CRV | co-leads on Oak seed | 2026 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/building-the-ai-native-identity-operating-system-why-we-re-backing-oak) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What is the \"fintech anti-portfolio\" he discussed on Tech.eu (2021)? Those are named passes, and the episode has not been transcribed yet.\n- Riding Unicorns (2025): the Celonis €100k pricing story, and which ex-Shopify executive he talked into raising (likely Polar's founder; unconfirmed).\n- Chain Reaction (2022): how Accel competed with crypto-native funds for Sorare, Nansen and Sky Mavis.\n- What Accel's personal check was in Oak's $60M seed, and how the three-way co-lead split.\n- Start date at Accel: 2013 (partner announcement) or 2014 (bio)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.accel.com/team/andrei-brasoveanu\n- https://www.accel.com/news/announcing-our-two-newest-london-partners-seth-pierrepont-andrei-brasoveanu\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctech/articles/0,7340,L-3868011,00.html\n- https://www.saastr.com/the-secret-to-smb-sales-2/\n- https://www.accel.com/news/deepening-our-commitment-to-vega\n- https://www.accel.com/news/building-the-ai-native-identity-operating-system-why-we-re-backing-oak\n- https://www.accel.com/news/cyera-oasis-securing-the-ai-native-enterprise\n- https://www.accel.com/news/the-rise-of-open-source-software-in-europe\n- https://podcast.tech.eu/episodes/accel-and-its-european-strategy-with-andrei-brasoveanu\n- https://www.metisstrategy.com/interview/andrei-brasoveanu/\n- https://ridingunicorns.buzzsprout.com/1620178/episodes/17526098-pattern-recognition-and-backing-europe-s-next-unicorns-with-andrei-brasoveanu-partner-accel\n- https://podm8.com/episodes/venture-europe/andrei-brasoveanu-prepared-mind-DWEvJDFnRr\n- https://www.newswire.com/news/vega-raises-120m-series-b-to-redefine-the-security-detection-and-response\n- Full list of Accel posts in data/rows/andrei-brasoveanu-voice-media.csv\n", "person:andrei-kozyrev": "\n# Andrei Kozyrev\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the only technical, ex-AI-operator GP on Abstract's four-person investment team, so he is the likely route into Abstract's AI deals. Yet no primary source names him on a single deal ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/), [src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he sits inside a firm that leads 80–90% of its deals by Fund III and has a $500M 2025 fund. As one of four investors, he gets decision rights early, as Abstract's hiring model intends ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). His edge over generalist peers is operating time inside Scale AI: he co-founded the Enterprise AI team and scaled it to 50 FTEs and millions in revenue ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)). He also has warm ties to two tier-one firms, Thrive (former EIR) and Sequoia (the connection that brought him to Abstract) ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/), [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- **Constraint:** he covers a ~200-company book with three colleagues, and the firm is known for co-investing alongside tier-one leads ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). He has no public writing or podcasts and no named deals, so his own track record isn't separable from Abstract's.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** nothing sourced. Abstract's general style is preemptive term sheets and running founders' next-round processes ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)), not passive index-style investing.\n\n## Differential insight\n- He came through Sequoia, not Thrive: \"Andrei Kozyrev arrived next, in 2024, through a connection at Sequoia.\" ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). Sequoia co-led Factory's Series B alongside Abstract ([src](https://www.factory.ai/news/series-b)). That overlap is a plausible deal-flow path, but it is unconfirmed.\n- The team is tiny relative to the book: \"The investment team: Kozyrev, Kwon, Davidov, and Naimi.\" ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). That was mid-2025, against ~200 companies across funds.\n- He is the 2024 instance of Naimi's hiring rule: \"Hire one exceptional 26-year-old every year\" with a review after three years ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). His review would land around 2027 (inference).\n- An aggregator-only, AI-written bio (low confidence) says he worked on \"multiple stealth projects such as Factory AI and Unify GTM\" ([src](https://theorg.com/org/abstract-ventures/org-chart/andrei-kozyrev)). Abstract is confirmed in both companies at firm level. Factory: Abstract co-led the $50M Series B at $300M on 2025-09-25 ([src](https://www.factory.ai/news/series-b)), and Factory raised $200M at $5B on 2026-09-15, about 16x in a year. Abstract is not named in that later round's investor list ([src](https://www.factory.ai/news/5-billion-valuation)). Unify: Abstract was in the Series A and Series B ([src](https://www.unifygtm.com/blog/series-a), [src](https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/unify-ai-gtm-pipeline-40-million-raise)).\n- Thrive (his previous seat) co-led Unify's seed ([src](https://www.unifygtm.com/blog/announcing-our-seed-round)). Abstract appears from the Series A onward ([src](https://www.unifygtm.com/blog/series-a)), which fits a Thrive-to-Abstract handoff. This is unconfirmed.\n- Photo metadata hint: his abstract.com headshot file is named `260529_...`, uploaded 2026-06 ([src](https://www.abstract.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/260529_AbstractVentures_AndreiKozyrev.png)). The team page was refreshed around May–June 2026, and the GP title may date from then, since TheOrg still lists \"Partner\". This is inference, not a sourced promotion date.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Abstract does back frontier names at seed, per its own portfolio list: Physical Intelligence, Standard Intelligence (video pre-training for computer-use agents), Periodic Labs (Series A), Sphere Semi (AI-designed analog chips) and Cognition ([src](https://www.abstract.com/companies-list/)). Kozyrev is the firm's technical AI hire, so he is the person to know there. The trouble is that none of these deals is attributed to him. The deals loosely tied to him (Factory and Unify) are AI-native automation and apps, which fits your Serval-style interest; they aren't labs or chips. Upgrade to high if a founder or filing puts him on PI, Standard Intelligence, Periodic or Sphere Semi.\n\n## Background\n- Cornell University, BA in Computer Science and Information Science ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)). TheOrg adds an MS in CS from Cornell, research at Cornell and Harvard, and a Machine Learning Engineer role at Spotify on personalization. All of these are aggregator-only and unconfirmed ([src](https://theorg.com/org/abstract-ventures/org-chart/andrei-kozyrev)).\n- Scale AI: Chief of Staff to Michael Kratsios (former US CTO); co-founded the Enterprise AI team, which grew from zero to millions in revenue and 50 FTEs ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)). The dates 2021–2023 come from TheOrg only.\n- Thrive Capital: Entrepreneur in Residence after Scale ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)). TheOrg says it was a short stint in 2023 spent exploring his own startup ideas.\n- Abstract: joined in 2024 via a Sequoia connection ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). TheOrg says he joined as Partner in January 2024; abstract.com lists him as General Partner (checked 2026-11-10) ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.abstract.com/people/ | Firm bio and headshot (verified 2026-11-10, HTTP 200 image/png) |\n| Substack / blog | not found | checked abstract.com; andreikozyrev.com did not resolve (2026-11-10) |\n| X | not found | no verified handle; web search budget exhausted this run, so not searched |\n| LinkedIn | not found | not verified; TheOrg profile (aggregator) at https://theorg.com/org/abstract-ventures/org-chart/andrei-kozyrev |\n| GitHub | not found | not checked (no verified handle) |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | — |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. Web search was rate-limited this run, so this is unchecked rather than confirmed absent.\n\n## How they think\n- No first-person writing, podcasts or posts found, so his theses are unknown.\n- Firm-level style he operates in: Abstract went from leading about 20% of its deals in Fund I to 80–90% by Fund III, and it wins with preemptive offers and by running founders' follow-on processes ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Colossus on Ramtin Naimi and Abstract (2025-07): the only piece that explains how he was hired and his place in the four-person team ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n2. Abstract people page: the authoritative bio and title ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)).\n3. Factory Series B post: the most significant AI round Abstract co-led in his tenure ([src](https://www.factory.ai/news/series-b)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found (no verified social accounts).\n\n## Portfolio\nNo primary source names him personally on any deal. The rows below are firm-level Abstract rounds during his tenure that an aggregator ties to him.\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Factory | Series B ($50M at $300M) | 2025-09-25 | co-led (Abstract firm-level) | NEA, Sequoia, J.P. Morgan, Nvidia, Mantis VC | [src](https://www.factory.ai/news/series-b) |\n| Unify | Series A ($12M) | about 9 months before B, so late 2024 (unconfirmed) | no (Emergence + Thrive led) | OpenAI Startup Fund, Neo, 20Sales, AltCap | [src](https://www.unifygtm.com/blog/series-a) |\n| Unify | Series B ($40M at $260M) | 2025-07-15 | no (Battery led) | OpenAI Startup Fund, Emergence, Thrive, TCP, Capital49 | [src](https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/unify-ai-gtm-pipeline-40-million-raise) |\n| Applied Labs | Seed ($4.2M) | 2025-01-27 | yes (Abstract) | Point72 Ventures, Outlander, Tetra | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/01/27/applied-labs-raises-4-2m-ai-powered-business-automation-platform). The founders are ex-Scale AI, so this is plausibly from his network, but his involvement is unconfirmed |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing confirmed. Timing on Factory: Abstract came in as a co-lead at the Series B, $300M post (2025-09). One year later Factory raised at $5B with Blackstone, Khosla, Sequoia, Insight, NEA, Mantis VC and others, and Abstract is not named ([src](https://www.factory.ai/news/series-b), [src](https://www.factory.ai/news/5-billion-valuation)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Abstract | General Partner | 2024 | [src](https://www.abstract.com/people/) |\n| Ramtin Naimi | Hired him (Abstract founder) | 2024 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Sequoia Capital | The connection that led to Abstract (person not named) | 2024 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Thrive Capital | Former EIR | 2023 (aggregator) | [src](https://www.abstract.com/people/) |\n| Michael Kratsios | Was his Chief of Staff at Scale AI | 2021–2023 (aggregator) | [src](https://www.abstract.com/people/) |\n| Scale AI | Co-founded Enterprise AI team | 2021–2023 (aggregator) | [src](https://www.abstract.com/people/) |\n| David Kwon, Alex Davidov | Fellow Abstract GPs | 2024 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which Abstract deals does he personally own? Ask the founders of Factory, Unify, Applied Labs, Standard Intelligence, Periodic Labs and Sphere Semi.\n- When was he promoted from Partner to General Partner? (The 2026-05 headshot hints at a mid-2026 page refresh.)\n- Who at Sequoia connected him to Naimi?\n- Does he appear as a related person on any Abstract Form D? EDGAR full-text search for \"Kozyrev\" across Form D filings returned 0 hits on 2026-11-10 ([src](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Kozyrev%22&forms=D)).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.abstract.com/people/\n- https://www.abstract.com/companies-list/\n- https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/\n- https://theorg.com/org/abstract-ventures/org-chart/andrei-kozyrev (aggregator)\n- https://www.factory.ai/news/series-b\n- https://www.factory.ai/news/5-billion-valuation\n- https://www.unifygtm.com/blog/announcing-our-seed-round\n- https://www.unifygtm.com/blog/series-a\n- https://www.unifygtm.com/blog/series-b\n- https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/unify-ai-gtm-pipeline-40-million-raise\n- https://siliconangle.com/2025/01/27/applied-labs-raises-4-2m-ai-powered-business-automation-platform\n- https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Kozyrev%22&forms=D\n", "person:andrej-karpathy": "\n# Andrej Karpathy\n\n## One-line take\nThe most-followed researcher-angel in AI: in Perplexity's seed, Pika's first rounds, World Labs, Etched, /dev/agents, Flapping Airplanes and about twenty more, and since May 2026 an Anthropic employee working on pretraining.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why founders want him:** his name is the strongest technical endorsement a new lab or infra company can show recruits. He is a talent magnet more than a source of capital.\n- **Vehicle:** personal cheques; no fund, syndicate, Form D or adviser registration found. Cheque sizes are not public. He does not list investments on [karpathy.ai](https://karpathy.ai/) and I found no statement from him about angel investing.\n- **Pattern:** he joins seeds led by a small cluster (Nat Friedman, Daniel Gross, Elad Gil) and rounds for labs founded by people he worked with at Stanford and OpenAI (Fei-Fei Li's World Labs; Hugo Barra and ex-Google leads at /dev/agents).\n- **Constraint:** he is now inside Anthropic on pretraining ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/05/19/who-is-andrej-karpathy-vibe-coding-anthropic-openai-rubiks-cube/)). Backing a competing lab is a conflict question, yet aggregators date three cheques after he joined.\n\n## Differential insight\n- \"Personal update: I've joined Anthropic\" (X, 2026-05-19, via [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2026/05/19/who-is-andrej-karpathy-vibe-coding-anthropic-openai-rubiks-cube/)). Post-join cheques per databases: Engram (2026-06-23), Etched Series C (2026-07-23), Mintive seed (2026-10-29) ([CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/andrej-karpathy)).\n- Pika's 2023 announcement names him in the earlier rounds with \"Elad Gil, Adam D'Angelo, Andrej Karpathy, Clem Delangue, Craig Kallman and Alex Chung\"; those rounds were led by Nat Friedman and Daniel Gross ([src](https://petapixel.com/2023/11/28/generative-video-startup-pika-labs-launches-version-1-0-raises-55-million-in-funding), via search snippet).\n- His public AI views are bearish on timelines: \"If I just average it out, it just feels like a decade to me.\" and \"Reinforcement learning is terrible.\" ([Dwarkesh, 2025-10-17](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/andrej-karpathy)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. He backs exactly my categories at seed: new labs (Flapping Airplanes, World Labs, Inception, Standard Intelligence, Engram), AI chips (Etched, MatX) and compute (Lambda, Prime Intellect). His presence in a seed is a signal worth tracking; he is not a co-lead.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford PhD; his site covers his Stanford, OpenAI and Tesla work ([src](https://karpathy.ai/)). Fei-Fei Li as adviser is widely reported, not re-sourced here.\n- OpenAI founding member (2015); left to lead AI at Tesla; back at OpenAI 2023-2024 ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/05/19/who-is-andrej-karpathy-vibe-coding-anthropic-openai-rubiks-cube/)).\n- Founded Eureka Labs (AI-native education), announced 2024-07 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/07/16/after-tesla-and-openai-andrej-karpathys-startup-aims-to-apply-ai-assistants-to-education)).\n- Joined Anthropic's pretraining team 2026-05, leading a new team that uses Claude to speed up pretraining research; sits under Nick Joseph.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://karpathy.ai/ | no investing page |\n| Substack / blog | https://karpathy.bearblog.dev/blog/ ; https://karpathy.github.io | |\n| X | https://twitter.com/karpathy | follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | n/a | |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/karpathy | nanoGPT, nanochat |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://www.youtube.com/@AndrejKarpathy | lectures |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-10 — Dwarkesh Podcast — AGI is still a decade away — [link](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/andrej-karpathy) — full transcript; first 100K characters mined (the rest, which covers education, not read).\n\n## How they think\n- Agents are a ten-year project; current ones lack continual learning and reliable computer use.\n- RL is a weak learning signal; he prefers approaches closer to how people learn.\n- The industry is \"making too big of a jump\" in its claims.\n- He coined \"vibe coding\"; his cheques into /dev/agents, Simile and Standard Intelligence fit a computer-use and agents thread despite the long timeline.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Dwarkesh interview](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/andrej-karpathy) (2025-10)\n- [Fortune profile on the Anthropic move](https://fortune.com/2026/05/19/who-is-andrej-karpathy-vibe-coding-anthropic-openai-rubiks-cube/) (2026-05)\n- His blog at https://karpathy.bearblog.dev/blog/ (not mined)\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI cheques. \"Ledger\" = already in data/rounds.csv or data/deals.csv before this pass; lead names on ledger rows are the ledger's. Cheque sizes never disclosed.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Seed-stage? | Lead / co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Adept | Series A $65M | 2022-04 | no | Greylock, Addition | ledger |\n| Perplexity | Seed; again Series B-1 | 2022-09; 2024-04 | yes | Elad Gil, Nat Friedman (co-leads) | ledger |\n| **Pika** | Pre-seed / seed | 2023 | yes | Nat Friedman, Daniel Gross (leads) | new ([src](https://petapixel.com/2023/11/28/generative-video-startup-pika-labs-launches-version-1-0-raises-55-million-in-funding)) |\n| Lamini | seed or Series A | 2024-05 | unclear | Amplify Partners | ledger |\n| World Labs | seed + A ($230M combined) | 2024-09 | unclear | a16z, NEA, Radical | ledger |\n| /dev/agents (Dreamer) | Seed $56M | 2024-11 | yes | Index, CapitalG | ledger |\n| Lambda | Series D $480M | 2025-02 | no | Andra Capital, SGW | ledger |\n| Prime Intellect | $15M round | 2025-02 | yes (Shizune labels it seed) | Founders Fund | ledger |\n| **Brain Co.** | Series A $30M | 2025-09-10 | no | n/a | new, aggregator ([src](https://shizune.co/investors/profile/andrej-karpathy-7d6e)) |\n| Inception | Seed $50M | 2025-11 | yes | Menlo | ledger |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Seed (Shizune: $180M, 2026-01-27) | 2026-01 | yes | GV, Index, Sequoia | ledger (round was unsplit; aggregator now dates it) |\n| Simile | Series A $100M | 2026-02 | no | Index | ledger (deals); added to rounds |\n| MatX | Series B | 2026-02 | no | Jane Street, Situational Awareness LP | ledger |\n| Standard Intelligence | Series A $75M | 2026-04 | no | Sequoia, Spark | ledger |\n| Decart | $300M round | 2026-05 | no | Radical Ventures | ledger |\n| Engram | $98M at launch | 2026-06 | unclear | General Catalyst, Modern Capital, Kleiner Perkins; Abbeel | ledger |\n| Etched | undisclosed earlier round; Series C | n/a; 2026-07 | no | a16z, Thiel, Masad | ledger |\n| **Rosebud AI** | undisclosed | n/a | n/a | n/a | new row from an existing lead ([src](https://rosebud.ai/company)) |\n| **Replicate** | undisclosed | n/a | n/a | n/a | new, aggregator ([src](https://seedtable.com/investors/andrej-karpathy/portfolio)) |\n\nNot written as rows: **Mintive** (seed $7M, 2026-10-29, CB Insights only; I could not tell what the company does); **Magic** (a profile site says co-founder Eric Steinberger announced his angel position in early 2024, with no link; unverified); Blueprint (Bryan Johnson; non-AI). Databases count 18-25 investments.\n\n**Hit pattern:** Perplexity (seed to tens of billions), Pika, World Labs, Etched ($10.3B), Lambda, Decart, MatX. Miss: Adept (acqui-hired by Amazon in 2024). No public pass or regret found.\n\n## Deal stories\n- No deal has a public first-person account from him. What is on record is co-investor context: Perplexity's seed (Gil and Friedman co-led; Abbeel, LeCun, Vaswani, Masad alongside) and Pika's early rounds (Friedman and Gross led).\n- Founders' words on why they wanted him: none found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Anthropic | Employee, pretraining team | 2026-05 | [src](https://fortune.com/2026/05/19/who-is-andrej-karpathy-vibe-coding-anthropic-openai-rubiks-cube/) |\n| Nick Joseph | Leads the organisation he joined | 2026-05 | same |\n| OpenAI | Founding member; two stints | 2015-17; 2023-24 | same |\n| Nat Friedman, Daniel Gross, Elad Gil | Leads he follows (Perplexity, Pika) | 2022 | [src](https://petapixel.com/2023/11/28/generative-video-startup-pika-labs-launches-version-1-0-raises-55-million-in-funding) |\n| Pieter Abbeel | Repeated co-angel (Perplexity seed, Engram) | 2022 | ledger |\n| Amjad Masad | Repeated co-angel (Perplexity seed, Etched Series C) | 2022 | ledger |\n| Fei-Fei Li | PhD adviser; he backed World Labs | n/a | ledger |\n\n## Open questions\n- Does Anthropic restrict his angel activity? Are the post-May-2026 cheques new money or earlier commitments announced late?\n- What happens to Eureka Labs.\n- Confirm Magic, Replicate and Mintive from primary posts.\n- Any first-person statement on why he invests: search his X replies.\n\n## Sources\n- https://fortune.com/2026/05/19/who-is-andrej-karpathy-vibe-coding-anthropic-openai-rubiks-cube/\n- https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/andrej-karpathy\n- https://karpathy.ai/\n- https://petapixel.com/2023/11/28/generative-video-startup-pika-labs-launches-version-1-0-raises-55-million-in-funding (403 on fetch; text via search)\n- Aggregators (secondary): https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/andrej-karpathy ; https://shizune.co/investors/profile/andrej-karpathy-7d6e ; https://seedtable.com/investors/andrej-karpathy/portfolio ; https://nextomoro.com/andrej-karpathy/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/07/16/after-tesla-and-openai-andrej-karpathys-startup-aims-to-apply-ai-assistants-to-education\n", "person:andrew-byrnes": "\n# Andrew Byrnes\n\n## One-line take\nRuns AI at Micron Ventures and is the Micron name on edge and physical-AI chip deals; still in the seat in 2026, he announced Micron's SiMa.ai investment on 2026-04-08 [src](https://sima.ai/press-release/sima-ai-strategic-investment-from-micron-to-scale-physical-ai/).\n\n## Crux\nHe is a corporate VC whose edge is access to Micron's engineers: he says deep-tech investing needs world-class engineering talent to test stacks and build theses [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rising-stars-qa-andrew-byrnes-micron-ventures/?format=md). He sits on the AI side of a $550M Micron-only program and backs teams whose products pull memory. The constraint is that of any CVC: small minority checks (no lead of a priced AI round found), conflicts when outside innovation is brought into Micron (he names this risk himself), and a mandate tied to Micron's product roadmap. He would not price a frontier-lab round.\n\n## Differential insight\n- He says the fund outperformed peers on pace and multiple, with no numbers: \"we've outperformed our peers on both deal velocity and MOIC\" [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rising-stars-qa-andrew-byrnes-micron-ventures/?format=md) (2022-06-23).\n- Comet Labs, the AI fund he ran before Micron, had a single LP: \"Lenovo was the sole LP.\" He says he was still managing that portfolio in 2022 [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rising-stars-qa-andrew-byrnes-micron-ventures/?format=md).\n- He built Micron's AI fund from scratch: \"I've helped build the fund from scratch and with a small team\" [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rising-stars-qa-andrew-byrnes-micron-ventures/?format=md).\n- His thesis link from generative AI to Micron: a \"massive compute workload built on the foundation of high-performance compute and high-bandwidth memory\" (2023-09-01) [src](https://www.micron.com/about/blog/applications/ai/how-we-invest-in-generative-ai).\n- On SiMa.ai: \"We're investing in SiMa.ai\" and calls them well positioned \"in the Physical AI era\" [src](https://sima.ai/press-release/sima-ai-strategic-investment-from-micron-to-scale-physical-ai/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. He is close to AI chips and physical AI at Series A/B and is a useful strategic co-investor in an AI-chip round. But he writes small, non-leading CVC checks and is not in frontier-lab seeds. Deals that matter: SiMa.ai (2026), plus the Micron Ventures chip book (Eliyan, Avicena, nEye, Normal Computing), where he is not personally named.\n\n## Background\n- Before venture: high-school teacher (chemistry, English, geometry); project manager and project-finance lead on utility-scale wind and solar in the US and Mexico; startup founder; materials science and engineering student; writer [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rising-stars-qa-andrew-byrnes-micron-ventures/?format=md). No dates given.\n- Comet Labs: led the investment team for about two years (Lenovo sole LP). TheOrg lists him as Portfolio Manager (aggregator) [src](https://theorg.com/org/comet-labs/org-chart/andrew-byrnes). The task brief said \"board of Comet Labs\"; no source supports a board seat there.\n- Micron Ventures: from 2019 (May 2019 per aggregator). Title \"Director, Venture Capital - Artificial Intelligence\" [src](https://www.micron.com/about/blog/blog-authors/author-bio/andrew-byrnes). Confirmed current on 2026-04-08 as \"Director of Venture Capital at Micron\" [src](https://sima.ai/press-release/sima-ai-strategic-investment-from-micron-to-scale-physical-ai/), and the quote is still live on SiMa.ai's homepage carousel [src](https://sima.ai/).\n- Education: not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.micron.com/about/blog/blog-authors/author-bio/andrew-byrnes | Micron author page, 9 posts |\n| Substack / blog | Micron blog (above) | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/byrnesandrew/ | via VC Sheet (aggregator) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2022-06 — Global Corporate Venturing — Rising Stars Q&A — https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rising-stars-qa-andrew-byrnes-micron-ventures — says the fund was built from scratch and names SambaNova and Volocopter as unicorns.\n- Undated — SEMI Silicon Valley Virtual Forum — speaker bio — https://www.semi.org/en/expositions-events/silicon-valley-virtual-forum-speaker-bio-andrew-byrnes — the page returned 403; not read.\n\n## How they think\n- \"Deep tech investing is hard\" [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rising-stars-qa-andrew-byrnes-micron-ventures/?format=md). He leans on in-house engineers for diligence.\n- Advice to CVCs: \"Eliminate personal biases and be willing to dream\" [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rising-stars-qa-andrew-byrnes-micron-ventures/?format=md). He also warns that the best startups often begin as ideas that seem foolish.\n- Blog title \"Micron Ventures backs teams that ignore hype and invest in the panic\" suggests a counter-cyclical stance (post not read; URL 404) [src](https://www.micron.com/about/blog/blog-authors/author-bio/andrew-byrnes).\n- He has a strong sustainability thread, with posts on data-center energy and industrial decarbonization [src](https://www.micron.com/about/blog/blog-authors/author-bio/andrew-byrnes).\n\n## Read these first\n- \"How we invest in generative AI\" (2023-09-01): the Fund I / Fund II split and the HBM thesis.\n- GCV Rising Stars Q&A (2022-06-23): his background, Comet Labs and how a CVC works.\n- SiMa.ai release (2026-04-08): his current physical-AI thesis.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X account located.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| SiMa.ai | Strategic (undisclosed) | 2026-04-08 | no (sole strategic in that announcement) | none named | [src](https://sima.ai/press-release/sima-ai-strategic-investment-from-micron-to-scale-physical-ai/) |\n| Atlas AI | Undisclosed (he wrote \"Atlas AI: Why we invest\") | not found | not found | not found | [src](https://www.micron.com/about/blog/blog-authors/author-bio/andrew-byrnes) |\n| READY Robotics | Undisclosed (he wrote \"Get ready for READY Robotics\") | not found | not found | not found | [src](https://www.micron.com/about/blog/blog-authors/author-bio/andrew-byrnes) |\n\nHypothesis, not fact: board seats at Multiscale Technologies and PingThings, per TheOrg profiles marked \"Unverified\" [src](https://theorg.com/org/multiscale-technologies/org-chart/andrew-byrnes) [src](https://theorg.com/org/pingthings/org-chart/andrew-byrnes). Search snippets also mention Preteckt. Aggregators (startupintros, vcbacked) list Micron Ventures in Multiscale's ~$11M Series A (2024-09); unconfirmed.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **SiMa.ai (2026).** The investment was announced together with a design-in of Micron LPDDR5X in SiMa.ai's Modalix MLSoC, and SoMs with Micron memory were available at launch [src](https://sima.ai/press-release/sima-ai-strategic-investment-from-micron-to-scale-physical-ai/). It came about six months before SiMa.ai's $150M Series C at $1.45B (2026-09-29), in which Micron is not named [src](https://w.media/sima-ai-raises-us-150-million-in-series-c-at-us-1-45-billion-valuation/). The intro path is not found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Micron Ventures | at: Director, Venture Capital - AI | 2019 | [src](https://www.micron.com/about/blog/blog-authors/author-bio/andrew-byrnes) |\n| Comet Labs | at: led investment team, then managed the portfolio | not found (about two years before Micron) | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rising-stars-qa-andrew-byrnes-micron-ventures/?format=md) |\n| Gayathri Radhakrishnan | Micron AI-fund colleague; both joined about 2019; she left for Hitachi Ventures in 2023 | 2019 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2023-gayathri-radhakrishnan/?format=md) |\n| Henry Huang | Micron Ventures colleague (Investment Director; ChipAgents) | not found | [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026072902/chipagents-expands-series-a-funding-to-134-million-as-demand-grows-for-agentic-ai-in-semiconductor-design/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is he on the Paradigm Fund investment committee? The release names only René Hartner.\n- Comet Labs vintage, fund size and whether Lenovo also backs any later fund.\n- Board seats (Multiscale, PingThings, Preteckt): confirm from company filings or LinkedIn.\n- A working headshot. Micron's asset URL returns HTML, not an image.\n\n## Sources\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rising-stars-qa-andrew-byrnes-micron-ventures/?format=md\n- https://www.micron.com/about/blog/blog-authors/author-bio/andrew-byrnes\n- https://www.micron.com/about/blog/applications/ai/how-we-invest-in-generative-ai\n- https://sima.ai/press-release/sima-ai-strategic-investment-from-micron-to-scale-physical-ai/\n- https://sima.ai/\n- https://theorg.com/org/multiscale-technologies/org-chart/andrew-byrnes (aggregator)\n- https://theorg.com/org/pingthings/org-chart/andrew-byrnes (aggregator)\n- https://theorg.com/org/comet-labs/org-chart/andrew-byrnes (aggregator)\n- https://www.vcsheet.com/who/andrew-byrnes (aggregator)\n", "person:andrew-feldman": "\n# Andrew Feldman\n\n## One-line take\nA two-time compute founder (SeaMicro, then Cerebras) who raised about $500M before Cerebras chips were in production and took it to a ~$95B IPO. He matters to me as a talent and relationship hub in AI chips, and as the best case study of who backs hardware early, not as a check-writer.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** a repeat team. Five of the six SeaMicro founding executives came back to build Cerebras ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)). That track record took the risk out of the 2016 pitch for Benchmark and Eclipse ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/)). He then lands one anchor customer and learns from it, first G42 and then OpenAI ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)).\n- **What constrains him:** customer concentration. G42 was 87% of revenue in the 2024 S-1 ([src](https://www.theregister.com/ai-ml/2026/05/15/cerebras-wafer-scale-ai-bet-delivers-blockbuster-ipo/5240821)) and still 24% in 2025 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebras)). The January 2026 OpenAI deal (750 MW through 2028, ~$10B) temporarily bars sales to Anthropic ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebras)). He is now a public-company CEO facing short sellers: Culper Research attacked wafer lifespan, and Feldman answered in a 2026-11-05 post ([src](https://www.cerebras.ai/blog/what-it-took-to-build-wafer-scale-and-why-it-lasts)).\n- **What he'd never do (by history):** give up wafer-scale. He kept the bet through ~18 months without a working chip while burning ~$8M a month ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Burn before the first working wafer.** He has said: \"We were burning $8 million a month.\" That was in 2017–2019, with board meetings every six weeks, before the first wafer worked in August 2019 (Feldman on LinkedIn, quoted by Eclipse) ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)).\n- **How board meetings went:** \"Not yet. Not yet. Not yet.\" (same source).\n- **Who believed first:** \"Eclipse was among the first to believe in us.\" The first Eclipse memo was dated March 2016, and the intro came through Pierre Lamond, a SeaMicro investor and friend (same source).\n- **How he sells to big customers:** \"first catch one and learn\" (20VC, as quoted by Eclipse; the original episode was not located) (same source).\n- **Why OpenAI signed:** after a pilot that followed Lior Susan's early-2025 approach to Sam Altman, he said Cerebras was \"just so much faster than the competition\" (No Priors, as quoted by Eclipse) (same source).\n- **How the pitch landed:** \"We hadn't just fallen off the back of a turnip truck.\" He used SeaMicro's exit to AMD to get past Benchmark's hardware skepticism. Bruce Dunlevie grilled him on packaging and cooling ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/)).\n- **Answer to the short seller (2026-11-05):** \"Most silicon is retired for economic reasons.\" He reports that fewer than 0.5% of deployed CS-3 wafers have been decommissioned since 2024, and that depreciation life moved from 1–3 to 3–5 years ([src](https://www.cerebras.ai/blog/what-it-took-to-build-wafer-scale-and-why-it-lasts)).\n- **A strategy shift he admits:** \"we had set out to build a private cloud\" (same post, explaining why the depreciation estimates moved).\n- **Why he took the 2026 IPO:** \"made sense to access the public markets\" ([src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-mints-two-billionaires-sets-stage-for-potential-ai-wave.html)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is an operator, not an investor. I found no personal angel investments or fund LP positions (searches were limited this session; see Open questions). He matters to me in three ways:\n1. **Talent.** Cerebras alumni are a likely source of AI-chip and inference founders. The SeaMicro-to-Cerebras team move shows his people follow him.\n2. **Map of AI-chip capital.** Eclipse (Lamond, Susan), Benchmark (Vishria), Foundation Capital, Altman, Brockman and Lip-Bu Tan were early. G42, Alpha Wave and Fidelity came later.\n3. **Downstream partner.** Cerebras is a compute vendor to frontier labs (OpenAI, Mistral, Cognition, Perplexity), so he is useful for diligence on inference demand.\n\nWashU's Scott Wilson says they kept some Cerebras on confidence in \"Andrew and the team\" ([20VC](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026)).\n\n## Background\n- Born and raised in Palo Alto. His parents were Stanford professors ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Feldman_(businessman))).\n- Stanford: BA in Economics and Political Science (1991), MBA (1997) (same source).\n- Riverstone Networks: VP marketing and corporate development, from founding through the 2001 IPO (same source).\n- Force10 Networks: VP product management, marketing and business development, after 2001. Dell later acquired it (same source).\n- SeaMicro: co-founded in 2007 with Gary Lauterbach and ran it as CEO. AMD agreed to buy it in February 2012 for ~$334M ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Feldman_(businessman))).\n- AMD: corporate VP running the Data Center Server Solutions group from 2012 (same source).\n- Cerebras: co-founded in 2015 with Lauterbach, Michael James, Sean Lie and Jean-Philippe Fricker ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebras)). IPO on 2026-05-14 at $185. His stake was ~10.3M shares, ~5.5%, worth ~$3.2B ([src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-mints-two-billionaires-sets-stage-for-potential-ai-wave.html)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.cerebras.ai | Company site |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.cerebras.ai/blog | CEO post on 2026-11-05; byline cadence unknown |\n| X | not confirmed | Handle not verified this session |\n| LinkedIn | not confirmed | Eclipse quotes his LinkedIn posts; URL not verified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Date unknown — 20VC — episode not located. Eclipse quotes him saying \"first catch one and learn\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)).\n- Date unknown (after early 2025) — No Priors — episode not located. Eclipse quotes him on OpenAI's pilot reaction (same source).\n- Date unknown — Alejandro Cremades / startupfundraising.com profile ([src](https://startupfundraising.com/library/articles/andrew-feldman)). Returned 403, not read.\n\n## How they think\n- **Inference speed is the wedge.** He expects \"fast inference becomes a larger share of AI workloads\" ([src](https://www.cerebras.ai/blog/what-it-took-to-build-wafer-scale-and-why-it-lasts)).\n- **Contrarian since 2016:** GPUs were poor for deep learning, even though they beat CPUs. This was the pitch that won Vishria ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/)).\n- **Learn from failure:** \"We learned from every failure\" ([src](https://www.cerebras.ai/blog/what-it-took-to-build-wafer-scale-and-why-it-lasts)).\n- **Called right:** wafer-scale for inference, and the 2025 pivot toward running its own cloud.\n- **Partly wrong:** the 2024 IPO attempt. The S-1 was filed in September 2024 and pulled almost a year later, amid G42 concentration and national-security scrutiny ([src](https://www.theregister.com/ai-ml/2026/05/15/cerebras-wafer-scale-ai-bet-delivers-blockbuster-ipo/5240821), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"What It Took to Build Wafer Scale, and Why It Lasts\" (2026-11-05). His own long-form engineering and economic defense ([src](https://www.cerebras.ai/blog/what-it-took-to-build-wafer-scale-and-why-it-lasts)).\n2. Eclipse, \"How an unreasonable pitch became the backbone of the reasoning era.\" The investor-side origin story with his quotes ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)).\n3. TechCrunch on how Benchmark nearly skipped the meeting. Pitch dynamics ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing confirmed. I couldn't verify his X or LinkedIn activity this session.\n\n## Portfolio\nThese are founder companies; I found no personal investments.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| SeaMicro | Founded (co-founder/CEO) | 2007 | n/a | | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Feldman_(businessman)) |\n| SeaMicro | Acquired by AMD (~$334M) | 2012-02 | n/a | | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Feldman_(businessman)) |\n| Cerebras | Series A ($27M) | 2016-05 | raised by Feldman | Benchmark, Foundation Capital, Eclipse | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebras) |\n| Cerebras | Series H ($1.0B) | 2026-01 | raised by Feldman | Benchmark ($225M), Alpha Wave, Fidelity | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebras) |\n| Cerebras | IPO ($5.55B raised) | 2026-05-14 | n/a | | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebras) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Cerebras Series A (2016).** Feldman went to Pierre Lamond first, who had invested in SeaMicro. Eclipse wrote its first memo in March 2016, and Lamond got the team a TSMC meeting through Morris Chang ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)). At Benchmark, Eric Vishria nearly declined the meeting and brought in Bruce Dunlevie for hardware diligence ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/)).\n  - **Who was in first:** sources disagree. Wikipedia lists Benchmark, Foundation and Eclipse in the May 2016 A ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebras)). MicroVentures (aggregator) puts only Foundation and Eclipse in the A and Benchmark in a $25M B ([src](https://microventures.com/microventures-portfolio-company-cerebras-history-and-milestones)). TechCrunch says Vishria co-led the $25M A.\n  - **Eclipse's first check:** ~$6.5M (secondary) ([src](https://inforcapital.com/news/for-eclipse-the-25b-cerebras-win-is-just-the-start-of-realizing-its-physical-world-thesis/)).\n  - **Who followed and when:** Coatue (Series B, Dec 2016, ~7 months later), then VY Capital (Series C, Jan 2017) ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebras)). MicroVentures adds Sequoia and Altimeter in the C. Altimeter and Coatue were in the 2018 D. Alpha Wave and ADG led the 2021 F.\n- **G42 (2021–).** It came through Lior Susan's relationship with Sheikh Tahnoon, via DriveNets, founded by Ido Susan. There were more than 20 trips to Abu Dhabi. A pilot led to Condor Galaxy ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)). In July 2023, G42 agreed to ~$100M for the first of up to nine systems ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebras)).\n- **OpenAI (2025–26).** Lior Susan approached Sam Altman in early 2025. A pilot followed, then a multi-year 750 MW deal ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)), reported as ~$10B through 2028. OpenAI got warrants on 33.4M shares ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebras)). Altman and Greg Brockman were early personal investors ([src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-mints-two-billionaires-sets-stage-for-potential-ai-wave.html)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Pierre Lamond (Eclipse) | SeaMicro investor and friend; first call for Cerebras; TSMC intro | SeaMicro era (~2007–12) | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era) |\n| Lior Susan (Eclipse) | Investor; brokered G42 and OpenAI | 2016 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era) |\n| Eric Vishria (Benchmark) | Series A co-lead investor | 2016 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/) |\n| Bruce Dunlevie (Benchmark) | Hardware diligence on the 2016 pitch | 2016 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/) |\n| Gary Lauterbach | Co-founder of SeaMicro and Cerebras | 2007 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebras) |\n| Sean Lie | Cerebras co-founder and CTO; ~3% stake | 2015 | [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-mints-two-billionaires-sets-stage-for-potential-ai-wave.html) |\n| Michael James; Jean-Philippe Fricker | Cerebras co-founders, ex-SeaMicro | 2015 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebras) |\n| Sam Altman; Greg Brockman | Early personal investors; OpenAI is now a customer | unknown | [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-mints-two-billionaires-sets-stage-for-potential-ai-wave.html) |\n| Lip-Bu Tan | Early investor | unknown | [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-mints-two-billionaires-sets-stage-for-potential-ai-wave.html) |\n| G42 / Sheikh Tahnoon | Anchor customer and financier | 2021 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era) |\n| Scott Wilson (WashU) | Indirect LP via Eclipse; backs \"Andrew and the team\" | unknown | [20VC](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Does he angel invest or sit as an LP in funds? None found. WebSearch budget ran out this session, so this is not a negative finding.\n- His X and LinkedIn handles, and their cadence.\n- The original 20VC and No Priors episodes (dates and timestamps) behind the Eclipse quotes.\n- Who was in Cerebras's Series A: was Benchmark in the A or the B? Wikipedia and TechCrunch say A; MicroVentures says B.\n- Which Cerebras alumni have left to found companies? This is the talent lead that matters most to me.\n- Is the Anthropic exclusivity carve-out in the OpenAI deal temporary, and when does it end?\n\n## Sources\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-engine-of-the-intelligent-world-goes-public\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebras\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Feldman_(businessman)\n- https://www.cerebras.ai/blog/what-it-took-to-build-wafer-scale-and-why-it-lasts\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-mints-two-billionaires-sets-stage-for-potential-ai-wave.html\n- https://www.theregister.com/ai-ml/2026/05/15/cerebras-wafer-scale-ai-bet-delivers-blockbuster-ipo/5240821\n- https://microventures.com/microventures-portfolio-company-cerebras-history-and-milestones (aggregator)\n- https://inforcapital.com/news/for-eclipse-the-25b-cerebras-win-is-just-the-start-of-realizing-its-physical-world-thesis/ (secondary)\n- https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026\n", "person:andrew-ferguson": "\n# Andrew Ferguson\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (strategics-2 sweep). Companion to [firms/databricks-ventures.md](../firms/databricks-ventures.md); method from [firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md).*\n\n## One-line take\nFerguson is a corporate-development lifer who built Databricks Ventures (DV) from scratch in December 2021 and still runs it. He is the person who decides whether a data or AI-infra startup gets a Databricks check and the go-to-market that comes with it. He never sets the price.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins allocation:** the money is the smallest part of what he offers. He calls a DV check \"sort of a stamp of approval from Databricks\" ([GCV Powerlist 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-andrew-ferguson/?format=md)). It comes with roadmap access, integration help and Data + AI Summit exposure ([DV page](https://www.databricks.com/databricks-ventures)). Lead VCs bring him into rounds because he can test a product with Databricks' own customers ([Fortune 2024-05-21](https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund)).\n- **What he signs, and who approves:** he sources and runs the deals and is the public byline. CEO Ali Ghodsi \"signs off on every investment\" ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund)). Approval is an ad hoc email thread: \"there are fewer ultimate deciders\" ([GCV 2026-03-09](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/information-technology/databricks-ventures-building-ecosystem/?format=md)). Since April 2026 he has had a boss inside corp dev, SVP Philip Kirk (ex-ServiceNow) ([GCV 2026-04-13](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/kirk-joins-databricks-corporate-development-head/?format=md)). Whether Ferguson now reports to Kirk is **unconfirmed**.\n- **Constraint:** \"We don't lead rounds\", and the lead \"sets the terms in series A or later rounds\" ([GCV 2026](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/information-technology/databricks-ventures-building-ecosystem/?format=md)). There is no board seat and only \"very lightweight\" strategic terms ([GCV 2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/databricks-how-to-follow-the-enterprise-software-cvc-playbook)). He has said AI valuations \"don't pencil out\" and make him \"uncomfortable and nervous\". He justifies DV's entries on strategic value instead ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund)).\n- **What he'd never do:** price or size a frontier-lab round, take a board seat, or invest for financial return alone.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The only disclosed DV check size is a contest prize.** The 2025 Built-On Databricks Startup Challenge grand prize was \"Up to $600,000 in Databricks Ventures investment\" plus $400K in credits. RadiantGraph won ([Databricks blog 2025-06-26](https://www.databricks.com/blog/innovation-meets-intelligence-announcing-winners-built-databricks-startup-challenge), Ferguson first author). The 2026 winners post (VisionHeight) gives no investment figure ([Databricks blog 2026-07-01](https://databricks.com/blog/celebrating-winners-2026-built-databricks-startup-challenge)). Whether the $600K was actually invested is **unconfirmed**.\n- **The accelerator money is \"Angel-style funding to catalyze growth\"**: up to $250K including credits, terms not disclosed ([Databricks blog 2025-09-18](https://www.databricks.com/blog/fueling-next-generation-ai-announcing-databricks-ai-accelerator-program)). This is the only route by which DV writes pre-seed and seed checks on purpose.\n- **Diligence is customer references, not models.** On Unstructured he said DV could \"validate the quality of the product offering by talking to our own customers\". He has passed on companies after \"mixed feedback\" from customers or poor experiences from Databricks staff ([Fortune 2024-05-21](https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund)). For a founder, the way in is Databricks customers who already use the product.\n- **The unit outgrew his own plan.** \"I just did not foresee the volume of investing that we would do\" ([GCV 2026-03-09](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/information-technology/databricks-ventures-building-ecosystem/?format=md)). In February 2023 he said there were \"no immediate plans\" to lead rounds ([GCV 2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/databricks-how-to-follow-the-enterprise-software-cvc-playbook)). By 2024 DV had led Cube's Series B ([DBTA](https://www.dbta.com/Editorial/News-Flashes/Cube-Raises-25M-to-Supercharge-the-Universal-Semantic-Layer-Backed-by-Databricks-164448.aspx)).\n- **The startup challenge doubles as a co-sourcing funnel with outside VCs.** 2025 judges were George Mathew (Insight) and Sarah Catanzaro (Amplify); 2026 judges were Sabrina Wu (Madrona) and Madison Faulkner (NEA) ([2025 post](https://www.databricks.com/blog/innovation-meets-intelligence-announcing-winners-built-databricks-startup-challenge), [2026 post](https://databricks.com/blog/celebrating-winners-2026-built-databricks-startup-challenge)).\n- **2026 focus is app-building.** He is \"spending a lot of time\" in \"the app-building space broadly defined\" ([GCV 2026](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/information-technology/databricks-ventures-building-ecosystem/?format=md)). This fits DV's Lovable Series B (December 2025) and Replit on the portfolio page ([firm file](../firms/databricks-ventures.md)).\n- **What he is NOT named on:** the AI deals the owner cares about most (Inferact's $150M seed, RadixArk's $100M seed, OpenRouter Series B, LanceDB Series A, Fireworks Series A) list \"Databricks\" or \"Databricks Ventures\" in company posts, but none names Ferguson ([Inferact](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference), [RadixArk](https://www.radixark.com/blog/radixark-launches-100m-seed), [OpenRouter](https://openrouter.ai/blog/announcements/series-b/), [LanceDB](https://lancedb.com/blog/series-a-funding)). His bylines are mostly data-ecosystem companies. Who inside Databricks sourced the inference-engine seeds is **unconfirmed**. The Berkeley/Ion Stoica link and Laude Ventures (Andy Konwinski) are the likelier paths.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a co-investor for a ~$20M frontier-lab seed and won't set its price. He is a later-round strategic follower with a parent that buys its portfolio companies (Arcion, Tecton, Neon; see the firm file). He matters to the owner in two ways: (1) a distribution and customer-reference partner for AI-infra and inference seeds that sell to Databricks customers, and (2) an exit path through Databricks M&A, now with Kirk above him. Deals that matter: Mistral AI (Series A with a partnership, 2024), Perplexity (A and B), Fireworks (A), Adaptive ML (seed), Inferact and RadixArk seeds (DV in, Ferguson not named).\n\n## Background\n- Princeton undergraduate (2001) and Harvard Business School MBA (2007), per MarketScreener (aggregator, **unconfirmed** in a primary source) ([MarketScreener](https://in.marketscreener.com/insider/ANDREW-FERGUSON-A3FCBO/)).\n- eBay: director of corporate development, six years ([GCV Powerlist 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-andrew-ferguson/?format=md)). MarketScreener gives 2007-2013. Its \"Director/Board Member\" label looks like bad normalization; no eBay board seat is supported.\n- New Relic: head of corporate development, then VP strategy and development, seven years ending 2020 ([GCV Powerlist 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-andrew-ferguson/?format=md)). Which New Relic acquisitions he ran is **not found**.\n- Databricks: joined 2020 as VP corporate development ([GCV Powerlist 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-andrew-ferguson/?format=md)). Named VP corporate development and ventures, leading DV, at launch in December 2021 ([GCV 2021-12-30](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/databricks-sets-up-venture-fund/?format=md)). Titled \"VP, Databricks Ventures\" by 2025-2026 ([speaker page](https://www.databricks.com/dataaisummit/speaker/andrew-ferguson)).\n- Named to the GCV Powerlist in 2025 ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-andrew-ferguson/?format=md)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.databricks.com/databricks-ventures | DV page; no personal bio or photo |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.databricks.com/blog/author/andrew-ferguson | 20 posts as of 2026-11-10; latest 2026-07-01 (startup challenge winners) |\n| X | not found | |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | No URL confirmed from a primary source |\n| GitHub | not found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n| Events | https://www.databricks.com/dataaisummit/speaker/andrew-ferguson | Data + AI Summit Startup Forum (2026) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- GVR Pod, \"Making sense of data\", 2023-01-30, host Fernando Moncada Rivera. Covers why DV doesn't lead or take board seats. No transcript was found ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gvr-pod-making-sense-of-data)).\n- Founder fireside with Barry Dauber (Databricks VP GenAI GTM), moderated by Guy Fighel (Hetz Ventures). Date not confirmed ([Luma](https://luma.com/ab9sutd0)).\n- Data + AI Summit 2026 Startup Forum ([speaker page](https://www.databricks.com/dataaisummit/speaker/andrew-ferguson)).\n- Long interviews: GCV 2023 ([link](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/databricks-how-to-follow-the-enterprise-software-cvc-playbook)), Fortune Term Sheet exclusive 2024 ([link](https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund)), GCV 2026 ([link](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/information-technology/databricks-ventures-building-ecosystem/?format=md)).\n\n## How they think\n- Strategy first, price second: \"more focused on whether it makes strategic sense to get into the round, than on the valuation\" ([GCV 2026](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/information-technology/databricks-ventures-building-ecosystem/?format=md)).\n- Screens AI apps for model-provider risk: whether the company is \"going to get displaced by the model providers\" (same source). He expects \"more than one winner\" (same source).\n- Uses investments to lock in partners: \"We use an investment to really tightly align\" (same source).\n- Treats the portfolio as a market sensor: \"We get a lot of feedback about what segments are waxing and waning\" (same source).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [GCV 2026-03-09, \"Databricks Ventures building ecosystem\"](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/information-technology/databricks-ventures-building-ecosystem/?format=md): the most candid interview (approval process, app-building focus).\n2. [Fortune Term Sheet 2024-05-21](https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund): AI valuations, diligence by customer reference, Ghodsi sign-off.\n3. [GCV 2023-02-14](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/databricks-how-to-follow-the-enterprise-software-cvc-playbook): the original follower and no-board-seat model.\n\n## Social engagement\nNo public X, Substack or LinkedIn activity was verified. His network shows up in deal posts instead: Databricks product and engineering co-signers (Patrick Wendell, Denny Lee, Kevin Rasmussen, Steve Sobel, Kevin Clugage), the DV team (Phil Shin, Philip Kirk) and the outside VCs who judge his startup challenge (Insight, Amplify, Madrona, NEA).\n\n## Portfolio\nOnly deals where Ferguson is personally named (quoted or bylined). For DV deals that don't name him, see [firms/databricks-ventures.md](../firms/databricks-ventures.md).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Labelbox | Series D ($110M) | 2022-01-06 | no | SoftBank Vision Fund 2 (lead); Snowpoint Ventures | [DV post, Ferguson + Kevin Clugage](https://www.databricks.com/blog/2022/01/06/why-we-invested-in-labelbox-streamline-unstructured-data-workflows-in-a-lakehouse.html); DV's \"first announced investment\" |\n| Tecton | Undisclosed | 2022-07-12 | no | Snowflake Ventures | [DV post](https://www.databricks.com/blog/2022/07/12/databricks-ventures-invests-in-tecton-an-enterprise-feature-platform-for-the-lakehouse.html) (sole Ferguson byline, verified); acquired by Databricks 2025 |\n| Immuta | Series E | 2023-05-04 | no | | [DV post](https://www.databricks.com/blog/strengthening-lakehouse-governance-ecosystem-databricks-ventures-invests-immuta) (with Jonathan Keller) |\n| Catalyst | Undisclosed | 2023-05-25 | no | | [DV post](https://www.databricks.com/blog/powering-customer-led-growth-databricks-ventures-invests-catalyst) |\n| Hightouch | Undisclosed ($38M round) | 2023-08-30 | no | | [DV post](https://www.databricks.com/blog/activating-data-lakehouse-databricks-ventures-invests-hightouch) |\n| Anomalo | Series B ($33M) | 2024-01-23 | no | SignalFire (lead) | [Anomalo](https://www.anomalo.com/blog/databricks-ventures-strategic-investment-in-anomalo-and-the-future-of-enterprise-data-ai/) (quoted) |\n| Mistral, Perplexity, Unstructured, Glean, Anomalo, Cleanlab | named in AI Fund launch post | 2024-05-21 | no | | [Databricks blog](https://www.databricks.com/blog/introducing-databricks-ai-fund) (launch post, not deal announcements) |\n| Cube (Cube.js) | Series B ($25M) | 2024-06-06 | **yes** (DV led) | Decibel; Bain Capital Ventures | [DBTA](https://www.dbta.com/Editorial/News-Flashes/Cube-Raises-25M-to-Supercharge-the-Universal-Semantic-Layer-Backed-by-Databricks-164448.aspx) (quoted) |\n| Galileo | Series B | 2024-10-24 | no | | [DV post](https://www.databricks.com/blog/bringing-together-data-intelligence-and-evaluation-intelligence-databricks-ventures-invests) |\n| Omni | Undisclosed | 2025-04-23 | no | | [DV post](https://www.databricks.com/blog/enabling-self-service-business-intelligence-all-databricks-ventures-invests-omni) |\n| LlamaIndex | Undisclosed | 2025-05-01 | no | | [DV post](https://www.databricks.com/blog/databricks-invests-llamaindex-advance-knowledge-agents-over-enterprise-data) (with Wendell, Lee, Shin) |\n| Theom | Series A | 2025-05-12 | no | | [DV post](https://www.databricks.com/blog/data-governance-and-security-ai-era-databricks-ventures-invests-theom) |\n| Noma Security | Undisclosed | 2025-06-05 | no | | [DV post](https://www.databricks.com/blog/securing-ai-lifecycle-databricks-ventures-invests-noma-security) |\n| Anvilogic | Undisclosed | 2025-06-09 | no | | [DV post](https://www.databricks.com/blog/shaping-future-soc-databricks-ventures-invests-anvilogic) |\n| RadiantGraph | Undisclosed (prize: up to $600K DV investment) | 2025-06-26 | n/a | | [Databricks blog](https://www.databricks.com/blog/innovation-meets-intelligence-announcing-winners-built-databricks-startup-challenge); closing **unconfirmed** |\n| AI Accelerator cohort 1 (Alpha Level, Datalinx AI, Deductive AI, Fleak, Ziggiz) | Pre-seed/Seed (up to $250K incl. credits) | 2025-09-18 | n/a | | [Databricks blog](https://www.databricks.com/blog/fueling-next-generation-ai-announcing-databricks-ai-accelerator-program) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Labelbox (DV's first deal):** the post opens by recalling that Databricks had announced DV \"last month\" and frames Labelbox as an existing lakehouse partner ([Databricks blog 2022-01-06](https://www.databricks.com/blog/2022/01/06/why-we-invested-in-labelbox-streamline-unstructured-data-workflows-in-a-lakehouse.html)). The pattern was set here: a follower check in a round led by a large financial investor (SoftBank Vision Fund 2), into an existing integration partner.\n- **Cube (his one known lead):** DV was named lead of Cube's $25M Series B, and Ferguson was quoted ([DBTA 2024-06-10](https://www.dbta.com/Editorial/News-Flashes/Cube-Raises-25M-to-Supercharge-the-Universal-Semantic-Layer-Backed-by-Databricks-164448.aspx)). It's a semantic-layer ecosystem company, not an AI lab.\n- **Unstructured:** customer validation was the deciding input. Databricks talked to its own customers about the product ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Databricks Ventures | at: VP, head of Databricks Ventures (founder of the unit) | 2021-12 | [GCV 2021](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/databricks-sets-up-venture-fund/?format=md) |\n| Databricks | at: VP corporate development | 2020 | [GCV Powerlist 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-andrew-ferguson/?format=md) |\n| New Relic | at: head of corp dev, then VP strategy and development (to 2020) | about 2013 | [GCV Powerlist 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-andrew-ferguson/?format=md) |\n| eBay | at: director, corporate development (about 2007-2013) | about 2007 | [GCV Powerlist 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-andrew-ferguson/?format=md) |\n| Ali Ghodsi | approver of every DV check | 2021 | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund) |\n| Philip Kirk | SVP corp dev and ventures above him | 2026-04 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/kirk-joins-databricks-corporate-development-head/?format=md) |\n| Phil Shin | deputy (Sr. Director, M&A and ventures) | 2022 | [BR Venture Fund](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2) |\n| Kevin Clugage | co-author, Labelbox post | 2022-01 | [Databricks blog](https://www.databricks.com/blog/2022/01/06/why-we-invested-in-labelbox-streamline-unstructured-data-workflows-in-a-lakehouse.html) |\n| Steve Sobel, Kevin Rasmussen | co-authors, startup challenge and AI Accelerator | 2025 | [Databricks blog](https://www.databricks.com/blog/fueling-next-generation-ai-announcing-databricks-ai-accelerator-program) |\n| Sarah Catanzaro (Amplify), George Mathew (Insight) | startup challenge judges | 2025-06 | [Databricks blog](https://www.databricks.com/blog/innovation-meets-intelligence-announcing-winners-built-databricks-startup-challenge) |\n| Sabrina Wu (Madrona), Madison Faulkner (NEA) | startup challenge judges | 2026-07 | [Databricks blog](https://databricks.com/blog/celebrating-winners-2026-built-databricks-startup-challenge) |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** (1) the Built-On Databricks Startup Challenge and the invitation-only AI Accelerator, both run by his team; (2) a lead VC in the Databricks VC Network (a16z, NEA, Menlo, Insight, Madrona and others; see the firm file) bringing DV in as a follower; (3) Databricks customers who can vouch for the product, since that is his diligence; (4) Data + AI Summit Startup Forum. No public email or social channel was found.\n\n## Open questions\n- Who sourced the Inferact, RadixArk and OpenRouter checks, and whether Ferguson or Kirk signed them.\n- His reporting line since Kirk arrived (April 2026).\n- Whether the RadiantGraph $600K prize investment closed, and the 2026 prize terms.\n- Which New Relic deals he ran (Opsmatic, SignifAI, IOpipe, Pixie are candidates; none confirmed).\n- Education (MarketScreener only). Exact eBay dates (2006 vs 2007 start).\n- Id note: `names.py --canon firm \"eBay\"` resolves to `ebay-ventures`, which is used here for his eBay corp dev job. The firm batch uses `ebay`. The main session should pick one.\n\n## Sources\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-andrew-ferguson/?format=md\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/databricks-sets-up-venture-fund/?format=md\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/databricks-how-to-follow-the-enterprise-software-cvc-playbook\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/information-technology/databricks-ventures-building-ecosystem/?format=md\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gvr-pod-making-sense-of-data\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/kirk-joins-databricks-corporate-development-head/?format=md\n- https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund\n- https://www.databricks.com/blog/author/andrew-ferguson\n- https://www.databricks.com/blog/2022/01/06/why-we-invested-in-labelbox-streamline-unstructured-data-workflows-in-a-lakehouse.html\n- https://www.databricks.com/blog/innovation-meets-intelligence-announcing-winners-built-databricks-startup-challenge\n- https://databricks.com/blog/celebrating-winners-2026-built-databricks-startup-challenge\n- https://www.databricks.com/blog/fueling-next-generation-ai-announcing-databricks-ai-accelerator-program\n- https://www.databricks.com/dataaisummit/speaker/andrew-ferguson\n- https://www.dbta.com/Editorial/News-Flashes/Cube-Raises-25M-to-Supercharge-the-Universal-Semantic-Layer-Backed-by-Databricks-164448.aspx\n- https://labelbox.com/blog/announcing-our-series-d/\n- https://in.marketscreener.com/insider/ANDREW-FERGUSON-A3FCBO/ (aggregator)\n- https://luma.com/ab9sutd0\n", "person:andrew-reed": "\n# Andrew Reed\n\n## One-line take\nOne of the seven GPs who now run Sequoia. He has the board seats at Harmonic and Parallel, co-signs SSI, and quietly spots technical talent before it raises (he made the Jared Quincy Davis / Mithril connection in 2021). But he writes growth checks into rounds others started.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** Reed is a growth-team GP who wins the priced round once a company or founder is already proven: SSI's first $1B round, Harmonic's $75M A, Parallel's $100M B at $2B. Long founder relationships help him here. Sequoia's Robinhood relationship, where he is the only partner listed, sits right next to Harmonic, which Robinhood CEO Vlad Tenev co-founded ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/robinhood/), [src](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/)).\n- **What constrains him:** he is almost never first money. On Parallel he came in about 27 months after the seed and five months after Kleiner and Index's A ([src](https://parallel.ai/blog/series-b)). On ElevenLabs he joined a round that a16z, Nat Friedman and Daniel Gross co-led ([src](https://elevenlabs.io/blog/series-b)).\n- **What he would never do (inferred, not sourced):** lead a speculative pre-company seed himself. His one pre-seed listing is Rec Room (2016) ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/andrew-reed/)).\n- **Why he matters now:** as a GP named in the 2026-11-04 memo, he is part of the small group that decides how Sequoia allocates after it cut back Arc and Scouts ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He is a talent router, not just a growth signer.** Shaun Maguire writes that Reed met Jared Quincy Davis in 2021, saw the shared interests, connected them, and Sequoia made Davis a Scout. Davis later founded Mithril (AI compute), where Sequoia was first money: \"In 2021, my partner Andrew Reed met Jared.\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/) (Maguire, 2024-03-21). Reed is not listed on Mithril himself, so the credit goes to Maguire while the sourcing came from Reed.\n- **SSI and Harmonic are paired with Maguire.** Sequoia lists both companies with exactly two partners, Reed and Maguire, both from 2024 ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/safe-superintelligence/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/harmonic/)). On Harmonic the board seat went to Reed: \"We wish to extend a big welcome to Sequoia partner Andrew Reed\" [src](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/) (2024-09-23). Which partner sourced which deal is not public.\n- **Harmonic sits next to his Robinhood relationship (inference).** Reed is the only Sequoia partner listed on Robinhood, which Sequoia partnered in 2018 ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/robinhood/)). Harmonic's board is Tenev, Achim and Reed ([src](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/)). Index's Jan Hammer, also a former Robinhood board member, came in at the same A as observer (see [jan-hammer](jan-hammer.md)). My read: Robinhood alumni boards carried over into Harmonic.\n- **On Parallel he took the seat over two junior names.** Sequoia lists Abhishek Malani and Sonali Singh on Parallel too ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/parallel-web-systems/)), but Parallel's post says \"Andrew Reed, Partner at Sequoia, joins our board.\" [src](https://parallel.ai/blog/series-b). He then co-hosted Training Data with Parag Agrawal on 2026-08-25 ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/parallels-parag-agrawal-building-a-new-web-for-ai-agents/id1750736528?i=1000785718190)). No transcript was available, so nothing he said is quoted here.\n- **How he judges founders (firm-site, low non-obviousness):** \"I care about performance, ethics, clarity of thought, teamwork, ambition—and will to win.\" The same page says he cares about these more than pedigree, and adds \"The second common thread is a focus on quality and craftsmanship.\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/andrew-reed/)\n- **Posts on X as @andrew__reed.** Sonali Singh cites him in a post about how posting in public (\"cringe\") is now accepted: \"Andrew Reed tweeted about it too.\" [src](https://singhso.substack.com/p/schadenfreude-and-fremdschamen-things) (2026-01-27). We couldn't fetch the tweet itself (X returned 402).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:** he is on two frontier-AI names I care about. He co-signs Safe Superintelligence (a new lab, first $1B round) and leads Harmonic (a mathematical-superintelligence lab, Series A, board seat).\n- **Against:** his entries are priced, growth-sized rounds that come after the first money, and he co-signs with Maguire rather than originating seeds. I won't be in competition with him at a $600M seed. He is more likely an up-round buyer of what I seed than a peer at entry. The Davis/Mithril story shows he does meet pre-company technical talent, which makes him a useful node for introductions.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** SSI, Harmonic, Parallel (agent web infrastructure), ElevenLabs (voice AI lab).\n\n## Background\n- Joined Sequoia in 2014, coming from quantitative investing: \"I had some depth on the quantitative side of investing\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/andrew-reed/).\n- His first partnered company on the page is Klarna (2010), which predates his 2014 join, so that listing likely reflects later involvement. Not explained.\n- Pronoun \"he\" follows Maguire's Mithril post ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/)).\n- Education and prior employers: not on the firm page. Nothing found (web search budget was exhausted this run).\n- 2026-11-04: named one of seven GPs who \"primarily own and operate Sequoia\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/andrew-reed/ | firm bio and portfolio list (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/andrew__reed | handle from the tweet link in [src](https://singhso.substack.com/p/schadenfreude-and-fremdschamen-things); follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Training Data (Sequoia), occasional co-host | [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/training-data/id1750736528) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-08 (25th): Training Data, \"Parallel's Parag Agrawal: Building a New Web for AI Agents\", co-hosted with Sonya Huang. [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/parallels-parag-agrawal-building-a-new-web-for-ai-agents/id1750736528?i=1000785718190). No transcript was available. The episode covers agent-first search, Parallel's incremental index, and paying content owners using Shapley values (from the episode summary only).\n\n## How they think\n- **Founder filter:** performance, ethics, clarity of thought and will to win, ahead of pedigree ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/andrew-reed/)).\n- **Second filter:** product quality and craftsmanship. His portfolio leans to well-crafted tools: Figma, Loom, Warp, Sourcegraph, Zapier, Strava ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/andrew-reed/)).\n- **AI pattern:** buys into technical-founder companies with \"superintelligence\" framing (SSI, Harmonic's \"mathematical superintelligence\") once credibility is established. Inference from the portfolio list, not from anything he has said.\n- Contrarian views and admitted mistakes: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Sequoia bio, his own founder criteria: https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/andrew-reed/\n2. Maguire on Mithril, which shows Reed routing talent in 2021: https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/\n3. Harmonic Series A, his board seat: https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/\n4. Parallel Series B, his board seat: https://parallel.ai/blog/series-b\n5. Training Data episode with Parag Agrawal (audio only): https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/parallels-parag-agrawal-building-a-new-web-for-ai-agents/id1750736528?i=1000785718190\n\n## Social engagement\n- X: @andrew__reed. Singh describes him as posting about the idea that \"cringe is in\", and her footnote suggests he posts often ([src](https://singhso.substack.com/p/schadenfreude-and-fremdschamen-things)).\n- Who he replies to and amplifies: not checked (X not fetchable this run).\n\n## Portfolio\nAI-relevant deals first. Sequoia's page gives stage and year only, and round details come from company or press sources.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Safe Superintelligence | First outside round, $1B+ at a reported $5B valuation (Sequoia lists it as Growth) | 2024-09-04 | Lead not disclosed. Sequoia was one of five named firms | a16z, DST Global, SV Angel, NFDG. Sequoia co-signer: Shaun Maguire | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/04/ilya-sutskevers-startup-safe-super-intelligence-raises-1b/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/safe-superintelligence/) |\n| Harmonic | Series A, $75M | 2024-09-23 | Yes, Sequoia led. Reed took the board seat | Index (Jan Hammer, observer), GreatPoint, Era Funds, Blossom, DST Global partners, Elefund, Nikesh Arora, Jared Leto. Co-signer: Maguire | [src](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/harmonic/) |\n| ElevenLabs | Series B, $80M (Sequoia lists partnered 2023) | announced 2024-01-22 | No: a16z, Nat Friedman and Daniel Gross co-led | SV Angel, Smash, BroadLight, Credo. Co-signer: James Flynn | [src](https://elevenlabs.io/blog/series-b), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/elevenlabs/) |\n| Parallel Web Systems | Series B, $100M at $2B | 2026-04-29 | Yes, Sequoia led. Reed took the board seat | Kleiner, Index, Khosla, First Round, Spark, Terrain. Co-signers: Abhishek Malani, Sonali Singh | [src](https://parallel.ai/blog/series-b), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/parallel-web-systems/) |\n| Neuralink | Series E, $650M (Sequoia lists partnered 2025) | 2025-06-02 | No, participant (no lead named) | ARK, Founders Fund, Thrive, Lightspeed, QIA, G42, Vy and others. Co-signers: James Flynn, Shaun Maguire | [src](https://neuralink.com/blog/neuralink-raises-650m-series-e/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/neuralink/) |\n| Warp | Growth | 2022 | not found | not found | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/andrew-reed/) |\n| Sourcegraph | Growth | 2020 | not found | not found | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/andrew-reed/) |\n| Zapier | Growth | 2020 | not found | not found | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/andrew-reed/) |\n| Pierre | Early (since acquired) | 2024 | not found | not found | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/andrew-reed/) |\n| Chainguard | Early | 2022 | not found | not found | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/andrew-reed/) |\n| Robinhood | Growth (page lists partnered 2018) | 2018 | not found | Reed is the only Sequoia partner listed | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/robinhood/) |\n\nNot AI, from the firm page: ARQ, Bolt, Front, Medallion, Odoo, Papaya, Peregrine, Phantom, Productboard, Rappi, Rec Room (Pre-Seed/Seed 2016), SendCutSend, Snapdocs, Strava, Tourlane, Vanta, Figma, GitHub, Klarna, Loom, Sumo Logic ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/andrew-reed/)).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Mithril (Reed is not the named partner).** Reed met Jared Quincy Davis in 2021 and introduced him to Maguire because of their shared interests. Sequoia made Davis a Scout, and Sequoia later put in Mithril's first money ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/)).\n- **Harmonic.** Sequoia led the $75M A with significant participation from Index, and Reed joined Tenev and Achim on the board ([src](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/)). No seed lead was found, so Sequoia's A is the first named institutional lead on record. The intro path through Robinhood is inference (see Differential insight).\n- **SSI.** Sequoia was among the named backers of the first outside round, about three months after the 2024 founding ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/04/ilya-sutskevers-startup-safe-super-intelligence-raises-1b/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/safe-superintelligence/)). Lead, check size and intro path were not found.\n- **Parallel.** Khosla, Index and First Round seeded it in January 2024, and Kleiner and Index co-led the A in November 2025 at $740M. Sequoia then led the B at $2B five months later, and Reed took the board seat ([src](https://parallel.ai/blog/series-b), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/29/parallel-web-systems-hits-2b-valuation-five-months-after-its-last-big-raise/)). That is a classic Sequoia growth entry.\n- **ElevenLabs.** Sequoia joined the a16z/Friedman/Gross co-led B as a participant. Reed and James Flynn are listed ([src](https://elevenlabs.io/blog/series-b), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/elevenlabs/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Shaun Maguire (Sequoia) | Co-partner on SSI, Harmonic and Neuralink. Reed introduced Jared Quincy Davis to him | 2021 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/) |\n| James Flynn (Sequoia) | Co-partner on ElevenLabs and Neuralink | 2023 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/elevenlabs/) |\n| Abhishek Malani (Sequoia) | Co-listed on Parallel | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/parallel-web-systems/) |\n| Sonali Singh (Sequoia) | Co-listed on Parallel. Cites Reed on her Substack | 2026 | [src](https://singhso.substack.com/p/schadenfreude-and-fremdschamen-things) |\n| Sonya Huang (Sequoia) | Training Data co-host | 2026 | [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/parallels-parag-agrawal-building-a-new-web-for-ai-agents/id1750736528?i=1000785718190) |\n| Vlad Tenev | Robinhood (Reed is the only listed Sequoia partner, 2018). Fellow Harmonic board member | 2018 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/robinhood/), [src](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/) |\n| Tudor Achim | Fellow Harmonic board member | 2024 | [src](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/) |\n| Parag Agrawal | Parallel board. Interviewed him on Training Data | 2026 | [src](https://parallel.ai/blog/series-b) |\n| Jared Quincy Davis | Met in 2021 and routed to Maguire, then Sequoia Scout, then Mithril | 2021 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/) |\n| Jan Hammer (Index) | Co-investor at the Harmonic A (observer seat) | 2024 | [src](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did Sequoia or Reed lead or co-lead SSI's first round, and how big was Sequoia's check? Did Sequoia follow on into later SSI rounds?\n- Harmonic: did Sequoia put in any pre-A money? Was the intro through Tenev and Robinhood?\n- Pull the Training Data transcript with Parag Agrawal to get Reed's own words on agents and the web.\n- His X feed (@andrew__reed): which researchers and founders does he amplify?\n- Education and pre-2014 career: nothing found (web search budget was exhausted this run).\n- Etched, Abhishek Malani and Peter Abbeel (from the user's prompt) are outside this profile; Malani is listed as a lead.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/andrew-reed/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/safe-superintelligence/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/harmonic/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/elevenlabs/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/parallel-web-systems/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/neuralink/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/robinhood/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/\n- https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/\n- https://parallel.ai/blog/series-b\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/29/parallel-web-systems-hits-2b-valuation-five-months-after-its-last-big-raise/\n- https://elevenlabs.io/blog/series-b\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/04/ilya-sutskevers-startup-safe-super-intelligence-raises-1b/\n- https://neuralink.com/blog/neuralink-raises-650m-series-e/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/parallels-parag-agrawal-building-a-new-web-for-ai-agents/id1750736528?i=1000785718190\n- https://singhso.substack.com/p/schadenfreude-and-fremdschamen-things\n", "person:andy-bredar": "\n# Andy Bredar\n\n## One-line take\nAndy Bredar is a junior investor at Radical's SF office. He reached venture through Anthropic's partnerships and go-to-market team after Bain's PE group. No source names him on a deal yet, so for now he is worth tracking as an early way into Radical's Bay Area flow and the Anthropic alumni network, not for a track record. [src](https://radical.vc/team/andy-bredar/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he could win:** he has an engineering degree (B.S. CS&E, summa cum laude, Santa Clara) plus commercial work inside a frontier lab, \"worked on Partnerships and Go-To-Market strategy at Anthropic\" [src](https://radical.vc/team/andy-bredar/). Few seed investors have that mix. It probably helps with AI-native application and enterprise-adoption companies and with Anthropic alumni starting companies (the network angle is an inference, not sourced).\n- **What constrains him:** he is junior. His title is \"Investor\", below Principal (Jules Qiu, Natasha Lim) and well below Partner in Radical's hierarchy [src](https://radical.vc/team/). Radical's lead deals are credited to partners on its posts and team pages, and his team page lists no portfolio companies. A WordPress search of Radical's posts, pages and company pages for \"Bredar\" found nothing (checked 2026-11-10).\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** non-AI deals. Radical invests only in AI.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He was at Radical earlier than the site suggests.** He commented \"Welcome to the team\" on Rich Kotite's \"joined Radical\" LinkedIn post. That post's activity ID decodes to 2026-02-20, so Bredar was on the team by then, and the date also pins Kotite's arrival, which the firm file lists as \"not found\". The radical.vc team page dates from the 2026-07-24 site rebuild and is not a join date. [src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/rich-kotite-34459415_excited-to-share-that-ive-joined-radical-activity-7430624722096320513-2F3P) [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrew-bredar/)\n- **He was close to the Colin Murdoch hire.** He reposted Murdoch's join post (activity ID decodes to 2026-09-23) and commented \"Welcome Colin!! So excited to have you on the team\". Murdoch, ex-DeepMind, joined as Strategic Advisor and says he chose Radical partly to work with Aaron Rosenberg again. [src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/cmurdoch_excited-to-share-that-im-joining-radical-activity-7508508167127908353-Ubdb) [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrew-bredar/)\n- **A possible compute sourcing thread (weak).** He liked Sail Research CEO Neil Movva's post, which says \"At Sail Research we scavenge for chips.\" Sail is not in Radical's portfolio according to this repo's Radical rows, so the like may point to a sourcing conversation. A like proves nothing. [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrew-bredar/)\n- **Trap to avoid.** An AI summary of his LinkedIn said \"Andy co-led\" Discovery Loop's seed with Khosla. The raw page shows that sentence is Aaron Rosenberg's own post (\"Along with Jordan Jacobs ... I am thrilled to co-lead\"), which Bredar reposted. Bredar is **not** credited on Discovery Loop. [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrew-bredar/)\n- Nothing found from his own writing, podcasts or X: he has no essays on radical.vc, no personal X account was found, and his personal site was down (see below).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Radical itself is a high fit (Discovery Loop, Veeda, Prime Intellect, Generalist). Bredar has no named deal, though, and he is not a decision-maker on $10M+ checks yet. What matters for you: (1) a route into Radical's SF deal flow and syndicates below the partner level; (2) Anthropic GTM and partnerships alumni, a pool that will produce AI-native automation founders (the Serval type you like); (3) a junior to re-check in 12–18 months for a first credited deal or a promotion to Principal. Deals of his that matter to you: none yet.\n\n## Background\n- Santa Clara University, B.S. Computer Science & Engineering, summa cum laude, minor in Mathematics [src](https://radical.vc/team/andy-bredar/). LinkedIn lists the Provost Scholarship (2018-09), Dean's List 2019–2022, the Carl H. Hayn Physics Prize (2020-06), Santa Clara Consulting VP of Finance and Sigma Chi fundraising chair [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrew-bredar/). Graduation was probably around 2022 (inference from the Dean's List years).\n- Bain & Company, Senior Associate Consultant, Private Equity Group, San Francisco (dates not found) [src](https://radical.vc/team/andy-bredar/)\n- Anthropic, Partnerships and Go-To-Market strategy (dates and title not found) [src](https://radical.vc/team/andy-bredar/)\n- Radical Ventures, Investor, SF (on the team by about 2026-02-20; see Differential insight)\n- From Denver; lists English and Portuguese on LinkedIn; his GitHub (AndrewBredar) holds only student-era projects from 2020–22 [src](https://github.com/AndrewBredar)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | andrew-bredar.com | Listed on LinkedIn and as his GitHub blog; did not resolve on https or http on 2026-11-10 (andrewbredar.github.io redirects to it) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | No radical.vc essays bylined to him (site search, 2026-11-10) |\n| X | nothing found | Team page links only the firm account (@radicalvcfund); GitHub twitter field is empty |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrew-bredar/ | 4,235 followers (2026-11-10); mostly reposts of Radical portfolio news |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/AndrewBredar | 13 repos, all student or hobby projects (2020–22) |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search budget was exhausted in this run, so only radical.vc and LinkedIn were checked.\n\n## How they think\n- No first-person theses found. His LinkedIn activity clusters on world models and physical AI (Veeda, Generalist GEN-1.5), AI for science (Discovery Loop), computer-use agents (Yutori Navigator n2), open training and compute infrastructure (Prime Intellect, which bought Moondream; Sail Research) [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrew-bredar/). That shows what he amplifies, not what he believes.\n- His LinkedIn About calls Radical \"a multi-stage AI-focused VC\" [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrew-bredar/)\n- Calls he got right or wrong: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\nNothing he has authored was found. Proxies:\n- His Radical bio: https://radical.vc/team/andy-bredar/\n- Rosenberg's Discovery Loop post, which Bredar reposted, to see the deal type his team pursues (via his LinkedIn activity)\n- \"The Rise of the NeoLab\" (Kotite and Rosenberg), Radical's current frontier-lab thesis: https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/\n\n## Social engagement\nOn LinkedIn he reposts Radical portfolio launches (Discovery Loop, Veeda, Generalist, Yutori, Prime Intellect), firm programs (the AI Founders Masterclass) and team hires (Murdoch). He likes posts from Rob Toews (a LeCun podcast teaser) and Neil Movva (Sail Research). He comments to welcome new colleagues (Kotite in Feb 2026, Murdoch in Sep 2026) [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrew-bredar/). Nobody was found amplifying him.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| nothing found | | | | | Radical team page lists no companies for him; radical.vc posts, pages and company pages contain no \"Bredar\" (WP search, 2026-11-10) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. Radical credits Discovery Loop to Jacobs and Rosenberg, Prime Intellect to Katz, and Generalist to Welch and Toews. Bredar only reposted these.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Investor, SF | by about 2026-02 (bound, not a join date) | [src](https://radical.vc/team/andy-bredar/) |\n| Anthropic | Former: Partnerships and GTM strategy | dates not found | [src](https://radical.vc/team/andy-bredar/) |\n| Bain & Company | Former: Senior Associate Consultant, PE Group | dates not found | [src](https://radical.vc/team/andy-bredar/) |\n| Rich Kotite | Radical colleague; welcomed him on his join post | about 2026-02-20 | [src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/rich-kotite-34459415_excited-to-share-that-ive-joined-radical-activity-7430624722096320513-2F3P) |\n| Colin Murdoch | Radical colleague (Strategic Advisor) | about 2026-09-23 | [src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/cmurdoch_excited-to-share-that-im-joining-radical-activity-7508508167127908353-Ubdb) |\n\n## Open questions\n- His actual join date and Anthropic tenure (LinkedIn hides the experience dates from logged-out viewers).\n- Whether he sources or diligences any 2026 Radical deal (Discovery Loop, Veeda, Prime Intellect) without being credited.\n- Whether the Sail Research engagement is a live deal.\n- Whether he can angel-invest, or holds Anthropic equity that ties him to that alumni network.\n- A podcast or X presence: the web search budget ran out, so search again next run.\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/andy-bredar/ (bio; headshot checked: HTTP 200, image/webp, 2026-11-10)\n- https://radical.vc/team/\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrew-bredar/ (public profile and activity, fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.linkedin.com/posts/rich-kotite-34459415_excited-to-share-that-ive-joined-radical-activity-7430624722096320513-2F3P\n- https://www.linkedin.com/posts/cmurdoch_excited-to-share-that-im-joining-radical-activity-7508508167127908353-Ubdb\n- https://github.com/AndrewBredar (GitHub API, 2026-11-10)\n- radical.vc WordPress REST search (posts, pages, company) for \"Bredar\": no results, 2026-11-10\n", "person:andy-duong": "\n# Andy Duong\n\n## One-line take\nA recently promoted investment director at Samsung Next who keeps showing up early-ish in AI infrastructure and agent rails, and who can put a Samsung strategic check into a frontier-adjacent lab (Odyssey) but cannot lead or price.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** Not price or terms: Samsung Next only takes \"minority, non-lead\" positions ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/)). He wins on the Samsung device and distribution angle, which his memos spell out. Perplexity was pitched as a way for consumer-electronics makers to \"diversify their search solutions\" ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-perplexity)).\n- **His pattern.** He backs abstraction layers over fragmented infrastructure: Unify (one API across models and frameworks), Expanso (compute where the data sits) and Shabodi (one API across telco networks), three memos between Apr and Sep 2024 ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-unify), [src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-expanso), [src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-shabodi)).\n- **What constrains him.** A corporate venture arm with no IC but also no lead rights, plus a disclaimer on every memo that the view \"does not reflect the vision or strategy\" of other Samsung units ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-perplexity)). Follow-ons depend on a Samsung commercial angle ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/)).\n- **What he'd never do.** Lead a round or set terms; no Duong deal in the repo is a lead.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Promotion is only visible in blog sign-offs.** Every memo from Apr to Sep 2024 ends \"Andy Duong is an investor at Samsung Next\" ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-shabodi)). The Aug 2025 Kite memo says \"Andy Duong is an investment director at Samsung Next\" ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-kite)). So he stepped up between Sep 2024 and Aug 2025. No announcement found.\n- **One thesis, three deals in five months.** Unify: \"automatically bridging the gap between different frameworks via a single line of code\" ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-unify)). Shabodi: \"a unified, simplified API framework that abstracts the complexity\" ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-shabodi)).\n- **The Kite AI re-up rode a pivot, not a fresh AI bet.** Samsung Next backed web3 data startup Zettablock about two years before the Kite Series A: \"Nearly two years ago, we invested in Zettablock\" ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-kite)). Duong says the end user turned out to be agents: \"ultimate user of this verifiable data wouldn't just be human developers, but autonomous agents\" (same source).\n- **Perplexity was a device-strategy option.** The May 2024 memo calls it \"a compelling opportunity for leaders in consumer electronics to diversify their search solutions\" ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-perplexity)). Samsung–Perplexity device talks were reported about a year later (see the samsung-next firm file).\n- **Odyssey: late in, not visibly doubled down.** He gave a technical reason, not a device reason: \"We were impressed with the rapid technical advances demonstrated by Odyssey-2 Pro\" ([src](https://pulse2.com/odyssey-ai-world-model-startup-secures-investment-from-nventures-and-samsung-next/), 2026-02-17). That came about two years after Air Street's late-2023 first check and the GV-led $9M seed in Jul 2024 (`data/rounds.csv`, [src](https://www.fenwick.com/insights/experience/fenwick-represents-odyssey-systems-in-9m-seed-funding)). Four months later Natural Capital led a $310M Series B at $1.45B, and Samsung Next is not named in that release ([src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026061701/odyssey-raises-310-million-to-accelerate-world-simulation/)). Not being named does not prove they didn't take part.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He sits close to frontier AI. Odyssey is a world-model lab, and Unify, Expanso and Kite are AI infrastructure. But he can't lead, his checks are strategic slices, and his book includes non-AI telco (Shabodi) and web3. He is useful as a **co-investor and a route into Samsung distribution** for a lab or AI-infrastructure company I back, not as a source of lead-able seed deals. The deals that matter to me are Odyssey (Strategic, Feb 2026) and Unify (2024). Seniority is set to `principal` because \"Investment Director\" is not an enum value; this is a mapping, not his title. Check capacity is set to `10m-plus` because the firm writes up to $25M per company, but it never leads.\n\n## Background\nNothing found beyond Samsung Next. I checked the Samsung Next team page, which shows initials only and an empty LinkedIn anchor ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/team)), and the blog author bylines in the site's data file ([src](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/69ab197ca148d582724c4ac3/6a6eb2e05b71437b805448fb_blog-posts.json)). Web search was unavailable this run (budget exhausted), so his education, prior employers, start date at Samsung Next and pronouns are unconfirmed. The earliest dated evidence is his Apr 2024 Unify byline. Samsung Next's Zettablock investment (~2023) may predate it, but it isn't attributed to him.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.samsungnext.com/team | Listed as Investment Director; no photo or bio (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.samsungnext.com/blog | 5 \"Why we invested\" bylines, Apr 2024 to Aug 2025 |\n| X | nothing found | not verified |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | team-page LinkedIn link is empty; not verified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was unavailable this run.\n\n## How they think\n- **Fragmentation is the opportunity.** All four 2024 memos open with a fragmentation problem (ML frameworks, edge data, telco APIs, LLM retrieval) and back the unifying layer ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-unify), [src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-expanso)).\n- **Founder screen is pedigree plus open-source traction.** He cites Ivy's 14k GitHub stars for Lenton and Kubeflow and Bacalhau for Aronchick ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-unify), [src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-expanso)).\n- **Calls.** Kite's agent-payments pivot validated holding Zettablock. Perplexity's valuation rose sharply after his 2024 entry (`data/rounds.csv`). Odyssey marked up to $1.45B four months after his entry ([src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026061701/odyssey-raises-310-million-to-accelerate-world-simulation/)).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Why we invested in Perplexity](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-perplexity) (May 2024): the device-maker search thesis.\n- [Why we invested in Kite](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-kite) (Aug 2025): how a web3 bet became an agent-infrastructure bet.\n- [Why we invested in Unify](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-unify) (Apr 2024): the fragmentation and abstraction thesis.\n- [Odyssey release quote](https://pulse2.com/odyssey-ai-world-model-startup-secures-investment-from-nventures-and-samsung-next/) (Feb 2026): his world-model rationale.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X or LinkedIn account was verified.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Zettablock (now Kite AI) | Undisclosed (~2023 entry; attribution to Duong unconfirmed) | ~2023 | no | unconfirmed | [src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-kite) |\n| Unify (LLM router, London) | Undisclosed | 2024-04 | no | YC, A.Capital, SignalFire, M12, Essence VC listed as backers (rounds not given) | [src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-unify), [src](https://unify.ai/) |\n| Expanso | Undisclosed (round unconfirmed) | 2024-05 | no | unconfirmed | [src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-expanso), [src](https://www.expanso.io/news/venturebeat-samsung-expanso-distributed-processing/) |\n| Perplexity | Undisclosed (likely 2024 Series B/B-1) | 2024-05 | no | unconfirmed | [src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-perplexity) |\n| Shabodi | Undisclosed | 2024-09 | no | unconfirmed | [src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-shabodi) |\n| Kite AI | Series A ($18M; total $33M), re-up | 2025-09-02 | no | General Catalyst, PayPal Ventures (co-leads) | [src](https://fortune.com/crypto/2025/09/02/kite-ai-blockchain-paypal-general-catalyst-18-million/) |\n| Odyssey AI | Strategic (NVentures, Samsung Next) | 2026-02-17 | no | NVentures | [src](https://pulse2.com/odyssey-ai-world-model-startup-secures-investment-from-nventures-and-samsung-next/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Kite AI.** Samsung Next first backed Zettablock (~2023) for web3 data infrastructure. The company renamed itself Kite and pivoted to identity and payments for agents. Samsung Next then re-upped in the $18M Series A co-led by General Catalyst and PayPal Ventures ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-kite)). This is his one clear double-down.\n- **Odyssey.** NVentures and Samsung Next came in together as strategics in Feb 2026, after GV, EQT and Air Street ([src](https://pulse2.com/odyssey-ai-world-model-startup-secures-investment-from-nventures-and-samsung-next/)). The intro path is unknown.\n- **Perplexity.** No round or intro path is disclosed. The memo reads as a commercial scouting thesis ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-perplexity)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Samsung Next | at: Investment Director (investor title earlier) | by 2024-04 | [src](https://www.samsungnext.com/team), [src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-unify) |\n| Raymond Liao | Samsung Next Managing Director, senior to him | unconfirmed | [src](https://www.samsungnext.com/team) |\n| Chi Zhang (Kite AI) | founder he re-upped on | ~2023 | [src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-kite) |\n| Daniel Lenton (Unify) | founder he backed | 2024-04 | [src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-unify) |\n| David Aronchick (Expanso) | founder he backed | 2024-05 | [src](https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-expanso) |\n| NVentures | co-strategic on Odyssey | 2026-02 | [src](https://pulse2.com/odyssey-ai-world-model-startup-secures-investment-from-nventures-and-samsung-next/) |\n\nLP links: none. He isn't known to be an LP anywhere, and Samsung Next has no outside LPs (EDGAR checked in `firms/samsung-next.md`). No `-lp.csv` was written.\n\n## Open questions\n- What did he do before Samsung Next, when did he start, and where did he study? (LinkedIn and search were not available.)\n- Did Samsung Next take part in Odyssey's $310M Series B, or in Perplexity's later rounds?\n- Who sourced Zettablock? Was it Duong?\n- Which Perplexity round did Samsung Next enter (B or B-1, 2024)?\n- Does Samsung Next have AI deals where Duong is involved but not bylined?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.samsungnext.com/team\n- https://www.samsungnext.com/\n- https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/69ab197ca148d582724c4ac3/6a6eb2e05b71437b805448fb_blog-posts.json\n- https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-perplexity\n- https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-unify\n- https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-expanso\n- https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-shabodi\n- https://www.samsungnext.com/blog?post=why-we-invested-in-kite\n- https://pulse2.com/odyssey-ai-world-model-startup-secures-investment-from-nventures-and-samsung-next/\n- https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026061701/odyssey-raises-310-million-to-accelerate-world-simulation/\n- https://www.fenwick.com/insights/experience/fenwick-represents-odyssey-systems-in-9m-seed-funding\n- https://fortune.com/crypto/2025/09/02/kite-ai-blockchain-paypal-general-catalyst-18-million/\n- https://unify.ai/\n- https://www.expanso.io/news/venturebeat-samsung-expanso-distributed-processing/\n- https://shabodi.com/news-and-announcements/\n", "person:andy-konwinski": "\n# Andy Konwinski\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (strategics-2 sweep), from the Databricks Ventures profile. The owner asked who the people around Databricks Ventures are. Konwinski is the founder-network node next to DV, not a DV employee (see \"Databricks Ventures: no confirmed role\").*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the best-connected private funder of Berkeley open-source AI research. He runs a $100M nonprofit that funds the projects and a $150M seed fund that backs the spinouts, which makes him a better route into Sky-lab inference and eval seeds than Databricks Ventures itself.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he is a founder-investor with two decacorns (Databricks, Perplexity) and a billionaire per Bloomberg ([src](https://news.bgov.com/tech-and-telecom-law/shunned-jehovahs-witness-becomes-a-billionaire-ai-evangelist), 2025-12-03). His LPs are researchers: Laude Fund I has \"over 50 leading computer scientists\", including Dave Patterson and Jeff Dean ([src](https://andykonwinski.com/2024/12/10/introducing-laude-ventures.html)). His nonprofit, Laude Institute, funds open research (Slingshots, Moonshots, the K Prize, Terminal-Bench) before any company exists ([src](https://www.laude.org/updates/hello-world), 2025-06-23). So he sees Berkeley and Stanford research years before a priced round.\n- **What constrains him:** the fund is small. It is $150M targeting about 25 companies ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/university/us-and-canada/databricks-cofounder-150m-spinout-fund-uc-berkeley/), 2024-12-11). In the big rounds his portfolio raises (Inferact's $150M seed, Arena's $100M seed and $150M A), Laude is a participant, not the price-setter ([Inferact](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference), [Arena](https://arena.ai/blog/series-a/)). He leads only small seeds (Voio $8.6M, Tensormesh $4.5M, Arcade $12M).\n- **What he'd never do (inferred from his writing):** back a closed, power-concentrating frontier lab as a mission bet. His 2026-07-02 essay is titled \"Concentration of power in AI is a risk, not a solution\" ([src](https://andykonwinski.com/2026/07/02/concentration-of-power-in-ai-is-a-risk-not-a-solution.html)). At Open Frontier he said frontier resources are \"being pulled back behind closed doors\" ([src](https://www.groundlevel-ai.com/p/top-ai-researchers-argue-us-leadership), 2026-07-01).\n- **Division of labour at Laude (observed from bylines, not stated):** Pete Sonsini fronts the enterprise-infra deals (Arcade, Tensormesh). Konwinski bylines the research and frontier-model deals (Voio; Arena, co-written with Sonsini). See Portfolio.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Perplexity came out of a researcher-LP fund, not a pitch.** Before Laude he and Andrew Krioukov ran CS Grad Ventures (CSGV), which took money from and invested in CS PhDs and professors. A CSGV LP (a Meta AI researcher) introduced him to Aravind Srinivas, and another CSGV LP, a Berkeley PhD, became Perplexity's founding engineer. CSGV's vision: \"democratize Venture Capital back to the researchers themselves\" ([src](https://andykonwinski.com/2024/12/10/introducing-laude-ventures.html), 2024-12-10). The LP base is the sourcing network.\n- **Laude has two bodies: a nonprofit upstream of a fund.** Laude Institute is a $100M personal pledge (\"I've committed $100 million to get it off the ground\") that gives fast grants and multi-year labs to researchers ([src](https://www.laude.org/updates/hello-world), 2025-06-23). Laude Ventures invests in research spinouts. The overlap shows in the portfolio: Arena (LMArena) is a Berkeley research project turned company, and Tensormesh commercializes LMCache ([src](https://laude.vc/news)). laude.org does **not** say grantees get priority with the fund, so treat the grant-to-seed pipeline as an observed overlap, not policy (**unconfirmed**). A Latent Space episode (2025-12-31) reportedly says Slingshots funded DSPy, Terminal-Bench and LMArena; I only saw a search summary of it, so this is **unconfirmed**.\n- **His target is \"the next Databricks\", not app companies.** The Fund I post: \"To succeed we would need to find the next Databricks.\" ([src](https://andykonwinski.com/2024/12/10/introducing-laude-ventures.html)).\n- **Laude's Arena thesis leans on Ion Stoica.** The Arena post (Sonsini and Konwinski, 2025-05-21) says Laude has \"built companies with Ion Stoica before\" ([src](https://laude.vc/news/lmarena-turning-research-driven-evaluation-into-ai-infrastructure)). Stoica is a Databricks co-founder and an Inferact and Arena founder. So Laude, DV and Stoica-led spinouts are one family.\n- **Re-up pattern:** Laude was in Arena's $100M seed (2025-05) and again in its $150M Series A (2026-01-06) ([seed](https://laude.vc/news/lmarena-turning-research-driven-evaluation-into-ai-infrastructure), [A](https://arena.ai/blog/series-a/)).\n- **The frontier-healthcare-model bet is his.** He wrote the Voio post: \"Imagine what happens when AI does for radiologists what it has done for software engineers.\" ([src](https://laude.vc/news/voio-building-frontier-ai-for-healthcare), 2025-11-20). Laude and The House Fund co-led Voio's $8.6M seed ([src](https://hitconsultant.net/2025/11/24/voio-emerges-from-stealth-with-8-6m-to-launch-worlds-most-accurate-medical-imaging-model/)).\n\n## Databricks Ventures: no confirmed role\n- One aggregator (MergerLinks) lists him as a Databricks Ventures \"Vice President\". This is **unconfirmed**. I checked his own about page ([src](https://andykonwinski.com/about/)), Wikipedia ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andy_Konwinski)), TIME100 AI 2026 ([src](https://time.com/collection/time100-ai/2026/andy-konwinski/)), the Laude team pages ([laude.vc](https://laude.vc/team), [laude.org](https://www.laude.org/)) and a web search for \"Andy Konwinski\" with \"Databricks Ventures\". None names a DV role. His own page gives only past Databricks titles (\"VP Product AI/ML\"). Wikipedia says he stepped back from day-to-day operations in 2019. So no `at:` row to DV was written.\n- **How he ties to DV:** Laude Ventures is on the Databricks VC Network list that DV's AI Accelerator introduces companies to ([DV release](https://www.databricks.com/company/newsroom/press-releases/databricks-launches-ai-accelerator-program-scale-next-generation-ai)). Laude and DV are both in Inferact's seed (2026-01) ([src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference)). Pete Sonsini (Laude GP) led Perplexity's Series A at NEA, in which DV also invested ([src](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/announcing-our-series-a-funding-round-and-mobile-app-launch)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He sits upstream of the Berkeley/Stanford open-source AI pipeline (vLLM/Inferact, LMArena, LMCache/Tensormesh, Terminal-Bench, Voio's Pillar-0). These are exactly the research-founder seeds that later become $100M+ seed rounds. For a ~$20M check into big seeds, he is a **co-investor and sourcing relationship**, not a price-setter: Laude follows a16z, Lightspeed and Felicis in the large rounds. He is also a possible **LP-network door**, since Laude's LP base is 50+ top computer scientists. Caveat: his open-frontier politics favour open-weight and academic projects. A closed frontier lab may not get his enthusiasm.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up a Jehovah's Witness in rural Wisconsin and later left ([TIME](https://time.com/collection/time100-ai/2026/andy-konwinski/)). B.S. UW–Madison. M.S. and Ph.D. UC Berkeley (advisor Randy Katz; thesis 2012 on multi-agent cluster scheduling); co-author of Mesos (NSDI 2011) ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andy_Konwinski)).\n- 2013: co-founded Databricks with Matei Zaharia, Ion Stoica, Ali Ghodsi and others. Early role was product-manager-like (community, conferences, deployments). Later \"VP Product AI/ML\". Stepped back from day-to-day in 2019 ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andy_Konwinski), [about](https://andykonwinski.com/about/)).\n- ~2021 (date from MarketScreener, a data site, **unconfirmed**): co-founded CS Grad Ventures with Andrew Krioukov ([src](https://andykonwinski.com/2024/12/10/introducing-laude-ventures.html)).\n- 2022: co-founder, early investor and founding President of Perplexity ([about](https://andykonwinski.com/about/)).\n- 2024-12: K Prize ($1M, contamination-free SWE-bench) announced at NeurIPS ([src](https://andykonwinski.com/2024/12/12/konwinski-prize.html)). Laude Ventures Fund I announced 2024-12-10 ([src](https://andykonwinski.com/2024/12/10/introducing-laude-ventures.html)).\n- 2025-06-23: launched Laude Institute with a $100M pledge. The board is Dave Patterson (chair), Jeff Dean and Joelle Pineau ([src](https://www.laude.org/updates/hello-world), [laude.org](https://www.laude.org/)).\n- Co-teaches Berkeley's PhD seminar \"Research to Startups\" ([about](https://andykonwinski.com/about/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://andykonwinski.com/ | Blog. Fund I post, K Prize post, 2026-07-02 power-concentration essay |\n| Substack / blog | https://andykonwinski.com/ | Same as above |\n| X | https://twitter.com/andykonwinski | Linked from his about page. Follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/andykon/ | Surfaced in search: \"Co-founder of Laude, Databricks, and...\" |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/andyk | Linked from his about page |\n| Podcasts (host) | Laudecast (Laude Institute) | NeurIPS 2025 interview series. YouTube: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rePCdzfITKc |\n| Firm | https://laude.vc/team | hello@laude.vc (firm contact on site) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-12 — Laudecast at NeurIPS 2025 — \"Open Frontier AI with Andy Konwinski\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rePCdzfITKc — not transcribed here.\n- 2025-12-31 — Latent Space — episode reportedly titled \"Beyond NSF, Slingshots, Open Frontiers\" — URL not confirmed; seen only as a search summary.\n- 2026-06-30 — Open Frontier (Laude Institute, San Francisco) — convener; attendees included Ion Stoica, Percy Liang, Nathan Lambert and Bryan Catanzaro — [src](https://www.groundlevel-ai.com/p/top-ai-researchers-argue-us-leadership).\n- 2025-05 — UC Berkeley CDSS commencement speaker — [src](https://cdss.berkeley.edu/news/andy-konwinski-speak-cdss-undergraduate-commencement-ceremonies-may-22).\n\n## How they think\n- Open frontier as a public good. \"The frontier will not close on its own\" ([TIME](https://time.com/collection/time100-ai/2026/andy-konwinski/)). He also says open science \"needs a heavyweight contender in the ring\" (same).\n- Research must ship. Laude's motto: ideas matter \"only if you ship them\" ([src](https://www.laude.org/updates/hello-world)).\n- Benchmarks as infrastructure: the K Prize (contamination-free SWE-bench), Terminal-Bench, and backing Arena.\n- Contrarian on safety: \"Safety is not secrecy. Safety is error correction.\" ([essay](https://andykonwinski.com/2026/07/02/concentration-of-power-in-ai-is-a-risk-not-a-solution.html)).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Introducing Laude Ventures](https://andykonwinski.com/2024/12/10/introducing-laude-ventures.html): the CSGV-to-Laude story and how Perplexity happened.\n- [Hello, world (Laude Institute)](https://www.laude.org/updates/hello-world): the $100M pledge and the Slingshots/Moonshots design.\n- [Concentration of power in AI is a risk, not a solution](https://andykonwinski.com/2026/07/02/concentration-of-power-in-ai-is-a-risk-not-a-solution.html): his politics on frontier labs.\n- [Why I built the Konwinski Prize](https://andykonwinski.com/2024/12/12/konwinski-prize.html): the benchmark-contamination view.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot analysed in this pass. His X handle is linked from his site. Laude's public posts amplify the Slingshots cohorts and Laudecast guests (Jeff Dean, Yoshua Bengio, Yejin Choi, Ion Stoica, per a LinkedIn summary seen in search; **unconfirmed** detail).\n\n## Portfolio\nDeals where **he is personally named**:\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Perplexity | Pre-seed / founding (he is co-founder and \"one of the first investors\") | 2022 | n/a (co-founder) | vehicle unconfirmed (CSGV likely) | [about](https://andykonwinski.com/about/) |\n| Arena (LMArena) | Seed ($100M) | 2025-05-21 | no (a16z and UC Investments led) | Lightspeed, Felicis, Kleiner Perkins, The House Fund | [Laude post, Sonsini and Konwinski](https://laude.vc/news/lmarena-turning-research-driven-evaluation-into-ai-infrastructure) |\n| Voio | Seed ($8.6M) | 2025-11-20 | co-lead (Laude with The House Fund) | The House Fund | [Laude post, Konwinski byline](https://laude.vc/news/voio-building-frontier-ai-for-healthcare), [HIT Consultant](https://hitconsultant.net/2025/11/24/voio-emerges-from-stealth-with-8-6m-to-launch-worlds-most-accurate-medical-imaging-model/) |\n\n**Laude Ventures firm book (not personally attributed to him):** Arcade seed $12M 2025-03 (Laude led; Sonsini sourced it, [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/18/arcade-raises-12m-from-perplexity-co-founders-new-fund-to-make-ai-agents-less-awful/)); Tensormesh seed $4.5M 2025-10 (Laude led; Sonsini quoted, [src](https://justainews.com/companies/funding-news/tensormesh-raises-4-5m-to-cut-ai-inference-costs-by-10x/)); Arena Series A $150M 2026-01 (re-up, [src](https://arena.ai/blog/series-a/)); Inferact seed $150M at $800M 2026-01 (participant next to DV, [src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference)). The full list on [laude.vc/portfolio](https://laude.vc/portfolio) has 19 named companies plus 4 stealth: Arcade, Arena, Clairvoyant Intelligence, Disarray, Eventual, Inferact, Intelligible, Invocate, Mithril, Narada, Normal, Perplexity, Prompt AI, SigIQ, Sooth Labs, Tabsdata, Tensormesh, Terrace Networks, VOIO. Two of the four stealth entries are tagged \"Frontier Models\".\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Perplexity (2022):** a CSGV LP who was a Meta AI researcher introduced Konwinski to Aravind Srinivas. Konwinski had co-taught a Berkeley startup class with Srinivas's PhD advisor. He invested, then co-founded the company. Another CSGV LP became the founding engineer ([src](https://andykonwinski.com/2024/12/10/introducing-laude-ventures.html)).\n- **Arcade (2025-03):** Sonsini had backed founder Alex Salazar's earlier company, Stormpath, and reached out when Salazar started again. It was Laude's \"first publicly announced\" check ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/18/arcade-raises-12m-from-perplexity-co-founders-new-fund-to-make-ai-agents-less-awful/)). Konwinski is not named on it.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Laude Ventures | Co-founder and GP | 2024-12 | [laude.vc/team](https://laude.vc/team) |\n| Laude Institute | Co-founder, board, $100M pledge | 2025-06-23 | [src](https://www.laude.org/updates/hello-world) |\n| CS Grad Ventures | Co-founder (with Andrew Krioukov) | ~2021 (unconfirmed) | [src](https://andykonwinski.com/2024/12/10/introducing-laude-ventures.html) |\n| Databricks | Co-founder; later VP Product AI/ML | 2013 | [about](https://andykonwinski.com/about/) |\n| Perplexity | Co-founder, early investor, founding President | 2022 | [about](https://andykonwinski.com/about/) |\n| Pete Sonsini | Laude co-founder and GP; previously backed him at Databricks (NEA) | 2024 | [src](https://andykonwinski.com/2024/12/10/introducing-laude-ventures.html) |\n| Andrew Krioukov | CSGV and Laude co-founder | ~2021 | [src](https://andykonwinski.com/2024/12/10/introducing-laude-ventures.html) |\n| Aravind Srinivas | Perplexity co-founder; Laude Fund I LP | 2022 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/university/us-and-canada/databricks-cofounder-150m-spinout-fund-uc-berkeley/) |\n| Jeff Dean, Dave Patterson | Laude Fund I LPs; Laude Institute board | 2024 | [src](https://andykonwinski.com/2024/12/10/introducing-laude-ventures.html) |\n| Ion Stoica | Databricks co-founder; founder of Inferact and Arena, both Laude portfolio companies | 2013 | [src](https://laude.vc/news/lmarena-turning-research-driven-evaluation-into-ai-infrastructure) |\n| Databricks Ventures | Laude is in the DV VC Network; co-investor in Inferact. **No confirmed DV role** | 2025-09 | [src](https://www.databricks.com/company/newsroom/press-releases/databricks-launches-ai-accelerator-program-scale-next-generation-ai) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Does the MergerLinks DV \"Vice President\" listing reflect a real current role, or a stale Databricks title? Check his LinkedIn directly.\n- Laude check sizes, and whether there is a Fund II (Fund I targets 25 companies, and 23 are already listed).\n- Which Slingshot or Moonshot grantees later took Laude Ventures money (the Latent Space episode is the lead). Which Moonshots got the $10M follow-on announced in October 2026 ([TIME](https://time.com/collection/time100-ai/2026/andy-konwinski/)).\n- The two stealth \"Frontier Models\" companies in the Laude portfolio.\n- Is he an LP or angel in other funds, or a personal angel in Inferact or RadixArk?\n\n## Sources\n- https://andykonwinski.com/about/\n- https://andykonwinski.com/2024/12/10/introducing-laude-ventures.html\n- https://andykonwinski.com/2024/12/12/konwinski-prize.html\n- https://andykonwinski.com/2026/07/02/concentration-of-power-in-ai-is-a-risk-not-a-solution.html\n- https://www.laude.org/ ; https://www.laude.org/updates/hello-world\n- https://laude.vc/team ; https://laude.vc/portfolio ; https://laude.vc/news\n- https://laude.vc/news/lmarena-turning-research-driven-evaluation-into-ai-infrastructure\n- https://laude.vc/news/voio-building-frontier-ai-for-healthcare\n- https://globalventuring.com/university/us-and-canada/databricks-cofounder-150m-spinout-fund-uc-berkeley/\n- https://time.com/collection/time100-ai/2026/andy-konwinski/\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andy_Konwinski\n- https://news.bgov.com/tech-and-telecom-law/shunned-jehovahs-witness-becomes-a-billionaire-ai-evangelist\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/18/arcade-raises-12m-from-perplexity-co-founders-new-fund-to-make-ai-agents-less-awful/\n- https://justainews.com/companies/funding-news/tensormesh-raises-4-5m-to-cut-ai-inference-costs-by-10x/\n- https://hitconsultant.net/2025/11/24/voio-emerges-from-stealth-with-8-6m-to-launch-worlds-most-accurate-medical-imaging-model/\n- https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference\n- https://arena.ai/blog/series-a/\n- https://www.groundlevel-ai.com/p/top-ai-researchers-argue-us-leadership\n- https://www.databricks.com/company/newsroom/press-releases/databricks-launches-ai-accelerator-program-scale-next-generation-ai\n", "person:anjney-midha": "\n# Anjney Midha\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the single most repeated lead name on new frontier-lab rounds in this repo (Mistral A, Periodic seed, River seed+A), and since late 2025 he does it from his own vehicle, AMP PBC, while a16z still lists him as a Venture Partner.\n\n## Crux\n- **Correction to the task's premise.** He is not simply \"a16z Venture Partner, still on the team page.\" The a16z author page does still list him as Venture Partner, Infra ([src](https://a16z.com/author/anjney-midha/), checked 2026-11-10). But press calls him a *former* a16z general partner who founded AMP PBC in 2026 ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/), 2026-08-11; [Forbes AU](https://www.forbes.com.au/news/innovation/former-openai-researcher-to-raise-500-million-for-ai-science-startup/), 2026-05-14). a16z's own April 2026 podcast page introduces him as \"founder of AMP PBC\" while keeping the Venture Partner bio ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/ben-horowitz-on-venture-capital-and-ai/), 2026-04-27). AMP was already co-leading rounds by Dec 2025 (Black Forest Labs Series B, [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier)). Treat him as an AMP GP with a residual a16z affiliation whose formal terms are not public.\n- **Why he wins.** His deals come from relationships that predate the company, not from process: his own Ubiquity6 co-founder at Sesame, a college friend at OpenRouter, mutual friends plus a hands-on stint in a Stanford physics lab before Periodic (sources below). Founders then take him with them: the same person led Periodic Labs and Black Forest Labs first at a16z, then again via AMP.\n- **What constrains him.** AMP's fund size, LPs and structure are undisclosed; at River he co-led alongside General Catalyst rather than alone, and Forbes' first reporting on River named only GC ([src](https://dealroom.co/news/128971-xai-co-founder-babuschkin-in-talks-to-raise-up-to-1b-for-new-ai-research/)). On Mistral he was second in after Lightspeed. Board load is heavy (seven boards on the a16z bio).\n- **What he would never do (inferred from his writing, not a stated rule).** Back a closed, proprietary-only lab thesis without an open or research-first angle: his a16z posts on Mistral, Black Forest Labs and LMArena all argue for openness or reliability infrastructure ([Mistral](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/); [BFL](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/)). The AMP checks into Anthropic G and H cut against this, so hold it loosely.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Same partner, two vehicles, same companies.** Periodic Labs: a16z led the $300M founding round with him writing \"we are thrilled to lead their $300M founding round\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-periodic-labs/), 2025-09-30); Forbes then reported a round \"led by AMP\" at a reported $7.5B ([src](https://www.forbes.com.au/news/innovation/former-openai-researcher-to-raise-500-million-for-ai-science-startup/), 2026-05-14). Black Forest Labs: a16z seed/Series A, then AMP co-led the $300M Series B, \"led by AMP and Salesforce Ventures\" ([src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier), 2025-12-01). This is the pattern to watch: founder loyalty is to him, not the a16z brand.\n- **AMP was live months before press called it a 2026 firm.** The BFL Series B release (2025-12-01) already quotes him as AMP, and AMP appears as a named investor in Anthropic's Series G (\"Altimeter, AMP PBC, Appaloosa LP\", [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-30-billion-series-g-funding-380-billion-post-money-valuation), 2026-02-12) and Series H (\"Significant investors in this round include AMP PBC\", [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h), 2026-05-28). Getting into both Anthropic rounds as a brand-new vehicle implies large LP backing (my inference; LPs unsourced).\n- **Sesame was co-incubated, not sourced.** He writes \"I've known the founding team for a while\" and describes \"months of our dining table working sessions\" before Sesame existed ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sesame-ai/), 2025-02-27). Co-founder Ankit Kumar is his own Ubiquity6 co-founder; Brendan Iribe was an angel in Ubiquity6 (same source).\n- **OpenRouter was a college friendship.** He and Alex Atallah \"became friends as undergrads over a decade ago\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-openrouter/), 2025-06-26).\n- **Periodic conviction came from doing the science.** He tested frontier models on condensed-matter data \"while working as a visiting scientist at Stanford's Department of Applied Physics\", then was introduced to Fedus and Cubuk \"by mutual friends\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-periodic-labs/), 2025-09-30).\n- **Mistral thesis was structural, rooted in Discord.** A digest of his TBPN interview quotes \"The bet for Mistral was very simple\" and attributes his conviction to Discord's data-residency constraints and Llama's authors leaving Meta ([digest, not transcript](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-05-14/anjney-midha-on-why-he-backed-mistrals-200m-series-a-sesame-ais-companion-hardware-bet-and-the-post-training-era), 2025-05-14). The same digest says he was an early angel in Anthropic (summary only, low confidence).\n- **Rajko Radovanovic is the quiet constant.** Co-bylined with him on Mistral, Luma and BFL ([Mistral](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/); [Luma](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-luma-ai/); [BFL](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/)). Whether Radovanovic moved to AMP is unknown.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He leads exactly the round type the owner wants to be in: very large founding rounds for researcher-led labs (Periodic $300M founding; River $1.1B seed+A co-lead). He is the gatekeeper on allocation in those rounds, so he is a co-invest target rather than a competitor. AMP is new and unbranded, which may make it more open to a $20M co-investor than a16z is. Deals that matter: Periodic Labs, River AI, Black Forest Labs, Mistral, Sesame. Caveat: AMP's appetite for outside co-investors and its ownership terms are unknown.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford undergraduate and graduate degrees ([a16z bio](https://a16z.com/author/anjney-midha/)); dates not given.\n- Partner at Kleiner Perkins, where the bio says he led the firm's first dedicated seed fund ([src](https://a16z.com/author/anjney-midha/)); dates not sourced.\n- Co-founder and CEO of Ubiquity6, backed by Brendan Iribe as angel ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sesame-ai/)); merged into Discord in 2021 ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/how-discord-became-a-developer-platform/)).\n- VP of Platform Ecosystem at Discord, overseeing developer products ([src](https://a16z.com/author/anjney-midha/)).\n- Joined a16z in 2023 ([src](https://a16z.com/author/anjney-midha/)). Title conflict: bio says \"Venture Partner\"; a16z podcast intros call him \"General Partner\" ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/how-discord-became-a-developer-platform/), 2024-05-10; [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/building-an-ai-physicist-chatgpt-co-creators-next-venture/), 2025-10-01); press calls him a former a16z general partner.\n- Founded AMP PBC; TechCrunch says 2026 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/)), but AMP co-led BFL's Series B on 2025-12-01.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/anjney-midha/ | a16z author page, still live 2026-11-10; no AMP website found |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing confirmed (web search budget exhausted this run) | |\n| LinkedIn | nothing confirmed | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | a16z podcast feed | Hosts many a16z AI episodes (Periodic, OpenAI, DeepMind Genie 3, Rick Rubin) per author page |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-04-27 — a16z podcast — Ben Horowitz on Venture Capital and AI — https://a16z.com/podcast/ben-horowitz-on-venture-capital-and-ai/ — introduced as \"founder of AMP PBC\"; topic is firm design and network effects. No transcript on page.\n- 2025-10-01 — a16z podcast — Building an AI Physicist (Fedus, Cubuk) — https://a16z.com/podcast/building-an-ai-physicist-chatgpt-co-creators-next-venture/ — host; Periodic board member.\n- 2025-05-14 — TBPN — Mistral, Sesame, post-training — https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-05-14/anjney-midha-on-why-he-backed-mistrals-200m-series-a-sesame-ais-companion-hardware-bet-and-the-post-training-era — digest says \"progress in AI is coming from post-training\"; digest, not transcript.\n- 2024-05-10 — a16z podcast — How Discord Became a Developer Platform — https://a16z.com/podcast/how-discord-became-a-developer-platform/\n- Others on the author page (undated there): Jensen Huang and Arthur Mensch on the global AI race; Sovereign AI; SB 1047 Q&A; Improving AI with Dario Amodei ([src](https://a16z.com/author/anjney-midha/)).\n\n## How they think\n- Open-source and open-weight models as the path to trusted AI (Mistral post: \"most promising path to achieve robust, widely adopted, and trusted AI systems\", [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/)).\n- Sovereign/European AI as a tailwind (TBPN digest; Jensen/Mensch episode).\n- Evals and reliability as infrastructure: LMArena is \"building the infrastructure to make AI as boring as databases\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-lmarena-the-reliability-layer-for-ai/)).\n- AI for science bottlenecked by lab data, which he tested himself at Stanford before Periodic.\n- Policy: vocal against SB 1047 (Q&A on author page).\n- Calls: Mistral A (€2B post) to Series D >€21B post; BFL seed to $3.25B Series B; Periodic $300M founding to reported $7.5B.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Investing in Periodic Labs (2025-09-30) — the Stanford visiting-scientist origin; best example of conviction-by-embedding.\n2. Investing in Sesame AI (2025-02-27) — shows co-incubation with his own co-founder.\n3. Investing in Mistral (2023-12-10) — the open-weights thesis.\n4. Investing in LMArena (2025-05-28) — evals as infrastructure.\n5. State of AI: 100T token study with OpenRouter (2025-12-04, with Malika Aubakirova) — https://a16z.com/state-of-ai/\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing confirmed this run (web search budget exhausted; X not fetched). Open question.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mistral AI (a16z) | Series A, €385M | 2023-12-11 | yes (a16z led) | General Catalyst; Lightspeed | [Latham](https://www.lw.com/en/news/2023/12/Latham%20Advises%20a16z%20on%20Mistral%20AI%20Series%20A%20Funding%20Round); [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/) |\n| Luma AI (a16z) | Series B | 2024-01-09 | yes | not stated | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-luma-ai/) |\n| Black Forest Labs (a16z) | Seed (a16z post) / Series A (company release) | 2024-08-01 | yes for Series A per company | BroadLight; Creandum; Earlybird; General Catalyst; Northzone; Nvidia | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/); [BFL](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier) |\n| Sesame (a16z) | Series A | 2025-02-27 | yes | not stated | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sesame-ai/) |\n| LMArena (a16z) | Seed | 2025-05-28 | not stated (\"founding investors\") | UC Investments | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-lmarena-the-reliability-layer-for-ai/) |\n| OpenRouter (a16z) | Round not named | 2025-06-26 | yes | not stated | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-openrouter/) |\n| Periodic Labs (a16z) | Founding round, $300M | 2025-09-30 | yes | Felicis; DST; NVentures; Accel (per repo rows) | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-periodic-labs/) |\n| Black Forest Labs (AMP) | Series B, $300M, $3.25B post | 2025-12-01 | co-lead | Salesforce Ventures (co-lead) | [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier) |\n| Anthropic (AMP) | Series G, $30B | 2026-02-12 | no | GIC; Coatue (leads) | [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-30-billion-series-g-funding-380-billion-post-money-valuation) |\n| Periodic Labs (AMP) | Reported $500M+ at $7.5B | 2026-05 (talks) | yes (reported) | undisclosed | [src](https://www.forbes.com.au/news/innovation/former-openai-researcher-to-raise-500-million-for-ai-science-startup/) |\n| Anthropic (AMP) | Series H, $65B | 2026-05-28 | no | Altimeter; Dragoneer; Greenoaks; Sequoia et al. | [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h) |\n| River AI (AMP) | Seed + Series A, $1.1B | 2026-08-11 | co-lead | General Catalyst (co-lead); Nvidia; AMD Ventures; YC; Temasek | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/) |\n| Mistral AI (a16z follow-ons) | Series B, C, D | 2024-06 to 2026-09 | no | various | see `data/rows/roundmap-mistral-ai-rounds.csv` |\n\n**Were they first?** Mistral: no, Lightspeed led the seed. Periodic: a16z led the founding round, but Felicis made a pre-incorporation commitment (per `people/lisha-li.md` context), so not strictly first. River: no, GC was reported first. BFL seed, Sesame, OpenRouter: relationship predates the company, so effectively first-in, but round-level timing vs other investors is not sourced.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Sesame:** co-founded by his Ubiquity6 co-founder Ankit Kumar and Ubiquity6 angel Brendan Iribe; he describes months of dining-table sessions before founding, then a16z led the Series A ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sesame-ai/)).\n- **OpenRouter:** college friendship with Alex Atallah; a16z led ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-openrouter/)). Later co-authored the 100T-token study with OpenRouter.\n- **Periodic Labs:** Stanford visiting-scientist work showed models were weak on experimental physics data; mutual friends introduced him to Fedus and Cubuk; a16z led the $300M founding round; AMP reportedly led the next round ([a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-periodic-labs/); [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com.au/news/innovation/former-openai-researcher-to-raise-500-million-for-ai-science-startup/)).\n- **Black Forest Labs:** a16z seed/Series A (2024), board seat; AMP then co-led Series B with Salesforce Ventures. His release quote: \"the rare combination of frontier research and disciplined execution\" ([src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier)).\n- **River AI:** Forbes in May 2026 reported GC in talks to lead; final $1.1B round added AMP as co-lead ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Andreessen Horowitz | Venture Partner, Infra (bio); \"General Partner\" in podcast intros | 2023 | [src](https://a16z.com/author/anjney-midha/) |\n| AMP PBC | Founder | 2025-12 or earlier (first co-lead) | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/) |\n| Ankit Kumar | Ubiquity6 co-founder; Sesame co-founder | Ubiquity6 era | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sesame-ai/) |\n| Brendan Iribe | Angel in Ubiquity6; Sesame co-founder | Ubiquity6 era | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sesame-ai/) |\n| Alex Atallah | College friend; OpenRouter co-founder | ~2015 (\"over a decade ago\") | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-openrouter/) |\n| Liam Fedus; Ekin Dogus Cubuk | Periodic Labs board | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-periodic-labs/) |\n| Rajko Radovanovic | Co-author on Mistral, Luma, BFL posts | 2023 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/) |\n| Matt Bornstein | Co-author on Mistral post | 2023 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/) |\n| Malika Aubakirova | Co-author, OpenRouter 100T token study | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/state-of-ai/) |\n| General Catalyst | Co-lead on River; follower in Mistral A and BFL A | 2023 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/) |\n| Salesforce Ventures | Co-lead on BFL Series B | 2025-12 | [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier) |\n| Ben Horowitz | Podcast guest interviewing on firm design | 2026-04 | [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/ben-horowitz-on-venture-capital-and-ai/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Has he formally left a16z, or is AMP a sanctioned side vehicle? a16z still lists him; press says \"former\".\n- AMP PBC: fund size, LPs, website, team, launch date. Why a public benefit corporation?\n- Did Rajko Radovanovic or Malika Aubakirova join AMP?\n- Round sizes for Luma B, Sesame A, LMArena seed, OpenRouter (not confirmed this run; search budget exhausted).\n- Periodic AMP round: final size and close date (company has not announced).\n- Is AMP open to $20M co-investors in its lab leads?\n- His X account and posting patterns.\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/anjney-midha/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-periodic-labs/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-luma-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sesame-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-lmarena-the-reliability-layer-for-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-openrouter/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/ben-horowitz-on-venture-capital-and-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/building-an-ai-physicist-chatgpt-co-creators-next-venture/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/how-discord-became-a-developer-platform/\n- https://a16z.com/state-of-ai/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/\n- https://www.forbes.com.au/news/innovation/former-openai-researcher-to-raise-500-million-for-ai-science-startup/\n- https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-30-billion-series-g-funding-380-billion-post-money-valuation\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h\n- https://www.lw.com/en/news/2023/12/Latham%20Advises%20a16z%20on%20Mistral%20AI%20Series%20A%20Funding%20Round\n- https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-05-14/anjney-midha-on-why-he-backed-mistrals-200m-series-a-sesame-ais-companion-hardware-bet-and-the-post-training-era (digest, not transcript)\n- https://dealroom.co/news/128971-xai-co-founder-babuschkin-in-talks-to-raise-up-to-1b-for-new-ai-research/ (aggregator)\n", "person:anthony-saleh": "\n# Anthony Saleh\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (owner pick). Pronouns: WndrCo's own bio uses \"he\" ([2025-07 team page](https://web.archive.org/web/20250701id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team)). Legal name on SEC filings: **Aymen Anthony Saleh** ([Dropbox IRA, EX-4.2](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1467623/000119312518055809/d451946dex42.htm)). A different Anthony Saleh is an executive officer of miRecule, Inc., a Maryland biotech ([Form D 2019-11](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1793053/000179305319000001/primary_doc.xml)). The address and sector don't match, so that filing is excluded here.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe brings music and culture deal flow into WndrCo, an AI-heavy firm. His own named book is consumer, fintech and crypto, and EvenUp is his only AI name. He's worth following as a culture-network gatekeeper, not as an AI investor.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** access. He has managed Nas since about 2007 ([So Money transcript](https://podcast.farnoosh.tv/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/Anthony-Saleh-TRANSCRIPT.pdf)) and runs Emagen (Nas, Kendrick Lamar, Future, Baby Keem per [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team)). With Nas he co-founded Queensbridge, which held pre-IPO stakes in Dropbox, Lyft and Amplitude (SEC exhibits below). That gives him a celebrity and consumer distribution network that tech founders want on their cap table.\n- **What constrains him:** small checks and a narrow sector range. Queensbridge wrote $100K-$500K checks into about 20 companies a year ([CNBC 2016](https://www.nbcnews.com/business/business-news/nas-half-man-half-venture-capitalist-n513286)). At WndrCo he co-runs a seed program of \"25 high-conviction bets each year\" ([wndr.vc/about](https://wndr.vc/about)). The firm's AI book (Harvey, Cursor, ElevenLabs, Abridge, Writer) sits in its *Ventures* growth strategy, not with him (see [firms/wndrco.md](../firms/wndrco.md)).\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** lead a technical frontier-AI or compute round. Nothing in his record touches research labs, chips or infrastructure.\n- **Model:** he's co-head of the seed funnel at an AI-heavy firm while personally carrying a consumer, crypto and culture mandate. ChenLi Wang (Exa, Socket) is the AI and devtools half of that seed team.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Legal name and fund vehicle from a filing.** He signed Dropbox's amended investors' rights agreement, dated 2014-01-30, as \"Aymen Anthony Saleh\", Managing Member of the GP of **Queensbridge Fund I, L.P.** So Queensbridge had a formal fund on Dropbox's pre-IPO cap table more than two years before WndrCo existed. This matters because WndrCo co-founder Sujay Jaswa was Dropbox's business founder and CFO, which is a plausible origin for Saleh's \"day one partner\" seat (inference) ([EX-4.2](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1467623/000119312518055809/d451946dex42.htm)).\n- **Three pre-IPO cap tables confirmed from filings rather than aggregators:** Dropbox (IRA dated 2014-01-30), Lyft (holder of \"Queensbridge Venture Partners, LLC\" on the A&R IRA of 2018-06-27, listed next to Live Nation Entertainment) and Amplitude (A&R IRA of 2021-05-28) ([Lyft EX-4.2](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1759509/000119312519059849/d633517dex42.htm), [Amplitude EX-4.3](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1866692/000119312521260696/d143868dex43.htm)).\n- **Board seat at WndrCo with Ann Daly.** Tinkergarten's Form D for an $18M raise (first sale 2019-03-11) lists Anthony Saleh and Ann Daly, WndrCo's advisory partner and ex-DreamWorks Animation president, as directors. It is one of the few board seats on record for him at WndrCo, and it is ed-tech/consumer ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1690745/000169074519000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **Emagen sits inside Live Nation's corporate tree (inference on the link).** Live Nation's subsidiary lists include \"Emagen WITH, LLC\" (FY2019) and \"Emagen Talent Group, LLC\" (FY2025). Emagen WITH became a guarantor on Live Nation's 2027 notes in October 2025 ([FY2019 Ex-21](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1335258/000133525820000028/lyv-20191231xex211.htm), [FY2025 Ex-21](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1335258/000133525826000009/lyv-20251231xex211subsidia.htm), [indenture](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1335258/000133525826000009/lyv-20251231xex1023xthirds.htm)). He joined C3 Presents as EVP of Management in December 2013 ([XXL](https://www.xxlmag.com/nas-manager-anthony-aymen-saleh-joins-c3-presents/)), and C3 is a Live Nation company. So his music business is probably part of Live Nation rather than independent. Live Nation was also a Lyft holder alongside Queensbridge.\n- **Queensbridge-era rules, from 2016:** checks \"from $100,000 to $500,000 in a company\", about 20 companies a year, and \"much more top-down than bottom-up as investors\" ([CNBC](https://www.nbcnews.com/business/business-news/nas-half-man-half-venture-capitalist-n513286)). Nas's main diligence worry was unethical leadership.\n- **Personal capital stance, 2015 (pre-WndrCo):** \"I don't see a case for holding onto a ton of dry powder\" ([So Money transcript](https://podcast.farnoosh.tv/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/Anthony-Saleh-TRANSCRIPT.pdf)). In the same interview he said Nas taught him to surface bad news early rather than \"string along the bad news\".\n- **\"Day one partner\" isn't in the filings.** The 2017 WndrCo LLC Form D names Katzenberg, Jaswa and Ann Daly as related persons, not Saleh. Later fund Form Ds name Jaswa, Katzenberg and Wang (see [firms/wndrco.md](../firms/wndrco.md)). The claim rests on the firm's own bio, and I couldn't read a 2016-17 team page (the Wix site in the archive renders empty).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** His named deals are consumer, fintech and crypto (Robinhood, Quince, MoonPay, Dapper Labs). The one AI name is EvenUp (vertical AI for personal-injury law), and it is an application company, not a lab, chip or automation-infra bet. Check sizes are sub-$10M seed checks. He has no visible presence in frontier-lab or compute rounds. He's useful to the owner only as (a) a route to celebrity and consumer distribution for a portfolio company, or (b) a co-investor in WndrCo seed rounds that ChenLi Wang or Chonghao Huang source. For AI at WndrCo, those two are the people to follow.\n\n## Background\n- ~2007: began managing Nas. By his own account he wasn't yet 21 when he negotiated his first European tour deal for Nas ([So Money transcript](https://podcast.farnoosh.tv/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/Anthony-Saleh-TRANSCRIPT.pdf)).\n- Executive producer on Nas's *Untitled* (2008) and *Life Is Good* (2012). EVP and GM at Atom Factory ([So Money](https://podcast.farnoosh.tv/episode/anthony-saleh)).\n- 2013-12: EVP of Management at C3 Presents ([XXL](https://www.xxlmag.com/nas-manager-anthony-aymen-saleh-joins-c3-presents/)).\n- ~2014: co-founded Queensbridge Venture Partners with Nas. Managing Member of the GP of Queensbridge Fund I, L.P. by 2014-01-30 ([EX-4.2](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1467623/000119312518055809/d451946dex42.htm)). Founded and runs Emagen Entertainment Group as CEO ([wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team); MarketScreener, an aggregator, dates the CEO role from 2012).\n- 2016: \"day one partner\" at WndrCo (firm bio). General Partner on every archived team page from 2021-12. Co-Head of Seed by 2026-11 ([wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team)).\n- Honours per the firm bio: Forbes 30 Under 30 (Music), Billboard Power 100 ([wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team)).\n- Education: nothing found in primary sources. In 2015 he said his skills were the \"total opposite\" of music (numbers-oriented) ([transcript](https://podcast.farnoosh.tv/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/Anthony-Saleh-TRANSCRIPT.pdf)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://wndr.vc/team | Firm bio, read 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | @dude_br0 | From VC Sheet (an aggregator); not verified |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/anthony-saleh-b231a779/ | Linked from wndr.vc |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2015-10 — So Money (Farnoosh Torabi) — \"Anthony Saleh\" — [episode](https://podcast.farnoosh.tv/episode/anthony-saleh), [transcript PDF](https://podcast.farnoosh.tv/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/Anthony-Saleh-TRANSCRIPT.pdf) — personal finance and his career path with Nas. Keeps no dry powder, delivers bad news early, values \"how you think versus what you know\".\n- 2016-02 — CNBC (Javier E. David) — \"Nas Is Like... Half Man, Half Venture Capitalist\" — [article](https://www.nbcnews.com/business/business-news/nas-half-man-half-venture-capitalist-n513286) — Queensbridge check sizes and its top-down approach.\n- Rap Radar Podcast Ep. 20 (date not confirmed; the page returned 403) — [Podchaser](https://www.podchaser.com/podcasts/rap-radar-podcast-2005/episodes/ep-20-anthony-saleh-74910) — not reviewed.\n- No WndrCo-era podcast or talk found.\n\n## How they think\n- Top-down: market size, product and founding team first, then intangibles such as grit ([CNBC 2016](https://www.nbcnews.com/business/business-news/nas-half-man-half-venture-capitalist-n513286)).\n- Treats founders like artists, with a long-term career-building frame. This comes from VC Sheet's paraphrase (aggregator, low confidence) ([VC Sheet](https://vcsheet.com/who/anthony-saleh)).\n- Calls that look right: early Queensbridge positions in Dropbox, Lyft, Coinbase and Robinhood (the last two per firm bio and press; Robinhood's date is disputed, see Portfolio). The firm credits him with WndrCo's Robinhood and Dapper Labs positions.\n- Calls that look wrong: none documented. Dapper Labs's valuation peaked in 2021 (per the wndr.vc portfolio value field, ~$7.6B), and its later markdowns aren't sourced here.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [wndr.vc/team](https://wndr.vc/team): his current deal list and mandate.\n2. [CNBC 2016](https://www.nbcnews.com/business/business-news/nas-half-man-half-venture-capitalist-n513286): Queensbridge rules of thumb.\n3. [So Money transcript (2015)](https://podcast.farnoosh.tv/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/Anthony-Saleh-TRANSCRIPT.pdf): the only long-form primary source in his own words.\n4. [Dropbox IRA signature page](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1467623/000119312518055809/d451946dex42.htm): the Queensbridge Fund I vehicle.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing reviewed. X could not be fetched, and the handle comes from an aggregator.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Dropbox | Undisclosed (Queensbridge Fund I on A&R IRA) | IRA dated 2014-01-30 | no | — | [EX-4.2](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1467623/000119312518055809/d451946dex42.htm) |\n| Robinhood | Seed (Queensbridge; 2013 per CoinDesk/Benzinga, 2014 per Hustle Fund — conflict) | 2013 or 2014 | no | — | [Hustle Fund](https://www.hustlefund.vc/post/angel-squad-nas-investments-how-hip-hops-best-lyricist-became-one-of-venture-capitals-best-early-betters) (secondary) |\n| Lyft | Undisclosed (Queensbridge VP LLC on A&R IRA) | IRA dated 2018-06-27 | no | Live Nation, Alibaba, Third Point, others | [EX-4.2](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1759509/000119312519059849/d633517dex42.htm) |\n| Amplitude | Undisclosed (Queensbridge VP LLC on A&R IRA) | IRA dated 2021-05-28 | no | Sequoia, Meritech, others | [EX-4.3](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1866692/000119312521260696/d143868dex43.htm) |\n| Tinkergarten | Undisclosed ($18M Form D; Saleh a director) | 2019-03-11 first sale | unknown | Ann Daly also a director | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1690745/000169074519000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Robinhood (WndrCo) | Undisclosed | not disclosed | firm says \"led\" | — | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Quince | Undisclosed (wndr.vc tags Seed and Venture; Jaswa also credited) | not disclosed | firm says \"led\" | — | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| EvenUp | Seed (WNDR seed strategy; stage tag only) | not disclosed | firm says \"led\" | SignalFire, DCM, NFX, Gokul Rajaram named as existing investors (2023-06) | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team), [BetaKit](https://betakit.com/evenup-raises-50-5-million-in-clio-backed-round-for-ai-that-turns-raw-files-into-legal-documents-for-personal-injury-lawyers/) |\n| MoonPay | Undisclosed | not disclosed | firm says \"led\" | — | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Dapper Labs | Undisclosed (WndrCo named among investors by 2019-09; Dapper had raised $12.3M in 2018-03) | by 2019-09 | firm says \"led\" | Green Bay Ventures, Steve Huffman (other early investors) | [Global Venturing](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2019/09/16/dapper-labs-taps-corporates-for-11m/), [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n\n**AI deals he is named on:** only EvenUp (vertical AI for personal-injury claims; wndr.vc tags it `seed`, valued ~$3B in the bundle). I found no date for WndrCo's entry. The June 2023 $50.5M round release names SignalFire, DCM, NFX and Gokul Rajaram as existing investors and does not mention WndrCo ([BetaKit](https://betakit.com/evenup-raises-50-5-million-in-clio-backed-round-for-ai-that-turns-raw-files-into-legal-documents-for-personal-injury-lawyers/)). WndrCo is absent from the round releases checked (2023 B, 2025 E), which fits a quiet seed-era entry (inference).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **How he got into investing:** through Nas. Nas \"took a gamble\" on him as a young manager ([transcript](https://podcast.farnoosh.tv/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/Anthony-Saleh-TRANSCRIPT.pdf)). Queensbridge then got into Silicon Valley rounds as a co-investor.\n- **How he got to WndrCo:** no source explains it. Queensbridge sat on Dropbox's cap table while Jaswa ran Dropbox's business side, so the Dropbox connection is the likely bridge (inference, unconfirmed).\n- **Sourcing at WndrCo:** consumer and crypto founders via the music and celebrity network. Nothing found on intro paths for specific deals.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| WndrCo | GP, Co-Head of Seed | 2016 (firm bio) | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Nas (Nasir Jones) | Manager; Queensbridge co-founder | ~2007 / ~2014 | [transcript](https://podcast.farnoosh.tv/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/Anthony-Saleh-TRANSCRIPT.pdf), [CNBC](https://www.nbcnews.com/business/business-news/nas-half-man-half-venture-capitalist-n513286) |\n| Queensbridge Venture Partners / Queensbridge Fund I, L.P. | Co-founder; Managing Member of GP | by 2014-01-30 | [EX-4.2](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1467623/000119312518055809/d451946dex42.htm) |\n| Emagen Entertainment Group | Founder, CEO | ~2012 | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| ChenLi Wang | Co-Head of Seed at WndrCo | 2026 | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Ann Daly | Co-director at Tinkergarten | 2019 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1690745/000169074519000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Sujay Jaswa | Partner; Dropbox cap-table overlap | 2014 / 2016 | [EX-4.2](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1467623/000119312518055809/d451946dex42.htm), [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| C3 Presents (Live Nation) | EVP of Management | 2013-12 | [XXL](https://www.xxlmag.com/nas-manager-anthony-aymen-saleh-joins-c3-presents/) |\n| Atom Factory | Former EVP and GM | pre-2013 | [So Money](https://podcast.farnoosh.tv/episode/anthony-saleh) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did WndrCo enter EvenUp, and at what size? No EvenUp Form D could be found on EDGAR under that name.\n- Which WndrCo vehicle held Robinhood, and when? Was it a 2016-2017 Holdings position or a secondary?\n- Is Queensbridge Fund I still active, and who are its LPs? No Form D was found under \"Queensbridge\".\n- Does Emagen WITH, LLC (the Live Nation subsidiary) belong to Saleh's Emagen? This would confirm the Live Nation ownership link.\n- Which recent WndrCo seed deals are his (Polymarket, MeshConnect, Tea, TBPN)? None is attributed.\n- Rap Radar Ep. 20 transcript.\n\n## Sources\n- https://wndr.vc/team ; https://wndr.vc/about ; wndr.vc JS bundle /assets/index-CuMFcD--.js (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20211206172753id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team ; https://web.archive.org/web/20240701id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team ; https://web.archive.org/web/20250701id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team ; https://web.archive.org/web/20260415id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1467623/000119312518055809/d451946dex42.htm (Dropbox IRA)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1759509/000119312519059849/d633517dex42.htm (Lyft IRA)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1866692/000119312521260696/d143868dex43.htm (Amplitude IRA)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1690745/000169074519000001/primary_doc.xml (Tinkergarten Form D)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1335258/000133525820000028/lyv-20191231xex211.htm ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1335258/000133525826000009/lyv-20251231xex211subsidia.htm ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1335258/000133525826000009/lyv-20251231xex1023xthirds.htm (Live Nation)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1793053/000179305319000001/primary_doc.xml (miRecule; different person)\n- https://www.nbcnews.com/business/business-news/nas-half-man-half-venture-capitalist-n513286\n- https://podcast.farnoosh.tv/episode/anthony-saleh ; https://podcast.farnoosh.tv/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/Anthony-Saleh-TRANSCRIPT.pdf\n- https://www.xxlmag.com/nas-manager-anthony-aymen-saleh-joins-c3-presents/\n- https://globalventuring.com/blog/2019/09/16/dapper-labs-taps-corporates-for-11m/\n- https://betakit.com/evenup-raises-50-5-million-in-clio-backed-round-for-ai-that-turns-raw-files-into-legal-documents-for-personal-injury-lawyers/\n- https://www.hustlefund.vc/post/angel-squad-nas-investments-how-hip-hops-best-lyricist-became-one-of-venture-capitals-best-early-betters (secondary)\n- https://vcsheet.com/who/anthony-saleh (aggregator)\n", "person:antoine-moyroud": "\n# Antoine Moyroud\n\n## One-line take\nHe is Lightspeed's Paris partner. He brought in and led Mistral AI's EUR105M seed when the company was about four weeks old, and he is one of three Lightspeed names on David Silver's $1.1B Ineffable Intelligence seed. That makes him the most direct Lightspeed route into European frontier labs.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He is a data-science operator (Dataiku, plus a data science master's) inside a multi-stage US fund. That lets him back researcher founders at a scale European seed funds can't reach. For Mistral he led the round itself instead of following a US lead. [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/antoine-moyroud/) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/13/frances-mistral-ai-blows-in-with-a-113m-seed-round-at-a-260m-valuation-to-take-on-openai/)\n- **How he decides.** He screens for scarce talent. He said only \"70-100 people\" worldwide had the Mistral founders' expertise, and he expects a few players to take most of the value, much as in cloud and databases. He also looks for founders who think past the model to its second-order effects. [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/13/frances-mistral-ai-blows-in-with-a-113m-seed-round-at-a-260m-valuation-to-take-on-openai/) [src](https://siliconangle.com/2023/06/13/paris-based-startup-mistral-ai-raises-113m-seed-funding/)\n- **Constraint.** On the biggest deals he works alongside US senior partners. Ineffable was co-authored with co-founder Ravi Mhatre and Raviraj Jain, and press named Mhatre and Jain, not him. After the Mistral seed he did not lead again: Lightspeed followed a16z into the A. [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/) [src](https://newsletter.lsvp.com/p/lightspeed-february-newsletter)\n- **What he'd never do (inferred).** Back a lab without a top-tier research pedigree. Both of his frontier bets are on named researchers from DeepMind or Meta. No source states this as a rule.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **First institutional money in Mistral, at about four weeks old.** The EUR105M seed (~EUR240M post, reported) was announced 2023-06-13, when the company was about a month old. TechCrunch says Moyroud led it for Lightspeed. \"only four weeks old\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/13/frances-mistral-ai-blows-in-with-a-113m-seed-round-at-a-260m-valuation-to-take-on-openai/)\n- **He saw the market before he saw Mistral.** He said Lightspeed had already met several LLM teams raising money in India, Europe and the US before meeting the Mistral founders. \"For us at Lightspeed, we didn't work in isolation.\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/13/frances-mistral-ai-blows-in-with-a-113m-seed-round-at-a-260m-valuation-to-take-on-openai/)\n- **What set Mistral apart from the other teams.** He said the other researcher-founders \"hadn't thought about the second order effects of LLM\" [src](https://siliconangle.com/2023/06/13/paris-based-startup-mistral-ai-raises-113m-seed-funding/)\n- **He underwrote a talent scarcity count.** \"only 70-100 people in the world right now with their expertise\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/13/frances-mistral-ai-blows-in-with-a-113m-seed-round-at-a-260m-valuation-to-take-on-openai/)\n- **He didn't keep the lead.** Less than six months later Lightspeed \"doubled down and participated\" in the a16z-led Series A (EUR385M at EUR2B). Lightspeed then stayed in the B, C and D as an existing investor. [src](https://newsletter.lsvp.com/p/lightspeed-february-newsletter) [src](https://www.lw.com/en/news/2023/12/Latham%20Advises%20a16z%20on%20Mistral%20AI%20Series%20A%20Funding%20Round) [src](https://mistral.ai/news/mistral-makes-sovereign-open-weight-ai-to-frontier/)\n- **Ineffable: Lightspeed came in late, not first.** Flying Fish wrote the first check, and in February 2026 Sequoia was the only reported lead. By the 2026-04-27 close Lightspeed was co-lead, with a post by Mhatre, Jain and Moyroud: \"we're co-leading its seed round\" [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/) [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=731373) (Flying Fish detail: repo card `companies/ineffable-intelligence.md`)\n- **Experience-data thesis.** The Ineffable post argues that RL from experience gets past the data wall: \"Human-generated data is finite. Experience is not.\" [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/)\n- **Sourcing channel.** He founded Curious Circle, which hosts conversations among founders, researchers, operators and technologists (firm bio, paraphrased). No dates or attendee lists were found. [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/antoine-moyroud/)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is a partner at a fund that can lead nine-figure seeds, and he has done it for a new frontier lab (Mistral) and co-led another (Ineffable, $1.1B at $5.1B). Both are the \"$600M seed for a new lab\" archetype in THESIS.md. CuspAI (AI for materials, Max Welling) and Kyber (real-time robot and drone control infrastructure) are adjacent AI bets. He is the person to know for European researcher-founded labs leaving DeepMind, Meta or FAIR Paris. Caveat: he is a lateral hire, not a fast-promoted principal, so the \"rising junior\" angle doesn't apply.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in the Netherlands and has lived in France, Germany, Spain and the UK. Speaks English, French, German and Spanish. [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/antoine-moyroud/)\n- Dual degree in Business Administration and Languages, University of Lyon. Master's in Data Science, IE Business School (Madrid). [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/antoine-moyroud/)\n- FlixBus, then Dataiku. Dates not found. [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/antoine-moyroud/)\n- Principal at EQT Ventures. Dates not found. [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/antoine-moyroud/)\n- Joined Lightspeed in 2022. He was titled Partner by the June 2023 Mistral announcement, and no source says whether he joined at that title. [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/antoine-moyroud/) [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-europes-next-great-generative-ai-startup-mistral-ai/)\n- Builds side projects and games on his personal site (ColorWalk, Flow Field Studio, Email Triage). [src](http://antoinemoyroud.com)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | http://antoinemoyroud.com | personal site with investments and side projects; blog link returned 404 on 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://twitter.com/antoine_moyroud | not readable (402) on 2026-11-10 |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/antoinemoyroud/ | |\n| GitHub | linked from personal site | not checked |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Curious Circle (conversation series) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. The search budget ran out this session, so this is not confirmed absent.\n\n## How they think\n- **Concentration.** He expects AI to look like cloud and databases, with a few players taking most of the value. [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/13/frances-mistral-ai-blows-in-with-a-113m-seed-round-at-a-260m-valuation-to-take-on-openai/)\n- **Talent scarcity as the underwriting unit.** He frames the deal around how few people worldwide have the founders' skills. [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/13/frances-mistral-ai-blows-in-with-a-113m-seed-round-at-a-260m-valuation-to-take-on-openai/)\n- **European open source as a wedge.** The seed post frames Mistral as a European open-source credible alternative to early LLM players. [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-europes-next-great-generative-ai-startup-mistral-ai/)\n- **Experience over human data** (co-authored post). [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/)\n- **Call he got right.** Mistral went from about EUR240M post at seed to more than EUR21B post at the Series D (2026-09). [src](https://mistral.ai/news/mistral-makes-sovereign-open-weight-ai-to-frontier/)\n\n## Read these first\n- Lightspeed's Mistral seed post (2023-06-13), authored by him: how he framed a European open-source lab. [link](https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-europes-next-great-generative-ai-startup-mistral-ai/)\n- TechCrunch on the Mistral seed: his only long on-record explanation of the deal. [link](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/13/frances-mistral-ai-blows-in-with-a-113m-seed-round-at-a-260m-valuation-to-take-on-openai/)\n- \"Move 37, ten years on\" (2026-04-27), the Ineffable thesis co-written with Mhatre and Jain. [link](https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X was not readable this session.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mistral AI | Seed EUR105M (~EUR240M post) | 2023-06-13 | yes (Lightspeed led; he brought the deal) | JCDecaux Holding; Motier Ventures; La Famiglia; Headline; Exor Ventures; Sofina; First Minute Capital; LocalGlobe; Eric Schmidt; Xavier Niel; Rodolphe Saade | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2023/06/13/paris-based-startup-mistral-ai-raises-113m-seed-funding/) |\n| Mistral AI | Series A EUR385M (EUR2B post) | 2023-12-11 | no (a16z led) | a16z; General Catalyst; Salesforce Ventures; BNP Paribas; Bpifrance | [src](https://www.lw.com/en/news/2023/12/Latham%20Advises%20a16z%20on%20Mistral%20AI%20Series%20A%20Funding%20Round) |\n| Mistral AI | Series B, C, D | 2024-06; 2025-09; 2026-09 | no (existing investor) | DST; General Catalyst; ASML; Samsung | [src](https://mistral.ai/fr/news/mistral-ai-raises-1-7-b-to-accelerate-technological-progress-with-ai) [src](https://mistral.ai/news/mistral-makes-sovereign-open-weight-ai-to-frontier/) |\n| Ineffable Intelligence | Seed $1.1B ($5.1B) | 2026-04-27 | co-lead (with Sequoia); co-signed with Ravi Mhatre and Raviraj Jain | Sequoia; Nvidia; Google; Index; DST Global; Flying Fish | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/ineffable-intelligence/) [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/27/deepmind-ineffable-intelligence-record-seed-funding-nvidia-google.html) |\n| CuspAI | Seed | 2024 | not stated | not found | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/cuspai/) |\n| Orqa | Seed (Lightspeed entry stage) | 2024 | not stated; co-signer Alexander Schmitt | not found | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/orqa/) |\n| Kyber | Seed | 2026 | not stated | not found | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/kyber/) |\n| Schematik | Pre-seed | 2026 | not stated | not found | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/schematik/) |\n| Naboo | Series B | 2026 | not stated | not found | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/naboo/) |\n| The Mobile First Company | not found | not found | not found | not found | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/antoine-moyroud/) |\n| Hook | not found | not found | not found | not found | personal site only [src](http://antoinemoyroud.com) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Mistral AI (seed, 2023).** Lightspeed and its partners had met several LLM teams in India, Europe and the US. The Mistral founders (Arthur Mensch, ex-DeepMind; Guillaume Lample and Timothee Lacroix, ex-Meta) stood out because they thought about the second-order effects of LLMs. Lightspeed led EUR105M about four weeks after founding. No other institutional investor is reported before that round, so Lightspeed was first institutional money. a16z (Anjney Midha) led the A less than six months later and Lightspeed followed. The first-meeting intro path is not public. [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/13/frances-mistral-ai-blows-in-with-a-113m-seed-round-at-a-260m-valuation-to-take-on-openai/) [src](https://siliconangle.com/2023/06/13/paris-based-startup-mistral-ai-raises-113m-seed-funding/) [src](https://newsletter.lsvp.com/p/lightspeed-february-newsletter)\n- **Ineffable Intelligence (seed, 2026).** David Silver incorporated the company in November 2025. Flying Fish (Heather Gorham) had built the relationship since early 2024 and wrote the first check. Sequoia was reported as lead in February 2026, and Lightspeed was co-lead by the April close. Moyroud is the Europe-based name of the three Lightspeed partners; his specific role in the deal is not described. [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=731373) [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/)\n- **CuspAI.** Founded in 2024 by Chad Edwards and Max Welling (AI for materials discovery). Lightspeed lists a 2024 seed with Moyroud. Kleiner Perkins later co-led a Series B in 2026-07. [src](https://lsvp.com/company/cuspai/) [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/cuspai-a-search-engine-for-materials-that-dont-exist-yet/)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lightspeed Venture Partners | Partner, Paris | 2022 | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/antoine-moyroud/) |\n| EQT Ventures | Principal (prior) | dates not found | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/antoine-moyroud/) |\n| Dataiku | prior employee | dates not found | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/antoine-moyroud/) |\n| Arthur Mensch (Mistral) | seed lead investor | 2023 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-europes-next-great-generative-ai-startup-mistral-ai/) |\n| Ravi Mhatre; Raviraj Jain | co-authors on the Ineffable co-lead | 2026 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/) |\n| David Silver (Ineffable) | seed co-lead investor | 2026 | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/ineffable-intelligence/) |\n| Alexander Schmitt (Lightspeed) | co-partner on Orqa | 2024 | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/orqa/) |\n| Anjney Midha (a16z) | led Mistral A after his seed | 2023 | [src](https://www.lw.com/en/news/2023/12/Latham%20Advises%20a16z%20on%20Mistral%20AI%20Series%20A%20Funding%20Round) |\n| Max Welling (CuspAI) | seed investor | 2024 | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/cuspai/) |\n| Curious Circle | founder | not found | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/antoine-moyroud/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Dates at EQT Ventures, and whether he joined Lightspeed as Partner or was promoted (LinkedIn).\n- How he first met the Mistral founders. Was it through Curious Circle, the Paris research scene or EQT networks?\n- His actual role on Ineffable versus Mhatre and Jain.\n- CuspAI seed: size, lead, and whether Lightspeed was first.\n- Podcasts and talks. None found this session because the search budget was exhausted.\n- Who \"Hook\" is (personal site only).\n\n## Sources\n- https://lsvp.com/team-member/antoine-moyroud/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-europes-next-great-generative-ai-startup-mistral-ai/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/13/frances-mistral-ai-blows-in-with-a-113m-seed-round-at-a-260m-valuation-to-take-on-openai/\n- https://siliconangle.com/2023/06/13/paris-based-startup-mistral-ai-raises-113m-seed-funding/\n- https://newsletter.lsvp.com/p/lightspeed-february-newsletter\n- https://www.lw.com/en/news/2023/12/Latham%20Advises%20a16z%20on%20Mistral%20AI%20Series%20A%20Funding%20Round\n- https://mistral.ai/fr/news/mistral-ai-raises-1-7-b-to-accelerate-technological-progress-with-ai\n- https://mistral.ai/news/mistral-makes-sovereign-open-weight-ai-to-frontier/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/ineffable-intelligence/\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/27/deepmind-ineffable-intelligence-record-seed-funding-nvidia-google.html\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=731373\n- https://lsvp.com/company/cuspai/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/orqa/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/kyber/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/schematik/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/naboo/\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/cuspai-a-search-engine-for-materials-that-dont-exist-yet/\n- http://antoinemoyroud.com\n", "person:anuj-mehndiratta": "\n# Anuj Mehndiratta\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the person who connects Thrive's two AI bets. At Thrive Capital he runs the firm's own AI tooling, and at Thrive Holdings he helped start the permanent-capital vehicle that buys services businesses and rebuilds them with AI. Its deals run from the Shield IT roll-up to a $15.3B ADT take-private, and he is a named spokesperson on each one. [src](https://newsroom.adt.com/financial/adt-to-be-acquired-by-thrive-holdings-in-15-3-billion-transaction) Following him is the clearest way to track whether \"AI-enabled private equity\" works at scale.\n\n## Crux\n*(Added 2026-11-09.)* He wins by being Thrive's operator-quant. He brings Blackstone PE data-science experience to a permanent-capital vehicle that buys services businesses, embeds its own engineers, and trades workflow data and equity for frontier-lab research talent. That model is how OpenAI got equity in Thrive Holdings for researchers instead of cash. [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/15/palantir-cio-jim-siders-leaves-for-thrive-capitals-shield-technology.html) The constraint is that the strategy depends on buying whole businesses at buyout scale (up to ADT). Results are company-reported and unproven, and his public voice is almost all press quotes. Based on how he talks, he would not force a top-down AI rollout on acquired firms (\"Think of this as an opt-in model\") [src](https://www.accountingtoday.com/news/crete-pa-plans-500-million-spend-to-buy-and-upgrade-firms-with-ai), and his firm says it will not run on a fund-life clock. [src](https://www.thriveholdings.com/long-humans)\n\n## Differential insight\n*(Added 2026-11-09. No podcast, Substack, X or YouTube material by him was found. Every item below comes from trade press or the firm site, so \"non-obvious\" here means buried, not secret.)*\n- **The OpenAI data-for-tech logic came about five months before the OpenAI deal.** In June 2025 he described Thrive's accounting platform as an asset OpenAI could use: \"We have accountants, workflows, all these benefits that OpenAI can benefit from.\" [src](https://www.accountingtoday.com/news/crete-pa-plans-500-million-spend-to-buy-and-upgrade-firms-with-ai) OpenAI's equity stake was announced 2025-12-01. [src](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/02/openai-buys-stake-in-thrive-holdings-to-push-ai-into-accounting-it-services)\n- **Ownership aligns the lab.** On why OpenAI is an owner rather than a vendor: \"By being an owner, they will be enabled to actually focus on end outcomes.\" [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/15/palantir-cio-jim-siders-leaves-for-thrive-capitals-shield-technology.html) This is a reusable template for labs swapping researchers for equity.\n- **Vertical deployment needs research talent.** The trigger was hitting \"research problems much sooner\" than expected. [src](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/02/openai-buys-stake-in-thrive-holdings-to-push-ai-into-accounting-it-services)\n- **Opt-in, local-first product method.** He said \"Think of this as an opt-in model\" and \"there are obviously patterns that we can start to abstract over time.\" [src](https://www.accountingtoday.com/news/crete-pa-plans-500-million-spend-to-buy-and-upgrade-firms-with-ai) The idea is to experiment per firm and then platformize. This matches the firm's unsigned manifesto line, \"Insights start local before they go national.\" [src](https://www.thriveholdings.com/long-humans)\n- **Central engineering embedded in each acquired company.** For Shield, he said Thrive Holdings' engineers are \"working closely with STP's partners to build and embed technology\". [src](https://www.channelinsider.com/news-and-trends/stp-it-services-platform-ai-growth/)\n- **Diligence on customer pain, not just cost.** Thrive studied \"all of the ways in which MSPs have perhaps been on their back foot\" with customers. [src](https://www.channelinsider.com/news-and-trends/stp-it-services-platform-ai-growth/)\n- **ADT timing.** Apollo \"sold the last of its remaining shares in May 2026\". About five months later, Apollo-managed funds are financing Thrive's take-private of the company Apollo once owned via a 2016 LBO. [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/adt-agrees-7-6-billion-212946818.html)\n- **Permanent-capital constraint.** The Thrive Holdings manifesto (unsigned, not attributed to him) says \"You cannot be long humans on a shot clock.\" [src](https://www.thriveholdings.com/long-humans)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** He works at buyout and growth scale (Shield >$100M, ADT ~$7.6B equity) on services roll-ups, not seed-stage frontier labs, chips or talent. He is not someone who would co-invest in a $600M lab seed through this vehicle. He is still worth tracking for two reasons. (1) Thrive Holdings is a direct competitor and benchmark for AI-native automation startups like Serval: Shield buys MSPs and rebuilds them with AI rather than selling software to them. (2) His OpenAI equity-for-researchers structure shows how frontier labs monetize research talent in verticals. Deals that matter to me: OpenAI–Thrive Holdings (2025-12) and Shield (2025-06 onward). Thrive Capital itself (OpenAI investor) is higher fit, but he is not its frontier-AI deal lead as far as sources show.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** B.S. in Applied Mathematics & Statistics and Computer Science, Johns Hopkins University. [src](https://ai4.com/speakers/anuj-mehndiratta/)\n- **Millennium Management:** quantitative analyst. No dates found. [src](https://ai4.com/speakers/anuj-mehndiratta/)\n- **Blackstone:** helped build, and eventually led, the data science team serving Blackstone's private equity funds. No dates found. [src](https://ai4.com/speakers/anuj-mehndiratta/)\n- **Earlier:** an aggregator lists CodeHS and Princeton Tutoring tutoring roles (unverified, aggregator only). [src](https://theorg.com/org/thrive-capital/org-chart/anuj-mehndiratta)\n- **Thrive Capital:** now a Partner. He leads the firm's product, engineering, data and AI work, including internal tools, and heads the Portfolio Impact team, which supports portfolio companies on research, finance, operations and go-to-market. [src](https://ai4.com/speakers/anuj-mehndiratta/) His earlier title appears to have been \"Head of Data & Product\" (unverified, aggregator only). [src](https://theorg.com/org/thrive-capital/org-chart/anuj-mehndiratta) Colossus (Oct 2025) calls him \"a partner and head of portfolio impact.\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) His join date at Thrive is not public (unverified).\n- **Thrive Holdings (2025):** he helped launch it [src](https://ai4.com/speakers/anuj-mehndiratta/), and Reuters described him as the Thrive Capital partner \"who oversees Thrive Holdings\" (Dec 2025). [src](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/02/openai-buys-stake-in-thrive-holdings-to-push-ai-into-accounting-it-services) Thrive Holdings' own releases call him a founding team member (Feb 2026) [src](https://itdigest.com/?p=77883) and a Partner at Thrive Holdings (Oct 2026). [src](https://newsroom.adt.com/financial/adt-to-be-acquired-by-thrive-holdings-in-15-3-billion-transaction)\n- **Personal:** he is the son of Indian immigrants to New Jersey and told Colossus he is \"still a believer in the American dream.\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No account located (2026-11-09) |\n| LinkedIn | [linkedin.com/in/anuj-mehndiratta-163b28105](https://www.linkedin.com/in/anuj-mehndiratta-163b28105/) | URL from an aggregator [src](https://shizune.co/investors/profile/anuj-mehndiratta). Not opened directly; follower count and cadence unknown |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-08 — Ai4 2026 (Las Vegas) — \"Conversation with Anuj Mehndiratta, Thrive Capital & Jim Siders, Shield\" — [link](https://ai4.com/sessions/conversation-with-anuj-mehndiratta-thrive-capital-and-jim-siders-shield/) — a fireside chat on Aug 5, 2026 about driving enterprise AI adoption \"from the inside out.\" It covered how Thrive Holdings' owner-operator, long-horizon model closes the gap between AI capability and business impact. No recording or transcript found.\n- 2025-10 — Colossus Review (issue 05), Jeremy Stern's profile of Thrive Capital — [link](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) — this is a cameo, not an interview. He demoed **Puck**, Thrive's internal AI agent, and **Thrive One**, the broader internal system Puck runs on. The article gives no detail on what either one does.\n- 2025-06 — Accounting Today interview on Crete's $500M plan — [link](https://www.accountingtoday.com/news/crete-pa-plans-500-million-spend-to-buy-and-upgrade-firms-with-ai) — his longest on-record remarks. He covered the opt-in model, abstracting patterns across firms, and OpenAI as a technology partner (added 2026-11-09).\n- 2025-12 — CNBC (Ashley Capoot) on Jim Siders joining Shield — [link](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/15/palantir-cio-jim-siders-leaves-for-thrive-capitals-shield-technology.html) — he explained why owning a stake aligns OpenAI on outcomes (added 2026-11-09).\n- No podcast appearances found. Josh Kushner's Invest Like the Best EP.459 (2026-02-18) has a \"Thrive Holdings: Inside-Out Disruption\" segment at 00:44:37, but the transcript is paywalled and it is not known whether he is named. [src](https://colossus.com/episode/concentration-and-conviction/)\n\n## How they think\nHis public statements are limited to press-release quotes and one TechCrunch interview. Everything below comes from those.\n- **Specialized AI drives enterprise adoption.** He has said \"specialized AI is core to driving enterprise AI adoption\" (Shield release, Feb 2026). [src](https://itdigest.com/?p=77883) The thesis is that vertical, workflow-specific AI deployed inside businesses you own beats selling horizontal tools from outside.\n- **Ownership aligns AI labs.** On the OpenAI deal, he said Thrive Holdings hit \"research problems much sooner\" than expected when deploying models, and that \"aligning OpenAI through ownership\" is powerful. OpenAI received equity in exchange for a dedicated research team, while Thrive Holdings keeps the IP. He also said the deal does not stop them from using other models, including open-source ones. [src](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/02/openai-buys-stake-in-thrive-holdings-to-push-ai-into-accounting-it-services) Another report paraphrases him as saying \"off-the-shelf\" AI was not enough for their needs (secondary). [src](https://techfundingnews.com/thrive-holdings-raises-2-billion-ai-services/)\n- **AI as workflow relief, not replacement.** On the planned regulatory-services platform for the built environment, he said AI won't replace field work, local judgment or professional sign-off. He said it can lighten permitting, reporting and compliance tracking, which he sees as a bottleneck caused by \"local, technical, and regulatory complexity\" (TechCrunch, Aug 2026). [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/openai-backed-thrive-holdings-raises-2b-to-bring-ai-to-the-enterprise/)\n- **Owner-operator over vendor.** The Ai4 session framed Thrive Holdings' edge as the owner-operator model with a long-term horizon. [src](https://ai4.com/sessions/conversation-with-anuj-mehndiratta-thrive-capital-and-jim-siders-shield/)\n- **Humility and opt-in with acquired firms (2025-06).** He said Thrive approaches partnerships \"with a lot of humility\" and that \"We're not going to force anything onto you.\" [src](https://www.accountingtoday.com/news/crete-pa-plans-500-million-spend-to-buy-and-upgrade-firms-with-ai)\n- **Data trust in consumer businesses.** On ADT, he said \"Protecting customers' homes also means protecting their data.\" [src](https://newsroom.adt.com/financial/adt-to-be-acquired-by-thrive-holdings-in-15-3-billion-transaction)\n- **Calls right or wrong:** too early to judge. Thrive Holdings' reported metrics come from the company and are unaudited: 70+ businesses on its platforms, and Current's TaxAI processing 7,000+ returns at 98% accuracy. [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/openai-backed-thrive-holdings-raises-2b-to-bring-ai-to-the-enterprise/)\n\n## Read these first\nHe has written nothing long-form. The best primary material:\n1. [TechCrunch, Aug 2026: Thrive Holdings raises $2B](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/openai-backed-thrive-holdings-raises-2b-to-bring-ai-to-the-enterprise/). His most extended on-record comments, and the third (regulatory/built-environment) platform.\n2. [Reuters via The Star, Dec 2025: OpenAI stake in Thrive Holdings](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/02/openai-buys-stake-in-thrive-holdings-to-push-ai-into-accounting-it-services). His reasoning for trading equity for embedded OpenAI researchers.\n3. [ADT release, Oct 2026](https://newsroom.adt.com/financial/adt-to-be-acquired-by-thrive-holdings-in-15-3-billion-transaction). Shows how far the strategy has scaled beyond small-firm roll-ups.\n4. [Colossus, Oct 2025](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/). Context on Thrive's internal AI (Puck, Thrive One) and the portfolio impact team's history.\n5. [Ai4 2026 session page](https://ai4.com/sessions/conversation-with-anuj-mehndiratta-thrive-capital-and-jim-siders-shield/). How he pitches the owner-operator model.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No active X account located, and LinkedIn activity was not checked directly. His public voice comes almost entirely through Thrive Holdings press releases and news quotes.\n\n## Portfolio\nThese are Thrive Holdings transactions where he is named as a spokesperson or founding team member. They are firm deals, not personal investments.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Shield Technology Partners (AI-enabled IT services platform) | Launch / initial funding, >$100M | 2025-06-05 | Thrive Holdings (with ZBS Partners) | ZBS Partners | [src](https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/thrive-holdings-zbs-partners-launch-shield-technology-partners-an-ai-enabled-platform-for-it-services-businesses-with-over-100m-in-initial-funding). His personal role is unverified; one aggregator attributes it to him [src](https://shizune.co/investors/profile/anuj-mehndiratta) |\n| Shield Technology Partners | $100M growth investment | 2026-02-02 | Yes (Thrive Holdings, sole) | none named | [src](https://itdigest.com/?p=77883) |\n| ADT Inc. | All-cash take-private, ~$15.3B EV / ~$7.6B equity | 2026-10-29 (announced; close expected Q1 2027) | Yes (Thrive Holdings acquirer) | Financing from Apollo-managed funds | [src](https://newsroom.adt.com/financial/adt-to-be-acquired-by-thrive-holdings-in-15-3-billion-transaction) |\n\nRelated, but his role is not confirmed:\n- **Current** (formerly Crete Professionals Alliance) is Thrive Holdings' accounting platform. Thrive Capital co-led its May 2024 round with Bessemer [src](https://www.cpapracticeadvisor.com/2024/05/06/crete-secures-funding-to-add-new-partner-firms-grow-tech-capabilities/104857/), and it rebranded in June 2026. [src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260602293257/en/Crete-Professionals-Alliance-Rebrands-as-Current-to-Equip-Independent-Accounting-Firms-to-Compete-at-Enterprise-Scale)\n\n## Deal stories\n- **OpenAI stake in Thrive Holdings (announced 2025-12-01).** This was a non-cash deal. OpenAI took ownership in exchange for a dedicated research team that works with Thrive's domain experts, using reinforcement learning with expert feedback. Thrive Holdings owns the resulting IP. Mehndiratta's account is that deploying models in accounting and IT ran into research problems early, so it made sense to align a lab through ownership. [src](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/02/openai-buys-stake-in-thrive-holdings-to-push-ai-into-accounting-it-services) A tax-return agent co-built by Thrive and OpenAI staff using Codex is reportedly used at Current (secondary). [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/thrive-holdings-2bn-12bn-valuation-softbank-ai-rollup)\n- **Shield.** The platform launched in June 2025 with ZBS Partners, which is led by Jake Sloane and Frank Zhang. [src](https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/thrive-holdings-zbs-partners-launch-shield-technology-partners-an-ai-enabled-platform-for-it-services-businesses-with-over-100m-in-initial-funding) Thrive then put in another $100M alone in Feb 2026, with Jim Siders as CEO. [src](https://itdigest.com/?p=77883) By Aug 2026, Mehndiratta and Siders were presenting it together as the model case at Ai4. [src](https://ai4.com/sessions/conversation-with-anuj-mehndiratta-thrive-capital-and-jim-siders-shield/) Thrive claims a 36x faster help-desk resolution time across about 20 companies (company-reported). [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/openai-backed-thrive-holdings-raises-2b-to-bring-ai-to-the-enterprise/)\n- **Shield recruiting (Dec 2025).** Jim Siders came from 12+ years at Palantir, where he started as a help-desk engineer and finished as CIO. Shield had seven companies at that point and expected over $100M revenue in 2025. Partner firms keep equity in their own companies. [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/15/palantir-cio-jim-siders-leaves-for-thrive-capitals-shield-technology.html)\n- **ADT (Oct 2026).** This is a big step up from tuck-in roll-ups of small firms to a public-company take-private, with Apollo-managed funds as sole financing provider. Mehndiratta and Kareem Zaki are both quoted as Partners at Thrive Holdings. [src](https://newsroom.adt.com/financial/adt-to-be-acquired-by-thrive-holdings-in-15-3-billion-transaction) How the deal originated is not public. Apollo owned ADT via a 2016 LBO and sold its last shares in May 2026 before financing this buyout. [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/adt-agrees-7-6-billion-212946818.html)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Thrive Capital | Partner; leads product/eng/data/AI and Portfolio Impact | unknown | [src](https://ai4.com/speakers/anuj-mehndiratta/) |\n| Thrive Holdings | Founding team member; Partner; \"oversees\" per Reuters | 2025 | [src](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/02/openai-buys-stake-in-thrive-holdings-to-push-ai-into-accounting-it-services) |\n| Kareem Zaki | Fellow Thrive Holdings founding member and partner | 2025 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/openai-backed-thrive-holdings-raises-2b-to-bring-ai-to-the-enterprise/) |\n| Joshua Kushner | Founder/CEO of Thrive Capital (his firm's head) | unknown | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Jim Siders | CEO of Shield; Ai4 2026 co-panelist | 2026 | [src](https://ai4.com/sessions/conversation-with-anuj-mehndiratta-thrive-capital-and-jim-siders-shield/) |\n| OpenAI | Equity partner in Thrive Holdings; embedded research team | 2025-12 | [src](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/02/openai-buys-stake-in-thrive-holdings-to-push-ai-into-accounting-it-services) |\n| Blackstone | Former employer; led PE data science team | unknown | [src](https://ai4.com/speakers/anuj-mehndiratta/) |\n| Millennium Management | Former employer; quantitative analyst | unknown | [src](https://ai4.com/speakers/anuj-mehndiratta/) |\n| Nabil Mallick | Thrive COO; first head of portfolio impact, the team he now leads | unknown | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Steve Stagner | CEO of Crete/Current; co-quoted on the $500M plan | 2025 | [src](https://www.accountingtoday.com/news/crete-pa-plans-500-million-spend-to-buy-and-upgrade-firms-with-ai) |\n| Apollo Global Management | Financing provider for the ADT take-private | 2026-10 | [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/adt-agrees-7-6-billion-212946818.html) |\n| Nworah Ayogu | Thrive Capital partner named alongside him in Colossus | unknown | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What do Puck and Thrive One actually do: deal sourcing, portfolio monitoring, memo drafting? How do they relate to Linus Lee's internal-tools work (see `linus-lee.md`)? Colossus credits Lee with building Thrive's internal tools and systems. [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)\n- When did he join Thrive Capital, and when did he leave Blackstone? No dated source was found. Check LinkedIn directly.\n- Was he involved in Thrive Capital's 2024 Crete (now Current) investment, or did his role start with Thrive Holdings?\n- Who leads the third Thrive Holdings platform (regulatory services for the built environment), and is he running it?\n- Did Nabil Mallick, the first head of portfolio impact per Colossus, hand the team to him, and when?\n- Has the Ai4 2026 fireside chat been recorded or transcribed?\n- Does Kushner name him in the paywalled Invest Like the Best EP.459 Thrive Holdings segment (00:44:37)? (Added 2026-11-09.)\n- What is his relationship, if any, to Thrive Eternal, the separate permanent-capital vehicle behind the 2026 SF Giants stake? [src](https://telecomlive.in/web/?p=1004775)\n- Did his June 2025 framing of OpenAI as a partner lead directly to the December 2025 equity deal, and who brokered it?\n- What does Thrive Holdings plan for ADT on AI, and will the go-shop (ends 2026-11-28) produce a rival bid? [src](https://newsroom.adt.com/financial/adt-to-be-acquired-by-thrive-holdings-in-15-3-billion-transaction)\n\n## Sources\n- https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/openai-backed-thrive-holdings-raises-2b-to-bring-ai-to-the-enterprise/\n- https://theorg.com/org/thrive-capital/org-chart/anuj-mehndiratta\n- https://ai4.com/speakers/anuj-mehndiratta/\n- https://ai4.com/sessions/conversation-with-anuj-mehndiratta-thrive-capital-and-jim-siders-shield/\n- https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/12/02/openai-buys-stake-in-thrive-holdings-to-push-ai-into-accounting-it-services\n- https://itdigest.com/?p=77883\n- https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/thrive-holdings-zbs-partners-launch-shield-technology-partners-an-ai-enabled-platform-for-it-services-businesses-with-over-100m-in-initial-funding\n- https://newsroom.adt.com/financial/adt-to-be-acquired-by-thrive-holdings-in-15-3-billion-transaction\n- https://shizune.co/investors/profile/anuj-mehndiratta\n- https://techfundingnews.com/thrive-holdings-raises-2-billion-ai-services/\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/thrive-holdings-2bn-12bn-valuation-softbank-ai-rollup\n- https://www.cpapracticeadvisor.com/2024/05/06/crete-secures-funding-to-add-new-partner-firms-grow-tech-capabilities/104857/\n- https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260602293257/en/Crete-Professionals-Alliance-Rebrands-as-Current-to-Equip-Independent-Accounting-Firms-to-Compete-at-Enterprise-Scale\n- https://www.accountingtoday.com/news/crete-pa-plans-500-million-spend-to-buy-and-upgrade-firms-with-ai (added 2026-11-09)\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/15/palantir-cio-jim-siders-leaves-for-thrive-capitals-shield-technology.html (added 2026-11-09)\n- https://www.channelinsider.com/news-and-trends/stp-it-services-platform-ai-growth/ (added 2026-11-09)\n- https://www.thriveholdings.com/long-humans (added 2026-11-09)\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/adt-agrees-7-6-billion-212946818.html (added 2026-11-09)\n- https://colossus.com/episode/concentration-and-conviction/ (added 2026-11-09)\n- https://telecomlive.in/web/?p=1004775 (added 2026-11-09)\n", "person:aqil-pasha": "\n# Aqil Pasha\n\n## One-line take\nThe Jump Capital investor who writes most publicly about the GPU, inference and agent-infrastructure layer. He co-wrote Jump's post on the $20M Standard Kernel seed that Jump led and wrote its inference-for-agents thesis alone, so he is the person to watch for Jump's next compute-layer bet ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/), [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/inference-was-built-for-requests-agents-need-something-different/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he thinks in systems. He is a chemical engineer and a former AWS solutions architect who supported startup customers. He writes from the infrastructure operator's side: kernels, KV-cache, GPU scheduling and fleet observability. That is rare in a fund best known for fintech and enterprise software ([src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/), [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/introducing-aqil-pasha-jumps-newest-vice-president/)).\n- **What he underwrites:** the software layer that raises utilization of hardware that already exists, not new chips or labs. On Standard Kernel the post argues the bottleneck \"isn't a hardware problem. It's a software problem\" ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/)). His agent-inference post moves the metric from tokens per second to \"time to useful work\" ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/inference-was-built-for-requests-agents-need-something-different/)).\n- **Constraints:** he is a VP, not a partner. All four portfolio companies on his team page are co-listed with partner Saaya Pal, and every \"why we invested\" post he is on is co-bylined with Pal ([src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/), [src](https://www.jumpcap.com/insights)). No source says he sourced or led any deal. Most of his deal volume is cybersecurity (Realm, Above, Pluto), not frontier AI.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred, low confidence):** back a point product that open source is likely to absorb, or \"yet another monitoring dashboard.\" He names OSS absorption as the hardest question in GPU infrastructure ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/three-things-im-watching-at-kubecon-and-why-they-matter/)).\n\n## Differential insight\nAll primary sources are Jump Capital blog posts with his byline. No podcast, Substack, talk or readable X posts were found: the web-search budget was exhausted this session and X returned HTTP 402.\n- **Solo agent-inference thesis (about Oct 2026, dated from image uploads):** \"Optimizing AI Infrastructure across the stack is a core focus for us at Jump.\" He names Baseten, Together and Fireworks as scaled incumbents and vLLM and SGLang as engines. The open gap, he argues, is co-designing execution, persistent state and workflow-aware routing for long-horizon agents ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/inference-was-built-for-requests-agents-need-something-different/)).\n- **His metric:** \"Speed is UX. And shrinking time to useful work is what ultimately matters.\" This is a clear screen for founders who pitch per-task latency rather than per-token throughput ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/inference-was-built-for-requests-agents-need-something-different/)).\n- **KV-cache as a newly fundable category:** \"Historically, KV-cache management has been a difficult area to justify a standalone solution.\" He argues long-horizon agents change that. He also flags fleet-wide GPU scheduling (successor to Run:AI) and inference observability ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/three-things-im-watching-at-kubecon-and-why-they-matter/)).\n- **Contrarian on observability:** \"I'm not advocating for yet another monitoring dashboard\". He wants an intelligence layer that crosses memory, compute and networking ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/three-things-im-watching-at-kubecon-and-why-they-matter/)).\n- **Kills-the-deal risk he names:** \"The harder question will always be what gets absorbed into the OSS ecosystem\" ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/three-things-im-watching-at-kubecon-and-why-they-matter/)).\n- **Standard Kernel framing (with Saaya Pal):** \"Most of the world's most expensive hardware is sitting idle.\" The post states Jump led the $20M seed. Founders are Anne Ouyang (ex-NVIDIA cuDNN, KernelBench co-author) and Chris Rinard ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/)).\n- **Physical-AI extension (about May 2026):** \"That shift creates an entirely new infrastructure opportunity.\" He reads robotics fleet observability as moving from build to buy, and names Foxglove ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/robotics-is-entering-its-operations-era/)).\n- **Agent observability as \"truth layer\" (with Pal):** \"a truth layer for agent execution\". They argue a neutral cross-provider telemetry standard could win over OpenAI or Anthropic native tracing ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/the-datadog-opportunity-for-ai-agents/)).\n- **GTM lens from his intro post (about Mar 2025):** \"Make your champions look good, and you'll have an easy way in.\" His stated value-add to founders is product strategy, messaging and go-to-market ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/introducing-aqil-pasha-jumps-newest-vice-president/), [src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/)).\n- **Deal timing, Standard Kernel:** Jump led the $20M seed, so Jump was at least lead on the priced seed ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/)). The company site lists General Catalyst, Felicis, Cowboy Ventures, Link Ventures, Essence VC, CoreWeave Ventures and Ericsson Ventures as backers. Angels are David M. Siegel, Jeff Dean, Jonathan Frankle, Michael Carbin and Walden Yan. The site does not say which round each joined ([src](https://standardkernel.com/)). **Who was first, and the gap to the next firm: not determined.** No dated announcement was found.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:** Standard Kernel is a $20M seed in AI compute efficiency (GPU kernels) from a KernelBench/Stanford team, with Jeff Dean and Jonathan Frankle as angels. That is squarely the \"AI semiconductors and compute\" part of THESIS.md. He is the Jump voice on inference, KV-cache and GPU scheduling, and he invites founders in that area to contact him ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/inference-was-built-for-requests-agents-need-something-different/)). He is a useful co-investor and diligence contact for compute-software seeds.\n- **Against:** he is a VP with no sourced lead role. Most of his portfolio is security (Realm, Above, Pluto). Jump Capital is a fintech and enterprise-software fund, not a lab or pre-training backer. The Flapping Airplanes check came from Jump Trading, not Jump Capital, and no Jump person is named on it (see `data/rows/jump-trading-deals.csv`).\n- **Deals that matter to you:** Standard Kernel (high). Above Security (medium: agent-identity insider risk). Realm Security (low: security data pipeline). Pluto Security (low: he is listed but not a byline author).\n\n## Background\n- **Early life:** born in Chennai, grew up in New Zealand and Saudi Arabia, moved to the U.S. for college ([src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/)).\n- **Education:** B.S. Chemical Engineering, UCLA. Graduate studies at MIT, exploring venture and entrepreneurship. Degree and years not stated ([src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/)).\n- **Amgen:** scientist (intro post), or product manager in pharma focused on data infrastructure (team page). Dates not given ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/introducing-aqil-pasha-jumps-newest-vice-president/), [src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/)).\n- **AWS:** solutions architect supporting startup customers. Dates not given ([src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/)).\n- **Seed-stage startup in Australia:** product marketing manager. Company and dates not given ([src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/)).\n- **Jump Capital:** Vice President. Joined around March 2025, inferred from the intro post's image path, not a stated date ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/introducing-aqil-pasha-jumps-newest-vice-president/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/ | Team bio and portfolio list (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.jumpcap.com/insights | 18 Jump posts with his byline (index as of 2026-11-10), about 7 solo; cadence about monthly, often tied to conferences (RSA, Black Hat, SRECon, KubeCon) |\n| X | https://x.com/pasha_aqil | Linked from the team page; contents not readable (HTTP 402); followers not found |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/aqilpasha/ | Linked from the team page; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. The web-search budget was exhausted this session, so this is untested, not a confirmed absence. His conference posts (RSA 2025 and 2026, Black Hat 2025 and 2026, SRECon, KubeCon) show he attends these events. No speaking role was found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Utilization over new silicon:** the leverage is in software that gets more out of GPUs already deployed (Standard Kernel, the KubeCon post).\n- **The unit of work is the agent task, not the request:** inference stacks need state, routing and scheduling that understand workflows ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/inference-was-built-for-requests-agents-need-something-different/)).\n- **Observability as a recurring motif:** for agents (\"truth layer\"), GPU fleets and robot fleets. He looks for intelligence layers, not dashboards.\n- **Cross-layer products earn durable value:** \"products that cross these boundaries (memory, compute, networking)\" ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/three-things-im-watching-at-kubecon-and-why-they-matter/)).\n- **Contrarian:** KV-cache management may now justify a standalone company. The main risk is OSS absorption (llm-d, vLLM, SGLang), not incumbents.\n- **Calls right or wrong:** too early to judge. No exits are sourced.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Inference Was Built for Requests. Agents Need Something Different.\" (solo): the clearest statement of his compute-layer thesis ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/inference-was-built-for-requests-agents-need-something-different/)).\n2. \"Why we invested in Standard Kernel\" (with Saaya Pal): the one high-fit deal ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/)).\n3. \"Three Things I'm Watching at KubeCon\" (solo): his three target categories and the OSS risk ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/three-things-im-watching-at-kubecon-and-why-they-matter/)).\n4. \"Robotics is Entering its Operations Era\" (solo): his physical-AI angle ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/robotics-is-entering-its-operations-era/)).\n5. \"The Datadog Opportunity for AI Agents\" (with Pal): agent observability thesis ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/the-datadog-opportunity-for-ai-agents/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot determined. X was unreadable (HTTP 402) and the search budget was exhausted. On the Jump blog he co-writes mostly with Saaya Pal, and also with Jason Felger and Eliza Chamberlain on conference recaps ([src](https://www.jumpcap.com/insights)).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Standard Kernel | Seed ($20M) | not stated | Jump led; his role not stated | General Catalyst, Felicis, Cowboy, Link, Essence VC, CoreWeave Ventures, Ericsson Ventures (round unknown) | [Jump](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/), [site](https://standardkernel.com/) |\n| Above Security | not disclosed | not stated | not stated | not named | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-above-security/) |\n| Realm Security | not disclosed | not stated | not stated | not named | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-realm-security-fixing-the-broken-security-data-layer/) |\n| Pluto Security | Series A ($33M) | not stated (2026 page) | Jump led; post by Pal and Felger, not Pasha | not named | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-pluto-security/), [team page](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Standard Kernel:** the post does not say how the deal came about or who sourced it. The founders met teaching Performance Engineering at MIT ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/)). Pasha did graduate work at MIT ([src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/)), but no source links that to the deal. Treat any MIT intro path as unconfirmed. After the seed, Standard Kernel published a CoreWeave inference-optimization case study, and CoreWeave Ventures is a backer ([src](https://standardkernel.com/)).\n- No other deal stories found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jump Capital | Vice President | ~2025 (inferred) | [src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/) |\n| Saaya Pal | co-author on most deal posts, including Standard Kernel, Realm and Above | 2025 | [src](https://www.jumpcap.com/insights) |\n| Jason Felger | co-author on conference posts | 2026 | [src](https://www.jumpcap.com/insights) |\n| Eliza Chamberlain | co-author on conference posts | 2026 | [src](https://www.jumpcap.com/insights) |\n| Anne Ouyang, Chris Rinard | founders of Standard Kernel, a portfolio company | — | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/) |\n| Aviv Nahum | CEO of Above Security, a portfolio company | — | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-above-security/) |\n| Amazon Web Services | former employer (solutions architect) | — | [src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/) |\n| Amgen | former employer | — | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/introducing-aqil-pasha-jumps-newest-vice-president/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did he source the Standard Kernel deal, or did Saaya Pal? What is the date of the seed, and who committed first among General Catalyst, Felicis and Cowboy?\n- Has Jump made an inference, KV-cache or GPU-scheduling investment since the October 2026 post invited founders?\n- What does his X feed show? Who does he amplify in the kernel and inference community (Hazy Research, vLLM, SGLang)?\n- Any podcast or conference talk? Re-run searches when the budget resets.\n- His exact check authority at Jump Capital (Fund 8: see the jump-capital lead).\n\n## People pass 2026-11-10\nUpdate made 2026-11-10. Web search was out of budget, so every page below was fetched directly.\n\n**Crux, revised.** Outside Jump's own blog, no source names him on any deal. On all three deals, the company or counterparty side names Saaya Pal or no Jump person at all:\n- Standard Kernel's seed announcement (2026-03-11) says the round was \"led by Jump Capital\", but the Jump person it quotes is Saaya Pal, Partner, not Pasha ([src](https://standardkernel.com/blog/announcing-our-seed-round-is-kernel-generation-solved/)).\n- Realm's About page lists its backers by person. The only Jump Capital person named is Saaya Pal ([src](https://realm.security/about-realm-security/)).\n- Above's $50M announcement names Ballistic, Merlin and Norwest as leads and CrowdStrike's Falcon Fund as a strategic investor. It does not mention Jump Capital at all ([src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/above-security-joins-microsoft-for-startups-pegasus-program-to-add-human-context-to-microsoft-purview-302928788.html)).\n\nHe is named on deals only through co-bylines on Jump's \"why we invested\" posts ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/), [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-realm-security-fixing-the-broken-security-data-layer/), [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-above-security/)). Read him as the analyst and thesis-writer on Pal's deals. No source shows him as the person who signs or prices them.\n\n**What he personally sources, signs or prices:** nothing sourced. His team page lists the companies he \"partners with\": Pluto, Above, Standard Kernel and Realm ([src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/)). Every one of them also carries Pal's name.\n\n**Whose money and who approves.** Jump Capital invests from fund vehicles. Fund 8 is $350M, writes checks of $1–4M and $8–15M ([src](https://www.jumpcap.com/insights/jump-capital-fund-8-three-shifts-shaping-ai)), and filed a Form D as JCDP-8 LLC. Jump Trading co-founders Paul Gurinas and William DiSomma are the executive officers on that filing ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2142408/000090514826003233/primary_doc.xml)). Who sits on the investment committee is not disclosed. Whether the LPs are outside investors or Jump Trading principals is also not disclosed: unconfirmed.\n\n**Every deal he is named on, with stage and timing:**\n| Company | Round | Date | Jump role | Who the company names | First in? | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Standard Kernel | Seed ($20M) | announced 2026-03-11 | lead | Saaya Pal (quoted) | **No.** General Catalyst published that it was \"leading the funding round for Standard Kernel\" on 2025-09-15, six months earlier. Whether that was the same round in two tranches or an earlier round is unconfirmed. | [co](https://standardkernel.com/blog/announcing-our-seed-round-is-kernel-generation-solved/), [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-standard-kernel) |\n| Realm Security | Series A ($15M) | 2025 (month not found) | lead; Glasswing Ventures and Accomplice participated, Presidio Ventures invested strategically | Saaya Pal (About page) | Jump's first round in Realm as far as found; earlier rounds not checked | [PR 2026-06-03](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/realmsecurity-more-than-doubles-customer-base-lands-global-materials-science-leader-as-enterprises-reclaim-control-of-security-data-302789568.html), [about](https://realm.security/about-realm-security/) |\n| Above Security | Undisclosed | Jump's entry round and date not found | \"an investor\"; not named on the $50M round | none | unconfirmed. The company launched publicly on 2026-03-03 with a Ballistic \"why we invested\" post. | [Jump](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-above-security/), [blog](https://www.above.security/blog), [PR](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/above-security-joins-microsoft-for-startups-pegasus-program-to-add-human-context-to-microsoft-purview-302928788.html) |\n\nNo re-ups were found for any of these companies.\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him.** His team page links X (https://x.com/pasha_aqil), LinkedIn (https://www.linkedin.com/in/aqilpasha/) and a protected email ([src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/)). His inference post invites founders building in this area to get in touch ([src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/inference-was-built-for-requests-agents-need-something-different/)). For a priced compute seed, Saaya Pal is the person the companies name, so a co-investor should approach both of them.\n\n**Fit, revised.** Still medium. He is a good diligence and thesis contact on kernels and inference. For $10M+ allocations and leads, the decision-maker the companies name is Pal. The 2025-09 General Catalyst lead also means Jump was not the earliest institutional money in Standard Kernel.\n\n**Open questions answered:** the Standard Kernel date is 2026-03-11. Pal, not Pasha, is the person the company names. Who sourced the deal is still unknown.\n\n## Sources\n- https://standardkernel.com/blog/announcing-our-seed-round-is-kernel-generation-solved/\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-standard-kernel\n- https://realm.security/about-realm-security/\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/realmsecurity-more-than-doubles-customer-base-lands-global-materials-science-leader-as-enterprises-reclaim-control-of-security-data-302789568.html\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/above-security-joins-microsoft-for-startups-pegasus-program-to-add-human-context-to-microsoft-purview-302928788.html\n- https://www.above.security/blog\n- https://www.jumpcap.com/insights/jump-capital-fund-8-three-shifts-shaping-ai\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2142408/000090514826003233/primary_doc.xml\n- https://jumpcap.com/team-members/aqil-pasha/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/introducing-aqil-pasha-jumps-newest-vice-president/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/inference-was-built-for-requests-agents-need-something-different/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/three-things-im-watching-at-kubecon-and-why-they-matter/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/robotics-is-entering-its-operations-era/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/the-datadog-opportunity-for-ai-agents/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-above-security/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-realm-security-fixing-the-broken-security-data-layer/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-pluto-security/\n- https://www.jumpcap.com/insights\n- https://standardkernel.com/\n", "person:arash-ferdowsi": "\n# Arash Ferdowsi\n\n## One-line take\nA quiet, high-volume founder-angel whose name is on Cursor's seed and on a long run of AI seed rounds, with no public voice at all.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why founders want him:** he is a technical co-founder of a company that went from YC to IPO, and founders list him as a peer. Reducto thanks \"founders we admire including Arash Ferdowsi (Dropbox)\" ([Reducto, 2024-10-02](https://reducto.ai/blog/seed-round)). Sujay Jaswa calls him the best judge of talent he knows (already in this repo).\n- **What constrains him:** he is a passive small-cheque angel. No board seats, no leads, no stated thesis found. There is nothing public to learn his criteria from.\n- **What he'd never do:** on the evidence, lead or price a round. Aggregators show one lead across his history (unverified).\n\n## The vehicle\nPersonal. Every announcement names him as an individual (\"Dropbox co-founder Arash Ferdowsi\"). No fund, syndicate, family-office name, Form D or Form ADV was found. One promotional blog says he is a visiting partner at Pear VC; this is not corroborated and is not recorded as fact. Cheque size is not disclosed in any source; aggregator estimates of $50K to $200K are unverified.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Cursor, before anyone else.** TechCrunch's seed story names only two angels: \"Dropbox co-founder Arash Ferdowsi and other angel investors\", beside Nat Friedman, when Anysphere had raised $11M in total ([2023-10-11](https://techcrunch.com/2023/10/11/anysphere-raises-8m-from-openai-to-build-an-ai-powered-ide/)).\n- **He follows specialist seed leads with research angels.** Vercept's $16M seed (Fifty Years lead) lists him with Eric Schmidt, Jeff Dean and Kyle Vogt ([GeekWire](https://www.geekwire.com/2025/seattle-startup-backed-by-former-google-ceo-lands-16m-to-automate-repetitive-tasks-on-a-computer/); read through search results).\n- **He is in UK and deep-tech rounds too.** The ledger has him in CuspAI's Series A through a Companies House filing and in Imbue's 2022 Series A.\n- **Volume.** VCBacked lists 53 portfolio companies, nearly all seed or pre-seed since 2022 ([aggregator](https://www.vcbacked.co/directory/investors/arash-ferdowsi)); PitchBook's public page says 83 (search summary).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High** as a signal and co-angel. He is early in AI-native developer and automation companies (Cursor, Reducto, Vercept) and in two lab-like companies (Imbue, CuspAI). He is not a source of large capital.\n\n## Background\n- Born 1985, Overland Park, Kansas; left MIT to co-found Dropbox with Drew Houston in 2007 ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arash_Ferdowsi)).\n- CTO of Dropbox from June 2007 to October 2016; left the company in 2020 (same source).\n- Accel's Sameer Gandhi met him and Houston in 2007, just out of YC (already in this repo).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | not found | |\n| Substack / blog | not found | |\n| X | not checked | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | not checked | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Two searches for interviews or podcasts about his angel investing returned only investor-database pages (2026-11-11).\n\n## How they think\nNo first-person material found. From the pattern of cheques: technical founders building developer tools, document and workflow automation, and AI infrastructure; many are Y Combinator companies (Reducto and Lemon Slice list YC as a co-investor); he comes in at seed beside a named institutional lead.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [TechCrunch on Cursor's seed](https://techcrunch.com/2023/10/11/anysphere-raises-8m-from-openai-to-build-an-ai-powered-ide/).\n2. [Reducto's seed post](https://reducto.ai/blog/seed-round): how founders describe him.\n3. [VCBacked list](https://www.vcbacked.co/directory/investors/arash-ferdowsi) (aggregator): the breadth.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI cheques. The ledger had seven before this update (Cursor, Reducto, Imbue, CuspAI, Laurel, Phonic, Reflex Robotics). Cheque sizes are not disclosed for any.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Seed or pre-seed? | Lead | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Imbue (as Generally Intelligent) | Series A ($20M) | 2022-10 | no (A) | see ledger | ledger |\n| Reflex Robotics | Seed (~$7M) | 2023-09 | seed | not stated | ledger (aggregator) |\n| Cursor (Anysphere) | Seed ($8M) | 2023-10 | seed | OpenAI Startup Fund | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2023/10/11/anysphere-raises-8m-from-openai-to-build-an-ai-powered-ide/) |\n| Reducto | Seed ($8.4M) | 2024-10 | seed | First Round Capital | [Reducto](https://reducto.ai/blog/seed-round) |\n| Vercept | Seed ($16M) | 2025-06 | seed | Fifty Years | new · [GeekWire](https://www.geekwire.com/2025/seattle-startup-backed-by-former-google-ceo-lands-16m-to-automate-repetitive-tasks-on-a-computer/) |\n| Laurel | Series C ($100M) | 2025-06 | no | see ledger | ledger |\n| CuspAI | Series A | 2025-08 | no (A) | see ledger | ledger (UK filing) |\n| Lemon Slice | Seed ($10.5M) | 2025-12 | seed | Matrix Partners, YC | new · [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/23/lemon-slice-nabs-10-5m-from-yc-and-matrix-to-build-out-its-digital-avatar-tech/) |\n| Phonic | Series A ($27.5M) | 2026-10 | no (A) | see ledger | ledger |\n| Elicit | Seed ($9M) | 2023-09 | seed | not verified | aggregator only |\n| Respell | Seed ($4.8M) | 2023-12 | seed | not verified | aggregator only |\n| Pilot AI | Angel ($2.2M) | 2023-03 | pre-seed | not verified | aggregator only |\n| Lavender | Series A ($11M) | 2023-02 | no | not verified | aggregator only |\n| Nim | Seed ($2.9M) | 2024-08 | seed | not verified | aggregator only |\n| Advex AI | Seed ($3.5M) | 2024-11 | seed | not verified | aggregator only |\n| JustPaid | $1.9M round | 2024-11 | seed | not verified | aggregator only |\n| Omi AI | Pre-seed ($2M) | 2025-01 | pre-seed | not verified | aggregator only |\n| BenchFlow | Pre-seed ($1M) | 2025-02 | pre-seed | not verified | aggregator only |\n| Paradigm | Seed ($5M) | 2025-08 | seed | not verified | aggregator only |\n| DianaHR | Seed ($3.7M) | 2025-09 | seed | not verified | aggregator only |\n| Wild Moose | Seed ($7M) | 2025-10 | seed | not verified | aggregator only |\n| Oneleet | Series A ($33M) | 2025-10 | no | not verified | aggregator only |\n| Warp | Series B ($60M) | 2026-06 | no | not verified | aggregator only (aifunding.me) |\n\nAll \"aggregator only\" rows come from [VCBacked](https://www.vcbacked.co/directory/investors/arash-ferdowsi) unless noted; none was checked against a company announcement, and none was written to the row files.\n\n**Hit pattern.** Cursor is the outlier: an $8M seed in 2023 that became one of the most valuable AI application companies. Reducto raised follow-on rounds quickly. Passes or regrets: none found; he has no public record of commenting on deals.\n\n## Deal stories\nNone documented in first person. How founders reach him is not stated in any source found; the visible pattern is YC and Dropbox-alumni networks (Sujay Jaswa's account of Dropbox's talent network is in this repo).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Drew Houston | Dropbox co-founder | 2007 | [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arash_Ferdowsi) |\n| Nat Friedman | co-angel in Cursor's seed | 2023 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2023/10/11/anysphere-raises-8m-from-openai-to-build-an-ai-powered-ide/) |\n| Eric Schmidt, Jeff Dean, Kyle Vogt | co-angels in Vercept | 2025 | [GeekWire](https://www.geekwire.com/2025/seattle-startup-backed-by-former-google-ceo-lands-16m-to-automate-repetitive-tasks-on-a-computer/) |\n| Emmett Shear | co-angel in Lemon Slice | 2025 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/23/lemon-slice-nabs-10-5m-from-yc-and-matrix-to-build-out-its-digital-avatar-tech/) |\n| First Round, SV Angel, BoxGroup, YC | funds he sits beside (Reducto) | 2024 | [Reducto](https://reducto.ai/blog/seed-round) |\n| Sujay Jaswa | ex-Dropbox colleague; praises his judgment of talent | 2010 | ledger |\n\n## Open questions\n- Cheque size and whether he invests through an entity.\n- Verify the aggregator-only AI seeds against company announcements (Elicit, Paradigm and Wild Moose first).\n- Whether the Pear VC visiting-partner claim is true.\n- Any interview at all.\n\n## Sources\nLinked inline. Aggregators are labelled as such.\n", "person:aravind-srinivas": "\n# Aravind Srinivas\n\n## One-line take\nThe Perplexity CEO is a steady small-cheque angel in AI infrastructure, chips and applications, and his cheques follow the investors and operators already around Perplexity.\n\n## Crux\nFounders want him because Perplexity is a large buyer and user of the things he funds (inference, voice, media generation, agents) and his name signals product taste. His selection is relational: he joins rounds led by his own backers (Nat Friedman and Daniel Gross, Kindred) and sits beside the same operator-angels. He does not lead, has no fund vehicle that could be found, and his cheque sizes are unknown; `check_capacity` sub-10m is an assumption. Time is the limit: he runs a company valued above $14B.\n\n## Differential insight\n- He is in both Kindred-led chip seeds three years apart, Extropic (2023) and Architect Labs (2026): \"Jeff Dean, Srinivas Narayanan, Lukasz Kaiser, Aravind Srinivas\" [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/architect-labs-democratizing-custom-chip-co-design/).\n- A founder on his advice: \"focus on being an AI company first (not just a sales company)\" (Supreet Hegde, Astra) [src](https://analyticsindiamag.com/ai-news-updates/perplexity-co-founder-aravind-srinivas-joins-ai-startup-astra-as-angel-investor/).\n- Guillermo Rauch and Jeff Lawson are beside him in the Julius seed [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/28/ai-data-analyst-startup-julius-nabs-10m-seed-round/) and again in Joyful Health's Series A [src](https://buttondown.com/raisetrack/archive/angel-registry-update-april-16-2026/).\n- His first known cheque, ElevenLabs' $19M Series A, was co-led by Nat Friedman and Daniel Gross, both early Perplexity backers [src](https://elevenlabs.io/blog/elevenlabs-launches-new-generative-voice-ai-products-and-announces-19m-series-a-round-led-by-nat-friedman-daniel-gross-and-andreessen-horowitz).\n- He is also an LP in Laude Ventures Fund I (ledger lp rows).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. He is not a source of allocation, but his seed cheques in chips (Extropic, Architect Labs) and infrastructure (fal, LiveKit, Prime Intellect, Daytona) are a useful early signal, and he is reachable through Kindred and Elad Gil.\n\n## Background\nIIT Madras; PhD in computer science at UC Berkeley under Pieter Abbeel; research stints including OpenAI; co-founded Perplexity in 2022 (ledger rels; [src](https://lexfridman.com/aravind-srinivas/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.perplexity.ai | |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | https://x.com/AravSrinivas | very active; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/aravind-srinivas-16051987 | from an aggregator newsletter [src](https://buttondown.com/raisetrack/archive/angel-registry-update-april-16-2026/) |\n| GitHub | not checked | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-06 — Lex Fridman Podcast #434 — https://lexfridman.com/aravind-srinivas/ — transcript exists; not mined for investing remarks\n- Logan Bartlett Show EP 97, Foundation Capital interview, Upfront Summit 2025 with Steve Jang — already in the media ledger\n- No episode where he talks about angel investing was found.\n\n## How they think\n- Product identity over go-to-market in the companies he backs (the Astra quote above).\n- Public statements about why he angel invests: nothing found. His X account was not searched post by post.\n\n## Read these first\n- Foundation Capital's interview on Perplexity (ledger media).\n- Lex Fridman #434 transcript: https://lexfridman.com/aravind-srinivas-transcript.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed in this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n13 sourced cheques; CB Insights counts 20 [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/aravind-srinivas), so about seven are unidentified. Seed-stage entries in bold.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors (lead first) | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ElevenLabs | Series A ($19M) | 2023-06-20 | no | Nat Friedman, Daniel Gross, a16z; angels Mike Krieger, Mustafa Suleyman, Guillermo Rauch | [src](https://elevenlabs.io/blog/elevenlabs-launches-new-generative-voice-ai-products-and-announces-19m-series-a-round-led-by-nat-friedman-daniel-gross-and-andreessen-horowitz) |\n| Extropic | **Seed ($14.1M)** | 2023-12-07 | no | Kindred Ventures; angels Garry Tan, Scott Belsky, Naval Ravikant | [src](https://theaiinsider.tech/2023/12/07/extropic-announces-its-14-1m-seed-round/) |\n| LiveKit | Series A ($22.5M) | 2024-06-04 | no | | ledger |\n| fal | Series A | 2024-09-18 | no | | ledger |\n| Pika | round not stated | | no | | [src](https://analyticsindiamag.com/ai-news-updates/perplexity-co-founder-aravind-srinivas-joins-ai-startup-astra-as-angel-investor/) |\n| Astra (AI sales agent) | angel, amount not stated | 2025-04 | no | | [src](https://analyticsindiamag.com/ai-news-updates/perplexity-co-founder-aravind-srinivas-joins-ai-startup-astra-as-angel-investor/) |\n| Julius AI | **Seed ($10M)** | 2025-07-28 | no | Bessemer; 8VC, YC, AI Grant; Guillermo Rauch, Jeff Lawson | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/28/ai-data-analyst-startup-julius-nabs-10m-seed-round/) |\n| Maximor | **Seed ($9M)** | 2025-10-01 | no | Foundation Capital; Gaia, Boldcap; Tien Tzuo | [src](https://ventureburn.com/maximor-secures-9m-to-expand-ai-finance-automation/) |\n| Fundamental | Series A (part of $255M) | 2026-02-05 | no | Oak HC/FT | ledger |\n| Joyful Health | Series A ($17M) | 2026-04 | no | CRV, XYZ, Designer Fund; Rauch, Lawson | [src](https://buttondown.com/raisetrack/archive/angel-registry-update-april-16-2026/) (aggregator) |\n| Architect Labs | **Seed ($24M)** | 2026-06-18 | no | Kindred Ventures; Jeff Dean, Lukasz Kaiser | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/architect-labs-democratizing-custom-chip-co-design/) |\n| Prime Intellect | Series A ($130M) | 2026-06/07 | no | ICONIQ, Radical, NVentures; John Schulman, Brendan Foody | ledger; [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/aravind-srinivas) |\n| Daytona | Series B ($60M) | 2026-10 | no | FirstMark, Pace; Patrick Collison, Brendan Foody | ledger; [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/aravind-srinivas) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Astra**: the founders, two IIT alumni, announced him as an angel in April 2025 and cited his product advice [src](https://analyticsindiamag.com/ai-news-updates/perplexity-co-founder-aravind-srinivas-joins-ai-startup-astra-as-angel-investor/). The same article says he committed $1M plus mentorship to Indian AI efforts.\n- No account of how he met the Extropic, fal or LiveKit founders was found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Kindred Ventures (Steve Jang) | lead he follows at seed (Extropic, Architect Labs) | 2023-12 | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/architect-labs-democratizing-custom-chip-co-design/) |\n| Nat Friedman, Daniel Gross | his backers; led the ElevenLabs round he joined | 2023-06 | [src](https://elevenlabs.io/blog/elevenlabs-launches-new-generative-voice-ai-products-and-announces-19m-series-a-round-led-by-nat-friedman-daniel-gross-and-andreessen-horowitz) |\n| Guillermo Rauch | co-angel in three rounds | 2023-06 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/28/ai-data-analyst-startup-julius-nabs-10m-seed-round/) |\n| Jeff Lawson | co-angel in two rounds | 2025-07 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/28/ai-data-analyst-startup-julius-nabs-10m-seed-round/) |\n| Foundation Capital | led Maximor; interviewed him | 2025-10 | [src](https://ventureburn.com/maximor-secures-9m-to-expand-ai-finance-automation/) |\n| Brendan Foody | co-angel in Prime Intellect and Daytona | 2026 | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/aravind-srinivas) |\n| Elad Gil | first investor in Perplexity | 2022 | ledger rels |\n| Laude Ventures | he is a Fund I LP | 2024 | ledger lp rows |\n\n## Hit pattern\nElevenLabs (in at the $19M A in 2023) and fal (A in 2024) are the clear early winners. Public passes or regrets: none found.\n\n## Open questions\n- Cheque size and whether he uses an entity or scout allocation.\n- The seven or so cheques CB Insights counts that are not named here; unconfirmed names seen in search summaries only: Nas.com, Eigen AI.\n- Which round his Pika investment was in.\n- Whether Perplexity is a customer of the companies he backs (fal, LiveKit, ElevenLabs).\n\n## Sources\nhttps://elevenlabs.io/blog/elevenlabs-launches-new-generative-voice-ai-products-and-announces-19m-series-a-round-led-by-nat-friedman-daniel-gross-and-andreessen-horowitz ; https://theaiinsider.tech/2023/12/07/extropic-announces-its-14-1m-seed-round/ ; https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/28/ai-data-analyst-startup-julius-nabs-10m-seed-round/ ; https://ventureburn.com/maximor-secures-9m-to-expand-ai-finance-automation/ ; https://analyticsindiamag.com/ai-news-updates/perplexity-co-founder-aravind-srinivas-joins-ai-startup-astra-as-angel-investor/ ; https://buttondown.com/raisetrack/archive/angel-registry-update-april-16-2026/ ; https://kindredventures.com/announcement/architect-labs-democratizing-custom-chip-co-design/ ; https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/aravind-srinivas ; https://lexfridman.com/aravind-srinivas/\n", "person:arjun-jain": "\n# Arjun Jain\n\n## One-line take\nA newly promoted NEA Partner (2026-02) whose edge is internal: Rick Yang credits his \"portfolio management practices\", not his deals. His AI exposure is Foresight (an NEA-led seed that NEA also uses as a vendor) and Neural Magic coverage ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-promotes-arjun-jain-to-partner-and-mason-murray-to-principal)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he runs NEA's portfolio-support and data machinery. The promotion release says he had a \"significant impact on the advancement of our portfolio management practices\" ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-promotes-arjun-jain-to-partner-and-mason-murray-to-principal)).\n- **Constraint:** no AI lead in his name. His CMS listings are mostly consumer and fintech (Mejuri, Gladly, GoodLeap, Ledge, Knotch, GRID), and several pre-date his arrival ([NEA CMS](https://www.nea.com/portfolio)).\n- **What he'd never do (by record):** nothing sourced.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **NEA invested in its own data vendor.** Foresight's $5.5M seed (2025-06, NEA lead; post by Faulkner, Tretikov and Jain) came out of NEA's own search for data infrastructure: \"NEA is no stranger to this dynamic, with volumes of data that span decades\" ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/foresight-the-data-ai-revolution-for-the-private-markets)). The post says NEA is building with Foresight \"as customers\" and has an internal task force of former engineers and data scientists. Jain's portfolio-management remit makes him the likely internal owner. That is an inference, not stated.\n- The Foresight founder, Jason Miller, \"has done this before at Greycroft, Point72 Ventures\" and BlackRock's Aladdin (same post). Faulkner was earlier Head of Data Science at Greycroft. A Greycroft link between the two is possible but unconfirmed.\n- His culture line: \"the concept of striving for a clean “karmic balance sheet.”\" ([src](https://www.nea.com/team/arjun-jain)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nLow for frontier AI deals. Medium as a back channel: as the partner running portfolio practices, he sees how NEA supports and marks companies across the book, which matters if NEA is your co-lead.\n\n## Background\n- Princeton University, BA in economics ([src](https://www.nea.com/team/arjun-jain)).\n- J.P. Morgan, Sales & Trading; then The Raine Group (merchant banking: TMT M&A and growth equity) ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-promotes-arjun-jain-to-partner-and-mason-murray-to-principal)).\n- NEA: joined 2021; Senior Associate 2023-05-16 ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-appoints-technology-investors-aaron-jacobson-and-vanessa-larco-to-senior-investing-team-roles-lily-huang-danielle-lay-and-luke-pappas-promoted-to-partner)); Principal 2024-06-13 ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-appoints-andrew-schoen-and-blake-wu-to-senior-investing-team-roles-promotes-alex-sharata-to-partner)); Partner 2026-02-03.\n- Private pilot (basic license in a 1962 Cessna 172C) and house/techno DJ, per his NEA Q&A.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.nea.com/team/arjun-jain | |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.nea.com/blog | 2 posts (Ledge 2023, Foresight 2025) |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/arjun-jain-50b0a810a/ | listed on NEA page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found.\n\n## How they think\n- Private markets are becoming \"a quant-style game\" where firms with data moats win (Foresight post, co-authored).\n- The founder-investor relationship is like a marriage: reliability, work ethic and humour in tough times (NEA Q&A).\n\n## Read these first\n- Foresight seed post (2025-06-26) ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/foresight-the-data-ai-revolution-for-the-private-markets)).\n- Ledge seed post with Rick Yang (2023-02-16) ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/ledge-automating-payment-operations-to-optimize-finance-teams)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nRows already in `data/rows/new-enterprise-associates-portfolio-deals.csv` (Foresight seed 2025, Ledge seed 2022).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Foresight | Seed ($5.5M) | 2025-06-26 | NEA lead (post co-author) | | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/foresight-the-data-ai-revolution-for-the-private-markets) |\n| Ledge | Seed | 2022 (post 2023-02) | NEA (post with Rick Yang) | | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/ledge-automating-payment-operations-to-optimize-finance-teams) |\n| Neural Magic | covered (CMS team; sold to Red Hat) | NEA first invested 2018 | n/a | | [NEA CMS](https://www.nea.com/portfolio/neural-magic) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Foresight:** NEA was the customer first (\"when we started searching for an end-to-end data infrastructure partner\") and then led the seed. Faulkner took the board seat ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/foresight-the-data-ai-revolution-for-the-private-markets)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| New Enterprise Associates | at: Partner | 2021 | [src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-promotes-arjun-jain-to-partner-and-mason-murray-to-principal) |\n| The Raine Group | at: merchant banker (dates unconfirmed) | before 2021 | [src](https://www.nea.com/team/arjun-jain) |\n| J.P. Morgan | at: Sales & Trading (dates unconfirmed) | before Raine | [src](https://www.nea.com/team/arjun-jain) |\n| Madison Faulkner | co-author, Foresight | 2025 | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/foresight-the-data-ai-revolution-for-the-private-markets) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Does he sit on any investment committee? Nothing announced; NEA's tech IC appointments named so far are Jacobson, Larco (2023) and Schoen (2024).\n- Who owns NEA's internal data and AI task force?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.nea.com/team/arjun-jain\n- https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-promotes-arjun-jain-to-partner-and-mason-murray-to-principal\n- https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-appoints-andrew-schoen-and-blake-wu-to-senior-investing-team-roles-promotes-alex-sharata-to-partner\n- https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-appoints-technology-investors-aaron-jacobson-and-vanessa-larco-to-senior-investing-team-roles-lily-huang-danielle-lay-and-luke-pappas-promoted-to-partner\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/foresight-the-data-ai-revolution-for-the-private-markets\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/ledge-automating-payment-operations-to-optimize-finance-teams\n", "person:artem-barsukov": "\n# Artem Barsukov\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. Every fact about him comes from aggregators: IVC Data, Massinvestor and MarketScreener. No primary source was found, so the employer stays **needs-id**. WebFetch returns model-summarized page text, so only short, distinctive strings are quoted. Seniority `principal` is my mapping of \"Investment Director\", the closest enum value for a sub-head CVC deal role. It is not a title any source confirms at Eclipse. `check_capacity: growth` reflects NVentures as a pool, which wrote at least $250M into Ineffable's seed. It is not a personal capacity, and it is void if the NVentures seat proves false.*\n\n## One-line take\nA possible junior-to-mid NVentures deal person under Siddeek. The supposed Eclipse-versus-NVentures conflict most likely isn't one: the dates read as Eclipse to 2021, then a Stanford MBA, then NVentures. He is worth one LinkedIn check, not a cultivation effort, until a primary source confirms the seat.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he might matter:** if the seat is real, he is one of only about four named NVentures staff below Siddeek ([sources/nvidia-check-writers.md](../sources/nvidia-check-writers.md)). NVentures is the NVIDIA pool that writes nine-figure checks into lab seeds such as Ineffable, Periodic and RadixArk ([firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md)). An investment director there would screen and run diligence on such deals. That is inference from his role; nothing ties him to a specific company.\n- **Constraint:** NVentures releases quote only Siddeek, and no NVIDIA individual is named on any 2025-26 frontier round in the repo ([sources/nvidia-check-writers.md](../sources/nvidia-check-writers.md)). He signs nothing publicly. At most he sources deals and builds the case for them.\n- **Background edge (inferred):** his Eclipse years (2019 to 2021) put him inside a physical-world and AI-infrastructure firm. That firm later co-built Bedrock Robotics, an NVentures portfolio company across three rounds. This overlap is an inferred bridge only. No source links him to Bedrock.\n- **What he would never do:** there is no evidence of personal angel checks or a fund of his own.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The two claims are probably sequential, not conflicting.** MarketScreener's positions table records his Eclipse role as \"Private Equity Analyst, ended 30/11/2021\" and shows \"No open positions remaining\" ([MarketScreener UK](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/ARTEM-BARSUKOV-A2VH60/)). Its education list includes a Stanford MBA (same source). Eclipse's own team page, checked 2026-11-09, lists no principal, associate or analyst titles, and he is not among the aggregator-only Eclipse names in the firm file ([firms/eclipse.md](../firms/eclipse.md)). That is consistent with someone who left in 2021 and inconsistent with a current Eclipse principal. The repo's \"Eclipse principal\" framing is the weaker reading: MarketScreener's prose says Principal, but its dated row says Analyst.\n- **Two independent aggregators place him at NVentures, not one.** Massinvestor lists \"Artem Barsukov, Investment Director\" next to Siddeek ([Massinvestor](https://www.massinvestordatabase.com/NVentures/investmentfirm.php)), and IVC Data does the same ([IVC Data](https://www.ivc-online.com/Google-Card?id=dad0240d-d667-f011-b819-00505695cd29)). This corrects the claim that only IVC Data names him. It is still aggregator-grade evidence, with no start date.\n- **Nothing from podcasts, X, Substack, conference panels, filings or company posts was found.** Three targeted searches on 2026-11-10 turned up nothing.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low** (as of 2026-11-10). He is named on no deals, his employer is unverified, and there is no public voice. He moves to **medium** on primary confirmation of the NVentures seat, such as LinkedIn, an NVIDIA blog byline, a company release naming him, or a conference bio. At that point he becomes a working-level route into the NVentures slot on big lab seeds (Ineffable, Periodic, RadixArk, Inherent). For the owner, the value is a co-investor contact on $20M-into-$600M-seed style rounds, where NVentures increasingly takes an allocation.\n\n## Background\n- Undergraduate degrees from the University of London and the Higher School of Economics; graduate degree from the LSE; Stanford graduate degree and MBA. All dates missing ([MarketScreener UK](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/ARTEM-BARSUKOV-A2VH60/), aggregator).\n- Eclipse Ventures: Private Equity Analyst, about 2019-08 to 2021-11-30 (start date from a search-engine snippet of a MarketScreener page, unverified; end date from [MarketScreener UK](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/ARTEM-BARSUKOV-A2VH60/)). The profile prose calls him a Principal. Search snippets mention earlier Bain & Company strategy work, but no page read here confirmed it, so treat it as unverified.\n- NVIDIA NVentures, Investment Director: start date not found ([IVC Data](https://www.ivc-online.com/Google-Card?id=dad0240d-d667-f011-b819-00505695cd29), [Massinvestor](https://www.massinvestordatabase.com/NVentures/investmentfirm.php)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | not a byline on blogs.nvidia.com as far as checked |\n| X | nothing found | searched 2026-11-10 |\n| LinkedIn | not located via search | the key verification step |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found (searched 2026-11-10).\n\n## How they think\nNothing sourced. Eclipse's mandate (physical economy, AI infrastructure, robotics) is the only proxy, and it is the firm's thesis, not his.\n\n## Read these first\nNothing of his found. For context, read [sources/nvidia-check-writers.md](../sources/nvidia-check-writers.md) and [people/mohamed-sid-siddeek.md](mohamed-sid-siddeek.md).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| none attributed | | | | | No NVentures or Eclipse release checked names him |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found.\n\n**How a founder reaches him:** no public channel. The practical route is the NVentures front door: introductions through Siddeek's network, or an NVIDIA Inception VC Alliance partner (see firm file). An Eclipse alumni introduction is a plausible warm path if the 2019-2021 stint is confirmed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NVIDIA (NVentures) | Investment Director (aggregator only; needs-id) | not found | [IVC Data](https://www.ivc-online.com/Google-Card?id=dad0240d-d667-f011-b819-00505695cd29), [Massinvestor](https://www.massinvestordatabase.com/NVentures/investmentfirm.php) |\n| Eclipse Ventures | Private Equity Analyst (prose: Principal), ended 2021-11-30 | ~2019 | [MarketScreener UK](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/ARTEM-BARSUKOV-A2VH60/) |\n| Mohamed \"Sid\" Siddeek | Presumed manager (head of NVentures) | not found | [Massinvestor](https://www.massinvestordatabase.com/NVentures/investmentfirm.php) lists both names |\n\n## Open questions\n- Primary confirmation of the NVentures seat and its start date. LinkedIn is the fastest check.\n- Whether he made Principal at Eclipse before leaving. MarketScreener's prose and its table disagree.\n- Which NVentures deals he worked on, especially robotics (Bedrock) or lab seeds.\n- **Name collision checked and rejected:** an OpenSanctions entry lists a Russian national with a near-identical Cyrillic name. Nothing links it to this investor. Do not merge the two.\n\n## Sources\n- IVC Data NVentures card: https://www.ivc-online.com/Google-Card?id=dad0240d-d667-f011-b819-00505695cd29 (aggregator)\n- Massinvestor NVentures page: https://www.massinvestordatabase.com/NVentures/investmentfirm.php (aggregator)\n- MarketScreener insider profile: https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/ARTEM-BARSUKOV-A2VH60/ (aggregator)\n- Repo: firms/nvidia.md, firms/eclipse.md, sources/nvidia-check-writers.md, sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md\n", "person:arul-kapoor": "\n# Arul Kapoor\n\n_Created 2026-11-10 from SEC Form D filings, Prod's IRS 990 and a 2023 Harvard Technology Review profile. Nothing he has said about Argo was found._\n\n## One-line take\nArul Kapoor is a Prod co-founder who turned Prod's founder network into Argo. Argo is a New York SPV shop that got $13–15M allocations in Mercor's and Etched's late rounds and is raising a $165M Fund I. He is one of the youngest GPs anywhere near $10B+ AI rounds.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins (inferred):** he helped build the network that produced Mercor and Etched. He was Prod's co-founder and president per a 2023 profile ([HTR](https://harvardtechnologyreview.com/2023/10/08/arul-kapoor-2023-top-ten-seniors-in-innovation/)) and its treasurer per the 990 ([990](https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/873826850)). Founders let him into rounds that institutional funds have to fight for.\n- **What constrains him:** no fund capital yet. Argo Fund I reported $0 sold against $165M as of 2026-05-14 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2134794/000090514826002284/primary_doc.xml)). Each deal needs its own SPV with 3–4 investors.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** price or lead a round. Argo only appears as a late, small name.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **SPV before fund.** Argo's first filed vehicle is \"Argo Mercor Series C SPV LP\" (first sale 2025-10-09, $13.002M, 3 investors) ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086863/000090514825003651/primary_doc.xml)). Fund I was filed seven months later.\n- **Etched within a day.** \"Argo ET 1 LP\" (Form D) first sold on 2026-07-24, the day after Etched's Series C post; it raised $15M from 4 investors ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2156772/000090514826004327/primary_doc.xml)). Reading \"ET\" as Etched is an inference.\n- **Ben Spector co-ran the first SPV.** The Mercor SPV's GP lists \"Benjamin Spector\" as a managing member alongside Kapoor ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086863/000090514825003651/primary_doc.xml)). Prod's 990 pairs \"Benjamin Spector (President)\" with \"Arul Kapoor (Treasurer)\" ([990](https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/873826850)). Spector is now Flapping Airplanes' CEO, and Argo later joined FA's backer list ([capture](https://web.archive.org/web/20260712223234/https://flappingairplanes.com/)). That the two Benjamin Spectors are the same person is inferred.\n- **Self-image (2023):** \"I see myself as a founder of a company.\" ([HTR](https://harvardtechnologyreview.com/2023/10/08/arul-kapoor-2023-top-ten-seniors-in-innovation/))\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. He is an emerging manager inside the founder network behind Mercor, Etched, Cursor and Flapping Airplanes, writing $10M+ late checks and raising a $165M debut fund. That makes him a co-investor, a source of SPV allocations, and a possible LP opportunity (Fund I). The deals that matter: Mercor C, Etched C and Growth, and the Flapping Airplanes seed.\n\n## Background\n- New York native; studied Computer Science and Math at Harvard with a concurrent master's in Statistics, and was named a 2023 Top Ten Senior in Innovation ([HTR](https://harvardtechnologyreview.com/2023/10/08/arul-kapoor-2023-top-ten-seniors-in-innovation/)).\n- Co-founder and President of Prod (MIT/Harvard nonprofit accelerator, formed 2021). Also co-founded Collab Rooms and interned at Alpine Investors, Point72 and Two Sigma ([HTR](https://harvardtechnologyreview.com/2023/10/08/arul-kapoor-2023-top-ten-seniors-in-innovation/)).\n- Treasurer of Prod Incorporated on its 990 filings ([990](https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/873826850)).\n- 2025–present: Argo (all Form Ds above). Jobs between Harvard and Argo were not confirmed. An aggregator (affluense.ai) says Alpine Investors, but that wasn't verified.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | none found | No Argo site; argo.vc is unrelated |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | https://x.com/kapoorarul | Search snippet shows Etched funding reposts; page returned HTTP 402, not read |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/arul-kapoor/ | Search title: \"Co-Founder of Prod\"; not read |\n| GitHub | not checked | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-10 — Harvard Technology Review — \"2023 Top Ten Seniors in Innovation\" written profile — [link](https://harvardtechnologyreview.com/2023/10/08/arul-kapoor-2023-top-ten-seniors-in-innovation/) — Prod's first cohort raised $25M at a $40M cap. No podcasts found.\n\n## How they think\nNothing public on investing found. From 2023: Prod is a nonprofit that takes no equity ([HTR](https://harvardtechnologyreview.com/2023/10/08/arul-kapoor-2023-top-ten-seniors-in-innovation/)). Argo is how that network's access now gets monetized (my inference).\n\n## Read these first\n- Argo Mercor Series C SPV Form D (who was in the first vehicle).\n- Argo Fund I Form D ($165M target).\n- Harvard Technology Review profile (2023).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed (X blocked).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mercor | Series C ($350M at $10B), via Argo Mercor Series C SPV LP | 2025-10 | no | Felicis (lead); Benchmark; General Catalyst | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086863/000090514825003651/primary_doc.xml) (inferred) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Seed ($180M), Argo named | 2026 (added Mar–Jul) | no | GV; Sequoia; Index | [capture](https://web.archive.org/web/20260712223234/https://flappingairplanes.com/) |\n| Etched | Series C ($300M at $10.3B), via Argo ET 1 (inferred) | 2026-07-23 | no | Sequoia (lead); a16z; Jane Street; Diffusion; SK Hynix | [Etched](https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference) |\n| Etched | Growth ($700M at $21B) | 2026-08-18 | no | Jane Street (lead) and others | [Etched](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one) |\n\nThese are firm-level (Argo) credits. Kapoor's personal role is inferred only from his signing every Argo filing.\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing public found. How Argo got its Mercor, Etched and FA allocations is unconfirmed; the Prod tie is the likely route.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Argo | Manager of GP of all vehicles | 2025 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086863/000090514825003651/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Prod | Co-founder, President (2023 profile), Treasurer (990) | 2021 | [990](https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/873826850) |\n| Quinn McIntyre | Co-manager on all Argo vehicles | 2025 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2134794/000090514826002284/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Ben Spector | Prod co-officer; co-GP of the Mercor SPV | 2021 | [990](https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/873826850) |\n| Robert Wachen (Etched) | Fellow Prod officer (director) | not dated | [990](https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/873826850) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who are Quinn McIntyre and Amol Punjabi?\n- Did Fund I close, and who are its LPs?\n- Which vehicle holds Flapping Airplanes and the Etched growth stake?\n- Did Argo take part in Mercor's $750M round (2026-11)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086863/000090514825003651/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2134794/000090514826002284/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2156772/000090514826004327/primary_doc.xml\n- https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/873826850\n- https://harvardtechnologyreview.com/2023/10/08/arul-kapoor-2023-top-ten-seniors-in-innovation/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20260712223234/https://flappingairplanes.com/\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one\n", "person:assaf-rappaport": "\n# Assaf Rappaport\n\n*Research note (2026-11-11): everything on his investing comes from Israeli business press (Calcalist/CTech, Ynet). I found no interview, podcast or post in which he talks about angel investing or AI in his own words, so there are no media rows and no first-person quotes.*\n\n## One-line take\nThe Wiz CEO turned his $32B exit into about 37 small angel positions and eight fund LP stakes, and in 2026 moved from security into AI infrastructure: Engram, Fundamental, Irregular, plus holdings in Anthropic, Decart and Cursor.\n\n## Crux\nFounders, especially Israeli ones, want the Wiz name on the cap table and the door it opens to Sequoia, Index, Cyberstarts and a16z, where he is also an LP. He is a passive spray investor: cheques of a few hundred thousand dollars, no boards. The limit is exactly that: no ownership, no pricing role, and a portfolio that mostly follows the firms that backed Wiz.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Cheque size and style: he \"invests no more than several hundred thousand dollars in each company\" and \"has largely adopted a passive investment strategy\" (Calcalist, 2026-07-15) [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rym9imnnmg).\n- Vehicle: a family office set up with the three other Wiz co-founders and run by Yotav Costica (Yinon Costica's brother, ex-More Investment House); name not public. Media assets (74% of Channel 13, 60% of Israel Bidur) go through Merit Spread, a public-benefit company [src](https://www.ynetnews.com/business/article/bksh1a44fe).\n- He is an LP in Cyberstarts, Sequoia, Andreessen Horowitz and Index, and in a pre-seed fund led by Merav Bahat: eight funds in all, five named [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rym9imnnmg).\n- With Bahat he is forming a $25-30M founder-led fund for AI infrastructure and AI security, \"designed to bridge the Israeli ecosystem with San Francisco's innovation hubs\", with Sequoia, Greylock and a16z participating (Calcalist, 2026-06-24) [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/ryxgc7kmgg).\n- Pace: at least ten reported cheques in the first half of 2026 [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/hytw6iqfze).\nRows: `data/rows/early-assaf-rappaport-insights.csv`.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. Not a lead and mostly security, but his 2026 cheques are in my categories (Engram memory layer, Fundamental tabular model, Irregular frontier security lab), and the Bahat fund will be an Israel to San Francisco feeder for AI-infra seeds. Worth tracking as a signal for Israeli research teams.\n\n## Background\nUnit 8200 officer; co-founded Adallom (sold to Microsoft, 2015) and ran Microsoft's Israel R&D; co-founded Wiz in 2020 with Yinon Costica, Ami Luttwak and Roy Reznik; turned down Google's $23B offer in 2024 and sold for $32B [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rym9imnnmg) [src](https://www.wiz.io/about).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.wiz.io/about | Company page with headshot |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | not found | |\n| LinkedIn | not used | |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNone found on investing or AI in this run.\n\n## How they think\n- No first-person material found. From behaviour: backs Israeli security founders early, often ex-Wiz or ex-8200, and co-invests with his co-founders.\n- Passed or regretted: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n- Calcalist 2026-07-15, \"Inside Assaf Rappaport's investment empire\" — the portfolio map.\n- Calcalist 2026-06-25, \"2026 deal spree\" — this year's cheques.\n- Calcalist 2026-06-24 — the Bahat fund.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot found.\n\n## Portfolio\nPress count: at least 37 active startups and 8 funds; about 25 are named. AI cheques found: about 15 (ledger had 6). S = seed-stage entry.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Lead / co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Atero (S) | Seed ($12M) | 2024 | No | Primary; lool | ledger |\n| Irregular | Series A ($80M at ~$450M) | 2025-09-17 | No | Sequoia; Redpoint | ledger |\n| Fundamental (S or A) | $30M seed + $225M A, announced together | 2026-02-05 | No | | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/hytw6iqfze) |\n| JetStream (S) | Seed ($34M) | 2026-03-03 | No | | ledger |\n| NewCore (S) | Seed ($66M with pre-seed) | 2026-06-15 | No | | ledger |\n| Engram (S) | First round ($98M at $600M) | 2026-06-23 | No | Sequoia; Kleiner; GC; Karpathy; Merav Bahat | ledger [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/ryxgc7kmgg) |\n| A Security (S) | Seed and follow-on ($37M) | 2026-06 | No | Yotam Segev | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/hytw6iqfze) |\n| Ocean Security (S) | Seed and A ($28M) | 2026 | No | | same |\n| Spirit Security | $50M, stealth | 2026-01 | No | | same |\n| Frame Security | $50M | 2026-05 | No | | same |\n| Anthropic | Not stated | n/a | No | | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rym9imnnmg) |\n| Decart | Not stated | n/a | No | | same |\n| Cursor (Anysphere) | Not stated; listed as an exit | n/a | No | | same |\n| Torq | Not stated | n/a | No | | same |\n| Aim Security | Not stated; sold to Cato for $350M | n/a | No | | same |\n\nOther named holdings (security or non-AI): Venice (A, 2026-02, IVP led), Genie Security ($3M, 2026-01; sold to Cyera for $130M), Deel, Zafran, PointFive, Descope, Salt Security, Dream, Guardio, and exits Armis, Talon, Axis Security, Noname, Dazz, Trail Security, Eureka.\n\nHit pattern: Cursor, Anthropic, Armis ($7.75B), Talon ($625M), Dazz (bought by Wiz itself). Who he follows: Sequoia (Irregular, Engram, Decart), Index (Venice), Cyberstarts.\n\n## Deal stories\nNo account of how a specific cheque happened was found. Structural path: founders reach him through the Wiz alumni network, Cyberstarts (Gili Raanan) or the family office run by Yotav Costica.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Wiz | Co-founder & CEO | 2020 | [src](https://www.wiz.io/about) |\n| Yinon Costica, Ami Luttwak, Roy Reznik | Co-founders; shared family office; frequent co-investors | n/a | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rym9imnnmg) |\n| Yotav Costica | Runs the family office | n/a | same |\n| Merav Bahat | Co-founding a $25-30M AI fund; co-angel in Engram | 2026 | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/ryxgc7kmgg) |\n| Gili Raanan (Cyberstarts) | Wiz backer; he is an LP; Channel 13 co-investor | n/a | [src](https://www.ynetnews.com/business/article/bksh1a44fe) |\n| Sequoia, Index, a16z | Wiz backers; he is an LP in their funds | n/a | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rym9imnnmg) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Name, GPs and first cheques of the Bahat fund.\n- How and when he got Anthropic and Cursor exposure (direct or SPV).\n- The three unnamed funds among the eight.\n\n## Sources\nhttps://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rym9imnnmg ; https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/hytw6iqfze ; https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/ryxgc7kmgg ; https://www.ynetnews.com/business/article/bksh1a44fe ; https://www.wiz.io/about ; ledger rows\n", "person:austin-liang": "\n# Austin Liang\n\n## One-line take\nLiang is the junior name on all three of ICONIQ's Anthropic rounds (F, G, H) and on Braintrust, Sierra, Databricks and Omni. The only post that credits them personally is a 2026 seed (Whirl AI), where Matt Jacobson calls them \"my partner on this investment.\" That makes them the working-level way into ICONIQ's AI growth book.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** they sit on the deal team under Matt Jacobson, the partner who co-signs almost every post they are on. Through that they are on the most important AI growth positions ICONIQ holds: Anthropic (lead on F, co-lead on G and H), Braintrust (lead on B) and Omni (lead on B and C). They are also on the firm's rare seed bet, Whirl AI.\n- **What constrains them:** they are never the senior name. Every post lists them after two to five partners or GPs, and there is no first-person writing, podcast or X presence from them. The capital is late-stage: ICONIQ met Anthropic in early 2023 but entered only at the $183B Series F in Sep 2025. On Sierra C and Databricks J, ICONIQ joined rounds others were already in.\n- **First in?** Only once: Whirl AI, where ICONIQ led the seed (2026-03-31). On every other AI deal they are named on, ICONIQ came in at Series B or later, after a16z, Greylock and others.\n- **What they'd never do:** nothing sourced. There is no first-person material.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Rank is hidden by ICONIQ's house title.** All 254 author-byline labels across ICONIQ Growth's 137 insight posts are one of three values: \"General Partner\" (85), \"Partner\" (94) or \"Investor\" (75). \"Investor\" covers everyone below partner, including Joe Fahn, Kelly Zheng and Shikhar Maheshwari. The only statement of their actual rank is a photo caption: \"ICONIQ Partner, Murali Joshi, and Principal, Austin Liang\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/dx-joins-atlassian-powering-developer-intelligence-for-the-ai-era), 2025-09-18). Posts through 2026-08-07 still say \"Investor\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/commerce-becomes-community-our-partnership-with-whatnot)), so the label tells you nothing about promotions. *Byline count from a crawl of the iconiq.com sitemap on 2026-11-10.*\n- **The one deal where they are credited personally is a seed.** The Whirl AI post opens: \"Written by Matt Jacobson with a special thanks to my partner on this investment, Austin Liang.\" ICONIQ led the seed ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/unlocking-its-transformation-moment-our-partnership-with-whirlai), 2026-03-31). The deal came from Jacobson's Snowflake board years: Jacobson met founder Sunny Bedi, Snowflake's former head of IT and previously acting VP of IT at Nvidia, at a Snowflake audit committee meeting. So it is a partner-network seed in AI-native IT automation, with Liang as the working partner.\n- **The Anthropic posts they co-signed show a 2.5-year courtship before the first check.** \"Since we first met Anthropic in early 2023\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-anthropic), 2025-09-02). ICONIQ then led the Series F, co-led the G ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/deepening-our-partnership-with-anthropic-the-claude-moment-for-ai)) and co-led the H \"for the third consecutive round\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/anthropic-series-h)). The ICONIQ sentences are firm voice, not theirs. Their bylines run from F to H, so they have been on the Anthropic team since at least Sep 2025.\n- **Repeat pairing on data infrastructure: Omni twice.** They co-signed the Omni Series B lead post (2025-03-14) and the Series C lead post (2026-04-23), both with Jacobson, Mariano Payano and Kelly Zheng. The C post says ICONIQ was \"doubling down and deepening our partnership\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/doubling-down-on-omni-building-the-governed-context-graph-for-enterprise-ai)). The thesis moved from the semantic layer to a \"governed context graph\" for enterprise AI.\n- **Braintrust's own post credits Jacobson, not Liang.** Braintrust's Series B post says \"Our Series B is led by ICONIQ\" and names Matt Jacobson ([src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/announcing-series-b), 2026-02-17). That suggests Liang does not hold the board-facing role. a16z (Martin Casado) led the Series A in Oct 2024 ([src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/announcing-series-a)), so ICONIQ was about 16 months behind a16z.\n- **Developer productivity: invested Mar 2025, exit Sep 2025.** They co-signed the DX investment post (2025-03-06) with Joshi and Jacobson ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/committing-to-the-future-of-engineering-intelligence-our-investment-in-dx)) and the Atlassian exit post 196 days later. The exit post says ICONIQ was \"DX's largest institutional investor\" and first met the team in 2023 ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/dx-joins-atlassian-powering-developer-intelligence-for-the-ai-era)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:** they are exactly the segment the owner named: a principal-level person at a fund that writes growth checks ($10M+), with sourced presence on a frontier lab (Anthropic F, G, H) and on AI infrastructure (Braintrust, Omni, Databricks). They are a plausible Series B/C partner for AI-native automation companies the owner seeds. Whirl AI shows ICONIQ will occasionally lead a seed for a founder a partner knows well.\n- **Against:** they are never first in except at Whirl. They are not the senior or board name, and Braintrust names only Jacobson. There is no first-person content to read their views from. They are not a lab-seed co-investor at the $600M scale the owner writes into, and ICONIQ came to Anthropic at $183B.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** Anthropic F, G and H; Braintrust B; Whirl AI seed (AI-native automation); Sierra C; Omni B and C; Databricks J.\n\n## Background\n- Earliest ICONIQ byline found: NinjaOne Series C, 2024-02-06, co-signed with Roy Luo ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/iconiq-growth-invests-in-ninjaone)). Start date not found.\n- Captioned \"Principal\" on 2025-09-18 ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/dx-joins-atlassian-powering-developer-intelligence-for-the-ai-era)).\n- Listed in the ICONIQ Growth team roll on the 2025 year-in-review page ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/2025-year-in-review)).\n- Education and pre-ICONIQ jobs: nothing found. LinkedIn (linkedin.com/in/austincliang, linked from ICONIQ bylines) blocked fetching, and web search was unavailable for this run.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [ICONIQ byline (Anthropic H)](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/anthropic-series-h) | ICONIQ has no public team-page profile; bylines only (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/austincliang/ | Linked from ICONIQ bylines; not fetchable |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. They are not named on the ICONIQ \"Uncommon Path\" video pages for Sunny Bedi (Whirl) or Ankur Goyal (Braintrust), both their deals ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/videos/the-uncommon-path-ankur-goyal-on-building-braintrust-and-helping-ai-teams-study-the-tape)).\n\n## How they think\nThere are no first-person views, so these are the theses on posts they co-signed (firm voice):\n- **Evals and observability as required AI infrastructure.** The Braintrust post says they are \"transitioning from best practices to essential infrastructure\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-braintrust-the-observability-layer-for-ai)).\n- **Agents as the new website.** The Sierra post: \"If in 1995 you needed a website, in 2025 you'll need an AI agent\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/revolutionizing-customer-experience-our-partnership-with-sierra)).\n- **Governed business context as the limit on trusting AI.** That is the Omni C thesis ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/doubling-down-on-omni-building-the-governed-context-graph-for-enterprise-ai)).\n- **Calls that worked:** DX (to Atlassian within about 6 months). Anthropic (entered at $183B; the Series H was at $965B per [murali-joshi](murali-joshi.md)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Whirl AI seed](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/unlocking-its-transformation-moment-our-partnership-with-whirlai): the only post that credits them by name, and the template for a partner-network seed.\n2. [Partnering with Anthropic (Series F)](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-anthropic): how ICONIQ got from first meeting in 2023 to leading the round in 2025.\n3. [Braintrust Series B](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-braintrust-the-observability-layer-for-ai): the AI-infrastructure thesis.\n4. [Omni Series C](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/doubling-down-on-omni-building-the-governed-context-graph-for-enterprise-ai): a follow-on thesis on AI and data trust.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. There is no public X or Substack presence, and LinkedIn is not fetchable.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| NinjaOne | Series C ($231.5M) | 2024-02-06 | yes | Frank Slootman, Amit Agarwal (operators) | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/iconiq-growth-invests-in-ninjaone) |\n| Sierra | Series C | 2024-10-30 | no | Greenoaks, Thrive Capital | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/revolutionizing-customer-experience-our-partnership-with-sierra) |\n| Databricks | Series J | 2024-12-19 | no | Thrive Capital, a16z | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/pioneering-the-future-of-data-and-ai-our-partnership-with-databricks) |\n| DX | undisclosed | 2025-03-06 | unknown | not named | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/committing-to-the-future-of-engineering-intelligence-our-investment-in-dx) |\n| Omni | Series B | 2025-03-14 | yes | not named | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/backing-omni-redefining-business-intelligence) |\n| Anthropic | Series F ($13B, $183B post) | 2025-09-02 | yes | Fidelity, Lightspeed (co-leads) | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-anthropic), [Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-series-f-at-usd183b-post-money-valuation) |\n| DX | exit (acquired by Atlassian) | 2025-09-18 | n/a | n/a | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/dx-joins-atlassian-powering-developer-intelligence-for-the-ai-era) |\n| Anthropic | Series G | 2026-02-12 | co-lead | GIC, Coatue (leads) | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/deepening-our-partnership-with-anthropic-the-claude-moment-for-ai) |\n| Braintrust | Series B ($80M) | 2026-02-17 | yes | a16z, Greylock, Elad Gil, basecase (returning) | [Braintrust](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/announcing-series-b) |\n| Whirl AI | Seed | 2026-03-31 | yes | not named | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/unlocking-its-transformation-moment-our-partnership-with-whirlai) |\n| Omni | Series C | 2026-04-23 | yes | not named | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/doubling-down-on-omni-building-the-governed-context-graph-for-enterprise-ai) |\n| Anthropic | Series H | 2026-05-28 | co-lead | Altimeter, Dragoneer, Greenoaks, Sequoia (leads) | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/anthropic-series-h), [Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h) |\n| Whatnot | Series G | 2026-08-07 | co-lead | not named | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/commerce-becomes-community-our-partnership-with-whatnot) |\n\nThey are also bylined on the Figma IPO post (2025-07-31) ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/celebrating-authentic-customer-obsessed-product-first-founders-figmas-ipo)). ICONIQ's Figma seed was in 2013, about a decade before their first byline (2024-02-06), so it is not counted as their deal.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Whirl AI:**\n  - Jacobson met Sunny Bedi at a Snowflake audit committee meeting while serving on Snowflake's board in 2020.\n  - Jacobson later recommended Bedi for GitLab's board.\n  - After Bedi left Snowflake, they talked on a walk in San Francisco about how AI could automate enterprise IT work.\n  - ICONIQ led the seed, with Liang as Jacobson's \"partner on this investment\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/unlocking-its-transformation-moment-our-partnership-with-whirlai)).\n- **Anthropic:**\n  - ICONIQ first met the company in early 2023 and introduced it to Fortune 500 executives along the way.\n  - It led the Series F in Sep 2025, then co-led the G and H ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-anthropic)).\n  - Liang's individual role is not described.\n- **Sierra:** ICONIQ says it has known Bret Taylor \"for over a decade.\" It joined the Series C alongside Greenoaks and Thrive ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/revolutionizing-customer-experience-our-partnership-with-sierra)).\n- **DX:** ICONIQ first met the team in 2023, invested in Mar 2025, and saw the Atlassian deal in Sep 2025 ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/dx-joins-atlassian-powering-developer-intelligence-for-the-ai-era)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Matt Jacobson (ICONIQ) | Senior co-signer on almost all their deals; called them \"my partner on this investment\" (Whirl) | 2024 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/unlocking-its-transformation-moment-our-partnership-with-whirlai) |\n| Murali Joshi (ICONIQ) | Co-signer on DX and Anthropic F, G, H; pictured together with Abi Noda | 2025 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/dx-joins-atlassian-powering-developer-intelligence-for-the-ai-era) |\n| Will Griffith (ICONIQ) | Co-signer on Sierra, Databricks, Figma, Anthropic | 2024 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/revolutionizing-customer-experience-our-partnership-with-sierra) |\n| Mariano Payano (ICONIQ) | Co-signer on Sierra, Omni B and C, Braintrust, Whatnot, Figma | 2024 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-braintrust-the-observability-layer-for-ai) |\n| Divesh Makan (ICONIQ) | Founder; senior name on Anthropic F, G, H | 2025 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/anthropic-series-h) |\n| Roy Luo (ICONIQ) | Co-signer on NinjaOne and Figma | 2024 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/iconiq-growth-invests-in-ninjaone) |\n| Joe Fahn (ICONIQ) | Fellow \"Investor\" on Anthropic G and H | 2026 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/deepening-our-partnership-with-anthropic-the-claude-moment-for-ai) |\n| Kelly Zheng (ICONIQ) | Fellow \"Investor\" on Omni B and C | 2025 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/backing-omni-redefining-business-intelligence) |\n| Sunny Bedi (Whirl AI) | Founder of the seed they worked on | 2026 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/unlocking-its-transformation-moment-our-partnership-with-whirlai) |\n| Abi Noda (DX) | Founder; pictured with them | 2025 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/dx-joins-atlassian-powering-developer-intelligence-for-the-ai-era) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did they join ICONIQ, and when were they promoted to Principal? (LinkedIn is the likely source; it was blocked.)\n- What do they personally own on Anthropic? Are they the day-to-day model or data person among the six names?\n- Who co-invested in the Whirl AI seed, and how big was it? Was ICONIQ's check over $10M?\n- What is their background before ICONIQ (banking, consulting, engineering)?\n- Do they have any public writing or talks? Web search was exhausted for this run, so this is not checked.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/iconiq-growth-invests-in-ninjaone\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/revolutionizing-customer-experience-our-partnership-with-sierra\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/pioneering-the-future-of-data-and-ai-our-partnership-with-databricks\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/committing-to-the-future-of-engineering-intelligence-our-investment-in-dx\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/backing-omni-redefining-business-intelligence\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/celebrating-authentic-customer-obsessed-product-first-founders-figmas-ipo\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-anthropic\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/dx-joins-atlassian-powering-developer-intelligence-for-the-ai-era\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/deepening-our-partnership-with-anthropic-the-claude-moment-for-ai\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-braintrust-the-observability-layer-for-ai\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/unlocking-its-transformation-moment-our-partnership-with-whirlai\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/doubling-down-on-omni-building-the-governed-context-graph-for-enterprise-ai\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/anthropic-series-h\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/commerce-becomes-community-our-partnership-with-whatnot\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/2025-year-in-review\n- https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/announcing-series-b\n- https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/announcing-series-a\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-series-f-at-usd183b-post-money-valuation\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h\n", "person:avery-klemmer": "\n# Avery Klemmer\n\n> Pronoun note: the brief for this profile said \"he\"; every source that uses a pronoun (SXSW London bio, Amherst event listing, Slush program snippet) says \"she\". This profile follows the sources.\n> Name note: Crunchbase lists the name as \"Avery Klemmer-Cashdan\" [src](https://www.crunchbase.com/person/avery-klemmer) (seen in search-result title only; page returned 403).\n\n## One-line take\nA low-profile Thrive investor who works across AI, infra and deep tech and, per Colossus, sits between portfolio companies like A24 and Anduril [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) — worth tracking as a window into Thrive's operating/portfolio layer rather than for public writing.\n\n## Crux\nA Thrive investor whose leverage is Thrive's own: huge, concentrated, patient checks plus pre-investment help that founders reward with scarce allocation. She shows up publicly as Thrive's face on Isomorphic Labs, the DeepMind spinout Thrive anchored at $600M before it was even raising [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/), and Colossus casts her as someone who bridges portfolio companies (A24, Anduril) rather than a single-sector picker [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/). Constraint: Thrive gives no individual credit for any deal [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/), so she has no personal track record, writing or check size on record. What she'd never do (inferred, unsourced): build a public personal brand apart from the firm.\n\n## Differential insight\nNothing found in her own words: no podcast, Substack, readable X posts or talk recording (searched 2026-11-09, including SXSW London video/recaps and the Amherst Student). What follows is the closest non-obvious material, mostly firm-level, mirrored in `data/rows/avery-klemmer-insights.csv`.\n- **Isomorphic panel (2026-06-03, SXSW London):** her only confirmed public stage was with Isomorphic President Max Jaderberg, moderated by NYT's Michael de la Merced. The session framed Thrive's role as \"how long-term investment can lay the groundwork for transformative scientific breakthroughs\" [src](https://sxswlondon.com/session/the-isomorphic-shift-accelerating-drug-discovery-at-digital-speed-6a1406d5). No recording or recap found.\n- **How Isomorphic happened (firm-level):** in fall 2024 Thrive heard Hassabis was weighing an outside raise; Kushner \"pitched Alphabet's leadership on allowing a tightly held round\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/). It became \"the first outside investment in an Alphabet spinout that wasn't raising\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/): $600M, Thrive-led, with GV and Alphabet, announced 2025-03-31 [src](https://isomorphiclabs.com/articles/isomorphic-labs-announces-600m-external-investment-round). Hassabis says Kushner told him \"Stop being so British about it.\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/). Thrive led again in the $2.1B Series B (May 2026) with Alphabet, GV, CapitalG, Temasek, MGX and the UK Sovereign AI Fund [src](https://www.isomorphiclabs.com/articles/isomorphic-labs-announces-series-b-investment-round).\n- **Turbopuffer allocation was earned (a company she lists):** founder Simon Eskildsen said on TBPN (2025-12-18) that Thrive was added because of what it \"has shown us over the past two years\", and that they \"compose the cap table the same way that we compose the team\". He rates investors by value added, with Thrive and Lachy Groom in the top tier [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-12-18/turbopuffers-simon-eskildsen-on-adding-thrive-capital-reaching-trillions-of-objects-and-building-a-concentrated-cap-table-like-a-team/transcript) (auto-generated transcript, so treat the wording as approximate). Her personal role in this is not confirmed.\n- **Role shape:** Colossus calls her \"an investor who bridges portfolio companies like A24 and Anduril\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/), which points to cross-portfolio work, not one sector.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Isomorphic's $600M first external round into a DeepMind spinout is the exact \"very large seed into a new lab\" shape in THESIS.md, and she is the named Thrive person on its public stage [src](https://sxswlondon.com/session/the-isomorphic-shift-accelerating-drug-discovery-at-digital-speed-6a1406d5). She also lists AI-infra names (Turbopuffer, ClickHouse, Databricks) and AI-native automation (Rogo, whose $50M Series B Thrive led in Apr 2025 [src](https://rogo.ai/news/rogo-announces-50m-series-b)) [src](https://sxswlondon.com/speakers/avery-klemmer-c8df16ca). Caveat: Thrive mostly writes growth-sized checks. Its Feb 2026 $10B fund reportedly set aside $1B for early stage [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/17/thrive-raises-10b-for-new-fund-its-largest-yet/) (per search snippet). She is a co-investor and talent signal, not a peer at my check size. Note: ClickHouse's $350M Series C was led by Khosla, not Thrive [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/05/29/clickhouse-reels-350m-high-speed-columnar-database), so her ClickHouse tie is \"works with\" only.\n\n## Background\n- Amherst College, B.A. Computer Science [src](https://sxswlondon.com/speakers/avery-klemmer-c8df16ca); class of 2016 [src](https://www.amherst.edu/news/events/calendar/node/935145) (event title \"i2i Alumni Talk with Avery Klemmer '16\", seen via search; page returned 405).\n- EY-Parthenon, Associate [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/avery-klemmer). Dates 2016–2018 appear only in a search-engine summary with no traceable source — (unverified).\n- FirstMark Capital, Investor, 2018–2020 [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/avery-klemmer); startupintros gives Jan 2018 – Dec 2020 [src](https://startupintros.com/people/avery-klemmer).\n- Thrive Capital, Investor (current) [src](https://sxswlondon.com/speakers/avery-klemmer-c8df16ca). **Join date not confirmed**: one search summary said Jan 2020 (no traceable source), which conflicts with startupintros' FirstMark end date of Dec 2020. Best estimate: joined around 2020–2021 (unverified).\n- Path into Thrive: no public account found. The Nov 2025 Amherst talk was billed as covering her path from Amherst to Thrive and Thrive's summer fellowship program [src](https://www.amherst.edu/news/events/calendar/node/935145) — content not published.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://twitter.com/averyklemmer [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/avery-klemmer) | Could not load (x.com paywall/402), 2026-11-09; follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/avery-klemmer [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/avery-klemmer) | Not fetched (login wall) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-11-16 — Amherst College i2i — \"i2i Alumni Talk with Avery Klemmer '16\" — [src](https://www.amherst.edu/news/events/calendar/node/935145) — billed topics: path from Amherst to Thrive, investing in tech, Thrive's summer fellowship. No recording or notes found.\n- 2025-11 (Slush, Helsinki, Nov 19–20) — Slush 2025 speaker — [src](https://slush.org/program/avery-klemmer) (page now 404; content known only from search snippet) — program bio said she focuses on companies at both infrastructure and application layers, especially teams pairing technical depth with product craft. Session title, format and co-speakers: not found.\n- 2026-06-03 — SXSW London (Truman Brewery, SXSW London Stage, 12:25–12:55) — \"The Isomorphic Shift: Accelerating Drug Discovery at Digital Speed\" — [src](https://sxswlondon.com/speakers/avery-klemmer-c8df16ca) — Panel with Max Jaderberg (President, Isomorphic Labs), moderated by Michael de la Merced (NYT), Health & Medtech track [src](https://sxswlondon.com/session/the-isomorphic-shift-accelerating-drug-discovery-at-digital-speed-6a1406d5) (update 2026-11-09). No recording or recap found as of 2026-11-09. Title ties it to Isomorphic Labs, a Thrive portfolio company she lists. Year inferred: June 3 is a Wednesday in 2026, and startupintros lists her for SXSW London 2026 [src](https://startupintros.com/people/avery-klemmer).\n- What she argued in any of these talks: **nothing found**. No transcripts, videos or write-ups located.\n\n## How they think\n- Only stated view on record: interest in companies across infra and application layers, favouring technical depth plus product craft [src](https://slush.org/program/avery-klemmer) (via search snippet).\n- Thrive context: Colossus reports Thrive credits no individual on any deal and says decisions are made as a team [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) — which explains the thin personal deal record.\n- Contrarian views / calls right or wrong: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\nNothing found — no essays, posts or transcripts located. Closest proxies:\n1. Colossus profile of Thrive (Oct 2025), which names her [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) — how the firm works.\n2. SXSW London speaker page [src](https://sxswlondon.com/speakers/avery-klemmer-c8df16ca) — her own list of portfolio partners.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X account exists [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/avery-klemmer) but could not be read.\n\n## Portfolio\nThrive does not credit individuals on deals [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/). \"Portfolio partners\" below are companies she says she works with, not proof she sourced or led the deal.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Doji | Seed ($14M) | 2025-05 | Thrive led (her attribution via aggregators only) | Seven Seven Six, angels | [round](https://retailtechinnovationhub.com/home/2025/5/16/personalised-fashion-shopping-startup-doji-bags-14-million-seed-round-led-by-thrive-capital); [her link](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/avery-klemmer) |\n| Isomorphic Labs | unknown | unknown | unknown | | [src](https://sxswlondon.com/speakers/avery-klemmer-c8df16ca) |\n| A24 | unknown | unknown | unknown | | [src](https://sxswlondon.com/speakers/avery-klemmer-c8df16ca) |\n| Databricks | unknown | unknown | unknown | | [src](https://sxswlondon.com/speakers/avery-klemmer-c8df16ca) |\n| ClickHouse | unknown | unknown | unknown | | [src](https://sxswlondon.com/speakers/avery-klemmer-c8df16ca) |\n| SpaceX | unknown | unknown | unknown | | [src](https://sxswlondon.com/speakers/avery-klemmer-c8df16ca) |\n| Turbopuffer | unknown | unknown | unknown | | [src](https://sxswlondon.com/speakers/avery-klemmer-c8df16ca) |\n| Rogo | unknown | unknown | unknown | | [src](https://sxswlondon.com/speakers/avery-klemmer-c8df16ca) |\n| Anduril | unknown | unknown | unknown | | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Isomorphic Labs (firm round detail) | First external $600M; Series B $2.1B | 2025-03-31; 2026-05-12 | Thrive led both | GV, Alphabet; then CapitalG, Temasek, MGX, UK Sovereign AI Fund | [$600M](https://isomorphiclabs.com/articles/isomorphic-labs-announces-600m-external-investment-round); [Series B](https://www.isomorphiclabs.com/articles/isomorphic-labs-announces-series-b-investment-round) |\n| Turbopuffer (firm round detail) | undisclosed | 2025-12-18 | not stated | Lux Capital, Lachy Groom | [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-12-18/turbopuffers-simon-eskildsen-on-adding-thrive-capital-reaching-trillions-of-objects-and-building-a-concentrated-cap-table-like-a-team) |\n| Rogo (firm round detail) | Series B ($50M) | 2025-04-30 | Thrive led | J.P. Morgan, Tiger Global | [src](https://rogo.ai/news/rogo-announces-50m-series-b) |\n| The Browser Company | unknown | unknown | unknown | | [src](https://startupintros.com/people/avery-klemmer) (aggregator) |\n\nDoji note: the Doji announcement names only Thrive, not any individual [src](https://retailtechinnovationhub.com/home/2025/5/16/personalised-fashion-shopping-startup-doji-bags-14-million-seed-round-led-by-thrive-capital). NFX Signal lists one investment for her but hides details behind login [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/avery-klemmer).\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found about her personally. Firm-level stories for companies she lists: Isomorphic (fall 2024 to Mar 2025, Kushner lobbied Alphabet to allow a closely held round) and Turbopuffer (Thrive added after about two years of help). See \"Differential insight\" above.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Thrive Capital | Investor | ~2020–2021 (unverified) | [src](https://sxswlondon.com/speakers/avery-klemmer-c8df16ca) |\n| FirstMark Capital | Investor (former) | 2018–2020 | [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/avery-klemmer) |\n| EY-Parthenon | Associate (former) | unknown | [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/avery-klemmer) |\n| Amherst College | Alumna, B.A. CS, '16; alumni speaker Nov 2025 | 2016 | [src](https://www.amherst.edu/news/events/calendar/node/935145) |\n| A24, Anduril | Bridges these Thrive portfolio companies | by 2025-10 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Max Jaderberg (Isomorphic Labs) | Co-panelist, SXSW London | 2026-06 | [src](https://sxswlondon.com/session/the-isomorphic-shift-accelerating-drug-discovery-at-digital-speed-6a1406d5) |\n| Ashwin Budhiraja, Yashoda Clark, Linus Lee, Sam Teller, Jed Feldman | Thrive colleagues named in the same Colossus sentence | by 2025-10 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- (2026-11-09) Is there a recording of the SXSW London Isomorphic panel? Check SXSW London's YouTube once it's posted. Did she personally work on the Isomorphic or Turbopuffer deals?\n- (2026-11-09) Isomorphic is reportedly in talks to raise about $5B at about $50B [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/isomorphic-labs-5bn-funding-talks-50bn-valuation) (search snippet, unverified). Watch whether Thrive leads a third time.\n- Exact Thrive start date, and whether FirstMark ended in 2020 or earlier (LinkedIn would settle it).\n- What she argued at Slush 2025 and SXSW London 2026 — look for Slush YouTube uploads and SXSW London session video.\n- Her role at Isomorphic Labs (the SXSW session suggests a close tie) and on Doji.\n- What \"bridges A24 and Anduril\" means in practice — a portfolio-ops/cross-portfolio role?\n- Which FirstMark deals she worked on.\n- Her part in Thrive's summer fellowship program.\n\n## Sources\n- (added 2026-11-09) https://sxswlondon.com/session/the-isomorphic-shift-accelerating-drug-discovery-at-digital-speed-6a1406d5\n- (added 2026-11-09) https://isomorphiclabs.com/articles/isomorphic-labs-announces-600m-external-investment-round\n- (added 2026-11-09) https://www.isomorphiclabs.com/articles/isomorphic-labs-announces-series-b-investment-round\n- (added 2026-11-09) https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-12-18/turbopuffers-simon-eskildsen-on-adding-thrive-capital-reaching-trillions-of-objects-and-building-a-concentrated-cap-table-like-a-team/transcript (auto-generated transcript)\n- (added 2026-11-09) https://rogo.ai/news/rogo-announces-50m-series-b\n- (added 2026-11-09) https://siliconangle.com/2025/05/29/clickhouse-reels-350m-high-speed-columnar-database\n- (added 2026-11-09) https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/17/thrive-raises-10b-for-new-fund-its-largest-yet/ (search snippet)\n- (added 2026-11-09) https://thenextweb.com/news/isomorphic-labs-5bn-funding-talks-50bn-valuation (search snippet)\n- https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/ (Jeremy Stern, Oct 2025)\n- https://signal.nfx.com/investors/avery-klemmer\n- https://sxswlondon.com/speakers/avery-klemmer-c8df16ca\n- https://slush.org/program/avery-klemmer (404 on fetch; search snippet only)\n- https://www.amherst.edu/news/events/calendar/node/935145 (405 on fetch; search snippet only)\n- https://www.crunchbase.com/person/avery-klemmer (403 on fetch; search title only)\n- https://startupintros.com/people/avery-klemmer (aggregator)\n- https://retailtechinnovationhub.com/home/2025/5/16/personalised-fashion-shopping-startup-doji-bags-14-million-seed-round-led-by-thrive-capital\n", "person:ayush-goradia": "\n# Ayush Goradia\n\n## One-line take\nNEA's New York principal for AI infrastructure and defense. He works on the firm's large defense leads (CHAOS Industries $275M C, Firestorm Labs $47M A) and on Databricks, Horizon3.ai and Clio. He was promoted in 2026-06 ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** Rick Yang: \"a distinctive ability to map a category from the top down\" (same release). His book leans growth and defense, where NEA writes its largest tech checks.\n- **Constraint:** like Kaplan, he has no NEA team page (404) and no CMS listing, so his named role in any round is not public (checked 2026-11-11).\n- **What he'd never do (by record):** nothing sourced.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **NEA's New York tech team has a Qatalyst pipeline.** Goradia \"spent two years at Qatalyst Partners in San Francisco\". Mustafa Neemuchwala, the 2025 partner whose coverage (CHAOS, Firestorm, Second Front, Veza) overlaps his almost exactly, also came from Qatalyst and UT Austin ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/new-enterprise-associates-announces-promotion-of-madison-and-mustafa-to-partner-1)). Neemuchwala has since disappeared from NEA's team page and every CMS portfolio entry, so Goradia looks like the successor on that book. The succession is inferred, not announced.\n- NEA led CHAOS Industries' $275M Series C (2025-05-05, Accel co-lead) ([src](https://www.abfjournal.com/ex-cia-directors-chaos-industries-lands-275mm-war-chest-to-disrupt-military-tech/)) and Firestorm Labs' $47M Series A (2025-07-22, including $12M J.P. Morgan venture debt) ([src](https://metrology.news/firestorm-labs-secures-47m-to-scale-point-of-need-drone-manufacturing)). Both are on his company list.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. Defense and AI infrastructure at growth stage. He is relevant if a lab you back sells into government or needs a growth co-lead from NEA's VGE fund.\n\n## Background\n- Houston native; University of Texas at Austin ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors)).\n- Qatalyst Partners, San Francisco, two years (same).\n- NEA: joined 2023 (New York); Senior Associate 2025-06 ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/new-enterprise-associates-announces-promotion-of-madison-and-mustafa-to-partner-1)); Principal 2026-06-29.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | none (no NEA team page as of 2026-11-11) | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not confirmed | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search for his name returned no NEA results.\n\n## How they think\nNothing in his own words found.\n\n## Read these first\n- NEA promotion release (2026-06-29) ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nNew rows in `data/rows/ayush-goradia-deals.csv`. Per NEA he \"works with\" these companies; his personal role in each round is unconfirmed.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CHAOS Industries | Series C ($275M) | 2025-05-05 | NEA lead | Accel (co-lead), StepStone, Valor | [src](https://www.abfjournal.com/ex-cia-directors-chaos-industries-lands-275mm-war-chest-to-disrupt-military-tech/) |\n| Horizon3.ai | Series D ($100M) | 2025-06-10 | NEA lead | | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/horizon3ai-leading-the-ai-vs-ai-era-of-cybersecurity) |\n| Firestorm Labs | Series A ($47M) | 2025-07-22 | NEA lead | Lockheed Martin Ventures, Booz Allen Ventures | [src](https://metrology.news/firestorm-labs-secures-47m-to-scale-point-of-need-drone-manufacturing) |\n| Databricks, Clio, Kaizen, Wonder, Veza, Second Front | covered | n/a | n/a | | [src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing sourced.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| New Enterprise Associates | at: Principal | 2023 | [src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors) |\n| Qatalyst Partners | at: banker (two years) | before 2023 | same |\n| Mustafa Neemuchwala | fellow Qatalyst and UT Austin alum; overlapping NEA coverage | 2023 | [src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/new-enterprise-associates-announces-promotion-of-madison-and-mustafa-to-partner-1) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did Neemuchwala leave NEA, and did Goradia inherit his board and observer seats (CHAOS, Firestorm, Second Front)?\n- What is his role at Clio (NEA's growth lead)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors\n- https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/new-enterprise-associates-announces-promotion-of-madison-and-mustafa-to-partner-1\n- https://www.abfjournal.com/ex-cia-directors-chaos-industries-lands-275mm-war-chest-to-disrupt-military-tech/\n- https://metrology.news/firestorm-labs-secures-47m-to-scale-point-of-need-drone-manufacturing\n", "person:baris-akis": "\n# Baris Akis\n\n## One-line take\nOne of the two people who own and approve everything at Human Capital ($4.4B, mostly non-US LP money); the only one named on a frontier-AI deal (Natural seed).\n\n## Crux\nAkis is a co-owner, not a deal-team hire: ADV Schedule A lists him as President, 25-50% owner and control person, with Armaan Ali holding 50-75% [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/296844/PDF/296844.pdf). No investment committee is disclosed, so a yes from Akis plus Ali is the firm's yes. He picks people the way a recruiter does, and the firm leads when it can also supply the hiring. He is not a frontier-model specialist; his named AI deal is an agent-payments seed.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Possible Boring Company board seat.** The Boring Company's Form D filed 2026-09-10 ($3.2B sold) lists \"Baris Akis\" as a Director, beside Antonio Gracias and John Hering [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1737630/000173763026000003/primary_doc.xml). No second source ties this director to Human Capital's President. Unconfirmed, but the name is rare.\n- **The investing arm is older than the \"talent agency\" story.** Form Ds list Akis and Ali as \"Managing Member of the General Partner\" of TCM II, L.P. ($25M offering, 2018-01) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1726020/000172602018000001/primary_doc.xml) and NTV II, L.P. ($75M, first filed 2019-07) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1782844/000178284420000001/primary_doc.xml). TechCrunch only covered the firm in 2020.\n- **Recruiter upbringing.** On the 2023 team page Akis writes that his mother \"ran an executive search firm in Turkey\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230810120235/https://human.capital/about).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. He can approve $10M+ checks and co-lead seeds, and Human Capital brings SPV capital to its deals. But his only named AI deal is application/infra (Natural), not a frontier lab. He is a peer co-investor or a source of hiring help, not a rising principal to back. Deals that matter: Natural seed (2025-10).\n\n## Background\n- Stanford graduate per TechCrunch [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/04/22/human-capital-a-talent-agency-for-engineers-picks-up-15-million-in-funding/). Degree and year unconfirmed.\n- Grew up in Turkey around his mother's executive-search firm [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230810120235/https://human.capital/about).\n- Co-founded Human Capital with Armaan Ali (ex-General Catalyst associate) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/04/22/human-capital-a-talent-agency-for-engineers-picks-up-15-million-in-funding/). Founding year unconfirmed (aggregators say 2015).\n- 2018-01: managing member of the GP of TCM II; 2019-07: same at NTV II (Form Ds above).\n- 2022-03: President title date on the ADV [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/296844/PDF/296844.pdf).\n- He got the seat by co-founding the firm.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://human.capital/about | Team page lists him as Co-Founder & President (archived 2023 copy has his bio) |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | none verified | |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- None found in this pass.\n\n## How they think\n- Backs people with \"fire in their belly\", then removes barriers and helps them hire (2023 team page bio) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230810120235/https://human.capital/about).\n- Says engineers and founders \"are actually building\" the future; the firm's centre of gravity is engineer-founders (same source).\n\n## Read these first\n- 2023 Human Capital team page bio (archived) — his own account of the talent lens.\n- TechCrunch 2020-04-22 — origin of the agency + fund model.\n- Human Capital ADV — ownership and who approves.\n\n## Social engagement\nNo public social accounts verified.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Natural | Seed ($9.8M) | 2025-10-23 | co-led | Abstract (co-lead), Forerunner, Terrain, Restive, Genius Ventures | [src](https://pulse2.com/natural-9-8-million-seed-funding-raised-for-building-financial-infrastructure-for-ai-agents) |\n\nHuman Capital returned in Natural's $30M Series A (2026-07, Forerunner lead), but no Human Capital person is named there [src](https://www.natural.com/blog/natural-series-a). Akis is not named on AIM, Humans& or Luel.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Natural seed (2025-10):** Human Capital was first in, co-leading with Abstract. Akis is quoted: \"We are excited to lead Natural's seed round\" [src](https://pulse2.com/natural-9-8-million-seed-funding-raised-for-building-financial-infrastructure-for-ai-agents). Pulse2 titles him \"Co-Founder and CEO\", which conflicts with the ADV and team page (President). The firm re-upped in the Series A.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Armaan Ali | Co-founder; co-managing member of TCM II and NTV II GPs | 2018 at latest | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1726020/000172602018000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| The Boring Company | Director (identity unconfirmed) | unknown | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1737630/000173763026000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Antonio Gracias, John Hering | Fellow Boring Company directors (if same person) | unknown | same |\n\n**How to reach him:** through the Human Capital team (human.capital), or via a co-investor from the Natural seed (Abstract, Forerunner). Co-investors should expect him to bring SPV capital and recruiting help. No public inbox found.\n\n## Open questions\n- Is the Boring Company director the same Baris Akis? A Boring or Human Capital source would settle it.\n- What were TCM II and NTV II invested in, and are they the pre-HCM funds?\n- Which AI deals beyond Natural he personally sponsors.\n\n## Sources\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/296844/PDF/296844.pdf\n- https://pulse2.com/natural-9-8-million-seed-funding-raised-for-building-financial-infrastructure-for-ai-agents\n- https://www.natural.com/blog/natural-series-a\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1737630/000173763026000003/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1726020/000172602018000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1782844/000178284420000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://techcrunch.com/2020/04/22/human-capital-a-talent-agency-for-engineers-picks-up-15-million-in-funding/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20230810120235/https://human.capital/about\n", "person:bastian-hasslinger": "\n# Bastian Hasslinger\n\n## One-line take\nThe London Index partner with the densest European book of AI-native automation deals: since January 2025 they have led seeds (nexos.ai, Ankar, Duvo, Arondite) and early A's (Scope, Conduct). Their founders are mostly repeat founders or ex-Palantir, and Hannah Seal is the usual senior co-signer.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** Hasslinger comes to deals through operator networks. Index already backed two founder groups before their new companies: Nord Security's founders (nexos.ai) and Rohlik's Tomas Čupr (Duvo). The other cluster is ex-Palantir forward-deployed engineers (Ankar, Conduct, and Arondite's CEO). Index then leads a large first institutional round ($8M to $15M) and follows on. Their stated filter is founders who \"execute intensively and attract exceptional people\" ([Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/bastian-hasslinger/)), plus a stated preference for ambition over category.\n- **Constraint:** Hasslinger rarely signs alone. Six of the eight AI posts carry a second, usually more senior, Index name (Hannah Seal, Jan Hammer, Stephane Kurgan, Sahir Azam). On Oratomic they are only tagged, under Martin Mignot's byline. In two deals they were not the first institutional money: Creandum backed Conduct before them, and Susa Ventures and Entrepreneurs First backed Scope before them. Once Index has seeded a company, other funds have led the next round (Atomico led Ankar's A, Balderton led Duvo's A).\n- **What they'd never do (inferred):** Lead a pre-training lab or chip company. Every AI deal on their page is the application or orchestration layer. Their only deep-tech exposure (Oratomic, Cowboy Space) comes through rounds led by other Index partners.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Palantir pattern is real, and nobody has written it down.** Ankar's founders \"previously built mission-critical software at Palantir\" ([Index, 2025-12-17](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ankar-raises-20m-series-a-to-accelerate-and-protect-innovation/)). Conduct's founders were senior leaders at Palantir ([Index, 2026-06-17](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/)). Arondite's CEO Will Blyth worked at Palantir and Helsing ([Index, 2025-05-01](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/)). That is three of seven AI companies with founders from the same employer.\n- **Index was in Ankar first and stayed in.** Index led the £3M seed in May 2025 ([Latham](https://www.lw.com/en/news/2025/06/latham-advises-index-ventures-on-ankar-seed-round), [Ankar](https://ankar.ai/blog/fortune-exclusive-palantir-alums-using-ai-to-streamline-patent-filing-secure-20-million-in-series-a-venture-funding)). Atomico led the $20M A seven months later, with Index following. Index's own A post doesn't say who led.\n- **Repeat-founder sourcing loop.** In the Duvo post, Index says \"We backed Tomas Čupr when he built Rohlik\" ([Index, 2025-12-02](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/duvo-raises-15m-to-give-retail-teams-an-ai-workforce-that-goes-live-in-weeks-cutting-manual-work-by-40/)). nexos.ai is Nord Security's founders' new company. Nexos co-founder Tomas Okmanas then put angel money into Nomos, which Hasslinger led ([Tech Funding News](https://techfundingnews.com/nomos-raises-20m-index-ventures-europe-energy-costs/)). The portfolio feeds itself.\n- **A decade-old relationship led to Nomos.** The Nomos post says: \"I first met co-founder Stefan almost a decade ago\" ([Index, 2026-09-15](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-a-new-energy-model-for-europe-our-investment-in-nomos/)). The byline is Hasslinger and Jan Hammer, and the post doesn't say which of them is speaking. Speedinvest led Nomos's pre-seed in 2024, so Index was not first ([search summary of Seedtable/Nordic9](https://nordic9.com/news/nomos-energy-closed-a-seed-round-led-by-speed/)).\n- **What they say they look for in founders.** In the Scope post: \"There's a type of founder we keep coming back to at Index\" ([BusinessCloud, 2026-05-20](https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/)). The rest of the passage is about founders who are obsessed with the problem; Scope's founders cold-called inspection firms and went out on inspections themselves. The Index post adds: \"Where they have conviction, they move with extraordinary speed\" ([Index, 2026-05-19](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/)).\n- **A thesis against horizontal enterprise AI.** On Conduct: \"The tighter the fit, the sharper the edge\" ([Index, 2026-06-17](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/)). On Duvo: \"Duvo inverts this completely\" (Hammer and Hasslinger, contrasting Duvo with horizontal tools that hunt for vertical use cases) ([Index, 2025-12-02](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/duvo-raises-15m-to-give-retail-teams-an-ai-workforce-that-goes-live-in-weeks-cutting-manual-work-by-40/)).\n- **Their writing view on AI and team size.** \"In the next 12 months, 84% of founders expect headcount to increase\" (Index/Slush survey of 600+ founders, co-written with Hannah Seal; [Index, 2024-11-21](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/scaling-through-chaos-in-the-age-of-ai/)). This was the basis of the Slush 2024 \"Scaling Through Chaos\" keynote shown on their team page.\n- **No podcasts and no X activity found.** Their X handle (@bastianhasslin1) is listed on the team page, but X returned a 402 and I couldn't read posts. Searches found no podcast or long-form interview. Their public voice is almost entirely co-written Index Perspectives.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High for AI-native automation; low for labs and chips.**\n- **Relevant to me:** nexos.ai (Index led the $8M seed and co-led the €30M A), Duvo (Index led the $15M seed, a retail AI workforce, Serval-type), Ankar (Index led the seed), Scope (Index led $20M; CEO Jonny Low reportedly came from the AI lab Conjecture, per one search summary, unconfirmed), and Conduct ($60M A co-led with ICONIQ).\n- **Why:** They lead $8M to $20M first institutional rounds into agent and automation companies in Europe. That lane overlaps mine directly; I'd be a co-investor or a competitor for allocation.\n- **Off-lens:** Oratomic (quantum) and Cowboy Space (orbital data centres) are compute-adjacent, but Hasslinger isn't the lead name on either.\n- **How useful they are to me:** a way into Europe's ex-Palantir and repeat-founder agent companies at seed. They are not a source of frontier-lab deals.\n\n## Background\n- Education: B.Sc. at KIT and M.Sc. at ETH Zurich ([Index](https://www.indexventures.com/team/bastian-hasslinger/)).\n- Search snippets of the Index page say they were one of the first team members at Alasco, a Munich construction software company. The current team page doesn't mention it (unconfirmed).\n- Picus Capital: led the Berlin office, investing at pre-seed and seed ([Index](https://www.indexventures.com/team/bastian-hasslinger/)). An aggregator says they covered Northern Europe from Munich, Berlin and Copenhagen (unverified).\n- Index Ventures (UK) LLP, Partner since 2023, per [MarketScreener](https://marketscreener.com/insider/BASTIAN-HASSLINGER-A3SJDH) (aggregator).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/bastian-hasslinger/ | Team page lists 10 companies |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/team-member/bastian-hasslinger/ | About 12 co-written Index Perspectives, 2024-11 to 2026-10 |\n| X | https://twitter.com/bastianhasslin1 | Couldn't be read (HTTP 402), 2026-11-10 |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/bastian-hasslinger/ | Not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-11 — Slush (Helsinki) — \"Scaling Through Chaos\" keynote, per the photo caption on the [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/bastian-hasslinger/). The companion write-up, co-written with Hannah Seal, is [Scaling Through Chaos in the Age of AI](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/scaling-through-chaos-in-the-age-of-ai/). Its argument: AI changes which roles startups hire for, not how many people they hire.\n- No podcast appearances found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Vertical depth over horizontal AI tools.** Enterprise-specific processes are a moat, not technical debt (Conduct). Agents should be briefed like trusted team members (Duvo).\n- **Founder obsession and speed.** They back founders who learned the industry by hand (Scope's cold-calls and site visits) and who move fast once convinced.\n- **Ambition over category.** The team page also lists quantum and space among their areas.\n- **Calls so far:** It's too early to judge outcomes. Duvo raised a €28.7M A led by Balderton ten months after Index's seed ([The Recursive, 2026-10-14](https://www.therecursive.com/rohlik-founder-tomas-cuprs-duvo-raises-eur28-7m-series-a-to-take-enterprise-ai-agents-to-the-us/)). Wordsmith hit a $100M valuation at its A ([Legal Technology](https://legaltechnology.com/wordsmith-ai-raises-25m-series-a-and-hits-100m-valuation-in-record-time/)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Conduct: a world where corporates move at startup speed](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/) — the clearest statement of the bet against horizontal enterprise AI.\n2. [Our investment in Scope](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/) — what they look for in founders.\n3. [Our investment in Nomos](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-a-new-energy-model-for-europe-our-investment-in-nomos/) — a decade-long relationship, and a company described as AI-native.\n4. [Scaling Through Chaos in the Age of AI](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/scaling-through-chaos-in-the-age-of-ai/) — the survey-based view on AI and headcount.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: X couldn't be read and LinkedIn wasn't fetched.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Wordsmith AI | $5M (2024, labelled seed in press) | 2024-06 | Index led | General Catalyst; Gareth Williams | [Legal Technology](https://legaltechnology.com/wordsmith-ai-raises-25m-series-a-and-hits-100m-valuation-in-record-time/) |\n| nexos.ai | Seed $8M | 2025-01-15 | Index led (Perspective by Hannah Seal + Hasslinger) | Creandum; Dig Ventures; angels Pomel, Siemiatkowski (Flat Capital), Paananen, Abrahami | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/nexosai-emerges-from-stealth-to-launch-ai-orchestration-platform-for-enterprise/) |\n| Arondite | Seed $12M | 2025-05-01 | Index led (Seal, Kurgan, Hasslinger); Concept Ventures and Creator Fund pre-seed first | Concept Ventures; Creator Fund | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/), [Calibre](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/) |\n| Ankar | Seed £3M | 2025-05-28 | Index led (Seal + Hasslinger) | Daphni; Motier Ventures; Booom; Puzzle Ventures; angels Pomel, Chaumond | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/from-idea-to-patent-ankar-ai-raises-3m-to-transform-rd/), [Latham](https://www.lw.com/en/news/2025/06/latham-advises-index-ventures-on-ankar-seed-round) |\n| Wordsmith AI | Series A $25M | 2025-06-03 | Index led (Seal + Hasslinger) | Scottish Enterprise | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/wordsmith-ai-raises-25m-series-a-to-deploy-ai-agents-and-transform-legal-roles/) |\n| nexos.ai | Series A €30M | 2025-10-21 | Co-led with Evantic Capital (Hasslinger + Seal) | Creandum; Dig Ventures | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/nexosai-raises-30m-series-a-to-expedite-enterprise-ai-adoption/) |\n| Duvo | Seed $15M | 2025-12-02 | Index led (Jan Hammer + Hasslinger) | Credo; Northzone; Puzzle; angels Reznik, Singleton, Flanagan | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/duvo-raises-15m-to-give-retail-teams-an-ai-workforce-that-goes-live-in-weeks-cutting-manual-work-by-40/), [TFN](https://techfundingnews.com/duvo-ai-retail-automation-seed-funding/) |\n| Ankar | Series A $20M | 2025-12-17 | No, Atomico led (Hasslinger sole author of Index post) | Atomico; Norrsken VC; Daphni | [Ankar](https://ankar.ai/blog/fortune-exclusive-palantir-alums-using-ai-to-streamline-patent-filing-secure-20-million-in-series-a-venture-funding) |\n| Scope | $20M (press calls it a Series A) | 2026-05-19 | Index led (Kurgan + Hasslinger) | Susa Ventures; Entrepreneurs First; Syndicate 1 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/) |\n| Conduct | Series A $60M | 2026-06-17 | Co-led with ICONIQ (Sahir Azam + Hasslinger) | ICONIQ; SAP; Creandum; Lucid Capital; Booom | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/), [WebProNews](https://www.webpronews.com/ex-palantir-engineers-land-60-million-to-crack-enterprise-code-before-sap-deadline-hits/) |\n| Nomos | €20M (unlabelled) | 2026-09-15 | Index led (Hasslinger + Hammer) | Speedinvest; Markus Villig; Daniel Dines; Tomas Okmanas | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-a-new-energy-model-for-europe-our-investment-in-nomos/), [TFN](https://techfundingnews.com/nomos-raises-20m-index-ventures-europe-energy-costs/) |\n| Duvo | Series A €28.7M | 2026-10-14 | No, Balderton led; Index followed on | Balderton; Northzone; Credo | [The Recursive](https://www.therecursive.com/rohlik-founder-tomas-cuprs-duvo-raises-eur28-7m-series-a-to-take-enterprise-ai-agents-to-the-us/) |\n| Oratomic (quantum) | Series A $300M (Jul) / later round (Oct) | 2026-10-08 | Tagged only; Mignot authored the post | ARCH; Spark; Khosla; GC; Bezos Expeditions | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/making-quantum-computing-real-our-investment-in-oratomic/), [TQI](https://thequantuminsider.com/2026/10/08/oratomic-secures-775-million-fault-tolerant-quantum-computers) |\n| Cowboy Space (ex-Aetherflux) | Series B $275M | 2026-05 | Index led; Jan Hammer is the named partner; Hasslinger's role not found | BEV; IVP; Construct; SAIC; NEA; a16z | [Hasslinger team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/bastian-hasslinger/), [pulse.bot](https://www.pulse.bot/venture-capital/news/cowboy-space-secures-275m-series-b-dac81192-2785-441d-899d-30203028658d/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **nexos.ai:** Index led the $8M seed when the company came out of stealth in January 2025. Nine months later it co-led the €30M A with Evantic. Both posts pair Hasslinger with Hannah Seal. Index was the first institutional lead. Creandum and Dig Ventures followed into the seed and the A.\n- **Ankar:** Hasslinger, Seal and \"Carlos\" (presumably another Index investor) were impressed \"from the first time\" they met the founders ([Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/from-idea-to-patent-ankar-ai-raises-3m-to-transform-rd/)). Index led the seed; seven months later Atomico led the A.\n- **Duvo:** This was a follow-the-founder deal from the Rohlik relationship, with Jan Hammer as the senior name. Index led the seed; Balderton led the A ten months later.\n- **Scope:** Index came in late. Susa Ventures and Entrepreneurs First were the first backers and \"doubled down in every round\" (founder quote reported in press). Index led the $20M round about two years after founding.\n- **Conduct:** Creandum, Lucid and Booom were earlier. Index entered at the A, co-leading with ICONIQ alongside SAP as a strategic investor. Sahir Azam (ex-MongoDB) is the senior co-signer; Emil Schaefer and Hannah Seal are also tagged.\n- **Nomos:** Sifted reported in June 2026 that Index was in talks to lead. The round was announced on 2026-09-15. One of the two authors had known the CTO-side co-founder Stefan Gerbes for about ten years.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hannah Seal (Index) | Most frequent co-author: nexos x2, Wordsmith, Ankar seed, Arondite, Slush report | 2024 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/team-member/bastian-hasslinger/3/) |\n| Jan Hammer (Index) | Co-signer on Duvo and Nomos | 2025 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-a-new-energy-model-for-europe-our-investment-in-nomos/) |\n| Stephane Kurgan (Index) | Co-signer on Scope and Arondite | 2025 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/) |\n| Sahir Azam (Index) | Co-signer on Conduct | 2026 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/) |\n| Martin Mignot, Simon Pastor (Index) | Co-tagged on Oratomic | 2026 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/making-quantum-computing-real-our-investment-in-oratomic/) |\n| Tomas Okmanas (Nord Security, nexos.ai) | Portfolio founder; angel in Hasslinger's Nomos deal | 2025 | [TFN](https://techfundingnews.com/nomos-raises-20m-index-ventures-europe-energy-costs/) |\n| Tomas Čupr (Rohlik, Duvo) | Repeat Index founder | 2025 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/duvo-raises-15m-to-give-retail-teams-an-ai-workforce-that-goes-live-in-weeks-cutting-manual-work-by-40/) |\n| Stefan Gerbes (Nomos) | Known for about ten years (byline shared with Hammer) | about 2016 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-a-new-energy-model-for-europe-our-investment-in-nomos/) |\n| Picus Capital | Former employer; led the Berlin office | before 2023 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/team/bastian-hasslinger/) |\n| Creandum | Repeated co-investor (nexos seed and A, Conduct) | 2025 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/nexosai-emerges-from-stealth-to-launch-ai-orchestration-platform-for-enterprise/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which of Hasslinger and Hammer met Stefan Gerbes ten years ago? If it was Hasslinger, was the company Alasco?\n- Does Scope CEO Jonny Low really come from Conjecture, the AI lab? One search summary says so and BusinessCloud doesn't mention it. If true, it's a path to London lab alumni.\n- What is Hasslinger's role on Cowboy Space? Jan Hammer is the named lead.\n- What does @bastianhasslin1 post on X?\n- Promotion timeline: did they join Index as a partner, or were they promoted in or before 2023?\n- Who is \"Carlos\" on the Ankar seed post?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/bastian-hasslinger/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/team-member/bastian-hasslinger/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/team-member/bastian-hasslinger/2/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/team-member/bastian-hasslinger/3/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/nexosai-emerges-from-stealth-to-launch-ai-orchestration-platform-for-enterprise/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/nexosai-raises-30m-series-a-to-expedite-enterprise-ai-adoption/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/from-idea-to-patent-ankar-ai-raises-3m-to-transform-rd/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ankar-raises-20m-series-a-to-accelerate-and-protect-innovation/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/wordsmith-ai-raises-25m-series-a-to-deploy-ai-agents-and-transform-legal-roles/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/duvo-raises-15m-to-give-retail-teams-an-ai-workforce-that-goes-live-in-weeks-cutting-manual-work-by-40/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-a-new-energy-model-for-europe-our-investment-in-nomos/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/making-quantum-computing-real-our-investment-in-oratomic/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/scaling-through-chaos-in-the-age-of-ai/\n- https://ankar.ai/blog/fortune-exclusive-palantir-alums-using-ai-to-streamline-patent-filing-secure-20-million-in-series-a-venture-funding\n- https://www.lw.com/en/news/2025/06/latham-advises-index-ventures-on-ankar-seed-round\n- https://techfundingnews.com/duvo-ai-retail-automation-seed-funding/\n- https://www.therecursive.com/rohlik-founder-tomas-cuprs-duvo-raises-eur28-7m-series-a-to-take-enterprise-ai-agents-to-the-us/\n- https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/\n- https://www.webpronews.com/ex-palantir-engineers-land-60-million-to-crack-enterprise-code-before-sap-deadline-hits/\n- https://techfundingnews.com/nomos-raises-20m-index-ventures-europe-energy-costs/\n- https://nordic9.com/news/nomos-energy-closed-a-seed-round-led-by-speed/\n- https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/\n- https://legaltechnology.com/wordsmith-ai-raises-25m-series-a-and-hits-100m-valuation-in-record-time/\n- https://thequantuminsider.com/2026/10/08/oratomic-secures-775-million-fault-tolerant-quantum-computers\n- https://www.pulse.bot/venture-capital/news/cowboy-space-secures-275m-series-b-dac81192-2785-441d-899d-30203028658d/\n- https://marketscreener.com/insider/BASTIAN-HASSLINGER-A3SJDH (aggregator)\n", "person:bella-garcia-camargo": "\n# Bella Garcia-Camargo\n\n> Pronoun note: Colossus uses \"she/her\" throughout (\"before she took the job\", \"already at her desk\") [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/). This profile follows that.\n> Name note: full name appears as Isabella Garcia-Camargo on Stanford pages [src](https://gostanford.com/sports/womens-rowing/roster/season/2018-19/player/isabella-garcia-camargo) [src](https://fsi.stanford.edu/node/4809). Conviction's Corridor post misspells it \"Bella Garcia-Carmago\" [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html).\n\n## One-line take\nOne of four investing staff at Sarah Guo's ~8-person Conviction [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/), with an unusual path (Stanford CS + national-team rower, Stanford Internet Observatory, Bridgewater macro/AI); her one public deal, Corridor (AI product security), sits right on her trust-and-safety / security background [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html).\n\n\n## Crux\n_Added 2026-11-09._ She wins on a narrow, network-sourced lane: Conviction hired her (start ~March 2025) to cover AI in \"cybersecurity or finance\" [src](https://x.com/mvernal/status/1897751959970750796), and her one public deal, Corridor, came out of founders she had already short-listed [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html) inside the Alex Stamos / Krebs Stamos Group / SIO security network that also referred her to the firm [src](https://x.com/mvernal/status/1897751959970750796). The constraint: she is one of four investors at an eight-person firm [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/), with no public sign that she leads lab, compute or chip deals; Conviction's hottest 2026 seed (Instinct) was Pranav Reddy's [src](https://multiples.vc/private-comps/instinct). No source says what she would never do.\n\n## Differential insight\n_Added 2026-11-09. Mirrored in `data/rows/bella-garcia-camargo-insights.csv`._\n- **Stamos was the hiring path, not just a past boss.** Vernal's welcome post says she was \"recruited by her professor (@alexstamos – ty for the referral!)\" [src](https://x.com/mvernal/status/1897751959970750796) (2025-03-06). This replaces the earlier \"2025 (inferred)\" join date. It does *not* say Stamos introduced Corridor.\n- **Explicit mandate from her GP:** \"If you are building an AI company – especially in cybersecurity or finance\" [src](https://x.com/mvernal/status/1897751959970750796).\n- **Corridor was pre-tracked:** \"Jack and Ashwin were at the top of my short list\" [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html). The seed post went out 2025-08-05, about five months after she joined [src](https://x.com/igarciacamargo/status/1952749268240117768).\n- **Shared Krebs Stamos Group history with Corridor's CEO:** she spent about two years at KSG per Susa [src](https://susaventures.substack.com/p/introducing-the-2022-susa-venture-fellows-class-4b3ddfe9d191); Jack Cable's bio lists KSG too, \"has worked at the Defense Digital Service, Krebs Stamos Group, and Vanta\" [src](https://www.lawfaremedia.org/contributors/jcable) (search snippet; page 403). Whether they overlapped is unconfirmed.\n- **Pre-Bridgewater thesis (Susa Venture Fellow, 2022):** \"Isabella is excited about all things cybersecurity, industrials, and defense.\" [src](https://susaventures.substack.com/p/introducing-the-2022-susa-venture-fellows-class-4b3ddfe9d191) (2022-10-27).\n- **Admitted hiring lesson from running EIP at SIO:** technical students lacked judgment; \"Those soft skills were equally if not, more valuable than technical skills.\" [src](https://stanfordrewired.com/post/extinguishing-misinfo-sio/) (student magazine, ~2021, date unconfirmed).\n- **How Guo recruited her:** a dare, not a pitch: \"make it the most aggressive thing that you could possibly be doing\", with an offer to call Kevin Weil at OpenAI [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) (quote from a fetch that mixed paraphrase, so treat as medium confidence).\n- **Embed as sourcing funnel:** \"Embed is a Schelling Point for the best early stage founders.\" [src](https://x.com/igarciacamargo/status/2082123165942055241) (2026-07-28).\n- **Timing:** Conviction's Corridor seed (Aug 2025) came about 7 months before Felicis led \"a $25 million Series A at a $200 million valuation\" [src](https://fortune.com/press-releases/corridor-raises-25m-series-a-ai-coding-security-2026-03-18) (2026-03-18).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n_Added 2026-11-09._ **Medium.** Her own sourced lane (AI application security, AI-for-finance) is adjacent to \"AI-native automation\" but not labs, pre-training or chips, and no deal of hers in those areas is public. She is medium because she is one of four investors at a firm that backed Mistral, Cognition and Sierra early [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/), and because she routes the Stamos security network into Conviction. The deal that matters is Corridor (seed to $200M Series A in about 7 months). For hot-deal flow at Conviction, Pranav Reddy (Instinct) looks like the higher-fit person to follow [src](https://multiples.vc/private-comps/instinct).\n\n## Background\n- **Stanford University, B.S. Computer Science** [src](https://fsi.stanford.edu/node/4809). Lightweight rower on Stanford women's rowing; listed as a junior in 2018-19, hometown Seattle, major Computer Science [src](https://gostanford.com/sports/womens-rowing/roster/season/2018-19/player/isabella-garcia-camargo). Honors listed there: two-time IRA Lightweight Team Champion (2017-18), CRCA Lightweight All-American (2018), Pac-12 All-Academic First Team (2019) [src](https://gostanford.com/sports/womens-rowing/roster/season/2018-19/player/isabella-garcia-camargo).\n- **U.S. rowing:** won the U23 lightweight double sculls at USRowing Trials (July 2018) to qualify for the U23 World Championships in Poznan [src](https://gostanford.com/news/2018/07/11/cardinal-to-u23-worlds). Colossus says she rowed \"for the U.S. national team\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- **Stanford Internet Observatory (SIO):** former research analyst and special projects manager; project manager for the Election Integrity Partnership and the Virality Project [src](https://fsi.stanford.edu/node/4809). An SIO post (seen via search snippet; page returned 403) called her the EIP's Product Manager and a CS graduate student at Stanford [src](https://fsi.stanford.edu/node/13909). Graduate degree completion unverified. Dates unverified (EIP covered the 2020 and 2022 cycles [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanford_Internet_Observatory)).\n- **Krebs Stamos Group (added 2026-11-09):** spent about two years there in cybersecurity; most client work covered threats to manufacturing and industrial automation [src](https://susaventures.substack.com/p/introducing-the-2022-susa-venture-fellows-class-4b3ddfe9d191). Vernal says Stamos, her professor, recruited her to \"his cybersecurity firm\" [src](https://x.com/mvernal/status/1897751959970750796). SIO tech team joined winter 2020 after Stamos's class [src](https://stanfordrewired.com/post/extinguishing-misinfo-sio/).\n- **Susa Ventures Venture Fellow, 2022 cohort (added 2026-11-09)** [src](https://susaventures.substack.com/p/introducing-the-2022-susa-venture-fellows-class-4b3ddfe9d191). LinkedIn lists Rhodes Finalist (2019, 2021) under honors [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/igarciacamargo/).\n- **Bridgewater Associates:** \"spent time at Bridgewater\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/). A third-party org chart listed her as an investment associate focused on AI/ML from October 2022 (seen via search snippet; page now 404) [src](https://theorg.com/org/bridgewater-associates/org-chart/isabella-garcia-camargo). Her X bio reportedly reads \"prev. macro & AI @bridgewater\" (search snippet only; X returned 402) [src](https://x.com/igarciacamargo).\n- **Conviction:** recruited \"out of Bridgewater the year Vernal arrived\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/). Mike Vernal joined as GP in January 2025 [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/31/guos-conviction-partners-adds-mike-vernal-as-gp-raises-230m-fund), so the join year is probably 2025. **Update 2026-11-09:** Vernal's welcome post is dated 2025-03-06, so she started around March 2025 [src](https://x.com/mvernal/status/1897751959970750796). When Conviction approached her she was weighing an OpenAI application-engineer offer [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- **Title:** Colossus (July 2026) calls her \"an investor at Conviction\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/). Evalyze lists \"VC Partner\" in the header but \"Investor\" in its About text [src](https://www.evalyze.ai/investors/bella-garcia-camargo). The Conviction homepage lists her name and X link with no title [src](https://www.conviction.com/). **\"Partner\" is unverified.**\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.conviction.com/ | Listed on firm homepage team section, no bio (checked 2026-11-09) [src](https://www.conviction.com/) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | One firm post: \"Investing in Corridor\" [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html) |\n| X | https://x.com/igarciacamargo | Display name \"Bella\"; linked from Conviction homepage [src](https://www.conviction.com/). 4,480 followers, 1,272 posts, bio \"investor @conviction, prev. macro & AI @bridgewater\" (via fxtwitter API, 2026-11-09) [src](https://api.fxtwitter.com/igarciacamargo). Posts go back to at least 2020-06-12 [src](https://twitter.com/igarciacamargo/status/1271251787190697984) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/igarciacamargo | Per Evalyze [src](https://www.evalyze.ai/investors/bella-garcia-camargo); not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Not a No Priors host; name absent from all 187 episodes in the No Priors RSS feed (checked 2026-11-09) [src](https://feeds.megaphone.fm/nopriors) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2021-05 — The Rhys Show — #85 \"Information Counterwarfare\" (49 min; SIO, EIP and Virality Project work, pre-investing) — [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/85-isabella-garcia-camargo-information-counterwarfare/id1254196635?i=1000522567133) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UB3H-IuWcGg). Transcript not reviewed. (Added 2026-11-09.)\n- RSA Conference has an expert page for her (403 on fetch; session titles unknown) [src](https://www.rsaconference.com/experts/isabella-garcia-camargo).\n- No guest appearances on No Priors [src](https://feeds.megaphone.fm/nopriors). No investing-era podcast found.\n- Featured in Colossus's July 2026 profile of Sarah Guo and Conviction (not an interview of her) [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n\n## How they think\nLight public record; what exists:\n- **Security should be built in, not added later:** in the Corridor post she argues reactive AppSec doesn't scale and that AI coding tools make \"Secure by Design\" practical [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html).\n- **Backs founders she was already tracking:** says Corridor's founders were \"at the top of my short list\" before she joined Conviction [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html).\n- **Takes on hard problems:** Guo told her the OpenAI application-engineer role was \"not aggressive enough for you\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/). Colossus describes sparring with Guo over founders as normal [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- Focus per a third-party directory: AI-native software, infra, enterprise apps [src](https://www.evalyze.ai/investors/bella-garcia-camargo) (secondary).\n- Calls right/wrong: nothing public.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Investing in Corridor\" (Conviction, Aug 2025): her only signed investment memo [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html). Date inferred from her X post of the link on 2025-08-05 [src](https://x.com/igarciacamargo/status/1952749268240117768).\n2. Colossus, \"Sarah Guo's Wager\" (July 2026): best third-party portrait of her and of how Conviction recruits [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n3. Her July 2026 X thread promoting Embed applications [src](https://x.com/igarciacamargo/status/2082123165942055241).\n\n## Social engagement\n- 2020: posted on SIO research into coordinated account activity around COVID (2020-06-12) [src](https://twitter.com/igarciacamargo/status/1271251787190697984).\n- 2025-08-05: shared the Corridor investment post [src](https://x.com/igarciacamargo/status/1952749268240117768).\n- 2026-07-28: promoted Conviction's Embed program (applications due 8/10), calling it \"a Schelling Point for the best early stage founders\" [src](https://x.com/igarciacamargo/status/2082123165942055241).\n- 2025-03-06: Mike Vernal's welcome post, tagging Stamos for the referral [src](https://x.com/mvernal/status/1897751959970750796).\n- Per search snippets, she also promoted a Conviction x OpenAI x HackMIT event (not fetched) [src](https://x.com/igarciacamargo).\n- Cadence, reply patterns, and amplifiers: not checked (timeline endpoints rate-limited).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Corridor (AI product security) | Seed, $5.4M | 2025-08-05 | Conviction reportedly led (secondary); she wrote Conviction's memo | Angels Alex Stamos, Casey Ellis, Jon Oberheide (secondary) | [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html) [src](https://www.axios.com/2025/08/05/corridor-ai-startup-alex-stamos) [src](https://inforcapital.com/news/these-26-year-old-founders-quit-dhs-and-built-a-200-million-ai-cyber-startup) |\n| Corridor | Series A, $25M at $200M valuation | 2026-03-18 | No (Felicis led); Conviction participated; Mike Vernal quoted, her role unverified | Felicis, Timeless, Artisanal Ventures, Lux Capital, Sunflower Capital, Datadog, SV Angel | [src](https://fortune.com/press-releases/corridor-raises-25m-series-a-ai-coding-security-2026-03-18) |\n\n**Conviction context (firm deals, not attributed to her):** Instinct seed (April 2026, ~$50M valuation) was represented by Pranav Reddy, not her [src](https://multiples.vc/private-comps/instinct). Baseten, Cartesia, Cognition, Harvey, Mistral, Sierra, among others on the homepage [src](https://www.conviction.com/). Other homepage launch posts (Latent, Sola) are signed by Pranav Reddy and Sarah Guo, not her [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/latent.html).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Corridor (2025):** she says she had the founders on her short list before joining Conviction [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html). The memo highlights Jack Cable's bug-bounty and CISA history, Ashwin Ramaswami's open source and law work, and Alex Stamos joining full time as CSO [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html). Possible intro path (unverified): she worked at SIO [src](https://fsi.stanford.edu/node/4809), which Stamos founded in 2019 [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanford_Internet_Observatory). No source says that's how the deal began.\n\n## Conviction (firm context)\n- Founded by Sarah Guo after she left Greylock in mid-2022 [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/31/guos-conviction-partners-adds-mike-vernal-as-gp-raises-230m-fund). Colossus dates the launch to October 2022 [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- Fund I: $101M. Fund II: $230M, announced 2025-01-31 with Mike Vernal as GP [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/31/guos-conviction-partners-adds-mike-vernal-as-gp-raises-230m-fund). Colossus (July 2026): three funds, \"nearly a billion dollars in all\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/). Fund III size not separately confirmed.\n- Writes $1M–$25M checks for \"AI-Native, 'Software 3.0'\" companies [src](https://www.conviction.com/). About eight people, four of them investors [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- Runs Embed, an early-stage program [src](https://embed.conviction.com/). Guo co-hosts No Priors with Elad Gil [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/31/guos-conviction-partners-adds-mike-vernal-as-gp-raises-230m-fund).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Conviction | Investor | 2025-03 (per Vernal welcome post) | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Sarah Guo | Founder of Conviction; recruited her and advised her on the OpenAI offer | 2025 (inferred) | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Mike Vernal | Conviction GP and colleague; she keeps \"a running list of facts about him\" | 2025 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Pranav Reddy | Conviction colleague; Guo's first hire | 2025 (inferred) | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Bridgewater Associates | Former employer (macro & AI); investment associate from Oct 2022 per secondary source | 2022 (secondary) | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) [src](https://theorg.com/org/bridgewater-associates/org-chart/isabella-garcia-camargo) |\n| Stanford Internet Observatory | Former research analyst / special projects manager; PM of Election Integrity Partnership and Virality Project | ~2020 | [src](https://fsi.stanford.edu/node/4809) [src](https://twitter.com/igarciacamargo/status/1271251787190697984) |\n| Alex Stamos | Founded SIO, where she worked; later Corridor CSO and seed angel | ~2020 (overlap inferred) | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanford_Internet_Observatory) [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html) |\n| Jack Cable, Ashwin Ramaswami | Corridor founders; she backed them at seed | 2025-08 | [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html) |\n| OpenAI | Offered her an application-engineer role, which she turned down | ~2025 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Alex Stamos (update) | Recruited her to his cybersecurity firm and referred her to Conviction | 2025-03 referral | [src](https://x.com/mvernal/status/1897751959970750796) |\n| Krebs Stamos Group | Former employer, ~2 years in cybersecurity | ~2020–2022 | [src](https://susaventures.substack.com/p/introducing-the-2022-susa-venture-fellows-class-4b3ddfe9d191) |\n| Susa Ventures / Courtney Buie Lipkin | Venture Fellow, 2022 cohort; Lipkin authored the class post | 2022 | [src](https://susaventures.substack.com/p/introducing-the-2022-susa-venture-fellows-class-4b3ddfe9d191) |\n| Kevin Weil | Guo offered to call him about her OpenAI options | ~2025 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Stanford Women's Rowing | Lightweight rower; alumna | ~2016–2020 | [src](https://gostanford.com/sports/womens-rowing/roster/season/2018-19/player/isabella-garcia-camargo) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Official title: \"Investor\" (Colossus) or \"Partner\"/\"VC Partner\" (Evalyze)? Check LinkedIn.\n- Exact start dates at SIO, Bridgewater, and Conviction. Did she finish a Stanford CS master's?\n- Did the SIO/Stamos connection lead to Corridor? Stamos referred *her* to Conviction (confirmed), but the Corridor intro path is still unconfirmed. Did she overlap with Jack Cable at Krebs Stamos Group? Is she on Corridor's board?\n- Listen to The Rhys Show #85 for her own words on judgment and speed (2021).\n- Identify @gradypb, whose \"competitive with a heart of gold\" heuristic Conviction uses for hiring.\n- Any finance-AI deals (the second half of her stated mandate)?\n- Other Conviction deals she worked on but didn't sign publicly.\n- X follower count, cadence, and who she engages with.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.conviction.com/\n- https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html\n- https://www.conviction.com/launches/latent.html\n- https://embed.conviction.com/\n- https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/31/guos-conviction-partners-adds-mike-vernal-as-gp-raises-230m-fund\n- https://www.evalyze.ai/investors/bella-garcia-camargo\n- https://theorg.com/org/bridgewater-associates/org-chart/isabella-garcia-camargo (404 on fetch; search snippet only)\n- https://gostanford.com/sports/womens-rowing/roster/season/2018-19/player/isabella-garcia-camargo\n- https://gostanford.com/news/2018/07/11/cardinal-to-u23-worlds\n- https://fsi.stanford.edu/node/4809\n- https://fsi.stanford.edu/node/13909 (403 on fetch; search snippet only)\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanford_Internet_Observatory\n- https://x.com/igarciacamargo\n- https://x.com/igarciacamargo/status/1952749268240117768\n- https://x.com/igarciacamargo/status/2082123165942055241\n- https://twitter.com/igarciacamargo/status/1271251787190697984\n- https://feeds.megaphone.fm/nopriors\n- https://www.axios.com/2025/08/05/corridor-ai-startup-alex-stamos (403 on fetch; date and size from URL and search snippet)\n- https://inforcapital.com/news/these-26-year-old-founders-quit-dhs-and-built-a-200-million-ai-cyber-startup\n- https://fortune.com/press-releases/corridor-raises-25m-series-a-ai-coding-security-2026-03-18 (paid press release)\n- https://x.com/mvernal/status/1897751959970750796 (read via api.fxtwitter.com, 2026-11-09)\n- https://api.fxtwitter.com/igarciacamargo\n- https://susaventures.substack.com/p/introducing-the-2022-susa-venture-fellows-class-4b3ddfe9d191\n- https://stanfordrewired.com/post/extinguishing-misinfo-sio/\n- https://www.lawfaremedia.org/contributors/jcable (403; search snippet only)\n- https://multiples.vc/private-comps/instinct (aggregator)\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/85-isabella-garcia-camargo-information-counterwarfare/id1254196635?i=1000522567133\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UB3H-IuWcGg\n- https://www.rsaconference.com/experts/isabella-garcia-camargo (403)\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/igarciacamargo/\n", "person:ben-spector": "\n# Ben Spector\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. Builds on companies/flapping-airplanes.md, people/mark-xu.md, people/david-cahn.md and patterns/2026-11-10-*.md, which already tell the Index/Sequoia deal story; this file cites rather than re-narrates it.*\n\n## One-line take\nBen Spector is the 25-year-old CEO of Flapping Airplanes and the sourcing node that four top firms came through; his tie to Cursor's Michael Truell goes back to a 2016 Two Sigma internship where the two built the Halite AI competition, years before Prod.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he sits at the intersection of two talent graphs that frontier-AI money chases. One is the Stanford Hazy Research GPU-kernel scene (ThunderKittens, megakernels, with Chris Ré) [src](https://benjaminfspector.com/). The other is the young-founder network of Prod, which Index says he co-founded and led and which played a role in the early shaping of Cursor, Mercor, Etched and Decart [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/).\n- **What investors are buying:** Index's own description is \"a firehose of ambitious ideas that can sound outlandish at first\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/). Brother Asher is cast as the filter on those ideas (same source).\n- **Constraint:** no commercial product a year in, and the public thesis moved from data efficiency at seed [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/) to performance per chip (megakernels) at the A (see companies/flapping-airplanes.md).\n- **What he'd never do (inferred from his own writing, not stated):** build a big, specialized, bureaucratic lab. His 2022 essay argues \"specialization is a tool of the bureaucrat\" and favours small, intense pairs [src](http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/sathm). He told the WSJ (via the company's X post) that small teams of brilliant young people are \"the kinds of organizations that actually win\" [src](https://x.com/flappyairplanes/status/2016590972050964522).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Cursor link is a 2016 friendship, not a Prod program.** The Halite README says the game \"was primarily created by Ben Spector and Michael Truell for Two Sigma during their summer 2016 internship\" [src](https://github.com/HaliteChallenge/Halite/blob/master/README.md). Truell is Cursor's CEO. Every other file in this repo routes the Cursor tie through Prod; the primary source shows it predates Prod. Ben still lists Halite under \"things i did\" [src](https://benjaminfspector.com/).\n- **He called AI-model economics \"semiconductor fabs\" in March 2023, and the company now sells chip efficiency.** His essay says \"the economics of FMs will be just like semiconductor fabs\" and \"Only Google and NVIDIA have cost-effective access to enormous volumes\" of compute [src](http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/ae). In an August 2023 update he wrote \"I want ridiculously low prices.\" The Series A pitch (more work per GPU) follows from this 2023 view (my inference).\n- **His hardware creed, verbatim:** \"Keep the tensor core fed. That's it.\" and AI should be reoriented \"around what maps well onto the hardware\" [src](https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2024-05-12-tk) (2024-05-12). The megakernel post claims the lowest-latency Llama-1B forward pass on H100 [src](https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2025-05-27-no-bubbles) (2025-05-27).\n- **The co-founder model is in a 2022 essay.** Ben argues \"the hive mind is an underutilized tool of project management\": two friends who collaborate on nearly everything [src](http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/sathm) (2022-10-02). Flapping is run by him, his brother and \"my good friend aidan\" [src](https://benjaminfspector.com/).\n- **Prod's ideation playbook is public.** His essay \"Effective startup ideation\" (2022-10-30) draws on Prod and says \"Within Prod, we put a great deal of emphasis on so-called first-principles thinking\" [src](http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/esi). It thanks a friend Oskar who runs Empiric Networks, and Alfred Spector (see Open questions).\n- **Index's first contact was accidental.** Shardul Shah met Ben in Chris Ré's office and told Xu \"Ben Spector is a sensation\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/). Already covered in people/mark-xu.md.\n- **Hummingbird met him before the A.** Mario Gabriele recalls a sit-down with Ben in SF \"earlier this year\", where Ben laid out ideas on a blackboard ([people/mario-gabriele.md](mario-gabriele.md), LinkedIn).\n- **Prod's track record (only figure found):** Hertz says Prod cohort companies raised $25M under his leadership [src](https://hertzfoundation.org/person/benjamin-spector). The figure is small next to Cursor and Mercor, so Prod's influence looks like network and advice rather than capital (my inference).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, as a sourcing node and founder, not as an investor.** THESIS.md covers investors and allocators; Ben is neither, and I found no angel checks. He matters because he is the gate that Index (Xu and Shah), Sequoia (Cahn), GV (via Ré) and Lux came through on a frontier lab with a $180M seed and a $1B A [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/). That round size is exactly where the owner writes ~$20M checks. Prod alumni and his Hazy Research co-authors are a repeat pipeline for frontier-AI talent and new labs.\n- Deals that matter: Flapping Airplanes seed (2026-01, co-led Index/GV/Sequoia) and Series A (2026-10-21, co-led Index/Lux) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/).\n- check_capacity is a placeholder; he is a check-taker.\n\n## Background\n- **Halite (summer 2016):** co-created Two Sigma's Halite AI programming competition with Michael Truell during an internship [src](https://github.com/HaliteChallenge/Halite/blob/master/README.md).\n- **MIT:** SB in CS and math, MEng in CS, advised by Tim Kraska [src](https://benjaminfspector.com/). MEng thesis on learned string indexes, 2022 [src](https://dspace.mit.edu/bitstream/handle/1721.1/144902/Spector-spectorb-meng-eecs-2022-thesis.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y). Founded Prod while at MIT [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/). Also presented SPARC tokamak plasma work at APS-DPP 2021 [src](https://benjaminfspector.com/).\n- **Stanford:** CS PhD student with Chris Ré; describes himself as \"formerly\" a third-year PhD student [src](https://benjaminfspector.com/). 2023 Hertz Fellow [src](https://hertzfoundation.org/person/benjamin-spector).\n- **Research:** ThunderKittens (ICLR 2025 spotlight) [src](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.20399); Scaling Laws for Precision (ICLR 2025 oral) [src](https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.04330); staged speculative decoding (ICML 2023 workshop) [src](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.04623).\n- **Flapping Airplanes:** founded late 2025 with brother Asher and Aidan Smith; CEO [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/flapping-airplanes-1b-6b-valuation). Sequoia gives his age as 25 [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://benjaminfspector.com/ | Bio, papers, 11 essays (2022-2025); checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/ae (personal essays); https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog (research posts) | Personal essays mostly 2022-2023; latest Hazy post 2025-09-28 |\n| X | https://x.com/bfspector | Handle tagged by @flappyairplanes and by Mark Xu ([src](https://x.com/flappyairplanes/status/2016590972050964522)). A search result also showed \"@spectorb\"; unverified |\n| LinkedIn | not found | Not verified; not guessed |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/benjaminfspector | \"Pilot. Former grad student.\"; 194 followers (API, 2026-11-10) |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only (below) |\n| Company | https://flappingairplanes.com/ ; https://x.com/flappyairplanes | Site is JS-rendered; no text readable |\n| Prod | https://www.prod.so/ | 501(c)(3); site names no people |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-02-10 — Equity (TechCrunch) — \"This Sequoia-backed lab thinks the brain is 'the floor, not the ceiling' for AI\" — https://podwise.ai/episodes/7158722 — Listing/summary only, no transcript. Themes: data efficiency, brain-inspired methods, hiring unconventional people. (summary, low confidence)\n- 2026-10-28 — Index Perspectives video, interviewed by Mark Xu — https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/catching-up-with-ben-spector-ceo-and-co-founder-of-flapping-airplanes/ — No transcript; page summary says he discussed why he founded Prod and testing research against strong baselines.\n- WSJ interview (2026-01, via company X post): small teams of brilliant young people are \"the kinds of organizations that actually win\" [src](https://x.com/flappyairplanes/status/2016590972050964522).\n\n## How they think\n- **Hardware-first AI.** Design models around what the GPU wants; keep tensor cores fed [src](https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2024-05-12-tk).\n- **Frontier models are fabs.** Expensive to build, quick to depreciate, last-gen capability becomes cheap [src](http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/ae) (2023).\n- **Youth and small teams.** \"Young person's AGI lab\" (Cahn's phrase) [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/); his WSJ line on small teams (above).\n- **Contrarian on scale.** \"Scale methods capture an increasing fraction of global wealth and talent\" (2022 essay on economic mobility) [src](http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/tsmie). Flapping is pitched as \"scale alone is not enough\" (Cahn) [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/).\n- **Ideation:** \"Almost everyone -- including very smart people -- is wrong about almost everything.\" [src](http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/esi).\n- **Calls right/wrong:** his 2023 bet that inference prices would collapse for last-gen models looks right in hindsight (my judgment, not a sourced claim).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [GPUs Go Brrr](https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2024-05-12-tk) (2024): the hardware creed behind ThunderKittens.\n2. [Look Ma, No Bubbles!](https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2025-05-27-no-bubbles) (2025): the megakernel work that became the Series A story.\n3. [AI economics](http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/ae) (2023): fab analogy for foundation models.\n4. [Specialization and the hive mind](http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/sathm) (2022): his model for small founding teams.\n5. [Effective startup ideation](http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/esi) (2022): the Prod playbook.\n\n## Social engagement\n- Posts sparingly on X around company news, e.g. \"Very grateful and excited to be working together every week!\" replying to Index's seed announcement [src](https://x.com/bfspector/status/2016578990354682063). Amplified by Index, Mark Xu, Sequoia and the company account. Full feed not read (X not fetchable).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| nothing found | | | | | No personal angel investments found in searches or aggregator snippets (2026-11-10) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Flapping seed (announced 2026-01-28):** Index came via two paths: Xu's Prod relationship with Ben and Harvard tie to Asher, and Shah's chance meeting in Ré's office [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/). Sequoia dates its partnership to 2025 [src](https://sequoiacap.com/companies/flapping-airplanes/). Detail in companies/flapping-airplanes.md.\n- **Flapping A (2026-10-21):** Index re-led with Lux, Xu took the board seat [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/).\n- **Halite (2016):** built with Michael Truell at Two Sigma, with Cornell Tech later co-running the competition [src](https://github.com/HaliteChallenge/Halite/blob/master/README.md).\n\n## Update 2026-11-10: Argo SPV role (from the Argo sweep)\n- A \"Benjamin Spector\" is a managing member of Argo SPV GP, LLC, the GP of Argo Mercor Series C SPV LP. That SPV's first sale was 2025-10-09; it sold $13,002,000 to 3 investors, and Arul Kapoor, Amol Punjabi and Quinn McIntyre are co-members ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086863/000090514825003651/primary_doc.xml)). Prod's 990 lists \"Benjamin Spector (President)\" with Arul Kapoor as Treasurer ([990](https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/873826850)), so this is very likely him (inferred by name + Prod tie). This qualifies the \"no angel checks found\" note above: he co-ran at least one investment vehicle.\n- Argo then appears on Flapping Airplanes' backer list, added between 2026-03-08 and 2026-07-12 ([capture](https://web.archive.org/web/20260712223234/https://flappingairplanes.com/)). He is not on Argo's 2026 filings (Fund I, Argo ET 1). See firms/argo.md.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Michael Truell (Cursor CEO) | Co-created Halite at Two Sigma | 2016 summer | [src](https://github.com/HaliteChallenge/Halite/blob/master/README.md) |\n| Chris Ré (Stanford) | PhD advisor; co-author on ThunderKittens and megakernels | by 2024 | [src](https://benjaminfspector.com/) |\n| Asher Spector | Brother, co-founder | founding late 2025 | [src](https://benjaminfspector.com/) |\n| Aidan Smith | \"good friend\", co-founder | founding late 2025 | [src](https://benjaminfspector.com/) |\n| Mark Xu (Index) | Relationship via Prod; seed/A lead, board | before 2026-01 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/) |\n| Shardul Shah (Index) | Met in Ré's office | before 2026-01 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/) |\n| David Cahn (Sequoia) | Backer, partnered 2025 | 2025 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/) |\n| Mario Gabriele (Hummingbird) | Met before the Series A | 2026 | people/mario-gabriele.md |\n| Tim Kraska | MIT advisor | MIT years | [src](https://benjaminfspector.com/) |\n| Hazy co-authors (Simran Arora, Dan Fu, Jordan Juravsky, Stuart Sul, Owen Dugan, Dylan Lim, Aaryan Singhal) | Co-authors on ThunderKittens / megakernel posts | 2024-2025 | [src](https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2025-05-27-no-bubbles), [src](https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2024-05-12-tk) |\n| Oskar (Empiric Networks) | \"my good friend\"; essay reviewer | by 2022 | [src](http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/esi) |\n| Hertz Foundation | 2023 Fellow, funded PhD | 2023 | [src](https://hertzfoundation.org/person/benjamin-spector) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Alfred Spector (needs-id).** Ben thanks \"Alfred Spector\" in his 2022 essay [src](http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/esi). Alfred Spector (ex-Google VP Research) was Two Sigma CTO from Oct 2015 to mid-2020 [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Spector), which overlaps Ben's 2016 Two Sigma internship, and is linked to the Hertz Foundation [src](https://www.hertzfoundation.org/people/alfred-spector/). These are overlaps only; no source states a family or personal relationship.\n- Which Prod alumni went on to Cursor, Mercor, Etched and Decart, and in what capacity? prod.so names no one.\n- Has Ben made any angel investments (e.g., in Prod companies)? None found.\n- Who were the Flapping seed angels? iq.wiki claims Ré, Karpathy and Jeff Dean (see companies/flapping-airplanes.md); unverified.\n- Which Hazy co-authors joined Flapping?\n- No transcript found for Equity or Index video.\n\n## Sources\n- https://benjaminfspector.com/ (personal site, read 2026-11-10)\n- http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/ae ; /esi ; /sathm ; /tsmie ; /tpw ; /tgg ; /tmaa\n- https://github.com/HaliteChallenge/Halite/blob/master/README.md\n- https://api.github.com/users/benjaminfspector\n- https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2024-05-12-tk ; https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2025-05-27-no-bubbles\n- https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.20399 ; https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.04330\n- https://hertzfoundation.org/person/benjamin-spector\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/ (2026-01-28)\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/ (2026-10-21)\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/catching-up-with-ben-spector-ceo-and-co-founder-of-flapping-airplanes/ (2026-10-28)\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/\n- https://x.com/flappyairplanes/status/2016590972050964522 ; https://x.com/bfspector/status/2016578990354682063 ; https://x.com/marklxu1/status/2016573836532097406\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/flapping-airplanes-1b-6b-valuation\n- https://podwise.ai/episodes/7158722 (summary only)\n- https://www.prod.so/\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Spector ; https://www.hertzfoundation.org/people/alfred-spector/\n", "person:benjamin-li": "\n# Benjamin Li\n\n## One-line take\nLi is Triatomic's channel to Asia-Pacific capital and deals. That makes him a possible door to APAC LPs and co-investors in large compute rounds, not a deal-maker to follow: no deal is publicly attributed to him.\n\n## Crux\nPronouns: he, per the site bio.\n- **Why he matters:** he started at Deutsche Asset Management in 2007 (the site gives no role) and was then MD at RAM AI as MD for \"international growth for Europe and Asia-Pacific\" from 2014 to 2020 ([site](https://triatomic.ai)). At Triatomic his remit is \"deal sourcing, investor relations, and business development\" in APAC ([site](https://triatomic.ai)). His skill set is capital formation and relationships, not technical diligence.\n- **Constraint:** he isn't a GP. The ADV lists only Zhou, Huber and Hochberg as owners of the GP and management entities ([ADV Schedules A/B](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf)). The adviser has 9 employees, 6 of whom do investment advisory work, and reports **0** firms or persons soliciting clients on its behalf (Items 5.A, 5.B(1) and 5.B(6) of the same ADV). This is a regulatory definition: the iCapital \"Eagle\" feeder, with $49.25M from 56 investors (see firms/triatomic.md), is still third-party wealth distribution. Direct APAC LP relationships, though, are in-house, and Li is the likely person handling them (inference). The ADV lists no Asian office: the only offices are Palo Alto and Incline Village, NV (Item 1.F, Schedule D 1.F).\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** lead or price a round. Investment decisions sit with the three GPs.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **His flagship fund reports no non-US investors, and that includes its offshore feeder.** In the ADV filed 2026-03-31, Triatomic Capital Private I LP is reported as a master fund, and its single master-feeder schedule names **Triatomic Capital Offshore Private I LP** (805-1284433276) as the feeder ([ADV Schedule D 7.B.1 Q6](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf)).\n  - That combined schedule has a $297.1M gross asset value and reports **0%** beneficial ownership by non-US persons (Q16).\n  - By contrast, the legacy Hongkou Opportunity II, III and V SPVs report 50%, 43% and 28%, and the Triatomic Capital Hybrid Master LP, with Hybrid LP and Hybrid Offshore Ltd. as feeders, reports 41%. Item 5.F(3) reports **$0** of regulatory AUM attributable to non-US clients. \"Clients\" there means the pooled vehicles, not the investors in them, so this does not contradict the 20% non-US figure in Item 5.C(2).\n  - The offshore feeder raised $24.0M from 3 investors ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2009963/000091957424000584/primary_doc.xml)). If the combined schedule includes the feeder's investors, as single master-feeder reporting is meant to, those 3 are probably US tax-exempt investors using an offshore blocker, not APAC money (inference).\n  - **Read-through:** the filings show no APAC LP capital in the core venture fund, despite a dedicated APAC IR head. Whatever non-US money the firm has sits in about $20M of legacy SPVs and the Hybrid sleeve. The filing doesn't say what Li has or hasn't raised: the role may be recent, aimed at a future Fund II, or mainly about sourcing.\n- **An offshore channel existed from day one.** Peter Zhou filed a Cayman feeder, Hongkou Capital Offshore Ltd., on the same day as the first Hongkou fund (2021-03-31). It had a $100K minimum, and Paras Malde and Amber Ramsey were directors ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852732/000090571821000483/primary_doc.xml)). A non-US LP base was part of the design before Li's title appeared.\n- **\"RAM AI\" means RAM Active Investments in Geneva**, a systematic long/short and emerging-markets equity manager ([HedgeNordic, 2017-11](https://hedgenordic.com/2017/11/ram-ai-from-emerging-markets-to-systematic-longshort/)). It is not the Australian Real Asset Management, which also has a Hong Kong arm. Zhou's own background is Coatue public and private books, so Li is a second hedge-fund-distribution hire at a firm that began as a hedge fund (see firms/triatomic.md).\n- **The dates conflict.** The firm site says RAM 2014–2020. Aggregators show RAM \"Jul 2021 - Present\" with an earlier 2014–2021 stint ([village.ai](https://village.ai/person/benjamin-li-MTQzMDY3OTI), [datanyze](https://www.datanyze.com/people/Ben-Li/1889696166), both aggregator search snippets). The site is primary, so it wins, but his Triatomic start date is unknown.\n- No podcasts, X account, Substack or quoted press were found. He has essentially no public voice.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**low.** He isn't a deal lead and no AI or compute deal is attributed to him. Triatomic's compute deals (MatX B, d-Matrix C co-lead, Gimlet A/B, Efficient B, iPronics A, Volantis A) name no partner, or name Zhou (see firms/triatomic.md). His use to the owner is narrow:\n1. He could be an introduction to Asian family offices or sovereign co-investors in large hardware rounds.\n2. He could explain how Triatomic structures non-US LP access (the Cayman feeders and the Hybrid sleeve), which is relevant if the owner raises from APAC.\n\nCalibration: rated low rather than skip because the APAC-LP angle is a possible path to the owner's own LPs.\n\n## Background\n- Cornell University: B.S. in Economics and Psychology, year not given ([site](https://triatomic.ai)).\n- 2007: started at Deutsche Asset Management ([site](https://triatomic.ai)).\n- 2014–2020: Managing Director at RAM AI (RAM Active Investments, Geneva), leading corporate strategic development and international growth for Europe and APAC ([site](https://triatomic.ai)). Aggregators extend this to 2021 or later (see above).\n- IMD: MBA, year not given ([site](https://triatomic.ai)).\n- Joined Triatomic as Head of International / APAC on a date not found. He is not named in any Triatomic or Hongkou Form D or in the ADV schedules.\n- 2024: became a member of the Cornell University Council ([site](https://triatomic.ai)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai) | Team bio (fetched 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | [linkedin.com/in/libenjamin](https://www.linkedin.com/in/libenjamin/) | Linked from the firm site; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found.\n\n## How they think\nNothing found in his own words. The only stated remit is sourcing, IR and business development in APAC ([site](https://triatomic.ai)).\n\n## Read these first\nNothing he has written was found. The best proxies:\n- The Triatomic team bio ([site](https://triatomic.ai)): his remit and career.\n- The Triatomic ADV, 2026-03-31 ([PDF](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf)): how much of each vehicle is owned by non-US persons, which is his IR territory.\n\n## Social engagement\nNo public social activity found.\n\n## Portfolio\nNo deals are publicly attributed to him. Firm-level deals are in firms/triatomic.md, which names no partner on most compute rounds.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. One firm-file observation, unconfirmed as anything he did: Triatomic's co-investors include Asia-linked capital, namely Horizon Ventures on Chemify A, Horizons and Tencent on Manifold A, Temasek and EDBI on d-Matrix C, and Prosperity7 on Gimlet. No source ties any of these to Li.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Triatomic Capital | Head of International / APAC | date unknown | [site](https://triatomic.ai) |\n| RAM Active Investments (RAM AI) | Managing Director | 2014–2020 | [site](https://triatomic.ai) |\n| Deutsche Asset Management | Early career | 2007 | [site](https://triatomic.ai) |\n| Cornell University Council | Member | 2024 | [site](https://triatomic.ai) |\n| Peter Zhou | Colleague (Triatomic GP and CEO) | date unknown | [site](https://triatomic.ai) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did he join Triatomic, and was he there before the 2022 rebrand from Hongkou?\n- Is he based in Hong Kong (aggregator) or Singapore?\n- Which APAC LPs, if any, are in Offshore Private I or the Hybrid Offshore feeder?\n- Did he source any of the Asia-linked co-investors in d-Matrix, Gimlet or Chemify?\n- Why do the dates disagree: RAM until 2020 per the site versus 2021 onward per aggregators?\n\n## Sources\n- https://triatomic.ai (team bio; raw HTML fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf (Form ADV, 2026-03-31)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852732/000090571821000483/primary_doc.xml (Hongkou Capital Offshore Ltd. Form D, 2021-03-31)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2009963/000091957424000584/primary_doc.xml (Offshore Private I Form D)\n- https://hedgenordic.com/2017/11/ram-ai-from-emerging-markets-to-systematic-longshort/ (RAM AI identity)\n- https://village.ai/person/benjamin-li-MTQzMDY3OTI and https://www.datanyze.com/people/Ben-Li/1889696166 (aggregators; search snippets only)\n- EDGAR full-text search for \"Benjamin Li\" with Triatomic or Hongkou: 0 hits (2026-11-10)\n", "person:bill-chen": "\n# Bill Chen\n\n## One-line take\nHe's a research hire at a16z Infra, recently out of OpenAI's Agents Post-Training team where he worked on GPT-6 evals. He isn't credited on any deal, so his value to you is access to OpenAI post-training and evals talent, not deal flow.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters.** He's one of very few VC staff who came straight out of a frontier lab's post-training org, and he worked on next-generation evals there. The a16z bio says he \"worked on the Agents Post-Training team at OpenAI, including on evaluations for GPT-6\" ([src](https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/)). That gives a16z Infra live technical judgment on agents, RL post-training and eval companies, plus a network into the people who leave OpenAI to found them.\n- **What constrains him.** His title is \"research partner\" and his remit is \"challenging technical problems in AI infrastructure\" ([src](https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/)). No source names him on a deal, no a16z post carries his byline, and the firm site lists no social links for him. Treat him as a diligence and sourcing node inside Infra, not a decision-maker. Investment decisions on that team sit with GPs such as Martin Casado and Jennifer Li ([src](https://a16z.com/infra/)).\n- **What he would never do.** Nothing sourced.\n- **Correction to the premise.** \"Partner\" in the page title overstates what can be verified. Nothing shows he has check authority, so `check_capacity` reflects a16z's capacity, not his.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Identity resolved (medium confidence).** The repo carried a \"Bill Chen\" lead as `needs-id` with the note \"common name\". That lead was a co-host of Cafe Compute @ NeurIPS, which BCV ran with sponsors Cerebras, BCV, OpenAI, Mercor and SF Compute ([src](https://luma.com/vqfwj0wx)).\n  - The co-host's Luma profile lists OpenAI as the affiliation and reads \"Frontier evals, agents post training\". It links to X `@realchillben` and LinkedIn `billchen99` ([src](https://luma.com/user/usr-Au7Koxor4gBNGDs)).\n  - That matches the a16z bio's \"Agents Post-Training team at OpenAI, including on evaluations for GPT-6\" ([src](https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/)).\n  - My conclusion: the NeurIPS co-host and the a16z research partner are very likely the same person, but nothing on either page links them directly.\n- **Already in Amanda Huang's circle before a16z.** If the identity holds, he co-hosted the NeurIPS 2025 Cafe Compute with BCV principal Amanda Huang while still at OpenAI ([src](https://luma.com/vqfwj0wx)). That is a pre-existing tie between a16z's new Infra research hire and BCV's AI-compute team.\n- **Joined recently.** His headshot was uploaded under the `/uploads/2026/10/` path ([src](https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/)), which suggests the page went live around October 2026. This is my inference from the URL, not an announcement.\n- **Founder before lab.** \"Before that, he was a Y Combinator-backed founder.\" ([src](https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/)) The company name was not found.\n- **Podcasts, X posts and talks.** Nothing found. X returned HTTP 402 to fetches, and the web-search budget for this run was exhausted before I could search for transcripts.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, for access rather than deals.** THESIS.md prizes frontier AI talent and post-training research. A person who just left OpenAI's agents post-training and GPT-6 evals work is a direct line to the researchers most likely to spin out new labs and RL or eval companies. That is exactly where $600M seeds come from.\n- **What it isn't.** He has no deals you can track, no timing signal, and no proof of check authority.\n- **What to do.** Treat him as a sourcing and diligence contact on post-training and evals talent, and watch which Infra deals he starts showing up on.\n\n## Background\n- Columbia University: \"Computer Science and Economics\" degree ([src](https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/)). Year not found.\n- Y Combinator-backed founder ([src](https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/)). Company and batch not found.\n- OpenAI, Agents Post-Training team, including GPT-6 evaluations ([src](https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/)). Dates not found. The Luma profile still showed the OpenAI affiliation ([src](https://luma.com/user/usr-Au7Koxor4gBNGDs)).\n- a16z, research partner on the Infra Investing Team ([src](https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/), [src](https://a16z.com/infra/)). Join date not found; ~Oct 2026 is inferred from the upload path.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/ | Bio only. No authored posts as of 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/realchillben | Taken from the Luma profile (identity medium confidence). Fetch returned 402, so follower count was not retrieved |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/billchen99 | Taken from the Luma profile (identity medium confidence). Not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-12 — Cafe Compute @ NeurIPS (San Diego) — co-host, not a talk ([src](https://luma.com/vqfwj0wx)). The date comes from the repo's Amanda Huang insight row, since the page itself shows no date.\n- Nothing else found. Web search was exhausted this run.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses.** Nothing sourced beyond his stated focus, \"challenging technical problems in AI infrastructure\" ([src](https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/)). His Luma bio, \"Frontier evals, agents post training\", is the clearest signal of his technical home turf ([src](https://luma.com/user/usr-Au7Koxor4gBNGDs)).\n- **Contrarian views.** Nothing found.\n- **Calls right or wrong.** Nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n- [a16z author page](https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/): the only primary bio.\n- [Luma profile](https://luma.com/user/usr-Au7Koxor4gBNGDs): his OpenAI-era self-description and social handles.\n- [Cafe Compute @ NeurIPS](https://luma.com/vqfwj0wx): shows his network with BCV, Cerebras, Mercor and SF Compute.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X was not fetchable (HTTP 402).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| nothing found | | | | | No a16z post or announcement names him on a deal as of 2026-11-10 ([src](https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/)) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Andreessen Horowitz (Infra) | Research partner | ~2026-10 (inferred) | [src](https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/) |\n| OpenAI | Former Agents Post-Training team, GPT-6 evals | dates not found | [src](https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/) |\n| Y Combinator | Backed founder | dates not found | [src](https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/) |\n| Amanda Huang (BCV) | Co-host, Cafe Compute @ NeurIPS (identity medium) | 2025-12 | [src](https://luma.com/vqfwj0wx) |\n| Martin Casado, Jennifer Li, Lisha Li, Jason Cui | a16z Infra teammates | ~2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/infra/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is the NeurIPS co-host `@realchillben` definitely the a16z hire? Confirm on LinkedIn `billchen99` or X.\n- What was the YC company and batch? When did he leave OpenAI?\n- Does \"research partner\" carry deal-sponsor rights at a16z Infra, or is it a technical-diligence role like a scout or EIR?\n- Which OpenAI post-training and evals alumni is he connected to, and is anyone spinning out?\n- Has he appeared on a podcast or posted any evals or agents takes on X?\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/bill-chen/\n- https://a16z.com/infra/\n- https://luma.com/user/usr-Au7Koxor4gBNGDs\n- https://luma.com/vqfwj0wx\n", "person:blake-byers": "\n# Blake Byers\n\n## One-line take\nAn ex-GV partner running a concentrated family office that got into Factory and The Bot Company at seed and xAI, Harmonic and Chai at Series A, and that quietly brings six or seven outside investors into its biggest positions.\n\n## Crux\nHe wins on three things: a bioengineering PhD that makes him a real diligence partner for AI-bio (Chai, Xaira, NewLimit), a decade of GV relationships, and his own money, which lets him commit fast and at size without a fund's ownership rules. He is constrained by concentration (about a dozen names on the site) and by being a one-person shop with a CFO. He does not lead priced rounds for others or sit on many boards; NewLimit is the exception because he co-founded it.\n\n## Differential insight\n- The firm's whole public statement: \"concentrated investment in companies we believe can have generational impact\" ([src](https://www.byerscap.com/)).\n- The \"single family office\" has outside money in deal vehicles. Form ADV lists \"CURIOSITY GROUP OPPORTUNITY ONE, LP\" ($19.9M gross, 6 owners, 17% held by him and related persons) and Opportunity Two ($5.1M, 7 owners, 14%), next to BHB Fund I ($25.1M, 1 owner, 100%) ([src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/337464/PDF/337464.pdf), filed 2026-02-13).\n- The adviser is SEC-registered, he holds 75% or more, and the only other officer is CFO Kenny Joo (since 01/2023) (same filing).\n- On NewLimit's $110M: it \"is a commitment over the lifetime of the company\", not cash in the bank ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/05/16/newlimit-cofounded-by-coinbase-ceo-brian-armstrong-raises-40m-to-extend-life)).\n- On AI-bio: \"we have to generate the data ourselves to improve the models even further\" (same article).\n- A different Byers vehicle, \"Byers Capital Group I, LP\", filed a Form D on 2026-10-01 with Chad Byers as executive officer ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2157273/000147083126000915/primary_doc.xml)). Chad is his younger brother and a Susa Ventures co-founder ([src](https://originsmedia.substack.com/p/chad-byers)); do not merge the two.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. Personal capital, seed and Series A entries into labs and lab-like companies (xAI, Harmonic, The Bot Company, Factory, Chai), no ownership rules. He is the kind of co-investor who can take a meaningful slice of a large seed, and the Curiosity Group vehicles show he syndicates to a small circle.\n\n## Background\n- PhD in bioengineering from Stanford ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/05/16/newlimit-cofounded-by-coinbase-ceo-brian-armstrong-raises-40m-to-extend-life)).\n- General partner at Google Ventures (GV); dates not found ([src](https://originsmedia.substack.com/p/chad-byers)).\n- Co-founded NewLimit with Brian Armstrong in 2021; investor and board member, not an operator (TechCrunch, same link).\n- Byers Capital founded 2021 (directory, [src](https://inforcapital.com/companies/byers-capital)); managing member of the adviser since 08/2023 (ADV).\n- Family: son of Kleiner Perkins co-founder Brook Byers; brother of Chad Byers ([src](https://originsmedia.substack.com/p/chad-byers)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.byerscap.com/ | One sentence and a portfolio list with entry stages; no contact details |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | https://x.com/byersblake | Not read for this profile |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | none found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-05 — TechCrunch interview on NewLimit's $40M Series A — [link](https://techcrunch.com/2023/05/16/newlimit-cofounded-by-coinbase-ceo-brian-armstrong-raises-40m-to-extend-life) — the only on-record interview found.\n- No podcast appearance found.\n\n## How they think\n- Concentration over coverage: about a dozen positions on the site.\n- Stage-agnostic by conviction: seed in Factory and The Bot Company, growth in OpenAI, Anthropic and SpaceX.\n- In AI-bio, proprietary data generation matters more than model architecture (TechCrunch quote above).\n- No public passes or regrets found.\n\n## Read these first\n- byerscap.com: the portfolio list with entry stage per company.\n- The Form ADV: how the vehicles are structured and who else is in them.\n- TechCrunch 2023 on NewLimit: his only extended quotes.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed; X was not read.\n\n## Portfolio\n**(early)** marks pre-seed and seed entries. The repo ledger had four names (Chai Discovery seed, NewLimit, The Bot Company from an aggregator, Adapter). His own site adds entry stages for eleven companies; it gives no dates or sizes, so dates below are the public round dates where known.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Adapter | Undisclosed (cumulative pre-launch funding; stage not stated) | 2026-07-14 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/deep-dive-adapter-launches-ai-cognition) |\n| Chai Discovery | Seed **(early)** | 2024-09-09 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement) |\n| NewLimit | Seed (founder commitment) **(early)** | 2021 | no | not listed | [src](https://pharmaphorum.com/news/newlimit-raises-435m-liver-trials-and-other-financings) |\n| The Bot Company | Seed ($150M at $550M; label inferred) **(early)** | 2024-05-13 | no | not listed | [src](https://siliconvalleyinvestclub.com/companies/bot/) |\n| Factory | Seed **(early)** | 2023 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.byerscap.com/) |\n| Harmonic | Series A ($75M) | 2024-09-23 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.byerscap.com/) |\n| xAI | Series A | not found | no | not listed | [src](https://www.byerscap.com/) |\n| OpenAI | Growth | not found | no | not listed | [src](https://www.byerscap.com/) |\n| Anthropic | Growth | not found | no | not listed | [src](https://www.byerscap.com/) |\n| Chai Discovery | Series A | not found | no | not listed | [src](https://www.byerscap.com/) |\n| The Bot Company | Seed **(early)** | 2024 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.byerscap.com/) |\n| NewLimit | Series A ($40M) | 2023-05-16 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.byerscap.com/) |\n| Neuralink | Series B | not found | no | not listed | [src](https://www.byerscap.com/) |\n| SpaceX | Growth | 2018 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.byerscap.com/) |\n| Robinhood | Seed **(early)** | not found | no | not listed | [src](https://www.byerscap.com/) |\n| Cowboy Space Corporation | Seed **(early)** | not found | no | not listed | [src](https://www.byerscap.com/) |\n| Xaira | Undisclosed | not found | no | not listed | [src](https://inforcapital.com/companies/byers-capital) |\n| OpenEvidence | Undisclosed | not found | no | not listed | [src](https://d1kn7cmwp1llb6.cloudfront.net/investors/blake-byers) |\n\n**Hit pattern.** Seed entries that became hot: Factory, The Bot Company, Robinhood, Cowboy Space. Series A entries: xAI, Harmonic, Chai Discovery, NewLimit. Late into OpenAI and Anthropic (growth). The site says Chai was a Series A entry, yet Chai's seed post of 2024-09-09 already names him ([src](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement)).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **NewLimit.** Co-founded in 2021; he and Armstrong pledged $110M over the company's life, then brought in Dimension, Kleiner Perkins and Founders Fund for the $40M Series A in May 2023 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/05/16/newlimit-cofounded-by-coinbase-ceo-brian-armstrong-raises-40m-to-extend-life)).\n- **Harmonic.** Listed on his site as a Series A entry; the company's $75M release (2024-09-23) does not name him ([src](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/)), so he was an unlisted participant.\n- **Adapter.** Byers Capital is named among backers at the July 2026 launch with GV and Bond (repo ledger, [src](https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/deep-dive-adapter-launches-ai-cognition)).\n- No intro path is sourced for xAI, Factory or The Bot Company.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Brian Armstrong | NewLimit co-founder; joint $110M commitment | 2021 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/05/16/newlimit-cofounded-by-coinbase-ceo-brian-armstrong-raises-40m-to-extend-life) |\n| GV | Former general partner; co-investor in Adapter (2026) | not found | [src](https://originsmedia.substack.com/p/chad-byers) |\n| Chad Byers (Susa Ventures) | Brother; separate \"Byers Capital Group\" fund filed 2026-10 | | [src](https://originsmedia.substack.com/p/chad-byers) |\n| Kleiner Perkins | Father Brook Byers co-founded it; KP invested in NewLimit's Series A | 2023 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/05/16/newlimit-cofounded-by-coinbase-ceo-brian-armstrong-raises-40m-to-extend-life) |\n| Kenny Joo | CFO of the adviser | 2023-01 | [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/337464/PDF/337464.pdf) |\n| \"Curiosity Group\" co-investors (6-7 unnamed owners) | Outside money in two deal vehicles | not found | same |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who are the Curiosity Group owners, and which company does each vehicle hold?\n- Size and date of the xAI, OpenAI and Anthropic positions.\n- Which venture funds he backs as an LP (a directory says \"a small number\"; none named).\n- X posts were not read; any public views on AI beyond bio are missing.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.byerscap.com/\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/337464/PDF/337464.pdf\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2157273/000147083126000915/primary_doc.xml\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/05/16/newlimit-cofounded-by-coinbase-ceo-brian-armstrong-raises-40m-to-extend-life\n- https://originsmedia.substack.com/p/chad-byers\n- https://inforcapital.com/companies/byers-capital (aggregator)\n- https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/\n", "person:bobby-yazdani": "\n# Bobby Yazdani\n\n## One-line take\nAn operator-angel who turned 200+ personal cheques into a $1.17B enterprise-infrastructure firm; the person behind Cota's Cast AI, Auradine and Upscale AI positions.\n\n## Crux\nHe wins on long relationships with enterprise and semiconductor founders, many from the Iranian-American engineering network visible in Cota's portfolio. He is constrained by fund size at the early stage (see firm page) and by being a follower in the hottest rounds.\n\n## Differential insight\n- The firm bio counts \"more than 200 private companies\" through his family office and Cota, and a 2014 CB Insights #1 ranking among angels for follow-on funding [src](https://www.cotacapital.com/team/bobby-yazdani/).\n- Legal name on filings is Babak Yazdani; he holds 25-50% of the management company, the same band as Babak Poushanchi [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/226750/PDF/226750.pdf).\n- On Cast AI: \"Managing cloud deployments effectively to control costs has become a priority\" [src](https://www.futuriom.com/articles/news/cast-ai-raises-10-million-from-cota-samsung-next/2021/10).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. Enterprise infrastructure specialist with real AI chip and networking exposure (Auradine/Velaura, Upscale AI, Quadric), but not a frontier-lab investor.\n\n## Background\nBorn in Tehran; school in England; BA applied mathematics, UC Berkeley. R&D roles at Oracle; founded Saba Software in 1997 and took it public in 2000; family office Signatures Capital from 2006; founded Cota in 2015 [src](https://www.cotacapital.com/team/bobby-yazdani/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.cotacapital.com/ | Firm site |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.cotacapital.com/knowledge-base/ | Firm essays |\n| X | https://x.com/BobbyYazdani | not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/bobby-yazdani-015a13a/ | |\n| GitHub | | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- n/d — Livewire Markets — \"Bobby Yazdani's best ideas lie beyond the hype of generative AI\" — [link](https://www.livewiremarkets.com/wires/bobby-yazdani-s-best-ideas-lie-beyond-the-hype-of-generative-ai) — blocked when fetched; not mined\n\n## How they think\nInfrastructure over applications, and \"beyond the hype of generative AI\" per the Livewire headline. Nothing else mined.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Firm bio](https://www.cotacapital.com/team/bobby-yazdani/).\n- [Cota's ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/226750/PDF/226750.pdf).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot mined.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cast AI | Series A ($10M) | 2021-10-14 | yes | Samsung Next | [src](https://www.futuriom.com/articles/news/cast-ai-raises-10-million-from-cota-samsung-next/2021/10) |\n| Google, Dropbox, Uber, Masimo, Berkeley Lights, Tubi, Truebill, Tenyx | angel / early (rounds not stated) | n/d | no | | [src](https://www.cotacapital.com/team/bobby-yazdani/) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found beyond the Cast AI quote.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Babak Poushanchi | Co-owner of Cota's management company | 2014-11 | [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/226750/PDF/226750.pdf) |\n| Pete Williams | Cota COO and co-owner; ex Saba CFO | 2016-10 | [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/226750/PDF/226750.pdf) |\n| Rajiv Khemani | Founder backed twice (Auradine, Upscale AI) | 2023 | [src](https://networkworld.com/article/4060135/upscale-emerges-from-stealth-with-100-million-seed-and-plans-to-democratize-ai-networking.html) |\n\n## Open questions\nThe Livewire interview; LPs in the evergreen fund; whether he sits on the Auradine/Velaura board.\n\n## Sources\nhttps://www.cotacapital.com/team/bobby-yazdani/ ; https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/226750/PDF/226750.pdf ; https://www.futuriom.com/articles/news/cast-ai-raises-10-million-from-cota-samsung-next/2021/10\n", "person:boris-bakech": "\n# Boris Bakech\n\n## One-line take\nC4's robotics partner, who turned a failed 2015 bet on Anki into a place in Bedrock Robotics' Series B eleven years later.\n\n## Crux\nHe wins by staying loyal to founders across companies. The Bedrock allocation came from the Anki relationship with Boris Sofman. His constraint is the fund's: small tickets, so he joins rounds others lead. He focuses on physical AI and commerce software, not labs.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Bedrock came from Anki.** \"first built through our investment in Anki over a decade ago\" ([announcement, 2026-02-04](https://c4v.com/news/c4-ventures-iii-makes-it-first-investment-in-bedrock-robotics/)). Sofman: \"I've known the C4 Ventures team for over a decade\" ([company page](https://c4v.com/companies/bedrock-robotics/)).\n- **He began as Cagni's analyst.** His bio: \"an analyst at Apple reporting directly to Pascal Cagni\" ([bio](https://c4v.com/people/team/boris-bakech/)).\n- **Bedrock marked up fast.** C4 entered at $1.75B in February 2026; C4's own news feed reports a $600M round at $4.1B in October 2026 ([news](https://c4v.com/news/bedrock-robotics-raises-600m-reaches-4-1b-valuation/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Robotics and space follow-on rounds; relevant as a European co-investor for physical-AI companies.\n\n## Background\nFrom his bio ([src](https://c4v.com/people/team/boris-bakech/)): Master in Management, ESSEC; Orange Business Services; assistant producer at Ubisoft Paris; analyst at Apple under Cagni; co-founded C4 Ventures. Dates other than 2011 (met Cagni at Apple, per the firm timeline) are not given.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://c4v.com/people/team/boris-bakech/ | Bio |\n| Substack / blog | not found | |\n| X | not checked | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found.\n\n## How they think\nOnly the firm's posts: AI should create impact \"where work actually happens: in the physical world\" (Bedrock announcement). No personal writing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Bedrock announcement](https://c4v.com/news/c4-ventures-iii-makes-it-first-investment-in-bedrock-robotics/).\n2. [His bio](https://c4v.com/people/team/boris-bakech/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bedrock Robotics | Series B ($270M at $1.75B) | 2026-02 | no | CapitalG, Valor Atreides AI Fund (co-leads) | [announcement](https://c4v.com/news/c4-ventures-iii-makes-it-first-investment-in-bedrock-robotics/) |\n| NEURA Robotics | Series B (EUR120M) | 2024-12 | no | not collected | [page](https://c4v.com/companies/neura-robotics/) |\n| Impulse Space | not stated | 2023-09 | no | not collected | [page](https://c4v.com/companies/impulse-space/) |\n| Riskified, Via, Lyst | earlier deals, exited | 2016-2021 | not stated | | [bio](https://c4v.com/people/team/boris-bakech/) |\n| Refurbed, Allisone, Cleeng | board seats | various | not stated | | [bio](https://c4v.com/people/team/boris-bakech/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Bedrock Robotics:** see Differential insight. First investment of C4 Ventures III.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Pascal Cagni | former boss at Apple; co-founder | 2011 | [who we are](https://c4v.com/about/who-we-are/) |\n| Boris Sofman | founder backed at Anki and Bedrock | 2015 | [announcement](https://c4v.com/news/c4-ventures-iii-makes-it-first-investment-in-bedrock-robotics/) |\n| Jean Weill | associate on Bedrock | 2026 | [page](https://c4v.com/companies/bedrock-robotics/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Size of the Bedrock cheque; whether C4 took part in the October 2026 round.\n\n## Sources\nLinked inline.\n", "person:boris-sofman": "\n# Boris Sofman\n\n## One-line take\nA three-time physical-autonomy operator (Anki, Waymo Via, Bedrock) whose own company went from a 2024 Eclipse co-build to a $4.1B Series C in about two years, and whose small angel book (Foxglove, Noetive) maps the Eclipse robotics network rather than an independent investing practice ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he is one of the few people who has shipped autonomy at scale twice, in consumer robots (Anki) and in Waymo's trucking and freeway programs, and investors price that directly. Eclipse's Aidan Madigan-Curtis framed Bedrock as backing one of \"only a small handful of people who can introduce autonomy to a new industry\" ([src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/bedrock-robotics-emerges-from-stealth-with-80m-in-funding-for-autonomous-construction-technology-302506159.html)). 8VC credits him and Kevin Peterson with a \"unique radar for where you can move quickly given the right constraints\" ([src](https://8vc.com/resources/bedrock-series-a)).\n- **What constrains him:** he is a full-time CEO of a company that has raised nearly $1B, so his angel activity is occasional, small and network-driven. Only two angel checks are public ([src](https://foxglove.dev/blog/foxglove-series-b), [src](https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed)).\n- **What he would never do (inferred, see podcast notes):** build new hardware from scratch when existing fleets can be retrofitted. Bedrock's pitch is \"upgrading existing fleets with sensors, compute, and intelligence\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/16/ex-waymo-engineers-launch-bedrock-robotics-with-80m-to-automate-construction/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Two Sigma Ventures thread (archive-sourced).** TSV backed Anki's Series A and B ([src, secondary](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anki_(American_company))). Sofman then appears on TSV's team page under \"Venture Partners & Advisors\" as \"Advisor\": absent from the 2023-06 snapshot, present from 2023-09, still listed in 2026-03 ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250609222741/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/)). TSV was in Bedrock's 2025 launch round ([src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/bedrock-robotics-emerges-from-stealth-with-80m-in-funding-for-autonomous-construction-technology-302506159.html)) and Bedrock's site lists \"Two Sigma\" first among key investors ([src](https://bedrockrobotics.com/about)). Unconfirmed: no source says the advisory role is how TSV got its Bedrock allocation.\n- **Bedrock was formed with Eclipse, not pitched to it.** \"Boris connected with Aidan as he began exploring what to build next\"; Eclipse partners \"spent months with Boris and Kevin identifying the right problem\" before incorporation ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock)). Peterson's path in was separate: he \"had previously worked with Eclipse Partner Seth Winterroth\" (same source).\n- **Anki rebuilt inside Bedrock.** Co-founder Tom Eliaz \"led their high-scale robotics cloud\" at Anki and Head of Program Management Meghan McDowell ran Anki programs ([src](https://bedrockrobotics.com/about)). Waymo had hired Sofman together with 13 ex-Anki staff in 2019 ([src](https://www.therobotreport.com/waymo-hires-former-anki-employees-including-co-founder-sofman/)). The team has moved with him twice.\n- **Angel checks follow the Eclipse graph.** Both public angel checks are Eclipse-backed robotics/autonomy infrastructure companies: Foxglove's Series B (Eclipse participated; Kevin Peterson also an angel) and Noetive's seed (Eclipse lead, a Ryan Gibson build) ([src](https://foxglove.dev/blog/foxglove-series-b), [src](https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed)).\n- **Eclipse never led again after the seed.** Eclipse led the seed; 8VC led the $60M Series A (\"$60 million Series A, led by 8VC\") ([src](https://8vc.com/resources/bedrock-series-a)); CapitalG and the Valor Atreides AI Fund co-led the B; CapitalG and Emergence co-led the C. Eclipse re-upped in each round ([src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/bedrock-robotics-raises-270-million-in-series-b-funding-to-accelerate-the-future-of-autonomous-construction-302679014.html), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/)).\n- Podcast insights (Inspired, 2026-06-03): see \"Podcast and talk appearances\" and `data/rows/boris-sofman-insights.csv`.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Not a fund and not a check writer at my scale: no disclosed angel check size and only two public angel deals, so `check_capacity: sub-10m`. He matters as (1) a top-tier physical-AI founder whose company ran Eclipse (formation) -> 8VC (A) -> CapitalG/Valor Atreides (B) -> CapitalG/Emergence (C) in about 15 months from launch; (2) a node in the Eclipse build network (Noetive, Foxglove) whose angel presence signals robotics-infra quality; (3) a reference and talent source for autonomy founders (Waymo trucking and Anki alumni). The deals that matter to me: Bedrock (the Eclipse co-build template that produces giant early rounds), Noetive (Eclipse $41M seed).\n\n## Background\n- PhD in Robotics, Carnegie Mellon University ([src](https://bedrockrobotics.com/about/team/boris-sofman)).\n- 2010: co-founded Anki with Mark Palatucci and Hanns Tappeiner (secondary [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anki_(American_company))). Backers included Andreessen Horowitz, Index Ventures and Two Sigma; J.P. Morgan led later rounds (same, secondary). Anki raised more than $200M and claimed nearly $100M revenue in 2017, then shut in 2019; Digital Dream Labs bought its assets ([src](https://www.therobotreport.com/waymo-hires-former-anki-employees-including-co-founder-sofman/)). The Inspired show notes credit Anki with more than 3.7M robots shipped ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/boris-sofman-went-from-leading-autonomous-trucking/id1455941009?i=1000770913351)).\n- 2019-06: joined Waymo with 13 ex-Anki employees as Director of Engineering, Head of Trucking ([src](https://www.therobotreport.com/waymo-hires-former-anki-employees-including-co-founder-sofman/)). Later also led perception, reliability and freeway driving for the robotaxi network ([src](https://bedrockrobotics.com/about/team/boris-sofman)); 8VC says he helmed the Waymo One freeway launch ([src](https://8vc.com/resources/bedrock-series-a)). Left in 2024: Alexa von Tobel says on Inspired that he \"left with a number of other amazing Waymo leaders in 2024\" (20:43), and he puts his Waymo tenure at about five years (05:07) ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/boris-sofman-went-from-leading-autonomous-trucking/id1455941009?i=1000770913351)). Month unconfirmed: the Eclipse, 8VC, TechCrunch and Bedrock pages I checked give none.\n- ~2023-09 onward: listed as Two Sigma Ventures advisor (archive bracket above).\n- 2024: co-founded Bedrock Robotics with Kevin Peterson, Tom Eliaz and Ajay Gummalla, co-built with Eclipse ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://bedrockrobotics.com/about/team/boris-sofman | Company bio page; returns 200 (checked 2026-11-11). No personal site found. |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/bsofman | Listed in Inspired show notes; returns 200 (2026-11-11); follower count not checked (X needs login) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/bsofman | Listed in Inspired show notes; LinkedIn refuses automated checks (HTTP 405), so not verified beyond that listing |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-06-03 — Inspired with Alexa von Tobel — \"Boris Sofman Went from Leading Autonomous Trucking at Waymo to Building Bedrock Robotics\" — [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/boris-sofman-went-from-leading-autonomous-trucking/id1455941009?i=1000770913351) — what stood out (machine transcript of the published audio; timestamps are audio positions):\n  - 05:07: about five years at Waymo, running trucking plus perception and other shared teams; drove the freeway-driverless launch (end of 2023).\n  - 09:50: much of the Bedrock team came from Waymo's trucking program: \"We just kind of really loved working together.\"\n  - 20:43: the host dates his Waymo exit to 2024, leaving with other Waymo leaders.\n  - 21:00-22:30: the origin. Waymo's ML-first stack generalized across cities and vehicles, so they looked for other diverse, fragmented domains and \"really landed\" on heavy machinery.\n  - 26:24-28:20: retrofit rather than new machines, because AV vehicle programs took years and hundreds of millions; excavators can fail safe to a stop.\n  - 33:09: the contractor's case is margin. Lower bid contingency means \"A GC will win every single bid.\"\n  - 35:48: physical AI will be vertically integrated, not one foundation model.\n  - 36:59: \"I'm pessimistic about this wave of humanoids\", especially generalist home robots.\n  - 41:36: he also seriously explored manufacturing automation.\n  - Nothing on fundraising, investors or Eclipse in the episode. Von Tobel names Bright AI and TreeSwift as physical-AI companies (36:18); the machine transcript does not make clear whether she or Inspired invested.\n- 2026-11-04 — Automated podcast (Automate.org) Episode 66 — [link](https://www.automate.org/automated-podcast/episodes/boris-sofman) — not mined (page returned 403).\n- 2020-05-06 — Autonocast #188 — \"Boris Sofman, Head of Trucking at Waymo\" — [link](https://www.autonocast.com/blog/2020/5/6/188-boris-sofman-head-of-trucking-at-waymo) — not mined.\n- Undated — Scale Exchange talk, \"Waymo autonomous trucking data journey\" — [link](https://exchange.scale.com/public/videos/waymo-autonomous-trucking-data-journey) — not mined (login wall).\n\n## How they think\n- Construction as the next autonomy market: \"Construction is one of the lifebloods of this country\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock)).\n- Reuse over reinvention: \"We used to have to reinvent everything\" (same source), describing how far autonomy tooling has come since Anki/Waymo; Bedrock retrofits existing machines rather than building its own.\n- Calls right: CapitalG says the founders bet fully autonomous equipment would arrive \"far sooner\" than experts expected and were \"resoundingly correct\" ([src](https://capitalg.com/insights/doubling-down-on-bedrock/)).\n- Calls wrong: Anki's consumer-robot model failed after one lost funding round (secondary [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anki_(American_company))); Waymo later shut the trucking program he had led ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/16/ex-waymo-engineers-launch-bedrock-robotics-with-80m-to-automate-construction/)).\n- Market selection: he looks for domains where data-driven autonomy transfers across messy, diverse environments, and avoids hyper-engineered single-site solutions (Inspired, 21:00-22:30).\n- Physical AI is a vertical-integration game, so expect several very large vertical winners rather than one model (Inspired, 35:48).\n- Bearish on near-term generalist and home humanoids; more positive on application-specific manipulation in warehouses and manufacturing (Inspired, 36:59-38:45).\n- Expects Waymo to restart trucking by about 2030 and robotaxis in every major US metro within three to four years (Inspired, 14:07-17:15).\n- Mantra: the best solutions come where unrelated industries overlap (Inspired, 40:48).\n\n## Read these first\n- Eclipse, \"Breaking ground with Bedrock\" (2026-04-30): the co-build origin and intro path. https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock\n- 8VC Series A post (2025-07-16): why the A lead backed him. https://8vc.com/resources/bedrock-series-a\n- CapitalG, \"Doubling down on Bedrock\" (2026-10-26): the B-to-C timing story. https://capitalg.com/insights/doubling-down-on-bedrock/\n- Inspired podcast (2026-06-03): his own account of Waymo, the Bedrock origin and humanoids. https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/boris-sofman-went-from-leading-autonomous-trucking/id1455941009?i=1000770913351\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed: X requires login for posts. Bedrock's company accounts (@BedrockRobotics) carry most announcements ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/boris-sofman-went-from-leading-autonomous-trucking/id1455941009?i=1000770913351)).\n\n## Portfolio\nAngel and operator checks (his own money):\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Foxglove | Series B ($40M; angel) | 2025-11-11 | no | Bessemer (lead), Eclipse, Amplify, Icehouse; angels incl. Kevin Peterson, Tobi Lutke, Alex Kendall | [src](https://foxglove.dev/blog/foxglove-series-b) |\n| Noetive | Seed ($41M; angel) | 2026-09-16 | no | Eclipse (lead), Craft, Westly Group, Factory and others; angels incl. Mike Schroepfer, Andrew Bosworth | [src](https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed) |\n\nCrunchbase also shows two investments, latest Noetive ([src, aggregator](https://www.crunchbase.com/person/boris-sofman)), consistent with the two above. Check sizes not disclosed.\n\nHis own company, Bedrock Robotics (founder, not investor):\n\n| Round | Date | Amount / valuation | Lead(s) | Notes | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Pre-seed (formation; Eclipse Venture Equity co-build) | 2024 | undisclosed | Eclipse | months of co-work before incorporation | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock) |\n| Seed + Series A (announced together) | 2025-07-16 | $80M total; A = $60M, so seed about $20M (inferred) | Eclipse (seed), 8VC (A) | TSV, Valor, NVentures, Crossbeam, Raine, Tishman Speyer, Atreides, Al Rajhi, Samsara; John Krafcik early investor | [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/bedrock-robotics-emerges-from-stealth-with-80m-in-funding-for-autonomous-construction-technology-302506159.html), [src](https://8vc.com/resources/bedrock-series-a) |\n| Series B | 2026-02-04 | $270M at ~$1.75B | CapitalG, Valor Atreides AI Fund | Eclipse, 8VC, Emergence, Xora, others | [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/bedrock-robotics-raises-270-million-in-series-b-funding-to-accelerate-the-future-of-autonomous-construction-302679014.html) |\n| Series C | 2026-10-26 | $600M at $4.1B | CapitalG, Emergence Capital | new: USIT, Bessemer, Temasek; existing incl. Eclipse, 8VC, Nvidia | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n**Bedrock (founder side).** Sofman went to Eclipse's Aidan Madigan-Curtis while choosing what to build after Waymo; Peterson came via Seth Winterroth. Madigan-Curtis and Ryan Gibson spent months with them choosing the problem and go-to-market before incorporation in 2024 ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock)). The company stayed quiet more than a year, then announced $80M on 2025-07-16 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/16/ex-waymo-engineers-launch-bedrock-robotics-with-80m-to-automate-construction/)). Timing gaps: launch to B about 6.5 months (2026-02-04); B to C about 8.5 months (2026-10-26), which CapitalG calls \"less than a year after leading their Series B\" ([src](https://capitalg.com/insights/doubling-down-on-bedrock/)). The C came two months after the excavators moved from pilots to live construction sites ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/)). Emergence's portfolio page says it partnered in 2025, before the B was announced (per existing repo rows citing https://www.emcap.com/portfolio/bedrock-robotics; not re-checked). Timing from his side: he left Waymo in 2024 (Inspired host, 20:43) and dates Bedrock's start to a little over two years before June 2026 (27:49), so the Eclipse co-build months began almost directly after the Waymo exit. Eclipse's role: formation partner and seed lead, then a steady re-upper while larger funds led each later round.\n\n**Noetive (angel).** Noetive is Ryan Gibson's next Eclipse build after Bedrock; Sofman is named as \"co-founder and CEO of Bedrock Robotics, Boris Sofman\" among its angels ([src](https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed)). Read as Eclipse founders backing each other's builds; how the allocation came about is not stated.\n\n**Foxglove (angel).** He and Bedrock CTO Kevin Peterson were both new angels in the Series B, which Bessemer led and Eclipse joined ([src](https://foxglove.dev/blog/foxglove-series-b)). Foxglove makes robotics data tooling, so this looks like a customer-adjacent infrastructure bet (inferred).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Eclipse (Aidan Madigan-Curtis, Ryan Gibson) | Bedrock co-build partners; seed lead | 2024 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock) |\n| Kevin Peterson | Bedrock co-founder/CTO, ex-Waymo; co-angel in Foxglove | 2024 | [src](https://foxglove.dev/blog/foxglove-series-b) |\n| Tom Eliaz | Bedrock co-founder; Anki colleague | Anki era | [src](https://bedrockrobotics.com/about) |\n| Ajay Gummalla | Bedrock co-founder; Waymo veteran | 2024 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/16/ex-waymo-engineers-launch-bedrock-robotics-with-80m-to-automate-construction/) |\n| Two Sigma Ventures | Advisor (listed); Anki and Bedrock investor | ~2023-09 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250609222741/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/) |\n| John Krafcik | Ex-Waymo CEO; early Bedrock investor | 2025-07 (disclosed) | [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/bedrock-robotics-emerges-from-stealth-with-80m-in-funding-for-autonomous-construction-technology-302506159.html) |\n| 8VC (Alex Kolicich) | Series A lead | 2025-07 | [src](https://8vc.com/resources/bedrock-series-a) |\n| CapitalG (Derek Zanutto) | Series B and C co-lead | 2026-02 | [src](https://capitalg.com/insights/doubling-down-on-bedrock/) |\n| Alexa von Tobel (Inspired Capital) | Podcast host | 2026-06 | [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/boris-sofman-went-from-leading-autonomous-trucking/id1455941009?i=1000770913351) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When exactly did he leave Waymo, and did Waymo or Alphabet take any Bedrock equity? (CapitalG is Alphabet's growth fund; not established as a Waymo-related link.)\n- Did his Two Sigma Ventures advisory role lead to TSV's Bedrock allocation?\n- Other angel checks beyond Foxglove and Noetive (Crunchbase shows only two).\n- Mine the Automate.org (2026-11) and Autonocast (2020) episodes.\n- Bedrock Form D filings: EDGAR full-text search for \"Bedrock Robotics\" returned nothing on 2026-11-11; the issuer may file under another name.\n\n## Sources\n- https://bedrockrobotics.com/about and https://bedrockrobotics.com/about/team/boris-sofman\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock\n- https://8vc.com/resources/bedrock-series-a\n- https://capitalg.com/insights/doubling-down-on-bedrock/\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/bedrock-robotics-emerges-from-stealth-with-80m-in-funding-for-autonomous-construction-technology-302506159.html\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/bedrock-robotics-raises-270-million-in-series-b-funding-to-accelerate-the-future-of-autonomous-construction-302679014.html\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/16/ex-waymo-engineers-launch-bedrock-robotics-with-80m-to-automate-construction/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/\n- https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed\n- https://foxglove.dev/blog/foxglove-series-b\n- https://www.therobotreport.com/waymo-hires-former-anki-employees-including-co-founder-sofman/\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anki_(American_company) (secondary)\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20250609222741/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/ (plus 2023-06, 2023-09 and 2026-03 snapshots)\n- https://www.crunchbase.com/person/boris-sofman (aggregator)\n- Inspired podcast (2026-06-03) audio via the publisher feed https://feeds.megaphone.fm/MANV9211661499. Quotes come from a machine transcript (Whisper base.en, run locally) of the published audio. Timestamps are audio positions.\n", "person:brad-lightcap": "\n# Brad Lightcap\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (strategics sweep 2). Rows: `data/rows/brad-lightcap-{deals,rels,insights,leads,tags,photos}.csv`. Fund detail is in [firms/openai-startup-fund.md](../firms/openai-startup-fund.md) and [sources/openai-startup-fund-check-writers.md](../sources/openai-startup-fund-check-writers.md).*\n\n**known_for:** OpenAI's first CFO and then COO (2018-2026), and the quoted \"manager\" of the OpenAI Startup Fund. He left in 2026 to start something unnamed.\n\n*Update 2026-11-10 (second pass): re-ran checks (0 hard errors); photo re-verified (HTTP 200, image/png) and added to `brad-lightcap-photos.csv`. No new deals or announcement of his next venture found.*\n\n## One-line take\nFor eight years he ran OpenAI's commercial and dealmaking side, and in late 2026 he is a free agent with an unnamed next venture \"not going far\" from OpenAI. That makes him worth knowing now, before he is a GP or a founder with a round to fill.\n\n## Crux\n- **What makes him win:** He built most of OpenAI's non-research functions. He was the OpenAI voice on its strategic deals (the Startup Fund's first deals, the Thrive Holdings stake, DeployCo), and Altman's trust is the asset. The first versions of finance, legal, people, go-to-market and partnerships were his ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/brad-lightcap-openais-longtime-coo-is-leaving-to-start-something-new/)).\n- **What constrains him:** On the fund he was the name in the press release, not the legal signer or the deal partner. Altman signed the Form Ds as manager of the GP until 2024-03, and TechCrunch credits Hathaway with leading Harvey and Speak ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1877240/000187724023000001/primary_doc.xml), [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/01/sam-altman-gives-up-control-of-openai-startup-fund-resolving-unusual-corporate-venture-structure/)). No personal angel checks were found.\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** Publicly back something that competes with OpenAI. His exit note says he believes in OpenAI \"more than ever\" ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2026/08/11/openai-exec-brad-lightcap-leaving-start-something-new/)).\n\n## What he personally signs, sources or prices\n| Pool | His role | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI Startup Fund I (2021-) | Public \"manager\", quoted on the first five announced deals. **Not a legal signer**: Altman signed the GP until 2024-03, then Hathaway | [openai.fund](https://openai.fund/news/introducing-our-first-investments), [1X](https://1x.tech/discover/1x-rasies-23-5m-in-series-a2-funding-led-by-open-ai), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1877240/000187724023000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| OpenAI balance sheet (strategic stakes) | Quoted principal on the no-cash equity stake in Thrive Holdings (2025-12) | [TMTPost](https://en.tmtpost.com/post/7790995) |\n| DeployCo (~$10B OpenAI-majority LLC, 2026-05) | Placed under his special-projects remit in the 2026-04 memo. Whether he led the raise is **unconfirmed** | [OfficeChai (paraphrase of memo)](https://officechai.com/ai/openais-ceo-of-applications-fidji-simo-goes-on-medical-leave-coo-brad-lightcap-moves-to-special-projects), [Reuters/FT via Yahoo](https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/openai-talks-commit-1-5-041319295.html) |\n| Personal / angel | Nothing found | Checked: press, fund releases, aggregator search (vcbeast returned 429) |\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The public title outlived the legal control.** His self-supplied bio for Milken's May 2024 conference still said: \"He also manages the OpenAI Startup Fund.\" [src](https://milkeninstitute.org/events/global-conference-2024/speakers/brad-lightcap) That was two months after Hathaway took over GP I (2024-03) ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/01/sam-altman-gives-up-control-of-openai-startup-fund-resolving-unusual-corporate-venture-structure/)). Read his fund title as a public title, not as control.\n- **OpenAI pays for strategic stakes with people, not cash.** He fronted the Thrive Holdings deal as a model for \"how businesses and industries around the world can deeply partner\" with OpenAI [src](https://en.tmtpost.com/post/7790995). No cash was reported, and \"If those companies succeed, OpenAI's stake will grow\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/01/openais-investment-into-thrive-holdings-is-its-latest-circular-deal/). The counterparty was Josh Kushner, his first call in the Nov 2023 crisis ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2023/12/07/joshua-kushner-thrive-openai-stripe-skims-warby-parker-kkr/)).\n- **DeployCo's terms look like debt.** Per FT via Reuters, the PE backers (TPG, Bain Capital, Advent, Brookfield, Goanna) get a guaranteed 17.5% annual return for five years, and \"OpenAI will have super-voting shares in DeployCo.\" [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/openai-talks-commit-1-5-041319295.html) The 2026-04 memo put DeployCo under Lightcap's special projects ([OfficeChai, paraphrase](https://officechai.com/ai/openais-ceo-of-applications-fidji-simo-goes-on-medical-leave-coo-brad-lightcap-moves-to-special-projects)).\n- **The exit was in two steps.** He was moved out of the COO role on 2026-04-03 into special projects reporting to Altman, and Denise Dresser took his commercial scope ([OfficeChai](https://officechai.com/ai/openais-ceo-of-applications-fidji-simo-goes-on-medical-leave-coo-brad-lightcap-moves-to-special-projects)). He announced his departure on 2026-08-11: \"I believe in OpenAI more than ever.\" [src](https://fortune.com/2026/08/11/openai-exec-brad-lightcap-leaving-start-something-new/)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, but for a new reason.** The task brief framed him as a commercial contact rather than a check-writer. That changed in 2026: he no longer holds OpenAI's commercial keys (Dresser does), and he is building something unnamed that he describes as close to OpenAI. For a $20M frontier-AI seed check, the likely roles are, in order:\n1. **Founder or GP of a new vehicle (unconfirmed).** His record (YC Continuity, OpenAI CFO, fund face, DeployCo structuring) fits a capital vehicle or an OpenAI-adjacent company. If it is a fund, he becomes a co-investor with unusual reach into OpenAI. If it is a company, it is a possible seed deal.\n2. **An intro path into OpenAI**, through Altman, Hathaway and Kushner.\n3. Not a price-setter in later rounds himself. The Startup Fund (Hathaway) and OpenAI corporate development (Albert Lee, reporting to Sarah Friar) now play that role ([firms/openai-startup-fund.md](../firms/openai-startup-fund.md)).\n\nDeals that matter to the owner: Harvey seed (fund led, 2022-11), 1X Series A2 (fund led, 2023-03). Both were first institutional or early priced money from a lab-affiliated fund.\n\n## Background\n- Duke University, degrees in economics and history ([Milken bio](https://milkeninstitute.org/events/global-conference-2024/speakers/brad-lightcap)).\n- JPMorgan, investment banking analyst in New York. Dropbox, head of strategic finance. Y Combinator, investor ([GCV Powerlist 2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2023-brad-lightcap/?format=md)); the Milken bio specifies YC Continuity. Dates for these roles are **unconfirmed**.\n- OpenAI: CFO from 2018, COO from May 2022 ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2023-brad-lightcap/?format=md)). Mandate expanded 2025-03 to \"overseeing day-to-day operations and international expansion\" plus corporate partnerships ([Silicon UK](https://silicon.co.uk/cloud/openai-lightcap-execs-605263)). Special projects 2026-04. Departure announced 2026-08-11, staying \"a few more weeks\" ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2026/08/11/openai-exec-brad-lightcap-leaving-start-something-new/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | [@bradlightcap](https://x.com/bradlightcap) | Listed in the Milken speaker page metadata; he posted his departure there (Fortune, 2026-08-11). Follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-04-01 — Uncapped with Jack Altman — #46 \"Brad Lightcap from OpenAI\" — [metacast](https://metacast.app/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/jR35iX38/uncapped-46-brad-lightcap-from-openai/KDtkftr0) — per the listing, about AI moving from chat to agents. Transcript not read (page 403). Recorded around the time of the 04-03 reshuffle.\n- 2024-05 — Milken Global Conference 2024 — speaker ([page](https://milkeninstitute.org/events/global-conference-2024/speakers/brad-lightcap)).\n\n## How they think\n- On the fund's thesis (2022-12): \"We believe there is an enormous amount of value yet to be unlocked for the world with AI\" ([openai.fund](https://openai.fund/news/introducing-our-first-investments)). The first four bets were apps built on OpenAI models (legal, video, notes, language), which is the customer-tie thesis.\n- On robotics (2023-03): 1X is \"at the forefront of augmenting labor\" ([1X](https://1x.tech/discover/1x-rasies-23-5m-in-series-a2-funding-led-by-open-ai)). This was the fund's first hardware bet.\n- On strategic partnerships (2025-12): equity for embedded talent as a repeatable template ([TMTPost](https://en.tmtpost.com/post/7790995)).\n\n## Read these first\n- [OpenAI Startup Fund, \"Introducing our first investments\" (2022-12-01)](https://openai.fund/news/introducing-our-first-investments): his quotes on all four deals.\n- [Fortune, departure (2026-08-11)](https://fortune.com/2026/08/11/openai-exec-brad-lightcap-leaving-start-something-new/).\n- [Reuters/FT on DeployCo terms (2026-04-22)](https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/openai-talks-commit-1-5-041319295.html).\n- [TechCrunch on the Thrive Holdings stake (2025-12-01)](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/01/openais-investment-into-thrive-holdings-is-its-latest-circular-deal/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot verified; the X account was not fetched.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Descript | Series C ($50M) | 2022-11 | yes (fund) | Daniel Gross | [openai.fund](https://openai.fund/news/introducing-our-first-investments) |\n| Harvey | Seed ($5M) | 2022-11-23 | yes (fund) | Elad Gil; Sarah Guo; Jeff Dean | [openai.fund](https://openai.fund/news/introducing-our-first-investments) |\n| Mem | Series A ($23.5M) | 2022-11 | yes (fund) | — | [openai.fund](https://openai.fund/news/introducing-our-first-investments) |\n| Speak | Series B ($27M) | 2022-11 | yes (fund) | — | [openai.fund](https://openai.fund/news/introducing-our-first-investments) |\n| 1X | Series A2 ($23.5M) | 2023-03-23 | yes (fund) | Tiger Global; Sandwater; Alliance Ventures; Skagerak | [1X](https://1x.tech/discover/1x-rasies-23-5m-in-series-a2-funding-led-by-open-ai) |\n| Thrive Holdings | Strategic (no-cash stake for embedded staff) | 2025-12-01 | no | Thrive Capital | [TMTPost](https://en.tmtpost.com/post/7790995) |\n\nRound sizes for Descript, Mem and Speak come from the TechCrunch 2025-03-01 roundup cited in the fund's rows. In every Startup Fund row he is the quoted face, not a confirmed deal lead.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Harvey:** the founders cold-emailed Sam Altman and Jason Kwon, not Lightcap, and TechCrunch credits Hathaway with leading the deal ([TechCrunch 2025-11-14](https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/14/inside-harvey-how-a-first-year-legal-associate-built-one-of-silicon-valleys-hottest-startups/), [TechCrunch 2024-04-01](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/01/sam-altman-gives-up-control-of-openai-startup-fund-resolving-unusual-corporate-venture-structure/)). Lightcap announced it.\n- **Thrive Holdings:** OpenAI provides engineering, research and product staff; its stake grows with portfolio outcomes ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/01/openais-investment-into-thrive-holdings-is-its-latest-circular-deal/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sam Altman | Reported to him in the special-projects role; earlier at YC together | 2026-04 | [OfficeChai](https://officechai.com/ai/openais-ceo-of-applications-fidji-simo-goes-on-medical-leave-coo-brad-lightcap-moves-to-special-projects), [Tribune](https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/business/openai-coo-brad-lightcap-departs-after-eight-years-to-start-something-new/) |\n| Ian Hathaway | Hathaway did the fund's deal work; Lightcap was its public face | 2021 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/01/sam-altman-gives-up-control-of-openai-startup-fund-resolving-unusual-corporate-venture-structure/) |\n| Josh Kushner | First call in the Nov 2023 crisis; counterparty on the Thrive Holdings stake | 2023-11 | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2023/12/07/joshua-kushner-thrive-openai-stripe-skims-warby-parker-kkr/) |\n| Denise Dresser | Took over his commercial scope | 2026-04 | [OfficeChai](https://officechai.com/ai/openais-ceo-of-applications-fidji-simo-goes-on-medical-leave-coo-brad-lightcap-moves-to-special-projects) |\n| Jack Altman | Uncapped #46 guest | 2026-04 | [metacast](https://metacast.app/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/jR35iX38/uncapped-46-brad-lightcap-from-openai/KDtkftr0) |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** OpenAI's channels are closed now that he has left. The paths are Jack Altman (who hosted him in 2026-04), Josh Kushner, or Ian Hathaway. Watch @bradlightcap for the announcement he promised (\"more to share soon\").\n\n## Open questions\n- What is the \"something new\"? Fund, company, or an OpenAI-affiliated vehicle? As of 2026-11-10 nothing has been announced in search results.\n- Did he lead the DeployCo raise and choose its PE backers, or only oversee it?\n- Did he hold any personal carry or GP economics in Fund I? None appears in the Form D or ADV.\n- Dates for the JPMorgan, Dropbox and YC Continuity roles.\n\n## Sources\n- https://openai.fund/news/introducing-our-first-investments\n- https://1x.tech/discover/1x-rasies-23-5m-in-series-a2-funding-led-by-open-ai\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2023-brad-lightcap/?format=md\n- https://milkeninstitute.org/events/global-conference-2024/speakers/brad-lightcap\n- https://www.insead.edu/bio/brad-lightcap\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1877240/000187724023000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/01/sam-altman-gives-up-control-of-openai-startup-fund-resolving-unusual-corporate-venture-structure/\n- https://silicon.co.uk/cloud/openai-lightcap-execs-605263\n- https://en.tmtpost.com/post/7790995\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/01/openais-investment-into-thrive-holdings-is-its-latest-circular-deal/\n- https://officechai.com/ai/openais-ceo-of-applications-fidji-simo-goes-on-medical-leave-coo-brad-lightcap-moves-to-special-projects\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/openai-talks-commit-1-5-041319295.html\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/brad-lightcap-openais-longtime-coo-is-leaving-to-start-something-new/\n- https://fortune.com/2026/08/11/openai-exec-brad-lightcap-leaving-start-something-new/\n- https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/business/openai-coo-brad-lightcap-departs-after-eight-years-to-start-something-new/\n- https://fortune.com/2023/12/07/joshua-kushner-thrive-openai-stripe-skims-warby-parker-kkr/\n- https://metacast.app/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/jR35iX38/uncapped-46-brad-lightcap-from-openai/KDtkftr0\n", "person:brad-svrluga": "\n# Brad Svrluga\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 by the Etched price-setters pass. I used three web searches and otherwise fetched known URLs. No full podcast transcript of Svrluga's own was available except a third-party auto-transcript of a 2026-02-10 interview (see the sourcing note under Differential insight).*\n\n## One-line take\nSvrluga does not do AI-chip deals. What they control is the size and split of Primary's funds, and that, not deal-picking, decides whether Primary can keep its stake in a breakout like Etched, which went from about a $34M seed price to $21B in 41 months.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** They are the fund-builder half of Primary. They co-founded the firm with Ben Sun in 2015 after co-founding High Peaks Venture Partners ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brad-svrluga)), and they sign the firm's capital story. They wrote the $625M Fund V post, \"the largest standalone seed firm in the market\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/scaling-for-the-ai-era-fund-v), 2026-02-10). Its LP pitch rests on Fund I: \"9 of the 25 investments in our first fund reached unicorn status\" (same source). They are a legal \"related person\" on the PVP Fund IV Form D ([src](https://filingflow.app/filings/0000897069-22-000445), 2022-07-01).\n- **What constrains them:** Their model is seed-first by design: \"Primary leads pre-seed and seed rounds across tech sectors\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/scaling-for-the-ai-era-fund-v)). Follow-on capital sits in separate, much smaller Select vehicles:\n  - Select II: $50M, beside a $150M Fund III ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/24/primary-venture-capital-third-fund/))\n  - Select III: reportedly $150M, beside a $275M Fund IV ([aggregator: Caplight](https://www.caplight.com/investor/primary))\n  - Fund V: no split disclosed\n\n  Etched's later rounds were $500M (B), $300M (C) and $700M ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/), [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference), [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one)). **Inference, not sourced:** a Select fund of $50–150M that serves a whole portfolio can't hold meaningful pro rata in a company priced at $5–21B. That probably explains why Etched's C post doesn't list Primary, while Etched's later $700M post does.\n- **What they'd never do (inference):** Turn Primary into a multistage platform. They frame the mega platforms as the competition: \"They're sucking a lot of oxygen out of the room\" ([transcript](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-02-10/primary-venture-partners-raises-625m-fund-v-betting-on-seed-as-mega-platforms-overextend-downmarket/transcript), 2026-02-10).\n\n## Differential insight\n*Sourcing note:* bullets marked \"transcript\" come from a third-party auto-generated transcript on TBPN Digest, dated 2026-02-10. The digest page does not name the original show. The thumbnail path suggests a YouTube segment (ID 9K0RY6Bet1I), but I haven't confirmed that. I used only phrases that came back identically in two separate reads of that page. The summarizer returned other lines in different wordings on each read, so I dropped those, including the line about seeding multiple competitors and backing up the truck for the winner.\n\n- **The Select fund only backs winners after the fact.** They describe it as a fund that \"doubles down on uh some of the biggest and most successful\" portfolio companies ([transcript](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-02-10/primary-venture-partners-raises-625m-fund-v-betting-on-seed-as-mega-platforms-overextend-downmarket/transcript), 2026-02-10). This is the mechanism behind Primary's Etched follow-ons. Who gets the Select dollars is a co-CIO decision, which makes Svrluga the gatekeeper for pro rata in compute winners. That last step is an inference from their title.\n- **They have a long memory of downmarket cycles.** They say they have seen \"three full cycles of of of the kind of big firms coming down market\" (same transcript). They read the current multistage push into seed as cyclical, not permanent.\n- **Their LP-mechanics view.** \"The asset class has not done a great job of returning more dollars\" (same transcript). They then say LPs \"trusted us with a big pile of new capital\" anyway. Pair this with the Fund V claim that \"our existing LPs have doubled down on our strategy\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/scaling-for-the-ai-era-fund-v)): Primary raises on re-ups, not on a newly named anchor. No Fund V LP is named.\n- **Their seed doctrine from 2021.** Seed had become \"the first third of the A\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/24/primary-venture-capital-third-fund/), 2021-02-24). Primary prices pre-product rounds: \"A whole bunch of what we do has always been way pre-product\" (same source). That doctrine is what let the firm lead Etched before tape-out. The Etched seed in 2023-03 was about $5.4M at about $34M ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=662004)).\n- **Etched was a first for the firm under their co-CIO watch, but they didn't champion it.** Primary says \"we had never made an investment in semis\" before Etched, and Brian Schechter \"has led these investments\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round), 2026-06-30). No source puts Svrluga on Etched. Fund V lists \"Infrastructure/Compute\" as a sector ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/scaling-for-the-ai-era-fund-v)), but Svrluga's own post never mentions Etched.\n- **Fund IV paperwork names a partner missing from the public roster.** The PVP Fund IV Form D lists Bradley Svrluga, Benjamin Sun, Anne Lysak, Cassie Young and Jason Shuman as related persons ([src](https://filingflow.app/filings/0000897069-22-000445), 2022-07-01). Anne Lysak does not appear in the firm file's people table. They are probably finance or operations staff, but that is unconfirmed (needs-id).\n- **The fund-size record conflicts, so treat the numbers loosely.**\n  - Caplight says Primary raised $425M in September 2022: $275M seed plus $150M Select ([aggregator](https://www.caplight.com/investor/primary)).\n  - A search snippet said a Select Fund III Form D also showed $275M. I didn't fetch that filing.\n  - The F4 FundFund profile dates the same raise to fall 2025 ([aggregator](https://f4.fund/firms/primary-venture-partners)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** They are not a frontier-AI or AI-chip dealmaker. They are on no compute, lab or pre-training deal, and their focus areas are enterprise software, supply chain and industrial tech ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brad-svrluga)). They matter to THESIS.md for two reasons:\n1. They sit next to high-fit names. As co-CIO and the author of the fund structure, they set the seed/Select split and so decide how long Primary can stay in compute winners like Etched. That makes them a gatekeeper for any co-investment or secondary block that might come out of Primary's compute book.\n2. They are a firsthand source on LP mechanics. They have raised five fund families, see LPs as \"starved for liquidity\" (transcript, single read, so not used as an insight row), and wrote the Fund V re-up story.\n\nThey are not a peer for $20M checks into $600M lab seeds. Primary leads seed rounds of single-digit millions. The deals that matter to me run through Schechter (Etched, Atero, Haiqu, The Biological Computing Company), not Svrluga.\n\n## Background\n- Has backed founders \"since 2000\" and co-founded High Peaks Venture Partners before Primary ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brad-svrluga)). I could not find the High Peaks founding date or what Svrluga did before it. A possible DFJ Gotham link is unconfirmed.\n- They met Ben Sun in 2013 for a lunch planned as one hour that \"led to Primary's founding\" (Harlem Capital write-up of a Ben Sun episode, [src](https://harlem.capital/inside-primary-vc-building-new-yorks-best-seed-firm/), paraphrased by the page).\n- Co-founded Primary in 2015 ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brad-svrluga)). They said in 2026 that they have \"been in this business for a couple of dozen years\" (transcript, single read).\n- Ranked #17 on Insider's Seed 100, per the team page (no date given).\n- Education: not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.primary.vc/team/brad-svrluga | Team page, portfolio list |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.primary.vc/insights | Bylined the Fund V post (2026-02-10). Older Primary posts are on Medium |\n| X | https://x.com/bradsvrluga | Linked from team page. Not read (X doesn't render without login). Follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/bradsvrluga/ | Linked from team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-02-10 — interview transcribed on TBPN Digest (original show not named on the page) — \"Primary Venture Partners raises $625M Fund V…\" — [transcript](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-02-10/primary-venture-partners-raises-625m-fund-v-betting-on-seed-as-mega-platforms-overextend-downmarket/transcript) — The Select fund doubles down on the biggest winners. They have seen three cycles of big firms coming downmarket. The current AI cycle is \"foundational and infrastructure-driven\" (digest paraphrase) and Bay Area-centric.\n- 2026-01 — Vintage Investment Partners 19th Annual VC Summit — \"Building the Next Gen Seed Platform\" — [page](https://vintage-ip.com/interviews/annual-summit-primary-ventures-building-the-next-gen-seed-platform-with-brad-svrluga/) — The landing page has only a summary, no transcript. This audience is fund-of-funds LPs.\n- Early 2026 (inferred from the \"kick off the year\" framing) — Innovation with Mark Peter Davis — \"The Gold Standard for Seed VC: Inside Primary\" — [page](https://innovationwithmpd.captivate.fm/episode/the-gold-standard-for-seed-vc-inside-primary) — Chapters cover \"Advice for Emerging VCs: Sourcing, Picking, and LPs\" and \"Scaling with Specialized Partners\". No transcript. The host calls them \"a mentor and a close friend of mine for years\".\n- 2021-04-07 — Cornell Tech @ Bloomberg, Ep. 42, with Scarlet Fu — [page](https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/cornell-tech-at-bloomberg-podcast/episode/episode-42---brad-svrluga-co-foundergp-primary-venture-partners-89600) — Covers New York focus and COVID. Not transcribed.\n- Undated — Sales Hacker podcast Ep. 14 (\"A Quick Guide to Seed Fundraising for Salespeople\") — [page](https://gtmnow.com/?p=80068). Also The Unconventional Path EP-75 and EP-141, and Story in a Bottle (2016). Found in search snippets only, not read.\n\n## How they think\n- **Seed is its own asset class, run at institutional scale.** \"We practice the craft of seed investing at institutional scale\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/scaling-for-the-ai-era-fund-v)). Primary is organized as eight sector partners plus a large operating team (same source).\n- **New York and density as a moat (2021).** The talent team's charge is to track \"the top 10% of all talent in New York City\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/24/primary-venture-capital-third-fund/)). By 2026 they say the firm does equal work on both coasts (transcript).\n- **Contrarian on multistage seeding.** They call index-style seeding by platforms \"not a great value proposition for seed founders\" (digest summary page; wording not re-confirmed in the transcript, so no insight row).\n- **AI view.** Vibe-coded demos don't replace domain expertise (transcript paraphrase). They call this AI cycle infrastructure-led and Bay-centric, with New York's turn coming in go-to-market and enterprise. They are not a compute bull in their own words, even though their firm runs a compute lane.\n- **Calls right/wrong:** Fund I produced 9 unicorns from 25 investments ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/scaling-for-the-ai-era-fund-v)). Their 2021 \"Trumpet the demise of New York\" quip to TechCrunch aged fine. I found no admitted mistakes.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Scaling for the AI era: Fund V (2026-02-10). Fund size, the 9-of-25 record, the LP re-up and the sector list. https://www.primary.vc/articles/scaling-for-the-ai-era-fund-v\n2. TechCrunch on Fund III and Select II (2021-02-24). The \"first third of the A\" doctrine and the Select structure. https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/24/primary-venture-capital-third-fund/\n3. The 2026-02-10 interview transcript. The Select mechanics and their view of the platforms. https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-02-10/primary-venture-partners-raises-625m-fund-v-betting-on-seed-as-mega-platforms-overextend-downmarket/transcript\n4. For contrast, Schechter's \"Why we invested in Etched in every round\" (2026-06-30). This is the compute book Svrluga funds but does not run. https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked. X (@bradsvrluga) did not render. Nothing found on who amplifies them.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Alloy | not stated (card) | 2015 | not stated | | [card](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization) |\n| Alma | not stated (card) | 2018 | not stated | | [card](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization) |\n| Bedrock | not stated (card) | 2020 | not stated | co-listed with Zach Fredericks | [card](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization) |\n| Forgepoint | Seed | 2026 | not stated | co-listed with Zach Fredericks | [card](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization) |\n| Black Crow AI, Cargado (page text says \"Cordage\"), Casap, Electric AI, Flock, HLTH, K Health, Kinetic, Latch, Ticketfly, TxVia, Vestwell | not stated | not stated | not stated | | [team page](https://www.primary.vc/team/brad-svrluga) |\n| **Etched** | not named on any round | — | — | — | Schechter \"has led these investments\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round)) |\n\n**Compute, AI-chip and frontier-AI deals: none found.** Svrluga's role in Etched is institutional only. They co-runs the IC and the fund that financed the seed (2023-03, about $34M post, Primary led), the A (2024-06-25, $120M, co-led with Positive Sum) and Primary's follow-ons in the B (2025-12, Stripes led, $5B post) and in the $700M round at $21B (2026-08-18, Jane Street led) ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond), [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/)). Primary was first: it set the only institutional price for about 33 months before Stripes. Sequoia's C (2026-07-23, $10.3B) does not list Primary ([src](https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference)). Several repo files say otherwise: companies/etched.md and patterns/co-investor-gangs.md put Primary, and even Schechter, in the C, and patterns/co-investor-gangs.md says Schechter is \"named in every round\". No primary source I've seen supports either claim.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Etched (institutional, not personal).** Primary says that in 2023 it \"had never made an investment in semis\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round)). So the seed meant the co-CIOs approving a first-ever chip bet at seed size. That was cheap in dollars and big in category risk. No source describes Svrluga's vote or view. Ramtin Naimi's point that the consensus flipped when Sequoia priced it (see people/ramtin-naimi.md) fits Primary's structure: Primary priced the risk, and the platforms priced the consensus. Primary's Select capacity was too small to do much in between (inference).\n- **Fund V (2026-02-10).** $625M across a \"pure seed fund\" and a Select fund (transcript). Existing LPs re-upped ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/scaling-for-the-ai-era-fund-v)). Its timing, five months before Etched's $5B became public, means Fund V's Select sleeve was set before the Etched mark showed up in the books (inference from dates).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Primary Venture Partners | Co-founder, Managing Partner, Co-CIO | 2015 | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brad-svrluga) |\n| Ben Sun | Co-founder and fellow Co-CIO | met 2013, founded 2015 | [src](https://harlem.capital/inside-primary-vc-building-new-yorks-best-seed-firm/) |\n| High Peaks Venture Partners | Co-founder (predecessor to Primary) | before 2015 | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brad-svrluga) |\n| PVP Fund IV, L.P. | Related person on Form D, with Sun, Anne Lysak, Cassie Young, Jason Shuman | 2022-07-01 | [src](https://filingflow.app/filings/0000897069-22-000445) |\n| Brian Schechter | Partner who leads Etched and the compute lane, which Svrluga's funds finance | 2023 (Etched seed) | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round) |\n| Zach Fredericks | Co-listed on Bedrock (2020) and Forgepoint (2026) | 2020 | [card](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization) |\n| Mark Peter Davis | Podcast host; calls Svrluga a mentor and close friend | not stated | [src](https://innovationwithmpd.captivate.fm/episode/the-gold-standard-for-seed-vc-inside-primary) |\n| Vintage Investment Partners | Speaker at its 19th Annual VC Summit (not evidence of an LP link) | 2026-01 | [src](https://vintage-ip.com/interviews/annual-summit-primary-ventures-building-the-next-gen-seed-platform-with-brad-svrluga/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What is Fund V's seed/Select split? Did Select money fund the Etched B and the $700M round? Did Primary skip the C by choice or for lack of capacity?\n- Select III size: Caplight says $150M, while a search snippet says the Form D showed $275M. Fetch the Select III Form D on EDGAR.\n- Who is Anne Lysak (Form D related person)? Possibly a CFO or COO.\n- High Peaks founding date, and anything Svrluga did before it. Education.\n- Does a transcript of the Mark Peter Davis episode exist? Its chapter \"Advice for Emerging VCs: Sourcing, Picking, and LPs\" is the best potential LP-mechanics source.\n- Fund V LP names. Is Vintage (a fund-of-funds) an LP in Primary? Check Vintage's site and Israeli filings.\n- Does the team page's \"Cargado\" mean Cordage (Fredericks' 2024 card)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.primary.vc/team/brad-svrluga\n- https://www.primary.vc/people\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/scaling-for-the-ai-era-fund-v (2026-02-10)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round (2026-06-30)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond (2026-02-10)\n- https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization (via firms/primary-venture-partners.md)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/24/primary-venture-capital-third-fund/\n- https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-02-10/primary-venture-partners-raises-625m-fund-v-betting-on-seed-as-mega-platforms-overextend-downmarket (and /transcript)\n- https://filingflow.app/filings/0000897069-22-000445 (Form D, PVP Fund IV, 2022-07-01)\n- https://www.caplight.com/investor/primary (aggregator) ; https://f4.fund/firms/primary-venture-partners (aggregator)\n- https://vintage-ip.com/interviews/annual-summit-primary-ventures-building-the-next-gen-seed-platform-with-brad-svrluga/\n- https://innovationwithmpd.captivate.fm/episode/the-gold-standard-for-seed-vc-inside-primary\n- https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/cornell-tech-at-bloomberg-podcast/episode/episode-42---brad-svrluga-co-foundergp-primary-venture-partners-89600\n- https://harlem.capital/inside-primary-vc-building-new-yorks-best-seed-firm/\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=662004 ; https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference ; https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one\n", "person:brandon-reeves": "\n# Brandon Reeves\n\n*Created 2026-11-09. Updated 2026-11-10: added Flapping Airplanes (the $1B Series A), a sharper crux (first-in on infra seeds, fast follower on consensus labs) and new leads; pronouns switched to \"they\" because no source I read gives pronouns. The web-search budget was exhausted in this run, so podcasts and X are still unread.*\n\n## One-line take\nReeves is the longest-tenured AI dealmaker on Lux's partner bench: the Lux name on Hugging Face in 2019, years before the AI consensus, and now the Lux partner quoted on Flapping Airplanes' $1B Series A in Oct 2026. They are a node more than a \"rising\" name: their portfolio founders become the angels and first customers for their next deals.\n\n## Crux\n- **Two modes.** Reeves is **first in on compute, infrastructure and new-lab seeds that Lux leads**: Hugging Face in 2019 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2019/12/17/hugging-face-raises-15-million-to-build-the-definitive-natural-language-processing-library/)), the Together seed in 2023 ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/together)), Sakana's founding round in 2024 ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/our-investment-in-japans-sakana-ai-to-build-nature-inspired-ai-foundation-models)), Phonic in 2025 and SkyPilot in 2026 ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/07/21/skypilot-from-databricks-cofounder-raises-20m-to-be-the-switzerland-of-ai-compute/)). They are a **fast follower writing large co-led checks into frontier names that are already consensus**. On Cognition, Lux came in after Founders Fund ([src](https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents)). On Flapping Airplanes, Index, GV and Sequoia did the $180M seed in January 2026, and Lux arrived at the $1B A about nine months later, at roughly 4x the seed price ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/)).\n- **What links the two modes is compute efficiency.** Both SkyPilot and Flapping Airplanes are bets on getting more out of existing chips. On Flapping Airplanes, Reeves says \"getting more out of what we have\" matters for \"the next frontier\" ([src](https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/)).\n- **The flywheel.** Reeves recycles the portfolio. Clem Delangue (Hugging Face) angels into Sakana and Phonic. Qasar Younis (Applied Intuition, a 2017 Lux deal) and Erik Bernhardsson (Modal) angel into Phonic ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale)). Crosby was diligenced through demand from Lux portfolio companies ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/were-backing-crosby-to-end-the-billiable-hour-for-laywers)).\n- **Constraints.**\n  - They work inside Lux's multi-stage process. Many deals are co-signed with Grace Isford, so personal attribution is often unclear.\n  - Lux's own site does not yet list Flapping Airplanes on their page, and the Lux company page returns 404 ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves)). Their deal role there rests on one press quote.\n  - Their public output is thin: no podcast, Substack or readable X content found.\n- **What they'd never do (inferred, not stated):** I found no sourced statement. Their AI deal pattern shows no consumer AI apps; every name is infrastructure, a lab or an agent.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Flapping Airplanes ($1B A, Oct 2026): Lux was a late entrant, not a seed backer.** Index co-led the January 2026 seed with GV and Sequoia, on Mark Xu's prior ties to the founders ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/)). Lux first shows up on 2026-07-31, when reports say \"Index Ventures and Lux Capital are leading talks\" at about $5B ([src](https://runtimewire.com/article/flapping-airplanes-5-billion-valuation-funding-talks)). The round closed on 2026-10-21 at a reported $6B, co-led by Index and Lux ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/)).\n  - So the gap from seed to Lux was about 6 months to talks and 9 months to the close, and the price rose from about $5B to $6B during the raise.\n  - Index's own post about the A names neither Lux nor Reeves ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/)). Mark Xu (Index) took the board seat ([src](https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/)); whether Reeves has one is unknown.\n- **Reeves's Flapping Airplanes thesis is compute efficiency, not data efficiency.** Reeves, quoted as the Lux partner, said \"getting more out of what we have\" matters for \"the next frontier\", and that the company is \"probably best positioned to do that\" ([src](https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/)). These are separate fragments relayed by a secondary outlet, not one continuous quote. This matches the company's megakernel and chip-utilisation pitch at the A rather than the data-efficiency story at the seed (see `companies/flapping-airplanes.md`).\n- **SkyPilot (July 2026): open source isn't the moat.** Reeves played down the risk that the code is free on GitHub, saying the product is \"probably like 1% of the way done\". Fortune says the bigger bet is tied to Ion Stoica himself ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/07/21/skypilot-from-databricks-cofounder-raises-20m-to-be-the-switzerland-of-ai-compute/)).\n- **Cognition: Lux came after Founders Fund.** Founders Fund led the $21M Series A in March 2024 ([src](https://cognition.com/blog/introducing-devin)). Cognition's Sept 2025 post credits \"Lux Capital and 8VC (who jointly led our previous round)\" ([src](https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents)). Reeves is the only Lux partner on Lux's Cognition page ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/cognition)).\n- **Phonic: the deal came from friendship and the portfolio.** The founders, who met at MIT and started the MIT ML Club, had long been friends with the Lux team. The Lux post is bylined by Isford and Reeves, and three Lux portfolio CEOs angelled in ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale)).\n- **Crosby: demand proven inside the portfolio.** The Lux post says Lux's \"portfolio companies were clamoring to use the product\". The co-founder John Sarihan is a Ramp alum; Ramp is a 2021 deal on Reeves's page ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/were-backing-crosby-to-end-the-billiable-hour-for-laywers)). The post's first-person author is unnamed.\n- **Sakana: Lux led the founding round, then co-led the A.** Lux led the $30M founding round in Jan 2024 (Isford wrote the post, Reeves co-signed). NEA joined Khosla and Lux as co-leads of the ~$200M A eight months later ([src](https://sakana.ai/series-a/)).\n- **Compute focus predates 2022 (aggregator).** TheOrg's unverified bio lists \"new compute architectures\" and ML infrastructure as focus areas ([src](https://theorg.com/org/lux-capital/org-chart/brandon-reeves)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Reeves backs frontier labs both at founding (Sakana) and at very large rounds (Flapping Airplanes' $1B A). They back AI compute and infrastructure at seed (Together, SkyPilot) and AI-native automation (Cognition, Phonic, Crosby). This is exactly the large-round lab territory where I write ~$20M checks.\n\nDeals that matter to me:\n- **Flapping Airplanes:** a new lab; Lux co-led the A.\n- **Sakana AI:** a lab; Lux led the founding round.\n- **SkyPilot:** AI compute; Lux-led seed in 2026.\n- **Together AI:** open-model compute; Lux-led seed.\n- **Cognition:** AI-native software engineering.\n\nHow to use them:\n- On **infrastructure and lab seeds**, Reeves is the lead to co-invest behind.\n- On **consensus labs** (Flapping Airplanes, Cognition), Lux arrives after the seed. A seed investor like me would sit ahead of them, and Reeves becomes a likely next-round lead to bring in.\n\nCaveat: Reeves is a senior partner at a multi-stage firm, not a scout.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Fort Lauderdale, Florida ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves)).\n- BS in Electrical Engineering, University of Central Florida; MBA, Harvard Business School. Dates not found ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves)).\n- Before Lux: investment team at Capricorn Investment Group. Dates not found ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves)).\n- At Lux by 2017 at the latest: Anduril and Applied Intuition (both 2017) are on their page ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves)). Exact join date not found.\n- Title: \"Partner, Investor\" on the Lux site (fetched 2026-11-10). The bio says they work on early- and growth-stage companies across AI, defense and manufacturing ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves)). TheOrg says General Partner (unverified, [src](https://theorg.com/org/lux-capital/org-chart/brandon-reeves)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves | Lux bio; 8 companies listed (no Flapping Airplanes or SkyPilot as of 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://medium.com/@brandon.reeves | Hosts the 2019 Hugging Face post; returned 403 again on 2026-11-10 |\n| X | https://twitter.com/breeves08 | Linked from the Lux bio; could not be fetched (402); follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/brandonreeves1/ | ~7K followers, Miami (fetched 2026-11-09) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-10: Lux Capital's 2nd AI Summit (NYC). Reeves interviewed Hugging Face CEO Clem Delangue on stage ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-2nd-ai-summit-puts-nyc-on-the-ai-map)). No quotes were captured.\n- No podcast appearances found. Web search was exhausted in both runs, so this is unverified, not a confirmed absence.\n\n## How they think\n- **Infrastructure over apps:** they back the substrate: an NLP library (Hugging Face), an open-model cloud (Together), compute orchestration (SkyPilot) and GPU-kernel efficiency (Flapping Airplanes).\n- **Efficiency as the next frontier:** \"getting more out of what we have\" ([src](https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/)).\n- **Founder pedigree is the moat for open source:** the SkyPilot bet is on Stoica, not the code ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/07/21/skypilot-from-databricks-cofounder-raises-20m-to-be-the-switzerland-of-ai-compute/)).\n- **The portfolio as diligence:** on Crosby, Lux waited until its own companies were buying ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/were-backing-crosby-to-end-the-billiable-hour-for-laywers)).\n- **Calls they got right:**\n  - Hugging Face: Lux led $15M in 2019, then the $100M round at ~$2B in 2022 ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=579586)).\n  - Together.\n  - Sakana.\n  - Flapping Airplanes and SkyPilot are too early to judge.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Our investment in Hugging Face\" (Medium, Dec 2019): their own early NLP thesis. https://medium.com/@brandon.reeves/our-investment-in-hugging-face-c8497a3519eb (not retrieved)\n2. Tech Funding News on Flapping Airplanes (Oct 2026): their quote on the $1B A. https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/\n3. Fortune on SkyPilot (Jul 2026): their framing of an open-source seed. https://fortune.com/2026/07/21/skypilot-from-databricks-cofounder-raises-20m-to-be-the-switzerland-of-ai-compute/\n4. Lux on Phonic (Apr 2025), co-bylined: https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale\n5. Lux on Sakana (Jan 2024), co-signed: https://www.luxcapital.com/news/our-investment-in-japans-sakana-ai-to-build-nature-inspired-ai-foundation-models\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X (@breeves08) could not be fetched, and LinkedIn showed no post content.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Hugging Face | $15M | 2019-12-17 | Lux led | A.Capital, Betaworks, Richard Socher, Greg Brockman, Kevin Durant | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2019/12/17/hugging-face-raises-15-million-to-build-the-definitive-natural-language-processing-library/) |\n| Hugging Face | $100M, ~$2B | 2022-05-09 | Lux led (Reeves not named) | Sequoia, Coatue | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=579586) |\n| Together AI | $20M seed | 2023-05-14 | Lux led (Reeves on the site card only) | Factory, SV Angel, First Round, Long Journey, A Capital | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2023/05/15/together-raises-20m-decentralize-open-source-ai-model-creation) |\n| Sakana AI | $30M founding | 2024-01-16 | Lux led (Isford and Reeves) | Khosla, NTT; angels Jeff Dean, Clem Delangue, Alexandr Wang | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/our-investment-in-japans-sakana-ai-to-build-nature-inspired-ai-foundation-models) |\n| Sakana AI | ~$200M Series A | 2024-09-04 | Co-led (NEA, Khosla, Lux) | NVIDIA, MUFG, SMBC, Mizuho | [src](https://sakana.ai/series-a/) |\n| Cognition | Round after Founders Fund's A | 2024 (Lux year) | Lux and 8VC co-led | 8VC | [src](https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents) |\n| Phonic | $4M seed | 2025-04-03 | Lux led (Isford and Reeves) | Qasar Younis, Clem Delangue, Erik Bernhardsson | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale) |\n| Valthos | unknown | 2025 (Lux year) | unknown | (Wolfe and Reeves) | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/valthos) |\n| Crosby | $60M Series B | 2026-03-31 | Lux led (Isford and Reeves tagged) | none named | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/were-backing-crosby-to-end-the-billiable-hour-for-laywers) |\n| SkyPilot | $20M seed | 2026-07-21 | Lux led; Reeves the quoted partner | Coatue, Amplify Partners | [src](https://fortune.com/2026/07/21/skypilot-from-databricks-cofounder-raises-20m-to-be-the-switzerland-of-ai-compute/) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | $1B Series A, ~$6B reported | 2026-10-21 | Co-led (Index, Lux); Reeves quoted, deal role unconfirmed | Sequoia, GV, Kleiner Perkins, Valor, Atreides, A*, NVIDIA | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/), [src](https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/) |\n| Physical Intelligence | unknown | 2024 (Lux year) | unknown | | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/physical-intelligence) (Reeves on a similar-company card only) |\n| Anduril | unknown | 2017 (Lux year) | unknown | | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves) |\n| Applied Intuition | unknown | 2017 (Lux year) | unknown | | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves) |\n| Ramp | unknown | 2021 (Lux year) | unknown | | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves) |\n| Hadrian | unknown | 2021 (Lux year) | unknown | | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Hugging Face (2019):** Lux led the $15M round, and Reeves wrote the Lux post arguing NLP lagged vision ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2019/12/17/hugging-face-raises-15-million-to-build-the-definitive-natural-language-processing-library/)). Lux led again in 2022, when Sequoia and Coatue came in about 2.4 years later ([src](https://huggingface.co/blog/series-c)). Whether Lux was the first institutional investor is unconfirmed.\n- **Together (2023):** Lux led the $20M seed. By March 2024, Kleiner, NEA and Emergence were existing investors, and Salesforce Ventures led a $106M round ([src](https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a2)). The next tier arrived within about 10 months.\n- **Sakana (2024):** Lux led the founding round with Khosla. NEA joined at the A eight months later ([src](https://sakana.ai/series-a/)). The intro path to David Ha and Llion Jones is not stated.\n- **SkyPilot (2026):** a Lux-led seed for the Berkeley Sky Computing project (Ion Stoica), framed as a bet on Stoica ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/07/21/skypilot-from-databricks-cofounder-raises-20m-to-be-the-switzerland-of-ai-compute/)). The intro path is not stated.\n- **Flapping Airplanes (2026):**\n  - Index sourced the seed through relationships. Mark Xu knew Asher Spector from Harvard, and Shardul Shah met Ben Spector in Chris Ré's office ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/)).\n  - Lux joined Index in leading the A talks by 2026-07-31 ([src](https://runtimewire.com/article/flapping-airplanes-5-billion-valuation-funding-talks)). The round closed at $1B on 2026-10-21 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/)).\n  - How Lux got in is not stated. One possible path is Chris Ré, who co-founded Together, a Lux-led seed, and whose Stanford lab produced the Flapping Airplanes kernel work. This is inference, not sourced.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lux Capital | Partner | by 2017 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves) |\n| Capricorn Investment Group | Former investment team | before Lux | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves) |\n| Grace Isford | Co-lead deal partner (Sakana, Phonic, Crosby) | 2024-01 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale) |\n| Josh Wolfe | Co-listed on Anduril and Valthos | 2017 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/valthos) |\n| Lan Jiang | Co-listed on site cards (Together, Physical Intelligence) | 2023 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/together) |\n| Clem Delangue (Hugging Face) | Portfolio CEO; angel in Sakana and Phonic; summit interview | 2019 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-2nd-ai-summit-puts-nyc-on-the-ai-map) |\n| Ion Stoica (SkyPilot) | Founder backed | 2026-07 | [src](https://fortune.com/2026/07/21/skypilot-from-databricks-cofounder-raises-20m-to-be-the-switzerland-of-ai-compute/) |\n| Index Ventures / Mark Xu | Co-lead on the Flapping Airplanes A (Xu on the board) | 2026-07 | [src](https://runtimewire.com/article/flapping-airplanes-5-billion-valuation-funding-talks), [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/) |\n| Ben Spector, Asher Spector, Aidan Smith | Founders of Flapping Airplanes (Lux co-led the A) | 2026-10 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/) |\n| Khosla Ventures | Co-investor (Sakana seed and A) | 2024-01 | [src](https://sakana.ai/series-a/) |\n| 8VC | Co-lead (Cognition) | 2024–25 | [src](https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents) |\n| Coatue; Amplify Partners | Co-investors (SkyPilot) | 2026-07 | [src](https://fortune.com/2026/07/21/skypilot-from-databricks-cofounder-raises-20m-to-be-the-switzerland-of-ai-compute/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Flapping Airplanes:**\n  - Is Reeves the Lux deal lead, and do they hold a board or observer seat? Lux has no company page (404), and the deal is not on their profile.\n  - How did Lux get into the A (a Chris Ré or Together path)?\n  - What was Lux's check size?\n- **Primary sources:** the Medium Hugging Face post (403) and X @breeves08 (402) are still unread. No podcast was found; search budget was exhausted.\n- **Hugging Face:** was Lux the first institutional investor?\n- **Cognition:** the exact round and date of the Lux and 8VC co-lead.\n- **Personal track record:** check sizes and board seats (Hugging Face, Together, SkyPilot).\n- **Physical Intelligence:** Reeves's role, if any (site-card association only).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves\n- https://theorg.com/org/lux-capital/org-chart/brandon-reeves\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/brandonreeves1/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2019/12/17/hugging-face-raises-15-million-to-build-the-definitive-natural-language-processing-library/\n- https://medium.com/@brandon.reeves/our-investment-in-hugging-face-c8497a3519eb (not retrieved)\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=579586\n- https://huggingface.co/blog/series-c\n- https://siliconangle.com/2023/05/15/together-raises-20m-decentralize-open-source-ai-model-creation\n- https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a2\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/our-investment-in-japans-sakana-ai-to-build-nature-inspired-ai-foundation-models\n- https://sakana.ai/series-a/\n- https://cognition.com/blog/introducing-devin\n- https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/cognition\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/were-backing-crosby-to-end-the-billiable-hour-for-laywers\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-2nd-ai-summit-puts-nyc-on-the-ai-map\n- https://fortune.com/2026/07/21/skypilot-from-databricks-cofounder-raises-20m-to-be-the-switzerland-of-ai-compute/\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/valthos\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/together\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/physical-intelligence\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/\n- https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/\n- https://runtimewire.com/article/flapping-airplanes-5-billion-valuation-funding-talks\n- https://siliconangle.com/year-old-ai-research-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-reported-6b-valuation\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/flapping-airplanes-1b-6b-valuation\n", "person:brett-rochkind": "\n# Brett Rochkind\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. The vehicle he invests from is in [softbank-vision-fund-2.md](../firms/softbank-vision-fund-2.md); the parent, the other pools and the OpenAI story are in [softbank-group.md](../firms/softbank-group.md); the deal-by-deal ledger is in [softbank-ai-ledger.md](../sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md).*\n\n## One-line take\nThe person at SoftBank who covers US AI-application companies. He joins late rounds with small tickets, and what he really brings is the Japanese telecom arm as a customer and reseller.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he gets into rounds:** not price and not size. His tickets come with SoftBank's Japan and Asia-Pacific network. Sierra, Perplexity, Wiz and Zenity each have a commercial tie to SoftBank Corp. (the telecom subsidiary) within months of the cheque.\n- **What constrains him:** he invests the parent's money from Vision Fund 2, and the large AI bets (OpenAI, Stargate, robotics) are decided by Masayoshi Son and run by other people. Reuters reports that Alex Clavel sits above the committee that deliberates on new investments ([Reuters via Yahoo, 2024-01-11](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html)). That he reports to Clavel is inferred, not confirmed.\n- **What he does not do:** seed or Series A. The earliest entry on his book is a Series C. Since 2022 he is not on record leading or pricing a round.\n- **Background that explains it:** 15 years of growth equity at General Atlantic. His own rule ends with \"money\": valuation and structure ([team page](https://visionfund.com/team/brett-rochkind)).\n\n## What he signs, sources and prices\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pool | Vision Fund 2 only. All 16 company pages that name him show fund = SVF2. No outside LPs; 98% parent money. | [team page](https://visionfund.com/team/brett-rochkind), [fund file](../firms/softbank-vision-fund-2.md) |\n| Does he approve? | No evidence that he does alone. An executive committee deliberates and Clavel was placed above it in July 2023. | [Reuters via Yahoo](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html) |\n| Does he lead and price? | Once on record: Remote, Series C, $300M at about $3B, 2022-04. Nothing since. | [Built In](https://builtin.com/articles/remote-raises-300m-hrtech-3b-valuation) |\n| Ticket size | $10M-$20M in Perplexity's $250M round. Reuters called the fund's cheques into Cato Networks (his company) and Tractable \"relatively small\". | [Reuters via Yahoo, 2024-06-27](https://sg.news.yahoo.com/softbank-invest-search-startup-perplexity-043717595.html) |\n| Not his | OpenAI (Vikas J. Parekh's page), Wayve (Matsui's team), Emergent, Cast AI, Tractable, poolside (Sumer Juneja's pages). | [visionfund.com/portfolio](https://visionfund.com/portfolio) pages, read 2026-11-10 |\n| Deal team | Kaz Yoshimaru (7 shared pages), Aaron Wong (6), Amit Lubovsky (4), Angela Du (2), Mike Guo (2). | same |\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The commercial tie and the cheque travel together.** Perplexity: SoftBank Corp. signed a Japan distribution deal on 2024-06-17 and said it \"has not invested in Perplexity via the strategic partnership deal\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/17/softbank-ties-up-with-perplexity)); the Vision Fund 2 ticket was reported ten days later. Sierra: the fund invested at the Japan launch on 2025-12-04 ([Axios](https://www.axios.com/2025/12/04/sierra-ai-softbank-investment-japan)), and on 2026-07-14 Sierra wrote \"SoftBank will be our exclusive sales partner in Japan\" ([Sierra](https://sierra.ai/blog/announcing-our-partnership-with-softbank-corp)).\n- **His ticket is the size of a seed check.** Bloomberg, relayed by Reuters, had the fund \"investing between $10 million and $20 million\" in Perplexity at $3B ([Reuters via Yahoo](https://sg.news.yahoo.com/softbank-invest-search-startup-perplexity-043717595.html)).\n- **The parent's engineers do diligence.** Classiq says the investment followed a review by SoftBank's R&D team, which \"validated Classiq's ability to generate high-performance quantum circuits\" ([Classiq, 2025-07-30](https://classiq.io/insights/softbank-vision-fund-2-makes-strategic-investment-in-classiq-expanding-series-c)).\n- **Wiz is the one AI-era deal where he is quoted.** \"Wiz is a game-changer for the cloud security market\" ([release, 2024-11-18](https://www.newswire.com/news/softbank-vision-fund-2-makes-strategic-investment-in-wiz-to-expand-22467663)). It was a stand-alone \"strategic investment\" six months after Wiz's $1B round, with no price given, and he is quoted beside Yossi Cohen, SoftBank's head of Israel investments.\n- **He tried to run his own fund once.** CRV hired him in 2017 for a \"growth equity fund that would be led by General Atlantic vet Brett Rochkind\", then scrapped it in May 2018 after raising it ([Axios, 2018-05-11](https://www.axios.com/2018/05/11/crv-scraps-growt-1526059785); read through a search snippet because the page returned 403).\n- **Israel thread.** He \"helped to open their office in Tel Aviv\" at Morgan Stanley ([team page](https://visionfund.com/team/brett-rochkind)); four of his 16 SoftBank companies are Israeli-founded (Wiz, Zenity, Cato Networks, Classiq).\n- **Zenity (unconfirmed detail).** The Series C release is reported to list SoftBank Corp. as a customer; I could only read that through a search summary of the [Business Wire release](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260803963850/en/), so it is not in the insight rows.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** He is a growth-stage co-investor, not a seed partner, a price-setter or an LP.\n- He enters at Series C or later; nothing on his book is a lab, a chip company or a seed.\n- Where he matters to me: a portfolio company selling AI applications to enterprises that wants Japan. The route is a small Vision Fund 2 ticket plus a SoftBank Corp. sales deal (Sierra is the model).\n- He does not set the price of later rounds in my companies. On Glean, Runway, Tines, AlphaSense and Zenity someone else led.\n- For a frontier lab, he is the wrong door. OpenAI went through Son and Parekh.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** NYU Stern (University Scholar); MBA, Columbia Business School ([team page](https://visionfund.com/team/brett-rochkind)).\n- **Morgan Stanley, 1998-2001:** analyst in the Technology Group, Menlo Park; helped open the Tel Aviv office (role: team page; dates: MarketScreener, an aggregator, via search).\n- **General Atlantic, 2002 to 2017-12:** started in New York, moved to Hong Kong in 2009, later led the Silicon Valley office and was global co-head of technology ([TechAviv](https://techaviv.com/people/brett-rochkind), secondary). Prior investments he names: Alibaba, Airbnb, Altair, AppDynamics, Box, IHS Markit, Meituan, MercadoLibre, Nubank, Uber.\n- **CRV, 2017-12 to 2018-05:** hired to run CRV's first growth fund; fund scrapped; stayed as senior advisor (Axios, as above). Aggregators list CRV to 2019.\n- **BRK Capital, to 2022:** his own investment office; part of a $40M StashFin round in 2021-04 ([Global Venturing](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rochkind-reportedly-joins-softbank)). Start date not found.\n- **SBIA, 2022 to now:** hire reported 2022-01-31 (Global Venturing, citing Silicon Valley Business Journal). Joined as Managing Partner.\n- Trustee of NYU; Leadership Council, Tipping Point Community (team page).\n- One aggregator (startupintros.com) says he was at Carlyle. No other source supports that; treat it as an error.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [visionfund.com/team/brett-rochkind](https://visionfund.com/team/brett-rochkind) | Loaded 2026-11-10. Bio, five-question Q&A, 16 portfolio companies |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No handle on his team page or TechAviv page |\n| LinkedIn | [linkedin.com/in/brochkind](https://www.linkedin.com/in/brochkind/) | URL as listed by TechAviv; not opened (login wall), so unverified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n**How to reach him.** No public channel he posts on. Routes seen in the sources:\n- Through a company on his book that wants Japan (the Sierra and Wiz pattern), or through the lead of a late round he joins (General Atlantic led Runway's Series D; it is his old firm).\n- Through his directors, Kaz Yoshimaru and Aaron Wong, who sit on almost every AI-application page with him.\n- For Israeli companies, through Yossi Cohen.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2013 — TechCrunch \"Ask a VC\" — as General Atlantic MD — [video page](https://techcrunch.com/video/ask-a-vc-general-atlantics-brett-rochkind) — listed in search results; not watched.\n- Nothing found from his SoftBank years (searched 2026-11-10).\n\n## How they think\n- **The four Ms:** market, management, business model, money. On the last: \"figuring out the investment, at what valuation, and how you can structure it\" ([team page](https://visionfund.com/team/brett-rochkind)).\n- Likes recurring-revenue software: he calls a high-margin SaaS model one of the best in the world (team page, paraphrased).\n- Reveres Son's Alibaba bet: \"That $20 million has returned more than $70 billion in gains\" (team page).\n- Contrarian views: nothing found.\n- Calls right or wrong: Remote was priced at about $3B in April 2022, the market peak; no later mark found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [His Vision Fund team page](https://visionfund.com/team/brett-rochkind): the only place he explains himself.\n2. [Sierra on SoftBank Corp.](https://sierra.ai/blog/announcing-our-partnership-with-softbank-corp): what a company on his book gets.\n3. [Wiz strategic investment release](https://www.newswire.com/news/softbank-vision-fund-2-makes-strategic-investment-in-wiz-to-expand-22467663): his quote and the Japan pitch.\n4. [Reuters on Clavel's Vision Fund](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html): who decides above him.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X account, no posts read.\n\n## Portfolio\nAll 16 companies on his team page, with the fund shown as SVF2 on each. \"Team\" is the list on the company's Vision Fund page; the pages do not state role or round.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Remote | Series C ($300M at about $3B) | 2022-04-05 | yes | Accel, Sequoia, Index (existing) | [Built In](https://builtin.com/articles/remote-raises-300m-hrtech-3b-valuation); he is quoted |\n| AlphaSense | Growth ($650M at $4B) | 2024-06-11 | no | Viking, BDT & MSD (co-leads) | [release](https://www.aap.com.au/aapreleases/cision20240611ae36716) |\n| Perplexity | Growth ($250M at $3B) | 2024-06-27 | no | Bessemer (lead, per later summaries) | [Reuters via Yahoo](https://sg.news.yahoo.com/softbank-invest-search-startup-perplexity-043717595.html) |\n| Glean | Series E ($260M at $4.6B) | 2024-09-10 | no | Altimeter, DST (co-leads) | [Glean](https://www.glean.com/blog/glean-series-e-prompting-launch) |\n| Wiz | Strategic (undisclosed) | 2024-11-18 | sole named investor | none named | [release](https://www.newswire.com/news/softbank-vision-fund-2-makes-strategic-investment-in-wiz-to-expand-22467663); he is quoted |\n| Tines | Series C ($125M at $1.125B) | 2025-02-11 | no | Goldman Sachs Alternatives (lead) | [Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-02-11/goldman-softbank-back-ai-startup-tines-valued-at-1-13-billion) (headline only) |\n| Runway | Series D ($308M) | 2025-04-03 | no | General Atlantic (lead) | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/runway) |\n| Classiq | Series C (extension) | 2025-07-30 | no | CDP Venture Capital | [Classiq](https://classiq.io/insights/softbank-vision-fund-2-makes-strategic-investment-in-classiq-expanding-series-c) |\n| Sierra | Strategic (undisclosed) | 2025-12-04 | not stated | not stated | [Axios](https://www.axios.com/2025/12/04/sierra-ai-softbank-investment-japan) (headline only) |\n| Zenity | Series C ($125M) | 2026-08-03 | no | Norwest (lead) | [SecurityWeek](https://www.securityweek.com/zenity-raises-125-million-in-series-c-funding/) |\n| Databricks | Undisclosed; round not identified | not found | unknown | not stated | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/databricks); unconfirmed |\n| Cato Networks | Undisclosed; round not checked | not found | unknown | | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/cato-networks) |\n| Fetch, ShipBob, Cargomatic, Quantstamp | Not AI; rounds not checked | | | | [team page](https://visionfund.com/team/brett-rochkind) |\n\nTiming against the first investor: every AI name above was entered at Series C or later, at least two priced rounds after the seed.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Remote (2022-04).** His first SoftBank deal and the only one he is on record leading. He said companies can now hire anywhere but that it is \"an intensive, costly and risky process\" ([Built In](https://builtin.com/articles/remote-raises-300m-hrtech-3b-valuation)).\n- **Perplexity (2024-06).** Telecom deal first, small fund ticket second, ten days apart. See above.\n- **Wiz (2024-11).** A cheque written between rounds to support Japan and Asia-Pacific sales. Google later agreed to buy Wiz (not checked here).\n- **Sierra (2025-12 to 2026-07).** Investment, Tokyo office, then exclusive resale by SoftBank Corp., which already used Sierra for its LINEMO brand.\n- First contact and intro paths: nothing found for any deal.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Kaz Yoshimaru | Director; on 7 of his company pages | not found | [Sierra page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/sierra) |\n| Aaron Wong | Director; on 6 of his company pages | not found | [Sierra page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/sierra) |\n| Amit Lubovsky | Director; security names (Wiz, Zenity, Tines, Cato) | not found | [Zenity page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/zenity) |\n| Yossi Cohen | Quoted together on Wiz | 2024-11 | [release](https://www.newswire.com/news/softbank-vision-fund-2-makes-strategic-investment-in-wiz-to-expand-22467663) |\n| Kentaro Matsui | Shares the Classiq page | 2025-07 | [Classiq page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/classiq) |\n| Alex Clavel | CEO of the manager; above the investment committee (reporting line inferred) | 2023-07 | [Reuters via Yahoo](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html) |\n| General Atlantic | Former firm (2002-2017); led Runway's Series D that he joined | 2002 | [TechAviv](https://techaviv.com/people/brett-rochkind) |\n| CRV | Former firm (2017-2018) | 2017-12 | [Axios](https://www.axios.com/2017/12/15/tech-investor-brett-rochkind-leaves-general-atlantic-1513301345) (snippet) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which Databricks round or secondary did Vision Fund 2 buy, and when?\n- Does he hold any board or observer seat? None found on any company.\n- Size of the Sierra, Wiz, Glean and Runway tickets.\n- Does SoftBank Corp. have a commercial deal with Glean or Runway? None found.\n- When did BRK Capital start, and is it still active?\n- Is the Zenity release's SoftBank Corp. customer reference accurate (page returned 403)?\n\n## Sources\n- [Vision Fund team page](https://visionfund.com/team/brett-rochkind) and the 16 company pages under [visionfund.com/portfolio](https://visionfund.com/portfolio), read 2026-11-10\n- [Global Venturing, 2022-01-31](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rochkind-reportedly-joins-softbank); [TechAviv profile](https://techaviv.com/people/brett-rochkind)\n- Axios: [2017-12-15](https://www.axios.com/2017/12/15/tech-investor-brett-rochkind-leaves-general-atlantic-1513301345), [2018-05-11](https://www.axios.com/2018/05/11/crv-scraps-growt-1526059785) (both 403; search snippets only)\n- [Reuters via Yahoo, 2024-01-11](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html); [Reuters via Yahoo, 2024-06-27](https://sg.news.yahoo.com/softbank-invest-search-startup-perplexity-043717595.html)\n- [TechCrunch, 2024-06-17](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/17/softbank-ties-up-with-perplexity); [Sierra, 2026-07-14](https://sierra.ai/blog/announcing-our-partnership-with-softbank-corp); [Axios, 2025-12-04](https://www.axios.com/2025/12/04/sierra-ai-softbank-investment-japan)\n- [Wiz release, 2024-11-18](https://www.newswire.com/news/softbank-vision-fund-2-makes-strategic-investment-in-wiz-to-expand-22467663); [Classiq, 2025-07-30](https://classiq.io/insights/softbank-vision-fund-2-makes-strategic-investment-in-classiq-expanding-series-c)\n- [Built In on Remote, 2022-04-05](https://builtin.com/articles/remote-raises-300m-hrtech-3b-valuation); [AlphaSense release, 2024-06-11](https://www.aap.com.au/aapreleases/cision20240611ae36716); [Glean, 2024-09-10](https://www.glean.com/blog/glean-series-e-prompting-launch)\n- [Bloomberg on Tines, 2025-02-11](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-02-11/goldman-softbank-back-ai-startup-tines-valued-at-1-13-billion) (headline); [SecurityWeek on Zenity, 2026-08-04](https://www.securityweek.com/zenity-raises-125-million-in-series-c-funding/)\n", "person:brian-schechter": "\n# Brian Schechter\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 by the Etched price-setters pass. Builds on `firms/primary-venture-partners.md` (not edited). Primary sources: Schechter's own posts on primary.vc (2024-06-25, 2026-02-10, 2026-06-30, 2026-07-23, 2026-11-09), his primary.vc team page, Etched's progress posts, a 2014 NBC/Entrepreneur profile of HowAboutWe and a 2025 Vintage interview page. X (@BSchech) returned HTTP 402 and was not read. The Compute 100 has no public transcripts; I read episode descriptions only.*\n\n## One-line take\nSchechter set the first price on Etched: a $5.4M seed at about $34M in March 2023 ([SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=662004)), Primary's first chip deal, at a time when \"Every major investor they pitched passed\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/)). He then co-led the A with Positive Sum, and no outside firm led a round for about 33 months, until Stripes priced the B at $5B. He has turned that one deal into a public compute practice that is now hunting the next seeds.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He had the thesis before he had the founder. He was quoted in the WSJ saying transformers opened a slot for new chips, and met Gavin Uberti \"That same day\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond)). He replaced the missing in-house semis expertise with outside experts: ASIC-startup veterans and incumbent engineers read Uberti's white paper and took calls with him. He underwrites the founder and the market size, not the chip.\n- **The founder lens:** He is a two-time founder and former teacher ([team page](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter)). He was a Primary-backed founder before he was a Primary partner: \"Primary was the only one that felt like a team member\" ([Vintage, 2025-07](https://vintage-ip.com/interviews/vc-insights-interview-with-brian-schechter-partner-at-primary-venture-partners/)). His team page says he backs outsider founders who take \"first principles\" approaches ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter)).\n- **Constraint:** Seed-only check capacity. Primary leads pre-seed and seed, and its later rounds come from the separate Select vehicles ([TechCrunch 2021](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/24/primary-venture-capital-third-fund/)). On Etched he could co-lead a $120M A, but from the B onward he was a follower and could not price. His diligence bench is also thin. His own call for startups names the \"thin bench of diligence partners\" as the reason seed funds avoid compute ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/primarys-call-for-compute-startups), 2026-11-09).\n- **What he'd never do (inference from his writing):** Back a compute team without seasoned silicon operators next to the first-principles founder, or lead a growth round.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He was a Primary portfolio founder first.** Primary's team page calls him a \"two-time founder\", and the Vintage interview says Primary was \"the only one that felt like a team member.\" That makes him a founder Primary converted into a partner, not a hire from another fund. No source says which of his companies Primary backed ([src](https://vintage-ip.com/interviews/vc-insights-interview-with-brian-schechter-partner-at-primary-venture-partners/), 2025-07).\n- **The thesis came first, the founder the same day.** \"That same day, I met Gavin Uberti\", meaning the day the WSJ ran his quote ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond), 2026-02-10). Primary had argued in the WSJ in January 2023 that transformers would open a slot for specialized chips ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/primarys-call-for-compute-startups)).\n- **A first-ever semis deal, de-risked with outside experts, not partners.** \"we had never made an investment in semis\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round), 2026-06-30). The experts \"started off as non-believers, borderline indignant\" and ended up backing Uberti (same source).\n- **His seed rule is two variables.** \"comes down to the scale of the market and the talent of the founders\" (same source). Price does not appear. Neither Schechter nor Primary ever states the $34M valuation in writing. The price is known only from SiliconANGLE.\n- **What licensed the leap.** A Sam Altman line, \"Speed is one of your main advantages over large competitors\", pushed Primary into semis ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware), 2024-06-25).\n- **Etched's pivot to systems came from his memo's diligence loop.** Mark Ross (ex-Cypress CTO, later Etched CTO) told Uberti \"People buy systems, not chips.\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round)).\n- **Strategic investors, not VCs, bridged the gap from seed to B.** Co-founder Robert Wachen \"brought over $100M from strategic investors\" (wording per the earlier repo pass; same source). TechCrunch adds that in 2023 the company was reportedly running month to month and close to out of cash ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/)).\n- **He re-prices conviction every round.** \"we have invested in every round and refreshed our underwriting each time\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round)).\n- **He claims structural alpha at seed in compute.** \"Alpha exists at seed for compute investing.\" He backs this with Primary's analysis of 1,300 compute deals against 8,500 software-infrastructure deals since 2015 ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond)).\n- **He is a bubble-aware bull.** \"We are undoubtedly in bubble territory\" (same source). He has been in tech since 2010: \"I got into tech in 2010\".\n- **His sparring partner is his junior.** \"Gaby and I are in deep debate with Claude around our compute thesis\" (same source). Gaby Lorenzi (Senior Associate) co-authors his posts and co-hosts the podcast.\n- **His jab at peers:** \"it’s easy for seed investors to agree and move on\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/primarys-call-for-compute-startups), 2026-11-09).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is the person who set the first price on the AI-semis seed that THESIS.md exists to catch. He is now publicly sourcing the next compute seeds across eight layers: 3D-chip metrology, materials, microfluidic cooling, power delivery, compiler and router, KV-cache memory, cold storage and compute finance. He also runs stealth bets in memory and alternative computing.\n\nHe is a **deal-flow and co-invest node, not a peer allocator**. His seed checks (sub-$10M) are far below my ~$20M into big seeds. His seeds are exactly where a $20M follow-on or co-lead fits, though: Primary could not lead Etched's B and needed outside capital at every step after the A.\n\nDeals that matter to me: Etched, Atero (acquired by Crusoe), The Biological Computing Company, Haiqu, Matterstack (firm-level, not personally attributed), and the unnamed stealth memory and alt-compute companies.\n\n## Background\n- Former teacher. NBC/Entrepreneur (2014-02-20) describes the HowAboutWe co-founders as teachers, one of whom taught in Washington, D.C. ([src](https://www.nbcnews.com/news/amp/wbna54450733)). Primary's page also says \"former teacher\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter)).\n- **HowAboutWe** (dating), co-founded in 2010 with Aaron Schildkrout. He was co-CEO and ran sales and marketing ([NBC 2014](https://www.nbcnews.com/news/amp/wbna54450733); [Laughing Squid](https://laughingsquid.com/?p=140756)). An aggregator search snippet says it was sold to IAC in 2014. I have not verified that.\n- **SelfMade**: co-founder and CEO, 2015–2020, per aggregators ([TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/primary-vc/org-chart/brian-schechter), search snippet). G20 Ventures wrote a \"why we invested\" post (Medium, 403 when fetched).\n- **Primary Venture Partners**: Partner since July 2020, per aggregators only. His earliest deal on his Primary team page is Plural (seed, 2020), which is consistent with that date ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter)). His current title is \"Partner, Compute\".\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter | Team page with portfolio list; checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.primary.vc/insights | Long first-person posts on Etched and compute, about quarterly in 2026 |\n| X | https://x.com/BSchech | Linked from team page; not readable (HTTP 402), follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/brian-schechter-1822ba16/ | Linked from team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://podcasts.apple.com/podcast/id1896779326 | The Compute 100, co-hosted with Gaby Lorenzi; 14 episodes, roughly weekly or biweekly since 2026-07 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-07 — Vintage Investment Partners \"VC Insights\". Interview on AI investing and the shift from generalist to specialist VC. The page has video only, plus one quote ([src](https://vintage-ip.com/interviews/vc-insights-interview-with-brian-schechter-partner-at-primary-venture-partners/)).\n- 2026-07 to 2026-11 — The Compute 100 (host). Guests include:\n  - Shahin Farshchi (Lux), 2026-07-22\n  - Alex Ksendzovsky and Jon Pomeraniec (The Biological Computing Company, his portfolio company), 2026-07-30\n  - Patrick Bowen (Neurophos), 2026-08-24\n  - Naveen Rao (Unconventional AI), 2026-09-22\n  - Austin Lyons (Chipstrat), 2026-10-06\n  - Jascha Achterberg (Callosum), 2026-10-13\n  - Barun Kar and Rajiv Khemani (Upscale AI), 2026-10-21\n  - Timothy Arcuri (UBS), early November 2026\n\n  These are episode descriptions only. No transcript was read ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/podcast/id1896779326)).\n- 2023-02 — WSJ, quoted on AI chip startups: \"There’s new openings for attack\" ([WSJ](https://www.wsj.com/articles/in-the-whirl-of-chatgpt-startups-see-an-opening-for-their-ai-chips-cb74798f), as quoted in his own post).\n\n## How they think\n- **Main theses:**\n  - Transformers and inference create room for specialist silicon.\n  - Compute is \"the great market opportunity of our generation\" (paraphrase of the A memo, per the Etched post).\n  - There is alpha at seed in compute because few VCs look there.\n  - Outsider founders working from first principles, paired with seasoned operators, build the winners ([team page](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter); [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond)).\n- **Contrarian views:**\n  - Backing a 20-something software engineer to build an ASIC in 2023.\n  - Calling a bubble while still leaning in.\n  - Saying seed funds are lazy about compute because their diligence bench is thin.\n- **Calls he got right:** Etched, from about $34M (2023-03) to $21B (2026-08) in roughly 41 months ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=662004); [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one)). Atero was acquired by Crusoe ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond)). Atero's price is not disclosed.\n- **Calls he got wrong:** none admitted in anything I read. At the A he wrote \"Semis is notoriously unforgiving\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Why we invested in Etched in every round](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round) (2026-06-30): his round-by-round underwriting memo, covering the experts, Ross and Wachen.\n2. [Our compute thesis: Etched and beyond](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond) (2026-02-10): the WSJ-day origin story, the data on alpha at seed, and his bubble stance.\n3. [Primary's call for compute startups](https://www.primary.vc/articles/primarys-call-for-compute-startups) (2026-11-09, with Lorenzi): the eight areas he is sourcing now.\n4. [Etched's Series A](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware) (2024-06-25): the Altman quote and the co-lead with Positive Sum.\n5. [Announcing Etched's Series C](https://www.primary.vc/articles/announcing-etcheds-series-c-led-by-sequoia-at-10-3b) (2026-07-23): \"The Etched thesis is simple.\"\n\n## Social engagement\nX was not readable (HTTP 402). In public he mostly writes long firm posts and runs The Compute 100. The guests show who he courts:\n- other chip founders (Neurophos, Unconventional AI, Callosum, Upscale AI, The Biological Computing Company)\n- one deep-tech VC peer (Farshchi at Lux)\n- sell-side and independent analysts (Arcuri, Lyons)\n\nI do not know who amplifies him.\n\n## Portfolio\nRounds on his primary.vc team page look like the company's current stage, not his entry round: Etched appears as \"Series B, 2023\" although he led its 2023 seed. I treat each year as the year of entry.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Etched | Seed, $5.4M at ~$34M | 2023-03 | yes | angels incl. Koko Xs (per TechCrunch 2024-12-13) | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond), [price](https://siliconangle.com/?p=662004) |\n| Etched | Series A, $120M | 2024-06-25 | co-lead (Positive Sum co-led) | Hummingbird, Two Sigma, Skybox Data Centers, Fundomo, Oceans, Peter Thiel, Thomas Dohmke, Amjad Masad, Jason Warner, Kyle Vogt | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware) |\n| Etched | Series B, $500M at $5B post | 2025-12 | no (Stripes led) | Stripes | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round), [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/) |\n| Etched | Series C, $300M at $10.3B | 2026-07-23 | no | **Primary's participation is not confirmed.** Etched names Sequoia, a16z, Jane Street, Diffusion, Argo, SK hynix | [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference) |\n| Etched | $700M at $21B | 2026-08-18 | no (Jane Street led) | Kleiner, Sequoia, a16z, Thiel, Tiger, BCV, Neo, Stripes, Positive Sum, Diffusion, Argo, Blackstone | [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one) |\n| Atero (GPU memory; acquired by Crusoe) | Seed | 2024 | not stated | not stated | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter), [acq](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond) |\n| Haiqu (quantum software) | Seed | 2025 | not stated | not stated | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter) |\n| The Biological Computing Company | Seed | 2025 | not stated | not stated | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter) |\n| Cake (open-source AI infrastructure) | Seed | 2021 | not stated | Tobias Citron co-listed | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter) |\n| Spark AI (acquired by John Deere) | Seed | 2021 | not stated | not stated | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter) |\n| Ark (bioprocess simulation) | Seed | 2021 | not stated | not stated | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter) |\n| Plural | Seed | 2020 | not stated | not stated | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter) |\n| Jed Security | Pre-seed | 2022 | not stated | not stated | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter) |\n| Teleskope | Series A (card) | 2023 | not stated | Tobias Citron co-listed | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter) |\n| Tabs | card says Series B | 2023 | not stated | not stated | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter) |\n| HumanX | Pre-seed | 2024 | not stated | not stated | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter) |\n| Stealth companies in memory and alternative computing | — | since 2023 | — | — | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond) |\n\nI found no prices, check sizes or lead status for any deal other than Etched.\n\n## Deal stories\n**Etched: first institutional money, alone for 33 months**\n- **2023-01:** Primary argues in the WSJ that transformers will open room for specialized chips.\n- **2023-02:** The WSJ runs Schechter's \"new openings for attack\" quote, and he meets Uberti the same day ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond)). No source says who made the introduction.\n- **Diligence:** Outside ASIC experts read the white paper and take calls with Uberti. They start hostile and finish as supporters. A reference from Uberti's OctoML manager calls him \"the best software engineer I’ve ever met\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round)).\n- **2023-03:** He leads a $5.4M seed at about $34M ([SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=662004)). Every major investor the founders pitched passed ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/)). An aggregator search snippet gives \"May 2023, $5M\" instead. I go with Primary's \"March 2023\".\n- **2023-03 to 2024-06:** No new institutional lead. Mark Ross's \"systems, not chips\" advice reshapes the company. Wachen raises more than $100M from strategic investors.\n- **2024-06-25:** Primary co-leads the $120M A with Positive Sum (Patrick O'Shaughnessy). SiliconANGLE says Primary \"led\" and names no co-lead. Primary's own post names the co-lead, and it is the stronger source. Uberti's Invest Like the Best episode had aired by then.\n- **2025-12:** Stripes leads $500M at a $5B post. This is the first round in about 33 months with an outside lead, and Primary participates. The price went from about $34M to $5B, roughly 150x.\n- **2026-07-23:** Sequoia leads $300M at $10.3B. Schechter and Lorenzi publish a welcome post, but no source confirms that Primary invested in the round.\n- **2026-08-18:** Jane Street leads $700M at $21B, and \"Primary\" is named among the investors.\n- **On consensus:** Ramtin Naimi (people/ramtin-naimi.md) says the consensus on Etched flipped only once \"Sequoia blessed him with a $10 billion valuation\". Schechter's price-setting window ran from 2023-03 to 2024-06. Everything after the B was price-taking.\n\n**Atero:** Seed in 2024; acquired by Crusoe. Terms and timing not found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Gavin Uberti (Etched CEO) | Seed lead; board member at Etched | 2023-02 | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond), [team page](https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter) |\n| Robert Wachen (Etched co-founder) | Portfolio founder; Schechter calls him \"a generational leader\" | 2023 | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round) |\n| Mark Ross (Etched CTO, ex-Cypress) | Diligence voice, then portfolio CTO | 2023 | same |\n| Gaby Lorenzi (Primary) | Junior partner on compute: co-author and podcast co-host | by 2026-02 | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/primarys-call-for-compute-startups) |\n| Tobias Citron (Primary) | Co-listed on Etched, Atero, Cake and Teleskope | 2021 | [src](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization) |\n| Patrick O'Shaughnessy (Positive Sum) | Co-lead on the Etched A | 2024-06 | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware) |\n| Stripes | Led the Etched B, which Primary followed | 2025-12 | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/) |\n| Sequoia Capital | Led the Etched C; Schechter welcomed Sequoia \"to the partnership\" | 2026-07 | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/announcing-etcheds-series-c-led-by-sequoia-at-10-3b) |\n| Jane Street | Led the $700M Etched round, in which Primary participated | 2026-08 | [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one) |\n| Crusoe | Acquired Atero, his 2024 seed | — | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond) |\n| Aaron Schildkrout | HowAboutWe co-founder and co-CEO | 2010 | [src](https://www.nbcnews.com/news/amp/wbna54450733) |\n| Shahin Farshchi (Lux) | Guest on The Compute 100 | 2026-07 | [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/podcast/id1896779326) |\n| Ben Sun, Brad Svrluga (Primary) | Founders of the firm he joined; Primary backed him as a founder first (company not named) | by 2020 | [src](https://vintage-ip.com/interviews/vc-insights-interview-with-brian-schechter-partner-at-primary-venture-partners/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Did Primary invest in the Etched Series C?** Etched's C post does not name Primary. Primary's C post says only that it \"invested in every round since then\", which reads as a description of the earlier rounds. Two repo files assert participation without support, and the main session should check them:\n  - `data/deals.csv` line 934 (brian-schechter, Etched Series C)\n  - `patterns/co-investor-gangs.md` line 150, which says Schechter \"co-led\" the C. That is unsupported.\n- Which of his companies did Primary back: HowAboutWe or SelfMade? The Vintage quote implies one of them.\n- The exact date he joined Primary, and his level on joining. July 2020 as partner comes from aggregators only.\n- Who introduced him to Uberti in February 2023?\n- Check sizes and ownership on Etched, and whether the B and the $700M money came from Primary Select.\n- The Compute 100 transcripts, which would give his own spoken account of the seed price negotiation.\n- Atero's acquisition terms and date. Matterstack's round and partner.\n- The names of the stealth memory and alternative-compute companies.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.primary.vc/team/brian-schechter\n- https://www.primary.vc/people\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond (2026-02-10)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round (2026-06-30)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware (2024-06-25)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/announcing-etcheds-series-c-led-by-sequoia-at-10-3b (2026-07-23)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/primarys-call-for-compute-startups (2026-11-09)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/scaling-for-the-ai-era-fund-v (2026-02-10)\n- https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference (2026-07-23)\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one (2026-08-18)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=662004 (2024-06-25)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/24/primary-venture-capital-third-fund/\n- https://vintage-ip.com/interviews/vc-insights-interview-with-brian-schechter-partner-at-primary-venture-partners/ (2025-07)\n- https://www.nbcnews.com/news/amp/wbna54450733 (2014-02-20)\n- https://laughingsquid.com/?p=140756\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/podcast/id1896779326\n- https://www.wsj.com/articles/in-the-whirl-of-chatgpt-startups-see-an-opening-for-their-ai-chips-cb74798f\n- Aggregators (secondary): https://theorg.com/org/primary-vc/org-chart/brian-schechter ; https://www.vcsheet.com/who/brian-schechter\n", "person:brian-zhan": "\n# Brian Zhan\n\n*Research note (2026-11-10): this run's web-search budget was used up before I started, so everything here comes from fetching known URLs and links on his personal site. Business Insider (three articles about him) could not be fetched, and no podcast transcript was reachable (YouTube and Apple pages return metadata only). The insight rows therefore lean on his site, Striker, CRV, Forbes and a Chinese press write-up (PEdaily). Treat the deal-level attribution with that in mind.*\n\n## One-line take\nA 29-year-old ex-Facebook Presto PM and former CRV Principal ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)). He was the junior investor next to Max Gazor on CRV's inception-stage lab run (Reflection, Skild, Dyna, Periodic) and now does the same thing at Striker, a ~10-company fund writing checks of up to $30M ([src](https://www.striker.vc/team/brian-zhan), [src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He is a technical investor whom researcher-founders vouch for. Reflection CEO Misha Laskin is quoted saying founders need investors who really understand the technical details, and that Zhan does ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)). He shows up before the company exists. CRV dates Reflection as founded and partnered in the same month (Feb 2024), and Dyna as partnered (Aug 2024) before it was founded (Sep 2024) ([src](https://www.crv.com/companies)).\n- **His self-told origin.** At Facebook he watched strong engineers struggle to raise because they had no business metrics, so he started angel investing in former colleagues' projects ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)).\n- **What constrains him.** Striker I is $165M from 12 investors ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084186/000208418625000003/primary_doc.xml)). It plans only about 10 companies, at up to $30M each ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)). Three consequences:\n  - He can't lead a $300M-$1B lab seed alone. He co-invests at inception or comes in as a large participant.\n  - Every slot is precious.\n  - The CRV-era board seats were observer roles under Gazor's lead.\n- **What he'd never do (inference, not stated).** Spray small checks or wait for revenue. Striker reports 100% of its investments were pre-revenue ([src](https://www.striker.vc/team/brian-zhan)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Contrarian on Reflection's seed price.** PEdaily says Reflection was already valued at $200M at seed, that most firms balked, and that Zhan invested anyway. In the source's words: \"多数机构望而却步，但Zhan力排众议出手\" ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml), 2025-12-05). This is a Chinese press retelling; the original English source (likely Business Insider) could not be fetched.\n- **\"Super-large seed\" fund design.** \"该基金每轮仅投10家公司，但单笔投资最高可达3000万美元\" ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)): about 10 companies, checks up to $30M. That is close to my own $20M lab-seed model. Striker's site gives the range as \"$5-30m on day zero\" ([src](https://www.striker.vc/)).\n- **Seed is moving earlier.** \"种子轮投资正变得越来越早\" ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)). He frames this as a structural shift that forces investors to act at the very start.\n- **AI for science is the next frontier.** \"AI在科学领域的潜力，将不亚于其在机器人或大模型中的影响\" ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)): science could matter as much as robotics or LLMs. This fits Periodic Labs (CRV, Sep 2025) and Edison Scientific (Striker, 2025) ([src](https://www.crv.com/companies), [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio)).\n- **Sourcing thesis.** From his own site: \"how the venture ecosystem often overlooks exceptional technical talent\" ([src](https://brianzhan.com/)).\n- **Founder endorsement.** Laskin, as quoted: \"创始人需要能真正理解技术细节的投资人\" ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)). It is rare to see a lab CEO publicly vouch for a then-junior investor.\n- **Started in data infrastructure.** His dormant Substack still reads \"Data and AI investor at CRV\" and cites Facebook Presto and Starburst ([src](https://brianzhan.substack.com/about)). His early wins (Voyage AI to MongoDB, Lepton AI to NVIDIA, LanceDB) are infrastructure. He moved to labs and robotics from there ([src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/brian-zhan/), [src](https://brianzhan.com/)).\n- **Current interest: RL environments.** His ICML 2026 workshop talk was titled \"The RL Environment Land Grab: What's Worth Backing and What Won't Work\" (2026-07-10). He shared the speaker list with Jerry Tworek (Core Automation) and Chelsea Finn ([src](https://sites.google.com/view/rlxf-icml2026/)). No abstract or recording was found.\n- **How he pitches Striker.** The Rho interview is titled \"Inside the Most Concentrated Fund in VC\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/oembed?url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3AJhnRMa6Ww&format=json)). The Nebius episode is \"How to Spot a $25 Billion Company Before It Has Revenue\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/oembed?url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vr1Y_CxFzS0&format=json)). Transcripts were not retrievable.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He works at inception in frontier labs, robotics foundation models and AI-for-science, with checks of $5-30M. That makes him the most direct peer and co-investor for $20M lab-seed checks. Striker's concentration (~10 bets, 12 LPs) also makes it a useful comparable for an LP pitch.\n\nDeals that matter to me:\n- Reflection AI: seed at a reported $200M valuation, now $8B.\n- Skild AI, Dyna Robotics and Periodic Labs.\n- At Striker: Ricursive Intelligence (AI chip design, which fits AI semis), Elorian AI, Edison Scientific and Inferact.\n\nThe ex-CRV relationships (Laskin, Pathak/Gupta, Gao/Ma/Yang, Fedus/Cubuk) are the high-value hop-3 targets.\n\n## Background\n- Northwestern University, BA in Computer Science, class of 2018 ([src](https://www.mccormick.northwestern.edu/news/articles/2025/12/students-named-to-forbes-30-under-30/)). In college he built physical-rehabilitation robots at the Rehabilitation Institute of Chicago ([src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/brian-zhan/)).\n- Product manager on Facebook's Presto team (later renamed Trino) ([src](https://brianzhan.substack.com/about)). The Delta podcast says he then led product at Starburst Data ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/nz/podcast/ep-53-brian-zhan-partner-at-striker-vp-and-investor/id1854467446?i=1000742401384)). Dates are not found.\n- Angel investing in former Facebook colleagues' projects on the side ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)).\n- CRV, Principal: data and AI, then labs and robotics. He was board observer at many portfolio companies ([src](https://www.striker.vc/team/brian-zhan)). His start date at CRV is not found. His crv.com profile now returns a 404.\n- Joined Striker Venture Partners as a Partner, announced around 2025-12-05 ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)). Northwestern calls him a \"founding partner\" ([src](https://www.mccormick.northwestern.edu/news/articles/2025/12/students-named-to-forbes-30-under-30/)).\n- Forbes 30 Under 30 (VC, 2026 list per the Forbes profile link) ([src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/brian-zhan/)). He also appears on Business Insider's 2026 lists of Rising VC Stars and Top Robotics Investors (links from [src](https://brianzhan.com/); not fetched).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://brianzhan.com/ | Press and talks hub (as of 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://brianzhan.substack.com/ | No posts; about page still says CRV |\n| X | https://x.com/brianzhan1 | Not fetchable (402); followers unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/bzhan/ | Guest view: Striker, Northwestern CS |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-12-23: Delta Podcast (Delta Institute), Ep. 53, \"Brian Zhan, Partner at Striker VP and Investor in Reflection, Skild, Periodic, Ricursive\" ([link](https://podcasts.apple.com/nz/podcast/ep-53-brian-zhan-partner-at-striker-vp-and-investor/id1854467446?i=1000742401384)). 27 minutes. No transcript retrieved.\n- Date unknown: Rho, \"Inside the Most Concentrated Fund in VC\" ([link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3AJhnRMa6Ww)). No transcript retrieved.\n- Date unknown: Nebius, \"How to Spot a $25 Billion Company Before It Has Revenue\" ([link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vr1Y_CxFzS0)). No transcript retrieved.\n- Date unknown: PyTorch Foundation keynote panel, \"Signal vs. Noise: Betting on AI's Next Wave\" ([link](https://youtu.be/IJPKA-l0N7Y)).\n- 2026-07-10: ICML 2026 RLxF workshop talk, \"The RL Environment Land Grab: What's Worth Backing and What Won't Work,\" plus a panel with Jesse Zhang, Roberta Raileanu, Yilun Du and Yiding Jiang ([link](https://sites.google.com/view/rlxf-icml2026/)).\n\n## How they think\n- **Back researchers before the company exists.** Inception or pre-founding checks, with technical help on roadmaps, the research-to-product path and engineering hiring ([src](https://www.striker.vc/team/brian-zhan)).\n- **Seed is moving earlier, and seeds are getting bigger.** That is the basis for the \"super-large seed\" fund ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)).\n- **AI for science is on par with robotics and LLMs** ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)).\n- **Contrarian call he got right.** Paying a $200M seed valuation for Reflection when most firms balked ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)). Reflection later raised at $8B (per repo `firms/crv.md`, citing [reflection.ai](https://reflection.ai/blog/building-frontier-open-intelligencence)).\n- **Skeptical, selective view of RL environments**, judging by the talk title ([src](https://sites.google.com/view/rlxf-icml2026/)).\n- **Calls that went wrong:** nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Striker profile](https://www.striker.vc/team/brian-zhan): his own framing (\"pathway to superintelligence\") and the company list.\n2. [PEdaily, 2025-12-05](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml): the Reflection contrarian story, fund design and his AI-for-science view.\n3. [Rho: Inside the Most Concentrated Fund in VC](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3AJhnRMa6Ww): likely the best primary source on Striker's construction (not yet transcribed).\n4. [Delta Podcast Ep. 53](https://podcasts.apple.com/nz/podcast/ep-53-brian-zhan-partner-at-striker-vp-and-investor/id1854467446?i=1000742401384): a research-community audience.\n5. [Business Insider, 2025-11](https://www.businessinsider.com/vc-deploying-a-bold-strategy-for-investing-in-ai-startups-2025-11): probably the English original of the PEdaily story (not fetched).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X returned HTTP 402 and couldn't be read. He speaks at research venues (ICML workshop, PyTorch Conference, Delta Institute) more than on investor podcasts, which suggests he targets researchers as an audience.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Reflection AI | $25M seed (CRV) | partnered 2024-02; announced 2025-03-07 | CRV co-led with Sequoia; Zhan's role is \"worked early\" / observer | Sequoia | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956), [src](https://www.crv.com/companies) |\n| Reflection AI | $105M Series A at $555M (CRV) | announced 2025-03-07 | CRV co-led with Lightspeed | Lightspeed | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956) |\n| Skild AI | unknown (CRV) | partnered 2024-05 (founded 2023-05) | not stated | Felicis, GC, Sequoia, SoftBank, Menlo, Lightspeed, Coatue, Amazon, Bezos, SV Angel (logos) | [src](https://www.crv.com/companies), [src](https://www.skild.ai/) |\n| Dyna Robotics | inception (CRV) | partnered 2024-08 (founded 2024-09) | not stated | not found | [src](https://www.crv.com/companies) |\n| Voyage AI | CRV (Bicer led A) | partnered 2024-08; acquired by MongoDB 2025-02 | no (Zhan supported) | not found | [src](https://www.crv.com/companies), [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/brian-zhan/) |\n| Lepton AI | unknown (CRV) | unknown; acquired by NVIDIA | not stated | not found | [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/brian-zhan/) |\n| Periodic Labs | unknown (CRV) | partnered 2025-09 | no | Periodic's site doesn't list CRV | [src](https://www.crv.com/companies) |\n| LanceDB | unknown (CRV, inferred) | unknown | unknown | not found | [src](https://brianzhan.com/) |\n| Ricursive Intelligence | inception (Striker) | 2025 | not stated | not found | [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio), [src](https://brianzhan.com/) |\n| Edison Scientific | inception (Striker) | 2025 | not stated | not found | [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio), [src](https://brianzhan.com/) |\n| Elorian AI | inception (Striker) | 2026 | not stated | not found | [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio), [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/brian-zhan) |\n\nInferact and Bay Security are also Striker inception investments ([src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio)), but neither is on Zhan's own list.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Reflection AI (timing).**\n  - CRV dates founding and partnering to the same month, Feb 2024 ([src](https://www.crv.com/companies)). Sequoia separately claims to be \"first and long-term partner\" (repo row, charlie-curnin).\n  - CRV and Sequoia co-led the $25M seed. CRV then co-led the $105M A with Lightspeed. Both rounds were disclosed together at launch on 2025-03-07 at a $555M valuation ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956)).\n  - PEdaily says the seed was priced at $200M and most firms balked ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)).\n  - The gap from first money to the next new lead (Lightspeed) was inside one pre-launch year.\n- **Dyna Robotics.** CRV partnered in Aug 2024, a month before Sep 2024 incorporation. That makes CRV a formation-stage backer ([src](https://www.crv.com/companies)). Both Gazor and Zhan claim the relationship ([src](https://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor)).\n- **Skild AI.** Not first in. The company was founded May 2023 and CRV partnered May 2024 ([src](https://www.crv.com/companies)).\n- **Periodic Labs.** CRV says it partnered in Sep 2025 ([src](https://www.crv.com/companies)), but Periodic's backer list omits CRV (per `firms/crv.md`). It was probably a small allocation in the $300M seed.\n- **The move to Striker.** He left CRV and joined Gazor's spinout as Partner, reported 2025-12-05 ([src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml)). How the lab relationships are split between CRV (pro-rata via Gazor as board partner) and Striker is not documented.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Max Gazor | Senior partner at CRV and Striker founder; shared Reflection and Dyna relationships | CRV era | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor), [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/brian-zhan/) |\n| CRV | Principal (former) | until 2025 | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/brian-zhan) |\n| Striker Venture Partners | Partner | 2025 | [src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml) |\n| Misha Laskin (Reflection AI) | Founder who publicly vouched for him | 2024 | [src](https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml) |\n| Ioannis Antonoglou (Reflection AI) | Founder relationship | 2024 | [src](https://www.crv.com/companies) |\n| Deepak Pathak, Abhinav Gupta (Skild AI) | Founder relationship | 2024 | [src](https://www.crv.com/companies) |\n| Lindon Gao, Jason Ma, York Yang (Dyna) | Founder relationship | 2024 | [src](https://www.crv.com/companies) |\n| Liam Fedus, Ekin Dogus Cubuk (Periodic Labs) | Founder relationship | 2025 | [src](https://www.crv.com/companies) |\n| Tengyu Ma (Voyage AI) | Founder relationship | 2024 | [src](https://www.crv.com/companies) |\n| Murat Bicer (CRV) | Co-listed on Voyage, Skild, LanceDB | CRV era | [src](https://www.crv.com/team/murat-bicer) |\n| Facebook Presto / Starburst Data | Former employer(s) | pre-CRV | [src](https://brianzhan.substack.com/about), [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/nz/podcast/ep-53-brian-zhan-partner-at-striker-vp-and-investor/id1854467446?i=1000742401384) |\n| Delta Institute | Podcast guest (researcher community) | 2025-12 | [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/nz/podcast/ep-53-brian-zhan-partner-at-striker-vp-and-investor/id1854467446?i=1000742401384) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Transcripts.** Transcribe the Rho, Nebius and Delta episodes. They likely hold his check sizes, ownership targets, intro paths (how he met Laskin and the Dyna founders) and funds he passed on.\n- **Business Insider.** Fetch the three articles (2025-11 strategy piece, 2026-01 Rising Stars, 2026-06 Robotics) for verbatim English quotes and to confirm the $200M Reflection seed figure.\n- **Striker attribution.** Who sourced Ricursive, Edison and Elorian: Zhan or Gazor?\n- **The 8 stealth Striker companies (2026).** Do any of them come out of the RL-environment thesis?\n- **Fund mechanics.** Striker's 12 LPs, and whether Striker shares allocations or co-invests with managers like me.\n- **Timeline.** His CRV start date and when Lepton was acquired.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.striker.vc/team/brian-zhan\n- https://www.striker.vc/\n- https://www.striker.vc/portfolio\n- https://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor\n- https://brianzhan.com/\n- https://brianzhan.substack.com/about\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/bzhan/\n- https://www.forbes.com/profile/brian-zhan/\n- https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml\n- https://www.mccormick.northwestern.edu/news/articles/2025/12/students-named-to-forbes-30-under-30/\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/nz/podcast/ep-53-brian-zhan-partner-at-striker-vp-and-investor/id1854467446?i=1000742401384\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3AJhnRMa6Ww (Rho)\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vr1Y_CxFzS0 (Nebius)\n- https://youtu.be/IJPKA-l0N7Y (PyTorch Foundation)\n- https://sites.google.com/view/rlxf-icml2026/\n- https://www.crv.com/companies\n- https://www.crv.com/team/murat-bicer\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956\n- https://www.skild.ai/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084186/000208418625000003/primary_doc.xml\n- Not fetched (blocked): Business Insider links listed on brianzhan.com\n", "person:bryan-gertzog": "\n# Bryan Gertzog\n\n## One-line take\nHe is a homegrown Stripes partner who went from analyst in 2014 to the Investment Committee. He is the only partner named on all three of Stripes' compute-infrastructure deals (Databricks, Crusoe, Etched) and co-ran the $500M Etched Series B at $5B (Dec 2025), the first growth price on the company, about seven months before Sequoia's $10.3B [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He is a growth underwriter with one repeatable instrument: lots of customer reference calls.\n  - On Orca (2021) it was \"CISOs we spoke with love Orca.\" On Island (2022) it was customer feedback \"some of the best and most consistent we've ever heard.\" On Etched (2026) it was customers calling it \"the first system that could truly transform their inference economics.\"\n  - He pairs that with a stage-in-then-lead playbook: take a small position, watch, then lead the step-up round (Snyk B to C; Island A to B).\n- **What constrains him.**\n  - Ken Fox, the founder, sits beside him on the big-ticket deals (Etched, Cognition, Snyk, Orca, Island, Axonius) and holds the Snyk board seat [src](https://snyk.io/about/board).\n  - He works out of a ~$1B flagship (Stripes VII) and leans on SPVs (see `firms/stripes.md`).\n  - He has no public voice: no podcast, X account or bylined essays outside the firm's deal memos.\n- **What he'd never do: seed.** Every one of his deals is labeled Growth or Early Growth on stripes.co.\n- **How far his remit has moved.** His 2019 bio says he focused on \"software and restaurants\" (Snooze is the pancake chain). Six years later he co-led a pre-volume-production AI chip company at $5B. That is the firm's style drift, carried by one person.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Stage-in-then-lead is the written playbook (Snyk).** Stripes joined Snyk's Accel-led Series B \"monitoring its progress to later take on a larger role.\" It then \"jumped at the opportunity to lead the Series C,\" $150M in Jan 2020. The memo is co-authored by Fox, Gertzog and Vidhan Bhatt [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240813193529/https://www.stripes.co/articles/developer-first-security-our-investment-in-snyk) [byline](https://web.archive.org/web/20230205015421/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog). For a seed investor, a Stripes check in round N is a signal that Stripes may want to price round N+1.\n- **The same pattern at Island.** \"We're thrilled to deepen our partnership with Island after investing in their Series A\" (Series B, $115M, 2022-03-23; Fox, Gertzog, Sardana, Kauber) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20241003234809/https://www.stripes.co/articles/the-first-enterprise-browser-our-investment-in-island).\n- **Etched broke the pattern.** Stripes wasn't in Etched's seed or its $120M Series A (Primary and Positive Sum), so the $5B Series B lead was a cold first entry [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round) [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched). That suggests the conviction came from the compute sweep (\"dozens of companies across compute\") rather than from a held position.\n- **The diligence instrument is customer love, not technical benchmarks.**\n  - Orca: \"CISOs we spoke with love Orca\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240525163314/https://www.stripes.co/articles/securing-the-next-big-attack-surface-our-investment-in-orca).\n  - Island: \"some of the best and most consistent we've ever heard\" (product feedback) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20241003234809/https://www.stripes.co/articles/the-first-enterprise-browser-our-investment-in-island).\n  - Etched: the customer quote above [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched).\n  - A seed AI-chip founder should expect Stripes to call the customers, not the chip architects.\n- **He came up through the job, from the analyst pool.** He joined in 2014 straight from Cornell (Dyson Scholar), after product marketing at Box and analytics at Affinity Solutions. The 2015 team page shows him as one of about ten undergrad-hire Analysts [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20150106214651/http://www.stripesgroup.com:80/team) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20191021155633/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog).\n- **The title ladder runs VP → Principal → Partner.** He was \"Vice President\" in 2019-10 and 2020-12, then \"Principal\" from 2021-03 to 2021-10, then \"Partner\" by 2022-01-18 [VP](https://web.archive.org/web/20201201030015/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog) [Principal](https://web.archive.org/web/20211027171100/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog) [Partner](https://web.archive.org/web/20220118110517/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog). At Stripes, Principal is the step just before Partner, which is not the industry norm. This confirms the firm-file \"title trap\" from the person side.\n- **The 2019 remit was \"software and restaurants\".** In his own bio, \"with a focus on software and restaurants,\" with Snooze and Udemy as active boards of involvement [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20191021155633/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog).\n- **Databricks and Crusoe landed on his page between 2024-11-15 and 2025-02-14.** The Nov 2024 snapshot lists only security and SaaS names. The Feb 2025 snapshot adds Crusoe and Databricks at the top [2024-11](https://web.archive.org/web/20241115025401/https://www.stripes.co/our-team/bryan-gertzog) [2025-02](https://web.archive.org/web/20250214041409/https://www.stripes.co/our-team/bryan-gertzog). That window contains Databricks' $10B Series J (announced 2024-12-17) and Crusoe's $600M Series D (Dec 2024). **Neither announcement names Stripes** [Databricks](https://www.databricks.com/company/newsroom/press-releases/databricks-raising-10b-series-j-investment-62b-valuation) [Crusoe D, press](https://builtin.com/articles/crusoe-raises-600m-series-d-2b-valuation-20241218). Crusoe's 31-investor Series E list (2025-10-24) doesn't name it either [src](https://www.cooley.com/news/coverage/2025/2025-10-24-crusoe-announces-%241-375-billion-oversubscribed-series-e). The entry vehicle, whether an unlisted allocation, a secondary or an SPV, is **unknown** (inference: consistent with the code-named co-invest vehicles in `firms/stripes.md`).\n- **His bio description shifted with the remit.** The 2024-11 snapshot says \"working with our enterprise companies\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20241115025401/https://www.stripes.co/our-team/bryan-gertzog). The 2026 page says \"working with our technology companies\" [src](https://www.stripes.co/our-team/bryan-gertzog).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium (downstream buyer, not a peer).** He never writes seed checks, and his checks are growth-sized. Why he still matters for THESIS.md:\n1. He co-ran the first growth price on Etched ($5B, Dec 2025), which the market later credited to Sequoia ($10.3B, Jul 2026). Since then, Etched has re-priced 4.2x to $21B.\n2. He is Stripes' continuity on compute: Databricks (2024), Crusoe and Etched (2025).\n3. His documented stage-in-then-lead behavior makes him a candidate Series B or C price-setter for the owner's AI-semis and new-lab seeds once customers are deploying, because his diligence keys on customer economics.\n\n**Deals that matter to the owner:** Etched, Crusoe, Databricks, Cognition and Applied Intuition. **Not his:** OpenAI (Fox + Andrew Xu) and Baseten (Ron Shah + Xu) [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/openai) [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/baseten).\n\n## Background\n- **Education.** Cornell, B.S. in Applied Economics and Management, summa cum laude; Dyson Scholar [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20161208084853/http://www.stripesgroup.com:80/team/bryan-gertzog).\n- **Before Stripes.** Product marketing at Box in the Bay Area, and Box campus ambassador at Cornell [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20161208084853/http://www.stripesgroup.com:80/team/bryan-gertzog). Analytics team at Affinity Solutions [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20191021155633/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog). Dates for both not stated.\n- **2014.** Joined Stripes Group as an Analyst; he appears on the 2015-01 team page [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20150106214651/http://www.stripesgroup.com:80/team). TheOrg says \"Partner since September 2014,\" which is an aggregator error given the archived titles.\n- **2016-12.** Senior Analyst, working on Refinery29, Flatiron Health and Netbiscuits [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20161208084853/http://www.stripesgroup.com:80/team/bryan-gertzog).\n- **2017-10.** Associate, working on Snooze, Refinery29 and Flatiron Health [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20171008155723/http://www.stripesgroup.com:80/team/bryan-gertzog).\n- **2019-10.** Vice President, \"software and restaurants\"; active on Snooze and Udemy; realized eMarketer, Flatiron Health and Upwork [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20191021155633/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog).\n- **2020-12.** VP, Software; active on Snyk, Verbit, Udemy, Dataiku, Legion, GlobalWebIndex and Snooze [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20201201030015/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog).\n- **2021-03 to 2021-10.** Principal [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20211027171100/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog).\n- **2022-01-18.** Partner, Software; adds Axonius, Oyster, Orca and Paradox [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20220118110517/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog).\n- **By 2024-11-15.** Partner and Investment Committee member [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20241115025401/https://www.stripes.co/our-team/bryan-gertzog).\n- **2026.** Partner (technology) and IC member. Current portfolio: Crusoe, Databricks, Etched, Snyk, Axonius, Island, Cognition, Orca, Verbit and Applied Intuition. Exited: Flatiron Health, Hunters, Paradox, Udemy and Upwork [src](https://www.stripes.co/our-team/bryan-gertzog).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.stripes.co/our-team/bryan-gertzog | Firm bio, 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | Stripes deal memos (archived) | Co-author of the Snyk, Dataiku, Legion, Axonius, Oyster, Orca, Paradox, Hunters and Island memos (2020-2022) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230205015421/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog). Nothing bylined after 2022; the Etched memo (2026-06-30) is unsigned |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/bryan-gertzog-34211355/ | Linked from the Stripes bio; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Two web searches for interviews, podcasts or panels returned no Gertzog results (2026-11-10).\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses.**\n  - Security hygiene: \"maintaining good security hygiene is one of the most important things\" (Orca).\n  - \"Cloud is the next big attack surface\" (Orca, 2021).\n  - Developer-first, bottoms-up adoption that gives \"a highly efficient sales motion\" (Snyk, 2020).\n  - \"Production is the product\" (Etched, 2026, unsigned firm memo).\n- **Recurring idea: product love, proven by customer calls, then a bigger check.** Each memo links the earlier deal to the next one: Axonius to Orca (asset visibility to cloud visibility), and Databricks and Crusoe to Etched (infra to silicon) [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched).\n- **Contrarian views.** None stated publicly. The contrarian act was pricing Etched at $5B before any brand-name growth fund. Even then, the Stripes memo frames inference as a consensus trend.\n- **Calls right.**\n  - Etched: $5B to $21B in eight months [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one).\n  - Applied Intuition: entered at $15B (Series F, 2025-06) [src](https://www.appliedintuition.com/news/series-f).\n  - Cognition: Stripes entered in 2025; Cognition raised its Series E at $48B on 2026-09-08 [src](https://cognition.com/blog/series-e).\n  - Exits: Flatiron Health, Upwork, Udemy (on his exited list).\n- **Calls wrong.** Nothing sourced.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Stripes on Etched (2026-06-30): the compute sweep, the customer-economics diligence and \"Production is the product\" [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched).\n2. Snyk memo (2020-01-20): the clearest statement of stage-in-then-lead [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240813193529/https://www.stripes.co/articles/developer-first-security-our-investment-in-snyk).\n3. Orca memo (2021-10-05, bylined Fox / Gertzog / Diorio): the CISO-reference diligence method [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240525163314/https://www.stripes.co/articles/securing-the-next-big-attack-surface-our-investment-in-orca).\n4. Island memo (2022-03-23): moving from Series A to Series B on customer feedback [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20241003234809/https://www.stripes.co/articles/the-first-enterprise-browser-our-investment-in-island).\n5. His 2019 bio: the \"software and restaurants\" remit, the baseline for measuring drift [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20191021155633/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public X account, and the only public channel is LinkedIn (not fetched).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Etched** | Series B, $500M at $5B post (Stripes \"Early Growth\") | 2025-12 (Bloomberg ~2026-01-13; announced 2026-06-30) | yes (Stripes led; team Fox, Gertzog, Charlie Carter) | Peter Thiel, Positive Sum, Ribbit, Jane Street, HRT, Two Sigma, VentureTech Alliance, Primary | [stripes](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched), [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/), [BLaw](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/ai-chip-startup-etched-says-jane-street-tsmc-linked-vc-invested) |\n| Etched | $700M at $21B (Stripes participated; personal role not stated) | 2026-08-18 | no (Jane Street) | Kleiner, Sequoia, a16z, Thiel, Tiger, BCV, Neo, Primary, Positive Sum, Diffusion, Argo, Blackstone | [etched.com](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one) |\n| **Crusoe** | Growth; round not stated (not named in Series D or E announcements) | \"since 2025\"; on his page by 2025-02-14 | unknown (team Gertzog, Carter; no Fox) | — | [stripes](https://www.stripes.co/investments/crusoe), [wayback](https://web.archive.org/web/20250214041409/https://www.stripes.co/our-team/bryan-gertzog) |\n| **Databricks** | Growth; round not stated (not named in the Series J release) | \"since 2024\"; added to his page between 2024-11 and 2025-02 | no (team Gertzog, Andrew Xu) | — | [stripes](https://www.stripes.co/investments/databricks) |\n| **Applied Intuition** | Series F + tender, $600M at $15B (Stripes a new investor) | 2025-06-17 | no (BlackRock and Kleiner led; team Gertzog, Maya Villasenor) | Franklin Templeton, QIA, ADIC, Premji, Greycroft, BAM Elevate, 137 Ventures | [AI](https://www.appliedintuition.com/news/series-f), [stripes](https://www.stripes.co/investments/applied-intuition) |\n| **Cognition** | Growth; entry round not stated (not named in the 2025-09-08 $10.2B round); participated in Series E, $2B+ at $48B | since 2025; Series E 2026-09-08 | no (Series E led by a16z and Accel; team Fox, Gertzog, Villasenor) | Founders Fund, GC, Avenir, Kleiner, Lux, 8VC, others | [stripes](https://www.stripes.co/investments/cognition), [Series E](https://cognition.com/blog/series-e) |\n| Snyk | Participated in Series B (Accel led), then led Series C $150M | B: not dated; C: 2020-01-20 | C: yes | Accel | [memo](https://web.archive.org/web/20240813193529/https://www.stripes.co/articles/developer-first-security-our-investment-in-snyk) |\n| Dataiku | Led Series D, $100M | 2020-08-24 | yes (memo by Ron Shah, Gertzog, Ripley Carroll) | — | [byline list](https://web.archive.org/web/20230205015421/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog) |\n| Legion | Series B | 2020-09-02 | not stated | — | [byline list](https://web.archive.org/web/20230205015421/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog) |\n| Axonius | Growth | 2021-03-01 | not stated | — | [byline list](https://web.archive.org/web/20230205015421/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog) |\n| Oyster | Growth | 2021-06-22 | not stated | — | [byline list](https://web.archive.org/web/20230205015421/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog) |\n| Island | Series A (2021), then Series B, $115M | B: 2022-03-23 | not stated | — | [memo](https://web.archive.org/web/20241003234809/https://www.stripes.co/articles/the-first-enterprise-browser-our-investment-in-island) |\n| Orca | Series C, $550M | 2021-10-05 | not stated | — | [memo](https://web.archive.org/web/20240525163314/https://www.stripes.co/articles/securing-the-next-big-attack-surface-our-investment-in-orca) |\n| Paradox (AI recruiting assistant \"Olivia\") | Growth | 2022-01-13 | not stated | — | [byline list](https://web.archive.org/web/20230205015421/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog) |\n| Hunters | Growth | 2022-01-24 | not stated | — | [byline list](https://web.archive.org/web/20230205015421/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog) |\n| Verbit, GlobalWebIndex, Udemy, Snooze | Growth | by 2020-12 | not stated | — | [bio 2020](https://web.archive.org/web/20201201030015/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog) |\n| Flatiron Health, Upwork, eMarketer, Refinery29 (support role as analyst/associate) | — | 2016-2019 | — | — | [bio 2016](https://web.archive.org/web/20161208084853/http://www.stripesgroup.com:80/team/bryan-gertzog), [bio 2019](https://web.archive.org/web/20191021155633/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog) |\n\n## Deal stories\n**Etched.**\n- **Was he first? No.**\n  - Primary seeded Etched in 2023-03, after majors passed [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/) [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond).\n  - Primary and Positive Sum co-led the $120M Series A in 2024-06.\n  - Stripes came in cold about 33 months after the seed and 18 months after the A, as **lead** of the $500M B at $5B (2025-12). This is the first growth-style price on the company.\n- **Gap to the next investors.**\n  - Bloomberg reported the round around 2026-01-13 [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-01-13/ai-chip-startup-etched-raises-500-million-to-take-on-nvidia), well before Sequoia.\n  - Sequoia led the C at $10.3B on 2026-07-23 (+7 months, 2.1x) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/).\n  - Jane Street led at $21B on 2026-08-18 (+8 months, 4.2x); Stripes participated [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one).\n- **Who did it.** The deal team was Ken Fox, Bryan Gertzog and Charlie Carter [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched). Who made first contact or championed the deal is **not in any source**.\n  - Gertzog is the likeliest champion. That is inference: he is the only partner on all three compute-infra deals, Fox is absent from Crusoe and Databricks, and Carter is the junior on both Etched and Crusoe.\n  - Caveat: Fox + Gertzog + one junior is also Stripes' standard formation on Snyk, Orca, Island and Axonius, so the roster alone doesn't prove who championed it.\n- **How they priced it.** Nothing found. No source describes how Stripes got to $5B, any internal debate, or its check size. The memo cites only customer diligence, talent pull and the inference thesis.\n\n**Snyk.** Stripes participated in the Accel-led B to watch the company, then led the $150M C in Jan 2020. The memo names Guy Podjarny and Peter McKay as the founders they met [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240813193529/https://www.stripes.co/articles/developer-first-security-our-investment-in-snyk). Ken Fox, not Gertzog, holds the board seat [src](https://snyk.io/about/board).\n\n**Island.** Stripes invested in the 2021 Series A after meeting Mike Fey and Dan Amiga, then \"deepened\" its position in the $115M B in 2022 on customer feedback [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20241003234809/https://www.stripes.co/articles/the-first-enterprise-browser-our-investment-in-island).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ken Fox (`ken-fox`) | Founder and senior partner. Co-named on Etched, Cognition, Snyk, Orca, Island and Axonius; holds the Snyk board seat | 2014 | [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched), [src](https://snyk.io/about/board) |\n| Charlie Carter (`charlie-carter`) | Junior on Etched and Crusoe with Gertzog | 2025 | [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/crusoe) |\n| Andrew Xu (`andrew-xu`) | Junior on Databricks with Gertzog | 2024 | [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/databricks) |\n| Maya Villasenor (`maya-villasenor`) | Junior on Applied Intuition, Cognition, Island, Axonius and Orca | 2021+ | [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/applied-intuition) |\n| Jahanvi Sardana (`jahanvi-sardana`, now Index) | Co-author of the Oyster, Axonius, Orca, Paradox, Hunters and Island memos | 2021-2022 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230205015421/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog) |\n| Ron Shah (`ron-shah`) | Co-author of the Dataiku memo (2020) | 2020 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230205015421/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog) |\n| John Diorio, Saagar Kulkarni, Fred Kauber, Vidhan Bhatt, Leroy Zhang, Ripley Carroll | Former Stripes co-authors on his memos (not on the 2026 team page) | 2020-2022 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230205015421/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog) |\n| Paul Melchiorre | Stripes operating partner; co-author of the Legion memo | 2020 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230205015421/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog) |\n| Gavin Uberti, Robert Wachen (Etched) | Portfolio founders | 2025 | [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched) |\n| Primary / Brian Schechter | Etched seed lead, in every round; Stripes priced after them | 2025 | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who sourced Etched for Stripes (Gertzog, Carter, Fox, or the research team under Basil Alomary), and was there an intro from Primary or Positive Sum?\n- Stripes' check inside the $500M, and whether a code-named co-invest SPV held it.\n- How Stripes entered Crusoe (early 2025) and Databricks (late 2024): neither primary round announcement names Stripes. A secondary, an SPV or an unlisted allocation?\n- Does he hold any board or observer seat on Etched or Crusoe? Nothing found.\n- His exact partner-promotion date (somewhere in 2021-10-27 to 2022-01-18) and IC-appointment date; LinkedIn may have both.\n- Did Stripes look at other AI-chip companies in the \"dozens across compute\" sweep (for example Groq, Cerebras, MatX, Tenstorrent)? Passes would show his price discipline.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.stripes.co/our-team/bryan-gertzog (2026 bio)\n- https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched, /crusoe, /databricks, /applied-intuition, /cognition, /openai, /baseten, /orca, /island, /axonius, /snyk (deal teams, fetched 2026-11-10)\n- Archived bios: https://web.archive.org/web/20150106214651/http://www.stripesgroup.com:80/team ; https://web.archive.org/web/20161208084853/http://www.stripesgroup.com:80/team/bryan-gertzog ; https://web.archive.org/web/20171008155723/http://www.stripesgroup.com:80/team/bryan-gertzog ; https://web.archive.org/web/20191021155633/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog ; https://web.archive.org/web/20201201030015/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog ; https://web.archive.org/web/20211027171100/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog ; https://web.archive.org/web/20220118110517/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog ; https://web.archive.org/web/20230205015421/https://www.stripes.co/team/bryan-gertzog ; https://web.archive.org/web/20241115025401/https://www.stripes.co/our-team/bryan-gertzog ; https://web.archive.org/web/20250214041409/https://www.stripes.co/our-team/bryan-gertzog\n- Archived memos: https://web.archive.org/web/20240813193529/https://www.stripes.co/articles/developer-first-security-our-investment-in-snyk ; https://web.archive.org/web/20240525163314/https://www.stripes.co/articles/securing-the-next-big-attack-surface-our-investment-in-orca ; https://web.archive.org/web/20241003234809/https://www.stripes.co/articles/the-first-enterprise-browser-our-investment-in-island\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/\n- https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/ai-chip-startup-etched-says-jane-street-tsmc-linked-vc-invested\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond ; https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round\n- https://www.appliedintuition.com/news/series-f\n- https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents ; https://cognition.com/blog/series-e\n- https://www.databricks.com/company/newsroom/press-releases/databricks-raising-10b-series-j-investment-62b-valuation\n- https://www.cooley.com/news/coverage/2025/2025-10-24-crusoe-announces-%241-375-billion-oversubscribed-series-e ; https://builtin.com/articles/crusoe-raises-600m-series-d-2b-valuation-20241218 (press, secondary)\n- https://snyk.io/about/board\n- https://theorg.com/org/stripes/org-chart/bryan-gertzog (aggregator; \"Partner since 2014\" contradicted by archives)\n", "person:bryan-offutt": "\n# Bryan Offutt\n\n## One-line take\nHe was the ML-infra deal finder behind Index's Lightning/Grid and Adaptive ML bets. Now he co-runs Hanabi Capital with ex-Index partner Mike Volpi, which turns up as a participant in nearly every hot AI-automation round of 2025–26 (Applied Compute, Cognition, Traversal, Simile, LiveKit, Netic).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He is a technical, infra-native evaluator. Index's own story of the Lightning deal has him judging the founders on the \"elegance\" of their code. He also has the Index/Volpi network behind him. As a result, Hanabi gets allocation in oversubscribed AI rounds (pre-launch Applied Compute, Index-led Simile and LiveKit) without leading.\n- **What constrains him.** Hanabi is a new fund (2025, aggregator) that has never been seen leading. Its check sizes and fund size are undisclosed. He has almost no public voice: no firm-site posts or podcast transcripts were found.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred, not sourced).** Price or lead a mega-round. Every Hanabi appearance found is \"with participation from\".\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Hanabi solved.** The repo carried Hanabi Capital as an unidentified, recurring co-investor. Traversal's launch post names the team: \"Bryan Offutt, Ishani Thakur & Mike Volpi of Hanabi\" [src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement). So Hanabi is an Index spin-out of two former Index partners.\n- **The real lead on Adaptive ML.** Index's company page tags only Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas. Fortune names Offutt: \"the Index Ventures partner who led the investment into Adaptive\" [src](https://fortune.com/2024/03/11/adaptive-startup-funding-falcon-ai-models-team-rlhf-rlaif/). He did the deal about six months before he left (end date 2024-09-30 per [MarketScreener](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/BRYAN-OFFUTT-A2K95M/), aggregator).\n- **The Lightning/Grid seed came through a call from a colleague.** Sarah Cannon found Lightning through its open-source traction and then: \"I promptly called my colleague Bryan Offutt who met Will and Luis in SF\". Index joined the seed \"days later\" and led the pre-launch Series A in 2020 [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/10/08/grid-ai-raises-18-6m-series-a-to-help-ai-researchers-and-engineers-bring-their-models-to-production/).\n- **Hanabi rides alongside Index.** Hanabi joined Index-led rounds at LiveKit (Series C, 2026-01-22) [src](https://livekit.com/blog/livekit-series-c/) and Simile ($100M round led by Index) [src](https://www.simile.com/blog/the-simulation-company). This is a pattern from primary posts, not a stated arrangement. It suggests the Volpi/Offutt relationships with Index are still live.\n- **Early in Applied Compute.** Hanabi was in the pre-launch $20M seed at $100M led by Benchmark's Victor Lazarte, \"alongside VC firms Conviction, Hanabi Capital and Definition\" [src](https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/ex-openai-applied-compute-raises-20m) (2025-06-27, paywalled preview). That is before Lux, Neo and Kleiner came in.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** At Index he chose AI-infra seeds well: Adaptive ML (RL/fine-tuning, ex-Falcon team) and Lightning (ML training). Hanabi sits in the AI-native automation rounds the owner likes: Applied Compute, Traversal, Cognition and Netic. But Hanabi follows rather than leads, and none of his deals is in pre-training labs or chips. He is most useful as a co-investor and a source of signal on who is getting allocation, and as the route to Mike Volpi. The deals that matter: Adaptive ML (seed lead, 2024), Applied Compute (seed, 2025), Traversal (seed+A, 2025).\n\n## Background\n- Index Ventures: Partner, 2018 to 2024-09-30 (aggregator dates: [MarketScreener](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/BRYAN-OFFUTT-A2K95M/)). He was active in ML/data infra from at least 2019 (Lightning/Grid).\n- Hanabi Capital: founder, 2025 (aggregator: [MarketScreener](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/BRYAN-OFFUTT-A2K95M/)). Partners named by Traversal: Offutt, Ishani Thakur and Mike Volpi [src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement).\n- Board: Great Expectations Labs / Superconductive Health, a data-quality company (aggregator: [MarketScreener](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/BRYAN-OFFUTT-A2K95M/)).\n- Education and pre-Index career: nothing found. The web search budget was exhausted in this run.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found (hanabi.vc and hanabi.capital do not resolve, 2026-11-10) | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found; no Index Perspectives byline found | |\n| X | not verified | |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The search budget was exhausted, so this is not a confirmed absence.\n\n## How they think\n- His Adaptive ML comments, paraphrased from Fortune: he valued founders who combine deep technical skill with business sense, and saw the hard part of RL-from-feedback products as getting end users to give feedback without burdening them [src](https://fortune.com/2024/03/11/adaptive-startup-funding-falcon-ai-models-team-rlhf-rlaif/).\n- Infra-first: his portfolio threads are data quality (Great Expectations), training infra (Lightning), post-training infra (Adaptive ML), real-time voice infra (LiveKit, as Hanabi) and AI SRE (Traversal, as Hanabi).\n- Contrarian views or admitted mistakes: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Fortune on the Adaptive ML seed, his only on-record investment comments found [src](https://fortune.com/2024/03/11/adaptive-startup-funding-falcon-ai-models-team-rlhf-rlaif/)\n2. TechCrunch on the Grid.ai Series A, for how the Lightning seed happened [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/10/08/grid-ai-raises-18-6m-series-a-to-help-ai-researchers-and-engineers-bring-their-models-to-production/)\n3. Traversal launch, which identifies the Hanabi team [src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X was not checked because the search budget was exhausted.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Adaptive ML | Seed $20M | 2024-03-11 | yes (Offutt, Index) | ICONIQ, Motier, Databricks Ventures, HuggingFund | [src](https://fortune.com/2024/03/11/adaptive-startup-funding-falcon-ai-models-team-rlhf-rlaif/) |\n| Grid.ai / Lightning | Seed | 2019 (about a year before 2020 A; exact date not given) | no (Index participated) | BCV (co-lead, per repo) | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/10/08/grid-ai-raises-18-6m-series-a-to-help-ai-researchers-and-engineers-bring-their-models-to-production/) |\n| Grid.ai / Lightning | Series A $18.6M | 2020 (closed summer; announced 2020-10-08) | Index led; Offutt's personal role in the A not stated | BCV, firstminute | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/10/08/grid-ai-raises-18-6m-series-a-to-help-ai-researchers-and-engineers-bring-their-models-to-production/) |\n| Great Expectations (Superconductive) | board seat; round not sourced | n/a | unknown | n/a | [aggregator](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/BRYAN-OFFUTT-A2K95M/) |\n| Traversal | Seed + Series A $48M (Hanabi) | 2025-06-18 | no (Sequoia, Kleiner led) | NFDG | [src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement) |\n| Applied Compute | Seed $20M at $100M (Hanabi; Offutt not named) | 2025-06-27 | no (Benchmark led) | Sequoia, Conviction, Definition, Zach Frankel | [src](https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/ex-openai-applied-compute-raises-20m) |\n| Applied Compute | B $80M; growth $350M (Hanabi) | 2026-04-08; 2026-11-02 | no | Kleiner led B; Elad Gil, Lux, Greenoaks, Neo | [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/fundraise), [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise) |\n| Cognition | $400M round; Series E (Hanabi) | 2025-09-08; 2026-09-08 | no | Founders Fund, Lux, D1 and others | [src](https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents), [src](https://cognition.com/blog/series-e) |\n| Netic | Series A $20M; Series B $23M (Hanabi) | 2025-06; 2025-11-13 | no (Founders Fund) | Greylock, Day One, SV Angel | [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm) |\n| LiveKit | Series C $100M at $1B (Hanabi) | 2026-01-22 | no (Index led) | Salesforce Ventures, Altimeter, Redpoint | [src](https://livekit.com/blog/livekit-series-c/) |\n| Simile | $100M (A, Index led); B >$200M (Hanabi) | 2026-02-12; 2026-07-30 | no | A*, BCV; Greenoaks/Index (B) | [src](https://www.simile.com/blog/the-simulation-company), [src](https://www.simile.com/blog/series-b) |\n\nThe Hanabi rows are firm-level. Offutt is personally named only on Traversal.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Lightning/Grid (2019–20).** Sarah Cannon learned of PyTorch Lightning's open-source traction and took the founders' call from a closet at a Helsinki conference. She then called Offutt, who met Will Falcon and Luis Capelo in SF and was impressed by their code. Index joined the seed (BCV co-led per repo) within days and led the $18.6M A pre-launch. **Senior/junior pairing:** Cannon originated and Offutt did the technical diligence [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/10/08/grid-ai-raises-18-6m-series-a-to-help-ai-researchers-and-engineers-bring-their-models-to-production/).\n- **Adaptive ML (2024-03).** Offutt led Index's $20M seed into the ex-Falcon (TII) team doing RLHF/RLAIF post-training infra. Index's company page later credits Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas, presumably the post-departure board handoff (inference) [src](https://fortune.com/2024/03/11/adaptive-startup-funding-falcon-ai-models-team-rlhf-rlaif/), [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/adaptive-ml/). Index was first institutional money, since this was the initial round.\n- **Traversal (2025).** Hanabi joined the combined seed + A led by Sequoia and Kleiner. All three Hanabi partners are thanked by name [src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Mike Volpi | Hanabi co-partner; former Index colleague | 2025 | [src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement) |\n| Ishani Thakur | Hanabi co-partner | 2025 | [src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement) |\n| Index Ventures | Partner, 2018–2024-09 | 2018 | [aggregator](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/BRYAN-OFFUTT-A2K95M/) |\n| Sarah Cannon | Index colleague; brought him into Lightning | 2019 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/10/08/grid-ai-raises-18-6m-series-a-to-help-ai-researchers-and-engineers-bring-their-models-to-production/) |\n| Will Falcon (Lightning) | Met founder pre-seed | 2019 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/10/08/grid-ai-raises-18-6m-series-a-to-help-ai-researchers-and-engineers-bring-their-models-to-production/) |\n| Adaptive ML founders (Launay, Pannier, Hesslow) | Seed lead | 2024 | [src](https://fortune.com/2024/03/11/adaptive-startup-funding-falcon-ai-models-team-rlhf-rlaif/) |\n| Great Expectations / Superconductive | Director | unknown | [aggregator](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/BRYAN-OFFUTT-A2K95M/) |\n| Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas | Inherited Adaptive ML at Index (inference) | 2024 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/adaptive-ml/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Hanabi's fund size, LPs and check sizes. Is Hanabi a pure-participant vehicle by design?\n- Is Hanabi's access to Index-led rounds (LiveKit, Simile) a formal arrangement or just relationships?\n- Who sources at Hanabi: Volpi's network, Offutt's infra network, or Ishani Thakur (background unknown)?\n- Offutt's X account and any podcast appearances. These were not checked because the search budget was exhausted.\n- Did Hanabi write any seed checks into labs or chips?\n- Pre-Index background and education.\n\n## Sources\n- https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement\n- https://fortune.com/2024/03/11/adaptive-startup-funding-falcon-ai-models-team-rlhf-rlaif/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2020/10/08/grid-ai-raises-18-6m-series-a-to-help-ai-researchers-and-engineers-bring-their-models-to-production/\n- https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/BRYAN-OFFUTT-A2K95M/ (aggregator)\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/adaptive-ml/\n- https://livekit.com/blog/livekit-series-c/\n- https://www.simile.com/blog/the-simulation-company\n- https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/ex-openai-applied-compute-raises-20m\n- Hanabi rows from repo-verified sources: https://appliedcompute.com/company/fundraise, https://cognition.com/blog/series-e, https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm, https://www.simile.com/blog/series-b\n", "person:bucky-moore": "\n# Bucky Moore\n\n## One-line take\nAn infra-and-developer-tools partner who took the Together AI board seat at Kleiner and then got credit at Lightspeed on Unconventional AI's $475M seed. He matters to you only if you can confirm where he sits now, because Lightspeed has quietly removed his profile.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** a long run as a developer-infrastructure investor (Teleport, Labelbox, Browserbase, Bun, Together AI) gives him credibility with technical founders. Lightspeed hired him laterally to co-lead enterprise investing in AI, infrastructure and cybersecurity ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-partner-bucky-moore-to-lightspeed/), 2025-05-05).\n- **Constraint:** at both firms his biggest AI names are shared credits. Mamoon Hamid bylined Kleiner's Together AI post ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/together-ai/)). At Lightspeed, Ravi Mhatre is co-listed on every one of his four AI company pages ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/unconventional-ai/)). He has no clean solo frontier-lab lead.\n- **What he'd never do:** nothing found. No source states a stage, valuation or sector line he won't cross.\n- **Status risk:** as of 2026-11-10, his Lightspeed team-member URL returns 404 ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/bucky-moore/)) and the team page doesn't list him ([src](https://lsvp.com/lightspeed-team/)). Company pages still credit him. That pattern usually means a departure.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Together AI deal and the board seat sit with different people.** Kleiner's post is bylined by Mamoon Hamid, but Together's own post says \"Bucky Moore from Kleiner Perkins will join the Together AI board\" ([src](https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a), 2023-11-29).\n- **He co-invested alongside Lightspeed before joining it.** Lightspeed's welcome post says he backed Nexthop, Cartesia and Materialize alongside Lightspeed while at Kleiner ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-partner-bucky-moore-to-lightspeed/)). The hire looks like it came out of those shared deals. Lightspeed's Nexthop page doesn't list him ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/nexthop-ai/)), so Nexthop stayed with Guru Chahal and Mhatre.\n- **He leaned toward people over product.** His deleted Lightspeed bio quoted him: \"I've come to appreciate the team is really all that really matters\" (via [Sourcery](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-bucky-moore-joins-30b-aum), 2025-05-05).\n- **He said leaving Kleiner was delicate.** In the Sourcery episode, he said a partner move means \"a lot of stakeholders that you have to respect\" ([src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-bucky-moore-joins-30b-aum)).\n- **He's worried about longer private holds.** On companies staying private: \"This double-edged sword creates new avenues for liquidity\" ([src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-bucky-moore-joins-30b-aum)). The full quote adds that it complicates early-stage returns.\n- **Cartesia: Lightspeed's page and the company's post give different dates.** Lightspeed's page lists Cartesia as a 2024 Series A ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/cartesia/)). Cartesia's own post says its $64M Series A, on 2025-03-11, was \"led by Kleiner Perkins\" ([src](https://cartesia.ai/blog/series-a)). Moore was still at Kleiner then. His Lightspeed credit is most likely inherited coverage of Lightspeed's earlier stake.\n- **His name on the Temporal page (Feb 2026) shows he was still at Lightspeed in early 2026.** Lightspeed's page lists him on the 2026 Series D ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/temporal/)), a $300M round led by a16z ([src](https://temporal.io/news/temporal-raises-300M-to-make-agentic-ai-real-for-companies), 2026-02-17). That puts any departure between Feb and Nov 2026.\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-10)\nMined from eleven of 22 catalogued items (2014-2026); rows in `data/rows/bucky-moore-voice-*.csv`. Captions are YouTube auto-transcripts.\n- **He said no to his own fund, then reportedly did it anyway.** In May 2025 he said starting a fund meant six months to find a partner plus 9-12 months raising, and asked \"do I really want to be out of the industry, out of the market\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OkdU58cfUI0), 2:33). On 20VC he called Lightspeed a chapter that \"could be the last\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Iscxo0mhp4Q), 1:08). Axios Pro Rata reported on 2026-10-15 that he is leaving to launch his own smaller firm (aggregator, [src](https://www.teahose.com/people/5618)). This resolves the 404 puzzle above, but is not confirmed from a primary source.\n- **Together AI: conviction came at meeting two.** He first met Vipul Ved Prakash just after the pivot away from \"the intersection of crypto and gpus\". Vipul was guarded at first. The second meeting brought Tri Dao's arrival as chief scientist, demand for compute outside the big clouds, and fast early growth ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5RylG1wt0eM), 4:51-6:23).\n- **He called the AI dev-platform shape before the deal.** In Feb 2023 he wrote that foundation models are a new cloud primitive and that the winning platform would have \"strong Python support\" and serverless GPUs ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/infrastructure-in-23/)). Nine months later he led Together's A.\n- **On neoclouds:** \"GPU reseller\" is a misreading, and the winners \"will look more like AWS or GCP\". Training compute is price-driven, so software only differentiates later ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bsC8lSJDW8c), 33:24-34:24). He sees alternative architectures such as Cartesia's state-space models as low-capex, startup-friendly ground (39:28).\n- **Mistakes he admits:** backing product or technology over the team's execution capacity ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Iscxo0mhp4Q), 37:47), and ignoring traction because he doubted the market (59:10). On a recent anonymised AI-app deal, his team waited to see which product was best and paid \"a lot higher\" a price. His lesson is that AI engineering talent is scarcer than domain expertise (39:50-40:20).\n- **Frontier labs:** a platform should \"either have to be in all of them or you have to pick one\" (54:01). He defended SSI's no-product strategy as buying \"less distraction and more ambition\" and wouldn't say whether Lightspeed invested (53:29). He thinks model-API revenue will \"yo-yo\", so products built on the models are the better business (51:28).\n- **House rules at Kleiner:** Kleiner would not back a portfolio company's competitor (\"a red line that we wouldn't cross\", [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Iscxo0mhp4Q), 9:19). He limited himself to the two lower layers of the AI stack and left apps to his partners ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H-4Mpy-_-do), 2023-06-08, 2:03).\n- **On seed pricing:** large funds are \"much more open-minded at the early stages on price\" because they plan to own the company all the way, which squeezes $30M seed funds ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OkdU58cfUI0), 28:03). When a market is undefined he tells founders that \"optionality is without a doubt your friend\" and to raise less ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Iscxo0mhp4Q), 23:30).\n- **Older deal origins:** Kleiner chased Netlify's founders to New York to win the Series B ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7YXZ8yPn8QI), Slush 2021, 7:48). The Materialize intro came via Cockroach Labs' Spencer, by inference Spencer Kimball. That post has no byline but is listed on his Kleiner profile ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/materialize-series-b/), 2020-11-30). His first corp-dev deal at Cisco was the failed bid for Martin Casado's Nicira, which VMware won ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bsC8lSJDW8c), 6:07).\n- **April 2023 predictions:** someone would release open weights and push the industry to follow. VCs were losing foundation-model rounds to strategics, who offer a cheaper cost of capital ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kps104aklvI), 13:48, 17:26).\n- **Career before Kleiner:** Battery Ventures, then Costanoa Ventures, after Cisco corp dev (2011-2014). He has a BS from USC ([src](https://techcrunch.com/author/bucky-moore/), author bio accessed 2026-11-10; Cisco dates from [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bsC8lSJDW8c), 11:11).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, conditional on where he is now.** The deal that matters most to you is **Unconventional AI**: a $475M seed for Naveen Rao's AI-hardware lab ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/), 2025-12-08). It is exactly your \"huge seed for a new compute lab\" pattern, and Lightspeed lists him as one of three partners on it. **Together AI** (AI compute cloud, his board seat) and **Cartesia** (state-space-model lab) are also on-thesis. The weakness is that his AI deals are all shared with Mhatre or Hamid, and the Lightspeed-era ones (Arena, Temporal) are later-stage follow-ons where Lightspeed wasn't first. If he has left to raise his own fund, he becomes a co-investor or LP-adjacent lead worth a call. If he has moved to another big platform, recalibrate to medium.\n\n## Background\n- About eleven years as a venture investor as of May 2025, mostly at Kleiner Perkins ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-partner-bucky-moore-to-lightspeed/)).\n- About 7.5 years at Kleiner Perkins ([src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-bucky-moore-joins-30b-aum)). He was Partner by 2023-11 ([src](https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a)). His Kleiner profile URL now returns 404 ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/bucky-moore/)).\n- Joined Lightspeed as Partner on 2025-05-05 to co-lead enterprise investing, focused on \"AI, infrastructure, and cybersecurity\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-partner-bucky-moore-to-lightspeed/)).\n- Not found: education, his pre-Kleiner firm, and his Kleiner start title and promotion dates.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | buckymoore.com does not resolve (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://substack.com/@buckymoore | Reader profile only; recommends Dwarkesh Podcast and From the New World. No posts. Identity unconfirmed |\n| X | https://x.com/buckymoore | Handle presumed; page not fetchable (402) |\n| LinkedIn | not fetched | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-05 — Sourcery (Molly O'Shea) — \"Bucky Moore joins $30B AUM Lightspeed\" — [link](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-bucky-moore-joins-30b-aum) — Calls AI \"an economic opportunity of a lifetime\". Talks about the stakeholder politics of leaving Kleiner and about longer private holds hurting early-stage returns. The host summarizes his founder filter as \"spiky\" founders with \"taste\". Those are the host's words, not his. Partly paywalled.\n\n## How they think\n- **Team over market:** \"the team is really all that really matters\" (deleted Lightspeed bio, via Sourcery).\n- **AI as an infrastructure layer:** the Together AI release has him framing AI as a new infrastructure layer for building software, with open-source models catching up to closed ones (paraphrased from [src](https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a)).\n- **Liquidity worry:** private companies staying private longer create liquidity paths but squeeze early-stage returns ([src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-bucky-moore-joins-30b-aum)).\n- Calls right or wrong: Bun (Kleiner-led seed, 2022) was acquired by Anthropic, announced 2025-12-02 ([src](https://bun.com/blog/bun-joins-anthropic)). No admitted mistakes found.\n\n## Read these first\n- Lightspeed welcome post: [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-partner-bucky-moore-to-lightspeed/). Lists his Kleiner leads and the deals he did alongside Lightspeed.\n- Sourcery episode: [src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-bucky-moore-joins-30b-aum). His only long-form interview found.\n- Together AI Series A post: [src](https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a). His board seat and his take on open-source AI.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X was not fetchable and search budget was exhausted this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Unconventional AI | Seed ($475M) | 2025-12-08 | Lightspeed reported as co-lead with a16z (Lightspeed co-lead not confirmed from primary source) | a16z (co-lead); Lightspeed partners Guru Chahal, Ravi Mhatre; Sequoia also at Pre-Seed/Seed | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/unconventional-ai/), [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/) |\n| Arena (ex-LMArena) | Series A ($150M) | 2026-01-06 | no (Felicis + UC Investments led) | a16z, Kleiner, House Fund, LDVP, Laude; Lightspeed partners James Alcorn, Mhatre | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/arena/), [arena](https://arena.ai/blog/series-a/) |\n| Temporal | Series D ($300M, $5B) | 2026-02-17 | no (a16z led; Lightspeed new investor) | Sapphire, Sequoia, Index, Tiger, GIC, Madrona, Amplify; Lightspeed partners Anoushka Vaswani, Mhatre, Justin Shen | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/temporal/), [temporal](https://temporal.io/news/temporal-raises-300M-to-make-agentic-ai-real-for-companies) |\n| Cartesia | Series A ($64M) | 2025-03-11 | Kleiner led (while he was there). Lightspeed page lists Lightspeed's entry as 2024 Series A | Lightspeed partners Raviraj Jain, Mhatre | [cartesia](https://cartesia.ai/blog/series-a), [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/cartesia/) |\n| Together AI | Series A ($102.5M) | 2023-11-29 | yes (Kleiner); board seat | NVIDIA, Emergence, NEA, Prosperity 7, Greycroft, 137 Ventures | [src](https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a) |\n| Browserbase | Series A ($21M) | 2024-11 | Kleiner co-led with CRV | CRV, Okta Ventures | [lsvp story](https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-partner-bucky-moore-to-lightspeed/), [pulse2](https://pulse2.com/browserbase-web-browser-automation-company-raises-21-million-series-a) |\n| Labelbox | early (round not sourced) | not sourced | \"led early investments\" per Lightspeed | | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-partner-bucky-moore-to-lightspeed/) |\n| Teleport | early (round not sourced) | not sourced | \"led early investments\" per Lightspeed | | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-partner-bucky-moore-to-lightspeed/) |\n| Bun (Oven) | Seed ($7M) | 2022 | Kleiner led; founder thanks \"Bucky & Leigh Marie\" | | [src](https://bun.com/blog/bun-joins-anthropic) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Together AI (Kleiner, 2023):** Kleiner led the $102.5M A and Moore took the board seat ([src](https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a)). Lux, Definition and others had already backed the seed, so Kleiner was not first.\n- **Cartesia:** Lightspeed was already in (its page says 2024), and Kleiner led the 2025-03-11 A ([src](https://cartesia.ai/blog/series-a)). The Lightspeed post says this was a deal he did alongside Lightspeed ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-partner-bucky-moore-to-lightspeed/)). Two months later he moved to Lightspeed.\n- **Unconventional AI (Lightspeed, Dec 2025):** a $475M seed for Naveen Rao's AI compute lab. The a16z post names itself as co-lead but not the other co-lead ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/)). The repo's earlier rows credit Lightspeed as the other co-lead from press (not re-verified this pass). Lightspeed's page lists Chahal, Mhatre and Moore. Who sourced it is not stated anywhere.\n- **Arena and Temporal (Lightspeed, 2026):** late-stage entries. Lightspeed came in as a participant in Arena's A after a16z's seed, and as a new investor in Temporal's D. Moore is one of three or four partners listed on each.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ravi Mhatre | Co-listed on all four Lightspeed AI deals | 2025 | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/unconventional-ai/) |\n| Guru Chahal | Co-partner on Unconventional AI | 2025 | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/unconventional-ai/) |\n| James Alcorn | Co-partner on Arena | 2025 | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/arena/) |\n| Raviraj Jain | Co-partner on Cartesia | 2024 | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/cartesia/) |\n| Anoushka Vaswani; Justin Shen | Co-partners on Temporal | 2026 | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/temporal/) |\n| Mamoon Hamid | Kleiner byline on Together AI; Moore took the board seat | 2023 | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/together-ai/) |\n| Leigh Marie Braswell | Co-credited on Bun seed | 2022 | [src](https://bun.com/blog/bun-joins-anthropic) |\n| Together AI | Board member (from the Kleiner Series A) | 2023-11 | [src](https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Has he left Lightspeed, and for where?** His profile and team-page listing are gone but company pages still credit him (2026-11-10). An earlier repo lead row (kleiner-perkins) carries an unverified summary claim that he left to start his own firm. Check LinkedIn and X. Web search budget ran out this pass.\n- Who at Lightspeed actually sourced Unconventional AI? Confirm Lightspeed's co-lead role from a primary source.\n- His pre-Kleiner firm, Kleiner start title and promotion dates (needed for fast_promotion).\n- Rounds and dates for Labelbox and Teleport. Whether he was on Windsurf (Sourcery lists it among his investments without saying his role).\n- Does he keep the Together AI board seat after leaving Kleiner?\n\n## Sources\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-partner-bucky-moore-to-lightspeed/\n- https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-bucky-moore-joins-30b-aum\n- https://lsvp.com/team-member/bucky-moore/ (404, 2026-11-10)\n- https://lsvp.com/lightspeed-team/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/unconventional-ai/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/arena/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/cartesia/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/temporal/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/nexthop-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/\n- https://arena.ai/blog/series-a/\n- https://temporal.io/news/temporal-raises-300M-to-make-agentic-ai-real-for-companies\n- https://cartesia.ai/blog/series-a\n- https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/together-ai/\n- https://bun.com/blog/bun-joins-anthropic\n- https://pulse2.com/browserbase-web-browser-automation-company-raises-21-million-series-a (secondary)\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/bucky-moore/ (404)\n- https://substack.com/@buckymoore\n", "person:carl-fritjofsson": "\n# Carl Fritjofsson\n\n## One-line take\nCreandum's San Francisco GP and its AI-infrastructure partner: H Company's $220M lab seed, nexos.ai, and a led seed in GPU virtualisation (Hosted.ai).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he is the bridge. European founders get a Creandum partner who lives in SF; US-facing infra founders get European capital with a brand.\n- **How he picks:** layers of the AI stack that become mandatory plumbing: model routing (nexos.ai), GPU utilisation (Hosted.ai), event data (Kurrent).\n- **Constraint:** in lab-scale rounds he is a small participant (\"alongside Accel, Eric Schmidt, UIPath, Amazon\").\n\n## Differential insight\n- On H Company: \"We believe that H Company represents the next frontier of generative AI\" ([src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-h-company)). This was a European seed fund writing into a $220M seed.\n- On Hosted.ai: \"We're proud to lead Hosted.ai's $19M Seed alongside People Ventures and Repeat VC.\" ([src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-hosted-ai-the-neoclouds-silver-lining)). His framing: neoclouds run at about 40% utilisation because GPU memory cannot be shared.\n- Creandum's 2026 promotion post says he \"joined as an associate\" and is now part of \"the owners' group of the franchise\" ([src](https://creandum.com/stories/promoting-jakob-stein-to-partner)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. Compute-layer seeds and a new-lab seed are squarely my lens, and he is building the SF team (Andrew McLean, Ghazwa Khalatbari) that will do more of them from Fund VIII.\n\n## Background\n- Management consulting, then co-founded bootstrapped ad network Adprofit with his now wife ([src](https://creandum.com/team/carl-fritjofsson)).\n- Creandum: joined as associate; General Partner since 2021-11; based in San Francisco (Creandum has had an SF presence since 2012).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://creandum.com/team/carl-fritjofsson | |\n| Substack / blog | https://creandum.com/stories | memos under his byline |\n| X | https://twitter.com/fritjofsson | follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/fritjofsson/ | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-02 — Momentum Podcast — [link](https://open.spotify.com/episode/6mQGpwIA6TTL9ep7iv03L0) — not transcribed.\n- 2023-09 — SaaS Revolution Show — What's required to raise Venture Capital when the world has changed — [link](https://thesaasrevolutionshow.simplecast.com/episodes/whats-required-to-raise-venture-capital-when-the-world-has-changed) — not transcribed.\n- 2023-05 — LA Venture Podcast — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r-j2KAPuI8U) — not transcribed.\n\n## How they think\n- Enterprises will run many models, so orchestration and routing is a durable layer (nexos.ai).\n- The AI build-out is sold \"like it's 2005\": whole GPU servers rented at low utilisation; software that shares GPUs is the fix (Hosted.ai).\n- Multi-agent, multimodal models as the step after general LLMs (H Company).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Backing Hosted·ai](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-hosted-ai-the-neoclouds-silver-lining) (2026-03)\n- [Backing H Company](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-h-company) (2024-05)\n- [Backing nexos.ai](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-nexos) (2025-01)\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI deals at Creandum.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Codesphere | not stated | 2024-05-17 | not stated | n/a | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-codesphere) |\n| H Company | Seed $220M | 2024-05-21 | No | Accel, Eric Schmidt, UiPath, Amazon, Xavier Niel, Yuri Milner | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-h-company) |\n| nexos.ai | Seed $8M | 2025-01-17 | No | Index Ventures, Dig Ventures | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-nexos) |\n| Hosted.ai | Seed $19M | 2026-03-19 | Yes | People Ventures, Repeat VC | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-hosted-ai-the-neoclouds-silver-lining) |\n\nAlso authored posts on Kurrent (2024-12) and Appcharge (2024-11).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **nexos.ai:** founder Tomas Okmanas (Nord Security, Oxylabs) started it after spending over $100K a month on LLM usage across his companies; Creandum joined Index and Dig in the $8M seed and followed in the EUR30M Series A at EUR300M (2025-10).\n- **Hosted.ai:** founders include Ditlev Bredahl (UK2 Group, OnApp); offices include Bangalore.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Creandum | General Partner; owner | 2021-11 (GP) | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/carl-fritjofsson-creandums-latest-general-partner) |\n| Index Ventures, Dig Ventures | Co-investors on nexos.ai (\"our friends at Index and Dig Ventures\") | 2025-01 | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-nexos) |\n| Tomas Okmanas | Founder backed | 2025-01 | same |\n| Charles Kantor, Karl Tuyls (H Company) | Founders backed | 2024-05 | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-h-company) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is he the partner on OLIX and Modal? Neither is attributed.\n- Creandum start date and promotion steps before GP.\n- What the SF office will lead from Fund VIII.\n\n## Sources\n- https://creandum.com/team/carl-fritjofsson\n- https://creandum.com/stories/backing-h-company , /backing-nexos , /backing-hosted-ai-the-neoclouds-silver-lining , /backing-codesphere\n- https://creandum.com/stories/promoting-jakob-stein-to-partner\n", "person:carlo-agostinelli": "\n# Carlo Agostinelli\n\n## One-line take\nCo-founder of Nova, the two-person \"talent investing\" firm with Koko Xs. He is its public voice on deals (Midas, Actor Labs) and its link to one of the richest family offices in private equity. Watch him for that capital, not for a deal record of his own.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He is a recruiter and connector more than a picker. His own X posts show him cold-pitching people onto the team ([src](https://x.com/carloagostinel2/status/1973394215611801652)) and celebrating founders. Koko Xs runs the investment framework (see `people/koko-xs.md`). Carlo brings a Stanford '23 network and the Agostinelli name: his father Robert co-founded Rhône ([src](https://britainweekly.co.uk/carlo-agostinelli), secondary bio). The family office invests in AI chips (Tenstorrent Series E, [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-announces-series-e)) and in defense robotics (the XTEND/JFB merger, [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2024306/000149315226006957/ex99-1.htm)).\n- **What constrains him:** The filings show almost no outside money behind his vehicles. He appears on exactly five Form Ds. Four of them show $0 sold: AV1 Fund I ($20M target, never amended), Nova Venture Fund II ($100M target), Nova Flappy and Nova Venture Fund III. The fifth, Nova Select, took $5M from one investor ([EDGAR full-text search](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Carlo%20Agostinelli%22&forms=D)). Nova therefore leads only small rounds ($10M Midas) and joins big ones through SPVs.\n- **What he'd never do (inference from his own post):** Treat a high valuation as success. In 2024 he wrote that the advice \"equating a high valuation with success, is flawed and needs to stop\" ([X, 2024-10-21](https://x.com/carloagostinel2/status/1848408945527763336)). Nova Flappy, which bought into Flapping Airplanes' A at a reported $6B valuation, cuts against that stance.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Of the five vehicles he is on, only Nova Select has sold anything.** EDGAR full-text search for \"Carlo Agostinelli\" returns exactly five Form Ds: AV1 Fund I (2025-01-16), Nova Venture Fund II (2026-02-27), Nova Flappy (2026-08-07), Nova Select (2026-09-14) and Nova Venture Fund III (2026-11-06). Only Nova Select shows money sold: \"$5,000,000\" from one investor, first sale 2026-09-03 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2153845/000215384526000001/primary_doc.xml)). These are filing snapshots. Funds often amend later.\n- **AV1 Fund I came first and was never topped up.** He filed it alone from 196 Banyan Road, Palm Beach, as \"Managing Director of the General Partner\", with a $20M target and $0 sold. The Form D gives 2024 as the year of incorporation and was filed 2025-01-16, the same month Nova started ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2045537/000204553725000001/primary_doc.xml)). The EDGAR submissions index shows no amendment ([src](https://data.sec.gov/submissions/CIK0002045537.json)). AV1 looks like his solo vehicle that was folded into Nova.\n- **Henry Lien moved from AV1 to Nova.** Carlo: \"One year ago today, I cold outreached @Henrymlien\" ([X, 2025-10-01](https://x.com/carloagostinel2/status/1973394215611801652)). That puts the outreach around 2024-10. An aggregator lists Henry Lien as \"chief of staff at AV1\" and an investor at Nova from 2025-01 (village.ai via search snippet; the page returned 403, [src](https://village.ai/person/henry-lien-NTczNTM2MTUy)). If accurate, Nova has a third investing person and an origin in AV1.\n- **The Actor Labs seed is now confirmed.** Eniac's own announcement thanks \"the guys at the Nova Global\" among the backers of the $4M seed ([src](https://www.eniac.vc/writings/actor-labs-raises-4m-seed-round-led-by-eniac-ventures)). Carlo publicly credited founder Lane Burgett: \"Couldn't be more proud of @laneburgett and the entire Actor team\" ([X, 2026-05-11](https://x.com/carloagostinel2/status/2053902578413711595)). The repo had this as \"unconfirmed\"; that label is wrong.\n- **He states a contrarian view on valuation:** \"equating a high valuation with success, is flawed and needs to stop\" ([X, 2024-10-21](https://x.com/carloagostinel2/status/1848408945527763336)). This predates Nova.\n- **The family office is concentrated in AI chips and defense, through vehicles not tied to Carlo.** \"Agostinelli Group\" (a self-described \"global single family office\", [src](https://www.agostinelligroup.com/)) is a strategic investor in the XTEND/JFB defense-robotics merger alongside Eric Trump ([8-K ex. 99.1, 2026-02-17](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2024306/000149315226006957/ex99-1.htm)). It joined a $9M private placement in Aureus Greenway, a drone and defense roll-up ([8-K, 2026-03-11](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2009312/000149315226009672/ex99-1.htm)), and Tenstorrent's $1.5B Series E ([src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-announces-series-e)). Its site lists an SF office and names no team. No source names Carlo at the Group.\n- **The Musk-adjacent family branch.** Massimo Agostinelli, Robert's son per Wikipedia ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Massimo_Agostinelli)), runs Think 1st Principles (T1P), the \"Muskonomy\" arm of the Agostinelli Family Office. A HEALWELL AI investment in xAI went through T1P ([BetaKit, 2024-06-28](https://betakit.com/healwell-ai-invests-2-7-million-cad-into-elon-musks-xai-to-tap-into-companys-expertise-resources/)). Massimo signed two 2026 SPV Form Ds:\n  - \"TBC a Series of T1P Capital LLC\": $1.447M from 3 investors; the related person is T1P AG, Wollerau, Switzerland ([2026-08-21](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2148168/000214816826000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - \"EREBOR III a series of Eagle X Holdings LLC\": $2,486,307 from 8 investors, administered by Allocations, at the same 196 Banyan Rd address as AV1 ([2026-11-06](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2163766/000216376626000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - Valor Equity Partners, the most frequent co-investor in Nova's deals (Vigil, Midas, Judgment), is also Musk-orbit capital. The overlap is suggestive, but no source draws the link.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High,** for two reasons, neither of which is a proven personal record:\n1. **Co-investor in my exact lane.** Nova is in Flapping Airplanes (frontier lab, via the Nova Flappy SPV), Actor Labs (robotics models), Midas (formal verification of AI, led) and Judgment Labs (agent evals). It recruits talent for portfolio companies.\n2. **Gateway to a single family office with AI-chip exposure (Tenstorrent).** The Agostinelli Group says it invests in venture \"with a concentrated long-term strategy\". A family office that buys concentrated AI-chip positions is the kind of LP that could back a $20M-check seed strategy. Whether it backs outside managers is unknown.\n\nCaveat: Carlo's own decision-making is undocumented. Nova's investment framework is published under Koko's name.\n\n## Background\n- Born in Paris, July 2000; French and American nationality (britainweekly, citing a UK company record; secondary) ([src](https://britainweekly.co.uk/carlo-agostinelli)).\n- Harrow School 2014–2019, then Stanford 2019–2023 in International Relations, where he played varsity soccer (same secondary source).\n- Director of Star Corporation London Ltd, 2020-07 to 2021-08 (dissolved), per the same source citing Companies House.\n- 2024: forms AV1 Fund I (the Form D gives 2024 as the year of incorporation). Around 2024-10 he cold-recruits Henry Lien (his X post).\n- 2025-01: co-founds Nova with Koko Xs (Crunchbase, aggregator: [src](https://www.crunchbase.com/person/carlo-agostinelli-2160)). Koko: \"Nova is a venture company I founded with my cofounder Carlo\" ([src](https://kokoxs.com/)).\n- Family: father Robert F. Agostinelli, co-founder and chairman of Rhône (secondary bio). Britainweekly names his mother as Mathilde Favier and a sibling as Héloïse Agostinelli.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found (nova.nexus is a login wall) | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Nova's essays are under Koko's name |\n| X | https://x.com/carloagostinel2 | Bio says co-founder of @novaholdings, SF; about 1,072 posts per a search snippet (2026-11); couldn't open (402). Ignore @C_Agostinelli2, a different person |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/carlo-agostinelli-0280aa168 | 6,171 followers per the post page (2026-11) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found where he speaks. About a month before 2026-11 he reshared Patrick O'Shaughnessy's interview with Noah Shinn of Instinct on LinkedIn ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/carlo-agostinelli-0280aa168_inside-the-personal-ai-assistant-growing-activity-7510484827427569664-yZWC)). He was the sharer, not the interviewer. No source shows Nova in Instinct (the seed was Conviction and Greenoaks).\n\n## How they think\n- **Thesis:** \"backing founders with the potential to become historical figures\" ([Midas release, 2026-02-05](https://pulse2.com/midas-10-million-funding/?noamp=mobile)). The same Napoleon framing shows up in his \"@kolelee_ meets Napoleon\" post about Vigil's founder ([X, 2025-05-09](https://x.com/carloagostinel2/status/1920712827020439568)).\n- **Contrarian:** high valuations are not success (X, 2024-10-21, above).\n- **Talent above all:** \"Talent is everything,\" in a post celebrating Mercor ([X, 2025-02-21](https://x.com/carloagostinel2/status/1892773923663802407)).\n- **Calls right or wrong:** Too early to tell. Instinct, which he amplified, went from a $50M seed to a $10B valuation within months, but Nova is not shown as an investor.\n\n## Read these first\n1. The Midas launch release, his only on-record quote ([src](https://pulse2.com/midas-10-million-funding/?noamp=mobile)).\n2. His valuation post ([X](https://x.com/carloagostinel2/status/1848408945527763336)).\n3. His Henry Lien cold-outreach post ([X](https://x.com/carloagostinel2/status/1973394215611801652)).\n4. Koko's Nova Business Model v1 (unanimous IC, so Carlo has a veto) ([src](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Nova-Business-Model-v1.pdf)).\n\n## Social engagement\nFrom search snippets only (X returned 402):\n- Celebrates founders: Actor (Lane Burgett), Vigil (Kole Lee), Mercor.\n- Cheers Owner.com's Adam Guild and Cory Levy (\"Only the beginning for @adamguild and certainly only the beginning for @cory\", [2025-05-13](https://x.com/carloagostinel2/status/1922326265823973503)).\n- Posts about team travel with Koko (\"Amsterdam ! @kokoxsu\", [2025-03-03](https://x.com/carloagostinel2/status/1896595410938208387)).\n- Tags @Henrymlien and @kokoxs often.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Midas (AI formal verification) | Seed ($10M launch round) | 2026-02-05 | Yes (Nova); Carlo quoted | Valor Equity Partners | [src](https://pulse2.com/midas-10-million-funding/?noamp=mobile) |\n| Actor Labs (robot models) | Seed ($4M) | announcement undated; Carlo's post 2026-05-11 | No (Eniac led) | Hyperion, Hummingbird, 2048 Ventures, Vanderbilt alumni, angels | [src](https://www.eniac.vc/writings/actor-labs-raises-4m-seed-round-led-by-eniac-ventures) |\n| Flapping Airplanes (frontier lab) | Series A ($1B; reported $6B) via Nova Flappy SPV; he is MD of the GP | SPV 2026-08-07; A 2026-10-21 | No (Index, Lux) | Sequoia, GV, Kleiner, NVIDIA, Kravis, Duquesne, Malka | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2146901/000123191926000844/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Judgment Labs (agent evals) | Series A (part of $32M combined); firm-level only, he is not named | 2026-05-12 | No (Lightspeed, Greenoaks) | Valor, SV Angel, Dynamic | [src](https://judgmentlabs.ai/blogs/announcing-32m-led-by-lightspeed) |\n| Vigil Labs (AI trading) | Seed $5.7M, Nova led; Crunchbase credits Koko | 2025-08-20 | Yes (Nova) | Lux, Pantera, SV Angel, Valor | [src](https://www.techflowpost.com/en-US/newsletter/95710) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Midas:** Nova led a $10M launch round for a company founded by IMO and IOI medalists. Carlo was the quoted partner. The first release named Valor as a co-lead; a corrected release names Nova alone ([src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/backed-openai-tesla-spacex-investors-130000334.html)). The intro path is not stated.\n- **Flapping Airplanes:** see `firms/nova-global.md`. The SPV was filed about 10 weeks before the A was announced. No source names Carlo as the person who ran the deal.\n- **Actor Labs:** Nova was a participant behind Eniac. Carlo is the Nova person who posted about it.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Koko Xs | Nova co-founder; co-MD on every Nova Form D | 2025-01 | [src](https://kokoxs.com/) |\n| Henry Lien | Cold-recruited by Carlo; investor at Nova; earlier \"chief of staff at AV1\" (aggregator) | ~2024-10 | [X](https://x.com/carloagostinel2/status/1973394215611801652) |\n| Robert F. Agostinelli (Rhône) | Father (secondary bio) | | [src](https://britainweekly.co.uk/carlo-agostinelli) |\n| Agostinelli Group (single family office) | Family office; no source names Carlo there | | [src](https://www.agostinelligroup.com/) |\n| Massimo Agostinelli (T1P) | Also Robert's son per Wikipedia; same Palm Beach address on Form Ds; sibling link not stated by any source | | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2163766/000216376626000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Lane Burgett (Actor Labs) | Portfolio founder | 2026 | [X](https://x.com/carloagostinel2/status/2053902578413711595) |\n| Kole Lee (Vigil Labs) | Portfolio founder | 2025 | [X](https://x.com/carloagostinel2/status/1920712827020439568) |\n| Midas founders Renzo Balcazar, Shalim Monteagudo-Contreras | Portfolio founders | 2026-02 | [src](https://quantumzeitgeist.com/midas-ai-ai-security/) |\n| Valor Equity Partners | Repeat co-investor (Vigil, Midas, Judgment) | 2025 | see Portfolio |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Who is Nova Select's single $5M investor?** Agostinelli family money is the obvious candidate, but nothing in the filing or elsewhere says so.\n- **Is Agostinelli family capital behind Nova at all?** Koko's site names Andreessen, Kravis, Druckenmiller and Malka as backers, and no Agostinelli. Neither EDGAR nor the press names an Agostinelli LP.\n- **Did AV1 Fund I ever take capital?** It has $0 on its only Form D and no amendment. What did it invest in before Nova?\n- **Is Massimo Carlo's half-brother?** Both are sons of Robert per separate sources, and they share the 196 Banyan Rd address. No single source states it.\n- **What are \"TBC\" (T1P series) and EREBOR III?** EREBOR III presumably holds Erebor bank shares, which is unconfirmed.\n- **Who is the Agostinelli Group's SF office, and does Carlo run its venture sleeve?** It lists an SF office, and Carlo is SF-based.\n- **Did Carlo introduce the Judgment Labs co-founders to a hire?** A search snippet of an unopened LinkedIn post suggests \"Carlo Agostinelli introduced me to Alex Shan, Joseph Camyre, and Andrew Li\". The speaker is unknown. Not verified.\n- **Is the \"Nova\" in Applied Compute's $350M round (2026-11-02) this Nova?** Lux is also in that round ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise)).\n- **Was Nova in Owner.com?** The Adam Guild and Cory Levy post suggests a link. Not confirmed.\n- **Ignore the namesake.** A Carlo Agostinelli at Ghisallo Capital (Hong Kong, per fintool) is likely a different person.\n\n## Sources\n- SEC EDGAR full-text search \"Carlo Agostinelli\" (Form D): https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Carlo%20Agostinelli%22&forms=D\n- Form Ds: AV1 Fund I https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2045537/000204553725000001/primary_doc.xml ; Nova Venture Fund II https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2110809/000123191926000227/primary_doc.xml ; Nova Flappy https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2146901/000123191926000844/primary_doc.xml ; Nova Select https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2153845/000215384526000001/primary_doc.xml ; Nova Venture Fund III https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2159208/000215920826000001/primary_doc.xml\n- Massimo Agostinelli Form Ds: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2148168/000214816826000001/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2163766/000216376626000001/primary_doc.xml\n- Agostinelli Group in 8-Ks: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2024306/000149315226006957/ex99-1.htm ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2009312/000149315226009672/ex99-1.htm ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2024306/000119312526091735/d26422d425.htm\n- https://www.agostinelligroup.com/ ; https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-announces-series-e\n- https://www.eniac.vc/writings/actor-labs-raises-4m-seed-round-led-by-eniac-ventures\n- https://pulse2.com/midas-10-million-funding/?noamp=mobile ; https://quantumzeitgeist.com/midas-ai-ai-security/ ; https://finance.yahoo.com/news/backed-openai-tesla-spacex-investors-130000334.html\n- X posts by @carloagostinel2 (seen via search snippets; dates decoded from post IDs): 1848408945527763336, 1892773923663802407, 1896595410938208387, 1920712827020439568, 1922326265823973503, 1973394215611801652, 2053902578413711595\n- https://britainweekly.co.uk/carlo-agostinelli (secondary bio, 2026-08-24)\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Massimo_Agostinelli ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Agostinelli\n- https://betakit.com/healwell-ai-invests-2-7-million-cad-into-elon-musks-xai-to-tap-into-companys-expertise-resources/\n- https://village.ai/person/henry-lien-NTczNTM2MTUy (aggregator; 403, search snippet only)\n- https://kokoxs.com/ ; firms/nova-global.md ; people/koko-xs.md\n", "person:carlos-gonzalez-cadenas": "\n# Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas\n\n## One-line take\nAn ex-operator (GoCardless, Skyscanner) who led Index's Tessl A and Tacto, but left the Index partnership in April 2026 and became COO of incident.io in September 2026, so they are now a relationship contact, not a co-investor.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they won:** Operator credibility with European SaaS and dev-tools founders, plus long London relationships. The Tessl deal came from knowing Guy Podjarny for a decade, and incident.io from leading its Seed and A.\n- **What constrained them:** They said at hire they were \"not joining to do seed\". Their focus was Europe and software. The AI work they actually originated is the application layer (AI-native dev tools, AI procurement), not labs, models or compute.\n- **Hidden fact:** Three of the five AI companies on their Index page were led by other partners: Adaptive ML (Offutt), EthonAI (Wilhelm) and the Cradle A (Dolfe).\n- **Now:** They resigned as an Index LLP member on 2026-04-01 and took an operating seat at a portfolio company.\n- **What they'd never do:** Nothing found suggests they would back a pre-training lab or chip company.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **They are no longer an Index check-writer.** Companies House shows the Index Ventures (UK) LLP membership \"Resigned on 1 April 2026\" ([src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/70SAtLnNRy3FTn41qnJdhPcqFgc/appointments)). The Index team page still lists them ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/carlos-gonzalez-cadenas/)).\n- **They joined incident.io as COO.** An aggregator relaying CEO Stephen Whitworth's LinkedIn post says they \"led its seed and Series A rounds at Index Ventures, joined as COO\" in September 2026 ([src](https://www.jackandjill.ai/companies/incident-io); [LinkedIn post](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/stephenwhitworth_really-happy-to-announce-that-carlos-gonzalez-cadenas-activity-7505565740134072320-47Kj), which could not be read verbatim). The same post says they moved to a Venture Partner role at Index earlier in 2026. That comes from a summary, so confidence is low.\n- **The AI credits on their page are inherited.**\n  - EthonAI: Reuters names \"Katharina Wilhelm, a partner at Index who led the investment\" ([src](https://sg.news.yahoo.com/index-ventures-backs-ai-manufacturing-080626804.html)).\n  - Adaptive ML: Fortune credits Bryan Offutt and does not mention Gonzalez-Cadenas ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/03/11/adaptive-startup-funding-falcon-ai-models-team-rlhf-rlaif/)).\n  - Cradle: Sofia Dolfe was the Index voice on the 2023 Series A ([src](https://www.biopharma-reporter.com/Article/2023/11/23/biotech-startup-cradle-raises-24-million-series-a-to-accelerate-ai-technology)). The Series B page names \"Carlos and Sofia\" ([src](https://www.cradle.bio/blog/series-b)).\n  - **Lesson for the roster sweep:** Index team pages aggregate portfolio companies. They do not attribute who originated a deal.\n- **Their style at hire:** \"I like conviction investing, I like going early\", but \"I'm not joining to do seed\" ([TechCrunch, 2020-11-20](https://techcrunch.com/2020/11/20/carlos-gonzalez-cadenas-interview/)).\n- **Their board model:** quick questions handled asynchronously on Slack, plus a few deep sessions a year on \"moments of truth\". In their words, \"the model is quite scalable because you are engaging 2-3 times per year\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/11/20/carlos-gonzalez-cadenas-interview/)).\n- **Tessl was a repeat-founder bet.** They credited Podjarny with \"a great track record of catalyzing cultural change in how developers work\" ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/11/14/tessl-funding-ai-software-development-platform/)). Index was not first in. GV and boldstart co-led a $25M seed in April 2024, and Index's $100M A followed about 7 months later.\n- **How they frame AI:** as a source of operational load on software teams. \"AI is accelerating software development and deployment\" (incident.io Series B, [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/incidentio-raises-62m-to-build-ai-agents-that-resolve-incidents-with-you-302424782.html)).\n- **Lead count:** A third-party count credits them with one Index-led Series A since 2024 ([PostRound, 2025-04-17](https://postround.substack.com/p/the-fund-codex-index-ventures); company unnamed). Low confidence.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.**\n- Their originated AI deals are application-layer Series A rounds in Europe: Tessl (AI-native dev) and Tacto (AI supply-chain automation, near the \"AI-native automation\" bucket).\n- They have no lab, pre-training or chip deals.\n- They said they were not a seed investor.\n- They left the Index partnership in April 2026 to run operations at incident.io.\n\n**Where the value is:**\n- As a relationship node into Index London: Wilhelm, Duizendstraal, Dolfe.\n- As a calibration point. The real AI originators behind \"their\" deals are Offutt (Adaptive ML, RL infra, high fit), Wilhelm and Dolfe.\n- Tacto is the closest deal to my AI-native-automation taste.\n\n## Background\n- Founded the travel startup Fogg, which Skyscanner acquired. They then became Skyscanner's CPO ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/carlos-gonzalez-cadenas/)).\n- At GoCardless they were CPO and CTO, then COO, and they remain a board director ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/carlos-gonzalez-cadenas/)). Start year 2017, per secondary directories only.\n- Angel in more than 50 companies by 2020 ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2020/11/20/carlos-gonzalez-cadenas-interview/)) and more than 60 per the Index bio, including Codat, cargo.one, Hopin and Shapr3D.\n- Partner at Index Ventures London: LLP member 2021-01-04 to 2026-04-01 ([Companies House](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/70SAtLnNRy3FTn41qnJdhPcqFgc/appointments)).\n- Venture Partner at Index from 2026-04, then COO of incident.io from 2026-09 ([src](https://www.jackandjill.ai/companies/incident-io)).\n- Director of Sylvera since 2022-01-24. Director of Pineapple Technology Ltd from 2022-06-16 to 2026-09-14 ([Companies House](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/70SAtLnNRy3FTn41qnJdhPcqFgc/appointments)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/carlos-gonzalez-cadenas/ | Index bio, still live 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Index Perspectives posts only |\n| X | nothing found | no handle confirmed |\n| LinkedIn | https://linkedin.com/in/carlosgonzalezcadenas | linked from Index bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2020-11: TechCrunch interview by Steve O'Hear on the move from operator to VC. It covers conviction investing, a Europe focus, and three deal-flow channels: operator network, co-investors and VC referrals ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/11/20/carlos-gonzalez-cadenas-interview/)).\n- 2025-11-05: incident.io fireside chat in London on founding, scaling, operating and investing ([Luma](https://luma.com/889waxz9); [incident.io on X](https://x.com/incident_io/status/1978854376882983353)). No recording found.\n- No podcast transcripts found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Conviction and early, but Series A as the lane.** They said they would invest across stages and not seed-only ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2020/11/20/carlos-gonzalez-cadenas-interview/)).\n- **Founder-movement framing.** They described Tessl as \"a movement to change how software gets made\" ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/11/14/tessl-funding-ai-software-development-platform/)).\n- **AI as an operational-complexity tailwind** for dev and infra tooling ([PRNewswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/incidentio-raises-62m-to-build-ai-agents-that-resolve-incidents-with-you-302424782.html)).\n- **European optimism.** They called 2020 \"the best moment\" for European entrepreneurship ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2020/11/20/carlos-gonzalez-cadenas-interview/)).\n- **Calls right or wrong:** incident.io grew from Seed through a $62M Series B in 2025 ([src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/incidentio-raises-62m-to-build-ai-agents-that-resolve-incidents-with-you-302424782.html)), a strong enough conviction that they joined it. Tessl's outcome is not yet known.\n\n## Read these first\n1. TechCrunch, 2020-11-20: their investing philosophy, sourcing channels and board model ([link](https://techcrunch.com/2020/11/20/carlos-gonzalez-cadenas-interview/)).\n2. Index post on Tessl, 2024-11-14, co-written with Duizendstraal: shows how they underwrite a repeat founder ([link](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/when-everyone-speaks-software-tessls-vision-for-ai-native-development/)).\n3. Index post on Tacto, 2023-12-12, co-written with Wilhelm ([link](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/tacto-secures-50m-to-tackle-disruptions-and-bureaucracy-in-industrial-supply-chains/)).\n4. Index post on the incident.io Series A, 2022-07-13 ([link](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/incidentio-raises-342m-to-help-businesses-build-resilience-in-the-face-of-failure/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No active X account was confirmed. Public output is Index Perspectives posts and press quotes. incident.io's own X account amplified the 2025 fireside chat ([src](https://x.com/incident_io/status/1978854376882983353)).\n\n## Portfolio\nAI-relevant deals, plus incident.io because it explains them.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Tessl | Series A, $100M (reported $750M valuation) | 2024-11-14 | Yes. Lead author for Index, with Juriaan Duizendstraal | Accel, GV, boldstart (GV and boldstart co-led the $25M seed in 2024-04, so Index was not first) | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/11/14/tessl-funding-ai-software-development-platform/), [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/when-everyone-speaks-software-tessls-vision-for-ai-native-development/) |\n| Tacto | €50M (stage not named) | 2023-12-12 | Co-led with Sequoia. Co-authored with Kathi Wilhelm | Sequoia | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/tacto-secures-50m-to-tackle-disruptions-and-bureaucracy-in-industrial-supply-chains/) |\n| incident.io | Seed | not confirmed | Yes, per the CEO's post (relayed) | Point Nine (earlier investor) | [jackandjill](https://www.jackandjill.ai/companies/incident-io) |\n| incident.io | Series A, $28.7M | 2022-07-13 | Yes. Wrote the Index post | Point Nine, Mike Krieger, Lachy Groom, Mantis VC | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/incidentio-raises-342m-to-help-businesses-build-resilience-in-the-face-of-failure/) |\n| incident.io | Series B, $62M (AI agents) | 2025-04-10 | No. Insight Partners led; they were quoted | Insight Partners, Point Nine | [PRNewswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/incidentio-raises-62m-to-build-ai-agents-that-resolve-incidents-with-you-302424782.html) |\n| Cradle | Series B, $73M | 2024-11-26 (page metadata says 2026-11-05) | No. IVP led; shared Index seat with Sofia Dolfe, who led the A | IVP, Kindred | [Cradle](https://www.cradle.bio/blog/series-b) |\n| EthonAI | Series A, CHF 15M (about $16.5M) | 2024-05-30 | Index led, but Katharina Wilhelm was the lead partner | General Catalyst, Earlybird, Founderful | [Reuters via Yahoo](https://sg.news.yahoo.com/index-ventures-backs-ai-manufacturing-080626804.html) |\n| Adaptive ML | Seed, $20M | 2024-03-11 | Index led, but Bryan Offutt was the lead partner; the seat was inherited | ICONIQ, Motier, Databricks Ventures, HuggingFund | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/03/11/adaptive-startup-funding-falcon-ai-models-team-rlhf-rlaif/) |\n\nNon-AI companies listed on their Index page: Causaly, DataSnipper, Pomelo, Sylvera (board seat since 2022-01-24), Taktile ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/carlos-gonzalez-cadenas/)). Rounds were not verified because the web-search budget ran out.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Tessl.**\n  - Index had known Podjarny for more than a decade. Their offices were near each other in London while Gonzalez-Cadenas was at GoCardless and Podjarny was building Snyk ([Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/when-everyone-speaks-software-tessls-vision-for-ai-native-development/)).\n  - GV and boldstart seeded the company in April 2024. Index then led the $100M A about 7 months later, at a reported $750M valuation, nine months after the company was founded ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/11/14/tessl-funding-ai-software-development-platform/)).\n  - Senior/junior pairing: Gonzalez-Cadenas with Juriaan Duizendstraal.\n- **Tacto.** Index and Sequoia co-led the €50M round, and the Index post presents it as Tacto's first Index money. The Index pairing was Gonzalez-Cadenas with Kathi Wilhelm. No intro path was found.\n- **incident.io.**\n  - They led the Seed and the Series A, sat close to the company for about five years, then joined as COO in 2026-09 ([jackandjill](https://www.jackandjill.ai/companies/incident-io)).\n  - The Series B was led by Insight Partners in 2025-04, with Index following on.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Index Ventures | Partner and LLP member, then Venture Partner | 2021-01-04 to 2026-04-01, then 2026-04 | [Companies House](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/70SAtLnNRy3FTn41qnJdhPcqFgc/appointments) |\n| incident.io / Stephen Whitworth | Led its Seed and A; now COO under the CEO | 2022 (A) to present | [jackandjill](https://www.jackandjill.ai/companies/incident-io) |\n| Guy Podjarny (Tessl, Snyk) | Known for more than a decade; Index invested in Tessl | 2024 investment | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/when-everyone-speaks-software-tessls-vision-for-ai-native-development/) |\n| Juriaan Duizendstraal (Index) | Co-author on Tessl | 2024-11 | same |\n| Kathi Wilhelm (Index) | Co-author on Tacto; led EthonAI | 2023-12 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/tacto-secures-50m-to-tackle-disruptions-and-bureaucracy-in-industrial-supply-chains/) |\n| Sofia Dolfe (Index) | Shared seat on Cradle | 2024-11 | [Cradle](https://www.cradle.bio/blog/series-b) |\n| Bryan Offutt (ex-Index) | Inherited Adaptive ML from them | 2024 | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/03/11/adaptive-startup-funding-falcon-ai-models-team-rlhf-rlaif/) |\n| GoCardless | Ex-CPO/CTO and COO; board director | not confirmed | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/team/carlos-gonzalez-cadenas/) |\n| Sylvera | Director | 2022-01-24 | [Companies House](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/70SAtLnNRy3FTn41qnJdhPcqFgc/appointments) |\n\n## Open questions\n- `firms/index-ventures.md` lists them as a current Partner. That should be updated to: LLP member resigned 2026-04-01, Venture Partner, now COO of incident.io. This is for the merging session; I did not edit the firm file.\n- Who at Index now holds the Tessl and Tacto board seats? Juriaan Duizendstraal or Kathi Wilhelm are the likely candidates.\n- Which company is Pineapple Technology Ltd? They were a director from 2022-06 to 2026-09.\n- Has Tacto raised again since 2023, and who led? Not checked because the search budget ran out.\n- Bryan Offutt's current seat. He is the real AI-infra originator behind Adaptive ML and a high-fit lead.\n- Does the Venture Partner title come with any check-writing or scout capacity at Index? Not confirmed.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/carlos-gonzalez-cadenas/\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/70SAtLnNRy3FTn41qnJdhPcqFgc/appointments\n- https://www.jackandjill.ai/companies/incident-io (aggregator relaying https://www.linkedin.com/posts/stephenwhitworth_really-happy-to-announce-that-carlos-gonzalez-cadenas-activity-7505565740134072320-47Kj)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2020/11/20/carlos-gonzalez-cadenas-interview/\n- https://fortune.com/2024/11/14/tessl-funding-ai-software-development-platform/\n- https://siliconangle.com/2024/11/14/ai-powered-developer-tooling-startup-tessl-raises-125m\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/when-everyone-speaks-software-tessls-vision-for-ai-native-development/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/tacto-secures-50m-to-tackle-disruptions-and-bureaucracy-in-industrial-supply-chains/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/incidentio-raises-342m-to-help-businesses-build-resilience-in-the-face-of-failure/\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/incidentio-raises-62m-to-build-ai-agents-that-resolve-incidents-with-you-302424782.html\n- https://www.cradle.bio/blog/series-b\n- https://www.biopharma-reporter.com/Article/2023/11/23/biotech-startup-cradle-raises-24-million-series-a-to-accelerate-ai-technology\n- https://sg.news.yahoo.com/index-ventures-backs-ai-manufacturing-080626804.html\n- https://fortune.com/2024/03/11/adaptive-startup-funding-falcon-ai-models-team-rlhf-rlaif/\n- https://postround.substack.com/p/the-fund-codex-index-ventures\n- https://luma.com/889waxz9\n- https://x.com/incident_io/status/1978854376882983353\n", "person:charlie-carter": "\n# Charlie Carter\n\n## One-line take\nCarter is the only non-partner on Stripes' deal team for the Etched $500M Series B at $5B, the first growth price on the most important AI-chip company in this repo, set about seven months before Sequoia's $10.3B round. Stripes' own pages also show Carter has been an \"Investor\" there far longer than a \"rising junior\" framing suggests [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched).\n\n## Crux\n- **What they are:** a long-tenured, deal-team analyst inside a growth firm, not an independent decision-maker. Stripes' deal pages name Carter on Legion (since 2020), CarOnSale, Balto, Fabric and Equip (since 2021) and Connecteam (since 2022). The 2025 entries are Etched, Crusoe and Flock Safety [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/legion), [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/caronsale).\n- **Why they matter:** Carter is the common junior name on both of Stripes' 2025 compute bets. Partner Bryan Gertzog is on both, and founder Ken Fox is added on Etched [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched), [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/crusoe). If the \"dozens of companies across compute\" sweep produced the Etched pick, Carter is the likeliest person to have built the comparison set. That is an inference; no source names who did the sweep.\n- **What constrains them:** still titled Investor after 5+ years of named deals. At Stripes, the investment committee is Fox and the partners [src](https://www.stripes.co/our-team), so Carter can't set price alone. The firm is growth-only, with no seed program.\n- **What they'd never do (by mandate):** a pre-product seed. Stripes' stage labels run from Early Growth to Growth Buyout [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The \"rising junior\" framing is wrong on tenure.** Stripes' own deal pages put Carter on Legion with a \"Since 2020\" label, and on four companies with \"Since 2021\" labels. The 2025-09 headshot date (`250909_Stripes_Charlie_...`) looks like a re-shoot, not a join date. Caveat: deal pages may show current coverage rather than the original deal team. The Legion page reads \"Aliza Reisner Principal Charlie Carter Investor\" [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/legion).\n- **Promotion is slow.** Named on deals since 2020 and still \"Investor\" in November 2026, so `fast_promotion: no`. The first visible promotion would come right after Etched's markup (4.2x in eight months) and would be a strong signal to watch for. Bio quote: \"Charlie is a member of the investment team with a focus on technology\" [src](https://www.stripes.co/our-team/charlie-carter).\n- **The Crusoe entry route is unexplained.** Stripes lists Crusoe as \"Growth\", \"Since 2025\", with team Gertzog and Carter [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/crusoe). Stripes isn't named in press lists for Crusoe's 2025-10 Series E ($1.38B, Valor and Mubadala co-led) [src](https://convergedigest.com/crusoe-hits-10b-valuation-to-build-gigawatt-scale-ai-data-centers/) or in the full named list for the 2026-09-17 Series F ($3.9B at $30.9B) [src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-f-funding). The likeliest routes are an unnamed Series E allocation or a secondary or tender purchase. This is unconfirmed.\n- **The Etched diligence crux was customer-sourced.** Stripes' memo says \"customers told us Etched was the first system that could truly transform their inference economics\". Reference calls are the work an Investor-level person typically runs (inference) [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched).\n- **Pre-AI pattern:** a Bain PE software-diligence background, then mid-market SaaS growth deals (workforce: Legion, Connecteam; contact-center AI: Balto; commerce: Fabric; marketplace: CarOnSale; health: Equip). That's a unit-economics diligence toolkit, which fits the \"inference economics\" framing on Etched. Bio: \"primarily supporting software diligences within the firm's Private Equity Group\" [src](https://www.stripes.co/our-team/charlie-carter).\n- **No public voice.** No X account, podcast, Substack or interview was found (searches on 2026-11-10). The only first-person text is Stripes' unsigned Etched memo.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Growth-stage and junior, so not a co-investor peer for $20M seed checks. But Carter sits on the step-up round for AI chips and compute (Etched B, Crusoe). Carter is who an AI-semis seed company from my portfolio would meet when it raises at a multi-billion price, and a cheap relationship to build before they're promoted. Deals that matter: **Etched** (Series B lead, $500M at $5B, 2025-12), **Crusoe** (Growth, 2025). Watch for: a promotion to VP, or Carter's name on a new chip or inference deal (Stripes' Baseten 2026 team is Ron Shah and Andrew Xu, not Carter) [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/baseten).\n\n## Background\n- B.S. in Business and Global Affairs, Georgetown University (year not stated) [src](https://www.stripes.co/our-team/charlie-carter).\n- Associate Consultant at Bain & Company, Private Equity Group, mainly supporting software diligence (dates not stated) [src](https://www.stripes.co/our-team/charlie-carter).\n- Stripes, Investor. First named deal is Legion, \"Since 2020\" [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/legion). The join date isn't stated, and LinkedIn wasn't fetched.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.stripes.co/our-team/charlie-carter | Firm bio, 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Searched 2026-11-10 |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/charlie-carter-4035ba162/ | Linked from the Stripes bio; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found (web searches 2026-11-10 for \"Charlie Carter\" with Stripes, Etched, Crusoe, Georgetown and Bain returned only unrelated people).\n\n## How they think\n- No first-person material. By inference from the deal set: a software-diligence analyst who moved into AI infrastructure through the firm's 2024-2025 pivot (Databricks 2024, then Crusoe, OpenAI, Cognition and Etched in 2025) [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched).\n- The firm-level thesis on Etched, which Carter worked on: \"inference is one of the defining trends in AI\" [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched).\n- Calls so far: Etched B at $5B, followed by the Sequoia C at $10.3B and the Jane Street round at $21B within about eight months [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one). Carter's personal contribution is unknown.\n\n## Read these first\n- Stripes' Etched investment overview (2026-06-30): the only written account of the compute sweep and diligence [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched).\n- `firms/stripes.md`: the deal-team map, title trap and co-invest vehicles.\n- Primary's \"every round\" Etched note, for the seed-side view of the same B round [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. There's no public social presence. Stripes posts only on LinkedIn [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched).\n\n## Portfolio\nRounds aren't stated on stripes.co for most deals. \"Since\" is Stripes' first-investment year.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Etched | Series B, $500M at $5B post | 2025-12 (disclosed 2026-06-30; Bloomberg reported the raise 2026-01-13) | yes (Stripes) | VentureTech Alliance, Jane Street, HRT, Two Sigma, Ribbit, Thiel, Karpathy, Fei-Fei Li | [Stripes](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched), [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/) |\n| Etched | $700M at $21B | 2026-08-18 | no (Jane Street) | Kleiner, Sequoia, a16z, Thiel, Tiger, BCV, Neo, Primary, Positive Sum, Diffusion, Argo, Blackstone | [etched.com](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one) (firm participation; Carter's role not stated) |\n| Crusoe | Growth (round not stated; not in named Series E or F lists) | since 2025 | unknown | — | [Stripes](https://www.stripes.co/investments/crusoe) |\n| Flock Safety | Growth | since 2025 | unknown | — | [Stripes](https://www.stripes.co/investments/flock-safety) (team: Ron Shah, Julie Herendeen, Carter) |\n| Legion | Early Growth | since 2020 | unknown | — | [Stripes](https://www.stripes.co/investments/legion) |\n| CarOnSale | Early Growth | since 2021 | unknown | — | [Stripes](https://www.stripes.co/investments/caronsale) (Carter is the only Stripes name) |\n| Balto | Early Growth | since 2021 | unknown | — | [Stripes](https://www.stripes.co/investments/balto) |\n| Fabric | Early Growth | since 2021 | unknown | — | [Stripes](https://www.stripes.co/investments/fabric) |\n| Equip | Early Growth | since 2021 | unknown | — | [Stripes](https://www.stripes.co/investments/equip) |\n| Connecteam | Early Growth | since 2022 | unknown | — | [Stripes](https://www.stripes.co/investments/connecteam) |\n\n## Deal stories\n**Etched (Carter's role and timing):**\n- Carter is listed on the Stripes team beside Ken Fox and Bryan Gertzog [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched). Who made first contact or championed the deal isn't stated anywhere found.\n- **Were they first? No on the company, yes on the price.** The sequence:\n  - Primary seed, $5.4M at about $34M (2023-03); majors passed [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/).\n  - $120M Series A, co-led by Primary and Positive Sum (2024-06) [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round).\n  - **Stripes $500M at $5B (2025-12)**, the first growth-style price, about 33 months after the seed.\n  - Sequoia $300M at $10.3B (2026-07-23), 2.1x Stripes [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/).\n  - Jane Street $700M at $21B (2026-08-18), 4.2x Stripes [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one).\n- Ramtin Naimi credits Sequoia's price as the consensus turn (`people/ramtin-naimi.md`). Stripes' deal team had priced it about seven months earlier, and the raise was reported in January [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-01-13/ai-chip-startup-etched-raises-500-million-to-take-on-nvidia).\n- **How they sourced it:** a compute-layer sweep, \"looking at dozens of companies across compute\" [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched).\n- **How they decided on price:** Stripes says nothing about price discipline. Its stated reasons were customer references on inference economics, the hiring of senior NVIDIA, Google TPU, Broadcom and SK Hynix leaders, and contracts in production. The $5B is about 147x the seed's post, and the round was a lead check far larger than Stripes' usual size (Fund VII sold about $1.05B). That points to co-invest vehicles (see `firms/stripes.md`). This is inference.\n\n**Crusoe:** Gertzog and Carter. \"Since 2025\", \"Growth\". No round or price was found. Stripes was late by any measure: Bain Capital Ventures and Founders Fund were seed backers in 2018-19, and Crusoe was valued at about $10B by 2025-10 [src](https://convergedigest.com/crusoe-hits-10b-valuation-to-build-gigawatt-scale-ai-data-centers/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Stripes | Investor, investment team (technology) | by 2020 (first named deal) | [src](https://www.stripes.co/our-team/charlie-carter) |\n| Bain & Company | Former Associate Consultant, PE Group | dates not stated | [src](https://www.stripes.co/our-team/charlie-carter) |\n| Bryan Gertzog | Deal-team partner on Etched and Crusoe | 2025 | [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/crusoe) |\n| Ken Fox | Deal-team partner on Etched | 2025 | [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched) |\n| Ron Shah | Deal-team partner on Flock Safety, Fabric | 2021 | [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/fabric) |\n| Oliver Chernyk | Co-deal-team (VP) on Connecteam | 2022 | [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/connecteam) |\n| Aliza Reisner | Co-deal-team (Principal) on Legion | 2020 | [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/legion) |\n| Marco Lorenzon | Co-deal-team (VP) on Equip | 2021 | [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/equip) |\n| Gavin Uberti, Robert Wachen | Founders of Etched (portfolio) | 2025 | [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched) |\n| Chase Lochmiller | CEO of Crusoe (portfolio) | 2025 | [src](https://www.stripes.co/investments/crusoe) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who on the Etched deal team made first contact, and what was the intro path? Did the \"dozens of companies across compute\" sweep include other chip startups (which ones, and who passed)?\n- What's the Crusoe entry route (Series E allocation, secondary, or tender) and its price?\n- When did Carter join Stripes? Do the \"Since 2020/2021\" labels reflect the original deal team? LinkedIn would settle both.\n- Did Carter get a promotion after the Etched markup? Re-check stripes.co/our-team in 2027.\n- Stripes' Etched dollar amount and which co-invest vehicle (if any) holds it.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.stripes.co/our-team/charlie-carter (bio, fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched (deal team and memo, 2026-06-30)\n- https://www.stripes.co/investments/crusoe, /flock-safety, /legion, /caronsale, /balto, /fabric, /equip, /connecteam, /baseten (deal teams, fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-01-13/ai-chip-startup-etched-raises-500-million-to-take-on-nvidia (URL date only; 403)\n- https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-f-funding\n- https://convergedigest.com/crusoe-hits-10b-valuation-to-build-gigawatt-scale-ai-data-centers/ (secondary)\n- `firms/stripes.md`, `people/ramtin-naimi.md`, `patterns/co-investor-gangs.md`\n", "person:charlie-curnin": "\n# Charlie Curnin\n\n## One-line take\nCo-named Sequoia partner (with Stephanie Zhan) on Sequoia's first-money seed into Reflection AI, a DeepMind-founded lab that later raised a $2B Series B at $8B. Where they went after Sequoia is the open question, and it decides whether they're worth tracking. ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/), [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=715519))\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they could win:** they sat on the deal team for one of the clearest \"first institutional money into a new frontier lab\" bets of the 2024 cycle. Sequoia partnered with Reflection in 2024 as an Arc company, before the March 2025 stealth launch ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/)).\n- **Constraint:** no public footprint beyond that one deal. There is no Sequoia bio (the people URL no longer lists them) and no podcasts, Substack or X content were found, so their own judgment can't be separated from Zhan's or Sequoia's.\n- **What they'd never do:** not knowable from sources.\n- Treat this as a deal-team credit on a high-fit lab, not yet as a proven independent investor.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Timing (first in):** Sequoia's post, co-signed by Curnin, calls the firm Reflection's \"first and long-term partner in this journey\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-reflection-toward-superintelligence-with-autonomous-coding/), 2025-03-07). The company page says \"Partnered 2024\" and Arc 2024 ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/)). CRV co-led the $25M seed. CRV and Lightspeed then co-led the $105M Series A, announced the same day ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956)). So Sequoia and CRV were first, and Lightspeed came in at the A, announced alongside it. The gap between the seed close and the A is not public.\n- **Closeness to founders:** the post says that working with Misha Laskin and Ioannis Antonoglou was \"one of the greatest adventures of our lives.\" That suggests hands-on Arc-style involvement during the year Reflection was in stealth, not a passive check ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-reflection-toward-superintelligence-with-autonomous-coding/)).\n- **Thesis at the time:** the post frames the bet as \"autonomous coding agents that handle workloads autonomously.\" That is the wedge Reflection used before it repositioned as an open frontier lab with its 2025-10 Series B ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-reflection-toward-superintelligence-with-autonomous-coding/), [src](https://reflection.ai/blog/building-frontier-open-intelligencence)).\n- **Departure signal:** Sequoia still names Curnin as a partner on the Reflection company page, but the Sequoia team page no longer lists them (checked 2026-11-09 via [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/charlie-curnin/), which renders the team listing without their name). The departure date is unknown.\n- No podcasts, Substack, X posts or talks by Curnin were found. The web-search budget was exhausted, so this is not proof that none exist.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium (provisional; could rise to high).** Their only sourced deal, Reflection AI (seed, 2024, first money), is exactly my category: a new frontier lab from DeepMind pre-training and post-training researchers. Later rounds were $2B at $8B in 2025-10 and a reported $2.5B Series C in 2026 ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=715519), [src](https://multiples.vc/private-comps/reflection-ai) aggregator). If they've left to start a fund or joined a new lab or seed firm, they could be a high-fit rising junior with Sequoia-trained access to lab founders. Until they're identified, it's one deal of unclear personal attribution.\n\n## Background\nNothing found beyond Sequoia Capital (investor; co-author of the 2025-03-07 Reflection post). Education, prior jobs, start and end dates at Sequoia, and current firm are all unknown.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | x.com/charliecurnin (unverified handle guess; returned HTTP 402 on 2026-11-09) | unconfirmed |\n| LinkedIn | linkedin.com/in/charliecurnin (unverified handle guess; blocked, HTTP 999) | unconfirmed |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found.\n\n## How they think\n- On the record only through the co-signed Reflection post: the next leap is autonomous coding agents, and Sequoia had \"deep conviction\" in the founders ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-reflection-toward-superintelligence-with-autonomous-coding/)).\n- No contrarian views or calls attributable to them individually were found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Partnering with Reflection: Toward Superintelligence with Autonomous Coding](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-reflection-toward-superintelligence-with-autonomous-coding/) (Zhan and Curnin, 2025-03-07). Their only known writing.\n2. [Sequoia company page: Reflection AI](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/). Partnered 2024, Arc 2024, and lists them as partner.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Reflection AI | Seed ($25M) | partnered 2024; announced 2025-03-07 | Sequoia co-led with CRV (Curnin co-named partner with Stephanie Zhan) | CRV | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Reflection AI:** Sequoia partnered in 2024 through Arc 2024 ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/)). The firm co-led the $25M seed with CRV. Reflection came out of stealth on 2025-03-07 with $130M total, including a $105M Series A co-led by CRV and Lightspeed at about $555M ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956)). Sequoia re-upped in the 2025-10 $2B Series B led by NVIDIA ([src](https://reflection.ai/blog/building-frontier-open-intelligencence)). The intro path to Laskin and Antonoglou and Curnin's personal role are not sourced.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sequoia Capital | former investor | unknown | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/) |\n| Stephanie Zhan | co-partner on Reflection AI; co-author | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-reflection-toward-superintelligence-with-autonomous-coding/) |\n| Misha Laskin | founder they backed (Reflection AI CEO) | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-reflection-toward-superintelligence-with-autonomous-coding/) |\n| Ioannis Antonoglou | founder they backed (Reflection AI) | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-reflection-toward-superintelligence-with-autonomous-coding/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Where did they go after Sequoia, and when? Check LinkedIn manually, the Reflection AI team page, and new fund filings (Form D).\n- Did they hold a Reflection board seat or observer role?\n- What other Sequoia deals (Arc or seed) did they work on? The Sequoia company pages list partners and could be checked one by one.\n- Pronouns: sources found don't state them. The SiliconANGLE summary may use \"he\", but that wasn't verified verbatim.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-reflection-toward-superintelligence-with-autonomous-coding/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/charlie-curnin/\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=715519\n- https://reflection.ai/blog/building-frontier-open-intelligencence\n- https://multiples.vc/private-comps/reflection-ai (aggregator)\n", "person:charly-mwangi": "\n# Charly Mwangi\n\n_Update 2026-11-10: rechecked Eclipse's team page, blog index and two 2026 Reichow posts, the Genesis AI site and an aggregator profile. Web search was unavailable again (budget exhausted), so podcasts, X and Genesis follow-on rounds are still untested. Pronouns: CIO Africa uses \"he\" ([src](https://cioafrica.co/charly-mwangi-joins-eclipse-ventures-as-partner))._\n\n## One-line take\nA manufacturing operator (Rivian EVP Manufacturing; earlier Tesla and Nissan) who became an Eclipse partner in June 2022 and was the Eclipse voice on Genesis AI's $105M seed, co-led with Khosla. That is his one sourced frontier-style lab deal ([src](https://cioafrica.co/charly-mwangi-joins-eclipse-ventures-as-partner), [src](https://www.roboticstomorrow.com/news/2025/07/01/genesis-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-105m-to-build-universal-robotics-foundation-model-and-horizontal-platform-for-general-purpose-physical-ai/25025/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he has run factory launches at scale. At Rivian he oversaw the launch of three vehicle models in about two years ([src](https://cioafrica.co/charly-mwangi-joins-eclipse-ventures-as-partner)). That makes him credible to founders who need to go from prototype to production. Inside Eclipse he is the partner whose manufacturing maxims other partners quote ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies)).\n- **How he underwrites AI:** he looks at robotics from the factory floor. His Genesis AI case is that automation stalls on the \"long tail of tasks\", so even highly automated industries rarely beat a 1:30 robot-to-human ratio. A general-purpose robotics model is his answer to that ([src](https://www.roboticstomorrow.com/news/2025/07/01/genesis-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-105m-to-build-universal-robotics-foundation-model-and-horizontal-platform-for-general-purpose-physical-ai/25025/)).\n- **Constraints:** he works inside Eclipse's \"physical industries\" mandate, so pure-software frontier labs are outside the remit unless they are embodied ([firm profile](../firms/eclipse-ventures.md)). He has no sourced public channel (no X, Substack or podcast found), so his views reach us mostly second-hand.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred, low confidence):** back hardware that ignores manufacturability. \"Best part is no part\" is his stated maxim ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies)). This is an inference from one maxim, not a stated rule.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **His shop-floor maxim, relayed by partner Greg Reichow (2026-05-21):** \"Charly Mwangi, likes to say the \\\"best part is no part\\\"\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies)). Reichow credits him, among others, with a five-step order: challenge requirements, delete parts, simplify, speed up, automate last.\n- **A Tesla-era link to Reichow:** \"Here is a five-step approach that I learned from him and others\" (Reichow, describing \"painful days at Tesla\") ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies)). This suggests the two overlapped at Tesla, which helps explain Mwangi's path into Eclipse. It is not confirmed.\n- **His Genesis AI thesis in numbers:** \"the robot-to-human ratio rarely exceeds 1:30\" ([src](https://www.roboticstomorrow.com/news/2025/07/01/genesis-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-105m-to-build-universal-robotics-foundation-model-and-horizontal-platform-for-general-purpose-physical-ai/25025/), 2025-07-01). He frames robotics foundation models as a fix for a manufacturing-labor ceiling, not as an AI bet.\n- **Genesis AI framing:** \"General-purpose robotics is the breakthrough we've been waiting for\" ([src](https://www.roboticstomorrow.com/news/2025/07/01/genesis-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-105m-to-build-universal-robotics-foundation-model-and-horizontal-platform-for-general-purpose-physical-ai/25025/)).\n- **Deal timing:** Genesis AI came out of stealth on 2025-07-01 with the $105M seed already closed. Eclipse and Khosla co-led, with Bpifrance, HSG, Eric Schmidt and Xavier Niel participating ([src](https://www.roboticstomorrow.com/news/2025/07/01/genesis-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-105m-to-build-universal-robotics-foundation-model-and-horizontal-platform-for-general-purpose-physical-ai/25025/)). The release does not say whether Eclipse or Khosla committed first, and no follow-on round was found. **Who was first: not determined.**\n- **Contrast with his co-lead's thesis:** Khosla's Kanu Gulati made a data-flywheel case for Genesis, saying \"collecting and aligning real-world data can be operationally complex\" ([src](https://www.roboticstomorrow.com/news/2025/07/01/genesis-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-105m-to-build-universal-robotics-foundation-model-and-horizontal-platform-for-general-purpose-physical-ai/25025/)). Mwangi made a labor-market case. The two co-leads underwrote the same lab on different theses: Khosla on the model and data, Eclipse on demand from the factory floor.\n- **Low profile inside Eclipse's own channels (checked 2026-11-10):** his team-page entry has a title only, with no bio and no personal links ([src](https://eclipse.capital/team/)). None of the first 12 posts on the Eclipse blog index (May to Oct 2026) is a Genesis AI post or carries his byline ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/)). Reichow's Aug 2026 scaling post does not mention him ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/industrial-scaling-playbook-leadership-at-the-next-level)). The Oct 2026 partner-promotion post (Ryan Gibson, Michael Laser) does not name him either ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- No podcast, Substack, X or talk by Mwangi himself was found. Web search was exhausted on both 2026-11-09 and 2026-11-10, so this is untested, not a confirmed absence.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:** Genesis AI is a new robotics foundation-model lab founded by technical talent from Mistral AI, Nvidia, Google and top universities. It raised a very large seed ($105M) co-led by Eclipse, and that is close to the \"large seed into a new lab\" pattern in THESIS.md ([src](https://www.roboticstomorrow.com/news/2025/07/01/genesis-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-105m-to-build-universal-robotics-foundation-model-and-horizontal-platform-for-general-purpose-physical-ai/25025/)). Eclipse, his firm, is the owner's named type of fund (THESIS.md).\n- **Against:** only one sourced AI deal. His edge is manufacturing operations, not pre-training research or AI chips. He has no public channel to learn from.\n- **Use:** the Eclipse contact for embodied-AI and robotics labs and for scaling hardware. He is a good diligence voice on any robotics-lab co-investment. His co-lead counterpart at Khosla on Genesis was Kanu Gulati.\n\n## Background\n- **Nissan:** prior role. Title and dates not found ([src](https://cioafrica.co/charly-mwangi-joins-eclipse-ventures-as-partner)).\n- **Tesla:** prior role. Title and dates not found ([src](https://cioafrica.co/charly-mwangi-joins-eclipse-ventures-as-partner)). Reichow's post implies a Tesla overlap with Reichow ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies)).\n- **Rivian:** EVP Manufacturing for about two years, overseeing three vehicle launches. Left in early June 2022 ([src](https://cioafrica.co/charly-mwangi-joins-eclipse-ventures-as-partner)).\n- **Eclipse:** Partner, announced June 2022 ([src](https://cioafrica.co/charly-mwangi-joins-eclipse-ventures-as-partner)). TheOrg, an unverified aggregator, gives September 2022 as the start date. Treat that date as low confidence ([aggregator](https://theorg.com/org/eclipse-ventures/org-chart/charly-mwangi)). Still listed as Partner on 2026-11-09 ([src](https://eclipse.capital/team/)).\n- **Education:** not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://eclipse.capital/team/ | Listed as Partner, title only, no bio and no personal X or LinkedIn link (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | No Eclipse blog byline found on the 2026-11-09 blog index |\n| X | nothing found | Not linked from the Eclipse team page |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | Fetch blocked (HTTP 999) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. The web search budget was exhausted on 2026-11-09 and again on 2026-11-10, so this is untested, not a confirmed absence.\n\n## How they think\n- **Delete before you automate:** \"best part is no part\", plus a five-step order that puts automation last ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies)).\n- **The automation ceiling is a long-tail problem:** dexterity, cognition, mobility and real-world reasoning keep robot-to-human ratios near 1:30. He backs general-purpose robotics models as the unlock ([src](https://www.roboticstomorrow.com/news/2025/07/01/genesis-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-105m-to-build-universal-robotics-foundation-model-and-horizontal-platform-for-general-purpose-physical-ai/25025/)).\n- **Operational value-add:** at joining, he said he would help physical-industry founders build more efficient, resilient and profitable companies (paraphrase of [src](https://cioafrica.co/charly-mwangi-joins-eclipse-ventures-as-partner)).\n- **Calls right or wrong:** too early to judge. Genesis AI's site says targeted customer deployments start by end-2026 ([src](https://www.genesis.ai/)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Genesis AI launch release, with his quote ([src](https://www.roboticstomorrow.com/news/2025/07/01/genesis-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-105m-to-build-universal-robotics-foundation-model-and-horizontal-platform-for-general-purpose-physical-ai/25025/)): his robotics-lab thesis.\n2. Greg Reichow, \"The Industrial Scaling Playbook\" (2026-05-21) ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies)): his manufacturing maxims, second-hand.\n3. CIO Africa on his move from Rivian to Eclipse (2022-06) ([src](https://cioafrica.co/charly-mwangi-joins-eclipse-ventures-as-partner)): his own words on leaving Rivian.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public X or Substack identified.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Genesis AI | Seed, $105M | 2025-07-01 | Co-led (Eclipse with Khosla Ventures); Mwangi was the quoted Eclipse partner; board seat not confirmed | Khosla Ventures; Bpifrance; HSG; Eric Schmidt; Xavier Niel | [src](https://www.roboticstomorrow.com/news/2025/07/01/genesis-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-105m-to-build-universal-robotics-foundation-model-and-horizontal-platform-for-general-purpose-physical-ai/25025/) |\n\nNo other deal attributed to him was found. The Eclipse firm profile lists no other Mwangi attributions ([firm profile](../firms/eclipse-ventures.md)).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Genesis AI (2025):** a Paris and Palo Alto robotics foundation-model lab led by CEO Zhou Xian ([src](https://www.roboticstomorrow.com/news/2025/07/01/genesis-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-105m-to-build-universal-robotics-foundation-model-and-horizontal-platform-for-general-purpose-physical-ai/25025/)). Eclipse and Khosla co-led the $105M seed. Mwangi gave the Eclipse quote and Kanu Gulati gave Khosla's. The intro path, which firm moved first, and board composition were not found. The product is Eno, with a waitlist for commercial and home use ([src](https://www.genesis.ai/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Eclipse | Partner | 2022-06 | [src](https://cioafrica.co/charly-mwangi-joins-eclipse-ventures-as-partner) |\n| Rivian | Former EVP Manufacturing (about two years) | ~2020 to 2022-06 | [src](https://cioafrica.co/charly-mwangi-joins-eclipse-ventures-as-partner) |\n| Tesla | Former employee (role not found) | not found | [src](https://cioafrica.co/charly-mwangi-joins-eclipse-ventures-as-partner) |\n| Nissan | Former employee (role not found) | not found | [src](https://cioafrica.co/charly-mwangi-joins-eclipse-ventures-as-partner) |\n| Greg Reichow | Eclipse partner; quotes Mwangi's maxims; likely Tesla overlap (unconfirmed) | not found | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies) |\n| Khosla Ventures / Kanu Gulati | Co-lead counterpart on the Genesis AI seed | 2025-07 | [src](https://www.roboticstomorrow.com/news/2025/07/01/genesis-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-105m-to-build-universal-robotics-foundation-model-and-horizontal-platform-for-general-purpose-physical-ai/25025/) |\n| Zhou Xian | Genesis AI CEO; portfolio founder | 2025-07 | [src](https://www.roboticstomorrow.com/news/2025/07/01/genesis-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-105m-to-build-universal-robotics-foundation-model-and-horizontal-platform-for-general-purpose-physical-ai/25025/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Does he hold the Genesis AI board seat? Who sourced the deal, and did Eclipse or Khosla commit first?\n- Has Genesis AI raised since the seed, and did Eclipse follow on?\n- Tesla and Nissan roles and dates. Did he overlap with Greg Reichow at Tesla?\n- Other deals: is he behind any other Eclipse robotics or manufacturing investments (e.g. Mind Robotics, which the firm profile left unchecked)?\n- Any podcasts, talks (manufacturing conferences) or an X account? The web search budget was exhausted before these could be searched.\n- Does the Genesis AI news page (https://www.genesis.ai/news) list a post-seed round? It did not render on 2026-11-10.\n- Did the Mistral AI alumni at Genesis come through Eclipse's network or Khosla's?\n- Is fellow partner Jiten Behl also ex-Rivian, which would make a Rivian-alumni channel into Eclipse? Unsourced.\n\n## Sources\n- https://cioafrica.co/charly-mwangi-joins-eclipse-ventures-as-partner\n- https://www.roboticstomorrow.com/news/2025/07/01/genesis-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-105m-to-build-universal-robotics-foundation-model-and-horizontal-platform-for-general-purpose-physical-ai/25025/\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies\n- https://eclipse.capital/team/\n- https://www.genesis.ai/\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/ (index, checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/industrial-scaling-playbook-leadership-at-the-next-level\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale\n- https://theorg.com/org/eclipse-ventures/org-chart/charly-mwangi (aggregator)\n", "person:chenli-wang": "\n# ChenLi Wang\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (owner pick). Sources: wndr.vc team data (site JS bundle), the archived 2024 wndrco.com partner page, WndrCo's Meter post, SEC EDGAR (every filing that names him: 10 full-text hits), the daydream Series A release, and press. Pronouns: the WndrCo bio uses \"he\". No podcast, Substack or X presence found, so differential insight comes from filings and the firm's own pages.*\n\n## One-line take\nWndrCo's day-one product operator who now co-runs its seed book. The filings show board seats the bios leave out (Netomi in 2021, daydream in 2025), and the firm has quietly rewritten his deal list toward AI-era names. Still, his personal book is security, networking and SaaS, not labs or chips.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins: he buys what he uses, then operates inside it.** He doubled as WndrCo's \"in-house director of IT\" and switched the firm's offices onto Meter soon after meeting the founders in 2021 ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/our-investment-in-meter)). He was also CPO at Aura and Point Wild, WndrCo's control deals ([src](https://wndr.vc/team)). The firm calls this plugging in as \"interim CEOs, COOs, or CPOs\" ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/the-founders-behind-the-founders)). Dropbox gives him the pattern: he was the 2nd business hire at about 30 people and built its freemium, product-led growth engine ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/2024id_/https://www.wndrco.com/partner/chenli-wang)).\n- **What the bio's \"led\" means:** wndr.vc says he \"Led investments in\" eight companies ([src](https://wndr.vc/team)). That means he sponsored them inside WndrCo, not that WndrCo led the round. Meter's $170M Series C is a WndrCo participation \"alongside General Catalyst, Microsoft\" ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/our-investment-in-meter)). The two rounds the record shows WndrCo leading on his watch are Netomi B (2021) and daydream A (2025-26).\n- **Constraint:** the seed vehicle is small. Seed III filed for $65M ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2058875/000205984325000002/primary_doc.xml)), and in 2024 WndrCo said it does about 15 seed deals a year at an average $500K ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/04/jeffrey-katzenberg-wndrco-450m-fund-venture-capital)). His bigger checks come through the Holdings vehicles: he is a named related person on Holdings II ($350.8M sold) and Holdings III ($500M target). That is why check_capacity is 10m-plus, not because of the seed fund.\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** lead a large seed into a pre-training lab. Nothing in the record points that way. His sectors are security (Material, Socket, Defakto, Aura), networking (Meter), SMB and product SaaS (CompanyCam, Webflow), and applied AI (Exa, Netomi, daydream, Partly).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **His deal list was rewritten between 2024 and 2026.** The 2024 bio said he led WndrCo's investments in \"Aura, Pango, Webflow, Modern Treasury, Pilot, Material Security, Meter, Socket\" ([2024](https://web.archive.org/web/2024id_/https://www.wndrco.com/partner/chenli-wang)). The 2026 bio lists \"CompanyCam, Exa, Material Security, Meter, Socket, Defakto, Webflow, Partly\" ([wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team)). Aura, Pango, Modern Treasury and Pilot dropped off; Exa, Defakto, CompanyCam and Partly came in. In 2024 he was also \"a general partner of WndrCo Growth,\" a title that no longer appears. My read: the firm is repositioning him from consumer security and fintech toward AI-era seed.\n- **Board seats that aren't in any bio, from Form D:**\n  - **Netomi** (AI customer service). Wang is listed as a director on the Form D filed 2021-09-21: first sale 2021-09-02, $27.0M sold of $30M, 6 investors ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1701548/000170154821000001/primary_doc.xml)). This matches the $30M Series B led by WndrCo, announced in November 2021 ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netomi)). WndrCo's firm page credits Netomi to Justin Wexler, who spoke for WndrCo at the 2026 Series C ([src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260429618643/en/)). So Wang appears to have held the board seat from the 2021 lead and Wexler took over the relationship later (inference).\n  - **daydream Labs** (AI-native SEO agency). Wang is listed as a director on the Form D filed 2025-07-22: first sale 2025-07-10, $16.6M sold of a $22.0M offering, 3 investors ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2023805/000123191925000126/primary_doc.xml)). The company announced a WndrCo-led \"$15M Series A\" on 2026-04-02 ([release](https://www.prnewswire.com/il/news-releases/daydream-raises-15m-series-a-to-build-the-worlds-best-ai-native-agency-for-seo-302732323.html)). If that is the same round (my inference: the release says $21M total raised, close to the $22M offering), WndrCo was in about **nine months before the public announcement**. Wang's quote in the release: \"AI is changing it faster than most companies can keep up.\"\n- **Filing titles vs. site titles.** He is \"Manager of the General Partner\" on Holdings II (2022) and \"Principal of the General Partner\" on Holdings III (2025) ([II](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924350/000192435022000001/primary_doc.xml), [III](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2075685/000123191925000103/primary_doc.xml)). He is **not** on the Seed III Form D, where only Jaswa appears ([Seed III](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2058875/000205984325000002/primary_doc.xml)). That is odd for the Co-Head of Seed. It suggests Jaswa and Katzenberg control the management company and the \"GP\" title is a working title, not an economic one (inference).\n- **He was in the Aura origin deal.** In 2018 he was a director of the WndrCo merger vehicles that took Intersections (Identity Guard) private, described as \"General Partner of WndrCo, LLC\" ([SC 13D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1095277/000119312518325662/d635534dsc13d.htm)). He then sat on the board of WC SACD Holdings with Hari Ravichandran in 2019 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1767658/000176765819000002/primary_doc.xml)). Aura's 2026 S-1/A doesn't name him: Aura's CPO since March 2026 is someone else, so his CPO stint ended before then.\n- **Name collision.** The repo already tags \"Daydream\" (slug `daydream`) as Julie Bornstein's AI fashion search. Wang's company is daydream Labs (Karunaratne and Rajinikanth), so I used slug `daydream-labs`.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is a seed partner at a firm that can also write growth checks, and he is useful for WndrCo's GTM, celebrity and Fortune-500 distribution. His own book is not frontier AI:\n- **Exa** is the only lens-relevant name (web search for AI agents, owner category ai-infra-compute). WndrCo's entry round is unconfirmed: wndr.vc files Exa under the Seed strategy ([src](https://wndr.vc/about)). Only an aggregator tear sheet puts WndrCo in the $85M Series B (2025-09). That round's press names Benchmark as lead with Lightspeed, YC and NVentures, and does not name WndrCo ([Built In](https://builtin.com/articles/exa-raises-85m-series-b-20250908)).\n- **Netomi** (ai-automation) and **daydream** (AI-native services) sit closest to the owner's interest in AI-native automation.\n- No pre-training labs, chips or compute. No LP activity found.\n\nHow useful he is: a co-investor and possible syndicate partner on AI-app and security seeds. Not a lead for $100M+ lab seeds. For a junior partner who might be a better contact, see Chonghao Huang (VP to Partner, Seed by 2026) in `firms/wndrco.md`.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford, dual BS/BA in Computer Science and Economics, with distinction ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/2024id_/https://www.wndrco.com/partner/chenli-wang))\n- Goldman Sachs investment banking, then New Enterprise Associates (venture), then Workday corporate strategy (same source; dates not given)\n- Dropbox: second business hire at about 30 employees; six years. Created and led business operations, growth, monetization, analytics and international, then led the core Dropbox App product org (same source; [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team))\n- WndrCo: day-one partner (2016) ([wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team)); \"General Partner of WndrCo, LLC\" by 2018-11 ([13D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1095277/000119312518325662/d635534dsc13d.htm)); CPO at Aura and Point Wild (dates not disclosed); also WndrCo's in-house IT director ([Meter post](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/our-investment-in-meter))\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://wndr.vc/team | Bio in the site's JS data; checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/chenliw/ | Linked from wndr.vc |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. WndrCo's podcast appearances (20VC, Speed of Culture, A Good Company) feature Katzenberg, Jaswa and Wexler, not Wang.\n\n## How they think\n- Product-led growth and freemium (the Dropbox playbook). His sectors are tools that spread bottom-up or sell by subscription: Webflow, CompanyCam, Meter (networking \"as simple as a utility\") ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/our-investment-in-meter)).\n- Security as a recurring theme: Material Security, Socket, Defakto, plus the Aura and Point Wild operating roles.\n- On AI he sees distribution shifts more than model bets. On daydream he said \"the SEO market is massive\" and that AI is changing it ([release](https://www.prnewswire.com/il/news-releases/daydream-raises-15m-series-a-to-build-the-worlds-best-ai-native-agency-for-seo-302732323.html)).\n- Calls right or wrong: Meter (WndrCo since 2021; ARR nearly quadrupled in the year before the $170M C, per WndrCo). Exa (Series B at $700M in 2025, up about 10x on its 2024 A per Bloomberg).\n\n## Read these first\n1. The 2024 wndrco.com partner page: the full career and the older deal list ([link](https://web.archive.org/web/2024id_/https://www.wndrco.com/partner/chenli-wang))\n2. \"The Last Networking Company: Our Investment in Meter\": shows how he sources by using the product ([link](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/our-investment-in-meter))\n3. The daydream Series A release: his only public quote on AI ([link](https://www.prnewswire.com/il/news-releases/daydream-raises-15m-series-a-to-build-the-worlds-best-ai-native-agency-for-seo-302732323.html))\n4. The Holdings III Form D: his formal role in the GP ([link](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2075685/000123191925000103/primary_doc.xml))\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public X or Substack presence that I could identify.\n\n## Portfolio\n\"Led?\" means WndrCo led the round, not that Wang sponsored it internally.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Exa (ai-infra-compute) | Undisclosed (WNDR seed strategy) | — | unknown | — | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Exa | Series B ($85M at $700M); WndrCo per aggregator only | 2025-09-03 | no | Benchmark (lead), Lightspeed, YC, NVentures | [tear sheet](https://siliconvalleyinvestclub.com/companies/exa/tear-sheet.pdf), [Built In](https://builtin.com/articles/exa-raises-85m-series-b-20250908) |\n| Netomi (ai-automation) | Series B ($30M); Wang director on Form D | 2021-09 (first sale) / 2021-11 (announced) | yes | Eldridge, Fin Capital, Indicator (aggregator) | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1701548/000170154821000001/primary_doc.xml), [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netomi) |\n| daydream Labs (ai-apps) | Series A ($15M announced; $22M Form D offering); Wang director | 2025-07-10 (first sale) / 2026-04-02 (announced) | yes | First Round, Basis Set | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2023805/000123191925000126/primary_doc.xml), [release](https://www.prnewswire.com/il/news-releases/daydream-raises-15m-series-a-to-build-the-worlds-best-ai-native-agency-for-seo-302732323.html) |\n| Partly (ai-apps) | Undisclosed | — | unknown | Octopus (A lead 2022-12), Blackbird, AirTree | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Meter | Undisclosed (first check) | 2021 | unknown | — | [Meter post](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/our-investment-in-meter) |\n| Meter | Series C ($170M) | 2025-06-12 | no | General Catalyst, Microsoft | same |\n| Socket | Undisclosed (WNDR seed strategy) | — | unknown | — | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Material Security | Undisclosed | — | unknown | — | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Defakto (fka SPIRL) | Undisclosed; WndrCo also in the $30.75M Series B (XYZ lead) | 2025-10-21 | no | XYZ Venture Capital, The General Partnership, Bloomberg Beta | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team), [RegTech Analyst](https://regtechanalyst.com/?p=78949) |\n| CompanyCam | Undisclosed | — | unknown | — | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Webflow | Undisclosed | — | unknown | — | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Aura / Pango (Point Wild), Modern Treasury, Pilot | Undisclosed (2024 bio list) | — | — | — | [2024 bio](https://web.archive.org/web/2024id_/https://www.wndrco.com/partner/chenli-wang) |\n| Intersections (Identity Guard) take-private (Aura origin) | Strategic (tender/merger) | 2018-11 | yes (WndrCo vehicle) | — | [13D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1095277/000119312518325662/d635534dsc13d.htm) |\n\nLeft out: PlayerZero. An aggregator (pipeseed) attributes it to Wang, but Fenwick's deal page names Green Bay Ventures (seed lead) and Foundation Capital (A lead), with no WndrCo ([Fenwick](https://www.fenwick.com/insights/experience/fenwick-represents-playerzero-in-20m-financing)).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Meter:** WndrCo met founders Sunil and Anil Varanasi in 2021. Soon after, Wang, as WndrCo's IT director, moved the offices onto Meter. WndrCo then held the position through the $170M Series C in June 2025, with both its offices on Meter ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/our-investment-in-meter)).\n- **daydream:** a WndrCo-led round. The Form D shows money in from 2025-07-10 with Wang on the board. The public \"$15M Series A\" came on 2026-04-02 with First Round and Basis Set. The founder reportedly raised after a YC rejection (headline only, not verified).\n- **Netomi:** WndrCo led the $30M B. Money was first in on 2021-09-02, and the round was announced 2021-11. Wang was on the board per the Form D. WndrCo followed in the 2026 $110M Series C led by Accenture Ventures, by then represented by Wexler.\n- **Aura:** in 2018 WndrCo took Intersections (Identity Guard) private through WC SACD vehicles with Wang as director. It merged with Hari Ravichandran's iSubscribed and became Aura; Wang later served as Aura CPO.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| WndrCo | Day-one GP; Co-Head of Seed; related person on Holdings II and III | 2016 | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2075685/000123191925000103/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Sujay Jaswa | Dropbox colleague, then WndrCo co-founder; co-director on the Aura vehicles | Dropbox era / 2016 | [13D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1095277/000119312518325662/d635534dsc13d.htm) |\n| Anthony Saleh | Co-Head of Seed | 2016 | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Hari Ravichandran | Aura CEO; co-director at WC SACD Holdings 2019; WndrCo venture partner | 2018 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1767658/000176765819000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Puneet Mehta (Netomi) | Board (director on Form D) | 2021 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1701548/000170154821000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Thenuka Karunaratne, Shravan Rajinikanth (daydream) | Board (director on Form D) | 2025 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2023805/000123191925000126/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Sunil and Anil Varanasi (Meter) | Investor and customer | 2021 | [Meter post](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/our-investment-in-meter) |\n| Dropbox | 2nd business hire; 6 years | pre-2016 | [2024 bio](https://web.archive.org/web/2024id_/https://www.wndrco.com/partner/chenli-wang) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What round did WndrCo first enter Exa in, and at what price? Only wndr.vc's \"Seed\" tag and an aggregator B listing exist. Exa's Form Ds are worth checking (EDGAR company search \"Exa Labs\").\n- Does he hold the daydream and Netomi seats today? Form Ds are point-in-time.\n- When did his CPO stints at Aura and Point Wild run? Aura has a new CPO as of March 2026.\n- Why isn't he on the Seed III Form D? Is the seed fund run by the management company with Jaswa as the only signatory?\n- First-check dates for Socket, Material Security, Defakto, CompanyCam, Webflow and Partly.\n\n## Sources\n- https://wndr.vc/team (bundle /assets/index-CuMFcD--.js, read 2026-11-10); https://wndr.vc/about\n- https://web.archive.org/web/2024id_/https://www.wndrco.com/partner/chenli-wang\n- https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/our-investment-in-meter\n- https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/the-founders-behind-the-founders\n- SEC: Holdings II D (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924350/000192435022000001/primary_doc.xml), Holdings III D (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2075685/000123191925000103/primary_doc.xml), Seed III D (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2058875/000205984325000002/primary_doc.xml), Netomi D (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1701548/000170154821000001/primary_doc.xml), daydream Labs D (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2023805/000123191925000126/primary_doc.xml), WC SACD Holdings D (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1767658/000176765819000002/primary_doc.xml), Intersections SC 13D (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1095277/000119312518325662/d635534dsc13d.htm), Aura S-1/A (checked: does not name him)\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/il/news-releases/daydream-raises-15m-series-a-to-build-the-worlds-best-ai-native-agency-for-seo-302732323.html\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netomi; https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260429618643/en/\n- https://builtin.com/articles/exa-raises-85m-series-b-20250908; https://siliconvalleyinvestclub.com/companies/exa/tear-sheet.pdf (aggregator)\n- https://regtechanalyst.com/?p=78949 (Defakto B); https://www.fenwick.com/insights/experience/fenwick-represents-playerzero-in-20m-financing\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/04/jeffrey-katzenberg-wndrco-450m-fund-venture-capital\n", "person:chetan-puttagunta": "\n# Chetan Puttagunta\n\nSources (Wikipedia; Eric Vishria speaking on Venture Stories) refer to him as \"he\", so this profile does too.\n\n## One-line take\nHe is Benchmark's AI applications and AI infrastructure lead (Manus, Legora, LangChain, Reducto, Starcloud). He moves from first look to handshake within days, before launch, and his open-source infrastructure record (MongoDB, MuleSoft, Elastic at NEA) explains how he picks enabling layers ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/benchmarks-chetan-puttagunta-on-the), [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E-JY9rfYJj4)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He spots product breakouts as a user (he signed up for Manus's beta hours after the demo) and then compresses diligence into a week, using Benchmark's equal partnership to rally the other partners behind one partner's conviction ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/benchmarks-chetan-puttagunta-on-the)).\n- **Board-first.** Every deal is meant to make him the company's primary board member, and he says valuation is not what governs decisions ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/benchmarks-chetan-puttagunta-on-the)).\n- **Constraint.** Each partner has capacity for about two deals a year ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E-JY9rfYJj4)).\n- **What he would never do.** Write a passive cheque without a board seat.\n- **How he sees risk.** He treats missing a winner, not backing a loser, as the real error ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/benchmarks-chetan-puttagunta-on-the)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Manus, a week from demo to handshake (March 2025).** He saw the demo on X, used the beta, texted his partners and did a Monday Zoom. He flew to Tokyo that Friday, the team pitched the partnership on Saturday, and he closed with CEO \"Red\": \"Red and I went for pizza and beer and got to a handshake.\" ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/benchmarks-chetan-puttagunta-on-the), 1:18-5:31)\n- **Legora, pre-demo-day.** He and Peter Fenton met Max Junestrand in February 2024, when the company had five people: \"It was still in Y Combinator and it hadn't gone through demo day yet.\" They invested in March 2024, three months before the announced seed ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E-JY9rfYJj4), 11:41).\n- **The 2022 AI fund avoided labs on purpose.** It bet on apps and app enablement (Sierra, Legora, Fireworks, Levelpath, LangChain): \"We invested in one open source one.\" The lab is unnamed ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/benchmarks-chetan-puttagunta-on-the), 39:16).\n- **Fund mechanics in his words.** Early 2026: \"We're a 500 million dollar fund with four equal partners.\" ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/benchmarks-chetan-puttagunta-on-the)) By mid-2026 he describes about $600M with five partners: \"each of us makes about two investments a year\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E-JY9rfYJj4)). There are no written ownership rules.\n- **New GPs buy in.** Benchmark partners \"write a check to buy your ownership interest\" on day one ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bmJl9FpZHCQ), 8:21, 2019).\n- **Mistakes are omissions.** \"mistake as an early stage investor is missing out on the companies that work\" ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/benchmarks-chetan-puttagunta-on-the)).\n- **Open door to newcomers.** He asks \"anyone entering especially the VC ecosystem reach out\", because he sees newcomers' networks as a sourcing channel. This is useful access for the owner ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MAsxKEkRebs), 38:24, 2019).\n- **Has known Jack Altman for about a decade.** He met Altman \"for the seed round and the series A round\" of Lattice, about ten years before Altman joined Benchmark ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E-JY9rfYJj4), 57:30).\n- **NEA-era entry point.** Elastic and MongoDB both had only \"single-digit Millions\" of run-rate revenue when he invested ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MAsxKEkRebs), 7:07).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He leads seed and Series A rounds in AI applications and AI infrastructure (Legora seed, Reducto A, LangChain, Manus, Starcloud's $170M A). He is a managing member of Benchmark's AI Infrastructure Fund, which put $225M into Cerebras's Series H ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm)). His current thesis, unlocking inference capacity \"wherever it's possible\", overlaps AI compute ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E-JY9rfYJj4)). Benchmark avoided labs in 2022, so he is a co-investor and board-seat partner for AI-native automation more than for new labs.\n\n## Background\n- Born 1986 in Hyderabad, India, and grew up in Maryland. Stanford BS in Electrical Engineering, 2007 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chetan_Puttagunta)).\n- Tried to build apps himself and calls himself a \"failed developer\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MAsxKEkRebs)).\n- Worked in tech M&A at Houlihan Lokey, then at H.I.G. Capital in San Francisco ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chetan_Puttagunta)).\n- 2011: joined NEA. October 2016: promoted to General Partner ([src](https://www.wsj.com/articles/nea-promotes-makhzoumi-and-puttagunta-to-general-partner-1477606259)). At NEA he sat on the boards of MongoDB (his first investment, per ACQ2), MuleSoft and Elastic ([src](https://www.acquired.fm/acq2-episodes/lp-show-preview-chetan-puttagunta-general-partner-at-benchmark)).\n- July 2018: joined Benchmark as General Partner ([src](https://abovethecrowd.com/2018/07/09/benchmarks-newest-general-partner-chetan-puttagunta/)).\n- Forbes 30 Under 30 (2015) and Midas List ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chetan_Puttagunta)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.benchmark.com | No personal page found |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/chetanp | Active in waves; ramped up 2018-2020 per The Peel ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/benchmarks-chetan-puttagunta-on-the)); profile not fetchable (HTTP 402) |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Says he rarely does events ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mWT26UTds9k)) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nThe full list (29 items) is in `data/rows/chetan-puttagunta-voice-media.csv`.\n- 2019-08: 20VC. Market size, talent, over-rotation to metrics. [link](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/chetanputtagunta)\n- 2019-09: Business Logic (Andy Qin). Failed developer, mentors (Harry Weller, Peter Fenton), back-channel references. [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MAsxKEkRebs)\n- 2019-11: ACQ2 LP Show. The story of his first investment, MongoDB (transcript not reached). [link](https://www.acquired.fm/acq2-episodes/lp-show-preview-chetan-puttagunta-general-partner-at-benchmark)\n- 2019-12: Venture Stories, with Eric Vishria. Open-source monetisation and the partner buy-in. [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bmJl9FpZHCQ)\n- 2020-01, 2020-03, 2020-08: Invest Like the Best EP.156, EP.162 (with Bill Gurley) and EP.188 (with Jeremiah Lowin). Transcripts are gated. [link](https://colossus.com/episode/puttagunta-go-slow-to-go-fast-software-building-and-investing/)\n- 2023-09: Startup Grind, with Caryn Marooney. Professional services as dogma; MindsDB in Q4 2022. [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mWT26UTds9k)\n- 2024-12: Invest Like the Best EP.400, with Modest Proposal. Scaling plateau and test-time compute (transcript gated). [link](https://colossus.com/episode/chetan-puttagunta-and-modest-proposal-capital-compute-ai-scaling/)\n- 2025-01: CNBC on DeepSeek. \"maybe a half generation behind\". [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FJvSFTMNTu4)\n- 2026-02: Uncapped #41, the Benchmark partnership. [link](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton)\n- 2026-03: The Peel. The Manus story, the fund model and AI versus legacy SaaS. [link](https://www.thespl.it/p/benchmarks-chetan-puttagunta-on-the)\n- 2026-06: GTMnow. The Legora origin, the Starcloud thesis and Jack Altman. [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E-JY9rfYJj4)\n\n## How they think\n- **Enabling layers for builders.** Developer tools, open source and now AI app enablement such as LangChain and Fireworks ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bmJl9FpZHCQ)).\n- **Not thesis-driven.** \"we're not thesis driven because we want to be surprised\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bmJl9FpZHCQ)).\n- **Contrarian on metrics.** \"there's been a huge over rotation towards being metrics driven\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MAsxKEkRebs)).\n- **Contrarian on services.** \"being scared of professional services is this weird dogma\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mWT26UTds9k)).\n- **AI-native apps beat SaaS incumbents.** He argues incumbents would have to re-architect, reprice and redistribute ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/benchmarks-chetan-puttagunta-on-the)).\n- **Calls he got right.** Open-source infrastructure IPOs (2017-2019), the AI app layer in 2022, and Manus (acquired by Meta).\n- **Calls that aged worse.** He championed Sketch against Figma in 2019 ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MAsxKEkRebs)).\n\n## Read these first\n- The Peel (2026-03): the Manus story and Benchmark's fund economics in his own words ([link](https://www.thespl.it/p/benchmarks-chetan-puttagunta-on-the)).\n- GTMnow (2026-06): the Legora timeline and how he builds conviction fast ([link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E-JY9rfYJj4)).\n- Venture Stories (2019-12): the open-source thesis and the partnership structure ([link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bmJl9FpZHCQ)).\n- Business Logic (2019-09): personal origin and board style ([link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MAsxKEkRebs)).\n\n## Social engagement\n- Posts on X as @chetanp in waves. He says he was most active during the 2018-2021 software IPO and pandemic period ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/benchmarks-chetan-puttagunta-on-the)).\n- Not fetched this run (X returned HTTP 402).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| MongoDB | early (NEA; his first investment) | pre-2017 | board | | [src](https://www.acquired.fm/acq2-episodes/lp-show-preview-chetan-puttagunta-general-partner-at-benchmark) |\n| MuleSoft | NEA | pre-2017 | board | | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chetan_Puttagunta) |\n| Elastic | NEA | 2014 | board (now chair) | Benchmark (Fenton) | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bmJl9FpZHCQ) |\n| Sketch | Benchmark | 2018-2019 | board | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MAsxKEkRebs) |\n| Airbyte | Series A ($26M) | 2021-05-25 | yes | | [src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20210525005226/en) |\n| Stytch | Series A ($30M) | 2021-07-14 | no (Thrive led) | Thrive; Coatue; Index | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2177105) |\n| MindsDB | Undisclosed | 2022-Q4 | | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mWT26UTds9k) |\n| Legora (Leya) | Seed ($10.5M) | invested 2024-03; announced 2024-05-16 | yes | | [src](https://www.artificiallawyer.com/2024/05/16/leya-ai-assistant-bags-10-5m-seed-funding-for-qa-review-drafting/) |\n| Manus | Series B (~$75M) | 2025-04 | yes | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/05/09/the-us-is-reviewing-benchmarks-investment-into-chinese-ai-startup-manus/) |\n| Reducto | Series A ($24.5M) | 2025-04-25 | yes | | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/04/25/exclusive-reducto-ai-document-parsing-startup-raises-24-5-million-series-a-led-by-benchmark/) |\n| fomo | Series A ($17M) | 2025-11 | yes | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/06/why-benchmark-made-a-rare-crypto-bet-on-trading-app-fomo-with-17m-series-a/) |\n| Starcloud | Series A ($170M at $1.1B) | 2026-03-30 | co-led | EQT Ventures | [src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260330024111/en/) |\n| LangChain | board (inherited) | | | | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chetan_Puttagunta) |\n| Modern Treasury, Duffel | board | | | | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chetan_Puttagunta) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Manus.** See Differential insight. He says the company reached $100M ARR in eight months and that the team built only on US models and clouds, which was his answer to criticism on X ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/benchmarks-chetan-puttagunta-on-the)). The US Treasury reviewed the deal ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/05/09/the-us-is-reviewing-benchmarks-investment-into-chinese-ai-startup-manus/)), and Meta later acquired Manus.\n- **Legora.** Three computer scientists with no legal background embedded themselves in a law firm for about a year. The prototype was ready in March 2024 and the product went GA in October 2024. Their pilots ran 30/60/90 days, and customers kept whatever integrations they built ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E-JY9rfYJj4)).\n- **Elastic.** Fenton led the 2012 Series A, and Puttagunta came in in 2014 at NEA. The shared board led to Benchmark recruiting him ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bmJl9FpZHCQ)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Peter Fenton | Elastic co-board member; Benchmark partner; \"biggest influence\" | 2014 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MAsxKEkRebs) |\n| Eric Vishria | Benchmark partner; co-guest on Venture Stories | 2018 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bmJl9FpZHCQ) |\n| Jack Altman | Met during Lattice's seed and Series A; Benchmark partner from 2026 | about 2016 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E-JY9rfYJj4) |\n| Caryn Marooney (Coatue) | Co-board member at Elastic and Airbyte | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mWT26UTds9k) |\n| Harry Weller (NEA, deceased) | Early mentor | 2011 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MAsxKEkRebs) |\n| Max Junestrand (Legora) | Board member | 2024-03 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E-JY9rfYJj4) |\n| Bill Gurley | Recruited him to Benchmark; co-guest on ILTB EP.162 | 2018 | [src](https://abovethecrowd.com/2018/07/09/benchmarks-newest-general-partner-chetan-puttagunta/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which open-weights lab did Benchmark back in 2022? He doesn't name it.\n- He says \"we're investors in Cursor\". Which round, and through which partner?\n- The ACQ2 MongoDB origin story and ILTB EP.156, EP.162 and EP.400 have gated or unreached transcripts. Mine them next.\n- Two mentors' names are garbled in the auto-captions. Probably Jon Sakoda and Ravi Viswanathan (NEA); needs confirmation.\n- What he said on Disrupt 2026, RAISE Summit 2026 and fomo's \"in the trenches\" Ep 100. Captions were rate-limited this run.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.thespl.it/p/benchmarks-chetan-puttagunta-on-the\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=diCadvZ7qUE\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E-JY9rfYJj4\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bmJl9FpZHCQ\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MAsxKEkRebs\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mWT26UTds9k\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FJvSFTMNTu4\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chetan_Puttagunta\n- https://www.acquired.fm/acq2-episodes/lp-show-preview-chetan-puttagunta-general-partner-at-benchmark\n- https://colossus.com/episode/puttagunta-go-slow-to-go-fast-software-building-and-investing/\n- https://abovethecrowd.com/2018/07/09/benchmarks-newest-general-partner-chetan-puttagunta/\n- https://www.wsj.com/articles/nea-promotes-makhzoumi-and-puttagunta-to-general-partner-1477606259\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm\n", "person:chonghao-huang": "\n# Chonghao Huang\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (owner pick). Public footprint is very thin: no X, Substack, podcast or firm bio found; WndrCo's site never carried a bio for them. Facts below come from archived WndrCo team pages (Wayback), the archived wndr.vc JS bundle, the Law.com and Artificial Lawyer reports on Truth Systems, SEC Form D, and aggregator snippets (Crunchbase, RocketReach, Equilar, LinkedIn search snippet), which are labelled as such.*\n\n## One-line take\nA recently promoted seed partner at WndrCo/WNDR: hired in as a VP (no \"Investor\" stint), made Partner, Seed between 2026-04-30 and 2026-06-10, the same window as the WNDR rebrand. Only one deal is tied to them, and only by an aggregator. Worth a relationship for co-investing in AI-app seeds, not a source of frontier-lab leads.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they could win:** they sit on a four-person seed team (ChenLi Wang and Anthony Saleh co-head it; Mel Demma is the other Partner, Seed) at a firm that also writes growth checks and sells Katzenberg access and Fortune 500 GTM to its companies ([team](https://wndr.vc/team), `firms/wndrco.md`). A seed partner there can show founders a path into WndrCo's later-stage checks.\n- **Constraint:** the seed vehicle is small. WndrCo Seed III LP filed for a $65M target ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2058875/000205984325000002/primary_doc.xml)), and the firm says it makes about 25 seed bets a year ([src](https://wndr.vc/about)). That points to sub-$3M first checks (inference). They cannot anchor a $10M+ seed on their own.\n- **What they'd likely never do (inference):** lead a $100M+ seed into a new lab. Nothing in WndrCo's seed record looks like that (see `firms/wndrco.md`).\n- **Unknown:** their own thesis. They have no public writing or talks to read.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **They came in laterally as a VP, not up from the \"Investor\" bench.** The 2024-11-13 team page lists every investor (Larry He, Sharon Pan, Amy Li, Ben Rathi and others) and not them. By 2025-03-24 the page reads \"Chonghao Huang Vice President\" ([2025-03-24](https://web.archive.org/web/20250324173935id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team), [2024-11-13](https://web.archive.org/web/20241113194728id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team)). So they joined between those dates. That is earlier than the 2025-07 first sighting noted in `firms/wndrco.md`.\n- **The promotion is dated to a six-week window, alongside the rebrand.** The wndrco.com team page still had them as VP on 2026-04-30 ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260430110940id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team)). The archived wndr.vc bundle of 2026-06-30 has `name:\"Chonghao Huang\",role:\"Partner, Seed\"` ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260630200655id_/https://www.wndr.vc/assets/index-CuMFcD--.js)). The 2026-06-10 wndr.vc/team snapshot loads the same content-hashed bundle ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260610052536id_/https://wndr.vc/team)). Because the bundle file name is a content hash, the title was live by 2026-06-10: the window is about six weeks (2026-04-30 to 2026-06-10). They went from VP to Partner about 15 months after first appearing (2025-03 to 2026-06).\n- **The only deal tied to them is a Gradient-led AI legal seed.** Truth Systems' $4M seed (2025-10-28) was led by Gradient, with \"Pear VC, a16z Scout, Silver Circle, and Wndr Co\" ([Artificial Lawyer](https://www.artificiallawyer.com/2025/10/28/truth-systems-bags-4m-to-enforce-ai-guardrails/); also [Law.com](https://www.law.com/legaltechnews/2025/10/28/governance-and-compliance-startup-truth-systems-announces-4m-seed-round-/)). Crunchbase lists Huang as the partner on WndrCo's Truthsystems seed (aggregator, search snippet; page not retrievable, 403) ([src](https://www.crunchbase.com/person/chonghao-huang)). WndrCo's participation is confirmed by press; their personal role is aggregator-only.\n- **Aggregators link them to three AI companies, two of them WndrCo portfolio.** RocketReach says they have \"previous roles at XDOF, Creatify AI, WNDR and CentML\" (search snippet, profile 403) ([src](https://rocketreach.co/chonghao-huang-email_717207027)). xDOF (WNDR seed + Series A) and Creatify (WndrCo co-led the $15.5M Series A) are WndrCo portfolio, so these are most likely deal or board-observer associations scraped from LinkedIn, not employment. CentML (AI inference optimization, Gradient-led seed 2023, bought by Nvidia 2025) has no public WndrCo link; it may be a pre-WndrCo job or an angel deal. Not confirmed.\n- **Education:** UC Berkeley per a LinkedIn search snippet ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/chonghaohuang/)). RocketReach adds a BS in Computer Science (aggregator). xDOF grew out of UC Berkeley's GELLO teleoperation project ([SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/2026/06/17/robotic-teleoperation-data-startup-xdof-launches-70m-funding/)), so a Berkeley network route into xDOF is plausible. That is a hypothesis, not a sourced intro path.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** They are exactly the owner's \"recently promoted partner at a fund that also writes growth checks\" segment, with a dated promotion. But the seed book is sub-$10M, they have no attributable frontier-lab, chip or compute deal, and the only deal tied to them (Truth Systems) is an AI-app seed led by someone else. Deals that would matter to the owner, if their involvement is confirmed: **xDOF** (robot training data; WNDR seed, then the $70M Series A in 2026-06, reportedly in Series B talks at ~$1.2B), **CentML** (ML compute optimization) and **Creatify** (AI ads). Useful as a co-investor or deal-flow peer on AI-native automation seeds and as a bridge into WndrCo's growth checks. Not an LP prospect.\n\n## Background\n- Education: University of California, Berkeley (LinkedIn search snippet); BS Computer Science per RocketReach (aggregator). Graduation year not found.\n- Before WndrCo: not confirmed. RocketReach lists CentML among \"previous roles\" (aggregator; see above).\n- WndrCo: joined as Vice President between 2024-11-13 and 2025-03-24 (archived team pages above). Listed as VP on every wndrco.com snapshot through 2026-04-30.\n- WNDR: Partner, Seed by 2026-06-10 (archived wndr.vc page and bundle, see above). Equilar shows \"Partner, Seed\" with a 06/15/2026 as-of date ([src](https://people.equilar.com/bio/person/chonghao-huang-wndrco/56168953)). That may be a data date, so it isn't used as the promotion date.\n- `fast_promotion: unknown`: their WndrCo tenure from VP to Partner was about 15 months, but their career start date and any pre-VP investing history aren't known, so the \"associate to partner in about 4 years\" test can't be applied. Note: `firms/wndrco.md` marks them `yes`; this profile does not, for that reason.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://wndr.vc/team | Name and title only, no bio (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Searched 2026-11-10 |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/chonghaohuang/ | Linked from wndr.vc; blocked to fetch |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found (searched web, X, Substack, Medium; 2026-11-10).\n\n## How they think\nNothing on record in their own words. Their one traced deal is an AI governance and compliance tool for law firms (Truth Systems), in a round alongside Gradient, Lightspeed, YC and Pear. The aggregator links are applied AI (xDOF robot data, Creatify AI ads) and ML infrastructure (CentML). That suggests an applied-AI and infrastructure lean, but this is inference from thin evidence.\n\n## Read these first\n- [wndr.vc/about](https://wndr.vc/about): the seed strategy they now run inside (\"25 high-conviction bets each year\").\n- [Artificial Lawyer on Truth Systems](https://www.artificiallawyer.com/2025/10/28/truth-systems-bags-4m-to-enforce-ai-guardrails/): the only round where WndrCo's seed participation and their attribution line up.\n- `firms/wndrco.md`: the firm context (Build vs Ventures vs Seed economics).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Truth Systems | Seed ($4M) | 2025-10-28 | No (Gradient led) | Lightspeed, F-Prime, Y Combinator, The LegalTech Fund, Pear VC, a16z Scout, Silver Circle | [Artificial Lawyer](https://www.artificiallawyer.com/2025/10/28/truth-systems-bags-4m-to-enforce-ai-guardrails/); Huang attribution: [Crunchbase](https://www.crunchbase.com/person/chonghao-huang) (aggregator) |\n| xDOF | Seed (WNDR seed), then Series A ($70M) | — / 2026-06-17 | No | Thrive, Spark, a16z, Lux (A) | Firm deal ([wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/portfolio), [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/2026/06/17/robotic-teleoperation-data-startup-xdof-launches-70m-funding/)); their link is RocketReach-only (needs-id) |\n| Creatify | Series A ($15.5M) | 2025-05-28 | WndrCo co-led (Katzenberg on board) | Kindred Ventures | Firm deal ([Creatify](https://creatify.ai/blog/announcing-our-15-5m-series-a-a-new-chapter-for-creatify)); their link is RocketReach-only (needs-id) |\n| Coverd | Seed | 2025 (date unconfirmed) | No (Yolo Investments reportedly led) | Volt Capital, a16z Games Speedrun | WndrCo in round per [VCBacked](https://www.vcbacked.co/company/coverd-inc) (aggregator); a LinkedIn feed snippet shows them sharing the announcement (needs-id) |\n\nOnly Truth Systems is written as a deal row. The rest are leads with `needs-id`.\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing public on how any deal happened.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| WndrCo / WNDR | Vice President, then Partner, Seed | 2024-11 to 2025-03 window; partner by 2026-06-10 | [2025-03](https://web.archive.org/web/20250324173935id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team), [2026-06](https://web.archive.org/web/20260630200655id_/https://www.wndr.vc/assets/index-CuMFcD--.js) |\n| ChenLi Wang, Anthony Saleh | Co-heads of Seed (their seed team leads) | 2026 | [team](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Mel Demma | Fellow Partner, Seed (new in 2026) | 2026 | [team](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Gradient Ventures | Co-investor (lead) on Truth Systems seed | 2025-10 | [Artificial Lawyer](https://www.artificiallawyer.com/2025/10/28/truth-systems-bags-4m-to-enforce-ai-guardrails/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which seed deals have they personally led at WNDR since mid-2026? The wndr.vc portfolio doesn't name a deal lead.\n- Was CentML an employer (before 2025) or a deal? If they were an employee at an ML-compute startup, they'd be a more technical seed partner than the record shows.\n- Are they WndrCo's board observer or deal lead on xDOF (Berkeley spinout; WNDR seed and A)? That would be the owner's most relevant link.\n- Coverd: confirm WndrCo's round and their role from the company's own announcement.\n- Does WNDR raise a new seed vehicle (Seed IV) with them as a related person on the Form D?\n\n## Sources\n- https://wndr.vc/team, https://wndr.vc/about (bundle /assets/index-CuMFcD--.js, read 2026-11-10)\n- Wayback: wndrco.com/team at 20241113194728, 20250324173935, 20260430110940; wndr.vc/team at 20260610052536; wndr.vc bundle at 20260630200655\n- SEC Form D, WndrCo Seed III LP: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2058875/000205984325000002/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.artificiallawyer.com/2025/10/28/truth-systems-bags-4m-to-enforce-ai-guardrails/ ; https://www.law.com/legaltechnews/2025/10/28/governance-and-compliance-startup-truth-systems-announces-4m-seed-round-/\n- https://siliconangle.com/2026/06/17/robotic-teleoperation-data-startup-xdof-launches-70m-funding/ ; https://creatify.ai/blog/announcing-our-15-5m-series-a-a-new-chapter-for-creatify\n- Aggregators: https://www.crunchbase.com/person/chonghao-huang ; https://rocketreach.co/chonghao-huang-email_717207027 ; https://people.equilar.com/bio/person/chonghao-huang-wndrco/56168953 ; https://www.vcbacked.co/company/coverd-inc ; https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/04/jeffrey-katzenberg-wndrco-450m-fund-venture-capital\n", "person:chris-paik": "\n# Chris Paik\n\n## One-line take\nHe ran Thrive's early consumer-internet deals (Twitch, Patreon, Grailed). Now, at Pace, he is one of the clearest bottom-up investors in AI's open, commoditizing layer (Cline, OpenRouter, Ollama, Comfy), and he publishes a sharp argument against frontier-lab economics.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he underwrites the *shape of a business*, not the founder's résumé. He calls himself a \"bottom-up investor\" and says bottom-up investors should source through outreach and product-tracking, not referrals [src](https://cpaik.com/). Cline shows the method: Pace spotted it on the OpenRouter leaderboards while it was still called \"Claude Dev\". They flew to Indianapolis and handed over a term sheet at a Panera Bread [src](https://cpaik.com/).\n- **Explanations are his moat:** he writes long framework essays (\"The End of Software\", \"Frontier\", \"The Watt Era\", \"Cursor's Problem\"). Dahl calls him \"one of the most intellectually rigorous people I know\" [src](https://newsletter.dialectic.fm/p/chris-paik-on-intentionality-incentives).\n- **What constrains him:** fund size. Pace manages about $400M across two funds ($150M + $250M) [src](https://venturecapitaljournal.com/pace-capital-amasses-250m-for-sophomore-fund). His largest disclosed lead is $50M (The Browser Company, 2024). Beyond that he joins rounds others lead, such as OpenRouter's B (CapitalG) and Ollama's B (Theory).\n- **What he'd never do (by stated view):** pay a momentum price for a frontier lab. He argues \"frontier\" means a capital treadmill, not a defensible asset [src](https://cpaik.com/). He also says competitive bidding makes VCs collectively irrational [src](https://cpaik.com/). His AI bets sit on the layer *underneath* the labs: routing, local and open models, model-agnostic agents.\n\n## Differential insight\nPrimary sources are his own essays at cpaik.com (a single-page app; essays are embedded in the site bundle, undated) and the Dialectic transcript (2025-01-18). Mirrored in `data/rows/chris-paik-insights.csv`.\n\n- **Sourcing motion, in his own words.** Bottom-up investors \"source through outreach\" and \"should not invest in referrals\" [src](https://cpaik.com/). That explains why his deals look like open-source/GitHub leaderboard finds (Cline, Comfy, Ollama, NetBird, AltStore) rather than network deals.\n- **How Cline happened.** \"We first encountered Cline early on the OpenRouter leaderboards\" [src](https://cpaik.com/). They flew to Indianapolis to meet Saoud Rizwan and handed over a term sheet at a Panera Bread. He writes \"We led Cline's seed round\" [src](https://cpaik.com/). Cline's announcement calls Pace co-lead with Emergence of the combined $32M seed + Series A (2025-07-31) [src](https://cline.bot/blog/cline-raises-32m-series-a-and-seed-funding-building-the-open-source-ai-coding-agent-that-enterprises-trust). The essay also shows Pace was already reading OpenRouter's leaderboard before it became an OpenRouter investor.\n- **Why Pace bought OpenRouter's Series B.** \"That is why Pace invested in OpenRouter's Series B\" [src](https://cpaik.com/). His thesis is that thousands of models need an exchange. The round was $113M, led by CapitalG, 2026-05-28 [src](https://openrouter.ai/blog/announcements/series-b/).\n- **Openly bearish on lab rent capture.** On \"frontier\": \"One word describes an asset, the other describes a treadmill\" [src](https://cpaik.com/). In \"The Watt Era\" he argues open weights end the closed labs' rent period. That is a direct counter-thesis to $600M lab seeds.\n- **He publicly predicted Cursor's margin problem.** In \"Cursor's Problem\" he calls flat-rate \"unlimited\" AI subscriptions \"underwriting risk\", with fixed revenue against variable cost [src](https://cpaik.com/). Cline, his own portfolio company, is pitched as the metered, pass-through alternative.\n- **On VC incentives (useful for reading other partners in this repo).** \"The vast majority of investors at firms will outsource their restraint to the firm\" [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/chris-paik). He describes junior partners chasing promotion as price-insensitive \"heat-seeking missiles\" [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/chris-paik).\n- **IC culture.** If the investment committee was unanimous, \"I would be really scared\" [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/chris-paik). He says he and Jordan Cooper \"disagree a lot on investments\" [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/chris-paik).\n- **Positioning Pace.** \"We think that venture is a services business\" [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/chris-paik). Pace's self-description is \"a pre-consensus investment firm in New York City\" [src](https://pacecapital.com/).\n- **At Thrive he was a partner from 2010, not a protégé.** AlleyWatch (2015) says he joined in 2010 and \"entered as a partner\" [src](https://alleywatch.com/2015/03/a-new-york-vc-spotlight-chris-paik/). Fortune (2014) credits him, not Kushner, with leading Thrive's Twitch investment (existing row in `thrive-capital-fullbook-insights.csv`) [src](https://fortune.com/2014/10/06/with-new-400-million-fund-thrive-capital-is-new-yorks-quietest-success-story/).\n- **Browser Company link back to Thrive.** Paik was named lead investor on The Browser Company's $50M round at $550M (2024-03-21) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/21/the-browser-company-raises-50-million-at-550-million-valuation/). Its founder, Josh Miller, was a former Thrive Capital investor (same source). Atlassian later acquired it (reported, not fetched).\n- **LP link.** UTIMCO is an LP in Pace Capital Fund II, with $7.2M invested at 0.96x as of 2026-05-31 (aggregator, via `lps/utimco.md` and `data/rows/utimco-lp.csv`).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He isn't a frontier-lab or pre-training investor, and he argues the closed-lab business is a treadmill, so he won't co-invest in $600M lab seeds. But he is a high-signal early investor in AI's open, developer-facing layer, and he finds it bottom-up from product leaderboards:\n- Deals that matter to me: **Cline** (seed lead, 2024–25), **OpenRouter** (Series B, 2026), **Ollama** (Series B, 2026, firm-level), **Comfy** (2026 Series A, firm-level) and **The Browser Company** (2024 lead).\n- Useful as a counter-view and for dealflow: his \"Watt Era\" and \"Frontier\" essays are the best-argued bear case on closed-lab margins in this repo. Pace sees open-source AI tooling before it is consensus.\n- Probably not a co-investor at my check size into labs. He could be a co-investor or referral source for AI-native automation and developer-infrastructure seeds.\n\n## Background\n- Harvard, Economics, with a secondary in Psychology [src](https://alleywatch.com/2015/03/a-new-york-vc-spotlight-chris-paik/). Sold a Gameboy online as a child, the origin story on his site [src](https://cpaik.com/).\n- 2010: joined Thrive Capital as a partner, shortly after its founding [src](https://alleywatch.com/2015/03/a-new-york-vc-spotlight-chris-paik/). He was there when Thrive was on its first $10M fund (secondary; search-result bios). In 2014 TechCrunch called him one of the partners \"all in their 20s\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/2014/10/06/thrive-400m).\n- Board at Venture for America (2015) [src](https://alleywatch.com/2015/03/a-new-york-vc-spotlight-chris-paik/).\n- About 2018: left Thrive after about 8 years [src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-why-vc-subsidizes-the-wrong-712). The exact date is unconfirmed; he was still listed at Thrive in June 2018 [src](https://mr-mag.com/grailed-lands-15-million-investment/).\n- 2019: co-founded Pace Capital with Jordan Cooper (ex-Wildcard/Hyperink CEO) (Crunchbase, aggregator). Fund I $150M, Fund II $250M [src](https://venturecapitaljournal.com/pace-capital-amasses-250m-for-sophomore-fund). Current team on pacecapital.com: Chris, Jordan, Tina (He), Aryan (Naik) [src](https://pacecapital.com/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://cpaik.com/ | Essays (~37), \"Frameworks\", talks; verified 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://cpaik.com/essays-content | Essays live on his site, not Substack |\n| X | https://x.com/cpaik | Linked from the Dialectic episode page; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | nothing verified | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none; YouTube clips embedded on cpaik.com (e.g. \"Reading the Comments with Chris Paik\") | |\n| Firm | https://pacecapital.com/ | Firm X: https://x.com/PaceCap |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-01-18 — Dialectic (Jackson Dahl) — Ep. 6 \"Intentionally in Search of the New\" — [transcript](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/chris-paik). Covers VC as a services business, restraint outsourced to the firm, unanimity as a red flag, and alignment with Jordan Cooper.\n- 2023-05-08 — 20VC (Harry Stebbings) — business-model/product fit, VC subsidizing the wrong businesses, misalignments between VCs, founders and LPs — [notes](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-why-vc-subsidizes-the-wrong-712). No transcript; not quoted.\n- Invest Like the Best EP.338 \"Venture Investing Frameworks\" (Colossus) — page returned 403; not quoted.\n- Origins (Notation Capital, LP-focused podcast), with Jordan Cooper — not fetched; worth mining for Pace's LP base.\n\n## How they think\n- **Top-down vs bottom-up**, for both investors and companies. He is bottom-up: he underwrites businesses, sources by outreach and reads systems books, not biographies [src](https://cpaik.com/).\n- **The End of Software / The Dawn of Infinite Code:** LLMs drive the cost of making software toward zero, as the internet did for content. Value moves to distribution, context and trust [src](https://cpaik.com/).\n- **Business-model/product fit:** flat-rate AI pricing is insurance without actuaries (\"Cursor's Problem\") [src](https://cpaik.com/).\n- **Open weights win the volume (\"The Watt Era\"):** proprietary periods are the slow part of a technology boom, so a router or exchange is needed [src](https://cpaik.com/).\n- **Rational irrationality of VC** (Prisoner's Dilemma + Dollar Auction) [src](https://cpaik.com/).\n- **Calls:** right on Twitch (Amazon bought it under a year after the Series C) and on Patreon. On Cursor's margin squeeze and open-weight adoption the jury is still out.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Top-Down and Bottom-Up Investors\": his self-diagnosis and sourcing rules [src](https://cpaik.com/).\n2. \"Frontier\": why labs renamed \"foundation\" to \"frontier\", and why that means a treadmill [src](https://cpaik.com/).\n3. \"The Watt Era\": the open-weight thesis and why Pace bought OpenRouter [src](https://cpaik.com/).\n4. \"Strong Winds, Big Sails: Why Cline Wins\": investment memo plus the deal story [src](https://cpaik.com/).\n5. \"Paying $3 for a Dollar\": game theory of VC overpayment [src](https://cpaik.com/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed (X not fetched). The site shows his output is essays and video, not threads.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Twitch (Thrive) | Series C ($20M) | 2013-09-30 | yes (Thrive); board | WestSummit, Take-Two, Alsop Louie, Bessemer | existing row in thrive-capital-fullbook-deals.csv; [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2014/10/06/with-new-400-million-fund-thrive-capital-is-new-yorks-quietest-success-story/) |\n| Patreon (Thrive) | Series B ($30M) | 2016-01-19 | Thrive led; Paik not named in article | Index, CRV, Allen & Co, Accomplice, Freestyle | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2016/01/19/patreon-gains-30-million-series-b-funding-experiencing-major-growth) (thrive-capital row) |\n| Patreon (Thrive) | Series C ($60M) | 2017-09-19 | yes, \"led by Chris Paik at Thrive Capital\" | Index, CRV, Freestyle, DFJ | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2017/09/19/patreon-60-million/) |\n| Grailed (Thrive) | Series A ($15M) | 2018-06-21 | no (Index led); Paik already on board | Index Ventures, Simon Ventures | [MR](https://mr-mag.com/grailed-lands-15-million-investment/) |\n| Unity (Thrive) | Undisclosed (he says he invested and was on the board; round not tied to him) | | | | [cpaik.com](https://cpaik.com/) |\n| Others at Thrive per AlleyWatch: Dispatch, Codecademy, Warby Parker, Artsy, Giftly | firm-level \"selected investments\" | | | | [AlleyWatch](https://alleywatch.com/2015/03/a-new-york-vc-spotlight-chris-paik/) |\n| The Browser Company (Pace) | Series B ($50M at $550M) | 2024-03-21 | yes; Paik named lead investor | Next Play Ventures (existing) | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/21/the-browser-company-raises-50-million-at-550-million-valuation/) |\n| Cline (Pace) | Seed (led; part of $32M seed + A) | announced 2025-07-31 | yes (seed); Emergence led the A | Emergence, 1984 Ventures, Essence VC, Cox Exponential | [cpaik.com](https://cpaik.com/), [Cline](https://cline.bot/blog/cline-raises-32m-series-a-and-seed-funding-building-the-open-source-ai-coding-agent-that-enterprises-trust) |\n| AltStore (Pace) | Series A ($6M; 15% stake) | 2025-10-07 | yes (sole investor) | none | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/07/alternative-app-store-altstore-raises-6m-connects-with-the-fediverse/); Paik essay \"We're excited to back AltStore\" [cpaik.com](https://cpaik.com/) |\n| OpenRouter (Pace) | Series B ($113M) | 2026-05-28 | no (CapitalG led) | NVentures, a16z, Menlo, ServiceNow, Snowflake, Databricks, MongoDB | [OpenRouter](https://openrouter.ai/blog/announcements/series-b/); Paik essay |\n| NetBird (Pace) | Series A (€8.5M / ~$10M) | 2026-01 | Pace led; partner not named | Nauta, InReach, Antler | [CISPA](https://cispa.de/en/netbird-series-a) |\n| Ollama (Pace) | Series B ($65M) | 2026-07-09 | no (Theory led); partner not named | Benchmark, 8VC, YC, 49 Palms, GTMFund | [Pulse 2.0](https://pulse2.com/ollama-raises-65-million-in-series-b-funding-to-expand-its-open-source-ai-platform/) |\n| Comfy Org (Pace) | Series A ($30M at $500M, Craft led; label per secondary coverage) | 2026-04-24 | no; partner not named | Craft, Chemistry, TruArrow | [Comfy blog](https://blog.comfy.org/p/comfyui-raises-30m-to-scale-open) (Pace in the earlier ~$17M round: search coverage only, unverified) |\n| Pace portfolio page also lists: Patreon, Infisical, Daytona, OrcaSlicer, Pax Historia, Overtone, Backyard AI, BracketBot, Discourse, Friend, Godmode, Mikoverse, Mulberry, Trolley, NamiComi, Nexus.gg, StationLabs, Tiltify | firm-level | | | | [pacecapital.com](https://pacecapital.com/) (site bundle) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Twitch (2013):** Fortune credits Paik with leading Thrive's investment. Amazon bought Twitch less than a year later (~$970M) (see thrive-capital rows). He later wrote that when he joined the board, making a living from playing games \"was beyond comprehension\" [src](https://cpaik.com/).\n- **Patreon:** Thrive led the B (2016) and the C (2017). TechCrunch names Paik on the C. He frames Patreon as infrastructure for internet-first entrepreneurship [src](https://cpaik.com/).\n- **Cline:** found on the OpenRouter leaderboard as \"Claude Dev\". Pace flew to Indianapolis and delivered a term sheet at a Panera Bread, then led the seed [src](https://cpaik.com/).\n- **AltStore:** he found it years earlier while looking for a way to sideload a manga reader (Paperback). The EU's DMA was the \"why now\". Pace funded the $6M Series A for 15% [src](https://cpaik.com/), [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/07/alternative-app-store-altstore-raises-6m-connects-with-the-fediverse/).\n- **The Browser Company:** led the $50M round in 2024 and wrote the public thesis that the browser becomes the OS [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/21/the-browser-company-raises-50-million-at-550-million-valuation/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Thrive Capital | Partner | 2010 – ~2018 | [AlleyWatch](https://alleywatch.com/2015/03/a-new-york-vc-spotlight-chris-paik/) |\n| Josh Kushner | Thrive founder; Paik's partner there for ~8 years | 2010 | [AlleyWatch](https://alleywatch.com/2015/03/a-new-york-vc-spotlight-chris-paik/) |\n| Will Gaybrick | Fellow Thrive partner in their 20s (2014) | 2014 | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2014/10/06/thrive-400m) |\n| Pace Capital / Jordan Cooper | Co-founder; IC partner | 2019 | [Dialectic](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/chris-paik) |\n| Josh Miller (The Browser Company) | Ex-Thrive investor turned founder; Paik led his 2024 round | 2024 | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/21/the-browser-company-raises-50-million-at-550-million-valuation/) |\n| Saoud Rizwan (Cline) | Founder; Pace led seed | 2024–25 | [cpaik.com](https://cpaik.com/) |\n| Jack Conte (Patreon) | Founder; Paik led Series C | 2017 | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2017/09/19/patreon-60-million/) |\n| Danny Rimer (Index) | Grailed board co-member (2018) | 2018 | [MR](https://mr-mag.com/grailed-lands-15-million-investment/) |\n| Jackson Dahl | Host and friend; calls him uniquely rigorous | ~2022 | [Dialectic](https://newsletter.dialectic.fm/p/chris-paik-on-intentionality-incentives) |\n| UTIMCO | LP in Pace Capital Fund II (aggregator) | | `lps/utimco.md` |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact Thrive exit date and title at exit (the Axios April 2018 \"changes afoot\" piece returned 403).\n- Which Pace deals are Paik's and which are Jordan Cooper's (NetBird, Ollama, Comfy, Daytona, Infisical, Friend).\n- Pace Fund III? Nothing found after Fund II ($250M). With OpenRouter's Stripe sale (Menlo, 2026-08) and The Browser Company's Atlassian sale, a raise is plausible.\n- Cline: CB Insights reports an OpenAI \"reverse acqui-hire\" (2026-01-29). Unverified; it matters for Pace's returns.\n- Is he still on Patreon's board? Some 2025 coverage implies so (unverified).\n- Mine the Invest Like the Best EP.338 and Origins (Notation) episodes for LP base and check-size rules.\n\n## Sources\n- https://cpaik.com/ (essays and about text embedded in the site bundle; fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://pacecapital.com/ (team and portfolio in the site bundle)\n- https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/chris-paik ; https://newsletter.dialectic.fm/p/chris-paik-on-intentionality-incentives\n- https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-why-vc-subsidizes-the-wrong-712\n- https://alleywatch.com/2015/03/a-new-york-vc-spotlight-chris-paik/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2014/10/06/thrive-400m\n- https://fortune.com/2014/10/06/with-new-400-million-fund-thrive-capital-is-new-yorks-quietest-success-story/ (via thrive-capital rows)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2017/09/19/patreon-60-million/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2016/01/19/patreon-gains-30-million-series-b-funding-experiencing-major-growth\n- https://mr-mag.com/grailed-lands-15-million-investment/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/21/the-browser-company-raises-50-million-at-550-million-valuation/\n- https://cline.bot/blog/cline-raises-32m-series-a-and-seed-funding-building-the-open-source-ai-coding-agent-that-enterprises-trust ; https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/cline-raises-32m-seed-and-series-funding-bring-agentic-ai-coding-enterprise\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/07/alternative-app-store-altstore-raises-6m-connects-with-the-fediverse/\n- https://openrouter.ai/blog/announcements/series-b/\n- https://cispa.de/en/netbird-series-a\n- https://venturecapitaljournal.com/pace-capital-amasses-250m-for-sophomore-fund (search snippet; SEC-filing-based)\n", "person:chris-re": "\n# Chris Ré\n\n## One-line take\nThe single most important upstream node in the repo for Stanford AI-systems talent: his own site publicly lists the labs he helped *before* they formed (Flapping Airplanes, Modular, Cognition, Simile and more), and GV, Index and Lux all walked into Flapping through him [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He is a co-founder, not just an angel. He co-founded Lattice, SambaNova, Snorkel, Together, Cartesia, Rox and Engram Labs, and says he helped other founders \"before they formed their companies\" [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php). His students become founders or professors: Alex Ratner (Snorkel), Albert Gu and Karan Goel (Cartesia), Ben Spector (Flapping Airplanes), Sabri Eyuboglu (stealth, which looks like Engram) [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/alumni.html).\n- **What constrains him:** He is a full-time academic with a large conflict list. His page says \"Please assume I'm conflicted\" [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php). His check size isn't public, so his capital matters less than his introductions.\n- **What he'd never do (per others):** GV's Dave Munichiello writes that he \"runs away from hype\" and avoids self-promotion. That is Munichiello's description, not Ré's own words [src](https://www.gv.com/news/snorkel-chris-re) (2020-08-25).\n- **For the owner:** you can't get his allocation by competing with GV, Index or Lux. You get it by being someone he trusts to back his students. The pre-formation list on his own page works like a pipeline map.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He publishes his pre-formation deal list.** One self-maintained disclosure page lists \"Founders that trusted me to help before they formed their companies\": 1001, Apex, Artera, Cognition, Flapping Airplanes, Flourish, Long Lake Management, Modular, Radical Numerics, Recursive Intelligence, Ricursive Intelligence, Sequence, Simile and Transfyer [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php) (page undated; read 2026-11-10). Several of these are repo deals (Flapping, Modular, Cognition, Simile). The rest (Radical Numerics, Ricursive, Apex, 1001, Sequence, Transfyer) are leads to check before the GV, Index and Lux rounds land.\n- **He has his own investment vehicle, Factory.** In his words: \"I built and own Factory with friends and family\" [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php). GV's Modular post lists him on that cap table as \"Chris Ré (Factory)\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference) (2026-06-25). His homepage says he has co-founded \"a number of companies and investment firms\" [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/~chrismre/). Factory's site, team, LPs and fund size aren't confirmed: factoryhq.ai is a \"deep tech investment\" site that names no people. One aggregator links Factory to Lip-Bu Tan, but I couldn't open it (HTTP 403) and no search corroborated it.\n- **He is a formal GV Advisor, and GV says so.** The quote: \"a GV Advisor who has teamed with us on more than a half-dozen companies\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales) (2024-11-19, Munichiello). GV met him around 2012 and invested in Lattice, SambaNova and Snorkel [src](https://www.gv.com/news/snorkel-chris-re) (2020-08-25). GV hand-picked him as a Modular co-investor [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference).\n- **He made the founding intro at Together.** Vipul Ved Prakash says the company formed \"when Chris introduced me to Ce\" [src](https://www.latent.space/p/together) (2024-02-08). Lux then led the $20M seed [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/05/15/together-raises-20m-to-build-open-source-generative-ai-models/).\n- **Downstream: his chip company is entangled with Intel's CEO.** Lip-Bu Tan has chaired SambaNova (a Ré co-founded company) since May 2024, and Tan's Walden International led SambaNova's 2018 round [src](https://www.trendforce.com/news/2025/10/31/news-intel-reportedly-eyes-ai-chip-startup-sambanova-where-ceo-lip-bu-tan-serves-as-executive-chairman/). Intel, which Tan has run since March 2025, explored buying SambaNova for about $1.6B. Those talks stalled. In Feb 2026 Vista led a $350M+ Series E, and Intel committed up to $150M [src](https://seekingalpha.com/news/4541271-sambanova-seeks-funding-after-intel-takeover-talks-stall-bloomberg) [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=731570). This means a Ré lab-to-company chip path has an Intel buyer-investor attached.\n- **His public thesis has been many models, not one.** He wrote \"we might enter an age of many models rather than of a monolithic few\" [src](https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2023-01-30-ai-linux) (2023-01-30). In 2025 the lab argued \"Demand is exploding faster than infrastructure can scale\" (co-authored with John Hennessy and Azalia Mirhoseini) [src](https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2025-11-18-intelligence-per-watt) (2025-11-11). Together (open models), Cartesia (SSMs), Flapping (kernel efficiency) and SambaNova (chips) all follow from these two views.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He sits upstream of almost every Stanford systems lab the owner cares about: SambaNova (AI chips), Together (compute), Cartesia (new architecture), Flapping Airplanes ($180M seed, then a $1B A), Engram ($98M first round) and Modular. He is not a fund the owner can co-invest alongside on terms. He is the person who decides which investors meet the founders first. The deals that matter most are the pre-formation list, especially Radical Numerics, Ricursive Intelligence and Engram.\n\n## Background\n- B.S. Cornell; Ph.D. University of Washington [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christopher_R%C3%A9).\n- Stanford CS faculty (Stanford AI Lab, CRFM, ML Group). Associate professor as of 2020 [src](https://www.gv.com/news/snorkel-chris-re), now full professor [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christopher_R%C3%A9).\n- MacArthur Fellow, 2015 [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christopher_R%C3%A9).\n- Co-founded Lattice.io, which Apple acquired in May 2017 [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christopher_R%C3%A9). Co-founded SambaNova in 2017 [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SambaNova_Systems), then Snorkel, Together (2022), Cartesia, Rox (2024) and Engram Labs [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://cs.stanford.edu/~chrismre/ | Homepage; includes the conflict and advising page https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php |\n| Substack / blog | https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog | Lab blog; Ré co-authors most posts |\n| X | https://twitter.com/hazyresearch | Lab account linked from his homepage. No personal account verified |\n| LinkedIn | not found | |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/HazyResearch | Lab org (ThunderKittens, Megakernels) |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://mlsys.stanford.edu/ | Stanford MLSys seminar/podcast, linked from his homepage |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-02-08 — Latent Space — Together AI episode (Ré not on mic) — https://www.latent.space/p/together — the founders describe him making the founding intro.\n- No podcast interview with Ré himself was found in one targeted search. Nothing found.\n\n## How they think\n- Many models rather than one monolith; data is the main lever (\"modifying the data is arguably the most reliable way to improve model performance\") [src](https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2023-01-30-ai-linux).\n- Efficiency is the bottleneck: intelligence per watt, local inference, and kernels (ThunderKittens, HipKittens, megakernels) [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/~chrismre/).\n- Lab as company factory: he describes the lab's goal as producing professors and entrepreneurs, and says more than a dozen lab members have started professorships [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/~chrismre/).\n- Calls that worked: SambaNova (reported $5B valuation at the SoftBank-led round, though the Intel talks priced it at about $1.6B), Together, Snorkel, and Lattice (sold to Apple).\n\n## Read these first\n- Startup and advising disclosure: https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php — the whole network on one page.\n- \"AI's Linux Moment\" (2023-01-30): https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2023-01-30-ai-linux — the open-model thesis behind Together.\n- Intelligence per Watt (2025-11-11): https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2025-11-18-intelligence-per-watt — the efficiency thesis.\n- ThunderKittens (2024-05-12): https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2024-05-12-tk — the kernel work Flapping Airplanes grew from.\n- GV, \"Lessons in Entrepreneurship from Chris Ré\" (2020-08-25): https://www.gv.com/news/snorkel-chris-re — how GV sees him.\n\n## Social engagement\nHe posts through the lab account (@hazyresearch) and the lab blog rather than a personal feed. Who amplifies him wasn't measured.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Lattice.io | co-founder | acq. 2017-05 | n/a | GV | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/snorkel-chris-re) |\n| SambaNova | co-founder | 2017 | n/a | GV, Walden International (2018) | [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php) |\n| Snorkel AI | co-founder | 2019-2020 | n/a | GV, Greylock | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/snorkel-chris-re) |\n| Together AI | co-founder | 2022-06 | n/a | Lux (seed lead, 2023-05) | [src](https://www.latent.space/p/together) |\n| Modular | first round (via Factory) | 2022 | no | GV (lead), Ram Shriram, Ron Conway, Saam Motamedi | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference) |\n| Rox | co-founder | 2024 | n/a | GV | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales) |\n| Cartesia | co-founder | seed 2024 | n/a | Index (seed lead), Kleiner (A) | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-next-generation-of-real-time-ai-models-our-investment-in-cartesia/) |\n| Cognition | pre-formation help; thanked in the launch post | 2024-03 | no | Founders Fund | [src](https://cognition.com/blog/introducing-devin) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | pre-formation help; angel/advisor (aggregator) | seed 2026-01 | no | Index, GV, Sequoia | [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php) |\n| Engram Labs | co-founder | first round 2026-06 | n/a | General Catalyst, Modern Capital | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-engram) |\n| Mercor, Modal, Applied Compute, Ramp, Base Power, AI21 and others | advisor or investor (role per company not stated) | n/s | no | n/s | [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Together:** Ré had been talking with Prakash about cutting costs, then introduced him to his former postdoc Ce Zhang. Prakash decided within minutes [src](https://www.latent.space/p/together). Lux led the seed 11 months after founding.\n- **Flapping Airplanes:** Index's Shardul Shah was \"catching up with Chris Ré\" when Ben Spector came by, and the two \"took an impromptu walk\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/). Ré lists Flapping among the founders he helped before formation [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php). GV (through Munichiello, a Ré advisor relationship) co-led the seed. How Lux got in is not confirmed.\n- **Modular:** GV \"advocated for\" Ré, under the Factory name, as a co-investor in the first round [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference).\n- **Rox:** co-founded with Ishan Mukherjee, a GV founder [src](https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Dave Munichiello (GV) | GV Advisor; met around 2012; backed Lattice, SambaNova, Snorkel, Rox, Modular | ~2012 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/snorkel-chris-re) |\n| Shardul Shah (Index) | Met Ben Spector in Ré's office; Cartesia seed | 2024 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/) |\n| Lux Capital | Led Together seed (2023); co-led Flapping A (2026) | 2023 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/05/15/together-raises-20m-to-build-open-source-generative-ai-models/) |\n| Ben Spector | PhD student (on leave), Flapping Airplanes founder | n/s | [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/alumni.html) |\n| Vipul Ved Prakash, Ce Zhang, Percy Liang | Together co-founders | 2022 | [src](https://www.latent.space/p/together) |\n| Albert Gu, Karan Goel | Former students, Cartesia co-founders | 2022-2023 | [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/alumni.html) |\n| Alex Ratner | Former student, Snorkel CEO | 2019 | [src](https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/alumni.html) |\n| Kunle Olukotun, Rodrigo Liang | SambaNova co-founders | 2017 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SambaNova_Systems) |\n| Lip-Bu Tan (Intel, Walden) | SambaNova chair since 2024-05; Walden led 2018 round | 2018 | [src](https://www.trendforce.com/news/2025/10/31/news-intel-reportedly-eyes-ai-chip-startup-sambanova-where-ceo-lip-bu-tan-serves-as-executive-chairman/) |\n| Ishan Mukherjee | Rox co-founder | 2024 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales) |\n| Ram Shriram, Ron Conway, Saam Motamedi | Modular first-round co-investors | 2022 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference) |\n| Sabri Eyuboglu, Dan Biderman | Engram co-founders (Eyuboglu is a former student) | 2026 | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-engram) |\n| John Hennessy, Azalia Mirhoseini | Intelligence-per-Watt co-authors | 2025 | [src](https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2025-11-18-intelligence-per-watt) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What is Factory: its fund size, its LPs, and whether factoryhq.ai is the same entity? Is Lip-Bu Tan involved, as one unopened aggregator suggests?\n- What are Radical Numerics, Ricursive Intelligence, Recursive Intelligence, Apex, 1001, Sequence, Transfyer and Long Lake Management, and who has funded them? Each is a pre-formation Ré company.\n- Is Sabri Eyuboglu's \"Stealth Co.\" Engram? The GC memo names Eyuboglu as a founder, so this is likely but unconfirmed.\n- Did Lux reach Flapping through Ré or Together?\n- Is Ré an LP anywhere? Nothing was found. I checked his disclosure page, his homepage and GV posts.\n\n## Sources\n- https://cs.stanford.edu/~chrismre/\n- https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php\n- https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/alumni.html\n- https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales\n- https://www.gv.com/news/snorkel-chris-re\n- https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference\n- https://www.latent.space/p/together\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/05/15/together-raises-20m-to-build-open-source-generative-ai-models/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-next-generation-of-real-time-ai-models-our-investment-in-cartesia/\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-engram\n- https://cognition.com/blog/introducing-devin\n- https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2023-01-30-ai-linux\n- https://hazyresearch.stanford.edu/blog/2025-11-18-intelligence-per-watt\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christopher_R%C3%A9\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SambaNova_Systems\n- https://www.trendforce.com/news/2025/10/31/news-intel-reportedly-eyes-ai-chip-startup-sambanova-where-ceo-lip-bu-tan-serves-as-executive-chairman/\n- https://seekingalpha.com/news/4541271-sambanova-seeks-funding-after-intel-takeover-talks-stall-bloomberg\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=731570\n- https://factoryhq.ai (unconfirmed match)\n", "person:christina-buchanan": "\n# Christina Buchanan\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. No source retrieved gives pronouns, so this page uses \"they\". Four name searches (with NVentures, with principal/investment, with KKR/Morgan Stanley/schools, with speaker/panel/podcast/board observer) returned nothing about them. Everything below rests on the Emerald AI company and team pages plus Emerald's round coverage.*\n\n## One-line take\nThe one NVentures investor below Siddeek whose name appears on a portfolio company: they hold NVIDIA's board observer seat at Emerald AI, a data-center power-flexibility startup in which NVentures followed three different leads across three rounds.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they matter:** NVentures publishes no team page ([nvidia.com](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/nventures/)) and every NVentures release found quotes only Siddeek (see [firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md)). Buchanan's Emerald seat is the only public trace of who below the head actually covers a company ([Emerald AI company page](https://www.emeraldai.co/company)).\n- **What constrains them:** a principal at a balance-sheet corporate VC. The pool can write nine-figure lab seeds (at least $250M into Ineffable, [TNW](https://thenextweb.com/news/ineffable-intelligence-david-silver-sequoia-nvidia-5-billion)), but no check, round or investment decision is attributed to Buchanan personally. `check_capacity: growth` describes the NVentures pool, not them.\n- **What they would never do (inferred):** sit as a voting director or lead-and-price. At Emerald, NVentures led none of the three announced rounds and Buchanan is listed as \"(Board Observer)\", one of five observers ([company page](https://www.emeraldai.co/company)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The observer seat is not a lead's seat.** NVentures was a non-lead participant in every Emerald round: the July 2025 $24.5M seed was Radical-led ([Radical](https://radical.vc/articles/reimagining-data-centers-as-energy-assets/)); the November 2025 $18M seed extension \"was led by Lowercarbon Capital\" with \"participation from NVentures\" ([Pulse 2.0, 2025-11-02](https://pulse2.com/emerald-ai-18-million-seed-extension-raised-to-advance-power-flexible-ai-infrastructure/)); the March 2026 $25M round \"was led by Energy Impact Partners\" ([Fortune, 2026-03-31](https://www.fortune.com/2026/03/31/emerald-ai-nvidia-fast-pass-data-center-grid-connects/)). Each of Radical, Lowercarbon and EIP also holds an observer or director seat, so NVIDIA got governance access without leading. Which round created Buchanan's seat is not stated anywhere.\n- **The GCV \"$22.7M seed extension\" matches no announced round.** GCV's 2026 Siddeek profile lists \"Emerald AI's $22.7m seed extension\" ([GCV 2026](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-mohamed-siddeek-nventures/?format=md)). Emerald's announced rounds are $18M (Nov 2025) and $25M (Mar 2026); Axios reported the 2026 raise as a ~$25M seed extension at a $250M post ([Axios Pro, 2026-02-19](https://axios.com/pro/climate-deals/2026/02/19/emerald-ai-varun-sivaram-nvidia-data-center-energy)), while Fortune calls it a \"strategic funding round\". $22.7M is most likely a filing or partial-close figure for the 2026 round; treat it as unverified.\n- **The NVIDIA voice on Emerald is not Buchanan's.** The NVIDIA person quoted in the March 2026 coverage is Marc Spieler, \"Nvidia senior managing director for global energy\" ([Fortune](https://www.fortune.com/2026/03/31/emerald-ai-nvidia-fast-pass-data-center-grid-connects/)). So the commercial relationship (the energy business unit) and the governance seat (NVentures principal) sit with different people. That matches Siddeek's pitch that NVentures sells go-to-market access, not just money.\n- **Title mismatch on Emerald's own site.** The team page headlines Buchanan as \"Board Director\" but the role line reads \"Principal, NVIDIA NVentures (Board Observer)\" ([team page](https://www.emeraldai.co/team-members/christina-buchanan)). The August 2026 Form D names only Sivaram, Toews and Specks as directors ([SEC Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2047797/000204779726000004/xslFormDX01/primary_doc.xml)), so \"observer\" is the correct reading.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Seniority is exactly the owner's principal-alpha target, and the pool can write $10M+ and nine-figure lab seeds. But:\n- The only sourced deal is Emerald AI, an AI-energy infrastructure company, not a frontier lab. No lab seed (Periodic, Ineffable, RadixArk, Inherent, Reflection) is tied to them.\n- A corporate-VC principal cannot be a syndicate partner the owner co-leads with; NVentures follows leads. The value is as an early read on which compute-adjacent seeds NVIDIA will back, and as a route to NVIDIA's energy and data-center teams for portfolio companies.\n- Promotion speed is unknown; if the Senior Associate -> Principal step is dated inside NVentures (launched 2023), they could be on a fast track worth tracking.\n\n## Background\n- Current: Principal, NVIDIA NVentures ([Emerald team page](https://www.emeraldai.co/team-members/christina-buchanan)). Start date not found.\n- Earlier (aggregator-only, unverified, no dates): Senior Associate at NVIDIA; KKR; Morgan Stanley (SignalHire/ZoomInfo, as recorded in `data/rows/nvidia-nventures-team-rels.csv`).\n- Education: nothing found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.emeraldai.co/team-members/christina-buchanan | Emerald AI board page entry, 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked (not used as a source) | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found (search 2026-11-10 for speaker, panel, podcast and board-observer mentions returned no hits for this name).\n\n## How they think\n- Nothing on record. No quote, post or talk found.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Emerald AI company page](https://www.emeraldai.co/company): the board and observer roster, showing who holds governance next to NVIDIA.\n- [Fortune, 2026-03-31](https://www.fortune.com/2026/03/31/emerald-ai-nvidia-fast-pass-data-center-grid-connects/): the EIP-led round and NVIDIA's energy-team involvement.\n- [GCV Powerlist 2026, Siddeek](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-mohamed-siddeek-nventures/?format=md): the NVentures deal list that includes Emerald.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Emerald AI | Seed ($24.5M) | 2025-07 | no (NVentures participated; Radical led) | Radical Ventures, Jeff Dean, John Kerry, Malcolm Turnbull, John Doerr | [Radical](https://radical.vc/articles/reimagining-data-centers-as-energy-assets/) |\n| Emerald AI | Seed extension ($18M) | 2025-11-02 | no (Lowercarbon led) | Radical, Salesforce Ventures, National Grid Partners, Amplo, Earthshot, Trust Ventures, John Doerr | [Pulse 2.0](https://pulse2.com/emerald-ai-18-million-seed-extension-raised-to-advance-power-flexible-ai-infrastructure/) |\n| Emerald AI | Strategic ($25M; Axios: seed extension at ~$250M post; GCV: $22.7M) | 2026-03-31 | no (EIP led) | Eaton, GE Vernova, Radical, Salesforce, Samsung, Siemens, IQT | [Fortune](https://www.fortune.com/2026/03/31/emerald-ai-nvidia-fast-pass-data-center-grid-connects/) |\n\nAll rows are NVentures (unit) participation. Buchanan is named only as the observer, not on any round.\n\n## Deal stories\n- Emerald AI: CEO Varun Sivaram; NVIDIA has also worked with Emerald commercially through its energy unit (Spieler quote, Fortune). How NVentures first engaged, and whether Buchanan sourced it, is not stated. A Form D of 2026-08-03 shows $90.2M sold toward a $150M offering ([SEC](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2047797/000204779726000004/xslFormDX01/primary_doc.xml)); whether NVentures is in it is not disclosed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NVIDIA NVentures | Principal | not found | [Emerald team page](https://www.emeraldai.co/team-members/christina-buchanan) |\n| Emerald AI | Board observer | not found | [Emerald company page](https://www.emeraldai.co/company) |\n| Mohamed \"Sid\" Siddeek | Head of the unit they work in (inferred reporting line) | | [GCV 2026](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-mohamed-siddeek-nventures/?format=md) |\n| Rob Toews, David Katz (Radical); Zachary Bogue (DCVC); Clay Dumas (Lowercarbon); Shayle Kann (EIP) | Fellow Emerald AI board members / observers | | [Emerald company page](https://www.emeraldai.co/company) |\n| Marc Spieler (NVIDIA global energy) | NVIDIA colleague on the Emerald relationship | 2026 | [Fortune](https://www.fortune.com/2026/03/31/emerald-ai-nvidia-fast-pass-data-center-grid-connects/) |\n\n## How a founder reaches them\nNo public channel. Practical routes: (1) through the lead investor of a round NVentures is likely to follow (here Radical's Toews or Katz, Lowercarbon's Dumas, EIP's Kann, all co-observers with them); (2) through Emerald's CEO Varun Sivaram; (3) through NVIDIA's Inception VC Alliance desk (see Sydney Sykes lead). Not sourced as a stated preference of theirs.\n\n## Open questions\n- Start date at NVentures, and whether they were promoted from Senior Associate there (would make them the one internal promotion inside NVentures).\n- Which Emerald round created the seat, and whether NVentures is in the 2026 Form D raise.\n- Whether they cover any lab seed (Periodic, Ineffable, RadixArk, Inherent) or only AI-energy and infrastructure.\n- Source of GCV's $22.7M figure.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.emeraldai.co/team-members/christina-buchanan\n- https://www.emeraldai.co/company\n- https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-mohamed-siddeek-nventures/?format=md\n- https://pulse2.com/emerald-ai-18-million-seed-extension-raised-to-advance-power-flexible-ai-infrastructure/\n- https://www.fortune.com/2026/03/31/emerald-ai-nvidia-fast-pass-data-center-grid-connects/\n- https://axios.com/pro/climate-deals/2026/02/19/emerald-ai-varun-sivaram-nvidia-data-center-energy\n- https://dealroom.co/news/144174-emerald-ai-discloses-90m-sold-towards-150m-offering-after-march-2026-rou/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2047797/000204779726000004/xslFormDX01/primary_doc.xml\n- https://radical.vc/articles/reimagining-data-centers-as-energy-assets/\n", "person:christopher-kauffman": "\n# Christopher Kauffman\n\n## One-line take\nA General Catalyst later-stage partner who is the sole GC person of record on Modular (GC led the $100M round in 2023; Qualcomm bought the company in July 2026) and who co-signed GC's lead of Together AI's $305M Series B. That makes them a quiet route into GC's AI compute-software growth checks.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** Kauffman is a former SoftBank Vision Fund frontier-tech VP who now sits in GC's later-stage enterprise/AI group. They are credited on the compute-layer names where GC wrote big lead checks: Modular, where the GC portfolio page credits only Kauffman ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/modular)), and Together AI, where they co-authored the investment post ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-together-ai)).\n- **What constrains them:** They work inside a big partnership. On Mistral they are one of five GC names under Hemant Taneja ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/mistral-ai)). The public memos carry senior bylines: Quentin Clark alone on Modular, and Marc Bhargava first on Together. Their personal voice is close to absent from public sources.\n- **What they'd never do (inferred):** Their record is Series A-plus and growth rounds with revenue or enterprise-contract evidence. Nothing found shows a pre-product lab seed.\n\n## Differential insight\n- On the GC site, the Modular portfolio page credits **only Kauffman** ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/modular)), but the 2023 investment memo is bylined to Quentin Clark alone. The memo thesis: \"infrastructure is operating materially below its full potential\" ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-modular), 2023-08-24). Read: Kauffman is now the owner of the relationship, while Clark signed the original deal. Inferred from the page credits, not confirmed.\n- Modular's outcome, unusual for a 2023 AI-infra lead: Qualcomm \"has completed its acquisition of Modular Inc.\" ([src](https://www.modular.com/blog/qualcomm-completes-acquisition-of-modular), 2026-07-29). The price was not disclosed. The last private mark was $1.6B in Sept 2025 ([src](https://www.modular.com/blog/modular-raises-250m-to-scale-ais-unified-compute-layer)).\n- On the Together AI Series B, GC's stated trigger was enterprise contract quality, not research: \"our conviction in the platform only grew as we observed\" the \"rotation into larger and longer enterprise contracts\" ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-together-ai), 2025-02-20). Co-authors: Marc Bhargava, Quentin Clark, Kauffman.\n- On the Together founder: \"an ambitious vision married with relentless execution\" ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-together-ai)).\n- Title conflict: the GC bio says Partner ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/christopher-kauffman)), and TheOrg (aggregator) also says Partner but lists Bain & Company as a prior employer, which the GC bio does not ([src](https://theorg.com/org/general-catalyst/org-chart/christopher-kauffman)). The Bain & Company link is unconfirmed.\n- No podcasts, X account, Substack or talks found. The GC page links only GC's corporate X account. Note: the web search budget for this run was used up, so coverage relied on direct fetches. See open questions.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **Matches my lens:** AI compute software (Modular), AI inference cloud (Together AI), and a frontier lab (Mistral, as one of several GC names). They sit at a fund that writes $100M+ leads, and per their bio they also work on GC's Creation strategy for AI-transformation platforms.\n- **Doesn't match:** Their entry points are Series A and later (Modular's $100M round, which was not its first; Together's B; Mistral's A and B), not seed. They are not a first check. Useful as a later-round partner for a lab or chip company after my seed, and as a read on how GC underwrites AI infrastructure growth.\n\n## Background\n- Indiana University Kelley School of Business, BS in Business (finance and psychology) ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/christopher-kauffman)). Years not found.\n- Associate, Golden Gate Capital (PE). Dates not found ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/christopher-kauffman)).\n- Vice President, Frontier Technologies, SoftBank Investment Advisers (Vision Fund). Dates not found ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/christopher-kauffman)).\n- VP Finance, Ruggable (e-commerce operator role). Dates not found ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/christopher-kauffman)).\n- General Catalyst: credited on Modular, backed since 2023 ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/modular)). They now focus on later-stage enterprise and AI, plus Creation-strategy AI-transformation platforms ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/christopher-kauffman)). GC join date and promotion to Partner: not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/christopher-kauffman | GC bio, 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | GC page links only @generalcatalyst |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/christopher-kauffman-119b0722/ | from GC bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. The web search budget was used up during this run, so a dedicated podcast and YouTube search is still open.\n\n## How they think\n- **Infrastructure efficiency as the investable layer:** the GC investment memos they are tied to stress platforms that sit between models and hardware (Modular's unified compute layer; Together's \"full stack infrastructure platform\") ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-together-ai)).\n- **Underwriting on contract quality:** the Together lead was triggered by watching enterprise contracts get bigger and longer ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-together-ai)).\n- **Right call:** Modular went from a GC-led $100M round (2023) to $1.6B (2025) to a Qualcomm acquisition (2026) ([src](https://www.modular.com/blog/qualcomm-completes-acquisition-of-modular)).\n- No sourced contrarian views or admitted mistakes.\n\n## Read these first\n- GC, \"Our investment in Together AI\" (2025-02-20; co-author): https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-together-ai\n- GC, Modular memo (2023-08-24; Quentin Clark byline, the deal Kauffman now owns): https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-modular\n- GC bio: https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/christopher-kauffman\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No personal X account is linked.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Modular | Series A ($100M; total $130M) | 2023-08-24 | yes (GC lead; Kauffman is sole GC credit) | GV, SV Angel, Greylock, Factory | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2023/08/24/modular-raises-100m-for-ai-dev-tools/), [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/modular) |\n| Modular | Third round ($250M at $1.6B) | 2025-09-24 | no (USIT / Thomas Tull led) | DFJ Growth, GV, Greylock | [src](https://www.modular.com/blog/modular-raises-250m-to-scale-ais-unified-compute-layer) |\n| Modular | Acquired by Qualcomm | 2026-07-29 | n/a | | [src](https://www.modular.com/blog/qualcomm-completes-acquisition-of-modular) |\n| Together AI | Series B ($305M at $3.3B) | 2025-02-20 | yes (GC lead; Prosperity7 co-lead; Kauffman co-author) | Salesforce Ventures, NVIDIA, Kleiner Perkins, Lux, Coatue, Emergence and others | [src](https://www.together.ai/blog/together-ai-announcing-305m-series-b), [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-together-ai) |\n| Mistral AI | Series A (EUR 385M, ~$2B val.) | 2023-12-11 | no (a16z led) | Lightspeed, Salesforce, BNP Paribas, Elad Gil, Conviction | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2023/12/11/mistral-ai-a-paris-based-openai-rival-closed-its-415-million-funding-round/), [GC page](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/mistral-ai) |\n| Mistral AI | Series B (EUR 600M, $6B val.) | 2024-06-11 | yes (GC lead) | Lightspeed, a16z, Nvidia, Samsung, Salesforce Ventures | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/11/paris-based-ai-startup-mistral-ai-raises-640-million/) |\n| Mistral AI | Series C (EUR 1.7B, EUR 11.7B post) | 2025-09-09 | no (ASML led) | DST, a16z, Bpifrance, Index, Lightspeed, NVIDIA | [src](https://mistral.ai/news/mistral-ai-raises-1-7-b-to-accelerate-technological-progress-with-ai) |\n| Verkada | Round unconfirmed (GC backed since 2025; Verkada cites a Series E at end of 2025) | 2025 | unknown | | [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/verkada), [Verkada](https://www.verkada.com/about/) |\n| Luma AI | Round unconfirmed (GC backed since 2023; listed on Kauffman bio; GC Luma page credits no one) | 2023 | unknown | | [bio](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/christopher-kauffman), [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/luma-ai) |\n| SonarSource | Unconfirmed (GC since 2022; on bio, page credits no one) | 2022 | unknown | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/sonarsource) |\n| Arrcus | Unconfirmed (GC since 2017; on bio, page credits no one) | 2017 | unknown | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/arrcus) |\n\nMistral credit: the GC page lists Kauffman with Hemant Taneja, Jeannette zu Fürstenberg, Quentin Clark and Alexandre Momeni. Kauffman's personal role is not specified.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Modular:** GC was not first in. The $100M brought total raised to $130M, so about $30M came earlier from other investors ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/08/24/modular-raises-100m-for-ai-dev-tools/)); the earlier lead was not confirmed this run. The Modular memo was signed by Quentin Clark ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-modular)), but Kauffman is the only GC credit today ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/modular)). GC followed on in the 2025 round, which USIT led ([src](https://www.modular.com/blog/modular-raises-250m-to-scale-ais-unified-compute-layer)), and the company exited to Qualcomm in July 2026 ([src](https://www.modular.com/blog/qualcomm-completes-acquisition-of-modular)).\n- **Together AI:** GC led the B. Earlier backers listed include Kleiner Perkins, Lux and Emergence, so GC was a later entrant ([src](https://www.together.ai/blog/together-ai-announcing-305m-series-b)). The GC memo says conviction grew as contracts got larger and longer ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-together-ai)).\n- **Mistral AI:** GC came in as a participant in the a16z-led Series A in Dec 2023, so not first in (the earlier lead was not sourced this run). Six months later GC led the Series B, described as \"an existing investor\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/11/paris-based-ai-startup-mistral-ai-raises-640-million/)). Kauffman's personal role inside the five-person GC credit is not sourced.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| General Catalyst | Partner (per bio) | by 2023 | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/christopher-kauffman |\n| Marc Bhargava | Co-author, Together AI B memo | 2025 | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-together-ai |\n| Quentin Clark | Co-author on Together; Modular memo author | 2023 | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-modular |\n| Hemant Taneja | Co-credited on Mistral AI | 2023 | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/mistral-ai |\n| Jeannette zu Fürstenberg | Co-credited on Mistral AI | 2023 | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/mistral-ai |\n| Alexandre Momeni | Co-credited on Mistral AI | 2023 | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/mistral-ai |\n| Chris Lattner (Modular) | Portfolio CEO | 2023 | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/modular |\n| SoftBank Vision Fund | Former VP, Frontier Technologies | unknown | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/christopher-kauffman |\n| Golden Gate Capital | Former Associate | unknown | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/christopher-kauffman |\n| Ruggable | Former VP Finance | unknown | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/christopher-kauffman |\n\n## Open questions\n- GC join date and promotion to Partner: these decide fast_promotion. Check LinkedIn and GC announcements.\n- Did Kauffman source Modular, or inherit it from Quentin Clark? Is Clark still at GC?\n- Which Verkada round did GC enter (2025)? Which Luma round in 2023, and was Kauffman on it?\n- Which \"Beacon\" is on their bio? GC's Beacon page (beacon.bio) credits Hemant Taneja, so it may be a different Creation-strategy company.\n- TheOrg lists Bain & Company. Is that real?\n- Any podcast, conference panel or SoftBank-era deal history? Search budget was used up this run, so retry.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/christopher-kauffman\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/modular\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-modular\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/08/24/modular-raises-100m-for-ai-dev-tools/\n- https://www.modular.com/blog/modular-raises-250m-to-scale-ais-unified-compute-layer\n- https://www.modular.com/blog/qualcomm-completes-acquisition-of-modular\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-together-ai\n- https://www.together.ai/blog/together-ai-announcing-305m-series-b\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/together-ai\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/mistral-ai\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/12/11/mistral-ai-a-paris-based-openai-rival-closed-its-415-million-funding-round/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/11/paris-based-ai-startup-mistral-ai-raises-640-million/\n- https://mistral.ai/news/mistral-ai-raises-1-7-b-to-accelerate-technological-progress-with-ai\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/verkada\n- https://www.verkada.com/about/\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/luma-ai\n- https://lumalabs.ai/news/series-c\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/sonarsource\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/arrcus\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/beacon\n- https://theorg.com/org/general-catalyst/org-chart/christopher-kauffman (aggregator)\n", "person:clem-delangue": "\n# Clem Delangue\n\n## One-line take\nThe most-networked open-source AI operator angel; through Factorial his picks get outside-LP money, but checks stay small and he is a co-investor, not a lead.\n\n## Crux\nDelangue sees open-source founders before anyone, because they ship on Hugging Face first. Factorial (Matt Hartman's firm) turns his angel habit into a formal vehicle: he picks, Hartman co-approves, economics split 50/50. He does not lead or price rounds; he is a signal name on cap tables. Constraint: a full-time CEO with a ~$100k-$500k check.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Factorial money on his deals is a fund with outside LPs, not his balance sheet; he keeps half the economics: \"manage their own portfolios and evenly split the economics with Factorial\" ([Fast Company](https://web.archive.org/web/20250202060626/https://www.fastcompany.com/90881930/hugging-face-ceo-factorial)).\n- Two-key approval: \"just one investment partner and Matt have to agree in order to invest\" ([factorialcap.com](https://factorialcap.com/)).\n- The real tie is Hartman: \"Matt Hartman was the first investor in technical companies like Huggingface\" ([factorialcap.com](https://factorialcap.com/)). Factorial is the first investor's way to keep Delangue's deal flow.\n- As of 2026-11-10 the factorialcap.com team section lists only Hartman and a chief of staff; Delangue is not named there. Whether he is still active is unconfirmed. Later deals (Flower Series A, Boltz) list him separately from Factorial Capital, so check-by-check attribution is ambiguous ([Flower](https://flower.ai/blog/2024-02-15-announcing-series-a)).\n- His filter: \"Can this be useful, impactful, helpful for the community?\" ([Fast Company](https://web.archive.org/web/20250202060626/https://www.fastcompany.com/90881930/hugging-face-ceo-factorial)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. Not a $20M co-lead partner, but an early signal: he was in Perplexity (2022), Sakana, Prime Intellect, Letta, Boltz and SkyPilot seeds, often before the big leads. Useful as a referral source and a co-investor name, not as a deal-doer to court.\n\n## Background\nCo-founded Hugging Face in 2016 (Hartman at Betaworks was its first investor, per [factorialcap.com](https://factorialcap.com/)). Became Factorial's inaugural partner at launch, 2023-04-28 ([Fast Company](https://web.archive.org/web/20250202060626/https://www.fastcompany.com/90881930/hugging-face-ceo-factorial)). Earlier career and education: not checked in this pass.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://factorialcap.com/huggingfund | HuggingFund page (JS-rendered; content not read this pass) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/ClementDelangue (unconfirmed, not loaded) | |\n| LinkedIn | nothing verified | |\n| GitHub / Hugging Face | https://huggingface.co/clem | ~3.3k HF followers (2026-11-10, HF API) |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing checked this pass.\n\n## How they think\n- Open source must keep pace with closed labs; capital matters because \"startups in AI right now need compute\" ([Fast Company](https://web.archive.org/web/20250202060626/https://www.fastcompany.com/90881930/hugging-face-ceo-factorial)).\n\n## Read these first\n- Fast Company launch piece, 2023-04-28 (link above).\n- factorialcap.com \"How we work\".\n\n## Social engagement\nNot researched this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Perplexity | Seed ($3.1M) | 2022-09 | no | Elad Gil, Nat Friedman | [src](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/announcing-our-series-a-funding-round-and-mobile-app-launch) |\n| Flower Labs | Pre-seed (inferred), via Factorial | by 2023-04-28 | no | Matt Hartman | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250202060626/https://www.fastcompany.com/90881930/hugging-face-ceo-factorial) |\n| Nomic AI | Undisclosed, via Factorial | by 2023-04-28 | no | Matt Hartman | same |\n| Sakana AI | Seed ($30M) | 2024-01-16 | no | Lux, Khosla | [src](https://sakana.ai/seed-round/) |\n| Adaptive ML | Seed ($20M), as \"HuggingFund by Factorial\" | 2024-03-11 | no | Index (lead) | [src](https://fortune.com/2024/03/11/adaptive-startup-funding-falcon-ai-models-team-rlhf-rlaif/) |\n| Prime Intellect | Seed ($5.5M), then $15M (re-up) | 2024-04-23; 2025-02-28 | no | CoinFund; Founders Fund | [src](https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/fundraise) |\n| Letta | Seed ($10M) | 2024-09-23 | no | Felicis | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/letta) |\n| Phonic | Seed ($4M) | 2025-04-03 | no | Lux | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale) |\n| Sophont | Seed ($9.2M), as \"Clement Delangue (Factorial Capital)\" | 2025-09-11 | no | Kindred (lead) | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/sophont-open-foundation-models-for-medicine/) |\n| Boltz | Seed ($28M) | 2026-01-08 | no | Amplify, a16z, Factorial | [src](https://boltz.com/manifesto) |\n| SkyPilot | Seed ($20M+) | 2026-07-21 | no | Lux (lead) | [src](https://skypilot.ai/blog/skypilot-the-company) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing beyond the attributions above was found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Matt Hartman / Factorial Capital | Inaugural investment partner; Hartman co-approves | 2023-04 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250202060626/https://www.fastcompany.com/90881930/hugging-face-ceo-factorial) |\n| Matt Hartman | First investor in Hugging Face (Betaworks) | 2016 | [src](https://factorialcap.com/) |\n| Lux Capital | Portfolio CEO; co-invests in Lux seeds (Sakana, Phonic, SkyPilot) | 2019 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-2nd-ai-summit-puts-nyc-on-the-ai-map) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is he still an active Factorial partner? Not on the 2026 team section.\n- Which later checks (Boltz, Flower Series A) are Factorial money versus personal?\n\n## Sources\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20250202060626/https://www.fastcompany.com/90881930/hugging-face-ceo-factorial\n- https://factorialcap.com/ and https://factorialcap.com/api/content\n- https://flower.ai/blog/2024-02-15-announcing-series-a\n- https://kindredventures.com/announcement/sophont-open-foundation-models-for-medicine/\n- https://fortune.com/2024/03/11/adaptive-startup-funding-falcon-ai-models-team-rlhf-rlaif/\n- https://huggingface.co/api/users/clem/overview\n", "person:clifford-platt": "\n# Clifford Platt (former NVIDIA)\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. **Duplicate slug warning:** `people/clifford-platt.md` already profiles the same person (same GCV source, same NVIDIA role). This file was requested under the slug `clifford-platt-former`. It repeats that profile and adds the Stage 2 Capital findings (headshot, CFA, banking background, LP seat). The main session should add `clifford-platt-former,clifford-platt` to `data/slug_aliases.csv` or fold this file into the canonical one. \"Senior director\" is mapped to seniority `vp` as the closest allowed value. That is a judgment call, not his title. Pronouns: GCV uses \"he\".*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is a departed mid-level operator from NVIDIA's strategic, compute-tied investing pool, and nobody has publicly replaced him. Track him as a relationship node that could explain how NVIDIA structured its 2024-25 lab and neocloud checks. He is not a check-writer to cultivate today.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he mattered.** He was one of the very few named people in NVIDIA's corporate-development investing pool, the pool behind the strategic, gigawatt-tied checks. It is separate from NVentures (see [firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md)). He held the investments seat from 2023 to July 2025 under Vishal Bhagwati ([GCV 2025-07-14](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md)). That window covers NVIDIA's move from late strategic follower to participant in lab rounds ([ledger](../sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md)). Placing him in that shift is inferred from the dates. No source credits him with it.\n- **What he personally signs or sources.** As far as any source shows, nothing. Corp-dev decisions sat with Bhagwati, and The Information reports Huang \"often sets prices himself\" ([Dealroom summary, 2026-10-07](https://dealroom.co/news/info-i81usb-where-nvidias-100-billion-dealmaking-juggernaut-will-go-next/)). A senior director in that structure worked deals rather than signing them. His sourcing edge, by role history, was VC relationships: he was hired into NVIDIA \"as head of venture capital business development\" ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md)). In other words, his job was to be the person VCs called to bring NVIDIA into rounds.\n- **The constraint.** He was a corporate employee spending balance-sheet money on a strategic mandate with no returns target. Siddeek says corp dev lacks \"a returns focus\" ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)). He never had a fund of his own.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred).** Lead a priced seed. Nothing in his record shows him leading anything. His only known personal investing is as an LP in someone else's fund (below).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He is a personal LP in a GTM-focused venture fund.** Stage 2 Capital lists him on its team pages under the \"Venture LP\" heading. The listing still shows his NVIDIA title: \"Senior Director, Corp Dev - Investments, NVIDIA\" ([Stage 2 Capital](https://www.stage2.capital/team/clifford-platt), checked 2026-11-10). Stage 2 calls its network \"1000+ GTM operators who invest\" (same site). This is the only evidence of him writing his own money. It goes into a B2B software and sales fund, not into frontier AI.\n- **The listing is stale.** Sixteen months after he left NVIDIA (2025-07), the LP page still shows the NVIDIA title. Combined with three searches that found no new employer, this suggests he has not publicly announced a new full-time role. That conclusion is an inference, and LinkedIn was not checked.\n- **His background is capital markets, not engineering or venture.** Stage 2 describes him as a \"CFA charterholder with investment banking experience in the technology, media and telecommunications space\", charterholder \"since 2003\" ([Stage 2 Capital](https://www.stage2.capital/team/clifford-platt)). This fills the gap before AWS. With roughly five years at Amazon to 2019 ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md)), the banking years fall roughly between the early 2000s and about 2014. That matches his \"25-year career\". For a founder, it means his instinct is deal structuring and valuation, the skills that compute-for-equity tranches need.\n- **He calls NVIDIA the high point of his career.** \"My time at Nvidia has been the most consequential of my 25-year career.\" He also said leaving \"was not an easy decision\" ([GCV 2025-07-14](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md)).\n- **No successor is visible.** Three searches on 2026-11-10 found no named successor in the corp-dev investments seat. The firm file's people table has no one between Bhagwati and Huang on the strategic side. For a founder, the working layer of NVIDIA's $1B+ strategic pool has no public face.\n- **Hyperscaler-VC pipeline.** He moved from AWS VC business development to NVIDIA in 2019. NVIDIA later hired Howard Wright from the AWS startup program in 2024 (firm file). Another Stage 2 LP listed beside him is Morgan Clark of AWS ([Stage 2](https://www.stage2.capital/team/clifford-platt)). This is a small but repeated AWS-alumni pattern. It rests on a few data points.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low** (2026-11-10). He is not an active investor in frontier AI that can be verified, he is not named on any round, his current role is unknown, and his only known LP seat is in a GTM and B2B fund. He would rise to **medium** as an informant or relationship lead if he turns up at a lab, a neocloud or a fund writing into big seeds. He would know how NVIDIA's strategic tranches get approved and structured: the OpenAI per-gigawatt staging, xAI's GPU-lease SPV and the Crusoe series (firm file). He is not an LP prospect for you on current evidence, since he writes small personal LP checks into operator-network funds. Deals that matter to you from his era, none attributed to him: Cohere, Poolside (Series B, 2024-10), Crusoe (Series D, 2024-12), OpenAI (2024-10) and xAI (Series C, 2024-12).\n\n**How a founder reaches him:** there is no public channel. The routes are the LinkedIn profile linked from Stage 2 ([linkedin.com/in/clifford-platt-cfa-9a885a](https://www.linkedin.com/in/clifford-platt-cfa-9a885a), not opened) or an intro through Stage 2 Capital's GP team. To reach NVIDIA's strategic pool itself, he is no longer the door: go through the lead investor, or through NVentures first and then corp dev (see [people/vishal-bhagwati.md](vishal-bhagwati.md)).\n\n## Background\n- CFA charterholder since 2003. Investment banking in technology, media and telecommunications; firms and dates not given ([Stage 2 Capital](https://www.stage2.capital/team/clifford-platt)).\n- Amazon, nearly five years, ending as \"venture capital business development principal\" at AWS ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md)).\n- NVIDIA, 2019: head of venture capital business development. \"A few months later\" he moved to strategic venture investments ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md)).\n- NVIDIA, 2023: Senior Director, Corporate Development (Investments), under Vishal Bhagwati ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md)).\n- Left NVIDIA, reported 2025-07-14. New role not disclosed then or found since ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md)).\n- Venture LP, Stage 2 Capital. Commitment date and fund vintage not given ([Stage 2 Capital](https://www.stage2.capital/team/clifford-platt)).\n- Education: not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [Stage 2 Capital LP page](https://www.stage2.capital/team/clifford-platt) | Venture LP listing with headshot; title not updated since NVIDIA; 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | searched 2026-11-10 |\n| LinkedIn | [clifford-platt-cfa-9a885a](https://www.linkedin.com/in/clifford-platt-cfa-9a885a) | linked from the Stage 2 page; not opened |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Searches on 2026-11-10 for his name with NVIDIA, with venture and board-observer terms, and with CFA and appointment terms turned up only the GCV piece and the Stage 2 page.\n\n## How they think\n- Nothing first-hand beyond the two departure quotes. He has no stated theses, contrarian views or calls.\n- What can be read from his choices: he committed his own money to an operator-network GTM fund, not an AI fund. That points to a B2B software, sales-led view of his own portfolio. This is inference from a single data point.\n\n## Read these first\n- [GCV, \"Platt leaves investor role at Nvidia\" (2025-07-14)](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md): his career path and the only quotes.\n- [Stage 2 Capital LP page](https://www.stage2.capital/team/clifford-platt): the CFA and banking background, plus the LP seat.\n- [firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md): the three-pool structure he worked inside.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| None personally attributed | | | | | GCV lists NVIDIA's activity in his final year without crediting him: Cohere, Poolside and Crusoe directly, plus OpenAI and xAI rounds ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md)) |\n\nThe rows below are context, not his deals. They show NVIDIA's timing on the companies GCV names, all later-stage:\n- Poolside: Series B, 2024-10-02, after the 2023 seed.\n- Crusoe: Series D, 2024-12-12, about five years after its seed.\n- OpenAI: $6.6B round, 2024-10.\n- xAI: Series C, 2024-12-23.\n\nNone of these is a seed. NVIDIA was never first in ([ledger](../sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md); firm file). Personal LP position: Stage 2 Capital (fund and vintage not stated).\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. No source describes his role in any specific transaction.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NVIDIA | VC business development, then strategic venture investments, then Sr Dir Corp Dev (Investments) | 2019 to 2025-07 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md) |\n| Vishal Bhagwati | Promoted under him (corp-dev chief) | 2023 to 2025-07 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md) |\n| Amazon / AWS | Venture capital business development principal | ~2014 to 2019 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md) |\n| Stage 2 Capital | Venture LP (personal) | not given | [Stage 2 Capital](https://www.stage2.capital/team/clifford-platt) |\n| Morgan Clark (AWS) | Fellow Stage 2 LP listed on the same page | not given | [Stage 2 Capital](https://www.stage2.capital/team/clifford-platt) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Where did he go after July 2025? The stale Stage 2 title suggests no announced move. LinkedIn would answer this.\n- Which of the 2024-25 strategic deals did he work on: Cohere, Poolside, Crusoe, OpenAI or xAI?\n- Who holds the corp-dev investments seat now? Three searches found no one.\n- Which Stage 2 fund and vintage is he in, and how big is the commitment? Not disclosed.\n- Which bank did he work at before AWS?\n\n## Sources\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md (2025-07-14)\n- https://www.stage2.capital/team/clifford-platt (checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md\n- https://dealroom.co/news/info-i81usb-where-nvidias-100-billion-dealmaking-juggernaut-will-go-next/\n- ../firms/nvidia.md, ../sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md, ./clifford-platt.md, ./vishal-bhagwati.md\n", "person:colin-beirne": "\n# Colin Beirne\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. Sources: his Deviation team page, Deviation's launch post, Deviation's Form ADV (CRD 343210, filed 2026-06-26, read in full text), SEC Form D filings (Walden Robotics, Clara/Brella Insurance, Steadily, Socure, a Swarm Aero SPV), company releases (Osmo, Swarm Aero, Walden Robotics), Pulse 2.0 launch coverage, and the existing repo file `firms/two-sigma-ventures.md`. Pronouns: his Deviation bio uses \"his\" [src](https://deviation.com/team/colin-beirne). The web search budget was used up before this pass started, and web.archive.org could not be fetched, so no podcast, X post or LinkedIn text was checked. Facts from the archived TSV bio come from rows already in the repo.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe built Two Sigma's venture arm over about 14 years, took it independent as Deviation in April 2026, and now personally controls the firm. In robotics he is the partner who signs and sits on boards, most recently Walden Robotics' $300M seed [src](https://waldenrobotics.com/news/walden-robotics-launches-from-stealth.md), [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/343210/PDF/343210.pdf).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he is the founder and the control person, not one partner among several. Deviation's ADV lists him at ownership code D (50 to 75%) of both Deviation Capital Advisers LP and Deviation Capital GP LLC. The other three managing members (Perovic, Golden, Sands-Ramshaw) are each under 5% [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/343210/PDF/343210.pdf). He also brings Two Sigma's technical bench: a council of seven senior Two Sigma leaders helps with sourcing and diligence [src](https://deviation.com/updates/introducing-deviation).\n- **What constrains him:** the new core fund is small. Deviation V had $141.4M gross assets from 26 beneficial owners when the ADV was filed [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/343210/PDF/343210.pdf). Large rounds like Walden's therefore need co-leads (Toyota) and possibly SPVs. Deviation runs \"Deviate\" co-invest vehicles, but none is tied to Walden in a filing (firms/two-sigma-ventures.md).\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** lead a late-stage frontier-lab round. Deviation says it invests at Seed and Series A [src](https://deviation.com/updates/introducing-deviation). His own stated style is to defer to founders [src](https://deviation.com/team/colin-beirne).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He controls Deviation; this isn't a four-way partnership.** The ADV's indirect-owner schedule codes him \"D - 50% but less than 75%\" in both the adviser LP and the GP LLC. The other founding partners are listed only as direct managing members under 5%. Two Sigma does not appear anywhere in the readable ADV text, although the launch post calls Two Sigma \"collaborators and investors\" [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/343210/PDF/343210.pdf), [src](https://deviation.com/updates/introducing-deviation).\n- **Walden Robotics is his deal, and now it's sourced.** Walden's own launch release names \"Colin Beirne, Founding Partner of Deviation Capital\" with a quote: \"Walden sits right at the center of that shift.\" Toyota and Deviation co-led [src](https://waldenrobotics.com/news/walden-robotics-launches-from-stealth.md). Before this, the attribution rested only on his being the sole non-founder director on the Form D [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2144409/000123191926000765/0001231919-26-000765-index.htm).\n- **His longest documented board seat is insurtech, not AI.** He is a director of Clara Insurance, renamed Brella Insurance (CIK 1798594), on Form Ds from 2020 through 2023. The 2023 filing shows $28.7M sold, first sale 2023-11-13 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1798594/000179859423000001/xslFormDX01/primary_doc.xml). The 2021 filing misspells his surname as \"Beire\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1798594/000179859421000002/primary_doc.xml). This fits \"Insurance Tech\" among his listed focus areas [src](https://deviation.com/team/colin-beirne).\n- **\"Managed by\" means custody, and filings back that up.** His Deviation page lists WHOOP, Etched, Steadily, Bedrock Robotics, Osmo and Socure as \"Select investments made by the TSV team prior to Deviation and managed by Colin\" [src](https://deviation.com/team/colin-beirne). None of the Steadily Form Ds (2021, 2023) or Socure Form Ds (2017, 2021) list him as a related person [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1908664/000190866423000001/0001908664-23-000001-index.htm), [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1566310/000123191921000083/0001231919-21-000083-index.htm).\n- **On selection, in his words:** \"truly exceptional companies in this space are rare and hard to spot\" [src](https://pulse2.com/deviation-capital-launches-with-2-billion-in-aum-following-spinout-from-two-sigma/). On his role: \"A VC's job should be a humble one\" [src](https://deviation.com/team/colin-beirne).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He matters most for **AI robotics at seed scale**. He co-led Walden Robotics' $300M seed at a $1.1B valuation, the kind of very large seed the owner writes $20M checks into, and he sits on the board [src](https://waldenrobotics.com/news/walden-robotics-launches-from-stealth.md). He also led Osmo's B (AI olfaction) and co-led Swarm Aero's A (defense drones). He is not a pre-training or frontier-lab investor. Etched (AI chips) is only in his custody, and no source says he originated it. As the controlling GP of a ~$141M core fund, he is a plausible co-lead partner on robotics seeds where the owner's $20M fills out the round. For the owner, the better junior contacts in the same book are Joe Botsch and Rohit Rao (firms/two-sigma-ventures.md).\n\n## Background\n- Born in Ireland and raised in the San Francisco Bay Area. He interned at startups such as Andromedia in the late 1990s, and a summer at DoubleClick first brought him to New York [src](https://deviation.com/team/colin-beirne).\n- Dual BA in Economics and in Law, Jurisprudence and Social Thought from Amherst College (years not stated) [src](https://deviation.com/team/colin-beirne).\n- Technology investment banker and corporate strategist (dates and employers not found) [src](https://deviation.com/team/colin-beirne).\n- Two Sigma, business strategy, from 2005. He began investing in private assets in 2007 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/profile/colin-beirne/) (as recorded in the repo; Wayback was not fetchable this pass).\n- Founded Two Sigma Ventures. His TSV bio says he started building it in 2011 and his Deviation bio says 2012. TSV's ADV lists him as Partner from 07/2018 (firms/two-sigma-ventures.md).\n- 04/2026: managing member of Deviation Capital, CRD 6956942 [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/343210/PDF/343210.pdf). Deviation launched publicly 2026-05-04 [src](https://deviation.com/updates/introducing-deviation).\n- **How he got the seat:** he built the venture business inside Two Sigma and then led it out. He did not join an existing partnership.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://deviation.com/team/colin-beirne | Bio, focus areas and \"managed by\" list; checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | TSV blog posts are on the dead twosigmaventures.com domain (Wayback only) |\n| X | https://x.com/csbeirne | Linked from his Deviation page; the account itself was not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/colin-beirne-526577/ | Linked from his Deviation page; not checked |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Search budget exhausted |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** through Deviation's New York office. His page lists focus areas of Manufacturing/Industrials, Robotics, Insurance Tech and Consumer, so robotics and industrial-AI pitches fit [src](https://deviation.com/team/colin-beirne). Joe Botsch and Rohit Rao are listed on Swarm Aero and Bedrock, so they are the likely first screen for robotics deals (firms/two-sigma-ventures.md). No intro path is documented. Co-investors seen next to him: Toyota (Walden), Silent Ventures (Swarm Aero), Lux and GV (Osmo).\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found (search budget exhausted before this pass; not checked).\n\n## How they think\n- **Data plus compute is the thesis, and \"AI\" is the new name for it.** He says what the market now calls AI was TSV's founding thesis [src](https://pulse2.com/deviation-capital-launches-with-2-billion-in-aum-following-spinout-from-two-sigma/).\n- **New AI modalities and embodied AI:** Osmo is \"unlocking an entirely new sensory dimension for AI\" [src](https://www.intelligentcio.com/north-america/2026/02/04/osmo-raises-us70m-series-b-funding-to-scale-ai-powered-olfaction-technology/). Walden sits on the shift toward \"a new generation of adaptable robots\" [src](https://waldenrobotics.com/news/walden-robotics-launches-from-stealth.md).\n- **Team-first, fast conviction:** on Swarm Aero, \"from our first meeting\" he saw unmatched technical depth [src](https://digitalmedianet.com/swarm-aero-announces-35m-series-a-financing-led-by-two-sigma-ventures-and-silent-ventures/amp/).\n- **Defers to founders** and sees his value as a zoomed-out view [src](https://deviation.com/team/colin-beirne).\n\n## Read these first\n- Deviation team page: his bio, his stated view of the VC role, and the \"managed by\" list [src](https://deviation.com/team/colin-beirne)\n- Introducing Deviation (2026-05-04): the spin-out, the Two Sigma council, Seed/A focus [src](https://deviation.com/updates/introducing-deviation)\n- Walden Robotics launch release: his deal and his quote [src](https://waldenrobotics.com/news/walden-robotics-launches-from-stealth.md)\n- Deviation Form ADV, Schedules A/B and Section 7.B.1: who owns the firm and how big the funds are [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/343210/PDF/343210.pdf)\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked (X not fetchable, search budget exhausted).\n\n## Portfolio\nOnly deals where a source names him personally. \"Managed by\" holdings are listed separately below.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Osmo (AI olfaction) | Series A ($60M) | 2022 | TSV post calls him \"our lead investor\"; Osmo names Lux, GV and Alexa Fund without lead roles (**unresolved**) | Lux Capital, GV, Alexa Fund | [TSV post](https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/how-osmos-scientist-founder-is-giving-computers-a-sense-of-smell/) |\n| Osmo | Series B ($70M) | 2026-02-04 | Yes (TSV led); he joined as board observer. **Re-up: first in at the A** | Valor, Atreides, Amplo, Collab Fund, Patrick Collison | [src](https://www.intelligentcio.com/north-america/2026/02/04/osmo-raises-us70m-series-b-funding-to-scale-ai-powered-olfaction-technology/) |\n| Swarm Aero (defense UAVs) | Series A ($35M) | 2026-03-10 | Co-led with Silent Ventures; quoted | Silent Ventures | [src](https://digitalmedianet.com/swarm-aero-announces-35m-series-a-financing-led-by-two-sigma-ventures-and-silent-ventures/amp/) |\n| Walden Robotics | Seed ($300M at $1.1B) | 2026-07-15 announced (Form D first sale 2026-05-26) | Co-led by Deviation and Toyota; he is quoted and is the only non-founder director | Toyota, NVIDIA, AE Industrial, Samsung, Prologis, CoreWeave, Lenovo | [release](https://waldenrobotics.com/news/walden-robotics-launches-from-stealth.md), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2144409/000123191926000765/0001231919-26-000765-index.htm) |\n| Clara / Brella Insurance (non-AI) | Undisclosed; Form Ds 2020 ($2.6M), 2021 ($13.9M), 2023 ($28.7M) | 2020-03 to 2023-11 | Not stated; director on all three | not stated | [2020](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1798594/000179859420000008/primary_doc.xml), [2023](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1798594/000179859423000001/xslFormDX01/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n**Custody only (\"managed by Colin at Deviation\"; origination not shown):** WHOOP, Etched, Steadily, Bedrock Robotics, Osmo, Socure [src](https://deviation.com/team/colin-beirne).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Osmo:** TSV came in at the 2022 Series A and led the 2026 Series B about four years later, when he took a board observer seat. That makes it a re-up, not a new entry [src](https://www.intelligentcio.com/north-america/2026/02/04/osmo-raises-us70m-series-b-funding-to-scale-ai-powered-olfaction-technology/).\n- **Walden Robotics:** this was Deviation's first big new deal after the spin-out. It came out of stealth with a $300M seed co-led by Deviation and Toyota, and he is the only non-founder director on the Form D. Russ Tedrake is CEO [src](https://waldenrobotics.com/news/walden-robotics-launches-from-stealth.md), [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2144409/000123191926000765/0001231919-26-000765-index.htm). The intro path is not documented.\n- **Swarm Aero:** he says conviction came at the first meeting. A Sydecar SPV (\"Swarm Aero Sep 2025 a Series of CGF2021\") also filed a $156.9K Form D in 2025, but it has no link to TSV [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2098475/000209847525000001/0002098475-25-000001-index.htm).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Deviation Capital | Founding Partner; managing member; 50-75% owner of the adviser LP and GP LLC | 2026-04 | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/343210/PDF/343210.pdf) |\n| Two Sigma Ventures | Founder and Partner | 2011/2012 to 2026-04 | [src](https://deviation.com/team/colin-beirne) |\n| Two Sigma | Business strategy; private investing from 2007 | 2005 | repo row (TSV bio) |\n| Dusan Perovic, Jonathan Golden, Sidney Sands-Ramshaw (Costabile) | Co-managing members at Deviation | 2026-04 | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/343210/PDF/343210.pdf) |\n| Walden Robotics | Director | 2026 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2144409/000123191926000765/0001231919-26-000765-index.htm) |\n| Brella Insurance (ex-Clara) | Director; CEO Veer Gidwaney | 2020 to at least 2023-11 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1798594/000179859423000001/xslFormDX01/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Osmo | Board observer | 2026-02 | [src](https://www.intelligentcio.com/north-america/2026/02/04/osmo-raises-us70m-series-b-funding-to-scale-ai-powered-olfaction-technology/) |\n| Lenore Champagne Beirne | **Not him.** Managing member of the GP of Bright Ventures Fund I. The repo's earlier \"Bright Ventures (Beirne)\" hit was this person; any family relationship is unconfirmed | n/a | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1865992/000186599222000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What size check did Deviation write into Walden, and did a Deviate co-invest SPV carry part of it?\n- Is Two Sigma an LP in Deviation V, or does it hold an economic stake below the ADV's reporting thresholds? The launch post says \"investors\" but the ADV shows no Two Sigma entity.\n- Who originated Etched and Bedrock at TSV? Vin Sachidananda's pages list Etched, and Rohit Rao's list Bedrock.\n- Podcast and X commentary: none checked this pass.\n\n## Sources\n- https://deviation.com/team/colin-beirne\n- https://deviation.com/updates/introducing-deviation\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/343210/PDF/343210.pdf\n- https://waldenrobotics.com/news/walden-robotics-launches-from-stealth.md\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2144409/000123191926000765/0001231919-26-000765-index.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1798594/000179859420000008/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1798594/000179859421000002/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1798594/000179859423000001/xslFormDX01/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1908664/000190866423000001/0001908664-23-000001-index.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1566310/000123191921000083/0001231919-21-000083-index.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1865992/000186599222000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2098475/000209847525000001/0002098475-25-000001-index.htm\n- https://pulse2.com/deviation-capital-launches-with-2-billion-in-aum-following-spinout-from-two-sigma/\n- https://www.intelligentcio.com/north-america/2026/02/04/osmo-raises-us70m-series-b-funding-to-scale-ai-powered-olfaction-technology/\n- https://digitalmedianet.com/swarm-aero-announces-35m-series-a-financing-led-by-two-sigma-ventures-and-silent-ventures/amp/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/how-osmos-scientist-founder-is-giving-computers-a-sense-of-smell/\n", "person:colin-delea": "\n# Colin Delea\n\n## One-line take\nDelea is Matt Nordby's number two, a PE-trained modeler who priced Lion Capital's SPAC deals with them and followed them to Flume as GP. They are the person to reach after Nordby, but no AI round names them yet.\n\n## Crux\n- Delea is a deal-mechanics operator, not a public sourcer. In SEC filings they appear drafting valuation waterfalls and joining diligence calls under Nordby [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000119312521217964/d192826ds4.htm).\n- The \"General Partner\" title carries no control. Flume's ADV and all four Form Ds name only Nordby, who owns the management company through Leykwise, LLC [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/326134/PDF/326134.pdf). Delea can work a deal up, but Nordby decides.\n- **Correction to the hypothesis.** Every transaction a primary source ties Delea to is non-AI: Rize Education (ed-tech board seat), Local Bounti (indoor agriculture SPAC) and World View (stratospheric balloon SPAC process). The idea that they run Flume's AI-compute deals rests only on a photo with Hydra Host's CEO. That is **unconfirmed**.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Priced Local Bounti.** As a Lion associate, Delea sent the revised valuation waterfall and sources and uses to Local Bounti's counsel on 2021-06-10: \"Mr. Delea sent to Orrick and Local Bounti a proposed revised valuation waterfall\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000119312521217964/d192826ds4.htm). They were also at the first management meeting (2021-03-02) and on merger-agreement calls with Nordby.\n- **Nordby's team has been the same two people since 2021.** The Leo Holdings II S-4 (World View) describes \"Mr. Nordby and two other principals of Leo, Robert King and Colin Delea\" pitching World View on a pivot from tourism to remote sensing in 2022-11 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1824153/000119312523094720/d436135ds4.htm). The S-4/A filings ran to 2023-09. Whether the deal closed is not shown in the sources fetched.\n- **The only filed board seat of any Flume person.** Rize Education's Form D (filed 2025-01-15, $12.0M sold, 16 investors) lists \"Colin Delea\" with \"Relationship: Director\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1848462/000184846225000001/primary_doc.xml). The 2023-09 Form D did not list them [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1848462/000184846223000001/primary_doc.xml), so they took the seat with the 2024-12 raise. Another director on the same filing is \"Kareem Zaki\". Whether that is Thrive Capital's Kareem Zaki (`kareem-zaki` in this repo) is **unconfirmed**.\n- **No seat on Flume's AI names.** The 2025 Hydra Host Form Ds list only Aaron Ginn and Garrett Johnson as directors [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1847209/000184720925000003/primary_doc.xml). The Positron Form Ds of 2025-07 and 2026-05 list Agrawal, Sohmers, Reid and Schottenstein [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2077932/000207793226000002/primary_doc.xml). Flume is a non-board check in its AI book.\n- **The aggregator's dates are wrong.** It says Delea was a Lion principal 2021-23. Archived Lion team pages instead show Delea absent on 2020-12-07, ASSOCIATE from 2021-04-17 to 2022-01-28, and PRINCIPAL from 2022-07-05 to 2023-02-05 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20210417122042/https://lioncapital.com/team/) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20220705224351/https://lioncapital.com/team/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** For promotion speed they fit: associate to GP in about 2.5 years [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20220705224351/https://lioncapital.com/team/). For the owner's purpose they fit less well:\n- Flume's funds are small ($230.4M gross across two funds per the ADV), and Flume joins AI rounds as a participant (Positron, Hydra Host, Parasail). Delea is not a $10M+ lead-check writer.\n- Their authority is delegated from Nordby.\n- No AI deal names them.\n\nThe value is as a co-investor contact into the Nevada/Tahoe AI-compute cluster (Positron in Reno, Hydra Host) and as a junior who will do the work on a syndicate. The AI deals that matter, all at the firm level with no person named: Positron Seed, Series A, Series B and Series C; Hydra Host Series A (2026); Parasail Series A (2026).\n\n## Background\n- Harvard University, undergraduate, 2017 (aggregator) [src](https://www.zonebourse.com/insider/COLIN-DELEA-A37FSY/). Roles from 2017 to 2021 are **unknown**.\n- Lion Capital (Americas), New York: Associate by 2021-04, Principal by 2022-07 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20210417122042/https://lioncapital.com/team/) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20220705224351/https://lioncapital.com/team/). They worked on the Leo Holdings SPACs alongside Lion founder Lyndon Lea and partner Matt Nordby [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000119312521217964/d192826ds4.htm). They were gone from Lion's team page by 2023-09-25 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230925072919/https://lioncapital.com/team/).\n- Flume Ventures, General Partner, from 2023-09 (aggregator date) [src](https://www.zonebourse.com/insider/COLIN-DELEA-A37FSY/) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240912121823/https://flume.ventures/community-2/). They got the seat by following Nordby out of Lion. Nordby launched Flume in 2023 [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-04-04/lion-capital-s-nordby-starts-flume-ventures-seeks-100-million).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.flume.ventures/about | Only an event photo caption as of 2026-11-10; the current site has no team bio |\n| Substack / blog | None found | |\n| X | None found | |\n| LinkedIn | Not verified | The aggregator shows 97 first-degree relations |\n| GitHub | None | |\n| Podcasts (host) | None | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- None found. Flume's about page shows Delea with Hydra Host CEO Aaron Ginn at \"Flume's Partner Summit Speaker Series\" [src](https://www.flume.ventures/about).\n\n## How they think\n- No public writing or talks were found. From the filings: valuation structure, sources and uses, and merger-agreement mechanics, which is PE and SPAC craft.\n\n## Read these first\n- Leo Holdings III S-4, \"Background of the Business Combination\" (Local Bounti) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000119312521217964/d192826ds4.htm)\n- Leo Holdings II S-4, background section (World View) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1824153/000119312523094720/d436135ds4.htm)\n- Rize Education Form D 2025-01-15 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1848462/000184846225000001/primary_doc.xml)\n\n## Social engagement\nNo public social presence found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Rize Education | Undisclosed ($12.0M sold; Form D) | 2024-12-31 | not disclosed; Delea is a director | not disclosed | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1848462/000184846225000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Local Bounti | IPO (de-SPAC with Leo Holdings III) | 2021-06 (merger agreement) | sponsor-side deal team (Lion) | Leo Holdings III | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000119312521217964/d192826ds4.htm) |\n| World View | merger process with Leo Holdings II; completion not confirmed | 2022-11 to 2023-09 | sponsor-side deal team (Lion) | | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1824153/000119312523094720/d436135ds4.htm) |\n\nNo AI deal names Delea personally. Flume's AI rounds (Positron Seed/A/B/C, Hydra Host Series A, Parasail Series A) are recorded under `flume-ventures`.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Local Bounti (2021).** Nordby screened the company with Nomura Greentech. Delea joined the 2021-03-02 management video call with Lea, Darwent and Nordby, then the 2021-03-14 unit-economics session. On 2021-06-10 they sent the revised waterfall, which counted shares issued on convertible-note conversion and added consideration for cash at close [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000119312521217964/d192826ds4.htm).\n- **Hydra Host.** Flume's site shows Delea with CEO Aaron Ginn at the Partner Summit Speaker Series [src](https://www.flume.ventures/about). That shows a relationship only. No round announcement or Form D names Delea.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Matt Nordby (matt-nordby) | Senior partner at Lion/Leo and now at Flume; Delea's principal | 2021 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000119312521217964/d192826ds4.htm) |\n| Lyndon Lea (lyndon-lea) | Lion Capital / Leo CEO on the Local Bounti deal team | 2021 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000119312521217964/d192826ds4.htm) |\n| Robert King (robert-king) | Fellow Lion principal on World View | 2022 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1824153/000119312523094720/d436135ds4.htm) |\n| Aaron Ginn (aaron-ginn) | Pictured together at Flume's Partner Summit | unknown | [src](https://www.flume.ventures/about) |\n| Rize Education (rize-education) | Director | 2024-12 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1848462/000184846225000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is Rize's director Kareem Zaki the same person as Thrive Capital's Kareem Zaki? If so, Delea sits on a board with Thrive.\n- What did Delea do between 2017 and 2021, before Lion? Banking is likely, but that is **unconfirmed**.\n- Did Delea source or price Flume's Positron or Hydra Host checks? Nothing public says.\n- How a founder reaches them: no direct channel was found. The paths are Nordby, the Partner Summit Speaker Series in Incline Village, or Rize's board. All are **unconfirmed** as routes that work.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000119312521217964/d192826ds4.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1824153/000119312523094720/d436135ds4.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1848462/000184846225000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1848462/000184846223000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1847209/000184720925000003/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2077932/000207793226000002/primary_doc.xml\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20210417122042/https://lioncapital.com/team/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20220705224351/https://lioncapital.com/team/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20230925072919/https://lioncapital.com/team/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20240912121823/https://flume.ventures/community-2/\n- https://www.flume.ventures/about\n- https://www.zonebourse.com/insider/COLIN-DELEA-A37FSY/\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/326134/PDF/326134.pdf\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-04-04/lion-capital-s-nordby-starts-flume-ventures-seeks-100-million\n", "person:corey-nobile": "\n# Corey Nobile\n\n## One-line take\nCorey Nobile is an almost invisible husband-and-wife GP who has grown from $1,000-minimum syndicates (2023) into a lead investor and board director at Fab2 (ex-Atomic Semi). The Fab2 round sold $556M ([fab2.com](https://fab2.com/), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2151752/000215175226000002/primary_doc.xml)). He is the clearest example in the repo of a non-lead aggregator turning into a lead in frontier compute.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He gets in early on deeptech carve-outs and hardware founders, then keeps re-upping through single-company vehicles as each company re-prices. Standard Nuclear and Fab2 show the pattern most clearly: in both he was among the first money and then doubled down (see Portfolio). He also turns allocations in hot rounds into large LP vehicles that institutions buy: Mercor ME-003 sold $118.5M and OLIX OLX-002 sold $82.1M, and StepStone's registered fund holds six Fundomo vehicles ([N-PORT](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1918642/000119312526376298/ea0303531-04_nportex.htm)).\n- **What constrains him.** In the AI-lab and AI-app names (Etched, Mercor, Flapping Airplanes, Sakana, OLIX) he participates rather than leads, so his allocation depends on the lead and the founders. Decision-making is narrow: filings name only him and his wife as GP managers. He has no public voice, so founders who have not met him have nothing to go on.\n- **What he'd never do (inference).** Build a content brand. I found no podcast, Substack or interview. His personal site has no bio, just lists, and ends with \"Side Quests: Eradicating ticks\" ([nobile.com](https://nobile.com/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He now leads, and sits on the board, in frontier chipmaking.** fab2.com lists its round as \"led by fundomo with naval ravikant\" ([fab2.com](https://fab2.com/), checked 2026-11-10). The Form D lists Nobile as a Director alongside Sam Zeloof and Jim Keller: $556,021,222 sold to 89 investors, first sale 2026-08-21 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2151752/000215175226000002/primary_doc.xml)). Fundomo's first vehicle in 2023 was \"AT Fundomo Mana Ventures Fund I\", which sold $3.57M to 392 investors ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1964393/000196439323000003/primary_doc.xml)). If AT = Atomic Semi (my inference; fundomo.com dates Atomic Semi to 2023), he went from retail syndicate to round lead in the same company in about 3.5 years.\n- **Standard Nuclear was a founding check and roughly 30x on paper at the IPO price.** Fundomo was one of four SAFE investors in Dec 2024, when the company was formed around assets bought out of the Ultra Safe Nuclear bankruptcy auction. The SAFEs converted to Seed-1 at $0.50, and Fundomo got 14,000,000 shares. It then put $10.0M into the Series A (Aug 2025) and $10.0M into the A-2 (Jan 2026) ([424B4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086716/000121390026078747/ea0276071-13.htm)). The IPO priced at $15.00 per share. My arithmetic: about 19.9M shares worth about $298M at the IPO price, on about $27M of cost.\n- **His voting control is narrower than \"sole GP\" implies.** He is \"the sole member of SN-001 GP and SN-002 GP\". But voting and dispositive power over the syndicate vehicle ST-1014 Fund I was \"delegated on an irrevocable basis\" to an unaffiliated adviser ([Form 4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084264/000208426426000006/primary_doc.xml)). That vehicle is a series of Fundomo Syndicates, administered by Belltower, and Joshua Cowdin signs for Fund GP, LLC ([424B4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086716/000121390026078747/ea0276071-13.htm)). The split: he controls the \"Fundomo XX-00N, LP\" vehicles himself, but not the syndicate series.\n- **A Fundomo vehicle with no \"Fundomo\" in its name.** \"Who Is John Galt, LP\" names Corey Nobile and Siranush Babakhanova as Managing Members of the GP. It sold $28.26M to 8 investors with a $100,000 minimum; first sale 2025-08-12 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084166/000208416625000001/primary_doc.xml)). StepStone's registered fund bought into it on 2025-08-22 at a cost of $9.36M ([N-PORT](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1918642/000119312526376298/ea0303531-04_nportex.htm)). This resolves the \"manager unknown\" on StepStone's John Galt position. The underlying company is not disclosed. One more filing artifact: the Fundomo ET001 (Etched) Form D was submitted under the same filer CIK (accession 0002084166-25-000002) on the same day.\n- **Vehicle digits look like deal-open dates (inference).** ST-1014's first close was in Dec 2024, consistent with a 14 Oct 2024 open ahead of the USNC auction. ET-0406's first sale was 2024-04-24 and ME-1217's was 2025-01-21. If the reading holds, he was working Standard Nuclear about two months before its SAFE.\n- **He presents personal and firm books separately.** nobile.com splits \"Fundomo Portfolio\" (Atomic Semi, Etched, Mercor, Flapping Airplanes, Ahead Computing, Tutor Intelligence, Sakana AI, Glyphic, Synex Medical) from \"Personal Investments\" (Anduril, Erebor, Jaza Energy, Discord, Colossal, Biofire, Loyal, Neros) ([nobile.com](https://nobile.com/)). fundomo.com lists Anduril, Erebor, Jaza Energy and Neros as firm portfolio ([fundomo.com](https://www.fundomo.com)). Jaza Energy is dated 2015, his earliest dated investment.\n- **The Tutor Intelligence co-lead is probably him personally.** The CEO letter says the round was \"co-led by Fundomo\" and thanks \"Rebecca, Corey, and their teams\" ([Tutor](https://tutorintelligence.com/blog/tutor-series-a-letter)). Rebecca is probably Rebecca Kaden of USV; that is my inference, since the letter uses first names only.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** His firm writes into exactly the owner's targets: AI chips and compute (Fab2/Atomic Semi, Etched, Tenstorrent, OLIX, AheadComputing), new labs (Flapping Airplanes seed and A, Sakana) and AI-native automation (Tutor Intelligence, Mercor). Unlike most allocators his size, he accepts small, non-lead allocations and does not insist on ownership, which matches the owner's stance. He is a natural co-investor or SPV partner on a $20M slice of a big lab seed. He also shows that a two-person shop can graduate to leading a $500M+ hardware round (Fab2). The deals that matter: Fab2 (lead and board), Flapping Airplanes (FA-001 $10.3M into the seed, FA-002 $24.85M into the A; mapping inferred), and the Etched series.\n\n## Background\n- **Before Fundomo:** angel investor from 2019 and at a family office 2019–2021, per TheOrg (aggregator, cited in `firms/fundomo.md`). That page now returns 404, so this is unconfirmed as of 2026-11-10. Earliest dated investment: Jaza Energy, 2015 ([fundomo.com](https://www.fundomo.com)).\n- **2021:** co-founded Circa Technologies; CEO and Chairman ([about.me](https://about.me/coreynobile)). Circa (circa.ai) calls itself \"The LCA Studio\" (life-cycle assessment, supply-chain data) ([circa.ai](https://circa.ai)).\n- **2023:** launched Fundomo with his wife, Siranush Babakhanova (MIT physics; Stanford biophysics PhD program). about.me says assets passed $500M within two years ([about.me](https://about.me/coreynobile)). First vehicle he signed: DI Fundomo SPV (first sale 2023-06-13, $573K, 82 investors, $1,000 minimum, 287 Park Ave S) ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1981491/000198149123000003/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **Boards:** a Fab2 director, confirmed by Form D. about.me says he \"serves on boards in the life sciences, semiconductor, and energy industries\" ([about.me](https://about.me/coreynobile)). Glyphic (board member) and Irradiant (board observer) rest only on TheOrg, which no longer resolves, so both are unconfirmed.\n- **Education:** not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://nobile.com/ | Lists only, no writing; contact is family@nobile.com (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Firm site | https://www.fundomo.com | Portfolio list only, no people |\n| about.me | https://about.me/coreynobile | Short bio; links to LinkedIn |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | not found | Firm account @fundomo per `firms/fundomo.md`, unreadable (402). No personal X found |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/coreynobile | Linked from about.me; returns 999 to scripts, contents not read |\n| GitHub | none found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was rate-limited in this pass. Direct checks of nobile.com, about.me, fundomo.com and EDGAR full-text search (36 hits for \"Corey Nobile\") turned up no interviews.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses (from how he labels things):** fundomo.com's tagline is \"State-scale systems\", and its categories are Computers, Control, Biology, Energy, Intelligence, Rocks and Misc ([fundomo.com](https://www.fundomo.com)). The book is built around physical bottlenecks: fabs, ASICs, optical processors, nuclear fuel and drilling.\n- **Pattern:** buy the bankruptcy or carve-out (Standard Nuclear from USNC assets), back the hardware prodigy early (Atomic Semi), then re-up at each step-up through a new vehicle.\n- **Science patronage:** he and his wife privately fund mathematics and physics research, including Jordan Cotler's lab at Harvard ([nobile.com](https://nobile.com/)).\n- **Calls so far:** Standard Nuclear (Seed-1 at $0.50, IPO at $15); Etched ET001, marked by StepStone from $8.22M cost to $15.06M FV ([N-PORT](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1918642/000119312526376298/ea0303531-04_nportex.htm)). No losses are public.\n\n## Read these first\n- nobile.com: how he splits the firm book from his personal one, plus the press links he chose for each company (Etched's $500M Bloomberg story, Karpathy's X post for Flapping Airplanes).\n- The Standard Nuclear 424B4, principal-stockholder and related-party sections: his full entry path.\n- The Fab2 Form D: the board seat and the size of the round he led.\n- The StepStone N-PORT: who buys his vehicles and at what marks.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public posting presence.\n\n## Portfolio\nOnly deals where a filing or company post names him personally. Prefix mappings marked * are inferences.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Atomic Semi (now Fab2)* | Early round via AT Fundomo Mana Ventures Fund I ($3.57M; Belltower-signed syndicate) | 2023-01-26 | no | Mana Ventures | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1964393/000196439323000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Atomic Semi (Fab2) | Series A ($556M sold to 89 investors); Nobile director | 2026-08-21 | **yes** | Naval Ravikant, Paradigm, Duquesne FO, Maverick Silicon, StepStone, Protagonist, UDC Ventures, Corner Capital | [fab2.com](https://fab2.com/), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2151752/000215175226000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Etched | Series A ($120M) via ET-0406 Fund I | 2024-04-24 | no | Primary, Positive Sum, Hummingbird | [Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware) |\n| Etched | ET001 SPV, $40.3M (round not stated) | 2025-06-05 | no | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084294/000208416625000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Etched | ET-002 SPV, $15.1M (probably the ~$500M Jan 2026 round he links on nobile.com) | 2025-12-31 | no | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2124223/000212422326000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Standard Nuclear | Seed (SAFE, converted to Seed-1 at $0.50) via ST-1014 Fund I, $7M | 2024-12 | no (one of 4 SAFE investors) | Decisive Point, Welara, Washington Harbour | [424B4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086716/000121390026078747/ea0276071-13.htm) |\n| Standard Nuclear | Series A ($70M) via SN-001, $10.0M | 2025-08-14 | no | same | same |\n| Standard Nuclear | Series A-2 via SN-002 / ST-1014, $10.0M | 2026-01-23 | no | same | same |\n| Flapping Airplanes* | Seed ($180M) via FA-001, $10.3M | 2026-01-23 | no | Index, GV, Sequoia | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2124227/000212422726000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Flapping Airplanes* | Series A ($1B) via FA-002, $24.85M | 2026-08-10 | no | Index, Lux, Sequoia, GV, Hummingbird | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2158561/000215856126000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Mercor* | ME-002 SPV, $44.5M (round unconfirmed) | 2025-11-12 | no | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2124232/000212423226000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Mercor* | ME-003 SPV, $118.5M (round unconfirmed) | 2026-08-10 | no | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2158633/000215863326000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Tutor Intelligence | Series A ($34M) | 2025-12-02 | co-lead (per company) | USV (lead), Neo | [Tutor](https://tutorintelligence.com/blog/tutor-series-a-letter) |\n| Sakana AI* | Series B via SA-001, $10.3M | 2026-02-01 | no | Lux, Khosla, NEA, In-Q-Tel, MUFG | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2124230/000212423026000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| OLIX | Series B ($312M) via OX-001 and OLX-002 ($82.1M) | 2026-03-30 / 2026-06-19 | no | Arm, HRT, Hummingbird, Plural | [OLX-002](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149938/000214993826000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Tenstorrent* | TT-001 ($31.0M), TT-002 ($12.5M) | 2026-03-30 | no | | [TT-001](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2139243/000213924326000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Glyphic* | GL-005 ($0.52M, 1 investor) | 2026-03-30 | unknown | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2124233/000212423326000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Unknown | Who Is John Galt, LP ($28.26M, 8 investors) | 2025-08-12 | unknown | StepStone (LP) | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084166/000208416625000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n**Timing summary:** first money or founding check in Standard Nuclear (Dec 2024) and probably Atomic Semi (2023). He doubled down at least twice in Standard Nuclear, Etched (4+ vehicles), Mercor (3), Flapping Airplanes (2), OLIX (2), Tenstorrent (2) and Fab2 (syndicate, then lead). He was never first money in the AI labs: Flapping Airplanes' seed was co-led by Index, GV and Sequoia.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Standard Nuclear.** Nobile's GPs were among four SAFE holders, alongside Decisive Point (whose Thomas Hendrix founded the company), Welara and Washington Harbour. The company was built on fuel assets bought at the USNC Section 363 auction in late 2024 ([424B4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086716/000121390026078747/ea0276071-13.htm)). The same four groups re-upped in the A and the A-2, so this was a closed founding syndicate rather than a competitive round. Fundomo held over 5% at the July 2026 IPO. Who introduced Nobile to Hendrix: not found.\n- **Fab2.** Syndicate exposure in 2023 (if AT = Atomic Semi), then the lead of the 2026 round with a board seat. Jessica Leao (then at Maverick) backed Atomic Semi early too (see `people/jessica-leao.md`). Nobile's intro path to Zeloof: not found.\n- **Flapping Airplanes.** See `firms/fundomo.md`. FA-001 had its first sale five days before the seed announcement, which means the allocation was committed before the round was public.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Siranush Babakhanova (Nobile) | Wife; Fundomo co-founder and co-manager | 2023 | [about.me](https://about.me/coreynobile) |\n| Fundomo | Co-founder, Managing Partner | 2023 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2023492/000202349224000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Circa Technologies | Co-founder, CEO and Chairman | 2021 | [about.me](https://about.me/coreynobile) |\n| Fab2 (Sam Zeloof, Jim Keller) | Board director; round lead | 2026 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2151752/000215175226000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Belltower Fund Group / Joshua Cowdin | Administrator of the Fundomo Syndicates series; holds delegated voting on ST-1014 | 2023 | [424B4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086716/000121390026078747/ea0276071-13.htm) |\n| StepStone Group | LP and secondary buyer of seven Nobile-managed vehicles, including John Galt | 2025 | [N-PORT](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1918642/000119312526376298/ea0303531-04_nportex.htm) |\n| Thomas Hendrix / Decisive Point | Co-founding investor in Standard Nuclear | 2024-12 | [424B4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086716/000121390026078747/ea0276071-13.htm) |\n| Josh Gruenstein (Tutor) | Portfolio CEO; co-led his Series A | 2025-12 | [Tutor](https://tutorintelligence.com/blog/tutor-series-a-letter) |\n| Jordan Cotler (Harvard) | Research patronage | not dated | [nobile.com](https://nobile.com/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which company sits under \"Who Is John Galt, LP\"? Erebor, Anduril and a Thiel-adjacent name are plausible given the Ayn Rand name, but this is a guess.\n- How did he meet Sam Zeloof, and was AT really Atomic Semi in 2023?\n- Are the Glyphic and Irradiant board roles real? Glyphic is confirmed only as an investment.\n- What was the family-office employer in 2019–2021, and what is his education?\n- What were the Mercor rounds behind ME-002 and ME-003, and is ME-1217 the Feb 2025 Series B?\n\n## Sources\n- https://nobile.com/ (JS bundle read 2026-11-10); https://about.me/coreynobile; https://www.fundomo.com; https://fab2.com/; https://circa.ai\n- SEC: Fab2 Form D https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2151752/000215175226000002/primary_doc.xml ; Who Is John Galt Form D https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084166/000208416625000001/primary_doc.xml ; DI Fundomo SPV https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1981491/000198149123000003/primary_doc.xml ; Standard Nuclear 424B4 https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086716/000121390026078747/ea0276071-13.htm ; Form 3/4 https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084264/000208426426000005/primary_doc.xml , https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084264/000208426426000006/primary_doc.xml ; StepStone N-PORT https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1918642/000119312526376298/ea0303531-04_nportex.htm ; EDGAR full-text search \"Corey Nobile\" (36 hits)\n- https://tutorintelligence.com/blog/tutor-series-a-letter ; https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware\n- Builds on `firms/fundomo.md` (Fundomo vehicle inventory)\n", "person:crystal-huang": "\n# Crystal Huang\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. The WebSearch budget was used up before this run started, so everything here comes from direct fetches: gv.com pages, GV's public content API (cdn/api for gv.com, project v5ygm6ip), company blogs, TechCrunch, HLTH and YouTube oEmbed. I read no podcasts, X or LinkedIn. Gaps mean \"not checked\", not \"doesn't exist\".*\n\n## One-line take\nGV General Partner who leads $10M–$50M Series A and B rounds in enterprise AI apps and infra. Her best deals are thesis-led and slow to develop: she meets a founder long before investing. Her former firms (GGV, NEA) often already sit on the cap table of companies she leads.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** she picks a category first (CPQ and billing, computer vision, legacy-app modernization, healthcare finance), then hunts for the company in it.\n  - CacheFlow: she met the founder \"over a year ago\" while looking for innovation in the category [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cacheflow-modernizing-saas).\n  - Roboflow: GV \"finally\" met the founders [src](https://www.gv.com/news/roboflow).\n  - Translucent: GV's conviction came from \"dozens of interviews\" with operators [src](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare).\n- **Constraints:** she works at the application and enterprise-infra layer, Series A and B. Her GV list has no lab, model-training or chip company [src](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang). GV's frontier-lab deals belong to Munichiello, Zeb and Hulme (see `firms/gv.md`).\n- **What she'd never do:** (my inference) lead a $600M lab seed, or a pre-product compute company. Her pitch is product- and developer-led adoption, not research bets [src](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Her former firms are already on the cap table when she leads.** At CacheFlow, GV's $10M round was \"joined by the company's existing investors GGV and Pelion Ventures\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cacheflow-modernizing-saas). GGV is her former firm [src](https://www.gv.com/news/new-general-partners). At Translucent, existing investors \"NEA, Virtue, and FPV Ventures\" continued [src](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv). NEA is also her former firm. My inference: her old-firm network supplies deal flow. No source says that is how either deal came in.\n- **She sources slowly, by category.** On CacheFlow: \"I connected with CEO and co-founder Sarika Garg over a year ago\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cacheflow-modernizing-saas) (post dated 2022-12-06).\n- **Google distribution is part of the pitch.** On Mechanical Orchard, GV says it won the lead \"getting lost together on the Vegas Strip during Google Next\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mechanical-orchard-ai) (2024-08-06, co-written with Michael McBride).\n- **She was early on enterprise generative AI.** Her Typeface post is dated 2023-02-27, about three months after ChatGPT's public launch. It says \"The future is incredibly bright for enterprise-grade generative AI\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/typeface-generative-ai-enterprise).\n- **Roboflow's Series B pulled in AI-insider angels:** Jeff Dean, Amjad Masad and Guillermo Rauch came in as new investors alongside GV's lead [src](https://roboflow.com/blog/series-b).\n- **She is GV's only listed partner on almost every company on her page.** GV's content API lists her as the sole GV investor on 13 of her 14 companies. Translucent has a second reference, presumably associate Mounisha Anumolu, who co-wrote the post (GV CMS query, 2026-11-10; [team page](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang), [Translucent post](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- She is not a lab, pre-training or compute investor. Nothing on her GV list is frontier-model or chip work [src](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang).\n- She is a real Series A/B lead in **AI-native automation**, the category I like (Serval-type), and can write $10M+ checks:\n  - DualEntry (\"The Agentic ERP\" per its [YouTube channel](https://www.youtube.com/@DualEntry))\n  - Translucent: agentic healthcare finance [src](https://www.translucent.ai/)\n  - Mechanical Orchard: AI application modernization [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mechanical-orchard-ai)\n- She is useful as a **follow-on lead** for seed companies I back in automation, and as a GV route into Google Cloud distribution.\n- Sync Labs is the closest she gets to research talent: a $5.5M seed GV led, spun out of Wav2Lip research at IIIT Hyderabad [src](https://sync.so/blog/sync-announces-5-5m-seed-round-to-make-video-content-creation-less-camera-dependent-using-ai).\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Vancouver, where her family ran a small business. B.A. in Government, Harvard [src](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang).\n- Started in technology M&A at **Blackstone** [src](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang). Dates not found.\n- Invested at **NEA** and **Notable Capital (formerly GGV)** [src](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang). Dates and order not found.\n- Joined **GV in 2021** [src](https://www.gv.com/news/new-general-partners). The month (the task brief says July) and her title at joining are not confirmed.\n- Bylined \"GV Partner\" on 2022-12-06 [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cacheflow-modernizing-saas).\n- Promoted to **General Partner on 2023-06-05**, alongside Brendan Bulik-Sullivan, Frédérique Dame and Issi Rozen. GV described her as \"integral to developing and scaling our investments in generative artificial intelligence\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/new-general-partners).\n- Was based in New York in 2023 [src](https://www.gv.com/news/new-general-partners). Now splits time between SF and NY [src](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang | GV bio and investments list, read 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | GV news posts: [Typeface](https://www.gv.com/news/typeface-generative-ai-enterprise), [CacheFlow](https://www.gv.com/news/cacheflow-modernizing-saas), [Mechanical Orchard](https://www.gv.com/news/mechanical-orchard-ai), [Roboflow](https://www.gv.com/news/roboflow), [Translucent](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare) | About one investment post a year |\n| X | nothing found | GV page lists no personal X account [src](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/crystalhuang89/ | Linked from GV page, not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-10 — TechCrunch Disrupt 2026, Builders Stage — \"The Zero-to-1K Playbook\" with Grant Lee (Gamma) and Elia Wallen (Engine) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/09/techcrunch-disrupt-2026-gammas-grant-lee-engines-elia-wallen-and-gvs-crystal-huang-on-landing-your-first-1000-customers). Video: [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rmMo0upMF7s). She was also a Startup Battlefield 200 judge. Transcript not read.\n- Date unknown — Google Cloud — \"Founder series panel: How to get $100 million in funding\" [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nh_yj1igL60). Linked from her GV profile. Transcript not read.\n- 2026-02-19 — DualEntry YouTube — \"Why GV (Google Ventures) Invested in DualEntry\" [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nUQ0tMn-VG4). Date from GV CMS. Transcript not read; this is the best place to learn the DualEntry deal story.\n\n## How they think\n- **Category theses before companies:**\n  - computer vision as a \"Cambrian explosion\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/roboflow)\n  - legacy CPQ and billing as \"inflexible, expensive\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cacheflow-modernizing-saas)\n  - legacy modernization that \"often fail[s]\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mechanical-orchard-ai)\n  - healthcare finance as \"grossly underserved\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare)\n- **Enterprise AI needs data control.** Her Typeface case rests on privacy, security and cheap retraining of off-the-shelf LLMs [src](https://www.gv.com/news/typeface-generative-ai-enterprise).\n- **She backs operator-founders.** Translucent's founder was CIO/CPTO at ChenMed [src](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare). Typeface's founder was Adobe's CTO and CPO [src](https://www.gv.com/news/typeface-generative-ai-enterprise). Mechanical Orchard's Rob Mee is named in the post [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mechanical-orchard-ai).\n- **Right or wrong calls:** CacheFlow is marked exited, but I couldn't confirm the acquirer or terms [src](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang). I found no valuation marks for Roboflow or Typeface.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Typeface: Generative AI for the Enterprise](https://www.gv.com/news/typeface-generative-ai-enterprise) (2023-02-27). Her early enterprise-genAI thesis.\n2. [Roboflow: Making the (Real) World Programmable](https://www.gv.com/news/roboflow) (2024-11-19). Her largest lead, and the computer-vision thesis.\n3. [Cacheflow](https://www.gv.com/news/cacheflow-modernizing-saas) (2022-12-06). The clearest statement of how she sources.\n4. [Translucent AI: Healthcare's Financial OS](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare) (2026-03-11). Her current vertical-agent thesis.\n5. [GV new general partners](https://www.gv.com/news/new-general-partners) (2023-06-05). How GV describes her.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. She has no personal X account listed, and I didn't read LinkedIn.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Roboflow | Series B, $40M | 2024-11-19 | yes (GV) | Craft Ventures, Y Combinator (existing); Valor, Frontline, Jeff Dean, Amjad Masad, Guillermo Rauch (new) | [roboflow](https://roboflow.com/blog/series-b), [gv](https://www.gv.com/news/roboflow) |\n| Translucent AI | Series A, $27M | 2026-03-11 | yes (GV) | NEA, Virtue, FPV Ventures (existing) | [translucent](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv), [hlth](https://hlth.com/insights/news/translucent-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv-to-advance-ai-financial-platform-for-health-systems-2026-03-12) |\n| Mechanical Orchard | Series B, $50M | 2024-08-06 | yes (GV) | not named | [gv](https://www.gv.com/news/mechanical-orchard-ai) |\n| Typeface | $65M (round not named in GV post) | 2023-02-27 | not stated; GP announcement says she \"led\" it | not named | [gv](https://www.gv.com/news/typeface-generative-ai-enterprise), [gv GP post](https://www.gv.com/news/new-general-partners) |\n| CacheFlow (exited) | $10M | 2022-12-06 | yes (GV) | GGV, Pelion Ventures (existing) | [gv](https://www.gv.com/news/cacheflow-modernizing-saas) |\n| Sync Labs (sync.) | Seed, $5.5M | 2024-08-16 | yes (GV) | Y Combinator, Creator Ventures, Rebel Fund | [sync](https://sync.so/blog/sync-announces-5-5m-seed-round-to-make-video-content-creation-less-camera-dependent-using-ai) |\n| Speakeasy | Series A, $15M (GV follow-on) | 2024-10-30 | no (FPV led) | FPV, Quiet Capital; angels Søren Bramer Schmidt, Clint Sharp, Arpit Patel | [speakeasy](https://www.speakeasy.com/post/fundraising-series-a) |\n| Pecan AI | not found | before 2023-06 | yes (per GV) | not found | [gv GP post](https://www.gv.com/news/new-general-partners) |\n| Sardine | not found | before 2023-06 | yes (per GV) | not found | [gv GP post](https://www.gv.com/news/new-general-partners) |\n| Sift | Series A, $17.5M (GV role not confirmed; page returned 429) | not found | unknown | not found | [spacenews (linked from GV profile)](https://spacenews.com/sift-raises-17-5m-in-series-a-round/) |\n| DualEntry | not found | GV video 2026-02-19 | unknown | not found | [gv](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang), [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nUQ0tMn-VG4) |\n| Rilla | not found | not found | unknown | not found | [gv](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang) |\n| Vareto | not found | not found | unknown | not found | [gv](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang) |\n| Omni Analytics | not found | not found | unknown | not found | [gv](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang) |\n\n## Deal stories\n### Roboflow, Series B (standout)\n- **What:** GV led a $40M Series B, announced 2024-11-19 [src](https://roboflow.com/blog/series-b). Roboflow launched in 2020 out of Y Combinator S20 [src](https://roboflow.com/about).\n- **How she found it:** her post frames computer vision as a market where \"multiple forces are converging\", then says GV was \"fortunate to finally meet Joseph and Brad\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/roboflow). That reads as category-first hunting that ended at Roboflow. The post gives no intro path.\n- **Who else was there:**\n  - Existing: Craft Ventures, Y Combinator.\n  - New: Valor, Frontline, and angels Jeff Dean, Amjad Masad and Guillermo Rauch [src](https://roboflow.com/blog/series-b).\n  - Roboflow's site also lists Lachy Groom and says investors include co-founders of OpenAI, Stripe, Firebase and Segment [src](https://roboflow.com/about).\n- **Traction at the time:** over 1M developers downloaded its open-source tools in the prior 30 days. Customers included BNSF, Pella and Relo Metrics [src](https://roboflow.com/blog/series-b). The site now says engineers at more than half the Fortune 100 use it [src](https://roboflow.com/about).\n- **Board seat:** the task brief says she joined the board in 2024-11. Neither the GV post, the Roboflow announcement nor Roboflow's about page mentions a board seat, so it is **unconfirmed**.\n- **Worth now:** no valuation found, and I found no later round on the pages I read.\n\n### Translucent AI, Series A\n- **The round:** $27M Series A led by GV. The release on 2026-03-11 calls it preemptive and oversubscribed. NEA, Virtue and FPV continued [src](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv). It followed a $7M seed in 2025-08.\n- **How GV got conviction:** \"dozens of interviews with clinical and finance operators\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare). The post was co-written with associate Mounisha Anumolu.\n- **Founder:** Jack O'Hara, formerly CIO/CPTO at ChenMed [src](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv).\n- **Possible intro path (my inference, unconfirmed):** NEA, her former firm, was an existing investor.\n\n### Mechanical Orchard, Series B\n- GV led the $50M Series B (2024-08-06), after \"many energizing meetings\" with Rob Mee, Matthew Work and the team, including at Google Next [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mechanical-orchard-ai). She co-wrote the post with Michael McBride.\n\n### CacheFlow\n- She met Sarika Garg more than a year before GV led a $10M round (2022-12-06), while looking for innovation in CPQ and billing. Existing investors GGV and Pelion joined [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cacheflow-modernizing-saas).\n- GV marks it exited [src](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang). Acquirer not found.\n\n### Typeface\n- Her post (2023-02-27) backed former Adobe CTO/CPO Abhay Parasnis and points to a press release mentioning $65M [src](https://www.gv.com/news/typeface-generative-ai-enterprise). GV's GP announcement says she led it [src](https://www.gv.com/news/new-general-partners). Co-investors not confirmed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| GV | General Partner | 2021 (GP 2023-06-05) | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/new-general-partners) |\n| NEA | Former investor; existing investor in Translucent when she led its Series A | dates not found | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang), [src](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv) |\n| Notable Capital (GGV) | Former investor; existing investor in CacheFlow when she led | dates not found | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang), [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cacheflow-modernizing-saas) |\n| Blackstone | Tech M&A, start of career | dates not found | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang) |\n| Mounisha Anumolu (GV) | Co-author on the Translucent deal | 2026 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare) |\n| Michael McBride (GV) | Co-author on the Mechanical Orchard deal | 2024 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mechanical-orchard-ai) |\n| FPV Ventures | Co-investor in Speakeasy (FPV led) and Translucent | 2024 | [src](https://www.speakeasy.com/post/fundraising-series-a) |\n| Craft Ventures | Co-investor in Roboflow | 2024 | [src](https://roboflow.com/blog/series-b) |\n| Jack O'Hara | Founder of Translucent, portfolio CEO | 2026 | [src](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv) |\n| Sarika Garg | Founder of CacheFlow; Huang met her about a year before investing | ~2021 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cacheflow-modernizing-saas) |\n| Abhay Parasnis | Founder of Typeface, portfolio CEO | 2023 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/typeface-generative-ai-enterprise) |\n| Rob Mee, Matthew Work | Mechanical Orchard leaders, portfolio | 2024 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mechanical-orchard-ai) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is she on the Roboflow board? What is Roboflow's valuation since the B?\n- Who acquired CacheFlow, when, and on what terms?\n- Rounds and dates for DualEntry, Rilla, Vareto, Omni, Sardine, Pecan and Sift. The DualEntry YouTube video probably holds the deal story.\n- Did the NEA and GGV connections actually bring in Translucent and CacheFlow?\n- The month she joined GV and her title at joining, which decides fast_promotion. LinkedIn should answer this.\n- Transcripts of the Disrupt 2026 and Google Cloud \"$100M\" panels, for verbatim rules of thumb.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.gv.com/team/crystal-huang\n- https://www.gv.com/news/new-general-partners\n- https://www.gv.com/news/roboflow\n- https://www.gv.com/news/typeface-generative-ai-enterprise\n- https://www.gv.com/news/cacheflow-modernizing-saas\n- https://www.gv.com/news/mechanical-orchard-ai\n- https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare\n- https://roboflow.com/blog/series-b\n- https://roboflow.com/about\n- https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv\n- https://hlth.com/insights/news/translucent-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv-to-advance-ai-financial-platform-for-health-systems-2026-03-12\n- https://sync.so/blog/sync-announces-5-5m-seed-round-to-make-video-content-creation-less-camera-dependent-using-ai\n- https://www.speakeasy.com/post/fundraising-series-a\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/09/techcrunch-disrupt-2026-gammas-grant-lee-engines-elia-wallen-and-gvs-crystal-huang-on-landing-your-first-1000-customers\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nUQ0tMn-VG4\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nh_yj1igL60\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rmMo0upMF7s\n- https://spacenews.com/sift-raises-17-5m-in-series-a-round/ (not readable, HTTP 429)\n- GV's public content API (Sanity project v5ygm6ip), queried 2026-11-10, for her articles, linked talks and portfolio attribution\n", "person:dan-ateya": "\n# Dan (Daniel) Ateya\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 as part of the strategic-investor map. Read with [firms/rtx-ventures.md](../firms/rtx-ventures.md), which covers the pool, the team and the full deal list. This page is about the person.*\n\n## One-line take\nAteya is a career corporate venture investor who has set up the same model twice (3M Ventures' 2015 strategy and Silicon Valley office, then RTX Ventures from nothing in 2022). He is the only name RTX puts on a deal, and the checks he controls are $2M to $10M of RTX's own money, so he matters to the owner as a small strategic co-investor in AI-chip and AI-for-science rounds, not as a price-setter.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins allocation:** he sells access to a prime, not capital. His own framing is that many corporate funds only serve internal development while his aim is to \"seed an ecosystem\" ([GCV, 2022-06-07](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/raytheons-venture-strategy/?format=md)). The proof points he cites are partnerships (Ursa Major motors on a Raytheon missile), and RTX publishes a partnership pipeline, not returns ([GCV Powerlist 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-daniel-ateya/?format=md)).\n- **What constrains him:** one pool, one LP, small tickets. Aviation Week, after interviewing him, wrote that the fund \"has RTX as the sole investor\" and has \"tended to make 5-10 investments of $2-10 million annually\" ([Aviation Week, 2024-11-27](https://aviationweek.com/mro/aircraft-propulsion/mro-memo-ventures-propulsion)). That is $10M to $100M a year at the outside. Deals go to an unnamed steering committee (see the firm page), and he says he kept the team small on purpose.\n- **What he personally signs, sources or prices:** unknown at the level of signatures. No filing, release or interview says who approves a deal or what his own limit is. What is sourced: he is the quoted RTX voice on six investments, and the unit has led or co-led five rounds (Impulse Space A, Phoenix Semiconductor seed, Radical AI seed, Darkhive B, Observable Space A). When RTX Ventures leads, the cheque implied by his $2M to $10M band is a minority of the round, so \"led\" here means set terms with a small anchor, not filled the round (inference from the band and round sizes; unconfirmed).\n- **What he'd never do (inferred from the book and his statements, not stated):** back a frontier model lab or a GPU cloud. He says the unit's map \"aligns nearly a hundred percent\" with the White House critical-technologies list ([GCV, 2023-02-02](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rtx-ventures-takes-aim-at-emerging-defence-technologies/?format=md)), and his one on-record AI-chip statement is about edge inference.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **A check-size band exists, and it is small.** \"tended to make 5-10 investments of $2-10 million annually\" ([Aviation Week, 2024-11-27](https://aviationweek.com/mro/aircraft-propulsion/mro-memo-ventures-propulsion); the reporter's words in a piece built on an interview with Ateya). The firm page had no primary number; this is the closest thing to one. The same piece says the fund \"has RTX as the sole investor\".\n- **The 2026 Director hire looks like his old 3M Ventures colleague.** RTX's team page lists \"Erik Paisley Director\" ([rtx.com](https://www.rtx.com/what-we-do/rtx-ventures)), new since December 2025. An Erik Paisley joined 3M Ventures in 2015, was promoted to director there in 2021, and holds a UVA MBA; he credits the same \"new strategy for ventures at 3M back in 2015\" that Ateya claims ([GCV, 2023-03-14](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/emerging-leaders-2023-erik-paisley/?format=md), [GCV, 2020](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gcv-rising-stars-awards-2020-erik-paisley/?format=md)). No source states the two are the same person, so this is **unconfirmed**. If it holds, Ateya is rebuilding his 3M bench at RTX.\n- **3M Ventures and RTX Ventures were in the same round in 2026.** JetZero's $175M Series B included \"the venture investment arm of RTX\" and \"the venture funds of 3M and United Airlines\" ([Washington Technology, 2026-01-15](https://www.washingtontechnology.com/companies/2026/01/defense-software-cyber-and-aircraft-highlight-week-venture-rounds/410709/)). Who brought whom is not stated.\n- **At 3M he led about a dozen deals himself.** \"I have helped lead 12 strategically aligned investments\" out of a 35-company portfolio ([GCV, 2020-01-29](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gcv-emerging-leaders-awards-2020-daniel-ateya/?format=md)). None of the 12 is named in any source found.\n- **He claims financial performance, not just strategic value.** \"The portfolio has been performing, financially, extremely well\" ([GCV Powerlist 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-daniel-ateya/?format=md)). RTX's FY2025 10-K reports $0.1B of fair-value gains on investments without saying which ones (firm page); the two fit together but the link is an inference.\n- **His AI-chip rationale is edge inference.** On Efficient Computer: \"energy-efficient processors that will empower AI applications across edge\" ([Efficient](https://www.efficient.computer/series-b)). No statement from him on training compute, model labs or Radical AI was found.\n- **He judges defence startups by the path to a programme of record.** In 2023: \"How many of them really get the programme of record\" ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rtx-ventures-takes-aim-at-emerging-defence-technologies/?format=md)). In 2026, on leading Darkhive's B, the stated use of funds is \"integration into operationally validated systems and new programs of record\" ([release](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/darkhive-announces-close-30-million-series-b-funding-round-led-rtx-ventures)).\n- **Title drift.** \"the vice-president and managing director of RTX Ventures\" in June 2022 ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/raytheons-venture-strategy/?format=md)), \"president and managing director\" by February 2023 ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rtx-ventures-takes-aim-at-emerging-defence-technologies/?format=md)). RTX's own team grid still says only \"Managing Director\" while the pull-quote on the same page says \"President and Managing Director\" ([rtx.com](https://www.rtx.com/what-we-do/rtx-ventures)). No announcement of a promotion was found; \"President\" may be a unit title, not an RTX corporate officer rank (unconfirmed).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** For: the unit he runs led a $55M AI-for-science seed (Radical AI, with NVentures in the round), entered two AI-chip companies at the first or second priced round and re-upped (EnCharge AI A and B, Efficient Computer A and B), and is listed by Extropic. Those are the owner's categories at the owner's stage. Against: the cheque is $2M to $10M, the pool has one LP, every holding must map to an RTX product, and there is no frontier-lab or pre-training exposure. He is a co-investor you would meet in an edge-AI chip round and a route to a defence customer for a portfolio company. He is not a later-round price-setter, not an LP in funds (none found), and RTX has not acquired any of his companies.\n\nDeals that matter to the owner: Radical AI seed (led, 2025-07), EnCharge AI A (2023-12) and B (2025-02), Efficient Computer A (2026-02) and B (2026-09), Extropic (round not found), Code Metal A and B (2025-11, 2026-02). He is personally quoted only on Efficient Computer B among these.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** PhD in engineering from the State University of New York at Buffalo, \"with a materials science, micro and nanofabrication and life sciences technologies focus\" ([GCV, 2019](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2019/01/30/gcv-rising-stars-awards-2019-daniel-ateya/?format=md)); later profiles call it mechanical engineering ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-daniel-ateya/?format=md)). MBA from the University of Virginia's Darden School ([GCV, 2020](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gcv-emerging-leaders-awards-2020-daniel-ateya/?format=md)). Dates not found.\n- **US Naval Research Laboratory**, Washington DC, after the PhD ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-daniel-ateya/?format=md)). Dates not found. A secondary newsletter says he worked on microfluidic chips for portable sensors there ([Ex Terra, 2025-08-26](https://www.exterrajsc.com/p/rtx-ventures-investor-spotlight), secondary and unverified).\n- **3M, 2010 to early 2022.** Joined 3M in 2010; about three years as business leader of an internal corporate venture in consumer electronics; then 3M Ventures from January 2015 as senior manager and head of the Silicon Valley office, which he set up ([GCV 2019](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2019/01/30/gcv-rising-stars-awards-2019-daniel-ateya/?format=md), [GCV 2020](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gcv-emerging-leaders-awards-2020-daniel-ateya/?format=md)). GCV later calls his last 3M title \"director\" ([GCV 2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rtx-ventures-takes-aim-at-emerging-defence-technologies/?format=md)). His boss was Ben Wright, director of 3M Ventures. Sources disagree on the length of the 3M Ventures stint (\"more than a decade\" in GCV 2024, \"nearly a decade\" in GCV 2025); the dated record is seven years in the venture unit and about twelve at 3M.\n- **RTX Ventures, February 2022 to now.** Joined \"in February 2022 to helm the new unit\" (GCV 2023); the unit was launched publicly in April 2022 and announced Hermeus in May ([GCV 2022](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/raytheons-venture-strategy/?format=md)). GCV Powerlist 2024, 2025 and 2026.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [rtx.com/what-we-do/rtx-ventures](https://www.rtx.com/what-we-do/rtx-ventures) | Team grid and his pull-quote; no contact form or email on the page (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | none found | No account located; not confirmed absent |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | No profile URL was confirmed from this environment; do not guess one |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only, see below |\n| Speaker bio | [aiaa.org/people/daniel-ateya](https://aiaa.org/people/daniel-ateya/) | AIAA speaker page titled \"Daniel Ateya President and Managing Director RTX Ventures\"; the page blocked automated reading, so its bio text is unread |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him.** No public inbox exists. The working routes, in order of evidence: (1) a co-investor he has shared rounds with repeatedly: In-Q-Tel, Lockheed Martin Ventures, J2 Ventures, Eclipse, Founders Fund, Lux (firm page); (2) an RTX business unit that already wants the product, since Forterra got the equity eight months after a Raytheon demo (firm page); (3) his team: the firm page lists Kyle Perez as the working investor and Sonny Tahiliani as the technical gate for chips; (4) the WT 360 episode's notes promise \"Ateya's advice for how to connect with the ventures team\" ([Washington Technology, 2023-09-18](https://washingtontechnology.com/podcasts/2023/09/wt-360-rtx-ventures-casts-its-net-wide-and-far-across-expanding-tech-ecosystem/390330/); audio not retrieved). His colleague Roman Mueller says the unit can go from introduction to investment in three weeks (firm page).\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-01 — Global Venturing Review — \"How to do defence tech well\" — [episode note](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gvr-podcast-rtx-ventures-defence-tech/?format=md) and [write-up](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rtx-ventures-takes-aim-at-emerging-defence-technologies/?format=md) — eight companies in year one, seed to Series E, the clearance \"chicken or egg problem\". Only the write-up was read, not a transcript.\n- 2023-09-18 — WT 360 (Washington Technology) — \"RTX Ventures casts its net wide and far across an expanding tech ecosystem\" — [episode page](https://washingtontechnology.com/podcasts/2023/09/wt-360-rtx-ventures-casts-its-net-wide-and-far-across-expanding-tech-ecosystem/390330/) — what happens after an investment and how to approach the team. Audio not retrieved.\n- 2024-11-27 — Aviation Week Engine Yearbook interview — [MRO Memo](https://aviationweek.com/mro/aircraft-propulsion/mro-memo-ventures-propulsion) — source of the $2M to $10M band; cites SpaceX, Anduril and Palantir as the reason venture money came to defence.\n- 2024-12-11 — Emerging Tech Horizons (NDIA ETI) — \"Connecting Silicon Valley to Defense Industry\" — [episode listing](https://directory.libsyn.com/episode/index/show/ndiaeti/id/34333460), recording date per [Audioscrape](https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/emerging-tech-horizons/episode/connecting-silicon-valley-to-defense-industry-25600) — no transcript exists; show notes only.\n- 2025-04-23 — RTX feature on VATN — [rtx.com](https://www.rtx.com/news/2025/04/23/swarms-arent-just-for-the-sky---how-undersea-vehicles-will-help-in-battle) — why a company spending $7.5B on R&D buys startup equity.\n\n## How they think\n- **Corporate venture as ecosystem-building.** \"Our investments will help seed an ecosystem of disruptive tech companies\" (GCV 2022). He contrasts this with funds that only feed internal R&D.\n- **Start small, show wins, then grow.** Quoted on the firm page; the team went from about six to seven investors in four years.\n- **Government priorities set the map.** The White House critical and emerging technologies list is his stated filter (GCV 2023).\n- **Stage is relative to capital intensity.** The unit invested from seed to Series E in its first year because a hardware Series E is not late (GCV 2023, reporter's paraphrase).\n- **The exit path is the programme of record.** He names the \"Valley of Death\" between prototype funding and production contracts as the central problem, and positions RTX as the guide to where government demand is (GCV 2023).\n- **Dual use matters because half of RTX is commercial.** \"There's a lot of application across both worlds\", naming AI and ML, communications and semiconductors (GCV 2023).\n- **Generative AI is on the list but not in the book.** GCV's 2024 profile says the unit is excited about \"the tectonic shift that generative AI has the potential to create\" (reporter's words). The AI holdings since then are chips, code translation and materials discovery, not model companies.\n- **Calls:** Hermeus as the first investment (2022) became a Pratt & Whitney engine customer and licensee by 2026 (firm page). EpiSci (2022, sole investor) was acquired by Applied Intuition in February 2025; RTX's page now labels it \"Acquired by Applied Intuition\", the unit's first visible exit. No write-off has been disclosed; Fortify left the portfolio page between September 2025 and now (firm page).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [GCV, 2023-02-02](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rtx-ventures-takes-aim-at-emerging-defence-technologies/?format=md): the fullest account of how he set the unit up and what he screens for.\n2. [Aviation Week, 2024-11-27](https://aviationweek.com/mro/aircraft-propulsion/mro-memo-ventures-propulsion): the only place a ticket size and the sole-investor structure are stated.\n3. [GCV Emerging Leaders 2020](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gcv-emerging-leaders-awards-2020-daniel-ateya/?format=md): his own description of the 3M years, which is the template for RTX.\n4. [GCV Powerlist 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-daniel-ateya/?format=md): his claims on financial performance and the partnership pipeline.\n5. [GCV, 2022-06-07](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/raytheons-venture-strategy/?format=md): launch Q&A, including the move from business-unit stakes to a central portfolio.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no located X account or newsletter. His public footprint is trade press (Global Venturing, Washington Technology, Aviation Week), RTX's own stories and deal releases.\n\n## Portfolio\nOnly rounds where he is personally quoted. The unit's full book is on the firm page.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Hermeus | Series B ($100M; RTX joined after the round was announced) | 2022-05 | no (Sam Altman led) | Founders Fund, In-Q-Tel | [Raytheon release](https://raytheon.mediaroom.com/2022-05-12-Raytheon-Technologies-venture-capital-group-invests-in-Hermeus) |\n| EpiSci (AI autonomy) | Strategic (minority; company's first outside money) | 2022-12 | sole | none | [release](https://iconnect007.com/article/134384/rtx-ventures-invests-in-episci-to-further-develop-next-generation-autonomy-solutions/134387/milaero) |\n| Impulse Space | Series A ($45M) | 2023-07 | yes | Founders Fund, Lux, Airbus Ventures, Space Capital | [Washington Technology](https://www.washingtontechnology.com/contracts/2023/07/rtxs-venture-arm-leads-financing-round-space-startup/388772/) |\n| VATN Systems (autonomous undersea vehicles) | Seed ($13M) | 2024-11 | no | Lockheed Martin Ventures, SAIC Ventures, In-Q-Tel | Quoted in the 2025-04 [RTX feature](https://www.rtx.com/news/2025/04/23/swarms-arent-just-for-the-sky---how-undersea-vehicles-will-help-in-battle), not in a round release |\n| Darkhive (autonomous drones) | Series B ($30M) | 2026-05 | yes | Draper Associates, Bison, Ten Eleven, Crosslink | [release](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/darkhive-announces-close-30-million-series-b-funding-round-led-rtx-ventures) |\n| Efficient Computer (AI chip) | Series B ($97M) | 2026-09 | no | TQ Ventures, USV, Toyota Ventures, Eclipse | [Efficient](https://www.efficient.computer/series-b) |\n\n**AI deals, stage and timing.** EpiSci: sole outside investor at the company's first raise, December 2022; exit to Applied Intuition 26 months later. Efficient Computer: the unit entered at the Series A in February 2026 and he is quoted at the Series B seven months later. VATN: seed. Darkhive: joined the 2024 Series A behind Ten Eleven, then led the B. He is **not** quoted on Radical AI, EnCharge AI, Code Metal or Extropic in any source found, so those are unit deals, not personal credits.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Hermeus (2022).** His first deal, three months after he joined, and into a round Sam Altman had already led. He called it \"our first investment\" and chose it for near-term defence use with a commercial upside ([Raytheon release](https://raytheon.mediaroom.com/2022-05-12-Raytheon-Technologies-venture-capital-group-invests-in-Hermeus)). The firm page tracks what followed: Hermeus built around a Pratt & Whitney engine and was licensed to manufacture it in 2026.\n- **EpiSci (2022).** A stand-alone minority investment with no syndicate. \"As an early investor we plan to support EpiSci's endeavors\" ([release](https://iconnect007.com/article/134384/rtx-ventures-invests-in-episci-to-further-develop-next-generation-autonomy-solutions/134387/milaero)). Applied Intuition bought the company in 2025 (firm page). This is the pattern to expect if he ever does an AI software company alone: small, strategic, and no blocking rights used.\n- **Darkhive (2024 to 2026).** Followed in the A, led the B two months after the Pentagon gave Darkhive a $49.7M APFIT programme (release above). He leads once the government customer is visible.\n- No intro path, first-meeting story or pricing detail is on record for any deal.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| RTX Ventures | President and Managing Director; founding head | 2022-02 | [GCV 2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rtx-ventures-takes-aim-at-emerging-defence-technologies/?format=md) |\n| 3M Ventures | Senior manager, later director; head of the Silicon Valley office | 2015-01 to 2022 | [GCV 2020](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gcv-emerging-leaders-awards-2020-daniel-ateya/?format=md) |\n| Ben Wright | His boss at 3M Ventures (director of the unit); nominated him for GCV awards three years running | 2015 | [GCV 2019](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2019/01/30/gcv-rising-stars-awards-2019-daniel-ateya/?format=md) |\n| Erik Paisley | 3M Ventures colleague 2015 to 2022; a person of the same name is a Director at RTX Ventures since 2026 (same person unconfirmed) | 2015 | [GCV 2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/emerging-leaders-2023-erik-paisley/?format=md), [rtx.com](https://www.rtx.com/what-we-do/rtx-ventures) |\n| RTX CTO office | \"Regular touchpoints\" to align on technical priorities (reporter's paraphrase) | 2022 | [GCV 2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rtx-ventures-takes-aim-at-emerging-defence-technologies/?format=md) |\n| US Naval Research Laboratory | Former researcher | dates not found | [GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-daniel-ateya/?format=md) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who sits on the steering committee, what he can approve alone, and whom he reports to (CFO, CTO or strategy).\n- Whether the RTX Ventures Erik Paisley is the 3M Ventures one. A second Darden link is possible too: aggregators give Kyle Perez a Darden MBA (firm page, unverified), and Ateya's MBA is from Darden.\n- Which twelve companies he led at 3M Ventures, and whether any were AI or semiconductor deals.\n- Whether he personally sourced Radical AI, the unit's largest led seed and the one closest to the owner's lens.\n- Whether \"President\" was a promotion with a date or a relabelled title.\n- The two podcasts (WT 360, Emerging Tech Horizons) have no transcripts; they are the likeliest place for unreported detail on check sizes and approval.\n\n## Sources\n- Global Venturing: [2022 Q&A](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/raytheons-venture-strategy/?format=md), [2023 feature](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/rtx-ventures-takes-aim-at-emerging-defence-technologies/?format=md), [2023 podcast note](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gvr-podcast-rtx-ventures-defence-tech/?format=md), [Rising Stars 2019](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2019/01/30/gcv-rising-stars-awards-2019-daniel-ateya/?format=md), [Emerging Leaders 2020](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gcv-emerging-leaders-awards-2020-daniel-ateya/?format=md), [Emerging Leaders 2021](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gcv-emerging-leaders-awards-2021-daniel-ateya/?format=md), Powerlist [2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-daniel-ateya/?format=md), [2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-daniel-ateya/?format=md), [2026](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-daniel-ateya-rtx-ventures/?format=md); Erik Paisley profiles [2020](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gcv-rising-stars-awards-2020-erik-paisley/?format=md), [2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/emerging-leaders-2023-erik-paisley/?format=md)\n- Aviation Week, 2024-11-27: https://aviationweek.com/mro/aircraft-propulsion/mro-memo-ventures-propulsion\n- Washington Technology: [WT 360 episode](https://washingtontechnology.com/podcasts/2023/09/wt-360-rtx-ventures-casts-its-net-wide-and-far-across-expanding-tech-ecosystem/390330/), [Impulse Space A](https://www.washingtontechnology.com/contracts/2023/07/rtxs-venture-arm-leads-financing-round-space-startup/388772/), [JetZero B](https://www.washingtontechnology.com/companies/2026/01/defense-software-cyber-and-aircraft-highlight-week-venture-rounds/410709/)\n- Releases: [Hermeus](https://raytheon.mediaroom.com/2022-05-12-Raytheon-Technologies-venture-capital-group-invests-in-Hermeus), [EpiSci](https://iconnect007.com/article/134384/rtx-ventures-invests-in-episci-to-further-develop-next-generation-autonomy-solutions/134387/milaero), [Darkhive](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/darkhive-announces-close-30-million-series-b-funding-round-led-rtx-ventures), [Efficient Computer](https://www.efficient.computer/series-b)\n- RTX: [Ventures page](https://www.rtx.com/what-we-do/rtx-ventures), [VATN feature](https://www.rtx.com/news/2025/04/23/swarms-arent-just-for-the-sky---how-undersea-vehicles-will-help-in-battle)\n- NDIA ETI podcast: [Libsyn listing](https://directory.libsyn.com/episode/index/show/ndiaeti/id/34333460), [Audioscrape page](https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/emerging-tech-horizons/episode/connecting-silicon-valley-to-defense-industry-25600)\n- Secondary: [Ex Terra newsletter](https://www.exterrajsc.com/p/rtx-ventures-investor-spotlight)\n- Row files: `data/rows/dan-ateya-{deals,rels,insights,leads}.csv`\n", "person:dan-zigmond": "\n# Dan Zigmond\n\n## One-line take\nA senior data-science operator (Google, YouTube, Facebook News Feed, Instagram, CZI, Apple) who has been Compound's part-time AI \"board partner\" since its 2016 rebrand. Use them as a diligence voice and a route into Big Tech data talent, not as a source of deals: no AI deal names them.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they matter:** about 30 years of running data-science orgs at consumer-scale companies. The Compound bio lists YouTube, Google Maps, Facebook News Feed, Instagram and the Chan Zuckerberg Initiative, and gives their current role as Director of Special Projects at Apple ([src](https://www.compound.vc/team/danz)).\n- **What they likely do at Compound:** technical diligence on data and ML, and access to operator networks, for a small seed firm. That is inference. No source says what they do day to day.\n- **Constraint:** they are part-time, and Compound's funds are small ($50M in 2016 per [AlleyWatch](https://www.alleywatch.com/?p=57808); up to $115M in 2026 per the Form Ds in `firms/compound.md`). Their own site now presents them mainly as an executive coach, Zen teacher and technical advisor ([src](https://www.shmonk.com/organizations.html)).\n- **What they'd never do:** lead or price a round. Nothing shows them with their own capital at scale or leading any deal.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Compound bio may be stale.** It still lists Apple as their current job (\"Director of Special Projects at Apple\", [compound.vc](https://www.compound.vc/team/danz)). Their own site uses the past tense: they have \"led teams at Apple, Instagram, Facebook, YouTube, and Google\" and calls themselves \"a longtime tech executive\" ([shmonk.com](https://www.shmonk.com/)). Their day job now looks like executive coaching plus advising founders and CTOs on \"AI strategy\" ([src](https://www.shmonk.com/organizations.html)). This is an inference from tense and framing. No source gives an end date at Apple.\n- **They are one of Compound's oldest outside partners.** They were the only board partner named when Metamorphic Ventures rebranded to Compound with a $50M fund. At the time they were described as \"Director of Analytics at Facebook\" ([AlleyWatch, ~Oct 2016](https://www.alleywatch.com/?p=57808)). Separately, a MarketScreener insider record shows Compound Advisor LLC from 2016-09-30 (aggregator, [src](https://ca.marketscreener.com/insider/DAN-ZIGMOND-A2LML1/)).\n- **Their operating philosophy is empiricist.** In a TechCrunch op-ed co-written with Tara Cottrell they argued: \"Our beliefs should be based on data.\" ([TechCrunch, 2016-09-06](https://techcrunch.com/2016/09/06/buddha-was-a-data-scientist/)).\n- **Possible AI advisory trail (aggregator only, low confidence).** MarketScreener lists them as a director or board member of Zindi, Inc., the data-science competition platform, and as a former advisor to AliveCor, Talla, Kyron, Earnest, Medium and Eat Just ([src](https://ca.marketscreener.com/insider/DAN-ZIGMOND-A2LML1/)). The same record contains clear errors (it says Google was \"founded in 2013\"), so treat these as leads, not facts.\n- **Food-tech data executive.** Before Facebook they worked at Hampton Creek (now Eat Just) ([AlleyWatch](https://www.alleywatch.com/?p=57808)). An aggregator gives the title as VP of Data, 2014–2015 (unverified).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**low.** They are part-time, has no AI, AI-chip, robotics, lab or bio-AI deal attributed to them, and sits at a sub-$10M seed firm. They are useful to the owner in two ways:\n- **Diligence.** They have deep data-science and ML organization experience at Google, Meta and Apple. They could reference-check founders from those orgs, and their site says they advise on \"AI strategy\" for CTOs.\n- **Talent.** As an executive coach to tech leaders, they probably see senior Big Tech AI people before they leave. That is inference.\n\nThey are not a co-investor peer for $20M checks.\n\n## Background\n- University of Pennsylvania undergraduate, conferred 1990 (aggregator, [MarketScreener](https://ca.marketscreener.com/insider/DAN-ZIGMOND-A2LML1/)).\n- Late-1990s interactive TV work: IETF RFCs 2718, 2728 and 2838 (1999–2000) ([src](https://www.shmonk.com/writing.html)). Aggregator-listed employers include WebTV and Wink Communications ([roundfunded](https://www.roundfunded.com/en/investor/dan-zigmond)).\n- **Google:** TV-ads research, including \"Google's Auction for TV Ads\" (ICALP 2009) ([src](https://www.shmonk.com/writing.html)). Data-science teams at YouTube and Google Maps ([compound.vc](https://www.compound.vc/team/danz)).\n- **Hampton Creek (Eat Just)**, before Facebook ([AlleyWatch](https://www.alleywatch.com/?p=57808)).\n- **Facebook:** Director of Analytics, about 2016 (News Feed and core products), then Instagram ([AlleyWatch](https://www.alleywatch.com/?p=57808); [compound.vc](https://www.compound.vc/team/danz)).\n- **Chan Zuckerberg Initiative:** data-science leadership ([compound.vc](https://www.compound.vc/team/danz)). Search snippets of aggregator profiles mention a Senior Director of Data Science and Engineering title for 2016–2019 (unverified).\n- **Apple:** Director of Special Projects ([compound.vc](https://www.compound.vc/team/danz)). Dates not found.\n- **Compound:** Board Partner from Oct 2016, now Venture Partner, Data Science & AI.\n- **Other:** Zen priest; author of *Buddha's Diet* and *Buddha's Office* (2019); trustee of Naropa University; computational-linguistics papers on the Pāli Canon (2021, 2023); epidemic-modeling papers in *Physical Biology* (2020–2021) ([src](https://www.shmonk.com/writing.html); [src](https://www.shmonk.com/organizations.html)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.shmonk.com/ | Coaching and teaching site, (c) 2026 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found (the Compound bio's X button is a dead \"#\" link) | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/danzigmond | not reviewed |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Zen and mindfulness podcasts only (for example, a *Buddha's Office* interview listed on [Listen Notes](https://www.listennotes.com/top-podcasts/dan-zigmond/)). Not reviewed, and not about investing.\n- Talks at Wisdom 2.0 and Inner MBA per their site ([src](https://www.shmonk.com/organizations.html)).\n- No investing or AI podcast found.\n\n## How they think\n- Empiricist: test, keep what works, drop what doesn't ([TechCrunch 2016](https://techcrunch.com/2016/09/06/buddha-was-a-data-scientist/)).\n- Applies computational methods to unusual domains (Pāli-canon NLP, epidemic models). That points to a technical, research-minded profile.\n- No public views on frontier AI, labs, chips or venture found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Compound bio](https://www.compound.vc/team/danz): their roles at a glance.\n2. [AlleyWatch on the 2016 Compound rebrand](https://www.alleywatch.com/?p=57808): when and why they joined.\n3. [Buddha Was a Data Scientist, TechCrunch 2016](https://techcrunch.com/2016/09/06/buddha-was-a-data-scientist/): their data-first worldview.\n4. [shmonk.com organizations page](https://www.shmonk.com/organizations.html): what they do now (coaching, AI-strategy advising).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. There is no active public X or Substack presence.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| nothing found | No deal names them personally. Compound's AI deals (Deepgram, Wayve, Runway, Prime Intellect, etc.) are firm-level, with no partner attributed except Dempsey on Runway | | | | [firms/compound.md](../firms/compound.md) |\n\nAggregator-only advisory and board ties (not investments, low confidence): Zindi (director), AliveCor, Talla, Kyron, Earnest, Medium, Eat Just, Nova Labs, Stowaway Cosmetics ([MarketScreener](https://ca.marketscreener.com/insider/DAN-ZIGMOND-A2LML1/)).\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Compound / Michael Dempsey | Board Partner, then Venture Partner | 2016-10 | [AlleyWatch](https://www.alleywatch.com/?p=57808) |\n| Tara Cottrell | Co-author (TechCrunch op-ed; *Buddha's Diet*) | 2016 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2016/09/06/buddha-was-a-data-scientist/) |\n| Apple | Director of Special Projects (current per Compound; possibly former) | unknown | [compound.vc](https://www.compound.vc/team/danz) |\n| Facebook / Instagram | Director of Analytics | ~2016 | [AlleyWatch](https://www.alleywatch.com/?p=57808) |\n| Chan Zuckerberg Initiative | Data-science leader | unknown | [compound.vc](https://www.compound.vc/team/danz) |\n| Zindi | Director (aggregator) | unknown | [MarketScreener](https://ca.marketscreener.com/insider/DAN-ZIGMOND-A2LML1/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is they still at Apple? What does \"Special Projects\" cover, and when did they join and leave?\n- Which Compound deals have they diligenced or sourced, especially \"Local Compute Co.\" and the stealth robotics companies?\n- When was they retitled from Board Partner to Venture Partner?\n- Is the Zindi board seat real, and did Compound invest?\n- Does the ~$150K typical check refer to their own angel checks?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.compound.vc/team/danz\n- https://www.alleywatch.com/?p=57808 (Compound rebrand / new fund, ~Oct 2016)\n- https://www.shmonk.com/ ; https://www.shmonk.com/organizations.html ; https://www.shmonk.com/writing.html\n- https://techcrunch.com/2016/09/06/buddha-was-a-data-scientist/\n- https://ca.marketscreener.com/insider/DAN-ZIGMOND-A2LML1/ (aggregator; contains errors)\n- https://www.roundfunded.com/en/investor/dan-zigmond (aggregator)\n- https://www.listennotes.com/top-podcasts/dan-zigmond/\n\n**Not confirmed:** the current Apple status; Apple, CZI and Facebook dates; the Zindi and other advisory roles (aggregator only); the check size; any deal attribution. Crunchbase (403) reportedly lists a Headspace \"VP of Data and AI\" role. A search found only Headspace job postings for that title, so it was not used.\n", "person:daniel-gross": "\n# Daniel Gross\n\n> Profile written 2026-11-10 (sweep user-funds-1). Web search was rate-limited for this pass; everything below comes from direct fetches of his site, company posts, SSI, Pioneer, AI Grant, YC, TIME, TechCrunch, Upstarts and ctech. Deals already in `data/deals.csv` are kept and cross-referenced; new ones are in `data/rows/daniel-gross-deals.csv`.\n\n## One-line take\nFor three years (2022 to 2025) he was, with Nat Friedman, the most aggressive individual lead in frontier AI: labs, chips, compute and the GPU-for-equity model. Since 2025-07 he has been inside Meta running compute and is **not an active investor**. Watch his book through Hersh Desai at NFDG, and watch him as a buyer of compute.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he won:** he was early on talent and on compute. In 2017 he started YC's AI track ([src](https://www.ycombinator.com/blog/yc-ai)). In 2022-08 he co-led Carmack's AGI seed, three months before ChatGPT ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2022/08/19/john-carmack-agi-keen-raises-20-million-from-sequoia-nat-friedman-and-others/)). In 2023 he turned scarce GPUs into deal access, describing the cluster as \"effectively the equivalent of a white hot oven\" ([src](https://time.com/collection/time100-ai/6310591/daniel-gross/)). Gross and Friedman traded cluster access for equity (same source).\n- **Constraint now:** he works for Meta. His own site says \"Runs compute for Meta\" ([src](https://danielgross.com/)). NFDG stopped new fund investing in 2025-07 and is run by Desai ([src](https://www.saastr.com/the-1-1b-vc-fund-that-4xd-in-two-years-then-got-acquired-by-meta)). He left SSI on 2025-06-29 ([src](https://ssi.inc/updates)).\n- **What he'd never do:** take a slow, committee-driven process. He decides on short deadlines: he gave the Andromeda CEO a weekend to accept ([src](https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/andromeda-ai-compute-startup-raises-60m)), and he says he prefers intuition and taste over data-driven optimisation ([src](https://fs.blog/knowledge-project/daniel-gross/), highlights page).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The compute desk.** In 2026-01 he posted on X that he was building a compute desk for Meta ([src](https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/andromeda-ai-compute-startup-raises-60m), Upstarts paraphrase; the X post itself was not fetched). His site now says \"Runs compute for Meta.\" ([src](https://danielgross.com/)). Upstarts also says NFDG \"had ended up running a compute trading operation\" via Andromeda. He is now doing the same job for a hyperscaler, which makes him a counterparty for AI-compute companies, not a check writer.\n- **How he hires.** He called Wil Moushey, a founder he had backed whose startup had just shut down, the Friday before Thanksgiving 2023: \"I need your answer on Monday\" ([src](https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/andromeda-ai-compute-startup-raises-60m)). Moushey became Andromeda's CEO. NFDG committed over $100M upfront and reached 4,000+ GPUs by early 2024 (same source). Friedman and Gross no longer have an active role in Andromeda.\n- **Compute-for-equity logic.** Models need the cluster \"just once or twice to train\" ([src](https://time.com/collection/time100-ai/6310591/daniel-gross/), 2023-09-07). That is why a shared, rotating cluster worked as a wedge for a portfolio.\n- **He led Perplexity personally.** \"The new investment was led by Daniel Gross\" (company post, 2024-04-23). The $62.7M round doubled Perplexity to over $1B and brought in Druckenmiller, Gerstner, Garry Tan, Dylan Field and Lip-Bu Tan ([src](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-launches-enterprise-pro)). He co-founded SSI two months later.\n- **The frontier-lab pattern predates NFDG.** Carmack's own words: the Keen round was \"led by @natfriedman and @danielgross\", with Sequoia only participating, on 2022-08-19 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2022/08/19/john-carmack-agi-keen-raises-20-million-from-sequoia-nat-friedman-and-others/)).\n- **Robot factories since 2017.** His YC AI post carried one RFS: \"We also have one specific RFS: Robot Factories.\" ([src](https://www.ycombinator.com/blog/yc-ai)). Seven years later he co-led The Bot Company's $150M launch round ([src](https://www.therobotreport.com/the-bot-company-led-by-kyle-vogt-brings-in-another-150m/)).\n- **The SSI exit was terse, and Meta came first.** SSI's note: \"as of June 29 he is officially no longer a part of SSI\" ([src](https://ssi.inc/updates)). ctech reports that Meta tried and failed to buy SSI outright, then hired Gross and Friedman and took a stake in NFDG ([src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/4oj53t7pf)). His farewell line: \"I expect miracles to follow\" (same source).\n- **He still re-ups after the move.** NewLimit's 2026-06-02 $435M Series C lists returning investors \"Nat Friedman/Daniel Gross\" ([src](https://blog.newlimit.com/p/newlimit-raises-435m-led-by-founders)). Personal or NFDG capital still follows winners, even with both of them at Meta.\n- **AI Grant terms:** \"$250k investment via a no cap, no discount MFN SAFE\". Friedman and Gross put $10M into AI Grant, which is now run by Hersh Desai and Lenny Bogdonoff ([src](https://aigrant.com/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High on the book, low as a current check writer.** His deals are exactly the owner's lens: a frontier lab (SSI, Keen, Magic), AI chips (MatX, Groq on his own site), compute (CoreWeave B, Andromeda) and very large seeds (The Bot Company $150M, EvolutionaryScale $142M, MatX $24.9M). He no longer deploys, though. Practical uses:\n1. **Hersh Desai controls NFDG's follow-ons**, so he is the person to contact for co-invest or secondaries in Gross's book.\n2. **Gross is a compute buyer at Meta**, relevant to any AI-infra or chip company the owner backs.\n3. **Signal value:** what he backed personally before NFDG (Keen 2022, Chroma 2022, AssemblyAI 2022) was early, and these are names the owner can track.\n\n## Background\n- Born 1991 in Jerusalem. Accepted to YC as a teenager, reportedly the youngest founder at the time ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daniel_Gross_(businessman)), citing Times of Israel).\n- 2010: co-founded Greplin (YC W10) with Robby Walker ([src](https://www.ycombinator.com/blog/welcome-daniel-nicole-stephanie-steven-and-tatyana)). Renamed Cue in 2012. Raised from Sequoia (2011) and Index (2012), per Wikipedia.\n- 2013: Apple acquired Cue for a reported $40-60M ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2013/10/03/cue-acquired-for-over-40m-likely-by-apple-to-compete-with-google-now/)). He ran AI and search projects at Apple from 2013 to 2017 ([src](https://danielgross.com/)).\n- 2017-01-10: joined YC as a partner ([src](https://www.ycombinator.com/blog/welcome-daniel-nicole-stephanie-steven-and-tatyana)). Launched the YC AI track on 2017-03-19 ([src](https://www.ycombinator.com/blog/yc-ai)). Departure date from YC not found.\n- 2018: co-founded Pioneer with Rishi Narang, a remote accelerator and fund ([src](https://pioneer.app/)). Pioneer says it backed 150+ companies and has made its final investment ([src](https://pioneer.app/blog/pioneer/)). The homepage dates the stop to 2024.\n- 2021 to 2023: AI Grant with Friedman. NFDG fund (about $1.1B). Andromeda cluster ([src](https://www.saastr.com/the-1-1b-vc-fund-that-4xd-in-two-years-then-got-acquired-by-meta)).\n- 2024-06-19: co-founded SSI with Ilya Sutskever and Daniel Levy ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/19/ilya-sutskever-openais-former-chief-scientist-launches-new-ai-company/)). Left 2025-06-29 ([src](https://ssi.inc/updates)).\n- 2025-07 to now: Meta. He runs compute ([src](https://danielgross.com/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://danielgross.com/ (dcgross.com redirects here) | Checked 2026-11-10: bio and investment list; contact d@dcgross.com |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Own site has no essays |\n| X | https://x.com/danielgross | Handle confirmed by Carmack's tag in TechCrunch 2022; follower count not checked (X not fetchable) |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | not checked | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2019-04-13: The Knowledge Project #56, \"Catalyzing Success\" ([src](https://fs.blog/knowledge-project/daniel-gross/)). On talent: \"there's a shortage of positive feedback\". On decisions: \"Let's use our intuition and taste to just make a decision.\" (Highlights page; the full transcript is members-only.)\n- 2023-08-24: Stratechery interview with Gross and Friedman on the AI hype cycle, data constraints and Nvidia ([src](https://stratechery.com/2023/an-interview-with-daniel-gross-and-nat-friedman-about-the-ai-hype-cycle/)). Paywalled, not mined.\n- 2023-09-07: TIME100 AI profile with quotes on compute ([src](https://time.com/collection/time100-ai/6310591/daniel-gross/)).\n\n## How they think\n- **Compute is the scarce input**, so own it and trade it for equity (TIME 2023). He has now carried that view into Meta.\n- **Talent arbitrage:** Pioneer's premise was finding ambitious people worldwide who lack opportunity, and giving them feedback ([src](https://fs.blog/knowledge-project/daniel-gross/)).\n- **Intuition over data:** he says data-driven optimisation traps companies at local maxima (same source).\n- **On capital for labs:** \"raising capital is not going to be one of them\" (SSI launch, [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/19/ilya-sutskever-openais-former-chief-scientist-launches-new-ai-company/)).\n- **Right calls:** ElevenLabs A (2023), Perplexity (2024), CoreWeave B (2023), MatX seed. **Open / wrong:** Pioneer stopped investing. His SSI tenure lasted about a year.\n\n## Read these first\n- YC AI post (2017): what he looked for in AI teams, plus the Robot Factories RFS ([src](https://www.ycombinator.com/blog/yc-ai)).\n- TIME100 AI (2023): the compute-as-oven thesis ([src](https://time.com/collection/time100-ai/6310591/daniel-gross/)).\n- Upstarts on Andromeda (2026-03): how the GPU desk was built and who runs it now ([src](https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/andromeda-ai-compute-startup-raises-60m)).\n- SSI updates: the exit note ([src](https://ssi.inc/updates)).\n- \"Talent\" (book with Tyler Cowen, Stripe Press 2022): not fetched in this pass (Stripe Press page returned 404).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot mined: X is not fetchable from this environment. His 2026-01 compute-desk post is known only through Upstarts.\n\n## Portfolio\nDeals where he is personally named. Rows marked † are new in this pass; the others already exist in `data/deals.csv`.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| AssemblyAI † | Series A ($28M) | 2022-03-04 | no | Accel (lead), Nat Friedman | [src](https://www.assemblyai.com/blog/series-a-announcement) |\n| Chroma † | Pre-seed (AI Grant) | 2022-05 | co-led | AIX, Bloomberg Beta, Friedman | [src](https://www.trychroma.com/blog/seed) |\n| Keen Technologies † | Seed ($20M) | 2022-08-19 | co-led | Friedman, Collison, Lutke, Sequoia, Jim Keller | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2022/08/19/john-carmack-agi-keen-raises-20-million-from-sequoia-nat-friedman-and-others/) |\n| CoreWeave | Series B ($221M) | 2023-04-20 | no | Magnetar (lead), NVIDIA, Friedman | [src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20230418006139/en/CoreWeave-Raises-%24221M-Series-B-to-Expand-Specialized-Cloud-Infrastructure-Powering-the-Generative-AI-and-Large-Language-Model-Boom) |\n| ElevenLabs | Series A ($19M) | 2023-06-20 | co-led | a16z, Friedman | [src](https://elevenlabs.io/blog/elevenlabs-launches-new-generative-voice-ai-products-and-announces-19m-series-a-round-led-by-nat-friedman-daniel-gross-and-andreessen-horowitz) |\n| ggml | Pre-seed | 2023-06 | yes | Friedman | [src](https://ggml.ai/) |\n| Pika | Pre-seed and seed | 2023 | yes | Friedman | [src](https://petapixel.com/2023/11/28/generative-video-startup-pika-labs-launches-version-1-0-raises-55-million-in-funding) |\n| Weights & Biases | Strategic ($50M at $1.25B) | 2023-08-10 | co-led | Friedman | [src](https://wandb.ai/site/press-release/weights-biases-raises-50-million-round-led-by-daniel-gross-and-nat-friedman-announces-wb-prompts/) |\n| AssemblyAI † | Series C ($50M) | 2023-12-03 | no | (returning) | [src](https://www.assemblyai.com/blog/announcing-our-50m-series-c-to-build-superhuman-speech-ai-models) |\n| MatX | Seed ($24.9M) | 2023-12 | yes | Friedman | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/11/22/ai-chip-startup-matx-founded-by-google-alums-raises-series-a-at-300m-valuation-sources-say/) |\n| Magic | Series B ($117M at $500M) | 2024-02-15 | yes | Friedman | [src](https://www.trendingtopics.eu/magic-in-wien-gegruendetes-ai-startup-holt-117-mio-dollar/) |\n| Perplexity † | Series B ($62.7M at >$1B; \"B-1\") | 2024-04-23 | yes | Druckenmiller, Gerstner, Garry Tan, Field | [src](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-launches-enterprise-pro) |\n| The Bot Company | Seed ($150M at $550M) | 2024-05 | co-led | Friedman, Nabeel Hyatt (Spark) | [src](https://www.therobotreport.com/the-bot-company-led-by-kyle-vogt-brings-in-another-150m/) |\n| Suno | Series B ($125M) | 2024-05-21 | no | Lightspeed (lead) | [src](https://suno.com/blog/fundraising-announcement-may-2024) |\n| Klarity | Series B ($70M) | 2024-06-24 | yes | Scale VP, Tola | [src](https://www.within.ai/resources/blog/series-b-funding) |\n| EvolutionaryScale | Seed ($142M) | 2024-06-25 | co-led | Friedman, Lux | [src](https://www.gunder.com/en/news-insights/client-news/ai-biology-startup-evolutionaryscale-announces-dollar142m-seed-financing) |\n| SSI | First round ($1B) | 2024-09-04 | no (insider; co-founder) | a16z, Sequoia, DST, SV Angel | [src](https://ssi.inc/updates) |\n| Basis | Series A ($34M) | 2024-12-17 | no | Khosla (lead) | [src](https://www.getbasis.ai/blogs/basis-raises-a-34m-series-a-led-by-khosla-ventures) |\n| Mechanize † | Seed (launch) | 2025-04-17 | no | | [src](https://www.mechanize.work/announcing-mechanize-inc) |\n| NewLimit † | Series B ($130M) | 2025-05-06 | no | | [src](https://blog.newlimit.com/p/newlimit-raises-130-million-series) |\n| Pulse † | Seed ($3.9M) | 2025-12-17 (blog) or 2025-02 (BI via Wikipedia); unconfirmed | co-led (as NFDG) | YC, Soma, Liquid 2 | [src](https://www.runpulse.com/blog/pulse-seed-nfdg-venture) |\n| NewLimit † | Series C ($435M) | 2026-06-02 | no | Founders Fund (lead), Kleiner, Abstract, Valor | [src](https://blog.newlimit.com/p/newlimit-raises-435m-led-by-founders) |\n| Inception †, Groq †, Character.AI †, Coinbase †, GitHub †, Figma †, Rippling †, Notion †, SpaceX †, Uber †, Instacart † | Undisclosed | n/a | n/a | | [own site](https://danielgross.com/), [TIME](https://time.com/collection/time100-ai/6310591/daniel-gross/), [Inception](https://www.inceptionlabs.ai/about) |\n\nNFDG-only deals that don't name him personally (Granola B, Juicebox seed, AIUC seed, Traversal, Generalist, Medra, XBOW) are in `firms/nfdg.md`.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Perplexity 2024-04:** a personal lead that doubled the valuation and pulled in a who's-who of angels. The intro path was not found.\n- **The Bot Company 2024-05:** a $150M launch round led by Friedman, Gross and Nabeel Hyatt, not by a fund. Greenoaks priced the next round at $2B about ten months later ([src](https://www.therobotreport.com/the-bot-company-led-by-kyle-vogt-brings-in-another-150m/)).\n- **SSI 2024-2025:** co-founder. The $1B first round came about 3 months after launch. Meta's failed acquisition attempt preceded his departure ([src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/4oj53t7pf)).\n- **Andromeda 2023:** Gross recruited the CEO over one weekend. It is now spun out with Paradigm and NFDG as investors ([src](https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/andromeda-ai-compute-startup-raises-60m)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Nat Friedman | Co-investor; NFDG co-founder; both at Meta | 2022 (Keen) | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2022/08/19/john-carmack-agi-keen-raises-20-million-from-sequoia-nat-friedman-and-others/) |\n| Ilya Sutskever, Daniel Levy | SSI co-founders (until 2025-06-29) | 2024-06 | [src](https://ssi.inc/updates) |\n| Hersh Desai | Runs NFDG and AI Grant now | n/a | [src](https://aigrant.com/) |\n| Wil Moushey | Hired as Andromeda CEO | 2023-11 | [src](https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/andromeda-ai-compute-startup-raises-60m) |\n| Rishi Narang | Pioneer co-founder | 2018 | [src](https://pioneer.app/) |\n| Kyle Vogt | Backed The Bot Company at launch | 2024 | [src](https://www.therobotreport.com/the-bot-company-led-by-kyle-vogt-brings-in-another-150m/) |\n| Meta | Employer; runs compute | 2025-07 | [src](https://danielgross.com/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- His formal title at Meta and whether the \"compute desk\" buys from neoclouds or startups.\n- Whether NewLimit 2026 and Pulse were personal checks or NFDG run-off, and which date is right for Pulse.\n- When he left YC (2017-2018?) and when he stepped back from Pioneer.\n- Round and date for his Groq and Character.AI checks.\n- The Talent book and the Stratechery transcripts were not mined.\n\n## Sources\n- https://danielgross.com/\n- https://ssi.inc/updates\n- https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/andromeda-ai-compute-startup-raises-60m\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/4oj53t7pf\n- https://time.com/collection/time100-ai/6310591/daniel-gross/\n- https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-launches-enterprise-pro\n- https://techcrunch.com/2022/08/19/john-carmack-agi-keen-raises-20-million-from-sequoia-nat-friedman-and-others/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/19/ilya-sutskever-openais-former-chief-scientist-launches-new-ai-company/\n- https://www.ycombinator.com/blog/welcome-daniel-nicole-stephanie-steven-and-tatyana\n- https://www.ycombinator.com/blog/yc-ai\n- https://pioneer.app/ and https://pioneer.app/blog/pioneer/\n- https://aigrant.com/\n- https://fs.blog/knowledge-project/daniel-gross/\n- https://blog.newlimit.com/p/newlimit-raises-435m-led-by-founders\n- https://www.runpulse.com/blog/pulse-seed-nfdg-venture\n- https://www.assemblyai.com/blog/series-a-announcement\n- https://www.therobotreport.com/the-bot-company-led-by-kyle-vogt-brings-in-another-150m/\n- https://www.saastr.com/the-1-1b-vc-fund-that-4xd-in-two-years-then-got-acquired-by-meta\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daniel_Gross_(businessman) (secondary)\n\nPhoto: none set. His own site has no headshot and there is no firm team page. A Wikimedia Commons photo (checked 2026-11-10: HTTP 200, image/jpeg) is already in `data/rows/nfdg-team-photos.csv`, but it is not from a firm or own site, so it is not used here.\n", "person:daniel-mulet": "\n# Daniel Mulet\n\n## One-line take\nDaniel Mulet is Radical's research scout for science and deep tech: a former Creative Destruction Lab quantum-program builder who now farms NeurIPS for pre-formation researcher-founders, and the partner on Artificial Agency. [src](https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/game-change-artificial-agency-raises-16m-aims-to-revolutionize-gaming/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He runs the researcher-cultivation side of Radical's \"company formation with academics\" model. He talks to researchers \"[for] three months [to] a year before they're ready to actually start the company,\" and uses conferences to recruit for portfolio companies and line up professors as diligence advisers. The Logic uses he/his for him. [src](https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/) (2026-01-06)\n- **What constrains him:** His remit is climate, life sciences, materials and deep tech, not frontier labs or compute [src](https://radical.vc/team/daniel-mulet/). Many of the companies on his team page were led by more senior partners (Poutanen on Crossing Minds and Genesis, Teja on Aspect, Toews on Reka, Katz on CentML), so his own lead record is thin and recent.\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** back an idea that a big lab will ship as a feature. On the last day of NeurIPS he tests whether ideas are feasible and whether big tech will build them first, citing 2017–2020 dev-tool startups that model makers made obsolete. [src](https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/)\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Pre-formation timeline:** \"We talk to these people [for] three months [to] a year\" before founding. Radical's sourcing starts well before a company exists. [src](https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/) (2026-01-06)\n- **Papers are stale by the conference:** \"None of the research is new\" because preprints are out \"for at least six months\", so NeurIPS is for people, not papers. [src](https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/)\n- **He sets his own research agenda from the conference:** \"come back with a research agenda for myself for the next year.\" [src](https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/)\n- **Recruiting as a value-add:** \"I talk the company up a little bit.\" He tells founders to \"bring the most impressive person you're trying to recruit\" to Radical's dinners. The 2025 dinner for about 30 people included founders from Artificial Agency, Cohere, P-1 and Yutori plus academics from MIT, Montreal, Stanford and Toronto. [src](https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/)\n- **World-models thesis:** with world models, \"you need less money and time to get to an economically viable task\" for robots. [src](https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/)\n- **Live deal flow from spinouts:** at NeurIPS 2025 he flagged two startups spinning out of doctoral work (a recommender system and chip-design tools). Radical \"closed one investment during the conference\". [src](https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/)\n- **Reasoning over scale:** his NeurIPS 2025 recap says \"The era of pure pattern matching is evolving into an era of deliberate reasoning\", and the essay is framed \"Smarter, Not Just Bigger\" (efficiency work such as Gated Attention). [src](https://radical.vc/articles/highlights-from-neurips-2025/) (2025-12)\n- **Quantum roots:** as CDL associate director in 2017 he pitched quantum ML partnerships: \"the time to start thinking about these partnerships is now.\" [src](https://betakit.com/application-deadline-for-creative-destruction-labs-quantum-machine-learning-stream-hits-july-31/) (2017-07-25)\n- **Timing, first in:** Radical was Chemix's pre-seed investor before Mayfield led the $7M seed in June 2022 [src](https://www.mayfield.com/?p=1516). Mulet is the Radical partner credited on Chemix [src](https://radical.vc/team/daniel-mulet/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** His sector (AI for science, materials, climate and bio) is adjacent to my lens, not core: he isn't on frontier-lab, chip or compute deals as the lead. He matters for two reasons. (1) He is the Radical person most visibly working the researcher pipeline at NeurIPS months before companies form, which is exactly where $600M new-lab seeds come from. (2) He is a Toronto/CDL ecosystem node (Hinton, Vector, U of T). The deals that matter to me are Reka (frontier lab; he is credited, Toews led) and CentML (compute; credited, Katz led). Chemix and Artificial Agency show his own early-entry style. As a principal-turned-partner in 2024 at a fund with a US$1B+ Breakouts vehicle, he fits the \"recently promoted partner at a fund that can write $10M+\" segment.\n\n## Background\n- Born and raised in Guatemala; Lycée Jules Verne du Guatemala baccalaureate 1996–2001 (LinkedIn mirror). [src](https://radical.vc/team/daniel-mulet/) [src](https://app.icebreaker.xyz/profiles/linkedin-daniel-mulet-3ab85620)\n- University of Guelph BA. Radical says Political Science & Economics; the LinkedIn mirror says International Development and Political Science, 2001–2006. LSE certificate in Development Economics/Entrepreneurship (2003, LinkedIn mirror). [src](https://radical.vc/team/daniel-mulet/) [src](https://app.icebreaker.xyz/profiles/linkedin-daniel-mulet-3ab85620)\n- NYU MS. Radical says Global Affairs; the LinkedIn mirror says Energy and Environmental Policy, 2009–2011. Sources conflict, and both are recorded here.\n- Creative Destruction Lab: associate director by July 2017. He helped scale CDL and \"built the world's first quantum software incubator\" (radical.vc bio). [src](https://betakit.com/application-deadline-for-creative-destruction-labs-quantum-machine-learning-stream-hits-july-31/) [src](https://radical.vc/team/daniel-mulet/)\n- Radical Ventures: Associate 2019-08, Principal 2022-01, Partner 2024-10 (LinkedIn mirror, aggregator; no firm announcement found). [src](https://app.icebreaker.xyz/profiles/linkedin-daniel-mulet-3ab85620)\n- An aggregator claims he co-founded \"Sweetbark Maple Syrup\". This is unverified.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/daniel-mulet/ | Firm bio, checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://radical.vc/articles/highlights-from-neurips-2025/ | Occasional Radical essays (NeurIPS recap 2025-12; Artificial Agency post 2024-07) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/dmulet | Unverified: listed by vcsheet (aggregator) only; x.com was not reachable (402). Not linked from radical.vc |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/daniel-mulet-3ab85620/ | Linked from his radical.vc page (verified) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-01 — The Logic (feature, not a podcast) — \"What an investor does at the world's biggest AI research conference\" by Murad Hemmadi — https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/ — a day-by-day account of how he sources at NeurIPS (see Differential insight).\n- 2017-07 — BetaKit — CDL quantum ML stream launch, quoted as associate director — https://betakit.com/application-deadline-for-creative-destruction-labs-quantum-machine-learning-stream-hits-july-31/\n- No podcast appearances found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** AI for scientific discovery (biology, chemistry, cosmology); world models and spatial intelligence lowering the cost of useful robotics; reasoning and efficiency over raw scale; AI in games as a testbed (Artificial Agency). [src](https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/highlights-from-neurips-2025/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/game-change-artificial-agency-raises-16m-aims-to-revolutionize-gaming/)\n- **Contrarian views:** conference papers are already stale (\"None of the research is new\"), and the edge is people and timing: \"finding the right investment opportunity with the right timing.\" [src](https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/)\n- **Calls:** early on photonic quantum through CDL (Xanadu later IPO'd; Radical was in its 2019 Series A, just before Mulet joined) [src](https://www.osler.com/en/expertise/deals-cases/xanadu-quantum-technologies-inc). Chemix pre-seed (first in). No admitted mistakes found.\n\n## Read these first\n- The Logic, \"What an investor does at the world's biggest AI research conference\" (2026-01-06): his sourcing method, in his own words.\n- \"Highlights From NeurIPS 2025\" (radical.vc, 2025-12): his technical priorities (System 2 reasoning, gated attention, spatial intelligence).\n- \"Game Change: Artificial Agency raises $16M\" (radical.vc, 2024-07-22): his one bylined investment post.\n- BetaKit CDL quantum ML stream (2017-07-25): his pre-VC role.\n\n## Social engagement\nvcsheet (aggregator) paraphrases his posts as covering applied AI in agriculture and biology, compute and data infrastructure, search and LLMs, North American ML ecosystems, teleoperation, and NeurIPS attendance [src](https://www.vcsheet.com/who/daniel-mulet). This was not verified on X directly.\n\n## Portfolio\nTwo tiers: deals where he is personally named (byline, board or quote), and companies only listed as \"works with\" on his team page.\n\n**Named on (byline/quote):**\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Artificial Agency | Seed ($16M total; reported as a $12M seed co-led by Radical and Toyota Ventures plus a $4M pre-seed) | 2024-07 | co-led (Radical + Toyota Ventures) | Toyota Ventures, Flying Fish, Kaya, BDC Deep Tech, Tirta Ventures | [Mulet byline](https://radical.vc/articles/game-change-artificial-agency-raises-16m-aims-to-revolutionize-gaming/), [Osler](https://osler.com/en/about-us/representative-work/artificial-agency) |\n\n**Team-page \"works with\" (his personal deal role unconfirmed):**\n| Company | Round (Radical) | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Chemix | Pre-seed (Radical was the pre-seed investor), then the $7M seed (Mayfield led) and later rounds | pre-2022-06; 2022-06 | no (seed) | Mayfield, Ibex | [Mayfield](https://www.mayfield.com/?p=1516), [team page](https://radical.vc/team/daniel-mulet/) |\n| You.com | Series A $25M (Radical's \"second investment\"; Radical not named in the Nov 2021 $20M seed) | 2022-07-14 | yes (firm) | Time Ventures, Breyer, Norwest, Day One | [The Logic](https://thelogic.co/briefing/radical-ventures-leads-us25m-series-a-in-you-com/), [Euronews seed](https://www.euronews.com/next/2021/11/10/you-funding) |\n| Reka | Seed (founding investor; Toews board) | 2022 | yes (firm; Toews) | — | [radical.vc](https://radical.vc/custom-ai-models-for-the-enterprise/) |\n| CentML | Seed $3.5M (founding; Katz byline) | 2023-06 | yes (firm; Katz) | — | [radical.vc](https://radical.vc/articles/centml-takes-on-global-ai-chip-bottleneck/) |\n| Crossing Minds | Series A $10M (Poutanen joined board) | 2021-10-18 | yes (firm; Poutanen) | Index, Partech, Lerer Hippeau | [release](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/crossing-minds-secures-10-million-124000779.html) |\n| Genesis Therapeutics (now Genesis Molecular AI) | Series A $52M (Rock Springs led; Poutanen named) | 2020-12-02 | no | Menlo, a16z | [The Logic](https://thelogic.co/briefing/genesis-therapeutics-raises-us52-million-from-major-investors-including-radical-ventures/) |\n| ClimateAi | Series A (Patel byline) | 2021-07 | yes (firm; Patel) | FootPrint Coalition | [radical.vc](https://radical.vc/articles/our-investment-in-climateai/) |\n| Aspect Biosystems (non-AI) | Series A US$20M (Teja board) | 2020-01 | yes (firm; Teja) | Pangaea, Pallasite, Rhino | [Private Capital Journal](https://privatecapitaljournal.com/aspect-biosystems-secures-us-20m-series-a-led-by-radical-ventures/) |\n| Xanadu (quantum, non-AI) | Series A $32M (OMERS led), before his 2019-08 start | 2019-06 | no | OMERS, Georgian, Real, Draper | [Osler](https://www.osler.com/en/expertise/deals-cases/xanadu-quantum-technologies-inc) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Artificial Agency (2024):** Edmonton team of ex-DeepMind / University of Alberta researchers building a generative \"behavior engine\" for game characters. Mulet wrote Radical's post, saying \"We are excited to partner with this exceptional team\" and framing games as an AI testbed. Its founders attended Radical's NeurIPS 2025 dinner, so he is still actively supporting them. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/game-change-artificial-agency-raises-16m-aims-to-revolutionize-gaming/) [src](https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/)\n- **Chemix:** Radical came in at pre-seed and then followed Mayfield's $7M seed (2022-06), a $10M seed extension (2023, Preqin, aggregator) and a $20M A (2024). This is the \"first in, then follow\" pattern. His role is credited on the team page only. [src](https://www.mayfield.com/?p=1516)\n- **Intrepid Labs (inference only):** Radical led an unannounced US$4M pre-seed in this U of Toronto self-driving-lab spinout, which surfaced only when Avant Bio led the $7M seed in May 2025 [src](https://thelogic.co/briefing/toronto-ai-drug-developer-intrepid-launches-with-us7m-in-seed-funding/). It sits squarely in his life-sciences remit, and he named it among portfolio companies at NeurIPS, but no source names him on it.\n- **NeurIPS 2025:** five or more researcher meetings, four events, three poster sessions, two private dinners and two robotics startup visits. Radical co-hosted a party with Cohere, and its Hinton–Dean fireside chat (moderated by Jordan Jacobs) was about three times oversubscribed. [src](https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Partner (Associate 2019-08, Principal 2022-01) | 2019-08 | [team](https://radical.vc/team/daniel-mulet/), [LinkedIn mirror](https://app.icebreaker.xyz/profiles/linkedin-daniel-mulet-3ab85620) |\n| Creative Destruction Lab | Former associate director; built the quantum incubator | by 2017-07 | [BetaKit](https://betakit.com/application-deadline-for-creative-destruction-labs-quantum-machine-learning-stream-hits-july-31/) |\n| Artificial Agency | Investor (bylined Radical post) | 2024-07 | [radical.vc](https://radical.vc/articles/game-change-artificial-agency-raises-16m-aims-to-revolutionize-gaming/) |\n| Toyota Ventures | Co-lead on Artificial Agency seed | 2024-07 | [Osler](https://osler.com/en/about-us/representative-work/artificial-agency) |\n| Jordan Jacobs | Colleague; both ran Radical's NeurIPS 2025 programme | 2025-12 | [The Logic](https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What was Radical's first You.com investment (before the July 2022 A), and did Radical follow into the B, C or D? Was Mulet the lead on it?\n- Which investment did Radical close during NeurIPS 2025, and which closed in early 2026? Were they the recommender or chip-design spinouts?\n- Is Mulet the partner on Intrepid Labs, and on the 2026 science deals (Discovery Loop, Inherent, Periodic Labs)?\n- Confirm the promotion dates with a firm announcement, and confirm his X handle.\n- Education conflict: is the NYU degree in Global Affairs or Energy and Environmental Policy?\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/daniel-mulet/ (2026-11-10)\n- https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/ (2026-01-06)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/highlights-from-neurips-2025/ (2025-12)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/game-change-artificial-agency-raises-16m-aims-to-revolutionize-gaming/ (2024-07-22)\n- https://osler.com/en/about-us/representative-work/artificial-agency\n- https://betakit.com/application-deadline-for-creative-destruction-labs-quantum-machine-learning-stream-hits-july-31/ (2017-07-25)\n- https://www.mayfield.com/?p=1516 (2022-06)\n- https://thelogic.co/briefing/radical-ventures-leads-us25m-series-a-in-you-com/ (2022-07-14)\n- https://www.euronews.com/next/2021/11/10/you-funding\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/news/crossing-minds-secures-10-million-124000779.html (2021-10-18)\n- https://thelogic.co/briefing/genesis-therapeutics-raises-us52-million-from-major-investors-including-radical-ventures/ (2020-12-02)\n- https://privatecapitaljournal.com/aspect-biosystems-secures-us-20m-series-a-led-by-radical-ventures/\n- https://www.osler.com/en/expertise/deals-cases/xanadu-quantum-technologies-inc\n- https://thelogic.co/briefing/toronto-ai-drug-developer-intrepid-launches-with-us7m-in-seed-funding/\n- Aggregators: https://app.icebreaker.xyz/profiles/linkedin-daniel-mulet-3ab85620, https://www.vcsheet.com/who/daniel-mulet\n", "person:daniel-pontecorvo": "\n# Daniel Pontecorvo\n\n*Created 2026-11-11. Sources: the Firmus release, his Signals and Threads episode (full transcript read), Jane Street's Street View post on the Texas data center, and Bloomberg's 2026-06-04 data-center report (paywalled past the lede). The two Dwarkesh videos could not be transcribed (captions blocked), so nothing here quotes them.*\n\n## One-line take\nNot a dealmaker: he is the internal compute buyer Jane Street put on the record for Firmus. He is the person to reach if you sell data-center capacity, power or cooling to Jane Street. He is not the person who approves a seed check ([src](https://firmus.co/newsroom/firmus-announces-fully-subscribed-usdusd2-billion-strategic-equity-investment-to-accelerate-nvidia-ai-factory-expansion-across-australia-and-asia-pacific)).\n\n## Crux\n- **What makes him matter:** his team owns Jane Street's physical stack end to end: lease negotiation, design, construction and operations ([src](https://signalsandthreads.com/the-thermodynamics-of-trading/), 24:06). When Jane Street takes equity in an AI-factory builder, he is the one who would judge whether the capacity is real.\n- **What he personally sources, signs or prices:** **no source says.** The Firmus release quotes him but does not say he sourced, priced or approved the investment ([src](https://firmus.co/newsroom/firmus-announces-fully-subscribed-usdusd2-billion-strategic-equity-investment-to-accelerate-nvidia-ai-factory-expansion-across-australia-and-asia-pacific)). Jane Street publishes no investment-approval process. The firm page notes it is run informally by senior staff, with no named approver ([firms/jane-street.md](../firms/jane-street.md)).\n- **Constraint:** power density. Rack designs rose from 10–15 kW a decade ago to 170 kW ([src](https://signalsandthreads.com/the-thermodynamics-of-trading/), 33:41). Building that capacity, not chips, is what limits growth.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** hand a site wholly to a third party. He keeps his own people on site and says so directly ([src](https://signalsandthreads.com/the-thermodynamics-of-trading/), 22:38).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The user fronts the deal.** On Firmus, Jane Street put its data-center head on the release, not a corp-dev or investing person. In his words, compute access is the reason: \"access to reliable, high-performance compute becomes increasingly important\" ([src](https://firmus.co/newsroom/firmus-announces-fully-subscribed-usdusd2-billion-strategic-equity-investment-to-accelerate-nvidia-ai-factory-expansion-across-australia-and-asia-pacific), 2026-08-07). This matches the Antithesis pattern on the firm page, where an engineer user was quoted.\n- **Hands-on with operators:** \"We don’t just rely on a third party.\" ([src](https://signalsandthreads.com/the-thermodynamics-of-trading/), 22:38). Relationships with third-party operators are built \"from day one\", over months or years. He wants bad news early: \"We’d rather hear the bad news than be surprised later.\" (24:06).\n- **Bespoke GPU sites, not only retrofits:** \"the more bespoke and custom data centers that we’re building\" (34:43). Retrofitted trading sites sometimes hold one GPU machine per rack.\n- **Outsider to finance:** Jane Street was \"my first time working at a trading firm or financial firm\" (39:44). His prior employer is not found.\n- **Unconfirmed link:** Bloomberg reported on 2026-06-04 that Jane Street plans to build and finance its own data center and has talked to tech, crypto and finance companies about it ([src](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/crypto/jane-street-plans-new-data-center-as-compute-power-runs-scarce)). The article (as far as readable) does not name him. That his team runs it is **inference only**.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** He writes no checks, runs no fund and has no investing promotion path. Firmus is a US$2B strategic round at more than US$10.5B post-money, not a seed. He is useful in two ways. (1) For a frontier-compute or infra portfolio company, he is the buyer-side diligence gate inside Jane Street, which leads compute rounds as a customer (Etched, per the firm page). (2) He is a signal: when he is quoted, Jane Street is buying supply, not taking a financial view.\n\n## Background\n- About 13 years at Jane Street on the physical engineering team as of 2025-07-25, so he joined around 2012 ([src](https://signalsandthreads.com/the-thermodynamics-of-trading/), 00:03).\n- Jane Street was his first trading or financial firm (39:44). Education and earlier employers: not found.\n- Early project: moved trading desks onto wheels before Jane Street's move into its current building, so desks could be moved quickly (39:44).\n- His team covers architecture, mechanical and electrical engineering, and construction management. It has just started an internship (57:46) ([src](https://signalsandthreads.com/the-thermodynamics-of-trading/)).\n- Title \"Head of Physical Engineering\" per the Firmus release, 2026-08-07.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | not found | No Jane Street team page lists him (the firm publishes none) |\n| Substack / blog | not found | |\n| X | not found | |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | No profile URL confirmed from a primary source |\n| GitHub | not found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only (below) |\n| Photo | none | No headshot on a Jane Street or personal page; video stills not used |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** no direct channel is published. Two paths fit the evidence (both inferred). (1) Through the operator relationship: become a data-center, power or cooling vendor or site partner, since his team deals with them \"from day one\". (2) Through Ron Minsky, who co-leads Jane Street's tech group and hosted him on both appearances ([src](https://www.janestreet.com/culture/street-view/a-look-inside-our-newest-data-center/)).\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-07 — Signals and Threads (Jane Street, host Ron Minsky) — Ep. 24 \"The Thermodynamics of Trading\" (2025-07-25) — https://signalsandthreads.com/the-thermodynamics-of-trading/ — rack density from 10–15 kW to 170 kW; in-house on-site response; machine learning forcing liquid cooling. Full transcript read.\n- 2026-05 — Dwarkesh Patel tour of Jane Street's Texas data center with Ron Minsky (4,032 GPUs, 56 racks, liquid-cooled, ~8,000 km fiber) — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8J-GUnfSqeE (2026-05-15). Facts per the [Street View post](https://www.janestreet.com/culture/street-view/a-look-inside-our-newest-data-center/). Not transcribed.\n- 2026-05 — \"Jane Street on GPUs, Trading, and Hiring: A Conversation with Dwarkesh\" (with Minsky) — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xKZ_8ULR91Y (2026-05-21). Not transcribed.\n\n## How they think\n- Build for **resiliency at the site** rather than failing over between sites, because trading cannot simply move (22:38).\n- **Proximity and optionality**: choose sites with room to grow so you never have to split across distant buildings (01:25).\n- Machine learning forces the industry to adopt liquid cooling quickly: anyone doing \"AI ML stuff will have to figure out pretty quickly\" (25:38).\n- No calls right or wrong on record.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Signals and Threads Ep. 24 transcript — his only long-form on-record interview: https://signalsandthreads.com/the-thermodynamics-of-trading/\n2. Firmus release, 2026-08-07 — his only deal quote.\n3. Street View post on the Texas data center — the scale he runs: https://www.janestreet.com/culture/street-view/a-look-inside-our-newest-data-center/\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public social accounts we could confirm.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Firmus | Strategic (US$2B at >US$10.5B post) | 2026-08-07 | no | Blackstone (new), Coatue (follow-on), NVIDIA (follow-on) | [src](https://firmus.co/newsroom/firmus-announces-fully-subscribed-usdusd2-billion-strategic-equity-investment-to-accelerate-nvidia-ai-factory-expansion-across-australia-and-asia-pacific) |\n\nHe is named personally only on Firmus. Jane Street's other compute deals (CoreWeave, Crusoe, Etched, Oxide) name no Jane Street person that we found, so none are attributed to him.\n\n## Deal stories\n**Firmus (2026-08-07).** Jane Street's place in the round is \"additional participation\", alongside Coatue and NVIDIA (follow-on) and Blackstone (new). The release does not say whether Jane Street is new or re-upping, so **first-in versus re-up is unconfirmed**. He frames the deal as securing compute, not as a return play. Intro path, timing of first contact and any capacity agreement with Firmus: not found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jane Street | at: Head of Physical Engineering | ~2012 | [src](https://signalsandthreads.com/the-thermodynamics-of-trading/) |\n| Ron Minsky | Colleague; hosted his podcast episode; co-led the Texas data-center tour | 2025 (on record) | [src](https://www.janestreet.com/culture/street-view/a-look-inside-our-newest-data-center/) |\n| Dwarkesh Patel | Toured the Texas site with him | 2026-05 | [src](https://www.janestreet.com/culture/street-view/a-look-inside-our-newest-data-center/) |\n| Firmus | Spoke for Jane Street on the strategic round | 2026-08 | [src](https://firmus.co/newsroom/firmus-announces-fully-subscribed-usdusd2-billion-strategic-equity-investment-to-accelerate-nvidia-ai-factory-expansion-across-australia-and-asia-pacific) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Does he sit on the approval path for compute-side equity (Firmus, Etched, Crusoe), or is he only the quoted user? Nothing found.\n- Is his team running the own-build data center Bloomberg reported (2026-06-04)? Unconfirmed.\n- A third-party summary of the 2026-05-21 Dwarkesh conversation attributes to *someone* in the room: modular offsite builds, skipping generator backup for non-critical GPU clusters, and keeping power and site commitments separate from chip purchases ([summary](https://songkwwwwwww.github.io/en/jane%20street/jane-street-gpus-trading-hiring/)). It does not say he said any of it. Check against the video before using.\n- Does Jane Street also have a capacity or offtake contract with Firmus? Not found.\n- Prior employer and education: not found.\n\n## Sources\n- https://firmus.co/newsroom/firmus-announces-fully-subscribed-usdusd2-billion-strategic-equity-investment-to-accelerate-nvidia-ai-factory-expansion-across-australia-and-asia-pacific\n- https://signalsandthreads.com/the-thermodynamics-of-trading/\n- https://www.janestreet.com/culture/street-view/a-look-inside-our-newest-data-center/\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8J-GUnfSqeE\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xKZ_8ULR91Y\n- https://news.bloomberglaw.com/crypto/jane-street-plans-new-data-center-as-compute-power-runs-scarce\n- https://songkwwwwwww.github.io/en/jane%20street/jane-street-gpus-trading-hiring/ (secondary summary; nothing attributed to him)\n", "person:danny-rimer": "\n# Danny Rimer\n\n## One-line take\nIndex's most senior London partner, and the name on both of its 2026 European new-lab bets: a co-lead on Inherent's $50M seed and a follower on Ineffable's $1.1B seed. [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/inherent-ai-50-million-seed-deepmind-faraday-science) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-superlearner-investing-in-david-silver-and-ineffable-intelligence/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** a founder-relationship franchise (Figma seed and board, Discord, DeepL) plus a flat-sized fund whose growth pool lets them lead late AI rounds. Figma's CEO describes their style as built on personal relationships (Spanish-language source, paraphrased). [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/)\n- **Pattern in AI:** they lead the later, bigger round after others took the first risk. Examples are Granola's Series C (fourth round), DeepL's $300M round and Instinct's Series B (firm co-lead). On labs they follow mega-leads (Ineffable) or co-lead smaller European seeds (Inherent). The pitch to founders is people and culture, not compute.\n- **What constrains them:** Index deliberately did not grow its funds, so it can't price a $1B lab seed. They are 55, at a firm with a partners-at-50 succession rule, though they said they have no intention to retire. [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-07-31/index-ventures-danny-rimer-on-leading-figmas-seed-round-it-was-a-feature-not-a-bug-that-it-was-gonna-take-that-long) [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/)\n- **What they'd never do (inferred):** chase hot AI coding startups. Bloomberg says Index avoided them, along with crypto and defense. [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/)\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Not first on Ineffable:** Index says it \"known David by reputation for years\". It had no prior tie to Silver. Flying Fish wrote the first check after courting Silver since early 2024, and Sequoia was reported as lead in February 2026. Index came in as a participant. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-superlearner-investing-in-david-silver-and-ineffable-intelligence/) [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/exclusive-ineffable-intelligence-adds-six-103555324.html)\n- **How they decide on labs:** responsibility and culture, not benchmarks: \"That shared sense of responsibility was essential to our decision to invest.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-superlearner-investing-in-david-silver-and-ineffable-intelligence/)\n- **Lab thesis (Inherent):** \"What it can't do yet is figure out which questions are worth asking\". Discovery and science labs, not scaling labs. [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/inherent-ai-50-million-seed-deepmind-faraday-science)\n- **Fund-size constraint, said out loud on TBPN (via digest):** \"Our contrarian element has been not to grow enormously.\" This explains participant roles on mega-seeds. [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-07-31/index-ventures-danny-rimer-on-leading-figmas-seed-round-it-was-a-feature-not-a-bug-that-it-was-gonna-take-that-long) (aggregator digest of the podcast)\n- **Patience for pre-product builds:** on Figma's roughly two-year pre-launch period, \"it was a feature, not a bug that it was gonna take that long\". That is a useful temperament for labs with no product. [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-07-31/index-ventures-danny-rimer-on-leading-figmas-seed-round-it-was-a-feature-not-a-bug-that-it-was-gonna-take-that-long)\n- **Observer, not director, on Granola:** \"Rimer will join the board as an observer.\" [src](https://laffaz.com/granola-ai-meeting-app-125m-series-c-unicorn)\n- **Succession framing:** Bloomberg (via Bloomberg Línea, in Spanish) quotes them as \"La sucesión es algo en lo que nos hemos inclinado\" (roughly: succession is something we've leaned into). They named Shardul Shah, Nina Achadjian, Martin Mignot and Jan Hammer as successors. [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, with caveats.** Inherent is exactly the thesis: an ex-DeepMind new lab in London, a $50M seed that Index co-led as the first disclosed money, with NVentures in the round. Ineffable shows Index will write into $1B+ lab seeds as a follower, the same seat I'd take, so Rimer is a natural co-investor and a source of European lab flow. Caveats: they weren't first on Ineffable, their other AI deals are later-stage apps (Granola, DeepL, Instinct, Daydream), and succession pressure means the junior pair (Georgia Stevenson in London, Mark Xu in New York) may be the better long-run contact. The earlier repo row said medium. I upgraded it on the strength of the Inherent co-lead.\n\n## Background\n- Underwriting analyst at Hambrecht & Quist, where they helped build the internet equity research group. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/danny-rimer/)\n- 1999: General Partner at The Barksdale Group. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/danny-rimer/)\n- 2002: joined Index and opened the London office. 2012: opened San Francisco. 2018: returned to London. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/danny-rimer/)\n- Younger brother of Index co-founder Neil Rimer. Age 55 as of January 2026. [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/)\n- Forbes has ranked them among the top investors for over a decade (firm bio). Ranked first on the 2025 European Midas list per Forbes Italy (via a search summary; not read directly). [src](https://forbes.it/2025/12/23/midas-lit-europe-migliori-investitori-continente)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/danny-rimer/ | Team page lists 37 companies (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ | Writes investment posts on Index Perspectives |\n| X | https://twitter.com/dannyrimer | Not reviewed; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/dannyrimer/ | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-07/08: TBPN, on Figma's IPO and leading its seed. Covered fund-size discipline, patience, and AI strengthening incumbents like Figma. [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-07-31/index-ventures-danny-rimer-on-leading-figmas-seed-round-it-was-a-feature-not-a-bug-that-it-was-gonna-take-that-long) (digest, not a full transcript)\n- No 2026 podcast on AI labs found.\n\n## How they think\n- **People and culture first:** \"At Index, we like to say it's all about the people.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-superlearner-investing-in-david-silver-and-ineffable-intelligence/)\n- **Labs for discovery and new learning paradigms**, not scale for its own sake: Silver's RL \"superlearner\" and Inherent's self-improving science agents. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inherent-designing-for-discovery/)\n- **AI strengthens incumbents:** they see AI mainly reinforcing established platforms, with human judgment still essential (TBPN digest paraphrase). [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-07-31/index-ventures-danny-rimer-on-leading-figmas-seed-round-it-was-a-feature-not-a-bug-that-it-was-gonna-take-that-long)\n- **Contrarian on fund size:** Index kept the same fund sizes for a decade. [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-07-31/index-ventures-danny-rimer-on-leading-figmas-seed-round-it-was-a-feature-not-a-bug-that-it-was-gonna-take-that-long)\n- **Calls right:** Figma (a $2M seed became a $2.2B stake per Bloomberg) and DeepL. **Open call:** a late entry into Ineffable at $5.1B. [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/)\n\n## Read these first\n1. [The Superlearner: Investing in David Silver and Ineffable Intelligence](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-superlearner-investing-in-david-silver-and-ineffable-intelligence/) (2026-04-27): how they justify a follow-on seat in a mega-seed lab.\n2. [Inherent: Designing for Discovery](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inherent-designing-for-discovery/) (2026-05-31, with Georgia Stevenson): their own lab co-lead.\n3. [TBPN digest on Figma's seed](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-07-31/index-ventures-danny-rimer-on-leading-figmas-seed-round-it-was-a-feature-not-a-bug-that-it-was-gonna-take-that-long): patience and fund-size discipline.\n4. [Bloomberg via Bloomberg Línea on Index succession](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/) (2026-01-08): firm structure and successors.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed. The X account (@dannyrimer) exists, but no posts were read for this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI deals they are personally named on:\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ineffable Intelligence | Seed $1.1B at $5.1B | 2026-04-27 | No. Participant; Rimer sole byline, Georgia Stevenson credited | Sequoia and Lightspeed (co-leads), Nvidia, Google, DST, BOND, EQT, Flying Fish (first check), UK Sovereign AI Unit, British Business Bank | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-superlearner-investing-in-david-silver-and-ineffable-intelligence/) |\n| Inherent | Seed $50M | 2026-05-30 | Co-led with Radical. Post co-written with Georgia Stevenson; TNW names Rimer as the Index partner | Radical, NVentures, Ex/Ante, Metaplanet, Macroscopic, Mythos | [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/inherent-ai-50-million-seed-deepmind-faraday-science) |\n| Instinct | Series B $250M at $2.5B (reported) | 2026-08 | Index co-led with Benchmark. Index team is Rimer and Mark Xu; Rimer's personal role not stated | Benchmark; earlier $100M from Conviction and Greenoaks | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2026/08/27/consumer-focused-ai-assistant-startup-instinct-reportedly-raising-250m/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/instinct/) |\n| Granola | Series C $125M at $1.5B | 2026-03-25 | Yes; board observer. Stevenson wrote the post | Kleiner (Mamoon Hamid), Lightspeed, Spark, NFDG | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/25/granola-raises-125m-hits-1-5b-valuation-as-it-expands-from-meeting-notetaker-to-enterprise-ai-app) |\n| DeepL | $300M at $2B | 2024-05-22 | Yes (Rimer led for Index) | ICONIQ Growth, Teachers' Venture Growth, IVP, Atomico, WiL | [src](https://www.deepl.com/de/press-release/deepl-announces-300-million-investment-at-2-billion-valuation-fueled-by-global-demand-for-ai-language-solutions) |\n| Daydream | undisclosed | 2024-06-20 | unknown (co-written with Vlad Loktev) | none named | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-the-fashion-industry-our-investment-in-daydream/) |\n\nNon-AI highlights from the team page: Figma (seed lead, board), Discord, Notion, Patreon, Glossier, 1stdibs, Dream Games. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/danny-rimer/)\n\n**Senior/junior pairing:** Rimer plus Georgia Stevenson on Ineffable, Inherent, Granola, Marker, Sourceful and Empathy. Rimer plus Mark Xu on Instinct. Rimer plus Vlad Loktev on Daydream.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Ineffable:** no prior relationship (\"by reputation\"). Index met Silver in person to hear the vision and joined the Sequoia/Lightspeed-led round. Flying Fish had been first, about two years earlier. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-superlearner-investing-in-david-silver-and-ineffable-intelligence/) [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/exclusive-ineffable-intelligence-adds-six-103555324.html)\n- **Inherent:** emerged from stealth on 2026-05-30 with the Index/Radical co-led seed. Matt Clifford is an adviser. No source says how Index met the founders. [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/inherent-ai-50-million-seed-deepmind-faraday-science)\n- **Granola:** Index entered at the fourth round. Before it came Lightspeed and betaworks (seed, May 2023), Spark (A, October 2024) and NFDG (B, May 2025). The valuation jumped about 6x from $250M. [src](https://laffaz.com/granola-ai-meeting-app-125m-series-c-unicorn)\n- **Instinct:** came after a $100M round from Conviction and Greenoaks. Index and Benchmark co-led the B. [src](https://siliconangle.com/2026/08/27/consumer-focused-ai-assistant-startup-instinct-reportedly-raising-250m/)\n- **Daydream:** sourced by tracking the founder over time: \"Julie is someone we've been following for years.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-the-fashion-industry-our-investment-in-daydream/)\n\n**How they source:** relationships and long-run tracking of known operators and famous researchers (Silver, the DeepMind alumni), mostly through the London and DeepMind orbit. No university or lab-spinout program shows up in sources. On US AI apps, deals come through a co-staffed junior partner (Mark Xu) or after earlier rounds have de-risked the company.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Neil Rimer | Brother; Index co-founder | | [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/) |\n| Georgia Stevenson | Junior pair on labs and Granola | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inherent-designing-for-discovery/) |\n| Mark Xu | Co-team on Instinct | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/instinct/) |\n| Vlad Loktev | Co-author on Daydream | 2024 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-the-fashion-industry-our-investment-in-daydream/) |\n| Radical Ventures | Inherent co-lead | 2026 | [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/inherent-ai-50-million-seed-deepmind-faraday-science) |\n| Mamoon Hamid (Kleiner) | Granola co-investor | 2026 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/25/granola-raises-125m-hits-1-5b-valuation-as-it-expands-from-meeting-notetaker-to-enterprise-ai-app) |\n| Dylan Field (Figma) | Seed lead; board member | | [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/) |\n| Mike Volpi | Co-designed Index succession plan | | [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who at Index sourced Inherent, and was there a pre-seed? The Sifted article is paywalled (403). [src](https://sifted.eu/articles/inherent-ai-funding-round-index-matt-clifford)\n- Index's check size into Ineffable.\n- Rimer's personal role on the Instinct B versus Mark Xu's, and whether Index joined the reported $1B Series C (secondary sources only).\n- A Sifted podcast snippet mentions an Index investor named \"James\" on Isomorphic, Ineffable and Callosum. The page 404s, so this is unidentified. It could be a second Index European lab investor.\n- X posting habits not yet reviewed.\n- When did Index first invest in DeepL?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/danny-rimer/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-superlearner-investing-in-david-silver-and-ineffable-intelligence/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inherent-designing-for-discovery/\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/inherent-ai-50-million-seed-deepmind-faraday-science\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/instinct/\n- https://siliconangle.com/2026/08/27/consumer-focused-ai-assistant-startup-instinct-reportedly-raising-250m/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/25/granola-raises-125m-hits-1-5b-valuation-as-it-expands-from-meeting-notetaker-to-enterprise-ai-app\n- https://laffaz.com/granola-ai-meeting-app-125m-series-c-unicorn\n- https://www.deepl.com/de/press-release/deepl-announces-300-million-investment-at-2-billion-valuation-fueled-by-global-demand-for-ai-language-solutions\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-the-fashion-industry-our-investment-in-daydream/\n- https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/ (Bloomberg, 2026-01-08)\n- https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-07-31/index-ventures-danny-rimer-on-leading-figmas-seed-round-it-was-a-feature-not-a-bug-that-it-was-gonna-take-that-long (aggregator)\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/exclusive-ineffable-intelligence-adds-six-103555324.html (Fortune)\n- https://forbes.it/2025/12/23/midas-lit-europe-migliori-investitori-continente\n", "person:dave-munichiello": "\n# Dave Munichiello\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. Pronouns: Fortune uses he/him [src](https://fortune.com/2022/08/26/one-of-gvs-top-enterprise-investors-lays-out-his-teams-strategy-for-2022/). X profile could not be read (HTTP 402). LinkedIn was not read.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is GV's compute and AI-systems partner, and almost all his frontier deals come through one academic, Chris Ré. Ré is a formal \"GV Advisor\" and sits behind Lattice, SambaNova, Snorkel, Modular, Rox and Flapping Airplanes [src](https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he has two sourcing engines that he relies on for years. The first is **Chris Ré and the Stanford systems lab**. Ré is a GV Advisor who \"has teamed with us on more than a half-dozen companies\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales). The second is the **Kiva Systems alumni network**: Ishan Mukherjee (four GV companies) and Rylan Hamilton (GV-led Series A, 2025) [src](https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales), [src](https://www.gv.com/news/blue-water-autonomy). He also cold-emails top technical people and waits: four years passed between his 2018 email to Chris Lattner and GV leading Modular's first round in 2022 [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference).\n- **His thesis:** software beats silicon. GV's stated conviction is that \"the software layer holds the enduring, defensible value\" over hardware [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference). He backed one chip company (SambaNova, 2018), but his best outcome is a compiler (Modular → Qualcomm, 2026). Flapping Airplanes is the same efficiency bet, made at the lab level.\n- **Constraints:** GV has one LP, Alphabet, which he calls permanent capital (TBPN, summary) [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-06-11/dave-munichiello-of-gv-on-investing-across-all-stages-at-yc-the-gitlab-story-passed-on-series-b-backed-it-after-and-google-as-a-permanent-lp). GV has no separate seed program and judges seed deals by Series A criteria (same source, auto-transcript). In frontier-lab rounds he co-leads with Sequoia and Index rather than leading alone.\n- **What he wouldn't do:** chase hype or point solutions. In his own words: \"We tend not to be hype driven\" and \"We're not looking for point solutions\" (GV podcast, 2023) [src](https://podcast.gv.com/s4e8-dave-munichiello-on-investing-in-ais-future/).\n\n## Differential insight\nThese are the things the GV firm file lacked: his own words and the mechanism behind his deal flow.\n\n- **Chris Ré is a formal GV Advisor, which explains the \"Ré orbit\" the firm file noticed.** Munichiello's Rox post says Ré \"has teamed with us on more than a half-dozen companies\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales) (2024-11-19). The Ré-linked deals:\n  - Lattice (Series A, 2016)\n  - SambaNova (Ré's technology, 2018)\n  - Snorkel (Ré co-founder, 2019)\n  - Modular (Ré as a co-investor GV \"advocated for\", 2022)\n  - Rox (Ré co-founder, 2024)\n  - Flapping Airplanes (Ben Spector is Ré's PhD student, 2026)\n\n  Sources: [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference), [src](https://talent4boards.com/sambanova-systems-adds-lip-bu-tan-and-dave-munichiello-to-board-along-with-56m-series-a-funding-co-led-by-walden-international-and-gv/), [src](https://digitalmedianet.com/stanford-ai-lab-spinout-snorkel-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-15m-in-funding-from-greylock-and-gv-to-make-ai-practical/amp/), [src](https://www.gv.com/news/why-we-invested-in-flapping-airplanes). Index also reached Ben Spector through Ré's office, so Ré is a shared gate on Flapping, not GV's alone [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/).\n- **Kiva Systems is his second network.**\n  - Ishan Mukherjee: \"We met 15 years ago when I was helping build a fast-moving Kiva Systems.\" They have since partnered across four GV companies: Premise, LatticeData, PixieLabs and Rox [src](https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales).\n  - Rylan Hamilton: the relationship was \"forged on the first day of grad school.\" Munichiello later hired him at Kiva, then led Blue Water Autonomy's Series A and joined its board [src](https://www.gv.com/news/blue-water-autonomy).\n- **The Lattner sequence.** His note to himself after meeting Lattner: \"Find a way to work with him in any capacity.\" The two hiked in Los Altos before Modular existed [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference). The gap from the 2018 cold email to the 2022 led round is four years.\n- **He curates his co-investors.** On Modular, GV \"advocated for\" Ram Shriram, Chris Ré, Ron Conway and Saam Motamedi in the first round, then advocated for Deep Nishar to lead the B [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference). Snorkel shows the same pattern: Motamedi (Greylock) was on the board [src](https://digitalmedianet.com/stanford-ai-lab-spinout-snorkel-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-15m-in-funding-from-greylock-and-gv-to-make-ai-practical/amp/).\n- **Flapping is framed as an anti-scale bet.** \"The next leap won't come from a bigger cluster\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/why-we-invested-in-flapping-airplanes) (2026-01-28). He calls Sequoia's David Cahn \"ever-contrarian\" and credits GV with a co-lead of the \"initial funding rounds\". Index's post also claims a co-lead [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/). The conflict is unresolved.\n- **Compute view (2023 GV podcast, labeled transcript):** \"Compute becomes cheaper and cheaper and cheaper over time.\" He says GV invests \"as low as chips, and compilers, and programming languages\" and is putting \"hundreds of millions of dollars in companies driven by AI\" [src](https://podcast.gv.com/s4e8-dave-munichiello-on-investing-in-ais-future/).\n- **Admitted pass (low confidence):** he says GV passed on GitLab's Series B and invested just after it. Quote: \"We actually passed on the Series B.\" This comes from TBPN Digest's auto-generated transcript, which has no speaker labels [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-06-11/dave-munichiello-of-gv-on-investing-across-all-stages-at-yc-the-gitlab-story-passed-on-series-b-backed-it-after-and-google-as-a-permanent-lp).\n- **Seed approach (low confidence, same auto-transcript):** \"We don't have a separate seed program.\" GV applies Series A criteria to seed deals [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-06-11/dave-munichiello-of-gv-on-investing-across-all-stages-at-yc-the-gitlab-story-passed-on-series-b-backed-it-after-and-google-as-a-permanent-lp/transcript).\n- **Valuation tolerance (2025):** \"Amazing companies sometimes are at prices that feel very uncomfortable\" [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/ai/venture-goog-munichiello-hulme-gv/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is the GV partner on frontier labs and AI compute, and the person to know at GV for AI chips and labs. The deals that matter to you:\n- **Flapping Airplanes:** seed co-lead in 2026, then a participant in the $1B Series A that Index and Lux co-led alongside NVIDIA [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/).\n- **SambaNova:** co-led the $56M Series A in 2018 and took a board seat.\n- **Modular:** led the first round in 2022; Qualcomm is acquiring it in 2026.\n- **Snorkel:** co-led the seed in 2019.\n- **Determined AI:** led the Series A; GV dates it 2018, TechCrunch reported it in 2019.\n\nWhy he matters to you:\n- **He won't compete with you on ownership.** GV's single permanent LP means no LP-driven ownership targets, so he is a natural co-investor in the big seed rounds you write.\n- **Watch Chris Ré's lab, not GV.** Munichiello is a lagging indicator of Ré. The next Ré student or spinout is likely to get a GV call early.\n- **Caveat:** he also manages consumer and enterprise teams, so frontier deals are a small slice of his time.\n\n## Background\n- B.S., Emory University; MBA, Harvard Business School [src](https://www.gv.com/team/dave-munichiello).\n- U.S. Army paratrooper and combat veteran, per GV [src](https://www.gv.com/team/dave-munichiello). TechCrunch (2013) instead says Air Force and a joint special-ops team [src](https://techcrunch.com/2013/11/06/readying-for-a-busy-2014-google-ventures-adds-two-new-investment-partners). The conflict is unresolved.\n- Kiva Systems for about four years, an early executive there (Amazon bought Kiva; it became Amazon Robotics) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2013/11/06/readying-for-a-busy-2014-google-ventures-adds-two-new-investment-partners). He calls Kiva's team \"reluctant roboticists\" [src](https://podcast.gv.com/s4e8-dave-munichiello-on-investing-in-ais-future/).\n- **Promotion story:**\n  - **2013-11:** joined Google Ventures as one of \"two new investment partners,\" focused on AI, security and enterprise [src](https://techcrunch.com/2013/11/06/readying-for-a-busy-2014-google-ventures-adds-two-new-investment-partners).\n  - **By 2018-03:** called \"general partner\" at the SambaNova Series A [src](https://siliconangle.com/2018/03/15/ai-chip-startup-sambanova-raises-56m-gv-others).\n  - **2022-08:** still a general partner, leading the consumer, enterprise and frontier teams [src](https://fortune.com/2022/08/26/one-of-gvs-top-enterprise-investors-lays-out-his-teams-strategy-for-2022/).\n  - **By 2025-06 / 2025-07:** called managing partner [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-06-11/dave-munichiello-of-gv-on-investing-across-all-stages-at-yc-the-gitlab-story-passed-on-series-b-backed-it-after-and-google-as-a-permanent-lp), [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/ai/venture-goog-munichiello-hulme-gv/).\n  - No GV announcement of either promotion was found.\n  - He joined at partner level, so the associate-to-partner fast track doesn't apply: `fast_promotion: no`.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.gv.com/team/dave-munichiello | GV bio and investment list, read 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.gv.com/news | Bylined GV posts: Modular (2026-06-25), Flapping (2026-01-28), Blue Water (2025-08-26), Rox (2024-11-19) |\n| X | http://twitter.com/davemuni | Handle from the GV page; profile not readable (HTTP 402), follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | http://www.linkedin.com/in/davemuni | From the GV page; not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- **2023-09-20, GV Theory and Practice S4E8, \"Dave Munichiello on Investing in AI's Future\"** [src](https://podcast.gv.com/s4e8-dave-munichiello-on-investing-in-ais-future/). Labeled transcript. Highlights:\n  - Kiva as \"reluctant roboticists\".\n  - Falling compute costs.\n  - Wants platforms, \"not looking for point solutions\".\n  - \"We didn't jump on the crypto train aggressively\".\n  - \"We have more questions than answers\" on AI.\n- **2025-06-11, TBPN at YC Demo Day** [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-06-11/dave-munichiello-of-gv-on-investing-across-all-stages-at-yc-the-gitlab-story-passed-on-series-b-backed-it-after-and-google-as-a-permanent-lp). Only the digest summary and its auto-transcript were read, not the video. Topics:\n  - The GitLab Series B pass.\n  - Google as \"a stable LP that's there forever\".\n  - No separate seed program.\n  - Doubts about the hottest Demo Day companies, given \"three months of data\".\n- **2026-08 (approx.), Bloomberg Technology, \"Silicon Valley Is Still the Center of AI, Says GV.\"** Read only through StartupHub's write-up; the video was not watched [src](https://www.startuphub.ai/ai-news/investors-news/2026/gv-s-dave-munichiello-on-ai-s-application-layer.md). Says GV tends \"to be quite contrarian\" and frames Modular as an open alternative to CUDA.\n\n## How they think\n- **Main theses:**\n  - Software over silicon, and portability across chips (Modular).\n  - Platforms over point solutions.\n  - AI for science as the next decade (Bloomberg, via StartupHub).\n  - Infrastructure early, then apps once there is proof of real usage [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/ai/venture-goog-munichiello-hulme-gv/).\n- **Contrarian views:**\n  - Backing an efficiency-first lab (Flapping) against \"scale orthodoxy\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/why-we-invested-in-flapping-airplanes).\n  - Skeptical of Demo Day heat.\n  - Called crossover funds \"unsophisticated dollars\" in 2022 [src](https://fortune.com/2022/08/26/one-of-gvs-top-enterprise-investors-lays-out-his-teams-strategy-for-2022/).\n- **Right:** Modular, four years of patience ending in a Qualcomm acquisition in 2026; Lattice → Apple; Determined → HPE [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference).\n- **Wrong:** passed on GitLab's Series B, then bought in right after (low confidence) [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-06-11/dave-munichiello-of-gv-on-investing-across-all-stages-at-yc-the-gitlab-story-passed-on-series-b-backed-it-after-and-google-as-a-permanent-lp).\n- **Unresolved:** SambaNova's outcome; none found in sources read.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Modular and Qualcomm: The Next Chapter for Unified AI Inference\" (2026-06-25). The cold-email origin, the co-investor list he curated, and GV's AI-infra track record with dates [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference).\n2. \"Why we invested in Flapping Airplanes\" (2026-01-28). His frontier-lab thesis [src](https://www.gv.com/news/why-we-invested-in-flapping-airplanes).\n3. Rox post (2024-11-19). The only place Ré is named a GV Advisor, plus the Kiva network [src](https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales).\n4. GV Theory and Practice S4E8 (2023). His own words on the AI stack [src](https://podcast.gv.com/s4e8-dave-munichiello-on-investing-in-ais-future/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. The X profile (@davemuni) couldn't be read. Most of his public writing is bylined GV posts.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Flapping Airplanes | Seed $180M ($1.5B val.) | 2026-01-28 | Co-lead (GV says with Sequoia; Index also claims co-lead; Menlo participated) | Sequoia (David Cahn), Index, Menlo | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/why-we-invested-in-flapping-airplanes), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Series A $1B ($6B val.) | 2026-10-21 | No (Index and Lux co-led) | Sequoia, Kleiner, Valor, Atreides, A*, NVIDIA | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/) |\n| Modular | First round / seed $30M | 2022 | Yes | Ram Shriram, Chris Ré, Ron Conway, Saam Motamedi | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference), [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/ai/venture-goog-munichiello-hulme-gv/) |\n| Modular | Series B $100M | n/a | No (Deep Nishar, General Catalyst, led) | | [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/ai/venture-goog-munichiello-hulme-gv/) |\n| Modular | Exit: Qualcomm acquisition | 2026-06 | n/a | | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference) |\n| SambaNova Systems | Series A $56M | 2018-03-15 | Co-lead with Walden International; board seat | Redline Capital, Atlantic Bridge | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2018/03/15/ai-chip-startup-sambanova-raises-56m-gv-others) |\n| Snorkel AI | Seed | 2019 (GV) | Co-lead | Greylock; In-Q-Tel in later round ($15M total disclosed 2020-07) | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference), [src](https://digitalmedianet.com/stanford-ai-lab-spinout-snorkel-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-15m-in-funding-from-greylock-and-gv-to-make-ai-practical/amp/) |\n| Determined AI | Series A $11M | 2018 per GV; announced 2019-03-13 | Yes | Amplify, CRV, Haystack, SV Angel | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2019/03/13/determined-ai-nabs-11m-series-a-to-help-democratize-ai-development/) |\n| Lattice (Lattice.io) | Series A | 2016 | n/a | | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference) |\n| Rox | Seed/A ($50M total) | 2024-11-19 | Not stated | Sequoia (Alfred Lin), General Catalyst | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales) |\n| Blue Water Autonomy | Series A | 2025-08-26 | Yes; board | not named | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/blue-water-autonomy) |\n| GitLab | Round after Series B | n/a | Led per GV file | | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mcbride-gp) |\n| Others (profile list, rounds not stated) | Wonder, Redpanda, Cockroach Labs, Plaid, Upbound, Zipline; exits Slack, Segment, ThousandEyes, CoreOS, Jet.com, Pixie Labs, Bugsnag | | | | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/dave-munichiello) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Modular:**\n  - 2018: he cold-emailed Chris Lattner. The first meeting went \"deep in the weeds of infrastructure\", and they took hikes in Los Altos.\n  - Lattner then teamed up with Tim Davis.\n  - 2022: GV led the first round, with a co-investor list GV says it \"advocated for\".\n  - Later: GV pushed for Deep Nishar to lead the B.\n  - 2026: Qualcomm acquisition announced [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference).\n  - Timing: GV was first institutional money.\n- **SambaNova:**\n  - Came out of stealth in March 2018 with a $56M A co-led by Walden (Lip-Bu Tan became chairman) and GV, with Munichiello joining the board [src](https://talent4boards.com/sambanova-systems-adds-lip-bu-tan-and-dave-munichiello-to-board-along-with-56m-series-a-funding-co-led-by-walden-international-and-gv/).\n  - Founded 2017 on Olukotun and Ré's Stanford technology. GV was in the first disclosed round.\n- **Snorkel:**\n  - GV says it co-led the seed in 2019 [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference). That was over a year before the company's July 2020 public launch with Greylock and In-Q-Tel.\n  - Ré is a co-founder; Motamedi (Greylock) took the board.\n- **Flapping Airplanes:**\n  - Munichiello's post doesn't say how GV met the team.\n  - Index met Ben Spector in Ré's office [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/). Sequoia dates its partnership to 2025 (per GV firm file).\n  - Inference, not confirmed: given Ré's GV Advisor role, the Ré channel is the likely GV entry point.\n  - GV was not first; at best it was simultaneous with Sequoia and Index.\n- **Rox:** Ishan Mukherjee (Kiva colleague, Pixie Labs co-founder) and Ré co-founded it. GV invested alongside Sequoia and General Catalyst [src](https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales).\n- **Blue Water Autonomy:** led the Series A for Rylan Hamilton, a grad-school friend he hired at Kiva [src](https://www.gv.com/news/blue-water-autonomy).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Chris Ré | GV Advisor; co-founder or source on Lattice, SambaNova, Snorkel, Rox; Modular co-investor; Ben Spector's PhD advisor | by 2016 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales) |\n| Chris Lattner | Cold email 2018 → Modular lead investor | 2018 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference) |\n| Tim Davis | Modular co-founder | 2022 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference) |\n| Ishan Mukherjee | Kiva colleague; four GV companies together | ~2009 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales) |\n| Rylan Hamilton | Grad-school friend; hired him at Kiva; Blue Water Series A lead | ~2006 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/blue-water-autonomy) |\n| David Cahn (Sequoia) | Flapping co-lead | 2026 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/why-we-invested-in-flapping-airplanes) |\n| Saam Motamedi (Greylock) | Snorkel co-investor; Modular co-investor GV advocated for | 2019 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference) |\n| Lip-Bu Tan (Walden) | SambaNova co-lead and fellow board member | 2018 | [src](https://talent4boards.com/sambanova-systems-adds-lip-bu-tan-and-dave-munichiello-to-board-along-with-56m-series-a-funding-co-led-by-walden-international-and-gv/) |\n| Deep Nishar | Modular Series B lead GV advocated for | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference) |\n| Ram Shriram, Ron Conway | Modular first-round co-investors | 2022 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference) |\n| Alfred Lin (Sequoia) | Rox co-investor | 2024 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales) |\n| Tom Hulme, David Krane, Krishna Yeshwant | GV managing-partner peers | n/a | [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/ai/venture-goog-munichiello-hulme-gv/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What is Ré's formal GV Advisor arrangement (carry, retainer, scope), and which Ré students are next?\n- When exactly was he made managing partner? No announcement was found.\n- How and when did GV first meet the Flapping founders, relative to Sequoia (2025) and Index? Was GV in before the January 2026 announcement?\n- SambaNova's later rounds and outcome, and whether he still holds the board seat.\n- Watch the TBPN video (2025-06-11) and the Bloomberg segment (2026-08) for verbatim quotes, and read his X account.\n- Military branch conflict: Army per GV vs Air Force per TechCrunch 2013.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.gv.com/team/dave-munichiello\n- https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference\n- https://www.gv.com/news/why-we-invested-in-flapping-airplanes\n- https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales\n- https://www.gv.com/news/blue-water-autonomy\n- https://podcast.gv.com/s4e8-dave-munichiello-on-investing-in-ais-future/\n- https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-06-11/dave-munichiello-of-gv-on-investing-across-all-stages-at-yc-the-gitlab-story-passed-on-series-b-backed-it-after-and-google-as-a-permanent-lp (summary + auto-transcript)\n- https://www.startuphub.ai/ai-news/investors-news/2026/gv-s-dave-munichiello-on-ai-s-application-layer.md (secondary write-up of Bloomberg video)\n- https://news.crunchbase.com/ai/venture-goog-munichiello-hulme-gv/\n- https://fortune.com/2022/08/26/one-of-gvs-top-enterprise-investors-lays-out-his-teams-strategy-for-2022/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2013/11/06/readying-for-a-busy-2014-google-ventures-adds-two-new-investment-partners\n- https://siliconangle.com/2018/03/15/ai-chip-startup-sambanova-raises-56m-gv-others\n- https://talent4boards.com/sambanova-systems-adds-lip-bu-tan-and-dave-munichiello-to-board-along-with-56m-series-a-funding-co-led-by-walden-international-and-gv/\n- https://digitalmedianet.com/stanford-ai-lab-spinout-snorkel-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-15m-in-funding-from-greylock-and-gv-to-make-ai-practical/amp/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2019/03/13/determined-ai-nabs-11m-series-a-to-help-democratize-ai-development/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/\n- https://www.gv.com/news/mcbride-gp (via GV firm file)\n", "person:david-cahn": "\n# David Cahn\n\n## One-line take\nSequoia's AI-capex skeptic (\"AI's $600B Question\") who turned that skepticism into a bet against pure scaling. Cahn was the Sequoia partner on Flapping Airplanes before it launched, a data-efficiency research lab that went from a $180M seed to a $1B Series A within a year. Cahn is now one of seven owning GPs at Sequoia.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why Cahn wins:** Cahn has a public, consistent macro thesis. GPU compute is overbuilt and turning into a commodity [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ais-600b-question/), and data, not compute, is the binding constraint [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/). That thesis tells founders which labs Cahn will back: research-first labs that spread bets over time, not cluster-scale races [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/29/flapping-airplanes-and-the-promise-of-research-driven-ai/). GV calls Cahn \"ever-contrarian\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/why-we-invested-in-flapping-airplanes).\n- **What constrains them:** Cahn rarely signs alone. Of the deals found, only Flapping Airplanes lists Cahn as Sequoia's sole partner [src](https://sequoiacap.com/companies/flapping-airplanes/). Every other deal is co-signed: with Roelof Botha (Sesame), Dean Meyer and Shaun Maguire or Bogomil Balkansky (Kela, AIR), Lauren Reeder, Abhishek Malani and Brian Halligan (Juicebox), and Pat Grady and Sonya Huang (Hugging Face) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/david-cahn/).\n- **What they'd never do (inferred from their writing, not stated):** their thesis argues against funding a lab whose only edge is buying more GPUs.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Thesis:** Cahn's stated reason for backing Flapping is \"Data is the bottleneck today to further AI scaling, not compute.\" [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/) (2026-01-28)\n- **Portfolio construction for research labs:** a lab should \"make lots of bets that have a low absolute probability of working\" [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/). Cahn wants money for \"projects that may take 5-10 years to come to fruition\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/29/flapping-airplanes-and-the-promise-of-research-driven-ai/).\n- **Hands-on recruiting after the check:** \"I have met nearly every candidate that has interviewed at Flapping Airplanes\" [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/). The talent pipeline is the asset Cahn underwrites, and they work it personally.\n- **Sourcing via Prod:** Cahn calls Ben Spector's Prod \"one of the most powerful talent incubators in Silicon Valley\" [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/). Prod alumni are a likely sourcing channel for Cahn; that is inference, not stated.\n- **Timing on Flapping:** Sequoia's page dates the partnership to 2025, before the public launch on 2026-01-28 (\"Partnered 2025\") [src](https://sequoiacap.com/companies/flapping-airplanes/). GV says it co-led \"the initial funding rounds\" (plural) \"alongside the ever-contrarian David Cahn at Sequoia\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/why-we-invested-in-flapping-airplanes). Cahn and GV were at the formation table. Index and Menlo are described as participating there, though Index's own post calls itself a co-lead.\n- **The $600B call:** \"AI's $200B question is now AI's $600B question.\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ais-600b-question/) (2024-06-20). Also: \"GPU computing is increasingly turning into a commodity, metered per hour.\" The same essay predicts that application builders \"will be rewarded handsomely.\"\n- **Capex as game theory:** \"The CapEx debate is a debate about speed, not about magnitude.\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ai-optimism-vs-ai-arms-race/) (2024-07-16)\n- **Fragility call:** \"If at any point the tech giants blink, demand all along the supply chain will decline precipitously\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/the-ai-supply-chain-tug-of-war/) (2024-08-12).\n- **Sesame sourcing was product-led:** the team used the voice demo repeatedly. \"We kept coming back, not because we needed to—but because we wanted to.\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sesame-a-new-era-for-voice/) (2025-10-21)\n- **Speed on AIR (with Meyer and Balkansky):** \"Yair finished his service on a Thursday, and we shook hands\" on the seed that Saturday, before the company had a name or product [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-air-securing-the-ai-supply-chain/).\n- **Kela origin:** an Israel trip in April 2024. \"we extended a breakfast meeting into a full-day conversation\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-kela-modern-defense-for-israel-and-western-allies/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Cahn is the Sequoia partner most explicitly backing new labs whose research thesis is not \"more compute.\" This is your core category, at the seed sizes you play in ($180M seed, then a $1B A). The deals that matter:\n- **Flapping Airplanes:** seed co-lead, partnered pre-launch in 2025.\n- **Sesame AI:** Series B co-lead; a voice model lab with full-stack speech generation.\n- **AIR:** seed lead; AI agent supply-chain security, an AI-native infrastructure play.\n- **Hugging Face:** 2022, a Coatue-era relationship.\n\nTheir compute-skeptic macro view is useful as a counterweight on AI chip deals. Practical angle: as one of seven owning GPs after the Nov 2026 consolidation, Cahn can commit Sequoia to large seed rounds. A co-investor bringing $20M into a new research lab is the shape of Flapping's seed syndicate.\n\n## Background\n- 2018: joined Coatue, working on software investments including Snowflake and Databricks [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/david-cahn/)\n- Promoted to partner at Coatue (AI and developer infrastructure), then General Partner & COO leading Coatue's venture business. Dates are not given on the page [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/david-cahn/)\n- 2023: joined Sequoia's partnership \"after working with the firm on HuggingFace and Notion\" (paraphrase of the bio) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/david-cahn/); year per [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)\n- 2026-11-04: named among the seven GPs who \"own and operate\" Sequoia [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)\n- Education: nothing found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/david-cahn/ | Firm bio; lists email dcahn@sequoiacap.com |\n| Substack / blog | Sequoia \"Perspectives\" essays (see Read these first) | About 8 essays, Sept 2023 to Dec 2024, then deal posts. TechCrunch calls the Flapping post a \"Substack post\"; a personal Substack is not confirmed |\n| X | x.com/DavidCahn6 (unverified handle; fetch returned 402) | not confirmed |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Undated (2025 likely, given the Deepseek/Stargate topic) — Gradient Dissent — \"Deepseek, Stargate and AI's $600 billion question with Sequoia Capital's David Cahn\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2Bpf7lOfYiA — linked from the Sequoia bio; transcript not retrieved.\n\n## How they think\n- **Compute is overbuilt and becoming a commodity.** Cahn wrote \"GPU capacity is getting overbuilt\" in Sept 2023 [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/follow-the-gpus-perspective/), then raised the $200B question to $600B in June 2024 [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ais-600b-question/).\n- **Hyperscaler capex is a game-theoretic arms race** in which Big Tech absorbs the risk for the whole supply chain [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ai-optimism-vs-ai-arms-race/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/the-ai-supply-chain-tug-of-war/).\n- **Physical buildout is the real economy story** (\"Steel, energy, trucking and construction\") [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ai-data-center-buildout/). This is consistent with Form Energy (2026) in their portfolio.\n- **Research paradigm over scaling paradigm.** AGI may be \"2-3 research breakthroughs away\" [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/).\n- **Developers as an AGI-scale outcome:** \"bringing 1B developers into the software economy could rival AGI\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/the-next-billion-developers-perspective/).\n- **Calls:** \"AI compute prices should continue their epic decline\" (Dec 2024) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ai-in-2025/), and \"2025 will be a stabilization year for AI CapEx.\" Hyperscaler capex kept rising in 2025, which arguably makes the stabilization call wrong; I have no source here to grade it.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Partnering with Flapping Airplanes\" (2026-01-28): the scaling-vs-research framework and how Cahn underwrites a young lab [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/)\n2. \"AI's $600B Question\" (2024-06-20): their best-known piece; the revenue-gap math [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ais-600b-question/)\n3. \"AI's $200B Question\" / Follow the GPUs (2023-09-20): the original version [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/follow-the-gpus-perspective/)\n4. \"The Game Theory of AI CapEx\" (2024-07-16) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ai-optimism-vs-ai-arms-race/)\n5. \"AI in 2025: Building Blocks Firmly in Place\" (2024-12-09) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ai-in-2025/)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found; X was not fetchable (402). Their essays are widely cited in coverage of the AI-capex debate, for example the \"$600B question\" framing that Sonya Huang's profile contrasts with her own revenue optimism (see people/sonya-huang.md).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Flapping Airplanes | Seed ($180M; reported ~$1.5B val.) | partnered 2025; announced 2026-01-28 | Co-led (Sequoia with GV, Index); Cahn sole Sequoia partner | GV (Munichiello), Index (Mark Xu, Shardul Shah), Menlo (Joff Redfern) | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/companies/flapping-airplanes/), [src](https://www.gv.com/news/why-we-invested-in-flapping-airplanes) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Series A ($1B; reported $6B) | 2026-10-21 | No (Index, Lux co-led) | GV, Kleiner Perkins, Valor, Atreides, A*, NVIDIA | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/) |\n| Sesame AI | Series B ($250M) | 2025-10-21 | Co-led (Sequoia); co-signed with Roelof Botha | Spark Capital | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sesame-a-new-era-for-voice/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/21/sesame-the-conversational-ai-startup-from-oculus-founders-raises-250m-and-launches-beta/) |\n| AIR | Seed | 2026 (pre-company) | Yes (Sequoia); with Balkansky, Meyer | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-air-securing-the-ai-supply-chain/) |\n| AIR | Series A | 2026-09-01 | No (participating) | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-air-securing-the-ai-supply-chain/) |\n| Juicebox | Series A | 2025-09-25 | Yes (Sequoia); with Malani, Reeder, Halligan | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/why-were-partnering-with-juicebox-the-recruiting-platform-founders-are-obsessed-with/) |\n| Kela | Seed ($10M) | announced 2025-03-17 (partnered 2024) | Yes (Sequoia); with Meyer, Maguire | Lux led the later A (Reeves, Wolfe) | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-kela-modern-defense-for-israel-and-western-allies/) |\n| Hugging Face | Series C ($100M) | 2022-05-09 | No (Lux led) | Sequoia, Coatue; Sequoia page lists Cahn, Grady, Huang. Which firm Cahn represented at the time is unclear | [src](https://huggingface.co/blog/series-c), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/hugging-face/) |\n| STARK | Early | 2024 | unknown | Luciana Lixandru also listed | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/david-cahn/) |\n| Astrocade | Growth | 2025 | unknown | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/david-cahn/) |\n| Form Energy | Growth | 2026 | unknown | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/david-cahn/) |\n| Notion | Growth | 2021 | unknown (predates their Sequoia start) | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/david-cahn/) |\n| Clay | listed \"Growth; Early\" | 2019 | unknown (predates their Sequoia start) | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/david-cahn/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Flapping Airplanes:** Cahn met Ben and Asher Spector (ages 25 and 26) to discuss the lab. Cahn describes them as building the \"young person's AGI lab\" [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/). Sequoia partnered in 2025, before the launch. GV and Sequoia co-led the initial rounds, and Index also calls itself a co-lead [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/). After the check, Cahn met nearly every candidate the lab interviewed. By the Series A (Index and Lux co-led) the pitch had shifted toward performance per GPU (see companies/flapping-airplanes.md). Sequoia did not lead the A.\n- **Sesame:** the Feb 2025 Maya/Miles demo drew more than 1M users in weeks. Sequoia's team kept using it, then co-led the Series B with Botha [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sesame-a-new-era-for-voice/).\n- **Kela:** Sequoia had an Israeli defense-tech thesis and reconnected with founders Alon and Hamutal on an April 2024 trip. Sequoia then went to Jerusalem to meet co-founder Jason Manne, and led the $10M seed after the summer [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-kela-modern-defense-for-israel-and-western-allies/).\n- **AIR:** Sequoia met Yair Saban and Niv Hoffman before the company had a name. Unit 8200 commanders gave references. The seed handshake came two days after Saban's service ended [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-air-securing-the-ai-supply-chain/).\n- **Juicebox:** an early-stage founder described the product to Sequoia as something they were obsessed with, and Sequoia's own recruiters already used it. The company had bootstrapped to $1M ARR with four people [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/why-were-partnering-with-juicebox-the-recruiting-platform-founders-are-obsessed-with/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sequoia Capital | GP (one of seven owning GPs) | 2023 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/) |\n| Coatue | Former partner, then GP & COO of its venture business | 2018 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/david-cahn/) |\n| Ben Spector, Asher Spector | Founders Cahn backed pre-launch (Flapping) | 2025 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/) |\n| Dave Munichiello (GV) | Co-lead on Flapping initial rounds | 2025 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/why-we-invested-in-flapping-airplanes) |\n| Roelof Botha | Co-signer, Sesame Series B | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/sesame-ai/) |\n| Dean Meyer | Co-signer, Kela and AIR (Israel node) | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/air/) |\n| Shaun Maguire | Co-signer, Kela | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-kela-modern-defense-for-israel-and-western-allies/) |\n| Bogomil Balkansky | Co-signer, AIR | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/air/) |\n| Lauren Reeder, Abhishek Malani, Brian Halligan | Co-signers, Juicebox Series A | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/juicebox/) |\n| Pat Grady, Sonya Huang | Co-listed on Hugging Face | 2022 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/hugging-face/) |\n| Luciana Lixandru | Co-listed on STARK | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/luciana-lixandru/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact date and round of Sequoia's first Flapping check in 2025. Were there multiple pre-launch tranches, given GV's plural \"initial funding rounds\"? Sequoia's check size?\n- Did Cahn come to the Spectors through Prod, or through someone in the Sequoia network?\n- Hugging Face 2022: was Cahn on the Series C for Coatue or for Sequoia? The bio implies Cahn worked with Sequoia on it while at Coatue.\n- Gradient Dissent transcript: get verbatim lines on DeepSeek, Stargate and compute pricing.\n- Confirm their X handle, and whether a personal Substack exists (TechCrunch calls the Flapping post a \"Substack post\").\n- Rounds and lead status for STARK, Astrocade and Form Energy.\n- Web search budget was exhausted mid-task. No secondary-press search was run for podcasts beyond the Sequoia bio links.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/david-cahn/\n- https://sequoiacap.com/companies/flapping-airplanes/\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-flapping-airplanes/\n- https://www.gv.com/news/why-we-invested-in-flapping-airplanes\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/29/flapping-airplanes-and-the-promise-of-research-driven-ai/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/sesame-ai/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sesame-a-new-era-for-voice/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/21/sesame-the-conversational-ai-startup-from-oculus-founders-raises-250m-and-launches-beta/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/air/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-air-securing-the-ai-supply-chain/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/juicebox/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/why-were-partnering-with-juicebox-the-recruiting-platform-founders-are-obsessed-with/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-kela-modern-defense-for-israel-and-western-allies/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/hugging-face/\n- https://huggingface.co/blog/series-c\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ais-600b-question/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/follow-the-gpus-perspective/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ai-optimism-vs-ai-arms-race/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/the-ai-supply-chain-tug-of-war/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ai-data-center-buildout/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ai-in-2024/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ai-in-2025/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/the-next-billion-developers-perspective/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2Bpf7lOfYiA\n", "person:david-goldschmidt": "\n# David (Dede) Goldschmidt\n\n## One-line take\nGoldschmidt runs the Samsung arm that co-leads AI-silicon rounds (interconnect, accelerators, AI-EDA). He is the person to know when a chip or AI-infra company wants Samsung as a strategic name.\n\n## Crux\nHe runs a strategic-first CVC that uses financial quality as a filter: \"At Catalyst Fund, we are probably in the middle\" [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/samsung-catalyst-fund-seeks-strategic-midpoint/). His edge is Samsung's foundry, memory and customer access. His constraints are a 12-person team that reports into SSIC, deals that must fit Samsung's business, and few outright leads [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-david-goldschmidt-samsung-catalyst-fund/). He would not back application-layer software without a hardware tie-in.\n\n## Differential insight\n- He frames valuation discipline like a financial VC: \"When making investments, we try to be disciplined\" [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/samsung-catalyst-fund-seeks-strategic-midpoint/) (2022-03-18).\n- Under him, GCV says, SCF's wins include Nvidia's licence deal with Groq and Marvell's purchases of Celestial AI and XConn [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-david-goldschmidt-samsung-catalyst-fund/).\n- Nothing found on podcasts, X or Substack.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. He is not a co-lead the owner would pick for ownership. But SCF is a frequent strategic co-lead in AI silicon (Normal Computing, OXMIQ, Eliyan, DreamBig, Tenstorrent), and it is an LP in AI Fund, Atomico, Lakestar and Speedinvest [src](https://samsungcatalyst.com/).\n\n## Background\n- He holds an M.S. in Solid State Physics and a B.S. in Math/Physics from Tel Aviv University [src](https://samsungcatalyst.com/team/david-dede-goldschmidt/).\n- He was VP Product Management/BD at ADC Broadband Multiservice Access, and earlier at Teledata (dates not on bio).\n- He was Managing Partner and CEO of Mofet Technology Fund, a listed VC, then Founder and GP of Valley Venture Capital (dates not on bio).\n- He joined SCF in 2015 and became its head in early 2021 [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-david-goldschmidt-samsung-catalyst-fund/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://samsungcatalyst.com/team/david-dede-goldschmidt/ | SCF bio |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2022-03 — GCV interview (print), \"Samsung Catalyst Fund seeks strategic midpoint\" — https://globalventuring.com/corporate/samsung-catalyst-fund-seeks-strategic-midpoint/ — he said the fund looks for investments covering \"the entire infrastructure stack\".\n- 2021-04 — SCF lists NEXUS:ISRAEL Dealmakers 2021 and a GSA conference panel in its news feed; neither transcript was checked [src](https://samsungcatalyst.com/news/).\n\n## How they think\n- He invests across the whole infrastructure stack, from the lowest silicon layer up.\n- He looks for financially strong startups because they make durable strategic partners.\n- In 2022 he was wary of dot-com-like valuations but said unicorns now have real revenue.\n\n## Read these first\n- GCV 2022 interview (strategy and valuation stance).\n- GCV Powerlist 2026 (wins and team structure).\n- Normal Computing release (why SCF led an AI-EDA round).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Normal Computing | Strategic ($50M) | 2026-03-25 | yes | Galvanize; Brevan Howard Macro Venture Fund; ArcTern; Celesta; Drive Capital; First Spark; Micron Ventures | [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/normal-computing-raises-50m-led-by-samsung-catalyst-to-accelerate-silicon-design-and-solve-ai-hardware-energy-crisis-302724819.html) |\n| OXMIQ Labs | Series A ($35M) | 2026-07-01 | co-led (Fundomo) | MediaTek; Pegatron VC; CDIB-TEN; Darwin Ventures; Morgan Creek Digital | [src](https://oxmiq.ai/blog-oxmiq-raises-35m-series-a) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- Normal Computing: he said the AI-native chip-design platform could give \"faster time-to-market\" to demanding chip customers. SCF led; Micron Ventures was an existing investor [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/normal-computing-raises-50m-led-by-samsung-catalyst-to-accelerate-silicon-design-and-solve-ai-hardware-energy-crisis-302724819.html).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Samsung Catalyst Fund (samsung) | Head | 2021 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-david-goldschmidt-samsung-catalyst-fund/) |\n| Ian Clow | Team (Investment Director) | ~2024 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/rising-stars-2026-ian-clow-samsung-catalyst-fund/) |\n| Raja Koduri (OXMIQ) | Portfolio founder he backed | 2026 | [src](https://oxmiq.ai/blog-oxmiq-raises-35m-series-a) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did he personally lead the Eliyan, DreamBig and Tenstorrent rounds, where Chisari was the quoted executive?\n- Does he sit on any portfolio boards?\n\n## Sources\n- https://samsungcatalyst.com/team/david-dede-goldschmidt/\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/samsung-catalyst-fund-seeks-strategic-midpoint/\n- https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-david-goldschmidt-samsung-catalyst-fund/\n- https://oxmiq.ai/blog-oxmiq-raises-35m-series-a\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/normal-computing-raises-50m-led-by-samsung-catalyst-to-accelerate-silicon-design-and-solve-ai-hardware-energy-crisis-302724819.html\n", "person:david-hirsch": "\n# David Hirsch\n\n## One-line take\nHirsch is the founder of the firm now known as Compound. He was one of Google's first New York employees, co-founded Metamorphic Ventures in 2008, and signed every fund filing through 2021. Since then the filings show him stepping back from the front seat: Michael Dempsey now signs, and Hirsch's public role is portfolio support and commercialization. He controls the GP, but nothing ties him personally to an AI deal.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he built the firm and holds GP control. He signed the Form Ds for Metamorphic FD (2015), Metamorphic III (2016) and Compound 2021, and he is a director of every Compound GP entity, including the 2026 fund and Explorer I ([2021 Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1839701/000120919121002941/primary_doc.xml); [2026](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2114614/000110465926026411/primary_doc.xml); [Explorer I](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2147721/000110465926093414/primary_doc.xml)). His edge for founders is go-to-market: he ran commercialization teams at Google ([bio](https://www.compound.vc/team/davidh)).\n- **What constrains him:** the firm's frontier-AI identity is Dempsey's. Public deal attribution, writing and theses all run through Dempsey and the research team ([firms/compound.md](../firms/compound.md)). Hirsch has no public writing, and the search turned up no podcasts. Fund size, $50M to $115M, limits checks to sub-$10M.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** give up ownership discipline. In 2016, under his leadership, the firm said its 7–10% ownership target \"hasn't changed\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2016/10/05/metamorphic-ventures-has-a-new-50-million-fund-and-a-new-brand/)). This is the opposite of the owner's flexible-ownership, $20M-into-big-seeds approach.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He founded the firm, but the site doesn't say so.** The team page says only \"General Partner\" focused on portfolio support. He co-founded Metamorphic in 2008 with Lewis Gersh, describing himself as \"an active, hands-on investor\" ([InformationWeek, 2008-09-19](https://www.informationweek.com/it-leadership/former-google-exec-launches-vc-fund)). Metamorphic Ventures LLC filed with the SEC on 2008-08-29 ([EDGAR](https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?action=getcompany&CIK=0001444392)).\n- **The filings show who leads now.** Hirsch signed the 2015, 2016 and 2021 Form Ds. Dempsey signed both 2026 Form Ds (\"nameOfSigner: Michael Dempsey\"), with Hirsch listed as the other director ([2026](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2114614/000110465926026411/primary_doc.xml); [Explorer I](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2147721/000110465926093414/primary_doc.xml)). This looks like a succession from founder to Dempsey (inference).\n- **Fund II was spread across many small LPs.** Metamorphic Ventures II/II-A sold $64,935,000 to 131 investors, with a $0 minimum investment ([Form D/A 2014-03-19](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1534833/000153483314000001/primary_doc.xml)). That averages about $0.5M per LP, which suggests a base of individuals and family offices, not institutions (inference).\n- **Fund III came in a third short of target after a co-founder left.** The January 2016 Form D for Metamorphic III, signed by Hirsch alone, registered **$75M** ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1664640/000166464016000001/primary_doc.xml)). TechCrunch reported the close as $50M, below Fund II's ~$70M, and linked the drop to Marc Michel leaving. Hirsch called the split \"completely amicable\" ([TechCrunch, 2016-10-05](https://techcrunch.com/2016/10/05/metamorphic-ventures-has-a-new-50-million-fund-and-a-new-brand/)).\n- **A $10M side vehicle in 2015.** Metamorphic Ventures FD, LLC sold $9,985,000 to 28 investors (first sale 2015-10-14). Hirsch signed it as \"A Managing Director of the Manager\" ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1657736/000165773615000001/primary_doc.xml)). The filing doesn't say what it was for. It may be a follow-on or co-invest sleeve, the 2015 counterpart to the 2026 Explorer I (inference).\n- **The AI pivot happened on his watch, in 2016.** At the rebrand, the firm said it \"is now focusing on artificial intelligence and regulated industries like healthcare\". One new bet was a stealth company recognizing objects around autonomous vehicles; Hirsch said it \"remains in stealth mode\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2016/10/05/metamorphic-ventures-has-a-new-50-million-fund-and-a-new-brand/)). Deepgram (2016-09) and Wayve (2017-09) fall in the same window ([firms/compound.md](../firms/compound.md)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**low** as a deal signal, though he is the path to Compound's GP and LP base. No AI, AI-chip, robotics or bio-AI deal names him personally. Compound's frontier-AI record (Deepgram, Wayve, Runway, Prime Intellect, General Intelligence Co.) is attributed to the firm or to Dempsey. His 2016 ownership rule (7–10%) and small checks run against a $20M big-seed approach. Talk to Hirsch about (a) how the LPs are structured (131 LPs in Fund II), (b) what Compound Explorer I is for, and (c) go-to-market help for portfolio companies. Talk to Dempsey for frontier-AI deal flow.\n\n## Background\n- Came from Snowball.com (games portal). Joined Google in **2000** and was the first manager of its B2B vertical markets team ([InformationWeek](https://www.informationweek.com/it-leadership/former-google-exec-launches-vc-fund)). He was Google's second employee in New York and stayed eight years ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2016/10/05/metamorphic-ventures-has-a-new-50-million-fund-and-a-new-brand/)). Compound's bio says he \"built and scaled commercialization teams at Google\" ([bio](https://www.compound.vc/team/davidh)).\n- Left Google around **February 2008**, seven months before the InformationWeek piece. By then he was already an angel in Amie Street ([InformationWeek](https://www.informationweek.com/it-leadership/former-google-exec-launches-vc-fund)). TechCrunch dates his move into VC to 2009, but the 2008 SEC filing and press point to 2008.\n- **2008:** co-founded Metamorphic Ventures with Lewis Gersh. Its focus was digital media, mobility and fintech, and early portfolio companies included FetchBack and Recognia ([InformationWeek](https://www.informationweek.com/it-leadership/former-google-exec-launches-vc-fund)). Marc Michel was later a co-founder and managing member on Fund II. The debut fund was $20M ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2016/10/05/metamorphic-ventures-has-a-new-50-million-fund-and-a-new-brand/)).\n- **2016-10:** rebranded the firm as Compound and ran it with Josh Nussbaum, a principal promoted from analyst ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2016/10/05/metamorphic-ventures-has-a-new-50-million-fund-and-a-new-brand/)).\n- **Education:** nothing found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [compound.vc/team/davidh](https://www.compound.vc/team/davidh) | Two-line bio |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | No posts by him on compound.vc/writing |\n| X | [@startupman](https://twitter.com/startupman) | Linked from the firm bio. x.com returned 402, so followers and posts were not checked (2026-11-10) |\n| LinkedIn | [davidhirsch2](https://www.linkedin.com/in/davidhirsch2/) | Linked from the firm bio; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. One targeted search turned up only people with the same name. Aggregator vcsheet summarizes his X posts as covering generative AI, autonomous systems, power-law returns and longevity ([vcsheet](https://www.vcsheet.com/who/david-hirsch), aggregator, low confidence, not verified on X).\n\n## How they think\n- **Hands-on, operator-style support.** \"Active, hands-on investor\" in 2008 ([InformationWeek](https://www.informationweek.com/it-leadership/former-google-exec-launches-vc-fund)). In 2026 he is the GP for \"portfolio support and commercialization\" ([bio](https://www.compound.vc/team/davidh)).\n- **Ownership discipline.** The 7–10% initial-ownership target held through the 2016 fund shrink ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2016/10/05/metamorphic-ventures-has-a-new-50-million-fund-and-a-new-brand/)).\n- **Calls that worked:** he moved the firm from adtech and digital media (Tapad $360M, Chango $122M, Tap Commerce about $100M exits) to AI and regulated industries in 2016, a year before Wayve and around the Deepgram seed ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2016/10/05/metamorphic-ventures-has-a-new-50-million-fund-and-a-new-brand/)). Calls that missed: Fund III closed at $50M against a $75M Form D target.\n- **Contrarian views:** nothing found in his own words.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [TechCrunch 2016-10-05](https://techcrunch.com/2016/10/05/metamorphic-ventures-has-a-new-50-million-fund-and-a-new-brand/): the rebrand, the AI pivot, check size and ownership, in Hirsch's era.\n2. [InformationWeek 2008-09-19](https://www.informationweek.com/it-leadership/former-google-exec-launches-vc-fund): the founding of Metamorphic and his path from Google.\n3. Form Ds, 2012–2026 (links above): the shift in GP control and the LP counts.\n4. [Compound 2023 annual letter](https://www.compound.vc/writing/compound-annual-letter-2023): not by Hirsch, but it describes the commercialization help he leads.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot verified; x.com was blocked. The aggregator says he credits partners such as Dempsey with leading on theses ([vcsheet](https://www.vcsheet.com/who/david-hirsch), aggregator).\n\n## Portfolio\nNo AI, AI-chip, robotics or bio-AI deal names Hirsch personally. Firm-level AI deals are in [firms/compound.md](../firms/compound.md).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Stealth AV object-recognition co. (unidentified) | Seed (assumed) | by 2016-10 | unknown | unknown | Hirsch spoke for the company ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2016/10/05/metamorphic-ventures-has-a-new-50-million-fund-and-a-new-brand/)) |\n| Amie Street | Angel (pre-Metamorphic) | by 2008-09 | unknown | unknown | [InformationWeek](https://www.informationweek.com/it-leadership/former-google-exec-launches-vc-fund) |\n| FetchBack, Recognia | Metamorphic I | by 2008-09 | unknown | unknown | firm-level ([InformationWeek](https://www.informationweek.com/it-leadership/former-google-exec-launches-vc-fund)) |\n| Indiegogo, Tapad, Chango, Tap Commerce, Pluot (Daily), Finova | Metamorphic I–III | 2008–2016 | unknown | unknown | firm-level ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2016/10/05/metamorphic-ventures-has-a-new-50-million-fund-and-a-new-brand/)) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found at the person level. On timing: the stealth AV-perception bet (2016) and Deepgram (seed 2016-09) predate Wayve (2017) and Runway (2018). All were made while Hirsch was the GP of record (Fund III signer), but no source says who sourced or led them.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Michael Dempsey | Co-director of Compound GP entities; signs 2026 filings | by 2021-01 | [2021 Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1839701/000120919121002941/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Lewis Gersh | Metamorphic co-founder | 2008 | [InformationWeek](https://www.informationweek.com/it-leadership/former-google-exec-launches-vc-fund) |\n| Marc Michel | Metamorphic co-founder and co-managing member; left 2016-01 to start Runway Venture Partners | 2012 (filing) – 2016 | [Form D/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1534833/000153483314000001/primary_doc.xml); [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2016/10/05/metamorphic-ventures-has-a-new-50-million-fund-and-a-new-brand/) |\n| Josh Nussbaum | Ran Compound with Hirsch as principal (analyst from 2012) | 2012 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2016/10/05/metamorphic-ventures-has-a-new-50-million-fund-and-a-new-brand/) |\n| Google | Early NY employee; B2B vertical markets manager | 2000–2008 | [InformationWeek](https://www.informationweek.com/it-leadership/former-google-exec-launches-vc-fund) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who was the 2016 stealth AV object-recognition company, and is it still on the portfolio page?\n- Did Hirsch personally source or sit on the board of Deepgram, Wayve or Osaro?\n- When did Dempsey become Managing Partner, and is Hirsch winding down? The 2026 signer change suggests a succession.\n- What is Compound Explorer I ($35M, 2026) for, and does it mirror the 2015 FD vehicle?\n- Where is Josh Nussbaum now? This is a fast-promotion lead (analyst 2012 → principal by 2016).\n- Hirsch's X posts (@startupman): his views on AI, which the aggregator mentions but which were not verified.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.compound.vc/team/davidh ; https://www.compound.vc/about\n- https://techcrunch.com/2016/10/05/metamorphic-ventures-has-a-new-50-million-fund-and-a-new-brand/\n- https://www.informationweek.com/it-leadership/former-google-exec-launches-vc-fund\n- SEC: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1534833/000153483314000001/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1657736/000165773615000001/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1664640/000166464016000001/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1839701/000120919121002941/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2114614/000110465926026411/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2147721/000110465926093414/primary_doc.xml ; EDGAR CIK 1444392 (Metamorphic Ventures LLC, REGDEX 2008-08-29)\n- https://www.compound.vc/writing/compound-annual-letter-2023\n- Aggregator: https://www.vcsheet.com/who/david-hirsch (self-flagged out of date)\n- Not reachable: https://searchengineland.com/david-hirsch-leaving-google-omid-kordestani-sells-6000-shares-of-stock-13072 (403); x.com/startupman (402)\n", "person:david-katz": "\n# David Katz\n\n## One-line take\nKatz is the partner who controls Radical's compute supply. Their team tracks GPU prices and contract terms and, by Radical's own account, has negotiated billions of dollars in compute deals for portfolio companies. Their own deals all sit on the compute layer: CentML (founding seed), Crusoe (Series E, co-authored with Rob Toews) and Prime Intellect (led the $130M A) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/how-agentic-activity-changes-the-risk-equation/).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** Radical turned GPU access into part of the product it offers founders, and Katz runs that function. Radical Reads (2026-10-13) says Katz's team tracks GPU availability, pricing and contract terms and connects portfolio companies with compute providers [src](https://radical.vc/articles/how-agentic-activity-changes-the-risk-equation/). That gives them a live, buyer's-side view of the compute market that few VCs have, and it is the obvious source of their infra deals (CentML optimises scarce GPUs; Crusoe builds AI data centres; Prime Intellect sells compute plus RL tooling).\n- **Firm power beyond deals:** Katz is one of only four named people (with Jordan Jacobs, John Megrue and CFO Blair Bernholtz) on the Form Ds for both Fund IV and Growth Fund I, listed as \"Executive Officer\" [Fund IV Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2065455/000095010325005206/primary_doc.xml) [Growth Fund I Form D/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2024166/000207751625000004/primary_doc.xml). Together with \"leads the Investment Team\" and \"oversees strategic initiatives and operations\" on the team page, this points to an operating-partner-turned-COO-of-investing role rather than a pure deal partner.\n- **What constrains them:** They came up as an operator and GTM partner, not as a researcher or a deal sourcer. Two of their three named deals came after other firms (Crusoe was first backed by BCV and Founders Fund in 2019; Prime Intellect raised a seed in 2024 and a Founders Fund round in 2025 before Radical led). Their public writing is thin and mostly about policy and the market rather than personal theses.\n- **What they'd never do (inference from their writing):** bet on a single closed model stack. The Prime Intellect post frames lock-in to one closed system as \"business risk\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/prime-intellect-owning-the-learning-loop/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Scale of the compute desk.** Radical, summarising a WSJ piece, says Katz's team \"has negotiated billions of dollars in compute deals over the past five years\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/how-agentic-activity-changes-the-risk-equation/) (2026-10-13). Five years puts the start at roughly their Sept 2021 arrival, so the compute work began while they were still an Operating Partner.\n- **Fund officer, not just a deal partner.** They are listed as \"Executive Officer\" on the Fund IV (2025) and Growth Fund I Form Ds, alongside Jacobs, Megrue and Bernholtz [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2065455/000095010325005206/primary_doc.xml). No other investing partner (Toews, Welch, Rosenberg and others) appears there.\n- **Against compute nationalism, for now.** In Radical's submission to the 2024 Canadian compute consultation, Katz argued that subsidies should fund compute \"regardless of its physical location\" and should go to AI companies rather than \"the suppliers (chipmakers, hyperscalers, data centre operators)\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/building-ai-infrastructure/) (2024-09-09). It is notable that a firm backed by Canadian pensions argued for foreign GPUs as a short-term bridge. They also argued for pooled procurement, buying \"by aggregating demand\", which is in effect a national version of Radical's own compute desk.\n- **Early read on chip scarcity.** In 2023 Katz wrote that the GPU market favours hyperscalers and \"well funded startups with strategic value to the cloud providers\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/cracking-the-ai-chip-shortage-strategies-and-opportunities/) (2023-07-24). That piece followed an FT feature on the AI chip market. Radical says the FT featured Katz; the FT article itself was not checked.\n- **Rent vs own the model loop.** Prime Intellect thesis: companies can now \"train models directly using their own data\" with RL, rather than renting frontier APIs [src](https://radical.vc/articles/prime-intellect-owning-the-learning-loop/) (2026-07-13).\n- **On Nvidia's moat.** Radical Reads reports that an FT piece on Triton quoted Katz explaining Nvidia's software stack, alongside CentML CEO Gennady Pekhimenko [src](https://radical.vc/articles/why-we-led-nabla-bios-series-a/) (2024-05-28). This is Radical's paraphrase, and the FT quote itself was not checked.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Compute and AI chips sit at the centre of your thesis, and Katz is Radical's compute node. The deals that matter: **Prime Intellect** (Radical led the $130M Series A in July 2026; decentralised and open frontier training plus RL, the \"new lab\" adjacent infra you like), **CentML** (Radical's founding seed in 2022–23, later shown as \"Acquired\" on Radical's portfolio page), and **Crusoe** (Series E in 2025, re-upped in the 2026 Series F through Breakouts). They are useful to you in two ways. First, as a co-investor who can bring GPU allocations to a new lab you back. Second, as a read on compute pricing. Caveat: they were not first in at Crusoe or Prime Intellect. Radical's growth money tends to arrive after the seed, so for your $20M-into-a-big-seed style Katz is more of a follow-on partner than a founding co-lead (CentML is the exception).\n\n## Background\n- BA in Philosophy, University of Western Ontario (1991); LLB, Osgoode Hall Law School (1995) [src](https://radical.vc/team/david-katz/)\n- \"started his career as a lawyer, but switched paths early\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/)\n- Founded InsLogic (P&C insurance marketplace, acquired by Capital One Bank) and Portfolios.com (creative freelancer marketplace, acquired by EQ Inc.); co-founded EQ Works (ML ad platforms); executive at Benecaid, where they created Honeybee (Benecaid and Honeybee were acquired by April International and are now part of Green Shield Canada). Dates for these roles are not on the bio [src](https://radical.vc/team/david-katz/)\n- Joined Radical in Sept 2021 as Operating Partner for portfolio go-to-market, in the same announcement that hired Rob Toews as the first SF investment partner [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/david-katz/ | Team bio, checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://radical.vc/ai-perspectives/ | Occasional Radical Reads bylines (2023-06, 2023-07, 2024-09, 2025-10 co-author, 2026-07) |\n| X | nothing found | The team page links only the firm account https://twitter.com/radicalvcfund, not a personal handle |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/davidkatz/ | Linked from the team page; profile contents not checked (common name) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- No podcast or talk appearances found. Web search was unavailable for this run (budget exhausted), so the check was limited to radical.vc.\n- 2023-07 — Financial Times — article on the AI chip market, featuring Katz according to Radical; not checked [src](https://radical.vc/articles/cracking-the-ai-chip-shortage-strategies-and-opportunities/)\n- 2024-05 — Financial Times — Triton/Nvidia software piece, quoting Katz according to Radical; not checked [src](https://radical.vc/articles/why-we-led-nabla-bios-series-a/)\n- 2026-10 — WSJ — \"The Desperate Hunt for AI Computing Power is Upending Silicon Valley\" covers Katz's compute team, according to Radical's summary; the WSJ text was not checked [src](https://radical.vc/articles/how-agentic-activity-changes-the-risk-equation/)\n\n## How they think\n- **Compute is the bottleneck, and access is a moat.** This runs from 2023 (\"very few companies capable of addressing the global compute bottleneck\", CentML post) to 2026 (the GPU desk).\n- **Software efficiency over new silicon.** Their first named bet was CentML's \"virtual chips\", which squeeze more out of existing and legacy GPUs, rather than a chip startup [src](https://radical.vc/articles/cracking-the-ai-chip-shortage-strategies-and-opportunities/).\n- **Decentralising frontier capability.** Prime Intellect is pitched as putting frontier-scale training \"within reach of any company ready to compete\" and avoiding single-vendor lock-in.\n- **Policy view:** subsidise demand (startups), not supply (hyperscalers); pool procurement; build domestic capacity over time using Canada's cold climate and renewable power [src](https://radical.vc/articles/building-ai-infrastructure/).\n- **Calls:** the 2023 CentML seed, a compute-efficiency bet, came before the 2025–26 inference boom, and the company is marked \"Acquired\" on Radical's portfolio page (buyer and price not checked here).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Prime Intellect: Owning the Learning Loop (2026-07-13), their clearest current thesis: own your RL loop rather than rent a frontier model. https://radical.vc/articles/prime-intellect-owning-the-learning-loop/\n2. Building AI Infrastructure (2024-09-09), Radical's Canadian compute-policy submission, summarised by Katz. https://radical.vc/articles/building-ai-infrastructure/\n3. Cracking the AI Chip Shortage (2023-07-24), who wins GPU allocation and why. https://radical.vc/articles/cracking-the-ai-chip-shortage-strategies-and-opportunities/\n4. CentML takes on global AI chip bottleneck (2023-06-12), their founding-seed memo. https://radical.vc/articles/centml-takes-on-global-ai-chip-bottleneck/\n5. Building AI Factories: Radical's investment in Crusoe (2025-10-27, with Rob Toews). https://radical.vc/articles/building-ai-factories-radicals-investment-in-crusoe/\n\n## Social engagement\nNo personal social account found. Their public voice comes through Radical Reads bylines and press quotes that Radical relays (FT, WSJ).\n\n## Portfolio\nEvery AI deal where Katz is personally named (byline or co-byline on Radical's investment post):\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CentML | Seed ($3.5M founding seed) | 2023-06-12 (portfolio page says 2022) | Lead not stated; \"founding\" seed from Radical with no co-investors named, so likely sole (inference) | none named | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/centml-takes-on-global-ai-chip-bottleneck/) |\n| Crusoe | Series E ($1.375B) | 2025-10 (post 2025-10-27) | No (co-authored post with Rob Toews; lead not named in post) | not named in post | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/building-ai-factories-radicals-investment-in-crusoe/) |\n| Prime Intellect | Series A ($130M) | 2026-07-13 | Yes (\"our lead investment\"); board not stated | NVIDIA Ventures, Intel Capital, Dell Technologies Capital, ICONIQ | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/prime-intellect-owning-the-learning-loop/) |\n\nFirm-level follow-on, not personally attributed to Katz: Radical re-upped in Crusoe's Series F ($3.9B) through the Breakouts Fund in Sept 2026 [src](https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/). Daniel Mulet's team page also lists CentML [src](https://radical.vc/team/daniel-mulet/).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **CentML (first in).** Radical made CentML's founding seed. CentML is a Toronto company led by Gennady Pekhimenko, a Vector Institute faculty member and University of Toronto professor. Katz's post presents it as a software answer to GPU scarcity, at a time when chips were \"highly concentrated in the hands of just a few chip producers\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/centml-takes-on-global-ai-chip-bottleneck/). The intro path isn't stated; the Vector/UofT link fits Radical's home network (inference).\n- **Crusoe (late entry, doubled down).** Crusoe's first institutional money came in 2019 from Bain Capital Ventures and Founders Fund, as a co-led seed [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crusoe-climb-betting-on-power-before-ai-was-cool/). Radical arrived about six years later at the oversubscribed Series E. Toews and Katz co-wrote the post, which calls the AI buildout \"the largest capital investment in the history of human civilization\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/building-ai-factories-radicals-investment-in-crusoe/). Radical then re-upped in the Series F through Breakouts.\n- **Prime Intellect (late lead).** The first round was a $5.5M seed in April 2024, co-led by CoinFund and Distributed Global [src](https://www.theblock.co/news/deals/2024-04-23-coinfund-and-distributed-global-lead-5-5-million-seed-round-for-decentralized-ai-firm-prime-intellect-290476). Founders Fund led a $15M round in February 2025 [src](https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/fundraise). Radical led the $130M Series A in July 2026, about 27 months after the first round, with NVIDIA, Intel and Dell corporate VCs alongside. Katz names CEO Vincent Weisser, CTO Johannes Hagemann and research lead Will Brown (creator of the verifiers RL library). Customer proof cited: Ramp's 35B-parameter agent beat a frontier model on spreadsheet retrieval [src](https://radical.vc/articles/prime-intellect-owning-the-learning-loop/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Partner (joined as Operating Partner) | 2021-09 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/) |\n| Rob Toews | Co-author on the Crusoe investment; joined in the same 2021 announcement | 2021-09 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/building-ai-factories-radicals-investment-in-crusoe/) |\n| Jordan Jacobs, John Megrue, Blair Bernholtz | Co-related persons on the Fund IV and Growth Fund I Form Ds | 2024 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2065455/000095010325005206/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Gennady Pekhimenko (CentML) | Founding-seed investor | 2023 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/centml-takes-on-global-ai-chip-bottleneck/) |\n| Vincent Weisser (Prime Intellect) | Lead Series A investor | 2026-07 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/prime-intellect-owning-the-learning-loop/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who acquired CentML, and at what price? Radical's portfolio marks it \"Acquired\". If the buyer was NVIDIA, as widely reported in 2025, that ties into Radical's NVIDIA co-investment pattern. Not verified here.\n- Does Katz sit on the Prime Intellect board? The post doesn't say.\n- When exactly did their title change from Operating Partner to Partner (between 2021-09 and 2023-07)?\n- Does the WSJ piece say how compute access is priced into deals (for example, GPU allocation in exchange for allocation in a round)?\n- Which compute providers does the desk use (Crusoe, CoreWeave, Google Cloud through the Founders compute cluster)?\n- Personal X handle: none found. Web search was exhausted during this run.\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/david-katz/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/centml-takes-on-global-ai-chip-bottleneck/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/cracking-the-ai-chip-shortage-strategies-and-opportunities/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/why-we-led-nabla-bios-series-a/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/building-ai-infrastructure/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/the-worlds-first-ai-native-investment-bank/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/building-ai-factories-radicals-investment-in-crusoe/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/prime-intellect-owning-the-learning-loop/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/how-agentic-activity-changes-the-risk-equation/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/\n- SEC Form D: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2065455/000095010325005206/primary_doc.xml (Fund IV); https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2024166/000207751625000004/primary_doc.xml (Growth Fund I)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crusoe-climb-betting-on-power-before-ai-was-cool/ ; https://www.theblock.co/news/deals/2024-04-23-coinfund-and-distributed-global-lead-5-5-million-seed-round-for-decentralized-ai-firm-prime-intellect-290476 ; https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/fundraise (taken from existing repo rows, not re-fetched)\n", "person:david-kwon": "\n# David Kwon\n\n## One-line take\nThe first investor Ramtin Naimi added to Abstract in six years (2023, via a Coatue connection), a growth-trained enterprise investor now holding a GP seat at an AI-heavy seed firm, but with no AI deal yet tied to him in a primary source ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/), [src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he could win:** he sits on a four-person investment team at a firm that by Fund III led 80-90% of its deals and raised $500M in 2025, so a GP there can lead seeds and write $10M+ checks ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). He was hired under Naimi's rule of one exceptional ~26-year-old a year, given real responsibility immediately and reviewed after three years ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). The 2023 hire plus a GP title by 2026 suggests he passed that review (inference, no promotion announcement found).\n- **Constraint:** his training is growth and enterprise software (Stripes, Greycroft), not technical AI ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)). Abstract's best-known AI wins (Hebbia, Krea, Applied Labs, Natural) are credited to Naimi, and none to Kwon ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- **What he'd never do:** nothing sourced.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Hiring path ran through Coatue, not Greycroft.** Colossus: \"David Kwon came first, through a connection at Coatue.\" Before 2023 the investment team was only Naimi and Davidov, for six years. The Coatue person is not named. ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/), July 2025)\n- **He is the first of Naimi's \"26-year-old per year\" hires.** The rule: \"Hire one exceptional 26-year-old every year\" with a review after three years. Kozyrev (2024, via Sequoia) was the second. So the review would have fallen around 2026, and he is GP on the firm page as of 2026-11-10. ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/), [src](https://www.abstract.com/people/))\n- **Growth-trained in a seed-lead seat.** \"He'd cut his teeth in growth investing at Stripes and Greycroft.\" This is unusual for a firm that now leads most of its seeds ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- **Headshot timing:** the firm's photo of him (and Kozyrev, Heckman) is in a 2026/06 upload folder named `260529_AbstractVentures_...`, which suggests a team-page refresh around 2026-05-29. That may be when the GP titles appeared (inference only) ([src](https://www.abstract.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/260529_AbstractVentures_DavidKwon.png)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is a GP at a high-fit firm. Abstract is AI-heavy at seed (Cognition, Physical Intelligence, Periodic Labs, Applied Compute, xAI on its portfolio page) and has the capital to lead ([src](https://www.abstract.com/companies-list/), via [firms/abstract.md](../firms/abstract.md)). But no frontier AI, chip or AI-automation deal is tied to him in a primary source. His only sourced deals are non-AI Greycroft enterprise and fintech names. He would be high if the unverified Deeptune claim (RL environments for labs; a16z-led $43M Series A, 2026-03-19, Abstract participated) is confirmed as his ([src](https://deeptune.com/blog/series-a/)). He is worth knowing as a likely co-investor contact at Abstract who is less crowded than Naimi.\n\n## Background\n- UC Berkeley, BA Economics ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)).\n- Stripes: started there, investing in growth-stage software and consumer products; dates not found ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)).\n- Greycroft: led enterprise software investments including Extra Card, ZenBusiness and MNTN Digital, all described on the bio as reaching unicorn valuations; dates not found ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)). He is not on greycroft.com/team as of 2026-11-10 (checked with WebFetch).\n- Abstract: joined 2023 as the first hire of the firm's recruiting push ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). He is General Partner on the firm page (checked 2026-11-10) ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)). TheOrg also lists him as General Partner but gives no dates ([src](https://theorg.com/org/abstract-ventures/org-chart/david-kwon), aggregator).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.abstract.com/people/ | Firm bio; no social links on the page (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | No firm blog exists ([firms/abstract.md](../firms/abstract.md)) |\n| X | not found | Not linked from the firm page; web search budget exhausted, so not checked further |\n| LinkedIn | not found | Not linked from the firm page; not guessed |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Naimi's Invest Like the Best episode (2025-07-29) is about the firm. Kwon is not confirmed as appearing in it ([src](https://colossus.com/?p=398983)).\n\n## How they think\nNothing sourced in his own words. The only sourced signal is structural: he is a growth/enterprise investor moved into a seed-lead role.\n\n## Read these first\n- Colossus profile of Naimi and Abstract (July 2025): the only source on how and why he was hired ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- Abstract team page bio ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X or LinkedIn handle is verified.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ZenBusiness (at Greycroft) | Series A ($15M; Greycroft led as returning backer). Which ZenBusiness round Kwon worked on is unconfirmed | 2019-09-25 | Greycroft led; his role not stated | Lerer Hippeau, Revolution Rise of the Rest, Rosecliff, Interlock, Recruit Strategic Partners | [bio](https://www.abstract.com/people/), [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2019/09/25/zenbusiness-raises-15m-to-help-founders-launch-and-grow-worry-free/) |\n| MNTN (\"MNTN Digital\", at Greycroft) | Undisclosed | not found | unknown | not found | [bio](https://www.abstract.com/people/) |\n| Extra (\"Extra Card\", at Greycroft) | Undisclosed | not found | unknown | not found | [bio](https://www.abstract.com/people/) |\n| Deeptune (at Abstract) | Series A ($43M, a16z lead; Abstract participated) | 2026-03-19 | no | a16z, 776, Inspired Capital | **Unconfirmed as his.** The company post names no Abstract partner ([src](https://deeptune.com/blog/series-a/)). No deals row written. |\n\nNo Abstract deal is tied to him by name in a primary source.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Timing, ZenBusiness:** Greycroft was already a backer before leading the $15M Series A in September 2019 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2019/09/25/zenbusiness-raises-15m-to-help-founders-launch-and-grow-worry-free/)). His exact entry point is unknown.\n- No Abstract deal story found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Abstract | at: General Partner | 2023 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Greycroft | at: led enterprise software investments (until about 2023) | not found | [src](https://www.abstract.com/people/) |\n| Stripes | at: growth investor (career start) | not found | [src](https://www.abstract.com/people/) |\n| Coatue (unnamed person) | connection that brought him to Abstract | 2023 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Ramtin Naimi | hired him; first of the \"one 26-year-old a year\" hires | 2023 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Andrei Kozyrev | fellow GP; second such hire (2024, via Sequoia) | 2024 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Deeptune:** an earlier search snippet credited him with a post calling Deeptune his first Abstract investment. The post was not located. deeptune.com/blog/series-a names Abstract Ventures but no partner. Unconfirmed.\n- **Who at Coatue** introduced him, and whether he worked at or with Coatue.\n- **Join title and GP promotion date:** not found on the firm site, Colossus or TheOrg. The 2026/06 headshot folder hints at a May 2026 team-page refresh.\n- **Greycroft dates and rounds** for Extra and MNTN: greycroft.com team, portfolio and news pages returned 403/404. Web search budget was exhausted this session, so press for those rounds was not found.\n- **SEC EDGAR:** a full-text search for \"David Kwon\" returns only an unrelated filer, \"Kwon David\" (CIK 1872194), an ADP officer in Roseland, NJ. That is a different person. No Form D related-person hit for him.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.abstract.com/people/\n- https://www.abstract.com/\n- https://www.abstract.com/companies-list/\n- https://www.abstract.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/260529_AbstractVentures_DavidKwon.png (curl -sIL: HTTP 200, image/png, 2026-11-10)\n- https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/\n- https://colossus.com/?p=398983\n- https://deeptune.com/blog/series-a/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2019/09/25/zenbusiness-raises-15m-to-help-founders-launch-and-grow-worry-free/\n- https://theorg.com/org/abstract-ventures/org-chart/david-kwon (aggregator)\n- https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22David+Kwon%22 (EDGAR full-text; unrelated hits only)\n", "person:dean-meyer": "\n# Dean Meyer\n\n## One-line take\nMeyer is Sequoia's Israel seed-sourcing node. Several senior partners (Maguire, Balkansky, Cahn) attach to their deals, and four of their seven listed companies are AI-agent or frontier-AI security, with Decart as the one true frontier-lab bet [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/dean-meyer/).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** proximity and speed in the Israeli Unit 8200 / Talpiot founder pool. They get to founders before there is a company. On AIR, Sequoia shook hands on the seed two days after a founder left military service [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-air-securing-the-ai-supply-chain/).\n- **Origin of the edge:** Sequoia hired them in March 2024 after co-investing with them in Eon's seed while they ran a US seed fund's Tel Aviv office [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/dean-meyer/).\n- **Constraint:** they are a node, not a solo decision-maker. Every deal is co-signed with a senior partner (Maguire on Decart, Irregular, Eon and Kela; Balkansky on Cymphony and AIR; Cahn on Kela and AIR). Their own thesis surface is narrow: Israeli security, defense and infrastructure.\n- **What they'd likely never do (inference, unsourced):** back a first-time founder without deep, long-term domain experience. Their stated filter is \"real experience in a particular problem space\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/dean-meyer/).\n\n## Differential insight\nNo podcasts, Substack, X account or talks by Meyer were found (web-search budget was exhausted mid-task; only direct fetches were possible). Everything below comes from Sequoia \"Partnering with\" posts that Meyer co-authored and from their first-person Sequoia bio. These details are rarely picked up by press.\n- **Co-investing was the hiring audition.** Meyer arranged a lunch with Eon's founder in Nov 2023 and brought Maguire. The seed came together in about a week, and Sequoia then hired Meyer: \"in March, we happily welcomed Dean to the Sequoia team\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/)\n- **Prior seat:** \"Eventually I opened the Tel Aviv office for a young U.S. seed fund.\" The fund is not named [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/dean-meyer/)\n- **Admitted mistake:** a college trading bot made money, then lost it all to automated trades because it had no safeguards. \"I built a trading bot that made quite a lot of money.\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/dean-meyer/)\n- **Pre-company commitment (AIR):** \"before they had a company, a name, or a product\". Also \"Yair finished his service on a Thursday, and we shook hands\" (on a seed that Saturday) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-air-securing-the-ai-supply-chain/)\n- **Agent-security thesis:** \"Every agent now imports plugins and skills from random places.\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-air-securing-the-ai-supply-chain/) Also \"Identity security and data security are no longer separable.\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-cymphony-security-unlocks-adoption/)\n- **Irregular conviction came from a demo** of an agent bypassing endpoint security: \"Dan pulled up a test-environment example of an AI agent\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-irregular-ahead-of-the-curve/)\n- **Cymphony intro path:** \"introduced separately by two people in our orbit\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-cymphony-security-unlocks-adoption/)\n- **Decart sourcing ran US-side:** \"From our first walk with Dean in the Bay Area\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-decart-the-future-of-ai-generated-experiences/)\n\n### Timing (first in? who followed?)\n- **Decart:** Sequoia led the seed (post dated 2024-10-31; $21M per the company timeline, quarter inferred) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-decart-the-future-of-ai-generated-experiences/) [src](https://www.decart.ai/about). Benchmark followed with a $32M Series A (TechCrunch caption: over $500M valuation). Next came $100M at $3.1B (Q3 2025, per a Fortune caption), then Radical Ventures led $300M on 2026-05-18, with NVIDIA, Atreides, Valor and Karpathy joining and Sequoia, Benchmark and Zeev returning [src](https://www.decart.ai/publications/decart-raises-300m-tech-leaders-back-the-company-as-both-customers-and-investors). Sequoia was first institutional; the next firm came in within about one quarter.\n- **Irregular:** Sequoia and Redpoint co-led $80M at about $450M (2025-09-17) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/17/irregular-raises-80-million-to-secure-frontier-ai-models/). Earlier rounds (as Pattern Labs) are unconfirmed, so \"first in\" is not established.\n- **Eon:** first meeting Nov 2023; the Sequoia-led seed came about a week later. Sequoia says it doubled down in the Series B [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/).\n- **Kela:** an April 2024 Israel trip led to a $10M Sequoia-led seed. Lux (Reeves, Wolfe) led the A [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-kela-modern-defense-for-israel-and-western-allies/).\n- **AIR:** a seed handshake before the company existed (pre-empting everyone); Sequoia \"doubling down\" in the A (2026-09-01) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-air-securing-the-ai-supply-chain/).\n- **Cymphony:** Sequoia led the seed (partnered 2024) and co-led the A (2026-09-09) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-cymphony-security-unlocks-adoption/).\n- **Glow:** came out of stealth with $180M at $1.2B (2026-07-22). Sequoia was one of four leads with Cyberstarts, Greenoaks and Redpoint [src](https://www.glow.io/news/glow-emerges-from-stealth-with-180-million).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium overall, with one high-fit deal.** The book is mostly Israeli security, defense and cloud infrastructure, which sits outside pre-training, labs and compute.\n- **Decart (high):** a real-time generative-video / world-model research lab, Sequoia-led seed, and now over $450M raised. It is the closest thing to a \"new lab\" in this book.\n- **Irregular (high-adjacent):** a frontier AI security lab that runs evaluations for Anthropic and OpenAI models [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/17/irregular-raises-80-million-to-secure-frontier-ai-models/). It is embedded in the frontier-lab supply chain.\n- **AIR and Cymphony (medium):** AI-native companies, but security tooling for agents rather than automation or research.\n- **Glow (low):** came out of stealth at $1.2B, too late and too large for my entry.\n- **Eon (skip) and Kela (low):** cloud backup and defense.\n\nWhy follow anyway: Meyer is the earliest Sequoia touchpoint for Israeli research talent, including founders leaving 8200, Talpiot and the Technion. A frontier-lab founder from Israel would likely meet them first. They are also a co-investor path into Sequoia-led Israeli seeds.\n\n## Background\n- Homeschooled through high school, focused on math and computer science. Moved to Amsterdam to play soccer as a youth [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/dean-meyer/)\n- College in Israel (institution not named). Worked as a data scientist at a startup while in college [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/dean-meyer/)\n- Opened the Tel Aviv office of a young US seed fund, backing infrastructure, cloud, data, AI and cyber founders. Dates and fund name not found [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/dean-meyer/)\n- Joined Sequoia in March 2024 [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/)\n- Interests: family, soccer, part-time archaeology, Paganini [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/dean-meyer/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/dean-meyer/ | First-person bio; email dmeyer@sequoiacap.com listed |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes via Sequoia \"Partnering with\" posts |\n| X | nothing found | Not linked from Sequoia page |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | Not linked; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found (web search unavailable this session). Related: Sequoia podcast \"Decart's Dean Leitersdorf on AI-Generated Video Games and Worlds\", hosted by Sonya Huang and Shaun Maguire, without Meyer [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/decart/).\n\n## How they think\n- **Deep domain experience over polish:** \"I look for people that have some real experience in a particular problem space\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/dean-meyer/)\n- **Security is the gate on agent adoption.** Agents import unvetted components at runtime, and identity and data security merge into one control layer (AIR, Cymphony, Glow, Irregular).\n- **Pre-empt at the idea stage** for elite-unit founders (AIR). Move within days (Eon, AIR).\n- **Calls so far:** Decart's valuation rose from seed to $3.1B within about a year, which looks right so far. Irregular went to about $450M. Too early to judge the rest.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Sequoia bio](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/dean-meyer/): first-person origin story, the trading-bot mistake, and their founder filter.\n- [Partnering with Eon](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/): how the co-investment became the hire.\n- [Partnering with Air](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-air-securing-the-ai-supply-chain/): the pre-company seed and the agent supply-chain thesis.\n- [Partnering with Irregular](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-irregular-ahead-of-the-curve/): frontier-lab security.\n- [Partnering with Cymphony](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-cymphony-security-unlocks-adoption/): identity and data security for agents.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public X or Substack presence identified.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Eon | Seed | 2023-11 (meeting) | Sequoia led (Meyer was at prior fund) | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/) |\n| Decart | Seed ($21M, inferred) | 2024-10-31 | Yes (Sequoia) | not confirmed at seed | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-decart-the-future-of-ai-generated-experiences/) [src](https://www.decart.ai/about) |\n| Decart | $300M (round unnamed) | 2026-05-18 | No (Radical) | NVIDIA, Atreides, Valor, Benchmark, Zeev, Karpathy | [src](https://www.decart.ai/publications/decart-raises-300m-tech-leaders-back-the-company-as-both-customers-and-investors) |\n| Kela | Seed ($10M) | 2024 | Yes (Sequoia) | Lux led A | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-kela-modern-defense-for-israel-and-western-allies/) |\n| Cymphony | Seed; A co-lead | partnered 2024; A 2026-09-09 | Yes | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-cymphony-security-unlocks-adoption/) |\n| Irregular | $80M at ~$450M | 2025-09-17 | Co-lead | Redpoint (co-lead), Assaf Rappaport | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/17/irregular-raises-80-million-to-secure-frontier-ai-models/) |\n| Glow | $180M at $1.2B (stealth exit) | 2026-07-22 | One of four leads | Cyberstarts, Greenoaks, Redpoint, Index, Swish, Lux | [src](https://www.glow.io/news/glow-emerges-from-stealth-with-180-million) |\n| AIR | Seed; A participation | 2026-09-01 | Seed yes | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-air-securing-the-ai-supply-chain/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Eon:** Meyer invited Ofir Ehrlich to a lunch originally planned as one-on-one and brought Shaun Maguire, who bonded with Ehrlich over cyber. The seed came together within about a week, Sequoia led, and Meyer joined Sequoia in March 2024 [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/).\n- **Decart:** the first meeting was a walk in the Bay Area with Dean Leitersdorf, who splits time with Tel Aviv. The full team was then met in Tel Aviv. Sequoia led the seed on technical depth and GPU efficiency [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-decart-the-future-of-ai-generated-experiences/).\n- **Irregular:** in one of the first meetings, Dan Lahav demoed an agent bypassing endpoint security. Sequoia co-led the $80M round with Redpoint [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-irregular-ahead-of-the-curve/) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/17/irregular-raises-80-million-to-secure-frontier-ai-models/).\n- **Kela:** Sequoia had independently formed an Israeli defense-tech thesis and knew two of the founders. On an April 2024 trip, a breakfast meeting turned into a full day, and Sequoia led the $10M seed [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-kela-modern-defense-for-israel-and-western-allies/).\n- **AIR:** Sequoia met two Unit 8200 vulnerability researchers before the company existed and ran references across Israeli cyber. They shook hands on the seed the Saturday after one founder left service [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-air-securing-the-ai-supply-chain/).\n- **Cymphony:** two people in Sequoia's orbit introduced the three Talpiot founders separately. Sequoia led the seed and co-led the A [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-cymphony-security-unlocks-adoption/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sequoia Capital | Partner (people page); brief says Investor (Core) | 2024-03 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/dean-meyer/) |\n| Unnamed young US seed fund | Opened its Tel Aviv office | before 2024-03 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/dean-meyer/) |\n| Shaun Maguire | Co-partner: Decart, Irregular, Eon, Kela, Glow | 2023-11 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/irregular/) |\n| Bogomil Balkansky | Co-partner: Cymphony, AIR | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-cymphony-security-unlocks-adoption/) |\n| David Cahn | Co-author: Kela, AIR | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-air-securing-the-ai-supply-chain/) |\n| Ofir Ehrlich (Eon) | Founder Meyer introduced to Sequoia | 2023-11 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/) |\n| Dean Leitersdorf (Decart) | Portfolio founder | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/decart/) |\n| Dan Lahav (Irregular) | Portfolio founder | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/irregular/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which US seed fund did Meyer open the Tel Aviv office for? Did that fund also invest in Eon's seed?\n- Meyer's title: Partner (Sequoia page) or Investor (Core)? Has there been a recent promotion?\n- Irregular's rounds before the $80M (as Pattern Labs): was Sequoia or Meyer's prior fund first in?\n- Decart's seed co-investors (was Zeev Ventures at seed?) and the exact seed date.\n- Does Meyer have an X, LinkedIn or podcast presence? Search was unavailable this session.\n- Is Meyer seeing Israeli pre-training or new-lab founders (e.g. Technion and 8200 alumni leaving frontier labs)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/dean-meyer/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-irregular-ahead-of-the-curve/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-decart-the-future-of-ai-generated-experiences/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-kela-modern-defense-for-israel-and-western-allies/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-cymphony-security-unlocks-adoption/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-air-securing-the-ai-supply-chain/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/decart/ ; /companies/irregular/ ; /companies/glow/ ; /companies/air/ ; /companies/cymphony/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/17/irregular-raises-80-million-to-secure-frontier-ai-models/\n- https://www.decart.ai/about\n- https://www.decart.ai/publications/decart-raises-300m-tech-leaders-back-the-company-as-both-customers-and-investors\n- https://www.glow.io/news/glow-emerges-from-stealth-with-180-million\n", "person:deedy-das": "\n# Deedy Das\n\n## One-line take\nAn ex-Glean founding engineer who uses Menlo's $100M Anthology Fund (with Anthropic) as a seed wedge into frontier-adjacent AI. Their first board seat was Goodfire, an interpretability lab, and they were on OpenRouter from seed through Stripe's acquisition. Pangram came from a conversation on X. Menlo's balance sheet then follows on with $50M-class leads.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win.** They pair a seed-sized wedge with multi-stage follow-on. The Anthology Fund lets Menlo enter at seed with checks from $100K ([src](https://menlovc.com/anthology-fund/)). Das then pushes for Menlo to lead the next round. Goodfire went from seed to a Menlo-led $50M A within a year, and OpenRouter from a March 2025 seed to a Menlo-led A in May 2025 ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/leading-goodfires-50m-series-a-to-interpret-how-ai-models-think/), [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/stripe-to-acquire-openrouter-why-everyone-is-obsessed-with-model-routing/)). They name the structural reason themselves: \"traditionally, multi-stage firms are not going to do a lot of seeds\" ([src](https://stackoverflow.blog/2024/09/27/deedy-das-meta-google-menlo-ventures-ai-anthropic/)).\n- **Sourcing edge.** They have a large technical audience on X and talk to founders there directly. Pangram started that way ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/investing-in-pangram-to-stop-ai-slop-on-the-internet/)). They also do outbound: Menlo published its first cold email to OpenRouter ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/stripe-to-acquire-openrouter-why-everyone-is-obsessed-with-model-routing/)).\n- **Constraints.**\n  - They are rarely first in. On Goodfire, Lightspeed was the seed partner. On Prime Intellect, Founders Fund led and Menlo only participated. On Modal and Greptile, Menlo entered at the C and B.\n  - Anthology deals carry an Anthropic-ecosystem lens. Many deals are co-signed with Matt Murphy or Tim Tully, so how much they can lead alone is still emerging.\n- **What they'd never do.** No direct quote found. Their Pangram memo praises founders who \"simply refused\" to build a humanizer. That reads as a values filter: they avoid companies that profit from undermining trust.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **OpenRouter was a first-weeks outbound deal.** \"OpenRouter was one of the first companies Deedy met after joining Menlo in 2024.\" The timeline: Anthology seed in March 2025, Menlo led the A in May 2025, then the Stripe deal was announced 2026-08-19 ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/stripe-to-acquire-openrouter-why-everyone-is-obsessed-with-model-routing/)). That is roughly 17 months from seed to exit. Das's own site gives the Stripe price as $8B ([src](https://debarghyadas.com)). The firm post itself gives no price.\n- **Contrarian view on routing.** They write that \"routing based solely on the prompt is actually a bit of a fool's errand.\" They also say OpenRouter's real product is being \"the best AI gateway\", not routing ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/stripe-to-acquire-openrouter-why-everyone-is-obsessed-with-model-routing/), 2026-08-19).\n- **Pangram was sourced on X.** \"I first began chatting with Max on X\". They followed up with an office visit in Brooklyn, then Menlo led the seed through Anthology ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/investing-in-pangram-to-stop-ai-slop-on-the-internet/), 2026-07-29). TechCrunch puts the raise at $9M with Haystack, ScOp, Script Capital and Cadenza ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/29/as-ai-content-floods-the-internet-pangram-raises-9m-to-detect-it/)).\n- **Goodfire was their first board seat.** They call it \"my first board seat\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/leading-goodfires-50m-series-a-to-interpret-how-ai-models-think/), 2025-04-17). The seed was alongside Lightspeed, and Goodfire was the first Anthology company to \"graduate\" to a full A. Goodfire's own post that day calls Das an \"investor\" at Menlo, not a partner ([src](https://www.goodfire.com/blog/announcing-our-50m-series-a)). That supports a promotion after April 2025.\n- **Why they left Glean.** \"I felt like I wasn't learning much\". They also say \"I never considered being a VC in my life\" ([src](https://stackoverflow.blog/2024/09/27/deedy-das-meta-google-menlo-ventures-ai-anthropic/), 2024-09-27).\n- **The data view they publish.** Menlo's 2026 enterprise report, which they co-authored, says \"Model capability—not price—is proving to be the decisive competitive advantage.\" It also says agents now generate about 80% of OpenRouter tokens ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/2026-the-state-of-enterprise-ai/), 2026-11-03). OpenRouter's token data gives Menlo a proprietary read on which models win. That is an informational edge on labs.\n- **Anthology graduation claim.** They write that \"Anthology companies show significantly higher graduation rates\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/q3-2025-anthology-fund-update/), 2025-10-24). As of 2026-06-23, Anthology had backed 60+ companies with three exits ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlo-turns-50-and-announces-3b-in-fresh-capital-to-go-all-in-on-ai/)).\n- **Correction to the brief's deal list.**\n  - **Prime Intellect.** Menlo's card says \"Series A, 2025\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/portfolio/prime-intellect/)). But Prime Intellect's own posts show a $15M raise on 2025-02-28, led by Founders Fund with Menlo participating ([src](https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/fundraise)). Its actual $130M Series A on 2026-07-08 was led by Radical Ventures and doesn't name Menlo ([src](https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/series-a)). Das is not named in either company post.\n  - **Inception, Ndea and Wispr Flow.** They appear on Das's team page, but Menlo names Tim Tully on Inception and Ndea, and TechCrunch names Matt Kraning on Wispr Flow ([src](https://menlovc.com/portfolio/inception/), [src](https://menlovc.com/portfolio/ndea/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/06/24/wispr-flow-raises-30m-from-menlo-ventures-for-its-ai-powered-dictation-app/)).\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-10)\nDrawn from two Latent Space transcripts (2023 and 2025), the Sourcery episode (2026), their own blog and their X archive. The full catalogue has 101 items, in `data/rows/deedy-das-voice-media.csv`.\n- **Anthology check range is $100K to $20M.** They say \"we do everything from, uh, like, 100K check to a $20 million check\" (00:45:33) ([src](https://latent.space/p/anthropic-glean-and-openrouter-how/)).\n  - Anthology picks companies in two lanes: ones strategically important to Anthropic, and great companies that use Claude heavily.\n  - It had funded about 40 companies by Nov 2025.\n- **OpenRouter began as a miss.**\n  - They admit \"I did not know he was taking investment or I would have invested\" (00:53:55).\n  - Then they flew to New York. Alex Atallah \"ignored me a bunch of times forever\", and they kept sending \"love letters\".\n  - They promised \"if you are ever going to raise, I will make it happen\" ([src](https://latent.space/p/anthropic-glean-and-openrouter-how/)).\n- **Goodfire was a conversion, not a thesis.** They \"didn't even think that was a market that was worth investing in\" until Anthropic's interest changed their view (00:48:13).\n  - On Prime Intellect, what they say they underwrote was \"access to talent\" ([src](https://latent.space/p/anthropic-glean-and-openrouter-how/)).\n- **Anthropic entry.** Menlo's first check went in when the company \"had no revenue\" ([src](https://latent.space/p/anthropic-glean-and-openrouter-how/)).\n- **Price ceiling on neolabs.** They say \"I just don't see how people can write a billion dollar seed\" ([src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-inside-anthropics-100m-anthology), 2026-02-05).\n  - On X they mock $100M \"elite researcher\" seeds ([src](https://x.com/deedydas/status/1964873989152793007)).\n  - They track the $1B-seed cohort: Lila, General Intuition, Periodic, Thinking Machines, SSI ([src](https://x.com/deedydas/status/1979392893870809598)).\n  - Implication: they are unlikely to co-lead at the owner's $600M-seed size. They are more useful earlier or at the A.\n- **Anti-thesis, people-first.**\n  - They say iconic companies almost never come \"from anyone's thesis area\" ([src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-inside-anthropics-100m-anthology)).\n  - That echoes 2023, before Menlo, when they said early bets were \"governed by my intuition of how they approach the problem\" ([src](https://latent.space/p/deedy-das/)).\n  - In the same 2023 episode, host Alessio Fanelli (Decibel) called them a scout. The transcript is garbled, so that is unconfirmed.\n- **Admitted miss, 2023.** They predicted LLM quality was \"near about like a, a maxima\" ([src](https://latent.space/p/deedy-das/), 00:59:17). Their 2026 Menlo report now argues capability, not price, wins.\n- **Pangram was foreshadowed.** In April 2023 they were already bullish on standardised watermarking of AI text ([src](https://latent.space/p/deedy-das/), 00:49:33), three years before leading the Pangram seed.\n- **Diligence alarm.**\n  - Nov 2025: an unincorporated startup was offered $10M, and a $20M A closed in 2 days with no dataroom opens. They say firms are \"skipping due diligence in its entirety\" ([src](https://x.com/deedydas/status/1987735293206892953)).\n  - They are also sceptical of expert-network calls behind $200M decisions ([src](https://x.com/deedydas/status/1900718976088342619)).\n- **Anthology portfolio wider than the team page.** As of Aug 2025 they named Mercor, Chai Discovery, Graphite, Wisdom AI and All Hands (OpenHands) alongside OpenRouter, Goodfire, Wispr Flow and Prime Intellect ([src](https://x.com/deedydas/status/1955659829030379985)).\n- **Founder filter dates to 2019.** They chose Glean because \"the Founder had run a large unicorn in the past\" ([src](https://debarghyadas.com/writes/why-i-left-google/)).\n- **Promotion dates now confirmed from their own post.** They joined Menlo on 2024-03-14 ([src](https://x.com/deedydas/status/1768316321790623965)) and were named Partner \"after a remarkable 18mos\" on 2025-09-15 ([src](https://x.com/deedydas/status/1967612890367861098)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.**\n- **For:**\n  - They are personally named on Goodfire (interpretability lab, seed 2024 then a Menlo-led $50M A), Prime Intellect (distributed training and open superintelligence, 2025 round) and Modal (compute).\n  - Anthology is a seed-stage, frontier-adjacent pipeline with Anthropic in the loop.\n  - They are a recently promoted partner at a $3B multi-stage firm that can write $10M+ follow-ons ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlo-turns-50-and-announces-3b-in-fresh-capital-to-go-all-in-on-ai/)). That is exactly the \"principal turned partner with growth-capable balance sheet\" segment.\n- **Against:**\n  - They are rarely the first institutional money on labs: Lightspeed was on Goodfire, Founders Fund on Prime Intellect.\n  - Most of the portfolio is AI infrastructure and tools (OpenRouter, Modal, Greptile, Fintool), not pre-training labs or chips.\n- **Useful as:** a co-investor or follow-on leader for seed labs you back, and a source of read-through on model market share via OpenRouter data.\n\n## Background\n- Cornell, BS and MS in computer science (AI/ML, information retrieval) ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/deedy-das/)).\n- About five years at Facebook and Google in technical roles, including Search ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/deedy-das/)).\n- Joined Glean (then Scio) in 2019 on the founding team with Arvind Jain. Moved from engineer to engineering manager, then created and led Glean Assistant. Stayed about four years ([src](https://stackoverflow.blog/2024/09/27/deedy-das-meta-google-menlo-ventures-ai-anthropic/), [src](https://debarghyadas.com)).\n- Joined Menlo Ventures in 2024 ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/stripe-to-acquire-openrouter-why-everyone-is-obsessed-with-model-routing/)). Titled Principal on 2024-09-27 ([src](https://stackoverflow.blog/2024/09/27/deedy-das-meta-google-menlo-ventures-ai-anthropic/)).\n- Partner by July 2026 at the latest (Pangram memo byline) ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/investing-in-pangram-to-stop-ai-slop-on-the-internet/)). The brief's dates (joined 2024-03-14, partner 2025-09-15) are unconfirmed. LinkedIn returned HTTP 999 and no promotion post was found.\n- Angel investments in Perplexity, Cartesia and Fulcrum ([src](https://debarghyadas.com)).\n- Pronouns: \"they\" here. Pronouns weren't confirmed verbatim in fetched sources.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://debarghyadas.com | Personal site with essays, talks and press list (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://debarghyadas.com/writes/ | EB-1A guide, Indian education, grad school stats |\n| X | https://x.com/deedydas | Main channel; follower count not fetched (x.com returned 402) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/debarghyadas | Not fetchable |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nListed on their own site ([src](https://debarghyadas.com)); none transcribed for this pass.\n- 2024-09-27: Stack Overflow podcast/blog, on Meta, Google, Menlo and Anthropic. Covers the Anthology rationale and why they left Glean ([src](https://stackoverflow.blog/2024/09/27/deedy-das-meta-google-menlo-ventures-ai-anthropic/)).\n- Undated:\n  - WTF with Nikhil Kamath (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g0CjWbgsdTQ)\n  - This Week in Startups (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3yVIV4Cy2w4)\n  - Latent Space II (https://youtu.be/8UDj3-JDfYY)\n  - Aarthi and Sriram Show (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AMNJDX49pes)\n  - Ventures with Grace (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F5gZL6wfO4M)\n  - AI50 with Hanh Brown, on AI investments (https://www.buzzsprout.com/765170/15098120-shaping-the-future-of-ai-investments-with-debarghya-deedy-das)\n  - Nextplay with Ben Lang\n  - Sourcery with Molly O'Shea, listed as TBD\n- Talks:\n  - Harvard Business School, Wharton and Kellogg (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cOhYUhsfeKc)\n  - TEDx Bangalore (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JY7pYSCX6Dc)\n\n## How they think\n- **Multi-model world.** Gateways win, and routing on the prompt alone is a \"fool's errand\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/stripe-to-acquire-openrouter-why-everyone-is-obsessed-with-model-routing/)).\n- **Capability over price.** Frontier capability, not price, decides enterprise model choice ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/2026-the-state-of-enterprise-ai/)).\n- **Interpretability as a platform.** In the Goodfire memo they describe it as \"brain surgery\" on neural networks ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/leading-goodfires-50m-series-a-to-interpret-how-ai-models-think/)).\n- **Product over tech.** Their bio line is that a fantastic product, \"not just tech\", retains users ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/deedy-das/)).\n- **Calls that went right:**\n  - OpenRouter: from seed to the Stripe acquisition in about 17 months.\n  - Goodfire: seed in 2024, then a $1.25B Series B in 2026 ([src](https://www.goodfire.com/blog/our-series-b)).\n  - Fintool: acquired by Microsoft in 2026 ([src](https://menlovc.com/portfolio/fintool/)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Stripe to Acquire OpenRouter: the full deal arc and their routing thesis ([link](https://menlovc.com/perspective/stripe-to-acquire-openrouter-why-everyone-is-obsessed-with-model-routing/)).\n2. Leading Goodfire's $50M Series A: their interpretability conviction and first board seat ([link](https://menlovc.com/perspective/leading-goodfires-50m-series-a-to-interpret-how-ai-models-think/)).\n3. Investing in Pangram: an X-sourced seed and their values filter ([link](https://menlovc.com/perspective/investing-in-pangram-to-stop-ai-slop-on-the-internet/)).\n4. 2026: The State of Enterprise AI: the market-share data lens ([link](https://menlovc.com/perspective/2026-the-state-of-enterprise-ai/)).\n5. Stack Overflow interview: the Anthology rationale ([link](https://stackoverflow.blog/2024/09/27/deedy-das-meta-google-menlo-ventures-ai-anthropic/)).\n\n## Social engagement\n- X is the main surface. They post Claude-built demos, such as a Splitwise clone and an AI-image detector ([src](https://x.com/deedydas/status/1828473931063533757), [src](https://x.com/deedydas/status/1822665923775611374)).\n- Other topics: immigration (EB-1A guide), India, and search ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/deedy-das/)).\n- Their press includes MIT Technology Review on GPT-4o Chinese tokens ([src](https://www.technologyreview.com/2024/05/17/1092649/gpt-4o-chinese-token-polluted/)).\n- Reply and amplify graph: nothing found, because X wasn't fetchable.\n\n## Portfolio\nDeals where Menlo names Das as partner of record ([team page](https://menlovc.com/team/deedy-das/)):\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Goodfire | Seed (Anthology) | 2024 | No (alongside Lightspeed) | Lightspeed; Menlo co-signer Tim Tully | [Menlo card](https://menlovc.com/portfolio/goodfire/), [memo](https://menlovc.com/perspective/leading-goodfires-50m-series-a-to-interpret-how-ai-models-think/) |\n| Goodfire | Series A, $50M | 2025-04-17 | Yes (Menlo); Das board | Lightspeed, Anthropic, B Capital, Work-Bench, Wing, South Park Commons | [src](https://www.goodfire.com/blog/announcing-our-50m-series-a) |\n| Goodfire | Series B, $150M at $1.25B | 2026-02-05 | No (B Capital) | Menlo, Lightspeed, DFJ Growth, Salesforce Ventures, Juniper, Eric Schmidt | [src](https://www.goodfire.com/blog/our-series-b) |\n| Prime Intellect | $15M raise (Menlo card says \"Series A, 2025\") | 2025-02-28 | No (Founders Fund) | Karpathy, Clem Delangue, Dylan Patel, Tri Dao, Emad Mostaque | [src](https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/fundraise), [card](https://menlovc.com/portfolio/prime-intellect/) |\n| OpenRouter | Seed (Anthology) | 2025-03 | Not stated; lead unnamed | Menlo co-signer Matt Murphy | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/stripe-to-acquire-openrouter-why-everyone-is-obsessed-with-model-routing/) |\n| OpenRouter | Series A ($40M combined announcement) | 2025-05 | Yes (Menlo); Murphy board, Das observer | not confirmed (GlobeNewswire returned 403) | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/stripe-to-acquire-openrouter-why-everyone-is-obsessed-with-model-routing/) |\n| OpenRouter | Series B, $113M at $1.3B | ~2026-02 | No (CapitalG) | Menlo participated | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/openrouter-now-processes-more-than-a-quadrillion-tokens-a-year/) |\n| Fintool | Seed | 2025 | Not stated | Acquired by Microsoft in 2026 | [src](https://menlovc.com/portfolio/fintool/) |\n| Modal | Series C | 2026 (about May) | Not stated | Menlo co-signer Matt Murphy; existing investors Redpoint, Lux, Amplify, GC, BCV | [card](https://menlovc.com/portfolio/modal/), [D post](https://modal.com/blog/1-billion-annualized-run-rate-series-d) |\n| Modal | Series D, $750M at $15.75B | 2026-10-14 | No (Accel) | Kleiner Perkins plus existing investors including Menlo | [src](https://modal.com/blog/1-billion-annualized-run-rate-series-d) |\n| Pangram | Seed, $9M (Anthology) | 2026-07-29 | Yes | Haystack, ScOp, Script Capital, Cadenza | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/29/as-ai-content-floods-the-internet-pangram-raises-9m-to-detect-it/) |\n| Greptile | Series B, $50M at $750M | 2026-10-19 | Yes; Das board | Jump Trading, Wing, Operator Collective, Benchmark (led the A), YC, Browder, Initialized | [src](https://www.greptile.com/blog/series-b) |\n| Angel: Perplexity, Cartesia, Fulcrum | n/a | undated | n/a | | [src](https://debarghyadas.com) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **OpenRouter.**\n  - Menlo reached out cold. The memo shows \"The first outbound email from Menlo to OpenRouter.\"\n  - It was one of the first companies Das met in 2024.\n  - Seed through Anthology in March 2025, then Menlo led the A in May 2025, with Das as board observer.\n  - Stripe acquisition announced 2026-08-19 ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/stripe-to-acquire-openrouter-why-everyone-is-obsessed-with-model-routing/)).\n- **Pangram.**\n  - Started as an X conversation with co-founder Max Spero after Das saw Pangram flagging \"slop posters\".\n  - Das then visited the Brooklyn office.\n  - They were convinced by the founders' refusal to build a humanizer, and Menlo led the seed through Anthology ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/investing-in-pangram-to-stop-ai-slop-on-the-internet/)).\n- **Goodfire.**\n  - Seed through Anthology alongside Lightspeed, as one of Anthology's first deals.\n  - Menlo led the $50M A in under a year from founding. Anthropic made its first direct investment in another company in that round. It was Das's first board seat ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/leading-goodfires-50m-series-a-to-interpret-how-ai-models-think/)).\n  - Intro path: nothing found.\n- **Greptile.** Benchmark led the $25M A on 2025-09-23, and Menlo led the B about 13 months later ([src](https://www.greptile.com/blog/series-b)). So Menlo came in late; intro path not found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Menlo Ventures | Principal, then Partner | 2024 | [src](https://menlovc.com/team/deedy-das/) |\n| Anthology Fund (Menlo x Anthropic) | One of six partners: Tully, Murphy, Redfern, Amy Wu Martin, Das, Ganesan | 2024 | [src](https://menlovc.com/anthology-fund/) |\n| Matt Murphy | Co-partner on OpenRouter and Modal; co-author | 2025 | [src](https://menlovc.com/portfolio/openrouter/) |\n| Tim Tully | Co-partner on Goodfire; co-author of the 2026 report | 2024 | [src](https://menlovc.com/portfolio/goodfire/) |\n| Arvind Jain (Glean) | Founding-team colleague | 2019 | [src](https://debarghyadas.com) |\n| Max Spero (Pangram) | Founder relationship formed on X | ~2026 | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/investing-in-pangram-to-stop-ai-slop-on-the-internet/) |\n| Lightspeed | Seed co-investor on Goodfire | 2024 | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/leading-goodfires-50m-series-a-to-interpret-how-ai-models-think/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact promotion dates. The brief's 2024-03-14 and 2025-09-15 need a LinkedIn or Menlo post.\n- Did Menlo join Prime Intellect's 2026 $130M A? Who at Menlo or Prime Intellect drove the 2025 entry?\n- Who led OpenRouter's seed, and what was the A amount on its own?\n- Goodfire intro path. Did it come through Anthropic?\n- Transcripts to read: Nikhil Kamath, TWiST and Latent Space II, for check-size and pass stories.\n\n## Sources\n- https://menlovc.com/team/deedy-das/\n- https://menlovc.com/anthology-fund/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/stripe-to-acquire-openrouter-why-everyone-is-obsessed-with-model-routing/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/openrouter-now-processes-more-than-a-quadrillion-tokens-a-year/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/investing-in-pangram-to-stop-ai-slop-on-the-internet/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/leading-goodfires-50m-series-a-to-interpret-how-ai-models-think/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/q3-2025-anthology-fund-update/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/2026-the-state-of-enterprise-ai/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlo-turns-50-and-announces-3b-in-fresh-capital-to-go-all-in-on-ai/\n- https://menlovc.com/portfolio/goodfire/, /openrouter/, /modal/, /prime-intellect/, /pangram/, /greptile/, /fintool/, /inception/, /ndea/\n- https://stackoverflow.blog/2024/09/27/deedy-das-meta-google-menlo-ventures-ai-anthropic/\n- https://debarghyadas.com\n- https://www.goodfire.com/blog/announcing-our-50m-series-a ; https://www.goodfire.com/blog/our-series-b\n- https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/fundraise ; https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/series-a\n- https://modal.com/blog/1-billion-annualized-run-rate-series-d\n- https://www.greptile.com/blog/series-b\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/29/as-ai-content-floods-the-internet-pangram-raises-9m-to-detect-it/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/06/inception-raises-50-million-to-build-diffusion-models-for-code-and-text/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/06/24/wispr-flow-raises-30m-from-menlo-ventures-for-its-ai-powered-dictation-app/\n", "person:delian-asparouhov": "\n# Delian Asparouhov\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. The web search budget for this run was used up before this profile started, so it was built from direct fetches only: the Founders Fund team page and API, the Uncapped #53 transcript, Via Satellite, EnduroSat's own releases, TechCrunch, CNBC and PR Newswire. Several gaps are listed under Open questions.*\n\n## One-line take\nThe Founders Fund partner most often named on early hard-tech deals, and the firm's clearest voice on seed prices. He also runs Varda as President, and he sources from Varda's own engineering problems. That is how he came to lead a 2026 seed in AI tooling for hardware engineers [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n\n## Crux\n**Why he wins.** He is an operator-investor. Varda was incubated inside Founders Fund: he and Trae Stephens recruited ex-SpaceX engineer Will Bruey to run it [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/08/space-manufacturing-startup-varda-incubated-at-founders-fund-emerges-with-9-million-in-funding/). His day job gives him live problems to invest against, and he says his investing \"solves a problem at work\" [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n\n**How he decides.** He maps a whole category before he commits. In AI tooling for hardware engineering he met 15 companies and backed the 16th [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n\n**What constrains him.** Price and process. He holds a strict seed-price line: 50-60% of his deals are sub-$30M post, and he aims to \"get 10%.\" Any check above a meaningful size still needs Peter Thiel's approval [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n\n**What he would never do.** Pay $75-100M post for a spin-out seed: \"That's just not the business that I'm in\" [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He holds a hard price line on seeds.** \"probably 50-60% of the investments that I do are these sub-$30 million post rounds.\" He cites a $6M-post seed from about two months before the 2026-06-30 recording [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n- **He refuses spin-out pricing.** Spin-out founders come to him with $75-100M-post offers. His answer: \"That's just not the business that I'm in\" [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n- **He targets about 10% ownership and a coaching role.** \"I like doing the early stage, get 10%\" [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n- **His AI seed came from Varda's own pain.** He wants tools for Varda's \"firmware engineers, thermal modeling, et cetera,\" and to cut the rote work done by junior mechanical engineers. He compares the shift to what Cognition and Cursor did for software engineers. After 15 misses: \"I met the 16th one and I was like, 'Great, this is the one.'\" FF led the seed. The company is not named [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n- **Investing is a side activity that feeds his day job.** He says it \"solves a problem at work.\" Note: the word \"exhaust\" is Ev Randle's paraphrase in a question, not Asparouhov's. `firms/founders-fund.md` currently attributes it to him [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n- **He says Peter still signs off on big checks.** \"every check above a meaningful size still requires Peter's approval.\" He describes it as the last step after one partner has built conviction [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n- **He sees the growth fund as mostly follow-on.** \"80% is doubling down.\" He gives Crusoe (\"originally started off as a Bitcoin miner\") as a favorite example. He says OpenAI, Anthropic, SpaceX and Anduril are \"all now largely priced in\" [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n- **He is bullish on Anthropic's revenue.** He calls it \"the first company since Google to actually show an even steeper curve\" [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n- **He polices China-linked deals in public.** On Benchmark's Manus round he posted \"wow, actions have consequences?\" on X, 2025-05-09 [src](https://twitter.com/zebulgar/status/1920908901597298900), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/05/09/the-us-is-reviewing-benchmarks-investment-into-chinese-ai-startup-manus/). On selling to China: \"I don't think we should be helping them\" [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is the clearest named early-stage decision-maker at Founders Fund, and he is building an AI-for-hardware-engineering seed book (an AI-native automation adjacency) from first-hand need [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan). He is also close to AI compute through Crusoe, which he cites as a doubling-down example. No source says he led the Crusoe deal.\n\nStructurally, though, he is never a peer in the deals I write. He prices seeds sub-$30M post, targets about 10%, and turns down $75-100M-post spin-outs. So he would not sit beside a ~$20M check in a $600M new-lab seed. Use him as:\n- a dealflow node in hard-tech and AI-for-engineering at seed;\n- a possible co-investor one round earlier than me;\n- a read on what Peter Thiel will approve.\n\nDeals that matter to me:\n- the unnamed AI-for-hardware-engineering seed (2026);\n- EnduroSat, as a signal of conviction in a non-US hard-tech company.\n\n## Background\n- Before FF: nothing confirmed this run. Prior roles, including any Khosla Ventures stint and any earlier company, need a source.\n- **2019-07-25:** Founders Fund team profile posted [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/delian-asparouhov/).\n- **2020-12-08:** Varda emerges from stealth with $9M. It was incubated at FF, and Asparouhov and Trae Stephens recruited CEO Will Bruey [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/08/space-manufacturing-startup-varda-incubated-at-founders-fund-emerges-with-9-million-in-funding/).\n- **2021-07-29:** CNBC calls him a \"Founders Fund principal\" and Varda co-founder [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2021/07/29/varda-space-raises-over-50-million-to-build-space-factories.html).\n- **By 2025-05-27:** Partner at FF and President of Varda [src](https://www.satellitetoday.com/manufacturing/2025/05/27/bulgarian-satellite-manufacturer-endurosat-raises-49m-led-by-founders-fund/).\n- **Hill & Valley Forum:** his FF bio says he co-created it. It connects Washington and Silicon Valley on technology and national security. Year not sourced [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/delian-asparouhov/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://foundersfund.com/team/delian-asparouhov/ | FF team bio |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | not searched (search budget exhausted) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/zebulgar | handle confirmed via the TechCrunch embed, 2025-05-09 [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/05/09/the-us-is-reviewing-benchmarks-investment-into-chinese-ai-startup-manus/). Profile fetch blocked, so follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | not confirmed | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-06-30 — Uncapped with Jack Altman, Ep. 53 — \"Founders Fund on Truth-Seeking, Taiwan, and Whether You Can Still Beat the S&P\" (with Trae Stephens; Ev Randle asks questions) — [link](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan) — stood out: sub-$30M-post seeds, the 10% target, the AI-for-hardware seed (the 16th company he met), Peter's approval, and the growth fund being 80% doubling down.\n- Other appearances: nothing found this run (search budget exhausted).\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses.** He wants AI tools that do for hardware engineers (firmware, thermal, mechanical) what Cognition and Cursor did for software engineers. He also builds space manufacturing through Varda [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n- **Founder-first.** On Monaco, founded by ex-FF partner Sam Blond: \"Monaco is Sam. Sam is Monaco\" [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n- **Contrarian views.** Seed discipline in a market of mega-seeds. He says the late-stage AI and defense names are \"largely priced in.\" He takes hawkish stances on China and Taiwan [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n- **Calls so far.** EnduroSat raised two much larger rounds within 16 months of FF's lead (see Deal stories). Varda went from a $9M seed in 2020 to $329M raised by 2025-07 [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/07/10/space-startup-varda-medicine-orbit.html). No wrong calls found.\n\n## Read these first\n- Uncapped #53 transcript: his pricing, sourcing and approval process in his own words [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n- TechCrunch, 2020-12-08: how Varda was incubated inside FF [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/08/space-manufacturing-startup-varda-incubated-at-founders-fund-emerges-with-9-million-in-funding/).\n- Via Satellite, 2025-05-27: the EnduroSat lead and why he backed it [src](https://www.satellitetoday.com/manufacturing/2025/05/27/bulgarian-satellite-manufacturer-endurosat-raises-49m-led-by-founders-fund/).\n- His Manus post on X: his public stance on investing in China [src](https://twitter.com/zebulgar/status/1920908901597298900).\n\n## Social engagement\nOnly one post was verified: the 2025-05-09 jab at Benchmark's Manus deal. Topics, reply patterns and amplifiers were not mapped this run.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| EnduroSat | EUR 43M (~$49M) | 2025-05-27 | Yes (FF), Asparouhov named and quoted | CEECAT Capital, Morphosis Capital, returning investors | [src](https://www.satellitetoday.com/manufacturing/2025/05/27/bulgarian-satellite-manufacturer-endurosat-raises-49m-led-by-founders-fund/) |\n| Unnamed AI-for-hardware-engineering startup | Seed | ~2026 (said 2026-06-30) | Yes, \"we just led the seed round on\" | not given | [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan) |\n| Unnamed seed | Seed, $6M post | ~2026-04 (about two months before recording) | Not stated | not given | [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan) |\n| Eight Sleep | not stated | not stated | Not stated; co-invested with Trae Stephens after Keith Rabois said \"Just try it\" | not given | [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan) |\n| Varda Space Industries | Seed $9M | 2020-12-08 | FF and Lux co-led (he is the co-founder, so this is not his investment) | Lux, Fifty Years, Also Capital, Naval Ravikant, others | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/08/space-manufacturing-startup-varda-incubated-at-founders-fund-emerges-with-9-million-in-funding/) |\n\n**Referenced but not attributed to him:** Crusoe, Ramp, Nominal, Hadrian, Monaco, Anduril [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan). FF co-led Hadrian's $260M Series C (2025-07-17) with Lux, but CNBC names no FF partner [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/07/17/hadrian-funding-round-thiel-founders-fund.html).\n\n## Deal stories\n**EnduroSat (the standout).** EnduroSat is a satellite maker in Sofia, Bulgaria, founded and run by Raycho Raychev. Its round history:\n- **2023-05-10:** a $10M Series A led by CEECAT Capital, a Central and Eastern Europe fund, with Freigeist Capital among returning investors [src](https://www.endurosat.com/news/endurosat-closes-series-a-investment/).\n- **2025-05-27:** Founders Fund led a EUR 43M (~$49M) round, with CEECAT, Morphosis Capital and returning investors. Asparouhov, named as FF partner and Varda president, explained the bet on product grounds. He said the Gen3 satellites move the company into \"a size class that will allow for almost any space opportunity\" and pointed to its flight heritage. The money went to scaling the 200-500 kg Gen3 platform and a 17,500 m² facility in Sofia [src](https://www.satellitetoday.com/manufacturing/2025/05/27/bulgarian-satellite-manufacturer-endurosat-raises-49m-led-by-founders-fund/).\n- **How FF found it:** no source fetched this run says who made the introduction or when first contact happened.\n\n**What happened next is the timing story:**\n- **2025-10-30, about five months later:** EnduroSat raised $104M. The release names Riot Ventures, GV, Lux Capital, the EIC Fund and Shrug Capital. Founders Fund is not named on that release [src](https://www.endurosat.com/news/endurosat-secures-104-million-to-scale-espa-class-satellite-production/).\n- **2026-09-16:** EnduroSat raised $205M, co-led by Riot Ventures and Atreides Management (Gavin Baker). FF is listed only as a participant, alongside GV, Lux, the EIC, House Capital, Omnes and others [src](https://www.endurosat.com/news/endurosat-raises-205m-to-scale-next-generation-space-fleets/).\n\nSo in about 16 months, a company FF led at ~$49M raised roughly $309M more. FF went from lead, to not named, to participant. EnduroSat reports 103 satellites launched and more than 200 delivered. No valuation is disclosed for any round [src](https://www.endurosat.com/news/endurosat-raises-205m-to-scale-next-generation-space-fleets/).\n\n**Unnamed AI-for-hardware-engineering seed (2026).** This deal came from his own job. He was frustrated that Varda's firmware, thermal and junior mechanical engineers do rote work that AI should remove. He met about 15 companies in the category, was disappointed by all of them, and then met the 16th. He calls its founder, product and idea the best he had seen. FF led the seed [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan).\n\n**Varda (founder, not an investment).** Asparouhov and Trae Stephens recruited Will Bruey out of SpaceX and incubated the company inside FF. The rounds:\n- **2020-12-08:** FF and Lux co-led a $9M seed [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/08/space-manufacturing-startup-varda-incubated-at-founders-fund-emerges-with-9-million-in-funding/).\n- **2021-07-29:** Khosla Ventures and Caffeinated Capital led a $42M Series A [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/07/29/varda-space-industries-closes-42m-series-a-for-off-planet-manufacturing/).\n- **2024-04-05:** Caffeinated Capital led a $90M Series B [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/varda-announces-90-million-series-b-funding-to-build-factories-in-space-302108949.html).\n- **2025-07-10:** Natural Capital and Shrug Capital led a $187M Series C, bringing the total to $329M [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/07/10/space-startup-varda-medicine-orbit.html).\n\nShrug Capital later appears in EnduroSat's 2025-10 round as well. That is a shared co-investor between his two space companies [src](https://www.endurosat.com/news/endurosat-secures-104-million-to-scale-espa-class-satellite-production/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Founders Fund | Partner (principal as of 2021-07) | 2019 (profile date) | [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/delian-asparouhov/), [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2021/07/29/varda-space-raises-over-50-million-to-build-space-factories.html) |\n| Varda Space Industries | Co-founder, President | 2020 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/08/space-manufacturing-startup-varda-incubated-at-founders-fund-emerges-with-9-million-in-funding/) |\n| Will Bruey | Varda co-founder and CEO; recruited by Asparouhov and Stephens | 2020 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/08/space-manufacturing-startup-varda-incubated-at-founders-fund-emerges-with-9-million-in-funding/) |\n| Trae Stephens | FF colleague; co-incubated Varda; co-invested in Eight Sleep | 2020 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/08/space-manufacturing-startup-varda-incubated-at-founders-fund-emerges-with-9-million-in-funding/), [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan) |\n| Peter Thiel | Approves his larger checks | | [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan) |\n| Raycho Raychev (EnduroSat) | Founder he backed | 2025-05 | [src](https://www.satellitetoday.com/manufacturing/2025/05/27/bulgarian-satellite-manufacturer-endurosat-raises-49m-led-by-founders-fund/) |\n| Lux Capital | Varda seed co-lead; later EnduroSat investor | 2020-12 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/08/space-manufacturing-startup-varda-incubated-at-founders-fund-emerges-with-9-million-in-funding/) |\n| Ev Randle; Jack Altman | Interviewed him on Uncapped #53 | 2026-06 | [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan) |\n| Keith Rabois | Prompted the Eight Sleep investment | | [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan) |\n| Sam Blond (Monaco) | Ex-FF partner; Asparouhov praises him as a founder | | [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan) |\n| Benchmark | Public critic of its Manus deal | 2025-05 | [src](https://twitter.com/zebulgar/status/1920908901597298900) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Which company is the AI-for-hardware-engineering seed FF led in 2026?** It is high lens-fit. Check his X posts and the FF portfolio around 2026-04 to 2026-06.\n- **What was the $6M-post seed (~2026-04)?**\n- **How did FF find EnduroSat?** Who made the intro, did he know Raychev through Bulgaria, and when was first contact?\n- **Did FF put money into EnduroSat's 2025-10 $104M round?** The release does not name FF.\n- **Which FF deals does he personally lead beyond EnduroSat?** Nominal and Hadrian are the obvious ones to check.\n- **When exactly was he promoted** from principal to partner?\n- **Earlier career** (pre-2019) and the Hill & Valley Forum founding year: unsourced this run.\n- **Did his public China stance cost FF any deals,** or shape its AI policy?\n\n## Sources\n- https://foundersfund.com/team/delian-asparouhov/\n- https://foundersfund.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/07/Delian.jpg (headshot, HTTP 200 on 2026-11-10)\n- https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan\n- https://www.satellitetoday.com/manufacturing/2025/05/27/bulgarian-satellite-manufacturer-endurosat-raises-49m-led-by-founders-fund/\n- https://www.endurosat.com/news/endurosat-closes-series-a-investment/\n- https://www.endurosat.com/news/endurosat-secures-104-million-to-scale-espa-class-satellite-production/\n- https://www.endurosat.com/news/endurosat-raises-205m-to-scale-next-generation-space-fleets/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/08/space-manufacturing-startup-varda-incubated-at-founders-fund-emerges-with-9-million-in-funding/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2021/07/29/varda-space-industries-closes-42m-series-a-for-off-planet-manufacturing/\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2021/07/29/varda-space-raises-over-50-million-to-build-space-factories.html\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/varda-announces-90-million-series-b-funding-to-build-factories-in-space-302108949.html\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2025/07/10/space-startup-varda-medicine-orbit.html\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2025/07/17/hadrian-funding-round-thiel-founders-fund.html\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/05/09/the-us-is-reviewing-benchmarks-investment-into-chinese-ai-startup-manus/\n- https://twitter.com/zebulgar/status/1920908901597298900\n", "person:derek-xiao": "\n# Derek Xiao\n\n## One-line take\nHe is Menlo's in-house AI researcher and the junior name on its inference, database and coding-agent deals. He co-signed the Anthropic Series C memo and the Gimlet Labs Series A, which Menlo led. But Menlo's own portfolio pages name a senior partner of record on every deal he is attached to, never him.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He writes the market maps, and the deals follow the maps. Menlo credits him with its \"widely-cited\" State of Enterprise AI reports ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/)). Those reports feed Menlo's AI-stack thesis: model, routing, compute, database. The thesis is then executed through Tim Tully and Matt Murphy. The clearest proof is Neon: Tully writes that Derek surfaced it in an office-kitchen conversation, and that conversation led to Menlo's Series B ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/neon-joins-databricks-to-build-the-future-of-ai-infrastructure-together/)).\n- **What constrains him.** He is not the partner of record anywhere. Each portfolio company on his Menlo team page carries a \"Partners\" field, and his name is never in it ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/)):\n  - Anthropic: Matt Murphy\n  - Eve, Neon, Unstructured, Gimlet: Tim Tully\n  - Fireworks: Tully and Murphy\n  - Factory: Murphy\n  - Mercor: Joff Redfern\n  - Higgsfield: Amy Wu Martin\n  \n  This includes all four companies his own bio says he \"partners with\" (Anthropic, Eve, Neon, Unstructured). It is the same CMS-versus-prose split this repo found for Abby Meyers and Amanda Huang at BCV. In practice he is a sourcer, diligence lead and memo author. He does not hold the term-sheet pen.\n- **What he would not do (by his own writing).** He would not back an AI app on the strength of a proprietary model or fine-tune. He calls those failed sources of differentiation at the app layer, and says the \"SaaS playbook\" of moats first is wrong for AI ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/there-are-no-ai-markets-only-proto-markets-why-the-saas-playbook-fails-in-ai/), 2026-01-15).\n- **A note on the task framing.** The \"named AI deals\" are byline and profile attributions, not deal ownership. Read them as \"Derek did the work under X's name.\"\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He sourced Neon.** Tim Tully: \"our teammate Derek mentioned Neon during a casual conversation\" in the Menlo Park office kitchen. Tully tested the product that night, met Nikita within days, and Menlo did the Series B in 2023. Databricks acquired Neon in 2025 ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/neon-joins-databricks-to-build-the-future-of-ai-infrastructure-together/), 2025-05-14). This is the only primary account found of him originating a deal, and the outcome was a strategic exit.\n- **Partner of record on none of his deals** ([team page CMS](https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/)). His bio says \"He partners with companies from seed through inflection\", but every company card lists a different Menlo partner (see Crux).\n- **Gimlet is his cleanest first-lead deal.**\n  - Tully and Xiao are the only two bylines on Menlo's post, which says \"Menlo Ventures is leading the company’s Series A\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-gimlet-the-multi-silicon-inference-cloud/), 2026-03-23).\n  - Gimlet's post puts the round at $80M, joined by Eclipse, Factory, Prosperity7 and Triatomic ([src](https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-a)).\n  - a16z led a $300M Series B \"just over 5 months\" later, and Menlo followed on ([src](https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-b), 2026-09-04).\n  - The thesis is multi-silicon inference: frontier labs \"increasingly pairing SRAM-centric accelerators with traditional GPUs to push inference speed\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-gimlet-the-multi-silicon-inference-cloud/)). Note that the post's first-person founder relationship (\"since my days at Splunk\") is Tully's, not his.\n- **Thesis came before the deal on Gimlet.** The memo opens: \"we’ve had a thesis searching for where agents will run\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-gimlet-the-multi-silicon-inference-cloud/)). His co-authored \"AI agents\" architecture piece ran 18 months earlier ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/ai-agents-a-new-architecture-for-enterprise-automation/), 2024-09-26).\n- **Factory: Menlo is missing from Factory's own announcement.**\n  - Factory's post (2026-09-15, $200M at $5B) names nine investors: Blackstone, Khosla, Sequoia, Insight, Evantic, Sound, NEA, Mantis, Clearlake. Menlo is not among them ([src](https://factory.com/news/5-billion-valuation)).\n  - Menlo's post, three weeks later, says only that it is \"joining the company’s latest financing\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-factory-the-system-that-builds-software/), 2026-10-05).\n  - So Menlo was a late or extension entrant, not a lead. The Menlo portfolio page labels the round Series D.\n- **Fireworks was a late participation, not a lead.** Fireworks' own release lists Menlo among participants in the $1.505B Series D at $17.5B ([src](https://fireworks.ai/news/fireworks-raises-1-5-billion-series-d), 2026-07-16). Benchmark led the 2024 A and Sequoia the 2024 B (repo rows).\n- **Anthology Fund mechanics, which he co-signed.** In the Q2 2025 update, co-bylined with Das, Murphy and Redfern, the $100M Menlo-Anthropic Anthology Fund says that after a small first check, \"we usually aim to lead the next round\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/q2-2025-update-from-the-anthology-fund/), 2025-08-13). Graphite (2025) was an Anthology deal he co-bylined with Rama Sekhar, and Cursor later acquired Graphite ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-graphite-code-review-for-the-ai-age/), [src](https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/)).\n- **Conflict worth noting (my observation).** The market-share reports he co-authors crown a Menlo portfolio company. The mid-2025 update says \"Anthropic is now the leading enterprise LLM provider with 32% market share\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/2025-mid-year-llm-market-update/), 2025-07-31). The data may be right, but Menlo has led an Anthropic round (D) and sits in its syndicate.\n- **Higgsfield founder read.** With Amy Wu Martin: \"we’ve found Alex to be an N-of-1 founder\" (Alex Mashrabov, ex-Head of Generative AI at Snap; seed 2024) ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-higgsfield-building-world-models-with-video/), 2024-04-03).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is a principal at a fund that writes $10M+ and growth checks (Anthropic, Fireworks), and his beat is the AI stack: frontier lab, inference, multi-silicon compute, coding agents. This is exactly the segment you flagged.\n\nThe deals that matter to you:\n- **Gimlet Labs.** Menlo led the $80M A, then a16z led the B about 5 months later. It is an AI-chips-adjacent inference cloud, and Eclipse is in the syndicate.\n- **Anthropic.** Series C participant, 2023.\n- **Fireworks.** Series D, late.\n- **Factory.** 2026, late.\n- **Neon.** He sourced it.\n\nCaveats:\n- He has no independent lead authority on record.\n- Menlo arrives at these companies mid-to-late (C/D) except Gimlet A and the Eve and Higgsfield seeds.\n- His essays argue against app-layer proprietary models. That is not against labs: he co-signed the Anthropic memo. But he is sceptical of \"model as moat\" for apps.\n\nHe is a good warm channel into Menlo's Anthology/Anthropic pipeline and a likely future partner, but not a co-lead today.\n\n## Background\n- Harvard: summa cum laude, Phi Beta Kappa, President of The Harvard Crimson ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/)). Class year not found.\n- Journalist at Forbes, where he began his career ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/)). Dates not found.\n- Bain & Company, advising technology investors on opportunities \"ranging from machine learning infrastructure to orbital launch\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/)). Dates not found.\n- Menlo Ventures:\n  - Earliest Menlo post tagged to him: 2022-03-21, co-bylined with Steve Sloane ([src](https://menlovc.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts?tags=180&per_page=100&_fields=date,link,title)).\n  - His headshot upload path is dated 2022/08 ([src](https://menlovc.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/derek_xiao-main-HEADSHOT.jpeg)).\n  - So he joined by early 2022, title at join not found. He is Principal now; the promotion date was not found. LinkedIn was blocked (HTTP 999), and web search was unavailable this run.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/ | Team page; 37 tagged Menlo posts 2022-03 to 2026-11 |\n| Substack / blog | https://menlovc.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts?tags=180&per_page=100&_fields=date,link,title | Menlo Perspective posts tagged derek-xiao (WordPress tag 180) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/derekgxiao | Linked from team page; not fetched |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/derekgxiao/ | Blocked (HTTP 999) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was unavailable this run, and no podcast was linked from Menlo pages. Worth a follow-up search, especially for State of Enterprise AI launch interviews.\n\n## How they think\n- **Proto-markets** ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/there-are-no-ai-markets-only-proto-markets-why-the-saas-playbook-fails-in-ai/), 2026-01-15, solo byline).\n  - \"AI markets feel chaotic because they are not markets at all.\"\n  - Winners show \"product plasticity and learning velocity.\" He uses Claude Code's \"hackable by design\" evolution as the case study.\n  - \"In AI, markets emerge before moats do.\"\n- **Systems of work** ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/ais-ui-problem-is-actually-a-new-era-of-software/), 2025-09-11, solo byline). Value has moved from UI and data to the invisible orchestration layer. He contrasts Cursor, Lovable and Claude Code: \"Same models. Same interfaces. Completely different products.\"\n- **Contrarian view.** At the app layer, proprietary foundation models, domain fine-tuning and search architectures like MCTS have \"failed to become durable sources of differentiation\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/there-are-no-ai-markets-only-proto-markets-why-the-saas-playbook-fails-in-ai/)).\n- **The token path** (team view he co-signs). AI infrastructure is reorganising along the \"token path\": compute, data and orchestration. The Fireworks memo maps Menlo's portfolio onto it ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-fireworks-the-runtime-for-specialized-intelligence/), 2026-07-16).\n- **Calls that held up.**\n  - Anthropic memo, 2023-05: called it \"one of the leading language model providers at the foundation layer.\"\n  - Neon, sourced in 2023: acquired by Databricks in 2025.\n  - Graphite, 2025: acquired by Cursor.\n  - Gimlet A: 5-month markup round.\n- **Pre-AI work.** His 2022 posts with Steve Sloane were valuation and macro analysis: early-stage private valuations lag public drops \"until two or three quarters after a public market dip\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/public-cloud-stocks-have-dropped-50-will-private-valuations-follow/)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [There Are No AI Markets, Only Proto-Markets](https://menlovc.com/perspective/there-are-no-ai-markets-only-proto-markets-why-the-saas-playbook-fails-in-ai/) (2026-01-15, solo). His core framework for judging AI apps.\n2. [AI's UI Problem Is Actually a New Era of Software](https://menlovc.com/perspective/ais-ui-problem-is-actually-a-new-era-of-software/) (2025-09-11, solo). The systems-of-work thesis.\n3. [Menlo's Investment in Gimlet](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-gimlet-the-multi-silicon-inference-cloud/) (2026-03-23, with Tully). Multi-silicon inference, his most lens-relevant memo.\n4. [2026: The State of Enterprise AI](https://menlovc.com/perspective/2026-the-state-of-enterprise-ai/) (2026-11-03, with Tully, Redfern, Das). Survey of nearly 400 enterprise buyers. Finds $177B deployment spend and that agents have \"overtaken humans as the dominant consumers of AI.\"\n5. [Neon Joins Databricks](https://menlovc.com/perspective/neon-joins-databricks-to-build-the-future-of-ai-infrastructure-together/) (2025-05-14, with Tully). The deal-origin story.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot fetched: X was not accessible this run. The team page says he follows \"commentary on the future of news\" on X ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/)). Within Menlo his most frequent co-bylines are:\n- Tim Tully (AI infra)\n- Matt Murphy (Anthropic, Typeface, True Anomaly, Fireworks, Factory)\n- Steve Sloane (2022 SaaS and supply chain)\n- JP Sanday and Greg Yap (health)\n- Joff Redfern and Deedy Das (reports)\n- Sabrina Lu and Sam Borja (2026 deals)\n\n## Portfolio\n`data/rows/derek-xiao-deals.csv` keeps only the AI deals; Obsidian, Cartwheel, True Anomaly, Grow Therapy and Nourish appear here only. Menlo partner of record is from the team-page CMS ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/)). Derek is never listed as partner.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Obsidian Security | Series C | 2022-04-14 | unknown | Menlo co-byline Venky Ganesan | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/our-investment-in-obsidian-security-securing-the-most-critical-business-applications-for-the-enterprise/) |\n| Eve (legal AI) | Seed | 2022 | unknown | Menlo partner Tim Tully | [src](https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/) |\n| Typeface | $65M round (Series A per tag) | 2023-02-27 | no evidence of lead | Lightspeed, GV, M12; Menlo co-byline Matt Murphy | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/our-investment-in-typeface-generative-ai-at-enterprise-speed-and-scale/) |\n| Anthropic | Series C ($450M) | 2023-05-23 | no (Spark Capital led) | Google, Salesforce Ventures, Sound, Zoom Ventures; Menlo co-byline Murphy | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/our-investment-in-anthropic-the-foundation-layer-for-generative-ai/), [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-series-c) |\n| Neon | Series B | 2023 | unknown | Menlo partner Tully; Derek sourced; acquired by Databricks 2025 | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/neon-joins-databricks-to-build-the-future-of-ai-infrastructure-together/) |\n| Cartwheel | Series A | 2023-09-26 | unknown | Menlo partner Greg Yap (non-AI) | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/our-investment-in-cartwheel-improving-youth-mental-health/) |\n| Eleos Health | Series B | 2023-11-08 | unknown | Menlo co-byline Steve Sloane | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/empowering-behavioral-health-providers-with-ai-our-investment-in-eleos/) |\n| OfferFit | Series B | 2023-11-14 | unknown | Menlo co-byline JP Sanday; acquired by Braze | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-offerfit-building-the-ml-powered-automated-experimentation-platform-marketers-need/) |\n| True Anomaly | Series B ($100M) | 2023-12-12 | participant | Eclipse, Riot Ventures; co-byline Murphy (space defense, non-AI) | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-true-anomaly-a-defense-prime-for-space/) |\n| Higgsfield | Seed | 2024-04-03 | unknown | Menlo co-byline Amy Wu Martin | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-higgsfield-building-world-models-with-video/) |\n| Unstructured | Series B | 2024 | unknown | Menlo partner Tully | [src](https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/) |\n| Mercor | Series B ($100M at $2B) | 2025-02-20 (Menlo page says 2024) | no (Felicis led) | Benchmark, General Catalyst, DST; Menlo partner Joff Redfern | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/20/mercor-an-ai-recruiting-startup-founded-by-21-year-olds-raises-100m-at-2b-valuation/), [src](https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/) |\n| Graphite | Series B (Menlo + Anthology Fund) | 2025-03-18 | unknown | co-byline Rama Sekhar; acquired by Cursor 2026 | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-graphite-code-review-for-the-ai-age/) |\n| Grow Therapy | Series D | 2026-03-03 | unknown | Murphy, JP Sanday, Sabrina Lu (non-AI) | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/fixing-behavioral-healths-broken-infrastructure-our-investment-in-grow-therapy/) |\n| Gimlet Labs | Series A ($80M) | 2026-03-23 | **yes (Menlo led)** | Eclipse, Factory, Prosperity7, Triatomic; co-byline Tully | [src](https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-a), [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-gimlet-the-multi-silicon-inference-cloud/) |\n| Nourish | Series C | 2026-05-19 | unknown | Greg Yap, JP Sanday, Sabrina Lu | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/building-the-backbone-of-metabolic-health-our-investment-in-nourish/) |\n| Assort Health | Series C | 2026-06-24 | unknown | Yap, Sanday, Murphy, Sam Borja | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/keeping-the-front-door-of-healthcare-open-our-investment-in-assort-health/) |\n| Fireworks AI | Series D ($1.505B at $17.5B) | 2026-07-16 | no (participant) | Atreides, Index, TCV co-leads per release; co-bylines Tully, Murphy, Sabrina Lu | [src](https://fireworks.ai/news/fireworks-raises-1-5-billion-series-d), [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-fireworks-the-runtime-for-specialized-intelligence/) |\n| Gimlet Labs | Series B ($300M) | 2026-09-04 | no (a16z led) | Sapphire, Eclipse, Tiger, Arm, Samsung and others | [src](https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-b) |\n| Factory | \"Series D\" per Menlo; Menlo not in Factory's $200M list | 2026-10-05 | no | co-bylines Murphy, Sam Borja, Sabrina Lu | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-factory-the-system-that-builds-software/), [src](https://factory.com/news/5-billion-valuation) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Neon (2023, Series B).** Derek mentioned Neon to Tully in the office kitchen. Tully stayed up testing it, met founder Nikita within days, and Menlo did the Series B. Tully says Menlo had been hunting a \"Snowflake equivalent\" for OLTP. Databricks acquired Neon on 2025-05-14 ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/neon-joins-databricks-to-build-the-future-of-ai-infrastructure-together/)).\n- **Gimlet Labs (2026, Series A lead).** The deal grew out of a thesis \"searching for where agents will run\". The founder link is Tully's: Zain Asgar, whom Tully knew from Splunk and whose Pixie Labs sold to New Relic in 2020. Menlo led the $80M A, and a16z led a $300M B about 5 months later ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-gimlet-the-multi-silicon-inference-cloud/), [src](https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-b)).\n- **Anthropic (2023, Series C).** He co-bylined the memo with Matt Murphy. Spark led, and Menlo participated ([src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-series-c)). Repo `companies/anthropic.md` covers Menlo leading the subsequent D. Derek's role in D is not documented.\n- **Factory (2026).** There was customer-reference diligence: speed was \"the theme we heard most consistently\". Menlo's entry came after Factory's own 2026-09-15 announcement and does not appear in it ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-factory-the-system-that-builds-software/), [src](https://factory.com/news/5-billion-valuation)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Tim Tully (Menlo) | Main co-author on infra deals (Neon, Gimlet, Fireworks, reports); partner of record | 2024 | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/neon-joins-databricks-to-build-the-future-of-ai-infrastructure-together/) |\n| Matt Murphy (Menlo) | Co-author on Typeface, Anthropic, True Anomaly, Fireworks, Factory; partner of record | 2023 | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/our-investment-in-anthropic-the-foundation-layer-for-generative-ai/) |\n| Steve Sloane (Menlo) | Co-author on his earliest Menlo posts (SaaS valuations, supply chain) | 2022 | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/public-cloud-stocks-have-dropped-50-will-private-valuations-follow/) |\n| Amy Wu Martin (Menlo) | Higgsfield and Barnyard Games co-author | 2023 | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-higgsfield-building-world-models-with-video/) |\n| Joff Redfern, Deedy Das (Menlo) | Report and Anthology co-authors | 2024 | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/2025-mid-year-llm-market-update/) |\n| JP Sanday, Greg Yap (Menlo) | Health deal co-authors | 2022 | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/keeping-the-front-door-of-healthcare-open-our-investment-in-assort-health/) |\n| Rama Sekhar (Menlo) | Graphite co-author | 2025 | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-graphite-code-review-for-the-ai-age/) |\n| Sabrina Lu, Sam Borja (Menlo) | Junior co-authors on 2026 deals | 2026 | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-factory-the-system-that-builds-software/) |\n| Anthology Fund (Menlo x Anthropic) | Co-author of Q2 2025 update | 2025 | [src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/q2-2025-update-from-the-anthology-fund/) |\n| Bain & Company | Former employer | before 2022 | [src](https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/) |\n| Forbes | Former employer (journalist) | before Bain | [src](https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/) |\n| The Harvard Crimson | President | college | [src](https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Menlo join date, title at join, and principal promotion date. LinkedIn was blocked and web search unavailable. Fast promotion stays unknown.\n- Factory: which round and price did Menlo enter at, given its absence from Factory's 2026-09-15 investor list? Was it an extension or secondary?\n- Did he work on Menlo's Anthropic Series D lead, or on later rounds?\n- Higgsfield seed: did Menlo lead, and at what size?\n- Is there any podcast or X thread where he speaks for himself beyond the two solo essays?\n- Is he on any board or observer seat (Gimlet)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://menlovc.com/team/derek-xiao/\n- https://menlovc.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts?tags=180&per_page=100&_fields=date,link,title\n- https://menlovc.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/derek_xiao-main-HEADSHOT.jpeg\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/there-are-no-ai-markets-only-proto-markets-why-the-saas-playbook-fails-in-ai/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/ais-ui-problem-is-actually-a-new-era-of-software/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-gimlet-the-multi-silicon-inference-cloud/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-fireworks-the-runtime-for-specialized-intelligence/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-factory-the-system-that-builds-software/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/neon-joins-databricks-to-build-the-future-of-ai-infrastructure-together/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/our-investment-in-anthropic-the-foundation-layer-for-generative-ai/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/our-investment-in-typeface-generative-ai-at-enterprise-speed-and-scale/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-higgsfield-building-world-models-with-video/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-true-anomaly-a-defense-prime-for-space/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlos-investment-in-graphite-code-review-for-the-ai-age/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/q2-2025-update-from-the-anthology-fund/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/2025-mid-year-llm-market-update/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/2026-the-state-of-enterprise-ai/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/public-cloud-stocks-have-dropped-50-will-private-valuations-follow/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/ai-agents-a-new-architecture-for-enterprise-automation/\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-series-c\n- https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-a\n- https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-b\n- https://factory.com/news/5-billion-valuation\n- https://fireworks.ai/news/fireworks-raises-1-5-billion-series-d\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/20/mercor-an-ai-recruiting-startup-founded-by-21-year-olds-raises-100m-at-2b-valuation/\n", "person:don-wilson": "\n# Don Wilson\n\n_Profile written 2026-11-11 (hft-semis sweep). Resolves the earlier `needs-id` lead: the \"Donald R. Wilson Jr.\" on the Silicon Derivatives Inc. Form Ds, at DRW's 540 W. Madison, Suite 2500 address, is DRW's founder (same legal name as on DRW's own bio) [src](https://www.drw.com/about/don-wilson/), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml). Earlier rows for him are in `data/rows/hft-semis-people-*.csv`. This pass's rows are in `data/rows/don-wilson-*.csv`._\n\n## One-line take\nWilson is the founder-CEO of the Chicago trading firm DRW. He is building GPU compute into a tradable commodity: he set up the index company (Silicon Data) and the spot market (Compute Exchange), and brought in CME for futures. In frontier-AI deals he is a **builder and board member of DRW-incubated compute-market companies**, not a check-writer in other people's rounds.\n\n## Crux\n- **What makes him win:** he has designed derivatives contracts himself, and DRW will be a natural market maker in whatever he lists. He says compute \"will become the largest commodity in the world\" [src](https://www.silicondata.com/news-room/cme-group-and-silicon-data-partner-to-launch-first-compute-futures) (CME release, 2026-05-12). The companies he backs are the pieces such a market needs: benchmarks, a spot venue, and a futures listing.\n- **Whose money and who approves:** DRW's own balance sheet. DRW VC has \"no outside investors\" [src](https://www.drw.com/vc). For Silicon Data and Compute Exchange he is personally on the cap table or the founding team (director on both Silicon Derivatives Form Ds; Compute Exchange co-founder), so the approver is Wilson himself. That he signs off is inference. His board seat and founder role are sourced.\n- **Separate from DRW VC:** DRW's own Wilson bio lists the DRWVC portfolio as \"Crusoe, Talos and OpenFin\" and does not mention Silicon Data or Compute Exchange [src](https://www.drw.com/about/don-wilson/). The compute-market companies run through Wilson and Head of Special Investments Spencer Powers ([profile](spencer-powers.md)), not through DRW VC Partner Kimberly Trautmann (inference from the filings and bio).\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** be a passive minority investor in a frontier-lab round. None of his named deals is a model lab. All of them are market infrastructure that DRW can trade around.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **On the Silicon Data board from the seed.** The seed Form D (first sale 2025-03-06, $4,724,994 sold, 12 investors, signed 2025-03-20) lists \"Donald R. Wilson Jr.\" as a director at DRW's address [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml). The 2026-08-04 Form D for the A ($30,499,940 sold of $34,999,934, 16 investors, first sale 2026-07-17) still lists him [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml). This corrects the earlier repo row that dated his seat to 2026-07-17.\n- **His only private-company board seat on EDGAR.** EDGAR full-text searches on 2026-11-11 for \"Donald R. Wilson\" in Form D filings returned two hits, both Silicon Derivatives Inc. [src](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Donald%20R.%20Wilson%22&forms=D). Of the private companies that file Form Ds, Silicon Data is the one where he has put his name on the filing.\n- **\"We set up two companies.\"** On Odd Lots (recorded live in Chicago, published 2025-09-29) he describes Compute Exchange and Silicon Data as companies \"we\" set up, not as investments [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/audio/2025-09-29/odd-lots-don-wilson-on-the-future-of-gpu-trading-podcast). He says Silicon Data's job is to build indices that can support listed futures. Quotes come from a third-party ASR transcript [transcript](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/odd-lots/the-king-of-chicago-trading-wants-to-build-a-gpu-market-bigger-than-oil), so wording may be slightly off.\n- **The sequence ends with CME on the cap table.** The order of events:\n  1. DRW co-led the seed (2025-03).\n  2. CME and Silicon Data announced compute futures, with Wilson quoted (2026-05-12) [src](https://www.silicondata.com/news-room/cme-group-and-silicon-data-partner-to-launch-first-compute-futures).\n  3. CME Group invested in the $30.5M Series A that Valor Atreides led (2026-08) [src](https://theaiinsider.tech/2026/08/24/silicon-data-secures-30-5m-series-a-to-build-the-independent-benchmark-layer-for-the-ai-compute-economy/).\n\n  The exchange that lists the contracts now owns part of the index provider. Wilson's company ends up as both benchmark and venue partner.\n- **Same CEO for both companies.** Carmen Li, Silicon Data's founder-CEO, also became Compute Exchange's CEO in October 2025 [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=792970). Wilson co-founded Compute Exchange with Suna Said of Woodside AI Venture Studio (same source). Woodside AI was also in the Silicon Data seed [src](https://www.silicondata.com/news-room/silicon-data-secures-4-7m-in-seed-funding-and-expands-compute-industry-data-product-suite).\n- **Unconfirmed:** both Form Ds are signed by \"Rongnan Li, CEO\". That this is Carmen Li's legal name is not confirmed by any source read, and neither is the Silicon Derivatives = Silicon Data match. Both are inferred from the amounts, dates and DRW address.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is a real principal who approves DRW balance-sheet money and sits on boards. But his AI activity is narrow: compute-market infrastructure he incubates himself. The deals are small ($4.7M seed; DRW was a participant in the $30.5M A). He is not a frontier-lab co-investor. For a $20M seed check, he matters as a **strategic co-investor and customer** for compute-finance, GPU-cloud or AI-trading founders. DRW also buys compute. It signed a dedicated Nvidia B300 cluster deal with QumulusAI [src](https://cryptobriefing.com/jump-trading-drw-compute-power-contracts/). The deal that matters is Silicon Data.\n\n## Background\n- Started in the industry in 1989 and traded Eurodollar options in the CME pit. Founded DRW in 1992. DRW's name comes from his trading badge and initials [src](https://leadforsociety.uchicago.edu/bio/don-wilson/), [Odd Lots](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/audio/2025-09-29/odd-lots-don-wilson-on-the-future-of-gpu-trading-podcast). The firm he traded for before 1992 was not found.\n- 2014: launched Cumberland (crypto liquidity) and co-founded Digital Asset [src](https://www.drw.com/about/don-wilson/).\n- Built Climate Vault with the University of Chicago, and guided DRW into real estate (Convexity Properties) and venture (DRWVC) [src](https://www.drw.com/about/don-wilson/).\n- Economics undergraduate at the University of Chicago, now a trustee. M32 world sailing champion in 2019, 2021 and 2022 [src](https://www.drw.com/about/don-wilson/).\n- How he got the seat: he founded the firm, so there is no promotion path.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.drw.com/about/don-wilson/ | DRW bio plus a feed of his appearances (checked 2026-11-11) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | not verified | No handle confirmed from a primary source |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only |\n| Bio (UChicago) | https://leadforsociety.uchicago.edu/bio/don-wilson/ | Headshot also hosted here |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him (inference):** go through the compute-market companies (Carmen Li, CEO of Silicon Data and Compute Exchange) or through Spencer Powers, DRW's Head of Special Investments and his co-director on Silicon Data's board. General venture pitches go to DRW VC [src](https://www.drw.com/vc). He appears on stage at Odd Lots live events in Chicago.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-09-29: Bloomberg Odd Lots (live, Chicago), \"The King of Chicago Trading Wants to Build a GPU Market Bigger Than Oil\" [link](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/audio/2025-09-29/odd-lots-don-wilson-on-the-future-of-gpu-trading-podcast). Topics: GPUs outspending crude oil within 10 years, how a GPU index could be built, the two companies, and the view that \"everything will be on chain\" within five years.\n- 2026-07-28: \"Don Wilson on perpetual futures\" (listed on DRW's bio page; not read) [src](https://www.drw.com/about/don-wilson/).\n- 2026-10-19: Bloomberg Live Odd Lots, \"Don Wilson on the GPU Market's Trillion Dollar Question\" (listed on DRW's bio page; not read) [src](https://www.drw.com/about/don-wilson/).\n- 2026-06-15: Odd Lots episode with Carmen Li. It describes the two companies as ones \"Wilson is invested in\" [transcript](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/odd-lots/carmen-lis-plan-to-build-a-futures-market-for-compute).\n\n## How they think\n- Compute is the next great commodity, and it needs benchmarks, a spot market and futures, in that order [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/audio/2025-09-29/odd-lots-don-wilson-on-the-future-of-gpu-trading-podcast).\n- He fixes badly designed contracts. On Odd Lots he tells how he spotted the convexity bias in IDCG's swap futures [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/odd-lots/the-king-of-chicago-trading-wants-to-build-a-gpu-market-bigger-than-oil).\n- He says of himself that he is \"always way too early\" (on tokenization). It reads as self-aware about timing risk (same transcript).\n\n## Read these first\n- CME and Silicon Data compute futures release (2026-05-12), his clearest statement of the thesis [src](https://www.silicondata.com/news-room/cme-group-and-silicon-data-partner-to-launch-first-compute-futures).\n- Odd Lots, 2025-09-29 [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/audio/2025-09-29/odd-lots-don-wilson-on-the-future-of-gpu-trading-podcast).\n- Silicon Data seed release with his quote [src](https://www.silicondata.com/news-room/silicon-data-secures-4-7m-in-seed-funding-and-expands-compute-industry-data-product-suite).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing verified.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Silicon Data (Silicon Derivatives Inc.) | Seed ($4.7M); Wilson a director and the only investor quoted | 2025-03-20 (Form D; release 2025-05-20) | co-led (DRW with Jump) | Jump Trading; Woodside AI; Wintermute Ventures; Sancus; SoGal | [release](https://www.silicondata.com/news-room/silicon-data-secures-4-7m-in-seed-funding-and-expands-compute-industry-data-product-suite), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Silicon Data (Silicon Derivatives Inc.) | Series A ($30.5M); still a director | 2026-08 (first sale 2026-07-17) | no (Valor Atreides AI Fund led) | CME Group; F-Prime; Samsung; VanEck | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml), [press](https://theaiinsider.tech/2026/08/24/silicon-data-secures-30-5m-series-a-to-build-the-independent-benchmark-layer-for-the-ai-compute-economy/) |\n| Compute Exchange | Co-founder; no round found | not found | n/a | Suna Said (co-founder) | [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=792970) |\n\nTiming: first in at Silicon Data (DRW incubated it and co-led the seed). He re-upped in the A but left the lead to Valor Atreides and kept the board seat. For Compute Exchange, EDGAR full-text search for \"Compute Exchange\" in Form D filings returned zero hits on 2026-11-11, so no round is known.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Silicon Data:** Carmen Li says she has worked on GPU indices since April 2023. The indices launched on the Bloomberg Terminal in 2025, and the CME partnership followed a year later [transcript](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/odd-lots/carmen-lis-plan-to-build-a-futures-market-for-compute). How Li and Wilson first met: not found.\n- **Compute Exchange:** co-founded by Wilson and Suna Said. Li took over as CEO in October 2025. In 2026 it opened a secondary market for used H100 and A100 GPUs [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=792970).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DRW | Founder and CEO | 1992 | [src](https://www.drw.com/about/don-wilson/) |\n| Silicon Derivatives Inc. (Silicon Data, inferred) | Director | 2025-03 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Compute Exchange | Co-founder | not found | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=792970) |\n| Cumberland | Launched (DRW crypto arm) | 2014 | [src](https://www.drw.com/about/don-wilson/) |\n| Digital Asset | Co-founder | 2014 | [src](https://www.drw.com/about/don-wilson/) |\n| Spencer Powers | Co-director at Silicon Derivatives; DRW Head of Special Investments | 2025-03 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Suna Said (Woodside AI) | Compute Exchange co-founder | not found | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=792970) |\n| Carmen Li | CEO of both companies he set up | 2025 | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=792970) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is \"Rongnan Li\", who signs the Form Ds, the same person as Carmen Li? Is Silicon Derivatives Inc. Silicon Data? Both are unconfirmed.\n- Is the money Wilson's personal money or DRW's? The filings name him as a director, not an investor.\n- Has Compute Exchange raised money, and who owns it?\n- Did DRW or Wilson back Polymarket? He mentions Shane Coplan by first name on Odd Lots, but no investment was confirmed.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.drw.com/about/don-wilson/\n- https://leadforsociety.uchicago.edu/bio/don-wilson/\n- https://www.drw.com/vc\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.silicondata.com/news-room/silicon-data-secures-4-7m-in-seed-funding-and-expands-compute-industry-data-product-suite\n- https://www.silicondata.com/news-room/cme-group-and-silicon-data-partner-to-launch-first-compute-futures\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=792970\n- https://theaiinsider.tech/2026/08/24/silicon-data-secures-30-5m-series-a-to-build-the-independent-benchmark-layer-for-the-ai-compute-economy/\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/audio/2025-09-29/odd-lots-don-wilson-on-the-future-of-gpu-trading-podcast\n- https://podscripts.co/podcasts/odd-lots/the-king-of-chicago-trading-wants-to-build-a-gpu-market-bigger-than-oil (third-party ASR transcript)\n- https://podscripts.co/podcasts/odd-lots/carmen-lis-plan-to-build-a-futures-market-for-compute (third-party ASR transcript)\n- https://cryptobriefing.com/jump-trading-drw-compute-power-contracts/\n", "person:doug-patti": "\n# Doug Patti\n\n## One-line take\nNot an investor: the engineer whose trial of a product is how a start-up ended up with Jane Street leading its Series A.\n\n## Crux\nHe matters as the model of how trading firms invest. The path was: engineers hear of a tool, one of them runs it on a hard internal system, it finds real bugs, the firm becomes a customer, and then the firm offers to lead a round. He is the named person at the \"runs it\" step. He has no cheque authority that any source documents, and Jane Street itself says leading early rounds is rare. To reach a firm like this, a founder has to win an engineer like him first.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He ran the first trial.** Ron Minsky: \"one of our engineers, a guy named Doug Patty\" tried Antithesis on Aria, a system that already had careful simulation testing (transcript spelling; [Signals and Threads, episode 26, 2026-03-16](https://signalsandthreads.com/why-testing-is-hard-and-how-to-fix-it/)).\n- **His stated bar is technical.** Antithesis helped \"uncover issues that no other testing method could find\" ([Series A release, 2025-12-03](https://technews180.com/funding-news/antithesis-raises-105m-series-a-led-by-jane-street/)).\n- **The firm rarely does this.** \"While leading an early round is rare for us\" (same release).\n- **He is a practitioner by background.** \"Before Jane Street, I worked at a few smaller web startups\" ([episode 14, 2022-04-20](https://signalsandthreads.com/state-machine-replication-and-why-you-should-care/), 00:00:34).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He will never lead a seed. He is a useful map of a route to trading-firm capital and a credible reference customer for infrastructure and testing companies. For AI-native automation and compute start-ups, the equivalent engineers at Jane Street, Jump and HRT are the people to win.\n\n## Background\n- Small web start-ups, writing Node.js and Ruby (his own account, episode 14).\n- Jane Street from about 2017: the host said in April 2022 that he had been there \"about five years\". Worked on Concord, the state-machine-replication system behind the firm's client business, and then Aria, its successor (episode 14 blurb).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | none | |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | not checked | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | not checked | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest on Jane Street's Signals and Threads |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2022-04 — Signals and Threads, episode 14 — State Machine Replication, and Why You Should Care — [link](https://signalsandthreads.com/state-machine-replication-and-why-you-should-care/) — how Concord works; his background\n- 2026-03 — Signals and Threads, episode 26 — Why Testing is Hard and How to Fix it — [link](https://signalsandthreads.com/why-testing-is-hard-and-how-to-fix-it/) — he is not a guest; Ron Minsky describes his Antithesis trial\n\n## How they think\nFrom episode 14: he explains system design in terms of determinism and replayable state, which is the same property Antithesis sells. No views on investing are on record.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Episode 26 transcript](https://signalsandthreads.com/why-testing-is-hard-and-how-to-fix-it/): the deal path, told by Jane Street.\n2. [Series A release](https://technews180.com/funding-news/antithesis-raises-105m-series-a-led-by-jane-street/): his quote.\n3. [Episode 14](https://signalsandthreads.com/state-machine-replication-and-why-you-should-care/): what he builds.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Antithesis (as Jane Street's technical champion, not as an investor) | Series A ($105M) | 2025-12-03 | Jane Street led | Amplify Partners, Spark Capital, Tamarack Global, First In Ventures, Teamworthy Ventures, Hyperion Capital, Patrick Collison, Dwarkesh Patel, Sholto Douglas | [release](https://technews180.com/funding-news/antithesis-raises-105m-series-a-led-by-jane-street/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Antithesis:** Jane Street engineers reached out to the company, held several conversations, and Patti tried it on Aria; the firm became a customer and later offered to lead the round (episode 26 and the release). Who inside Jane Street made the investment decision is not stated.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jane Street | employer | about 2017 | [episode 14](https://signalsandthreads.com/state-machine-replication-and-why-you-should-care/) |\n| Ron Minsky | Jane Street colleague and podcast host who credits him | 2022 | [episode 26](https://signalsandthreads.com/why-testing-is-hard-and-how-to-fix-it/) |\n| Will Wilson (Antithesis) | founder of the company he tested | 2025 | [release](https://technews180.com/funding-news/antithesis-raises-105m-series-a-led-by-jane-street/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who at Jane Street approves strategic investments.\n- Whether he has championed any other vendor.\n\n## Sources\nLinked inline.\n", "person:drew-gray": "\n# Drew Gray\n\nCompound's site and bio use \"Drew Gray\". Older Compound pages (2019–2022) and the 2015 Cruise hiring story use \"Andrew Gray\". The Compound URL slug (`drewg`), the Tesla → Cruise → Otto → Uber ATG career line, and his own Medium handle (@drewjgray, matching linkedin.com/in/drewjgray) tie these to one person. Pronouns: Compound's 2019–2022 bios use \"he\" ([wayback](https://web.archive.org/web/20210623091132/https://compound.vc/about)).\n\n## One-line take\nHe is a part-time technical venture partner from the first generation of self-driving engineers (Tesla Autopilot 2013, Cruise VP Eng 2015, Otto/Uber ATG, Voyage CTO 2018). He has been Dempsey's AV/robotics diligence brain since at least 2018, but no public deal carries his name and his attention is now on ocean tech in Hawaii.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** operator credibility from six AV teams. In his own words: \"After leading six different teams in the AV industry\" ([Voyage post, 2018-07-19](https://news.voyage.auto/voyages-unique-approach-to-autonomous-vehicles-and-why-i-joined-as-cto-390cfbf55739)). For Compound, a small seed fund that backed Wayve in 2017 and holds stealth humanoid and robotics-lab positions (per `firms/compound.md`), he is the person who can judge an autonomy or perception founder technically. Compound's own 2021 bio frames him as an ML expert with \"deep professional experience applied to robotics\" ([wayback](https://web.archive.org/web/20210623091132/https://compound.vc/about)).\n- **The Dempsey link predates the title.** His July 2018 Voyage CTO post thanks Michael Dempsey by name, and by August 2019 he was a Compound Board Partner. The relationship is personal and operator-to-investor, not a recruiting hire.\n- **Constraint:** he is part-time and remote (Kawaihae, HI), and since about 2020 his main work has been ocean conservation. He co-founded Aqualink (free reef-monitoring buoys, launched 2020-10-06), is CTO of Coral Gardeners, and co-founded the ocean-sensing company View Into The Blue / Fathom Ocean. No Compound investment is publicly attributed to him in roughly seven years on the roster.\n- **What he'd likely never back (inference from his 2018 writing, not a stated rule):** an AV or robotics company that insists on building every layer in-house. In 2018 he argued that winners now buy sensors, simulation, mapping and middleware and keep only core autonomy in-house.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Build vs. buy is his core operating thesis.** He wrote \"Voyage moves incredibly quickly by embracing the supporting ecosystem heavily\" while keeping core autonomy in-house ([Voyage post, 2018-07-19](https://news.voyage.auto/voyages-unique-approach-to-autonomous-vehicles-and-why-i-joined-as-cto-390cfbf55739); [archive](https://web.archive.org/web/20220823111013/https://news.voyage.auto/voyages-unique-approach-to-autonomous-vehicles-and-why-i-joined-as-cto-390cfbf55739)).\n- **His lesson from six AV teams is about what to avoid:** \"Most important is knowing what not to do!\" (same post).\n- **Early customer of a stealth simulation startup.** In 2018 Voyage was \"the first customer of a stealth, cutting-edge simulation product\" that \"wasn't even available a year ago\" (same post). The vendor is unnamed. That fits Applied Intuition's founding timing, but this is an inference only; recorded as a needs-id lead.\n- **Dempsey reviewed his Voyage post.** \"Thanks to Luke Beard, Tom Hauburger, Oliver Cameron, and Michael Dempsey\" (same post, 2018-07-19). This is the earliest public trace of the Gray–Dempsey link, about a year before the Board Partner listing.\n- **Board Partner while he was a sitting AV CTO.** In 2019 Compound wrote \"Andrew Gray is a Board Partner at Compound\" and noted he was focused on deep learning for self-driving as CTO of Voyage ([wayback 2019-10-14](https://web.archive.org/web/20191014015954/https://compound.vc/about)). He was not on the 2017-08-09 page.\n- **How he came to the ocean.** His Aqualink launch post says the team was \"entirely new to the science of the ocean\". The buoys are given away: \"Aqualink is giving these away for free.\" The sign-off names \"Pete, Lyndon, and Drew\" ([Medium, 2020-10-06](https://medium.com/aqualink/introducing-aqualink-dd1023393b8); [archive](https://web.archive.org/web/20201107175046/https://medium.com/aqualink/introducing-aqualink-dd1023393b8)). Who \"Pete\" and \"Lyndon\" are is not stated; recorded as needs-id.\n- **Cruise timing.** TechCrunch reported on 2015-09-18 that Cruise hired Andrew Gray from Tesla as VP of Engineering; he had \"previously lead autopilot R&D at Tesla\". The hire came alongside Cruise's $12.5M Series A led by Spark Capital and Sam Altman ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/?p=1211414)). He was there before GM bought Cruise in 2016.\n- **Udacity origin of the Voyage tie.** He met Voyage CEO Oliver Cameron in 2016 at Udacity's self-driving nanodegree, where he taught deep-learning content ([TechCrunch 2018-07-19](https://techcrunch.com/2018/07/19/self-driving-car-startup-voyage-brings-on-ex-tesla-cruise-and-uber-exec-as-cto/)). Cameron now runs Odyssey, a world-models lab (aggregator, [src](https://siliconvalleyinvestclub.com/ar/companies/odyssey/team/oliver-cameron/)). That makes Gray a plausible back-channel to a high-fit founder; the back-channel is an inference.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**low** as an investor to track, **medium** as a diligence contact. No AI, chip or robotics deal is publicly attributed to him. He is a part-time venture partner at a sub-$150M fund, and his day job is ocean tech. For the owner he is useful in two ways:\n1. A technical reference call on AV, perception and robotics founders, especially Tesla Autopilot, Cruise, Otto/Uber ATG and Voyage alumni.\n2. A warm path to Oliver Cameron (Odyssey, world models), which is the high-fit lead he surfaces.\n\nCompound's stealth \"Robotics Lab Co.\" and \"Industrial/Military Humanoid Robotics Co.\" positions are where his diligence would most plausibly have counted (inference, unconfirmed).\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** PhD from UC Berkeley in intelligent systems per View Into The Blue ([src](https://www.viewintotheblue.com/team)); aggregators say \"Controls\". Year not found.\n- **Tesla:** started 2013 (TheOrg, aggregator, [src](https://theorg.com/org/fathom-ocean/org-chart/drew-gray)). Compound calls him Tesla's first hire to lead Autopilot development ([src](https://www.compound.vc/team/drewg)). View Into The Blue calls him the founding engineer of Autopilot ([src](https://www.viewintotheblue.com/team)).\n- **Cruise:** VP of Engineering from about 2015-09 ([TechCrunch 2015-09-18](https://techcrunch.com/?p=1211414)). End date not found.\n- **Udacity:** taught deep-learning content in the self-driving nanodegree, 2016 ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2018/07/19/self-driving-car-startup-voyage-brings-on-ex-tesla-cruise-and-uber-exec-as-cto/)).\n- **Otto:** Director of Engineering; dates not found ([src](https://www.compound.vc/team/drewg)).\n- **Uber ATG:** director of engineering for deep learning and perception in San Francisco; dates not found ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2018/07/19/self-driving-car-startup-voyage-brings-on-ex-tesla-cruise-and-uber-exec-as-cto/)).\n- **Voyage:** CTO from about June 2018; he had been there about a month as of 2018-07-19 ([post](https://news.voyage.auto/voyages-unique-approach-to-autonomous-vehicles-and-why-i-joined-as-cto-390cfbf55739)). End date about 2020 per aggregators ([TheOrg search](https://theorg.com/org/coral-gardeners/org-chart/drew-gray)). Cruise acquired Voyage on 2021-03-15 ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2021/03/15/cruise-acquires-self-driving-startup-voyage/)).\n- **Compound:** Board Partner by 2019-08 → Venture Partner by 2023-05 → Venture Partner, AI & Robotics (see promotion_path).\n- **Aqualink:** co-founder; launch post 2020-10-06 ([Medium](https://medium.com/aqualink/introducing-aqualink-dd1023393b8)). Left in 2022 per aggregator.\n- **Coral Gardeners:** CTO since about Feb 2020 per aggregator ([TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/coral-gardeners/org-chart/drew-gray)); confirmed as current CTO by Compound ([src](https://www.compound.vc/team/drewg)).\n- **View Into The Blue / Fathom Ocean:** co-founder and CEO with Trevor Mendelow ([src](https://www.viewintotheblue.com/team)). TheOrg lists \"Fathom Ocean\" co-founder from about Sep 2021 ([aggregator](https://theorg.com/org/fathom-ocean/org-chart/drew-gray)). Whether Fathom Ocean is a rename or a sister entity is unclear.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.compound.vc/team/drewg | Compound bio; social links on the page are placeholders (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://medium.com/@drewjgray | Medium; known posts 2018 (Voyage) and 2020 (Aqualink); dormant |\n| X | not found | Compound page lists X only as a placeholder link |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/drewjgray/ | not read (login wall) |\n| GitHub | not found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2019-11 — Smarter Cars podcast — \"Ep. 32 — Drew Gray, Voyage\" — [link](https://medium.com/smart-cars-a-podcast-about-autonomous-vehicles/ep-32-drew-gray-voyage-e28428ff2225) — deep dive on deep learning in AVs. Page returned 403 and has no archive copy, so not reviewed and no quotes taken.\n- WildLabs case study on Aqualink, \"autonomous cars → reef monitoring\" — [link](https://wildlabs.net/resources/case-studies/autonomous-cars-aqualink-developing-reef-monitoring-system) — 403, not reviewed.\n\n## How they think\n- **Go-to-market before tech:** pick a domain where every route is a \"canonical route\" with real demand, instead of a theoretical shuttle (Voyage and The Villages, 2018 post).\n- **Partner ecosystem over vertical integration:** buy sensors, simulation, mapping, middleware and teleoperation; build core autonomy (2018 post).\n- **Measure first, then model:** Aqualink's plan was to collect in-situ data plus tagged imagery as a dataset that \"future machine learning algorithms\" can use (2020 post). This is a data-first, robotics-style view applied to climate.\n- **Calls right or wrong:** Voyage's retirement-community strategy ended in an acquisition by Cruise (2021-03-15) after $52M raised, not a standalone robotaxi business ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2021/03/15/cruise-acquires-self-driving-startup-voyage/)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Voyage's Unique Approach to Autonomous Vehicles and Why I Joined as CTO](https://news.voyage.auto/voyages-unique-approach-to-autonomous-vehicles-and-why-i-joined-as-cto-390cfbf55739) (2018-07-19): his build-vs-buy and go-to-market thesis, and his thanks to Dempsey.\n2. [Introducing Aqualink](https://medium.com/aqualink/introducing-aqualink-dd1023393b8) (2020-10-06): why he moved from AV to ocean sensing.\n3. [Compound bio](https://www.compound.vc/team/drewg): current framing.\n4. [TechCrunch on Cruise hire, 2015](https://techcrunch.com/?p=1211414): Tesla → Cruise timing.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X account located. Medium is dormant after 2020.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| none publicly attributed | — | — | — | — | Compound bio and portfolio page name no deals for him ([bio](https://www.compound.vc/team/drewg), [portfolio](https://www.compound.vc/portfolio2)) |\n\nFirm-level Compound AI/robotics holdings where his remit would apply (attribution unconfirmed): Wayve (seed 2017, before his listing), Osaro, SparkAI, Fort Robotics, AIFleet, Robotics Lab Co., Industrial/Military Humanoid Robotics Co., Retail Robotics Co. ([portfolio](https://www.compound.vc/portfolio2)).\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found for investments. Operator-side stories:\n- **Voyage hire (2018):** the tie goes back to Udacity in 2016, where Cameron ran curriculum and Gray taught deep learning ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2018/07/19/self-driving-car-startup-voyage-brings-on-ex-tesla-cruise-and-uber-exec-as-cto/)).\n- **Cruise hire (2015):** he joined from Tesla as VP Engineering in the same news cycle as Cruise's $12.5M Series A (Spark Capital, Sam Altman) ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/?p=1211414)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Michael Dempsey / Compound | Thanked in Gray's Voyage post; then Board Partner → Venture Partner at Compound | 2018 | [post](https://news.voyage.auto/voyages-unique-approach-to-autonomous-vehicles-and-why-i-joined-as-cto-390cfbf55739); [wayback](https://web.archive.org/web/20190824213111/https://compound.vc/about) |\n| Oliver Cameron (Voyage → Cruise → Odyssey) | Udacity colleague 2016; his CEO at Voyage 2018– | 2016 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2018/07/19/self-driving-car-startup-voyage-brings-on-ex-tesla-cruise-and-uber-exec-as-cto/) |\n| Kyle Vogt / Cruise | Hired him as VP Engineering | 2015 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/?p=1211414) |\n| Luke Beard, Tom Hauburger | Thanked in the Voyage post (Voyage colleagues, inference) | 2018 | [post](https://news.voyage.auto/voyages-unique-approach-to-autonomous-vehicles-and-why-i-joined-as-cto-390cfbf55739) |\n| Dan Zigmond | Fellow Compound Board Partner on the 2019 roster | 2019 | [wayback](https://web.archive.org/web/20190824213111/https://compound.vc/about) |\n| \"Pete\" and \"Lyndon\" (Aqualink) | Aqualink co-team | 2020 | [Medium](https://medium.com/aqualink/introducing-aqualink-dd1023393b8) |\n| Sofar Ocean | Aqualink's buoy design partner | 2020 | [Medium](https://medium.com/aqualink/introducing-aqualink-dd1023393b8) |\n| Trevor Mendelow | Co-founder, View Into The Blue / Fathom Ocean | ~2021 | [src](https://www.viewintotheblue.com/team) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which Compound deals did he diligence or source? Ask Dempsey about Robotics Lab Co. and the humanoid position.\n- Who was the stealth simulation vendor in 2018 (Applied Intuition?), and did Compound or Gray invest?\n- Who are \"Pete\" and \"Lyndon\" at Aqualink?\n- Is Fathom Ocean a rename of View Into The Blue? Does it have outside investors (Compound?)?\n- Exact Tesla, Otto and Uber ATG dates, and the Voyage exit date.\n- The Smarter Cars Ep. 32 transcript.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.compound.vc/team/drewg ; https://www.compound.vc/portfolio2\n- Wayback, compound.vc/about: 20170809143201, 20190824213111, 20191014015954, 20210623091132, 20220921083638, 20230525215747\n- https://news.voyage.auto/voyages-unique-approach-to-autonomous-vehicles-and-why-i-joined-as-cto-390cfbf55739 (via web.archive.org/web/20220823111013)\n- https://medium.com/aqualink/introducing-aqualink-dd1023393b8 (via web.archive.org/web/20201107175046) ; https://web.archive.org/web/20191112134000/https://medium.com/@drewjgray\n- https://techcrunch.com/2018/07/19/self-driving-car-startup-voyage-brings-on-ex-tesla-cruise-and-uber-exec-as-cto/\n- https://techcrunch.com/?p=1211414 (2015-09-18, Cruise)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2021/03/15/cruise-acquires-self-driving-startup-voyage/\n- https://www.viewintotheblue.com/team\n- Aggregators: https://www.evalyze.ai/investors/andrew-gray ; https://theorg.com/org/fathom-ocean/org-chart/drew-gray ; https://theorg.com/org/coral-gardeners/org-chart/drew-gray ; https://siliconvalleyinvestclub.com/ar/companies/odyssey/team/oliver-cameron/\n- Not reviewed (403): Smarter Cars Ep. 32; WildLabs case study; Axios 2018 scoop\n", "person:dusan-perovic": "\n# Dusan Perovic\n\n*Created 2026-11-10 (sweep worktree \"faceless\"). Sources were fetched directly. Web search was out of budget in this run, so podcasts, talks and X were not searched (see Open questions).*\n\n## One-line take\nHe holds the AI-bio book that Two Sigma Ventures (TSV) carried into Deviation Capital. He was early money in Recursion, insitro and Terray. He is also one of four signing principals of Deviation's GP, so he is a decision-maker, not just a deal partner.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** Two Sigma's data-science credibility in front of computational biology founders. Before TSV he spent years at Two Sigma evaluating new data sources for its trading algorithms [src](https://deviation.com/team/dusan-perovic). His stated screen is that life-sciences companies need a data-science team and a data strategy from day one [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/congratulating-dusan-perovic-and-frances-schwiep-on-their-promotion-to-partner/). Deviation also keeps a seven-person Two Sigma \"Technical Expert Council\" for sourcing and diligence [src](https://deviation.com/updates/introducing-deviation).\n- **What constrains him:** fund size and stage. Deviation invests \"at Seed and Series A\" [src](https://deviation.com/updates/introducing-deviation). TSV core funds were roughly $200-300M (see `firms/two-sigma-ventures.md`). In the large AI-bio rounds he joined (Recursion B-D, insitro B/C, Terray B) TSV was a participant, not the lead.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** lead a $100M+ round alone. Bigger allocation in his deals goes through deal-by-deal Deviate SPVs (see below).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **His only personally named round is a non-AI company.** Gameto's $33M Series B (2024-05-29) was co-led by TSV and RA Capital. The release quotes him as Partner: \"pioneering approach to IVF has the potential to impact families and societies\" [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/gameto-raises-33-million-in-series-b-financing-to-advance-development-and-commercialization-of-novel-fertility-treatments-302157456.html). TSV is not in the 2020 seed or the 2022 $20M A [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/gameto-raises-23m-to-redefine-reproductive-longevity-with-reprogrammed-ovarian-cells-301456850.html). TSV re-upped in the $44M C, which Overwater led (2025-08-12) [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/gametos-44m-series-c-to-help-redefine-reproductive-care-as-first-patients-recruited-for-pivotal-stem-cell-phase-3-trial-302527828.html). Then \"Ferring Signs $1.4 Billion Deal to Buy Gameto Fertility Startup\" (2026-10-27, Bloomberg headline posted on [Deviation's updates page](https://deviation.com/updates)). That is about 29 months from co-lead to exit. Deal terms are unconfirmed because Bloomberg is paywalled.\n- **He signs for the GP.** All five `Deviation Co-Invest LLC - Deviate I-V` Form Ds (2026-06 to 2026-09, $0.4M-$10.3M each) list him as \"Managing Member of the Managing Member of the Issuer\". So do Beirne, Sidney Sands-Ramshaw (Costabile) and Jonathan Golden [D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2147382/000214738226000001/primary_doc.xml), [D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2153331/000215333126000001/primary_doc.xml). The SPVs do not name their target companies.\n- **Board seat outside bio.** He is a Director of Freeform Future Corp. (AI-native metal 3D printing) on its Form D signed 2026-01-07. That filing shows $58.9M sold of $67.1M, first sale 2025-12-23, from 21 investors, which matches the $67M Series B announced 2026-02-19 [D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1778615/000177861526000001/primary_doc.xml), [src](https://siliconangle.com/2026/02/19/freeform-reels-67m-3d-metal-printing-system/). He wrote TSV's 2023 Freeform post, which says TSV had known CTO TJ Ronacher for over six years [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/announcing-our-investment-in-freeform/). Freeform is the only portfolio board seat found for him on EDGAR. An EDGAR full-text search of \"Perovic\" (2026-11-10) returns no other Form D for him besides the Deviate SPVs.\n- **He shares the bio book with an investor.** Elianna Knight (Yale MD, protein-engineering ML) lists Xaira and Gameto as managed by her too, plus Iambic and Arda [src](https://deviation.com/team/elianna-knight). They co-wrote TSV's Arda post (2024).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is a founding partner with GP signing authority, but not a fast riser: 14 years from Two Sigma to Partner. The owner writes ~$20M checks into frontier-AI seeds. His AI-bio deals (Recursion, insitro, Terray, Xaira) are the closest to that lens, but in most of them TSV followed into large rounds rather than leading. He matters as (a) a co-investor with Two Sigma technical diligence behind him in techbio and AI-for-science seeds, and (b) a route into Deviation SPV allocations. The deals that matter: Terray seed co-lead with Digitalis (2020) and the Freeform board seat (physical-AI manufacturing).\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in then-Yugoslavia, where he helped his parents launch a private-label soda business. Attended an international boarding school in Norway [src](https://deviation.com/team/dusan-perovic).\n- BSE in electrical engineering, Princeton University, with minors in German literature and Dance [src](https://deviation.com/team/dusan-perovic).\n- Healthcare management consulting (firm not named) [src](https://deviation.com/team/dusan-perovic).\n- Two Sigma, from 2007: company growth strategy, launching new business units, and data-source research for the investment algorithms [src](https://deviation.com/team/dusan-perovic), [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/congratulating-dusan-perovic-and-frances-schwiep-on-their-promotion-to-partner/).\n- TSV Principal 2016 (on the 2019 roster [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20190301185709/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/)), then Partner effective 2021-01-05 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/congratulating-dusan-perovic-and-frances-schwiep-on-their-promotion-to-partner/).\n- **How he got the seat:** an internal move from the hedge fund's data-research side into its in-house venture arm, then the 2026 spin-out. Deviation's launch (2026-05-04) names him among the \"former TSV partners\" who are founding partners [src](https://deviation.com/updates/introducing-deviation). The ADV lists him as a principal [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/343210/PDF/343210.pdf).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://deviation.com/team/dusan-perovic | Bio, focus areas, managed companies (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://deviation.com/updates | Firm updates; his TSV posts are on Wayback copies of twosigmaventures.com |\n| X | nothing found (firm: https://x.com/deviationcap) | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/dusan-perovic-7a012a4/ | Linked from his Deviation page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** Deviation lists no application form or email, only LinkedIn and X [src](https://deviation.com/team/dusan-perovic). For techbio, Elianna Knight is the likely first contact. A co-investor wanting more allocation would go through the Deviate SPVs, whose GP he co-manages. Both of these routes are inferred.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was not available in this run, so this was not checked.\n\n## How they think\n- Life-sciences companies need data science and a data strategy \"from the get go\" (TSV promotion post, 2021).\n- \"Science is the engine of human progress.\" [src](https://deviation.com/team/dusan-perovic)\n- Willing to back hardware outside bio (Freeform) when he has a long relationship with the founder.\n- Calls: early in Recursion (Series B 2017, IPO 2021) and insitro (by its 2020 B). Gameto exit ($1.4B, 2026). Zymergen, credited to him in the 2021 promotion post, later collapsed. That was a miss, though his round is unconfirmed.\n\n## Read these first\n- His Deviation bio: https://deviation.com/team/dusan-perovic\n- TSV promotion post (2021-01-05): https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/congratulating-dusan-perovic-and-frances-schwiep-on-their-promotion-to-partner/\n- His Freeform post (2023): https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/announcing-our-investment-in-freeform/\n- His Hexagon Bio Series A post (2020): https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/announcing-our-investment-in-hexagon-bios-47m-series-a/\n- Deviation launch post: https://deviation.com/updates/introducing-deviation\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public X account that we could find.\n\n## Portfolio\nAttribution basis: the TSV promotion post credits him with these companies, his Deviation page lists them as \"managed by\" him, or he wrote the investment post. Except for Gameto, no round release names him personally. Rows marked * are in `data/rows/two-sigma-ventures-portfolio-deals.csv`. New rows are in `data/rows/dusan-perovic-deals.csv`.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Recursion* | Series B ($60M), entry | 2017-10-03 | no | | [src](https://globalventuring.com/recursion-racks-up-60m-series-b) |\n| Recursion* | Series C ($121M), re-up | 2019-07-15 | no | | [src](https://www.biospace.com/recursion-pharmaceuticals-secures-121-million-in-series-c-financing) |\n| Recursion* | Series D ($239M), re-up | 2020-09-09 | no | | [src](https://recursion.com/news/series-d) |\n| insitro* | Series B ($143M; a16z led); TSV already an existing investor | 2020-05-26 | no | a16z, CPP, T. Rowe, GV | [src](https://www.biospace.com/insitro-announces-143-million-raised-in-series-b-financing) |\n| insitro* | Series C ($400M), re-up | 2021-03-15 | no | | [src](https://www.insitro.com/news/insitro-raises-400-million-in-series-c-financing-2/) |\n| Terray Therapeutics* | Seed ($20M), entry | 2020 | co-led | Digitalis | [src](https://www.biospace.com/terray-therapeutics-now-has-80-million-to-fund-ai-driven-drug-discovery-efforts) |\n| Terray Therapeutics* | Series A ($60M) / Series B ($120M), re-ups | 2022-02 / 2024-10 | no | NVentures, Bedford Ridge | [src](https://www.biospace.com/press-releases/terray-therapeutics-closes-oversubscribed-120m-series-b-to-advance-its-generative-ai-pipeline-of-small-molecule-therapeutics-to-the-clinic) |\n| Hexagon Bio* | Series A ($47M), authored post | 2020-09-15 | not stated | The Column Group, 8VC | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/announcing-our-investment-in-hexagon-bios-47m-series-a/) |\n| Freeform* | Entry (stealth exit, $45M to date), authored post | 2023-02-01 | not claimed | Threshold, Founders Fund, 8VC | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/announcing-our-investment-in-freeform/) |\n| Freeform* | Series B ($67M); **board director** on Form D | 2026-02-19 (first sale 2025-12-23) | not stated | NVentures, AE Ventures, Founders Fund, Threshold | [D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1778615/000177861526000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Arda Therapeutics* | Undisclosed, co-authored post with Knight | 2024-10-15 | not stated | | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/arda-therapeutics-harnessing-data-science-to-target-chronic-diseases-at-the-cellular-level/) |\n| Gameto (non-AI) | Series B ($33M), entry, **named and quoted** | 2024-05-29 | co-led with RA Capital | Insight, Future Ventures, BOLD, Lux | [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/gameto-raises-33-million-in-series-b-financing-to-advance-development-and-commercialization-of-novel-fertility-treatments-302157456.html) |\n| Gameto (non-AI) | Series C ($44M), re-up, no person named | 2025-08-12 | no (Overwater led) | Insight, RA Capital | [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/gametos-44m-series-c-to-help-redefine-reproductive-care-as-first-patients-recruited-for-pivotal-stem-cell-phase-3-trial-302527828.html) |\n| Xaira | Unconfirmed: listed as managed by him, round not found | | | | [src](https://deviation.com/team/dusan-perovic) |\n| Kallyope (non-AI) | Unconfirmed round. Kallyope releases appear on trigemxbio.com from 2026, so a rename to TriGemX Bio is likely but not stated | | | | [src](https://deviation.com/team/dusan-perovic) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Freeform:** sourced through a relationship. TSV knew co-founder and CTO TJ Ronacher for 6+ years before investing in 2023. Perovic wrote the post and by 2025-12 held a board seat (see above).\n- **Gameto:** he came in as co-lead at the B, after Future Ventures led the A. The company was sold to Ferring about 29 months later.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Two Sigma | Strategy and data-source research | 2007 | [src](https://deviation.com/team/dusan-perovic) |\n| Two Sigma Ventures | Principal, then Partner (2021-01-05) | 2016 to 2026-04 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/congratulating-dusan-perovic-and-frances-schwiep-on-their-promotion-to-partner/) |\n| Deviation Capital | Founding Partner; managing member of GP of Deviate I-V SPVs | 2026-04 | [src](https://deviation.com/updates/introducing-deviation), [D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2147382/000214738226000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Colin Beirne | Co-founding partner; co-managing member on SPVs | 2007 | [D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2153331/000215333126000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Elianna Knight | Shares the AI-bio book (Xaira, Gameto, Arda) | ~2023 | [src](https://deviation.com/team/elianna-knight) |\n| Freeform (Erik Palitsch, TJ Ronacher) | Board director | 2025-12 | [D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1778615/000177861526000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| RA Capital | Co-led Gameto Series B | 2024-05 | [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/gameto-raises-33-million-in-series-b-financing-to-advance-development-and-commercialization-of-novel-fertility-treatments-302157456.html) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which company did each Deviate SPV buy into? Deviate II ($10.3M, first sale 2026-07-09) lines up in time with Walden Robotics, but that is unconfirmed.\n- Did TSV invest in Xaira's $1B launch (2024-04)? The BusinessWire release returned 403.\n- Gameto–Ferring terms (upfront vs milestones) and TSV's return.\n- Check size: no source gives his personal check. `sub-10m` follows the firm's fund sizes, which is in tension with ~$2B AUM across vehicles.\n- Podcasts, conference talks (BIO, JPM) and his X presence: not searched this run because the search budget was exhausted.\n\n## Sources\n- https://deviation.com/team/dusan-perovic\n- https://deviation.com/team/elianna-knight\n- https://deviation.com/updates\n- https://deviation.com/updates/introducing-deviation\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1778615/000177861526000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2144214/000214421426000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2147382/000214738226000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2147383/000214738326000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2153331/000215333126000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2152931/000215293126000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/gameto-raises-33-million-in-series-b-financing-to-advance-development-and-commercialization-of-novel-fertility-treatments-302157456.html\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/gametos-44m-series-c-to-help-redefine-reproductive-care-as-first-patients-recruited-for-pivotal-stem-cell-phase-3-trial-302527828.html\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/gameto-raises-23m-to-redefine-reproductive-longevity-with-reprogrammed-ovarian-cells-301456850.html\n- https://siliconangle.com/2026/02/19/freeform-reels-67m-3d-metal-printing-system/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/congratulating-dusan-perovic-and-frances-schwiep-on-their-promotion-to-partner/\n- https://trigemxbio.com/news\n", "person:dylan-patel": "\n# Dylan Patel\n\n## One-line take\nThe most-read AI chip and data-center analyst is now also a GP. In public they have rated chip startups' odds of success under 1%. Their $500M fund then co-led Positron at $5B. Read their calls as both research and deal flow.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** Patel sells data to hyperscalers, chip companies, private equity and hedge funds. That gives them a near-unique view of wafer orders, data-center builds and cluster pricing, and they turn the same view into investments.\n- **What constrains them:** conflict. Since 2021 their posts have carried a note that SemiAnalysis clients and staff may hold positions in the companies covered [src](https://newsletter.semianalysis.com/p/graphcore-looks-like-a-complete-failure). On air they decline to talk up their own holdings [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGhlJqnECd0).\n- **What they would never do:** back a chip startup on a \"faster than Nvidia\" pitch without a performance-per-TCO case. They also would not back one built on a fixed bet about model shape, like the first-wave chips that put large SRAM on the die.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Positron, before and after.** In February 2026 they called Etched, MatX and Positron exciting but \"very very skeptical\", and put every one at \"less than 1%\" odds [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DqBMzuzxZog). Seven months later SemiAnalysis Capital co-led Positron's Series C and Patel took a board seat [src](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/).\n- **Early MatX and Lemurian call (Nov 2023).** They named the MatX founders (Reiner Pope, Mike Gunter) and Lemurian's Jay Dawani as making \"much more rational bets\" than first-wave chips, well before MatX's large rounds [src](https://www.latent.space/p/semianalysis) (00:29:27).\n- **Valuation ceiling for hardware startups (2022).** \"most successful hardware startups have had valuations peak in the $1B to $2B range\". They praised Ayar Labs for turning away VC money and favouring strategic investors [src](https://newsletter.semianalysis.com/p/ayar-labs-co-packaged-optics-revolution). Positron's C also carried strategics (Cisco, HRT, Naver) [src](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/).\n- **Co-investor link with Conviction.** On No Priors they realise Sarah Guo \"led the round\" of a datacenter automation company they had angel invested in. The company is not named [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGhlJqnECd0) (34:37).\n- **Naveen Rao origin.** In 2019, while still anonymous, they \"baited him on the internet\", then moved to DMs and a call. Now they back Rao's Unconventional AI as \"exciting, probably won't work\" [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/dylan-patel-of-semianalysis-why-hardware-software-co-design-is-ais-real-100x).\n- **Compute advice for a new lab.** As hypothetical head of compute for SSI with $1B, they would buy the biggest single-site cluster: \"256 GPUs or even 4K GPUs is kind of cope\" [src](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/dylan-jon) (01:10:12). In Nov 2026 they said it is \"hell\" for well-funded neolabs to get compute, with clouds asking for half up front [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uA5NMUadmgg) (15:33).\n- **Admitted misses.** In July 2021 they said Nvidia was \"not currently worth more than $500B\" [src](https://newsletter.semianalysis.com/p/nvidias-2021-bubble-eerily-similar). In Oct 2024 they said compute cost would \"continue to tank\" [src](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/dylan-jon). By Nov 2026 they were \"surprised\" by how far prices had spiked [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uA5NMUadmgg).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Patel is now a $500M AI-chips and compute GP who co-leads large rounds and takes board seats: Positron Series C, $875M at $5B [src](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/). They also angel-invested in Prime Intellect (2024 seed, 2025 round) [src](https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/fundraise). Their research shapes how every chip and neocloud deal gets underwritten. check_capacity is **growth** because the first ticket we can see is a co-lead of a $375M Series C tranche. The fund also sold $500M per Form D [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2145695/000090514826004552/primary_doc.xml). For the owner, Patel is both a possible co-investor in chip rounds and a reference check on any compute-heavy lab seed.\n\n## Background\n- Immigrant parents; grew up in rural Georgia. Got into hardware at 8 fixing an Xbox 360 red-ring fault through forums, then moderated forums and subreddits as a tween and teen [src](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/dylan-jon) (01:50:20).\n- Started investing in semiconductor stocks with internship money at 18-19 [src](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/dylan-jon).\n- College in data science, then about three years at a financial company building hurricane, earthquake and wildfire simulations. Employer not named [src](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/dylan-jon).\n- 2020: quiet-quit and travelled the US national parks; consulting for investors, VCs and companies picked up mid-2020. End-2021: a paid post on photoresist brought 40 paid subscribers overnight [src](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/dylan-jon). They also date consulting to 2017 and the blog to Nov 2021 [src](https://www.latent.space/p/semianalysis).\n- SemiAnalysis had 14 people across the US, Japan, Taiwan, Singapore and France by Oct 2024 [src](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/dylan-jon). By Nov 2026 it ran a chip reverse-engineering lab in Oregon and the InferenceX benchmark [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uA5NMUadmgg).\n- SemiAnalysis Capital Fund I: Form D 2026-07-29 at a $400M target; later reported $500M sold [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2145695/000090514826004552/primary_doc.xml).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://semianalysis.com | Research, data products, consulting |\n| Substack / blog | https://newsletter.semianalysis.com | 348 posts 2020-05 to 2026-11, almost all bylined Patel; mostly paywalled after 2022 |\n| X | https://x.com/dylan522p | Handle as commonly cited; not fetched this run |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Trm74fovjaA | SemiAnalysis Weekly (with Jordan Nanos) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-09 — ChinaTalk — Huawei, SMIC and export controls — https://www.chinatalk.media/p/huawei-smic-and-future-of-export\n- 2023-11 — Latent Space — The State of Silicon and the GPU Poors — https://www.latent.space/p/semianalysis — first-wave chips' SRAM bet was \"really dumb\"; MatX and Lemurian \"much more rational\"\n- 2024-10 — Dwarkesh Podcast (with Asianometry) — https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/dylan-jon — origin story; SSI compute plan; bubble view\n- 2024-11 — Stratechery — interview with Doug O'Laughlin — https://stratechery.com/2024/an-interview-with-dylan-patel-and-doug-olaughlin-about-the-current-state-of-semiconductors-and-semianalysis/ (paywalled)\n- 2024-12 — BG2 — AI Semiconductor Landscape — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QVcSBHhcFbg\n- 2025-02 — Lex Fridman #459 (with Nathan Lambert) — https://lexfridman.com/deepseek-dylan-patel-nathan-lambert/\n- 2025-08 — No Priors Ep. 127 — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGhlJqnECd0 — neocloud shakeout; co-investment with Conviction; Cognition poker story\n- 2025-09 — Invest Like the Best EP.442 — https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/dylan-patel-inside-the-trillion-dollar-ai-buildout/id1154105909?i=1000729311690\n- 2026-02 — MAD Podcast — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DqBMzuzxZog — \"less than 1%\" odds for Etched, MatX and Positron\n- 2026-03 — Dwarkesh Podcast — three bottlenecks — https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/dylan-patel\n- 2026-06 — Training Data (Sequoia) — https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/dylan-patel-of-semianalysis-why-hardware-software-co-design-is-ais-real-100x — Naveen Rao, Crusoe, Jensen's multipolar strategy\n- 2026-11 — Modal Runtime 2026 talk — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uA5NMUadmgg — compute prices spiking; neolab compute squeeze\n- Full list: `data/rows/dylan-patel-voice-media.csv` (106 items)\n\n## How they think\n- **Performance per TCO is the test** for any chip, not peak speed. They applied it to Groq in 2024 [src](https://newsletter.semianalysis.com/p/groq-inference-tokenomics-speed-but).\n- **Incumbents usually win:** AMD, Trainium and TPU are likelier second sources than any chip startup [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGhlJqnECd0). Yet they find the new wave \"much more exciting than the first wave\" because those founders start from a view of model shape [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DqBMzuzxZog).\n- **Neoclouds:** VC-backed GPU clouds cannot live on real-estate-style 10-15% returns, so many will fail [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGhlJqnECd0). Winners such as Crusoe are \"hyper-levered equity owners\" racing to deliver [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/dylan-patel-of-semianalysis-why-hardware-software-co-design-is-ais-real-100x).\n- **Nvidia as kingmaker:** Jensen funds neolabs for a multipolar world, and Nvidia backstops cluster loans [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/dylan-patel-of-semianalysis-why-hardware-software-co-design-is-ais-real-100x), [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uA5NMUadmgg).\n- **Calls right:** Graphcore \"cannot keep up\" (2021) [src](https://newsletter.semianalysis.com/p/graphcore-looks-like-a-complete-failure); Rivos spotted in stealth (2021) [src](https://newsletter.semianalysis.com/p/rivos-inc-a-chip-off-the-old-block). **Calls wrong:** Nvidia capped at $500B (2021) [src](https://newsletter.semianalysis.com/p/nvidias-2021-bubble-eerily-similar); compute cost would \"continue to tank\" (2024) [src](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/dylan-jon).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Dwarkesh with Asianometry (2024) — origin story, how a new lab should buy compute, bubble view. https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/dylan-jon\n2. Latent Space, GPU Poors (2023) — their framework for which chip startups can win. https://www.latent.space/p/semianalysis\n3. MAD Podcast (2026) — the \"less than 1%\" view on Positron's cohort, months before the Positron deal. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DqBMzuzxZog\n4. Ayar Labs post (2022) — their view on hardware-startup valuations and strategic money. https://newsletter.semianalysis.com/p/ayar-labs-co-packaged-optics-revolution\n5. Training Data with Sequoia (2026) — Naveen Rao, Crusoe, Nvidia's motives. https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/dylan-patel-of-semianalysis-why-hardware-software-co-design-is-ais-real-100x\n\n## Social engagement\nX not fetched this run. From what was read: Patel describes the main business, besides \"shitposting\", as selling data [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uA5NMUadmgg). Sam Altman amplified their \"GPU poors\" framing [src](https://www.latent.space/p/semianalysis). They recur on Dwarkesh, Latent Space, ChinaTalk, a16z and TBPN.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Positron | Series C ($875M at $5B post) | 2026-09-10 | co-led (SemiAnalysis Capital); board seat | NEA; Atreides; Andra; Valor; Jim Clark | [src](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/) |\n| Prime Intellect | Seed ($5.5M) | 2024-04-23 | no (personal angel) | CoinFund; Distributed Global; Clem Delangue | [src](https://www.theblock.co/news/deals/2024-04-23-coinfund-and-distributed-global-lead-5-5-million-seed-round-for-decentralized-ai-firm-prime-intellect-290476) |\n| Prime Intellect | $15M round | 2025-02-28 | no (angel) | Founders Fund (lead); Andrej Karpathy; Tri Dao | [src](https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/fundraise) |\n| Unnamed datacenter automation co. | round led by Conviction | before 2025-08 | no (angel) | Conviction (lead) | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGhlJqnECd0) |\n| Fluidstack | reported SPV (~$50M) | 2026-02 | unknown | — | aggregator only [src](https://aiwiki.ai/wiki/dylan_patel) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Positron:** In Feb 2026 Patel put Positron with Etched and MatX at under 1% odds while calling the cohort exciting [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DqBMzuzxZog). In Sep 2026 SemiAnalysis Capital co-led the $375M first tranche and Patel joined the board with Gavin Baker and Forest Baskett [src](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/). What changed in between is not public.\n- **Conviction co-investment:** Patel and Sarah Guo found out on air that they were both in the same datacenter automation round, with Guo leading [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGhlJqnECd0).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Naveen Rao / Unconventional AI | Known since 2019; public backer of the long-term bet | 2019 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/dylan-patel-of-semianalysis-why-hardware-software-co-design-is-ais-real-100x) |\n| Sarah Guo / Conviction | Co-investor (Guo led, Patel angel) | before 2025-08 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGhlJqnECd0) |\n| Gavin Baker / Atreides | Fellow Positron director | 2026-09 | [src](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/) |\n| Doug O'Laughlin | SemiAnalysis colleague; regular co-guest | 2022 or earlier | [src](https://www.chinatalk.media/p/huawei-smic-and-future-of-export) |\n| Guido Appenzeller (a16z) | Recurring podcast host | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/dylan-patel-on-the-ai-chip-race-nvidia-intel-the-us-government-vs-china/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which datacenter automation company did they co-invest in with Conviction?\n- What changed between the Feb 2026 \"less than 1%\" view and the Sep 2026 Positron co-lead? Did they back Positron personally before the C?\n- Fluidstack SPV: confirm size and date from a primary source (Form D or press).\n- Who are the 120 Fund I LPs, and do any hedge-fund clients of SemiAnalysis overlap?\n- The pre-SemiAnalysis employer is described only as a financial company doing catastrophe simulation; not identified.\n- X archive (dylan522p) and the 2022 ChinaTalk year-in-review were not mined.\n\n## Update 2026-11-11\nExtends the profile; nothing above was rewritten.\n\n- **Positron was a re-up, not a convert.** Positron's own Series B release (2026-02-04) quotes \"Dylan Patel, founder and CEO of SemiAnalysis, an advisor and investor in Positron\" [src](https://qatar.websummit.com/wp-media/2026/02/Positron-__-Series-B-Press-Release.docx.pdf). The MAD Podcast where they gave Positron under 1% odds came out the next day [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DqBMzuzxZog). So the Sep 2026 Series C co-lead and board seat [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/positron-ai-raises-875-million-at-a-5-billion-valuation-to-bring-its-next-generation-inference-silicon-to-market-302874601.html) doubled down on a company they already held and advised. The release wording could mean SemiAnalysis rather than Patel personally; the entry round and check size are unconfirmed.\n- **Fund grew out of an SPV.** The Information (via Techmeme, 2026-02-18) reported a $50M SPV into Fluidstack's round, while SemiAnalysis was only in early talks about a fund [src](https://www.techmeme.com/260218/p63). The SPV is not on EDGAR, so it stays unconfirmed. ADV lists Patel as sole managing member of SemiAnalysis Capital Management since 03/2026 [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/343762/PDF/343762.pdf).\n- **Angel book reaches the training stack, not just silicon.** HUD $16M Series A (2026-06-30, Standard Capital lead), where they are listed with OpenAI's Roon [src](https://www.hud.ai/blog/announcing-16m-series-a). Also Adaptive ML seed (2024-03, Index lead) [src](https://www.adaptive-ml.com/post/adaptive-raises-seed), Prime Intellect seed (2024-04) then a re-up in its $15M round (2025-02) [src](https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/fundraise), and zeroRISC seed (2025-06, silicon security) [src](https://www.securityweek.com/zerorisc-raises-10-million-for-open-source-silicon-security-solutions/).\n- **Reported but unconfirmed:** stakes in about 20 startups including Thinking Machines Lab and Enfabrica (Chinese-language profile via a broker) [src](https://www.itiger.com/news/1116756015). Volantis is listed as an early backer only on secondary sites.\n- **What they personally sign:** the Fund I, SPV I and SPV II Form Ds, and every vehicle as the GP's sole manager [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/343762/PDF/343762.pdf). No other named decision-maker was found at SemiAnalysis Capital (see firms/semianalysis-capital.md).\n- **How to reach them:** for a lab-adjacent seed (evals, RL environments, training infra), the angel route has worked even after the fund launched (HUD). For chip and memory deals, the warm paths are SemiAnalysis client relationships and co-leads they have done deals with: NEA, Atreides, Valor (Positron), and Primary, B Capital and Hyperion (TeRAM).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/dylan-jon\n- https://www.latent.space/p/semianalysis\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DqBMzuzxZog (transcript: https://pod.liujiacai.net/2026-02-05-dylan-patel-nvidia-s-new-moat-why-china-is-semiconductor-pilled/transcript/)\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGhlJqnECd0\n- https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/dylan-patel-of-semianalysis-why-hardware-software-co-design-is-ais-real-100x\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uA5NMUadmgg\n- https://newsletter.semianalysis.com/p/graphcore-looks-like-a-complete-failure\n- https://newsletter.semianalysis.com/p/nvidias-2021-bubble-eerily-similar\n- https://newsletter.semianalysis.com/p/ayar-labs-co-packaged-optics-revolution\n- https://newsletter.semianalysis.com/p/rivos-inc-a-chip-off-the-old-block\n- https://newsletter.semianalysis.com/p/groq-inference-tokenomics-speed-but\n- https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/\n- https://www.hud.ai/blog/announcing-16m-series-a\n- https://qatar.websummit.com/wp-media/2026/02/Positron-__-Series-B-Press-Release.docx.pdf\n- https://www.adaptive-ml.com/post/adaptive-raises-seed\n- https://www.securityweek.com/zerorisc-raises-10-million-for-open-source-silicon-security-solutions/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2145695/000090514826004552/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/fundraise\n- https://aiwiki.ai/wiki/dylan_patel (aggregator)\n", "person:ed-sim": "\n# Ed Sim\n\n## One-line take\nThe founder of boldstart and the person on its hot security and agent deals: he co-led Protect AI's first round and sat on its board to the Palo Alto Networks exit, and co-led the first rounds of Tessl and Keycard.\n\n## Crux\nHe wins by being the investor a repeat technical founder calls before the company exists, then moving the same day. His edge is enterprise buyers, not research: he sits in Miami and New York to hear what large companies will pay for. The constraint is fund size against round size; a $250M fund cannot keep up with a lab-scale round, so he uses opportunity funds and single-asset vehicles. He does not back model labs and says value accrues above them.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Same-day decisions, referral-only sourcing: \"we can move very fast\" ([WSJ, 2025-07-08](https://boldstart.vc/news/wsj-boldstart-ventures-raises-250-million-ai-fund-looking-to-move-fast/)).\n- What he underwrites with no product: \"You see the world moving around talent magnets\" (same source).\n- Inception is about timing, not size: \"an Inception round is unbounded by size.\" ([TheSequence, 2025-01-23](https://thesequence.substack.com/p/the-sequence-chat-475-ed-sim-forbes)).\n- Where he thinks AI value goes: \"the last mile in the enterprise is the longest mile.\" (same source).\n- On structure in a downturn: \"maintaining your valuation is not great if you take a 2x liquidation preference.\" ([TechCrunch, 2022-06-01](https://techcrunch.com/2022/06/01/boldstart-ventures-has-two-new-funds-to-plug-into-teams-with-an-idea-and-little-else)).\n- Protect AI was a years-old relationship: \"We first met Ian Swanson years ago when he was founder and CEO.\" ([boldstart, 2022-12-15](https://boldstart.vc/news/protect-ai-mlsecops-shift-left-security-for-ai-ml-welcome-to-boldstart/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh for AI-native automation and agents at the first cheque (Clay, CrewAI, Tessl, Keycard, Baz). Low for labs and chips: he has said he prefers the application layer. He is a likely co-investor, and competitor, for any automation company the owner wants at formation.\n\n## Background\nHarvard College (economics, lacrosse). Started at J.P. Morgan building quantitative trading models. Co-founded Dawntreader Ventures, where he led first rounds in Greenplum, GoToMeeting and LivePerson. Co-founded MState with IBM (backed Fireblocks at formation). Founded boldstart in 2010 with a $1M fund. All from his bio ([src](https://boldstart.vc/team/ed-sim/)) and TechCrunch ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2022/06/01/boldstart-ventures-has-two-new-funds-to-plug-into-teams-with-an-idea-and-little-else)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://boldstart.vc/team/ed-sim/ | firm bio |\n| Substack / blog | \"What's Hot in AI/Infra/VC\" newsletter (named on bio) | weekly; URL not checked |\n| X | https://x.com/edsim | follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/edsim | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-08 — Trends with Friends — Inside AI's New VC Reality — [link](https://boldstart.vc/resources/ed-joins-trends-with-friends-podcast-inside-ais-new-vc-reality/) — not mined\n- 2025-05 — Carta Podcast — Backing Founders from Day Zero — [link](https://boldstart.vc/resources/ed-sim-on-the-carta-podcast-backing-founders-from-day-zero/) — show notes only: inception rounds split into \"Discovery, Classic, and Jumbo\"; a story about stopping a founder from selling too early (details not in notes)\n- 2025-01 — TheSequence Chat #475 — written Q&A — [link](https://thesequence.substack.com/p/the-sequence-chat-475-ed-sim-forbes) — mined\n- undated — Venture Unlocked, CXOTalk, #MiamiTech Pod — see media rows — not mined\n\n## How they think\n- Invest before incorporation; be the one place a founder goes whatever the round size.\n- Talent magnets over ideas.\n- Enterprise deployment is the hard part of AI; application and security layers capture the value.\n- Raise less: the Carta notes summarise him as saying too much capital is \"the tail wagging the dog\" (show-notes wording, not a verified quote).\n\n## Read these first\n- [TheSequence Chat #475](https://thesequence.substack.com/p/the-sequence-chat-475-ed-sim-forbes): his clearest written statement of inception investing and his AI views.\n- [WSJ on Fund VII](https://boldstart.vc/news/wsj-boldstart-ventures-raises-250-million-ai-fund-looking-to-move-fast/): pace, cheque sizes, how deals arrive.\n- [Protect AI welcome post](https://boldstart.vc/news/protect-ai-mlsecops-shift-left-security-for-ai-ml-welcome-to-boldstart/): how a first cheque comes from an old relationship.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed in this pass (X not fetched).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Protect AI | Seed ($13.5M), board | 2022-12-15 | co-led | Acrew, Knollwood, Pelion, Avisio; Shlomo Kramer, Nir Polak, Dimitri Sirota | [src](https://boldstart.vc/news/protect-ai-mlsecops-shift-left-security-for-ai-ml-welcome-to-boldstart/) |\n| Tessl | Seed (inception) | 2024-04 | co-led | GV (Tom Hulme) | [src](https://boldstart.vc/team/ed-sim/); ledger |\n| Keycard | Seed + Series A ($38M) | 2025-10-21 | co-led | a16z, Acrew | [src](https://boldstart.vc/team/ed-sim/); ledger |\n| Surf AI | not stated | 2024 | not stated | | [src](https://boldstart.vc/team/ed-sim/) |\n| Netpreme | Seed, board (Form D) | 2025-06 | not stated | | ledger |\n| Clay | Seed (inception) | 2017 | no | BoxGroup, First Round | [src](https://clay.com/blog/clay-raises-62m-to-turn-any-growth-idea-into-reality) |\n| Spectro Cloud | Seed | 2019 | no | | [src](https://boldstart.vc/team/ed-sim/) |\n| Wallaroo | Seed, board (Form D) | 2018-05 | not stated | | ledger |\n| Snyk | first cheque | 2015 | yes | | [src](https://boldstart.vc/team/ed-sim/) |\n| Non-AI | Kustomer, Superhuman, SecurityScorecard, Front, Hypernative, Blockdaemon | | | | [src](https://boldstart.vc/team/ed-sim/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Protect AI:** knew Swanson from DataScience.com; co-led the first round with Acrew when Swanson left AWS; joined the board with Acrew's Mark Kraynak ([src](https://protectai.com/newsroom/protect-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-13.5m-funding-new-product)); the company sold to Palo Alto Networks (announced 2025-04, [src](https://boldstart.vc/news/wsj-boldstart-ventures-raises-250-million-ai-fund-looking-to-move-fast/)).\n- **Tessl:** third company with Snyk founder Guy Podjarny; boldstart's largest first cheque, $12.5M (existing ledger lead).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Eliot Durbin | co-GP on every fund filing | 2015 or earlier | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1649084/000164908416000004/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Ian Swanson | repeat founder relationship | pre-2022 | [src](https://boldstart.vc/news/protect-ai-mlsecops-shift-left-security-for-ai-ml-welcome-to-boldstart/) |\n| Guy Podjarny | three companies together (Snyk, Tessl) | 2015 | ledger |\n| Acrew Capital (Mark Kraynak) | co-lead on Protect AI and Keycard | 2022 | [src](https://protectai.com/newsroom/protect-ai-emerges-from-stealth-with-13.5m-funding-new-product) |\n| Tom Hulme (GV) | co-lead on Tessl | 2024 | ledger |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which asset is Boldstart Crow, LLC ($38.2M, 2026-05)?\n- Who are the 68 investors in Fund VII?\n- His podcast episodes have no transcripts in this repo; the Carta and Trends with Friends episodes are the ones to transcribe.\n\n## Sources\nSee [firms/boldstart-ventures.md](../firms/boldstart-ventures.md); all links above.\n", "person:elad-gil": "\n# Elad Gil\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. Sources use \"he\" for Gil ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/03/elad-gil-on-which-ai-markets-have-winners-and-which-are-still-wide-open/)). Builds on repo rows already merged for Cognition, Crosby, Periodic Labs, Serval, Harvey, Magic, Perplexity and Applied Compute (`data/rounds.csv`, `data/deals.csv`, `patterns/co-investor-gangs.md`). The fund sizes below come from SEC Form D filings (EDGAR full-text search for \"Elad Gil\"), not press.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the one-person capital platform of frontier AI. He was first money in Perplexity and early in Harvey, OpenAI, Anthropic, Mistral and Cognition. His SEC filings show a growth sleeve several times larger than his venture sleeve, which is how a solo GP came to lead Applied Compute's $350M round at $3.25B ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He was in the AI network before AI was consensus. He says he began investing in generative AI in 2021 because \"The step between 2 and 3 was so large\" (GPT-2 to GPT-3) ([TechCrunch, 2025-11-03](https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/03/elad-gil-on-which-ai-markets-have-winners-and-which-are-still-wide-open/)). He was also \"trolling AI researchers\" and talking about AI when few others were, so founders came to him: Aravind Srinivas cold-pinged him on LinkedIn while at OpenAI ([Tim Ferriss #863 transcript, 2026-04-30](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/)). His 2021 SEC filing for a small dedicated vehicle, \"Cosmic AI, LP\" ($15.2M, 3 investors, first sale 2021-06-02), dates that conviction with a primary source ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1864490/000186449021000002/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **The capital machine.** Each fund generation is paired: an \"Aleph\" vehicle, a larger \"Bet\" vehicle, and since 2024 a \"Bet ... Durable\" vehicle, all with Elad Gil as the only named person ([Fund IV Form Ds](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084728/000123191925000237/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - Fund IV (first sale 2025-09-10): Aleph 4 $400M, Bet 4 $2.0B and Bet 4 Durable $379M sold, 129 investors each.\n  - The investor base grew from 16 (Fund I, 2020) to 26 (2021) to 54 (2023) to 129 (2025).\n  - The Bet sleeve is 5x the Aleph sleeve. Reading Bet as the later-stage pool is inference, but it matches his leading growth rounds.\n- **What constrains him.**\n  - He is a single GP. A 36Kr piece relaying his podcast says his team has 4 investment staff without partner status plus 6 ops and finance people ([36Kr, 2025-12-01](https://eu.36kr.com/en/p/3576438059858824)). Bandwidth is the limit, which pushes him toward concentrated, later checks.\n  - His public view is that new foundation labs are hard to fund: \"it is unlikely that many new core LLM companies will get started\" ([AI Market Clarity, 2025-07-22](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/ai-market-clarity)).\n- **What he'd never do.**\n  - He avoids \"science projects\": \"I've largely avoided those things\" (quantum and similar) ([Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/)).\n  - He diligences market before team (\"I index a lot on market early\"), about 90% of the time ([Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/)).\n  - Exceptions are people-first bets like Perplexity. Periodic Labs (2025 seed) is a frontier-science bet he joined as one angel among many, not as lead ([periodic.com](https://periodic.com/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **A Fund V is already filed: $3.76B across three vehicles, nothing sold yet.** On 2026-08-24 he filed Form Ds for Cosmic - Aleph 5 ($460M), Bet 5 ($2.7B) and Bet 5 Durable ($600M). All three show first sale \"yet to occur\" and $0 sold ([Aleph 5](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2150243/000123191926001058/primary_doc.xml), [Bet 5](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2150244/000123191926001057/primary_doc.xml), [Bet 5 Durable](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2150245/000123191926001056/primary_doc.xml)). No press coverage of Fund V was found.\n- **Fund IV closed at $2.779B, not the \"$1.5B\" first reported.** The Form Ds show $400M + $2.0B + $379M sold, first sale 2025-09-10 ([Aleph 4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084729/000123191925000234/primary_doc.xml), [Bet 4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084728/000123191925000237/primary_doc.xml), [Bet 4 Durable](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084730/000123191925000241/primary_doc.xml)). Press had reported a $1.5B-to-$2B raise, later a $3B target ([36Kr summary](https://eu.36kr.com/en/p/3576438059858824)).\n- **Fund III is confirmed at $1.095B by filing.** The first Aleph 3 filing (2022-04-13) offered $820M with $0 sold. The 2023-11-03 amendment shows $1.095B sold to 54 investors ([2022](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1922165/000192216522000002/primary_doc.xml), [2023 amendment](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1922165/000192216523000001/primary_doc.xml)). That matches 36Kr's \"third fund $1.095B.\"\n- **A \"Durable\" pool appeared in 2024.** Bet 3 Durable sold $680M to 34 investors, first sale 2024-09-04 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2034543/000203454324000002/primary_doc.xml)). Business Insider's \"raised $1 billion\" story ran on 2024-10-24 ([BI via AOL](https://www.aol.com/elad-gil-raised-1-billion-090003323.html)). The timing fits, but the amounts don't reconcile, so the link is unconfirmed.\n- **Large single-deal SPVs with very few LPs continue.**\n  - Cosmic Par1: $250.2M from 2 investors, 2022-01 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1888825/000188882522000003/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - Cosmic 20253: $75M from 4 investors, 2025-10 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2074851/000123191925000447/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - Cosmic 20261: $49M from 4 investors, 2026-03 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2126899/000123191926000342/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - The underlying companies are not named in the filings.\n- **The SPV habit started because he ran out of personal money in Stripe.** \"I first invested in Stripe when it was eight people.\" His first SPV was around Stripe's Series C, followed by Instacart and Coinbase SPVs ([Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/)).\n- **His biggest regret is under-sizing winners.** He says he is \"really upset with myself for not being more aggressive\" on companies he was already in ([Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/)). This explains why he now leads late rounds in his own early names: Harvey B co-lead, then the Applied Compute $350M lead.\n- **Cash audits at later stage.** He says his fund \"actually does cash reconciliations\" on later-stage deals, but he collapses diligence to one or two core questions ([Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/)).\n- **Perplexity origin.** Aravind Srinivas cold-pinged him on LinkedIn while at OpenAI. They then met every two weeks, and Srinivas would build what they had discussed within a week ([Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/)). Gil and Nat Friedman co-led the seed ([Perplexity](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/announcing-our-series-a-funding-round-and-mobile-app-launch)).\n- **Brain Co is an incubation with Jared Kushner.** Josh Kushner (Thrive) introduced Jared Kushner and Gil in 2023. The idea came from a February 2024 meeting in San Francisco with Luis Videgaray. Affinity Partners and Gil Capital co-led a $30M Series A. Gil and Kushner now refer deals to each other as Affinity moves into AI ([Forbes ES, 2025-09-11](https://forbes.es/empresas/795587/como-la-nueva-startup-de-jared-kushner-esta-ayudando-a-las-empresas-a-integrar-la-ia-con-exito/), [Ynet](https://www.ynetnews.com/business/article/rky2jx4ixg)).\n- **He calls the compute ceiling an oligopoly enforcer.** He wrote that \"no one lab is able to break significantly ahead until 2028 at the soonest\" ([Random thoughts, 2026-04-20](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/random-thoughts-while-gazing-at-the)). He also says most AI companies should consider selling while valuations are high: \"many should if they can while everything is on the upswing\" (OpenAI and Anthropic are the exceptions).\n- **His frame for the AI researcher pay spike.** \"The top AI researchers became post-economic all at once\" ([same post](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/random-thoughts-while-gazing-at-the)). This bears on how new labs recruit.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.**\n- He backs frontier labs (OpenAI, Anthropic, Mistral, Magic, Periodic Labs) and AI-native automation (Cognition, Decagon, Serval, Applied Compute, Harvey, Crosby).\n- He is first money often enough to matter: the Perplexity seed co-lead, the Harvey seed (2022-11), and Applied Compute from launch.\n- He is the most likely co-investor in, or competitor for, a large lab seed. He came into Periodic's $300M seed as an angel.\n- His stated view that few new LLM companies will start is a contrarian signal against my $600M-seed-for-new-labs thesis. Expect him to pass on, or angel only into, new pre-training labs unless the team is exceptional.\n- As an LP he is not a fit (no LP commitments found). As a co-investor and source of deals he is high value.\n\n## Background\n- PhD from MIT; degrees in math and biology ([eladgil.com](https://eladgil.com)).\n- Google: worked on AI and mobile, around the Android acquisition (2005). Left in 2007 ([36Kr](https://eu.36kr.com/en/p/3576438059858824), [eladgil.com](https://eladgil.com)).\n- Co-founded Mixer Labs, which Twitter acquired. Then VP at Twitter, where he ran product and strategy work ([eladgil.com](https://eladgil.com)).\n- Co-founded Color (Color Genomics, later Color Health). Form Ds under that name run 2016-2021 ([EDGAR](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1683327/000168332721000002/primary_doc.xml)).\n- Angel investing from about 2009: early Airbnb, Stripe (at eight people), Coinbase, Instacart, Square, Pinterest, Figma, Notion ([36Kr](https://eu.36kr.com/en/p/3576438059858824), [Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/)).\n- Wrote the *High Growth Handbook*. He is writing a \"zero to one\" sequel ([Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/)).\n- Pre-fund vehicles named for him appear on EDGAR: Dihedral Group (2016), Trihedral Group (2019), Elad Investments LP Series 1 (2019) ([EDGAR search](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Elad%20Gil%22&forms=D)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://eladgil.com | Bio, portfolio lists (AI, past, founded/incubated), checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://blog.eladgil.com | \"Elad Blog\"; about 2 to 4 long posts a year in 2025-26 |\n| X | https://x.com/eladgil | Linked from his site; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | His site does not link one; LinkedIn blocks fetch |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | No Priors: https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/no-priors-artificial-intelligence-technology-startups/id1668002688 | Co-hosts with Sarah Guo (Conviction). nopriors.com now redirects to a parked lander (2026-11-10) |\n| Book | https://growth.eladgil.com | High Growth Handbook |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-04 — The Tim Ferriss Show #863 — \"Consigliere to Empire Builders\" — [transcript](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/). Covers the SPV origin at Stripe, market-first diligence (90%), the Perplexity LinkedIn intro, Anduril via Trae Stephens, regret at under-sizing, and cash audits.\n- 2025-11 — TechCrunch Disrupt stage — [write-up](https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/03/elad-gil-on-which-ai-markets-have-winners-and-which-are-still-wide-open/). Says he started in gen AI in 2021. \"AI was the one market where the more I learn, the less I know.\"\n- 2025-09 — No Priors with Jared Kushner — \"BrainCo, Affinity Partners, and the Geopolitics of AI\" ([Pocket Casts listing](https://pocketcasts.com/podcast/no-priors-artificial-intelligence-technology-startups/b87d28b0-81ba-013b-f2c6-0acc26574db2/jared-kushner-brainco-affinity-partners-and-the-geopolitics-of-ai/8503d3d2-47d7-4caa-ab88-5d94e376322a)). Transcript not read.\n- 2025 — Sourcery with Jared Kushner and Eric Wu on Brain Co ([listing](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/elad-gil-jared-kushner-and-eric-wu)). Not read.\n- 2025-04 — Uncapped with Jack Altman, #6 ([listing](https://metacast.app/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/jR35iX38/uncapped-6-elad-gil/ei8ITJ50)). Not read; chapters cover leverage on time.\n\n## How they think\n- **Market first, team second, about 90% of the time.** Great teams get \"crushed by terrible markets\" ([Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/)).\n- **The \"GPT ladder\".** Each model step opens new markets, so build against customers before the model is good enough: \"Building early against customers pre-model fidelity allows you to capture market share\" ([AI Market Clarity](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/ai-market-clarity)).\n- **AI sells labor, not seats.** \"AI is about selling units of labor online\" ([Random thoughts](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/random-thoughts-while-gazing-at-the)). This is why he backs Decagon, Harvey, Serval, Crosby and Applied Compute.\n- **Settled markets (2025).**\n  - LLMs: a handful of players.\n  - Code: Cursor, Cognition, with Magic an \"outlier\".\n  - Legal: Harvey.\n  - Scribing: Abridge.\n  - Support: Decagon and Sierra.\n  - Search: Perplexity.\n  - Open markets: accounting, compliance, financial tools, sales agents and security, where \"I and my team are highly interested in investing\" ([AI Market Clarity](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/ai-market-clarity)).\n- **Exit timing.** Most AI companies should consider exits in the next 12 to 18 months; OpenAI and Anthropic should not ([Random thoughts](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/random-thoughts-while-gazing-at-the)).\n- **Calls he got right.** GPT-3-era conviction (2021). Perplexity first round. Harvey seed 2022-11, before GPT-4. Defense (Anduril) after Google dropped Maven. Calls that were wrong: none sourced.\n- **Geography.** SF is the GenAI \"super cluster\", with about 91% of AI private market cap per his team's analysis ([blog, 2026-04-16](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/unicorn-market-cap-2026-sf-is-the), [Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [AI Market Clarity (2025-07-22)](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/ai-market-clarity): which AI markets have settled, where he is hunting, and the GPT ladder.\n2. [Random thoughts while gazing at the misty AI Frontier (2026-04-20)](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/random-thoughts-while-gazing-at-the): the compute-enforced oligopoly, his advice to exit now, and the post-economic researcher.\n3. [Tim Ferriss #863 transcript (2026-04-30)](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/): the most candid account of his process, SPVs and regrets.\n4. [Things I Don't Know About AI (2024-02-21)](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/things-i-dont-know-about-ai): his open questions on lab and app market structure.\n5. [TechCrunch Disrupt write-up (2025-11-03)](https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/03/elad-gil-on-which-ai-markets-have-winners-and-which-are-still-wide-open/): his market map, as said on stage.\n\n## Social engagement\n- His X is linked from his site and posts invite founders to \"ping\" him. Reply and amplification patterns were not analysed; follower count not checked.\n- No Priors with Sarah Guo is his main recurring channel. Guests include lab founders and Jared Kushner.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Perplexity | Seed $3.1M | 2022-09 | Co-lead with Nat Friedman | Karpathy, LeCun, Vaswani, Masad, Delangue | [Perplexity](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/announcing-our-series-a-funding-round-and-mobile-app-launch) |\n| Perplexity | A $25.6M; B $73.6M | 2023-03; 2024-01 | No (returning) | not checked | [A](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/announcing-our-series-a-funding-round-and-mobile-app-launch), [B](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-raises-series-b-funding-round) |\n| Harvey | Seed $5M | 2022-11-23 | No | OpenAI Startup Fund (lead), Jeff Dean | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2022/11/23/harvey-which-uses-ai-to-answer-legal-questions-lands-cash-from-openai/) |\n| Harvey | B $80M at $715M | 2023-12-19 | Co-lead with Kleiner Perkins | not checked | [Bloomberg Law](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/artificial-intelligence/legal-tech-harvey-ai-valued-at-715-million-in-latest-funding) |\n| Harvey | C through growth ($15.5B) | 2024-07 to 2026-09 | No | Kleiner + Coatue co-led E | [E](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-e), [2026](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-dollar550m-at-a-dollar155b-valuation-to-help-legal-teams-own-their-intelligence) |\n| Magic | A $23M; B $117M; $320M | 2023-02; 2024-02; 2024-08 | No | Magic's posts name no lead; press attributions conflict (see `data/rounds.csv`) | [A](https://magic.dev/blog/series-a), [2024-08](https://magic.dev/blog/100m-token-context-windows) |\n| Mistral AI | Series A (EUR385M), per aggregator lists only | 2023-12 | No | a16z (lead), Lightspeed | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2023/12/11/mistral-ai-a-paris-based-openai-rival-closed-its-415-million-funding-round/); also listed on [eladgil.com](https://eladgil.com) |\n| Contextual AI | Seed $20M | 2023-06-07 | No | BCV (lead), Lightspeed, Sarah Guo | [BCV](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/) |\n| Cognition | Supporter at Devin launch; >$400M; D | 2024-03; 2025-09; 2026-05 | No | Founders Fund; Lux, GC, 8VC | [launch](https://cognition.com/blog/introducing-devin), [D](https://cognition.com/blog/series-d) |\n| HeyGen | A $60M | 2024-06 | No | Benchmark (lead), Thrive, Conviction | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/) |\n| Braintrust | Founded/incubated per his site; A; B $80M | 2024-10; 2026-02-17 | No | a16z (A lead), ICONIQ (B lead), Greylock | [site](https://eladgil.com), [B](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/announcing-series-b) |\n| Decagon | B $65M | 2024-10-15 | No | BCV (lead), A*, Accel, BOND, ACME | [B](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-b) |\n| Decagon | D $250M at $4.5B | 2026-01-28 | No | Coatue + Index (co-leads), a16z, A*, Accel, BCV | [D](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-d-announcement) |\n| Brain Co | Series A $30M | 2025-09 | Co-lead (Gil Capital) with Affinity Partners | Brian Armstrong, Patrick Collison, Reid Hoffman, Nikesh Arora | [Forbes ES](https://forbes.es/empresas/795587/como-la-nueva-startup-de-jared-kushner-esta-ayudando-a-las-empresas-a-integrar-la-ia-con-exito/) |\n| Periodic Labs | Seed $300M (angel) | 2025-09-30 | No | a16z (lead), Felicis, Jeff Bezos, Eric Schmidt, Jeff Dean | [periodic.com](https://periodic.com/) |\n| Crosby | A $20M; B $60M | 2025-10-08; 2026-03-31 | No | Index, BCV, Sequoia, Lux | [A](https://crosby.ai/blog/series-a-and-faster-agreements), [B](https://crosby.ai/blog/series-b-planting-our-flag) |\n| Applied Compute | Launch backer; B $80M; growth $350M at $3.25B | 2025-10-29; 2026-04-08; 2026-11-02 | Led the $350M | Benchmark, Sequoia, Lux, Kleiner, NVIDIA, Blackstone | [launch](https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific), [$350M](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise) |\n| Serval | C $150M at $3B (existing) | 2026-10-29 | No | Sequoia, GC, Redpoint, Conviction | [SaaS News](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/serval-raises-150m-series-c/) |\n| OpenAI, Anthropic, Character, Abridge, Cerebras, Waymo, SpaceX, Anduril, Scale AI | rounds not sourced | | | | listed on [eladgil.com](https://eladgil.com) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Perplexity (2022).** Aravind Srinivas, then at OpenAI, messaged him on LinkedIn after hearing Gil was one of the few people talking about AI. They met every two weeks before he invested. He calls it a \"people first\" exception to his market-first rule ([Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/)). He co-led the seed with Nat Friedman ([Perplexity](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/announcing-our-series-a-funding-round-and-mobile-app-launch)). He was first money.\n- **Harvey.** He was in the 2022-11 seed behind the OpenAI Startup Fund ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2022/11/23/harvey-which-uses-ai-to-answer-legal-questions-lands-cash-from-openai/)), then co-led the B with Kleiner ([Bloomberg Law](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/artificial-intelligence/legal-tech-harvey-ai-valued-at-715-million-in-latest-funding)). He says he funded Harvey because they were \"the only people doing anything\" in that market ([Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/)). A 36Kr piece says he claimed to have led Harvey's 2025 rounds. Harvey's own Series E post names Kleiner and Coatue as co-leads with Gil as a participant ([Harvey](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-e)), so treat the 36Kr claim as unconfirmed.\n- **Applied Compute.** He was a backer at launch (2025-10), continued in Kleiner's $1.3B round, then led the $350M at $3.25B ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise)). That is angel to lead in 13 months, the clearest example of his growth sleeve at work.\n- **Anduril.** After Google dropped Maven he went looking for a defense company, and met Trae Stephens at a brunch ([Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/)).\n- **Stripe.** He emailed Patrick Collison after selling Mixer Labs to Twitter and invested at eight people ([Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/)).\n- **Brain Co.** He co-founded and incubated it with Jared Kushner and Luis Videgaray after Josh Kushner's 2023 introduction ([Forbes ES](https://forbes.es/empresas/795587/como-la-nueva-startup-de-jared-kushner-esta-ayudando-a-las-empresas-a-integrar-la-ia-con-exito/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sarah Guo (Conviction) | No Priors co-host; co-angel in Contextual AI seed ([BCV](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/)) | start date not confirmed | [Apple Podcasts](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/no-priors-artificial-intelligence-technology-startups/id1668002688) |\n| Nat Friedman | Co-led Perplexity seed | 2022 | [Perplexity](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/announcing-our-series-a-funding-round-and-mobile-app-launch) |\n| Aravind Srinivas (Perplexity) | Cold LinkedIn intro, then biweekly meetings and first check | 2022 | [Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/) |\n| Kleiner Perkins | Co-led Harvey B | 2023-12 | [Bloomberg Law](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/artificial-intelligence/legal-tech-harvey-ai-valued-at-715-million-in-latest-funding) |\n| Jared Kushner / Affinity Partners | Brain Co co-founders; co-led its A; refer deals to each other | 2023 (met); 2024-02 (idea) | [Forbes ES](https://forbes.es/empresas/795587/como-la-nueva-startup-de-jared-kushner-esta-ayudando-a-las-empresas-a-integrar-la-ia-con-exito/) |\n| Josh Kushner (Thrive) | Introduced Gil to Jared Kushner | 2023 | [Forbes ES](https://forbes.es/empresas/795587/como-la-nueva-startup-de-jared-kushner-esta-ayudando-a-las-empresas-a-integrar-la-ia-con-exito/) |\n| Eric Wu (Opendoor) | Brain Co co-founder and chairman | 2023 | [Forbes ES](https://forbes.es/empresas/795587/como-la-nueva-startup-de-jared-kushner-esta-ayudando-a-las-empresas-a-integrar-la-ia-con-exito/) |\n| Trae Stephens (Anduril, Founders Fund) | Anduril intro | not dated | [Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/) |\n| Patrick Collison (Stripe) | Gil invested at eight people; Collison is a co-angel in Brain Co and Crosby | ~2010-11 (not dated) | [Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/) |\n| Tim Ferriss | Host; says they talk about investing \"a lot\" | 2026-04 (episode) | [Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/) |\n| Shreyan (surname unknown) | Team member who ran the unicorn market-cap analysis | not dated | [Ferriss](https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Who are the 129 LPs in Fund IV?** No LP is named in any source read. Checked: Form Ds (which name no LPs), the BI fund story, the 36Kr summary, one LP-focused web search, and `lps/`. Look next at public pension and university tables (UTIMCO, UC) for \"Cosmic\" vehicles, and at 990-PF schedules.\n- **Is Gil himself an LP in other funds (Conviction, emerging AI managers)?** Nothing found, so no `-lp.csv` was written.\n- **What do Aleph, Bet and Durable each mean?** Early vs. later vs. long-hold is my inference.\n- **Which companies sit behind Cosmic Par1 ($250.2M, 2 LPs, 2022-01), Cosmic 20253 ($75M, 2025-10) and Cosmic 20261 ($49M, 2026-03)?**\n- **Firm naming:** Gil&Co, Gil Capital and Cosmic. Is there a registered adviser (Form ADV)?\n- **Entry dates for OpenAI, Anthropic, Serval, Cognition and Mistral.** Serval matters most for my lens.\n- **Has Fund V started selling (first close)?**\n\n## Sources\n- https://eladgil.com\n- https://blog.eladgil.com/p/ai-market-clarity\n- https://blog.eladgil.com/p/random-thoughts-while-gazing-at-the\n- https://blog.eladgil.com/p/things-i-dont-know-about-ai\n- https://blog.eladgil.com/p/unicorn-market-cap-2026-sf-is-the\n- https://tim.blog/2026/04/30/elad-gil-transcript/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/03/elad-gil-on-which-ai-markets-have-winners-and-which-are-still-wide-open/\n- https://www.aol.com/elad-gil-raised-1-billion-090003323.html\n- https://eu.36kr.com/en/p/3576438059858824 (secondary; relays The Information and his podcast)\n- https://forbes.es/empresas/795587/como-la-nueva-startup-de-jared-kushner-esta-ayudando-a-las-empresas-a-integrar-la-ia-con-exito/\n- https://www.ynetnews.com/business/article/rky2jx4ixg\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/no-priors-artificial-intelligence-technology-startups/id1668002688\n- SEC Form D filings: Aleph 1 https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1807070/000180707020000002/primary_doc.xml ; Aleph 2 https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1874224/000187422421000002/primary_doc.xml ; Aleph 3 https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1922165/000192216522000002/primary_doc.xml and https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1922165/000192216523000001/primary_doc.xml ; Bet 3 Durable https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2034543/000203454324000002/primary_doc.xml ; Fund IV https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084729/000123191925000234/primary_doc.xml , https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084728/000123191925000237/primary_doc.xml , https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084730/000123191925000241/primary_doc.xml ; Fund V https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2150243/000123191926001058/primary_doc.xml , https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2150244/000123191926001057/primary_doc.xml , https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2150245/000123191926001056/primary_doc.xml ; Cosmic AI https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1864490/000186449021000002/primary_doc.xml ; Cosmic Par1 https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1888825/000188882522000003/primary_doc.xml ; Cosmic 20253 https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2074851/000123191925000447/primary_doc.xml ; Cosmic 20261 https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2126899/000123191926000342/primary_doc.xml\n- Company posts: Perplexity, Harvey, Magic, Cognition, Decagon, Braintrust, Crosby, Applied Compute, Periodic (linked inline)\n", "person:elena-sakach": "\n# Elena Sakach\n\n## One-line take\nShe is GV's newest partner and a growth-minded fintech and AI-apps investor from Coatue. Crunchbase News credits her with \"helping lead\" GV's stake in humans&, the $480M-seed frontier lab, so she is GV's one sub-GP partner with a lab on her list. No source shows she sourced it or that GV led.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** a private-equity and crossover training (Goldman TMT, TPG software, Coatue) and a \"compounding business\" lens. She says AI has weakened technology as a moat and that defensibility now sits in data, distribution, customer relationships and research talent ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp)). That frame suits large rounds in AI-native automation (Tennr, Basis) and makes her comfortable with big-ticket AI.\n- **What constrains her:** she is a partner, not a GP, at a single-LP (Alphabet) fund. Crunchbase says she focuses on growth-stage companies ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp)). Every round tied to her that can be dated shows GV as a participant, never the lead: humans& seed (SV Angel and Georges Harik co-led) ([src](https://humansand.ai/)) and the Tennr Series C (IVP led) ([src](https://fortune.com/2025/06/18/tennr-health-tech-ai-patient-referral-ivp-a16z-lightspeed-iconiq-series-c/)).\n- **What she'd never do (per her own words):** fund a small idea just because the round is big. She said \"You cannot make a small idea large simply by investing more capital\" ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The humans& credit is secondary and soft.** Crunchbase's author, not Sakach, writes that she \"helped lead the firm's investments in high-profile startups such as Humans&\" ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp)). The article never discusses humans& with her. Her GV profile lists humans& with no role ([src](https://www.gv.com/team/elena-sakach)). GV is a named participant, not a lead ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/20/humans-a-human-centric-ai-startup-founded-by-anthropic-xai-google-alums-raised-480m-seed-round/)).\n- **Stripe, Plaid and Gusto predate her.** GV's own welcome post (2024-05-20) already lists Stripe, Monzo, Plaid, Gusto, GoCardless and Ethos as GV portfolio ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/welcoming-elena-sakach)). The list comes from a fetch summary of the post; the exact wording is unverified. So her Stripe and Plaid credits must be follow-ons or inherited board-adjacent coverage, not origination. Crunchbase's \"helped lead ... Stripe\" overstates it.\n- **Tennr is the cleanest datable deal in her tenure.** GV came in as a new investor in Tennr's $101M Series C at $605M (led by IVP, 2025-06-18), about 13 months after she joined ([src](https://fortune.com/2025/06/18/tennr-health-tech-ai-patient-referral-ivp-a16z-lightspeed-iconiq-series-c/)). Fortune does not name her. Her own line, that healthcare automation \"enables providers to serve more patients\", reads like the Tennr memo ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp)).\n- **Her barbell.** In fintech she sees two kinds of opportunity: \"very early, highly novel ideas, often AI-driven\" and late-stage compounders with strong retention ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp)). That explains a book that mixes humans& (very early, novel) with Stripe and Ramp (late compounders), with little in between.\n- **Moat view.** \"AI has reduced technology as a durable moat,\" she said, and \"The dividing line is roughly pre- and post-ChatGPT\" ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp)). Pre-ChatGPT incumbents must replatform. She prefers companies built on the newer architecture from day one.\n- **Valuation stance.** \"The critical question is not valuation alone\" ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp)). She separates today's AI from 2021 because AI is a real platform shift. She is not a price-disciplined holdout, so she is more likely to engage with a $4B-plus lab seed.\n- **GV recruited her in.** The welcome post was co-signed by Dave Munichiello and Tom Hulme. It says she was promoted at Coatue in under a year and that she launched a banking career \"by cold-calling her way into Wall Street\" ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/welcoming-elena-sakach)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:** humans& is exactly my kind of deal: a $480M seed at $4.48B into a three-month-old lab from Anthropic, xAI and Google alumni ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/20/humans-a-human-centric-ai-startup-founded-by-anthropic-xai-google-alums-raised-480m-seed-round/)). Tennr and Basis are AI-native automation, the Serval-like bucket in THESIS.md. Her valuation tolerance and her \"very early, highly novel\" bucket match giant-seed co-investing.\n- **Against:** she is not first in. GV followed SV Angel and Harik on humans& and IVP on Tennr. Her core is fintech and growth. No sourced evidence ties her to pre-training, chips or compute. Her humans& role is a secondary-source claim.\n- **Use her as:** a growth-stage GV contact for a lab's next round or for AI-automation co-invests, not a first-money partner.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** humans&, Tennr, Basis.\n\n## Background\n- From the Midwest ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/welcoming-elena-sakach)). B.B.A. in Finance, William & Mary ([src](https://www.gv.com/team/elena-sakach)). Graduation year not found.\n- Goldman Sachs, Technology, Media and Telecom investment banking. Dates not found ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp)).\n- TPG, software and fintech investing across buyouts and minority deals (TPG's software or tech investing practice). Dates not found ([src](https://www.gv.com/team/elena-sakach)).\n- Coatue from 2021, investing from seed through public markets. GV says she became a partner there in under a year and led fintech and software deals ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp), [src](https://www.gv.com/news/welcoming-elena-sakach)). No Coatue deals named in any source found.\n- GV Partner, San Francisco, from 2024-05 (announced 2024-05-20) ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/welcoming-elena-sakach)).\n- `fast_promotion: unknown` because her Goldman start date isn't sourced. The Coatue promotion was fast, but she arrived there as an experienced investor.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.gv.com/team/elena-sakach | GV profile |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/elena_sakach | Linked from GV profile; could not fetch (HTTP 402), 2026-11-10 |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/elenasakach/ | Linked from GV profile; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-02 — Crunchbase News interview (print, not podcast) — \"How This GV Investor Looks For The Next Stripe...\" — [link](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp) — the moat and barbell quotes above.\n- No podcast or video found. Web search was exhausted for this session, so this needs another pass.\n\n## How they think\n- **Compounding businesses:** she backs products that get more valuable the more customers use them. Strong fintech has trust, room for upsell and cross-sell, and an infrastructure role. She cites Stripe and Monzo ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp)).\n- **Generalist identity:** \"I consider myself an investor first\" ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp)).\n- **AI defensibility** moves to data, distribution, relationships and research talent ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp)).\n- **AI that expands capacity beats AI that cuts cost.** She called automation inside financial institutions a large opportunity ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp)).\n- **Her diligence checklist:** market scale, tailwinds, \"why now\", team, and whether the company can change a whole industry ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp)).\n- **Calls right or wrong:** too early to judge. Tennr is up from GV's entry at $605M only if a later round exists, and none was found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Crunchbase News interview, 2026-02-26: her whole framework in one place ([link](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp)).\n2. GV welcome post, 2024-05-20: who recruited her and her intended mandate ([link](https://www.gv.com/news/welcoming-elena-sakach)).\n3. GV team profile: her current deal list ([link](https://www.gv.com/team/elena-sakach)).\n4. humans& seed coverage: the round she is credited on ([link](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/20/humans-a-human-centric-ai-startup-founded-by-anthropic-xai-google-alums-raised-480m-seed-round/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X was not fetchable (HTTP 402) and LinkedIn was not fetched.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| humans& | Seed, $480M at $4.48B (GV a participant; her round not stated in the Crunchbase article) | 2026-01-20 | No (SV Angel and Georges Harik co-led); Crunchbase says she \"helped lead\" GV's investment | NVIDIA, Jeff Bezos, Emerson Collective, Forerunner, Felicis, CRV, Conviction, others | [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp), [src](https://humansand.ai/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/20/humans-a-human-centric-ai-startup-founded-by-anthropic-xai-google-alums-raised-480m-seed-round/) |\n| Tennr | Series C, $101M at $605M (GV a new investor; her role not named by Fortune) | 2025-06-18 | No (IVP led) | IVP, ICONIQ, a16z, Lightspeed | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/06/18/tennr-health-tech-ai-patient-referral-ivp-a16z-lightspeed-iconiq-series-c/), [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp) |\n| Basis | Round not stated | n/a | Crunchbase says \"helped lead\" | n/a | [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp) |\n| Ramp | Round not stated | n/a | Crunchbase says \"helped lead\" | n/a | [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp) |\n| Stripe | Round not stated (already in GV portfolio before she joined) | n/a | Crunchbase says \"helped lead\" | n/a | [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp), [src](https://www.gv.com/news/welcoming-elena-sakach) |\n| Plaid | Round not stated (already in GV portfolio before she joined) | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/elena-sakach), [src](https://www.gv.com/news/welcoming-elena-sakach) |\n| Gusto | Round not stated (already in GV portfolio before she joined) | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/elena-sakach), [src](https://www.gv.com/news/welcoming-elena-sakach) |\n| Wealth.com | Round not stated | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/elena-sakach) |\n| Blockaid | Round not stated | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/elena-sakach) |\n| Waymo | Round not stated (Alphabet company) | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/elena-sakach) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **humans& (standout).**\n  - *What happened:* humans& announced a $480M seed at a $4.48B valuation on 2026-01-20, about three months after founding. The founders are Andi Peng (ex-Anthropic), Georges Harik (Google's seventh employee), Eric Zelikman and Yuchen He (ex-xAI) and Noah Goodman (Stanford) ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/20/humans-a-human-centric-ai-startup-founded-by-anthropic-xai-google-alums-raised-480m-seed-round/)).\n  - *Who was there:* SV Angel and Harik co-led ([src](https://humansand.ai/)). TechCrunch names \"VC firms SV Angel, GV\" alongside NVIDIA and Jeff Bezos. The Google-alumni circle around Harik also invested: GV founder Bill Maris personally, Marissa Mayer, Anne Wojcicki and Zak Stone ([src](https://humansand.ai/)).\n  - *How GV got in:* not documented. The Harik-Google tie is the obvious path, but no source says Sakach found or ran it. Crunchbase's \"helped lead\" (2026-02-26, secondary) is the only credit.\n  - *What it's worth now:* no later round found. Search was exhausted in this session, so this is unconfirmed. GV's check size is unknown.\n- **Tennr.** GV was one of two new investors, with ICONIQ, in the IVP-led $101M Series C at $605M on 2025-06-18 ([src](https://fortune.com/2025/06/18/tennr-health-tech-ai-patient-referral-ivp-a16z-lightspeed-iconiq-series-c/)). Foundation Capital (Joanne Chen) led Tennr's earlier $3.25M seed; date not shown ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/news)). Sakach's role is per Crunchbase only.\n- **Basis, Ramp, Stripe:** no deal stories found.\n- **How she sources:** no source describes it. Her pattern (participant in rounds led by others, plus follow-ons in existing GV fintech names) suggests access through GV's platform and existing relationships rather than first-check origination. That is an inference, not sourced.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| GV | Partner | 2024-05 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/welcoming-elena-sakach) |\n| Dave Munichiello | GV colleague; co-signed her welcome post | 2024-05 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/welcoming-elena-sakach) |\n| Tom Hulme | GV colleague; co-signed her welcome post | 2024-05 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/welcoming-elena-sakach) |\n| Coatue | Former partner | 2021–2024 | [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp) |\n| TPG | Former investor (software and fintech) | n/a | [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp) |\n| Goldman Sachs | Former TMT investment banker | n/a | [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp) |\n| SV Angel; Georges Harik | Co-investors (leads) in humans& seed | 2026-01 | [src](https://humansand.ai/) |\n| IVP | Co-investor (lead) in Tennr Series C | 2025-06 | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/06/18/tennr-health-tech-ai-patient-referral-ivp-a16z-lightspeed-iconiq-series-c/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did she personally run GV's humans& check, and how large was it? Who introduced GV: Harik, Maris or someone else?\n- Has humans& raised again since 2026-01? What is the current mark?\n- Which Basis and Ramp rounds did GV join under her, and when?\n- Wealth.com and Blockaid: round and year.\n- Any podcasts or X threads (X was not fetchable). Search budget was exhausted this session.\n- Her title and promotion history at Coatue (only \"promoted in under one year\" is sourced).\n\n## Sources\n- https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/gv-fintech-ai-startup-investor-sakach-stripe-ramp (secondary, 2026-02-26)\n- https://www.gv.com/team/elena-sakach\n- https://www.gv.com/news/welcoming-elena-sakach (2024-05-20)\n- https://humansand.ai/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/20/humans-a-human-centric-ai-startup-founded-by-anthropic-xai-google-alums-raised-480m-seed-round/\n- https://fortune.com/2025/06/18/tennr-health-tech-ai-patient-referral-ivp-a16z-lightspeed-iconiq-series-c/\n", "person:ellen-chisa": "\n# Ellen Chisa\n\n## One-line take\nA founder-turned-partner at a $250M seed fund who is attached to Generalist AI from its first round; the rising name at boldstart for anything technical and pre-product.\n\n## Crux\nShe was a boldstart portfolio founder before she was an investor there, so she sells founders on having been on their side of the table. Her remit is pre-product, developer-focused companies. Generalist shows she can reach a robotics lab through a personal relationship. The limit is the firm's size: follow-on firepower comes from vehicles she does not control.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Hired from the firm's own cap table: she joined \"after having worked with the team as a founder at Dark.\" ([boldstart bio](https://boldstart.vc/team/ellen-chisa/)).\n- Generalist: the firm's unsigned post says its author has known co-founder Andy Barry for nearly two decades and that the founders came to boldstart; she is the partner pictured with the team ([src](https://boldstart.vc/news/generalistai-when-robots-start-to-improvise-welcome-to-boldstart/)). Whether she is the author is not confirmed.\n- Board seat: the existing ledger row gives her the Generalist board seat from an aggregator; Form D-based data lists only the three founders as directors (search snippet of Seedtable, not opened). Treat the seat as unconfirmed.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. A partner at a fund that can write up to $15M, with the first cheque in a robotics foundation-model company that later raised $400M. She is the person to know at boldstart for research-founder companies.\n\n## Background\nEarly product roles at Microsoft (first cross-platform Office Mobile), Kickstarter and Lola; then co-founder and CEO of Dark, a boldstart-backed programming language and editor; joined boldstart in January 2021 ([src](https://boldstart.vc/team/ellen-chisa/)). Education not on the bio.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://boldstart.vc/team/ellen-chisa/ | firm bio |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/ellenchisa | not fetched |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/ellenchisa/ | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found in this pass (one Rosenfeld Media \"Design in Product\" listing surfaced in search but was not opened).\n\n## How they think\n- Her bio describes the job as investing in and supporting founders building \"pre-product, dev-focused, enterprise companies\" ([src](https://boldstart.vc/team/ellen-chisa/)).\n- No essays or interviews found; this section is thin.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Generalist welcome post](https://boldstart.vc/news/generalistai-when-robots-start-to-improvise-welcome-to-boldstart/): the firm's account of the deal she is attached to.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed (X not fetched).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Generalist AI | Seed (inception) | 2024-03 (month from aggregator) | co-led | NVIDIA (co-lead); Spark Capital, NFDG, Bezos Expeditions | [src](https://boldstart.vc/news/generalistai-when-robots-start-to-improvise-welcome-to-boldstart/) |\n\nNo other deal is attributed to her by name in any source found.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Generalist AI:** Pete Florence, Andy Zeng and Andy Barry told boldstart the time was right for a general-purpose robot; boldstart co-led the inception round with NVIDIA roughly two years before the April 2026 post, and the company had raised $140M by then ([src](https://boldstart.vc/news/generalistai-when-robots-start-to-improvise-welcome-to-boldstart/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ed Sim, Eliot Durbin | her investors at Dark, now her partners | pre-2021 | [src](https://boldstart.vc/team/ellen-chisa/) |\n| Andy Barry (Generalist) | long personal relationship with the post's author (possibly her) | about 2006 | [src](https://boldstart.vc/news/generalistai-when-robots-start-to-improvise-welcome-to-boldstart/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is she the author of the Generalist post, and how does she know Andy Barry?\n- When was she made partner, and what was her title on joining?\n- Which other boldstart companies are hers (Topos Bio? Netpreme?).\n\n## Sources\n- https://boldstart.vc/team/ellen-chisa/\n- https://boldstart.vc/news/generalistai-when-robots-start-to-improvise-welcome-to-boldstart/\n- https://boldstart.vc/news/wsj-boldstart-ventures-raises-250-million-ai-fund-looking-to-move-fast/\n- https://www.vcbeast.com/investors/ellen-chisa (aggregator)\n", "person:emil-schaefer": "\n# Emil Schaefer\n\n## One-line take\nA London growth investor at Index who appears as the second, junior name on two AI deals that senior partners led: Chai Discovery's Series C (Nina Achadjian) and Conduct's Series A (Sahir Azam and Bastian Hasslinger). He matters as a connector to those partners, not as a source of early deals.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he has growth-stage diligence training from KKR Tech Growth [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/), and he sits on a team that can write $60M–$400M lead checks. Index led Chai's $400M Series C [src](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design) and co-led Conduct's $60M Series A [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/).\n- **What constrains him:** in every AI deal found, a senior partner wrote the post and he is only tagged. Index was not first in either company. Chai's seed (2024-09) came from Thrive, OpenAI and Dimension, and its Series B (2025-12) from Oak HC/FT and General Catalyst [src](https://www.oakhcft.com/news/chai-discovery-announces-130-million-series-b-to-transform-molecular-discovery). Creandum, Lucid Capital and Booom backed Conduct before Index [src](https://www.webpronews.com/ex-palantir-engineers-land-60-million-to-crack-enterprise-code-before-sap-deadline-hits/).\n- **What he'd never do (inferred from his role):** lead a seed for a new lab. His mandate is growth-stage companies across industries and regions [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/).\n\n## Differential insight\nNo first-person material was found. He has no byline on the Index Perspectives index, which was checked on 2026-11-10 [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/), and the research turned up no podcasts, X account or talks. WebSearch was unavailable during this run (the session's search budget was spent), so off-site sources were not searched. The points below are structural and come from firm pages.\n- **His tag means different things on the two deals.** On Chai he is on the company page next to Nina Achadjian [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/chai-discovery/). On Conduct he is tagged on the post, but the company page lists only Sahir Azam and Bastian Hasslinger [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/conduct/). He looks like a deal-team member on Chai and support on Conduct.\n- **Chai: Index paid up rather than got in early.** Index led the C at a reported $3.8B. The release names Index alongside Kleiner Perkins, Sequoia and Dimension [src](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design). Achadjian's own deal-origin line is \"I knew this was someone we had to get to know.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/transforming-biology-from-science-to-engineering-our-investment-in-chai-discovery/)\n- **Conduct: a shared lead at the A.** Index's post says it is \"co-leading their $60 million Series A along with ICONIQ\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/). The post is dated 2026-06-17.\n- **A third company that isn't AI.** Garner Health (data, healthcare) lists him with Martin Mignot and Jahanvi Sardana [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/garner-health/). That suggests he works across Index's growth book rather than in an AI-specific pod (an inference).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** THESIS.md rates \"mostly growth or late stage\" as low, and his firm page describes him as a growth-stage investor [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/). He has no seed, lab, pre-training or chip deal on record. Two named deals are adjacent to my lens:\n- **Conduct**: AI-native enterprise automation built by ex-Palantir founders, which is the closest to my lens. He is only tagged on it.\n- **Chai Discovery**: frontier AI for biology, but entered at the C.\n\nWhy he's still worth tracking:\n1. As a growth-side contact at Index for follow-on rounds after my seeds.\n2. For the career path: Picus Capital is a seed-stage firm, and he started his career there [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/).\n\n## Background\n- BSc, University of Amsterdam [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/)\n- MSc, London School of Economics [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/)\n- Started his career at Picus Capital; dates not stated [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/)\n- KKR Tech Growth, London; dates not stated [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/)\n- Index Ventures, London, growth team; join date not stated [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/)\n- Former professional field hockey player [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/ | Firm team page |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | No Index Perspectives byline as of 2026-11-10 |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/emil-schaefer-203636179/ | Linked from the Index page; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. WebSearch was unavailable in this run, so this is not a confirmed negative.\n\n## How they think\n- Nothing first-person was found.\n- From his role: a growth mandate across industries and regions, helping founders scale companies that define their category [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/).\n- Contrarian views and calls he got right or wrong: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/): his mandate, his career path and the companies he's listed on.\n2. [Chai Discovery post by Nina Achadjian](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/transforming-biology-from-science-to-engineering-our-investment-in-chai-discovery/): the AI-bio thesis behind the deal he is on.\n3. [Conduct post by Sahir Azam and Bastian Hasslinger](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/): the case for vertical enterprise AI.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Chai Discovery (AI drug discovery) | Series C ($400M at $3.8B) | 2026-07-14 | Index led. He is the second Index name after Achadjian, who bylined the post | Index team: Nina Achadjian. Round: Kleiner Perkins, Sequoia, Dimension; new investors BCV, Battery, Baillie Gifford, BDT & MSD, Sapphire, Avra; existing investors Thrive, OpenAI, Oak HC/FT, Menlo, General Catalyst and others | [BioSpace](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design), [Index company page](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/chai-discovery/) |\n| Conduct (enterprise AI automation) | Series A ($60M) | 2026-06-17 | Index co-led with ICONIQ. He is tagged only and not on the company page | Index team: Sahir Azam and Bastian Hasslinger (bylines), Hannah Seal (tagged). Round: ICONIQ; earlier investors Creandum, Lucid Capital and Booom | [Index post](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/), [Index company page](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/conduct/), [WebProNews](https://www.webpronews.com/ex-palantir-engineers-land-60-million-to-crack-enterprise-code-before-sap-deadline-hits/) |\n| Garner Health (not AI; data and healthcare) | not found | not found | not found | Index team: Martin Mignot, Jahanvi Sardana | [Index company page](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/garner-health/) |\n\n**Senior/junior pairing:** a different senior partner on each company: Achadjian on Chai, Azam and Hasslinger (with Seal) on Conduct, and Mignot on Garner.\n\n**Timing:**\n- Chai: Index came in about 22 months after the 2024-09 seed and after the 2025-12 Series B [src](https://www.oakhcft.com/news/chai-discovery-announces-130-million-series-b-to-transform-molecular-discovery).\n- Conduct: Index came in after the earlier Creandum, Lucid and Booom money [src](https://www.webpronews.com/ex-palantir-engineers-land-60-million-to-crack-enterprise-code-before-sap-deadline-hits/).\n- Index was not first in any of his AI deals.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Chai Discovery:** the origin story is Achadjian's, not his. She wrote that she knew Joshua Meier was someone Index \"had to get to know\" within minutes of meeting him [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/transforming-biology-from-science-to-engineering-our-investment-in-chai-discovery/). No source gives his role in the deal. A plausible guess is growth-side diligence on a $3.8B round, but that is unconfirmed.\n- **Conduct:** the post doesn't say how Index met the founders [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/). The founders are ex-Palantir, which matches Hasslinger's pattern (see people/bastian-hasslinger.md).\n- **How he sources:** no source says. The evidence suggests he joins deals that senior partners originate rather than bringing his own, but that is an inference.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Nina Achadjian (Index) | Co-signer on Chai Discovery; she bylined the post | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/chai-discovery/) |\n| Sahir Azam (Index) | Co-tagged on Conduct; Azam co-bylined | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/) |\n| Bastian Hasslinger (Index) | Co-tagged on Conduct; Hasslinger co-bylined | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/) |\n| Hannah Seal (Index) | Co-tagged on Conduct | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/) |\n| Martin Mignot (Index) | Listed together on Garner Health | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/garner-health/) |\n| Jahanvi Sardana (Index) | Listed together on Garner Health | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/garner-health/) |\n| KKR Tech Growth | Former employer | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/) |\n| Picus Capital | First employer | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What is his title and when did he join Index? (LinkedIn wasn't fetched.)\n- What deals did he work on at KKR Tech Growth? Any AI or compute?\n- Did he work on any AI-native seeds at Picus Capital?\n- Who sourced his part of Chai, and was there any Index exposure before the C?\n- Garner Health: which round did Index enter, and when?\n- Is he on other Index AI growth rounds that aren't tagged on posts?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/emil-schaefer/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/chai-discovery/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/conduct/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/garner-health/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/transforming-biology-from-science-to-engineering-our-investment-in-chai-discovery/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/\n- https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design\n- https://www.oakhcft.com/news/chai-discovery-announces-130-million-series-b-to-transform-molecular-discovery (via people/nina-achadjian.md)\n- https://www.webpronews.com/ex-palantir-engineers-land-60-million-to-crack-enterprise-code-before-sap-deadline-hits/ (via people/bastian-hasslinger.md)\n- https://www.garnerhealth.com/press\n", "person:emily-zhao": "\n# Emily Zhao\n\n## One-line take\nZhao is the Salesforce Ventures (SFV) investor whose name is on most of its frontier-lab and AI-infra book: Anthropic C/F/G, Cohere, Hugging Face D (led), Together A2 (led), fal, Goodfire, Upscale AI and Arena. A founder who already has a priced round and wants a corporate later-round check with Fortune 500 distribution should talk to her ([SFV bio](https://salesforceventures.com/people/emily-zhao/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins.** She brings Salesforce's balance sheet and its enterprise channel. SFV credits its Innovation Forums and Demo Days with Anthropic customers Accenture, Barclays and Coinbase ([SFV, 2025-09-02](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/anthropics-13b-series-f/)). On a contested deal she moves in person: Hugging Face was \"won\" on a trip to Paris weeks after a July 4th first meeting ([SFV, 2026-09-03](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/congratulations-hugging-face/)).\n- **What constrains her.** She does not control the money. SFV has no outside LPs and no closed fund, and its approver is John Somorjai, who runs both SFV and corporate development ([firms/salesforce-ventures.md](../firms/salesforce-ventures.md)). Her book is sized by Salesforce's appetite: the $1B Generative AI allocation was more than 85% deployed by 2025-10, and no new AI allocation has been announced since.\n- **Her pattern since 2025.** She tracks a company for 8 to 24 months and enters at its second to fourth priced round, often at a step-up:\n  - fal: first met early 2024, entered the Series C in 2025-07.\n  - Chainguard: two years of meetings before the Series D.\n  - Arena: met in summer 2025, entered the Series B at $3.1B in 2026-10, after the A at $1.7B.\n  - Upscale AI: she was not in the $100M+ seed and came in at the A-1 at $2B.\n- **What she would rarely do.** Lead or price a seed. The only exception found is the Fundamental seed, which SFV co-led.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **She came in late at Arena and paid about 1.8x the A price.** \"We first met Anastasios in the summer of 2025\" ([SFV, 2026-10-08](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-definitive-model-ranking-platform/)). Arena's seed (2025-05, $600M) and Series A (2026-01, $1.7B, led by Felicis and UC Investments) went ahead without SFV. SFV entered the Series B at $3.1B ([Arena](https://arena.ai/blog/series-b)). The courtship included Arena's CEO running a workshop at SFV's Elevate CEO summit. Reading (mine): her edge on these deals is the relationship and the corporate's ability to pay a later price, not getting in early.\n- **Her one seed is a foundation-model company.** \"excited to have co-led their Seed and to be participating in their Series A\" ([SFV, 2026-02-05](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-structured-data/)). Fundamental builds a Large Tabular Model with DeepMind and Isomorphic alumni. It built in stealth for 18 months and announced the A with an AWS partnership. Seed date and size are not disclosed; Headline's post puts the seed at about $30M ([Headline](https://blog.headline.com/p/backing-fundamental-bringing-tabular); unconfirmed). This is the closest thing in her book to the owner's deal type.\n- **The Anthropic underwriting started from scepticism.** \"we remained skeptical: how could an intelligent chatbot revolutionize the economy?\" ([SFV, 2026-02-12](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-anthropic/)). What changed their minds was Constitutional AI, which they tested with Fortune 500 CIOs and Salesforce product teams and read as a selling point to enterprises. The first meeting with Dario Amodei was in early 2023 in San Francisco. The same post calls every later round, \"from Series D through Series G\", an easy decision.\n- **She uses Salesforce's own customer base as diligence.** At Chainguard SFV cited \"widespread adoption of Chainguard across the Salesforce Ventures portfolio\" and the wider Salesforce ecosystem ([SFV, 2025-04-23](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-chainguard/)). The firm-level examples are Argmax and Resolve AI ([firms/salesforce-ventures.md](../firms/salesforce-ventures.md)).\n- **Robotics was a deliberate sweep before any check.** \"our team has met with more than 50 researchers and founders in robotics\" over seven months. The result was a published seven-criteria framework, written with Pascha Hao, that covers demo forensics (staged, sped up, teleoperated) and quality, throughput and robustness metrics ([SFV, 2025-08-14](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-guide-to-investing-in-robotics/)). Fei-Fei Li prompted it: \"Fei-Fei Li told us she’s waiting for just one investor\" to write the guide. The Dyna Robotics Series A followed a month later.\n- **Two of her early AI bets ended in sales to strategic buyers.** Hugging Face D (led 2023-08 at $4.5B) is being bought by NVIDIA for $12.9B (2026-09). Protect AI (entered 2023-07) went to Palo Alto Networks (2025-04) ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/congratulations-hugging-face/); [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/congratulations-protect-ai/)).\n- **Her bio's \"led\" means she ran SFV's side of the deal, not that SFV led the round.** The bio says she \"led several investments\", naming Anthropic, Hugging Face, Together AI, fal, Runway and Cohere ([SFV bio](https://salesforceventures.com/people/emily-zhao/)). SFV set the price only at Hugging Face D and Together A2; the rest were participations.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** She is not a seed co-investor for frontier labs, with Fundamental as the single exception. Her value to a ~$20M seed check comes later:\n1. **Series B to D price-setter or participant** for an AI-infra or lab company that needs a corporate check plus Fortune 500 distribution.\n2. **Robotics follow-on.** Dyna A and the published framework show she is active in robotics.\n3. **A path to strategic exits.** Hugging Face went to NVIDIA and Protect AI to Palo Alto Networks.\n\nFor an AI-native automation company that sells into CRM or service workflows, she is the route to Salesforce as a customer.\n\nShe is not an LP. Deals that matter to me: Fundamental seed co-lead, Anthropic C, Together A2 lead, Hugging Face D lead, Upscale AI A-1.\n\n## Background\n- BA in Economics and Art History, Dartmouth ([SFV bio](https://salesforceventures.com/people/emily-zhao/)).\n- Analyst, JPMorgan Investment Banking; then Associate, Blackstone Private Equity (corporate buyouts). Dates unconfirmed (same source).\n- Investor, Avenir Growth Capital, New York (vertical SaaS, health tech, applications), until 2022. Start date unconfirmed (same source).\n- **Spring 2022:** joined Salesforce Ventures; her SFV people page was created 2022-06-06 (WP API). She helped launch the Generative AI Fund ($250M in 2023-03, $1B by 2024-09).\n- **By 2026-06-22:** titled Managing Director in the Upscale AI release ([Pulse 2.0](https://pulse2.com/upscale-ai-raises-190-million-series-a-1-at-2-billion-valuation-for-ai-networking-infrastructure-company/amp/)). Earlier titles were not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [salesforceventures.com/people/emily-zhao](https://salesforceventures.com/people/emily-zhao/) | Live 2026-11-10; bio and deal list (13 companies incl. Vercel, Chainguard, \"Fabric\") |\n| Substack / blog | [SFV Perspectives, her byline](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/) | 24 posts 2023-05 to 2026-10, mostly deal posts plus AI infra (2024-03) and robotics (2025-08) theses |\n| X | not found | Not linked from SFV; none verified |\n| LinkedIn | not found | Not linked from SFV page (field empty in WP API); none verified |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\nTo reach her: a warm intro from a portfolio founder (fal, Together, Goodfire, Arena, Upscale AI) or a co-investor on those rounds (Kleiner, Lightspeed, Mayfield, Premji). SFV's Elevate CEO summit is where she meets founders before investing, as with Arena. Her co-authors Nathan Ho and Pascha Hao are the junior entry points. No direct email is published.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. Web search was unavailable this session, so I checked only SFV's site and press releases.\n\n## How they think\n- **Value sits at the model layer, then moves down the stack.** In 2023 she held that value would accrue to foundation models first (Anthropic, Cohere). In 2024 she mapped GPU clouds ([AI Infrastructure Explained, 2024-03-20](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ai-infrastructure-explained/)). By 2026 she was at networking silicon (Upscale AI). Her Upscale quote stresses systems \"that avoid vendor lock-in\" ([Pulse 2.0](https://pulse2.com/upscale-ai-raises-190-million-series-a-1-at-2-billion-valuation-for-ai-networking-infrastructure-company/amp/)).\n- **She underwrites enterprise reliability.** Goodfire was framed as fixing enterprise ROI through model control, \"the best independent team doing interpretability research\" ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-goodfire/)). Arena was framed as neutral evaluation data.\n- **Robotics needs patient capital.** She says it \"will require patient capital, a long-term view, and deep conviction\" (robotics guide).\n- **Calls so far.** Right: Hugging Face (about 2.9x in three years on the D price) and Anthropic (SFV's stake is about 45% of Salesforce's strategic portfolio by 2026-07, [10-Q](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1108524/000110852626000190/crm-20260731.htm)). Too early for robotics and networking.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Behind the Investment: Anthropic](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-anthropic/): how the 2023 lab thesis was formed, and the scepticism behind it.\n2. [Salesforce Ventures Guide to Investing in Robotics](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-guide-to-investing-in-robotics/): her diligence checklist.\n3. [Congratulations, Hugging Face!](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/congratulations-hugging-face/): how she wins a competitive round.\n4. [AI Infrastructure Explained](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ai-infrastructure-explained/): her map of the infra stack (her only solo byline).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public X or LinkedIn account was verified.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Anthropic | Series C ($450M) | 2023-05-23 | no | Spark (lead), Google, Menlo | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-anthropic/) |\n| Cohere | Series C ($270M) | 2023-06-08 | no | Inovia (lead), NVIDIA, Oracle | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-cohere/) |\n| Runway | Series C extension ($141M) | 2023-06-29 | no | Google, NVIDIA | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-runway/) |\n| Protect AI | Undisclosed | 2023-07-26 | unknown | | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-protect-ai/) |\n| Hugging Face | Series D ($235M at $4.5B) | 2023-08-24 | **yes** | Google, Amazon, NVIDIA | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-hugging-face/) |\n| Together AI | Series A2 ($106M) | 2024-03-13 | **yes** | Coatue, Lux, Kleiner | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-together-ai/) |\n| Chainguard | Series D ($356M) | 2025-04-23 | no | IVP, Kleiner | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-chainguard/) |\n| fal | Series C ($125M at $1.5B) | 2025-07-31 | no | Meritech (lead), a16z | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-fal/) |\n| Cohere | Series D-II ($500M at $6.8B) | 2025-08-14 | no | Radical, Inovia | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/strengthening-our-commitment-to-cohere/) |\n| Anthropic | Series F ($13B at $183B) | 2025-09-02 | no | ICONIQ, Fidelity, Lightspeed | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/anthropics-13b-series-f/) |\n| Dyna Robotics | Series A ($120M) | 2025-09-15 | no | CRV, First Round | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-dyna-robotics/) |\n| Vercel | Series F ($300M) | 2025-09-30 | no | Accel, GIC | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/deepening-our-commitment-vercel/) |\n| Fundamental | Seed | undisclosed | **co-led** | | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-structured-data/) |\n| Fundamental | Series A ($225M) | 2026-02-05 | no | Oak HC/FT (lead), Valor, Battery | same |\n| Goodfire | Series B ($150M) | 2026-02-05 | no | B Capital (lead), Menlo, Lightspeed | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-goodfire/) |\n| Anthropic | Series G | 2026-02 | no | | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-anthropic/) |\n| Supabase | Series F ($500M at $10.5B) | 2026-06-04 | no | GIC (lead), Accel, Coatue | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-backend-for-the-ai-era/) |\n| Upscale AI | Series A-1 ($190M at $2B) | 2026-06-22 | no | Premji (lead), Mayfield | [Pulse 2.0](https://pulse2.com/upscale-ai-raises-190-million-series-a-1-at-2-billion-valuation-for-ai-networking-infrastructure-company/amp/) |\n| Arena | Series B ($200M at $3.1B) | 2026-10-08 | no | Lightspeed, Khosla (co-leads) | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-definitive-model-ranking-platform/) |\n\nAlso on her SFV page: \"Fabric\" (needs-id). Other re-ups (Cohere D 2024, Together B 2025 and C 2026, fal D 2025) are in `data/rows/salesforce-ventures-portfolio-deals.csv`.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Hugging Face D (2023).** They met the team over July 4th, flew to Paris \"a few weeks later\" and won a competitive round as lead. The thesis was \"A GitHub for AI\". NVIDIA agreed to buy the company three years later ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/congratulations-hugging-face/)).\n- **Anthropic C (2023).** The first meeting with Dario Amodei was in early 2023 in SF. The Salesforce AI Research team under Silvio Savarese helped form the model-layer thesis. The Constitutional AI pitch was tested on Fortune 500 CIOs ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-anthropic/)).\n- **Together A2 (2024).** First met the CEO in summer 2023 and led the A2 about eight months later ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-together-ai/)).\n- **Arena B (2026).** Met the CEO in summer 2025. He ran a workshop at SFV's Elevate CEO summit, and SFV joined the B about a year after first meeting ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-definitive-model-ranking-platform/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Salesforce Ventures | Managing Director | 2022 | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/emily-zhao/) |\n| Avenir Growth Capital | Investor (former) | until 2022 | same |\n| Blackstone | PE Associate (former) | unconfirmed | same |\n| JPMorgan | IB Analyst (former) | unconfirmed | same |\n| Paul Drews | co-signer: Anthropic C/F/G, Runway, Dyna, Vercel, Supabase | 2023 | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-anthropic/) |\n| Rob Keith | co-signer: Cohere, Protect AI, Hugging Face, Together, fal, Fundamental | 2023 | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-cohere/) |\n| Nathan Ho | most frequent 2025-26 co-author | 2025 | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-goodfire/) |\n| Pascha Hao | robotics series co-author; Dyna | 2025 | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-guide-to-investing-in-robotics/) |\n| Fei-Fei Li | asked for the robotics guide | 2025 | same |\n\n## Open questions\n- Her title at hire in 2022, and the date she became MD. A Wayback check of the SFV page was not run.\n- Her Avenir, Blackstone and JPMorgan dates (LinkedIn not verified).\n- Fundamental seed date and size, and whether she holds a board or observer seat there or at Upscale AI.\n- Which \"Fabric\" is on her page.\n- Whether she approves anything herself or everything goes to Somorjai, and her check bands per deal.\n\n## Sources\n- https://salesforceventures.com/people/emily-zhao/ (WP API, modified 2026-10-19)\n- https://salesforceventures.com/wp-json/wp/v2/perspectives?coauthors=112 (her 24 bylined posts)\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-anthropic/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/anthropics-13b-series-f/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/congratulations-hugging-face/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-definitive-model-ranking-platform/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-structured-data/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-chainguard/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-fal/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-guide-to-investing-in-robotics/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-open-networking-stack-for-the-ai-era/\n- https://pulse2.com/upscale-ai-raises-190-million-series-a-1-at-2-billion-valuation-for-ai-networking-infrastructure-company/amp/\n- https://arena.ai/blog/series-b\n- https://blog.headline.com/p/backing-fundamental-bringing-tabular\n", "person:enrique-salem": "\n# Enrique Salem\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the ex-Symantec CEO who runs BCV's security and infrastructure practice. He led the first institutional round in Crusoe in 2018–19, years before AI compute was a category. He is one of three BCV partners on poolside, where BCV led the $500M Series B. Day to day, though, he backs security companies, many of them AI-native. Few of his deals are frontier labs.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he brings a CEO's network and board credibility. He ran Symantec, sat on the Mandiant and Atlassian boards, and advised the White House. Founders he once acquired or worked with come back to him. Prophet Security's co-founder Kamal Shah worked with him after Symantec bought Clearwell, and BCV then incubated Prophet in BCV Labs and led its seed ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/prophet-security-is-using-ai-to-cut-mean-time-to-response-by-10x-for-security-operations-teams/)). He can also write growth checks. BCV leads he wrote up include Docker ($105M C), HYCU ($87.5M A) and Moveworks ($30M A).\n- **What constrains him:** his mandate is enterprise infrastructure and security, built around CISO buyers. Most of his 42 portfolio records in BCV's CMS are security or data-infra companies ([src](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%20%26%26%20references%28%226e1619e4-cea3-438f-bfec-60478db54b77%22%29%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Eslug.current%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Etitle%7D)). On the frontier-AI deals, poolside and Unstructured, he shares credit with Rak Garg or Saanya Ojha.\n- **How he picks (in his own words):** people first. He says \"Look for great people and expect exceptional results,\" and committed to Moveworks right after his first meeting with Bhavin Shah ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/enrique-salem/)).\n- **What he'd never do:** he would not back security products that interrupt users or create noise. His \"three I's\" test (invisible, invincible, inexpensive) says the cure can't be worse than the threat ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/ai-agents-are-armed-for-battle-adapting-security-infrastructure-and-shipping-new-solutions/)). No sourced statement on frontier labs.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He admits BCV doubted Crusoe's pivot from crypto to AI.** \"we were skeptical that it was the right time.\" BCV backed it anyway because it trusted Chase Lochmiller's execution ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crusoe-climb-betting-on-power-before-ai-was-cool/)).\n- **Crusoe timing:** BCV's July 2018 memo called Crusoe \"a mobile data center operator that has semi proprietary access to free electricity.\" BCV co-led the seed with Founders Fund. Stefan Cohen (now at Bain Capital Crypto) made the intro and Salem led the investment. Valor's Series B came in April 2021, roughly two and a half years after the memo ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crusoe-climb-betting-on-power-before-ai-was-cool/), [src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-series-b)).\n- **Prophet Security came out of BCV's own incubator and his Symantec past.** The founders spent fall 2023 in BCV Labs. BCV writes: \"Enrique was CEO of the cybersecurity corporation Symantec when it acquired Clearwell\" (Kamal Shah had worked at Clearwell). Then: \"We are pleased to have led the $11 million seed round\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/prophet-security-is-using-ai-to-cut-mean-time-to-response-by-10x-for-security-operations-teams/)).\n- **He decides fast on people.** On Moveworks: \"I knew from our first meeting that he had something special.\" BCV invested \"shortly after that meeting\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/enrique-salem/)). BCV led the $30M Series A in 2019 ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-answer-to-our-it-service-desk-woes-moveworks/), [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/moveworks-moves-work-beyond-chatbots/)).\n- **Unstructured: seed, then the A.** BCV \"partnered at the seed. Now, we're excited to invest in the A\" (2023-07, with Madrona, Shield, M12, LangChain and Weaviate) ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/how-unstructured-is-powering-the-llm-data-stack/)).\n- **poolside: BCV's diligence point was the team build right after the seed.** The founders were \"hiring experts in GTM, design, product engineering and large model training right after the seed\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/poolside-is-scaling-a-team-to-help-billions-code-and-realize-agi/)). BCV then led the $500M Series B on 2024-10-02 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/10/02/ai-coding-startup-poolside-raises-500m-from-ebay-nvidia-and-others/)).\n- **He is skeptical of AI agents in security for now.** \"the jury's out on how fast the deployment of AI agents for security\" will become trustworthy ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/ai-agents-are-armed-for-battle-adapting-security-infrastructure-and-shipping-new-solutions/)).\n- **Hands-on board style.** On Evident.io (his first BCV deal, Series A 2014, sold to Palo Alto Networks for $300M in 2018), he helped with \"recruiting their CFO and putting together their financial plans\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/backedbybcv-the-future-is-evident/)).\n- **He is the firm's voice on promotions and Israel.** He wrote the promotion posts for Ron Miasnik and for Slater Stich and Saanya Ojha. The Miasnik post says BCV invested \"more than $150M over the past year\" in Israel (Dream, Grain, Daylight) ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-ron-miasnik-our-newest-partner/), [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-slater-stich-and-saanya-ojha-our-newest-partners/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Two deals matter to you:\n- **Crusoe** (AI compute): first institutional equity, co-led with Founders Fund in 2018–19.\n- **poolside** (frontier coding lab): BCV was in at the seed and led the $500M Series B. Salem is one of three partners on the deal.\n\nModal (inference cloud, growth 2026) and Unstructured (LLM data ETL, seed + A) are adjacent AI infrastructure. Everything else is AI-native security: Prophet, Straiker, Cogent, Nebulock, Dream and Halcyon. That's useful if you look at agentic security, but it isn't pre-training or new labs. He is not a principal or a recent promotion, so he falls outside your \"alpha in juniors\" segment. His value to you is as the senior sponsor behind the people who are in it: Garg, Ojha and Miasnik. He also signs off on the large checks.\n\n## Background\n- Born in Barranquilla, Colombia. BA in Computer Science, Dartmouth ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Salem)).\n- VP at Peter Norton Computing, which Symantec acquired in 1990 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Salem)). He says he wrote \"the first lines of cybersecurity code for personal computers\" in the late 1980s ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/ai-vs-ai-the-new-frontline-in-the-billion-threat-cyber-war/)).\n- VP roles at Security Pacific Merchant Bank, Ask Jeeves and Oblix. CEO of Brightmail until Symantec bought it in 2004 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Salem)).\n- Symantec COO, then CEO from April 2009 to July 2012, when the board forced him out ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Salem)).\n- President's Management Advisory Board from 2011 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Salem)).\n- Joined BCV in 2014 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Salem)). Bylined \"Managing Director\" in 2018 ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/backedbybcv-the-future-is-evident/)) and \"Partner\" in 2022 ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-leads-105m-series-c-in-docker-enabling-modern-software-teams-to-ship-quickly-and-securely/)).\n- Independent boards: Mandiant, Atlassian, ForeScout ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Salem)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://baincapitalventures.com/team/enrique-salem/ | BCV profile with Q&A; 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://baincapitalventures.com/insights/ | ~37 BCV posts list him as author or co-author (2017–2026), per the CMS |\n| X | nothing found | No personal X on the BCV profile |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/enriquesalem | Listed on BCV profile; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found in this pass. The web-search budget ran out, so podcasts and talks weren't searched. His op-ed \"AI vs. AI\" ran in a version on MarketWatch ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/ai-vs-ai-the-new-frontline-in-the-billion-threat-cyber-war/)).\n\n## How they think\n- **Three I's for security:** \"invisible, invincible and inexpensive.\" You can't skip any of the three ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/ai-agents-are-armed-for-battle-adapting-security-infrastructure-and-shipping-new-solutions/)).\n- **AI agents will reshape attack surfaces** the way email did in the 1990s. Expect agent impersonation and data poisoning ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/ai-vs-ai-the-new-frontline-in-the-billion-threat-cyber-war/)).\n- **Developer-led, open-source infrastructure.** BCV has been \"investing in open-source companies for almost ten years\" (as of 2022) ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-leads-105m-series-c-in-docker-enabling-modern-software-teams-to-ship-quickly-and-securely/)).\n- **Contrarian call he got right:** Crusoe's energy dislocation thesis, which he says other VCs find too capital-intensive ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crusoe-climb-betting-on-power-before-ai-was-cool/)).\n- **Mistake admitted:** BCV was skeptical of Crusoe's move away from crypto (same source).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Crusoe's climb](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crusoe-climb-betting-on-power-before-ai-was-cool/): the 2018 memo, the co-lead and his admitted skepticism.\n2. [AI Agents are Armed for Battle](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/ai-agents-are-armed-for-battle-adapting-security-infrastructure-and-shipping-new-solutions/): his three-I's investment filter.\n3. [Prophet Security](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/prophet-security-is-using-ai-to-cut-mean-time-to-response-by-10x-for-security-operations-teams/): how a BCV Labs incubation and old Symantec ties turn into a led seed.\n4. [Moveworks Series A](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-answer-to-our-it-service-desk-woes-moveworks/): an early enterprise-AI thesis from 2019.\n5. [AI vs. AI](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/ai-vs-ai-the-new-frontline-in-the-billion-threat-cyber-war/): his view of the threat landscape now that agents exist.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. There's no personal X account, and his public voice is the BCV blog. He co-writes most often with Saanya Ojha and Rak Garg (per CMS author tags).\n\n## Portfolio\nAI-relevant deals first. Source for the partner tag is BCV's CMS ([src](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%20%26%26%20references%28%226e1619e4-cea3-438f-bfec-60478db54b77%22%29%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Eslug.current%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Etitle%7D)).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Crusoe | Seed | memo 2018-07; CMS year 2019 | Co-led (with Founders Fund); Salem led for BCV | Founders Fund | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crusoe-climb-betting-on-power-before-ai-was-cool/) |\n| Crusoe | Series B $128M | 2021-04-26 | No (Valor led) | Valor, Lowercarbon, FF, DRW and others | [src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-series-b) |\n| Crusoe | Series C $350M | 2022-04-21 | No (BCV returning) | Valor, FF, Lowercarbon, Felicis and others | [src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-millions-in-new-capital) |\n| poolside | Seed | 2023 | Unknown | Rak Garg and Saanya Ojha are co-partners on the CMS record | [CMS](https://baincapitalventures.com/portfolio/poolside/) |\n| poolside | Series B $500M (~$3B val.) | 2024-10-02 | BCV led (partner on the round not named) | eBay, Nvidia, LG Tech Ventures, Felicis, Redpoint, DST and others | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/10/02/ai-coding-startup-poolside-raises-500m-from-ebay-nvidia-and-others/) |\n| Unstructured | Seed, then Series A | seed n/d; A 2023-07 | Unknown | Madrona, Shield, M12, LangChain, Weaviate (A); co-partner Rak Garg | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/how-unstructured-is-powering-the-llm-data-stack/) |\n| Modal | Growth (CMS) | 2026 | Unknown | BCV also listed in the 2026-10 Series D (Accel led) per an aggregator | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/portfolio/modal/), [aggregator](https://siliconvalleyinvestclub.com/2026/10/16/modal-labs-raises-750-million-at-a-15-75-billion-valuation/) |\n| Moveworks (ServiceNow) | Series A $30M | 2019-04 | Yes (BCV led) | nothing found | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/moveworks-moves-work-beyond-chatbots/) |\n| Prophet Security | Seed $11M | 2024-04-23 | Yes (BCV led); incubated in BCV Labs | Security angels; co-partners Rak Garg, Aaref Hilaly | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/prophet-security-is-using-ai-to-cut-mean-time-to-response-by-10x-for-security-operations-teams/) |\n| Straiker | Early (CMS) | 2024 | Unknown | co-partner Rak Garg | [CMS](https://baincapitalventures.com/portfolio/straiker/) |\n| Cogent Security | Series A | 2026 | Unknown | co-partner Rak Garg | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-more-cogent-approach-to-vulnerability-management/) |\n| Nebulock | Seed (CMS) | 2025 | Unknown | co-partner Rak Garg | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/proactive-by-default-how-nebulock-rethinks-threat-hunting/) |\n| Dream | Growth (CMS) | 2025 | Unknown | co-authors Ojha, Miasnik | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/from-offensive-to-defensive-how-shalev-hulio-and-sebastian-kurz-are-keeping-nations-and-networks/) |\n| Halcyon | Growth (CMS) | 2023 | Unknown | co-partner Saanya Ojha | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/halcyon-is-ai-powered-cybersecurity-company-dedicated-to-stopping-ransomware/) |\n| Astronomer | Growth (CMS) | 2025 | Unknown | co-partner Saanya Ojha | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/from-airflow-to-dataops-astronomer-is-building-the-data-control-plane-for-the-enterprise/) |\n| Adaptive | Growth (CMS) | 2025 | Unknown | nothing found | [CMS](https://baincapitalventures.com/portfolio/adaptive/) |\n| Docker | Series C $105M | 2022-03-31 | Yes (BCV led) | nothing found | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-leads-105m-series-c-in-docker-enabling-modern-software-teams-to-ship-quickly-and-securely/) |\n| HYCU | Series A $87.5M | 2021-03-30 | Yes (BCV led) | co-partner Saanya Ojha (CMS) | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/data-backup-in-a-hybrid-multi-cloud-world-bcv-leads-87-5-million-series-a-in-hycu/) |\n| VISO Trust | Series A $11M | 2022-03-03 | Yes (BCV led) | co-partner Rak Garg | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-leads-11m-series-a-in-viso-trust-making-vendor-onboarding-safe-fast-and-compliant/) |\n| Evident.io | Series A | 2014 | Unknown | Exit: Palo Alto Networks, $300M, 2018-03 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/backedbybcv-the-future-is-evident/) |\n| Others (CMS) | various | 2012–2026 | n/a | Redis, Sysdig, DocuSign, Rubrik, Armis, Attivo, CloudGenix, Awake, Nightfall, Veza, JupiterOne, Smallstep, StarTree, Stacklok, Daylight, Cape, Armadin and others | [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%20%26%26%20references%28%226e1619e4-cea3-438f-bfec-60478db54b77%22%29%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Eslug.current%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Etitle%7D) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Crusoe (2018–19):** Stefan Cohen brought Chase Lochmiller and Cully Cavness to BCV. The July 2018 memo pitched Crusoe as stranded-gas bitcoin mining in mobile data centers. BCV and Founders Fund co-led the seed, and Salem led the investment for BCV. BCV doubted the later move away from crypto but trusted Lochmiller to execute. BCV returned in the B (2021) and C (2022) ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crusoe-climb-betting-on-power-before-ai-was-cool/), [src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-millions-in-new-capital)).\n- **Prophet Security (2023–24):** Kamal Shah and Vibhav Sreekanti incubated in BCV Labs in Palo Alto in fall 2023. Three BCV partners had prior ties to Kamal: Aaref Hilaly (Clearwell), Salem (Symantec bought Clearwell) and Ajay Agarwal (Trilogy). BCV led the $11M seed ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/prophet-security-is-using-ai-to-cut-mean-time-to-response-by-10x-for-security-operations-teams/)).\n- **Moveworks (2019):** Salem met Bhavin Shah, and BCV invested shortly after, leading the $30M Series A. ServiceNow later agreed to acquire the company ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/enrique-salem/), [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-answer-to-our-it-service-desk-woes-moveworks/)).\n- **Unstructured (2022–23):** BCV met founder Brian Raymond over dinner in San Francisco, backed the seed and returned for the A ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/how-unstructured-is-powering-the-llm-data-stack/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Stefan Cohen (Bain Capital Crypto) | Brought Crusoe to BCV | 2018 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crusoe-climb-betting-on-power-before-ai-was-cool/) |\n| Founders Fund | Co-lead on Crusoe seed | 2018–19 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crusoe-climb-betting-on-power-before-ai-was-cool/) |\n| Chase Lochmiller, Cully Cavness | Crusoe founders he backed | 2018 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crusoe-climb-betting-on-power-before-ai-was-cool/) |\n| Rak Garg (BCV) | Most frequent co-partner (poolside, Unstructured, Prophet, Straiker, Cogent, Nebulock) | 2021 | [CMS](https://baincapitalventures.com/portfolio/poolside/) |\n| Saanya Ojha (BCV) | Co-partner (poolside, Halcyon, HYCU, Astronomer, Cape); he wrote her hire post | 2023-01 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-slater-stich-and-saanya-ojha-our-newest-partners/) |\n| Ron Miasnik (BCV) | Wrote his promotion post; co-wrote the Dream post | 2023-12 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-ron-miasnik-our-newest-partner/) |\n| Kamal Shah (Prophet Security) | Worked together after Symantec bought Clearwell | 2012 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/prophet-security-is-using-ai-to-cut-mean-time-to-response-by-10x-for-security-operations-teams/) |\n| Bhavin Shah (Moveworks) | Founder; Salem's example of his investing ethos | 2019 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/enrique-salem/) |\n| Eiso Kant, Jason Warner (poolside) | Founders of a company he is a partner on | 2023 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/poolside-is-scaling-a-team-to-help-billions-code-and-realize-agi/) |\n| Mandiant, Atlassian, ForeScout | Independent board member | n/d | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Salem) |\n| Ajay Agarwal (BCV) | Co-executive officer on BCV Form Ds | n/d | [src](https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?company=bain+capital+venture&type=D&owner=include&count=100&action=getcompany) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which BCV partner actually led the poolside $500M Series B? The CMS lists Garg, Salem and Ojha but doesn't say who.\n- Crusoe check size, and whether BCV was in the 2019 Series A.\n- The CMS lists him on Redis (2012) and Sysdig/Lightbend (2013), but Wikipedia says he joined in 2014. Did he inherit those deals, or did he join earlier as an advisor?\n- Modal: which round BCV entered in 2026 (the CMS record was created 2026-06-02), and whether he leads.\n- Podcasts and talks weren't searched; the web-search budget was exhausted this run.\n\n## Sources\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/team/enrique-salem/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crusoe-climb-betting-on-power-before-ai-was-cool/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/prophet-security-is-using-ai-to-cut-mean-time-to-response-by-10x-for-security-operations-teams/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/ai-agents-are-armed-for-battle-adapting-security-infrastructure-and-shipping-new-solutions/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/ai-vs-ai-the-new-frontline-in-the-billion-threat-cyber-war/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/how-unstructured-is-powering-the-llm-data-stack/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/poolside-is-scaling-a-team-to-help-billions-code-and-realize-agi/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-answer-to-our-it-service-desk-woes-moveworks/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/moveworks-moves-work-beyond-chatbots/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/backedbybcv-the-future-is-evident/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-leads-105m-series-c-in-docker-enabling-modern-software-teams-to-ship-quickly-and-securely/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-ron-miasnik-our-newest-partner/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-slater-stich-and-saanya-ojha-our-newest-partners/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-more-cogent-approach-to-vulnerability-management/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/from-offensive-to-defensive-how-shalev-hulio-and-sebastian-kurz-are-keeping-nations-and-networks/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/10/02/ai-coding-startup-poolside-raises-500m-from-ebay-nvidia-and-others/\n- https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-series-b\n- https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-millions-in-new-capital\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Salem (secondary)\n- BCV Sanity CMS query (portfolio records tagged to him), linked in the Portfolio section\n- https://siliconvalleyinvestclub.com/2026/10/16/modal-labs-raises-750-million-at-a-15-75-billion-valuation/ (aggregator)\n", "person:eric-reiner": "\n# Eric Reiner\n\n## One-line take\nRuns the small fund that keeps showing up in the seed of Israeli-founded AI companies before the mega-rounds, and uses SPVs to stay in them [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2156197/000215619726000001/primary_doc.xml).\n\n## Crux\nHe wins by being local in Tel Aviv and useful in New York, and by riding next to Sequoia and Index instead of against them. His constraint is capital: a $140M fund cannot defend ownership in a company raising $550M, hence the SPV. He does not lead late rounds.\n\n## Differential insight\n- \"We are so perfectly positioned for this next wave that it's almost unfair\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/ld98pybhp).\n- He has moved to Israel and says Vine is neither an American nor an Israeli fund [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/ld98pybhp).\n- He signed the Form D for Vine Wonderful Opportunity II: $45.0M from 38 investors, first sale 2026-08-19 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2156197/000215619726000001/primary_doc.xml).\n- Fund III's Form D adds Adam Valkin (ex-General Catalyst) as a related person beside him and Povitsky [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2078928/000207892825000001/primary_doc.xml).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. Early, and adjacent to AI-native automation (Wonderful) and AI infrastructure (Sail Research, Ornn), but not a research-talent investor. Worth knowing as a co-investor in Israeli-founded AI seeds and as someone who syndicates SPVs.\n\n## Background\n- Investor at Insight Partners and TA Associates; co-founded Vine before 30; Forbes 30 Under 30 [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/vine-ventures-closes-fund-ii-with-140m-for-early-stage-technology-investments-tripling-fund-i-301573106.html).\n- Co-founded Sinai Ventures before Vine (aggregator profiles; not verified).\n- Sole related person on Fund I (2020) and Fund II (2021) Form Ds [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001812355/000181235520000002/primary_doc.xml).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.vineventures.com/ | no bio page |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | not found | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-10 — CTech Tech1 conference — interview with Amy Shapiro — [link](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/ld98pybhp) — Israel's next wave; only one direct quote published\n\n## How they think\n- Bets on Israeli founders selling to the US, in AI infrastructure, networking, cyber and defense [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/ld98pybhp).\n- \"Our mission is to identify founders and markets poised for massive outcomes.\" [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/vine-ventures-closes-fund-ii-with-140m-for-early-stage-technology-investments-tripling-fund-i-301573106.html)\n- Passes or mistakes: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n- CTech Tech1 interview [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/ld98pybhp).\n- Fund II release [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/vine-ventures-closes-fund-ii-with-140m-for-early-stage-technology-investments-tripling-fund-i-301573106.html).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nSee [firms/vine-ventures.md](../firms/vine-ventures.md). No release names him on a specific round; the items below are the ones tied to him directly.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Wonderful | Series C via SPV ($45M) | 2026-08 | no (Insight led) | 38 SPV investors | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2156197/000215619726000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Eon (non-AI) | Seed | 2024 | no (Sequoia led) | Meron; Eight Roads | [src](https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-israeli-cloud-backup-co-eon-raises-77m-1001491019) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing first-hand found. The Eon seed is told from Sequoia's side: Vine's Dean Meyer brought Shaun Maguire to a lunch with Ofir Ehrlich and Sequoia led a week later (see [dean-meyer](dean-meyer.md)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Dan Povitsky | co-founder | 2020 | [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/vine-ventures-closes-fund-ii-with-140m-for-early-stage-technology-investments-tripling-fund-i-301573106.html) |\n| Adam Valkin | partner; Fund III related person | 2025 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2078928/000207892825000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Dean Meyer | former Head of Israel, now Sequoia | until 2024-03 | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/bkrmstm29) |\n| Tiger Global; Bain Capital | reported backers of Vine | by 2022 | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/bkrmstm29) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who the 38 SPV investors are.\n- His own account of the Wonderful and Eon seeds.\n- A checked headshot (none on the firm site).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/ld98pybhp\n- https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/vine-ventures-closes-fund-ii-with-140m-for-early-stage-technology-investments-tripling-fund-i-301573106.html\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2156197/000215619726000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2078928/000207892825000001/primary_doc.xml\n", "person:eric-rosenblum": "\n# Eric Rosenblum\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the person Temasek's early-stage fund hired to do its US AI infrastructure and deep-tech deals, and he came from a fund that was already in two Xora companies.\n\n## Crux\nHis edge is a mix of operating and investing history: product at Google and Palantir, two exits as an operator, then early deep-tech investing at Foothill [src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push). At Xora he gets Temasek-scale follow-on behind him, but he is new, he has no announced Xora deal yet, and Xora's own team page did not list him on 2026-11-11 [src](https://xora.vc/team).\n\n## Differential insight\n- He was hired out of the co-investor syndicate: Foothill's portfolio named in the release includes Amperesand and HyperLight, both Xora companies [src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push).\n- His own pitch for Xora: \"operational capabilities as former entrepreneurs go far beyond capital\" [src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push).\n- Phil Inagaki's reason for the hire: a \"rare full stack background\" [src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. He will source US seed and Series A deals in AI infrastructure and chips for a fund that leads $50M seeds.\n\n## Background\nProduct leadership at Google and Palantir; COO of Drawbridge (acquired by LinkedIn); CEO of Smartpay (acquired by Ping An); Managing Partner of Foothill Ventures; General Partner at Xora from April 2026. Dates of the earlier moves are not in the release [src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://xora.vc | not on the team page as of 2026-11-11 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found in this pass.\n\n## How they think\n- AI is \"one of the most significant innovation cycles in decades\" in his words [src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push).\n- Nothing else found in this pass.\n\n## Read these first\n- The Xora appointment release (April 2026) [src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Amperesand | at Foothill; round not stated | | | Xora | [src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push) |\n| HyperLight | at Foothill; round not stated | | | Xora | [src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push) |\n| Coreshell | at Foothill; round not stated | | | | [src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push) |\n| AM Batteries | at Foothill; round not stated | | | | [src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Phil Inagaki | hired him; co-investor before that | 2026-04 | [src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push) |\n| Foothill Ventures | former Managing Partner | until 2026-04 | [src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push) |\n\n## Open questions\nHis first Xora deal; whether he still holds a Foothill role; why he is missing from the team page.\n\n## Sources\nhttps://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push ; https://xora.vc/team\n", "person:eric-schmidt": "\n# Eric Schmidt\n\n## One-line take\nThe individual who appears most often in new-lab seed rounds on both sides of the Atlantic, through at least four different vehicles, and whose name on a round tells you the lab has a policy and sovereign-capital story as well as a research one.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why founders want him:** he brings government access (he chaired the National Security Commission on AI and funds the Special Competitive Studies Project), a public platform, and capital that can scale from an angel cheque to control of a company. Labs with a national or open-weights angle (Mistral, H, Kyutai and Gradium, AMI Labs, Reflection AI) take him early.\n- **What constrains him:** breadth. He is in dozens of AI companies through overlapping vehicles and is now a full-time CEO at Relativity Space. He is a signal and a door-opener, not a working board member. Not every vehicle is well run: Steel Perlot had to ask Hillspire to cover payroll ([Forbes, 2023-10-23](https://www.forbes.com.au/news/innovation/former-google-ceo-launched-a-100-million-company-with-his-girlfriend-its-not-going-well/)).\n- **What he'd never do (inferred from his writing):** back a single national sprint to superintelligence. His paper with Dan Hendrycks and Alexandr Wang argues such a bid invites sabotage ([Superintelligence Strategy](https://www.nationalsecurity.ai/)).\n\n## The vehicle\n| Vehicle | What it is | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Personal (\"angel\") | Company releases name him as an individual: Anthropic A, Inflection, Mistral seed, H, Periodic Labs, Genesis AI, Gradium, AMI Labs, Vercept | ledger rounds; [GeekWire on Vercept](https://www.geekwire.com/2025/seattle-startup-backed-by-former-google-ceo-lands-16m-to-automate-repetitive-tasks-on-a-computer/) |\n| Hillspire | Family office of Eric and Wendy Schmidt; he is co-manager of Hillspire Holdings ([Alphabet 13G/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1652044/000095017025109161/primary_doc.xml)). Named in General Intuition A, Mistral D, Adapter, Hooglee. A third-party blog says CNBC ranked it the most active direct-investing US family office in 2025 with 15 deals (not verified) | ledger; [familyofficehub](https://familyofficehub.io/blog/the-eric-schmidt-family-office-hillspire/) (aggregator) |\n| First Spark Ventures | Deep-tech fund run by Manish Kothari; press calls it \"Eric Schmidt's First Spark Ventures\". Co-led Altera and SynthLabs seeds, Normal Computing seed | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/02/29/synth-labs-ai-alignment-startup-ex-google-ceo-eric-schmidt-microsoft/) |\n| Innovation Endeavors | He is co-founder, non-managing member and anchor LP; not a decision-maker on deals | ledger (lp_commitments) |\n| Schmidt Sciences / Schmidt Ventures | Philanthropic money: Kyutai's founding donation | ledger |\n| Steel Perlot | Incubator he chaired, launched with Michelle Ritter; invested in Pryon, Tristero, Keeta | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com.au/news/innovation/former-google-ceo-launched-a-100-million-company-with-his-girlfriend-its-not-going-well/) |\n| As an LP | Radical Ventures Fund III, Firstminute II | ledger (lp_commitments) |\n\nTypical cheque size: not published for any AI round. Which vehicle wrote a given cheque is often unclear: companies name \"Eric Schmidt\" where databases list Hillspire (Goodfire, Reflection AI, Gradium).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Open source is the gap he invests against.** \"China is likely to be the leader in open source unless something changes\" ([TED2025 transcript](https://singjupost.com/transcript-of-the-ai-revolution-is-underhyped-eric-schmidt/), 2025-04-11).\n- **Power is his stated bottleneck.** \"we need another 90 gigawatts of power in America\" (same talk; he says he testified on it that week).\n- **How he took over Relativity Space, in his words:** \"I bought a rocket company because it was like, interesting\" (same talk).\n- **His red line:** recursive self-improvement \"which you can't control\" is where he says systems should be unplugged (same talk).\n- **He founds as well as funds.** STAT's headline: \"AI biotech founded by ex-Google CEO Eric Schmidt is raising $150 million\" (Hologen, [2026-01-28](https://www.statnews.com/?p=1429525)).\n- **He writes small angel cheques too.** Vercept's $16M seed, led by Fifty Years, lists him beside Jeff Dean, Kyle Vogt and Arash Ferdowsi ([GeekWire](https://www.geekwire.com/2025/seattle-startup-backed-by-former-google-ceo-lands-16m-to-automate-repetitive-tasks-on-a-computer/); read through search results).\n- **France is a pattern.** He recurs beside Xavier Niel and Rodolphe Saadé: Kyutai (2023 donation), Mistral seed, H, Gradium seed, AMI Labs ([Sifted on Gradium](https://sifted.eu/articles/gradium-70m-seed-voice-ai); ledger).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is in the exact rounds I care about: $100M+ seeds for new labs (Inflection $225M, H $220M, Periodic $300M, AMI $1.03B, Genesis $105M). He is the co-investor most likely to be on a new lab's cap table before a lead is set. He does not lead or price.\n\n## Background\n- CEO of Google 2001 to 2011; Executive Chairman of Google, then Alphabet, to 2017 ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Schmidt)).\n- Co-founded Innovation Endeavors (2010) and Schmidt Futures (2017) (ledger).\n- CEO and controlling investor of Relativity Space since March 2025 (ledger).\n- Chairman of SandboxAQ ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/10/18/eric-schmidts-sandboxaq-aims-for-5b-valuation-for-its-ai-quantum-google-moonshot)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | not checked | |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.nationalsecurity.ai/ | Superintelligence Strategy paper site |\n| X | not checked | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-04 — TED2025 — The AI revolution is underhyped — [talk](https://www.ted.com/talks/eric_schmidt_the_ai_revolution_is_underhyped) — open-source and China, power, Relativity, the unplug criteria. Mined from a third-party transcript.\n- 2025-03 — Superintelligence Strategy (paper with Dan Hendrycks and Alexandr Wang) — [site](https://www.nationalsecurity.ai/) — the MAIM deterrence idea. Mined (abstract only).\n- Not catalogued: his books, congressional testimony, the 2024 Stanford talk, and many podcast interviews. This list is thin relative to what exists.\n\n## How they think\n- AI is under-hyped, not over-hyped; planning and agents are the step change (TED2025).\n- The US-China contest defines the field; chips, open source and energy are the levers.\n- He is pro-acceleration with named stop conditions, and against a state-led race for unilateral dominance.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [TED2025 talk](https://www.ted.com/talks/eric_schmidt_the_ai_revolution_is_underhyped): the whole worldview in 25 minutes.\n2. [Superintelligence Strategy](https://www.nationalsecurity.ai/): where he differs from the \"Manhattan Project\" camp.\n3. [Forbes on Steel Perlot](https://www.forbes.com.au/news/innovation/former-google-ceo-launched-a-100-million-company-with-his-girlfriend-its-not-going-well/): how loosely some of his vehicles are run.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked.\n\n## Portfolio\nEarly-stage AI cheques. \"Ledger\" means the row already existed in data/rounds.csv or data/deals.csv; \"new\" rows were added in this update. Cheque sizes are not disclosed for any of them.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Seed/pre-seed/A? | Vehicle as named | Lead | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Abacus.AI (as RealityEngines.AI) | Seed ($5.25M) | 2019-06 | seed | personal; co-led with Ram Shriram | co-lead | new · [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2019/06/12/realityengines-ai-raises-5-25m-seed-round-to-make-ml-easier-for-enterprises/) |\n| Anthropic | Series A ($124M) | 2021-05 | A | personal | Jaan Tallinn | ledger |\n| Inflection AI | Seed ($225M); Series A ($1.3B) | 2022-05; 2023-06 | seed, A | personal | see ledger | ledger |\n| Normal Computing | Seed ($8.5M) | 2022-12 | seed | First Spark | First Spark co-lead | ledger |\n| Mistral AI | Seed (EUR105M); Series D | 2023-06; 2026-09 | seed | personal; Hillspire | Lightspeed; Samsung | ledger |\n| Imbue | Series B extension ($12M) | 2023-10 | no | personal | n/a | ledger |\n| Kyutai | founding donation | 2023-11 | non-profit | Schmidt Sciences | Iliad, CMA CGM | ledger |\n| SynthLabs | Seed | 2024-02 | seed | First Spark | First Spark, M12 | ledger |\n| Altera / Fundamental Research Labs | Seed ($9M) | 2024-04 | seed | personal and First Spark | First Spark, Patron | ledger |\n| H Company | Seed ($220M) | 2024-05 | seed | personal | none named | ledger |\n| Stability AI | recap round; Series B | 2024-06; 2026-08 | no | personal | n/a | ledger |\n| Magic | $320M round | 2024-08 | no | personal | disputed | ledger |\n| Vercept | Seed ($16M) | 2025-06 | seed | personal | Fifty Years | new · [GeekWire](https://www.geekwire.com/2025/seattle-startup-backed-by-former-google-ceo-lands-16m-to-automate-repetitive-tasks-on-a-computer/) |\n| Trustible | Seed ($4.6M) | 2025-06 | seed | \"Office of Eric Schmidt\" | Lookout Ventures | ledger |\n| Genesis AI | Seed ($105M) | 2025-07 | seed | personal | Eclipse, Khosla | ledger |\n| Periodic Labs | Seed ($300M) | 2025-09 | seed | personal | a16z | ledger |\n| Reflection AI | Series B ($2B) | 2025-10 | no | personal (databases say Hillspire) | NVIDIA | ledger |\n| Gradium | Seed ($70M) | 2025-12 | seed | personal | FirstMark, Eurazeo (per ledger) | ledger |\n| Goodfire | Series B ($150M) | 2026-02 | no | personal (a blog says Hillspire) | B Capital | ledger |\n| AMI Labs | Seed ($1.03B) | 2026-03 | seed | personal | see ledger | ledger |\n| General Intuition | Series A ($320M) | 2026-06 | A | Hillspire | Khosla Ventures | ledger |\n| Adapter | cumulative $17.8M | 2026-07 | early | Hillspire | not stated | ledger |\n| Hologen | Seed (he is the founder) | not stated | seed | personal, with SW7 | n/a | new · [STAT](https://www.statnews.com/?p=1429525) |\n| Hooglee | incubation | 2025-01 | incubation | Hillspire | n/a | ledger |\n| Augment | Series B ($227M) | 2024-04 | no | Innovation Endeavors | see ledger | ledger |\n| Inception, Mithril, NewLimit | named backer; round unknown | not stated | unknown | personal | n/a | ledger |\n| Flower Labs | Pre-seed; Series A | 2023; 2024-02 | pre-seed, A | First Spark | First Spark led the pre-seed | ledger |\n| Radical Numerics | Seed | 2026-06 | seed | First Spark | not stated | ledger |\n| Retab | Pre-seed ($3.5M), \"via StemAI\" | not stated | pre-seed | StemAI | not stated | lead only; search summary, unverified |\n| SandboxAQ | he is chairman; investor per search summary | 2022 onward | no | personal | n/a | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/10/18/eric-schmidts-sandboxaq-aims-for-5b-valuation-for-its-ai-quantum-google-moonshot) |\n| Entrepreneurs First | Series D | 2026-03 | no | not stated | not stated | aggregator (CB Insights), unverified |\n| Pryon, Keeta, Tristero | via Steel Perlot | 2022-23 | unknown | Steel Perlot | n/a | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com.au/news/innovation/former-google-ceo-launched-a-100-million-company-with-his-girlfriend-its-not-going-well/) |\n\n**Hit pattern.** Of the seed and Series A cheques, the ones that became hot are Anthropic (Series A 2021), Mistral (seed 2023), Inflection (seed; later absorbed by Microsoft), Periodic Labs, Reflection AI and AMI Labs. Stability AI and Imbue were rescue or late cheques. Publicly stated passes or regrets on AI deals: none found.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Anthropic Series A (2021):** named as an individual beside Jaan Tallinn's lead, three years before the frontier-lab funding wave (ledger).\n- **French labs:** donor to Kyutai in November 2023 with Niel and Saadé, then an angel in its spin-out Gradium in December 2025 (ledger).\n- **Vercept:** an Allen Institute alumni team; Fifty Years led; he came in as one of four well-known angels.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Xavier Niel, Rodolphe Saadé | repeat French co-investors (Kyutai, Mistral, H, Gradium) | 2023 | [Sifted](https://sifted.eu/articles/gradium-70m-seed-voice-ai) |\n| Manish Kothari (First Spark) | runs his deep-tech fund | not stated | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/02/29/synth-labs-ai-alignment-startup-ex-google-ceo-eric-schmidt-microsoft/) |\n| Jeff Dean, Kyle Vogt, Arash Ferdowsi | co-angels in Vercept | 2025 | [GeekWire](https://www.geekwire.com/2025/seattle-startup-backed-by-former-google-ceo-lands-16m-to-automate-repetitive-tasks-on-a-computer/) |\n| Dan Hendrycks, Alexandr Wang | co-authors | 2025 | [paper](https://www.nationalsecurity.ai/) |\n| Michelle Ritter | Steel Perlot co-founder | 2021 | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com.au/news/innovation/former-google-ceo-launched-a-100-million-company-with-his-girlfriend-its-not-going-well/) |\n| Innovation Endeavors (Dror Berman) | anchor LP, non-managing | 2010 | ledger |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who at Hillspire sources and approves AI cheques (one database names Chuck Chai as president and CIO; unverified).\n- Cheque sizes in the lab seeds.\n- His podcast back-catalogue is not mined: books, the Stanford 2024 talk, congressional testimony.\n\n## Sources\nLinked inline; ledger rows in data/rounds.csv, data/deals.csv and data/lp_commitments.csv.\n", "person:eric-vishria": "\n# Eric Vishria\n\n## One-line take\nBenchmark's compute and inference partner. He was a co-lead in Cerebras's first institutional round (2016) and led the first institutional round at Fireworks AI (2024). On both, Sequoia came in later. His public view is that value builds up in chips and inference while foundation models depreciate ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/), [20VC](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-benchmarks-eric-vishria-on-where-is-the-value-in-ai-chips-models-or-apps--why-nvidia-will-not-be-the-only-game-in-town--the-commoditisation-of-foundation-models--which-ai-apps-have-sustaining-value-vs-hype-and-short-term-revenue)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he backs teams early, in the first institutional round, and holds the board seat for a decade. Cerebras is the proof. It was \"five founders and a deck\" and Benchmark's first hardware deal in ten years. He took the meeting about 18 months into being a VC and was won over by the team slide and Feldman's argument that GPUs are the wrong tool. He was still on the board at the 2026 IPO ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/)).\n- **What constrains him:** Benchmark's model. A five-or-so person equal partnership with no sector specialists, where the binding constraint is partner time with founders. He says plainly that this \"doesn't scale\" ([Uncapped Ep. 41](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton), [20VC](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-benchmarks-eric-vishria-on-where-is-the-value-in-ai-chips-models-or-apps--why-nvidia-will-not-be-the-only-game-in-town--the-commoditisation-of-foundation-models--which-ai-apps-have-sustaining-value-vs-hype-and-short-term-revenue/segment/688.99)).\n- **What he'd never do:** act as a sector specialist or a passive check-writer. He calls the founder relationship \"a sparring partner and a sounding board,\" not advice, and Benchmark always takes a board seat ([Uncapped Ep. 41](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton)). He also does not believe in foundation models as a durable store of value ([20VC, 2024-09](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-benchmarks-eric-vishria-on-where-is-the-value-in-ai-chips-models-or-apps--why-nvidia-will-not-be-the-only-game-in-town--the-commoditisation-of-foundation-models--which-ai-apps-have-sustaining-value-vs-hype-and-short-term-revenue)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Bill Campbell backed his hiring.** Gurley's own 2014 announcement says \"Our friend and mentor the great Bill Campbell\" advocated for Vishria's candidacy. Campbell knew him through RockMelt ([Above the Crowd, 2014-07-23](https://abovethecrowd.com/2014/07/23/meet-benchmarks-new-partner-eric-vishria/)). Benchmark followed him as a founder before hiring him; he never interned or worked as an associate there.\n- **On models vs. chips (2024-09):** \"Foundational models are the fastest depreciating asset in human history.\" In the same teaser he says \"I don't believe NVIDIA is going to be the only game in town\" ([20VC, recorded 2024-09-25](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-benchmarks-eric-vishria-on-where-is-the-value-in-ai-chips-models-or-apps--why-nvidia-will-not-be-the-only-game-in-town--the-commoditisation-of-foundation-models--which-ai-apps-have-sustaining-value-vs-hype-and-short-term-revenue)). This matches his book: Cerebras (chips) and Fireworks (inference), and no frontier-model lab.\n- **Pace:** in 2024 Benchmark had \"been more active than we've been since 2010 and 2011\" (same 20VC teaser).\n- **Inference is a hard engineering problem, not a commodity:** \"running those models is damn hard\". He names Fireworks, Baseten and Together, and AWS/Azure/GCP running \"stock open-source models\" ([Invest Like the Best EP.486, 2026-08-11](https://colossus.com/episode/sandcastles-and-silicon/); login-walled excerpt).\n- **How the Cerebras pitch actually went:** \"Most of that meeting was like a dog watching TV for me.\" He asked his assistant \"Why did you let me take this meeting?\" Benchmark's partners lacked hardware expertise, so he brought in founding partner Bruce Dunlevie to grill Feldman on packaging and cooling ([TechCrunch, 2026-05-14](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/)).\n- **The hard moment at Cerebras was 2022, not 2016:** \"You don't have a lot of traction on the company yet, so yeah, that was where it got really tough\" (same source).\n- **Decision rule at Benchmark:** \"No, it does not have to be unanimous.\" \"So one of the five of us falls in love with something,\" and the others then diligence and vote ([20VC, via Deciphr transcript excerpt](https://www.deciphr.ai/podcast/20vc-benchmarks-eric-vishria-on-why-career-vcs-have-advantages-most-under-appreciate-why-we-are-in-an-infrastructure-renaissance--what-makes-the-best-vc-partnership); episode date not found).\n- **On the economics:** \"This strategy is not financially maximal, for us.\" The real leap, in his view, came when the founders gave away residual economics to new partners: \"And then they gave it away. I think that was the leap.\" ([Uncapped Ep. 41, 2026-02-04](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton); the page's transcript is AI-generated and lightly edited.)\n- **First deal:** Confluent was \"my first investment\" (2014-11) ([20VC segment](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-benchmarks-eric-vishria-on-where-is-the-value-in-ai-chips-models-or-apps--why-nvidia-will-not-be-the-only-game-in-town--the-commoditisation-of-foundation-models--which-ai-apps-have-sustaining-value-vs-hype-and-short-term-revenue/segment/3517.981), [TechCrunch 2014-11-06](https://techcrunch.com/2014/11/06/confluent/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is the partner at a top early-stage firm with the strongest AI-compute record:\n- **Cerebras (AI chips):** Series A co-lead in May 2016 with Foundation (Vassallo) and Eclipse (Lamond, later Susan). Board seat from 2016 through the 2026 IPO. He kept following on through the G and H, including $225M from Benchmark's AI Infrastructure Fund in the H ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm), [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/)).\n- **Fireworks AI (inference):** led the ~$25M Series A, the first institutional round, around 2024-03, and joined the board. Sequoia (Sonya Huang) led the B about four months later ([TheSaaSNews](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/fireworks-ai-secures-25-million-in-series-a), [Fireworks Series C post](https://fireworks.ai/blog/fireworks-ai-series-c-funding)).\n- **Quilter (AI for PCB / electronics design):** Series A lead (~$10M, late 2023 / early 2024), alongside Lip-Bu Tan ([Quilter](https://quilter.ai/blog/series-b)).\n\nThere are two caveats for me. First, he has said publicly that foundation models are depreciating assets, so he is unlikely to anchor a $600M new-lab seed (no such deal found). Second, Benchmark's partners take a board seat on everything, so co-investing alongside him means following, not sharing the lead. He is a good co-investor signal on chips and inference, not on labs.\n\n## Background\n- Born 1979. B.S. in Mathematical and Computational Science from Stanford, graduating at 19 ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Vishria)).\n- Investment banking at Broadview International, then an early employee at Loudcloud, where he knew Ben Horowitz ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Vishria), [TechCrunch 2014](https://techcrunch.com/2014/07/23/eric-vishria-benchmark/)).\n- VP of marketing at Opsware at 26. HP acquired Opsware for $1.65B in 2007, and he became an HP Software VP ([TechCrunch 2014](https://techcrunch.com/2014/07/23/eric-vishria-benchmark/), [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Vishria)).\n- 2008-10: co-founded RockMelt (a social browser) with Tim Howes and was its CEO. Yahoo bought it in 2013 for a reported $60–70M, and he was a Yahoo VP of product (News, Weather) ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Vishria), [TechCrunch 2014](https://techcrunch.com/2014/07/23/eric-vishria-benchmark/)).\n- 2014-07: joined Benchmark as its fifth equal GP, the first addition in about six years: \"you'll be an equal partner and that's it\" ([TechCrunch 2014](https://techcrunch.com/2014/07/23/eric-vishria-benchmark/), [Above the Crowd](https://abovethecrowd.com/2014/07/23/meet-benchmarks-new-partner-eric-vishria/)).\n- 2026: listed as a managing member of the Benchmark GP on the 2026 Form D, alongside Altman, Fenton, Hu, Puttagunta and Randle ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2138636/000213863626000001/primary_doc.xml), as recorded in `firms/benchmark.md`).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found (benchmark.com has no team pages) | [benchmark.com](https://www.benchmark.com/) checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | — |\n| X | not verified | x.com returned 402; search found no confirmed handle (2026-11-10) |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | — |\n| GitHub | nothing found | — |\n| Podcasts (host) | none as host; frequent guest (see below) | Uncapped, 20VC, Invest Like the Best, The Peel, Logan Bartlett |\n| Forbes profile | [forbes.com/profile/eric-vishria](https://www.forbes.com/profile/eric-vishria/) | Bio; no Midas ranks shown on page |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-09 — Uncapped #57 — Andrew Feldman (Cerebras), with Vishria — [RSS](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss) — chips, supply chain, TSMC (per `people/jack-altman.md`; not re-fetched).\n- 2026-08-11 — Invest Like the Best EP.486, \"Sandcastles & Silicon\" — [colossus](https://colossus.com/episode/sandcastles-and-silicon/) — inference is \"damn hard\"; chapters on energy as the binding constraint, the Cerebras story, robotics, raising a growth fund, and \"What Geoff Hinton Got Wrong\" (only the excerpt was readable).\n- 2026-02-04 — Uncapped Ep. 41, \"The Benchmark Partnership\" (with Fenton, Puttagunta, Randle) — [link](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton) — \"not financially maximal\"; partner time is the constraint; \"the infrastructure cycle is going to continue.\"\n- 2025-06-05 — The Peel (Turner Novak), \"Going Zero to $100M ARR in 12 Months, Archetypes of Top AI Founders\" — [podwise](https://podwise.ai/episodes/4747284) — has a chapter on Cerebras as an \"insight-driven investment\"; no transcript read.\n- 2024-09 (recorded 09-25) — 20VC, \"Where is the Value in AI: Chips, Models or Apps\" — [audioscrape](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-benchmarks-eric-vishria-on-where-is-the-value-in-ai-chips-models-or-apps--why-nvidia-will-not-be-the-only-game-in-town--the-commoditisation-of-foundation-models--which-ai-apps-have-sustaining-value-vs-hype-and-short-term-revenue) — \"fastest depreciating asset in human history.\"\n- Date not found — 20VC, \"Why Career VCs Have Advantages… Infrastructure Renaissance\" — [deciphr](https://www.deciphr.ai/podcast/20vc-benchmarks-eric-vishria-on-why-career-vcs-have-advantages-most-under-appreciate-why-we-are-in-an-infrastructure-renaissance--what-makes-the-best-vc-partnership) — \"I think we're in the midst of an infrastructure renaissance.\"\n- 2023 (rebroadcast) — Turpentine VC E78, with Sarah Tavel — [metacast](https://metacast.app/podcast/turpentine-vc-venture-capital-and-investing/LUJoKypp/e78-why-benchmark-is-different-than-other-firms-with-sarah-tavel-and-eric-vishria/MFSDCetl) — not read.\n- Undated — Logan Bartlett Show EP 90, \"Behind the Scenes of Benchmark's Boldest Bets\" — [metacast](https://metacast.app/podcast/the-logan-bartlett-show/N2TWqA1K/ep-90-eric-vishria-general-partner-benchmark-capital-behind-the-scenes-of-benchmark-s-boldest-bets/etcmcgOo) — not read.\n\n## How they think\n- **Value flows to chips and inference, not models.** Models depreciate fast, NVIDIA won't be alone, and serving huge models efficiently is a hard engineering moat (20VC 2024; ILTB 2026).\n- **Long infrastructure cycle:** \"infrastructure renaissance\" (20VC) and \"the infrastructure cycle is going to continue\" (Uncapped 2026).\n- **Team first, even outside his expertise.** Cerebras was won on the team slide plus a simple contrarian insight about GPUs. He openly says he didn't follow the hardware and borrowed Dunlevie's judgment (TechCrunch 2026).\n- **Contrarian about scale:** he accepts that Benchmark's model is not \"financially maximal\" in exchange for partner engagement and equal economics (Uncapped 2026).\n- **Right:** Cerebras (2016 to the 2026 IPO), Confluent and Amplitude (both IPO'd in 2021). **Hard stretch:** Cerebras's 2022 raise with little traction, and the 2024 IPO attempt that was pulled ([`companies/cerebras.md`](../companies/cerebras.md)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. TechCrunch, 2026-05-14: how he nearly skipped the Cerebras meeting, plus the cost basis ([link](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/)).\n2. Uncapped Ep. 41: the Benchmark partnership in his own words ([link](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton)).\n3. 20VC, 2024-09: chips vs. models vs. apps ([link](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-benchmarks-eric-vishria-on-where-is-the-value-in-ai-chips-models-or-apps--why-nvidia-will-not-be-the-only-game-in-town--the-commoditisation-of-foundation-models--which-ai-apps-have-sustaining-value-vs-hype-and-short-term-revenue)).\n4. Invest Like the Best EP.486, \"Sandcastles & Silicon\": his most recent long-form view on AI infrastructure and energy ([link](https://colossus.com/episode/sandcastles-and-silicon/)).\n5. Bill Gurley's 2014 hiring post ([link](https://abovethecrowd.com/2014/07/23/meet-benchmarks-new-partner-eric-vishria/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing verified. No confirmed X account was found (x.com was paywalled and search returned nothing). His public voice is almost entirely podcasts, often joint appearances with Benchmark partners (Tavel, Fenton, Puttagunta, Randle) or portfolio CEOs (Feldman).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Confluent | Series A (his first deal) | 2014-11 | yes | — | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2014/11/06/confluent/), [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Vishria) |\n| Bugsnag | Series A ($7.2M) | 2015-07 | yes (Benchmark) | — | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2015/07/07/bugsnag-nabs-7-2-million-in-series-a-funding-from-benchmark/) |\n| Amplitude | Series A | 2015-08 | yes; board | — | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2015/08/05/amplitude-series-a/) |\n| Cerebras | Series A (~$25–27M) | 2016-05 | co-lead; board 2016–IPO | Foundation Capital; Eclipse | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm), [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/) |\n| Cerebras | Series G (Benchmark $25M) | 2025-09-30 | no | Fidelity; Atreides | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm) |\n| Cerebras | Series H (Benchmark AI Infra Fund $225M) | 2026-01/02 | no lead found | Alpha Wave; Fidelity | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm) |\n| Benchling | led; board (round not confirmed) | by 2020 | yes | — | [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Vishria), [TechCrunch 2020](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw) |\n| Contentful | led; board (round not confirmed) | — | yes | — | [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Vishria) |\n| Pixie Labs | Series A | 2020 | yes | — | [Forbes profile](https://www.forbes.com/profile/eric-vishria/) (acquired by New Relic) |\n| Quilter | Series A (~$10M) | 2023-12 / 2024-02 | yes | Lip-Bu Tan | [Quilter](https://quilter.ai/blog/series-b) |\n| Fireworks AI | Series A ($25M) | 2024-03 | yes; board | Sequoia; Databricks Ventures; Frank Slootman; Alexandr Wang | [TheSaaSNews](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/fireworks-ai-secures-25-million-in-series-a) (secondary), [Fireworks](https://fireworks.ai/blog/fireworks-ai-series-c-funding) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Cerebras (2016).** Feldman went first to Pierre Lamond (Eclipse), who had backed SeaMicro, and Eclipse wrote its memo in March 2016 ([Eclipse](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)). So Vishria was not first to the company, but he was a co-lead in the first institutional round. He nearly declined the meeting, was won over by the team slide and the line that GPUs \"suck for deep learning,\" and pulled in Dunlevie for hardware diligence. Benchmark bought about 80% of its shares for about $18M in early rounds and paid about $250M more later. At the IPO open the stake was worth about $3.3B ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/)).\n- **Fireworks AI (2024).** Benchmark led the first institutional round (~$25M) with Sequoia participating. Sequoia's Huang then led the $52M B in 2024-07, about four months later ([Fireworks B post](https://fireworks.ai/blog/fireworks-series-b-funding-sequoia)). This is the same Benchmark-then-Sequoia relay the repo sees at LangChain (`patterns/co-investor-gangs.md`). Vishria is the Benchmark partner on two of the four instances (Fireworks and Cerebras).\n- **Quilter (2023/24).** He led the A. Index (Nina Achadjian) led the $25.5M B in 2025-10, about 20 months later ([Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-era-for-electronics-design-our-investment-in-quilter/), [Quilter](https://quilter.ai/blog/series-b)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bill Campbell | Mentor; advocated for his Benchmark candidacy | by 2014 | [Above the Crowd](https://abovethecrowd.com/2014/07/23/meet-benchmarks-new-partner-eric-vishria/) |\n| Bill Gurley | Recruited and announced him at Benchmark | 2014 | [Above the Crowd](https://abovethecrowd.com/2014/07/23/meet-benchmarks-new-partner-eric-vishria/) |\n| Ben Horowitz | Worked together at Loudcloud / Opsware | ~2000s | [TechCrunch 2014](https://techcrunch.com/2014/07/23/eric-vishria-benchmark/) |\n| Tim Howes | RockMelt co-founder | 2008 | [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Vishria) |\n| Andrew Feldman | Cerebras CEO; board relationship | 2016 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/) |\n| Bruce Dunlevie | Benchmark founder; ran the Cerebras hardware diligence with him | 2016 | same |\n| Steve Vassallo; Lior Susan | Fellow Cerebras Series A-elected directors | 2016 / 2023 | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm) |\n| Sonya Huang (Sequoia) | Fireworks co-investor; led the B after his A | 2024 | [Fireworks](https://fireworks.ai/blog/fireworks-ai-series-c-funding) |\n| Lip-Bu Tan | Fellow Quilter backer | 2024 | [Quilter](https://quilter.ai/blog/series-b) |\n| Miles Grimshaw | Co-director on the Benchling board before Grimshaw joined Benchmark | by 2020 | [TechCrunch 2020](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw) |\n| Peter Fenton; Chetan Puttagunta; Everett Randle | Benchmark partners; joint Uncapped episode | 2026-02 | [Uncapped](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton) |\n| Jack Altman | Uncapped host, now inside Benchmark; co-hosted the Feldman episode | 2026 | [RSS](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss) |\n\n**LP links:** none found. I checked a web search for him as an LP or fund backer (only aggregator \"angel\" listings came up) and found no GP announcement naming him. No `-lp.csv` was written.\n\n## Open questions\n- Did he personally source Sierra or Sunday Robotics? An aggregator summary of ILTB EP.486 lists them with his name. The repo attributes Sierra to Fenton, and Sunday's B names Benchmark with no partner. Not attributed here.\n- Is he a managing member of the Benchmark AI Infrastructure Fund, or only the Cerebras deal partner?\n- The Fireworks Series A date (2024-01 vs. 2024-03) and whether there was a prior seed.\n- Airplane.dev: Vince Hankes (Thrive) praised an \"Eric Fishria\" board member, and Benchmark was an Airplane investor. Is Vishria Benchmark's partner there?\n- Full transcripts of ILTB EP.486 (paywalled) and The Peel (newsletter): the Cerebras, robotics, \"What Geoff Hinton Got Wrong,\" and growth-fund chapters.\n- Verified X and LinkedIn URLs.\n\n## Sources\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Vishria\n- https://techcrunch.com/2014/07/23/eric-vishria-benchmark/\n- https://abovethecrowd.com/2014/07/23/meet-benchmarks-new-partner-eric-vishria/\n- https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton\n- https://audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-benchmarks-eric-vishria-on-where-is-the-value-in-ai-chips-models-or-apps--why-nvidia-will-not-be-the-only-game-in-town--the-commoditisation-of-foundation-models--which-ai-apps-have-sustaining-value-vs-hype-and-short-term-revenue\n- https://www.deciphr.ai/podcast/20vc-benchmarks-eric-vishria-on-why-career-vcs-have-advantages-most-under-appreciate-why-we-are-in-an-infrastructure-renaissance--what-makes-the-best-vc-partnership\n- https://colossus.com/episode/sandcastles-and-silicon/\n- https://podwise.ai/episodes/4747284\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era\n- https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/fireworks-ai-secures-25-million-in-series-a\n- https://fireworks.ai/blog/fireworks-ai-series-c-funding\n- https://fireworks.ai/blog/fireworks-series-b-funding-sequoia\n- https://quilter.ai/blog/series-b\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-era-for-electronics-design-our-investment-in-quilter/\n- https://www.forbes.com/profile/eric-vishria/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2014/11/06/confluent/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2015/08/05/amplitude-series-a/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2015/07/07/bugsnag-nabs-7-2-million-in-series-a-funding-from-benchmark/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw\n", "person:erica-brescia": "\n# Erica Brescia\n\n## One-line take\nFormer GitHub COO (through the Copilot launch) who led Redpoint's $26M poolside seed. She says openly that she writes up to ~$20M checks, has never seen a term sheet fail to close, and has dropped \"founder-market fit\" in favour of very young, fast founders.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** two decades as an operator in open source and dev tools (BitRock 2005, Bitnami, then GitHub COO), plus a referencing network she says reaches anyone in one or two clicks ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2iYQ7k4Ogac)). Redpoint's operator recruits (Satish Dharmaraj, then Jason Warner, then her) produce deals internally. poolside was incubated inside the firm ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA)).\n- **Constraints:** she sees ~100 companies for every 2 she diligences, and closed 7 deals in her first 18 months. Her checks are capped by Redpoint's early-fund mandate (seed to B, mostly A). Every deal must pass an IPO-path test ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2iYQ7k4Ogac)).\n- **What she wouldn't do:** back a non-technical CEO of a developer-first company who has no technical co-founder ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2iYQ7k4Ogac)), or bet on an open-source project run by a single company with no neutral governance ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=msdC5AQvbOE)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- Her career runs on one intro chain. Satish Dharmaraj introduced her to Satya Nadella in 2012. That led to a Bitnami deal with Microsoft, then acquisition talks, then GitHub, and Dharmaraj later recruited her to Redpoint: \"is actually the guy who introduced me to\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA), 17:32). They first met when Dharmaraj ran Zimbra and she was trying to sell to him: \"he was the only customer we didn't close\" ([src](https://www.tbpndigest.com/story/2025-11-03/redpoints-erica-brescia-on-why-margins-dont-matter-yet-and-the-rise-of-the-20-year-old-founder/transcript)).\n- Her funnel: of every 100 companies she meets ~15 and takes ~5 to a second meeting. About 2 get real diligence, with a memo of roughly 8 core pages: \"out of every 100 companies you know I probably meet with\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2iYQ7k4Ogac), 25:56).\n- On term sheets: \"I have never had a deal fall apart after the term sheet.\" She says Redpoint's historical failures could be counted on one hand ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2iYQ7k4Ogac), 26:58).\n- poolside was incubated at Redpoint, and she calls the $26M seed \"Beefy\": \"we just announced a 26 million dollar seed round which is Beefy\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA), 26:25). In 2025 she said its own data centre was \"a plan from the day zero\" and that she does not expect a compute glut ([src](https://www.tbpndigest.com/story/2025-11-03/redpoints-erica-brescia-on-why-margins-dont-matter-yet-and-the-rise-of-the-20-year-old-founder/transcript)).\n- In 2008, BitRock walked away from VC term sheets: \"we had term sheets and everything and then the market really tanked\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qEchR6mcDow), 11:14). Bitnami's exit started when Microsoft heard \"we were gonna go raise actual institutional funding\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA), 9:02).\n- In 2018 she faulted Docker: \"they gave away so much initially without a clear business model\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=msdC5AQvbOE), 31:23). She later backed Docker founder Solomon Hykes's Dagger ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA)).\n- In July 2023 she named Restate (from the Flink creators) as a Redpoint seed before it was announced: \"we haven't actually officially announced it\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA), 29:38).\n- By late 2025 her founder profile had changed. Redpoint is backing \"20some founders who have never really had a job before.\" On AI apps she said \"I don't care about margins at all right now.\" ([src](https://www.tbpndigest.com/story/2025-11-03/redpoints-erica-brescia-on-why-margins-dont-matter-yet-and-the-rise-of-the-20-year-old-founder/transcript))\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-11)\n- Check size: \"range from the low single-digit millions up to around $20M.\" Redpoint's early fund IX is a $650M vehicle, and most of her 2023 deals were Series A ([src](https://www.saastr.com/doubling-down-erica-brescia-managing-director-at-redpoint-ventures/)). The early team writes \"up to 15 or 20 million dollars\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ePTfF_LVHvk), 2:30).\n- Pace: \"I've been here a year and a half I've done seven\" investments, five Series A and two seed ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA), 27:29).\n- Redpoint pays MDs equally rather than by deal: \"equal (not deal-by-deal) comp\" ([src](https://www.saastr.com/doubling-down-erica-brescia-managing-director-at-redpoint-ventures/)). LP demand can reach \"two or three times the interest,\" so partners cap their own commitments ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xis3suV4ZNQ), 22:25).\n- A pass: Redpoint looked at a viral open-source project that went from 0 to 12k stars and \"didn't end up investing.\" She puts more weight on followers and ~10% month-on-month community growth than on stars ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ePTfF_LVHvk), 6:57).\n- Bitnami raised ~$1M in total. Its founders offered to repay their convertible note before converting it at the cap ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YwtvwLxPSys), 12:31). She also says \"venture is not right for everybody\" ([src](https://console.dev/podcast/s04e02-building-tools-devs-love-erica-brescia)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** She personally led a frontier-lab-style seed: poolside's $26M seed in 2023, a foundation model for code that Redpoint incubated ([src](https://www.saastr.com/doubling-down-erica-brescia-managing-director-at-redpoint-ventures/)). She writes $10M+ checks, and her book is AI infrastructure, dev tools and AI security at seed and Series A, which overlaps with AI-native automation (Trunk Tools, Vega, JetStream). She is the natural Redpoint entry point for code-model and dev-infra labs. She is less relevant for chips, and her open-source and governance lens makes her cautious about single-company projects.\n\n## Background\n- Studied investment finance, not software ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA)). Worked at T-Mobile in 2005 while helping Daniel Lopez on BitRock ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YwtvwLxPSys)).\n- 2005-2019: co-founder of BitRock and then Bitnami (YC W13), as COO and later CEO. Bitnami grew to ~75 people on contracts with cloud vendors and was sold to VMware in 2019 ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA)).\n- c.2017-2018: investment partner at X Factor Ventures, a $3M fund spun out of Flybridge that wrote $100K checks into companies with female founders ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=htbXZ83meRo)).\n- 2019-06 to 2021-12: COO of GitHub. Over that time GitHub grew from about 1,000 to 2,000+ staff; she launched GitHub India and was there for the Copilot launch ([src](https://github.blog/2019-06-11-hello-github-im-erica-brescia), [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qEchR6mcDow)).\n- 2022-01 to present: Managing Director at Redpoint ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ePTfF_LVHvk), [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/erica-brescia/)). She also sits on the Linux Foundation board.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/erica-brescia/ | Redpoint bio and content list |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes Redpoint \"Fresh Ink\" memos (Zed, Trunk Tools, poolside) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/ericabrescia | not read this pass |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/ebrescia/ | |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/ebrescia | Profile README lists talks and interviews from her GitHub years |\n| Podcasts (host) | Unsupervised Learning (Redpoint), co-host | Also posted short founder-advice videos on her own YouTube channel (Apr-May 2023) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2017-12 — Top Founders (Nathan Latka) — Bitnami economics — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YwtvwLxPSys — revenue came from BD contracts with ~14 cloud vendors; ~$1M raised; note converted at the cap.\n- 2018-03 — Linux Foundation OSLS panel — Open Source Investments — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=msdC5AQvbOE — Docker critique; governance as a diligence filter.\n- 2018-07 — theCUBE — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=htbXZ83meRo — economics of the X Factor Ventures fund.\n- 2020-10 — Underscore VC Core Collective — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qEchR6mcDow — walked away from 2008 term sheets.\n- 2022-08 — Hanselminutes #853 — https://hanselminutes.com/853/redpoints-erica-brescia-supports-tech-founders — LP and GP mechanics at Redpoint.\n- 2022-11 — TLV Partners Brainfest — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ePTfF_LVHvk — check sizes; the passed-on viral OSS project.\n- 2022-12 — WILCO — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2iYQ7k4Ogac — her funnel, term-sheet record, IPO bar.\n- 2023-05 — Console DevTools S04E02 — https://console.dev/podcast/s04e02-building-tools-devs-love-erica-brescia\n- 2023-07 — Janakiram MSV \"Encounter\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA — intro chain, poolside incubation, deal pace.\n- 2025-11 — TBPN — transcript — margins, young founders, compute glut.\n- Full list of 86 items: `data/rows/erica-brescia-voice-media.csv`.\n\n## How they think\n- Developer experience comes first. A dev tool must be a 10x improvement, and if it isn't \"stop do not pass go\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gBH3HALEFz8)).\n- She sees cloud native as moving from unbundling to re-bundling: simplification bets (Xata, Restate, Railway) because operating Kubernetes has become too hard ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA)).\n- She is an AI optimist. In 2024 she wrote that within 5-7 years most code will be written by computers ([src](https://www.saastr.com/doubling-down-erica-brescia-managing-director-at-redpoint-ventures/)). After the Copilot launch she went through a short \"darkness\" worrying about coding jobs ([src](https://www.tbpndigest.com/story/2025-11-03/redpoints-erica-brescia-on-why-margins-dont-matter-yet-and-the-rise-of-the-20-year-old-founder/transcript)).\n- On open source: company-owned projects rarely draw outside contributors, and once you open-source something it is hard to go back ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ePTfF_LVHvk), [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=msdC5AQvbOE)).\n- A call that held up: she backed poolside's vertical integration (its own data centre) from day zero.\n\n## Read these first\n- WILCO 2022 (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2iYQ7k4Ogac): her funnel, diligence and term-sheet discipline in her own words.\n- Janakiram 2023 (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA): the intro chain, poolside incubation and her portfolio theses.\n- SaaStr \"Doubling Down\" 2024 (https://www.saastr.com/doubling-down-erica-brescia-managing-director-at-redpoint-ventures/): check sizes, fund size, why she led poolside.\n- TBPN 2025 (transcript link above): her current view on young founders, margins and compute.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing gathered this pass. Her X account (@ericabrescia) was not read. Redpoint amplifies her through Termsheet Teardown videos co-hosted with Jordan Segall.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| poolside | Seed ($26M) | 2023-05 | yes (Redpoint incubated) | Bain Capital Ventures | [src](https://www.saastr.com/doubling-down-erica-brescia-managing-director-at-redpoint-ventures/) |\n| Railway | Series A ($20M) | 2022-07 | not confirmed | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Mv1AoqjiEmc) |\n| Zed | Series A ($10M) | 2023-03 | Redpoint (with Patrick Chase) | | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/fresh-ink-welcoming-zed-and-the-future-of-coding-at-the-speed-of-thought/) |\n| Dagger | Series A | | | | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/erica-brescia/) |\n| Xata | Series A | | | | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/erica-brescia/) |\n| Restate | Seed | 2023 (announced after July) | | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA) |\n| Fixie | | | listed in her SaaStr bio | | [src](https://www.saastr.com/doubling-down-erica-brescia-managing-director-at-redpoint-ventures/) |\n| Trunk Tools | Series A ($20M) | 2024-08 | yes | Innovation Endeavors | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/trunk-tools) |\n| Vega | Series A | 2025-09 | | | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/vega) |\n| Fable Security | Series A | 2025-07 | | | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/fable-security) |\n| JetStream | Seed ($34M) | 2026-03 | yes (aggregator) | | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/jetstream) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **poolside:** she has known Jason Warner for 10+ years, since his Canonical days. Warner joined Redpoint about six months before her, and Redpoint incubated his company with Eiso Kant. Redpoint led the $26M seed, which she justified by founder-market fit (Copilot was built by Warner's team) ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA), [src](https://www.saastr.com/doubling-down-erica-brescia-managing-director-at-redpoint-ventures/)).\n- **Dagger:** a reunion with Solomon Hykes, from the Docker and Bitnami era ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA)).\n- **Bitnami sale (as founder):** Microsoft opened acquisition talks after hearing about a planned institutional raise, and VMware won the process. She stepped out of the deal to join Nat Friedman at GitHub ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Satish Dharmaraj | Redpoint partner; introduced her to Satya Nadella; recruited her | ~2008 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA) |\n| Jason Warner | Redpoint colleague; poolside co-founder | 10+ yrs before 2023 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA) |\n| Nat Friedman | Recruited her to GitHub | 2018-19 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA) |\n| Daniel Lopez | Co-founder of BitRock and Bitnami | 2005 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YwtvwLxPSys) |\n| Jordan Segall | Security co-lead; Termsheet Teardown co-host | 2022 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ePTfF_LVHvk) |\n| Patrick Chase | Co-led the Zed deal | 2023 | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/fresh-ink-welcoming-zed-and-the-future-of-coding-at-the-speed-of-thought/) |\n| Chip Hazard / X Factor Ventures | Fund partner | c.2017 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=htbXZ83meRo) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who is \"Lorra\", the fast-growing Brescia-backed company mentioned on TBPN (the transcript garbles the name)?\n- What size and terms did Redpoint take in the poolside Series A/B, and is Brescia on the board?\n- Her X posting, not yet read.\n- The Vintage VC Insights video (2025) and Founder Storiez (2026) were not captioned. Both are likely to cover her current decision rules.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u_wrxbGoeMA\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2iYQ7k4Ogac\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ePTfF_LVHvk\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qEchR6mcDow\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YwtvwLxPSys\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=msdC5AQvbOE\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=htbXZ83meRo\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xis3suV4ZNQ\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a6uIFI-sGl8\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gBH3HALEFz8\n- https://www.saastr.com/doubling-down-erica-brescia-managing-director-at-redpoint-ventures/\n- https://console.dev/podcast/s04e02-building-tools-devs-love-erica-brescia\n- https://www.tbpndigest.com/story/2025-11-03/redpoints-erica-brescia-on-why-margins-dont-matter-yet-and-the-rise-of-the-20-year-old-founder/transcript\n- https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/erica-brescia/\n- https://github.blog/2019-06-11-hello-github-im-erica-brescia\n", "person:erik-lundberg": "\n# Erik Lundberg\n\n## One-line take\nMichigan's CIO since the post was created in 1999, whose stated doctrine is that only a few venture managers matter and you must back them early; the office he runs anchored Sam Altman's small Hydrazine IV alone and holds an early $20M OpenAI stake [src](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally) [src](https://fortune.com/2023/12/19/sam-altman-university-michigan-venture-fund/) [src](https://www.govtech.com/education/higher-ed/university-of-michigan-expects-2b-windfall-from-ai-investment).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win.** A power-law view of venture (\"only a handful of managers actually create most of the value\") combined with a willingness to commit before managers are proven, because good capacity-constrained managers close to new LPs [src](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally) (II, 2015-07-09). Tenure is the edge: 25+ years in one seat, building long GP relationships (e.g. Hydrazine Fund II and Fund IV) [src](https://fortune.com/2023/12/19/sam-altman-university-michigan-venture-fund/). Peer CIO Scott Wilson (WashU) singles out \"what Eric's accomplished in Michigan\" over 20 years [src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) (~52:30).\n- **What constrains them.** Regents-approved policy: directs capped at 100bps of the LTP, illiquid managers generally under 3% without IAC review, commitments above 1% of LTP need committee approval [src](https://regents.umich.edu/files/meetings/09-24/2024-09-IX-1.pdf). Fund checks rarely exceed $50M [src](https://fortune.com/2023/12/19/sam-altman-university-michigan-venture-fund/). A public university, so commitments surface through FOIA (Fortune's 2023 list came that way).\n- **What they'd never do.** Use the endowment to support local or university startups for non-return reasons (\"That's not what we use the endowment for\"), or value new technology themselves: spinout deals need a credible VC lead (\"We're not technology experts\") [src](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally).\n\n## Differential insight\nCaveat: no podcast, Substack, X or talk by Lundberg was found. Web search budget was exhausted early in this session, so only known URLs were fetched. Everything below is from a 2015 Institutional Investor interview (press), a firm-site bio, or the 20VC transcript (Wilson speaking). Treat non-obviousness accordingly.\n- Emerging-manager doctrine, in his words: \"You've got to be early because these strategies can be cash-constrained.\" [src](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally) (2015-07-09).\n- Power law: \"only a handful of managers actually create most of the value in venture capital\" [src](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally).\n- Below top quartile: \"you make no money; it's that dramatic.\" [src](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally).\n- Contrarian geography bet early: \"About a third of our venture portfolio is in China now\" (2015) [src](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally). Also \"I think Africa has great promise.\" (same source), accessed via an unnamed Africa fund of funds.\n- Team design: \"I think everybody should be a generalist.\" and \"we make better decisions as a team when everybody weighs in\" [src](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally). Implication (our inference): a pitch can reach the whole team, not one venture specialist.\n- How venture access started: in the 1990s an Investment Advisory Committee member gave \"great help\" getting into the best funds; the member is unnamed [src](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally).\n- **Timing (institutional, not a personal deal):** U-M put $20M into OpenAI \"in the early days\", before Microsoft's 2019 investment; Khosla Ventures and the Aphorism Foundation put in $50M at about the same time; the stake is \"estimated to be worth $2 billion\" [src](https://www.govtech.com/education/higher-ed/university-of-michigan-expects-2b-windfall-from-ai-investment) (Detroit News via GovTech, 2026-05-11, from Musk v. Altman exhibits first reported by Business Insider). Lundberg's personal role and the intro path are undocumented; the Altman/Hydrazine relationship is the plausible route (our inference).\n- Peer signal: Wilson (WashU) names him first among endowments he most respects: \"what Eric's accomplished in Michigan\" [src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) (20VC, 2026-11-08, ~52:30; transcript reads \"Eric\").\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High fit, lower reachability.**\n- Doctrine fits an early, concentrated frontier-AI manager: be early, power law, long re-ups [src](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally).\n- Under him the office anchored a small talent-driven fund alone (Hydrazine IV; Dan Feder: Michigan is \"the only outside investor\") and backs Eclipse with >$50M, which THESIS.md names as the owner's type of fund [src](https://fortune.com/2023/12/19/sam-altman-university-michigan-venture-fund/).\n- Frontier-lab exposure that has paid: early OpenAI direct [src](https://www.govtech.com/education/higher-ed/university-of-michigan-expects-2b-windfall-from-ai-investment).\n- Ticket sizes ($15-75M fund checks) are far below WashU's ~$200M bar, so an LP check is plausible.\n- Reachability: he is the CIO of a ~$21B portfolio; the practical entry is the venture team (Dan Feder was senior MD in 2023; current role unverified). Access historically relationship-driven. Status in 2026 not reconfirmed (see Open questions).\n- Deals that matter to me: OpenAI (direct), Hydrazine II/IV and Apollo Projects (Altman funds), Eclipse Ventures.\n\n## Background\n- Native of Norway; came to the U.S. for college [src](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally).\n- BBA, University of Wisconsin; MBA (international business and finance), Ohio State University; dates not given (same source).\n- Corporate finance at Wisconsin Bell, then international equity analyst in Ameritech's pension department in Chicago, then investment strategist leading Ameritech's asset allocation and its U.S./international equity program (dates not given; same source).\n- 1999: recruited by Michigan's regents as its first CIO; endowment then $2.5B, ranked 17th (same source).\n- 2015: age 55, endowment near $10B (same source).\n- Listed as CIO in U-M's FY2025 annual report as \"L. Erik Lundberg, CFA\" [src](https://2025.annualreport.umich.edu/officers-staff/). Firm-site bio (undated) says he leads about 20 investment professionals and the portfolio grew to over $19B [src](https://ceritypartners.com/team/erik-lundberg/); this figure trails the FY2025 $21.2B endowment, so the bio is likely stale.\n- Awards per that bio: II Award for Excellence (endowments) 2007; Public Endowment CIO of the Decade 2015; II Allocators' Choice Endowment CIO of the Year 2024 [src](https://ceritypartners.com/team/erik-lundberg/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://2025.annualreport.umich.edu/officers-staff/ | U-M officer listing (FY2025) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found (not searched; search budget exhausted) | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. Search budget was exhausted before podcast/talk searches could run; worth a follow-up pass.\n- 2015-07 — Institutional Investor (print interview, not a podcast) — \"University of Michigan CIO Erik Lundberg thinks globally, locally\" — [link](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally) — be early with cash-constrained managers; a third of venture in China.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** venture is a power-law manager game; get in early before good managers close; alternatives-heavy (60% in 2015) [src](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally).\n- **Contrarian views:** large China venture allocation in 2015 and interest in Africa (same source); generalist team with no asset-class silos (same source).\n- **Calls right:** early OpenAI direct (reported ~$2B) [src](https://www.govtech.com/education/higher-ed/university-of-michigan-expects-2b-windfall-from-ai-investment); top-decile 20-year returns per U-M [src](https://2025.annualreport.umich.edu/uploads/fy25-financial-report.pdf). Personal attribution of specific calls is not documented.\n- **Calls wrong:** nothing found. The 2015 China weighting would be worth checking against later outcomes.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Institutional Investor, 2015-07-09: his own words on venture, emerging managers, spinouts and team design [link](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally).\n2. Fortune, 2023-12-19: FOIA list of U-M VC fund checks, including Hydrazine [link](https://fortune.com/2023/12/19/sam-altman-university-michigan-venture-fund/).\n3. U-M LTP Investment Policy Statement, 2024-09: the rules he operates under [link](https://regents.umich.edu/files/meetings/09-24/2024-09-IX-1.pdf).\n4. `lps/umich-endowment.md` in this repo: the institutional profile.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public social presence identified.\n\n## Portfolio\nLundberg is an allocator; these are U-M institutional positions made during his tenure, not personal deals.\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | early round, $20M direct | before 2019 (exact date unknown) | unknown | Khosla Ventures and Aphorism Foundation ($50M \"at about the same time\") | [GovTech/Detroit News 2026-05-11](https://www.govtech.com/education/higher-ed/university-of-michigan-expects-2b-windfall-from-ai-investment) |\n| Hydrazine Capital (fund) | LP: $105M Fund II, $75M Fund IV | Fund IV 2023 | sole outside LP in IV | n/a | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2023/12/19/sam-altman-university-michigan-venture-fund/) |\n| Apollo Projects (fund) | LP: $18.7M | unknown | n/a | n/a | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2023/12/19/sam-altman-university-michigan-venture-fund/) |\n| Eclipse Ventures (fund) | LP: >$50M | unknown | n/a | n/a | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2023/12/19/sam-altman-university-michigan-venture-fund/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **OpenAI:** discovered via exhibits in Musk v. Altman, first reported by Business Insider; U-M did not comment [src](https://www.govtech.com/education/higher-ed/university-of-michigan-expects-2b-windfall-from-ai-investment). Intro path and Lundberg's role: nothing found.\n- **Hydrazine IV:** Michigan was the only outside investor; Dan Feder (not Lundberg) described it as a \"longstanding relationship with Sam\" [src](https://fortune.com/2023/12/19/sam-altman-university-michigan-venture-fund/).\n- **1990s venture access:** opened by an unnamed IAC member [src](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| University of Michigan Investment Office | First CIO | 1999 | [II 2015](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally); [FY2025 report](https://2025.annualreport.umich.edu/officers-staff/) |\n| Ameritech | Investment strategist, asset allocation (prior) | until 1999 | [II 2015](https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally) |\n| Scott Wilson (WashU) | Peer CIO who respects him | n/a | [20VC ~52:30](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n| Dan Feder (U-M) | Colleague; senior MD of investments (2023) | unknown | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2023/12/19/sam-altman-university-michigan-venture-fund/) |\n| Renaissance Venture Capital Fund (Detroit) | Director | unknown | [Cerity bio](https://ceritypartners.com/team/erik-lundberg/) |\n| Father Flanagan's Fund for Needy Children | Director | unknown | [Cerity bio](https://ceritypartners.com/team/erik-lundberg/) |\n| Skillman Foundation | Investment advisory committee | unknown | [Cerity bio](https://ceritypartners.com/team/erik-lundberg/) |\n| Cerity Partners / Agility (OCIO) | Investment advisory committee | unknown | [Cerity bio](https://ceritypartners.com/team/erik-lundberg/) |\n| Alternative Investment Management, Inc. | CIO Advisory Council | unknown | [Cerity bio](https://ceritypartners.com/team/erik-lundberg/) |\n| TIFF; State of Michigan pension funds; Capital International EM Growth Fund; Okabena | Past adviser | unknown | [Cerity bio](https://ceritypartners.com/team/erik-lundberg/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **2026 status not reconfirmed.** Latest primary listing is the FY2025 annual report; the FY2026 report URL did not resolve and search budget was exhausted. Check for retirement/successor news (he was 55 in 2015, so ~66 in 2026).\n- Any podcast, conference talk or video interview (e.g. II Allocators' Choice 2024 coverage)? None found this pass.\n- His personal role in the OpenAI direct, and the exact date/round.\n- Is Dan Feder still the venture lead? Who would see a first-time AI manager?\n- Identity of the 1990s IAC member who opened venture access.\n- Did the 2015 China venture weighting pay off or get cut?\n- Pronouns: none confirmed from sources read; \"they\" used where needed.\n\n## Sources\n- Institutional Investor, 2015-07-09: https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/index%2ephp/article/2bsvdo3iiteanztl1k5xc/corner-office/university-of-michigan-cio-erik-lundberg-thinks-globally-locally\n- Fortune, 2023-12-19: https://fortune.com/2023/12/19/sam-altman-university-michigan-venture-fund/\n- GovTech / Detroit News, 2026-05-11: https://www.govtech.com/education/higher-ed/university-of-michigan-expects-2b-windfall-from-ai-investment\n- U-M FY2025 annual report, officers: https://2025.annualreport.umich.edu/officers-staff/\n- U-M FY2025 financial report: https://2025.annualreport.umich.edu/uploads/fy25-financial-report.pdf\n- U-M Regents IPS, 2024-09: https://regents.umich.edu/files/meetings/09-24/2024-09-IX-1.pdf\n- Cerity Partners bio (undated, firm site): https://ceritypartners.com/team/erik-lundberg/\n- 20VC with Scott Wilson, 2026-11-08: https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026 (local transcript: sources/transcripts/2026-11-08-20vc-scott-wilson-washu.txt, line 1107)\n- Not fetched (403): https://news.umich.edu/lundberg-appointed-to-new-position-as-chief-investment-officer\n", "person:erik-nordlander": "\n# Erik Nordlander\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. Main primary source: his 58-minute Software Engineering Daily interview (episode 1707, published 2024-07-03), read in full from the transcript SE Daily hosts [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt). The show's intro copy uses \"he\". X (@ejn) could not be read (x.com returned 402). LinkedIn was not read.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is GV's quiet deep-tech GP, and his two biggest frontier bets share a pattern. He was at the MIT competition where Lightmatter launched in 2017 and has backed it through its $4.4B Series D. He co-led CTRL-labs' Series A with Lux in 2018, saw it sell to Facebook, and is now back with the same founder, Thomas Reardon, and the same co-investor, Lux, in Flourish, a neuro-AI lab that raised $500M in 2026.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:**\n  - He is still a working engineer, so he builds conviction on technology before revenue exists. In his words, \"many of us at GV can speak that language\" [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n  - He follows his companies for 8 to 10 years and keeps backing the same founders.\n  - Alphabet is GV's only LP and lets him stay in \"every single round\" of a company.\n- **What constrains him:**\n  - He does not take hard lead-alone positions in the biggest rounds. Lightmatter's C-2 was a co-lead with Viking, and GV only participated in the Series B and Series D.\n  - The board seat at Lightmatter went to another GV partner, Tyson Clark, when GV led in 2019 [src](https://globalventuring.com/university/gv-sees-lightmatter-for-series-a-extension/).\n  - He says checks under about $1M don't make sense for a fund GV's size.\n- **What he'd never do:**\n  - Push portfolio companies onto Google products: \"I'd feel awful\" doing that, he says.\n  - Back things that are \"ethically not aligned with us\".\n  - Treat early revenue as the main signal. He is wary of companies that could turn revenue on \"two days later\", because others could copy them.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Flourish deal is a repeat-founder and repeat-co-investor bet.** This answers the firm file's open question about which Flourish he backed.\n  - The Flourish on his GV list links to flourishlabs.ai, which calls itself \"a Neuro AI company\" [src](https://www.flourishlabs.ai) [src](https://www.gv.com/team/erik-nordlander).\n  - Wired (Steven Levy, 2026-06-04) says Flourish is co-founded by Thomas Reardon. It describes Reardon as the co-founder of a company that built a mind-control wristband and that was acquired, after which he spent six years at Meta. Wired names Bezos, Lux Capital, Google Ventures and Catalio as backers [src](https://hn.jakechampion.name/reader/https://www.wired.com/story/jeff-bezos-is-funding-a-wild-hunt-for-the-brains-core-algorithm/).\n  - That earlier company is CTRL-labs. In 2018, GV and Lux co-led its $28M Series A, with Reardon as CEO [src](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2018/06/01/gv-helps-send-ctrl-labs-a-28m-signal/?format=md). GV led its $28M follow-on in February 2019, and Nordlander was the GV partner quoted [src](https://venturebeat.com/2019/02/22/ctrl-labs-raises-28-million-from-gv-alexa-fund-to-develop-neural-interface-technology/). Facebook bought it in September 2019, reportedly for $500M to $1B [src](https://techcrunch.com/2019/09/23/facebook-buys-startup-building-neural-monitoring-armband).\n  - CTRL-labs sits on Nordlander's exited list [src](https://www.gv.com/team/erik-nordlander). He alludes to it himself: \"I've had big exits to Facebook. I guess, now Meta\" [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n  - The CTRL-labs-to-Flourish link is my inference from the matching founder description, but every piece of it is sourced.\n- **Lightmatter origin: he was there before the company existed.** Nordlander says he was \"lucky enough to be there as they launched in an entrepreneurship competition\" [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt). A secondary source says he judged the 2017 MIT $100K, which Lightmatter won [src](https://pulse2.com/boston-lightmatter-gv-funding/).\n- **His own framing of Lightmatter:** a \"six, or seven-year overnight success\". He says when and how the market would come to it was very uncertain at founding [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- **Rule of thumb on stage:** \"my joke is I'll go as early as they'll let me\". He says that \"can often be at inception\", but typically a seed of several million dollars [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- **Floor on check size:** \"If it's under a million, the math doesn't make sense\" for GV [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- **Incentives:** \"I don't have any Google stock from GV\". He says GV partners are paid carry like any VC and GV can back companies \"good for Google, bad for Google\" [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- **Internal role that is not on gv.com:** he runs GV's \"macro group\", which brings in outside macro speakers. He says macro increasingly affects venture [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- **Founder network as a product:** for solo founders he connects \"these other pods of founders\" so they can compare notes [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- **Cockroach Labs origin: an ex-Google network deal.** He \"personally worked closely with Spencer\" (Kimball) at Google. He read Cockroach's first white paper in early 2014 [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- **A mistake he admits:** early at GV he \"was a little bit too concerned\" about startups' tech-stack choices [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- **Same co-investors at seed.** On both CTRL-labs and Lightmatter, Spark Capital and Matrix Partners were the earlier backers, and GV led a later $28M or $22M round in February 2019 [src](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2019/02/25/ctrl-labs-senses-gv-in-28m-funding-round/?format=md) [src](https://globalventuring.com/university/gv-sees-lightmatter-for-series-a-extension/). Spark and Matrix may be a recurring Boston deep-tech feeder for him. That is my inference.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** The firm-level GV file rated him medium because Flourish was unidentified. Identifying it raises him to high.\n- **Lightmatter** is AI semiconductors and compute: photonic interconnect for AI training and inference. He was present at its 2017 launch competition. GV led the $22M Series A extension (2019-02), participated in the B (2021), co-led the $155M C-2 at $1.2B (2023-12) and participated in the $400M D at $4.4B (2024-10).\n- **Flourish** is a new lab with a $500M war chest at a reported $2.5B valuation (2026-06). That is exactly the \"very large seed for a new lab\" shape you write $20M checks into. The co-investors are Bezos, Lux and Catalio.\n- **His co-investor pattern is Lux.** Lux co-led CTRL-labs with GV in 2018, backs Flourish, and co-led Flapping Airplanes' Series A alongside NVIDIA, with GV as an earlier backer. If you are working the NVIDIA + GV + Lux overlap, he is GV's Lux-adjacent partner on neuro and compute bets. Munichiello is the GV partner on Flapping.\n- **Caveats:**\n  - He is not a principal or recently promoted partner. He has been at GV since 2010.\n  - GV is a single-LP corporate arm, so it is never an LP prospect for you.\n  - He co-leads or follows rather than leading frontier rounds alone.\n  - His day-to-day deal flow is mostly dev tools: Warp, FlutterFlow, Blacksmith, StackBlitz.\n\n## Background\n- Taught himself to program young. Studied computer science and engineering at MIT and did graduate research at CSAIL [src](https://www.gv.com/team/erik-nordlander) [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- Joined Google after grad school and moved from Boston to the West Coast. Worked on search and Gmail infrastructure, including a distributed file system. Later moved to Google New York, where he led display ad serving, DoubleClick integration and ad ML models [src](https://www.gv.com/team/erik-nordlander).\n- While still an engineer, he helped Google corp dev evaluate acquisition targets for tech and talent, which he calls \"VC 101\" [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- \"at the end of 2010, I made the leap\" to GV [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt). He opened and runs GV's New York operations and leads its technical team [src](https://www.gv.com/team/erik-nordlander).\n- The date he became General Partner was not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.gv.com/team/erik-nordlander | GV bio and portfolio list (read 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.gv.com/news | GV posts under his byline: Warp (2025-08), Blacksmith (2025-09), Nebex (2026-06), Scope3 (2024-10), FlutterFlow, Neo4j, Dandelion |\n| X | https://x.com/ejn | He says he shares \"stuff about technology in our portfolio companies\" [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt). Not read (402). Follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/eriknordlander/ | Linked from GV page; not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-07 — Software Engineering Daily — \"Google Ventures with Erik Nordlander\" (ep. 1707, host Gregor Vand) — [transcript](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt) — Topics: check sizes ($few M to $25–30M), going \"as early as they'll let me\", Lightmatter as a slow-build success, Cockroach from the Google Spanner network, GV's carry-not-Google-stock incentives, and the macro group he runs.\n- 2025 — GV event with Zach Lloyd (Warp) on AI agents; written up in his GV post [src](https://www.gv.com/news/ai-agent-warp). Recording not found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Technology moat over early revenue.** \"many of our best bets\" had years of zero or marginal revenue while a technical moat was built. Cockroach and Lightmatter are his examples [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- **Patience as the edge.** He sees seed and A investments as possibly a \"10-year relationship\". He is fine waiting several years for the first dollar of revenue on complex builds [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- **AI in two buckets:** companies *for* AI (infrastructure, e.g. Lightmatter's bandwidth between chips for training and inference) and companies *with* AI (applications such as Typeface and Synthesia). In mid-2024 he said it \"still... feels quite early\" [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- **Contrarian on AI and jobs:** \"I still think we have way too few software engineers\" [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- **Valuation-agnostic:** if you are in the top 10–25% of companies, there are great returns \"regardless\" of the macro cycle [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- **Calls he got right:**\n  - Lightmatter (from the 2017 competition to $4.4B in 2024).\n  - CTRL-labs (co-led the A in 2018, Facebook exit in 2019).\n  - Warp: GV invested in April 2020, before agentic coding [src](https://www.gv.com/news/ai-agent-warp). He admits he first thought the idea was \"just not that interesting\" [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n\n## Read these first\n1. SE Daily ep. 1707 transcript (2024-07): the only long-form record of how he decides [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n2. Wired on Flourish (2026-06-04): the Reardon thesis he has now backed twice [src](https://hn.jakechampion.name/reader/https://www.wired.com/story/jeff-bezos-is-funding-a-wild-hunt-for-the-brains-core-algorithm/).\n3. His GV Warp/AI-agents post (2025-08) [src](https://www.gv.com/news/ai-agent-warp).\n4. His GV Nebex seed post (2026-06), a true seed lead outside AI [src](https://www.gv.com/news/nebex-space-economic-operating-system).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked. x.com/ejn returned 402. By his own description he posts about technology and portfolio companies [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n\n## Portfolio\nFrontier AI, AI chips and compute first, then other deals.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Lightmatter | Series A extension, $22M (Series A total $33M) | 2019-02 | Yes (GV led; Tyson Clark took the board seat) | Matrix, Spark (co-led the initial $11M tranche the year before) | [src](https://globalventuring.com/university/gv-sees-lightmatter-for-series-a-extension/), [GV case study naming Nordlander](https://gv.com/case-study/lightmatter) |\n| Lightmatter | Series B, $80M | 2021-05 | No (Viking led) | HPE, Lockheed Martin, Matrix, SIP Global, Spark | [src](https://globalventuring.com/university/lightmatter-illuminates-80m-series-b/?format=md) |\n| Lightmatter | Series C (May 2023, $154M) | 2023-05 | GV participation not confirmed | not checked | [src](https://www.maginative.com/article/lightmatter-closes-155m-funding-round-at/) |\n| Lightmatter | Series C-2, $155M at $1.2B+ | 2023-12-19 | Co-led | Viking Global (co-lead); existing investors | [src](https://www.maginative.com/article/lightmatter-closes-155m-funding-round-at/) |\n| Lightmatter | Series D, $400M at ~$4.4B | 2024-10-22 | No | T. Rowe Price (lead, per MicroVentures), Fidelity | [src](https://optics.org/news/15/10/36), [src](https://microventures.com/microventures-portfolio-company-lightmatters-history-and-milestones) |\n| Flourish (Flourish Labs, neuro AI) | Round type not stated; $500M raised, reported $2.5B valuation | announced 2026-06-04 | Not stated (Bezos the largest named backer) | Jeff Bezos, Lux Capital, Catalio | [src](https://hn.jakechampion.name/reader/https://www.wired.com/story/jeff-bezos-is-funding-a-wild-hunt-for-the-brains-core-algorithm/), [GV profile list](https://www.gv.com/team/erik-nordlander) |\n| CTRL-labs (exited to Facebook 2019) | Series A, $28M | 2018-05 | Co-led with Lux | Alexa Fund, Vulcan, Founders Fund; existing Spark, Matrix, Breyer, Fuel | [src](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2018/06/01/gv-helps-send-ctrl-labs-a-28m-signal/?format=md) |\n| CTRL-labs | Follow-on, $28M (total $67M) | 2019-02-22 | Yes | Alexa Fund, Lux, Spark, Matrix, Breyer, Fuel | [src](https://venturebeat.com/2019/02/22/ctrl-labs-raises-28-million-from-gv-alexa-fund-to-develop-neural-interface-technology/) |\n| Continua (social AI agents) | Seed, $8M | 2025-08-12 | Yes (GV led) | Bessemer, angels | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/08/12/google-vet-raises-8m-for-continua-to-bring-ai-agents-to-group-chats) (Nordlander attribution from GV profile list only) |\n| Clarifai (exited) | not checked | | | | [GV profile list](https://www.gv.com/team/erik-nordlander) |\n| Altana AI | not checked | | | | [GV profile list](https://www.gv.com/team/erik-nordlander) |\n| Graft, Adapter, Glass Imaging | not checked | | | | [GV profile list](https://www.gv.com/team/erik-nordlander) |\n| Warp | First GV investment | 2020-04 | not stated | | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/ai-agent-warp) |\n| Blacksmith | Seed, then Series A | 2025-09 (A) | Led the A | | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/ai-blacksmith-infrastructure) |\n| Nebex | Seed | 2026-06 | Yes | | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/nebex-space-economic-operating-system) |\n| Scope3 | Latest round | 2024-10 | Yes (with Roni Hiranand) | | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/scope3-ai-sustainable) |\n| Cockroach Labs, Neo4j, FlutterFlow, StackBlitz, Quadratic, Jetify, Crash Override, Dandelion, Gravity; exits LedgerX, Rocketrip, Carmera | not checked | | | | [GV profile list](https://www.gv.com/team/erik-nordlander) |\n\n**Timing: was he first?**\n- **Lightmatter:** no, as an investor. Matrix and Spark co-led the initial $11M tranche, and GV led the extension about a year later [src](https://globalventuring.com/university/gv-sees-lightmatter-for-series-a-extension/). But Nordlander met the team at the 2017 MIT $100K, before any institutional round.\n- **CTRL-labs:** no. Spark, Matrix, Breyer and Fuel were already investors when GV and Lux co-led the A.\n- **Flourish:** order of entry is unknown. Bezos was pitched in December 2025, and the funding was public on 2026-06-04.\n- **Continua:** yes, GV led the seed.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Lightmatter:**\n  - The company launched at the 2017 MIT $100K, which it won. Nordlander was there, per his own account, and was a judge, per a secondary source [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt) [src](https://pulse2.com/boston-lightmatter-gv-funding/).\n  - GV led the $22M extension in February 2019 and Tyson Clark joined the board [src](https://globalventuring.com/university/gv-sees-lightmatter-for-series-a-extension/).\n  - By December 2023 Nordlander was the GV voice on the C-2 co-lead, saying the demand for new compute and chip communication is \"unprecedented\" [src](https://www.maginative.com/article/lightmatter-closes-155m-funding-round-at/).\n  - On the D: \"We've proudly supported Lightmatter since the beginning\" [src](https://optics.org/news/15/10/36).\n  - How and when the deal responsibility passed from Clark to Nordlander was not found.\n- **CTRL-labs to Flourish:**\n  - GV and Lux co-led CTRL-labs' A (2018), and GV led the 2019 follow-on, with Nordlander praising the team of \"top neuroscientists, engineers, and developers\" [src](https://venturebeat.com/2019/02/22/ctrl-labs-raises-28-million-from-gv-alexa-fund-to-develop-neural-interface-technology/).\n  - Facebook acquired the company in September 2019 [src](https://techcrunch.com/2019/09/23/facebook-buys-startup-building-neural-monitoring-armband).\n  - After six years at Meta, Reardon co-founded Flourish with ex-Amazon S-team executive Rob Williams and Joshua Vogelstein. GV and Lux are both investors again [src](https://hn.jakechampion.name/reader/https://www.wired.com/story/jeff-bezos-is-funding-a-wild-hunt-for-the-brains-core-algorithm/).\n  - GV added Flourish to its CMS on 2026-06-16 (page metadata). Nordlander's own role in the round is not described publicly.\n- **Cockroach Labs:** an ex-Google founder deal. He had worked with Spencer Kimball at Google and read the first white paper in early 2014 [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n- **Warp:** Zach Lloyd \"came to me pretty early on\". Nordlander was initially skeptical, then came round to the view that the terminal is the most widely used developer tool and had not been improved [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| GV | General Partner; runs New York operations; leads technical team | 2010 | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/erik-nordlander) |\n| Thomas Reardon | Backed twice: CTRL-labs (A co-lead 2018, follow-on lead 2019), then Flourish (2026) | 2018 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2018/06/01/gv-helps-send-ctrl-labs-a-28m-signal/?format=md), [src](https://hn.jakechampion.name/reader/https://www.wired.com/story/jeff-bezos-is-funding-a-wild-hunt-for-the-brains-core-algorithm/) |\n| Lux Capital (Josh Wolfe) | Co-lead on CTRL-labs A; co-investor in Flourish | 2018 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2018/06/01/gv-helps-send-ctrl-labs-a-28m-signal/?format=md), [Wolfe's post](https://medium.com/lux-capital/mind-control-343b5d5f148f) |\n| Nick Harris, Darius Bunandar, Thomas Graham (Lightmatter founders) | GV investor of record | 2017 (competition); 2019 (investment) | [src](https://gv.com/case-study/lightmatter) |\n| Viking Global Investors | Co-led Lightmatter C-2 | 2023 | [src](https://www.maginative.com/article/lightmatter-closes-155m-funding-round-at/) |\n| Matrix Partners, Spark Capital | Earlier backers in both Lightmatter and CTRL-labs, before GV's 2019 leads | 2018 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/university/gv-sees-lightmatter-for-series-a-extension/), [src](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2019/02/25/ctrl-labs-senses-gv-in-28m-funding-round/?format=md) |\n| Tyson Clark (GV) | Took the Lightmatter board seat at GV's 2019 lead | 2019 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/university/gv-sees-lightmatter-for-series-a-extension/) |\n| Spencer Kimball (Cockroach Labs) | Former Google colleague; portfolio founder | Google era | [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt) |\n| Zach Lloyd (Warp) | Portfolio founder | 2020 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/ai-agent-warp) |\n| David Petrou (Continua) | GV led his seed (Nordlander attribution from profile list) | 2025 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/08/12/google-vet-raises-8m-for-continua-to-bring-ai-agents-to-group-chats) |\n| Roni Hiranand (GV) | Co-byline on Scope3 | 2024 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/scope3-ai-sustainable) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What was GV's role in the Flourish round (lead or participant, check size)? Did Nordlander meet Reardon again through the CTRL-labs/Meta relationship, or through Lux?\n- When and why did the Lightmatter relationship pass from Tyson Clark to Nordlander? Is Nordlander on the Lightmatter board now?\n- Did GV participate in Lightmatter's May 2023 $154M Series C?\n- Is NVIDIA an investor in Lightmatter or Flourish? Not checked.\n- What were GV's rounds in Clarifai, Altana AI, Graft and Adapter, and is Nordlander personally the partner on each?\n- When was he promoted to General Partner?\n- What does he post on X (@ejn)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.gv.com/team/erik-nordlander (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt (SE Daily ep. 1707, 2024-07-03)\n- https://gv.com/case-study/lightmatter\n- https://globalventuring.com/university/gv-sees-lightmatter-for-series-a-extension/ (2019-02-28)\n- https://pulse2.com/boston-lightmatter-gv-funding/ (2019-03-01, secondary)\n- https://globalventuring.com/university/lightmatter-illuminates-80m-series-b/?format=md (2021-05-10)\n- https://www.maginative.com/article/lightmatter-closes-155m-funding-round-at/ (2023-12-19)\n- https://optics.org/news/15/10/36 (2024-10-22)\n- https://microventures.com/microventures-portfolio-company-lightmatters-history-and-milestones (secondary)\n- https://www.flourishlabs.ai\n- https://hn.jakechampion.name/reader/https://www.wired.com/story/jeff-bezos-is-funding-a-wild-hunt-for-the-brains-core-algorithm/ (Wired, 2026-06-04, via reader mirror)\n- https://globalventuring.com/blog/2018/06/01/gv-helps-send-ctrl-labs-a-28m-signal/?format=md (2018-06-01)\n- https://globalventuring.com/blog/2019/02/25/ctrl-labs-senses-gv-in-28m-funding-round/?format=md (2019-02-25)\n- https://venturebeat.com/2019/02/22/ctrl-labs-raises-28-million-from-gv-alexa-fund-to-develop-neural-interface-technology/ (2019-02-22)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2019/09/23/facebook-buys-startup-building-neural-monitoring-armband (2019-09-23)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/08/12/google-vet-raises-8m-for-continua-to-bring-ai-agents-to-group-chats (2025-08-12)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/ai-agent-warp (2025-08-05); https://www.gv.com/news/ai-blacksmith-infrastructure; https://www.gv.com/news/nebex-space-economic-operating-system; https://www.gv.com/news/scope3-ai-sustainable\n", "person:erin-price-wright": "\n# Erin Price-Wright\n\n## One-line take\nAn ex-Palantir product lead who moved from Index to a16z American Dynamism. She now writes the a16z checks into the physical stack around AI: power for data centers (Base Power, Heron Power), AI for hardware design (Diode), minerals, motors and defense power. She doesn't do labs or chips. [src](https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** She is an engineer-operator (Stanford BS Engineering, Oxford MSc Maths and CS). She spent years at Palantir, from Forward Deployed Engineer to Head of Product for the data analytics and ML platform, and helped build its Industrials business [src](https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/). That gives her credibility with technical hard-tech founders, often ex-Tesla or ex-SpaceX, and with industrial buyers.\n- **Core thesis:** Power, more than GPUs, is the binding constraint on AI data centers. AI is best suited to complex, safety-critical physical work that hand-coded rules can't handle [src](https://a16z.com/ai-for-the-physical-world/). Her portfolio follows that line: grid-edge batteries (Base), solid-state transformers (Heron), mining (Mariana), motors (Westmag), PCB design (Diode).\n- **Constraint:** Her remit is American Dynamism: US industrial, energy and defense. That excludes pre-training labs, model companies and non-US deals. She works as a pair with an investing partner, usually Ryan McEntush, and her named rounds are mostly Series A and B, not inception.\n- **What she'd never do (inferred, no quote):** back \"AI-design software\" that doesn't ship a physical result. Her Diode memo says engineers want \"working boards\" [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-diode/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Power over GPUs (2024-05-06):** Her first a16z post argues power access, not GPU access, gates new AI data centers. She wrote \"Given our aging, overtaxed electric grid\" and called it \"a problem uniquely suited for AI to help solve\". [src](https://a16z.com/ai-for-the-physical-world/)\n- **Big timeline call:** \"our physical world will be completely transformed by AI\". She put the window at 5–10 years. [src](https://a16z.com/ai-for-the-physical-world/)\n- **Autonomy is a product problem:** In her stack essay (2024-09-05) she says winning operator trust is \"more of a product problem than a technical one.\" That is the Palantir FDE view. [src](https://a16z.com/a-software-driven-autonomy-stack-is-taking-shape/)\n- **Output, not tools:** The Diode memo, co-written with Zabie Elmgren, says engineers \"aren't asking for more AI-design software; they're asking for working boards.\" She prefers full-stack companies that deliver the physical outcome. [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-diode/)\n- **Full-stack again:** Mariana Minerals \"is full-stack. It designs, permits, finances, commissions, and operates.\" A China framing: \"We cannot outcompete China with whitepapers and pilots.\" [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mariana-minerals/)\n- **Personal origin of the Base Power thesis:** The Base Series B memo (2025-04-09) opens with a multi-day outage at her home while pregnant. She calls Base \"a bet on a software-native utility, purpose-built for a new kind of grid.\" [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-base-power/)\n- **Data-center demand as the Heron thesis:** \"AI data centers draw enormous amounts of power\". Heron is building a planned 40 GW US factory for solid-state transformers. Drew Baglino, ex-Tesla, founded it. [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-heron-power/)\n- **Lead at seed:** On Westmag (2026-06-02), co-written with Oliver Hsu: \"We're proud to lead their seed round\". This is the only round where an a16z post written by her states that a16z led. [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-westmag/)\n- **Index-era credit is murky:** a16z's bio credits her with Phaidra, BRINC and ClickHouse from Index [src](https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/). But Index's current company pages name Martin Mignot on Phaidra [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/phaidra/) and Vlad Loktev on BRINC [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/brinc/). Mignot wrote Index's \"Compute is king\" Phaidra post on 2024-07-02, after she left [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/compute-is-king-our-investment-in-phaidra/). Index reassigned her deals when she left.\n- **Twelve Labs:** Index's 2022-03-16 post quotes Kelly Toole, not her [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/unlocking-the-power-of-video-with-twelve-labs/). The Business Insider claim that she led it is in the roster lead notes only; no BI URL was read this run. Treat it as unconfirmed.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** She is close to compute but not in it. Her deals sit in the physical layer under AI (power, grid, cooling, hardware design), not in pre-training labs or AI chips.\n- **Most relevant to me:**\n  - Phaidra: RL-based cooling for AI data centers; now ships with NVIDIA, CoreWeave and Applied Digital [src](https://www.phaidra.ai/blog). Her named Index deal; year and round not stated.\n  - Heron Power: Series B, power hardware for AI data centers.\n  - Base Power: Series B.\n  - Diode: Series A, AI for PCB design, which is hardware design tooling.\n- **Why only medium:** Nothing at seed in labs or chips. The American Dynamism mandate is US-industrial, and a16z writes large rounds on its own. She is a useful co-investor or diligence contact for compute-power and physical-AI deals, not a lab-seed partner.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Arizona [src](https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/)\n- BS Engineering, Stanford; MSc Mathematics and Computer Science, Oxford [src](https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/)\n- Palantir: Forward Deployed Engineer, then helped build the Industrials business, then helped build and run Product. Head of Product for the data analytics and ML platform. Dates not found. [src](https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/)\n- Index Ventures: Partner, software infrastructure and applied AI. Start date not found; departure 2024-04 per the Index roster lead (headshot uploaded 2024/04). [src](https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/)\n- a16z: General Partner, American Dynamism. First byline 2024-05-06. [src](https://a16z.com/ai-for-the-physical-world/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/ | a16z bio and 24+ posts and podcasts, as of 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes on a16z.com |\n| X | https://x.com/espricewright | Linked from a16z bio; couldn't load (402), so follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | Not checked this run |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | a16z podcast | Hosted \"The SpaceX and Tesla Playbook for Hard Tech Startups\" (2026-03-27) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-12-25: a16z, \"Big Ideas 2026: Physical AI and the Industrial Stack\". Her segment is on \"factory-first principles\" (page summary; no transcript). [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/big-ideas-2026-physical-ai-and-the-industrial-stack/)\n- 2026-03-27: a16z, \"The SpaceX and Tesla Playbook for Hard Tech Startups\". She hosts Chandler Luzsicza (Galadyne) and Turner Caldwell (Mariana). No transcript. [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/the-spacex-and-tesla-playbook-for-hard-tech-startups/)\n- 2026-05-13: a16z, \"Energy, Minerals, and the Physical Stack Behind AI\", with Turner Caldwell and Drew Baglino. No transcript on the page. [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/energy-minerals-and-the-physical-stack-behind-ai/)\n- Also listed on her author page, undated: \"The Missing Power Layer of Modern Warfare\", \"Rebuilding The American Shipyard\", \"How Radiant and Heron Are Rethinking Power Generation and Delivery\", \"Tesla's Road Ahead: The Bitter Lesson in Robotics\", \"Autonomy Across Air, Land, and Sea\", \"America's Energy Problem\". [src](https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/)\n\n## How they think\n- **Power is the AI bottleneck.** The grid, transformers and batteries are where the AI build-out gets stuck. [src](https://a16z.com/ai-for-the-physical-world/)\n- **Full-stack beats tooling:** Mariana and Diode. [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mariana-minerals/)\n- **Software decoupling from hardware** opens autonomy beyond cars. The controls layer is the most bespoke, and trust is a product problem. [src](https://a16z.com/a-software-driven-autonomy-stack-is-taking-shape/)\n- **Ex-Tesla and ex-SpaceX operators** as founders: Baglino at Heron; Tesla/SpaceX alumni on her podcast. [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/the-spacex-and-tesla-playbook-for-hard-tech-startups/)\n- **Calls:** Her 2024 view that power, not GPUs, would gate data centers was early relative to the 2025–26 wave of grid and power deals. Phaidra later raised a $50M Series B led by Collaborative Fund (2025-10) [src](https://www.phaidra.ai/news).\n\n## Read these first\n- \"AI for the Physical World\" (2024-05-06): her founding thesis at a16z. [link](https://a16z.com/ai-for-the-physical-world/)\n- \"A Software-Driven Autonomy Stack Is Taking Shape\" (2024-09-05): the four-layer autonomy stack. [link](https://a16z.com/a-software-driven-autonomy-stack-is-taking-shape/)\n- \"Investing in Base Power\" (2025-04-09): grid thesis with a personal origin. [link](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-base-power/)\n- \"Investing in Diode\" (2025-07-23): AI for hardware design, outcome over tooling. [link](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-diode/)\n- \"Investing in Heron Power\" (2026-02-18): AI data-center power demand. [link](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-heron-power/)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X (@espricewright) could not be loaded this run.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Base Power | Series B | 2025-04-09 | Bio lists it among deals she led; post doesn't name the round lead | not stated | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-base-power/) |\n| Mariana Minerals | not stated | 2025-07-21 | Bio lists as led | co-signer Ryan McEntush (a16z) | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mariana-minerals/) |\n| Diode Computing | Series A | 2025-07-23 | Bio lists as led | co-signer Zabie Elmgren (a16z) | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-diode/) |\n| Heron Power | Series B | 2026-02-18 | Bio lists as led | co-signer Ryan McEntush | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-heron-power/) |\n| Chariot Defense | Series A | 2026-02-25 | not stated | co-signer Ryan McEntush | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-chariot-defense/) |\n| Ulysses | Series A | 2026-04-16 | not stated | co-signer Ryan McEntush | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-ulysses/) |\n| Westmag | Seed | 2026-06-02 | yes (\"proud to lead their seed round\") | co-signer Oliver Hsu | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-westmag/) |\n| XDOF | not stated | not stated | Bio lists as led | not stated | [src](https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/) |\n| Mind Robotics | undisclosed | 2026-03-11 | no (Sarah Wang's post; she's in the thanks) | Sarah Wang | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mind-robotics/) |\n| Galadyne, Swan, Airbase | not stated | not stated | not stated | | [src](https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/) (select-investment logos only) |\n| Phaidra (at Index) | not stated | not stated | unknown | Index now credits Martin Mignot | [src](https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/) |\n| BRINC Drones (at Index) | not stated | not stated | unknown | Index now credits Vlad Loktev | [src](https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/) |\n| ClickHouse (at Index) | not stated | not stated | unknown | | [src](https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/) |\n| Twelve Labs (at Index, disputed) | Index investment (round not stated in post) | 2022-03-16 | disputed | Kelly Toole quoted on the Index post | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/unlocking-the-power-of-video-with-twelve-labs/) |\n\n**Timing vs others:**\n- Westmag is the only deal with a sourced a16z lead at seed.\n- Base and Heron are Series B entries, so a16z was not first there.\n- **Phaidra (Index):** Helena was the largest investor in the $25M Series A in 2022 [src](https://www.helena.org/projects/phaidra/). Index's $12M came in 2024-07, after her departure [src](https://www.phaidra.ai/news). Whether Index invested before 2024 under her is unconfirmed.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Base Power:** The memo ties the thesis to her own multi-day home power outage. [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-base-power/)\n- **Westmag:** a16z led the seed. Founder David Hansen runs a semi-automated motor line in South San Francisco that is already shipping. [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-westmag/)\n- **Heron and Mariana:** Both founders appear on her podcasts, a repeat-founder content loop. [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/energy-minerals-and-the-physical-stack-behind-ai/)\n- **Intro paths:** No source names one.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ryan McEntush (a16z) | Junior co-signer on Mariana, Heron, Chariot, Ulysses | 2025-07 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-heron-power/) |\n| Zabie Elmgren (a16z) | Co-signer on Diode; Big Ideas 2026 panel | 2025-07 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-diode/) |\n| Oliver Hsu (a16z) | Co-signer on Westmag seed | 2026-06 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-westmag/) |\n| Sarah Wang (a16z) | Thanked on Mind Robotics | 2026-03 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mind-robotics/) |\n| Drew Baglino (Heron) | Portfolio founder; podcast guest | 2026-02 | [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/energy-minerals-and-the-physical-stack-behind-ai/) |\n| Turner Caldwell (Mariana) | Portfolio founder; two podcasts | 2025-07 | [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/the-spacex-and-tesla-playbook-for-hard-tech-startups/) |\n| Martin Mignot (Index) | Now holds Phaidra at Index | 2024-07 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/phaidra/) |\n| Index Ventures | Former Partner | until 2024-04 | [src](https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/) |\n| Palantir | Former FDE and Head of Product | | [src](https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What did she personally do on Phaidra at Index: first check, round and year? Did Index invest before the 2024-07 $12M?\n- Twelve Labs: find the Business Insider article and reconcile it with Kelly Toole and Shardul Shah.\n- What are the a16z round sizes and leads on Base, Heron and Diode? The posts don't state them.\n- XDOF: what is it, and what round?\n- Is there an X or podcast transcript with her verbatim on data-center power or chips?\n- Did Zabie Elmgren's status change? (Not on the current a16z team list per roster.)\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/erin-price-wright/\n- https://a16z.com/ai-for-the-physical-world/\n- https://a16z.com/a-software-driven-autonomy-stack-is-taking-shape/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-base-power/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-diode/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mariana-minerals/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-heron-power/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-chariot-defense/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-ulysses/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-westmag/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mind-robotics/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/big-ideas-2026-physical-ai-and-the-industrial-stack/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/the-spacex-and-tesla-playbook-for-hard-tech-startups/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/energy-minerals-and-the-physical-stack-behind-ai/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/compute-is-king-our-investment-in-phaidra/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/phaidra/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/brinc/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/unlocking-the-power-of-video-with-twelve-labs/\n- https://www.phaidra.ai/news\n- https://www.phaidra.ai/blog\n- https://www.helena.org/projects/phaidra/\n\nNote: the web-search budget ran out this run. Everything above comes from direct fetches of known URLs. Business Insider, X and LinkedIn were not read.\n", "person:erwann-le-lannou": "\n# Erwann Le Lannou\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. The web-search budget for this run was exhausted before this profile began. Everything below comes from direct fetches of sovereignai.gov.uk, xtxmarkets.com, company sites and Companies House. LinkedIn (HTTP 999) and f4.fund (HTTP 403) could not be read. Nothing here confirms his employer or title. Treat the profile as an identification note, not a confirmed profile.*\n\n## One-line take\nThe earlier pass placed him at XTX Ventures as a Partner. But the five companies his LinkedIn reportedly lists (Ineffable Intelligence, Isomorphic Labs, Callosum, OLIX, CuspAI) are the first five equity investments of the **UK Sovereign AI Fund** ([portfolio](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/portfolio)), and XTX is not in any of those rounds in the repo. He is most likely an investor at Sovereign AI, not XTX (my inference, unconfirmed).\n\n## Crux\n- **Identity is the crux.** The reported LinkedIn headline was \"Sovereign AI\" (search snippet, earlier pass; [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/erwann-le-lannou/), not fetchable). Sovereign AI's portfolio page lists eight companies: Callosum, Isomorphic Labs, Ineffable, CuspAI, OLIX, Basecamp Research, Augur and Potential Sciences ([src](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/portfolio)). His reported list is that portfolio minus the later deals. Basecamp is called the fund's sixth equity investment (September 2026) ([src](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-basecamp-research-to-help-design-the-next-generation-of-medicines)). So a list of \"the first five\" points to someone at the fund through its first five deals (inference).\n- **The XTX link is unsupported.** None of the five rounds in `data/rounds.csv` / `data/deals.csv` lists XTX Ventures or XTX Markets. The xtxmarkets.com/ventures page names no staff ([src](https://www.xtxmarkets.com/ventures/)). The only XTX attribution is an earlier search snippet and an f4.fund directory snippet (403). One possibility: he held an XTX role earlier and moved. This is unconfirmed.\n- **What he could personally do at Sovereign AI (if he is there).** This is fund-level only. The fund writes £1m-£10m direct equity cheques, usually alongside a private lead who negotiates terms ([FAQ](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/faqs)). Every deal post quotes Josephine Kant (Head of Ventures, later titled Investment Partner), and no post quotes him ([Ineffable](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-ineffable-intelligence), [Potential Sciences](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-potential-sciences-to-improve-the-data-behind-molecular-ai)). If he works there, he is probably a deal-team member doing diligence rather than the person who signs or prices (inference).\n- **What he would never do (fund constraint):** back a company without a lasting UK presence. The FAQ expects most of the portfolio to be UK-headquartered ([src](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/faqs)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- His reported portfolio exactly matches Sovereign AI's first five deals, which reattributes him from XTX to the UK government fund (inference; the fund's portfolio spans \"frontier AI to bioengineering\") ([src](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/portfolio)).\n- He is **not on the public team page**. That page lists only Suzanne Ashman (Managing Partner), Josephine Kant (Investment Partner), Will Bushby (Investor) and James Wise (Chair) ([src](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/about)). He may be off-page, seconded from government or another body, have left, or never have been there.\n- Whose money (if Sovereign AI): it is \"Funded by the UK Government\" and \"backed by over £600 million\". The government takes an equity stake, with follow-on expected from the British Business Bank ([FAQ](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/faqs)). There is no outside LP base.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, if confirmed at Sovereign AI.** The fund sits in the largest UK frontier-lab rounds of 2026 as a small, non-lead co-investor: Ineffable's $1.1B seed, Isomorphic's $2.1B B, CuspAI's $450M B and OLIX's rounds. Someone on its deal team sees every UK lab cap table early and can offer founders up to 1 million GPU hours of sovereign compute ([home](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/)). For a $20M seed cheque into a UK lab, Sovereign AI is a natural co-investor and source of compute, not a competitor for allocation. **Low, if he is at XTX**: no XTX deal is tied to him.\n\n## Background\n- **Unconfirmed:** an earlier pass's search snippet described a UCL computational-psychiatry PhD in reinforcement learning ([lead row source](https://www.linkedin.com/in/erwann-le-lannou/)). This was not verified this pass, and OpenAlex returned 429.\n- **Companies House:** an officer search for \"Erwann Le Lannou\" returns no exact match. The nearest is \"Christophe Erwan Michael Le Lannou\" (born 1972), who is likely a different person ([src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/search/officers?q=Erwann+Le+Lannou)). So no UK directorships were found for him.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | none found | not on sovereignai.gov.uk/about or xtxmarkets.com/ventures (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | none found | the Sovereign AI Substack is linked from requestforfounders; no post by him seen |\n| X | none found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/erwann-le-lannou/ | returned HTTP 999; contents known only from an earlier search snippet |\n| GitHub | none found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\nFounder route (fund-level): Sovereign AI's \"Get in Touch\" form ([about#contact](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/about)) or the compute application linked from the [FAQ](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/faqs). No personal contact route was found.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. The search budget was exhausted, so this is unsearched rather than confirmed absent.\n\n## How they think\n- Nothing in his own words was found. For the fund-level view, Kant says Sovereign AI backs founders \"bold enough\" to pursue foundation-model bets ([src](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/requestforfounders)).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Sovereign AI portfolio](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/portfolio): the list his reported LinkedIn mirrors.\n- [Sovereign AI FAQ](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/faqs): cheque size, whose money and what the government gets.\n- [Our first investments](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/our-first-investments): the Callosum first deal (16 April 2026) and the compute programme.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nHe is **not personally named on any deal by any source reached.** Below is the fund-level record for the companies his LinkedIn reportedly lists. These are UK Sovereign AI Fund deals, and his role in each is unconfirmed.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Callosum | Undisclosed (Sovereign AI's first equity deal); then Seed $100M | 2026-04-16; 2026-08-20 | no | Atomico (seed lead), Plural, DCVC | [src](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/our-first-investments) [src](https://www.callosum.com/team) |\n| Ineffable Intelligence | Seed ($1.1B at $5.1B) | 2026-04-27 | no | Sequoia, Lightspeed (co-leads), British Business Bank | [src](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-ineffable-intelligence) |\n| Isomorphic Labs | Series B ($2.1B) | 2026-05-12 | no | Thrive (lead), Alphabet, GV | [src](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-isomorphic-labs) |\n| CuspAI | Series B ($450M) | 2026-07-20 | no | Kleiner Perkins, NEA | [src](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-cuspai-to-reinvent-how-the-world-discovers-new-materials) |\n| OLIX | Series A ($220M); existing holder in Series B ($312M) | 2026; 2026-08-03 | no | Hummingbird, Plural, Creandum, Phoenix Court | [src](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-olix-the-startup-reinventing-the-infrastructure-that-powers-ai) [src](https://olix.com/news/company-raises-series-b) |\n\nRound dates other than Callosum's first deal come from the repo's existing rows.\n\n## Deal stories\nNone sourced. In every Sovereign AI post the investor voice is Josephine Kant. For example, on OLIX: \"I left my first meeting with James convinced...\" ([src](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-olix-the-startup-reinventing-the-infrastructure-that-powers-ai)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| UK Sovereign AI Fund | probable employer (unconfirmed; portfolio match) | unknown | [src](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/portfolio) |\n| XTX Ventures | reported \"Partner\" (unconfirmed; snippet only) | unknown | [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/erwann-le-lannou/) |\n| Josephine Kant | probable colleague; the fund's named deal voice | unknown | [src](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/about) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Read his LinkedIn from a logged-in browser: current title, start date, and whether XTX is a past role.\n- Is he a civil servant or secondee in the DSIT Sovereign AI Unit (`uk-sovereign-ai-unit`) rather than fund staff? This would explain why he is absent from the team page.\n- Did he hold the Callosum, Ineffable or Isomorphic diligence, or only list fund deals?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/ ; /about ; /portfolio ; /faqs ; /insights ; /requestforfounders\n- https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/our-first-investments\n- https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-ineffable-intelligence\n- https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-isomorphic-labs\n- https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-cuspai-to-reinvent-how-the-world-discovers-new-materials\n- https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-olix-the-startup-reinventing-the-infrastructure-that-powers-ai\n- https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-basecamp-research-to-help-design-the-next-generation-of-medicines\n- https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-potential-sciences-to-improve-the-data-behind-molecular-ai\n- https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-tendrils-to-develop-general-purpose-chips\n- https://www.xtxmarkets.com/ventures/\n- https://www.callosum.com/team ; https://olix.com/news/company-raises-series-b ; https://ineffable.ai/\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/search/officers?q=Erwann+Le+Lannou\n- Not fetchable: https://www.linkedin.com/in/erwann-le-lannou/ (999), https://f4.fund/firms/xtx-ventures (403)\n", "person:eryk-dobrushkin": "\n# Eryk Dobrushkin\n\n## One-line take\nAn ex-Databricks chief of staff who works as the second name next to Index's senior SF partners (Achadjian, Azam, Loktev). The one deal with their byline is a seed in a model-eval company used by frontier labs, which makes them a useful window into what Index's SF team is seeing early.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** operator credibility from running the CEO's office at Databricks, so they know how enterprise infra and AI platforms get built and sold [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/eryk-dobrushkin/). They are also plugged into the Harvard founder network: the Intelligence founders were Harvard CS students, and Dobrushkin holds a Harvard BA in applied math and economics [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/eryk-dobrushkin/).\n- **Constraint:** on every deal found, a more senior Index partner wrote the post or took the board seat: Achadjian on Physical Intelligence, Quilter and Revel; Azam on LiveKit; Loktev as co-author on Intelligence. Their own byline appears only once, on the Intelligence seed. On frontier AI they have been a late entrant, joining Physical Intelligence at the $5.6B Series B, about 20 months after the seed [src](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/robotics-startup-physical-intelligence-valued-at-5-6-billion).\n- **Would never (inferred):** no sign of solo-leading a frontier-lab seed. They work inside Index's team-deal model.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Intelligence deal origin:** the deal came in through three separate referrals in one day, followed by a same-evening meeting at Nina Achadjian's apartment. \"in the span of 24 hours, three people reached out independently\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/) (2026-08-03). The sourcing ran through networks and moved fast, not through an outbound process.\n- **Their thesis on the AI bottleneck:** the post they co-wrote argues the limit is proving grounds, not model capability. \"The constraint isn't intelligence or raw capability.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/)\n- **Frontier-lab adjacency:** the company they backed is used by the labs themselves. \"Google used Design Arena to test Gemini 3 ahead of launch\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/). The post also names OpenAI, Thinking Machines Lab, xAI and Meta as users, which gives Index a view into lab eval demand.\n- **The seed was priced against heavy traction:** \"scaling from $5 million to $60 million in ARR in six months\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/). A $7.9M seed on that much revenue means a small, high-quality entry.\n- **Tagged but silent:** on LiveKit, Quilter and Revel they appear only in the \"In this post\" tag line of posts written by others [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-era-for-electronics-design-our-investment-in-quilter/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/great-hardware-deserves-great-software-investing-in-revel/). This looks like the deal-team (junior) role.\n- No podcasts, talks, X account or Substack found. The Index profile lists no personal X account [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/eryk-dobrushkin/). The web search budget ran out this run, so podcast coverage is unverified.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** They have no new-lab, pre-training or chip deal at seed. Physical Intelligence is a frontier robotics lab, but they came in at a growth price. Intelligence (model eval and RL environments for labs) is the closest fit to THESIS.md, an AI-native layer sold to frontier labs. LiveKit (voice and physical-AI agent infrastructure) and Quilter (AI PCB design automation) are AI-native automation and infra. They are worth tracking as a rising SF partner who can bring Index's $10M+ capacity to robotics and eval deals, and as a route to Achadjian's book.\n\n## Background\n- Harvard University, B.A. in Applied Math and Economics (years not stated) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/eryk-dobrushkin/)\n- Boston Consulting Group, enterprise software focus (dates not stated) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/eryk-dobrushkin/)\n- Databricks, Chief of Staff to CEO Ali Ghodsi (dates not stated) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/eryk-dobrushkin/)\n- Index Ventures, Partner, San Francisco. Focus is enterprise software, infrastructure, robotics and AI across all stages. Join date not stated [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/eryk-dobrushkin/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/eryk-dobrushkin/ | Firm profile, 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | One Index Perspectives post (co-authored) |\n| X | nothing found | None listed on firm profile |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/eryk/ | Linked from firm profile; not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. The search budget was exhausted this run, so this is not a confirmed absence.\n\n## How they think\n- **Main thesis:** AI is limited by real-world proving grounds and feedback, not raw capability. They back the \"feedback and distribution layer for AI\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/)\n- **Recurring pattern:** AI meeting the physical or hardware world: Physical Intelligence (robot foundation models), Quilter (AI PCB layout), Revel (hardware test and control software), LiveKit (voice, video and physical AI agents) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/eryk-dobrushkin/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/revel/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/livekit/)\n- **Contrarian views:** nothing found beyond the eval thesis.\n- **Calls right or wrong:** too early to judge. Physical Intelligence was reportedly in talks at ~$20B in October 2026, up from the $5.6B Series B entry [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/physical-intelligence-talks-raise-1b-202852726.html).\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Proving AI in the Real World: Our Investment in Intelligence\" (2026-08-03, with Vlad Loktev): their only byline and the clearest statement of their thesis [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/)\n2. Achadjian, \"A New Era for Electronics Design: Our Investment in Quilter\" (2025-10-07): a deal they are tagged on [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-era-for-electronics-design-our-investment-in-quilter/)\n3. Azam, \"See, Hear, Speak\" on LiveKit (2026-01-22): a deal they are tagged on [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. They have no public X presence listed and no posts found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Intelligence (Design Arena) | Seed ($7.9M) | 2026-08-03 | Not stated (Index \"as part of\" the round) | Not named. Felicis venture partner Michael Chen is a Design Arena angel ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner)) | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/) |\n| Physical Intelligence | Series B ($600M at $5.6B) | 2025-11-20 | No. CapitalG led; Index was a new investor | CapitalG; Lux; Thrive; Jeff Bezos; T. Rowe Price; Bond; Redpoint; Sequoia; OpenAI | [src](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/robotics-startup-physical-intelligence-valued-at-5-6-billion) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/physical-intelligence/) |\n| LiveKit | Series C (size not disclosed in post) | 2026-01-22 | Yes, Index (Sahir Azam led and took the board seat) | Not named | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/) |\n| Quilter | Series B ($25.5M per Index; $25M per company) | 2025-10-07 | Yes, Index (Achadjian led and took the board seat) | Benchmark (Eric Vishria, Series A lead) named by the company; not confirmed in this round | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-era-for-electronics-design-our-investment-in-quilter/) [src](https://quilter.ai/blog/series-b) |\n| Revel (hardware test and control software) | Series B ($150M) | 2026-02-26 | Yes, Index (post by Achadjian) | Not named. Dylan Field is called an early investor | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/great-hardware-deserves-great-software-investing-in-revel/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Intelligence (Design Arena), seed 2026-08.** Founders Grace Li and Kamryn Ohly were Harvard CS students who built a game engine at a hackathon, applied to YC and then built Design Arena. Three people referred Grace to Index independently within 24 hours. Index met her that morning, then met both founders that evening at Nina Achadjian's apartment. The post is bylined by Venture Partner Vlad Loktev and Dobrushkin. Loktev is the senior name and Dobrushkin the deal partner. Timing: Index came in at seed after the product had reached 5.5M users and $60M ARR. Whether anyone invested before Index is not stated (YC is implied by the application, but participation is not confirmed) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/).\n- **Physical Intelligence, Series B 2025-11.** Index was a new investor in CapitalG's $600M round, about 20 months after Thrive's $70M seed (2024-03) and 12 months after the $400M Series A. The pairing was senior Nina Achadjian with Dobrushkin. Index was not first. Thrive, Khosla, Lux, Sequoia and OpenAI were in at seed [src](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/robotics-startup-physical-intelligence-valued-at-5-6-billion) [src](https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/).\n- **Quilter, Series B 2025-10.** Index led $25.5M. Achadjian took the board seat and Dobrushkin is tagged. Benchmark's Eric Vishria led the earlier Series A, so Index came in second [src](https://quilter.ai/blog/series-b).\n- **LiveKit, Series C 2026-01.** Sahir Azam led and joined the board. Dobrushkin and Sofia Dolfe are tagged [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/livekit/).\n- **Revel, Series B 2026-02.** Index led $150M. Achadjian wrote the post and Dobrushkin is tagged [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/revel/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ali Ghodsi (Databricks) | Former boss; Dobrushkin was Ghodsi's chief of staff | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/eryk-dobrushkin/) |\n| Nina Achadjian (Index) | Senior pairing on Physical Intelligence, Quilter and Revel; hosted the Intelligence founder meeting | 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/physical-intelligence/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/) |\n| Vlad Loktev (Index) | Co-author on the Intelligence seed | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/) |\n| Sahir Azam (Index) | Senior lead on LiveKit Series C | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/livekit/) |\n| Sofia Dolfe (Index) | Co-tagged on LiveKit | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/livekit/) |\n| Grace Li, Kamryn Ohly (Intelligence) | Founders backed at seed | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/) |\n| Boston Consulting Group | Former employer | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/eryk-dobrushkin/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did they join Index, and were they promoted from principal? LinkedIn (linkedin.com/in/eryk) would show the dates and whether fast_promotion applies.\n- Their Databricks tenure dates, and whether their network reaches Databricks/MosaicML alumni founding labs.\n- Who led the Intelligence seed, who the other investors were, and who the three referrers were.\n- Any podcast or X presence. This run's search budget was exhausted before it could be checked.\n- Whether they work on any undisclosed Index robotics or neolab seeds beyond the five companies listed.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/eryk-dobrushkin/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/livekit/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/physical-intelligence/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/quilter/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/revel/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-era-for-electronics-design-our-investment-in-quilter/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/great-hardware-deserves-great-software-investing-in-revel/\n- https://quilter.ai/blog/series-b\n- https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/robotics-startup-physical-intelligence-valued-at-5-6-billion\n- https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/physical-intelligence-talks-raise-1b-202852726.html\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner\n", "person:ethan-batraski": "\n# Ethan Batraski\n\n## One-line take\nHe is one of six managing members of Venrock's $650M Fund X GP. He is the firm's hard-tech lead: he led Skyryse, Atom Computing, Astranis and ABL in his first three years, then a run of open-source data-infra A rounds, and now leads Venrock's AI/compute/defense \"American Frontier\" thesis. He is not a frontier-lab seed writer.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He maps a field before he meets the team. On Atom Computing he spent years comparing quantum approaches before he met Ben Bloom ([src](https://medium.com/ethanjb/a-new-paradigm-in-quantum-computing-venrocks-investment-into-atom-computing-cea467daaeb)). With open-source founders he wins on technical fluency. Astronomer's CEO says he knew Airflow better than most of the company and that he arrived by cold email ([src](https://www.astronomer.io/blog/letter-from-the-ceo-our-story-so-far/)).\n- **His edge.** Ten-plus years as a product executive (Yahoo Search, Box through IPO, Facebook Ads) gives him a GTM and deployment lens. His Series A rule is to underwrite deployment risk, not science risk ([src](https://ethanjamesb.substack.com/p/the-valley-of-death-frontier-companies)).\n- **What constrains him.** Venrock leads $5M–$50M early rounds out of a $650M fund ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1998991/000110465924002265/primary_doc.xml)). That size rules out $100M+ lab seeds.\n- **AI model companies.** On Venrock's AI-model deals (Archetype AI, Oumi), the partner who is quoted and takes the board seat is Ganesh Srinivasan, not Batraski. Batraski writes the public thesis.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred).** Chase a subsidized hype cycle on momentum. He calls venture-funded booms \"very predictable\" ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/09/03/how-techs-booms-and-busts-have-evolved-over-time-venrocks-ethan-batraski/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **His deals close long before the press.**\n  - The Astranis Series B Form D shows a first sale on 2019-07-19 with him already a director. Venrock's lead was announced 2020-02-13, about 7 months later ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1715660/000171566020000001/primary_doc.xml), [press](https://www.satellitetoday.com/business/2020/02/13/microsatellite-startup-astranis-raises-90m-led-by-venrock/)).\n  - ABL's $49M Series A had its first sale on 2020-03-06 and was announced in Aug 2020 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1807044/000156761920006876/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - Decodable's A had its first sale on 2021-09-28 and was announced 2022-02-22 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1893157/000123191922000009/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - Venrock's promotion post alluded to this: \"three additional companies that have not been publicly announced\" ([src](https://www.venrock.com/insights/ethan-batraski-and-racquel-bracken-promoted-to-partner/)).\n- **He is in Venrock's inner circle.** The Venrock Associates X Form D ($650M, first sale 2024-01-04) lists only six people as \"Managing Member of the General Partner\": Batraski, Beim, Bracken, Kocher, Roberts and Srinivasan. Fund IX ($423.45M, 2021) had listed eight, including Ascher, Pakman and Samuels ([X](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1998991/000110465924002265/primary_doc.xml), [IX](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840617/000120919121003616/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **Astronomer came from a cold email.** The CEO wrote: \"good fortune came to us in an email from Ethan Batraski\" ([src](https://www.astronomer.io/blog/letter-from-the-ceo-our-story-so-far/)). The same letter calls him a \"General Partner\", but Venrock's own title for him is Partner.\n- **ABL Space Systems is now Long Wall.** His 2020 Series A lead has been recast as a missile-defense interceptor company \"Building on seven years of R&D at ABL\" ([src](https://www.lwall.com/)). Venrock's portfolio lists it only as Long Wall, with first investment \"Seed 2019\" ([src](https://www.venrock.com/companies/long-wall/)). A 2019 seed check is unconfirmed: the earliest Form D naming him is March 2020.\n- **Board seats that appear only in Form Ds.**\n  - Tembo (Postgres): $14.0M, first sale 2024-05-10, co-director DJ Patil ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2024088/000202408824000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - Osmo Systems: $450K sold, 2021-03-16 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1716708/000171670821000006/primary_doc.xml)). This is not the AI olfaction company Osmo. I left it out of the rows because the repo's id checker merges it into `osmo`.\n  - I found no announcement naming him on either deal.\n- **How he views AI.** He sees AI as another venture-subsidized boom, with GPU burn standing in for the DTC ad burn he watched from Facebook Ads: \"offering an AI service at a significant loss—VC dollars subsidizing it\" ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/09/03/how-techs-booms-and-busts-have-evolved-over-time-venrocks-ethan-batraski/)).\n- **His Series A test:** \"Customer interest is cheap. Customer commitment is expensive.\" ([src](https://ethanjamesb.substack.com/p/the-valley-of-death-frontier-companies)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For.** He is an early hard-tech lead at a fund that can write $10M+ checks. He was first institutional lead on Atom Computing (quantum compute, seed Aug 2018) and Skyryse (autonomy, seed+A 2018). He backs open, American AI (wrote the Oumi thesis) and AI-adjacent infrastructure (Euclid Power, compute and memory writing).\n- **Against.** He is not a pre-training or frontier-lab investor. Venrock's AI-model deals (Archetype AI, Oumi) run through Ganesh Srinivasan. Much of his 2020–2024 book is non-AI data infrastructure (Astronomer, Decodable, Meltano, SQLite Cloud, Tembo).\n- **The deals that matter to me:** Atom Computing, Skyryse, Astranis, and Euclid Power as an AI-energy angle.\n- **How I'd use him.** A co-lead or Series A follow-on partner for compute, quantum or defense-AI companies I seed. Ganesh Srinivasan is probably the better Venrock contact for AI labs.\n\n## Background\n- **Before Venrock.** All from Venrock's archived 2018 bio ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20181129072224/https://www.venrock.com/teammember/ethan-batraski/)). Dates are from Stackforce (aggregator) and unconfirmed.\n  - Consumer products at Symantec.\n  - Led Products & Experience at Yahoo! Search (Group Director, Search, ~2009–2012).\n  - Founding product and design leader at RiseSmart (acquired by Randstad).\n  - Headcase Labs (acquired).\n  - VP Product & Design at Adchemy (acquired by @WalmartLabs).\n  - VP Products and Design at Box through its IPO (~2012–2014).\n  - Partner at ProFounder, a pre-seed deep-tech angel syndicate (~2014–2017).\n  - Product leader at Facebook running a $15B revenue (Ads) business (~2016–2017). Fortune confirms he \"led ads at Facebook\" during the 2013–2016 DTC boom ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/09/03/how-techs-booms-and-busts-have-evolved-over-time-venrocks-ethan-batraski/)).\n- **At Venrock.**\n  - Joined in 2017 as Vice President, with a remit in robotics, aviation, space, autonomy, intelligent agents and farming ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20181129072224/https://www.venrock.com/teammember/ethan-batraski/)).\n  - Promoted to Partner on 2020-01-13 ([src](https://www.venrock.com/insights/ethan-batraski-and-racquel-bracken-promoted-to-partner/)).\n  - Manager on the Venrock IX Form D (2021). Managing Member of the GP on the Venrock X Form D (2024).\n  - Current focus: AI, compute and defense ([src](https://www.venrock.com/teammember/ethan-batraski/)).\n- **Patents.** The 2018 bio says 16 issued or pending; the current bio says lead or co-lead inventor on 12.\n- **Education.** Nothing found.\n\n## Where to find them\nChecked 2026-11-10.\n\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.ethanjb.com/ | Live. Terminal-style page listing 8 board companies and 13 dispatches |\n| Substack / blog | https://ethanjamesb.substack.com/ | Roughly monthly in 2025–26. Older posts at https://medium.com/ethanjb (10 posts, 2018–2022) |\n| X | https://x.com/ethanjb | Linked from his site and Venrock's bio. Follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/ethanjb/ | Linked from Venrock's bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://www.venrock.com/podcasts/ai-smart-stupid/ | Hosts Venrock podcast episodes (Running Through Walls / The Outer Join) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-09 — Fortune Term Sheet interview (Allie Garfinkle) — [link](https://fortune.com/2024/09/03/how-techs-booms-and-busts-have-evolved-over-time-venrocks-ethan-batraski/) — booms are predictable; AI GPU burn mirrors DTC ad burn; Dollar Shave Club was the one DTC winner.\n- 2023-06 — America Builds Podcast 006 — [Venrock link](https://www.venrock.com/news_item/america-builds-podcast-006-ethan-batraski-partner-at-venrock/) — not mined.\n- 2021-03 — \"Venrock's Ethan Batraski on the Promise of the Space Industry\" — [Venrock link](https://www.venrock.com/news_item/venrocks-ethan-batraski-on-the-promise-of-the-space-industry/) — not mined.\n- Undated — Venrock podcasts he hosts: AI: Smart/Stupid (with Melanie Mitchell), Empowering Data Engineers, plus GTM episodes with ex-Astronomer, DeepL, Confluent and Roblox executives — [list](https://www.venrock.com/podcasts/ai-smart-stupid/) — not mined.\n- AI Infra Summit speaker page — [link](https://www.ai-infra-summit.com/speakers/ethan-batraski) — not mined.\n\n## How they think\n- **\"American Frontier.\"** Over the next 30 years, capital will move from software to the physical world: AI, semiconductors and compute, energy, defense, and industrial roll-ups ([src](https://ethanjamesb.substack.com/p/the-american-frontier-a-trillion)).\n- **Series A is the valley of death for frontier companies.** Investors use SaaS mental models when the real risk is deployment: qualification, procurement and manufacturing ([src](https://ethanjamesb.substack.com/p/the-valley-of-death-frontier-companies)).\n- **Open-source AI as US strategy.** He framed DeepSeek as validation of the Oumi bet ([src](https://ethanjamesb.substack.com/p/why-we-invested-in-oumi-open-source)).\n- **Commercial open source.** He wrote a 3-part playbook (community, adoption, GTM) that underpins Astronomer, Decodable and Meltano ([src](https://medium.com/ethanjb/building-a-commercial-open-source-company-part-1-community-market-fit-3069dcac4ebd)).\n- **Contrarian.** He says VCs act as if every trend lasts forever, and that AI service losses are VC-subsidized ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/09/03/how-techs-booms-and-busts-have-evolved-over-time-venrocks-ethan-batraski/)).\n- **Calls so far.**\n  - Right: Skyryse reached a $1.15B Series C with him still on the board ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1685409/000168540925000005/primary_doc.xml)). Atom Computing is now a leading neutral-atom quantum company. Astranis was an early lead.\n  - Mixed: ABL's launch business was recast as Long Wall (missile defense).\n\n## Read these first\n- [The Valley of Death: Frontier Companies & the Series A](https://ethanjamesb.substack.com/p/the-valley-of-death-frontier-companies) — his underwriting checklist.\n- [The American Frontier](https://ethanjamesb.substack.com/p/the-american-frontier-a-trillion) — Venrock's current thesis map, which he leads.\n- [Venrock's investment into Atom Computing](https://medium.com/ethanjb/a-new-paradigm-in-quantum-computing-venrocks-investment-into-atom-computing-cea467daaeb) — how he surveys a field before backing a team.\n- [Fortune on booms and busts](https://fortune.com/2024/09/03/how-techs-booms-and-busts-have-evolved-over-time-venrocks-ethan-batraski/) — his cycle skepticism.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked. X (@ethanjb) was not fetched in this run. The Substack is cross-posted from ethanjb.com.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Skyryse | Series A (seed + A, $25M) | 2018-08-28 | yes (board) | Eclipse, Stanford, Industry Ventures, Trucks VC, Cantos, Engage | [src](https://globalventuring.com/skyryse-glides-steady-with-25m) |\n| Atom Computing | Seed (~$5M) | 2018-08 | yes | n/a | [src](https://medium.com/ethanjb/a-new-paradigm-in-quantum-computing-venrocks-investment-into-atom-computing-cea467daaeb) |\n| Astranis | Series B ($40M equity + $50M debt) | 2020-02-13 (first sale 2019-07-19) | yes (board) | a16z, Fifty Years, Refactor, YC, TriplePoint (debt) | [src](https://www.satellitetoday.com/business/2020/02/13/microsatellite-startup-astranis-raises-90m-led-by-venrock/) |\n| ABL Space Systems (now Long Wall) | Series A ($49M) | 2020-03-06 (first sale) | yes (board) | New Science Ventures, Lynett Capital, Lockheed Martin Ventures | [src](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2020/08/05/abl-reaches-new-heights-with-49m/) |\n| Astronomer | Series A ($13.6M) | 2020 | yes (board) | Bain Capital Ventures, Refinery, Grand, Sierra, Frontline | [src](https://www.astronomer.io/blog/letter-from-the-ceo-our-story-so-far/) |\n| Osmo Systems | Undisclosed (Form D $450K) | 2021-03-16 | unknown (director) | n/a | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1716708/000171670821000006/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Decodable | Series A ($20M) | 2022-02-22 (first sale 2021-09-28) | co-lead (board) | Bain Capital Ventures, DJ Patil, Olivier Pomel | [src](https://decodable.co/blog/decodable-closes-20m-round) |\n| Meltano | Seed extension ($8.2M) | 2022-06-08 | yes | GV, Uncorrelated, Data Tech Fund | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2022/06/08/gitlab-spinoff-meltano-raises-another-8-2m-for-its-open-source-dataops-platform) |\n| SQLite Cloud | Seed (~$4.3M) | 2023-07 | unconfirmed | n/a | [aggregator](https://www.cbinsights.com/company/sqlite-cloud/financials) |\n| Archetype AI | Seed ($13M) | 2024-04-05 | Venrock led; his role unconfirmed (Srinivasan on board) | Amazon Industrial Innovation Fund, Hitachi Ventures | [src](https://www.gunder.com/en/news-insights/client-news/archetype-ai-announces-dollar13m-seed-led-by-venrock) |\n| Tembo | Undisclosed (Form D $14.0M) | 2024-05-10 | unknown (director) | DJ Patil (director) | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2024088/000202408824000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Oumi | Seed ($10M) | 2025-01-29 | Venrock co-led with Obvious; his role partial (wrote thesis) | Obvious, Plug and Play, Ascend | [src](https://oumi.ai/press-release) |\n| Euclid Power | Series A ($20M) | 2025-09-04 | yes | HSBC AM, Spero, Toba, Designer Fund | [src](https://ethanjamesb.substack.com/p/why-we-invested-in-euclid-power-the) |\n| Phononic | Venrock Series A 2009 | n/a | no; listed as \"involved\" on his bio only | n/a | [src](https://www.venrock.com/teammember/ethan-batraski/) |\n\nAI, robotics and autonomy deals he is named on: Skyryse (autonomy), Atom Computing (quantum compute), Archetype AI (physical AI; his role unconfirmed), Oumi (open AI lab; thesis author), Euclid Power (AI-native energy services). On Astranis and ABL/Long Wall he is named, but they are space and defense hardware, not AI.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Atom Computing (2018).** He surveyed superconducting, ion-trap and spin approaches, concluded the field was still at the \"pre-transistor\" stage, then met Ben Bloom. Bloom had built the world's fastest atomic clock and later worked at Intel and Rigetti. Batraski judged neutral atoms a \"tractable engineering problem\" and led the seed in August 2018 ([src](https://medium.com/ethanjb/a-new-paradigm-in-quantum-computing-venrocks-investment-into-atom-computing-cea467daaeb)).\n- **Astronomer (2020).** The company was planning its A when COVID hit. Batraski emailed in cold, out-knew the team on Airflow, and led the round. The Airflow 2.0 release in Dec 2020 followed ([src](https://www.astronomer.io/blog/letter-from-the-ceo-our-story-so-far/)).\n- **Skyryse (2018).** Venrock led the $25M seed+A at stealth exit, with Eclipse in the syndicate ([src](https://globalventuring.com/skyryse-glides-steady-with-25m)). He has stayed on the board through the 2025 Series C. Intro path not found.\n- **Euclid Power (2025).** A thesis buy on AI's power bottleneck: he calls Euclid \"the infrastructure behind the infrastructure.\" Venrock led the A ([src](https://ethanjamesb.substack.com/p/why-we-invested-in-euclid-power-the)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Venrock | Vice President | 2017 to 2020-01 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20181129072224/https://www.venrock.com/teammember/ethan-batraski/) |\n| Venrock | Partner; Managing Member of Fund X GP | 2020-01-13 | [src](https://www.venrock.com/insights/ethan-batraski-and-racquel-bracken-promoted-to-partner/) |\n| ProFounder | Partner (pre-seed angel syndicate) | ~2014 to 2017 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20181129072224/https://www.venrock.com/teammember/ethan-batraski/) |\n| Ganesh Srinivasan | Venrock co-GP; public lead on Archetype AI and Oumi | 2024 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1998991/000110465924002265/primary_doc.xml) |\n| DJ Patil | Co-director at Tembo; angel in Decodable | 2024 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2024088/000202408824000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Lior Susan (Eclipse) | Co-director at Skyryse; Eclipse co-invested in his 2018 round | 2025 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1685409/000168540925000005/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Dan Berkenstock | Joined Astranis board alongside him | 2020 | [src](https://www.satellitetoday.com/business/2020/02/13/microsatellite-startup-astranis-raises-90m-led-by-venrock/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- How he met Skyryse's Mark Groden, and the intro path for the 2018 round.\n- Whether Venrock wrote a 2019 seed check into ABL, as its \"Long Wall, Seed 2019\" record suggests.\n- What his role is at Archetype AI and Oumi (board or observer), and who runs Venrock's AI-model deals day to day.\n- What Osmo Systems does, and whether his Tembo and Osmo seats are Venrock or personal.\n- Whether the five unnamed board-company tags on ethanjb.com (AI, AI-infra, compute, energy, defense) include stealth defense or compute deals not yet public.\n- Mine the 2026 Substack posts on memory chips, Claude Code and quantum, and the America Builds episode, for more voice.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.venrock.com/teammember/ethan-batraski/ and the WP API record (https://www.venrock.com/wp-json/wp/v2/teammember?slug=ethan-batraski)\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20181129072224/https://www.venrock.com/teammember/ethan-batraski/\n- https://www.venrock.com/insights/ethan-batraski-and-racquel-bracken-promoted-to-partner/\n- Venrock portfolio API: https://www.venrock.com/wp-json/wp/v2/investment (Euclid, Oumi, Archetype AI, Astronomer, Long Wall, Astranis, Atom Computing and Skyryse are tagged to him)\n- https://www.ethanjb.com/ ; https://ethanjamesb.substack.com/ ; https://medium.com/ethanjb\n- SEC Form Ds: Venrock IX (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840617/000120919121003616/primary_doc.xml), Venrock X (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1998991/000110465924002265/primary_doc.xml), Astranis, ABL, Decodable, Tembo, Osmo Systems, Skyryse (links above)\n- https://www.astronomer.io/blog/letter-from-the-ceo-our-story-so-far/\n- https://fortune.com/2024/09/03/how-techs-booms-and-busts-have-evolved-over-time-venrocks-ethan-batraski/\n- https://www.satellitetoday.com/business/2020/02/13/microsatellite-startup-astranis-raises-90m-led-by-venrock/\n- https://globalventuring.com/blog/2020/08/05/abl-reaches-new-heights-with-49m/\n- https://decodable.co/blog/decodable-closes-20m-round\n- https://techcrunch.com/2022/06/08/gitlab-spinoff-meltano-raises-another-8-2m-for-its-open-source-dataops-platform\n- https://oumi.ai/press-release ; https://www.gunder.com/en/news-insights/client-news/archetype-ai-announces-dollar13m-seed-led-by-venrock ; https://www.lwall.com/\n- Aggregators (secondary): Stackforce, TheOrg, CB Insights, Crunchbase\n", "person:ethan-kurzweil": "\n# Ethan Kurzweil\n\n## One-line take\nThe developer-platform partner behind Twilio and Twitch at Bessemer, now co-leading AI infrastructure Series As at Chemistry: Wafer (inference on AMD GPUs), Meticulous, Granola and Comfy.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** patience and product use. He tracked Wafer's CEO from YC demo day through a pivot, and won Granola by being a power user who sent a 19-point feedback list.\n- **Constraint:** one of three cheque-writers in a $355M fund; in rounds above about $50M he participates and does not lead.\n- **Would not do:** hand a company to a platform team. The firm's pitch is that the partners are the service.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Wafer: \"Emi was clearly smart, talented, passionate, and driven\", yet the first product was failing when they met ([src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/our-series-a-in-wafer)).\n- His thesis on Wafer is non-NVIDIA supply: \"the first provider to make AMD GPUs competitive with NVIDIA's\" (same post).\n- Granola: \"that email into the void led to an early investment\" ([src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/granola-from-users-to-believers)).\n- He wrote the post hiring an ex-Fireworks engineer because \"some of the most important questions are moving below the application layer\" ([src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/welcome-ayaan-one-layer-deeper)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. Inference clouds and compute markets at Series A are close to my AI-compute interest. Deals that matter: Wafer, plus Liquid Compute if it is his (partner not stated).\n\n## Background\n- The Wall Street Journal, then Linden Lab (Second Life) (dates not stated) ([src](https://www.chemistry.vc/team/ethan-kurzweil)).\n- Bessemer Venture Partners 2008-2024: Twilio, Twitch, HashiCorp, LaunchDarkly, PagerDuty, Intercom (board).\n- Co-founded Chemistry with Kristina Shen and Mark Goldberg, announced 2024-10-22.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.chemistry.vc/team/ethan-kurzweil | |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.chemistry.vc/blog | most frequent author on the firm blog |\n| X | https://x.com/ethankurz | follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/ethankurz/ | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-10 — Venture Unlocked #143 — The blueprint for starting a new firm with Chemistry Ventures — [link](https://ventureunlocked.substack.com/p/the-blueprint-for-starting-a-new) — show notes only; not transcribed.\n\n## How they think\n- Developer tools become consumer platforms (\"Vibe Code Me a River\").\n- Enterprise AI is won by builders who master trust and \"selective autonomy\".\n- Open systems beat closed ones for creative tooling (Comfy).\n- Infrastructure questions (compute pricing, silicon supply, power) now matter more than app features.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Our Series A in Wafer](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/our-series-a-in-wafer): how he sources and what he thinks about inference.\n- [From Users to Believers: Granola](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/granola-from-users-to-believers): the product-feedback sourcing play.\n- [The Selective Autonomy Imperative](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/the-selective-autonomy-imperative): enterprise agents thesis (not mined).\n- [Welcome Ayaan: One Layer Deeper](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/welcome-ayaan-one-layer-deeper): where the firm is heading.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed.\n\n## Portfolio\nChemistry-era, AI only.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Comfy (ComfyUI) | not stated | 2025-09-16 | not stated | n/a | [src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/why-we-invested-in-comfy) |\n| Granola | between Series B and C | 2025 | No | Index, Kleiner Perkins (C leads) | [src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/granola-from-users-to-believers) |\n| Meticulous | Series A $15M | 2026-07-15 | Yes | n/a | [src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/why-we-invested-in-meticulous) |\n| Wafer | Series A $40M | 2026-09-01 | Co-lead | Marathon, Y Combinator, angels | [src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/our-series-a-in-wafer) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Wafer:** met Emi Andere over a meal in Dogpatch shortly after YC demo day. The original product was not working. Andere pivoted to an inference cloud; they kept meeting until the round closed. At the A, Wafer was serving 2T+ tokens a month.\n- **Granola:** after a May 2025 offsite poll showed the whole firm ran on Granola, Chemistry emailed the founders a 19-point list of feedback and requests. Months later that became an investment, ahead of the $125M Series C led by Index and KP.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Kristina Shen | Co-founder; overlapped at Bessemer 2013-19 | 2013 | [src](https://ventureunlocked.substack.com/p/the-blueprint-for-starting-a-new) |\n| Mark Goldberg | Co-founder; worked deals and boards together | n/a | [src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/introducing-chemistry) |\n| Emi Andere (Wafer) | Backed after long courtship | 2026 | [src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/our-series-a-in-wafer) |\n| Bessemer Venture Partners | Former partner, 16 years | 2008-2024 | [src](https://www.chemistry.vc/team/ethan-kurzweil) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who at Chemistry did Liquid Compute and Proximal, and at what round?\n- Board seats at Wafer and Meticulous (not stated in the posts).\n- What he said on Venture Unlocked about non-consensus decisions (needs a transcript).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.chemistry.vc/team/ethan-kurzweil\n- https://www.chemistry.vc/post/our-series-a-in-wafer\n- https://www.chemistry.vc/post/granola-from-users-to-believers\n- https://www.chemistry.vc/post/welcome-ayaan-one-layer-deeper\n- https://www.chemistry.vc/post/introducing-chemistry\n- https://ventureunlocked.substack.com/p/the-blueprint-for-starting-a-new\n", "person:evan-hahn": "\n# Evan Hahn\n\n## One-line take\nHe brought a decade-scale Insight growth-deal machine (more than $2B deployed, 40+ companies) to Absolute Partners, the seed firm he co-runs with his sister Sabrina, so he is worth following as the operating half of a small fund that gets into frontier-AI rounds. Little of the frontier-AI book is demonstrably his.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** volume and sourcing. Forbes says he \"closed the most deals of anyone under 30\" at Insight [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/evan-hahn/). Insight's bio credits him with sourcing Databricks, Weights & Biases, Run:AI, JFrog, Keyfactor, Middesk and Granulate [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/team/evan-hahn/).\n- **What constrains him:**\n  - He has never been first money. Every Insight deal naming him is a priced Series A–D or growth round with earlier VCs already in.\n  - His best-documented personal deals are devops and security software (PlexTrac, Middesk, JFrog), not labs or pre-training.\n  - At Absolute, the frontier-AI record (Flapping Airplanes, fal, Together) is Sabrina's SH Fund record [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/).\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** lead or price a large neolab seed. Absolute is a participant-check firm, and he is its fund-operations signatory, not the deal brand.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Venture family (filing, high non-obvious).** A 2010 Schedule 13D filed by James Hahn of Alpine, NJ, says \"Evan Hahn (“Evan”) is the son of the Reporting Person\". It names Sabrina Hahn as his daughter in the same paragraph. James Hahn's vehicle, Alpine Venture Associates, owned 50/50 with his wife Lisa Yoo, is a venture firm: \"The Principal business of AVA is venture capital investment management\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1444183/000143774910002304/aa2hahn_sc13d-072210.htm). Sabrina's 2022 SH Fund Form D was filed from Alpine, NJ [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1912867/000092542122000003/primary_doc.xml). Matching this Evan to the 13D's Evan is my inference (same sibling names, same town). Whether AVA or the family is an LP in SH Fund or Absolute is **unconfirmed**.\n- **PlexTrac is the deal most clearly his own.** It is a Boise cybersecurity company, not AI. The release quotes him: \"said Insight Partners Vice President Evan Hahn\" [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/plextrac-raises-70m-series-b-funding-round-to-fuel-growth-in-proactive-cybersecurity-management/). He is listed first on the Insight team [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/portfolio/plextrac/) and as a Director on PlexTrac's own Form D [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1786090/000178609022000002/primary_doc.xml).\n- **His \"sourced\" claims don't always match Insight's deal teams.** The bio says \"Evan sourced and invested in companies including Databricks\" [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/team/evan-hahn/). Insight's Databricks page lists only Deven Parekh [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/portfolio/databricks/). incident.io lists only Kitty Qu [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/portfolio/incident-io/), and SpecterOps lists Triplett and Narayan [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/portfolio/specterops/). Read \"sourced\" as sourcing credit, not partner of record.\n- **He left Insight softly.** \"He now serves as a Senior Advisor to Insight Partners.\" [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/team/evan-hahn/). The WordPress API shows his bio was last modified 2026-05-27 [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/wp-json/wp/v2/sfuser/5541). That is the same day Forbes updated Sabrina's profile to name Absolute, \"cofounded with her brother Evan Hahn\" [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/). Insight is also named as an LP in Sabrina's fund [src](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/), so the exit looks friendly and possibly capital-linked.\n- **He runs Absolute's paperwork.** He signs both 2026 Form Ds, AP Capital I and AP Align I, as \"Manager of the General Partner of the Issuer\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2140744/000090514826003382/primary_doc.xml), [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149542/000090514826003539/primary_doc.xml).\n- **Scale at Insight:** he \"deployed more than $2 billion and sourced investments into more than 40 companies\" and was \"a board director or observer at 10 portfolio companies\" as of Jan 2024 [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/evan-hahn/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- His own named deals are growth-stage devops, security and 2021-era MLOps: Run:ai, W&B, Deci, Granulate. That is AI infra, but only at Series A–B and later.\n- The deal that touches THESIS.md directly is **Sable** (AI-native automation; $45M round 2026-07-16 led by Sequoia and 8VC, with Evan and Sabrina in as individuals) [src](https://www.finanznachrichten.de/nachrichten-2026-07/69050088-sable-raises-dollar-45m-to-build-the-first-ai-employee-that-can-click-see-and-explain-200.htm).\n- Absolute's portfolio fits closely (Flapping Airplanes, Mirendil, Periodic, Serval, fal, Together), but every source attributes it to Sabrina [src](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/).\n- **Why he matters to me:**\n  - He is probably the LP and operations interface at Absolute, a two-person fund backed by Sequoia and Insight as LPs that gets into $1B neolab rounds without leading.\n  - He is a warm path to Insight's AI MDs (Jaffe, Mathew, Wardi), whom he worked alongside on Run:ai, W&B and Deci.\n\n## Background\n- Harvard College; Men's Varsity Squash [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/team/evan-hahn/).\n- **Insight Partners, about 2018–2026:**\n  - Started as an Analyst [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/team/evan-hahn/). The join year is uncertain. Forbes (Jan 2024) says \"six years ago\", which gives about 2018 [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/evan-hahn/), and his earliest portfolio deal-team entries are JFrog (Sep 2018) and Keyfactor (Dec 2018). Insight's bio says \"a decade\", which would put it nearer 2016.\n  - Vice President by 2022-02-02 [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/plextrac-raises-70m-series-b-funding-round-to-fuel-growth-in-proactive-cybersecurity-management/).\n  - Principal by 2023-11-28; Forbes 30 Under 30 VC 2024 [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/award-2024-forbes-30-under-30/).\n  - Then Managing Director; date not found [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/team/evan-hahn/).\n  - Senior Advisor by 2026 [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/team/evan-hahn/).\n- **Absolute Partners, 2026:** co-founder and Managing Partner with Sabrina Hahn [src](https://www.privateequityinternational.com/institution-profiles/absolute-partners.html). AP Capital I LP Form D filed 2026-07-29; AP Align I LP filed 2026-08-10. Both show an indefinite offering, $0 sold at filing.\n- Forbes says he is \"the youngest person of color in a senior team member role\" at Insight [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/evan-hahn/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.insightpartners.com/team/evan-hahn/ | Insight Senior Advisor bio; checked 2026-11-10. No Absolute Partners website found |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Insight site search returns no posts by him |\n| X | not verified | Insight page links only corporate accounts; web search budget exhausted this run, so no handle confirmed |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | not linked from Insight or Forbes |\n| GitHub | not verified | common name, so there is a risk of a same-name engineer |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| Forbes profile | https://www.forbes.com/profile/evan-hahn/ | 30 Under 30 VC 2024; last updated 2024-01-01 |\n| PEI profile | https://www.privateequityinternational.com/institution-profiles/absolute-partners.html | Absolute Partners; lists both founders |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Insight's site search for \"Hahn\" returns only his team page and the 2023-11-28 Forbes award post [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/award-2024-forbes-30-under-30/). Web search was unavailable for this run, so external podcasts are unchecked.\n\n## How they think\n- **Stated focus:** \"Hahn prioritizes investments in AI, security, data and developer tools\" [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/evan-hahn/).\n- **His only on-record investment quote** is on PlexTrac. He calls it \"filling a critical need for streamlined reporting\" in security [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/plextrac-raises-70m-series-b-funding-round-to-fuel-growth-in-proactive-cybersecurity-management/). It is a classic growth-equity case: fast growth plus customer satisfaction.\n- **Calls that worked:** Granulate (Series A 2020, sold to Intel for $650M), Run:ai and Deci (both acquired by NVIDIA in 2024), and JFrog (IPO 2020) [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/evan-hahn/), [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/ai-is-poised-to-reshape-industries-and-redefine-possibilities-in-enterprise/).\n- **Contrarian views:** none on record.\n\n## Read these first\n- Insight bio: https://www.insightpartners.com/team/evan-hahn/. The canonical deal list, with the split between \"sourced\" and \"investments also include\".\n- Forbes 30U30 profile: https://www.forbes.com/profile/evan-hahn/. The only third-party account of his scale and board count.\n- PlexTrac Series B release: https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/plextrac-raises-70m-series-b-funding-round-to-fuel-growth-in-proactive-cybersecurity-management/. His only quoted thesis.\n- AP Capital I Form D: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2140744/000090514826003382/primary_doc.xml. His role at Absolute.\n- James Hahn 2010 13D: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1444183/000143774910002304/aa2hahn_sc13d-072210.htm. The family venture background.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No verified X, LinkedIn or Substack. Web search was unavailable this run.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| JFrog | Series D ($165M); Insight initial Sep 2018 | 2018-10-04 | Insight led; Horing board | — | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/jfrog-secures-165-million-investment-to-lead-universal-devops-in-the-enterprise/) |\n| Keyfactor | Growth ($77M); initial Dec 2018 | 2019-01-22 | Insight | — | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/keyfactor-raises-77-million-from-insight-venture-partners/) |\n| Granulate | Series A ($12M) | 2020-04-22 | Insight led; Jaffe board | TLV Partners, Hetz | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/granulate-secures-12m-in-series-a-funding/) |\n| Postman | Series C ($150M at $2B) | 2020-06-11 | Insight led | CRV, Nexus | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/postman-closes-150-million-series-c-funding-at-a-2-billion-valuation/) |\n| Run:ai | Series B ($30M) | 2021-01-26 | Insight led | — | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/runai-raises-30m-series-b-funding/) |\n| Weights & Biases | Series B ($45M) | 2021-02-01 | Insight led | — | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/weights-and-biases-raises-45m-series-b-to-expand-beyond-experiment-tracking-for-machine-learning-practitioners-everywhere/) |\n| Cognigy | Series B ($44M) | 2021-06-02 | unknown | — | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/portfolio/cognigy/) |\n| Databricks | Series H ($1.6B at $38B) | 2021-08-31 | no | — | [src](https://databricks.com/company/newsroom/press-releases/databricks-raises-1-6-billion-series-h-investment-at-38-billion-valuation); Insight page lists only Parekh |\n| Deci AI | Series A ($21M) | 2021-10-20 | Insight led | — | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/deci-raises-21m-to-enable-the-world-to-benefit-from-the-full-potential-of-ai/) |\n| PlexTrac | Series B ($70M); board director | 2022-02-02 | Insight led; Hahn quoted | Madrona, Noro-Moseley, StageDotO | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/plextrac-raises-70m-series-b-funding-round-to-fuel-growth-in-proactive-cybersecurity-management/) |\n| Middesk | Series B ($57M) | 2022-06-09 | Insight co-led with Canapi; Hahn listed first | Sequoia, Accel, Gaingels | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/leading-business-identity-platform-middesk-raises-57m-series-b-co-led-by-insight-partners-and-canapi-ventures/) |\n| incident.io | Series B ($62M) | 2025-04-10 | Insight led; his role unconfirmed | Index, Point Nine | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/incident-io-raises-62m-to-build-ai-agents-that-resolve-incidents-with-you/) |\n| Cloudsmith | Undisclosed (Insight initial Feb 2025; probably the B) | 2025-02 | unknown | TCV | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/portfolio/cloudsmith/) |\n| Cloudsmith | Series C ($72M); Insight doubled down | 2026-04-23 | TCV led | TCV | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/cloudsmith-raises-72m-series-c-led-by-tcv-and-insight-partners-to-control-and-secure-the-ai-powered-software-supply-chain/) |\n| SpecterOps | Undisclosed | not found | unknown | — | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/team/evan-hahn/) |\n| Sable | $45M round (Series A per prior KB row), as an individual | 2026-07-16 | no | Sequoia, 8VC (leads), BoxGroup, SV Angel, Valor Atreides AI Fund, Sabrina Hahn | [src](https://www.finanznachrichten.de/nachrichten-2026-07/69050088-sable-raises-dollar-45m-to-build-the-first-ai-employee-that-can-click-see-and-explain-200.htm) |\n\n**Timing summary.**\n- Never first in. Postman: Insight \"joins previous investors CRV and Nexus\". Granulate A came after TLV and Hetz. Middesk B came after Sequoia and Accel. JFrog was a D.\n- The one documented double-down is Cloudsmith: Insight went in Feb 2025, then backed the Apr 2026 Series C (\"double down\"). It is not known whether Hahn worked the follow-on.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **PlexTrac (2022):** Insight led the $70M B ten months after a $10M Series A. Hahn, then VP, was the quoted voice and took a board seat. He sat alongside Madrona's Soma Somasegar [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1786090/000178609022000002/primary_doc.xml).\n- **Middesk (2022):** Insight co-led the $57M B with Canapi. Hahn tops Insight's deal-team list ahead of co-founder Jeff Horing, but Horing gave the quote [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/leading-business-identity-platform-middesk-raises-57m-series-b-co-led-by-insight-partners-and-canapi-ventures/). Hahn probably sourced and ran the deal (per his bio); the board seat is unconfirmed.\n- **Sable (2026):** both siblings came in as individuals behind Sequoia (Shaun Maguire) and 8VC (Joe Lonsdale). The founders are \"a group of Harvard graduates\", and the Hahns are Harvard alumni. The intro path is unconfirmed.\n- Intro paths for the Insight deals: nothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sabrina Hahn | Sister; co-founder and co-MP of Absolute Partners | 2026 (firm) | [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/) |\n| James Hahn | Father (per 2010 13D; identity match inferred); President of Alpine Venture Associates, a VC manager | — | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1444183/000143774910002304/aa2hahn_sc13d-072210.htm) |\n| Insight Partners | Analyst to MD; now Senior Advisor; also LP in Sabrina's fund | ~2018 | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/team/evan-hahn/) |\n| Jeff Horing (Insight co-founder) | On his deal teams for JFrog, Granulate, Postman, Run:ai, W&B, Cognigy, Middesk | 2018 | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/portfolio/jfrog/) |\n| Lonne Jaffe, Teddie Wardi, George Mathew (Insight) | AI-infra deal-team co-members (Run:ai, Granulate, W&B, Deci) | 2020–21 | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/portfolio/run-ai/) |\n| Thomas Krane, Michael Triplett (Insight) | Security and devtools co-members (Keyfactor, Postman, PlexTrac, Cloudsmith) | 2018–25 | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/portfolio/plextrac/) |\n| Soma Somasegar (Madrona) | Co-director on PlexTrac board | 2022 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1786090/000178609022000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is Alpine Venture Associates, or the Hahn family, an LP in SH Fund or AP Capital I / AP Align I? Check AVA filings and any Form ADV.\n- What is AP Align I? Possibly a co-invest sidecar; the Form D doesn't say.\n- When exactly did he become MD, and when did he leave the MD seat? (Bio last modified 2026-05-27.)\n- Which Absolute deals, if any, did Evan originate? Is his role mainly LP and operations?\n- Does he still hold the PlexTrac and other board seats (Forbes counted 10)?\n- Verify his X and LinkedIn handles in a run with web search.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.insightpartners.com/team/evan-hahn/\n- https://www.insightpartners.com/wp-json/wp/v2/sfuser/5541\n- https://www.forbes.com/profile/evan-hahn/\n- https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/\n- https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/award-2024-forbes-30-under-30/\n- https://www.privateequityinternational.com/institution-profiles/absolute-partners.html\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2140744/000090514826003382/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149542/000090514826003539/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1912867/000092542122000003/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1444183/000143774910002304/aa2hahn_sc13d-072210.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1786090/000178609022000002/primary_doc.xml\n- Insight portfolio pages: jfrog, keyfactor, granulate, postman, run-ai, weights-biases, cognigy, deci, plextrac, middesk, cloudsmith, databricks, incident-io, specterops (https://www.insightpartners.com/portfolio/<slug>/)\n- Insight round posts linked in the Portfolio table\n- https://www.finanznachrichten.de/nachrichten-2026-07/69050088-sable-raises-dollar-45m-to-build-the-first-ai-employee-that-can-click-see-and-explain-200.htm\n- https://www.sabrinahahn.com/\n", "person:evan-jaysane-darr": "\n# Evan Jaysane-Darr\n\n## One-line take\nValor's only Investment Team Principal is a former LP. He ran Invesco's venture fund-of-funds, and no Valor AI deal names him.\n\n**known_for:** Valor's only Investment Team Principal; previously ran Invesco Private Capital's venture fund-of-funds, including a $250M CalSTRS emerging-manager vehicle.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** he holds the one Principal seat on Valor's Investment Team ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/evan-jaysane-darr)). Valor writes growth checks into AI compute and frontier names (xAI, Cerebras, Positron, Celero; see `firms/valor-equity-partners.md`), so the seat sits next to large checks.\n- **What constrains him:** he is a lateral hire from the LP side. He went from Managing Partner at Invesco Private Capital to Principal at Valor, a lower title ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/evan-jaysane-darr)). No source shows him sourcing, pricing or signing a Valor deal. Deal authority at Valor appears to sit with Antonio Gracias and the partners (firm page).\n- **What he'd likely be good for:** fund-level and LP-side conversations, and possibly fund or co-invest structures. Valor runs several vehicles (Valor Atreides AI fund, Valor Compute Infrastructure, VSV). This is an inference from his background; no source confirms his role in any of them.\n\n## Differential insight\n- On SEC filings he was an \"Executive Officer, Promoter\" of the GP of **CalSTRS New & Next Generation Manager Fund IV, L.P.**, a $250M vehicle sold in full to one investor (first sale 2017-03-31). The name and filing quote: \"CALSTRS NEW & NEXT GENERATION MANAGER FUND IV\" ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1700578/000170057817000001/primary_doc.xml)). It reads as a CalSTRS emerging-manager separate account managed by Invesco. That CalSTRS is the single LP is inferred from the fund name; the filing does not say so. Being a listed officer is not proof that he picked its managers.\n- Invesco Venture Alpha Fund II, his last Invesco vehicle, targeted $400M. By Dec 2021 it had sold $255.3M to **212 investors**, first sale 2020-08-07 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1818096/000181809621000001/primary_doc.xml)). 212 LPs for a VC fund-of-funds suggests a wealth or feeder channel.\n- Invesco Venture Alpha Fund I targeted $300M. By June 2018 it had sold only $71.65M to 3 investors ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1699299/000169929918000002/primary_doc.xml)). The franchise was mid-sized.\n- He also ran Invesco's \"direct investment business\", so he has done direct and co-invest deals, not only fund commitments ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/evan-jaysane-darr)).\n- **Negative finding:** EDGAR full-text search for \"Jaysane-Darr\" returns only the Invesco and CalSTRS vehicles above. No Valor Form D lists him as a related person.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is a principal at a fund that writes growth-sized AI checks, which matches the segment the owner hunts in. But he has no sourced personal AI deal, and his edge is the LP and fund-of-funds side. `check_capacity: growth` describes Valor's funds, not his personal authority; no source says he can sign. He is useful for understanding how Valor packages vehicles and co-invests. He is not, on the evidence, the person to call about a frontier seed. Watch for a promotion to partner or for his name on an AI deal.\n\n## Background\n- B.A. History, Washington and Lee University; M.B.A., INSEAD; CFA charterholder ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/evan-jaysane-darr)).\n- Vice President, Alternative Investment group, Atlantic Trust (dates not disclosed) ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/evan-jaysane-darr)).\n- Managing Partner, Invesco Private Capital. He ran its venture and growth fund-of-funds and its direct investments ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/evan-jaysane-darr)). Form Ds name him from March 2017 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1699299/000169929917000001/primary_doc.xml)) through December 2021 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1818096/000181809621000001/primary_doc.xml)). His start date and exact exit are unconfirmed.\n- Joined Valor in 2022 as a Principal on the Investment Team ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/evan-jaysane-darr)). An archived copy of the bio from 2023-03 shows the same wording ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230315112549/https://www.valorep.com/team/evan-jaysane-darr)). How he got the seat is not sourced.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.valorep.com/team/evan-jaysane-darr | Valor team bio, live 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Not checked by search (search budget exhausted) |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was unavailable this pass, so this is unchecked rather than a confirmed absence.\n\n## How they think\nNothing in his own words found. From his background: an LP-side allocator with a CFA, used to judging managers and taking directs alongside them.\n\n## Read these first\n- Valor team bio ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/evan-jaysane-darr))\n- CalSTRS New & Next Generation Manager Fund IV Form D ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1700578/000170057817000001/primary_doc.xml))\n- Invesco Venture Alpha Fund II Form D/A ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1818096/000181809621000001/primary_doc.xml))\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| none found | | | | | We checked all ~30 `valor-equity-partners` rows in data/deals.csv (xAI, Cerebras, Positron, Celero, Bedrock Robotics, Flapping Airplanes, Cognition and others); none names an individual Valor person |\n\nFund vehicles where he was a listed GP officer. These are fund-of-funds commitments, not company rounds:\n| Vehicle | Size | Investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Invesco Venture Alpha Fund, L.P. | $300M target; $71.65M sold by 2018-06 | 3 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1699299/000169929918000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| CalSTRS New & Next Generation Manager Fund IV, L.P. | $250M, fully sold, first sale 2017-03-31 | 1 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1700578/000170057817000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Invesco Venture Alpha Fund II, L.P. | $400M target; $255.3M sold by 2021-12 | 212 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1818096/000181809621000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Valor Equity Partners | at: Principal, Investment Team | 2022 | [src](https://www.valorep.com/team/evan-jaysane-darr) |\n| Invesco Private Capital | at: Managing Partner | by 2017 (filing evidence) to ~2022 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1699299/000169929917000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Atlantic Trust | at: Vice President, Alternative Investment group | unconfirmed | [src](https://www.valorep.com/team/evan-jaysane-darr) |\n| CalSTRS | Executive officer of the GP of its New & Next Generation Manager Fund IV (Invesco-run) | 2017 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1700578/000170057817000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Theresa Boyd | Co-listed Invesco Private Capital officer on all three vehicles | 2017 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1818096/000181809621000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Kelvin (or Kevin) Liu | Co-listed Invesco officer; the filings spell the name both ways | 2017 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1700578/000170057817000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** the only verified channel is the Valor team page. No email, X or LinkedIn handle was verified. The likeliest way in is through the LP or fund side: a GP raising a fund, or a co-investor building a vehicle. That is an inference, unconfirmed.\n\n## Open questions\n- Which VC managers did Invesco Venture Alpha I and II and CalSTRS NNG IV back? CalSTRS board materials may list them, and they would show which AI-focused emerging managers he underwrote.\n- What does he do at Valor: deals, the Valor Atreides AI fund, Valor Compute Infrastructure, or LP and co-invest structuring?\n- When did he leave Invesco, and who took over the program (Theresa Boyd?).\n- Press, podcast and LinkedIn coverage went unchecked because the web search budget was exhausted this pass.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.valorep.com/team/evan-jaysane-darr\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20230315112549/https://www.valorep.com/team/evan-jaysane-darr\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1699299/000169929917000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1699299/000169929918000002/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1700578/000170057817000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1818096/000181809620000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1818096/000181809621000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Jaysane-Darr%22 (EDGAR full-text search)\n", "person:everett-randle": "\n# Everett (Ev) Randle\n\nSources (TechCrunch, Gumloop's own post, aidirectory) refer to him as \"he\", so this profile does too.\n\n*Update 2026-11-10: added Uncapped Ep. 41 (Benchmark partnership, 2026-02-04), Uncapped Ep. 53 (Founders Fund, 2026-06-30) and Benchmark Breakfast #1 (2026-10-29). Also added the Kleiner and Founders Fund portfolio list from a podcast bio, the Cerebras AI Infrastructure Fund role and a confirmed X handle. Web search budget was exhausted this run, so all new material comes from direct fetches.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is Benchmark's newest GP, a growth investor by training (Bond, Founders Fund, Kleiner, where a podcast bio says he invested in Anthropic) now leading earlier rounds. He is the Benchmark partner aimed most squarely at AI-native enterprise automation, the Serval-type category ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/12/gumloop-lands-50m-from-benchmark-to-turn-every-employee-into-an-ai-agent-builder), [src](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** His thesis comes from the person, his evidence from the customer. He says \"90% of the story\" on Benchmark's great investments \"is the person\" ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton), 2026-02-04). He then checks conviction against usage, not the pitch: on Gumloop he ran customer deep dives before meeting the founder ([src](https://www.gumloop.com/blog/series-b)). He sells himself as an active \"sparring partner\" and explicitly rejects his old firm Founders Fund's passive \"do no harm\" stance ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton)). That is how he won a pre-emptive round from a founder who was \"in no rush to raise\" and named him personally.\n- **His thesis.** Enterprise automation is \"the biggest category in enterprise AI\", and enterprises want model-agnostic tooling ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/12/gumloop-lands-50m-from-benchmark-to-turn-every-employee-into-an-ai-agent-builder)). He measures AI apps by gross dollars per customer, not gross margin ([src](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/everett-randle)). He also says founder centricity matters more in AI because the foundations shift so fast ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton)).\n- **What constrains him.** Benchmark's punchcard: \"you might only have ten meaningful partnerships\". He says that rules out \"doing eight investments a year\" ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton)). So he rations board-level partnerships and wants high ownership. His only confirmed Benchmark lead came in at Series B, about 14 months after Nexus led the A ([src](https://www.gumloop.com/blog/gumloops-17m-series-a)).\n- **What he'd never do.** Underwrite a growth price that assumes the company beats base rates. He says investors are \"over-underwriting relative to historical norms by 20X\" ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan), 2026-06-30). He also would not run a spray-and-pray mega fund (20VC episode title, [src](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/everett-randle)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Punchcard rule, in his words:** \"you might only have ten meaningful partnerships\". He says this cannot coexist with eight investments a year per partner ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton), 2026-02-04).\n- **Openly breaks with Founders Fund's style.** He says FF \"really leans on this kind of Hippocratic Oath of VC\". He thinks \"almost every single founder could use feedback, a sparring partner\". He calls the \"no board seat\" pitch a symptom of founders' bad experiences with average board members (same source).\n- **Benchmark's AI wins were not a thesis.** From outside, Fireworks, Mercor, Sierra and Legora looked thematic. On joining he found \"These guys had no idea what they were doing!\" and that the story was the founder each time (same source).\n- **Founder centricity in AI:** \"Founder centricity as an investment strategy matters more now\" because \"the sands are shifting beneath the founder's feet\" in AI (same source).\n- **Closes by hosting dinners and giving direct feedback.** On an unannounced deal, he hosted the founders for dinner and critiqued their commercial strategy. A founder told him the team would make them \"better founders\" (same source; company not named).\n- **Growth-pricing scepticism:** at $100M ARR priced at $10B, the implied path to $1.4B ARR has a base rate of about 5%. Investors are therefore \"over-underwriting relative to historical norms by 20X\" ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan), 2026-06-30).\n- **Founders Fund lesson from 2021.** At an FF offsite in 2021 the team agreed to back only its best companies, and made two investments that held up. He calls \"the winter of 2021\" the worst time to invest in 15 to 20 years (same source). He also flags FF's 1% personal-allocation rule as a conviction signal.\n- **Diligence ran through customers before the founder.** The Gumloop CEO wrote that before their first meeting Randle \"already understood our whole business\" ([src](https://www.gumloop.com/blog/series-b), 2026-03-12). At one customer, staff were given Gumloop and two competitors, and six months later only Gumloop was still in use ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/12/gumloop-lands-50m-from-benchmark-to-turn-every-employee-into-an-ai-agent-builder), paraphrased, no row).\n- **Pre-emptive deal.** Gumloop \"were in no rush to raise\". The founder chose Benchmark, \"specifically Ev Randle\" ([src](https://www.gumloop.com/blog/series-b)).\n- **Model-agnostic thesis:** \"They want to use all of them\", about enterprises holding credits with several labs ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/12/gumloop-lands-50m-from-benchmark-to-turn-every-employee-into-an-ai-agent-builder)).\n- **Kleiner-era portfolio, from a host-written bio, not his words.** The Uncapped Ep. 41 description says that at Kleiner he invested in Anthropic, Chainguard, Databricks, Flock Safety and SpaceX. Via Founders Fund and personal capital he invested in Rippling, Ramp, Wave, Faire and Figma ([src](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss)). This independently corroborates the aidirectory list the 2026-11-09 version left out. Rounds and dates are not found.\n- **Public voice on frontier models.** He co-hosts *Benchmark Breakfast* with Jack Altman, billed as a window into Benchmark's Monday no-agenda partner meetings. Episode #1 (2026-10-29) covered the Anthropic S-1 revenue-vs-compute chart, \"own your intelligence\", open vs closed source, abliterated models, and bio and robotics ([src](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss)). Transcript not read.\n- **AI infrastructure money.** A Cerebras S-1/A, as already recorded in this repo, lists him among the managing members of the new Benchmark AI Infrastructure Fund, which put $225M into the Series H ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm); I could not re-locate the footnote in my fetch, so medium confidence). This makes him a fund-level manager on the deal, not the deal partner. Eric Vishria is the Cerebras partner.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is the Benchmark partner most clearly aimed at AI-native automation (Serval-type), and his only confirmed Benchmark lead is Gumloop ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/12/gumloop-lands-50m-from-benchmark-to-turn-every-employee-into-an-ai-agent-builder)). He also has frontier-lab exposure through an Anthropic investment at Kleiner (bio-sourced) and a managing-member seat on Benchmark's AI Infrastructure Fund (Cerebras H).\n- **Timing.** He is not first in. In Gumloop, First Round and YC came first, then Nexus led the A in Jan 2025, and he arrived about 14 months later at the B ([src](https://www.gumloop.com/blog/gumloops-17m-series-a)). For me, he is a natural **follow-on lead or up-round buyer** for a portfolio company, not a seed co-investor.\n- **Co-investing on a $20M cheque.** The punchcard logic (\"ten meaningful partnerships\") means he wants concentrated, board-level ownership. In a big seed he would want to lead and own, not sit beside me. That leaves him as the best Benchmark contact for selling a later round, not for syndicating mine.\n- **What matters to me:** Gumloop (Series B lead, 2026-03-12), Anthropic (Kleiner, bio-sourced), Cerebras (AI Infra Fund manager).\n\n## Background\n- Bond (Mary Meeker's growth firm): investor. Dates not found ([src](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/everett-randle)).\n- Founders Fund: investor. Rippling, Ramp, Wave, Faire and Figma are bio-listed via FF and personal capital ([src](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss)). He was at FF during the 2021 offsite he described ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan)). Exact dates not found.\n- Kleiner Perkins: Partner, invested in Anthropic, Chainguard, Databricks, Flock Safety and SpaceX (bio, [src](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss)). Dates not found.\n- Benchmark: General Partner since about Oct 2025. TechCrunch says he joined \"last October\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/12/gumloop-lands-50m-from-benchmark-to-turn-every-employee-into-an-ai-agent-builder)). He said \"Three months\" on 2026-02-04, which is consistent ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton)). He is listed as a GP managing member on the 2026 Form D ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2138636/000213863626000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- Education: nothing found. Benchmark Breakfast #1 has a segment on his father and \"the promise of bio\" ([src](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss)); no detail.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | benchmark.com lists no partners |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Guessed Substack URLs returned 404 (2026-11-09) |\n| X | [x.com/everettrandle](https://x.com/everettrandle) | Handle linked from the Uncapped/Benchmark Breakfast feed ([src](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss)); follower count not checked (x.com blocked) |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Benchmark Breakfast (co-host with Jack Altman), [feed](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss) | #1 on 2026-10-29, billed as recurring; he also co-interviews on Uncapped (Ep. 53) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-10 — Benchmark Breakfast #1 — \"Jack and Ev\" — [feed](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss). Anthropic S-1, revenue vs compute, code generation as \"the most impactful PMF in history\" (chapter title), open vs closed, abliterated models, physical abundance. Transcript not read.\n- 2026-06 — Uncapped Ep. 53 — \"Founders Fund on Truth-Seeking, Taiwan…\" — [link](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan). He questions Trae Stephens and Delian Asparouhov. Stood out: the 20x over-underwriting maths, the 2021 offsite story, and the \"absolute bar\" for doubling down on portfolio companies.\n- 2026-02 — Uncapped Ep. 41 — \"The Benchmark Partnership\" (with Fenton, Vishria, Puttagunta) — [link](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton). Stood out: the punchcard, rejecting FF's \"do no harm\", and \"90% of the story\" is the founder.\n- 2025-11 (date conflict: the page byline says 2025-11-10 and the metadata says 2026-11-03) — 20VC — \"Why Margins Matter Less in AI & Why Mega Funds Will Not Produce Good Returns\" — [link](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/everett-randle). Episode topics only; no transcript on the page.\n\n## How they think\n- **Category view:** enterprise automation is the largest enterprise-AI category ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/12/gumloop-lands-50m-from-benchmark-to-turn-every-employee-into-an-ai-agent-builder)).\n- **People over themes:** the great Benchmark AI deals were founder bets, not a plan ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton)).\n- **Active board style:** feedback and sparring rather than \"do no harm\" (same source).\n- **Base rates for growth pricing:** a $10B price on $100M ARR implies about 20x the historical odds ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan)). Doubling down on a portfolio company \"has to meet the absolute bar\" (same source).\n- **Revenue scale over margin purity** in AI apps, and labs as the biggest threat to app companies (20VC topics, [src](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/everett-randle)).\n- **Contrarian:** mega funds are the new Tiger (same source).\n- **Calls:** Anthropic at Kleiner (bio) looks strong in hindsight. Gumloop is too early to judge.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Uncapped Ep. 41: his clearest statement of the Benchmark model and his break with FF ([link](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton)).\n2. TechCrunch on Gumloop's Series B: his on-record automation thesis ([link](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/12/gumloop-lands-50m-from-benchmark-to-turn-every-employee-into-an-ai-agent-builder)).\n3. Gumloop's Series B post: how he won the deal ([link](https://www.gumloop.com/blog/series-b)).\n4. Uncapped Ep. 53: growth-pricing base rates and FF lessons ([link](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan)).\n5. Benchmark Breakfast #1: his live views on the Anthropic S-1 and open vs closed models ([feed](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot observed, because X was unreachable. Public output runs mostly through Jack Altman's Uncapped feed: he appears on Ep. 41 and 53 and co-hosts Benchmark Breakfast ([src](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss)).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Gumloop | Series B ($50M) | 2026-03-12 | yes | Nexus VP; First Round Capital; Y Combinator; BoxGroup; The Cannon Project; Shopify Ventures | [src](https://www.gumloop.com/blog/series-b) |\n| Cerebras | Series H (Benchmark AI Infra Fund $225M) | 2026-01 to 02 | no (fund managing member; Vishria is deal partner) | Alpha Wave; Fidelity | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm) (repo-recorded) |\n| Anthropic | not found (at Kleiner) | not found | not found | | [bio](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss) |\n| Chainguard | not found (at Kleiner) | not found | not found | | [bio](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss) |\n| Databricks | not found (at Kleiner) | not found | not found | | [bio](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss) |\n| Flock Safety | not found (at Kleiner) | not found | not found | | [bio](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss) |\n| SpaceX | not found (at Kleiner) | not found | not found | | [bio](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss) |\n| Rippling; Ramp; Wave; Faire; Figma | not found (FF or personal) | not found | not found | | [bio](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss) |\n\nCaptions (Kleiner Series B, June 2023) is still unconfirmed for him.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Gumloop (Series B, 2026-03-12).** Gumloop started in mid-2023 as a side project in Vancouver. It raised a seed, then a $17M Series A in Jan 2025 led by Nexus VP with First Round, YC and angels ([src](https://www.gumloop.com/blog/gumloops-17m-series-a)). Randle did customer deep dives before meeting CEO Max Brodeur-Urbas, and found one customer where Gumloop beat two competitors on retained usage ([src](https://www.gumloop.com/blog/series-b), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/12/gumloop-lands-50m-from-benchmark-to-turn-every-employee-into-an-ai-agent-builder)). The company was \"in no rush to raise\" but named Randle as the reason for Benchmark, and existing backers followed. **Timing:** about 14 months after the A. Board seat not confirmed.\n- **Unnamed Benchmark deal (by 2026-02).** He hosted the founders for dinner and gave blunt commercial-strategy feedback. A founder said the team would make them \"better founders\" ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton)). Company not named; plausibly Gumloop, given the timing, but unconfirmed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Benchmark | General Partner; Form D managing member | 2025-10 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/12/gumloop-lands-50m-from-benchmark-to-turn-every-employee-into-an-ai-agent-builder), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2138636/000213863626000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Jack Altman | Benchmark partner; co-host of Benchmark Breakfast | 2026-10 | [src](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss) |\n| Peter Fenton; Eric Vishria; Chetan Puttagunta | Benchmark partners; joint Uncapped appearance | 2026-02 | [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton) |\n| Trae Stephens; Delian Asparouhov | former Founders Fund colleagues; he interviewed them | 2026-06 | [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan) |\n| Kleiner Perkins | former Partner | dates not found | [src](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/everett-randle) |\n| Founders Fund | former investor | dates not found | [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan) |\n| Bond | former investor | dates not found | [src](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/everett-randle) |\n| Max Brodeur-Urbas (Gumloop) | lead investor in founder's Series B | 2026-03 | [src](https://www.gumloop.com/blog/series-b) |\n| Nexus Venture Partners | co-investor (Gumloop; Nexus led the A) | 2026-03 | [src](https://www.gumloop.com/blog/series-b) |\n| Peter Thiel; Mary Meeker; Mamoon Hamid | lessons cited on 20VC | n/a | [src](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/everett-randle) |\n| Harry Stebbings (20VC) | podcast host | 2025-11 | [src](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/everett-randle) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Benchmark Breakfast #1 transcript: his own words on the Anthropic S-1, \"own your intelligence\" and the inference cloud.\n- Which Kleiner rounds was he the deal partner on (Anthropic, Chainguard, Databricks), versus participating? Primary confirmation needed.\n- Is the Cerebras AI Infrastructure Fund a recurring vehicle he co-manages for more AI compute deals?\n- Which company is the unnamed dinner deal? Has he done any seed or Series A at Benchmark?\n- \"Fireworks\" (transcript) vs \"Firework\" (20VC page) in Benchmark's last fund: which company?\n- Does he hold the Gumloop board seat? Location and education.\n\n## Sources\n- https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton\n- https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan\n- https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/12/gumloop-lands-50m-from-benchmark-to-turn-every-employee-into-an-ai-agent-builder\n- https://www.gumloop.com/blog/series-b\n- https://www.gumloop.com/blog/gumloops-17m-series-a\n- https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/everett-randle\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2138636/000213863626000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://aidirectory.com/news/benchmark-adds-everett-randle-general-partner-ai-investment-views (aggregator)\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/captions-series-b/ (author not confirmed)\n", "person:fred-ehrsam": "\n# Fred Ehrsam\n\n> Written 2026-11-11 (sweep early-backers-1). **Thin page.** He does not publish a portfolio and no interview about his AI investing was found. Cheques come from company announcements already in the ledger, his Paradigm bio, and aggregators (labelled). check_capacity is set to sub-10m only because no personal cheque size is public; he could plainly write more.\n\n## One-line take\nA crypto billionaire who stepped back from his fund to work on science, now runs a brain-interface company, and writes personal cheques into AI and bio companies; his own bio says that includes early positions in OpenAI and Anthropic.\n\n## Crux\nFounders take his money because he is a founder with a public-company outcome, patient personal capital and no fund mandate. He follows top leads into technical companies (Thrive and OpenAI in Chai, Founders Fund in Cognition) and re-ups. The constraint is attention: he is CEO of Nudge and only an advisor at Paradigm. He is not a lead and nothing suggests he wants to be.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Hidden lab exposure:** \"Fred has been an early investor in OpenAI, Anthropic, Anduril, SpaceX\" ([Paradigm team page](https://www.paradigm.xyz/team/fredehrsam)). No round, date or vehicle is given, and he is not named in any announcement for those companies found here.\n- **No longer a GP:** the same page titles him \"Co-Founder & Senior Advisor\". Bloomberg reported in October 2023 that he gave up the managing partner role to spend time on science ([src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-10-24/paradigm-s-ehrsam-relinquishes-managing-partner-role-at-crypto-focused-vc-firm)); The Information later ran a piece headlined \"Fred Ehrsam's Quiet Quitting at Paradigm\" ([src](https://www.theinformation.com/articles/fred-ehrsams-quiet-quitting-at-paradigm), paywalled, body not read).\n- **Re-ups:** he is the one seed angel Chai names again among existing investors in its Series A ([src](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/series-a-announcement), existing ledger insight).\n- **Policy seat:** appointed to the President's Council of Advisors on Science and Technology in 2026 ([src](https://www.whitehouse.gov/articles/2026/03/president-trump-announces-appointments-to-presidents-council-of-advisors-on-science-and-technology/), via Wikipedia; not opened).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. His cheques sit in the owner's territory (labs, AI bio, AI infrastructure, chips via Atomic Semi), and his name next to Thrive on a seed is a signal. He is not a competitor for allocation and not a lead. The more useful link is Paradigm itself, which has moved into AI (it is an investor in Andromeda with NFDG, per the NFDG page), and Matt Huang, who advises NFDG in run-off.\n\n## Background\nDuke (computer science and economics, 2010); Goldman Sachs FX trader 2010 to 2012; co-founded Coinbase with Brian Armstrong in 2012 and was president until January 2017, remaining a director; co-founded Paradigm with Matt Huang in 2018; Paradigm closed a $2.5B fund in November 2021; stepped down as managing partner in October 2023; incorporated Nudge in 2024 with Jeremy Barenholtz (ex-Neuralink) and Quintin Frerichs; Nudge raised a $100M Series A led by Thrive and Greenoaks in July 2025 ([Wikipedia with its cited sources](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fred_Ehrsam); [Contrary Research](https://research.contrary.com/company/nudge)).\n\n## Vehicle\nPersonal. He is named as an individual in every announcement found. No family-office name, fund, Form D or syndicate surfaced. One aggregator claim that he is an LP in a 2022 Web3 fund is already in the ledger and unverified.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.paradigm.xyz/team/fredehrsam | firm bio |\n| Substack / blog | nothing current found | older crypto essays not reviewed |\n| X | https://x.com/FEhrsam | linked from the Paradigm page; not fetched |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/fredehrsam/ | linked from the Paradigm page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-06 — Bloomberg — Crypto Billionaire Hires Neuralink Employees for Brain Startup — [link](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-06-10/coinbase-s-ehrsam-hires-neuralink-employees-for-brain-startup-nudge) — not read (paywall)\n- 2023-10 — Bloomberg — relinquishes managing partner role — [link](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-10-24/paradigm-s-ehrsam-relinquishes-managing-partner-role-at-crypto-focused-vc-firm) — not read (paywall)\n- undated — Bloomberg Studio 1.0 — Fred Ehrsam — [link](https://omny.fm/shows/studio-1-0/fred-ehrsam) — not mined\n- 2017-01 — Fortune — Coinbase exit interview — [link](https://fortune.com/2017/01/21/bitcoin-ethereum-coinbase-cofounder-fred-ehrsam-exit-interview/) — not mined\n- Nothing found where he talks about AI or about angel investing.\n\n## How they think\nNothing in his own words on AI was found in this pass. What the record shows: he left a managing-partner seat for science, started a hardware neuroscience company, and backs technical founders next to Thrive, OpenAI and Founders Fund.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Paradigm team page](https://www.paradigm.xyz/team/fredehrsam): the only place his frontier-lab positions are stated.\n- [Chai Discovery Series A post](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/series-a-announcement): shows him re-upping alongside Thrive and OpenAI.\n- [Contrary Research on Nudge](https://research.contrary.com/company/nudge): what he is building now.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed (X not fetched).\n\n## Portfolio\n**P/S** = in at pre-seed or seed.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | early investor; round not stated | not stated | no | | [src](https://www.paradigm.xyz/team/fredehrsam) |\n| Anthropic | early investor; round not stated | not stated | no | | [src](https://www.paradigm.xyz/team/fredehrsam) |\n| Foxglove **P/S** | Seed ($3.7M) | 2022-01 | no | Kyle Vogt also an angel | [src](https://foxglove.dev/blog/foxglove-raises-seed-funding-3-7m) |\n| Wispr AI **P/S** | Seed ($10M) | 2022-10 | no | | [Seedtable](https://seedtable.com/investors/fred-ehrsam) (aggregator snippet) |\n| Atomic Semi **P/S** | Seed (reported engaged) | 2023-01 | no | OpenAI Startup Fund, Nat Friedman, Naval Ravikant | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/01/10/openai-in-talks-to-back-zeloof-and-chip-legend-kellers-startup-at-100-million-valuation/) (ledger) |\n| Cognition | thanked at Devin launch (Series A period) | 2024-03-12 | no | Founders Fund led | [src](https://cognition.com/blog/introducing-devin) |\n| Chai Discovery **P/S** | Seed; re-upped in Series A 2025-08 | 2024-09-09 | no | Thrive, OpenAI, Dimension, Neo, Lachy Groom | [src](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement) |\n| OpenRouter | listed among notable investors; round not stated | 2025-06 | no | | [Sacra](https://sacra.com/chat/h/6cd2893f-55e0-4ec0-a67a-3f6edc6d092b/) (secondary) |\n| NewLimit | early angel, 2021 to 2024 | not stated | no | Brian Armstrong is a co-founder | [src](https://blog.newlimit.com/p/newlimit-raises-130-million-series) |\n| Non-AI or unverified | Anduril, SpaceX (own bio); Neuralink (per Philip Clark, ledger lead); Arcadia Medicine angel 2025-10, Proto-Town angel 2026-04, Pilgrim seed II 2026-09 ([CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/fred-ehrsam), aggregator) | | | | |\n\nAggregators count 31 to 55 investments and mix personal cheques with Paradigm deals.\n\n## Hit pattern\n- Seed cheques that became hot: Chai Discovery; OpenAI and Anthropic if the bio's \"early\" means what it says.\n- Follows: Thrive (Chai; Thrive then co-led his own company's Series A), Founders Fund (Cognition), OpenAI Startup Fund (Atomic Semi).\n- Passes or regrets: nothing found.\n\n## Co-investors\n| Who | Where | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Thrive Capital | Chai seed and A; led Nudge's A with Greenoaks | ledger |\n| Nat Friedman | Atomic Semi | ledger |\n| Kyle Vogt | Foxglove seed | [src](https://foxglove.dev/blog/foxglove-raises-seed-funding-3-7m) |\n| Lachy Groom | Chai | ledger |\n| Peter Thiel, Sholto Douglas, Logan Graham | Pilgrim seed II | [CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/fred-ehrsam) (aggregator) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNone found in his or founders' words.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Matt Huang | Paradigm co-founder | 2018 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fred_Ehrsam) |\n| Brian Armstrong | Coinbase co-founder; Ehrsam an angel in Armstrong's NewLimit | 2012 | ledger |\n| Jeremy Barenholtz | Nudge co-founder, ex-Neuralink | 2024 | [src](https://research.contrary.com/company/nudge) |\n| Thrive Capital, Greenoaks | led Nudge's $100M Series A | 2025-07 | ledger |\n| Steve Jang | knew him from Coinbase's Asia work; Jang became a Paradigm LP | ledger insight | ledger |\n\n## Open questions\n- When and through what vehicle did he invest in OpenAI and Anthropic?\n- Is the Cognition and OpenRouter money personal? Both sources are thin.\n- What does The Information piece say about his role and his personal investing?\n- Does he have a family office? None surfaced.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.paradigm.xyz/team/fredehrsam\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fred_Ehrsam (secondary; cites Bloomberg, Fortune, WSJ, White House)\n- https://research.contrary.com/company/nudge\n- https://www.theinformation.com/articles/fred-ehrsams-quiet-quitting-at-paradigm (headline only)\n- https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/fred-ehrsam (aggregator)\n- https://seedtable.com/investors/fred-ehrsam (aggregator snippet)\n- Ledger rows in `data/rounds.csv` (each with its own source URL)\n", "person:gabi-gabriela-marques": "\n# Gabi (Gabriela) Marques\n\n## One-line take\nMarques is a junior name on the Positive Sum team page with no deal attributed to them anywhere: not Etched, not any other AI or compute deal. They are worth tracking only as a possible future associate or principal at a firm whose fund base is growing fast ($400M Fund IV plus a $100M Conviction Fund filed 2026-08), not as a current price-setter.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they might matter:** Positive Sum is scaling. Fund IV ($400M target) and the Conviction Fund ($100M target) were filed in August 2026 ([Fund IV Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149092/000123191926000870/primary_doc.xml), [Conviction Fund Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149093/000123191926000906/primary_doc.xml)). A small deal team with more capital to deploy usually means juniors get promoted or leave. Marques is one of very few non-media names on the team, so they are the obvious internal candidate. This is inference, not sourced.\n- **What constrains them:** attribution. Every source on Etched, the firm's defining bet, credits Patrick O'Shaughnessy personally. No source names any other Positive Sum person on Etched, Cognition, Physical Intelligence or Flapping Airplanes (see `firms/positive-sum.md`). The one aggregator profile says outright that deals are not attributed to them individually ([startupintros, aggregator](https://startupintros.com/people/gabriela-marques)).\n- **What they'd never do:** nothing found. They have no public writing, podcast appearances or indexed X posts, so no stated views exist to infer from.\n- **Bottom line for Etched:** Marques is **not** an Etched price-setter. The Series A price was set by the Primary and Positive Sum co-leads, and the source names O'Shaughnessy ([Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware)). No source places Marques in that deal or any later one.\n\n## Differential insight\nNo primary-source insight was found. They have no podcast appearances, no Substack and no indexed X posts; the X profile itself returned HTTP 402 to the fetcher. The only items come from an aggregator and are low confidence:\n- The aggregator dates their start at Positive Sum to 2022: \"Before joining the firm in 2022\" ([startupintros, aggregator](https://startupintros.com/people/gabriela-marques), page updated 2026-07-13).\n- The aggregator says deal credit sits elsewhere: \"specific deals are not attributed to her individually\" (same source). This is consistent with the firm file, where every Etched source names O'Shaughnessy alone.\n- Identity check (firm site, primary): the Positive Sum team page lists \"Gabi Marques\" and links to [x.com/gabi_imarques](https://x.com/gabi_imarques). The aggregator gives the LinkedIn as [linkedin.com/in/gabrielaimarques](https://www.linkedin.com/in/gabrielaimarques/). Both handles contain \"imarques\", so the firm-site \"Gabi\" and the aggregator's \"Gabriela\" are probably the same person. That is still unconfirmed by a primary source, so the identity stays needs-id.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low** for now. No AI-chip, compute, lab or frontier-AI deal is attributed to them, they have no check authority on record, and the one fact on tenure points to about 4 years at Analyst without a promotion. The firm is a high fit (Etched co-lead, Physical Intelligence, Flapping Airplanes, Cognition), but the person isn't yet. Upgrade to medium if a Positive Sum announcement or LinkedIn shows a promotion to associate or principal, or if they are named on a Fund IV or Conviction Fund deal.\n\n## Background\n- Managing Startup Coach, University of Notre Dame IDEA Center. Dates not given (aggregator only: [startupintros](https://startupintros.com/people/gabriela-marques)). A search of Notre Dame IDEA Center pages turned up no mention of them.\n- Analyst, Positive Sum, from 2022 (aggregator only, same source). The firm's team page lists the name without a title ([team](https://www.positivesum.com/team)).\n- Education: nothing found. The Notre Dame link is plausible but unconfirmed.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | [x.com/gabi_imarques](https://x.com/gabi_imarques) | Linked from the firm team page. Content could not be fetched (HTTP 402, 2026-11-10), and the handle is not indexed by search |\n| LinkedIn | [linkedin.com/in/gabrielaimarques](https://www.linkedin.com/in/gabrielaimarques/) | From an aggregator; not opened |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Not a guest or host on Invest Like the Best as far as searches show |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. A search for \"Gabi Marques\" alongside Colossus or Invest Like the Best returned no episode.\n\n## How they think\nNothing found. They have no public writing or talks.\n\n## Read these first\nNothing found. Read `firms/positive-sum.md` instead, for how the firm, and in practice O'Shaughnessy, decides.\n\n## Social engagement\nUnknown. The X content could not be fetched.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| None attributed | — | — | — | — | Aggregator says no deals are attributed to them individually ([startupintros](https://startupintros.com/people/gabriela-marques)) |\n\nThe firm's Etched sequence, for context (not their deals): Primary seed $5.4M at about $34M (2023-03) -> **Positive Sum + Primary co-led $120M Series A (2024-06-25)** ([Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware)) -> Stripes $500M at $5B post (2025-12; disclosed 2026-06-30) ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/)) -> Sequoia $300M Series C at $10B pre (2026-07-23) -> Jane Street-led $700M at $21B (2026-08-18), with Positive Sum still named ([Etched](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one)). There is no evidence Marques sourced, diligenced or priced any of these.\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. No source describes Marques's role in any deal, whether sourcing, diligence or pricing.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Positive Sum | Analyst (team-page listing; title per aggregator) | 2022 (aggregator) | [team](https://www.positivesum.com/team), [startupintros](https://startupintros.com/people/gabriela-marques) |\n| University of Notre Dame IDEA Center | Former Managing Startup Coach | dates not found | [startupintros, aggregator](https://startupintros.com/people/gabriela-marques) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is the firm-site \"Gabi Marques\" (x.com/gabi_imarques) the same person as the aggregator's \"Gabriela Marques\" (linkedin.com/in/gabrielaimarques)? Probably yes, but a LinkedIn view would confirm it.\n- Has there been a promotion since 2022? Check LinkedIn and any Positive Sum Fund IV announcement.\n- Did they work on Etched diligence, or on the Positive Sum EAI Holdings SPV (Form D 2026-03, unconfirmed as Etched)?\n- The repo has a duplicate lead slug, `gabi-marques` (in `data/rows/positive-sum-firm-leads.csv`). The main session should alias it to `gabi-gabriela-marques`.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.positivesum.com/team (firm site; lists \"Gabi Marques\" with an X link; fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://startupintros.com/people/gabriela-marques (aggregator; title, NYC, 2022 start, Notre Dame IDEA Center, LinkedIn; page updated 2026-07-13)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one\n- SEC Form Ds: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149092/000123191926000870/primary_doc.xml , https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149093/000123191926000906/primary_doc.xml\n- `firms/positive-sum.md`\n", "person:gabriele-papievyte": "\n# Gabriele Papievyte\n\n> Research note (2026-11-10): the run's shared web-search budget was used up before this profile started. Everything here comes from direct fetches of GCV (2024-01-25 and 2026-06-22), FX News Group, the SPhotonix release (Yahoo Finance), Fortune, The Next Web, TheOrg, Qureight's about page and conceptionx.org, plus the repo's XTX rows and Companies House notes. LinkedIn, Inovia's team page and its web.archive.org copy could not be fetched. A rerun with search would add education, podcasts and any deal quotes. Pronoun \"she\" comes from GCV (\"She sits on the boards…\") [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/).\n\n## One-line take\nShe is the one named investor at XTX Ventures and the person a founder actually reaches. She went from investment manager to head of the unit in about five months. She holds the board seats on XTX's AI-chip and AI-datacentre-optics names, but her tickets are $0.5M–$5M, so she is a co-investor, not an anchor.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** XTX's money comes with a quant parent's compute and ML know-how, and she is the access point to it. She was promoted to head in 2024-01, about five months after joining in 2023-08 [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/). She now sits on the boards of Tenstorrent, Qureight, Oriole Networks and Turbine [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/).\n- **What constrains her:** it is the parent's balance sheet, not a fund, and she does not sign off. The UK vehicle (XTX Investments UK Ltd) has directors Oliver Scott and Sunil Samani, not her. Its ultimate controlling party is Alex Gerko, and it put only £7.5m into new investments in 2025 (Companies House, summarised on [firms/xtx-ventures.md](../firms/xtx-ventures.md)) [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/11074405/officers). The last described investment committee (2020) was the two co-CEOs plus internal tech, quant and regulation experts [src](https://qz.com/1923323/xtx-makes-venture-capital-investments-in-machine-learning-firms/). When she took over, the unit wrote \"between $500,000 and $5m per deal\" into late-seed to Series A ML companies [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/).\n- **What she'd never do (inferred):** lead or anchor a $20M+ seed out of the ventures vehicle. Large lab tickets (Flapping Airplanes, Proxima Fusion, Tenstorrent) appear to sit with the parent or another group entity. No source names her on them.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Most of her board seats were inherited, not sourced (dates-based inference).** GCV's 2024 note gives her IOMED and Turbine seats: \"She sits on the boards of portfolio companies Iomed\" [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/). Her predecessor Alexandre Perrin self-reports both as 2021–23 XTX deals (IOMED initial, Turbine follow-on) [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014). Tenstorrent is booked as \"XTX Markets\" (Series D 2024-12, Series E 2026-10), and the parent CTO Joshua Leahy was quoted on the D [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-closes-693m-of-series-d-funding-led-by-samsung-securities-and-afw-partners) [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-announces-series-e). Her seat there looks like oversight of a parent bet. The deals whose dates fit her as first-in are **Oriole Networks** (seed co-lead 2024-03, two months after she became head) and **SPhotonix** (pre-seed co-lead 2025-12, where she is the only XTX person quoted).\n- **The ventures arm still runs the parent's market-structure bucket.** Under her, \"recent deals\" include ElectronX (power derivatives) and A5X (a Brazilian derivatives exchange). The unit also took a large exit: \"XTX received £19m for its stake\" in Aquis Exchange [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/). So her job is part AI venture and part strategic trading-infrastructure investing.\n- **Founder channel: Conception X.** She \"helps lead the unit's partnership with deeptech venture scheme Conception X\" [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/). conceptionx.org shows an `xtx.svg` logo under Key partners (filename only) [src](https://www.conceptionx.org/). Together with the UCL spinout Oriole, this points to a UK academic-spinout pipeline. Her own SPhotonix quote says the same: \"supporting leading academic and deep‑tech teams as they move from lab to market\" [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/sphotonix-secures-4-5-million-154200176.html).\n- **Concentrated health-AI stakes.** XTX \"reportedly holds a 23% stake\" in Lucida Medical (ML prostate-MRI diagnosis) [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/). That is a much larger ownership than its co-investor role in lab rounds.\n- **Unreconciled counts:** GCV said \"nearly 30 active portfolio companies\" in 2024-01 [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/), and \"over 100\" in 2026 [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/). This is probably active versus all-time, but unconfirmed.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** She is the right person to call for XTX money, and her Tenstorrent and Oriole seats put her in AI chips and AI-datacentre optics. But the ventures ticket ($0.5M–$5M) and the UK vehicle's £7.5m of new money in 2025 make her a syndicate co-investor, not a co-lead for a $20M seed. Uses for me:\n1. A technical co-investor in UK/European AI-infra seeds (photonics, chips, cooling such as Corintis).\n2. A route into the parent's balance sheet for larger lab rounds (Flapping Airplanes, Proxima Fusion). No source shows she controls those, so this is unconfirmed.\n3. A source of spinout deal flow through Conception X and UCL.\n\n## Background\n- **Inovia Capital:** investment associate. She \"helped launch its European operations\" [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/) and sat on the boards of Zwift and Neo4j [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/). Start date not found; left by 2023-08.\n- **XTX Ventures:** investment manager from 2023-08. FX News Group says \"mid last year\" [src](https://fxnewsgroup.com/forex-news/institutional/exclusive-xtx-markets-names-gabriele-papievyte-to-head-ventures-unit/). Head from 2024-01 [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/). Her predecessor as sole full-time investor, Alexandre Perrin, left in 2023-06 [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014), so she likely got the seat because she was the only investor left when the role was open (inference).\n- **GCV Powerlist 2026:** named among the 100 leading corporate venturing professionals (2026-06-22) [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/).\n- **Education:** not found. LinkedIn was not fetchable, and GCV and FX News Group do not mention it.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.xtxmarkets.com/ventures/ | No team listed; contact ventures@xtxmarkets.com (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | Not fetched; the XTX Ventures showcase is https://uk.linkedin.com/showcase/xtx-ventures/ |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| Events (2026-11-11) | https://www.thinkingaboutthinking.org/ae-global-summit | Panel 2026-11-27, Friends House London; also Future of Computing 2025 co-organiser and GCV Symposium 2026 speaker |\n| TheOrg (aggregator) | https://theorg.com/org/qureight/org-chart/gabriele-papievyte | \"Investor and Board Observer\" at Qureight; profile marked unverified |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-03-26: \"The Future of Supercompute\" panel, Future of Computing Conference London; she was also a co-organiser [src](https://www.future-of-computing.com/conference-2025-london/).\n- 2026-11-27 (upcoming): \"How to build a trillion-pound AI company\" panel, AE Global Summit London [src](https://www.thinkingaboutthinking.org/ae-global-summit).\n- No podcasts found (searched 2026-11-11).\n\n## How they think\n- She favours academic and deep-tech teams moving \"from lab to market\" (SPhotonix release, 2025-12-04) [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/sphotonix-secures-4-5-million-154200176.html).\n- The unit's mandate under her: \"invest in technical founders who combine deep AI and machine learning expertise\" with clear conviction [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/).\n- Stage: late-seed to Series A in 2024 [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/). The site now says \"Seed to Series B - and beyond\" [src](https://www.xtxmarkets.com/ventures/).\n- Contrarian views and calls she got right or wrong: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n- GCV, \"Papievyte to lead XTX Ventures\" (2024-01-25): role, check size and board seats. https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/\n- GCV Powerlist 2026 profile (2026-06-22): recent deals, exits and board seats. https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/\n- SPhotonix pre-seed release (2025-12-04): her only on-record deal quote found. https://finance.yahoo.com/news/sphotonix-secures-4-5-million-154200176.html\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X, Substack or podcast presence was located.\n\n## Portfolio\nDeals where she is personally named are marked **named**. Everything else is a unit deal during her tenure with no person attributed.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| SPhotonix (5D optical storage, optics) | Pre-seed $4.5M; **named** (quoted); first in | 2025-12-04 | co-led with Creator Fund | Creator Fund (Alexandra Ntemourtsidou); angels | [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/sphotonix-secures-4-5-million-154200176.html) |\n| Oriole Networks (optical switching for AI datacentres) | Seed £10M (XTX co-lead per one outlet); board seat per GCV, not named on the round | 2024-03-27 | co-led | UCL Technology Fund; Clean Growth Fund; Dorilton | [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/ucl-spinout-bags-10m-ai-super-brains-100x-faster-llm-training) |\n| Oriole Networks | Series A $22M; re-up | 2024-10-21 | no (Plural led) | Plural; UCL Technology Fund; Clean Growth Fund; Dorilton | [src](https://fortune.com/2024/10/21/oriole-networks-series-a-22-milliion-photonics-startups-ai-data-center-energy-demands/) |\n| Tenstorrent (AI chips) | Board seat per GCV (director vs observer unconfirmed). XTX Markets in Series D $693M and Series E $1.5B; not named on either round | 2024-12-02 / 2026-10-19 | no | Samsung Securities; AFW; Fidelity; others | [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/) [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-announces-series-e) |\n| Qureight (AI lung/heart trial imaging) | Board observer (TheOrg; Qureight lists her as neither director nor observer); round unknown | unknown | unknown | Molten; Hargreave Hale AIM VCT; others | [src](https://theorg.com/org/qureight/org-chart/gabriele-papievyte) [src](https://www.qureight.com/about) |\n| Turbine (AI cell simulation) | Board seat (inherited; Perrin-era follow-on) | before 2024-01 | unknown | | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/) |\n| IOMED (medical-record data) | Board seat (inherited; Perrin-era initial) | before 2024-01 | unknown | | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/) |\n| Lucida Medical, Corintis, InvenireX, ElectronX, A5X, X-energy | Unit \"recent deals\"; she is not named | 2024–2026 | unknown | X-energy alongside Jane Street and Amazon | [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/) |\n\nNo source names her on Flapping Airplanes, Proxima Fusion, Maven Robotics, Atomic Machines, Gimlet Labs or Anthropic.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **SPhotonix (2025-12):** a Southampton-rooted photonics spinout (CSO Prof. Peter Kazansky). The $4.5M pre-seed was co-led with Creator Fund, and she is the only XTX person quoted [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/sphotonix-secures-4-5-million-154200176.html). The intro path is unknown. The paid release also says CEO Ilya Kazansky \"led\" the raise, so the lead language is loose.\n- **Oriole Networks (2024):** a UCL spinout. XTX co-led the seed two months after her promotion and followed in Plural's Series A seven months later [src](https://fortune.com/2024/10/21/oriole-networks-series-a-22-milliion-photonics-startups-ai-data-center-energy-demands/). Her board seat (GCV 2026) suggests she is the deal owner. No source says she sourced it.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| XTX Ventures | at: Investment Manager → Head | 2023-08 / 2024-01 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/) |\n| Inovia Capital | at: Investment Associate (left by 2023-08) | unknown | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/) |\n| Conception X | helps lead XTX's partnership | by 2024-01 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/) |\n| Creator Fund (Alexandra Ntemourtsidou) | co-lead on SPhotonix | 2025-12 | [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/sphotonix-secures-4-5-million-154200176.html) |\n| Alexandre Perrin | predecessor as head; she inherited his IOMED and Turbine positions | 2023 | [src](https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014) |\n| Alex Gerko / Zar Amrolia | parent co-CEOs; last-known investment committee (2020) | | [src](https://qz.com/1923323/xtx-makes-venture-capital-investments-in-machine-learning-firms/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is the Tenstorrent seat a directorship or an observer seat, and when did XTX first invest?\n- Who sits on today's investment committee, and does she have sign-off up to some amount?\n- Which entity books the large lab tickets (Flapping, Proxima), and is she involved?\n- Education and the start date at Inovia.\n- Whether XTX is Erwann Le Lannou's (unconfirmed) employer, and how AI-lab deals split between him and her.\n\n## Update 2026-11-11 (research pass 2)\n\nWeb search was available this pass. New checks: Companies House officer and PSC registers (Oriole Networks, Qureight, Lucida Medical), an officer search on her surname, the GCV Powerlist re-fetch, GCV's March 2026 \"standout women in CVC\" piece, conference agendas, the Kopa.ai coverage, and SEC EDGAR for Tenstorrent.\n\n**1. Her UK \"board seats\" are not directorships.**\n- Companies House lists 7 officers at Oriole Networks Ltd (14802813), none of them her. The directors are CEO James Regan, Prof Georgios Zervas, Plural's Ian Hogarth (appointed 2024-08-19, at the Series A) and UCLTF Corporate Directors Ltd [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14802813/officers).\n- Qureight Ltd (11132399) lists 15 current and former officers, none of them her [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/11132399/officers).\n- An officer search on \"Papievyte\" and its variants returns only one unrelated person [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/search/officers?q=Papievyte).\n- So GCV's \"serves on the boards\" [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/) means non-statutory roles on the UK names, most likely observer seats. That is unconfirmed, because observer seats are never filed. Either way she has no vote.\n- XTX co-led the Oriole seed, but Plural took the directorship at the Series A.\n- **Tenstorrent stays unresolved.** It is not a UK company. EDGAR has Tenstorrent Inc. Form Ds only up to 2021 [src](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Tenstorrent%22&forms=D). Press on the Series E filing names other directors and no one from XTX [src](https://pulse2.com/tenstorrent-raises-1-5-billion-series-e-at-6-2-billion-valuation/), so treat her Tenstorrent seat as an observer seat until a source says otherwise.\n\n**2. XTX has taken control-sized stakes before her time.**\n- XTX Investments UK Ltd was a registrable person at Lucida Medical with \"Ownership of shares – More than 25% but not more than 50%\". It was notified 2021-02-26 and ceased 2022-10-17, with a one-month re-listing in 2023-05/06 that looks like a filing correction [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/12320979/persons-with-significant-control).\n- \"Ceased\" means the stake fell below 25%, which matches GCV's later \"23%\". It does not mean XTX exited.\n- This was a Perrin-era position. So Lucida is not a deal she sourced, even though GCV lists it among \"recent deals\".\n- XTX holds no Lucida directorship; the current directors are Rix, Tuch and Fisher [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/12320979/officers).\n\n**3. The unit is small.** GCV (2026-03-06) puts it at \"around 35 portfolio companies at present\" [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/consumer/standout-women-in-cvc-in-uk/). That supports the earlier \"nearly 30\" (2024-01) and suggests the Powerlist's \"over 100\" is all-time.\n\n**4. New unit deal in her tenure: Kopa.ai (Vilnius, agentic AI for e-commerce).**\n- €2M seed on 2026-05-28, co-led by XTX Ventures and Practica Capital. Inovia Capital, her former firm, participated, as did angel Etan Ilfeld [src](https://tech.eu/2026/05/28/kopaai-raises-eur2m-to-build-ai-agents-for-end-to-end-e-commerce-operations/).\n- No XTX person is named or quoted (tech.eu, vestbee). Her role is unconfirmed, though her dates and the Inovia overlap fit.\n\n**5. Event circuit: the best cold route.**\n- She co-organised the Future of Computing Conference (London, 2025-03-26, hosted at Barclays). Her co-organisers came from Conception X (Carrie Baptist), IQ Capital and Silicon Catalyst UK [src](https://www.future-of-computing.com/conference-2025-london/).\n- She sat on its \"Future of Supercompute\" panel with Oriole CEO James Regan and Majestic Labs co-founder Ofer Shacham [src](https://www.future-of-computing.com/conference-2025-london/).\n- She is listed as a 2026 GCV Symposium speaker [src](https://www.gcvsymposium.com/speakers-2026).\n- **Upcoming:** a panel on \"How to build a trillion-pound AI company\" at the AE Global Summit, Friends House London, 2026-11-27 10:00. XTX Markets is an annual sponsor of that summit [src](https://www.thinkingaboutthinking.org/ae-global-summit).\n\n**6. Exit in her tenure:** GCV lists Prior Labs among XTX's exits [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/). SAP closed its acquisition on 2026-07-17 [src](https://priorlabs.ai/blog-posts/priorlabs-sap).\n\n**Background (unconfirmed, aggregator only):**\n- MarketScreener lists an undergraduate degree from Sciences Po and a graduate degree from LSE. It also gives Inovia until 2022-12-31 and XTX from 2023-07-31 [src](https://ca.marketscreener.com/insider/GABRIELE-PAPIEVYTE-A30TVJ/).\n- startupintros.com contradicts itself (it shows Inovia as current and adds a Balderton role) and is disregarded.\n- If the Inovia end date is right, there is a gap of about seven months before XTX. It is unexplained; one search snippet mentions Octopus Ventures, which was not verified.\n\n**Updated read for my lens:**\n- Still medium.\n- She is a non-voting, small-ticket co-investor at a unit with about 35 live names.\n- Her value is in three things: a technical co-investor on UK and European AI-hardware seeds, the London deeptech-hardware event network (Future of Computing, Conception X), and a possible bridge to the parent balance sheet.\n- Best time to meet her is the 2026-11-27 AE Global Summit panel.\n\n## Sources\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/papievyte-to-lead-xtx-ventures/\n- https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/\n- https://fxnewsgroup.com/forex-news/institutional/exclusive-xtx-markets-names-gabriele-papievyte-to-head-ventures-unit/\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/news/sphotonix-secures-4-5-million-154200176.html\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/ucl-spinout-bags-10m-ai-super-brains-100x-faster-llm-training\n- https://fortune.com/2024/10/21/oriole-networks-series-a-22-milliion-photonics-startups-ai-data-center-energy-demands/\n- https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-closes-693m-of-series-d-funding-led-by-samsung-securities-and-afw-partners\n- https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-announces-series-e\n- https://theorg.com/org/qureight/org-chart/gabriele-papievyte (aggregator)\n- https://www.qureight.com/about\n- https://www.conceptionx.org/\n- https://www.hecalumni.fr/cv/alexandre-perrin/hec/2014\n- https://qz.com/1923323/xtx-makes-venture-capital-investments-in-machine-learning-firms/\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/11074405/officers\n- https://www.xtxmarkets.com/ventures/\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14802813/officers\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/11132399/officers\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/12320979/persons-with-significant-control\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/consumer/standout-women-in-cvc-in-uk/\n- https://tech.eu/2026/05/28/kopaai-raises-eur2m-to-build-ai-agents-for-end-to-end-e-commerce-operations/\n- https://www.future-of-computing.com/conference-2025-london/\n- https://www.thinkingaboutthinking.org/ae-global-summit\n- https://www.gcvsymposium.com/speakers-2026\n- https://ca.marketscreener.com/insider/GABRIELE-PAPIEVYTE-A30TVJ/ (aggregator)\n", "person:gaby-lorenzi": "\n# Gaby Lorenzi\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 by the Etched price-setters pass. Builds on `firms/primary-venture-partners.md` and `people/brian-schechter.md` (neither edited). Primary sources: her primary.vc team page, the raw HTML of every one of Primary's 55 posts (checked for her name), the Compute 100 site and its private list, the podcast RSS feed (all 14 episode descriptions), Etched's progress posts and a UCLA student event page. I used three web searches. None of them found an X account, a Substack or any interview where she is the guest. The podcast has no public transcript. I started a local Whisper transcription and stopped it, because a two-host show without speaker labels cannot tell her lines apart from Schechter's, and the quote rule needs that.*\n\n## One-line take\nShe is the only investor at Primary besides Brian Schechter whose title is compute. She did not set the Etched price; Schechter did that in 2023. She runs the machinery Primary now uses to find the next Etched: a public ranked list of 100 compute companies, a podcast with the founders on it, a daily newsletter and a call for startups.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she matters:** Primary's compute practice is two people. Schechter's 2026-02 thesis post names her as his thinking partner: \"Gaby and I are in deep debate with Claude around our compute thesis\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond)). She is credited as co-creator of The Compute 100, a ranked list of 50 public and 50 private compute companies ([src](https://thecompute100.com)). Its podcast puts private-company CEOs in front of Primary (Upscale AI, Callosum, Neurophos, Unconventional AI, Efficient Computer). It is a deal-flow funnel that looks like media.\n- **What she brings (inference from her background):** She is a data scientist. She studied applied math and CS and built recommendation systems at T. Rowe Price ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/gaby-lorenzi)). The list is a screen built from data: private companies were \"selected based on capital raised, revenue, headcount growth\" ([src](https://thecompute100.com/private)). Nothing says she built that screen herself.\n- **Constraint:** She has no named deal, no board seat and no check authority that any source records. Primary is a seed firm, and on Etched it moved from price-setter to participant once Stripes came in ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/scaling-for-the-ai-era-fund-v)). She cannot anchor the $10M+ rounds THESIS.md cares about.\n- **What she'd never do (firm-level inference):** Back a compute team with no seasoned silicon operators. The co-signed call for startups says the best founders \"balance first-principles and AI-native thinking\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/primarys-call-for-compute-startups)), and Primary's thesis post wants that ambition paired with \"seasoned builders\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond)).\n\n## Differential insight\nEvery quote below was checked against the page's raw HTML on 2026-11-10. There is no first-person source where she speaks alone. Her voice so far is the joint byline with Schechter.\n\n- **She was in the room on the compute thesis before she got a byline.** Schechter's post of 2026-02-10: \"Gaby and I are in deep debate with Claude\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond)). The \"sparring partner\" is partly a human-plus-model loop. Primary says it is \"restructuring our workflows around AI tools\" (same source).\n- **She was already close to Schechter by May 2025.** His solo post The Token Exponential (dated 2025-05-31 on the page) mentions \"Gaby's dad... is writing a novel with AI\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-token-exponential)). This is an upper bound on when she joined, inferred and not stated. The page's structured metadata says 2026-03-20, so the date is uncertain.\n- **The Compute 100 is a sourcing screen, not just a podcast.** It is credited as \"Created by Brian Schechter, Gaby Lorenzi\" ([src](https://thecompute100.com)). It launched on 2026-05-20 with a podcast, a daily newsletter and X ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/introducing-the-compute-100)). The private list carries valuation, funding and investors for 50 companies, and every page ends with a \"BUILDING IN COMPUTE?\" link to founders@primary.vc. In effect, Primary has published its target map.\n- **Her name is on the Etched C post, but the post does not say Primary invested.** Primary's post for the C (2026-07-23, Sequoia lead, $10.3B) shows author cards for Schechter and Lorenzi. Its structured metadata names Schechter only. It welcomes Sequoia \"to the partnership\" and points to \"why we've invested in every round\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/announcing-etcheds-series-c-led-by-sequoia-at-10-3b)). Etched's own C post does not list Primary ([src](https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference)).\n- **The co-signed rule on why seed avoids compute:** \"it's easy for seed investors to agree and move on\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/primarys-call-for-compute-startups), 2026-11-09). The reasons given include a \"thin bench of diligence partners\". This is the gap she and the outside-expert diligence model exist to fill.\n- **Where the co-signed call says the margin goes:** It is software, not silicon. The software that moves tokens \"is where a lot of the margin will sit\" (same source). And for agent memory: \"Neutral across models, hardware, and runtimes, or it's a feature.\"\n- **Her profile on the C post and the call reads \"infrastructure, spanning hardware and software across compute, data, developer tools, and AI\"** ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/gaby-lorenzi)). That is a broader remit than chips. The podcast guest mix reflects it: Modal, TensorWave, Crusoe, Upscale AI.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High as a source and an early-warning system. Not a co-investor peer.**\n- Her job is to find AI-semis and compute seeds before anyone else does. That is exactly THESIS.md's \"AI semiconductors and compute\" lane, and she sits at the firm that set the first price on Etched.\n- The private Compute 100 list is a ready-made watchlist. It includes the large-seed compute shapes you write into: Unconventional AI ($475M raised at a $4.5B valuation per the list, described on the podcast as \"one of the largest seed rounds ever\") and Callosum ($100M seed led by Atomico) ([src](https://thecompute100.com/private), [podcast feed](https://anchor.fm/s/10f3acc34/podcast/rss)).\n- **Limits:** She has no named deals and no check authority on record, and Primary does not write $20M into $600M seeds. Use her for sourcing and diligence, and for early signals of which seeds Primary is about to lead.\n- **Deals that matter to you:** Etched (as co-signer of the C post only), plus the Compute 100 podcast guests listed below.\n\n## Background\n| When | What | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Not dated | Born and raised in Baltimore | [src](https://luma.com/znj6sb6v) |\n| to 2021 | UCLA, Applied Math and Computer Science (class of 2021). Led Bruin Ventures, raced for Duffl | [src](https://luma.com/znj6sb6v) |\n| UCLA years | Co-founded Bruin Ventures, \"partnering with LA venture firms on research and strategy\" | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/gaby-lorenzi) |\n| not dated | Data science at startups (unnamed) | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/gaby-lorenzi) |\n| not dated | Data Scientist, T. Rowe Price, building recommendation systems | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/gaby-lorenzi) |\n| by 2025-05-31 (inferred) | At or close to Primary (named in Schechter's post) | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-token-exponential) |\n| 2026-02-28 | Associate, Primary (speaker title for the Girls Into VC at UCLA summit; the event's start_at on the page is 2026-02-28) | [src](https://luma.com/znj6sb6v) |\n| by 2026-11-10 | Senior Associate, Compute | [src](https://www.primary.vc/people) |\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.primary.vc/team/gaby-lorenzi | Team page. Lists no portfolio items and no articles (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://newsletter.thecompute100.com/ | The Compute 100 daily newsletter. Posts are unbylined, daily on weekdays (2026-11) |\n| X | nothing found | No personal handle found. The Compute 100 site links to an X account |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/gaby-lorenzi/ | Linked from thecompute100.com. Not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://podcasts.apple.com/podcast/id1896779326 ; https://open.spotify.com/show/65JM7piHg9CINWis3uFMns | The Compute 100, co-hosted with Schechter. 14 episodes from 2026-05-20 to 2026-11-03, roughly every one to three weeks ([RSS](https://anchor.fm/s/10f3acc34/podcast/rss)) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nAll are episodes of The Compute 100 that she co-hosts. I read the descriptions only, from the [RSS feed](https://anchor.fm/s/10f3acc34/podcast/rss).\n- 2026-05-20: Justin Selig, Efficient Computer. Selig was an early Cerebras engineer, then an investor at Eclipse, then joined Efficient.\n- 2026-05-21: Erik Bernhardsson, Modal. Valued at $4.65B per the description.\n- 2026-05-22: Lisa Spelman, Cornelis Networks. She was formerly GM of Xeon at Intel.\n- 2026-05-26: Jeff Wittich, Ampere. Acquired by SoftBank for $6.5B.\n- 2026-06-17: Jeff Tatarchuk, TensorWave. An AMD-only neocloud; $350M B co-led by Magnetar and AMD Ventures.\n- 2026-07-09: Chase Lochmiller, Crusoe. Crusoe acquired Primary's portfolio company Atero.\n- 2026-07-22: Shahin Farshchi, Lux. Topics included younger chip founders and the shrinking set of venture-scale outcomes.\n- 2026-07-30: The Biological Computing Company (Primary portfolio). $50M seed.\n- 2026-08-24: Patrick Bowen, Neurophos. Optical compute for prefill; $110M A backed by M12.\n- 2026-09-22: Naveen Rao, Unconventional AI. Brain-inspired chips; launched with \"nearly half a billion dollars at a $4.5B valuation\".\n- 2026-10-06: Austin Lyons, Chipstrat.\n- 2026-10-13: Jascha Achterberg, Callosum. Heterogeneous compute; $100M seed led by Atomico.\n- 2026-10-21: Barun Kar and Rajiv Khemani, Upscale AI. AI networking, spun out of Auradine.\n- 2026-11-03: Timothy Arcuri, UBS. Topics included the Cerebras–OpenAI deal and where startups can compete.\n- 2026-02-28: Speaker at the Girls Into VC at UCLA 2026 Summit, billed as an Associate at Primary ([src](https://luma.com/znj6sb6v)).\n\n## How they think\nHer stated views exist only as jointly bylined Primary posts, so treat them as hers and Schechter's together.\n- **Seed alpha in compute.** \"Our thesis is that there is alpha at seed for compute\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/primarys-call-for-compute-startups)).\n- **Compute is a supply chain that ends in tokens.** The call covers eight areas: 3D-chip metrology, materials (including Matterstack), in-chip cooling, power delivery, a token compiler and router, an agent memory layer, AI data cold storage, and a financial layer for compute (same source).\n- **Neutrality is the moat for infrastructure software.** \"or it's a feature\" (same source).\n- **Bubble-aware but long demand.** \"In the AI era, more is never enough\" (same source).\n- **Calls right or wrong:** none attributable to her yet.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Primary's Call for Compute Startups](https://www.primary.vc/articles/primarys-call-for-compute-startups) (2026-11-09, co-signed): the eight areas she is sourcing in now.\n2. [The Compute 100 private list](https://thecompute100.com/private): the 50-company target map, with funding, valuation and investors.\n3. [Introducing The Compute 100](https://www.primary.vc/articles/introducing-the-compute-100) (2026-05-20, co-signed): how the list was built.\n4. [Announcing Etched's Series C](https://www.primary.vc/articles/announcing-etcheds-series-c-led-by-sequoia-at-10-3b) (2026-07-23, her card alongside Schechter's).\n5. [Our compute thesis](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond) (2026-02-10, Schechter, names her).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. There is no personal X account, and her LinkedIn was not read. The Compute 100 brand runs X, a newsletter and a podcast. None of its output is bylined to her.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Etched | Series C, $300M at $10.3B. Her role: author card on Primary's announcement only. Primary's participation is not confirmed (Etched's post omits it) | 2026-07-23 | no (Sequoia led) | a16z, Jane Street, Diffusion, Argo, SK hynix | [Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/announcing-etcheds-series-c-led-by-sequoia-at-10-3b), [Etched](https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference) |\n| Etched | $700M at $21B. Primary participated. She is not named | 2026-08-18 | no (Jane Street led) | Kleiner, Sequoia, a16z, Thiel, Tiger, BCV, Neo, Stripes, Positive Sum, Diffusion, Argo, Blackstone | [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one) |\n\nNo portfolio card or deal post names her as an investor ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/gaby-lorenzi), which lists \"No items found\"). She is not recorded on Primary's other compute deals: Etched seed 2023-03 and A 2024-06 (Schechter), Atero 2024, Haiqu 2025, The Biological Computing Company 2025, or Matterstack.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Etched timing, and where she sits in it:**\n  - The first price was Primary's ~$34M seed in 2023-03 (Schechter). That was about two years before any trace of her at Primary ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=662004)).\n  - She first appears on Etched as co-signer of the C post, 40 months after the seed and the day Sequoia priced it at $10.3B.\n  - Ramtin Naimi said on ILTB that consensus turned when \"Sequoia blessed him with a $10 billion valuation\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4), via `people/ramtin-naimi.md`). Her public involvement starts at the consensus moment, not the contrarian one.\n- **How she sources:** The pipeline runs from the Compute 100 list to podcast invitations to the founders@primary.vc call-to-action, then to the eight-area call ([src](https://thecompute100.com), [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/primarys-call-for-compute-startups)). No intro path for any specific deal is sourced.\n- **How she prices:** Nothing found. No source gives her view on valuation or ownership.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Brian Schechter (Primary) | Senior partner on compute. Co-creator of The Compute 100, co-host, co-signer of posts, thesis debate partner | by 2026-02 (likely by 2025-05) | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond), [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-token-exponential) |\n| Primary Venture Partners | Senior Associate, Compute (earlier Associate) | not found | [src](https://www.primary.vc/people) |\n| Bruin Ventures (UCLA) | Co-founder | UCLA years, to 2021 | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/gaby-lorenzi) |\n| T. Rowe Price | Former Data Scientist | dates not found | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/gaby-lorenzi) |\n| Shahin Farshchi (Lux) | Podcast guest | 2026-07-22 | [RSS](https://anchor.fm/s/10f3acc34/podcast/rss) |\n| Naveen Rao (Unconventional AI) | Podcast guest | 2026-09-22 | [RSS](https://anchor.fm/s/10f3acc34/podcast/rss) |\n| Justin Selig (Efficient; ex-Eclipse, ex-Cerebras) | Podcast guest | 2026-05-20 | [RSS](https://anchor.fm/s/10f3acc34/podcast/rss) |\n| Chase Lochmiller (Crusoe, acquirer of Atero) | Podcast guest | 2026-07-09 | [RSS](https://anchor.fm/s/10f3acc34/podcast/rss) |\n| Gavin Uberti, Robert Wachen (Etched) | Named on the C post she co-signed | 2026-07-23 | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/announcing-etcheds-series-c-led-by-sequoia-at-10-3b) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did she join Primary? The promotion from Associate to Senior Associate came after about 2026-02-28; the exact date is not known. LinkedIn (linkedin.com/in/gaby-lorenzi) is the next source to check.\n- Did Primary invest in Etched's C? Etched omits it. `patterns/co-investor-gangs.md` says Schechter \"co-led\" the C, and no source I read supports that.\n- Which deals has she worked? Matterstack and the unnamed stealth memory and alternative-compute companies are candidates. None is sourced.\n- Who on the Compute 100 private list has Primary met or term-sheeted? Is the list a pipeline tracker?\n- Podcast transcripts: are there any lines where she identifies herself, especially on pricing or founder judgment?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.primary.vc/team/gaby-lorenzi ; https://www.primary.vc/people\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/primarys-call-for-compute-startups (2026-11-09)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/announcing-etcheds-series-c-led-by-sequoia-at-10-3b (2026-07-23)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/introducing-the-compute-100 (2026-05-20)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond (2026-02-10)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-token-exponential (dated 2025-05-31 on page)\n- https://thecompute100.com ; https://thecompute100.com/private ; https://newsletter.thecompute100.com/\n- https://anchor.fm/s/10f3acc34/podcast/rss ; https://podcasts.apple.com/podcast/id1896779326\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference ; https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one\n- https://luma.com/znj6sb6v (Girls Into VC at UCLA 2026 Summit)\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=662004 ; https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4 (via people/ramtin-naimi.md)\n", "person:garry-tan": "\n# Garry Tan\n\n## One-line take\nThe person who decides which AI founders get YC's stamp each batch, and whose own record (Coinbase and Instacart from inside YC, a $7M Fund I that returned mostly on those two) shows that their edge is seeing products before anyone else does, not picking themes.\n\n## Crux\nTan wins on proximity and product judgement. As a designer-engineer inside YC they saw every team's product before Demo Day, and their two great seed checks came from that position: Coinbase (they say they voted it into YC S12) and Instacart (they say they flagged it in mid-batch). The test they state is whether they would want to work for that founder [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan). They learned the cost of not owning enough the hard way: Fund II kept YC-sized checks as the fund grew 5X. That is why Initialized moved to leading rounds with a single yes or no [src](https://hackernoon.com/ending-the-crypto-winter-with-garry-tan-and-brett-gibson-of-initialized-capital-39e758df4d0e). At YC today they are limited by the standard-deal format: tiny fixed checks across hundreds of companies. They frame YC companies as \"mice\" playing in the labs' playground, not as rival labs [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tcffqaIi-Jg). Nothing in these sources shows them writing lab-scale ($100M+) checks personally; their leverage is selection and signal, not check size.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Initialized Fund I ($7M, 2012) was \"70% Coinbase and 30% Instacart\"; they cite 2X DPI and roughly 11-14X TVPI as of 2020 [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan)\n- First LP money came from Alex Bangash of Trusted Insight, introduced by Jessica Livingston; Bangash \"put together the first $2 million, $3 million of our $7 million fund\" [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan)\n- Mistake on ownership: Fund II ($40M) kept $50-200K checks: \"We learned the hard way that percentage ownership matters a ton.\" [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan)\n- Why ownership matters at seed, in their telling: \"LPs are still looking for things that look like Sequoia or Benchmark\" [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan)\n- The Coinbase Demo Day note (cap in the mid-teens of millions) \"was totally undersubscribed.\" [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan)\n- On Instacart: \"I definitely flagged them and helped them get into YC about halfway through.\" [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan)\n- YC's standard-deal history: the SV Angel / Yuri Milner auto-invest was a handshake that Harj Taggar negotiated. \"Sam Altman and I had to take that program in-house\" with an institutional vehicle in 2014 [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan)\n- From 2018 Initialized leads with a unilateral decision: \"if it's a yes, they're done fundraising.\" It sometimes gets to yes in three days, and at least two companies it first turned down were funded after fixing the reason it gave [src](https://hackernoon.com/ending-the-crypto-winter-with-garry-tan-and-brett-gibson-of-initialized-capital-39e758df4d0e)\n- Coinbase entry price: \"I paid 15 cents to buy shares of coinbase nine years ago\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SwvoR9mLTXM)\n- Their Posterous co-founder Brett Gibson \"rewrote Paul Graham's code for applications and recommendation systems\". YC's selection software was partly built by Tan's own co-founder [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=33Go0tzj6fw)\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-11)\n- The older archive (2018-2022) is more candid about the economics of a fund than their YC-era talks. On Acquired they gave Fund I's concentration, its DPI, the first LP and how the YC standard deal came about [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan).\n- Their decision rules are about the product and the builder, not the theme: a working TestFlight beat the many grocery pitches [src](https://hackernoon.com/ending-the-crypto-winter-with-garry-tan-and-brett-gibson-of-initialized-capital-39e758df4d0e); \"personally i love funding builders\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x5YApjnTG10); they ask what founders believe that nobody else does [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SwvoR9mLTXM).\n- They admit their mistakes on the record: turning down Thiel's offer to join Palantir at the start ($200M+ by their count) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dtnG0ELjvcM), under-owning in Fund II, and Benchmark's pass on Posterous [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan).\n- On AI they cast the current wave as the start of a new geek-led cycle they want to be early in [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TwDJhUJL-5o), with YC founders as the \"mice\" next to the big labs [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tcffqaIi-Jg).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. YC under Tan is where many AI-native automation companies get their first check, and close to half of S23 used LLMs [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TwDJhUJL-5o). They are not a co-investor at $20M scale themselves. They matter as a source of deal flow and signal, and as a way into Demo Day timing. Their Initialized story is also a template for the owner's own LP pitch: a tiny insider fund, backed by a fund-of-funds allocator (Trusted Insight), that learned to care about ownership. They featured Extropic's AI chip on launch day [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h8LSgzIR1g8).\n\n## Background\n- Stanford (they say it helped their career a lot) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GknGzw0Nqpw); Microsoft after graduating in 2003 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dtnG0ELjvcM)\n- Software for Peter Thiel's Clarium Capital, 2005 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x5YApjnTG10); Palantir, employee ~10, built a product team [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dtnG0ELjvcM)\n- Co-founded Posterous with Brett Gibson (YC S08) [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan)\n- YC designer in residence 2011, then partner; built Bookface [src](https://hackernoon.com/ending-the-crypto-winter-with-garry-tan-and-brett-gibson-of-initialized-capital-39e758df4d0e) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=33Go0tzj6fw)\n- Co-founded Initialized 2012 with Alexis Ohanian and Harj Taggar; left YC Nov 2015 [src](https://blog.garrytan.com/thank-you-y-combinator); $770M committed across five core funds by April 2021 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SwvoR9mLTXM)\n- YC President & CEO from January 2023 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=33Go0tzj6fw)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://garrytan.com | not checked |\n| Substack / blog | https://blog.garrytan.com | Posthaven; dense 2011-2015, weekly video companion posts 2019-2020 |\n| X | https://x.com/garrytan | very active; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Lightcone (YC) https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TwDJhUJL-5o ; How To Build The Future (YC) | Lightcone roughly monthly since Feb 2024 |\n| YouTube | https://www.youtube.com/@garrytan | ~156 videos 2019-2026 (full list in garry-tan-voice-media.csv) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2018-10 — Hacker Noon Podcast — Ending the Crypto Winter (with Brett Gibson) — [link](https://hackernoon.com/ending-the-crypto-winter-with-garry-tan-and-brett-gibson-of-initialized-capital-39e758df4d0e) — the shift from $50K checks to unilateral yes/no; decisions in 3 days\n- 2019-04 — 20VC — ownership as funds grow — [link](https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-initializeds-garry-tan-on-the-most-important-thing-a-seed-investor-can-do-for-founders-how-ownership-requirements-change-with-evolution-of-funds--why-there-is-not-too-much-capital-chasing-too-few-deals-2) — not mined (no transcript)\n- 2020-09 — Acquired ACQ2 — How YC Rewrote the Seed Playbook — [link](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan) — Fund I returns, first LP, ownership lesson, standard deal history\n- 2021-04 — own channel — I funded Coinbase in 2012 — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x5YApjnTG10) — three reasons they said yes\n- 2021-04 — own channel — You can be a VC (I'm hiring) — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SwvoR9mLTXM) — 15-cent Coinbase basis, $770M AUM, founder filter\n- 2022-12 — own channel — The Future of Initialized Capital — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=33Go0tzj6fw) — handover to Jen Wolf and Brett Gibson\n- 2023-10 — Stanford eCorner — Unconventional Advice for Founders — [link](https://stvp.stanford.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2024/09/unconventional-advice-for-founders-entire-talk-transcript.pdf) — not mined (the transcript PDF would not download)\n- 2024-02 — Lightcone ep. 1 — The Truth About Building AI Startups Today — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TwDJhUJL-5o)\n- 2024-05 — own channel x No Priors — Sarah Guo and Elad Gil — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tcffqaIi-Jg)\n- 2025-04 — Knowledge Project; Uncapped with Jack Altman — not mined\n\n## How they think\n- Builders over credentials: their complaint about venture is that it over-relies on credentials, not that capital is too plentiful [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GknGzw0Nqpw)\n- Contrarian and right: Coinbase thesis rested on Mt. Gox's bad UX and a fiat-risk reading list from Clarium [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x5YApjnTG10)\n- Heat is not a signal either way [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan)\n- Calls right: Coinbase, Instacart (Fund I); Cruise, Flexport, Opendoor, Patreon in Fund II [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan). Calls wrong: Palantir co-founding offer; Fund II check sizing\n\n## Read these first\n1. Acquired ACQ2 (2020): the most candid account of fund economics, LPs and the YC deal [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan)\n2. Hacker Noon podcast (2018): how Initialized decides and how its check sizes changed [src](https://hackernoon.com/ending-the-crypto-winter-with-garry-tan-and-brett-gibson-of-initialized-capital-39e758df4d0e)\n3. \"I funded Coinbase in 2012\" (2021) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x5YApjnTG10)\n4. \"You can be a VC\" (2021): how they think about LPs and returns [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SwvoR9mLTXM)\n5. Lightcone ep. 1 (2024): YC's AI framing [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TwDJhUJL-5o)\n\n## Social engagement\nNot analysed in this pass. Their YouTube channel features their portfolio founders (Parker Conrad, Ryan Petersen, Brian Armstrong) and investor peers (Elad Gil, Sarah Guo, Hans Tung) [src](https://rosetta.to/u/garrytan).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Coinbase | Seed (YC S12 Demo Day note, cap mid-teens $M) | 2012 | no | | [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan) |\n| Instacart | Seed (Initialized Fund I) | 2012 | unknown | | [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan) |\n| Opendoor, Cruise, Flexport, Patreon | Seed (Initialized Fund II) | 2013-2015 | unknown | | [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan) |\n| CoinTracker | Seed | c. 2018 | unknown | | [src](https://hackernoon.com/ending-the-crypto-winter-with-garry-tan-and-brett-gibson-of-initialized-capital-39e758df4d0e) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Coinbase (2012):** Brian Armstrong sent Tan 0.05 BTC through the Bitbank prototype and asked for co-founder advice. Tan was reading S12 applications and says the infinity vote in Paul Graham's system guaranteed an interview. They invested at Demo Day into an undersubscribed note [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x5YApjnTG10) [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan).\n- **Instacart (2012):** Apoorva Mehta applied after the deadline. They were the first grocery-delivery pitch to send a working TestFlight, then had a driver deliver beer to Tan at YC. Tan says they flagged the company into the batch [src](https://hackernoon.com/ending-the-crypto-winter-with-garry-tan-and-brett-gibson-of-initialized-capital-39e758df4d0e) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SwvoR9mLTXM).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Alex Bangash (Trusted Insight) | First institutional LP in Initialized Fund I | 2012 | [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan) |\n| Jessica Livingston | Introduced them to Bangash; YC co-founder | 2008 | [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan) |\n| Brett Gibson | Posterous co-founder; Initialized MP | 2008 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=33Go0tzj6fw) |\n| Harj Taggar, Alexis Ohanian | Initialized co-founders as YC younger partners | 2012 | [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan) |\n| Sam Altman | Brought YC standard deal in-house together | 2014 | [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan) |\n| Peter Thiel | Recruited them to Palantir; Clarium | 2003 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dtnG0ELjvcM) |\n| Brian Armstrong | First-check investor in Coinbase | 2012 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x5YApjnTG10) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact Initialized checks into Coinbase and Instacart, and their personal versus fund split (they cite $300K invested in the video).\n- Did they or YC invest in Extropic? They featured the chip but no investment is sourced here.\n- How YC's standard deal and its pro-rata behaviour have changed for AI companies since 2023. The Lightcone episodes from 2024 to 2026 are catalogued but not mined.\n- The 20VC 2019 and Invest Like the Best EP. 267 episodes have no transcripts we could reach.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/how-yc-rewrote-the-seed-playbook-with-garry-tan\n- https://hackernoon.com/ending-the-crypto-winter-with-garry-tan-and-brett-gibson-of-initialized-capital-39e758df4d0e\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x5YApjnTG10\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SwvoR9mLTXM\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=33Go0tzj6fw\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dtnG0ELjvcM\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GknGzw0Nqpw\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TwDJhUJL-5o\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tcffqaIi-Jg\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h8LSgzIR1g8\n- https://blog.garrytan.com/thank-you-y-combinator\n- https://rosetta.to/u/garrytan (caption transcripts)\n", "person:gaurav-ahuja": "\n# Gaurav Ahuja\n\n## One-line take\nA Thrive-trained fintech partner who built a company (Imprint) from inside the fund, then left in 2024 to run Timeless Partners, a small \"hold forever\" vehicle whose book has quietly tilted to frontier AI, robotics and defense.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** Thrive gave them access to fintech infrastructure rounds early (Dutchie, Check, Parafin, Stytch, TruckSmarter), and they proved they could build, not just pick, by co-founding Imprint while a Thrive partner [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2232832). That operator-plus-investor profile, and the Thrive network, now get Timeless small allocations next to Benchmark, Sequoia, Greylock and Lux.\n- **What constrains them:** capital. Every Timeless vehicle on EDGAR is SPV-sized ($2.1M-$14.5M). The debut venture fund filed at a $30M target, and the evergreen \"Capital\" vehicle reports $31.75M from 9 investors as of 2026-11-02 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021071/000202107126000001/primary_doc.xml). Timeless participates; it is not on record leading a round.\n- **What they'd never do:** sell early because a fund clock says so. Timeless pitches itself as built to \"remain on the cap table for as long as greatness takes\" [src](https://www.timelesspartners.com/beliefs).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Imprint was built inside Thrive.** A sitting Thrive partner co-founded and chaired a portfolio fintech. TechCrunch: \"Imprint was also co-founded by a VC\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2232832) (2021-11-15). Thrive put money into the ~$15M seed (with Affirm) and the $38M Series A.\n- **How they judged Stytch:** Thrive called developers who used the product and came away impressed by its \"best-in-class API docs and speed to go live\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2177105) (2021-07-14). Developer reference calls, not a market-size memo.\n- **Timeless runs a hedge-fund vehicle alongside its VC funds.** On EDGAR, Ahuja signs as \"Managing Director of the General Partner\" for eight Timeless entities. One of them, Timeless Partners Capital, LP, files as a *Hedge Fund* (first sale 2024-11-01; $11.94M from 7 investors by 2025-11; $31.75M from 9 investors by 2026-11-02). Next to it sit venture SPVs: One Horizon-Timeless SPV I ($2.14M, Jun 2024), Opportunity Fund I ($5.6M, Sep 2024), Opportunity Fund II ($3.29M, May 2025), Timeless Ivy ($3.33M, May 2026) and Timeless Omega ($14.5M from 5 investors, Aug 2026), plus Ventures I ($30M target, Aug 2024) and Ventures II (Mar 2026) (Form D primary docs: [Capital](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021071/000202107126000001/primary_doc.xml), [SPV I](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2025944/000202594424000002/primary_doc.xml), [Opp I](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2035655/000203565524000001/primary_doc.xml), [Opp II](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2072462/000207246225000001/primary_doc.xml), [Ivy](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2135999/000213599926000001/primary_doc.xml), [Omega](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2150249/000215024926000001/primary_doc.xml), [Ventures I](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2025309/000202530924000002/primary_doc.xml), [Ventures II](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2112529/000211252926000001/primary_doc.xml)). An evergreen, hedge-fund-style wrapper fits the stated thesis: \"Most funds are structured to sell before greatness begins.\" [src](https://www.timelesspartners.com/beliefs)\n- **The Timeless book breaks from the Thrive fintech beat.** The firm's partners page lists Applied Compute (ex-OpenAI RL agents), Mecka (physical-AI data), Manifold (warehouse robotics), Diffractive Labs (AI scientist for materials), Gallatin (defense logistics), Geolocked (data-center cooling), Caylex (agents that operate software), SpaceX, Polymarket and Mandolin, alongside Imprint [src](https://www.timelesspartners.com/partners). None of this appears in press about them.\n- **Content as a sourcing channel.** Timeless publishes \"The 100 Year Conversation\"; No. 001 (2025-10-07) is a long interview with Michael Dell [src](https://www.timelesspartners.com/journal/conversations/michael-dell). Founder-legacy content looks like the brand play for getting access as a small check.\n- **Confirmed Timeless participations:** ai.work's $10M seed (Jun 2025, led by A* and lool ventures) names Timeless Partners [src](https://www.01net.it/ai-work-exits-stealth-with-10-million-seed-and-the-next-generation-of-autonomous-employees-for-the-enterprise/). Mandolin's seed (co-led by SignalFire and Maverick) had \"participation from Timeless\", per the company's own post as quoted in search results (summary; post not fetched); Mandolin logo is on [src](https://www.timelesspartners.com/partners).\n- **Name-collision warning.** MarketScreener/Zonebourse merges this person with a ChrysCapital senior partner of the same name (UVA 2001, INSEAD 2006) [src](https://www.zonebourse.com/insider/GAURAV-AHUJA-A0EOMY/). Its \"Thrive 2017-2024\" dates come from that merged record, so treat them as unreliable.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** As a lead investor or anchor they are a poor fit: Timeless writes SPV-scale checks and doesn't meet the \"funds that can write $10M+\" filter. The Thrive-era book (Imprint, Stytch, Check, Parafin, Dutchie, TruckSmarter) is fintech and SaaS, not AI. What is relevant is the Timeless portfolio as a **co-investor signal and source of intros** in frontier AI and adjacent compute: Applied Compute, Mecka, Manifold, Diffractive, Geolocked (data-center cooling), Gallatin and Caylex. They are also a possible small LP-or-co-invest counterparty for very large seeds, given the \"never sell\" stance and the hedge-fund wrapper. Worth a relationship, not a priority profile.\n\n## Background\n- Born and raised in Michigan; Cranbrook Schools class of 2010 [src](https://schools.cranbrook.edu/list-detail?pk=252558).\n- University of Michigan Ross, BBA with high distinction (also pre-med sciences) [src](https://build.org/?p=15242).\n- McKinsey & Co, Technology, Media and Telecom practice [src](https://build.org/?p=15242). Years not given.\n- TA Associates, San Francisco, later-stage enterprise software [src](https://build.org/?p=15242). An aggregator dates this 2016-2017, but its record is conflated (see above) [src](https://www.zonebourse.com/insider/GAURAV-AHUJA-A0EOMY/).\n- Thrive Capital: titled \"Investor at Thrive Capital\" in the Aug 2020 Dutchie release (per search summary; release not fetched) [src](https://benzinga.com/z/17136952). Partner by Dec 2021 [src](https://americankahani.com/?p=12893). \"Partner at Thrive Capital\" at TruckSmarter [src](https://www.trucksmarter.com/blog/introducing-trucksmarter-putting-drivers-first). \"Thrive General Partner\" on Acquired, Dec 2023 [src](https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/visa-follow-up-and-todays-payments-ecosystem-with-gaurav-ahuja). Exact start year unsourced. Left by mid-2024: first Timeless Form D was 2024-06-25 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2025944/000202594424000002/primary_doc.xml). An aggregator gives the end date as 2024 [src](https://www.zonebourse.com/insider/GAURAV-AHUJA-A0EOMY/).\n- Co-founded Imprint in 2020 with Daragh Murphy (CEO) and serves as chairman [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2232832).\n- Forbes 30 Under 30, Venture Capital. The Dec 2021 coverage implies the 2022 list [src](https://americankahani.com/?p=12893). Cranbrook says 2023 [src](https://schools.cranbrook.edu/list-detail?pk=252558).\n- Founded Timeless Partners in 2024; the debut fund was reported at $30-40M (PitchBook, Jul 2024, via aggregator) [src](https://f4.fund/firms/timeless-partners).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.timelesspartners.com | Firm site; doesn't name people. Linked to Ahuja through portfolio overlap with Draftboard and Form Ds (inferred) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Timeless \"Conversations\" journal is the closest thing |\n| X | nothing found | no verified account as of 2026-11-10 |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/ahujag/ | per Draftboard profile; not opened |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Timeless \"The 100 Year Conversation\" (firm-hosted; host not named) | https://www.timelesspartners.com/journal/conversations/michael-dell |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-12-03 — ACQ2 by Acquired — \"Visa Follow-Up and Today's Payments Ecosystem (with Gaurav Ahuja)\" — https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/visa-follow-up-and-todays-payments-ecosystem-with-gaurav-ahuja — show notes say they were a research source for Acquired's Visa episode. Topics: interchange erosion, real-time payments, startup openings, the Visa/Mastercard duopoly. No transcript text was retrievable, so no quotes.\n\n## How they think\n- **Embedded finance:** fintech wins by letting non-banks own the financial product (co-brand cards, payroll, merchant cash advances). Imprint's pitch was to cut out the middlemen and send economics \"back to brands and their customers\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2232832).\n- **Developer diligence:** judge API companies by talking to the developers who use them [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2177105).\n- **Timeless thesis (firm voice):** the most valuable companies were the most doubted; breakthroughs follow a decade of hidden work; great founders start in bear markets [src](https://www.timelesspartners.com/beliefs).\n- Calls right or wrong: Stytch reached $1B on its Nov 2021 Series B (search snippet in Thrive rows). Imprint went on to raise later rounds from Ribbit and Khosla (see thrive-capital rows). Nothing found on misses.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Timeless \"Beliefs\" page: the hold-forever thesis in their own framing. https://www.timelesspartners.com/beliefs\n2. TechCrunch on Imprint's Series A (2021-11-15): the investor-founder setup. https://techcrunch.com/?p=2232832\n3. TechCrunch on Stytch's Series A (2021-07-14): how they diligence developer tools. https://techcrunch.com/?p=2177105\n4. Timeless \"In Conversation with Michael Dell\" (2025-10-07): what they value in founders. https://www.timelesspartners.com/journal/conversations/michael-dell\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No verified X or Substack; the \"Gaurav\" handles in search results belong to other people.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Dutchie (Thrive) | Series B ($35M) | 2020-08 | Thrive led; Ahuja on board | Casa Verde, Gron, Howard Schultz | [src](https://cheddar.com/media/cannabis-e-commerce-platform-dutchie-raises-million-former-starbucks-ceo-among-investors), board [src](https://americankahani.com/?p=12893) |\n| Check (Thrive) | Series B ($35M) | 2021-01-27 | Stripe and Thrive; Ahuja on board | Stripe | [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/payroll-infrastructure-startup-check-announces-its-public-launch-with-35-million-series-b-investment-from-stripe-and-thrive-301215921.html) |\n| Stytch (Thrive) | Series A ($30M) | 2021-07-14 | Yes; board | Coatue, Benchmark, Index | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2177105) (row already in thrive-capital-fullbook) |\n| Parafin (Thrive) | Seed + Series A ($34M) | 2021-09-21 | Co-led with Ribbit; Ahuja on board | Ribbit | [src](https://www.parafin.com/blog/parafin-series-a-financing) |\n| Imprint (Thrive; co-founder) | Seed (~$15M), then Series A ($38M) | 2020-21; 2021-11-15 | Seed from Affirm and Thrive; A co-led by Kleiner and Stripe | Affirm, Kleiner, Stripe | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2232832) |\n| LeafLink (Thrive) | board observer | by 2021 | n/a | | [src](https://americankahani.com/?p=12893) |\n| TruckSmarter (Thrive) | Series B ($25M) | 2022-09-27 | Thrive led; Ahuja quoted | Founders Fund, a16z, Bain Capital Ventures, FJ Labs, Fin Capital | [src](https://www.trucksmarter.com/blog/trucksmarter-raises-25m-to-grow-owner-operator-ecosystem) |\n| ai.work (Timeless) | Seed ($10M) | 2025-06-10 | No (A* and lool led) | firstminute, SV Angel, Eckstein | [src](https://www.01net.it/ai-work-exits-stealth-with-10-million-seed-and-the-next-generation-of-autonomous-employees-for-the-enterprise/) |\n| Mandolin (healthcare AI; Timeless) | Seed (participant) | 2025 | No (SignalFire and Maverick co-led) | SV Angel; Greylock led the A | logo on firm page [src](https://www.timelesspartners.com/partners); seed participation per company post seen only via search summary |\n| Corridor (AI coding security; Timeless) | Series A ($25M at $200M) | 2026-03-18 | No (Felicis led) | Conviction, Lux, Sunflower, Datadog, SV Angel | [src](https://fortune.com/press-releases/corridor-raises-25m-series-a-ai-coding-security-2026-03-18). The release names \"Timeless\", but the logo on Timeless's page links to corridoradvisors.com, a different company, so attribution is likely but not certain |\n| Applied Compute (Timeless) | Growth ($350M at $3.25B) | 2026-11-02 | No (Elad Gil led) | Kleiner, Lux, NVIDIA, Blackstone, Definition | [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise) |\n| Mecka, Manifold, Diffractive, Gallatin, Geolocked, Caylex, Blackstar, Embo, Instinct, Showrunner, River (useriver.com, not River AI), Rune, Sailor Health, Vitalis AI, Yuzu, SpaceX, Polymarket (Timeless) | round unknown | 2024-26 | No lead on record | | firm partners page [src](https://www.timelesspartners.com/partners) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Imprint:** co-founded in 2020 while Ahuja was a Thrive partner, with Daragh Murphy as CEO. Thrive and Affirm funded the ~$15M seed. The Series A drew Kleiner, Stripe and celebrity and CEO angels (James Corden, Lloyd Blankfein) [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2232832). It's a template for Thrive-incubated fintech.\n- **Stytch:** Thrive led the $30M Series A after reference calls with developers, and Ahuja joined the board alongside Benchmark's Chetan Puttagunta [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2177105).\n- **Leaving Thrive:** no announcement found. Timeless's first filed vehicle, One Horizon-Timeless SPV I (first sale 2024-06-14), appeared about four months after the aggregator's Thrive end date.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Thrive Capital | investor -> partner -> GP; left | before 2020 to 2024 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2232832) |\n| Timeless Partners | founder; Managing Director of GP | 2024 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2025309/000202530924000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Imprint / Daragh Murphy | co-founder and chairman / co-founder | 2020 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2232832) |\n| Josh Kushner | former boss (Thrive founder) | | [src](https://f4.fund/firms/timeless-partners) |\n| Chetan Puttagunta (Benchmark) | Stytch co-board member | 2021 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2177105) |\n| Eshan Shetty | listed as Timeless partner (aggregator) | | Crunchbase via search snippet; unverified |\n| Michael Dell | Timeless podcast guest | 2025-10 | [src](https://www.timelesspartners.com/journal/conversations/michael-dell) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who are the 9 investors in Timeless Partners Capital, LP? Is Thrive or Kushner an LP or anchor?\n- Which company sits behind each SPV (One Horizon, Opportunity I and II, Ivy, Omega)? Omega ($14.5M, Aug 2026) is the biggest; Applied Compute's Nov 2026 growth round is one candidate (unverified).\n- Exact Thrive start date and the date of their partner promotion.\n- What did they say on ACQ2 (transcript)? Do they host the Dell conversation?\n- Is Eshan Shetty real and current at Timeless?\n\n## Sources\n- https://techcrunch.com/?p=2232832\n- https://techcrunch.com/?p=2177105\n- https://schools.cranbrook.edu/list-detail?pk=252558\n- https://build.org/?p=15242\n- https://americankahani.com/?p=12893\n- https://www.timelesspartners.com/partners\n- https://www.timelesspartners.com/beliefs\n- https://www.timelesspartners.com/journal/conversations/michael-dell\n- SEC Form D primary docs: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2025309/000202530924000002/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2112529/000211252926000001/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021071/000202107124000002/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021071/000202107125000002/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021071/000202107126000001/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2035655/000203565524000001/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2072462/000207246225000001/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2150249/000215024926000001/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2135999/000213599926000001/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2025944/000202594424000002/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.acquired.fm/episodes/visa-follow-up-and-todays-payments-ecosystem-with-gaurav-ahuja\n- https://www.parafin.com/blog/parafin-series-a-financing\n- https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/payroll-infrastructure-startup-check-announces-its-public-launch-with-35-million-series-b-investment-from-stripe-and-thrive-301215921.html\n- https://cheddar.com/media/cannabis-e-commerce-platform-dutchie-raises-million-former-starbucks-ceo-among-investors\n- https://www.trucksmarter.com/blog/trucksmarter-raises-25m-to-grow-owner-operator-ecosystem\n- https://www.trucksmarter.com/blog/introducing-trucksmarter-putting-drivers-first\n- https://www.01net.it/ai-work-exits-stealth-with-10-million-seed-and-the-next-generation-of-autonomous-employees-for-the-enterprise/\n- https://whisperlist.draftboard.com/vc/34771243-c36a-45f4-b6a1-d4a013e47e43/gaurav-ahuja-timeless (aggregator)\n- https://f4.fund/firms/timeless-partners (aggregator; 403 on fetch, content via search snippet)\n- https://www.zonebourse.com/insider/GAURAV-AHUJA-A0EOMY/ (aggregator; conflated record)\n", "person:gavin-baker": "\n# Gavin Baker\n\n*Research note (2026-11-10): the web-search budget for this run was spent before this profile started. Everything below comes from direct fetches of company releases, SEC EDGAR, the Atreides site and earlier files in this repo. No podcast transcript or X post could be fetched (Colossus returned 403). That is the main gap: every insight below is from releases and filings, not from his own long-form talk.*\n\n## One-line take\nThe one Atreides person who is personally named on private deals. He enters AI chip and networking companies quietly early, then co-leads the step-up round and takes the board seat there. That makes him a likely Series B/C buyer and board member for a compute company someone else seeded.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he runs a public book that is a concentrated AI-compute bet (SpaceX, Micron, Cerebras, Astera, Credo, NVIDIA, CoreWeave at 2026-06-30 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1777813/000177781326000009/ATREIDES13FXMLQ2.xml)). Private chip and networking founders get a large cheque plus a buyer who knows the public comps. His thesis line on Crusoe: \"the economics flow to the lowest-cost producer of intelligence\" [src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-f-funding).\n- **Structural tie to Valor:** he is a promoter and the CIO of a co-investment manager on Valor Atreides AI I. The fund has two co-GPs (Valor AI Associates I and Atreides AI Associates I) and sold $758.7M to 664 investors at a $50k minimum [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2033590/000101297526000109/primary_doc.xml). In joint Valor and Atreides deals, the board seat often goes to him rather than to a Valor partner (Celero, Aria, Positron below).\n- **Constraint:** the private money is wealth-channel and SPV money (Jefferies, iCapital, Morgan Stanley, UBS; a Goldman feeder filed 2026-11-04), not a classic VC LP base (see [firms/atreides-management.md](../firms/atreides-management.md)). That favours rounds that wealth clients recognise.\n- **What he would likely never do:** lead a sub-$10M pre-seed or take a board seat at entry. In every case found, his seat comes at a later, larger round. That is an absence of evidence, not proof.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Board seat comes at the step-up, not at entry.** Celero: Atreides was a Series B participant in Nov 2025 ([CapitalG](https://capitalg.com/insights/celero-bringing-coherence-to-ai-scale-networking/)). It co-led the Series C in Sep 2026, and Baker is listed as a director on the C Form D, \"Gavin Baker: Director\" ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2019351/000201935126000001/primary_doc.xml)). Positron: Atreides was in the $23.5M seed in Feb 2025 ([The Star](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/02/12/ai-chip-startup-positron-raises-235-million-seed-round-to-take-on-nvidia)) and co-led the Series A in Jul 2025 ([Converge](https://convergedigest.com/positron-ai-raises-51-6m-series-a-to-scale-custom-inference-silicon/?amp=1)). The May 2026 Series B Form D lists no Atreides director ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2077932/000207793226000002/primary_doc.xml)). Only at the Sep 2026 Series C does Verdict report he \"will take a seat on Positron's board\" ([Verdict](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/)). That seat is announced but not yet confirmed in a filing. Crusoe follows the same arc: participant in the 2022 Series C and the 2025 Series E, then co-lead of the $3.9B Series F at $30.9B ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-f-funding)).\n- **Positron board composition:** Forest Baskett (NEA), Thomas Jermoluk (Jim Clark's office), Baker, and Dylan Patel of SemiAnalysis ([Verdict](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/)). He now shares a board with the most-read independent semiconductor analyst. For a chip founder, that board is itself a signal.\n- **Networking is his chosen bottleneck:** \"Networking is one of the most consequential bets in the AI infrastructure stack\" (Aria, [datacenter.news](https://datacenter.news/story/aria-launches-deep-networking-platform-with-usd-125-million)). His seats are Enfabrica (Series B lead 2023), Xsight (2021 co-lead; seat per an aggregator), Aria (2026) and Celero (2026). All four are AI-cluster networking silicon. Enfabrica ended in an Nvidia deal of more than $900M to hire the CEO and license the technology ([DCD](https://www.datacenterdynamics.com/en/news/nvidia-hires-staff-from-networking-firm-enfabrica-and-signs-tech-license-in-900m-deal-report/)).\n- **Pre-Atreides venture experience:** the firm's own bio says he helped lead Fidelity's venture investing from 2013 to 2017 ([src](https://atreidesmgmt.com/team/gavin-baker/)). An aggregator lists board-observer roles from that era at Nutanix, Roku, 23andMe, Jet.com, AppNexus and Dataminr ([The Marque](https://www.themarque.com/profile/gavin-baker); aggregator, unconfirmed).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a seed investor in my sense. Positron's seed is the only seed found, and that was a $23.5M round with Valor where he took no seat. But he is the most likely Series B/C co-lead and board member for an AI chip or networking company I back at seed, and he does not need rigid ownership: he co-leads with Valor, CapitalG, NEA or Index. The deals that matter to me are Positron (seed → C), Celero, Enfabrica and Aria. For frontier labs, Atreides joined Flapping Airplanes only at the $1B Series A, and no person is named there.\n\n## Background\n- Dartmouth College, AB in economics and history ([firm bio](https://atreidesmgmt.com/team/gavin-baker/)). An aggregator gives 1994–1999 ([The Marque](https://www.themarque.com/profile/gavin-baker)).\n- Fidelity Investments, 1999–2017 ([firm bio](https://atreidesmgmt.com/team/gavin-baker/)):\n  - Started as an analyst covering semiconductors, pharma, retail, consumer packaged goods and telecom.\n  - PM of Select Pharmaceuticals (2002–05), Select Telecommunications (2007–09) and Select Wireless (2007–11).\n  - PM of OTC Portfolio, 2009–2017. He left on 2017-09-18, when Sonu Kalra took over the fund (existing repo rel row, citing Reuters via Business Insider).\n  - Helped lead Fidelity venture investments, 2013–17. The aggregator credits Uber, SpaceX, Redfin and Twilio ([The Marque](https://www.themarque.com/profile/gavin-baker)). TechCrunch confirms SpaceX ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/10/these-are-the-investors-funding-musks-97-billion-openai-takeover-attempt)).\n- Founded Atreides Management in 2019 and has been MP & CIO since ([firm bio](https://atreidesmgmt.com/team/gavin-baker/)). He got the seat by founding the firm; there was no promotion.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://atreidesmgmt.com/team/gavin-baker/ | firm bio, fetched 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | not verified this run (search budget exhausted) | |\n| LinkedIn | not verified this run | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Known as a guest on compute podcasts. No episode page could be fetched (Colossus 403) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Not fetched this run. A \"Liquidity Summit\" talk with Antonio Gracias is referenced in [firms/valor-equity-partners.md](../firms/valor-equity-partners.md) as a summary only. Next run: pull his Invest Like the Best transcripts for verbatim rules of thumb.\n\n## How they think\n- **Lowest-cost producer wins:** \"the economics flow to the lowest-cost producer of intelligence\" (Crusoe F, [src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-f-funding)).\n- **Open and frontier models together:** \"both frontier and open models will increasingly be used together\" (Fireworks D, [src](https://fireworks.ai/news/fireworks-raises-1-5-billion-series-d)).\n- **Networking as a bottleneck bet** (Aria, above).\n- **Application layer, selectively:** he led Legora's Series E, saying \"the bar for software is high\" ([src](https://legora.com/newsroom/legora-raises-550-million-series-e-at-8.8-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-growth)).\n- **Space as compute-adjacent:** on EnduroSat he said \"lower-cost access to orbit is expanding the set of companies\" ([src](https://www.endurosat.com/news/endurosat-raises-205m-to-scale-next-generation-space-fleets/)). It fits the SpaceX-heavy public book.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Crusoe Series F release](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-f-funding): his cost-of-intelligence thesis.\n- [Positron $875M, Verdict](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/): the board line-up.\n- [Celero Series C Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2019351/000201935126000001/primary_doc.xml): a filed board seat.\n- [Valor Atreides AI I Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2033590/000101297526000109/primary_doc.xml): the joint-fund structure.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked this run (no X fetch).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Xsight Labs | Series D | 2021-03-04 | co-lead (Atreides) | Valor (co-lead), Intel Capital, M12 | [Globes](https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-israeli-chip-co-xsight-labs-raises-over-100m-1001362863) (firm only; seat per aggregator) |\n| WEKA | Series D ($135M) | 2022-11-15 | no (Generation IM led) | | [Generation IM](https://www.generationim.com/our-thinking/news/weka-raises-135m-to-fuel-hypergrowth-and-global-expansion/) (he is quoted) |\n| Enfabrica | Series B ($125M) | 2023-09-12 | yes, plus board | Sutter Hill, NVIDIA | [Reuters via The Star](https://thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2023/09/12/ai-chip-startup-enfabrica-raises-125-million-with-backing-from-nvidia) |\n| WEKA | Series E ($140M) | 2024-05-15 | no | | [WEKA](https://www.weka.io/news/weka-nets-140m-in-series-e-funding-at-1-6b-valuation) (re-up) |\n| Positron | Seed ($23.5M) | 2025-02-11 | lead not stated | Valor, Flume, Resilience Reserve | [The Star](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/02/12/ai-chip-startup-positron-raises-235-million-seed-round-to-take-on-nvidia) (firm only; first in) |\n| Positron | Series A ($51.6M) | 2025-07-28 | co-lead (firm) | Valor, DFJ Growth | [Converge](https://convergedigest.com/positron-ai-raises-51-6m-series-a-to-scale-custom-inference-silicon/?amp=1) (firm only) |\n| Aria Networks | first round ($125M) | 2026-04-08 | no (Sutter Hill) | Valor, Eclipse | [datacenter.news](https://datacenter.news/story/aria-launches-deep-networking-platform-with-usd-125-million) (board; quoted) |\n| Fireworks AI | Series D ($1.505B at $17.5B) | 2026-07-16 | co-lead | Index, TCV | [Fireworks](https://fireworks.ai/news/fireworks-raises-1-5-billion-series-d) (quoted) |\n| Celero Communications | Series C ($275M) | 2026-09-08 | co-lead | Valor, CapitalG | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2019351/000201935126000001/primary_doc.xml) (director) |\n| Positron | Series C ($875M at $5B post) | 2026-09-10 | co-lead (first tranche) | NEA, Valor, Andra, SemiAnalysis Capital | [Verdict](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/) (board announced; no filing yet) |\n| EnduroSat | Growth ($205M; stage not named) | 2026-09-16 | co-lead | Riot Ventures (co-lead) | [EnduroSat](https://www.endurosat.com/news/endurosat-raises-205m-to-scale-next-generation-space-fleets/) (quoted) |\n| Crusoe | Series F ($3.9B at $30.9B) | 2026-09-17 | co-lead | Mubadala, Valor | [Crusoe](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-f-funding) (quoted) |\n| Legora | Series E ($550M at $8.8B) | 2026-10-21 | lead | Baron, Capital Group, Fidelity, Accel, Benchmark | [Legora](https://legora.com/newsroom/legora-raises-550-million-series-e-at-8.8-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-growth) (quoted) |\n| Valor Atreides AI I | fund ($758.7M sold) | first sale 2025-01-14 | co-manager / promoter | Valor (Gracias, Shulkin, Sabater) | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2033590/000101297526000109/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Celero:** a 10-month upgrade from Series B participant to Series C co-lead, then the board seat. CapitalG's James Luo also joined the board as a co-lead ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2019351/000201935126000001/primary_doc.xml)). Valor co-led but holds no seat on the filing. So in this joint deal, Atreides is the governance side.\n- **Positron:** Valor and Atreides are together from the seed on. The pre-C board had no Atreides director (Series B Form D). At the C, the board line-up is announced with Baker and Dylan Patel, whose SemiAnalysis Capital also co-led ([Verdict](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/)).\n- **Enfabrica:** he led the 2023 Series B and took the seat. About two years later Nvidia hired the CEO and licensed the technology for more than $900M ([DCD](https://www.datacenterdynamics.com/en/news/nvidia-hires-staff-from-networking-firm-enfabrica-and-signs-tech-license-in-900m-deal-report/)).\n- **Intro paths:** not found for any deal.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Atreides Management | founder, MP & CIO | 2019 | [firm bio](https://atreidesmgmt.com/team/gavin-baker/) |\n| Fidelity | analyst to PM (OTC Portfolio); helped lead venture investing 2013–17 | 1999 to 2017 | [firm bio](https://atreidesmgmt.com/team/gavin-baker/) |\n| Antonio Gracias / Valor Equity Partners | co-promoters of Valor Atreides AI I; Baker is CIO of the Atreides co-manager, not a Valor employee | 2025-01 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2033590/000101297526000109/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Stefan Dyckerhoff (Sutter Hill) | fellow director at Aria and Celero | 2026 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2019351/000201935126000001/primary_doc.xml), [datacenter.news](https://datacenter.news/story/aria-launches-deep-networking-platform-with-usd-125-million) |\n| Dylan Patel (SemiAnalysis), Forest Baskett (NEA), Thomas Jermoluk | fellow Positron directors (announced) | 2026-09 | [Verdict](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/) |\n| James Luo (CapitalG) | fellow Celero director | 2026 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2019351/000201935126000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** no direct channel was verified. The firm site lists only a contact form ([src](https://www.atreidesmgmt.com/team/)). The paths that the evidence supports are fellow directors: Dyckerhoff (Sutter Hill), Patel (SemiAnalysis), Luo (CapitalG). The other path is Valor (Gracias), with which he co-leads most AI growth rounds.\n\n## Open questions\n- Confirm the Positron board seat once the Series C Form D is filed.\n- Who at Atreides does the diligence on these deals? No one below Baker is named on any deal (see the firm page).\n- Pull his podcast transcripts (Invest Like the Best and others) for verbatim rules on private checks, pricing and board seats.\n- Did any of the Flapping Airplanes Series A cheque come through Valor Atreides AI I?\n\n## Sources\n- https://atreidesmgmt.com/team/gavin-baker/ ; https://www.atreidesmgmt.com/team/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2019351/000201935126000001/primary_doc.xml (Celero C Form D)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2077932/000207793226000002/primary_doc.xml (Positron B Form D)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2033590/000101297526000109/primary_doc.xml (Valor Atreides AI I)\n- https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/ ; https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/02/12/ai-chip-startup-positron-raises-235-million-seed-round-to-take-on-nvidia ; https://convergedigest.com/positron-ai-raises-51-6m-series-a-to-scale-custom-inference-silicon/?amp=1\n- https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-f-funding ; https://fireworks.ai/news/fireworks-raises-1-5-billion-series-d ; https://legora.com/newsroom/legora-raises-550-million-series-e-at-8.8-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-growth ; https://www.endurosat.com/news/endurosat-raises-205m-to-scale-next-generation-space-fleets/\n- https://datacenter.news/story/aria-launches-deep-networking-platform-with-usd-125-million ; https://thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2023/09/12/ai-chip-startup-enfabrica-raises-125-million-with-backing-from-nvidia ; https://www.generationim.com/our-thinking/news/weka-raises-135m-to-fuel-hypergrowth-and-global-expansion/\n- https://www.themarque.com/profile/gavin-baker (aggregator)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1777813/000177781326000009/ATREIDES13FXMLQ2.xml (13F)\n", "person:gavin-uberti": "\n# Gavin Uberti\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. Builds on `companies/etched.md`, `people/brian-schechter.md`, `people/patrick-oshaughnessy.md`, `people/koko-xs.md` and `patterns/2026-11-10-etched-price-setters.md` (none edited). Primary sources: both Invest Like the Best transcripts (EP.356, 2023-12-12; EP.480, 2026-06-30, read in full from podscripts), the etched.com leadership block, his GitHub, the Harvard Crimson Thiel article and TechCrunch. X could not be read (HTTP 402) and no handle could be verified. Pronouns: TechCrunch's Found podcast page uses \"he\" for him [src](https://techcrunch.com/podcast/etched-founder-gavin-uberti-thinks-his-company-stands-on-the-shoulders-of-giants/).*\n\n**Caution on EP.480 attribution.** The EP.480 transcript has no speaker labels and two founders on it (Uberti and Robert Wachen; the transcript garbles Wachen's name). The fundraising passage contains \"I remember Gavin and I were like sitting down\", so that block is Wachen's voice. Below, EP.480 quotes are pinned to Uberti only where the host addresses him or the content is his own life story. Everything else is labelled \"a founder\" or \"Wachen\".\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the founder whose 2023 \"transformers will win\" bet turned a ~$34M seed into a $21B company in about 41 months ([SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=662004); [Etched](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one)). He is the reference case for the owner's thesis that frontier-compute seeds are mispriced because majors pass on young founders.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He is a kernels engineer, not a chip-industry insider. He started writing kernels at Xnor.ai at 17, then did the same at OctoML, and concluded that fast decode is about data movement, not math ([EP.480](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/etched-building-ai-hardware-to-make-inference-faster-and-cheaper-invest-like-the-best-ep480), ~24:14). From there he made one narrow bet early: \"In 2022, we made a bet that transformers would take over the world\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/25/etched-is-building-an-ai-chip-that-only-runs-transformer-models/)). He then hired \"legends\" (Mark Ross from Cypress, Brian Loiler from Nvidia's HGX/DGX team, Ajat Hukkoo from Broadcom and Intel) to cover his lack of tape-out experience ([etched.com](https://www.etched.com/)).\n- **Constraint.** The model is concentrated and capital-hungry. It only works if transformer-family models stay dominant (\"under 100 models that actually mattered\", per a founder on EP.480, ~51:31), and it needed about $100M before a chip existed. Every major Valley investor passed on that ask in late 2023 (EP.480, ~01:02:43).\n- **What he'd never do.** He won't build a general graph compiler or support arbitrary PyTorch/ONNX graphs; Etched chose kernels first instead (founder, EP.480, ~51:31). He also won't do a cautious test-chip-first plan. The founders' line is that they would \"go all the way\" rather than \"half-ass it\" (Wachen-voiced block, EP.480, ~01:04:22).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Model-specific silicon was the founding thesis, stated on air in 2023:** \"I think the best approach here is model-specific chips.\" ([EP.356](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/gavin-uberti-real-time-ai-the-future-of-ai-hardware-invest-like-the-best-ep356), 23:27, 2023-12-12).\n- **Founder-stated seed size differs from the press figure.** Uberti: \"Then we raised $5.5 million.\" (EP.356, ~01:09:30). SiliconANGLE says $5.4M and TechCrunch implies $5.36M. That is close enough to be one round, but in EP.480 a founder speaks of \"the $15 million we had in the bank\" in late 2023 (~01:02:14). That implies roughly $10M of capital that no press round explains: possibly an unannounced extension, SAFEs or the debt below. Unreconciled.\n- **Mark Ross was recruited before he was hired.** Uberti says Ross first told them \"you're going to need two to three million dollars\" (EP.356, ~01:09:08). After the raise they went back to Ross, who became chief hardware architect (EP.356). On EP.480 he calls Ross \"one of our earliest, earliest supporters\" (~06:16).\n- **The A was a pile of soft commits, not a priced lead (Wachen-voiced).** After every major investor passed, they phoned anyone who knew an investor. They brought a spreadsheet to a board meeting showing \"these are all like soft commits\" totalling about $103M and took it (EP.480, ~01:05:09). Primary and Positive Sum were later announced as co-leads ([Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware)).\n- **Non-dilutive capital bridged the gap VCs wouldn't (Wachen-voiced).** \"we actually went out and got a debt provider\" for the tape-out (~01:03:57). Synopsys let them use \"their emulators on extremely favorable terms where you pay over many years\" (~01:06:08). TSMC worked with them before the $100M was raised. None of this is in press.\n- **Talent rejections turned into angel checks.** Of three ex-Nvidia rack leads they mapped, Loiler joined. Of the two retirees: \"And the other two guys end up investing, by the way.\" (founder, EP.480, ~31:03). These are unnamed Etched angels and a strong needs-id lead.\n- **High-frequency traders were the first technical believers.** \"the only people that took us seriously were in high-frequency trading.\" A founder adds that \"dozens\" of HFT people joined the team (EP.480, ~52:12). This prefigures the cap table: Two Sigma in the A, Jane Street from the B, and HRT and Jump in later financings ([companies/etched.md](../companies/etched.md)). Speaker unlabeled; the kernels framing suggests Uberti, but this is not confirmed.\n- **\"Bimodal\" hiring: legends plus naive first-principles talent.** \"We have a pretty bimodal talent philosophy.\" (founder, EP.480, ~28:55). The \"naive\" half includes Sanford, Uberti's two-person FTC robotics partner from high school, who built a cold-plate rig in his first week (EP.480, ~31:42).\n- **The Thiel Fellowship came after the money, not before.** The Crimson lists \"Gavin J. Uberti ’25\" in the 2024 Thiel class with Zhu and Wachen, alongside Mercor's Adarsh Hiremath ([Crimson](https://www.thecrimson.com/article/2024/3/27/thiel-fellowship-startups-2024/); the date comes from the URL path). Primary's seed was in March 2023, so the fellowship postdates first institutional money by about a year.\n- **Contrarian AI views from 2023:** \"I don't believe in this concept of a fast takeoff.\" (EP.356, 04:54). Also \"I don't think there's a future for medium-sized models\" (~100B parameters; EP.356, ~01:18:21). On Nvidia: \"they're stuck in the innovator's dilemma\" (EP.356, ~59:38).\n- **He claimed a lead over incumbents early:** \"I think we have more than an 18-month head start on them\" ([TechCrunch Found, 2024-08-20](https://techcrunch.com/podcast/etched-founder-gavin-uberti-thinks-his-company-stands-on-the-shoulders-of-giants/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is a founder, not an allocator, but he matters to `THESIS.md` in three ways:\n1. **Reference deal.** Etched is the AI-semis seed the thesis is built to catch. Primary was first in at ~$34M (2023-03), the A came about 15 months later, and growth leads arrived only after hardware proof ([companies/etched.md](../companies/etched.md)).\n2. **Deal-flow node for the next compute founders.** His hiring map covers Nvidia rack veterans, HFT kernel engineers and young robotics and maths talent. That is exactly the pool new chip and lab founders come out of. The two retired Nvidia leads who invested are an angel network worth identifying.\n3. **Small angel footprint** (aggregator only): Nous Research seed (2024-01) and Augustus Series B (2026-07). Nous is an open-model lab, which fits the frontier-lab interest.\n\n## Background\n- High school: FTC robotics with partner Sanford. They ran a two-person team instead of the usual ~20, held a world-record score at one point and were \"rated by OPR third in the world for software development\" (EP.480, ~26:05). He credits a teacher, Gunnar (surname garbled in the transcript), who left Microsoft to teach and run robotics (EP.356, closing question).\n- About 17: first job writing kernels at Xnor.ai, later acquired by Apple (EP.480, ~24:14; the transcript renders it \"Exnor\").\n- Harvard: Math 55 alumnus; Etched's site calls him a \"World math champion\" ([etched.com](https://www.etched.com/)). He was class of '25 per the Crimson and dropped out.\n- Gap year at OctoML (TVM compiler company), working as a digital nomad. The transformer-ASIC idea started then (EP.356, ~01:07:47). He developed TVM's Cortex-M backend ([etched.com](https://www.etched.com/)); apache/tvm is pinned on his [GitHub](https://github.com/guberti).\n- 2022: co-founded Etched with Chris Zhu and Robert Wachen ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/25/etched-is-building-an-ai-chip-that-only-runs-transformer-models/)). 2023-02: met Brian Schechter; 2023-03: Primary-led seed ([Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond)). 2024: Thiel Fellow (Crimson).\n- Age: described as 21 in Dec 2023 (EP.356 intro). In EP.480 the company is described as run by \"two, what, 24-year-olds now\".\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.etched.com/ | Company site with leadership bio and headshot; no personal site found (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Etched \"Progress\" posts are unsigned ([etched.com/progress](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one)) |\n| X | not verified | Handle guesses returned 402 or no results; not recorded |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/gavin-uberti-467738158/ | Linked from his own GitHub. A search also surfaced linkedin.com/in/guberti, which is not verified as his |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/guberti | \"Building hardware @ Etched\"; pins apache/tvm and a witness-encryption demo |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only, see below |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-12 — Invest Like the Best EP.356 — \"Real-Time AI & The Future of AI Hardware\" — [podscripts](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/gavin-uberti-real-time-ai-the-future-of-ai-hardware-invest-like-the-best-ep356) — model-specific chips; no fast takeoff; the Mark Ross recruiting chain; \"$5.5 million\" seed. Aired about six months before host Patrick O'Shaughnessy's Positive Sum co-led the A.\n- 2024-08 — TechCrunch Found — \"Etched founder Gavin Uberti thinks his company stands on the 'shoulders of giants'\" — [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/podcast/etched-founder-gavin-uberti-thinks-his-company-stands-on-the-shoulders-of-giants/) — claims an 18-month head start; Harvard dropout story.\n- 2026-06 — Invest Like the Best EP.480 — \"Etched - Building AI Hardware...\" with Robert Wachen — [podscripts](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/etched-building-ai-hardware-to-make-inference-faster-and-cheaper-invest-like-the-best-ep480) — the fundraising near-death, debt provider, Synopsys terms, bimodal hiring and HFT believers. Aired the day TechCrunch disclosed the $5B B.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** model-specific ASICs beat general accelerators for inference (EP.356). Inference is the battleground. Vertical integration: \"the best vendor is no vendor\", with Etched building chips, boards, cold plates, interconnect and rack (EP.480, ~27:06; Uberti answering the robotics-analogy question). Velocity: \"you win by shipping\" (same passage).\n- **Contrarian views:** no fast takeoff, and no future for ~100B-parameter models (EP.356). Kernels first, no compiler (EP.480).\n- **Calls right:** transformer dominance (2022 bet) and inference demand; the company reached $21B by 2026-08 ([Etched](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one)). **Calls wrong or pending:** the 2024 \"18-month head start\" claim against incumbents is untested. Mistakes admitted: nothing explicit found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. EP.356 transcript (2023): his own first-principles case, a year before anyone priced it.\n2. EP.480 transcript (2026): how the A was really assembled and how they hire.\n3. [Primary, \"Why we invested in Etched in every round\"](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round): the investor's view, including the OctoML reference \"Gavin is the best software engineer I've ever met\".\n4. [TechCrunch 2024-06-25](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/25/etched-is-building-an-ai-chip-that-only-runs-transformer-models/): Sohu launch claims.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X could not be read and no handle was verified. On GitHub he is public mainly through TVM.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Nous Research | Seed ($5.2M) | 2024-01-10 | no (aggregator says Distributed Global / OSS Capital led) | not confirmed | [CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/gavin-uberti) (aggregator; company name for this row from search snippet only) |\n| Augustus (global dollar bank) | Series B ($180M at $1B) | 2026-07-21 | no (Tiger Global led) | Hummingbird, QED, Variant, CMT Digital per press; Uberti not named in press | [CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/gavin-uberti) (aggregator); [round](https://fintech.global/2026/07/21/augustus-lands-180m-series-b-to-dollarise-the-world/) |\n\nBoth rows are aggregator-only and unconfirmed by primary sources.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Etched seed (his raise).** Mark Ross said $2–3M was needed to start. They raised $5.5M (Uberti, EP.356). Brian Schechter says he met Uberti the same day the WSJ ran his chip quote and led in March 2023 ([Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond)). Koko Xs was on the seed cap table with a personal check ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc)).\n- **Etched A.** They wrote a 30-page memo at the end of 2023, and every major investor passed. The round came together from $1–10M soft commits to about $103M, then a debt provider covered the tape-out (EP.480, Wachen-voiced). Primary and Positive Sum co-led, announced 2024-06-25 ([Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware)). O'Shaughnessy says his decision was made in 2023 (`people/patrick-oshaughnessy.md`).\n- **Later rounds** \"from those investors just doubling and tripling down\" (EP.480, ~01:05:50): Stripes B at $5B, Sequoia C at $10.3B, then the Jane Street-led $700M at $21B (see `companies/etched.md`).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Robert Wachen | Co-founder, President | 2022 | [etched.com](https://www.etched.com/) |\n| Chris Zhu | Co-founder | 2022 | [etched.com](https://www.etched.com/) |\n| Mark Ross | Early adviser, then chief hardware architect / CTO | pre-seed | EP.356; [etched.com](https://www.etched.com/) |\n| Brian Schechter (Primary) | Seed lead, board | 2023-02 | [Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond) |\n| Patrick O'Shaughnessy (Positive Sum) | Interviewer x2, A co-lead | 2023-12 | EP.356 / EP.480 |\n| Koko Xs | Seed angel | 2023 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc) |\n| Brian Loiler | Recruited ex-Nvidia HGX/DGX lead, VP Platform | ~2024 | EP.480; [etched.com](https://www.etched.com/) |\n| Ajat Hukkoo | VP Engineering (ex-Broadcom, Intel) | 2023 | EP.356; [etched.com](https://www.etched.com/) |\n| Sanford (surname unknown) | High-school FTC robotics partner; recruited to Etched platform work | high school | EP.480 |\n| Adarsh Hiremath (Mercor) | 2024 Thiel Fellowship classmate (Harvard) | 2024 | [Crimson](https://www.thecrimson.com/article/2024/3/27/thiel-fellowship-startups-2024/) |\n| Sonya Huang, Abhishek Malani (Sequoia) | Series C partners | 2026 | [Sequoia](https://sequoiacap.com/founder/gavin-uberti) |\n| OctoML / Xnor.ai | Former employers | ~2019–2022 | EP.356; EP.480; [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/25/etched-is-building-an-ai-chip-that-only-runs-transformer-models/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who are the two retired Nvidia rack leaders who declined to join but invested?\n- What explains \"$15 million in the bank\" in late 2023 against a ~$5.4M seed: an extension, SAFEs or debt? Who was the debt provider?\n- Which \"early investor\" told O'Shaughnessy the \"born too late to explore the world\" line (EP.356)?\n- Can the Nous Research and Augustus checks be confirmed from primary sources? Has he angel-invested in other chip or lab founders?\n- His X handle, and who he amplifies.\n\n## Sources\n- https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/gavin-uberti-real-time-ai-the-future-of-ai-hardware-invest-like-the-best-ep356\n- https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/etched-building-ai-hardware-to-make-inference-faster-and-cheaper-invest-like-the-best-ep480\n- https://www.etched.com/ ; https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one\n- https://github.com/guberti\n- https://www.thecrimson.com/article/2024/3/27/thiel-fellowship-startups-2024/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/25/etched-is-building-an-ai-chip-that-only-runs-transformer-models/\n- https://techcrunch.com/podcast/etched-founder-gavin-uberti-thinks-his-company-stands-on-the-shoulders-of-giants/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond ; https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round ; https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=662004\n- https://sequoiacap.com/founder/gavin-uberti\n- https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/gavin-uberti (aggregator)\n- https://fintech.global/2026/07/21/augustus-lands-180m-series-b-to-dollarise-the-world/\n", "person:george-mathew": "\n# George Mathew\n\n## One-line take\nA former Alteryx President/COO and Kespry CEO whom Jeff Horing hired in 2021 to take Insight down-market into Series A/B AI and data infrastructure. He did 26 deals in his first 14 months and now sometimes co-leads model-company pre-seeds (Fastino) and leads seeds (Runware) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7a0NsX-4FQY).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He is an operator with a data-first thesis. He backs the \"delightful tool for a new technical persona\" (W&B for ML practitioners, as Alteryx and Tableau were for analysts), and Insight's balance sheet lets him lead an A and keep funding through growth. He uses seed managers as a feeder network.\n- **What constrains him:** Insight's growth-equity DNA. He wants product-market-fit and NDR signals (he cites 140-200% NDR), he dislikes heavy structure early, and he admits he cannot spot the \"generational\" founder, so he screens for longevity instead.\n- **What he'd never do:** By his own rule, take more than a 1x liquidation preference on an early deal. He also says he would not issue a term sheet after a first meeting, though the Acceldata story shows he can mobilize within a day once a thesis is formed.\n- **Model-moat view:** He doesn't see the model itself as a moat; he sees proprietary data and workflow as the moat. That puts him on the application and infra side of frontier AI, not the lab side.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Insight's founder sent him down-market: Jeff Horing's idea was \"we should be going a little bit earlier into series a series b companies\". He then \"did 26 deals in the last 14 months\" (AI Council, 2022-04-14, 0:00:45) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7a0NsX-4FQY)\n- 2022 seed stance: Insight generally doesn't write seed checks; it tries to \"support incredible seed stage investment firms\" and take the A/B (MLOps Coffee #126, 2022-10-04, 0:30:27) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u0Dyb1lpfg0). By 2025: \"I occasionally will do seed investment\" (Boardroom Club, 0:08:25) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WzlFoTX7f34). The drift matches the Fastino pre-seed and Runware seed.\n- Term rule: \"I personally haven't done anything beyond the 1X liquidation\" preference on early deals (0:44:12) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u0Dyb1lpfg0)\n- He says \"we don't take first meetings and send off term sheets\" (0:46:28) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u0Dyb1lpfg0). Yet Acceldata's CEO recalls \"we spoke on a friday morning in may and then saturday morning\" Insight's whole team was there (2021-10-19) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UOnLupxfVCk)\n- W&B origin: he had \"spoken at conferences organized by Biewald during his time at CrowdFlower\" before leading the $45M B, his first Insight deal [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/weights-and-biases-ceo-lukas-biewald-on-building-an-ai-developer-powerhouse/)\n- Best portfolio companies \"now reaching 200 ndr 200 net dollar retention\" (2022, 0:35:48) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u0Dyb1lpfg0)\n- Jasper was \"near uh triple digit millions\" ARR less than two years in (May 2023, 0:14:35) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MikkYZMCdcI)\n- Insight recruited him from its own portfolio network: Insight was a growth partner to Alteryx, then \"Insight reached out to have me join as a venture capitalist\" while he was at Kespry (2024-03-04, 0:03:07) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UaYP5QHx1aQ)\n- On models: \"it's actually going to be the data itself\" that forms the moat, not the model (0:23:34) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UaYP5QHx1aQ)\n- Kespry operator-era pricing: \"the drone is a subscription it's part of the subscription\", and the drone is taken back on churn (Hardwired NYC 2017-12-19, 0:29:57) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WcTcfcdGDPM)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a frontier-lab or chip investor. His centre is Series A/B AI infrastructure, MLOps/LLMOps, data, and now agent infrastructure (CrewAI, HoneyHive, Tavily, Relevance AI). Three things matter for the owner:\n- He now co-leads model-company pre-seeds (Fastino, $7M with M12) and leads infra seeds (Runware, $13M).\n- He explicitly relies on seed managers to hand him founders, which makes him a natural Series A follow-on partner for the owner's seeds.\n- Insight can carry a company from A through growth (Anaconda C, Databricks J).\n\nOwnership discipline appears moderate: he avoids structure early and does not talk about ownership targets.\n\n## Background\n- B.S. from Cornell (computational biology per Alteryx 2011 release; neurobiology per later profiles), MBA from Duke [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/alteryx-names-analytics-executive-and-thought-leader-george-mathew-as-president-and-coo-125949388.html)\n- He was studying for the MCAT when the Mosaic browser pulled him into mid-90s e-commerce startups in California [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/SED1665_Insight_Partners.txt)\n- salesforce.com: early employee; director of technical account management, plus M&A and field services (dates undisclosed) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/01/11/industrial-drone-platform-kespry-brings-in-new-ceo/)\n- SAP: GM of Business Intelligence; led the SAP BusinessObjects Explorer in-memory analytics team [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/alteryx-names-analytics-executive-and-thought-leader-george-mathew-as-president-and-coo-125949388.html)\n- Alteryx: President & COO from 2011-07, scaling it toward its 2017 IPO, which he says came at under $100M run-rate [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WzlFoTX7f34)\n- Kespry: Chairman & CEO from 2017-01, succeeding founder Paul Doersch; ran an industrial drone plus deep-learning analytics company; raised a Series C with Shell and ABB [src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/01/11/industrial-drone-platform-kespry-brings-in-new-ceo/) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WcTcfcdGDPM)\n- Insight Partners: Managing Director from about 2021-02; first deal was leading the W&B Series B [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WzlFoTX7f34)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.insightpartners.com/team/george-mathew/ | Firm bio (2026-11-11) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/ | Co-authors Insight \"Ideas\" pieces (MLOps 2022, GenAI stack 2023, LLMOps 2023, AI security 2025); no personal blog found |\n| X | nothing found | Not checked against a confirmed handle |\n| LinkedIn | nothing confirmed | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Moderates Insight ScaleUp:AI panels and firesides |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2012-2014: theCUBE (Strata-Hadoop, BigDataNYC/SV, Tableau Conference) as Alteryx President/COO [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t0ymk_FGJ08)\n- 2015-06: Spark Summit fireside (Alteryx) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RuCyvkj43lo)\n- 2017-12: FirstMark Hardwired NYC: Kespry's drone-as-subscription model, Series C strategics [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WcTcfcdGDPM)\n- 2018-02: CXOTalk #277: revenue more than tripled after FAA Part 107 [src](https://www.cxotalk.com/episode/industrial-drones-cool-tech-business)\n- 2021-10: Acceldata Series B interview with Rohit Choudhary [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UOnLupxfVCk)\n- 2021-12: Abacus AI investor panel with Matt Turck and a Coatue partner [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jGZPAt2zzQo)\n- 2022-04: AI Council panel (Sequoia, Foundation Capital, Insight): Horing's mandate, 26 deals in 14 months [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7a0NsX-4FQY)\n- 2022-10: MLOps Coffee Sessions #126: stage, liquidation preferences, sourcing, NDR [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u0Dyb1lpfg0)\n- 2023-05: SuperDataScience #679: stage weightings, Jasper ARR, PLG plus enterprise flywheel [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MikkYZMCdcI)\n- 2024-03: Machine & Deep Learning Israel: 10x/100x rule, data as moat, Insight-Alteryx link [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UaYP5QHx1aQ)\n- 2024-04: Software Engineering Daily #1665 (recorded about Nov 2023): incumbents' advantage, founder archetype [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/2024/04/17/startup-investing-with-george-mathew/)\n- 2025-03: MLOps Podcast #297, \"From Rules to Reasoning Engines\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5_JlBK8TJ6U)\n- 2025-07: Boardroom Club AI Summit: stage rule, Databricks J, IPO bar [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WzlFoTX7f34)\n- Full catalogue (75 items): `data/rows/george-mathew-voice-media.csv`\n\n## How they think\n- **Data-first stack thesis:** modern data stack, then MLOps, then LLMOps, then GenAI apps, now agents. Every layer depends on reliable data [src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/SED1665_Insight_Partners.txt)\n- **Delight for a new persona:** the generational tool is the one practitioners can't live without (Alteryx, Tableau, W&B) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u0Dyb1lpfg0)\n- **Stage weights:** team at pre-seed, product and design partners at seed, traction at A, consolidation and scale at growth [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WzlFoTX7f34)\n- **Contrarian views:** the model is not the moat; small domain models will coexist with frontier models; incumbents with data and distribution are advantaged in GenAI [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UaYP5QHx1aQ)\n- **Calls:**\n  - Right: W&B, which grew ARR 20x after the B and was later acquired by CoreWeave.\n  - Partly right: in Oct 2022 he predicted a trillion-parameter LLM by mid-2023 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u0Dyb1lpfg0)\n  - Unclear: Jasper's later trajectory after his 2023 nine-figure-ARR comment (nothing sourced here).\n\n## Read these first\n- MLOps Coffee Sessions #126 (2022): the most candid deal mechanics on record (stage, 1x preference, sourcing, NDR) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u0Dyb1lpfg0)\n- AI Council data-infra panel (2022): how he got to Insight and his deal pace [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7a0NsX-4FQY)\n- How Lukas Biewald grew W&B (Insight, 2023): the origin of his first deal [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/weights-and-biases-ceo-lukas-biewald-on-building-an-ai-developer-powerhouse/)\n- Navigating the LLMops landscape (2023, co-authored) [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/llmops-mlops-what-you-need-to-know/)\n- Boardroom Club AI Summit (2025): current stage rule and his seed drift [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WzlFoTX7f34)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: no X handle was confirmed in this pass. His public voice runs through Insight Ideas posts, ScaleUp:AI moderation, and portfolio-company channels (Acceldata, Skyflow firesides).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Weights & Biases | Series B ($45M) | 2021-02 | yes | | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/weights-and-biases-raises-45m-series-b-to-expand-beyond-experiment-tracking-for-machine-learning-practitioners-everywhere/) |\n| Explorium | Series C ($75M) | 2021-05 | yes | | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/explorium-closes-75m-series-c-amid-soaring-demand-for-external-data/) |\n| Fiddler | Series B ($32M) | 2021-06 | yes | | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/fiddler-raises-32-million-in-series-b-funding-as-it-leads-the-market-of-machine-learning-explainability-and-performance-management/) |\n| Acceldata | Series B ($35M) | 2021-09 | yes | | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/acceldata-raises-35-million-series-b-to-deliver-the-worlds-first-enterprise-data-observability-cloud/) |\n| Landing AI | Undisclosed | 2021-11 | unknown | | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/landing-ai-secures-funding-to-unlock-power-of-small-datasets-unleashing-next-era-of-ai/) |\n| Deepdub | Series A ($20M) | 2022-02 | yes | | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/deepdub-raises-20-million-in-series-a-funding-led-by-insight-partners-to-bring-ai-based-dubbing-global/) |\n| Shoreline | Undisclosed | 2022-03 | unknown | | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/production-ops-finally-behind-the-investment-shoreline/) |\n| Fastino | Pre-seed ($7M) | 2024-11 | co-led | M12 (co-lead); NEA; CRV; Valor | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/fastino-emerges-from-stealth-with-task-optimized-llms-1000x-faster-than-leading-models-no-need-for-gpus/) |\n| HoneyHive | Undisclosed | 2025-04 | unknown | | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/behind-the-investment-honeyhive/) |\n| Anaconda | Series C (>$150M) | 2025-07 | yes | | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/anaconda-raises-over-150m-in-series-c-funding-to-power-ai-for-the-enterprise/) |\n| Tavily | Series A ($20M of $25M) | 2025-08 | co-led | Alpha Wave | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/tavily-raises-25-million-to-power-the-internet-of-agents/) |\n| Runware | Seed ($13M) | 2025-09 | yes | a16z Speedrun; Begin Capital; Zero Prime | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/runware-raises-13m-seed-to-help-customers-achieve-up-to-10x-cost-savings-on-ai-media-generation/) |\n| ExaCare AI | Series A ($30M) | 2025-10 | yes | Foundation Capital | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/exacare-ai-raises-30m-series-a-to-reimagine-admissions-and-launch-a-powerful-suite-of-ai-agents-for-skilled-nursing-home-care/) |\n| Databricks | Series J (Insight co-lead; his own account) | 2024-12 | co-led | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WzlFoTX7f34) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Weights & Biases (2021-02):** Mathew knew Lukas Biewald from speaking at CrowdFlower's conferences. W&B was fielding inbound interest; they \"hit it off\". He saw it as the first delightful product for the ML-practitioner persona, and Insight led the $45M B before commercialization really started. He says ARR grew 20x after the round and that customers included OpenAI, Anthropic, Meta and Nvidia [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/weights-and-biases-ceo-lukas-biewald-on-building-an-ai-developer-powerhouse/) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WzlFoTX7f34)\n- **Acceldata (2021):** The first call was on a Friday morning in May, and Insight's whole team was there on Saturday. Mathew pitched it as the only multi-dimensional data observability platform among point tools. Insight led the $35M B [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UOnLupxfVCk)\n- **Metabase:** He backed it on open-source signals (downloads, pull requests, contributors) ahead of commercialization, which then worked [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u0Dyb1lpfg0)\n- **Jasper (2022):** He saw it as a prosumer product, a \"10x mech suit\" for content writers built on GPT-3. It was near nine-figure ARR by May 2023 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MikkYZMCdcI)\n- **His own move to Insight:** Insight had been a growth partner to Alteryx while he was COO. Jeff Horing then recruited him from Kespry to lead Insight's push into Series A/B [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UaYP5QHx1aQ) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7a0NsX-4FQY)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jeff Horing (Insight) | Recruited him; set the earlier-stage mandate; co-deal-team | 2021 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7a0NsX-4FQY) |\n| Lukas Biewald (W&B) | Knew him from CrowdFlower conferences; first Insight deal | pre-2021 | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/weights-and-biases-ceo-lukas-biewald-on-building-an-ai-developer-powerhouse/) |\n| Lonne Jaffe, Teddie Wardi, Sophie Beshar (Insight) | Co-authors and co-deal-team on the MLOps/LLMOps thesis | 2021 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u0Dyb1lpfg0) |\n| Rohit Choudhary (Acceldata) | Board; led Series B | 2021 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UOnLupxfVCk) |\n| Matt Turck (FirstMark) | Hosted him at Hardwired (2017); co-panelist (2021) | 2017 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WcTcfcdGDPM) |\n| Dean Stoecker (Alteryx) | Former boss (CEO) | 2011 | [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/alteryx-names-analytics-executive-and-thought-leader-george-mathew-as-president-and-coo-125949388.html) |\n| Paul Doersch (Kespry) | Succeeded the founder as CEO | 2017 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/01/11/industrial-drone-platform-kespry-brings-in-new-ceo/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact Alteryx departure month and Insight start date (aggregator says Feb 2021).\n- Who introduced Fastino and Runware, and what size the Fastino pre-seed check was.\n- His Kespry investors (Lightspeed, Cisco Investments, the Series C strategics): do any later co-invest with him?\n- The MLOps #297 (2025) and UNPAK3D (2025) episodes and the 2024 ScaleUp:AI sessions are unmined; UNPAK3D has a \"lessons from failure\" chapter.\n- His X handle has not been identified.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7a0NsX-4FQY\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u0Dyb1lpfg0\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MikkYZMCdcI\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UOnLupxfVCk\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UaYP5QHx1aQ\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WzlFoTX7f34\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WcTcfcdGDPM\n- https://www.cxotalk.com/episode/industrial-drones-cool-tech-business\n- https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/SED1665_Insight_Partners.txt\n- https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/weights-and-biases-ceo-lukas-biewald-on-building-an-ai-developer-powerhouse/\n- https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/alteryx-names-analytics-executive-and-thought-leader-george-mathew-as-president-and-coo-125949388.html\n- https://techcrunch.com/2017/01/11/industrial-drone-platform-kespry-brings-in-new-ceo/\n- https://www.insightpartners.com/team/george-mathew/\n- https://vcsheet.com/who/george-mathew (aggregator)\n", "person:georges-harik": "\n# Georges Harik\n\n\n## One-line take\nAn individual who co-leads institution-sized AI rounds out of his own operator network (imo alumni, Google peers, research co-authors), and the anchor around whom SV Angel, Felicis and the Google-alumni angels cluster.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he co-leads where he has built something himself. humans& is his own lab, which he co-founded and co-led ([src](https://humansand.ai/)). Deep Infra was founded by the team that ran imo's infrastructure, and he co-founded imo ([src](https://deepinfra.com/about)). He doesn't source cold. His deals come from people he has managed, employed or written papers with.\n- **What constrains him:** no fund, team or LP base was found. His capital is personal (Google-era wealth plus imo), so his deal flow is limited to his own network. He is also now an operator at humans&, which competes for his time.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred, unconfirmed):** nothing on record shows him taking a passive small allocation in a consensus round late. Every AI deal dated here is either first-in (founder or angel) or a co-lead.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Deep Infra is an imo spinout, and he doubled down.** Deep Infra says \"We previously built and operated the infrastructure behind imo messenger\" ([src](https://deepinfra.com/about)). Its advisor roster lists him as \"Co-founder @imo.im\" ([src](https://deepinfra.com/about)). He went from advisor/angel to co-lead of the $107M Series B: \"The round is co-led by 500 Global and Georges Harik\" ([src](https://deepinfra.com/series-b), 2026-05-04). The repo had this down as an unusual angel-as-lead deal. It is really an operator backing his former infrastructure team.\n- **The imo company is PageBites, Inc.** imo's developer is listed as Pagebites ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imo.im), secondary). The PageBites Form D (filed 2013-05-09) lists Georges Harik as Director and Ralph Harik as President & CEO, at a Palo Alto address ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1500172/000150017213000004/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **humans& was built from a research collaboration two years earlier.** Quiet-STaR (arXiv, March 2024) lists Harik with the affiliation \"Notbad AI Inc\", co-authoring with Eric Zelikman and Noah Goodman, both later humans& co-founders ([src](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.09629v2)). Aydin Senkut points to the same paper: \"When he co-authored Quiet-STaR with Eric Zelikman\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/humansand-announcement)). Notbad AI ships open reasoning-model weights (Notbad v1.1 Mistral 24B) ([src](https://notbad.ai/)).\n- **There is a reciprocal angel loop.** Deep Infra CEO Nikola Borisov, listed as \"Nikola Petrov Borisov\", is an individual investor in humans&. So are James Hong and Mitesh Agrawal, who sit next to Harik on Deep Infra's advisor/angel roster ([src](https://humansand.ai/), [src](https://deepinfra.com/about)). SV Angel, his humans& co-lead, is also a Deep Infra investor (its logo is on the about page) ([src](https://deepinfra.com/about)).\n- **He has angeled for at least 17 years.** In 2009 TechCrunch wrote \"He's also an active angel investor\" and noted that he backed VentureBeat. It also named FriendFeed's Paul Buchheit and Sanjeev Singh as imo advisors ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20210108060102/https://social.techcrunch.com/2009/02/06/imoim-is-the-best-im-web-service-youve-never-heard-of/)).\n- **He has quiet non-AI stakes that only filings show.** He is named as an investor on DiCE Molecules' investor rights agreement (2021-08-20, filed with the S-1/A) ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1645569/000119312521259250/d134346dex42.htm)) and on Eargo's (2018-12-19) ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1719395/000095012319010430/filename4.htm)).\n- **The Senkut link explains Felicis's access.** Senkut writes that Harik was his engineering manager at Silicon Graphics, and that he followed Harik to Google ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/humansand-announcement)). Felicis sits beside Harik in humans& (seed), Deep Infra (B) and Diffbot (A, 2016) ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=267415)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He co-led a frontier-lab mega-seed (humans&, $480M at $4.48B) and an inference-compute round (Deep Infra B), which is the owner's core territory and check range. He is a co-lead and gatekeeper, not a fund that would LP-back or co-invest on the owner's terms. The useful angles are (a) access to humans& follow-ons and to whatever comes out of Notbad AI or imo alumni next, and (b) mapping the Harik, SV Angel and Felicis triangle that recurs across these deals. He is not an LP prospect: no fund commitments were found.\n\n## Background\n- Silicon Graphics: engineering manager; he managed Aydin Senkut about three decades before 2026 ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/humansand-announcement)). Years unconfirmed.\n- Google: seventh employee, who \"helped build its first advertising systems\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/20/humans-a-human-centric-ai-startup-founded-by-anthropic-xai-google-alums-raised-480m-seed-round/)). He managed several early products ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20210108060102/https://social.techcrunch.com/2009/02/06/imoim-is-the-best-im-web-service-youve-never-heard-of/)). Wikipedia (secondary) calls him Gmail's product management director around April 2005 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gmail)). His start and leave dates are unconfirmed.\n- imo.im (PageBites, Inc.): co-founder ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20210108060102/https://social.techcrunch.com/2009/02/06/imoim-is-the-best-im-web-service-youve-never-heard-of/)), and a director on the 2013 Form D ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1500172/000150017213000004/primary_doc.xml)). imo launched in 2007 (Wikipedia, secondary).\n- Notbad AI Inc: his affiliation on Quiet-STaR in March 2024 ([src](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.09629v2)).\n- humans&: co-founder; the lab launched 2026-01-20 ([src](https://humansand.ai/)).\n- Education: not found in the sources checked.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | harik.org and georgesharik.com returned no content (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing verified | Only @notbadai (company) found, linked from https://notbad.ai/ |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | |\n| GitHub | not verified | Notbad AI weights are on Hugging Face: https://huggingface.co/notbadai/notbad_v1_1_mistral_24b |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n| Company pages | https://humansand.ai/ ; https://notbad.ai/ ; https://deepinfra.com/about | Verified 2026-11-10 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was rate-limited this run, so podcasts, YouTube and X were not checked. This is a gap, not evidence of absence.\n\n## How they think\n- **Research-led theses:** reasoning before speaking (Quiet-STaR), then long-horizon multi-agent RL at humans& ([src](https://humansand.ai/)). Notbad AI's stated aim is \"algorithms for generalized reasoning training\" ([src](https://notbad.ai/)).\n- **Infrastructure ownership:** he backs Deep Infra, whose thesis is that inference, not training, will dominate enterprise workloads ([src](https://deepinfra.com/series-b)). That is the company's view, not his own words.\n- **Contrarian views and calls:** no first-person statements found.\n\n## Read these first\n- Quiet-STaR paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.09629. The research thread that became humans&.\n- humans& launch page: https://humansand.ai/. The lab's thesis and full investor list.\n- Aydin Senkut's humans& post: https://www.felicis.com/blog/humansand-announcement. The SGI and Google relationship.\n- Deep Infra about page: https://deepinfra.com/about. The imo lineage and the advisor/angel roster.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found (no personal X or blog was verified; search was rate-limited).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| humans& | Seed ($480M at $4.48B) | 2026-01-20 | Yes, co-lead with SV Angel (and co-founder) | NVIDIA, Jeff Bezos, GV, Emerson, Forerunner, S32, DCVC, Felicis, CRV, Conviction, Bloomberg Beta | [src](https://humansand.ai/) |\n| Deep Infra | Series B ($107M) | 2026-05-04 | Yes, co-lead with 500 Global; already an advisor/angel (earlier entry date unconfirmed) | A.Capital, Crescent Cove, Felicis, NVIDIA, Peak6, Samsung Next, Supermicro, Upper90 | [src](https://deepinfra.com/series-b) |\n| Diffbot | Series A ($10M) | 2016-02-11 | No (Tencent led) | Felicis, Valor, Amplify, Andy Bechtolsheim, Bill Lee | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=267415) |\n| DiCE Molecules | Undisclosed (holder as of 2021-08-20) | 2021-08-20 | No | Alexandria, Janus Henderson, Deep Track, Northpond | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1645569/000119312521259250/d134346dex42.htm) |\n| Eargo | Undisclosed (holder as of 2018-12-19) | 2018-12-19 | No | Birchmere, StartX, Schwab trust, Henry Kravis | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1719395/000095012319010430/filename4.htm) |\n| VentureBeat | Undisclosed (angel) | before 2009-02 | No | not stated | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20210108060102/https://social.techcrunch.com/2009/02/06/imoim-is-the-best-im-web-service-youve-never-heard-of/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **humans& (seed, 2026-01):** he was first in by construction, as a co-founder. The founding core came out of research collaboration: Zelikman and Goodman co-authored Quiet-STaR with him in 2024 ([src](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.09629)). SV Angel co-led. Senkut's Felicis came in on a 30-year personal tie ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/humansand-announcement)). Google-alumni angels (Bill Maris, Marissa Mayer, Anne Wojcicki, Zak Stone) and Deep Infra people (Borisov, Hong, Agrawal) fill out the individual list ([src](https://humansand.ai/)). The split between his check and SV Angel's is not disclosed.\n- **Deep Infra (B, 2026-05):** the founders (Borisov, Papoutsis, Kanapin) ran imo's infrastructure ([src](https://deepinfra.com/about)), and he co-founded imo. He was an advisor/angel before the B and then co-led it. The prior round's Form D (first sale 2024-11-22, $20.6M of $29.0M sold, 17 investors) names no investors, so whether he was in that round is unconfirmed ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2047472/000204747224000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ralph Harik | imo co-founder (PageBites CEO); co-investor in humans& and Deep Infra; kinship not stated in any source | by 2009 | [src](https://deepinfra.com/about) |\n| Aydin Senkut (Felicis) | Former report at SGI; followed him to Google; co-investor | 1990s | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/humansand-announcement) |\n| Eric Zelikman, Noah Goodman | Quiet-STaR co-authors, then humans& co-founders | 2024-03 | [src](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.09629) |\n| Varuna Jayasiri | Notbad AI colleague (labml.ai) | 2024-03 | [src](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.09629v2) |\n| Nikola Borisov (Deep Infra) | Former imo infra team; Harik backs Deep Infra; Borisov invests in humans& | unconfirmed | [src](https://deepinfra.com/about) |\n| SV Angel | humans& seed co-lead; also a Deep Infra investor | 2026-01 | [src](https://humansand.ai/) |\n| 500 Global | Deep Infra B co-lead | 2026-05 | [src](https://deepinfra.com/series-b) |\n| Paul Buchheit, Sanjeev Singh | imo advisors (2009) | 2009 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20210108060102/https://social.techcrunch.com/2009/02/06/imoim-is-the-best-im-web-service-youve-never-heard-of/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What were his check sizes in humans& and Deep Infra B, and how was the lead split with SV Angel and 500 Global?\n- When did he first invest in Deep Infra (seed, Series A or angel)?\n- Who funds Notbad AI, and did it fold into humans&?\n- Is Ralph Harik a relative? No source checked says so.\n- What other AI angel checks (2020–2026) has he made? Only those on company pages and EDGAR were found.\n- Is there a personal vehicle (family office or LLC)? None found on EDGAR.\n- Note: EDGAR also returns unrelated Hariks (Daniil Harik at Cognuse; Mario Harik at QXO). Don't conflate them.\n\n## Sources\n- https://humansand.ai/\n- https://boba.humansand.ai/blog/\n- https://deepinfra.com/series-b\n- https://deepinfra.com/about\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/humansand-announcement\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/20/humans-a-human-centric-ai-startup-founded-by-anthropic-xai-google-alums-raised-480m-seed-round/\n- https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.09629 ; https://arxiv.org/html/2403.09629v2\n- https://notbad.ai/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20210108060102/https://social.techcrunch.com/2009/02/06/imoim-is-the-best-im-web-service-youve-never-heard-of/\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imo.im (secondary)\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gmail (secondary)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1500172/000150017213000004/primary_doc.xml (PageBites Form D)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1645569/000119312521259250/d134346dex42.htm (DiCE IRA)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1719395/000095012319010430/filename4.htm (Eargo IRA)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2047472/000204747224000001/primary_doc.xml (Deep Infra Form D 2024)\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=267415\n- Photo: none. Checked deepinfra.com/about (name card only, no image), humansand.ai, notbad.ai, harik.org and georgesharik.com.\n", "person:georgia-stevenson": "\n# Georgia Stevenson\n\n## One-line take\nShe is a London partner at Index who led a researcher-founded foundation-model seed (Mirelo) in her own name. She also sits next to Danny Rimer on both of Index's 2026 European new-lab bets, Ineffable and Inherent. That makes her the most likely junior-to-mid point of entry into Index's European lab deal flow. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/musicians-turned-ai-researchers-at-mirelo-raise-41m-to-unmute-videos-games-and-beyond/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/georgia-stevenson/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** a consistent, written thesis about AI that makes people more capable, not redundant (Granola, Marker, Mirelo). It comes with an operator's grounding from early Deliveroo, and she sits beside Danny Rimer on almost every deal she touches. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/georgia-stevenson/)\n- **What constrains her:** on the two labs, the bylines put Rimer in front. He is the sole author on Ineffable, and the Inherent post is co-written. On Ineffable, Index was also a late participant in a Sequoia and Lightspeed round, not the price-setter. Her own confirmed lead is on the product and modality side (audio). [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-superlearner-investing-in-david-silver-and-ineffable-intelligence/)\n- **What she'd likely never do:** back \"replace the human\" automation for its own sake. Her posts argue against it more than once (see below). This is inferred from her writing and labelled as such.\n\n**Correction to the premise:** the brief said \"no title\" and treated her as unconfirmed junior. Index's own Mirelo page bylines her \"Partner at Index Ventures\" and calls her \"the partner at Index Ventures who led the investment\". The Marker post bylines her \"Index Partner\". [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/musicians-turned-ai-researchers-at-mirelo-raise-41m-to-unmute-videos-games-and-beyond/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/writing-friction-with-marker/)\n\n## Differential insight\nEvery item here comes from Index's own site, because web search was exhausted for this run. Nothing here is \"hidden\", but the synthesis isn't in press.\n- **She led Mirelo personally.** Index calls her \"the partner at Index Ventures who led the investment\". That was a $41M seed, co-led with a16z, on 2025-12-15. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/musicians-turned-ai-researchers-at-mirelo-raise-41m-to-unmute-videos-games-and-beyond/)\n- **Mirelo's angel bench is a network tell.** The named angels are Arthur Mensch, Thomas Wolf, Jakob Uszkoreit, Léon Bottou, Thomas Hofmann and six others. That is a European ML-research crowd in a seed she led. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/musicians-turned-ai-researchers-at-mirelo-raise-41m-to-unmute-videos-games-and-beyond/)\n- **Her anti-replacement thesis**, from Marker: \"AI can make you a better writer, not a redundant one\". [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/writing-friction-with-marker/)\n- **A contrarian line in favour of friction**, from Marker: \"outsourcing your thinking drives the cerebral equivalent of backache\". [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/writing-friction-with-marker/)\n- **Granola is framed as a capture layer, not a productivity tool:** \"meetings are often where ideas become companies\". [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/granola-investing-in-what-makes-us-human/)\n- **Inherent thesis** (co-written with Rimer): the team is \"obsessed with organizational design as a research problem in its own right\". [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inherent-designing-for-discovery/)\n- **Timing on Ineffable:** Index was not first in. Flying Fish wrote the first check and Sequoia and Lightspeed co-led. [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/exclusive-ineffable-intelligence-adds-six-103555324.html) Index admits no prior relationship: \"We've known David by reputation for years.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-superlearner-investing-in-david-silver-and-ineffable-intelligence/)\n- **Timing on Inherent:** Index co-led the $50M seed with Radical (2026-05-31). I found no earlier check by anyone. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inherent-designing-for-discovery/)\n- **Pairing pattern:** Rimer is co-listed with her on Granola, Ineffable, Inherent, Marker, Sourceful and Empathy. Mirelo lists only her. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/mirelo/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/marker/)\n\nMirrored in `data/rows/georgia-stevenson-insights.csv`.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High**, with a counterweight. She is on two new-lab seeds that matter to THESIS.md: Ineffable (a $1.1B seed) and Inherent (a $50M seed of DeepMind alumni). She also personally led a researcher-founded foundation-model seed (Mirelo), and she isn't yet a press name. That is exactly the \"rising junior doing hot deals\" profile. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/inherent/)\n\nThe counterweight: her bio puts \"AI-native products\" first, and her one confirmed lead is a modality model, not a frontier lab. Much of her listed portfolio is consumer or marketplace and far from the thesis: Bloom & Wild, Motorway, Linktree, GetHarley, Kindred. On the labs, Rimer is visibly the senior name. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/georgia-stevenson/)\n\nDeals that matter to me: Inherent, Ineffable, Mirelo, then Granola and Marker (AI-native products).\n\n## Background\n- History degree from St John's College, Cambridge (dates not given). [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/georgia-stevenson/)\n- Early Deliveroo: door-to-door restaurant sales and launching towns and cities outside London (dates not given). [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/georgia-stevenson/)\n- Index Ventures, London. Her earliest dated byline I found is Valdera, 2024-10-03. Her join date is not found. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/valdera-secures-15-million-to-help-manufacturers-make-the-next-generation-of-products-faster-safer-and-more-sustainably-than-ever-before/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/georgia-stevenson/ | Index bio; lists 14 companies (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | Index Perspectives (via company pages) | Roughly one post per deal; 2024-10, 2025-12, 2026-03, 2026-05, 2026-07 |\n| X | nothing found | Not searched (search budget exhausted) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/georgia-stevenson-94677982/ | Linked from her Index page; not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was exhausted this run, so this is unverified rather than confirmed absent.\n\n## How they think\n- **Main thesis:** AI should amplify human judgment, feeling and creativity. Granola is \"investing in what makes us human\"; Marker is the right kind of friction; Mirelo is sound as emotional power. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/granola-investing-in-what-makes-us-human/)\n- **Research companies:** on labs she writes alongside Rimer. Inherent is backed for self-improving AI for scientific discovery and for treating organizational design as research. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inherent-designing-for-discovery/)\n- **Neglected modalities:** in the Mirelo post she argues sound is an afterthought in generative video. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/musicians-turned-ai-researchers-at-mirelo-raise-41m-to-unmute-videos-games-and-beyond/)\n- **Ethics as a diligence lens:** she flags ethical training data at Mirelo, and governance and second-order effects at Inherent.\n- **Calls right or wrong:** it is too early to say. Granola went to an Index-led $125M Series C. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/granola-investing-in-what-makes-us-human/)\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Writing Friction with Marker](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/writing-friction-with-marker/) (2026-07-09): her clearest statement of the human-augmenting thesis.\n2. [Inherent: Designing for Discovery](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inherent-designing-for-discovery/) (2026-05-31): how she and Rimer underwrite a new lab.\n3. [Mirelo $41M seed](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/musicians-turned-ai-researchers-at-mirelo-raise-41m-to-unmute-videos-games-and-beyond/) (2025-12-15): her own lead on a foundation-model seed, plus the angel list.\n4. [Granola: Investing in What Makes Us Human](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/granola-investing-in-what-makes-us-human/) (2026-03-25): her product taste.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X and LinkedIn were not read this run.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mirelo | Seed $41M | 2025-12-15 | Yes, she led (Index co-led with a16z) | a16z; angels incl. Mensch, Wolf, Uszkoreit | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/musicians-turned-ai-researchers-at-mirelo-raise-41m-to-unmute-videos-games-and-beyond/) |\n| Inherent | Seed $50M | 2026-05-31 | Index co-led; her role not stated | Radical Ventures | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inherent-designing-for-discovery/) |\n| Ineffable Intelligence | Seed $1.1B at $5.1B | 2026-04-27 | No (participant) | Sequoia, Lightspeed (co-leads); Nvidia; Google; DST | [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/27/deepmind-ineffable-intelligence-record-seed-funding-nvidia-google.html) |\n| Granola | Series C $125M | 2026-03-25 | Index led; her role not stated | not named | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/granola-investing-in-what-makes-us-human/) |\n| Marker | Seed (undisclosed) | 2026-07-09 | Index led; her role not stated | not named | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/writing-friction-with-marker/) |\n| Valdera | Series A $15M | 2024-10-03 | Index led; co-wrote with Jan Hammer | Consumer-goods executive angels | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/valdera-secures-15-million-to-help-manufacturers-make-the-next-generation-of-products-faster-safer-and-more-sustainably-than-ever-before/) |\n| Sourceful | Series A $20M | not read | not confirmed | not read | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/sourceful/) |\n| Empathy | not read | not read | not confirmed | not read | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/empathy/) |\n| Bloom & Wild, GetHarley, Kindred, Linktree, Motorway, Plain | not read | not read | not confirmed | not read | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/georgia-stevenson/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Mirelo:** she is credited with leading the $41M seed, co-led with a16z. The founders are musician-researchers (ex-Amazon, Google Brain, Max Planck and ETH). The intro path was not found. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/musicians-turned-ai-researchers-at-mirelo-raise-41m-to-unmute-videos-games-and-beyond/)\n- **Inherent:** this is a London public benefit corporation lab of DeepMind alumni (Collins, Hughes, Kirsch) plus Aleksiev, building \"Faraday\". Index co-led the $50M seed with Radical, and Matt Clifford is an advisor. The intro path is not stated. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inherent-designing-for-discovery/)\n- **Ineffable:** Index joined the record European seed as a participant, with a Rimer byline. Index says it knew Silver \"by reputation\", so it was not a relationship-led entry. Flying Fish had courted Silver since early 2024. [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/exclusive-ineffable-intelligence-adds-six-103555324.html)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Danny Rimer (Index) | Recurring deal partner on 6 of her listed companies | at least 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/inherent/) |\n| Jan Hammer (Index) | Co-author on Valdera | 2024-10 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/valdera-secures-15-million-to-help-manufacturers-make-the-next-generation-of-products-faster-safer-and-more-sustainably-than-ever-before/) |\n| Andreessen Horowitz | Co-lead on Mirelo | 2025-12 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/musicians-turned-ai-researchers-at-mirelo-raise-41m-to-unmute-videos-games-and-beyond/) |\n| Radical Ventures | Co-lead on Inherent | 2026-05 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inherent-designing-for-discovery/) |\n| David Silver | Index deal team on Ineffable; \"partners closely\" per bio | 2026-04 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/georgia-stevenson/) |\n| Deliveroo | Early employee | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/georgia-stevenson/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who sourced Inherent: her, Rimer, or Radical? Was there a pre-seed?\n- Does she hold board seats on Mirelo or Inherent?\n- When did she join Index, and when did she make Partner?\n- Did Index invest in Granola before the Series C?\n- Has she appeared on any podcast or posted on X? Search was exhausted this run, so this is unverified.\n- Which Index partner led Index's position in Flapping Airplanes, and was she involved?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/georgia-stevenson/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/musicians-turned-ai-researchers-at-mirelo-raise-41m-to-unmute-videos-games-and-beyond/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inherent-designing-for-discovery/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-superlearner-investing-in-david-silver-and-ineffable-intelligence/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/granola-investing-in-what-makes-us-human/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/writing-friction-with-marker/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/valdera-secures-15-million-to-help-manufacturers-make-the-next-generation-of-products-faster-safer-and-more-sustainably-than-ever-before/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/mirelo/ ; /companies/inherent/ ; /companies/ineffable/ ; /companies/granola/ ; /companies/marker/ ; /companies/sourceful/ ; /companies/empathy/ ; /companies/valdera/\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/27/deepmind-ineffable-intelligence-record-seed-funding-nvidia-google.html (round terms, via repo company card)\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/exclusive-ineffable-intelligence-adds-six-103555324.html (Flying Fish first check, via repo company card)\n", "person:gerard-bernabeu-altayo": "\n# Gerard Bernabeu Altayo\n\n*Written 2026-11-11 by the hft-semis sweep. Identity: the HRT Compute Systems Lead, the GTC 2026 speaker and the Fermilab/PIC engineer are linked by the same full name in a narrow field (HPC infrastructure). GitHub user `gerardba` (\"Gerard Bernabeu\", Fermilab) and the INSPIRE-HEP author record (PIC, then Fermilab) are the same person. Search snippets show a LinkedIn profile titled \"Gerard Bernabeu Altayó - Hudson River Trading\", which ties the lab career to HRT. That page was not fetched, so the link is strong but rests on a search snippet.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the hands-on evaluator who decides which GPU cloud HRT runs on. A chip or cloud founder has to win him before HRT's ventures desk ever matters, and he is quoted on two of HRT's three named cloud deals.\n\n## Crux\n- **Background:** a career infrastructure operator from physics labs. He ran storage and grid at the Spanish WLCG Tier-1 (PIC), then storage, private cloud and HPC at Fermilab, before running compute at HRT. He is not a quant and not an investor.\n- **What he weighs:** in public he talks about iteration speed, technical depth, operations, and power and cooling, never price. The deals he fronts buy capacity that already exists: spot and dynamically scheduled GPUs on Google Cloud, and Blackwell systems Lambda had already installed.\n- **What constrains him:** he owns infrastructure, not capital. No source puts him on HRT's Investment Committee, and he has no named role on any equity deal.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He is the voice on HRT's GPU cloud buying across two years and two vendors, not only Lambda.** He was quoted as \"compute lead\" when HRT and Google Cloud announced their 2024 collaboration: \"Google Cloud provides an efficient, flexible platform to rapidly iterate on ideas\" ([Traders Magazine, 2024-07-19](https://www.tradersmagazine.com/xtra/google-cloud-hudson-river-trading-partner-on-automated-trading/)). By 2026 his title was Compute Systems Lead ([Lambda, 2026-05-20](https://lambda.com/blog/lambda-partners-with-hudson-river-trading-to-power-quantitative-research-and-development)). Kevin Lee (Head of R&D) fronted the larger CoreWeave contract instead ([CoreWeave IR](https://investors.coreweave.com/news/news-details/2026/Hudson-River-Trading-to-Build-Next-Gen-Research-Platform-Powered-by-NVIDIA-Vera-Rubin-NVL72-on-CoreWeave-Cloud/default.aspx)). That suggests a split: Lee for strategic multi-year platforms, Bernabeu Altayo for the systems choice. The split is an inference.\n- **The Lambda deal was speed-to-capacity, not a new build.** Lambda's CTO told Reuters it used \"Nvidia systems that Lambda has already purchased and installed in a data center\" ([Reuters via The Star, 2026-05-20](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/05/20/lambda-wins-cloud-deal-with-hudson-river-trading-to-supply-access-to-nvidia-chips)). Reuters also says the deal covers renting \"more than 1,000\" Blackwell systems (same source). Two months earlier he gave an NVIDIA GTC talk, \"The Blueprint for a Modern Resource-Responsible AI Factory\" ([NVIDIA, S82132, 2026-03-17](https://www.nvidia.com/gtc/session-catalog/sessions/gtc26-s82132/)), filed under Data Center / Cloud. So his public framing is about power and cooling limits, which is the pitch a power-efficient accelerator founder should lead with.\n- **He came from physics-lab storage and grid work, not from finance.** He was lead author of a CHEP 2010 paper on http/WebDAV data access at PIC, with a pic.es address ([J. Phys. Conf. Ser. 331](https://indico.in2p3.fr/event/7291/contributions/41319/attachments/33249/40953/1742-6596_331_7_072003.pdf)). A 2017 PIC paper footnotes him as \"Currently at FNAL, Chicago, USA\" ([PoS ISGC2017](https://pos.sissa.it/293/011/pdf)). At Fermilab he co-wrote work on SR-IOV InfiniBand virtualization for HPC on FermiCloud ([FERMILAB-CONF-14-470](http://lss.fnal.gov/archive/2014/conf/fermilab-conf-14-470-cd.pdf)). He presented Fermilab's 110+ PB storage estate at HEPiX in 2015 ([Indico](https://indico.cern.ch/event/384358/contributions/909222/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium, as a reference and channel, not as a co-investor.** He writes no checks and will never lead or join a seed. For a $20M frontier-AI seed in chips, inference systems or GPU cloud, his evaluation is what turns HRT from a logo into a customer, and at HRT customer status comes before an investment (Etched was tested, then invested in, per [Reuters via Investing.com](https://uk.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/highfrequency-traders-race-to-build-ai-computing-power-93CH-4927634)). The deals that matter: Lambda (2026-05) and Google Cloud (2024-07) as a customer. Indirectly, HRT's chip bets (Etched, Positron, OLIX, Eridu) are the kind of hardware his team would evaluate, but no source names him on any of them.\n\n## Background\n- **by 2010 to about 2013: Port d'Informació Científica (PIC), Barcelona**, the Spanish WLCG Tier-1. Storage and grid engineer: dCache, WebDAV and Nagios monitoring. Sources: [CHEP 2010 paper](https://indico.in2p3.fr/event/7291/contributions/41319/attachments/33249/40953/1742-6596_331_7_072003.pdf) and [dCache workshop 2012 slides](https://www.dcache.org/manuals/2012/workshop/slides/gerard-nagios-probes.pdf) (PIC email per search snippet). Exact dates unconfirmed.\n- **by 2014-11 to at least 2017: Fermilab Scientific Computing Division.** He worked on FermiCloud (OpenNebula, InfiniBand SR-IOV; [paper](http://lss.fnal.gov/archive/2014/conf/fermilab-conf-14-470-cd.pdf)) and scientific storage ([HEPiX Fall 2015](https://indico.cern.ch/event/384358/contributions/909222/)). He co-authored FEL simulation on distributed computing ([IPAC2016](https://www.osti.gov/biblio/1329064)). A USQCD All-Hands 2017 deck under his name is a Fermilab facilities report (title per search result `usqcd.org/meetings/allHands2017/slides/Bernabeu.pdf`; the file did not render). His GitHub profile lists Fermilab ([github.com/gerardba](https://github.com/gerardba)). The end date is unconfirmed.\n- **by 2024-07: Hudson River Trading.** \"Compute lead\" ([Traders Magazine](https://www.tradersmagazine.com/xtra/google-cloud-hudson-river-trading-partner-on-automated-trading/)), then \"Compute Systems Lead\" ([NVIDIA GTC 2026](https://www.nvidia.com/gtc/session-catalog/sessions/gtc26-s82132/); [Lambda](https://lambda.com/blog/lambda-partners-with-hudson-river-trading-to-power-quantitative-research-and-development)). His HRT start date is unconfirmed.\n- **Education:** nothing found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/gerard-bernabeu-altay%C3%B3-362a4323/ | Seen only as a search result titled \"Gerard Bernabeu Altayó - Hudson River Trading\" (2026-11-11). Not fetched |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/gerardba | 5 public repos (dCache probes, puppet modules), last active about 2015; still lists Fermilab (checked via API 2026-11-11) |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n| Talks | https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/on-demand/session/gtc26-s82132/ | NVIDIA GTC 2026 on-demand session S82132 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-03-17: NVIDIA GTC San Jose, \"The Blueprint for a Modern Resource-Responsible AI Factory\" (S82132), Data Center / Cloud track. [Catalog](https://www.nvidia.com/gtc/session-catalog/sessions/gtc26-s82132/), [on-demand](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/on-demand/session/gtc26-s82132/). Only the abstract stub was readable; the recording was not watched.\n- 2017-04: USQCD All-Hands, Fermilab facilities report. Title from a search result only.\n- 2015-10-14: HEPiX Fall 2015 (BNL), \"Scientific Data Storage at FNAL\" ([Indico](https://indico.cern.ch/event/384358/contributions/909222/)).\n- 2012: dCache workshop, Nagios probes for dCache ([slides](https://www.dcache.org/manuals/2012/workshop/slides/gerard-nagios-probes.pdf)).\n\n## How they think\n- **Speed of iteration:** \"rapidly iterate on ideas\" (Google Cloud, 2024).\n- **Vendor choice:** \"technical depth and operational clarity\" (Lambda, 2026).\n- **Power, cooling and latency** as the design limits of an AI factory (GTC 2026 title and track).\n- **A multi-cloud buyer:** Google Cloud, Lambda and CoreWeave all sit beside HRT's own data centres. No contrarian views or public calls found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Lambda x HRT release (2026-05-20)](https://lambda.com/blog/lambda-partners-with-hudson-river-trading-to-power-quantitative-research-and-development): his stated reasons for choosing Lambda.\n2. [GTC 2026 S82132](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/on-demand/session/gtc26-s82132/): his only long-form public talk at HRT (not yet watched).\n3. [Google Cloud x HRT (2024-07)](https://www.tradersmagazine.com/xtra/google-cloud-hudson-river-trading-partner-on-automated-trading/): the earlier cloud decision.\n4. [Reuters on the Lambda deal](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/05/20/lambda-wins-cloud-deal-with-hudson-river-trading-to-supply-access-to-nvidia-chips): the size of the deal (more than 1,000 Blackwell systems) and that the capacity was already installed.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public X or Substack, and his GitHub has been inactive since about 2015.\n\n## Portfolio\nNo equity investments. Compute purchases he is personally named on:\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Lambda | Customer contract (HGX B200; Reuters: more than 1,000 Blackwell systems rented). Not an investment | 2026-05-20 | n/a | n/a | [Lambda](https://lambda.com/blog/lambda-partners-with-hudson-river-trading-to-power-quantitative-research-and-development) |\n| Google Cloud | Customer collaboration (NVIDIA GPUs, Dynamic Workload Scheduler, Spot VMs). Not an investment | 2024-07-18 | n/a | n/a | [Traders Magazine](https://www.tradersmagazine.com/xtra/google-cloud-hudson-river-trading-partner-on-automated-trading/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Lambda (2026-05):** HRT rented existing Lambda capacity: HGX B200 plus networking, storage and orchestration. He is the only HRT person quoted. Lambda CTO Stephen Balaban and NVIDIA's Ioana Boier are also quoted ([Lambda](https://lambda.com/blog/lambda-partners-with-hudson-river-trading-to-power-quantitative-research-and-development)). Financial terms were not disclosed ([Reuters via The Star](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/05/20/lambda-wins-cloud-deal-with-hudson-river-trading-to-supply-access-to-nvidia-chips)). Who made the introduction is unknown; NVIDIA's capital-markets team appearing in the release suggests NVIDIA brokered it (inference).\n- **Google Cloud (2024-07):** HRT moved research and trading-simulation workloads beyond its on-premise machines onto Google Cloud GPUs, using Spot VMs and the Dynamic Workload Scheduler. He is the only HRT voice ([Traders Magazine](https://www.tradersmagazine.com/xtra/google-cloud-hudson-river-trading-partner-on-automated-trading/)).\n\n## How a founder or co-investor reaches them\nNo direct channel is published. The routes, in order:\n1. NVIDIA's capital-markets team (Ioana Boier), which appeared in the Lambda release.\n2. His GTC talk and the HPC infrastructure community (HEPiX and dCache alumni from PIC and Fermilab).\n3. HRT's published ventures inbox, strategicvc@hudson-trading.com ([ventures page](https://www.hudsonrivertrading.com/ventures/)), which reaches the investment side, not him.\n\nAll of these routes are inferred; none is sourced as how he takes meetings.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hudson River Trading | at: Compute Systems Lead (\"compute lead\" in 2024) | by 2024-07 | [Traders Magazine](https://www.tradersmagazine.com/xtra/google-cloud-hudson-river-trading-partner-on-automated-trading/), [Lambda](https://lambda.com/blog/lambda-partners-with-hudson-river-trading-to-power-quantitative-research-and-development) |\n| Fermilab | at: Scientific Computing Division engineer (former) | by 2014-11 | [FERMILAB-CONF-14-470](http://lss.fnal.gov/archive/2014/conf/fermilab-conf-14-470-cd.pdf) |\n| Port d'Informació Científica (PIC) | at: storage/grid engineer (former) | by 2010 | [CHEP 2010](https://indico.in2p3.fr/event/7291/contributions/41319/attachments/33249/40953/1742-6596_331_7_072003.pdf) |\n| Lambda | customer-side evaluator (HGX B200) | 2026-05-20 | [Lambda](https://lambda.com/blog/lambda-partners-with-hudson-river-trading-to-power-quantitative-research-and-development) |\n| Google Cloud | customer-side voice (GPU research platform) | 2024-07-18 | [Traders Magazine](https://www.tradersmagazine.com/xtra/google-cloud-hudson-river-trading-partner-on-automated-trading/) |\n| Kevin Lee | HRT colleague (Head of R&D, CoreWeave deal) | unknown | [CoreWeave IR](https://investors.coreweave.com/news/news-details/2026/Hudson-River-Trading-to-Build-Next-Gen-Research-Platform-Powered-by-NVIDIA-Vera-Rubin-NVL72-on-CoreWeave-Cloud/default.aspx) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did he join HRT, and in what role? The LinkedIn page was not fetched.\n- Does Compute Systems evaluate non-NVIDIA accelerators (Etched testing per Reuters)? Is he in that loop?\n- What does the GTC S82132 recording say about vendor selection, power and on-prem versus cloud?\n- Does he have any advisory or angel role at a chip or cloud startup? None found.\n\n## Sources\n- https://lambda.com/blog/lambda-partners-with-hudson-river-trading-to-power-quantitative-research-and-development\n- https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/05/20/lambda-wins-cloud-deal-with-hudson-river-trading-to-supply-access-to-nvidia-chips\n- https://www.tradersmagazine.com/xtra/google-cloud-hudson-river-trading-partner-on-automated-trading/\n- https://www.nvidia.com/gtc/session-catalog/sessions/gtc26-s82132/\n- https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/on-demand/session/gtc26-s82132/\n- https://indico.in2p3.fr/event/7291/contributions/41319/attachments/33249/40953/1742-6596_331_7_072003.pdf\n- https://pos.sissa.it/293/011/pdf\n- http://lss.fnal.gov/archive/2014/conf/fermilab-conf-14-470-cd.pdf\n- https://www.osti.gov/biblio/1329064\n- https://indico.cern.ch/event/384358/contributions/909222/\n- https://github.com/gerardba\n- https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1332665\n- https://investors.coreweave.com/news/news-details/2026/Hudson-River-Trading-to-Build-Next-Gen-Research-Platform-Powered-by-NVIDIA-Vera-Rubin-NVL72-on-CoreWeave-Cloud/default.aspx\n- https://uk.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/highfrequency-traders-race-to-build-ai-computing-power-93CH-4927634\n- https://www.hudsonrivertrading.com/ventures/\n", "person:gili-raanan": "\n# Gili Raanan\n\n## One-line take\nThe seed investor with the single best fund-one result of the last decade, now pointing the same pre-product method at AI-security companies.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he funds a team before there is an idea and lets a network of security buyers shape the product. He concentrates hard: 12% of Fund I went into Wiz's seed.\n- **What constrains him:** security only, and the buyer network is also the firm's main reputational risk after the 2024 Calcalist investigation.\n- **What he'd never do:** raise a large seed fund. Fund I was $54M and Fund IV $62.4M.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He caps the seed fund and turns LPs away.** \"I had to disappoint a lot of prospective investors\" ([Fortune, 2024-10-21](https://fortune.com/2024/10/21/exclusive-gili-raanans-cyberstarts-raises-60-million-for-fourth-seed-fund/)).\n- **On the CISO programme after the investigation:** \"It's completely detached from the commercial aspects of the business\" (same piece). He confirmed the fund-level points for advisers were scrapped in 2024.\n- **He started when the sector was unloved.** \"When I started Cyberstarts, cybersecurity wasn't hot\" (same piece).\n- **Wiz numbers:** \"invested $6.4 million from its first $54 million fund\"; the fund held 4.1% at exit and had already sold $120M in secondaries ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/19/how-a-6m-bet-on-wiz-turned-into-a-massive-200x-return-for-one-early-backer)).\n- **A single investor funded his $300M employee-liquidity vehicle** ([Form D, 2025-07-09](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2075202/000207520225000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **Peer view:** Greylock's Saam Motamedi named him as the investor whose portfolio he would most want (already in this repo under saam-motamedi).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Not frontier AI, but the clearest example of what I care about structurally: a concentrated specialist seed fund, founder LPs, a first cheque before product, and a separate vehicle to hold ownership. His 2025-26 seeds (Glow, NewCore, Vega) are AI-era security and were priced into unicorn territory within a year.\n\n## Background\n- Founded Sanctum (1997) and NLayers; both were acquired ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gili_Raanan)).\n- General Partner at Sequoia Capital Israel for about nine years ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/19/how-a-6m-bet-on-wiz-turned-into-a-massive-200x-return-for-one-early-backer)).\n- Founded Cyberstarts in 2018 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1735657/000173565718000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://cyberstarts.com/approach-and-team | No personal bio on the site |\n| Substack / blog | not found | |\n| X | not found | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Firm YouTube: https://www.youtube.com/@cyberstarts-vc |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-03 — 20VC — 19 Company Portfolio, 1 Decacorn, 7 Unicorns, 4 Acquisitions — [link](https://thetwentyminutevc.libsyn.com/20vc-19-company-portfolio-1-decacorn-7-unicorns-4-acquisitions-one-of-the-best-seed-investors-of-all-time-on-how-to-pick-generational-defining-founders-why-nothing-but-the-founder-matters-why-the-best-investors-are-never-happy-w-gili-raanan) — not mined (no transcript read)\n- 2026-03 — Sourcery — Google Completes $32B Wiz Acquisition — [link](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-google-completes-32b-wiz) — recorded in London 2026-02-12; show notes say he covers investing \"before product, team, or code exists\"; not mined\n- 2024-10 — Fortune Term Sheet interview — [link](https://fortune.com/2024/10/21/exclusive-gili-raanans-cyberstarts-raises-60-million-for-fourth-seed-fund/) — mined\n\n## How they think\n- People over ideas; the idea can come after the cheque (show notes and firm site; his own words not transcribed here).\n- Security demand keeps growing with each platform shift; he cited cloud migration in 2024 and AI agents in 2026.\n- Seed funds should stay small; scale belongs in a separate vehicle.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Fortune, Oct 2024](https://fortune.com/2024/10/21/exclusive-gili-raanans-cyberstarts-raises-60-million-for-fourth-seed-fund/): fund size, LPs, his answer on Sunrise.\n2. [TechCrunch, Mar 2025](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/19/how-a-6m-bet-on-wiz-turned-into-a-massive-200x-return-for-one-early-backer): the Wiz maths.\n3. The 20VC episode (not yet mined).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found on X or Substack. He speaks through press interviews and a few podcasts.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Glow | Seed ($20M) | 2025 | co-led | Sequoia (Shaun Maguire) | [Calcalist](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/hky511mc4gx) |\n| Glow | Series B ($100M) | 2026-07 | co-led | Sequoia, Greenoaks, Redpoint | [release](https://www.glow.io/news/glow-emerges-from-stealth-with-180-million) |\n| Wiz | Seed | 2020-02 | first seed investor | Sequoia | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/19/how-a-6m-bet-on-wiz-turned-into-a-massive-200x-return-for-one-early-backer) |\n| Other Cyberstarts companies | see firm page | | | | [firm](../firms/cyberstarts.md) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Wiz:** seed in February 2020, $6.4M. He had worked with the Wiz founders' earlier company Adallom while at Sequoia (per Wikipedia; not verified in a primary source). Assaf Rappaport later became a Cyberstarts LP.\n- **Glow:** co-led the seed with Sequoia. \"Great companies are ultimately defined by the people who build them\" ([release](https://www.glow.io/news/glow-emerges-from-stealth-with-180-million)). A former Cyberstarts partner, Emily Heath, is Glow's COO.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sequoia Capital | former firm; Fund I LP (press); co-lead on Wiz and Glow | 2018 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/19/how-a-6m-bet-on-wiz-turned-into-a-massive-200x-return-for-one-early-backer) |\n| Assaf Rappaport | Wiz founder he seeded; LP | 2020 | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/10/21/exclusive-gili-raanans-cyberstarts-raises-60-million-for-fourth-seed-fund/) |\n| Nir Zuk, Shlomo Kramer | LPs | not stated | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/10/21/exclusive-gili-raanans-cyberstarts-raises-60-million-for-fourth-seed-fund/) |\n| Shardul Shah (Index) | repeat co-investor (Wiz, NewCore, Linx) | not stated | [firm page](../firms/cyberstarts.md) |\n| Lior Simon | his general partner on Vega and NewCore | 2020 | [profile](lior-simon.md) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What he says on 20VC and Sourcery about picking founders (needs a transcript).\n- Who funded the Liquidity Fund.\n\n## Sources\nLinked inline; Form D filings on the firm page.\n", "person:grace-isford": "\n# Grace Isford\n\n_Updated 2026-11-10: corrected Sakana follow-on timing from Sakana's own pages (Series A co-lead, Series B participation), added the Tokyo-childhood quote, the 2024 summit unicorn stat, the Sourcery LangChain attribution and new leads. Web search budget was exhausted this session, so only direct fetches were possible; no new podcast transcripts or X posts were reached._\n\n## One-line take\nA young Lux partner in NYC who co-signed the founding round of a new frontier lab (Sakana AI) and leads seed deals on the agent and voice layer. She tracks AI talent migration as a leading indicator, which makes her one of the better-placed rising investors on frontier AI talent.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** She builds the map before she chases the deal. Examples are the NYC AI Directory and AI Map she started from an April 2024 callout ([src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/grace-isford-lux-capital-computer)), Lux's annual NYC AI Summits that she co-hosts ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-3rd-annual-ai-summit-a-canvas-for-creativity)), and her own count of researchers leaving Meta and Google for Lux portfolio companies ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/the-great-talent-dividend-and-nycs-ai-opportunity)). She then fills her seeds with Lux portfolio CEOs as angels. On Phonic those were the CEOs of Applied Intuition, Hugging Face and Modal ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale)).\n- **What constrains her:** Her frontier-lab exposure runs through a pair. She co-signed Sakana with Brandon Reeves ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/graceisford_brandon-reeves-and-i-are-excited-to-announce-activity-7153040459794190336-kywQ)). Lux's own company pages attribute Cognition to Reeves ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/cognition)), Together to Reeves, Isford and Lan Jiang ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/together)), and Physical Intelligence to Reeves and Jiang (per the existing Lux profile, [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/physical-intelligence)). The deals she signs alone or with someone other than Reeves sit on the application, agent and voice layer: Maven AGI with Bilal Zuberi ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/our-investment-in-maven-agi-reimagining-customer-support-with-ai-agents)), and Phonic, led with Reeves ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale)). Treat Cognition, Modal and Physical Intelligence on her bio page as Lux-team exposure, not proof that she sourced them.\n- **What she'd never do (inference, not sourced):** back a thin \"AI-labelled\" app without a technical insight or proprietary data. She writes that founders should \"Avoid smacking an 'AI' label on the business\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/the-great-talent-dividend-and-nycs-ai-opportunity)), and the Phonic post says Lux loves \"technical founders with technical insights\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Models are a depreciating asset.** She writes that \"Foundation models are the fastest depreciating asset on the market.\" Her conclusion is that apps win on data, evals, scaffolding and UX, not on which model they call. The post is Feb 2025; one fetch named Nithanth Ram as co-author ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/beyond-the-consensus-navigating-ai)). This is an awkward position for a lab investor, and it matters for a lab thesis: she backs labs for research, not for model moats.\n- **Agent reliability compounds badly.** Her framing: \"the 99% agent ends up at only 60% accuracy\" once steps chain together ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/beyond-the-consensus-navigating-ai)). The same \"AI agents struggle with reliability\" line shows up as the thesis for Phonic two months later ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale)). Her written thesis predicts her deals.\n- **Talent flow is the signal.** She writes that \"recruiting and retaining great talent is a leading indicator.\" She counts 23 Meta people and 30 Google (Brain and DeepMind) people moving to Lux portfolio companies since 2020, including MosaicML, Runway, LangChain, Hugging Face and Sakana ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/the-great-talent-dividend-and-nycs-ai-opportunity), 2025-04-17). That is the same lens as THESIS.md.\n- **The question has shifted.** At the 2025 Lux AI Summit she reframed AI's central question as \"what should we build?\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-3rd-annual-ai-summit-a-canvas-for-creativity)).\n- **Sakana timing: first in, then held the lead.** Lux \"is leading the $30M founding round of Sakana AI\" ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/graceisford_brandon-reeves-and-i-are-excited-to-announce-activity-7153040459794190336-kywQ), 2024-01-16). Lux was the first institutional money into the lab; Khosla, NTT, KDDI and Sony came in the same round. About 7.5 months later Sakana's ~$200M Series A was \"led by New Enterprise Associates, Khosla Ventures and Lux Capital\", with NVIDIA participating ([src](https://sakana.ai/series-a/), 2024-09-04). So NEA was the new lead that followed, and Lux re-upped as co-lead rather than ceding the round. In the ~$200M (¥32B) Series B at about ¥432B (~$2.7B) post-money, Lux is listed only as a participant, alongside MUFG, Khosla, NEA, Google and In-Q-Tel ([src](https://sakana.ai/series-b/), 2025-11-17). Sourcery's host notes give a $100M Series A at $1B in June 2024, which conflicts with Sakana's own page; I trust Sakana's page ([src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/grace-isford-lux-capital-computer)). Founding round to ~$2.7B took about 22 months.\n- **Tokyo is a personal thread, not just a thesis.** In the Datacast takeaways she says: \"I had the experience of living in Tokyo for a period of my childhood.\" This is a summary section, not a transcript, so confidence is low ([src](https://datacast.substack.com/p/episode-107-investing-at-the-nexus)). It is the most plausible personal link to Lux leading a Tokyo lab's founding round. The intro path itself is still unsourced.\n- **She counts unicorns as a market-timing signal.** At the 2024 Lux AI Summit she is quoted as saying \"There have been 20 new unicorns in AI in 2023\", with ten more by October 2024 ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-2nd-ai-summit-puts-nyc-on-the-ai-map), 2024-10-03, Unum Lux byline).\n- **Her own deal count is larger than her bylines.** The Sourcery host notes credit her with sourcing and leading 8 Lux investments and list Runway, LangChain and Maven AGI among them. This is secondary (host notes, paywalled episode, no quote from her) ([src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/grace-isford-lux-capital-computer)). LangChain does not appear on her Lux bio page.\n- **Phonic came from relationships, not inbound.** The founders were \"longtime friends of the Lux family\". They co-founded the MIT ML Club; one came from MosaicML (a Lux company) and one from Genesis Therapeutics ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale)). This is a Lux-portfolio talent pipeline turning into a seed.\n- **Crosby: a long courtship.** The Lux post on Crosby's $60M Series B, which Lux led, says \"I first met Ryan in late 2023 in New York.\" The byline is \"Unum Lux\", so the speaker is not confirmed as Isford ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/were-backing-crosby-to-end-the-billiable-hour-for-laywers), 2026-03-31). Lux's news index lists Isford and Reeves on it ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news)).\n- **Founder filter.** Per Datacast show notes (secondary summary), she looks for ambitious founders with a strong perspective, true technology, and early customer love. On hiring: \"If it is not a hell yes, it is a no.\" ([src](https://datacast.substack.com/p/episode-107-investing-at-the-nexus))\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** She was in at day zero of a new frontier lab (Sakana's founding round), and her written thesis is about AI talent leaving big labs, which matches THESIS.md closely. She also leads agent and voice seeds (Phonic, Maven AGI) that sit near the AI-native automation category. The deals that matter to me:\n- Sakana AI: founding round, a new lab, Japan. Lux then co-led the ~$200M Series A with NEA and Khosla, and stayed in the Series B at ~$2.7B ([src](https://sakana.ai/series-a/), [src](https://sakana.ai/series-b/)). This is the pattern I want to co-invest behind: first check, then defend the position.\n- Together AI: 2023 seed, open-model compute; she is listed on the company card\n- Phonic: $4M seed she led, voice-agent infrastructure\n- Crosby: AI-native legal automation, a Lux-led $60M Series B; her role is not confirmed\n- Maven AGI: agentic customer support\n\nCaveats: Lux's check sizes and ownership targets are not sourced for her. Her lab deals are co-signed with Reeves. If I want co-invest access into labs, the pair (Isford plus Reeves) is the unit, not her alone.\n\n## Background\n- Originally from Connecticut ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/grace-isford)). She lived in Tokyo for part of her childhood (Datacast takeaways). That is a plausible thread to the Sakana deal, but it is an inference ([src](https://datacast.substack.com/p/episode-107-investing-at-the-nexus)).\n- Her Lux bio describes her as a generalist investing at the intersection of the physical world and software, LLM applications and infrastructure software ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/grace-isford)).\n- Stanford: BS and MS in Management Science and Engineering. Mayfield Fellow and co-president of Stanford Women in Business ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/grace-isford)).\n- Internships at Stanford Management Company (the endowment, on the LP side), Handshake (product) and Stripes Group ([src](https://datacast.substack.com/p/episode-107-investing-at-the-nexus)).\n- Canvas Ventures: started as a campus scout while a student and became a principal. She worked on Series A deals in Vendia and Robocorp ([src](https://datacast.substack.com/p/episode-107-investing-at-the-nexus)). Per Sourcery she sourced $100M of deployed capital there and worked with 10 portfolio companies ([src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/grace-isford-lux-capital-computer)).\n- Lux Capital: joined as principal in early 2022 and was promoted to partner in Dec 2022 ([src](https://datacast.substack.com/p/episode-107-investing-at-the-nexus)). Sourcery calls her the firm's youngest partner, a 2024 Forbes 30 Under 30 in VC, and says she has sourced and led 8 Lux investments ([src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/grace-isford-lux-capital-computer)).\n- Board of the Stanford Technology Ventures Program ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/grace-isford)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.luxcapital.com/people/grace-isford | Lux bio |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.luxcapital.com/news | Writes Lux posts: thesis pieces and deal announcements, several a year. Earlier Medium essays on the API economy (2020-21), per Datacast |\n| X | https://x.com/graceisford | Fetch failed (HTTP 402); followers and cadence not retrieved |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/graceisford/ | Announces deals here (Sakana post) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-02 (recorded 2022-04): Datacast, ep. 107, \"Investing at the Nexus of Computational Sciences\" ([src](https://datacast.substack.com/p/episode-107-investing-at-the-nexus)). Covers her path from campus scout to Canvas, her Vendia and Robocorp Series As, Web3 infra views, and her founder checklist. Only show notes were read; there was no transcript.\n- 2024-02-29: Bloomberg TV, \"Lux Capital's Isford Sees Unprecedented AI Demand in NYC\" ([src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/videos/2024-02-29/lux-capital-s-isford-sees-unprecedented-ai-demand-in-nyc-video)). The content was not retrieved.\n- 2024-08-23: Sourcery with Molly O'Shea, \"Computer Science is 'So Hot Right Now'\" ([src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/grace-isford-lux-capital-computer)). Only the host's notes were read; no verbatim Isford quotes were captured.\n- 2024-10, 2025-10: co-host of Lux's 2nd and 3rd AI Summits in NYC ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-2nd-ai-summit-puts-nyc-on-the-ai-map), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-3rd-annual-ai-summit-a-canvas-for-creativity)). 2025 speakers included Scott Wu (Cognition), David Ha (Sakana), Lachy Groom and Karol Hausman (Physical Intelligence), Naveen Rao (Unconventional AI), and Tri Dao and Vipul Ved Prakash (Together).\n\n## How they think\n- **Main theses:** agents are not reliable yet, so reliability infrastructure is the opportunity (voice in Phonic, support in Maven AGI). Foundation models commoditize, so moats come from data, evals and UX. The next frontier is robotics, defense, manufacturing and life sciences ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/beyond-the-consensus-navigating-ai)). NYC is an AI hub fed by a \"talent dividend\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/the-great-talent-dividend-and-nycs-ai-opportunity)).\n- **Contrarian views:** \"GPT wrappers are cool.\" She defends app-layer companies against the wrapper critique ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/beyond-the-consensus-navigating-ai)). She bet early that Japan becomes \"a pivotal hub in a new global axis of AI\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/our-investment-in-japans-sakana-ai-to-build-nature-inspired-ai-foundation-models)).\n- **Calls right or wrong:** Sakana went from its founding round to a reported $1B valuation in about five months (secondary, Sourcery). In 2022 she was bullish on Web3 infrastructure (Datacast). That has not visibly carried into her Lux deals, and nothing found shows a Web3 deal at Lux.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Beyond the Consensus: Navigating AI's Frontier in 2025\" (2025-02-25), her clearest thesis on agents, model commoditization and moats ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/beyond-the-consensus-navigating-ai)).\n2. \"The Great Talent Dividend and NYC's AI Opportunity\" (2025-04-17), her talent-migration data and why NYC ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/the-great-talent-dividend-and-nycs-ai-opportunity)).\n3. The Sakana AI investment post (2024-01-16), the frontier-lab thesis ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/our-investment-in-japans-sakana-ai-to-build-nature-inspired-ai-foundation-models)).\n4. The Phonic investment post (2025-04-03), which shows how she assembles a seed ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale)).\n\n## Social engagement\nX could not be retrieved (HTTP 402). On LinkedIn she announces deals and tags co-investors and founders. The Sakana post tagged Khosla, NTT, KDDI, Sony, Jeff Dean, Clem Delangue, Alexandr Wang and a set of Japanese and US funds ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/graceisford_brandon-reeves-and-i-are-excited-to-announce-activity-7153040459794190336-kywQ)). Who amplifies her: nothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n\"Attribution\" separates deals she signed from companies merely listed on her bio page.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Attribution | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Sakana AI | $30M founding round | 2024-01-16 | Yes (Lux), co-signed with Reeves | Khosla, NTT, KDDI, Sony; angels Jeff Dean, Clem Delangue, Alexandr Wang | Signed (LinkedIn post) | [src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/graceisford_brandon-reeves-and-i-are-excited-to-announce-activity-7153040459794190336-kywQ) |\n| Sakana AI | ~$200M Series A | 2024-09-04 | Co-lead (NEA, Khosla, Lux) | Translink, 500 Global, NVIDIA; MUFG, SMBC, Mizuho, NEC, KDDI, Fujitsu and other strategics; Global Brain, JAFCO, Miyako | Lux-level; her personal role in the A not stated | [src](https://sakana.ai/series-a/) |\n| Sakana AI | ~$200M (¥32B) Series B, ~¥432B (~$2.7B) post | 2025-11-17 | No lead named; Lux participated | MUFG, Khosla, Factorial, Macquarie, Salesforce Ventures, Google, NEA, Geodesic, In-Q-Tel and others | Lux-level | [src](https://sakana.ai/series-b/) |\n| LangChain | not stated (Sourcery cites the Feb 2024 $25M Series A) | | not stated | | Sourcery host notes say she sourced and led it (secondary) | [src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/grace-isford-lux-capital-computer) |\n| Phonic | $4M seed | 2025-04-03 | Yes (Lux) | Qasar Younis, Clem Delangue, Erik Bernhardsson | Signed with Reeves | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale) |\n| Maven AGI | $28M financing (stage and lead not stated) | 2024-05-29 | Not stated | M13, E14, Mentors Fund; execs from OpenAI, Google, HubSpot, Stripe | Signed with Bilal Zuberi | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/our-investment-in-maven-agi-reimagining-customer-support-with-ai-agents) |\n| Crosby | $60M Series B | 2026-03-31 | Yes (Lux) | none named | Listed under her name in Lux news index; post bylined \"Unum Lux\" | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/were-backing-crosby-to-end-the-billiable-hour-for-laywers) |\n| Runway | Series C | not stated | not stated | | She invested for Lux (Datacast notes) | [src](https://datacast.substack.com/p/episode-107-investing-at-the-nexus) |\n| Together AI | Lux investment 2023 (the 2023-05 $20M seed, [src](https://siliconangle.com/2023/05/15/together-raises-20m-decentralize-open-source-ai-model-creation)) | 2023 | Lux led per the Lux firm profile | | Named on company card with Reeves and Jiang | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/together) |\n| Modal | Lux investment 2022 | 2022 | unknown | | Bio page only; company card names no Lux person | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/modal) |\n| Cognition | Lux investment 2024 | 2024 | unknown | | Bio page only; company card names Reeves | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/cognition) |\n| Physical Intelligence | seed and later | 2024 | No (Thrive led the seed per aggregator, [src](https://startupintros.com/news/2024-03-12-physical-intelligence-seed)) | | Bio page only | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/grace-isford) |\n| Anduril | not stated | | | | Bio page only | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/grace-isford) |\n| Vendia | Series A (at Canvas) | | | | Datacast notes | [src](https://datacast.substack.com/p/episode-107-investing-at-the-nexus) |\n| Robocorp | Series A (at Canvas) | | | | Datacast notes | [src](https://datacast.substack.com/p/episode-107-investing-at-the-nexus) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Sakana AI (Jan 2024):** a founding round for a Tokyo lab started by David Ha and Llion Jones, with Ren Ito tagged ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/graceisford_brandon-reeves-and-i-are-excited-to-announce-activity-7153040459794190336-kywQ)). The thesis was that \"the next generation of AI will require further fundamental research\", plus Japan as a new AI hub ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/our-investment-in-japans-sakana-ai-to-build-nature-inspired-ai-foundation-models)). The intro path is not in the sources. Lux was first institutional; Khosla and Japanese strategics joined the same round. NEA arrived as a new co-lead for the ~$200M Series A in Sept 2024, with Lux and Khosla co-leading again ([src](https://sakana.ai/series-a/)). Lux participated in the Nov 2025 Series B ([src](https://sakana.ai/series-b/)).\n- **Phonic (Apr 2025):** the founders (first names Moin and Nikhil) met as MIT undergrads and co-founded the MIT ML Club, then went to MosaicML and Genesis Therapeutics. Lux knew them as \"longtime friends of the Lux family.\" The seed was rounded out with three Lux portfolio CEOs as angels ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale)).\n- **Crosby (Mar 2026):** per the Lux post, the author first met co-founder Ryan Daniels in late 2023 in New York. Co-founder John Sarihan was a top engineer at Lux portfolio company Ramp. Lux led the $60M Series B ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/were-backing-crosby-to-end-the-billiable-hour-for-laywers)). The author is not confirmed as Isford, and Lux's earlier involvement is not in the post.\n- **Maven AGI (May 2024):** co-signed with Bilal Zuberi. The founders are Jonathan Corbin, Eugene Mann and Sami Shalabi ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/our-investment-in-maven-agi-reimagining-customer-support-with-ai-agents)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Brandon Reeves (Lux) | Co-signing partner on Sakana and Phonic; co-hosted the 2024 AI Summit | 2024 | [src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/graceisford_brandon-reeves-and-i-are-excited-to-announce-activity-7153040459794190336-kywQ) |\n| Bilal Zuberi (Lux) | Co-signer on Maven AGI | 2024 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/our-investment-in-maven-agi-reimagining-customer-support-with-ai-agents) |\n| Josh Wolfe (Lux) | Co-author of the AI Summit posts | 2024 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-2nd-ai-summit-puts-nyc-on-the-ai-map) |\n| Tess van Stekelenburg (Lux) | Co-author of the 2025 AI Summit post | 2025 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-3rd-annual-ai-summit-a-canvas-for-creativity) |\n| Lan Jiang (Lux) | Co-listed on Together | 2023 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/together) |\n| Nithanth Ram | Named co-author on \"Beyond the Consensus\" (one fetch) | 2025 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/beyond-the-consensus-navigating-ai) |\n| David Ha, Llion Jones (Sakana) | Portfolio founders | 2024 | [src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/graceisford_brandon-reeves-and-i-are-excited-to-announce-activity-7153040459794190336-kywQ) |\n| New Enterprise Associates | Co-lead with Lux and Khosla on Sakana's Series A | 2024-09 | [src](https://sakana.ai/series-a/) |\n| Khosla Ventures | Co-investor in Sakana's founding round | 2024 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/our-investment-in-japans-sakana-ai-to-build-nature-inspired-ai-foundation-models) |\n| Canvas Ventures | Campus scout, then principal | before 2022 | [src](https://datacast.substack.com/p/episode-107-investing-at-the-nexus) |\n| Stanford Technology Ventures Program | Board member | | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/grace-isford) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Runway Series C: the date, the lead, and her role in winning it.\n- Did she source Together, or join Reeves on it? Same question for Modal (2022, the year she joined) and Cognition.\n- Her personal check size, and Lux's ownership targets on seeds she leads.\n- How Lux got to Sakana (the intro path to David Ha and Llion Jones), and whether her time in Tokyo mattered.\n- Was Lux in Crosby before the Series B, and who wrote \"I first met Ryan\"?\n- Her X activity and who amplifies her (fetch failed). Transcripts of Sourcery and Bloomberg were not read.\n- The NYC AI Directory: does she still run it, and has it produced deals?\n- LangChain: did she source Lux's position (Sourcery says so), and in which round?\n- Did she or Reeves run Lux's side of the Sakana Series A and B?\n- Web search was exhausted again on 2026-11-10. Run a follow-up for other podcasts, such as Lux's Riskgaming, and for any Substack.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/people/grace-isford\n- https://datacast.substack.com/p/episode-107-investing-at-the-nexus\n- https://www.linkedin.com/posts/graceisford_brandon-reeves-and-i-are-excited-to-announce-activity-7153040459794190336-kywQ\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/our-investment-in-japans-sakana-ai-to-build-nature-inspired-ai-foundation-models\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-our-investment-in-phonic-making-voice-agents-reliable-at-scale\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/beyond-the-consensus-navigating-ai\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/the-great-talent-dividend-and-nycs-ai-opportunity\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/our-investment-in-maven-agi-reimagining-customer-support-with-ai-agents\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/were-backing-crosby-to-end-the-billiable-hour-for-laywers\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-3rd-annual-ai-summit-a-canvas-for-creativity\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-2nd-ai-summit-puts-nyc-on-the-ai-map\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news\n- https://www.sourcery.vc/p/grace-isford-lux-capital-computer (secondary: host notes)\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/videos/2024-02-29/lux-capital-s-isford-sees-unprecedented-ai-demand-in-nyc-video\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/together\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/modal\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/cognition\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/sakana\n- https://sakana.ai/series-a/\n- https://sakana.ai/series-b/\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-grace-isford-sees-unprecedented-ai-demand-in-nyc (headline only)\n", "person:greg-brockman": "\n# Greg Brockman\n\n*Research note (2026-11-11): thin page. He does not publish a portfolio and I found no interview or post where he discusses angel investing. Crunchbase (38 personal investments, not fetchable) and other aggregators disagree on counts; one aggregator (Pipeseed) lists Anthropic, xAI, Mistral and Cohere under his name, which I treat as wrong. No headshot URL from his own site or OpenAI could be verified (openai.com blocks fetches), so `photo` is empty. Entity registry lists this id with kind `firm`; it should be `person`.*\n\n## One-line take\nOpenAI's president writes small personal cheques into the tooling and compute layer around OpenAI (Hugging Face, Baseten, Braintrust, Roboflow, Lambda, Cerebras) and, in Chai Discovery, invested beside OpenAI itself.\n\n## Crux\nFounders want him because a cheque from the person who runs OpenAI's engineering and compute is a customer and distribution signal. He never leads and appears as one name in a list of founder-CEO angels. The constraint is conflict: his job is OpenAI, so his personal cheques sit in infrastructure and applications, not in competing labs, and he has made few visible new investments since 2024.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Chai Discovery's seed was \"led by Thrive Capital, OpenAI, and Dimension Capital\" with Brockman listed separately as an angel: company money and the president's personal money in the same round [src](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement) (2024-09-09).\n- In Braintrust's seed he is listed as \"Greg Brockman, Co-founder and President of OpenAI\" in an angel roster of AI-infra CEOs (Hugging Face, Vercel, Together, Baseten, Datadog) behind Greylock [src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round) (2023-12-13).\n- Baseten's 2022 A named \"a group of outstanding angels including Greg Brockman\" next to Lachy Groom, another ex-Stripe investor who reappears with him in Chai [src](https://www.baseten.co/blog/announcing-our-series-a/).\n- Cerebras: an early angel with Sam Altman; the stake was worth about $24.2M at the 2026 IPO (ledger, CNBC), small next to OpenAI's later 750 MW contract.\nRows: `data/rows/early-greg-brockman-insights.csv`.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. Not an allocator to track for deal flow, but his early chip cheques (Cerebras, Graphcore) and lab-adjacent seed (Chai) are in my categories, and his presence marks companies OpenAI is likely to buy from or partner with.\n\n## Background\nStripe's first CTO (joined 2010, left 2015); co-founded OpenAI in December 2015 [src](https://blog.gregbrockman.com/my-path-to-openai). President of OpenAI; took a leave in late 2024 and returned (ledger).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://gregbrockman.com | Personal site |\n| Substack / blog | https://blog.gregbrockman.com/ | Older essays, including \"My path to OpenAI\" |\n| X | https://x.com/gdb | Not reviewed in this run |\n| LinkedIn | not used | |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/gdb | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-09 — a16z — Greg Brockman on why OpenAI says we're entering the AGI era — [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/greg-brockman-on-why-openai-says-were-entering-the-agi-era/) — summary only: agents working 24 hours, 10,000 agents on Navier-Stokes; no investing content in the summary\n- 2023-05 — Possible (Reid Hoffman) — with Sam Altman — in ledger\n- 2019-05 — Greymatter — with Reid Hoffman — in ledger\n\n## How they think\n- Publicly he talks about OpenAI's roadmap and compute, not about investing.\n- Pattern from cheques: backs developer tooling he would use, and hardware that might break the GPU bottleneck.\n- No stated passes or regrets found.\n\n## Read these first\n- \"My path to OpenAI\" (blog) — how he chooses what to work on.\n- Braintrust seed post — shows the company he keeps as an angel.\n- Chai Discovery seed post — the OpenAI-plus-personal pattern.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed in this run.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI cheques found: 14 companies (ledger had 11). S = seed-stage entry.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Lead / co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | Founding pledges | 2015-12 | Co-founder | Altman; Musk; Thiel; Hoffman | ledger |\n| Cerebras | Early angel (round undisclosed) | n/a | No | Sam Altman; Lip-Bu Tan | ledger |\n| Graphcore | Individual investor | 2018-12 | No | BMW; Microsoft | ledger |\n| Rasa | Series A | 2019-04-11 | No | Accel led | ledger |\n| Hugging Face | Series A ($15M) | 2019-12-17 | No | Lux led; Richard Socher | ledger |\n| Roboflow | Series A | 2021-09-16 | No | | ledger |\n| Baseten | Series A | 2022-04-26 | No | Greylock; South Park Commons; Lachy Groom | [src](https://www.baseten.co/blog/announcing-our-series-a/) |\n| Gantry | Series A | 2022-06-07 | No | Amplify; Coatue | ledger |\n| Uplimit (S) | Seed (~$8.5M) | 2022-09 | No | | aggregator [src](https://www.crunchbase.com/person/greg-brockman) |\n| Lambda | Series B ($44M) | 2023-03-21 | No | | ledger |\n| Braintrust (S) | Seed ($5.1M); also in the 2024 A | 2023-12-13 | No | Greylock led; Elad Gil | [src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round) |\n| Chai Discovery (S) | Seed (~$30M) | 2024-09-09 | No | Thrive; OpenAI; Dimension | [src](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement) |\n| Scale AI | Round not stated | n/a | No | | secondary [src](https://www.woshipm.com/?p=5777785) |\n| Replit | Round not stated | n/a | No | | secondary [src](https://www.woshipm.com/?p=5777785) |\n\nNon-AI or unclear, aggregator only: Retool, Gel (EdgeDB), Command E, Sandbar (2023 financial-crime software, not the 2025 AI hardware company of the same name). CB Insights also lists a Feb 2026 entry for \"Leading the Future\", which is a political committee, not a startup.\n\nHit pattern: Hugging Face (A), Cerebras (IPO 2026), Lambda, Scale AI, Baseten, Braintrust, Chai Discovery. Vehicle: personal name in every announcement; no entity or Form D found. Cheque size: not disclosed (one aggregator's \"$1.5M sweet spot\" is uncorroborated).\n\n## Deal stories\nNo first-hand account found. The repeat names around him are ex-Stripe (Lachy Groom, Will Gaybrick in Chai) and Greylock as lead (Baseten, Braintrust).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | Co-founder & President | 2015-12 | [src](https://blog.gregbrockman.com/my-path-to-openai) |\n| Stripe | Former CTO | 2010 to 2015 | [src](https://blog.gregbrockman.com/my-path-to-openai) |\n| Sam Altman | Co-founder; co-angel in Cerebras | 2015 | ledger |\n| Lachy Groom | Co-investor (Baseten 2022, Chai 2024) | 2022 | [src](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement) |\n| Greylock | Lead he follows (Baseten, Braintrust) | 2022 | [src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Full Crunchbase list (38) and any cheques after 2024.\n- Whether he invests through a family office.\n- Anything he has said on angel investing (none found).\n\n## Sources\nhttps://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round ; https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement ; https://www.baseten.co/blog/announcing-our-series-a/ ; https://blog.gregbrockman.com/my-path-to-openai ; https://a16z.com/podcast/greg-brockman-on-why-openai-says-were-entering-the-agi-era/ ; https://www.woshipm.com/?p=5777785 (secondary) ; https://www.crunchbase.com/person/greg-brockman (aggregator, via search snippet) ; ledger rows\n", "person:greg-reichow": "\n# Greg Reichow\n\n_Profile created 2026-11-10. Web search was exhausted this run. Everything below comes from direct fetches: Eclipse's team page and its public CMS records for his bio and the companies he is partner of record on, his 14 Eclipse blog posts (2019–2026), SEC EDGAR Form D filings found by a full-text search for \"Reichow\" plus each company's filing list, and company announcement pages._\n\n## One-line take\nHe is Eclipse's quiet AI-silicon board member. The repo knew him for VulcanForms and Efficient, but SEC filings show he has held Tenstorrent's Eclipse board seat since its first 2017 Form D, and he now sits on AheadComputing's board beside Jim Keller.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he spent a career taking hardware from pilot to volume, at Cypress, then SunPower, then Tesla from the Roadster to Model S/X ([src](https://eclipse.capital/team/greg-reichow)). Founders building chips and factories want that ramp experience on their board. People he worked with years ago bring him deals: VulcanForms' CEO worked with him at Cypress and Tesla, and Reliable Robotics' CEO ran Autopilot while Reichow ran Tesla manufacturing.\n- **His usual move:** write the first or second check, take a board seat, stay for years, and keep following on. Examples: VulcanForms (seed, a preempted Series A, then co-lead of the D), Efficient (seed lead, then A and B), and Tenstorrent (board from 2017 through the 2021 Series C).\n- **Constraint:** he is a scale-up operator and judges teams by how fast they iterate on hardware. Nothing shows him backing software-only or model companies. Gimlet is the closest, and there Eclipse followed on rather than led.\n- **What he'd never do:** back a pre-training lab, or a team \"whose proudest artifact is their CAD model\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies)). He tells founders: \"I don't want to see your CAD model.\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He has been on Tenstorrent's board since its first US filing.** Tenstorrent's first Form D (first sale 2017-08-28, $11M offering, 6 investors) lists \"Reichow Greg\" at Eclipse's office, \"514 High St., Suite 4\" ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1718995/000089979717000006/primary_doc.xml)). He is on every later Tenstorrent Form D through the 2021 Series C D/A, where Jim Keller also appears ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1718995/000089979721000014/primary_doc.xml)). Before this, the repo had Eclipse in Tenstorrent only as an undated early backer. His 2020 Tenstorrent post: \"When we first partnered with the company, Ljubisa had two goals\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/artificial-neural-networks-should-work-like-the-one-in-our-head)).\n- **He shares two boards with Jim Keller.** Keller was a seed investor and director at AheadComputing. The company says the seed was \"led by Eclipse with participation from Maverick Capital, Fundomo, EPIQ Capital Group, and Jim Keller\" ([src](https://www.aheadcomputing.com/press-releases/aheadcomputing-raises-21-5m-seed-round-and-introduces-breakthrough-microprocessor-architecture-designed-for-next-era-of-general-purpose-computing)). Reichow joined that board by the 2025-10 Form D/A ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2035028/000203502825000004/primary_doc.xml)). The Tenstorrent seat is a plausible intro path, but that is not confirmed.\n- **AheadComputing has raised about $111M that I found no announcement for.** One Form D shows first sale 2026-06-05, \"Total Amount Sold $ 47,830,674 USD\", 17 investors ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2035028/000203502826000001/xslFormDX01/primary_doc.xml)). Another shows first sale 2026-10-08, $63.5M sold of $80M, 8 investors ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2035028/000203502826000002/xslFormDX01/primary_doc.xml)). Reichow is a director on both, and a new director, Andrew Homan, appears on both. The last announced total was $53M ([src](https://www.aheadcomputing.com/press-releases/aheadcomputing-inc-raises-additional-30m-seed2-round-to-reimagine-cpu-architecture----pr-newswire)). Stage names and Eclipse's participation are unconfirmed.\n- **His network goes back to Cypress.** Kevin Kassekert, now CEO of VulcanForms: \"I’ve worked with Greg across Cypress Semiconductor, Tesla, and now VulcanForms\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/team/greg-reichow)).\n- **How the VulcanForms deal started:** he and Martin Feldmann \"initially connected over a much older manufacturing technology\", die casting. Eclipse wrote \"a small seed investment\" when the company was two founders, then \"we preempted the Series A\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/catalyzing-innovation-and-u-s-economic-growth-our-investment-in-vulcanforms)).\n- **He decides on site visits.** On a visit to Arc during the Series A: \"At that moment I knew they would succeed.\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies)).\n- **His capital rule:** \"your cash burn is the integral under the curve\" (the area under the staffing curve), and cash spent after production starts costs the most ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies)).\n- **A mistake he admits:** he accepted Elon Musk's split where design happens in the studio and manufacturing builds it afterwards. His section on it is titled \"How I Learned This (The Expensive Way)\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies)).\n- **His AI-chip thesis is about energy:** \"solving AI’s energy problem would require rethinking computing from the ground up\" ([src](https://www.efficient.computer/series-b)).\n- **The two sides describe the AheadComputing seed differently.** Eclipse's post says it is \"thrilled to be participating in the company’s $21.5M seed round\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/a-breakthrough-in-processing-power-our-investment-in-aheadcomputing)). The company says Eclipse led it.\n- **Board seats outside Eclipse's current company list:** Symbio Robotics (Form Ds 2018 and 2020) ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1738149/000173814918000001/primary_doc.xml)) and 6 River Systems (Series A and B, 2017–18) ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1658013/000165801317000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He backs AI chips and compute at the first institutional round and holds the board seat himself. Deals that matter to you:\n- **Tenstorrent:** board from the 2017 Form D; Series E at $6.2B in 2026 ([src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-announces-series-e)).\n- **Efficient Computer:** seed lead in 2024, Series B at $650M in 2026.\n- **AheadComputing:** seed lead, Seed2 co-lead, and two large unannounced 2026 raises.\n- **Gimlet Labs:** he is Eclipse's partner of record on this inference cloud for agents, which followed through the A and the $300M B.\n\nHe is useful as a co-investor or board ally for silicon and compute companies, not for model labs. About half his named book is manufacturing and energy (VulcanForms, Arc, Tandem PV, Peak Energy, The Nuclear Company), which is low fit.\n\n## Background\n- B.S. Mechanical Engineering, University of Minnesota ([src](https://eclipse.capital/team/greg-reichow)).\n- Semiconductor industry, in manufacturing and quality roles ([src](https://eclipse.capital/team/greg-reichow)). This included Cypress Semiconductor, where he presented quarterly operations reviews to T.J. Rodgers ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/industrial-scaling-playbook-leadership-at-the-next-level)). Dates not found.\n- SunPower: SVP Operations. He scaled manufacturing from pilot to more than $2B in revenue ([src](https://eclipse.capital/team/greg-reichow)). Dates not found.\n- Tesla, from 2011: \"When I joined Tesla in 2011\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/create-a-meaningful-business-category-and-the-market-will-follow)). He was executive leader of global manufacturing, automation engineering, supply chain and product excellence, from the Roadster to Model S/X at more than an $8B run rate ([src](https://eclipse.capital/team/greg-reichow)). He calls himself \"VP of Global Manufacturing\" around 2015 ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/reliable-robotics-operates-aircraft-autonomously-from-takeoff-to-landing)).\n- Eclipse partner since 2016 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2019/02/06/eclipse-ventures-in-palo-alto-just-added-two-more-big-wheels-as-general-partners)). He was hired directly as partner; no promotion is recorded.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://eclipse.capital/team/greg-reichow | Eclipse bio (checked 2026-11-10); no personal site found |\n| Substack / blog | https://eclipse.capital/blog/ | 14 Eclipse posts 2019–2026; \"Industrial Scaling Playbook\" series in 2026 |\n| X | not found | Eclipse CMS lists no X handle for him |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/gregreichow/ | Linked from his Eclipse CMS record; not opened |\n| GitHub | not found | Bio says he enjoys \"developing software\"; no account found |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | not tested (no web search) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Podcasts and YouTube were not tested because web search was exhausted. The Eclipse CMS tags him on press pieces: Axios, 2026-02-05, on the manufacturing deal boom ([src](https://www.axios.com/pro/supply-chain-deals/2026/02/05/eclipse-manufacturing-deal-boom)); Manufacturing Dive, 2026-04-27, on Eclipse's $1.3B raise ([src](https://www.manufacturingdive.com/news/vc-firm-eclipse-raises-1-b-physical-industries-university-endowments/818546/)); and Canary Media, 2026-04-20, on Tandem PV ([src](https://www.canarymedia.com/articles/solar/perovskites-mass-production-tandem-fremont)). None of these were read.\n\n## How they think\n- **Rate of learning is the metric.** Build your own product on your own pilot line; \"This can never be outsourced if you want to win\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-industrial-scaling-playbook-why-most-hardware-startups-fail)).\n- **Against the \"design for manufacturing\" handoff,** and also against overcorrecting to off-the-shelf parts. Name the 3–5 places where you push manufacturing physics, and give each one an owner from day one ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies)).\n- **Compute:** efficiency and general-purpose programmability beat GPU-style specialisation (Tenstorrent 2020 post; Efficient; AheadComputing's RISC-V CPUs) ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/artificial-neural-networks-should-work-like-the-one-in-our-head)).\n- **Leadership at scale:** \"no\" means not now; push decisions down; keep teams to 8–10 people; \"Use anger only to correct cultural misalignment\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/industrial-scaling-playbook-leadership-at-the-next-level)).\n- **Calls he got right:** Tenstorrent from 2017 to $6.2B (2026); Efficient from seed to $650M.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Why the 'Design for Manufacturing' Mindset Kills Companies\" (2026-05-21): his diligence lens, the Arc visit, and his Tesla mistake ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies)).\n2. The VulcanForms investment post (2022-07-05): a full seed-to-preempt deal story ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/catalyzing-innovation-and-u-s-economic-growth-our-investment-in-vulcanforms)).\n3. \"Artificial Neural Networks Should Work Like the One in Our Head\" (2020-08-20): his Tenstorrent thesis ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/artificial-neural-networks-should-work-like-the-one-in-our-head)).\n4. \"Leadership at the Next Level\" (2026-08-05): his Cypress and Tesla management lessons ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/industrial-scaling-playbook-leadership-at-the-next-level)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNo personal X or Substack found. His public voice is Eclipse's blog. He amplifies portfolio founders (Feldman of Cerebras, Kassekert) and Eclipse partners (Charly Mwangi, Aidan Madigan-Curtis) ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies)).\n\n## Portfolio\nCompanies where Eclipse's site names him as partner: Tenstorrent, Gimlet, Reliable Robotics, VulcanForms, Peak Energy, Efficient Computer, Tandem PV, The Nuclear Company, AheadComputing and Arc (each page checked, e.g. [src](https://eclipse.capital/companies/tenstorrent)). Board seats from SEC filings add Symbio Robotics and 6 River Systems.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Tenstorrent | Series A (inferred; $11M Form D); board | 2017-08 | unknown | Real Ventures | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1718995/000089979717000006/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Tenstorrent | Series B (inferred; $20.5M Form D) | 2019-01 | unknown | | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1718995/000089979719000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Tenstorrent | Series C ($200M+ at $1B) | 2021-05 | no (Fidelity) | Epic CG, Moore | [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-raises-over-200-million-at-1-billion-valuation-to-create-programmable-high-performance-ai-computers) |\n| Efficient Computer | Seed ($16M), first money | 2024-03 | yes | none named | [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/efficient-computer-emerges-from-stealth-to-introduce-worlds-most-energy-efficient-general-purpose-computer-chip-302082126.html) |\n| Efficient Computer | Series A ($60M) | 2026-02 | no | | [src](https://convergedigest.com/efficient-computer-raises-60m-to-tackle-ais-energy-constraint-with-fabric-architecture/?amp=1) |\n| Efficient Computer | Series B ($97M at $650M) | 2026-09 | no | TQ, USV, Toyota Ventures, RTX Ventures | [src](https://www.efficient.computer/series-b) |\n| AheadComputing | Seed ($21.5M) | 2025-02 | yes (company's wording) | Maverick, Fundomo, EPIQ, Jim Keller | [src](https://www.aheadcomputing.com/press-releases/aheadcomputing-raises-21-5m-seed-round-and-introduces-breakthrough-microprocessor-architecture-designed-for-next-era-of-general-purpose-computing) |\n| AheadComputing | Seed2 ($30M); board | 2026-01 | co-lead | Toyota Ventures, Cambium | [src](https://www.aheadcomputing.com/press-releases/aheadcomputing-inc-raises-additional-30m-seed2-round-to-reimagine-cpu-architecture----pr-newswire) |\n| Gimlet Labs | Series A ($80M) | 2026-03 | no (Menlo) | Factory, Prosperity7, Triatomic | [src](https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-a) |\n| Gimlet Labs | Series B ($300M) | 2026-09 | no (a16z) | Sapphire, Menlo, Arm, M12 | [src](https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-b) |\n| VulcanForms | Seed (small) | ~2017 | likely | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/catalyzing-innovation-and-u-s-economic-growth-our-investment-in-vulcanforms) |\n| VulcanForms | Series A (preempted) | not stated | yes | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/catalyzing-innovation-and-u-s-economic-growth-our-investment-in-vulcanforms) |\n| VulcanForms | Series D ($220M) | 2026-01 | co-lead | 1789 Capital | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/Putting-Pedal-to-Metal) |\n| Reliable Robotics | Series B ($25M) | 2020-08 | yes | Pathbreaker, Teamworthy | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/reliable-robotics-operates-aircraft-autonomously-from-takeoff-to-landing) |\n| Arc Boats | Series A | not stated | considered leading | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies) |\n| Arc Boats | Series B | 2023-09 | unknown | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/powering-the-next-wave-of-the-marine-industry-our-series-b-in-arc) |\n| Tandem PV | Series A ($50M equity and debt) | 2025-03 | yes | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/unlocking-us-energy-independence) |\n| Peak Energy | Undisclosed ($108M; GM led) | 2026-10 | no | GM | [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-21/gm-leads-108-million-funding-round-for-sodium-ion-battery-startup) |\n| The Nuclear Company | round not found | | | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/companies/the-nuclear-company) |\n| Symbio Robotics | Series A (inferred; $14.65M); board | 2018-04 | unknown | | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1738149/000173814918000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Symbio Robotics | Series B (inferred; $11.5M sold) | 2020-09 | unknown | | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1738149/000173814920000004/primary_doc.xml) |\n| 6 River Systems | Series A ($15M); board | 2017-05 | unknown | | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1658013/000165801317000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| 6 River Systems | Series B ($25M) | 2018-01 | no | | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1658013/000165801318000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **VulcanForms:** Martin Feldmann (ex-Daimler castings) reached out to Reichow, who had built a castings facility for Tesla. Eclipse wrote a small seed. The first prototype was commissioned within nine months and outperformed every metal printer then on sale, so Eclipse preempted the Series A. Kassekert, his colleague from Cypress and Tesla, later became CEO, and Eclipse co-led the $220M D ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/catalyzing-innovation-and-u-s-economic-growth-our-investment-in-vulcanforms), [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/Putting-Pedal-to-Metal)).\n- **Efficient Computer:** an Eclipse colleague, Justin Selig, found Efficient through a former colleague who had joined as a design engineer ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/pioneering-energy-efficient-computing-our-seed-investment-in-efficient-computer)). Reichow is the partner of record and the public voice: \"Eclipse backed Efficient from the very beginning\" ([src](https://www.efficient.computer/series-b)).\n- **Reliable Robotics:** Reichow watched Robert Rose's team ship Tesla's first Autopilot and says that shaped the decision. Lightspeed led the first round and Eclipse the second ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/reliable-robotics-operates-aircraft-autonomously-from-takeoff-to-landing)).\n- **AheadComputing:** the ex-Intel architects pitched the idea inside Intel and were turned down. Eclipse backed the seed, with Jim Keller investing alongside ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/a-breakthrough-in-processing-power-our-investment-in-aheadcomputing)). The intro path is not stated.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Eclipse | Partner | 2016 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2019/02/06/eclipse-ventures-in-palo-alto-just-added-two-more-big-wheels-as-general-partners) |\n| Tesla | Exec leader, global manufacturing | 2011 to ~2016 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/create-a-meaningful-business-category-and-the-market-will-follow) |\n| SunPower | SVP Operations | dates not found | [src](https://eclipse.capital/team/greg-reichow) |\n| Cypress Semiconductor | Manufacturing and quality roles | dates not found | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/industrial-scaling-playbook-leadership-at-the-next-level) |\n| Jim Keller | Co-director at Tenstorrent and AheadComputing | 2021 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1718995/000089979721000014/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Kevin Kassekert | Colleague at Cypress and Tesla; VulcanForms CEO | Cypress era | [src](https://eclipse.capital/team/greg-reichow) |\n| Robert Rose | Tesla overlap; Reliable Robotics | ~2015 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/reliable-robotics-operates-aircraft-autonomously-from-takeoff-to-landing) |\n| Charly Mwangi | Eclipse partner; source of the five-step method | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-design-for-manufacturing-kills-companies) |\n| Justin Selig | Eclipse colleague who sourced Efficient | 2024 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/pioneering-energy-efficient-computing-our-seed-investment-in-efficient-computer) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who led AheadComputing's unannounced 2026 raises ($47.8M in June, $63.5M in October), and who is Andrew Homan?\n- Is he still on Tenstorrent's board after the 2023–2026 rounds?\n- Was Eclipse in Gimlet's $12M seed, and who sourced Gimlet?\n- Symbio Robotics' outcome; it is not on Eclipse's current company list.\n- SunPower and Cypress dates; his exact Tesla exit date.\n- Any podcast or talk appearances (untested).\n\n## Sources\n- https://eclipse.capital/team/greg-reichow\n- https://eclipse.capital/companies/tenstorrent ; /Gimlet ; /efficient-computer ; /aheadcomputing ; /vulcanforms ; /arc ; /peak-energy ; /tandem-pv ; /the-nuclear-company ; /reliable-robotics\n- https://techcrunch.com/2019/02/06/eclipse-ventures-in-palo-alto-just-added-two-more-big-wheels-as-general-partners\n- Eclipse blog posts cited inline (2019–2026)\n- SEC Form D: Tenstorrent (CIK 1718995), AheadComputing (CIK 2035028), Symbio Robotics (CIK 1738149), 6 River Systems (CIK 1658013)\n- https://www.efficient.computer/series-b\n- AheadComputing press releases (seed, Seed2)\n- https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-a ; https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-b\n", "person:guido-appenzeller": "\n# Guido Appenzeller\n\n## One-line take\na16z's ex-Intel and ex-VMware silicon and networking operator. They co-signed the $475M Unconventional AI seed, led the Netris Series A personally with a board seat, and are the firm's in-house technical judge of whether a chip or networking team can actually take share from NVIDIA and Arista.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win.** They sat inside the incumbents a new AI-silicon or networking company has to beat. The a16z bio lists Intel (CTO of the Data Platforms Group), VMware (CTO of Cloud & Networking) and Big Switch Networks (co-founder) ([src](https://a16z.com/author/guido-appenzeller/)). One a16z podcast page describes the Intel role as CTO of Intel's \"Data Center and AI business unit\" ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/dylan-patel-on-the-ai-chip-race-nvidia-intel-the-us-government-vs-china/)). That background makes them the natural diligence voice when a16z writes nine-figure silicon checks.\n- **Underwriting bar.** A chip has to deliver a step-change, not incremental gains. The Unconventional post (shared byline) says \"step-change performance improvement is necessary to carve out space\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/)). The economic frame behind it is their solo-authored LLMflation essay: cost for an equivalent model falls about 10x a year ([src](https://a16z.com/llmflation-llm-inference-cost/)).\n- **What constrains them.** Most bylines are shared. The silicon deals pair them with Raghu Raghuram (and Bornstein or Xu), and the agent and dev-tool deals pair them with Martin Casado and Yoko Li. Netris is the only deal where a source names them as the person who led ([src](https://netris.io/news/press-release/andreessen-horowitz-leads-netris-series-a-to-accelerate-adoption-of-gpu-network-automation-and-multi-tenancy-across-ai-cloud-operators-worldwide/)). On the big checks they read as the technical voice, not obviously the sole decision-maker.\n- **What they'd likely never do** (my inference, not sourced). Back a \"me-too NVIDIA clone\" or a GPU reseller. Their 2023 co-authored essay calls compute capacity \"a commodity\" ([src](https://a16z.com/navigating-the-high-cost-of-ai-compute/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Netris is their personal deal.** \"Partner Guido Appenzeller led the round and is joining the Netris board.\" Co-signers Raghuram and Cui are not the partner of record ([src](https://netris.io/news/press-release/andreessen-horowitz-leads-netris-series-a-to-accelerate-adoption-of-gpu-network-automation-and-multi-tenancy-across-ai-cloud-operators-worldwide/), 2026-06-25). Their own quoted rationale: \"legacy automation was never built for that.\"\n- **Nexthop: a16z did not lead.** The firm's post calls a16z \"a major investor in Nexthop's $500 million Series B\" ([src](https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop), 2026-03-10). It never claims the lead, so treat claims of an a16z lead as wrong or unresolved. The thesis (shared byline): \"It's why companies like this almost never get started\". This refers to how capital- and talent-intensive AI networking hardware is, and explains why a16z backed an Arista veteran of 15+ years, Anshul Sadana.\n- **Silicon bar.** \"real innovation at the hardware layer is necessary to achieve intelligence at scale.\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/), 2025-12-08, shared with Bornstein and Raghuram)\n- **LLMflation (solo-authored).** \"For an LLM of equivalent performance, the cost is decreasing by 10x every year.\" ([src](https://a16z.com/llmflation-llm-inference-cost/), 2024-11-12). This is their cleanest personal thesis. It implies demand for compute keeps compounding and hardware has to keep jumping.\n- **Compute economics, 2023.** Startups \"spend more than 80% of their total capital raised on compute resources\", and \"Compute capacity on specific hardware is a commodity.\" ([src](https://a16z.com/navigating-the-high-cost-of-ai-compute/), 2023-04-27, shared with Bornstein and Casado)\n- **Policy view.** \"Compute-based thresholds quickly become outdated, since computers become faster over time.\" They argue for revenue-based thresholds to protect Little Tech ([src](https://a16z.com/to-help-ai-startups-compete-use-revenue-based-thresholds/), 2025-05-28, with Matt Perault).\n- **Dev-tool and agent contrarianism** (shared with Li and Casado):\n  - Relace: \"code generation is a parlor trick\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-relace/)).\n  - Runta: \"this isn't yet another sandbox cloud\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-runta/)).\n  - QuiverAI: SVGs are \"also visual code\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-quiverai/)).\n- **Corporate news.** Unconventional AI announced plans to acquire NeuReality on 2026-10-26 ([src](https://unconv.ai/)). The seed-stage AI-silicon lab is already consolidating chip assets within a year of its seed.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** They are a16z's technical gatekeeper on exactly the category in THESIS.md: AI semiconductors and compute, written as very large seeds.\n- **Deals that matter to me:**\n  - **Unconventional AI**, $475M seed, co-led, 2025-12-08. This is the template for the \"$600M seeds for new labs\" check I want to join.\n  - **Nexthop AI**, $500M Series B. AI networking hardware.\n  - **Netris**, $15M Series A. They led it and took the board seat.\n- **Secondary:** Mirelo (an audio foundation model, seed co-lead), QuiverAI (a vector-graphics model, seed lead), Runta (agent infrastructure, seed lead) and Relace (coding-agent infrastructure, Series A lead).\n- **Why they matter to me:** they are the person who will form a16z's technical view on any chip or networking company I'm co-investing in, so their view is worth having early.\n- **Caveat:** they are not a \"rising junior\". This is a lateral senior hire, so the fast-promotion lens doesn't apply. The alpha here is in their technical filter and their founder graph (Naveen Rao, Anshul Sadana), not in being under-the-radar.\n\n## Background\n- Holds a Stanford PhD, and was a Consulting Assistant Professor at Stanford, 2008–2010 ([src](https://a16z.com/author/guido-appenzeller/)).\n- Co-founded Voltage Security and Big Switch Networks. Received a SIGCOMM Test of Time Award (same src).\n- Operating roles: CTO of Cloud & Networking at VMware, Chief Product Officer at Yubico, and CTO of Intel's Data Platforms Group (same src). Years for each move were not found in fetched sources. LinkedIn and Wikipedia did not load.\n- **a16z:** described as \"an investor at Andreessen Horowitz\" on the earliest byline found, 2022-11-16 ([src](https://a16z.com/art-isnt-dead-its-just-machine-generated/)). Called \"a16z partner\" by 2025-09-22 ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/dylan-patel-on-the-ai-chip-race-nvidia-intel-the-us-government-vs-china/)). Exact join date and title-change date were not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/guido-appenzeller/ | a16z author page; about 32 posts and podcasts listed (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.a16z.news/ | Nexthop post is on a16z.news |\n| X | nothing found | handle not verified in this pass |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | not loadable |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | a16z podcast (co-host on chips episodes) | see below |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-03 — a16z — \"DeepSeek, Reasoning Models, and the Future of LLMs\" (video explainer with Marco Mascorro) — [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/deepseek-reasoning-models-and-the-future-of-llms/). No transcript on the page.\n- 2025-09 — a16z — \"Dylan Patel on the AI Chip Race: NVIDIA, Intel & the US Government vs. China\" — [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/dylan-patel-on-the-ai-chip-race-nvidia-intel-the-us-government-vs-china/). No transcript on the page, so no quotes taken.\n- 2026-07 — a16z — \"Can Anyone Catch NVIDIA? The Future of Chips and Infrastructure\" (replay, with Dylan Patel and Erin Price-Wright) — [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/can-anyone-catch-nvidia-the-future-of-chips-and-infrastructure/). No transcript on the page.\n- Also on the author page, undated in the fetch: \"The $3 Trillion AI Coding Opportunity\", \"Google DeepMind Developers: How Nano Banana Was Made\", the Sourcegraph conversation with Beyang Liu, and \"Sovereign AI\" with Anjney Midha.\n\n## How they think\n- **Inference keeps getting cheaper.** Inference cost deflation (\"LLMflation\") is the macro tailwind. Cheaper inference creates more demand, which rewards step-change hardware.\n- **Value sits in infrastructure.** Their January 2023 co-authored map judged infrastructure vendors \"likely the biggest winners\" and found no systemic moats at the app layer ([src](https://a16z.com/who-owns-the-generative-ai-platform/)). Their later deals follow this: chips, networking, agent runtimes.\n- **Contrarian bets:**\n  - Raw code generation is commoditized. The bottleneck is running and merging code (Relace).\n  - Agents need a whole computer, not a sandbox (Runta).\n  - Every video model will ship audio (Mirelo).\n- **Calls, right or wrong:** too early to judge marks. Unconventional's NeuReality acquisition plan (2026-10) shows the company is moving fast.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Welcome to LLMflation](https://a16z.com/llmflation-llm-inference-cost/) (2024-11-12). Solo-authored, and their core economic model.\n2. [Investing in Unconventional](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/) (2025-12-08). The silicon underwriting bar.\n3. [Investing in Nexthop AI](https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop) (2026-03-10). Why AI networking hardware rarely gets founded.\n4. [Navigating the High Cost of AI Compute](https://a16z.com/navigating-the-high-cost-of-ai-compute/) (2023-04-27). Compute as the dominant use of startup capital.\n5. [Who Owns the Generative AI Platform?](https://a16z.com/who-owns-the-generative-ai-platform/) (2023-01-19). Where value accrues.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X was not fetched and no handle was verified. Their public output runs mainly through a16z posts and podcasts, co-written with the Infra team (Casado, Bornstein, Li, Raghuram).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Unconventional AI | Seed ($475M) | 2025-12-08 | Co-lead (other co-lead not named in a16z post); first institutional by construction | a16z co-signers Bornstein, Raghuram | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/) |\n| Mirelo | Seed (undisclosed) | 2025-12-15 | Co-lead; first institutional by construction | a16z co-signer Justine Moore | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirelo/) |\n| QuiverAI | Seed (undisclosed) | 2026-02-25 | Lead | Li, Casado | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-quiverai/) |\n| Nexthop AI | Series B ($500M) | 2026-03-10 | No / disputed (\"a major investor\"); not first in | Raghuram, Xu | [a16z.news](https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop) |\n| Netris | Series A ($15M) | 2026-06-25 | Yes, they personally led and took the board seat; first-in unknown | Raghuram, Cui | [Netris PR](https://netris.io/news/press-release/andreessen-horowitz-leads-netris-series-a-to-accelerate-adoption-of-gpu-network-automation-and-multi-tenancy-across-ai-cloud-operators-worldwide/) |\n| Runta | Seed (undisclosed) | 2026-07-16 | Lead | Casado, Li | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-runta/) |\n| Relace | Series A ($23M) | 2025-10-08 | Yes (a16z board seat); not first in, since YC was already involved | Matrix Partners, Y Combinator; co-signers Li, Casado | [Relace](https://www.relace.ai/blog/series-a-announcement) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Unconventional AI.** a16z co-led a $475M seed for Naveen Rao. Rao's prior exits were Nervana to Intel and Mosaic to Databricks ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/)). The intro path was not found. Both Rao and Appenzeller have Intel in their history, but no source ties the two together, so treat it as an open question.\n- **Netris.** Appenzeller led the round and joined the board. The founders a16z names are Alex Saroyan, Arsen Arakelyan and Tigran Martirosyan ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-netris/)). How a16z met them was not found.\n- **Nexthop.** a16z joined as \"a major investor\" behind Anshul Sadana, who spent 15+ years at Arista ([src](https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop)). Who led was not resolved here. The roster row says some outlets name Lightspeed as lead, but that is unverified.\n- **How they source** (my read from byline patterns, not a sourced statement). There appear to be two lanes:\n  - Silicon and networking with Raghu Raghuram, the ex-VMware CEO, in a VMware-era networking circle that also includes the Big Switch background.\n  - Agent and dev-tool infrastructure with Casado and Li.\n\n  Podcasts with SemiAnalysis's Dylan Patel suggest they track the chip supply chain closely.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Martin Casado (a16z) | Co-author on Runta, QuiverAI, Relace and the 2022–23 gen-AI essays | 2022 | [src](https://a16z.com/author/guido-appenzeller/) |\n| Yoko Li (a16z) | Co-author on Runta, QuiverAI, Relace | 2022 | [src](https://a16z.com/author/guido-appenzeller/) |\n| Raghu Raghuram (a16z) | Co-signer on Unconventional, Nexthop, Netris | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/) |\n| Matt Bornstein (a16z) | Co-signer on Unconventional; long-time co-author | 2022 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/) |\n| Jason Cui (a16z) | Co-signer on Netris | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-netris/) |\n| Shangda Xu (a16z) | Co-signer on Nexthop | 2026 | [src](https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop) |\n| Justine Moore (a16z) | Co-signer on Mirelo | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirelo/) |\n| Naveen Rao | Backed with a $475M seed co-lead | 2025-12 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/) |\n| Netris | Board member | 2026-06 | [src](https://netris.io/news/press-release/andreessen-horowitz-leads-netris-series-a-to-accelerate-adoption-of-gpu-network-automation-and-multi-tenancy-across-ai-cloud-operators-worldwide/) |\n| Dylan Patel (SemiAnalysis) | Recurring podcast conversation | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/dylan-patel-on-the-ai-chip-race-nvidia-intel-the-us-government-vs-china/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who was the other co-lead on the Unconventional AI $475M seed, and what was the valuation? Searches were exhausted this pass, so it is unverified.\n- Who led the Nexthop $500M Series B (a16z vs. Lightspeed), and who was in at seed or Series A?\n- Who was the other co-lead on the Mirelo seed, and how big was the round?\n- What is their exact a16z join date and title history? Was \"partner\" a promotion or the entry title?\n- Do the Intel-era links (Rao's Nervana went to Intel) explain the Unconventional deal flow?\n- Pull transcripts of the Dylan Patel episodes for verbatim views on NVIDIA and custom silicon.\n- Verify their X handle and posting pattern.\n- What are the terms of the Unconventional–NeuReality acquisition, and does it imply a follow-on round?\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/guido-appenzeller/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/\n- https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-netris/\n- https://netris.io/news/press-release/andreessen-horowitz-leads-netris-series-a-to-accelerate-adoption-of-gpu-network-automation-and-multi-tenancy-across-ai-cloud-operators-worldwide/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirelo/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-runta/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-quiverai/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-relace/\n- https://www.relace.ai/blog/series-a-announcement\n- https://unconv.ai/\n- https://a16z.com/llmflation-llm-inference-cost/\n- https://a16z.com/navigating-the-high-cost-of-ai-compute/\n- https://a16z.com/who-owns-the-generative-ai-platform/\n- https://a16z.com/to-help-ai-startups-compete-use-revenue-based-thresholds/\n- https://a16z.com/art-isnt-dead-its-just-machine-generated/\n- https://a16z.com/ai-canon/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/dylan-patel-on-the-ai-chip-race-nvidia-intel-the-us-government-vs-china/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/can-anyone-catch-nvidia-the-future-of-chips-and-infrastructure/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/deepseek-reasoning-models-and-the-future-of-llms/\n- Secondary (repo): data/rows/roster-andreessen-horowitz-deals.csv (Nexthop lead dispute, co-investor list)\n", "person:guillermo-rauch": "\n# Guillermo Rauch\n\n## One-line take\nThe Vercel CEO is the most frequent founder-angel in AI infrastructure and developer tools: 30-plus sourced AI cheques, usually at seed, often in companies that build on or sell through Vercel.\n\n## Crux\nHe wins because the product is the deal flow: AI founders build on Vercel, then ask the CEO for advice, and a cheque follows. He adds distribution (Vercel's developer audience and marketplace) more than capital. He is constrained by attention, which he names as the real cost of angel investing, and by cheque size: he is a name on the cap table, not a round-maker, with Inngest's seed the one sourced exception. He does not run a fund; no Form D, scout programme or syndicate was found.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Deal flow is inbound from the platform: \"A lot of these companies and entrepreneurs are building on Vercel\" ([src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/the-founders-writing-checks-into), 2024-10-31).\n- He also cold-emails. Newcomer says \"that's how he got connected to Scale AI during the company's seed round\" ([src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/the-founders-writing-checks-into)).\n- His own words on that cheque: \"I invested in this company called scale AI\" ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/guillermo-rauch-why-great-companies-are-defined-by-how-many-things-they-say-no-t), 20VC, 2023-10-06; auto-generated transcript).\n- On screening: \"you can lose a lot of deals if you just look for expertise sometimes\" (same episode).\n- On the cost: \"I think people underestimate just how much their own time goes into thinking\" (same episode).\n- He prices rounds when he wants to: Inngest's $3M seed was co-led by him with Glenn Solomon and Tom Preston-Werner, and the Series A post still lists \"continued participation from A16z, Notable, Afore, and Guillermo Rauch\" ([src](https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-inngest-series-a)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. He is in the seed or first round of several names that matter here: Conway Research (2026), Wafer (AI chips tooling, Series A 2026), Luminal, SkyPilot, fal, Browserbase, Braintrust, Odyssey, Moonlake, Black Forest Labs. He is a useful co-investor signal and a route to AI-infra founders, not a competitor for allocation.\n\n## Background\n- Founder and CEO of Vercel (formerly ZEIT); creator of Next.js and Socket.IO ([src](https://rauchg.com/about)).\n- Earlier startups Cloudup and LearnBoost; Cloudup was sold to Automattic (WordPress) ([src](https://rauchg.com/about)).\n- Angel investing dates back at least to Auth0 and Scale AI's seed ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/guillermo-rauch-why-great-companies-are-defined-by-how-many-things-they-say-no-t)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://rauchg.com | Essays; no investments page (rauchg.com/investments returns 404 as of 2026-11-11) |\n| Substack / blog | https://rauchg.com | |\n| X | https://x.com/rauchg | Main channel; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/rauchg | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-10 — 20VC E1069 — \"Why Great Companies are Defined by How Many Things They Say No To\" — [link](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/guillermo-rauch-why-great-companies-are-defined-by-how-many-things-they-say-no-t) — names Scale AI and Auth0 as investments; market first, then founder fit.\n- 2024-10 — Newcomer — \"The Founders Writing Checks Into AI Startups\" — [link](https://www.newcomer.co/p/the-founders-writing-checks-into) — first of 13 founder-angels profiled; rest is paywalled.\n- 2024-11 — Kindred Ventures Lunch & Learn — [link](https://kindredventures.com/events/kindred-lunch-learn-guillermo-rauch-founder-ceo-vercel/) — not read.\n\n## How they think\n- Market first: \"ultimately it's all about the market right\" (20VC, same link).\n- Founder must be unusually well placed for that market; pure domain expertise is not the filter.\n- On AI returns in 2023 he expected a minority of bets to pay: \"I think we will see a lot of positive Roi\" (20VC, same link).\n- Regret named publicly: admired Stripe early and did not invest (20VC).\n\n## Read these first\n- 20VC E1069 quick-fire round (after 1:06:26): the only place found where he talks about angel investing directly.\n- Newcomer's founder-angel piece: how deals reach him.\n- Inngest's seed and Series A posts: what it looks like when he leads and keeps following.\n\n## Social engagement\nPosts mostly about Vercel, AI SDK, v0 and frontend performance on X; individual posts were not read for this profile (X not fetched). Open item.\n\n## Portfolio\nSourced cheques only. **(early)** marks pre-seed and seed entries. Rows marked \"Already in rounds ledger\" in the row file were in the repo before this pass. Directories count 113 (CB Insights) to 176 (PitchBook) total investments, most of them non-AI developer tools ([src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/guillermo-rauch)).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Black Forest Labs | Series B | 2025-12-01 | no | not listed | [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier) |\n| Braintrust | Pre-seed **(early)** | 2023 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round) |\n| Braintrust | Series A | 2024-10-08 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/announcing-series-a) |\n| Browserbase | Seed **(early)** | 2024-06-06 | no | not listed | [src](https://venturebeat.com/ai/exclusive-browserbase-launches-headless-browser-platform-that-lets-llms-automate-web-tasks) |\n| Chroma | Seed ($18M) **(early)** | 2023-04-07 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.trychroma.com/blog/seed) |\n| fal | Series A | 2024-09-18 | no | not listed | [src](https://blog.fal.ai/generative-media-needs-speed-fal-has-raised-23m-to-accelerate/) |\n| Inngest | Seed ($3M) **(early)** | 2023-07-12 | co-led | not listed | [src](https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-inngest-seed-financing) |\n| Inngest | Seed extension ($6.1M) **(early)** | 2024-01-30 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-funding-from-a16z) |\n| Inngest | Series A ($21M) | 2025-09-16 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-inngest-series-a) |\n| LastMile AI | Seed ($10M) **(early)** | 2023-09-14 | no | not listed | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/09/14/lastmile-ai-closes-10m-seed-round-to-operationalize-ai-models/) |\n| LiveKit | Series A ($22.5M at ~$110M) | 2024-06-04 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.forbesargentina.com/innovacion/crean-sistema-nervioso-ia-su-startup-ya-vale-us-110-millones-quienes-son-hacen-n54253/amp) |\n| Luminal | Seed ($5.3M) **(early)** | 2025-11-17 | no | not listed | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/17/luminal-raises-5-3-million-to-build-a-better-gpu-code-framework/) |\n| Melius | Seed + Series A (announced together, $25M total) **(early)** | 2026-10-06 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.melius.com/blog/melius-seed-and-series-a) |\n| Moonlake AI | Seed ($28M) **(early)** | 2025-10-01 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.enterpriseaiworld.com/Articles/News/News/Moonlake-AI-Emerges-from-Stealth-with-%2428M-Seed-to-Support-Vibe-Coding-Interactive-Worlds-171928.aspx) |\n| Natural | Series A | 2026-07-20 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.natural.com/blog/natural-series-a) |\n| Odyssey AI | Seed ($9M; Air Street first check late 2023) **(early)** | 2024-07-08 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.fenwick.com/insights/experience/fenwick-represents-odyssey-systems-in-9m-seed-funding) |\n| Outtake | Series B ($40M) | 2026-01-28 | no | not listed | [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/outtake-raises-40m-series-b-163000387.html) |\n| Perplexity | Series B | 2024-01-04 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-raises-series-b-funding-round) |\n| Phonic | Series A | 2026-10-29 | no | not listed | [src](https://phonic.ai/blog/phonic-1) |\n| PlayerZero | Seed ($5M; disclosed with Series A) **(early)** | 2025-07-30 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.fenwick.com/insights/experience/fenwick-represents-playerzero-in-20m-financing) |\n| Railway | Series A ($20M) | 2022-05-31 | no | not listed | [src](https://blog.railway.com/p/series-a) |\n| Roboflow | Series B | 2024-11-19 | no | not listed | [src](https://roboflow.com/blog/series-b) |\n| SkyPilot | Seed ($20M+) **(early)** | 2026-07-21 | no | not listed | [src](https://skypilot.ai/blog/skypilot-the-company) |\n| Substrate.run | Seed ($8M) **(early)** | 2024-06-20 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20240620159165/en) |\n| Substrate.run | Undisclosed (backers listed after rebrand to Zo Computer) | not found | no | not listed | [src](https://www.zo.computer/) |\n| Superlogical | Seed **(early)** | 2026-07-29 | no | not listed | [src](https://notablecap.com/blog/backing-mitchell-hashimoto-again-12-years-later) |\n| Wafer | Series A ($40M) | 2026-09-01 | no | not listed | [src](https://pulse2.com/wafer-raises-40-million-series-a-to-scale-ai-inference-optimization-platform/) |\n| Conway Research | Seed (first round) **(early)** | 2026-10-02 | no | not listed | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=3174503) |\n| Scale AI | Seed (2016; invested after YC) **(early)** | 2016 | no | Y Combinator | [src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/the-founders-writing-checks-into) |\n| Simile | Series A ($100M) | 2026-02-12 | no | Index Ventures (lead); Andrej Karpathy; Fei-Fei Li; Adam D'Angelo; Scott Belsky | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/life-the-universe-and-simile-leading-similes-series-a/) |\n| Flora | Series A ($42M) | 2026-01-27 | no | Redpoint Ventures (lead); Menlo Ventures; Justin Kan; Cyan Banister | [src](https://pulse2.com/flora-42-million-series-a/) |\n| Yutori | Seed ($15M) **(early)** | 2025-03-27 | no | Radical Ventures (lead); Felicis; Elad Gil; Sarah Guo; Jeff Dean; Fei-Fei Li; Amjad Masad | [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/yutori-launches-stealth-15m-seed-funding-build-consumer-ai-assistants) |\n| Zo Computer | Seed **(early)** | 2024 | no | Lightspeed; Decibel; Craft; Patrick Collison; John Collison; Clem Delangue | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/zo/) |\n| Applied Labs | Seed ($4.2M) **(early)** | 2025-01 | no | Abstract (lead); Point72 Ventures; Outlander; Tetra; Akshat Bubna; Ali Rowghani | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/guillermo-rauch) |\n| Lightfield | Series A ($47M) | 2026-09-09 | no | Andreessen Horowitz; Coatue; Greylock; Lightspeed; Maverick Capital; Scott Belsky; Mati Staniszewski; Michael Grinich | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/guillermo-rauch) |\n| Auth0 | Undisclosed (early; 'one of my earliest') | not found | no | not listed | [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/guillermo-rauch-why-great-companies-are-defined-by-how-many-things-they-say-no-t) |\n| Resend | Series A ($18M) | 2024-12-04 | no | Andreessen Horowitz; Y Combinator; Basecase; Michael Grinich; Ali Rowghani | [src](https://resend.com/blog/series-a) |\n\nAggregator-only and unconfirmed: Supabase, Replit, Warp, Convex ([src](https://www.pipeseed.com/investor/guillermo-rauch-954386)); Pocket ($11M, 2026-06-29) and Tonada (pre-seed, 2026-06-17) ([src](https://aifunding.me/investors/guillermo-rauch)); e2e pre-seed (2026-09-15, CB Insights); Amie seed ([src](https://neuronfeed.com/investors/guillermo-rauch)).\n\n**Hit pattern.** Early cheques that became hot: Scale AI (seed), Perplexity (Series B, 2024), fal, Browserbase, LiveKit, Braintrust (pre-seed), Black Forest Labs. Publicly regretted pass: Stripe.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Scale AI (seed).** Cold outreach during the seed; invested after the team finished YC ([src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/the-founders-writing-checks-into)).\n- **Inngest.** Co-led the $3M seed in July 2023, then followed in the a16z-led extension (January 2024) and the $21M Series A (September 2025) ([src](https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-inngest-series-a)).\n- **Braintrust.** In the pre-seed with Howie Liu and Jack Altman, all listed by operating title ([src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round)); followed in the Series A.\n- **Black Forest Labs.** Joined the $300M Series B in December 2025; Vercel is a BFL customer (repo ledger note).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jeff Dean | Repeat angel co-investor: Moonlake, SkyPilot, Roboflow, Yutori, Wafer | 2024 | [src](https://skypilot.ai/blog/skypilot-the-company) |\n| Amjad Masad | Repeat angel co-investor: Chroma, Roboflow, SkyPilot, Yutori | 2023 | [src](https://www.trychroma.com/blog/seed) |\n| Scott Belsky | Repeat angel co-investor: Simile, Extra, Lightfield | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/life-the-universe-and-simile-leading-similes-series-a/) |\n| a16z infrastructure team | The lead most often beside him: Inngest extension and A, Resend A, Clerk, Lightfield A | 2023 | [src](https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-inngest-series-a) |\n| Howie Liu, Jack Altman | Braintrust pre-seed and seed | 2023 | [src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round) |\n| Glenn Solomon (GGV / Notable), Tom Preston-Werner | Co-led Inngest seed | 2023-07 | [src](https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-inngest-series-a) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is there a vehicle (LLC or family office) behind the larger cheques in Black Forest Labs and Perplexity? None found.\n- Which cheques came through Vercel customers versus cold outreach? Only Scale AI is sourced.\n- X posts on investing were not read.\n- The paywalled part of the Newcomer piece may list his AI count.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.newcomer.co/p/the-founders-writing-checks-into\n- https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/guillermo-rauch-why-great-companies-are-defined-by-how-many-things-they-say-no-t (auto-generated transcript)\n- https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-inngest-series-a\n- https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round\n- https://rauchg.com/about\n- https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/guillermo-rauch (aggregator)\n- https://signal.nfx.com/investors/guillermo-rauch (aggregator)\n- https://neuronfeed.com/investors/guillermo-rauch (aggregator)\n- Repo rounds and deals ledgers for the rows marked \"Already in rounds ledger\"\n", "person:guru-chahal": "\n# Guru Chahal\n\n## One-line take\nHe is Lightspeed's AI-silicon and compute partner: an ex-networking founder who led Nexthop AI (A and B), co-led Naveen Rao's $475M Unconventional AI seed, and led Ricursive's $300M A. His edge comes from long operator relationships, not from being first into labs.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He spent about five years as fourth co-founder of Avi Networks before returning to Lightspeed in 2019 ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-investors-gaurav-gupta-and-guru-chahal-to-lightspeed/)). That gives him operator trust with infrastructure founders. Nexthop came from a relationship he says Lightspeed held \"for over a decade\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/nexthop-ai-next-generation-networking-for-an-ai-driven-world/)). He also has Lightspeed's balance sheet behind him: he can lead a $110M A, a $300M A or a $500M B.\n- **What constrains him.** On the frontier-lab deals he is usually not first and not alone.\n  - Ricursive: Sequoia led the seed, and Lightspeed came in at the A under two months later at $4B ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/16/how-ricursive-intelligence-raised-335m-at-a-4b-valuation-in-4-months/)).\n  - Anthropic: Lightspeed's entry was the 2024 Series D ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/anthropic/)).\n  - Almost every big AI memo is co-signed with Ravi Mhatre, Lightspeed's co-founder. Nexthop is the one memo he signs alone.\n- **What he would not do.** Nothing stated outright. His pattern suggests he won't skip an energy- or efficiency-first thesis for a pure scaling bet: on Unconventional AI he wrote \"We’re scaling on power and cost, not efficiency.\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-unconventional-ai-biology-scale-efficiency-for-the-ai-era/)). This is my inference.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Nexthop was a 10-year relationship.** \"We've known Anshul Sadana, founder of Nexthop AI, for over a decade\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/nexthop-ai-next-generation-networking-for-an-ai-driven-world/), 2025-03-25). He is the sole author of that memo; his other AI memos are co-signed. Lightspeed then led the $500M Series B at $4.2B ([src](https://nexthop.ai/news-and-event/nexthop-ai-accelerates-into-hypergrowth-with-oversubscribed-500m-series-b-funding-catapulting-the-companys-valuation-to-4-2-billion/), 2026-03-10).\n- **The networking thesis predates Nexthop by five years.** His 2020 personal post argued network infrastructure was investable again, citing cheap merchant silicon, 5G and multi-cloud: \"the network is the lifeblood of our economy\" ([Medium feed](https://medium.com/feed/@guruchahal), 2020-07-27).\n- **Decides fast when the team is rare.** On Warp (Kevin Weil and DJ Patil's robotic chemistry lab): \"we committed to working with them in the very first meeting\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/why-were-backing-warp/), 2026-10-22).\n- **Exa: originate at A, then follow behind bigger leads.**\n  - Led the $17M A (2024-07-16, [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/exa-redesigning-search-for-ai/)).\n  - Followed in Benchmark's $85M B (2025-09-03, [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/search-perfected-for-ai-why-were-doubling-down-on-exa/)).\n  - Followed in a16z's $250M C (2026-05-20, [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/tripling-down-on-exa-to-build-the-search-engine-for-ai/)).\n\n  He co-wrote all three memos with Nnamdi Iregbulem. The 2024 thesis: \"AI agents don't need to see ads, they need to see results.\"\n- **Writes research, not just deal memos.** With Anushka De he argues multi-agent RL during training may be the next scaling axis, and that reward is the blocker: \"The unsolved juggernaut appears to be signal.\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/collective-model-intelligence-the-next-scaling-axis/), 2026-09-18).\n- **A customer-first operator.** At Avi he wrote \"I've had a chance to speak with over 150 customers about our technology\" ([Medium feed](https://medium.com/feed/@guruchahal); the feed carries a 2020 date, but the content was written while he was at Avi).\n- **Admits a miss, then pays to fix it.** On Cylake's $245M round, which Lightspeed led, the memo says of Palo Alto Networks: \"We didn't get to partner with Nir then. Today we fixed that.\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-cylake/), 2026-09-08). This is the same pattern as Ricursive and Anthropic: Lightspeed buys back into a founder or a lab at size rather than being first.\n- **Nominal: a quiet triple-up into hardware-engineering AI.** (Added 2026-11-10.)\n  - Lightspeed entered at the Series B in 2025, with Chahal, Mhatre and Cader listed ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/nominal/)).\n  - It followed in Founders Fund's $80M B-2 at $1B (2026-03; [secondary src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-nominal-hits-1b-founders), per another repo row).\n  - It now leads the C. The memo, by Chahal, Duesterhoeft and Cader, is stamped 2026-11-09: \"Every machine is a test stand\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/hardware-engineering-gets-its-intelligence-layer/)).\n\n  Other repo profiles tag Nominal as non-AI hardware-test software. Chahal's memo recasts it as an agentic AI company: \"A general-purpose LLM simply does not have that context.\"\n- **A second, small-check seed book that press misses.** (Added 2026-11-10.) Lightspeed company pages list him on 2024-25 AI seeds:\n  - Straiker (Seed 2024, with Bucky Moore; [src](https://lsvp.com/company/straiker/))\n  - Zo Computer (Seed 2024, with Iregbulem; [src](https://lsvp.com/company/zo/))\n  - Big Sur AI (Seed 2024, alone; [src](https://lsvp.com/company/big-sur-ai/))\n  - Virtue AI (Seed 2025) and Nuance Labs (Seed 2025)\n\n  So he is not only a $100M+ check writer. He also seeds agent and AI-security companies, usually paired with a junior or newer partner.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is the Lightspeed partner closest to AI chips and compute:\n- Unconventional AI seed: $475M at $4.5B, co-led by Lightspeed and a16z.\n- Ricursive (AI chip design): $300M A.\n- Nexthop AI (AI networking): A and B.\n- Alif Semiconductor: Series C.\n- Fireworks AI: C co-lead.\n- Anthropic: D and E.\n\n**The seed co-lead pattern is the one to watch.** Unconventional AI shows Lightspeed will co-lead a $475M seed for a new hardware lab. That is the same check shape as your ~$20M slot in a mega-seed, so he is a natural co-investor or allocation source on lab seeds.\n\n**Calibration note on the \"principal / fast promotion\" bucket.** He does not fit it. He is a 2012-vintage investor with an operator detour, not a fast-promoted junior. The alpha here is domain depth (networking and silicon), not under-the-radar status. Treat his juniors and co-signers as the alpha hunt instead: Anushka De, and possibly Nnamdi Iregbulem and James Alcorn.\n\n## Background\n- Before 2012: his startup Nuova Systems was acquired by Cisco ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/guru-chahal/)). He held product management, marketing, strategy and early sales roles (same source).\n- Early 2012: joined Lightspeed as an investor and worked on Zscaler, Avere Systems and Avi Networks ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-investors-gaurav-gupta-and-guru-chahal-to-lightspeed/)).\n- About five years at Avi Networks as fourth co-founder. VMware acquired Avi in 2019 ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/guru-chahal/)).\n- 2019-06-26: Lightspeed announced his return as an enterprise investor, in a post by Arif Janmohamed and Ravi Mhatre ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-investors-gaurav-gupta-and-guru-chahal-to-lightspeed/)).\n- Now Partner, Menlo Park. Focus: security, DevOps, observability, cloud infrastructure and AI ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/guru-chahal/)).\n- Education: nothing found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://lsvp.com/team-member/guru-chahal/ | Firm bio and portfolio |\n| Substack / blog | https://guruchahal.medium.com/ | Personal Medium; last post on the feed is 2023-02; recent writing is on lsvp.com/stories |\n| X | https://twitter.com/guruchahal | Linked from firm bio; not read (fetch blocked), follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/guruchahal/ | Linked from firm bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The web-search budget ran out during this run, so podcasts and talks were not searched. This is a gap, not evidence that none exist.\n\n## How they think\n- **Infrastructure transitions reopen markets for incumbents' challengers.** He made this case for networking in 2020 (merchant silicon, multi-cloud) and again for Nexthop in 2025: \"Current networking technology has not kept up with the growth of the cloud\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/nexthop-ai-next-generation-networking-for-an-ai-driven-world/)).\n- **Efficiency over brute scaling** in AI hardware (Unconventional AI). Recursive hardware and software co-design (Ricursive): \"a recursive loop between hardware and software\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-ricursive-intelligence-ai-for-chip-design-and-chip-design-for-ai/)).\n- **Agents become the main consumers of the web and of infrastructure** (Exa, Judgment Labs, Virtue AI, A Security).\n- **Calls so far.**\n  - Exa: rose from a $17M A to a $250M C.\n  - Nexthop: A to a $4.2B B within a year.\n  - Ricursive: priced at $4B within two months of seed.\n\n  All are unrealized. Earlier exits: Zscaler (IPO), Avere (Microsoft) and Avi (VMware) ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/guru-chahal/)).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Nexthop AI: Next-generation Networking for an AI-driven World](https://lsvp.com/stories/nexthop-ai-next-generation-networking-for-an-ai-driven-world/): his only solo AI memo, and it shows the relationship-sourcing model.\n- [Investing in Unconventional AI](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-unconventional-ai-biology-scale-efficiency-for-the-ai-era/): the AI-hardware efficiency thesis behind a $475M seed.\n- [Collective Model Intelligence: The Next Scaling Axis?](https://lsvp.com/stories/collective-model-intelligence-the-next-scaling-axis/): research-level thinking on multi-agent RL.\n- [Why We're Backing Warp](https://lsvp.com/stories/why-were-backing-warp/): a decision in the first meeting.\n- [Four macro trends driving network infrastructure investments](https://guruchahal.medium.com/four-macro-trends-driving-network-infrastructure-investments-6404116243c) (2020): the seed of the Nexthop thesis.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked: the X fetch was not attempted and web search was exhausted. Unknown.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ricursive Intelligence | Series A $300M, $4B post | 2026-01-26 | yes (Lightspeed) | DST, NVentures, Felicis, 49 Palms, Radical AI, Sequoia; LS co-signers Mhatre, Cader | [src](https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/) |\n| Unconventional AI | Seed $475M, $4.5B | 2025-12-08 | co-lead with a16z | Sequoia, Lux, DCVC, Future Ventures, Jeff Bezos; LS co-signers Mhatre, Moore | [src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/) |\n| Nexthop AI | Series A $110M | 2025-03-25 | yes | not named; LS Mhatre on company page | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/nexthop-ai-next-generation-networking-for-an-ai-driven-world/) |\n| Nexthop AI | Series B $500M, $4.2B | 2026-03-10 | yes | a16z, Altimeter, existing investors | [src](https://nexthop.ai/news-and-event/nexthop-ai-accelerates-into-hypergrowth-with-oversubscribed-500m-series-b-funding-catapulting-the-companys-valuation-to-4-2-billion/) |\n| Fireworks AI | Series C $250M, $4B | 2025-10-28 | co-lead | Index, Evantic (co-leads), Sequoia | [src](https://fireworks.ai/news/fireworks-ai-raises-250m-series-c) |\n| Anthropic | Series D | 2024 | no | LS Mhatre, Duesterhoeft | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/anthropic/) |\n| Anthropic | Series E $3.5B, $61.5B post | 2025-03-03 | yes | memo co-signed Mhatre (first author), Duesterhoeft | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-announces-lead-investment-in-anthropics-3-5b-series-e-financing/) |\n| Exa | Series A $17M | 2024-07-16 | yes | NVIDIA Ventures, YC; co-author Iregbulem | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/exa-redesigning-search-for-ai/) |\n| Exa | Series B $85M | 2025-09-03 | no (Benchmark) | YC, NVIDIA Ventures | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/search-perfected-for-ai-why-were-doubling-down-on-exa/) |\n| Exa | Series C $250M | 2026-05-20 | no (a16z) | Benchmark, YC, NVIDIA Ventures | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/tripling-down-on-exa-to-build-the-search-engine-for-ai/) |\n| Warp (Warp Sciences) | Series A | 2026-10-22 | yes | LS Mhatre, Shelley Chu, Jonathan MacQuitty | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/why-were-backing-warp/) |\n| Cylake | $245M financing | 2026-09-08 | yes | memo with Mhatre | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-cylake/) |\n| Sandstone | Series A $30M | 2026-06-09 | yes | memo with Lisa Han | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/the-operating-system-for-in-house-legal-our-series-a-in-sandstone/) |\n| A Security | not stated | 2026-06-08 | unknown | memo with Tal Morgenstern | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/outgunning-ai-weaponization-our-investment-in-a-security/) |\n| Alif Semiconductor | Series C | year not given | unknown | LS Janmohamed, Ephrati, Aggarwal | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/alif-semiconductor/) |\n| Unsloth | not given | not given | unknown | LS Alcorn, Iregbulem, Mohapatra | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/unsloth/) |\n| Augment | Series B | 2024 | unknown | LS Mhatre | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/augment/) |\n| Pika | Series A | 2023 | unknown | LS Lisa Han | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/pika/) |\n| Virtue AI | Seed | 2025 | unknown | LS Alcorn | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/virtue-ai/) |\n| Nuance Labs | Seed | 2025 | unknown | LS Iregbulem | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/nuance-labs/) |\n| Judgment Labs | Series A | 2026 | unknown | LS Alcorn | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/judgment-labs/) |\n| Omnifold | Series A | 2025 | unknown | LS Janmohamed, Raviraj Jain | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/omnifold/) |\n| Tennr | Series B | year not given | unknown | LS Imanbayev, Matinfar, Fargnoli | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/tennr/) |\n| Nominal | Series B | 2025 | unknown | LS Mhatre, Cader | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/nominal/) |\n| Nominal | Series B-2 ($80M at $1B) | 2026-03-05 | no (Founders Fund) | Sequoia, Lux, General Catalyst, Red Glass | [secondary](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-nominal-hits-1b-founders) |\n| Nominal | Series C | 2026-11-09 | yes | memo with Duesterhoeft, Cader | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/hardware-engineering-gets-its-intelligence-layer/) |\n| Straiker | Seed | 2024 | unknown | LS Bucky Moore, Janmohamed | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/straiker/) |\n| Zo Computer | Seed | 2024 | unknown | LS Iregbulem | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/zo/) |\n| Big Sur AI | Seed | 2024 | unknown (sole LS name) | none listed | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/big-sur-ai/) |\n| Eve (legal AI) | Series A | 2024 | unknown (a16z announced its own A lead 2025-01) | LS Ephrati, Mhatre | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/eve/) |\n| Ketryx | Series A | 2023 | unknown | LS Janmohamed, Raviraj Jain | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/ketryx/) |\n| Tana | not given | not given | unknown | LS Iregbulem | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/tana/) |\n\nHis team page lists 42 companies as of 2026-11-10. Ones not covered above: Aril Inc, Chaos Labs, Clutch, Infiot, Origami Logic and Rocksalt (Seed 2025). Their pages 404'd or showed no AI angle. Non-AI and older holdings on the firm page include Zscaler, Avere, Avi, Pensando, Noname Security, Cyera, Chainguard, Descope, ngrok and Shortwave ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/guru-chahal/)).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Nexthop AI.** Lightspeed knew Anshul Sadana, who spent 17 years at Arista, for over a decade. When he started Nexthop in 2024, \"it was clear we wanted to back Sadana\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/nexthop-ai-next-generation-networking-for-an-ai-driven-world/)). Lightspeed led the $110M A and then the $500M B. The B came about a year later, with a16z and Altimeter in.\n- **Ricursive Intelligence: not first in.** Sequoia led the $35M seed. Lightspeed led the $300M A at $4B less than two months after launch ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/16/how-ricursive-intelligence-raised-335m-at-a-4b-valuation-in-4-months/), [aijourn](https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/)). How Lightspeed met Anna Goldie and Azalia Mirhoseini is not stated.\n- **Unconventional AI.** Lightspeed and a16z are named as leads of the $475M seed. Rao is putting in $10M personally ([src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/)). Chahal is first author of Lightspeed's memo. Sequoia's site claims a Pre-Seed/Seed partnership in 2025, so whether Lightspeed was first in is contested.\n- **Anthropic.** Lightspeed first engaged in January 2023, when Anthropic had fewer than 100 staff. It took a Series D allocation in 2024 and led the E in 2025 ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-announces-lead-investment-in-anthropics-3-5b-series-e-financing/)). Mhatre is first author, so Chahal's personal role is likely secondary.\n- **Warp.** Lightspeed committed in the first meeting and led the A ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/why-were-backing-warp/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ravi Mhatre | Co-signer on Anthropic, Unconventional, Ricursive, Warp, Cylake; co-wrote his return post | 2019 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-investors-gaurav-gupta-and-guru-chahal-to-lightspeed/) |\n| Nnamdi Iregbulem | Co-author on all three Exa memos; Nuance Labs, Unsloth | 2024 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/exa-redesigning-search-for-ai/) |\n| James Alcorn | Virtue AI, Judgment Labs, Unsloth | 2025 | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/virtue-ai/) |\n| Jonah Cader | Ricursive A co-author | 2026 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-ricursive-intelligence-ai-for-chip-design-and-chip-design-for-ai/) |\n| Bucky Moore | Unconventional AI co-author | 2025 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-unconventional-ai-biology-scale-efficiency-for-the-ai-era/) |\n| Anushka De | Research memo co-author | 2026 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/collective-model-intelligence-the-next-scaling-axis/) |\n| Arif Janmohamed | Wrote his return post; Alif, Omnifold | 2019 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-investors-gaurav-gupta-and-guru-chahal-to-lightspeed/) |\n| Anshul Sadana (Nexthop) | Known 10+ years | before 2015 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/nexthop-ai-next-generation-networking-for-an-ai-driven-world/) |\n| Avi Networks | Fourth co-founder | until 2019 | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/guru-chahal/) |\n| Nuova Systems | Prior startup (acquired by Cisco) | before 2012 | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/guru-chahal/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What was his title on returning in 2019, and when was he made Partner? Neither post states it.\n- How did Lightspeed source Ricursive? Was it Chahal, or Cader on the growth side?\n- Did Lightspeed write the first check into Unconventional AI, or did Sequoia's Pre-Seed?\n- Exa seed: was Lightspeed in before the A?\n- Unsloth, Alif and Pika: round, date and whether Lightspeed led.\n- Anushka De's role at Lightspeed.\n- Podcasts, talks and X: still not searched. On 2026-11-10 web search was exhausted again and x.com returned HTTP 402.\n- Nominal C: the amount, the co-investors, and whether Lightspeed led the 2025 B are all unknown.\n- Straiker, Zo and Big Sur seeds: did he lead them? The company pages don't say.\n\n## Sources\n- https://lsvp.com/team-member/guru-chahal/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-investors-gaurav-gupta-and-guru-chahal-to-lightspeed/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/nexthop-ai-next-generation-networking-for-an-ai-driven-world/\n- https://nexthop.ai/news-and-event/nexthop-ai-accelerates-into-hypergrowth-with-oversubscribed-500m-series-b-funding-catapulting-the-companys-valuation-to-4-2-billion/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-ricursive-intelligence-ai-for-chip-design-and-chip-design-for-ai/\n- https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/16/how-ricursive-intelligence-raised-335m-at-a-4b-valuation-in-4-months/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-unconventional-ai-biology-scale-efficiency-for-the-ai-era/\n- https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/\n- https://fireworks.ai/news/fireworks-ai-raises-250m-series-c\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-announces-lead-investment-in-anthropics-3-5b-series-e-financing/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/exa-redesigning-search-for-ai/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/search-perfected-for-ai-why-were-doubling-down-on-exa/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/tripling-down-on-exa-to-build-the-search-engine-for-ai/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/collective-model-intelligence-the-next-scaling-axis/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/why-were-backing-warp/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-cylake/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/the-operating-system-for-in-house-legal-our-series-a-in-sandstone/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/outgunning-ai-weaponization-our-investment-in-a-security/\n- https://medium.com/feed/@guruchahal\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/hardware-engineering-gets-its-intelligence-layer/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/nominal/, /straiker/, /zo/, /big-sur-ai/, /eve/, /ketryx/, /tana/, /rocksalt/\n- https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-nominal-hits-1b-founders (secondary, via another repo row)\n- Lightspeed company pages: anthropic, nexthop-ai, ricursive-intelligence, unconventional-ai, fireworks-ai, exa, alif-semiconductor, unsloth, augment, pika, virtue-ai, nuance-labs, judgment-labs, omnifold, tennr (https://lsvp.com/company/<slug>/)\n", "person:hannah-seal": "\n# Hannah Seal\n\n## One-line take\nLondon partner at Index who leads AI-agent and AI-native automation companies from seed and then keeps leading or following on at fast step-ups. Wonderful went from a $34M seed (2025-07) to a $5B Series C (2026-09), and she co-signed the seed, the A and the B memo. She doesn't do labs or chips. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/index-ventures-triples-down-as-wonderful-raises-150m-series-b-to-scale-its-hyper-local-enterprise-ai-platform/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** She leads seeds big enough to be an early A, either $34M for Wonderful [src](https://www.newswire.com/news/wonderful-raises-34-million-seed-round-to-accelerate-enterprise-ai-22600316) or about $10M for Arondite [src](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/). Then she has Index lead the next round within months: Wonderful's A came four months after the seed [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl), and nexos.ai's A came nine months after an Index-led seed [src](https://nexos.ai/blog/nexos-launch/).\n- **Thesis:** Enterprise AI fails on execution, not on model quality. So she backs companies with forward-deployed teams, local general managers and agents running in production.\n- **Constraints:** Her stated remit is B2B software and enterprise AI applications [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/hannah-seal/). She has no named pre-training, lab or compute deals. Index's Flapping Airplanes A was linked from the Wonderful page, but no source names her on it.\n- **What she'd never do, from her own words:** back founders who aren't in it \"for the long run\" or chase quick flips [src](https://fortune.com/europe/2024/07/10/vc-firm-index-ventures-mistral-revolut-slack-2-3-billion-venture-capital-fund-invest-ai-champions/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Wonderful memo:** It says agents need people on the ground as well as technology: \"enterprise AI will not scale through technology alone\". The memo argues for full-stack teams that are \"co-located and forward-deployed into customer environments\". Co-written with Juriaan Duizendstraal, 2026-03-12. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/index-ventures-triples-down-as-wonderful-raises-150m-series-b-to-scale-its-hyper-local-enterprise-ai-platform/)\n- **Wonderful timing:** Index treats re-upping three times in a year as unusual: \"It's not often that we make three investments in a company within a year.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/index-ventures-triples-down-as-wonderful-raises-150m-series-b-to-scale-its-hyper-local-enterprise-ai-platform/)\n- **Go-to-market frame:** In her interview with Bar Winkler (2026-02-18), she compared Wonderful's strategy to a consumer company: \"A consumer-style land grab, but in the enterprise.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/from-assistive-ai-to-authoritative-systems-how-wonderful-is-taking-enterprise-ai-out-of-pilot-mode/)\n- **Ambition said on stage:** At Mind the Tech London (2025-09-17), only weeks after the seed, she said of Wonderful: \"Bigger than Wiz, why not?\" She also defended high AI prices: \"That justifies strong valuations in many cases.\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/skkxfj00sll)\n- **What she looks for in AI founders:** She named the traits as \"flexibility, resilience, and the ability to thrive in constant change\". She said they are especially strong in Israeli founders. [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/skkxfj00sll)\n- **Arondite origin:** Index says of the defence-AI company, \"We've known Arondite since inception.\" Co-authored with Stephane Kurgan and Bastian Hasslinger. A pre-seed led by Concept Ventures and Creator Fund came first. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/) [pre-seed src](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/)\n- **Sourcing pattern (my synthesis from three sourced founder bios, no quote):** Palantir alumni founded three of her AI deals:\n  - Arondite: CEO Will Blyth was at Palantir and Helsing [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/)\n  - Ankar: Tamar Gomez and Wiem Gharbi [src](https://sifted.eu/articles/palantir-alumni-ankar-ai-patents)\n  - Conduct: \"three former Palantir leaders\", where she is tagged on the post [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/)\n\n  Palantir's London alumni network looks like a repeat source for her.\n- **Repeat founders:** nexos.ai is run by the Nord Security and Oxylabs founders, Tomas Okmanas and Eimantas Sabaliauskas [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/nexosai-raises-30m-series-a-to-expedite-enterprise-ai-adoption/). Wordsmith CEO Ross McNairn had sold a previous startup to Skyscanner, and Skyscanner founder Gareth Williams was an angel in the seed [src](https://silicon.co.uk/press-release/wordsmith-raises-5m-to-empower-lawyers-to-scale-with-ai).\n- **Founder screen (2023):** \"I look for founders with a deep and specific insight of their market.\" From the Form Ventures interview on regulated markets. [src](https://formventures.substack.com/i/101904558/form-updates)\n- **Co-investing with small funds:** In the same interview, Index Origin II is described as prioritising co-investing with seed funds, solo GPs and angels. That makes it a possible co-investment path for a small manager. [src](https://formventures.substack.com/i/101904558/form-updates)\n- **Policy voice:** In a Times op-ed republished by Index (2025-06-05), she argued the UK's EMI share-option rules shut out AI startups such as Tessl: \"This is economic self-harm.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/our-secret-weapon-for-attracting-world-class-tech-talent-is-broken/)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, but narrow.** Her work is AI-native automation and agents at seed and Series A, the Serval end of THESIS.md:\n- Wonderful: led the seed and the A\n- Wordsmith AI: led the seed and the A\n- nexos.ai: co-led the A\n- Ankar: led the seed\n- Arondite: led the seed (defence AI / human-machine teaming)\n\nShe has no pre-training, new-lab or AI-chip deals on record. For a frontier-lab seed she is a co-investor or a route into Index's London partnership, not the decision maker.\n\nShe writes big seeds for enterprise-agent companies, such as $34M, and the Origin fund co-invests with seed funds and solo GPs. Both make her a credible co-investor or a source of follow-on capital in AI-native automation.\n\n## Background\n- BA in Economics, University of Cambridge, 2006–2009 [src](https://theorg.com/org/datasnipper/org-chart/hannah-seal) (aggregator); Index confirms the Cambridge Economics degree [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/hannah-seal/)\n- eBay UK: managed the consumer marketplace business [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/hannah-seal/)\n- Ocado: set up the General Merchandise business [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/hannah-seal/)\n- Index Ventures from July 2016 [src](https://www.crunchbase.com/person/hannah-seal) (aggregator); Index says she has been there \"close to a decade\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/hannah-seal/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/hannah-seal/ | Index bio; Perspectives archive at /perspectives/team-member/hannah-seal/ (7 pages) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes on Index Perspectives and in The Times |\n| X | nothing found | Index page links only @indexventures |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/hannah-seal-78938a22/ | linked from Index page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-02-18: Index Perspectives interview with Bar Winkler, \"From Assistive AI to Authoritative Systems\". She frames Wonderful as an enterprise land grab. [link](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/from-assistive-ai-to-authoritative-systems-how-wonderful-is-taking-enterprise-ai-out-of-pilot-mode/)\n- 2025-09-17: Mind the Tech London (Calcalist), on stage with Wonderful CEO Bar Winkler. She said \"Bigger than Wiz, why not?\" and defended AI valuations. [link](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/skkxfj00sll)\n- 2024-10-10: EUVC E365, \"Index Ventures' Hannah Seal on latest fund announcement and building your team from 0 to 1000\". No transcript found. [link](https://en-us.spreaker.com/episode/e365-euvc-index-ventures-hannah-seal-on-latest-fund-announcement-and-building-your-team-from-0-to-1000--62233746)\n- 2024-07-10: Fortune interview on Index's $2.3B funds. She said \"we're really at the earliest innings\" on AI and \"The era of the quick buck mentality is over\". [link](https://fortune.com/europe/2024/07/10/vc-firm-index-ventures-mistral-revolut-slack-2-3-billion-venture-capital-fund-invest-ai-champions/)\n- 2023-02-10: Form Ventures' Form Playbook interview on investing in regulated markets (Fonoa, Loctax). [link](https://formventures.substack.com/i/101904558/form-updates)\n- Undated:\n  - Venture Europe, \"Hannah Seal – Future of Work\" [link](https://podm8.com/episodes/venture-europe/hannah-seal-future-of-work-9CtbMnHwyj)\n  - Pitch Deck S5 Ep11 [link](https://podm8.com/episodes/pitch-deck/s5-ep11-hannah-seal-index-ventures-2w1mCPek_6)\n  - Not listened to; nothing quoted from either.\n\n## How they think\n- **Execution beats models:** In enterprise AI, the gap is deployment, not capability. That points to forward-deployed teams and local general managers. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/index-ventures-triples-down-as-wonderful-raises-150m-series-b-to-scale-its-hyper-local-enterprise-ai-platform/)\n- **From assistive to authoritative:** She wants agents that own workflows, not co-pilots. On Wordsmith's Series A she said \"They're not just building a co-pilot, they're creating the foundational infrastructure\" [src](https://pulse2.com/wordsmith-ai-25-million-series-a-raised-for-scaling-legal-ai-agent-infrastructure/). On Wordsmith's seed she framed the same idea as \"not about replacement but augmentation\" [src](https://silicon.co.uk/press-release/wordsmith-raises-5m-to-empower-lawyers-to-scale-with-ai). Her view moved from augmenting people (2024) to agents owning the work (2025–26).\n- **Europe and Israel first:** \"We think Europe is going to be one of the most important ecosystems for AI.\" [src](https://fortune.com/europe/2024/07/10/vc-firm-index-ventures-mistral-revolut-slack-2-3-billion-venture-capital-fund-invest-ai-champions/) Wonderful launched in Israel, Greece and Belgium before the US [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl).\n- **Comfortable with price:** She says AI value \"justifies strong valuations in many cases\" but warns against \"flash-in-the-pan\" companies [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/skkxfj00sll).\n- **Calls so far:** Wonderful is the standout, at $5B by September 2026 [src](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/wonderful-closes-550m-series-c-at-5b-nearly-tripling-march-valuation-as) (aggregator). DataSnipper reached a $1B valuation in a Series B led by Index, 2024-02 [src](https://www.zawya.com/en/world/uk-and-europe/ventures-invests-100mln-in-dutch-ai-start-up-r758inhe). No public misses found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Wonderful Series B memo, \"$2b Valuation in 8 Months\", with Duizendstraal, 2026-03-12: her full thesis on enterprise AI [link](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/index-ventures-triples-down-as-wonderful-raises-150m-series-b-to-scale-its-hyper-local-enterprise-ai-platform/)\n2. \"From Assistive AI to Authoritative Systems\", 2026-02-18: how she questions a founder [link](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/from-assistive-ai-to-authoritative-systems-how-wonderful-is-taking-enterprise-ai-out-of-pilot-mode/)\n3. Calcalist, Mind the Tech London, 2025-09-17: her ambition and view on valuations, in her own words [link](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/skkxfj00sll)\n4. Form Playbook interview, 2023: her founder screen and Index Origin's approach to co-investing [link](https://formventures.substack.com/i/101904558/form-updates)\n5. \"Our secret weapon for attracting world-class tech talent is broken\", 2025-06-05: her policy views [link](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/our-secret-weapon-for-attracting-world-class-tech-talent-is-broken/)\n\n## Social engagement\nNo personal X account found. She posts through Index Perspectives, The Times op-eds and conference stages (Mind the Tech, Money20/20 Europe, SaaStock). [src](https://europe.money2020.com/agenda/speakers/hannah-seal-s102-97514) [src](https://www.saastock.com/speakers/hannah-seal). LinkedIn activity not reviewed.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI deals first, then her other named companies.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Index co-signers / co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Wordsmith AI | Seed $5M | 2024-06-20 | Yes. Index led and Seal personally led | General Catalyst; Gareth Williams (angel) | [src](https://silicon.co.uk/press-release/wordsmith-raises-5m-to-empower-lawyers-to-scale-with-ai) |\n| nexos.ai | Seed $8M | 2025-01-15 | Index led; Seal's role on the seed not confirmed | Creandum; Dig Ventures | [src](https://nexos.ai/blog/nexos-launch/) |\n| Arondite | Seed (~$10M from Index; $12M total with pre-seed) | 2025-05-01 | Index led. Post co-authored by Seal, Stephane Kurgan and Bastian Hasslinger | Concept Ventures and Creator Fund led the earlier $2.25M pre-seed | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/) [src](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/) |\n| Ankar | Seed £3M | 2025-05-28 | Index led; Seal quoted | Daphni; Motier; Booom; Puzzle; angels Olivier Pomel, Julien Chaumond | [src](https://tech.eu/2025/05/28/ankar-raises-3m-seed-to-protect-intellectual-property-with-ai-powered-platform/) |\n| Wordsmith AI | Series A $25M (~$100M valuation) | 2025-06-03 | Index led; Seal quoted | Scottish Enterprise also an investor | [src](https://legaltechnology.com/wordsmith-ai-raises-25m-series-a-and-hits-100m-valuation-in-record-time/) [src](https://pulse2.com/wordsmith-ai-25-million-series-a-raised-for-scaling-legal-ai-agent-infrastructure/) |\n| Wonderful | Seed $34M | 2025-07-02 | Index led. Seal and Juriaan Duizendstraal co-wrote the Perspective | Bessemer; Vine Ventures | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/wonderful-raises-34m-to-accelerate-enterprise-ai-adoption-in-non-english-speaking-markets/) [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/skkxfj00sll) |\n| nexos.ai | Series A €30M | 2025-10-21 | Index co-led with Evantic Capital. Seal and Bastian Hasslinger wrote the Perspective | Evantic Capital | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/nexosai-raises-30m-series-a-to-expedite-enterprise-ai-adoption/) |\n| Wonderful | Series A $100M (~$700M valuation, per press) | 2025-11-11 | Index led. Seal and Duizendstraal | Insight Partners; IVP; Bessemer; Vine | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/wonderful-secures-100m-to-drive-adoption-of-ai-agents-globally/) |\n| Ankar | Series A $20M | 2025-12 | No, Atomico led; Index participated. Seal's role not confirmed | Atomico; Norrsken VC; Daphni | [src](https://ankar.ai/blog/ankar-raises-20m-in-series-a-funding-led-by-atomico-to-transform-how-breakthrough-ideas-become-protected-innovation) |\n| Wonderful | Series B $150M at $2B | 2026-03-12 | No, Insight led. Memo by Seal and Duizendstraal | Insight; IVP; Bessemer; Vine | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/index-ventures-triples-down-as-wonderful-raises-150m-series-b-to-scale-its-hyper-local-enterprise-ai-platform/) |\n| Conduct | Series A $60M | 2026-06-17 | Index co-led with ICONIQ. Post by Sahir Azam and Hasslinger; Seal tagged only | ICONIQ | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/) |\n| Wonderful | Series C $550M at $5B | 2026-09-02 | No, Insight led; Index participated. Seal's role not confirmed | Salesforce; IVP; Vine; 9Yards; Bessemer | [src](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/wonderful-closes-550m-series-c-at-5b-nearly-tripling-march-valuation-as) (aggregator) |\n| DataSnipper | Series B $100M ($1B valuation) | 2024-02 | Index led; Seal on board per TheOrg (aggregator) | | [src](https://www.zawya.com/en/world/uk-and-europe/ventures-invests-100mln-in-dutch-ai-start-up-r758inhe) |\n\n**Other companies on her Index page (non-AI or no round detail found):** Beamery, Beauty Pie, flatfair, Fonoa, Loctax, Multiverse, MyHeritage, Peanut, Remote, SeedLegals, Sofía, Tiney, Workbounce [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/hannah-seal/). Of these:\n- Workbounce: Index Origin led the seed, 2022-03 [src](https://www.lw.com/news/latham-advises-index-ventues-on-workbounce-seed-round)\n- Loctax: Index led the $12M Series A, 2022 [src](https://www.lw.com/en/news/2022/09/latham-advises-index-ventures-on-loctax-series-a)\n\n**Timing (was Index first?):**\n- **Wonderful:** Index led the launch seed. Bessemer and Vine came in alongside. Insight and IVP arrived four months later at the A, and Insight then led the B and C. Index was the first institutional lead.\n- **Wordsmith:** Index led the seed and the A about a year later.\n- **nexos.ai:** Index led the seed and co-led the A nine months later.\n- **Ankar:** Index led the seed. Atomico led the A about seven months later.\n- **Arondite:** Index was not first. Concept Ventures and Creator Fund led the pre-seed, although Index says it had known the company since inception.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Wonderful:**\n  - 2025-07-02: Index-led $34M seed at launch.\n  - 2025-09-17: Seal on stage with CEO Bar Winkler calling it a possible \"bigger than Wiz\".\n  - 2025-11-11: Index-led $100M A.\n  - 2026-03-12: Insight-led $150M B at $2B, with Index \"tripling down\".\n  - 2026-09: Insight-led $550M C at $5B.\n\n  Seal paired with Juriaan Duizendstraal, who runs Index's Israel activity [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl). The likely split is Duizendstraal on the Tel Aviv relationship and Seal on enterprise AI and the European launch markets. This is inferred, not sourced. How Index first met Bar Winkler and Roey Lalazar is not public; the memo says only that they were special from the first meeting.\n- **Arondite:** Index says it had known the company since inception. The Perspective is co-signed by Seal, Kurgan and Hasslinger. Hasslinger and Kurgan look like the defence and deep-tech partners, and Seal the enterprise partner. That split is inferred. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/)\n- **nexos.ai:** Repeat Lithuanian founders from Nord Security. Index led the seed, then Seal and Hasslinger co-signed the A co-lead with Evantic. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/nexosai-raises-30m-series-a-to-expedite-enterprise-ai-adoption/)\n- **Wordsmith:** Seal personally led the $5M seed, then Index led the $25M A at about $100M valuation. Search snippets list Index portfolio companies Remote and Multiverse among Wordsmith's customers. That is unverified; I didn't open the source page. [src](https://silicon.co.uk/press-release/wordsmith-raises-5m-to-empower-lawyers-to-scale-with-ai)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Juriaan Duizendstraal (Index) | Co-signer on Wonderful seed, A and B memos | 2025-07 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/wonderful-raises-34m-to-accelerate-enterprise-ai-adoption-in-non-english-speaking-markets/) |\n| Bastian Hasslinger (Index) | Co-signer on nexos.ai A and Arondite; co-tagged on Conduct | 2025-05 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/nexosai-raises-30m-series-a-to-expedite-enterprise-ai-adoption/) |\n| Stephane Kurgan (Index) | Co-signer on Arondite | 2025-05 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/) |\n| Sahir Azam (Index) | Conduct A author; Seal tagged | 2026-06 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/) |\n| Bar Winkler (Wonderful CEO) | Portfolio founder; shared stage and interview | 2025-07 | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/skkxfj00sll) |\n| Ross McNairn (Wordsmith CEO) | Portfolio founder; Seal led seed | 2024-06 | [src](https://silicon.co.uk/press-release/wordsmith-raises-5m-to-empower-lawyers-to-scale-with-ai) |\n| Tomas Okmanas (nexos.ai) | Portfolio founder | 2025-01 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/nexosai-raises-30m-series-a-to-expedite-enterprise-ai-adoption/) |\n| Will Blyth (Arondite) | Portfolio founder | 2025-05 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/) |\n| Tamar Gomez, Wiem Gharbi (Ankar) | Portfolio founders | 2025-05 | [src](https://tech.eu/2025/05/28/ankar-raises-3m-seed-to-protect-intellectual-property-with-ai-powered-platform/) |\n| Insight Partners | Follow-on lead in Wonderful (B, C) | 2025-11 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/index-ventures-triples-down-as-wonderful-raises-150m-series-b-to-scale-its-hyper-local-enterprise-ai-platform/) |\n| Vidya Peters (DataSnipper CEO) | Portfolio CEO; Seal interviewed her for Index | n/a | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/catching-up-with-vidya-peters-ceo-of-datasnipper/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who introduced Index to Bar Winkler and Roey Lalazar, and did Seal or Duizendstraal start the relationship?\n- Was Seal on nexos.ai's January 2025 seed, or only the A?\n- Does she have any exposure to Index's frontier-lab deals, such as Flapping Airplanes? None found.\n- Her principal-to-partner promotion date. EUVC E365 has no transcript; listen to it for her rules of thumb.\n- Is the Palantir-alumni pattern deliberate sourcing, for example through Index's Palantir links?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/hannah-seal/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/team-member/hannah-seal/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/wonderful-raises-34m-to-accelerate-enterprise-ai-adoption-in-non-english-speaking-markets/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/wonderful-secures-100m-to-drive-adoption-of-ai-agents-globally/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/index-ventures-triples-down-as-wonderful-raises-150m-series-b-to-scale-its-hyper-local-enterprise-ai-platform/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/from-assistive-ai-to-authoritative-systems-how-wonderful-is-taking-enterprise-ai-out-of-pilot-mode/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/nexosai-raises-30m-series-a-to-expedite-enterprise-ai-adoption/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/our-secret-weapon-for-attracting-world-class-tech-talent-is-broken/\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/skkxfj00sll\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl\n- https://fortune.com/europe/2024/07/10/vc-firm-index-ventures-mistral-revolut-slack-2-3-billion-venture-capital-fund-invest-ai-champions/\n- https://formventures.substack.com/i/101904558/form-updates\n- https://silicon.co.uk/press-release/wordsmith-raises-5m-to-empower-lawyers-to-scale-with-ai\n- https://pulse2.com/wordsmith-ai-25-million-series-a-raised-for-scaling-legal-ai-agent-infrastructure/\n- https://legaltechnology.com/wordsmith-ai-raises-25m-series-a-and-hits-100m-valuation-in-record-time/\n- https://tech.eu/2025/05/28/ankar-raises-3m-seed-to-protect-intellectual-property-with-ai-powered-platform/\n- https://ankar.ai/blog/ankar-raises-20m-in-series-a-funding-led-by-atomico-to-transform-how-breakthrough-ideas-become-protected-innovation\n- https://sifted.eu/articles/palantir-alumni-ankar-ai-patents\n- https://nexos.ai/blog/nexos-launch/\n- https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/\n- https://www.newswire.com/news/wonderful-raises-34-million-seed-round-to-accelerate-enterprise-ai-22600316\n- https://aiweekly.co/alerts/wonderful-closes-550m-series-c-at-5b-nearly-tripling-march-valuation-as (aggregator)\n- https://www.zawya.com/en/world/uk-and-europe/ventures-invests-100mln-in-dutch-ai-start-up-r758inhe\n- https://www.crunchbase.com/person/hannah-seal (aggregator)\n- https://theorg.com/org/datasnipper/org-chart/hannah-seal (aggregator)\n- https://en-us.spreaker.com/episode/e365-euvc-index-ventures-hannah-seal-on-latest-fund-announcement-and-building-your-team-from-0-to-1000--62233746\n", "person:hansae-catlett": "\n# Hansae Catlett\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. The shared web-search budget was used up before this task started, so this profile rests on direct fetches of the HOF bio, the Parable and Cassidy announcements, and the MarketScreener aggregator. LinkedIn (HTTP 999), X (402), Bessemer's news page (empty shell) and the MBA Fund site (no domain found) could not be read. The StartupIntros page that tied Catlett to Cassidy now returns \"News Not Found\".*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the only HOF person outside the three co-founders who is named on a deal HOF led: the $16.5M Parable seed in November 2025. That makes him the clearest owner of HOF's young, first-check early-stage effort, a different motion from the firm's usual buying into late AI rounds.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He gets three kinds of access at once. He has a Bessemer cloud/AI pattern-recognition background, his seed fund (The MBA Fund, founded 2017 per an aggregator) gets him in early through elite-school founder networks, and HOF gives him a balance sheet that can lead $10M+ early rounds. The bio scopes his HOF job as \"leading the firm's early stage investment strategy in applied AI\" [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/).\n- **What constrains him:** HOF's money and brand point toward late, brand-name AI rounds (Anthropic, OpenAI, xAI, fal) driven by the co-founders (see `firms/hof-capital.md`). His early-stage lane is applied AI and B2B software, not labs or chips. On the public record he has one confirmed HOF lead in about 2.7 years.\n- **What he'd likely never do (inference):** lead a frontier-lab mega-seed. Nothing in his record touches pre-training, labs or semis. His theses are about enterprise workflow and data.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **His one confirmed HOF lead is a data layer, not a model play.** On Parable, he says enterprises racing into AI \"most lack the baseline data to do it effectively\" [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-impact-measurement-startup-parable-raises-16-5-million-302604577.html) (2025-11-04). He backs measurement and instrumentation as the precondition for AI adoption.\n- **His framing is AI as an org redesign, not a feature.** He said Parable lets companies embrace AI \"not as a bolt-on tool\" [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-impact-measurement-startup-parable-raises-16-5-million-302604577.html). This matches an AI-native automation view of the enterprise.\n- **He leads repeat founders at seed.** Parable was \"started by four second-time founders\" [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-impact-measurement-startup-parable-raises-16-5-million-302604577.html), and HOF led above a set of existing seed funds (Lasagna, Panache, Supercharge, Triple Impact).\n- **An attribution gap on HOF's most thesis-relevant early deal.** Cassidy (no-code AI agents and workflows, close to the Serval type) announced $10M \"led by HOF Capital\" [src](https://cassidyai.com/blog/announcing-cassidys-10m-series-a) on 2025-09-08 and names no HOF person. Catlett's applied-AI mandate makes him the plausible owner, but the only page that named him (StartupIntros) now returns not found [src](https://startupintros.com/news/cassidy-series-a-2025-09). **Not counted.**\n- **His sourcing edge is school alumni.** The MBA Fund \"partners with promising alumni and student entrepreneurs from top universities\" [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/). That is a first-check channel he brings into HOF. Hightouch and FlutterFlow came through it (fund-level names, not necessarily his personal checks).\n- **His board seats are a Bessemer legacy.** An aggregator lists him as a director of Luxury Presence since 2021-04 (still active) and of Rainforest QA until 2022-11 [src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/HANSAE-CATLETT-A2SS5F/). Luxury Presence is also in his Bessemer deal list [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/). Aggregator only, so medium confidence.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:** he can lead $10M+ seeds and A's (Parable $16.5M, probably Cassidy $10M), and he is the early-stage door into a firm whose late-stage LP machine (Morgan Stanley wealth channel, Strategic Opportunities funds) follows labs and compute. If Cassidy is confirmed as his, he also backs AI-native automation of the Serval type.\n- **Against:** no frontier-lab, pre-training or AI-chip deal anywhere in his record. Parable is applied-AI enterprise analytics. His HOF record is thin (one named lead).\n- **The deals that matter to me:** Parable (seed lead), Cassidy (attribution unconfirmed). From Bessemer: EvenUp and Jasper (applied AI). From the MBA Fund: Anything and Vooma AI (AI-native apps).\n- **How I would use him:** as a co-lead or follow-on partner for AI-native automation seeds, and as a route to HOF's late-stage capital for my lab companies. He is not a lab-seed peer.\n\n## Background\n- Harvard, honors degree in biomedical engineering and philosophy [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/). Dates not found.\n- Founder of SoundScope, Inc., ended 2013-12 (aggregator) [src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/HANSAE-CATLETT-A2SS5F/).\n- Consultant/advisor at TechnoServe (to 2016-06) and The Bridgespan Group (to 2016-08) (aggregator) [src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/HANSAE-CATLETT-A2SS5F/).\n- Stanford MBA and MPP [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/). Year not found; given the consulting end dates, probably around 2016 to 2018 (inference).\n- Helped lead investment strategy at Scott Cook's and Steve Ballmer's family offices (enterprise SaaS, biomedical informatics, edtech). Worked with the Floodgate and Slow Ventures investment teams. Held product and strategy roles at startups [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/). Dates not found.\n- Co-founder and GP of The MBA Fund, founded 2017-12-31 per aggregator [src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/HANSAE-CATLETT-A2SS5F/).\n- Bessemer Venture Partners: \"helping to lead the firm's investments in cloud and AI\" [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/). Promoted to Vice President; the announcement URL exists but its body would not render [src](https://www.bvp.com/news/bessemer-venture-partners-promotes-hansae-catlett-to-vice-president). Start and promotion dates not found. MarketScreener lists him under \"Deer Management Co. LLC\" (I identify this as Bessemer's management entity) [src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/HANSAE-CATLETT-A2SS5F/).\n- HOF Capital, Partner, start date 2024-02-29 (aggregator) [src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/HANSAE-CATLETT-A2SS5F/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/ | Firm bio, read 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing confirmed | x.com/hansae returned 402; handle unverified |\n| LinkedIn | nothing confirmed | Guessed URL blocked (999); not verified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found (search budget exhausted; needs a follow-up pass).\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** AI adoption in enterprises is blocked by a missing baseline. Measure how work happens first, then automate (Parable quote). AI should be a structural transformation, not a bolt-on [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-impact-measurement-startup-parable-raises-16-5-million-302604577.html).\n- **Recurring sectors:** applied AI, vertical SaaS (ServiceTitan, Luxury Presence, MaintainX, Rilla), dev tools and data infrastructure (Hightouch, FlutterFlow), legal AI (EvenUp), generative-AI apps (Jasper) [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/).\n- **Contrarian views:** nothing found.\n- **Calls right or wrong:** ServiceTitan went public (NASDAQ: TTAN) and Canva is in his Bessemer list [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/). Both are firm-level wins; his personal role is not stated.\n\n## Read these first\n- HOF bio: scope of his mandate and named deals [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/).\n- Parable seed release: his only on-record thesis quote at HOF [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-impact-measurement-startup-parable-raises-16-5-million-302604577.html).\n- Cassidy Series A post: HOF's AI-automation lead, with no partner named [src](https://cassidyai.com/blog/announcing-cassidys-10m-series-a).\n- Nothing written by him was found.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X and LinkedIn could not be read.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Parable | Seed, $16.5M | 2025-11-04 | Yes (HOF led; he is the quoted HOF person) | Story Ventures, InMotion Ventures; existing Lasagna, Panache, Supercharge, Triple Impact Capital; angels from founders of HubSpot, Vimeo, Deel, Ramp, Superhuman | [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-impact-measurement-startup-parable-raises-16-5-million-302604577.html) |\n| Cassidy | Series A, $10M | 2025-09-08 | HOF led; **his role unconfirmed, not counted** | The General Partnership, Neo, Alumni Ventures, Quest Venture Partners | [src](https://cassidyai.com/blog/announcing-cassidys-10m-series-a) |\n| Luxury Presence | Board director since 2021-04 (Bessemer era) | 2021-04 | n/a | not found | [src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/HANSAE-CATLETT-A2SS5F/) (aggregator) |\n| Rainforest QA | Board director, ended 2022-11 (Bessemer era) | to 2022-11 | n/a | not found | [src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/HANSAE-CATLETT-A2SS5F/) (aggregator) |\n| Canva, EvenUp, Guild, Jasper, Luxury Presence, MaintainX, Nala, Rilla, ServiceTitan, Sila, Spot AI, TRM Labs | Bessemer deals he \"helped lead\" (bio lists them together) | not stated | Bessemer, role per bio | not stated | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/) |\n| Anything, FlutterFlow, Hightouch, Jeeves, Seso, Vooma AI | The MBA Fund \"notable investments\" (fund-level, seed) | not stated | not stated | not stated | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n### Parable seed (the standout)\n- **What:** Parable describes itself as an intelligence layer for enterprise operations. It passively tracks how teams spend time across processes and projects, sets a baseline, drives AI automation and change management, then measures the impact. CEO Adam Schwartz. The company came out of stealth with the round [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-impact-measurement-startup-parable-raises-16-5-million-302604577.html).\n- **When:** announced 2025-11-04 from New York, about 20 months after Catlett joined HOF (2024-02-29, aggregator).\n- **Who else was there:** HOF led the $16.5M seed. Story Ventures and InMotion Ventures joined. Earlier backers Lasagna, Panache, Supercharge and Triple Impact Capital (spelled \"Tripe\" in the release) returned. Angels included founders of HubSpot, Vimeo, Deel, Ramp and Superhuman [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-impact-measurement-startup-parable-raises-16-5-million-302604577.html).\n- **Timing:** the existing-investor list means seed funds were in before HOF. HOF came in as the large lead on a round that also served as the stealth exit. The gap between the earlier checks and HOF's lead is not dated in any source.\n- **How he found it:** **not found.** No source describes the intro. The New York base (shared by Parable and HOF) and the four second-time founders are the only clues. Founder names other than Schwartz were not given.\n- **Why he committed:** in his words, enterprises lack the baseline data for AI, and Parable turns AI into a structural change. \"We see an enormous market opportunity here\" [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-impact-measurement-startup-parable-raises-16-5-million-302604577.html).\n- **What it's worth now:** **not found.** No later round or valuation was reachable (parable.com returned 403).\n\n### Cassidy Series A (not counted)\nHOF led Cassidy's $10M A on 2025-09-08, alongside The General Partnership, Neo, Alumni Ventures and Quest Venture Partners. The post is signed by Justin Fineberg and names no HOF person [src](https://cassidyai.com/blog/announcing-cassidys-10m-series-a). The aggregator page that once named Catlett is gone. Treat it as an open question.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| HOF Capital | Partner (early stage) | 2024-02-29 (aggregator) | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/), [src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/HANSAE-CATLETT-A2SS5F/) |\n| The MBA Fund | Co-founder and GP | 2017-12-31 (aggregator) | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/), [src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/HANSAE-CATLETT-A2SS5F/) |\n| Bessemer Venture Partners | Vice President (cloud and AI) | not found | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/) |\n| Scott Cook family office; Steve Ballmer family office | Helped lead investment strategy | not found | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/) |\n| Floodgate; Slow Ventures | Worked with investment teams | not found | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/) |\n| Parable / Adam Schwartz (CEO) | Seed lead investor | 2025-11-04 | [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-impact-measurement-startup-parable-raises-16-5-million-302604577.html) |\n| Luxury Presence | Board director | 2021-04 (aggregator) | [src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/HANSAE-CATLETT-A2SS5F/) |\n| Rainforest QA | Board director (former) | until 2022-11 (aggregator) | [src](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/HANSAE-CATLETT-A2SS5F/) |\n| Story Ventures, InMotion Ventures, Lasagna, Panache, Supercharge, Triple Impact | Co-investors on Parable | 2025-11 | [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-impact-measurement-startup-parable-raises-16-5-million-302604577.html) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who sourced Parable, and how? What size was HOF's check, and is there a later round or markup?\n- Is Catlett the HOF owner of Cassidy? Ask Justin Fineberg's team or check a HOF LinkedIn post.\n- Who are the other MBA Fund co-GPs, how big is the fund, and does it still invest?\n- When did he join and get promoted at Bessemer, and which of the Bessemer deals did he personally source? (The bio lists them as a group.)\n- Has he led anything at HOF in 2026? Nothing found in this pass.\n- Is he involved in HOF's compute and lab deals (Compute Infrastructure vehicle, Extropic)? No evidence either way.\n\n## Sources\n- https://hofcapital.com/team/hansae-catlett/ (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-impact-measurement-startup-parable-raises-16-5-million-302604577.html (2025-11-04)\n- https://cassidyai.com/blog/announcing-cassidys-10m-series-a (2025-09-08)\n- https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/HANSAE-CATLETT-A2SS5F/ (aggregator, read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.bvp.com/news/bessemer-venture-partners-promotes-hansae-catlett-to-vice-president (body would not render)\n- https://startupintros.com/news/cassidy-series-a-2025-09 (now returns \"News Not Found\")\n- firms/hof-capital.md (repo)\n", "person:haya-hanna": "\n# Haya Hanna\n\n## One-line take\nThey were the deep-tech lead at Hummingbird, the top-performing seed shop that led OLIX's $220M round. Sifted reports they left in 2026 to raise their own fund, which makes them an emerging manager to call before the fund closes. No source yet credits them with a deal.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they could win:** they held the only deep-tech-titled seat at a concentrated, founder-obsessed firm during the years it moved into AI chips and compute: Etched (2024), OLIX (led, 2026-02), Watney (co-led) and MicroAGI (led) ([TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/hummingbird-ventures/org-chart/haya-hanna), aggregator; [firms/hummingbird-ventures.md](../firms/hummingbird-ventures.md)). Before that they were at Elaia, a Paris deep-tech VC (TheOrg lists Elaia as a prior company; no title or dates given).\n- **Constraint:** Hummingbird decides on two keys: Van den Brande and Ileri must both agree ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)). Its announcements credit the firm, not individuals. So a junior's track record there is invisible from outside, and an LP backing them would rely on references. A first-time fund will also be much smaller than the $10M+ tickets the old seat could reach.\n- **What they'd never do:** nothing found. No talks, posts or writing by them were found.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The departure is the signal.** Sifted (2026-11-04) reports they left Hummingbird earlier in 2026 and are in early talks to raise a fund ([sifted](https://sifted.eu/articles/ex-hummingbird-new-vc-firm)). This was seen only as a search snippet in an earlier pass, and the page returned 403 on fetch. No verbatim quote is available, so there is no insight row for it.\n- TheOrg still lists them as current at Hummingbird, so the departure rests on the Sifted snippet plus their absence from the team page. TheOrg's unverified profile describes them as an \"experienced investor with a focus on deep technology\" ([TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/hummingbird-ventures/org-chart/haya-hanna), aggregator).\n- **The seat they held led OLIX's February 2026 round.** Dealroom calls OLIX a \"secretive UK chip venture\" ([dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/127009-hummingbird-targets-800m-raise-to-back-misfit-founders-worldwide/)). The same item names no individual investor, only the founding and managing partners for their Midas listing. Attribution to Hanna is unconfirmed.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium, could become high.** In my lens terms they are a European deep-tech emerging manager out of a seed shop that is now in AI chips (Etched, OLIX) and physical AI (MicroAGI, Watney) ([portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio)). If they sourced or ran OLIX or Etched, they are exactly the \"rising junior doing hot deals before they're famous\". Their new fund could then be a high-fit co-investor or a source of deal flow in European compute and photonics. Today that rests on title and timing only. The deals that matter, if confirmed: OLIX (photonic AI chips, Hummingbird-led $220M, 2026-02) and Etched (transformer ASIC, Hummingbird entered 2024). A first-time fund is too small to anchor $600M seeds, but it is a good early-access partner.\n\n## Background\n- **Segment coding:** I coded seniority as `principal` from the vertical-lead title \"Investor - Deep Tech Lead\" (TheOrg, aggregator); it was not a partner title. I coded check_capacity as `sub-10m` because they now have no fund and are only in early talks on a first one. The Hummingbird seat itself could reach $10M+: the firm led OLIX's $220M round and MicroAGI's $55M seed. No headshot was found: they are not on the Hummingbird or Elaia team pages, and TheOrg shows only the firm logo.\n- **Elaia** (Paris deep-tech and digital VC): listed as a prior company on TheOrg; title and dates not found ([TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/hummingbird-ventures/org-chart/haya-hanna), aggregator). Elaia's team page does not list them now ([elaia](https://www.elaia.com/team), read 2026-11-10).\n- **Hummingbird Ventures:** Investor, Deep Tech Lead; start date not found (TheOrg, aggregator). Not on the current team page ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team), read 2026-11-10), which is consistent with a departure.\n- **2026:** left Hummingbird; reportedly in early talks to raise their own fund ([sifted](https://sifted.eu/articles/ex-hummingbird-new-vc-firm), 2026-11-04, snippet only).\n- Education: not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found (not searched: search budget exhausted) | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| TheOrg | https://theorg.com/org/hummingbird-ventures/org-chart/haya-hanna | aggregator; unverified; Paris |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found.\n\n## How they think\nNothing found: no writing, talks or posts by them were located. The only signal is the \"deep technology\" focus on TheOrg.\n\n## Read these first\n- Sifted, 2026-11-04, on their new fund: https://sifted.eu/articles/ex-hummingbird-new-vc-firm (paywalled or blocked; read it by hand)\n- The Generalist on Hummingbird's model, for the house style they trained in: https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nNo deal names them. These Hummingbird deep-tech entries fell in their tenure window by firm credit only, not personal attribution:\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Etched (firm-level, not attributed) | Early | 2024 | not found | not found | [portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio) |\n| OLIX (firm-level, not attributed) | $220M round | 2026-02 | Hummingbird led | not found | [dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/127009-hummingbird-targets-800m-raise-to-back-misfit-founders-worldwide/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n**Standout deal: none confirmed.** The candidate is OLIX. Hummingbird led its $220M round in February 2026 ([dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/127009-hummingbird-targets-800m-raise-to-back-misfit-founders-worldwide/)). Fundomo then led a $312M Series B on 2026-08-03 at a reported £3.3B valuation, with Arm, HRT and the UK Sovereign AI fund ([tech.eu](https://tech.eu/2026/08/03/uk-chip-startup-olix-raises-ps312m-at-ps33bn-valuation/)). That is a large mark-up on the February round, which was valued at about $1B+ per the firm file. How Hummingbird found OLIX, when the first meeting happened and who ran the deal: nothing found. The same is true of Etched (2024). Neither the company announcements nor the firm's press name a Hummingbird junior.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hummingbird Ventures | Investor, Deep Tech Lead (former; left 2026) | not found | [TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/hummingbird-ventures/org-chart/haya-hanna), [sifted](https://sifted.eu/articles/ex-hummingbird-new-vc-firm) |\n| Elaia | Prior employer (role not found) | not found | [TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/hummingbird-ventures/org-chart/haya-hanna) |\n| Peter Devlin | Hummingbird colleague; holds the deep-tech remit now (inference from titles; overlap window unknown) | not found | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Read the Sifted article (2026-11-04): fund name, target size, co-founders, thesis, anchor LPs.\n- Their exact Hummingbird start date, and whether they ran OLIX, Etched, Watney or MicroAGI. Ask OLIX's founders or Fundomo, or check the LinkedIn experience section by hand.\n- Their Elaia title, years and deals.\n- Whether the new fund will co-invest with Hummingbird or take Nomads money. Nomads backs insurgent managers below $250M AUM ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)).\n- Photo: none found; LinkedIn CDN links are excluded by the rules.\n\n## Sources\n- https://theorg.com/org/hummingbird-ventures/org-chart/haya-hanna (aggregator, read 2026-11-10)\n- https://sifted.eu/articles/ex-hummingbird-new-vc-firm (2026-11-04; 403 on fetch; known only from an earlier search snippet)\n- https://www.hummingbird.vc/team ; https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.elaia.com/team (read 2026-11-10; not listed)\n- https://dealroom.co/news/127009-hummingbird-targets-800m-raise-to-back-misfit-founders-worldwide/\n- https://tech.eu/2026/08/03/uk-chip-startup-olix-raises-ps312m-at-ps33bn-valuation/\n- https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird\n- Note: the web-search budget for this run was exhausted before any search ran, so this profile rests on direct fetches only. LinkedIn, X and Crunchbase (403) were not reachable.\n", "person:hemant-taneja": "\n# Hemant Taneja\n\n## One-line take\n*Updated 2026-11-10 (second pass): added his 20VC transcript (Sept 2025), Anthropic rounds E, G and H, the Mistral intro path, and career dates.*\n\nHis signature is what lets GC co-lead a $1.1B seed for a two-month-old lab (River AI). The relationships behind those bets run years deep, and his named AI list is about twice as long as GC's own \"AI\" tag suggests.\n\n## Crux\n- **In his own words, he is a picker who now thinks he should have indexed AI.** He says GC chased \"the best one\" in AI the way it picked Stripe over Square, and that \"in hindsight we should just gone and index those\" ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum) at 1:20:43, auto-captions). He regrets passing on OpenAI over its structure, and credits Lightspeed and Yuri Milner for indexing well. The 2025-26 pattern (four Anthropic rounds, River, General Intuition, Engram, Cognition) is the correction.\n- **Self-imposed limits:** venture funds stay small (\"we're not going to make our venture funds bigger\", target 4-5x), and he tries \"not to have more than 10 to 15% in a single company\" per fund, so big positions get built across funds and rounds (same source).\n- **Why he wins:** he runs GC as a capital platform rather than a fund. Creation (incubation), the Customer Value Fund, HATCo (which reaches about 15% of US healthcare operations), the GC Institute, and SMAs mean he can offer a founder distribution and every stage of capital ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/fundxii)). He co-founds instead of waiting for deal flow: Livongo, Commure, Hippocratic AI, Transcarent and Percepta ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/hemant-taneja)).\n- **What constrains him:** scale and a mandate for applied AI. His own bio centres on \"applied AI as the engine for abundance and resilience\" ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/hemant-taneja)). His Q2-2026 review argues that outcomes are concentrating and that venture must innovate on capital itself ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/rethinking-venture-in-an-era-of-concentration)). The result is a bias toward platform plays (a GC-owned Percepta, a hospital, roll-ups) over picking.\n- **What he'd never do:** stay a passive minority holder. Every frontier name shows him re-upping: Mistral seed, then A, then a co-led B ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/tripling-down-on-mistral-ai)); Hippocratic seed, A and C; Applied Intuition B lead, then E; Substrate seed, then the 2025 round.\n\n## Differential insight\n**Added 2026-11-10 from the 20VC interview with Harry Stebbings (recorded Sept 2025; quotes are from auto-generated captions, so spelling is as transcribed) ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum)):**\n- **Mistral intro path: he was the first VC Arthur Mensch met.** \"I got introduced to him by Jean Charles at Alan\", and \"I was the first VC he ever met\". Mensch took the call on a park bench in Paris, and Taneja told him \"I've never had such a bad pitch\". Lightspeed still led the seed; GC only co-led at the Series B. He adds that \"Janette's on the board there\": the Mistral board seat is Jeannette zu Fürstenberg's, not his.\n- **Anthropic: first check was the $61.5B Series E (March 2025), not the Series F.** \"we put in a few hundred million\" at $60B, and he says yes to \"another few hundred million at 180\". His reason for entering then: coding had made Anthropic a business, not an AGI bet; \"risk adjusted that was the round that was interesting to do\". On the $183B round: \"This round probably was the cheapest round that got done this year\" on a revenue multiple.\n- **He regrets passing on OpenAI.** Asked about not investing because of the structure: \"many people overthought it and I overthought it as well\". What he says he lost is the learning from a front-row seat.\n- **He now leans toward indexing a platform shift.** \"you're better off backing all of them than trying to pick\". It is the thing he changed his mind on most in the prior 12 months.\n- **Biggest seed miss: Coinbase.** \"Paul Graham asked me to look at the seed round of Coinbase\"; he passed because he saw \"a Bitcoin ATM\". He also declined to fund Drew Houston's Dropbox after offering him a job at GC.\n- **He lost the Series A of Stripe, Samsara and Snap and bought in afterwards.** \"I lost the series A of Stripe\"; \"if I'm not losing I'm not winning\". He has since \"invested in Stripe 14 times in the last 15 years\", and Stripe is GC's most concentrated position at \"about a billion dollars\". This is the template for Anthropic: lose or skip the early round, then build the position every round.\n- **Price is not a reason to pass.** \"investors use price as a reason to pass\" when they lack conviction elsewhere; he quotes GC co-founder Joel Cutler: \"Price only hurts once.\"\n- **Seed is the stated core.** \"we will lose the right to exist and we're paranoid about that\" if GC stops doing early-stage well. He frames it as ownership and relationship \"versus the sizes of check\".\n- **Scale of ambition:** over 20 years \"we'll probably invest what 300 billion, 500 billion into the world\".\n\n**Added 2026-11-10 from press and company announcements:**\n- **Anthropic became GC's largest single investment at the $965B Series H (May 2026).** Taneja on X, as quoted by Inshorts/Firstpost: \"We are quadrupling down on Anthropic\" and \"We first invested four rounds ago\" ([src](https://inshorts.com/en/amp_news/quadrupling-down-on-anthropic--indian-origin-general-catalyst-ceo-1780051538433), secondary; the X post itself was not fetched). Anthropic's own releases list GC in the E ([src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-series-e-at-usd61-5b-post-money-valuation)), G ([src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-30-billion-series-g-funding-380-billion-post-money-valuation)) and H ([src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h)), never as lead or co-lead.\n\n**From the first pass (GC pages and releases):**\n- **The River AI deal rested on an old relationship.** In the launch interview Taneja asks Babuschkin to go back to \"when we met, and what got you excited about starting xAI\" ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/own-your-ai-experience), 2026-08-11). So the $1.1B co-lead of a two-month-old lab came from a relationship that started around xAI's 2023 founding, not a cold process. The Babuschkin profile had the intro path as \"nothing found\".\n- **He holds a board seat that GC's own site doesn't credit.** The Chai Discovery Series B release says \"Hemant Taneja from General Catalyst will be joining the board\" ([src](https://www.oakhcft.com/news/chai-discovery-announces-130-million-series-b-to-transform-molecular-discovery), 2025-12-15). GC's Chai page lists Viboch, Momeni, Neupane and Pai, but not him ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/chai-discovery)).\n- **GC owns its own lab-like asset.** Percepta is \"fully owned by General Catalyst\" and runs a frontier research lab led by Athul Paul Jacob and Costis Daskalakis, with AWS and Anthropic as partners ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/unveiling-percepta), 2025-10-02). Taneja co-founded it with ex-Palantir Hirsh Jain.\n- **He admitted mistakes in a letter to LPs.** He wrote that GC too \"made a handful of investments that were paying far ahead\". He also said GC's SAIL vehicles, its version of SPACs, \"are winding down as the IPO markets remain challenged\" ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/hemants-year-end-letter-to-lps), Dec 2022).\n- **He criticises his own industry.** \"Dare I say it: venture has gotten comfortable and lazy.\" On crypto: \"the ‘killer app’ was greed itself\" (same letter).\n- **He claims GC invented the AI roll-up.** Long Lake post (Taneja's byline first): \"Long Lake was the first expression of the “AI roll-up” as a concept\" ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/manufacturing-outliers-long-lake), 2026-09-28).\n- **He was early on physical AI.** \"In 2019, we had conviction that a Tier-1 software supplier will emerge\" was the reason GC led Applied Intuition's Series B ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-applied-intuition)).\n- **About a quarter of Fund XII is single-LP money.** It includes \"$2B of separately managed accounts\" out of about $8B, and no LP is named ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/fundxii)).\n- **How he sources founders.** He is \"drawn to founders that tackle sectors that are largely ignored by others\". Samsara came through the MIT network, after he \"served together on the board of Highfive\" with Sanjit Biswas ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/a-milestone-for-samsara), 2021-12-20).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Second-pass note (2026-11-10):** the 20VC transcript sharpens this. He says GC wants to be at incubation or seed, and otherwise buys into \"iconic companies\" at growth and keeps buying (Stripe 14 times; Anthropic four rounds running at a few hundred million each). So for a new lab he is either in at the first round (Mistral, River AI) or arrives late with size. His stated move toward indexing means GC will probably want a slot in most large lab seeds from here, which makes GC a likely co-investor next to a $20M check, not a competitor for it. Still medium: his own money goes in at growth scale and his thesis is applied AI.\n\n**Medium, and this deliberately qualifies the firm file's line that \"the relevant surface is not Hemant Taneja.\"** On the letter of THESIS.md his named deals look high: River AI ($1.1B seed+A co-lead), Mistral (seed, then A, then co-led B), General Intuition ($133.7M seed co-lead), Engram (first-round co-lead), Moonvalley (seed co-lead), Substrate (chip-fab seed) and Anthropic. But he is the *signature* that makes a $1B seed possible, not the person who sources most of them. Bhargava, Clark and Rimpel are co-credited on nearly every frontier deal, and GC's pages put Taneja's name first by default. Practical read: if the owner wants a $20M allocation alongside GC in a giant lab seed, Taneja's yes matters at the top. River shows these deals come from Taneja's own long-standing founder relationships, so a direct relationship with him is worth having. For deal flow, though, go to Bhargava, Clark and Rimpel. Health-AI (Hippocratic, Chai, Manas, Commure) and defense/autonomy (Helsing, Applied Intuition) are where he personally holds the thesis.\n\n## Background\n- Five MIT degrees: M.Eng. EECS, S.M. Operations Research, S.B. Biology, S.B. Mathematics, S.B. EECS ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/hemant-taneja)).\n- Born February 1975 in Delhi; moved to Boston during high school (Wikipedia, [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hemant_Taneja)).\n- Founder and CEO of Isovia, a mobile software company, before GC (aggregator: MarketScreener, [src](https://ch.marketscreener.com/insider/HEMANT-TANEJA-A05L5N/)).\n- Joined GC in 2002 as entrepreneur-in-residence; Managing Director 2002-2021 (aggregator: LinkedIn mirror, [src](https://app.icebreaker.xyz/profiles/linkedin-hemanttaneja)). He says \"I have been doing this for 25 years\" ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum), Sept 2025).\n- Moved from Boston to the Bay Area and lost his first competitive Series As there (ClassDojo, Stripe, Samsara, Snap) before winning Gusto ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum)).\n- CEO since 2021 (Wikipedia citing the FT, [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hemant_Taneja)); a Turpentine VC episode description calls it a five-year succession plan (summary only, [src](https://metacast.app/podcast/turpentine-vc-venture-capital-and-investing/LUJoKypp/e74-general-catalyst-ceo-on-running-vc-like-an-enduring-company/PYFQL3ey)).\n- Founder and executive chairman of HATCo since 2023; CEO of GC's wealth RIA since 2023; former director of Livongo and Teladoc (aggregators, same two links).\n- Was on GC's first investment in Stripe \"11 years ago\" as of Dec 2021, so about 2010 ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/a-milestone-for-samsara)).\n- Co-founded Livongo, which \"started as a whiteboard exercise between Glen Tullman and Hemant Taneja\" ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/chris-bischoff)) and sold to Teladoc for $18.5B ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/hemant-taneja)).\n- Managing Partner by 2021 ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/kenneth-c-frazier-merck-executive-chairman-and-former-ceo-joins-general-catalyst-as-chairman-of-its-health-assurance-initiatives)); CEO by Jan 2025 ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/aws-and-general-catalyst-join-forces-to-transform-health-care-with-ai)).\n- Author of four books, including *The Transformation Principles* (2025) ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/hemant-taneja)).\n- Year he joined GC: not stated on GC's site; 2002 comes from the aggregator sources above.\n- Pronouns: Wikipedia uses \"he\"; GC's bio is first person.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/hemant-taneja | GC bio and investment list; checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories | His essays and quarterly reviews appear as GC Stories (about 60 carry his byline) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/htaneja | Linked from the GC team page; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/pub/hemant-taneja/0/15/a44 | Linked from the GC team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only; see appearances below |\n| LinkedIn (current URL) | https://www.linkedin.com/in/hemanttaneja | Loads (HTTP 200, 2026-11-10); follower count not checked |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-07 — \"A Conversation with GC CEO Hemant Taneja\" — https://podwise.ai/dashboard/episodes/5247342 — value concentration in a few AI companies; page blocked, AI summary only, not quoted.\n- 2026-04 — Sourcery (Molly O'Shea) — \"General Catalyst's First-Ever Quarterly Review\" — https://youtu.be/hWI258ZHbxE — show notes list a $43B ecosystem, the software buyout model, Anthropic's Series G, a $5B India commitment and whether GC goes public ([notes](https://www.castfox.net/podcast/sourcery-6853139/episode/general-catalysts-first-ever-quarterly-review-ceo-hemant-taneja-381633)). No transcript pulled.\n- 2026-01 — All-In at CES with McKinsey's Bob Sternfels — announced in https://kyodonewsprwire.jp/index.php/release/202511189362 — not transcribed.\n- 2025-09 — 20VC (Harry Stebbings) — \"Lessons Scaling GC to $40BN in AUM\" — transcript https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum (video https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DWvYbRzC-g0) — **the best single source**: Anthropic entry and check size, Mistral intro, OpenAI regret, indexing, lost Series As, concentration cap.\n- 2025-09 — The Information's TITV — \"Global AI Investing & Energy Needs\" — https://pocketcasts.com/podcast/the-informations-titv/55c18e90-4082-0133-be80-0d11918ab357/hemant-taneja-on-global-ai-investing-energy-needs-ai-team-inefficiency-sep-24-2025/6db540ca-f4f3-4111-9749-7c2f50fe82c6 — not transcribed.\n- 2025-08 — The Generalist (Mario Gabriele) — \"Inside General Catalyst\" — https://www.generalist.com/p/inside-general-catalyst-hemant-taneja — notes only: GC as a \"strategic conglomerate\" with venture at its core; servant leadership learned from Ken Chenault; names Stanislas Polu and Harrison Chase. No transcript on the page.\n- 2025-01 — Turpentine VC E74 — \"Running VC Like an Enduring Company\" — https://metacast.app/podcast/turpentine-vc-venture-capital-and-investing/LUJoKypp/e74-general-catalyst-ceo-on-running-vc-like-an-enduring-company/PYFQL3ey — page blocked; description only.\n- Undated — Invest Like the Best EP.382 — \"Engineering Global Resilience\" — https://globalplayer.com/podcasts/episodes/7DriuSt — transcript page blocked.\n- 2026-08 — GC video interview — \"Own Your AI Experience\" with Igor Babuschkin — https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/own-your-ai-experience — revealed that the two met around xAI's founding; framed GC as \"creating unfair advantages for founders\".\n- 2024 — StrictlyVC Download (hosts Connie Loizos, Alex Gove) — Customer Value strategy — named in https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/financed-to-endure (episode link not verified).\n- 2021-01 — conversation with Nitin Nohria on \"algorithmic canaries\" — https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/putting-algorithmic-canaries-into-practice.\n- First-pass note: the three entries above came from GC pages only; the list at the top of this section was added in the second pass.\n\n## How they think\n- **Incubate or seed, otherwise buy the icon and keep buying.** His stated rule: GC wants to incubate or seed, and if it misses, it invests at growth in companies it thinks compound for a long time (Stripe 14 times since 2010; Helsing and Anduril every round) ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum)).\n- **Growth rounds are underwritten on economics, not AGI.** Anthropic at $60B qualified because coding revenue was real; he weighs revenue multiple (about 20x at the $183B round against 50-100x for peers) (same source).\n- **Losing is a signal you are in the right fights.** A win rate just over 30% against five to seven top firms is fine (same source).\n- **Concentration with a cap:** largest positions should be built in one fund before crossing to the next, capped at about 10-15% of a fund (same source).\n- **Open-source bet on Mistral is explicit:** he sees it as the only Western open-source lab enterprises care about, and admits he \"was anxious about it\" (same source).\n- **Applied AI over research:** AI matters to him as transformation of healthcare, defense, industrials, energy and financial services ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/hemant-taneja)). The 2025 framework has three pillars: intelligence, infrastructure and workforce ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/business-transformation-with-applied-ai)).\n- **Open weights as strategy:** \"open source principles have been the cornerstone of our mission\" (Mistral A post, [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-mistral-ai)); River pitched as US open-weight leadership ([src](https://river.ai/series-seed-series-a-funding)).\n- **Build, don't just back:** Creation has hatched 45+ companies ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/fundxii)). The 2024 hospital purchase (Summa Health, [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-acquisition-of-summa-health)) and Percepta extend that.\n- **Liquidity is the industry's binding constraint:** \"VC isn’t dead, but we think it’s in need of an accelerant\"; his fix is CAC financing ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/financed-to-endure)).\n- **Calls that went right:** health assurance thesis in 2019, before COVID; Samsara 2017; Stripe about 2010; Applied Intuition 2019 (all self-reported in GC posts). **Calls that went wrong:** SAIL SPAC vehicles; overpaying in 2021 (LP letter).\n\n## Read these first\n0. [20VC transcript, Sept 2025](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum): added in the second pass and now the first thing to read; everything he says about Anthropic, Mistral, OpenAI and indexing is here.\n1. [Hemant's Year-End Letter to LPs](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/hemants-year-end-letter-to-lps) (2022): candid on mistakes and on what he thinks venture is for.\n2. [Announcing Fund XII](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/fundxii) (2024): how the platform and funds are split.\n3. [Unveiling Percepta](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/unveiling-percepta) (2025): GC owning an AI lab and transformation company outright.\n4. [Own Your AI Experience](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/own-your-ai-experience) (2026): his frontier-lab conversation with Babuschkin.\n5. [Rethinking Venture in an Era of Concentration](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/rethinking-venture-in-an-era-of-concentration) (2026): his Q2 review.\n\n## Social engagement\nX was not fetched directly. One X post is known through press: his May 2026 \"quadrupling down on Anthropic\" post ([src](https://inshorts.com/en/amp_news/quadrupling-down-on-anthropic--indian-origin-general-catalyst-ceo-1780051538433)). On GC's site he co-signs posts with Paul Kwan (defense, energy), Alexandre Momeni (health AI, Mistral), Marc Bhargava (Creation), Neeraj Arora (India) and Jeannette zu Fürstenberg (Europe).\n\n## Portfolio\n**How many AI companies.** GC's site credits Taneja on **103 companies** (crawl of all 620 portfolio pages on 2026-11-10). Only **14** carry GC's own \"Industry: Artificial Intelligence\" tag, which is the count in the firm file. By this repo's taxonomy, at least **28** are AI, because GC files many AI companies under Healthcare, Defense or Enterprise. Below is every AI deal he leads, sits on the board of or is named on. Full rows: `data/rows/hemant-taneja-deals.csv`. \"Named\" means an \"Investors:\" credit on GC's page or a byline on GC's post, which is not proof that he led.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Latent Space | Undisclosed (backed since 2015) | 2015 | ? | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/latent-space) |\n| Commure | Creation (Taneja co-founder) | 2017 | yes | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/announcing-commure) |\n| Applied Intuition | Series B ($40M) | 2019 | yes | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-applied-intuition) |\n| Applied Intuition | Series E ($250M), re-up | 2024-03 | no | | same |\n| Mandl / Nirvana | Undisclosed (backed since 2020) | 2020 | ? | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/mandl) |\n| Athelas (now Commure) | Undisclosed (since 2021) | 2021 | ? | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/athelas) |\n| Beacon Software | Undisclosed (since 2021) | 2021 | ? | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/beacon) |\n| Black Ore | Undisclosed (since 2022) | 2022 | ? | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/black-ore) |\n| Substrate | Seed, then 2025 round | ~2022; 2025-10 | no | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-substrate) |\n| Hippocratic AI | Seed ($50M, co-created) → A ($53M) → C ($126M) | 2023-05; 2024-03; 2025-11 | co-led; co-led; no | a16z; Avenir (C lead) | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-co-creation-of-hippocratic-ai) |\n| Mistral AI | Seed → Series A → Series B co-lead | 2023-06; 2023-12; 2024-06 | no; no; co-led | Lightspeed; a16z; DST | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/tripling-down-on-mistral-ai) |\n| Helsing | Series B (€209M) | 2023-09 | yes | Saab | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-helsing) |\n| Latent Health | Undisclosed (since 2023) | 2023 | ? | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/latent-health) |\n| Chalk | Undisclosed (since 2023) | 2023 | ? | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/chalk) |\n| Crescendo | Creation incubation | 2024-01 | yes | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-creation-of-crescendo) |\n| Long Lake | Creation first investment | 2024-02 | yes | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/manufacturing-outliers-long-lake) |\n| Different | Undisclosed (since 2024) | 2024 | ? | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/different) |\n| Moonvalley | Seed ($70M) | 2024-11 | co-led | Khosla | [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-company-moonvalley-raises-70-million-in-funding-to-develop-future-of-generative-media-302307953.html) |\n| Manas AI | Seed ($24.6M) | 2025-01 | co-led | Reid Hoffman | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-manas-ai) |\n| Prepared | Series C ($80M) | 2025-05 | yes | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-prepared) |\n| Cognition | Undisclosed (since 2025) | 2025 | ? | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/cognition) |\n| Transfyr | Undisclosed (since 2025) | 2025 | ? | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/transfyr) |\n| **Anthropic** | **Series E ($3.5B at $61.5B), first check, \"a few hundred million\"** | 2025-03 | no | Lightspeed (lead) | [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-series-e-at-usd61-5b-post-money-valuation) |\n| Anthropic | Series F ($13B at $183B), second check, another few hundred million | 2025-09 | no | ICONIQ, Fidelity, Lightspeed | [src](https://www.generalatlantic.com/media-article/anthropic-raises-13b-series-f-at-183b-post-money-valuation/) |\n| Anthropic | Series G ($30B at $380B) | 2026-02 | no | GIC, Coatue (leads) | [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-30-billion-series-g-funding-380-billion-post-money-valuation) |\n| Anthropic | Series H ($65B at $965B), GC's largest single investment | 2026-05 | no | Altimeter, Dragoneer, Greenoaks, Sequoia (leads) | [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h) |\n| Interaction Co (Poke) | Seed | 2025-09 | yes | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/seeding-the-future-with-the-interaction-company-of-california) |\n| Percepta | GC-owned (co-founder) | 2025-10 | yes | AWS, Anthropic (partners) | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/unveiling-percepta) |\n| General Intuition | Seed ($133.7M) | 2025-10 | co-led | Khosla | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/16/general-intuition-lands-134m-seed-to-teach-agents-spatial-reasoning/) |\n| **Chai Discovery** | **Series B ($130M), board seat** | 2025-12 | co-led | Oak HC/FT, Thrive, OpenAI | [src](https://www.oakhcft.com/news/chai-discovery-announces-130-million-series-b-to-transform-molecular-discovery) |\n| Engram | Seed (first round) | 2026-06 | co-led | | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-engram) |\n| River AI | Seed + Series A ($1.1B) | 2026-08 | co-led | AMP PBC, NVIDIA, AMD | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/) |\n\n**Timing pattern.** He was first or at founding on Hippocratic, Commure, Crescendo, Long Lake, Percepta, Interaction Co, River AI and Mistral (\"at their inception\", [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/tripling-down-on-mistral-ai)). He came in late on Anthropic (Series E in March 2025, four years after founding and after Spark, Google, Amazon, Menlo and Lightspeed; corrected in the second pass from \"Series F\"), then took every round since (F, G, H) and Chai (at the B, after Thrive, OpenAI and Menlo). He doubled down on Mistral (three rounds in 12 months), Hippocratic (three rounds), Applied Intuition and Substrate.\n\n**Non-AI credits on GC pages** (for completeness, not in the rows): Stripe, Snap, Samsara, Anduril (tagged non-AI in this repo), Gusto, Ro, Polymarket, Circle, Livongo, Pacific Fusion and about 60 more health and fintech names.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Anthropic (2025-26), added second pass:** GC had no position until the March 2025 Series E at $61.5B, led by Lightspeed. Taneja's stated trigger was coding traction turning a model lab into an enterprise business; revenue was \"under a billion\" when GC underwrote it and beat GC's model. A few hundred million went in at E and again at F ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum)). GC is then named in the G and H, and the H is described as GC's largest single investment ever ([src](https://inshorts.com/en/amp_news/quadrupling-down-on-anthropic--indian-origin-general-catalyst-ceo-1780051538433)). Harry Stebbings opens the topic with \"you've got to thank Isan for this one\"; who that is could not be identified. GC's page credits Taneja and Christopher Kauffman.\n- **Mistral (2023), added second pass:** Jean-Charles Samuelian of Alan introduced Taneja to Arthur Mensch before Mensch had met any VC. The first pitch went badly by Taneja's account, yet GC joined the seed that Lightspeed led, then the A, then co-led the B. Jeannette zu Fürstenberg holds the board seat ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum)).\n- **River AI (2026):** Forbes reported talks to lead up to $1B in May 2026, before any product existed (see `people/igor-babuschkin.md`). The final round was $1.1B, co-led with AMP PBC. Taneja himself did the launch interview, which shows the relationship began when Babuschkin was starting xAI ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/own-your-ai-experience)).\n- **Mistral (2023-24):** GC says it partnered \"at their inception\", joined a16z's A, then co-led the B with DST six months later ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/tripling-down-on-mistral-ai)).\n- **Hippocratic AI (2023):** Co-created with Munjal Shah and a16z out of GC's health-assurance ecosystem ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-co-creation-of-hippocratic-ai)). Taneja at the Series C: \"We believed in Hippocratic AI and the team since inception\" ([src](http://hippocraticai.com/hippocratic-ai-announces-series-c-funding-126-million)).\n- **Long Lake (2024):** Began as conversations between Alex Taubman and Marc Bhargava; Taneja and Ken Chenault then joined. Creation led the first round in Feb 2024, and the company has made 40 acquisitions since ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/manufacturing-outliers-long-lake)).\n- **Commure + Athelas (2023):** Taneja merged two GC companies into one AI-augmented healthcare platform, the \"Amazon of healthcare\" ecosystem idea ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/commure-and-athelas)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Arthur Mensch (Mistral) | First VC Mensch met; intro by Jean-Charles Samuelian (Alan) | 2023 | [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum) |\n| Jean-Charles Samuelian (Alan) | Introduced him to Mensch | 2023 | [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum) |\n| Jeannette zu Fürstenberg | GC partner who holds the Mistral board seat; seeded Helsing | 2023- | [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum) |\n| Christopher Kauffman | Co-credited on Anthropic | 2025 | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/anthropic) |\n| Paul Graham | Offered him the Coinbase seed, which he passed on | ~2012 | [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum) |\n| Joel Cutler | GC co-founder; source of \"Price only hurts once\" | 2002- | [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum) |\n| Drew Houston | Interned via Taneja; Taneja did not fund Dropbox | ~2007 | [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum) |\n| Igor Babuschkin (River AI) | Met around xAI's founding; co-led River | ~2023 | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/own-your-ai-experience) |\n| Annie Lamont (Oak HC/FT) | Co-board members, Chai Discovery | 2025-12 | [src](https://www.oakhcft.com/news/chai-discovery-announces-130-million-series-b-to-transform-molecular-discovery) |\n| Hirsh Jain | Co-founder, Percepta | 2025 | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/unveiling-percepta) |\n| Munjal Shah | Co-created Hippocratic AI | 2023 | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-co-creation-of-hippocratic-ai) |\n| Glen Tullman | Co-founded Livongo, Transcarent | — | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/chris-bischoff) |\n| Sanjit Biswas (Samsara) | MIT network; Highfive board together | pre-2017 | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/a-milestone-for-samsara) |\n| Alex Taubman | Co-created Long Lake | 2024 | [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/manufacturing-outliers-long-lake) |\n| Marc Bhargava, Quentin Clark, Max Rimpel | Co-credited on frontier deals | 2024- | GC company pages |\n| Paul Kwan, Alexandre Momeni | Co-authors on defense and health-AI posts | 2023- | GC stories |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who is \"Isan\", whom Harry Stebbings credits for GC's Anthropic entry? (auto-caption spelling; needs-id lead.)\n- Size of GC's Series G and H Anthropic checks; only \"largest single investment\" is reported, and the X post was not fetched.\n- Board seats at Applied Intuition and Hippocratic AI appear only in a search snippet of aggregator profiles; not confirmed on any page fetched. Confirmed: Chai Discovery only.\n- Transcripts still to pull: Sourcery (2026-04), Invest Like the Best EP.382, Turpentine VC E74, The Generalist (all blocked or without transcript).\n- Was Taneja named on Adept? GC invested in March 2023, but the post has no byline ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-adept-empowering-knowledge-workers-with-ai)).\n- Did GC invest in xAI? Taneja's phrase \"The xAI story became part of that\" is ambiguous.\n- Other board seats: Commure and Samsara are likely but unconfirmed (the Samsara IR page didn't render).\n- Hippocratic Series B (2025-01) participation. (Anthropic E, G and H participation is now confirmed from Anthropic's releases.)\n- First pass pulled no podcast transcripts; the second pass pulled one (20VC).\n- LP status: nothing found that shows him as an LP in any fund.\n\n## Sources\n- https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/general-catalyst-ceo-hemant-taneja-lessons-scaling-gc-to-40bn-in-aum (20VC transcript, auto-captions; video https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DWvYbRzC-g0)\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-series-e-at-usd61-5b-post-money-valuation\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-30-billion-series-g-funding-380-billion-post-money-valuation\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/companies/anthropic\n- https://inshorts.com/en/amp_news/quadrupling-down-on-anthropic--indian-origin-general-catalyst-ceo-1780051538433 (secondary, cites Firstpost and Taneja's X post)\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hemant_Taneja (secondary)\n- https://app.icebreaker.xyz/profiles/linkedin-hemanttaneja (aggregator)\n- https://ch.marketscreener.com/insider/HEMANT-TANEJA-A05L5N/ (aggregator)\n- https://www.generalist.com/p/inside-general-catalyst-hemant-taneja (show notes only)\n- https://www.castfox.net/podcast/sourcery-6853139/episode/general-catalysts-first-ever-quarterly-review-ceo-hemant-taneja-381633 (show notes only)\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/hemant-taneja\n- GC portfolio pages, all 620 crawled from https://www.generalcatalyst.com/sitemap.xml on 2026-11-10 (103 credit Taneja)\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/own-your-ai-experience\n- https://www.oakhcft.com/news/chai-discovery-announces-130-million-series-b-to-transform-molecular-discovery\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/unveiling-percepta\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/hemants-year-end-letter-to-lps\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/fundxii\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/financed-to-endure\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/manufacturing-outliers-long-lake\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-applied-intuition\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-mistral-ai\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/tripling-down-on-mistral-ai\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-co-creation-of-hippocratic-ai\n- http://hippocraticai.com/hippocratic-ai-announces-series-c-funding-126-million\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/a-milestone-for-samsara\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/commure-and-athelas\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/business-transformation-with-applied-ai\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-substrate\n- https://river.ai/series-seed-series-a-funding\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/\n", "person:henry-kravis": "\n# Henry Kravis\n\n*Created 2026-11-10 at hop depth 2, building on mentions in `firms/nova-global.md`, `people/koko-xs.md`, `people/stanley-druckenmiller.md`, `people/micky-malka.md`, `people/pat-grady.md`, `companies/flapping-airplanes.md` and the 2026-11-10 pattern files.*\n\n## One-line take\nThe leveraged-buyout pioneer is now an individual LP and late-round angel in AI. He matters to the owner as a possible anchor LP for a young manager, not as a deal source to follow.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he gets allocation:** His name helps a cap table, and the founders and young managers who want it come to him. He shows up as a named individual next to institutional leads in Flapping Airplanes' A ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/)), Rogo's C ([rogo.com](https://rogo.com/blog/scaling-rogo-to-build-the-future-of-investment-banking-our-75m-series-c-and-european-expansion)) and PhysicsX's A ([SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=638683)).\n- **The pattern:** He is never first and never leads. In every AI deal found, he joins a round after a top fund has priced it: General Catalyst (PhysicsX A), Sequoia (Rogo C), Blackstone (Norm Ai), Thrive/DST (OpenEvidence) and Index/Lux (Flapping A). His AI bets are applied AI in industries he knows (finance, legal, medicine, engineering), plus one lab. That matches what he reportedly told Columbia students in 2026: KKR goes after the AI \"picks and shovels\" rather than picking AI winners ([Columbia Business School](https://business.columbia.edu/insights/henry-kravis-career-lessons-mba-students-need)).\n- **Where he is different from other billionaire angels:** He backs new managers. With George Roberts, he was an LP in Ansa Capital's $100M debut fund, started by an ex-KKR Growth principal, alongside Princeton's endowment and Peter Chernin (Bloomberg, 2022-04-26, [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-04-26/kkr-founders-bet-on-former-executive-s-100-million-venture-fund)). He is also a named backer of Koko Xs's Nova ([kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/)), and reportedly of ParaFi's 2026 fund (Bloomberg headline, [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-03-23/henry-kravis-backed-parafi-raises-125-million-for-new-fund)).\n- **What constrains him / what he'd never do:** He would not lead a seed or price a lab round. His access to new managers runs through people he knows (an ex-KKR principal, the Rhône-connected Nova team), so a cold approach is unlikely to work.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He hired a VC to run his personal startup book.** Jed Lenzner joined him from Tusk Venture Partners as he put \"millions of dollars\" into startups such as Kalshi, Ethena and 100Plus ([Bloomberg Law, 2022-01-21](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/business-and-practice/henry-kravis-builds-his-own-venture-portfolio-outside-of-kkr)). For the owner, Lenzner (not Kravis) is probably the practical contact. Lenzner's current role is unconfirmed.\n- **Bloomberg calls it a long-running effort, not a retirement hobby:** \"a decade-long drive to sink more of his personal wealth into young, fast-growing companies\" ([Bloomberg Law, 2022-01-21](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/business-and-practice/henry-kravis-builds-his-own-venture-portfolio-outside-of-kkr)).\n- **He co-LPs debut funds next to institutional emerging-manager money.** Ansa's first close also included PRINCO (Princeton), Accolade Partners and Financial Investments Corp. The fund writes \"$5 million to $15 million\" initial checks into growth-stage companies (Business Wire 2022-04-22 via search; [src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20220422005564/en/Ansa-Capital-Launches-With-Inaugural-Fund-Announces-$100-Million-First-Close)). That gives a sense of the size of manager he will back.\n- **He says plainly that he can't pick AI winners.** An aggregator transcript of his 2026-06-17 Columbia fireside chat has him saying he \"could not tell you which AI company is going to work or not\" ([CEOInterviews, low confidence](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/995962/)). Columbia's own write-up describes KKR's AI exposure as \"picks and shovels\" (data centers, cloud infrastructure) ([Columbia](https://business.columbia.edu/insights/henry-kravis-career-lessons-mba-students-need)). His one lab bet (Flapping Airplanes) came through the Nova circle, not his own sourcing.\n- **Kalshi, early:** TechCrunch named him among Kalshi's investors in August 2021, alongside Sequoia, Charles Schwab and SV Angel ([TechCrunch, 2021-08-30](https://techcrunch.com/2021/08/30/this-sequoia-and-henry-kravis-backed-prediction-market-wants-to-turn-opinions-into-money/)). That was years before Kalshi's 2025 run. One aggregator places him in the seed; that is unverified.\n- **How Koko Xs uses him:** Koko's draft \"Rise of the Career Investor\" mentions Kravis only as the person who \"invented the LBO at Bear Stearns in the 70s,\" as a history of esoteric strategies, not as an LP pitch ([PDF](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/DRAFT-Rise-of-the-Career-Investor.pdf)). The repo's existing description of him as an \"archetype\" in that draft overstates what the draft says.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The deal side alone would be low: he joins late, follows a lead, and invests mostly in applied AI (PhysicsX, Rogo, Norm Ai, OpenEvidence) or outside AI (Kalshi, Illumix, Semafor). The LP side lifts the rating. He has backed at least two young managers at debut or early stage (Ansa Fund I, Nova), and he puts money into the same lab rounds as his managers (Flapping A). Deals that matter to me: **Flapping Airplanes A (2026-10)** and **PhysicsX A (2023-11)**. Ansa itself is growth-stage, not AI-lab, so it shows his LP behavior rather than any AI thesis.\n\n## Background\n- Born 1944-01-06 in Tulsa, Oklahoma ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Kravis)).\n- BA, Claremont McKenna College, 1967; MBA, Columbia Business School, 1969 ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Kravis)).\n- Worked at Bear Stearns under Jerome Kohlberg with cousin George Roberts, and became a partner there ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Kravis)).\n- Co-founded KKR in 1976. Raised the first institutional fund (about $30M) in 1978 and led the 1988 RJR Nabisco buyout ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Kravis); [KKR history](https://kkr.com/about/history/our-journey/founding-kkr)).\n- Stepped down as co-CEO in October 2021 and became co-executive chairman ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Kravis)).\n- Married to economist Marie-Josée Kravis (née Drouin) since 1994 ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Kravis)).\n- Philanthropy: $100M to Columbia Business School (Kravis Hall), $100M (2022) plus $40M (2023) to Memorial Sloan Kettering, and $100M (2024) to Loomis Chaffee ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Kravis)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [KKR: Founding KKR](https://kkr.com/about/history/our-journey/founding-kkr) | Firm history page. The KKR leadership-profile URLs I tried returned 404 (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No verified personal account found |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | No verified profile URL |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only (see below) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-06-17: Columbia Business School Distinguished Speaker Series, fireside chat with Jorge Guzman ([event page](https://events.business.columbia.edu/event/conversation-henry-kravis-co-founder-and-co-executive-chairman-kkr); [write-up](https://business.columbia.edu/insights/henry-kravis-career-lessons-mba-students-need)). Covered KKR's AI \"picks and shovels\" approach and why he would not start another PE fund today (the second point is from an aggregator transcript only, [CEOInterviews](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/995962/)). Fortune's follow-up piece: [2026-06-30](https://fortune.com/2026/06/30/wall-street-kkr-cofounder-henry-kravis-early-career-lesson-from-roy-disney-come-prepared-led-to-later-billionaire-success-gen-z-advice/).\n- 2025-12-01: Elad Gil, \"Henry Kravis on Building KKR, Deal-Making, and the Future of AI in Private Equity\". Covers roll-ups in the age of AI and his partnership with Roberts. Only an aggregator listing was found ([Podwise](https://podwise.ai/episodes/6149718)); no transcript was fetched.\n- 2023-10-17 (recorded): Goldman Sachs Exchanges, PE culture and markets ([Goldman Sachs](https://www.goldmansachs.com/insights/goldman-sachs-exchanges/kkr-henry-kravis-on-private-equity-culture-and-global-markets)).\n- Undated (posted 2021): Talks at GS. He mentions personally investing in startups and in medicine, per an aggregator transcript ([CEOInterviews](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/985738/)).\n\n## How they think\n- **Curiosity as the investor's trait.** In the Talks at GS transcript, he says a good investor \"has to be curious\" ([CEOInterviews](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/985738/), aggregator).\n- **On AI, he says he can't pick winners.** He prefers infrastructure exposure at KKR and buys applied AI personally where he knows the end market (finance, legal, medicine).\n- **Permanent capital and roll-ups.** In the Columbia transcript (aggregator), he says he would build a roll-up rather than start another PE fund. This fits Elad Gil's AI-roll-up interest and the topic of the Gil episode.\n- **Calls he got right:** Kalshi by 2021; PhysicsX at the A (2023), before its 2025 B. It's too early to judge Flapping.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Bloomberg, \"Henry Kravis builds his own venture portfolio outside of KKR\" (2022-01-21)](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/business-and-practice/henry-kravis-builds-his-own-venture-portfolio-outside-of-kkr): the personal book, and Jed Lenzner's hire.\n2. [Ansa Capital launch release (2022-04-22)](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20220422005564/en/Ansa-Capital-Launches-With-Inaugural-Fund-Announces-$100-Million-First-Close): the debut fund he backed, with its co-LPs.\n3. [Columbia Business School write-up (2026)](https://business.columbia.edu/insights/henry-kravis-career-lessons-mba-students-need): his AI \"picks and shovels\" framing.\n4. [kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/): Nova's backer list, with Kravis next to Andreessen, Druckenmiller and Malka.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no verified X or LinkedIn presence, and his public voice is limited to conference talks and podcasts.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Flapping Airplanes | Series A ($1B; ~$6B reported) | 2026-10-21 | no (Index, Lux) | Sequoia, Duquesne FO, Micky Malka, Steven A. Cohen, Nova SPV | [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/) |\n| Rogo | Series C $75M | 2026-01-28 | no (Sequoia) | Wells Fargo, Thrive, Khosla, Tiger, J.P. Morgan | [rogo.com](https://rogo.com/blog/scaling-rogo-to-build-the-future-of-investment-banking-our-75m-series-c-and-european-expansion) |\n| OpenEvidence | listed as prior investor at $250M Series D | before 2026-01-21 | no (Thrive, DST co-led D) | Thrive, DST | [Business Wire (via search; not fetched)](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260121029132/en) |\n| Norm Ai | $50M Blackstone round (as an individual backer) | 2025-11 | no (Blackstone) | Bain Capital, Vanguard, Coatue, Craft, Tony James, Philippe Laffont, Marc Benioff | [Pulse 2.0 (secondary)](https://pulse2.com/norm-ai-50-million-funding/amp/) |\n| PhysicsX | Series A $32M | 2023-11-27 | no (General Catalyst) | Standard Industries, NGP, Radius Capital | [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=638683) |\n| Illumix | Series A $18M | 2023-03 | no | LightShed Ventures | [Yahoo Finance](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/illumix-closes-series-round-funding-133000832.html) |\n| Kalshi | early (round unconfirmed) | by 2021-08-30 | no | Sequoia, Charles Schwab, SV Angel | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2021/08/30/this-sequoia-and-henry-kravis-backed-prediction-market-wants-to-turn-opinions-into-money/) |\n| Ethena; 100Plus | undisclosed | by 2022-01 | no | 100Plus seed also lists George Roberts (Crunchbase, aggregator) | [Bloomberg Law](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/business-and-practice/henry-kravis-builds-his-own-venture-portfolio-outside-of-kkr) |\n| **LP:** Ansa Capital Fund I | $100M first close | 2022-04 | n/a | George Roberts, PRINCO, Accolade, FIC, Peter Chernin | [Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-04-26/kkr-founders-bet-on-former-executive-s-100-million-venture-fund) |\n| **LP:** Nova (Koko Xs) | vehicle not specified | by 2026 | n/a | Andreessen, Druckenmiller, Malka | [kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/) |\n| **LP:** ParaFi new venture fund ($125M) | backer | 2026-03 | n/a | | [Bloomberg headline](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-03-23/henry-kravis-backed-parafi-raises-125-million-for-new-fund) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Flapping Airplanes A (2026-10):** He comes in as an individual alongside two other Nova backers (Druckenmiller via Duquesne, and Malka). Nova filed a single-deal SPV about 10 weeks before the announcement (see `firms/nova-global.md`). Whether Kravis came in through Nova or directly is unknown.\n- **Ansa (2022-04):** Co-founder Allan Jean-Baptiste was a principal in KKR's tech growth group. The ex-KKR tie is the only stated link ([Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-04-26/kkr-founders-bet-on-former-executive-s-100-million-venture-fund)).\n- **Norm Ai (2025-11):** A Blackstone-led round with other PE and hedge-fund individuals (Tony James of Blackstone, Philippe Laffont) — a regulatory-AI deal for the PE world ([Pulse 2.0](https://pulse2.com/norm-ai-50-million-funding/amp/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| George Roberts | Cousin, KKR co-founder, co-LP in Ansa | 1976 (KKR) | [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Kravis); [Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-04-26/kkr-founders-bet-on-former-executive-s-100-million-venture-fund) |\n| Jed Lenzner | Joined him from Tusk Venture Partners to work on personal startup investing | 2022 | [Bloomberg Law](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/business-and-practice/henry-kravis-builds-his-own-venture-portfolio-outside-of-kkr) |\n| Koko Xs / Nova | LP / backer | by 2026 | [kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/) |\n| Allan Jean-Baptiste, Marco DeMeireles (Ansa) | LP in their debut fund | 2022 | [Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-04-26/kkr-founders-bet-on-former-executive-s-100-million-venture-fund) |\n| Stanley Druckenmiller, Micky Malka, Marc Andreessen | Fellow Nova backers; Druckenmiller and Malka are also in the Flapping A | 2026 | [kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/) |\n| Peter Chernin, PRINCO, Accolade Partners | Co-LPs in Ansa Fund I | 2022 | [Business Wire](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20220422005564/en/Ansa-Capital-Launches-With-Inaugural-Fund-Announces-$100-Million-First-Close) |\n| ParaFi | Backer | by 2026-03 | [Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-03-23/henry-kravis-backed-parafi-raises-125-million-for-new-fund) |\n| Elad Gil | Interviewed him on a podcast | 2025-12 | [Podwise](https://podwise.ai/episodes/6149718) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Family-office vehicle.** Altss (an aggregator with no primary citations) lists \"Stonecroft Management LLC\" (2022, with a ParaFi allocation and a Thrive/Joshua Kushner tie) and \"REIK & Co.\" (1996) ([Altss](https://altss.com/profile/stonecroft-management-llc-henry-r-kravis-family-office)). Check SEC IAPD/ADV and Form D related-person fields.\n- Is Jed Lenzner still running the personal book? Who sources the AI deals now?\n- Which Nova vehicle is he in (Fund II, Nova Select's single $5M investor, or the Flapping SPV)?\n- Crunchbase (aggregator) also lists Semafor (2026-01), Setpoint (2024-08) and Applied Intuition (2024-03) rounds. Confirm whether he was a personal participant in each.\n- What did he say about AI roll-ups on the Elad Gil episode? Get the transcript.\n- Is \"Ethena\" (2022) the crypto Ethena Labs or a different company? (needs-id)\n\n## Sources\n- https://news.bloomberglaw.com/business-and-practice/henry-kravis-builds-his-own-venture-portfolio-outside-of-kkr\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-04-26/kkr-founders-bet-on-former-executive-s-100-million-venture-fund\n- https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20220422005564/en/Ansa-Capital-Launches-With-Inaugural-Fund-Announces-$100-Million-First-Close\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-03-23/henry-kravis-backed-parafi-raises-125-million-for-new-fund\n- https://kokoxs.com/\n- https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/\n- https://rogo.com/blog/scaling-rogo-to-build-the-future-of-investment-banking-our-75m-series-c-and-european-expansion\n- https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260121029132/en\n- https://pulse2.com/norm-ai-50-million-funding/amp/\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=638683\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/news/illumix-closes-series-round-funding-133000832.html\n- https://techcrunch.com/2021/08/30/this-sequoia-and-henry-kravis-backed-prediction-market-wants-to-turn-opinions-into-money/\n- https://business.columbia.edu/insights/henry-kravis-career-lessons-mba-students-need\n- https://events.business.columbia.edu/event/conversation-henry-kravis-co-founder-and-co-executive-chairman-kkr\n- https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/995962/ (aggregator transcript)\n- https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/985738/ (aggregator transcript)\n- https://podwise.ai/episodes/6149718 (aggregator)\n- https://www.goldmansachs.com/insights/goldman-sachs-exchanges/kkr-henry-kravis-on-private-equity-culture-and-global-markets\n- https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/DRAFT-Rise-of-the-Career-Investor.pdf\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Kravis\n- https://kkr.com/about/history/our-journey/founding-kkr\n- https://altss.com/profile/stonecroft-management-llc-henry-r-kravis-family-office (aggregator)\n", "person:howard-wright": "\n# Howard Wright\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (NVIDIA people task). Sources use \"he\" for him (SportsVideo 2020, GeekWire via search, GENCI release). `check_capacity: sub-10m` is forced by the enum, which has no zero value. He writes no checks at all, and the value describes no check size. `seniority: vp` is his actual title. Both podcast appearances found are untranscribed, so no quote below comes from audio.*\n\n## One-line take\nWright runs NVIDIA's free startup program, the only NVIDIA door a founder can open without an introduction. It is the top of the funnel that NVentures and corporate development pick from, but he takes no equity and signs nothing, so he is an intro channel and not a co-investor.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** Inception is NVIDIA's mass-market startup layer. NVIDIA's own author bio (2024) says it supports more than 22,000 startups, and his EIC Summit 2026 speaker page says more than 35,000 ([NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/howardwright/), [EIC](https://eic.ec.europa.eu/howard-wright_en)). Membership roughly grew by half in under two years. Entry is self-serve: \"There are no application fees, deadlines, or cohorts.\" No equity is taken ([nvidia.com/startups](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/)).\n- **What he controls:** credits, preferred hardware pricing, co-marketing, partner bundles (Microsoft for Startups, 2024), sovereign compute access (AI Factory France, 2025-2026), and the investor-intro layer: the **Inception VC Alliance** and **Inception Capital Connect**. Investor intros are gated on eligibility, and \"funding status\" is used to decide who fits ([nvidia.com/startups](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/)).\n- **Constraint:** no capital. NVentures (Siddeek) and corporate development (Bhagwati) hold the equity ([firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md)). No source describes a formal handoff from Inception to NVentures, and no source names Wright on any investment.\n- **What he'd never do:** take equity or price a round. He also cannot admit GPU clouds. Inception lists \"Cloud service providers\" and \"Resellers and distributors\" among organizations that \"do not qualify\" ([nvidia.com/startups](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/)), which is the category NVIDIA's balance sheet funds most often (Crusoe, Fireworks, fal, Deep Infra, Firmus in the [ledger](../sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md)). That last point is my inference from putting the two together, not a stated policy link.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Inception membership comes before NVentures money, with a lag of about two years.** Euronews reports Moon Surgical joined Inception and NVIDIA invested \"about two years\" later; Siddeek then sat as board observer ([Euronews via Yahoo, 2024-07-06](https://www.yahoo.com/news/little-known-nvidia-programme-backing-070006781.html)). Separately, Wright's own October 2024 post features Artisight as an Inception member ([NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/nvidia-inception-microsoft-startups-ai-healthcare/)), and GCV lists ArtiSight in the NVentures portfolio (firm file, [GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md)). Two cases are not a stated pipeline, but they fit one. The Euronews line is paraphrased because no short verbatim fragment was captured; the insight row uses a quote from Wright's own post.\n- **He brought AWS's playbook and kept the AWS tie.** At AWS he cited \"more than 6,000 million dollars invested in incentives\" for startups (\"Con más de 6.000 millones de dólares invertidos en incentivos\", [Forbes Colombia, 2024-01-10](https://forbes.co/2024/01/10/tecnologia/startups-nube-amazon-web-services/)). Two years after leaving, his VivaTech 2026 session (\"Growth & Scale: From Pre-Seed to Series A\") sits in the **AWS Founders Lounge** ([NVIDIA VivaTech page](https://www.nvidia.com/en-eu/events/vivatech/); the day of the session is not resolved on the page). This is a third AWS-to-NVIDIA data point after [Clifford Platt](clifford-platt.md), and it suggests the hyperscaler startup desks still co-host.\n- **He now pitches to investors, not just founders.** He gave the plenary keynote \"Inside NVIDIA: AI, Startups, and the New Rules of Early-Stage Investing\" at the Angel Capital Association Summit, 2026-04-22, with Todd Federman ([ACA](https://events.angelcapitalassociation.org/summit2026/session/4009827/keynote-inside-nvidia-ai-startups-and-the-new-rules-of-early-stage-investing)). Taken with the VC Alliance, Inception is NVIDIA's investor-relations surface for early-stage capital.\n- **Sovereign-compute gatekeeper in Europe.** AI Factory France gives Inception members with European R&D access to French and European NVIDIA supercomputers (Jean Zay). Wright: \"France has a strong foundation for the next wave of AI innovation\" ([GENCI, 2026-06-18](https://www.genci.fr/en/news/ai-factory-france-nvidia-program-launches-vivatech-accelerating-ai-innovation-french-and)). Early-access startups named are Pleias, Nebula and Ryax Technologies (same source). Inception membership is the eligibility gate for public compute here.\n- **Background not in the NVIDIA bio:** a Sway Minds recap calls him a former professional basketball player and quotes him: \"Adaptation is the throughline\" ([Sway Capital recap, 2024-09-30](https://sway.capital/insights/recap-sway-minds-2024-from-court-to-code-with-howard-wright)). Team and years are not given. The EIC page also adds \"partnership roles at Intel Capital\", the only venture-side line in his history ([EIC](https://eic.ec.europa.eu/howard-wright_en), [Startup Grind](https://www.startupgrind.com/events/details/startup-grind-san-jose-presents-sgsj-hosting-howard-wright-vp-startup-ecosystem-nvidia/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** He writes no checks and selects nothing at the $20M-into-a-big-seed level. The program is built for breadth (35k+ members, self-serve entry, under-10-years-old bar), not for the handful of frontier-lab seeds in THESIS.md. None of the NVIDIA lab rounds in this repo (Periodic, Ineffable, Reflection, Thinking Machines, humans&) carries any Inception attribution. Two narrow uses: (1) the VC Alliance is a free deal-flow feed, worth joining as an investor if it is open to the owner's fund; (2) he is a credible person to ask \"which Inception members are NVIDIA engineers spending time on,\" because the Moon Surgical and Artisight cases suggest NVentures later invests in some members. Upgrade to medium only if a source shows Inception formally feeding NVentures deal flow.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford, BA in qualitative economics ([SportsVideo 2020-12-04](https://www.sportsvideo.org/2020/12/04/new-c360-ceo-howard-wright-sees-broadcast-tech-putting-fans-on-the-field-of-play/); [NVIDIA blog bio](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/howardwright/)).\n- Former professional basketball player, details not given ([Sway recap](https://sway.capital/insights/recap-sway-minds-2024-from-court-to-code-with-howard-wright)).\n- Qualcomm, then Intel: VP of global business development at Intel Sports ([SportsVideo](https://www.sportsvideo.org/2020/12/04/new-c360-ceo-howard-wright-sees-broadcast-tech-putting-fans-on-the-field-of-play/)), plus \"partnership roles at Intel Capital\" ([Startup Grind](https://www.startupgrind.com/events/details/startup-grind-san-jose-presents-sgsj-hosting-howard-wright-vp-startup-ecosystem-nvidia/)). No dates.\n- CEO of C360 Technologies (Pittsburgh, immersive sports video), announced 2020-12-04 ([SportsVideo](https://www.sportsvideo.org/2020/12/04/new-c360-ceo-howard-wright-sees-broadcast-tech-putting-fans-on-the-field-of-play/)). Aggregator profiles (Stackforce, MarketScreener; seen in search snippets only, not retrieved) give 2020-2022.\n- AWS, VP and Global Head of Startups. In role as of 2024-01-10 ([Forbes Colombia](https://forbes.co/2024/01/10/tecnologia/startups-nube-amazon-web-services/)). Start of April 2022 comes from an aggregator snippet only (Stackforce, not retrieved). Successor Jon Jones was named later in 2024 (GeekWire, seen in a search snippet; the page returned 403).\n- NVIDIA, joined 2024 as VP of NVIDIA Inception ([NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/howardwright/)). First NVIDIA byline 2024-10-21. Billed as VP of Startup Ecosystem from 2025 onward ([Venture with Grace notes, 2025-02-21](https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/venture-with-grace/episode/nvidia-vp-howard-wright-on-accelerating-the-global-ai-startup-ecosystem-62195); [EIC](https://eic.ec.europa.eu/howard-wright_en)). Whether that is a promotion or a renaming is not sourced.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/howardwright/ | NVIDIA blog author page; two posts (2024-10, 2024-11) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | not searched in depth |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-09-30: Sway Minds 2024, \"From Court to Code\", with Soledad O'Brien. Recap quotes: \"From sports to startups to semiconductors, the ability to pivot with purpose is everything\" ([Sway](https://sway.capital/insights/recap-sway-minds-2024-from-court-to-code-with-howard-wright)).\n- 2025-02-21: Venture with Grace, \"NVIDIA VP Howard Wright on Accelerating the Global AI Startup Ecosystem\". **Not transcribed**; show notes only ([audioscrape](https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/venture-with-grace/episode/nvidia-vp-howard-wright-on-accelerating-the-global-ai-startup-ecosystem-62195)).\n- 2025-05-22: Washington AI Network Podcast ep. 53 (Tammy Haddad), recorded at GTC 2025. **Not transcribed**; show notes say Inception supports over 27,000 startups (summary only, low confidence) ([audioboom](https://audioboom.com/posts/8724487)).\n- 2025-11-25: Adopt AI Paris keynote on the startup ecosystem; the same day, AI Factory France and Inception collaboration announced ([Intelligent CIO](https://www.intelligentcio.com/eu/2025/11/25/ai-factory-france-supercharges-ai-adoption-by-french-start-ups-and-smes-with-nvidia/), [NVIDIA Adopt AI](https://www.nvidia.com/en-eu/events/adopt-ai/)).\n- 2026-04-22: Angel Capital Association Summit, plenary keynote with Todd Federman ([ACA](https://events.angelcapitalassociation.org/summit2026/session/4009827/keynote-inside-nvidia-ai-startups-and-the-new-rules-of-early-stage-investing)).\n- 2026 (May listing): EIC Summit 2026, \"From Tech Push to Market Pull: Unlocking Europe's AI Startup Potential\" (talk title per search snippet; speaker page at [EIC](https://eic.ec.europa.eu/howard-wright_en)).\n- 2026-06 (17-19): VivaTech, \"Growth & Scale: From Pre-Seed to Series A\", AWS Founders Lounge ([NVIDIA](https://www.nvidia.com/en-eu/events/vivatech/)).\n- Upcoming: Startup Grind San Jose fireside on how founders can build relationships with NVIDIA. The page gives conflicting dates (body says Wednesday Nov 11; header says Feb 11, 2027 PST) ([Startup Grind](https://www.startupgrind.com/events/details/startup-grind-san-jose-presents-sgsj-hosting-howard-wright-vp-startup-ecosystem-nvidia/)).\n\n## How they think\n- Platform-as-ecosystem: bundles credits, SDKs and partner programs rather than capital. In his Japan post: \"The unsung stars here are software development kits\" ([NVIDIA blog, 2024-11-12](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/japan-startups-ai-innovation-accelerated-computing/)).\n- Founder-curiosity over scale, in his own words: \"The most powerful AI breakthroughs don't just come from scale\" ([Sway](https://sway.capital/insights/recap-sway-minds-2024-from-court-to-code-with-howard-wright)).\n- Speed from experiment to deployment is the recurring pitch (GENCI 2026, Intelligent CIO 2025).\n- No contrarian views or calls on record.\n\n## Read these first\n- [NVIDIA and Microsoft give AI startups a double dose of acceleration (2024-10-21)](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/nvidia-inception-microsoft-startups-ai-healthcare/): what Inception actually hands out, and the VC Alliance as the funding lever.\n- [nvidia.com/startups](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/): eligibility, exclusions (CSPs, crypto, consultancies), Capital Connect.\n- [GENCI AI Factory France release (2026-06-18)](https://www.genci.fr/en/news/ai-factory-france-nvidia-program-launches-vivatech-accelerating-ai-innovation-french-and): Inception as the gate to public supercomputers.\n- [Euronews via Yahoo (2024-07-06)](https://www.yahoo.com/news/little-known-nvidia-programme-backing-070006781.html): Moon Surgical's Inception-to-NVentures path.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found beyond event and NVIDIA blog appearances.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| None | n/a | n/a | n/a | n/a | Inception takes no equity ([nvidia.com/startups](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/)); no round names him |\n\nProgram members he has featured (not investments): Artisight, Pangaea Data, Artificial, BeeKeeperAI, Niramai (2024-10 Microsoft post); AiHUB, Kotoba Technologies, APTO, ugo, Asilla (2024-11 Japan post); Pleias, Nebula, Ryax Technologies (AI Factory France early access, 2026-06).\n\n## Deal stories\nNone: he is not on any deal. Moon Surgical is the closest program-to-equity story. It joined Inception, NVIDIA invested $55M about two years later (2023), and Siddeek took the observer seat ([Euronews via Yahoo](https://www.yahoo.com/news/little-known-nvidia-programme-backing-070006781.html)). Wright joined in 2024, after that deal, so it predates him.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NVIDIA (Inception) | VP, NVIDIA Inception / VP Startup Ecosystem | 2024 | [NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/howardwright/) |\n| Amazon Web Services | VP and Global Head of Startups | until 2024 | [Forbes Colombia](https://forbes.co/2024/01/10/tecnologia/startups-nube-amazon-web-services/) |\n| C360 Technologies | CEO | 2020-12 | [SportsVideo](https://www.sportsvideo.org/2020/12/04/new-c360-ceo-howard-wright-sees-broadcast-tech-putting-fans-on-the-field-of-play/) |\n| Intel / Intel Capital; Qualcomm | VP global BD (Intel Sports); partnership roles | before 2020 | [SportsVideo](https://www.sportsvideo.org/2020/12/04/new-c360-ceo-howard-wright-sees-broadcast-tech-putting-fans-on-the-field-of-play/), [Startup Grind](https://www.startupgrind.com/events/details/startup-grind-san-jose-presents-sgsj-hosting-howard-wright-vp-startup-ecosystem-nvidia/) |\n| GENCI / AI Factory France | Program partner (Inception members get supercomputer access) | 2025-11 | [GENCI](https://www.genci.fr/en/news/ai-factory-france-nvidia-program-launches-vivatech-accelerating-ai-innovation-french-and) |\n| Microsoft for Startups | Joint startup offer | 2024-10 | [NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/nvidia-inception-microsoft-startups-ai-healthcare/) |\n| Serge Lemonde | Inception colleague (Senior Director, Inception Global per a 2025 conference listing; reporting line not sourced) | 2025 | [firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md) |\n| Angel Capital Association / Todd Federman | Keynote co-speaker | 2026-04-22 | [ACA](https://events.angelcapitalassociation.org/summit2026/session/4009827/keynote-inside-nvidia-ai-startups-and-the-new-rules-of-early-stage-investing) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is there a formal Inception-to-NVentures referral path (Moon Surgical, Artisight), or is the overlap incidental?\n- Which VC firms are in the Inception VC Alliance, and can the owner's fund join it?\n- Who does Wright report to, and is Inception in the same org as NVentures?\n- Was \"VP Startup Ecosystem\" a 2025 promotion or a rename?\n- The two podcasts (Venture with Grace, Washington AI Network) need transcripts before any quote can be used.\n\n## Sources\n- https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/howardwright/\n- https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/nvidia-inception-microsoft-startups-ai-healthcare/\n- https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/japan-startups-ai-innovation-accelerated-computing/\n- https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/\n- https://eic.ec.europa.eu/howard-wright_en\n- https://www.startupgrind.com/events/details/startup-grind-san-jose-presents-sgsj-hosting-howard-wright-vp-startup-ecosystem-nvidia/\n- https://events.angelcapitalassociation.org/summit2026/session/4009827/keynote-inside-nvidia-ai-startups-and-the-new-rules-of-early-stage-investing\n- https://www.genci.fr/en/news/ai-factory-france-nvidia-program-launches-vivatech-accelerating-ai-innovation-french-and\n- https://www.intelligentcio.com/eu/2025/11/25/ai-factory-france-supercharges-ai-adoption-by-french-start-ups-and-smes-with-nvidia/\n- https://www.nvidia.com/en-eu/events/vivatech/\n- https://www.yahoo.com/news/little-known-nvidia-programme-backing-070006781.html\n- https://sway.capital/insights/recap-sway-minds-2024-from-court-to-code-with-howard-wright\n- https://www.sportsvideo.org/2020/12/04/new-c360-ceo-howard-wright-sees-broadcast-tech-putting-fans-on-the-field-of-play/\n- https://forbes.co/2024/01/10/tecnologia/startups-nube-amazon-web-services/\n- https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/venture-with-grace/episode/nvidia-vp-howard-wright-on-accelerating-the-global-ai-startup-ecosystem-62195\n- https://audioboom.com/posts/8724487\n- ../firms/nvidia.md ; ../sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md ; clifford-platt.md\n", "person:howie-liu": "\n# Howie Liu\n\n## One-line take\nA quiet customer-CEO angel: small cheques, about 15 to 22 investments, but early in Adept, Braintrust, Fireworks and Decagon. This page is thin on his own words about investing because none were found.\n\n## Crux\nFounders want him as a reference customer and a known SaaS CEO on the cap table, not for capital. He appears in clusters of operator-CEOs (with Jack Altman, Guillermo Rauch, Elad Gil) in rounds led by Greylock, a16z or Sequoia; he follows those leads and does not front-run them. He is constrained by a full-time CEO job and small tickets. No fund, scout role or SPV was found.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Braintrust lists him by title beside two other operator-CEOs: \"Howie Liu, CEO of Airtable; Jack Altman, Founder of Lattice; Guillermo Rauch, CEO of Vercel;\" ([src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round), 2023-12-13).\n- Directories say he \"typically invests between $5,000 and $50,000 in seed and pre-seed rounds\" ([src](https://connectsafely.ai/investors/howie-liu), aggregator).\n- In May 2023 he described himself as slow to follow trends: \"I'm generally not the type of person who jumps on bandwagon or hype trends,\" ([src](https://dot.la/howie-liu-airtable-2659955374.html)). The AI cheques cluster in 2022 to 2024.\n- Lenny's Podcast (2025-08-31) summary describes him as one of the \"IC CEOs\" who codes daily; summary only, transcript paywalled ([src](https://www.lennysnewsletter.com/p/how-we-restructured-airtables-entire-org-for-ai)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. The early cheques are in the right companies (Adept's Series A in 2022, Braintrust's pre-seed, Fireworks before its Series B), but he is a passive co-investor with no public investing voice. Useful mainly as a signal that an AI tool has enterprise-SaaS buyers.\n\n## Background\n- Co-founder and CEO of Airtable ([src](https://dot.la/howie-liu-airtable-2659955374.html)).\n- Earlier co-founded Etacts, a CRM startup acquired by Salesforce (per 20VC show notes, [src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20product-enterprises-are-not-adopting-71c)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | none found | |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | https://x.com/howietl | Not read for this profile |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | none found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNone of these was mined for transcript quotes; all are about Airtable, not investing.\n- 2025-09 — Grit — \"Airtable's AI Reboot with CEO Howie Liu\" — [link](https://getpodcast.com/podcast/go-to-market-grit/airtables-ai-reboot-with-ceo-howie-liu_0f04aeff79) — show notes mention cap tables and control.\n- 2025-08 — Lenny's Podcast — \"How we restructured Airtable's entire org for AI\" — [link](https://www.lennysnewsletter.com/p/how-we-restructured-airtables-entire-org-for-ai)\n- 2025-08 — Summation (Auren Hoffman) — [link](https://metacast.app/podcast/summation-with-auren-hoffman/SFMVNXev/airtable-ceo-howie-liu-excel-vs-access-founder-mode-and-the-future-of-saas/94LFiPIM)\n- 2025-05 — Superhuman AI — [link](https://metacast.app/podcast/superhuman-ai-decoding-the-future/vdU1E0lX/how-airtable-is-letting-anyone-build-with-ai-with-ceo-howie-liu/SvWdKCxS)\n- 2024-05 — Invest Like the Best EP.375 — \"Building Airtable\" — [link](https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/episode/howie-liu---building-airtable---invest-like-the-best-ep375) — has a capital-allocation segment.\n- 2023-08 — 20VC 20Product — \"Enterprises Are Not Adopting AI Yet\" — [link](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20product-enterprises-are-not-adopting-71c)\n- 2023-05 — dot.LA interview — [link](https://dot.la/howie-liu-airtable-2659955374.html) — \"With AI, you can actually just see for yourself.\"\n\n## How they think\n- AI as something to test hands-on, not theorise about (dot.LA quote above).\n- 2023 view (episode title only): enterprises were not yet adopting AI and services-heavy vendors would win the rollout.\n- Nothing found on how he picks angel investments, and no public pass or regret.\n\n## Read these first\n- Braintrust's seed post: the clearest picture of the company he keeps as an angel.\n- Invest Like the Best EP.375: the longest interview; the capital-allocation section is the most likely place for investing views (not yet read).\n- Grit, September 2025: cap tables and control.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed; X was not read.\n\n## Portfolio\nSourced cheques only. **(early)** marks pre-seed and seed entries. The repo ledger had four AI companies (Adept, Braintrust, Decagon, Fireworks AI); this pass adds Wordsmith AI and Sundial with a source, and four more names from aggregators only.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Adept | Series A ($65M) | 2022-04-26 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20220426005963/en/) |\n| Braintrust | Pre-seed **(early)** | 2023 | no | not listed | [src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round) |\n| Decagon | Series A | 2024-06-18 | no | not listed | [src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-a) |\n| Fireworks AI | Series A (or earlier; round not stated) | not found | no | not listed | [src](https://fireworks.ai/blog/fireworks-series-b-funding-sequoia) |\n| Wordsmith AI | Seed ($5M) **(early)** | 2024-06-20 | no | General Catalyst (lead;  Hannah Seal); Julie Zhuo; Gareth Williams | [src](https://startupintros.com/news/2024-06-01-wordsmith-ai-seed) |\n| Sundial | Series A ($16M) | 2024-08-06 | no | Sequoia Capital; Electric Capital; Slow Ventures; Drew Houston; Tobias Lutke; Fidji Simo; Shishir Mehrotra; Amjad Masad | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/howie-liu) |\n| Fireworks AI | Series B ($52M) | 2024-07-08 | no | Sequoia Capital (lead); Benchmark; NVIDIA; AMD Ventures; Databricks Ventures; Alexandr Wang; Sheryl Sandberg; Frank Slootman | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/howie-liu) |\n| Runway | Undisclosed (aggregators list a later-stage cheque) | not found | no | not listed | [src](https://connectsafely.ai/investors/howie-liu) |\n| Ampersand | Undisclosed | not found | no | not listed | [src](https://connectsafely.ai/investors/howie-liu) |\n| 8Flow | Undisclosed | not found | no | not listed | [src](https://connectsafely.ai/investors/howie-liu) |\n| Dust | Seed ($5M) **(early)** | 2023-06 | unknown | Sequoia Capital (lead) | [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/howie-liu) |\n\nAlso listed by aggregators with no round: Galileo AI (CB Insights shows an exit dated 2025-05-20) and Airplane ([src](https://connectsafely.ai/investors/howie-liu)). Counts: 15 investments and 6 exits (CB Insights), 17 (Seedtable), 22 (Shizune).\n\n**Hit pattern.** Early cheques that became hot: Braintrust (pre-seed, 2023), Decagon (Series A, 2024), Fireworks AI (before the 2024 Series B). Adept (Series A, 2022) was hot at the time. No public passes or regrets found.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Braintrust (2023).** In the pre-seed and named again in the $5.1M Greylock-led seed, as one of a group of operator-CEOs ([src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round)). The post does not say why the angels invested.\n- **Adept (2022).** Named among the angels in the $65M Series A with Scott Belsky and Andrej Karpathy (repo ledger, BusinessWire release).\n- No intro path is sourced for any deal.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jack Altman | Angel co-investor: Braintrust (2023), Decagon Series A (2024) | 2023 | [src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round) |\n| Guillermo Rauch | Angel co-investor: Braintrust | 2023 | [src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round) |\n| Elad Gil | Co-investor: Braintrust seed, Decagon Series A | 2023 | [src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round) |\n| Sequoia | Lead he sits behind: Fireworks B, Sundial A, Dust seed (aggregator) | 2023 | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/howie-liu) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Any first-hand statement on angel investing: none found in about a dozen searches.\n- Vehicle and cheque size: only directory figures.\n- The two locked seed rounds on CB Insights (2023-11-01 and 2024-02-06) are unidentified.\n- Runway, Ampersand, 8Flow and Dust need a primary source.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round\n- https://dot.la/howie-liu-airtable-2659955374.html\n- https://www.lennysnewsletter.com/p/how-we-restructured-airtables-entire-org-for-ai (summary only)\n- https://startupintros.com/news/2024-06-01-wordsmith-ai-seed (directory citing TechCrunch)\n- https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/howie-liu (aggregator)\n- https://connectsafely.ai/investors/howie-liu (aggregator)\n- https://signal.nfx.com/investors/howie-liu (aggregator)\n- https://20vc.substack.com/p/20product-enterprises-are-not-adopting-71c\n", "person:hunter-somerville": "\n# Hunter Somerville\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the named StepStone VC partner on the Form Ds for StepStone's venture fund-of-funds, micro-manager and secondaries vehicles, and he put his name on a first-time $40M AI seed fund. That makes him the most concrete institutional LP contact in the repo for a frontier-AI seed manager.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He sits on a platform that does primaries, small-manager programs (VC Micro, Micro Growth Equity), secondaries and co-invest under one team. A manager relationship he opens can feed all four. He co-led the $3.3B VC Secondaries VI, the largest VC secondaries fund at close [src](https://www.gilman.edu/news-posts/~board/news/post/avirett-01-and-somerville-00-lead-stepstone-to-closure-of-largest-ever-vc-secondaries-fund).\n- **What constrains him.** The flagship VC Global Partners XII had raised $833.7M from 131 investors [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2038396/000101297526000354/primary_doc.xml). Pacing that much capital pulls most dollars toward established franchises. Emerging managers come in through micro programs.\n- **What he'd never do (his stated filter).** He won't treat venture as an index. He discounts track records built on tiny angel checks or on deals someone \"sourced\" but didn't cover. He won't back a spinout on a standalone basis [podcast](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/how-i-invest-with-david-weisburd/episode/e167-the-hardest-questions-limited-partners-ask-gps-wstepstones-hunter-somerville). The transcript labels all speakers \"Host\", so attribution is likely but not certain.\n- **What he backs in practice.** For Seven Stars he underwrote a first-time GP's relationships with AI companies, not a track record [src](https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/seven-stars-announces-first-fund-to-back-the-visionaries-shaping-ais-future).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He was publicly first among institutions into a first-time AI seed fund.** StepStone and Sapphire were the only LPs named in Seven Stars' June 2025 launch. Somerville: \"We're proud to invest in his oversubscribed inaugural fund\" [src](https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/seven-stars-announces-first-fund-to-back-the-visionaries-shaping-ais-future).\n- **What he underwrote was access.** He cited Lee's \"extensive relationships and trusted reputation with leading AI companies\" [src](https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/seven-stars-announces-first-fund-to-back-the-visionaries-shaping-ais-future).\n- **The angel-track-record trap.** He rejects records made of \"very tiny angel checks that are just not indicative of their ability to execute\" (2:03) [podcast](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/how-i-invest-with-david-weisburd/episode/e167-the-hardest-questions-limited-partners-ask-gps-wstepstones-hunter-somerville).\n- **Sourced versus covered.** He is wary of \"a body of work that maybe they sourced, but they didn't cover\" (2:03) [same](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/how-i-invest-with-david-weisburd/episode/e167-the-hardest-questions-limited-partners-ask-gps-wstepstones-hunter-somerville). A frontier-lab or big-fund spinout should show deals they led or sat on.\n- **Spinouts.** He says \"some of those are regrettable churns\" and calls a spinout \"not something we want to do on a standalone basis\" (3:08) [same](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/how-i-invest-with-david-weisburd/episode/e167-the-hardest-questions-limited-partners-ask-gps-wstepstones-hunter-somerville).\n- **A single-LP mandate he co-runs.** StepStone VC Aspen LP took $113.7M from exactly one investor in January 2026 [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2107189/000101297526000078/primary_doc.xml). That makes it a separately managed account. The client is not named.\n- **New micro-manager vehicle.** StepStone VC Micro-K II LP filed in June 2026 with no sales yet. He is a named related person [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2133412/000213341226000001/primary_doc.xml). This is the program a sub-$100M AI seed fund would raise from.\n- **Crypto fund-of-funds.** StepStone VC Blockchain II had $22.29M from 57 investors (first sale 2025-07-24) [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2058666/000205866626000001/primary_doc.xml).\n- **Merger economics.** In September 2021 he signed StepStone's amended registration rights agreement individually, as one of the \"Greenspring Holders\", alongside Avirett, Adair Newhall and Lindsay Redfield [8-K exhibit](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1796022/000119312521277301/d173286dex104.htm). He holds StepStone equity from the deal.\n- **Former board seat.** He was a Xometry director from May 2019 for Greenspring and sat on its audit committee in the IPO draft filing [DRS](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1657573/000095012321004543/filename1.htm). EDGAR full-text search finds no later Xometry filing naming him. My inference is that he left around the June 2021 IPO.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, as an LP.** He is the named partner on StepStone's micro-manager and VC fund-of-funds vehicles. He has publicly backed a first-time $40M AI seed fund. His platform's evergreen SPRING fund holds Fundomo's Etched and OLIX SPVs ([StepStone profile](../lps/stepstone-group.md), [Fundomo](../firms/fundomo.md)). SPRING's portfolio manager is Brian Borton, so Somerville's personal role in those SPVs is not confirmed.\n\nTwo cautions:\n- His stated filters (no angel-only track records, no \"sourced but not covered\") mean a $20M-check frontier-AI manager needs evidence of having led deals.\n- His team watches spinouts before they launch, so it will already have a view on someone leaving a lab or a big fund.\n\n## Background\n- Gilman School, class of 2000 [src](https://www.gilman.edu/news-posts/~board/news/post/avirett-01-and-somerville-00-lead-stepstone-to-closure-of-largest-ever-vc-secondaries-fund).\n- BA in International Relations from the University of Pennsylvania, with a minor in history. MBA from the University of Maryland's Robert H. Smith School of Business [StepStone event bio](https://stepstonegroup.swoogo.com/venture25/speaker/1354877/hunter-somerville), [Xometry DRS](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1657573/000095012321004543/filename1.htm). MarketScreener (aggregator) gives the years as 2004 and 2008.\n- Associate at Camden Private Capital, which he left in January 2011 [bio](https://stepstonegroup.swoogo.com/venture25/speaker/1354877/hunter-somerville). The end date comes from MarketScreener (aggregator).\n- Greenspring Associates from February 2011 (TheOrg, aggregator):\n  - Principal, 2014-06\n  - Partner, 2017-02\n  - General Partner, 2019-02 [Xometry DRS](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1657573/000095012321004543/filename1.htm)\n- Partner at StepStone from September 2021, after StepStone acquired Greenspring [bio](https://stepstonegroup.swoogo.com/venture25/speaker/1354877/hunter-somerville). The month comes from MarketScreener and TheOrg (aggregators).\n- He was an earlier director of OPAQ Networks and is named on its 2018 Form D [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1681692/000168169218000003/primary_doc.xml).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [StepStone Venture '25 speaker bio](https://stepstonegroup.swoogo.com/venture25/speaker/1354877/hunter-somerville) | StepStone's own event page; there's no personal site (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Searched 2026-11-10 |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | Search returned no LinkedIn URL (2026-11-10) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (guest) | [How I Invest E167 (2025-05)](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/how-i-invest-with-david-weisburd/episode/e167-the-hardest-questions-limited-partners-ask-gps-wstepstones-hunter-somerville); [20VC (2019-01)](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-the-framework-lps-use-to-assess-fa1); 10X Capital Podcast E52 \"How to Scale LP Capital\" ([metacast listing](https://metacast.app/podcast/10x-capital-podcast/XVLupvgK/e52-stepstone-s-hunter-somerville-on-how-to-scale-lp-capital/adRTfZwN), page returned 403) | He's a guest, not a host |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2019-01: 20VC, \"The Framework LPs Use to Assess Fund Managers\" [link](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-the-framework-lps-use-to-assess-fa1). The show notes list his Greenspring LPAC seats (Pear, Foundry Group, Scale VP, BullPen) and Amplify Partners as his latest investment.\n- 2024 (date unconfirmed): 10X Capital Podcast E52, \"StepStone's Hunter Somerville on How to Scale LP Capital\" [listing](https://metacast.app/podcast/10x-capital-podcast/XVLupvgK/e52-stepstone-s-hunter-somerville-on-how-to-scale-lp-capital/adRTfZwN). The transcript wasn't retrieved.\n- 2025-05: How I Invest with David Weisburd, E167, \"The Hardest Questions LPs Ask GPs\" [link](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/how-i-invest-with-david-weisburd/episode/e167-the-hardest-questions-limited-partners-ask-gps-wstepstones-hunter-somerville). He says emerging managers face \"a harder fundraising market\" but aren't dead. He sees fund demand at \"50 to 300 million\", and says LPs should be \"very picky among the spinouts\". Only a preview of the transcript was retrieved.\n\n## How they think\n- **Venture isn't an index.** He does manager selection with \"a prepared mind\" and tracks managers' bodies of work before they raise [podcast](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/how-i-invest-with-david-weisburd/episode/e167-the-hardest-questions-limited-partners-ask-gps-wstepstones-hunter-somerville).\n- **Fund size.** He expects LP demand to shift toward roughly $50M to $300M funds [podcast](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/how-i-invest-with-david-weisburd/episode/e167-the-hardest-questions-limited-partners-ask-gps-wstepstones-hunter-somerville).\n- **Contrarian views.**\n  - The emerging-manager market will split into \"have or have not\".\n  - Some spinouts are regrettable churn, not the stars [podcast](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/how-i-invest-with-david-weisburd/episode/e167-the-hardest-questions-limited-partners-ask-gps-wstepstones-hunter-somerville).\n- **Calls.**\n  - Amplify Partners: committed by January 2019 (Greenspring); outcome not assessed [20VC](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-the-framework-lps-use-to-assess-fa1).\n  - Seven Stars I (2025): too early to judge.\n\n## Read these first\n1. The How I Invest E167 transcript: his full emerging-manager and spinout filter.\n2. The Seven Stars launch release: what he says he underwrote in a first-time AI GP.\n3. The VC Global Partners XII Form D: fund scale and co-signers.\n4. The 20VC 2019 show notes: his Greenspring-era LPAC book.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public X or Substack presence, and his views surface only through podcasts and StepStone press releases.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Xometry (board) | Pre-IPO (Greenspring) | 2019-05 board seat | unknown | Highland, Foundry Group, BMW i Ventures | [DRS](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1657573/000095012321004543/filename1.htm) |\n| OPAQ Networks (board) | $22.5M equity (Form D) | 2018-03 | unknown | n/a | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1681692/000168169218000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Seven Stars Ventures I (fund) | LP commitment | 2025-06 | anchor-tier (named) | Sapphire Partners | [release](https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/seven-stars-announces-first-fund-to-back-the-visionaries-shaping-ais-future) |\n| Amplify Partners (fund) | LP commitment (Greenspring) | by 2019-01 | n/a | n/a | [20VC](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-the-framework-lps-use-to-assess-fa1) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Seven Stars I (2025).** Steven Lee, an SV Angel alum, raised a $40M first fund in about five weeks. Two institutions were named publicly: StepStone, with Somerville quoted, and Sapphire Partners. The release says Somerville backed the fund for Lee's AI-company relationships [src](https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/seven-stars-announces-first-fund-to-back-the-visionaries-shaping-ais-future), [repo](../firms/seven-stars.md). The intro path is not found.\n- **Xometry.** Greenspring's position came with a board seat from May 2019, two years before the 2021 IPO [DRS](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1657573/000095012321004543/filename1.htm).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| John Avirett | Co-lead on VC Secondaries VI; fellow Gilman alum | 2024 (fund close) | [src](https://www.gilman.edu/news-posts/~board/news/post/avirett-01-and-somerville-00-lead-stepstone-to-closure-of-largest-ever-vc-secondaries-fund) |\n| Adair Newhall, Lindsay Redfield | Ex-Greenspring partners; co-signers on the merger rights agreement and on Form Ds | 2021 | [8-K ex.](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1796022/000119312521277301/d173286dex104.htm) |\n| Brian Borton | SPRING portfolio manager; co-related person on VC Form Ds | 2025 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2038396/000101297526000354/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Steven Lee (Seven Stars) | Backed his first fund | 2025 | [release](https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/seven-stars-announces-first-fund-to-back-the-visionaries-shaping-ais-future) |\n| Nate Leung (Sapphire) | Co-named LP on Seven Stars I | 2025 | same |\n| Pear, Foundry Group, Scale VP, BullPen Capital | LPAC seats for Greenspring | as of 2019 | [20VC](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-the-framework-lps-use-to-assess-fa1) |\n| Xometry board (George Hornig, Seth Levine, Craig Driscoll) | Fellow directors | 2019 | [DRS](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1657573/000095012321004543/filename1.htm) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who is the single investor in StepStone VC Aspen ($113.7M)?\n- Does Somerville or Borton decide SPRING's AI SPV co-invests (Fundomo Etched and OLIX)?\n- Which other first-time AI funds has VC Micro or Micro-K II backed without an announcement?\n- Get the full E167 and 10X Capital transcripts with speaker labels.\n- Find his LinkedIn URL and confirm when he left the Xometry board.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/seven-stars-announces-first-fund-to-back-the-visionaries-shaping-ais-future\n- https://audioscrape.com/podcast/how-i-invest-with-david-weisburd/episode/e167-the-hardest-questions-limited-partners-ask-gps-wstepstones-hunter-somerville\n- https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-the-framework-lps-use-to-assess-fa1\n- https://www.gilman.edu/news-posts/~board/news/post/avirett-01-and-somerville-00-lead-stepstone-to-closure-of-largest-ever-vc-secondaries-fund\n- https://stepstonegroup.swoogo.com/venture25/speaker/1354877/hunter-somerville\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1657573/000095012321004543/filename1.htm (Xometry DRS)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1796022/000119312521277301/d173286dex104.htm (StepStone 8-K exhibit)\n- Form Ds: GP XII https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2038396/000101297526000354/primary_doc.xml ; Aspen https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2107189/000101297526000078/primary_doc.xml ; Blockchain II https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2058666/000205866626000001/primary_doc.xml ; Micro Growth Equity V https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2075056/000101297526000661/primary_doc.xml ; Micro-K II https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2133412/000213341226000001/primary_doc.xml ; Secondaries VII https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2100671/000101297526000367/primary_doc.xml ; OPAQ https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1681692/000168169218000003/primary_doc.xml\n- Aggregators (secondary): https://be.marketscreener.com/insider/HUNTER-SOMERVILLE-A0LJ5X/ , https://theorg.com/org/stepstone-group/org-chart/hunter-somerville\n", "person:ian-hathaway": "\n# Ian Hathaway\n\n## One-line take\nBehind \"OpenAI Ventures\" there is essentially one person. Hathaway went from senior associate at a seed fund to legal controller of a lab-affiliated fund that turned $175M into about $3.8B gross. That fund now runs $400M of OpenAI's own money.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he was first institutional money in Harvey and Cursor, with OpenAI's name behind him. He builds the angel syndicate around his seed: he sent Harvey's founders to Sarah Guo, \"and OpenAI invested beside her\" ([Colossus](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/)). TechCrunch credits him with leading Ambience, Cursor, Harvey and Speak ([TechCrunch 2024-04-01](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/01/sam-altman-gives-up-control-of-openai-startup-fund-resolving-unusual-corporate-venture-structure/)).\n- **Constraint:** almost no public footprint. He has one quote in a funding release (Adaptive Security, 2025-09) and no podcasts or posts found. His brand is OpenAI's. Fund II has OpenAI as the reported sole LP, and reporting says OpenAI was looking for \"an experienced venture capitalist to run it\" ([Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/147196-openai-prepares-400m-second-venture-fund/)). His day-to-day seat on Fund II is therefore **unconfirmed**, even though he signed its Form D.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** back a foundation-model lab that competes with OpenAI. No such deal appears in the fund's book.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He owns the GP, not OpenAI.** The adviser's Form ADV lists him as manager of OpenAI Startup Fund Management LLC, and as member and manager of OpenAI Startup Fund GP I with ownership code E (75%+) since 03/2024 ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/318221/PDF/318221.pdf)). The 2024 press said control \"transferred to Hathaway\". The filing shows that means him personally.\n- **He signed before he controlled.** SPV I's Form D (2024-02-02), filed while Altman still controlled Fund I, names Hathaway and Bobby Wu as executive officers ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2003722/000200372224000001/primary_doc.xml)). Every later SPV (II-VI) and Fund II names only Hathaway.\n- **His marks:** Fund I's gross value was $3.77B against $175.25M raised; SPV V was $411M against $44.3M ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/318221/PDF/318221.pdf)).\n- **His only release quote** praises Adaptive's \"incredible product speed\" on a follow-on the fund did alone ([PRN 2025-09-09](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/openai-fund-extends-investment-in-adaptive-security-raising-total-to-55-million-in-series-a-302549706.html)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is the lab-side seed investor most likely to be in, or to introduce, AI-native application and automation companies at seed. His check size ($5M-$45M) fits next to a $20M check. He is not a lab mega-seed price-setter, and his corporate counterpart for big checks is Albert Lee.\n\n## Background\nUndergraduate at Duke (aggregator, [MarketScreener](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/IAN-HATHAWAY-A36783)). He spent \"four years at VC firm Haystack\" ([GCV 2026](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-ian-hathaway-openai-startup-fund/?format=md)), as Senior Associate 2018-2021 per an aggregator. He joined OpenAI in 2021 to run the fund and oversaw Converge from December 2022 ([GCV 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-ian-hathaway/?format=md)). He took control in March 2024.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://openai.fund | No team page; no posts since 2023-12 (as of 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | nothing verified | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-09-11, Taipei: a public forum hosted by Speak, a portfolio company. It was his first visit to Taiwan, and the topic was the next steps in AI development. Digitimes calls him \"managing partner\"; the article is paywalled after the first lines ([Digitimes](https://www.digitimes.com/news/a20250909PD207.html)). No podcasts found.\n\n## How they think\nNothing in his own words beyond the Adaptive quote. The fund's stated filter is companies that \"deeply integrate cutting edge AI models\" ([openai.fund](https://openai.fund/news/introducing-our-first-investments)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Form ADV, OpenAI Startup Fund Management LLC](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/318221/PDF/318221.pdf): control, vehicles, marks.\n2. [TechCrunch 2024-04-01](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/01/sam-altman-gives-up-control-of-openai-startup-fund-resolving-unusual-corporate-venture-structure/): the handover.\n3. [Colossus on Sarah Guo](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/): how he routed Harvey.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nCredited personally: Harvey (seed 2022), Speak (B 2022), Cursor (seed 2023), Ambience Healthcare (B 2024), Adaptive Security (A extension 2025). Fund-level book: see [firms/openai-startup-fund.md](../firms/openai-startup-fund.md).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Harvey:** the founders cold-emailed Altman and Jason Kwon. The fund led the $5M seed and introduced the angels; Guo wrote her check personally (repo insights; [Colossus](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/)).\n- **Cursor:** the fund led the $8M seed in October 2023 ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2023/10/11/anysphere-raises-8m-from-openai-to-build-an-ai-powered-ide/)). Dealroom estimates about 10,000x if the SpaceX $60B deal completes ([GCV 2026](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-ian-hathaway-openai-startup-fund/?format=md)). That is an estimate.\n\n## Relationships\n- Sarah Guo (Conviction): Harvey referral, and co-investor in Harvey and Worktrace.\n- Sam Altman: predecessor as legal controller. Brad Lightcap: the fund's public \"manager\" in 2022-23.\n- Semil Shah (Haystack): former employer (inferred from Haystack tenure; no direct source on their relationship).\n\n## Open questions\n- Is he the day-to-day lead of Fund II, or did OpenAI hire someone over him?\n- Who are the 13 other Fund I LPs besides Microsoft, and who owns the SPVs (2-7 holders each)?\n- Does his 75% GP stake carry economics (carry) or only control?\n- Which company sits in SPV IV and SPV V (6.9x and 9.3x gross)? **Hypothesis, unconfirmed (2026-11-10):** SPV IV's first sale (2024-07-22) is one day before Harvey's Series C (2024-07-23, OpenAI named, [Harvey](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-c)). A Harvey stake bought at $1.5B fits a roughly 7x mark. SPV V's first sale (2024-12-05) is five days before Speak's $1B Series C (2024-12-10). But a 9.3x mark on Speak would need about a $9B valuation, which is unlikely, so SPV V could hold a different December 2024 round. The only support is date adjacency.\n- Name collision: a different Ian Hathaway (Far Out Ventures, FOVC Fund I Form D 2022; co-author with Brad Feld) appears in EDGAR full-text search. Do not merge the two.\n\n## Update 2026-11-10 (strategics sweep)\n- **He signs higher up the GP stack on Fund II.** SPV VI's Form D (2025-08-04) names him \"Manager of the Managing Member of the General Partner\". That means his control runs through OpenAI Startup Fund GP I LLC, of which he is a 75%+ member ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2079027/000207902725000001/primary_doc.xml)). Fund II's Form D (2026-08-26) names him \"Managing Member of the General Partner\" directly ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2133720/000213372026000001/primary_doc.xml)). Both list the issuer address as 1455 3rd Street, San Francisco.\n- **Fund II is fully sold.** The offering is $400,000,000 and the amount sold is $400,000,000. There is one investor, the first sale was 2026-08-11, the minimum is $0, and the filing is typed as a venture capital fund ([rendered Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2133720/000213372026000001/xslFormDX01/primary_doc.xml)). So the open question below (\"did OpenAI hire someone over him?\") now has evidence against it: he controls the Fund II GP. That is control of the vehicle, not proof that he runs the day-to-day deals, so the question stays **open**.\n- **Fund-level deals from GCV 2024:** the fund co-led Ambience's B and joined Class Companion, Anysphere and Harvey's $80M Series B (2023-12). He is not personally named on these ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-ian-hathaway/?format=md)). That puts the fund in Harvey's seed, B, C, D and E.\n- **The pools he controls, using the strategic-investor method:**\n\n| Pool | Money | Size | Vintage | Signer | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI Startup Fund I, L.P. | Outside LPs (14 investors incl. Microsoft); OpenAI itself not an LP per press | $175.25M | 2021 | Altman until 2024-03, then Hathaway via GP I LLC | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/318221/PDF/318221.pdf) |\n| SPV I-VI | 2-7 holders each, deal-by-deal | $5M-$69.5M each | 2024-25 | Hathaway (SPV I with Bobby Wu) | [sources table](../sources/openai-startup-fund-check-writers.md) |\n| OpenAI Startup Fund II, L.P. | Single investor, reported as OpenAI | $400M, fully sold | 2026-08 | Hathaway as Managing Member of the GP | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2133720/000213372026000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n- **The strategic tie** at fund level: the fund's filter is companies that \"deeply integrate cutting edge AI models\" ([openai.fund](https://openai.fund/news/introducing-our-first-investments)), and Converge is the accelerator he oversaw ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-ian-hathaway/?format=md)). No deal discloses compute, contract or model-access terms, so any such terms are **unconfirmed**.\n- **How to reach him:** no public channels. The verified routes are warm intros through portfolio founders (Harvey, Cursor, Speak, Ambience, Adaptive) and through co-investors he routes deals to, such as Sarah Guo (Conviction). His only public appearance found was hosted by a portfolio company.\n- **For the owner:** he is a co-investor at seed and A, not a later-round price-setter. Fund II (all parent money) makes him a lab-strategic check-writer whose $5M-$45M leads fit next to a $20M check. He is the person to know at \"OpenAI Ventures\".\n\n## Sources\nSee the links inline above, plus [sources/openai-startup-fund-check-writers.md](../sources/openai-startup-fund-check-writers.md).\n", "person:igor-babuschkin": "\n# Igor Babuschkin\n\n_Updated 2026-11-09: template upgrade; added Crux, Differential insight, Fit with my lens from Unsupervised Learning Ep 92 transcript._\n\n## One-line take\nxAI co-founder who left in Aug 2025 to start an AI-safety venture firm, then pivoted to founding River AI, which raised $1.1B in seed + Series A two months after launch. Follow him for where top lab talent thinks open-weight training and \"owned\" personal AI are heading [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/).\n\n## Crux\nA lapsed investor who went back to building. After xAI he angel-invested in AI safety startups, got impatient waiting on other founders, and started River AI instead [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8). He wins on credibility as a lab builder (AlphaStar, OpenAI reasoning, Colossus) and on his belief that labs are won by a critical mass of talent [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8). He is constrained by a commercial gap: River needs its open-weights training API to pay its way before it can train frontier models [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8). He'd never back a \"rent intelligence by the token\" model; he frames centralised closed labs as the problem [src](https://river.ai/introducing-river-ai.html).\n\n## Differential insight\nQuotes below come from the auto-generated YouTube captions of Unsupervised Learning Ep 92 (2026-07-31), checked for clean spans. Mirrored in `data/rows/igor-babuschkin-insights.csv`.\n- **Babuschkin Ventures was angel investing, not a fund.** After xAI he \"decided to do a bit of angel investment in AI safety companies\" (00:10:43) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n- **Why he stopped investing.** Waiting on founders made him impatient: \"I wanted to start something new myself so I can help push\" (00:11:19) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n- **How xAI really started.** A chance encounter with Musk: he \"randomly happened to be at the Tesla office\" (00:44:38). Musk reached out later when starting the lab (00:45:25) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n- **Talent rule.** Building a lab hinges on one thing: \"you have to get that critical mass of talent\" (00:45:42). He recruited old friends for xAI [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n- **Early call on OpenAI.** From inside the early reasoning team, \"it was pretty clear that open was going to win in many ways\" (00:42:22; captions render OpenAI as \"open\"). He credits a smaller, aligned, talent-dense team [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n- **Closed labs squeezed.** \"you're kind of starting to get squeezed in a little bit\" (00:26:58): too strong to release, with open weights close behind [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n- **Enterprise moat test.** Most exposed is a company where \"everything that makes your company unique and special is already out there\" (00:31:32) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n- **River's real constraint.** He'd like to train the best US open model, but first wants open weights to pay \"so that the company can be self-sustaining\" (00:35:43) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n- **Colossus method.** \"really the key to making Colossus work was just questioning\" how others built data centers; vendor quotes ran above a year (00:47:34) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n- **Admitted miss.** \"I was expecting kind of smooth progress\" on coding models; the late-2025 usage jump surprised him (00:55:50) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n- **River's comparable.** He describes River API as a reinforcement learning and fine-tuning service like Thinking Machines' product (00:13:00) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** As an investor he is a weak fit: no fund found, no sourced check sizes, and his only reported angel deal (RadixArk) is aggregator-only. He has said he left investing for building [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8). As a node he is high-value. River AI is the kind of new lab THESIS.md targets ($1.1B seed + Series A led by General Catalyst and AMP PBC) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/). His ex-xAI and ex-OpenAI talent graph is where the next lab founders come from. Deals that matter: River AI (next round), RadixArk (AI infra seed), possibly Humans& (unconfirmed).\n\n## Background\n- Physics: studied physics and worked on particle physics at CERN before moving to AI [src](https://startupfortune.com/igor-babuschkin-built-elon-musks-ai-supercluster-and-now-wants-to-give-that-power-back-to-ordinary-users/). Specifics (TU Dortmund degree, LHCb summer student, exact years) come only from wiki/aggregator pages (unverified) [src](https://www.aiwiki.ai/wiki/igor_babuschkin).\n- Google DeepMind: research engineer. Technical lead on AlphaStar, the StarCraft II system [src](https://the-decoder.com/a-conversation-with-max-tegmark-inspired-ai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-shift-to-safer-ai/). Worked on generative modelling and reinforcement learning [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/river-ai-1-1-billion-babuschkin-general-catalyst-open-weights). Some outlets credit him with AlphaCode instead (or as well) [src](https://dealroom.co/news/128971-xai-co-founder-babuschkin-in-talks-to-raise-up-to-1b-for-new-ai-research/); sources conflict on which projects were his. Joined 2017, per a wiki (unverified) [src](https://www.aiwiki.ai/wiki/igor_babuschkin).\n- OpenAI: member of technical staff [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/08/13/xai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-leaving-company-focus-ai-safety/), described as leading large-scale training efforts [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/river-ai-1-1-billion-babuschkin-general-catalyst-open-weights). Dates (~2020 to 2022, then back to DeepMind until Feb 2023) are wiki-only (unverified) [src](https://www.aiwiki.ai/wiki/igor_babuschkin).\n- xAI, 2023 to 2025-08-13: co-founded with Elon Musk in 2023 [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/igor-babuschkin-exits-xai-create-093518481.html). Built many early training-job tools, then ran engineering across infrastructure, product and applied AI [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/igor-babuschkin-exits-xai-create-093518481.html). He says he helped build the Memphis supercluster in about 120 days [src](https://the-decoder.com/a-conversation-with-max-tegmark-inspired-ai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-shift-to-safer-ai/); another outlet says 122 days [src](https://startupfortune.com/igor-babuschkin-built-elon-musks-ai-supercluster-and-now-wants-to-give-that-power-back-to-ordinary-users/). Last day: 2025-08-13 [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/08/13/xai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-leaving-company-focus-ai-safety/).\n- Babuschkin Ventures, announced 2025-08-13: a firm to fund AI safety research and back startups building \"AI and agentic systems that advance humanity\" [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/08/13/xai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-leaving-company-focus-ai-safety/). No fund size, LPs, team or portfolio has been reported (see Portfolio).\n- River AI, 2026: incorporated in Nevada on 2026-04-20 [src](https://dealroom.co/news/128971-xai-co-founder-babuschkin-in-talks-to-raise-up-to-1b-for-new-ai-research/). Funding talks reported 2026-05-14 [src](https://www.forbes.com/sites/rashishrivastava/2026/05/14/xai-cofounder-igor-babuschkin-in-talks-to-raise-up-to-1-billion-for-a-new-ai-startup/). Public launch post 2026-06-10 [src](https://river.ai/introducing-river-ai.html). $1.1B seed + Series A announced 2026-08-11 [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/). HQ: Palo Alto [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/river-ai-1-1-billion-babuschkin-general-catalyst-open-weights).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://river.ai (company) [src](https://river.ai/introducing-river-ai.html) | No personal site found. No standalone Babuschkin Ventures site found. |\n| Substack / blog | River AI blog [src](https://river.ai/introducing-river-ai.html) | Launch post 2026-06-10 is his main long-form writing found |\n| X | https://x.com/ibab [src](https://digg.com/rankings/ibab) | About 111.8K followers per a third-party ranking page (digg.com), checked 2026-11-09 [src](https://digg.com/rankings/ibab). Bio: CEO & Co-Founder @river_ai_inc [src](https://digg.com/rankings/ibab). Posting cadence not measured (X not fetchable). |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found (not checked successfully) | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-07-31: Unsupervised Learning (Jacob Effron, Redpoint), Ep 92, \"xAI Co-Founder Unpacks the Future of Model Development\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8), [listing](https://podcasts.apple.com/es/podcast/ep-92-xai-co-founder-unpacks-the-future-of-model-development/id1672188924?i=1000779292714). Stood out: Babuschkin Ventures was really angel investing; how xAI began at the Tesla office; River's three bets (training API, per-user personalization, local hardware); closed labs being squeezed. See Differential insight.\n- 2026-08-13: TBPN, multi-guest episode with Brannin McBee, Garret Langley, Sonya Huang, Sean Cole [src](https://podbay.fm/p/tbpn/e/1786655553). Not listened to or transcribed in this pass.\n\n## How they think\n- **Ownership is the alignment strategy.** His River launch post argues that AI built around the person it serves, and controlled by them, is aligned by construction. He writes that River doesn't treat \"alignment and safety as separate problems\" [src](https://river.ai/introducing-river-ai.html). This reframes the Aug 2025 \"AI safety fund\" mandate as a product thesis.\n- **Against renting intelligence by the token.** He argues the current setup, where a few labs own the models and rent them out, doesn't suit personal AI. His answer is to rebuild training, models, the product layer and hardware [src](https://river.ai/introducing-river-ai.html), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/).\n- **Open weights.** He says AI \"should be open, freely available, and affordable\" [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/river-ai-1-1-billion-babuschkin-general-catalyst-open-weights). River's first product is an API for LoRA and RL fine-tuning of open-weight models, where customers keep their checkpoints [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/).\n- **Replacement isn't inevitable.** He expects AI to soon do most economically valuable work, but rejects replacing humans as the default outcome. He pictures personal agents as \"guardian angels\" [src](https://river.ai/introducing-river-ai.html).\n- **Safety turn (Aug 2025).** He cites a dinner with Max Tegmark (Future of Life Institute) about AI that lets children flourish as the trigger for leaving xAI to focus on safety [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/08/13/xai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-leaving-company-focus-ai-safety/). He stayed optimistic on capabilities: \"The singularity is near, but humanity's future is bright!\" [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/08/13/xai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-leaving-company-focus-ai-safety/). He did not tie his exit to Grok's 2025 controversies. Press drew that link [src](https://the-decoder.com/a-conversation-with-max-tegmark-inspired-ai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-shift-to-safer-ai/).\n- **Small, talent-dense teams** [src](https://river.ai/introducing-river-ai.html). On the podcast he credits OpenAI's early win to a critical mass of talent density [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n- **Distributed post-training.** Centralised post-training with hired experts hits diminishing returns. He wants companies and individuals to post-train locally instead of buying intelligence by the token [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n- **Slowdown is unrealistic, so accelerate alignment.** He doubts AI can be slowed. He backs open models near the danger threshold so more people can work on alignment [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n- **Most urgent risk is inequality, not takeover** [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n- Calls right/wrong: right on reasoning and on OpenAI winning (his own account); wrong on coding progress being smooth [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8). River is too early to judge.\n\n## Read these first\n0. Unsupervised Learning Ep 92 (2026-07-31): the best first-person account of his career, why he quit angel investing, and River's three bets [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n1. \"Introducing River AI\" (2026-06-10): the full thesis on owned personal AI, the end-to-end stack, and alignment through ownership [src](https://river.ai/introducing-river-ai.html).\n2. His Aug 2025 xAI farewell post on X, as reported: the Tegmark dinner and the Babuschkin Ventures mandate [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/08/13/xai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-leaving-company-focus-ai-safety/).\n3. TechCrunch on the $1.1B round: the product as it actually ships (RL/LoRA API, \"neocloud\") vs. the hardware vision [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/).\n\n## Social engagement\n- About 111.8K X followers per a third-party ranking, checked 2026-11-09. That page lists Andrej Karpathy, Sam Altman and Pieter Abbeel among his top followers, but this is not evidence of interaction [src](https://digg.com/rankings/ibab).\n- His xAI exit post drew a public thank-you from Elon Musk [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/igor-babuschkin-exits-xai-create-093518481.html).\n- Posting topics, reply and repost patterns: not measured (X not fetchable in this pass).\n\n## Portfolio\n**Babuschkin Ventures:** no verifiable investments found. Coverage of the firm is almost all from launch week (Aug 2025) [src](https://www.wionews.com/business-economy/xai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-departs-to-launch-ai-safety-venture-1755168046255). Dealroom's profile of him lists no Babuschkin Ventures deals [src](https://dealroom.co/people/igor-babuschkin/). The firm may have been dormant or folded into River AI (unverified).\n\n**Personal / angel:**\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| RadixArk (SGLang / Miles) | $100M Seed | 2026-05 | no (angel) | Accel (lead), Spark Capital (co-lead), NVentures, AMD, MediaTek, Databricks, others [src](https://www.radixark.com/blog/radixark-launches-100m-seed) | [src](https://buttondown.com/AngelRegistry/archive/angel-registry-update-may-6-2026/), [src](https://dealroom.co/people/igor-babuschkin/). Aggregator-only: he is not named in RadixArk's own post. |\n\n**River AI cap table (as founder):**\n| Investor | Round | Date | Led? | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| General Catalyst | Seed + Series A, $1.1B combined | 2026-08-11 | yes (co-lead) | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/) |\n| AMP PBC (Anjney Midha) | Seed + Series A | 2026-08-11 | yes (co-lead) | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/) |\n| Nvidia | Seed + Series A (strategic) | 2026-08-11 | no | [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/river-ai-1-1-billion-babuschkin-general-catalyst-open-weights) |\n| AMD Ventures | Seed + Series A (strategic) | 2026-08-11 | no | [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/river-ai-1-1-billion-babuschkin-general-catalyst-open-weights) |\n| Y Combinator | Seed + Series A | 2026-08-11 | no | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/) |\n| Temasek | Seed + Series A | 2026-08-11 | no | [src](https://www.dealstreetasia.com/?p=491862) |\n| Igor Babuschkin (personal) | initial financing, up to $100M | reported 2026-05-14 | n/a | Forbes report, unconfirmed by the company [src](https://www.forbes.com/sites/rashishrivastava/2026/05/14/xai-cofounder-igor-babuschkin-in-talks-to-raise-up-to-1-billion-for-a-new-ai-startup/) |\n\nValuation: not disclosed by River [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/river-ai-1-1-billion-babuschkin-general-catalyst-open-weights). About $5B is attributed to The New York Times by aggregators [src](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/babuschkins-river-ai-closes-11b-at-5b-for-user-owned-ai). Forbes reported the May ask as \"up to $5B\" [src](https://dealroom.co/news/128971-xai-co-founder-babuschkin-in-talks-to-raise-up-to-1b-for-new-ai-research/). Dealroom shows $6.6B [src](https://dealroom.co/people/igor-babuschkin/). Treat all of these as unconfirmed. The split between seed and Series A was not disclosed [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/river-ai-1-1-billion-babuschkin-general-catalyst-open-weights).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **River AI raise.** Forbes reported talks on 2026-05-14, before any product existed. General Catalyst was in talks to lead up to $1B at up to $5B, and one investor reportedly called it \"just an idea on a napkin\" [src](https://dealroom.co/news/128971-xai-co-founder-babuschkin-in-talks-to-raise-up-to-1b-for-new-ai-research/). The final round came in above target at $1.1B, with AMP PBC added as co-lead [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/river-ai-1-1-billion-babuschkin-general-catalyst-open-weights). AMP PBC is the 2026 firm of ex-a16z GP Anjney Midha [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/). General Catalyst CEO Hemant Taneja framed the bet as national strategy: US AI leadership \"urgently requires leadership in open weight models\" [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/river-ai-1-1-billion-babuschkin-general-catalyst-open-weights). Intro path and first meeting: nothing found.\n- **Positioning gap.** Press framed River as \"user-owned personal AI\" [src](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/babuschkins-river-ai-closes-11b-at-5b-for-user-owned-ai). What it sells today is an enterprise and developer RL/LoRA training API (\"neocloud\"), with personal agents and hardware as the long-term plan [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/).\n- **Babuschkin Ventures → River.** The exit-to-fund-to-startup arc took about 8 months, from the Aug 2025 fund announcement to the Apr 2026 incorporation. ~~No public explanation of what happened to the fund was found.~~ Answered 2026-11-09: he says he made angel investments in AI safety companies, grew impatient waiting on founders, and decided to build himself [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n- **xAI origin.** He met Musk by chance at the Tesla office; they agreed the world needed another lab with a different philosophy; Musk later reached out to start xAI, and he recruited old friends [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| xAI / Elon Musk | Co-founder; ran engineering | 2023 to 2025-08-13 | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/08/13/xai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-leaving-company-focus-ai-safety/) |\n| Google DeepMind | Research engineer; AlphaStar technical lead | dates unverified | [src](https://the-decoder.com/a-conversation-with-max-tegmark-inspired-ai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-shift-to-safer-ai/) |\n| OpenAI | Member of technical staff | dates unverified | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/08/13/xai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-leaving-company-focus-ai-safety/) |\n| Max Tegmark (Future of Life Institute) | Conversation that inspired his safety turn | 2025 | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/08/13/xai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-leaving-company-focus-ai-safety/) |\n| Babuschkin Ventures | Founder | 2025-08 | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/08/13/xai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-leaving-company-focus-ai-safety/) |\n| River AI | Founder & CEO | 2026-04-20 (incorporation) | [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/river-ai-1-1-billion-babuschkin-general-catalyst-open-weights) |\n| Ievgen and Dima (Dmytro) Soboliev | River co-founders (ex-xAI engineers, twins) | 2026 | Bloomberg via [src](https://hub.baai.ac.cn/view/55773) and [src](https://www.kucoin.com/news/flash/xai-co-founder-launches-river-ai-to-build-user-controlled-ai-agents) (unverified; Bloomberg original not read) |\n| Vincent Stark | River team; ex-xAI, oversaw product safety until 2025-12 | 2026 | Bloomberg via [src](https://hub.baai.ac.cn/view/55773) (unverified) |\n| Lily Lim | River general counsel; ex-xAI general counsel | 2026 | Bloomberg via [src](https://www.kucoin.com/news/flash/xai-co-founder-launches-river-ai-to-build-user-controlled-ai-agents) (unverified) |\n| General Catalyst (Hemant Taneja) | Co-lead investor in River | 2026-08-11 | [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/river-ai-1-1-billion-babuschkin-general-catalyst-open-weights) |\n| AMP PBC (Anjney Midha) | Co-lead investor in River | 2026-08-11 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/) |\n| Jacob Effron (Redpoint) | Podcast host, Ep 92 | 2026-07-31 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8) |\n| Greg Brockman (needs-id) | Pre-training collaborator at OpenAI; captions read \"Greg Bachmann\" | OpenAI era | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8) |\n| RadixArk (Ying Sheng, Banghua Zhu) | Angel investor | 2026-05 | [src](https://buttondown.com/AngelRegistry/archive/angel-registry-update-may-6-2026/) (aggregator) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did Babuschkin Ventures make any investments, raise outside capital, or get folded into River? Partly answered 2026-11-09: he describes it as angel investing in AI safety companies, then River [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8). Which companies and check sizes are still unknown.\n- He wrote two fiction pieces in early 2026 (a sorcerer's-apprentice dystopia and a \"prayer to the machine god\" piece), per the Ep 92 host [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8). Location not found (probably X); find them for Read these first.\n- Transcribe the TBPN 2026-08-13 segment.\n- Angel leads that could not be confirmed (pages not loadable or not naming him): Humans& seed, Jan 2026 (lead from a startupintros listing that now returns \"not found\"); Dust seed, Jun 2023 (Employbl/PipeSeed lists); Exa, May 2026 (PitchBook snippet). His PitchBook profile reportedly shows 4 investments [src](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/investor/530051-68) (page 403, unverified).\n- Did his up-to-$100M personal commitment to River actually go in?\n- Read the Bloomberg 2026-06-10 launch story to confirm co-founder names and roles.\n- Valuation: ~$5B (NYT, via aggregators) vs. $6.6B (Dealroom). Which is right?\n- When does River's personal-agent product or hardware ship, and who are the first customers?\n- Primary-source confirmation of his DeepMind/OpenAI dates and of AlphaStar vs. AlphaCode credit.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8 (Unsupervised Learning Ep 92, auto-captions; added 2026-11-09)\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/es/podcast/ep-92-xai-co-founder-unpacks-the-future-of-model-development/id1672188924?i=1000779292714\n- https://podbay.fm/p/tbpn/e/1786655553 (listing only, 403)\n- https://www.36kr.com/p/3864280493118470 (Humans& mention)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/11/general-catalyst-leads-1-1b-round-into-2-month-old-river-ai/\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/news/igor-babuschkin-exits-xai-create-093518481.html\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/river-ai-1-1-billion-babuschkin-general-catalyst-open-weights\n- https://www.dealstreetasia.com/?p=491862\n- https://aiweekly.co/alerts/babuschkins-river-ai-closes-11b-at-5b-for-user-owned-ai\n- https://www.forbes.com/sites/rashishrivastava/2026/05/14/xai-cofounder-igor-babuschkin-in-talks-to-raise-up-to-1-billion-for-a-new-ai-startup/ (403; content via Dealroom summary)\n- https://dealroom.co/news/128971-xai-co-founder-babuschkin-in-talks-to-raise-up-to-1b-for-new-ai-research/\n- https://dealroom.co/people/igor-babuschkin/\n- https://river.ai/introducing-river-ai.html\n- https://siliconangle.com/2025/08/13/xai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-leaving-company-focus-ai-safety/\n- https://the-decoder.com/a-conversation-with-max-tegmark-inspired-ai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-shift-to-safer-ai/\n- https://www.wionews.com/business-economy/xai-co-founder-igor-babuschkin-departs-to-launch-ai-safety-venture-1755168046255\n- https://startupfortune.com/igor-babuschkin-built-elon-musks-ai-supercluster-and-now-wants-to-give-that-power-back-to-ordinary-users/\n- https://hub.baai.ac.cn/view/55773\n- https://www.kucoin.com/news/flash/xai-co-founder-launches-river-ai-to-build-user-controlled-ai-agents\n- https://digg.com/rankings/ibab\n- https://buttondown.com/AngelRegistry/archive/angel-registry-update-may-6-2026/\n- https://www.radixark.com/blog/radixark-launches-100m-seed\n- https://www.aiwiki.ai/wiki/igor_babuschkin (wiki; background dates only, unverified)\n- https://pitchbook.com/profiles/investor/530051-68 (403)\n", "person:ishani-thakur": "\n# Ishani Thakur\n\n## One-line take\nA former DeepMind/MIT robotics and RL researcher who left Index with Mike Volpi and Bryan Offutt to be a Partner at Hanabi Capital. Hanabi turns up early in a run of AI-native automation rounds (Traversal, Applied Compute, Simile, Cognition), but her own name is on only one of them so far.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** She has real research credibility in robotics and RL from DeepMind and MIT ([src](https://www.hanabi.com/team/ishani-thakur)). She also sits inside a small ex-Index partnership (Volpi, Offutt, Shreyan Jain) whose network gets Hanabi into hot AI rounds as a named participant ([src](https://www.hanabi.com/team)).\n- **What constrains her:** Every Hanabi check found so far is a participation, not a lead. Her one personal credit, Traversal, is signed jointly with Offutt and Volpi, so her own sourcing can't be separated from theirs ([src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement)). Hanabi's site lists no portfolio ([src](https://www.hanabi.com/companies)).\n- **What she would never do:** Nothing in the sources says. Her bio's focus on the \"next frontier\" points away from non-technical deals, but that is an inference.\n\n## Differential insight\nSearch budget for this run ran out after the first queries, so no podcast, X or talk material was found. That is a gap, not proof none exists. What primary sources do show:\n- **Identity resolved.** The Hanabi team page confirms she is a Hanabi Partner and formerly Index. This replaces the earlier single-aggregator \"Hanabi co-founder\" claim. Her bio reads: \"Previously, she was a Partner at Index Ventures\" ([src](https://www.hanabi.com/team/ishani-thakur)). Note that the aggregator calls her an Index \"investor\" who joined in 2023. Hanabi says \"Partner\"; the title at departure is unconfirmed.\n- **Research pedigree.** \"She did Robotics and RL research at DeepMind and MIT\" ([src](https://www.hanabi.com/team/ishani-thakur)). Her bio also lists engineering at Google, Lyft Level 5 and Confluent. Confluent is a Volpi board company ([src](https://podcast.app/uncapped-52-mike-volpi-from-hanabi-capital-e457843013)), which hints at an intro path into Index. That link is unconfirmed.\n- **Her stated focus:** \"investing in companies enabling the next frontier in technology\" ([src](https://www.hanabi.com/team/ishani-thakur)).\n- **Her only personally named AI deal.** Traversal's launch credits \"Bryan Offutt, Ishani Thakur & Mike Volpi of Hanabi\" on the $48M seed + Series A, which Sequoia and Kleiner Perkins led ([src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:** She has a technical research background in RL and robotics. Hanabi keeps getting into AI-native automation companies early: Applied Compute's pre-launch $20M seed, Traversal's seed+A and Simile's A. These are close to the \"Serval-type\" names in THESIS.md.\n- **Against:** No pre-training lab, AI chip or compute deal is attributed to her. Hanabi doesn't lead. Her personal attribution rests on a single deal.\n- **What would make her high:** If she is the Hanabi partner on Applied Compute (ex-OpenAI founders) or Simile, she becomes a useful co-investor and reference for research-team seeds. Robotics is also flagged as a Hanabi topic in the Volpi podcast notes ([src](https://podcast.app/uncapped-52-mike-volpi-from-hanabi-capital-e457843013)), which fits her background.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** BS in Computer Science, MIT ([src](https://www.hanabi.com/team/ishani-thakur)).\n- **Research:** Robotics and RL research at DeepMind and MIT. Earlier repo notes say CSAIL; that comes from an aggregator. Dates are not given ([src](https://www.hanabi.com/team/ishani-thakur)).\n- **Engineering:** Google, Lyft Level 5 and Confluent, per the Hanabi bio. Order and dates are not given ([src](https://www.hanabi.com/team/ishani-thakur)).\n- **Index Ventures:**\n  - Joined in 2023, per an aggregator. That TheOrg page now returns 404 ([aggregator](https://theorg.com/org/index-ventures/org-chart/ishani-thakur)).\n  - Hanabi calls her a former Partner ([src](https://www.hanabi.com/team/ishani-thakur)).\n  - She is no longer on the Index team page.\n- **Hanabi Capital:** Partner, alongside Mike Volpi, Shreyan Jain and Bryan Offutt ([src](https://www.hanabi.com/team)). She was named at Hanabi on the Traversal launch, dated 2025-06-20 per repo rows; the page itself shows no date ([src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.hanabi.com/team/ishani-thakur | Firm bio, no date, as of 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Bio links none |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found (not verified) | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The search budget ran out, so this was not exhaustively checked.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** The only sourced statement is the \"next frontier in technology\" focus and working with \"mission obsessed\" people ([src](https://www.hanabi.com/team/ishani-thakur)). Hanabi's firm-level focus, from Offutt's bio, is \"investing in the next generation of AI and infrastructure businesses\" ([src](https://www.hanabi.com/team/bryan-offutt)).\n- **Contrarian views:** nothing found.\n- **Calls right or wrong:** Too early to say. Traversal is her one attributed deal.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Hanabi bio: the only primary self-description ([src](https://www.hanabi.com/team/ishani-thakur)).\n2. Traversal launch post: her one named deal, and the Hanabi trio that signed it ([src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement)).\n3. Uncapped #52 with Mike Volpi (2026-06-10): how Hanabi was designed. She is not mentioned in the show notes ([src](https://podcast.app/uncapped-52-mike-volpi-from-hanabi-capital-e457843013)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Traversal | Seed + Series A ($48M combined) | 2025-06-20 (repo rows; page undated) | no | Sequoia, Kleiner Perkins (co-leads); NFDG. Hanabi co-signers: Bryan Offutt, Mike Volpi | [src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement) |\n| *Hanabi firm-level, Thakur NOT named:* Applied Compute | Seed (pre-launch, $20M at $100M) | 2025-06-27 | no | Benchmark (Lazarte, lead); Sequoia; Conviction; Definition | [src](https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/ex-openai-applied-compute-raises-20m) |\n| *Hanabi firm-level, not named:* Simile | Series A ($100M) | 2026-02-12 | no | Index (lead); A*; BCV; Karpathy; Fei-Fei Li | [src](https://www.simile.com/blog/the-simulation-company) |\n| *Hanabi firm-level, not named:* Cognition | >$400M at $10.2B | 2025-09-08 | no | Founders Fund (lead); Lux; 8VC; Neo; others | [src](https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents) |\n| *Hanabi firm-level, not named:* Netic | Series A / B | 2025 | no | Founders Fund; Greylock | [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm) |\n\n## Deal stories\n**Traversal.**\n- Hanabi came in at the first disclosed money, the combined seed + Series A. It sat alongside the Sequoia (Balkansky, Curnin) and Kleiner (Hamid, Naganath) co-leads, so Hanabi was not first ahead of the leads ([src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement)).\n- The pairing was all three senior Hanabi partners together: Thakur, Offutt (Index investor since 2018) and Volpi (a former Index GP). That reads as a firm-wide check, not a deal she sourced on her own.\n- The intro path is not found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hanabi Capital | Partner | by 2025-06 | [src](https://www.hanabi.com/team) |\n| Index Ventures | Former Partner per Hanabi bio (investor from 2023 per aggregator) | 2023 | [src](https://www.hanabi.com/team/ishani-thakur) |\n| Mike Volpi | Hanabi partner; co-signer on Traversal | 2025 | [src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement) |\n| Bryan Offutt | Hanabi partner, ex-Index; co-signer on Traversal | 2025 | [src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement) |\n| Shreyan Jain | Hanabi partner | | [src](https://www.hanabi.com/team) |\n| DeepMind | Former robotics/RL researcher | | [src](https://www.hanabi.com/team/ishani-thakur) |\n| MIT | BS in CS; robotics/RL research | | [src](https://www.hanabi.com/team/ishani-thakur) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is she the Hanabi partner on Applied Compute, Simile, Cognition or Netic? None of those posts names a Hanabi individual.\n- What exactly did she do at Index? Which deals is she on, and when was she promoted to Partner (if she was, before leaving)?\n- When did she join Hanabi, and is she a co-founder? Only a single aggregator says so.\n- What did she work on at DeepMind and MIT, and when? Do those labs give her a talent-sourcing edge, for example with robotics founders coming out of DeepMind?\n- How big is Hanabi's fund, and who are its LPs? Hanabi \"design\" is covered in Uncapped #52; a transcript is needed.\n- Does she have an X account, talks or podcasts? Search was not completed.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.hanabi.com/team/ishani-thakur\n- https://www.hanabi.com/team\n- https://www.hanabi.com/team/bryan-offutt\n- https://www.hanabi.com/companies\n- https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement\n- https://podcast.app/uncapped-52-mike-volpi-from-hanabi-capital-e457843013\n- https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/ex-openai-applied-compute-raises-20m\n- https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific\n- https://www.simile.com/blog/the-simulation-company\n- https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents\n- https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm\n- (aggregator, now 404) https://theorg.com/org/index-ventures/org-chart/ishani-thakur\n", "person:jack-altman": "\n# Jack Altman\n\n## One-line take\nAn operator-turned-investor who uses a podcast to build relationships. They took a $425M solo fund and a podcast audience into the Benchmark partnership. Their best AI calls (David AI, Legora, Monaco) are applied AI and AI data, not labs or chips.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** access. That comes from a Lattice founder's credibility (YC W16, $3B valuation), the Altman name, and the Uncapped podcast. The podcast doubles as a channel for founders, LPs and firms: they hosted two of their own Fund II LPs on it, and aired a full-partnership Benchmark episode 13 days before joining Benchmark. They raised Fund II ($275M) in about a week ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/16/jack-altman-raised-a-new-275m-early-stage-fund-in-a-mere-week/)).\n- **How they work:** they back founders, then take board seats and do the work. They publicly attack the \"no board seat\" pitch.\n- **Constraints:**\n  - By their own account they lack the \"oddities\" of great investors.\n  - At Benchmark, everything runs through a five-person flat partnership.\n  - They name neolabs and heavy robotics as categories that don't start with a normal seed check, so these sit outside a seed-fund playbook.\n- **Not first in on the headline deals:** at Legora and Monaco, they came in behind the firm that was already there (Benchmark at Legora, Founders Fund at Monaco).\n- **What they'd never do:** sell capital as \"no board seat,\" or run venture as a dollars-first factory.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **They name the categories big seed checks can't reach.** Asked A* which companies fund size keeps them out of, they said: \"I'm thinking of neolabs, or heavy robotics projects, or things like that\" ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/how-ai-is-rewriting-seed-stage-investing), 2026-05-20). This is where their lens stops relative to $600M lab seeds.\n- **Self-assessed limit:** \"I don't think I've got the oddities that sometimes I wish I had\" ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton), 2026-02-04).\n- **Board seats as work, not control:** \"It's become a thing to sell \"no board seat\"\" ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton)). This matches their own pattern of joining the boards of Owner (2022), David AI (2025) and Monaco (2026).\n- **Monaco timing (first Benchmark deal was a follow-on):** they told Founders Fund's partners \"you guys led a prior round and we did a recent one\" ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan), 2026-06-30). Monaco's CEO Sam Blond is an ex-Founders Fund partner. Altman's stated read: \"Usually you can kind of smell the founder everywhere in a company\" (same source).\n- **Legora timing (they were not first):** to Chetan Puttagunta, \"You did the seed\" ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton)). Legora's own post says the $25M Series A (2024-07-17) was led by Redpoint \"with participation from existing investors Benchmark\", and that Alt Capital was new in the round ([src](https://legora.com/blog/announcing-25m-in-series-a)). So Altman co-invested behind Benchmark about 19 months before joining it.\n- **David AI (their clearest lead in AI):**\n  - First Round led the $5M seed on 2025-01-16 ([src](https://www.withdavid.ai/news/announcing-our-5m-seed-round-led-by-first-round)).\n  - About 4 months later, Alt Capital and Amplify co-led the $25M Series A, and the company's announcement said it was excited \"to welcome Jack Altman to our board\" ([src](https://www.withdavid.ai/news/announcing-our-25m-series-a), 2025-05-22).\n  - About 4.5 months after that, Meritech and NVIDIA led a $50M Series B, with Alt following on ([src](https://www.withdavid.ai/news/announcing-our-50m-series-b), 2025-10-08).\n  - David AI sells audio training data to \"most of the leading AI labs\" (same source).\n- **The podcast doubled as an LP channel:**\n  - Uncapped #14 (2025-06-24) hosted UMich's Dan Feder, and #34 (2025-11-25) hosted TrueBridge's Mel Williams. Both are Alt Capital II LPs per WSJ ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor))).\n  - The #34 notes list \"Mel's team has backed firms like Thrive, Founders Fund, Sequoia, and Alt Capital\" ([src](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss)).\n- **The podcast as Benchmark courtship:** Uncapped #41, in which \"the Benchmark partnership explains why they've resisted scale\", aired 2026-02-04 ([src](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss)). Benchmark announced Altman as GP on 2026-02-17 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/17/jack-altman-joins-benchmark-as-gp/)). Peter Fenton had already been a guest in 2025-07 (#18).\n- **Why Benchmark:** at Benchmark \"everything is designed so that there's literally nothing else to do but invest\" ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan)). They set this against Founders Fund, where partners also build companies.\n- **Contrarian macro view:** \"the value of bits goes down, the value of stuff goes up\" ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/bret-taylor-on-ai-and-the-future), 2026-02-19). This fits Antares (nuclear) and the bio and robotics close of Benchmark Breakfast #1.\n- **Admitted miss:** on lawyers adopting AI quickly, \"I don't know if I personally would have seen it coming\" ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/building-an-ai-native-software-company), 2026-03-13).\n- **On lab talent:** of Ilya Sutskever and Greg Brockman's era, \"They were doing it when it was really not cool\" ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton)). They prize conviction shown at a painful time.\n- **How they got into venture:** in first-person show notes about Owner's Adam Guild, they wrote that the board role \"made me realize I want to do venture for the rest of my career\" ([src](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss), Uncapped #4, 2025-04-01).\n- **Fund mechanics:** Alt Capital II closed \"from start to finish\" in a week, and Sam Altman was not an LP (WSJ via [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/16/jack-altman-raised-a-new-275m-early-stage-fund-in-a-mere-week/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **Why not high:** they have no lab, chip or compute deal of their own. Their portfolio is applied AI (Legora, Monaco, Pursuit), restaurant software (Owner) and hard tech outside AI (Antares). They also say neolabs don't fit a $5–10M seed.\n- **Why not low:**\n  - **David AI** (training data for frontier labs, with NVIDIA in the B) is close to my compute and labs lens.\n  - **Monaco** is the AI-native automation shape I like (Serval-like).\n  - They now sit inside Benchmark, next to Vishria (Cerebras) and Randle (Anthropic at KP). Benchmark Breakfast gives an ongoing public read of the partnership's views on frontier models: the Anthropic S-1, the inference-cloud market, open versus closed models.\n  - Their LP network (Cendana, TrueBridge, UMich) is directly useful to me as a manager.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** David AI, Monaco, Legora (as a timing case), Antares (data-center power), and the 2024 accelerator with Microsoft GPU access.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in a St. Louis suburb ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor))). They are the brother of Sam Altman and Max Altman.\n- Princeton, B.A. economics, 2011 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor))).\n- Gleacher & Company (NY), about 1 year. Then Teespring, rising to VP business development ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor))). TechCrunch says Teespring grew from 10 to 500 employees during that time ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2016/05/12/lattice-be-productive/)).\n- 2015: co-founded Lattice with Eric Koslow. Lattice went through YC Winter '16 (\"When I did YC in Winter '16\", [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/y-combinator-in-the-age-of-ai-ep)). Thrive led the 2016 seed ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2016/05/12/lattice-be-productive/)). Lattice reached a $3B valuation by 2022, and they stepped down as CEO in Dec 2023 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor))).\n- They ran two funds as Altman Capital, about $100M across roughly 100 deals. They also had a role at Hydrazine ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor))).\n- 2021: co-led Routable's $30M Series B with Sam Altman ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/04/15/altman-brothers-lead-b2b-payment-startup-routables-30m-series-b/)).\n- 2022: Altman Capital led Owner.com's Series A ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2022/03/03/owner-com-independent-restaurants/)).\n- Feb 2024: Alt Capital Fund I, $150M. Apr 2024: enterprise-AI accelerator in which Alt and nine other firms got access to Microsoft GPU clusters (WSJ via [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor))).\n- Sep 2025: Fund II, $275M. LPs include Cendana, UMich and TrueBridge ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor))).\n- 2026-02-17: Benchmark GP. The Alt teammates followed, and Altman kept their board seats ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/17/jack-altman-joins-benchmark-as-gp/)). Fund II stopped new investing ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor))).\n- 2021 book: *People Strategy* ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor))).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.altcap.com/ | Alt Capital page is a bare logo as of 2026-11-09 |\n| Substack / blog | https://uncappedpod.com/ | Uncapped Substack with full transcripts since Jan 2026 (byline \"Nate\"). Personal https://jackaltman.substack.com/ has one 2019 post |\n| X | https://x.com/jaltma | Linked from every episode; could not fetch (402), follower count not found |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | TechCrunch quotes their LinkedIn post on joining Benchmark |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Uncapped with Jack Altman; Benchmark Breakfast | RSS https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss. Uncapped #1–#58 ran 2025-03 to 2026-10, roughly weekly then biweekly. Benchmark Breakfast #1 aired 2026-10-29 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nAll are episodes they hosted. No guest appearances were found.\n- 2026-10 — Benchmark Breakfast #1 (with Ev Randle) — [RSS](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss) — covered the Anthropic S-1 revenue-vs-compute chart, sizing the inference cloud, open versus closed models, \"abliterated\" open-weight models, and bio and robotics.\n- 2026-09 — Uncapped #57, Andrew Feldman (Cerebras), with Eric Vishria — [RSS](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss) — chips, the supply chain, TSMC.\n- 2026-07 — Uncapped #54, Sam Blond (Monaco) — a portfolio CEO episode two months after the Benchmark deal.\n- 2026-06 — Uncapped #53, Trae Stephens and Delian Asparouhov — [transcript](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan) — Benchmark vs Founders Fund, Monaco round order, chips and Taiwan.\n- 2026-05 — Uncapped #49, Hartz and Siegel (A*) — [transcript](https://uncappedpod.com/p/how-ai-is-rewriting-seed-stage-investing) — the \"venture arrogance score,\" and neolabs as outside seed reach.\n- 2026-03 — Uncapped #44, Max Junestrand (Legora), with Chetan Puttagunta — [transcript](https://uncappedpod.com/p/building-an-ai-native-software-company).\n- 2026-02 — Uncapped #41, The Benchmark Partnership — [transcript](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton) — aired 13 days before their GP announcement.\n- 2025-11 — Uncapped #34, Mel Williams (TrueBridge, an LP); 2025-06 — #14, Dan Feder (UMich, an LP); 2025-06 — #13, Sam Altman; 2025-05 — #11, Jordan Bramble (Antares, portfolio); 2025-04 — #4, Adam Guild (Owner, portfolio) — [RSS](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss).\n\n## How they think\n- **Founders over theses.** Closing the Benchmark episode, they praised the partners for answering \"founders\" rather than giving a high-minded thesis ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton)).\n- **Boards mean work.** Investors who can't add more money have to tell founders the truth, and \"no board seat\" is a pitch for deploying more capital with less help (same source).\n- **Physical over digital in the long run** (\"bits down, stuff up\"). They are worried about chip fabs and Taiwan ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan)).\n- **Skeptical of mega-funds at seed.** They take Josh Kopelman's \"venture arrogance score\" seriously ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/how-ai-is-rewriting-seed-stage-investing)).\n- **Calls:**\n  - Right: Owner (seed, then led the A); David AI (from a $25M A to a $50M B in about 4.5 months); Legora (from the $25M Series A in 2024 to an $8.8B Series E in Oct 2026, [src](https://legora.com/newsroom)).\n  - Admitted miss: didn't foresee how fast lawyers would adopt AI.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Uncapped #41, The Benchmark Partnership](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton): their questions show what they value: boards, oddity, Legora.\n2. [Uncapped #53, Founders Fund](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan): Benchmark vs Founders Fund structure, Monaco round order, chips.\n3. [Uncapped #49, A*](https://uncappedpod.com/p/how-ai-is-rewriting-seed-stage-investing): fund size versus seed, and neolabs.\n4. Benchmark Breakfast #1 ([RSS](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss)): their current frontier-AI views alongside Ev Randle.\n5. [David AI Series A post](https://www.withdavid.ai/news/announcing-our-25m-series-a): the clearest record of an AI deal they led.\n\n## Social engagement\nThe X account (@jaltma) could not be fetched, so posting topics and amplification patterns were not verified. On the podcast, guests skew toward top-tier GPs (Sequoia, a16z, Founders Fund, Khosla, Thrive), their own LPs (TrueBridge, UMich), portfolio CEOs (Owner, Antares, Legora, Monaco) and OpenAI (Sam Altman, Brad Lightcap) ([src](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss)).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| David AI | Series A $25M | 2025-05-22 | Co-led (with Amplify); board | First Round, YC, BoxGroup | [src](https://www.withdavid.ai/news/announcing-our-25m-series-a) |\n| David AI | Series B $50M | 2025-10-08 | No (Meritech) | NVIDIA, First Round, Amplify, YC | [src](https://www.withdavid.ai/news/announcing-our-50m-series-b) |\n| Legora | Series A $25M | 2024-07-17 | No (Redpoint) | Benchmark, YC, Wayfinder | [src](https://legora.com/blog/announcing-25m-in-series-a) |\n| Monaco | Series B | 2026-05 | Yes (Benchmark); board | Founders Fund led the prior round | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor)), [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan) |\n| Owner.com | Series A | 2022-03-03 | Yes (Altman Capital); board | Redpoint, Day One, SaaStr Fund, Browder | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2022/03/03/owner-com-independent-restaurants/) |\n| Antares | Series B $96M | 2025-12-02 | No (Shine) | Caffeinated, FiftyThree Stations, Industrious | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/02/microreactor-startup-antares-raises-96m-for-land-sea-and-space-based-nuclear-power/) |\n| Pursuit | pre-A (unspecified) | before 2026-04 | Unknown | Bill Gurley, 87 Capital | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/29/bill-gurley-jack-altman-back-startup-pursuit-which-helps-companies-sell-to-government/) |\n| Drafted | seed | 2025-12 | No | Convective, Patrick Collison, Josh Buckley | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/23/this-founder-just-landed-backing-for-a-second-go-at-the-same-problem-affordable-custom-home-design/) |\n| Routable | Series B $30M | 2021-04-15 | Co-led with Sam Altman | Flexport, angels | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/04/15/altman-brothers-lead-b2b-payment-startup-routables-30m-series-b/) |\n| Rippling, CompLabs | unknown | — | — | — | PitchBook via [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/17/jack-altman-joins-benchmark-as-gp/) |\n| Figma, Flexport, Opendoor, Finch, Durable | unknown | — | — | — | [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor)) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Owner.com:** Altman Capital was in at the seed. Owner raised a Series A it didn't need in order to \"deepen its relationship\" with Altman Capital, which led the round, and Altman joined the board ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2022/03/03/owner-com-independent-restaurants/)). Owner's valuation went from $200M (2024) to $1B (2025) ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor))). They say this board role is why they went into venture full-time.\n- **Legora:** Chetan Puttagunta (Benchmark) did the seed while Legora was in YC in Stockholm, when Harvey already existed ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton)). Alt Capital joined the Redpoint-led A in July 2024. Ten months later Legora raised a Series B at a $675M valuation ([src](https://legora.com/blog/series-b)). How Altman got into the A was not found.\n- **David AI:** Altman took a board seat at the Series A, about 10 months after the company was founded ([src](https://www.withdavid.ai/news/announcing-our-25m-series-a)). Within 5 months the company had several Mag7 firms as customers and NVIDIA as an investor ([src](https://www.withdavid.ai/news/announcing-our-50m-series-b)).\n- **Monaco:** Sam Blond spent about a year at Founders Fund before founding Monaco. Founders Fund led a prior round, then Benchmark (Altman) did the next one in May 2026 and Altman joined the board ([src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan); [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor))). Monaco's site lists Peter Thiel, Garry Tan and Ryan Petersen as investors ([src](https://www.monaco.com/)). Business Insider's account of the deal could not be fetched.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Benchmark | General Partner | 2026-02 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/17/jack-altman-joins-benchmark-as-gp/) |\n| Ev Randle | Co-host, Benchmark Breakfast | 2026-10 | [src](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss) |\n| Chetan Puttagunta | Legora co-investor (Chetan seed, Alt A); partner | 2024-07 | [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/building-an-ai-native-software-company) |\n| Eric Vishria, Peter Fenton | Partners; podcast guests before the hire | 2025-07 | [src](https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss) |\n| Sam Altman | Brother; co-led Routable; not an Alt II LP | — | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/16/jack-altman-raised-a-new-275m-early-stage-fund-in-a-mere-week/) |\n| Max Altman | Brother; Alt investor (Wikipedia) vs Saga Ventures co-founder (TechCrunch) | — | needs-id |\n| TrueBridge (Mel Williams), UMich (Dan Feder), Cendana | Alt Capital II LPs; first two were podcast guests | 2025 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor)) |\n| Sam Blond (Monaco), Adam Guild (Owner), David AI | Board seats | 2022–2026 | see Portfolio |\n| Founders Fund (Delian Asparouhov, Trae Stephens) | Monaco co-investors | 2026 | [src](https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan) |\n| Redpoint; First Round; Amplify | Co-investors (Legora/Owner; David AI) | 2022–2025 | see Portfolio |\n| Eric Koslow | Lattice co-founder | 2015 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor)) |\n| Bala Chandrasekaran, Vivek Katara | Alt Capital partners; Benchmark status unknown | — | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor)) |\n| Microsoft | GPU access for the 2024 accelerator | 2024-04 | WSJ via [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor)) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What did the 2024 accelerator cohort include, and did any companies train models on the Microsoft GPUs? The WSJ article was paywalled and the web-search budget had run out.\n- What is Monaco's Series B size and who co-invested? Business Insider and Bloomberg returned 403.\n- Which Alt Capital staff are now at Benchmark, and in what roles? Who is \"Nate,\" the Uncapped Substack author?\n- Has Altman done any Benchmark deal since Monaco? None found as of 2026-11-09.\n- Would they ever back a neolab or chip company at Benchmark, given their own framing of fund-size limits?\n- What happens to Alt Capital II's uncalled capital, and how do the LPs (Cendana, TrueBridge) feel about it?\n- Their X posts were not reviewed.\n\n## Sources\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Altman_(investor)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/17/jack-altman-joins-benchmark-as-gp/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/16/jack-altman-raised-a-new-275m-early-stage-fund-in-a-mere-week/\n- https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss\n- https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton\n- https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan\n- https://uncappedpod.com/p/how-ai-is-rewriting-seed-stage-investing\n- https://uncappedpod.com/p/building-an-ai-native-software-company\n- https://uncappedpod.com/p/bret-taylor-on-ai-and-the-future\n- https://uncappedpod.com/p/y-combinator-in-the-age-of-ai-ep\n- https://www.withdavid.ai/news/announcing-our-5m-seed-round-led-by-first-round\n- https://www.withdavid.ai/news/announcing-our-25m-series-a\n- https://www.withdavid.ai/news/announcing-our-50m-series-b\n- https://legora.com/blog/announcing-25m-in-series-a\n- https://legora.com/blog/series-b\n- https://legora.com/newsroom\n- https://www.monaco.com/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2022/03/03/owner-com-independent-restaurants/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/02/microreactor-startup-antares-raises-96m-for-land-sea-and-space-based-nuclear-power/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/29/bill-gurley-jack-altman-back-startup-pursuit-which-helps-companies-sell-to-government/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/23/this-founder-just-landed-backing-for-a-second-go-at-the-same-problem-affordable-custom-home-design/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2021/04/15/altman-brothers-lead-b2b-payment-startup-routables-30m-series-b/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2016/05/12/lattice-be-productive/\n\n## Update 2026-11-11 (early-backers sweep)\n- Firm page added: [alt-capital](../firms/alt-capital.md).\n- Form D detail: Alt Capital I sold $150M to 48 investors ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2008825/000200882524000001/primary_doc.xml)); Alt Capital II shows $275M sold to 58 investors with a first sale on 2025-09-02 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2083180/000123191925000200/primary_doc.xml)). Altman is the only individual named on either.\n- Alt Capital still appears in rounds dated after the Benchmark move (depthfirst B, Avoca B, Town A, Trebellar A per [CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/altman-capital), aggregator), so Fund I reserves are still being deployed.\n", "person:jahanvi-sardana": "\n# Jahanvi Sardana\n\n## One-line take\nA New York Index investor who sources by knowing founders for years before they raise. Their one AI deal that fits the lens is Crosby (AI-native law firm), and they came in a round after Sequoia and BCV, not first.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** they hold long relationships with founders and back the person before the idea. They met Crosby's CEO at his previous company, A.Team, and say they knew they would back Arca's founder before he had picked what to build. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arca-a-calmer-relationship-with-money/)\n- **The pattern:** AI-native *services*. Crosby is a law firm and Arca is a wealth advisor; in both, humans do the work and agents back them up. This is the \"automation\" end of the lens, not labs, pre-training or chips.\n- **What constrains them:** the book is fintech, healthcare and security (Lithic, Thatch, Garner, Sublime). On AI deals, a senior partner (Martin Mignot) is tagged alongside them. On Crosby they priced in after a Sequoia/BCV seed.\n- **What they'd never do (inference, not stated):** no sourced sign they would back a pre-product research lab or a hardware company. Nothing in the book or their posts touches compute.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Crosby came from two warm paths, A.Team and Ramp.** They write: \"I first met Ryan Daniels years ago, when he was building A.Team\". They also say they heard about CTO John Sarihan \"through our friends at Ramp.\" Ramp's founders were already angels in the Crosby seed, per Sequoia's post (see [alfred-lin](alfred-lin.md)). [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/)\n- **They used the Index portfolio as live distribution for Crosby.** Per the post, at an Index event portfolio companies \"including Cartesia, Parallel, and others converted on the spot.\" This doubles as customer diligence. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/)\n- **They back the founder before the idea.** On Arca they write that Index wanted to \"back him long before he knew what he wanted to build.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arca-a-calmer-relationship-with-money/) (2026-06-25)\n- **The decision rule they publish:** \"invest for strength, not for the absence of weakness.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/jahanvi-sardana/)\n- **A title signal the team page lacks:** a photo caption on the Arca post reads \"Index Partner Jahanvi Sardana\", but the team page still says \"Early-stage and growth investor\". The promotion date is not announced, so treat it as unconfirmed. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arca-a-calmer-relationship-with-money/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/jahanvi-sardana/)\n- **The Crosby lead is contested.** Index says \"We couldn't be more thrilled to lead Crosby's Series A\". BCV calls the A BCV-led, and Crosby's own post lists Index, BCV and Elad Gil without naming a lead, so it was most likely a co-lead (an inference). [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/) [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crosby-is-redefining-legal-work-with-ai-powered-contract-automation/) [src](https://crosby.ai/blog/series-a-and-faster-agreements)\n- **Security origin story:** Lookout founder Kevin Mahaffey \"introduced us to Sublime's founders\" years before the round. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/achieving-the-sublime-in-email-security-announcing-our-20m-series-a-investment/)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** Crosby (AI-native law firm, Series A 2025-10) is shaped like the AI-native automation companies the owner likes (Serval). Arca (AI-native wealth advisor, 2026) is adjacent. Beyond those two there is nothing on labs, pre-training, chips or compute. They were not first on Crosby: Sequoia and BCV co-led the seed about four months earlier. They are still worth knowing as an NY entry point into Index's AI-native-services pod, and for warm paths to Ramp and A.Team.\n\n## Background\n- B.S. in Finance and Business Analytics, The Wharton School (graduation year not stated). [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/jahanvi-sardana/)\n- Stripes (growth investor): backed Island, Axonius, Lithic and Oyster; dates not found. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/jahanvi-sardana/)\n- Index Ventures, New York: earliest authored post is 2024-04-24 (Sublime), so they joined by then. Exact join date not found. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/achieving-the-sublime-in-email-security-announcing-our-20m-series-a-investment/)\n- Captioned \"Index Partner\" by 2026-06-25. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arca-a-calmer-relationship-with-money/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/jahanvi-sardana/ | Index team page; 5 authored perspectives (as of 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes on Index Perspectives |\n| X | nothing found | Team page lists no personal X |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/jahanvi-sardana-4519a19a/ | From the Index team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. WebSearch budget was exhausted during this run, so podcast and X searches were not run. Only Index pages and Crosby's blog were fetched directly.\n\n## How they think\n- **Founder before idea.** They stay close to founders for years and then commit, sometimes before the company exists (Arca, Crosby). [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arca-a-calmer-relationship-with-money/)\n- **AI-native services over AI tools.** Both AI deals are full-service firms (law, wealth) run by humans and backed by agents. They frame Crosby as revenue enablement, not just legal: \"You don't need to squint to see the product-market fit.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/)\n- **Strength over flawlessness** as the published decision rule. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/jahanvi-sardana/)\n- Calls right or wrong: Crosby raised a $60M Series B co-led by Lux and Index about six months after the A, so the A was marked up quickly. [src](https://crosby.ai/blog/series-b-planting-our-flag)\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Contracts at the Speed of Business: Crosby](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/) (2025-10-08): the sourcing path and the portfolio-distribution tactic.\n2. [Arca: A calmer relationship with money](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arca-a-calmer-relationship-with-money/) (2026-06-25): backing the founder before the idea, the AI-native advisor model, and the \"Partner\" caption.\n3. [Garner: Bringing Intelligence to Healthcare's Hardest Choice](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/garner-bringing-intelligence-to-healthcares-hardest-choice/) (2026-05-28): the AI-as-data-layer framing in healthcare.\n4. [Sublime $20M Series A](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/achieving-the-sublime-in-email-security-announcing-our-20m-series-a-investment/) (2024-04-24): co-written with Shardul Shah; an intro through the portfolio.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. There is no personal X listed, and searches were not run (budget exhausted).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Crosby (AI-native law firm) | Series A ($20M) | 2025-10-08 | Index claims lead; BCV also claims it; Crosby names no lead | Bain Capital Ventures, Elad Gil, Sequoia, Cooley LLP, Patrick Collison; Martin Mignot tagged from Index | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/) [Crosby](https://crosby.ai/blog/series-a-and-faster-agreements) |\n| Crosby | Series B ($60M) | 2026-03-31 | Index co-led with Lux per Crosby; Sardana not named on B | Lux, Sequoia, Elad Gil, 01 Advisors, BCV | [Crosby](https://crosby.ai/blog/series-b-planting-our-flag) |\n| Arca (AI-native wealth management) | not stated | 2026-06-25 (post) | not stated | not stated; no other Index person tagged | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arca-a-calmer-relationship-with-money/) |\n| Garner Health (AI-powered healthcare data) | not stated | 2026-05-28 (post) | not stated | Index team: Emil Schaefer, Martin Mignot | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/garner-bringing-intelligence-to-healthcares-hardest-choice/) |\n| Thatch (ICHRA health benefits) | not stated | 2024-09-05 (post) | not stated | Co-byline Martin Mignot | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/empowering-choice-thatch-and-a-new-era-of-freedom-in-healthcare/) |\n| Sublime Security (email security) | Series A ($20M) | 2024-04-24 | not stated | Decibel; Dmitri Alperovitch (board); co-byline Shardul Shah | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/achieving-the-sublime-in-email-security-announcing-our-20m-series-a-investment/) |\n| Lithic (fintech) | not stated | not stated | not stated | Backed at Stripes, and also on the Index page | [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/jahanvi-sardana/) |\n| Island, Axonius, Oyster | not stated | not stated (Stripes era) | not stated | Stripes | [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/jahanvi-sardana/) |\n\n**Timing on AI deals:** on Crosby, Sequoia (Alfred Lin, Josephine Chen) and BCV co-led the $5.8M seed on 2025-06-17. Index came in at the A, about 3.7 months later, so it was not first. [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-crosby-a-law-firm-at-the-speed-of-ai/) On Arca, no earlier investors were found.\n\n**Senior/junior pairing:** Sardana holds the byline on Crosby and Martin Mignot (16-year Index partner) is tagged. Mignot co-signs Thatch and Garner, and Shardul Shah co-signs Sublime. Arca is a solo byline, which is consistent with the Partner caption. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/crosby/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arca-a-calmer-relationship-with-money/)\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Crosby:** they met CEO Ryan Daniels years earlier, while he was building A.Team with Raphael Ouzan, and heard about CTO John Sarihan through Ramp. Daniels had been an EIR at BCV, which incubated Crosby. Index won a lead or co-lead role at the A and ran a portfolio demo at an Index event, where Cartesia, Parallel and others signed up. Index co-led the B again with Lux. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/) [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crosby-is-redefining-legal-work-with-ai-powered-contract-automation/)\n- **Arca:** Index knew founder Rron Rexha for years and wanted to back him before he had an idea. Per the photo captions, the deal was signed on a napkin and they went to a Knicks game together. Bill McNabb, Jason Wenk and Morgan Housel are named as supporters. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arca-a-calmer-relationship-with-money/)\n- **Sublime:** intro from Lookout founder Kevin Mahaffey, an Index portfolio founder, a few years before the A. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/achieving-the-sublime-in-email-security-announcing-our-20m-series-a-investment/)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Martin Mignot (Index) | Senior partner tagged on Crosby; co-byline on Thatch; co-listed on Garner | 2024 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/empowering-choice-thatch-and-a-new-era-of-freedom-in-healthcare/) |\n| Shardul Shah (Index) | Co-byline on Sublime Series A | 2024 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/achieving-the-sublime-in-email-security-announcing-our-20m-series-a-investment/) |\n| Emil Schaefer (Index) | Co-listed on Garner Health | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/garner-health/) |\n| Ryan Daniels (Crosby CEO) | Knew him from his A.Team days | \"years ago\" before 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/) |\n| Ramp (founders) | Source of the Crosby CTO intro (\"our friends at Ramp\") | before 2025-10 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/) |\n| Rron Rexha (Arca CEO) | Long relationship, backed before the idea | \"for years\" before 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arca-a-calmer-relationship-with-money/) |\n| Kevin Mahaffey (Lookout) | Introduced Sublime's founders | before 2024 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/achieving-the-sublime-in-email-security-announcing-our-20m-series-a-investment/) |\n| Stripes | Former firm | dates not found | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/jahanvi-sardana/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did they join Index and when were they promoted to Partner? The caption says Partner but the team page doesn't. Check LinkedIn or an Index announcement.\n- What is Arca's round, size and co-investors? Is Index the first institutional money?\n- Were they involved in the Crosby Series B (Index co-led), or did a senior partner take it?\n- Are there any podcasts or X activity? Not searched this run because the budget was exhausted.\n- Do they cover any other AI-native services companies in NY that aren't yet on the team page?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/jahanvi-sardana/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arca-a-calmer-relationship-with-money/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/garner-bringing-intelligence-to-healthcares-hardest-choice/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/empowering-choice-thatch-and-a-new-era-of-freedom-in-healthcare/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/achieving-the-sublime-in-email-security-announcing-our-20m-series-a-investment/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/crosby/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/garner-health/\n- https://crosby.ai/blog/series-a-and-faster-agreements\n- https://crosby.ai/blog/series-b-planting-our-flag\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crosby-is-redefining-legal-work-with-ai-powered-contract-automation/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-crosby-a-law-firm-at-the-speed-of-ai/\n", "person:james-alcorn": "\n# James Alcorn\n\n## One-line take\n_Updated 2026-11-10 (second pass): re-checked every Lightspeed company page and company announcement; added Argilla co-lead, Periodic Labs backer-list check, Inferact Berkeley Chancellor's Fund. Web search budget was exhausted, so the primary-voice gap (podcasts, X) remains._\n\nHe is a 2024 lateral hire at Lightspeed whose real edge is the UC Berkeley Sky Lab pipeline: Arena, Virtue AI, Inferact (vLLM) and Judgment Labs. On the famous labs (Thinking Machines, Reflection, Periodic Labs) he sits on the deal team but is not first money.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He is a Berkeley economics alum ([bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/james-alcorn/)) and co-authored Lightspeed's $500k founding contribution to UC Berkeley's Sky Computing Lab, dated 2025-10-01 ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-sky-lab-supporting-uc-berkeleys-next-generation-of-entrepreneurs/)). That post names Ion Stoica (Arena) and Dawn Song (Virtue AI) as existing Lightspeed partnerships. Inferact's founding-member block includes Stoica and Joseph Gonzalez, both Sky Lab faculty ([src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference)). At least three of his eight named Lightspeed companies trace to that one lab cluster, and he helped institutionalize the relationship.\n- **Where he is actually first.** Lightspeed **led the Judgment Labs seed** and co-led the Series A with Greenoaks ([src](https://judgmentlabs.ai/blogs/announcing-32m-led-by-lightspeed)). It **co-led Inferact's $150M seed at $800M** with a16z ([src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference)). Both companies name him on their Lightspeed pages (Judgment: [src](https://lsvp.com/company/judgment-labs/); Inferact: [bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/james-alcorn/)).\n- **What constrains him.** On the headline labs he shares credit with senior Lightspeed partners: Ravi Mhatre on Thinking Machines, Periodic Labs and Arena, and Raviraj Jain (board) on Reflection. Lightspeed was a participant, not first, on the Thinking Machines seed and the Arena Series A. His personal lead authority on the big lab checks is unproven.\n- **What he would never do.** Nothing found in his own words. His only published thesis is open models and enterprise control of AI (Reflection memo), so a closed-model, API-only bet would be off-pattern. This is inference, not his statement.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Sky Lab is a sourcing engine, not CSR.** Alcorn co-authored the post saying \"Lightspeed has made $500k founding contribution to the partnership\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-sky-lab-supporting-uc-berkeleys-next-generation-of-entrepreneurs/), 2025-10-01). The same post credits \"Ion Stoica (with whom Lightspeed partnered with on Arena Intelligence\" and Dawn Song \"(with whom Lightspeed partnered with on Virtue AI)\". Three months later, Lightspeed co-led the seed of Inferact, the vLLM company whose founding members include Stoica and Gonzalez.\n- **Arena: in before the A was announced, then led the B.** The Sky Lab post (2025-10) already calls Arena a Lightspeed partnership. Arena's own blog lists Lightspeed only as a participant in the $150M Series A, January 2026, which Felicis and UC Investments led. Lightspeed then co-led the $200M Series B at $3.1B: \"Our Series B is co-led by Lightspeed Venture Partners and Khosla Ventures\" ([src](https://arena.ai/blog/series-b), 2026-10-08). No source names Alcorn personally on the B.\n- **Judgment Labs is a clean first-money deal.** \"Our Seed financing was led by Lightspeed Venture Partners\" ([src](https://judgmentlabs.ai/blogs/announcing-32m-led-by-lightspeed), 2026-05-12). Greenoaks came in later as Series A co-lead with Lightspeed. Other backers: Nova, Valor, Dynamic, Chris Manning, and the founders of DoorDash and Mercor.\n- **Inferact co-lead, not an a16z solo deal.** The company post says the round was \"led by a16z and Lightspeed\" ([src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference), 2026-01). a16z's own post reads as if a16z led alone.\n- **Reflection thesis, in his byline** (with Raviraj Jain): \"Open models can give organizations full control of their intelligence layer\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/towards-superintelligence-reflection-ai/)). The post was first published March 2025 and updated in August 2026 to reframe Reflection as \"now a frontier lab and a full-stack AI partner\".\n- **Origin of his AI conviction.** The Lightspeed bio says he \"developed early conviction in AI when, in 2018, he found the Word2Vec paper\" and that \"AI has reshuffled the deck\" ([bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/james-alcorn/)).\n- **Zetta pattern.** At Zetta he was an inception-stage AI investor. Cleric announced it was \"backed by Zetta Venture Partners in a $4.3M seed round\" ([src](https://cleric.ai/blog), 2024-03-21). Cleric's post does not name him; his bio claims it.\n- **Periodic Labs does not list Lightspeed.** Lightspeed's page tags Periodic as \"Series A, 2025\" with Alcorn and Mhatre ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/periodic-labs/), checked 2026-11-10). Periodic's own site, dated 2026-10-01, names a16z, Felicis, DST, NVentures, Accel and four angels as the investors \"who share our vision\" and omits Lightspeed ([src](https://periodic.com/)). CRV and Radical are also missing from that list, so this looks like a small allocation or a later round rather than a lead. Do not read Periodic as an Alcorn conviction bet.\n- **Inferact's round also included UC Berkeley's own money.** The company lists the \"UC Berkeley Chancellor's Fund\" among participants next to Lightspeed ([src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference)). Together with UC Investments co-leading Arena's A, the university is on both sides of his Sky Lab deals.\n- **Zetta: he co-led, not just participated.** Argilla's $1.6M round was \"co-led by Zetta Venture Partners\" and Caixa Capital Risc ([src](https://argilla.io/blog/argilla-raises-1-6m), 2023-01-25). Hugging Face later acquired Argilla (2024-06). Neither post names Alcorn; his bio claims Argilla. Zetta's current team page does not list him at all ([src](https://www.zettavp.com/team)).\n- **No podcasts, Substack or talks found.** X (x.com/JamesAlcorn94) and LinkedIn could not be fetched, and the web-search budget for this run was exhausted. See Open questions.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.**\n- **Deals that matter to you:** seed and early checks into frontier and new labs (Thinking Machines $2B seed, Reflection Series A, Periodic Labs), AI inference compute (Inferact's $150M seed is exactly the large-seed shape you write), and AI-native infra (Unsloth, Judgment Labs, Arena).\n- **Caveat:** on the famous labs he is a passenger next to Mhatre and Jain. His own edge, and the place he is first, is the Berkeley open-source infra layer (vLLM/Inferact, Arena, Judgment). For you he is more useful as a **co-investor and signal on Berkeley-originated labs and infra** than as a Thinking Machines-style allocator.\n- **Not \"principal alpha\" in the strict sense.** He was already a partner for six years before Lightspeed, so he is a senior lateral, not a fast-promoted junior. He fits the \"under-the-radar partner at a $10M+ fund\" bucket instead.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Brisbane, Australia. Economics degree, UC Berkeley ([bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/james-alcorn/)). Year not found.\n- Found the Word2Vec paper in 2018, the start of his AI conviction ([bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/james-alcorn/)).\n- Zetta Venture Partners: \"for 6 years he backed teams from inception building AI-native applications and infrastructure\" ([bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/james-alcorn/)). Bio lists Weaviate, Argilla and Cleric. Exact start and end years are not found; inferred as roughly 2018 to 2024 from the 2024 Lightspeed join, so treat them as unconfirmed.\n- Lightspeed Venture Partners, Partner, joined 2024: \"James joined Lightspeed in 2024 to partner with founders in AI and infrastructure\" ([bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/james-alcorn/)). The headshot upload path is dated 2024/07.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://lsvp.com/team-member/james-alcorn/ | Lightspeed bio; email jalcorn@lsvp.com listed |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Two Lightspeed stories co-authored (Reflection, Sky Lab) |\n| X | https://x.com/JamesAlcorn94 | Linked from bio; not fetchable (402) on 2026-11-10 |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/james-alcorn-b04880ba/ | Linked from bio; not fetchable |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. Web search was unavailable in both passes (2026-11-10) (budget exhausted), so treat this as not searched, not as confirmed absent.\n\n## How they think\n- **Open models and sovereignty.** Enterprises and governments want control of their intelligence layer and to avoid vendor lock-in (Reflection memo, [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/towards-superintelligence-reflection-ai/)).\n- **Replatforming.** \"AI has reshuffled the deck\": the AI shift reopens markets to startups ([bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/james-alcorn/)).\n- **Research-lab adjacency.** He backs companies that come out of academic open-source projects (vLLM, LMArena) and lab faculty (Stoica, Song), and puts money into the lab itself (Sky Lab contribution).\n- **Calls so far.** Arena went from Series A participant (Jan 2026) to a $3.1B Series B co-led by Lightspeed (Oct 2026). Reflection went from the ~$545-555M post Series A (2025-03) to an $8B Series B (2025-10). Neither proves his personal call.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Lightspeed + Sky Lab](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-sky-lab-supporting-uc-berkeleys-next-generation-of-entrepreneurs/) (2025-10-01, with Faraz Fatemi): the sourcing map.\n2. [Open Intelligence for Everyone: Reflection AI](https://lsvp.com/stories/towards-superintelligence-reflection-ai/) (2025-03, updated 2026-08, with Raviraj Jain): his open-model thesis.\n3. [Lightspeed bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/james-alcorn/): portfolio list and the Word2Vec origin.\n4. [Judgment Labs $32M announcement](https://judgmentlabs.ai/blogs/announcing-32m-led-by-lightspeed): the cleanest Lightspeed-led seed on his list.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X could not be fetched on 2026-11-10.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Thinking Machines Lab | Seed ($2B at $12B) | 2025-07 | No (a16z led) | Ravi Mhatre, Lisa Han (LSVP); Nvidia, Accel, AMD, Cisco, Jane Street, GV, Conviction | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/thinking-machines/); [Lightspeed in seed](https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/accel-reportedly-talks-lead-1b-193629728.html) |\n| Reflection AI | Series A ($105M; $130M with seed) | 2025-03-07 | Co-led (with CRV) | Raviraj Jain (LSVP, board); Sequoia and CRV co-led the earlier seed | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/reflection/); [memo](https://lsvp.com/stories/towards-superintelligence-reflection-ai/) |\n| Periodic Labs | Listed as \"Series A\", 2025 | 2025 | Unknown, likely small | Ravi Mhatre (LSVP). Lightspeed absent from public $300M seed lists (a16z led, Felicis first check) and from periodic.com's investor list as of 2026-10-01 | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/periodic-labs/) |\n| Arena (LMArena) | Listed \"Series A, 2025\"; Arena dates the $150M A to Jan 2026 | 2026-01 | No (Felicis and UC Investments led) | Bucky Moore, Ravi Mhatre (LSVP); a16z, Kleiner Perkins, The House Fund, LDVP, Laude | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/arena/); [arena.ai](https://arena.ai/blog/series-b) |\n| Arena (firm follow-on) | Series B ($200M at $3.1B) | 2026-10-08 | Lightspeed co-led with Khosla; Alcorn not named on this round | Salesforce Ventures, 01 Advisors, Dell Tech Capital, Acrew, a16z, Felicis, AMP PBC | [arena.ai](https://arena.ai/blog/series-b) |\n| Inferact (vLLM) | Seed ($150M at $800M) | 2026-01 | Co-led (with a16z) | Sequoia, Altimeter, Redpoint, ZhenFund, The House Fund, Striker, Laude, Databricks Ventures | [inferact](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference); [bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/james-alcorn/) |\n| Judgment Labs | Seed | before 2026-05 | Yes (Lightspeed led) | Guru Chahal (LSVP) | [judgment](https://judgmentlabs.ai/blogs/announcing-32m-led-by-lightspeed) |\n| Judgment Labs | Series A ($32M total with seed) | 2026-05-12 | Co-led (with Greenoaks) | Nova, Valor, Dynamic, Chris Manning | [judgment](https://judgmentlabs.ai/blogs/announcing-32m-led-by-lightspeed); [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/judgment-labs/) |\n| Virtue AI | Seed | 2025 | Unknown | Guru Chahal (LSVP); Dawn Song (founder) | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/virtue-ai/) |\n| Unsloth | Not stated | not stated | Unknown | Guru Chahal, Nnamdi Iregbulem, Hemant Mohapatra (LSVP) | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/unsloth/) |\n| Cleric (at Zetta) | Seed ($4.3M) | 2024-03-21 | Only investor named | none named | [cleric](https://cleric.ai/blog) |\n| Weaviate (at Zetta) | not found | | | | [bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/james-alcorn/) |\n| Argilla (at Zetta) | Seed ($1.6M) | 2023-01-25 | Zetta co-led (with Caixa Capital Risc); acquired by Hugging Face 2024-06 | Caixa Capital Risc, ENIAC Ventures | [argilla](https://argilla.io/blog/argilla-raises-1-6m); [bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/james-alcorn/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Reflection.** Lightspeed did not come in first. Sequoia and CRV co-led the 2024 seed. Lightspeed co-led the Series A with CRV (announced 2025-03-07), with Jain taking the board seat, and re-upped in the Nvidia-led $2B Series B (see `data/rows/roundmap-reflection-ai-rounds.csv`). Lightspeed's memo says: \"We're thrilled to partner with Reflection and co-lead their Series A.\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/towards-superintelligence-reflection-ai/))\n- **Thinking Machines.** Lightspeed was missing from the company's July 2025 seed list. A 2026-09 report citing The Information later named it ([src](https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/accel-reportedly-talks-lead-1b-193629728.html)). This was a participation check with three Lightspeed partners named.\n- **Arena and Inferact.** Both came out of the Berkeley Sky Lab orbit (Stoica). Lightspeed's Sky Lab gift (2025-10) sat between its Arena entry and its Inferact seed co-lead (2026-01). The intro path is not documented; Sky Lab is the probable channel (inference).\n- **Judgment Labs.** Lightspeed led the seed, then shared the Series A with Greenoaks. Who introduced the founders is not documented.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ravi Mhatre | Lightspeed co-deal-team on Thinking Machines, Periodic Labs, Arena | 2025 | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/periodic-labs/) |\n| Raviraj Jain | Co-author and co-deal-team on Reflection (board seat per press; lsvp.com page lists both with no board designation) | 2025 | [memo](https://lsvp.com/stories/towards-superintelligence-reflection-ai/) |\n| Guru Chahal | Co-deal-team on Virtue AI, Judgment Labs, Unsloth | 2025 | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/judgment-labs/) |\n| Bucky Moore | Co-deal-team on Arena | 2025 | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/arena/) |\n| Lisa Han | Co-deal-team on Thinking Machines | 2025 | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/thinking-machines/) |\n| Faraz Fatemi | Co-author of the Sky Lab post | 2025-10 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-sky-lab-supporting-uc-berkeleys-next-generation-of-entrepreneurs/) |\n| Ion Stoica | Lightspeed partnership on Arena; Inferact founding member | 2025 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-sky-lab-supporting-uc-berkeleys-next-generation-of-entrepreneurs/) |\n| Dawn Song | Lightspeed partnership on Virtue AI | 2025 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-sky-lab-supporting-uc-berkeleys-next-generation-of-entrepreneurs/) |\n| Zetta Venture Partners | Former firm (Partner, 6 years) | until 2024 | [bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/james-alcorn/) |\n| Greenoaks | Series A co-lead on Judgment Labs | 2026 | [src](https://judgmentlabs.ai/blogs/announcing-32m-led-by-lightspeed) |\n| a16z | Seed co-lead on Inferact | 2026 | [src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Periodic Labs discrepancy.** lsvp.com lists \"Series A, 2025\" with Alcorn and Mhatre ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/periodic-labs/)). Yet no public source on the $300M seed (2025-09-30, a16z led, Felicis first check) or the reported AMP-led 2026 round names Lightspeed. When did Lightspeed get in, and in what round?\n- **Arena discrepancy.** lsvp.com says \"Series A, 2025\", while Arena dates its $150M Series A to January 2026. Did Lightspeed commit in late 2025, as the October 2025 Sky Lab post implies?\n- **Virtue AI.** On 2026-11-10, virtueai.com 301-redirects to fortinet.com/products/fortiaigate. Neither that Fortinet page nor its press list mentions Virtue AI. Is there an unannounced acquisition or asset deal? Unconfirmed; do not treat it as an exit.\n- **Unsloth.** No round, date or amount found.\n- **Primary voice.** Search X (@JamesAlcorn94), podcasts and Berkeley events for anything in his own words. Search was unavailable this run.\n- **Zetta dates** and his personal role on Weaviate and Argilla. Zetta's team page no longer lists him, and neither Argilla's nor Cleric's announcements name an individual.\n- **Virtue AI round.** Lightspeed lists it only as \"Seed, 2025\" with no amount; founders are Bo Li, Dawn Song, Sanmi Koyejo and Carlos Guestrin ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/virtue-ai/)). Whether Lightspeed led needs a press source.\n- **Why so few deals?** Eight Lightspeed companies in ~2.5 years, with only two where Lightspeed demonstrably led (Judgment seed, Inferact seed co-lead). Either he is a deal-team second on most, or several deals are unannounced. His bio list is the full public record found.\n\n## Sources\n- https://lsvp.com/team-member/james-alcorn/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/towards-superintelligence-reflection-ai/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-sky-lab-supporting-uc-berkeleys-next-generation-of-entrepreneurs/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/thinking-machines/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/reflection/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/periodic-labs/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/arena/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/unsloth/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/virtue-ai/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/judgment-labs/\n- https://arena.ai/blog/series-b\n- https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference\n- https://judgmentlabs.ai/blogs/announcing-32m-led-by-lightspeed\n- https://cleric.ai/blog\n- https://argilla.io/blog/argilla-raises-1-6m\n- https://periodic.com/\n- https://www.zettavp.com/team\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/accel-reportedly-talks-lead-1b-193629728.html\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/lmarena-announcement\n- https://www.ycombinator.com/companies/unsloth-ai\n- Internal: data/rows/roundmap-reflection-ai-rounds.csv, data/rows/roundmap-periodic-labs-rounds.csv, data/rows/roundmap-thinking-machines-lab-rounds.csv\n", "person:james-detweiler": "\n# James Detweiler\n\n## One-line take\nHe is Felicis's AI hire who became GP. He joined in 2022 to build the AI portfolio and was promoted to GP about four years later ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner), 2026-02-12). The firm credits him with bringing Skild AI in at its Series A. He co-signed Felicis's Ricursive Intelligence and Mercor posts and was thanked on Periodic Labs.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He combines research proximity with essay-first theses. He is a Dartmouth physics and mathematical-finance graduate ([src](https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler)) who writes long thematic pieces before he invests: agents (2024-08), ML serving (2023-08), avatars (2025-05), healthcare authorization (2025-06), human/agent partitioning (2025-08) and \"The Great Splintering\" (2026-04). He then turns up in person at labs, symposia and robotics demos.\n  - Ricursive came from a seat next to Anna Goldie at a research symposium, plus Felicis's sponsorship of Azalia Mirhoseini's Stanford lab ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/ricursive-intelligence-a-step-change-on-the-path-to-agi)).\n  - Skild came from a trip to Pittsburgh to see the robots ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-skild)).\n- **What constrains him.** On the frontier-AI names he is rarely first in and rarely the sole lead:\n  - Periodic Labs: Peter Deng wrote the first check; Detweiler is only thanked.\n  - Ricursive: Sequoia led the seed and Lightspeed led the A.\n  - Skild: the Series A lead is not stated in Felicis's post.\n\n  His own clean leads are Series A/B application and infra deals: Predibase A, DeepJudge A, Mercor B, Singularity Defense A (co-lead). He usually co-signs with a senior partner: Senkut, Peechu, Delcambre, Deng, Faga or Pezeshki.\n- **What he would never do (inferred from his writing, not stated).** Bet on total automation or on centralized aggregators. His 2025 partition essay argues that agent value does not come from automating everything. His 2026 essay argues that trust resists aggregation. Both are quoted below.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He was a Felicis LP before he was a Felicis investor.** The team page says he was an investor at SVB Capital \"and LP in Felicis\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler)). That makes him an LP-side hire, which is useful context for the owner's LP questions.\n- **Ricursive sourcing was a symposium seat.** The post says he managed to \"score a seat next to Anna at a research symposium\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/ricursive-intelligence-a-step-change-on-the-path-to-agi), 2026-02-10). Felicis is also a SEAMS affiliate at Stanford and sponsors Mirhoseini's Scaling Intelligence Lab (same src). The lab sponsorship works as a sourcing channel into AI-for-chips.\n- **Ricursive thesis, in their words:** \"chip design had become the primary bottleneck in AI scaling\" (same src). That is a direct AI-semis thesis, which matches the owner's lens.\n- **Skild is his sourced deal.** \"He brought Skild AI (now valued at $14B) to Felicis at the series A\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner)). The Skild post frames the bet as \"The answer lies with scale.\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-skild), 2024-07-09)\n- **Mandate in one phrase.** He backs \"founders solving “root node” problems in AI\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner)). The post also says he \"has sourced, led or co-led investments in over 25+ companies\" across AI, robotics, vertical software and defense (same src).\n- **Mercor relationship.** The promotion post says Brendan Foody at Mercor is someone with whom he's \"earned trust\", by showing up early with conviction and bonding over F1 (same src). The Mercor B post, co-signed with Sundeep Peechu, says \"we are leading Mercor's Series B\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-mercor), 2025-02-20).\n- **Agent-automation view (relevant to Serval-type companies).** \"The success of enterprises deploying AI agents won’t come from automating everything\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/ai-partition-function), 2025-08-06). Key intent \"lives in people’s heads, not spreadsheets or logs\" (same src).\n- **Anti-aggregation view (2026).** \"The more you scale trust, the more it dilutes.\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-great-splintering), 2026-04-13). The essay names Mercor, Paraform and Anthropic.\n- **Early agent call.** In Aug 2024 he wrote that \"browsing the web could become as archaic as renting a DVD\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-agentic-web), 2024-08-14). The essay maps Sierra, Decagon, Poolside, Cognition, Factory, Browserbase, Modal and others.\n- **Solcoa was a handshake in the lab.** The team \"shook hands to back them before we even left the lab\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-magnetic-moment-solcoa-investment), 2026-09-24). Felicis co-led the pre-seed and is in the seed (same src). Solcoa makes rare-earth magnets, so it is not an AI deal.\n- **Byline caveat.** Felicis shows his current title (\"General Partner\") on posts from 2023-2024, such as Predibase and Flower. Old bylines therefore do not prove what title he held then.\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-10)\nNew points from his panel, his Felicis Q&A and his 2023-2024 essays. Rows are in `data/rows/james-detweiler-voice-insights.csv`.\n- **Check range, stated on stage.** Felicis writes \"$40 million checks into seed through series B stage startups\", with a floor of $1M ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aCoJYfRTkM4), 0:11, 2024-05-29).\n- **Title ladder.** In May 2024 he called himself \"a partner at Felicis\", while the video description bills him as \"Deal Partner\" (same src). His promotion to Partner was reported on 2025-04-09 ([src](https://vcwire.tech/2025/04/09/felicis-promotes-james-detweiler-to-partner/)) and announced in a Felicis X post ([src](https://x.com/felicis/status/1909649369131053505)), and GP followed in Feb 2026 ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner)). That is three titles in about two years.\n- **SVB record in his words.** He made 10 startup investments and 9 fund investments at SVB Capital. His best exit was Volterra, sold to F5 for $500M. From the LP seat he noted that Felicis adds 1% on top of each check for founder development ([src](https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler)).\n- **He moved early on AI.** He left SVB in 2020 to \"stake my career on AI\" at Zetta, and says he wrote about GPT-3 there in 2021 (that writing was not found). In his first Felicis month, ChatGPT launched and Felicis led Runway's Series C. He pitched and set up Felicis Fellows, which gave $200K to four AI researchers (same src).\n- **His agent call is a year older than we had it.** \"The AI Agent Abstraction\" (2023-04-21) predicted that agents were coming. It also warned that agent claims \"should be taken with a fistful of salt\" unless five things are met: privacy, on-device inference, agent-to-agent interaction, memory and quality controls ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/ai-agent-abstraction)).\n- **2023 view on compute and labs.** Before Series A, startups should rent foundation-model APIs and not train their own models, unless they have proprietary data or distribution. Owning GPUs is an edge for labs like OpenAI and Mistral. He doubted GPU-rental margins, citing CoreWeave ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/a-shift-in-ml-deployment), 2023-08-07).\n- **How deals start.** On Predibase, \"We RSVP’d on a whim\" to its first community meetup in late 2022, and Felicis then led the round ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/predibase)). On Cambridge Terahertz, a portfolio CTO's reference (\"Nathan is the best hardware person I know\") is quoted. Felicis led the seed alongside Amazon and Tishman Speyer ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-cambridge-terahertz), 2025-07-24).\n- **Recurring screens:**\n  - An open-source community is a prerequisite for winning in ML infra (Predibase, Flower, Zylon).\n  - Physicists port well into ML ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/minecraft-physics-ml)).\n  - Capital is \"potential energy\": he backs founders whose traction exceeds the capital they absorb ([src](https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler)).\n- **A view that changed.** In May 2024 he said AI automation of regulated, face-to-face work such as healthcare and teaching was not yet open ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aCoJYfRTkM4), 13:36). By Jun 2025 he was writing a bullish essay on healthcare-authorization AI ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/healthcare-authorization-ai)).\n- **Robotics timing.** On the May 2024 panel he predicted a mainstream home robot that does more than vacuum, \"maybe like folding your laundry\" (same YouTube src, 19:32). That was six weeks before the Skild post.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.**\n- **For:**\n  - Named on Periodic Labs, the $300M seed into a new lab, where Felicis wrote the first check.\n  - Co-author on Ricursive Intelligence, an AI-for-chip-design lab.\n  - Sourced Skild AI, a robotics foundation model.\n  - Writes on agent automation (the partition function).\n  - Can sit on the other side of large seed or Series A rounds: Felicis led a $100M Series B (Mercor).\n  - Newly promoted GP, which is exactly the alpha profile the owner wants.\n- **Against:** on the lab deals he is a second name, not the originator. Deng originated Periodic. Delcambre held the Mirhoseini relationship on Ricursive. Sequoia and Lightspeed were ahead of Felicis on Ricursive.\n- **Deals that matter to the owner:** Periodic Labs, Ricursive, Skild, Mercor, Predibase, Flower, Moondream.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Santa Clara and spent a few childhood years in Florence ([src](https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler)).\n- Dartmouth College, summa cum laude, physics and mathematical finance ([src](https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler)). Graduation year not found.\n- Investor at Zetta Venture Partners, which the team page calls \"the first AI-focused venture fund\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler)). Dates not found.\n- Investor at SVB Capital; Board Observer at Shield AI; LP in Felicis ([src](https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler)). Dates not found.\n- Joined Felicis in 2022 \"to build out our AI portfolio\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler)). His entry title of VP comes from MarketScreener (aggregator) and was not re-verified in this run.\n- Promoted to General Partner on 2026-02-12, announced alongside Eric Flaningam (to Partner) and Michael Chen (Venture Partner) ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler | team page; lists email james@felicis.com in the promotion post |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.felicis.com/blog/archive | ~18 posts authored or co-authored, 2023-2026 |\n| X | https://x.com/detweilerjames | linked from team page; not read in this run |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/detweiler/ | linked from team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- **Tech.eu Summit London, 2024-05-29.** He moderated \"Privacy, Design, and Defensibility in the AI Era\" with Zylon, Robin AI and Flower Labs ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aCoJYfRTkM4)). Mined on 2026-11-10; see the section below.\n- No podcast guest spots were found (checked 2026-11-10 with web search and a YouTube search). X (x.com/detweilerjames) could not be read because of rate limits.\n\n## How they think\n- **\"Root node\" problems.** His stated mandate covers foundational infrastructure, such as robotics models, AI-for-chips and AI labor markets ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner)).\n- **Scale as the robotics answer.** On Skild: \"The answer lies with scale.\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-skild))\n- **Partition, not full automation.** The human/agent boundary should adapt to context ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/ai-partition-function)).\n- **Trust resists aggregation.** Software is no longer the moat, and value moves to trust and human presence ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-great-splintering)). This is contrarian against the \"AI favors incumbents and aggregators\" view.\n- **Interdisciplinary sourcing.** He writes that new inventions \"tend to sprout at the intersection of disciplines\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/minecraft-physics-ml), 2024-04-08). The same essay names Altera and Groundlight.\n- **Calls that look right.** He wrote \"The Agentic Web\" (2024-08) before the 2025 agent wave. He brought Skild in at Series A; Felicis now says it is valued at $14B. He co-signed Mercor's B (2025-02), and Mercor later scaled sharply (see `data/rows/roundmap-mercor-rounds.csv`).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [A step-change on the path to AGI (Ricursive)](https://www.felicis.com/blog/ricursive-intelligence-a-step-change-on-the-path-to-agi): how he and Delcambre sourced an AI-for-chips lab.\n2. [The AI Partition Function](https://www.felicis.com/blog/ai-partition-function): his agent-automation framework.\n3. [The Great Splintering](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-great-splintering): his 2026 worldview on where value goes.\n4. [The Agentic Web](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-agentic-web): his 2024 market map of agents.\n5. [Investing in Skild](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-skild): the robotics foundation-model bet.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked. X was not readable and search was unavailable in this run.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Periodic Labs | Seed ($300M) | 2025-09-30 | no (a16z led per repo record; Felicis wrote first check, originated by Peter Deng) | a16z; DST; NVentures; Accel | [src](https://felicis.com/blog/periodic-labs-investment) |\n| Ricursive Intelligence | Series A ($300M at $4B) | 2026-02-10 (post); round Jan 2026 | no (Lightspeed led per TechCrunch) | Lightspeed; Nvidia; Sequoia led prior $35M seed | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/ricursive-intelligence-a-step-change-on-the-path-to-agi), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/16/how-ricursive-intelligence-raised-335m-at-a-4b-valuation-in-4-months/) |\n| Skild AI | Series A | 2024-07-09 | not stated | not named in post; Skild's site logos include Lightspeed, Coatue, SoftBank, Sequoia, GC, Menlo, Bezos (round unspecified, per repo CRV row) | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-skild) |\n| Mercor | Series B ($100M at $2B) | 2025-02-20 | yes | Benchmark; General Catalyst; DST Global; Menlo (per repo Mercor row) | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-mercor) |\n| Predibase | Series A | 2023-05-31 | yes | not named | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/predibase) |\n| Flower Labs | Series A ($15M Felicis investment) | 2024-02-15 | not stated | not named | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-flower) |\n| DeepJudge | Series A | 2025-11-04 | yes | not named | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/deepjudge) |\n| Extra (BuildForever) | Seed | 2026-04-21 | not stated | angels incl. Paul Buchheit, Fidji Simo, Guillermo Rauch | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/extra-launch-build-forever-seed-round) |\n| Singularity Defense | Series A | 2026-07-14 | co-lead | Khosla Ventures (co-lead) | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/air-defense-at-scale-our-investment-in-singularity-defense) |\n| Solcoa | Pre-seed (co-led) then Seed | 2026-09-24 (post) | pre-seed co-led; seed not stated | not named | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-magnetic-moment-solcoa-investment) |\n| Cambridge Terahertz | not found | not found | not found | co-authored with Sundeep Peechu | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-cambridge-terahertz) |\n| Moondream | not found | not found | not found | not found | [src](https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler) (featured list only) |\n| Tera AI | not found | not found | not found | not found | [src](https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler) (featured list only) |\n| Paraform, Operand, Zylon, Deeptrace, Third Dimension | not found | not found | not found | not found | [src](https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler) (featured list); Paraform and Operand also named in the promotion post |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Ricursive Intelligence.** Delcambre had known Azalia Mirhoseini for years, and Felicis sponsors her Stanford Scaling Intelligence Lab. Detweiler sat next to Anna Goldie at a research symposium ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/ricursive-intelligence-a-step-change-on-the-path-to-agi)). Timing: Sequoia led the $35M seed (about 2025-12) and Lightspeed led the $300M A in Jan 2026, about four months after launch ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/16/how-ricursive-intelligence-raised-335m-at-a-4b-valuation-in-4-months/)). Felicis came in at the A, so it was not first.\n- **Skild AI.** Detweiler brought the deal to Felicis at the Series A ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner)). He and Senkut visited Pittsburgh to see the robots ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-skild)). The promotion post says he worked with Deepak Pathak there. Timing: CRV partnered in May 2024 (per the repo's CRV row), before Felicis's July 2024 post, so Felicis was not first.\n- **Periodic Labs.** Peter Deng originated it, committing before incorporation. Detweiler is thanked \"for your partnership on our investment\" ([src](https://felicis.com/blog/periodic-labs-investment), 2025-09-30). His exact role is not stated.\n- **Mercor.** Felicis led the $100M B, co-signed by Peechu and Detweiler ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-mercor)). The promotion post credits Detweiler with trust-building with Brendan Foody. Who sourced it is not stated. Felicis was not first in, since the B followed earlier rounds.\n- **Solcoa.** Felicis committed in the lab on first visit ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-magnetic-moment-solcoa-investment)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Aydin Senkut | co-signer on Skild, Singularity; announced his GP promotion | 2024 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-skild) |\n| Michelle Delcambre | co-author on Ricursive | 2026 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/ricursive-intelligence-a-step-change-on-the-path-to-agi) |\n| Peter Deng | thanked Detweiler on Periodic; co-author on Extra | 2025 | [src](https://felicis.com/blog/periodic-labs-investment) |\n| Sundeep Peechu | co-author on Mercor, Cambridge Terahertz | 2025 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-mercor) |\n| Niki Pezeshki | co-author on Predibase, Flower | 2023 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/predibase) |\n| Viviana Faga | co-author on DeepJudge | 2025 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/deepjudge) |\n| Anna Goldie | met at research symposium; Ricursive founder | before 2026-02 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/ricursive-intelligence-a-step-change-on-the-path-to-agi) |\n| Brendan Foody | Mercor founder; trust relationship | by 2025 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner) |\n| Deepak Pathak | Skild founder; worked with in Pittsburgh | 2024 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner) |\n| Zetta Venture Partners | former employer | pre-2022 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler) |\n| SVB Capital | former employer; Felicis LP role there | pre-2022 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler) |\n| Shield AI | board observer (SVB Capital) | pre-2022 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler) |\n| Khosla Ventures | co-lead on Singularity Defense A | 2026 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/air-defense-at-scale-our-investment-in-singularity-defense) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What did he do on Periodic beyond partnership: did he run diligence, or does he hold the board or observer seat?\n- Moondream and Tera AI: round, date and whether Felicis led (no Felicis post found).\n- Skild Series A size, leads and Felicis check size (not confirmed this run; search was unavailable).\n- Dates at Zetta and SVB Capital, and his entry title at Felicis (VP is from an aggregator only).\n- X feed and any podcast appearances. Not checked; worth a follow-up run.\n- Did his SVB Capital LP-side relationship with Felicis lead to his hire?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.felicis.com/team/james-detweiler\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/ricursive-intelligence-a-step-change-on-the-path-to-agi\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/16/how-ricursive-intelligence-raised-335m-at-a-4b-valuation-in-4-months/\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-skild\n- https://felicis.com/blog/periodic-labs-investment\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-mercor\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/predibase\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-flower\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/deepjudge\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-great-splintering\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/ai-partition-function\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-agentic-web\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/avatars-the-next-interface-is-a-face\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/a-shift-in-ml-deployment\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/minecraft-physics-ml\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/healthcare-authorization-ai\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-magnetic-moment-solcoa-investment\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/air-defense-at-scale-our-investment-in-singularity-defense\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/extra-launch-build-forever-seed-round\n- Repo rows: data/rows/periodic-labs-deals.csv, data/rows/mercor-deals.csv, data/rows/roundmap-mercor-rounds.csv (Mercor co-investors, Periodic lead), brian-zhan rows (CRV in Skild May 2024)\n", "person:james-flynn": "\n# James Flynn\n\n## One-line take\nA growth-trained Sequoia partner (ex-General Atlantic) who keeps showing up as the second signer on Sequoia's newest AI deals, including the seed lead of Corma, a self-described frontier lab for cyber defense, so they are a good read on which young AI companies Sequoia is actually pricing.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win.** They sit at the join between Sequoia's seed and growth benches. They co-sign with Sonya Huang on generative media and dev tools (fal, Zed, Preview), with Shaun Maguire on Corma and Neros, with Pat Grady on OpenEvidence, Rogo and Chai, and with Anas Biad on Mecka ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/james-flynn/)).\n- **Their filter.** They say they are \"more focused on the people and story than any given sector\", and that change comes from \"a very small number of companies\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/james-flynn/)).\n- **What constrains them.** They are never the sole public name on a frontier-lab deal. On Corma they are one of four authors, behind Maguire and Huang, with Carl Eschenbach also signing ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-corma-closing-the-defensive-cybersecurity-gap/)). They have no podcasts or long-form writing of their own beyond co-authored partnership posts (none found).\n- **What they would not do.** Nothing sourced.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Sequoia files 2025-founded Mecka under a growth entry.** Mecka was founded in 2025, and Sequoia's table on their page tags the 2026 entry as \"Growth\". That suggests a large first cheque into a very young physical-AI data company, not a classic seed ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/james-flynn/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/mecka/)). The Mecka post lists strategic backers NVIDIA, Samsung, Microsoft and Qualcomm, plus ex-Tesla Optimus head Milan Kovac ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mecka/)).\n- **The Mecka thesis (Biad and Flynn).** \"The brain needs data that wasn't collected.\" In their view, physical AGI needs both data capture and deployment on the ground ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mecka/)).\n- **Corma is pitched as a frontier lab.** It is \"the first frontier AI lab for defensive cybersecurity\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/corma/)). Sequoia says it is \"thrilled to lead Corma's seed round\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-corma-closing-the-defensive-cybersecurity-gap/)). The RL framing: \"Cybersecurity, like Go or chess, is a two-player, zero-sum game\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-corma-closing-the-defensive-cybersecurity-gap/)).\n- **fal as a compute bet.** \"We are at the beginning of a compute explosion in generative video.\" Huang and Flynn wrote this when Sequoia led fal's Series D ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-fal-the-generative-media-company/)).\n- **The Preview thesis.** \"the models are now expressive enough to become the new camera\". Another line: \"Value will accrue to where the work actually happens\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-preview-lights-inference-action/)).\n- **Their personal operating rule.** \"We live in a probabilistic world.\" They also describe squash as having instilled \"a hatred of losing\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/james-flynn/)).\n- **Zed timing quirk.** Sequoia's Zed page dates the partnership to 2024 (entry tagged Growth). The Series B that Sequoia led was announced 2025-08-20 ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/zed/), [src](https://zed.dev/blog/sequoia-backs-zed)). So the relationship predates the public lead by up to about a year, but the 2024 transaction was not found.\n- **ElevenLabs timing quirk.** Sequoia's page says it partnered in 2023, but the first public round naming Sequoia is the 2024-01-22 Series B ($80M, co-led by a16z, Nat Friedman and Daniel Gross), where Sequoia only participated ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/elevenlabs/), [src](https://elevenlabs.io/blog/series-b)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **Why it matters to me.** They are a named Sequoia signer on Corma (frontier-lab seed, Sequoia led), Preview (AI-video seed, Sequoia led), Mecka (physical-AI data) and fal (inference and compute for generative media). These are useful co-investor signals.\n- **Why not high.** Most of their book is growth entries (Anthropic 2026, Chai Series C, ElevenLabs, Rogo, fal Series D). On the frontier-lab seed they are the junior name next to Maguire and Huang. For a $20M check into a big lab seed, the owner of the deal at Sequoia is more likely Maguire or Huang. Flynn is the person who carries it into later rounds.\n\n## Background\n- **Education.** Penn, with Wharton mentioned; degree not stated. They played squash and was on the team at Penn ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/james-flynn/)).\n- **Before Sequoia.** General Atlantic, where they saw \"scaling tech companies up close\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/james-flynn/)). Dates not found.\n- **At Sequoia.** Join date not found. Their earliest dated personal partnership on the page is Neros, a seed-stage defense drone company, in 2023. They are also listed on older relationships (Clay 2019, Neuralink 2021, OpenAI 2021, Zipline 2012) that likely predate them, which the page does not separate ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/james-flynn/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/james-flynn/ | Firm profile; jflynn@sequoiacap.com listed |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/jamescflynn1 | Linked from firm page; could not be fetched (402), follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/james-flynn-964231191 | Linked from firm page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. The web search budget ran out this run, so no podcast search was possible. The ElevenLabs page lists a Sequoia podcast with Mati Staniszewski hosted by Pat Grady, not Flynn ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/elevenlabs/)).\n\n## How they think\n- **Outlier power law.** \"there's this extreme outlier dynamic\". They are drawn to founders with \"hunger and audacity\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/james-flynn/)).\n- **People over sector.** They have spent time across AI, fintech, consumer and hardtech ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/james-flynn/)).\n- **Infrastructure and data layers in AI.** Their bets favour the bottleneck layers:\n  - Compute for generative video (fal)\n  - Training data for robotics (Mecka)\n  - The workflow layer where value accrues (Preview)\n  - Defence-side scaling laws (Corma)\n- **Calls right or wrong.** Too early to judge from sourced marks. fal tripled its valuation into the Series D, per the round coverage recorded in this repo ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-fal-the-generative-media-company/)).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Partnering with Corma](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-corma-closing-the-defensive-cybersecurity-gap/): frontier-lab seed logic for cyber defense.\n- [Partnering with Mecka](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mecka/): the physical-AI data thesis, co-written with Anas Biad.\n- [Partnering with fal](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-fal-the-generative-media-company/): the generative-media compute thesis.\n- [Partnering with Preview](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-preview-lights-inference-action/): the AI video workflow seed.\n- [Their firm profile](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/james-flynn/): their personal operating rules.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed: X could not be fetched and the search budget was exhausted.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI deals they are named on. The Sequoia co-signers listed come from Sequoia company pages and articles.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Corma (frontier AI lab, cyber defense) | Seed (size not disclosed) | 2026-08-10 | Yes, Sequoia led | Sequoia: Shaun Maguire, Sonya Huang, Carl Eschenbach; outside investors not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-corma-closing-the-defensive-cybersecurity-gap/) |\n| Preview (AI video production) | Seed (size not disclosed) | 2026-08-12 | Yes, Sequoia led | Sequoia: Sonya Huang; outside investors not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-preview-lights-inference-action/) |\n| Mecka (physical-AI data and deployment) | Round not disclosed; Sequoia tags entry \"Growth\" | 2026-10-07 | Unknown | Sequoia: Anas Biad; NVIDIA, Samsung, Microsoft, Qualcomm; Tony Xu, Frank Slootman, Milan Kovac, Christopher Payne, Jonathan Chadwick | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mecka/) |\n| Chai Discovery (AI drug design) | Series C ($400M at $3.8B) | 2026-07-14 | No, Index led | Sequoia: Pat Grady, Sonali Singh | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/chai-discovery/), [src](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design) |\n| Anthropic | Series G participant (2026-02), then Series H co-lead (2026-05-28) | 2026 | Sequoia co-led the Series H (Alfred Lin named) | Sequoia: Alfred Lin, Lauren Reeder, Sonya Huang | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/anthropic/), [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h) |\n| fal | Series D ($140M at $4.5B) | 2025-12-09 | Yes, Sequoia led | Sequoia: Sonya Huang; Kleiner Perkins, NVentures, Alkeon, a16z, Bessemer | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-fal-the-generative-media-company/) |\n| Zed (AI code editor) | Series B ($32M); Sequoia page says partnered 2024 | 2025-08-20 | Yes, Sequoia led | Sequoia: Sonya Huang; existing investors not named | [src](https://zed.dev/blog/sequoia-backs-zed), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/zed/) |\n| Rogo (AI for finance) | Entry tagged Growth | 2025 | Unknown | Sequoia: Pat Grady, Brian Halligan | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/rogo/) |\n| OpenEvidence (AI for doctors) | Entry tagged Early; Sequoia funding at a $1B valuation reported Feb 2025 (CNBC, linked from Sequoia page) | 2024 (partnered) | Lead per Sequoia's own \"Partnering with\" post by Pat Grady; round terms not fetched | Sequoia: Pat Grady | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/openevidence/) |\n| ElevenLabs | Sequoia page says partnered 2023 (Growth); first public round naming Sequoia is the Series B ($80M) | 2024-01-22 | No (a16z, Nat Friedman, Daniel Gross co-led) | Sequoia: Andrew Reed | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/elevenlabs/), [src](https://elevenlabs.io/blog/series-b) |\n| OpenAI | 2021 share sale (exact transaction not identified) | 2021 | No | Sequoia: Alfred Lin, Pat Grady, Sonya Huang; likely predates Flynn | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/companies/openai/) |\n| Neros (defense drones, adjacent) | Pre-Seed/Seed | 2023 | Unknown | Sequoia: Shaun Maguire | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/neros/) |\n\n**Timing: was Sequoia first?**\n- **Probably first institutional money:**\n  - Corma seed, where Sequoia led and no other investor is named ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-corma-closing-the-defensive-cybersecurity-gap/)).\n  - Preview seed, same ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-preview-lights-inference-action/)).\n- **Late, as a growth entrant:**\n  - Chai: Thrive was in at the 2024 seed, about two years before Sequoia ([src](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design)).\n  - fal: Series D lead.\n  - Zed: Series B lead, after earlier backers.\n  - ElevenLabs: Series B participant.\n  - Anthropic: Series G and H.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Corma.** Sequoia calls founder Alon Pluda \"one of the most elite hackers in the world\". It led the seed with a four-partner byline that spans Sequoia's seed and growth benches, with Carl Eschenbach as an operator signer. The intro path is not stated ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-corma-closing-the-defensive-cybersecurity-gap/)).\n- **Preview.** The founders, Stefan Fejes and Veljko Tornjanski, are childhood friends from Serbia who were on Veed's founding team. At Vizcom, Tornjanski was the first hire and became Head of AI, and Fejes led product. Sequoia led the seed. The intro path is not stated, but the Veed and Vizcom lineage is the visible route ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-preview-lights-inference-action/)).\n- **Mecka.** Founded by Josh Gao and Jason Chong in 2025. Sequoia's announcement came alongside strategic corporates (NVIDIA, Samsung, Microsoft, Qualcomm) and operator angels ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mecka/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/mecka/)).\n- **How they source.** No direct statement found. The pattern is that they co-sign with a domain partner who owns the relationship:\n  - Huang for generative media and dev tools\n  - Maguire for security and defense\n  - Grady for applied AI\n  - Biad for physical AI\n- **Lab and university links.** None sourced.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sonya Huang (Sequoia) | Co-signer on fal, Zed, Preview, Corma, Anthropic | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/james-flynn/) |\n| Shaun Maguire (Sequoia) | Co-signer on Corma, Neros | 2023 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/neros/) |\n| Pat Grady (Sequoia) | Co-signer on OpenEvidence, Rogo, Chai Discovery | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/openevidence/) |\n| Anas Biad (Sequoia) | Co-author on Mecka | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mecka/) |\n| Carl Eschenbach (Sequoia) | Co-author on Corma | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-corma-closing-the-defensive-cybersecurity-gap/) |\n| Andrew Reed (Sequoia) | Co-signer on ElevenLabs | 2023 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/elevenlabs/) |\n| Alfred Lin, Lauren Reeder (Sequoia) | Co-signers on Anthropic | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/anthropic/) |\n| Sonali Singh (Sequoia) | Co-signer on Chai Discovery | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/chai-discovery/) |\n| Luciana Lixandru, George Robson (Sequoia) | Co-authors on Aspora (fintech) | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/james-flynn/) |\n| Brian Halligan (Sequoia) | Co-signer on Rogo | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/rogo/) |\n| Alon Pluda (Corma) | Portfolio founder | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/corma/) |\n| Josh Gao, Jason Chong (Mecka) | Portfolio founders | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/mecka/) |\n| General Atlantic | Former employer | dates not found | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/james-flynn/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Corma seed size, other investors, and the team's lab pedigree. Is it in the $100M+ \"lab seed\" range?\n- Mecka round size, and why Sequoia tags a 2025-founded company's entry as Growth.\n- When did they join Sequoia, and when were they promoted from \"Investor (Core)\" to Partner?\n- What were Sequoia's 2023 ElevenLabs and 2024 Zed entry transactions, before the public rounds?\n- Do they have podcast appearances or an active X feed? Search budget was exhausted this run, so a follow-up run is needed.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/james-flynn/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/corma/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-corma-closing-the-defensive-cybersecurity-gap/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/preview/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-preview-lights-inference-action/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/mecka/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mecka/\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-fal-the-generative-media-company/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/fal/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/zed/\n- https://zed.dev/blog/sequoia-backs-zed\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/elevenlabs/\n- https://elevenlabs.io/blog/series-b\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/openevidence/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/rogo/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/anthropic/\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/chai-discovery/\n- https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design\n- https://sequoiacap.com/companies/openai/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/neros/\n", "person:james-kaplan": "\n# James Kaplan\n\n## One-line take\nA former founder made NEA Principal in 2026-06 after three years. He works on more of NEA's AI-application book than any other junior: Perplexity, ElevenLabs, Samaya, Ceramic, Genmo, August, Aurelian ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** Rick Yang's promotion quote: \"having founded a startup himself, he has deep empathy for founders\" ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors)). Every NEA early-stage AI-app lead from 2024 to 2025 lists him.\n- **Constraint:** invisible on NEA's site. He has no team page (nea.com/team/james-kaplan returns 404) and the portfolio CMS lists him on no company, so his role in each round can't be read from NEA's data (checked 2026-11-11).\n- **What he'd never do (by record):** nothing sourced.\n\n## Differential insight\n- His stated beat is wider than \"AI apps\": \"a sharp focus on AI-native applications across vertical software, gaming, and entertainment\" (same release). Gaming and entertainment are new for NEA's AI book, and Fenris Creations (games) is on his list.\n- **Timing:** he joined in 2023, just after NEA led Perplexity's Series A (2023-03-28) ([src](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/announcing-our-series-a-funding-round-and-mobile-app-launch)), and now works on Perplexity. He came in to cover a position NEA already held, not to source it.\n- **Unconfirmed:** NEA says he \"founded a consumer mental health startup\" but doesn't name it. An aggregator profile ties a \"James K\" to Trill Project (Gen Z peer-support app, acquired by Blue Fever in 2021) ([aggregator](https://village.ai/person/james-k-MTMzODE3NDc)). That is not enough to confirm.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. He isn't on lab deals, but he is the junior most likely to know NEA's app-layer AI pipeline and to be first contact for vertical AI A rounds.\n\n## Background\n- University of Southern California ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors)).\n- Credit Suisse; GlossGenius; founded a consumer mental-health startup (name unconfirmed) (same source).\n- NEA: joined 2023 (Menlo Park); Senior Associate 2025-06 ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/new-enterprise-associates-announces-promotion-of-madison-and-mustafa-to-partner-1)); Principal 2026-06-29.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | none (no NEA team page as of 2026-11-11) | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found (no NEA post authored) | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not confirmed | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Search results for his name were dominated by the jazz author James Kaplan.\n\n## How they think\nNothing in his own words found. The NEA release is the only source.\n\n## Read these first\n- NEA promotion release (2026-06-29) ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors)).\n- Aurelian Series A post, the deal where NEA's page names no partner but he is listed as working on it ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/aurelian-ai-agents-powering-the-911-call-centers-of-the-future)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nNew rows in `data/rows/james-kaplan-deals.csv`. NEA's release says he \"works closely with\" these companies; his personal role in each round is unconfirmed.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ElevenLabs | Series C | 2025-01-30 | no | a16z, ICONIQ | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/elevenlabs-ais-natural-interface) |\n| Genmo | Series A ($30M) | 2024-10-22 | NEA lead | | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/genmos-open-source-genai-model-aims-to-power-the-future-of-video) |\n| Ceramic | Seed | 2025-03-05 | NEA lead | IBM, Samsung Next | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/ceramic-infrastructure-for-every-ai-native-company) |\n| Samaya AI | Undisclosed ($43.5M) | 2025-05-14 | NEA lead | | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/samaya-ai-supercharging-financial-services) |\n| August | Seed ($7M) | 2025-08-05 | NEA co-lead | Pear VC | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/august-high-impact-configurable-legal-ai-for-midsize-firms) |\n| Aurelian | Series A ($14M) | 2025-08-27 | NEA lead | | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/aurelian-ai-agents-powering-the-911-call-centers-of-the-future) |\n| Perplexity, Echodyne, Fenris Creations, Kira | covered | n/a | n/a | | [src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing sourced on how any of these deals came to him.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| New Enterprise Associates | at: Principal | 2023 | [src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors) |\n| GlossGenius | at: role unconfirmed | before 2023 | same |\n| Credit Suisse | at: role unconfirmed | before 2023 | same |\n| Rick Yang | promoted him (Head of Technology) | 2026 | same |\n\n## Open questions\n- Name of his mental-health startup (Trill Project is a lead, not confirmed).\n- Which of the 2025 AI-app leads did he source?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors\n- https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/new-enterprise-associates-announces-promotion-of-madison-and-mustafa-to-partner-1\n- https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/announcing-our-series-a-funding-round-and-mobile-app-launch\n- https://village.ai/person/james-k-MTMzODE3NDc (aggregator, unconfirmed)\n", "person:jan-hammer": "\n# Jan Hammer\n\n## One-line take\nA fintech partner who is quietly Index's person on two frontier labs. Index is on Mistral's first seed round (Hammer is one of two Index people named on the company page). Hammer also holds the Harmonic observer seat, a lab co-founded by Vlad Tenev, whose Robinhood board Hammer sat on for about eight years.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win.** Long, trust-heavy founder relationships that compound over a decade. Robinhood ran from a $500K seed in 2013 to a board seat until 2021. Those relationships turn into the next deal:\n  - Tenev's next company is Harmonic.\n  - The Alan portfolio visit is how Index met Mistral.\n  - Rohlik founder Tomas Cupr's next company is Duvo.\n  - Hammer also has a dense Czech and CEE network: Resistant AI, Rohlik, Productboard, Credo Ventures and Daniel Dines.\n- **Constraints.**\n  - They are not a lab-sourcing specialist. On both frontier labs they followed someone else's lead: Lightspeed on Mistral and Sequoia on Harmonic.\n  - In mid-2024 they were reported as sceptical that new model companies could take on incumbents directly.\n- **What they'd never do.** The record suggests they won't seek a lab cold, outside a founder relationship. Their frontier-AI exposure comes through people they already backed.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Mistral path ran through Hammer's own portfolio company.** Julia Andre writes that \"my colleague Jan Hammer and I were visiting the Paris HQ of Alan\" when they first came across Mistral [src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise) (2025-09-09). Hammer wrote Index's Alan posts and led its 2026 round ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/our-continued-investment-in-alan/)). So the Mistral intro came from a Hammer relationship.\n- **Index calls its Mistral check the first seed check.** Andre wrote \"after cutting that first seed check for Mistral\" [src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise). Lightspeed nonetheless led the June 2023 seed, and Index participated [src](https://siliconangle.com/2023/06/13/paris-based-startup-mistral-ai-raises-113m-seed-funding/). \"First\" most likely means Index's first check, not first in the cap table.\n- **The Harmonic seat is the Robinhood relationship carried over.** This is confirmed by Harmonic's own release: \"Index Ventures partner Jan Hammer, who will join as an observer.\" [src](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/) (2024-09-23). Tenev co-founded Harmonic, and Hammer sat on the Robinhood board alongside Tenev from at least 2016 to 2021 [src](https://robinhood.com/us/en/newsroom/neas-scott-sandell-joins-robinhoods-board/). The link between the two is my inference; no source states the intro path.\n- **Robinhood story.** Index says Hammer was introduced to Tenev and Bhatt through General Atlantic connections, ran a Google Hangouts call, and then negotiated the $13M Series A in spring 2014. Hammer's own summary of the pitch: \"The vision was, essentially, stocks on mobile for millennials\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/trade-winds-the-rise-reckoning-and-reimagining-of-vlad-tenev/) (2025-10-01).\n- **They left the Robinhood board to go back to early stage.** Robinhood said Hammer left the board \"as he continues his focus on investing in early-stage companies and founders\" [src](https://robinhood.com/us/en/newsroom/robinhood-markets-welcomes-frances-frei-to-its-board-of-directors/) (2021-11-16). The AI deals came after that move.\n- **Model-layer scepticism, 2024 (aggregator).** Eulerpool's write-up of the July 2024 fund-raise quotes Hammer as saying \"we don't see any new model companies competing on their own turf\". It also quotes them calling the model layer \"a pretty capital-intensive game\". This is a year after the Mistral seed. The quotes were originally given to the FT; I only have the aggregator copy [src](https://eulerpool.com/en/news/ai/index-ventures-bets-on-ai-23-billion-us-dollars).\n- **Reference checks through board peers.** Before leading Moment, Hammer had Moment's CEO speak with Lee Olesky, who sat with Hammer on the Credit Benchmark board. Olesky's verdict: \"These guys are the real deal. They're onto something special.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/moments-moment-investing-in-the-future-of-fixed-income/) (2025-07-09).\n- **Repeat founders from their own book.** For Duvo, Hammer and Hasslinger wrote \"They're operators who lived the pain firsthand.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/duvo-raises-15m-to-give-retail-teams-an-ai-workforce-that-goes-live-in-weeks-cutting-manual-work-by-40/). Index backed Cupr's Rohlik before Duvo.\n- **AI security call, 2020.** At the Resistant AI seed, Hammer said: \"As machine learning takes more and more nuanced financial decisions, it needs to be protected.\" [src](https://tech.eu/2020/04/30/resistant-ai-funding/) (2020-04-30).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.**\n- They are on two frontier labs:\n  - Mistral, the June 2023 seed.\n  - Harmonic, with the observer seat at Series A, plus participation in the Series B and Series C.\n- They lead AI-native automation rounds: Duvo's $15M seed and Moment's $36M Series B and $78M Series C.\n- They can anchor $10M+ checks.\n- The most useful thing for me is the sourcing pattern. Hammer's frontier-AI deals come from the next ventures of long-time portfolio founders (Tenev, Cupr) and from portfolio-office visits (Alan to Mistral). So the way to reach them is through the founders they've already backed.\n- Their reported 2024 scepticism about new model labs suggests they would co-invest in a lab with a lead rather than lead one themselves.\n\n## Background\n- Graduated from Oxford University, then an MBA with Distinction from INSEAD [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/jan-hammer/).\n- Investment banker at Morgan Stanley, then investor at General Atlantic, then joined Index in 2010 [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/jan-hammer/).\n- Czech. CzechCrunch calls them one of the most influential Czechs in global venture [src](https://cc.cz/podporil-rohlik-i-productboard-ted-ma-hvezdny-fond-pro-startupy-pripravene-dalsi-miliardy/) (2022-11-17).\n- Works with Tate in London on its Central and Eastern European art collection [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/jan-hammer/).\n- Ranked #1 on the Forbes Midas List Europe for four straight years, ending in 2022 when Danny Rimer took the top spot [src](https://forbes.it/2022/12/12/midas-list-europe-migliori-venture-capitalist-europa/). Ranked #7 in Europe in 2025 [src](https://forbes.it/2025/12/23/midas-lit-europe-migliori-investitori-continente). These rankings come from search summaries of the Forbes pages, not fetched directly.\n- Past boards: Robinhood (about 2013 to 2021) and Adyen (per the Moment Series B coverage summary [src](https://www.fintechfutures.com/venture-capital-funding/fixed-income-fintech-moment-raises-36m-series-b)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/jan-hammer/ | Lists 37 portfolio companies (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/team-member/jan-hammer | Index Perspectives, 11 pages; writes deal posts (Moment, Alan, Nomos, Duvo) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/intent/user?screen_name=janatindex | @janatindex; not fetched, follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/hammerjan/ | not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2018-11-02: Sunday Times \"Danny in the Valley\", \"Index Ventures' Danny Rimer and Jan Hammer: Quinoa-bots and sneaker heads\". Covers scooters, insurance, the end of cash and SoftBank. No transcript found [src](https://shows.acast.com/dannyinthevalley/episodes/indexventures).\n- 2017: TechCrunch video with Martin Mignot, \"Index Ventures spells out the ABCs of investing\". Not watched [src](https://techcrunch.com/video/index-ventures-spells-out-the-abcs-of-investing).\n- 2024-07: Comments to the FT on Index's $2.3B fund-raise, calling AI a \"platform shift\" [src](https://www.pymnts.com/?p=1973909).\n- No AI-focused podcast found.\n\n## How they think\n- **AI as a platform shift.** In 2024 they compared AI to PCs, smartphones and the cloud [src](https://www.pymnts.com/?p=1973909).\n- **Model layer is capital-intensive.** They doubt new model companies can take incumbents head-on, though specialised niches could work. Source is an aggregator: [src](https://eulerpool.com/en/news/ai/index-ventures-bets-on-ai-23-billion-us-dollars). Harmonic, a maths-specialist lab, fits that \"niche\" framing.\n- **Macro doesn't matter for exceptional founders.** Their 2022 quote in Czech, \"Výjimeční podnikatelé uspějí bez ohledu na makroekonomiku\", means \"Exceptional entrepreneurs succeed regardless of macroeconomics\" [src](https://cc.cz/podporil-rohlik-i-productboard-ted-ma-hvezdny-fond-pro-startupy-pripravene-dalsi-miliardy/).\n- **Patience on timing.** On Moment: \"Sometimes it takes a decade for the right idea, the right market, and the right people to come together\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/moments-moment-investing-in-the-future-of-fixed-income/).\n- **Calls right:** Robinhood ($500K seed, a stake worth more than $3B at the IPO), Adyen, Wise, Mistral.\n- **Calls that may be wrong:** the 2024 model-layer scepticism, given Mistral's later €11.7B Series C, which Index joined.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Trade Winds: Vlad Tenev\" (Index, 2025-10-01). The Robinhood origin story and the Tenev relationship behind Harmonic. https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/trade-winds-the-rise-reckoning-and-reimagining-of-vlad-tenev/\n2. \"Moment's Moment\" (Hammer, 2025-07-09). Shows the board-peer reference-check method. https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/moments-moment-investing-in-the-future-of-fixed-income/\n3. \"Mistral AI for tomorrow's enterprise\" (Julia Andre, 2025-09-09). The Alan-office origin of Mistral. https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise\n4. Duvo post (Hammer and Hasslinger, 2025-12-02). The repeat-founder, AI-workforce thesis. https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/duvo-raises-15m-to-give-retail-teams-an-ai-workforce-that-goes-live-in-weeks-cutting-manual-work-by-40/\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X (@janatindex) was not reachable for this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mistral | Seed (€105M / $113M) | 2023-06-13 | No (Lightspeed led) | Redpoint, Xavier Niel, JCDecaux, Eric Schmidt, La Famiglia | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2023/06/13/paris-based-startup-mistral-ai-raises-113m-seed-funding/) |\n| Mistral | Series C (€1.7B, €11.7B post-money) | 2025-09-09 | No (ASML led); Index followed on | ASML | [src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise) |\n| Harmonic | Series A ($75M, $325M post-money) | 2024-09-23 | No (Sequoia led); Hammer is board observer | Sequoia (Andrew Reed, board), GreatPoint, Era Funds, Blossom, Elefund | [src](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/) |\n| Harmonic | Series B ($100M) | 2025-07-09 | No (Kleiner led) | Kleiner (Ilya Fushman, observer), Paradigm, Ribbit, Sequoia, Charlie Cheever | [src](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-b/) |\n| Harmonic | Series C ($120M, $1.45B) | 2025-11-25 | No (Ribbit led) | Sequoia, Kleiner, Emerson Collective | [src](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-c/) |\n| Duvo | Seed ($15M) | 2025-12-02 | Yes (Index) | Credo, Northzone, Puzzle; angels Roy Reznik, David Singleton | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=722991) |\n| Duvo | Series A (€28.55M / $32M) | 2026-10-14 | No (Balderton led); Index followed on | Northzone, Credo | [src](https://www.therecursive.com/rohlik-founder-tomas-cuprs-duvo-raises-eur28-7m-series-a-to-take-enterprise-ai-agents-to-the-us/) |\n| Moment | Series B ($36M) | 2025-07-09 | Yes (Index) | a16z, Lightspeed, Venrock, Neo, Contrary | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/moments-moment-investing-in-the-future-of-fixed-income/) |\n| Moment | Series C ($78M) | 2026-05-19 | Yes (Index) | not named | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/from-300b-to-10-trillion-how-moment-became-the-ai-operating-system-for-investment-management/) |\n| Resistant AI | Seed ($2.75M) | 2020-04-30 | Co-led (Index and Credo) | Credo, Seedcamp, Daniel Dines, Michal Pechoucek | [src](https://tech.eu/2020/04/30/resistant-ai-funding/) |\n| Resistant AI | Series A ($16.6M) | ~2021 (sources disagree) | No (GV led) | GV, Credo, Seedcamp | [src](https://dev.globalprivatecapital.org/newsroom/gv-leads-usd16-6m-series-a-for-czech-republics-resistant-ai/) |\n| Valdera | Series A ($15M) | 2024-10-03 | Index led; Hammer's role not stated | Susa, Lerer Hippeau, BoxGroup | [src](https://finsmes.com/?p=335259) |\n| Nomos | Early round (€20M) | ~2026-09 | Index led (Hasslinger lead partner; Hammer co-author) | Markus Villig, Daniel Dines, Tomas Okmanas | [src](https://techfundingnews.com/nomos-raises-20m-index-ventures-europe-energy-costs/) |\n| Alan | New round (~$6B valuation) | 2026-03-11 | Yes (Index) | not named | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/our-continued-investment-in-alan/) |\n| Robinhood (context) | Seed $500K, then Series A ($13M) | 2013-10, then 2014 | Index the largest seed investor; Hammer negotiated the Series A | GV (Blake Byers), NEA, Ribbit, DST later | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/trade-winds-the-rise-reckoning-and-reimagining-of-vlad-tenev/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Mistral (2023).** Hammer and Julia Andre were visiting the Paris HQ of Alan, Hammer's portfolio company, when they met Arthur Mensch. Mistral had \"barely been incorporated\" at the time [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/backing-mistral-from-first-check-to-europes-next-powerhouse/). Index then wrote a check into the June 2023 seed, which Lightspeed led.\n  - **Pairing:** Julia Andre is the public face and author. Hammer is the senior partner in the room and one of two Index names on the company page [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/mistral/).\n  - **Timing:** Index came in at the founding round alongside Lightspeed, about four weeks after incorporation [src](https://www.irishtimes.com/technology/2023/06/14/four-week-old-ai-start-up-raises-record-105m-in-european-push).\n- **Harmonic (2024).** Index came in at the Series A, behind Sequoia's lead, with \"significant participation\". Hammer took the observer seat [src](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/).\n  - Hammer is the only Index person on the company page [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/harmonic/).\n  - The Series B observer role comes from a search summary of the BusinessWire release (\"existing board observer Jan Hammer from Index\"). The harmonic.fun Series B page itself does not name Hammer. Treat this as medium confidence.\n  - I found no evidence of Index in the Harmonic seed.\n- **Robinhood (2013–14).** Introduced through General Atlantic contacts. Hammer took a Google Hangouts call with the founders, and Index wrote $500K in October 2013. Hammer then negotiated the $13M Series A in spring 2014 [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/trade-winds-the-rise-reckoning-and-reimagining-of-vlad-tenev/).\n- **Moment (2025).** Hammer started talks with CEO Dylan Parker early in 2025. They then ran a reference conversation with Lee Olesky before leading the $36M Series B [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/moments-moment-investing-in-the-future-of-fixed-income/). Board seat per press summaries [src](https://www.fintechfutures.com/venture-capital-funding/fixed-income-fintech-moment-raises-36m-series-b). Index led again with the $78M Series C 10 months later.\n- **Duvo (2025).** Index re-backed Rohlik founder Tomas Cupr.\n  - **Pairing:** Hammer (senior) with Bastian Hasslinger [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/duvo-raises-15m-to-give-retail-teams-an-ai-workforce-that-goes-live-in-weeks-cutting-manual-work-by-40/).\n  - Credo Ventures co-invested, as they did at Resistant AI. That makes two Hammer-era Czech AI deals done alongside Credo.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Vlad Tenev | Robinhood seed investor and board member; Harmonic board observer | 2013 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/trade-winds-the-rise-reckoning-and-reimagining-of-vlad-tenev/) |\n| Julia Andre (Index) | Co-sourced Mistral; tagged on the Moment Series C post | ~2023 | [src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise) |\n| Bastian Hasslinger (Index) | Junior partner on Duvo; co-author on Nomos | 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/duvo-raises-15m-to-give-retail-teams-an-ai-workforce-that-goes-live-in-weeks-cutting-manual-work-by-40/) |\n| Arthur Mensch (Mistral) | Met at Alan's Paris HQ | ~2023 | [src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise) |\n| Andrew Reed (Sequoia) | Harmonic board member alongside Hammer (observer) | 2024 | [src](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/) |\n| Tomas Cupr | Rohlik backer, then Duvo seed lead | pre-2022 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/duvo-raises-15m-to-give-retail-teams-an-ai-workforce-that-goes-live-in-weeks-cutting-manual-work-by-40/) |\n| Credo Ventures (Ondrej Bartos) | Co-led Resistant AI seed; co-invested in Duvo seed | 2020 | [src](https://tech.eu/2020/04/30/resistant-ai-funding/) |\n| Lee Olesky | Fellow Credit Benchmark board member; reference on Moment | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/moments-moment-investing-in-the-future-of-fixed-income/) |\n| Martin Rehak (Resistant AI) | Seed co-lead | 2020 | [src](https://tech.eu/2020/04/30/resistant-ai-funding/) |\n| Dylan Parker (Moment) | Series B/C lead; board | 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/moments-moment-investing-in-the-future-of-fixed-income/) |\n| Danny Rimer (Index) | Co-guest on the 2018 Sunday Times podcast | 2018 | [src](https://shows.acast.com/dannyinthevalley/episodes/indexventures) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did Hammer personally source Harmonic through Tenev before the Series A? Did Index see the seed? The intro path is my inference, not stated anywhere.\n- I need the primary BusinessWire text confirming Hammer's continuing observer seat in the July 2025 Series B (the page returned 403).\n- I need the original FT piece (2024-07-09, George Hammond and Tim Bradshaw) to confirm the model-layer quotes now attributed via Eulerpool.\n- Who held internal deal credit on Mistral: Andre or Hammer? Does either have a board or observer seat?\n- Is Hammer on the Valdera board? The release does not name them.\n- What does @janatindex post? X was not reached.\n- Is Hammer in Tenev's or Harmonic's next ventures, or in any other new lab spun from the Robinhood alumni network?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/jan-hammer/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/team-member/jan-hammer\n- https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/backing-mistral-from-first-check-to-europes-next-powerhouse/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/mistral/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/harmonic/\n- https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/\n- https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-b/\n- https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-c/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/trade-winds-the-rise-reckoning-and-reimagining-of-vlad-tenev/\n- https://robinhood.com/us/en/newsroom/robinhood-markets-welcomes-frances-frei-to-its-board-of-directors/\n- https://robinhood.com/us/en/newsroom/neas-scott-sandell-joins-robinhoods-board/\n- https://siliconvalleyinvestclub.com/2021/07/29/one-vcs-blind-faith-in-robinhood-turned-500000-into-3-billion/ (aggregator repost of Bloomberg)\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/moments-moment-investing-in-the-future-of-fixed-income/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/from-300b-to-10-trillion-how-moment-became-the-ai-operating-system-for-investment-management/\n- https://www.fintechfutures.com/venture-capital-funding/fixed-income-fintech-moment-raises-36m-series-b\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/duvo-raises-15m-to-give-retail-teams-an-ai-workforce-that-goes-live-in-weeks-cutting-manual-work-by-40/\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=722991\n- https://www.therecursive.com/rohlik-founder-tomas-cuprs-duvo-raises-eur28-7m-series-a-to-take-enterprise-ai-agents-to-the-us/\n- https://tech.eu/2020/04/30/resistant-ai-funding/\n- https://dev.globalprivatecapital.org/newsroom/gv-leads-usd16-6m-series-a-for-czech-republics-resistant-ai/\n- https://finsmes.com/?p=335259\n- https://techfundingnews.com/nomos-raises-20m-index-ventures-europe-energy-costs/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/our-continued-investment-in-alan/\n- https://siliconangle.com/2023/06/13/paris-based-startup-mistral-ai-raises-113m-seed-funding/\n- https://www.irishtimes.com/technology/2023/06/14/four-week-old-ai-start-up-raises-record-105m-in-european-push\n- https://eulerpool.com/en/news/ai/index-ventures-bets-on-ai-23-billion-us-dollars (aggregator)\n- https://www.pymnts.com/?p=1973909\n- https://cc.cz/podporil-rohlik-i-productboard-ted-ma-hvezdny-fond-pro-startupy-pripravene-dalsi-miliardy/\n- https://shows.acast.com/dannyinthevalley/episodes/indexventures\n- https://forbes.it/2022/12/12/midas-list-europe-migliori-venture-capitalist-europa/\n- https://forbes.it/2025/12/23/midas-lit-europe-migliori-investitori-continente\n", "person:jared-weinstein": "\n# Jared Weinstein\n\n## One-line take\nThrive's co-founding partner and COO for eleven years, who left in 2022, still invests as an LP in Thrive, and now seeds and advises emerging fund managers from Birmingham [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein).\n\n**Correction to the brief:** he is not a current Thrive partner. Justworks' 2022 S-1/A gives his COO tenure as 2011 to 2022 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1623414/000162828022018013/justworkss-1a4.htm). He confirms he left in 2022, after \"Eleven plus years\" [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein). No source I found names him on Dutchie; that deal stays attributed to the firm (see Portfolio).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he mattered at Thrive:** he built the firm, not the deal book. Kushner recruited him at a brunch in their second year of business school. Kushner had one year of banking; Weinstein had seven years in the White House. He ran the operating side as COO, set the culture (no \"back office\", team-wide deal credit) and handled crises (Will Gaybrick leaving for Stripe, LP and company problems) [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein).\n- **What constrains him now:** he has no fund vehicle. He invests personally, sits on legacy boards, and spends most of his time on Birmingham civic work (Small Magic, Overton). He is open about not knowing whether he will build \"a firm one day or a role leading something\" [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein).\n- **What he'd never do:** chase individual credit. He kept Thrive off the Midas List because deals were team-sourced, underwritten and won [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein). Nor would he dictate strategy to an early founder: his stated lesson is that Thrive's mistakes were \"choosing the wrong setup for a founder\" (same src).\n- **Why he's useful to the owner:** he is an ex-GP who now seeds and advises new managers, is still an LP in Thrive, and is close to Kushner and the partners he describes as playing \"the long game\" (Zaki, Grimshaw, Hankes, Clark). He is a warm path into Thrive and a plausible early backer or advisor for a new manager, not an AI deal source.\n\n## Differential insight\nAll from his first-ever interview, Dialectic ep. 44 with Jackson Dahl, 2026-04-20 [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein). Mirrored in `data/rows/jared-weinstein-insights.csv`.\n- **He is now an LP in Thrive.** After leaving he kept some boards and continues to \"invest in the firm\" (2:06:46): \"I still take phone calls, help with things, and invest in the firm.\"\n- **Venture partner role considered and declined.** \"We talked about whether I should stay on in more of a venture partner role\" (2:06:46). He chose a clean step back to care for his mother.\n- **He backs emerging managers now.** \"There are also emerging people building funds who are interested in my help\" (2:33:04). The episode description says he will \"seed and advise new investors\".\n- **Thrive never applied for the Midas List, by design.** \"I never wanted us to apply for the Midas List\" (1:42:39). His reason: someone sourced a deal, someone else underwrote it, a third person helped win it. This is a structural reason why per-partner deal attribution at Thrive is thin in press.\n- **Thrive's early posture was to co-invest small.** They sought \"a small check alongside Sequoia or Tiger\" (1:25:53). Tiger Global's build was the 2010 reference point, and he sat in on other firms' Monday meetings to learn.\n- **Twitch was first a pass.** Chris Paik called to say Thrive had passed and was wrong. Weinstein told him he had articulated it poorly, helped him make the case, and Paik \"went back to the team and ultimately did the deal\" (1:47:42).\n- **Early Thrive's edge was software in old industries.** Their core competency was \"enabling these traditional industries\" (1:27:32), with Oscar named as the healthcare example.\n- **Where he spent his partner time.** \"I found myself still gravitating towards early founders\" as Thrive moved into growth (1:44:57). He wanted to be the \"first call, first partner\".\n- **He nearly went to Viking Global.** Before Thrive he \"almost went to do a COO track at a hedge fund, Viking Global\" (1:18:42).\n- **Small Magic is run like a Thrive incubation.** Bloomberg Philanthropies' Providence model was brought to Birmingham with the mayor's office: \"I played venture investor, board chairman, and co-founder\" (2:31:01). Ruth Anne Moss is the founder.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a frontier-AI investor and has no AI deals on record. He matters to the owner in two ways:\n1. **Path into Thrive.** He co-founded it, is still an LP and a friend of Kushner, and is close to Zaki, Grimshaw, Hankes and Clark (Clark is the Thrive partner on Physical Intelligence, Cursor and Mesh).\n2. **Possible backer or advisor for a new manager.** He says he seeds and advises emerging fund builders. No check size or named fund is public.\n\nThe deals that matter to the owner are none of his own; Overton's only AI touch is backing Birmingham AI, a local community group [src](https://www.overton.ventures/).\n\n## Background\n- Born 1979 in Birmingham, Alabama; Mountain Brook High School (Wikipedia, needs citations) [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jared_Weinstein).\n- Duke University, BA public policy (2002 per Wikipedia). He cold-called the Bush campaign for months before an envelope-stuffing job; Karl Rove later promised him a job; West Wing intern summer 2001 [src](https://talkbusiness.net/2026/04/weinstein-highlights-work-as-venture-capitalist-president-bush-aide-palantir-adviser/).\n- White House, seven years. Scheduling office at 22, aide to Chiefs of Staff Andy Card and Josh Bolten, then personal aide to President George W. Bush, 2006-06-14 to 2009-01-20 [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jared_Weinstein).\n- Briefly deputy chief of staff in the Office of George W. Bush in Dallas after the presidency [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jared_Weinstein).\n- Stanford GSB MBA (2011). Met Kushner at Harvard's pre-MBA \"Analytics\" course before choosing Stanford [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein).\n- Palantir: early adviser while at Stanford; Joe Lonsdale and Shyam Sankar asked him to \"map DC\" for them. He teed up bank conversations during the mortgage crisis and got stock, which he says he sold early [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein), [src](https://talkbusiness.net/2026/04/weinstein-highlights-work-as-venture-capitalist-president-bush-aide-palantir-adviser/). Wikipedia dates the Palantir role 2011 to 2015.\n- Thrive Capital: Partner from Sept 2011, COO 2011 to 2022 (S-1/A). Early team per him: Kushner, Chris Paik, Will Gaybrick and himself [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein).\n- Founded the Overton Project in 2013 to bring national programs to Birmingham (Breakthrough Collaborative, Venture for America, Microsoft TEALS) [src](https://www.thirdsectorcap.org/advisory-board-2/jared-weinstein/).\n- Left Thrive in 2022, partly to care for his mother; returned to Birmingham [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.overton.ventures/ | Overton; no team page or bio (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/JaredBWeinstein | linked from the Dialectic episode page; URL resolves (200), activity not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/jared-weinstein-3b22b538/ | linked from Dialectic; resolves (200) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n| Small Magic | https://smallmagic.org/ | linked from Dialectic; blocks scripted fetches (403), so not verified |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-04-20 — Dialectic (Jackson Dahl) — ep. 44 \"Within Earshot, Out of Camera Shot\" — https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein — billed as his first interview; covers founding Thrive, culture, leaving, LP stake, Overton.\n- 2026-04-21 — Onward FX, Bentonville (Startup NWA / Arkansas Economic Development Commission) — keynote with Steve Nelson (Carbon) — https://talkbusiness.net/2026/04/weinstein-highlights-work-as-venture-capitalist-president-bush-aide-palantir-adviser/ — named Thrive passes on Uber, DoorDash and Nubank as stories VCs rarely tell.\n\n## How they think\n- **Team over stars:** shared deal credit, no Midas List, \"one-team\" culture including assistants in investment discussions [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein).\n- **Founder-opportunity fit:** the right founder for a specific opportunity matters as much as product-market fit; Thrive's mistakes were wrong founder setups (same src).\n- **Long view for juniors:** the investors who succeeded at Thrive didn't try to do a deal in their first six months (same src).\n- **Calm in crisis:** after the White House, Thrive crises felt manageable; \"never as bad as it looks\" (same src).\n- **Calls named:** Twitch (initially passed, then done after Paik's case); passes on Uber, DoorDash, Nubank (Talk Business, 2026-04-21).\n\n## Read these first\n- Dialectic ep. 44 transcript (2026-04-20): the only long-form interview; best on early Thrive and his current role [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein).\n- Justworks S-1/A (2022-06-27): authoritative dates for his Thrive titles and board seat [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1623414/000162828022018013/justworkss-1a4.htm).\n- Talk Business, Onward FX keynote recap (2026-04-21) [src](https://talkbusiness.net/2026/04/weinstein-highlights-work-as-venture-capitalist-president-bush-aide-palantir-adviser/).\n- Colossus on Kushner (Oct 2025), for context on the firm he helped build [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. The X account exists but its posts were not reviewed this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\nDeals where he is personally named. Rows already in `data/rows/thrive-capital-fullbook-deals.csv` under his slug are marked \"(existing row)\".\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Justworks | Series A (Thrive III designee; Weinstein on board since 2015-11) | 2014 (round per repo row attributed to will-gaybrick) | yes (Thrive) | Index Ventures | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1623414/000162828022018013/justworkss-1a4.htm) |\n| Justworks | Series E ($50M at $17.00/share; Thrive entities $3,000,007) | 2020-01 | no | Bain Capital Ventures, Index, Redpoint | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1623414/000162828022018013/justworkss-1a4.htm) |\n| Cityblock Health | Series A ($20.8M); board observer | 2018-01-04 | no | Maverick Ventures (lead), Sidewalk Labs | [src](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2018/01/05/cityblock-attracts-series-a-capital/?format=md) (existing row) |\n| LeafLink | Series B ($35M); quoted; Thrive's first cannabis deal | 2019-08-07 | yes | Nosara, Lerer Hippeau, Wisdom VC, TIA Ventures, L2 | [src](https://www.newcannabisventures.com/cannabis-wholesaler-marketplace-operator-leaflink-raises-35-million/) (existing row) |\n| Raisin | Series C (~$32M), \"led by\" him per one aggregator; date unverified | 2017-01 (aggregator) | yes (aggregator) | — | [aggregator](https://startupintros.com/news/raisin-series-c-2017-01) |\n| Capsule, Joyable, OpenGov, Raisin | board seats (no rounds or dates given) | before 2018 | — | — | [bio](https://www.thirdsectorcap.org/advisory-board-2/jared-weinstein/) |\n| Oscar Health | founding board (Thrive incubation) | 2012-13 | — | — | [bio](https://www.thirdsectorcap.org/advisory-board-2/jared-weinstein/) |\n\n- **Dutchie: checked and not found.** Thrive is in Dutchie's Aug 2020 Series B and Oct 2021 Series D, but no press release, quote or board record I found names Weinstein. The row stays under `thrive-capital`.\n- **Since leaving (2022 on):** no named startup deals found. Overton lists Mobley and Partners, AlabamaCreates, Birmingham AI and Birmingham Legion under \"Invest\" with no rounds or amounts [src](https://www.overton.ventures/).\n- Current directorships per an aggregator: Justworks, LeafLink, National Medal of Honor Museum Foundation [src](https://in.marketscreener.com/insider/JARED-WEINSTEIN-A2MBJ9/).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **LeafLink (2019):** Thrive's first cannabis deal, framed as B2B tooling for a regulated industry; Weinstein argued the industry needs professional tools for operations, financing and compliance [src](https://www.newcannabisventures.com/cannabis-wholesaler-marketplace-operator-leaflink-raises-35-million/).\n- **Justworks:** board seat held as the Series A designee of Thrive Capital Partners III, from Nov 2015 through the 2022 IPO filing [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1623414/000162828022018013/justworkss-1a4.htm).\n- **Twitch:** see Differential insight; Paik's conviction, Weinstein as the internal coach.\n- **Small Magic:** incubated \"the same way we would do at Thrive\", with Bloomberg Philanthropies and the Birmingham mayor's office [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Thrive Capital | co-founder, Partner and COO; now LP | 2011-09 to 2022 | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1623414/000162828022018013/justworkss-1a4.htm), [Dialectic](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein) |\n| Josh Kushner | co-founder; \"close friend\" | ~2009 (Harvard Analytics) | [Dialectic](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein) |\n| Chris Paik | early Thrive colleague (Twitch); was at Kushner's 25th | ~2010 | [Dialectic](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein) |\n| Will Gaybrick | early Thrive colleague; left for Stripe | 2011 | [Dialectic](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein) |\n| Nitin Nohria | worked with him at Thrive; mentor | Nohria joined Thrive 2022 | [Dialectic](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein) |\n| Kareem Zaki, Miles Grimshaw, Vince Hankes, Philip Clark | names them as Thrive's long-game success stories | — | [Dialectic](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein) |\n| Justworks | director (Thrive III designee) | 2015-11 | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1623414/000162828022018013/justworkss-1a4.htm) |\n| Cityblock Health | board observer | 2018-01 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2018/01/05/cityblock-attracts-series-a-capital/?format=md) |\n| LeafLink | director (aggregator) | 2019 | [aggregator](https://in.marketscreener.com/insider/JARED-WEINSTEIN-A2MBJ9/) |\n| Palantir | early adviser | 2011 to 2015 (Wikipedia) | [Dialectic](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein) |\n| Overton | founder | 2013 | [bio](https://www.thirdsectorcap.org/advisory-board-2/jared-weinstein/) |\n| Small Magic | co-founder, board chair, funder | — | [Dialectic](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein) |\n| Tom Tierney (Bridgespan) | mentor | — | [Dialectic](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein) |\n| Third Sector Capital Partners | advisory board | ~2018 | [bio](https://www.thirdsectorcap.org/advisory-board-2/jared-weinstein/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which emerging funds has he seeded or advised since 2022, and at what size? Nothing named in public.\n- Size and vehicle of his LP stake in Thrive (which fund).\n- Raisin \"led by\" attribution and date: only one aggregator; check Raisin's own 2017 release.\n- Was he on Dutchie's board or the deal partner? No evidence found.\n- Is the X account active, and who does he engage with?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein (podcast transcript, 2026-04-20)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1623414/000162828022018013/justworkss-1a4.htm (filing, 2022-06-27)\n- https://talkbusiness.net/2026/04/weinstein-highlights-work-as-venture-capitalist-president-bush-aide-palantir-adviser/ (press, 2026-04-21)\n- https://www.thirdsectorcap.org/advisory-board-2/jared-weinstein/ (bio)\n- https://www.overton.ventures/ (own org site)\n- https://globalventuring.com/blog/2018/01/05/cityblock-attracts-series-a-capital/?format=md (press)\n- https://www.newcannabisventures.com/cannabis-wholesaler-marketplace-operator-leaflink-raises-35-million/ (press)\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jared_Weinstein (stub; needs citations)\n- https://in.marketscreener.com/insider/JARED-WEINSTEIN-A2MBJ9/ (aggregator)\n- https://startupintros.com/news/raisin-series-c-2017-01 (aggregator, weak)\n", "person:jason-cui": "\n# Jason Cui\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the junior partner on a16z's Infra team who sits closest to compute: he co-signed the $150M Inferact (vLLM) seed, the Netris GPU-network Series A and the $250M Exa Series C, all within about six months of his first byline.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He is an operator who built developer and serverless-compute products at Databricks and founded and sold an infrastructure startup (Jemi) ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jason-cui/)). That is the profile a16z pairs with its infra GPs on technical deals. In every case he co-authors with a GP:\n  - Inferact: Bornstein and Raghuram\n  - Netris: Appenzeller and Raghuram\n  - Exa: Wang and Li\n\n  ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jason-cui/))\n- **What constrains him.** No source shows him as the lead decision-maker on any deal. Netris's own release says Guido Appenzeller \"led the round and is joining the Netris board\" ([src](https://netris.io/news/press-release/andreessen-horowitz-leads-netris-series-a-to-accelerate-adoption-of-gpu-network-automation-and-multi-tenancy-across-ai-cloud-operators-worldwide/)). He is the third or fourth name on every byline.\n- **What he does.** He writes thesis essays first and the deal follows:\n  - \"Search Wars: Episode 2\" (2025-11-13) preceded the Exa Series C lead (2026-05-20) by about six months.\n  - \"Your Data Agents Need Context\" (2026-03-10) is a public thesis with no deal attached yet.\n- **What he would never do.** Nothing sourced. His own essays argue against fully automated context-gathering for enterprise agents ([src](https://a16z.com/your-data-agents-need-context/)), which suggests he is skeptical of pure-autonomy agent pitches.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Inferact timing: a16z was in years before the seed.** \"we've been small-scale supporters of the vLLM project since 2023.\" Also: \"the first a16z open source AI grant was made to the vLLM team\", and the first vLLM meetup was hosted in a16z's office ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/), 2026-01-22). So a16z had a relationship with the founders years before the round. That is a relationship edge, not an earlier equity entry: all 11 seed investors came in at the same round. The grant wording was confirmed only through a fetch summarizer.\n- **The Inferact seed was a co-lead, not a solo lead.** a16z's post says a16z is leading. Inferact's own post says the $150M at an $800M valuation was \"led by a16z and Lightspeed\". Other participants were Sequoia, Altimeter, Redpoint, ZhenFund, The House Fund, Striker Venture Partners, Laude Ventures, Databricks Ventures, GC&H and the UC Berkeley Chancellor's Fund ([src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference)). Note the Databricks Ventures participation, his former employer.\n- **The Inferact thesis in his byline.** \"We're rapidly approaching a second phase that's bottlenecked by inference.\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/))\n- **Netris: he co-signed but did not lead.** \"Partner Guido Appenzeller led the round and is joining the Netris board.\" It was a $15M Series A ([src](https://netris.io/news/press-release/andreessen-horowitz-leads-netris-series-a-to-accelerate-adoption-of-gpu-network-automation-and-multi-tenancy-across-ai-cloud-operators-worldwide/), 2026-06-25). The diligence angle in the a16z post: \"if the network is down, the data center with its expensive GPUs stops working.\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-netris/))\n- **Exa: a16z came in late and paid up.**\n  - Lightspeed, Nvidia and YC backed the 2024 Series A ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/07/16/exa-raises-17m-lightspeed-nvidia-ycombinator-google-ai-models/)).\n  - Benchmark led the $85M Series B at a $700M valuation on 2025-09-03 ([src](https://exa.ai/blog/announcing-series-b)).\n  - a16z then led the C: \"Exa just raised $250M at a $2.2B valuation led by a16z\" ([src](https://exa.ai/blog/announcing-series-c), 2026-05-20). That is about 3x in roughly eight months.\n  - Lisha Li, a16z's Infra partner, made seed checks into \"fal, Periodic Labs, Exa, and others\" before joining a16z, per her firm bio ([src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/)). That makes her a likely internal warm path to the Exa C, but there is no source saying she was involved.\n- **His Search Wars call, six months before Exa.** \"deep research will become the dominant and most monetizable form of agentic search\" ([src](https://a16z.com/search-wars-episode-2/), 2025-11-13). The essay also says most organizations expect to \"rely on third-party search providers\", which is the buy-not-build case for Exa and Parallel.\n- **Data-agent view, drawn from his Databricks background.** \"data and analytics agents are essentially useless without the right context\". He also writes \"Realistically not every enterprise can (or should) build this in house\" ([src](https://a16z.com/your-data-agents-need-context/), 2026-03-10). He has a public thesis on the context layer for enterprise agents with no deal announced yet, so watch for one.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, with a caveat.**\n- **For:** he is exactly the owner's target tier, a non-GP partner at a fund that writes $10M+ and growth checks, whose bylines sit on compute.\n  - Inferact is a $150M seed at $800M for the vLLM maintainers. That is the \"very large seed\" shape the owner writes $20M into, in AI compute/inference ([src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference)).\n  - Netris is GPU-cloud network infrastructure.\n  - Exa is AI-native search infrastructure but growth stage (low by itself).\n- **Caveat:** no evidence that he leads or holds board seats, and he has no pre-training lab deals. Treat him as a scout and translator for the a16z infra GPs (Bornstein, Appenzeller, Raghuram), and as a route to co-invest allocations in a16z-led compute seeds, not as an independent decision-maker.\n\n## Background\n- Harvard, degree in Computer Science ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jason-cui/)); graduation year not found.\n- Founder of Jemi, \"a commerce infrastructure platform backed by Y Combinator, General Catalyst\" and Kleiner Perkins, acquired in 2023 ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jason-cui/)). Co-founders, YC batch and acquirer were not found; the YC page has only a one-line description ([src](https://www.ycombinator.com/companies/jemi)).\n- Product management lead at Databricks, working on developer experience and serverless compute ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jason-cui/)). Dates not found; presumably after the 2023 Jemi sale (inference).\n- Joined a16z as a partner. Join date not found; LinkedIn was blocked (HTTP 999). The headshot is filed under an `uploads/2025/09` path, which hints at a profile created around September 2025 (inference, not a source). His earliest byline is 2025-11-13.\n- Outside work, per the firm bio: running, electronic music, jiu jitsu ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jason-cui/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/jason-cui/ | firm author page, 5 pieces as of 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/JasonSCui | Handle linked from his a16z author page (checked 2026-11-10); x.com fetch returned HTTP 402, so followers and cadence unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/jasonscui/ | Linked from his a16z author page (checked 2026-11-10); profile itself not loadable |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The web-search budget was exhausted this run, so only direct page fetches were possible.\n\n## How they think\n- **Inference becomes the bottleneck.** The second phase of AI is bound by inference, so open-source serving engines (vLLM) become the control point ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/)).\n- **Networking is the hidden single point of failure in GPU clouds** ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-netris/)).\n- **Search is being rebuilt for agents.** The competition is between API providers, and deep research is the most monetizable form ([src](https://a16z.com/search-wars-episode-2/)).\n- **Enterprise agents fail on context, not SQL.** A context layer that combines automated construction with human refinement is needed ([src](https://a16z.com/your-data-agents-need-context/)).\n- **Right or wrong calls:** too early to judge. Exa's valuation went from $700M to $2.2B into a16z's own lead.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Investing in Inferact](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/): the inference thesis and the vLLM history.\n- [Search Wars: Episode 2](https://a16z.com/search-wars-episode-2/): his market map for agent search, which preceded the Exa lead.\n- [Your Data Agents Need Context](https://a16z.com/your-data-agents-need-context/): the clearest sign of what he is hunting next.\n- [Investing in Netris](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-netris/): the GPU-cloud networking diligence.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. His X account (@JasonSCui, linked from the firm page) could not be loaded (HTTP 402), so his topics and amplifiers are unknown.\n\n## Portfolio\n**Completeness check (2026-11-10 update):** the raw HTML of his a16z author page links exactly three \"Investing in\" announcements (Inferact, Exa, Netris) plus two essays; the page's paginated archive (`/page/2/`) returns 404, so this is his full a16z byline record. No board seat is stated on any of the three, and no angel or pre-a16z investments are disclosed. Web search was unavailable this run (budget exhausted), so deals named only in third-party press could be missing.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Inferact (vLLM) | Seed, $150M at $800M | 2026-01-22 | a16z co-led with Lightspeed; Cui third of three bylines | Sequoia, Altimeter, Redpoint, ZhenFund, The House Fund, Striker VP, Laude Ventures, Databricks Ventures, GC&H, UC Berkeley Chancellor's Fund | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/), [Inferact](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference) |\n| Exa | Series C, $250M at $2.2B | 2026-05-20 | a16z led; Cui fourth of four bylines | none named in post; prior: Benchmark, Lightspeed, YC, NVentures | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/), [Exa](https://exa.ai/blog/announcing-series-c) |\n| Netris | Series A, $15M | 2026-06-25 | a16z led; Appenzeller led and took the board seat | none named | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-netris/), [Netris](https://netris.io/news/press-release/andreessen-horowitz-leads-netris-series-a-to-accelerate-adoption-of-gpu-network-automation-and-multi-tenancy-across-ai-cloud-operators-worldwide/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Inferact.**\n  - a16z had backed vLLM since 2023 with small grants, including its first open-source AI grant, and hosted the first vLLM meetup.\n  - When the maintainers (Simon Mo, Woosuk Kwon, Kaichao You, Roger Wang) incorporated, a16z co-led the $150M seed with Lightspeed ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/), [src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference)).\n  - Cui's specific role in sourcing is not stated. The firm relationship predates his tenure.\n- **Exa.**\n  - Cui co-wrote the November 2025 search market map that profiled Exa.\n  - a16z then led the May 2026 Series C at a $2.2B valuation, after Benchmark's September 2025 B at $700M.\n  - The deal was co-signed with Growth GPs (Wang, Zhang) and an Infra GP (Li), so it is a cross-fund deal ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/)).\n- **Netris.**\n  - The diligence leaned on customer calls (\"we were impressed by the team\").\n  - Appenzeller led and took the board seat ([src](https://netris.io/news/press-release/andreessen-horowitz-leads-netris-series-a-to-accelerate-adoption-of-gpu-network-automation-and-multi-tenancy-across-ai-cloud-operators-worldwide/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Andreessen Horowitz | Partner, Infra | join date not found | [src](https://a16z.com/author/jason-cui/) |\n| Matt Bornstein | Co-author, Inferact | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/) |\n| Raghu Raghuram | Co-author, Inferact and Netris | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-netris/) |\n| Guido Appenzeller | Co-author, Netris (Appenzeller led and holds the board seat) | 2026 | [src](https://netris.io/news/press-release/andreessen-horowitz-leads-netris-series-a-to-accelerate-adoption-of-gpu-network-automation-and-multi-tenancy-across-ai-cloud-operators-worldwide/) |\n| Jennifer Li | Co-author: Search Wars, Data Agents, Exa | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/your-data-agents-need-context/) |\n| Sarah Wang | Co-author: Search Wars, Exa | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/search-wars-episode-2/) |\n| Stephenie Zhang | Co-author: Search Wars, Exa | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/search-wars-episode-2/) |\n| Databricks | Former product management lead | dates not found | [src](https://a16z.com/author/jason-cui/) |\n| Jemi | Founder (YC, General Catalyst, Kleiner Perkins backed; acquired 2023) | | [src](https://a16z.com/author/jason-cui/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- His a16z join date, and whether he was hired as a partner or promoted into the role. LinkedIn was blocked, and there was no search budget to look for announcements.\n- Who acquired Jemi, and who his co-founders were.\n- Exa's founders Will Bryk and Jeffrey Wang started their search engine at Harvard, per a16z's Exa post ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/)), and Cui is a Harvard CS graduate. A school-network link to the Exa C is plausible but unsourced (inference only).\n- Whether he personally sourced Inferact or Exa, or only executed. Is a Databricks connection behind Databricks Ventures' participation in Inferact?\n- Any data-agent or context-layer investment following his March 2026 essay.\n- Podcasts, X handle and talks: not searched (search budget exhausted).\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/jason-cui/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/\n- https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-netris/\n- https://netris.io/news/press-release/andreessen-horowitz-leads-netris-series-a-to-accelerate-adoption-of-gpu-network-automation-and-multi-tenancy-across-ai-cloud-operators-worldwide/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/\n- https://exa.ai/blog/announcing-series-c\n- https://exa.ai/blog/announcing-series-b\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/07/16/exa-raises-17m-lightspeed-nvidia-ycombinator-google-ai-models/ (linked from Exa's blog; not fetched)\n- https://a16z.com/search-wars-episode-2/\n- https://a16z.com/your-data-agents-need-context/\n- https://www.ycombinator.com/companies/jemi\n- https://x.com/JasonSCui (linked from firm page; not loadable)\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/jasonscui/ (linked from firm page; not loadable)\n", "person:jason-kelly": "\n# Jason Kelly\n\n## One-line take\nKelly is not a check-writer. He is the operator running the largest working AI-driven autonomous wet lab in the US, and the person who ran the GPT-5 lab experiment with OpenAI. As a Compound venture partner, he is best used as a technical diligence node and a source of AI-for-science deal flow, not as a co-investor.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** in February 2026 Ginkgo plugged an OpenAI reasoning model into its robotic lab and beat a published biochemistry benchmark by 40%. Kelly took the conclusion that experiment throughput, not model IQ, wins science: \"What really let it break through wasn't that it was so smart\" ([Sequoia Training Data, 2026-03-24](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/)). That puts him at the junction of frontier labs, AI-for-science and lab robotics. Few people in those categories have his operating data, about 50 robots and 50 scientists dogfooding them, scaling to 100.\n- **Constraint:** time and conflict. Compound itself says \"99.9% of Jason's time is continuing to build Ginkgo\" ([src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/welcoming-jason-kelly-as-a-venture-partner-at-compound)). Ginkgo sells autonomous labs and cloud-lab services, so any autonomous-lab startup is a potential competitor or customer. No deal names him personally.\n- **What he'd never do:** back humanoids for the lab or \"digital twin\" simulation as a substitute for real experiments. He says no to humanoids in the lab and \"Oh, make a digital twin. No.\" ([Sequoia](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/)). He also says he dropped the science half of the problem, design models, to focus on the engineering half, the lab (same source).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **First money into Ginkgo was Bryan Johnson, then \"pudgy VC,\" as an angel after YC.** Quote: \"Our angel? Mr. Bryan Johnson.\" ([Sequoia, 2026-03-24](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/)). This fits with OS Fund (Johnson's fund) appearing in the March 2015 $9M Series A with Felicis and Data Collective ([BusinessWire](https://businesswire.com/news/home/20150318005213/en/Ginkgo-Bioworks-Raises-9-Million-Funding-Felicis)).\n- **The YC intro was a cold email to Altman.** Kelly answered Altman's 2014 deep-tech blog post: \"I wrote him an email\" ([Sequoia](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/)). Ginkgo bootstrapped from 2008 to 2014 because \"in biotech VC, they really don't like, like, young people\" (same). His OpenAI relationship goes back to this point, more than a decade before the GPT-5 lab result.\n- **Admitted mistake: he tried to do both design and lab.** Quote: \"I went after both initially, and I kind of took my licks for that.\" He now treats AI design models (he names Chai, Boltz and Arc's Evo 2) as tools a reasoning model calls, not as the core ([Sequoia](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/)).\n- **Contrarian: dumber AIs with a robot lab beat smarter scientists.** Quote: \"They can even be dumber than the scientists. I think they win.\" His argument is economic. Today less than 5% of research spend goes on reagents, and robot labs flip that to 90%, which gives about 10x the data per dollar (same source).\n- **Anti-humanoid, anti-sim.** On humanoids: \"There are much better ways to do that than, like, walk them bipedally\". He points to track-and-arm carts (RACs) instead (same). This matters because Compound's portfolio page lists a stealth \"Industrial/Military Humanoid Robotics Co.\" ([portfolio](https://www.compound.vc/portfolio2)). Kelly's lab-specific skepticism doesn't necessarily extend to industrial humanoids.\n- **He thinks venture's model breaks under AI-driven discovery.** He asks Sequoia \"what's venture look like in that scenario?\" if labs start producing breakthroughs like a room-temperature semiconductor (same). He also argues that OpenAI's valuation \"signals fundamental research in industry can be valuable\" (same).\n- **Consumer biotech is his application thesis, and it overlaps with Compound.** Quote: \"the therapeutics industry today is really the disease industry.\" He also points to weekly molecular blood monitoring and GLP-1s as consumer products (same). Compound's welcome post cites trading notes on \"consumer biotech and wearables\" ([src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/welcoming-jason-kelly-as-a-venture-partner-at-compound)).\n- **The Compound tie predates the title by five months.** Shelby Newsad's X post of 2026-03-11 announced a Boston Idea Factory event: \"@CompoundVC and @Ginkgo are joining forces\". It thanks Kelly for supporting it ([X](https://x.com/shelbynewsad/status/2031739230594937307)). The venture-partner announcement followed on 2026-08-19, and the relationship began on X (\"We initially connected with Jason on X\") ([src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/welcoming-jason-kelly-as-a-venture-partner-at-compound)).\n- **Startups he names as peers or targets:** Medra (transcribed \"Meddra\"), which does robotic-arm lab automation, and Hadrian, a high-mix/low-volume manufacturing analog. He says \"I know them well\" of Colossal ([Sequoia](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/)). These are sourcing signals, not deals.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a capital source for $20M checks. Compound is a sub-$150M-fund firm and he is 0.1% time. He is still high-value as a **node**:\n1. He ran the most-cited reasoning-model-plus-robot-lab result with OpenAI. That makes him a reference and diligence voice for AI-for-science neolabs (the category of Periodic Labs and similar large seeds), and a likely early see on autonomous-lab founders.\n2. He has a long, personal YC/Altman line, and he chaired the US National Security Commission on Emerging Biotechnology (named chair Dec 2022, succeeded by Sen. Todd Young Sept 2024) ([Senate Armed Services](https://www.armed-services.senate.gov/press-releases/armed-services-committees-leadership-announces-chair-and-vice-chair-selections-for-national-security-commission-on-emerging-biotechnology); [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Security_Commission_on_Emerging_Biotechnology)). That gives him DOE and Genesis Mission access; Ginkgo sold 97 robots to DOE.\n\nDeals that matter to me: none personally attributed. Watch Compound's stealth \"Chemical Synthesis Co.\", \"Robotics Lab Co.\" and bio-AI seeds after 2026-08 for his fingerprints (inference).\n\n## Background\n- Co-founded Ginkgo Bioworks in 2008 out of MIT grad school (PhD in bioengineering, MIT, per his own account) ([Sequoia](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/)). Bootstrapped on grants and service work until 2014.\n- Ginkgo went through YC in summer 2014, the first biotech in YC ([TechCrunch 2015](https://techcrunch.com/2015/03/18/ginkgo-bioworks-lands-9-million-in-series-a-funding-to-build-out-new-lab-facilities)). Series A $9M in March 2015 (Felicis, OS Fund, Data Collective) ([BusinessWire](https://businesswire.com/news/home/20150318005213/en/Ginkgo-Bioworks-Raises-9-Million-Funding-Felicis)). Series B $45M four months later, led by Viking Global (secondary, per search snippets of [SynBioBeta](https://synbiobeta.com/ginkgo-bioworks-closes-45m-series-b)). Public via SPAC in 2021 ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2021/07/08/going-public-spac-gingko-bioworks)).\n- Chair of the National Security Commission on Emerging Biotechnology from Dec 2022 until Sept 2024 (sources above).\n- February 2026: Ginkgo and OpenAI announced the GPT-5-driven autonomous lab result, a 40% improvement in cell-free protein synthesis cost ([SynBioBeta release](https://synbiobeta.com/read/ginkgo-bioworks-autonomous-laboratory-driven-by-openai-s-gpt-5-achieves-40-improvement-over-state-of-the-art-scientific-benchmark)). March 2026: Ginkgo Cloud Lab launched, with experiments from $39 ([Sequoia](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/)).\n- 2026-08-19: became Venture Partner, Biology & Healthcare at Compound ([src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/welcoming-jason-kelly-as-a-venture-partner-at-compound)).\n- Crunchbase lists him as a co-founder of Joyn Bio and shows an Allonnia \"partner investment\" ([Crunchbase](https://crunchbase.com/person/jason-kelly-2), aggregator). Both are Ginkgo-affiliated, not personal VC deals.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://ginkgobioworks.com | Company site, linked from X bio |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/jrkelly | 34,428 followers, 15,137 posts, joined 2008-05 (fxtwitter API, 2026-11-10). Bio: Ginkgo CEO, former Chair of NSCEB |\n| LinkedIn | not linked on Compound bio | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-03 — Sequoia Training Data — \"How Autonomous Labs Will Transform Scientific Research\" — [link](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/) — the OpenAI experiment, Waymo analogy (high mix/low volume), RAC carts, DOE 97-robot sale, anti-humanoid, consumer biotech, Bryan Johnson as first angel. Full transcript read.\n- 2026-05-26 — NPR — \"Researchers are building AI-powered robot labs\" — [link](https://www.kunc.org/npr-news/2026-05-26/researchers-are-building-ai-powered-robot-labs-what-does-this-mean-for-science) — early years: \"we could not raise venture capital\"; a coming culture clash when everyday people can ask scientific questions. Drew Endy is quoted on risks.\n- Not reviewed: Go To Market Grit, \"Life finds a way\" ([chartable](https://chartable.com/podcasts/go-to-market-grit/episodes/159933867-ceo-and-cofounder-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly-life-finds-a-way)); Once a Scientist ([buzzsprout](https://www.buzzsprout.com/975439/episodes/18743589)); a podcast titled \"Jason Kelly - Ginkgo Bioworks CEO and Co-Founder on Automation, AI Data & Biotech's Reset\" (Audible listing).\n\n## How they think\n- **Main theses:** experimental throughput beats model intelligence. Science should be priced by usage (reagents), not overhead. Autonomous labs make labs *smaller* and centralized, like a \"local cloud\". Make biology programmable so \"kids someday\" can design it. He sees a national-security race with China on research per dollar ([Sequoia](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/)).\n- **Contrarian views:** no humanoids in labs; no digital twins; AI is the first tech wave that matters to biopharma (\"The tech guys finally did something cool\"); consumer and longevity biotech beat disease therapeutics on market size.\n- **Calls right or wrong:** by his own account he was wrong to chase design and lab at once (above). Ginkgo's post-SPAC history is outside this profile's scope; not reviewed.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Sequoia Training Data transcript (2026-03): the fullest statement of his autonomous-lab and AI-scientist thesis.\n2. Compound welcome post (2026-08-19): the scope of his role and the 99.9% time caveat.\n3. Ginkgo/OpenAI GPT-5 autonomous lab release (2026-02): the result everything else rests on ([SynBioBeta](https://synbiobeta.com/read/ginkgo-bioworks-autonomous-laboratory-driven-by-openai-s-gpt-5-achieves-40-improvement-over-state-of-the-art-scientific-benchmark)).\n4. NPR feature (2026-05): an outside skeptic's framing (Drew Endy).\n\n## Social engagement\nThe X bio is Ginkgo/$DNA and NSCEB oriented. The timeline was not readable (X blocks scraping; site search returned nothing), so reply and amplification patterns were not found. Shelby Newsad (Compound) amplifies him and tagged him on the Idea Factory event ([X](https://x.com/shelbynewsad/status/2031739230594937307)).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| No AI, AI-chip, robotics or bio-AI deal names him personally | — | — | — | — | Compound portfolio and writing pages checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Unacast (location data; identity of this \"Jason Kelly\" unverified) | Series A | 2016 | unknown | unknown | [CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/jason-kelly), aggregator, low confidence |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found for deals he invested in. The founder-side story of Ginkgo's own funding: a cold email to Altman after Altman's 2014 deep-tech post led to YC in summer 2014. Bryan Johnson came in as the first angel after YC, then the March 2015 Series A (Felicis, OS Fund, Data Collective) ([Sequoia](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/); [BusinessWire](https://businesswire.com/news/home/20150318005213/en/Ginkgo-Bioworks-Raises-9-Million-Funding-Felicis)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Compound | Venture Partner, Biology & Healthcare | 2026-08-19 | [src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/welcoming-jason-kelly-as-a-venture-partner-at-compound) |\n| Shelby Newsad (Compound) | Met on X over autonomous science; ran an Idea Factory event together; wrote his welcome post | by 2026-03-11 | [X](https://x.com/shelbynewsad/status/2031739230594937307); [src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/welcoming-jason-kelly-as-a-venture-partner-at-compound) |\n| Sam Altman / Y Combinator | Cold email led to YC S14 | 2014 | [Sequoia](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/) |\n| OpenAI | GPT-5 autonomous-lab research collaboration | 2026-02 | [SynBioBeta](https://synbiobeta.com/read/ginkgo-bioworks-autonomous-laboratory-driven-by-openai-s-gpt-5-achieves-40-improvement-over-state-of-the-art-scientific-benchmark) |\n| Bryan Johnson (OS Fund) | First angel investor in Ginkgo after YC | 2014 | [Sequoia](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/) |\n| Felicis, Data Collective (DCVC), OS Fund | Ginkgo Series A investors | 2015-03 | [BusinessWire](https://businesswire.com/news/home/20150318005213/en/Ginkgo-Bioworks-Raises-9-Million-Funding-Felicis) |\n| Sonya Huang, Pat Grady (Sequoia) | Podcast hosts; Sequoia was a Ginkgo pitch target (\"When I was talking to Sequoia\") | 2026-03 | [Sequoia](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/) |\n| Reshma Shetty | Ginkgo co-founder; worked on the OpenAI collaboration | 2008 | [NPR](https://www.kunc.org/npr-news/2026-05-26/researchers-are-building-ai-powered-robot-labs-what-does-this-mean-for-science) |\n| US DOE / PNNL (Genesis Mission) | Customer; 97-robot autonomous lab | 2026 | [Sequoia](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/) |\n| NSCEB (Sen. Todd Young, Michelle Rozo) | Chair 2022-12 to 2024-09; Rozo vice chair; Young successor | 2022-12 | [SASC](https://www.armed-services.senate.gov/press-releases/armed-services-committees-leadership-announces-chair-and-vice-chair-selections-for-national-security-commission-on-emerging-biotechnology) |\n| Colossal Biosciences | \"I know them well\" | unknown | [Sequoia](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which Compound deals since 2026-08 he has sourced or diligenced. Check the stealth \"Robotics Lab Co.\" and \"Chemical Synthesis Co.\" on [portfolio2](https://www.compound.vc/portfolio2).\n- Does he angel-invest personally in AI-for-science or autonomous-lab startups (Medra, others)? No primary record was found. The CB Insights Unacast entry may be a different Jason Kelly.\n- How he handles conflicts between Compound deals and Ginkgo's autonomous-lab and Cloud Lab business.\n- Whether OpenAI and the frontier labs see Ginkgo as a training-data partner (he floated feeding results back into model weights). That would make him a node into lab-side AI-for-science deals.\n- The X timeline was not read; a pass through his posts on autonomous science in 2024–2026 would surface more names.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.compound.vc/writing/welcoming-jason-kelly-as-a-venture-partner-at-compound (2026-08-19)\n- https://www.compound.vc/team/jason ; https://www.compound.vc/portfolio2\n- https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/ (full transcript; episode dated 2026-03-24 per [Pocket Casts](https://pocketcasts.com/podcast/training-data/4cd0e900-0a48-013d-5da1-0acc26574db2/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/3b17771b-c741-4abe-8ac8-801c220350d3))\n- https://x.com/shelbynewsad/status/2031739230594937307 (2026-03-11, read via fxtwitter API)\n- https://x.com/jrkelly (profile stats via fxtwitter API, 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.kunc.org/npr-news/2026-05-26/researchers-are-building-ai-powered-robot-labs-what-does-this-mean-for-science\n- https://synbiobeta.com/read/ginkgo-bioworks-autonomous-laboratory-driven-by-openai-s-gpt-5-achieves-40-improvement-over-state-of-the-art-scientific-benchmark\n- https://businesswire.com/news/home/20150318005213/en/Ginkgo-Bioworks-Raises-9-Million-Funding-Felicis ; https://techcrunch.com/2015/03/18/ginkgo-bioworks-lands-9-million-in-series-a-funding-to-build-out-new-lab-facilities\n- https://www.armed-services.senate.gov/press-releases/armed-services-committees-leadership-announces-chair-and-vice-chair-selections-for-national-security-commission-on-emerging-biotechnology ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Security_Commission_on_Emerging_Biotechnology\n- Aggregators: https://crunchbase.com/person/jason-kelly-2 ; https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/jason-kelly ; https://www.roundfunded.com/en/investor/jason-kelly\n\n**Not confirmed:** any personal deal; the angel check size; the Series B lead (snippet only); the exact NSCEB end date beyond \"Sept 2024\"; the spelling of \"Meddra\" (likely Medra); whether \"functions\" in the transcript means Function Health.\n", "person:jason-warner": "\n# Jason Warner\n\n## One-line take\nAn operator who spent two years as a Redpoint growth MD, then left to run poolside, a code-first frontier lab. He matters to you as a frontier-lab founder and a node for dev-tools and AI deals, not as someone writing venture checks today.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he thinks like an architect. He bets on \"primitives\" other companies build on: GitHub Actions, Alchemy, and now a code model he treats as the route to AGI. He also sets explicit goals, and rarely: GitHub's Microsoft offer was a written goal in his plan ([src](https://changelog.com/podcast/395)).\n- **What constrains him:** as poolside CEO he lives on the compute treadmill. He describes energy, chips and cluster size as the only variables that matter ([src](https://invent.psu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/transcript-Developing-the-future-of-AI-with-poolside_edited.pdf)).\n- **What he'd never do:** back an investment on spreadsheet metrics alone. He also refuses to sell \"vibe coding\" into high-consequence environments ([src](https://chainofthought.show/podcast/23-first-code-then-agi-softwares-event-horizon-with-poolside-founders-jason-warner-and-eiso-k/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **How poolside started.** Warner met Eiso Kant in 2017 while trying to buy Kant's company, source{d}, to be GitHub's AI layer. Quote: \"I met my co founder when I tried to acquire his company\" ([src](https://invent.psu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/transcript-Developing-the-future-of-AI-with-poolside_edited.pdf), 00:11:54).\n- **A near-miss compute deal.** Before taking any money, poolside weighed an OpenAI-style 49/51 compute deal with a \"more minor hyperscaler\" (not Microsoft). Quote: \"contemplating whether or not we would have to take an open AI style relationship\" ([src](https://invent.psu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/transcript-Developing-the-future-of-AI-with-poolside_edited.pdf), 00:24:48).\n- **His frontier-AI rule.** Only energy, chips and the AI layers matter, and he thinks investors underweight energy and chips. Quote: \"there's only three things that matter when it comes to frontier AI\" ([src](https://invent.psu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/transcript-Developing-the-future-of-AI-with-poolside_edited.pdf), 00:22:31).\n- **Redpoint check size.** Growth checks typically ran $25M-$100M. Quote: \"invest somewhere between twenty-five and a hundred million dollars\" ([src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1422892/10059714/transcript), 38:18).\n- **Redpoint screen.** First, is the curve going up. Second, does the company matter for its space and will others build on it. Quote: \"Is this an important company? Does this company incredibly matter for its space?\" ([src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1422892/10059714/transcript), 38:45).\n- **Omega IV's fund strategy.** He chose Redpoint because Omega IV was run for cash multiples, not IRR or markups. Quote: \"they want to be a multiples-return fund\" ([src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1422892/10059714/transcript), 20:43).\n- **Crossover investors come later.** Redpoint's growth bets were made expecting crossover funds to buy in at the next round. Quote: \"crossovers or YOLOs want to come in to the stuff that we invest later\" ([src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1422892/10059714/transcript), 40:29).\n- **First Redpoint deal.** His first Redpoint investment was Alchemy, which he called \"AWS for crypto\". Quote: \"my very first investment is one called Alchemy\" ([src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1422892/10059714/transcript), 20:43).\n- **Crypto rule.** Back the centralized layer of any \"decentralized\" wave. Quote: \"what's the centralized component of this that I can invest in?\" ([src](https://stackoverflow.blog/2022/05/31/jason-warner-cto-github-redpoint-ventures-web3)).\n- **Pitched a GitHub buy before joining it.** While at Heroku he drafted a plan for Salesforce to buy GitHub. Quote: \"a plan while I was at Salesforce to pitch up to buy GitHub\" ([src](https://changelog.com/podcast/395)).\n- **Angel network with Nat Friedman.** As GitHub CTO under Nat Friedman, he said one of the two was likely an investor in any interesting dev-tools startup. Quote: \"either one of us is likely an investor in one of them, too.\" ([src](https://moderncto.io/231-jason-warner-cto-at-github/), 00:07:37).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- He no longer deploys fund capital: he left Redpoint in 2023-06 to run poolside ([src](https://www.forbes.com/sites/alexkonrad/2021/07/08/github-cto-jason-warner-joins-redpoint-new-725-million-growth-fund/)). At Redpoint he was a growth investor writing $25M-$100M checks, not a seed partner.\n- What matters to you:\n  - **poolside** is a frontier lab. Its seed was led and incubated by Redpoint ([src](https://medium.com/redpoint-ventures/fresh-ink-hello-poolside-384923ff3ad4)).\n  - **Etched (AI chips):** he is named as an angel in Etched's 2024 Series A ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware)).\n  - **Network:** links to Eiso Kant, Nat Friedman, Redpoint's Jacob Effron, and a long list of dev-infra companies he backed as an angel.\n- **How to use him:** as a reference point and a co-investor or angel for compute, code-model and AI-chip deals, not as a lead.\n\n## Background\n- **IBM:** high-school co-op carrying computers. First real IBM project was the 2000 Sydney Olympics site ([src](https://www-webflow.osohq.com/post/developer-den-with-jason-warner)).\n- **Education:** BS in Computer Science, Penn State; MS, Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute ([src](https://techcrunch.com/author/jason-warner/)).\n- **Early jobs:** Microcast, a 1999 video-streaming startup ([src](https://invent.psu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/transcript-Developing-the-future-of-AI-with-poolside_edited.pdf)), then Enron in 2000, then SBC/SNET ([src](https://www-webflow.osohq.com/post/developer-den-with-jason-warner)).\n- **Canonical:** ran product engineering for Ubuntu Desktop and Ubuntu Phone. **Heroku:** VP Engineering / GM ([src](https://techcrunch.com/author/jason-warner/)).\n- **GitHub, 2017-2021:** SVP Technology, then CTO. Shipped Actions, Packages, Advanced Security and Codespaces, and incubated Copilot ([src](https://techcrunch.com/author/jason-warner/)).\n- **Redpoint Ventures, 2021-07 to 2023-06:** Managing Director on the Omega IV growth fund ([src](https://www.forbes.com/sites/alexkonrad/2021/07/08/github-cto-jason-warner-joins-redpoint-new-725-million-growth-fund/)).\n- **poolside, 2023 to now:** co-founder and CEO.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://poolside.ai | company site |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | no personal blog found; TechCrunch contributor page lists no articles |\n| X | https://x.com/jasoncwarner | handle given by him on Dev Interrupted (2022); not crawled |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | not checked |\n| GitHub | nothing found | not checked |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lcX4pNaNahQ | \"FAQs with Jason\", short Redpoint YouTube series (2021-23) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nThe full catalogue (72 items) is in `data/rows/jason-warner-voice-media.csv`. The highlights:\n- **2020-05, The Changelog #395** ([link](https://changelog.com/podcast/395)): skipping CI for Actions \"got us acquired\"; the Microsoft offer arrived the first week of April 2018.\n- **2020-10, Modern CTO #231** ([link](https://moderncto.io/231-jason-warner-cto-at-github/)): the Nat Friedman angel-network line.\n- **2021-08, Oso Developer Den** ([link](https://www-webflow.osohq.com/post/developer-den-with-jason-warner)): most VCs are wrong on TAM; form your own technical view first.\n- **2022-02, Dev Interrupted #65** ([link](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1422892/10059714/transcript)): the $25M-$100M checks, the \"important company\" test and Alchemy.\n- **2022-05, Stack Overflow Podcast** ([link](https://stackoverflow.blog/2022/05/31/jason-warner-cto-github-redpoint-ventures-web3)): \"entrepreneurs deserve better\"; a crypto-crash call.\n- **2023-02, Logan Bartlett Show EP 52 with Jacob Effron** ([link](https://theloganbartlettshow.simplecast.com/episodes/ep-52-jason-warner-jacob-effron-redpoint-ventures-former-github-cto-explains-ai-for-dummies-0L6hPeGY)): recorded months before poolside launched. Not mined; no transcript reachable.\n- **2024-08, Dare to Disrupt (Penn State)** ([link](https://invent.psu.edu/podcast/developing-the-future-of-ai-with-poolside-co-founder-jason-warner/)): the source{d} origin, the hyperscaler 49/51 offer, the 10k-GPU cluster.\n- **2025-03, HumanX** ([link](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/10/poolside-ceo-says-most-companies-shouldnt-build-foundation-models/)): most companies shouldn't build foundation models.\n- **2025-05, Chain of Thought with Eiso Kant** ([link](https://chainofthought.show/podcast/23-first-code-then-agi-softwares-event-horizon-with-poolside-founders-jason-warner-and-eiso-k/)): in 2023 \"People thought we were nuts.\"\n\n## How they think\n- **Primitives over products.** He backs things others will build on (Dev Interrupted, Stack Overflow).\n- **A technical view first.** He forms his own view of how something works before reading metrics or TAM (Oso, Stack Overflow).\n- **Portfolio of bets.** Exploratory bets should be right only 20-25% of the time ([src](https://changelog.com/podcast/395)).\n- **Contrarian views:**\n  - Specialised code models matter more than general-purpose AI ([src](https://invent.psu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/transcript-Developing-the-future-of-AI-with-poolside_edited.pdf)).\n  - Most crypto funding of 2020-22 was garbage, even while he backed crypto infrastructure ([src](https://stackoverflow.blog/2022/05/31/jason-warner-cto-github-redpoint-ventures-web3)).\n- **Calls that held up:** the May 2022 downturn call; scaling-plus-RL in 2023, when \"people thought we were nuts.\"\n\n## Read these first\n- **Dev Interrupted transcript (2022):** the only place he describes Redpoint's growth process and check size ([link](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1422892/10059714/transcript)).\n- **Dare to Disrupt transcript (2024):** the poolside origin and compute economics ([link](https://invent.psu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/transcript-Developing-the-future-of-AI-with-poolside_edited.pdf)).\n- **Changelog #395 (2020):** how GitHub positioned itself for the Microsoft sale ([link](https://changelog.com/podcast/395)).\n- **Stack Overflow Podcast (2022):** why an operator became a VC ([link](https://stackoverflow.blog/2022/05/31/jason-warner-cto-github-redpoint-ventures-web3)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X (@jasoncwarner) was not crawled.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Alchemy | Undisclosed (his first Redpoint investment) | 2021-22 | Redpoint (his deal) | | [src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1422892/10059714/transcript) |\n| Etched | Series A ($120M) | 2024-06-25 | no (angel) | Primary Venture Partners; Positive Sum | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware) |\n| Temporal, Census, Hex, Tinybird, Common Room, Meroxa, Railway, Supabase, Rewatch, Sym, Endgame, Stytch, Meticulous, GraphCDN, Infra, Drata | angel (rounds undisclosed) | pre-2022 | no | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/author/jason-warner/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **poolside (2017 to 2023).**\n  - In 2017, as GitHub CTO, he tried to buy Kant's source{d}. It failed, and they became friends.\n  - In 2023 they decided to \"go in\" rather than sit on the sidelines.\n  - Before taking any money they nearly signed a 49/51 compute-for-equity deal with a smaller hyperscaler. The company's name came out of that meeting.\n  - Redpoint, his own firm, led the seed ([src](https://invent.psu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/transcript-Developing-the-future-of-AI-with-poolside_edited.pdf); [src](https://chainofthought.show/podcast/23-first-code-then-agi-softwares-event-horizon-with-poolside-founders-jason-warner-and-eiso-k/); [src](https://medium.com/redpoint-ventures/fresh-ink-hello-poolside-384923ff3ad4)).\n- **GitHub to Microsoft (2017-18).**\n  - At Heroku he had drafted a plan for Salesforce to buy GitHub. He took that plan to GitHub with \"get acquisition offer\" as a goal.\n  - GitHub skipped a full CI product for Actions and pitched every major cloud on being an end-to-end platform.\n  - The offer came the first week of April 2018 ([src](https://changelog.com/podcast/395)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Eiso Kant | poolside co-founder; met on a failed acquisition | 2017 | [src](https://invent.psu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/transcript-Developing-the-future-of-AI-with-poolside_edited.pdf) |\n| Nat Friedman | GitHub CEO while Warner was CTO; fellow dev-tools angel | 2018 | [src](https://moderncto.io/231-jason-warner-cto-at-github/) |\n| Redpoint Ventures | Managing Director 2021-07 to 2023-06; led poolside seed | 2021 | [src](https://www.forbes.com/sites/alexkonrad/2021/07/08/github-cto-jason-warner-joins-redpoint-new-725-million-growth-fund/) |\n| Jacob Effron | Redpoint colleague; co-guest on the AI episode (Feb 2023) | 2023 | [src](https://theloganbartlettshow.simplecast.com/episodes/ep-52-jason-warner-jacob-effron-redpoint-ventures-former-github-cto-explains-ai-for-dummies-0L6hPeGY) |\n| Microsoft | acquirer of GitHub; he stayed post-close | 2018 | [src](https://changelog.com/podcast/395) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Which hyperscaler** offered the 49/51 deal? Check whether it later invested.\n- **Logan Bartlett EP 52 (Feb 2023).** What did he and Effron say about AI investing just before poolside? No transcript was reachable.\n- **Launch House \"From GitHub CTO to VC & Web3\"** ([link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CNTDuceaQM4)): 78 minutes on his VC thesis. Not mined, because YouTube blocked the transcript fetch.\n- **Redpoint deals beyond Alchemy.** Which other deals did he personally lead at Redpoint in 2021-23?\n\n## Sources\n- **Podcast transcripts and interviews:**\n  - https://www.buzzsprout.com/1422892/10059714/transcript\n  - https://stackoverflow.blog/2022/05/31/jason-warner-cto-github-redpoint-ventures-web3\n  - https://changelog.com/podcast/395\n  - https://moderncto.io/231-jason-warner-cto-at-github/\n  - https://www-webflow.osohq.com/post/developer-den-with-jason-warner\n  - https://invent.psu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/transcript-Developing-the-future-of-AI-with-poolside_edited.pdf\n  - https://chainofthought.show/podcast/23-first-code-then-agi-softwares-event-horizon-with-poolside-founders-jason-warner-and-eiso-k/\n- **Bio, press and deal sources:**\n  - https://techcrunch.com/author/jason-warner/\n  - https://www.forbes.com/sites/alexkonrad/2021/07/08/github-cto-jason-warner-joins-redpoint-new-725-million-growth-fund/\n  - https://medium.com/redpoint-ventures/fresh-ink-hello-poolside-384923ff3ad4\n  - https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware\n  - https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/10/poolside-ceo-says-most-companies-shouldnt-build-foundation-models/\n- **Full media list:** `data/rows/jason-warner-voice-media.csv`\n", "person:jaya-gupta": "\n# Jaya Gupta\n\n## One-line take\nThey are a thesis-first enterprise-AI partner whose essays (service-as-software, context graphs) come before Foundation's deals. They sourced Docket cold and Fulcrum through McKinsey; Foundation led Fulcrum's seed at zero customers. Worth following for AI-native automation deal flow, not for labs or compute.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win.** They write the frame and then back the founders who fit it. The context-graphs essay with Ashu Garg named Maximor and PlayerZero as examples, then brought in \"100s of pitches\" ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/context-graphs-one-month-in)). Their sourcing is personal and early. They pushed for a meeting with a founder who wasn't raising (Docket), and they backed a former McKinsey colleague before Fulcrum had customers.\n- **Constraints.** Every visible deal is co-signed by a GP (Ashu Garg or Joanne Chen). Their lane is Foundation's enterprise application layer. No pre-training, lab or chip deal is attributed to them.\n- **What they wouldn't do.** Their own thesis says not to build where model providers will \"eat everything\" ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/when-model-providers-eat-everything-a-survival-guide-for-service-as-software-startups)). They favour workflow-owning apps with proprietary feedback loops over thin wrappers. That is an inference from their writing, not a stated rule.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Docket was a cold, founder-not-raising pursuit.** Arjun Pillai had left ZoomInfo two days earlier and wasn't looking for VC money. Jaya wrote: \"I insisted on setting up a meeting\". Foundation then made a $5M seed investment, and the whole process took 20 days ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/docket-from-idea-to-investment-in-20-days)). The post doesn't say who else was in the seed. The $15M A followed in 2024-07.\n- **Fulcrum came through McKinsey, with no customers.** \"I first met Arjun Mangla when we worked together at McKinsey.\" Foundation led the seed when \"the company had zero customers\" ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-context-graph-for-insurance-why-we’re-doubling-down-on-fulcrum)). CRV led the next round about two years later, which makes Foundation roughly two years ahead of CRV.\n- **Maximor is now Hyphenate, with a $45M A.** A Foundation founder profile says \"Hyphenate (formerly Maximor)\". It also says Foundation \"followed on its seed check\" in a $45M Series A ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/always-audit-ready-how-hyphenate-s-founders-are-teaching-ai-to-run-finance)). The seed was 2025-09, so the step-up took about a year or less (the A's date is not confirmed).\n- **Maximor's seed was decided over dinner.** Ashu Garg wrote: \"Six hours later, Jaya and I were struck\" ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-always-on-finance-team-our-investment-in-maximor)).\n- **The context-graphs essay spread inside the labs.** The authors say \"the piece was circulating in company Slack channels as a must-read\" at OpenAI and Anthropic. This is relayed second-hand by the authors ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/context-graphs-one-month-in)). Aaron Levie amplified it on X ([src](https://x.com/levie/status/2007958155137876183)).\n- **PlayerZero diligence ran through a research-advisor reference.** Foundation checked with Matei Zaharia, who called founder Animesh Koratana \"one of his best undergraduate students ever.\" ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/leading-playerzero-s-series-a)).\n- **Core thesis line:** \"Enterprise systems were built to record end state, not reasoning.\" ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/the-compounding-loop-enterprise-software-never-had)).\n- **Podcast host with lab researchers.** They host Foundation's *AI in the Real World*. Guests have included Anthropic's Rohan Taori, Cartesia's Karan Goel and Stanford's Avanika Narayan and Jon Saad-Falcon (intelligence per watt, local AI). Jaya asked about the labs: \"What lock-in do they have right now?\" ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/maximizing-intelligence-per-watt-avanika-narayan-jon-saad-falcon-stanford-ai-cs-ph.d.s)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, on the THESIS rubric (AI-native automation at seed/Series A), with a co-investability caveat.** They are a rising early-stage investor who backs companies at seed, sometimes with no customers. Their deals are AI-native automation: Maximor/Hyphenate (finance close), PlayerZero (production engineering), Fulcrum (insurance policy checking) and Docket (sales engineering). The caveat: they are application-layer, not frontier labs, pre-training or compute. Foundation's check sizes ($5M–$15M rounds) are far below my ~$20M mega-seed checks. They are useful as a **co-investor and deal-flow node for Serval-like automation companies**, and as a link to Stanford research talent through the podcast. They are not a source of lab deals. Deals that matter to me: Hyphenate/Maximor, PlayerZero, Fulcrum, Docket.\n\n## Background\n- Started building at 19, including a startup that failed. Joined a startup while at Georgia Tech, which was later acquired. Has worked at two startups, both acquired. Degree not listed. ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/team-members/jaya-gupta))\n- McKinsey technology practice: software diligences and go-to-market work for startups. Dates not found. ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/team-members/jaya-gupta))\n- Joined Foundation Capital as chief of staff (portfolio operations and strategy), then moved to working directly with founders. Now a Partner in Enterprise. Promotion dates not found. ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/team-members/jaya-gupta))\n- Founded Give a Birthday, a nonprofit. Builds community across the IIT, Berkeley and Georgia Tech networks. ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/team-members/jaya-gupta))\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://foundationcapital.com/team-members/jaya-gupta | Firm bio |\n| Substack / blog | https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/all | About 27 co-authored Foundation \"Ideas\" pieces, mostly with Ashu Garg or Joanne Chen (as of 2026-11-10) |\n| X | nothing confirmed | x.com fetch blocked; handle not verified |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Foundation Capital *AI in the Real World* | e.g. [Rohan Taori](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-next-generation-of-ai-models-rohan-taori-researcher-at-anthropic-alpaca-co-creator), [intelligence per watt](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/maximizing-intelligence-per-watt-avanika-narayan-jon-saad-falcon-stanford-ai-cs-ph.d.s) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Date not shown — *AI in the Real World* — \"Maximizing intelligence per watt\" with Avanika Narayan and Jon Saad-Falcon (Stanford) — [link](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/maximizing-intelligence-per-watt-avanika-narayan-jon-saad-falcon-stanford-ai-cs-ph.d.s) — They probed lab lock-in (\"What lock-in do they have right now?\") and noted that capital buildout assumes centralized cloud inference.\n- Date not shown — *AI in the Real World* — Rohan Taori (Anthropic multimodal pre-training, Alpaca) — [link](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-next-generation-of-ai-models-rohan-taori-researcher-at-anthropic-alpaca-co-creator) — Show notes only, no transcript.\n- Date not shown — *AI in the Real World* — Karan Goel (Cartesia), state space models — [link](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/the-promise-of-state-space-models---karan-goel-(co-founder-ceo-of-cartesia)) — not read.\n- Date not shown — \"The case for context graphs\" with Aaron Levie (Box) — [link](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/the-case-for-context-graphs) — not read.\n- Portfolio-founder episodes: Arjun Mangla (Fulcrum), Ajay Krishna (Maximor) — listed on [ideas index](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/all).\n\n## How they think\n- **Service-as-software.** AI sells completed work rather than seats. This is a series of essays with Ashu Garg and Joanne Chen ([index](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/all)).\n- **Context graphs and decision traces.** Agents sit in the execution path, so they can record *why* a decision was made. That record becomes a new system of record, and incumbents that store only end state are badly placed to build it ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/context-graphs-ais-trillion-dollar-opportunity), [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/the-compounding-loop-enterprise-software-never-had)).\n- **Contrarian view.** As model providers move up the stack, legacy data stockpiles matter less. Proprietary RL and feedback loops in high-exception workflows are the moat: \"the highest leverage asset isn’t a legacy dataset\" ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/when-model-providers-eat-everything-a-survival-guide-for-service-as-software-startups)).\n- **AgentSRE.** AIOps is a misnomer. Automating SRE work is put at $100B+, and the observability thesis came before the PlayerZero A ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/goodbye-aiops-welcome-agentsres-the-next-100b-opportunity); authorship on page unclear).\n- **Calls so far.** Maximor went from seed to a $45M A as Hyphenate, and Fulcrum got a CRV-led A. Both look right early. No misses found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [AI's trillion-dollar opportunity: Context graphs](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/context-graphs-ais-trillion-dollar-opportunity): the viral frame behind their deal flow.\n2. [When model providers eat everything](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/when-model-providers-eat-everything-a-survival-guide-for-service-as-software-startups): how they underwrite app-layer survival against the labs.\n3. [Docket: from idea to investment in 20 days](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/docket-from-idea-to-investment-in-20-days): their sourcing style, in their own words.\n4. [Building the context graph for insurance (Fulcrum)](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-context-graph-for-insurance-why-we’re-doubling-down-on-fulcrum): pre-customer conviction through a McKinsey network.\n5. [Context graphs: one month in](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/context-graphs-one-month-in): reception, critics, and which companies they see as building it.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing confirmed. Their X profile could not be fetched (HTTP 402), and web search was unavailable this run. Known amplifiers of their writing: Aaron Levie ([src](https://x.com/levie/status/2007958155137876183)), and Dharmesh Shah and Arvind Jain, both quoted in [one month in](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/context-graphs-one-month-in).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Docket | Seed ($5M) | c. 2023 (per post) | lead not stated; Foundation invested, Jaya sourced | not named | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/docket-from-idea-to-investment-in-20-days) |\n| Docket | Series A ($15M) | 2024-07 | not stated | not found | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/docket-from-idea-to-investment-in-20-days) |\n| Fulcrum | Seed | c. 2024 (\"two years ago\") | yes (Foundation) | not found | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-context-graph-for-insurance-why-we’re-doubling-down-on-fulcrum) |\n| Fulcrum | Series A ($25M total raised per post) | 2026-01 | no, CRV led | CRV | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-context-graph-for-insurance-why-we’re-doubling-down-on-fulcrum) |\n| PlayerZero | Series A ($15M) | 2025-07 | Foundation led; Jaya co-authored the post (personal co-lead unconfirmed) | not named | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/leading-playerzero-s-series-a) |\n| Maximor (now Hyphenate) | Seed ($9M) | 2025-09 | yes (co-led with Ashu Garg) | Gaia Ventures; Boldcap | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-always-on-finance-team-our-investment-in-maximor), [src](https://fintech.global/2025/09/30/maximor-raises-9m-to-expand-ai-finance-automation/) |\n| Hyphenate (ex-Maximor) | Series A ($45M) | 2026 (date not stated) | no, follow-on | lead not stated | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/always-audit-ready-how-hyphenate-s-founders-are-teaching-ai-to-run-finance) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Docket.** Jaya admired Arjun Pillai's work and reached out about a Foundation event, two days after he left ZoomInfo to start his third company. He wasn't raising. They insisted on meeting. Ashu Garg then met him in a park near Bangalore for four hours. Foundation committed a $5M seed within 20 days of the start of the process ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/docket-from-idea-to-investment-in-20-days)). Who led the $15M A is not stated in the post.\n- **Fulcrum.** Jaya and Arjun Mangla were colleagues at McKinsey. Foundation led the seed at zero customers on founder conviction. Two years later CRV led the next round, and the post (with Joanne Chen) frames it as \"doubling down\" ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-context-graph-for-insurance-why-we’re-doubling-down-on-fulcrum)).\n- **Maximor/Hyphenate.** A planned short meeting with Ram Krishnamurthy and Ajay Krishna Amudan (both IIT Madras, then more than a decade at Microsoft) ran to a six-hour dinner. Foundation became \"day-zero\" partners and led the $9M seed ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-always-on-finance-team-our-investment-in-maximor)). The company later renamed itself Hyphenate and raised a $45M A, in which Foundation followed on ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/always-audit-ready-how-hyphenate-s-founders-are-teaching-ai-to-run-finance)).\n- **PlayerZero.** Foundation had backed Animesh Koratana \"from PlayerZero's earliest stage\" and led the $15M A. Diligence included Matei Zaharia, Koratana's Stanford DAWN advisor ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/leading-playerzero-s-series-a)). Jaya's role at the earliest stage is not confirmed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Foundation Capital | Partner, Enterprise (joined as chief of staff) | not found | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/team-members/jaya-gupta) |\n| Ashu Garg | Co-author and co-lead (context graphs, Maximor, PlayerZero, Docket) | — | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/context-graphs-ais-trillion-dollar-opportunity) |\n| Joanne Chen | Co-author (Fulcrum, service-as-software) | — | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-context-graph-for-insurance-why-we’re-doubling-down-on-fulcrum) |\n| Arjun Mangla (Fulcrum) | Former McKinsey colleague; investor | McKinsey era | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-context-graph-for-insurance-why-we’re-doubling-down-on-fulcrum) |\n| Arjun Pillai (Docket) | Sourced founder | c. 2023 | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/docket-from-idea-to-investment-in-20-days) |\n| Ram Krishnamurthy and Ajay Krishna Amudan (Hyphenate) | Seed co-lead | 2025 | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-always-on-finance-team-our-investment-in-maximor) |\n| Animesh Koratana (PlayerZero) | Co-authored Foundation's Series A post (Foundation led) | 2025 | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/leading-playerzero-s-series-a) |\n| CRV | Followed Foundation into Fulcrum (A lead) | 2026-01 | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-context-graph-for-insurance-why-we’re-doubling-down-on-fulcrum) |\n| Rohan Taori, Karan Goel, Avanika Narayan, Jon Saad-Falcon | Podcast guests (*AI in the Real World*) | — | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/all) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Research limits for this run: web search budget was exhausted and X returned 402. Nearly all insight here comes from Foundation's own site, so differential insight from outside sources is thin. The Maximor six-hour-dinner quote was taken from existing foundation-capital rows, not re-fetched.\n- Whether Foundation was the first institutional investor in Docket.\n- Their X handle and audience size, and whether the context-graphs launch was driven by their own X post. X was blocked, and the search budget was exhausted this run.\n- Who led Hyphenate's $45M A, and when it happened.\n- Who led Docket's $15M A, and whether Foundation's $5M was a standalone seed.\n- Whether they sourced or merely co-led PlayerZero, given Foundation's earlier involvement.\n- Whether they have any investments in research-heavy or infrastructure companies (e.g. from the Stanford intelligence-per-watt and Hazy Research circle, or Cartesia).\n- Dates of the move from chief of staff to Partner, and dates at McKinsey.\n\n## Sources\n- https://foundationcapital.com/team-members/jaya-gupta\n- https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/all\n- https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/docket-from-idea-to-investment-in-20-days\n- https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-context-graph-for-insurance-why-we’re-doubling-down-on-fulcrum\n- https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/context-graphs-ais-trillion-dollar-opportunity\n- https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/context-graphs-one-month-in\n- https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/the-compounding-loop-enterprise-software-never-had\n- https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/when-model-providers-eat-everything-a-survival-guide-for-service-as-software-startups\n- https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/leading-playerzero-s-series-a\n- https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-always-on-finance-team-our-investment-in-maximor\n- https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/always-audit-ready-how-hyphenate-s-founders-are-teaching-ai-to-run-finance\n- https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/goodbye-aiops-welcome-agentsres-the-next-100b-opportunity\n- https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/maximizing-intelligence-per-watt-avanika-narayan-jon-saad-falcon-stanford-ai-cs-ph.d.s\n- https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-next-generation-of-ai-models-rohan-taori-researcher-at-anthropic-alpaca-co-creator\n- https://fintech.global/2025/09/30/maximor-raises-9m-to-expand-ai-finance-automation/ (secondary)\n- https://x.com/levie/status/2007958155137876183\n", "person:jazmin-medina": "\n# Jazmin Medina\n\n*Created 2026-11-10 (faceless-investor people pass). Sources: the DESV team page, the ASU Foundation board page, an archived NewView Capital homepage (2019 to 2020), Paystand's archived board page, TechCrunch, Veryfi's own announcement, and a Topia SEC Form D. Web search was exhausted for this run, so every page below was fetched directly.*\n\n## One-line take\nShe holds the most senior investing title on D. E. Shaw Ventures' team page, but no source names her on any DESV deal, including OpenAI and Anthropic. Her only sourced lead deals are two NewView Capital fintech and ops-software growth rounds, Paystand (2021) and Veryfi (2021), where she took the board seat ([src](https://ventures.deshaw.com/team), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/07/23/paystand-banks-50m-to-make-b2b-payments-cashless-and-with-no-fees/), [src](https://www.veryfi.com/news/series-a-nvc/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** she is a growth-stage board operator who came up through a secondary-style fund. NewView Capital was formed by buying a slice of NEA's late-stage portfolio in a direct sale, and Medina was one of its first two Principals ([src](http://web.archive.org/web/20190302075628/https://newviewcap.com/), [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20200426132742/https://newviewcap.com/)). There she both inherited board seats (Topia, by May 2020) and led new fintech growth rounds (Veryfi Series A, Paystand Series C) ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1813040/000181304020000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **What constrains her:** at DESV her bio describes her role in the same words as the three Investment Leads' bios. Each \"contributes to the research and analysis of investments in emerging private companies\" ([src](https://ventures.deshaw.com/team)). The firm's own text gives the Partner no stated sign-off authority. Capital allocation sits with Executive Committee member Edwin Jager and the group's Risk Committee (see [firms/d-e-shaw-ventures.md](../firms/d-e-shaw-ventures.md)).\n- **What she'd never do (inferred):** seed or pre-seed. DESV invests \"after seed funding\" and her own record is Series A and later ([src](https://ventures.deshaw.com/)).\n\n## Differential insight\nRows mirrored in `data/rows/jazmin-medina-insights.csv`.\n- **She was a Topia director before she led any new deal.** Topia's Form D (filed 2020-05-26; $11.2M sold) lists \"Jazmin Medina\" as a director at NewView's Burlingame address. Topia was not a NewView-led round in any press I found. This fits NewView's model of buying NEA's portfolio tail and taking its board seats ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1813040/000181304020000001/primary_doc.xml)). Whether NewView inherited the Topia stake from NEA is **unconfirmed**.\n- **Her one AI-adjacent deal was document AI, 2021.** NewView led Veryfi's $12M Series A, and \"Jazmin Medina of NewView Capital will be joining the Veryfi board\" ([src](https://www.veryfi.com/news/series-a-nvc/)). The page shows a 2023-03-28 byline, but the page's metadata says it was published 2021-04-26, and the Wayback Machine first captured it on 2021-05-07. So the round is April 2021.\n- **Her stated fintech view (2021):** \"There is a wide blue ocean of the payment industry\". She said NewView, though a generalist, was \"rooted in fintech and fintech infrastructure\" (TechCrunch paraphrase) ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/07/23/paystand-banks-50m-to-make-b2b-payments-cashless-and-with-no-fees/)).\n- **Stale board listing.** Paystand's board page still listed her as \"Principal at NewView Capital focusing on Fintech\" in the Wayback capture of 2026-10-15, two years after she moved to DESV ([src](http://web.archive.org/web/20261015210048/https://www.paystand.com/about)). Her ASU Foundation bio uses the past tense instead: she \"previously served on several financial technology and consumer software startup boards including Paystand, Veryfi, Hearsay Systems and Topia\" ([src](https://www.asufoundation.org/boards.html)). The Paystand listing is most likely stale, which means she gave up her NewView boards when she moved to DESV.\n- **Diversity-capital network.** She sits on the investment committee of the Cap Table Coalition, which creates allocation access for Black, Latinx and underrepresented investors. She also serves on the HBS Alumni Board and HBS Global Advisory Board ([src](https://www.asufoundation.org/boards.html)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** DESV is post-seed and growth, and its frontier-lab cheques (OpenAI 2026 co-lead, Anthropic Series G co-lead) carry no named individual. Her own record is fintech and SaaS growth (Paystand, Topia, Hearsay) plus one document-AI Series A (Veryfi). Nothing ties her to pre-training, labs, chips or AI-native automation at seed. She may still be useful as a **co-investor contact for later rounds** of my seed companies, since DESV has deep, partly house-money pockets. She could also be a route into DESV's pipeline for growth-stage AI rounds, but no source shows she sourced any of DESV's AI deals.\n\n## Background\n- B.S. finance, Arizona State University W. P. Carey School of Business, 2009. Inducted into the W. P. Carey Hall of Fame. Past member of the ASU Foundation Next Generation Council; now on the ASU Foundation board of directors ([src](https://www.asufoundation.org/boards.html)).\n- Goldman Sachs, New York: investment banking (financial institutions and health care coverage). Paystand's bio adds that she worked for the Chief of Staff to the CEO ([src](https://ventures.deshaw.com/team), [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20230602223435/https://www.paystand.com/about)). Dates not given.\n- Advised C-level executives on product and go-to-market at Warner Bros and Jaunt (dates not given) ([src](http://web.archive.org/web/20230602223435/https://www.paystand.com/about)).\n- Co-founder and COO, Bundler TV, a media-tech and subscription-management startup in Los Angeles (dates not given) ([src](https://www.asufoundation.org/boards.html)).\n- MBA, Harvard Business School, 2015 ([src](https://www.asufoundation.org/boards.html)).\n- NewView Capital, Principal: listed on the team page from the first capture (2019-03-02) through 2020-04. DESV calls her a \"founding member of the investment team\", focused on consumer, fintech and SaaS ([src](http://web.archive.org/web/20190302075628/https://newviewcap.com/), [src](https://ventures.deshaw.com/team)). TechCrunch still gives her title as Principal in 2021-07. No later promotion found.\n- 2023: San Francisco Business Times 40 Under 40, Venture Capital Journal Rising Stars ([src](https://www.asufoundation.org/boards.html)).\n- 2024: joined the D. E. Shaw group as Investment Partner, DESV, in San Francisco ([src](https://ventures.deshaw.com/team)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://ventures.deshaw.com/team | DESV team bio (verified 2026-11-10); firm inbox ventures@deshaw.com, 150 California St, San Francisco |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | not searched (search budget exhausted) |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | Paystand's archived page links a LinkedIn profile; URL not checked |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| Boards | https://www.asufoundation.org/boards.html | ASU Foundation board of directors; HBS Alumni Board |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found (web search was unavailable this run; not ruled out).\n\n## How they think\n- Generalist on paper, fintech infrastructure in practice: \"rooted in fintech and fintech infrastructure\" (TechCrunch paraphrase, 2021) ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/07/23/paystand-banks-50m-to-make-b2b-payments-cashless-and-with-no-fees/)).\n- Backs digitisation of lagging back-office categories: B2B payments (Paystand) and unstructured-document extraction (Veryfi). In her words, a company has a \"glaring hole\" if it isn't digital ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/07/23/paystand-banks-50m-to-make-b2b-payments-cashless-and-with-no-fees/)).\n- Calls right or wrong: nothing sourced.\n\n## Read these first\n- TechCrunch on Paystand's Series C (2021): her only on-record investment comments ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/07/23/paystand-banks-50m-to-make-b2b-payments-cashless-and-with-no-fees/)).\n- Veryfi Series A announcement (2021) ([src](https://www.veryfi.com/news/series-a-nvc/)).\n- ASU Foundation board bio: the fullest list of her boards and affiliations ([src](https://www.asufoundation.org/boards.html)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Topia | Undisclosed (Form D, $11.2M sold) | 2020-05 | unknown; she is listed as director | not disclosed | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1813040/000181304020000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Veryfi | Series A ($12M) | 2021-04-26 | yes (NewView led; she joined the board) | TI Platform; Willard Family; Act One Ventures | [src](https://www.veryfi.com/news/series-a-nvc/) |\n| Paystand | Series C ($50M) | 2021-07-23 | yes (NewView led; she joined the board) | SoftBank SB Opportunity Fund; King River Capital | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/07/23/paystand-banks-50m-to-make-b2b-payments-cashless-and-with-no-fees/) |\n| Hearsay Systems | board seat only (round unknown) | unknown | unknown | | [src](https://www.asufoundation.org/boards.html) (unconfirmed elsewhere) |\n| D. E. Shaw Ventures book (OpenAI, Anthropic, Baseten, ElevenLabs, etc.) | none named | 2024 to 2026 | n/a | | no source names her ([firm page](../firms/d-e-shaw-ventures.md)) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Paystand Series C (2021-07):** NewView led the $50M round, and Medina took the board seat. She told TechCrunch she had \"strong conviction\" in CEO Jeremy Almond's mid-market B2B payments thesis ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/07/23/paystand-banks-50m-to-make-b2b-payments-cashless-and-with-no-fees/)). First contact and intro path are not sourced.\n- **Veryfi Series A (2021-04):** NewView led the round, with existing investor Act One Ventures, and Medina joined the board ([src](https://www.veryfi.com/news/series-a-nvc/)). Intro path not sourced.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| D. E. Shaw Ventures | at: Investment Partner | 2024 | [src](https://ventures.deshaw.com/team) |\n| NewView Capital | at: Principal (founding investment team) | by 2019-03, until 2024 | [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20190302075628/https://newviewcap.com/) |\n| Bundler TV | at: co-founder and COO | unknown | [src](https://www.asufoundation.org/boards.html) |\n| Goldman Sachs | at: investment banking | ~2009 (unconfirmed) | [src](https://ventures.deshaw.com/team) |\n| Paystand | board director (past per ASU bio) | 2021-07 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/07/23/paystand-banks-50m-to-make-b2b-payments-cashless-and-with-no-fees/) |\n| Veryfi | board director (past per ASU bio) | 2021-04 | [src](https://www.veryfi.com/news/series-a-nvc/) |\n| Topia | board director (past per ASU bio) | by 2020-05 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1813040/000181304020000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Hearsay Systems | past board director (ASU bio only) | unknown | [src](https://www.asufoundation.org/boards.html) |\n| Cap Table Coalition | investment committee member | unknown | [src](https://www.asufoundation.org/boards.html) |\n| Edwin Jager | reports into his unit (Fundamental Equities) | 2024 | [src](https://ventures.deshaw.com/team) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did she source or work on any DESV AI deal (Baseten, ElevenLabs, Physical Intelligence, Snorkel AI)? No source says so.\n- Is she on DESV's investment committee, or does Jager or the Risk Committee approve alone?\n- Her ASU bio says she \"previously served\" on the Paystand, Veryfi, Hearsay and Topia boards. When each seat ended is not dated.\n- Was Hearsay a seat inherited from NEA's portfolio via the NewView spin-out?\n\n## Sources\n- https://ventures.deshaw.com/team ; https://ventures.deshaw.com/\n- https://www.asufoundation.org/boards.html\n- http://web.archive.org/web/20190302075628/https://newviewcap.com/ ; http://web.archive.org/web/20200426132742/https://newviewcap.com/\n- http://web.archive.org/web/20230602223435/https://www.paystand.com/about ; http://web.archive.org/web/20261015210048/https://www.paystand.com/about\n- https://techcrunch.com/2021/07/23/paystand-banks-50m-to-make-b2b-payments-cashless-and-with-no-fees/\n- https://www.veryfi.com/news/series-a-nvc/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1813040/000181304020000001/primary_doc.xml\n", "person:jeff-bezos": "\n# Jeff Bezos\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the largest individual cheque in new-lab seeds: when a research team raises $300M or more at founding, his name is usually in the first round, either personally or as Bezos Expeditions.\n\n## Crux\nHe wins because he can write a nine-figure cheque at seed without a fund's ownership maths, and founders get his name without a board-driven investor. He says openly that AI is a bubble and funds it anyway on a portfolio argument: \"the good ideas will pay for all of the losers.\" [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/02/jeff-bezos-family-office-backed-five-ai-startups-in-june.html). The constraint is attention, not money: since November 2025 he is co-CEO of his own lab, Project Prometheus, which competes for the same researchers and investors as the labs he backs. He does not set terms the way a venture lead does; he joins rounds assembled by Lux, Thrive, Khosla or the founders.\n\n## Differential insight\n- CNBC on Fintrx data: five direct deals in June 2026 alone and \"eight direct investments in private companies\" in 2026, the most of any family office tracked [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/02/jeff-bezos-family-office-backed-five-ai-startups-in-june.html).\n- Press releases alternate between \"Jeff Bezos\" and \"Bezos Expeditions\" for the same office. The ledger had Flourish as a personal angel cheque; CNBC says Bezos Expeditions co-led it, and co-led CuspAI too [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/02/jeff-bezos-family-office-backed-five-ai-startups-in-june.html).\n- Prometheus in his words is about shortening the path from \"dream to manufacturing at rate\" [src](https://letsdatascience.com/news/bezos-expeditions-backs-five-ai-startups-in-june-b3af9cfa); his outside cheques sit in the same physical-AI chain (robot models, chips, materials, world models).\n- On the bubble: \"This is real, the benefits to society from AI are going to be gigantic.\" [src](https://www.entrepreneur.com/business-news/amazon-founder-jeff-bezos-says-were-in-an-ai-bubble/497930).\n- Filing-grade holders are single-member LLCs (Explore Holdings, Explore Investments), per the existing [lps/bezos-expeditions.md](../lps/bezos-expeditions.md).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. He backs exactly the deals in my lens (new labs, AI chips, physical AI at seed), but he is a co-investor to expect in a round, not a peer or a source of allocation. Deals that matter: Periodic Labs seed, Unconventional AI seed, humans& seed, AMI Labs seed, Physical Intelligence A, General Intuition A.\n\n## Background\nFounded Amazon in 1994; stepped down as CEO in 2021 (not re-sourced here). Bezos Expeditions has been his family office since about 2006 (ledger rels). Co-founder and co-CEO of Project Prometheus with Vik Bajaj since November 2025 [src](https://letsdatascience.com/news/bezos-expeditions-backs-five-ai-startups-in-june-b3af9cfa).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.bezosexpeditions.com | portfolio page; not opened in this pass |\n| Substack / blog | none | |\n| X | https://x.com/JeffBezos | not checked for cadence |\n| LinkedIn | none found | |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-12 — Lex Fridman Podcast #405 — Jeff Bezos: Amazon and Blue Origin — https://lexfridman.com/jeff-bezos/ — transcript exists; not mined\n- 2025-10 — Italian Tech Week, Turin — fireside — [src](https://www.entrepreneur.com/business-news/amazon-founder-jeff-bezos-says-were-in-an-ai-bubble/497930) — called AI an \"industrial bubble\" whose benefits are real\n- 2026-07 — CNBC report on his family office — [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/02/jeff-bezos-family-office-backed-five-ai-startups-in-june.html) — quotes him on winners paying for losers\n\n## How they think\n- Industrial bubbles are acceptable because the inventions survive the losses [src](https://www.entrepreneur.com/business-news/amazon-founder-jeff-bezos-says-were-in-an-ai-bubble/497930).\n- Fund many experiments; let winners pay [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/02/jeff-bezos-family-office-backed-five-ai-startups-in-june.html).\n- Physical AI and manufacturing are the centre of his own effort [src](https://letsdatascience.com/news/bezos-expeditions-backs-five-ai-startups-in-june-b3af9cfa).\n- No statement by him about angel investing as a practice was found.\n\n## Read these first\n- CNBC, 2026-07-02, on the June deals [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/02/jeff-bezos-family-office-backed-five-ai-startups-in-june.html).\n- Entrepreneur's report of the Italian Tech Week remarks [src](https://www.entrepreneur.com/business-news/amazon-founder-jeff-bezos-says-were-in-an-ai-bubble/497930).\n- Lex Fridman #405 transcript: https://lexfridman.com/jeff-bezos-transcript (not mined).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed in this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI cheques only. \"Personal\" means the release names him; \"BE\" means it names Bezos Expeditions. Seed-stage entries are marked. Sources for ledger rows are in `data/rounds.csv`; rows new in this pass carry a link.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Vicarious | Series B extension (personal) | 2014-04 | no | Yang, Friis, Benioff | ledger |\n| insitro | Series A launch (BE) | 2018-05 | no | | ledger |\n| Perplexity | Series B, B-1, E (BE) | 2024-01 to 2025-06 | no | IVP, NEA, NVIDIA | ledger |\n| Figure AI | Series B (BE) | 2024-02 | no | Microsoft, OpenAI, NVIDIA, Intel Capital | ledger |\n| Generalist | **Seed** (BE), then A and B | 2024-03 | no | NVIDIA, Boldstart, Spark | ledger |\n| Skild AI | Series A (BE), then C | 2024-07 | lead | Lightspeed, Coatue, SoftBank | ledger |\n| Contextual AI | Series A (BE) | 2024-08 | no | Greycroft, Lightspeed | ledger |\n| Physical Intelligence | Series A (personal), then B | 2024-11 | lead | Thrive, Lux | ledger |\n| Tenstorrent | Series D (BE) | 2024-12 | no | | ledger |\n| Toloka | Strategic (BE) | 2025-05 | lead | | ledger |\n| FieldAI | Growth (BE) | 2025-08 | no | Intel Capital, NVIDIA | ledger |\n| Periodic Labs | **Seed** (personal) | 2025-09 | no | a16z and others | ledger |\n| Project Prometheus | **Seed / launch round, $6.2B** (personal; co-founder) | 2025-11 | lead | | [src](https://letsdatascience.com/news/bezos-expeditions-backs-five-ai-startups-in-june-b3af9cfa) |\n| Profluent | Growth (BE) | 2025-11 | co-lead | Altimeter | ledger |\n| Archetype AI | Series A (BE) | 2025-11 | no | IAG, Hitachi Ventures | ledger |\n| Unconventional AI | **Seed** (personal) | 2025-12 | no | | ledger |\n| humans& | **Seed** (personal) | 2026-01 | no | NVIDIA, SV Angel, GV | ledger |\n| AMI Labs | **Seed, $1.03B** (BE) | 2026-03 | co-lead | | ledger |\n| Flourish | **Seed / inception raise** (ledger: personal; CNBC: BE co-led) | 2026 | co-lead | Lux | [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/02/jeff-bezos-family-office-backed-five-ai-startups-in-june.html) |\n| Project Prometheus | Series B, $12B at about $41B (BE) | 2026-06 | no | JPMorgan, BlackRock, Goldman Sachs, DST, Arch | [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/bezos-prometheus-ai-41-billion-valuation-12b-raise) |\n| General Intuition | Series A (personal) | 2026-06 | no | General Catalyst, Hillspire | ledger |\n| CuspAI | Series B (BE; CNBC says co-led) | 2026-07 | co-lead | UK Sovereign AI fund | [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/02/jeff-bezos-family-office-backed-five-ai-startups-in-june.html) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Physical Intelligence**: he, Thrive and Lux formed the Series A core and stayed in when CapitalG led the B (ledger insights). No account of who introduced him was found.\n- **Flourish**: built inside Lux; raised about $500M at inception with him as a backer (ledger insight from Lux's Q1 2026 report).\n- No founder quote on why they wanted his money was found in this pass.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bezos Expeditions | his family office; Melinda Lewison runs it day to day | 2006 | [lps/bezos-expeditions.md](../lps/bezos-expeditions.md) |\n| Vik Bajaj | co-CEO of Project Prometheus | 2025-11 | [src](https://letsdatascience.com/news/bezos-expeditions-backs-five-ai-startups-in-june-b3af9cfa) |\n| Lux Capital | repeat co-investor (Physical Intelligence, Flourish) | 2024-11 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report) |\n| Thrive Capital | repeat co-investor (Physical Intelligence) | 2024-11 | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=676543) |\n| NVIDIA | co-investor in six companies (Perplexity, Contextual, FieldAI, Figure, Generalist, Skild) | 2024 | ledger insight |\n| Coatue | co-anchor of its CTEK vehicle with Michael Dell's office | | ledger lp row |\n\n## Hit pattern\nEarly cheques that became hot: Generalist (seed 2024, $3B by August 2026), Skild (A lead 2024), Physical Intelligence (A lead 2024), Perplexity (B 2024). Public passes or regrets on AI deals: none found.\n\n## Open questions\n- Cheque size in any of the seed rounds.\n- Who at Bezos Expeditions sources the lab seeds (Lewison or others).\n- Any pre-2023 AI seed cheques beyond Vicarious and insitro; the office's own portfolio page was not read.\n- Whether Prometheus now limits what he will fund elsewhere.\n\n## Sources\nhttps://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/02/jeff-bezos-family-office-backed-five-ai-startups-in-june.html ; https://letsdatascience.com/news/bezos-expeditions-backs-five-ai-startups-in-june-b3af9cfa ; https://thenextweb.com/news/bezos-prometheus-ai-41-billion-valuation-12b-raise ; https://www.entrepreneur.com/business-news/amazon-founder-jeff-bezos-says-were-in-an-ai-bubble/497930 ; https://lexfridman.com/jeff-bezos/ ; ledger rows in data/rounds.csv and data/insights.csv\n", "person:jeff-dean": "\n# Jeff Dean\n\n## One-line take\nHis name on a seed cap table is the strongest technical signal an AI company can buy, and he gives it out often: 43 named AI companies in this repo, 30 of them entered at pre-seed or seed.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why founders want him:** signal, not money. A co-investor put it as \"an AI visionary on your cap table\" [src](https://fortune.com/2025/07/22/jeff-dean-googles-chief-scientist-is-quietly-betting-on-the-next-wave-of-ai-startups/). No source gives a cheque size, and he is never the lead.\n- **How he picks:** deeply technical teams, usually out of Google Brain, DeepMind, Berkeley or Stanford, in three buckets: AI tooling and infrastructure, LLM-era applications, and AI for science [src](https://fortune.com/2025/07/22/jeff-dean-googles-chief-scientist-is-quietly-betting-on-the-next-wave-of-ai-startups/).\n- **What changed:** until August 2026 he invested while being Google's chief scientist, including in companies that compete with Google. He then left to found Discovery Loop as CEO [src](https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop). He kept writing cheques afterwards (Mintive, October 2026) [src](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/agent-identity-startup-mintive-lands-7m-seed-from-cowboy-with-jeff-dean-and).\n- **What he would not do:** lead, price a round or take a board seat. Treat him as a marker of technical quality, not of deal terms.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Fortune counted 37 AI startups in about two years to July 2025; a Zetta partner said \"He's been just incredibly active\" [src](https://fortune.com/2025/07/22/jeff-dean-googles-chief-scientist-is-quietly-betting-on-the-next-wave-of-ai-startups/). CB Insights lists 95 investments in total (aggregator) [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/jeff-dean), so about half of the book is still unnamed here.\n- Zetta has done three bio+AI companies alongside him [src](https://fortune.com/2025/07/22/jeff-dean-googles-chief-scientist-is-quietly-betting-on-the-next-wave-of-ai-startups/).\n- Fortune called him \"surprisingly independent in his investing\": Perplexity competes with Google Search and he backed it while at Google [src](https://fortune.com/2025/07/22/jeff-dean-googles-chief-scientist-is-quietly-betting-on-the-next-wave-of-ai-startups/).\n- When he raised for his own company he picked Radical and Khosla as co-leads, the two firms whose rounds he most often joins as an angel (Radical: P-1 AI, Yutori, Emerald AI, Latent Labs; Khosla: lead on Basis, co-investor in Sakana). Discovery Loop says \"Our general approach is to automate the experimental loop\" [src](https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop).\n- He backs former Brain colleagues: Mintive's founders Jiquan Ngiam and Vijay Vasudevan are ex-Google Brain; the round was announced \"With Jeff Dean and Andrej Karpathy as Angels\" [src](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/agent-identity-startup-mintive-lands-7m-seed-from-cowboy-with-jeff-dean-and).\n- His test for a startup idea (YC interview, read only through a Chinese write-up, so low confidence): work where the best model succeeds 0-1% of the time, not 20% [src](https://www.36kr.com/p/3927354387544195).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. He is the cleanest example of a researcher-angel whose presence marks frontier technical talent at seed. Deals that matter to me: Periodic Labs, Flapping Airplanes, Sakana AI, Elorian AI, Trajectory (new labs), Mithril, Wafer, Standard Kernel, Architect Labs (compute and chips), Cursor, Harvey, Resolve AI (automation). He is also now a founder raising large rounds himself (Discovery Loop).\n\n## Background\n- Joined Google in 1999; co-founded Google Brain; later chief scientist across Google DeepMind and Google Research. Left in August 2026 after 27 years (summary source) [src](https://www.36kr.com/p/3927354387544195).\n- August 2026: co-founded Discovery Loop, a public benefit corporation, with Sanjay Ghemawat, Quoc Le and Oriol Vinyals; he is CEO. Radical and Khosla co-led; Lightspeed, Kleiner Perkins, Doerr Capital and Alphabet participated [src](https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop).\n- Earliest angel cheque found: Poplar Homes, 2016 (not AI) [src](https://fortune.com/2025/07/22/jeff-dean-googles-chief-scientist-is-quietly-betting-on-the-next-wave-of-ai-startups/).\n\n## Vehicle and cheque size\nPersonal cheques as far as any source shows. No fund, SPV, Form D or scout role found. Cheque size is not disclosed in any source read. He is also an LP in Laude Ventures' first fund per the existing pete-sonsini row in this repo.\n\n## How founders get to him\n- Former colleagues from Google Brain and Google Research (Mintive, Sakana, Ideogram, Periodic Labs founders).\n- Through lead investors who use him as technical validation: Radical (P-1 AI, Yutori, Emerald AI, Latent Labs), Zetta (three bio+AI companies), AIX Ventures (Marimo, Moonlake, Profluent, Ideogram).\n- No evidence of an application route or a scout.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://research.google/people/jeff/ | Google-era page |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop | Company blog |\n| X | https://x.com/JeffDean | Active; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | | not checked |\n| GitHub | | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-08 — Y Combinator — interview with Diana Hu — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CxXgV54KzpQ — advice to founders on picking domains models cannot yet do; not mined from the original, only via [36kr](https://www.36kr.com/p/3927354387544195)\n- 2026-08-05 — Discovery Loop blog — Announcing Discovery Loop — [link](https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop)\n- 2025-07-22 — Fortune Term Sheet — profile of his angel investing (he is not quoted) — [link](https://fortune.com/2025/07/22/jeff-dean-googles-chief-scientist-is-quietly-betting-on-the-next-wave-of-ai-startups/)\n\n## How they think\n- Automate the experimental loop of ML research first, then other sciences [src](https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop).\n- Look for problems where current models are near 0%, not 20% (summary source) [src](https://www.36kr.com/p/3927354387544195).\n- Calls right so far: Harvey seed (2022), Cursor Series A (2024), Perplexity, Sakana founding round, Periodic Labs. No public pass or regret found.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Fortune, 2025-07-22](https://fortune.com/2025/07/22/jeff-dean-googles-chief-scientist-is-quietly-betting-on-the-next-wave-of-ai-startups/): the only piece that counts and themes his cheques.\n- [Announcing Discovery Loop](https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop): what he is building and who he chose to fund it.\n- [36kr write-up of the YC interview](https://www.36kr.com/p/3927354387544195): his advice to founders (translation; verify against the video).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot mined this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n43 named AI companies; 30 entered at pre-seed or seed. Rows marked [aggregator] in the row files come from CB Insights or deal newsletters and need a primary source. Not in the table: Rosebud AI (angel per [company page](https://rosebud.ai/company), round not stated), Poplar Homes (2016, not AI), Discovery Loop (his own company). Unconfirmed: Cara, Copperhelm (search snippets only).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Role | Early (pre-seed/seed)? | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mintive | Seed ($7M) | 2026-10-29 | angel | yes | [src](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/agent-identity-startup-mintive-lands-7m-seed-from-cowboy-with-jeff-dean-and) |\n| Phinity | Seed ($5.2M) | 2026-10-08 | angel | yes | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/jeff-dean) |\n| The Biological Computing Co | Seed ($25M; second seed) | 2026-09-22 | angel | yes | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/jeff-dean) |\n| Wafer | Series A ($40M) | 2026-09-01 | angel | no | [src](https://pulse2.com/wafer-raises-40-million-series-a-to-scale-ai-inference-optimization-platform/) |\n| P-1 AI | Series A ($50M initial close) | 2026-07-29 | angel | no | [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026072916/engineering-ai-startup-p-1-ai-announces-its-series-a-financing-led-by-nea-adding-jeff-immelt-to-the-company-s-board/) |\n| SkyPilot | Seed ($20M+) | 2026-07-21 | angel | yes | [src](https://skypilot.ai/blog/skypilot-the-company) |\n| Runta | Seed | 2026-07-16 | angel | yes | [src](https://runta.com/blog/runta-the-execution-layer-for-agents/) |\n| Bespoke Labs | Seed ($8.25M) | 2026-07-06 | angel | yes | [src](https://www.bespokelabs.ai/blog/bespoke-labs-raises-40m-to-build-environments-that-enable-reliable-agents) |\n| Architect Labs | Seed | 2026-06-18 | angel | yes | [src](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/06/18/architect-labs-raises-24-million-to-take-on-broadcom-marvell-custom-chip-business) |\n| Trajectory | Seed ($15M) | 2026-05-29 | angel | yes | [src](https://fundraiseinsider.com/blog/trajectory-raises-15m-seed-to-build-continual-learning-platform/) |\n| Altara | Seed ($7M) | 2026-05 | angel | yes | [src](https://buttondown.com/AngelRegistry/archive/angel-registry-update-may-7-2026/) |\n| Elorian AI | Seed | 2026-04-09 | angel | yes | [src](https://elorian.ai/) |\n| Wafer | Seed ($4M) | 2026-04 | angel | yes | [src](https://cryptobriefing.com/wafer-raises-40m-200m-valuation/) |\n| Nomadic | Seed ($8.4M) | 2026-04 | angel | yes | [src](https://buttondown.com/AngelRegistry/archive/angel-registry-update-april-1-2026) |\n| Standard Kernel | Seed | 2026-03-11 | angel | yes | [src](https://standardkernel.com/blog/announcing-our-seed-round-is-kernel-generation-solved/) |\n| depthfirst | Series A ($40M) | 2026-01-14 | angel | no | [src](https://pulse2.com/depthfirst-40-million-series-a/) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Seed or Series A (not split by source) | 2026 | angel | yes | [src](https://flappingairplanes.com/) |\n| Edison Scientific | Seed | 2025-12-18 | angel | yes | [src](https://geo.sig.ai/brands/edison-scientific.md) |\n| Moonlake AI | Seed ($28M) | 2025-10-01 | angel | yes | [src](https://www.enterpriseaiworld.com/Articles/News/News/Moonlake-AI-Emerges-from-Stealth-with-%2428M-Seed-to-Support-Vibe-Coding-Interactive-Worlds-171928.aspx) |\n| Periodic Labs | Seed | 2025-09-30 | participant | yes | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/20/top-openai-google-brain-researchers-set-off-a-300m-vc-frenzy-for-their-startup-periodic-labs/) |\n| Sophont | Seed ($9.22M combined pre-seed + seed) | 2025-09-11 | angel | yes | [src](https://sophont.med/blog/seed_announcement) |\n| Emerald AI | Seed ($24.5M; launch from stealth) | 2025-07-01 | angel | yes | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/reimagining-data-centers-as-energy-assets/) |\n| P-1 AI | Seed ($23M) | 2025-04-28 | angel | yes | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-the-physical-world-with-artificial-general-engineering-intelligence/) |\n| Yutori | Seed ($15M) | 2025-03-27 | angel | yes | [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/yutori-launches-stealth-15m-seed-funding-build-consumer-ai-assistants) |\n| Latent Labs | Series A ($40M) | 2025-02-13 | angel | no | [src](https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/latent-labs-secures-50m-in-funding-to-realize-the-potential-of-ai-powered-programmable-biology) |\n| General Proximity Bio | Seed ($8M, oversubscribed) | 2024-12-18 | angel | yes | [src](https://generalproximity.bio/newsposts/general-proximity-de-stealths-with-16m-to-pioneer-next-gen-induced-proximity-medicines-for-undruggable-targets) |\n| Basis | Series A ($34M) | 2024-12-17 | angel | no | [src](https://www.getbasis.ai/blogs/basis-raises-a-34m-series-a-led-by-khosla-ventures) |\n| marimo | Seed ($5M; AIX Ventures lead) | 2024-11-19 | angel | yes | [src](https://marimo.io/blog/seed-announcement) |\n| Roboflow | Series B | 2024-11-19 | angel | no | [src](https://roboflow.com/blog/series-b) |\n| TollBit | Series A ($24M) | 2024-10-22 | angel | no | [src](https://finsmes.com/?p=338077) |\n| Resolve AI | Seed | 2024-10 | angel | yes | [src](https://resolve.ai/blog/introducing-resolve-ai) |\n| Letta | Seed ($10M) | 2024-09-23 | angel | yes | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/letta) |\n| Cursor | Series A | 2024-08-22 | participant | no | [src](https://cursor.com/blog/series-a) |\n| Odyssey AI | Seed ($9M; Air Street first check late 2023) | 2024-07-08 | angel | yes | [src](https://www.fenwick.com/insights/experience/fenwick-represents-odyssey-systems-in-9m-seed-funding) |\n| LiveKit | Series A ($22.5M at ~$110M) | 2024-06-04 | angel | no | [src](https://www.forbesargentina.com/innovacion/crean-sistema-nervioso-ia-su-startup-ya-vale-us-110-millones-quienes-son-hacen-n54253/amp) |\n| Mithril | Series A ($80M seed + A combined; ~$350M valuation) | 2024-03-21 | angel | no | [src](https://fortune.com/2024/03/21/foundry-stealth-launch-350-million-valuation/) |\n| Profluent | Series A ($35M; label inferred, release says 'additional funding') | 2024-03-21 | angel | no | [src](https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/profluent-secures-35m-in-additional-funding-and-key-industry-experts-to-scale-foundational-ai-models-for-biomedicine-and-tackle-first-vertical-in-gene-editing) |\n| Sakana AI | Seed | 2024-01-16 | angel | yes | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/our-investment-in-japans-sakana-ai-to-build-nature-inspired-ai-foundation-models) |\n| Axiom Bio | Seed ($8M, late 2024) | 2024 | angel | yes | [src](https://www.amplifypartners.com/blog-posts/eliminating-drug-toxicity-with-ai-our-investment-in-axiom) |\n| Ideogram | Seed ($16.5M) | 2023-08-22 | angel | yes | [src](https://finsmes.com/2023/08/ideogram-raises-usd16-5m-in-seed-funding.html) |\n| Harvey | Seed | 2022-11-23 | participant | yes | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2022/11/23/harvey-which-uses-ai-to-answer-legal-questions-lands-cash-from-openai/) |\n| Covariant | Undisclosed (seed or Series A; pre-B backers) | 2017-2019 | angel | no | [src](https://globalventuring.com/university/covariant-covets-40m) |\n| Workhelix | Undisclosed | n/d | angel | no | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/07/22/jeff-dean-googles-chief-scientist-is-quietly-betting-on-the-next-wave-of-ai-startups/) |\n| Perplexity | Undisclosed | n/d | angel | no | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/07/22/jeff-dean-googles-chief-scientist-is-quietly-betting-on-the-next-wave-of-ai-startups/) |\n| World Labs | Undisclosed | n/d | angel | no | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/07/22/jeff-dean-googles-chief-scientist-is-quietly-betting-on-the-next-wave-of-ai-startups/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Harvey (seed, November 2022):** in the OpenAI Startup Fund's $5M seed next to Elad Gil and Sarah Guo; the earliest of his cheques that became a top-tier company (existing ledger rows).\n- **Mintive (seed, October 2026):** Cowboy led $7M; Coatue, Operator Collective, Hustle Fund; angels Dean, Karpathy, Frederic Kerrest; founders are ex-Google Brain [src](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/agent-identity-startup-mintive-lands-7m-seed-from-cowboy-with-jeff-dean-and).\n- **Discovery Loop (August 2026):** he was the founder, not the angel; Radical and Khosla co-led [src](https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Angel in at least four Radical-led rounds; Radical co-led Discovery Loop | 2025 | [src](https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop) |\n| Khosla Ventures | Angel in Khosla-led Basis Series A and in Sakana (Khosla co-investor); Khosla co-led Discovery Loop | 2024 | [src](https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop) |\n| Zetta Venture Partners (Dylan Reid) | Three bio+AI co-investments | | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/07/22/jeff-dean-googles-chief-scientist-is-quietly-betting-on-the-next-wave-of-ai-startups/) |\n| Andrej Karpathy | Repeated co-angel (Ideogram, Mintive, Flapping Airplanes) | 2023 | [src](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/agent-identity-startup-mintive-lands-7m-seed-from-cowboy-with-jeff-dean-and) |\n| Sanjay Ghemawat, Quoc Le, Oriol Vinyals | Discovery Loop co-founders | 2026-08 | [src](https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Cheque size and whether there is an entity behind the cheques.\n- The roughly 50 investments CB Insights counts that are not named here.\n- Does he keep angel investing now that Discovery Loop competes with some of his portfolio (Periodic Labs, Edison Scientific)?\n- Confirm Cara, Copperhelm, Phinity and The Biological Computing Co from primary sources.\n\n## Sources\nhttps://fortune.com/2025/07/22/jeff-dean-googles-chief-scientist-is-quietly-betting-on-the-next-wave-of-ai-startups/ ; https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop ; https://www.36kr.com/p/3927354387544195 ; https://aiweekly.co/alerts/agent-identity-startup-mintive-lands-7m-seed-from-cowboy-with-jeff-dean-and ; https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/jeff-dean ; https://buttondown.com/AngelRegistry/archive/angel-registry-update-april-1-2026 ; https://buttondown.com/AngelRegistry/archive/angel-registry-update-may-7-2026/ ; https://rosebud.ai/company\n", "person:jeff-herbst": "\n# Jeff Herbst\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. The brief's line \"no personal deal credit\" holds for his NVIDIA years only: no round names him personally at GPU Ventures. At GFT he is named. He gives the investor quote and is listed as a board member in Higgsfield's Series A release.*\n\n## One-line take\nHerbst was NVIDIA's original venture and startup-ecosystem builder, and he now runs a $140M seed/A fund. That fund led Higgsfield's Series A about one year before Higgsfield reached a $5.4B valuation. He is a useful NVIDIA-network relationship, but GFT is not a co-investor for $600M lab seeds.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** the network. He ran NVIDIA business development for 20 years, founded its venture and acquisitions programs (\"more than 40 global investments and 20 acquisitions valued over $8B\") and led Inception ([GFT bio](https://www.gft.vc/jeff-herbst)). He says roughly 15,000 startups have been through Inception, which gives him reach among founders, VCs, bankers and lawyers ([GCV 2023-09-21](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/gft-ventures-launches-with-140m/?format=md)). GFT also brings in corporates (SK Hynix, Naver and others) as LPs and uses them to test and later sell to portfolio companies.\n- **Constraint:** fund size and stage. Fund I closed at $140M ([GCV 2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/gft-ventures-launches-with-140m/?format=md)). First checks are $3M to $4M for 10-15% ownership, and he requires a built product and \"first customer traction\". As of Q1 2025 about half of committed capital was still undeployed ([GFT Q1 2025](https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q1-2025-update)). No Fund II appears in public sources (searched 2026-11-10).\n- **What he'd never do (sourced):** back pre-product companies or chase hot rounds. His stated sweet spot is traction, and he rejects \"deal-chasing VCs\" ([GCV 2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/gft-ventures-launches-with-140m/?format=md)). He insists on \"reasonable valuations\" ([GFT Q2 2024](https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q2-2024-update)). A pre-product $600M lab seed fails both tests.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Higgsfield timing.** GFT led Higgsfield's $50M Series A on 2025-09-09, and Herbst is named as \"Board Member of Higgsfield AI\" ([PRNewswire via Digital Media Net](https://digitalmedianet.com/higgsfield-announces-50m-series-a-to-propel-click-to-video-ai-for-social-media/amp/)). Accel then led an $80M Series A extension at over $1.3B in January 2026, and GFT reportedly took part ([The Star, 2026-01-15](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/01/15/ai-video-startup-higgsfield-hits-13-billion-valuation-with-latest-funding)). DST then led a $400M Series B at $5.4B on 2026-08-17 ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/17/higgsfield-raises-400m-series-b-quadrupling-its-valuation-in-8-months-to-5-4b/)). GFT is not named in the B syndicate. Valuation rose about 5x in 11 months from GFT's lead (a ~$1.0B post is reported by an aggregator only). He pitched the deal with his NVIDIA history: \"Higgsfield now stands at an equally profound inflection point\".\n- **A fund-cycle constraint the bios miss.** The fund is a 2021 vintage that only closed in 2023 at $140M. In Q1 2025 the GPs wrote that \"approximately 50% of our committed capital\" remained ([GFT Q1 2025](https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q1-2025-update)). In March 2023 they told LPs that \"now would be an ideal time\" to add capital ([GFT Q1 2023](https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q1-2023-update)). Together these point to slow deployment and a long fundraise. The 2021 launch interview set a $100M target and said it was \"about halfway\" there ([GCV 2021-09-28](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/jeff-herbst-managing-partner-gft-ventures/?format=md)).\n- **Corporate LPs act as a pre-investment test bed.** About 15-20% of Fund I comes from corporates, at least four of them, some running their own CVCs. Herbst says GFT will \"test some of the business models on them before we invest\" ([GCV 2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/gft-ventures-launches-with-140m/?format=md)). He says the corporates have no strategic say. GCV disclosed that it is itself a minor LP. Named LPs at first close: SK Hynix, Naver, Mirae Asset Global Investments and US angels ([GCV 2021-07-28](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/herbst-leaves-nvidia-to-set-up-gft-ventures/?format=md)).\n- **NVIDIA's former chief scientist sits inside GFT.** David Kirk, NVIDIA's former chief scientist and VP of architecture, is listed as GFT Senior Advisor ([GFT team](https://www.gft.vc/david-kirk)). This is a technical-diligence link to NVIDIA's old guard that appears on no press page.\n- **The GPU Ventures track record runs through Herbst and Eum together.** Eum (ex-Samsung Ventures, TransLink co-founder) and Herbst co-invested in SoundHound. GCV credits their combined 78 deals with a 29% gross IRR. TuSimple, a GPU Ventures/Inception deal, returned 46x at its April 2021 IPO ([GCV 2021-07-28](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/herbst-leaves-nvidia-to-set-up-gft-ventures/?format=md)). GFT's \"prior investments\" page combines both men's histories (TuSimple, SoundHound, Coupang, SoFi, Deepgram, DeepMap, H2O.ai, Keyhole and others) without saying which partner did which ([gft.vc/prior-investments](https://www.gft.vc/prior-investments)). Do not credit any single one of these to Herbst.\n- **Robotics conversion.** In Q2 2024 GFT wrote that it had struggled to find robotics deals that met its criteria. That changed after \"a small investment in K-Scale Labs\" in April 2024, made after meeting roboticists working \"out of a garage in Palo Alto\" ([GFT Q2 2024](https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q2-2024-update)). By Q1 2025, Anyware Robotics was its third physical-AI deal.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium** (2026-11-10). Network value is high and check-size fit is low.\n- Against: the stage gate. He invests once there is \"first customer traction\" and an existing product ([GCV 2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/gft-ventures-launches-with-140m/?format=md)). He targets 10-15% ownership with $3M to $4M checks from a $140M fund. That is the opposite of a $20M non-controlling check into a pre-product lab seed. No frontier lab, AI chip or compute deal appears in the GFT portfolio.\n- For: he is a direct line into the NVIDIA business-development and Inception alumni network, with Kirk as advisor. His AI-native application and robotics bets (Higgsfield, Anyware, K-Scale) fit the AI-native-automation side of the thesis. He could be a co-investor or referral source at the Seed+/A stage of automation companies, not at lab seeds.\n- Deals that matter to me: Higgsfield (Series A lead, board), Anyware Robotics (Seed+ lead), LinqAlpha (Series A co-lead), K-Scale Labs (small early check).\n\n## Background\n- BS with honors in Computer Science and Economics, Brown University. JD, Stanford ([GFT bio](https://www.gft.vc/jeff-herbst)).\n- Partner, Wilson Sonsini, Palo Alto. Then head of business development at AltaVista ([GFT bio](https://www.gft.vc/jeff-herbst)). Dates not given.\n- NVIDIA, 2001 to mid-2021: VP of Business Development. Founded GPU Ventures and the acquisitions program, and ran the Inception accelerator ([GCV 2021-09-28](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/jeff-herbst-managing-partner-gft-ventures/?format=md); [NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/?p=47030)). Through GPU Ventures and Inception he invested $111M across 41 companies ([GCV 2021-07-28](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/herbst-leaves-nvidia-to-set-up-gft-ventures/?format=md)).\n- GFT Ventures (Global Frontier Technology), founded March 2021 with Jay Eum ([GFT Q1 2023](https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q1-2023-update)). Herbst's departure from NVIDIA was reported 2021-07-28. Fund I first close 2021; public launch at a $140M close in September 2023 ([GCV 2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/gft-ventures-launches-with-140m/?format=md)).\n- Chairs the GCV AI Council, per his 2021 interview ([GCV 2021-09-28](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/jeff-herbst-managing-partner-gft-ventures/?format=md)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.gft.vc/jeff-herbst | GFT bio; contact team@gft.vc per newsletters |\n| Substack / blog | https://gftventures.substack.com | Quarterly GFT newsletter, co-signed with Jay Eum (2023 to 2025 issues seen) |\n| X | nothing found | not searched in depth |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/jeff-herbst-9012/ | linked from GFT bio; follower count not checked |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2021: GCV Digital Forum on AI, fireside chat with George Hoyem (In-Q-Tel) ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/herbst-leaves-nvidia-to-set-up-gft-ventures/?format=md)). Transcript not found.\n- 2023-03-20: GFT virtual fireside with Eli David (Deep Instinct, DeepCube), moderated by Joseph Lee, GFT head of IR ([GFT Q1 2023](https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q1-2023-update)). Recording not found.\n- 2022-03-08: KED Global interview. Herbst said the top 5-10% of funds get the best deal flow, and that GFT is \"value-added\" rather than cheerleaders ([KED Global](https://amp.kedglobal.com/newsAmp/ked202203080007)). Wording came through a fetch summary because the raw page was blocked, so it is medium confidence.\n\n## How they think\n- **AI as a super-cycle:** \"the most disruptive technology cycle most of us will see in our lifetimes\" ([GCV 2021](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/jeff-herbst-managing-partner-gft-ventures/?format=md)). In 2025 he praised DeepSeek's open-source work as an accelerant ([GFT Q1 2025](https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q1-2025-update)).\n- **Pattern recognition over momentum:** \"If you just chase the hottest deals ... you might have a hard time making money\" (paraphrased). The core of his pitch is conviction at an inflection point ([GCV 2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/gft-ventures-launches-with-140m/?format=md)).\n- **Money is a commodity:** \"money is just money\". He says the value lies in strategy, go-to-market and corporate partnerships ([GCV 2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/gft-ventures-launches-with-140m/?format=md)).\n- **Valuation discipline:** he welcomed the 2023 \"rationalization\" of valuations ([GFT Q1 2023](https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q1-2023-update)).\n- Calls: Higgsfield (A lead at about $1B, later $5.4B) looks right so far. TuSimple returned 46x at IPO but later collapsed as a company. That last point is not sourced here.\n\n## Read these first\n- [GCV 2023-09-21, \"Ex-CVCs launch GFT Ventures with $140m\"](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/gft-ventures-launches-with-140m/?format=md): check size, ownership, traction gate, corporate-LP model.\n- [GCV 2021-09-28 interview](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/jeff-herbst-managing-partner-gft-ventures/?format=md): founding thesis and \"proprietary deal flow\" claim.\n- [GFT Q1 2025 newsletter](https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q1-2025-update): deployment pace and the turn to physical AI.\n- [Higgsfield Series A release](https://digitalmedianet.com/higgsfield-announces-50m-series-a-to-propel-click-to-video-ai-for-social-media/amp/): his deal language and board seat.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found beyond the GFT newsletter (not searched on X in depth).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Higgsfield | Series A ($50M) | 2025-09-09 | yes (GFT); Herbst board member | BroadLight, NextEquity, AI Capital Partners, Menlo Ventures, Alpha Square Group | [PRNewswire via DMN](https://digitalmedianet.com/higgsfield-announces-50m-series-a-to-propel-click-to-video-ai-for-social-media/amp/) |\n| Higgsfield | Series A extension ($80M, >$1.3B) | 2026-01-15 | no (Accel led) | Accel | [The Star](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/01/15/ai-video-startup-higgsfield-hits-13-billion-valuation-with-latest-funding) (GFT participation reported) |\n| LinqAlpha | Series A ($22M) | 2026-07-02 | co-lead (GFT) | AVP, Atinum Investment | [DealStreetAsia](https://www.dealstreetasia.com/stories/linqalpha-series-a-487633); partner on deal not named |\n| Anyware Robotics | Seed (Series Seed Plus) | Q1 2025 | yes (GFT) | not given | [GFT Q1 2025](https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q1-2025-update) |\n| K-Scale Labs | Undisclosed (\"small investment\") | 2024-04 | no | not given | [GFT Q2 2024](https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q2-2024-update) |\n| Knit | Seed (Series Seed) | H1 2024 | not stated | not given | [GFT Q2 2024](https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q2-2024-update) |\n| Mperativ | Undisclosed (GFT's first lead, 2021) | 2021 | yes | not given | [GCV 2021-09-28](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/jeff-herbst-managing-partner-gft-ventures/?format=md) (unnamed AI marketing company); named in [KED 2022](https://amp.kedglobal.com/newsAmp/ked202203080007) |\n| Figment, Mars Auto, Switchboard, Artisight, BRIA, Lyzr, Qortex, Daash, Go.AI, Avelis Health, Rabot | various | 2021-2026 | mostly unknown | | [gft.vc/portfolio](https://www.gft.vc/portfolio); stages unverified |\n| TuSimple (NVIDIA GPU Ventures/Inception era) | NVIDIA-era investment; round not verified | pre-2021 | no | | [GCV 2021-07-28](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/herbst-leaves-nvidia-to-set-up-gft-ventures/?format=md); 46x at April 2021 IPO; credited to the unit, not to him by name |\n\nAttribution: all GFT rows are firm deals. Herbst is personally named only on Higgsfield. The LinqAlpha and Anyware leads may have been run by Eum or by partners Ivy Nguyen or Keith Lee.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Higgsfield.** The release frames the round around Herbst's NVIDIA history: he helped build the platform that \"today powers AI-native applications like Higgsfield\". Founder Alex Mashrabov was formerly head of generative AI at Snap. GFT entered at the A, not the seed. Intro path not found.\n- **K-Scale Labs.** GFT met \"three extremely brilliant roboticists\" working out of a Palo Alto garage and made a small investment in April 2024 ([GFT Q2 2024](https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q2-2024-update)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jay Eum | Co-founding MP at GFT; friends and co-investors for about two decades | 2021 (GFT) | [GCV 2021-09-28](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/jeff-herbst-managing-partner-gft-ventures/?format=md) |\n| David Kirk | GFT Senior Advisor; former NVIDIA chief scientist | not dated | [gft.vc](https://www.gft.vc/david-kirk) |\n| Ivy Nguyen, Keith Lee | GFT Partners | not dated | [gft.vc/team](https://www.gft.vc/team) |\n| Alex Mashrabov (Higgsfield) | Board member at Higgsfield | 2025-09 | [Higgsfield release](https://digitalmedianet.com/higgsfield-announces-50m-series-a-to-propel-click-to-video-ai-for-social-media/amp/) |\n| SK Hynix, Naver, Mirae Asset | LPs in GFT Fund I | 2021 | [GCV 2021-07-28](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/herbst-leaves-nvidia-to-set-up-gft-ventures/?format=md) |\n| Global Corporate Venturing | minor LP in Fund I; he chairs GCV AI Council | 2021/2023 | [GCV 2023](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/gft-ventures-launches-with-140m/?format=md) |\n| NVIDIA | VP Business Development; founder of GPU Ventures and Inception | 2001-2021 | [GCV 2021-09-28](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/jeff-herbst-managing-partner-gft-ventures/?format=md) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did GFT participate in Higgsfield's August 2026 Series B? TechCrunch does not name it.\n- Does a Fund II exist or is one being raised? Nothing public as of 2026-11-10.\n- Which GPU Ventures deals did Herbst personally source (TuSimple round and year)? Did he sit on any NVIDIA-era boards?\n- Does he still refer founders to NVentures or Inception, and how does he relate to Sid Siddeek?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.gft.vc/jeff-herbst ; https://www.gft.vc/team ; https://www.gft.vc/david-kirk ; https://www.gft.vc/portfolio ; https://www.gft.vc/prior-investments\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/herbst-leaves-nvidia-to-set-up-gft-ventures/?format=md (2021-07-28)\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/jeff-herbst-managing-partner-gft-ventures/?format=md (2021-09-28)\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/gft-ventures-launches-with-140m/?format=md (2023-09-21)\n- https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q1-2023-update ; https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q2-2024-update ; https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q1-2025-update\n- https://digitalmedianet.com/higgsfield-announces-50m-series-a-to-propel-click-to-video-ai-for-social-media/amp/ (2025-09-09)\n- https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/01/15/ai-video-startup-higgsfield-hits-13-billion-valuation-with-latest-funding\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/17/higgsfield-raises-400m-series-b-quadrupling-its-valuation-in-8-months-to-5-4b/\n- https://www.dealstreetasia.com/stories/linqalpha-series-a-487633\n- https://amp.kedglobal.com/newsAmp/ked202203080007 (2022-03-08; via fetch summary)\n- https://blogs.nvidia.com/?p=47030\n", "person:jeff-horing": "\n# Jeff Horing\n\n## One-line take\nHe co-founded and still runs the investment engine of Insight Partners, the outbound-sourcing machine for software. He has now said on the record that Insight stays out of frontier-lab rounds and buys AI at the application layer. That leaves the large lab seeds to investors like the owner.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** coverage and the double-down. He has 60-80 young analysts cold-calling companies worldwide. He runs one fund across all stages, so a $5M first check can grow to a $1B position, and he says Insight's follow-on checks \"were our best checks\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18). He started Insight by copying Kevin Landry's TA Associates cold-call playbook [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18).\n- **What constrains him:** he admits the firm is weak at the early edge. \"we have some disadvantages on early relative to California\". Early stage is about 10% of the ~$12B fund [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18). Gross retention is a hard filter (\"we will not do a low gross retention business today\"), and that rules out most pre-revenue bets.\n- **What he'd never do:** compete in the foundation-model race. \"We have not entered the race for basic models\" and, on Ilya Sutskever's SSI round, \"we won't be competing in this race\" [src](https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001509703). Insight's Anthropic stake came through its public strategy, not the core fund [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18).\n\n## Differential insight\n- Five-ingredient checklist for a perfect investment: \"It's big ROI, big ASP, time to value, CEO and tech\". He says Wiz checked all five. ILTB 0:18:30 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18)\n- Insight backed Wiz \"when it had zero revenue\" and Monday.com at about $5M in size. ILTB 0:55:01 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18)\n- The Anthropic stake was \"more driven from our public strategy just to be clear\", not the core fund. He also admits being late: \"two years ago I would have had a much more positive view earlier\". ILTB 0:55:31-0:56:00 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18)\n- Uber pass: \"We fought like hell as a partnership over it and we finally passed.\" He calls it a huge mistake. ILTB 1:21:31 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18)\n- Fund mix: \"I think early stage is like 10% call it growth is probably 30%\". Venture buyouts are 30-40% and LBOs about 20%. The target is about 30% gross, with buyouts five points lower. ILTB 0:52:30-0:54:00 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18)\n- He says he is the only partner who does \"concept deals\" (team and tech with few numbers) at a big price, kept small by check size, and he is not doing them now. ILTB 1:16:01 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18)\n- IC structure: about eight pods, and he makes \"sure that an investment committee member owns every single deal\". ILTB 1:15:00 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18)\n- AI timeline: at FII in Oct 2023 he was the skeptic (\"I feel like we might be at Peak expectations\") and pushed 10% productivity gains across the existing book [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DdBI_jfkg38). By Sept 2025, \"we probably have 25 agentic AI bets\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18).\n- In 2020 he admitted a too-conservative partner cost them deals: \"We could have been a much bigger company today\". He also explained why small checks don't scare him: \"The most we can lose is the check itself\" [src](https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001315695)\n- Founder rule: if gross retention is under 90%, \"I would rethink\" the business [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/syc2yiwybl)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Insight can write growth-size AI checks and is moving earlier in applied AI (Wonderful from Series A to C in ~10 months, Darwin AI Series A, Trunk Tools, ScaleOps). But Horing has said outright that Insight will not compete for frontier-lab rounds such as SSI's. Its Anthropic exposure sits in the public book, not the venture fund. So Insight is a likely follow-on partner and buyer of AI-native automation companies the owner backs early. It is not a competitor or co-investor in $600M lab seeds. Deals that matter here: Wonderful, Run:ai (NVIDIA exit), Weights & Biases, Wiz, and Anthropic via the public strategy.\n\n## Background\n- Goldman Sachs, then Warburg Pincus. He calls Warburg his first job, says he was hired after many rejections, and says he sourced deals there by cold-calling [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/team/jeff-horing/) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18)\n- Co-founded Insight Partners in 1995 with Jerry Murdock, starting from a $16M blind pool raised from wealthy individuals [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18) [src](https://fortune.com/article/insight-partners-analysts-venture-capital-jeff-horing-software-investing/)\n- MIT and University of Pennsylvania graduate [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/team/jeff-horing/)\n- Has invested in 250+ companies. The team page names Monday.com, nCino, JFrog, Alteryx, AirWatch, Shutterstock, TeamViewer, Wiz, Wix and Greenfield Online [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/team/jeff-horing/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.insightpartners.com/team/jeff-horing/ | Firm bio (checked 2026-11-11) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Co-bylines on Insight \"Ideas\" deal posts only |\n| X | nothing found | No personal account found |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | O'Shaughnessy says there is \"basically nothing available\" about him online (ILTB 0:07:01) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-09 — Invest Like the Best EP.440 — \"Building Insight Partners\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18 — five ingredients, one-fund double-downs, Uber pass, Anthropic via public strategy, 25 agentic AI bets\n- 2025-11 — Startup Nation Central MNC Summit fireside with Avi Hasson — https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/syc2yiwybl — under 90% gross retention: rethink the business; AI \"five times\" cloud\n- 2025-10 — ScaleUp:AI 2025 opening keynote — https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/scaleupai-2025-recap/ — software and services blur; the \"80% world\"\n- 2025-04 — Insight analyst sourcing summit (Fortune) — https://fortune.com/article/insight-partners-analysts-venture-capital-jeff-horing-software-investing/ — \"Sourcing is everything\"\n- 2023-10 — FII7 panel with Brad Gerstner and Umesh Sachdev — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DdBI_jfkg38 — AI closer to mobile than to the internet; warned of peak expectations\n- 2018-12 — Startup for Startup (monday.com) podcast, quoted by Globes in 2020 — https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001315695 — the $6B fund \"not enough money\"; the conservative-partner mistake\n- 2017-07 — VTS Accelerate VC panel with Scott Maxwell and Jeremy Levine — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xj6B-w6sc-E — captions too garbled to mine\n\n## How they think\n- Software is the best asset class because revenue recurs and gross margins are high. Gross dollar retention plus CAC (an LTV/CAC view) drive exit value. He calls net retention one of the least informative metrics [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18)\n- \"Stage is not a strategy to me\" (said to LPs). Everything between seed and buyout is a risk-adjusted spreadsheet [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18)\n- Contrarian on \"AI kills SaaS\": \"It was always a business knowledge barrier.\" He sees AI as a TAM accelerator for incumbents [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18)\n- Wrong calls he admits: Uber pass; late on Anthropic; consumer and West Coast categories where \"our misses are so high\"; deals done over Zoom in 2020-21. Right calls: Wiz at zero revenue; Twitter exit before Musk [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18)\n- Israel: \"Israel is the highest density geography in our portfolio\" (2018) [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctech/articles/0,7340,L-3734443,00.html). In 2025 CTech reported that 87% of Insight's Israeli investments are early stage [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rkarqovxgg)\n\n## Read these first\n- Invest Like the Best EP.440 (2025): the only long-form interview; the whole operating model.\n- Globes, May 2025: the clearest statement that Insight stays out of foundation-model rounds.\n- CTech, May 2025: Wiz pricing and valuation leaps of faith on talent.\n- FII 2023 panel: his pre-agentic AI stance, useful for timing Insight's AI shift.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No personal X or Substack, and he rarely gives interviews. Insight's own 2026 predictions post calls the ILTB episode a \"rare interview\" [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/2026-investor-predictions/).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Wonderful | Series A / B / C | 2025-11 / 2026-03 / 2026-09 | B and C led | Index, IVP, Bessemer, Vine | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/tripling-down-on-wonderful-building-the-ai-operating-system-for-the-enterprise/) |\n| ScaleOps | Series C ($130M) | 2026-03-30 | yes | Lightspeed, NFX, Glilot | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/scaleops-raises-130m-series-c-at-over-800m-valuation-to-lead-the-future-of-autonomous-cloud-and-ai-infrastructure-resource-management/) |\n| Darwin AI | Series A ($15M) | 2025-10-23 | yes | | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/darwin-ai-raises-15-million-series-a-led-by-insight-partners-to-help-governments-build-ai-solutions-responsibly-and-at-scale/) |\n| Trunk Tools | Series B ($40M) | 2025-07-24 | yes | Redpoint, Innovation Endeavors | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/trunk-tools-closes-40m-series-b-to-lead-constructions-ai-transformation/) |\n| Run:ai | Series B ($30M) | 2021-01-26 | yes | | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/runai-raises-30m-series-b-funding/) |\n| Weights & Biases | Series B ($45M) | 2021-02-01 | yes | | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/weights-and-biases-raises-45m-series-b-to-expand-beyond-experiment-tracking-for-machine-learning-practitioners-everywhere/) |\n| JFrog | Series D ($165M) | 2018-10-04 | yes | | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/jfrog-secures-165-million-investment-to-lead-universal-devops-in-the-enterprise/) |\n| Wiz | early (zero revenue) | not dated in source | unknown | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Wiz:** Insight backed it at zero revenue and paid \"a big price\" because it loved the team. Horing says Wiz doubled net new bookings for six years [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18). He says every round looked expensive from outside and every round paid off [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rkarqovxgg).\n- **Anthropic:** the stake came through Insight's public strategy. Dario Amodei's articulation of the moat and the coding-driven inflection changed his mind, about two years later than he wishes [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18).\n- **Uber:** they passed after a partnership fight at what he calls a really attractive round. **Twitter:** they \"managed to get Twitter over the line\" and exited before Musk [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18).\n- **Early buyouts:** around 1999 Insight bought an insurance-software company from an insurer with 2x leverage. Captions say \"Veraphor\"; it is probably Vertafore, which needs confirmation [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18).\n- **Wonderful:** Insight evaluated it three times in under a year and led the B and the C [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/tripling-down-on-wonderful-building-the-ai-operating-system-for-the-enterprise/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jerry Murdock | Insight co-founder (retired) | 1995 | [src](https://fortune.com/article/insight-partners-analysts-venture-capital-jeff-horing-software-investing/) |\n| Kevin Landry (TA Associates) | Hearing his talk inspired Insight's cold-call model | early 1990s | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18) |\n| Steve Friedman (Goldman; identity to confirm) | Mentor in Insight's first decade | ~1995 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18) |\n| Robert Rubin | Mentor, introduced by Friedman | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18) |\n| Jeff Lieberman, Michael Triplett (captions: \"Mike Triplet\") | Early partners who built out sourcing | late 1990s | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18) |\n| Scott Maxwell (OpenView) | Insight alum turned fund founder; 2017 panel together | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xj6B-w6sc-E) |\n| Avi Hasson (Startup Nation Central) | Interviewer, MNC Summit 2025 | 2025 | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/syc2yiwybl) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which Insight alum did he \"bend over backwards\" to keep? That fund raised about $1B on its own record (ILTB 0:56:32).\n- Confirm \"Veraphor\" = Vertafore and \"Steve Freeman\" = Stephen Friedman.\n- Was Insight's Anthropic exposure a direct round allocation or public-vehicle secondaries? What size?\n- ScaleUp:AI 2025 keynote video is not public. The Dec 2018 Startup for Startup episode has no episode URL.\n- CTech (Oct 2024) reports his role in removing OpenWeb's CEO; worth reading for his governance style.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g4zzkuFyI18\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DdBI_jfkg38\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xj6B-w6sc-E\n- https://www.insightpartners.com/team/jeff-horing/\n- https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001509703\n- https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001315695\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rkarqovxgg\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/syc2yiwybl\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctech/articles/0,7340,L-3734443,00.html\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctech/articles/0,7340,L-3792070,00.html\n- https://fortune.com/article/insight-partners-analysts-venture-capital-jeff-horing-software-investing/\n- https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/scaleupai-2025-recap/\n- https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/tripling-down-on-wonderful-building-the-ai-operating-system-for-the-enterprise/\n- https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/2026-investor-predictions/\n", "person:jeff-huber": "\n# Jeff Huber\n\nPronouns: SEC filings call him \"Mr. Huber\" and use \"he\", so this file does too.\n\n**Identity caveat.** A different Jeff Huber co-founded Chroma, the vector database company, and appears on AI podcasts such as TBPN and ThursdAI. Don't merge the two. This profile covers the Triatomic, GRAIL and Google Jeff Huber. The photo comes from UC Berkeley's CDSS Advisory Board page ([cdss.berkeley.edu](https://cdss.berkeley.edu/jeff-huber)), whose bio names Triatomic, GRAIL and Google. The Triatomic site has no headshots.\n\n## One-line take\nHuber is a former Google SVP and GRAIL's founding CEO. He sits on boards first and has his fund write the check later. That got him onto d-Matrix's board about 17 months before Triatomic co-led its $275M Series C. He also personally led an AI-agent seed (Coworker.ai), which makes him the Triatomic partner nearest to the owner's AI-chips and AI-automation lens.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he has credibility from running Ads, Maps and Apps at Google and building GRAIL, and he sits on many boards. Two of his compute-adjacent deals show the same board-first pattern:\n  - **d-Matrix.** He was named a d-Matrix board member on 2024-06-27 ([Summit 8-K ex.99.1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929824000103/a2024_prx0627xnewdirecto.htm)). Triatomic then co-led the Series C on 2025-11-12.\n  - **Zapata.** He was a Zapata director from 2021-07 to 2024-10. Triatomic then led Zapata's $15M recapitalization in 2026-04.\n  - **Manifold.** Here the seat came with the check: he joined the board as Triatomic led the Series A, which was the fund's first-ever investment ([BioSpace](https://www.biospace.com/the-father-of-genomics-launches-manifold-bio-with-40-million)).\n- **What constrains him:** a co-equal GP seat in a small vehicle (about $346M regulatory AUM). He shares the GP LLC with Peter Zhou and Steve Hochberg, each holding 25–50% ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf)), and about half the portfolio is bio. His public-board network, which the task brief cites as the edge, **largely wound down in 2026**:\n  - He left Upstart on 2026-05-28 ([Upstart 8-K ex.99.1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1647639/000164763926000048/upst991newbodq22026.htm)).\n  - His EA seat ended when EA was taken private on 2026-08-04 ([Form 4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1463740/000071251526000142/xslF345X06/form4.xml)).\n  - He resigned from Zapata in 2024-10 ([8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000095017024115639/ck0001843714-20241015.htm)).\n  - Summit Therapeutics is now his only public board.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred from his deals):** be the first-money seed investor in a frontier lab. All of his documented AI entries come after a pre-seed or an earlier institutional round.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Manifold was Triatomic's first investment.** In his words, \"we're excited to partner with Manifold Bio as our first investment\" (2022-07-14, [BioSpace](https://www.biospace.com/the-father-of-genomics-launches-manifold-bio-with-40-million)). So the fund opened with an engineered-biology deal led by Huber, not with a compute deal.\n- **His d-Matrix board seat came long before the C round.** Summit's appointment release lists \"Zapata Computing Inc., Weta FX Ltd, d-Matrix Corp., and the Parker Institute\" among his boards on 2024-06-27 ([8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929824000103/a2024_prx0627xnewdirecto.htm)). Summit's April 2025 proxy repeats it ([DEF 14A 2025](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929825000075/smmt-20250429.htm)). The Series C co-lead came on 2025-11-12. Triatomic isn't named in d-Matrix's $110M Series B (2023-09, led by Temasek). The round in which Triatomic first bought in hasn't been found.\n- **His thesis on d-Matrix:** \"AI inference is becoming the dominant cost in production AI systems\" (Series C release, 2025-11, [StorageNewsletter mirror](https://www.storagenewsletter.com/?p=297415)).\n- **He personally led an AI-agent seed.** The release says it was \"led by Jeff Huber, former SVP of Google Ads, Maps, and Workspace\". That was Coworker.ai's $13M seed, announced 2025-05-20 ([PR Newswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/coworkerai-launches-the-first-ai-agent-with-deep-company-context-backed-by-13m-seed-round-302459262.html)). He said \"OM1 lays the groundwork for a whole new category of enterprise software.\" He wasn't first money: Soma Capital, Focal, Mischief and Karman did a $3.5M pre-seed first (same source). Triatomic's principal, Willem van den Bosch, came from Soma ([triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai)), which suggests a possible intro path (inference, not confirmed).\n- **He co-invests with Zapata's chairman.** Clark Golestani is an angel in Coworker alongside Huber. The release lists \"Clark Golestani, Ken Hausman, and Jack Greenfield (K2 Access Fund)\" ([PR Newswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/coworkerai-launches-the-first-ai-agent-with-deep-company-context-backed-by-13m-seed-round-302459262.html)). Golestani sat on Zapata's board with Huber ([424B3](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000119312524018137/d469525d424b3.htm)) and is now its chairman and a 19.2% holder ([DEF 14A 2026-09](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000168316826007111/zapata_def14a.htm)).\n- **Zapata was a rescue of a company he had left.** He resigned on 2024-10-15 \"in connection with the previously announced cessation of the operations\" ([8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000095017024115639/ck0001843714-20241015.htm)). Eighteen months later Triatomic led the $15M round ([8-K ex.99.1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000107997326000531/ex99x1.htm)). Private I now holds 9.9% of the Series D preferred plus a $500K note and warrant ([DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000168316826007111/zapata_def14a.htm)). This corrects \"director since 2021-07\" in the firm file: he is no longer on Zapata's board.\n- **Filings disagree on who founded Triatomic.** Zapata's 424B3 says \"Mr. Huber is the founder and has been the Managing Partner of Triatomic\". Summit calls him \"co-founder\". The ADV shows the adviser as Peter Zhou's entity (Hongkou) from 07/2020, with Huber joining the GP LLC in 05/2022 ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf)).\n- **Filings disagree on his GRAIL CEO tenure.** Summit's proxies say \"Founding Chief Executive Officer from 2016-2017\", then vice chairman to 2021 ([DEF 14A 2026](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929826000029/smmt-20260417.htm)). Zapata's 424B3 says CEO and Vice Chairman \"from January 2016 to September 2021\". Summit's version is the more precise one.\n- **Two GRAIL co-founders now sit on Summit's board.** Mostafa Ronaghi, ex-Illumina CTO, \"co-founded GRAIL\" in 2016 and joined Summit's board in 2024-04, two months before Huber ([DEF 14A 2026](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929826000029/smmt-20260417.htm)).\n- **He is cashing out of his EA stock.** A trust he controls filed Form 144s in 2025-11 to sell EA shares: \"sold by The Maywood Trust , Jeffrey Huber as Trustee\" ([Form 144](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1463740/000196858225001085/xsl144X01/primary_doc.xml)). The rest was cashed out at $210 per share in the take-private.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, with caveats.** Two of his deals hit the owner's lens directly:\n- **AI-native automation at seed:** he personally led Coworker.ai's $13M seed (2025-05). It's an enterprise agent with \"organizational memory\", the closest thing in the Triatomic set to Serval.\n- **AI chips:** he has sat on d-Matrix's board since at least 2024-06, and Triatomic co-led its $275M Series C (2025-11, $2B valuation).\n\nThe caveats:\n- He isn't first money in any AI deal found.\n- He has no frontier-lab or pre-training deals.\n- About half his board work is bio: Manifold, Mammoth, Genalyte, Summit, Parker Institute.\n\n**How to use him:** as a syndicate partner and board-level operator for AI-inference hardware and enterprise-agent rounds at $10–20M. His Google infrastructure background gives him real diligence depth on serving costs. His board slots freed up in 2026 (Upstart and EA are gone), so he may have room for new seats.\n\n## Background\n- B.S. in Computer Engineering, University of Illinois; MBA, Harvard Business School ([Summit DEF 14A 2026](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929826000029/smmt-20260417.htm)).\n- SVP of Engineering at Excite@Home (dates not stated) (same source).\n- 2001–2003: VP of Architecture and Systems Development at eBay (same source).\n- 2003–2016: Senior Vice President at Google. He led Ads (2003–2011), Apps (2005–2010) and Maps (2011–2013), then co-founded Google's life-sciences effort in Google[x] (2013–2016) ([Zapata 424B3](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000119312524018137/d469525d424b3.htm); [Summit 8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929824000103/a2024_prx0627xnewdirecto.htm)).\n- 2016–2017: GRAIL founding CEO; vice chairman 2016–2021 ([Summit DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929826000029/smmt-20260417.htm)).\n- From 2020: co-founder and president of OpenCovidScreen, a COVID-testing nonprofit ([424B3](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000119312524018137/d469525d424b3.htm)).\n- From 2022-02: Managing Partner at Triatomic (424B3). He has been a GP LLC member since 05/2022 (ADV).\n- Public boards:\n  - Electronic Arts, 2009-06 to 2026-08-04 (take-private).\n  - Illumina: former director; EDGAR shows a 2014 Form 3 ([EDGAR owner](https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/own-disp?action=getowner&CIK=0001463740)).\n  - Upstart, 2021-07 to 2026-05-28.\n  - Zapata, 2021-07 to 2024-10-15.\n  - Summit Therapeutics, 2024-06 to present.\n- Private and nonprofit boards (per the 2024 and 2026 Summit filings):\n  - Current: Mammoth Biosciences, d-Matrix, Manifold Bio, Openwater, Genalyte (chair, per [triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai)), Weta FX / Weta Digital, Parker Institute for Cancer Immunotherapy.\n  - Former: Aldevron (2019–2021) and GRAIL.\n- Chairs UC Berkeley's CDSS Advisory Board ([cdss.berkeley.edu](https://cdss.berkeley.edu/jeff-huber)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai) | Team bio; no headshot (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing verified | No handle confirmed. Don't confuse with Chroma's Jeff Huber |\n| LinkedIn | [linkedin.com/in/jeffhuber](https://www.linkedin.com/in/jeffhuber/) | Linked from the triatomic.ai team bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n| Other | [UC Berkeley CDSS Advisory Board](https://cdss.berkeley.edu/jeff-huber) | Chair; source of the headshot |\n| SEC (insider filings) | [EDGAR CIK 1463740](https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/own-disp?action=getowner&CIK=0001463740) | Form 3/4/144 for EA, Upstart, Summit, Zapata, Illumina |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-06-24: Insightful Investor #76, \"Innovation, Google, GRAIL, Triatomic\" ([episode](https://getpodcast.com/podcast/insightful-investor/76-jeff-huber-innovation-google-grail-triatomic_87a80abc40); [feed](https://feeds.captivate.fm/insightful-investor/)).\n  - The host is Alex Shahidi of Evoke Advisors (MAI Capital), a large RIA. The episode description mentions backing \"century-defining\" companies.\n  - No transcript exists online. The audio (58MB) wasn't transcribed this session.\n- 2023-10-17: The Long Run with Luke Timmerman, Ep146, \"Jeff Huber on Life in Tech and Biotech\" ([RSS](https://feeds.soundcloud.com/users/soundcloud:users:317770704/sounds.rss)). It covers his path from tech into biotech. Not transcribed.\n- 2016-10: O'Reilly Next:Economy, \"Grail: A conversation with Jeff Huber\" ([O'Reilly](https://www.oreilly.com/radar/grail-a-conversation-with-jeff-huber/)). Video only; no quotable text.\n\n## How they think\n- **Inference economics:** he sees inference as the dominant cost in production AI, which is why he backs purpose-built in-memory compute (d-Matrix release, 2025-11).\n- **AI agents need company context:** he was \"blown away by Coworker's architecture\" and sees organizational memory as a new category of enterprise software (PR Newswire, 2025-05).\n- **Engineered biology as a fund pillar:** Manifold was \"the power of engineered biology, one of the key pillars\" (2022-07).\n- **Quantum utility:** he frames Zapata as \"translating quantum advantage into quantum utility\" (2026-04 8-K).\n- **Calls he got right or wrong:** Zapata failed as a public company. Operations ceased in 2024-10 while he was a director. He came back as the lead investor in the recap, which is either conviction or sunk-cost risk; it's too early to judge.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [d-Matrix Series C release (2025-11)](https://www.storagenewsletter.com/?p=297415): his inference-cost thesis.\n2. [Coworker.ai seed release (2025-05-20)](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/coworkerai-launches-the-first-ai-agent-with-deep-company-context-backed-by-13m-seed-round-302459262.html): his personal lead in an AI-agent seed and his view of the category.\n3. [BioSpace on the Manifold Series A (2022-07-14)](https://www.biospace.com/the-father-of-genomics-launches-manifold-bio-with-40-million): Triatomic's first investment.\n4. [Summit DEF 14A (2026-04-17)](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929826000029/smmt-20260417.htm): his most precise current bio and board list.\n5. [Zapata 8-K ex.99.1 (2026-04-23)](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000107997326000531/ex99x1.htm): the recap he led.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No verified X account or blog. LinkedIn wasn't readable without logging in.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Manifold Bio | Series A $40M | 2022-07-14 | Yes (Triatomic's first investment; he took a board seat) | Section 32, FPV, Horizons, Tencent, Playground Global, Fifty Years, FAST | [BioSpace](https://www.biospace.com/the-father-of-genomics-launches-manifold-bio-with-40-million) |\n| d-Matrix | Entry round undisclosed; board seat by 2024-06-27 | ≤2024-06 | unknown | — | [Summit 8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929824000103/a2024_prx0627xnewdirecto.htm) |\n| Coworker.ai | Seed $13M | 2025-05-20 | **Yes, personally named** | Abstract (Ramtin Naimi), Operator Collective (Mallun Yen), Eniac (Tim Young), Clark Golestani, Ken Hausman, Jack Greenfield (K2 Access Fund) | [PR Newswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/coworkerai-launches-the-first-ai-agent-with-deep-company-context-backed-by-13m-seed-round-302459262.html) |\n| d-Matrix | Series C $275M ($2B valuation) | 2025-11-12 | Co-lead (with Bullhound, Temasek); he is quoted | QIA, EDBI, M12, Nautilus, Industry Ventures, Mirae | [StorageNewsletter](https://www.storagenewsletter.com/?p=297415); [Converge Digest](https://convergedigest.com/d-matrix-raises-275-million-to-accelerate-the-age-of-ai-inference/?amp=1) |\n| Zapata Quantum (OTC: ZPTA) | $15M strategic financing | 2026-04-23 | Yes (Triatomic; he is quoted) | \"other strategic investors\" | [8-K ex.99.1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000107997326000531/ex99x1.htm) |\n\nOther boards where no Triatomic round is documented: Mammoth Biosciences, Openwater, Genalyte, Weta FX. No partner is named on the firm's other compute deals (MatX, Gimlet, Efficient, Volantis, Chalk) ([firms/triatomic.md](../firms/triatomic.md)).\n\n**Timing: was he first?** No, in any AI deal found.\n- Coworker had a pre-seed from Soma, Focal, Mischief and Karman.\n- d-Matrix had earlier money from Playground, M12 and Temasek (the Series B, 2023-09, [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/2023/09/06/d-matrix-raises-110m-commercialize-next-gen-generative-ai-processing-chips)).\n- Manifold had Playground, Fifty Years and FAST before its A.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Zapata (2021–2026).**\n  - He joined the board in 2021-07, before Triatomic existed, and stayed through the 2024 SPAC merger with Andretti Acquisition Corp.\n  - In 2023 he received $260,250 in director option awards ([424B3](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000119312524018137/d469525d424b3.htm)).\n  - He resigned with the other outside directors on 2024-10-15, when operations ceased.\n  - Sumit Kapur, the CFO turned CEO, ran a year-long restructuring. Triatomic then led the $15M round in 2026-04.\n  - Golestani, his fellow Zapata director and Coworker co-angel, is now chairman. The intro path wasn't stated, but the board network is the obvious one.\n- **Coworker.ai (2025).** Alex Calder and Bradford Church, both ex-Uber, founded it. The pre-seed came from Soma Capital and others. Huber led the $13M seed in his own name with Triatomic listed ([PR Newswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/coworkerai-launches-the-first-ai-agent-with-deep-company-context-backed-by-13m-seed-round-302459262.html)). Coworker's logo is on triatomic.ai.\n- **d-Matrix (≤2024 to 2025).** He was on the board by mid-2024 and co-led the C, quoted alongside Per Roman of Bullhound. The rest of the board is Sasha Ostojic (Playground), Michael Stewart (M12), Connie Sheng (Nautilus), Russell Tham (Temasek) and Per Roman ([d-matrix.ai/about](https://www.d-matrix.ai/about/)).\n- **Manifold Bio (2022).** It was Triatomic's first investment, led by Huber with a board seat. George Church is the scientific founder ([BioSpace](https://www.biospace.com/the-father-of-genomics-launches-manifold-bio-with-40-million)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Triatomic Capital | GP / Managing Partner; GP LLC member 25–50% | 2022-02 (424B3) / 05/2022 (ADV) | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf) |\n| Peter Zhou | Fellow Triatomic GP; controls the adviser | 2022 | ADV |\n| Steve Hochberg | Fellow Triatomic GP | 2022-05 | ADV |\n| d-Matrix | Board director | ≤2024-06 | [Summit 8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929824000103/a2024_prx0627xnewdirecto.htm); [d-matrix.ai/about](https://www.d-matrix.ai/about/) |\n| Coworker.ai | Seed lead | 2025-05 | [PR Newswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/coworkerai-launches-the-first-ai-agent-with-deep-company-context-backed-by-13m-seed-round-302459262.html) |\n| Clark Golestani | Zapata board colleague; Coworker co-angel | 2021–2024 / 2025 | 424B3; PR Newswire |\n| Mostafa Ronaghi | GRAIL co-founder; Summit board colleague | 2016 / 2024 | [Summit DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929826000029/smmt-20260417.htm) |\n| Zapata Quantum | Director 2021-07 to 2024-10-15; lead investor 2026-04 | 2021 | [8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000095017024115639/ck0001843714-20241015.htm) |\n| Summit Therapeutics | Director | 2024-06 | [8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929824000103/a2024_prx0627xnewdirecto.htm) |\n| Upstart | Director until 2026-05-28 | 2021-07 | [8-K ex.99.1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1647639/000164763926000048/upst991newbodq22026.htm) |\n| Electronic Arts | Director until the take-private on 2026-08-04 | 2009-06 | [Form 4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1463740/000071251526000142/xslF345X06/form4.xml) |\n| Manifold Bio | Board; Series A lead | 2022-07 | [BioSpace](https://www.biospace.com/the-father-of-genomics-launches-manifold-bio-with-40-million) |\n| Mammoth Biosciences, Openwater, Genalyte, Weta FX, Parker Institute | Board | by 2024-06 | Summit 8-K |\n| GRAIL | Founding CEO 2016–2017; vice chair to 2021 | 2016 | Summit DEF 14A |\n| Google | SVP | 2003–2016 | Summit DEF 14A |\n| Alex Shahidi / Evoke Advisors | Podcast host (Insightful Investor #76) | 2025-06 | [feed](https://feeds.captivate.fm/insightful-investor/) |\n| UC Berkeley CDSS | Advisory Board chair | — | [cdss.berkeley.edu](https://cdss.berkeley.edu/jeff-huber) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which round did Triatomic first enter d-Matrix in, before 2024-06? Was it a 2024 extension? Did he join the board as an investor or as an independent director?\n- Did Soma Capital (van den Bosch's old firm) refer Coworker to Triatomic?\n- Is Evoke Advisors (MAI Capital), which hosted him, an LP or distribution channel for Triatomic's wealth-heavy LP base? Not established.\n- Is he an LP in any other fund? Nothing found.\n- Does the Insightful Investor episode contain check-size or sourcing rules? It needs a transcript.\n- Do his Weta FX and Openwater seats relate to any Triatomic position?\n- What size and terms did Triatomic get in the Zapata recap? The DEF 14A shows 9.9% of the Series D, a $500K note and a warrant; the total dollars Triatomic committed aren't stated.\n\n## Sources\n- https://triatomic.ai (fetched 2026-11-10)\n- Form ADV (2026-03-31): https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf\n- Zapata 424B3 (2024-01-29): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000119312524018137/d469525d424b3.htm\n- Zapata 8-K (2024-10-18, director resignations): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000095017024115639/ck0001843714-20241015.htm\n- Zapata 8-K ex.99.1 (2026-04-23): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000107997326000531/ex99x1.htm\n- Zapata DEF 14A (2026-09): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1843714/000168316826007111/zapata_def14a.htm\n- Summit 8-K ex.99.1 (2024-06-27): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929824000103/a2024_prx0627xnewdirecto.htm\n- Summit DEF 14A (2025-04-29): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929825000075/smmt-20250429.htm\n- Summit DEF 14A (2026-04-17): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1599298/000159929826000029/smmt-20260417.htm\n- Upstart 8-K ex.99.1 (2026-05-19): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1647639/000164763926000048/upst991newbodq22026.htm\n- EA Form 4 (2026-08-04): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1463740/000071251526000142/xslF345X06/form4.xml\n- EA Form 144 (2025-11-20): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1463740/000196858225001085/xsl144X01/primary_doc.xml\n- Summit Form 4 (2026-01-02): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1463740/000146374026000003/xslF345X05/wk-form4_1767743373.xml\n- EDGAR insider page: https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/own-disp?action=getowner&CIK=0001463740\n- d-Matrix board: https://www.d-matrix.ai/about/\n- d-Matrix Series C release: https://www.storagenewsletter.com/?p=297415; https://convergedigest.com/d-matrix-raises-275-million-to-accelerate-the-age-of-ai-inference/?amp=1\n- d-Matrix Series B: https://siliconangle.com/2023/09/06/d-matrix-raises-110m-commercialize-next-gen-generative-ai-processing-chips\n- Coworker.ai seed: https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/coworkerai-launches-the-first-ai-agent-with-deep-company-context-backed-by-13m-seed-round-302459262.html\n- Manifold Bio: https://www.biospace.com/the-father-of-genomics-launches-manifold-bio-with-40-million\n- UC Berkeley CDSS: https://cdss.berkeley.edu/jeff-huber\n- Podcasts: https://feeds.captivate.fm/insightful-investor/; https://feeds.soundcloud.com/users/soundcloud:users:317770704/sounds.rss; https://www.oreilly.com/radar/grail-a-conversation-with-jeff-huber/\n\n**Not confirmed:**\n- When and in which round Triatomic first invested in d-Matrix.\n- The Coworker intro path.\n- Any LP commitments.\n- His X handle.\n- Podcast content beyond the episode descriptions.\n- Excite@Home dates.\n- When his Illumina directorship started and ended (only the 2014 Form 3 is seen).\n", "person:jeffrey-katzenberg": "\n# Jeffrey Katzenberg\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (owner pick, hop 1 from `firms/wndrco.md`). Primary sources: the Aura Consolidated Group S-1/A (2026-09-02), the wndr.vc site bundle, SEC Form Ds for WndrCo and Quibi vehicles, company releases (Twelve Labs, Creatify, Orby), and interviews (Crunchbase News, Business Insider on the Sourcery podcast, Jefferies, CNBC). Sources use \"he\" and \"Mr. Katzenberg\". No podcast transcript was readable; quotes below come from articles and releases that quote him directly.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is WndrCo's door-opener: his Fortune 500 and C-suite network gets WndrCo into hot late-stage AI rounds and gets portfolio companies enterprise customers. He is not a frontier-lab picker, but he is a useful co-investor and distribution partner for AI apps and automation.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** access. The firm's pitch is his Fortune 500 relationships. He says WndrCo has \"a pretty extraordinary network of relationships with the Fortune 500 companies\" ([Crunchbase News, 2025-12-08](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/)). Crunchbase reports that he and Justin Wexler run the enterprise AI practice and help portfolio companies win customers such as Nike, Delta, the NBA and Disney ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/)). Partner Sujay Jaswa says he \"can open doors few can\" ([2024 fund release, archived](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/wndrco-announces-closing-of-over-460-million-for-newest-funds)).\n- **What constrains him:** he does not do technical diligence on models. His stated filter is the application layer and trust in founders, not research ([Crunchbase](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/)). The venture vehicles are mid-sized (Holdings II closed at $350.8M, [Form D/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924350/000192435024000001/primary_doc.xml)), so his \"led\" deals in $10B+ names are allocations, not price-setting leads.\n- **Where the money really is:** Aura. He is on Aura's board (since 2021) and on its remuneration and nomination committee. He is listed as beneficial owner of 54,187,308 Aura shares (24%), and his family trust holds 1,412,429 of them directly ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm)).\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** lead a priced seed into a new pre-training lab. No such deal is on record. His closest frontier-lab exposure is a participating check in Twelve Labs' seed extension (2022).\n- **Scar tissue:** Quibi. He founded it and raised $1B in 2018 and another $750M from 78 investors in 2020 ([2018 D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1748421/000174842118000001/primary_doc.xml), [2020 D/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1748421/000174842120000002/primary_doc.xml)). It shut in 2020. In 2024 he was openly cautious on AI pricing: \"We don't know. So caution seems appropriate.\" ([CNBC via NBC NY, 2024-06-04](https://www.nbcnewyork.com/news/business/money-report/hollywoods-jeffrey-katzenberg-bets-on-cybersecurity-avoids-digital-media-as-he-seeks-out-startups/5474726/?amp=1)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **His Aura stake is held partly through a personal co-invest vehicle.** The S-1/A footnote says \"Katzenberg Family Trust is the sole member of WC K Aura Holdco\" ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm)). WndrCo Holdings, LLC manages that vehicle, which holds 1,412,429 shares. In effect he is a direct LP/co-investor alongside his own firm's Build bet. This is not on wndr.vc or in press.\n- **The S-1 splits his \"led\" deals by vehicle and era.** In the original 2016 holding company he led Airtable, Deel, Placer.ai and ID.me. Since 2020 he has been \"Co-Founder and Managing Partner of WndrCo Capital Management, a venture capital firm\" and led Anduril, Waymo, ElevenLabs, Deel, Meter, Granola, Harvey and Abridge ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm)). That separate registered-adviser-style entity starts in 2020, four years before WndrCo said it raised its \"first classic venture funds\" (2024). The wndr.vc bio leaves out Granola and calls him \"Founding Partner\", not Managing Partner ([wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team)).\n- **He selects for apps, not models.** WndrCo is most excited by \"the simplest applications that actually change how people go about their lives\". The deciding factor \"can be summed up in a simple word: trust.\" ([Crunchbase, 2025-12-08](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/)).\n- **Many AI winners, not one.** \"It's not going to be a zero-sum game, a winner-take-all.\" He said this on the Sourcery podcast, with ChenLi Wang ([Business Insider via AOL, 2025-12-30](https://www.aol.com/articles/dreamworks-ceo-turned-vc-jeff-052214635.html)). This fits a book that spreads across many late-stage app leaders rather than concentrating in a lab.\n- **Rule of thumb:** \"you need to be a dreamer yourself — while knowing not all dreams come true\" ([Jefferies, 2025-10-22](https://www.jefferies.com/insights/markets/lessons-on-entrepreneurship-and-investing-from-jeffrey-katzenberg/)). On Speed of Culture, Suzy's episode page quotes him: \"We're truffle hunters. We're out looking for the rarest of the rare.\" ([Suzy, 2025-03-13](https://www.suzy.com/blog/jeffrey-katzenberg-justin-wexler-founder-managing-general-partner-wndrco); page quote, transcript PDF not read).\n- **Board seats follow storytelling and media companies.** He joined Creatify's board on the Series A, saying \"Great technology turns storytelling into real business results.\" ([Creatify, 2025-05-28](https://creatify.ai/blog/announcing-our-15-5m-series-a-a-new-chapter-for-creatify)). He joined Kimbal Musk's drone-show company Nova Sky Stories as an advisor when WndrCo joined its $50M round ([startuphub.ai, 2025-08-22](https://www.startuphub.ai/news/nova-sky-stories-raises-50m-for-drone-entertainment-expansion)). The press release calls the role board member and advisor (per search snippet; the BusinessWire page returned 403).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a frontier-lab or pre-training investor, and he will not anchor a $600M lab seed. Three things still make him relevant:\n1. **AI-native automation at Series A** (high-fit pattern): WndrCo co-led Orby AI's $30M Series A (2024-06-27) with NEA and Wing, and he was the quoted partner. He also co-led Creatify's $15.5M A with Kindred (2025-05-28) and took the board seat.\n2. **Early lab-adjacent:** he was named on Twelve Labs' $12M seed extension (2022-12-05, Radical lead), about nine months after Index's $5M seed ([KED Global](https://www.kedglobal.com/newsView/ked202212060019)).\n3. **Distribution:** for an owner's automation company, he is a source of Fortune 500 intros and a likely co-investor in a later round, not a seed partner.\n\nTreat him as a downstream syndicate partner and a GTM door, not a peer at the lab-seed table.\n\n## Background\n- President of Production, Paramount Pictures (before 1984); Chairman, The Walt Disney Studios, 1984-1994 ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm)). On leaving Disney he said he was fired \"unapologetically, unceremoniously\" ([Metis Strategy, 2025-08-07](https://www.metisstrategy.com/interview/jeffrey-katzenberg/)).\n- Co-founded DreamWorks LLC in October 1994 (sold to Paramount January 2006); CEO and director of DreamWorks Animation, 2004-2016 ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm)). wndr.vc says DreamWorks Animation sold to Comcast for $3.8B ([wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team)).\n- Zynga director, April 2011 to June 2014 ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm)).\n- Co-founded WndrCo with Sujay Jaswa in 2016. WndrCo, LLC's first sale was 2017-01-25 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1695036/000169503617000001/primary_doc.xml)). He founded Quibi in 2018 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1748421/000174842118000001/primary_doc.xml)). He has been Managing Partner of WndrCo Capital Management since 2020 ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm)).\n- Nonprofit boards: Cedars-Sinai, CalArts, Michael J. Fox Foundation, Motion Picture & Television Fund Foundation, USC School of Cinematic Arts, and others ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://wndr.vc/team | Founding Partner bio, 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing verified | no personal account found; firm is @wndrco |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/jeffrey-katzenberg-4b3b47123/ | linked from wndr.vc |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2022-11 — 20VC (with Sujay Jaswa) — show notes only; covers Quibi and risk appetite ([archived](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/the-fast-decaying-value-of-operator-experience-silicon-valleys-future-the-gen-z-workforce---insights-from-jeffrey-katzenberg-sujay-jaswa))\n- 2025-03 — Speed of Culture (Suzy), with Justin Wexler — the \"truffle hunters\" line ([src](https://www.suzy.com/blog/jeffrey-katzenberg-justin-wexler-founder-managing-general-partner-wndrco))\n- 2025-08 — Technovation with Peter High (Metis Strategy) — DreamWorks, Disney, WndrCo; page lists his boards (Aura, Pango, WndrHLTH, UnifyAI, Creatify) ([src](https://www.metisstrategy.com/interview/jeffrey-katzenberg/))\n- 2025-09 (approx.) — Pioneers of AI (Rana el Kaliouby), with ChenLi Wang — storytelling, AI and the Kimbal Musk partnership ([listing](https://pocketcasts.com/podcast/pioneers-of-ai/1672fcb0-3d78-013d-0376-0affdaeeef4b/the-power-of-storytelling-in-vcs-with-jeffrey-katzenberg-and-chenli-wang/8d7f0318-fb9f-4b8e-b2cc-37d6f6607f3f); page not read)\n- 2025-10 — Jefferies insights interview — \"dreamer\" and optimism/skepticism ([src](https://www.jefferies.com/insights/markets/lessons-on-entrepreneurship-and-investing-from-jeffrey-katzenberg/))\n- 2025-12 — Crunchbase News video Q&A, with Wexler — Fortune 500 network and trust ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/))\n- 2025-12 — Sourcery, with ChenLi Wang — AI is not winner-take-all ([BI via AOL](https://www.aol.com/articles/dreamworks-ceo-turned-vc-jeff-052214635.html))\n\n## How they think\n- **AI is a platform shift, apps win:** \"AI for us actually seems to be that next great, transformative moment\" ([TechCrunch, 2024-06-04](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/04/jeffrey-katzenberg-wndrco-450m-fund-venture-capital)). By late 2025 he favoured simple applications and many winners.\n- **Founders over metrics:** trust is the filter. Wang says the pair have never assessed their \"best humans based on benchmarks\" ([BI via AOL](https://www.aol.com/articles/dreamworks-ceo-turned-vc-jeff-052214635.html)).\n- **Calls:** right on Airtable, Deel, Waymo and Anduril (firm-level allocations). Wrong on Quibi ($1.75B raised, shut 2020). Orby AI no longer appears on the 2026 wndr.vc portfolio list, which may be a quiet miss ([wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/portfolio)).\n\n## Read these first\n- Aura S-1/A director bio and ownership footnotes 15 and 24: his deals split by vehicle, and his personal Aura vehicle.\n- Crunchbase News Q&A (2025-12-08): how the enterprise AI practice sells Fortune 500 access.\n- BI on the Sourcery episode (2025-12-30): his view that AI will not be winner-take-all.\n- TechCrunch fund-close piece (2024-06-04): why WndrCo moved into conventional funds.\n\n## Social engagement\nNo personal X or Substack found. He appears through firm podcasts, usually paired with Wexler (enterprise AI) or Wang (seed).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Twelve Labs | Seed extension ($12M; seed total $17M) | 2022-12-05 | no (Radical led) | Index Ventures, Spring Ventures; angels Jay Simons, Nicolas Dessaigne, Lukas Biewald | [PRWeb](https://www.prweb.com/releases/2022/12/prweb19055328.htm) |\n| Writer | Series B ($100M) | 2023-09-18 | no (ICONIQ led); quoted | Balderton, Insight, Aspect, Accenture, Vanguard | [archived WndrCo post](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/writer-the-full-stack-generative-ai-platform-announces-100-million-series-b-to-help-deliver-generative-ai-to-the-enterprise) |\n| Orby AI | Series A ($30M) | 2024-06-27 | co-led (quoted) | NEA, Wing VC (co-leads), Pear VC | [archived WndrCo post](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/orby-ai-raises-30-million-to-deliver-the-first-large-action-model-for-ai-automation-unlocking-unrivaled-enterprise-efficiencies) |\n| Creatify | Series A ($15.5M) | 2025-05-28 | co-led; board seat | Kindred Ventures (co-lead); earlier NFDG, Millennium New Horizons, Creator Ventures, Leadout | [Creatify](https://creatify.ai/blog/announcing-our-15-5m-series-a-a-new-chapter-for-creatify) |\n| Nova Sky Stories | $50M round | 2025-08-22 | participant; advisor/board | not disclosed | [startuphub.ai](https://www.startuphub.ai/news/nova-sky-stories-raises-50m-for-drone-entertainment-expansion) |\n| ElevenLabs | Undisclosed (WndrCo in the $180M C per aggregator) | 2025-01 | led (per bio) | ICONIQ, a16z | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm), [aggregator](https://seedtable.com/companies/elevenlabs/funding-rounds/series-c-2025-01) |\n| Harvey | Undisclosed (WndrCo in F and the $550M round) | 2025-12 / 2026-09 | led (per bio) | Sequoia, Kleiner, EQT | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm) |\n| Abridge | Undisclosed (WndrCo in the $300M E) | 2025-06-27 | led (per bio) | a16z, Khosla | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm) |\n| Anduril, Waymo, Deel, Meter, Granola | Undisclosed | 2020 onward (WndrCo Capital Management) | led (per bio) | — | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm) |\n| Airtable, Placer.ai, ID.me, Deel | Undisclosed | 2016 onward (WndrCo holding company) | led (per bio) | — | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm) |\n| NexHealth | Undisclosed | — | — | — | [archived 20VC notes](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/the-fast-decaying-value-of-operator-experience-silicon-valleys-future-the-gen-z-workforce---insights-from-jeffrey-katzenberg-sujay-jaswa) |\n| Quibi | $1B founding raise; $750M second raise (78 investors) | 2018-07-31; 2020 | founder | WndrCo Holdings promoter; Meg Whitman CEO | [2018 D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1748421/000174842118000001/primary_doc.xml), [2020 D/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1748421/000174842120000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Aura, Point Wild, WndrHLTH, UnifyCX | Build (holding company) | Aura board since 2021 | control | Sujay Jaswa | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm), [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Twelve Labs (2022):** WndrCo came in as a new investor in the seed extension that Radical led, about nine months after Index's $5M seed (March 2022). The extension was announced with an Oracle OCI partnership. The release says \"New investors include Jeffrey Katzenberg's WndrCo and Spring Ventures\" ([PRWeb](https://www.prweb.com/releases/2022/12/prweb19055328.htm)). The intro path was not found.\n- **Orby AI (2024):** WndrCo co-led the $30M A with NEA and Wing VC, and he was quoted on AI agents. Orby is absent from the 2026 wndr.vc portfolio page.\n- **Creatify (2025):** WndrCo co-led with Kindred Ventures after an NFDG-backed earlier round, and he took the board seat. An etcentric headline put WndrCo's check at $1.5M, which is unconfirmed ([etcentric](https://www.etcentric.org/?p=193427)).\n- **Nova Sky Stories (2025):** He joined as an advisor when WndrCo joined the $50M round, the company's first raise since buying Intel's drone-light-show unit ([startuphub.ai](https://www.startuphub.ai/news/nova-sky-stories-raises-50m-for-drone-entertainment-expansion)). The Pioneers of AI listing calls it \"WndrCo's recent partnership with Kimbal Musk\".\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| WndrCo | Founding Partner / Managing Partner | 2016 | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team), [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm) |\n| Sujay Jaswa | Co-founder; co-manager of WndrCo Holdings, LLC | 2016 | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm) |\n| Justin Wexler | Co-runs the enterprise AI practice | 2017 | [Crunchbase](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/) |\n| ChenLi Wang | Podcast partner on founder evaluation (Sourcery, Pioneers of AI) | 2016 | [BI via AOL](https://www.aol.com/articles/dreamworks-ceo-turned-vc-jeff-052214635.html) |\n| Aura (Hari Ravichandran) | Director; 24% beneficial ownership via WndrCo entities | 2021 | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm) |\n| Point Wild | Director | — | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm) |\n| Creatify | Director | 2025-05 | [Creatify](https://creatify.ai/blog/announcing-our-15-5m-series-a-a-new-chapter-for-creatify) |\n| WndrHLTH, UnifyAI | Director | — | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Kimbal Musk / Nova Sky Stories | Advisor (board per release) | 2025-08 | [startuphub.ai](https://www.startuphub.ai/news/nova-sky-stories-raises-50m-for-drone-entertainment-expansion) |\n| Meg Whitman, Ann Daly | Quibi related persons on Form Ds | 2018 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1748421/000174842118000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Matthew Stepka (Machina Ventures) | Fellow Aura director (from July 2026) | 2026 | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm) |\n| Zynga | Director | 2011-2014 | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which Harvey, ElevenLabs and Waymo rounds did he personally lead, and how large were the checks? Is the Holdings ATM LP (first sale 2026-09-09) a Harvey SPV?\n- Did he lead Granola? The S-1 says yes, but the wndr.vc bio omits it.\n- Is he an LP in any outside fund? EDGAR full-text search for \"Katzenberg\" in Form Ds returns only WndrCo and Quibi vehicles. A \"Howard Katzenberg\" on Glean Analytics/Glean IP Holdings Form Ds is a different person.\n- Read the Speed of Culture transcript PDF and the Pioneers of AI episode for sourcing detail and the Kimbal Musk deal.\n- Why did Orby AI drop off the WNDR portfolio page?\n\n## Sources\n- Aura Consolidated Group S-1/A (2026-09-02): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm\n- wndr.vc team bio (site bundle /assets/index-CuMFcD--.js, read 2026-11-10): https://wndr.vc/team\n- Form Ds: WndrCo LLC https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1695036/000169503617000001/primary_doc.xml ; Quibi 2018 https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1748421/000174842118000001/primary_doc.xml ; Quibi 2020 https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1748421/000174842120000002/primary_doc.xml ; Holdings II https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924350/000192435024000001/primary_doc.xml\n- Twelve Labs release: https://www.prweb.com/releases/2022/12/prweb19055328.htm ; KED Global https://www.kedglobal.com/newsView/ked202212060019\n- Creatify: https://creatify.ai/blog/announcing-our-15-5m-series-a-a-new-chapter-for-creatify\n- Nova Sky Stories: https://www.startuphub.ai/news/nova-sky-stories-raises-50m-for-drone-entertainment-expansion\n- Crunchbase News: https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/\n- Business Insider via AOL: https://www.aol.com/articles/dreamworks-ceo-turned-vc-jeff-052214635.html\n- Jefferies: https://www.jefferies.com/insights/markets/lessons-on-entrepreneurship-and-investing-from-jeffrey-katzenberg/\n- CNBC via NBC NY: https://www.nbcnewyork.com/news/business/money-report/hollywoods-jeffrey-katzenberg-bets-on-cybersecurity-avoids-digital-media-as-he-seeks-out-startups/5474726/?amp=1\n- Suzy / Speed of Culture: https://www.suzy.com/blog/jeffrey-katzenberg-justin-wexler-founder-managing-general-partner-wndrco\n- Metis Strategy: https://www.metisstrategy.com/interview/jeffrey-katzenberg/\n- TechCrunch: https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/04/jeffrey-katzenberg-wndrco-450m-fund-venture-capital\n", "person:jeffrey-nykun": "\n# Jeffrey Nykun\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (owner pick). Sources: wndr.vc team data (site JS bundle), archived wndrco.com team pages (2022-01 to 2026-04), superunlimited.com leadership page, SEC EDGAR (full-text search for \"Nykun\" returns 3 documents; Cider Mill Form Ds and 13Fs), and press on Placer.ai and Yassir rounds. Sources call him \"he\" and \"Jeff\".*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is WndrCo's public-markets-trained diligence chief in New York and its authorized signer on fund deals. Useful to know if WndrCo is in a growth round with you; not a frontier-AI sourcing node.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He brings Tiger-cub-style diligence to a firm built around Katzenberg's access and Dropbox operators. WndrCo says he \"oversees the firm's diligence and research activities\" ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250701id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team)). His hedge-fund coverage was business services, fintech and consumer, and his board seats match that: Placer.ai (location data), Yassir (super-app) and Super Unlimited (VPN/eSIM) ([src](https://wndr.vc/team)).\n- **Operational role:** He signs for both of WndrCo's vehicle families. He signed Figma's 2024 investors' rights agreement for WndrCo Holdings II LP ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1579878/000162828025033742/exhibit42-sx1.htm)) and Gemini's 2025 registration rights agreement for a WndrCo LLC subsidiary ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055592/000205559225000009/exhibit42gemini-regrightsa.htm)). That makes him an execution and governance partner, not just an analyst.\n- **Constraint:** He has no early-stage or AI deal on record. Seed at WndrCo belongs to ChenLi Wang and Anthony Saleh, and enterprise AI to Justin Wexler (see `firms/wndrco.md`).\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** source a pre-product research lab. Nothing in his 15 years points there.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **His Super Unlimited bio lists two employers that WndrCo's own bio leaves out:** Artisan Partners (Antero Peak Group) and Luxor Capital. It also calls WndrCo \"a ~$750 million investment firm that builds and buys consumer technology businesses\" ([src](https://www.superunlimited.com)). That figure is below the firm's own \"$1.3B managed since 2016\" claim (see `firms/wndrco.md`), which suggests a narrower count, probably the Build holding company only (inference).\n- **Authorized signatory for both WndrCo families:** \"WNDRCO HOLDINGS II LP ... /s/ Jeffrey Nykun\" on Figma's A&R IRA dated 2024-05-15 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1579878/000162828025033742/exhibit42-sx1.htm)), and \"WC INVESTOR TWO HOLDINGS, LLC\" (sole member WndrCo Holdings, LLC, manager WndrCo LLC) on Gemini's agreement dated 2025-09-12 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055592/000205559225000009/exhibit42gemini-regrightsa.htm)). A signature shows authority, not that he sourced the deals. But the Gemini stake appears nowhere in WndrCo's portfolio pages or `firms/wndrco.md`.\n- **Cider Mill was smaller than the bio says, and it wound down.** His bio describes it as \"a $400 million AUM long-short hedge fund\" ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250701id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team)). Form D/As filed 2018-03-29 show $101.6M sold to 13 investors in the domestic fund ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1670573/000095014218000697/primary_doc.xml)) and $77M sold to 5 investors in the offshore fund ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1670572/000095014218000698/primary_doc.xml)). The 13F long book was $140.5M at 2016-12-31 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1672201/000167220117000002/CiderMill_13FXML_12312016.xml)) and $72.2M at 2019-03-31 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1672201/000167220119000004/CiderMill_13F_03312019.xml)), after which 13F filings stop. 13Fs cover only US long equity, so this does not disprove \"$400M\" gross. It does show the fund shrinking into 2019. An aggregator dates his Cider Mill tenure 2014–2019 ([aggregator](https://ch.zonebourse.com/insider/JEFFREY-NYKUN-A3K2ID/)).\n- **Payments-heavy book:** Cider Mill's last 13F was led by Worldpay, Berry Global, FIS, Adobe and Salesforce, with FleetCor, TransUnion, S&P Global, ICE and Global Payments also held ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1672201/000167220119000004/CiderMill_13F_03312019.xml)). That matches his stated fintech and business-services coverage. Cider Mill's 13Fs were signed by Thomas Wilcox as Managing Member. This makes Wilcox the likely \"former Shumway Capital CIO\" in his bio (identity inferred from the filings, not stated).\n- **Hired in as GP.** He is listed as \"General Partner\" on the 2022-01-01 team snapshot, when Justin Wexler was still unlabelled staff ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20220101id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team)). An aggregator gives his start as May 2021 ([aggregator](https://ch.zonebourse.com/insider/JEFFREY-NYKUN-A3K2ID/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** His named deals and boards are Super Unlimited (privacy/VPN/eSIM, WndrCo Build), Yassir (North African super-app, Series A 2021), Placer.ai (location analytics, Series C 2022), and as signatory Figma (2024 secondary/common purchase) and Gemini (crypto). None is frontier AI, a lab, chips or AI-native automation, and none is seed. He matters to you in two cases: when WndrCo diligences a growth AI round you are also in (Harvey, Cursor and Abridge are firm-level, not attributed to him), or as a route to the Tiger-cub/Shumway network. He is not an LP prospect: no fund commitments of his were found.\n\n## Background\n- Princeton University, AB/BA Economics, magna cum laude ([src](https://wndr.vc/team)). An aggregator gives the year as 2000 ([aggregator](https://ch.zonebourse.com/insider/JEFFREY-NYKUN-A3K2ID/)).\n- Morgan Stanley, M&A investment banking analyst; then TPG, private equity associate ([src](https://wndr.vc/team)).\n- Luxor Capital, Senior Analyst (dates not found; [src](https://www.superunlimited.com)).\n- Shumway Capital, Managing Director. The firm is described as a \"$9 billion AUM Tiger Cub\" ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250701id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team)). An aggregator gives 2005–2011 ([aggregator](https://ch.zonebourse.com/insider/JEFFREY-NYKUN-A3K2ID/)).\n- Cider Mill Investments (Greenwich, CT), co-founder and Managing Director, with Shumway's former CIO and COO. Funds were first filed 2016-03-31 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1670573/000095014216003415/primary_doc.xml)); an aggregator gives 2014–2019.\n- Artisan Partners, Senior Analyst in the Antero Peak Group, which ran long-only and long-short strategies ([src](https://www.superunlimited.com)). This was \"most recently\" before WndrCo, so roughly 2019–2021 (inference).\n- WndrCo, General Partner from 2021; leads the New York office ([src](https://wndr.vc/team)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://wndr.vc/team | Firm bio (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/jeff-nykun-977a31181/ | Linked from wndr.vc |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. No podcast, conference or article appearance turned up in search or on WndrCo's news archive.\n\n## How they think\n- No public writing or talks, so his views are inferred from his record: fundamental long-short analysis of business services, fintech and consumer ([src](https://wndr.vc/team)), applied to private growth companies with data or transaction businesses (Placer.ai, Yassir).\n- Contrarian views: nothing found.\n- Calls right or wrong: Placer.ai's $1B Series C (2022) is a firm-level call. Cider Mill's shrinkage into 2019 (13F data above) is the only visible track-record signal, and it is ambiguous.\n\n## Read these first\n- WndrCo team bio (archived 2025-07): https://web.archive.org/web/20250701id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team (career and role).\n- Super Unlimited leadership page: https://www.superunlimited.com (fuller career, including Artisan and Luxor).\n- Figma A&R IRA signature page (2024): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1579878/000162828025033742/exhibit42-sx1.htm (his signing role).\n- Cider Mill 13F, Q1 2019: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1672201/000167220119000004/CiderMill_13F_03312019.xml (his last hedge-fund book).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public social presence.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Super Unlimited (VPN, eSIM, 2nd Line+) | Undisclosed (WndrCo Build; board member) | — | WndrCo control/build | 8VC; Bing Gordon on board | [src](https://www.superunlimited.com), [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Yassir | Series A ($30M), WndrCo participant; Nykun board member/observer | 2021-11-29 | No single lead named | DN Capital, Kismet, Spike, Quiet, Endeavor Catalyst, FJ Labs, VentureSouq | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2021/11/29/algerias-yassir-picks-up-30m-to-build-a-super-app-in-north-africa/) |\n| Placer.ai | Series C ($100M at $1B), WndrCo participant; Nykun board member/observer | 2022-01-12 | No (Josh Buckley led) | Fifth Wall, Array, Lachy Groom, MMC | [VentureBeat](https://venturebeat.com/business/location-analytics-company-placer-ai-nabs-100m-to-glean-insights-from-foot-traffic) |\n| Figma | Secondary (Class A common purchase, A&R IRA), signatory for WndrCo Holdings II | 2024-05-15 | No | ICONIQ, Thrive, Greenoaks, Sequoia (SC U.S. Growth IX), SV Angel Growth | [SEC](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1579878/000162828025033742/exhibit42-sx1.htm) |\n| Gemini | Undisclosed (pre-IPO stake held by WC Investor Two Holdings); signatory on registration rights agreement | 2025-09-12 | No | Winklevoss Capital, Jane Street, Van Eck, x15ventures | [SEC](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055592/000205559225000009/exhibit42gemini-regrightsa.htm) |\n\nBoard seats come from the firm bio. The bio does not say which round each seat came with. The Yassir Series A falls about six months after his start, but no source credits him with sourcing it.\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found on how any of his deals came about. Super Unlimited is a WndrCo Build company: Jaswa chairs it, venture partner Tanuj Chatterjee is CEO, and Nykun and Bing Gordon (Kleiner Perkins, EA co-founder) sit on the board ([src](https://www.superunlimited.com)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| WndrCo | General Partner; leads NY office, diligence and research | 2021 | [src](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Shumway Capital | Managing Director | 2005–2011 (aggregator) | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250701id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team) |\n| Cider Mill Investments | Co-founder, Managing Director | 2014–2019 (aggregator); funds filed 2016 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1670573/000095014218000697/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Thomas Wilcox | Cider Mill managing member and 13F signer; likely the former Shumway CIO he reunited with (inferred) | 2016 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1672201/000167220119000004/CiderMill_13F_03312019.xml) |\n| Artisan Partners (Antero Peak Group) | Senior Analyst | before 2021 | [src](https://www.superunlimited.com) |\n| Luxor Capital | Senior Analyst | dates not found | [src](https://www.superunlimited.com) |\n| Sujay Jaswa | Fellow board member at Super Unlimited (Jaswa chairs it) | — | [src](https://www.superunlimited.com) |\n| Tanuj Chatterjee | Super Unlimited CEO, WndrCo venture partner | — | [src](https://www.superunlimited.com) |\n| Bing Gordon | Fellow Super Unlimited board member | — | [src](https://www.superunlimited.com) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which round brought the Gemini stake into WndrCo LLC (WC Investor Two Holdings)? It might be the 2021 round; not confirmed.\n- Was Figma's 2024-05 purchase WndrCo's first Figma position, or a top-up? Jaswa is credited with leading Figma.\n- Super Unlimited's tag is wrong. `data/company_tags.csv` says \"Mobile gaming studio\", but superunlimited.com describes VPN, eSIM and security products. The owner should fix the base tag.\n- Cider Mill's \"$400M AUM\" versus Form D and 13F totals of about $180M.\n- Has he worked on any WndrCo AI growth deal (Harvey, Cursor, Abridge)? None is attributed to him.\n\n## Sources\n- https://wndr.vc/team (JS bundle /assets/index-CuMFcD--.js, read 2026-11-10)\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20220101id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team ; .../20230101id_/... ; .../20240701id_/... ; .../20250701id_/... ; .../20260415id_/...\n- https://www.superunlimited.com (leadership bios, read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1579878/000162828025033742/exhibit42-sx1.htm (Figma A&R IRA, 2024-05-15)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055592/000205559225000009/exhibit42gemini-regrightsa.htm (Gemini RRA, 2025-09-12)\n- Cider Mill: Form D https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1670573/000095014216003415/primary_doc.xml ; D/A https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1670573/000095014218000697/primary_doc.xml ; offshore D/A https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1670572/000095014218000698/primary_doc.xml ; 13Fs https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1672201/000167220117000002/CiderMill_13FXML_12312016.xml and https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1672201/000167220119000004/CiderMill_13F_03312019.xml\n- https://techcrunch.com/2021/11/29/algerias-yassir-picks-up-30m-to-build-a-super-app-in-north-africa/\n- https://venturebeat.com/business/location-analytics-company-placer-ai-nabs-100m-to-glean-insights-from-foot-traffic\n- Aggregator: https://ch.zonebourse.com/insider/JEFFREY-NYKUN-A3K2ID/ (job dates, Princeton 2000)\n", "person:jennifer-li": "\n# Jennifer Li\n\n## One-line take\nShe is the a16z Infra GP behind its generative-media and voice book: she co-led ElevenLabs' Series A, led fal's seed, and co-signed a16z's Ideogram and Phota creative-model deals. In October 2026 she solo-bylined Preference Model, an RL-environments company for frontier labs founded by an early Anthropic pretraining engineer. That deal is the reason to watch her.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins.** She is a product operator, not a researcher. She led product at Solvvy (acquired by Zoom) and ran self-service and analytics products at AppDynamics (acquired by Cisco for $3.7B) ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jennifer-li/)). Her stated filter is that the tech is \"only like half or maybe 60% of the equation\" and founders still have to package it ([GTMnow, 2026-04-28](https://www.buzzsprout.com/2090897/episodes/19088734-vc-inside-a16z-s-1-7b-infrastructure-bet-jennifer-li-general-partner)). She gets in years before the round and then backs the winner repeatedly. She met fal's founders \"three years ago\" before the 2025 Series B ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-fal/)), and met ElevenLabs when it \"was two people in London\" ([src](https://a16z.com/where-you-build-is-who-you-are-the-elevenlabs-story/)).\n- **What constrains her.** Board load and scope:\n  - She sits on 9 boards (ElevenLabs, fal, Mintlify, Mux, Pylon, Rasa, Reducto, Stainless, Stacker) and observes on 5 more (Fivetran, Ideogram, MotherDuck, NX, Vantage) ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jennifer-li/)).\n  - Her own deals are devtools, data infra and creative models, not pre-training labs. Lab-scale and compute deals on the Infra team sit with Casado, Midha and others. When she names OpenAI, Thinking Machines and SSI, she is describing the firm's book, not hers ([GTMnow](https://www.buzzsprout.com/2090897/episodes/19088734-vc-inside-a16z-s-1-7b-infrastructure-bet-jennifer-li-general-partner)).\n  - She co-signs most big deals with Casado, Sarah Wang or Yoko Li.\n- **What she would never do.** Nothing sourced. By her own framing, she would not back a research-only team with no product instinct.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The fal sequence is lead, then not-lead, then lead again.** a16z led fal's ~$9M seed with Village Global. Kindred Ventures, not a16z, led the $14M Series A, with a16z only participating. \"The Seed round was led by Andreessen Horowitz\" ([fal blog, 2024-09-18](https://blog.fal.ai/generative-media-needs-speed-fal-has-raised-23m-to-accelerate/)). a16z then co-led the Series B with Notable and took the board seat (2025-02-12, [src](https://blog.fal.ai/fal-raises-49m-series-b-to-power-the-future-of-ai-video/)). So she let another firm price the A rather than lose the company, then came back.\n- **She met fal before it was fal.** \"When I first met the cofounders Burkay Gur and Gorkem Yurtseven three years ago\" (post dated 2025-02-12, so about 2022). The founders went through \"a few iterations on different ideas\" before generative-media infra ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-fal/)).\n- **The ElevenLabs trigger was a demo crossing the uncanny valley.** She had been watching synthetic voice for years: \"we've been tracking the first synthetic voice space for many years.\" The demo she remembers was a Gandalf-style voice narrating a book ([GTMnow transcript, 2026-04-28](https://www.buzzsprout.com/2090897/episodes/19088734-vc-inside-a16z-s-1-7b-infrastructure-bet-jennifer-li-general-partner)). a16z co-led the June 2023 $19M Series A with Nat Friedman and Daniel Gross ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-elevenlabs/)). Credo and Concept were already in, so a16z was not first ([ElevenLabs](https://elevenlabs.io/blog/elevenlabs-launches-new-generative-voice-ai-products-and-announces-19m-series-a-round-led-by-nat-friedman-daniel-gross-and-andreessen-horowitz)). The episode notes say she backed it \"from Series A all the way through Series D.\"\n- **Her founder test: ship a patchwork before the model can.** The best founders \"build a patchwork version of that functionality into the application ahead of time.\" They then backfill it when model capability arrives a quarter or two later ([GTMnow transcript](https://www.buzzsprout.com/2090897/episodes/19088734-vc-inside-a16z-s-1-7b-infrastructure-bet-jennifer-li-general-partner)).\n- **She bets on default-brand \"kingmaking\" in AI.** She says AI categories produce a single default brand, with ElevenLabs standing for voice and fal for media models, rather than the usual enterprise oligopoly: \"you can call it kingmaking or like a brand effect\" (same source).\n- **Open source catching up is the \"best news.\"** On 2026-27: \"open source is catching up really closely and really fast.\" She also named world models and VLMs as the next unlocks (same source).\n- **Preference Model is her most lab-adjacent bet, and it is hers alone.** The 2026-10-07 a16z post is bylined by her only. It describes RL environments for \"the domain that matters most to the labs right now: AI research and ML engineering itself.\" Founder Jennifer Zhou, \"an early member of Anthropic's team,\" built Claude's pretraining datasets and tokenizers ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-preference-model/)). Marco Mascorro's own site credits himself as the a16z seed lead ([src](https://mascobot.com/investing)). That he has left and she alone holds the byline suggests she inherited the deal. This is inference.\n- **The GTMnow team says they co-invest with her.** The host said \"We've done some deals with Jennifer,\" so GTMfund is a seed-stage co-investor and feeder ([GTMnow](https://www.buzzsprout.com/2090897/episodes/19088734-vc-inside-a16z-s-1-7b-infrastructure-bet-jennifer-li-general-partner)).\n- **Casado tied her promotion to autonomy.** \"Promoting Jennifer to general partner means that much more autonomy in deal making\" ([TechCrunch, 2024-04-19](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/19/andreessen-horowitz-jennifer-li-125-billion-dollar-infrastructure-fund/)). Before that, as an investing partner, she already wrote checks and took board seats (Mux, MotherDuck, ElevenLabs). (This row already exists in `data/insights.csv`; it is not re-emitted.)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Her book is AI devtools, data infra and generative-media models, which are not pre-training labs or chips. Deals that matter to me:\n- **Preference Model** (RL environments for labs, ex-Anthropic founder, 2026-10): high relevance.\n- **TypeSafe AI** ($870M Series A at $7.5B, 2026-10-09): she co-signed, but Casado took the board seat.\n- **ElevenLabs** (2023 Series A co-lead, then A to C): proof she can hold a model company through growth.\n- **fal** (seed lead): generative-media inference.\n- **Ideogram**: ex-Google Imagen team; a16z in since seed, led the A.\n\nShe is a useful co-investor and reference for applied-model and inference companies. She is not the a16z partner for a $600M new-lab seed.\n\n## Background\n- Born and raised in northern China; moved to the U.S. for school ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jennifer-li/)).\n- MS Software Engineering, Carnegie Mellon; MS Technology Management, RPI ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jennifer-li/)).\n- AppDynamics: ran self-service and analytics products. Cisco later acquired it for $3.7B ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jennifer-li/)). Dates not stated.\n- Kleiner Perkins Product Fellow, 2016 ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jennifer-li/)).\n- Solvvy: led product at this conversational-AI company, later acquired by Zoom ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jennifer-li/); TechCrunch says Zoom bought it in 2022, [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/19/andreessen-horowitz-jennifer-li-125-billion-dollar-infrastructure-fund/)).\n- Joined a16z in 2018 as an investing partner on Infra. Helped find Fivetran and dbt, both led by Casado ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/19/andreessen-horowitz-jennifer-li-125-billion-dollar-infrastructure-fund/)).\n- Promoted to GP in April 2024, a16z's 27th GP, in her early 30s, to help invest the $1.25B Infra fund ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/19/andreessen-horowitz-jennifer-li-125-billion-dollar-infrastructure-fund/)). The next Infra allocation is $1.7B of a16z's $15B raise ([TechCrunch Equity, 2026-02-04](https://techcrunch.com/podcast/what-a16z-is-actually-funding-and-what-its-ignoring-when-it-comes-to-ai-infra/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/jennifer-li/ | a16z author page: bio, boards, ~47 posts and podcasts (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes on a16z.com (e.g. \"Your Data Agents Need Context\", \"State of Generative Media 2026\") |\n| X | https://x.com/jenniferhli | Linked from the a16z page; follower count not verified (X returned 402) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/jenniferhli/ | Linked from the a16z page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none as host | Frequent guest/interviewer on a16z podcasts (fal, Mintlify, ElevenLabs, Vals episodes) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-04-28 — GTMnow Podcast — \"VC: Inside a16z's $1.7B Infrastructure Bet\" — [link](https://www.buzzsprout.com/2090897/episodes/19088734-vc-inside-a16z-s-1-7b-infrastructure-bet-jennifer-li-general-partner) — the 60% tech / product split, patchwork-ahead-of-models, kingmaking, ElevenLabs demo, open source catching up.\n- 2026-02-04 — TechCrunch Equity (Julie Bort) — \"What a16z is actually funding (and what it's ignoring) when it comes to AI infra\" — [link](https://techcrunch.com/podcast/what-a16z-is-actually-funding-and-what-its-ignoring-when-it-comes-to-ai-infra/) — page has no quotes from her; only the episode teaser was read.\n- 2025-11-04 — a16z Runtime / essay — \"Where you build is who you are: the ElevenLabs story\" — [link](https://a16z.com/where-you-build-is-who-you-are-the-elevenlabs-story/).\n- Undated a16z podcasts (from the author page): \"Who Grades the AI Models? | Ben Horowitz & Rayan Krishnan\" (Vals), \"The Next Frontier of AI Video Is Control\", \"Performance and Passion: Fal's Approach to AI Inference\", \"How Mintlify Is Rebuilding Documentation for Coding Agents\", \"Ryo Lu (Cursor)\", \"ElevenLabs CEO: Why Voice is the Next AI Interface\" ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jennifer-li/)). None were listened to for this profile.\n\n## How they think\n- **Infra is layered, not replaced.** Storage, compute, orchestration, agent tools and memory are all being rebuilt for AI workloads on top of older layers ([GTMnow](https://www.buzzsprout.com/2090897/episodes/19088734-vc-inside-a16z-s-1-7b-infrastructure-bet-jennifer-li-general-partner)).\n- **Product wins at least 40% of the game**, even in research-driven categories.\n- **Default-brand winners:** ElevenLabs means voice and fal means media models. GTM speed matters more than in classic enterprise.\n- **Open source is closing on frontier models.** That favors composite, multi-model systems; world models and VLMs come next.\n- **The API is the product in the AI era**: \"in the AI era, the API interface is the product\" ([Stainless post, 2024-12-10](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-stainless/)).\n- **Calls that look right:** ElevenLabs Series A (2023, now cited at $11B+ in [GTMnow show notes](https://www.buzzsprout.com/2090897/episodes/19088734-vc-inside-a16z-s-1-7b-infrastructure-bet-jennifer-li-general-partner)), fal seed. Calls that look wrong: nothing sourced.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Investing in Preference Model](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-preference-model/) (2026-10-07): her solo bet on RL environments for labs.\n- [Investing in fal](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-fal/) (2025-02-12): how she picks infra founders and how long she tracks them.\n- [Where you build is who you are: the ElevenLabs story](https://a16z.com/where-you-build-is-who-you-are-the-elevenlabs-story/) (2025-11-04).\n- [The State of Generative Media 2026](https://a16z.com/the-state-of-generative-media-2026/) (with Justine Moore).\n- [Your Data Agents Need Context](https://a16z.com/your-data-agents-need-context/) (with Jason Cui).\n\n## Social engagement\nX could not be fetched (HTTP 402), so there is no sourced read on replies or amplification. Her co-author graph on a16z.com is her internal network:\n- Infra: Yoko Li (Mintlify, Stainless, Reducto, Phota, Vals), Martin Casado, Sarah Wang, Jason Cui, Raghu Raghuram, Shangda Xu.\n- Consumer and creative: Justine Moore, Bryan Kim.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ElevenLabs | Series A ($19M) | 2023-06-20 | co-led (a16z) | Nat Friedman, Daniel Gross (co-leads); Credo, Concept | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-elevenlabs/) |\n| fal | Seed (~$9M) | undisclosed (announced 2024-09-18) | yes (a16z) | Village Global | [fal](https://blog.fal.ai/generative-media-needs-speed-fal-has-raised-23m-to-accelerate/) |\n| fal | Series A ($14M) | 2024-09-18 | no | Kindred Ventures (lead), First Round | [fal](https://blog.fal.ai/generative-media-needs-speed-fal-has-raised-23m-to-accelerate/) |\n| fal | Series B ($49M) | 2025-02-12 | co-led; board | Notable Capital (Glenn Solomon) | [fal](https://blog.fal.ai/fal-raises-49m-series-b-to-power-the-future-of-ai-video/) |\n| Ideogram | Series A | 2024-02-28 | yes (a16z); observer | a16z in since seed; co-signer Casado | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-ideogram/) |\n| Mintlify | Series A | 2024-09-05 | yes (a16z); board | co-signer Yoko Li | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mintlify/) |\n| Stainless | undisclosed | 2024-12-10 | not stated; board | co-signer Yoko Li | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-stainless/) |\n| Phota Labs | Seed | 2025-09-16 | yes (a16z) | co-signers Yoko Li, Casado | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-phota-labs/) |\n| Reducto | undisclosed | 2025-10-14 | not stated; board | co-signer Yoko Li | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-reducto/) |\n| Exa | Series C ($250M) | 2026-05-20 | yes (a16z) | Sarah Wang, Jason Cui co-signers | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/) |\n| Vals AI | Series A ($40M at $400M) | 2026-08-13 | yes (a16z) | 8VC, Pear, Bloomberg Beta (existing) | [Vals](https://www.vals.ai/blogs/series-a) |\n| Preference Model | undisclosed (Mascorro says seed) | 2026-10-07 | not stated | solo byline | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-preference-model/) |\n| TypeSafe AI | Series A ($870M at $7.5B) | 2026-10-09 | yes (a16z); Casado board | Sequoia, DCVC (existing) | [TypeSafe](https://typesafe.ai/blog/series-ai) |\n| Mux, MotherDuck | led a16z investment | pre-2024 | yes | — | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/19/andreessen-horowitz-jennifer-li-125-billion-dollar-infrastructure-fund/) |\n| Pylon, Resend, Upstash, Crossbeam, Stacker, Vantage | a16z announcements | various | not checked | — | [author page](https://a16z.com/author/jennifer-li/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **fal.** She met Burkay Gur and Gorkem Yurtseven in about 2022, while they were still iterating on ideas, and a16z led the seed. Kindred Ventures led the Series A. She co-led the Series B with Glenn Solomon of Notable, and both joined the board. Sonya Huang (Sequoia) led the Series D at $4.5B in December 2025, with a16z participating ([fal B](https://blog.fal.ai/fal-raises-49m-series-b-to-power-the-future-of-ai-video/), [TechCrunch D](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/09/fal-nabs-140m-in-fresh-funding-led-by-sequoia-tripling-valuation-to-4-5b/)).\n- **ElevenLabs.** a16z had tracked synthetic voice for years. The founders' demo convinced her that it had finally crossed the uncanny valley. The Series A was co-led with Nat Friedman and Daniel Gross after a small inception round by Credo and Concept. The GTMnow host put that round at $2M, but this is unverified. She took the board seat ([a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-elevenlabs/), [GTMnow](https://www.buzzsprout.com/2090897/episodes/19088734-vc-inside-a16z-s-1-7b-infrastructure-bet-jennifer-li-general-partner)).\n- **TypeSafe AI.** The a16z post says \"We've long been fans of Diogo,\" with alignment going back \"many years.\" Five a16z authors co-signed, including both founders of the firm ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-typesafe-ai/)).\n- **Preference Model.** The founders are Jennifer Zhou (ex-Anthropic pretraining data) and Ning Cao (ex-DatologyAI). a16z's post went up the week they open-sourced their Karotte RL framework ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-preference-model/)). Who sourced the deal is disputed: Mascorro's site claims the seed lead ([src](https://mascobot.com/investing)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Martin Casado | Infra GP and senior partner; co-led Fivetran/dbt sourcing; framed her GP promotion | 2018 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/19/andreessen-horowitz-jennifer-li-125-billion-dollar-infrastructure-fund/) |\n| Yoko Li (a16z) | Most frequent co-signer: Mintlify, Stainless, Phota, Reducto, Vals | 2024 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mintlify/) |\n| Sarah Wang (a16z) | Co-signer on Crossbeam, Exa, TypeSafe | 2021 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-crossbeam/) |\n| Justine Moore, Bryan Kim (a16z) | ElevenLabs Series A co-authors | 2023 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-elevenlabs/) |\n| Nat Friedman, Daniel Gross | ElevenLabs Series A co-leads | 2023 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-elevenlabs/) |\n| Glenn Solomon (Notable Capital) | fal Series B co-lead and co-board member | 2025 | [fal](https://blog.fal.ai/fal-raises-49m-series-b-to-power-the-future-of-ai-video/) |\n| Kindred Ventures | Led fal's Series A after her seed | 2024 | [fal](https://blog.fal.ai/generative-media-needs-speed-fal-has-raised-23m-to-accelerate/) |\n| Sonya Huang (Sequoia) | Led fal's Series D; Sequoia followed in TypeSafe | 2025 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/09/fal-nabs-140m-in-fresh-funding-led-by-sequoia-tripling-valuation-to-4-5b/) |\n| Mati and Piotr (ElevenLabs founders) | Board member since Series A | 2023 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/where-you-build-is-who-you-are-the-elevenlabs-story/) |\n| Burkay Gur, Gorkem Yurtseven (fal) | Board; first met about 2022 | 2022 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-fal/) |\n| Jennifer Zhou, Ning Cao (Preference Model) | Bylined the investment | 2026 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-preference-model/) |\n| Marco Mascorro | Ex-a16z; claims the Preference Model seed lead she bylined | 2026 | [mascobot.com](https://mascobot.com/investing) |\n| GTMfund | Host said \"We've done some deals with Jennifer\" | 2026 | [GTMnow](https://www.buzzsprout.com/2090897/episodes/19088734-vc-inside-a16z-s-1-7b-infrastructure-bet-jennifer-li-general-partner) |\n\n## LP links\nNothing found showing her as an LP in any fund (checked the a16z bio, TechCrunch and GTMnow). No `-lp.csv` was written.\n\n## Open questions\n- What was the Preference Model round size, and did a16z lead? Did she take a board seat?\n- When exactly was fal's seed, and was a16z the first institutional money?\n- What are the full round terms for Stainless and Reducto, and did a16z lead?\n- Does she angel-invest personally, or sit as an LP in seed funds (for example GTMfund or the ElevenLabs inception investors)?\n- What did she say in the TechCrunch Equity episode? It was not transcribed here.\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/jennifer-li/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/19/andreessen-horowitz-jennifer-li-125-billion-dollar-infrastructure-fund/\n- https://www.buzzsprout.com/2090897/episodes/19088734-vc-inside-a16z-s-1-7b-infrastructure-bet-jennifer-li-general-partner\n- https://techcrunch.com/podcast/what-a16z-is-actually-funding-and-what-its-ignoring-when-it-comes-to-ai-infra/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-fal/\n- https://blog.fal.ai/generative-media-needs-speed-fal-has-raised-23m-to-accelerate/\n- https://blog.fal.ai/fal-raises-49m-series-b-to-power-the-future-of-ai-video/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/09/fal-nabs-140m-in-fresh-funding-led-by-sequoia-tripling-valuation-to-4-5b/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-elevenlabs/\n- https://elevenlabs.io/blog/elevenlabs-launches-new-generative-voice-ai-products-and-announces-19m-series-a-round-led-by-nat-friedman-daniel-gross-and-andreessen-horowitz\n- https://a16z.com/where-you-build-is-who-you-are-the-elevenlabs-story/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-preference-model/\n- https://mascobot.com/investing\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-typesafe-ai/\n- https://typesafe.ai/blog/series-ai\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-ideogram/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-phota-labs/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mintlify/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-stainless/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-reducto/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/\n- https://www.vals.ai/blogs/series-a\n", "person:jensen-huang": "\n# Jensen Huang\n\n## One-line take\nHuang is the real pricer behind NVIDIA's $10B-plus checks into frontier labs. He treats each check as a way to win a customer, not as a sourced venture bet. He is worth modelling as the biggest force behind lab-round valuations, but he is not someone the owner can build a relationship with.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he doesn't go looking for deals. Labs offer him allocation because NVIDIA's compute is the scarce input. In his own words on OpenAI, \"they're giving us the opportunity to invest\" ([BG2, 2025-09-26, third-party transcript](https://singjupost.com/transcript-nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-on-openai-future-of-compute-and-the-american-dream-bg2-pod/)). On xAI, Musk offered NVIDIA a place in the round ([Business Insider via AOL, 2025-10-08](https://www.aol.com/articles/jensen-huang-says-regrets-not-133927715.html)). His leverage is the price and the speed, not diligence. The Information reports he \"often sets prices himself\", and that he pushed the Hugging Face price ($12.9B) through fast once Salesforce showed interest ([Dealroom summary of The Information, 2026-10-07](https://dealroom.co/news/info-i81usb-where-nvidias-100-billion-dealmaking-juggernaut-will-go-next/)).\n- **His stated thesis:** every investment is \"associated with expanding the reach of CUDA\". He also says NVIDIA takes a share of the lab's company instead of giving up a share of its own (Q3 FY26 call, 2025-11-19, [third-party transcript](https://transcripts.platformaeronaut.com/transcripts/NVDA-3Q26-transcript)). The check is a way to win a customer, and he also expects \"extraordinary returns\" (same call).\n- **What constrains him:** the circularity, and the IPO window. The Sept 2025 OpenAI letter of intent (up to $100B, staged per gigawatt) became a $30B check. By March 2026 he said the $100B was \"probably not in the cards\" because OpenAI is going public, and that the OpenAI and Anthropic checks are probably NVIDIA's last in each ([pymnts, 2026-03-04](https://www.pymnts.com/artificial-intelligence-2/2026/nvidia-signals-final-investments-in-openai-and-anthropic/), [Yahoo Finance](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-100b-123431494.html)).\n- **What he'd never do:** pick one lab. He backs OpenAI, xAI and Anthropic alike. He invested in Anthropic five months after publicly saying he disagreed with almost everything its CEO says, so ideology doesn't stop him. Winning the customer comes first.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He admits he underinvested early, twice.** On OpenAI, he said NVIDIA was invited in early, \"We were so poor, we were so poor we didn't invest enough\" ([BG2, 2025-09-26](https://singjupost.com/transcript-nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-on-openai-future-of-compute-and-the-american-dream-bg2-pod/)). On xAI, his only regret \"is that I didn't give him more money\" ([CNBC via Business Insider/AOL, 2025-10-08](https://www.aol.com/articles/jensen-huang-says-regrets-not-133927715.html)). This \"should have written more\" reflex explains why NVIDIA's lab checks got so much bigger in 2025-26 (see the ledger in `sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md`).\n- **He says the investment is not tied to the GPU purchase.** On OpenAI: \"The investment side of it is not tied to anything\" ([BG2](https://singjupost.com/transcript-nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-on-openai-future-of-compute-and-the-american-dream-bg2-pod/)). The press release that same week says the $100B is invested \"progressively as each gigawatt is deployed\" ([NVIDIA newsroom, 2025-09-22](https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/openai-and-nvidia-announce-strategic-partnership-to-deploy-10gw-of-nvidia-systems)). He talks about equity as a separate opportunity, while the documents tie it to deployment.\n- **The Anthropic turn: a public enemy became a $10B position in five months.** At VivaTech (2025-06-11) he said Amodei believes \"AI is so scary that only they should do it\" ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2025/06/11/nvidia-jensen-huang-disagress-anthropic-ceo-dario-amodei-ai-jobs/)). On 2025-11-18 NVIDIA committed up to $10B to Anthropic, alongside Anthropic's commitment to up to 1GW of NVIDIA systems ([Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/microsoft-nvidia-anthropic-announce-strategic-partnerships)). By May 2026 he explained the deal as buying a customer: \"Our coverage for Anthropic had been largely zero until this\" ([TechCrunch, 2026-05-20](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/20/nvidia-posts-another-record-quarter-reveals-43-billion-of-holdings-in-startups/)).\n- **He says he would invest in \"almost everything\" Musk does.** \"Almost everything Elon's part of, you really want to be part of as well\" ([BI/AOL](https://www.aol.com/articles/jensen-huang-says-regrets-not-133927715.html)). This is a founder-following rule, not a sector thesis.\n- **He frames the IPO as the end of the window.** \"This might be the last time we'll have the opportunity to invest in a consequential company like this\" ([pymnts, 2026-03-04](https://www.pymnts.com/artificial-intelligence-2/2026/nvidia-signals-final-investments-in-openai-and-anthropic/)). This implies NVIDIA's big strategic money moves down to the next tier of labs (Thinking Machines, Reflection, Mistral, the 2026 lab seeds) once OpenAI and Anthropic list.\n- **The founder rule of thumb he repeats:** \"create a startup before the market grows\" and take \"100% of a tiny company\" ([BG2](https://singjupost.com/transcript-nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-on-openai-future-of-compute-and-the-american-dream-bg2-pod/)). This is founder advice, not investing advice. But it matches NVIDIA showing up in the first priced round of new labs from mid-2025.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium, as a market force. Skip, as a relationship.** He is the final pricer on NVIDIA's strategic checks into frontier labs and compute (OpenAI, Anthropic, xAI, and, per the firm file, Thinking Machines, Reflection and Mistral). NVIDIA is the most common co-investor in the $600M-class lab seeds THESIS.md targets. Knowing his logic (win the customer, take equity instead of giving it, act before the IPO) helps predict when NVIDIA shows up and how far it pushes prices. But he is not a principal or a recently promoted partner, not an LP, and not reachable for the owner. The relationship runs company to company, through lab founders. For the owner the people to know are the operators: Mohamed \"Sid\" Siddeek (NVentures, `people/mohamed-sid-siddeek.md`) and Vishal Bhagwati (corporate development, `people/vishal-bhagwati.md`).\n\n## Background\n- BSEE, Oregon State University; MSEE, Stanford ([NVIDIA bio](https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/bios/jensen-huang)). Years not given in the source retrieved.\n- LSI Logic and Advanced Micro Devices before NVIDIA (same bio; dates not given there).\n- Founded NVIDIA in 1993 and has been president, CEO and a director ever since (same bio).\n- Member of the National Academy of Engineering; appointed to the President's Council of Advisors on Science and Technology in 2026 (same bio).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/bios/jensen-huang | Official bio |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No personal X account confirmed in sources checked |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest appearances only (BG2 2025-09-26) and quarterly earnings calls |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-06-11 — VivaTech Paris press briefing — criticized Amodei's view on jobs and safety ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2025/06/11/nvidia-jensen-huang-disagress-anthropic-ceo-dario-amodei-ai-jobs/)).\n- 2025-09-26 — BG2 Pod (Brad Gerstner, Bill Gurley) — \"NVIDIA: OpenAI, Future of Compute, and the American Dream\" — OpenAI as a likely \"next multi-trillion dollar hyperscale company\"; his regret at underinvesting early; investments in xAI and CoreWeave ([third-party transcript](https://singjupost.com/transcript-nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-on-openai-future-of-compute-and-the-american-dream-bg2-pod/)).\n- 2025-10-08 — CNBC interview — xAI regret: \"I didn't give him more money\" ([BI via AOL](https://www.aol.com/articles/jensen-huang-says-regrets-not-133927715.html)).\n- 2025-11-19 — Q3 FY26 earnings call — gave the CUDA-reach and equity-for-equity investment thesis ([third-party transcript](https://transcripts.platformaeronaut.com/transcripts/NVDA-3Q26-transcript)).\n- 2026-03-04 — Morgan Stanley TMT conference — said the $100B OpenAI deal was \"probably not in the cards\" and the $30B check is likely the last before the IPO ([pymnts](https://www.pymnts.com/artificial-intelligence-2/2026/nvidia-signals-final-investments-in-openai-and-anthropic/)).\n- 2026-05-20 — Q1 FY27 earnings call — \"coverage for Anthropic had been largely zero\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/20/nvidia-posts-another-record-quarter-reveals-43-billion-of-holdings-in-startups/)).\n\n## How they think\n- **Equity as part of selling the platform.** Investments expand CUDA's reach and buy customer coverage (Q3 FY26; May 2026 Anthropic remark).\n- **Back consequential, once-in-a-generation companies before they list.** He calls OpenAI \"some of the smartest investments we can possibly imagine\" ([BG2](https://singjupost.com/transcript-nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-on-openai-future-of-compute-and-the-american-dream-bg2-pod/)).\n- **Contrarian view:** he rejects AI-safety exceptionalism and job-loss doom ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2025/06/11/nvidia-jensen-huang-disagress-anthropic-ceo-dario-amodei-ai-jobs/)), and still funds the lab that holds those views.\n- **Calls he admits were wrong:** too little in OpenAI early, too little in xAI (see above). The $100B OpenAI headline in Sept 2025 shrank to $30B by March 2026.\n\n## Read these first\n- BG2 Pod, 2025-09-26: the fullest version of his investing logic and regrets, in his own words ([transcript](https://singjupost.com/transcript-nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-on-openai-future-of-compute-and-the-american-dream-bg2-pod/)).\n- Q3 FY26 earnings call, 2025-11-19: the CUDA-reach and \"share of their company\" thesis ([transcript](https://transcripts.platformaeronaut.com/transcripts/NVDA-3Q26-transcript)).\n- Morgan Stanley TMT, 2026-03-04: the pre-IPO \"last time\" framing ([pymnts](https://www.pymnts.com/artificial-intelligence-2/2026/nvidia-signals-final-investments-in-openai-and-anthropic/)).\n- The Information via Dealroom, 2026-10-07: who really prices NVIDIA's deals ([Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/info-i81usb-where-nvidias-100-billion-dealmaking-juggernaut-will-go-next/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No personal social account was confirmed. He speaks through keynotes, earnings calls and invited podcast and TV interviews.\n\n## Portfolio\nThese are NVIDIA's deals, not his personally. A row is listed only where a source ties Huang himself to the deal, and the \"Led?\" column says what kind of tie it is. For NVIDIA's full 62-round ledger see `sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md`.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | LOI: up to $100B staged per GW deployed (10GW) | 2025-09-22 | NVIDIA alone; Huang quoted in the release | none (bilateral) | [NVIDIA newsroom](https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/openai-and-nvidia-announce-strategic-partnership-to-deploy-10gw-of-nvidia-systems) |\n| OpenAI | $30B investment, part of the $110B-$122B 2026 round | 2026-02/03 | no; Huang described it at Morgan Stanley TMT | a16z, D. E. Shaw, MGX, SoftBank, T. Rowe, TPG (co-leads per ledger); Amazon; Microsoft | [pymnts](https://www.pymnts.com/artificial-intelligence-2/2026/nvidia-signals-final-investments-in-openai-and-anthropic/), [Bloomberg Law](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/tech-and-telecom-law/openai-valued-at-852-billion-after-closing-122-billion-round) |\n| Anthropic | Strategic, up to $10B | 2025-11-18 | no lead named; Huang appeared with Amodei and Nadella, with no quote from him in the release | Microsoft (up to $5B) | [Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/microsoft-nvidia-anthropic-announce-strategic-partnerships) |\n| xAI | Series C ($6B) and later financing | 2024-12-23 / 2025-10 | no; Huang confirmed \"we're an investor already\" | a16z, BlackRock, Fidelity, Sequoia, Valor, AMD and others (Series C) | [xAI](https://x.ai/news/series-c), [BI/AOL](https://www.aol.com/articles/jensen-huang-says-regrets-not-133927715.html) |\n| Hugging Face | Reported $12.9B price (deal type not stated) | reported 2026-10 | reportedly priced by Huang | Salesforce and others showed interest | [Dealroom summary of The Information](https://dealroom.co/news/info-i81usb-where-nvidias-100-billion-dealmaking-juggernaut-will-go-next/) (low confidence) |\n\n**Timing: was he first?** Never. For OpenAI, he says NVIDIA was invited in early and underinvested. Its first priced entry in the repo is the Oct 2024 $6.6B round, about 5 years after Microsoft's 2019 check ([ledger](../sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md)). Anthropic came about 54 months after its 2021 Series A. xAI came at Series C, about 7 months after Series B. He arrives at scale, once a lab has already proven it can buy compute.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **OpenAI.** The relationship predates the equity. His release calls it a decade of \"pushing each other\" from \"the first DGX supercomputer\". On the Q3 FY26 call he says the relationship dates to 2016 (third-party transcript). The 2025-09-22 letter of intent tied up to $100B to 10GW of deployment. By 2026-03-04 he called the $100B \"probably not in the cards\" and said NVIDIA had finalized $30B, because OpenAI would IPO \"toward the end of the year\" ([pymnts](https://www.pymnts.com/artificial-intelligence-2/2026/nvidia-signals-final-investments-in-openai-and-anthropic/)).\n- **Anthropic.** It went from a public spat (2025-06-11) to a three-way Microsoft, NVIDIA and Anthropic deal (2025-11-18), with Anthropic committing to up to 1GW of NVIDIA compute. He later called it a coverage win, since Anthropic had been \"largely zero\" on NVIDIA (May 2026 call).\n- **xAI.** Musk offered NVIDIA a slot in the round; Huang said he was glad to be asked and wished he had written more ([BI/AOL](https://www.aol.com/articles/jensen-huang-says-regrets-not-133927715.html)).\n- **Hugging Face.** Reportedly he moved fast once Salesforce and others circled, at more than 80x the reported $150M annualized revenue ([Dealroom summary of The Information](https://dealroom.co/news/info-i81usb-where-nvidias-100-billion-dealmaking-juggernaut-will-go-next/)). Low confidence: it is a summary, and the deal type and close are not confirmed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sam Altman / OpenAI | Supplier, then investor; LOI and $30B check | \"a decade\" before 2025 (first DGX) | [NVIDIA newsroom](https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/openai-and-nvidia-announce-strategic-partnership-to-deploy-10gw-of-nvidia-systems) |\n| Elon Musk / xAI | Investor; \"you really want to be part of\" what Musk does | by 2025-10 | [BI/AOL](https://www.aol.com/articles/jensen-huang-says-regrets-not-133927715.html) |\n| Dario Amodei / Anthropic | Public critic (2025-06), then up to $10B strategic investor (2025-11) | 2025 | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2025/06/11/nvidia-jensen-huang-disagress-anthropic-ceo-dario-amodei-ai-jobs/), [Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/microsoft-nvidia-anthropic-announce-strategic-partnerships) |\n| Satya Nadella / Microsoft | Co-investor and co-announcer of the Anthropic deal | 2025-11 | [Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/microsoft-nvidia-anthropic-announce-strategic-partnerships) |\n| Vishal Bhagwati | Runs NVIDIA corporate development; Huang reportedly sets prices over him | not found | [Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/info-i81usb-where-nvidias-100-billion-dealmaking-juggernaut-will-go-next/) |\n| Brad Gerstner, Bill Gurley (BG2) | Podcast hosts; long-form interview | 2025-09 | [transcript](https://singjupost.com/transcript-nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-on-openai-future-of-compute-and-the-american-dream-bg2-pod/) |\n| CoreWeave | Investment he cites as a win (\"How smart was that?\") | not dated | [BG2 transcript](https://singjupost.com/transcript-nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-on-openai-future-of-compute-and-the-american-dream-bg2-pod/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Whether Huang personally signs off on NVentures lab-seed checks (Ineffable, Periodic, Thinking Machines seed), or only on corporate-development checks. No source found.\n- The exact year of the first DGX delivery to OpenAI, from a primary source. The 2016 date rests on a third-party call transcript.\n- Is the Hugging Face $12.9B an acquisition or a minority investment, and did it close? The only source is a summary of paywalled reporting.\n- Any personal (non-NVIDIA) angel or fund investments. None found.\n- BG2 and Q3 FY26 quotes come from third-party transcripts, not official ones (same caveat as `firms/nvidia.md`).\n- `data/rows/nvidia-map-leads.csv` lists Huang at hop_depth 2. This profile uses hop_depth 1 as the task specified; the main session should reconcile.\n\n## Sources\n- NVIDIA bio: https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/bios/jensen-huang\n- BG2 Pod transcript (third-party), 2025-09-26: https://singjupost.com/transcript-nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-on-openai-future-of-compute-and-the-american-dream-bg2-pod/\n- Q3 FY26 call transcript (third-party), 2025-11-19: https://transcripts.platformaeronaut.com/transcripts/NVDA-3Q26-transcript\n- OpenAI LOI, 2025-09-22: https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/openai-and-nvidia-announce-strategic-partnership-to-deploy-10gw-of-nvidia-systems\n- Anthropic/Microsoft/NVIDIA, 2025-11-18: https://www.anthropic.com/news/microsoft-nvidia-anthropic-announce-strategic-partnerships\n- Fortune on VivaTech, 2025-06-11: https://fortune.com/2025/06/11/nvidia-jensen-huang-disagress-anthropic-ceo-dario-amodei-ai-jobs/\n- Business Insider via AOL on CNBC xAI interview, 2025-10-08: https://www.aol.com/articles/jensen-huang-says-regrets-not-133927715.html\n- pymnts on Morgan Stanley TMT, 2026-03-04: https://www.pymnts.com/artificial-intelligence-2/2026/nvidia-signals-final-investments-in-openai-and-anthropic/\n- Yahoo Finance on Morgan Stanley TMT: https://finance.yahoo.com/news/nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-100b-123431494.html\n- TechCrunch on Q1 FY27, 2026-05-20: https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/20/nvidia-posts-another-record-quarter-reveals-43-billion-of-holdings-in-startups/\n- The Information via Dealroom (summary), 2026-10-07: https://dealroom.co/news/info-i81usb-where-nvidias-100-billion-dealmaking-juggernaut-will-go-next/\n- xAI Series C: https://x.ai/news/series-c\n- Bloomberg Law, OpenAI round: https://news.bloomberglaw.com/tech-and-telecom-law/openai-valued-at-852-billion-after-closing-122-billion-round\n", "person:jerry-yang": "\n# Jerry Yang\n\n> Profile written 2026-11-10 (owner pick). Builds on firms/ame-cloud-ventures.md (written the same day). Primary sources: Workday 2026 proxy and Alibaba FY2026 20-F (EDGAR), the Village Global 2019 interview, and company announcements. Web search was rate-limited this pass, so podcasts, X and recent interviews (for example a 2025 Yahoo Finance interview) were not mined.\n\n## One-line take\nHe is a network node more than a lead investor. He is still an Alibaba director, sits on Workday's board Investment Committee, chaired Stanford's trustees from 2021 to 2025, and shows up as a friendly name in AI seeds such as Humans& and Entire.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** his name and the access it brings. He led Yahoo's investments in Yahoo Japan and Alibaba ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1327811/000110465926055687/tm261347-2_def14a.htm)), so founders and co-investors such as Bezos, Benioff, Kravis and Druckenmiller treat him as peer capital. AME appears to be his own money (no Form D or ADV under any AME name; that it is his capital is an inference, see the firm file), so he has no fund cycle and no ownership target.\n- **Filter:** \"anything that looks and smells like Yahoo! we won't do\". He wants data-driven companies with deep science or deep tech ([src](https://www.villageglobal.com/insights/lessons-from-luminaries-jerry-yang-on-yahoo-chinese-tech-and-early-stage-investing), 2019-09-09).\n- **Constraint:** a tiny team (three investors on a site frozen around 2017 to 2020). He has never been seen leading or pricing an AI round. He also has heavy board commitments: Workday, Alibaba, and Stanford until 2025.\n- **What he'd never do:** back consumer-internet companies that look like Yahoo (his own words). There is also no evidence that he takes board seats in AI companies.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The \"first or second check\" claim is not what his AI record shows.** He says \"A lot of times we're the first or second check in\" ([src](https://www.villageglobal.com/insights/lessons-from-luminaries-jerry-yang-on-yahoo-chinese-tech-and-early-stage-investing)). His personally named AI deals, however, came at Series A or later: Hebbia A in 2022, Vianai B in 2021 and Vicarious B ext in 2014. Only Entire (2026) and Humans& (2026, through AME) are seed entries.\n- **Hebbia: he came in at the A, not the seed.** The post lists seed joiners separately and he is not among them. He appears under \"Several of the world's boldest search and AI investors also participated\" ([src](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of), 2022-09-27).\n- **His personal checks can be sizeable.** In Vicarious (2014) he joined Bezos, Janus Friis and Benioff, and co-founder Scott Phoenix said the checks were \"definitely not 'angel-sized.'\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2014/04/07/vicarious/)).\n- **He sees deals from the buyer's side at Workday.** He has been a Workday director since 2013 and sits on the board Investment Committee (\"Member of the Investment Committee\"). That committee reviews and approves certain M&A and investments ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1327811/000110465926055687/tm261347-2_def14a.htm), proxy filed 2026-05-05). For an enterprise-AI founder, he is both a possible angel and a route to an acquirer.\n- **He was at the top of the Stanford board.** He was Chair \"from July 2021 to July 2025\" and has been a trustee again since October 2017 (also 2005 to 2015, including vice chair) ([Workday proxy](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1327811/000110465926055687/tm261347-2_def14a.htm); [Alibaba 20-F](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1577552/000119312526231755/baba-20260331.htm)). Any role in Stanford Management Company manager picks is unconfirmed.\n- **He still has deep exposure to Chinese company boards.** He \"has been our director since September 2014\" at Alibaba, after serving 2005 to 2012 as Yahoo's designee, and he sat on Lenovo's board from November 2014 to November 2023 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1577552/000119312526231755/baba-20260331.htm)). That could matter for CFIUS-sensitive frontier-AI cap tables. This is my inference; no source flags it as an issue.\n- **He follows founders.** AME backed Naveen Rao twice: the Nervana Series A in 2015 and the MosaicML debut round in 2021 ([src](https://siliconangle.com/2021/10/13/naveen-raos-startup-mosaicml-exits-stealth-mission-improve-ml-training/)).\n- **He backs emerging managers as an LP.** He is in Abstract Fund I ([src](https://colossus.com/episode/building-abstract/)) and is one of Village Global's \"luminary LPs\" ([src](https://www.villageglobal.com/insights/lessons-from-luminaries-jerry-yang-on-yahoo-chinese-tech-and-early-stage-investing)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a lead and not a check-size peer. He is a useful co-investor and LP-style node with three strengths:\n- he is in the Humans& $480M seed (frontier lab, through AME) and the Entire $60M seed;\n- he has a long AI-chip thread through AME: Nervana, Mythic and MosaicML;\n- he backs emerging managers as an LP (Abstract, Village Global), so he is a plausible individual LP for a concentrated early-AI fund. That last point is unconfirmed: nothing says he takes new GP pitches cold, and AME's site prefers intros through its network ([src](https://www.amecloudventures.com/)).\n\nDeals that matter to me: Humans& (2026 seed), MosaicML (2021) and Nervana (2015) via AME, and Entire (2026 seed).\n\n## Background\n- Stanford BS and MS in electrical engineering ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1577552/000119312526231755/baba-20260331.htm)).\n- Co-founded Yahoo, where he was \"Chief Yahoo\" and a director from March 1995 to January 2012, and CEO from June 2007 to January 2009 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1577552/000119312526231755/baba-20260331.htm)).\n- Director of Yahoo Japan, January 1996 to January 2012 (same source).\n- Cisco director, July 2000 to November 2012 (same source).\n- Founded AME Cloud Ventures in March 2012 (same source).\n- Alibaba director from October 2005 to January 2012, and again from September 2014 to present (same source).\n- Lenovo director, November 2014 to November 2023 (same source).\n- Workday director since 2013; current term runs to 2028 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1327811/000110465926055687/tm261347-2_def14a.htm)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.amecloudventures.com/ | AME site, HTTP 200 on 2026-11-10; stale (c.2017 to 2020 content) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | none verified | no account linked from the AME site; not checked further (search rate-limited) |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2019-09 — Village Global, Lessons From Luminaries — [link](https://www.villageglobal.com/insights/lessons-from-luminaries-jerry-yang-on-yahoo-chinese-tech-and-early-stage-investing). He laid out AME's three founding principles. He also retold Steve Jobs telling him he was \"too nice to be CEO\".\n- Other appearances were not mined this pass (search rate-limited).\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** data-driven businesses; deep science or deep tech; no Yahoo-like consumer internet ([src](https://www.villageglobal.com/insights/lessons-from-luminaries-jerry-yang-on-yahoo-chinese-tech-and-early-stage-investing)).\n- **On China:** he describes successive generations of Chinese founders trying to one-up the generation before (same source).\n- **Calls he got right:** Alibaba and Yahoo Japan as Yahoo's dealmaker; AI chips before the boom (Nervana, which Intel acquired; MosaicML, which Databricks acquired; both via AME).\n- **Calls he got wrong:** he admits, through Jobs's critique, that he was too soft as CEO (same source). Inside AME, Zymergen (A and B) later collapsed (see firm file).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Village Global interview, 2019: his filter and stage claim, in his own words.\n2. Workday 2026 proxy bio: current boards, Stanford chair dates, Investment Committee.\n3. Alibaba FY2026 20-F director bio: a full, dated career history.\n4. firms/ame-cloud-ventures.md: the AME book and its syndicate partners.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No active public X presence was verified.\n\n## Portfolio\nDeals where he is personally named (AME-only deals are in data/rows/ame-cloud-ventures-portfolio-deals.csv).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Vicarious | Series B (extension) | 2014-04-07 | no | Jeff Bezos, Janus Friis, Marc Benioff | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2014/04/07/vicarious/) |\n| Vianai Systems | Series B ($140M) | 2021-06-16 | no | SoftBank Vision Fund 2 (lead), Jim Davidson, Henry Kravis, George Roberts | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2167160) |\n| Hebbia | Series A ($30M) | 2022-09-27 | no | Index Ventures (lead), Radical, Thiel, Druckenmiller, Alexandr Wang, Ram Shriram | [src](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of) |\n| Ema | Series A ($25M) | 2024-03-05 | no | Accel, Section 32, Prosus. **Aggregator only** | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/micky-malka) |\n| Humans& | Seed ($480M at $4.48B) | 2026-01-20 | no | SV Angel and Georges Harik (leads), NVIDIA, Bezos, GV. Credited as \"AME Cloud Ventures (Jerry Yang)\" | [src](https://humansand.ai/) |\n| Entire | Seed ($60M) | 2026-02-10 | no | Felicis (lead), Madrona, M12, Basis Set, Olivier Pomel, Garry Tan | [src](https://entire.com/blog/hello-entire-world) |\n| RoboForce | Series A ($52M) | 2026-03-16 | no | YZi Labs (lead), Myron Scholes. The release returned 403 on this pass, so it was not re-verified | [src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260316568211/en/) |\n\nTiming: none of these is a first-in or a lead. The only follow-on pattern is founder-following through AME (Rao), not doubling down personally in the same company. The AME vehicle does re-up across rounds (Recursion, BigHat, Osaro; see firm file).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Vicarious (2014):** he arrived as a late Series B addition alongside Bezos, Friis and Benioff, after Formation 8 and Khosla were already in ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2014/04/07/vicarious/)). The intro path is unknown.\n- **Hebbia (2022):** he joined at the A as part of a \"search and AI\" angel bloc that included Ram Shriram, a Google backer ([src](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of)). The intro path is unknown.\n- **Alibaba (2005):** Workday's proxy says he led Yahoo's investment in Alibaba ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1327811/000110465926055687/tm261347-2_def14a.htm)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AME Cloud Ventures | Founding Partner | 2012-03 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1577552/000119312526231755/baba-20260331.htm) |\n| Yahoo | Co-founder; CEO 2007-2009 | 1995-03 to 2012-01 | same |\n| Alibaba Group | Director | 2014-09 (also 2005-2012) | same |\n| Workday | Director; Investment Committee | 2013 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1327811/000110465926055687/tm261347-2_def14a.htm) |\n| Lenovo | Director | 2014-11 to 2023-11 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1577552/000119312526231755/baba-20260331.htm) |\n| Stanford University | Trustee; Chair 2021-07 to 2025-07 | 2017-10 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1327811/000110465926055687/tm261347-2_def14a.htm) |\n| Abstract (Ramtin Naimi) | LP, Fund I | 2018 | [src](https://colossus.com/episode/building-abstract/) |\n| Village Global | LP | unknown | [src](https://www.villageglobal.com/insights/lessons-from-luminaries-jerry-yang-on-yahoo-chinese-tech-and-early-stage-investing) |\n| Naveen Rao | backed twice via AME (Nervana, MosaicML) | 2015 | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2021/10/13/naveen-raos-startup-mosaicml-exits-stealth-mission-improve-ml-training/) |\n| Jeff Chung, Wretch Chien, Nick Adams (former MD) | AME team | 2012 to 2014 | [src](https://www.amecloudventures.com/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Check sizes: none is disclosed. Is AME capital entirely his, or are there co-investors from the family or Asia?\n- Is he an LP in any AI-focused emerging fund beyond Abstract and Village Global?\n- Was he in the Vianai $50M seed, and was the Vianai check personal or through AME?\n- Is RoboForce a personal check or an AME check? (Databases disagree.)\n- Did the Workday Investment Committee touch Workday's AI acquisitions (for example Paradox and Sana in 2025)? That is not checked.\n- What has he said recently (podcasts, the 2025 Yahoo Finance interview)? Not mined because search was rate-limited.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1327811/000110465926055687/tm261347-2_def14a.htm (Workday DEF 14A, 2026-05-05)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1577552/000119312526231755/baba-20260331.htm (Alibaba 20-F FY2026, filed 2026-05-20)\n- https://www.villageglobal.com/insights/lessons-from-luminaries-jerry-yang-on-yahoo-chinese-tech-and-early-stage-investing\n- https://techcrunch.com/2014/04/07/vicarious/\n- https://techcrunch.com/?p=2167160\n- https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of\n- https://humansand.ai/\n- https://entire.com/blog/hello-entire-world\n- https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260316568211/en/\n- https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/micky-malka (aggregator)\n- https://colossus.com/episode/building-abstract/\n- https://siliconangle.com/2021/10/13/naveen-raos-startup-mosaicml-exits-stealth-mission-improve-ml-training/\n- https://www.amecloudventures.com/\n", "person:jessica-leao": "\n# Jessica Leão\n\n*Updated 2026-11-10 (second pass). Added: the Naveen Rao repeat-founder pattern, Maverick's own portfolio data on MosaicML, Maverick Silicon identified as a Maverick Capital vehicle launched in 2024, and fab2's 2026 Form D. Web search was unavailable this pass, so everything new comes from direct fetches of primary pages and SEC EDGAR.*\n\n## One-line take\nShe is an AI-chip investor who has backed Naveen Rao twice: MosaicML at Maverick Ventures and Unconventional AI at Decibel. She also backed Jim Keller and Sam Zeloof's fab startup Atomic Semi (now fab2), and she has just joined a16z American Dynamism. No a16z deal names her yet.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins: founder continuity.** Her two biggest AI-compute deals are the same founder. Maverick Ventures pictures \"Naveen (MosaicML, acq. Databricks)\" among its marquee founders ([src](https://maverickcapital.com/ventures), from the site's JS bundle). She then led Decibel's investment in Rao's next company, Unconventional AI ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/)). Her edge is staying close to elite chip founders across their companies, not cold sourcing.\n- **Also: a Palantir FDE in a hardware lane.** She was a Forward Deployed Engineer on Gotham ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/)). That background fits American Dynamism's government-buyer work.\n- **What constrains her.**\n  - Her title is ambiguous. The author page says \"Partner\", but the team page says \"Investing Partner\" ([src](https://a16z.com/american-dynamism/)).\n  - Her deal record before a16z is all participant-level. No round announcement, Form D or board list we found names her personally.\n  - At a16z, the AI/Infra team (Bornstein, Appenzeller) wrote the Unconventional AI memo ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/)), so AI chips already have owners there.\n- **What she'd never do.** The American Dynamism mandate is companies that \"support the national interest\" ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/)). So: no consumer AI apps and no non-US labs.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **She has backed the same founder twice (new this pass).** Maverick Capital's site lists MosaicML (Rao's first company) as a featured Maverick Ventures portfolio company, with \"firstPartnered\" set to \"Early Stage\". Its founder photo caption names \"Naveen (MosaicML, acq. Databricks)\" ([src](https://maverickcapital.com/ventures)). a16z's bio says she backed MosaicML at Maverick and later led Decibel's investment in Unconventional AI, which is also Rao's ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/)).\n  - Inference: she probably got into Unconventional AI through the MosaicML relationship.\n  - That makes her one of the few investors to have held Rao's companies at two different firms. Now that she is at a16z, the firm that co-led the $475M Unconventional seed ([src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/)), she sits on both sides of that cap table's history.\n- **Maverick came into MosaicML after the seed, not at inception.** The October 2021 $37M seed was led by Lux, DCVC and Playground, with Future, AME, Correlation and E14 also named. Maverick is not among them ([src](https://siliconangle.com/2021/10/13/naveen-raos-startup-mosaicml-exits-stealth-mission-improve-ml-training/)). Maverick labels its entry \"Early Stage\" ([src](https://maverickcapital.com/ventures)), so it was a follow-on position. She was not first.\n- **\"Maverick Silicon\" is now resolved.** It is a Maverick Capital vehicle launched in 2024 ([src](https://maverickcapital.com/silicon)). Its Managing Partner, Andrew Homan, previously \"led Maverick Capital's investments across the semiconductor, hardware, and software industries\". He sits on the boards of Empower Semiconductor, SiMa Technologies, TensorWave and Upscale AI ([src](https://maverickcapital.com/silicon), from the JS bundle). fab2 lists \"Maverick Silicon\" as an investor ([src](https://fab2.com/)).\n  - So Maverick's Atomic Semi position, which she is credited with, most likely moved into or was followed on by the Silicon vehicle, which Homan runs, not her.\n  - Homan is the likely senior partner on her Maverick semis deals. That is an inference: no source pairs them.\n- **Atomic Semi became fab2 and raised a very large round after she left.** fab2's site names founders Sam Zeloof and Jim Keller ([src](https://fab2.com/)). Its first Form D (filed 2026-09-08; first sale 2026-08-21; incorporated 2022) reports $556,021,222 sold of a $587,997,030 offering, from 89 investors. Zeloof, Keller and Corey Nobile are listed as related persons ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2151752/000215175226000002/primary_doc.xml)). Her Maverick-era entry was early, at a fraction of the current scale. She is not on the filing.\n- **Decibel is otherwise a software/security fund.** Unconventional AI is the only company in Decibel's \"Data, ML / AI Infra\" list that touches hardware ([src](https://www.decibel.vc/companies)). Decibel's sitemap shows no partner-welcome post or article for her ([src](https://www.decibel.vc/sitemap.xml)). Unconventional AI was effectively her solo hardware bet at Decibel.\n- **No public voice yet.** Her a16z author page lists no articles. Its X embed shows only short replies, e.g. \"Co-sign\" reposting a16z's Sarah Wang (@sarahdingwang), and a reply to Justine Moore (@venturetwins) ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/)). Her network inside a16z is visible here, but there is no thesis content.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Her lane, AI compute substrates and fabs, is core to the owner's frontier-AI, early-stage, big-seed lens. Unconventional AI's $475M seed at a reported $4.5B is exactly the mega-seed shape the owner writes $20M checks into. Her repeat-founder access to Rao matters, and so does her early Keller/Zeloof link (fab2's 2026 round of roughly $556M).\n\nCaveats:\n- She has not signed an a16z deal yet.\n- Her title may be \"Investing Partner\".\n- The American Dynamism remit pulls toward defense and space.\n\nUse her as a co-investor relationship on US AI-hardware formation rounds, and as a route to Rao's next moves.\n\n## Background\n- Born in Brazil, came to the US for university, now a US citizen ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/)).\n- BA from Yale; MBA from Stanford GSB; years not found ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/)).\n- Palantir Technologies, Forward Deployed Engineer on Gotham; dates not found ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/)).\n- Maverick Capital ventures team. She backed MosaicML (Maverick entry \"Early Stage\", after the Oct 2021 seed) and Atomic Semi (fab2's entity was incorporated in 2022) ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/), [src](https://maverickcapital.com/ventures), [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2151752/000215175226000002/primary_doc.xml)). That puts her at Maverick until at least 2022. Her exact dates were not found.\n- Decibel Partners, Partner. She led early-stage hardware and infra deals, including Unconventional AI (which emerged in 2025). She is no longer on the team page ([src](https://www.decibel.vc/team)).\n- a16z American Dynamism. Start date not stated; after Dec 2025; headshot uploaded 2026-05 ([src](https://a16z.com/american-dynamism/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/ | Bio plus X embed; no articles (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/jesslionness | Linked from the a16z AD team page and embedded on the author page; mostly short replies; follower count not fetchable |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found (not fetched) | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was unavailable in both passes. Decibel's article sitemap (158 URLs) has no talk or post by her ([src](https://www.decibel.vc/sitemap.xml)).\n\n## How they think\n- Stated focus: semis, advanced manufacturing, defense, space, energy and supply chain ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/)).\n- Pattern (my inference from the deals): **back proven compute founders on their next, more radical substrate.**\n  - Rao: from training efficiency at MosaicML to physical/analog compute at Unconventional AI.\n  - Keller and Zeloof: small, fast fabs at Atomic Semi, now fab2.\n- Calls right: MosaicML sold to Databricks in June 2023. Atomic Semi/fab2 raised about $556M in 2026 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2151752/000215175226000002/primary_doc.xml)). Unconventional AI raised a $475M seed. No mistakes found on record.\n\n## Read these first\n1. a16z author bio, the only first-person-adjacent source ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/)).\n2. \"Introducing Unconventional AI\" (2025-12-08) ([src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/)).\n3. Unconventional AI, \"How to improve AI energy efficiency by 1000x\" (2026-05-07). This is the company's thesis, not hers ([src](https://unconv.ai/blog/)).\n4. fab2's site, for what the Atomic Semi bet became ([src](https://fab2.com/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nThe X embed on her a16z page shows replies and reposts to a16z colleagues Sarah Wang (@sarahdingwang) and Justine Moore (@venturetwins). No thesis posts were visible ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/)). Full timeline not fetchable.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Unconventional AI (at Decibel) | Seed (Decibel position; Decibel not named in the $475M seed post) | 2025-12-08 (seed announced; Decibel's entry date not found) | She led Decibel's check (bio); Decibel did not lead the round | Lightspeed and a16z (co-leads); Sequoia, Lux, DCVC, Future Ventures, Jeff Bezos | [src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/), [src](https://www.decibel.vc/companies), [src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/) |\n| MosaicML (at Maverick Ventures) | Undisclosed (Maverick says \"Early Stage\"; not in the Oct 2021 seed) | after 2021-10; exited to Databricks 2023-06 | no evidence she led | Seed holders: Lux, DCVC, Playground, Future, AME, Correlation, E14 | [src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/), [src](https://maverickcapital.com/ventures), [src](https://siliconangle.com/2021/10/13/naveen-raos-startup-mosaicml-exits-stealth-mission-improve-ml-training/) |\n| Atomic Semi, now fab2 (at Maverick) | Undisclosed (Maverick entry, before the fab2 rename) | not found (entity incorporated 2022) | unknown | fab2 lists Fundomo, Naval Ravikant, Corner Capital, Paradigm, Duquesne Family Office, Maverick Silicon, StepStone, Protagonist, UDC Ventures | [src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/), [src](https://fab2.com/) |\n\nNo board seats found. No a16z deal names her as of 2026-11-10.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Rao, twice.**\n  - Maverick held MosaicML from an early-stage round after the October 2021 seed ([src](https://maverickcapital.com/ventures)).\n  - After Databricks bought MosaicML (2023), Rao started Unconventional AI. a16z credits her with leading Decibel's investment in it ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/)).\n  - The Decibel check does not appear in the 2025-12-08 announcement ([src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/)). That points to a smaller participation, or an entry before the announced round. Unresolved.\n  - Since then, Unconventional AI has agreed to acquire NeuReality (announced 2026-10-26) ([src](https://unconv.ai/blog/unconventional-ai-to-acquire-neureality/)).\n- **Atomic Semi to fab2.**\n  - She backed it at Maverick ([src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/)).\n  - Fundomo syndicate vehicles that appear to be Atomic Semi filed in January 2023, which suggests an early seed-era raise (this is another agent's inference, recorded in `data/rows/fundomo-deals.csv`).\n  - In 2026 the company, as Fab2, Inc., sold about $556M to 89 investors ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2151752/000215175226000002/primary_doc.xml)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Andreessen Horowitz (American Dynamism) | Partner / Investing Partner | after 2025-12 | [src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/) |\n| Decibel Partners | Former Partner | undated | [src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/) |\n| Maverick Capital (Maverick Ventures) | Former ventures team member | undated (until at least 2022) | [src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/) |\n| Andrew Homan (Maverick Silicon) | Ran Maverick's semis investing while she was there; inferred colleague, not confirmed | — | [src](https://maverickcapital.com/silicon) |\n| Palantir Technologies | Former FDE (Gotham) | undated | [src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/) |\n| Naveen Rao | Backed two of his companies (MosaicML at Maverick, Unconventional AI at Decibel) | MosaicML era | [src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/), [src](https://maverickcapital.com/ventures) |\n| Sam Zeloof, Jim Keller (fab2) | Backed Atomic Semi at Maverick | undated | [src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/), [src](https://fab2.com/) |\n| Sarah Wang, Justine Moore (a16z) | X interactions | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What was Decibel's entry round and check size in Unconventional AI? Did it come before the $475M seed? Check for a Form D for Unconventional AI.\n- Which MosaicML round did Maverick join? MosaicML raised more after the 2021 seed; that round has not been verified here.\n- Did she work with Andrew Homan on the Atomic Semi deal, and did Maverick Silicon take over that position?\n- Is her title \"Partner\" or \"Investing Partner\", and how much can she sign for on her own?\n- Exact dates for Palantir, Maverick, Decibel and her a16z start.\n- Watch for her first a16z American Dynamism byline. A Rao-network or Keller-network deal would confirm the pattern.\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/jessica-leao/\n- https://a16z.com/american-dynamism/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/\n- https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/\n- https://unconv.ai/blog/\n- https://unconv.ai/blog/unconventional-ai-to-acquire-neureality/\n- https://www.decibel.vc/companies\n- https://www.decibel.vc/team\n- https://www.decibel.vc/sitemap.xml\n- https://maverickcapital.com/ventures (portfolio and captions read from the site's JS bundle /assets/index-DiqRUxv5.js)\n- https://maverickcapital.com/silicon (team bios from the same bundle)\n- https://fab2.com/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2151752/000215175226000002/primary_doc.xml (Fab2, Inc. Form D, 2026-09-08)\n- https://siliconangle.com/2021/10/13/naveen-raos-startup-mosaicml-exits-stealth-mission-improve-ml-training/\n- https://x.com/jesslionness (not fetchable directly)\n", "person:joe-botsch": "\n# Joe Botsch\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. Sources: Deviation team page, Wayback copies of his Two Sigma Ventures profile (four captures, 2025-02-09 to 2026-01-20) and his RTP Global profile (captures 2021-10 to 2024-10), his X profile, and portfolio-company announcements. The web search budget was used up before this pass started, so everything here comes from direct page fetches. I could not check LinkedIn, podcasts, or the SDF and Bytewax funding announcements. Pronouns: both the RTP and TSV bios use \"he\".*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is a lateral-hire Principal at Deviation, the firm Two Sigma Ventures spun out as, who holds its AI-infra and logistics book. His own firm's compliance note says he has never co-led a deal at TSV or Deviation.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He jumped two titles by changing firms. RTP Global still listed him as an \"Associate\" in 2024-10, and TSV hired him as a Principal in 2025 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20241005050451/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/), [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260120173317/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/profile/joe-botsch/). He covers unglamorous categories (supply chain, manufacturing, devtools, security) that the rest of the Deviation team does not, and he is the only Principal on its investing team [src](https://deviation.com/team).\n- **What constrains him:** He has no lead record at the firm yet. TSV's own profile says his three RTP co-leads (SDF Labs, Bytewax, Alvys) are \"a complete record\" of the deals he co-led [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260120173317/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/profile/joe-botsch/). Deviation credits him only as *manager* of deals \"made by the Two Sigma Ventures team\" [src](https://deviation.com/team/joe-botsch). The funds are small: Fund IV and Opportunity II together were ~$400M (see `firms/two-sigma-ventures.md`). Who approves a deal at Deviation is **unconfirmed**. It is presumably the four Founding Partners, but no source says so.\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** lead a $20M+ frontier-model round alone. Neither his firm's check sizes nor his title support it.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **His co-lead record is short, and his firm has said so on the record.** The TSV profile carries an SEC-marketing-rule-style disclaimer: \"TSV believes that this is a complete record of investments Mr. Botsch co-led\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260120173317/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/profile/joe-botsch/). As of 2026-01-20 that list was SDF Labs, Bytewax and Alvys, all at RTP Global, with nothing at TSV.\n- **The deals Deviation lists are custody, not origination.** The page says the deals were \"made by the Two Sigma Ventures team prior to Deviation and managed by Joe at Deviation\" [src](https://deviation.com/team/joe-botsch). Artemis, Highbeam and Swarm Aero all name TSV, if at all, as a participant. No release names him. The Swarm Aero Series A is credited to Colin Beirne as Partner and is also listed under Rohit Rao [src](https://digitalmedianet.com/swarm-aero-announces-35m-series-a-financing-led-by-two-sigma-ventures-and-silent-ventures/amp/), [src](https://deviation.com/team/rohit-rao).\n- **\"Co-led\" is generous for Alvys.** Every company and lead-investor source says Bonfire led the $6.3M seed (2023-06-22) \"with participation from RTP Global\" [src](https://bonfirevc.com/news/why-we-invested-in-alvys), [src](https://www.freightcaviar.com/alvys-secures-6-3m-seed-funding/). RTP then led Alvys's $40M Series B on 2025-09-29, after he had left for TSV [src](https://www.alvys.com/blog), [src](https://alvys.com/news). His seed bet paid off for RTP's book, not for Deviation's.\n- **SDF links him to TSV before he was hired (inference).** He co-led SDF Labs at RTP [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260120173317/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/profile/joe-botsch/). Vin Sachidananda's Radical page credits SDF as one of his own TSV deals [src](https://radical.vc/team/vin-sachidananda/). SDF was acquired by dbt Labs in early 2025 [src](https://www.getdbt.com/blog/dbt-labs-acquires-sdf-labs). It is plausible that the co-investment led to his TSV hire, but no source says so.\n- **His investing self-image:** his pinned X post (2024-03-12) is \"A vc portfolio is basically one long parlay\" [src](https://x.com/jbotsch). On RTP's page he said he likes founders \"from non-traditional backgrounds, in overlooked industries\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240229022707/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is a Principal, which is the seniority the owner hunts for, and he holds an AI-infra and security book (Artemis is a $70M seed+A alongside Felicis). Against that: (1) by his firm's own disclosure he has led nothing at TSV or Deviation; (2) Deviation's check sizes are sub-$10M on most leads; (3) his natural lane is applied and vertical AI (logistics, fintech ops, devtools), not frontier labs. He is useful as an early co-investor scout in AI-native security and supply-chain software. Watch for his first Deviation-era lead out of Deviation V. That will show whether he has signing power.\n\n## Background\n- BS in Computer Science (and Mathematics per RTP), Brandeis University [src](https://deviation.com/team/joe-botsch), [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240229022707/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/).\n- Early career: product manager and data scientist at early-stage startups in Boston [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20211025043442/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/).\n- MassChallenge: Head of Research and Analytics. Also a local director at Founder Institute, helping enterprise-software and robotics startups run corporate pilots [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20211025043442/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/).\n- OpenView: Associate. He worked on enterprise software, supply chain, infrastructure and security, and \"sourced and helped lead\" Encamp. Dates are unconfirmed; he left in 2021 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20211025043442/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/).\n- RTP Global, New York: joined 2021 as \"Investor\" and was titled \"Associate, US\" from 2023-09 to 2024-10. He focused on AI/ML, infrastructure and vertical SaaS. RTP listed his notable investments as Alvys, DoControl, Daylight, Hypr, Instill, SmarterSorting, Guardhat, Bytewax and SDF Labs [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20241005050451/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/).\n- Two Sigma Ventures: Principal, joined 2025. He was on the TSV profile by the 2025-02-09 capture [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260120173317/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/profile/joe-botsch/).\n- Deviation Capital: Principal from the 2026-04 spin-out [src](https://deviation.com/team/joe-botsch).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [deviation.com/team/joe-botsch](https://deviation.com/team/joe-botsch) | Deviation team page (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | [@jbotsch](https://x.com/jbotsch) | 986 followers, 5,756 posts. Bio: \"VC @ Deviationcap, formerly @ rtpvc, @ OpenView\". Posts mostly sports and VC banter; active 2026-11 (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| LinkedIn | [linkedin.com/in/jbotsch](https://www.linkedin.com/in/jbotsch) | Linked from his Deviation page; not opened |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found (web search unavailable this pass).\n\n## How they think\n- Thesis areas over time: enterprise software, supply chain, infra and security (OpenView) -> AI/ML, infra, vertical SaaS (RTP) -> AI/ML, infra and security, vertical software in supply chain and manufacturing (TSV) -> AI Infra, Supply Chain & Logistics, Robotics, DevTools (Deviation) [src](https://deviation.com/team/joe-botsch).\n- He values the team over moats. He amplified a post arguing that team matters more than \"some imagined structural moat\" (2026-10-22) [src](https://x.com/jbotsch).\n- Calls: SDF (acquired by dbt Labs, 2025) and Alvys (up-round Series B, 2025) both came from his RTP book. No calls found at TSV or Deviation.\n\n## Read these first\n- His Deviation page, for the \"managed by\" wording and focus areas [src](https://deviation.com/team/joe-botsch).\n- His archived TSV profile, for the complete-record disclaimer [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260120173317/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/profile/joe-botsch/).\n- His RTP profile, for his founder preferences and full RTP list [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240229022707/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/).\n\n## Social engagement\nOn X he posts about Boston sports, the NBA and VC culture. Recent posts tag @nikhilvnamburi and @fendien. He quote-posts Anduril and defense-valuation news [src](https://x.com/jbotsch). I did not map who amplifies him (needs login).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Alvys | Seed ($6.3M) | 2023-06-22 | RTP participated; TSV calls it a Botsch co-lead | Bonfire Ventures (lead) | [src](https://bonfirevc.com/news/why-we-invested-in-alvys) |\n| SDF Labs | Undisclosed (co-led at RTP) | unconfirmed | co-led | Two Sigma Ventures (inferred via Sachidananda) | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260120173317/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/profile/joe-botsch/) |\n| Bytewax | Undisclosed (co-led at RTP) | unconfirmed | co-led | unconfirmed | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260120173317/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/profile/joe-botsch/) |\n| Encamp | Undisclosed (at OpenView; \"sourced and helped lead\") | unconfirmed | unconfirmed | | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20211025043442/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/) |\n| Highbeam | Series A ($30M) | 2025-09 | no (managed by him at Deviation) | Acrew Capital (lead); Vesey; FirstMark; Mayfield | [src](https://www.highbeam.co/blog/series-a-announcement) |\n| Artemis | Seed ($70M combined seed and Series A) | 2026-04 | no (managed by him at Deviation) | Felicis (lead of A) | [src](https://deviation.com/team/joe-botsch) |\n| Swarm Aero | Series A ($35M) | 2026-03 | TSV co-led; credited to Beirne; Botsch manages | Silent Ventures (co-lead) | [src](https://deviation.com/team/joe-botsch) |\n\nTiming: none of these is a re-up that names him. Highbeam was TSV's first entry (Series A after a FirstMark/Mayfield seed). Artemis was a first entry at formation.\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing primary found. Highbeam's own post names \"Two Sigma Ventures\" as a participant but no individual [src](https://www.highbeam.co/blog/series-a-announcement). The Artemis launch release does not name TSV or him [src](https://www.newswire.com/news/artemis-emerges-from-stealth-with-70m-to-rebuild-security-operations-for-ai).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Deviation Capital | at: Principal | 2026-04 | [src](https://deviation.com/team/joe-botsch) |\n| Two Sigma Ventures | at: Principal | 2025 (to 2026-04) | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260120173317/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/profile/joe-botsch/) |\n| RTP Global | at: Investor / Associate, US | 2021 (to 2025) | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20241005050451/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/) |\n| OpenView | at: Associate | unconfirmed (to 2021) | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20211025043442/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/) |\n| MassChallenge | at: Head of Research and Analytics | unconfirmed | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20211025043442/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/) |\n| Founder Institute | at: Local Director | unconfirmed | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20211025043442/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/) |\n| Vin Sachidananda | both credited with SDF at different firms (inference) | | [src](https://radical.vc/team/vin-sachidananda/) |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** Deviation lists no personal email. The firm's contact route is its site [src](https://deviation.com/team). His X DMs are the most direct public channel [src](https://x.com/jbotsch). Bonfire Ventures (Alvys) and Acrew Capital (Highbeam) have co-invested alongside him.\n\n## Open questions\n- What round and date were the SDF and Bytewax co-leads at RTP? Did TSV and RTP co-invest in SDF?\n- Will he be named as the lead on any Deviation V deal? A Form D \"related person\" or board seat would settle it.\n- Does he sit on the Artemis board, or only observe? Neither is confirmed.\n- What is the investment committee structure at Deviation (unconfirmed)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://deviation.com/team/joe-botsch\n- https://deviation.com/team\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20260120173317/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/profile/joe-botsch/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20211025043442/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20240229022707/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20241005050451/https://rtp.vc/team/joe-botsch/\n- https://x.com/jbotsch\n- https://bonfirevc.com/news/why-we-invested-in-alvys\n- https://www.freightcaviar.com/alvys-secures-6-3m-seed-funding/\n- https://alvys.com/news\n- https://www.alvys.com/blog\n- https://www.highbeam.co/blog/series-a-announcement\n- https://www.newswire.com/news/artemis-emerges-from-stealth-with-70m-to-rebuild-security-operations-for-ai\n- https://digitalmedianet.com/swarm-aero-announces-35m-series-a-financing-led-by-two-sigma-ventures-and-silent-ventures/amp/\n- https://radical.vc/team/vin-sachidananda/\n- https://www.getdbt.com/blog/dbt-labs-acquires-sdf-labs\n", "person:joey-krug": "\n# Joey Krug\n\n## One-line take\nHe is Founders Fund's crypto partner who led Polymarket's $45M Series B in May 2024, months before the US election made prediction markets mainstream [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/294367/polymarket-raises-45-million-from-peter-thiels-founders-fund-vitalik-buterin-and-others). He is also a co-founder of Augur, the earlier on-chain prediction market [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augur_(software)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He is a founder-turned-investor in his own category. He co-founded the Forecast Foundation (Augur) in 2014 [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augur_(software)), so he had spent a decade on prediction markets before he led the Polymarket B. At Pantera he led first institutional rounds in Starkware, Arbitrum and Alchemy [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/joey-krug/).\n- **His mandate:** FF hired him to set \"the next decade of Founders Fund's crypto strategy\" [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/226680/founders-fund-pantera-executive). The mandate is crypto, not AI.\n- **How he decides (sourced only for Lighter):** He backs the founder and team first. He said they were \"85% to 90% of why we made the investment\" [src](https://fortune.com/2025/11/11/lighter-fundraise-founders-fund-ribbit-capital-haun-ventures-robinhood-vladimir-novakovski/).\n- **What constrains him:** He is a crypto specialist inside a generalist firm. No source ties him to any AI deal (see Prime Intellect under Open questions).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Usage before conviction.** Before the Polymarket B, the FF team already used the product as a news feed: \"we developed a habit of checking Polymarket at times of breaking news\" [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/294367/polymarket-raises-45-million-from-peter-thiels-founders-fund-vitalik-buterin-and-others) (2024-05-14). The deal followed product love inside the firm, not a thesis memo.\n- **He called prediction markets \"real\" before the election did.** \"Polymarket has finally made the vision for prediction markets a reality\" [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/294367/polymarket-raises-45-million-from-peter-thiels-founders-fund-vitalik-buterin-and-others). From an Augur co-founder, this reads as a verdict on his own earlier attempt.\n- **He is a jockey bettor.** On Lighter: \"85% to 90% of why we made the investment\" was Vlad Novakovski and his team [src](https://fortune.com/2025/11/11/lighter-fundraise-founders-fund-ribbit-capital-haun-ventures-robinhood-vladimir-novakovski/) (2025-11-11).\n- **Mandate in his own words:** \"I'll be focused on defining the next decade of Founders Fund's crypto strategy\" [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/226680/founders-fund-pantera-executive) (2023-04-17).\n- **Inside FF's intellectual circle.** John Luttig thanks him as a reviewer of his 2024 and 2025 AI essays [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent). So he gives feedback on FF's AI thinking even though he doesn't lead AI deals.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is a proven outlier picker, with a $45M B lead that was marked at about $8B pre-money 17 months later (see Deal stories). He is also a reachable FF partner who can lead $10M+ rounds. But his book is crypto and prediction markets, which my lens doesn't cover: no frontier-lab, AI-chip or AI-automation deal is attributed to him. The deals that matter to me: Polymarket (a lesson in timing and conviction), and possibly Prime Intellect (decentralized AI training, FF led the $15M round), if his role there is ever confirmed. It is not confirmed today.\n\n## Background\n- Began programming in middle school and studied computer science in college; school and dates not given [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/joey-krug/).\n- Built a sound-based point-of-sale payment system; name and date not given [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/joey-krug/).\n- 2014: co-founded the Forecast Foundation (Augur) with Jack Peterson and Jeremy Gardner [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augur_(software)). Augur crowdfunded in 2015-08 and launched in 2018-07 (same src).\n- Co-founded Eco, a consumer finance app; date not given [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/joey-krug/).\n- 2016: started angel investing and co-ran a top-ranked AngelList Syndicate [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/joey-krug/).\n- Thiel Fellow and Edmund Hillary Fellow; years not given [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/joey-krug/).\n- 2017 to 2023-02: Pantera Capital, eventually Co-CIO [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/226680/founders-fund-pantera-executive).\n- 2023-04: Partner, Founders Fund [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/226680/founders-fund-pantera-executive). His FF profile was posted 2023-06-09 (from firms/founders-fund.md).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://foundersfund.com/team/joey-krug/ | FF bio |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/joeykrug | handle inferred from URL; fetch returned HTTP 402, follower count not retrieved; he announced the FF move on Twitter [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/226680/founders-fund-pantera-executive) |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The web-search budget ran out before podcasts could be searched.\n\n## How they think\n- **Prediction markets as a real product:** He says Polymarket made the vision \"a reality\" [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/294367/polymarket-raises-45-million-from-peter-thiels-founders-fund-vitalik-buterin-and-others).\n- **Team over market:** On Lighter, he credited the founder and team with most of the decision [src](https://fortune.com/2025/11/11/lighter-fundraise-founders-fund-ribbit-capital-haun-ventures-robinhood-vladimir-novakovski/).\n- **Contrarian views:** nothing sourced.\n- **Calls right:** Polymarket B (2024-05) before the ICE investment at about $8B pre-money (2025-10) [src](https://ir.theice.com/press/news-details/2025/ICE-Announces-Strategic-Investment-in-Polymarket/default.aspx). Calls wrong: nothing sourced.\n\n## Read these first\n- The Block on the Polymarket B, which has both of his Polymarket quotes [link](https://www.theblock.co/post/294367/polymarket-raises-45-million-from-peter-thiels-founders-fund-vitalik-buterin-and-others).\n- Fortune on Lighter, where he explains his team-first rationale [link](https://fortune.com/2025/11/11/lighter-fundraise-founders-fund-ribbit-capital-haun-ventures-robinhood-vladimir-novakovski/).\n- The Block on his FF hire and crypto mandate [link](https://www.theblock.co/post/226680/founders-fund-pantera-executive).\n- His FF bio, the only source for his Pantera-era deals [link](https://foundersfund.com/team/joey-krug/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. The X fetch was blocked (HTTP 402).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Polymarket | Series B ($45M) | 2024-05-14 | Yes (FF led; Krug quoted as Partner) | 1confirmation, ParaFi, Vitalik Buterin, Dragonfly, Kevin Hartz | [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/294367/polymarket-raises-45-million-from-peter-thiels-founders-fund-vitalik-buterin-and-others) |\n| Lighter | $68M round at ~$1.5B (valuation from two anonymous sources) | 2025-11-11 | Co-lead (FF with Ribbit Capital; Krug quoted) | Ribbit Capital, Haun Ventures, Robinhood | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/11/11/lighter-fundraise-founders-fund-ribbit-capital-haun-ventures-robinhood-vladimir-novakovski/) |\n| Starkware | First institutional round (Pantera era, not FF) | not sourced | \"led\" per bio | not sourced | [bio](https://foundersfund.com/team/joey-krug/) |\n| Arbitrum | First institutional round (Pantera era, not FF) | not sourced | \"led\" per bio | not sourced | [bio](https://foundersfund.com/team/joey-krug/) |\n| Alchemy | First institutional round (Pantera era, not FF) | not sourced | \"led\" per bio | not sourced | [bio](https://foundersfund.com/team/joey-krug/) |\n| Forecast Foundation / Augur | Co-founder (not an investment) | 2014 | — | — | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augur_(software)) |\n| Eco | Co-founder (not an investment) | not sourced | — | — | [bio](https://foundersfund.com/team/joey-krug/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n**Polymarket Series B (2024-05-14).**\n- **How they found it:** The Block's story suggests the deal grew out of product usage. FF staff \"developed a habit of checking Polymarket at times of breaking news\" before the round [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/294367/polymarket-raises-45-million-from-peter-thiels-founders-fund-vitalik-buterin-and-others). No source names an intro path. His Augur history (2014) means he knew the category's failure modes a decade earlier [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augur_(software)).\n- **Who else was there:** FF led the $45M B. Existing investors 1confirmation and ParaFi joined, along with Vitalik Buterin, Dragonfly and Kevin Hartz. The same announcement disclosed a previously unannounced $25M Series A led by General Catalyst, with Joe Gebbia and Polychain. That brought the total to $70M [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/294367/polymarket-raises-45-million-from-peter-thiels-founders-fund-vitalik-buterin-and-others). Abstract's Ramtin Naimi has also called Abstract a big investor in this B [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) (from people/ramtin-naimi.md).\n- **Timing:** General Catalyst came in first (the A), then FF led the B. Next came 1789 Capital in 2025-08 (Wikipedia, secondary) [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polymarket). Then ICE invested up to $2B in cash at about $8B pre-money on 2025-10-07 [src](https://ir.theice.com/press/news-details/2025/ICE-Announces-Strategic-Investment-in-Polymarket/default.aspx).\n- **What it's worth now:** Wikipedia (secondary, and internally inconsistent between $8B and $9B for the ICE deal) says the company was valued at $9B by 2026-02 [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polymarket). FF's B valuation is not disclosed, so the multiple can't be computed. The step to an ~$8B pre-money valuation in 17 months is the most sourced data point. Wikipedia's Bloomberg citation says the \"most recent raise\" was led by FF, but no amount or date is sourced, so it is not counted as a separate Krug deal.\n\n**Lighter (2025-11-11).** FF and Ribbit co-led a $68M round at about $1.5B. Haun Ventures and Robinhood participated. Krug said founder Vlad Novakovski and his team were \"85% to 90% of why we made the investment\" [src](https://fortune.com/2025/11/11/lighter-fundraise-founders-fund-ribbit-capital-haun-ventures-robinhood-vladimir-novakovski/). Fortune does not say how FF first met the company.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Founders Fund | Partner (crypto) | 2023-04 | [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/226680/founders-fund-pantera-executive) |\n| Pantera Capital | Co-CIO; joined 2017, left 2023-02 | 2017 | [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/226680/founders-fund-pantera-executive) |\n| Jack Peterson; Jeremy Gardner | Augur / Forecast Foundation co-founders | 2014 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augur_(software)) |\n| Vitalik Buterin | Forecast Foundation adviser (per Wikipedia); co-investor in Polymarket B | 2014 / 2024 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augur_(software)), [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/294367/polymarket-raises-45-million-from-peter-thiels-founders-fund-vitalik-buterin-and-others) |\n| Vlad Novakovski (Lighter) | Backed founder; FF co-led | 2025 | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/11/11/lighter-fundraise-founders-fund-ribbit-capital-haun-ventures-robinhood-vladimir-novakovski/) |\n| Ribbit Capital | Co-lead on Lighter | 2025 | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/11/11/lighter-fundraise-founders-fund-ribbit-capital-haun-ventures-robinhood-vladimir-novakovski/) |\n| John Luttig (FF) | Colleague; reviewer of Luttig's essays | 2024 | [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent) |\n| Thiel Fellowship | Fellow | not sourced | [bio](https://foundersfund.com/team/joey-krug/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Prime Intellect:** FF led its $15M round (2025-02-28) [src](https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/fundraise). An earlier lead row guessed Krug owned the deal, but the announcement names no FF partner. It is unconfirmed, and it is the only path to a higher lens fit.\n- Did FF lead a later Polymarket round (Wikipedia's Bloomberg citation), and was Krug on it?\n- Did Bridget Harris (FF associate, ex-Pantera) follow him from Pantera? Her profile date (2023-08) fits that, but nothing confirms it.\n- Podcasts and X: not searched, because the search budget ran out. Augur-era and Pantera-era interviews probably hold his rules of thumb.\n- What other crypto deals has he led at FF since 2023?\n\n## Sources\n- https://foundersfund.com/team/joey-krug/\n- https://foundersfund.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/Joey.jpg (photo, checked HTTP 200 on 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.theblock.co/post/226680/founders-fund-pantera-executive\n- https://www.theblock.co/post/294367/polymarket-raises-45-million-from-peter-thiels-founders-fund-vitalik-buterin-and-others\n- https://fortune.com/2025/11/11/lighter-fundraise-founders-fund-ribbit-capital-haun-ventures-robinhood-vladimir-novakovski/\n- https://ir.theice.com/press/news-details/2025/ICE-Announces-Strategic-Investment-in-Polymarket/default.aspx\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polymarket (secondary)\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augur_(software) (secondary)\n- https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/fundraise\n- https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4 (via people/ramtin-naimi.md)\n", "person:john-hering": "\n# John Hering\n\n## One-line take\nThe person at Vy who holds the Musk relationship and, per a 2026 proxy filing, co-controls Vy Fund III with Tamas. For a frontier-AI co-investor he is the door into Vy. He is not a seed investor.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he does the work, not just the wiring. He registered the \"Neuralace\" domains to hand Musk before Neuralink had its name, worked on Starlink hiring and financial models from 2019 with a SpaceX badge, and sits on the Boring board ([cc.cz](https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/), [FT via ChainCatcher](https://www.chaincatcher.com/en/article/2289437), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1737630/000173763026000003/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **What he signs:** he and Alexander Tamas each own part of Vy GP Fund III Limited and share voting and investment control of Fund III's holdings ([TON Strategy DEF 14A, 2026-04-30](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1566610/000149315226020423/formdef14a.htm)). So for Fund III he is a signatory, not just a sourcer. Fund II's GP is held solely by CFO Katja Lake (same filing), so control differs by vehicle.\n- **Constraint:** his time. Sequoia's Shaun Maguire says he spent seven years at about 75% capacity working only for Musk ([cc.cz](https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/)). Vy also stopped taking outside money in July 2025 ([Forbes TR](https://www.forbes.com.tr/para-yatirim/elon-musk-destekcisi-vy-capital-buyuk-kazanclarin-ardindan-dis-yatirimcilara-kapilarini-kapatti)). It now invests its partners' own capital.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** spray small seed checks. His record is concentrated late-stage bets plus board seats in a handful of companies.\n\n## Differential insight\n- He and Tamas co-control Fund III: \"Alexander Tamas and John Hering, each a beneficial owner of Vy GP Fund III Limited\" ([DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1566610/000149315226020423/formdef14a.htm)).\n- He built the Musk relationship through service: \"Hering ale postupně začal dělat víc než jen posílat peníze.\" In English: he gradually started doing more than just sending money. The Neuralace domain gift is the example ([cc.cz, 2026-09-15](https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/)).\n- Maguire: \"Sedm let pracoval na sedmdesát pět procent své kapacity jen pro Elona.\" In English: for seven years he worked at 75% of his capacity just for Elon ([cc.cz](https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/)).\n- **He holds an AI board seat that was not in the repo.** He is a director of Counterpart Holdings, an insurer that underwrites with \"agentic\" AI, on its 2026 Form D ($50M from 7 investors, first sale 2026-04-06) ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1903766/000095013826000007/primary_doc.xml)). Ex-Vy MD Pablo Mendoza held that seat on the 2021-12 ($20M, 1 investor) and 2023-05 ($16M) Form Ds ([2022](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1903766/000095013822000006/primary_doc.xml), [2023](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1903766/000095013823000078/primary_doc.xml)). The seat looks like Vy's, and Hering took it over when Mendoza left. That is an inference: no filing names Vy as the investor.\n- He was an angel before Vy. \"John Hering, Trustee of the John Hering Living Trust\" held Cyanogen Series C stock alongside Redpoint and Index on the 2014-12-24 investors' rights agreement, with notices going to his Lookout email ([Cyngn S-1/A Ex 10.6](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1874097/000121390021053094/fs12021a2ex10-6_cyngninc.htm)).\n- He ran a personal SpaceX friends syndicate at about a $10B valuation. Sujay Jaswa says he got in that way, around 00:53 ([YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY)). If the ~$10B mark is right, this probably came before Vy's 2016 entry (inference).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a seed partner and Vy is closed to outside LPs, so he is neither a co-lead on a $600M lab seed nor an LP for me. He matters in three ways. He is the human route into Vy, which leads non-consensus rounds (Cerebras C 2017, Neuralink C 2021, Boring C co-lead 2022) and was in both 2024 xAI rounds ([vy-capital](../firms/vy-capital.md)). He is a Fund III signatory. And he holds the Counterpart (AI-native insurance) board seat. No AI deal names him personally as the investor. Cerebras and xAI are credited only to the firm.\n\n## Background\n- Studied Public Policy, Planning and Development at USC ([prospectus](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm)).\n- 2005: became known for scanning celebrities' phones over Bluetooth at the Oscars with classmates ([cc.cz](https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/)). Flexilis Inc. was incorporated in 2005. Its 2009 Form D lists Hering and James Burgess as officers, with David Weiden and Kevin Hartz as directors, and $1M sold ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1438425/000143842509000002/primary_doc.xml)). Flexilis became Lookout; the rename date was not confirmed.\n- Lookout CEO until March 2014, then co-founder and executive director; 43 patents ([prospectus](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm)). Still a Lookout director on the 2022 Form D/A ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1855544/000185554422000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- Coalition co-founder (incorporated 2017) and director on its 2022 Form D, beside CEO Joshua Motta, Nick Shalek and Shardul Shah ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1937129/000193712922000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **Joining Vy:** the prospectus calls him \"a founding Partner\". cc.cz says Tamas founded Vy and teamed up with Hering in 2015 ([src](https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/)). The join year is unconfirmed. He reached Musk through Antonio Gracias (same source).\n- CEO and director of Vy Global Growth, a $575M SPAC (IPO Oct 2020). It returned its trust in Sept 2022 without doing a deal ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465922102994/tm2226464d1_ex99-2.htm)).\n- Director of Fort Point Beer Co (San Francisco) on Form Ds from 2016 to 2022 ([2016](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679045/000167904516000001/primary_doc.xml), [2022](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679045/000167904522000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.vycapital.com/ | Logo-only site; no team page (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing verified | Web search was unavailable, so no handle was confirmed |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was unavailable for this run. Vy publishes almost nothing.\n\n## How they think\n- Concentrated, decades-long holds in a few companies. In Vy's words, it aims to \"own them for decades\" ([10-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465922043213/vygg-20211231x10k.htm)).\n- Counter-cyclical conviction. Vy put more than $100M into SpaceX right after the Sept 2016 Falcon 9 explosion, when other investors pulled back ([cc.cz](https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/)). FT credits the check to Hering ([src](https://www.chaincatcher.com/en/article/2289437)).\n- Founder-operator to the bone: he co-founded two security companies, and Coalition and Counterpart both use software to underwrite insurance.\n\n## Read these first\n- VYGG 424B4 bio and Vy description ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm)).\n- The VYGG letter returning the SPAC trust, Sept 2022 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465922102994/tm2226464d1_ex99-2.htm)).\n- cc.cz profile of Tamas and Hering, Sept 2026 ([src](https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cyanogen (personal, via living trust) | Series C | 2014-12-24 (agreement date) | no | Redpoint, Index, Rosehearty, Aviv Nevo | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1874097/000121390021053094/fs12021a2ex10-6_cyngninc.htm) |\n| SpaceX (personal friends syndicate) | Undisclosed (~$10B valuation) | not dated | organiser | friends incl. Sujay Jaswa | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY) |\n| SpaceX (Vy) | Undisclosed ($100M+, after the failure) | 2016-09 | no | Valor/Gracias route | [src](https://www.chaincatcher.com/en/article/2289437) |\n| The Boring Company | Director; Vy in the $3.2B Form D round | first sale 2026-08-21 | unknown | 141 investors | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1737630/000173763026000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Counterpart (AI insurance) | Director; $50M Form D | 2026-04-06 | unknown | 7 investors | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1903766/000095013826000007/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Coalition | Co-founder, director; Vy-backed | inc. 2017 | n/a | Hillhouse, Ribbit (per prospectus) | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm) |\n| TON Strategy | Vy Fund III shares (he co-controls) | PIPE 2025-08 | no | | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1566610/000149315226020423/formdef14a.htm) |\n\nAI deals naming him personally: **none confirmed.** Counterpart is the only AI-native company where he is named, and only as a director.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **SpaceX:** Gracias brought Vy to its first SpaceX deal in 2016. After the September 2016 explosion, the fund put in more than $100M. Hering then gave Starlink much of his time, helping with hiring and financial models ([cc.cz](https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/)).\n- **Neuralink:** the Neuralace domain gesture came before Vy led the Series C ($205M, 2021-07) ([Teslarati](https://www.teslarati.com/neuralink-205-million-investment-round)). No source ties him personally to the round.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Alexander Tamas | Co-runs Vy; co-owner of the Fund III GP | ~2015 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1566610/000149315226020423/formdef14a.htm) |\n| Antonio Gracias (Valor) | Introduced him to Musk; fellow Boring director | 2016 | [src](https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/) |\n| Shaun Maguire (Sequoia) | Vouches for him on the record | | [src](https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/) |\n| Sujay Jaswa (WndrCo) | Friend; joined his SpaceX syndicate | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY) |\n| Pablo Mendoza (ex-Vy, xAI) | Preceded him in the Counterpart board seat | 2021 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1903766/000095013822000006/primary_doc.xml) |\n| James Burgess, Kevin Mahaffey | Lookout co-founders | 2005 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1438425/000143842509000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Jared Birchall | Fellow Boring director (Musk's family office) | | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1737630/000173763026000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n**How to reach him:** through Gracias (Valor), Maguire (Sequoia), Jaswa (WndrCo) or the Lookout founders (Burgess, Mahaffey), all of whom have a sourced tie. A Musk-company or AI-insurance angle fits his current boards. No public inbox or conference circuit was found.\n\n## Open questions\n- His true Vy join year (2013 vs 2015).\n- Who the investor was in Counterpart's 2021 $20M Form D, and whether Vy led.\n- Who Baris Akis (Boring) represents. (Jon Shulkin, the other long-standing Counterpart director, is Valor's Co-President per the repo ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-shulkin)), so Counterpart is a Vy plus Valor board, matching Valor's portfolio listing.)\n- Whether he is a GP co-owner of Vy Nous, VY Cerebrum or the Vy Space vehicles.\n- The date and size of the SpaceX friends syndicate.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1566610/000149315226020423/formdef14a.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1737630/000173763026000003/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1903766/000095013826000007/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1903766/000095013822000006/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1903766/000095013823000078/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1874097/000121390021053094/fs12021a2ex10-6_cyngninc.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1438425/000143842509000002/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1855544/000185554422000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1937129/000193712922000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679045/000167904522000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://cc.cz/elon-musk-nebo-nic-neznama-dvojice-si-vybojovala-jeho-prizen-a-ve-spacex-dnes-drzi-podil-za-850-miliard/\n- https://www.chaincatcher.com/en/article/2289437\n- https://www.forbes.com.tr/para-yatirim/elon-musk-destekcisi-vy-capital-buyuk-kazanclarin-ardindan-dis-yatirimcilara-kapilarini-kapatti\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY\n- https://www.teslarati.com/neuralink-205-million-investment-round\n", "person:john-lilly": "\n# John Lilly\n\n## One-line take\nA tier-1 software VC (Greylock: Instagram, Figma) who left venture in 2019 and returned in April 2026 as an advising partner to his old friend Schroepfer. His frame for AI is a supply crunch: shortages in compute, power, transformers and chips.\n\n## Crux\nHe wins on relationships and long patience. He took Figma from a 2014 pass to an investment after about a year and a half of relationship-building, and joined Duolingo's board after passing on it about five times. He is constrained because he is now an advisor and angel, not a GP writing fund checks. No Gigascale deal is attributed to him. His role is board-level company building (\"from breakthrough to scaled infrastructure\", [src](https://gigascale.com/team/john-lilly/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- He calls himself an \"advisory partner at a firm called Gigascale Capital\". He describes the firm as moving beyond climate to energy, supply chain and materials, with \"data centers and AI are pulling a lot of stuff now\" ([Tech Overflow](https://www.buzzsprout.com/2533149/episodes/19087552-venture-investing-with-the-vc-who-invested-in-insta-and-figma-with-john-lilly), 2026-04-28, auto-generated transcript).\n- AI supply-crunch view: \"What happens when you run out of transformers?\" (same).\n- Admitted pass: on Duolingo, \"I just didn't think the business was very good\". He later joined its board through the relationship (same).\n- He and Schroepfer go back to Reactivity (Schroepfer became a partner there in 1999-11) and Mozilla 2005–2008 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Schroepfer)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nLow as a direct co-investor: he no longer runs a fund, and Gigascale's checks are small. Medium as a connector to Figma/Duolingo-era founders and to Schroepfer. A \"John Lilly\" is in the Terraton seed angel list, which suggests he still angel-invests (unconfirmed identity).\n\n## Background\n- BS in Computer Systems Engineering and MS in CS (HCI), Stanford. Apple research. Trilogy executive team. Co-founded Reactivity (founding CEO, later CTO), sold to Cisco in 2007 ([src](https://gigascale.com/team/john-lilly/)).\n- Mozilla COO, then CEO 2008-01 to 2010-11 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Lilly_(computer_scientist))).\n- Greylock 2011 to 2019-01 (the firm says \"8 years as a General Partner\"; Greylock's alumni page now lists him as Venture Partner, see `data/rows/greylock-team-rels.csv`). Led Instagram, Dropbox, Figma, Tumblr and Quip ([src](https://gigascale.com/team/john-lilly/)). Co-led Clockwise's Series A with Accel ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/delivering-time-with-clockwise)).\n- Lecturer at Stanford GSB. Involved with VotingWorks. Boards: Figma, Duolingo, Nuro.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://gigascale.com/team/john-lilly/ | |\n| Substack / blog | not checked | |\n| X | @johnolilly (not verified this run) | |\n| LinkedIn | not used | |\n| GitHub | — | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-04 — Tech Overflow S2E9 — the \"see, win, decide, build\" framework; the Instagram and Figma stories; AI-driven shortages; Gigascale — [link](https://www.buzzsprout.com/2533149/episodes/19087552-venture-investing-with-the-vc-who-invested-in-insta-and-figma-with-john-lilly)\n\n## How they think\n- Venture is \"see, win, decide, build\", credited to his former Greylock partner. \"You need to win the right to invest\" (Tech Overflow).\n- Curiosity matters more than a technical background for investors (same).\n- Technology optimist: humans create challenges and solve them, including AI and data centers (same).\n\n## Read these first\n1. The Tech Overflow episode (2026-04): his only recent long-form interview found.\n2. His Gigascale bio: the role scope.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot researched this run.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Instagram, Dropbox, Figma, Tumblr, Quip | Greylock investments | 2011–2019 | led (per bio) | — | [src](https://gigascale.com/team/john-lilly/) |\n| Clockwise | Series A | 2019 | co-led | Accel | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/delivering-time-with-clockwise) |\n| Terraton | Seed $11.5M (angel; identity unconfirmed) | 2025-08-27 | no | Lowercarbon, Gigascale | [src](https://eznewswire.com/newsroom/terraton-raises-11-5m-biochar-carbon-removal-emerging-markets) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- Figma: an early \"no\" in 2014, then a year and a half of breakfasts before investing (Tech Overflow).\n- Instagram: during the six weeks of Greylock diligence, Android launched and daily signups went from a few hundred thousand to a million (Tech Overflow).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Mike Schroepfer | Close friend; Reactivity and Mozilla colleague; Gigascale founder | 1999 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Schroepfer) |\n| Greylock | Former partner (2011–2019) | 2011 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Lilly_(computer_scientist)) |\n| Figma, Duolingo, Nuro | Board director | various | [src](https://gigascale.com/team/john-lilly/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is he the \"John Lilly\" angel in Terraton?\n- Has he made any check through Gigascale, or does he only advise?\n- His recent X posts on AI compute and power (not fetched).\n\n## Sources\nhttps://gigascale.com/team/john-lilly/; https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260416023129/en/Gigascale-Capital-Welcomes-John-Lilly-as-Advising-Partner; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Lilly_(computer_scientist); https://www.buzzsprout.com/2533149/episodes/19087552-venture-investing-with-the-vc-who-invested-in-insta-and-figma-with-john-lilly\n", "person:john-luttig": "\n# John Luttig\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the Founders Fund partner whose bio lists the firm's frontier-lab book (Scale AI, OpenAI, Anthropic, Cognition) [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/john-luttig/). He also writes the clearest public case that the AI talent war consolidates the frontier and makes stage discipline obsolete [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He sits on Founders Fund's most important AI relationships. The firm bio says he \"has worked on\" Scale AI, OpenAI, Anthropic and Cognition [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/john-luttig/). He also writes long, sourced essays that the firm's own partners and associates review, as well as investors at other firms [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent).\n- **His model:** Talent follows a power law. Mission-driven labs get more research per dollar than mercenary rivals. Researcher pay is rising so fast that the frontier consolidates around incumbents that can fund it [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent). He bets that closed models win, because training costs are so high [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/the-future-of-foundation-models-is).\n- **Constraint:** He is a partner at a firm whose centre of gravity is now growth-stage AI; see the Anthropic co-lead [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/09/founders-fund-nears-6-billion-close-for-latest-growth-fund-sources-say/). No source says he personally led any round. His lab credit is firm-level.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred from his writing):** hold to a fixed seed or Series A box, or refuse to re-enter a company he passed on. He writes that re-entering \"at 2-3x the valuation\" can be right [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent). He would also be sceptical of an underfunded new lab trying to compete on research talent.\n\n## Differential insight\nEvery quote below was checked against the downloaded page text on 2026-11-09.\n- **Stage discipline is dead.** \"Being a rigid seed or Series A-only investor in 2025 is anachronistic.\" [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent) (2025-07-13)\n- **Re-enter after a miss.** For companies passed on six months earlier, \"the right decision is to invest today at 2-3x the valuation.\" [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent)\n- **The talent war consolidates the frontier.** \"The talent war is a net-consolidating force on the AI research frontier.\" He argues that big researcher pay makes it nearly impossible for new entrants to compete, and compares this to starting a new quant fund [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent).\n- **New labs need giant rounds.** He names SSI, Thinking Machines and Physical Intelligence as companies that needed massive rounds just to get a shot at the frontier, because \"A single research hire can cost the entirety of a Series A\" [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent). This is the strongest outside argument *for* the $600M-seed pattern in THESIS.md, even though his overall view is that the frontier consolidates.\n- **Mission is a moat.** For OpenAI and Anthropic, \"cult status effectively creates a multiplier on their R&D budgets\", and \"money alone has not been enough\" for top researchers to defect [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent).\n- **Talent power law.** \"1,000x the baseline impact\" [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent).\n- **Closed beats open.** Winning frontier models \"should and will be closed-source\", and \"open and closed-source AI cannot both dominate in the limit\" [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/the-future-of-foundation-models-is) (2024-05-21). This sits awkwardly with Founders Fund's Prime Intellect bet; see `firms/founders-fund.md`.\n- **AI chips: Cerebras.** In late 2023 he wrote \"Cerebras is the most credible semiconductor startup.\", while also calling Nvidia \"the only practical choice.\" [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/nvidia-envy-understanding-the-gpu) (2023-11-10).\n- **Compute barriers.** He described \"an immovable wall of $100m+ LLM training runs\", and wrote that \"AWS and Azure do not want to be GPU rental businesses\" [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/nvidia-envy-understanding-the-gpu).\n- **Against indexing.** In 2021 he wrote \"you need to believe in companies when others don't.\" He also warned that \"if you index across every domain, you lose your differentiating features\" [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/indexmindset).\n- **Timing (his own disclosure):** \"Founders Fund invested in Rippling in 2020 and has a board seat.\" [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/rippling). The board member is not named.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**high**, as someone to track and as a debating partner, not as a seed co-lead. His bio covers four of the most important frontier names: Scale AI, OpenAI, Anthropic and Cognition [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/john-luttig/). His stage-agnostic stance matches mine: big seeds, willingness to pay up, and no rigid ownership discipline. His Cerebras call lines up with the Eclipse calibration in THESIS.md. The tension is that his central thesis (frontier consolidation) argues against most new-lab seeds. Read his view as the bear case I need to beat, and his \"massive round for a shot on goal\" framing as the bull case. The deals that matter to me are Cognition (Founders Fund led the March 2024 Series A, so the firm was first into the announced institutional round) and Anthropic and OpenAI (lab access). Scale AI matters less, as data infrastructure for the labs. The rest of the bio (Figma, Newfront, Flock, Loop, Specter, Rippling) is off-lens.\n\n## Background\n- BS in Computer Science, Stanford. He was Editor-in-Chief of the Stanford Review [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/john-luttig/). Dates not given.\n- Partner at Founders Fund. His firm profile was posted on 2017-02-01, which is not a confirmed start date (see `firms/founders-fund.md`) [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/john-luttig/).\n- No earlier jobs are listed on the bio. Nothing else found; web search budget was exhausted this run.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://foundersfund.com/team/john-luttig/ | firm bio |\n| Substack / blog | https://blog.johnluttig.com | \"luttig's learnings\". 12 posts from 2020-07 to 2025-07, about one or two a year; subscriber count not shown (as of 2026-11-09) [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/archive) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/absoluttig | handle @absoluttig, from the Substack About page [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/about); follower count not retrieved (fetch blocked) |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was unavailable this run (budget exhausted), so this section has not been properly searched. See Open questions.\n\n## How they think\n- **Main theses:** Talent is the scarce input in AI, and researcher pay is repricing labour. Mission-driven labs hold their researchers better than mercenary rivals. Closed models capture most of the value. Compute is Nvidia-dominated, and neoclouds have room because hyperscalers don't want to rent GPUs [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent), [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/the-future-of-foundation-models-is), [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/nvidia-envy-understanding-the-gpu).\n- **Contrarian views:** He argues against stage discipline and against index-style venture. He also says new research entrants are nearly impossible, which is contrarian against the 2025–26 wave of new-lab seeds.\n- **Calls right or wrong:** His 2024 closed-source call fits Anthropic and OpenAI dominance so far. His 2023 Cerebras call has not been checked against what happened later. He warned of acquihire-style deals, citing Google's Character AI and Windsurf deals, which he says signal how much big companies will pay for talent [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [hypercapitalism and the AI talent wars](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent) (2025-07-13): his stage-agnostic and consolidation thesis. Essential.\n2. [The future of foundation models is closed-source](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/the-future-of-foundation-models-is) (2024-05-21): why capital intensity favours closed labs.\n3. [Nvidia Envy: understanding the GPU gold rush](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/nvidia-envy-understanding-the-gpu) (2023-11-10): his compute and chips map, including the Cerebras call.\n4. [The index mindset](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/indexmindset) (2021-09-15): his case for conviction over indexing.\n5. [Rippling and the return of ambition](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/rippling) (2022-04-05): his founder-quality lens (\"compound\" products).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot retrieved, because the X fetch was blocked (HTTP 402). The best proxy is his essays' acknowledgment lists. They repeatedly thank Philip Clark (Thrive), Lachy Groom, Axel Ericsson, Cat Wu and Divyahans Gupta, and Founders Fund colleagues Joey Krug, Robert Windesheim, Koren Gilbai and Michael Solana [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent), [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/the-future-of-foundation-models-is), [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/nvidia-envy-understanding-the-gpu).\n\n## Portfolio\nAttribution is firm-level: the bio says he \"has worked on\" these companies. No source says he led a round personally.\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cognition | Series A ($21M) | 2024-03-12 | Founders Fund led | Patrick Collison, John Collison, Elad Gil, Sarah Guo (named supporters) | [src](https://cognition.com/blog/introducing-devin), [bio](https://foundersfund.com/team/john-luttig/) |\n| Anthropic | $30B at $380B post (Founders Fund's first direct investment) | 2026-02 | Founders Fund co-lead | D. E. Shaw, Dragoneer, ICONIQ, MGX | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/09/founders-fund-nears-6-billion-close-for-latest-growth-fund-sources-say/), [bio](https://foundersfund.com/team/john-luttig/) |\n| OpenAI | unknown | | unknown | | [bio](https://foundersfund.com/team/john-luttig/) |\n| Scale AI | Founders Fund led Series C (secondary source) | 2019 | Founders Fund lead | | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Founders_Fund), [bio](https://foundersfund.com/team/john-luttig/) |\n| Rippling | unknown; Founders Fund holds a board seat | 2020 | unknown | | [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/rippling) |\n| Figma, Newfront Insurance, Flock Safety, Loop Logistics, Specter | unknown | | unknown | | [bio](https://foundersfund.com/team/john-luttig/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Cognition, first in:** Founders Fund led the $21M Series A in March 2024, the first announced institutional round [src](https://cognition.com/blog/introducing-devin). Lux and 8VC co-led the next round. Founders Fund then led again in Sept 2025, a >$400M round at a $10.2B post [src](https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents). The bio credits Luttig with working on Cognition, but which partner led is unconfirmed.\n- **Anthropic, late:** Founders Fund's first direct Anthropic investment was the 2026 round [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/09/founders-fund-nears-6-billion-close-for-latest-growth-fund-sources-say/). It came about 21 months after his 2024 closed-source essay, which argued the case for closed labs. Founders Fund was a follower here, not first in.\n- **Rippling:** His essay discloses that Founders Fund invested in 2020 and holds a board seat. It does not say who holds the seat [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/rippling).\n- No intro paths or first-contact stories found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Founders Fund | Partner | 2017 (profile post date) | [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/john-luttig/) |\n| Stanford Review | Former Editor-in-Chief | — | [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/john-luttig/) |\n| Joey Krug (Founders Fund) | Colleague; reviewer of his 2024 and 2025 essays | 2024 | [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent) |\n| Philip Clark (Thrive) | Reviewer of his 2023, 2024 and 2025 essays | 2023 | [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent) |\n| Lachy Groom | Reviewer of his 2023 and 2025 essays | 2023 | [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/nvidia-envy-understanding-the-gpu) |\n| Robert Windesheim, Koren Gilbai (Founders Fund associates) | Essay reviewers | 2024–25 | [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent), [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/the-future-of-foundation-models-is) |\n| Parker Conrad (Rippling) | Subject of his case study; Founders Fund board seat at Rippling | 2020 | [src](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/rippling) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did he personally lead or hold the board seat on any of Cognition, Scale AI or Rippling? Check Cognition and Rippling board listings.\n- Podcast and talk appearances, and X activity and follower count. Not searched this run because the search budget was exhausted.\n- Has he publicly updated the 2025 consolidation view now that many new labs have raised giant seeds? Did Founders Fund back any of SSI, Thinking Machines or Physical Intelligence?\n- Did Founders Fund ever invest in Cerebras after his 2023 call?\n- The essays thank \"Melisa Tokmak\" (2025) and \"Melisa Tokmak Luttig\" (2023). Their relationship to him is not stated, so don't infer one.\n\n## Sources\n- https://foundersfund.com/team/john-luttig/\n- https://blog.johnluttig.com/archive\n- https://blog.johnluttig.com/about\n- https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent\n- https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/the-future-of-foundation-models-is\n- https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/nvidia-envy-understanding-the-gpu\n- https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/indexmindset\n- https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/rippling\n- https://cognition.com/blog/introducing-devin\n- https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/09/founders-fund-nears-6-billion-close-for-latest-growth-fund-sources-say/\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Founders_Fund (secondary)\n", "person:john-somorjai": "\n# John Somorjai\n\n## One-line take\nFounded Salesforce Ventures (SFV) with Marc Benioff in 2009 and now holds the single seat above both SFV and Salesforce corporate M&A. He is not a deal partner; he is the approver whose job connects a Salesforce check to a later Salesforce acquisition.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He controls one balance sheet through two doors: venture (SFV, about $11.3B carrying value at 2026-07-31, see [firms/salesforce-ventures.md](../firms/salesforce-ventures.md)) and M&A (80+ acquisitions including ExactTarget, MuleSoft, Tableau and Slack [src](https://salesforceventures.com/people/john-somorjai/)). The firm's own 15-year report says SFV started when \"Marc Benioff and Head of Corporate Development John Somorjai came together\" [src](https://salesforceventures.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/15-Years-of-Salesforce-Ventures.pdf). The venture arm was born inside M&A.\n- **His stated edge** is demand information, not price: \"we understand buying signals\", meaning SFV knows what Salesforce customers will buy (podcast, quotes edited by SFV [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/john-somorjai-ask-more-of-ai-podcast/)).\n- **What constrains him.** All SFV money is Salesforce's own; there are no outside LPs. Its AI \"funds\" ($50M in 2017; $250M to $500M to $1B in 2023-24) are allocations announced by the parent, so marks hit Salesforce's P&L and Anthropic is now about 45% of the portfolio (firm page). He has no AI deal bylines; partners such as Paul Drews and Emily Zhao source and price the deals.\n- **What he would never do** (inferred, unconfirmed): back a company whose product competes head-on with Salesforce's core CRM or agent stack without a partnership attached. Every AI-fund announcement he has fronted ties the money to Salesforce's platform or ecosystem.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The venture arm was born as an M&A funnel.** The co-founder was the head of corporate development, per SFV's own report: \"Marc Benioff and Head of Corporate Development John Somorjai came together\" [src](https://salesforceventures.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/15-Years-of-Salesforce-Ventures.pdf). The 2023 promotion that put him formally over both ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/powerlist-2024-john-somorjai/?format=md)) made official what had been true since 2009. Portfolio-to-acquisition cases: Airkit and Spiff (2023, \"both Salesforce Ventures portfolio companies\", GCV), Convergence (SFV pre-seed Sep 2024, acquisition agreed May 2025) and Qualified (Dec 2025) (firm page).\n- **His investing edge, in his words, is demand data:** \"we understand buying signals\" [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/john-somorjai-ask-more-of-ai-podcast/). SFV says it edited these quotes, so treat them as summary-grade.\n- **The Gen AI Fund served two goals.** On the same podcast he described it as getting into the best companies \"while building an ecosystem of partners around our internal AI efforts\" [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/john-somorjai-ask-more-of-ai-podcast/). That is why Anthropic and Cohere were announced as product partners on the day of the fund launch (firm page).\n- **His 2025 line moved from models to businesses:** \"The companies that will win aren't just building better models\" (Oct 2025, at $850M+ deployed of the $1B) [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-deploys-over-850m-of-its-1b-ai-fund-to-back-the-ai-revolution/).\n- **2017 origin of the AI program:** \"We want more of those companies to do these innovations on Salesforce's platform\" (as EVP, Salesforce Ventures, launching the $50M AI fund) [src](https://fortune.com/2017/09/19/salesforce-ventures-artificial-intelligence-ai-fund/).\n- **Reporting line (unconfirmed).** A 2016 Chinese translation of a US article says he reported directly to Benioff, had previously worked at Oracle, and did 31 SFV deals that year, the latest a $35M SessionM round [src](https://developer.aliyun.com/article/146342). The original English article was not found, and the Oracle detail is not on his SFV bio or in GCV (which names Ingenio). No source describes an investment committee or who approves above him in 2023-26.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a co-investor in $20M frontier-lab seeds: SFV has never been found in a frontier-lab seed, and he personally prices nothing. He matters to you in two ways. (1) **Acquirer and exit path** for AI-native automation companies that sell into the enterprise. He approves both the SFV check and the later acquisition, and Convergence went from SFV pre-seed to an agreed sale in about 8 months. (2) **Later-round price-setter or anchor**: SFV's AI-fund allocations decide whether it shows up in your companies' B-D rounds. For an actual deal, the people to know are Emily Zhao and Paul Drews (labs and infra) and Alexandra Quie (EMEA agents). Somorjai is the person behind them who says yes, and the person to know if you want Salesforce as a buyer.\n\n## Background\n- B.A., Dartmouth College; J.D., UC Berkeley [src](https://salesforceventures.com/people/john-somorjai/).\n- VP Business Development, Ingenio, up to its acquisition by AT&T; dates unconfirmed [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/powerlist-2024-john-somorjai/?format=md). An Oracle stint appears only in the 2016 translated profile (unconfirmed) [src](https://developer.aliyun.com/article/146342).\n- Joined Salesforce in 2005 to lead strategic transactions (later EVP Corporate Development) [src](https://salesforceventures.com/people/john-somorjai/).\n- 2008-09: co-founded SFV with Benioff to fund AppExchange partners during the financial crisis [src](https://salesforceventures.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/15-Years-of-Salesforce-Ventures.pdf).\n- NVCA board, 2017-2021 [src](https://salesforceventures.com/people/john-somorjai/).\n- 2023: promoted to Chief Corporate Development and Investments Officer [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/powerlist-2024-john-somorjai/?format=md).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://salesforceventures.com/people/john-somorjai/ | SFV bio with \"Get in Touch\"; loads 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | SFV \"Perspectives\" posts quote him only on fund announcements |\n| X | https://x.com/jsomorjai | Handle listed on SFV bio; page resolves 2026-11-10; activity not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/johnsomorjai/ | Listed on SFV bio; not opened |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-01 — Ask More of AI (host Clara Shih, then CEO of Salesforce AI) — on the founding of SFV and the AI portfolio — https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/john-somorjai-ask-more-of-ai-podcast/ — he names Runway, Typeface and AutogenAI as the app-layer bets and calls buying-signal knowledge SFV's edge (quotes edited by SFV).\n\n## How they think\n- Venture as ecosystem infrastructure: SFV started in order to \"become the anchor investor of a fundraising round and pull in other investors\" for AppExchange partners (DocuSign, Box) [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/john-somorjai-ask-more-of-ai-podcast/).\n- Productivity ROI as the AI buying test: he cites McKinsey's $4T-a-year savings figure and argues buyers will pay for large efficiency gains (same source).\n- 2025: the winners build durable businesses around AI, not just better models [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-deploys-over-850m-of-its-1b-ai-fund-to-back-the-ai-revolution/).\n- Calls: early cloud-era bets (DocuSign, Box, Snowflake, Zoom, Databricks per bio) and the 2023 decision to buy into the model layer (Anthropic stake about $5B by 2026, firm page). Wrong calls: nothing sourced.\n\n## Read these first\n- SFV bio: the 80+ acquisitions list and the dual role [src](https://salesforceventures.com/people/john-somorjai/).\n- 15 Years of Salesforce Ventures (Nov 2024), co-signed with Patel and Drews [src](https://salesforceventures.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/15-Years-of-Salesforce-Ventures.pdf).\n- Ask More of AI podcast recap (Jan 2024) [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/john-somorjai-ask-more-of-ai-podcast/).\n- $1B AI expansion (Sep 2024) and $850M-deployed (Oct 2025) releases [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-expands-ai-investment-to-1-billion/), [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-deploys-over-850m-of-its-1b-ai-fund-to-back-the-ai-revolution/).\n\n## Social engagement\nX handle @jsomorjai is listed on his SFV bio; posting cadence and engagement were not checked (web search was exhausted this session, so nothing beyond direct fetches).\n\n## Portfolio\nHe has no personal deal bylines, so no deal rows were written (`data/rows/john-somorjai-deals.csv` is header-only). The fund-level actions he is named on:\n\n| Company / fund | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| $50M AI fund (Einstein era) | fund announcement | 2017-09 | n/a | n/a | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2017/09/19/salesforce-ventures-artificial-intelligence-ai-fund/) |\n| Generative AI Fund $250M to $500M | fund announcement | 2023-03 / 2023-06 | n/a | n/a | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/generative-ai-fund/) |\n| AI fund to $1B (quoted) | fund announcement | 2024-09 | n/a | n/a | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-expands-ai-investment-to-1-billion/) |\n| $850M+ deployed of $1B (quoted) | update | 2025-10 | n/a | n/a | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-deploys-over-850m-of-its-1b-ai-fund-to-back-the-ai-revolution/) |\n| Airkit (AI customer-service agents) | acquisition by Salesforce, overseen | 2023 | n/a | SFV portfolio company | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/powerlist-2024-john-somorjai/?format=md) |\n| Spiff (non-AI) | acquisition by Salesforce, overseen | announced 2023-12 | n/a | SFV portfolio company | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/powerlist-2024-john-somorjai/?format=md) |\n| SessionM (non-AI) | $35M round (unconfirmed) | 2016 | unconfirmed | | [translated profile](https://developer.aliyun.com/article/146342) |\n\nAI companies he names as SFV bets (no byline): Runway, Typeface, AutogenAI (2024 podcast), and Runway, Typeface, Hugging Face, Cohere and Anthropic (GCV 2024).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Convergence (unconfirmed for him personally).** SFV joined the $12M pre-seed in Sep 2024 (Alexandra Quie). Salesforce agreed to acquire the company in May 2025 to anchor a London AI lab for Agentforce (firm page; [Salesforce](https://salesforce.com/news/stories/salesforce-signs-definitive-agreement-to-acquire-convergence-ai/)). As the officer over both SFV and M&A, he would sit over both steps. No source names him on either.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Marc Benioff | Co-founded SFV; reports to him (reporting line unconfirmed after 2016) | 2009 | [15-year report](https://salesforceventures.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/15-Years-of-Salesforce-Ventures.pdf), [aliyun 2016](https://developer.aliyun.com/article/146342) |\n| Paul Drews | SFV Managing Partner; co-signed the 15-year letter | 2024 (letter) | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/15-Years-of-Salesforce-Ventures.pdf) |\n| Khushboo Patel | SFV COO; co-signed the 15-year letter | 2024 (letter) | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/15-Years-of-Salesforce-Ventures.pdf) |\n| Clara Shih | Interviewed him on Ask More of AI | 2024-01 | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/john-somorjai-ask-more-of-ai-podcast/) |\n| NVCA | Board member | 2017-2021 | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/people/john-somorjai/) |\n| Ingenio | VP Business Development (prior to Salesforce) | until 2005 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/powerlist-2024-john-somorjai/?format=md) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who he reports to now (Benioff? CFO?), and whether SFV has a formal investment committee and what dollar threshold goes above him. Unconfirmed; check the Salesforce proxy and 10-K executive-officer list (SEC returned 404 this session).\n- Which AI acquisitions after 2023 (Convergence, Qualified, Regrello, Informatica) he personally ran. GCV 2024 covers only Spiff and Airkit.\n- Whether an SFV stake now gives Salesforce any formal ROFR or acquisition preference: nothing found.\n- The Oracle stint and the 2016 SessionM attribution need the original English article.\n- Whether he is still in both roles at 2026-11-10: the SFV team page still lists him as President; the corp-dev title was not re-checked (no web search this session).\n\n## Sources\n- https://salesforceventures.com/people/john-somorjai/\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/powerlist-2024-john-somorjai/?format=md\n- https://fortune.com/2017/09/19/salesforce-ventures-artificial-intelligence-ai-fund/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/15-Years-of-Salesforce-Ventures.pdf\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/john-somorjai-ask-more-of-ai-podcast/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/generative-ai-fund/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-expands-ai-investment-to-1-billion/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-deploys-over-850m-of-its-1b-ai-fund-to-back-the-ai-revolution/\n- https://www.salesforce.com/news/stories/salesforce-signs-definitive-agreement-to-acquire-spiff/\n- https://salesforce.com/news/stories/salesforce-signs-definitive-agreement-to-acquire-convergence-ai/\n- https://developer.aliyun.com/article/146342 (2016 Chinese translation; low confidence)\n", "person:john-wesley": "\n# John Wesley\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. Pronouns: GCV uses \"he/his\", so this file does too. \"Investment Director\" is mapped to seniority `principal` as the closest allowed value. The aggregator title \"Sr. Director\" would map to `vp`, but it is unverified and is not used. He has given no public quotes that were found. Every quote below is source text about him or about the unit.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is NVentures's longest-tenured investor under Sid Siddeek and its finance and biotech operator, sitting inside a unit that co-leads $100M+ tech-bio rounds. His name is on none of them, so treat him as a door into the NVentures bio book, not a proven check-signer.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** NVentures relaunched in 2023 \"with John Wesley as investment director\" ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md)). In late 2026 he still calls himself a \"founding team member\" ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/john-wesley-68a18b/), fetched 2026-11-10). His background is unusual for a corporate VC: hedge-fund SVP (Pequot), then two biotech operating roles as finance lead (Omniox VP finance and BD, Dren Bio CFO). That fits a unit whose sector concentration is tech-bio (Terray, Inceptive, Generate, Relation, Genesis; see [firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md)).\n- **Constraint:** NVentures invests from NVIDIA's balance sheet, sized \"a few million dollars to tens of millions per deal\" ([GCV 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)). Siddeek is the quoted voice where anyone is quoted (Relation seed, 2024), and on the Terray B the NVIDIA voice was healthcare VP Kimberly Powell, not anyone at NVentures ([BioSpace 2024-10-18](https://www.biospace.com/press-releases/terray-therapeutics-closes-oversubscribed-120m-series-b-to-advance-its-generative-ai-pipeline-of-small-molecule-therapeutics-to-the-clinic)). On the evidence, he prepares and works deals and the head signs them. That is inference: nothing states his signing authority.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** take the board seat on a co-led round. On both NVentures bio co-leads checked (Terray B, Relation seed), the seat went to the financial co-lead.\n- **Attribution gap:** GCV lists Terray, Inceptive and ArtiSight as NVentures portfolio in his award profile but does not credit him with any of them. The task's hypothesis that he is \"likely the working investor on 2023-24 bio deals\" fits the timing (NVentures first went into Terray around November 2023, after the relaunch). It remains unproven.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Founding-era hire, possibly pre-relaunch.** He describes himself as an \"Investment director and founding team member of NVentures\" ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/john-wesley-68a18b/), fetched 2026-11-10). ContactOut, an aggregator, dates his NVIDIA start to Jan 2022 ([ContactOut](https://contactout.com/John-Wesley-8114627)). His LinkedIn also lists an \"AI Foundations\" certificate issued Jan 2022. Together these suggest he joined during the 2022 build-out before the 2023 public relaunch. This is a medium-to-low confidence read; the aggregator record also carries a \"Manager, Global Quantum Computing BD (June 2023–present)\" title that looks like a different John Wesley.\n- **He kept an outside biotech governance seat through NVIDIA.** His LinkedIn reads \"Director from 2012-2020, currently board observer\" at Omniox ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/john-wesley-68a18b/)), and GCV says he \"still holds\" the observer position ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md)). Omniox is a non-AI oxygen-delivery biotech that predates NVIDIA. It is his only sourced board or observer seat, and it shows NVentures permits outside observer roles.\n- **NVentures co-leads its bio rounds but leaves the board to the other lead.** On the Terray B (Oct 2024, $120M), NVentures co-led as an \"existing investor\", yet \"Sid Shenai, who partnered with Bedford Ridge Capital on this financing, will join Terray's Board\" ([BioSpace](https://www.biospace.com/press-releases/terray-therapeutics-closes-oversubscribed-120m-series-b-to-advance-its-generative-ai-pipeline-of-small-molecule-therapeutics-to-the-clinic)). The Relation seed followed the same pattern, with DCVC taking the seat ([DCVC](https://www.dcvc.com/press-releases/relation-therapeutics-secures-35-million-of-new-seed-financing-led-by-dcvc-and-co-lead-nventures-nvidias-venture-arm/)). For a founder this means an NVentures co-lead brings price-setting money without governance. For Wesley it means no board footprint shows up publicly.\n- **NVIDIA's public voice on bio deals is the healthcare business unit.** Kimberly Powell, VP of healthcare, gave the NVIDIA quote on the Terray B ([BioSpace](https://www.biospace.com/press-releases/terray-therapeutics-closes-oversubscribed-120m-series-b-to-advance-its-generative-ai-pipeline-of-small-molecule-therapeutics-to-the-clinic)). That cuts against the firm file's line that no NVIDIA individual is named on rounds. It also points to a second route in, through the BioNeMo and healthcare team.\n- **Podcasts, X, Substack, talks:** nothing found in three targeted searches (2026-11-10).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium** (as of 2026-11-10). His lane is tech-bio rather than labs, chips or compute, which keeps him off the core of the lens. The unit he sits in, though, co-leads $100M+ A and B rounds, and one of the deals of his era is frontier-AI talent: Inceptive was founded by Transformer co-author Jakob Uszkoreit, and NVentures co-led its $100M Series A with a16z in Sept 2023 ([Maginative](https://www.maginative.com/article/inceptive-raises-100-million-to-design-new-vaccines-and-therapies-with-ai)). NVentures came in at the A, not the seed. For my $20M-into-big-seeds style he is a co-investor contact for AI-bio labs with a frontier-lab pedigree, not a lead source for pre-training labs. Deals that matter to me, none credited to him: Inceptive A, and Genesis Therapeutics (later Genesis Molecular AI, where Siddeek, not Wesley, is listed on the team page).\n\n## Background\n- Yale degree; Wharton MBA ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md)). CFA charterholder per LinkedIn certifications ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/john-wesley-68a18b/)).\n- Morgan Stanley, Bain Capital, Concourse Capital Management, HIG Capital; then SVP at hedge fund Pequot Capital Management. No dates given ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md)).\n- Omniox, about four years as VP finance and business development. The company was \"Wesley's entry into early-stage tech\". Director 2012-2020, then board observer ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md), [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/john-wesley-68a18b/)).\n- Dren Bio (protein engineering), CFO from 2020 ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md)).\n- NVIDIA / NVentures: investment director at the 2023 relaunch. Possible Jan 2022 start per aggregator only. Still at NVIDIA per LinkedIn, 2026-11.\n- GCV Top 50 Emerging Leaders in corporate venturing, 2024.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | no NVIDIA team page |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | searched 2026-11-10 |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/john-wesley-68a18b/ | Current company NVIDIA; About line confirms the role (fetched 2026-11-10) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found (searched 2026-11-10).\n\n## How they think\n- Nothing first-hand. The unit's stated criteria are team strength, durability of the technology platform and market potential ([GCV 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)). They are Siddeek's words, not his.\n- Inferred from his career: a finance and structuring lens (hedge fund, then CFO). He is likely the person modelling valuation and terms on bio rounds. Unverified.\n\n## Read these first\n- [GCV Emerging Leaders 2024: John Wesley](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md): the only profile of him.\n- [GCV 2024 Siddeek interview](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md): the unit's check range and sourcing.\n- [Terray Series B release](https://www.biospace.com/press-releases/terray-therapeutics-closes-oversubscribed-120m-series-b-to-advance-its-generative-ai-pipeline-of-small-molecule-therapeutics-to-the-clinic): how an NVentures bio co-lead is structured.\n- [firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md): the three NVIDIA investing pools.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| None personally attributed | | | | | GCV lists Terray, Inceptive and ArtiSight as NVentures portfolio in his profile without crediting him ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md)) |\n\nUnit-level context, NOT his deals:\n| Company | Round | Date | NVentures role | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Inceptive | Series A ($100M) | 2023-09-07 | co-led with a16z; Obvious participated. NVentures not in the 2022 seed | [Maginative](https://www.maginative.com/article/inceptive-raises-100-million-to-design-new-vaccines-and-therapies-with-ai) |\n| Terray Therapeutics | Strategic (undisclosed equity) | ~2023-11 | first NVentures check, after the Madrona-led Series A of Feb 2022 | search snippet of FinSMEs ([src](https://www.finsmes.com/?p=293374)); page not readable, low confidence |\n| Terray Therapeutics | Series B ($120M) | 2024-10-17 | co-led as existing investor with Bedford Ridge; no board seat | [BioSpace](https://www.biospace.com/press-releases/terray-therapeutics-closes-oversubscribed-120m-series-b-to-advance-its-generative-ai-pipeline-of-small-molecule-therapeutics-to-the-clinic) |\n| ArtiSight | Series B (reported $42M) | 2024 | NVIDIA participation per ZoomInfo only; unconfirmed | [ZoomInfo](https://www.zoominfo.com/financial/artisight/527598188) (aggregator) |\n\nTiming: in both sourced cases NVentures came in after the first institutional round: the Inceptive A after the 2022 seed, Terray more than 18 months after the Madrona A. It then led or co-led a priced round. It was a later-stage co-lead, not first money.\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found that names him. The Terray path, a small strategic check (~Nov 2023) followed by a co-lead of the next priced round (Oct 2024), is the visible NVentures pattern in bio.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NVIDIA / NVentures | Investment Director, founding team member | 2023 (possibly 2022) | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md), [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/john-wesley-68a18b/) |\n| Mohamed \"Sid\" Siddeek | Head of NVentures; his boss by structure | 2023 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md) |\n| Omniox | VP finance & BD; director 2012-2020; board observer since | 2012 | [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/john-wesley-68a18b/) |\n| Dren Bio | CFO | 2020 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md) |\n| Pequot Capital Management | SVP | undated | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md) |\n\n**How a founder reaches him (inference):** there is no public channel and no inbound address. GCV says NVentures has \"a relatively traditional way of finding investments\" through internal and external relationships ([GCV 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)). Routes in: (1) LinkedIn, which he keeps current; (2) a co-lead who has already done a round with NVentures (a16z bio, DCVC, Bedford Ridge, Madrona); (3) the NVIDIA healthcare unit under Kimberly Powell, which speaks on these deals; (4) NVIDIA Inception, which takes no equity.\n\n## Open questions\n- Which 2023-24 bio deals did he run: Inceptive A, Terray, Genesis, Relation, Generate? A company release or Form D naming him would settle it.\n- Is \"Sr. Director\" a real promotion, and when? Only aggregators show it.\n- Does NVentures's bio work route through Powell's healthcare unit before reaching the venture team?\n- Confirm the date and amount of the first NVentures check into Terray (~Nov 2023).\n- For the main session: `firms/nvidia.md` says no NVIDIA individual is named on rounds. Kimberly Powell is quoted on the Terray B, so that line needs a caveat. Lead rows for john-wesley in other files should flip to `profiled`.\n\n## Sources\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md (2024-03-11)\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/john-wesley-68a18b/ (fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md (2024-03-22)\n- https://www.biospace.com/press-releases/terray-therapeutics-closes-oversubscribed-120m-series-b-to-advance-its-generative-ai-pipeline-of-small-molecule-therapeutics-to-the-clinic (2024-10-18)\n- https://www.maginative.com/article/inceptive-raises-100-million-to-design-new-vaccines-and-therapies-with-ai (2023-09-07)\n- https://www.dcvc.com/press-releases/relation-therapeutics-secures-35-million-of-new-seed-financing-led-by-dcvc-and-co-lead-nventures-nvidias-venture-arm/\n- Aggregators: https://contactout.com/John-Wesley-8114627 ; https://www.zoominfo.com/financial/artisight/527598188 ; https://www.finsmes.com/?p=293374 (snippet only)\n- ../firms/nvidia.md ; ../sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md ; ../people/mohamed-sid-siddeek.md\n", "person:jon-koval": "\n# Jon Koval\n\n*Research note (2026-11-10): web search was exhausted for this run, so everything here comes from pages fetched directly: the valorep.com bio and its Squarespace JSON, the Valor Atreides AI I Form D, EDGAR full-text search, Sopris Capital's own sites, and the four deal announcements. I did not use web.archive.org. No podcast, X or press source was reachable. Pronouns: the bio uses only \"Jon\", so this page uses \"they\".*\n\n## One-line take\nKoval is the one Valor-side name attached to the $759M Valor Atreides AI fund, but they work on the fund rather than sign for it. Treat them as a diligence and portfolio-finance contact, not a decision-maker.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they matter:** Koval is a Valor investment-team VP whose bio says their job includes \"supporting\" the Valor Atreides AI fund. That fund co-led Bedrock B, Watney A and Flow B and joined Sable A ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval)).\n- **What constrains them:** Koval has no approval role. The fund's Form D lists eight related persons and Koval is not one of them ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2033590/000101297526000109/primary_doc.xml)). They are not quoted in any release and hold no board seat that any source shows.\n- **What their time goes to:** much of it is not deal work. The bio describes spells inside portfolio companies \"in an operational finance role\" and federal advisory work ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval)).\n- **What they'd never do:** price or sign a deal alone. At Valor, Gracias and the partners approve, and Gracias is the one quoted on Atreides-fund deals ([Bedrock B release](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/bedrock-robotics-raises-270-million-in-series-b-funding-to-accelerate-the-future-of-autonomous-construction-302679014.html)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **They staff the fund but do not sign it.** The bio says Koval supports \"the Valor Atreides AI fund\" ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval)). The fund's Form D lists eight related persons: Gracias, Shulkin, Sabater, Swanson and Dean for Valor, and Baker, Malone and Finn for Atreides. Koval is not among them ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2033590/000101297526000109/primary_doc.xml)). EDGAR full-text search finds no filing that names \"Jon Koval\" or \"Jonathan Koval\" ([efts](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Jon%20Koval%22)).\n- **The bio line probably entered in May 2026 (unconfirmed).** The page record was created on 2022-03-03, which fits \"joined Valor in 2022\". It was last edited on 2026-05-01 ([JSON](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval?format=json); the date is read from the Squarespace object id and the `updatedOn` field). That edit falls after Bedrock B (2026-02) and before Sable A (2026-07), Watney A (2026-09) and Flow B (2026-09). I could not confirm whether the Atreides-fund sentence or the VP title was added in that edit, because I did not consult archived copies.\n- **They moved from one man's money to retail money.** Sopris Capital, Koval's prior firm, is a 25-year-old Aspen firm whose founder, Andrew M. Paul, says they started it \"to invest my own capital\" ([src](https://sopriscapitalgrowth.com/team/)). The firm says it does not raise outside money. The fund Koval now supports is the opposite: $758.7M from 664 investors, a $50,000 minimum, and placement through Jefferies, iCapital, Morgan Stanley, Compass and UBS ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2033590/000101297526000109/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **They have done government work.** The bio says Koval \"served as a Senior Advisor across a number of domestic security federal government agencies\" while at Valor ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval)). The agencies and dates are not named. The same pattern shows up in other Valor staff: Gruenbaum (GSA), Baker (ODNI) and Malcher (VA) (see `firms/valor-equity-partners.md`).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** Koval is a VP who is not named on any deal, holds no board seat, and is not a fund promoter. Neither the check nor the price runs through them.\n\nThe fund itself is relevant to me because it backs AI robotics and automation at Series A and B:\n- Bedrock B ($270M, co-lead)\n- Watney A ($80M, co-lead)\n- Flow Engineering B ($50M, co-lead)\n- Sable A ($45M, participant)\n\nIts partner-level decision-maker is Gracias. Koval is useful as a warm diligence contact on deals that fund is already looking at, not as a co-lead partner for a $20M seed check.\n\n## Background\n| When | What | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| unknown | B.S.B.A. in Finance and Economics with honors, The Ohio State University, Fisher College of Business | [bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval) |\n| before 2022 (dates unconfirmed) | Analyst, Sopris Capital. The Valor bio calls it a VC firm focused on SaaS, healthcare and tech-enabled services. Koval is not on today's Sopris Growth team page | [bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval), [Sopris team](https://sopriscapitalgrowth.com/team/) |\n| 2022 | Joined Valor's Investment Team. The bio page record dates to 2022-03-03; the title at entry is not stated | [bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval), [JSON](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval?format=json) |\n| 2022–present | Vice President, Investment Team. Supports the flagship Growth funds and the Valor Atreides AI fund, has done operational finance work at portfolio companies, and has been a federal Senior Advisor (agencies and dates unnamed) | [bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval) |\n\nHow they got the seat: nothing found beyond the bio. There is no announcement of the hire and no announcement of a promotion.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval | Firm bio, verified 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Not checked beyond the firm site; search was unavailable |\n| LinkedIn | nothing verified | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\nHow a founder or co-investor reaches them: there is no public email or social handle. The verified route is Valor's Chicago office, 320 N. Sangamon Street, Suite 1200 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2082328/000208232826000002/primary_doc.xml)). The practical route is an existing Valor Atreides portfolio company, such as Bedrock, Watney, Sable or Flow, where Koval may have worked on the diligence (unconfirmed).\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Search was unavailable in this run.\n\n## How they think\nNothing on the record. Koval has no public writing or talks that I could find.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Valor bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval): the only primary source on Koval's role.\n2. [Valor Atreides AI I Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2033590/000101297526000109/primary_doc.xml): who actually signs for the fund Koval supports.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nNo source names Koval on any deal. These are the fund's rounds that Koval's role may have touched; Koval's involvement in each is unconfirmed:\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bedrock Robotics | Series B ($270M at ~$1.75B) | 2026-02-04 | Fund co-led; Gracias quoted | CapitalG (co-lead), Xora, 8VC, Eclipse | [release](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/bedrock-robotics-raises-270-million-in-series-b-funding-to-accelerate-the-future-of-autonomous-construction-302679014.html) |\n| Sable | Series A ($45M) | 2026-07-16 | Participant | Sequoia and 8VC (leads), BoxGroup, SV Angel | [release](https://www.finanznachrichten.de/nachrichten-2026-07/69050088-sable-raises-dollar-45m-to-build-the-first-ai-employee-that-can-click-see-and-explain-200.htm) |\n| Watney Robotics | Series A ($80M) | 2026-09 | Fund co-led | Hummingbird (co-lead); Conviction, Abstract and A* returning | [Dealroom, secondary](https://dealroom.co/news/151986-data-centre-robot-startup-watney-raises-80m-series-a/) |\n| Flow Engineering | Series B ($50M at $750M post) | 2026-09-30 | Valor and Atreides co-led | Sequoia, EQT | [press, secondary](https://cryptobriefing.com/valor-atreides-flow-engineering-50m-series-b/) |\n\nAll four rounds are already in the deals data under `valor-equity-partners`. `data/rows/jon-koval-deals.csv` is intentionally header-only.\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. No release, blog post or filing names Koval.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Valor Equity Partners | at: Vice President, Investment Team | 2022 | [bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval) |\n| Valor Atreides AI Fund | Supports the fund; not a Form D related person | unconfirmed | [bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2033590/000101297526000109/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Sopris Capital | at: Analyst (until 2022) | unconfirmed | [bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval) |\n| US federal domestic-security agencies | Senior Advisor | unconfirmed | [bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which agencies Koval advised, and when. Was it part of the 2025 Valor-to-government wave?\n- When the Atreides-fund sentence and the VP title entered the bio. Archived copies of the page would settle this; I did not consult them in this run.\n- Which portfolio companies Koval did operational finance work for.\n- Koval's dates at Sopris, and whether Sopris was then branded as a venture firm. The WordPress install's original host name was \"live-sopris-venture-capital\", which suggests it was ([wp-json](https://sopriscapitalgrowth.com/wp-json/wp/v2/pages?per_page=50)).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval and https://www.valorep.com/team/jon-koval?format=json\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2033590/000101297526000109/primary_doc.xml\n- https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Jon%20Koval%22 (0 hits; \"Jonathan Koval\" also 0)\n- https://sopriscapitalgrowth.com/team/, https://sopriscapital.com/\n- Deal releases linked in the Portfolio table\n", "person:jonah-cader": "\n# Jonah Cader\n\n## One-line take\nA new, technically trained Lightspeed growth partner in New York. Their only deal from the start is the $300M Ricursive Intelligence Series A, an AI-for-chip-design lab. Helion and Science Corp look like inherited coverage.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they could win.** Lightspeed's growth bench is mostly generalists. Cader brings a rare stack for it:\n  - Stanford M.S. in CS (AI specialization) and Stanford MBA\n  - a Brown ScB in computational neuroscience\n  - Google[x] industrial robotics, Stanford HAI, and a NeurIPS co-authorship\n  - McKinsey semiconductor and fiber-infrastructure clients\n  - Valor Equity Partners Principal covering AI, defense and deep tech\n\n  ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/jonah-cader/)) That profile fits Lightspeed's frontier-hardware growth deals: chip design, fusion, BCI.\n- **What constrains them.** They are a lateral partner hire, so promotion speed can't be measured. They also appear to be very recent. The headshot sits in the `/uploads/2026/01/` folder ([img](https://lsvp.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/jonah-cader-1-1024x1024.webp)), and their first byline is dated 2026-01-26 ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-ricursive-intelligence-ai-for-chip-design-and-chip-design-for-ai/)). That points to arriving around Jan 2026, but this is an inference, not a published date. On every deal they sit with Ravi Mhatre, Lightspeed's co-founder, so their independent authority is unproven.\n- **Never first in.**\n  - Ricursive: Sequoia led the $35M seed before Lightspeed led the A ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/16/how-ricursive-intelligence-raised-335m-at-a-4b-valuation-in-4-months/)).\n  - Science Corp: Lightspeed is a \"pre-existing\" investor in the C ([src](https://science.xyz/news/series-c)).\n  - Helion: the Jan 2025 Series F memo does not name them ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-helion-abundant-intelligence-requires-abundant-energy/)).\n- **Only one of the three is AI.** Of the three named deals, only Ricursive is AI in the owner's sense. Helion is fusion, which Lightspeed frames as energy for AI. Science Corp is a clinical-stage BCI and medtech company.\n\n## Differential insight\nSources were thin. No podcasts, talks or posts were found (X returned HTTP 402, and the web-search budget for this run was exhausted). Everything below comes from firm and company pages, read closely.\n\n- **Probably post-Helion, Jan 2026 arrival (inference).**\n  - The Helion Series F memo (2025-01-28) was written by Raviraj Jain and Ravi Mhatre only ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-helion-abundant-intelligence-requires-abundant-energy/)).\n  - Cader's headshot sits in a 2026/01 upload path, and their first memo is 2026-01-26.\n  - No partner-announcement post exists in the Lightspeed stories list ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/)). A quiet hire.\n  - So Helion (Series F, 2025, per [lsvp.com/company/helion](https://lsvp.com/company/helion/)) is board or coverage work handed over, not a deal they sourced.\n- **Ricursive is their deal of record.** They co-authored the lead memo with Guru Chahal and Ravi Mhatre: \"That’s why we’re thrilled to be leading Ricursive’s Series A.\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-ricursive-intelligence-ai-for-chip-design-and-chip-design-for-ai/), 2026-01-26).\n  - The memo's bet is on the team: \"This is the team that blazed the trail of AI-enabled chip design.\" That points to AlphaChip, used on four generations of Google's TPU.\n  - The thesis is democratization. Chip design today \"limits the benefits of custom silicon to only those companies\" with large design teams.\n- **Timing on Ricursive.**\n  - Sequoia led a $35M seed.\n  - Lightspeed led the $300M A at $4B post on 2026-01-26, about four months after launch, for $335M total ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/16/how-ricursive-intelligence-raised-335m-at-a-4b-valuation-in-4-months/), [AI Journal](https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/)).\n  - The A syndicate: DST Global, NVentures, Felicis, 49 Palms Ventures, Radical AI and Sequoia.\n  - Only Guru Chahal is quoted in the press release. Cader is not.\n- **Science Corp Series C** ($230M, 2026-03-05). Lightspeed is listed among the \"pre-existing\" investors, alongside Khosla, YC, IQT and Quiet Capital. No lead is named, and no Lightspeed individual is named ([src](https://science.xyz/news/series-c)). Lightspeed's page puts Cader on the deal with Ravi Mhatre and Venture Partner Paul Murphy ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/science-corporation/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:**\n  - Ricursive is squarely in the owner's sector: an AI-chips frontier lab with Google AlphaChip founders, Anna Goldie and Azalia Mirhoseini.\n  - Cader is a technically literate growth partner at a firm that leads $300M A rounds into new labs. That makes them a natural follow-on or co-investor after a seed you lead.\n  - They are new and in New York, so likely under-networked with seed managers. That makes them reachable now.\n- **Against:**\n  - They are growth-only. By the THESIS rubric, growth would normally mean low.\n  - They were never first in.\n  - Two of their three named deals are not AI (fusion, BCI medtech).\n  - They hold no seed-stage authority.\n- **Calibration:** I chose medium over low by following the abby-meyers precedent: growth partner, real compute exposure.\n\n## Background\n- ScB in Computational Neuroscience, Brown. Co-authored undergraduate research at NeurIPS ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/jonah-cader/)).\n- Stanford HAI; Google[x] (industrial robotics) ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/jonah-cader/)). Dates not published.\n- McKinsey & Company, strategy consultant to Engagement Manager, serving semiconductor, fiber-infrastructure and big tech clients ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/jonah-cader/)). Dates not published.\n- Stanford MBA and M.S. in CS (AI specialization) ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/jonah-cader/)). Dates not published.\n- Principal, Valor Equity Partners, focused on AI, defense and deep tech ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/jonah-cader/)). Dates not published. They are not on the current Valor team page ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team)), which is expected after a departure and proves nothing more.\n- Partner, Lightspeed Venture Partners, New York. Join date not published; about Jan 2026 is inferred (see Crux).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://lsvp.com/team-member/jonah-cader/ | Lightspeed team page; email jonah@lsvp.com listed |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | One Lightspeed memo (Ricursive, co-author) |\n| X | https://x.com/jonahcader | Linked from the Lightspeed page; fetch returned 402, so follower count and posts unverified (2026-11-10) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/jonah-cader/ | Linked from the Lightspeed page; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was exhausted this run, so this is a gap, not proof of absence.\n\n## How they think\n- **AI-hardware recursion** (from the co-authored Ricursive memo): \"Ricursive is building a recursive loop between hardware and software\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-ricursive-intelligence-ai-for-chip-design-and-chip-design-for-ai/)).\n- **Team-first underwriting at growth prices.** They backed a company about four months old at $4B post, on the founders' AlphaChip production record.\n- **Democratizing custom silicon** as a market-expansion thesis.\n- Contrarian views and calls: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Investing in Ricursive Intelligence: AI for Chip Design and Chip Design for AI](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-ricursive-intelligence-ai-for-chip-design-and-chip-design-for-ai/) (2026-01-26, with Chahal and Mhatre). Their only byline found.\n- [Lightspeed team page](https://lsvp.com/team-member/jonah-cader/). Background stack and focus.\n- [Investing in Helion](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-helion-abundant-intelligence-requires-abundant-energy/). Not theirs, but it is the firm's thesis for the board seat they now share.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X was unreachable (HTTP 402).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ricursive Intelligence | Series A ($300M; $4B post) | 2026-01-26 | Yes (Lightspeed led) | DST Global; NVentures; Felicis; 49 Palms; Radical AI; Sequoia (seed lead). Lightspeed team: Guru Chahal, Ravi Mhatre | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/ricursive-intelligence/), [AI Journal](https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/) |\n| Science Corporation | Series C ($230M) | 2026-03-05 | Not stated; Lightspeed is \"pre-existing\" | Khosla; Y Combinator; IQT; Quiet Capital. Lightspeed team: Ravi Mhatre, Paul Murphy | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/science-corporation/), [science.xyz](https://science.xyz/news/series-c) |\n| Helion | Series F ($425M) | 2025-01-28 | Not stated in memo | Sam Altman; SoftBank Vision Fund 2; Mithril; Capricorn. Lightspeed team: Ravi Mhatre, Raviraj Jain | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/helion/), [memo](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-helion-abundant-intelligence-requires-abundant-energy/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Ricursive Intelligence.**\n  - The company launched in late 2025.\n  - Sequoia led a $35M seed. Lightspeed then led the $300M A about four months after launch ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/16/how-ricursive-intelligence-raised-335m-at-a-4b-valuation-in-4-months/)).\n  - The memo was published the day of the announcement, so Cader worked on the deal right at the start of their Lightspeed tenure.\n  - Guru Chahal (Menlo Park) is the public face of the deal and the only partner quoted in press.\n  - Intro path and first contact: nothing found.\n- **Science Corp and Helion.** Both were existing Lightspeed positions. Cader's role is not described anywhere.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lightspeed Venture Partners | Partner, New York | ~2026 (inferred) | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/jonah-cader/) |\n| Valor Equity Partners | Former Principal (AI, defense, deep tech) | not published | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/jonah-cader/) |\n| McKinsey & Company | Former Engagement Manager | not published | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/jonah-cader/) |\n| Google[x] | Former, industrial robotics | not published | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/jonah-cader/) |\n| Stanford HAI | Former | not published | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/jonah-cader/) |\n| Guru Chahal | Co-author, Ricursive memo; co-deal team | 2026 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-ricursive-intelligence-ai-for-chip-design-and-chip-design-for-ai/) |\n| Ravi Mhatre | Co-deal team on Ricursive, Science and Helion | 2026 | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/ricursive-intelligence/) |\n| Raviraj Jain | Co-deal team, Helion | 2026 | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/helion/) |\n| Paul Murphy | Co-deal team, Science Corp | 2026 | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/science-corporation/) |\n| Anna Goldie, Azalia Mirhoseini | Portfolio founders (Ricursive) | 2026 | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/ricursive-intelligence/) |\n| Max Hodak | Portfolio founder (Science Corp) | 2026 | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/science-corporation/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact Lightspeed join date and Valor tenure (LinkedIn not fetched; no announcement found).\n- Which Valor deals did they work on? Valor's AI and deep-tech exposure is large, and whether they touched xAI or other labs is unconfirmed.\n- Do they hold board seats or observer roles at Ricursive, Science or Helion?\n- What do they post on X? Any podcasts since joining?\n- Do they source on their own from New York, or support the Menlo Park partners?\n\n## Sources\n- https://lsvp.com/team-member/jonah-cader/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/ricursive-intelligence/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/science-corporation/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/helion/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-ricursive-intelligence-ai-for-chip-design-and-chip-design-for-ai/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-helion-abundant-intelligence-requires-abundant-energy/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/\n- https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/16/how-ricursive-intelligence-raised-335m-at-a-4b-valuation-in-4-months/\n- https://science.xyz/news/series-c\n- https://www.valorep.com/team\n", "person:jonathan-heiliger": "\n# Jonathan Heiliger\n\n## One-line take\nHe is an infrastructure operator (GlobalCenter, Opsware, Facebook) who now runs a small, two-GP seed fund. The fund mostly backs DevOps, infra and SaaS, but he is also the named VVUS partner on OLIX, the London optical-compute chip company now valued at $3.3B. That makes him a quiet early link into AI compute that the press doesn't show.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** Founders building infrastructure trust him because he has run it at scale: Facebook's users grew from 35M to 800M+ on his watch ([8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1407739/000119312511305837/d254523dex991.htm)). He sells himself as a mentor before he sells capital. At LaunchDarkly he lost the Series A, kept helping the founders for a year, and was then invited to co-lead the B ([post](https://medium.com/vertexventures/behind-the-scenes-of-our-investment-in-launchdarkly-5718f1a52412)).\n- **What constrains him.** The fund is small: Fund I $150.95M, Fund II $150M, Fund III $170.1M sold against a $200M target ([Form Ds](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1870781/000187078122000001/primary_doc.xml)). The firm range is $500k-$10M. He gets past the size limit with SPVs he signs himself.\n- **What he'd never do.** He wouldn't take a deal on a market-size slide. The firm's stated bias is toward founders who lived the pain themselves ([LaunchDarkly post](https://medium.com/vertexventures/behind-the-scenes-of-our-investment-in-launchdarkly-5718f1a52412); [Rhee post](https://www.vvus.com/news/what-i-look-for-in-today-s-entrepreneurs/)). Nothing shows him writing growth-stage checks or leading large AI-lab rounds.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The OLIX link is real, though the round release doesn't show it.** VVUS's own portfolio lists OLIX (\"formerly Flux Computing\") with Heiliger as partner: \"OLIX (formerly Flux Computing) sees a more promising way forward: optical computing.\" ([vvus.com](https://www.vvus.com/portfolio/flux-computing/)). He became an OLIX director on 2025-06-24, alongside Saul Klein and about seven months before Hummingbird's $220M ([Companies House](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/15585099/officers)). OLIX's $312M Series B release names Hummingbird, Crane, Plural, Creandum, Phoenix Court, Transition, Fundomo, Arm, HRT and Reed Hastings, but not Vertex ([olix.com](https://olix.com/news/company-raises-series-b)). This settles the repo's earlier `needs-id` lead. Which VVUS vehicle invested, and how much, is unconfirmed.\n- **He signs the SPVs; Rhee signs the funds.** VVUS SPV 1 raised $38,088,605 from 12 investors (first sale 2020-10-30), and SPV 2 raised $47,250,000 from just 3 investors (2021-07-16). Heiliger signed both ([SPV 1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1828616/000182861620000002/primary_doc.xml), [SPV 2](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1872587/000187258721000001/primary_doc.xml)). SPV 3 ($4.66M) was signed by Sandeep Bhadra ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1874019/000187401921000001/primary_doc.xml)). The fund Form Ds are signed by In Sik Rhee. A three-LP $47M SPV is larger than any check from the $200M main fund. The SPV targets aren't disclosed; VGS (he \"extended\" that relationship in Dec 2020) and LaunchDarkly are plausible but unconfirmed.\n- **He is the control person on Fund I.** The Desktop Metal resale prospectus lists 427,717 shares held by Vertex Ventures US Fund I and says \"Jonathan Heiliger is the Manager of Vertex.\" ([424B3, 2022](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1754820/000110465922055351/tm2212816d6_s3.htm)).\n- **Losing a round, then winning the next.** On LaunchDarkly he wrote \"we didn’t win the right to invest in LaunchDarkly’s hotly contested Series A.\" He then spent a year introducing customers and recruits, co-led the 2017 Series B with Redpoint, and followed on in the $44M Bessemer-led Series C (2019) ([post](https://medium.com/vertexventures/behind-the-scenes-of-our-investment-in-launchdarkly-5718f1a52412), mirrored at [vvus.com/news](https://www.vvus.com/news/)). His first contact with Edith Harbaugh was at the Lobby Conference in 2016.\n- **He got Valtix through a referral.** He met Valtix CEO Vishal Jain \"in late 2017 through a mutual friend\", a technologist who vouched for Jain after knowing him for about 20 years ([post](https://medium.com/vertexventures/ushering-in-the-future-of-cloud-network-security-with-valtix-3a66262e9e42)). Cisco announced its intent to acquire Valtix in Feb 2023 ([vvus.com/news](https://www.vvus.com/news/)).\n- **The Opsware tie predates Vertex.** He was Opsware's \"first hire\" under founders Ben Horowitz, Marc Andreessen, In Sik Rhee and Tim Howes ([Heavybit Ep. 23](https://www.heavybit.com/library/podcasts/venture-confidential/ep-23-counseling-founders-with-jonathan-heiliger-of-vertex-ventures/)). His VVUS co-GP, Rhee, was one of his first bosses.\n- **A pre-Facebook startup.** In early 2007 he raised \"a little bit of seed capital from Sequoia and North Bridge\" for a CDN-capacity startup before joining Facebook that summer ([Heavybit](https://www.heavybit.com/library/podcasts/venture-confidential/ep-23-counseling-founders-with-jonathan-heiliger-of-vertex-ventures/)). His first company, ISI, which he started at 19, \"was backed by Mike Moritz of Sequoia\" (same source).\n- **On power.** \"The one person the founder cannot fire is their investor.\" On pattern-matching: \"the 101st time I was wrong, or time changes.\" ([Heavybit](https://www.heavybit.com/library/podcasts/venture-confidential/ep-23-counseling-founders-with-jonathan-heiliger-of-vertex-ventures/)).\n- **He stays on the board across rounds.** He is listed as Metaview director on every Form D from the $7.65M seed (2021) through a $60.0M raise (first sale 2026-07-24) ([2021](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1894764/000189476421000001/primary_doc.xml), [2026](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1894764/000189476426000002/primary_doc.xml)). Whether VVUS took pro rata in later rounds is not disclosed.\n- **Portfolio CEOs invest in his next deals.** LaunchDarkly CEO Edith Harbaugh was an angel in Elotl's seed, which he led ([vvus.com](https://www.vvus.com/news/elotl-raises-5-million-to-build-nodeless-kubernetes-and-simplify-cloud-infrastructure-for-developers/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Most of the book is DevOps, security and B2B SaaS, at $500k-$10M from a roughly $200M fund. That's far from $20M checks into $600M lab seeds. Two things lift him above low:\n1. **OLIX** (ai-chips, optical compute). He has been a director since June 2025, before the $220M and $312M rounds.\n2. **Smithery** (MCP registry and agent infra; acquired by Arcade 2026-08-05, [src](https://www.arcade.dev/blog/smithery-joins-arcade)). This shows he is active in agent infrastructure.\n\nHe is useful as an early-signal source on compute and infrastructure founders and as a co-investor who adds operating help. He is unlikely to be a large-round partner. The firm's LPs are undisclosed, so he is not an LP lead.\n\n## Background\n- **ISI (age 19), then GlobalCenter.** Founded ISI at 19, backed by Mike Moritz. ISI became part of GlobalCenter, where he was co-founder and CTO. GlobalCenter was sold to Frontier Communications, which was then bought by Global Crossing ([Heavybit](https://www.heavybit.com/library/podcasts/venture-confidential/ep-23-counseling-founders-with-jonathan-heiliger-of-vertex-ventures/); [8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1407739/000119312511305837/d254523dex991.htm)).\n- **Global Crossing.** VP of technology. He founded its corporate venture group in 1999 ([Heavybit](https://www.heavybit.com/library/podcasts/venture-confidential/ep-23-counseling-founders-with-jonathan-heiliger-of-vertex-ventures/)).\n- **Opsware.** First hire, then ran product and engineering for about three years; COO per his firm bio ([Heavybit](https://www.heavybit.com/library/podcasts/venture-confidential/ep-23-counseling-founders-with-jonathan-heiliger-of-vertex-ventures/); [VVUS](https://www.vvus.com/team/jonathan-heiliger/)). Exact dates are not in the sources.\n- **Walmart.com.** IT for the online store; dates not given (same source).\n- **Sequoia Capital.** Technology adviser for \"a couple of years\", before 2007 (same source).\n- **Early 2007.** A CDN startup seeded by Sequoia and North Bridge (same source).\n- **Facebook.** VP Technical Operations from summer 2007. The end date conflicts across sources: the DuPont Fabros 8-K says 2007-2011 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1407739/000119312511305837/d254523dex991.htm)); Heavybit's guest blurb says \"2007 until 2012\"; he says \"five years\". TechCrunch called him \"former VP of Technical Operations at Facebook\" by May 2012 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2012/05/31/diffbot-raises-2-million-seed-round-for-web-content-extraction-technology/)).\n- **Public boards.**\n  - Jive Software: director by its 2011 IPO ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1462633/000119312511335017/d211300ds1a.htm)); Form 4s run to 2015-05 ([EDGAR](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Jonathan%20Heiliger%22)).\n  - DuPont Fabros Technology: director from 2011-11-09 ([8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1407739/000119312511305837/d254523dex991.htm)).\n- **2012-2014.** Angel and director: Diffbot (2012), OneID (director, Form D 2012). Whether he was at North Bridge Venture Partners as a partner in this period is **unconfirmed**; web search was unavailable this pass.\n- **2015.** Co-founded VVUS with In Sik Rhee ([VVUS](https://www.vvus.com/team/jonathan-heiliger/)).\n- **Other roles.** Chairman of Webmonsters; on the Exploratorium board (same source).\n- Education: nothing found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.vvus.com/team/jonathan-heiliger/ | Firm bio, verified 200 on 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://medium.com/@heiligerj | Medium; linked from the vvus.com news index. Posts 2019-2022; nothing newer found |\n| X | https://twitter.com/heiligerj | Linked from his VVUS bio; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/jheiliger | Linked from his VVUS bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2020-11 — Heavybit Venture Confidential, Ep. 23 — \"Counseling Founders with Jonathan Heiliger\" — [link](https://www.heavybit.com/library/podcasts/venture-confidential/ep-23-counseling-founders-with-jonathan-heiliger-of-vertex-ventures/). Career history, the Opsware first hire, the founder-can't-fire-investor line, and roughly half the fund going to \"Nouveau infrastructure\".\n- 2021-03 — Vertex Intersect fireside with Armon Dadgar (HashiCorp) — [link](https://vertexventures.wistia.com/medias/82g0jqbtu8) (not reviewed).\n- 2022-02 — The Sure Shot Entrepreneur, ep. 84 — [link](https://podcast.sure.ventures/episodes/tsse84-investors-solve-problems-jonathan-vertex) (not reviewed).\n- 2022-03 — Startup Investing / Vitalize with Justin Gordon — [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/embed/je8VPNPvoB8) (listed on [vvus.com/news](https://www.vvus.com/news/); the podcast page now 404s; not reviewed).\n- 2022-09 — This Week in Enterprise Tech 512 — listed on [vvus.com/news](https://www.vvus.com/news/) (not reviewed).\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses.**\n  - \"Nouveau infrastructure\" for DevOps and SRE teams: feature flags (LaunchDarkly), service catalogs (OpsLevel), nodeless Kubernetes (Elotl), cloud network security (Valtix).\n  - SaaS that frees up frontline workers' time (Loris, Metaview).\n  - Company formation: he incubated SpaceIQ and says he works on companies \"brewing in the oven\" ([bio](https://www.vvus.com/team/jonathan-heiliger/)).\n- **Edge vs cloud.** His 2019 post \"Looking down from the clouds, I see the edge\" argued that edge computing would complement the cloud ([vvus.com/news](https://www.vvus.com/news/)).\n- **Contrarian views.** Experience can mislead (\"time changes\"). Founders should date investors before marrying them, because investors are the one party founders can't fire.\n- **Calls.**\n  - Right: LaunchDarkly (26 to 270 customers in about 15 months before the B, per his post), HUMAN, Valtix (to Cisco), Cloud Academy (to QA), SpaceIQ (to WeWork), Coolan (to Salesforce), Loris (to Contentsquare). Exits are per the [VVUS portfolio](https://www.vvus.com/portfolio/).\n  - Mixed: Desktop Metal (public via SPAC, then declined); outcome for VVUS undisclosed.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Behind the Scenes of our Investment in LaunchDarkly](https://medium.com/vertexventures/behind-the-scenes-of-our-investment-in-launchdarkly-5718f1a52412) (2019): a full account of losing a round, then winning the next.\n2. [Heavybit Venture Confidential Ep. 23](https://www.heavybit.com/library/podcasts/venture-confidential/ep-23-counseling-founders-with-jonathan-heiliger-of-vertex-ventures/) (2020): career history and how he thinks about founders.\n3. [Ushering in the future of cloud network security with Valtix](https://medium.com/vertexventures/ushering-in-the-future-of-cloud-network-security-with-valtix-3a66262e9e42) (2019): how he sources through referrals.\n4. [We have $200M reasons...](https://www.vvus.com/news/we-have-200m-reasons-to-be-excited-about-enterprise-founders/) (2022, by Bhadra): Fund III strategy, a small concentrated portfolio.\n5. [Partners and Investors: Why every startup needs both](https://heiligerj.medium.com/partners-and-investors-why-every-startup-needs-both-670456c4f639) (2022; Medium blocked fetching, not read).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed. X could not be fetched and web search was rate-limited. His Medium posting stops around 2022 on the vvus.com news index.\n\n## Portfolio\nVVUS attributes all of the following to Heiliger in its own portfolio data ([vvus.com/portfolio](https://www.vvus.com/portfolio/)). Rounds are filled only where a primary source gives them.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OLIX (ex-Flux Computing) | Seed (inferred; allotment 2025-04-17); director 2025-06-24 | 2025 | unknown | Phoenix Court / Saul Klein | [vvus](https://www.vvus.com/portfolio/flux-computing/), [CH](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/15585099/officers) |\n| Smithery | Undisclosed (acquired by Arcade 2026-08-05) | unknown | unknown | | [vvus](https://www.vvus.com/portfolio/smithery/), [Arcade](https://www.arcade.dev/blog/smithery-joins-arcade) |\n| Cakewalk | Undisclosed | unknown | unknown | | [vvus](https://www.vvus.com/portfolio/cakewalk/) |\n| Metaview | Seed ($6M) | 2021-12-01 | yes, board | Fly Ventures, Seedcamp, Village Global, PrimeSet, Moving Capital, Palumni VC | [vvus](https://www.vvus.com/news/reinventing-the-interview-experience-announcing-metaviews-dollar6m-seed-round/) |\n| Elotl | Seed ($5M) | 2021-11-02 | yes, board | Essence VC, HearstLab, Telescopic, XYZ, Dan Scheinman, Edith Harbaugh | [vvus](https://www.vvus.com/news/elotl-raises-5-million-to-build-nodeless-kubernetes-and-simplify-cloud-infrastructure-for-developers/) |\n| LaunchDarkly | Series B | 2017 | co-led with Redpoint | Redpoint | [post](https://medium.com/vertexventures/behind-the-scenes-of-our-investment-in-launchdarkly-5718f1a52412) |\n| LaunchDarkly | Series C ($44M) | 2019-03 | no (Bessemer led) | Bessemer | same |\n| Valtix | Undisclosed (part of $14M initial funding) | ~2018-03 | unknown | | [post](https://medium.com/vertexventures/ushering-in-the-future-of-cloud-network-security-with-valtix-3a66262e9e42) |\n| VGS | Undisclosed (follow-on, \"Extending our relationship\") | 2020-12 | unknown | | [vvus.com/news](https://www.vvus.com/news/) |\n| Loris | Undisclosed ($5.14M Form D; director) | 2019-11-27 | unknown | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1729180/000172918019000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| HUMAN Security (ex-PerimeterX) | Undisclosed; director of Singularity TopCo LLC (2024 Form Ds) | 2024 | unknown | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1944306/000194430624000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Desktop Metal | Undisclosed (Fund I held 427,717 shares in 2022) | unknown | unknown | | [424B3](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1754820/000110465922055351/tm2212816d6_s3.htm) |\n| OpsLevel, Higharc, Juno, DOR, Vintra, Simppler, Coolan, Cloud Academy, SpaceIQ | Undisclosed | | | | [vvus.com/portfolio](https://www.vvus.com/portfolio/) |\n| Diffbot (angel) | Seed ($2M) | 2012-05-31 | no | Sky Dayton, Andy Bechtolsheim, Joi Ito, Elad Gil, Matrix | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2012/05/31/diffbot-raises-2-million-seed-round-for-web-content-extraction-technology/) |\n| OneID (director) | Undisclosed ($7M Form D) | 2012-03-30 | unknown | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1525897/000152589712000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| ThousandEyes (angel) | Undisclosed | unknown | unknown | | [Heavybit](https://www.heavybit.com/library/podcasts/venture-confidential/ep-23-counseling-founders-with-jonathan-heiliger-of-vertex-ventures/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **LaunchDarkly.**\n  - 2016: met Edith Harbaugh at the Lobby Conference.\n  - Months later, a mutual friend got him in front of Harbaugh and John Kodumal, and he pitched himself as a mentor.\n  - Lost the Series A. Kept introducing customers and recruits.\n  - About a year later, Harbaugh invited him in. He co-led the 2017 Series B with Redpoint.\n  - 2019: followed on in Bessemer's $44M Series C.\n  - In that roughly 15-month stretch the company went from 26 to 270 customers ([post](https://medium.com/vertexventures/behind-the-scenes-of-our-investment-in-launchdarkly-5718f1a52412)).\n- **Valtix.** A trusted technologist introduced him to Vishal Jain in late 2017. Within days he met the other co-founders, and VVUS partnered about 15 months before June 2019. Cisco announced its intent to acquire in Feb 2023 ([post](https://medium.com/vertexventures/ushering-in-the-future-of-cloud-network-security-with-valtix-3a66262e9e42)).\n- **Metaview.** VVUS led the $6M seed. Heiliger took the board seat and Chase Roberts became an observer ([src](https://www.vvus.com/news/reinventing-the-interview-experience-announcing-metaviews-dollar6m-seed-round/)). He is still listed as director on the 2026 Form D ($60.0M).\n- **OLIX.** He became a director on 2025-06-24, the same day as Saul Klein ([CH](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/15585099/officers)). How he got in, which vehicle invested, and the check size are all unconfirmed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| In Sik Rhee | Co-GP at VVUS; Opsware co-founder who hired him as first employee | Opsware era (date not given); VVUS 2015 | [Heavybit](https://www.heavybit.com/library/podcasts/venture-confidential/ep-23-counseling-founders-with-jonathan-heiliger-of-vertex-ventures/) |\n| Ben Horowitz / Marc Andreessen | Opsware founders, his early bosses | Opsware era | same |\n| Mike Moritz | Backed his first company, ISI | his age 19 | same |\n| Sandeep Bhadra | VVUS partner, promoted to GP in 2022; co-named on Fund III Form D | 2017 | [vvus](https://www.vvus.com/news/we-have-200m-reasons-to-be-excited-about-enterprise-founders/) |\n| Chase Roberts | VVUS principal from 2019; Metaview board observer | 2019 | [vvus.com/news](https://www.vvus.com/news/) |\n| Edith Harbaugh | LaunchDarkly CEO; angel in his Elotl seed | 2016 | [post](https://medium.com/vertexventures/behind-the-scenes-of-our-investment-in-launchdarkly-5718f1a52412) |\n| Saul Klein | Co-director at OLIX | 2025-06-24 | [CH](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/15585099/officers) |\n| Lammot J. du Pont | DFT chairman who knew him through Facebook | 2011 | [8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1407739/000119312511305837/d254523dex991.htm) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **OLIX.** Which VVUS vehicle invested (Fund III or an SPV)? What check, and in which round? Did VVUS put money into the $220M or $312M rounds?\n- **SPVs.** What were SPV 1 ($38.1M, 2020) and SPV 2 ($47.25M, three LPs, 2021) for?\n- **Fund IV.** No Form D for a Fund IV appears on EDGAR (company search \"Vertex Ventures US\" and \"VVUS\"; last is Fund III, 2021-2022). Has VVUS raised since? The current team page shows only Rhee and Heiliger as investors. Sandeep Bhadra (GP 2022), Megan Reynolds and Noah Carr (announced 2024-09) no longer appear.\n- **LPs.** Who are the LPs? Vertex Holdings / Temasek is the obvious guess, but nothing in EDGAR or on the firm site confirms it. Fund I's first Form D showed 3 investors and $107.5M sold.\n- **2012-2014.** Was he a partner at North Bridge Venture Partners?\n- **Facebook end date.** 2011 or 2012?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.vvus.com/team/jonathan-heiliger/\n- https://www.vvus.com/\n- https://www.vvus.com/portfolio/\n- https://www.vvus.com/portfolio/flux-computing/\n- https://www.vvus.com/news/\n- https://www.heavybit.com/library/podcasts/venture-confidential/ep-23-counseling-founders-with-jonathan-heiliger-of-vertex-ventures/\n- https://medium.com/vertexventures/behind-the-scenes-of-our-investment-in-launchdarkly-5718f1a52412 (text read from the vvus.com mirror)\n- https://medium.com/vertexventures/ushering-in-the-future-of-cloud-network-security-with-valtix-3a66262e9e42 (same)\n- https://www.vvus.com/news/reinventing-the-interview-experience-announcing-metaviews-dollar6m-seed-round/\n- https://www.vvus.com/news/elotl-raises-5-million-to-build-nodeless-kubernetes-and-simplify-cloud-infrastructure-for-developers/\n- https://www.vvus.com/news/we-have-200m-reasons-to-be-excited-about-enterprise-founders/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1407739/000119312511305837/d254523dex991.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1754820/000110465922055351/tm2212816d6_s3.htm\n- SEC Form Ds: VVUS Fund I/II/III, Collaborators II/III, SPV 1/2/3, Metaview (2021, 2024, 2025, 2026), Loris, Singularity TopCo, OneID, Jive (links inline)\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/15585099/officers\n- https://olix.com/news/company-raises-series-b\n- https://www.arcade.dev/blog/smithery-joins-arcade\n- https://techcrunch.com/2012/05/31/diffbot-raises-2-million-seed-round-for-web-content-extraction-technology/\n- Web search was rate-limited this pass; no aggregator was used.\n", "person:jordan-jacobs": "\n# Jordan Jacobs\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the person to watch for **pre-incorporation access to star researchers**. He worked with Cohere's founders for six months before the company existed and incubated World Labs with Fei-Fei Li for a year before it was incorporated (it then sold to AMD for $8.2B). He also co-led Jeff Dean's Discovery Loop seed, and since September 2026 he has a growth fund of more than $1B to keep following these companies. [src](https://thelogic.co/news/the-interview/radicals-jordan-jacobs-on-investing-in-ai-before-its-breakout-moment-and-after/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/amd-acquires-world-labs/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He gets in first through relationships that predate the company. That covers 3 of his 5 clearly personal deals: BenchSci (a personal angel check in 2016, before Fund I), Cohere (about six months of work before incorporation) and World Labs (weekly incubation for a year). He co-founded the Vector Institute with Hinton, so Toronto's leading researchers (Hinton, Li, Urtasun, Fidler) are his LPs, angels and, eventually, founders. [src](https://www.investpsp.com/en/news/radical-ventures-launches-usd-350-million-vc-fund-focused-on-artificial-intelligence)\n- **His pitch to founders is technical depth, and he says it lets him pay less:** founders \"will take our money even at a discount compared to the top funds\" (2023). [src](https://thelogic.co/news/the-interview/radicals-jordan-jacobs-on-investing-in-ai-before-its-breakout-moment-and-after/)\n- **What constrains him:** fund size relative to his own winners. In 2023 he said of the $550M fund, \"At some point we get tapped out.\" It took three years to fix that with Breakouts (more than $1B at first close, 2026-09). His LP base is mostly Canadian pensions and banks. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/)\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** back a non-technical generalist founder or a non-AI deal. He predicted \"tons of money lost\" by generalists in the AI boom.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He named the fund-size problem in 2023 and built the fix in 2026.** \"It doesn't work in a $550-million fund. At some point we get tapped out.\" [src](https://thelogic.co/news/the-interview/radicals-jordan-jacobs-on-investing-in-ai-before-its-breakout-moment-and-after/) (The Logic, 2023-07-14). The Breakouts launch three years later uses the same framing: Radical could not \"back companies through later rounds of hyper-growth\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/).\n- **Cohere was incubated before incorporation, not only World Labs.** Jacobs: \"worked with them for six months before they even incorporated the company.\" The firm's public story credits only World Labs with pre-formation work. [src](https://thelogic.co/news/the-interview/radicals-jordan-jacobs-on-investing-in-ai-before-its-breakout-moment-and-after/)\n- **The Transformer paper decided Radical's founding thesis:** \"That paper helped put our decision over the top\". The firm's LLM thesis dates to late 2018 or early 2019, before Fund II launched. [src](https://thelogic.co/news/the-interview/radicals-jordan-jacobs-on-investing-in-ai-before-its-breakout-moment-and-after/)\n- **He claims a price advantage:** founders take Radical's money \"even at a discount compared to the top funds\". The word \"founders\" is the article's bracketed insertion, so read it as paraphrase-plus-quote. [src](https://thelogic.co/news/the-interview/radicals-jordan-jacobs-on-investing-in-ai-before-its-breakout-moment-and-after/)\n- **He claims a high hit rate:** \"maybe it's 35 or 40 per cent that have huge upside\", against a normal 10–20%. He gives this as the reason reserves ran out. [src](https://thelogic.co/news/the-interview/radicals-jordan-jacobs-on-investing-in-ai-before-its-breakout-moment-and-after/)\n- **His ownership math is a worked example, not a disclosed stake:** \"If a company ends up being a $10-billion company, we own 20 per cent of it\" (shortened by the fetcher) is hypothetical. Do not read it as Radical's Cohere stake.\n- **Selectivity:** \"Radical has looked at over 8400 start-ups and invested in 44.\" This is Dominic Barton reporting from the 2024 AGM, not Jacobs directly. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/from-pioneer-to-leader-insights-from-radical-ventures-agm/)\n- **World Labs incubation:** \"We scheduled at least weekly meetings to incubate the concept\", starting a year before incorporation and before the co-founders joined. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/amd-acquires-world-labs/)\n- **He sources at the stage, then invests:** he moderated the Hinton–Dean fireside at NeurIPS 2025 (2025-12). About eight months later he co-led Dean's Discovery Loop seed, and the founders say they \"selected Radical Ventures and Khosla Ventures to lead\". [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-collaboration-that-built-modern-ai/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-reads-jeff-dean-on-launching-discovery-loop/)\n- **He is pro open-weight:** open models let organisations \"own their intelligence rather than rent it\" (2026-09-08). [src](https://radical.vc/articles/a-world-model-for-spatial-intelligence/)\n- **The founders seeded Fund I with their own angel book:** \"first invested in the company in 2016 as very early angels\" (BenchSci, with Poutanen). [src](https://radical.vc/articles/key-questions-ai-founders-must-ask-themselves/)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is the Radical partner on new labs at the founding stage (Cohere, World Labs, Discovery Loop) and on world models (Decart), which matches your seed-into-new-labs lens. Discovery Loop and the Radical/Khosla pairing (also on Veeda) are the co-invest patterns to watch. Breakouts means he can now fund the very large rounds where your $20M check would sit beside his, so he is a natural co-investor rather than a competitor for ownership. As an LP route he is low fit: his LP base is Canadian pensions and banks.\n\n## Background\n- Early career: technology and entertainment lawyer; JD from Osgoode Hall Law School (the team page says he was admitted at 19). No dates given. [src](https://radical.vc/team/jordan-jacobs/)\n- Founded SpyBox Media (date not found). [src](https://radical.vc/team/jordan-jacobs/)\n- 2011: co-founded Milq with Tomi Poutanen and Don MacKinnon. It later became Hark Audio. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/ai-founders-master-class/)\n- 2016: spun Layer 6 AI out of Milq and served as co-CEO. TD Bank Group acquired it in January 2018, about 15 months after launch. [src](https://www.investpsp.com/en/news/radical-ventures-launches-usd-350-million-vc-fund-focused-on-artificial-intelligence)\n- 2017: co-founded the Vector Institute with Poutanen, Hinton, UofT ML professors and Ed Clark (same source).\n- 2017-06: co-founded Radical with Poutanen as a small seed fund. 2018-01 to 2019-05: SVP and Chief AI Officer at TD. 2019-05: left TD and raised the US$350M Fund II. [src](https://thelogic.co/news/the-interview/radicals-jordan-jacobs-on-investing-in-ai-before-its-breakout-moment-and-after/)\n- Boards (non-portfolio): Director of CIFAR and of Tennis Canada; member of the University of Waterloo President's International Advisory Board. [src](https://radical.vc/team/jordan-jacobs/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/jordan-jacobs/ | Loads (2026-11-10). Team bio |\n| Substack / blog | https://radical.vc/?s=Jordan+Jacobs | Occasional Radical Reads bylines (Breakouts, Atlas, World Labs/AMD, Discovery Loop) |\n| X | none found | The X link on his team page is the firm account (@radicalvcfund), not personal |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/jordanjacobs1/ | Linked from team page; returned HTTP 999 (bot block), not verified directly |\n| GitHub | none found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://radical.vc/articles/the-collaboration-that-built-modern-ai/ | Moderates Radical Talks / Masterclass sessions (Hinton, Dean, Gomez) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-12 — Radical Talks / NeurIPS 2025 — \"The Collaboration That Built Modern AI\" (Hinton and Dean) — https://radical.vc/articles/the-collaboration-that-built-modern-ai/ — he moderated; the page has no quotes from him. Video: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ue9MWfvMylE\n- 2024-10 — Radical Talks — Geoffrey Hinton on his Nobel Prize — https://radical.vc/articles/exclusive-geoffrey-hinton-on-his-nobel-prize/ — moderator.\n- 2023-07 — The Logic, \"The Interview\" (Murad Hemmadi) — https://thelogic.co/news/the-interview/radicals-jordan-jacobs-on-investing-in-ai-before-its-breakout-moment-and-after/ — his most candid source: fund-size limits, the Cohere pre-incorporation work, price discount, hit rate.\n- 2022-11 — AI Founders Masterclass — interview with Aidan Gomez — https://radical.vc/articles/aidan-gomez-on-building-an-ai-business/\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** \"AI will replace software and unlock groundbreaking scientific discoveries\" (the founding thesis, restated at the 2024 AGM). Discovery Loop is, in his words, \"a direct expression of that belief\". [src](https://radical.vc/articles/our-investment-in-discovery-loop/)\n- **Founders:** \"We like to invest in founders who are leading the field technically\". He defends academics as founders.\n- **Contrarian views:** in 2023 he predicted large losses by generalist AI investors; in 2026 he argued for open-weight models.\n- **Calls he got right:** LLMs in 2018–19 (Cohere), spatial intelligence (World Labs, an $8.2B exit to AMD within about 2.5 years of its first round), and the need for a growth vehicle.\n- **Calls he got wrong:** none admitted in any source read.\n\n## Read these first\n1. The Logic interview (2023-07-14): fund size, Cohere's origin, pricing. https://thelogic.co/news/the-interview/radicals-jordan-jacobs-on-investing-in-ai-before-its-breakout-moment-and-after/\n2. \"AMD Acquires World Labs\" (2026-10-05): the incubation playbook. https://radical.vc/articles/amd-acquires-world-labs/\n3. \"Introducing Radical Breakouts Fund\" (2026-09-21): why a growth fund now. https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/\n4. \"Our Investment in Discovery Loop\" (2026-08-05, with Rosenberg). https://radical.vc/articles/our-investment-in-discovery-loop/\n\n## Social engagement\nNo personal X account was found. He is visible through Radical bylines and as the moderator who puts Hinton, Li, Dean and Gomez on stage. Fei-Fei Li amplifies him (\"I have known Jordan and the team at Radical for years\", 2023-02-13) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/fei-fei-li-why-i-joined-radical-ventures/).\n\n## Portfolio\nThe row list is in `data/rows/jordan-jacobs-deals.csv` (10 rows, 7 companies; the Cohere Series D and Waabi lines below are firm-level only and are kept out of his rows). The \"Basis\" column says why each deal is his. **No source read gives him a board seat at any portfolio company.**\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| BenchSci | Undisclosed (personal angel) | 2016 | no | Tomi Poutanen | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/key-questions-ai-founders-must-ask-themselves/) |\n| Cohere | Seed (founding) | 2019 | yes, first in | — | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/from-pioneer-to-leader-insights-from-radical-ventures-agm/) |\n| Cohere | Series A ($40M) | 2021-09 | no (Index led) | Index, Section 32, Hinton, Li | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/cohere-raises-40-million-series-a-financing-to-make-nlp-safe-and-accessible-to-any-business/) |\n| Cohere | Series D ($500M at $6.8B) | 2025-08 | co-led (firm-level; Jacobs not named) | Inovia, AMD, NVIDIA, PSP | [src](https://www.torys.com/en/work/2025/08/4e762eb5-37a5-4e34-8245-c611c4f323e1) |\n| World Labs | Seed (pre-launch) | 2024-04 | no, but incubated it | a16z, NEA | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/amd-acquires-world-labs/) |\n| World Labs | Series A ($230M combined) | 2024-09 | co-led | a16z, NEA, Hinton, Karpathy | [src](https://thelogic.co/briefing/ai-pioneer-fei-fi-leis-world-labs-raises-us230m-co-led-by-radical-ventures/) |\n| Decart | Growth ($300M) | 2026-05 | yes; not first in | Sequoia, Benchmark, Zeev, NVIDIA | [src](https://www.decart.ai/publications/decart-raises-300m-tech-leaders-back-the-company-as-both-customers-and-investors) |\n| Discovery Loop | Seed | 2026-08 | co-led with Khosla | Lightspeed, Kleiner, Doerr, Alphabet | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/our-investment-in-discovery-loop/) |\n| Discovery Loop | Seed (Breakouts) | 2026-09 | no | — | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/) |\n| Etched | Growth (Breakouts) | 2026-09 | no; named in his post only | Stripes, Jane Street | same |\n| Crusoe | Series F (Breakouts) | 2026-09 | no; named in his post only | Atreides, Mubadala, Valor | same |\n| Waabi | Series A ($83.5M) | 2021-06 | no (Khosla led); weak attribution | Khosla, Uber, Hinton, Li | [src](https://www.torys.com/work/2021/06/0a25068b-2fb6-45a0-91c3-53a62a7a6e3f) |\n\n**Timing pattern:** He came first and doubled down on Cohere (seed, A, D) and World Labs (incubation, seed, A), and backed Discovery Loop twice within seven weeks. Decart is the exception, a late growth lead after Sequoia, Benchmark and Zeev.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Cohere (2019):** Radical's LLM thesis formed in late 2018 or early 2019, tipped by the Transformer paper. It weighed the gap in serving customers who were not on Microsoft's cloud or who ran on-premises, worked with Gomez's team for about six months before incorporation, then led the seed. Radical re-upped in the Index-led Series A in 2021 and co-led the $500M Series D in 2025.\n- **World Labs (2023–2026):** Li joined Radical as Scientific Partner in early 2023. She and Jacobs had discussed a spatial-intelligence company \"years before\" it was formed, and they then met weekly for a year before incorporation. Radical came in before launch, co-led the $230M round in 2024, and saw AMD buy the company for $8.2B in October 2026. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/amd-acquires-world-labs/)\n- **Discovery Loop (2026):** Dean's team pitched Radical \"a few weeks back\" before the August announcement and picked Radical and Khosla to co-lead. Jacobs had moderated Dean at NeurIPS eight months earlier. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-reads-jeff-dean-on-launching-discovery-loop/)\n- **Decart (2026):** Radical's first entry, and a lead of $300M. Jacobs supplied the quote: \"Decart is the engine powering the future of real-time media and physical AI.\" [src](https://www.decart.ai/publications/decart-raises-300m-tech-leaders-back-the-company-as-both-customers-and-investors)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Tomi Poutanen | Co-founder of Milq, Layer 6, Vector and Radical | 2011 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/ai-founders-master-class/) |\n| Fei-Fei Li | Co-incubated World Labs; Radical Scientific Partner and LP | 2023-02 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/fei-fei-li-why-i-joined-radical-ventures/) |\n| Geoffrey Hinton | Vector co-founder; Radical LP (press); interviewed by him | 2017 | [src](https://www.investpsp.com/en/news/radical-ventures-launches-usd-350-million-vc-fund-focused-on-artificial-intelligence) |\n| Jeff Dean | Moderated him in 2025-12; co-led his seed in 2026-08 | 2025-12 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-collaboration-that-built-modern-ai/) |\n| Aidan Gomez | Founding investor in Cohere | 2019 | [src](https://thelogic.co/news/the-interview/radicals-jordan-jacobs-on-investing-in-ai-before-its-breakout-moment-and-after/) |\n| Khosla Ventures | Co-lead on Discovery Loop (and, at firm level, Veeda and Waabi) | 2026-08 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-reads-jeff-dean-on-launching-discovery-loop/) |\n| Aaron Rosenberg | Co-author and co-lead on Discovery Loop | 2026-08 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/our-investment-in-discovery-loop/) |\n| Ed Clark | Vector co-founder; ex-TD CEO; Radical partner | 2017 | [src](https://www.investpsp.com/en/news/radical-ventures-launches-usd-350-million-vc-fund-focused-on-artificial-intelligence) |\n| TD Bank Group | Bought Layer 6, employed him as Chief AI Officer, now a Radical LP | 2018-01 | same |\n| Inovia Capital | Firm-level co-lead on Cohere's Series D | 2025-08 | [src](https://www.torys.com/en/work/2025/08/4e762eb5-37a5-4e34-8245-c611c4f323e1) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Board seats:** No source read puts him on any portfolio board. Cohere's named board additions are Volpi (Index) and Pichette (Inovia). Is he on Cohere's or World Labs' board? Web search was exhausted this run, so this could not be checked.\n- Who at Radical actually ran the Waabi, Etched and Crusoe deals? His only link to them is that his posts name them.\n- What happened to Benji Sucher (a named 2019 co-founder and Fund II GP)?\n- How large is Radical's stake in Cohere? Dealroom (aggregator) estimates 12.5%.\n- Is he an LP in any other fund? Nothing found (no `-lp.csv` row file written).\n- Was Decart's 2026 \"acquisition\" (Dealroom) real?\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/jordan-jacobs/ (bio, boards; fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://thelogic.co/news/the-interview/radicals-jordan-jacobs-on-investing-in-ai-before-its-breakout-moment-and-after/ (2023-07-14)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/amd-acquires-world-labs/ (2026-10-05)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/ (2026-09-21)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/a-world-model-for-spatial-intelligence/ (2026-09-08)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/our-investment-in-discovery-loop/ (2026-08-05); https://radical.vc/articles/radical-reads-jeff-dean-on-launching-discovery-loop/ (2026-08-09)\n- https://www.decart.ai/publications/decart-raises-300m-tech-leaders-back-the-company-as-both-customers-and-investors (2026-05-18)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/cohere-raises-40-million-series-a-financing-to-make-nlp-safe-and-accessible-to-any-business/ (2021-09-13)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/from-pioneer-to-leader-insights-from-radical-ventures-agm/ (2024-06-18)\n- https://www.investpsp.com/en/news/radical-ventures-launches-usd-350-million-vc-fund-focused-on-artificial-intelligence (2019-05-07)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/ai-founders-master-class/; https://radical.vc/articles/fei-fei-li-why-i-joined-radical-ventures/; https://radical.vc/articles/the-collaboration-that-built-modern-ai/; https://radical.vc/articles/exclusive-geoffrey-hinton-on-his-nobel-prize/; https://radical.vc/articles/aidan-gomez-on-building-an-ai-business/\n- https://www.torys.com/en/work/2025/08/4e762eb5-37a5-4e34-8245-c611c4f323e1; https://thelogic.co/briefing/ai-pioneer-fei-fi-leis-world-labs-raises-us230m-co-led-by-radical-ventures/; https://betakit.com/former-nvidia-lab-leader-sanja-fidler-launches-veeda-ai-to-tackle-world-models/\n- Secondary: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cohere; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vector_Institute_(Canada)\n", "person:jordan-segall": "\n# Jordan Segall\n\n## One-line take\nHe is a fast-promoted Redpoint early-stage partner (Principal in Jan 2023, Partner by Feb 2024). He sits on three deals that matter to this lens: the week-old Mistral seed, the Serval Series A and the Irregular frontier-security lab. He says openly that he now pays up for winners rather than haggling on price.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:**\n  - He is a former engineer and forward-deployed engineer (Palantir, C3.ai pre-sales) who chases founders hard in person. Redpoint flew to Paris for Mistral and to Berlin on two hours' notice for another deal.\n  - He invests from a written thesis: Redpoint drafted an ITSM memo in June 2024 and hunted for a team for three years before backing Serval.\n  - He brings Redpoint's open-source playbook (HashiCorp, ClickHouse, CockroachDB) to founders. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY) [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-agents-for-it-service-management-our-investment-in-serval---/)\n- **What constrains him:**\n  - Fund math. He says a company has to be able to reach $5B or more to return the $650M early fund, so Redpoint passes on good companies that would sell for a few hundred million. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY)\n  - He is still a partner, not a managing director. On frontier deals he works alongside MDs (Satish Dharmaraj on Mistral; Chase and Bard on Serval; Raney and Senthilkumar on Irregular).\n- **What he would never do:**\n  - Back a frontier-lab spinout on pedigree alone. He says Redpoint has \"passed on many\" OpenAI alumni and asks what experience is unique to the founder. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY)\n  - Force a founder to hold out against a life-changing sale. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY)\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Mistral timing.** Redpoint invested when Mistral was about a week old: \"they literally started company like a week ago\". The team flew to Paris to win the deal: \"we actually flew out to Paris to see them\". [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY) (2024-02-17, 40:25 and 42:10)\n- **Passes on lab alumni.** \"passed on many open AI employees that have left\". He looks for experience unique to the founder, such as being the person who shipped GPT-3 fine-tuning, and asks whether the labs will commoditize the product. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY) (32:05)\n- **Fund-size bar.** He says a company must be \"like a $5 billion dollar plus company\" to return Redpoint's $650M fund. He adds that many companies Redpoint passes on \"are actually incredible Angel Investments\". [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY) (27:43, 35:51)\n- **Redpoint fund sizes (2025).** \"We have a $650 million early stage fund, $750 million growth fund.\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPDvm8UlHw0) (2025-05-08, 8:59)\n- **Price discipline, changed mind.** \"being too price sensitive doesn't work\". When he is convinced a company is the winner in a large market, he now thinks paying up is worth it. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPDvm8UlHw0) (28:40)\n- **Berlin deal.** A rival firm flew to the founder and hired a private chef in London. \"we ended up getting on a plane two hours later to Berlin\", and Redpoint won. The company is unnamed. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPDvm8UlHw0) (19:44)\n- **Term-sheet poems.** Before ChatGPT, \"Redpoint would commission me to create poems for founders\" who received term sheets. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPDvm8UlHw0) (20:09)\n- **Railway, both rounds.** \"I led the Seed round and the Series A round of Railway\". He is on the board. [src](https://nextplayso.substack.com/p/recommendations-from-redpoint-partner) (2025-12-15)\n- **Serval was thesis-first.** \"That line comes from a memo we wrote in June 2024\". Redpoint \"even explored incubating one ourselves\" before finding Serval. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-agents-for-it-service-management-our-investment-in-serval---/) (2025-10-31)\n- **Redpoint's model-layer bets (Feb 2024).** He named Mistral and Poolside at the model layer and Modal at inference: \"we've invested in a bunch of the foundation model layer\". [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY) (38:06)\n- **Peer co-investing.** \"James and I just did a deal together\", referring to James Green of CRV. He is part of a small peer circle with Morgan Gerlak (TCV). [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPDvm8UlHw0) (5:21)\n- **Seeds before product.** \"2 of the last 3 open source seeds we invested in\" that year were pre-product. He says \"I have led many seeds that have yet to release one!\" [src](https://jordansegall.substack.com/p/so-how-many-stars-is-enough-a-data) (2023-02-01)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.**\n- **Frontier labs:** Mistral AI seed in 2023 (with Satish Dharmaraj).\n- **AI compute:** Mithril (formerly Foundry) Series A, a GPU cloud.\n- **Frontier AI security:** Irregular, $80M round, with Scott Raney and Sai Senthilkumar. Irregular's wedge is its access to the labs.\n- **AI-native automation:** Serval Series A, the owner's named example.\n- **Pricing:** his stated change of mind (\"being too price sensitive doesn't work\") matches the owner's relaxed view on ownership.\n- **Caveats:** he is a partner rather than an MD. Much of his book is devtools and security (DragonflyDB, Restate, Vega, Push, Fable, imper.ai, JetStream) rather than labs.\n\n## Background\n- **Stanford:** studied computer science with an AI concentration. He was the first mentorship director at StartX, interviewing mentors (including many VCs), which is where his interest in venture started. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY) (0:49–1:37). An MS in Management Science & Engineering is per the GHP #92 show notes. [src](https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/generation-h/episodes/GHP-92---VC-14---Backing-Founders-at-the-Earliest-Stages-of-the-Climb-with-Jordan-Segall-e1ts98s)\n- **Four startups, at every stage:**\n  - Call9 (YC): first engineer.\n  - RelateIQ: acquired by Salesforce, a Redpoint company.\n  - Palantir: forward-deployed engineer.\n  - C3.ai: led pre-sales data-science teams before the IPO. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY) (2:52)\n- **McKinsey:** \"I woke up one day and was like, I don't like coding\". He then spent a stint at McKinsey learning the business side. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPDvm8UlHw0) (1:52)\n- **Unusual Ventures:** his first venture job, which he joined after C3.ai. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY) (3:18)\n  - How he got in: a C3.ai friend answered a Craigslist EA ad, and the recruiter, who placed EAs at VC firms, introduced him to Unusual. [src](https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/generation-h/episodes/GHP-92---VC-14---Backing-Founders-at-the-Earliest-Stages-of-the-Climb-with-Jordan-Segall-e1ts98s) (7:01–7:33, machine transcription)\n  - Why Redpoint: he had known some of its team for 10+ years, since Stanford. [src](https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/generation-h/episodes/GHP-92---VC-14---Backing-Founders-at-the-Earliest-Stages-of-the-Climb-with-Jordan-Segall-e1ts98s) (7:44)\n- **Redpoint, 2021–present:**\n  - Joined in 2021 (firm bio). [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/jordan-segall/)\n  - Listed as Principal in Jan 2023. [src](https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/generation-h/episodes/GHP-92---VC-14---Backing-Founders-at-the-Earliest-Stages-of-the-Climb-with-Jordan-Segall-e1ts98s)\n  - In Feb 2024 he said he had been there about 2.5 years and was a partner on the early team. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY) (3:43)\n- **Unverified:** the Venture with Grace host also said he had been at General Catalyst. He did not confirm this in his answer, so it is left out.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/jordan-segall/ | Firm bio and deal list (accessed 2026-11-11) |\n| Substack / blog | https://jordansegall.substack.com/ | Segfault: 7 posts, all Feb–Oct 2023; dormant since |\n| X | https://twitter.com/jordan_segall | Not fetchable (402); follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/jordan-segall-7b938632/ | From firm bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://feeds.simplecast.com/dOSE_bdP | Unsupervised Learning co-host. Hosted or co-hosted at least Ep 4, 12, 17, 25, 28, 31, 34 and 39 (2023–24); not seen as host after mid-2024 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2022-07 — Redpoint YouTube — Railway $20M Series A Termsheet Teardown, with Erica Brescia and Jake Cooper — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Mv1AoqjiEmc — transcript not read\n- 2023-01 — Generation Hustle Podcast #92 — \"Backing Founders at the Earliest Stages of the Climb\" (as Principal; includes a Railway case study) — https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/generation-h/episodes/GHP-92---VC-14---Backing-Founders-at-the-Earliest-Stages-of-the-Climb-with-Jordan-Segall-e1ts98s — on Railway diligence he spoke to 15+ users who called the product \"a magical experience\". He named HashiCorp trading at about $4B as the hardest underwriting problem of the downturn. (6:00–26:00 machine-transcribed)\n- 2023-04 to 2024-07 — Unsupervised Learning (co-host): Fixie (Matt Welsh), EleutherAI (Aran Komatsuzaki), Nomic (Andriy Mulyar), Intercom (Des Traynor), LangChain (Harrison Chase), Mistral (Arthur Mensch), Answer.ai (Eric Ries and Jeremy Howard), GitHub (Thomas Dohmke)\n  - In the LangChain debrief he said a top-five bank's compliance chief wanted to be \"like the Third Bank\" to ship customer-facing LLMs. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xLtZ2kyoYic) (64:14)\n- 2024-02 — Venture with Grace — \"Fueling Innovation in Tech\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY — covers fund math, the Mistral seed story, passing on OpenAI alumni and open-source benchmarks\n- 2025-05 — Founder Storiez — \"Rising Stars in Venture Capital\", with James Green (CRV) and Morgan Gerlak (TCV) — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPDvm8UlHw0 — covers the Berlin deal, the poems and his change of mind on price\n- 2025-06 — Redpoint InfraRed Summit — presented the AI, DevTools, Security, Data and Cloud sections with Sai Senthilkumar — https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-update-2025-infrared-report/\n- 2025 — Redpoint deal videos: Motif, Push Security, Fable Security, Vega, Irregular\n\n## How they think\n- **Founder-market fit first:**\n  - At seed, the team and storytelling can carry a pre-traction round on their own.\n  - At Series A he adds design partners and community momentum.\n  - At Series B he adds revenue. [src](https://jordansegall.substack.com/p/so-how-many-stars-is-enough-a-data)\n- **The S-1 test:** he asks whether he can picture the company's S-1. Even bottoms-up devtools need a path to several hundred customers paying $100k or more a year. [src](https://jordansegall.substack.com/p/the-fallacy-of-forever-bottoms-up)\n- **Open-source companies as social networks:** community comes before revenue, with about 10% month-over-month growth as the benchmark. [src](https://jordansegall.substack.com/p/so-how-many-stars-is-enough-a-data)\n- **Pre-empting:** meet companies right after their seed, make intros up to 12 months ahead of the next round, and avoid \"shotgun weddings\". [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY) (46:50)\n- **Contrarian views:**\n  - \"I say bollocks!\" to the idea that revenue matters at an open-source seed.\n  - He is bullish on open-source models: after Meta's compute build-out, he said there should be no more doubt that open-source models would be \"pretty damn good\". [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fi4-kSuaw40) (55:12)\n- **Calls:**\n  - **Right:** Mistral at one week old; the Serval ITSM thesis, which Sequoia later pre-empted.\n  - **Admitted miss:** supporting founders who sold early when Redpoint thought the company could have gone public. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY) (59:07)\n\n## Read these first\n1. Venture with Grace interview (Feb 2024): the fullest account of his decision rules, the fund math and the Mistral story. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY\n2. \"So How Many Stars Is Enough?\" (Feb 2023): his seed and Series A rubric. https://jordansegall.substack.com/p/so-how-many-stars-is-enough-a-data\n3. Serval investment memo (Oct 2025): how he hunts from a thesis. https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-agents-for-it-service-management-our-investment-in-serval---/\n4. Founder Storiez panel (May 2025): price discipline, competitive deals and his peer network. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPDvm8UlHw0\n5. Series B survey (Oct 2023): benchmarks, plus a list of 16 peer investors. https://jordansegall.substack.com/p/i-need-how-much-revenue-a-metrics\n\n## Social engagement\n- X (@jordan_segall) could not be fetched, so there is no first-hand read of what he posts or amplifies.\n- In his Substack he invites founders to DM him on X. [src](https://jordansegall.substack.com/p/welcome-to-segfault)\n- Public output is mostly through Redpoint channels: Unsupervised Learning, deal videos and InfraRed.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Railway | Seed and Series A ($20M) | 2022-07 (A) | yes (own statement, both rounds) | | [src](https://nextplayso.substack.com/p/recommendations-from-redpoint-partner) |\n| DragonflyDB | Seed ($21M) | 2023-03-21 | with Satish Dharmaraj | Quiet Capital | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/fresh-ink-welcoming-dragonflydb-the-modern-in-memory-datastore/) |\n| Mistral AI | Seed | 2023-06 | no (Lightspeed led) | Satish Dharmaraj (Redpoint) | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/mistral-ai) |\n| Restate | Seed | 2024 | unknown | | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/jordan-segall/) |\n| Mithril (Foundry) | Series A | 2024 | unknown | | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/jordan-segall/) |\n| Motif | Series A | 2025-01 | unknown | Alex Bard | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=34J4UBu9NKA) |\n| Push Security | Series B ($30M) | 2025-04 | yes (Redpoint led) | Erica Brescia | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7We4UBSq4NE) |\n| Fable Security | Series A ($31M) | 2025-07 | unknown | Erica Brescia | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ihc58eym2FA) |\n| Vega | Series A ($65M) | 2025-09 | yes (Redpoint led) | Erica Brescia | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Sr0yAvZ9j0c) |\n| Irregular | $80M | 2025-09 | yes (named as leading) | Scott Raney, Sai Senthilkumar | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7XSCmAtCHNQ) |\n| Serval | Series A ($47M) | 2025 | yes | Patrick Chase, Alex Bard | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-agents-for-it-service-management-our-investment-in-serval---/) |\n| imper.ai | Seed ($28M) | 2025-12 | unknown | Erica Brescia | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/imper) |\n| Flora | Series A ($42M) | 2026-01 | yes | Alex Bard | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/flora-fauna) |\n| JetStream | Seed ($34M) | 2026-03 | yes | Erica Brescia | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/jetstream) |\n| Cyberhaven | Series B | | unknown | | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/jordan-segall/) |\n| Personal open-source portfolio (as of 2023) | | | | DragonflyDB, Great Expectations, Heartex, Tabular, ProjectDiscovery | [src](https://jordansegall.substack.com/p/so-how-many-stars-is-enough-a-data) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Mistral AI (2023):**\n  - Redpoint invested about a week after the company was founded, in a \"quite large round\".\n  - The bet was on the Llama/DeepMind team, an open-source \"European counterpart to OpenAI\", and Arthur Mensch being \"incredibly commercial oriented\".\n  - Redpoint flew to Paris to build the relationship and pitched its open-source track record. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY) (19:56, 40:25–42:36)\n- **Serval (2025):**\n  - Redpoint wrote a June 2024 memo arguing that code generation could unseat ServiceNow.\n  - Over three years they met nearly every ITSM startup and considered incubating one themselves.\n  - In the first meeting, Jake Stauch showed that automating a task permanently should take less time than doing it once by hand. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-agents-for-it-service-management-our-investment-in-serval---/)\n- **Unnamed Berlin deal:**\n  - Redpoint had courted the founder for a long time.\n  - A rival firm flew to him and hosted him with a private chef in London.\n  - Segall's team was on a plane to Berlin two hours later and won the deal. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPDvm8UlHw0) (19:19–20:09)\n- **Railway:** he led the Seed and Series A, sits on the board, and calls Jake Cooper's mission to \"destroy Kubernetes\" his model of founder grit. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPDvm8UlHw0) (13:07)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Redpoint Ventures | Partner, early stage | 2021 | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/jordan-segall/) |\n| Unusual Ventures | First VC job (former) | until 2021 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY) |\n| Satish Dharmaraj (Redpoint) | Co-lead on Mistral and DragonflyDB | 2023 | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/fresh-ink-welcoming-dragonflydb-the-modern-in-memory-datastore/) |\n| Patrick Chase, Alex Bard (Redpoint) | Serval co-leads | 2025 | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-agents-for-it-service-management-our-investment-in-serval---/) |\n| Erica Brescia (Redpoint) | Security co-lead; podcast co-host | 2022 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Sr0yAvZ9j0c) |\n| Jacob Effron (Redpoint) | Unsupervised Learning co-host | 2023 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_N2KPEdh69s) |\n| James Green (CRV) | Co-investor and peer | 2025 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPDvm8UlHw0) |\n| Morgan Gerlak (TCV) | Peer circle | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPDvm8UlHw0) |\n| Jake Cooper (Railway) | Board member | 2022 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPDvm8UlHw0) |\n| 16 peer investors (Lux, Thrive, Greylock, Index, Menlo, BCV, a16z, Felicis and others) | Series B survey respondents | 2023 | [src](https://jordansegall.substack.com/p/i-need-how-much-revenue-a-metrics) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which company was the Berlin deal? Restate is a guess: its founders created Apache Flink, which came out of Berlin. Unconfirmed.\n- Which deal did he do with James Green (CRV) in 2025?\n- Which firm led the Railway seed, and when did he lead it? Was it before Redpoint's 2022 Series A, and at which firm?\n- How large was Redpoint's Mistral seed check? What were his role on Mithril and how did that deal originate?\n- Archived X threads could not be fetched.\n- What was in GHP #92 outside the 6:00–26:00 segment that was transcribed (the macro and fundraising advice part)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/jordan-segall/\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAnuKvwm3BY\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPDvm8UlHw0\n- https://jordansegall.substack.com/archive\n- https://jordansegall.substack.com/p/so-how-many-stars-is-enough-a-data\n- https://jordansegall.substack.com/p/the-fallacy-of-forever-bottoms-up\n- https://jordansegall.substack.com/p/i-need-how-much-revenue-a-metrics\n- https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-agents-for-it-service-management-our-investment-in-serval---/\n- https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/fresh-ink-welcoming-dragonflydb-the-modern-in-memory-datastore/\n- https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/25-for-25/\n- https://nextplayso.substack.com/p/recommendations-from-redpoint-partner\n- https://feeds.simplecast.com/dOSE_bdP\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xLtZ2kyoYic\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fi4-kSuaw40\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7XSCmAtCHNQ\n- https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/generation-h/episodes/GHP-92---VC-14---Backing-Founders-at-the-Earliest-Stages-of-the-Climb-with-Jordan-Segall-e1ts98s\n", "person:jordan-windmueller": "\n# Jordan Windmueller\n\n## One-line take\nA* Partner who signs their name to the firm's pre-seed and seed calls on research-team and physical-AI companies (Antioch, Simile, Sekai, Fragment), making them the most findable non-founder sourcing node at A*, but A* stays out of the mega-seeds my lens targets.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** Windmueller is an operator-turned-investor. They spent about two years running finance and business operations at Snorkel AI ([aggregator](https://startupintros.com/people/jordan-windmueller)). They pair that with A*'s talent-first sourcing (\"We generally map talent not markets,\" per co-founder Bennett Siegel; [podcast](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/)). That gets them in before a company has a name. For example, A* met Antioch's founders when \"the company had no product or name\" ([A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/antioch)), and led Andera's seed two weeks after first meeting the founders ([A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/andera)).\n- **Constraint:** A* says it \"can't really do\" the $10-100M seeds that multi-stage funds now absorb ([podcast](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/)). Windmueller's lane is therefore small pre-seed and seed lead or co-lead checks. A* then follows on non-pro-rata into later rounds led by others: Greylock, Lightspeed, Greenoaks and 8VC.\n- **What they'd never do (inferred):** lead a frontier-lab mega-seed. Even in A*'s best AI deals, the A or B has gone to a larger fund.\n\n## Differential insight\nA* publishes its news posts without bylines. The posts link to Windmueller only through their public LinkedIn activity, which marks the Antioch and Simile posts as \"Jordan's article.\" I read LinkedIn through an unauthenticated fetch summarizer, so every row that depends on LinkedIn is low confidence.\n\n- **Antioch timing: A* was first in, about 13 months before Greylock.** \"We led the company's pre-seed just over a year ago\" ([A*, 2026-09-08](https://www.a-star.co/news/antioch)). The first meeting was a coffee in New York with Harry Mellsop and Alex Langshur, who had earlier sold Transpose to Chainalysis. Greylock led the $32M A. LinkedIn credits this post to Windmueller.\n- **Simile origin: a Stanford campus coffee.** A* \"first met him over coffee at Bytes Cafe on Stanford's campus\" ([A*, 2026-07-30](https://www.a-star.co/news/simile)). The \"him\" is Joon Sung Park, who was then weighing a company with his PhD advisors Percy Liang and Michael Bernstein. A* co-led the seed. Greenoaks led the $2B B just over a year later, which was A*'s third check into Simile. LinkedIn credits this post to Windmueller.\n- **Andera speed: seed led two weeks after the first meeting.** \"Two weeks after meeting Aryo and Tinah, we led their seed round\" ([A*, 2026-07-20](https://www.a-star.co/news/andera)). A* met the founders in Oct 2024, when the company was about two months old. Lightspeed then led the $37M A.\n- **Sekai: they personally co-led.** Their comment on the founder's Series B post reads: \"it's a privilege to work together!!\" ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/jordanwindmueller/)). The same post, as fetched, says A* co-led the pre-seed and that Keith Rabois / Khosla led the B. Confidence is low (fetch summary).\n- **Sail Research and the Hazy Research thread.** A* is listed in Sail's seed, which Sequoia led, \"with A* Abstract\" ([Sail](https://sailresearch.com/blog/sail-raises-80m)). Tri Dao (Together AI) is a named angel in the same round. Windmueller reshared the announcement. This is the only sourced point where A* and Stanford systems talent overlap in one cap table. Whether Windmueller connects A* to Flapping Airplanes through the Hazy Research network is still **my inference**, and nothing I found confirms it.\n- **Snorkel lineage.** Snorkel was \"Founded out of the Stanford AI Lab in 2019\" ([Snorkel](https://snorkel.ai/about-us/)). Windmueller was Head, then VP, of Finance & BizOps there from 2021 to 2023 (aggregator), and they still publicly congratulate Snorkel CEO Alex Ratner on milestones (LinkedIn).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** As a co-investor they are low fit: A* writes pre-seed and seed checks (aggregators put the range at $1-10M) and says it cannot play $10-100M seeds, so they will not sit in a $600M lab seed with me. As a **sourcing node** they are high fit. They see Stanford and MIT research spinouts and physical-AI teams 12+ months before Greylock, Lightspeed, Greenoaks and 8VC. The deals that matter to me are:\n- **Sail Research** (long-horizon agent infrastructure; Sequoia seed, Kleiner A)\n- **Watney** (data-center automation robotics; Valor Atreides AI Fund and Hummingbird co-led the $80M A)\n- **Antioch** (sim/testing for autonomous machines)\n- **Simile** (simulated behavioral agents)\n- **Andera** (AI-native automation for audit and compliance, my Serval-style category)\n\nThey also reacted to Ricursive Intelligence's open-weight model release. That points to a possible pre-training interest, unconfirmed.\n\n## Background\n- Education: Penn (BA Political Science) and Wharton (BS Econ, Finance), both summa cum laude, per [NFX Signal](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/jordan-windmueller) (aggregator). LinkedIn lists Stanford GSB (Arjay Miller Scholar; VC Club Co-President) with no dates ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/jordanwindmueller/)).\n- Bessemer Venture Partners, Analyst then Associate, 2014-2018 ([NFX](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/jordan-windmueller), aggregator).\n- Earlier: BlackRock Private Equity Partners, Schooner Capital, U.S. Department of Commerce, HBS research for Noam Wasserman (NFX, undated). [startupintros](https://startupintros.com/people/jordan-windmueller) also lists Insight Partners and Goldman Sachs. That aggregator is internally inconsistent, so treat it as unverified.\n- EvolutionIQ: interim Head of Product and first employee (NFX, undated).\n- Snorkel AI: Head of Finance, then VP Finance & Business Operations, about 2021-2023 ([startupintros](https://startupintros.com/people/jordan-windmueller), aggregator).\n- A*: joined about 2023 as Principal, now Partner ([A* team](https://www.a-star.co/team)).\n- Co-founder of the Women of Startup Nation Accelerator (Israel, with Google), 2018-present ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/jordanwindmueller/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.a-star.co/team | Title \"Partner\" (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | A* news posts (unbylined) | LinkedIn credits the Antioch and Simile posts to them |\n| X | nothing found | Not searched (search budget exhausted) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/jordanwindmueller/ | About 5K followers per fetch summary (2026-11-10). Posts each A* deal announcement. |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was unavailable this run (the per-turn search budget was exhausted), so this is not a confirmed absence.\n\n## How they think\n- **Get in before the company exists, then double down.** Every A* post they are tied to has the same shape: a coffee meeting before a product or name, an A* lead or co-lead at pre-seed or seed, and a larger firm leading the A or B, with A* participating (\"expanding\" or \"doubling down\").\n- **Physical-world loops.** The Antioch post closes: \"Software ate the world when building became a fast, continuous cycle\" ([A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/antioch)). The physical-AI cluster they post about is Antioch, Watney and Fragment.\n- **AI as a decision layer.** The Simile post argues Simile's market is the value of better decisions, not market-research spend ([A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/simile)).\n- Calls right or wrong: it is too early to say. The step-ups to date are Simile at a $2B B and Sekai's Series B; ai.work was acquired by ServiceNow (LinkedIn, A* involvement unconfirmed).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Partnering with Antioch](https://www.a-star.co/news/antioch): their article per LinkedIn. Shows the pre-name pre-seed lead and the physical-AI thesis.\n2. [Backing Simile](https://www.a-star.co/news/simile): their article per LinkedIn. Covers the Stanford research-spinout sourcing and the three-check follow-on pattern.\n3. [Backing Andera](https://www.a-star.co/news/andera): the two-week seed lead and the AI-native compliance thesis. Authorship is not confirmed.\n4. [Announcing A* III](https://www.a-star.co/news/fundiii): the firm's $450M fund and its \"We commit early\" framing.\n\n## Social engagement\nOn LinkedIn they reshare portfolio rounds (Fragment, Watney, Sail, Antioch), congratulate founders (Sekai, ai.work) and keep up with the Snorkel network (Alex Ratner). They liked a post by Saam Motamedi (Greylock), who leads the Antioch A. They reacted to Ricursive Intelligence's Ripple-65B release. All of this comes from a fetch summary of the public profile on 2026-11-10.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Antioch | Pre-seed | about Aug 2025 | A* led | | [A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/antioch) |\n| Antioch | Series A $32M | 2026-09-08 | no | Greylock (lead) | [A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/antioch) |\n| Simile | Seed | 2025 | A* co-led | | [A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/simile) |\n| Simile | Series B ($2B val.) | 2026-07-30 | no | Greenoaks (lead) | [A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/simile) |\n| Andera | Seed | Oct/Nov 2024 | A* led | | [A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/andera) |\n| Andera | Series A $37M | 2026-07-20 | no | Lightspeed (lead); Bain Capital Ventures | [A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/andera) |\n| Sekai | Pre-seed | undated | A* co-led (they were thanked) | | [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/jordanwindmueller/) (summary) |\n| Fragment | Seed (part of $56M seed+A) | about late Oct/early Nov 2026 announce | A* co-led with General Catalyst | 8VC led A | [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/jordanwindmueller/) (summary) |\n| Sail Research | Seed (part of $80M) | 2026-06-24 | no | Sequoia (lead); Abstract; angels John Hennessy, Lip-Bu Tan, Tri Dao | [Sail](https://sailresearch.com/blog/sail-raises-80m) |\n| Watney | Seed | 2025-01 | A* co-led | | [A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/watney) |\n\nTheir personal deal-lead role is confirmed only for Sekai (co-lead thanks) and for authorship of the Antioch and Simile posts. Every other row is an A* firm deal they publicly amplify.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Antioch:** A* met the founders over coffee in New York before the company had a product or name, and led the pre-seed. About 13 months later, Greylock led a $32M A and A* \"expanded\" its position. Ring (Amazon) is cited as a customer ([A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/antioch)).\n- **Simile:** A* had tracked Joon Sung Park's research and met him at Bytes Cafe at Stanford while he was weighing a company with Percy Liang and Michael Bernstein. A* co-led the seed, and Lainie Yallen (ex-Hebbia GTM) joined soon after. Greenoaks led a $2B B about a year later ([A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/simile)).\n- **Andera:** A* met MIT co-founders Aryo Patel and Tinah Hong in Oct 2024 and led the seed two weeks later ([A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/andera)).\n- **Watney:** A* met the founders while they were building strawberry-picking robots. They pivoted to data centers, and A* co-led the seed in Jan 2025 ([A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/watney)). The A was co-led by Valor Atreides AI Fund and Hummingbird (LinkedIn summary).\n- Intro paths: nothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A* | Partner (earlier Principal) | about 2023 | [A* team](https://www.a-star.co/team); [startupintros](https://startupintros.com/people/jordan-windmueller) (aggregator) |\n| Snorkel AI / Alex Ratner | Former Head then VP Finance & BizOps; still engages publicly | 2021-2023 | [startupintros](https://startupintros.com/people/jordan-windmueller) (aggregator); LinkedIn |\n| Bessemer Venture Partners | Analyst then Associate | 2014-2018 | [NFX](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/jordan-windmueller) (aggregator) |\n| EvolutionIQ | First employee / interim Head of Product | undated | [NFX](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/jordan-windmueller) (aggregator) |\n| Lucky Z. (Sekai) | Founder they backed at pre-seed | | [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/jordanwindmueller/) |\n| Pranav Mulgund, Naveen Kashyap (Fragment) | Founders, seed co-led by A* | | LinkedIn |\n| General Catalyst | Seed co-lead on Fragment | | LinkedIn |\n| Women of Startup Nation Accelerator | Co-founder | 2018 | LinkedIn |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is Windmueller A*'s deal partner on Flapping Airplanes, and did they reach the Flapping founders through Snorkel / Hazy Research (Chris Ré's lab)? My inference only; not confirmed.\n- Who at A* sourced Sail Research, and does the Tri Dao angel check connect to Windmueller?\n- Are they the deal partner on Watney, Fragment and Andera, or only amplifying firm deals?\n- What are Sekai and Fragment? Identify each company and its sector. One is possibly consumer AI music/video, the other possibly physical-world data.\n- Is the Ricursive Intelligence reaction a sign of investment interest in pre-training?\n- Do they have any X, podcast or talk presence? Search was unavailable this run.\n- Penn/Wharton vs Stanford GSB, and Boston vs NYC: resolve the aggregator conflicts.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.a-star.co/team\n- https://www.a-star.co/news/antioch\n- https://www.a-star.co/news/simile\n- https://www.a-star.co/news/andera\n- https://www.a-star.co/news/watney\n- https://www.a-star.co/news/fundiii\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/jordanwindmueller/ (public profile via fetch summary)\n- https://sailresearch.com/blog/sail-raises-80m\n- https://snorkel.ai/about-us/\n- https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/\n- https://signal.nfx.com/investors/jordan-windmueller (aggregator)\n- https://startupintros.com/people/jordan-windmueller (aggregator)\n", "person:josh-kushner": "\n# Joshua (Josh) Kushner\n\n## One-line take\nThe person who actually wins Thrive's frontier-AI deals: he filed OpenAI's only term sheet, created Isomorphic's first outside round by lobbying Alphabet, and led Physical Intelligence's seed. He then hands the boards to partners, which is why so few deals carry his name.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he shows up in person, alone and at scale, when others hesitate. Thrive was the only term sheet on OpenAI's ~$130M secondary at ~$29B [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/). It led the next two OpenAI financings. He flew to Tel Aviv in wartime to close Wiz [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/), and to the West Coast twice in three days to close OpenAI at ~$150B [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw).\n- **He creates rounds rather than joins them:** Isomorphic wasn't raising. He pitched Alphabet leadership to allow a tightly held round, and Thrive became its first outside money [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/).\n- **What constrains him:** his attention. He is the firm's CEO and also does Oscar (vice-chair), A24 (board, 2024), the Lakers purchase (2026) and media. So he originates and then delegates boards: Hankes holds Isomorphic, Grimshaw holds Cursor, and Clark holds Pace and Mesh [src](../firms/thrive-capital.md). The capital is growth-heavy: only $1B of Thrive X's $10B is early-stage [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/17/thrive-raises-10b-for-new-fund-its-largest-yet/).\n- **What he'd never do (by record):** spray small checks, or join a new-lab mega-seed he didn't originate. No SSI, Thinking Machines, Periodic or Reflection appears in his record. His frontier-lab exposure is OpenAI only.\n\n## Why the repo had only 2 companies on him\nBefore this profile, merged `data/deals.csv` attributed only OpenAI and Physical Intelligence to `josh-kushner`. There are four reasons:\n1. **Thrive names no individual on most deals.** Press releases quote partners (Clark, Zaki, Mallick) or no one at all.\n2. **He originates and then hands off.** The board and deal-partner credit goes to Hankes, Grimshaw and Clark, so their profiles absorb the deals.\n3. **The Wiz and Isomorphic origination rows only arrived with the latest Thrive pass.**\n4. **The slug is split.** Rows use `josh-kushner`, `joshua-kushner` and now `joshua-josh-kushner`.\n\nWith sources, his named AI list is OpenAI ×3, Physical Intelligence, Isomorphic and Wiz ×2. Ellis is attributable only through his relationship with its founder. Other Thrive AI deals (Cursor, Databricks, Chai, Sierra, OpenEvidence) name no Kushner role in any source found, so they are not credited to him.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The OpenAI edge was nerve, not access.** Hankes says many investors saw the same closed GPT-4 demo: \"we were on that call like many other investors\". He describes his reaction alongside Kushner as \"we looked at each other like wow\" [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/vince-hankes-why-we-put-300m-into-openai-sam-altman-s-pitch-lessons-from-josh-ku) (20VC E1009, 2023-05-03, 14:11). Thrive still filed the only term sheet.\n- **The ~$150B OpenAI close was personal shuttle diplomacy.** It came amid Brockman's leave and Schulman's exit. Kushner had \"flown back and forth to the West Coast two times in three days\", the deal was agreed on a Wednesday night, and he arrived at the Thursday 7am meeting with \"three next ideas\" [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw) (Grimshaw on Dialectic, ~1:37).\n- **He hires and backs will over credentials.** Grimshaw says he \"really leaned into people who had a lot of will\" [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw). He also quotes Kushner's culture line: \"It's not just winning; it's the way we win.\"\n- **The BCI view reversed.** In 2023 he said of brain-machine interfaces, \"We have not spent time on that\" [src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-154-josh-kushner-and-kareem). By July 2025 Thrive had co-led Nudge's $100M Series A, worked by Clark [src](https://news.bitcoin.com/coinbase-co-founder-fred-ehrsam-raises-100m-for-brain-interface-startup/).\n- **The OpenAI crisis made the relationship.** Lightcap's first call after Altman's ouster \"was to Joshua Kushner, Thrive's founder.\" [src](https://fortune.com/2023/12/07/joshua-kushner-thrive-openai-stripe-skims-warby-parker-kkr/) Altman says Kushner \"didn't leave his hotel room for 72 hours\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/). Thrive then took the $1B option at $150B through Jan 2026 (same src).\n- **Ellis correction.** The task premise says Kushner invested personally. TechCrunch lists **Thrive Capital** among Ellis's investors, not Kushner [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/31/repeat-founder-ryan-williams-raises-10m-seed-for-an-ai-startup-for-private-credit-managers/). The real link is that founder Ryan Williams co-founded Cadre with Josh and Jared Kushner in 2014 (same src). That makes it a founder-loyalty check, not a thesis bet.\n- **He is a GP-to-GP backer.** He is an LP in Abstract Ventures Fund I ($100M, 2018–19) [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/).\n- **He is a network broker.** He introduced Elad Gil to his brother Jared, which led to Brain Co [src](https://forbes.es/empresas/795587/como-la-nueva-startup-de-jared-kushner-esta-ayudando-a-las-empresas-a-integrar-la-ia-con-exito/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:** he has led the kind of rounds you'd want to co-invest in: the $70M PI seed (robotics) and the Isomorphic first round (AI bio). He is also the single decision-maker on OpenAI.\n- **Against:** his personal record is growth-sized and almost entirely late. Three OpenAI rounds and Wiz were all at $12B+ valuations. He has never led a new-lab mega-seed. As an LP he has backed only one emerging manager (Abstract), and that was a consumer/generalist fund.\n- **Use him as:** a signal source (where Thrive anchors next) and a possible co-lead on a robotics or AI-bio seed of $50M+. Don't treat him as an LP prospect.\n- **Deals that matter to you:** Physical Intelligence Seed 2024, Isomorphic Series A 2025 and OpenAI 2023.\n\n## Background\n- Born 1985-06-12, Livingston, NJ. Harvard College BA 2008; Harvard Business School MBA 2011 [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joshua_Kushner).\n- Co-founded Vostu (a social gaming network) as a Harvard junior [src](https://www.thecrimson.com/article/2007/3/7/students-start-spanish-social-site-facebook/).\n- Spent one year in Goldman Sachs private equity, working on distressed debt [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joshua_Kushner).\n- **2009:** founded Thrive. Joel Cutler (General Catalyst) put in $1M of seed money [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/).\n- **~2011:** Princeton (Andy Golden) backed the first institutional fund (same src).\n- **2012:** co-founded Oscar Health; he is vice-chairman.\n- **2014–15:** co-founded Cadre with Jared Kushner and Ryan Williams.\n- **2024:** joined the A24 board.\n- **2026-08:** co-buyer of the Lakers with Bob Iger [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joshua_Kushner).\n- Forbes put his net worth at $16.7B in Aug 2026 (cited via Wikipedia).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://thrivecap.com | Firm one-pager; no bio or team page (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://medium.com/@thrivecapital/thrive-vii-4bc8527151bb | Firm fund letters on Medium; nothing personal found |\n| X | not verified | No handle confirmed this pass |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Not a host. Best reference: [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joshua_Kushner) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023 (posted 2024-02-10) — Bloomberg-style Q&A with Kareem Zaki, transcribed in A Letter a Day #154 — [link](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-154-josh-kushner-and-kareem). He said Thrive set out \"to not look like anyone else\" and works as a flywheel, and that it had not spent time on BCI.\n- Invest Like the Best: Patrick O'Shaughnessy references an earlier episode in which Kushner called Zaki the best healthcare investor in the world [src](https://colossus.com/episode/small-ideas-attract-competition/). The episode itself was not located this pass.\n- Colossus profile (Oct 2025) — [link](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/). This is the best single source.\n\n## How they think\n- **Ideas lead, not process:** \"The idea for Thrive came purely from where we believed the world was moving.\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)\n- **Enterprise value fixes everything:** his OpenAI thesis was that if the product could create that much value, the other risks were solvable (same src).\n- **Contrarian by temperament:** \"I have learned not to care too much about what the world thinks.\" (same src)\n- **Founder first, and concentration:** \"we've had a founder first mindset\" [src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-154-josh-kushner-and-kareem).\n- **Calls he got right:** Instagram ($12M about 72 hours before the Facebook deal), Spotify, GitHub, Wiz ($32B Google deal) and OpenAI (from ~$29B to $500B+) [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/).\n- **Calls he got wrong:** none admitted in the sources read. Vostu scaled back in 2013.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Colossus: Joshua Kushner and Thrive](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) — origin stories for OpenAI, Isomorphic, Wiz and Stripe.\n2. [20VC E1009, Vince Hankes](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/vince-hankes-why-we-put-300m-into-openai-sam-altman-s-pitch-lessons-from-josh-ku) — OpenAI decision from inside, and lessons from Kushner.\n3. [Dialectic: Miles Grimshaw](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw) — how Kushner hires, and the $150B OpenAI close.\n4. [A Letter a Day #154](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-154-josh-kushner-and-kareem) — Kushner in his own words on the Thrive model.\n5. [Fortune, Dec 2023](https://fortune.com/2023/12/07/joshua-kushner-thrive-openai-stripe-skims-warby-parker-kkr/) — his role in the OpenAI crisis.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No verified X account, and his public voice comes through interviews and colleagues.\n\n## Portfolio\nThese are the AI deals (plus Wiz) where he is personally named. Full rows are in `data/rows/joshua-josh-kushner-deals.csv`.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | Secondary (~$130M of ~$300M at $27–29B) | 2023-04 (Colossus says the term sheet was spring 2022; unresolved) | yes (sole term sheet) | Sequoia, a16z, Founders Fund, K2 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/04/28/openai-funding-valuation-chatgpt/) |\n| OpenAI | Tender (~$86B; agreed Aug 2023, closed Feb 2024) | 2024-02 | yes (double-down) | — | [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2023/11/30/openai-tender-offer-on-track-despite-leadership-fracas-sources.html) |\n| OpenAI | Growth ($6.6B at $157B; ~$1B plus a $1B option) | 2024-10-02 | yes (third straight lead) | Microsoft, NVIDIA, SoftBank, Khosla | [src](https://fortune.com/2024/10/02/openai-officially-raises-6-6-billion-funding-deal-157-billion-valuation-sam-altman-thrive-capital/) |\n| Physical Intelligence | Seed ($70M) | 2024-03-12 | yes (first institutional money) | Khosla, Lux, OpenAI, Sequoia | [src](https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/) |\n| Wiz | Series E ($1B at $12B) | 2024-05-07 | co-led | a16z, Lightspeed | [src](https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001478169) |\n| Wiz | Tender ($16B) | 2024 | yes | — | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Isomorphic Labs | Series A ($600M; first external money) | 2025-03-31 | yes | GV, Alphabet | [src](https://isomorphiclabs.com/articles/isomorphic-labs-announces-600m-external-investment-round) |\n| Ellis | Seed ($10M) | 2026-07-30 | no (firm; personal check unconfirmed) | First Round (lead), Khosla | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/31/repeat-founder-ryan-williams-raises-10m-seed-for-an-ai-startup-for-private-credit-managers/) |\n\n**Timing:**\n- **OpenAI:** he was first among the 2023 VC cohort with a term sheet, then doubled down twice within 18 months.\n- **Physical Intelligence:** he was the first lead. Thrive came back as co-lead of the Series A eight months later.\n- **Isomorphic:** he was first in, ahead of all outside money. Thrive led again at the $2.1B Series B (2026-05), with Hankes on the board.\n\n**Boards:** no AI board seat was found. His known seats are Oscar (vice-chair) and A24 (2024).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **OpenAI:**\n  - Hankes and Kushner were on the closed GPT-4 demo call. Thrive submitted the only term sheet [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/vince-hankes-why-we-put-300m-into-openai-sam-altman-s-pitch-lessons-from-josh-ku), [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/).\n  - In Aug 2023 Thrive agreed to anchor $400M of a $500M employee tender, and it had ~$700M committed by 2023-11-17 (Colossus).\n  - The tender survived the ouster, with Kushner acting as Lightcap's confidant [src](https://fortune.com/2023/12/07/joshua-kushner-thrive-openai-stripe-skims-warby-parker-kkr/).\n  - In 2024 he negotiated $1B at $150B plus an exclusive $1B option at that price [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/).\n- **Isomorphic:** in fall 2024 Thrive heard that Hassabis was weighing outside money. Kushner pitched Alphabet leadership on a tightly held round and told Hassabis \"Stop being so British about it.\" The result was the $600M round in Mar 2025 (Colossus).\n- **Wiz:** after a call with Assaf Rappaport, Kushner and Clark flew to Tel Aviv during the war and agreed terms within days. Thrive's ~$1B stake was built across the $12B round and the $16B tender, ahead of Google's $32B deal (Colossus).\n- **Physical Intelligence:** Wired credits Thrive under Kushner with leading the seed. Lachy Groom, a co-founder, mentions a call with Kushner [src](https://www.wired.com/story/physical-intelligence-ai-robotics-startup/). The intro path is not disclosed.\n- **Ellis:** Ryan Williams is his Cadre co-founder (2014). Thrive joined the First Round-led seed [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/31/repeat-founder-ryan-williams-raises-10m-seed-for-an-ai-startup-for-private-credit-managers/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sam Altman / Brad Lightcap (OpenAI) | Lead investor and crisis confidant | 2023 | [src](https://fortune.com/2023/12/07/joshua-kushner-thrive-openai-stripe-skims-warby-parker-kkr/) |\n| Demis Hassabis / Alphabet | Created Isomorphic's first outside round | 2024 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Vince Hankes | Partner; OpenAI demo call; holds the Isomorphic board seat | 2019 | [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/vince-hankes-why-we-put-300m-into-openai-sam-altman-s-pitch-lessons-from-josh-ku) |\n| Philip Clark | Partner; flew with him to Tel Aviv for Wiz | 2024 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Miles Grimshaw | Hired 2013, re-hired from Benchmark 2024 | 2013 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/05/miles-grimshaw-leaves-benchmark-to-re-join-kushners-thrive-capital) |\n| Lachy Groom | PI co-founder; direct line during the seed | 2024 | [src](https://www.wired.com/story/physical-intelligence-ai-robotics-startup/) |\n| Joel Cutler (General Catalyst) | First $1M into Thrive | 2009 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Andy Golden (Princeton) | First institutional LP | ~2011 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Ramtin Naimi (Abstract) | Kushner is an LP in Fund I | 2018 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Jared Kushner / Elad Gil | Introduced Gil to Jared (Brain Co) | 2023 | [src](https://forbes.es/empresas/795587/como-la-nueva-startup-de-jared-kushner-esta-ayudando-a-las-empresas-a-integrar-la-ia-con-exito/) |\n| Ryan Williams | Cadre co-founder; Ellis founder | 2014 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/31/repeat-founder-ryan-williams-raises-10m-seed-for-an-ai-startup-for-private-credit-managers/) |\n| Bob Iger | Thrive GP-stake holder; Lakers co-buyer | 2023 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joshua_Kushner) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **OpenAI entry date:** was the first term sheet in spring 2022 (Colossus) or early 2023 (Fortune and TechCrunch)?\n- **The $1B option:** did Thrive exercise the $1B-at-$150B option before Jan 2026?\n- **Physical Intelligence:** who introduced Kushner, and was it Groom, Altman or Lux?\n- **Personal AI angel checks** outside Thrive: none found. This was limited by the search budget, so it should be rechecked.\n- **Invest Like the Best:** the Kushner episode transcript has not been read.\n- **X handle:** none verified.\n- **Thrive Holdings:** his personal role is not stated in TechCrunch, which names Mehndiratta and Zaki as founding members.\n\n## Sources\n- https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/\n- https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/vince-hankes-why-we-put-300m-into-openai-sam-altman-s-pitch-lessons-from-josh-ku\n- https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw\n- https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-154-josh-kushner-and-kareem\n- https://fortune.com/2023/12/07/joshua-kushner-thrive-openai-stripe-skims-warby-parker-kkr/\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joshua_Kushner\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/31/repeat-founder-ryan-williams-raises-10m-seed-for-an-ai-startup-for-private-credit-managers/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/openai-backed-thrive-holdings-raises-2b-to-bring-ai-to-the-enterprise/\n- https://www.wired.com/story/physical-intelligence-ai-robotics-startup/\n- https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/04/28/openai-funding-valuation-chatgpt/ ; https://fortune.com/2024/10/02/openai-officially-raises-6-6-billion-funding-deal-157-billion-valuation-sam-altman-thrive-capital/ ; https://isomorphiclabs.com/articles/isomorphic-labs-announces-600m-external-investment-round ; https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001478169 ; https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/\n- Note: WebSearch budget was exhausted this turn; all facts come from direct fetches or existing repo rows.\n", "person:josh-wolfe": "\n# Josh Wolfe\n\n## One-line take\nWolfe co-founded Lux and writes its quarterly LP letters. He is worth following because those letters say, ahead of the deals, which AI consensus Lux is betting against: open models over closed labs, edge over data centers, and new non-LLM labs like Sakana and Flourish ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q4-2024-report), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** Wolfe's stated filter is to find \"which input the consensus has not yet priced\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report)). Lux then builds or backs the counter-position early, often as the first investor or by incubating the company itself. Examples are Anduril (Lux, 2017), Valthos (a Lux Labs de novo) and Flourish (incubated inside Lux) ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/anduril), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report)). WashU's CIO says he copied this idea-sourcing approach ([src](https://blog.riskgaming.com/p/shredding-the-lp-asset-management-playbook-with-scott-wilson-cio-of-washington-university-in-st-louis)).\n- **Sourcing model:** Lux runs on networks, not data. Wolfe writes \"we knew a guy who knew a guy\" and \"There's no Bloomberg terminal for that\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q4-2025-report)). Wilson recalls Wolfe's answer as \"there are links in the chain everywhere\" ([src](https://blog.riskgaming.com/p/shredding-the-lp-asset-management-playbook-with-scott-wilson-cio-of-washington-university-in-st-louis)).\n- **Constraint:** Wolfe is publicly bearish on the frontier-lab capex race. He calls the big labs \"individually rational yet collectively irrational\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report)), and says \"AI winners will not be those who train the biggest models\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q4-2024-report)). So Lux is unlikely to lead a scale-maximalist LLM lab mega-seed. It does back labs with a non-consensus architecture (Sakana, Flourish). In Q1 2026 Lux's biggest stated \"position\" was dry powder from the $1.5B Lux IX ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report)).\n- **Timing pattern:** he wants to be first (\"I can't stand the idea that someone else might fund them before me\"; [src](https://alumni.cornell.edu/article/josh-wolfe-99-invests-in-human-ambition/)), and in AI labs Lux gets there by incubating (Flourish) or leading the founding round (Sakana). Flapping Airplanes is the exception: Index was first, and Lux bought in as co-lead of the $1B A ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/), [src](https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/)).\n- **What he'd never do:** he names the sectors outright: \"We don't invest in gaming, e-commerce, or edtech\" ([src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/end2evyv9), 2025-05-26; Israeli outlet, wording may be translated). By inference from the letters, he also won't back the consensus. He prices the crowd as the risk: \"Markets pay high prices for cheery consensus while alpha hides at the edges\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report)).\n\n## Differential insight\n*Updated 2026-11-10: added first-check temperament, spin-out sourcing, Flapping Airplanes timing, Crosby, sector exclusions and the defense fund. Guest podcasts and X are still not covered (web search budget exhausted this run).*\n\n- **First-check jealousy.** \"I can't stand the idea that someone else might fund them before me\" ([src](https://alumni.cornell.edu/article/josh-wolfe-99-invests-in-human-ambition/)). He calls his checks into founders \"tens of millions of dollars,\" and his filter is grit: \"Chips on shoulders put chips in pockets\" (same source).\n- **Labs as spin-outs.** Lux Labs goes after teams: \"We'll find a team and actually convince them that they should spin out.\" A bet can start from a thesis, a breakthrough, or \"a breakthrough scientist, an individual\" ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/11/01/luxs-josh-wolfe-on-launching-lux-labs-and-making-science-fiction-reality/), 2024-11-01).\n- **Flapping Airplanes: Lux followed, it did not lead in.** Index led the $180M seed (\"Since we led their seed round earlier this year\") and took the board seat (Mark Xu). Lux came in about nine months later as co-lead of the $1B A at a reported $6B ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/), [src](https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/), 2026-10-21). Index's post never names Lux. Brandon Reeves, not Wolfe, is the Lux partner quoted. This is a case where Lux bought a seat in a new lab after the seed rather than being first.\n- **AI-native services, not tools.** Lux led Crosby's $60M Series B. Crosby pairs LLMs with in-house lawyers who take responsibility for the work: \"Crosby isn't an AI assistant for lawyers\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/were-backing-crosby-to-end-the-billiable-hour-for-laywers), 2026-03-31).\n- **Defense scale.** Wolfe says Lux has put over $1B into defense tech and \"raised an additional $200 million fund dedicated entirely to the defense sector.\" He also likes stealth: \"You don't want to attract competition before you've made enough progress\" ([src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/end2evyv9), 2025-05-26).\n- **Talent as capital.** In 2023 testimony he wrote that talent, like capital, \"goes where it is welcome,\" and urged the US to speed up China's brain drain through talent visas ([src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116281/witnesses/HHRG-118-ZS00-Wstate-WolfeJ-20230726.pdf), 2023-07-26).\n- **Sourcing ideas (as WashU tells it).** Wilson asked Wolfe how Lux sources ideas and says WashU's approach is \"pretty similar to how we do it.\" He paraphrases Wolfe's answer as \"logically it doesn't make sense, but there are links in the chain everywhere\" ([src](https://blog.riskgaming.com/p/shredding-the-lp-asset-management-playbook-with-scott-wilson-cio-of-washington-university-in-st-louis), 2022-05).\n- **WashU uses Lux as a \"prepared mind\" feed.** Wilson asks Lux for its \"five best companies,\" so WashU can act directly if something happens later ([src](https://blog.riskgaming.com/p/shredding-the-lp-asset-management-playbook-with-scott-wilson-cio-of-washington-university-in-st-louis)). This is a co-invest and direct path routed through the GP.\n- **How WashU found Lux.** The link was \"an overlap in one of those early automated manufacturing companies.\" The company is not named ([src](https://blog.riskgaming.com/p/shredding-the-lp-asset-management-playbook-with-scott-wilson-cio-of-washington-university-in-st-louis)).\n- **An LP that can't re-up.** Wolfe told a story about an LP's whiteboard of managers that filled up until the LP said \"It's not that I don't want to invest, I just can't\" ([src](https://blog.riskgaming.com/p/shredding-the-lp-asset-management-playbook-with-scott-wilson-cio-of-washington-university-in-st-louis)). This is a denominator and LP-capacity signal for emerging managers.\n- **Lux's origin capital.** Bill Conway, co-founder of Carlyle, \"put us in business with his capital\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report), 2025-11-20).\n- **Flourish, the Lux-incubated lab.** Flourish was \"incubated inside Lux and raised half a billion from inception.\" Its founder is Thomas Reardon (CTRL-labs, sold to Meta). The thesis is distributed, embodied, continuously learning AI, not a frozen data-center model ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report), 2026-05-21). No press announcement was found. This is a THESIS-shaped new lab with a very large first raise.\n- **Edge over data center.** \"The opportunity for AI at the edge will dwarf the data center AI opportunity\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report)). He groups Cognition, Hugging Face, Modal and Together into an \"edge-AI thesis,\" and names edge inference chips \"from Mythic to Majestic\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report)).\n- **The frontier-lab bear case.** Labs are \"each chasing 100% market share while together spending 400% or more in capex\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report)). In the post-DeepSeek letter: \"The pendulum has now swung decisively in favor of open-source AI\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q4-2024-report), 2025-02-13).\n- **Sakana as a geographic hedge.** Lux backed Sakana \"as a hedge against the assumption that frontier AI remains North American\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report)).\n- **Venture concentration.** \"The venture minnows are going extinct; the megas are consolidating,\" with nearly 75% of Q1 2026 capital going to five firms ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report)). Wolfe positions Lux as \"bespoke craft,\" not asset management ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q4-2025-report)).\n- **Realized AI exit.** EvolutionaryScale was acquired by the Chan Zuckerberg Initiative and produced a Lux distribution ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q4-2025-report)).\n- **Mistakes:** none admitted in the letters read. The Q4 2024 letter repeats an earlier skeptical call on \"the overcapitalization of modular nuclear\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q4-2024-report)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Wolfe runs a firm that leads founding rounds of non-consensus labs (Sakana, $30M, Lux led), incubated a lab that raised about $500M from inception (Flourish), and holds edge AI chip bets (Mythic, Majestic). See [firms/lux-capital.md](../firms/lux-capital.md), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q4-2024-report), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report) and [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report).\n- **Tension with my lens:** he is openly skeptical of scale-maximalist frontier labs. A $600M seed pitch built on \"more GPUs\" is the opposite of his thesis. A new lab with a novel architecture, an edge or efficiency story, or a non-US base is squarely his thesis.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** Flapping Airplanes (Lux co-led the $1B A after Index's seed; [src](https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/)), Crosby (AI-native legal services, Lux led the B; [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/were-backing-crosby-to-end-the-billiable-hour-for-laywers)), Flourish (new lab; how its $500M was syndicated is unknown), Sakana, Together, Hugging Face, Physical Intelligence, Cognition, SkyPilot, Mythic and Majestic (edge chips), and Valthos (AI biodefense de novo).\n- **Timing read:** Wolfe wants to be first (\"I can't stand the idea that someone else might fund them before me\"; [src](https://alumni.cornell.edu/article/josh-wolfe-99-invests-in-human-ambition/)), and in labs he gets there by incubating (Flourish) or leading founding rounds (Sakana). When he isn't first, Lux can still write a $100M-scale co-lead check (Flapping Airplanes). For a $20M seed check, Lux is a likely co-investor in a new lab only if the lab is non-consensus on architecture or efficiency.\n- **LP angle:** Wolfe is WashU's GP contact. WashU gets its \"inside view\" from Lux ([src](https://blog.riskgaming.com/p/shredding-the-lp-asset-management-playbook-with-scott-wilson-cio-of-washington-university-in-st-louis)). For me he is a co-investor and idea source, not an LP path.\n\n## Background\n- Cornell, Dyson School, BS in finance, 1999 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josh_Wolfe)). The Lux bio describes him as a \"published scientist\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/josh-wolfe)).\n- Co-founded Lux in 2000 with Peter Hébert and Robert Paull ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josh_Wolfe)). Conway's capital started the firm ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report)).\n- Grew up in Brooklyn. Trustee of the Santa Fe Institute. Co-founder and former chair of Coney Island Prep. Has co-founded 20+ Lux companies ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/josh-wolfe)).\n- Gave written testimony to the House Select Committee on the CCP (2023). Met Netanyahu with other tech investors on 2025-09-29 about AI for Israel's military and economy ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josh_Wolfe); secondary source, flagged for notability).\n- Pre-Lux jobs: not confirmed in the sources read. As a high-school student he interned at an immunopathology lab at Downstate Hospital in Brooklyn under Dr. Dominick Auci, and started at Cornell in biology ([src](https://alumni.cornell.edu/article/josh-wolfe-99-invests-in-human-ambition/)).\n- Lux was \"a $5 billion venture fund\" at the time of his 2023 testimony ([src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116281/witnesses/HHRG-118-ZS00-Wstate-WolfeJ-20230726.pdf)). In 2026 it raised the $1.5B Fund IX and says 44 people manage $7B ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-lux-ventures-ix), 2026-01-07).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.luxcapital.com/people/josh-wolfe | Bio and company list |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.luxcapital.com/news (quarterly LP letters) | Quarterly, public; Q4 2023 to Q1 2026 seen (checked 2026-11-09) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/wolfejosh | Linked from Lux bio; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/josh-wolfe-7883 | From Lux bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Securities / Riskgaming (Lux) | Wolfe co-hosts some episodes, e.g. the 2022 WashU one ([src](https://blog.riskgaming.com/p/shredding-the-lp-asset-management-playbook-with-scott-wilson-cio-of-washington-university-in-st-louis)) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2022-05 — Securities / Riskgaming (Lux) — \"Shredding the LP asset management playbook\" with Scott Wilson and Danny Crichton — [link](https://blog.riskgaming.com/p/shredding-the-lp-asset-management-playbook-with-scott-wilson-cio-of-washington-university-in-st-louis) — the LP whiteboard story; Wilson on copying Lux's sourcing.\n- 2025-04-28 — Lux essay \"The Case for Sci-Tech Diplomacy\" with Brett McGurk ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news)). Not read.\n- 2023-07-26 — House Select Committee on the CCP — written testimony — [link](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116281/witnesses/HHRG-118-ZS00-Wstate-WolfeJ-20230726.pdf) — talent \"goes where it is welcome\"; names Anduril, Saildrone, Applied Intuition, Primer, Kallyope.\n- 2024-11-01 — Fortune interview on Lux Labs — [link](https://fortune.com/2024/11/01/luxs-josh-wolfe-on-launching-lux-labs-and-making-science-fiction-reality/) — convincing teams to spin out; Variant Bio created \"where none existed.\"\n- 2025-05-26 — Calcalist interview on defense — [link](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/end2evyv9) — sector exclusions, $200M defense fund, Kela \"will be a billion-dollar company.\"\n- Undated — Cornell alumni profile — [link](https://alumni.cornell.edu/article/josh-wolfe-99-invests-in-human-ambition/) — first-check jealousy; \"Chips on shoulders put chips in pockets.\"\n- Guest podcast appearances (Invest Like the Best, 20VC and others): still not checked as of 2026-11-10. The web search budget ran out again, and a guessed 20VC URL returned 404. Barron's, WSJ and Bloomberg interviews (2025) were paywalled.\n\n## How they think\n- **Find what consensus hasn't priced:** \"Belief precedes understanding; capital chases conviction\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q2-2025-report)).\n- **AI value moves away from the biggest models:** toward open source, orchestration (\"the bottleneck is no longer computation but coordination,\" quoting Together's founders), memory, and edge inference ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q4-2024-report)).\n- **Bio-inspired efficiency:** he contrasts frontier training at \"600 megawatts\" with a 20-watt brain, and says brute force is not biology ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report)).\n- **Defense as a core sector:** Anduril, Saildrone, Hadrian, and Israeli defense tech (Kela, Covenant and others) ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report)).\n- **Calls:** he called open source the winner after DeepSeek (Feb 2025). Exits since then include EvolutionaryScale to CZI and Chronosphere to Palo Alto Networks ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q4-2025-report)). Whether the closed-lab bear case is right is still unresolved.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Lux Q1 2026 Report: Flourish, edge AI, dry powder and venture concentration ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report)).\n2. Lux Q3 2025 Report: frontier labs as \"collectively irrational\", Valthos, edge chips, and the Conway origin story ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report)).\n3. Lux Q4 2024 Report: the post-DeepSeek open-source thesis and the closed-lab business-model risk ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q4-2024-report)).\n4. Riskgaming with Scott Wilson (2022): how an LP uses him ([src](https://blog.riskgaming.com/p/shredding-the-lp-asset-management-playbook-with-scott-wilson-cio-of-washington-university-in-st-louis)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked. X was not read this session.\n\n## Portfolio\nFirm-level Lux deals unless noted. Lux attributes deals per partner only loosely, through site cards.\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Anduril | Lux investment; bio calls Lux \"first investor\" | 2017 | Not stated | — | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/anduril), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report); Wolfe + Reeves per [Reeves page](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves) |\n| Flourish | Incubated in Lux; ~$500M raised \"from inception\" | ≤2026-05 | Lux incubated | not found | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report) |\n| Valthos | Lux Labs de novo | 2025 | Lux co-founded | not found | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report); Wolfe + Reeves site card [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/valthos) |\n| Sakana AI | Founding round ($30M) | 2024-01 | Lux led | Khosla, NTT | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q4-2024-report), [firm file](../firms/lux-capital.md) |\n| Together, Hugging Face, Cognition, Physical Intelligence, Runway, Applied Intuition, Hadrian, Impulse Space, Osmo, Erebor, Eikon, Variant Bio, Chronosphere, Auris | Listed in Wolfe's bio as first-investor companies (firm-level) | various | — | — | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/josh-wolfe) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Series A ($1B, reported $6B valuation) | 2026-10-21 | Co-led (Lux with Index); Wolfe's role not stated | Sequoia, GV, Kleiner Perkins, Valor, A*, NVIDIA, Atreides | [src](https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/) |\n| Crosby | Series B ($60M) | 2026-03-31 | Lux led (partner not named) | not stated | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/were-backing-crosby-to-end-the-billiable-hour-for-laywers) |\n| Variant Bio | Lux company creation | not stated | Lux created | — | [src](https://fortune.com/2024/11/01/luxs-josh-wolfe-on-launching-lux-labs-and-making-science-fiction-reality/) |\n| Kela | Israeli defense tech | not stated | unknown | — | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/end2evyv9) |\n| Mythic, Majestic | Edge inference chips | not found | — | — | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Flourish:** Thomas Reardon (CTRL-labs, Internet Explorer) and team founded it inside Lux on a thesis \"that begins where the AI consensus ends.\" It raised about $500M from inception ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report)). Reardon is also a Lux venture partner ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people)). This is a repeat founder: Lux's CTRL-labs investment ended in the Meta sale ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q2-2025-report)). The lead and syndicate are unknown.\n- **Flapping Airplanes:** Index led the $180M seed (at a reported $1.5B valuation) and Mark Xu joined the board. About nine months later Lux co-led the $1B A with Index. Reeves gave Lux's rationale: \"getting more out of what we have\" is the next frontier ([src](https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/), [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/)). That fits Wolfe's efficiency-over-capex thesis. How Lux got in, and whether Wolfe was involved, is not sourced.\n- **Valthos:** co-founded by Lux Labs with Kathleen McMahon, formerly head of life sciences at Palantir ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report)).\n- **Lux itself:** Conway's capital \"put us in business.\" The letter's wording says someone \"led us to\" Conway; the introducer was not captured ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report)).\n- **WashU to Lux:** the two were linked through overlap in an early automated-manufacturing company (unnamed). WashU now asks Wolfe for Lux's top five portfolio companies ([src](https://blog.riskgaming.com/p/shredding-the-lp-asset-management-playbook-with-scott-wilson-cio-of-washington-university-in-st-louis)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lux Capital | Co-founder, Partner | 2000 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josh_Wolfe) |\n| Peter Hébert, Robert Paull | Lux co-founders | 2000 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josh_Wolfe) |\n| Scott Wilson (WashU) | LP; asked him how Lux sources ideas | by 2022 | [src](https://blog.riskgaming.com/p/shredding-the-lp-asset-management-playbook-with-scott-wilson-cio-of-washington-university-in-st-louis) |\n| Bill Conway (Carlyle) | Early capital that started Lux | 2000s | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report) |\n| Thomas Reardon | Flourish founder (incubated at Lux); CTRL-labs | — | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report) |\n| Brandon Reeves | Co-listed on Anduril and Valthos | 2017 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/valthos) |\n| Brett McGurk | Co-author; Lux venture partner | 2025 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news) |\n| Danny Crichton | Co-host, Riskgaming | 2022 | [src](https://blog.riskgaming.com/p/shredding-the-lp-asset-management-playbook-with-scott-wilson-cio-of-washington-university-in-st-louis) |\n| Kathleen McMahon | Valthos co-founder with Lux Labs | 2025 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report) |\n| Index Ventures | Co-lead with Lux on Flapping Airplanes A (firm-level) | 2026-10 | [src](https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/) |\n| Dominick Auci | High-school lab mentor | pre-1995 (approx.) | [src](https://alumni.cornell.edu/article/josh-wolfe-99-invests-in-human-ambition/) |\n| Santa Fe Institute | Trustee | — | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/josh-wolfe) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Flapping Airplanes: who at Lux sourced the A, whether Wolfe was involved, and whether Lux tried for the seed.\n- Crosby: who led the seed and A, and whether Lux was in before the B.\n- Nova (nova.nexus, \"a global talent investing firm\"): is it the \"Nova\" in Flapping Airplanes' A syndicate? Unconfirmed.\n- Flourish: founding date, round structure, who co-invested in the ~$500M, and whether outside seed checks were possible. This is the top THESIS item.\n- Mythic and Majestic: Lux's round, timing and lead.\n- Wolfe's personal board seats and lead attributions beyond Anduril and Valthos.\n- Guest podcasts (Invest Like the Best, 20VC and others) and X posts, for verbatim rules of thumb and passes. Not checked: search budget exhausted.\n- Who \"led us to\" Bill Conway.\n- Which automated-manufacturing company linked WashU and Lux.\n\n## Sources\n- https://blog.riskgaming.com/p/shredding-the-lp-asset-management-playbook-with-scott-wilson-cio-of-washington-university-in-st-louis\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/people/josh-wolfe\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q4-2025-report\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q2-2025-report\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q4-2024-report\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/anduril\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/valthos\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/people/brandon-reeves (via firms/lux-capital.md)\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josh_Wolfe (secondary)\n- https://alumni.cornell.edu/article/josh-wolfe-99-invests-in-human-ambition/\n- https://fortune.com/2024/11/01/luxs-josh-wolfe-on-launching-lux-labs-and-making-science-fiction-reality/\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/end2evyv9\n- https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116281/witnesses/HHRG-118-ZS00-Wstate-WolfeJ-20230726.pdf\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/announcing-lux-ventures-ix\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/were-backing-crosby-to-end-the-billiable-hour-for-laywers\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/\n- https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/\n- https://www.nova.nexus/\n", "person:josh-woods": "\n# Josh Woods\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the person whose name Google put on its TPU 8 launch, which makes him the most public evaluator of non-Nvidia AI compute at any trading firm. He buys and evaluates compute and does not allocate capital.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** Citadel Securities is Google's showcase trading customer for TPUs. Woods's quote in the TPU 8 launch post is the firm's on-record verdict on Ironwood [src](https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/infrastructure-and-cloud/google-cloud/eighth-generation-tpu-agentic-era/). He runs the technology teams that build the firm's trading systems [src](https://www.citadelsecurities.com/who-we-are/leadership/joshua-woods/).\n- **What constrains him:** The firm rents compute from hyperscalers rather than building or investing. Its president, Jim Esposito, frames the strategy as using AWS, Azure and Google, with Google as the focus [src](https://uk.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/highfrequency-traders-race-to-build-ai-computing-power-93CH-4927634).\n- **What he would not do (as far as sources show):** write venture checks. No investment by Citadel Securities or by Woods personally was found.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The workload is training, not just inference or latency.** His quote ends on transforming \"our training economics and push the boundaries of scalability\" [src](https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/infrastructure-and-cloud/google-cloud/eighth-generation-tpu-agentic-era/). This is a market maker committing on the record to TPU 8t and 8i on announcement day (2026-04-22), before general availability. The quote is an image in Google's post (alt text \"Quote from Josh Woods, CTO, Citadel Securities\"), so it is easy to miss in text search.\n- **The numbers are his, not just Google's.** \"two to four times faster and at a 30% lower cost\" on Ironwood (same source). Reuters later attributed the same figures to the firm without naming him [src](https://uk.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/highfrequency-traders-race-to-build-ai-computing-power-93CH-4927634).\n- **Who runs the platform day to day.** Google's 2024 case study was co-bylined by **Costas Bekas, Head of Research Platform**, not Woods. It says the Google relationship began in 2017 and the platform can spin up over 1 million cores [src](https://cloud.google.com/transform/citadel-securities-reimagine-quantitative-reseach-cloud-scale-speed). At the firm's 2026-10-26 conference, **Jeff Maurone, Technology COO**, gave the scale: more than 500 quant researchers and over 200 PB of data [src](https://www.citadelsecurities.com/news-and-insights/future-of-global-markets-2026-conference/technology-scale-execution/). For a chip pitch, Bekas is probably the hands-on evaluator and Woods the sign-off. That split is an inference and unconfirmed.\n- **Market-structure seat.** He was appointed to the OCC (Options Clearing Corporation) board and its Technology Committee in 2024-06 [src](https://mondovisione.com/news/occ-welcomes-citadel-securities-cto-josh-woods-to-board-of-directors-2024610).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** He signs no checks, and Citadel Securities has no venture vehicle in the sources checked. He is useful as a **customer and diligence reference** for an alternative-chip or AI-compute company. His public quote is the strongest third-party evidence that a latency-obsessed trading firm will move training workloads off Nvidia. He is not a source of capital. No AI deal names him.\n\n## Background\n- **2003:** a sophomore in computer science at Iowa State, he was on the team that tied for 43rd of 70 at the 27th ACM ICPC World Finals in Beverly Hills [src](https://iowastatedaily.com/197599/news/students-produce-a-fair-showing-at-international-programming-contest). B.S. in Computer Science, Iowa State [src](https://www.citadelsecurities.com/who-we-are/leadership/joshua-woods/).\n- **IBM:** created IBM Connections Files and filed patents in programming languages and internet architectures [src](https://www.citadelsecurities.com/who-we-are/leadership/joshua-woods/).\n- **SaaS start-up:** led product and engineering, \"ultimately landing the team at Salesforce\" (same source). The start-up is not named, and whether he was employed by Salesforce is unconfirmed.\n- **Oracle:** engineering leader for infrastructure and cloud initiatives (same source). OCC gives the title as Senior Director of Engineering [src](https://mondovisione.com/news/occ-welcomes-citadel-securities-cto-josh-woods-to-board-of-directors-2024610).\n- **Citadel Securities:** CTO by 2022-10-25, when the firm published a recruiting piece with him under that title [src](https://www.citadelsecurities.com/careers/career-perspectives/what-draws-top-technology-talent-to-citadel-securities/).\n- Dates for the IBM, start-up and Oracle roles and for his Citadel Securities start are **unconfirmed**. One aggregator page (village.ai) reportedly lists Oracle 2014-04 to 2016-01, IBM 2009-08 to 2011-07 and Salesforce 2012 to 2014. That page could not be fetched (Cloudflare check), so those dates are left out of the rows.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.citadelsecurities.com/who-we-are/leadership/joshua-woods/ | Firm leadership bio (checked 2026-11-11) |\n| Substack / blog | none found | Searched 2026-11-11 |\n| X | none found | No account located |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | Not guessed |\n| GitHub | none found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | No podcast or conference talk found. The firm's 2026 Future of Global Markets tech session was given by Jeff Maurone, not Woods |\n| Board | OCC board of directors | Member Director and Technology Committee [src](https://mondovisione.com/news/occ-welcomes-citadel-securities-cto-josh-woods-to-board-of-directors-2024610) |\n\n**How a founder reaches him:** no public channel was found. The realistic routes are:\n- Through Google Cloud's TPU account team, since Citadel Securities is a named reference customer.\n- Through Costas Bekas (research platform), the operational owner of the compute.\n\nThese routes are inferred and unconfirmed.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2022-10 — Citadel Securities careers video — \"What draws top technology talent to Citadel Securities?\" — https://www.citadelsecurities.com/careers/career-perspectives/what-draws-top-technology-talent-to-citadel-securities/ — a recruiting piece about the firm's emphasis on technology. Not mined for compute views.\n- No podcasts or conference talks found.\n\n## How they think\n- Measures compute by price-performance and training cost, not by vendor loyalty. That is the framing of the TPU quote [src](https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/infrastructure-and-cloud/google-cloud/eighth-generation-tpu-agentic-era/).\n- Nothing else in his own words was found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Google's TPU 8 launch post, with his quote image (2026-04-22) [src](https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/infrastructure-and-cloud/google-cloud/eighth-generation-tpu-agentic-era/).\n2. Google Cloud's case study on the Citadel Securities research platform, by Costas Bekas (2024-04-10) [src](https://cloud.google.com/transform/citadel-securities-reimagine-quantitative-reseach-cloud-scale-speed).\n3. The Reuters piece on high-frequency traders building AI compute, quoting Esposito (2026-10-23) [src](https://uk.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/highfrequency-traders-race-to-build-ai-computing-power-93CH-4927634).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| none found | | | | | Citadel Securities is a Google Cloud TPU customer (2026-04-22), not an investor [src](https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/infrastructure-and-cloud/google-cloud/eighth-generation-tpu-agentic-era/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Google Cloud TPU adoption.** This was a vendor relationship, not an investment.\n  - The Google relationship began in 2017 on the research platform [src](https://cloud.google.com/transform/citadel-securities-reimagine-quantitative-reseach-cloud-scale-speed).\n  - Production on 7th-generation Ironwood TPUs came next, followed by Woods's on-record commitment to TPU 8t and 8i at Cloud Next on 2026-04-22 [src](https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/infrastructure-and-cloud/google-cloud/eighth-generation-tpu-agentic-era/).\n  - There was no \"first in\" or re-up, since no equity was involved.\n- The Nscale rounds credited to \"Citadel\" are not connected to him or to Citadel Securities in any source. They remain unconfirmed and are booked under `citadel`.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Citadel Securities | Chief Technology Officer | by 2022-10 | [src](https://www.citadelsecurities.com/careers/career-perspectives/what-draws-top-technology-talent-to-citadel-securities/) |\n| Oracle | Senior Director of Engineering (former; dates unconfirmed) | | [src](https://mondovisione.com/news/occ-welcomes-citadel-securities-cto-josh-woods-to-board-of-directors-2024610) |\n| IBM | Engineer; created IBM Connections Files (former; dates unconfirmed) | | [src](https://www.citadelsecurities.com/who-we-are/leadership/joshua-woods/) |\n| Options Clearing Corporation | Member Director; Technology Committee | 2024-06 | [src](https://mondovisione.com/news/occ-welcomes-citadel-securities-cto-josh-woods-to-board-of-directors-2024610) |\n| Google Cloud | Executive at a customer (TPU reference quote) | 2026-04-22 | [src](https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/infrastructure-and-cloud/google-cloud/eighth-generation-tpu-agentic-era/) |\n| Costas Bekas | Colleague; Head of Research Platform | by 2024-04 | [src](https://cloud.google.com/transform/citadel-securities-reimagine-quantitative-reseach-cloud-scale-speed) |\n| Jeff Maurone | Colleague; Technology COO | by 2026-10 | [src](https://www.citadelsecurities.com/news-and-insights/future-of-global-markets-2026-conference/technology-scale-execution/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did he join Citadel Securities, and was he hired as CTO?\n- Does Bekas report to him? Who signs compute contracts?\n- Has Citadel Securities evaluated non-Google accelerators (Groq, Cerebras, Etched and others)? Nothing found.\n- Which Citadel entity funded Nscale? Unresolved.\n\n## Note 2026-11-11 (pass 2)\n- **He was promoted from inside.** A firm interview dated 2018-05-04 calls him \"Head of Systematic Trading Technology at Citadel Securities\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260117043509/https://www.citadelsecurities.com/careers/career-perspectives/tech-career-roadmap-tech-vs-finance-josh-woods/). He was CTO by 2022-10. So the CTO seat came from running the systematic trading stack. The date of the change is unconfirmed.\n- **How he worked in 2018.** He spent his time \"discussing or reviewing technical details related to projects\", and he worked with the teams that \"drive requirements for our trading platform\" (same source). A compute pitch will be judged on its technical detail. The research teams that set compute requirements are the likely way in. That route is inferred.\n- **Google's TPU 8 post names only one customer, Citadel Securities.** The post's own text names no other customer, and Woods's quote appears only as an image (alt text \"Quote from Josh Woods, CTO, Citadel Securities\") [src](https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/infrastructure-and-cloud/google-cloud/eighth-generation-tpu-agentic-era/). Checked 2026-11-11.\n- **The relationship is also owned above him.** A day earlier (2026-04-21), **Peng Zhao, CEO of Citadel Securities** (per the speaker listing), gave a Cloud Next Leaders Circle session with Google's Amin Vahdat on the technology's \"transformative impact on Citadel Securities\" [src](https://www.googlecloudevents.com/next-vegas/session/3920366/the-physics-of-ai-infrastructure-for-the-next-era-of-innovation). For a non-Nvidia chip pitch, expect top-of-house sign-off as well as Woods's.\n- **Still no investment.** No deal names Woods, and no Citadel Securities venture vehicle was found. `data/rows/josh-woods-deals.csv` is header-only on purpose.\n- **Whose money:** his budget is technology and compute spend from Citadel Securities' own balance sheet. No outside LPs. Who approves large compute contracts is unconfirmed.\n- **Photo:** the photo URL in the front matter returned 403 to scripted requests on 2026-11-11 (the firm site blocks bots). Whether it loads in a browser was not re-verified.\n- **Checked with nothing new found:** the OCC board bio page and theocc.com press release (both 403), and web searches for any 2025–2026 interview, podcast or talk by him on AI compute.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.citadelsecurities.com/who-we-are/leadership/joshua-woods/\n- https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/infrastructure-and-cloud/google-cloud/eighth-generation-tpu-agentic-era/\n- https://mondovisione.com/news/occ-welcomes-citadel-securities-cto-josh-woods-to-board-of-directors-2024610\n- https://www.citadelsecurities.com/careers/career-perspectives/what-draws-top-technology-talent-to-citadel-securities/\n- https://cloud.google.com/transform/citadel-securities-reimagine-quantitative-reseach-cloud-scale-speed\n- https://www.citadelsecurities.com/news-and-insights/future-of-global-markets-2026-conference/technology-scale-execution/\n- https://uk.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/highfrequency-traders-race-to-build-ai-computing-power-93CH-4927634\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20260117043509/https://www.citadelsecurities.com/careers/career-perspectives/tech-career-roadmap-tech-vs-finance-josh-woods/\n- https://www.googlecloudevents.com/next-vegas/session/3920366/the-physics-of-ai-infrastructure-for-the-next-era-of-innovation\n- https://iowastatedaily.com/197599/news/students-produce-a-fair-showing-at-international-programming-contest\n", "person:joshua-leahy": "\n# Joshua Leahy\n\n## One-line take\n**Update 2026-11-11: he left XTX in spring 2026 (confirmed by XTX to press, 2026-04-13); see \"People pass 2026-11-11\". Older text below that says his title is unconfirmed or that he \"probably has not left\" is superseded.**\n\nThe engineer behind XTX Markets' compute (25,000+ GPUs, a €1bn+ Finnish data centre) and the only named XTX person on its AI-chip money. He is a technical validator and buyer of compute, not a venture check-signer, and his board role ended in March 2026.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** XTX Markets is one of the largest private ML compute users outside the labs. Its \"research cluster\" has over 25,000 GPUs and 650 PB of storage, and Leahy, as CTO, fronts the €1bn+ Kajaani build because the firm's \"need for compute has outgrown available leasing options\" (2025-01-22) [src](https://files.xtxmarkets.com/publications/kajaani/index.html). When XTX Markets (the parent, not XTX Ventures) put money into Tenstorrent's Series D, he was the one quoted [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-closes-693m-of-series-d-funding-led-by-samsung-securities-and-afw-partners). So on chip and compute deals his view counts as a customer's view.\n- **What constrains him:** he has no venture title. XTX's venture investments are approved by an investment committee that in 2020 was Gerko, Amrolia and \"the company's tech, quant, and regulation experts\" [src](https://qz.com/1923323/xtx-makes-venture-capital-investments-in-machine-learning-firms/). It is plausible but unconfirmed that he is one of those tech experts. The money is Alex Gerko's group balance sheet (see `firms/xtx-ventures.md`), not a fund with LPs.\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** lead a seed round on his own. Nothing ties him to an early-stage deal, and the one deal he is named on is a $693M late-stage chip round.\n- **Status flag:** Companies House shows he resigned as a director of XTX Markets Limited, XTX Holdings (UK) Limited, XTX Markets Trading Limited and Coloco Limited, all on 2026-03-30 [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/wvWAQmdsQXai0QfdJSGGeNyJIE0/appointments). He was appointed to the first three on 2025-09-25, alongside Bernard Denis III, Michael Irwin and Jonathan Nye, who are all still active [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/09415174/officers). His XTX Research LLP membership (since 2015-07-01) is still active [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/OC397853/officers), and he was committing code to XTX's TernFS repository as late as 2026-03-19 [src](https://github.com/XTXMarkets/ternfs/commits?author=jleahy-xtx). So he probably has not left the group, but **whether he is still CTO as of 2026-11-10 is unconfirmed**.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **A hands-on CTO who still ships code.** The GitHub account `jleahy-xtx` (\"Joshua Leahy\") has 44 commits on XTX's open-sourced exabyte filesystem TernFS. In March 2026 they included an NFS server, \"ternvfs first cut\" and integration tests [src](https://github.com/XTXMarkets/ternfs/commits?author=jleahy-xtx). That is a 13-year XTX/GSA veteran personally writing infrastructure code, which matters for anyone pitching compute or storage: the diligence will be done by someone who builds it. (The account name matches; that it is the CTO himself is a strong inference, not confirmed by XTX.)\n- **Chip-design interest predates the Tenstorrent deal.** A personal GitHub account `jleahy` (name \"Joshua Leahy\", London) forks RISC-V and hardware-verification tools: Ibex (a RISC-V core), riscv-formal, Verilator, and a transmission-line simulator for PCBs [src](https://github.com/jleahy?tab=repositories). It also has one commit on TernFS [src](https://github.com/XTXMarkets/ternfs/graphs/contributors), which links it to the XTX account. Tenstorrent is a RISC-V company; his Series D quote praised its open-source approach \"in the proprietary and often secretive world of AI accelerators\" [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-closes-693m-of-series-d-funding-led-by-samsung-securities-and-afw-partners). Linking the personal account to the CTO is an inference from name, city and the shared repo.\n- **He predates XTX itself.** He was a member of HFFX LLP from 2013-09-01. That LLP's designated members are Alexander Gerko and Dmitry Shakin, and GSA Capital Services Limited was a designated member until 2021 [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/OC358538/officers). XTX began in 2015 as a spin-out from GSA Capital. He then joined XTX Research LLP on its first membership date, 2015-07-01 [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/OC397853/officers). He is one of the small group who followed Gerko out of GSA. The person-match across the three Companies House officer records rests on the same name, the same birth month (July 1989) and the same XTX address; it is an inference.\n- **Infrastructure as a moat, in his words:** owning data centres lets XTX deploy compute \"on our terms, cost-effectively\" [src](https://files.xtxmarkets.com/publications/kajaani/index.html). He also says \"We are building ahead of our needs\". That is a buyer who prefers owning to renting, which matters if you are selling him inference or cloud capacity.\n- **Coloco Limited.** He was a director 2023-03-31 to 2026-03-30 of Coloco Limited, an XTX-addressed IT-services company that Gerko and Amrolia directed before him [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/13291939/officers). The name suggests a colocation or data-centre entity, but its purpose is unconfirmed.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low** as an investor contact. He does not run a venture book, is not named on any seed, and his only named deal is a growth-stage chip round. **Medium** as a technical reference and back-channel: if a frontier-AI seed company is building chips, compilers, storage or training infrastructure, XTX's CTO is a credible customer-diligence voice and a gateway to XTX Markets' balance sheet (Tenstorrent D and E, Gimlet Labs B). Deals that matter to me: Tenstorrent (ai-chips), Gimlet Labs (inference), and indirectly Flapping Airplanes, where XTX Ventures invested but no XTX person is named.\n\n## Background\n- Born July 1989, British (Companies House) [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/wvWAQmdsQXai0QfdJSGGeNyJIE0/appointments).\n- 2013-09-01: member, HFFX LLP (GSA Capital group LLP; dissolved) [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/VjLJJPhX38h7AmA-Lpsd__XIvfo/appointments).\n- 2015-07-01 to present: member, XTX Research LLP, the XTX staff partnership (still active on the register) [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/h1xNZW-oUN4Fh8AkPXKDlB_0KHc/appointments).\n- By 2024-12-02: Chief Technology Officer, XTX Markets [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-closes-693m-of-series-d-funding-led-by-samsung-securities-and-afw-partners). The date he became CTO was not found.\n- 2023-03-31 to 2026-03-30: director, Coloco Limited. 2025-09-25 to 2026-03-30: director, XTX Markets Limited, XTX Holdings (UK) Limited and XTX Markets Trading Limited [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/wvWAQmdsQXai0QfdJSGGeNyJIE0/appointments).\n- Education: not found. A 2013 fork of an \"Oxford term dates\" library on his personal GitHub hints at Oxford, but that is unconfirmed [src](https://github.com/jleahy?tab=repositories).\n- **How he got the seat (inference):** early technologist in Gerko's GSA-era team (HFFX LLP, 2013), then a founding-cohort member of XTX Research LLP in 2015, then CTO. It is an internal rise, not a hire.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | XTX has no team page (checked xtxmarkets.com 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.xtxmarkets.com/tech/ | XTX Tech Blog; posts are unsigned (TernFS post, 2025-09) |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| Hacker News | https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=jleahy | Self-identified as XTX CTO 2025-09-23; still commenting 2026-10 (chip layout) |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | not verified; web search budget exhausted |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/jleahy-xtx | Work account, name \"Joshua Leahy\", created 2025-09-02; 44 TernFS commits through 2026-03 |\n| GitHub (personal, inferred) | https://github.com/jleahy | Name \"Joshua Leahy\", London; RISC-V and verification forks |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was unavailable this pass (shared budget exhausted), so this is unchecked rather than confirmed absent.\n\n## How they think\n- Own the infrastructure rather than lease it: \"invest in our own infrastructure\" so XTX can \"continue to leverage\" it over time [src](https://files.xtxmarkets.com/publications/kajaani/index.html).\n- Build ahead of demand: \"We are building ahead of our needs to establish a backbone\" [src](https://files.xtxmarkets.com/publications/kajaani/index.html).\n- He values openness in accelerator vendors: Tenstorrent's \"open-source driven approach\" was the stated reason for backing it [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-closes-693m-of-series-d-funding-led-by-samsung-securities-and-afw-partners).\n\n## Read these first\n- Kajaani data-centre announcement (2025-01-22), his compute strategy in his own words [src](https://files.xtxmarkets.com/publications/kajaani/index.html).\n- Tenstorrent Series D release (2024-12-02), his only investment quote [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-closes-693m-of-series-d-funding-led-by-samsung-securities-and-afw-partners).\n- TernFS post on the XTX Tech Blog (2025-09), the infrastructure he helps build [src](https://www.xtxmarkets.com/tech/2025-ternfs/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public social accounts were identified; his public footprint is GitHub commits and two press quotes.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Tenstorrent | Series D ($693M+) | 2024-12-02 | no (investor booked as XTX Markets; Leahy quoted) | Samsung Securities, AFW Partners (co-leads); Fidelity, Baillie Gifford, Bezos Expeditions, LG Technology Ventures, Hyundai Motor Group and others | [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-closes-693m-of-series-d-funding-led-by-samsung-securities-and-afw-partners) |\n| Tenstorrent | Series E ($1.5B) | 2026-10-19 | no; XTX Markets re-up, **Leahy not named** | Trace Capital, Innovation X (leads) | [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-announces-series-e) |\n| Gimlet Labs | Series B ($300M) | 2026-09-04 | no; XTX Markets new investor, **Leahy not named** | a16z (lead), Hudson River Trading, Arm and others | [src](https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-b) |\n\nOnly the first row is a deal he is personally named on. The other two are XTX Markets (parent) deals in his domain, listed for timing.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Tenstorrent:** XTX Markets entered at the Series D (2024-12-02, $693M+ at a $2B pre-money per the repo row), with Leahy quoted, and re-upped in the Series E (2026-10-19), where no XTX person is named. The repo has no earlier XTX entry, so the D looks like its first round. Separately, the Head of XTX Ventures, Gabriele Papievyte, is listed by GCV as holding a Tenstorrent board seat (director or observer unconfirmed) [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/). So the CTO gave the technical endorsement and the ventures head holds the governance seat.\n- No intro-path or first-contact story was found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| XTX Markets | CTO (title sourced 2024-12 and 2025-01; current status unconfirmed) | unknown | [src](https://files.xtxmarkets.com/publications/kajaani/index.html) |\n| XTX Markets Limited / XTX Holdings (UK) / XTX Markets Trading | Director, 2025-09-25 to 2026-03-30 | 2025-09-25 | [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/wvWAQmdsQXai0QfdJSGGeNyJIE0/appointments) |\n| XTX Research LLP | Member (active) | 2015-07-01 | [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/OC397853/officers) |\n| HFFX LLP (GSA Capital group) | Member | 2013-09-01 | [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/OC358538/officers) |\n| Coloco Limited | Director, 2023-03-31 to 2026-03-30 | 2023-03-31 | [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/13291939/officers) |\n| Alex Gerko | Co-member of HFFX LLP and XTX Research LLP; XTX founder | 2013 | [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/OC358538/officers) |\n| Tenstorrent | Quoted backer (XTX Markets) | 2024-12 | [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-closes-693m-of-series-d-funding-led-by-samsung-securities-and-afw-partners) |\n\n## How a founder or co-investor reaches him\nNo public channel was found: no X, no talks, and no team page. The practical routes (inference) are (1) through XTX Ventures (Gabriele Papievyte), which handles inbound and holds the Tenstorrent seat; (2) through the TernFS open-source project on GitHub, where he commits personally; (3) as a compute or chip vendor, since he is the buyer behind the Kajaani build.\n\n## Open questions\n- ~~Is he still CTO?~~ Answered 2026-11-11: no, he left XTX (see People pass 2026-11-11). Still open: why, where he went, and why the directors appointed with him stayed.\n- Why is he still an active XTX Research LLP member seven months after leaving? Filing lag, or retained economics?\n- Who replaced him as CTO? No successor was named.\n- Does he sit on the XTX venture investment committee?\n- What did XTX Markets put into Tenstorrent D and E, and which entity holds it?\n- What does Coloco Limited do?\n- Education (Oxford is a hint only).\n\n## People pass 2026-11-11\n\n**He has left XTX.** Finance Magnates reported on 2026-04-13 that XTX confirmed by email that its CTO had left, and quoted Alex Gerko: \"Leahy has informed us of his decision to leave the firm\" and thanked him for his work at XTX \"since its inception\" [src](https://www.financemagnates.com/executives/after-more-than-a-decade-at-xtx-markets-cto-joshua-leahy-steps-down-also-as-director/). The trigger was the TM01 filed 2026-04-13 (\"Termination of appointment of Joshua Leahy as a director on 30 March 2026\") [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/09415174/filing-history). No successor and no next role were reported. This supersedes the \"current title unconfirmed\" hedges above: he was CTO until about 2026-03 (exact exit date not given; the directorship end is the dated anchor). His last authored TernFS commits are dated 2026-03-19 [src](https://github.com/XTXMarkets/ternfs/commits?author=jleahy-xtx); a later committer date (2026-06-16) on those commits reflects a rebase or push, not work in June.\n\n**The loose end: the LLP.** Companies House still lists him as an active member of XTX Research LLP (appointed 2015-07-01, no resignation) on 2026-11-11, and the LLP's 2026 filings record member changes for others (Gainullin out 2026-04-16; Chen in 2026-04-06; Jindel in 2026-08-14) but none for him [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/OC397853/filing-history). Press says \"full departure\". Both are stated here; which is right is unresolved. Possible readings (inference): a slow filing, or he keeps a partnership interest after his employment ended, which could come with a non-compete. Either way, do not assume he is free to found or join a competitor yet.\n\n**Where he went: a sourced negative.** The Companies House officer search shows three records for Joshua Leahy born July 1989, all at XTX's R7, 14-18 Handyside Street address, with 4, 1 and 1 appointments: the XTX/Coloco director record, the XTX Research LLP member record and the HFFX LLP record [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/search/officers?q=Joshua+Leahy). No new UK company directed by him appears as of 2026-11-11. A web search found no report of a new role or startup. Not checked: LinkedIn (no profile verified), US state registries, EDGAR Form D.\n\n**Identity confirmed, and a channel.** The Hacker News account `jleahy` wrote \"(disclaimer: CTO of XTX)\" in the TernFS launch thread on 2025-09-23 [src](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=45343790), and answered design questions about TernFS in that thread [src](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=45386171). This upgrades the \"inferred\" personal GitHub account `jleahy` (same handle, same name, London, one TernFS commit) to very likely. HN is now his only known public outlet.\n\n**What he is thinking about now.** On 2026-10-10, after leaving XTX, `jleahy` replied on an HN thread about hexagonal chip layouts: \"Sadly diagonal wires aren’t allowed by the fabs (at least on layers that would be relevant here)\" [src](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50037505). With his RISC-V core, formal-verification and Verilator forks [src](https://github.com/jleahy?tab=repositories), this is the only signal of his next move: silicon or compute infrastructure, not trading. It is one comment; treat it as a hint.\n\n**What changes for my lens.** As an investor contact for XTX he is now **gone**: XTX's Tenstorrent Series E re-up (2026-10-19) and Gimlet Labs Series B (2026-09-04) came after he left, which explains why no person is named on them. Route XTX chip and compute deals through Gabriele Papievyte (XTX Ventures) instead. As a **person to watch** he moves up: a 37-year-old (born July 1989) who ran the compute for a 25,000-GPU, €1bn-data-centre trading firm, with chip-design interests, and no announced next step, is a plausible future founder or technical co-founder in AI infrastructure or chips. That is exactly the kind of very early frontier-AI seed the lens targets. Lens fit raised to medium for that reason; it is a watch, not a contact.\n\n**How to reach him now (inference).** Not through XTX. Hacker News (`jleahy`) is public but not a messaging channel; a warm route is ex-XTX or GSA engineers, Tenstorrent (where he was the named backer), or the TernFS contributor list on GitHub [src](https://github.com/XTXMarkets/ternfs/graphs/contributors).\n\n**Deals.** Unchanged: the only deal he is personally named on is Tenstorrent Series D ($693M+, 2024-12-02), which was XTX Markets' first Tenstorrent round (no earlier XTX entry found). The Series E re-up (2026-10-19) is XTX Markets' alone, with no person named, and post-dates his exit.\n\nSources added this pass: https://www.financemagnates.com/executives/after-more-than-a-decade-at-xtx-markets-cto-joshua-leahy-steps-down-also-as-director/ ; https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/09415174/filing-history ; https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/OC397853/filing-history ; https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=45343790 ; https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50037505 ; https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=jleahy\n\n## Sources\n- https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-closes-693m-of-series-d-funding-led-by-samsung-securities-and-afw-partners\n- https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-announces-series-e\n- https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-b\n- https://files.xtxmarkets.com/publications/kajaani/index.html\n- https://www.xtxmarkets.com/tech/2025-ternfs/\n- https://qz.com/1923323/xtx-makes-venture-capital-investments-in-machine-learning-firms/\n- https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-gabriele-papievyte-xtx-ventures/\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/search/officers?q=Joshua+Leahy\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/wvWAQmdsQXai0QfdJSGGeNyJIE0/appointments\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/h1xNZW-oUN4Fh8AkPXKDlB_0KHc/appointments\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/VjLJJPhX38h7AmA-Lpsd__XIvfo/appointments\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/09415174/officers\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/OC397853/officers\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/OC358538/officers\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/13291939/officers\n- https://github.com/jleahy-xtx ; https://github.com/XTXMarkets/ternfs/commits?author=jleahy-xtx\n- https://github.com/jleahy?tab=repositories\n- Checked with nothing found: xtxmarkets.com home and news pages (no leadership names), the XTX Tech Blog TernFS post (unsigned). Web search was unavailable (shared budget exhausted), so LinkedIn, podcasts and talks were not searched.\n", "person:jules-qiu": "\n# Jules Qiu\n\n## One-line take\nA crossover-trained investor (Permira, then Coatue VP on Distyl AI, Persona and Miter) who moved down-title into Radical's San Francisco early-stage team in 2025. As of 2026-11-10 she has no Radical deal in her name, so she is worth tracking before her first lead, not after. [src](https://radical.vc/team/jules-qiu/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she could win:** She brings growth-fund pattern recognition from Coatue on enterprise AI. Distyl AI went from a 2023 seed to a $1.8B valuation by Sept 2025, with Coatue in the A and B (and the seed, per aggregator). She now sits inside a firm whose edge is pre-company formation with famous researchers. The firm bio frames her job as helping AI founders translate \"technical and product vision into generational companies\". [src](https://radical.vc/team/jules-qiu/) [src](https://pulse2.com/distyl-ai-175-million-at-1-8-billion-valuation-raised-for-helping-businesses-become-ai-native/)\n- **What constrains her:** She is a Principal behind about 13 named partners. Senior partners (Jacobs, Toews, Welch, Rosenberg, Katz) carry Radical's named AI deals. I checked all 279 radical.vc articles: she has one byline, a co-authored robotics essay (2025-08-25). No investment post, portfolio page or press release I found names her on a Radical deal. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/will-reinforcement-learning-take-us-to-agi-copy/) [src](https://radical.vc/team/)\n- **What she'd never do (inference):** Non-AI deals at Radical, since the firm is AI-only. Her Coatue names include a non-AI company (Persona, identity verification), so the AI-only filter is new to her.\n- **Pattern worth noting:** The Coatue-to-Radical move suits Radical's 2024–26 build-out of growth capital (Growth Fund I, then the US$1B+ Breakouts Fund). Coatue-trained underwriting is useful there, but the bio places her in early-stage founder partnership, not the growth team. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/)\n\n## Differential insight\n- **One byline in 279 posts.** I scraped every radical.vc article byline on 2026-11-10. Her only byline is the August 2025 robotics essay, shared with Molly Welch and Rob Toews. That makes the \"Welch–Toews robotics\" circle her apparent in-firm home. Radical's robotics deals in 2025–26 (Generalist, led $400M B in 2026, with Welch named) came from that circle. Her role in them is unconfirmed. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/will-reinforcement-learning-take-us-to-agi-copy/)\n- **Robotics thesis (co-authored, cannot be attributed to her alone):** The robotics \"GPT-4 moment\" may already be under way but invisible, because the field lacks benchmarks: \"there are no solid, standard benchmarks to measure progress in robotics\". The essay splits robotics companies into generalists and vertical specialists, and predicts robots could be as numerous as iPhones within a decade. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/will-reinforcement-learning-take-us-to-agi-copy/)\n- **The dinner that produced the essay** brought together Maven Robotics, Zeromatter, Dyna Robotics, Tesla, XDOF, Physical Intelligence and UCR. That is a list of who Radical's SF team was courting in robotics in mid-2025. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/will-reinforcement-learning-take-us-to-agi-copy/)\n- **Coatue Women in AI dinners (2023).** On LinkedIn she reposted Caryn Marooney's write-up of Coatue's Women in AI Dinner Series (NYC and SF, April 2023). This is a repost, not her own words. It points to a sourcing and community channel she likely helped run at Coatue (inference). [src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/jules-qiu-841132127_ai-nyc-sf-activity-7064702832171515904-O332)\n- **Down-title move.** She went from Vice President at Coatue to Principal at Radical. Moving down a title to get early-stage, founder-facing work at an AI-only firm is a signal about where she wants to be. Her motive is not stated in any source.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Radical is a high-fit firm for frontier AI: it was the founding investor in Cohere, Reka and CentML, incubated World Labs, and co-led Discovery Loop. Qiu sits in the SF office and has written on robotics foundation models, both close to my lens. But she has no named deal of her own, so there is no record yet. The deals that matter if she surfaces on them: Radical's robotics bets (Generalist), and any new SF neolab seed. Her Coatue-era Distyl AI exposure is AI-native automation, which matches my Serval-type interest. Watch for her first named lead as a signal that she is getting partner sponsorship.\n\n## Background\n- Harvard College, joint degree in math and history, magna cum laude [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20250909033856/https://radical.vc/team/jules-qiu/). The aggregator RocketReach gives 2015–2019 and Phi Beta Kappa (unverified) [src](https://rocketreach.co/jules-qiu-email_110012413).\n- Permira, investment professional, 2021–22 (aggregator; unverified) [src](https://village.ai/person/jules-qiu-NTYyNDcwOTg3).\n- Coatue, Vice President (firm bio); she \"partnered with companies such as Persona, Distyl AI, and Miter\" [src](https://radical.vc/team/jules-qiu/). The aggregator dates this 2022–25 (from Aug 2022 per Village) [src](https://village.ai/person/jules-qiu-NTYyNDcwOTg3).\n- Radical Ventures, Principal, San Francisco. Earliest primary evidence is her 2025-08-25 byline; the 2025-09-09 Wayback snapshot already shows the title \"Principal\". The current page's WordPress publish date (2026-07-24) is a site rebuild, not a join date. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/will-reinforcement-learning-take-us-to-agi-copy/) [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20250909033856/https://radical.vc/team/jules-qiu/)\n- Pronouns: the Radical bio uses \"she\".\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/jules-qiu/ | Firm bio; checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | One co-authored Radical Reads essay (2025-08-25) |\n| X | nothing found | No personal X account found; the radical.vc bio page links only @radicalvcfund |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/jules-qiu-841132127/ | Handle seen on her 2023 Coatue-era repost; LinkedIn page shows 3,266 followers, 16 posts (fetched 2026-11-10). Search also returns linkedin.com/in/jules-qiu/ titled \"Investor at Radical Ventures\"; LinkedIn blocked fetching it, so which URL is canonical is unconfirmed |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Radical Talks is hosted by Molly Welch; no Qiu episode found |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. No Radical Talks episode features her (checked the radical.vc archive, 2026-11-10).\n\n## How they think\n- **Robotics (co-authored):** progress is hidden by the lack of benchmarks; winners will be either embodiment-agnostic generalists or vertical-first specialists; robot fleets could rival the iPhone installed base within a decade. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/will-reinforcement-learning-take-us-to-agi-copy/)\n- **Enterprise AI (inferred from her Coatue names):** applied, deployment-heavy AI (Distyl) and vertical software (Miter, construction payroll). That points to a services-plus-software, AI-native operations taste. This is inference, not her words.\n- Calls right or wrong: Distyl AI went from a 2023 seed to $1.8B by Sept 2025, with Coatue in the A and B (seed per aggregator); how much was her call is not documented.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Why Robotics' Breakthrough Moment Could Already Be Here (Welch, Toews and Qiu, 2025-08-25): her only byline, and it shows the SF team's robotics map. https://radical.vc/articles/will-reinforcement-learning-take-us-to-agi-copy/\n2. Radical team bio: how the firm positions her. https://radical.vc/team/jules-qiu/\n3. Introducing the Radical Breakouts Fund: context for why Radical hires growth-trained investors. https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/\n\n## Social engagement\nLittle public activity. On LinkedIn she reposted Coatue's Women in AI dinner write-up by Caryn Marooney (2023). The search index also shows a 2022 post about Stability AI's $1B raise (Coatue co-led); that post now returns 404, so its content is unverified. No X presence found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures deals | none named | — | — | — | 279 radical.vc article bylines and 62 portfolio pages checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Distyl AI (at Coatue) | Undisclosed (bio names no round) | Coatue tenure 2022–25 | unknown | Coatue in seed (2023), Series A ($20M, Nov 2024, Lightspeed led) and Series B ($175M at $1.8B, Sep 2025, Khosla and Lightspeed co-led) | [bio](https://radical.vc/team/jules-qiu/), [A](https://finsmes.com/2024/11/distyl-raises-20m-in-series-a-funding.html), [B](https://pulse2.com/distyl-ai-175-million-at-1-8-billion-valuation-raised-for-helping-businesses-become-ai-native/) |\n| Persona (at Coatue) | Undisclosed (bio names no round) | Coatue tenure 2022–25 | unknown | Coatue returning backer in Series D ($200M at $2B, 2025-04-30; Founders Fund and Ribbit co-led) | [bio](https://radical.vc/team/jules-qiu/), [D](https://siliconangle.com/?p=694627) |\n| Miter (at Coatue) | Undisclosed (bio names no round) | Coatue tenure 2022–25 | unknown | Coatue and Bessemer co-led a $23M round (2025-05); Battery led $40M B (2026-09) with Coatue | [bio](https://radical.vc/team/jules-qiu/), [src](https://www.miter.com/resources/miter-raises-38m-from-bessemer-and-coatue-to-help-contractors-build-with-confidence/) |\n\nThe bio says she \"partnered with\" these companies but names no round. The rounds above are Coatue's rounds in each company, not proof that she worked on any one of them.\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. No intro path, first contact or board role is documented for any deal. No source says whether she was first in or doubled down on anything.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Principal, SF | by 2025-08 | [src](https://radical.vc/team/jules-qiu/) |\n| Coatue | Vice President (formerly) | 2022 (aggregator) | [src](https://radical.vc/team/jules-qiu/) |\n| Permira | Investment professional (formerly; aggregator) | 2021 | [src](https://village.ai/person/jules-qiu-NTYyNDcwOTg3) |\n| Molly Welch | Co-author, robotics essay | 2025-08 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/will-reinforcement-learning-take-us-to-agi-copy/) |\n| Rob Toews | Co-author, robotics essay | 2025-08 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/will-reinforcement-learning-take-us-to-agi-copy/) |\n| Caryn Marooney (Coatue) | Reposted her Women in AI dinner write-up | 2023 | [src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/jules-qiu-841132127_ai-nyc-sf-activity-7064702832171515904-O332) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which Radical deals has she worked on since 2025? Check Generalist, Yutori, Spara, P-1 AI and Outset announcements for her name, and the 2026 Masterclass cohort.\n- Exact Radical join date (LinkedIn is blocked; the Wayback bound is 2025-09-09, the byline 2025-08-25).\n- Which Coatue rounds she worked on in Distyl, Persona and Miter, and whether she held board observer seats.\n- Was she on Coatue's Stability AI deal (the 2022 LinkedIn post is gone)?\n- Did she run Coatue's Women in AI dinner series, and has she continued a similar format at Radical?\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/jules-qiu/ (current bio; WP API record 397, published 2026-07-24 in the site rebuild)\n- http://web.archive.org/web/20250909033856/https://radical.vc/team/jules-qiu/ (earliest archived bio, includes Harvard)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/will-reinforcement-learning-take-us-to-agi-copy/ (2025-08-25 essay)\n- Byline scrape of all 279 posts via https://radical.vc/wp-json/wp/v2/posts (2026-11-10)\n- https://www.linkedin.com/posts/jules-qiu-841132127_ai-nyc-sf-activity-7064702832171515904-O332\n- https://village.ai/person/jules-qiu-NTYyNDcwOTg3 and https://rocketreach.co/jules-qiu-email_110012413 (aggregators)\n- https://finsmes.com/2024/11/distyl-raises-20m-in-series-a-funding.html ; https://pulse2.com/distyl-ai-175-million-at-1-8-billion-valuation-raised-for-helping-businesses-become-ai-native/\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=694627 (Persona Series D)\n- https://www.miter.com/resources/miter-raises-38m-from-bessemer-and-coatue-to-help-contractors-build-with-confidence/ ; https://www.constructiondive.com/press-release/20260930-miter-the-ai-platform-for-construction-raises-40-million-for-the-built-w/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/\n", "person:julia-andre": "\n# Julia Andre\n\n## One-line take\nIndex's Paris-facing London partner, who heard about Mistral while it was still being incubated inside Alan's office. She remains on Index's Mistral relationship through the 2026 Series D. The rest of her book is mostly fintech plus one AI-native automation lead (Parallel), so she is a channel into the Paris AI founder network, not a frontier-lab specialist.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** She has access to the Paris founder network built around Index's existing French portfolio. The Mistral lead came while she and Jan Hammer were visiting portfolio company Alan, whose CEO Jean-Charles Samuelian-Werve was \"incubating an open-source AI startup called Mistral a couple of floors below\" ([src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise), 2025-09-09). Arthur Mensch then turns up as an angel in her 2026 Parallel Series A ([src](https://thenextweb.com/news/parallel-hospital-ai-agents-20m-index-ventures)), so the network runs both ways.\n- **Constraint:** Her book is dominated by fintech: Adfin, Apron, Codat, fomo, Hyperline, Moment, Montonio, Solvo, Spiko, Tema and Sylvera ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/julia-andre/)). On Mistral, Index was a participant in Lightspeed's 2023 seed, not the lead ([src](https://pulse2.com/mistral-ai-113-million-in-seed-funding-raised/)). Jan Hammer, a senior partner, is co-listed on Mistral.\n- **What she'd likely never do (inference from the portfolio, not stated by her):** A pre-training, chip or compute bet of her own. Her AI theses are about enterprise deployment (\"customized intelligence at its core\") and computer-use agents layered on top of existing software.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Deal origin.** Mistral reached Index through a portfolio company's office, not through outbound work. Alan's CEO was \"incubating an open-source AI startup called Mistral a couple of floors below\" ([src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise), 2025-09-09).\n- **Timing.** In 2026 she wrote that she met Arthur Mensch \"three and a half years ago\" (about early 2023), when \"Mistral had barely been incorporated\" ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/backing-mistral-from-first-check-to-europes-next-powerhouse/), 2026-09-08). The seed was announced 2023-06-20 with Lightspeed leading ([src](https://pulse2.com/mistral-ai-113-million-in-seed-funding-raised/)). Index was early but was not the lead.\n- **Why she committed.** She describes the first meeting as \"more than enough to convince us we needed to be part of that journey\". She says it was Arthur's conviction that drove it, not traction ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/backing-mistral-from-first-check-to-europes-next-powerhouse/)).\n- **How she frames frontier labs.** She frames Mistral by sovereignty and control, calling it \"the only EU-native frontier lab\". Her post also says \"control over intelligence systems is a critical strategic edge\" ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/backing-mistral-from-first-check-to-europes-next-powerhouse/)).\n- **Her agent thesis (Parallel).** \"Computer-use agents can now navigate existing software the way a human would.\" On this view there is no integration or rip-and-replace, and she calls Alan the healthcare exception that proves how hard the sector is ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/parallel-raises-20m-to-tackle-hospital-inefficiencies-with-ai-agents/), 2026-03-19).\n- **Founder read on Parallel.** \"Paul is a fast-moving operator with strategic long term vision\" ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/parallel-raises-20m-to-tackle-hospital-inefficiencies-with-ai-agents/)).\n- **Data trap.** Index's /companies/parallel/ page is the hospital-admin Parallel (beparallel), not Parag Agrawal's Parallel Web Systems. Shardul Shah is on the Web Systems deal, not Andre ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/parallel/)).\n- **Data trap.** Aggregators (Draftboard) credit her with Adaptive ML. Index's own Adaptive ML page lists only Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/adaptive-ml/)), and Fortune names Bryan Offutt as leading it. Don't credit Andre with Adaptive ML.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For high:** She is on Mistral, the one EU frontier lab, and has been since before the seed was announced. She led Parallel's $20M Series A in AI-native hospital automation (2026-03), which is the Serval-like category the thesis likes. She also has a two-way relationship with Arthur Mensch, who angel-invested in her deal.\n- **Against high:**\n  - Index followed Lightspeed in the Mistral seed rather than leading it.\n  - Jan Hammer is the senior partner on Mistral.\n  - No pre-training, chip or compute deals carry her name.\n  - Roughly 15 of her 17 listed companies are fintech, commerce or SaaS.\n- **Recalibration vs prior \"medium\":** Medium stands. She is useful as a co-investor and route into Paris AI founders (Mistral alumni, the Alan/Hexa/Frst ecosystem), not as a frontier-lab lead.\n\n## Background\n- MSc in engineering from Stanford, where she researched anomaly detection. A second engineering master's from École Nationale des Ponts et Chaussées ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/julia-andre/)).\n- At Palantir she led commercial deployments and analytics for enterprise clients ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/julia-andre/)). Crunchbase gives the title as \"Business Development and Execution Lead\" (aggregator, [src](https://www.crunchbase.com/person/julia-andre)).\n- A Runway Series listing says she designed a museum in Dubai early in her career and describes her return to VC \"after a break\" (aggregator listing, [src](https://languatalk.com/langua/podcast_episodes/1803231487); not verified against audio).\n- Joined Index Ventures in 2019 to focus on B2B SaaS and fintech across Europe ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/julia-andre/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/julia-andre/ | Index team page; no title shown (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/team-member/julia-andre/ | Index Perspectives; deal posts, about 1–3 per year |\n| X | nothing found | Team page links only @indexventures |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/julia-andre-4928145b/ | from Index team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- **2021-03 (date per aggregator listing):** Runway Series Originals ep. 46 (French), presented as \"VC & Principal at Index Ventures\". The topics listed are the Dubai museum and backing B2B/fintech founders ([src](https://languatalk.com/langua/podcast_episodes/1803231487)). No transcript was accessed, so nothing is quoted from it.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:**\n  - Enterprise AI as core infrastructure: \"every major company will need to have a customized intelligence at its core\" (2025-09).\n  - Sovereignty and control over data and costs as Mistral's wedge (2026-09).\n  - Computer-use agents that sit on legacy software in regulated sectors (Parallel, 2026-03).\n  - Financial infrastructure and tokenisation: Spiko, fomo.\n- **Calls she got right:** Mistral. She saw it pre-seed through the Alan connection, and Index has followed on through the 2025 Series C (ASML-led, €11.7bn post) and the 2026 Series D (aggregators report €3bn at about €21bn post).\n- **Contrarian views:** nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Mistral: AI for tomorrow's enterprise](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise) (2025-09-09): the Alan \"couple of floors below\" origin and her enterprise-AI thesis.\n2. [Backing Mistral, From First Check to Europe's Next Powerhouse](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/backing-mistral-from-first-check-to-europes-next-powerhouse/) (2026-09-08): her first meeting, conviction and sovereignty framing.\n3. [Parallel raises $20M](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/parallel-raises-20m-to-tackle-hospital-inefficiencies-with-ai-agents/) (2026-03-19): her computer-use agent thesis.\n4. [fomo's $75M Series B](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/on-chain-trading-goes-mainstream-fomos-75-million-series-b/): her fintech and crypto side (not read in full).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: there is no personal X account, and her public writing is limited to Index Perspectives.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mistral AI | Seed ($113M / €105M) | 2023-06-20 | No (Lightspeed led; Index participated, its \"first seed check\") | Redpoint, Xavier Niel, JCDecaux, Rodolphe Saadé, Motier, La Famiglia, Headline, Exor, Sofina, First Minute, LocalGlobe, Bpifrance, Eric Schmidt; Index co-lead on the relationship: Jan Hammer | [src](https://pulse2.com/mistral-ai-113-million-in-seed-funding-raised/), [src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise) |\n| Mistral AI | Series C (€1.7bn, €11.7bn post) | 2025-09-09 | No (ASML led) | ASML | [src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise) |\n| Mistral AI | Series D (record European round; aggregators: €3bn at about €21bn post) | 2026-09 | No (lead contested in aggregators) | existing investors incl. a16z, Lightspeed, ASML, NVIDIA (aggregator) | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/backing-mistral-from-first-check-to-europes-next-powerhouse/), [src](https://w.media/mistral-ai-raises-e3-billion-in-record-european-tech-funding-round/) |\n| Parallel (hospital AI agents) | Series A $20M | 2026-03-19 | Yes (Index led; Andre authored the post and is the only Index name on the company page) | Frst, Y Combinator, Hexa; angels Arthur Mensch, Vincent Bouvier, Felix Blossier, Quentin de Metz | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/parallel-raises-20m-to-tackle-hospital-inefficiencies-with-ai-agents/), [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/parallel-hospital-ai-agents-20m-index-ventures) |\n| Spiko | Series A $22M | 2025-07-17 | Index led (aggregator credits Andre) | not checked | [src](https://www.spiko.io/blog/spiko-raises-22m-led-by-index-ventures) |\n| Moment | not stated | not stated | not stated | | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/julia-andre/) |\n| Other listed (non-AI): Adfin, Ankorstore, Apron, Birdie, Codat, fomo, Hyperline, Jump, Montonio, Solvo, Strapi, Sylvera, Tema | — | — | — | — | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/julia-andre/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Mistral (2023):**\n  - Andre and Jan Hammer were visiting Alan's Paris office. Alan is an Index portfolio company where Index was an early investor. Alan's CEO mentioned he was incubating Mistral downstairs. They met Arthur Mensch, \"fresh out of DeepMind\", and decided to invest ([src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise); [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/backing-mistral-from-first-check-to-europes-next-powerhouse/)).\n  - Index came into the June 2023 seed as a participant behind Lightspeed.\n  - Index re-upped in the 2025 Series C and the 2026 Series D, and Andre wrote both posts.\n  - **Senior/junior pairing:** Hammer (senior) with Andre. She is the public author.\n- **Parallel (2026):**\n  - The company was founded in 2024 and went through YC W24. Its $3.5M seed was led by Frst in spring 2025 ([src](https://nordic9.com/news/parallel-raised-35-million-in-a-seed-round-led-by-frst-with-participation-from-y-combinator-bpifrance-kima-ventures-better-angle-and-business-angels/)).\n  - Index led the $20M A less than a year later. Andre is the sole Index name on the deal.\n  - Timing: Index was second in, after Frst and YC.\n  - The intro path is not stated. Mensch's presence as an angel suggests overlap with the Paris network, but that is not confirmed as the intro.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jan Hammer (Index) | Co-listed on Mistral; she calls him \"my partner Jan\" | ~2023 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/backing-mistral-from-first-check-to-europes-next-powerhouse/) |\n| Arthur Mensch (Mistral) | Met at incubation; later angel in her Parallel deal | ~2023 | [src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise) |\n| Jean-Charles Samuelian-Werve (Alan) | Alan CEO who surfaced Mistral to Index | ~2023 | [src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise) |\n| Paul Lafforgue (Parallel CEO) | Index led his Series A | 2026-03 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/parallel-raises-20m-to-tackle-hospital-inefficiencies-with-ai-agents/) |\n| Frst | Seed lead ahead of her Parallel A | 2025 | [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/parallel-hospital-ai-agents-20m-index-ventures) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Index's check size in the Mistral seed, and whether Andre is on any Mistral governance body.\n- Did Index join the Mistral Series A (2023-12, a16z-led) and Series B (2024)? The Index posts mention only the seed, the Series C and the Series D.\n- When was she promoted to Partner? No Index announcement was found.\n- What is the Parallel intro path: Mensch, Frst or Hexa?\n- Does she see Mistral-alumni spinouts in Paris? That would be the highest-value path for my lens.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/julia-andre/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/team-member/julia-andre/\n- https://indexventures.com/perspectives/mistral-ai-for-tomorrows-enterprise\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/backing-mistral-from-first-check-to-europes-next-powerhouse/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/mistral/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/parallel-raises-20m-to-tackle-hospital-inefficiencies-with-ai-agents/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/parallel/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/adaptive-ml/\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/parallel-hospital-ai-agents-20m-index-ventures\n- https://tech.eu/2026/03/19/parallel-raises-20m-to-scale-ai-solutions-for-hospital-administration/\n- https://nordic9.com/news/parallel-raised-35-million-in-a-seed-round-led-by-frst-with-participation-from-y-combinator-bpifrance-kima-ventures-better-angle-and-business-angels/\n- https://pulse2.com/mistral-ai-113-million-in-seed-funding-raised/\n- https://w.media/mistral-ai-raises-e3-billion-in-record-european-tech-funding-round/\n- https://www.spiko.io/blog/spiko-raises-22m-led-by-index-ventures\n- https://www.crunchbase.com/person/julia-andre (aggregator)\n- https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/JULIA-ANDRE-A2XJV6/ (aggregator)\n- https://languatalk.com/langua/podcast_episodes/1803231487 (aggregator listing)\n", "person:julia-hawkins": "\n# Julia Hawkins\n\nknown_for: LocalGlobe GP for health and science; firm credits her with CuspAI, CoMind and Parsed (sold to Baseten).\n\n## One-line take\nThe LocalGlobe partner to reach for science-heavy AI (materials, bio, health), not for frontier-lab rounds.\n\n## Crux\nPhoenix Court says she \"majoring in health and science\" and lists key investments Accurx, BioOrbit, CoMind, CuspAI, Early, Oxford Nanopore, Parsed (acq. Baseten), Robeauté, Spore.Bio, Vira Health, YuLife. [src](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team) Her bio frames the job as supporting founders and portfolio companies, so how much she sources versus supports is unconfirmed. The money is LocalGlobe's funds with outside LPs (e.g. LocalGlobe XII LP on the CuspAI seed, per the firm page); who approves is not stated publicly.\n\n## Differential insight\n- The firm credits her, not a frontier-AI specialist, with CuspAI and Parsed: \"Parsed (acq. Baseten)\". [src](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team)\n- Her bio lists no AI theme at all; the remit is \"majoring in health and science\". [src](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team)\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. CuspAI (AI materials, LocalGlobe in 2024 seed, Latitude in 2025 Series A per repo filings) and Parsed are on her list, so she is a possible co-investor for science-AI seeds. Check size is sub-$10M at LocalGlobe seed; she would not anchor a $20M seed alone (unconfirmed).\n\n## Background\nPrior: Goldman Sachs, Last.fm, BBC, Universal Music; \"24 years of experience as an operator, investor and advisor\". [src](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team) Dates, titles and when she joined LocalGlobe: unconfirmed.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team | Firm team page, 2026-11-10 |\n| X | https://twitter.com/svennj | Linked from firm page; activity not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/juliahawkins01/ | Linked from firm page |\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CuspAI | Undisclosed (her round unconfirmed) | | unknown | | [firm](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team) |\n| CoMind | Undisclosed | | unknown | | [firm](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team) |\n| Parsed | Undisclosed (acq. Baseten) | | unknown | | [firm](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team) |\nNon-AI key investments: Accurx, BioOrbit, Early, Oxford Nanopore, Robeauté, Spore.Bio, Vira Health, YuLife. [src](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team)\n\n## Deal stories\nNone found. No announcement I read names her on a specific round; board seats unconfirmed.\n\n## How to reach her\nVia the Phoenix Court team page links (LinkedIn, X) or a warm intro through CuspAI / CoMind founders. No public deal-flow address found.\n\n## Open questions\n- Which rounds of CuspAI and Parsed she led internally; board seats.\n- When she joined and whether she was promoted to GP.\n\n## What I checked\nPhoenix Court team page and bio JSON (found); localglobe.vc team URLs (404); Companies House officer search for \"Julia Hawkins\" (7 matches, none at a LocalGlobe/Phoenix Court address, so no directorships confirmed). Web search budget ran out before press and podcast searches.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team\n", "person:juliette-bolea": "\n# Juliette Bolea\n\n## One-line take\nShe is a Principal who arrived at Menlo around late September 2026 from Sound Ventures (Ashton Kutcher and Guy Oseary's fund), where Menlo says she led the Serval investment. That is a useful early read on AI-native automation. But no Serval round source names Sound Ventures at all, so her timing and entry price are unverified.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she could win.** She has a growth-investing background (Activant, New York) and spent her early-stage years at Sound Ventures. There Menlo credits her with leading four AI-era deals, including Serval, and with supporting Anthropic. She also sat on five boards ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/)). Menlo pitches her edge as narrative and go-to-market help: she \"works closely with founders to articulate why their products matter\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/)). She now has Menlo's balance sheet behind her (Menlo announced $3B in fresh capital on 2026-06-23 ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlo-turns-50-and-announces-3b-in-fresh-capital-to-go-all-in-on-ai/))).\n- **Premise check: the \"led Serval\" claim is not visible in round-level sources.**\n  - Menlo's bio says \"She led investments in Serval, Warp, WitnessAI, and Anomaly\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/)).\n  - Serval's Series A release itemizes Redpoint (lead), First Round, General Catalyst, Box Group, Bessemer and Chemistry \"and others\" ([src](https://www.ciodive.com/press-release/20251020-serval-raises-52m-to-eliminate-it-tickets-forever-with-ai-agents-1/)).\n  - Sequoia's long Serval origin story never mentions Sound Ventures, Kutcher or Bolea ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/access-granted/)).\n  - The repo's own Serval file shows Sound in none of the seed, A, B or C investor lists (`companies/serval.md`).\n\n  So \"led\" most likely means she led *Sound's* investment internally, not that Sound led a round. Which round Sound entered, and at what price, is unknown. The same gap holds for Warp (GV's post names no co-investors ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/ai-agent-warp))) and WitnessAI (no funding post found on witness.ai).\n- **Constraint.** She is new at Menlo: the team page went live 2026-09-28 ([WP API](https://menlovc.com/wp-json/wp/v2/teams/22475)), and no Menlo deals are listed. As a principal, she is unlikely to hold sole decision authority on a $20M+ check. Her mandate is \"consumer and AI-native applications\" ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/)), not labs, chips or pre-training.\n- **What she would never do:** nothing sourced.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Menlo join date is recoverable from the CMS.** Menlo's WordPress record for her team page (post 22475) has `date` 2026-09-28 and `modified` 2026-10-14 ([WP API](https://menlovc.com/wp-json/wp/v2/teams/22475)). This is a publish date, so it is an upper bound on her start, not a confirmed join date. No Menlo welcome post exists on Perspective pages 1–2. The only 2026 team announcement there is Alex Kurland's partner post (2026-09-08) ([src](https://menlovc.com/perspective/welcome-alex-kurland-menlos-newest-partner/)).\n- **Sound is absent from Serval's itemized Series A list:** \"participation from First Round, General Catalyst, Box Group, Bessemer Venture Partners, Chemistry\" ([src](https://www.ciodive.com/press-release/20251020-serval-raises-52m-to-eliminate-it-tickets-forever-with-ai-agents-1/), 2025-10-20). Sound may be among the unnamed \"others\", or it may have come in via a later secondary or an SPV. All unconfirmed.\n- **Candidate (unconfirmed) for Serval's first term sheet.** Sequoia says the first offer came in early 2024 from \"a small early stage firm, impressed by Stauch and McLeod's track record\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/access-granted/)). The firm is unnamed. Sound Ventures is a plausible candidate alongside Chemistry, Box Group and ALT Capital. No source links Sound to it, so this is recorded as `needs-id`.\n- **Board load before Menlo.** The Menlo bio says she served on five boards at Sound but doesn't name them ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/)). That is a lot of board work for a non-partner at a celebrity-anchored fund.\n- **No podcasts, Substack, talks or posts by her were found.** Her X account ([x.com/BoleaJuliette](https://x.com/BoleaJuliette)) could not be read (HTTP 402). The web-search budget for this run was exhausted, so podcast and press coverage was not searched. This section is thin for that reason, not because nothing exists.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The owner's roster lead rated her high. I'm marking her down to medium for these reasons.\n- **For:** Serval is the THESIS's named example of AI-native automation, and Menlo credits her with leading Sound's investment in it. She also touched Anthropic (support role, Sound in the May 2023 Series C ([src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-series-c))). She now sits at a $3B-fresh-capital firm that can write $10M+ checks, and she is a principal, which is the alpha segment the owner wants.\n- **Against:**\n  - Her mandate is consumer and AI-native apps, not labs, chips or pre-training.\n  - She has no Menlo deals yet.\n  - Promotion dates are not found, so speed is unknown.\n  - Serval timing is unverified, but that is an open question, not a lens input. Even without it, the apps-only mandate keeps her at medium.\n- **Deals that matter:** Serval (high fit), WitnessAI (AI security, medium), Warp (agentic dev tooling, medium), Anthropic (support role only).\n- **Useful as:** an early-stage AI-apps co-investor at Menlo, and a bridge to Sound Ventures' deal flow and to Kutcher and Oseary.\n\n## Background\nAll facts below are from the Menlo bio ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/)), which gives no dates.\n- **Education:** University of Southern California.\n- **Personal:** grew up in Miami; bilingual in English and Spanish; dual U.S./Mexico citizen.\n- **Lazard:** began her career there. Dates and group not found.\n- **Activant Capital, New York:** \"invested in growth-stage technology companies\". She is not on Activant's current team page ([src](https://activantcapital.com/team)). Dates not found.\n- **Sound Ventures:** worked with Ashton Kutcher and Guy Oseary. Led Serval, Warp, WitnessAI and Anomaly; supported Anthropic among others; served on five boards. Title and dates not found.\n- **Menlo Ventures:** Principal. Team page published 2026-09-28 ([WP API](https://menlovc.com/wp-json/wp/v2/teams/22475)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/ | Menlo bio; page published 2026-09-28 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | No Menlo Perspective posts by her as of 2026-11-10 |\n| X | https://x.com/BoleaJuliette | linked from the Menlo bio; not readable (402); follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/juliette-bolea-a7248b119 | linked from the Menlo bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Search budget was exhausted this run, so this is unsearched rather than confirmed empty.\n\n## How they think\n- **Thesis (per firm bio):** early-stage consumer and AI-native applications. Her value-add is storytelling, positioning and go-to-market ([src](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/)).\n- **How she sources:** nothing found. No primary source explains how she finds deals. The Menlo bio describes only her post-investment help (narrative, positioning, go-to-market).\n- **Firm co-signers:** no source names a Sound Ventures co-signer on Serval, Warp, WitnessAI or Anomaly.\n- **Contrarian views:** nothing found.\n- **Calls right or wrong:** Serval went from a 2024 seed to a $3B Series C on 2026-10-29 (`companies/serval.md`, [src](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/serval-raises-150m-series-c/)). That is a strong call if Sound entered early, but the entry round is unknown.\n\n## Read these first\n- Menlo team bio: the only primary statement of her record ([link](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/)).\n- Sequoia, \"Access Granted\": Serval's origin story, including the unnamed first-term-sheet firm ([link](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/access-granted/)).\n- Serval Series A release: the itemized investor list without Sound ([link](https://www.ciodive.com/press-release/20251020-serval-raises-52m-to-eliminate-it-tickets-forever-with-ai-agents-1/)).\n- No writing of her own was found.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X was not readable.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Serval (AI-native ITSM) | unknown: Sound not named in any seed, A, B or C source | unknown | led Sound's investment per Menlo; round lead unknown | Seed: First Round and GC co-led; A: Redpoint led; B and C: Sequoia led | [Menlo](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/), [A release](https://www.ciodive.com/press-release/20251020-serval-raises-52m-to-eliminate-it-tickets-forever-with-ai-agents-1/) |\n| Warp (likely warp.dev, agentic dev environment) | unknown | unknown | led Sound's investment per Menlo | GV invested in April 2020 ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/ai-agent-warp)) | [Menlo](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/) |\n| WitnessAI (AI security and governance) | unknown | unknown | led Sound's investment per Menlo | not found | [Menlo](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/) |\n| Anomaly (needs-id: which company) | unknown | unknown | led Sound's investment per Menlo | not found | [Menlo](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/) |\n| Anthropic | Series C ($450M), with Sound participating | 2023-05-23 | no: she \"supported\" it; Spark led | Spark Capital led | [Menlo](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/), [Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-series-c) |\n| Menlo deals | none listed as of 2026-11-10 | | | | [Menlo](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/) |\n\n\"Anomaly\" is ambiguous. The repo's True Anomaly (space and defense, backed by Eclipse and Accel) is probably not it, but no source says either way.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Serval: was she first?** Unknown.\n  - Serval's first term sheet came in early 2024 from an unnamed \"small early stage firm\". First Round and General Catalyst then co-led a $4.5M seed (committed March 2024, closed April 2024) ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/access-granted/), `companies/serval.md`).\n  - If Sound was that firm, she was among the first in, about 16–18 months before Redpoint's A. If Sound came in later (an unnamed A participant, a secondary, or a pre-B check), she was not.\n  - No source settles it.\n- **Warp, WitnessAI, Anomaly:** no deal stories found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Menlo Ventures | Principal | team page published 2026-09-28 | [src](https://menlovc.com/wp-json/wp/v2/teams/22475) |\n| Sound Ventures | investor (title not given); led 4 deals, 5 boards | dates not found | [src](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/) |\n| Ashton Kutcher | worked with at Sound Ventures | dates not found | [src](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/) |\n| Guy Oseary | worked with at Sound Ventures | dates not found | [src](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/) |\n| Activant Capital | growth investor, New York | dates not found | [src](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/) |\n| Lazard | first job | dates not found | [src](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/) |\n| Serval (Jake Stauch, Alex McLeod) | Sound deal lead | unknown | [src](https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which Serval round did Sound Ventures enter, at what valuation, and for how much? Was Sound the unnamed first-term-sheet firm?\n- Which \"Anomaly\" is meant? And which five boards did she hold at Sound?\n- What were her title and dates at Sound and Activant? Without them, the promotion speed can't be assessed.\n- What is her first Menlo deal? Does she work on Anthology Fund (Anthropic) deals, given her Sound-era Anthropic support role?\n- What does she post on X? (It needs an authenticated read.)\n- Podcasts and press: unsearched this run because the web-search budget was exhausted. Rerun the search.\n\n## Sources\n- https://menlovc.com/team/juliette-bolea/\n- https://menlovc.com/wp-json/wp/v2/teams/22475\n- https://menlovc.com/wp-json/wp/v2/search?search=bolea\n- https://menlovc.com/team/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/welcome-alex-kurland-menlos-newest-partner/\n- https://menlovc.com/perspective/menlo-turns-50-and-announces-3b-in-fresh-capital-to-go-all-in-on-ai/\n- https://www.ciodive.com/press-release/20251020-serval-raises-52m-to-eliminate-it-tickets-forever-with-ai-agents-1/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/access-granted/\n- https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/serval-raises-150m-series-c/\n- https://www.gv.com/news/ai-agent-warp\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-series-c\n- https://activantcapital.com/team\n- Repo: companies/serval.md\n", "person:juriaan-duizendstraal": "\n# Juriaan Duizendstraal\n\n## One-line take\nJuriaan Duizendstraal is Index Ventures' man on the ground in Tel Aviv. He co-led the fastest AI-native automation deal in Index's Israel book (Wonderful: $34M seed, then $100M A about four months later) and he is the local partner tagged on Shardul Shah's large Israeli security and AI seeds (Enigma, NewCore, 7AI). He is not a frontier-lab or chip investor.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** physical presence plus Index's balance sheet. He says Index needs someone \"on the ground\" to win first deals in Israel, and he can offer founders $5M to $100M from one firm, so Index can lead the seed and then pre-empt the A (Wonderful) [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl).\n- **How the work is split:** on the Israeli security mega-seeds (Enigma, NewCore, 7AI), Shardul Shah writes the post, owns the founder relationship and takes the board seat. Duizendstraal is tagged alongside Mark Xu [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/), [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/securitys-agentic-era-starts-here-our-investment-in-7ai/), [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/rebuilding-identity-for-the-agentic-era-our-investment-in-newcore/). He is the named author on the smaller, earlier Israeli deals he sourced himself: Venice, Astelia, and Wonderful (co-authored with Hannah Seal) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/venice-security-emerges-from-stealth-with-33m-in-funding-to-redefine-enterprise-privileged-access-management-in-the-ai-era/), [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/astelia-raises-35m-to-turn-exposure-management-from-guesswork-into-ai-powered-precision/), [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/wonderful-secures-100m-to-drive-adoption-of-ai-agents-globally/).\n- **Constraint:** his geography and sector are narrow: Israel; cyber, AI and infrastructure [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl). On no source is he the lead name on a frontier lab, pre-training or chip deal.\n- **What he would not do (inferred, low confidence):** go after Israeli AI labs on his own. He calls Decart and Irregular promising, but the interview does not say Index invested in either [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Sourcing rule:** \"You need someone 'on the ground' to secure the first deals.\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl) (Calcalist, 2025-11-11)\n- **Check range for the Israel book:** \"Our investments range from $5 million to $100 million,\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl)\n- **Venice: met two and a half years before launch.** He met CEO Rotem Lurie in summer 2023 and says \"as soon as we spoke, I knew I had to partner with her.\" Index then led the $8M seed. Venice only came out of stealth in 2026-02, with IVP leading the A [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/venice-security-emerges-from-stealth-with-33m-in-funding-to-redefine-enterprise-privileged-access-management-in-the-ai-era/).\n- **Astelia: not first in.** \"I first met Alon, Astelia's CEO, just after their Seed round\". Index then led the $35M A [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/astelia-raises-35m-to-turn-exposure-management-from-guesswork-into-ai-powered-precision/).\n- **Wonderful thesis: go non-English first.** \"Most companies start in the U.S., but Wonderful did the opposite\" (it started in Israel, Greece and Belgium) [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl).\n- **Talent view: Israeli AI founders come from the military, not academia.** \"Many entrepreneurs emerge from military units with AI and cyber experience rather than academia,\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl). This matters to my lens: he does not frame Israel as a pre-training research talent pool.\n- **Israel's AI catch-up call:** \"Initially, Israel lagged slightly behind, but in the past year, it has caught up.\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl)\n- **How he got into venture:** after the Liberty Media fund, \"Then, I was introduced to Sarit Firon, who joined Team8,\" and he joined Team8's first fund. Index approached him later [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl).\n- **Pace:** six partners invest in Israel, completing 16 deals and $300M in three years, with seven new deals in 2025: \"This year alone, we have made seven new deals.\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl)\n- **Tessl co-author.** He co-wrote the Tessl post with Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas. Index's tie to founder Guy Podjarny goes back over a decade and runs through Gonzalez-Cadenas, not Duizendstraal [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/when-everyone-speaks-software-tessls-vision-for-ai-native-development/).\n- **Not found:** no podcast, X account, Substack or talk by him. The Calcalist interview is the only first-person source.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The deals that matter to me:\n- **Wonderful:** AI-native automation (multilingual enterprise AI agents). Index led the $34M seed (2025-07) and the $100M A (2025-11), which Duizendstraal co-led with Hannah Seal [src](https://www.newswire.com/news/wonderful-secures-100m-to-drive-enterprise-adoption-of-ai-agents-globally). By the $150M B it was valued at $2B [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/index-ventures-triples-down-as-wonderful-raises-150m-series-b-to-scale-its-hyper-local-enterprise-ai-platform/).\n- **Enigma:** a $71M seed for a new human-machine interaction company. Shah led it; Duizendstraal is tagged [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/).\n- **Tessl:** AI-native software development, $100M A [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/when-everyone-speaks-software-tessls-vision-for-ai-native-development/).\n- **7AI and NewCore:** agentic security.\n\nWhy not high: no labs, no pre-training and no chips. He is useful as a route into Israeli AI-native and security seeds, and as a read on which Israeli AI companies Index will pre-empt. An aggregator ties him to NextSilicon (HPC/AI chips), probably from his Liberty years, but I could not confirm it.\n\n## Background\n- Born in the Netherlands; Calcalist quotes him as half Dutch, half French. He grew up in Spain and has lived in Israel about 17 years (Calcalist, 2025-11). The Index bio says about 12 years, so that figure is probably stale [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl), [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/juriaan-duizendstraal/).\n- B.A. in Business Administration and Finance, Reichman University [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/juriaan-duizendstraal/).\n- Served about two and a half years in the IDF Finance Department. Founded a business consultancy for startups [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/juriaan-duizendstraal/).\n- Liberty Technology Ventures, a fund under Liberty Media: about 4.5 years, starting \"about a decade ago\" as of 2025 [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl).\n- Team8, first fund, after an introduction to Sarit Firon [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl).\n- Index Ventures: says Index approached him \"four years ago\" as of 2025-11. Calcalist reported his appointment as Index's first Tel Aviv-based Partner on 2022-11-09 [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl), [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rj11ytkkbj). He manages Index's Israel office, which opened at the end of 2022 [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/bkirsog00r).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/juriaan-duizendstraal/ | Index bio and portfolio list (2026-11) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/team-member/juriaan-duizendstraal/ | Index Perspectives where he is author or tagged |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/juriaan-duizendstraal | Linked from the Index bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-11 — Calcalist CTech interview (print, not a podcast) — https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl — covers the \"on the ground\" sourcing rule, the $5M to $100M check range, the Wonderful thesis, and the view that Israeli AI founders come from military units.\n- No podcasts or talks found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** go international from day one, non-English first (Wonderful). Agentic security and identity (Venice, NewCore, 7AI, Linx). Israeli serial founders are now more ambitious: \"Today's entrepreneurs operate on a different level\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl).\n- **Contrarian-ish:** he calls rising competition and valuations in Israel \"healthy for the ecosystem\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl).\n- **Calls:** Wonderful went from seed (2025-07) to a $2B valuation (2026-03) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/index-ventures-triples-down-as-wonderful-raises-150m-series-b-to-scale-its-hyper-local-enterprise-ai-platform/). Nothing found on calls that went wrong.\n\n## Read these first\n- Calcalist interview, 2025-11: his only first-person account of how Index works in Israel [link](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl).\n- Venice post, 2026-02: the deal he authored, with a sourcing timeline [link](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/venice-security-emerges-from-stealth-with-33m-in-funding-to-redefine-enterprise-privileged-access-management-in-the-ai-era/).\n- Tessl post, co-authored with Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas, 2024-11 [link](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/when-everyone-speaks-software-tessls-vision-for-ai-native-development/).\n- Wonderful $100M A post with Hannah Seal, 2025-11 [link](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/wonderful-secures-100m-to-drive-adoption-of-ai-agents-globally/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public X account was located, and he has no public posting footprint beyond Index Perspectives.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Wonderful | Seed $34M | 2025-07-02 | Index led | Bessemer, Vine Ventures | [src](https://www.newswire.com/news/wonderful-raises-34-million-seed-round-to-accelerate-enterprise-ai-22600316) |\n| Wonderful | Series A $100M | 2025-11-11 | Index led; co-led by Hannah Seal and Duizendstraal | Insight Partners, IVP, Bessemer, Vine | [src](https://www.newswire.com/news/wonderful-secures-100m-to-drive-enterprise-adoption-of-ai-agents-globally) |\n| Wonderful | Series B $150M ($2B valuation) | 2026-03-12 | No (Insight led); Seal and Duizendstraal co-wrote | Index, IVP, Bessemer, Vine | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/index-ventures-triples-down-as-wonderful-raises-150m-series-b-to-scale-its-hyper-local-enterprise-ai-platform/) |\n| Enigma | Seed $71M | 2026-07-27 | Index led (Shah author); tagged | Ribbit Capital (Micky Malka) | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/) |\n| Tessl | Series A $100M | 2024-11-14 | Index led; co-author with Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas | GV, boldstart, Accel | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/when-everyone-speaks-software-tessls-vision-for-ai-native-development/), [src](https://cityam.com/?p=2282039) |\n| 7AI | Series A $130M | 2025-12-04 | Index led (Shah author and board); tagged | Blackstone Innovations, Greylock, CRV, Spark | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/securitys-agentic-era-starts-here-our-investment-in-7ai/) |\n| NewCore | First round $66M | 2026-06-15 | Lead not stated (Shah author); tagged | Cyberstarts, Evolution Equity | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/rebuilding-identity-for-the-agentic-era-our-investment-in-newcore/) |\n| Venice | Seed $8M | not stated (after summer 2023) | Index led; Duizendstraal author | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/venice-security-emerges-from-stealth-with-33m-in-funding-to-redefine-enterprise-privileged-access-management-in-the-ai-era/) |\n| Venice | Series A $25M | announced 2026-02-18 | No (IVP led) | Vine, Holly Ventures; angels Assaf Rappaport, Dor Knafo, Gil Azrielant | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/venice-security-emerges-from-stealth-with-33m-in-funding-to-redefine-enterprise-privileged-access-management-in-the-ai-era/) |\n| Astelia | Series A $35M | 2026-02-24 | Index led; Duizendstraal author | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/astelia-raises-35m-to-turn-exposure-management-from-guesswork-into-ai-powered-precision/) |\n| Linx Security | $33M (stealth exit) | 2024 | Index co-led with Cyberstarts | Cyberstarts | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/linx-security-emerges-from-stealth-with-33m-in-funding-to-revolutionize-identity-security-and-governance/), [src](https://shalomtelaviv.com/linx-security-emerges-from-stealth-with-33m-in-funding-to-revolutionize-identity-security-and-governance) |\n| PointFive | Seed $16M; Series A $20M | not stated | Index posts on his page | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/team-member/juriaan-duizendstraal/3/) |\n| Ryft | $8M | not stated | Index post on his page | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/team-member/juriaan-duizendstraal/2/) |\n| Frame Security | $50M | 2026-05-11 | Index with Team8 and Picture Capital; his role not stated | Team8, Picture Capital | [src](https://fortune.com/2026/05/11/exclusive-frame-index-ventures-phishing-deepfakes-cyber-security/) |\n| Apex, Glow | not stated | not stated | listed on his bio only | | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/juriaan-duizendstraal/) |\n| NextSilicon | unconfirmed | | Aggregator only; possibly from his Liberty years | | [src](https://vcbeast.com/investors/juriaan-duizendstraal) (aggregator) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Wonderful (first in, then pre-empted the A).** Index led the $34M seed in 2025-07, in the company's founding year. About four months later it led the $100M A, which Hannah Seal (London) and Duizendstraal co-led [src](https://www.newswire.com/news/wonderful-raises-34-million-seed-round-to-accelerate-enterprise-ai-22600316), [src](https://www.newswire.com/news/wonderful-secures-100m-to-drive-enterprise-adoption-of-ai-agents-globally). Insight Partners joined at the A and led the $150M B in 2026-03, Index's third check in under a year [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/index-ventures-triples-down-as-wonderful-raises-150m-series-b-to-scale-its-hyper-local-enterprise-ai-platform/). The pairing is Seal, a London partner, for the European expansion and Duizendstraal for the Tel Aviv founders. That reading is inferred from geography and is not stated in any source. How Index first met Bar Winkler: not found.\n- **Venice (first institutional lead, long lead time).** He met Rotem Lurie in summer 2023, and Index led the $8M seed. IVP led the $25M A, and the company launched in 2026-02 [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/venice-security-emerges-from-stealth-with-33m-in-funding-to-redefine-enterprise-privileged-access-management-in-the-ai-era/). Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas is also tagged on the post.\n- **Astelia (second in).** He met CEO Alon just after the seed. Index led the $35M A [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/astelia-raises-35m-to-turn-exposure-management-from-guesswork-into-ai-powered-precision/).\n- **Tessl (second in).** Boldstart and GV led a $25M seed in 2024-04. Index led the $100M A in 2024-11 at up to $750M [src](https://cityam.com/?p=2282039). The relationship runs through Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas, who has known Podjarny since the Snyk days [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/when-everyone-speaks-software-tessls-vision-for-ai-native-development/).\n- **Enigma, NewCore, 7AI (Shah's deals).** Shah shook hands with Enigma's founders before incorporation [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/). Shah has known NewCore's Zohar Alon for over a decade, since Dome9 [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/rebuilding-identity-for-the-agentic-era-our-investment-in-newcore/). Shah takes the 7AI board seat [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/securitys-agentic-era-starts-here-our-investment-in-7ai/). On all three, Duizendstraal is only tagged; his role is not described.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Shardul Shah (Index) | Senior partner on Index's Israel deals; Duizendstraal tagged on Shah's Enigma, NewCore and 7AI posts | 2022 | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rj11ytkkbj) |\n| Mark Xu (Index) | Co-tagged on Enigma and 7AI | 2025-12 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/securitys-agentic-era-starts-here-our-investment-in-7ai/) |\n| Hannah Seal (Index) | Co-led the Wonderful A; co-wrote the A and B posts | 2025-11 | [src](https://www.newswire.com/news/wonderful-secures-100m-to-drive-enterprise-adoption-of-ai-agents-globally) |\n| Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas (Index) | Co-wrote the Tessl post; co-tagged on Venice | 2024-11 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/when-everyone-speaks-software-tessls-vision-for-ai-native-development/) |\n| Sarit Firon / Team8 | Introduction that led him to join Team8's first fund | not dated | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl) |\n| Liberty Technology Ventures | Prior employer, about 4.5 years | about 2015 | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl) |\n| Rotem Lurie (Venice) | Founder he backed after meeting in summer 2023 | 2023 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/venice-security-emerges-from-stealth-with-33m-in-funding-to-redefine-enterprise-privileged-access-management-in-the-ai-era/) |\n| Bar Winkler (Wonderful) | Founder; Index led the seed and the A | 2025 | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did he personally source Wonderful, or was it Hannah Seal? Who made the intro to Bar Winkler?\n- Was he involved in NextSilicon at Liberty? Several sources name a Liberty entity in NextSilicon's 2020/2021 rounds, but the round labels conflict [src](https://seedtable.com/companies/nextsilicon/funding-rounds/series-c-2021-06) (aggregator).\n- Does Index's Israel team look at Israeli AI labs (Decart, Irregular), or is it only tracking them?\n- Who are the other \"six partners\" who invest in Israel?\n- Glow (Sequoia, Cyberstarts and others co-led a $180M A, 2026-07) and Frame: is his role personal or firm-level?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/juriaan-duizendstraal/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/team-member/juriaan-duizendstraal/ (pages 2 and 3)\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rjoh6tllzl (2025-11-11)\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rj11ytkkbj (2022-11-09)\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/bkirsog00r (2024-07-15)\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/venice-security-emerges-from-stealth-with-33m-in-funding-to-redefine-enterprise-privileged-access-management-in-the-ai-era/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/astelia-raises-35m-to-turn-exposure-management-from-guesswork-into-ai-powered-precision/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/when-everyone-speaks-software-tessls-vision-for-ai-native-development/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/wonderful-secures-100m-to-drive-adoption-of-ai-agents-globally/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/index-ventures-triples-down-as-wonderful-raises-150m-series-b-to-scale-its-hyper-local-enterprise-ai-platform/\n- https://www.newswire.com/news/wonderful-raises-34-million-seed-round-to-accelerate-enterprise-ai-22600316\n- https://www.newswire.com/news/wonderful-secures-100m-to-drive-enterprise-adoption-of-ai-agents-globally\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/securitys-agentic-era-starts-here-our-investment-in-7ai/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/rebuilding-identity-for-the-agentic-era-our-investment-in-newcore/\n- https://cityam.com/?p=2282039\n- https://fortune.com/2026/05/11/exclusive-frame-index-ventures-phishing-deepfakes-cyber-security/\n- https://vcbeast.com/investors/juriaan-duizendstraal (aggregator)\n", "person:justin-selig": "\n# Justin Selig\n\n_Profile written 2026-11-10. Web search was unavailable for this run (budget used up), so this profile is built from his own site (justinselig.com), Eclipse blog posts, Efficient Computer's site and The Compute 100 podcast feed. Pronouns: The Compute 100 feed uses \"he\" ([src](https://anchor.fm/s/10f3acc34/podcast/rss))._\n\n**Seniority note:** `associate` is the closest value in the list, and it is unconfirmed. His only self-described VC title is \"Eclipse Investor\" / \"an investor over at Eclipse Ventures\" ([src](https://justinselig.com/2024/05/16/sf-hw-meetup)). No partner or principal title appears anywhere I checked. **Check capacity:** `sub-10m`. He has no fund today. The \"tens of millions\" he deployed at Eclipse is a cumulative total, not the size of a single check he could write.\n\n## One-line take\nWorth following as an insider node, not as capital. He was early at Cerebras and was inside Eclipse during its 2023 Cerebras rescue. He sourced Efficient Computer's seed, then left VC to operate there.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he has been on every side of an AI chip company. He wrote Cerebras kernels, pushed for and led the Cerebras SDK, sourced and diligenced chip deals at Eclipse, and now does growth and product at Efficient ([src](https://justinselig.com/2020/07/08/life-story)). His deal edge was his network from his engineering years. Efficient came to him through a former colleague who had joined it as an engineer ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/pioneering-energy-efficient-computing-our-seed-investment-in-efficient-computer)).\n- **Constraint:** he is no longer an allocator. At Eclipse he was an investor-level hire, and partners were the ones of record (Reichow on Efficient). He describes VC as communication and outbound sales and chose to go back to operating ([src](https://justinselig.com/2020/07/08/life-story)).\n- **What he'd never do (from his own advice):** back a chip startup that fights CUDA head-on as a general platform instead of going vertical ([src](https://justinselig.com/2024/05/16/sf-hw-meetup)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Cerebras pre-mortem.** In Feb 2023, shortly after joining Eclipse, he circulated a 50-slide deck titled \"Cerebras Pre-Mortem & Strategy Proposal\" to the firm and to key board members. He says Eclipse then \"intensified its involvement with the company.\" At the time Cerebras was burning cash and running layoffs ([src](https://justinselig.com/2026/05/18/cerebras)).\n- **How the G42 deal really happened.** By his account, \"Lior had made an introduction to G42 and organized talks\". The G42 deal was signed 2023-04-28 and made G42 Cerebras' largest customer ([src](https://justinselig.com/2026/05/18/cerebras)). This credits Lior Susan, not Cerebras management, with the turnaround intro.\n- **An Eclipse-incubated datacenter JV.** Behind the scenes he was \"leading talks of a datacenter joint-venture\". The JV was meant to make G42's Condor Galaxy available to the public ([src](https://justinselig.com/2026/05/18/cerebras)). That probably explains his line \"I was responsible for creating a new company\" at Eclipse ([src](https://justinselig.com/2020/07/08/life-story)). The link between the two is my inference, and I found no JV name or outcome.\n- **Investor to operator at the same company.** He sourced Efficient at Eclipse in early 2024. By Nov 2024 he wrote \"I run Product at Efficient Computer\" ([src](https://justinselig.com/2020/07/08/life-story)). Efficient's site now lists him as Director of Growth Strategy ([src](https://www.efficient.computer/about)).\n- **Compute contrarian takes (SF Hardware Meetup, 2024-05-16):** \"don’t try to compete directly with NVIDIA\". He also argues the datacenter power problem is \"not actually about the generation of power\" but about distribution, meaning utility line upgrades ([src](https://justinselig.com/2024/05/16/sf-hw-meetup)).\n- **Stage remit at Eclipse:** \"I focus specifically on Pre-Seed through Series A companies\" ([src](https://justinselig.com/2024/05/16/sf-hw-meetup)).\n- Already in the repo: the Efficient deal origin through a former colleague ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/pioneering-energy-efficient-computing-our-seed-investment-in-efficient-computer)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The deals are on-thesis: an AI-chip seed (Efficient, first institutional money, led by Eclipse) and an Eclipse-led AI design-automation seed extension (Augmenta). But he left Eclipse in 2024 and controls no capital now. His value to you is three things:\n- Diligence and reference calls on Cerebras, Eclipse, Efficient and AI-chip software stacks.\n- Access to the ex-Cerebras engineer network that produced the Efficient deal.\n- A view into how Eclipse (Lior Susan, Pierre Lamond) works hands-on on a struggling portfolio chip company.\n\nHe could become an angel or a scout, but I found no evidence of either.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Manhattan and went to Brooklyn Tech. BS and MEng in Electrical and Computer Engineering from Cornell. Internships at SpaceX (2016) and Uber ATG (2017) ([src](https://justinselig.com/2020/07/08/life-story), [sitemap](https://justinselig.com/sitemap.xml)).\n- **Cerebras, end of 2017 to 2022:** first new-grad hire in the hardware systems org. He cold-emailed VP Bill Lynch to get in. He built kernel software, pitched an external SDK in 2019, and led it across engineering, product, marketing, sales and support ([src](https://justinselig.com/2020/07/08/life-story)). The Compute 100 feed says he joined in 2018 and stayed 4 years ([src](https://anchor.fm/s/10f3acc34/podcast/rss)). I have not resolved the 2017 vs 2018 discrepancy.\n- **2022: Playground Global Fellow** while trying to found a low-field MRI company, which petered out ([src](https://justinselig.com/2020/07/08/life-story)).\n- **2022 to 2024: investor at Eclipse**, hired by \"Pierre Lamond’s firm\" ([src](https://justinselig.com/2020/07/08/life-story)). He was still at Eclipse on 2024-05-16 ([src](https://justinselig.com/2024/05/16/sf-hw-meetup)) and had left by the Nov 2024 update of his life story. The exact exit month is unconfirmed.\n- **2024 to now: Efficient Computer.** First Product, now Director of Growth Strategy ([src](https://www.efficient.computer/about)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://justinselig.com | Life story and blog; last post 2026-05-20 (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://justinselig.com/blog | A few posts a year; many are cross-posts of Eclipse blog pieces |\n| X | https://x.com/justinsselig | Linked from his site; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/justinselig | Linked from his site and from Efficient's JSON-LD; his site says LinkedIn is the best way to reach him |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/JustinSelig | Linked from his site |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-05 — The Compute 100 (Primary; Brian Schechter, Gaby Lorenzi) — \"Justin Selig, Director at Efficient Computer\" — [feed](https://anchor.fm/s/10f3acc34/podcast/rss), [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WmOyUQON1y0) — 57 min. I could not get a transcript, so the only description is the hosts' blurb.\n- 2026-03 — SBT Industries Podcast — his history and Efficient — [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t1hFQUKMAw4) via [his site](https://justinselig.com/2026/03/05/sbt-podcast). Not transcribed.\n- 2024-05 — 114th SF Hardware Meetup — AI infrastructure talk — [transcript on his site](https://justinselig.com/2024/05/16/sf-hw-meetup). Points that stood out: optics and cooling as the next datacenter cost centers, power distribution as the real bottleneck, and the advice not to fight CUDA head-on.\n- 2023-11 — Eclectic Convergence panel, Cornell Tech — \"What Makes a Business Venture Backable\" — [src](https://justinselig.com/2023/11/08/eclectic-convergence).\n\n## How they think\n- Infrastructure lags applications, and value goes to infrastructure that wins on the workloads that last. AI is broader than LLM linear algebra, so general-purpose compute matters ([src](https://justinselig.com/2024/06/21/eclipse-chip-startups)).\n- Large-scale classical HPC plus AI is underrated next to the GPU boom ([src](https://justinselig.com/2023/11/01/long-live-high-performance-computing)).\n- Make compute more efficient instead of patching power supply, which is the Efficient thesis ([src](https://justinselig.com/2024/05/16/sf-hw-meetup)).\n- **Calls he got right:** early conviction on AI ASICs in 2017. He also saw Cerebras' 2023 trough from the inside and lived through the recovery to its 2026 IPO ([src](https://justinselig.com/2026/05/18/cerebras)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Cerebras IPO: It Takes a Village](https://justinselig.com/2026/05/18/cerebras): the insider account of the 2023 Cerebras rescue (pre-mortem deck, G42 intro, datacenter JV).\n2. [My (Long) Story](https://justinselig.com/2020/07/08/life-story): the path from Cerebras to Playground, Eclipse and Efficient, and his view of VC.\n3. [AI Infrastructure Talk (SF Hardware Meetup)](https://justinselig.com/2024/05/16/sf-hw-meetup): where he thinks AI compute bottlenecks really are.\n4. [Top 10 Challenges Facing Chip Startups](https://justinselig.com/2024/06/21/eclipse-chip-startups): his AI-chip investing framework from his Eclipse days.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked: X could not be read without search, and I fetched no posts. His site points people to LinkedIn.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Efficient Computer | Seed ($16M) | 2024-03-07 | Eclipse led; Selig sourced it and co-authored the post with Greg Reichow | not stated in the post | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/pioneering-energy-efficient-computing-our-seed-investment-in-efficient-computer) |\n| Augmenta | Seed ($11.75M seed extension) | 2023-06-21 | Eclipse led; Selig co-authored the post with Ryan Gibson and Charly Mwangi (his exact role is not stated) | not stated | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/automating-building-design-our-investment-in-augmenta-2) |\n\n**Timing:** Efficient was Eclipse's first institutional round, so Eclipse was first in. Selig was gone before Eclipse re-upped in the Series A (2026-02, led by Triatomic) and Series B (2026-09, led by TQ), so there was no double-down on his watch. For Augmenta, I could not confirm whether Eclipse was in before the extension. Cerebras and Tenstorrent are Eclipse positions that predate him and are not his deals.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Efficient Computer:** a former colleague of Selig's joined Efficient as a digital design engineer and put the company on his radar. Eclipse then led the $16M seed. CEO Brandon Lucia and CTO Graham Gobieski came out of Carnegie Mellon ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/pioneering-energy-efficient-computing-our-seed-investment-in-efficient-computer)). Within months Selig had moved to Efficient himself ([src](https://justinselig.com/2020/07/08/life-story)).\n- **Cerebras, 2023 (not an investment by him):** the pre-mortem deck, Lior Susan's G42 intro, and the datacenter JV talks Selig led ([src](https://justinselig.com/2026/05/18/cerebras)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Eclipse | Investor (pre-seed to Series A) | 2022 to 2024 | [src](https://justinselig.com/2020/07/08/life-story) |\n| Cerebras | Kernel engineer, then SDK lead | 2017/18 to 2022 | [src](https://justinselig.com/2020/07/08/life-story) |\n| Playground Global | Fellow | 2022 | [src](https://justinselig.com/2020/07/08/life-story) |\n| Efficient Computer | Product, then Director of Growth Strategy | 2024 | [src](https://www.efficient.computer/about) |\n| Greg Reichow | Co-author on the Efficient seed post; partner of record | 2024 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/pioneering-energy-efficient-computing-our-seed-investment-in-efficient-computer) |\n| Ryan Gibson, Charly Mwangi | Co-authors on the Augmenta post and the AI product-design post | 2023 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/automating-building-design-our-investment-in-augmenta-2) |\n| Lior Susan | Worked alongside him on Eclipse's Cerebras intervention | 2023 | [src](https://justinselig.com/2026/05/18/cerebras) |\n| Pierre Lamond | Eclipse co-founder who hired him; Selig calls him a hero | 2022 | [src](https://justinselig.com/2026/05/18/cerebras) |\n| Bill Lynch | Cerebras VP who hired him | 2017 | [src](https://justinselig.com/2020/07/08/life-story) |\n| Brian Schechter, Gaby Lorenzi (Primary) | Podcast hosts | 2026-05 | [src](https://anchor.fm/s/10f3acc34/podcast/rss) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What was the Condor Galaxy datacenter JV? Was it formed, what was it called, and is Eclipse in it?\n- His exact Eclipse title and exit month (LinkedIn not fetched).\n- Does he angel-invest or scout for anyone now?\n- What did he say on The Compute 100 and SBT (no transcripts)?\n- Who was \"Taggart\", the VC friend who pushed him toward investing?\n\n## Sources\n- https://justinselig.com/2020/07/08/life-story\n- https://justinselig.com/2026/05/18/cerebras\n- https://justinselig.com/2024/05/16/sf-hw-meetup\n- https://justinselig.com/2024/06/21/eclipse-chip-startups\n- https://justinselig.com/2023/11/01/long-live-high-performance-computing\n- https://justinselig.com/2024/02/14/augmenta-software\n- https://justinselig.com/sitemap.xml\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/pioneering-energy-efficient-computing-our-seed-investment-in-efficient-computer\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/automating-building-design-our-investment-in-augmenta-2\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/designing-physical-products-in-the-ai-era\n- https://www.efficient.computer/about\n- https://www.efficient.computer/series-b\n- https://anchor.fm/s/10f3acc34/podcast/rss\n", "person:justin-wexler": "\n# Justin Wexler\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (owner pick, surfaced by `firms/wndrco.md`). Sources: the wndr.vc team bio (read from the site's JS bundle), SEC Form D filings for Alembic, Netomi and Writer (all six Form Ds that name him), a Crunchbase News video Q&A (2025-12-08), company releases and press. The Venture Capital Journal promotion profile (2025-07-08) is paywalled; only its headline and summary line were readable. Web Archive fetches were rate-limited, so the 2024 \"Investor\" title comes from the citation in `firms/wndrco.md`.*\n\n**Segment note:** `fast_promotion` is set to **no** here. He joined in 2017 and became GP around early 2025, about 8 years, which is outside the \"about 4 years or less\" rule. `firms/wndrco.md` has `yes` for him, so the merge should resolve the conflict. The notable part isn't the speed: he went from \"Investor\" straight to General Partner with no Partner step in between.\n\n## One-line take\nWndrCo's enterprise-AI partner: an 8-year insider just made GP, who wins by getting AI-app startups into the Fortune 500 with Katzenberg, then handing them to Accenture as a follow-on lead. Worth knowing as a channel to enterprise customers, not as a frontier-lab investor.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** distribution. His bio says he \"facilitated some of the largest deals\" between WndrCo portfolio companies and the Fortune 500 ([wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team)). HumanX names Delta, Disney, Cigna, Mars and MetLife ([HumanX](https://www.humanx.co/us/speakers/justin-wexler)). His two board companies sell to the same buyers: Delta and Mars are Alembic customers ([release](https://martechseries.com/?p=390452)), and Delta and MetLife are Netomi customers ([SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=744160)). His diagnosis is that enterprise AI fails on organizational alignment, not on the technology (see Differential insight).\n- **The repeat pattern:** a WndrCo-backed AI app lands Fortune 500 logos, and then **Accenture leads or co-leads the next round**: Alembic Series B (co-lead with Prysm, 2025-11) and Netomi Series C (Accenture Ventures led, 2026-04). Accenture also took part in Writer's 2023 Series B, and Wexler's HumanX 2026 session was with Accenture Song. Accenture appears to be the step after Fortune 500 proof (inference from three rounds).\n- **Constraint:** he works at the application layer by mandate. Katzenberg said in the same interview: \"We're not in the LLM business.\" ([Crunchbase News, 2025-12-08](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/)). WndrCo's venture vehicles are mid-sized (Holdings II closed at $350.8M; see `firms/wndrco.md`), so his lead checks are Series A size, roughly $5-15M.\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** lead a research-lab seed, or back a product built for SMB first that later moves upmarket. He says the winners \"built for enterprise from day one\".\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Netomi board seat was ChenLi Wang's first.** The Form D for the September 2021 round (the $30M Series B WndrCo led) lists Netomi's directors as Puneet Mehta, Michael Brown, Clint Smith and \"CHENLI WANG\". It does not list Wexler ([Form D 2021-09-21](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1701548/000170154821000001/primary_doc.xml)). By the November 2025 Form D, the WndrCo directors are Wexler and Katzenberg (spelled \"Katsenberg\" in the filing) ([Form D 2025-11-18](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1701548/000123191925000519/primary_doc.xml)). The wndr.vc bio says he \"led\" Netomi, but the 2021 filing points to Wang holding the seat at the time. Wexler most likely took it over later.\n- **Katzenberg was already a Netomi director in November 2025.** Reuters said he joined \"as part of\" the April 2026 Series C ([Reuters via MarketScreener](https://uk.marketscreener.com/news/ai-customer-service-startup-netomi-raises-110-million-ce7f58dbd18df125)). The Form D for a $55M offering ($43M sold to 3 investors, first sale 2025-11-03) already lists him. That filing is probably the first tranche of the $110M Series C; the filing does not say so.\n- **Alembic's \"$14M Series A\" was $15.97M** sold to 10 investors, with first sale 2024-01-30, the day before the board listing. Wexler was already a director on that filing ([Form D 2024-02-16](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1972964/000197296424000002/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **Alembic raised quietly between its A and B, with Wexler on the board throughout.** The Form Ds show $1.78M from 1 investor (first sale 2024-06-28, [D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1972964/000197296424000003/primary_doc.xml)), $11.28M from 16 investors (2025-03-17, [D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1972964/000197296425000002/primary_doc.xml)), and then the B starting 2025-07-03. None of the interim money was announced.\n- **The \"$145M Series B\" was $93.5M of equity.** The D/A shows a $95M offering, $93.51M sold, 11 investors, first sale 2025-07-03, and adds Matthew Roberts (Prysm) and Sean Byrnes as directors ([D/A 2025-11-07](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1972964/000197296425000007/primary_doc.xml)). The remaining ~$51M is consistent with the \"growth funding from SLW\" in the release ([release](https://martechseries.com/?p=390452)). That reading is inference. The money closed about 4.5 months before the 2025-11-17 announcement.\n- **Return on his lead:** the B was \"a 15.7x increase in valuation over their Series A round\" ([release](https://martechseries.com/?p=390452)). Adweek reports a $645M valuation ([Adweek](https://www.adweek.com/media/alembic-series-b-145-million-jeffrey-katzenberg-wndrco-prysm-accenture/)), which implies roughly $41M at the A (inference).\n- **His Writer board seat isn't in the filings.** HumanX says he sits on Writer's board ([HumanX](https://www.humanx.co/us/speakers/justin-wexler)). Writer's November 2024 Form D lists eight directors (Habib, Alshikh, Stein, Fonstad, Bouck, Pepper, Toews, Patnam) and not Wexler ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2044986/000204498624000002/primary_doc.xml)). The bio's \"led investments in Writer\" can only mean he led *WndrCo's* participation: ICONIQ led the B.\n- **The failure mode he sells against:** \"We actually counted one company that did like 200 meetings with a startup\", and it went nowhere for lack of organizational alignment ([Crunchbase News, 2025-12-08](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/)).\n- **Contract length as a signal:** \"We've seen a one-year deal turn into a three-year deal\". He adds that some companies are now discussing four- or five-year contracts ([same](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/)).\n- **Founder filter:** \"They built with compliance from day one.\" and \"It's a pretty high bar to be a great AI founder.\" ([same](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/)).\n- **View on value capture:** application companies \"are actually the ones who choose the infrastructure that's best for them\", including the LLMs. He compares this to Salesforce bundling CRM ([same](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/)).\n- **The compute angle in his own portfolio:** Alembic spent B money on its \"second supercomputing cluster that Alembic owns outright\", an NVIDIA NVL72 superPOD at Equinix. Its research runs on spiking neural networks, and NVIDIA was its \"founding enterprise customer\" ([release](https://martechseries.com/?p=390452)). This is the nearest thing to a lab in his book.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For you:** Alembic is an applied-research company that owns its compute, and Netomi is AI-native customer-service automation. Both are the kind of AI automation business you like (e.g., Serval). He was early at Alembic (A lead, 2024-01) and had a 15.7x mark-up by 2025-11. He is a recently promoted GP at a firm that also writes growth checks. His real value to you is as a **co-investor who brings Fortune 500 customers** (Delta, Mars, MetLife, Disney) and an Accenture follow-on path.\n- **Against:** he doesn't do labs, pre-training or chips. His firm says it is \"not in the LLM business\". He can't anchor a $20M check into a $600M lab seed, and nothing in his record suggests he would try.\n- **Deals that matter to you:** Alembic (A lead, board), Netomi (board).\n\n## Background\n- BS/BA, Washington University in St. Louis; MBA, MIT Sloan ([wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team)).\n- Began at Technology Crossover Ventures (TCV) ([wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team)). MarketScreener (aggregator) lists him as a former analyst at \"TCMI, Inc.\", TCV's management company ([MarketScreener](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/JUSTIN-WEXLER-A2168S/)). Dates not found.\n- Joined WndrCo in 2017 ([wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team)). He was \"Justin Wexler from WndrCo\" with no title in the 2023 Writer release ([archived release](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/writer-the-full-stack-generative-ai-platform-announces-100-million-series-b-to-help-deliver-generative-ai-to-the-enterprise)) and \"Investor\" on the mid-2024 team page (cited in `firms/wndrco.md`). He is \"General Partner\" on the Speed of Culture page dated 2025-03-13 ([Suzy](https://www.suzy.com/blog/jeffrey-katzenberg-justin-wexler-founder-managing-general-partner-wndrco)). The VCJ profile (2025-07-08) is headlined \"Enterprise expertise elevates Wexler to WndrCo's partnership ranks\" and credits his \"tech sales experience\" ([VCJ/PEI](https://www.privateequityinternational.com/enterprise-expertise-elevates-wexler-to-wndrcos-partnership-ranks/)). Its body is paywalled; the article's key-mentions list includes Bessemer, Foundation Capital, Salesforce and TCV, with no context visible.\n- TheOrg (aggregator, unverified) calls him \"Venture Partner\" and \"President at Netomi\", with past logos Pango, Bessemer, ForgePoint and TCV ([TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/wndrco/org-chart/justin-wexler)). This is discounted: the firm site and filings contradict the titles. Pango was a WndrCo-controlled company (now part of Point Wild), so a stint there is plausible but unconfirmed.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://wndr.vc/team | Firm bio, 2026-11 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No personal account found |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/justinawexler/ | Linked from wndr.vc |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-03-13 — The Speed of Culture (Suzy, host Matt Britton), with Katzenberg. Katzenberg's \"truffle hunters\" line is the episode's pull quote; no verbatim Wexler line was found in the page's static text ([src](https://www.suzy.com/blog/jeffrey-katzenberg-justin-wexler-founder-managing-general-partner-wndrco))\n- 2025 (about May) — A Good Company (Michael Kassan), with Katzenberg, on AI in storytelling and marketing (dated via `firms/wndrco.md`; episode not opened)\n- 2025-12-08 — Crunchbase News video Q&A, with Katzenberg. The 200-meetings story, multi-year contracts, the build-for-enterprise-from-day-one filter, apps choosing their own infrastructure ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/))\n- 2026-04 — HumanX San Francisco: \"What It Takes to Win the Fortune 100 in the AI Era\", with WndrCo and Accenture Song ([src](https://www.humanx.co/us/speakers/justin-wexler))\n\n## How they think\n- **Thesis:** in enterprise AI, organizational alignment is the binding constraint; the technology is not. A startup alone can't learn the organization while selling into it, so WndrCo plays broker ([Crunchbase News](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/)).\n- **Build for enterprise from day one:** he is skeptical of SMB-first startups that try to move upmarket. Compliance (HIPAA, GDPR) has to be built in from the start.\n- **Contrarian view:** the application layer will choose its own infrastructure and LLMs. He compares this to Salesforce bundling CRM, and names Harvey and \"Bridge\" (probably Abridge, a WndrCo portfolio company) as examples.\n- **Model-agnostic:** some of his companies partner with OpenAI, Anthropic or Gemini; others, like Writer, train their own models. \"There's been success with both.\"\n- **Calls:** Alembic (A in early 2024, 15.7x mark-up by late 2025) is the clearest win on record. No mistakes or passes found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Crunchbase News Q&A with Katzenberg (2025-12-08): his only long-form on-record view of enterprise AI ([link](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/))\n2. Alembic Series B release (2025-11-17): his board quote, plus the compute and Accenture story ([link](https://martechseries.com/?p=390452))\n3. Alembic and Netomi Form Ds: who actually sat on the boards, and when the money closed ([Alembic D/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1972964/000197296425000007/primary_doc.xml), [Netomi 2025](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1701548/000123191925000519/primary_doc.xml))\n4. VCJ promotion profile (2025-07-08, paywalled) ([link](https://www.privateequityinternational.com/enterprise-expertise-elevates-wexler-to-wndrcos-partnership-ranks/))\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public X account or newsletter, and appears only alongside Katzenberg in firm-arranged podcasts and interviews.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Netomi | Series B ($30M at $210M post) | 2021-11-17 (first sale 2021-09-02) | WndrCo led; the board seat went to ChenLi Wang, not Wexler | Eldridge, Fin Venture Capital | [SaaS News](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/netomi-raises-30-million-in-series-b/), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1701548/000170154821000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Writer | Series B ($100M) | 2023-09-18 | no (ICONIQ Growth led); Wexler quoted | Balderton, Insight, Aspect, Accenture, Vanguard | [archived WndrCo post](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/writer-the-full-stack-generative-ai-platform-announces-100-million-series-b-to-help-deliver-generative-ai-to-the-enterprise) |\n| Alembic | Series A ($14M announced; $15.97M per Form D) | 2024-02-15 (first sale 2024-01-30) | yes; board | MXV Capital, Liquid 2 Ventures | [Built In](https://builtin.com/articles/alembic-raises-14m-series-a-20240215), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1972964/000197296424000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Alembic | Series B ($145M incl. SLW growth funding; $93.5M equity per D/A; $645M valuation per Adweek) | 2025-11-17 (first sale 2025-07-03) | no (Prysm and Accenture co-led); board | Prysm, Accenture, SLW, Liquid 2, NextEquity, Friends & Family Capital | [release](https://martechseries.com/?p=390452), [D/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1972964/000197296425000007/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Netomi | Series C ($110M; the $43M Form D in 2025-11 is probably its first tranche) | 2026-04-30 | no (Accenture Ventures led); board | Adobe Ventures, SLW, NAVER Ventures, Metis Strategy, Fin Capital | [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=744160), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1701548/000123191925000519/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Airtable, Webflow, Sparrow | GTM support (wndr.vc); HumanX lists Cursor, Airtable, Webflow | — | no deal attributed | — | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team), [HumanX](https://www.humanx.co/us/speakers/justin-wexler) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Alembic:** Wexler: \"When I first began to get to know Tomás, it became clear\" that Alembic could define enterprise intelligence ([release, 2025-11-17](https://martechseries.com/?p=390452)). He led the A and was on the board by the first sale. Katzenberg says WndrCo then introduced Alembic to \"some of the world's largest brands\" ([same](https://martechseries.com/?p=390452)). Per the Wall Street Journal, as relayed by Adweek, WndrCo helped Alembic win Delta and Mars ([Adweek](https://www.adweek.com/media/alembic-series-b-145-million-jeffrey-katzenberg-wndrco-prysm-accenture/)). Accenture then co-led the B. The intro path to Tomás Puig is not public.\n- **Netomi:** WndrCo led the 2021 B with Wang on the board. By 2025-11 the WndrCo seats belonged to Wexler and Katzenberg. Reuters paraphrases Wexler on the Series C: Netomi has raised over $160M, and \"hundreds of Accenture employees\" trained on its product will roll it out to customers ([Reuters via MarketScreener, 2026-04-30](https://uk.marketscreener.com/news/ai-customer-service-startup-netomi-raises-110-million-ce7f58dbd18df125)).\n- **Writer:** WndrCo joined the 2023 B, with Wexler quoted that Writer \"will continue to resonate among the Fortune 500 and beyond\" ([archived release](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/writer-the-full-stack-generative-ai-platform-announces-100-million-series-b-to-help-deliver-generative-ai-to-the-enterprise)). WndrCo is not named in the Series C coverage ([Built In](https://builtin.com/articles/writer-raises-200m-series-c-1b-valuation-20241112)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jeffrey Katzenberg (WndrCo) | Co-runs the enterprise-AI practice; co-director at Netomi | 2017 / by 2025-11 | [Crunchbase News](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1701548/000123191925000519/primary_doc.xml) |\n| ChenLi Wang (WndrCo) | Held the Netomi board seat in 2021, before Wexler | 2021 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1701548/000170154821000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Tomás Puig (Alembic CEO) | Wexler led the A and sits on the board | 2024-01 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1972964/000197296424000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Laura Maness | Fellow Alembic director (affiliation unknown) | 2024 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1972964/000197296424000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Matthew Roberts (Prysm Capital), Sean Byrnes | Joined the Alembic board with the B | 2025 | [D/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1972964/000197296425000007/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Puneet Mehta (Netomi CEO) | Board colleague | by 2025-11 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1701548/000123191925000519/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Accenture / Accenture Ventures / Accenture Song | Follow-on lead at Alembic B and Netomi C; HumanX co-panel | 2023 (Writer B) | [Alembic](https://martechseries.com/?p=390452), [Netomi](https://siliconangle.com/?p=744160), [HumanX](https://www.humanx.co/us/speakers/justin-wexler) |\n| SLW (full name not given in sources) | Co-investor at Alembic B and Netomi C | 2025 | [Alembic](https://martechseries.com/?p=390452), [Netomi](https://siliconangle.com/?p=744160) |\n| TCV | Former employer | before 2017 | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When and why did the Netomi seat move from ChenLi Wang to Wexler? MarketScreener's \"director since 2020/2021\" conflicts with the 2021 Form D.\n- Is he on Writer's board, as HumanX says? The November 2024 Form D says no.\n- What tech-sales job does VCJ mean, and was it at Pango? The full VCJ article would settle this and his exact promotion date.\n- Who are Laura Maness and Sean Byrnes (Alembic directors)? Possibly the MXV Capital and Accenture representatives; unconfirmed.\n- Does he have a seed mandate at all, or is he venture/growth only? The seed team (Wang, Saleh, Huang, Demma) is separate on wndr.vc.\n- Is he an LP anywhere? Nothing found.\n\n## Sources\n- https://wndr.vc/team (bio in /assets/index-CuMFcD--.js, read 2026-11-10); photo https://www.wndr.vc/team-pics/justin-wexler.jpg (HTTP 200)\n- https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/qa-producer-katzenberg-wexler-wndrco-enterprise-ai-investment/ (2025-12-08)\n- https://www.suzy.com/blog/jeffrey-katzenberg-justin-wexler-founder-managing-general-partner-wndrco (2025-03-13)\n- https://www.privateequityinternational.com/enterprise-expertise-elevates-wexler-to-wndrcos-partnership-ranks/ (VCJ, 2025-07-08, paywalled)\n- https://www.humanx.co/us/speakers/justin-wexler\n- https://martechseries.com/?p=390452 (Alembic B release, Business Wire, 2025-11-17); https://siliconangle.com/?p=720579; https://www.adweek.com/media/alembic-series-b-145-million-jeffrey-katzenberg-wndrco-prysm-accenture/\n- https://builtin.com/articles/alembic-raises-14m-series-a-20240215\n- https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/netomi-raises-30-million-in-series-b/ ; https://siliconangle.com/?p=744160 ; https://uk.marketscreener.com/news/ai-customer-service-startup-netomi-raises-110-million-ce7f58dbd18df125\n- https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/writer-the-full-stack-generative-ai-platform-announces-100-million-series-b-to-help-deliver-generative-ai-to-the-enterprise ; https://builtin.com/articles/writer-raises-200m-series-c-1b-valuation-20241112\n- SEC Form Ds: Alembic [2024-02-16](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1972964/000197296424000002/primary_doc.xml), [2024-07-09](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1972964/000197296424000003/primary_doc.xml), [2025-03-28](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1972964/000197296425000002/primary_doc.xml), [2025-07-15](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1972964/000197296425000005/primary_doc.xml), [D/A 2025-11-07](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1972964/000197296425000007/primary_doc.xml); Netomi [2020-07-07](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1701548/000170154820000004/primary_doc.xml), [2021-09-21](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1701548/000170154821000001/primary_doc.xml), [2025-11-18](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1701548/000123191925000519/primary_doc.xml); Writer [2024-11-21](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2044986/000204498624000002/primary_doc.xml); EDGAR full-text search for \"Justin Wexler\" (other hits are different people: a Comtech executive and a \"Justin Wexler Feil\")\n- Aggregators (secondary): https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/JUSTIN-WEXLER-A2168S/ ; https://theorg.com/org/wndrco/org-chart/justin-wexler\n", "person:justin-yannix": "\n# Justin Yannix\n\n## One-line take\nA growth-stage VP who spent 2024 to 2026 inside Premji Invest's AI-infrastructure push and is now one of Valor's newest investors. He is worth knowing as a way into Valor's AI-chip and compute book, not as someone who leads seed deals.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he has deal reps on the AI-infrastructure stack. He co-signed Premji's posts on Baseten (inference), Upscale AI (AI networking silicon) and Auradine/Velaura (ASICs), and he held a board seat at Velaura while at Premji ([archived Premji bio](https://web.archive.org/web/20260710194016/https://www.premjiinvest.com/team/justin-yannix)).\n- **What constrains him:** he was always the junior name on these deals. On all six posts his byline comes after Sandesh Patnam or Akshay Kini, never first. Premji's own bio said he \"supports the firm's efforts in growth-stage investing\". Valor's bio says he \"led investments in AI, semiconductor, infrastructure, and fintech companies\" ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/justin-yannix)). Only Valor uses the word \"led\", so treat that claim as unconfirmed. At Valor he is a VP, and Valor's investment committee approves the money.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred from the Premji posts he co-signed):** back a competitor of an existing portfolio company, or make a \"FOMO bet\" before a category consolidates ([Baseten post](https://web.archive.org/web/20251015210936/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/inference-pricing-is-a-race-to-the-bottom-why-baseten-is-different)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Premji removed his name from its live site.** On 2026-11-10, premjiinvest.com/team/justin-yannix falls back to the People index, and his name appears on none of the 196 pages in the sitemap. That includes the six investment posts he co-signed. Those posts now list only the other Premji authors. The credits that name him survive only in Wayback snapshots: Writer \"Author(s) Akshay Kini Justin Yannix Vedant Agrawal Sandesh Patnam\" ([archived 2024-11-12](https://web.archive.org/web/20241112221731/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/writer-genai-with-roi)). Why they were removed is unconfirmed. Premji's portfolio pages added a \"PI TEAM\" field only after July 2026, so I can't say whether he was ever listed there.\n- **He was on the Upscale AI Series A, not the A-1.** The January 2026 Series A post credits \"Sandesh Patnam Akshay Kini Justin Yannix Alex Zhang\" ([archived 2026-01-29](https://web.archive.org/web/20260129230302/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/premji-invest-upscale-ai-partnership)). The 2026-06-22 A-1 section on the live page credits only Patnam, Kini and Zhang ([live](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/premji-invest-upscale-ai-partnership)). The live page removed his name everywhere, so this alone doesn't prove he had left by June 2026. His Premji bio was still live in the 2026-07-10 snapshot. Departure date: unconfirmed (between July and November 2026, or earlier if the bio was simply stale).\n- **His Premji board seats:** Mapbox, Cohesity, Icertis, Outreach and Velaura. The bio lists them under \"Board Seats\" ([archived bio](https://web.archive.org/web/20260710194016/https://www.premjiinvest.com/team/justin-yannix)). Whether these were director or observer seats is unconfirmed.\n- **The Premji method he co-signed:** \"We made a deliberate call to observe but not allocate capital\". Premji tracked about 25–30 inference companies from January 2024 and invested only in Baseten's Series D, 21 months later. The post also says \"We don't make competing investments and typically like to size up massively\" ([archived Baseten post](https://web.archive.org/web/20251015210936/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/inference-pricing-is-a-race-to-the-bottom-why-baseten-is-different)). These are firm posts with several authors, not his own words.\n- **His own stated aim** (the pull-quote on his Premji bio): \"partnering with founders and teams as they build generational, category-defining companies\" ([archived bio](https://web.archive.org/web/20260710194016/https://www.premjiinvest.com/team/justin-yannix)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** His sourced history is all growth rounds of $135M to $600M: EvenUp D, Writer C, Applied Intuition F, Baseten D, Auradine C and Upscale AI A. THESIS.md rates growth investing low. Two things raise him to medium:\n- **The AI-chip work:** the Velaura board seat, Auradine/Velaura, Upscale AI and Tensordyne.\n- **The Valor seat:** Valor writes large checks into compute and AI hardware.\n\nHe is a useful co-investor contact for a later round of a lab or chip company you back at seed. He is not a seed decision-maker. The narrower claim that holds up: among the VPs on Valor's team page (David Malcher, Charlotte Wang, Ben Rosenbaum), he is the only one whose bio cites AI and semiconductor deals ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/justin-yannix)). I haven't confirmed which Valor deals he works on. As of 2026-11-10, Valor's site names him on no deal.\n\n## Background\n- J.P. Morgan, investment banking: dates unconfirmed ([Valor](https://www.valorep.com/team/justin-yannix)).\n- Qatalyst Partners, technology investment banking. He advised on M&A and strategic engagements. Dates unconfirmed ([archived Premji bio](https://web.archive.org/web/20260710194016/https://www.premjiinvest.com/team/justin-yannix)).\n- Warburg Pincus, private equity on the technology team, focused on growth-stage enterprise software and fintech. Dates unconfirmed (same source).\n- Premji Invest, 2024 to 2026: he \"joined Premji Invest in 2024\", with growth stage and enterprise technology as his focus. Valor gives his title there as Vice President. The earliest archived bio is from 2024-09-19 ([archived](https://web.archive.org/web/20240919200450/https://www.premjiinvest.com/team/justin-yannix)).\n- Valor Equity Partners: VP on the Investment Team, joined 2026 ([src](https://www.valorep.com/team/justin-yannix)).\n- B.A. in Economics from the University of Chicago, summa cum laude and Phi Beta Kappa. He sits on the College Leadership Council. He grew up in the Boston area (sources: Valor and the Premji bio).\n- How he got the Valor seat: unconfirmed. A path from PE into a growth VP role is the usual route into Valor's Investment Team; the other VPs come from Vista, Moelis and Broad Sky ([firm page](../firms/valor-equity-partners.md)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.valorep.com/team/justin-yannix | Valor bio, checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | His Premji co-authored posts are listed under Read these first (archived) |\n| X | nothing found | Web search budget was exhausted, so this wasn't searched |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/jyannix/ | Linked from his archived Premji bio, 2026-07-10 |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search budget was exhausted during this pass, so this wasn't searched.\n\n## How they think\nThese ideas come from Premji posts he co-signed. They are the firm's views, not his alone:\n- **Horizon 1-2-3 framework:** Premji waits until a market is clear (Horizon 2–3) and then sizes up hard in the one leader it picks (Baseten post).\n- **Inference:** it makes money when sold as a reliable system, not a point optimization (Baseten post).\n- **AI networking:** the \"networking wall\" means network spend rises relative to compute spend as clusters grow (Upscale AI post).\n- **Repeat founders:** Premji backed Rajiv Khemani three times (Innovium, then Auradine/Velaura, then Upscale AI) (Auradine and Upscale posts).\n- **Enterprise AI:** foundation models win general-purpose tasks, so the opportunity is in vertical apps (EvenUp, Hippocratic) and deep workflow platforms (Writer) (Writer post).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Inference pricing is a race to the bottom. Why Baseten is different (2025-09-08, archived)](https://web.archive.org/web/20251015210936/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/inference-pricing-is-a-race-to-the-bottom-why-baseten-is-different): the clearest statement of how Premji times a category.\n- [Premji Invest & Upscale AI Partnership (2026-01-21, archived)](https://web.archive.org/web/20260129230302/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/premji-invest-upscale-ai-partnership): the AI networking-silicon thesis.\n- [Auradine: Building America's Bitcoin Mining Hardware Champion (2025-04-17, archived)](https://web.archive.org/web/20250419003739/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/auradine-building-americas-bitcoin-mining-hardware-champion): the US-made ASIC and supply-chain argument.\n- [Writer: GenAI with ROI (2024-11-12, archived)](https://web.archive.org/web/20241112221731/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/writer-genai-with-roi): the enterprise GenAI platform thesis.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X and LinkedIn activity weren't checked because the search budget ran out.\n\n## Portfolio\n\"Named\" means his name appears in the post's author list. No source says he personally led any of these deals.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| EvenUp | Series D ($135M) | 2024-10-08 | no (Premji participant); named | | [archived post](https://web.archive.org/web/20241110191729/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/evenup-pioneering-vertical-ai-to-disrupt-personal-injury-law-and-more) |\n| Writer | Series C ($200M at $1.9B) | 2024-11-12 | Premji co-led; named | Radical Ventures, ICONIQ Growth | [archived post](https://web.archive.org/web/20241112221731/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/writer-genai-with-roi) |\n| Velaura AI (as Auradine) | Series C ($153M) | 2025-04-16 | no (StepStone led); named; board seat | StepStone, Maverick Silicon, Qualcomm Ventures, Mayfield | [archived post](https://web.archive.org/web/20250419003739/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/auradine-building-americas-bitcoin-mining-hardware-champion) |\n| Applied Intuition | Series F ($600M incl. tender at $15B) | 2025-06-17 | no (BlackRock, Kleiner Perkins co-led); named | | [archived post](https://web.archive.org/web/20250713102422/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/applied-intuition-driving-the-future-of-vehicle-autonomy-and-intelligent-machines) |\n| Baseten | Series D ($150M) | 2025-09-05 | no (BOND led); named | | [archived post](https://web.archive.org/web/20251015210936/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/inference-pricing-is-a-race-to-the-bottom-why-baseten-is-different) |\n| Upscale AI | Series A ($200M; >$1B) | 2026-01-21 | Premji co-led; named | Tiger Global, Xora Innovation | [archived post](https://web.archive.org/web/20260129230302/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/premji-invest-upscale-ai-partnership) |\n| Tensordyne, Runway, Navan, Chainalysis, Icertis | round unconfirmed | | | | Listed under \"Investments\" on [archived bio](https://web.archive.org/web/20260710194016/https://www.premjiinvest.com/team/justin-yannix) |\n| Mapbox, Cohesity, Icertis, Outreach, Velaura | board seats | | | | [archived bio](https://web.archive.org/web/20260710194016/https://www.premjiinvest.com/team/justin-yannix) |\n\n**Timing:**\n- **Velaura:** Premji first came in at the Auradine Series C, its first round in the company. The firm re-upped in Velaura's August 2026 Series A ([repo rounds](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260818925932/en/Velaura-AI-Raises-%24110-Million-Series-A-to-Advance-the-Next-Generation-of-Ultra-Low-Power-AI-Compute-Infrastructure)). His role in that re-up is unconfirmed.\n- **Upscale AI:** Premji first came in at the Series A. The seed was led by Mayfield and Maverick Silicon. Premji then led the A-1, and that post doesn't credit him.\n- **Writer and EvenUp:** these were Premji's growth-stage entry rounds.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Baseten:** Premji watched the inference market from January 2024. It invested only in the Series D, after \"21 months\" of consolidation. The post asks openly \"Why do we win the deal?\" ([archived](https://web.archive.org/web/20251015210936/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/inference-pricing-is-a-race-to-the-bottom-why-baseten-is-different)).\n- **Auradine and Upscale AI:** both came from Premji's earlier backing of Rajiv Khemani at Innovium, where Premji led the Series E before Marvell bought the company.\n- **Who sourced each deal:** unconfirmed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Valor Equity Partners | at: Vice President, Investment Team | 2026 | [valorep.com](https://www.valorep.com/team/justin-yannix) |\n| Premji Invest | at: Vice President (title per Valor); growth investing | 2024–2026 | [archived bio](https://web.archive.org/web/20240919200450/https://www.premjiinvest.com/team/justin-yannix) |\n| Warburg Pincus | at: private equity investor, technology | dates unconfirmed | [valorep.com](https://www.valorep.com/team/justin-yannix) |\n| Qatalyst Partners | at: investment banking | dates unconfirmed | [valorep.com](https://www.valorep.com/team/justin-yannix) |\n| J.P. Morgan | at: investment banking | dates unconfirmed | [valorep.com](https://www.valorep.com/team/justin-yannix) |\n| Sandesh Patnam (Premji) | co-author on all six posts | 2024 | archived posts above |\n| Akshay Kini (Premji) | co-author on all six posts | 2024 | archived posts above |\n| Vedant Agrawal (Premji) | co-author on the Writer and Baseten posts | 2024 | archived posts above |\n| Alex Zhang (Premji) | co-author on the Upscale AI post | 2026 | archived post above (the repo id `alex-zhang` is a different person; no id used) |\n| Rajiv Khemani | founder of Auradine/Velaura and Upscale AI; Yannix sat on the Velaura board | 2025 | archived posts above |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which Valor deals is he on? Which Valor partner does he work under?\n- When exactly did he leave Premji, and why did Premji remove his name from its posts?\n- Were his Premji board seats director or observer seats?\n- What were his dates at Warburg Pincus? Did he work on any AI or chip deals there?\n- How to reach him: there is no public email. The routes are the LinkedIn profile above or a Valor partner. Unconfirmed whether he takes cold inbound.\n\n## Checked with no result (2026-11-10)\n- The live premjiinvest.com sitemap (196 pages): no mention of him.\n- Live Premji portfolio \"PI TEAM\" fields: he is not listed.\n- valorep.com: of the 172 sitemap URLs that aren't team pages, I fetched the first 150. None mentions him. His only Valor page is his team bio ([sitemap](https://www.valorep.com/sitemap.xml)).\n- SEC EDGAR full-text search for \"Justin Yannix\" on Form D: 0 hits ([efts](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Justin%20Yannix%22&forms=D)).\n- The web search budget was exhausted, so podcasts, X and press were not searched.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.valorep.com/team/justin-yannix\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20240919200450/https://www.premjiinvest.com/team/justin-yannix\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20260710194016/https://www.premjiinvest.com/team/justin-yannix\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20241110191729/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/evenup-pioneering-vertical-ai-to-disrupt-personal-injury-law-and-more\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20241112221731/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/writer-genai-with-roi\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20250419003739/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/auradine-building-americas-bitcoin-mining-hardware-champion\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20250713102422/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/applied-intuition-driving-the-future-of-vehicle-autonomy-and-intelligent-machines\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20251015210936/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/inference-pricing-is-a-race-to-the-bottom-why-baseten-is-different\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20260129230302/https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/premji-invest-upscale-ai-partnership\n- https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/premji-invest-upscale-ai-partnership\n- https://www.premjiinvest.com/portfolio/tensordyne\n", "person:justine-moore": "\n# Justine Moore\n\n## One-line take\na16z's generative-media specialist who sits on the infra team and has been a named author on the firm's seed into Black Forest Labs, the Mirelo seed co-lead and the WaveForms AI investment, with a run of a16z-led seeds and A's in image, video, audio and character models since 2024.\n\n## Crux\nShe wins by being the a16z partner who has watched creative AI (image, video, voice, music) most closely for the longest stretch. She has published on it since early 2023 [src](https://a16z.com/generative-ai-the-next-consumer-platform/) and on AI video since January 2024 [src](https://a16z.com/why-2023-was-ai-videos-breakout-year-and-what-to-expect-in-2024/), so model-lab founders in the field already know her name. Her stated thesis is that no single video model wins at everything, so specialized models and product layers can win [src](https://a16z.com/there-is-no-god-tier-video-model-but-there-is-something-better/). That pulls her toward small, research-heavy media labs (BFL, Mirelo, WaveForms, Shizuku) rather than general-purpose frontier labs.\n\nConstraints: on the large lab deals she is almost always one of three or four a16z authors, typically alongside infra GPs such as Martin Casado, Guido Appenzeller or Anjney Midha. It is unclear from the sources whether she is the decision-maker or the domain lead. Her deals are concentrated in generative media and consumer AI. She has no sourced deals in pre-training labs for general intelligence, chips or compute.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Specialization thesis, which explains the lab picks.** She argues a single best video model does not exist: \"there is no “God Model” that’s great at everything.\" She frames the next phase as \"the product era of video models\" [src](https://a16z.com/there-is-no-god-tier-video-model-but-there-is-something-better/) (2025-10-22). Mirelo, a video-to-sound lab she co-led two months later, fits that pattern exactly.\n- **She picks labs on model benchmarking.** In the Mirelo memo the a16z team says that after evaluating models, \"Mirelo stood out as a clear leader.\" [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirelo/) (2025-12-15).\n- **Open weights were part of the BFL case.** The BFL seed memo stresses that \"BFL is committed to continuing the open weights approach\" [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/) (2024-08-01). Her 2026 report with Jennifer Li adds that \"Enterprises are increasingly gravitating toward open-source models for production not because they’re cheaper\" [src](https://a16z.com/the-state-of-generative-media-2026/) (2026-02-19).\n- **Long courtships before checks.** On Krea, the team had \"partnered with the Krea team\" for 16 months before announcing [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-krea/) (2025-04-07). On Hedra, the a16z team had known Michael Lingelbach \"over a period of several years\" and the company came through a16z speedrun [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-hedra/) (2025-05-15). The pattern: get close early (speedrun, community, content), then lead the priced round.\n- **Thesis published before the deals.** In January 2024 she wrote \"we’re also bullish on an AI-native editing platform\" [src](https://a16z.com/why-2023-was-ai-videos-breakout-year-and-what-to-expect-in-2024/). In 2025 she followed with \"It's time for agentic video editing,\" and in 2026 she led the Glif seed [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-glif/).\n- **Contrarian take on emotion over IQ (WaveForms).** The WaveForms memo she co-wrote argues that for many use cases \"EQ is equally (if not more) important than IQ.\" [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-waveforms-ai/) (2024-12-09).\n- **Enterprise model concentration data.** The 2026 report says \"OpenAI, Gemini, and Anthropic together command 89% of enterprise wallet share.\" [src](https://a16z.com/the-state-of-generative-media-2026/). This is useful as an outside view on where frontier-lab revenue sits.\n- **Her view on vibe coding.** She argues vibe coding reached only power users, and that \"you need to ship products to consumers\" [src](https://a16z.com/most-people-cant-vibe-code-heres-how-we-fix-that/) (2026-02-03). This is the thesis behind the Wabi $20M pre-seed she led with Anish Acharya [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-wabi/).\n- Note: the two 2026 podcast pages (\"AI, Design, and the Power of Open Models\" with Ideogram's Mohammad Norouzi, and \"AI Micro Dramas\" on New Economies) have only editorial summaries on a16z.com, with no transcript and no direct quotes. Nothing from them is used as her words.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** She is a named a16z author on seeds for new model labs: Black Forest Labs ($31M seed, a16z led), Mirelo ($41M seed, a16z co-led with Index) and WaveForms AI (Alexis Conneau, Coralie Lemaitre). She also led seeds for Shizuku AI and Glif. These are exactly the \"new labs just starting out\" in THESIS.md, though in generative media rather than general pre-training. Town ($55M Series A, an AI assistant that works across a user's tools) touches AI-native automation. a16z writes $10M+ checks, so she is a natural co-lead counterpart on big seeds for media and model labs. The minus: her lab exposure is media models, not frontier LLMs, chips or compute, and her promotion timing is unverified.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford: BA Economics and MBA (a16z firm bio) [src](https://a16z.com/author/justine-moore/)\n- Goldman Sachs, Private Equity team (dates not sourced) [src](https://a16z.com/author/justine-moore/)\n- CRV, early-stage investor focused on consumer and SMB tech (dates not sourced; not listed on CRV's current team page) [src](https://a16z.com/author/justine-moore/)\n- a16z: co-author of \"Generative AI: The Next Consumer Platform\" on 2023-02-07, so she was at the firm by then [src](https://a16z.com/generative-ai-the-next-consumer-platform/). Now Partner on the Infra investing team, covering AI foundation models and applications [src](https://a16z.com/author/justine-moore/).\n- Promotion: aggregators say she joined in 2022 and made partner in 2023, but no firm announcement or press source confirming either date was found. **fast_promotion: unknown.**\n- Her X handle \"venturetwins\" refers to her twin, Olivia Moore, who is also an a16z partner and a frequent co-author [src](https://a16z.com/author/justine-moore/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/justine-moore/ | 40+ pieces; posts every few weeks (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found beyond a16z.com | |\n| X | https://x.com/venturetwins | Linked from the a16z author page; X returned HTTP 402, so follower count was not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/justinemoore94/ | Linked from the a16z author page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | a16z podcast regular (with Olivia Moore) | e.g. \"What You Missed in AI This Week\" |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-07 — New Economies (a16z) — \"AI Micro Dramas, Generative Media, and the Future of Creativity\" — https://a16z.com/podcast/ai-micro-dramas-generative-media-and-the-future-of-creativity/ — summary only, no transcript\n- 2026-06 — a16z — \"AI, Design, and the Power of Open Models\" with Mohammad Norouzi (Ideogram) and Yoko Li — https://a16z.com/podcast/ai-design-and-the-power-of-open-models/ — summary only, no transcript\n- 2025 — a16z — \"Google DeepMind Lead Researchers on Genie 3 & the Future of World-Building\" — listed on the author page; not fetched\n- 2025 — a16z — \"Grok, Genie 3, GPT-5 & the Rise of Vibe Coding\" with Olivia Moore — listed on the author page; not fetched\n\n## How they think\n- Video and image models are hitting a plateau on benchmarks, so the edge moves to specialized models and product workflow (\"product era\") [src](https://a16z.com/there-is-no-god-tier-video-model-but-there-is-something-better/)\n- Open weights matter, both for developers (BFL) and for enterprise production use [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/) [src](https://a16z.com/the-state-of-generative-media-2026/)\n- Emotional intelligence and character are model-level opportunities (WaveForms, Shizuku AI) [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-waveforms-ai/) [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-shizuku-ai/)\n- Consumer distribution needs products, not developer tools (vibe coding piece, Wabi) [src](https://a16z.com/most-people-cant-vibe-code-heres-how-we-fix-that/)\n- Calls so far: ElevenLabs Series A co-lead in June 2023 and the BFL seed in August 2024 both look strongly right on later momentum (valuation data not sourced here).\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"There is no God Tier video model\" (2025-10-22): her core specialization thesis. https://a16z.com/there-is-no-god-tier-video-model-but-there-is-something-better/\n2. \"Investing in Black Forest Labs\" (2024-08-01): the lab seed memo. https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/\n3. \"Investing in Mirelo\" (2025-12-15): how she benchmarks labs. https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirelo/\n4. \"The State of Generative Media 2026\" (2026-02-19): data on enterprise model use. https://a16z.com/the-state-of-generative-media-2026/\n5. \"Why 2023 Was AI Video's Breakout Year\" (2024-01-31): the early map of the space. https://a16z.com/why-2023-was-ai-videos-breakout-year-and-what-to-expect-in-2024/\n\n## Social engagement\nX (@venturetwins) could not be fetched (HTTP 402), so there is no sourced read on who she replies to or amplifies. Her co-authors on a16z.com show her internal network: Olivia Moore, Anish Acharya, Bryan Kim and Alex Rampell (consumer); Marco Mascorro, Martin Casado, Guido Appenzeller, Anjney Midha, Jennifer Li and Matt Bornstein (infra).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ElevenLabs | Series A | 2023-06-20 | Co-led (a16z with Nat Friedman and Daniel Gross) | Nat Friedman; Daniel Gross | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-elevenlabs/) |\n| Black Forest Labs | Seed ($31M) | 2024-08-01 | Yes (a16z lead) | General Catalyst; MätchVC; angels Brendan Iribe, Michael Ovitz, Garry Tan, Timo Aila, Vladlen Koltun | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/) [src](https://bfl.ai/announcements/24-08-01-bfl) |\n| Viggle | Series A ($19M) | 2024-08-26 | Yes (a16z lead); her personal role comes only from the firm bio | Two Small Fish | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/26/viggle-makes-controllable-ai-characters-for-memes-and-visualizing-ideas/) [src](https://a16z.com/author/justine-moore/) |\n| WaveForms AI | Not stated | 2024-12-09 | Not stated | Not named | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-waveforms-ai/) |\n| Krea | Not stated | 2025-04-07 | Not stated | Not named | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-krea/) |\n| Hedra | Series A ($32M) | 2025-05-15 | Yes (a16z Infra fund lead; Matt Bornstein on board) | Earlier investors (unnamed in the article) | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/05/15/hedra-the-app-used-to-make-talking-baby-podcasts-raises-32m-from-a16z/) |\n| Wabi | Pre-seed ($20M) | 2025-11-05 | Yes | Not named | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-wabi/) |\n| Mirelo | Seed ($41M) | 2025-12-15 | Co-led (with Index Ventures) | Index Ventures; Atlantic | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirelo/) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/15/mirelo-raises-41m-from-index-and-a16z-to-solve-ai-videos-silent-problem/) |\n| Shizuku AI | Seed | 2026-02-09 | Yes | Not named | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-shizuku-ai/) |\n| Glif | Seed | 2026-04-23 | Yes (sole a16z author) | Not named | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-glif/) |\n| Town | Series A ($55M) | 2026-06-03 | Yes | Forerunner; First Round; Alt Capital; Conviction | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-town/) |\n| Highstock | Series A ($30M) | 2026-09-10 | Yes | Not named | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-highstock/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Black Forest Labs (first institutional lead).** a16z led the $31M seed announced on BFL's launch day, 2024-08-01. General Catalyst and MätchVC are described only as follow-up investors, so a16z was the first institution in on the priced round [src](https://bfl.ai/announcements/24-08-01-bfl). Moore is the fourth author on the memo, after Anjney Midha, Marco Mascorro and Rajko Radovanovic. Midha most likely ran the deal, but that is unconfirmed.\n- **Mirelo (not first).** Atlantic led an earlier undisclosed pre-seed. a16z and Index co-led the $41M seed about two years after the company was founded [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/15/mirelo-raises-41m-from-index-and-a16z-to-solve-ai-videos-silent-problem/). Moore co-authored with Guido Appenzeller. The memo says the team benchmarked models before picking Mirelo [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirelo/).\n- **Hedra (not first at the priced round, but in early through speedrun).** a16z speedrun was in the $10M seed with Index and Abstract. The a16z Infra fund then led the $32M Series A [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/05/15/hedra-the-app-used-to-make-talking-baby-podcasts-raises-32m-from-a16z/).\n- **ElevenLabs (not first).** a16z co-led the Series A with Nat Friedman and Daniel Gross in June 2023 [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-elevenlabs/). Earlier investors were not checked in this pass.\n- **Krea.** The 16-month working relationship before the announcement suggests a16z first engaged around December 2023 (arithmetic from the memo) [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-krea/).\n- **WaveForms AI.** The memo names Alexis Conneau (CEO) and Coralie Lemaitre. The round, size and lead were not confirmed because the press fetch failed [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-waveforms-ai/).\n- **Glif.** The only memo in this set where she is the sole author: she led the seed for Fabian Stelzer and Jamie Wilkinson (co-founder of Know Your Meme) [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-glif/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Andreessen Horowitz | Partner, Infra investing team | by 2023-02 | [src](https://a16z.com/author/justine-moore/) |\n| Olivia Moore | Twin; a16z partner; frequent co-author (Krea, Highstock, consumer reports) | | [src](https://a16z.com/author/justine-moore/) |\n| Marco Mascorro | Co-author on BFL, WaveForms, Shizuku | 2024 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-shizuku-ai/) |\n| Martin Casado | Co-author on WaveForms, Shizuku | 2024 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-waveforms-ai/) |\n| Anjney Midha | Co-author on BFL seed | 2024 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/) |\n| Guido Appenzeller | Co-author on Mirelo seed | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirelo/) |\n| Anish Acharya | Co-author on Krea, Wabi | 2023 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-wabi/) |\n| Jennifer Li | Co-author on ElevenLabs, State of Generative Media 2026 | 2023 | [src](https://a16z.com/the-state-of-generative-media-2026/) |\n| Matt Bornstein | Co-author on Hedra; Hedra board | 2025 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/05/15/hedra-the-app-used-to-make-talking-baby-podcasts-raises-32m-from-a16z/) |\n| Alex Rampell | Co-author on Town | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-town/) |\n| CRV | Former employer | | [src](https://a16z.com/author/justine-moore/) |\n| Goldman Sachs | Former employer (PE) | | [src](https://a16z.com/author/justine-moore/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Partner promotion date: is there an a16z promotions post or LinkedIn entry? It is needed to set fast_promotion.\n- Who at a16z actually sourced BFL: Midha or Moore?\n- WaveForms round: size, lead and whether a16z was first.\n- Viggle: confirm she is the deal partner beyond the firm bio.\n- X (@venturetwins): who she amplifies; any posts on how deals came about.\n- The New Economies and Ideogram episodes need transcripts for verbatim quotes.\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/justine-moore/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/\n- https://bfl.ai/announcements/24-08-01-bfl\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirelo/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/15/mirelo-raises-41m-from-index-and-a16z-to-solve-ai-videos-silent-problem/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-waveforms-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-shizuku-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-hedra/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/05/15/hedra-the-app-used-to-make-talking-baby-podcasts-raises-32m-from-a16z/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-krea/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-elevenlabs/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-glif/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-wabi/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-highstock/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-town/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/26/viggle-makes-controllable-ai-characters-for-memes-and-visualizing-ideas/\n- https://a16z.com/there-is-no-god-tier-video-model-but-there-is-something-better/\n- https://a16z.com/the-state-of-generative-media-2026/\n- https://a16z.com/most-people-cant-vibe-code-heres-how-we-fix-that/\n- https://a16z.com/why-2023-was-ai-videos-breakout-year-and-what-to-expect-in-2024/\n- https://a16z.com/generative-ai-the-next-consumer-platform/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/ai-design-and-the-power-of-open-models/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/ai-micro-dramas-generative-media-and-the-future-of-creativity/\n- Web search budget was exhausted this turn; research used direct fetches only.\n", "person:kareem-zaki": "\n# Kareem Zaki\n\n## One-line take\nThrive's in-house healthcare company-builder: he co-founded and incubated Cedar, Cadence, and (per Colossus) Rightway inside the firm. Josh Kushner calls him \"the single best healthcare investor in the world.\" He now helps run Thrive Holdings, Thrive's AI-enabled roll-up vehicle ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/openai-backed-thrive-holdings-raises-2b-to-bring-ai-to-the-enterprise/)).\n\n## Crux\n*(Added 2026-11-09.)* Zaki wins by **building** companies, not by competing for them. He incubates inside Thrive, sitting at the founding table of Cedar and Cadence, and then concentrates capital behind the winners. He says \"the vast majority of our capital is concentrated in 15 names\" ([src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep392), ILTB, 2024-10-08). He believes only about 10–20 companies a year reach the scale that matters, so he avoids \"good deals\": small, safe-looking ideas that attract crowds ([src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep392)). He is constrained by healthcare's slow sales cycles. In healthcare he builds inside the incumbent system and rejects spray-and-pray venture math there. He would not run a political, permission-seeking investment committee or a broad diversified portfolio.\n\n## Differential insight\n*(Added 2026-11-09. All rows come from the ILTB Ep. 392 transcript on podscripts or the Bloomberg transcript in A Letter a Day. The podscripts transcript has no speaker labels, so I attributed lines to Zaki from context; confidence is medium. Mirrored in `data/rows/kareem-zaki-insights.csv`.)*\n- **Concentration number.** \"the vast majority of our capital is concentrated in 15 names.\" He argues that concentration binds Thrive closer to founders ([src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep392), ~11:52).\n- **Anti-\"good deal\" rule.** \"one of the worst traps in venture is a good deal.\" A deal can look de-risked on paper while the idea is small and crowded ([src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep392), ~16:15).\n- **Size of the hunting ground.** About \"10 to 20 in any given year\" reach category-defining scale ([src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep392), ~16:49).\n- **Healthcare contrarian view.** \"I just don't think venture math works in health care.\" Health systems need vendors that survive, so a portfolio where most companies die gets no traction. This explains why he builds a few durable companies \"in the system\" instead of backing many ([src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep392), ~52:56–55:37).\n- **Why fund sizes grew.** \"we just felt underpowered relative to our vision.\" He gives the ~$2B Stripe check and the Ramp round Thrive led after COVID as examples ([src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep392), ~15:44).\n- **Investment committee critique.** At many firms \"investment committee is more political than it actually is truth-seeking.\" Thrive's answer is a small, trusted team ([src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep392), ~30:09).\n- **Ambition self-test.** If a pitch is \"not resonating with a generalist crowd,\" he is \"thinking too small\" ([src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep392), ~1:00:08).\n- **Fool's-gold filter.** The key check is \"quality of the market\": how durable the tailwind is and whether the unit economics are real once cheap capital is gone ([src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep392), ~45:12).\n- **Building is sourcing.** \"being able to build companies, I think, allows us to bring in more people\" early ([src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-154-josh-kushner-and-kareem), 2023).\n- **Time horizon as an edge.** \"how do we use time horizon as a competitive advantage when we invest?\" ([src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-154-josh-kushner-and-kareem), 2023).\n- **Not found:** personal check sizes, named deals he passed on, or a specific admitted mistake. On mistakes he speaks only generally: \"There's no way to be perfect\" ([src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep392)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a frontier-lab or AI-chip seed investor. His own deals are healthcare incubations (Cedar, Cadence), fintech growth (Stripe, Ramp, Robinhood), and AI-enabled services roll-ups at Thrive Holdings. Thrive is a multi-stage, concentrated firm, and the OpenAI relationship sits at the firm level: he says \"We've supported that company through multiple rounds\" ([src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep392)). Two things matter to me:\n1. **Thrive Holdings** is a large AI-native automation vehicle, adjacent to my Serval-type interest. It is a likely buyer of, or competitor to, AI-services startups ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/openai-backed-thrive-holdings-raises-2b-to-bring-ai-to-the-enterprise/)).\n2. His concentration logic (15 names, 10–20 category-definers a year) is a useful model for sizing ~$20M checks into very large seeds.\n\nHe is unlikely to be a co-investor at my seed check sizes.\n\n## Background\n- Son of Coptic Christian immigrants from Cairo. His father redid his medical training in Cleveland ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/), Oct 2025).\n- He chose Blackstone's private equity group over applying to medical school, to learn about businesses, the economy, and healthcare ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). He spent three years in PE at Blackstone ([src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-thrive-capitals-kareem-zaki-a83)). MarketScreener lists his Blackstone role as ending 2014-01 ([src](https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/KAREEM-ZAKI-A2F3HT/)).\n- While at Blackstone he informally helped Josh Kushner with Oscar Health on nights and weekends. He then joined Thrive around 2014 ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). MarketScreener gives his Thrive start as 2014-09 ([src](https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/KAREEM-ZAKI-A2F3HT/)). Invest Like the Best has a chapter titled \"Reconnecting with Josh,\" which implies they knew each other before ([src](https://colossus.com/?p=48040)).\n- Colossus says Kushner met Zaki and Vince Mallick at Harvard Business School ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). Zaki's own degrees: (unverified).\n- Forbes 30 Under 30, Venture Capital, 2018. This rests on a third-party profile seen only as a search snippet; the page now 404s and the Forbes list itself was not checked (unverified) ([src](https://upcarta.onrender.com/profile/kareemszaki)).\n- Now a General Partner at Thrive ([src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-thrive-capitals-kareem-zaki-a83)). Colossus names him one of Thrive's three main faces, with Kushner and Mallick. Partners call him the firm's best \"top-down\" and \"long-term\" thinker ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- Founding member of Thrive Holdings ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/openai-backed-thrive-holdings-raises-2b-to-bring-ai-to-the-enterprise/), 2026-08-12).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | No personal site. Thrive's site has no individual bios that I could confirm. |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | @kareemszaki (likely) | An Oct 2024 X post by a colleague tags \"@kareemszaki\" when praising his ILTB episode ([src](https://x.com/andrewlangadams/status/1848142140108656647)). Activity level not checked (2026-11-09). |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | Not fetched. Third-party profiles cite LinkedIn data. |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2021-11 — 20VC (Harry Stebbings) — \"Thrive Capital's Kareem Zaki on The One Rule That Drives Investment Decision-Making and Focus at Thrive...\" — [link](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-thrive-capitals-kareem-zaki-a83) — Topics: Thrive's \"one rule,\" why large institutions will enter VC, and building a culture for young talent. The show notes list him as co-founder and board member of Cedar, Nava, Scope Security, and Cadence.\n- 2021-10 (published 2022-01-24) — Finding Genius — \"Cadence: Christopher Altchek & Kareem Zaki\" — [link](https://findinggenius.substack.com/p/cadence-christopher-altchek-and-kareem) — The Cadence launch story and the remote chronic-care thesis (see Deal stories).\n- 2023 (transcript published 2024-02-10) — Bloomberg interview with Josh Kushner, transcribed in A Letter a Day #154 — [link](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-154-josh-kushner-and-kareem) — Macro conviction plus company-level precision. Digital health took off after COVID as insurers and governments began paying for it. Software and hardware are converging.\n- 2024-10-08 — Invest Like the Best (Patrick O'Shaughnessy), Ep. 392 — \"Small Ideas Attract Competition\" — [link](https://colossus.com/?p=48040) — Topics: generalism, spotting category-defining companies (SpaceX, OpenAI), and chapters on healthcare's challenges, approach to healthcare innovation, and competition in healthcare. A third-party transcript with no speaker labels is on podscripts ([src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep392)). See Differential insight for the lines quoted from it (added 2026-11-09).\n- 2025-02 — Turpentine VC, E76 — \"Thrive Capital's Bold Bets on Category-Defining Companies with Kareem Zaki\" — [link](https://metacast.app/podcast/turpentine-vc-venture-capital-and-investing/LUJoKypp/e76-thrive-capital-s-bold-bets-on-category-defining-companies-with-kareem-zaki/aHs2DHLD) — Per a third-party summary, Thrive is \"opportunistic and concentrated both in people and ideas.\" In healthcare it prefers building with incumbent systems over replacing them ([summary](https://listenlite.horsy.ai/blog/turpentine_vc_thrive_capitals_bold_bets_on_categorydefining_companies_with_kareem_zaki)). The episode date comes from a search snippet (unverified), and the metacast page returned 403.\n\n## How they think\n- **Category-defining companies with long tailwinds.** He wants to \"keep the main thing the main thing\" ([summary of Turpentine E76](https://listenlite.horsy.ai/blog/turpentine_vc_thrive_capitals_bold_bets_on_categorydefining_companies_with_kareem_zaki)). The ILTB episode is built on the same frame ([src](https://colossus.com/?p=48040)).\n- **Tech is horizontal, so be a generalist.** He is quoted saying \"Tech is a horizontal sector, not an isolated vertical\" ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). Colossus credits him with helping make Thrive sector- and geography-agnostic, citing Nubank and Robinhood ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). ILTB argues generalism produces more creative ideas ([src](https://colossus.com/?p=48040)).\n- **Top-down macro, then precision.** First get macro conviction and be early to the trend, then be precise at the company level ([src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-154-josh-kushner-and-kareem)). He also warns that the short term is \"actually very hard to predict\" ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- **Building is how you earn the right to invest.** Thrive incubates companies in-house. That makes the firm understand how hard execution is, which attracts founders early and gives it access later ([src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-154-josh-kushner-and-kareem)). Thrive aims to act as a thought partner and sometimes as a co-founder ([summary](https://listenlite.horsy.ai/blog/turpentine_vc_thrive_capitals_bold_bets_on_categorydefining_companies_with_kareem_zaki)).\n- **Healthcare thesis: fix the plumbing, partner with incumbents.** His companies cluster around the patient financial and administrative layer:\n  - Cedar: patient billing and payments ([src](https://findinggenius.substack.com/p/cedar)).\n  - Rightway: care navigation and a PBM ([src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/rightway-raises-100m-at-1-1b-valuation-to-continue-redefining-the-member-experience-through-better-care-navigation-and-a-new-to-the-world-pbm-301257115.html)).\n  - Nava: benefits brokerage ([src](https://coverager.com/nava-benefits-raises-40-million/)).\n  - Cadence: remote chronic care delivered through health systems ([src](https://www.cadence.care/post/introducing-cadence-new-health-tech-company)).\n\n  The pattern is selling to or alongside health systems, not around them. For example, Cadence launched with LifePoint Health as its founding partner ([src](https://www.cadence.care/post/introducing-cadence-new-health-tech-company)).\n- **Build for all of America.** A third-party summary of ILTB says he stresses building for the broader American population, not just New York and San Francisco ([src](https://podpulse.ai/podcast-notes-and-takeaways/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy-kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep391)). This came from a search snippet and is weakly sourced.\n- **Calls that worked:**\n  - Cedar, Rightway, and Cadence each reached a $1B+ valuation ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). Rightway hit $1.1B in Mar 2021 ([src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/rightway-raises-100m-at-1-1b-valuation-to-continue-redefining-the-member-experience-through-better-care-navigation-and-a-new-to-the-world-pbm-301257115.html)). Cadence hit $1B in Dec 2021, four months after launch ([src](https://www.fiercehealthcare.com/digital-health/remote-care-management-platform-cadence-hits-1b-valuation-100m-series-b)).\n  - He co-led Thrive's nearly $2B check into Stripe in 2023, Thrive's largest at that point ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- **Calls that went wrong:** nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Colossus, \"The New World\" (Jeremy Stern, Oct 2025). The best account of his background, his role at Thrive, and the three unicorns he incubated ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n2. Invest Like the Best Ep. 392, \"Small Ideas Attract Competition.\" His longest on-record talk, with dedicated healthcare chapters at 51:30–59:18 ([src](https://colossus.com/?p=48040)).\n3. Turpentine VC E76. Thrive's strategy, talent model, and founder partnership in his words ([src](https://metacast.app/podcast/turpentine-vc-venture-capital-and-investing/LUJoKypp/e76-thrive-capital-s-bold-bets-on-category-defining-companies-with-kareem-zaki/aHs2DHLD)).\n4. A Letter a Day #154. Bloomberg transcript with Kushner on the flywheel between building and investing ([src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-154-josh-kushner-and-kareem)).\n5. Finding Genius on Cadence. How an incubation runs in practice ([src](https://findinggenius.substack.com/p/cadence-christopher-altchek-and-kareem)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no confirmed active X or Substack presence. Thrive deliberately keeps a low public profile, which fits ([src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-154-josh-kushner-and-kareem)). He is amplified mostly through Kushner's public praise, quoted on ILTB and in Colossus ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cedar | Co-founder; board from 2016 | 2016 | n/a (co-founder) | — | [src](https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/KAREEM-ZAKI-A2F3HT/), [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Cedar | Series A ($13M) | 2017-12 | Unclear (Beckers names all four firms as leads; Zaki's role unverified) | Founders Fund, Martin Ventures, SV Angel | [src](https://www.beckersasc.com/asc-coding-billing-and-collections/after-raising-13m-cedar-launches-provider-patient-billing-platform-5-insights.html) |\n| Cedar | Series C ($102M) | 2020-06 | No (a16z led) | a16z, Founders Fund | [src](https://fintech.global/2020/06/24/healthcare-payments-app-cedar-nets-102m-in-its-series-c-round/) |\n| Rightway | Series B ($20M) | 2019-11 | Yes (Thrive led; Zaki quoted) | — | [src](https://www.biospace.com/rightway-healthcare-secures-20-million-in-series-b-financing-to-expand-its-innovative-healthcare-navigation-platform) |\n| Rightway | Series C ($100M, $1.1B val.) | 2021-03 | No (Khosla led) | Khosla Ventures, Tiger Global | [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/rightway-raises-100m-at-1-1b-valuation-to-continue-redefining-the-member-experience-through-better-care-navigation-and-a-new-to-the-world-pbm-301257115.html) |\n| Nava Benefits | Co-founder | 2019 | n/a (co-founder) | — | [src](https://coverager.com/nava-benefits-raises-40-million/) |\n| Nava Benefits | Series B ($40M) | undated (likely ~2022, unverified) | Yes (Thrive led) | GV, Homebrew, K5, Avid, Quiet, Sound Ventures | [src](https://coverager.com/nava-benefits-raises-40-million/) |\n| Cadence | Co-founder; launch round ($41M) | 2021-08-05 | Co-led (General Catalyst and Thrive) | General Catalyst, Adam Boehler, Metrodora Ventures, Martin Ventures | [src](https://www.cadence.care/post/introducing-cadence-new-health-tech-company) |\n| Cadence | Series B ($100M, $1B val.) | 2021-12-14 | No (Coatue led) | Coatue, General Catalyst | [src](https://www.fiercehealthcare.com/digital-health/remote-care-management-platform-cadence-hits-1b-valuation-100m-series-b) |\n| Stripe | Series I (>$6.5B) | 2023-03 | No round lead named; Zaki co-led Thrive's ~$2B check | a16z, Baillie Gifford, Founders Fund, General Catalyst, MSD, GIC, Temasek, GS AWM | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/), [src](https://stripe.com/en-bg/newsroom/news/stripe-series-i) |\n| Scope Security | Co-founder / incubation (unverified) | 2019 (unverified) | — | — | [src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-thrive-capitals-kareem-zaki-a83) |\n| Motion Clinical | Board director (round unknown) | — | — | — | [src](https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/KAREEM-ZAKI-A2F3HT/) |\n| Affirm, Lemonade, Ramp, Trade Republic, Robinhood | Investments (rounds and dates unknown) | — | — | — | [src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-thrive-capitals-kareem-zaki-a83), [src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-154-josh-kushner-and-kareem) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Cedar (2016).** Florian Otto, an ex-maxillofacial surgeon who worked at McKinsey and was head of sales at ZocDoc, started Cedar after his wife's 2015 ER visit led to a confusing stream of bills and a collector ([src](https://findinggenius.substack.com/p/cedar)). Several sources list the co-founders as Otto, Arel Lidow, and Zaki, founded in New York in 2016 (secondary; [src](https://www.vcsheet.com/company/cedar)). Zaki has been on the board since 2016 ([src](https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/KAREEM-ZAKI-A2F3HT/)). Colossus calls Cedar one of the companies he \"co-founded and incubated\" at Thrive ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). Founders Fund and Thrive came in on the $13M Series A in Dec 2017 ([src](https://www.beckersasc.com/asc-coding-billing-and-collections/after-raising-13m-cedar-launches-provider-patient-billing-platform-5-insights.html)). How Otto and Zaki met: (unverified). That Oscar was the pattern-setter is plausible, but no source ties Cedar's origin to Oscar.\n- **Cadence (2020–21).** Chris Altchek founded Mic (2011–2018). After selling it, he joined Thrive as an entrepreneur-in-residence and worked with Thrive's incubations team on health tech ([src](https://findinggenius.substack.com/p/cadence-christopher-altchek-and-kareem)). Out of that came Cadence, with Altchek as CEO and Zaki as co-founder and board member. It launched on 2021-08-05 with $41M co-led by General Catalyst and Thrive. LifePoint Health was the founding health-system partner, starting with heart failure patients in one market ([src](https://www.cadence.care/post/introducing-cadence-new-health-tech-company)). Four months later Coatue led a $100M round at a $1B valuation ([src](https://www.fiercehealthcare.com/digital-health/remote-care-management-platform-cadence-hits-1b-valuation-100m-series-b)). The thesis: chronic conditions such as hypertension, heart disease, and Type 2 diabetes are badly managed outside \"the four walls of a hospital,\" and no platform unifies patient data ([src](https://findinggenius.substack.com/p/cadence-christopher-altchek-and-kareem)). This is the clearest documented example of the Thrive EIR-to-incubation model. MarketScreener now lists his Cadence directorship as \"former\" ([src](https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/KAREEM-ZAKI-A2F3HT/)).\n- **Rightway (2017–).** Colossus counts Rightway among the three unicorns Zaki \"co-founded and incubated\" ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). Rightway's own materials credit Jordan Feldman (CEO) and his father, Dr. Ted Feldman, as founders in 2017 (secondary, via search; [src](https://www.rightwayhealthcare.com/about)). Thrive led the $20M Series B in Nov 2019, and Zaki said there is \"significant demand for an improved healthcare navigation solution\" ([src](https://www.biospace.com/rightway-healthcare-secures-20-million-in-series-b-financing-to-expand-its-innovative-healthcare-navigation-platform)). The exact nature of his co-founding role is unresolved.\n- **Nava Benefits (2019).** A benefits brokerage for small and mid-sized companies, co-founded with CEO Brandon Weber. Thrive led the $40M Series B ([src](https://coverager.com/nava-benefits-raises-40-million/)).\n- **Oscar Health.** Kushner and Mario Schlosser founded Oscar after reading the ACA. Zaki helped informally on nights and weekends while at Blackstone, and Colossus places this right before he joined Thrive ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). No formal Oscar role for Zaki found.\n- **Stripe (2023).** He co-led Thrive's nearly $2B check in Stripe's 2023 round, Thrive's largest check to that point. Colossus also says he helped raise about $5B more ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). The round was Stripe's >$6.5B Series I at a $50B valuation ([src](https://stripe.com/en-bg/newsroom/news/stripe-series-i)).\n- **Thrive Holdings (2025–).** A Thrive spinout that buys traditional services businesses and puts AI into their workflows, \"akin to a private equity firm for AI.\"\n  - Zaki and Anuj Mehndiratta are named as founding members.\n  - OpenAI took an ownership stake in Dec 2025.\n  - In Aug 2026 it raised $2B at a $12B valuation from SoftBank, D1, and Altimeter, with more than 70 businesses on its platforms.\n  - It runs two platforms: Current (accounting) and Shield (IT). Part of the new money funds a third platform for regulatory services for the built environment.\n  - Zaki's pitch: AI \"partnered with... the experts and practitioners\" can compress regulatory bottlenecks while keeping safety standards high ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/openai-backed-thrive-holdings-raises-2b-to-bring-ai-to-the-enterprise/)).\n  - Shield launched 2025-06-05 with more than $100M from Thrive Holdings and ZBS Partners ([src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250605512954/en)).\n  - It fits his background: Blackstone PE combined with Thrive's company building.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Thrive Capital | General Partner | 2014 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/), [src](https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/KAREEM-ZAKI-A2F3HT/) |\n| Thrive Holdings | Founding member | 2025 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/openai-backed-thrive-holdings-raises-2b-to-bring-ai-to-the-enterprise/) |\n| Blackstone | PE investor (about 3 years) | until 2014-01 | [src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-thrive-capitals-kareem-zaki-a83), [src](https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/KAREEM-ZAKI-A2F3HT/) |\n| Josh Kushner | Thrive founder and partner; Zaki helped informally on Oscar pre-Thrive | pre-2014 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Vince Mallick | Thrive partner; the three main faces of Thrive | 2014 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Anuj Mehndiratta | Fellow founding member of Thrive Holdings | 2025 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/openai-backed-thrive-holdings-raises-2b-to-bring-ai-to-the-enterprise/) |\n| Florian Otto | Cedar co-founder (CEO) | 2016 | [src](https://www.vcsheet.com/company/cedar) |\n| Cedar | Co-founder, board director | 2016 | [src](https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/KAREEM-ZAKI-A2F3HT/) |\n| Chris Altchek | Cadence co-founder (CEO); ex-Thrive EIR | 2020–21 | [src](https://findinggenius.substack.com/p/cadence-christopher-altchek-and-kareem) |\n| Cadence | Co-founder, board (now former) | 2021 | [src](https://www.cadence.care/post/introducing-cadence-new-health-tech-company) |\n| Jordan Feldman | Rightway CEO; Thrive led Series B | 2019 | [src](https://www.biospace.com/rightway-healthcare-secures-20-million-in-series-b-financing-to-expand-its-innovative-healthcare-navigation-platform) |\n| Brandon Weber | Nava co-founder (CEO) | 2019 | [src](https://coverager.com/nava-benefits-raises-40-million/) |\n| Motion Clinical | Board director | unknown | [src](https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/KAREEM-ZAKI-A2F3HT/) |\n| Oscar Health | Informal helper before joining Thrive | pre-2014 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| General Catalyst | Co-lead on Cadence launch round | 2021 | [src](https://www.cadence.care/post/introducing-cadence-new-health-tech-company) |\n| Founders Fund | Co-investor in Cedar Series A | 2017 | [src](https://www.beckersasc.com/asc-coding-billing-and-collections/after-raising-13m-cedar-launches-provider-patient-billing-platform-5-insights.html) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Rightway: what does \"co-founded and incubated\" mean when Rightway credits the Feldmans? Did Thrive seed the idea or the founding team?\n- Cedar: how did Otto and Lidow come to Thrive, and was Cedar spun out of Oscar-era learnings?\n- Scope Security: is it the healthcare security company he co-founded in 2019? Its status today?\n- Motion Clinical: incubation or investment? When did he join the board?\n- ILTB Ep. 392 is now covered via the podscripts transcript (2026-11-09). Still missing: transcripts of Turpentine E76 and E14 (on talent: hiring people with 0–4 years of experience) and the 20VC \"one rule\" episode.\n- Did he personally drive any part of Thrive's OpenAI position? He speaks of it only as \"we.\"\n- Named deals he passed on, or a specific admitted mistake: none found.\n- Identify the X user @andrewlangadams, who says they work with Zaki daily.\n- Confirm the Forbes 30 Under 30 2018 listing and his education (HBS?) from primary sources.\n- Thrive Holdings: his exact title and remit, and whether he leads the new built-environment regulatory platform.\n- Whether he still sits on the Cedar, Rightway, and Nava boards. One aggregator mentions an Alliant acquisition of Nava (Aug 2026, unverified).\n\n## Sources\n- https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/ (Jeremy Stern, Colossus, Oct 2025)\n- https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-thrive-capitals-kareem-zaki-a83\n- https://colossus.com/?p=48040 (Invest Like the Best Ep. 392)\n- https://audioscrape.com/podcast/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/episode/kareem-zaki---small-ideas-attract-competition---invest-like-the-best-ep392\n- https://metacast.app/podcast/turpentine-vc-venture-capital-and-investing/LUJoKypp/e76-thrive-capital-s-bold-bets-on-category-defining-companies-with-kareem-zaki/aHs2DHLD (403 on fetch)\n- https://listenlite.horsy.ai/blog/turpentine_vc_thrive_capitals_bold_bets_on_categorydefining_companies_with_kareem_zaki (third-party summary)\n- https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-154-josh-kushner-and-kareem\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/openai-backed-thrive-holdings-raises-2b-to-bring-ai-to-the-enterprise/\n- https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250605512954/en\n- https://findinggenius.substack.com/p/cadence-christopher-altchek-and-kareem\n- https://findinggenius.substack.com/p/cedar\n- https://www.cadence.care/post/introducing-cadence-new-health-tech-company\n- https://www.fiercehealthcare.com/digital-health/remote-care-management-platform-cadence-hits-1b-valuation-100m-series-b\n- https://www.biospace.com/rightway-healthcare-secures-20-million-in-series-b-financing-to-expand-its-innovative-healthcare-navigation-platform\n- https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/rightway-raises-100m-at-1-1b-valuation-to-continue-redefining-the-member-experience-through-better-care-navigation-and-a-new-to-the-world-pbm-301257115.html\n- https://www.rightwayhealthcare.com/about\n- https://coverager.com/nava-benefits-raises-40-million/\n- https://www.beckersasc.com/asc-coding-billing-and-collections/after-raising-13m-cedar-launches-provider-patient-billing-platform-5-insights.html\n- https://fintech.global/2020/06/24/healthcare-payments-app-cedar-nets-102m-in-its-series-c-round/\n- https://www.vcsheet.com/company/cedar\n- https://stripe.com/en-bg/newsroom/news/stripe-series-i\n- https://de.marketscreener.com/insider/KAREEM-ZAKI-A2F3HT/\n- https://www.crunchbase.com/person/kareem-zaki (403 on fetch)\n- https://upcarta.onrender.com/profile/kareemszaki (404 on fetch; search snippet only)\n- https://podpulse.ai/podcast-notes-and-takeaways/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy-kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep391 (search snippet only)\n- https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/kareem-zaki-small-ideas-attract-competition-invest-like-the-best-ep392 (third-party ILTB transcript, no speaker labels; accessed 2026-11-09)\n- https://x.com/andrewlangadams/status/1848142140108656647\n- https://x.com/patrick_oshag/status/1843645915388883193\n", "person:karim-atiyeh": "\n# Karim Atiyeh\n\n## One-line take\nThe technical half of Ramp's founding pair: his angel cheque tends to mean a product has been looked at by one of the most demanding AI-tool buyers in New York.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why founders want him:** Ramp is both a reference customer and a talent brand. He says of Ramp, \"We are avid buyers and testers of a lot of different tools\" [src](https://8vc.com/resources/karim-atiyeh-ramp-fireside-chat).\n- **Pattern:** applications and agent tooling, not models: Cognition, Crosby, Campfire, Unify, AgentMail, plus Prime Intellect on the compute side. His stated view: \"There aren't enough efforts going into the application layer\" [src](https://8vc.com/resources/karim-atiyeh-ramp-fireside-chat).\n- **Constraint:** co-CEO of Ramp since June 2026 [src](https://www.paymentsdive.com/news/ramp-names-karim-atiyeh-co-ceo-glyman-cto-Rahul-Sengottuvelu/824283/). Personal cheques only; no lead, no board.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Founders reach him through Ramp alumni. Unify's seed post, written by a co-founder who was on Ramp's early team, thanks \"Ramp's founders Eric Glyman, Karim Atiyeh, and Gene Lee\" [src](https://www.unifygtm.com/blog/announcing-our-seed-round).\n- On the co-CEO move he wrote \"This only makes it formal\", and \"Strategy decisions are increasingly technology and systems-design decisions\" [src](https://www.paymentsdive.com/news/ramp-names-karim-atiyeh-co-ceo-glyman-cto-Rahul-Sengottuvelu/824283/).\n- CB Insights counts 42 investments (aggregator) [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/karim-atiyeh); only 10 companies are named in this repo, so most of the book is unlisted or pre-AI (Atrium, Ro, Nuvocargo, Very Good Security, Medal per [AngelBacked](https://www.angelbacked.co/investor/karim-atiyeh-paribus)).\n- Bind AI (October 2026) has the same co-investors as 1Fort (Bonfire, Draper), so it may be the same company renamed (aggregator; not confirmed) [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/karim-atiyeh).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh for AI-native automation: his cheques are a usable early signal for application and agent companies that a serious operator would buy. Deals that matter: Cognition (2024), Crosby (seed 2025), Prime Intellect (A 2026), AgentMail (seed 2026).\n\n## Background\nCo-founded Paribus with Eric Glyman in 2014; Capital One bought it two years later. Co-founded Ramp in 2019 as CTO; became co-CEO in late June 2026, with Rahul Sengottuvelu (head of applied AI) taking over as CTO [src](https://www.paymentsdive.com/news/ramp-names-karim-atiyeh-co-ceo-glyman-cto-Rahul-Sengottuvelu/824283/).\n\n## Vehicle and cheque size\nPersonal, as far as sources show; in Unify's seed with Eric Glyman and Gene Lee. No fund or SPV found. Cheque size not disclosed.\n\n## How founders get to him\nRamp alumni and Ramp vendors. Unify's co-founder Austin Hughes joined Ramp in 2020 [src](https://www.unifygtm.com/blog/announcing-our-seed-round). No evidence of cold inbound working.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://ramp.com | Company |\n| Substack / blog | | |\n| X | https://x.com/karimatiyeh | not checked |\n| LinkedIn | | Posts quoted by Payments Dive, 2026-07 |\n| GitHub | | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-04-25 (event February 2024) — Chat8VC, New York — fireside with Nik Koblov and 8VC's Bhaskar Ghosh — [link](https://8vc.com/resources/karim-atiyeh-ramp-fireside-chat) — application layer, hybrid model strategy, stack-ranking priorities\n- 2026-07-02 — Payments Dive — co-CEO announcement, quoting his LinkedIn post — [link](https://www.paymentsdive.com/news/ramp-names-karim-atiyeh-co-ceo-glyman-cto-Rahul-Sengottuvelu/824283/)\n\n## How they think\n- Prioritisation: \"I religiously sit down and stack rank priorities\" [src](https://8vc.com/resources/karim-atiyeh-ramp-fireside-chat).\n- Model strategy is hybrid: small special-purpose models where Ramp has data, general models elsewhere [src](https://8vc.com/resources/karim-atiyeh-ramp-fireside-chat).\n- Nothing public on how he picks angel investments, and no pass or regret found.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Chat8VC fireside](https://8vc.com/resources/karim-atiyeh-ramp-fireside-chat): the only long-form source on how he thinks about AI products.\n- [Payments Dive on the co-CEO change](https://www.paymentsdive.com/news/ramp-names-karim-atiyeh-co-ceo-glyman-cto-Rahul-Sengottuvelu/824283/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot mined this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n10 named companies (5 at pre-seed or seed). Rows from CB Insights and Seedtable are aggregator-only.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Role | Early (pre-seed/seed)? | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bind AI | Seed ($10M) | 2026-10-14 | angel | yes | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/karim-atiyeh) |\n| Augustus | Series B ($180M) | 2026-07-21 | angel | no | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/karim-atiyeh) |\n| Prime Intellect | Series A | 2026-07-08 | angel | no | [src](https://intelcapital.com/prime-intellect-raises-130m-series-a-to-build-the-open-superintelligence-stack) |\n| AgentMail | Seed ($6M) | 2026-03-10 | angel | yes | [src](https://www.agentmail.to/blog/agentmail-seed-launch) |\n| Campfire | Series B ($65M) | 2025-10-16 | angel | no | [src](https://pulse2.com/campfire-65-million-series-b-raised-to-advance-ai-based-finance-transformation) |\n| Crosby | Seed | 2025-06-17 | angel | yes | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/06/17/sequoia-backed-crosby-launches-a-new-kind-of-ai-powered-law-firm) |\n| Granola | Series B ($43M) | 2025-05-14 | angel | no | [src](https://seedtable.com/investors/karim-atiyeh) |\n| 1Fort | Seed | 2025-04 | angel | yes | [src](https://seedtable.com/investors/karim-atiyeh) |\n| Cognition | Series A | 2024-03-12 | participant | no | [src](https://cognition.com/blog/introducing-devin) |\n| Unify | Seed ($6.6M) | 2024-02 | angel | yes | [src](https://www.unifygtm.com/blog/announcing-our-seed-round) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Unify (seed, $6.6M):** OpenAI Startup Fund, Thrive and Emergence led; all three Ramp founders joined as angels; the founder link is a former Ramp growth lead [src](https://www.unifygtm.com/blog/announcing-our-seed-round).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Eric Glyman | Co-founder (Paribus, Ramp); co-angel | 2014 | [src](https://www.paymentsdive.com/news/ramp-names-karim-atiyeh-co-ceo-glyman-cto-Rahul-Sengottuvelu/824283/) |\n| Gene Lee | Ramp co-founder; co-angel in Unify | 2019 | [src](https://www.unifygtm.com/blog/announcing-our-seed-round) |\n| Rahul Sengottuvelu | Succeeded him as Ramp CTO | 2026-06 | [src](https://www.paymentsdive.com/news/ramp-names-karim-atiyeh-co-ceo-glyman-cto-Rahul-Sengottuvelu/824283/) |\n| 8VC (Bhaskar Ghosh) | Hosted Ramp fireside | 2024 | [src](https://8vc.com/resources/karim-atiyeh-ramp-fireside-chat) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Cheque size; whether there is a joint vehicle with Glyman.\n- Which lead investors he follows: Thrive and Founders Fund (Ramp's own backers) appear in several of his deals but this is not established.\n- Confirm Granola, 1Fort, Bind AI and AI or Not from primary sources.\n- Thin on voice: no podcast found where he talks about investing.\n\n## Sources\nhttps://8vc.com/resources/karim-atiyeh-ramp-fireside-chat ; https://www.paymentsdive.com/news/ramp-names-karim-atiyeh-co-ceo-glyman-cto-Rahul-Sengottuvelu/824283/ ; https://www.unifygtm.com/blog/announcing-our-seed-round ; https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/karim-atiyeh ; https://seedtable.com/investors/karim-atiyeh (403 when fetched; snippet only) ; https://www.angelbacked.co/investor/karim-atiyeh-paribus\n", "person:karol-niewiadomski": "\n# Karol Niewiadomski\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. The vehicle is in [softbank-vision-fund-2.md](../firms/softbank-vision-fund-2.md), the parent and its other pools in [softbank-group.md](../firms/softbank-group.md), and the deal ledger in [softbank-ai-ledger.md](../sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md).*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the working partner under Vikas Parekh on SoftBank's OpenAI position and has been since the first check, but he is a late-stage fintech investor by training and has no other AI deal. He is a person who knows the OpenAI file, not a person who decides it.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** proximity to the largest position in the fund. The Vision Fund's OpenAI page listed only two people on 2024-11-09, five weeks after the first $500M check: \"Karol Niewiadomski Partner, Americas\" and Parekh ([archived page](https://web.archive.org/web/20241109135953/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai)). A third, Kiran Kazmi, appears between 2025-10 and 2026-01 ([archived page](https://web.archive.org/web/20260124121850/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai)). SoftBank has since put a cumulative $64.6B into OpenAI through Vision Fund 2 ([ledger](../sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md)).\n- **What he personally signs, sources or prices:** nothing that is documented for OpenAI. No release, filing or press report names him on any OpenAI tranche. The public record has Masayoshi Son negotiating and SoftBank Group's board and CFO approving ([softbank-group.md](../firms/softbank-group.md)). For Vision Fund 2 in general, a US prospectus says the manager, SB Global Advisers, is \"responsible for making all decisions related to the acquisition, structuring, financing and disposal\" of the fund's investments, and names its directors as Alex Clavel, Yoshimitsu Goto and Navneet Govil ([Ethos 424B4, 2026-01-28](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1788451/000119312526029993/d901135d424b4.htm)). He is not one of them. His role on OpenAI is unconfirmed; the likely reading is execution and portfolio monitoring.\n- **What he did sign his name to:** in 2021 he was SoftBank's quoted investor on three rounds that SoftBank led and priced (Revolut, Forto, Envelop Risk) and took a supervisory board seat on a fourth (MODIVO). All four were Vision Fund 2 deals in Europe.\n- **What constrains him:** the fund is about half OpenAI by cost and makes few new investments ([softbank-vision-fund-2.md](../firms/softbank-vision-fund-2.md)). No new company has been added to his page since 2021 except OpenAI.\n- **What he'd never do:** a seed. His earliest entry is a $130M Series B. He has no commercial tie to offer a company either; nothing on compute, distribution or customer commitments is attached to any deal he is named on.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He was on the OpenAI team from the start, before SoftBank became OpenAI's largest outside investor.** The 2024-11-09 archive of the fund page shows \"Karol Niewiadomski Partner, Americas\" beside Parekh ([archived page](https://web.archive.org/web/20241109135953/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai)). The 2025-02-11 archive shows Parekh alone, and he is back by 2025-06-17; whether that gap is real or a page-rendering fault is unconfirmed.\n- **OpenAI is the outlier in his book.** In his own words he has spent \"the majority of my time in fintech, consumer internet\", and some in software ([Vision Fund](https://visionfund.com/team/karol-niewiadomski)). His page lists eight companies. Seven are 2021 deals in fintech, logistics, consumer and computer vision.\n- **He changed region in late 2021 and his portfolio did not.** Archived team pages give \"Karol Niewiadomski Director, EMEAI\" on [2021-08-05](https://web.archive.org/web/20210805164722/https://visionfund.com/team/karol-niewiadomski), \"Director, Americas\" on [2021-12-04](https://web.archive.org/web/20211204190453/https://visionfund.com/team/karol-niewiadomski) and \"Partner, Americas\" on [2022-05-20](https://web.archive.org/web/20220520162655/https://visionfund.com/team/karol-niewiadomski). Six of his eight companies are still tagged EMEA. The Partner promotion therefore falls between 2021-12 and 2022-05; no announcement was found.\n- **He inherited the fintech names from partners who left.** Klarna's 2021 release quoted Yanni Pipilis and Ethos's quoted Munish Varma ([TechCrunch on Klarna](https://techcrunch.com/2021/06/10/fintech-giant-klarna-raises-639m-at-a-45-6b-valuation/), [TechCrunch on Ethos](https://techcrunch.com/2021/07/21/ethos-picks-up-100m-at-a-2-7b-valuation-for-a-big-data-platform-to-improve-life-insurance-accessibility)). Both were reported to \"step down and join Misra's $6bn new fund\" in 2022-08 ([GCV, 2022-08-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/juneja-expands-role-softbank/?format=md)). His name is now on both pages, and he is the only name on Ethos. That he took them over on their departure is my inference, not a stated fact.\n- **His MODIVO deal was debt in equity clothing.** SoftBank bought PLN 500M of \"convertible bonds\" in eobuwie.pl, to convert at an IPO planned within 12 to 24 months, and he \"will join the Supervisory Board of eobuwie.pl\" ([MODIVO release, 2021](https://modivoplatform.com/en/eobuwie-pl-will-obtain-pln-500-million-from-softbank-vision-fund-2)). The IPO did not happen. In 2023-11 the maturity was pushed to 2026-04 with a \"revised fixed interest rate of 10.99% per annum\" ([CCC report 58/2023](https://modivoplatform.com/en/current-report-no-58-2023)). On 2025-06-12 MODIVO \"made an early full redemption\" using a UniCredit loan ([CCC report 50/2025](https://modivoplatform.com/en/current-report-no-50-2025)). The fund got its money and interest back; it never held equity.\n- **Ethos listed well below SoftBank's price.** SoftBank's $100M in 2021 matches the Series D-1 in the prospectus: 2.192M shares at \"Series D-1 $45.6162 per share\" after a 1-for-7 reverse split. The IPO on 2026-01-28 priced at $19.00. SoftBank-affiliated entities held 3,128,902 Class A shares before and after, so they sold none ([Ethos 424B4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1788451/000119312526029993/d901135d424b4.htm)). At the IPO price that holding is about $59M (my arithmetic). The prospectus lists no SoftBank director.\n- **Quoted, but not always on the board.** On Envelop Risk's $130M Series B he gave the SoftBank quote as \"senior investor\", and Neil Cunha-Gomes \"will join Envelop Risk's board of directors\" ([Insurance Journal, 2021-09-14](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/international/2021/09/14/631646.htm)).\n- **He has invested his own money before.** \"became an active angel investor myself\", then \"established my own little specialist financing business\" ([Vision Fund](https://visionfund.com/team/karol-niewiadomski)). No angel deal or business name was found.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** He works growth and pre-IPO rounds from a captive fund that is 98% SoftBank Group's own money. For a ~$20M check into a frontier-AI seed he is not a co-investor, not an LP, and not the person who sets a later price.\n\nWhat he is: **a well-placed contact on the OpenAI position.** If I need to understand how SoftBank's OpenAI stake is being managed (tranche timing, secondary appetite, how the fund thinks about the listing), he and Parekh are the two people who have been on the file since 2024-10. He is also a reasonable contact for any company of mine that later needs a large fintech or consumer growth round.\n\n**Who to know instead, for my purposes:** for a new-lab or compute deal with SoftBank, the decision is Masayoshi Son's and the fund's approvers (Alex Clavel). For earlier AI checks from Vision Fund 2, Sumer Juneja's group (Sarthak Misra, Nahoko Hoshino) is closer to my stage; see [sumer-juneja.md](sumer-juneja.md).\n\nThe one deal that matters to my lens is **OpenAI**, and SoftBank entered it about nine years after founding.\n\n## Background\n- MSc in Finance, Warsaw School of Economics; Master's in International Management, University of Cologne ([Vision Fund](https://visionfund.com/team/karol-niewiadomski)). Dates not found.\n- An investment bank in London \"right out of university\" (bank not named; same source).\n- Silver Lake Partners, principal (same source). Dates not found.\n- Angel investing, then his own \"specialist financing business\" (same source). Not named; dates not found.\n- SoftBank: join date not found. He says he first heard of the Vision Fund and got an offer \"a year and a half later\" (same source). The fund was announced in 2016-10, which suggests about 2018; unconfirmed.\n- Director, EMEAI by 2021-08; Director, Americas by 2021-12; Partner, Americas by 2022-05 (archived team pages, linked above).\n- Press titles in 2021: \"senior investor for SoftBank Investment Advisers\" (Revolut, Forto, Envelop Risk) and \"Investment Director\" (MODIVO).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [visionfund.com/team/karol-niewiadomski](https://visionfund.com/team/karol-niewiadomski) | Firm bio with a five-question Q&A. Loads as of 2026-11-10. The only first-person text found |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | No profile URL could be confirmed by search; not listed on the firm page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\nHow to reach him: no public channel. The firm page has no contact link. The practical routes are a warm introduction through a co-investor in OpenAI, or through Vikas Parekh's Americas team. He says his favourite part of the job is \"getting to meet the founders\" (firm page), but nothing shows him taking inbound.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. No podcast, panel or conference talk came up in search.\n\n## How they think\n- **Sector:** fintech first. He calls it \"the first area where tech enablement really took hold\" (firm page).\n- **Process:** diligence-led. Trust comes when \"during intense due diligence you begin to really understand the company\" (firm page). This is a late-stage rhythm.\n- **Founders:** he looks for persistence under bad news, \"having your eyes wide open\" (firm page).\n- **Pattern in his deals:** SoftBank leads and sets the price, often as the sole or dominant new investor (Ethos sole investor; MODIVO sole bond subscriber; Envelop Risk no co-investor named). All were struck in mid-2021.\n- **Calls right or wrong:** Revolut at $33B in 2021 has worked. Ethos listed below the entry price. Oosto was sold in 2025 for a reported $125M after a $235M round. MODIVO came back as repaid debt. Klarna listed in 2025-09; I did not check its price against the 2021 entry.\n- No contrarian views on record.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [His Vision Fund Q&A](https://visionfund.com/team/karol-niewiadomski): the only place he speaks in the first person.\n2. [Archived OpenAI team page, 2024-11-09](https://web.archive.org/web/20241109135953/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai): shows the original two-person team.\n3. [CCC report 58/2023](https://modivoplatform.com/en/current-report-no-58-2023): the MODIVO bond terms, the clearest view of a structure he worked.\n4. [Ethos 424B4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1788451/000119312526029993/d901135d424b4.htm): who legally decides for Vision Fund 2, and the Series D-1 price.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X account or public posting was identified.\n\n## Portfolio\nAll positions are held by SoftBank Vision Fund 2. \"Led?\" refers to SoftBank, not to him personally.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | His documented role | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | Growth ($6.6B at $157B); later tranches to $64.6B cumulative | 2024-10 onward | no in 2024; lead or co-lead from 2025 | Thrive, Microsoft, NVIDIA and others | On the fund-page team from 2024-11-09. Tranche and role not stated | [archive](https://web.archive.org/web/20241109135953/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai) |\n| Revolut | Series E ($800M at $33B) | 2021-07 | yes, co-lead | Tiger Global | Quoted as senior investor | [Slaughter and May](https://www.slaughterandmay.com/recent-work/softbank-vision-fund-2-as-lead-investor-in-revolut-s-series-f-fundraising-valuing-revolut-at-33-billion/) |\n| Forto | Growth ($240M at $1.2B) | 2021-06 | yes | Citi Ventures, Unbound | Quoted as senior investor | [The Loadstar](https://theloadstar.com/softbanks-financial-gamble-on-tech-firms-like-forto-staying-ahead-of-the-game/) |\n| Envelop Risk | Series B ($130M) | 2021-09 | yes | none named | Quoted; board seat went to Neil Cunha-Gomes | [Insurance Journal](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/international/2021/09/14/631646.htm) |\n| MODIVO (eobuwie.pl) | Debt (PLN 500M convertible bonds) | 2021-10 (issue) | yes, sole | none | Named to the Supervisory Board. Redeemed 2025-06 | [MODIVO](https://modivoplatform.com/en/eobuwie-pl-will-obtain-pln-500-million-from-softbank-vision-fund-2) |\n| Ethos Life | Series D-1 ($100M at $2.7B) | 2021-07 | yes, sole | none | Only name on the fund page. Release quoted Munish Varma. Board seat per aggregators only; unconfirmed | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/ethos) |\n| Klarna | Growth ($639M at $45.6B) | 2021-06 | yes | Adit, Honeycomb, WestCap | On the fund-page team with Jaime Ocampo. Release quoted Yanni Pipilis | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/klarna) |\n| Oosto (AnyVision) | Growth ($235M) | 2021-07 | yes, co-lead | Eldridge | On the fund-page team with Amit Lubovsky. Role not stated | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/oosto) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **OpenAI.** No account of his part exists. What is documented: he and Parekh were the page team within five weeks of the first check; Kazmi joined about a year later, after the $40B round. How the deal itself happened (Son's pursuit of a larger stake, the tender, the tranches) is in the [ledger](../sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md).\n- **MODIVO.** A pre-IPO bond with mandatory conversion at listing and a voluntary conversion right at an agreed PLN 6B equity value. When the IPO slipped, the 2023 amendment extended maturity, raised the coupon to 10.99% (capitalised, non-cash) and removed the issuer's early-repayment right. MODIVO still redeemed early in 2025, which the CCC report describes as exercising its right; the terms that allowed it are not explained. This is the SoftBank pattern of structured downside protection in a 2021 deal.\n- **Ethos.** SoftBank was the sole investor in a $100M round two months after a $200M round at just over $2B (TechCrunch). The company listed four and a half years later at well under that price. SoftBank held through the IPO.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Vikas J. Parekh | Managing Partner, Americas; the senior name on the OpenAI team. Reporting line not stated | 2024-11 | [archive](https://web.archive.org/web/20241109135953/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai) |\n| Kiran Kazmi | Director, Americas; third OpenAI team member | by 2026-01 | [archive](https://web.archive.org/web/20260124121850/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai) |\n| Jaime Ocampo | Director, Americas; with him on Klarna, Revolut and MODIVO | not stated | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/revolut) |\n| Nahoko Hoshino | Director, EMEA; with him on Forto | not stated | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/forto) |\n| Neil Cunha-Gomes | Took the Envelop Risk board seat on his deal | 2021-09 | [Insurance Journal](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/international/2021/09/14/631646.htm) |\n| Yanni Pipilis, Munish Varma | Former managing partners who fronted Klarna and Ethos; left 2022-08 | 2021 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/juneja-expands-role-softbank/?format=md) |\n| Silver Lake | Former employer (principal) | dates not found | [Vision Fund](https://visionfund.com/team/karol-niewiadomski) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What he does on OpenAI: execution, monitoring, or the syndication of the co-investor tranches. Nothing public says.\n- The date and announcement of his promotion to Partner (window: 2021-12 to 2022-05).\n- When he joined SoftBank, and his city now.\n- Whether he sat on the Ethos board. PitchBook and MarketScreener list him as a director or former director; the IPO prospectus does not name him.\n- His angel portfolio and the name of his financing business.\n- Why he disappears from the archived OpenAI page of 2025-02-11.\n- Revolut's own release was not retrievable (403); the quote and title come from the law firm's page.\n\n## Sources\n- https://visionfund.com/team/karol-niewiadomski\n- https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai and archives of 2024-11-09, 2025-02-11, 2025-06-17, 2025-10-10, 2026-01-24\n- Archived team pages: https://web.archive.org/web/20210805164722/https://visionfund.com/team/karol-niewiadomski , https://web.archive.org/web/20211204190453/https://visionfund.com/team/karol-niewiadomski , https://web.archive.org/web/20220520162655/https://visionfund.com/team/karol-niewiadomski\n- https://visionfund.com/portfolio/klarna , /revolut , /ethos , /forto , /envelop-risk , /modivo , /oosto\n- https://www.slaughterandmay.com/recent-work/softbank-vision-fund-2-as-lead-investor-in-revolut-s-series-f-fundraising-valuing-revolut-at-33-billion/\n- https://theloadstar.com/softbanks-financial-gamble-on-tech-firms-like-forto-staying-ahead-of-the-game/\n- https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/international/2021/09/14/631646.htm\n- https://modivoplatform.com/en/eobuwie-pl-will-obtain-pln-500-million-from-softbank-vision-fund-2\n- https://modivoplatform.com/en/current-report-no-58-2023\n- https://modivoplatform.com/en/current-report-no-50-2025\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1788451/000119312526029993/d901135d424b4.htm\n- https://techcrunch.com/2021/07/21/ethos-picks-up-100m-at-a-2-7b-valuation-for-a-big-data-platform-to-improve-life-insurance-accessibility\n- https://techcrunch.com/2021/06/10/fintech-giant-klarna-raises-639m-at-a-45-6b-valuation/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/20/sources-ai-vision-startup-metropolis-is-buying-oosto-formerly-known-as-anyvision-for-just-125m\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/juneja-expands-role-softbank/?format=md\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sbia-scores-four-managing-partners/?format=md (2021-11 promotions; he is not named)\n- Aggregators, secondary: https://www.crunchbase.com/person/karol-niewiadomski , https://pitchbook.com/profiles/person/31299-58P , https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/KAROL-NIEWIADOMSKI-A3DCTP/\n", "person:kathi-wilhelm": "\n# Kathi (Katharina) Wilhelm\n\n## One-line take\nA Berlin partner who left Index for Creandum in 2026 and, within months, co-led a $40M seed into an Imperial College robot-foundation-model lab (Machenta). She is the most direct European seed path into physical-AI research teams that I have found in the Index alumni set.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** she does hands-on technical diligence with research founders. For Machenta she brought her own props into the lab to test one-shot robot learning ([src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-machenta)).\n- **Track record:** she pairs that with a DACH/industrial network from Index, where she led the EthonAI Series A (ETH Zurich PhDs) ([src](https://sg.news.yahoo.com/index-ventures-backs-ai-manufacturing-080626804.html)) and was named alongside Danny Rimer on DeepL's $300M round ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/fluency-is-currency-deepl/)).\n- **Constraints:** she is new at Creandum, a European early-stage firm. Her public deals so far are $11M and $40M seeds, so she is a co-lead partner, not a $600M-lab-seed anchor.\n- **What she'd never do:** nothing sourced. Her stated taste favors \"boring spaces\" and applied science (manufacturing, drug trials, robots) over model-layer consumer plays ([src](https://creandum.com/team/kathi-wilhelm)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Machenta (lab visit):** she personally stress-tested the robot in the founders' lab, and it worked after one demo with one object: \"We showed the robot the task once, with one object.\" ([src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-machenta), 2026-10-27).\n- **Machenta (thesis):** the bet is that robot learning is compute-bound rather than data-bound, which is a compute-thesis angle on robotics: \"Machenta is bounded by compute.\" ([src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-machenta)).\n- **Machenta (one-shot learning):** she frames the case against the field's data-collection race this way: \"humans don't need post-training, so why should robots?\" ([src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-machenta)).\n- **Machenta (lab lift-out):** this is effectively an acqui-founding of a university lab. Vosylius was Ed Johns' most senior PhD student, and almost the whole Imperial Robot Learning Lab joined Machenta ([src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-machenta)). No quote is short enough to row, so it is summary-only.\n- **Atlas Bio:** she led the $11M seed, calling human-drug-response prediction \"probably the highest-stakes of all\" ([src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-atlas-bio), 2026-10-27).\n- **EthonAI timing:** at Index she led EthonAI's Series A as a later entrant. General Catalyst, Earlybird and Founderful were existing investors: \"Katharina Wilhelm, Partner at Index\" ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ethonai-series-a/), 2024-05-30). Index's EthonAI company page lists only Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas, so firm portfolio pages hide who originated the deal ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/ethonai/)).\n- **DeepL credit:** her role on DeepL is visible only in the post's standfirst, \"Index Partners Danny and Kathi share why Index is leading a $300m round\" ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/fluency-is-currency-deepl/), 2024-05-22). The byline and the Index company page credit only Danny Rimer ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/deepl/)).\n- **Self-description:** \"I strive to be a founder's first call when things get messy.\" ([src](https://creandum.com/team/kathi-wilhelm)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium, trending high.**\n- **Machenta (high fit):** a $40M seed, co-led with USV, for a robot foundation model from Imperial's Robot Learning Lab ([src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-machenta); [src](https://www.usv.com/writing/physical-vibe-coding-our-investment-in-machenta)). That is a research-lab spinout whose thesis is compute-bound.\n- **Atlas Bio:** an AI-for-science foundation-model application, adjacent to my lens.\n- **EthonAI and Tacto:** AI-native industrial automation (medium).\n- **DeepL:** a growth-stage language-AI round (low for my stage).\n\nShe is a useful co-investor and source of deal flow for European physical-AI and AI-for-science research spinouts. She is not a source for US new-lab mega-seeds.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** studied at St. Gallen, then an MBA at Harvard Business School, where she first encountered VC ([src](https://creandum.com/team/kathi-wilhelm)). Dates are not on the page.\n- **Before VC:** consultant at BCG, which took her to the US ([src](https://creandum.com/team/kathi-wilhelm)).\n- **Index Ventures, about 2021-2026:** led DACH work. She was titled \"Partner at Index\" by 2024-05-30 ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ethonai-series-a/)). The 2021 start date comes from the index-ventures-roster brief and is unconfirmed by a primary source here.\n- **Creandum, 2026:** moved to Creandum Berlin as a Partner, reported 2026-03-09 ([src](https://www.deutsche-startups.de/2026/03/09/levelbuild-taxdoo-pivot-join-capital-katharina-wilhelm-index-creandum/)). That page returned 403 to me, so the date comes from the roster brief. Creandum describes about eight years of investing and says she leads deals across Europe and the US ([src](https://creandum.com/team/kathi-wilhelm)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://creandum.com/team/kathi-wilhelm | Creandum bio, 2026 |\n| Substack / blog | https://creandum.com/stories | Authored \"Backing Machenta\" and \"Backing Atlas Bio\" (2026-10-27) |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/kathiwilhelm/ | linked from Creandum bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The web search budget ran out during this run, so podcasts and talks were not searched. This is a gap, not evidence that none exist.\n\n## How they think\n- **Physical AI:** robots should learn like humans, from one demonstration. Progress should scale with compute in simulation rather than with real-world data collection ([src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-machenta)).\n- **Industrial AI:** the value is in making sense of factory sensor data. AI is a tool to amplify human ingenuity, not replace it ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ethonai-series-a/)). See also the Reuters quote ([src](https://sg.news.yahoo.com/index-ventures-backs-ai-manufacturing-080626804.html)).\n- **AI for science:** foundation models now make tractable prediction problems that were not tractable two years earlier, including predicting human drug response before the first patient is enrolled ([src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-atlas-bio)).\n- **Contrarian taste:** she likes \"boring spaces\" others dismiss ([src](https://creandum.com/team/kathi-wilhelm)). In practice that has meant SME supply chains (Tacto) and manufacturing quality (EthonAI).\n- **Calls right or wrong:** too early to judge. DeepL's $300M round at Index was a large late-stage European AI round ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/fluency-is-currency-deepl/)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Backing Machenta: the era of Instant Robot Learning](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-machenta) (2026-10-27): her physical-AI thesis and her lab-visit diligence.\n2. [Backing Atlas Bio: The end of trial and error](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-atlas-bio) (2026-10-27): her AI-for-science thesis.\n3. [EthonAI Series A, Index Perspective](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ethonai-series-a/) (2024-05-30): her industrial-AI view at Index.\n4. [Tacto secures EUR50m](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/tacto-secures-50m-to-tackle-disruptions-and-bureaucracy-in-industrial-supply-chains/) (2023-12-12): the SME supply-chain thesis, co-written with Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No personal X account was located, and web search was unavailable for this run.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Machenta | Seed, $40M | 2026-10-27 (announced) | Co-led (Creandum) | USV (co-lead; Rebecca Kaden authored USV post) | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-machenta), [src](https://www.usv.com/writing/physical-vibe-coding-our-investment-in-machenta) |\n| Atlas Bio | Seed, $11M | 2026-10-27 (announced) | Yes (Creandum led) | none named | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-atlas-bio) |\n| EthonAI (at Index) | Series A, CHF15M (~$16.5M) | 2024-05-30 | Yes (Index led; she led for Index) | General Catalyst; Earlybird; Founderful (existing) | [src](https://sg.news.yahoo.com/index-ventures-backs-ai-manufacturing-080626804.html), [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ethonai-series-a/) |\n| DeepL (at Index) | $300M round (stage not named) | 2024-05-22 | Index led; named with Danny Rimer | not named on page | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/fluency-is-currency-deepl/) |\n| Tacto (at Index) | EUR50M (stage not named) | 2023-12-12 | Co-led (Index + Sequoia) | Sequoia (co-lead) | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/tacto-secures-50m-to-tackle-disruptions-and-bureaucracy-in-industrial-supply-chains/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Machenta (Creandum, 2026):**\n  - Ed Johns pitched the core idea at the first meeting with Creandum.\n  - Earlier in 2026, she visited the lab and tested the robot with her own props: Creandum caps, a roll of paper and a lucky cat. It completed the task after a single demonstration, even when the objects were moved ([src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-machenta)).\n  - Johns founded Imperial's Robot Learning Lab and had led robot learning at Dyson. Vosylius, his most senior PhD student, was first author on the Instant Policy paper.\n  - Creandum co-led with USV ([src](https://www.usv.com/writing/physical-vibe-coding-our-investment-in-machenta)).\n  - Whether this was Machenta's first institutional money, and who came first between Creandum and USV, is not stated.\n- **Atlas Bio (Creandum, 2026):**\n  - Simon (surname per Creandum's linked LinkedIn: Romanski) walked Creandum through his vision of simulating human biology.\n  - Creandum led the $11M seed ([src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-atlas-bio)).\n  - The intro path is not stated.\n- **EthonAI (Index, 2024):**\n  - Index came in as the Series A lead after General Catalyst, Earlybird and Founderful, so Index was not first.\n  - The founders are ETH Zurich PhDs ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ethonai-series-a/)).\n  - Reuters names her as the Index partner who led ([src](https://sg.news.yahoo.com/index-ventures-backs-ai-manufacturing-080626804.html)).\n  - The aggregator TheOrg listed her on the EthonAI board (from the roster brief; the page now returns 404).\n- **Tacto (Index, 2023):**\n  - Index and Sequoia co-led the EUR50M round. The post says Tacto \"joins the Index family\", which marks Index's first entry.\n  - Senior/junior pairing: she co-authored the post with Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/tacto-secures-50m-to-tackle-disruptions-and-bureaucracy-in-industrial-supply-chains/)). Index's Tacto page lists only Gonzalez-Cadenas ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/tacto/)).\n- **DeepL (Index, 2024):**\n  - She was named with Danny Rimer as Index partners on the $300M round ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/fluency-is-currency-deepl/)).\n  - Rimer is the senior name and holds the company-page credit, so she likely ran the work on the ground in DACH. That is an inference, not sourced.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Creandum | Partner, Berlin | 2026 | [src](https://creandum.com/team/kathi-wilhelm) |\n| Index Ventures | Partner (DACH), departed 2026 | ~2021 (roster brief) | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ethonai-series-a/) |\n| Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas | Index co-author on Tacto | 2023-12 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/tacto-secures-50m-to-tackle-disruptions-and-bureaucracy-in-industrial-supply-chains/) |\n| Danny Rimer | Named with her on DeepL $300M round | 2024-05 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/fluency-is-currency-deepl/) |\n| Rebecca Kaden (USV) | Co-lead on Machenta seed | 2026-10 | [src](https://www.usv.com/writing/physical-vibe-coding-our-investment-in-machenta) |\n| Ed Johns, Vitalis Vosylius (Machenta) | Portfolio founders; she is Creandum lead | 2026 | [src](https://creandum.com/commitments/machenta) |\n| Atlas Bio founders | Portfolio; she is Creandum lead | 2026 | [src](https://creandum.com/commitments/atlas-bio) |\n| EthonAI founders (Julian Senoner, Bernhard Kratzwald, Torbjorn Netland) | Led Series A at Index | 2024-05 | [src](https://sg.news.yahoo.com/index-ventures-backs-ai-manufacturing-080626804.html) |\n| BCG; Harvard Business School; University of St. Gallen | Former employer / schools | before VC | [src](https://creandum.com/team/kathi-wilhelm) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did Index or Creandum pick up any Machenta seed allocation before USV? Was Machenta's $40M its first institutional round?\n- Who replaced her DACH coverage at Index? Did any Index DACH companies follow her or ask for Creandum co-investment?\n- What were her Index promotion dates (Principal to Partner)? This decides `fast_promotion`. LinkedIn would answer it but could not be fetched.\n- Was the EthonAI board seat (aggregator only) passed to someone at Index on her departure?\n- Podcast, talk or X presence: not searched, because the web search budget ran out this run.\n- Was the 2026-03-09 deutsche-startups report of her move accurate? The page returned 403. Creandum's site confirms she is now a Partner there.\n- The headshot URL (ctfassets) returned 403 to a direct check. It may need re-verification from a browser.\n\n## Sources\n- https://creandum.com/team/kathi-wilhelm\n- https://creandum.com/stories\n- https://creandum.com/stories/backing-machenta\n- https://creandum.com/stories/backing-atlas-bio\n- https://creandum.com/commitments/machenta\n- https://creandum.com/commitments/atlas-bio\n- https://machenta.com\n- https://www.usv.com/writing/physical-vibe-coding-our-investment-in-machenta\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ethonai-series-a/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/ethonai/\n- https://sg.news.yahoo.com/index-ventures-backs-ai-manufacturing-080626804.html (Reuters, 2024-05-30)\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/tacto-secures-50m-to-tackle-disruptions-and-bureaucracy-in-industrial-supply-chains/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/tacto/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/fluency-is-currency-deepl/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/deepl/\n- https://www.deutsche-startups.de/2026/03/09/levelbuild-taxdoo-pivot-join-capital-katharina-wilhelm-index-creandum/ (403 on fetch; date from roster brief)\n- https://theorg.com/org/index-ventures/org-chart/katharina-wilhelm (aggregator; 404 on fetch 2026-11-10)\n", "person:katie-haun": "\n# Katie Haun\n\n*Profile created 2026-11-10 (owner pick, queue run 3). Web search was rate-limited this turn, so it rests on primary sources fetched directly: haun.co (team page and 30+ posts), SEC EDGAR (Coinbase proxies, 8-K, Form 4/144, TLGY and Circle filings), Form ADV via IAPD, and the Flapping Airplanes JS bundle. No podcasts or interviews were read.*\n\n## One-line take\nHer fund is a fintech and crypto fund, but her personal cap table is frontier AI: Anthropic, OpenAI, Cerebras, Groq, Physical Intelligence, Cognition [src](https://haun.co/team), and in 2026 Flapping Airplanes [src](https://flappingairplanes.com/). That makes her a co-investor and allocator node for the owner, not a fund to pitch.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** regulatory credibility nobody else in venture has. She spent more than a decade as a federal prosecutor, set up DOJ's first digital-currency task force, and sat on CFIUS. She was Coinbase's first independent director, from May 2017 through the IPO [src](https://haun.co/team) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978823000065/coin-20230428.htm). That record let her raise $1.5B for a debut fund in March 2022 [src](https://haun.co/writing/introducing-haun-ventures) and a $1B Fund II in May 2026 [src](https://haun.co/writing/fundii). Sovereign wealth funds are among her LPs [src](https://haun.co/team) (Sanabil/PIF is one [src](https://sanabil-api.sanabil.com/api/portfolio)).\n- **What constrains her:** the fund is tied to digital assets. The 2026 thesis is financial infrastructure, tokenized assets and agent payments [src](https://haun.co/writing/fundii), not models or chips. She also delegates most deals. Every haun.co portfolio announcement I read (Jota, Erebor, BVNK, XFX, Meanwhile, Conduit, Commonware, River Markets and others) was written by another partner, not by her.\n- **What she'd never do (inferred):** lead a pre-revenue frontier-lab round from the fund. Her AI exposure goes through her personal account. The firm's team page states those holdings are \"made in an individual capacity and not on behalf of Haun Ventures\" [src](https://haun.co/team).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Coinbase paid her firm $1.0M a quarter for Chris Lehane's services while she sat on Coinbase's board.** The fee ran from February 2023 to April 2024 and was \"a quarterly fee of $1.0 million\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978824000080/coin-20240425.htm). It offset Haun Ventures' cost of employing Lehane [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978824000133/coin-20240724.htm). Lehane was Haun's Chief Strategy Officer from March 2022 to April 2024. He then became OpenAI's \"Vice President for Global Affairs\" and joined Coinbase's board in July 2024 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978826000045/coin-20260424.htm). So her firm was the waystation for OpenAI's head of policy.\n- **She left the Coinbase board in 2024 after seven years.** On 2024-04-18 she told the board she \"would not stand for re-election\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978824000080/coin-20240425.htm). Before that, she and her vehicle Gherardesca LLC filed eight Form 144 notices, selling about $7M of COIN between 2023-11 and 2024-03 [src](https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?action=getcompany&CIK=0001851425&type=144).\n- **Her personal portfolio is the frontier-AI list.** The firm page says she is \"a personal investor in companies like Anduril, Anthropic, Cerebras\". The list goes on to Cognition, Databricks, Groq, OpenAI, Physical Intelligence, Replit and SpaceX [src](https://haun.co/team). Rounds, dates and sizes are not given. The page calls the list \"only a subset\". Her personal vehicle is probably Gherardesca LLC: she has managed it since 2021-01, and it is the general partner of Gherardesca Management LP (2021-07) [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/318409/PDF/318409.pdf) (inference: the ADV does not say what Gherardesca holds).\n- **Flapping Airplanes names her as an individual backer.** The site says it is \"also delighted to be supported by Jeff Dean, Stanley Druckenmiller, Katie Haun\", alongside Karpathy, Kravis, Malka and Chris Ré. The same block says the company has raised \"a $180M seed round and a $1B Series A\". It does not say which round she joined [src](https://flappingairplanes.com/) (verified in the site's JS bundle, 2026-11-10).\n- **Form ADV shows how Fund II's LP base differs from Fund I's.** In Fund I's feeders, funds of funds own 28%, non-US persons own 30%, and there are 182 beneficial owners. In Fund II's feeders the figures are 20%, 42% and 51. Fund II's master funds also take money through a parallel vehicle, \"Essential Innovation Partners LP\", plus a separate Opportunities LP (~$100M GAV, 2 beneficial owners) [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/318409/PDF/318409.pdf). Total regulatory AUM is $2,489,719,681 across 10 accounts (ADV filed 2026-03-31). Fund II gross asset values at fiscal year-end were $383M (Acceleration) and $443M (Early Stage). That is GAV, not commitments; the announced size is $1B. Who stands behind Essential Innovation Partners is unconfirmed.\n- **Fund II is smaller, and she reframed the firm around \"the future of finance\".** The 2022 launch was $1.5B for \"web3\" [src](https://haun.co/writing/introducing-haun-ventures). In 2026 she announced \"$1 billion in new funds\" with three themes: financial infrastructure, new assets and markets, and the agentic economy. Her line on agents: \"a world in which computers are the customers\" [src](https://haun.co/writing/fundii).\n- **The firm became a registered investment adviser, not an exempt VC adviser, in July 2023.** It withdrew its exempt-reporting status on 2023-06-23 and SEC registration took effect on 2023-07-24 [src](https://adviserinfo.sec.gov/firm/summary/318409). One plausible reason is room to hold liquid tokens beyond the VC exemption's limits (inference). The adviser's former names, \"KRH Partners\" and \"KRH Management LP\", are her initials, and she is the sole managing member of the GP [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/318409/PDF/318409.pdf).\n- **She hires people she met across the table.** She met Diogo Mónica when Anchorage was an a16z portfolio company, and later competed with him from the Coinbase board. When she planned her own firm, \"he was one of the first people I called\". He joined as GP in March 2024 [src](https://haun.co/writing/diogo-monica).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The fund itself is low fit. It backs stablecoins, banks, prediction markets and crypto infrastructure, and none of its announced deals is a lab or a chip company. The repo's earlier `skip` on Haun Ventures (\"crypto-only, irrelevant\") misses Katie personally, though. Her own book includes Anthropic, OpenAI, Cerebras, Groq, Physical Intelligence and Cognition [src](https://haun.co/team), and she is in Flapping Airplanes, a 2026 frontier lab that raised a $180M seed and a $1B A [src](https://flappingairplanes.com/). What matters for me:\n- **Co-investor and signal:** she gets allocations in hot labs, either as a high-profile angel or through Gherardesca. Her stage of entry is unknown in every case.\n- **LP network:** her LP base is about 42% non-US in Fund II and includes sovereigns (Sanabil). She could introduce me to Gulf and sovereign capital.\n- **Policy edge for AI companies:** the Lehane-to-OpenAI path, and a newly hired ex-FBI cyber agent who works with portfolio founders [src](https://haun.co/writing/every-haun-founder-now-has-a-former-fbi-agent-in-their-corner).\n- **Fund-level AI-adjacent deals:** Erebor plans GPU equipment financing for AI datacenters [src](https://haun.co/writing/erebor), and Jota is a conversational AI financial agent [src](https://haun.co/writing/jota). Mónica, not Katie, wrote both.\n\n## Background\n- B.A. summa cum laude, International Relations, Boston University. J.D. with honors, Stanford Law School [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978823000065/coin-20230428.htm).\n- Attorney at Sidley Austin, 2001–2006. Clerked for Justice Anthony Kennedy, 2004–2005 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978823000065/coin-20230428.htm).\n- U.S. Department of Justice, 2006-09 to 2017-05. Roles: Assistant U.S. Attorney and Digital Currency Coordinator, Counselor to the Attorney General, and Counsel to the Assistant Attorney General for National Security [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978823000065/coin-20230428.htm). Her cases included the Silk Road task force; she also served on CFIUS [src](https://haun.co/team). She set up a crypto task force in 2014 [src](https://haun.co/writing/introducing-haun-ventures).\n- Coinbase independent director from 2017-05. Brian Armstrong recruited her [src](https://haun.co/writing/introducing-haun-ventures). She chaired Nominating & Governance [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978823000065/coin-20230428.htm) and stepped down at the 2024 annual meeting [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978824000080/coin-20240425.htm).\n- Andreessen Horowitz General Partner, 2018-06 to 2022-01 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978823000065/coin-20230428.htm). She co-led the crypto franchise with Chris Dixon across three funds [src](https://haun.co/team) [src](https://haun.co/writing/introducing-haun-ventures).\n- Founded Haun Ventures. The ADV dates her control to 12/2021; the proxy says January 2022 [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/318409/PDF/318409.pdf).\n- Has lectured at Stanford GSB and Stanford Law School [src](https://haun.co/team).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://haun.co/team | Firm bio, verified 2026-11-10. katiehaun.com resolves but serves an empty page (114 bytes), so it is not a channel |\n| Substack / blog | https://haun.co/writing | Her posts: Fund I (2022-03), FTX post with Fred Wilson (2022-11), Diogo hire (2024-03), GENIUS Act (2025-06), Fund II (2026-05), FBI hire (2026-10) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/katie_haun | Linked from the firm team page. Follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | nothing verified | linkedin.com/in/katiehaun returned 999 (blocked) and is unverified. Firm page: https://www.linkedin.com/company/haun-ventures/ |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNot researched this pass: web search was rate-limited and I found no transcript on her own sites. The firm page says she has testified before both houses of Congress [src](https://haun.co/team). Transcripts of that testimony would be the best next primary source.\n\n## How they think\n- **Regulation is the moat.** She calls her investing \"the intersection of technology and regulation\" and says it takes \"requisite fearlessness\" [src](https://haun.co/writing/fundii). She called the Chevron reversal \"the most significant court case for technology policy\" in years [src](https://haun.co/writing/a-watershed-moment-for-u-s-financial-innovation).\n- **Value-add means legal and security cover:** she hired an ex-FBI cyber agent for portfolio founders in 2026-10 [src](https://haun.co/writing/every-haun-founder-now-has-a-former-fbi-agent-in-their-corner). In 2022 the firm's pitch was \"system change\" and policy campaigns [src](https://haun.co/writing/introducing-haun-ventures).\n- **LP selection by mission:** \"Our focus on system change was a key consideration in selecting our LPs\" [src](https://haun.co/writing/introducing-haun-ventures).\n- **Thesis drift, 2022 to 2026:** from web3 consumer, creators and gaming [src](https://haun.co/writing/introducing-haun-ventures) to stablecoins, tokenization, prediction markets and agent payments [src](https://haun.co/writing/fundii).\n- **Calls:** after FTX she and Fred Wilson argued that regulated, US-based companies (Coinbase, Kraken, Anchorage) would survive while offshore centralized lenders failed [src](https://haun.co/writing/taking-a-long-term-view-of-web3). She later hired Anchorage's co-founder.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Announcing Fund II (2026-05-04): the current thesis, including the agentic economy [src](https://haun.co/writing/fundii).\n2. Introducing Haun Ventures (2022-03-22): her path, the fund structure and how she chose LPs [src](https://haun.co/writing/introducing-haun-ventures).\n3. Diogo Mónica (2024-03-20): how she recruits partners [src](https://haun.co/writing/diogo-monica).\n4. Coinbase DEF 14A 2024: the Haun Ventures fee arrangement and her exit from the board [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978824000080/coin-20240425.htm).\n5. Haun Ventures Form ADV (2026-03-31): funds, LP mix and ownership [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/318409/PDF/318409.pdf).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot researched (X not read). From the repo: an aggregator lists her among Sabrina Hahn's X followers, in a crypto-heavy cluster [src](https://twitterscore.io/twitter/sabrinahahn/) (aggregator).\n\n## Portfolio\n**Personally named (individual capacity):**\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Flapping Airplanes | Undisclosed ($180M seed Jan 2026 or $1B Series A Oct 2026; site doesn't say) | 2026 | No (individual supporter) | Jeff Dean, Druckenmiller, Karpathy, Kravis, Malka, Chris Ré; Index, GV, Sequoia, Lux | [src](https://flappingairplanes.com/) |\n| Anthropic, OpenAI, Cerebras (IPO), Groq (acq. by NVIDIA), Physical Intelligence, Cognition, Replit, Databricks | Undisclosed | not given | No (personal) | n/a | [src](https://haun.co/team) |\n| Anduril, Coinbase (IPO), Deel, Figma (IPO), Revolut, SpaceX (IPO), Edison (unidentified), Neko (unidentified) | Undisclosed | not given | No (personal) | n/a | [src](https://haun.co/team) |\n| Coinbase | Director stake: 140,570 Class A shares incl. Gherardesca LLC, per 2024 proxy | 2017-05 (board) | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978824000080/coin-20240425.htm) |\n\n**Firm-level (Haun Ventures; Katie not named as the deal partner):**\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Haun role | Named Haun partner | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Jota | Series A (US$30M, US$185M post) | 2026-06-29 / post 2026-07-02 | Led; first Brazil deal | Diogo Mónica | [src](https://haun.co/writing/jota) |\n| Erebor | Series B (+ \"very first round\") | 2026-02-08 | Backed since first round; board | Diogo Mónica | [src](https://haun.co/writing/erebor) |\n| River Markets | Seed | 2026-08-11 | Led | Mark Beylin | [src](https://haun.co/writing/the-execution-layer-for-prediction-markets) |\n| Circle ARC token | Strategic (token presale, $222M at $3B FDV) | 2026-05-11 | Participant | n/a | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1876042/000187604226000148/final_05x11q1epr30.htm) |\n| Lighter | Round ($68M at ~$1.5B) | 2025-11-11 | Participant (FF and Ribbit co-led) | n/a | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/11/11/lighter-fundraise-founders-fund-ribbit-capital-haun-ventures-robinhood-vladimir-novakovski/) |\n| Meanwhile | $82M round | 2025-10-07 | Co-lead with Bain Capital Crypto | Chris Ahn | [src](https://haun.co/writing/meanwhile) |\n| StableCoinX / TLGY (Ethena treasury) | PIPE (initial and additional) | 2025-07 to 2025-09 | Participant | n/a | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1879814/000121390025085171/ea025545901ex99-1_tlgyacq.htm) |\n| The Clearing Company | Seed $15M | 2025-08-27 | Participant (USV led) | n/a | [src](https://www.theblock.co/news/deals/2025-08-27-former-polymarket-exec-raises-15-million-from-coinbase-and-usv-for-rival-prediction-platform-368519) |\n| XFX | Seed | 2025-06-17 | Led | Chris Ahn | [src](https://haun.co/writing/xfx) |\n| BVNK | Series B | 2024-12-17 | Led | Diogo Mónica | [src](https://haun.co/writing/bvnk) |\n| Commonware | Seed | 2024-12-11 | Participant with Dragonfly | Breck Stodghill | [src](https://haun.co/writing/commonware) |\n| Conduit | Series A | 2024-06-25 | Co-lead with Paradigm | Chris Ahn | [src](https://haun.co/writing/conduit) |\n\n**At a16z (2018–2022):** I could not source any company where she was the named a16z partner.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Flapping Airplanes:** the company lists her as an individual supporter next to Karpathy, Jeff Dean and Druckenmiller [src](https://flappingairplanes.com/). The intro path, the round and the check size were not found.\n- **Erebor (firm):** founded by Palmer Luckey and others. Haun says it has backed Erebor \"since their very first round\"; Mónica joined the board at the Series B. The bank plans GPU equipment financing for AI datacenters [src](https://haun.co/writing/erebor). This is the firm's clearest AI-compute angle.\n- **Lehane arrangement:** he joined in 2022-03 as CSO. Coinbase reimbursed his cost from 2023-02, ending 2024-04, when he left for OpenAI [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978826000045/coin-20260424.htm) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978824000133/coin-20240724.htm).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Haun Ventures | Founder & CEO; managing member of GP | 2021-12 | [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/318409/PDF/318409.pdf) |\n| Andreessen Horowitz | General Partner (former), co-led crypto | 2018-06 to 2022-01 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978823000065/coin-20230428.htm) |\n| U.S. Department of Justice | Prosecutor (former) | 2006-09 to 2017-05 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978823000065/coin-20230428.htm) |\n| Sidley Austin | Attorney (former) | 2001 to 2006 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978823000065/coin-20230428.htm) |\n| Coinbase | Independent director | 2017-05 to 2024 AGM | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978824000080/coin-20240425.htm) |\n| Brian Armstrong | Recruited her to the Coinbase board | 2017 | [src](https://haun.co/writing/introducing-haun-ventures) |\n| Chris Dixon | Former partner; co-launched a16z crypto | 2018 | [src](https://haun.co/writing/introducing-haun-ventures) |\n| Fred Wilson | Coinbase co-director; co-authored post-FTX essay | 2022-11 | [src](https://haun.co/writing/taking-a-long-term-view-of-web3) |\n| Chris Lehane | Hired as Haun CSO; now OpenAI VP Global Affairs | 2022-03 to 2024-04 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978826000045/coin-20260424.htm) |\n| Diogo Mónica | Recruited as GP | 2024-03 | [src](https://haun.co/writing/diogo-monica) |\n| Donovan McKendrick | Hired ex-FBI agent for portfolio support | 2026-10 | [src](https://haun.co/writing/every-haun-founder-now-has-a-former-fbi-agent-in-their-corner) |\n| Gherardesca LLC | Manager (personal vehicle) | 2021-01 | [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/318409/PDF/318409.pdf) |\n| Sanabil Investments (PIF) | LP in Haun Ventures (fund not named) | 2023 | [src](https://sanabil-api.sanabil.com/api/portfolio) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Entry rounds and dates for her Anthropic, OpenAI, Cerebras, Groq, PI and Cognition stakes. Direct, SPV or through Gherardesca?\n- Which Flapping Airplanes round she joined, and who introduced her (Malka? Druckenmiller?).\n- Who the LP behind \"Essential Innovation Partners\" is (2 beneficial owners, parallel feeder into Fund II).\n- Which companies \"Edison\" and \"Neko\" are on her holdings list (Neko Health? Edison Scientific?).\n- Which deals she personally led at a16z (2018–2022) and at Haun Ventures. Her congressional testimony and podcast appearances.\n\n## Sources\n- https://haun.co/team (bio, personal holdings, team, photo; fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://haun.co/writing/introducing-haun-ventures, https://haun.co/writing/fundii, https://haun.co/writing/diogo-monica, https://haun.co/writing/a-watershed-moment-for-u-s-financial-innovation, https://haun.co/writing/taking-a-long-term-view-of-web3, https://haun.co/writing/every-haun-founder-now-has-a-former-fbi-agent-in-their-corner\n- https://haun.co/writing/jota, /erebor, /bvnk, /xfx, /meanwhile, /conduit, /commonware, /the-execution-layer-for-prediction-markets\n- Coinbase DEF 14A 2023 https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978823000065/coin-20230428.htm ; 2024 https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978824000080/coin-20240425.htm ; 2026 https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978826000045/coin-20260424.htm ; 8-K 2024-07-25 https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1679788/000167978824000133/coin-20240724.htm\n- Kathryn Haun Form 4/144 filings, CIK 1851425 https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?action=getcompany&CIK=0001851425\n- Haun Ventures Management LP Form ADV (CRD 318409) https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/318409/PDF/318409.pdf ; https://adviserinfo.sec.gov/firm/summary/318409\n- Circle Q1'26 8-K https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1876042/000187604226000148/final_05x11q1epr30.htm ; TLGY 8-K https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1879814/000121390025085171/ea025545901ex99-1_tlgyacq.htm\n- https://flappingairplanes.com/ (JS bundle /assets/index-DtZtnXfl.js)\n- Repo: people/joey-krug.md, people/tahseen-rashid.md, people/smac.md, companies/flapping-airplanes.md, data/lp_commitments.csv (Sanabil)\n", "person:kay-min": "\n# Kay Min\n\n*Profile written 2026-11-10. Pronouns: she/her, as used in her Cisco bios ([2014](https://web.archive.org/web/20140503044721/http://www.ciscoinvestments.com:80/team), [2017](https://web.archive.org/web/20170211045126/http://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/)).*\n\n## One-line take\nShe is the one Cisco person who covers silicon and optics both as an investor and as an acquirer. Her deals have turned into Cisco acquisitions (Luxtera), so for an AI-chip or photonics founder she is the route to Cisco money and to a possible Cisco buyout.\n\n## Crux\n- **What makes her win:** 15 years as the only owner of one domain inside Cisco Corporate Development, which runs both venture investing and M&A. Her bio says she is \"responsible for all of Cisco's acquisitions and investments in Hardware\" ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/kay-min)), and Cisco's Silicon & Optics page lists no one else ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/expertise/silicon-and-optics)). She also comes from optical networking herself: product manager at Pirelli and Lucent, and on the founding team of Photuris.\n- **What constrains her:** it is Cisco's balance sheet, and the money is approved by an Investment Review Board where Finance is in the room ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/kay-min)). In AI rounds Cisco joins as a strategic participant and has not been the lead on any of her AI deals (see the [firm page](../firms/cisco-investments.md)). Her title is a rung below VP Aleem Rizvon, who is listed with her on every AI-silicon deal.\n- **What she wouldn't do:** back technology that lacks a market. In her words, she wants startups that \"don't exist solely as a solution looking for a problem\" ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/kay-min)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Invest, then acquire.** On Cisco's Luxtera page she is the only Cisco team member listed, and the page notes \"Cisco acquired Luxtera in 2019.\" ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/luxtera)). Lux puts the price at $660M ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/peter-hebert)). Her bio describes her best moments as \"signing merger agreements\" ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/kay-min)).\n- **Who approves, and the upside.** She describes Finance asking \"Can we write a bigger check?\" during an Investment Review Board approval ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/kay-min)). This is the first named approval body for Cisco Investments in this repo. The firm page had marked \"who decides\" as unconfirmed.\n- **She backed the same founder twice.** Ramin Farjadrad is listed in Aquantia's leadership, an older deal where Min is the only Cisco name ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/aquantia)). He is now CEO of Eliyan, and Min is on Cisco's Eliyan team for the 2026 round ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/eliyan)). Whether the Eliyan deal came through that earlier relationship is **unconfirmed**.\n- **Most of her book is hidden.** The Silicon & Optics page says Cisco has \"a number of active portfolio companies (that are confidential)\" ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/expertise/silicon-and-optics)). Public rosters therefore show fewer of her deals than she has done.\n- **She also covers fund (LP) positions.** She is the only Cisco name on Cisco's pages for Walden Catalyst (Lip-Bu Tan and Young Sohn), Walden Riverwood Ventures II, OMERS Ventures Fund II, Plexo Capital and Bull City Venture Partners ([Walden Catalyst](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/walden-catalyst), [OMERS](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/omers-ventures-fund-ii)). The OMERS link fits her earlier \"geographic responsibility of Canada\" ([2014 bio](https://web.archive.org/web/20140503044721/http://www.ciscoinvestments.com:80/team)).\n- **Business units sponsor her deals.** Her Qunnect post quotes two Cisco SVPs, Vijoy Pandey of Outshift and Bill Gartner of Optical. Pandey: \"Their room-temperature approach aligns with our quantum data center vision.\" ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/from-theory-to-reality-future-quantum-networking-qunnect)). A founder should expect to need a business-unit champion.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** She is not a co-lead for a $20M seed: Cisco joins later AI-silicon rounds as a strategic participant (Groq Series D, Positron Series C, Eliyan's $145M round, the CoreWeave secondary). She also misses the \"recently promoted\" profile, with four title steps in 15 years. She still matters for three reasons:\n1. For any AI-chip, interconnect or photonics seed the owner backs, she is the gatekeeper to Cisco as a follow-on investor, design partner and possible acquirer (Luxtera shows the path).\n2. Her LP seats at Walden Catalyst and OMERS Ventures connect her to deep-tech GPs who co-invest at seed.\n3. Chuki Ofili, the junior on all her AI rosters, may be the faster-rising person to watch (**unconfirmed**; no promotion data).\n\nAI deals that matter: Groq, Positron, Eliyan, Furiosa AI, Xscape Photonics, CoreWeave.\n\n## Background\n- BS in ceramic (materials science) engineering, Rutgers University. MBA, Georgetown University ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/kay-min)).\n- Product development and product management in optical networking at Pirelli and Lucent (Bell Labs). Founding team member of Photuris and also worked at Mahi; then investment banking and venture capital ([2014 bio](https://web.archive.org/web/20140503044721/http://www.ciscoinvestments.com:80/team)). Dates for each role are **unconfirmed**.\n- 2011: joined Cisco Corporate Development ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20140503044721/http://www.ciscoinvestments.com:80/team)).\n- 2014: Manager, segment leader for next-generation routing and switching, covering routing, optical and photonics, plus Canada ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20140503044721/http://www.ciscoinvestments.com:80/team)).\n- 2017 and 2019: Director, Domain Leader for Silicon and Geo Leader for Canada ([2017](https://web.archive.org/web/20170211045126/http://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/), [2019](https://web.archive.org/web/20190719015620/https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/)).\n- 2021: Sr. Director ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20210729115249/https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team)). 2026: Senior Director, Silicon and Optics ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/kay-min)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/kay-min | Cisco bio, checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/from-theory-to-reality-future-quantum-networking-qunnect | Writes Cisco Investments deal posts (Aliro 2023, Qunnect 2025) |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found (not checked; search budget exhausted) | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\nWays to reach her: Cisco only offers a contact form ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/contact-us)). The practical routes are an introduction from the founder of one of her portfolio companies (Groq, Positron, Eliyan, Xscape, Furiosa, Qunnect) or from a GP where Cisco is an LP (Walden Catalyst, OMERS Ventures), or a Cisco business-unit sponsor such as Optical (Bill Gartner).\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-10 — Cisco Investments blog — \"Cisco Investments Takes a Quantum Leap\": her interview with Aliro CTO Prineha Narang, published as a post with her byline ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/cisco-investments-takes-a-quantum-leap)).\n- 2019-08 — Cisco Investments — \"Silicon: Why We Doubled Down When Other Investors Looked Elsewhere\": she explains Cisco's silicon focus and cites OmniTier ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/silicon-double-down)). The post's text is a summary only; the full talk was not found.\n- No other podcasts or conference talks found (web search budget exhausted this run).\n\n## How they think\n- Silicon was a contrarian bet: Cisco \"doubled down when other investors looked elsewhere\" (2019) ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/silicon-double-down)).\n- She values practical deployment. Aliro and Qunnect were both pitched as quantum networking that runs over existing fiber without a forklift upgrade ([Aliro](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/cisco-investments-takes-a-quantum-leap), [Qunnect](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/from-theory-to-reality-future-quantum-networking-qunnect)).\n- She invests for an operating company: she says she is happiest when she can \"invest in and acquire companies for an operating company\" ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/kay-min)).\n\n## Read these first\n- Her Cisco bio, for the IRB and merger-signing quotes ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/kay-min)).\n- Her Qunnect post (2025), for how a deal is framed around business-unit sponsors ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/from-theory-to-reality-future-quantum-networking-qunnect)).\n- Her Aliro post (2023), which shows her quantum thesis ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/cisco-investments-takes-a-quantum-leap)).\n- The Silicon & Optics domain page ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/expertise/silicon-and-optics)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public X or podcast presence surfaced.\n\n## Portfolio\nEvery row comes from her name on Cisco's own portfolio pages (fetched 2026-11-10). Those pages do not say whether she led, sat on the board, or was there at close.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Groq | Series D ($640M at $2.8B); Cisco also in Series E 2025 | 2024-08 | no | BlackRock (lead) | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/groq) |\n| CoreWeave | Secondary ($650M share sale) | 2024-11 | no | Pure Storage, Coatue, Fidelity | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/coreweave) |\n| Qunnect | Series A extension ($10M) | 2025-06 | no | Airbus Ventures (lead), Quantonation | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/from-theory-to-reality-future-quantum-networking-qunnect) |\n| Eliyan | Undisclosed ($145M at $1B) | 2026-07 | no | | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/eliyan) |\n| Positron | Series C ($875M incl. C-1; $5B post) | 2026-09 | no | | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/positron) |\n| Xscape Photonics | Undisclosed (before 2024-10, inferred) | unconfirmed | no | | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/xscape-photonics) |\n| Furiosa AI | Undisclosed | unconfirmed | no | | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/furiosa-ai) |\n| Atom Computing | Undisclosed | unconfirmed | no | | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/atom-computing) |\n| Aliro Quantum | Undisclosed | 2023-10 (post date) | unknown | | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/cisco-investments-takes-a-quantum-leap) |\n| OmniTier | Undisclosed | 2019-08 (post date) | unknown | | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/silicon-double-down) |\n| Luxtera | Undisclosed; Cisco acquired it in 2019 | unconfirmed | unknown | | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/luxtera) |\n| Aquantia | Undisclosed | unconfirmed | unknown | | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/aquantia) |\n| eSilicon | Undisclosed; acquired by Inphi and Synopsys in 2020 | unconfirmed | unknown | | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/esilicon) |\n| Ambiq Micro, Nantero, AnDAPT, Aviz Networks, Opanga | Undisclosed | unconfirmed | unknown | | [Ambiq](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/ambiq-micro), [Nantero](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/nantero), [AnDAPT](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/andapt), [Aviz](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/aviz-networks), [Opanga](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/opanga) |\n\nTiming: none of the AI rounds above was Cisco's first-in seed. Cisco came in at Series D for Groq and re-upped in Series E ([firm rows](../firms/cisco-investments.md)). Her earliest documented AI-adjacent stage is the Qunnect Series A extension.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Qunnect (2025):** She wrote the announcement post. The quotes in it come from Cisco business units, Outshift (Pandey) and Optical (Gartner), which suggests those units sponsored the deal internally ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/from-theory-to-reality-future-quantum-networking-qunnect)). CRN picked up her statement ([src](https://www.crn.com/news/networking/2025/cisco-reveals-history-making-investment-in-quantum-networking-upstart-qunnect)).\n- **Luxtera (to 2019):** She was the only Cisco investment contact before Cisco acquired the company ([src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/luxtera)). Her role in the acquisition itself is **unconfirmed**.\n- How the AI-chip deals were sourced (intro paths) is **unconfirmed**.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Cisco Investments | Senior Director (joined 2011) | 2011 | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/kay-min) |\n| Aleem Rizvon | VP; listed with her on all AI-silicon rosters | 2024 | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/positron) |\n| Chuki Ofili, Farouk Elnaas | Juniors on her AI-silicon rosters | 2024 | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/eliyan) |\n| Ramin Farjadrad | Founder she backed twice (Aquantia, Eliyan) | unconfirmed | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/aquantia) |\n| Walden Catalyst (Lip-Bu Tan, Young Sohn) | Cisco is an LP; she is the Cisco contact | unconfirmed | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/walden-catalyst) |\n| Bill Gartner, Vijoy Pandey | Cisco business-unit sponsors on Qunnect | 2025 | [src](https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/from-theory-to-reality-future-quantum-networking-qunnect) |\n| Photuris, Mahi, Lucent, Pirelli | Prior employers | unconfirmed | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20140503044721/http://www.ciscoinvestments.com:80/team) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did the Eliyan investment come through Farjadrad's Aquantia relationship?\n- Board or observer seats at Groq, Positron, Eliyan or Xscape (none found on Cisco pages).\n- The Investment Review Board: who sits on it, and is there a size threshold above which Derek Idemoto or the CFO has to sign?\n- Rounds and dates for Furiosa, Xscape, Atom Computing, Luxtera and Aquantia.\n- Did she run the Luxtera acquisition as well as the investment?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/kay-min\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20140503044721/http://www.ciscoinvestments.com:80/team\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20170211045126/http://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20190719015620/https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20210729115249/https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/team\n- https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/expertise/silicon-and-optics\n- https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/from-theory-to-reality-future-quantum-networking-qunnect\n- https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/cisco-investments-takes-a-quantum-leap\n- https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/posts/silicon-double-down\n- https://www.crn.com/news/networking/2025/cisco-reveals-history-making-investment-in-quantum-networking-upstart-qunnect\n- Cisco portfolio pages: groq, coreweave, qunnect, positron, eliyan, xscape-photonics, furiosa-ai, atom-computing, aliro, luxtera, aquantia, esilicon, ambiq-micro, nantero, andapt, aviz-networks, opanga, walden-catalyst, walden-riverwood-ventures-ii-wrv, omers-ventures-fund-ii, plexo-capital, bull-city-venture-partners (all at https://www.ciscoinvestments.com/portfolio/<name>, fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/people/peter-hebert\n", "person:ken-asada": "\n# Ken Asada\n\n## One-line take\nThe person who decides which Japanese and Korean AI companies get Salesforce balance-sheet money: a door into Japan enterprise distribution and a late-round follower in labs, not a seed co-lead.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he has worked at three corporate VCs (Intel Capital, NTT DoCoMo Ventures, Salesforce Ventures) and is the only Salesforce partner in Tokyo. Every 2025-26 SFV Japan/Korea AI deal post carries his byline with Principal Sho Yamanaka ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/ken-asada/)).\n- **What constrains him:** SFV has one pool, Salesforce's balance sheet, with John Somorjai over it. The deals have to fit Salesforce, and MarqVision was screened on \"alignment with the Salesforce ecosystem\" ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-marqvision/)).\n- **Where he leads:** only Japanese industrial and vertical AI: ALGO ARTIS B led ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ai-powered-operational-planning-for-heavy-industry/)) and Resilire co-led with DNX ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/supply-chain-intelligence-for-manufacturing/)).\n- **Where he follows:** at the frontier layer. He came into Sakana after the Series B had closed and joined Kotoba's third seed behind Kindred.\n- **What he would not do (inferred, not stated):** lead or price a lab's first round.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Sakana was a late add-on, not a priced entry.**\n  - TechCrunch's 2025-11-17 list of Series B investors ($135M at $2.65B post) has no Salesforce Ventures ([TC](https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/17/sakana-ai-raises-135m-series-b-at-a-2-65b-valuation-to-continue-building-ai-models-for-japan/)).\n  - Asada's post announcing SFV's participation is dated 2026-02-17 and names the strategic hook: \"as they scale alongside Agentforce\" ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-work-for-japanese-enterprises/)).\n  - No commercial contract, compute deal or exclusivity is disclosed.\n- **He followed even at seed.**\n  - Kotoba's $10M round was its third seed, led by Kindred, with $23M raised in total ([GamesBeat](https://gamesbeat.com/kotoba-technologies-raises-10m-for-real-time-voice-ai-platform-in-east-asia/)).\n  - Kotoba had already raised an $11.83M Seed 2 in 2025 ([BusinessWire](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250805128670/en/Kotoba-Technologies-Raises-USD-11.83-Million-in-Seed-2-Funding-to-Accelerate-Commercialization-of-AI-powered-Simultaneous-Interpretation)).\n  - His screen: \"combines exceptional research capabilities with strong business execution\" (same GamesBeat piece).\n- **His ex-employer's group sits beside him.**\n  - NTT Group was a strategic investor in Sakana's 2024 seed ([Sakana](https://sakana.ai/seed-round/)) and a co-investor in Mujin's Series D, where Asada is named ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-mujin/)).\n  - Whether his DoCoMo ties sourced either deal is **unconfirmed**.\n- **His book is manufacturing, not CRM.**\n  - ALGO ARTIS, Resilire, CADDi and Mujin are all Japanese heavy industry.\n  - SFV's CADDi post calls manufacturing \"one of the largest sectors still without a category-defining AI platform\" ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-ai-data-platform-for-manufacturing/)).\n- **Not Shinji Asada.**\n  - Shinji Asada joined SFV in 2015 from Itochu Technology Ventures and ran the $100M Japan Trailblazer Fund announced 2018-12 ([GCV 2020](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gcv-emerging-leaders-awards-2020-shinji-asada/?format=md), [Salesforce IR](https://investor.salesforce.com/news/news-details/2018/Salesforce-Ventures-Introduces-100-Million-Japan-Trailblazer-Fund/default.aspx)).\n  - Ken succeeded him as Japan head; the date is unconfirmed.\n  - Whether Ken's deals draw on a \"Trailblazer\" allocation is **unconfirmed**. SFV labels are internal allocations of the parent balance sheet (see [firms/salesforce-ventures.md](../firms/salesforce-ventures.md)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **Lab exposure is thin:** one frontier lab (Sakana, entered late at a $2.65B+ price) and one researcher-founder speech lab (Kotoba, followed at the third seed). The rest is Japan/Korea enterprise and industrial AI.\n- **For a ~$20M check into a frontier-AI seed:**\n  - He is not a co-lead at that size.\n  - He is a likely later-round price-taker, and a source of Japan enterprise distribution through Salesforce Japan and Agentforce.\n  - He is the person to call if a portfolio lab wants Japanese enterprise customers or a Japan/Korea strategic in a B round.\n- **Acquirer:** not him directly; Salesforce M&A runs under Somorjai.\n- **LP:** no.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** BA Economics, Keio University; MBA (finance), London Business School ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/ken-asada/)).\n- **Career** (all dates unconfirmed; the SFV bio gives none and LinkedIn was blocked):\n  - IBM Japan, systems engineer and strategy consultant.\n  - Intel Capital, Investment Director.\n  - NTT DoCoMo Ventures, Managing Director.\n  - Salesforce Ventures, Partner & Head of Japan ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/ken-asada/)).\n- **Experience:** more than a decade in VC, across AI, communications, crowdsourcing, enterprise software and security (same source).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://salesforceventures.com/people/ken-asada/ | Verified 2026-11-10; team page, Tokyo office |\n| Substack / blog | https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ | Co-bylines every Japan/Korea deal post (about 10 in 2025-26) |\n| X | nothing found | SFV page links only @salesforcevc |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/kenasada/ | Linked from the SFV page; content not readable (HTTP 999) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\nThe page's mailto link is malformed, so no email is listed here. **How to reach him:**\n- Through Sho Yamanaka (Principal) or Associate Niklas Tawast, who cover Japan/Korea/Taiwan.\n- Through Kindred Ventures (Kotoba), DNX Ventures (Resilire) or the Sakana Series B syndicate (Khosla, Lux, NEA, MUFG).\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was unavailable this session, so this is not a complete check.\n\n## How they think\n- Researcher-founders who can also sell: \"Kotoba's founders are frontier AI researchers who chose to build\" ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/toward-de-facto-of-speech-ai-in-east-asia/)).\n- Human-in-the-loop optimisation for heavy industry: \"The operator remains in the driver's seat\" (ALGO ARTIS, [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ai-powered-operational-planning-for-heavy-industry/)).\n- Network-effect data moats in supply chains (Resilire), and the view that Japanese enterprise AI is untapped: \"The market for enterprise AI in Japan is largely untapped\" (GenerativeX, [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/accelerating-ai-adoption-in-japan/)).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Sakana post (2026-02)](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-work-for-japanese-enterprises/): the Agentforce tie.\n- [Kotoba post (2026-06)](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/toward-de-facto-of-speech-ai-in-east-asia/): his frontier-researcher screen.\n- [ALGO ARTIS post (2026-06)](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ai-powered-operational-planning-for-heavy-industry/): what he leads.\n- [CADDi post (2026-09)](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-ai-data-platform-for-manufacturing/): the manufacturing thesis.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public X account; his public voice is the co-bylined SFV posts.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Datumo (KR, AI evaluation) | Series B ($15.5M) | 2025-08-11 | no | KB Investment, Shinhan, Kiwoom, SBI, Samsung Securities | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-datumo/) |\n| MarqVision (KR, AI brand protection) | Series B ($48M) | 2025-09-15 | not stated | Peak XV, HSG, Altos, Atinum, Coral, YC | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-marqvision/) |\n| Mujin (JP, robotics) | Series D | 2025-12-16 | no | NTT Group, QIA, Mitsubishi HC Capital Realty | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-mujin/) |\n| Sakana AI (JP, frontier lab) | Series B (joined after the 2025-11 close) | 2026-02-17 | no | Factorial, MUFG, Khosla, Google | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-work-for-japanese-enterprises/) |\n| GenerativeX (JP) | Undisclosed | 2026-06-01 | not stated | not stated | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/accelerating-ai-adoption-in-japan/) |\n| ALGO ARTIS (JP) | Series B | 2026-06-09 | **yes** | UTEC, DeNA, K4 Ventures | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ai-powered-operational-planning-for-heavy-industry/) |\n| Kotoba (JP, speech AI) | Seed ($10M, third seed) | 2026-06-23 | no (Kindred led) | Sony Innovation Fund | [GamesBeat](https://gamesbeat.com/kotoba-technologies-raises-10m-for-real-time-voice-ai-platform-in-east-asia/) |\n| Resilire (JP) | Undisclosed | 2026-08-19 | **co-led** | DNX Ventures | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/supply-chain-intelligence-for-manufacturing/) |\n| CADDi (JP) | Series D | 2026-09-15 | not stated | not stated | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-ai-data-platform-for-manufacturing/) |\n| Flyle (JP) | Undisclosed | 2026-10-06 | not stated | not stated | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ai-native-customer-feedback-analysis/) |\n\nHis SFV page also lists Autoro, Baseconnect, DIGGLE, Hubble, Knowledge Work, Leadplus, LEALA, Megazone Cloud, Moneytree, oVice, Prime Number, Willbox and xbit, all undated. Some may predate his tenure (unconfirmed) ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/ken-asada/)).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Sakana:** see Differential insight. SFV entered after the priced close. The post's only stated tie is scaling \"alongside Agentforce\", in Somorjai's words.\n- **Kotoba:** Kindred led the third seed. Asada and Yamanaka joined alongside Sony Innovation Fund.\n- No intro paths were found for any deal.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Salesforce Ventures | Partner & Head of Japan | unconfirmed | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/ken-asada/) |\n| NTT DoCoMo Ventures | former MD | unconfirmed | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/ken-asada/) |\n| Intel Capital | former Investment Director | unconfirmed | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/ken-asada/) |\n| IBM Japan | former SE / strategy consultant | unconfirmed | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/ken-asada/) |\n| Sho Yamanaka | Principal, co-author on all Japan/Korea deals | 2025 | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-datumo/) |\n| Shinji Asada | predecessor as SFV Japan head (different person) | unconfirmed | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gcv-emerging-leaders-awards-2020-shinji-asada/?format=md) |\n| John Somorjai | SFV President; approver over all pools | — | [GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/powerlist-2024-john-somorjai/?format=md) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did he join SFV, and when did he take over from Shinji Asada?\n- What are the check sizes on Sakana and Kotoba?\n- Does Sakana have a commercial Agentforce or Salesforce Japan contract?\n- Did his NTT ties source Sakana or Mujin?\n- Does he sit on any boards (ALGO ARTIS as lead investor)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://salesforceventures.com/people/ken-asada/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-work-for-japanese-enterprises/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/17/sakana-ai-raises-135m-series-b-at-a-2-65b-valuation-to-continue-building-ai-models-for-japan/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/toward-de-facto-of-speech-ai-in-east-asia/\n- https://gamesbeat.com/kotoba-technologies-raises-10m-for-real-time-voice-ai-platform-in-east-asia/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ai-powered-operational-planning-for-heavy-industry/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/supply-chain-intelligence-for-manufacturing/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-ai-data-platform-for-manufacturing/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-datumo/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-marqvision/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-mujin/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/accelerating-ai-adoption-in-japan/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ai-native-customer-feedback-analysis/\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/gcv-emerging-leaders-awards-2020-shinji-asada/?format=md\n- https://sakana.ai/seed-round/\n", "person:kentaro-matsui": "\n# Kentaro Matsui\n\n## One-line take\nSoftBank's former M&A banker turned in-house deal man: the only investor with a title at both the Vision Fund manager and the parent, and the person who put Wayve in front of Masayoshi Son ([Vision Fund feature, 2026-02-06](https://visionfund.com/insights/wayve-alex-kendall-road-to-self-driving-cars)).\n\n## Crux\nMatsui wins on proximity to Son, not on sourcing reach. He advised SoftBank on Vodafone Japan, Sprint and Arm as a Mizuho banker, was hired into the parent's Finance Unit in 2016, and has moved between the parent and the fund three times since ([DiDi F-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1764757/000104746921001194/a2243272zf-1.htm); [SBG 2016-11-01](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20161101); [SBG 2022-11-21](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20221121)). His value to a founder is that he can carry a company to Son himself: on Wayve he says \"When I showed him Wayve's end-to-end approach, it immediately clicked with him\" ([Vision Fund feature](https://visionfund.com/insights/wayve-alex-kendall-road-to-self-driving-cars)). The constraint is that he does not decide. He is not on the parent's five-person Investment Committee and is not one of the parent's six corporate officers ([SBG 2026-06-24](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260624); committee membership per [firms/softbank-group.md](../firms/softbank-group.md)). He would never do a seed: every round he is named on is a Series C or later, and the smallest is about $100M.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He pitched Wayve to Son personally.** \"When I showed him Wayve's end-to-end approach, it immediately clicked with him\" ([Vision Fund feature, 2026-02-06](https://visionfund.com/insights/wayve-alex-kendall-road-to-self-driving-cars)). The Series C release names the lead as \"SoftBank Group\", not a Vision Fund ([Wayve, 2024-05-07](https://wayve.ai/press/series-c/)).\n- **He is not on the fund's own Wayve team list.** The Vision Fund portfolio page for Wayve lists Max Ohrstrand, Grong Wang and Jake Qu, and not Matsui, although he holds the board seat and is quoted on both rounds ([visionfund.com/portfolio/wayve](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/wayve), fetched 2026-11-10). He is listed on the team for QuEra, Keenon, Gaussian, DiDi Autonomous, AI Medical Service, LegalOn, Classiq, Terabase and Brain Corp. Reading: Wayve came in through his parent seat and Son, and the fund team now runs it. That reading is mine, not stated by SoftBank.\n- **He kept signing Vision Fund 2 deals while he was not a managing partner.** In the Keenon and Gaussian releases (2021-09, 2021-11) he is \"Managing Director at SoftBank Group, former Managing Partner at SoftBank Investment Advisers\" ([Keenon](https://kyodonewsprwire.jp/release/202109150116); [Gaussian](https://gausium.com/news/gaussian-robotics-secures-188-million-in-series-c-funding/)). By 2022-06 he is \"Managing Partner at SoftBank Investment Advisers\" again ([LegalForce](https://en.legalontech.jp/2966/)). The title follows the reorganisation of the day; the access does not change.\n- **The 2020 move was a demotion on paper only.** Bloomberg reported he would \"transition to a senior advisory role at SoftBank Group\" while continuing \"select Vision Fund activities\" ([Bloomberg via Yahoo, 2020-05-29](https://uk.finance.yahoo.com/news/softbank-hands-roles-two-vision-020000527.html)). A year later the DiDi prospectus gives his parent title as \"Managing Director, Head of Capital Management Department\" ([DiDi F-1, 2021-06](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1764757/000104746921001194/a2243272zf-1.htm)).\n- **The New Business Office was created for him.** The parent set up the department and named him head in the same two-line release: \"Establishment of Department: New Business Office\" ([SBG 2022-11-21](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20221121)). What the office does is not published anywhere I could find. Unconfirmed.\n- **Vision Fund 2 dropped its $100M floor, which is how Japan and smaller deals opened up.** \"2号ファンドでは投資先がよりアーリーな企業になり\" (Fund 2 targets became earlier-stage, with smaller checks) ([Toyo Keizai, 2021-11-12](https://toyokeizai.net/articles/-/575872?display=b)). Bloomberg in 2022 put his Japan team at \"six members\" ([Bloomberg via The Star, 2022-04-29](https://www.thestar.com.my/business/business-news/2022/04/29/vision-fund-begins-to-devote-cash-to-japan)). \"Earlier\" here still means Series C.\n- **His robotics deals come with SoftBank Robotics distribution.** The Gaussian release says \"SoftBank Robotics is partnering with Gaussian Robotics on the global go-to-market\" ([Gaussian, 2021-11-10](https://gausium.com/news/gaussian-robotics-secures-188-million-in-series-c-funding/)). This is the strategic tie in his book: money plus a SoftBank operating company as channel.\n- **He frames quantum as an AI bet.** On QuEra: \"shaping the future of computation as the next step of AI\" ([QuEra release via The Quantum Insider, 2025-02-11](https://thequantuminsider.com/2025/02/11/quera-computing-completes-230-million-financing-to-accelerate-development-of-large-scale-fault-tolerant-quantum-computers/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium, and only for one purpose.** He is not a seed co-investor: no deal of his is earlier than a Series C. He matters in two cases. (1) A physical-AI or autonomy company in my portfolio that needs a $500M+ later round: he is the person who can take it to Son, and SoftBank led and then re-upped at Wayve ($1.05B C in 2024, co-led $1.2B D in 2026). (2) Japan distribution for a robotics company through SoftBank Robotics. He shares a cap table with Eclipse (my type of fund) at Wayve, which is the natural warm path. For frontier labs, chips and AI-native automation he is not the contact: those go through Son and the parent directly (OpenAI, Ampere), and he is not named on any of them.\n\n## Background\n- Born 1973. BA in law, Keio University, 1995; LL.M., NYU School of Law, 2001 ([Toyo Keizai](https://toyokeizai.net/articles/-/575872?display=b); [DiDi F-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1764757/000104746921001194/a2243272zf-1.htm)).\n- **2002 to 2016, Mizuho Securities.** Fourteen years in structured finance, buyout finance and M&A; left as Joint Head of the Strategic Solutions Department. Advised SoftBank on the Vodafone Japan, Sprint and Arm acquisitions (DiDi F-1).\n- **2016-11, SoftBank Group.** Deputy Head of Finance Unit and General Manager of a newly created Investment Planning Office ([SBG 2016-11-01](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20161101)).\n- **2017-06.** Corporate Officer, Deputy Head of Finance Unit and Head of Legal Unit ([SBG 2017-06-21](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20170621)). Held until 2018-06 (DiDi F-1).\n- **2018.** Managing Partner at SoftBank Investment Advisers, Tokyo, covering Asia and China: DiDi, Full Truck Alliance, Ping An Good Doctor (Bloomberg 2020). DiDi director from 2019-10; stepped down when the F-1 went effective in 2021-06 (DiDi F-1).\n- **2020-05.** Moved back to the parent in the reshuffle that followed the Vision Fund's $18B loss (Bloomberg 2020). Parent title by 2021-06: Managing Director, Head of Capital Management Department (DiDi F-1). Toyo Keizai dates his managing-partner term as 2018 to 2021.\n- **2022-04.** Described again as a Vision Fund managing partner, overseeing Japan (Bloomberg via The Star). **2022-11-21:** also Head of the parent's New Business Office (SBG).\n- **2024-05.** Joined Wayve's board with the Series C ([Wayve](https://wayve.ai/press/series-c/)). **2026-02:** titled \"Managing Partner at SoftBank Global Advisers\" (the Vision Fund 2 manager) on the Series D ([Wayve](https://wayve.ai/press/series-d/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [visionfund.com/team/kentaro-matsui](https://visionfund.com/team/kentaro-matsui) | Live on 2026-11-10; bio, Q&A and portfolio list |\n| Substack / blog | not found | |\n| X | not found | No account found |\n| LinkedIn | not found | Not linked from the fund page; none verified |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\nNo direct contact is published. The working routes are a warm intro from a Wayve co-investor (Eclipse, Balderton) or from a founder in his book, or through his directors (Jake Qu, Hiroshi Sensaki, Peter Park), who appear on most of his deals.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2021-11 — Toyo Keizai interview — why the Vision Fund turned to Japan — [link](https://toyokeizai.net/articles/-/575872?display=b) — Fund 1's $100M minimum kept it out of Japan; Fund 2 writes smaller checks.\n- 2022-04 — Bloomberg interview, Tokyo — [link](https://www.thestar.com.my/business/business-news/2022/04/29/vision-fund-begins-to-devote-cash-to-japan) — Japan's weight in the portfolio will \"definitely\" rise; interest in medical tech, SaaS and robotics.\n- 2026-02 — Vision Fund feature on Wayve — [link](https://visionfund.com/insights/wayve-alex-kendall-road-to-self-driving-cars) — how he showed Wayve to Son.\n- No podcast or conference talk found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Ecosystem over capital.** \"It's not just about our capital, it's our ecosystem\" ([fund page Q&A](https://visionfund.com/team/kentaro-matsui)). In practice: SoftBank Robotics as channel for Gaussian; group companies as partners for Japanese portfolio companies (Toyo Keizai; from the paywalled part, seen only in a search summary, so low confidence).\n- **Comfortable being called reckless.** His stated lesson is that conventional wisdom has often been \"completely wrong\" (fund page Q&A). This is the house view, learned on Vodafone Japan, Sprint and Arm.\n- **Physical AI and autonomy are the through-line.** Brain Corp, DiDi Autonomous, Keenon, Gaussian, Wayve: machines that navigate. He bought into Son's view that lidar-and-maps autonomy would not scale and backed end-to-end learning at Wayve.\n- **Calls:** too early to score in public data. He was DiDi's SoftBank director from 2019 until its 2021 IPO. At Wayve, SoftBank re-upped 21 months after leading the C, at $8.6B post-money; the C valuation was not disclosed.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Vision Fund feature on Wayve](https://visionfund.com/insights/wayve-alex-kendall-road-to-self-driving-cars): the only first-person account of how one of his deals reached Son.\n2. [DiDi F-1 director biography](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1764757/000104746921001194/a2243272zf-1.htm): the cleanest dated career record, in a filing.\n3. [Toyo Keizai interview](https://toyokeizai.net/articles/-/575872?display=b): Fund 1 against Fund 2 in his words (paywalled after page one).\n4. [Bloomberg, 2020-05-29](https://uk.finance.yahoo.com/news/softbank-hands-roles-two-vision-020000527.html): the move back to the parent.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public social presence; everything on record is a press-release quote or an interview arranged by SoftBank.\n\n## Portfolio\n\"Named how\" separates a release quote from a fund-page team listing. Pool is as the source states it.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Named how | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Wayve | Series C ($1.05B) | 2024-05-07 | yes (SoftBank Group) | NVIDIA, Microsoft | quoted; board seat | [Wayve](https://wayve.ai/press/series-c/) |\n| Wayve | Series D ($1.2B at $8.6B post) | 2026-02-25 | co-lead (Vision Fund 2) | Eclipse, Balderton (co-leads) | quoted | [Wayve](https://wayve.ai/press/series-d/) |\n| QuEra Computing | Growth (over $230M; $60M conditional) | 2025-02-11 | not stated | Google, Valor Equity Partners, QVT, Safar | quoted | [release](https://thequantuminsider.com/2025/02/11/quera-computing-completes-230-million-financing-to-accelerate-development-of-large-scale-fault-tolerant-quantum-computers/) |\n| LegalOn Technologies (then LegalForce) | Series D (about JPY 13.7B) | 2022-06-23 | yes (Vision Fund 2) | Sequoia China, Goldman Sachs, WiL, Mizuho Capital, MUFG Capital | quoted | [LegalForce](https://en.legalontech.jp/2966/) |\n| AI Medical Service | Series C (amount and lead unconfirmed here) | 2022-04-26 | unconfirmed | not stated | interviewed on the deal | [Bloomberg via The Star](https://www.thestar.com.my/business/business-news/2022/04/29/vision-fund-begins-to-devote-cash-to-japan) |\n| Gaussian Robotics | Series C ($188M) | 2021-11-10 | co-lead (Vision Fund 2 with Capital Today) | Capital Today (co-lead); others not checked | quoted | [Gaussian](https://gausium.com/news/gaussian-robotics-secures-188-million-in-series-c-funding/) |\n| Keenon Robotics | Series D ($200M) | 2021-09-15 | yes (Vision Fund 2) | CICC ALPHA, Prosperity7 | quoted | [Keenon](https://kyodonewsprwire.jp/release/202109150116) |\n| DiDi Autonomous Driving | Growth (over $500M, first outside round) | 2020-05-29 | yes (Vision Fund 2) | not stated | fund-page listing only | [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2020/05/29/softbank-leads-500-million-investment-in-chinese-uber-rival-didi.html), [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/didi-autonomous) |\n| Classiq | Series C extension | 2025-07-30 | no | CDP Venture Capital | fund-page listing only | [Classiq](https://classiq.io/insights/softbank-vision-fund-2-makes-strategic-investment-in-classiq-expanding-series-c) |\n| DiDi | Growth (Vision Fund 1 position) | board 2019-10 to 2021-06 | n/a | n/a | director | [DiDi F-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1764757/000104746921001194/a2243272zf-1.htm) |\n\nAlso on his fund page, with no round detail checked: Brain Corp (Vision Fund 1), Terabase, Aculys, Beisen, Dianxiaomi, Full Truck Alliance, Hesi (expense software, not the lidar maker Hesai), Huice, OPay, Ping An Good Doctor, Ping An HealthKonnect, Soda, ZhongAn ([fund page](https://visionfund.com/team/kentaro-matsui)). No row was written for Brain Corp because the name resolver maps it to an existing id, `brain-co`, that looks like a different company.\n\n**Relation to SoftBank's own business:** Wayve, DiDi Autonomous: autonomy, adjacent to Arm and to SoftBank's robotics push; no supply contract disclosed. Keenon, Gaussian: service robots, sold through SoftBank Robotics (partner). LegalOn, AI Medical Service: Japanese AI software, no tie stated. QuEra, Classiq: quantum, none.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Wayve (2024 to 2026).** Son wanted autonomy but distrusted the prevailing approach. Matsui showed him Wayve's end-to-end model and Son \"got very excited\" ([Vision Fund feature](https://visionfund.com/insights/wayve-alex-kendall-road-to-self-driving-cars)). SoftBank Group led the $1.05B Series C in May 2024 and took a board seat, which Matsui holds ([Wayve](https://wayve.ai/press/series-c/)). SoftBank entered at the C; Microsoft was already an investor and Eclipse an earlier backer, per the same release. In February 2026 Vision Fund 2 co-led the $1.2B D with Eclipse and Balderton at $8.6B post ([Wayve](https://wayve.ai/press/series-d/)). The lead moved from \"SoftBank Group\" to \"Vision Fund 2\" between rounds; the fund page books the holding to SVF2. Who first introduced Wayve to Matsui: not found.\n- **Japan push (2021 to 2022).** After Fund 2 dropped the $100M minimum, his six-person Tokyo team did Aculys and Soda in late 2021, AI Medical Service in April 2022 and LegalForce in June 2022 (Bloomberg via The Star; LegalForce release).\n- **China service robots (2021).** Keenon ($200M D, led) and Gaussian ($188M C, co-led) within two months, both with Jake Qu, both tied to SoftBank Robotics distribution.\n\n## Relationships\n- **Masayoshi Son.** Direct line; pitched Wayve to him. Banker to Son's three largest acquisitions before joining.\n- **Alex Kendall** (Wayve CEO). Board colleague since 2024-05.\n- **Jake Qu, Hiroshi Sensaki, Peter Park** (Directors, Asia). His working team: Qu on the robotics and autonomy deals and Wayve, Sensaki on the Japan deals and QuEra, Park on QuEra, Terabase and Classiq (fund portfolio pages, fetched 2026-11-10).\n- **Max Ohrstrand, Grong Wang** (EMEA). Run Wayve day to day for the fund.\n- **Eclipse, Balderton.** Co-leads on the Wayve D.\n- **Akshay Naheta.** Moved to the parent in the same 2020 reshuffle (Bloomberg 2020).\n\n## Open questions\n- What the New Business Office does, its budget, and whether it can invest off the parent's balance sheet. Nothing published.\n- Whether he sits on the Vision Fund investment committee. Membership is not disclosed.\n- SoftBank's dollar share of either Wayve round.\n- Exact month he regained the managing-partner title (between 2021-11 and 2022-04).\n\n*Profile written 2026-11-10.*\n", "person:kevin-hartz": "\n# Kevin Hartz\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. Builds on firms/a-star.md, people/ramtin-naimi.md, firms/abstract.md, firms/nova-global.md and patterns/2026-11-10-who-backs-which-fund.md, and cites them rather than repeating them. Slug note: older rows use `a` for A*. This file uses the canonical `a-star` (firms/a-star.md), so the main session should reconcile them. Sources refer to him as \"he\".*\n\n## One-line take\nHartz is a second-generation PayPal-network operator who runs A* as a small, relationship-led seed fund. His wider value is as a connector and LP: he anchored Abstract Fund I, backed Koko Xs's first fund, and is the kind of GP who says out loud which allocators back concentrated new managers.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** prior relationships that become seeds. Decagon came from his angel check in a founder's earlier, failed company. A* itself came from a Ramp seed angel check, which is how he met Bennett Siegel [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/). On Uncapped (2026-05) he says the relationship-sourced half of A*'s seeds has returned disproportionately [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/).\n- **What constrains him:** fund size, which he keeps small on purpose. He says \"we stayed small so we can be agile\" and that he has seen funds' behavior change as they grow [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/). The cost is that large hardware and lab rounds pass A* by. He calls those \"tough… to see and have them go by us\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/).\n- **What he'd never do:** pay up in late rounds of a failing company (he calls that \"the sucker's bet\") or do roll-ups (\"We will not be doing any roll-ups\") [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/). He also thinks AI is heading to \"the mother of all bubbles\", though not yet [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/).\n- **Return bar:** his threshold is a 100x post-dilution change in share price [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **A* began with a Ramp angel check.** He met Siegel, then at Coatue, while angel-investing in Ramp's seed: \"we first met through Ramp, and that was at the seed stage\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/) (Uncapped, 2026-05-12).\n- **He names Brian Singerman's new LP vehicle as a backer of concentrated managers.** He says \"Brian Singerman is creating this LP fund to find new managers\" so they can go all in [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/). The repo already has this as GPx (Singerman and Lee Linden), with lens_fit high (leads.csv, surfaced by jack-altman). Hartz is a warm path to it.\n- **He is Ramtin Naimi's closest investing confidant and a deal pipe.**\n  - Colossus (2025-07) says Naimi talks to \"Kevie\" about four hours a week and that A* sits a block from Abstract [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/).\n  - Hebbia: Naimi spotted Hartz as an angel on Hebbia's Floodgate announcement. \"A quick text to Hartz yielded an introduction\" to George Sivulka [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/).\n  - In 2017 Hartz was the second link in the chain that sold 20% of Abstract's management company. Cyan Banister introduced Naimi to Hartz, and Hartz introduced him to Rabois and Dixon [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/).\n- **Decagon was a bet on a failed team.** Hartz was an angel in Ashwin Sreenivas's earlier company, Helia. Helia's founders included Russell Kaplan, now president of Cognition [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/). On The Peel he says \"We seeded Decagon\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/). (Both are already in insights.csv.)\n- **He admits passing on Lyft.** He spent time with Lyft, \"kept looking at the numbers\", and says \"I was very wrong on that one\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/) (The Peel, 2026-01-08).\n- **He learned conviction from Singerman.** He recalls Singerman's question at Founders Fund, \"Are you sure you have absolute conviction?\" He says high conviction from one partner made the partner vote almost irrelevant [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/). Note that this is Hartz quoting Singerman.\n- **He says the teen-founder tilt was inbound, not planned:** \"we didn't really seek it out. It's happening.\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/). TechCrunch (2025-10-18) puts teen founders at close to 20% of recent deals, up from about 5% two years earlier [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/kevin-hartz-just-closed-third-184505752.html). He credits the Thiel Fellowship as \"very prescient\" about dropouts [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/).\n- **He thinks follow-ons matter more than the seed:** \"follow-on decisions are actually in many cases more important\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/).\n- **A* incubates companies, not only funds them.** Hartz names three incubations:\n  - Sauron, home security, which he calls \"My little darling incubation.\"\n  - Multiply, AI mortgages. The transcript says it is \"backed by Moon and Kleiner\". \"Moon\" is unidentified.\n  - 15th, an AI tax accountant.\n  [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/)\n- **Sauron's origin.** TechCrunch (2025-12-28) says his San Francisco system \"failed to alert him as an intruder rang his doorbell\". He co-founded Sauron with Jack Abraham (Atomic) in 2024. It has raised $24M from 8VC, Atomic, A* and executives behind Flock Safety and Palantir. Sonos's former CPO, Max Bouvat-Merlin, became CEO, and delivery slipped to late 2026 at the earliest [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/28/from-sonos-to-sauron-new-ceo-takes-on-high-end-home-security-startup-still-in-development/). His stated playbook is Tesla's Roadster-first route: \"positioning Sauron as a luxury good\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HzY-gpa2T80&t=1901s) (Oxford Union, 2026-05-08).\n- **His first exit went to a hotel-movie company.** His first company, ConnectGroup (hotel internet), sold to LodgeNet. He jokes \"we sold the company to an adult film conglomerate\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HzY-gpa2T80&t=493s). He put the proceeds into PayPal's seed: \"I was an investor in the seed round\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HzY-gpa2T80&t=586s). On The Peel he puts the share at \"like 100%\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/). The two accounts differ, so treat the share as uncertain.\n- **His Xoom regret is market size,** not the exit: \"I just couldn't imagine how big the market could have been\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HzY-gpa2T80&t=752s).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** As a deal partner he is a poor fit for the owner's checks. A* writes $3–5M average checks and cannot do $10–100M lab seeds (firms/a-star.md). Its Flapping Airplanes position is a non-lead Series A participation. Where he matters to the owner:\n1. **LP and allocator access.** He is a GP who personally LPs into young, concentrated managers: an anchor in Abstract Fund I and a backer of Koko Xs Fund I. He also points to Singerman's GPx as an allocator for concentrated new managers. A first-time frontier-AI manager could reach this group through him.\n2. **Early AI-app timing.** He was in Decagon at seed (2024) through a relationship, and A* co-led seeds in Simile and Watney.\n3. **Network node.** He connects PayPal and Founders Fund (Thiel, Sacks, Levchin are A* Fund I LPs), the Naimi/Abstract group, and teen and dropout founders (Thiel Fellows, Z Fellows).\n\n## Background\n- Stanford, BA and BS in history and applied earth sciences; Oxford (University College), MSt in British history [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kevin_Hartz). Years not sourced.\n- Silicon Graphics: product manager on the Cosmo Player VR browser [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kevin_Hartz).\n- ConnectGroup (hotel high-speed internet), sold to LodgeNet [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HzY-gpa2T80&t=493s). Proceeds went into PayPal (Confinity) at seed.\n- Xoom: co-founded 2001 with Alan Braverman, CEO to 2005, on the board through the 2013 IPO and the 2015 sale to PayPal [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kevin_Hartz). The SPAC prospectus gives the price as $1.1B [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1816613/000110465920096906/tm2023765-13_424b4.htm).\n- Eventbrite: co-founded 2006 with Julia Hartz (his wife) and Renaud Visage. CEO to 2016, then executive chairman. Did not stand for board re-election in 2024-06 [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kevin_Hartz). Bending Spoons agreed to buy Eventbrite for about $500M in cash ($4.50 a share), announced 2025-12-02, against a $1.76B valuation at the 2018 IPO [src](https://meetings.skift.com/2025/12/02/bending-spoons-to-acquire-eventbrite-in-500-million-cash-deal/).\n- Founders Fund partner, 2016-09 to 2018-06 [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kevin_Hartz).\n- SPACs: \"one\" ($200M IPO, 2020-08, sponsored by A-star) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1816613/000110465920096906/tm2023765-13_424b4.htm) merged with Markforged at a $2.1B equity value [src](https://3dprint.com/279359/3d-printing-startup-markforged-to-go-public-via-spac-merger-with-blank-check-firm/). A second SPAC, \"two\", filed for $200M on 2021-03-02 [src](https://www.renaissancecapital.com/IPO-Center/News/78660/Tech-SPAC-two-files-for-a-$200-million-IPO). Its merger target is not confirmed.\n- A*: co-founded 2020 with Bennett Siegel and Gautam Gupta. Fund III is $450M (2026) [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/kevin-hartz-just-closed-third-184505752.html).\n- Sauron: co-founder and chairman, 2024 [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/28/from-sonos-to-sauron-new-ceo-takes-on-high-end-home-security-startup-still-in-development/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.a-star.co/team | Firm team page, title only, no bio (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | A* posts deal notes at https://www.a-star.co/news, mostly unbylined |\n| X | https://x.com/kevinhartz | Handle from a third-party analytics page ([superx](https://superx.so/creators/kevinhartz)); not opened directly; follower count omitted (no as-of date) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/hartz/ | Linked from A*'s own team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only (see below) |\n| Firm X | https://x.com/A_StarVC | A* account, from the team page |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-05 — Uncapped with Jack Altman #49 — \"How AI is rewriting seed-stage investing\" (with Siegel) — [transcript](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/), [episode](https://uncappedpod.com/p/how-ai-is-rewriting-seed-stage-investing) — Ramp as the origin of A*; Singerman's LP fund; \"mother of all bubbles\"; follow-ons beat initial checks.\n- 2026-05-08 — Oxford Union — interview — [video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HzY-gpa2T80), [transcript](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/942752/) — ConnectGroup sold to LodgeNet; PayPal seed; Xoom market-size regret; Sauron as a Tesla-style luxury entry.\n- 2026-01-08 — The Peel with Turner Novak — \"Backing Teen Founders, Lessons from the PayPal Mafia\" — [transcript](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/) — Lyft pass; Singerman's conviction test; incubations (Sauron, Multiply, 15th); 100x bar.\n- 2025-09-30 — 30 Years in 30 Minutes — [transcript](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/364590/) — says he \"started too late\"; \"there's no need to wait\".\n- Earlier — 20VC with Troy Steckenrider on SPACs — [link](https://thetwentyminutevc.libsyn.com/20vc-spacs-what-are-they-why-now-how-do-they-change-the-venture-landscape-are-they-better-than-ipos-direct-listings-how-should-founders-think-about-them-kevin-hartz-troy-steckenrider-a) (not reviewed).\n\n## How they think\n- **Talent veins:** finding founders is \"a lot like mining\" for a vein of gold, and the founders he backs have \"had to overcome some kind of obstacles\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/).\n- **Language as signal:** \"a great founder will have every word very much meaning\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/).\n- **Young founders:** \"these founders can kind of see around corners\"; \"They're fearless.\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HzY-gpa2T80&t=1639s).\n- **Concentration:** \"putting more and more capital to work in a few names\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HzY-gpa2T80&t=1239s). He credits Founders Fund (SpaceX) with pioneering concentrated bets [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/).\n- **Contrarian:** a bear on the AI cycle while investing in it. He calls large funds \"big funds, big fees\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/).\n- **Right:** PayPal seed; Airbnb, Pinterest and Uber (prospectus list) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1816613/000110465920096906/tm2023765-13_424b4.htm); Decagon seed; Ramp seed.\n- **Wrong (his words):** Lyft pass; underestimating Xoom's market. Eventbrite's sale for about $500M against a $1.76B IPO valuation is a downstream fact, not a self-assessment.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Uncapped #49 transcript: his fund-size, follow-on and LP views in his own words [link](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/).\n2. The Peel transcript: the PayPal-to-A* arc, the Lyft pass and the incubations [link](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/).\n3. Colossus on Ramtin Naimi: Hartz as the intro engine and deal partner behind Abstract [link](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/).\n4. A* Fund III note: \"Seed has become a market of access\" [link](https://www.a-star.co/news/fundiii).\n5. TechCrunch on Sauron: his operator streak in 2024–26 [link](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/28/from-sonos-to-sauron-new-ceo-takes-on-high-end-home-security-startup-still-in-development/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed. His X account was not readable without login, and only a third-party analytics page confirmed the handle. Who he amplifies is unknown.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| PayPal (Confinity) | Seed (personal) | ~1999 (not sourced) | no | n/a | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HzY-gpa2T80&t=586s) |\n| Airbnb, Pinterest, Trulia, Opendoor | Personal angel (rounds not given) | n/a | no | n/a | [prospectus](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1816613/000110465920096906/tm2023765-13_424b4.htm) |\n| Uber | Series B (personal; \"didn't get in till Series B\") | n/a | no | Cyan Banister was in seed | [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/) |\n| Ramp | Seed (personal angel) | ~2019 (not sourced) | no | n/a | [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/) |\n| Helia (Ashwin Sreenivas, Russell Kaplan) | Angel | n/a | no | n/a | [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/) |\n| Hebbia | Angel (on Floodgate announcement) | ~2020 | no | Floodgate | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Decagon | Seed via A* (a16z led), then every round | 2024 | no | a16z, Accel, BCV | [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/) |\n| Polymarket | Series B ($45M, personal) | 2024-05-14 | no | Founders Fund (lead), Vitalik Buterin, Dragonfly | [src](https://www.theblock.co/post/294367/polymarket-raises-45-million-from-peter-thiels-founders-fund-vitalik-buterin-and-others) |\n| Sauron | Co-founder; A* invested ($24M raised in total) | 2024– | incubation | 8VC, Atomic | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/28/from-sonos-to-sauron-new-ceo-takes-on-high-end-home-security-startup-still-in-development/) |\n| Multiply (AI mortgages) | A* incubation | n/a | yes | Kleiner (per Hartz) | [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/) |\n| 15th (AI tax accountant) | A* incubation | n/a | yes | n/a | [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/) |\n| Markforged | SPAC sponsor (\"one\"); joined board | 2021 | yes (sponsor) | n/a | [src](https://3dprint.com/279359/3d-printing-startup-markforged-to-go-public-via-spac-merger-with-blank-check-firm/) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Personal angel per aggregator only (low) | n/a | no | n/a | [aggregator](https://siliconvalleyinvestclub.com/companies/flappingairplanes/) |\n\nOther A* deals (Simile, Watney, Antioch, Andera, Mercor, Cognition and others) are in firms/a-star.md. Hartz's personal role in them is not sourced.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Decagon (2024):** Hartz backed Helia, which failed, then the founder's next team. Siegel says there \"wasn't really an idea\" at first. a16z led the seed and A* stayed in every round (firms/a-star.md; [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/)).\n- **Hebbia (2020):** Naimi spotted Hartz's name on the Floodgate announcement and texted him, and Hartz introduced him to Sivulka [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/).\n- **Abstract management company (c. 2017):** Banister introduced Naimi to Hartz. Hartz recalls thinking \"If you were this good not knowing anybody\", and introduced Naimi to Rabois and Dixon. Nine people bought 20% for $10M and became anchor LPs in the $100M Fund I [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/).\n- **Sauron (2024):** the founding story is a failed doorbell alert, with co-founder Abraham facing the same problem in Miami Beach [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/28/from-sonos-to-sauron-new-ceo-takes-on-high-end-home-security-startup-still-in-development/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bennett Siegel | A* co-founder; met through Ramp's seed, when Siegel was at Coatue | pre-2020 | [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/) |\n| Gautam Gupta | A* co-founder | 2020 | [src](https://www.a-star.co/team) |\n| Julia Hartz | Wife; Eventbrite co-founder | 2006 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kevin_Hartz) |\n| Ramtin Naimi (Abstract) | Closest investing confidant; anchor LP and mgmt-co investor | 2017 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Cyan Banister | Introduced Naimi to him; he admires her \"weird things\" sourcing | 2017 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/), [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/) |\n| Brian Singerman | Founders Fund colleague; source of the conviction test; building an LP fund for new managers | 2016 | [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/) |\n| Peter Thiel, David Sacks, Max Levchin | PayPal network; A* Fund I LPs | ~1999 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/21/kevin-hartzs-a-raises-its-second-oversubscribed-fund-in-two-years/) |\n| Jack Abraham (Atomic) | Sauron co-founder | 2024 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/28/from-sonos-to-sauron-new-ceo-takes-on-high-end-home-security-startup-still-in-development/) |\n| Ashwin Sreenivas, Russell Kaplan | Helia angel, then Decagon | pre-2023 | [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/) |\n| Koko Xs (Nova) | LP in Fund I | 2024 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc) |\n| Troy Steckenrider, Spike Lipkin | SPAC co-founders (\"one\") | 2020 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1816613/000110465920096906/tm2023765-13_424b4.htm) |\n| Finn Murphy | Friend, per The Peel (host's description) | n/a | [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What Multiply's investor named \"Moon\" refers to (possibly Moonshots, or a transcription error). Also who founded Multiply and 15th, and when.\n- What \"two\" merged with: Logistic Properties of the Americas is suggested only by a logo caption.\n- Whether Hartz is an LP in GPx (Singerman and Linden), or GPx in A*.\n- Whether he personally angel-invested in Flapping Airplanes (aggregator only), and whether Mach Industries is an A* deal (named on The Peel as an example only).\n- How much of A* Fund III comes from Carnegie Mellon, and which other endowments are in it.\n\n## Sources\n- https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/ (Uncapped #49 transcript, 2026-05-12)\n- https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/627043/ (The Peel transcript, 2026-01-08)\n- https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/942752/ and https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HzY-gpa2T80 (Oxford Union, 2026-05-08)\n- https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/364590/ (30 Years in 30 Minutes, 2025-09-30)\n- https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/ (2025-07)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/28/from-sonos-to-sauron-new-ceo-takes-on-high-end-home-security-startup-still-in-development/\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kevin_Hartz\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1816613/000110465920096906/tm2023765-13_424b4.htm\n- https://3dprint.com/279359/3d-printing-startup-markforged-to-go-public-via-spac-merger-with-blank-check-firm/\n- https://www.renaissancecapital.com/IPO-Center/News/78660/Tech-SPAC-two-files-for-a-$200-million-IPO\n- https://meetings.skift.com/2025/12/02/bending-spoons-to-acquire-eventbrite-in-500-million-cash-deal/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/21/kevin-hartzs-a-raises-its-second-oversubscribed-fund-in-two-years/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/kevin-hartz-just-closed-third-184505752.html\n- https://www.theblock.co/post/294367/polymarket-raises-45-million-from-peter-thiels-founders-fund-vitalik-buterin-and-others\n- https://www.a-star.co/team ; https://www.a-star.co/news/fundiii\n- https://superx.so/creators/kevinhartz (third-party, handle only)\n- https://siliconvalleyinvestclub.com/companies/flappingairplanes/ (aggregator)\n", "person:kevin-lee": "\n# Kevin Lee\n\n*Written 2026-11-11 by the hft-semis sweep. Identity check: \"Kevin Lee\" is a common name. This profile covers only the HRT Head of Research & Development. The 2022 Built In interview and the 2026 CoreWeave release are linked by the same title at the same firm. No photo or other bio confirms that they are the same person, so treat the link as strong but unproven. A ZoomInfo page for a \"Kevin Lee\" with a ThinKom Solutions email is a different person and is not used.*\n\n## One-line take\nKevin Lee is not an investor. He is the research-side buyer whose compute choices decide which AI infrastructure HRT pays for, and HRT's balance-sheet ventures desk then tends to back that same supply chain (Etched: tested first, then invested).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** as head of R&D he is the public owner of HRT's AI research platform. He framed the CoreWeave contract as a research decision: \"the AI platform we build on matters as much as the models we build\" ([CoreWeave IR, 2026-08-20](https://investors.coreweave.com/news/news-details/2026/Hudson-River-Trading-to-Build-Next-Gen-Research-Platform-Powered-by-NVIDIA-Vera-Rubin-NVL72-on-CoreWeave-Cloud/default.aspx)).\n- **Constraint:** he spends HRT's money on compute, not on equity. No source places him on HRT's internal Investment Committee or on any venture round.\n- **What he'd never do:** nothing sourced.\n- **Insider view:** he is an HRT lifer who joined straight out of school around 2012 ([Built In, 2022-11-04](https://www.builtinaustin.com/articles/college-career-fairs-look-orange-why-you-want-your-first-job-be-hudson-river-trading)), so his authority comes from his research seniority inside HRT, not from an outside hire.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Selection criterion for compute vendors: production reliability, not price or peak specs.** He said CoreWeave was chosen because they \"understand what it takes to run AI in demanding production environments\" ([CoreWeave IR](https://investors.coreweave.com/news/news-details/2026/Hudson-River-Trading-to-Build-Next-Gen-Research-Platform-Powered-by-NVIDIA-Vera-Rubin-NVL72-on-CoreWeave-Cloud/default.aspx)). That is the bar an inference-chip founder (Etched, Positron, OLIX) has to clear in an HRT evaluation.\n- **Flat decision path, from his own 2022 interview:** \"There isn't really a need to run up the management chain\" to start work on something, and \"You have a lot of freedom to pick what you want to do\" ([Built In, 2022-11-04](https://www.builtinaustin.com/articles/college-career-fairs-look-orange-why-you-want-your-first-job-be-hudson-river-trading)). Read with the 2026 deal, this suggests a hardware evaluation can start with a researcher and need no executive sponsor. That reading is my inference.\n- **Timing:** \"HRT became a CoreWeave customer in March\" 2026 ([Crypto Briefing, 2026-08-20](https://cryptobriefing.com/hudson-river-trading-signs-multibillion-dollar-coreweave-ai-computing-deal/)). The multi-billion commitment came about five months after the first contract. Correction: the earlier repo row credited this \"since 2026-03\" date to Channel Insider, but Channel Insider only says the deal \"expands an existing relationship\" ([src](https://www.channelinsider.com/ai/news-hudson-river-trading-coreweave-ai-deal/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He allocates no venture capital, so he is not a co-investor or LP for the owner. He matters as the evaluation gate. HRT's AI book (Etched Series B, Eridu Series A co-lead, OLIX B, Positron C, Gimlet B; see [firms/hudson-river-trading.md](../firms/hudson-river-trading.md)) follows its compute supply chain. Reuters reports that HRT is \"testing Etched chips\" and has invested ([src](https://uk.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/highfrequency-traders-race-to-build-ai-computing-power-93CH-4927634)). For an AI-chip or compute company in the owner's portfolio, Lee's org is the route to HRT as a customer, and possibly to a strategic check after that. No source shows him in the investment decision.\n\n## Background\n- Studied math and computer science. He thought his choices were software engineering or a math PhD until a college roommate told him about HRT ([Built In, 2022-11-04](https://www.builtinaustin.com/articles/college-career-fairs-look-orange-why-you-want-your-first-job-be-hudson-river-trading)). The school is not named.\n- Joined HRT \"10 years ago\" as of the November 2022 interview, so about 2012, straight from school (same source).\n- Head of Research and Development by 2022-11 (same source), and still Head of Research & Development on 2026-08-20 ([CoreWeave IR](https://investors.coreweave.com/news/news-details/2026/Hudson-River-Trading-to-Build-Next-Gen-Research-Platform-Powered-by-NVIDIA-Vera-Rubin-NVL72-on-CoreWeave-Cloud/default.aspx)).\n- How he got the seat: promoted internally. The intermediate titles are not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | Not on HRT's [machine learning page](https://www.hudsonrivertrading.com/machine-learning/), which names only Iain Dunning and interns (checked 2026-11-11) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | No HRT tech-blog posts by him found |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | The name is too common to pin down a profile without a primary link |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\nHow a founder or co-investor reaches him: no direct channel is published. CoreWeave's release lists only press@coreweave.com. For HRT, the published venture inbox is strategicvc@hudson-trading.com ([HRT ventures page](https://www.hudsonrivertrading.com/ventures/)). For a compute vendor, the practical route is through HRT's compute-systems org (Gerard Bernabeu Altayo, named on the Lambda deal), or through a cloud partner HRT already uses (CoreWeave, Lambda). This route is inferred, not sourced.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2022-11 — Built In (written interview, Avery Komlofske) — \"College career fairs look orange…\" — [link](https://www.builtinaustin.com/articles/college-career-fairs-look-orange-why-you-want-your-first-job-be-hudson-river-trading) — Describes a flat structure where researchers choose their own projects.\n- 2026-08-20 — CoreWeave press release (quoted) — [link](https://investors.coreweave.com/news/news-details/2026/Hudson-River-Trading-to-Build-Next-Gen-Research-Platform-Powered-by-NVIDIA-Vera-Rubin-NVL72-on-CoreWeave-Cloud/default.aspx)\n- No podcasts or conference talks found.\n\n## How they think\n- Infrastructure is part of the research edge, not a commodity: \"the AI platform we build on matters as much as the models we build.\"\n- Picks vendors on operational track record, saying CoreWeave can run AI \"in demanding production environments.\"\n- Prefers bottom-up project choice over top-down assignment (2022).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [CoreWeave IR release, 2026-08-20](https://investors.coreweave.com/news/news-details/2026/Hudson-River-Trading-to-Build-Next-Gen-Research-Platform-Powered-by-NVIDIA-Vera-Rubin-NVL72-on-CoreWeave-Cloud/default.aspx): his only on-record words about compute.\n2. [Built In, 2022-11-04](https://www.builtinaustin.com/articles/college-career-fairs-look-orange-why-you-want-your-first-job-be-hudson-river-trading): his background and how HRT research runs.\n3. [Reuters via Investing.com, 2026-10-23](https://uk.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/highfrequency-traders-race-to-build-ai-computing-power-93CH-4927634): HRT's compute build-out and the Etched test-then-invest link. He is not named in it.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| none | | | | | No venture deal names him. The CoreWeave agreement below is a customer contract and is deliberately **not** written as a deal row. |\n\nDeals he is personally named on:\n- **CoreWeave** (customer contract, not an investment): multi-year, multi-billion-dollar cloud agreement for NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 and HGX B200 clusters, with Spectrum-X Direct Connect to HRT's on-premises systems. Announced 2026-08-20. It expanded a relationship that began in 2026-03, so it is a re-up, not HRT's first contract. He is the only HRT person quoted ([CoreWeave IR](https://investors.coreweave.com/news/news-details/2026/Hudson-River-Trading-to-Build-Next-Gen-Research-Platform-Powered-by-NVIDIA-Vera-Rubin-NVL72-on-CoreWeave-Cloud/default.aspx); [Crypto Briefing](https://cryptobriefing.com/hudson-river-trading-signs-multibillion-dollar-coreweave-ai-computing-deal/)). Bloomberg is cited as saying the deal is \"worth billions of dollars\" ([Channel Insider](https://www.channelinsider.com/ai/news-hudson-river-trading-coreweave-ai-deal/)).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **CoreWeave:** HRT started as a customer in 2026-03, and the multi-billion-dollar expansion was announced 2026-08-20. Lee's stated reasons were production reliability and CoreWeave's ability to scale with HRT. CoreWeave's quote came from CRO Jon Jones. Whether this is the same Jon Jones as the repo's `jon-jones` (former AWS head of startups) is unconfirmed. The intro path and the price are not disclosed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hudson River Trading | at: Head of Research & Development | about 2012 (joined); title held by 2022-11 | [Built In](https://www.builtinaustin.com/articles/college-career-fairs-look-orange-why-you-want-your-first-job-be-hudson-river-trading) |\n| CoreWeave | customer-side executive on HRT's cloud contract | 2026-08-20 | [CoreWeave IR](https://investors.coreweave.com/news/news-details/2026/Hudson-River-Trading-to-Build-Next-Gen-Research-Platform-Powered-by-NVIDIA-Vera-Rubin-NVL72-on-CoreWeave-Cloud/default.aspx) |\n| Iain Dunning (HRT Head of AI) | colleague; the public voice on HRT's compute spend | unknown | [Reuters](https://uk.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/highfrequency-traders-race-to-build-ai-computing-power-93CH-4927634) |\n| Gerard Bernabeu Altayo (HRT Compute Systems Lead) | colleague; named on the Lambda B200 deal | unknown | [Lambda](https://lambda.com/blog/lambda-partners-with-hudson-river-trading-to-power-quantitative-research-and-development) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Does R&D sign off on chip evaluations (Etched, Positron, OLIX) that come before HRT Ventures invests? Is Lee on the Investment Committee?\n- Which school, and which titles did he hold between 2012 and 2022?\n- How R&D (Lee) and AI (Dunning) split ownership of the compute budget.\n- A verified headshot or primary profile link.\n\n## Re-check 2026-11-11 (second pass)\n- Re-fetched the [Built In interview](https://www.builtinaustin.com/articles/college-career-fairs-look-orange-why-you-want-your-first-job-be-hudson-river-trading): it still gives his title as head of research and development, the \"10 years ago\" join date and the math and CS background, and still names no school.\n- [Crypto Briefing](https://cryptobriefing.com/hudson-river-trading-signs-multibillion-dollar-coreweave-ai-computing-deal/) carries two more Lee remarks that the CoreWeave release does not have, probably from the Bloomberg interview (unconfirmed). He said more compute lets researchers \"run more experiments\", and that CoreWeave \"performed well in delivering infrastructure on schedule\". So his vendor test is delivery to schedule on earlier, smaller orders. CoreWeave got the multi-billion expansion after about five months of delivery under the March 2026 contract.\n- Two web searches pairing his name with HRT, R&D titles and schools turned up no new primary source: no LinkedIn, no conference talk, no school and no ventures or IC role. The firm page's people table credited the \"customer since 2026-03\" date to Channel Insider. That attribution is now corrected to Crypto Briefing.\n\n## Sources\n- https://investors.coreweave.com/news/news-details/2026/Hudson-River-Trading-to-Build-Next-Gen-Research-Platform-Powered-by-NVIDIA-Vera-Rubin-NVL72-on-CoreWeave-Cloud/default.aspx\n- https://www.coreweave.com/news/hudson-river-trading-to-build-next-gen-research-platform-powered-by-nvidia-vera-rubin-nvl72-on-coreweave-cloud\n- https://pulse2.com/coreweave-and-hudson-river-trading-sign-multi-billion-dollar-ai-cloud-deal-for-nvidia-vera-rubin-research-platform/\n- https://cryptobriefing.com/hudson-river-trading-signs-multibillion-dollar-coreweave-ai-computing-deal/\n- https://www.channelinsider.com/ai/news-hudson-river-trading-coreweave-ai-deal/\n- https://www.builtinaustin.com/articles/college-career-fairs-look-orange-why-you-want-your-first-job-be-hudson-river-trading\n- https://uk.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/highfrequency-traders-race-to-build-ai-computing-power-93CH-4927634\n- https://www.hudsonrivertrading.com/machine-learning/\n- https://www.hudsonrivertrading.com/ventures/\n- Not used (different person): ZoomInfo \"Kevin Lee\" with a thinkom.com email.\n", "person:kevin-zhang": "\n# Kevin Zhang\n\n## One-line take\nHe is BCV's quiet inception-stage partner. He got into PyTorch Lightning's founder before the company was incorporated. He has since moved from 2020-era workflow automation to frontier-adjacent bets. These include Hyde, a post-training stack for enterprises; Sentience, personal models; and a $75M seed in Solcoa, a rare-earth metals company. He also co-signed BCV's $1.6B Fund XI letter.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he meets technical founders very early and stays with them for years before writing the check.\n  - He met Will Falcon of Lightning AI over lunch \"long before he incorporated.\" BCV then co-led the seed in late 2019 ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/kevin-zhang/), [src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/our-investment-in-grid/)).\n  - He knew Sam Kececi of Sentience for \"the past couple of years\" before leading the 2026 seed ([src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/your-best-ai-assistant-is-you/)).\n  - BCV incubated Magical through an EIR, then co-led its seed ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/automation-for-everyone/)).\n- **What constrains him:** his record is mostly product-led SaaS and fintech from 2019 to 2022 (Alloy, Hightouch, Relay, Column Tax, Playbook). He has never been named on a frontier-lab or AI-chip round. His AI deals are applied AI or infrastructure seeds of $5–10M, Solcoa aside. The firm's lab and chip exposure sits with Slater Stich, Saanya Ojha and Merritt Hummer.\n- **What he'd never do:** chase hype. He says \"I try to avoid the latest hype\" and favours \"founders with independent conviction\" ([src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/about/)). He has also written skeptically about human-data vendors that train AI on expert labour, a contrarian view in 2026 (see below).\n- **Seniority signal:** he co-authored the Fund XI announcement (2026-09-16) with Slater Stich and Christina Melas-Kyriazi. He also wrote Alysaa Co's partner-promotion post. That places him in the firm's leadership voice ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/capital-s-duty-to-the-future/), [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-alysaa-co-our-newest-partner/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Lightning AI started with a lunch before incorporation.** \"I first met Will Falcon over lunch at a Thai restaurant\" near Facebook's NYC office ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/kevin-zhang/)). BCV \"co-led a seed financing\" in late 2019 ([src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/our-investment-in-grid/)). Index's Sarah Cannon says Index decided to join the seed \"days later\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/10/08/grid-ai-raises-18-6m-series-a-to-help-ai-researchers-and-engineers-bring-their-models-to-production/)). Index led the $18.6M-total Series A in 2020 and BCV re-invested. BCV was in at seed with Index, and Kevin's pre-incorporation lunch suggests he was the earliest contact. That he was strictly first to commit is not confirmed.\n- **Contrarian on human-data vendors.** In his Sentience memo he wrote that there is \"something Faustian in the transactions that training data companies are making\" with human experts. He argued that perpetual licences bought at hourly rates are not a fair trade ([src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/your-best-ai-assistant-is-you/)). This cuts against the 2025–26 consensus on expert-data marketplaces.\n- **His thesis against general models.** Large models are \"a composite voice and judgment of millions of people,\" so value lies in scaling one individual's judgment ([src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/your-best-ai-assistant-is-you/)). Hyde, his 2025 seed, sells the enterprise version of the same idea, \"Your model. Your weights.\" ([src](https://hyde.ai/)).\n- **Sentience credit mismatch.** His memo says the deal was \"in collaboration with my partner Ryan Kim\" ([src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/your-best-ai-assistant-is-you/)). BCV's CMS portfolio record lists only Kevin ([src](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22the-sentience-company%22%5D%7B...%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Efirstname%7D)). Ryan Kim did the work; Kevin holds the record.\n- **\"Led\" is loose in his own telling.** He calls Hightouch one of \"the first four investments I led for BCV\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/kevin-zhang/)). His own Hightouch post announces only BCV's \"participation in a $12M Series A\" ([src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/putting-data-in-the-hands-of-business-users/)). Read \"led\" as \"was BCV's partner on.\"\n- **Sourcing by cold outreach.** Alloy's founder says he \"cold emailed us right after we launched our MVP on Product Hunt,\" before they were full time ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/alloy/)). BCV then led the $5M seed ([src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/automating-the-e-commerce-stack-our-investment-in-alloy/)).\n- **Sourcing by incubation.** For Magical, BCV \"helped incubate, co-led a seed financing\" and put money into the Series A ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/automation-for-everyone/)). The incubation ran through EIR Harpaul Sambhi, who joined BCV after LinkedIn and Microsoft ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/welcome-harpaul-sambhi/)).\n- **Sourcing through scouts and seed funds.** In his Fund X note he says BCV partners \"with seed investors and scouts to extend our reach\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/)). Alysaa Co built that scout programme. Her Canadian network brought in Solcoa's founder ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-alysaa-co-our-newest-partner/)).\n- **The biggest check he is named on is not AI software.** Solcoa, a rare-earth metals company and his CMS record with Alysaa Co, announced a \"$75m seed financing\" with BCV and J.P. Morgan on 2026-09-24 ([src](https://solcoaindustries.com/)). BCV's share is undisclosed. It shows he can sit on $50M+ seed-stage syndicates, which matters for large-seed co-investing.\n- **Market read on seed prices (2023).** He said \"multiple $30 to $40 million post\" seeds were happening at top firms ([src](https://fortune.com/2023/02/28/bain-capital-ventures-two-funds-2-billion/)).\n- **Mentor to Rak Garg.** BCV Labs lead Rak Garg calls him \"a long-time mentor.\" Their link was a shared interest in product-led growth ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/get-to-know-a-bcv-associate-rak-garg/)). This is a likely internal route into BCV Labs research deals.\n- **He spotted a fast promotion.** His post says Alysaa Co reached partner in about four years. In that time she led diligence for 35 investment committees and built the scout programme ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-alysaa-co-our-newest-partner/)). She is a lead in her own right.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:**\n  - He is genuinely early. He sat at the founder lunch before incorporation (Lightning AI), incubates (Magical) and takes cold Product Hunt-stage meetings (Alloy).\n  - His 2025–26 AI deals sit next to my thesis. Hyde is a post-training and inference stack for enterprises, and Sentience builds personal models. Magical and Klutch are AI-native automation in the Serval mould.\n  - He sits at a fund that can follow at growth ($1.6B Fund XI) and joins $75M seeds (Solcoa). That makes him a useful co-investor or step-up partner for my $20M checks into large seeds.\n- **Against:**\n  - No frontier lab, pre-training or AI-chip deal is named to him.\n  - Most of the portfolio is fintech and SaaS.\n  - His contrarian stance on expert-data vendors means he would not be my co-investor in that category.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** Lightning AI (seed, 2019), Hyde (seed, 2025), Sentience (seed, 2026), Magical (incubated seed), Klutch (seed, 2025), Solcoa ($75M seed, 2026, as a large-seed comp).\n\n## Background\n- Columbia College. There he led the student entrepreneurship society. He is now a board member of the Columbia College Alumni Association and an organizer of Columbia in Tech ([src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/about/)). Degree and year were not found.\n- The BCV CMS career timeline lists Addepar (1 year), a Bain Capital-branded logo (1 year), Fundera (3 years) and BCV (10 years). The order and exact dates were not given ([src](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_id%3D%3D%22ndF450ZgZJsxSk7ku18Bya%22%5D%5B0%5D.pastExperience)).\n- At Fundera he was the first business hire and Head of Product. The company helped small businesses secure $200M+ and was acquired by NerdWallet ([src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/about/)).\n- At BCV:\n  - He co-wrote the 2019 Ike Series A memo with Ajay Agarwal ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/we-like-ike-investing-in-autonomous-freight/)).\n  - He was named among the SF early-stage partners in Aaref Hilaly's welcome post ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/welcoming-our-newest-partner-aaref-hilaly/)).\n  - Fortune called him \"a partner at BCV\" on 2023-02-28 ([src](https://fortune.com/2023/02/28/bain-capital-ventures-two-funds-2-billion/)).\n  - He co-authored the Fund XI announcement on 2026-09-16 ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/capital-s-duty-to-the-future/)).\n- **Promotion story:** the date he became partner was not found. He describes Lightning AI (seed, late 2019) as among the first investments he led, so he had lead authority by 2019, about three years after joining around 2016. That makes his promotion plausibly fast, but the dates are unsourced, so fast_promotion is set to unknown.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://baincapitalventures.com/team/kevin-zhang/ | BCV profile with Q&A |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/ | Ghost blog \"Efficiency Frontier\"; 7 posts 2020-06 to 2026-03 per [RSS](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/rss/); very low cadence |\n| X | https://x.com/kevinzhang | Not readable (fetch blocked); follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/kevinzhang711 | Not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-04 — \"Sentience x Bain Capital Ventures: Outlier Briefings\" (video), on why individual context beats general-purpose AI ([listed on](https://sentience.com/company)). No transcript was retrieved.\n- 2026-03 — BCV film \"Every AI Feels the Same, Sentience is Changing That\" (Kevin is listed as author) ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/video-every-ai-feels-the-same-sentience-is-changing-that/)).\n- No podcast appearances were found. Web search was unavailable this run, so coverage is incomplete.\n\n## How they think\n- **Product-led, user-first software (2020):** buyers have shifted from the economic buyer to the end user ([src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/the-next-20-year-cycle-in-business-software/), co-written with Ajay Agarwal).\n- **From workflow to automation:** the battlefield is moving from facilitating work to \"actually doing it.\" This is the logic behind Magical and later Klutch's agents ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/automation-for-everyone/)).\n- **Individual over composite intelligence:** he backs personal and proprietary models over rented general models (Sentience, Hyde) ([src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/your-best-ai-assistant-is-you/)).\n- **Autonomy needs constraints:** \"in autonomy, constraints are king,\" a lesson from Kiva applied to highway trucking (Ike, co-written) ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/we-like-ike-investing-in-autonomous-freight/)).\n- **Capital as a duty:** the Fund XI letter frames risk capital as a duty to the future in a \"post-AGI economy\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/capital-s-duty-to-the-future/)).\n- **Calls that went right:**\n  - Lightning AI before incorporation.\n  - Hightouch at Series A.\n  - Mintlify at seed (2022). Mintlify now serves Anthropic, Scale and Cursor docs, per his 2025 post ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/mintlify-is-built-for-builders/)).\n  - Command E (acquired by Dropbox), Column Tax and Aqueduct (both marked Acquired in the CMS).\n- **Calls that went wrong:** none admitted publicly. Ike's later outcome was not checked.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Your Best AI Assistant is You\" (2026-03-26): the Sentience memo. It sets out his anti-composite-model and anti-data-vendor views ([link](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/your-best-ai-assistant-is-you/)).\n2. \"Lightning Doesn't Strike Twice\" (2020-10-08): the Grid AI / Lightning seed and Series A memo ([link](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/our-investment-in-grid/)).\n3. \"Capital's Duty to the Future\" (2026-09-16): the Fund XI letter he co-wrote ([link](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/capital-s-duty-to-the-future/)).\n4. \"Automation for Everyone\": the Magical incubation story ([link](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/automation-for-everyone/)).\n5. \"Meet Alysaa Co, Our Newest Partner\" (2026-01-14): what he rewards in junior investors ([link](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-alysaa-co-our-newest-partner/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing confirmed. X could not be fetched. His blog cross-posts BCV investment memos.\n\n## Portfolio\nEvery row comes from the BCV CMS partner field unless noted ([CMS query](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26references(%22ndF450ZgZJsxSk7ku18Bya%22)%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Ename%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Efirstname%7D)). AI-relevant deals come first.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Lightning AI (Grid AI) | Seed | late 2019 | Co-led | Index Ventures (joined seed \"days later\") | [src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/our-investment-in-grid/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/10/08/grid-ai-raises-18-6m-series-a-to-help-ai-researchers-and-engineers-bring-their-models-to-production/) |\n| Lightning AI (Grid AI) | Series A ($18.6M total raised) | 2020-10-08 | No (Index led) | Index Ventures, firstminute | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/10/08/grid-ai-raises-18-6m-series-a-to-help-ai-researchers-and-engineers-bring-their-models-to-production/) |\n| Ike (autonomous trucking) | Series A ($52M) | 2019 (memo cites the Eisenhower convoy centennial) | Yes (BCV led; memo co-written with Ajay Agarwal) | Redpoint, Fontinalis, Neo, Basis Set | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/we-like-ike-investing-in-autonomous-freight/) |\n| Magical (AI automation) | Incubation, then seed, then Series A | 2020 (CMS) | Co-led seed | Ajay Agarwal (BCV co-partner) | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/automation-for-everyone/) |\n| Vesta (AI mortgage lending) | Seed | 2020 (CMS) | He describes it as led | not found | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/kevin-zhang/) |\n| Alloy Automation | Seed ($5M) | 2021-02-02 | Yes | Abstract Ventures, BoxGroup, Red Antler, angels | [src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/automating-the-e-commerce-stack-our-investment-in-alloy/) |\n| Hightouch | Series A ($12M) | 2021 (post 2021-07-28) | No (\"participation\") | not named | [src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/putting-data-in-the-hands-of-business-users/) |\n| Mintlify (AI docs) | Seed | 2022 (CMS) | Unknown | not found | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/mintlify-is-built-for-builders/) |\n| Silna Health (AI prior authorization) | Early | 2024 (CMS) | Unknown | Alysaa Co (BCV) | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rebuilding-trust-in-healthcare-how-silna-health-s-prior-authorization-solution-aligns-patients/) |\n| Klutch (AI agents for construction) | Seed | 2025 (CMS) | Unknown | not found | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-ai-to-the-construction-site/) |\n| Hyde (enterprise post-training and inference) | Seed | 2025 (CMS; record created 2026-06-24) | Unknown | General Catalyst (named as backer) | [src](https://hyde.ai/blog/own-your-intelligence) |\n| The Sentience Company | Seed ($6.5M) | 2026-03-26 | Yes (BCV led; with Ryan Kim) | South Park Commons, Daybreak | [src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/your-best-ai-assistant-is-you/), [src](https://sentience.com/company) |\n| Solcoa (rare-earth metals) | Seed ($75M) | 2026-09-24 | Unknown | J.P. Morgan; Alysaa Co (BCV) | [src](https://solcoaindustries.com/) |\n| Relay Financial | Series A | 2021-05-13 | Unknown | Keri Gohman (BCV) | [src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/running-with-business-owners-accountants-our-series-a-investment-in-relay/) |\n| Clockwise | Series B | 2020-06 | BCV led | Ajay Agarwal, Steven Lee (BCV) | [src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/productivity-software-in-2020/) |\n| Loyal; Hera; Lithos Carbon; Playbook; Finley; Column Tax; Aqueduct; Command E; Skip; Pallet; Forus | Seed or early, per CMS | 2020–2026 | Unknown | Christina Melas-Kyriazi (Loyal), Alysaa Co (Hera), Aaref Hilaly (Lithos) | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/kevin-zhang/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Lightning AI:**\n  - Kevin met Will Falcon, then a PhD student with LeCun and Cho who was also working at FAIR, near Facebook's NYC office, before incorporation ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/kevin-zhang/)).\n  - BCV co-led the late-2019 seed. Index's Sarah Cannon heard about Lightning at a Helsinki conference and called Bryan Offutt, who had met the founders. Index joined the seed within days ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/10/08/grid-ai-raises-18-6m-series-a-to-help-ai-researchers-and-engineers-bring-their-models-to-production/)).\n  - Index led the Series A and BCV re-invested ([src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/our-investment-in-grid/)).\n- **Sentience:**\n  - Kevin knew Sam Kececi for about two years ([src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/your-best-ai-assistant-is-you/)).\n  - Sam was demoing a pre-launch Sentience at South Park Commons NYC by 2025-10-29 ([listed on](https://sentience.com/company)).\n  - BCV led the $6.5M seed, announced 2026-03-26 with a Business Wire release and a BCV film ([src](https://sentience.com/company)).\n- **Alloy:**\n  - He sent a cold email after the Product Hunt MVP launch, while the founders were still part time and pivoting through YC.\n  - BCV then led the $5M seed ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/alloy/), [src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/automating-the-e-commerce-stack-our-investment-in-alloy/)).\n- **Magical:** BCV EIR Harpaul Sambhi became a co-founder. BCV incubated the company, co-led the seed and added money in the Series A ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/automation-for-everyone/), [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/welcome-harpaul-sambhi/)).\n- **Solcoa:** Alysaa Co's work with Canadian founders \"led us to partnerships with founders including Hooman Reza Nezhad at Solcoa\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-alysaa-co-our-newest-partner/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Will Falcon (Lightning AI) | Founder he backed before incorporation | 2019 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/kevin-zhang/) |\n| Ajay Agarwal (BCV) | Frequent co-author and co-partner (Ike, Magical, Clockwise, Airbase, 20-year cycle essay) | 2019 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/we-like-ike-investing-in-autonomous-freight/) |\n| Rak Garg (BCV) | Mentee (\"long-time mentor\") | before Rak joined BCV | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/get-to-know-a-bcv-associate-rak-garg/) |\n| Alysaa Co (BCV) | Co-partner on Solcoa, Hera and Silna; he wrote her promotion post | ~2022 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-alysaa-co-our-newest-partner/) |\n| Ryan Kim (BCV) | Co-partner on Sentience | 2026 | [src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/your-best-ai-assistant-is-you/) |\n| Slater Stich, Christina Melas-Kyriazi (BCV) | Co-authors of the Fund XI letter | 2026-09 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/capital-s-duty-to-the-future/) |\n| Sam Kececi (Sentience) | Founder; known about 2 years before the seed | ~2024 | [src](https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/your-best-ai-assistant-is-you/) |\n| Anirudh Sanga (Hyde) | CEO of a portfolio company | 2025 | [src](https://hyde.ai/blog/own-your-intelligence) |\n| Index Ventures (Sarah Cannon, Bryan Offutt) | Co-investors in the Lightning seed; Index led the Series A | 2019 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/10/08/grid-ai-raises-18-6m-series-a-to-help-ai-researchers-and-engineers-bring-their-models-to-production/) |\n| General Catalyst | Co-backer of Hyde | 2025 | [src](https://hyde.ai/blog/own-your-intelligence) |\n| South Park Commons | Co-investor in the Sentience seed | 2026 | [src](https://sentience.com/company) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Hyde's seed: size, date, whether BCV or General Catalyst led, and who introduced it. The CMS record appeared only in 2026-06, though it is dated 2025.\n- His promotion date to Partner and his joining title. LinkedIn was not fetched.\n- Whether BCV kept investing in Lightning AI after the Series A (the 2022 Series B and later).\n- The \"Outlier Briefings\" video transcript, for first-person quotes on personal AI.\n- His X activity (blocked) and any podcasts. Web search was unavailable this run.\n- Ike's outcome, and whether he took a board seat.\n\n## Sources\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/team/kevin-zhang/\n- BCV CMS (Sanity) team and portfolio records: https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production\n- https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/about/ and https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/rss/\n- https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/your-best-ai-assistant-is-you/\n- https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/our-investment-in-grid/\n- https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/automating-the-e-commerce-stack-our-investment-in-alloy/\n- https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/putting-data-in-the-hands-of-business-users/\n- https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/running-with-business-owners-accountants-our-series-a-investment-in-relay/\n- https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/the-next-20-year-cycle-in-business-software/\n- https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/productivity-software-in-2020/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/capital-s-duty-to-the-future/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-alysaa-co-our-newest-partner/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/automation-for-everyone/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/welcome-harpaul-sambhi/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/alloy/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/we-like-ike-investing-in-autonomous-freight/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/get-to-know-a-bcv-associate-rak-garg/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/welcoming-our-newest-partner-aaref-hilaly/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-ai-to-the-construction-site/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/mintlify-is-built-for-builders/\n- https://fortune.com/2023/02/28/bain-capital-ventures-two-funds-2-billion/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2020/10/08/grid-ai-raises-18-6m-series-a-to-help-ai-researchers-and-engineers-bring-their-models-to-production/\n- https://hyde.ai/ and https://hyde.ai/blog/own-your-intelligence\n- https://sentience.com/company\n- https://solcoaindustries.com/\n", "person:kimberly-tan": "\n# Kimberly Tan\n\n## One-line take\nShe is a16z's sub-GP apps investor who creates companies rather than finding them: Decagon's founders met at an a16z retreat and she wrote the first check at inception ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-decagon/)). She now runs the firm's \"AI replaces RPA and BPO\" thesis through Sola, Petual and Meridian. (Pronoun from the a16z bio.)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** a16z can put founders in a room before there is a company. Decagon's founders met at an a16z-hosted retreat, and Tan \"introduced the founders to each other and led the round at company inception\" ([src](https://a16z.com/author/kimberly-tan/)). That is company creation, not deal flow. It produced a $4.5B company by January 2026 ([src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-d-announcement)).\n- **What she backs:** AI that takes labor budgets in back-office and outsourced work: support (Decagon), process automation (Sola), audit (Petual) and AI-enabled services firms (Pantheon). Her screen is a use case with \"exceptionally clear ROI\" ([src](https://a16z.com/unbundling-the-bpo-how-ai-will-disrupt-outsourced-work/)).\n- **What constrains her:** she is an Investing Partner, not a GP. Her posts are often co-signed (Jeff Silverstein on Decagon, Jennifer Li on Sola, Brian Roberts and James da Costa on Petual), and on Petual the press names GP Brian Roberts, not Tan, as the board member ([src](https://regtechanalyst.com/?p=85555)), and the big Decagon follow-on (the $1.5B C co-lead) is a16z's, with no source naming her as its lead. At Decagon a16z did not hold its line through the middle rounds: Accel led the A and BCV led the B, with a16z not named on the B ([src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-a), [src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-b)).\n- **What she'd never do:** nothing in her record touches model labs, pre-training, chips or compute. She is an application-layer investor.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Decagon was built at an a16z retreat.** The founders \"met at an a16z retreat we hosted last year\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-decagon/), 2024-06-18). Her bio adds that she introduced them ([src](https://a16z.com/author/kimberly-tan/)). Other investor bios (Sarah Wang's) list Decagon without saying who originated it.\n- **First money, about 11 months ahead of Accel.** a16z wrote \"the first check into Decagon at inception\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-decagon/)). The a16z-recorded initial funding date is 2023-07-28, and Accel's $30M A was announced 2024-06-18 alongside the $5M seed ([src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-a)).\n- **a16z lost the A and B, then came back to co-lead the C.** The June 2024 seed post said a16z planned to deepen its commitment, but Accel led the A, BCV led the $65M B (a16z not named), and only the $131M C at $1.5B was \"Co-led by returning investors Accel and Andreessen Horowitz\" ([src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-c-announcement), 2025-06-22). The seed lead doubled down only at the third priced round.\n- **She saw the idea search before the product.** \"We saw them go through the idea maze.\" She also said many expected Decagon to be \"just going to be a GPT wrapper\" ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/can-ai-agents-finally-fix-customer-support/), 2024-12-18).\n- **Pantheon is a personal lead missing from her bio list.** \"I'm thrilled to have led their most recent round and to join their board.\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-pantheon/), 2024-10-22). The bio's company list (Pave, Petual, Meridian, Saronic, Sola, Supermove, Vesta) leaves it out, so bios undercount her deals.\n- **She backs AI services firms, not only software.** \"Pantheon is an AI-enabled architectural firm\" that sells finished designs, with architects checking output ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-pantheon/)). That matches her BPO piece, which lists full-stack outcome firms (Foundation, Long Lake) next to software plays ([src](https://a16z.com/unbundling-the-bpo-how-ai-will-disrupt-outsourced-work/)).\n- **The BPO moat is the business model.** She sees \"a fundamental business model mismatch between building AI-native products and running a BPO\", because BPOs bill time and materials ([src](https://a16z.com/unbundling-the-bpo-how-ai-will-disrupt-outsourced-work/), 2025-02-13).\n- **Second money on Sola.** Conviction (Sarah Guo) led Sola's $3.5M seed, and Tan led the $17.5M A with Jennifer Li ([src](https://www.sola.ai/series-a-announcement)). Customers called it \"the first AI product where they saw immediate ROI\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sola/)).\n- **She is publicly bullish on enterprise adoption.** On the MIT \"pilots fail\" figure: \"we find that statistic hard to believe\" ([src](https://a16z.com/where-enterprises-are-actually-adopting-ai/), 2026-04-08).\n\n- **(Added 2026-11-10, second pass) She leads where she already knows the founders.** On Pantheon: \"I've personally known both David and Tiger for years now.\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-pantheon/), 2024-10-22). With Decagon (she introduced the founders), both personal seed-stage leads predate the company. Sola, where she came in at the A behind Conviction, is the exception.\n- **Petual: a16z led, but the board seat is Brian Roberts's.** The a16z post she first-signed only says \"we're excited to announce our investment in Petual\" and never says led or board ([src](https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-petual)). Press reports a $20M seed led by a16z with First Round Capital, Cowboy Ventures and Elad Gil ([src](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/petual-raises-20-million-in-funding)), and names GP Brian Roberts as the a16z board member, quoting him on a company \"able to reach large enterprise customers so early in their journey\" ([src](https://regtechanalyst.com/?p=85555), 2026-04-23). Her bio lists Petual and her NFX Signal profile claims lead plus board ([src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/kimberly-tan), aggregator). A traded.co headline says the $20M spans a pre-seed led by First Round and a seed led by a16z; that page would not load, so \"a16z was second money\" is low confidence.\n- **\"Meridian\" in her bio is the AI financial-modelling startup.** Meridian (Longitude Labs) raised a $17M seed at $100M post, \"led by Andreessen Horowitz and The General Partnership\", on 2026-02-11 ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=730753)), with QED, FPV and Litquidity Ventures ([src](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/meridian-raises-17-million-seed-round)). Decagon, her seed company, was already a Meridian customer at launch (same SiliconANGLE piece). No source names her on the round. This is not the construction-AI Meridian that Noetive bought.\n- **Pantheon may have pivoted out of her thesis.** The old domain (withpantheon.ai) no longer resolves, and pantheon.inc now describes \"a research lab focused on robot intelligence and data\" ([src](https://pantheon.inc), checked 2026-11-10; footer \"Pantheon Industries, Inc.\"). A recruiting-page snippet names the same founders (David Mace, Tiger Sun) at pantheon.inc; I could not open that page, so the link is unconfirmed. If true, her AI-services-firm bet became a robotics data lab within two years. Press snippets put the 2024 round at $25M led by a16z with Felicis and 8VC ([src](https://finsmes.com/?p=338072), 403 on fetch); vcbacked instead shows a $12.6M seed in July 2024 ([src](https://www.vcbacked.co/company/pantheon-ai), aggregator).\n- **On pricing:** \"seat-based doesn't actually make as much sense going forward.\" ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/can-ai-agents-finally-fix-customer-support/), 2024-12-18).\n- **Check size (aggregator only):** NFX Signal lists a $10M sweet spot in a $10K to $100M range, and credits her with Prepared (board observer) and Saronic ([src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/kimberly-tan)). Treat as self-reported.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High (adjacent).** She is a principal-level partner at a fund that can write $10M+ checks, and she leads seed and A rounds in AI-native automation, the Serval-like category in `THESIS.md`. Decagon (seed at inception), Sola (A), Petual ($20M seed, 2026) and Meridian ($17M seed, 2026) are the deals that matter. Her seeds are small ($5M to $20M rounds), so she is a co-investor for automation seeds, not for $600M lab seeds. She is not a source for frontier labs, pre-training, chips or compute. Her edge is company creation through a16z founder networks, which makes her worth knowing for automation co-investment and founder intros, not for lab rounds.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford University, graduated with Distinction and Phi Beta Kappa. As a student she was a representative and full voting member of the Stanford Board of Trustees finance committee ([src](https://a16z.com/author/kimberly-tan/)).\n- Joined a16z in 2019 on the enterprise team ([src](https://a16z.com/author/kimberly-tan/)). Her earliest a16z writing is on SaaS efficiency and fintech in vertical SaaS, with Kristina Shen, Seema Amble and Angela Strange ([src](https://a16z.com/fintech-scales-vertical-saas/)).\n- Jobs before a16z (aggregator profiles only, no dates): McKinsey & Company (consulting), World Bank Group treasury (finance and markets), Goldman Sachs (investment banking), Internews (fellowship) ([src](https://whisperlist.draftboard.com/vc/377a5bcd-c534-47af-9153-19522ef6f17b/kimberly-tan-andreessen-horowitz), [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/kimberly-tan)). LinkedIn not fetched.\n- Based in San Francisco ([src](https://a16z.com/author/kimberly-tan/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/kimberly-tan/ | a16z author page; 26+ pieces (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes on a16z.com |\n| X | https://x.com/kimberlywtan | Linked from a16z bio; page not loadable (HTTP 402), so follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://linkedin.com/in/kimberlywtan/ | Linked from a16z bio; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://a16z.com/podcast/how-enterprise-ai-really-gets-deployed/ | Co-hosts a16z show episodes with portfolio founders (not her own show) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-12 — a16z — 2024 Big Ideas: developer influence, maritime upgrades, video intelligence — https://a16z.com/podcast/2024-big-ideas-developer-influence-maritime-upgrades-and-new-video-intelligence/ — contributor; the page does not say which idea was hers.\n- 2024-12 — a16z — Can AI Agents Finally Fix Customer Support? (with Jesse Zhang) — https://a16z.com/podcast/can-ai-agents-finally-fix-customer-support/ — \"We saw them go through the idea maze.\"; the early \"GPT wrapper\" skepticism.\n- 2025-01-22 — a16z — RIP to RPA: How AI Makes Operations Work (with Steph Smith) — https://a16z.com/podcast/rip-to-rpa-how-ai-makes-operations-work/ — no transcript on page; summary covers referral-management examples.\n- 2025 — a16z — Agents, Lawyers, and LLMs (with Aatish Nayak) — https://a16z.com/podcast/agents-lawyers-and-llms/ — not transcribed here.\n- 2025 — a16z — Unbundling the BPO — https://a16z.com/podcast/unbundling-the-bpo-how-ai-is-disrupting-outsourced-work/ — audio version of the BPO essay.\n- 2025 — OpenAI DevDay: Building AI for the Enterprise with Decagon and Clay — https://a16z.com/openai-devday-building-ai-for-the-enterprise-with-decagon-and-clay/ — not read.\n- 2026-03-20 — a16z — How AI Is Unbundling the $300 Billion BPO Industry — https://a16z.com/podcast/how-ai-is-unbundling-the-300-billion-bpo-industry/ — solo talk; per the page summary she covers voice and browser agents and the need for clear KPIs (summary only, no transcript).\n- 2026-07-31 — a16z — How Enterprise AI Really Gets Deployed (with Sarah Wang, Jesse Zhang, Ashwin Sreenivas) — https://a16z.com/podcast/how-enterprise-ai-really-gets-deployed/ — no transcript on page.\n\n- 2026-09 — EO (YouTube) — The Real Differentiator an a16z Partner Looks For — repost: https://startup.whatfinger.com/2026/09/03/the-real-differentiator-an-a16z-partner-looks-for-kimberly-tan-a16z-investing-partner/ — summary only; chapter titles are \"Build Your Moat at the Customer's Desk\", \"Don't Sell AI, Sell Proven ROI\", \"Know Where Automation Should Stop\". Original video URL not found; worth transcribing.\n\n## How they think\n- **Main theses:** LLMs finally make RPA's original vision possible ([src](https://a16z.com/rip-to-rpa-the-rise-of-intelligent-automation/), 2024-11-13). AI unbundles the BPO industry, category by category (support, freight audit, healthcare RCM) ([src](https://a16z.com/unbundling-the-bpo-how-ai-will-disrupt-outsourced-work/)). AI apps sell into labor budgets ([src](https://a16z.com/insights-for-enterprise-ai-builders/)).\n- **Contrarian views:** incumbent BPOs are structurally unable to adopt AI, and enterprise adoption is real despite the MIT study ([src](https://a16z.com/where-enterprises-are-actually-adopting-ai/)).\n- **Pricing:** per-seat pricing does not fit agents; she pushes outcome or usage pricing ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/can-ai-agents-finally-fix-customer-support/)).\n- **Calls right:** Decagon, from seed at inception to $4.5B in about 2.5 years. Calls wrong: nothing she has admitted. Pantheon may no longer be the architecture firm she backed (see above, unconfirmed). One gap: a16z was not named on Decagon's A or B.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Investing in Decagon (2024-06-18) — https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-decagon/ — the retreat origin and \"first check\" claim.\n2. Unbundling the BPO (2025-02-13) — https://a16z.com/unbundling-the-bpo-how-ai-will-disrupt-outsourced-work/ — her market map and ROI screen.\n3. RIP to RPA (2024-11-13) — https://a16z.com/rip-to-rpa-the-rise-of-intelligent-automation/ — the automation thesis behind Sola.\n4. Investing in Pantheon (2024-10-22) — https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-pantheon/ — the AI-services-firm model.\n5. Where Enterprises are Actually Adopting AI (2026-04-08) — https://a16z.com/where-enterprises-are-actually-adopting-ai/ — her current read on demand.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: X returned HTTP 402 and could not be read. On a16z she mostly co-produces content with portfolio founders (Decagon, Prepared's Michael Chime) and apps-team colleagues (Joe Schmidt, Marc Andrusko, Olivia Moore, Seema Amble).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Decagon | Seed ($5M) | 2023-07-28 (a16z initial); announced 2024-06-18 | yes, first check; board | A*, Elad Gil (around seed/A); Accel led $30M A | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-decagon/), [src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-a) |\n| Decagon | Series C ($131M at $1.5B) | 2025-06-22 | co-led (a16z with Accel); her personal role not stated | A*, BCV, BOND, Avra, Forerunner, Ribbit | [src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-c-announcement) |\n| Decagon | Series D ($250M at $4.5B) | 2026-01-27 | no (Coatue, Index) | A*, Accel, BCV, Elad Gil, Forerunner, Ribbit, T.Capital | [src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-d-announcement) |\n| Pantheon | Seed (reported $25M; aggregator says $12.6M seed 2024-07) | 2024-10-22 | yes; board | Felicis, 8VC (press snippet and aggregator) | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-pantheon/), [src](https://www.vcbacked.co/company/pantheon-ai) |\n| Sola | Series A ($17.5M) | 2025-08-14 | yes (with Jennifer Li); board | Conviction (seed lead), Y Combinator | [src](https://www.sola.ai/series-a-announcement) |\n| Petual | Seed ($20M) | 2026-04-23 | a16z led; lead partner unclear (Brian Roberts holds the board seat per press; her NFX profile claims lead and board) | First Round Capital, Cowboy Ventures, Elad Gil | [src](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/petual-raises-20-million-in-funding), [src](https://regtechanalyst.com/?p=85555) |\n| Meridian (Longitude Labs) | Seed ($17M at $100M post) | 2026-02-11 | a16z co-led with The General Partnership; her role not stated (bio list only) | QED Investors, FPV Ventures, Litquidity Ventures | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=730753), [src](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/meridian-raises-17-million-seed-round) |\n| Prepared | Series A | 2023-11-01 | no (David Ulevitch led); board observer per aggregators only | n/a | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-prepared/), [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/kimberly-tan) |\n| Saronic Technologies | Undisclosed | n/a | not stated; bio says \"invested in and/or\" board, and the a16z posts are Katherine Boyle's | n/a | [src](https://a16z.com/author/kimberly-tan/), [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-saronic/) |\n| Mem | n/a | n/a | aggregator list only (AI notes app) | n/a | [src](https://whisperlist.draftboard.com/vc/377a5bcd-c534-47af-9153-19522ef6f17b/kimberly-tan-andreessen-horowitz) |\n| Pave, Supermove, Vesta | n/a | n/a | bio list only; non-AI (comp software, moving software, mortgage software) | n/a | [src](https://a16z.com/author/kimberly-tan/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Decagon:** Jesse Zhang and Ashwin Sreenivas, both repeat founders, met at an a16z-hosted retreat. Tan introduced them and a16z wrote the first check at inception ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-decagon/), [src](https://a16z.com/author/kimberly-tan/)). Other records in this repo say Kevin Hartz (A*) reached the deal through an angel check in Sreenivas's earlier company Helia, so A* had its own route in. Accel (Ivan Zhou) led the A and BCV (Aaref Hilaly) led the B. a16z re-entered as co-lead of the C, and Tan co-hosts Decagon content with Sarah Wang.\n- **Sola:** Conviction seeded it. Tan and Jennifer Li led the $17.5M A, citing 5x year-to-date revenue growth and customers' immediate ROI ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sola/), [src](https://www.sola.ai/series-a-announcement)).\n- **Pantheon:** She led the most recent round and joined the board of an AI-enabled architecture firm founded by David Mace (sold SwiftScale Biologics to Resilience) and Tiger Sun (ex-Scale engineer), both of whom she had known for years ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-pantheon/)). The company now appears to be a robot-data lab at pantheon.inc (unconfirmed).\n- **Meridian:** a16z co-led the $17M seed with The General Partnership in February 2026; the company had signed $5M of contracts in December 2025 and counted Decagon as a customer ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=730753)). How the deal reached Tan is not public; the Decagon customer link is the obvious path but is my inference.\n- **Petual:** She co-wrote the post with Brian Roberts (a16z GP) and James da Costa (a16z partner). Founder Snir Kodesh led engineering at Retool and held senior engineering roles at Lyft; a16z led the $20M seed with First Round, Cowboy and Elad Gil, and Roberts took the board seat ([src](https://regtechanalyst.com/?p=85555)). The company does AI for internal audit and SOX testing, and the post argues \"SOX is just the beginning.\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-petual/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Andreessen Horowitz | Investing Partner, Enterprise | 2019 | [src](https://a16z.com/author/kimberly-tan/) |\n| Jesse Zhang, Ashwin Sreenivas | Introduced them; seed lead; board | 2023 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-decagon/) |\n| Jeff Silverstein | Co-author of Decagon seed post | 2024 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-decagon/) |\n| Sarah Wang | Co-host of Decagon podcast; also lists Decagon in her bio | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/how-enterprise-ai-really-gets-deployed/) |\n| Jennifer Li | Co-lead partner on Sola A | 2025 | [src](https://www.sola.ai/series-a-announcement) |\n| Sarah Guo (Conviction) | Seed lead whose deal Tan followed with the A | 2025 | [src](https://www.sola.ai/series-a-announcement) |\n| Brian Roberts (a16z GP), James da Costa | Co-authors on Petual; Roberts holds the board seat | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-petual/), [src](https://regtechanalyst.com/?p=85555) |\n| David Mace, Tiger Sun | Known \"for years\" before she led Pantheon | 2024 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-pantheon/) |\n| Elad Gil | Co-investor in Decagon and Petual | 2024 | [src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-a), [src](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/petual-raises-20-million-in-funding) |\n| The General Partnership | a16z's co-lead on Meridian seed | 2026 | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=730753) |\n| Michael Chime (Prepared) | Co-author; Prepared was David Ulevitch's Series A | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/can-ai-help-save-lives/), [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-prepared/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did a16z take pro-rata in Decagon's A and B? The company posts don't name a16z on either. Who at a16z led the C?\n- Pantheon: confirm the pivot to robotics (pantheon.inc) and whether she is still on the board; confirm $25M vs $12.6M.\n- Petual: did First Round lead a pre-seed before a16z? Is Tan a board member or observer alongside Brian Roberts?\n- Meridian: which a16z partner led, and did the deal come through Decagon?\n- What is her actual role at Saronic and Prepared (aggregators say observer)?\n- Find and transcribe the EO video (Sept 2026).\n- Promotion dates: her title before Investing Partner, and whether she is on a GP track.\n- Transcripts of the BPO and RPA podcasts for more verbatim rules of thumb. Search budget ran out in this run, and X was not readable.\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/kimberly-tan/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-decagon/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sola/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-petual/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-pantheon/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-saronic/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-prepared/\n- https://a16z.com/rip-to-rpa-the-rise-of-intelligent-automation/\n- https://a16z.com/unbundling-the-bpo-how-ai-will-disrupt-outsourced-work/\n- https://a16z.com/where-enterprises-are-actually-adopting-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/insights-for-enterprise-ai-builders/\n- https://a16z.com/can-ai-help-save-lives/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/can-ai-agents-finally-fix-customer-support/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/how-enterprise-ai-really-gets-deployed/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/how-ai-is-unbundling-the-300-billion-bpo-industry/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/2024-big-ideas-developer-influence-maritime-upgrades-and-new-video-intelligence/\n- https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-petual\n- https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/petual-raises-20-million-in-funding\n- https://regtechanalyst.com/?p=85555\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=730753\n- https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/meridian-raises-17-million-seed-round\n- https://pantheon.inc\n- https://www.vcbacked.co/company/pantheon-ai (aggregator)\n- https://finsmes.com/?p=338072 (403; search snippet only)\n- https://signal.nfx.com/investors/kimberly-tan (aggregator)\n- https://whisperlist.draftboard.com/vc/377a5bcd-c534-47af-9153-19522ef6f17b/kimberly-tan-andreessen-horowitz (aggregator)\n- https://startup.whatfinger.com/2026/09/03/the-real-differentiator-an-a16z-partner-looks-for-kimberly-tan-a16z-investing-partner/ (summary)\n- https://decagon.ai/blog/series-a\n- https://decagon.ai/blog/series-b\n- https://decagon.ai/blog/series-c-announcement\n- https://decagon.ai/blog/series-d-announcement\n- https://www.sola.ai/series-a-announcement\n", "person:kiran-kazmi": "\n# Kiran Kazmi\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. The pools of money, the investment committee and the OpenAI mechanics are in [firms/softbank-group.md](../firms/softbank-group.md) and [firms/softbank-vision-fund-2.md](../firms/softbank-vision-fund-2.md). The round-by-round ledger is in [sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md](../sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md). The senior people on the same deals are [Vikas J. Parekh](vikas-parekh.md) and [Karol Niewiadomski](karol-niewiadomski.md). Pronouns: no source uses one, so this page uses the name.*\n\nknown_for: SoftBank director under Vikas Parekh, listed on the OpenAI, Arm and WeWork teams; covers chips and foundation models; public only since about January 2026.\n\n## One-line take\nThe most junior named person on SoftBank's two largest positions, but the evidence says \"assigned to watch them\", not \"did the deals\": Kazmi was added to all three pages more than a year after the first OpenAI check and after the Arm stake had been sold.\n\n## Crux\nKazmi is a private-equity-trained investor (Citigroup, then BC Partners) who joined SoftBank's parent-side international unit in New York in 2020 and now works as Vikas Parekh's director on the parent's strategic holdings. The edge is proximity: OpenAI and Arm are where SoftBank's money and attention are, and Kazmi states a focus on chips and foundation models. The constraint is total: nothing on these positions is Kazmi's to source, price or approve. OpenAI is Masayoshi Son's deal, and the fund's manager makes the final decisions. Nothing found suggests Kazmi does seed or Series A rounds.\n\n## The six method questions, for this person\n\n**1. Which pool Kazmi invests from.**\n- *Hired into the parent side.* The bio says \"Kiran joined SoftBank Group International’s New York office in 2020\" ([team page](https://visionfund.com/team/kiran-kazmi)). SoftBank Group International is not the fund manager. SoftBank's own description: \"SBGI is responsible for all SoftBank Group Corp. activities outside of Japan\" ([SBG, 2022-06-22](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20220622)). Whether Kazmi's employer later changed to SoftBank Investment Advisers is unconfirmed; an aggregator names SB Investment Advisers (UK) ([MarketScreener](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/KIRAN-KAZMI-A2CWAY/)).\n- *Vision Fund 2* (the parent's money plus Son's co-investment): OpenAI and WeWork, both labelled SVF2 on the fund site ([OpenAI page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai), [WeWork page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/wework)).\n- *Vision Fund 1* (outside LPs): the Arm stake, labelled SVF1 and marked \"fully divested\" ([Arm page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/arm)). The parent bought that stake from the fund in 2023-08 (ledger), so in practice Arm is now a parent holding.\n\n**2. What Kazmi signs, sources or prices.** Nothing, on the evidence. No release, filing or press report read names Kazmi on any negotiation. A Symbotic proxy says the fund's manager is \"exclusively responsible for making final decisions\" (quoted in [Parekh's profile](vikas-parekh.md) from the [2026-01-16 proxy](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837240/000119312526015381/sym-20260116.htm); not re-read for this page). Parent deals go to the SoftBank Group investment committee and Son ([firm page](../firms/softbank-group.md)). A director's work at this level is diligence, modelling, monitoring and preparing papers; that description is inference, not sourced.\n\n**3. The strategic tie.** Kazmi's three positions are all ones where SoftBank is more than a shareholder: OpenAI (Stargate and the Japan joint venture, see firm page), Arm (majority-owned subsidiary) and WeWork (rescued and restructured by SoftBank). None is a plain venture round. Kazmi's own part in any of the commercial arrangements is not documented.\n\n**4. Deal flow and terms.** No lead, no priced round and no re-up is attributed to Kazmi. The stated focus is \"infrastructure and platform investments across semiconductors and foundational AI models and agents\" ([team page](https://visionfund.com/team/kiran-kazmi)). At SoftBank that means late-stage and control deals.\n\n**5. AI book against SoftBank's products.**\n\n| Company | Pool | How it relates to SoftBank | Kazmi's documented role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | SVF2 | Partner and customer (Stargate, SB OpenAI Japan) | Page team, added between 2025-10-10 and 2026-01-24 |\n| Arm | SVF1 stake, divested; parent majority-owned | Core subsidiary | Page team, added between 2025-11-07 and 2026-01-24 |\n| WeWork | SVF2 | Turnaround; not AI | Page team, added between 2025-12-13 and 2026-04-20 |\n\n**6. For the owner.** Not a co-investor, not a price-setter, not an LP. Kazmi is a possible working-level contact inside the team that watches OpenAI and Arm, and a name to re-check in two to three years. The person to know on this team is still Parekh.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Hired by the parent, not the fund.** \"Kiran joined SoftBank Group International’s New York office in 2020\" ([team page](https://visionfund.com/team/kiran-kazmi)). Parekh also carries an SBGI title in a 2026 SEC filing (his profile). The OpenAI \"Vision Fund team\" is a parent-side group shown on the fund's site.\n- **The public listing can be dated to within four weeks, and it brackets the OpenAI close.** The archived team list of 2025-12-13 carries Kazmi's entry in the page data but switched off: `\"slug\":\"kiran-kazmi\",\"published_to_prod\":false` ([archive](https://web.archive.org/web/20251213002939/https://visionfund.com/team)). It is absent on [2025-11-06](https://web.archive.org/web/20251106234343/https://visionfund.com/team) and live on [2026-01-09](https://web.archive.org/web/20260109143705/https://visionfund.com/team). SoftBank's $22.5B second close into OpenAI was 2025-12-26 (ledger). Whether the listing marks a promotion to Director is **unconfirmed**.\n- **Not on the public OpenAI team for the entry.** The [2025-10-10 archive](https://web.archive.org/web/20251010155717/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai) lists Niewiadomski and Parekh only; the [2026-01-24 archive](https://web.archive.org/web/20260124121850/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai) adds \"Kiran Kazmi | Director, Americas\". That is about 14 months after the first $500M check.\n- **Added to Arm after the fund had sold it.** The Arm page says \"Our investment in Arm has now been fully divested.\" ([page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/arm)), and listed Parekh alone on [2025-11-07](https://web.archive.org/web/20251107004115/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/arm). A page team is a coverage map, not a deal credit. This applies to every SoftBank person whose deals in this repo come from page teams.\n- **The book is Parekh's signature work plus OpenAI.** GCV credited Parekh in 2021 with \"leading workspace operator WeWork’s restructuring efforts and the buyout of semiconductor designer Arm\" ([GCV, 2021-11-23](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sbia-scores-four-managing-partners/?format=md)). Kazmi's three pages are those two plus OpenAI, and Parekh is on all three.\n- **A cohort, not a single elevation.** Four other Americas Directors (Aaron Wong, Alex Fortmuller, Jessica Gao, Kaz Yoshimaru) first appear on the team list between 2025-11-06 and 2025-12-13; Kazmi follows by 2026-01-09. Director is the lowest tier the site lists. Cause (promotion round or a decision to show more of the team) unconfirmed.\n- **No reliable title history.** The only dated source is an aggregator that contradicts itself: \"currently an Investment Director at SB Investment Advisers (UK) Ltd. since 2020\" in the summary, but \"Private Equity Investor\" from 2019-12-31 in its list ([MarketScreener](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/KIRAN-KAZMI-A2CWAY/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** Kazmi works growth-stage and control positions at a captive fund that writes no seed checks, and has no decision rights. The rising-junior case fails on evidence: no promotion can be dated, and no deal can be attributed. What keeps it from \"skip\": the stated coverage (chips, foundation models, agents) matches the lens exactly, and Kazmi sits on the team that manages SoftBank's OpenAI and Arm positions. Useful as a working-level read on how SoftBank thinks about those, and as a later-round contact for a lab or chip company that wants SoftBank in a growth round.\n\n## Background\n- BS in Economics, The Wharton School, University of Pennsylvania (year not stated) ([team page](https://visionfund.com/team/kiran-kazmi)).\n- Investment banking at Citigroup (dates not found) (same).\n- Private equity at BC Partners (same). Dates are aggregator-only: about 2016 to 2018 or 2019 ([MarketScreener](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/KIRAN-KAZMI-A2CWAY/)); unconfirmed.\n- SoftBank Group International, New York, from 2020 (team page). Title at joining not stated.\n- Director, Americas, on the Vision Fund site by 2026-01-09 (archives above).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [visionfund.com/team/kiran-kazmi](https://visionfund.com/team/kiran-kazmi) | Fetched 2026-11-10; bio, five-question Q&A, three portfolio companies |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Not searched by handle; no account surfaced in web search |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | Search did not return a profile; do not guess a URL |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The fund-site Q&A is the only first-person material.\n\n## How they think\nAll from the fund-site Q&A ([team page](https://visionfund.com/team/kiran-kazmi)); it is marketing copy, so weigh it lightly.\n- Left private equity to move from optimising older businesses to backing new technology companies.\n- Credits Son for the attraction: \"Masa’s willingness to back bold founders and visionary ideas\".\n- Focus: \"infrastructure and platform investments across semiconductors and foundational AI models and agents\".\n- To founders: \"candid, constructive conversations that push us forward together\".\n- Contrarian views, and calls right or wrong: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Kazmi's team page](https://visionfund.com/team/kiran-kazmi): the only first-person source.\n2. [Parekh's profile](vikas-parekh.md): the decision path above Kazmi.\n3. [SoftBank AI ledger](../sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md): what the OpenAI tranches were and when.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public posting located.\n\n## Portfolio\nPage-team listings only; no deal role is stated for any of them.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | Growth ($122B round; three $10B SoftBank tranches); first funding event after Kazmi was listed | 2026-03-31 | SoftBank co-led; Kazmi's role not stated | Amazon, NVIDIA, a16z, D. E. Shaw | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai) |\n| Arm | Undisclosed (coverage of a divested SVF1 stake) | listed by 2026-01-24 | no | n/a | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/arm) |\n| WeWork | Undisclosed (coverage of an SVF2 turnaround position) | listed by 2026-04-20 | no | n/a | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/wework) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. No account of Kazmi's part in any transaction exists in the sources read. The OpenAI story (Son's pursuit, tranches, bridge loans) is in the ledger and names only Son and Goto.\n\n## How a founder or co-investor reaches Kazmi\nNo public channel was verified. Sourced routes: (1) through Parekh or Niewiadomski, who share the OpenAI page; (2) the Wharton and BC Partners networks; (3) SoftBank's New York office. A cold approach about a seed round would be off-mandate.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Vikas J. Parekh | Managing Partner on all three of Kazmi's pages; reporting line inferred | not stated | [team page](https://visionfund.com/team/kiran-kazmi) |\n| Karol Niewiadomski | Partner on the OpenAI page team | by 2026-01 | [archive](https://web.archive.org/web/20260124121850/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai) |\n| SoftBank Group International | Joined its New York office | 2020 | [team page](https://visionfund.com/team/kiran-kazmi) |\n| BC Partners | Former employer (private equity) | about 2016 (aggregator) | [MarketScreener](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/KIRAN-KAZMI-A2CWAY/) |\n| Citigroup | Former employer (investment banking) | not found | [team page](https://visionfund.com/team/kiran-kazmi) |\n| Aaron Wong, Alex Fortmuller, Jessica Gao, Kaz Yoshimaru | Same late-2025 cohort of Americas Directors on the team list | 2025-11 to 2026-01 | [archive](https://web.archive.org/web/20251213002939/https://visionfund.com/team) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did Kazmi get the Director title, and from what? LinkedIn would settle it; not retrievable here.\n- Was Kazmi on the WeWork work in 2020 to 2023 at SBGI? Joining SBGI New York in 2020 fits, but nothing confirms it.\n- Does \"semiconductors\" mean Ampere, Graphcore and the Arm margin loans (parent deals) or fund investments such as SambaNova? Kazmi is not on the SambaNova page.\n- Did the late-2025 cohort reflect promotions? Check whether any of the five were on earlier pages under another title.\n\n## Sources\n- [Vision Fund team page, Kiran Kazmi](https://visionfund.com/team/kiran-kazmi) (fetched 2026-11-10); photo checked, HTTP 200 image/png\n- Fund-site company pages: [OpenAI](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai), [Arm](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/arm), [WeWork](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/wework)\n- Wayback Machine, team list: [2025-11-06](https://web.archive.org/web/20251106234343/https://visionfund.com/team), [2025-12-13](https://web.archive.org/web/20251213002939/https://visionfund.com/team), [2026-01-09](https://web.archive.org/web/20260109143705/https://visionfund.com/team)\n- Wayback Machine, company pages: OpenAI [2025-10-10](https://web.archive.org/web/20251010155717/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai) and [2026-01-24](https://web.archive.org/web/20260124121850/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai); Arm [2025-11-07](https://web.archive.org/web/20251107004115/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/arm) and [2026-01-24](https://web.archive.org/web/20260124122422/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/arm); WeWork [2025-12-13](https://web.archive.org/web/20251213011043/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/wework) and [2026-04-20](https://web.archive.org/web/20260420093938/https://visionfund.com/portfolio/wework)\n- [SoftBank Group, 2022-06-22](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20220622) (what SBGI is)\n- [Global Corporate Venturing, 2021-11-23](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sbia-scores-four-managing-partners/?format=md) (Parekh's credits)\n- [MarketScreener](https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/KIRAN-KAZMI-A2CWAY/) (aggregator; internally inconsistent)\n", "person:koko-xs": "\n# Koko Xs\n\n## One-line take\nKoko Xs is a 22–23-year-old manager (21 in December 2024, per [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc)) who writes the most explicit public playbook I've found for \"talent investing\": back people before there is a company or a round. Behind that playbook sit billionaire LPs who also show up personally in frontier-lab rounds (Kravis, Druckenmiller, Malka). His long 2025–26 essay series on Substack (shared as PDFs) is the real source here. It contains admissions you won't see in press: follow-on mistakes, a plan to make half as many investments, and Micky Malka shaping the firm's identity.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** Access comes through people, and he treats sourcing as the whole job. Nova measures itself on meeting founders before they found anything. One of its tracked metrics is \"Percentage of founders we invest in whom we met pre-founding,\" and he says the firm \"generally avoid[s] live rounds\" ([src](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Nova-Business-Model-v1.pdf), [src](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Investing-Learnings-v1.pdf)). Two things sit on top of that. One is a Lux lineage: he started under Brandon Reeves, and Lux is an LP in his first fund. The other is an LP roster of famous individuals, which gets Nova into mega-rounds through SPVs (Nova Flappy).\n- **What constrains him:** Capital and track record. Fund II ($100M target) and Fund III showed $0 sold on their Form Ds ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2110809/000123191926000227/primary_doc.xml), [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2159208/000215920826000001/primary_doc.xml)). In his own words, the firm has \"no formal framework whatsoever\" for follow-ons, and its ownership could have been 50–100% higher with front-loaded reserves ([src](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Investing-Learnings-v1.pdf)).\n- **The tension to watch:** The stated model is conviction before a company exists. The round data I can see shows something else: Koko is on good cap tables (Etched, Mercor, Mach), but other firms priced and led those rounds (Primary, General Catalyst, Sequoia). Nova was the first institutional lead only on small rounds (Vigil $5.7M, Midas $10M), and it joined Flapping Airplanes' $1B Series A, a live mega-round, through an SPV. The pre-founding relationships may be real but invisible in round data. That is an open question, not proof of a gap.\n- **What he'd never do:** Back a founder without \"aptitude.\" He says it outright: \"we absolutely will not invest in an individual without aptitude\" ([src](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Annual-Letter-2025_-Craft.pdf)). By his own statement, he also won't underwrite the idea or the market over the person.\n\n## Differential insight\nAll of these come from his own essays (PDFs on kokospotlights.me, linked from [endmoor.substack.com](https://endmoor.substack.com/)). None appears in press or on nova.nexus.\n\n- **He avoids live rounds.** \"meeting a founder during fundraising is already too late for us\" ([Investing Learnings v1, 2025-12-19](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Investing-Learnings-v1.pdf)). The Nova Flappy SPV into Flapping's $1B A is the visible exception.\n- **Micky Malka shaped the firm's identity.** \"Micky Malka gave us the line that unlocked it: identity comes before thesis.\" ([Annual Letter 2025: Craft, 2025-12-20](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Annual-Letter-2025_-Craft.pdf)). Malka is also a named Nova backer ([src](https://kokoxs.com/)) and an individual investor in the Flapping A. Ribbit, Malka's firm, reportedly led Vigil's follow-on after Nova. The essays also cite Ribbit I–III as a concentration model. Malka looks like Koko's most influential patron.\n- **Follow-on mistake, admitted.** \"our ownership could’ve been 50-100% higher if we frontloaded reserves\" ([src](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Investing-Learnings-v1.pdf)). The essay adds that if they follow on at all, they are \"probably by default not aggressive enough.\"\n- **He plans to make far fewer investments.** \"our investment cadence should be at least cut in half going forward\" ([src](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Investing-Learnings-v1.pdf)).\n- **He claims pricing power at inception.** \"we’ve been able to demand discounted market prices at inception rounds\" (same source). He credits this to a record of taking founders \"from -1 to 0,\" and adds that \"we’ve won allocation before even committing because our founders went to bat.\"\n- **Concentration models: Ribbit, Hummingbird, Founders Fund.** He studied Ribbit I–III, Hummingbird I–III and Founders Fund I–IV. He notes that \"Some managers (like Hummingbird) routinely invest 10% of the fund at first check.\" For small funds, the stated target is \"a target blended position cost basis sub $100M\" (same source). Hummingbird is also in the Flapping A.\n- **Nova tracks entry-timing metrics.** Its output metrics include \"Percentage of founders we invest in whom we met pre-founding,\" average months from first meeting to investing, the percentage of founders where Nova was the first investor, and average entry ownership and price ([Nova Business Model v1, 2025-12-20](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Nova-Business-Model-v1.pdf)).\n- **Sourcing runs on an AI scout plus talent clusters.** \"These features are then fed into our Talent Scout Agent\" ([Letter to Investors 2Q26, 2026-07-17](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Excerpt-Letter-to-Investors-2Q26.pdf)). The same letter says the agent learns the team's taste \"much like an apprentice\" (and adds \"Claude learns.\"). It maps clusters too: Olympiad medalists, CTF players, chess grandmasters under 22, and \"Atlas Fellows are AGI-native rationalists who were safety-pilled\". Atlas Fellows is the one cluster that points at future frontier-AI founders.\n- **The best sourcing channel is existing founders.** He describes a \"nearest-neighbor search in our key clients’ talent networks\" as the highest signal-to-noise channel, ahead of talent mapping and cold outreach ([Venture Service Flywheel, 2026-02-19](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Venture-Service-Flywheel.pdf)). That matches the Etched origin story, where Mach founder Ethan Thornton introduced him to Gavin Uberti ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc)).\n- **A scarcity thesis about investors.** \"rough estimate of ~50 active elite craftspeople globally in any given year\". He argues that taste is passed on through apprenticeship to about 150 people within one or two degrees of greatness (same Flywheel essay). This is how he explains his own Lux and Reeves start.\n- **The idea is a false signal.** From the Founder Profiles study of 45 founders with $5B+ outcomes: \"how often the “idea” is a false signal at inception\" (roughly half the time), plus \"prodigy signal is real\" ([Craft](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Annual-Letter-2025_-Craft.pdf)).\n- **The decision rule is unanimity.** \"the final decision on core investments requires buy-in from the whole team\" ([Business Model v1](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Nova-Business-Model-v1.pdf)). Every member can \"pound the table.\" In practice that means both co-founders, Koko and Carlo.\n- **The endgame is a Blackstone-style platform, not a seed fund.** He describes \"a Blackstone-shaped opportunity to be the chief capitalist of the innovation economy\" ([Annual Letter 2025: Build, 2025-12-22](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Annual-Letter-2025_-Build.pdf)). He also predicts venture index funds, startup derivatives, and firms sharing carry with founders.\n- **Growth investors are moving upstream.** Growth-specialist friends tell him \"they need to meet the founders at seed or even inception\" ([The Great Man Theory of Venture, 2025-11-08](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/The-Great-Man-Theory-of-Venture.pdf)).\n- **Identity corroboration (new).** kokospotlights.me, which hosts every Substack PDF, is an earlier personal site. It describes \"Koko Xu\" as an NYU Courant math student and lists a paper, \"Notes on Strategy & Ops from Mach\" (March 2024) ([src](https://kokospotlights.me/)). Both details match TechCrunch's Koko (NYU math, Mach head of growth).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**high.**\n- **Why:** He is a young emerging manager doing exactly the frontier-AI talent cohort I care about: Etched (AI chips), Mercor (AI-native labor and data), and Flapping Airplanes (a new lab), plus AI-verification and AI-trading seeds. His LPs (Kravis, Druckenmiller, Malka, Andreessen, Nomads) are individuals who back very young, concentrated managers, which is my LP question too.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** Etched (seed participant), Flapping Airplanes (Series A via SPV; seed per aggregator only), Mercor (seed-era), Midas (Nova led), Vigil Labs (Nova led).\n- **As a co-investor:** He wants to own the relationship before the round. I write $20M checks into rounds that are already live. Those are complementary positions: he could be a source of pre-round lab founders, and he needs SPV partners for big rounds.\n- **Caveat:** Fund capital is unproven ($0 sold on the Fund II and Fund III Form Ds). By his own account, check discipline after entry is weak.\n\n## Background\n- **Origins:** Born in Shenzhen, moved to a small town in Massachusetts at 12; a U.S. citizen ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc)). TechCrunch uses \"he.\"\n- **Education:** NYU, math ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc)). The earlier site describes a 2.5-year track at NYU Courant with an economics minor ([src](https://kokospotlights.me/)). He considered a math PhD and cold-emailed about 3,000 PhDs, which led to Lux ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc)).\n- **Lux Capital:** Summer associate under Brandon Reeves; left after a few months (TechCrunch).\n- **Mach Industries (2023):** Head of growth. Mach closed a $79M Series A about four months after a Sequoia-led $5.7M seed. On operating, he told TechCrunch it was \"a lot of doing, not a lot of thinking.\"\n- **Early checks:** Invested about $140K of personal savings in seed rounds, including Etched (TechCrunch).\n- **2024-03-27 (self-dated; published 2026-01-03):** Drafted \"Rise of the Career Investor,\" an extension of a \"Venture Company Blueprint (v0)\" ([src](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/DRAFT-Rise-of-the-Career-Investor.pdf)). If the self-reported date is accurate, he was planning a firm before he had a fund.\n- **2024:** Started fundraising in April. Form D for Koko Xsu Venture Co Fund I filed 2024-08-15 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2030439/000203043924000001/primary_doc.xml)). $17.5M committed by December 2024, anchored by Nomads (TechCrunch).\n- **2025:** Co-founded Nova with Carlo Agostinelli ([src](https://kokoxs.com/)). Signs Nova's Form Ds as MD of the GP.\n\n**Identity note (inference):** I've linked these names into one person, but no single source states it.\n- Form Ds are signed \"Endmoor Xsu.\"\n- The first fund is \"Koko Xsu Venture Co Fund I.\"\n- The X handle is @kokoxsu.\n- kokoxs.com links to endmoor.substack.com.\n- That Substack's byline is \"Endmoor Faunce,\" yet several of its PDFs carry the byline \"Koko Xs\" (Business Model v1, Annual Letters, Project Magnus).\n- The PDF host, kokospotlights.me, belongs to \"Koko Xu.\"\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://kokoxs.com/ ; https://www.nova.nexus/ (login wall) ; https://kokospotlights.me/ (earlier personal site, hosts essay PDFs) | |\n| Substack / blog | https://endmoor.substack.com/ | About monthly, 20+ posts from 2025-12 to 2026-10; each post links a PDF. Byline \"Endmoor Faunce\" (as of 2026-11-10) |\n| X | https://x.com/kokoxsu (linked from kokoxs.com) | Not read: X returned 402. Nova's handle is @novaholdings |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/kokoxs/ | Not opened |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| Other | https://founderprofiles.ai/ (Nova's Founder Profiles library; page did not render) ; reading lists on Google Drive (linked from kokoxs.com) | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The WebSearch budget ran out before podcast or YouTube discovery. That doesn't mean none exist. See Open questions.\n\n## How they think\n- **\"The Great Man Theory of Venture\" (2025-11-08):** Capital abundance compresses margins and shifts power to founders. In the end state, \"the atomic unit of the venture universe ceases to be companies, but people\" ([src](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/The-Great-Man-Theory-of-Venture.pdf)). He models venture's future on talent agencies and search funds.\n- **See-Pick-Win-Help, with Help as the multiplier:** \"Help is not just ¼th of the job\" ([src](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Venture-Service-Flywheel.pdf)). An unnamed early mentor taught him the framework.\n- **Underwriting framework (2026-10-31):**\n  - Adolescence Theory (\"Individuals are in some form their fifteen-year-old selves\").\n  - Out-of-distribution (OOD) motivation as input and OOD aptitude as output, with the psyche read as a time series.\n  - Comparison against historic peers of the same archetype (\"Are they more ood motivated than Napoleon at the same age?\") ([src](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Talent-Investing_-Underwriting.pdf)).\n- **Archetype \"baseball cards\":** Gifted Prodigies, Hardened Missionaries, Genius Killers, Relentless Hustlers, Entrepreneurial Scientists and others. Every person in the database gets a 1–5 star rating ([Craft](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Annual-Letter-2025_-Craft.pdf)).\n- **Project Magnus (2026-02-20):** Mock founder memos on Jobs, Musk, Gates, Zuckerberg and Page, written as if dated at founding ([src](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Project-Magnus_-Memos.pdf)). These are retrospective training exercises, not deals.\n- **Founder Profiles Learnings v1 (2026-08-01):** Lists \"non-obvious predictors\" across a dataset of 179–233 founders. They include a self-invented income mechanism before age 13, selling a service to one's own peer group as a minor, turning down a first exit, and zero or near-zero operating experience at inception. The founding-age signal is bimodal: \"Being ordinary at 27 is the non-signal\" ([src](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Founder-Profiles_-Learnings-v1.pdf)).\n- **Contrarian views:** He dismisses old-guard firms as \"soulless\" ([Unpacking Venture Alpha](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Unpacking-Venture-Alpha.pdf)). He calls himself \"spearfishing over netfishing\" and frames the job as a sourcing problem, not an evaluation problem (Craft).\n- **Calls he got right or wrong:**\n  - Right: early checks into Etched (now valued at $21B, per companies/etched.md) and Mercor.\n  - Wrong, self-admitted: under-reserving on follow-ons, too many investments, and being \"too fearful to commit to our (eventual) model\" in early 2025 (Craft).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Investing Learnings (v1)](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Investing-Learnings-v1.pdf): the candid page, with mistakes, the concentration math and the no-live-rounds rule.\n2. [Nova Business Model (v1)](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Nova-Business-Model-v1.pdf): the operating system, including the timing metrics and the unanimous IC.\n3. [The Great Man Theory of Venture](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/The-Great-Man-Theory-of-Venture.pdf): the core thesis.\n4. [Letter to Investors 2Q26 (excerpt)](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Excerpt-Letter-to-Investors-2Q26.pdf): the LP pitch, the Talent Scout Agent and the talent clusters.\n5. [Annual Letter 2025: Craft](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Annual-Letter-2025_-Craft.pdf): how the thesis evolved, and the Malka credit.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X returned 402 and the search budget was exhausted, so I couldn't see posting topics, replies or amplifiers. The only X content seen is Kole Lee crediting @novaholdings as the Vigil lead (via search snippet, recorded in nova-global).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Etched | Seed (personal check, pre-Nova) | Round led 2023-03 by Primary; Koko's entry date not stated | No (Primary Venture Partners led) | Primary | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc), [Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond) |\n| Mach Industries | Seed (pre-Nova; he was head of growth) | 2023 | No (Sequoia led $5.7M) | Sequoia | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc) |\n| Mercor | Seed-era (\"seed partners to the founders\") | 2023 | No (General Catalyst led, per aggregator) | General Catalyst | [src](https://kokoxs.com/), [aggregator](https://startupintros.com/news/mercor-seed-2023-08) |\n| Vigil Labs | Seed $5.7M | 2025-08-20 | Yes (Nova; Crunchbase names Koko as partner) | Lux, Pantera, SV Angel, Soma, Valor | [src](https://www.techflowpost.com/en-US/newsletter/95710), [Crunchbase](https://www.crunchbase.com/funding_round/vigil-labs-seed--619c0bdf) (aggregator) |\n| Midas | $10M launch round | 2026-02-05 | Yes (Nova; Carlo is the public voice) | Valor | [src](https://pulse2.com/midas-10-million-funding/?noamp=mobile) |\n| Judgment Labs | Series A (part of $32M combined) | 2026-05-12 | No (Greenoaks, Lightspeed) | Valor, Dynamic, Chris Manning | [src](https://judgmentlabs.ai/blogs/announcing-32m-led-by-lightspeed) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Seed $180M (aggregator only) | 2026-01-28 | No (Index, GV, Sequoia) | Index, GV, Sequoia, Menlo | [src](https://iq.wiki/wiki/flapping-airplanes) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Series A $1B via \"Nova Flappy, LP\" SPV (he signed the Form D) | SPV filed 2026-08-07; A announced 2026-10-21 | No (Index, Lux) | Index, Lux, Sequoia, Hummingbird, Kravis, Duquesne, Malka | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2146901/000123191926000844/primary_doc.xml), [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Etched:** Mach founder Ethan Thornton introduced Koko to co-founder Gavin Uberti, and Koko got onto the seed cap table with personal savings ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc)).\n  - **Timing:** Primary led the seed in March 2023. The next institutional round, the Series A co-led with Positive Sum, came about 15 months later in June 2024, after most majors had passed (companies/etched.md). Koko was in the earliest priced round but was not first institutional money.\n- **Mach:** He was an insider (head of growth) when the company went from a Sequoia seed to a $79M Series A in about four months (TechCrunch). This is founder-network access, not a lead.\n- **Vigil Labs:** This is Nova's first visible lead ($5.7M, August 2025). Lux, Reeves's firm, followed.\n  - **Later round:** a $20M seed led by Ribbit, per a jobs listing ([src](https://simplify.jobs/c/Vigil-Labs)).\n  - **The pattern:** a Nova lead, followed by Lux and then by Malka's firm. Malka and Lux are both Nova LPs or patrons.\n- **Flapping Airplanes A:** Koko signed the Nova Flappy SPV Form D on 2026-08-07, about 10 weeks before the A was announced and about a week after Forbes reported Series A talks (see firms/nova-global.md). This contradicts his own \"avoid live rounds\" rule, so it is best read as LP-driven co-investment. Lux co-led the round, and Kravis, Druckenmiller (through Duquesne Family Office) and Malka were individual investors. No source says how the allocation was obtained.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Carlo Agostinelli | Nova co-founder and co-MD | 2025 | [src](https://kokoxs.com/) |\n| Micky Malka (Ribbit) | Nova backer; credited with \"identity comes before thesis\" | by 2025-12 | [src](https://kokoxs.com/), [src](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Annual-Letter-2025_-Craft.pdf) |\n| Brandon Reeves (Lux) | Mentor; Koko was his summer associate | ~2023 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc) |\n| Lux Capital, Josh Wolfe | LPs in Fund I | 2024 | same |\n| Nomads | Anchor LP, Fund I | 2024 | same |\n| Marc Andreessen, Chris Dixon, Kevin Hartz | LPs in Fund I | 2024 | same |\n| Henry Kravis, Stan Druckenmiller | Nova backers | by 2026 | [src](https://kokoxs.com/) |\n| Ethan Thornton (Mach) | Former boss; introduced Gavin Uberti | 2023 | TechCrunch |\n| Gavin Uberti (Etched) | Portfolio founder | 2023 | TechCrunch |\n\n## Open questions\n- Podcasts, X posts and talks: not searched (budget exhausted). Next step: search \"Koko Xs\" plus podcast, \"Endmoor\", and @kokoxsu.\n- Did Nova (or Koko) actually invest in the Flapping Airplanes seed, or only in the A? Who got Nova the A allocation: Lux/Reeves, or the LPs?\n- Who is the investor in Nova Select, LP ($5M, one investor)? Malka, Kravis, or family capital?\n- Does Fund II or III ever report sales? What is the size of Nova's actual deployable capital?\n- Which current portfolio founders did Nova meet before they founded anything? The Business Model essay says this is tracked but publishes no numbers.\n- Who is the unnamed early mentor who taught him See-Pick-Win-Help (Reeves is plausible but unconfirmed)? And who is \"Jeremy Stern\" (named in a linked PDF filename)?\n- Etched check size and the exact Mercor round: not disclosed.\n\n## Sources\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc (2024-12-13)\n- https://kokoxs.com/\n- https://endmoor.substack.com/ (archive read via https://endmoor.substack.com/api/v1/archive)\n- https://kokospotlights.me/\n- https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/The-Great-Man-Theory-of-Venture.pdf (2025-11-08)\n- https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Investing-Learnings-v1.pdf (2025-12-19)\n- https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Nova-Business-Model-v1.pdf (2025-12-20)\n- https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Annual-Letter-2025_-Craft.pdf (2025-12-20)\n- https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Annual-Letter-2025_-Build.pdf (2025-12-22)\n- https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/DRAFT-Rise-of-the-Career-Investor.pdf (draft dated 2024-03-27)\n- https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Venture-Service-Flywheel.pdf (2026-02-19)\n- https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Project-Magnus_-Memos.pdf (2026-02-20)\n- https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Unpacking-Venture-Alpha.pdf (2026-03-28)\n- https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Excerpt-Letter-to-Investors-2Q26.pdf (2026-07-17)\n- https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Founder-Profiles_-Learnings-v1.pdf (2026-08-01)\n- https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Talent-Investing_-Underwriting.pdf (2026-10-31)\n- SEC Form D: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2030439/000203043924000001/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2110809/000123191926000227/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2146901/000123191926000844/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2159208/000215920826000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond\n- https://www.techflowpost.com/en-US/newsletter/95710 ; https://pulse2.com/midas-10-million-funding/?noamp=mobile ; https://judgmentlabs.ai/blogs/announcing-32m-led-by-lightspeed\n- https://iq.wiki/wiki/flapping-airplanes (aggregator) ; https://simplify.jobs/c/Vigil-Labs (aggregator) ; https://startupintros.com/news/mercor-seed-2023-08 (aggregator) ; https://www.crunchbase.com/funding_round/vigil-labs-seed--619c0bdf (aggregator, not opened)\n- https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/\n", "person:konstantine-buhler": "\n# Konstantine Buhler\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the Sequoia GP whose name sits on the firm's only two formation-stage AI chip deals (Unconventional AI 2025, NUVACORE 2026), and he is usually the senior signature on deals that junior partners source ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/konstantine-buhler/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** Buhler gives the senior-GP signature on Sequoia's seed-stage AI infrastructure deals. NUVACORE is the one hard chip deal: a formation-stage seed with Liam Corrigan, where Bloomberg names Sequoia as the only backer ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/nuvacore/), [src](https://news.bgov.com/artificial-intelligence/sequoia-backed-chip-startup-looks-to-overhaul-cpus-for-ai-era)). Most of his other AI deals are co-signed with a more junior partner: Reeder on XBOW, Enter and Sail; Bek on Dust; Josephine Chen on Rox ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/sail-research/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/dust/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-rox-every-seller-needs-an-agent-swarm/)). He has written publicly about AI since 2023, which suggests an ML-practitioner background (Kaggle era, graph thinking) more than a semis one ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-hema-raghavan/)).\n- **What constrains him.** His chips record is thinner than the firm site implies. Sequoia lists Unconventional AI under Buhler as sole partner. But an earlier repo deal row records that press investor lists for that seed name a16z and Lightspeed as co-leads and don't name Sequoia. That is not re-verified this session; if it holds, Sequoia was not the lead and he was not first ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/unconventional-ai/)). Arc, which fed him XBOW and Rox (Rox's company page shows \"Arc 2024\"), was shut down in Nov 2026 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/rox/)).\n- **What he'd never do (inferred, unsourced).** I found no record of him backing a pre-training or frontier model lab. His AI book is hardware at the bottom, plus agents and applications built on others' models. As a GP he also carries growth exposure (Citadel Securities 2022, Verkada, Waymo 2026), so seed is not his only lane ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/konstantine-buhler/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **NUVACORE is the real chip seed. Unconventional AI is not.** Bloomberg (2026-04-15) reported NUVACORE was finalizing a seed round with Sequoia backing. The Sequoia page pairs Buhler with Liam Corrigan, and both \"Founded\" and \"Partnered\" read 2026, so Sequoia came in at formation ([src](https://news.bgov.com/artificial-intelligence/sequoia-backed-chip-startup-looks-to-overhaul-cpus-for-ai-era), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/nuvacore/)). On Unconventional AI, Sequoia lists only Buhler and gives no round. Press seed lists credit a16z and Lightspeed ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/unconventional-ai/)).\n- **Compute-price arbitrage view (Sail).** He and Reeder quote Sail's mantra: \"there are no bad chips, only bad prices.\" They also say Sequoia has been a partner \"from day 0\", ahead of Kleiner's Series A ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sail-agents-without-limits/), 2026-10-02).\n- **Kumo came from a Kaggle-era connection.** Buhler said of Kumo cofounder \"Yuri\" (the transcript spelling, probably Jure Leskovec): \"in the Kaggle era, which was how I first met your cofounder\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-hema-raghavan/)). Kumo was partnered early in 2021 and later acquired ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/konstantine-buhler/)).\n- **Graph view of learning.** He called graphs \"the most general data type\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-hema-raghavan/)).\n- **Dust: seed lead, and he credits the founders' calls.** \"We at Sequoia were thrilled to lead their seed round\" (2023-07-03) ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-dust-llm-powered-productivity/)). Eighteen months later, on Training Data, he said \"their predictions have been consistently prescient\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-dust/)).\n- **Investment filter: AI must be the business.** In June 2023 he said he funds companies where AI is the lever, not where \"is it just peripheral to the business\" applies. Another quote: \"You want to fund the one that's going to see the biggest transformation\" ([src](https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/27/how-an-early-stage-investor-is-trying-to-find-the-winners-in-ai.html)).\n- **Precise language over \"AI\".** In \"AI and the Frontier Paradox\" (2023-07-18) he argues that \"AI\" is a moving label and that \"The precision of language is the key to category creation\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ai-paradox-perspective/)).\n- **Stochastic mindset.** In a 2025-01-22 essay he argues that AI moves work from deterministic to probabilistic output: \"AI tools will change the way we work by changing the way we think\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/stochastic-mindset-perspective/)).\n- **Discrepancy: Listen Labs.** Listen Labs appears on Buhler's profile as Pre-Seed/Seed 2023. The company page names Bryan Schreier and Charlie Curnin as partners and shows \"Arc 2023\". Buhler's visible role is co-hosting the podcast with Sonya Huang ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/listen-labs/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/knowing-what-your-customers-want-all-the-time-listen-labs-alfred-wahlforss/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, with a caveat.** NUVACORE is a Sequoia-led formation seed in an AI-era CPU from Gerard Williams (ex-Apple, sold a chip startup to Qualcomm). That is exactly the owner's AI-semis lane. Bloomberg (Apr 2026) reported a Series A expected within months, which is a co-invest window ([src](https://news.bgov.com/artificial-intelligence/sequoia-backed-chip-startup-looks-to-overhaul-cpus-for-ai-era)). An earlier repo row also notes an Oct 2026 Reuters report of a >$200M raise, but I did not find its URL this session. Unconventional AI (Naveen Rao) is also in-lens, but Buhler/Sequoia's role there is unclear. Sail (inference arbitrage) and XBOW are adjacent. Rox, Probook, Dust and Enter are AI-native automation in the Serval mould. He is a useful counterpart for mega-seeds in chips. He doesn't touch pre-training labs, so he is not a route into new labs.\n\n## Background\n- Interested in AI \"since college and grad school\" (firm bio summary; school not stated) ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/konstantine-buhler/)).\n- Says he met Kumo's cofounder during \"the Kaggle era\", implying data-science practice before investing ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-hema-raghavan/)).\n- Earliest Sequoia deal on his profile: Ethos, partnered early in 2017 ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/konstantine-buhler/)). Join date not sourced.\n- Named a GP who will \"own and operate\" Sequoia in the Nov 2026 memo ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)).\n- Bio mentions a Greek-immigrant grandfather (street vendor turned small-business owner) and a Nikola Tesla hero; motto \"Simplify. Focus.\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/konstantine-buhler/)).\n- Education and pre-Sequoia employer: nothing found (web search budget exhausted; Business Insider 2021 profile could not be fetched).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/konstantine-buhler/ | Firm profile; email listed on page |\n| Substack / blog | Sequoia perspectives (see Read these first) | Roughly one essay per year, 2023–2025 |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found (not fetched) | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Sequoia Training Data (episode co-host) | Dust, Kumo (Hema Raghavan), Listen Labs episodes |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-05-03 — Bloomberg TV — \"Sequoia's Buhler: I encourage people to use AI\" — https://www.bloomberg.com/news/videos/2023-05-03/sequoia-s-buhler-i-encourage-people-to-use-ai-video — not transcribed.\n- 2023-06 — CNBC Technology Executive Council dinner — AI should be core to the business, not peripheral — https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/27/how-an-early-stage-investor-is-trying-to-find-the-winners-in-ai.html\n- 2024-03-21 — Bloomberg TV — \"network of AIs working together\" — https://www.bloomberg.com/news/videos/2024-03-21/sequoia-s-buhler-on-network-of-ais-working-together-video — not transcribed.\n- 2024-03-26 — Sequoia AI Ascent 2024 opening, with Grady and Huang — https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/the-ai-opportunity-sequoia-capital-s-ai-ascent-2024-opening-remarks (per people/sonya-huang.md)\n- 2024-12-03 — Bloomberg TV — \"Why 2025 will be the year of AI agents\" — https://www.bloomberg.com/news/videos/2024-12-03/why-2025-will-be-the-year-of-ai-agents-video — not transcribed.\n- 2025-01-24 — CNBC full interview (Kate Rooney), AI predictions for 2025 — https://www.cnbc.com/video/2025/01/24/watch-cnbcs-full-interview-with-sequoia-capital-partner-konstantine-buhler.html — no transcript on page.\n- 2025-05-07 and 2026-04-30 — AI Ascent keynotes with Grady and Huang (per people/sonya-huang.md).\n- Training Data — Dust co-founders (with Grady) — https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-dust/ — \"I'm not slacking my colleagues, I'm slacking assistants.\"\n- Training Data — Hema Raghavan, Kumo (with Huang) — https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-hema-raghavan/ — the Kaggle-era connection and graphs as the general data type.\n- Sequoia podcast — Listen Labs' Alfred Wahlforss (with Huang) — https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/knowing-what-your-customers-want-all-the-time-listen-labs-alfred-wahlforss/ — \"I'm a humanist, absolutely.\"\n- Episode dates for the Sequoia-hosted podcasts are not shown on the pages; the metadata date is a render date.\n\n## How they think\n- **AI must transform the core work.** He backs companies where AI changes most of the work (his example is the typist, not the laundromat) ([src](https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/27/how-an-early-stage-investor-is-trying-to-find-the-winners-in-ai.html)).\n- **Agents as coworkers.** He argued for multi-agent assistants early (Dust 2023, Rox \"agent swarms\" 2024, Bloomberg \"year of AI agents\" Dec 2024) ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-rox-every-seller-needs-an-agent-swarm/)).\n- **Compute economics.** He supports compute-price arbitrage (Sail) and efficiency-first hardware (Unconventional AI's \"biology-scale efficiency\"; NUVACORE's area-efficient CPU) ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/unconventional-ai/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/nuvacore/)).\n- **Graphs and ensembles.** His ML intuition comes from Kaggle ensembles and GNNs ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-hema-raghavan/)).\n- **Calls so far.** XBOW (seed 2024) reached a $120M Series C in Mar 2026 at a valuation above $1B ([src](https://xbow.com/news/xbow-raises-120m-to-scale)). Kumo was acquired. Quilt (seed 2023) was acquired ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/konstantine-buhler/)). Outcome values not sourced.\n\n## Read these first\n- \"AI and the Frontier Paradox\" (2023-07-18): AI is a moving label, so name the specific technology. https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ai-paradox-perspective/\n- \"The Stochastic Mindset\" (2025-01-22): AI makes work probabilistic and acts as an exoskeleton for builders. https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/stochastic-mindset-perspective/\n- \"AI Recruits a New Hybrid Workforce\" (2023-01-26): apps we work *on* versus *with*. https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ai-for-work-perspective/\n- Training Data with Hema Raghavan: his ML roots and graph thesis. https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-hema-raghavan/\n- \"Partnering with Sail\" (with Reeder, 2026-10-02): the closest he has written to a compute thesis. https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sail-agents-without-limits/\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X account located. His public voice is Sequoia essays, Training Data co-hosting, and Bloomberg/CNBC TV.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI-relevant deals he is named on (Sequoia profile and company pages). The \"first partnered\" stage is Sequoia's own label.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| NUVACORE (AI-era CPU core; Gerard Williams, John Bruno, Ram Srinivasan) | Seed (Pre-Seed/Seed per Sequoia) | 2026-04 (Bloomberg 2026-04-15: seed being finalized) | Likely: Bloomberg names only Sequoia as backer, and an earlier repo row says Sequoia led. Co-signed with Liam Corrigan | not disclosed | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/nuvacore/), [src](https://news.bgov.com/artificial-intelligence/sequoia-backed-chip-startup-looks-to-overhaul-cpus-for-ai-era) |\n| Unconventional AI (AI hardware; Naveen Rao, Michael Carbin, Sara Achour, MeeLan Lee) | Pre-Seed/Seed per Sequoia | 2025 | Unlikely. An earlier repo row says press credits a16z and Lightspeed as co-leads and omits Sequoia; not re-verified here. Sole Sequoia partner | not verified this session | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/unconventional-ai/) |\n| Sail Research (inference for long-running agents) | Seed (\"from day 0\"; Pre-Seed/Seed per Sequoia) | partnered 2025; announced 2026-06-24 | Seed led by Sequoia per repo deal row; the Sequoia post doesn't say. With Lauren Reeder | Kleiner Perkins (A lead), Redpoint, Theory, CRV, A* | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sail-agents-without-limits/), [src](https://sailresearch.com/blog/sail-raises-80m) |\n| XBOW (AI offensive security; Oege de Moor) | Seed (via Arc) | 2024-07-30 | Yes, Sequoia. With Lauren Reeder (per people/lauren-reeder.md) | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-xbow-the-gold-standard-in-offensive-security/) |\n| Xaira (AI drug R&D) | unknown (Early per profile) | 2024 | unknown. Sole Sequoia partner | not checked | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/xaira/) |\n| Rox (agent swarms for revenue teams) | Seed (via Arc 2024), then Series A | 2024-11-19 post | Seed yes, Sequoia; A participation. With Josephine Chen on post | GV, General Catalyst (seed) | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-rox-every-seller-needs-an-agent-swarm/) |\n| Enter (AI legal document analysis, Brazil founders) | Pre-Seed/Seed per profile | 2024 | unknown. With Lauren Reeder | not checked | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/enter/) |\n| Dust (LLM agents for work; Stanislas Polu, ex-OpenAI) | Seed | 2023-07-03 | Yes, Sequoia. With Julien Bek | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-dust-llm-powered-productivity/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/dust/) |\n| Listen Labs (AI customer research) | Pre-Seed/Seed per his profile; Arc 2023 | 2023 | unknown. Company page names Schreier and Curnin, not Buhler | not checked | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/listen-labs/) |\n| Probook (AI OS for home services) | Seed, then Series A | partnered 2025; post 2026-06-23 | not stated. Sole Sequoia partner | not checked | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-probook-ai-for-the-trades/) |\n| Kumo (graph ML / predictive AI; Jure Leskovec) | Early | 2021 (acquired) | unknown | not checked | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/konstantine-buhler/) |\n| Quilt | Pre-Seed/Seed | 2023 (acquired) | unknown | not checked | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/konstantine-buhler/) |\n| Ent; Odessia | Pre-Seed/Seed | 2025 | unknown; AI relevance unconfirmed | not checked | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/konstantine-buhler/) |\n| Non-AI or growth: Citadel Securities (Growth 2022), Verkada (Growth 2019), Waymo (2026), CaptivateIQ (2020), Draftea (2021), EDX Markets (2022), Ethos (2017, IPO) | various | | | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/konstantine-buhler/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **XBOW.** Oege de Moor presented at an Arc story slam and joined Arc. Sequoia then led the seed with Reeder and Buhler. (Per people/lauren-reeder.md; the XBOW page fetch failed this session.) ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-xbow-the-gold-standard-in-offensive-security/))\n- **Dust.** Polu and Hubert were early Stripe hires after Stripe bought their prior startup. Polu then did research engineering at OpenAI. Sequoia led the seed while they were building the team in Paris. Julien Bek is co-listed. Intro path not stated ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-dust-llm-powered-productivity/)).\n- **Kumo.** He knew Leskovec from the Kaggle era, so the relationship predates the deal ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-hema-raghavan/)).\n- **NUVACORE.** Founded and partnered in 2026. By April 2026 Bloomberg reported a seed being finalized with a Series A expected within months, and Corrigan is the junior on the deal. Intro path not found (Bloomberg says Williams earlier sold a chip venture to Qualcomm; Sequoia's link to it not checked) ([src](https://news.bgov.com/artificial-intelligence/sequoia-backed-chip-startup-looks-to-overhaul-cpus-for-ai-era)).\n- **Rox.** Arc 2024, then a Sequoia-led seed with GV and General Catalyst. Sequoia uses Rox internally ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-rox-every-seller-needs-an-agent-swarm/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/rox/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sequoia Capital | General Partner (one of seven) | named 2026-11 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/) |\n| Lauren Reeder | Co-partner on XBOW, Enter, Sail | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/enter/) |\n| Liam Corrigan | Co-partner on NUVACORE | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/nuvacore/) |\n| Julien Bek | Co-partner on Dust | 2023 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/dust/) |\n| Josephine Chen | Co-author of the Rox partnering post | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-rox-every-seller-needs-an-agent-swarm/) |\n| Pat Grady | Training Data co-host (Dust); AI Ascent co-presenter | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-dust/) |\n| Sonya Huang | Podcast co-host (Kumo, Listen Labs); AI Ascent | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-hema-raghavan/) |\n| Jure Leskovec (Kumo) | Known since the Kaggle era (transcript spells \"Yuri\") | pre-2021 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-hema-raghavan/) |\n| Naveen Rao (Unconventional AI) | Sequoia partner of record | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/unconventional-ai/) |\n| Gerard Williams (NUVACORE) | Seed partner | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/nuvacore/) |\n| Stanislas Polu, Gabriel Hubert (Dust) | Seed lead partner | 2023 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-dust-llm-powered-productivity/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did Sequoia actually invest in Unconventional AI's seed, and how large was the check? Bloomberg and TechCrunch lists omit Sequoia. Check Form D filings and Naveen Rao's announcement.\n- Who leads NUVACORE's Series A, and at what size? Find the Reuters Oct 2026 report a repo row mentions. Is there an allocation for outside money?\n- Education, pre-Sequoia employer, join year and GP promotion date (Business Insider 2021 profile, LinkedIn).\n- Why does his profile list Listen Labs when the company page doesn't name him?\n- Xaira: round, check size and whether Sequoia led (not verified this session).\n- Does Corrigan source the chip deals and Buhler sign them, or the reverse? Who brought Gerard Williams to Sequoia?\n- Transcripts of the CNBC Jan 2025 and Bloomberg Dec 2024 interviews for agents and compute views.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/konstantine-buhler/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/unconventional-ai/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/nuvacore/\n- https://news.bgov.com/artificial-intelligence/sequoia-backed-chip-startup-looks-to-overhaul-cpus-for-ai-era\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sail-agents-without-limits/\n- https://sailresearch.com/blog/sail-raises-80m\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-xbow-the-gold-standard-in-offensive-security/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/xaira/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/dust/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-dust-llm-powered-productivity/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/listen-labs/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/probook/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-probook-ai-for-the-trades/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/rox/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-rox-every-seller-needs-an-agent-swarm/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/enter/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ai-paradox-perspective/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/stochastic-mindset-perspective/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/ai-for-work-perspective/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-dust/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-hema-raghavan/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/knowing-what-your-customers-want-all-the-time-listen-labs-alfred-wahlforss/\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/27/how-an-early-stage-investor-is-trying-to-find-the-winners-in-ai.html\n- https://www.cnbc.com/video/2025/01/24/watch-cnbcs-full-interview-with-sequoia-capital-partner-konstantine-buhler.html\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/\n", "person:kris-fredrickson": "\n# Kris Fredrickson\n\nLegal name on SEC filings: Kristian Fredrickson.\n\n## One-line take\nFormer Coatue growth managing partner who ran a concentrated $175M solo fund (Verified Capital) and then co-founded Diffusion with Victor Lazarte. Diffusion is a $1.25B debut AI fund, and it co-led Harvey's $550M round ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/27/from-8vc-to-benchmark-heres-whats-hiding-in-october-sec-filings/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He builds long, hands-on relationships with founders and turns them into allocation. Two examples:\n  - Harvey's CEO says Fredrickson helped on big early decisions, then became an LP in Verified.\n  - Perplexity's CEO calls him a great ally ([src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224)).\n  - Diffusion then co-led Harvey as a new firm ([src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-dollar550m-at-a-dollar155b-valuation-to-help-legal-teams-own-their-intelligence)).\n- **What constrains him:** He now sits on two platforms (Verified and Diffusion), and how the two split deals is undisclosed. Growth-stage entry prices are high.\n- **What he'd never do:** Run a broad portfolio. Verified was built for 8 to 10 bets, with no ownership or board requirements ([src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Flexibility on terms in exchange for selectivity:** \"tengo que ser flexible en todo lo demás\" (\"I have to be flexible on everything else\") ([src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224), Forbes Spanish syndication, 2025-07-22).\n- **Perplexity board:** He took Emil Michael's board seat after investing in April 2024 at about $2.5B. Aravind Srinivas called him \"un gran aliado en muchos aspectos\" (\"a great ally in many ways\") ([src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224)).\n- **Instacart, 2017:** He pushed to invest when Amazon bought Whole Foods and others doubted the company. Apoorva Mehta called the investment crucial and later became an LP in Verified ([src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224)).\n- **Bloomberg framed Diffusion as his firm:** Coverage of the Harvey round calls Diffusion \"a new firm cofounded by former Coatue Management investor Kris Fredrickson\" and does not mention Lazarte ([src](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/harvey-raises-550m-at-156b-co-led-by-lightspeed-and-diffusion), 2026-09-09).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:** He commands growth-stage capital ($1.25B), concentrates conviction, and has a frontier-adjacent book (Perplexity, Harvey, and through Diffusion, Etched and Cognition).\n- **Against:** He comes in at growth and late stage, not on large seeds into research teams. Nothing found in pre-training labs.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** Perplexity (board), and Diffusion's stakes in Etched and Applied Compute.\n\n## Background\n- **Goldman Sachs:** worked on tech deals ([src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224)). A secondary profile lists the title as VP; dates not found.\n- **Curology:** co-founded the prescription skincare startup ([src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224)). Dates not found.\n- **Benchmark:** invested with Benchmark ([src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224)). TechCrunch calls him a former Benchmark VC ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/27/from-8vc-to-benchmark-heres-whats-hiding-in-october-sec-filings/)). Title and dates not found.\n- **Coatue:** Managing Partner on the growth strategy; retired in 2022. Backed Chime, Marqeta, Checkout.com and Instacart (2017) ([src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224)).\n- **2021:** listed as a former director of Pill Club Holdings ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1719865/000171986521000002/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **2025:** Verified Capital Fund I.\n  - Form D filed 2025-04-29 for a $175M offering ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2041499/000204149925000002/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - Closed at $175M in July 2025 ([src](https://techfundingnews.com/big-bet-on-ai-coatue-investor-kris-fredrickson-launches-verified-capital-a-175m-fund/)).\n  - Verified V1 LLC: $25M from 6 investors, 2026-06 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2138406/000213840626000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **2026-06:** co-founded Diffusion with Victor Lazarte.\n  - Managing Director of Diffusion ONE GP; signs the Form Ds.\n  - Diffusion ONE sold $1.252B to 113 investors ([Form D/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2141438/000123191926001442/primary_doc.xml)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | No Diffusion or Verified team page found |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-07 — TBPN — a digest item covers his $175M concentrated fund with 8–10 core positions ([src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-07-15/kris-fredrickson-launches-175m-concentrated-fund-with-810-core-positions-bullish-on-ma-resurgence)). It is a summary only and was not verified as a transcript.\n\n## How they think\n- **Concentration:** 8–10 companies per fund. He expected Verified to take about two years to deploy, slower than the usual three-year pitch ([src](https://techfundingnews.com/big-bet-on-ai-coatue-investor-kris-fredrickson-launches-verified-capital-a-175m-fund/)).\n- **No stage or sector preference:** He is strict on company quality and flexible on terms ([src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224)).\n- **Calls:**\n  - Instacart in 2017, against the Whole Foods narrative.\n  - Perplexity at about $2.5B in 2024.\n  - Harvey from the Series B (2023) through Diffusion's co-lead at $15.5B (2026).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Forbes (Iain Martin, 2025-07, Spanish syndication): the Verified launch, his strategy and founder testimonials ([src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224)).\n2. TechCrunch, 2026-10-27: the $1.252B close of Diffusion ONE ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/27/from-8vc-to-benchmark-heres-whats-hiding-in-october-sec-filings/)).\n3. Harvey's $550M announcement ([src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-dollar550m-at-a-dollar155b-valuation-to-help-legal-teams-own-their-intelligence)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Instacart | Investment (Coatue) | 2017 | unknown | | [src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224) |\n| Chime, Marqeta, Checkout.com | Growth (Coatue) | not found | unknown | | [src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224) |\n| Harvey | Series B | 2023 | unknown | | [src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224) |\n| Perplexity | Growth (~$2.5B valuation); board seat | 2024-04 | unknown | | [src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224) |\n| Atlas (credit card) | Series B ($27M) | ~2024-12 | unknown | | [src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224) |\n| Harvey | Series E (angel) | 2025-06 | No | | [src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-e) |\n| Harvey (via Diffusion) | Growth ($550M at $15.5B) | 2026-09-09 | Yes, co-lead with Lightspeed (firm-level) | Sapphire, Whale Rock, Sequoia, Kleiner, a16z, Coatue, GIC, Verified Capital | [src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-dollar550m-at-a-dollar155b-valuation-to-help-legal-teams-own-their-intelligence) |\n| Etched (via Diffusion) | Series C ($300M at $10.3B) | 2026-07-23 | No | Sequoia (lead) | [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference) |\n\nHe is not personally named on any Diffusion deal post. The Diffusion rows are firm-level credits.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Harvey:** Winston Weinberg says Fredrickson helped from the start on major decisions, including an acquisition. Weinberg then invested in Verified ([src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224)). Fredrickson backed the Series B, angel-invested in the Series E, and then Diffusion co-led the $550M round.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Diffusion | Co-founder, MD of the GP | 2026-06 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/27/from-8vc-to-benchmark-heres-whats-hiding-in-october-sec-filings/) |\n| Victor Lazarte | Diffusion co-MD | 2026-06 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2141438/000214143826000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Verified Capital | Founder, Managing Member of the GP | 2025-04 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2041499/000204149925000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Coatue | Managing Partner (growth), until 2022 | not found | [src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224) |\n| Benchmark | Former investor | not found | [src](https://techfundingnews.com/big-bet-on-ai-coatue-investor-kris-fredrickson-launches-verified-capital-a-175m-fund/) |\n| Curology | Co-founder | not found | [src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224) |\n| Perplexity (Aravind Srinivas) | Board director | 2024 | [src](https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224) |\n| Apoorva Mehta, Brian Armstrong, Winston Weinberg, Elad Gil, Adams Street, Commonfund, Northwestern endowment | LPs in Verified Capital Fund I | 2025 | [src](https://techfundingnews.com/big-bet-on-ai-coatue-investor-kris-fredrickson-launches-verified-capital-a-175m-fund/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What are his Benchmark and Coatue tenure dates? When was he promoted to Managing Partner at Coatue?\n- How do Verified and Diffusion split deals, and is Verified still investing?\n- Did any Verified LPs commit to Diffusion ONE?\n- Who leads which Diffusion deals, him or Lazarte?\n\n## Sources\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/27/from-8vc-to-benchmark-heres-whats-hiding-in-october-sec-filings/\n- https://aiweekly.co/alerts/harvey-raises-550m-at-156b-co-led-by-lightspeed-and-diffusion\n- https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1329224\n- https://techfundingnews.com/big-bet-on-ai-coatue-investor-kris-fredrickson-launches-verified-capital-a-175m-fund/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2141438/000214143826000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2141438/000123191926001442/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2041499/000204149925000002/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2138406/000213840626000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1719865/000171986521000002/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-e\n- https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-dollar550m-at-a-dollar155b-valuation-to-help-legal-teams-own-their-intelligence\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference\n- https://www.vcsheet.com/who/kris-fredrickson (aggregator; photo)\n", "person:kristina-shen": "\n# Kristina Shen\n\n## One-line take\nFormer a16z B2B lead who now backs AI-native automation at Series A from Chemistry: Assort Health (co-lead), Serval, Nova Intelligence, Didero, and a slice of Atoms.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** a long B2B software record (ServiceTitan from 2013, Mux, Pave, Wrapbook, Decagon) and a reputation for backing non-consensus categories. ServiceTitan's founder calls her an \"independent thinker\" ([src](https://www.chemistry.vc/team/kristina-shen)).\n- **Constraint:** fund size. She co-leads $20-30M As; she is a participant in the $47M Serval A and the $1.7B Atoms round.\n- **Pattern:** AI that does a job inside an old workflow (medical front desk, IT service desk, SAP customisation, procurement).\n\n## Differential insight\n- Assort Health was Chemistry's first announced deal, six months after the fund closed: \"We are thrilled to co-lead Assort Health’s recent funding round alongside First Round Capital.\" ([src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/why-we-invested-in-assort-health)).\n- Serval appears on her own select-investments list but has no Chemistry memo; the firm entered in the Redpoint-led Series A (2025-10) as a participant ([src](https://www.chemistry.vc/team/kristina-shen)).\n- She is the Chemistry partner on physical AI: co-author of \"From Demo to Business: The Race in Physical AI\" and the Atoms post (not mined).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. Serval is the example I use for AI-native automation, and she is the partner on it at Chemistry. Assort, Nova and Didero are the same shape.\n\n## Background\n- Bessemer Venture Partners, partner, 2013-2019 (seven years): ServiceTitan, Gainsight, Instructure.\n- Andreessen Horowitz, General Partner, 2019-2024, led B2B software investing: Mux, Pave, Wrapbook, Rutter.\n- Co-founded Chemistry, announced 2024-10-22 ([src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/introducing-chemistry)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.chemistry.vc/team/kristina-shen | |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.chemistry.vc/blog | memos and theme posts |\n| X | https://x.com/kshenster | follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/kristina-shen/ | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Elements (Chemistry interview series, with partners) | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-10 — Venture Unlocked #143 — The blueprint for starting a new firm — [link](https://ventureunlocked.substack.com/p/the-blueprint-for-starting-a-new) — show notes only; not transcribed.\n\n## How they think\n- Voice AI arrives in waves; healthcare front-office is an early one (Assort).\n- Physical AI is moving \"from demo to business\" (Atoms).\n- Legacy enterprise software (SAP) is an AI coding market of its own (Nova Intelligence).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Why We Invested in Assort Health](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/why-we-invested-in-assort-health)\n- [The 4 Waves of Voice AI](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/the-4-waves-of-voice-ai) (not mined)\n- [From Demo to Business: The Race in Physical AI](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/from-demo-to-business-the-race-in-physical-ai) (not mined)\n- [Our Series A in Nova Intelligence](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/the-ai-platform-for-the-software-that-runs-the-world)\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed.\n\n## Portfolio\nChemistry-era, AI only.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Assort Health | Series A $22M | 2025-04-16 | Co-lead | First Round Capital | [src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/why-we-invested-in-assort-health) |\n| Serval | Series A $47M | 2025-10-21 | No | Redpoint (lead), First Round, General Catalyst, Box Group, Bessemer | [src](https://www.fenwick.com/insights/experience/fenwick-represents-serval-in-47m-series-a-funding) |\n| Didero | Series A $30M | 2026-02-12 | Co-lead | Headline, M12 | [src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/our-series-a-in-didero) |\n| Nova Intelligence | Series A | 2026-05-05 | Yes | Accel, Conviction, SAP.io | [src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/the-ai-platform-for-the-software-that-runs-the-world) |\n| Atoms | $1.7B round | 2026-07-22 | No | a16z (lead) | [src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/the-robots-are-reporting-for-work) |\n\nPre-Chemistry deals that still show on her page: Decagon, Tennr, Equals, Macro, Mux, Pave, ServiceTitan, Sprig, Wrapbook.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Assort Health:** co-led with First Round, the existing seed backer; Chemistry returned in the Series B (2025-09) and C (2026-06, $1.2B).\n- **Serval:** nothing public on how she got in; see Open questions.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ethan Kurzweil | Co-founder; both at Bessemer 2013-19 | 2013 | [src](https://ventureunlocked.substack.com/p/the-blueprint-for-starting-a-new) |\n| Mark Goldberg | Co-founder | 2024 | [src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/introducing-chemistry) |\n| Bohan Lou | Co-author on Nova Intelligence and physical AI | 2024-10 | [src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/the-ai-platform-for-the-software-that-runs-the-world) |\n| First Round Capital | Co-lead on Assort Health; co-investor on Serval | 2025 | [src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/why-we-invested-in-assort-health) |\n| Andreessen Horowitz | Former GP; followed a16z into Atoms | 2019-2024 | [src](https://www.chemistry.vc/post/the-robots-are-reporting-for-work) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Intro path into Serval, and whether Chemistry had any seed exposure.\n- Board seats at Assort, Nova, Didero.\n- Why she left a16z in 2024 (needs the Venture Unlocked audio).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.chemistry.vc/team/kristina-shen\n- https://www.chemistry.vc/post/why-we-invested-in-assort-health\n- https://www.chemistry.vc/post/our-series-a-in-didero\n- https://www.chemistry.vc/post/the-ai-platform-for-the-software-that-runs-the-world\n- https://www.chemistry.vc/post/the-robots-are-reporting-for-work\n- https://ventureunlocked.substack.com/p/the-blueprint-for-starting-a-new\n", "person:kyle-vogt": "\n# Kyle Vogt\n\n> Written 2026-11-11 (sweep early-backers-1). **Thin page.** He has no portfolio page and has not, in anything found, talked about his angel investing. Cheques come from company announcements already in the ledger plus aggregators (labelled). Quotes are from one transcript (Cheeky Pint, 2025-06). X was not fetched.\n\n## One-line take\nA three-time founder who writes personal cheques into robotics, autonomy and compute companies, usually founded by people from the self-driving world, while running his own $2B robotics company.\n\n## Crux\nFounders take his money for the name and for hard-won knowledge of shipping autonomy; at least one cheque went to someone he had bought a company from (Odyssey's CEO sold Voyage to Cruise). He is a signal cheque, not a lead, and his time belongs to The Bot Company. He says he will never sell a company again, which tells you how he will advise the founders he backs.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **On selling Cruise:** \"I was naive about the ability to get a large corporation\" to follow his plan ([Cheeky Pint transcript, 2025-06-25](https://cheekypint.transistor.fm/3/transcript)).\n- **His conclusion:** \"Experience has told me now that that is not the path\" (same source).\n- **What he looks for in robotics:** \"you don't need a robot that's repeatable, you need a robot that's adaptable\" (same source).\n- **Why he discounts demos:** \"The bar for commercial success for self-driving was five, six 9s of reliability.\" (same source).\n- **On other angels (already in the ledger):** he says that when he hears of a hot company, Aneel Ranadive is already in it (see the `aneel-ranadive` insight row).\n- **Team-size view:** he predicts the next $100B company will have under 100 people and says The Bot Company will too (same transcript, paraphrase).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. His cheques cluster where the owner invests (AI chips: Etched; world models: Odyssey; agents lab: Imbue; inference: Wafer; automation: Basis), so his name on a seed cap table is a useful signal. He is not a lead, has no fund and does not publish. The stronger lens item is The Bot Company itself (seed led by Friedman, Gross and Spark; Greenoaks at $2B).\n\n## Background\nCo-founded Justin.tv and Twitch, then Cruise in 2013; Spark led Cruise's Series A in 2015; GM acquired it in 2016; he resigned as CEO in November 2023. Co-founded The Bot Company in May 2024 with Paril Jain (ex-Tesla) and Luke Holoubek (ex-Cruise), raising $150M from Nat Friedman, Daniel Gross, Nabeel Hyatt, the Collisons and others ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/05/13/cruise-founder-kyle-vogt-is-back-with-a-robot-startup/)); Greenoaks led a further $150M at $2B in March 2025 (ledger).\n\n## Vehicle\nPersonal cheques; he is named as an individual in every announcement. No fund, Form D, scout programme or syndicate found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | not checked in this pass | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-06 — Cheeky Pint (John Collison) — home robots and why he'll never sell another company — [link](https://cheekypint.transistor.fm/3) — mined\n- 2025-02 — No Priors — Building Hard Tech in Hard Markets — [link](https://metacast.app/podcast/no-priors-artificial-intelligence-technology/jyrcnElu/building-hard-tech-in-hard-markets-kyle-vogt-on-cruise-twitch-and-the-bot-company/CRaios0j) — not mined; has an \"Advice to other founders\" segment at 40:21\n- undated — Uncapped with Jack Altman #32 — [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/py/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/id1801867202?l=en) — not mined\n- undated — Go/No-Go — AI, home robotics and small teams — [link](https://rss.com/podcasts/go-no-go/2371250) — not mined\n\n## How they think\n- Adaptable, learned systems beat precise, repeatable ones.\n- Reliability is the product; each extra nine costs about ten times the work.\n- Small teams; do not sell to a large corporation.\n- Nothing found on how he picks angel investments, or on any company he passed on.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Cheeky Pint transcript](https://cheekypint.transistor.fm/3/transcript): the only long primary source used here.\n- [Nabeel Hyatt on Cruise](https://medium.com/spark-capital/on-cruise-cc51c782d70f): how his first big investor relationship formed (already mined in the ledger).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed (X not fetched).\n\n## Portfolio\n**P/S** = in at pre-seed or seed.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Foxglove **P/S** | Seed ($3.7M); Series A 2022-10 | 2022-01 | no | Fred Ehrsam also an angel | [src](https://foxglove.dev/blog/foxglove-raises-seed-funding-3-7m) |\n| Reflex Robotics **P/S** | Seed (~$7M) | 2023-09 | no | | [src](https://www.vcbacked.co/company/reflex-robotics) (aggregator) |\n| Imbue | Series B ($200M) | 2023-09-07 | no | | [src](https://imbue.com/company/introducing-imbue/) |\n| Etched | Series A | 2024-06-25 | no | not recorded | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/25/etched-is-building-an-ai-chip-that-only-runs-transformer-models/) |\n| Odyssey **P/S** | Seed ($9M); listed again in B 2026-06 | 2024-07-08 | no | Air Street first; Elad Gil, Garry Tan, Jeff Dean in the B | [src](https://www.fenwick.com/insights/experience/fenwick-represents-odyssey-systems-in-9m-seed-funding); B per [CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/kyle-vogt) (aggregator) |\n| Basis | Series A ($34M); listed again in B 2026-02 | 2024-12-17 | no | Khosla led; NFDG | [src](https://www.getbasis.ai/blogs/basis-raises-a-34m-series-a-led-by-khosla-ventures); B per CB Insights (aggregator) |\n| Origin Lab **P/S** | Seed ($8M) | 2026-05-13 | no | Lightspeed | [src](https://originlab.ai/blog/seed-announcement-lightspeed) |\n| Wafer | Series A ($40M) | 2026-09-01 | no | Jeff Dean, Guillermo Rauch, AMD Ventures, Fifty Years | [src](https://pulse2.com/wafer-raises-40-million-series-a-to-scale-ai-inference-optimization-platform/) |\n| Warp (payroll; not the terminal company) | Series A ($18M) 2025-06; B ($60M) 2026-06 | 2025-06-18 | no | Drew Houston, Arash Ferdowsi, Sound Ventures | [CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/kyle-vogt) (aggregator) |\n| Hestus **P/S** | Seed | 2025-01 | no | | CB Insights, [Clera](https://www.getclera.com/investors/kyle-vogt) (aggregators; amounts conflict) |\n| Yuma AI **P/S** | Seed ($5M); also AI Grant batch 3 | 2024-10 | no | | Clera (aggregator); [src](https://aigrant.com/) for the batch |\n| Mach9 | not stated | not stated | no | | Clera (aggregator) |\n| Older / non-AI | Aquarium, Triplebyte, Terminal49, Plane, Odys Aviation, BetterBrand, ShareWell, Apres, Pulp Culture, DoseDr | | | | Clera (aggregator) |\n\nChecked and **not** confirmed: Wordware's $30M seed (an aggregator lists him; the company's own post does not name him, [src](https://blog.wordware.ai/seed-round)). Aggregators count 15 to 63 investments in total.\n\n## Hit pattern\n- Seed cheques that became hot: Odyssey (seed 2024, $310M B in 2026), Foxglove.\n- Later-stage entries into hot companies: Etched (A), Imbue (B), Basis (A), Wafer (A).\n- Passes or regrets: nothing found.\n\n## Co-investors\n| Who | Where | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fifty Years | Wafer; also in The Bot Company's seed | [CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/kyle-vogt) (aggregator); ledger |\n| Jeff Dean, Guillermo Rauch, Elad Gil | Wafer and Odyssey B | CB Insights (aggregator) |\n| Fred Ehrsam | Foxglove seed | [src](https://foxglove.dev/blog/foxglove-raises-seed-funding-3-7m) |\n| NFDG / Nat Friedman | Basis A; they also co-led his own company's seed | ledger |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Odyssey:** the CEO sold Voyage to Cruise, so this is a former-acquiree relationship (existing ledger lead, [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026061701/odyssey-raises-310-million-to-accelerate-world-simulation/)).\n- **Cruise (as founder):** Nabeel Hyatt passed on the first pitch, Vogt went quiet for nine months, came back with full self-driving and Spark led the A (existing ledger insights).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Nabeel Hyatt (Spark) | led Cruise A; co-backed The Bot Company | 2014 | ledger |\n| Nat Friedman, Daniel Gross | co-led The Bot Company seed | 2024 | ledger |\n| Greenoaks | led The Bot Company at $2B | 2025-03 | ledger |\n| Paril Jain, Luke Holoubek | co-founders | 2024 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/05/13/cruise-founder-kyle-vogt-is-back-with-a-robot-startup/) |\n| Odyssey's CEO (Cameron) | sold Voyage to Cruise; Vogt later an angel | not dated | ledger |\n\n## Open questions\n- No primary list of his cheques exists. The Hestus, Yuma, Mach9 and Warp entries need company-post confirmation.\n- How do founders reach him? Nothing found; the pattern suggests ex-Cruise and self-driving networks.\n- The No Priors and Uncapped episodes may cover investing; neither has a transcript here.\n\n## Sources\n- https://cheekypint.transistor.fm/3/transcript\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/05/13/cruise-founder-kyle-vogt-is-back-with-a-robot-startup/\n- https://blog.wordware.ai/seed-round\n- https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/kyle-vogt (aggregator)\n- https://www.getclera.com/investors/kyle-vogt (aggregator)\n- Ledger rows in `data/rounds.csv` (each with its own source URL)\n", "person:lachy-groom": "\n# Lachy Groom\n\n_New profile, 2026-11-10. It builds on existing repo rows (turbopuffer, Standard Intelligence, Physical Intelligence, Chai Discovery, Miles Grimshaw, Jill Chase) and adds an SEC filing pass: 36 Form Ds and LGFM's Form ADV._\n\n## One-line take\nThe press calls him a Stripe-alum angel. The SEC filings show a one-person fund complex with about $3.6B of gross assets across 34 vehicles. He co-founded Physical Intelligence and was first money into research bets such as Standard Intelligence and turbopuffer, so he is the clearest example in this repo of a solo GP who prices frontier companies before the brand-name funds ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/301031/PDF/301031.pdf), [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He gets in before anyone else and is useful to the founder. He spent about five years after Stripe looking for a company to start ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/)). That search produced a habit of tracking researchers before they spin out (Levine, Finn and Hausman for Physical Intelligence). Founders choose him over funds:\n  - turbopuffer's founder put him in the top tier for value added ([src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-12-18/turbopuffers-simon-eskildsen-on-adding-thrive-capital-reaching-trillions-of-objects-and-building-a-concentrated-cap-table-like-a-team/transcript)).\n  - By Galen Mead's account, Standard Intelligence got its seed from Groom when \"no one else had the conviction\" ([X](https://x.com/G413N)).\n- **The structure behind it:** a sole-owner adviser (LGFM, LLC; Groom holds 75% or more) runs early funds (LGF I to LGF4), \"Scale\" growth funds and dozens of single-deal SPVs. Fund-of-funds own much of the capital ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/301031/PDF/301031.pdf)).\n- **Constraints:**\n  - His time is now split with running Physical Intelligence.\n  - He has no partners named in any filing.\n  - The SPV-heavy model makes him a concentrated, deal-by-deal allocator rather than a portfolio builder.\n- **What he wouldn't do:** give investors a commercialization timeline. He says so directly: \"I don't give investors answers on commercialization\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/)).\n\n## Differential insight\nRows are mirrored in `data/rows/lachy-groom-insights.csv`.\n- **He is not a \"solo angel investing his own money.\" He runs a $3.6B fund complex.** LGFM, LLC's ADV (annual amendment dated 2026-03-31) lists 34 private funds with about $3.58B of combined gross assets. The largest:\n\n  | Vehicle | Gross assets | Beneficial owners |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | LGF Scale II | $835M | 90 |\n  | LGF Scale I | $734M | 98 |\n  | LGF3 | $375M | 108 |\n  | Old Tree | $328M | 86 |\n  | LGF II | $304M | 82 |\n  | LGF I | $211M (raised ~$48.8M from 44 investors) | 46 |\n  | LGF4 | $210M | 25 |\n\n  Schedule A lists \"Groom, Lachy\" as member and manager since 02/2019, ownership code E (75% or more) ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/301031/PDF/301031.pdf); [LGF I Form D/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1766090/000176609020000001/primary_doc.xml)). Hustle Fund's 2026 article still says he \"doesn't run a traditional fund\" ([Hustle Fund](https://www.hustlefund.vc/post/lachy-groom-investments-how-stripes-former-executive-became-one-of-the-best-angel-investors-in-tech)).\n- **A joint vehicle with Sam Altman.** \"SAM & LACHY INVESTMENTS (H1), L.P.\" filed a Form D on 2020-07-21: an $18M offering with 5 investors, run by LGF Opportunity GP I ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1817239/000181723920000001/primary_doc.xml)). By the 2026 ADV it had 7 beneficial owners, 53% held by fund-of-funds, and $0 gross assets, so it was likely realized or distributed ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/301031/PDF/301031.pdf)). Wired calls Groom \"a friend of OpenAI CEO Sam Altman\" ([Wired mirror](https://wirednews.pages.dev/story/physical-intelligence-ai-robotics-startup/)). The filing turns that into a documented co-investing relationship, which supports the repo's view that OpenAI's Physical Intelligence seed check came through this friendship. Figma's investor-rights exhibit lists \"LGF Scale I, L.P.\" and \"Sam Altman\" next to each other ([Figma S-1 Ex 4.2](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1579878/000162828025033742/exhibit42-sx1.htm)).\n- **His \"solo\" capital is largely institutional fund-of-funds money.** Share owned by fund-of-funds, per the ADV:\n\n  | Vehicle | FoF share | Beneficial owners |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | LGF Melodic Possibility | 100% | 118 |\n  | LGF Fly Nation | 99% | |\n  | LGF Clinking Magnificence | 94% | |\n  | LGF I | 68% | |\n  | Old Tree | 60% | |\n  | LGF Scale II | 50% | |\n  | LGF II | 44% | |\n\n  LGF4 is the exception: 0% FoF, only 25 owners and 30% non-US ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/301031/PDF/301031.pdf)). The FoF names aren't disclosed, but this is the LP pattern THESIS.md asks about: fund-of-funds anchoring a concentrated, one-person AI manager.\n- **SPV-per-deal machine.** Since 2019 he has filed about two dozen single-deal LPs with whimsical two-word names (Fly Nation, Marvelous Wool, Inquisitive Imagination, Enchanted Intelligence, and others), mostly with 1 to 18 investors ([EDGAR full-text search](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Lachy%20Groom%22&forms=D)). Two can be partly decoded:\n  - LGF Marvelous Wool ($4.8M, 3 investors, April 2020) is a Figma holder ([Figma S-1 Ex 4.2](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1579878/000162828025033742/exhibit42-sx1.htm)).\n  - LGF Enchanted Intelligence ($48.35M, 9 investors, June 2022) now carries $120M of gross assets ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1887315/000188731522000001/primary_doc.xml); [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/301031/PDF/301031.pdf)). Its underlying company is not disclosed.\n- **New funds in 2026.** \"Old Scale III, L.P.\" and \"New Grove, L.P.\" each filed a $300M Form D on 2026-07-09, signed by \"Lachlan Groom\" from the same West Hollywood address ([Old Scale III](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2143724/000214372426000001/primary_doc.xml), [New Grove](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2142809/000214280926000001/primary_doc.xml)). Axios reported a pair of $300M funds the next day, one for early-stage deals and one for even earlier ones ([Axios, paywalled; headline and teaser only](https://www.axios.com/pro/fintech-deals/2026/07/10/lachy-groom-new-funds)). The names suggest Old Scale III follows LGF Scale I/II as the growth vehicle. Dropping the \"LGF\" brand is new.\n- **Deal origin of Physical Intelligence.** \"I was looking for five years for the company to go start post-Stripe\". He followed Levine and Finn's research, then went to Karol Hausman when he heard they might start a company ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/)).\n- **He treats investing as a side activity.** He describes himself as \"on vacation much more as an investor\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/)), even while a $600M fund pair is being raised.\n- **SPAC board seat with Pierre Lamond.** On 2020-08-10, the sponsor of the SPAC \"one\" (which later merged with Markforged) transferred 25,000 founder shares each to Groom, Michelle Gill, Gautam Gupta, Trina Spear and Laura de Petra, and 30,000 to Pierre Lamond ([424B4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1816613/000110465920096906/tm2023765-13_424b4.htm)). The filing is under his legal name, \"Groom Lachlan Norman\" ([EDGAR Form 3](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1816613/000110465920096881/)). Lamond is likely the Eclipse partner on the Cerebras deal, but that isn't confirmed (needs-id).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He matches THESIS.md on almost every axis:\n- **First money into research labs:** Standard Intelligence's seed (2024-11), about 17 months before Sequoia and Spark ([X](https://x.com/G413N), [si.inc](https://si.inc/posts/a/)).\n- **Co-founder of a frontier robotics lab:** Physical Intelligence, $5.6B ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/)).\n- **AI infra and AI chips:** sole first investor in turbopuffer (2024-01) and an Etched angel.\n- **Repeat Chai Discovery backer** from the seed through the Series C.\n- **The kind of manager I'd be:** concentrated, SPV-heavy, without rigid ownership discipline, and backed by fund-of-funds.\n\nDeals that matter to me: Physical Intelligence, Standard Intelligence, turbopuffer, Etched, Chai Discovery.\n\n## Background\n- Born in Perth, Western Australia. Learned to code at about 11 and sold online businesses as a teenager. TechCrunch says he sold his first company at 13 ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/); [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lachy_Groom), secondary).\n- Stripe, roughly 2014 to 2018 (dates per Hustle Fund, secondary). Wikipedia says he was employee #30, worked on product, growth and operations, ran Issuing, and led the launches in Singapore, Hong Kong and New Zealand, citing Business Insider ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lachy_Groom)). CapitalG's Jill Chase lists him as an \"early stripe employee\" ([CapitalG](https://capitalg.com/team/jill-greenberg-chase)).\n- **2019-02:** LGFM, LLC formed with Groom as member and manager. First LGF I Form D filed 2019-02-14 with a $30M target; it closed at $48.8M ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/301031/PDF/301031.pdf); [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1766090/000176609019000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **2020:** LGF II ($100M target) and LGF Scale I ($300M target) ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1811471/000181147120000001/primary_doc.xml), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1835376/000183537620000001/primary_doc.xml)). Also a 2020 SPAC board seat (see above).\n- **2021:** LGF3 ($250M target) and LGF Scale II ($500M target) ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1868817/000186881721000001/primary_doc.xml), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1890986/000189098621000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **2022:** LGF4 ($250M target) ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1955041/000195504122000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **2024:** co-founded Physical Intelligence; Thrive led the $70M seed in March 2024 ([Maginative](https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/)). TechCrunch (2026-01) gives his age as 31.\n- **2026-07:** Old Scale III and New Grove, $300M each.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://lachygroom.com | Did not resolve on 2026-11-10 (connection refused); not verified |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/lachygroom | Handle not verified: X returned 402 and syndication was rate-limited on 2026-11-10 |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/lachygroom | Unverified (LinkedIn returned 999 to automated fetch) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n| SEC adviser record | https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/301031/PDF/301031.pdf | LGFM, LLC, CRD 301031, exempt reporting adviser; best primary source on him |\n| Wikipedia | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lachy_Groom | Secondary |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- No long-form podcast with Groom as guest was found. His public voice is mostly quoted press (TechCrunch 2026-01-30; Wired 2024-11-14).\n- 2026-01 — TechCrunch profile, \"A peek inside Physical Intelligence\". Quotes: \"I don't give investors answers on commercialization\"; \"There's no limit to how much money we can really put to work\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/)).\n- 2024-11 — Wired's Physical Intelligence feature. He comes in to discuss the business side and mentions a recent call with Thrive's Josh Kushner ([Wired mirror](https://wirednews.pages.dev/story/physical-intelligence-ai-robotics-startup/); fetched as a summary, so low confidence on exact wording).\n- An X-posted lab interview by Sachin Shah is referenced on a bookmark archive; the original was not retrieved.\n\n## How they think\n- **Research first, revenue later:** he won't give a commercialization timeline, and backs pure research companies (\"It's such a pure company\") ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/)).\n- **Compute-maximalist** at Physical Intelligence: \"There's no limit to how much money we can really put to work\" (same source).\n- **Hardware humility:** \"Hardware is just really hard\" (same source). Physical Intelligence builds models rather than robots. His first robotics investment was Standard Bots in 2021. That is a different company from Standard Intelligence (same source).\n- **Founder-friendly, concentrated cap tables:** turbopuffer has only two outside investors, Groom and Thrive ([Dialectic](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw)).\n- **Calls he got right:**\n  - Figma before its IPO, held through an SPV and LGF Scale I.\n  - Notion, Ramp and Lattice early ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/)).\n  - Standard Intelligence before Sequoia.\n- **Calls he got wrong:** none sourced.\n\n## Read these first\n1. LGFM, LLC Form ADV (2026-03-31): the whole fund complex, its sizes and its FoF ownership ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/301031/PDF/301031.pdf)).\n2. TechCrunch, \"A peek inside Physical Intelligence\" (2026-01-30): his own account of the five-year search and his investor stance ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/)).\n3. turbopuffer founder podcasts: why a founder picked him over funds ([PMF Show](https://turbopuffer.com/blog/podcast-pmf-show-he-built-new-database-bedroom-now-powers-cursor-notion-anthropic), [Latent Space](https://turbopuffer.com/blog/podcast-latent-space)).\n4. Galen Mead on X: the Standard Intelligence seed story ([X](https://x.com/G413N)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed: X was unreachable (402, then rate-limited, on 2026-11-10). John Luttig thanks him as a reviewer of essays in 2023 and 2025 ([Luttig](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent)). This repo has that lead marked needs-id; the context (AI talent essays, overlapping investor circles) makes it very likely the same person.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Physical Intelligence | Co-founder; seed $70M | 2024-03 | co-founder (Thrive led) | Thrive, Khosla, Lux, Sequoia, OpenAI | [Maginative](https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/) |\n| Standard Intelligence | Seed | 2024-11 | yes (founder's account) | not disclosed | [X](https://x.com/G413N) |\n| turbopuffer | First round (sole investor) | 2024-01 | yes | none | [turbopuffer](https://turbopuffer.com/blog/podcast-pmf-show-he-built-new-database-bedroom-now-powers-cursor-notion-anthropic) |\n| turbopuffer | Thrive round (follow-on) | 2025-12 | no | Thrive | [TBPN](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-12-18/turbopuffers-simon-eskildsen-on-adding-thrive-capital-reaching-trillions-of-objects-and-building-a-concentrated-cap-table-like-a-team/transcript) |\n| Chai Discovery | Seed ~$30M; A $70M; B $130M; C $400M | 2024-09 to 2026-07 | no | Thrive, OpenAI, Dimension, Neo and others | [Chai](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement), [BioSpace](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design) |\n| Etched | angel (round undisclosed) | n/a | no | | [Etched](https://www.etched.com/) |\n| Roboflow | listed investor | n/a | | | [Roboflow](https://roboflow.com/about) |\n| Standard Bots | first robotics investment | 2021 | | | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/) |\n| Figma | via LGF Marvelous Wool and LGF Scale I | SPV 2020-04 | no | a16z, Founders Fund, Index, Sam Altman | [Figma S-1 Ex 4.2](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1579878/000162828025033742/exhibit42-sx1.htm) |\n| Blues | first LGF3 investment, co-led with Sequoia (per Wikipedia); prior backer in the A1 | 2022-03; 2023-01 | co-led (secondary) | Sequoia, Positive Sum | [PR Newswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/blues-wireless-raises-32m-to-accelerate-enterprise-adoption-of-cellular-iot-301714865.html) |\n| incident.io | Series A | 2022-07 | no | Index, Point Nine | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/incidentio-raises-342m-to-help-businesses-build-resilience-in-the-face-of-failure/) |\n| Routable | Series B | 2021-04 | no | Sam and Jack Altman (leads) | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2021/04/15/altman-brothers-lead-b2b-payment-startup-routables-30m-series-b/) |\n| Meter | $35M raise with Sam Altman | 2024-02 | unknown | Sam Altman | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/sites/alexkonrad/2024/02/13/meter-raises-35-million-sam-altman-lachy-groom/) (paywalled; headline only) |\n| Notion, Ramp, Lattice | early | n/a | | | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Physical Intelligence:**\n  - **Origin:** a five-year search for a company to found after Stripe. He followed Sergey Levine and Chelsea Finn's lab work, heard rumours that they might start something, and found Karol Hausman ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/)).\n  - **Funding path:** Thrive (Josh Kushner) led the seed. OpenAI's check is framed as a talent bet, and Wired calls Groom an Altman friend. CapitalG's growth lead says it has watched and backed Groom for years, a link through Stripe ([CapitalG](https://capitalg.com/insights/physical-intelligence-bringing-general-purpose-ai-into-the-physical-world/)).\n  - **Pattern:** every major investor relationship runs through Groom, not the research CEO.\n- **Standard Intelligence:** per founder Galen Mead, Groom called on Thanksgiving Day 2024 and offered to lead the seed. The company had weeks of runway and no other investor would back a pure research bet. Sequoia and Spark led a $75M A on 2026-04-30 ([si.inc](https://si.inc/posts/a/)). si.inc's own announcement does not name Groom. The claim rests on Mead's X post, whose status URL was not captured: medium confidence.\n- **turbopuffer:** the founders ran on personal credit cards, then raised once in January 2024 from Groom alone. Eskildsen recalls asking \"can we raise like 700k?\" ([turbopuffer](https://turbopuffer.com/blog/video-ai-engineer-pragmatic-engineer)). Groom offered help with candidates and customers rather than database expertise. Thrive joined about 23 months later.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sam Altman | Joint SPV \"Sam & Lachy Investments (H1)\"; co-investor (Figma, Routable, Meter); Wired calls them friends | 2020 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1817239/000181723920000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Karol Hausman, Sergey Levine, Chelsea Finn | Physical Intelligence co-founders | 2024 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/) |\n| Josh Kushner / Thrive Capital | PI seed lead; co-investor in turbopuffer and Chai | 2024 | [Wired mirror](https://wirednews.pages.dev/story/physical-intelligence-ai-robotics-startup/) |\n| Miles Grimshaw (Thrive) | only other outside investor in turbopuffer | 2025 | [Dialectic](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw) |\n| Jill Chase / CapitalG | Physical Intelligence Series B lead; lists Groom as a founder she partners with | 2025 | [CapitalG](https://capitalg.com/team/jill-greenberg-chase) |\n| Galen Mead (Standard Intelligence) | seed lead | 2024-11 | [X](https://x.com/G413N) |\n| Simon Eskildsen (turbopuffer) | sole first investor | 2024-01 | [turbopuffer](https://turbopuffer.com/blog/podcast-pmf-show-he-built-new-database-bedroom-now-powers-cursor-notion-anthropic) |\n| Pierre Lamond, Michelle Gill, Gautam Gupta, Trina Spear, Laura de Petra | fellow founder-share recipients on SPAC \"one\" (Markforged) | 2020-08 | [424B4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1816613/000110465920096906/tm2023765-13_424b4.htm) |\n| Stripe | early employee (around #30), head of Issuing | ~2014-2018 | [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lachy_Groom) (secondary) |\n| John Luttig | thanked as a reviewer of his essays | 2023 | [Luttig](https://blog.johnluttig.com/p/hypercapitalism-and-the-ai-talent) |\n| Frank, Rimerman + Co. | auditor of the LGF funds | n/a | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/301031/PDF/301031.pdf) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Who are the fund-of-funds LPs?** FoF owners hold 44 to 100% of several vehicles. Checked: the ADV (percentages only), EDGAR full-text search for \"LGF3\", \"LGF Scale\", \"LGF4\" and \"Old Tree\" (the \"LGF3\" hits in Stone Ridge N-PORTs are loan IDs, not LGF), and Figma's exhibit. Next: Cendana, StepStone, Industry Ventures and Sapphire manager lists, and public pension tables.\n- **Old Tree, L.P.** ($328M gross assets, 86 owners, 60% FoF): no Form D surfaced under his name. Is it a continuation vehicle or an unannounced fund?\n- **Which companies sit inside LGF Enchanted Intelligence** ($120M GAV), LGF Simple Trapeze ($135M) and LGF Inquisitive Imagination ($108M)? These are the big SPV winners.\n- What did Sam & Lachy Investments (H1) buy, and when was it realized?\n- Status URL for Galen Mead's Thanksgiving post, and the Standard Intelligence seed amount.\n- Full Axios piece: the LP makeup and close of Old Scale III and New Grove.\n- Groom's X handle and posting patterns (unreachable this run).\n- Confirm that the Pierre Lamond on the SPAC is the Eclipse partner.\n\n## Sources\n- LGFM, LLC Form ADV (CRD 301031), 2026-03-31: https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/301031/PDF/301031.pdf\n- EDGAR full-text search, Form Ds naming \"Lachy Groom\": https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Lachy%20Groom%22&forms=D\n- Form Ds:\n  - LGF I: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1766090/000176609020000001/primary_doc.xml\n  - LGF II: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1811471/000181147120000001/primary_doc.xml\n  - LGF Scale I: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1835376/000183537620000001/primary_doc.xml\n  - LGF3: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1868817/000186881721000001/primary_doc.xml\n  - LGF Scale II: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1890986/000189098621000001/primary_doc.xml\n  - LGF4: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1955041/000195504122000001/primary_doc.xml\n  - Sam & Lachy (H1): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1817239/000181723920000001/primary_doc.xml\n  - Enchanted Intelligence: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1887315/000188731522000001/primary_doc.xml\n  - Old Scale III: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2143724/000214372426000001/primary_doc.xml\n  - New Grove: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2142809/000214280926000001/primary_doc.xml\n- Figma S-1 Exhibit 4.2: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1579878/000162828025033742/exhibit42-sx1.htm\n- \"one\" SPAC 424B4: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1816613/000110465920096906/tm2023765-13_424b4.htm\n- TechCrunch 2026-01-30: https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/30/physical-intelligence-stripe-veteran-lachy-grooms-latest-bet-is-building-silicon-valleys-buzziest-robot-brains/\n- Wired (mirror) 2024-11-14: https://wirednews.pages.dev/story/physical-intelligence-ai-robotics-startup/\n- Axios 2026-07-10 (paywalled): https://www.axios.com/pro/fintech-deals/2026/07/10/lachy-groom-new-funds\n- Wikipedia (secondary): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lachy_Groom\n- Hustle Fund (secondary, contradicted by filings): https://www.hustlefund.vc/post/lachy-groom-investments-how-stripes-former-executive-became-one-of-the-best-angel-investors-in-tech\n- Galen Mead on X: https://x.com/G413N\n- si.inc Series A: https://si.inc/posts/a/\n- Dialectic (Grimshaw): https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw\n- turbopuffer podcasts:\n  - https://turbopuffer.com/blog/podcast-pmf-show-he-built-new-database-bedroom-now-powers-cursor-notion-anthropic\n  - https://turbopuffer.com/blog/podcast-latent-space\n  - https://turbopuffer.com/blog/video-ai-engineer-pragmatic-engineer\n- CapitalG:\n  - https://capitalg.com/team/jill-greenberg-chase\n  - https://capitalg.com/insights/physical-intelligence-bringing-general-purpose-ai-into-the-physical-world/\n- Maginative (PI seed): https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/\n", "person:lan-jiang": "\n# Lan Jiang\n\n## One-line take\nA young Lux partner whose name sits on three of Lux's highest-value AI cards: Together AI (2023), Physical Intelligence (2024) and Applied Compute (2025). Applied Compute was valued at $3.25B on 2026-11-02 ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies), [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise)). Treat her as a deal-team member behind Brandon Reeves, not a confirmed lead. Her public voice is close to zero.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she could win.** She is a technical generalist: a Stanford CS degree in two and a half years ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang)), a TreeHacks co-director, a Dorm Room Fund managing partner, and seven-plus years of hands-on research ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang)). That profile lets her work AI-infrastructure deals alongside Brandon Reeves, Lux's AI lead. Every one of her three AI cards is co-listed with Reeves ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies)).\n- **What constrains her.** Lux's AI book is run by Reeves (and Grace Isford), and every non-founder investor at Lux holds the same \"Partner\" title ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people)). So the title doesn't tell you seniority, and no announcement, press release or company blog names her on any deal. Her attribution rests on Lux website markup alone.\n- **What she would never do.** Nothing found. No source records a stated rule, pass or anti-thesis.\n\n## Differential insight\nSearch budget for this run was exhausted before any web search ran, so no podcasts, Substack, X posts or talks were found. Every item below comes from direct page fetches. Nothing here is her own voice.\n- **Attribution is markup, and Lux's own pages disagree.** The companies index links her to Physical Intelligence (2024, with Reeves), Together.xyz (2023, with Reeves and Isford) and Applied Compute (2025, with Reeves) ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies)). Applied Compute's own Lux page lists no partners, but its card in the \"Similar companies\" sections of the Together and Physical Intelligence pages lists Reeves and Jiang ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/applied-compute), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/together)). Low certainty on her role in any one deal.\n- **Applied Compute timing (Lux was early, not first and not lead).** The founders' Oct-2025 launch post lists Lux third among backers: \"backed by $80m in funding from Benchmark, Sequoia, Lux\" ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific)). Lux came back on 2026-04-08: Kleiner Perkins led $80M at a $1.3B post-money, \"with continued participation from Elad Gil, Lux, Greenoaks, Neo, Hanabi\" ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/fundraise)). Lux came back again on 2026-11-02: Elad Gil led $350M at $3.25B, with Lux among the participants: \"Applied Compute hits $100M run rate and raises $350M\" ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise)). Roughly 13 months took it from launch to a $3.25B valuation, with Lux in all three disclosed rounds.\n- **Together: Lux was first in and led.** On Together's $20M seed: \"The round was led by Lux Capital\" (2023-05-15) ([src](https://siliconangle.com/2023/05/15/together-raises-20m-decentralize-open-source-ai-model-creation)). The announcement names no Lux person.\n- **Physical Intelligence: Lux followed Thrive.** Thrive led the $70M seed (2024-03-12), with Khosla, Lux, OpenAI and Sequoia in the round. That comes from an aggregator, and it names no Lux person ([src](https://startupintros.com/news/2024-03-12-physical-intelligence-seed)). Lux's bio pages still count PI among its \"first investor\" companies ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang)), which fits participating in the first round without leading it.\n- **Stated focus is broader than AI.** \"Her interests span computational analytics in biotech and healthcare\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang)). The page also lists data and ML infrastructure, platform therapeutics, developer tooling and AI applications.\n- **DRF network.** At Dorm Room Fund \"she founded the Female Investors’ Track\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang)), and she also led the Female Founders' Track. DRF now lists her in its alumni showcase as a Lux partner ([src](https://dormroomfund.com/our-team)). That makes her a likely route into DRF's student-founder pipeline (inference).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The companies are high fit, but her personal role is unconfirmed.\n- Applied Compute (custom enterprise models and agent workforces) is AI-native automation of the kind I like.\n- Physical Intelligence is a frontier robotics-foundation-model lab whose seed was a very large one.\n- Together AI is open-model compute and infrastructure.\n\nPulling the other way: no source names her as lead, board member or sponsor. She has no public writing or posts to learn from, and Lux participates rather than leads in the later Applied Compute and PI rounds. She is useful as a junior contact into Lux's AI deal team and as a possible co-investor signal. The higher-leverage Lux contact is still Reeves.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford, BS Computer Science, finished in two and a half years; the page says top of class and Phi Beta Kappa. Years are not stated ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang)).\n- At Stanford: co-director of TreeHacks; Managing Partner of Dorm Room Fund, where she founded the Female Investors' Track and led the Female Founders' Track ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang)).\n- Seven-plus years of research: glioblastoma segmentation, nanocomposite fluid-flow simulation, and Alzheimer's drug therapies. No institutions or dates are given ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang)).\n- Lux Capital, Partner (Investing). Start date not found ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people)). Her earliest linked card is Together (Lux investment 2023) ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/together)), so she has likely been at Lux since 2023 or earlier (inference).\n- Pronouns: the Lux bio uses she/her ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang | Lux bio, 2026-11-09 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Lux news index shows no posts by her (2026-11-09) ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news)) |\n| X | nothing found | Lux bio's X link is a placeholder \"#\" |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/lanjiang9 | Linked from Lux bio; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- nothing found. The Lux news and podcast index shows no episode featuring her (checked 2026-11-09) ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news)). Web search was unavailable this run, so external podcasts were not checked.\n\n## How they think\n- Stated theses (firm bio only): computational analytics in bio and health, data and ML infrastructure, platform therapeutics, developer tooling, and AI applications ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang)).\n- Revealed pattern (inference from her card links): model-builder infrastructure. That means open-model cloud (Together), a robot foundation model (PI), and post-training and RL for enterprise-specific models (Applied Compute).\n- Contrarian views: nothing found.\n- Calls right or wrong: all three linked AI companies were marked up sharply afterwards. Applied Compute went from $1.3B to $3.25B in seven months ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/fundraise), [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise)). Her personal role in these calls is unconfirmed.\n\n## Read these first\n- Lux bio: the only first-party description of her focus ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang)).\n- Applied Compute, \"It's time to get specific\" (2025-10-29): the thesis of the company most linked to her recent work ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific)).\n- Applied Compute, \"Moats Need Models\" (2026-06-10): listed on the company blog but not read here, so its content is not summarized ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/blog)).\n\nNothing written by her was found.\n\n## Social engagement\nnothing found (no X handle identified; LinkedIn not fetchable).\n\n## Portfolio\nAttribution to her comes from Lux site markup only. No announcement names her.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Together AI | Seed, $20M | 2023-05-15 | Yes (Lux) | Factory, SV Angel, First Round, Long Journey, A Capital, Robot Ventures, Common Metal, Definition, Susa, Cadenza | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2023/05/15/together-raises-20m-decentralize-open-source-ai-model-creation), [card](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/together) |\n| Physical Intelligence | Seed, $70M | 2024-03-12 | No (Thrive led) | Thrive, Khosla, OpenAI, Sequoia | [src (aggregator)](https://startupintros.com/news/2024-03-12-physical-intelligence-seed), [card](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/physical-intelligence) |\n| Applied Compute | Early funding (total $80M disclosed) | by 2025-10-29 | Not stated | Benchmark, Sequoia, Hanabi, Neo, Definition, Elad Gil, Victor Lazarte, Omri Casspi | [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific), [card](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies) |\n| Applied Compute | $80M at $1.3B post | 2026-04-08 | No (Kleiner Perkins led) | Elad Gil, Greenoaks, Neo, Hanabi | [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/fundraise) |\n| Applied Compute | $350M at $3.25B | 2026-11-02 | No (Elad Gil led) | Kleiner Perkins, Hanabi, NVIDIA, Blackstone, Shopify Ventures, HubSpot Ventures, Diffusion, Definition, Timeless, Layer Global, Neo, Abstract, Nova, Quantum Light, First Harmonic, Swish | [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise) |\n\n## Deal stories\nnothing found. No source describes how she met any founder or what her role was. The Applied Compute sequence is the most useful timing signal. Benchmark and Sequoia are named ahead of Lux in the launch post. Lux then kept following through the Kleiner-led and Elad Gil-led rounds ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific), [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/fundraise), [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lux Capital | Partner (Investing) | not found | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang) |\n| Brandon Reeves (Lux) | Co-listed on Together, PI and Applied Compute cards | 2023 (earliest card) | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies) |\n| Grace Isford (Lux) | Co-listed on Together card | 2023 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/together) |\n| Dorm Room Fund | Former Managing Partner; listed alumna | not found | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang), [src](https://dormroomfund.com/our-team) |\n| TreeHacks (Stanford) | Former co-director | not found | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is she on any board or board-observer seat (PI, Together, Applied Compute)? Check the Delaware/company filings and LinkedIn.\n- Who sourced Applied Compute for Lux, and was Lux in the earliest round alongside Benchmark? Lux's card says the investment year is 2025.\n- Does she have an X account or any podcast appearances? Rerun web search, which was unavailable this run.\n- Which bio and therapeutics companies is she on? Her stated focus includes them, but no bio card links her.\n- Her graduation year and Lux start date, which would show how fast she reached Partner.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/people/lan-jiang\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/people\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/together\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/physical-intelligence\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/applied-compute\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news\n- https://dormroomfund.com/our-team\n- https://siliconangle.com/2023/05/15/together-raises-20m-decentralize-open-source-ai-model-creation\n- https://startupintros.com/news/2024-03-12-physical-intelligence-seed (aggregator)\n- https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific\n- https://appliedcompute.com/company/fundraise\n- https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise\n", "person:lan-xuezhao": "\n# Lan Xuezhao\n\n## One-line take\nThe single decision-maker at Basis Set: an AI-only seed investor since 2017 whose fund shows up quietly in Sakana, CuspAI, Norm and, by its own listing, Thinking Machines.\n\n## Crux\nShe has been AI-only for nine years and controls every fund vehicle herself, so she can commit alone and early. She keeps funds small on purpose, which LPs reward and which limits her to a participant's share in lab rounds. Her identity is anti-consensus; she would not follow a brand-name lead for validation.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Stated creed: \"We are not consensus driven or optimized for comfort.\" ([Fund IV letter, 2026-01-26](https://basisset.com/insights/announcing-our-250m-fund-iv)).\n- To founders: \"If you just want validation, you will make our lives too easy.\" (same source).\n- Fund size is a choice: \"We're pretty careful with consistency and discipline on fund size,\" ([Newcomer, 2024-02-01](https://www.newcomer.co/p/exclusive-basis-set-raises-185-million)).\n- Her LP's view: \"She runs it like a company,\" said Foundry's Jaclyn Hester (same source).\n- 2017, before AI was a label people paid for: \"AI is not the goal, it's something that solves a problem.\" ([TechCrunch, 2017-08-31](https://techcrunch.com/2017/08/31/basis-set-ventures-gets-real-to-get-to-the-heart-of-ai-startups)).\n- The Fund I Form D names a second related person, Zhijian Peng, who never appears again ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1703747/000170374717000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- Scale AI was a personal seed cheque in 2016, before the fund existed ([bio](https://www.basisset.com/team-members/dr-lan-xuezhao)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. A concentrated, emerging-style AI seed manager who backs new labs (Sakana, Cusp, Thinking Machines by her own listing) and automation companies (Mem0, Norm), with LPs (Foundry, Pivotal) that back small AI funds. Useful both as a co-investor and as a model for what LPs say they like.\n\n## Background\nMA statistics and PhD psychology, University of Michigan; published brain-modelling research; founded and exited a brain-training company; McKinsey; joined Dropbox in 2013 and built its corporate development strategy team; first seed investment in Scale in 2016; founded Basis Set in 2017 ([src](https://www.basisset.com/team-members/dr-lan-xuezhao)). Board member of Michigan's School of Information (same source).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.basisset.com/team-members/dr-lan-xuezhao | firm bio |\n| Substack / blog | https://basisset.com/insights/announcing-our-250m-fund-iv | firm Insights; she signs fund letters |\n| X | https://twitter.com/xuezhao | not fetched |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/lanxuezhao/ | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | co-hosted one Origins episode |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-07 — The Data Minute (Carta) — The Case Against Obvious Deals — [link](https://thedataminute.buzzsprout.com/2306470/episodes/19536280-the-case-against-obvious-deals-lan-xuezhao-founder-managing-partner-basis-set-ventures) — show notes only: non-pedigree founders, \"speed of learning\", LPs since Fund I, recycling. Not mined (no transcript).\n- 2021-07 — Venture Unlocked — data science and psychology in deals, LP/GP fit — [link](https://ventureunlocked.substack.com/p/lanxuezhao) — show notes only\n- undated — Origins (Notation) — Investing in AI — [link](https://chartable.com/podcasts/origins-a-podcast-about-limited-partners-created-by-notation-capital/episodes/137328828-investing-in-ai-nick-beezer-cohost-dr-lan-xuezhao) — not mined\n- undated — Heavybit Venture Confidential Ep. 21 — [link](https://www.heavybit.com/library/podcasts/venture-confidential/ep-21-feat-lan-xuezhao-of-basis-set-ventures) — not mined\n\n## How they think\n- Commit before the category exists; do not borrow conviction.\n- Measure founders (psychometrics, \"Founder Superpowers\") and use software (Pascal) to find them outside the usual networks.\n- Keep the fund small and the team technical.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Fund IV letter](https://basisset.com/insights/announcing-our-250m-fund-iv): her creed in her own words.\n- [Newcomer on Fund III](https://www.newcomer.co/p/exclusive-basis-set-raises-185-million): LPs and fund-size discipline.\n- [TechCrunch 2017](https://techcrunch.com/2017/08/31/basis-set-ventures-gets-real-to-get-to-the-heart-of-ai-startups): the original thesis and cheque size.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed (X not fetched).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Scale AI | Seed (personal) | 2016 | no | | [src](https://www.basisset.com/team-members/dr-lan-xuezhao) |\n| Sakana AI | Founding seed ($30M) | 2024-01-16 | no | Lux, Khosla, NTT, KDDI, Sony | ledger |\n| Norm AI | Seed ($11.1M) | 2024-01-23 | no | | ledger |\n| CuspAI | Seed ($30M), board | 2024-06-18 | no | Hoxton (lead), Lightspeed | ledger |\n| Mem0 | Series A ($20M) | 2025-10-28 | yes | Kindred, Peak XV, GitHub Fund, YC | ledger |\n| Thinking Machines | not stated | not stated | no | | [src](https://www.basisset.com/portfolio) |\n| Others | see [firm page](../firms/basis-set-ventures.md) | | | | |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found on how any single deal happened. The Carta show notes say she backed Quince as a supply-chain company, not a consumer brand, and Scale before the fund existed; no detail beyond that.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Foundry (Jaclyn Hester) | LP | by 2023 | [src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/exclusive-basis-set-raises-185-million) |\n| Pivotal Ventures | LP | by 2023 | [src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/exclusive-basis-set-raises-185-million) |\n| Wilson Kyi | only other investing partner | not published | [src](https://www.basisset.com/team) |\n| Zhijian Peng | co-related person on Fund I filing | 2017 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1703747/000170374717000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n## Open questions\n- How did she get into Sakana, CuspAI and Thinking Machines? No source found.\n- Who is Zhijian Peng?\n- What are the \"Set Builders\" vehicles?\n- Transcribe the 2026 Carta episode; it is the most recent long interview.\n\n## Sources\nSee [firms/basis-set-ventures.md](../firms/basis-set-ventures.md); all links above.\n", "person:lauren-reeder": "\n# Lauren Reeder\n\n## One-line take\nSequoia's seed partner for the LLM infrastructure stack. She uses the Open Source Fellowship and pull from portfolio engineering teams to reach inference and post-training talent (vLLM, Trajectory, Sail) before they form companies. [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/lauren-reeder/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** she is a developer-tools product person (Segment, Twilio) who sources from what Sequoia's portfolio engineers actually use. With Bogomil Balkansky she co-launched the no-strings Open Source Fellowship in 2023, which lets her befriend key maintainers years before a company exists. FastAPI went from inaugural fellow (2023) to a Sequoia-led seed (2025). vLLM went from fellow (2024) to Inferact (2025). [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/building-the-future-meet-the-2024-sequoia-open-source-fellows/) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-fastapi-labs-simplified-app-deployment/)\n- **What constrains her:** early access does not guarantee the lead. On Inferact, a16z and Lightspeed led the $150M round at $800M and Sequoia only participated. [src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference) On the largest AI deals she co-partners with senior partners (Grady on Trajectory, Lin on Firetiger, Buhler on XBOW and Sail). Arc, the funnel that brought her XBOW, has been shut down. [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)\n- **What she'd never do (inferred):** she has no record of backing a pre-training lab or a chip company. Her lane is the layer above models: inference, post-training, agent runtime, dev tooling and AI-native automation.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **vLLM origin story.** By spring 2024 several Sequoia portfolio companies were praising vLLM, so she cold-messaged on X and Simon Mo invited her to the lab: \"Reeder reached out on X, and Mo invited her\". [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/building-the-future-meet-the-2024-sequoia-open-source-fellows/) This was about 18 months before Inferact's $150M launch round. [src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference)\n- **Screen for open source:** community-run rather than one maintainer, \"But this was absolutely community-run\" (nearly 80% of vLLM contributions came from outside the lab). [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/building-the-future-meet-the-2024-sequoia-open-source-fellows/)\n- **Neutrality as a criterion (Chatbot Arena):** \"committed to being fundamentally neutral was actually really important to us\". [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/building-the-future-meet-the-2024-sequoia-open-source-fellows/)\n- **Fellowship to seed:** Sebastián Ramírez was \"the inaugural recipient of our Open Source Fellowship\" before Sequoia led FastAPI Labs' seed. [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-fastapi-labs-simplified-app-deployment/)\n- **XBOW came in through Arc.** Oege de Moor spoke at an Arc story slam and Sequoia was \"grateful for the chance to lead their seed round\" (with Buhler, 2024-07-30). [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-xbow-the-gold-standard-in-offensive-security/)\n- **Compute pricing view (Sail):** she and Buhler quote Sail's mantra, \"there are no bad chips, only bad prices\"; Sequoia was a partner \"from day 0\". [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sail-agents-without-limits/)\n- **Trajectory pricing:** \"our $45M Series A led by Sequoia at a $300M valuation\" (2026-11-02); $60M total across seed and A. [src](https://trajectory.ai/field-notes/introducing-the-trajectory-platform)\n- **Thesis line:** \"the next generation of giants is being created now\" (LLM infra stack). [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/lauren-reeder/)\n- I found no podcast, Substack or X posts by her in this pass because the web-search budget was exhausted. All quotes come from Sequoia, company and press pages.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** She backs AI infrastructure and AI-native automation at inception or seed, which overlaps the lens directly. The deals that matter to me:\n- **Trajectory**: post-training and continual learning. The likely co-investor entry point is Conviction, which may have been in before Sequoia (unconfirmed).\n- **Inferact**: vLLM; inference talent.\n- **Sail Research**: agent inference and compute-price arbitrage.\n- **XBOW**: AI-native offensive security automation.\n- **Firetiger**: agentic observability, exited 2026.\n\nShe is a sourcing node for open-source inference talent, and she is often not the round lead (Inferact). That leaves room for a $20M check alongside her. She does not do pre-training labs or chips, so she is medium for that part of the lens.\n\n## Background\n- Software engineer building online exam software in college. [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/lauren-reeder/)\n- Software engineer and product leader at Segment and Twilio. Dates not found. [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/lauren-reeder/)\n- Partner on Sequoia's seed/early-stage team. Start date not found; her earliest listed partnership is Stainless in 2022. [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/stainless/)\n- Education: nothing found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/lauren-reeder/ | Firm bio; email listed |\n| Substack / blog | Sequoia articles (XBOW, Sail, FastAPI, Firetiger, Juicebox) | Partnership posts, 2024–2026 |\n| X | nothing found (she used X to reach vLLM, per Sequoia) | handle not confirmed |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found (search budget exhausted; check Sequoia Training Data and open-source/infra podcasts next).\n\n## How they think\n- The LLM infrastructure stack is settling and the next infra giants are being created now. Work with AI starts in coding and spreads to other roles. [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/lauren-reeder/)\n- Open-source pull from the portfolio is a leading indicator of a company. Fund the maintainers early with no strings attached. [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/building-the-future-meet-the-2024-sequoia-open-source-fellows/)\n- Software development is a series of shifting bottlenecks: \"You remove one bottleneck, only to discover the next.\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-fastapi-labs-simplified-app-deployment/)\n- Agents running for hours and days need new inference infrastructure (Sail). Ops work can be done by always-on agents (Firetiger). [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sail-agents-without-limits/) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-firetiger-validation-at-the-speed-of-ai/)\n- Calls so far: XBOW went from seed (2024) to a valuation over $1B (2026). Stainless, Firetiger and Pierre are listed as acquired in 2026, with acquirers not named. Inferact's lead went to other firms.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Meet the 2024 Sequoia Open Source Fellows](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/building-the-future-meet-the-2024-sequoia-open-source-fellows/): the vLLM sourcing story in her own words.\n- [Partnering with Sail: Agents Without Limits](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sail-agents-without-limits/): her inference and compute thesis.\n- [Partnering with XBOW](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-xbow-the-gold-standard-in-offensive-security/): the Arc seed.\n- [Partnering with FastAPI Labs](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-fastapi-labs-simplified-app-deployment/): the fellowship-to-seed path.\n- [Partnering with Firetiger](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-firetiger-validation-at-the-speed-of-ai/): AI-native ops automation.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. Her X account is unconfirmed, though Sequoia says she used X for outreach to vLLM.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Stainless | Seed ($3.5M); Sequoia partnered 2022 | 2024-04-24 | unknown | The General Partnership | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/24/stainless-is-helping-openai-anthropic-and-others-build-sdks-for-their-apis/) |\n| XBOW | Seed | 2024-07-30 | yes (Sequoia, with Buhler) | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-xbow-the-gold-standard-in-offensive-security/) |\n| XBOW | Series C $120M (existing investor) | 2026-03-18 | no (DFJ Growth, Northzone) | Sofina, Alkeon, Altimeter, NFDG | [src](https://xbow.com/news/xbow-raises-120m-to-scale) |\n| Enter | Pre-Seed/Seed (per site) | 2024 | unknown | Buhler co-partner | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/enter/) |\n| Pierre Computer Company | unknown | 2024 | unknown | Andrew Reed co-partner | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/pierre/) |\n| FastAPI Labs | Seed | 2025-05-05 | yes (Sequoia) | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-fastapi-labs-simplified-app-deployment/) |\n| Juicebox | Series A | 2025-09-25 | yes (Sequoia) | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/why-were-partnering-with-juicebox-the-recruiting-platform-founders-are-obsessed-with/) |\n| Firetiger | unknown | 2025 | unknown | Alfred Lin co-partner | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/firetiger/) |\n| Sail Research | from day 0 | 2025 | unknown | Buhler co-partner | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sail-agents-without-limits/) |\n| Inferact | $150M at $800M (round unnamed) | 2026-01 | no (a16z, Lightspeed) | Altimeter, Redpoint, ZhenFund, others | [src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference) |\n| Trajectory | Series A $45M at $300M | 2026-11-02 | yes (Sequoia, with Grady) | NVIDIA, Bessemer, Bain Capital Ventures | [src](https://trajectory.ai/field-notes/introducing-the-trajectory-platform) |\n| Anthropic, Deno, Statsig, Pace | listed on bio | | | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/lauren-reeder/) |\n\nPace's company page names only Jess Lee as partner, so Reeder's role there is likely a board or later involvement. [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/pace/)\n\n## Deal stories\n- **vLLM to Inferact:** portfolio engineers praised vLLM, so she reached out cold on X and visited the lab, where she met Mo, Kwon and Li. vLLM joined the 2024 Open Source Fellowship. Inferact was founded and partnered in 2025, then raised $150M led by a16z and Lightspeed in January 2026 with Sequoia participating. [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/building-the-future-meet-the-2024-sequoia-open-source-fellows/) [src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference)\n- **XBOW:** Oege de Moor spoke at the Arc story slam and was invited into Arc. Reeder and Buhler led the seed in 2024. By March 2026 XBOW had raised a $120M Series C led by DFJ Growth and Northzone at a valuation over $1B, with Sequoia as an existing investor. [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-xbow-the-gold-standard-in-offensive-security/) [src](https://xbow.com/news/xbow-raises-120m-to-scale)\n- **FastAPI Labs:** Ramírez was the inaugural 2023 fellow. Sequoia led the seed in 2025. [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-fastapi-labs-simplified-app-deployment/)\n- **Trajectory:** founded 2025 by Ronak Malde, Arjun Karanam and Michael Elabd. Reeder and Grady partnered in 2026, and Sequoia led the $45M Series A at $300M. Conviction, NVIDIA and others are listed as backers. Who led the seed is not confirmed. [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/trajectory/) [src](https://trajectory.ai)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Konstantine Buhler | Co-partner: XBOW, Enter, Sail | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/sail-research/) |\n| Bogomil Balkansky | Co-launched Open Source Fellowship; FastAPI co-partner | 2023 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/building-the-future-meet-the-2024-sequoia-open-source-fellows/) |\n| Pat Grady | Co-partner: Trajectory | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/trajectory/) |\n| Alfred Lin | Co-partner: Firetiger | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/firetiger/) |\n| Andrew Reed | Co-partner: Pierre | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/pierre/) |\n| David Cahn, Abhishek Malani, Brian Halligan | Co-partners: Juicebox | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/juicebox/) |\n| Simon Mo, Woosuk Kwon, Zhuohan Li | vLLM fellows, then Inferact (Mo, Kwon) | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/building-the-future-meet-the-2024-sequoia-open-source-fellows/) |\n| Oege de Moor | XBOW founder | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-xbow-the-gold-standard-in-offensive-security/) |\n| Sebastián Ramírez | Fellow, then FastAPI Labs seed | 2023 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-fastapi-labs-simplified-app-deployment/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who led Trajectory's seed (about $15M implied by $60M total less the $45M A)? Was it Conviction?\n- Is Trajectory the same company as the \"Trajectory Labs\" in the sonali-singh leads, and is Arjun Karanam the \"QuantumArjun\" handle?\n- What is the Open Source Fellowship's grant size? Who are the 2025 and 2026 fellows (the next Inferact)?\n- What is her X handle, and has she appeared on any podcasts (Training Data or others)?\n- Who acquired Stainless, Firetiger and Pierre in 2026?\n- XBOW's Series A and B leads and timing. Altimeter appears as an existing investor, so it was possibly an earlier lead.\n- With Arc and Scouts shut down, does her inception sourcing shift entirely to the fellowship and portfolio pull?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/lauren-reeder/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/building-the-future-meet-the-2024-sequoia-open-source-fellows/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-fastapi-labs-simplified-app-deployment/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-xbow-the-gold-standard-in-offensive-security/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sail-agents-without-limits/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-firetiger-validation-at-the-speed-of-ai/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/why-were-partnering-with-juicebox-the-recruiting-platform-founders-are-obsessed-with/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/trajectory/ , /inferact/ , /sail-research/ , /enter/ , /fastapi-labs/ , /stainless/ , /firetiger/ , /pierre/ , /xbow/ , /juicebox/ , /pace/\n- https://trajectory.ai and https://trajectory.ai/field-notes/introducing-the-trajectory-platform\n- https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference\n- https://xbow.com/news/xbow-raises-120m-to-scale\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/24/stainless-is-helping-openai-anthropic-and-others-build-sdks-for-their-apis/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/ (via sequoia-capital rows)\n", "person:lee-fixel": "\n# Lee Fixel\n\n> Update 2026-11-10 (photo): headshot from his WashU IMC board profile, uploaded 2026-10-26 ([endowment.wustl.edu](https://endowment.wustl.edu/people/lee-fixel-bsba-02/)); that bio calls Addition focused on \"early and growth stage technology companies\".\n> Update 2026-11-10: added Render deal timing (first meeting Feb 2020, Addition-led Series A), Fixel's two-question unit-economics test in the founder's own words, and Snorkel's 2026 Series E. Web search was unavailable this run (budget exhausted), so the additions come from direct fetches of company posts.\n\n## One-line take\nThe former head of Tiger Global's private equity business, now sole decision-maker at Addition. He matters to me less as a fund I'd copy and more as the most important outside node in WashU's investment network: an alumnus, a donor, a director on the WashU IMC board and the manager its CIO rates highest.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** pricing judgement applied across stages. Scott Wilson, WashU's CIO, sums up the edge as \"disciplined around price, but will pay up for super high-quality assets\", plus underwriting that dissects business models and management teams. He also says Fixel steps away \"when things get too f- frothy\" ([20VC, ~00:51–52 and ~00:36](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026); Wilson speaking, not Fixel). The track record behind that reputation: the early Flipkart bet and Spotify at Tiger ([ai-cio, 2019](https://www.ai-cio.com/news/tiger-private-equity-head-exit-june/)), and Tiger's lead of Peloton's 2014 Series B ([TechCrunch, 2019-03-14](https://techcrunch.com/2019/03/14/lee-fixel-is-leaving-tiger-global/)).\n- **What constrains him:** Addition raises $1.3–1.5B per vintage, with about $7B AUM by 2022 ([Wikipedia, secondary](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Addition_(investment_firm))). Wikipedia describes one partner with final authority, so deal flow is bottlenecked on him. He has almost no public voice: I found no podcast, Substack, X account or talk of his own.\n- **What he'd never do:** by Wilson's account, chase rounds he thinks are frothy. No source has Fixel saying this himself. Treat it as a second-hand trait, not a stated rule.\n\n## Differential insight\nThe honest finding: **Fixel has essentially no primary public surface.** Everything below is third parties describing him. The new material is the 2026-11-08 20VC episode, where WashU's CIO talks about him at length.\n\n- **WashU's CIO calls him the top manager in the portfolio.** Asked which single fund he'd keep, Wilson picks Addition. Quote: \"I think he's the best investor in our portfolio.\" Later in the same answer: \"probably the most talented investor in our portfolio\" ([20VC ~00:51–52](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026)).\n- **His pricing rule, in Wilson's words:** \"disciplined around price, but will pay up for super high-quality assets\" ([20VC ~00:51](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026)). Wilson brings him up as the counterexample to paying very high revenue multiples for AI companies, saying Fixel is \"very pragmatic about... stepping away\" when things get frothy (~00:36).\n- **Credited with WashU's SpaceX win, as a director.** Wilson says \"Lee and the board, and Adam should get a lot of credit for, for the big win in SpaceX.\" At one point SpaceX was more than 20% of the portfolio, and WashU used continuation vehicles to hold it through the IPO ([20VC ~00:13](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026)). This reads as board-level governance credit (backing concentration). No source says Addition invested in SpaceX. \"Adam\" is likely WashU MD Adam Kurkiewicz, but that's unconfirmed.\n- **Verified board seat.** The task brief called his WUIMC board seat unverified. The FY2024–25 annual report confirms it, listing \"Lee J. Fixel, BSBA '02, Founder and Managing Partner, Addition\" under \"WashU IMC BOARD OF DIRECTORS\". Fellow directors include chair Eric Upin (Point Olema) and Kelli Washington (CIO, The California Endowment) ([WashU IMC Annual Report 2024–25, p.25](https://endowment.wustl.edu/files/2025/12/CRTV-9068-Endowment-Annual-Report-24-25-Digital.pdf)).\n- **He briefs podcast hosts before they interview his LPs.** Harry Stebbings calls Fixel a \"mutual friend\" and says \"he told me that I had to ask about concentration limits\" ([20VC ~00:13](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026)). That's a small but real sign that Fixel shapes how WashU's story gets told, and that he's close to Stebbings.\n- **Three-way WashU tie: alumnus, donor and director.** He holds a BSBA from WashU, class of 2002 (annual report above). The Lauren and Lee Fixel Family Foundation backed WashU's FARM launch in 2025 and endows two \"Lauren and Lee Fixel Distinguished Professors\" ([WashU Source, 2025-02](https://source.washu.edu/2025/02/washu-launches-farm-to-drive-food-system-transformation-2/)).\n- **Timing on the one frontier-AI-adjacent deal I could verify (Hugging Face): he came in second, not first.** Lux led Hugging Face's $15M round on 2019-12-17 ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2019/12/17/hugging-face-raises-15-million-to-build-the-definitive-natural-language-processing-library/)). About 15 months later, Addition led the $40M Series B, and Lux, A.Capital and Betaworks followed on ([TechCrunch, 2021-03-11](https://techcrunch.com/2021/03/11/hugging-face-raises-40-million-for-its-natural-language-processing-library/)). Lux then led the 2022 Series C, Sequoia's first check ([lux-capital rows](../data/rows/lux-capital-deals.csv)). The pattern is a second institutional lead after a science-first seed firm, not first money in.\n- **(2026-11-10) The closest thing to his own voice: a founder's account of the first meeting.** In Render's Series A post, Anurag Goel writes that he first met \"Lee\" in February 2020. Fixel asked two questions only: \"what a typical customer pays for Render\" and how much a typical DevOps engineer earns. In other words, he priced the product against the salary it replaces. The post links \"Lee\" to Fixel's Forbes profile ([Render blog, 2021-11-22](https://render.com/blog/render-series-a)). This is the price discipline Wilson describes, seen from the founder's side: unit economics in the first meeting.\n- **(2026-11-10) Render timing: a 21-month courtship, then the lead, then he held.** General Catalyst and South Park Commons Fund were already in. Addition led the $20M Series A on 2021-11-22, about 21 months after the first meeting ([Render, Series A](https://render.com/blog/render-series-a)). Bessemer led the $50M B on 2023-06-20, with Addition following on ([Series B](https://render.com/blog/render-series-b)). Georgian led the $80M C on 2025-01-21 ([Series C](https://render.com/blog/series-c)) and a $100M extension at $1.5B on 2026-02-17, with Addition still listed among \"major partners\" ([extension](https://render.com/blog/series-c-extension)). The pattern matches Hugging Face: Addition comes in second as the A or B lead after seed specialists, then stays on the cap table while others lead the later rounds.\n- **(2026-11-10) Snorkel AI is now his most thesis-adjacent holding.** Snorkel's $350M Series E at $3.5B (2026-09-22) was led by Insight and S32, and lists Addition among existing investors. Snorkel describes the round as funding \"the frontier lab for AI data\" ([Snorkel blog](https://snorkel.ai/blog/data-2-0-and-the-research-era-of-ai-data/)). Addition's entry round is still unsourced. Snorkel's own 2021 Series C post ($85M at $1B, 2021-08-09) names no lead ([Snorkel](https://snorkel.ai/blog/snorkel-ai-raises-85m-series-c/)).\n- **Tiger's own exit letter (2019):** called him \"a world-class investor across multiple sectors and stages\". TechCrunch also reported that Bill Gurley had publicly called him \"among the smartest investors on the scene\" (a TechCrunch paraphrase, not Gurley verbatim) ([TechCrunch, 2019-03-14](https://techcrunch.com/2019/03/14/lee-fixel-is-leaving-tiger-global/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low** on strategy, **medium-to-high** as a network node.\n- **Strategy:** THESIS.md names Addition as \"not my type of fund\" (growth, very price-disciplined). Addition writes $1B+ funds, leads B-and-later rounds (for example the Hugging Face Series B) and earns its edge on price, the opposite of writing $20M checks into $600M lab seeds. I found no sourced Addition deal in a pre-training lab or an AI chip company.\n- **AI-relevant deals:** Hugging Face (led the Series B in 2021), Render (led the Series A in 2021; AI-era cloud for developers), Snorkel AI (existing investor at the 2026 Series E, which pitches it as a \"frontier lab for AI data\"), plus companies Forbes lists without round detail: Snorkel AI and Render (AI infrastructure), Applied Intuition, and OneChronos (described as an AI market optimizer) ([Forbes profile](https://www.forbes.com/profile/lee-fixel/)). Zap Energy (fusion) is on Wikipedia's list ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Addition_(investment_firm))).\n- **Why still follow him:** he is the most direct route to WashU's CIO and board, and I share a WashU network with them. Wilson starts deals \"on second or third base\" from trusted GPs, and Fixel is the GP he rates highest. Wilson never says, though, that Fixel feeds WashU's direct deals; that link is my inference, not on record. WashU itself is a poor LP fit for me (1% per idea, about $200M). Fixel matters as a reference and a door, not a source of capital.\n\n## Background\n- Born 1980, raised near Fort Lauderdale, Florida ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lee_Fixel)).\n- BSBA, Olin Business School, Washington University in St. Louis, class of 2002 ([WashU IMC Annual Report 2024–25](https://endowment.wustl.edu/files/2025/12/CRTV-9068-Endowment-Annual-Report-24-25-Digital.pdf)). CFA charterholder. Started at Alkeon Capital Management ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lee_Fixel)).\n- 2006: joined Tiger Global and rose from analyst to partner and head of its $13B private equity business ([ai-cio, 2019](https://www.ai-cio.com/news/tiger-private-equity-head-exit-june/), [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2019/03/14/lee-fixel-is-leaving-tiger-global/)).\n- 2019-03: announced he was leaving Tiger at the end of June 2019. Scott Shleifer took over the business and co-manages its portfolio with Chase Coleman. Fixel kept some boards ([ai-cio](https://www.ai-cio.com/news/tiger-private-equity-head-exit-june/)).\n- 2020: founded Addition. Fund sizes: Addition One $1.3B (2020), Two $1.4B (2020), Three $1.5B (2021), Four $1.5B (2022), Five $1.5B (2023); about $7B AUM by 2022 ([Wikipedia, secondary](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Addition_(investment_firm))). Forbes says Addition's first IPO was dLocal in 2021 ([Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/lee-fixel/)).\n- Forbes lists: 14 appearances as of the 2026-05-27 profile update ([Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/lee-fixel/)).\n- Philanthropy: in 2019 the family foundation gave $20M to the University of Florida for the Norman Fixel Institute ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2019/03/14/lee-fixel-is-leaving-tiger-global/)). In 2025 it backed WashU's FARM ([WashU Source](https://source.washu.edu/2025/02/washu-launches-farm-to-drive-food-system-transformation-2/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.addition.com/ | Header and footer only, no team or portfolio (as of 2026-11-09) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | Not checked (search budget exhausted) |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- No appearances of his own found. Caveat: the web-search budget for this run was exhausted, so I couldn't search broadly. The checks were direct fetches of known pages.\n- 2026-11 — 20VC (as subject, not guest) — \"Scott Wilson, CIO @ WashU\" — https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026 — Wilson's account of him, covered under Differential insight.\n\n## How they think\n- **Price discipline with exceptions for quality**, per Wilson (20VC, above). Nothing in his own words.\n- **Cross-stage, cross-geography:** at Tiger, early Flipkart (India) and Peloton's Series B through to late-stage Facebook and Spotify ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lee_Fixel), [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2019/03/14/lee-fixel-is-leaving-tiger-global/)). Addition's portfolio follows the same pattern: Satispay (Italy), Delhivery (India), dLocal (LatAm payments) ([Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/lee-fixel/), [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Addition_(investment_firm))).\n- **Supports concentration:** credited with backing WashU's decision to hold SpaceX past 20% of the portfolio (20VC ~00:13).\n- **Calls right:** Flipkart (Walmart deal, about $3B to Tiger), Spotify (about $2B of value to Tiger) ([ai-cio](https://www.ai-cio.com/news/tiger-private-equity-head-exit-june/)). Calls wrong: nothing sourced found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. 20VC with Scott Wilson, ~00:13 and ~00:36 and ~00:51–00:52 — the only recent, detailed view of how an allocator sees him. https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026\n2. Anurag Goel, \"Render's $20 Million Series A\" (2021-11-22) — the only first-hand account of how Fixel opens a diligence conversation. https://render.com/blog/render-series-a\n3. TechCrunch, \"Lee Fixel is leaving Tiger Global\" (2019-03-14) — the Tiger track record and how peers saw him. https://techcrunch.com/2019/03/14/lee-fixel-is-leaving-tiger-global/\n4. WashU IMC Annual Report 2024–25, p.25 board page — confirms the board seat and class year. https://endowment.wustl.edu/files/2025/12/CRTV-9068-Endowment-Annual-Report-24-25-Digital.pdf\n5. Livemint, \"How Lee Fixel, Flipkart's godfather in New York...\" (2018) — the Flipkart origin story. Couldn't fetch (site blocked), not read. https://www.livemint.com/Companies/5Cds0A5aOSil178CM4J9vM/How-Lee-Fixel-Flipkarts-godfather-in-New-York-changed-Ind.html\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public X or Substack identified. Bill Gurley amplified him in 2019 (via TechCrunch). Harry Stebbings calls him a friend (20VC 2026-11-08).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Hugging Face | Series B ($40M) | 2021-03-11 | Yes (Addition) | Lux Capital, A.Capital, Betaworks; angels incl. Olivier Pomel, Alex Wang, Richard Socher | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2021/03/11/hugging-face-raises-40-million-for-its-natural-language-processing-library/) |\n| Snorkel AI | entry round unknown; existing investor at Series E ($350M, $3.5B) | 2026-09-22 | No (Insight, S32 led) | Third Point, March, Blumberg, Allegis, Standard VC, Frontline; existing Lightspeed, Greylock, GV, P7, Wells Fargo, Walden Catalyst, Factory | [Snorkel](https://snorkel.ai/blog/data-2-0-and-the-research-era-of-ai-data/) |\n| Render | Series A ($20M) | 2021-11-22 | Yes (Addition) | General Catalyst, South Park Commons Fund (existing) | [Render](https://render.com/blog/render-series-a) |\n| Render | Series B ($50M) | 2023-06-20 | No (Bessemer led) | General Catalyst, SPC Fund | [Render](https://render.com/blog/render-series-b) |\n| Render | Series C ($80M) | 2025-01-21 | No (Georgian led) | 01A, avra, Bessemer, General Catalyst, SPC Fund | [Render](https://render.com/blog/series-c) |\n| Render | Series C extension ($100M at $1.5B) | 2026-02-17 | No (Georgian led) | Bessemer, General Catalyst, 01A | [Render](https://render.com/blog/series-c-extension) |\n| Applied Intuition | unknown ($15B valuation per Forbes) | unknown | unknown | | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/lee-fixel/) |\n| OneChronos | unknown | unknown | unknown | | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/lee-fixel/) |\n| Base Power | unknown ($4B valuation per Forbes) | unknown | unknown | | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/lee-fixel/) |\n| Satispay | unknown ($1.05B valuation per Forbes) | unknown | unknown | | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/lee-fixel/) |\n| dLocal | pre-IPO (IPO 2021, Addition's first) | 2021 IPO | unknown | | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/lee-fixel/) |\n| Zap Energy, Chainalysis, Snyk, Delhivery, Stripe | unknown | unknown | unknown | | [Wikipedia (secondary)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Addition_(investment_firm)) |\n| Flipkart (Tiger era) | early | pre-2018 | unknown | | [ai-cio](https://www.ai-cio.com/news/tiger-private-equity-head-exit-june/) |\n| Peloton (Tiger era) | Series B | 2014 | Yes (Tiger) | | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2019/03/14/lee-fixel-is-leaving-tiger-global/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Hugging Face (2021):** Addition led the Series B about 15 months after Lux's $15M round, and Lux followed on. Then Lux led the 2022 Series C, Sequoia's entry. How Addition got the deal is not public ([TechCrunch 2021](https://techcrunch.com/2021/03/11/hugging-face-raises-40-million-for-its-natural-language-processing-library/), [TechCrunch 2019](https://techcrunch.com/2019/12/17/hugging-face-raises-15-million-to-build-the-definitive-natural-language-processing-library/)). One small overlap: WashU is a Lux LP ([firms/lux-capital.md](../firms/lux-capital.md)).\n- **Render (2020–2026):** Goel met Fixel in February 2020, which is before Addition's first fund (2020). Fixel's opening questions were about customer price compared with a DevOps engineer's salary. Addition led the Series A 21 months later and has stayed in through B, C and the 2026 extension. Who made the introduction isn't public ([Render, Series A](https://render.com/blog/render-series-a)).\n- **SpaceX at WashU:** Fixel's credited role is as a board member backing concentration and holds through continuation vehicles (Wilson, 20VC ~00:13). The origin of WashU's SpaceX position is covered in Scott Wilson's files (Vy Capital, then direct).\n- **Flipkart (Tiger):** Livemint calls him \"Flipkart's godfather\" in its 2018 headline, but I couldn't fetch the article. He stayed on the Flipkart board after leaving Tiger ([ai-cio](https://www.ai-cio.com/news/tiger-private-equity-head-exit-june/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Addition | Founder and managing partner | 2020 | [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Addition_(investment_firm)), [WashU AR](https://endowment.wustl.edu/files/2025/12/CRTV-9068-Endowment-Annual-Report-24-25-Digital.pdf) |\n| Tiger Global | Partner and head of private equity, 2006 to 2019-06 | 2006 | [ai-cio](https://www.ai-cio.com/news/tiger-private-equity-head-exit-june/) |\n| WashU IMC | Board director (on FY2024–25 report) | unknown | [WashU AR p.25](https://endowment.wustl.edu/files/2025/12/CRTV-9068-Endowment-Annual-Report-24-25-Digital.pdf) |\n| Washington University in St. Louis | Alumnus (BSBA '02); donor (FARM, Fixel professorships) | 2002 | [WashU AR](https://endowment.wustl.edu/files/2025/12/CRTV-9068-Endowment-Annual-Report-24-25-Digital.pdf), [WashU Source](https://source.washu.edu/2025/02/washu-launches-farm-to-drive-food-system-transformation-2/) |\n| Scott Wilson (WashU CIO) | Core manager relationship; Wilson's top-rated investor | unknown | [20VC](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n| Harry Stebbings (20VC) | \"Mutual friend\" (Stebbings's words) | unknown | [20VC ~00:13](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n| Eric Upin; Kelli Washington; Andrew Bursky | Fellow WashU IMC directors | unknown | [WashU AR p.25](https://endowment.wustl.edu/files/2025/12/CRTV-9068-Endowment-Annual-Report-24-25-Digital.pdf) |\n| Scott Shleifer; Chase Coleman | Tiger colleagues who took over his portfolios | 2006 | [ai-cio](https://www.ai-cio.com/news/tiger-private-equity-head-exit-june/) |\n| Bill Gurley | Publicly praised him (per TechCrunch) | by 2019 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2019/03/14/lee-fixel-is-leaving-tiger-global/) |\n| Anurag Goel (Render founder) | Addition led his Series A; first met 2020-02 | 2020 | [Render](https://render.com/blog/render-series-a) |\n| Flipkart; Peloton; Warby Parker | Board seats (as of 2019) | unknown | [ai-cio](https://www.ai-cio.com/news/tiger-private-equity-head-exit-june/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is WashU an LP in Addition? Wilson calls Addition one of \"our core partners\", which suggests it, but no commitment is on record. A board member managing WashU money would also raise a conflict-of-interest question worth checking.\n- Does Addition hold SpaceX directly, or is Fixel's credit purely board governance?\n- Has Addition done any AI deal at seed or Series A, or any pre-training lab or AI chip deal? Hugging Face is the only verified AI lead found.\n- Render is now resolved (Addition led the A). Still open: Addition's entry round in Snorkel (2021 Series C post names no lead); Applied Intuition (Addition is not named in the 2025 Series F post, which BlackRock and Kleiner led, [Applied](https://www.appliedintuition.com/blog/series-f)); OneChronos and Base Power (their sites name no investors). This needs search, which was exhausted again on 2026-11-10.\n- Who introduced Goel to Fixel in February 2020? General Catalyst and South Park Commons are the obvious candidates, but that's unconfirmed.\n- The Livemint Flipkart story (2018) and The Information's \"A Tiger in Silicon Valley\" (2013) are unread and may hold the intro paths.\n- Calibration conflict in earlier rows: `scott-wilson-leads.csv` rated Fixel `medium`, `scott-wilson-extra-leads.csv` rated him `low`. This profile goes with `low` on strategy fit, per THESIS.md.\n- Also in earlier rows: `scott-wilson-rels.csv` still marks the board seat \"unverified\". It is now verified.\n\n## Sources\n- 20VC, Scott Wilson, aired 2026-11-08 — https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026 (local transcript: sources/transcripts/2026-11-08-20vc-scott-wilson-washu.txt)\n- WashU IMC Annual Report 2024–25 — https://endowment.wustl.edu/files/2025/12/CRTV-9068-Endowment-Annual-Report-24-25-Digital.pdf\n- WashU Source, FARM launch (2025-02) — https://source.washu.edu/2025/02/washu-launches-farm-to-drive-food-system-transformation-2/\n- TechCrunch, 2019-03-14 — https://techcrunch.com/2019/03/14/lee-fixel-is-leaving-tiger-global/\n- ai-cio, 2019 — https://www.ai-cio.com/news/tiger-private-equity-head-exit-june/\n- TechCrunch, Hugging Face Series B, 2021-03-11 — https://techcrunch.com/2021/03/11/hugging-face-raises-40-million-for-its-natural-language-processing-library/\n- TechCrunch, Hugging Face $15M, 2019-12-17 — https://techcrunch.com/2019/12/17/hugging-face-raises-15-million-to-build-the-definitive-natural-language-processing-library/\n- Forbes profile (updated 2026-05-27) — https://www.forbes.com/profile/lee-fixel/\n- Wikipedia, Lee Fixel — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lee_Fixel (secondary)\n- Wikipedia, Addition — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Addition_(investment_firm) (secondary)\n- Render blog, Series A (2021-11-22) — https://render.com/blog/render-series-a\n- Render blog, Series B (2023-06-20) — https://render.com/blog/render-series-b\n- Render blog, Series C (2025-01-21) — https://render.com/blog/series-c\n- Render blog, Series C extension (2026-02-17) — https://render.com/blog/series-c-extension\n- Snorkel blog, Series E (2026-09-22) — https://snorkel.ai/blog/data-2-0-and-the-research-era-of-ai-data/\n- Snorkel blog, Series C (2021-08-09) — https://snorkel.ai/blog/snorkel-ai-raises-85m-series-c/\n- Applied Intuition blog, Series F (2025-06-17) — https://www.appliedintuition.com/blog/series-f\n- Addition site — https://www.addition.com/\n", "person:leigh-marie-braswell": "\n# Leigh Marie Braswell\n\n## One-line take\nBraswell is Kleiner's partner for AI-native applications and agents. Applied Compute, a team from OpenAI's RL group, is the one frontier-talent name in their book, and Kleiner bought in on a markup, not at the first check.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** they have engineering credibility from Scale AI, where they were an early engineer and the first PM on 3D annotation ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/)). Fortune describes them as having \"a reputation for backing technical winners\" ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/04/27/avoca-ai-agents-missed-calls-hvac-plumbing-roofing-kleiner-perkins-chen-shrivastava-braswell/)). Their usual move is to lead the Series A or an up-round once a technical team has early traction: Avoca A, Applied Compute $80M at $1.3B, Reevo co-lead, Nooks B.\n- **Constraint:** they sit inside a multi-stage firm where Mamoon Hamid and Aditya Naganath own the lab and compute lane (Flapping Airplanes, Mirendil, Etched, Sail; see `firms/kleiner-perkins.md`). Braswell's page lists no labs, chips or compute ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/)). Partner-page credit is also shared. Bun's seed is credited to \"Bucky & Leigh Marie\" ([src](https://bun.com/blog/bun-joins-anthropic)), and on Reevo the Kleiner quote in the press is Hamid's ([src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/reevo-debuts-80-million-funding-171500872.html)).\n- **What they'd never do (inference):** write the first check into a pre-product lab, or claim a moat that doesn't exist. They prefer \"head start\" to \"first-mover advantage\" because \"It's more intellectually honest\" ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/04/27/avoca-ai-agents-missed-calls-hvac-plumbing-roofing-kleiner-perkins-chen-shrivastava-braswell/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Bun is a shared credit that predates their Kleiner tenure.** Bun's founder writes \"We raised a $7 million seed round led by Kleiner Perkins\" and thanks \"Bucky & Leigh Marie\" ([src](https://bun.com/blog/bun-joins-anthropic), 2025-12-02). Braswell's partner page dates Bun to 2022, but their bio says they joined Kleiner in 2023 ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/)). So the \"Bun, acquired by Anthropic\" win belongs at least partly to Bucky Moore. Neon, Chronosphere and Persona (2021) also predate Kleiner, and are probably Founders Fund-era deals (inference).\n- **Applied Compute: Kleiner was not first in.** The company launched on 2025-10-29 with Benchmark, Sequoia, Lux, Hanabi, Neo, Definition, Elad Gil, Victor Lazarte and Omri Casspi as backers ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific)). About five months later, on 2026-04-08, Kleiner led $80M at $1.3B, a round \"led by Kleiner Perkins with continued participation\" ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/fundraise)). Seven months after that, Elad Gil led $350M at $3.25B, and Kleiner only participated ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise)). The pattern is a Series-A-style lead into proven RL talent at a markup, then a pro-rata follow. It is not a first-check pattern.\n- **Contrarian framing on moats.** They say \"I like the phrase 'head start' better\" than first-mover advantage ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/04/27/avoca-ai-agents-missed-calls-hvac-plumbing-roofing-kleiner-perkins-chen-shrivastava-braswell/), 2026-04-27).\n- **Sourcing thesis: AI agents for markets that tech ignores.** On Avoca (HVAC, plumbing, roofing): \"definitely an industry that's been overlooked by Silicon Valley\". They call Avoca \"a necessary bridge between Silicon Valley and Main Street\" ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/04/27/avoca-ai-agents-missed-calls-hvac-plumbing-roofing-kleiner-perkins-chen-shrivastava-braswell/)).\n- **Attribution caution (Reevo).** Kleiner co-led Reevo's $80M launch with Khosla, and Reevo is on Braswell's page. The only Kleiner voice in the coverage is Hamid: \"They've flipped traditional CRM on its head\" ([src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/reevo-debuts-80-million-funding-171500872.html)).\n- **Podcast host role.** They co-hosted a Kleiner \"Builders\" episode on physical AI with Josh Coyne and RJ Scaringe (Rivian / Mind Robotics), seen as a LinkedIn repost ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/leigh-marie-braswell)). I could not retrieve the episode, so I have no quotes from it.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Braswell is a strong Series A partner for AI-native automation, which is the Serval-like part of `THESIS.md`. They are not in the pre-training, lab or chip lane. Deals that matter to me:\n- **Applied Compute (high):** ex-OpenAI founders from Codex, o1 and RL training infrastructure ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific)), now at $3.25B. This is the frontier-talent signal I look for, but Kleiner arrived after launch.\n- **Avoca (high, AI-native automation):** a YC seed, then a Kleiner-led A, then a $1B Series B co-led by Meritech and General Catalyst. Braswell is on the board ([src](https://www.avoca.ai/blog/avoca-raises-125m-series-b-1b-valuation), [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/)).\n- **Poetic (high, AI-native automation):** \"runbooks that run as software\". Braswell is on the board (2026).\n- **Engram (medium):** an AI memory layer (2026). Braswell reposted Engram's post on RL and text-based memory.\n- **Nooks, Reevo (medium):** AI sales agents.\n\nFor co-investing, Braswell is a likely Series A lead after my seed in AI-native automation companies. They are not a seed co-investor in new labs.\n\n## Background\n- From Alabama; MIT graduate. Their first job was competitive math tutoring ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/)).\n- Scale AI: early engineer, then first PM, building and later leading the 3D annotation products. Dates not found ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/)).\n- Founders Fund: started investing there. LinkedIn headline says \"principal at Founders Fund\". Dates not visible ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/leigh-marie-braswell)).\n- Kleiner Perkins: Partner, joined in 2023 per the bio ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/)). However, Bun (2022) is credited to them on a Kleiner-led seed, so the transition timing is unresolved.\n- Interests per the bio: poker, long-distance running, scuba diving.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/ | Firm page; no personal site found (leighmariebraswell.com does not resolve, 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | substack.com/@lmbraswell did not render |\n| X | https://x.com/LM_Braswell | Linked from firm page; fetch blocked (HTTP 402), follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/leigh-marie-braswell | Active reposting of portfolio news (Applied Compute, Engram, Nooks, Poetic) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Kleiner \"Builders\" (co-host on at least one episode) | Episode URL not retrieved |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026 — Kleiner Perkins \"Builders\" — physical AI with RJ Scaringe, co-hosted with Josh Coyne — seen via LinkedIn repost ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/leigh-marie-braswell)). Transcript not retrieved.\n- Nothing else found. The web-search budget for this run was exhausted, so podcast, talk and X coverage is **unverified, not absent**.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** vertical AI agents that bring frontier capability to non-tech industries (Avoca); automation of operations work (Poetic runbooks, Nooks and Reevo for sales); infrastructure for agents (Applied Compute's company-specific RL agents, Engram memory) ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/)).\n- **Contrarian:** they treat AI app leads as a \"head start\", not a moat ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/04/27/avoca-ai-agents-missed-calls-hvac-plumbing-roofing-kleiner-perkins-chen-shrivastava-braswell/)).\n- **Calls right:** Windsurf (2024, acquired by Google per the page), Bun (shared credit, acquired by Anthropic), Applied Compute (about 2.5x markup in 7 months), Avoca ($1B at the B). Calls wrong: none found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Fortune on Avoca (2026-04-27): the only source found with Braswell's own words ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/04/27/avoca-ai-agents-missed-calls-hvac-plumbing-roofing-kleiner-perkins-chen-shrivastava-braswell/)).\n2. Applied Compute launch post: the founders' OpenAI RL pedigree ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific)).\n3. Bun joins Anthropic: shows who actually got Kleiner into Bun ([src](https://bun.com/blog/bun-joins-anthropic)).\n4. Kleiner partner page: the portfolio, with stage at entry and board seats ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nLinkedIn activity is mostly amplification of portfolio news: Applied Compute's $350M round and Yash Patil's update, Engram's RL/memory post, Nooks' $500M and Node launch, and Poetic absorbing the TruckSmarter team. They leave short congratulations on Scale and Figma posts ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/leigh-marie-braswell)). X activity was not retrievable.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Applied Compute | $80M at $1.3B post | 2026-04-08 | Kleiner led (partner not named) | Elad Gil, Lux, Greenoaks, Neo, Hanabi | [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/fundraise) |\n| Applied Compute | $350M at $3.25B | 2026-11-02 | No (Elad Gil led) | Lux, Hanabi, NVIDIA, Blackstone, Definition, Neo, Nova, Abstract and others | [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise) |\n| Avoca | Series A | not given | Kleiner led | YC (seed 2024) | [src](https://fortune.com/2026/04/27/avoca-ai-agents-missed-calls-hvac-plumbing-roofing-kleiner-perkins-chen-shrivastava-braswell/) |\n| Avoca | Series B, $1B valuation | 2026-04-27 | No (Meritech, GC) | Amplify, Nexus, YC | [src](https://www.avoca.ai/blog/avoca-raises-125m-series-b-1b-valuation) |\n| Reevo | $80M launch | 2025-11-05 | Co-led with Khosla | Zhu Ventures | [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/reevo-debuts-80-million-funding-171500872.html) |\n| Nooks | $43M Series B | 2024-10-24 | Kleiner named (lead not explicit) | not named | [src](https://www.nooks.ai/blog-posts/series-b) |\n| Bun (Oven) | $7M seed | 2022 | Kleiner led (shared credit with Bucky) | not named | [src](https://bun.com/blog/bun-joins-anthropic) |\n| Windsurf | Early | 2024 | not stated | not found | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/) |\n| Poetic (board) | Early | 2026 | not stated | not found | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/) |\n| Engram | Early | 2026 | not stated | not found | same |\n| Trovy | Early | 2026 | not stated | not found | same |\n| Convoke | Early | 2024 | not stated | not found | same |\n| Neon / Chronosphere / Persona | Early / Growth / Growth | 2021 | not stated (predates Kleiner) | not found | same |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Applied Compute:** the founders came from OpenAI's Codex, o1 and RL-infrastructure teams and launched with Benchmark, Sequoia and Lux ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific)). Kleiner led the next round at $1.3B. No source says how Braswell met the team, and they hold no board seat ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/)). Elad Gil, an angel since launch, led the $3.25B round.\n- **Avoca:** a YC 2024 seed, then a Kleiner-led A with Braswell on the board, then a $1B Series B led by Meritech and General Catalyst ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/04/27/avoca-ai-agents-missed-calls-hvac-plumbing-roofing-kleiner-perkins-chen-shrivastava-braswell/)). The intro path was not reported.\n- **Bun:** Kleiner led the $7M seed, credited by Bun's founder to Bucky and Leigh Marie. Khosla led the $19M A. Anthropic acquired Bun (announced 2025-12-02) ([src](https://bun.com/blog/bun-joins-anthropic)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Kleiner Perkins | Partner | 2023 | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/) |\n| Founders Fund | Principal (former) | — | [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/leigh-marie-braswell) |\n| Scale AI | Early engineer; first PM (former) | — | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/) |\n| Bucky Moore | Co-credited on Bun seed | 2022 | [src](https://bun.com/blog/bun-joins-anthropic) |\n| Josh Coyne | Builders podcast co-host | 2026 | [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/leigh-marie-braswell) |\n| Mamoon Hamid | Co-listed on Reevo | 2025 | [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/reevo-debuts-80-million-funding-171500872.html) |\n| Elad Gil | Co-investor, Applied Compute | 2026 | [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise) |\n| Avoca, Poetic | Board | 2026 | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did Braswell source Applied Compute? How did Kleiner win the lead over the existing Benchmark and Sequoia backers?\n- Bun is dated 2022, but they joined Kleiner in 2023. Were they a scout or advisor first? Which firm did Bucky Moore move to?\n- Were Neon, Chronosphere and Persona Founders Fund deals? What were Braswell's Founders Fund years?\n- Get the Builders episode transcript, X posts and any podcast appearances (search budget was exhausted this run).\n- Engram: founders, round, and how the RL-memory thesis connects to Applied Compute.\n- Was the Ryan Beiermeister LinkedIn congrats (needs-id lead) this Braswell? If so, it is an active Founders Fund alumni tie.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/leigh-marie-braswell\n- https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific\n- https://appliedcompute.com/company/fundraise\n- https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise\n- https://fortune.com/2026/04/27/avoca-ai-agents-missed-calls-hvac-plumbing-roofing-kleiner-perkins-chen-shrivastava-braswell/\n- https://www.avoca.ai/blog/avoca-raises-125m-series-b-1b-valuation\n- https://bun.com/blog/bun-joins-anthropic\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/news/reevo-debuts-80-million-funding-171500872.html\n- https://www.nooks.ai/blog-posts/series-b\n- https://www.nooks.ai/blog-posts/introducing-node-revenue-agent-platform\n", "person:liam-corrigan": "\n# Liam Corrigan\n\n## One-line take\nA 2026 Sequoia partner whose only two named deals are a hardware-and-energy pair for AI compute: the Sequoia-only seed of NUVACORE, a clean-sheet CPU core for AI and agentic workloads, and the $1B Valar Atomics round for nuclear-powered AI campuses. Worth watching as Sequoia's newest physics-trained person on the AI compute stack.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** Corrigan has a physics background (Cosmic Microwave Background research) and an Olympic gold in rowing. Sequoia's leadership has publicly marked him as one of two \"rising stars\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)).\n- **Pattern so far:** Every deal pairs him with a senior GP on hardware. On NUVACORE it is Konstantine Buhler, at the seed. On Valar Atomics it is Shaun Maguire, at growth ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/nuvacore/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/valar-atomics/)).\n- **Constraints:** He has no solo-lead record yet, and no public writing beyond his firm bio. He is about 29 (born 1997, [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liam_Corrigan)).\n- **What he'd likely pass on:** Inferred from his bio, not stated by him. His stated filter is purpose plus \"technical excellence\", which points away from thin application-layer software ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **His tags are compute-stack tags.** The structured data on Sequoia's page tags him with hardware, semiconductors, physics and energy, not AI software. That fits a chips-plus-power remit and explains why both deals are hardware. Quote: \"Deep tech\",\"Hardware\",\"Semiconductors\",\"Physics\",\"Energy\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/), page JSON-LD)\n- **Self-described loser-to-winner record.** His bio frames the job around persistence through losses. That is a useful signal for how he would back a long silicon or nuclear cycle. Quote: \"My final record in championship races was 3 – 36\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/))\n- **He pitches himself as a teammate through losses**, not as a picker. Quote: \"a teammate that is consistent through losses and wins\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/))\n- **His filter is technical excellence plus purpose.** Quote: \"art is at its highest form when manifested with technical excellence\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/))\n- **NUVACORE timing: Sequoia was the only named backer at launch.** NUVACORE's own investor page lists only Sequoia at its April 2026 launch, so Corrigan and Buhler were first in, with no co-investor named. Quote: \"launched in April 2026 with the backing of Sequoia Capital\" ([src](https://nuvacore.ai/investors))\n- **Valar timing: Sequoia came in late, and the board seat went to Maguire.** Riot Ventures led Valar's $19M seed in Feb 2025 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/20/valar-atomics-comes-out-of-stealth-with-19m-and-a-pilot-reactor-site/)). Sequoia led the $1B round about 18 months later, in Aug 2026. Corrigan is co-listed as the junior partner on that round. Quote: \"Sequoia partner Shaun Maguire joining its board\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/03/sequoias-shaun-maguire-leads-1b-round-for-nuclear-startup-valar-atomics/))\n- **The memo gives context for his seed-plus-growth mix.** The same Nov 2026 memo that calls him a rising star also merges Sequoia's early and growth teams. That is why a \"seed/early\" partner already sits on a growth-stage deal. Quote: \"merging its early-stage and growth teams into one\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/))\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High** on direction, **unproven** on independence.\n- **NUVACORE** is squarely in the \"AI chips/compute\" lane of THESIS.md: a very early, single-investor seed of a CPU core built for AI and agentic workloads ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/nuvacore/)).\n- **Valar Atomics** is energy for compute (\"power the next generation of AI\", per Sequoia's page). It is adjacent to the thesis but a growth-stage mega-round, and Maguire's deal ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/valar-atomics/)).\n- **Why it matters to me:** Corrigan is a likely early contact for chip and power founders coming to Sequoia. As a $20M-check co-investor, I would expect him to be the working-level partner on seed-stage silicon at Sequoia, sitting under Buhler. Sequoia rarely shares seed allocations, so treat him as a signal and an introduction source, not a syndicate partner. That last point is my inference.\n\n## Background\n- **Birth:** 1997. Wikipedia gives his birthplace as Old Lyme, Connecticut ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liam_Corrigan)). His own bio says he moved from Canada to the US with his parents when young ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/)). The two sources don't agree.\n- **Academics:** studied physics and did research on the Cosmic Microwave Background ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/)). He rowed at Harvard ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liam_Corrigan), Harvard Crimson roster link). His degree year was not confirmed.\n- **2020 Tokyo Olympics:** 4th place in the US men's eight ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liam_Corrigan)).\n- **2022:** member of the winning Oxford crew in the Boat Race, which implies graduate study at Oxford; the program was not confirmed ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liam_Corrigan)).\n- **2023:** silver in the men's four at the World Championships ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liam_Corrigan)).\n- **2024 Paris Olympics:** gold in the men's coxless four ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liam_Corrigan)). His bio says he rowed competitively from 14 to 26 ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/)).\n- **2026:** Partner on Sequoia's seed/early team ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/), page meta and JSON-LD \"jobTitle\":\"Partner\"). The joining date and any jobs between 2024 and 2026 were not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/ | firm bio, 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/lmcorrigan1 | linked from Sequoia page; could not be read (paywalled to fetcher) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/liamcorrigan1/ | linked from Sequoia page; not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found as an investor. The web-search budget was exhausted before podcasts could be searched.\n\n## How they think\n- **Mind-body framing:** \"great companies test their excellence by crafting tangible change\". This is a bias toward physical, tangible products ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/)).\n- **Purpose framing:** he wants to back purpose-driven founders and describes his own purpose as \"helping to scale beauty in the world\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/)).\n- **Contrarian views:** nothing found.\n- **Calls right or wrong:** too early to call.\n\n## Read these first\n1. His Sequoia bio, the only first-person text found ([link](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/)).\n2. NUVACORE's launch post, \"Engineered for Altitude\" (2026-04-15), the thesis of his seed deal ([link](https://nuvacore.ai/news/engineered-for-altitude-why-nuvacore-is-rewriting-the-rules-of-silicon)).\n3. The TechCrunch write-up of the \"Sequoia of Tomorrow\" memo naming him a rising star ([link](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. His X account (@lmcorrigan1) could not be fetched.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| NUVACORE (clean-sheet CPU core for AI/agentic workloads) | Pre-Seed/Seed (size not disclosed) | 2026-04 (launch) | Sequoia was the sole named backer; personal lead not stated. Sequoia co-partner: Konstantine Buhler (GP) | none named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/), [src](https://nuvacore.ai/investors) |\n| Valar Atomics (factory-built nuclear for AI \"Gigasites\") | $1B equity round, about $6B valuation per Bloomberg via TC | 2026-08-03 | Sequoia led; Shaun Maguire took the board seat; Corrigan co-listed | Apandion, Atreides, Conviction, Dream Ventures, HOF Capital, Point72, Riot Ventures, Snowpoint, Valor Equity Partners | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/03/sequoias-shaun-maguire-leads-1b-round-for-nuclear-startup-valar-atomics/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/valar-atomics/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **NUVACORE.** The company came out on 2026-04-15 with Sequoia as its only named backer. The founders are Gerard Williams III, John Bruno and Ram Srinivasan ([src](https://nuvacore.ai/news/engineered-for-altitude-why-nuvacore-is-rewriting-the-rules-of-silicon)). Sequoia's company page lists Konstantine Buhler and Liam Corrigan as partners, with \"Founded 2026, Partnered 2026\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/nuvacore/)). So Sequoia was in at founding, with Buhler, the GP who also did Unconventional AI and Sail, as the senior partner. The intro path and Corrigan's specific role were not found.\n- **Valar Atomics.** Riot Ventures led a $19M seed in Feb 2025 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/20/valar-atomics-comes-out-of-stealth-with-19m-and-a-pilot-reactor-site/)). In July 2026 Sequoia was reported as the expected lead at about $6B. Part of that capital had earlier been raised at a $2B valuation ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/17/nuclear-startup-valar-atomics-in-talks-to-raise-new-funding-at-6b-valuation/)). The round was confirmed on 2026-08-03 at $1B, and Maguire joined the board ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/03/sequoias-shaun-maguire-leads-1b-round-for-nuclear-startup-valar-atomics/)). Corrigan is the second listed Sequoia partner. Sequoia was not first in; the gap from seed to its entry was about 18 months.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sequoia Capital | Partner, seed/early team; memo \"rising star\" | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/) |\n| Konstantine Buhler | Senior co-partner on NUVACORE | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/nuvacore/) |\n| Shaun Maguire | Senior co-partner on Valar Atomics (Maguire led, took board) | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/valar-atomics/) |\n| Sonali Singh | The other \"rising star\" named in the same memo | 2026 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/) |\n| Gerard Williams III, John Bruno, Ram Srinivasan | NUVACORE founders he partners with | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/nuvacore/) |\n| Isaiah Taylor | Valar Atomics founder he partners with | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/valar-atomics/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did he join Sequoia, and was it straight in as partner? This decides `fast_promotion`. What did he do between Paris 2024 and joining?\n- Did he source NUVACORE, or did Buhler? The intro path is unknown. NUVACORE's round size is also unknown.\n- What are the founders' prior roles? The Nuvia/Qualcomm lineage is widely assumed but was not sourced in this pass ([Qualcomm release title only](https://www.qualcomm.com/news/releases/2021/01/qualcomm-acquire-nuvia)).\n- What is his Oxford graduate program, and is there a physics or CMB publication?\n- What does his X feed (@lmcorrigan1) say about chips and power?\n- The Canada and Old Lyme, CT birthplace accounts need reconciling.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liam-corrigan/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/nuvacore/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/valar-atomics/\n- https://nuvacore.ai/investors\n- https://nuvacore.ai/leadership\n- https://nuvacore.ai/news/engineered-for-altitude-why-nuvacore-is-rewriting-the-rules-of-silicon\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/03/sequoias-shaun-maguire-leads-1b-round-for-nuclear-startup-valar-atomics/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/17/nuclear-startup-valar-atomics-in-talks-to-raise-new-funding-at-6b-valuation/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/20/valar-atomics-comes-out-of-stealth-with-19m-and-a-pilot-reactor-site/\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liam_Corrigan\n", "person:linus-lee": "\n# Linus Lee\n\n## One-line take\nAn engineer-researcher, not a dealmaker, who sits inside Thrive helping portfolio companies build with frontier AI; worth following because his essays on AI interfaces, notation, and interpretable latent spaces were early and still shape how builders think about \"beyond chat\" ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/latent/), [src](https://aicouncil.com/profile/linus-lee)).\n\n## Crux\n_Added 2026-11-09._ He wins as the builder inside Thrive, not as a dealmaker: he leads the LLM work on a five-or-six-person internal team. Their mandate is an \"Ironman suit\" for Thrive's roughly 80 staff, plus making the firm more software-defined ([src](https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee)). His edge is turning interpretability and representation research into tools for judging people and companies. His constraint is that he says outright \"I'm not an investor\" ([src](https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee)): no sourced checks, deals or allocation power. He is the wrong door for capital and the right door for how Thrive maps talent. On this evidence he wouldn't automate judgment away. He says that even a compute-driven founder-discovery machine would still need \"the judgment of the people\" ([src](https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee)).\n\n## Differential insight\n_Added 2026-11-09. Each item is mirrored in `data/rows/linus-lee-insights.csv`._\n- **Thrive's internal team is tiny and he kickstarted its LLM layer.** \"We have a team of five or six of us\" (designer, product engineers, data people). The team predates him, but he \"kickstarted\" the LLM parts. Their stack: a data layer, an intelligence layer, a UI (calendar, news feed, search, research agent), and one-off automations even if only two people use them ([src](https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee), Dialectic ep 24, 2025-08-04, 1:14:58).\n- **Ambition: a compute-to-founder-discovery machine.** Spend X thousand dollars of compute and \"you get one new, extra interesting founder that we discover in the world.\" He frames it as making parts of Thrive's business a \"scaling equation\", with people's judgment still needed ([src](https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee), 1:28:09).\n- **Talent-mapping view:** \"I can get a list of everyone that has ever worked at OpenAI.\" The signal he wants is motive, meaning \"what game they're playing\". He also complains that investors' words such as \"spiky\", \"crafty\" and \"cracked\" lack a real vocabulary ([src](https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee), Embeddings of People segment, 0:57:42).\n- **Venture as services:** \"venture is fundamentally a services business—a financial services business.\" In his view, the scarce AI inputs are tacit know-how of the job and a feedback sandbox to learn decisions in ([src](https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee), 1:14:58).\n- **Admitted struggle:** he spent about two years trying to turn his latent-space and interpretability toys (the Prism lineage) into products, and calls it \"the difficult part\". He also cites growing pains from \"not being able to find great use cases for it\" ([src](https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee)).\n- **Goodfire endorsement:** \"A really cool company called Goodfire is applying this in a research domain.\" He means interpretability on biology models ([src](https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee), 0:48:06 segment). No source shows a Thrive investment in Goodfire.\n- **Token scale, May 2026:** asked about Thrive using \"tens of billions of tokens\", he said \"We're quite a bit beyond it now, both in magnitude\". He added that the team now measures adoption and results ([src](https://petesoder.substack.com/p/linus-lee-says-your-agent-is-too), 2026-05-07, answers edited for clarity).\n- **Agent rule of thumb:** \"You can think of a sub-agent as just a very expensive tool.\" The first prototype \"throw[s] everything into the model context\", which is a baseline and not the end state. This is the substance behind the \"bigger context windows are the wrong instinct\" framing ([src](https://petesoder.substack.com/p/linus-lee-says-your-agent-is-too)). It resolves the earlier \"(unverified)\" note on his context-window view.\n- **Flora tie:** a podcast host read out a passage from Linus's writing that calls Flora's founder a friend: he wants artists to \"speak beauty into existence\". Linus confirmed \"I know I said it\" ([src](https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee)). Flora's founder is Weber Wong, ex-Menlo Ventures ([src](https://itp.nyu.edu/itp/?p=15764)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a check-writer and has no sourced deals, so he is not a co-investor or LP target. He matters for three reasons. (1) He is the operator behind Thrive's internal AI and talent-mapping tooling, and Thrive is a frontier-AI capital pool. (2) His explicit interest is mapping frontier talent by motive, for example ex-OpenAI people ([src](https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee)). That is the same problem my thesis rests on. (3) He is a credible technical signal on interpretability companies (Goodfire) and AI-native tooling. Use him for technical diligence views and talent-graph thinking, not for allocation.\n\n## Background\n- UC Berkeley undergrad from fall 2018; left Berkeley in 2021 ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/drf/)). Was involved with Cal Hacks ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/drf/)).\n- Dorm Room Fund, SF investment team, late 2018 to 2021 ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/drf/)).\n- Software engineer at Replit, Hack Club, and Spensa. Confirmed by his own site and The Gradient's bio, but neither gives dates ([src](https://thesephist.com/), [src](https://thegradientpub.substack.com/p/linus-lee-at-the-boundary-of-machine)).\n- Ideaflow: worked there briefly one summer, then joined the core team full-time from Feb 2021 ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/ideaflow/), post dated 2021-01-28).\n- 2022: a year as an independent researcher in NYC. During it he was Betaworks' first Researcher in Residence ([src](https://www.betaworks.com/event/ai-week-how-to-think-in-the-third-millennium), [src](https://thegradientpub.substack.com/p/linus-lee-at-the-boundary-of-machine)). The Betaworks event page is dated 2022-12-01 (later marked postponed) and already calls him a Notion Research Engineer, so his Notion start may predate the Jan 2023 post (unverified) ([src](https://www.betaworks.com/event/ai-week-how-to-think-in-the-third-millennium)).\n- Jan 2023: joined Notion's AI team. The announcement post is dated 2023-01-17 ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/notion/)). He joined after Notion's original AI prototype, which he credits to Simon Last and Ivan Zhao ([src](https://www.latent.space/p/ai-interfaces-and-notion)). His own title was Research Engineer ([src](https://thesephist.com/)). Outset Capital's event series called him \"lead for Notion AI\" (Aug 2023) ([src](https://thursdaynightsinai.substack.com/p/recording-notion-ai-lead-linus-lee)).\n- Aug 2024: left Notion's AI engineering team ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/thrive/)).\n- Sept 2024: joined Thrive Capital as an EIR and advisor. He helps Thrive's founders understand and deploy AI, and he keeps doing interpretability and interface research. Announced 2024-09-11 ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/thrive/)).\n- By 2026: Head of AI at Thrive, on the product and engineering team, supporting portfolio companies on integrating frontier AI ([src](https://aicouncil.com/profile/linus-lee)). His X bio reads \"engineering @thrivecapital\" ([src](https://superx.so/creators/thesephist)). Date of the title change: (unverified).\n- Path into Thrive: he says he got to know Thrive \"over the past couple of years\" and admired its \"depth of partnership with founders\" ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/thrive/)). No intro person is named.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://thesephist.com/ | Hub for bio, talks, projects, and research ([src](https://thesephist.com/)) |\n| Substack / blog | https://thesephist.com/posts/ | About 80+ essays since 2020. Several posts a month in 2020–2022, about 10 in mid-2024, and only a few a year since (latest 2026-11-09) ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/)). Also has a microblog at https://stream.thesephist.com/ ([src](https://thesephist.com/)) |\n| X | https://x.com/thesephist | About 42.8k followers, 21.8k lifetime posts. Recent activity looks sparse (third-party snapshot, about 2026-11-09) ([src](https://superx.so/creators/thesephist)) |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | No LinkedIn link on his site ([src](https://thesephist.com/)) |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/thesephist | 2.8k followers as of 2026-11-09. Pinned repos: monocle (1.5k stars), tabloid (630), libsearch (387), torus (346), oak (251), unim.press (250) ([src](https://github.com/thesephist)) |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nAll dates and links come from his talks list unless another source is given ([src](https://thesephist.com/)).\n- 2026-05-07 — Zero Prime podcast ep 37 (Pete Soderling, AI Council) — \"Everyone wants bigger context windows. Linus Lee thinks that's the wrong instinct\" — https://www.buzzsprout.com/2033251/episodes/19147629-ep-37-everyone-wants-bigger-context-windows-linus-lee-thinks-that-s-the-wrong-instinct — written Q&A at https://petesoder.substack.com/p/linus-lee-says-your-agent-is-too. Covers sub-agents as expensive tools, the token-scale failure mode, and Thrive's work on \"one very specific team\" (added 2026-11-09)\n- 2026-06 — Cursor Compile '26 — \"Notational Intelligence\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rv_VS189aVI — talk at the Cursor developer conference. Cursor is a Thrive portfolio company.\n- 2026-05 — AI Council — \"Context Engineering at the Frontier\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=knUetkjGbpw — posted on Thrive Capital's YouTube channel ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=knUetkjGbpw)). A podcast listing says he argued bigger context windows are the wrong approach for agents (unverified; summary from a search snippet, page not read directly).\n- 2025-08 — Dialectic — \"Engineering for aliveness\" — https://dialectic.fm/linus-lee\n  - Update 2026-11-09: full transcript read (host Jackson Dahl, published 2025-08-04). Thrive segment at 1:14:58 covers the internal tools team, the \"Ironman suit\" mandate, and venture as a services business. See \"Differential insight\" ([src](https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee)).\n- 2024-12 — Thinking with Sand (MIT Media Lab virtual series) — \"Thinking about representations\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Tt_hDVN5TZE\n- 2024-07 — South Park Commons Demo Night — \"A notebook for seeing with language models\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bPSq7oAd6-o\n- 2024-04 — AI in Production — \"Seeing like a language model\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PU1Sy7A3ftY\n- 2024-04 — Cognitive Revolution — \"Interfacing with AI\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t8QJEGILYfA\n- 2024-02 — AI-First User Interfaces — \"Seeing like a model\" (Prism demo) — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=73JkiEClyO4\n- 2023-12-12 — Every, \"How Do You Use ChatGPT?\" with Dan Shipper — https://every.to/chain-of-thought/how-an-ai-researcher-uses-chatgpt-and-notion-ai — on using AI without losing your own judgment, plus personal prototypes like an iMessage bot ([src](https://every.to/chain-of-thought/how-an-ai-researcher-uses-chatgpt-and-notion-ai))\n- 2023-11 — Unsupervised Learning — \"A Full Deep Dive into Notion AI\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LEIfoMjDDXs\n- 2023-10 — AI Engineer Summit — \"The Hidden Life of Embeddings\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YvobVu1l7GI\n- 2023-08-22 — Thursday Nights in AI (Outset Capital and Generally Intelligent; Ali Rohde, Kanjun Qiu) — https://thursdaynightsinai.substack.com/p/recording-notion-ai-lead-linus-lee — argued that chat throws away context (the document, cursor, collaborators) and that AI augments more than it replaces ([src](https://thursdaynightsinai.substack.com/p/recording-notion-ai-lead-linus-lee))\n- 2023-06-01 — Latent Space (swyx, Alessio) — \"Building the AI × UX Scenius\" — https://www.latent.space/p/ai-interfaces-and-notion — \"Most knowledge work isn't a text-generation task.\" He also suggested models should sometimes write auditable programs ([src](https://www.latent.space/p/ai-interfaces-and-notion))\n- 2023-04 — LLMs in Production — \"Generative Interfaces beyond Chat\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rd-J3hmycQs\n- 2023-04 — Lightning AI Meetup — \"Building Generative Interfaces\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jIHDQNWoMjA\n- 2023-03 — MIT Generative AI Summit — \"The Future of Creation\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fyjuddq9Csg\n- 2023-01-19 — The Gradient Podcast ep. 56 (Daniel Bashir) — \"At the Boundary of Machine and Mind\" — https://thegradientpub.substack.com/p/linus-lee-at-the-boundary-of-machine — covered notation vs. language, why prompt engineering is inefficient, and Monocle ([src](https://thegradientpub.substack.com/p/linus-lee-at-the-boundary-of-machine)). His site lists it as Dec '22.\n- 2022-08 — Betaworks Render — \"Sentence Gradients\" — https://vimeo.com/showcase/9758634/video/740893906\n- 2021-12 — Go Time — \"Go beyond work\" — https://changelog.com/gotime/212\n- 2021-12 and 2021-05 — GopherCon and GopherCon EU — \"When Toy Languages Grow Up\" — https://youtu.be/ALwmdcFiuGg\n- 2021-11 — Metamuse — \"Self-made tools\" — https://museapp.com/podcast/42-self-made-tools/\n- 2021-08 — The Changelog — \"Building software for yourself\" — https://changelog.com/podcast/455\n\n## How they think\n- **Chat is a transitional interface.** Exposing raw text completion is likely a weak long-term bet. Shared documents and workspaces beat conversation for complex work: \"Conversations are a terrible way to keep track of information\" ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/latent/)).\n- **Let users steer meaning directly.** Instead of prompt-and-retry, let users navigate a model's latent space and drag text toward a concept ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/latent/)).\n- **Interpretability as an interface primitive, not just a safety tool.** Prism trained sparse autoencoders on about 31M sentence embeddings to surface tens of thousands of human-readable features you can edit ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/prism/)).\n- **A \"synthesizer for thought.\"** Math turned sound into synth components. Interpretable LMs could do the same for writing: heatmaps, semantic diffs, and shareable style features ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/synth/)).\n- **Notation as intelligence.** Better notations expand what people can think ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/notation/), [src](https://thegradientpub.substack.com/p/linus-lee-at-the-boundary-of-machine)).\n- **Agent engineering, 2025 view:** compose *tasks* (inputs, outputs, and evals), not *implementations* (prompts, tools, models), so parts can be swapped as models improve. This is consistent with the Bitter Lesson ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/task-composition/)).\n- **AI as augmentation.** Humans keep taste and final judgment ([src](https://thursdaynightsinai.substack.com/p/recording-notion-ai-lead-linus-lee)).\n- **Contrarian views:** argued against chat as the endpoint in 2022, before ChatGPT launched ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/latent/), dated 2022-05-01). Argued prompt engineering is an inefficient control surface ([src](https://thegradientpub.substack.com/p/linus-lee-at-the-boundary-of-machine)). Reportedly sceptical of ever-larger context windows for agents (unverified; see AI Council talk above).\n- **Calls right or wrong:** The 2022 \"beyond chat / shared workspace\" thesis anticipated the later move to canvas, document, and agent-workspace interfaces, but that is this profile's interpretation, not a sourced claim. His SAE-on-embeddings report was published 2024-06-22 ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/)). The latent.space wiki says most of the research happened Dec 2023 to Feb 2024 (secondary; the wiki notes it is pending editorial review) ([src](https://latent.space/wiki/linus-lee-582ed9e8/)). No sourced wrong calls found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Imagining better interfaces to language models\" (2022-05-01) — the founding \"beyond chat\" essay ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/latent/)).\n2. \"Notational intelligence\" (2022-01-02) — why notation, not just language, is how people think better ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/notation/)).\n3. \"Prism: mapping interpretable concepts and features in a latent space of language\" (2024-06-22) — his main research report on SAEs over embeddings ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/prism/)).\n4. \"Synthesizer for thought\" (2024-06-23) — the product vision that follows from Prism ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/synth/)).\n5. \"Compose tasks, not implementations\" (2025-08-17) — his most practical piece for teams building agents, written during the Thrive era ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/task-composition/)).\n- Bonus, for how he thinks about tools and craft: \"Build UNIX, not Uber\" ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/legacy/)) and \"Good creative tools are virtuosic and open-ended\" ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/virtuosity/)).\n\n## Social engagement\n- Recent X posts sampled in a third-party snapshot are sparse and personal, such as replies about where good work comes from ([src](https://superx.so/creators/thesephist)). Lifetime volume is high (21.8k posts since Dec 2012) ([src](https://superx.so/creators/thesephist)).\n- Topic and reply-graph analysis of X: nothing found. X could not be read directly, and the SuperX breakdown is paywalled ([src](https://superx.so/creators/thesephist)).\n- His network, judging from podcasts: the AI × UX / tools-for-thought crowd. On Latent Space he cited Andy Matuschak, Geoffrey Litt, Maggie Appleton, and Paul Butler (AI×UX NYC) ([src](https://www.latent.space/p/ai-interfaces-and-notion)). These are mentions, not confirmed close ties.\n- Amplified or hosted by swyx and Alessio (Latent Space) ([src](https://www.latent.space/p/ai-interfaces-and-notion)), Dan Shipper (Every) ([src](https://every.to/chain-of-thought/how-an-ai-researcher-uses-chatgpt-and-notion-ai)), Outset Capital and Generally Intelligent ([src](https://thursdaynightsinai.substack.com/p/recording-notion-ai-lead-linus-lee)), Betaworks ([src](https://www.betaworks.com/event/ai-week-how-to-think-in-the-third-millennium)), and Thrive itself (AI Council talk on Thrive's YouTube) ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=knUetkjGbpw)).\n\n## Portfolio\nNo personal investments found. Searches for angel deals turned up nothing, and his role at Thrive is engineering and platform work, not deal-leading ([src](https://aicouncil.com/profile/linus-lee), [src](https://thesephist.com/posts/thrive/)). Dorm Room Fund (2018–2021) was a team role, and his post names no companies ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/drf/)).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| nothing found | | | | | |\n\nThrive portfolio companies he has publicly touched (these are not his deals):\n- Cursor (Anysphere): he gave a talk at Cursor's Compile '26 conference in June 2026 ([src](https://thesephist.com/), [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rv_VS189aVI)). Thrive led Anysphere's $900M round in June 2025 ([src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/06/05/anysphere-raises-900m-ai-powered-cursor-code-editor)). No source shows he worked on the deal.\n- No essay names a specific Thrive portfolio company. His Thrive announcement names none ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/thrive/)).\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. He is not a deal lead, and no sourced story ties him to a specific Thrive investment.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Thrive Capital | EIR and advisor, later Head of AI | 2024-09 | [src](https://thesephist.com/posts/thrive/), [src](https://aicouncil.com/profile/linus-lee) |\n| Notion | Research Engineer, AI team | 2023-01 to 2024-08 | [src](https://thesephist.com/posts/notion/), [src](https://thesephist.com/posts/thrive/) |\n| Simon Last, Ivan Zhao (Notion) | He credits them with the original Notion AI prototype he joined after | 2023 | [src](https://www.latent.space/p/ai-interfaces-and-notion) |\n| Betaworks | First Researcher in Residence | 2022 | [src](https://www.betaworks.com/event/ai-week-how-to-think-in-the-third-millennium) |\n| Replit | Former software engineer (dates unverified) | unverified | [src](https://thegradientpub.substack.com/p/linus-lee-at-the-boundary-of-machine) |\n| Hack Club | Former software engineer (dates unverified) | unverified | [src](https://thesephist.com/) |\n| Spensa | Former software engineer (dates unverified) | unverified | [src](https://thesephist.com/) |\n| Ideaflow | Core team, full-time | 2021-02 | [src](https://thesephist.com/posts/ideaflow/) |\n| Dorm Room Fund | SF investment team member | 2018 to 2021 | [src](https://thesephist.com/posts/drf/) |\n| Dan Shipper (Every) | Acknowledged in Prism report; hosted him on podcast | 2023-12 | [src](https://thesephist.com/posts/prism/), [src](https://every.to/chain-of-thought/how-an-ai-researcher-uses-chatgpt-and-notion-ai) |\n| Gytis Daujotas, Matthew Siu, Theo Bleier, Maya Bakir | Acknowledged in Prism report | 2024-06 | [src](https://thesephist.com/posts/prism/) |\n| swyx, Alessio Fanelli (Latent Space) | Podcast hosts | 2023-06 | [src](https://www.latent.space/p/ai-interfaces-and-notion) |\n| Goodfire | Publicly praised its interpretability work (no investment link found) | 2025-08 | [src](https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee) |\n| Weber Wong (Flora) | Friend, per his writing as read on Dialectic; identity via NYU ITP | by 2025 | [src](https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee), [src](https://itp.nyu.edu/itp/?p=15764) |\n| Jackson Dahl (Dialectic) | Podcast host | 2025-08 | [src](https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee) |\n| Pete Soderling (AI Council, Zero Prime) | Interviewer; AI Council speaker host | 2026-05 | [src](https://petesoder.substack.com/p/linus-lee-says-your-agent-is-too) |\n| Kanjun Qiu, Ali Rohde | Fireside chat hosts (Thursday Nights in AI) | 2023-08 | [src](https://thursdaynightsinai.substack.com/p/recording-notion-ai-lead-linus-lee) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did his title change from EIR/advisor to Head of AI? Which portfolio companies has he worked hands-on with? Neither is public.\n- Exact dates at Replit, Hack Club, and Spensa.\n- Did he speak at Cursor Compile because of Thrive's Cursor stake, or independently?\n- Two research reports not covered above: \"Contra: a steerable latent variable model for language\" ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/contra/)) and \"Inventing writing from scratch\" ([src](https://thesephist.com/posts/glyph/)), both listed on his research page ([src](https://thesephist.com/research/)). Neither has been read yet.\n- Is \"lost to wonder\" (his X bio, losttowonder.com) a new project? The site shows only that phrase, and ownership is unconfirmed ([src](https://superx.so/creators/thesephist)).\n- Does he make personal angel investments? Nothing found.\n- What exactly does he argue about context windows in the AI Council 2026 talk? Needs a transcript.\n- His X reply graph: who he actually talks with.\n- (2026-11-09) Does Thrive's internal people-data tooling feed sourcing for frontier-lab and AI deals? Does Thrive hold Goodfire or Flora? Nothing found.\n- (2026-11-09) Where did he first state the \"tens of billions of tokens\" figure? The interviewer says he said it publicly, but the source was not found.\n\n## Sources\n- https://thesephist.com/\n- https://thesephist.com/posts/\n- https://thesephist.com/posts/thrive/\n- https://thesephist.com/posts/notion/\n- https://thesephist.com/posts/drf/\n- https://thesephist.com/posts/ideaflow/\n- https://thesephist.com/posts/latent/\n- https://thesephist.com/posts/notation/\n- https://thesephist.com/posts/prism/\n- https://thesephist.com/posts/synth/\n- https://thesephist.com/posts/task-composition/\n- https://thesephist.com/posts/legacy/\n- https://thesephist.com/posts/virtuosity/\n- https://thesephist.com/research/\n- https://github.com/thesephist\n- https://superx.so/creators/thesephist\n- https://aicouncil.com/profile/linus-lee\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=knUetkjGbpw\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rv_VS189aVI\n- https://www.latent.space/p/ai-interfaces-and-notion\n- https://latent.space/wiki/linus-lee-582ed9e8/\n- https://thursdaynightsinai.substack.com/p/recording-notion-ai-lead-linus-lee\n- https://every.to/chain-of-thought/how-an-ai-researcher-uses-chatgpt-and-notion-ai\n- https://thegradientpub.substack.com/p/linus-lee-at-the-boundary-of-machine\n- https://www.betaworks.com/event/ai-week-how-to-think-in-the-third-millennium\n- https://siliconangle.com/2025/06/05/anysphere-raises-900m-ai-powered-cursor-code-editor\n- https://www.dialectic.fm/linus-lee (full transcript, Dialectic ep 24, 2025-08-04)\n- https://newsletter.dialectic.fm/p/dreaming-of-software-that-makes-us\n- https://petesoder.substack.com/p/linus-lee-says-your-agent-is-too (2026-05-07)\n- https://www.buzzsprout.com/2033251/episodes/19147629-ep-37-everyone-wants-bigger-context-windows-linus-lee-thinks-that-s-the-wrong-instinct\n- https://itp.nyu.edu/itp/?p=15764\n- https://thesephist.com/posts/flame/ (2026-11-09; no investing content)\n- https://thesephist.com/posts/wonder/ (2025-04-12)\n", "person:lior-simon": "\n# Lior Simon\n\n## One-line take\nThe Cyberstarts partner whose name is on the firm's newest AI-security first cheques, apart from Raanan's Glow.\n\n## Crux\nHe is the working partner on the 2025-26 seeds. He wins on the firm's method (first cheque, CISO feedback loop), not on a personal brand: there is no bio on the firm's site and no public writing. The constraint is the same as the firm's: security only, and Raanan is the only named principal on the fund filings.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Vega: first money in.** The launch release names \"Cyberstarts, which were their first seed investors\", ahead of Accel leading the combined $65M ([release, 2025-09-16](https://www.newswire.com/news/vega-raises-65-million-in-funding-to-redefine-the-boundaries-of-22638374)). Vega raised a $120M Series B five months later (ledger).\n- **NewCore: pre-seed co-lead with Index's Shardul Shah.** The $16M pre-seed and the expanded seed were announced together as $66M ([SaaS News](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/newcore-raises-66m-seed/)).\n- **His framing of NewCore:** identity built \"as if cybersecurity experts had designed it from day one\" ([Ynet](https://www.ynetnews.com/business/article/hkif7cabge)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Security, not frontier AI, but he is a rising partner doing first cheques in AI-native companies that reached large rounds within months.\n\n## Background\nNot found. The firm site has no bio. Calcalist coverage of Fund II (2020) reports his appointment as partner (seen in search results; article not opened).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://cyberstarts.com/approach-and-team | Name and photo only |\n| Substack / blog | not found | |\n| X | not found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://il.linkedin.com/in/lior-simon-b4610577 | surfaced in search; not opened |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found.\n\n## How they think\nOnly press quotes exist. On Vega he praised the founders as \"seasoned leaders and visionary builders\" ([release](https://www.newswire.com/news/vega-raises-65-million-in-funding-to-redefine-the-boundaries-of-22638374)). No essays or talks found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Vega launch release](https://www.newswire.com/news/vega-raises-65-million-in-funding-to-redefine-the-boundaries-of-22638374).\n2. [Ynet on NewCore](https://www.ynetnews.com/business/article/hkif7cabge).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Vega | Seed (part of $65M seed + A) | 2025-09 (announced) | first seed investor | Accel (lead), Redpoint, CRV | [release](https://www.newswire.com/news/vega-raises-65-million-in-funding-to-redefine-the-boundaries-of-22638374) |\n| NewCore | Pre-seed ($16M) | 2025 | co-led | Index Ventures | [SaaS News](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/newcore-raises-66m-seed/) |\n| NewCore | Seed (expanded; $66M total) | 2026-06 | unclear | Evolution Equity, Index | [Ynet](https://www.ynetnews.com/business/article/hkif7cabge) |\n\n## Deal stories\nSee Differential insight. Intro paths to the Vega and NewCore founders are not documented.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Gili Raanan | founder of his firm | 2020 | [firm page](../firms/cyberstarts.md) |\n| Shardul Shah (Index) | co-lead on NewCore's pre-seed | 2025 | [SaaS News](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/newcore-raises-66m-seed/) |\n| Accel | led Vega after his seed | 2025 | [release](https://www.newswire.com/news/vega-raises-65-million-in-funding-to-redefine-the-boundaries-of-22638374) |\n\n## Open questions\n- His background before Cyberstarts and the exact date he became general partner.\n- Which other portfolio companies he leads.\n\n## Sources\nLinked inline.\n", "person:lior-susan": "\n# Lior Susan\n\nPronouns: he/him. The Cerebras S-1/A uses \"Mr. Susan\" and \"his\" ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm)).\n\nUpdate 2026-11-10: resolved DataPelago (compute-acceleration board seat), added a second source for the Cerebras Series A size, and re-checked Eclipse's blog for new Susan bylines (none since 2026-09-24). WebSearch was unavailable this session (turn budget exhausted). Sourcery (403), Bloomberg (403) and X (402 previously) stayed unreachable, so there are still no podcast or X quotes of his.\n\n## One-line take\nSusan is the controlling GP behind one of the few early AI-chip wins that returned a fund: Eclipse co-led Cerebras's 2016 Series A and still held 7.3% before the May 2026 IPO. He is the person who made that position pay by brokering the G42 and OpenAI customer deals himself ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm), [Eclipse](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He invests like an operator who sells for the company. On Cerebras he was not a passive Series A holder. He used his relationship with Sheikh Tahnoon, which runs through his brother Ido Susan's company DriveNets, to open G42, which made up 85% of Cerebras's 2024 revenue. He then raised fast inference with Sam Altman in early 2025, which led to the 750 MW OpenAI agreement ([Eclipse](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era), [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm)). He backs physical, capital-heavy companies, holds through long stretches without a product (Cerebras had 18 months of no working chip at $8M a month), and adds to winners through SPVs and a continuity fund.\n- **What constrains him:** His attention. Since August 2023 he has also been CEO of Bright Machines, an Eclipse company he co-founded; it is his third stint as CEO. He chairs Owlet and sits on at least ten private boards ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm)). His scope is physical industry by his own definition, so pure-software labs fall outside it ([Made in Eclipse](https://eclipse.capital/blog/Made-in-Eclipse)).\n- **What he'd never do (inferred from his own scope statement, not stated as a rule):** back a consumer or knowledge-work software company. He wrote that Eclipse is \"not disrupting consumer sectors and knowledge work\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/Made-in-Eclipse)). His AI exposure is compute, power and physical AI, not model labs.\n\n## Differential insight\nAll mirrored in `data/rows/lior-susan-insights.csv`. Note: no podcast transcript, Substack or X post of his was reachable this session. WebSearch was exhausted, X returned HTTP 402, and the Sourcery page returned 403. The insights below come from the S-1/A filing, his own bylined posts, an Eclipse-authored post that quotes him and Feldman, and the WashU 20VC transcript already in this repo.\n\n- **How Eclipse holds Cerebras (filing).** Eclipse's 13,466,197 shares sit in four vehicles: Fund I (5.5M), **Eclipse SPV II (6.5M, the largest block)**, SPV XIII (0.6M) and Continuity Fund I (0.8M). The S-1/A says \"Mr. Susan is the sole managing member of the general partner\" of each one. His winner-scaling method is deal-by-deal SPVs plus a continuity vehicle on top of a small Fund I position ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm)).\n- **Who was in the Series A (filing).** \"Messrs. Susan, Vassallo, and Vishria were elected by the holders of our Series A\" preferred. Vassallo is Foundation Capital and Vishria is Benchmark, so Eclipse, Foundation and Benchmark all have Series A board seats. All three, plus Alpha Wave and Fidelity, hold more than 5% ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm)). The Series A preferred carries a $26.97M liquidation preference (same filing), which roughly sizes the round.\n- **Timing.** Eclipse wrote its first Cerebras memo \"In March 2016, less than a year after founding Eclipse\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)). Feldman: \"Eclipse was among the first to believe in us.\" (same post).\n- **Patience under burn.** Feldman wrote that \"Pierre and Lior didn't flinch\" during the 2017–2019 stretch with no working chip ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)).\n- **G42 deal origin.** \"We needed someone bold enough to take the risk,\" Susan said, explaining why the first big customer had to be outside Silicon Valley. Eclipse and Cerebras made more than twenty trips to Abu Dhabi ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)).\n- **OpenAI deal origin.** Susan said there was \"nobody bigger or better than OpenAI\" to validate fast inference, and he raised it with Altman, who was already an early Cerebras investor, in early 2025 ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)).\n- **How he framed Cerebras.** \"we didn't see another chip company,\" and backing it was \"an audacious bet against the consensus\" ([his IPO post](https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-engine-of-the-intelligent-world-goes-public)).\n- **Origin of the thesis, plus an admitted miss.** At Flex in Zhuhai in 2013 he was told a factory would be running in nine months: \"And I just didn't believe it.\" It was built on time, and that is the root of his conviction about build speed ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/Made-in-Eclipse), [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/building-turbines-on-ai-timelines)).\n- **The power bottleneck is his current AI bet.** \"Without energy, we have nothing.\" He frames Parallax as closing the gap in AI power supply ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/building-turbines-on-ai-timelines)).\n- **Second compute board seat (new 2026-11-10).** DataPelago, where he sits on the board per the S-1/A, is compute infrastructure. Its engine runs on GPUs, FPGAs and CPUs and \"accelerates data processing for GenAI and Lakehouse Analytics.\" Eclipse is listed first among its investors, alongside Qualcomm Ventures, Alter, Taiwania Capital and Nautilus ([datapelago.ai](https://www.datapelago.ai/), checked 2026-11-10). Round, date and check size were not found.\n- **Series A timing cross-check.** TechCrunch puts the Series A at $25M and says Benchmark's Vishria co-led it. Benchmark bought ~80% of its shares in early rounds for ~$18M and the rest later for ~$250M. Eclipse is not mentioned in the piece ([TechCrunch, 2026-05-14](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/)). Read together with Susan's \"co-led\" post and the S-1/A director election, this points to an Eclipse-Benchmark co-lead with Foundation in the round. Eclipse's March 2016 memo suggests Eclipse was in early, but no source says who committed first.\n- **LP view (WashU).** WashU's CIO, Scott Wilson: \"We got Cerebras through a fund called Eclipse\"; Harry Stebbings replies \"Oh yeah, Lior.\" ([20VC](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026), ~51:00; transcript at `sources/transcripts/2026-11-08-20vc-scott-wilson-washu.txt`).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, with a caveat on scope.**\n- **AI chips and compute (core of my lens):** Cerebras. He was in the 2016 Series A, before AI hardware was fashionable, and his customer introductions created most of its revenue. This is the template I want to learn from.\n- **Physical-AI and AI-native automation:** Noetive (frontier-AI operational intelligence, $41M seed led by Eclipse, his co-byline) and Bright Machines (software-defined manufacturing, which he runs). Both are close to my \"Serval-like\" automation interest, but for industrial work rather than knowledge work.\n- **AI infrastructure power:** Parallax ($42M seed co-led with Lux, Founders Fund and Valor; his byline). The Series A was led by Greylock and General Catalyst.\n- **Large seeds:** Eclipse leads $15M–$42M first rounds and builds companies in-house, which fits beside my ~$20M checks.\n- **Caveats:** He does not back foundation-model labs. Most of his boards are industrial, health or aerospace (Owlet, Skyryse, Ursa Major, Augury). DataPelago (board seat) is GPU/FPGA data-processing acceleration for GenAI workloads, so it is a second compute-adjacent seat ([src](https://www.datapelago.ai/)). Genesis AI ($105M seed) and Oxide are Eclipse deals that fit my lens, but other partners' names are on them (Charly Mwangi, Seth Winterroth), not his. Winterroth bylined the post on Eclipse leading Oxide's $445M Series D ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/built-for-the-compute-ownership-era)).\n\n## Background\n- Former member of an \"elite Special Forces unit in the Israel Defense Force\" (dates not given) ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm)).\n- 2008-04 to 2012: Advisor at Intucell (self-optimizing network software), through its sale to Cisco (S-1/A).\n- Part of Formation 8, which later disbanded; role and dates not given ([TechCrunch 2019](https://techcrunch.com/2019/02/06/eclipse-ventures-in-palo-alto-just-added-two-more-big-wheels-as-general-partners)).\n- 2012-06 to 2015-01: Founder and General Partner of LabIX, the hardware investment platform of Flextronics (S-1/A). He was in Zhuhai for Flex in 2013 and returned to the US in 2014 ([Made in Eclipse](https://eclipse.capital/blog/Made-in-Eclipse)).\n- 2015-01 to present: Founder and Managing Partner of Eclipse (S-1/A). The founding year conflicts across his own writing: \"This is why we started Eclipse in 2015\" ([Made in Eclipse](https://eclipse.capital/blog/Made-in-Eclipse)) versus 2016 \"was also the year we founded Eclipse\" ([IPO post](https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-engine-of-the-intelligent-world-goes-public)).\n- 2018-01 to present: co-founder and Executive Chairman of Bright Machines. CEO 2018-01 to 2018-05, 2021-12 to 2022-12, and 2023-08 to present (S-1/A).\n- Age 42 as of the S-1/A (2026-05). Education not stated in any source reached.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://eclipse.capital/team/ | Listed as Founder and CEO (checked 2026-11-09 by another agent; see `firms/eclipse.md`) |\n| Substack / blog | https://eclipse.capital/blog | Bylined posts: Made in Eclipse (2025-12-18), Cerebras IPO (2026-05-14), Noetive (2026-09-16, with Ryan Gibson), Parallax (2026-09-24) |\n| X | unverified (x.com/liorsusan returned HTTP 402) | Handle and follower count not confirmed |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-04-07: Bloomberg Tech video on the $1.3B Fund VI and Early Growth III raise ([src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/videos/2026-04-07/eclipse-raises-1-3b-to-back-manufacturing-robotics-video)). Not watched.\n- 2026-05: StrictlyVC event after the Cerebras IPO, reported in secondary copies only ([src](https://inforcapital.com/news/for-eclipse-the-25b-cerebras-win-is-just-the-start-of-realizing-its-physical-world-thesis/)). The secondary piece paraphrases him and has no direct quotes.\n- Sourcery episode surfaced by an earlier agent ([src](https://podbay.fm/p/sourcery/e/1789992954)). Returned 403; guest unconfirmed.\n- No transcript of his own reached this session.\n\n## How they think\n- **Physical economy over SaaS.** He argues the Silicon Valley SaaS playbook won't rebuild industry ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/Made-in-Eclipse)). He has said ~85% of global output is physical (secondary paraphrase, [src](https://inforcapital.com/news/for-eclipse-the-25b-cerebras-win-is-just-the-start-of-realizing-its-physical-world-thesis/)).\n- **Ecosystem as moat.** Portfolio companies share suppliers, customers and talent, described as a \"connected industrial economy\" ([Fund VI post](https://eclipse.capital/blog/announcing-eclipse-fund-vi-and-early-growth-iii), firm-authored).\n- **Compute as a rebuild, not a component.** He saw Cerebras as computing \"rebuilt around intelligence\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-engine-of-the-intelligent-world-goes-public)).\n- **Energy is the AI bottleneck** ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/building-turbines-on-ai-timelines)).\n- **Industrial policy, not protectionism.** He wants US industrial resilience, not punitive or isolationist measures ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/Made-in-Eclipse)).\n- **Calls:** Right on Cerebras (~$147M in, ~$2.5B at IPO, secondary: [src](https://inforcapital.com/news/for-eclipse-the-25b-cerebras-win-is-just-the-start-of-realizing-its-physical-world-thesis/)). Mixed on Bright Machines: he has had to step back in as CEO three times (S-1/A dates). That is an inference; no source calls it a failure. Lucira Health (he was on the board 2020–2022) is not assessed here.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"How an Unreasonable Pitch Became the Backbone of the Reasoning Era\" (Eclipse, 2026-06-24): the full Cerebras story, including the G42 and OpenAI intros ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)).\n2. \"The Engine of the Intelligent World Goes Public\" (Susan, 2026-05-14): why he backed Cerebras ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-engine-of-the-intelligent-world-goes-public)).\n3. \"Made in Eclipse\" (Susan, 2025-12-18): the Zhuhai origin story and his thesis ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/Made-in-Eclipse)).\n4. \"Building Turbines on AI Timelines\" (Susan, 2026-09-24): his AI-power thesis ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/building-turbines-on-ai-timelines)).\n5. Cerebras S-1/A, management and principal-stockholders sections: his bio, boards and holding vehicles ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X was not reachable (HTTP 402), and no reply or amplification graph was checked.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cerebras Systems | Series A (preferred liq. pref. $26.97M) | 2016 (memo 2016-03) | co-led | Benchmark (Vishria), Foundation Capital (Vassallo) also elected Series A directors | [IPO post](https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-engine-of-the-intelligent-world-goes-public), [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm) |\n| Cerebras Systems | Board seat; 7.3% pre-IPO via 4 Eclipse vehicles | 2023-04 (board) | n/a | Alpha Wave, Fidelity (>5% holders) | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm) |\n| Bright Machines | Co-founder; Exec Chair, CEO | 2018-01 | n/a | | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm) |\n| Noetive | Seed $41M | 2026-09-16 | yes (Eclipse) | Craft, Westly Group, Swish, Factory, Incite, Gigascale, Operator Partners, Liquid 2 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy) |\n| Parallax | Seed $42M | announced 2026-09-24 | co-led | Lux, Founders Fund, Valor | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/building-turbines-on-ai-timelines) |\n| Prime Minute | Board (Eclipse led $15M pre-seed) | board by 2026-05; round 2026-09-24 | yes (Eclipse) | 8VC (per firms/eclipse.md) | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm), [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-resilience-prime) |\n| Owlet | Board, Chair | board 2015-03; chair 2021-07 | | | S-1/A |\n| DataPelago | Board seat; Eclipse listed as investor (GPU/FPGA data-processing acceleration for GenAI) | round/date not found | | Alter, Qualcomm Ventures, Taiwania Capital, Nautilus Venture Partners | S-1/A; [datapelago.ai](https://www.datapelago.ai/) |\n| Skyryse, Ursa Major, Augury, Cybertoka, Dutch Pet, The Heart Company | Board seats | as of 2026-05 | | | S-1/A |\n| Lucira Health | Board | 2020-08 to 2022-12 | | | S-1/A |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Cerebras.** Feldman reached out early to Pierre Lamond, who had backed him at SeaMicro. Eclipse wrote its memo in March 2016 and co-led the Series A. Susan, Vassallo (Foundation) and Vishria (Benchmark) hold the Series A board seats; Susan's board service dates from April 2023, and the earlier seat-holder is not stated ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm)). Lamond's call to Morris Chang got Cerebras its TSMC meeting. Through 2017–2019 there was no working chip, and the team says the Eclipse partners held firm. Susan then used his brother's DriveNets relationship to reach Sheikh Tahnoon and G42 (a pilot, then Condor Galaxy), and he pitched Altman in early 2025, which led to the 750 MW agreement signed on Christmas Eve ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)). He grew the position through Eclipse SPV II, SPV XIII and Continuity Fund I. WashU got its exposure as an Eclipse LP ([20VC](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026)).\n- **Noetive.** Built in-house with Chief AI Officer Amir Frenkel; Eclipse led the $41M seed. Susan co-authored the post with Ryan Gibson ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy)).\n- **Parallax.** Susan bylined the announcement. He co-led the $42M seed; Greylock and General Catalyst came in later at the $75M Series A ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/building-turbines-on-ai-timelines)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Eclipse | Founder, Managing Partner; sole managing member of the Cerebras-holding GPs | 2015-01 | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm) |\n| Andrew Feldman (Cerebras) | Portfolio CEO; board colleague | 2016 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era) |\n| Pierre Lamond | Eclipse co-founding partner (now Emeritus); Cerebras originator | 2015/16 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era) |\n| Ido Susan / DriveNets | Brother; founder of DriveNets, his channel to Sheikh Tahnoon | n/a | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era) |\n| Sheikh Tahnoon bin Zayed / G42 | Relationship he used to open G42 | before ~2021 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era) |\n| Sam Altman | He pitched Altman on Cerebras fast inference | 2025 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era) |\n| Steve Vassallo (Foundation), Eric Vishria (Benchmark) | Fellow Series A-elected Cerebras directors | 2023-04 | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm) |\n| Ryan Gibson | Eclipse partner; co-author on Noetive | 2022 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy) |\n| Flex (Flextronics) | Founded LabIX inside Flex | 2012-06 | S-1/A |\n| Formation 8 | Former affiliation | n/a | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2019/02/06/eclipse-ventures-in-palo-alto-just-added-two-more-big-wheels-as-general-partners) |\n| WashU endowment (Scott Wilson) | Eclipse LP; got Cerebras via Eclipse | n/a | [20VC](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n\nSlug note: rows use `eclipse`. The repo has both `firms/eclipse.md` and `firms/eclipse-ventures.md`, which the main session should merge.\n\n## Open questions\n- Does he have an X account, and what does he post? (402 this session.)\n- Podcast transcripts: none reached. The Sourcery and Bloomberg segments are worth pulling.\n- Who held Eclipse's Cerebras board seat before 2023-04 (likely Lamond, unconfirmed)?\n- Size and timing of SPV II and SPV XIII: which rounds did they fund, and who were the LPs (WashU?)?\n- DataPelago: round, date and Eclipse's check size (the company is resolved as compute acceleration; see above).\n- Has Eclipse made any AI-chip bet after Cerebras? AheadComputing is listed under \"Advanced Compute\" on Eclipse's site, but he is not tied to it.\n- Bright Machines: why the three CEO stints, and how they affect his time at Eclipse.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2021728/000162828026033143/cerebras-sx1a2.htm (Cerebras S-1/A, filed 2026-05-11)\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-engine-of-the-intelligent-world-goes-public\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/Made-in-Eclipse\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/building-turbines-on-ai-timelines\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/announcing-eclipse-fund-vi-and-early-growth-iii\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-resilience-prime (via firms/eclipse.md)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2019/02/06/eclipse-ventures-in-palo-alto-just-added-two-more-big-wheels-as-general-partners\n- https://inforcapital.com/news/for-eclipse-the-25b-cerebras-win-is-just-the-start-of-realizing-its-physical-world-thesis/ (secondary)\n- https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026 and sources/transcripts/2026-11-08-20vc-scott-wilson-washu.txt\n- https://www.datapelago.ai/ (checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/videos/2026-04-07/eclipse-raises-1-3b-to-back-manufacturing-robotics-video (not watched)\n", "person:lisa-han": "\n# Lisa Han\n\n## One-line take\nShe is an operator-turned-Lightspeed partner who writes the memos on Lightspeed's applied-AI leads (Suno B, Distyl A, Sandstone A, Luel seed). On Thinking Machines, though, she is one of three names on the firm's page and wrote nothing of her own.\n\n## Crux\n- **Correction to the premise: not a fast promotion.** Lightspeed hired her straight in as a partner on 2022-10-17. The announcement says \"new partners Lisa Han and Yonit Wiseman join the enterprise team\" and calls her \"a 2019 graduate of our Lightspeed Scouts program\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/from-tel-aviv-to-the-bay-scaling-the-lightspeed-enterprise-team/)). She came laterally from Gradient Ventures (Google's AI fund). There was never an associate or principal step at Lightspeed. The real pattern is **scout to lateral partner**: Lightspeed tested her as a scout for about 3 years, then hired her at partner level.\n- **Why she wins.** She was the first business hire at two startups, Ramp and Atrium ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/lisa-han/)), so founders see her as one of them. She also writes thesis-first market maps that come before deals: the legaltech piece (2023-12), the enterprise-AI market map (2024-08) and the voice piece (2024-09). The deals she leads follow those theses (EvenUp, then Sandstone, in legal AI; Distyl in enterprise GenAI; Luel in training data).\n- **What constrains her.** She sits on the Enterprise team. Her personal authority shows on **$30M-scale applied-AI A rounds and seeds**, not on frontier-lab rounds. On Thinking Machines, Lightspeed's page lists Ravi Mhatre (co-founder) first and James Alcorn too ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/thinking-machines/)). a16z led that round ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/15/mira-muratis-thinking-machines-lab-is-worth-12b-in-seed-round/)).\n- **What she would not do (by her own writing).** Back an AI legal startup with too-broad scope. She believes winners take \"a pointed approach to solving 2-3 key workflows\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/legaltech-x-ai-the-lightspeed-view/)).\n\n## Differential insight\nAlmost everything here comes from Lightspeed's own site. Off-site sourcing (podcasts, X, press interviews) could not be run because the session's web-search budget was used up (see Open questions).\n- **Scout-to-partner path.** She was \"a 2019 graduate of our Lightspeed Scouts program\" and was hired as a partner in 2022 ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/from-tel-aviv-to-the-bay-scaling-the-lightspeed-enterprise-team/), 2022-10-17). That makes the scout program a partner pipeline at Lightspeed, not only a deal-flow tool.\n- **Her own operating scar shapes her legal-AI thesis.** On Atrium she writes: \"This was felt when I was helping scale Atrium in the early days.\" Her point is that services-plus-software legal models break as clients scale ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/legaltech-x-ai-the-lightspeed-view/), 2023-12-12). In the same post she quotes the Atrium co-founder (Augie Rakow) on why it failed. The line is his, not hers. Lightspeed later led EvenUp's Series C (2023) and Sandstone's $30M Series A (2026), and she is named on both.\n- **Legal-AI thesis in her words.** Winners will take \"a pointed approach to solving 2-3 key workflows\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/legaltech-x-ai-the-lightspeed-view/)). Sandstone's memo says \"We led Sandstone's $30M Series A\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/the-operating-system-for-in-house-legal-our-series-a-in-sandstone/), 2026-06-09, co-authored with Guru Chahal). The photo caption shows her with founder Nick Fleisher.\n- **Training-data thesis (most thesis-relevant).** On Luel: \"we're leading Luel's 31.2M financing\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-luel-the-marketplace-for-multimodal-ai-training-data/), 2026-05-15). She is the first-listed author, with Raviraj Jain. Their thesis: scraped web data \"has hit its ceiling\". Their sourcing note: \"We first met William and Inigo earlier this year\". So the deal went from first meeting to term sheet within a few months of 2026.\n- **Suno is her memo.** \"Suno's Hit Factory\" opens \"we're leading a Series B in Suno\", and she is the **sole author** ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/sunos-hit-factory/), 2024-05-20). Lightspeed's Suno page lists her and Bejul Somaia, plus a blank partner slot ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/suno/)). The repo's roster row had named Michael Mignano for Suno.\n- **Pika attribution moved over time.** TechCrunch quoted **Michael Mignano** for Lightspeed when it led Pika's $55M round on 2023-11-28 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/11/28/pika-labs-which-is-building-ai-tools-to-generate-and-edit-videos-raises-55m/)). Lightspeed's Pika page now lists Lisa Han and Guru Chahal, plus a blank partner slot ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/pika/)). My inference, not confirmed: she took over coverage after Mignano left. She probably did not originate Pika.\n- **Character.AI attribution can no longer be found.** The repo's roster cited `lsvp.com/?p=16302` for her Character.AI involvement. On 2026-11-10 that URL serves a Nominal Series C post, `/company/character-ai/` returns 404, and Character.AI does not appear on lsvp.com/companies. Her Character.AI link is **unconfirmed**.\n- **Enterprise-AI view.** \"we believe enterprise apps will form the backbone\" of the AI market, and the biggest change comes from \"Compound AI\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/ai-enterprise-market-map-cutting-through-the-hype/), 2024-08-29, co-authored with Nnamdi Iregbulem).\n- **Voice view.** \"we are on the brink of a voice revolution\". She names Cartesia and Kyutai on state-space and open-source voice models ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/the-future-of-voice-our-thoughts-on-how-it-will-transform-conversational-ai/), 2024-09-26).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **Evidence splits by deal type.**\n  - She personally writes and leads applied-AI rounds: Distyl A, Suno B, Sandstone A and Luel seed.\n  - On the frontier-lab deal you care about most, Thinking Machines (seed, 2025, a16z lead), she is one of three Lightspeed names. Ravi Mhatre is listed first, and nothing shows she drove it.\n- **Most relevant to you:** Luel. It is a seed for rights-cleared multimodal training data sold to \"Generative AI labs, robotics companies, and speech research teams\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/luel/)). That sits next to the pre-training ecosystem you back.\n- **AI-native automation:** Distyl (Lightspeed led the Series A, 2024) is close to your Serval-type interest.\n- **Calibration point.** On Base Power she is a follow-on co-signer (Series B 2025, then the Series C). The $1B Series C was led by Addition, which THESIS.md flags as not your type of fund ([src](https://basepowercompany.com/press)).\n- **What she offers you:** a useful co-investor and A-round lead for applied/automation AI companies you seed. She is not a frontier-lab gatekeeper; that is Mhatre.\n\n## Background\n- Middlebury College, B.A. Economics ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/lisa-han/)).\n- Product Manager at IBM, the start of her career. Dates: nothing found ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/lisa-han/)).\n- Head of business development at Atrium. She helped scale it \"from 10 employees and $0 revenues to a team of more than 250 and $20 million+ in revenues\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/from-tel-aviv-to-the-bay-scaling-the-lightspeed-enterprise-team/)). Dates: nothing found.\n- First business hire at Ramp. She started business development and partnerships and helped take it from pre-launch to $100mm in transaction volume ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/lisa-han/)). Dates: nothing found.\n- 2019: Lightspeed Scout. She wrote early checks into Rutter and Secureframe ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/lisa-han/)).\n- Investor at Gradient Ventures, covering SaaS, AI and fintech. Dates: nothing found ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/from-tel-aviv-to-the-bay-scaling-the-lightspeed-enterprise-team/)).\n- 2022-10-17: joined Lightspeed as a Partner on the Enterprise team ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/from-tel-aviv-to-the-bay-scaling-the-lightspeed-enterprise-team/)).\n- Angel investments: Pave, Hightouch, Unit, Secureframe, Rutter ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/lisa-han/)).\n- Former competitive figure skater and now a skating coach. Originally from Boston. Lightspeed uses she/her ([src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/lisa-han/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://lsvp.com/team-member/lisa-han/ | Lightspeed team page; lists 9 investments and 8 stories (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://lsvp.com/team-member/lisa-han/ | Lightspeed \"Stories\": 8 posts 2023-12 to 2026-06 |\n| X | nothing found | not searched (search budget exhausted) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/lisabethhan/ | linked from Lightspeed page; not readable (HTTP 999) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The web-search budget was used up before any podcast or talk search could run.\n\n## How they think\n- **Vertical before horizontal.** In legal, she favors 2–3 key workflows over platform scope ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/legaltech-x-ai-the-lightspeed-view/)). Her enterprise-AI map expects vertical tools to win first and end-to-end workflow automation to come later ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/ai-enterprise-market-map-cutting-through-the-hype/)).\n- **Business-model shifts unlock software.** She argues the move from the billable hour toward flat or contingency fees is what finally makes legal AI sellable ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/legaltech-x-ai-the-lightspeed-view/)).\n- **Data wall.** She holds that next-generation voice, robotics and video models need net-new human-generated multimodal data ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-luel-the-marketplace-for-multimodal-ai-training-data/)).\n- **Services-heavy AI can beat the consultancies.** Distyl is framed as AI-native talent beating Accenture and EY ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-distyl-transforming-enterprises-with-genai/)).\n- **Calls, so far.** Suno (B, 2024) and EvenUp (C, 2023) have both raised large later rounds per general press. Not re-verified here.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Legaltech x AI: The Lightspeed View](https://lsvp.com/stories/legaltech-x-ai-the-lightspeed-view/) (2023-12-12): her solo thesis, including the Atrium lesson.\n2. [Our Investment in Luel](https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-luel-the-marketplace-for-multimodal-ai-training-data/) (2026-05-15): the training-data thesis behind her most frontier-adjacent seed.\n3. [Suno's Hit Factory](https://lsvp.com/stories/sunos-hit-factory/) (2024-05-20): her solo memo on a Lightspeed-led Series B.\n4. [AI Enterprise Market Map](https://lsvp.com/stories/ai-enterprise-market-map-cutting-through-the-hype/) (2024-08-29): her enterprise-AI worldview.\n5. [Sandstone Series A](https://lsvp.com/stories/the-operating-system-for-in-house-legal-our-series-a-in-sandstone/) (2026-06-09): where her legal thesis turned into a lead.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X and LinkedIn activity were not searched because the search budget was used up.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Thinking Machines Lab | Seed ($2B at $12B) | 2025-07 | No. a16z led; her personal role is unclear | Ravi Mhatre, James Alcorn (LSVP); a16z (lead), Nvidia, Accel, ServiceNow, Cisco, AMD, Jane Street | [LSVP](https://lsvp.com/company/thinking-machines/), [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/15/mira-muratis-thinking-machines-lab-is-worth-12b-in-seed-round/) |\n| Luel | Seed ($31.2M) | 2026-05-15 | Lightspeed led; she is first-listed author | Raviraj Jain (LSVP); General Catalyst, SV Angel | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-luel-the-marketplace-for-multimodal-ai-training-data/) |\n| Suno | Series B | 2024-05-20 | Lightspeed led; she is sole memo author | Bejul Somaia (LSVP page); Nat Friedman & Daniel Gross, Matrix, Founder Collective | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/sunos-hit-factory/) |\n| Pika | Series A ($55M total raise) | 2023-11-28 | Lightspeed led; press quoted Mignano, not her | Guru Chahal (LSVP page); Homebrew, Conviction, SV Angel | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2023/11/28/pika-labs-which-is-building-ai-tools-to-generate-and-edit-videos-raises-55m/), [LSVP](https://lsvp.com/company/pika/) |\n| Distyl | Series A | 2024-11-19 | Lightspeed led; co-authored memo | Raviraj Jain (LSVP) | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-distyl-transforming-enterprises-with-genai/) |\n| Sandstone | Series A ($30M) | 2026-06-09 | Lightspeed led; co-authored memo | Guru Chahal (LSVP) | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/the-operating-system-for-in-house-legal-our-series-a-in-sandstone/) |\n| Base Power | Series B, then Series C ($1B) | 2025-04-09 / 2025-10-08 | No (C led by Addition) | Sebastian Duesterhoeft (LSVP); a16z (B) | [LSVP](https://lsvp.com/company/base-power/), [memo](https://lsvp.com/stories/modernizing-the-electric-grid-our-investment-in-base-power/), [press](https://basepowercompany.com/press) |\n| EvenUp | Series C | 2023 | Lightspeed led; her role among 4 LSVP names is unclear | Duesterhoeft, Jain, Janmohamed (LSVP) | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/evenup/) |\n| Rocksalt | Seed | 2025 | unknown | Janmohamed, Chahal, Jain (LSVP) | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/rocksalt/) |\n| Ramp | Series E | 2025 | No (multi-partner growth) | Sachin Patel and 4 other LSVP partners | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/ramp/) |\n| Character.AI | unconfirmed | n/a | unknown | n/a | old roster citation lsvp.com/?p=16302 now serves a Nominal post |\n| Rutter, Secureframe | early checks (Scout) | 2019+ | n/a | n/a | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/lisa-han/) |\n| Pave, Hightouch, Unit | angel | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/lisa-han/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Was she first? (Thinking Machines.)** No. a16z led the $2B seed ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/15/mira-muratis-thinking-machines-lab-is-worth-12b-in-seed-round/)). Lightspeed's page lists Mhatre, Han and Alcorn ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/thinking-machines/)). Lightspeed was in the seed, not a later round, but it was not the lead, and no memo from her exists.\n- **Base Power.** Both a16z and Lightspeed entered at the Series B, announced 2025-04-09 ([a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-base-power/); the Bloomberg headline on [Base's press page](https://basepowercompany.com/press) names both). Lightspeed \"deepen[ed] our partnership\" at the $1B Series C ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/modernizing-the-electric-grid-our-investment-in-base-power/), 2025-10-08). Han co-authored that memo with Sebastian Duesterhoeft.\n- **Luel.** Lightspeed met the founders in early 2026 and led a $31.2M round announced 2026-05-15. The founders came out of YC and had been a competitive Fortnite duo. General Catalyst and SV Angel were alongside ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-luel-the-marketplace-for-multimodal-ai-training-data/)).\n- **Suno.** Lightspeed led the Series B. Total raised reached $125M. Nat Friedman and Daniel Gross, Matrix and Founder Collective were earlier backers, so Lightspeed was **not first in** ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/sunos-hit-factory/)).\n- **Pika.** Lightspeed led the round announced 2023-11-28. TechCrunch carried Mignano's quote, which suggests he originated it ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/11/28/pika-labs-which-is-building-ai-tools-to-generate-and-edit-videos-raises-55m/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lightspeed Venture Partners | Scout 2019; Partner (Enterprise) from 2022-10-17 | 2019 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/from-tel-aviv-to-the-bay-scaling-the-lightspeed-enterprise-team/) |\n| Raviraj Jain | Co-author on Distyl and Luel; co-named on EvenUp and Rocksalt | 2024 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-distyl-transforming-enterprises-with-genai/) |\n| Guru Chahal | Co-author on Sandstone; co-named on Pika and Rocksalt | 2023 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/the-operating-system-for-in-house-legal-our-series-a-in-sandstone/) |\n| Sebastian Duesterhoeft | Co-author on Base Power | 2025 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/modernizing-the-electric-grid-our-investment-in-base-power/) |\n| Ravi Mhatre | Co-named on Thinking Machines; co-signed her hire post | 2022 | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/thinking-machines/) |\n| Arif Janmohamed | Co-signed her hire post; co-named on EvenUp and Rocksalt | 2022 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/from-tel-aviv-to-the-bay-scaling-the-lightspeed-enterprise-team/) |\n| Nnamdi Iregbulem | Co-author on the enterprise-AI market map | 2024 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/ai-enterprise-market-map-cutting-through-the-hype/) |\n| Michael Mignano | Earlier Lightspeed lead voice on Pika; now left Lightspeed | 2023 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/11/28/pika-labs-which-is-building-ai-tools-to-generate-and-edit-videos-raises-55m/) |\n| Gradient Ventures | Former employer (Investor) | before 2022 | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/lisa-han/) |\n| Ramp | First business hire; now a Lightspeed portfolio company with her name on it | before 2022 | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/lisa-han/) |\n| Atrium | Head of business development | before Ramp | [src](https://lsvp.com/team-member/lisa-han/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Search gap:** the web-search budget was used up in this run. Podcasts, X, press interviews and her LinkedIn dates (Gradient, Ramp, Atrium) are all unsearched. A follow-up pass should fill them.\n- **Character.AI:** is she on it? The old Lightspeed citation now resolves to a Nominal post, and no Character.AI company page exists.\n- **Thinking Machines:** did she do any of the work (diligence, relationship), or is she listed by Enterprise-team convention?\n- **Pika and Suno coverage:** did she inherit board or coverage roles from Mignano after he left?\n- **Luel:** who sourced it (YC, General Catalyst)? Did Lightspeed price the round?\n\n## Sources\n- https://lsvp.com/team-member/lisa-han/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/from-tel-aviv-to-the-bay-scaling-the-lightspeed-enterprise-team/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/legaltech-x-ai-the-lightspeed-view/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/ai-enterprise-market-map-cutting-through-the-hype/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/the-future-of-voice-our-thoughts-on-how-it-will-transform-conversational-ai/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/sunos-hit-factory/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-distyl-transforming-enterprises-with-genai/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/modernizing-the-electric-grid-our-investment-in-base-power/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-luel-the-marketplace-for-multimodal-ai-training-data/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/the-operating-system-for-in-house-legal-our-series-a-in-sandstone/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/thinking-machines/ ; /company/suno/ ; /company/pika/ ; /company/distyl/ ; /company/luel/ ; /company/base-power/ ; /company/evenup/ ; /company/rocksalt/ ; /company/ramp/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/15/mira-muratis-thinking-machines-lab-is-worth-12b-in-seed-round/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/11/28/pika-labs-which-is-building-ai-tools-to-generate-and-edit-videos-raises-55m/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-base-power/\n- https://basepowercompany.com/press\n", "person:lisha-li": "\n# Lisha Li\n\n## One-line take\nA deep-learning PhD and eight-year founder who joined a16z Infra in roughly spring 2026 to cover frontier models and AI for science. Before joining, they had angel checks in fal, Periodic Labs and Exa, all three of which a16z later backed in size. They are a new partner with no a16z deal byline yet, so they are easy to reach now, before their first board seat.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** they have a researcher's network that goes back a decade. In 2016, as a Berkeley stats PhD student, they wrote up talks by Sutskever and Karpathy [src](https://lishali.github.io/rework_deep_learning/). Both later angel-invested in their company, alongside Jeff Dean [src](https://rosebud.ai/company). They also had early angel access to frontier-adjacent companies (fal, Periodic Labs, Exa) [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/).\n- **What constrains them:** they are new inside a large partnership. a16z's frontier-lab deals are run by others: Anjney Midha on Periodic Labs, and Sarah Wang, Jennifer Li and others on Exa. They still sit on Rosebud's board [src](https://rosebud.ai/company). As a newly hired investment partner, their sign-off authority on a16z's large checks is unverified.\n- **What they'd never do (inferred, low confidence):** in 2016 they argued for selling deep-learning platforms to businesses rather than building thin vertical apps. They would probably avoid wrapper companies whose accuracy customers won't pay for [src](https://lishali.github.io/rework_deep_learning/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Their angel book became a16z's portfolio.** The a16z bio credits \"seed checks into fal, Periodic Labs, Exa, and others.\" [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/). a16z led fal's seed [src](https://blog.fal.ai/generative-media-needs-speed-fal-has-raised-23m-to-accelerate/), led Periodic's $300M founding round on 2025-09-30 [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-periodic-labs/) and led Exa's Series C on 2026-05-20 [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/). Nothing found says whether their checks came before a16z's. The overlap means they arrived already holding three of the firm's AI-infra names.\n- **Join timing comes from two artifacts, not an announcement.** Their a16z headshot was uploaded in 2026/03 (image path) [src](https://d1lamhf6l6yk6d.cloudfront.net/uploads/2026/03/Lisha-Li-1024x1024.png). Rosebud says Vladimir Glazachev has been CEO since April 2026 [src](https://rosebud.ai/company). Rosebud says Li \"continues to serve on its board.\" [src](https://rosebud.ai/company). Both point to a move around Mar–Apr 2026. No firm announcement was found.\n- **Their 2016 view on deep-learning startups:** \"it seems easier to be business facing and build a platform to do DL\" [src](https://lishali.github.io/rework_deep_learning/). They also wrote that \"the companies that can afford it have already built DL teams\" [src](https://lishali.github.io/rework_deep_learning/). That is an early version of the incumbent talent-moat argument, and it fits backing frontier labs and infra over applications.\n- **Their market test:** \"find a vertical that is ok with the accuracy level\" [src](https://lishali.github.io/rework_deep_learning/). They judge whether a market is ready by whether customers tolerate current model accuracy.\n- **Their a16z output is math and AI, not deal posts.** They hosted Daniel Litt (2026-09-01) [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/daniel-litt-the-mathematicians-guide-to-ai-2/) and OpenAI's Mehtaab Sawhney and Mark Sellke (2026-09-08) [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/openai-researchers-on-the-future-of-mathematical-reasoning/). Their logic and foundations background makes AI-for-math their likely niche. Those guests are sourcing leads into AI-for-math talent.\n- **Their own X list of what they back (update 2026-11-10):** in a reply shown on the a16z bio, they wrote \"investing in companies that sell to technical buyers\" and listed Cursor, fal, Periodic Labs, ElevenLabs and OpenRouter [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/). Cursor, ElevenLabs and OpenRouter are not in the bio's angel list. The tweet may describe a16z's portfolio rather than personal checks, so they are logged as `needs-id` leads, not deals.\n- **None of the company pages name them as an investor (checked 2026-11-10).** fal's seed+A post names only a16z and Village Global (seed), Kindred, First Round and four angels (Series A) [src](https://blog.fal.ai/generative-media-needs-speed-fal-has-raised-23m-to-accelerate/). Periodic lists Bezos, Elad Gil, Eric Schmidt and Jeff Dean as individuals [src](https://periodic.com). Exa lists only funds [src](https://exa.ai/about). Their checks were small enough to go unlisted, so the a16z bio is the only primary source.\n- **Rosebud's cap table is a Tier-1 researcher roster.** It lists angels Andrej Karpathy, Ilya Sutskever, Jeff Dean, Balaji Srinivasan, Kevin Lin and Adam D'Angelo [src](https://rosebud.ai/company). Their access to pre-training talent rests on these relationships.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Their remit is frontier AI models plus AI for science, the core of THESIS.md, and they sit on the a16z Infra team, which writes very large seeds into labs (Periodic's $300M founding round). They angel-invested in Periodic Labs, the exact archetype of a $300M seed into a new lab. They are newly hired and have no named a16z deals yet, so a relationship built now is with a rising partner before their first marquee lead. Caveat: whether they can lead a $10M+ check on their own authority is unverified. check_capacity is set to `growth` because that is the firm's capacity, not their proven personal authority.\n\n## Background\n- Came to the Bay Area in 2011 for a PhD at UC Berkeley. They started in logic and the foundations of mathematics, then \"pivoted to deep learning\" [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/). Their 2016 personal site lists them as \"Current PhD Student at UC Berkeley Statistics\" [src](https://lishali.github.io).\n- 2019: founded Rosebud AI (YC S19), now an AI coding platform for making browser games [src](https://rosebud.ai/company), [src](https://rosebud.ai). Founder for about eight years per the a16z bio [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/). Glazachev took over as CEO in April 2026, and they remain on the board [src](https://rosebud.ai/company).\n- Active angel before a16z: fal, Periodic Labs, Exa \"and others\" [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/).\n- 2026 (month unconfirmed, likely Mar–Apr): joined a16z as investment partner on the Infra team [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/), [src](https://a16z.com/infra/).\n- Any investing role before Rosebud: not confirmed in sources fetched.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | http://www.lishali.com (redirects to beacons.ai/lishali, 403 to fetch); https://lishali.github.io | GitHub site has 2016 deep-learning posts |\n| Substack / blog | https://lishali.github.io | Dormant since 2016 |\n| X | https://x.com/lishali88 | Could not fetch (402); follower count not found |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/lisha-li-700985a/ | Could not fetch |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/lishali | 52 repos; org affiliation \"Kreativai\" (as of 2026-11-10) |\n| Podcasts (host) | a16z Infra video/podcast series | Math and AI conversations, Sep 2026 |\n| TikTok | https://www.tiktok.com/@lishali_88 | Linked from a16z bio; not fetched |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-09-01: a16z, \"Daniel Litt: The Mathematician's Guide to AI\" (host). Covers an AI solution to an Erdős problem and where models fall short on intuition [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/daniel-litt-the-mathematicians-guide-to-ai-2/). No transcript on page, so none of their quotes are captured.\n- 2026-09-08: a16z, \"OpenAI Researchers on the Future of Mathematical Reasoning,\" with Mehtaab Sawhney and Mark Sellke (host). Covers sphere packing and a non-sofic group construction [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/openai-researchers-on-the-future-of-mathematical-reasoning/). No transcript on page.\n- 2016-02: personal blog recap of the Rework Deep Learning conference [src](https://lishali.github.io/rework_deep_learning/).\n\n## How they think\n- Platforms over verticals: selling deep-learning capability to businesses beats building vertical products on today's accuracy (2016) [src](https://lishali.github.io/rework_deep_learning/).\n- Incumbents with money hire the talent first, so startups need either a platform angle or a vertical where today's accuracy is already good enough [src](https://lishali.github.io/rework_deep_learning/).\n- At a16z: frontier models, their infra, AI for science and creative applications [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/). Their hosting choices point to AI-for-math as a thesis area (inference from two episodes).\n- Calls right or wrong: the angel picks fal, Periodic and Exa all later raised large rounds led or joined by a16z, Sequoia and others (see Portfolio). Entry rounds and returns are unknown.\n\n## Read these first\n1. a16z author bio: their focus areas and angel book [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/)\n2. \"Deep Learning and Startups\" (2016): their early framework for where deep-learning companies can win [src](https://lishali.github.io/rework_deep_learning/)\n3. Daniel Litt episode (2026-09-01): their AI-for-math interest [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/daniel-litt-the-mathematicians-guide-to-ai-2/)\n4. OpenAI math researchers episode (2026-09-08) [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/openai-researchers-on-the-future-of-mathematical-reasoning/)\n\n## Social engagement\nX could not be fetched directly (HTTP 402). The a16z bio embeds recent posts (as of 2026-11-10): a reply listing \"cursor, fal, periodic labs, elevenlabs, openrouter\" as companies that sell to technical buyers, a post promoting a game built on @Rosebud_AI, and a reply to @CompleteSkeptic [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/). They still promote Rosebud publicly after the CEO handoff. Who they amplify: nothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Periodic Labs | Seed (angel check; exact round unconfirmed) | before a16z join (2026); company founded ~2025 | no | a16z (lead of $300M founding round, 2025-09-30), Felicis, DST, NVentures, Accel | [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/), [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-periodic-labs/) |\n| fal | Seed (angel check; exact round unconfirmed) | before 2026 | no | a16z led seed with Village Global; Kindred led $14M Series A with a16z and First Round (announced 2024-09-18); Li not named on that post | [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/), [src](https://blog.fal.ai/generative-media-needs-speed-fal-has-raised-23m-to-accelerate/) |\n| Exa | Seed (angel check; exact round unconfirmed) | before 2026 | no | a16z later led Series C (2026-05-20) | [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/), [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/) |\n| Rosebud AI | Founder; board member | 2019– | n/a | YC S19; angels Karpathy, Sutskever, Jeff Dean, Balaji Srinivasan, Kevin Lin, Adam D'Angelo | [src](https://rosebud.ai/company) |\n\n| Cursor, ElevenLabs, OpenRouter | needs-id: named in their X reply about \"investing in companies that sell to technical buyers\"; not confirmed as personal checks | unknown | no | n/a | [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/) |\n\nNo a16z deal names them as partner of record, and no board seat other than Rosebud's was found, as of 2026-11-10 [src](https://a16z.com/infra/), [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/). Why the list is short: they joined around spring 2026, and none of the portfolio companies' own pages name small angels (see Differential insight).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Periodic Labs:** they were an angel. The a16z lead was run by Anjney Midha [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-periodic-labs/). Whether their check came before Felicis's pre-incorporation commitment or a16z's term sheet is unknown, so they can't be credited as first in.\n- **Exa:** an a16z team (Sarah Wang, Jennifer Li, Stephenie Zhang, Jason Cui) led the Series C on 2026-05-20, around when they joined [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/). They aren't named on the post. Their prior angel position may have counted as a reference, but nothing confirms it.\n- Intro paths: nothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Andreessen Horowitz (Infra) | Investment partner | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/) |\n| Rosebud AI | Founder (2019), board member | 2019 | [src](https://rosebud.ai/company) |\n| Vladimir Glazachev | Successor CEO at Rosebud (Apr 2026) | 2026-04 | [src](https://rosebud.ai/company) |\n| Andrej Karpathy | Angel in Rosebud | unknown | [src](https://rosebud.ai/company) |\n| Ilya Sutskever | Angel in Rosebud | unknown | [src](https://rosebud.ai/company) |\n| Jeff Dean | Angel in Rosebud (also Periodic angel) | unknown | [src](https://rosebud.ai/company), [src](https://periodic.com) |\n| Adam D'Angelo | Angel in Rosebud | unknown | [src](https://rosebud.ai/company) |\n| Balaji Srinivasan | Angel in Rosebud | unknown | [src](https://rosebud.ai/company) |\n| Kevin Lin | Angel in Rosebud | unknown | [src](https://rosebud.ai/company) |\n| Daniel Litt | Podcast guest | 2026-09 | [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/daniel-litt-the-mathematicians-guide-to-ai-2/) |\n| Mehtaab Sawhney; Mark Sellke (OpenAI) | Podcast guests | 2026-09 | [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/openai-researchers-on-the-future-of-mathematical-reasoning/) |\n| Periodic Labs, fal, Exa | Angel investor | before 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact a16z start date and title history: is \"investment partner\" a step below general partner?\n- Their personal sign-off authority on a16z checks.\n- Entry dates and rounds of their angel checks in Periodic, fal and Exa. Were they ahead of a16z on any of them?\n- Any pre-Rosebud investing role. LinkedIn could not be fetched.\n- Their X posting (HTTP 402). \"Kreativai\" is a GitHub user account with one repo (kreativai.github.io), possibly an earlier Rosebud name; unconfirmed [src](https://github.com/Kreativai).\n- Are Cursor, ElevenLabs and OpenRouter personal angel checks or a16z portfolio names?\n- Rosebud's funding rounds: no amounts or dates on its own page or YC page [src](https://www.ycombinator.com/companies/rosebud-ai); Crunchbase returned 403.\n- First a16z deal credit, especially in AI-for-math or AI-for-science labs.\n- Note: the WebSearch budget was exhausted in both passes (again on 2026-11-10); only direct page fetches were possible. Press on their a16z hire and podcast transcripts were not found.\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/lisha-li/\n- https://a16z.com/infra/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/daniel-litt-the-mathematicians-guide-to-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/daniel-litt-the-mathematicians-guide-to-ai-2/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/openai-researchers-on-the-future-of-mathematical-reasoning/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-periodic-labs/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/\n- https://blog.fal.ai/generative-media-needs-speed-fal-has-raised-23m-to-accelerate/\n- https://periodic.com\n- https://exa.ai/about\n- https://www.ycombinator.com/companies/rosebud-ai\n- https://github.com/Kreativai\n- https://rosebud.ai/company\n- https://rosebud.ai\n- https://lishali.github.io\n- https://lishali.github.io/rework_deep_learning/\n- https://github.com/lishali\n- https://d1lamhf6l6yk6d.cloudfront.net/uploads/2026/03/Lisha-Li-1024x1024.png\n", "person:liu-jiang": "\n# Liu Jiang\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. Sources: sunflowercapital.co (testimonials, team, portfolio), Sunflower's SEC Form Ds and Form ADV, the archived 2019 Sequoia bio, and a16z's Tecton post. The web-search budget was used up during this pass, so podcasts, X and LinkedIn were not read. Pronouns she/her follow founder testimonials and her Sequoia bio.*\n\n## One-line take\nShe is the one person who decides at Sunflower Capital, a $150M seed fund. She writes the first institutional check into AI and dev-infra pre-seeds (E2B, TollBit) and is quietly on the cap tables of the frontier-AI seeds the owner targets.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** she meets teams before they are companies. She wrote Athelas's first check through Dorm Room Fund as a student, and she co-led Tecton's seed at Sequoia while the founders were still building Michelangelo inside Uber [src](https://sunflowercapital.co/). Her stated rule: \"the strongest relationships are built outside of formal fundraising processes\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20191213075916/https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liu-jiang/).\n- **What she personally signs:** everything. She is the only individual on the Fund I, SPV I and Fund II Form Ds and the only Schedule A owner of the adviser: Managing Member since 09/2022, ownership code E (75%+), control person [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/323327/PDF/323327.pdf), [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1943848/000194384822000003/primary_doc.xml). Max Wang and Mo Punjwani appear on no filing.\n- **Constraint:** fund size. Fund II is $150M from 69 Form D investors [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2060407/000206040725000001/primary_doc.xml). An aggregator says it runs no reserves and writes $1-3M first checks [src](https://www.everythingstartups.com/vc-funds/sunflower-capital) (unconfirmed). In $50-200M frontier seeds she can only be a small participant.\n- **What she'd never do (inferred):** lead a $10M+ round or price a growth deal. The firm's stated job is \"the first check to founders before their first line of code\" [src](https://sunflowercapital.co/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Her pre-seed is the first institutional money, and she re-ups.** E2B CEO: \"Liu led our pre-seed.\" [src](https://sunflowercapital.co/). cc.cz listed her as \"Liu Jang\" [src](https://cc.cz/cc25/e2b/). Sunflower then followed in Decibel's $11.5M seed (2024-10) and Insight's $21M A (2025-07) [src](https://en.ain.ua/2024/10/18/e2b-raises-11m-in-seed-funding), [src](https://e2b.dev/blog/series-a). Temporal: \"Liu invested in our seed and then doubled down\" [src](https://sunflowercapital.co/).\n- **Her seed bets end up inside AI and data platforms.** The portfolio page marks Statsig \"Affiliated with OpenAI\", Tabular \"Affiliated with Databricks\" and VoidZero \"Affiliated with Cloudflare\" [src](https://sunflowercapital.co/portfolio). Tecton, which she co-led at Sequoia, was also acquired by Databricks (repo row sourced to [a16z](https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/)). Two of her infra bets (Tecton, Tabular) went to the same acquirer.\n- **She is in frontier-AI rounds without being named in them.** Mirendil, Elorian, Applied Compute, Volantis and Embo sit under \"AI\" on her portfolio page [src](https://sunflowercapital.co/portfolio). None of their round announcements in the repo (Mirendil $200M seed, Elorian $55M seed, Applied Compute seed through $350M growth, Volantis $88M A) names Sunflower. That points to small allocations, possibly through the SPVs: the ADV shows SPV II with a $200K minimum and a vehicle called \"Sunflower Ivy, LLC\" [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/323327/PDF/323327.pdf). The round and vehicle for each company are **unconfirmed**.\n- **She runs the next raise.** ConductorAI's CEO: \"She also helped orchestrate our Series A\". Knock credits her as \"an instrumental part of our early fundraising\" [src](https://sunflowercapital.co/). She knows who leads the round after hers, which makes her a good source of deal flow for a $20M seed check.\n- **Tecton timing.** Martin Casado: \"We initially did the seed investment in Tecton at the end of 2018\" [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-tecton/). Kevin Stumpf says she co-led that seed and the A [src](https://sunflowercapital.co/). So she was a lead-capable Sequoia seed investor by 2018, earlier than an aggregator's \"partner from 2020\" implies.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, as a sourcing node; not as a co-lead.** She cannot write $10M+ checks, but she is the first institutional check into AI-infra companies (E2B, TollBit, Letta's seed) that later raise the large rounds the owner writes. She orchestrates those next rounds, and she already sits in Mirendil, Elorian, Applied Compute, Volantis and Embo. Deals that matter: E2B (AI agent sandboxes), TollBit (AI content licensing), Letta (stateful agents), plus the frontier names above.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford. Her 2019 Sequoia bio notes engineering and management-science training and prior Dropbox experience. The firm page draws this from the archived bio; it could not be re-read this pass (archive blocked) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20191213075916/https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liu-jiang/).\n- Dorm Room Fund: wrote Athelas's first check while a student, then helped lead its 2016 seed [src](https://sunflowercapital.co/). Title and dates unconfirmed.\n- Sequoia Capital, Seed/Early team. Her 2019 page lists Athelas, Lilt and Trupo. She co-led Tecton's seed (2018-12) and Series A [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20191213075916/https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liu-jiang/), [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-tecton/). Exact title and leave date unconfirmed. The live Sequoia page now returns 404.\n- Sunflower Capital: Managing Member since 09/2022 (ADV). Fund I first Form D 2022-08-24 (target $30M), closed at $64.5M. Fund II $150M in 2025, raised \"in just a month from about 20 institutions\" per the WSJ headline the firm reposts [src](https://sunflowercapital.co/).\n- How she got the seat: she founded the firm. Her track record from Sequoia and Dorm Room Fund (Tecton, Verkada, Semgrep, Athelas/Commure, Retool) is what she raised on. The asterisked logos on the portfolio page are probably prior-firm deals (inference).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://sunflowercapital.co/ | Contact form (\"Let's talk\"); verified 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | @seedtosunflower (firm) | Mentioned in the earlier firm pass; not re-verified here |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/liujiang1/ | Linked from the firm team list (verified 2026-11-10) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. The search budget ran out before podcasts could be checked.\n\n## How they think\n- Build relationships before the raise, and tell hesitant founders to reach out anyway [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20191213075916/https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liu-jiang/).\n- Thesis-first: Rerun's CEO says Sunflower moved fast because of market work it had already done [src](https://sunflowercapital.co/).\n- Value-add is people and capital: \"She's really well connected with top talent and investors.\" (Clay's Kareem Amin) [src](https://sunflowercapital.co/).\n- Calls that look right: Tecton (exit to Databricks), Verkada, Athelas/Commure, PostHog, Temporal, E2B.\n\n## Read these first\n- Founder testimonials on https://sunflowercapital.co/: the best primary record of what she did on each deal.\n- Portfolio page with categories and exit notes: https://sunflowercapital.co/portfolio\n- Form ADV (CRD 323327), which shows the ownership and vehicles: https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/323327/PDF/323327.pdf\n- Archived Sequoia bio (2019): https://web.archive.org/web/20191213075916/https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liu-jiang/\n\n## Social engagement\nNot read this pass (X and LinkedIn not fetched).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| E2B | Pre-seed (~$2.5M) | 2023-09 | yes | Kaya VC; angels | [site](https://sunflowercapital.co/), [cc.cz](https://cc.cz/cc25/e2b/) |\n| E2B | Seed $11.5M; Series A $21M (firm re-ups) | 2024-10; 2025-07 | no | Decibel; Insight | [ain](https://en.ain.ua/2024/10/18/e2b-raises-11m-in-seed-funding), [e2b](https://e2b.dev/blog/series-a) |\n| TollBit | Seed $7M (founder: pre-seed) | 2024-03 | yes | AIX; Lerer Hippeau; Operator Collective | [PR](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/leading-media-and-tech-investors-bet-on-tollbit---the-first-platform-to-tackle-the-new-economics-of-content-in-the-ai-era-302080097.html) |\n| Letta | Seed $10M | 2024-09 | no | Felicis (lead) | [Felicis](https://www.felicis.com/blog/letta) |\n| Tecton (at Sequoia) | Seed; Series A | 2018-12; A undated | co-led | a16z | [site](https://sunflowercapital.co/), [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-tecton/) |\n| Athelas | First check (DRF); Seed | 2016 | seed: helped lead | | [site](https://sunflowercapital.co/) |\n| Lilt, Trupo (at Sequoia) | unconfirmed | | | | [Sequoia 2019](https://web.archive.org/web/20191213075916/https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liu-jiang/) |\n| Railway; tldraw | Pre-seed | unconfirmed | no | | [site](https://sunflowercapital.co/) |\n| PostHog | Seed | 2020 | no | | [site](https://sunflowercapital.co/) |\n| Temporal | Seed, then later rounds | unconfirmed | no | | [site](https://sunflowercapital.co/) |\n| Hadrian, Verkada, Semgrep, Knock, Clay, Warp, Houm, AlpacaML, Tabular, ConductorAI, Canopy Labs, Comfy, Rerun | named in testimonials; round unconfirmed | | | | [site](https://sunflowercapital.co/) |\n| Mirendil, Elorian, Applied Compute, Volantis, Embo, Ollama, Cohere, Roboflow, Serval, Corridor | firm portfolio (Sunflower); her personal role is unconfirmed beyond sole-GP approval | | | | [portfolio](https://sunflowercapital.co/portfolio) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Tecton:** she knew Kevin Stumpf and Mike Del Balso inside Uber (Michelangelo) and co-led the seed (end of 2018, with a16z) and the Series A [src](https://sunflowercapital.co/), [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-tecton/).\n- **Athelas:** she wrote the first check through Dorm Room Fund, then helped lead the 2016 seed. Athelas later merged with Commure, which is asterisked on her portfolio page [src](https://sunflowercapital.co/).\n- **E2B:** a Czech team. She led the ~$2.5M pre-seed in 2023 and Sunflower followed in the seed and A [src](https://cc.cz/cc25/e2b/).\n- **TollBit:** the founders say she impressed them \"even before leading our pre-seed\" [src](https://sunflowercapital.co/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sunflower Capital | at: Founder & Managing Member (sole control person) | 2022-09 | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/323327/PDF/323327.pdf) |\n| Sequoia Capital | at: Seed/Early investor (former) | by 2018 | [Sequoia 2019](https://web.archive.org/web/20191213075916/https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liu-jiang/) |\n| Dorm Room Fund | at: student investor (former) | c.2016 | [site](https://sunflowercapital.co/) |\n| Martin Casado / a16z | Tecton seed co-lead | 2018 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-tecton/) |\n| Felicis, Decibel, Insight, Lightspeed | lead the rounds after hers (Letta, E2B, TollBit) | 2024- | [Felicis](https://www.felicis.com/blog/letta), [e2b](https://e2b.dev/blog/series-a) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which rounds and vehicles (Fund II vs SPV II vs Sunflower Ivy) hold Mirendil, Elorian, Applied Compute, Volantis and Embo, and at what size?\n- Her exact Sequoia title and leave date. Whether Railway, PostHog, Temporal, tldraw and Hadrian were Sequoia, personal or Sunflower checks.\n- The Lilt and Trupo rounds. The Tecton Series A date.\n- Whether Fund II really keeps no reserves (aggregator only).\n- Podcasts and X: not read this pass.\n\n## Update 2026-11-11\n*Second pass. Searched for podcasts, interviews, X and quotes (\"said Liu Jiang\"), the WSJ Fund II story, Industry Ventures' post, Venture Capital Journal, 100 Women in AI, and 2026 round announcements naming Sunflower. **No podcast, talk or bylined writing by her was found**, and no press release quotes her: she says nothing in public beyond founder testimonials on her own site. The WSJ article (2025-04-23, \"Sunflower Capital, Led by Sequoia Alum, Collects $150 Million\") is paywalled. The Industry Ventures post (\"Sunflower Capital Announces $250M Funds I and II\", 2025-05-14) has only a headline [src](https://www.industryventures.com/sunflower-capital-announces-250m-funds-i-and-ii/). Since Goldman bought Industry Ventures (2026-01-02), that LP link now sits inside Goldman's XIG platform (the post is not an LP confirmation).*\n\n**New findings**\n- **E2B is now a scale company that has raised little.** She led its ~$2.5M pre-seed in 2023. On 2026-11-05 it said it had \"passed $100 million in annualized run rate (ARR)\", up from $8M a year earlier, on only $38M raised in total (the headline says $120M; the body says $100M) [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/e2b-which-gives-ai-agents-their-own-cloud-computers-passes-120-million-0). A large round is likely next. She held the first institutional check and has followed in every round since, so she is a natural route into it.\n- **Fast promotion at Sequoia.** 100 Women in AI (she is #94, Los Angeles) says \"she became its youngest partner after starting as an intern\" while at Stanford. It also says she has backed \"over 20 unicorns\", naming dbt Labs and Vercel, neither of which is on the Sunflower site [src](https://www.100womeninai.com/liu-jiang). The Sequoia partner years 2015-2020 appear only in a Venture Capital Journal search snippet and aggregators (unconfirmed). That snippet also says the first fund targeted $30M, which matches the Form D. Caution: startupintros.com merges her with a different Liu Jiang (Trends Media Group, Chinese consumer-internet deals). Ignore that profile.\n- **Fund II now joins seed and Series A rounds led by others, not only pre-product leads.** Unannounced-by-her participations in 2026: Corridor A ($25M, Felicis), Ciridae seed ($20M, Accel), Adaptive Innovations A ($50M, Felicis/BCV), Podium Automation A ($18M, Construct) and The Better Money Company seed ($10M, a16z crypto) [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/sectors/technology/articles/ciridae-raises-20-million-led-140000061.html), [src](https://pulse2.com/adaptive-innovations-raises-60-million-to-build-ai-native-home-health-provider/), [src](https://raising.fi/news/podium-automation-series-a-june-2026), [src](https://fortune.com/2026/03/31/the-better-money-company-stablecoin-clearinghouse-a16z-crypto-seed-round/), [src](https://www.corridor.dev/blog/series-a). Ciridae (Jack Soslow) and Better Money (Sam Broner) were both founded by former a16z investors, with a16z in both rounds. That is a repeat co-investor path (pattern from two cases).\n- **Frontier labs: still aggregator-only.** Search snippets say PitchBook lists Sunflower among Mirendil's investors and Forge lists it in Elorian's seed rounds. Both pages returned 403, so this is **unconfirmed**. The Applied Compute funding table (siliconvalleyinvestclub) names Sunflower in none of its four rounds, so Sunflower's entry into Applied Compute remains unknown.\n- Neither press release names Liu personally. Every 2026 deal above is a firm-level row (`sunflower-capital`). She approves all of them as sole GP (Form D/ADV), but no source puts her name on these deals.\n\n**Portfolio additions**\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| dbt Labs; Vercel | named as her unicorn investments; round and vehicle unconfirmed | | | | [100WAI](https://www.100womeninai.com/liu-jiang) |\n| Ciridae (firm) | Seed $20M | 2026-05-11 | no | Accel (lead), a16z, GC, Backcountry | [Yahoo](https://finance.yahoo.com/sectors/technology/articles/ciridae-raises-20-million-led-140000061.html) |\n| Adaptive Innovations (firm) | Series A $50M | 2026-06-06 | no | Felicis, BCV (leads), Optum, Conviction, BoxGroup | [pulse2](https://pulse2.com/adaptive-innovations-raises-60-million-to-build-ai-native-home-health-provider/) |\n| Podium Automation (firm) | Series A $18M | 2026-06-15 | no | Construct (lead), a16z | [raising.fi](https://raising.fi/news/podium-automation-series-a-june-2026) |\n| The Better Money Company (firm) | Seed $10M | 2026-03-31 | no | a16z crypto (lead), BoxGroup | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2026/03/31/the-better-money-company-stablecoin-clearinghouse-a16z-crypto-seed-round/) |\n\n**Fit, revised:** still high as a sourcing node. The concrete action is E2B, where the next round will be far larger than anything Sunflower can lead. Her network also reaches the Felicis/Accel/a16z leads she keeps following.\n\n**Still unconfirmed:** her exact Sequoia partner dates; the vehicle behind dbt Labs and Vercel; any Mirendil, Elorian, Applied Compute, Volantis or Embo round; the Singularity \"Sunflower\". There is no public X account under her name; the @seedtosunflower handle is still unverified.\n\n## Sources\n- https://sunflowercapital.co/ ; https://sunflowercapital.co/portfolio (fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/323327/PDF/323327.pdf\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1943848/000194384822000003/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2060407/000206040725000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20191213075916/https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/liu-jiang/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-tecton/ ; https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/\n- https://cc.cz/cc25/e2b/ ; https://en.ain.ua/2024/10/18/e2b-raises-11m-in-seed-funding ; https://e2b.dev/blog/series-a ; https://www.felicis.com/blog/letta ; https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/leading-media-and-tech-investors-bet-on-tollbit---the-first-platform-to-tackle-the-new-economics-of-content-in-the-ai-era-302080097.html\n- https://www.everythingstartups.com/vc-funds/sunflower-capital (aggregator)\n- 2026-11-11: https://www.100womeninai.com/liu-jiang ; https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/e2b-which-gives-ai-agents-their-own-cloud-computers-passes-120-million-0 ; https://www.industryventures.com/sunflower-capital-announces-250m-funds-i-and-ii/ ; https://finance.yahoo.com/sectors/technology/articles/ciridae-raises-20-million-led-140000061.html ; https://pulse2.com/adaptive-innovations-raises-60-million-to-build-ai-native-home-health-provider/ ; https://raising.fi/news/podium-automation-series-a-june-2026 ; https://fortune.com/2026/03/31/the-better-money-company-stablecoin-clearinghouse-a16z-crypto-seed-round/ ; https://www.corridor.dev/blog/series-a ; https://siliconvalleyinvestclub.com/companies/applied-compute/\n", "person:logan-bartlett": "\n# Logan Bartlett\n\n## One-line take\nRedpoint's Series B/C growth partner and host of The Logan Bartlett Show. Their interviews give a clear, candid record of how a disciplined, smaller growth fund decides, and Bartlett is openly skeptical that much AI equity value will go to venture-backed startups.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why Bartlett wins:** Bartlett combines a banker's valuation discipline with a large media network. The firm brand gets Redpoint into the room and Bartlett wins on their own name. Bartlett says the podcast makes them \"better at my job in a meaningful way\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ldc-byqwnLc).\n- **What constrains them:**\n  - Bartlett invests at Series B/C only, from a fund kept deliberately small ($725M in 2021).\n  - Bartlett caps themself at about 8-10 boards.\n  - By their own account Bartlett is not psychologically suited to seed [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BFgqq-CMgRY).\n- **What Bartlett would never do:**\n  - Treat small checks as options and then elbow other investors out of later rounds.\n  - Use board votes to oust a founder-CEO, except in ethical or catastrophic cases [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Snowflake was their biggest miss.** Bartlett passed at Battery: \"the customer calls at the time were kind of raw about the business,\" and AWS Redshift looked unbeatable. Their lesson: \"if you feel like it's inevitable get on board\" (20VC, 2022-08-30, 35:31 and 37:02) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo)\n- **Braze (then Appboy) was their best investment.** Bartlett spent \"18 months of haggling with them on and off over price\" at a 90-120 post, expecting a $300-400M acquisition; the company IPO'd at about $5B. Their takeaway: \"don't get too hung up on the 10 million here 10 million there\" (32:01) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo)\n- **Underwriting bar:** \"three to five x with 10x plus upside.\" Bartlett underwrites to multiple on dollars rather than ownership. In their words, \"we really focus on the the outcomes more than the percentages,\" and Bartlett contrasts this with Redpoint's early team (28:01, 33:30) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo)\n- **Fund mechanics:** Redpoint's 2021 early-growth fund targeted $650M: \"650, we ended up at 725.\" LPs kept asking how they would survive against Tiger, so the team built a \"battle card\" for why they stayed small (2023-07-21, 53:02) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pE-M0-byxTA)\n- **Fund structure:** the growth fund is raised separately from the early fund, with separate decisions and hiring, \"and not be a follow-on vehicle\" (Turpentine VC, 2023-11-28, 32:01) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BFgqq-CMgRY)\n- **Market-timing rule:** whenever the market forces Redpoint to \"rethink our investment prosecution and investment committee,\" it has been a terrible time to invest in that category. Bartlett cites mobile, SaaS and crypto, and is wary of AI on the same grounds (54:32) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pE-M0-byxTA)\n- **On AI equity value (2023):** \"I'm probably a skeptic of like the near-term equity value\" going to venture-backed AI startups beyond OpenAI and a short list. This episode has no speaker labels, so the attribution comes from context. Bartlett adds that it is \"easier to predict who the losers are than who the winners are\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pE-M0-byxTA) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BFgqq-CMgRY)\n- **AcuityMD deal path:** Eric Vishria (Benchmark) did the seed/A. Bartlett led the next round: \"the series a was more like a series b.\" The two had already sat together on the Amplitude board (57:31) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo)\n- **How Redpoint got them:** Redpoint turned them down in 2013 and kept in touch for six years. In 2019 their default was \"I probably would have raised my own fund,\" but Redpoint closed them in about two and a half weeks (48:30) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BFgqq-CMgRY)\n- **Most underrated angels:** Zach Weinberg and Nat Turner (Operator Partners). Bartlett borrows Weinberg's test that \"the best founders can can tell a very high level story\" and also go deep on detail (55:02) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo)\n- **Founder secondary:** \"very comfortable with secondary for uh for Founders\" at Series B/C, because it keeps founders' ambitions aligned with going big (Ordinary Astronauts, 2022-07-28, 26:31) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3n20XeEn-vk)\n- **Size of the asset class:** \"I don't think we're like 20 to 30% overfunded.\" Bartlett means several times overfunded, and expects venture to shrink (55:00) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BFgqq-CMgRY)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **Against:** Bartlett is a Series B/C enterprise growth investor, not a seed or lab investor, and says early stage would wear them down.\n- **For:**\n  - Bartlett is a likely next-round lead for AI-native automation and apps, such as Legora (led the Series A), Aaru (led the $80M A), Scribe, n8n and Glow.\n  - Their podcast reaches frontier-lab CEOs (Dario Amodei, Sam Altman, Alexandr Wang, Aravind Srinivas).\n  - Their fund-mechanics and AI-valuation views are a useful read on what a disciplined growth buyer will pay for.\n- **Not a fit for:** co-leading a $600M lab seed.\n\n## Background\n- Investment banking out of undergrad at a small boutique software-advisory bank, working alongside Vista, TA and JMI. Says Bartlett joined tech in 2011 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pE-M0-byxTA)\n- Interviewed at Redpoint and Battery in 2013. Redpoint passed and Battery hired them; Bartlett was there about six years [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BFgqq-CMgRY)\n- Joined Redpoint as Managing Director, signing in December 2019 and starting in early 2020 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo) [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/logan-bartlett/)\n- Started the Cartoon Avatars podcast in January-February 2022 with Zach Weinberg and Nikita, later renamed The Logan Bartlett Show [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3n20XeEn-vk)\n- Forbes Midas Brink list, 2024 [src](https://www.forbes.com/sites/truebridge/2024/06/04/venture-capitals-up-and-comers-the-2024-midas-brink-list/)\n- Education: nothing found beyond their own remark that Bartlett went to a liberal-arts school.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/logan-bartlett/ | Firm bio |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/ | Co-authors Redpoint market updates and investment memos |\n| X | https://twitter.com/loganbartlett | Follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/loganbartlett/ | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/the-logan-bartlett-show/id1606770839 | 150 numbered episodes, 2022-01 to 2025-09 (feed); also co-hosts This Won't Last (2024-25) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2016-04: 20VC, \"The Rise Of The Celebrity VC & The Pros and Cons Of Staying Private\" (at Battery). [link](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-the-rise-of-the-celebrity-vc-904). No transcript read.\n- 2022-07: Ordinary Astronauts (Every), rebroadcast on their feed. [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3n20XeEn-vk). Valuation as a function of cash needed; comfortable with founder secondary; why Bartlett started the podcast.\n- 2022-08: 20VC #920. [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo). Braze hit, Snowflake miss, 3-5x/10x underwriting, board philosophy.\n- 2023-07: Industry Episode with Zach Weinberg. [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pE-M0-byxTA). The $650M to $725M fund; AI equity skepticism; the \"rethink IC\" timing rule.\n- 2023-10: Every Podcast Mistake in 85 Episodes. [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ldc-byqwnLc). Still does all guest outreach themself.\n- 2023-11: Turpentine VC E15. [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BFgqq-CMgRY). Redpoint structure, the 2013 pass, venture overfunded; re-released 2025-03.\n- 2025-02: CNBC on DeepSeek. [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JpRd_lo2_Ps). Not mined.\n- 2025-06: Venture Unlocked with Samir Kaji. [link](https://ventureunlocked.substack.com/p/from-zero-to-billions-decoding-startup). Covers Series B underwriting and AI revenue durability. Not mined.\n- The full catalogue of 186 items is in `data/rows/logan-bartlett-voice-media.csv`.\n\n## How they think\n- **Underwriting:** probability-weighted outcomes, which Bartlett likens to blackjack. Bartlett says their outcome scenarios often undershoot the winners [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo)\n- **Firm strategy:** each partner must stand for something and the firm must be more than the sum of its partners. Bartlett thinks of it as the part before the @ (the person) and the part after it (the firm) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo)\n- **Contrarian views:**\n  - The \"death of the middle tier\" of funds was overstated.\n  - Venture is several times overfunded.\n  - Each tech wave produces only a handful of big winners [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BFgqq-CMgRY)\n- **Calls right or wrong:**\n  - Wrong on Snowflake (passed) and on Braze's ceiling.\n  - In 2022 Bartlett predicted many zeros among recent crypto fund vintages [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo)\n\n## Read these first\n1. 20VC #920 (2022): the clearest account of their rules, hit and miss. [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo)\n2. Turpentine VC (2023): Redpoint's structure and their own path. [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BFgqq-CMgRY)\n3. Industry Episode with Zach Weinberg (2023): fund size and AI skepticism. [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pE-M0-byxTA)\n4. Aaru memo (2026): a recent AI deal written in their own words. [link](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/a-step-towards-predicting-the-future-our-investment-in-aaru-)\n\n## Social engagement\n- Bartlett describes their early X persona as an ironic, always-in-character voice, like The Colbert Report for tech.\n- Bartlett booked early podcast guests through X: Parker Conrad followed them and Bartlett sent a DM [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3n20XeEn-vk)\n- Frequent co-hosts and amplifiers: Zach Weinberg, Keith Rabois and Kevin Ryan on This Won't Last [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/this-wont-last/id1765665937)\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Braze (Appboy) | Undisclosed (at Battery) | before 2020 | unknown | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo) |\n| Pendo | Undisclosed (at Battery) | before 2016-04 | unknown | | [src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-the-rise-of-the-celebrity-vc-904) |\n| StreamSets | Undisclosed (at Battery) | before 2016-04 | unknown | | [src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-the-rise-of-the-celebrity-vc-904) |\n| AcuityMD | Series A (described as really a B) | 2022 | yes (their words) | Benchmark (seed/A) | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo) |\n| Crossbeam | Series B | | unknown | FirstMark (A), Uncork (seed), a16z | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/logan-bartlett/) |\n| Scribe | Series B | 2024-02-15 | unknown | | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/fresh-ink-welcoming-scribe-making-everything-work-in-the-modern-workplace) |\n| Legora | Series A ($25M) | 2024-07-17 | yes | Benchmark; Y Combinator | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/legora) |\n| Sesame | Series B | 2025-05-22 | unknown | | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/sesame) |\n| n8n | Series C ($180M) | 2025-10-09 | no | Accel (lead) | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/n8n) |\n| Aaru | Series A ($80M per Redpoint memo) | 2025-12-05 | yes | | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/aaru) |\n| Glow | Series B ($180M total) | 2026-07-22 | co-led | Sequoia; Cyberstarts; Greenoaks | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/glow) |\n| Ramp, Cyera, Cribl, Monte Carlo, FloQast, Materialize, Workato | Series B / C+ | | unknown | | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/logan-bartlett/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Braze:** Bartlett negotiated on and off with the company for about 18 months at Battery over a 90-120 post-money. Bartlett thought a $300-400M sale was the likely outcome; the company went public at about $5B [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo)\n- **Snowflake:** Bartlett passed at Battery after weak customer calls and fear of Redshift. Redpoint was later an investor [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo)\n- **AcuityMD:** a Benchmark (Vishria) seed/A followed by their lead. Bartlett bet on an underserved vertical with little venture-backed competition, comparing the opportunity to Veeva and ServiceTitan [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Battery Ventures | Investor, about 6 years | 2013 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BFgqq-CMgRY) |\n| Redpoint Ventures | Managing Director | 2020-01 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo) |\n| Neeraj Agrawal | Battery mentor | 2013 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo) |\n| Eric Vishria | Co-board member (Amplitude, AcuityMD) | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo) |\n| Zach Weinberg | Podcast co-host | 2022 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3n20XeEn-vk) |\n| Elliot Geidt | Redpoint partner who passed in 2013 and recruited them in 2019 | 2013 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BFgqq-CMgRY) |\n| Matt Turck, Andy McLoughlin | Crossbeam co-board members | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo) |\n| Jacob Effron, Enzo Wiener | Redpoint co-investors on Legora, Sesame, Scribe and Aaru | 2024 | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/legora) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What did Bartlett say on Venture Unlocked (2025) about underwriting AI revenue durability? The page has no transcript.\n- The 2016 20VC episode is their oldest item and has no transcript.\n- Which company was the one where Bartlett \"lost faith\" through missed execution (20VC 2022, 37:31)?\n- What is the Redpoint growth fund size after the 2021 $725M fund?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HmJtosI1yQo\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BFgqq-CMgRY\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3n20XeEn-vk\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pE-M0-byxTA\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ldc-byqwnLc\n- https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/logan-bartlett/\n- https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-the-rise-of-the-celebrity-vc-904\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/the-logan-bartlett-show/id1606770839\n- https://www.forbes.com/sites/truebridge/2024/06/04/venture-capitals-up-and-comers-the-2024-midas-brink-list/\n", "person:lukas-decoster": "\n# Lukas Decoster\n\n![Lukas Decoster](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6a13f4c5d24200cb849a68be/6a13f4c5d24200cb849a69ae_HB_240724_0311%20%281%29.avif)\n\nPhoto from the [Hummingbird team page](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team). The URL returned HTTP 200 (image/avif) on 2026-11-10.\n\n## One-line take\nDecoster is Hummingbird's fund lawyer and operator who became a partner. They are the only Hummingbird partner outside the two founders whom the firm's own site ties by name to portfolio companies: Basecamp Research (AI for biology, valued at $800M in 2026 per a Reuters headline) and Automata (lab automation) ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)). For you, they matter less as a deal sourcer and more as the person who structured Hummingbird's funds and runs its investor relations.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** they built the firm's machinery. The team page says they joined in 2016 and structured fund management, portfolio investments and investor relations, and that they still lead the operations team ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)). An older bio, mirrored on TheOrg, calls them \"the firm's CFO and General Counsel\" ([TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/hummingbird-ventures/org-chart/lukas-decoster), aggregator). The firm now credits them on two deals, which suggests a path from back office to an investing seat.\n- **Constraint:** they don't hold the decision keys. The Generalist reports that both Firat Ileri and Barend Van den Brande must agree on every investment ([Generalist 2023-12-03](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)). \"Involved with\" on the team page could mean board or governance work rather than sourcing. No source says which it is.\n- **What they'd never do (inference, unsourced):** nothing they have said in public was found, so there is no stated \"never\".\n- **The honest negative:** no press release, round announcement, podcast or post names Decoster on either deal. The only attribution is the firm's own bio line.\n\n## Differential insight\nThe web-search budget was used up before this profile started, so this is built only from direct fetches of the firm page, company pages, TechCrunch, The Generalist and TheOrg. No podcast, Substack, X post or talk by Decoster was found or reachable.\n\n- **Title history points to a CFO/GC-to-partner path.** The older bio (TheOrg mirror) reads \"Lukas is the firm's CFO and General Counsel\". The current team page reads \"Lukas is a Partner at Hummingbird\" and adds the deal credits ([TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/hummingbird-ventures/org-chart/lukas-decoster), aggregator; [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)). The promotion date is not found.\n- **Basecamp grew about 11x in valuation from 2022 to 2026.** TechCrunch: \"PitchBook put its last valuation, from 2022, at a very modest $71 million\" ([TC 2024-10-09](https://techcrunch.com/2024/10/09/basecamp-research-taps-60m-to-build-a-gpt-for-biology/)). Reuters headline, 2026-09-23: \"Basecamp valued at $800 million after $140 million funding round\" (seen in the list on [Basecamp's news page](https://www.basecamp-research.com/news-research). The [Reuters article](https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/ai-based-drug-developer-basecamp-valued-800-million-after-140-million-funding-2026-09-23/) itself could not be fetched).\n- **Hummingbird was early in Basecamp, alongside True Ventures and Valo.** TechCrunch: \"its previous investors include Hummingbird, True Ventures, and strategic backer Valo\" ([TC 2024-10-09](https://techcrunch.com/2024/10/09/basecamp-research-taps-60m-to-build-a-gpt-for-biology/)). Hummingbird's portfolio page labels Basecamp \"Pre-Seed/Seed\" with the year 2020 ([portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio)). It is not clear whether 2020 is the investment year or the founding year.\n- **Hummingbird followed on in Automata's Series B.** Automata's release: \"with participation from returning investors Hummingbird, Latitude Ventures\" ([Automata 2022-02-22](https://automata.tech/company-news/automata-raises-us50-million-in-series-b-financing-to-accelerate-automation-in-life-sciences)).\n- **Their pre-VC work was building funds, not startups.** Team page: \"providing all-around assistance in the structuring, setup and governance of VC/PE funds\" ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)). That makes them an expert on how a manager's fund is put together, which is directly useful to you as an emerging manager.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Basecamp Research is AI-for-biology foundation-model work (the EDEN model family), now backed by NVIDIA and by Menlo's Anthology Fund (the Anthropic fund) ([Series C release](https://www.basecamp-research.com/news-research/basecamp-research-raises-140m-to-advance-ai-designed-therapeutics), [homepage](https://www.basecamp-research.com/)). That sits next to your frontier-AI lens but isn't a lab or a chip bet. Automata, a life-sciences robotics company, fits loosely under AI-native automation. Decoster has no decision rights and has no public voice, so they won't be your route into Hummingbird's AI deals (that is Ileri; see [firms/hummingbird-ventures.md](../firms/hummingbird-ventures.md)). Where they are useful is LP mechanics. They structured and run IR for a firm that has raised about €540M across seven vintages and in 2026 added a growth vehicle ([firm file](../firms/hummingbird-ventures.md)). They are the person who would know Hummingbird's LP base and how its fund-of-funds sibling Nomads is wired.\n\n## Background\n- Before 2016: corporate/M&A lawyer for several venture firms and startups. They worked on structuring, setting up and governing VC/PE funds, plus transactional work for early- and growth-stage startups ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)). The employer firm or firms are not named.\n- 2016: joined Hummingbird and structured its operations (fund management, portfolio investments, investor relations). They still lead the operations team ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)).\n- Undated: CFO and General Counsel title (older bio via [TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/hummingbird-ventures/org-chart/lukas-decoster), aggregator).\n- Current: Partner, tagged \"Both\" (investing and operations) on the team page ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)).\n- Education: MBA, Fordham Gabelli School of Business (New York); Master of Science in Law (JD), Ghent University ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)). Years not stated.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [hummingbird.vc/team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) | Firm bio (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Not searchable this run |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | Not searchable this run |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The search budget was exhausted, so this is not a confirmed absence.\n\n## How they think\n- Nothing in their own words was found.\n- What the record implies: their background is fund structure and governance. The two named deals are both UK life-science platforms (Basecamp: biodiversity data plus AI; Automata: lab robotics), both in Hummingbird's biotech sector on its [portfolio page](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio). That suggests a deep-tech/bio governance seat rather than consumer or AI-infrastructure sourcing.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Hummingbird team bio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team): the only first-party attribution of deals and role.\n2. [The Generalist on Hummingbird (2023-12-03)](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird): the two-key decision model, which limits what any non-founder partner decides. It does not mention Decoster.\n3. [TechCrunch on Basecamp's Series B (2024-10-09)](https://techcrunch.com/2024/10/09/basecamp-research-taps-60m-to-build-a-gpt-for-biology/): early investor list and the 2022 valuation.\n4. [Automata Series B release (2022-02-22)](https://automata.tech/company-news/automata-raises-us50-million-in-series-b-financing-to-accelerate-automation-in-life-sciences): Hummingbird as a returning investor.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Basecamp Research | Hummingbird entry round not stated (portfolio stage label \"Pre-Seed/Seed\", year label 2020) | not stated | not stated | True Ventures, Valo (earlier investors per TC) | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team), [portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio), [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2024/10/09/basecamp-research-taps-60m-to-build-a-gpt-for-biology/) |\n| Automata | Hummingbird entry round not stated (portfolio stage label \"Early\"); returning investor in the $50M Series B | Series B 2022-02-22 | no (Octopus Ventures led the B) | Octopus Ventures, Latitude Ventures, ABB Technology Ventures, Isomer Capital, In-Q-Tel | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team), [Automata](https://automata.tech/company-news/automata-raises-us50-million-in-series-b-financing-to-accelerate-automation-in-life-sciences) |\n\nThe firm credits Decoster only with being \"involved\". Neither source says they personally sourced or led any round.\n\n## Deal stories\n**Basecamp Research (standout).**\n- *How they found it:* not found. No source names who sourced it. Hummingbird's portfolio page lists Basecamp as a UK, Pre-Seed/Seed company with the year 2020 ([portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio)). Former Hummingbird biotech investor Pablo Lubroth is listed on TheOrg as a Basecamp board member ([TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/basecamp-research/org-chart/pablo-lubroth), aggregator, undated). So the sourcing credit may sit with Lubroth or the founders, and Decoster's \"involvement\" may be governance. This is unconfirmed.\n- *Who else was there early:* True Ventures and strategic backer Valo, named by TechCrunch with Hummingbird as \"previous investors\" ([TC 2024-10-09](https://techcrunch.com/2024/10/09/basecamp-research-taps-60m-to-build-a-gpt-for-biology/)).\n- *Round history:*\n  - 2022-12-13: $20M (WSJ headline listed on [Basecamp's news page](https://www.basecamp-research.com/news-research); the article itself was paywalled and not read). PitchBook valued the company at $71M in 2022, per TechCrunch.\n  - 2024-10-09: $60M Series B led by Singular, with S32 and redalpine. By then the company had raised $85M to date ([TC](https://techcrunch.com/2024/10/09/basecamp-research-taps-60m-to-build-a-gpt-for-biology/)).\n  - 2025-09: NVIDIA announced an investment as part of its UK push (Bloomberg/Sifted headlines on the [news page](https://www.basecamp-research.com/news-research)).\n  - 2026-09-23: $140M Series C led by S32, with Menlo's Anthology Fund, NVIDIA, the NATO Innovation Fund, True Ventures, Singular, redalpine and others ([release](https://www.basecamp-research.com/news-research/basecamp-research-raises-140m-to-advance-ai-designed-therapeutics)). Hummingbird is not named in the Series C release, but its logo is still on Basecamp's investor list ([homepage](https://www.basecamp-research.com/)).\n- *What it's worth now:* $800M post-money after the Series C, per the Reuters headline (2026-09-23; [news page](https://www.basecamp-research.com/news-research)). That is about 11x the 2022 PitchBook figure. Hummingbird's ownership, entry price and any multiple are not found.\n\n**Automata.**\n- *How they found it / when:* not found. Hummingbird's portfolio lists Automata as a UK \"Early\" company with the year 2015 ([portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio)); 2015 is probably the founding year.\n- *Who else was there:* by February 2022, Hummingbird was a returning investor alongside Latitude Ventures, ABB Technology Ventures and Isomer Capital. That $50M Series B was led by Octopus Ventures, with In-Q-Tel as a strategic investor ([release](https://automata.tech/company-news/automata-raises-us50-million-in-series-b-financing-to-accelerate-automation-in-life-sciences)).\n- *Later:* the company listed a $40M raise on 2023-10-03 backed by Dimension and A.P. Moller Holding. It then raised a $45M Series C led by Dimension on 2026-01-29, with Danaher Ventures, Tru Arrow Partners, Octopus Ventures and Entrepreneurs First ([news](https://automata.tech/about-us/company-news), [Series C](https://automata.tech/company-news/automata-raises-45m-series-c-funding)). Hummingbird is not named in the Series C release.\n- *Worth now:* no valuation found.\n\n**How they source:** nothing specific to Decoster was found. At the firm level, The Generalist describes outbound sourcing by every team member and a two-partner consensus to invest ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hummingbird Ventures | Partner; leads operations | 2016 | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) |\n| Firat Ileri, Barend Van den Brande | The two partners whose agreement every Hummingbird investment needs | not stated | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Basecamp Research | \"Involved with\" (role not stated) | not stated | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) |\n| Automata | \"Involved with\" (role not stated) | not stated | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) |\n| Pablo Lubroth | Former Hummingbird colleague (overlap years not found); listed as a Basecamp board member (aggregator) | not stated | [TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/basecamp-research/org-chart/pablo-lubroth) |\n| Kobe Hendrickx | Finance Director on the operations team Decoster leads | not stated | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When were they made Partner? An archived team page or a LinkedIn check would date it. That would settle fast_promotion.\n- Do they hold the Hummingbird board or observer seat at Basecamp or Automata? Check UK Companies House officer listings for both companies.\n- Which round did Hummingbird enter Basecamp in (2020 seed?), and at what valuation?\n- Did Hummingbird take pro rata in Basecamp's Series C? It is absent from the release but still has a logo on the site.\n- As head of IR: who are Hummingbird's LPs for the 2026 growth vehicle, and how does Nomads (the fund-of-funds) relate to the main funds?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.hummingbird.vc/team (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://theorg.com/org/hummingbird-ventures/org-chart/lukas-decoster (aggregator)\n- https://theorg.com/org/basecamp-research/org-chart/pablo-lubroth (aggregator)\n- https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird (2023-12-03)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/10/09/basecamp-research-taps-60m-to-build-a-gpt-for-biology/\n- https://www.basecamp-research.com/ and https://www.basecamp-research.com/news-research\n- https://www.basecamp-research.com/news-research/basecamp-research-raises-140m-to-advance-ai-designed-therapeutics (2026-09-23)\n- https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/ai-based-drug-developer-basecamp-valued-800-million-after-140-million-funding-2026-09-23/ (headline only, not fetched)\n- https://automata.tech/about-us/company-news\n- https://automata.tech/company-news/automata-raises-us50-million-in-series-b-financing-to-accelerate-automation-in-life-sciences (2022-02-22)\n- https://automata.tech/company-news/automata-raises-45m-series-c-funding (2026-01-29)\n", "person:luke-pappas": "\n# Luke Pappas\n\n## One-line take\nA homegrown NEA partner who moved from Menlo Park to London and now does NEA's European applied-AI deals from seed (Corca, LizzyAI) to growth (Synthesia D), but he works inside Philip Chopin's Europe office rather than running it.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He has NEA's balance sheet behind him and plays the long game on relationships. In both Sana and DJUST he kept talking to a founder who wasn't raising, and later won the round ([Sana post](https://www.nea.com/blog/our-investment-in-sana-labs-ai-powered-learning-and-knowledge), [DJUST post](https://www.nea.com/blog/our-investment-in-djust-making-b2b-commerce-easy-for-everyone)).\n- **What constrains him:**\n  - He is a Partner but not, as far as the record shows, on an investment committee. NEA retired the \"General Partner\" title in 2023, and in May 2023 only Jacobson and Larco got IC seats while Pappas got the Partner title ([NEA, 2023-05-16](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-appoints-technology-investors-aaron-jacobson-and-vanessa-larco-to-senior-investing-team-roles-lily-huang-danielle-lay-and-luke-pappas-promoted-to-partner)).\n  - In Europe, Philip Chopin is the senior name. Chopin was hired in 2022 as NEA's first European-based investing partner and was named MD, Head of Europe on 2026-06-29 ([NEA](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors)). Chopin is co-author on most of Pappas's European deal posts.\n- **What he'd never do:** Nothing he has said rules a category out. His published deals are applied software (enterprise AI apps, B2B commerce, compliance, video). None are models, chips or compute.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Synthesia was a top target from the day the London office opened.** NEA moved fast when the Series D came up, and it became NEA's largest European check: \"our largest single check in our European portfolio to date\" ([NEA blog, 2025-01-15](https://www.nea.com/blog/synthesia-ai-video-creation)). The same post says \"we dropped everything and ran as quickly as we could\".\n- **Sana was a missed round that NEA won six months later.** \"Unfortunately, we met Sana a few weeks too late\". They met Joel Hellermark at Slush 2022, after the Series B was agreed. NEA then tracked the company for about six months, put ~$28M into the Series B extension (2023-05/06), and led the $55M Series C at a $500M valuation (2024-10-30). Workday agreed to buy Sana for ~$1.1B on 2025-09-16. Sources: [NEA 2023](https://www.nea.com/blog/our-investment-in-sana-labs-ai-powered-learning-and-knowledge), [DLA Piper 2023](https://sweden.dlapiper.com/sv/node/4665), [DLA Piper 2024](https://sweden.dlapiper.com/en/news/dla-piper-has-advised-sana-labs-ab-connection-its-recent-55-million-series-c-funding-round), [Workday](https://investor.workday.com/files/doc_news/Workday-Signs-Definitive-Agreement-to-Acquire-Sana-09-16-2025-2025.pdf).\n- **DJUST started as a cold coffee in Paris with a founder who wasn't raising.** \"DJUST wasn't planning on raising capital at the time\" ([NEA blog, 2023-06-12](https://www.nea.com/blog/our-investment-in-djust-making-b2b-commerce-easy-for-everyone)). NEA kept talking through the 2022–23 winter and then did the Series A alongside Elaia. This is the same pattern as Sana: get in before the process, stay close, and win the next round.\n- **His thesis on Europe.** \"Europe today is where the US was in the 2010s\" ([NEA team page](https://www.nea.com/team/luke-pappas)).\n- **US and European pitches differ in style.** In 2021, before he moved to London, he wrote that European pitches \"focus more on immediate operating goals as opposed to the grand vision\". His advice to founders is to tune the pitch to a US VC's expectation of a 10-year story ([NEA blog, 2021-11-23](https://www.nea.com/blog/lisbon-the-new-sf-my-observations-from-web-summit)).\n- **He was a founder before he was a banker.** The Scaling Stateside show notes say he \"founded a product used across Stanford University\" and link to QueueStatus, an online queue tool used for office hours ([podcast page](https://scalingstateside.transistor.fm/7), [queuestatus.com](https://queuestatus.com/)). That he founded it is the podcast's description; we have not confirmed it independently.\n- **He worked on NEA's internal firm deals, not only portfolio deals.** The 2023 promotion release credits him with NEA's 2018 secondary sale and with forming Connect Ventures, NEA's joint venture with CAA ([NEA, 2023-05-16](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-appoints-technology-investors-aaron-jacobson-and-vanessa-larco-to-senior-investing-team-roles-lily-huang-danielle-lay-and-luke-pappas-promoted-to-partner)). This points to firm-builder work and close access to the senior partners.\n- **NEA culture on promotions.** Tony Florence, in the same release: \"promotions typically come after someone has already been doing the job\". In other words, the 2023 title lagged the work.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **Why not high:**\n  - His book is application-layer software, mostly European: Synthesia (AI video), Sana (enterprise knowledge AI), spektr (compliance agents), LizzyAI (AI interviews), DJUST (B2B commerce).\n  - There are no labs, pre-training, chips or compute deals.\n  - He has no investment committee seat on record.\n- **Why not low:**\n  - He is a live Partner at a fund that writes growth checks, with real seed activity in AI-native automation. spektr's AI agents and LizzyAI are close to the Serval-type companies the owner likes.\n  - Corca is the one deal toward the frontier: an AI workspace for mathematics, co-led with NVIDIA's NVentures ([NEA blog](https://www.nea.com/blog/investing-in-corca-the-ide-for-mathematics)).\n- **Use:** He is a useful co-investor or contact for European AI-automation companies at seed to Series A, and for an introduction to NEA's growth capital later.\n- **Deals that matter to the owner:** Corca, spektr, LizzyAI, Synthesia.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford University: dual BS in computer science and in management science & engineering. He played four years of varsity baseball ([NEA team page](https://www.nea.com/team/luke-pappas)). Graduation year: unconfirmed.\n- Founded QueueStatus, a queue-management product used at Stanford, per the podcast show notes ([Scaling Stateside](https://scalingstateside.transistor.fm/7)). Dates: unconfirmed.\n- Technology Investment Banking Team at Morgan Stanley ([NEA team page](https://www.nea.com/team/luke-pappas)). The podcast says he worked \"on landmark IPOs\". Dates: unconfirmed.\n- Joined NEA in 2017. Tony Florence \"has worked closely with both investors since they joined the firm in 2017\" ([NEA, 2023-05-16](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-appoints-technology-investors-aaron-jacobson-and-vanessa-larco-to-senior-investing-team-roles-lily-huang-danielle-lay-and-luke-pappas-promoted-to-partner)). His entry title (associate or senior associate) is unconfirmed; no NEA source we read gives it.\n- His early NEA team work was in Menlo Park on US consumer and esports deals: PlayVS (2018), Gen.G (2019) and Sitetracker (2017), all on NEA portfolio pages.\n- Promoted to Partner on 2023-05-16 (same source). That is about 6 years from joining, so `fast_promotion: no`.\n- Moved from California to London. The podcast covers the move ([Scaling Stateside, 2026-01-14](https://scalingstateside.transistor.fm/7)); the date of the move is unconfirmed. His team page lists him under region \"UK\".\n- Also works on Connect Ventures, NEA's joint venture with CAA ([NEA team page](https://www.nea.com/team/luke-pappas)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.nea.com/team/luke-pappas | NEA team page with Q&A and portfolio list; checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.nea.com/team/luke-pappas | Writes NEA investment posts, roughly 1–2 a year (Content tab); no personal blog found |\n| X | nothing found | No account confirmed |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/luke-pappas-78ba5699/ | Linked from NEA team page and the podcast page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-01 — Scaling Stateside (David Rose, Matt Oxley; sponsored by Wilson Sonsini) — E7 \"How US VCs Actually Think (If You're a European Founder)\" — https://scalingstateside.transistor.fm/7. Chapters cover his early career, investment thesis, sector focus, sourcing deals and moving from the US to the UK. The transcript page served only the episode notes, so this is **not mined**; no quotes taken.\n- 2025-02 — Sifted article on NEA in Europe (https://sifted.eu/articles/nea-portfolio-europe-ai). Search snippets say he is quoted on European AI talent and energy. The page returned 403, so it is **not read**.\n\n## How they think\n- **Storytelling is the key seed-stage skill.** He said so in 2021 and repeats it on his team page ([blog](https://www.nea.com/blog/lisbon-the-new-sf-my-observations-from-web-summit), [team page](https://www.nea.com/team/luke-pappas)).\n- **Europe is catching up.** Past exits, better founder talent, and governments that now favour entrepreneurs ([team page](https://www.nea.com/team/luke-pappas)).\n- **The context layer is the bottleneck for enterprise AI.** The Sana 2024 post argues enterprise AI is limited by context and UI rather than by models ([NEA](https://www.nea.com/blog/sana-is-using-ai-to-solve-knowledge-for-enterprises)).\n- **Orchestration over rip-and-replace.** The spektr post frames the company as an agent \"control plane\" that sits on top of a bank's existing compliance systems ([NEA, 2026-04-21](https://www.nea.com/blog/how-spektr-is-building-the-compliance-infrastructure-layer-for-the-ai-era)).\n- **Calls so far:**\n  - Sana is a clear win: led the extension and Series C, then the ~$1.1B Workday exit within about 2.3 years of entry.\n  - Synthesia: early 2025 growth entry at $2.1B ([SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=683933)). Outcome open.\n  - 2021: he was enthusiastic about Web3 and the metaverse ([blog](https://www.nea.com/blog/lisbon-the-new-sf-my-observations-from-web-summit)). None of his deals followed it.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Synthesia: AI to Empower Video Generation for the Enterprise](https://www.nea.com/blog/synthesia-ai-video-creation) (2025-01-15): how NEA prioritised Synthesia from London and the size of the check.\n- [Our Investment in Sana Labs](https://www.nea.com/blog/our-investment-in-sana-labs-ai-powered-learning-and-knowledge) (2023-06-01): the missed round, the six months of tracking, and the dozens of reference calls.\n- [Our Investment in DJUST](https://www.nea.com/blog/our-investment-in-djust-making-b2b-commerce-easy-for-everyone) (2023-06-12): sourcing a Series A from a founder who wasn't raising.\n- [Investing in Corca](https://www.nea.com/blog/investing-in-corca-the-ide-for-mathematics) (2026-07-09): his frontier-adjacent seed thesis.\n- [Lisbon the New SF?](https://www.nea.com/blog/lisbon-the-new-sf-my-observations-from-web-summit) (2021-11-23): his US vs. Europe founder framing, written before the move.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: no X account confirmed. His public writing is NEA blog posts and press quotes (for example the LizzyAI release, [Pulse 2.0](https://pulse2.com/lizzyai-5-million-seed-funding/)). LinkedIn activity was not checked (login wall).\n\n## Portfolio\nRows only for deals after he joined in 2017. Konux (first invested 2016) and MiiM (2015) predate him, so he was added to their teams later.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Sitetracker | Undisclosed (NEA first invested 2017) | 2017 | unknown | | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/portfolio/sitetracker) |\n| Aquabyte | Seed (NEA first invested 2017) | 2017 | unknown | | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/portfolio/aquabyte) (acquired by Vitruvian) |\n| Nordsense | Undisclosed (NEA first invested 2017) | 2017 | unknown | | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/portfolio/nordsense) |\n| PlayVS | Undisclosed (NEA first invested 2018) | 2018 | unknown | Rick Yang board | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/portfolio/playvs) |\n| Cleo (family benefits) | Undisclosed (NEA first invested 2018) | 2018 | unknown | | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/portfolio/cleo) |\n| Gen.G | Undisclosed (NEA first invested 2019) | 2019 | unknown | Rick Yang board | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/portfolio/gen-g) |\n| Inkitt | Series B ($59M) | 2021-10-04 | yes (Sandell; co-lead with Axel Springer per some reports) | Kleiner Perkins, HV Capital, Redalpine, Speedinvest | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2021/10/04/inkitt-inks-a-59m-investment-wants-to-turn-its-data-driven-self-publishing-platform-into-a-multi-media-powerhouse) |\n| Bliq | Series A ($13.5M) | 2022-05-11 | yes | Speedinvest, Proeza, Revel | [tech.eu](https://tech.eu/2022/05/11/distributor-cap-for-gig-economy-drivers-bliq-ignites-135-million/) |\n| Minimum | Undisclosed (NEA first invested 2022) | 2022 | unknown | Tony Florence team | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/portfolio/minimum) |\n| Modyfi | Seed ($7M; NEA page says first invested 2022, announced 2023-08) | 2022 | yes | | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/portfolio/modyfi) |\n| Sana | Series B extension (~$28M from NEA) | 2023-05-31 | yes (NEA sole new investor) | EQT Ventures, Menlo, Workday Ventures | [DLA Piper](https://sweden.dlapiper.com/sv/node/4665) |\n| DJUST | Series A | 2023-06-12 | unknown | Elaia | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/blog/our-investment-in-djust-making-b2b-commerce-easy-for-everyone) |\n| Sana | Series C ($55M at $500M) | 2024-10-30 | yes | Menlo Ventures | [DLA Piper](https://sweden.dlapiper.com/en/news/dla-piper-has-advised-sana-labs-ab-connection-its-recent-55-million-series-c-funding-round) |\n| Synthesia | Series D ($180M at $2.1B) | 2025-01-15 | yes (co-led per his bio) | GV, MMC, FirstMark, WiL, Atlassian Ventures, PSP Growth | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/blog/synthesia-ai-video-creation), [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=683933) |\n| LizzyAI | Seed ($5M) | 2025-12-02 | yes | Speedinvest, Zero Prime Ventures | [Pulse 2.0](https://pulse2.com/lizzyai-5-million-seed-funding/) |\n| spektr | Series A ($20M) | 2026-04-16 | yes | | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/blog/how-spektr-is-building-the-compliance-infrastructure-layer-for-the-ai-era) |\n| Corca | Seed ($7.8M) | 2026-07-09 | yes (co-lead with NVentures) | NVentures, Daft Capital, Bloomberg Beta | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/blog/investing-in-corca-the-ide-for-mathematics) |\n\nOther companies he has worked with:\n- **Not in the deal rows:** Konux, MiiM.\n- **Named in the 2023 release only:** goop and \"With\". These are not on his 2026 team page, and \"With\" is not identified.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Sana:**\n  - NEA met Joel Hellermark at Slush in 2022, a few weeks after Sana had agreed a Series B.\n  - They kept in close contact for about six months, ran \"dozens of reference calls\" with customers, and then put ~$28M into the Series B extension. Scott Sandell joined as director and Chopin as observer ([NEA](https://www.nea.com/blog/our-investment-in-sana-labs-ai-powered-learning-and-knowledge), [DLA Piper](https://sweden.dlapiper.com/sv/node/4665)).\n  - NEA led the 2024 Series C, and Workday agreed to buy Sana in 2025.\n  - Pappas is listed as a board member or observer on his team page.\n- **DJUST:**\n  - NEA met founder Arnaud Rihiant (ex-Mirakl) at a Paris coffee shop in winter 2022, while DJUST was well funded by its Elaia seed and not raising.\n  - NEA stayed in touch until DJUST chose to raise, then invested in the Series A ([NEA](https://www.nea.com/blog/our-investment-in-djust-making-b2b-commerce-easy-for-everyone)).\n- **Synthesia:**\n  - It was on NEA London's \"dream list\" from when the office opened.\n  - The Series D came up in late 2024 and NEA led it.\n  - Co-authored with Chopin. Mark Hawkins (growth) is on the NEA team, and Chopin holds the board seat ([NEA](https://www.nea.com/blog/synthesia-ai-video-creation)).\n- **Corca:**\n  - Co-led the $7.8M seed with NVentures, co-authored with Principal Mason Murray. Pappas holds the board seat (NEA portfolio page).\n  - The founders came from grocery delivery: Anton Gladkoborodov taught himself physics after his exit ([NEA](https://www.nea.com/blog/investing-in-corca-the-ide-for-mathematics)).\n  - The NEA portfolio page says first invested 2025, but the announcement was 2026-07-09. So NEA likely committed months before the announcement (unconfirmed).\n- **spektr:**\n  - Led the Series A, and Pappas holds the board seat.\n  - The thesis cites Paul Graham's essay on \"taste\" in founders ([NEA](https://www.nea.com/blog/how-spektr-is-building-the-compliance-infrastructure-layer-for-the-ai-era)).\n- **How he sources (from the evidence):**\n  - Conferences: Slush for Sana, Web Summit in 2021.\n  - Cold coffees with founders who aren't raising: DJUST.\n  - \"Dream lists\" kept by the London office: Synthesia.\n  - Long tracking periods before a round opens: Sana, DJUST.\n  - NEA's European network brings co-investors back: Speedinvest in Bliq, Inkitt and LizzyAI.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NEA (new-enterprise-associates) | at: Partner, Technology (London) | 2017 | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-appoints-technology-investors-aaron-jacobson-and-vanessa-larco-to-senior-investing-team-roles-lily-huang-danielle-lay-and-luke-pappas-promoted-to-partner) |\n| Morgan Stanley | at: Technology Investment Banking (former) | unconfirmed | [NEA team page](https://www.nea.com/team/luke-pappas) |\n| Connect Ventures (NEA x CAA JV) | works on / helped form | unconfirmed | [NEA team page](https://www.nea.com/team/luke-pappas) |\n| QueueStatus | founder (per podcast notes) | unconfirmed | [Scaling Stateside](https://scalingstateside.transistor.fm/7) |\n| Philip Chopin | co-author and co-dealmaker on Sana, DJUST, Synthesia, spektr, LizzyAI; his Head of Europe | 2022 | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors) |\n| Tony Florence | mentor since 2017; DJUST and Minimum co-team | 2017 | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-appoints-technology-investors-aaron-jacobson-and-vanessa-larco-to-senior-investing-team-roles-lily-huang-danielle-lay-and-luke-pappas-promoted-to-partner) |\n| Scott Sandell | co-author on Sana; Sandell is on the Bliq and Inkitt teams | 2021 | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/blog/our-investment-in-sana-labs-ai-powered-learning-and-knowledge) |\n| Mason Murray | co-author on Corca | 2026 | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/blog/investing-in-corca-the-ide-for-mathematics) |\n| Rick Yang | PlayVS and Gen.G board while Pappas was on the team; Corca team | 2018 | [NEA](https://www.nea.com/portfolio/playvs) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Does he sit on NEA's Venture Capital or Venture Growth Equity Investment Committee? There is no announcement of it.\n- His title when he joined NEA in 2017, and when he moved to London (2022 with Chopin, or later).\n- The Scaling Stateside E7 transcript: the sourcing chapter at 34:18 is the best source still unmined.\n- The full Sifted text (403).\n- Whether he holds the Synthesia board seat. His bio says \"co-led\", and Chopin is listed on the board.\n- Whether NEA's first Modyfi check was in 2022 (portfolio page) or 2023 (announcement).\n- Who \"With\" is, from the 2023 release.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.nea.com/team/luke-pappas\n- https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-appoints-technology-investors-aaron-jacobson-and-vanessa-larco-to-senior-investing-team-roles-lily-huang-danielle-lay-and-luke-pappas-promoted-to-partner\n- https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-names-philip-chopin-managing-director-head-of-europe-and-promotes-two-investors\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/synthesia-ai-video-creation\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/our-investment-in-sana-labs-ai-powered-learning-and-knowledge\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/sana-is-using-ai-to-solve-knowledge-for-enterprises\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/our-investment-in-djust-making-b2b-commerce-easy-for-everyone\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/how-spektr-is-building-the-compliance-infrastructure-layer-for-the-ai-era\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/investing-in-corca-the-ide-for-mathematics\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/lisbon-the-new-sf-my-observations-from-web-summit\n- https://scalingstateside.transistor.fm/7\n- https://sweden.dlapiper.com/sv/node/4665\n- https://sweden.dlapiper.com/en/news/dla-piper-has-advised-sana-labs-ab-connection-its-recent-55-million-series-c-funding-round\n- https://investor.workday.com/files/doc_news/Workday-Signs-Definitive-Agreement-to-Acquire-Sana-09-16-2025-2025.pdf\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=683933\n- https://tech.eu/2022/05/11/distributor-cap-for-gig-economy-drivers-bliq-ignites-135-million/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2021/10/04/inkitt-inks-a-59m-investment-wants-to-turn-its-data-driven-self-publishing-platform-into-a-multi-media-powerhouse\n- https://pulse2.com/lizzyai-5-million-seed-funding/\n- https://queuestatus.com/\n- NEA portfolio pages: aquabyte, bliq, cleo, djust, gen-g, inkitt, konux, lizzyai, miim, minimum, modyfi, nordsense, playvs, sitetracker, spektr (https://www.nea.com/portfolio/<slug>)\n", "person:lydia-day": "\n# Lydia Day\n\n## One-line take\nA junior on Redpoint's growth team whose bio lists three frontier-AI names (Inferact/vLLM, Irregular, Physical Intelligence), though Redpoint's own company pages give the lead credit for each to senior partners. She is worth watching for a promotion, but she does not yet make decisions. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/lydia-day/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she matters:** She sits on Redpoint's growth team ($10M+ and growth checks), co-authors the team's market-data reports with MD Logan Bartlett and Principal Adil Bhatia, and has supported deals in inference infrastructure (Inferact), frontier-model security (Irregular) and robotics foundation models (Physical Intelligence). [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/) [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/private-markets-update-march-2025/)\n- **Constraint:** She is an associate, and Redpoint's company pages name senior partners on every deal in her bio:\n  - Irregular: Sai Senthilkumar and Scott Raney. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/irregular/)\n  - Inferact: Satish Dharmaraj and Renee Shah, both on the early team. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/inferact-vllm/)\n- **What she would not do:** Lead or sign a check alone. Her edge is analysis (her M&A background includes semis), not sourcing. That is an inference from her title and the deal credits.\n- **What to watch:** A promotion to Principal. The growth team's associate-to-principal path runs through Bhatia's current role.\n\n## Differential insight\nNothing found from her own voice: no personal X account, podcast or talk turned up. What follows comes from reports she co-authored with Logan Bartlett and Adil Bhatia, plus Redpoint's company pages. The reports are written as \"we\", so the views are the team's, not hers alone. Web search was unavailable for this run (budget exhausted), so podcasts and X were not searched. Only direct fetches were used.\n\n- **Her bio credits are support roles.** Redpoint's company pages name senior partners as the leads on each deal in her bio. Her bio lists Inferact as a \"Seed\" even though the Inferact page credits early-team partners, which suggests the growth team worked on a deal sourced by the early team. On Irregular: \"Sai Senthilkumar and Scott Raney\" are listed as the leads. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/irregular/) [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/inferact-vllm/)\n- **Thesis on the size and timing of AI.** The team argues the AI cycle looks more like the internet than the cloud, so the generational companies may come later: \"this opportunity may resemble the internet era more than the cloud computing wave.\" (2025-03-17) [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-market-update-march-2025/)\n- **AI premium.** The team says AI companies raise bigger rounds at higher valuations: \"AI companies are consistently raising larger rounds, at higher valuations.\" (2025-03-17) [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/private-markets-update-march-2025/)\n- **Capital concentration and spending on compute.** The team notes that easy access to capital lets companies stay private and pay for compute: \"The access to capital is allowing companies to delay going public.\" (2025-03-17) [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/private-markets-update-march-2025/)\n- **Background in semis M&A.** Her bio says she advised on technology M&A that included \"semis\" before Redpoint. That is relevant to chips and compute diligence. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/lydia-day/)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For it:** She is close to three thesis-adjacent deals: Inferact (vLLM inference, a $150M launch round), Irregular (frontier-AI security, an $80M round co-led by Redpoint) and Physical Intelligence (a robotics foundation-model lab). She is also a junior at a fund that can write $10M+ and growth checks, which is exactly the \"principal or rising associate\" segment the owner wants.\n- **Against it:** She is an associate with no visible decision rights and no public voice. Redpoint was not first on any of these three deals.\n- **Why follow her:** She could become a future co-investor contact and an early signal of where Redpoint's growth team is heading in AI infrastructure. She is not a check-writer today.\n\n## Background\n- **Before Redpoint:** Two years at Qatalyst Partners, advising on technology M&A across AI, software, infrastructure, semis and fintech. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/lydia-day/)\n- **2024:** Joined Redpoint as Associate, growth. Her focus is early-growth investing in AI, infrastructure and enterprise software. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/lydia-day/)\n- **Education:** Not found on the Redpoint page.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/lydia-day/ | Firm bio, as of 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Co-authors Redpoint market updates |\n| X | nothing found | Bio links only @redpoint |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/lydia-day-03597a150/ | Linked from the firm bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. Web search was unavailable for this run. Next step: check Logan Bartlett's podcast feed and the Redpoint \"InfraRed\" events for her.\n\n## How they think\nThese are team views from reports she co-authored, not personal statements.\n- AI is a platform shift closer to the internet than to the cloud, and big winners may arrive years later. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-market-update-march-2025/)\n- AI makes up about half of Redpoint's core deal universe. AI companies grow faster and raise at a premium. The top decile carries even more premium. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/private-markets-update-march-2025/)\n- Series B and C valuations look more reasonable than in 2021–22 once growth is taken into account. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/private-markets-update-march-2025/)\n- Calls right or wrong: not yet assessable.\n\n## Read these first\n- AI Market Update (March 2025): the team's framing of AI as like the internet rather than the cloud. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-market-update-march-2025/)\n- Private Markets Update (March 2025): AI valuation premiums, concentration, and the return of Series B and C. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/private-markets-update-march-2025/)\n- Market Update: Exits & Liquidity (March 2025). [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/market-update-exits-and-liquidity/)\n- Redpoint's 2026 Market Update lists her as featured, but the page body was not retrievable. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/reports/2026-market-update)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. She has no public X account.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Inferact (vLLM) | Seed / launch round ($150M at $800M) | 2026-01 | No. a16z and Lightspeed led. Redpoint credits Dharmaraj and Shah. | Sequoia, Altimeter, ZhenFund, The House Fund, Striker, Laude, Databricks Ventures | [src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference) [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/inferact-vllm/) |\n| Irregular | Series A ($80M, ~$450M valuation reported) | 2025-09-17 | Redpoint co-led with Sequoia. Redpoint credits Senthilkumar and Raney. | Sequoia; Assaf Rappaport | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/17/irregular-raises-80-million-to-secure-frontier-ai-models/) [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/irregular/) |\n| Physical Intelligence | Series A (per Redpoint; first partnered 2024) | 2024 | Unknown. The Redpoint page names no partner. | Not confirmed for that round | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/physical-intelligence/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Inferact.** Redpoint was not first.\n  - vLLM was a 2024 Sequoia Open Source Fellowship project. Striker lists an Inception check. a16z and Lightspeed led the January 2026 launch round. (Repo rows: lauren-reeder, nikhil-suresh.) [src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference)\n  - Redpoint's own page credits early-team partners, so Day's bio credit suggests growth-team support on an early-team deal.\n- **Irregular.** Redpoint co-led the $80M round with Sequoia, alongside Wiz CEO Assaf Rappaport. This was the first round reported publicly, and earlier rounds (when the company was Pattern Labs) were not confirmed. [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/17/irregular-raises-80-million-to-secure-frontier-ai-models/)\n  - That makes it the closest to \"first institutional money\" of her three deals, but the deal is credited to Senthilkumar and Raney. [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/irregular/)\n- **Physical Intelligence.** Redpoint came in about 8 months or more after the Thrive-led $70M seed in March 2024 (Khosla, Lux, OpenAI and Sequoia joined that seed). [src](https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/)\n  - Redpoint calls its entry the \"Series A\" in 2024, which most likely matches PI's ~$400M round in November 2024. Redpoint's participation in that round is not confirmed in press I could fetch.\n  - Redpoint is reported in the $600M Series B in November 2025. [src](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/robotics-startup-physical-intelligence-valued-at-5-6-billion)\n- **How she sources:** No evidence that she originates deals. She appears to source through the growth team's data work (the market updates) and support on partner-led deals. This is an inference with no source; open question.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Redpoint Ventures | Associate, growth | 2024 | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/lydia-day/) |\n| Qatalyst Partners | Former tech M&A banker (2 yrs) | until 2024 | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/lydia-day/) |\n| Logan Bartlett | Co-author of market updates (MD, growth) | 2025 | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/private-markets-update-march-2025/) |\n| Adil Bhatia | Co-author of market updates (Principal, growth) | 2025 | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-market-update-march-2025/) |\n| Sai Senthilkumar; Scott Raney | Redpoint leads on Irregular, a deal in her bio | 2025 | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/irregular/) |\n| Satish Dharmaraj; Renee Shah | Redpoint leads on Inferact, a deal in her bio | 2026 | [src](https://www.redpoint.com/companies/inferact-vllm/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What exactly did she do on Physical Intelligence, and which Redpoint partner owns PI?\n- Has she been promoted to Principal? Watch Redpoint's announcements; none found as of 2026-11-10.\n- Which Qatalyst semis deals did she work on? This may point to chips diligence ability.\n- Does she appear on Logan Bartlett's podcast or at Redpoint InfraRed events? Not checked, because web search was unavailable.\n- Where did she study, and where is she based?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/lydia-day/\n- https://www.redpoint.com/our-people/\n- https://www.redpoint.com/companies/irregular/\n- https://www.redpoint.com/companies/inferact-vllm/\n- https://www.redpoint.com/companies/physical-intelligence/\n- https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/ai-market-update-march-2025/\n- https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/private-markets-update-march-2025/\n- https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/written/market-update-exits-and-liquidity/\n- https://www.redpoint.com/reports/2026-market-update\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/17/irregular-raises-80-million-to-secure-frontier-ai-models/\n- https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference\n- https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/\n- https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/robotics-startup-physical-intelligence-valued-at-5-6-billion\n", "person:mackenzie-morehead": "\n# Mackenzie Morehead\n\nPronouns: no source states them, so this file uses \"they\". An unverified TheOrg page uses \"he\" for a \"Mack Morehead\" at 10T Holdings, but nothing ties that page to this person.\n\n## One-line take\nCompound's in-house technical researcher. They write long, quantitative essays on scaling laws, AI compute, HBM and memory, neural-net potentials and autonomous science, and those essays are the clearest public view of what Compound will back next in AI-for-science and new-physics compute. They aren't named on any deal.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they matter:** Morehead is the research engine behind Compound's thesis-first model. The firm's writing hub lists their 2026 essays as Compound writing ([src](https://www.compound.vc/writing)). The firm bio says they focus on \"where a wide collection of emerging technologies are going\" and also spend time on incubations ([bio](https://www.compound.vc/team/mackenzie)).\n- **Core view:** robust scaling laws are rare outside LLMs and image generation. The edge in robotics and bio is a data engine that scales cheaply, plus elite cross-disciplinary ML talent ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/the-fickleness-of-scaling-laws/), 2026-02-19).\n- **Constraint:** Researcher title at a $55–115M-vehicle firm (fund sizes from the Compound firm file's Form Ds). They have no visible check authority, no deal attribution, and no promotion on record.\n- **What they'd never do:** treat a general \"discovery engine\" as a business by itself. They say Compound has \"gradually updated our views towards being more product-centric\" after watching Orbital Materials ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/what-is-quantum-advantage-actually-worth/), 2026-11-02).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Compound holds an undisclosed local-compute position, and Morehead's essay dates it.** They wrote \"Compound invested in a stealth startup focused on enabling high-performance local compute.\" The essay ties this to a thesis that cheap continual fine-tuning and LoRA adapters will push models toward local deployment ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/continual-learning-frontiers/), 2025-08-13). This is very likely the \"Local Compute Co.\" on Compound's portfolio page ([src](https://www.compound.vc/portfolio2)). The match is an inference.\n- **Memory as a moat:** \"memory being one of the few true moats in AI.\" They see a \"window for startups to commercialize\" memory systems ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/continual-learning-frontiers/)).\n- **More bullish than Dwarkesh on continual learning.** They expect systems to clear his bar \"by 2030 without any fundamentally new technology\" ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/continual-learning-frontiers/)).\n- **Talent thesis for AI-for-science:** \"Perhaps it's time for bio and robotics founders to start recruiting at Jane Street\" ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/the-fickleness-of-scaling-laws/)).\n- **Robotics paradigm bet:** Compound is \"particularly excited about\" world modeling and RL from robot rollouts, more than VLAs. Wayve and Runway are cited as the portfolio proof ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/the-fickleness-of-scaling-laws/)).\n- **An admission from inside the portfolio.** \"We (and sometimes our portco's too) have been surprised\" by the infrastructure effort that robust scaling requires ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/the-fickleness-of-scaling-laws/)).\n- **Practical rule:** start small. \"The 50M parameter class models will be pathetically useless,\" but they're cheap enough to show how scaling trajectories differ ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/the-fickleness-of-scaling-laws/)).\n- **AI chip startups:** a winning datacenter-chip startup can expect \"a 10-20% terminal market share.\" Compound has \"spoken with several companies\" that want to embed alternative-physics accelerators beside a non-Nvidia vendor's GPU or CPU, so they can use that vendor's software stack ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/compute-moonshots/), 2024-08-26).\n- **After HBM:** \"memory is no longer a commodity product.\" The opportunity they see is in new physics, such as antiferromagnetic magnonics and vertical ferroelectric memory, possibly accelerated with Orbital Materials-style AI discovery ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/what-comes-after-hbm/), 2026-09-01).\n- **Quantum:** they're skeptical. They see materials and drug applications as roughly single-digit-billion revenue pools against $25–30B raised. They cite Oratomic raising $775M in its first year to build a cryptography-breaking ASIC ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/what-is-quantum-advantage-actually-worth/), 2026-11-02).\n- **Autonomous science:** \"we at Compound will take the under\" on the 2050 Nobel Turing Grand Challenge ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/autonomous-science-part-i-everythings-an-api-away/), 2025-02-04).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The subject matter is high-fit: pre-training scaling, AI chips and compute moonshots, HBM successors, continual learning and memory, and robotics world models. Morehead is a strong read for the technical diligence the owner needs on new labs and chips. As a co-investor or deal source, though, the fit is weak. They hold a Researcher title, are named on no deals, have no promotion on record, and Compound writes small checks. The deals that matter, all firm-level: the stealth local-compute startup, Orbital Materials (AI materials and data-center hardware), Achira (hybrid NNP drug discovery), and Wayve and Runway (world models). **Use:** email them about the compute-moonshot and post-HBM theses. Ask what Compound's incubations are and whether they are tied to Compound Explorer I (link is an inference from the firm file).\n\n## Background\n- Before or alongside Compound: the personal blog starts 2022-08-08 with macro and crypto pieces (\"Money & Inflation\", \"Cryptonetworks as Sovereign Digital Economies\"), followed by macro outlooks in 2023 ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/page/2/)). The macro and markets bent fits a public-markets background, but no source confirms prior employers.\n- 2023-11-21: published a long series, \"The Century of Biology\" ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/introduction/)).\n- By 2024-08-08: writing as \"we at Compound\" ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/untitled-2/)). The April 2024 attention essay doesn't mention Compound ([src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/is-attention-all-you-need/)). The start date isn't confirmed.\n- Education: not found.\n- Identity lead: TheOrg lists a \"Mack Morehead,\" Associate at 10T Holdings and formerly on SkyBridge Capital's investment team (unverified) ([src](https://theorg.com/org/10t-holdings/org-chart/mack-morehead)). Logged as `needs-id`; not assumed to be the same person.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.mackenziemorehead.com | Ghost blog; about 1 long essay a month in 2026 (as of 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://mmorehead.substack.com | \"Things to Chew On,\" a curated reading digest; subscriber count not shown |\n| X | https://x.com/mackenziejem | follower count not retrieved (X returned 402) |\n| LinkedIn | not found | the firm page's link is a placeholder |\n| GitHub | not found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Scaling needs preconditions.** Diverse task-relevant data, sound algorithms and infrastructure all have to be in place. Most technical domains are data-poor, so the data engine is the moat.\n- **Robotics:** on-embodiment field data is stubbornly hard to shortcut. Bets on world models and RL from rollouts over VLAs.\n- **Compute:** digital scaling gets more expensive with each step, so new-physics startups (photonics, thermodynamic, in-memory, neuromorphic) have the most convexity. Edge and niche HPC are easier entry points than the datacenter.\n- **Business-model realism:** they've moved from platforms toward product-centric discovery companies (Orbital lesson). They're skeptical of quantum TAMs.\n- **Timelines:** bullish. Continual learning by about 2030 with existing tools, and autonomous science before 2050.\n- **Calls:** \"Is Attention All You Need?\" (2024-04) predicted SOTA models won't stay pure full self-attention. Hybrid attention architectures have spread since, but that's a general observation; no scoring source was found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [The Fickleness of Scaling Laws](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/the-fickleness-of-scaling-laws/) (2026-02): the core thesis on data engines across bio, robotics and world models.\n2. [Continual Learning Frontiers](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/continual-learning-frontiers/) (2025-08): memory as a moat; discloses the stealth local-compute investment.\n3. [Compute Moonshots](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/compute-moonshots/) (2024-08): the new-physics AI chip map and its market-share math.\n4. [What Comes After HBM](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/what-comes-after-hbm/) (2026-09): memory-supercycle view and exotic memory bets.\n5. [What Is Quantum Advantage Actually Worth?](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/what-is-quantum-advantage-actually-worth/) (2026-11): the product-centric update.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot retrieved; X blocked fetching. The essays link to and cite Michael Dempsey, Eric Jang, Jim Fan, Mo AlQuraishi, Dwarkesh Patel, SemiAnalysis and Epoch.\n\n## Portfolio\nThese are firm-level holdings that Morehead discloses or works with. Morehead is not named as the deal person on any of them.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Stealth high-performance local-compute startup (likely \"Local Compute Co.\") | unknown | by 2025-08-13 | unknown | unknown | [src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/continual-learning-frontiers/) |\n| Orbital Materials | seed 2023 (firm file); Morehead says \"our portfolio company\" | 2026-09-01 mention | no | see firm file | [src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/what-comes-after-hbm/) |\n| Achira | seed (firm file) | 2026-02-19 mention | no | see firm file | [src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/the-fickleness-of-scaling-laws/) |\n| Wayve, Runway | see firm file | — | — | — | [src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/the-fickleness-of-scaling-laws/) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. No source names Morehead on a deal, and no incubation is publicly named. The closest thing to a timing signal is the local-compute disclosure, which shows the position predates August 2025.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Compound | Researcher; also works on incubations | by 2024-08 | [bio](https://www.compound.vc/team/mackenzie); [src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/untitled-2/) |\n| Michael Dempsey | Compound MP; Morehead's essays link to his posts | by 2024-08 | [src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/the-fickleness-of-scaling-laws/) |\n| Orbital Materials | works with the portfolio company | by 2026 | [src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/what-is-quantum-advantage-actually-worth/) |\n| Achira | works with the portfolio company | by 2026 | [src](https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/what-is-quantum-advantage-actually-worth/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which incubations do they run, and are they linked to Compound Explorer I ($35M, 2026-08)?\n- What is the stealth local-compute company?\n- When did they join Compound, and what did they do before? Are they the same person as \"Mack Morehead\" of 10T or SkyBridge?\n- Has Compound backed any of the new-physics compute companies the essays name (Neurophos, Extropic, Normal Computing)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.compound.vc/team/mackenzie ; https://www.compound.vc/writing ; https://www.compound.vc/portfolio2\n- https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/ and /page/2/\n- https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/the-fickleness-of-scaling-laws/\n- https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/continual-learning-frontiers/\n- https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/compute-moonshots/\n- https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/what-comes-after-hbm/\n- https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/what-is-quantum-advantage-actually-worth/\n- https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/autonomous-science-part-i-everythings-an-api-away/\n- https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/untitled-2/ (Neural Net Potentials)\n- https://www.mackenziemorehead.com/is-attention-all-you-need/\n- https://mmorehead.substack.com/about\n- https://theorg.com/org/10t-holdings/org-chart/mack-morehead (aggregator; identity unconfirmed)\n", "person:madison-faulkner": "\n# Madison Faulkner\n\n## One-line take\nNEA's fastest-rising AI-infra partner (Principal 2024, Partner 2025-06). She wrote the code-modernization thesis, led NEA into Factory, and then left NEA in 2026-05 to become Factory's Head of Strategy. Lila Tretikov took her board seat ([src](https://techintelpro.com/news/ai/enterprise-ai/factory-adds-madison-faulkner-as-strategy-head-lila-tretikov-to-board)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she won deals:** an ex-data-science operator (Facebook/FAIR, Greycroft, Thrasio) who diligenced like a buyer: \"every deal through the lens of my Head of Data and AI peers\" ([src](https://www.nea.com/team/madison-faulkner)). She is named on World Labs' A, Ceramic's seed, Factory's B, Foresight's seed (board) and Namespace's A (board).\n- **What constrained her:** the pull back to building. She told Factory \"backing them was never going to be enough, I wanted to help build it\" ([src](https://techintelpro.com/news/ai/enterprise-ai/factory-adds-madison-faulkner-as-strategy-head-lila-tretikov-to-board)).\n- **What she'd never back (by her writing):** SWE-benchmark chasers and pure code-generation tools, which she sees incumbents commoditizing ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/how-ai-will-modernize-enterprise-code)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Thesis, then lead, then job, in 15 months.** In 2025-02 she wrote \"great opportunity is not beating the latest benchmark\" (with Maanasi Garg) ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/how-ai-will-modernize-enterprise-code)). In 2025-09 NEA co-led Factory's $50M B with her on the board ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/factory-the-platform-for-agent-native-development)). Factory then raised a $150M C at $1.5B (2026-04) and joined it in 2026-05, before the $200M round at $5B (2026-09-15) ([src](https://factory.ai/news/5-billion-valuation)). She joined between the C and the $5B round.\n- **NEA hasn't taken her off its site.** Her NEA page still says \"Venture Advisor\", and the CMS still shows her as board director on Namespace and Foresight and observer on Datafold ([NEA CMS](https://www.nea.com/portfolio)). Whether she still holds those seats is unconfirmed.\n- Tretikov on her departure: \"She's going to go build the thing we kept talking about\" ([src](https://techintelpro.com/news/ai/enterprise-ai/factory-adds-madison-faulkner-as-strategy-head-lila-tretikov-to-board)).\n- **Golden Analytics came from NEA's own bench.** Founder François Ajenstat (ex-Tableau) had worked with NEA \"through his role as an NEA Venture Advisor\" before NEA backed his company (2026 seed; Faulkner post) ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/golden-analytics-data-deserves-better)).\n- Her Factory post leaned on METR's finding that Cursor-style tools \"slowed down developers by 19%\" to argue against IDE-first products ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/factory-the-platform-for-agent-native-development)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh as a node, not as a check-writer. She now runs strategy at a $5B coding-agent company that sits on NEA's, Sequoia's and Khosla's cap tables, and she co-wrote NEA's neolab memo. As a former NEA partner, her NEA-era deals (World Labs, Factory, Ceramic, Namespace, Foresight) count toward NEA's book via the rels row with `until`.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford University, BS in Management Science and Engineering ([src](https://www.nea.com/team/madison-faulkner)).\n- Facebook: data science roles on the ad auction and deep learning with FAIR ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/new-enterprise-associates-announces-promotion-of-madison-and-mustafa-to-partner-1)).\n- Greycroft: Head of Data Science. Thrasio: Head of Data Science and ML (same).\n- Costanoa Ventures: Vice President (worked with Delphina, Probabl, Mindtrip, Noteable, Rafay, Fixify) ([src](https://www.nea.com/team/madison-faulkner)).\n- NEA: Principal 2024; Partner 2025-06-05; left 2026-05 for Factory ([src](https://techintelpro.com/news/ai/enterprise-ai/factory-adds-madison-faulkner-as-strategy-head-lila-tretikov-to-board); Business Wire original dated 2026-05-26 to 05-28 across reprints).\n- Grew up in Colorado; rodeo performer and professional cliff diver (NEA Q&A).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.nea.com/team/madison-faulkner | stale; still lists her as NEA Venture Advisor |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.nea.com/blog | 9 NEA posts, 2024-12 to 2026-06 |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/madisonhfaulkner/ | listed on NEA page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- AI Infra Summit speaker page ([src](https://www.ai-infra-summit.com/speakers/madison-faulkner)); not read.\n- TechCrunch author page ([src](https://techcrunch.com/author/madison-faulkner)); not read.\n\n## How they think\n- Code generation is commoditizing. The value is in \"enterprise abstraction\": migration, continuous review and testing, and human-agent collaboration ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/how-ai-will-modernize-enterprise-code)).\n- Legacy BI can't be fixed by bolting on an LLM ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/golden-analytics-data-deserves-better)).\n- Agents need computers, not CI pipelines (Namespace post) ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/namespace-cicd-is-dead-agents-need-computers)).\n\n## Read these first\n- \"Beyond Beating the SWE-Bench: How AI Will Modernize Enterprise Code\" (2025-02-27) ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/how-ai-will-modernize-enterprise-code)).\n- Factory Series B post (2025-09-25) ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/factory-the-platform-for-agent-native-development)).\n- \"The AI Neolab Wild West\" (2026-06-17, co-author) ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/the-ai-neolab-wild-west)).\n- Golden Analytics post (2026-04-07) ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/golden-analytics-data-deserves-better)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nDeal rows already in `data/rows/new-enterprise-associates-portfolio-deals.csv` (World Labs A, Backflip, Ceramic seed, Foresight seed, Factory B, Namespace A, Golden Analytics seed).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| World Labs | Series A (~$100M) | 2024-08 | NEA lead (CMS team) | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/14/nea-led-a-100m-round-into-fei-fei-lis-new-ai-startup-now-valued-at-over-1b) |\n| Backflip AI | Undisclosed ($30M) | 2024-12-18 | with a16z; observer | | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/neas-investment-in-backflip-the-future-of-ai-powered-3d-design) |\n| Ceramic | Seed | 2025-03-05 | NEA lead | IBM, Samsung Next | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/ceramic-infrastructure-for-every-ai-native-company) |\n| Foresight | Seed ($5.5M) | 2025-06-26 | NEA lead; board | | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/foresight-the-data-ai-revolution-for-the-private-markets) |\n| Factory | Series B ($50M at $300M) | 2025-09-25 | NEA lead; board until 2026-05 | Sequoia, J.P. Morgan, NVIDIA | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/factory-the-platform-for-agent-native-development) |\n| Namespace | Series A | 2026-03-23 | NEA lead; board | | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/namespace-cicd-is-dead-agents-need-computers) |\n| Golden Analytics | Seed | 2026 | NEA (post author) | | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/golden-analytics-data-deserves-better) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Factory:** covered above. Her board seat passed to Tretikov when she joined the company. Factory CEO Matan Grinberg: \"joining the team full-time was the only logical next step\" ([src](https://techintelpro.com/news/ai/enterprise-ai/factory-adds-madison-faulkner-as-strategy-head-lila-tretikov-to-board)).\n- **Foresight:** founder Jason Miller \"has done this before at Greycroft\", where Faulkner was Head of Data Science. A prior working relationship is plausible but unconfirmed ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/foresight-the-data-ai-revolution-for-the-private-markets)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Factory | at: Head of Strategy | 2026-05 | [src](https://techintelpro.com/news/ai/enterprise-ai/factory-adds-madison-faulkner-as-strategy-head-lila-tretikov-to-board) |\n| New Enterprise Associates | at: Principal then Partner (now listed as Venture Advisor) | 2024 to 2026-05 | [src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/new-enterprise-associates-announces-promotion-of-madison-and-mustafa-to-partner-1) |\n| Costanoa Ventures | at: Vice President | before 2024 | [src](https://www.nea.com/team/madison-faulkner) |\n| Greycroft | at: Head of Data Science | before Costanoa | same |\n| Lila Tretikov | NEA partner; took her Factory board seat | 2026-05 | [src](https://techintelpro.com/news/ai/enterprise-ai/factory-adds-madison-faulkner-as-strategy-head-lila-tretikov-to-board) |\n| Matan Grinberg | Factory CEO; her new boss | 2026-05 | same |\n\n## Open questions\n- Does she keep NEA board seats (Namespace, Foresight) as Venture Advisor?\n- Who is Maanasi Garg (her 2025 co-author; NEA role unknown)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.nea.com/team/madison-faulkner\n- https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/new-enterprise-associates-announces-promotion-of-madison-and-mustafa-to-partner-1\n- https://techintelpro.com/news/ai/enterprise-ai/factory-adds-madison-faulkner-as-strategy-head-lila-tretikov-to-board (Business Wire reprint)\n- https://factory.ai/news/5-billion-valuation\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/how-ai-will-modernize-enterprise-code\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/factory-the-platform-for-agent-native-development\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/golden-analytics-data-deserves-better\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/foresight-the-data-ai-revolution-for-the-private-markets\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/namespace-cicd-is-dead-agents-need-computers\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/the-ai-neolab-wild-west\n", "person:malika-aubakirova": "\n# Malika Aubakirova\n\n## One-line take\nA data-heavy a16z AI Infra investor who co-signed the a16z-led $200M Mirendil seed and led the authorship of the 100-trillion-token OpenRouter study. Their current status is the open question: they may have left to run product at a stealth neolab.\n\n## Crux\n- **Edge:** They do the analytical work behind a16z's AI Infra team. The OpenRouter paper credits them with \"full preparation of the paper\", which gives them a usage-level view of which models and providers actually win ([src](https://openrouter.ai/state-of-ai)).\n- **Constraint:** On the one frontier-lab deal we can find (Mirendil), they are the second byline under Matt Bornstein. The deal credit is probably Bornstein's, and nothing shows them leading a deal alone.\n- **Status:** If the self-reported move to a stealth neolab is real, they become a high-signal operator and angel lead rather than a check writer.\n- **What they'd never do:** unknown. No primary-source statement was found.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Main author of the a16z x OpenRouter 100T-token study.** The paper names them \"the lead author\", responsible for experiment design, implementation and analysis. Midha is credited with framing ([src](https://openrouter.ai/state-of-ai), 2025-12). The a16z version is bylined Aubakirova and Midha ([src](https://a16z.com/state-of-ai/), 2025-12-04). This means they, not the GP, ran the data work.\n- **Open-weight share has plateaued, by their data.** The paper says open-weight usage stabilises \"roughly 30%\" of tokens ([src](https://openrouter.ai/state-of-ai)). That matters for open-model labs like Mirendil, which a16z backed six months later.\n- **Roleplay leads open-model usage.** The paper says \"Over half of open source model usage was for roleplay and storytelling\" ([src](https://openrouter.ai/state-of-ai)). This is a contrarian read on what open models are actually used for.\n- **Agentic inference is the growth vector.** The paper's heading is \"Agentic Inference Is the New Default\" ([src](https://openrouter.ai/state-of-ai)).\n- **Mirendil thesis (co-signed with Bornstein).** The bet is open frontier models plus a \"lab-grade research platform\" for ordinary engineers. The talent hook is that Harsh Mehta \"built the first version of the Anthropic autoresearch platform\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirendil/), 2026-06-24).\n- **Conflicting status.** A self-reported Leland coaching profile, captured earlier by the a16z roster pass, lists a16z from 2024 to May 2026, then Head of Product at a stealth AI neolab ([src, now 404](https://www.joinleland.com/coach/malika-a-1/venture-capital)). Yet their name is on a16z's 2026-06-24 Mirendil post. Two readings fit:\n  - They left in May 2026 and the post went out after they left.\n  - The Leland dates are wrong.\n\n  Neither the author page nor the team page shows them on 2026-11-10 ([src](https://a16z.com/author/malika-aubakirova/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High**, with caveats. Both of their named items sit squarely in the thesis:\n- Mirendil, a new frontier lab with a $200M seed\n- The usage study, which is market intelligence on model and inference share\n\nIf they now run product at a stealth neolab, they are a high-value talent node to reach before that lab raises. As an investor, they are not a $10M+ decision-maker on any sourced evidence. Bornstein is the a16z deal lead.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford MBA (from the task context; not confirmed in this pass).\n- a16z AI Infra, 2024 onward (self-reported Leland profile, now 404, unconfirmed).\n- Partner title on the a16z author page, per an earlier roster capture ([src](https://a16z.com/author/malika-aubakirova)). The page no longer renders a bio, so this could not be re-confirmed.\n- Possibly Head of Product at a stealth neolab from May 2026 (self-reported, unconfirmed). The lab's name is unknown.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/malika-aubakirova/ | Author page; no bio rendered 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found (not verified) | |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found (not fetched) | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search budget was exhausted in this run, so podcast and talk discovery was not done.\n\n## How they think\n- **Measure actual usage, not benchmarks.** The OpenRouter study argues that model choice tracks personality and \"breakthrough moments\" more than leaderboard rank ([src](https://a16z.com/state-of-ai/)).\n- **Open frontier models need an independent home.** The Mirendil post says there \"should be an independent company focused on this problem\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirendil/)).\n- **Calls right or wrong:** too early to judge.\n\n## Read these first\n1. State of AI: 100T Token Study with OpenRouter ([a16z](https://a16z.com/state-of-ai/), [full paper](https://openrouter.ai/state-of-ai)). Their main piece of analysis.\n2. Investing in Mirendil ([a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirendil/)). Their only sourced deal memo.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X was not reachable (HTTP 402).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mirendil | Seed ($200M) | 2026-06-24 | yes (a16z co-lead with Kleiner Perkins); Aubakirova is second byline to Matt Bornstein | Kleiner Perkins (co-lead, Mamoon Hamid); NVIDIA came in after the seed | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirendil/), [Mirendil](https://mirendil.com/news/announcing-mirendil) |\n| OpenRouter (research partnership, not a deal) | n/a | 2025-12-04 | n/a | Anjney Midha (a16z) | [src](https://a16z.com/state-of-ai/) |\n\n**Timing.** Mirendil names only a16z and Kleiner as seed leads, so a16z was in at the first priced institutional round. NVIDIA \"followed\" ([src](https://mirendil.com/news/announcing-mirendil)). Whether Aubakirova personally sourced it is unknown.\n\n## Deal stories\n**Mirendil.** The a16z post, bylined Bornstein and Aubakirova, centres the bet on four people:\n- Neyshabur, who did AI for science at Google and Anthropic\n- Mehta, who built Anthropic's autoresearch platform\n- Salehian, from xAI's ML engineering team\n- Rezaei, a 23-year-old MIT olympiad medalist\n\n([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirendil/)). The intro path is not disclosed. Kleiner's post traces the Neyshabur and Mehta pairing to a cold email at Google (see `mamoon-hamid` rows).\n\n**How they source.** No primary statement was found. The only signal is indirect: the OpenRouter partnership gives the a16z infra team proprietary usage data, a plausible way to spot models and teams early. That is inference, not sourced.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Matt Bornstein (a16z) | Co-author, Mirendil seed post | 2026-06 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirendil/) |\n| Anjney Midha (a16z) | Co-author, OpenRouter study | 2025-12 | [src](https://a16z.com/state-of-ai/) |\n| Alex Atallah (OpenRouter) | Co-author, OpenRouter study | 2025-12 | [src](https://openrouter.ai/state-of-ai) |\n| Chris Clark, Justin Summerville (OpenRouter) | Co-authors | 2025-12 | [src](https://openrouter.ai/state-of-ai) |\n| Mirendil founders (Neyshabur, Mehta, Salehian, Rezaei) | Portfolio (a16z) | 2026-06 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirendil/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did they leave a16z? If so, when, and which stealth neolab are they at? The Leland profile is now 404. Check LinkedIn and X directly.\n- Is the Partner title real, and when was it granted?\n- Did they source Mirendil, or only co-author the memo?\n- Are there other a16z investing posts with their byline? The search page returned none.\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirendil/\n- https://a16z.com/state-of-ai/\n- https://openrouter.ai/state-of-ai\n- https://mirendil.com/news/announcing-mirendil\n- https://a16z.com/author/malika-aubakirova/\n- https://www.joinleland.com/coach/malika-a-1/venture-capital (404 on 2026-11-10; earlier capture via the a16z roster pass)\n", "person:mamoon-hamid": "\n# Mamoon Hamid\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the person who decides Kleiner's frontier-lab bets in 2026: Mirendil (co-led $200M seed), Etched, Flapping Airplanes and Discovery Loop all sit on his page. In 2023 he said on the record that software companies don't need mega rounds, so watch how he prices labs differently from software.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he rebuilt Kleiner into a small partnership that is measured like a sales team. Kleiner tracks whether it saw every seed, A and B done by 40–50 peer firms each Monday. It targets 60–70% coverage and a 100% win rate on deals it wants. About 80% of investments come through founder referrals ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf), Venture Unlocked, 2023-11-01). His first Kleiner deal, leading Figma's $25M Series B, is now roughly 90x ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/01/31/inside-vc-firm-kleiner-perkins-turnaround-mamoon-hamid/), 2026-01-31).\n- **Constraint / tension:** in Nov 2023 he called the growth mega-round asset class something that \"just didn't totally need to exist\" and asked why a software business would raise \"half a billion, a billion dollars\" ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)). By 2026 he was co-leading a $200M seed (Mirendil), joining Etched's $700M round at $21B, and is listed on Flapping Airplanes, which raised a $1B Series A ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/), [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/)). The read: his capital discipline applies to software, and labs and chips are a separate bucket for him. No source has him explaining that switch yet.\n- **What he'd never do (sourced):** geographic funds, biotech and a standalone growth practice were cut in the 2017–18 reboot (\"We don't have geographical funds\") ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)). He also won't make judgment an OKR, because it isn't real-time enough.\n- **Edge in your lane:** he started as a Xilinx FPGA engineer and joined USVP for its semiconductor reputation ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)). His page carries AI chips and compute: Alif (2019), Akeana (2021), Together.ai (2023), Nexthop AI (2025) and Etched (2026) ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Coverage OKR on peers' deals.** Each Monday the team marks every seed, A and B announced by a 40–50 firm peer set as seen or not seen. The coverage target rose from 60% to 65%, peaking near 70%, and is split by sector: \"we track religiously all series As that get done by our peer set\" ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf), 2023-11-01).\n- **Win-rate OKR, with every loss post-mortemed:** \"having a hundred percent win rate on an investment that we want to do\" ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)).\n- **Admitted weakness: losing deals by under-selling.** \"We may come across as too pragmatic and substantial but not excited enough.\" He half-seriously asks whether Kleiner should bake cookies to win deals ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)).\n- **Five-year anti-portfolio review by partner failure mode:** \"We do a five-year look back on judgement.\" Each partner's failure mode is named (people, pricing, over-weighting go-to-market, false positives on good data) ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)).\n- **Sourcing:** \"about 80% of all investments that we do are through founder referrals.\" That includes founders Kleiner met and did not back ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)). Glean is the clean example: Kleiner met Arvind Jain in late 2018 through Coda founder Shishir Mehrotra ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/case-study/glean/)), and Coda is a 2014 company on Hamid's own page ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/)).\n- **Kleiner was not his first choice, and he was not Kleiner's.** TechCrunch reported that Chamath Palihapitiya, his Social Capital co-founder, had declined John Doerr's invitation to lead Kleiner. Doerr's pitch was quoted as \"I want to talk about Kleiner 3.0\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/08/03/mamood-hamid-is-reportedly-heading-from-social-capital-to-kleiner-perkins/), 2017-08-03). Hamid credits Ted Schlein: \"he was really primarily responsible for recruiting me\" ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)). Fortune adds \"Schlein had begun courting Hamid for the top job\" ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/01/31/inside-vc-firm-kleiner-perkins-turnaround-mamoon-hamid/)).\n- **Hiring philosophy: grow your own.** Venture is a craft \"best learned through practice.\" Bucky Moore joined in Jan 2018 as a principal and \"very quickly became a partner.\" Annie Case and Josh Coyne went from associate to partner ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)). This matters for the owner's principal-to-partner screen: Kleiner promotes internally.\n- **Scout fund, 2018:** \"In 2018, Hamid and Fushman instituted a new scout fund\" to cut decision time from weeks to days ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/01/31/inside-vc-firm-kleiner-perkins-turnaround-mamoon-hamid/)).\n- **Etched entry came late and through a conference.** \"Rob and I happened to overlap in Paris for the RAISE Summit\" ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/etched/), 2026-08-18; quote via fetch summary, low confidence). He says he spent \"a lot of time with the team over the past few months\" before the $21B round ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/etched/)).\n- **AI layer view (2024):** he is skeptical of the middle layer, saying there is \"a lot of time being spent on a lot of the middle layer\", and believes \"you're overfunding some companies\" ([src](https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-kleiner-perkins-mamoon-hamid-on-investing-lessons-from-leading-rounds-in-figma-slack-and-rippling--lessons-building-a-generational-defining-firm-with-kleiner-perkins--ai-where-value-accrues-startups-vs-incumbents--scaling-laws), 20VC 2024-10-21; AI-generated transcript, first 4 minutes only).\n- **Mentor:** he names \"my mentor, Erwin Fetterman\" in the transcript. That is likely Irwin Federman of USVP, but it is unconfirmed, so it is recorded as needs-id ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is the Kleiner name on the 2026 new-lab rounds you target. The timing splits two ways:\n- **First or co-first:** Glean (pre-founding round, incubated in Kleiner's office) ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/case-study/glean/)) and Mirendil (co-led the $200M seed with a16z, \"from day one\") ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/mirendil-building-the-system-that-builds-systems/), [src](https://mirendil.com/news/announcing-mirendil)).\n- **Late or participant:**\n  - Etched: Primary seeded in 2023, and Jane Street led the $700M round in 2026 ([src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one)).\n  - Discovery Loop: Radical and Khosla led ([src](https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop)).\n  - Flapping Airplanes: Index and Lux co-led the A. No source names a Kleiner partner on the deal, and Kleiner's seed entry is disputed in this repo ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/)).\n- **Chips and compute:** Alif, Akeana, Nexthop AI and Together.ai ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/)).\n- **AI-native automation (Serval-like):** Glean, Moveworks, Rogo, Reevo and Nooks.\n\nHe is useful as a co-investor signal and as a lab-round anchor, not as an LP prospect.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Frankfurt, Germany. He has also lived in Lahore, West Lafayette, Boston and the Bay Area, and speaks German, Urdu, Hindi, Punjabi and English. His first job was as a data analyst at Deutsche Bank ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/)).\n- He earned a BS in ECE from Purdue, then an MS from Stanford and an MBA from Harvard Business School. He applied only to HBS, modeling himself on John Doerr ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/), [src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)).\n- 1997: Xilinx, at age 19, as a support engineer helping Cisco and HP design in FPGAs. He also did technical diligence for Xilinx's venture arm ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)).\n- 2005: joined USVP as an associate, chosen for its semiconductor reputation, and became a partner. Box (2007) dates from this period ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf), [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/)).\n- 2011: co-founded Social Capital with Chamath Palihapitiya and Ted Maidenberg and led its venture practice. He cites Slack and Yammer as the deals that \"put us on the map\" ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_Capital_(venture_capital_firm)), [src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)).\n- 2017-08: moved to Kleiner Perkins ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/08/03/mamood-hamid-is-reportedly-heading-from-social-capital-to-kleiner-perkins/)). With Ilya Fushman he ran the reboot: a smaller partnership (five partners versus ten before he joined, per Fortune), growth spun out as Bond, and China funds dropped ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/01/31/inside-vc-firm-kleiner-perkins-turnaround-mamoon-hamid/), [src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)).\n- Title conflict: Kleiner's site says \"Partner\". The 2017 announcement said general partner and managing member. Form Ds list Hamid and Fushman as the only Executive Officers ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2115014/000211501426000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/) | Firm page with full portfolio and years; verified 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | [Kleiner Perspectives](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/) (bylined posts: Etched, Mirendil, Together, Glean, Rippling, Slack); [medium.com/@mamoonha](https://medium.com/@mamoonha) | The Medium account surfaced in search, but the fetch returned 403, so it is unverified |\n| X | [x.com/mamoonha](https://x.com/mamoonha) | Linked from the KP page; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | [linkedin.com/in/mamoonha](https://www.linkedin.com/in/mamoonha) | Linked from the KP page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none; guest only (see below) | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-07: Bloomberg Masters in Business (Barry Ritholtz), \"Mamoon Hamid, Kleiner Perkins on AI Investing\". [Transcript](https://ritholtz.com/2026/07/transcript-mamoon-hamid/) blocked (403), not read. **Most important unread source.**\n- 2026-01-31: Fortune, Allie Garfinkle, Kleiner turnaround profile. Highlights: \"I think you just have to be paranoid\"; Figma Series B ~90x; scout fund ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/01/31/inside-vc-firm-kleiner-perkins-turnaround-mamoon-hamid/)).\n- 2025-07-09: Uncapped with Jack Altman #16. Chapters include assessing founders, Kleiner's strategy, and family and faith ([listing](https://metacast.app/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/jR35iX38/uncapped-16-mamoon-hamid-from-kleiner-perkins/DRIXi0fX)). Transcript not read.\n- 2024-10-21: 20VC: \"I love products that create markets,\" says he is skeptical of the AI middle layer, and argues the scarce professions to augment are doctors, lawyers and developers ([src](https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-kleiner-perkins-mamoon-hamid-on-investing-lessons-from-leading-rounds-in-figma-slack-and-rippling--lessons-building-a-generational-defining-firm-with-kleiner-perkins--ai-where-value-accrues-startups-vs-incumbents--scaling-laws)).\n- 2023-11-01: Venture Unlocked (Samir Kaji). The full transcript (organized by Sajith Pai) is the primary source for the OKRs, referral share and failure-mode review ([episode](https://ventureunlocked.substack.com/p/going-back-to-the-foundation-of-kleiner), [transcript](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)).\n- 2023 (re-released 2024-11): Turpentine VC E63, with Ilya Fushman, on continuing Kleiner's legacy ([listing](https://metacast.app/podcast/turpentine-vc-venture-capital-and-investing/LUJoKypp/e63-how-mamoon-hamid-and-ilya-fushman-continued-kleiner-perkins-legacy/jbnnmApf)). Not read.\n- 2019-05-13: 20VC on the $600M \"Back To The Future\" fund and price sensitivity at Series A ([src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-kleiner-perkins-mamoon-hamid-d4f)). Show notes only.\n\n## How they think\n- **Products that create markets.** He likes companies that \"get to create the playing field\" (Slack, Figma) ([src](https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-kleiner-perkins-mamoon-hamid-on-investing-lessons-from-leading-rounds-in-figma-slack-and-rippling--lessons-building-a-generational-defining-firm-with-kleiner-perkins--ai-where-value-accrues-startups-vs-incumbents--scaling-laws)). His career lesson, credited to his mentor, is to \"play our game, play on our turf\" ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)).\n- **Focus over platform.** Firms should either be platforms or small, same-office partnerships, and Kleiner is the latter ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)).\n- **Contrarian (2023):** the Tiger/Coatue growth asset class \"just didn't totally need to exist\", and software doesn't need $500M–$1B ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)).\n- **2026 thesis on inference:** inference is becoming the most important AI infrastructure market, and the core metrics are tokens per dollar and per watt (Etched post, [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/etched/); summarized, not verbatim).\n- **2026 thesis on automated AI R&D:** Mirendil is a lab built around AI doing AI research ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/mirendil-building-the-system-that-builds-systems/)).\n- **Calls right:**\n  - Figma: first Kleiner deal, roughly 90x.\n  - Slack: first three rounds after the 2014 relaunch ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/slack/)).\n  - Glean, Rippling.\n- **Calls wrong:** none documented. He admits Kleiner loses deals by under-selling.\n\n## Read these first\n1. The Venture Unlocked transcript (2023), which lays out the whole operating system ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)).\n2. Fortune, \"Kleiner Perkins was written off...\" (2026-01-31), the outside view of the turnaround ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/01/31/inside-vc-firm-kleiner-perkins-turnaround-mamoon-hamid/)).\n3. Kleiner's Etched post (2026-08-18), his chip and inference thesis written in an engineer's voice ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/etched/)).\n4. Kleiner's Mirendil post (2026-06-24), his lab thesis ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/mirendil-building-the-system-that-builds-systems/)).\n5. The Masters in Business transcript (2026-07), the most recent long-form interview; not yet read ([src](https://ritholtz.com/2026/07/transcript-mamoon-hamid/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked: X could not be fetched this pass. His handle is @mamoonha ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/)).\n\n## Portfolio\nYears and stages come from his KP page ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/)) unless another source is given.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Box | early (USVP era) | 2007 | n/s | | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/) |\n| Slack | first three rounds post-relaunch (Social Capital) | 2014 | yes | John Doerr, Ilya Fushman (other firms then) | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/slack/) |\n| Coda | early | 2014 | n/s | | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/) |\n| Figma | Series B $25M | 2017 (KP page) | yes, first KP deal | | [src](https://fortune.com/2026/01/31/inside-vc-firm-kleiner-perkins-turnaround-mamoon-hamid/) |\n| Glean | Series A | 2019 | yes (KP case study) | Lightspeed (Ravi Mhatre) per repo pattern file | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/case-study/glean/) |\n| Rippling | Series A | 2019 | yes | Ilya Fushman | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/rippling/) |\n| Alif (AI embedded chips) | early | 2019 | n/s | | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/) |\n| Akeana (RISC-V) | early | 2021 | n/s | | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/) |\n| Together.ai | Series A $102.5M (KP led; Moore took the board seat) | 2023-11 | KP led | | [src](https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a) |\n| Harvey | early | 2023 | n/s | | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/) |\n| Anthropic | growth | 2025 | no | | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/) |\n| Nexthop AI | early | 2025 | n/s | | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/) |\n| Parallel Web Systems | Series A (board) | 2025-11 | co-lead with Index | Index, Khosla, First Round | [src](https://parallel.ai/blog/series-a) |\n| Rogo | Series D | 2026-04-29 | yes | Sequoia, Thrive, Khosla | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/rogo-the-ai-platform-for-global-finance/) |\n| Mirendil | Seed $200M | 2026-06-24 | co-lead | a16z | [src](https://mirendil.com/news/announcing-mirendil) |\n| Discovery Loop | initial round | 2026-08-05 | no | Radical, Khosla (leads), Lightspeed, Doerr Capital, Alphabet | [src](https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop) |\n| Etched | $700M at $21B | 2026-08-18 | no | Jane Street (lead) | [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Series A $1B (KP participant; seed entry disputed) | 2026-10-21 | no | Index, Lux (co-leads) | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/) |\n| Instinct (personal agent) | early | 2026 | n/s | | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Figma (first Kleiner deal).** He led the $25M Series B. Dylan Field recalled: \"Mamoon treated it like it was the most obvious thing\" ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/01/31/inside-vc-firm-kleiner-perkins-turnaround-mamoon-hamid/)). In his own words: \"Figma actually turns out it was my first investment when I got to KP\" ([src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf)).\n- **Glean.** Shishir Mehrotra (Coda, a portfolio founder) introduced Arvind Jain in late 2018. The team was incubated in Kleiner's office, and Kleiner then led the 2019 A ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/case-study/glean/)). It is the template for his referral loop.\n- **Etched.** He met Rob Wachen at the RAISE Summit in Paris and spent a few months with the team. He bonded with them over low-voltage transistor design, which goes back to his EE thesis ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/etched/)). Kleiner entered at $21B, about 40 months after Primary's seed (repo pattern file).\n- **Mirendil.** Kleiner co-led the seed with a16z. Behnam Neyshabur and Harsh Mehta met at Google through a cold email years before ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/mirendil-building-the-system-that-builds-systems/)). The intro path to Kleiner was not found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ilya Fushman | co-leader of Kleiner; co-Executive Officer on Form D; Slack co-investor | 2014 (Slack), 2018 (KP) | [src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf) |\n| Ted Schlein | recruited him to Kleiner | before 2017 | [src](https://fortune.com/2026/01/31/inside-vc-firm-kleiner-perkins-turnaround-mamoon-hamid/) |\n| John Doerr | role model (\"number one person on my list\"); now partner | 2017 | [src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf) |\n| Chamath Palihapitiya | Social Capital co-founder; declined Doerr's offer to lead Kleiner | 2011 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/08/03/mamood-hamid-is-reportedly-heading-from-social-capital-to-kleiner-perkins/) |\n| \"Erwin Fetterman\" (likely Irwin Federman, USVP; needs-id) | mentor | 2005 | [src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf) |\n| Bucky Moore, Annie Case, Josh Coyne, Everett Randle, Leigh Marie Braswell | partners he hired or promoted | 2018–2023 | [src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf) |\n| Nadia Cochinwala | co-author on 5 of her 6 KP posts | 2025 | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/rogo-the-ai-platform-for-global-finance/) |\n| Dylan Field | Figma founder; first KP deal | 2017 | [src](https://fortune.com/2026/01/31/inside-vc-firm-kleiner-perkins-turnaround-mamoon-hamid/) |\n| Shishir Mehrotra | Coda founder; referred Glean | 2018 | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/case-study/glean/) |\n| Shardul Shah, Vinod Khosla, Josh Kopelman | Parallel board peers | 2025-11 | [src](https://parallel.ai/blog/series-a) |\n| Matt Bornstein / a16z | Mirendil seed co-lead | 2026-06 | [src](https://mirendil.com/news/announcing-mirendil) |\n| Robert Wachen | Etched co-founder, met in Paris | 2026 | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/etched/) |\n| Mary Meeker | growth team spun out as Bond during the reboot | 2018 | [src](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Read the Masters in Business transcript (2026-07) and the Uncapped transcript. Both are likely to cover the 2026 lab and mega-seed switch in his own words.\n- How did Kleiner get into Mirendil and Flapping Airplanes? Was Kleiner in Flapping's seed?\n- Fortune lists Safe Superintelligence, Harmonic and Synthesia among Kleiner AI bets. Which partner holds SSI? It is not on Hamid's page.\n- Is he an LP in any emerging fund? I checked press and LP directories (the directory page returned 403) and found no sourced LP link, so no `-lp.csv` was written.\n- Confirm the mentor's identity (Irwin Federman?).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/\n- https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf (Venture Unlocked transcript, 2023-11-01) / https://ventureunlocked.substack.com/p/going-back-to-the-foundation-of-kleiner\n- https://fortune.com/2026/01/31/inside-vc-firm-kleiner-perkins-turnaround-mamoon-hamid/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2017/08/03/mamood-hamid-is-reportedly-heading-from-social-capital-to-kleiner-perkins/\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/etched/\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/mirendil-building-the-system-that-builds-systems/\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/case-study/glean/\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/slack/\n- https://parallel.ai/blog/series-a\n- https://mirendil.com/news/announcing-mirendil\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one\n- https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/\n- https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-kleiner-perkins-mamoon-hamid-on-investing-lessons-from-leading-rounds-in-figma-slack-and-rippling--lessons-building-a-generational-defining-firm-with-kleiner-perkins--ai-where-value-accrues-startups-vs-incumbents--scaling-laws\n- https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-kleiner-perkins-mamoon-hamid-d4f\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2115014/000211501426000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_Capital_(venture_capital_firm)\n", "person:marc-andrusko": "\n# Marc Andrusko\n\n## One-line take\nAn ex-Goldman, ex-Plaid fintech investor who became one of a16z's most prolific writers on B2B AI apps (Rillet, Eve, Sphere). In March 2026 he left for Oak HC/FT [src](https://oakhcft.com/news/welcoming-marc-andrusko-to-oak-hc-ft). Worth following for his skeptical diligence rules on AI-app defensibility, not for frontier labs.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he is an operator-analyst who knows finance from three seats: rates trading at Goldman, data pipes at Plaid, and AI apps at a16z [src](https://oakhcft.com/news/welcoming-marc-andrusko-to-oak-hc-ft).\n- **How he judges AI apps:** through business models and data access. He asks whether a buyer's billing model rewards efficiency, whether the startup owns its data, and whether forward-deployed work turns into product.\n- **Constraint:** he is a fintech and vertical-software specialist. Oak HC/FT is a healthcare and fintech firm with growth-equity roots.\n- **What he'd never do:** underwrite a \"Palantir for X\" company whose deployments are all bespoke [src](https://a16z.com/the-palantirization-of-everything/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Palantir for X:** most FDE-heavy AI startups risk becoming services firms. In his words, \"You're 'Accenture for X' with a nicer front-end.\" [src](https://a16z.com/the-palantirization-of-everything/) (2026-01-16)\n- **Diligence question for FDE-heavy companies:** \"Does the amount of forward-deployed effort go down meaningfully over time?\" If the answers are vague, he says repeatability is hard to underwrite. [src](https://a16z.com/the-palantirization-of-everything/)\n- **Why a16z backed Eve:** the thesis was the plaintiff-law business model (\"a big part of our thesis was that the plaintiff law model…\"). Contingency fees reward efficiency; hourly billing does not. [src](https://a16z.com/law-order-gpu/) (2025-09-23)\n- **On Harvey:** usage inside BigLaw is low, but \"nobody gets fired for buying Harvey\". Its moat is brand, not data network effects. [src](https://a16z.com/law-order-gpu/)\n- **On frontier labs moving up the stack:** \"Now the farms are running restaurants too.\" He argues apps survive through regulated or proprietary data, citing vLex and OpenEvidence. [src](https://a16z.com/fruits-of-the-walled-garden/) (2025-10-09)\n- **On incumbents restricting data access:** he calls it \"API war by attrition, not revocation\", meaning more rate limits and fees rather than shutoffs. His Plaid precedent: users left banks that blocked their fintech apps. [src](https://a16z.com/api-battleground-platform-wars/) (2025-08-07)\n- **Camber timing:** a16z was in \"first as an angel investor, then in leading their Series A\", and it led the Series B in Feb 2025. [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-camber/)\n- **Greg Ulrich and Mastercard:** Ulrich took the Chief AI and Data Officer job after the CEO called while he was at an a16z event. Andrusko joked, \"we're solely responsible for you getting the job.\" [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/in-the-vault-how-ai-is-powering-payments-with-greg-ulrich-mastercard/)\n- **Henry R. Kravis** co-invested in two a16z-led fintech Series As that Andrusko co-signed: Moment in 2023 [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-moment/) and Crux in 2024 [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-crux/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He invests in AI-native automation of finance and legal work. Examples: Rillet (Series B co-lead, 2025) [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-rillet/), Eve (Series A, 2025) [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-eve/) and Sphere (Series A lead and board seat, 2025) [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sphere/). That overlaps with the thesis's AI-automation leg (the Serval type).\n\nHe does not do pre-training, labs or compute. His new firm, Oak HC/FT, is fintech and healthcare, early and growth stage. He is a useful co-investor or diligence voice on vertical AI apps, but not a frontier-lab node.\n\n## Background\n- Duke University, magna cum laude, in Public Policy, Environmental Science, and Markets & Management [src](https://oakhcft.com/news/welcoming-marc-andrusko-to-oak-hc-ft).\n- Goldman Sachs, Vice President: corporate development, strategy, and interest-rate products sales and trading. Dates not found [src](https://oakhcft.com/news/welcoming-marc-andrusko-to-oak-hc-ft).\n- Plaid: led the personal financial management vertical, building consumer partnerships. Dates not found [src](https://oakhcft.com/news/welcoming-marc-andrusko-to-oak-hc-ft).\n- a16z fintech team:\n  - Earliest byline found is 2021-12-20 [src](https://a16z.com/newsletter/the-big-ideas-that-fintech-will-tackle-in-2022-december-2021-fintech-newsletter/).\n  - Described as \"investment partner for a16z focused on fintech\" in Mar 2023 [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/payments-payors-fintechs-role-with-kurt-adams/).\n  - Later listed as partner, B2B AI applications and fintech.\n  - An aggregator gives a 2021 start, but that is unconfirmed.\n- Oak HC/FT, Partner, announced 2026-03-18 [src](https://oakhcft.com/news/welcoming-marc-andrusko-to-oak-hc-ft). He is no longer on the a16z team page (checked 2026-11-10).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.oakhcft.com/team-members/marc-andrusko | Oak HC/FT bio (2026) |\n| Substack / blog | https://a16z.com/author/marc-andrusko/ | a16z author archive, about 40 pieces 2021-2026; page now empty, Wayback copy 2026-03-04 |\n| X | https://x.com/mandrusko1 | Handle from a16z author bio links; profile not readable (paywalled fetch) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/marc-andrusko-4680ab25/ | Linked from Oak bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://a16z.com/podcasts/a16z-live/ | Co-host of a16z Fintech's \"In the Vault\" series (Citi, Mastercard) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-03: a16z Bio Eats World, \"Payments & Payors\" with Kurt Adams (Optum Financial). [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/payments-payors-fintechs-role-with-kurt-adams/) No transcript on the page.\n- 2024-08: In the Vault, with Tim Karpoff (Citi). [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/in-the-vault-tim-karpoff-citi/) He asks whether boards favour the \"cost out\" use of GenAI over revenue use.\n- 2024-11: a16z Podcast, \"Death of a Salesforce\" with Joe Schmidt. [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/death-of-a-salesforce-why-ai-will-transform-sales/)\n- 2025-02: In the Vault, with Greg Ulrich (Mastercard). [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/in-the-vault-how-ai-is-powering-payments-with-greg-ulrich-mastercard/) He warns startups not to lead by asking incumbents for their data.\n- 2025-04: Wharton FinTech Podcast (guest), hosted by Zoey Tang. [link](https://soundcloud.com/wft/marc-andusko-partner-at-andreessen-horowitz-investing-in-fintech-b2b-software) Not mined; no transcript.\n- 2025-12: a16z Big Ideas 2026, \"The Agentic Interface\". [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/big-ideas-2026-the-agentic-interface/) His segment is on moving from prompting to execution.\n\n## How they think\n- **Business model over technology:** sell AI where efficiency raises the buyer's revenue (contingency law, flat-fee work), not into hourly billers [src](https://a16z.com/law-order-gpu/).\n- **Data access is the moat:** he expects incumbents to tax API access [src](https://a16z.com/api-battleground-platform-wars/) and favours apps with proprietary or regulated data [src](https://a16z.com/fruits-of-the-walled-garden/).\n- **Margins:** he tolerates lower gross margins for much larger contracts, but not founders pretending to be SaaS [src](https://a16z.com/the-palantirization-of-everything/).\n- **Older fintech views:**\n  - Neobank savings yields are \"CAC in disguise\" (2022) [src](https://a16z.com/newsletter/deposit-beta-cac-in-disguise-november-2022-fintech-newsletter/).\n  - Infrastructure companies should start with one narrow, mission-critical pain point [src](https://a16z.com/foundations-for-successful-fintech-infrastructure-and-several-tradeoffs-to-consider/).\n  - Early pricing should be simple and value-based [src](https://a16z.com/the-b2b-fintech-pricing-journey/).\n- **Calls:** in Jan 2023 he predicted a year of fintech M&A [src](https://a16z.com/newsletter/2023-a-year-for-fintech-ma-january-2023-fintech-newsletter/). Not graded.\n\n## Read these first\n- [The Palantirization of everything](https://a16z.com/the-palantirization-of-everything/) (2026-01-16): his diligence questions for FDE-heavy AI apps.\n- [Law & Order: GPU](https://a16z.com/law-order-gpu/) (2025-09-23): the Eve thesis and his read on Harvey.\n- [The API Battleground](https://a16z.com/api-battleground-platform-wars/) (2025-08-07): data access as AI-app risk, through a Plaid lens.\n- [Fruits of the Walled Garden](https://a16z.com/fruits-of-the-walled-garden/) (2025-10-09): how apps defend against labs moving up the stack.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. The X handle @mandrusko1 is listed in a16z bios, but the profile could not be fetched.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Method | Series A | 2023-01 | a16z led (co-signed with Anish Acharya) | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-method/) |\n| Moment | Series A | 2023-09 | a16z led (co-signed with David Haber) | Venrock, Contrary, Neo, Henry R. Kravis | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-moment/) |\n| Crux | Series A | 2024-01 | a16z led (co-signed with Haber) | Lowercarbon, Ardent, New System, Overture, Canapi, Orsted, LS Power, Hartree, CIV, Kravis | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-crux/) |\n| Eve | Series A | 2025-01 | a16z led (co-signed with Haber) | Lightspeed, Menlo | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-eve/) |\n| Camber | Series B | 2025-02 | a16z led (co-signed with Haber); a16z earlier angel and A lead | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-camber/) |\n| Rillet | Series B | 2025-08 | a16z co-led (with Seema Amble, Alex Rampell) | ICONIQ | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-rillet/) |\n| Sphere | Series A | 2025-11 | Led; Andrusko joined the board | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sphere/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Sphere:** CEO Nick Rudder pivoted after his previous YC startup received tax notices from several countries. Andrusko led the A, citing AI-app customers (ElevenLabs, Lovable, Replit, Runway, Windsurf) and about two legacy migrations a week [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sphere/).\n- **Crux:** a16z had known founders Alfred Johnson and Allen Kramer \"for several years\" before leading the A [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-crux/).\n- **Eve:** chosen for its contingency-fee buyer economics [src](https://a16z.com/law-order-gpu/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Oak HC/FT | Partner | 2026-03 | [src](https://oakhcft.com/news/welcoming-marc-andrusko-to-oak-hc-ft) |\n| Andreessen Horowitz | Partner, B2B AI apps and fintech (former) | by 2021-12 to 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/newsletter/the-big-ideas-that-fintech-will-tackle-in-2022-december-2021-fintech-newsletter/) |\n| Plaid | Led PFM vertical (former) | | [src](https://oakhcft.com/news/welcoming-marc-andrusko-to-oak-hc-ft) |\n| Goldman Sachs | Vice President (former) | | [src](https://oakhcft.com/news/welcoming-marc-andrusko-to-oak-hc-ft) |\n| Matt Streisfeld | Oak HC/FT GP who announced his hire | 2026 | [src](https://oakhcft.com/news/welcoming-marc-andrusko-to-oak-hc-ft) |\n| David Haber | Frequent a16z co-signer (Moment, Crux, Eve, Camber) and podcast co-host | 2023 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-camber/) |\n| Seema Amble | Frequent a16z co-author (pricing, accounting AI, ERPs, Rillet) | 2022 | [src](https://a16z.com/the-b2b-fintech-pricing-journey/) |\n| Greg Ulrich (Mastercard) | Podcast guest he has known \"for a bit\" | 2024 | [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/in-the-vault-how-ai-is-powering-payments-with-greg-ulrich-mastercard/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- His exact a16z start date, and the dates of his promotions to investment partner and partner.\n- Whether he kept the Sphere board seat after moving to Oak.\n- His first deals at Oak HC/FT. Oak's 2026 fintech posts (Spade B, Cordant, Latitude) do not name him.\n- The Wharton FinTech episode (Apr 2025) has no transcript; transcribing it would give his own account of his career path.\n\n## Sources\n- https://oakhcft.com/news/welcoming-marc-andrusko-to-oak-hc-ft\n- https://www.oakhcft.com/team-members/marc-andrusko\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20260304101554/https://a16z.com/author/marc-andrusko/\n- https://a16z.com/the-palantirization-of-everything/\n- https://a16z.com/law-order-gpu/\n- https://a16z.com/api-battleground-platform-wars/\n- https://a16z.com/fruits-of-the-walled-garden/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sphere/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-camber/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-crux/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-moment/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-method/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/in-the-vault-how-ai-is-powering-payments-with-greg-ulrich-mastercard/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/in-the-vault-tim-karpoff-citi/\n- https://a16z.com/newsletter/deposit-beta-cac-in-disguise-november-2022-fintech-newsletter/\n- https://a16z.com/foundations-for-successful-fintech-infrastructure-and-several-tradeoffs-to-consider/\n- https://a16z.com/the-b2b-fintech-pricing-journey/\n- https://iq.wiki/wiki/marc-andrusko (aggregator, background only)\n", "person:marc-stad": "\n# Marc Stad\n\n## One-line take\nFounder-owner of a ~$45B crossover fund that buys proven AI leaders at huge scale (OpenAI, Anthropic). In his rare long talks he says this AI cycle is CapEx-heavy and may favour incumbents, which is why he waits rather than seeds [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable).\n\n## Crux\n**Why he wins:** he controls the firm (50-75% of the top holdco [filing](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/161443/PDF/161443.pdf)) and runs a flexible public/private pool. He is willing to invent structures: structured deals (18 by 2023, Spotify 2016), convertibles, and tenders assembled overnight (Uber) [src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-92-marc-stad-2023) [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable).\n**What constrains him:** a deliberately tiny team (about one hire a year early on) and a seven-rule checklist that most companies fail. He also admits that 2021 is when he broke those rules.\n**What he'd never do:** act like a VC. \"remember, we're not venture capitalists\" [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable). He invests once the model is proven, and has said SaaS is no longer where he is focused.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Uber tender (deal-origin):** Stad pitched Uber a $1B+ employee tender, then lost it to SoftBank: \"we're going to do it with SoftBank, not with you.\" He woke at 3am and assembled $6B of indications by 9:30am (an Asian strategic and sovereign, then General Atlantic, Tiger and TPG). He sent a 24-hour offer with a split-if-SoftBank-signs clause and ended up in a ~$12B joint tender [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable).\n- **Buffett by cold email:** \"Dear Deb, I have a deal for Warren.\" Buffett called back in two minutes, refused to join a club deal and took a Berkshire-only convertible (\"Let's do a convertible that just Berkshire can do with Dragoneer\"). That lifted the offer from $6.5B to $10B [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable).\n- **Mistake admitted:** 2021 was \"the only period\" Dragoneer broke its own rules: \"we did make some investments that we look back and regret\" [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable).\n- **AI view:** this cycle is CapEx-heavy, so \"will the winners of the next generation just be the incumbents getting bigger\". Also \"We don't know which ones will have the moat\" [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable).\n- **Pre-Dragoneer frontier-lab exposure:** \"we backed Palantir and Facebook and DeepMind\" before the 2012 launch, from a pool that was half IGSB money and half David Bonderman's [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable).\n- **LP mechanics:** allocators could not classify the fund, so he pitched \"a bucket called miscellaneous\". He tried not to report monthly or even quarterly numbers, and admits \"That didn't work so well\" [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable).\n- **Structured deals:** \"we've done 18 structured deals\". Spotify 2016 protected the downside while keeping the upside [src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-92-marc-stad-2023). A ~$2B MercadoLibre raise combined PayPal, Dragoneer and public holders: \"we could raise two billion dollars by bringing in paypal\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4umo8Tpxobk) (00:56:53).\n- **Zombie unicorns (May 2023):** says far more 2020-21 unicorns will be marked to zero than people admit: \"is a heck of a lot higher than anyone's talking about\" [src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-92-marc-stad-2023).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** Dragoneer is growth and crossover only. Its AI deals are mega-rounds: OpenAI's Aug-2025 tranche with $2.8B [src](https://fortune.com/2025/08/01/openai-funding-oversubscribed-early-investors-new-partners-dragoneer/), an Anthropic Series G co-lead and the Series H lead [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h). Useful as a **downstream buyer** of frontier-lab equity, and as a signal of how late-stage capital prices moats. The one early-stage thread worth confirming is the pre-2012 DeepMind position.\n\n## Background\n- First in his family to finish high school; grew up in Montebello / East LA; applied to 21 colleges; Harvard [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4umo8Tpxobk) (00:47:20).\n- Wrote Robert Rubin's endorsement speech for Al Gore at 20. Rubin got him an interview, which led to a Salomon Smith Barney internship (~2000) [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable) [src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-92-marc-stad-2023).\n- McKinsey, then about 25 PE rejections, then a middle-market PE firm, then TPG after a walk-in interview (Tom Reinhardt, Jim Coulter) [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable).\n- Stanford GSB MBA '08. IGSB summer 2007 ($1M, all into the MercadoLibre IPO, which went from $18 to $80). Then IGSB partner/PM; moved to Hong Kong in 2010 at Bonderman's urging [src](https://time.com/7202470/five-lessons-mentor-trust-work-mentorship/).\n- Founded Dragoneer in 2012 with $180M [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable). Fortune 40 Under 40, 2018 [src](https://fortune.com/ranking/40-under-40/2018/marc-stad/).\n- 2026: buying the controlling stake in the Timberwolves and Lynx at $4.5B through his family office, not Dragoneer [src](https://www.axios.com/2026/08/21/minnesota-timberwolves-lynx-stad-lore-arod). In 2022 he described himself as \"part owner of a national hockey team\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4umo8Tpxobk) (00:54:06).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.dragoneer.com | Home, contact and LP portal only; no team page (2026-11) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | One TIME op-ed (2024-12) |\n| X | nothing found | No account found |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | Not checked (LinkedIn excluded) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Rare guest; the GSB host says he \"doesn't do a lot of media\" |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-11 — Stanford GSB View From The Top — \"Obtaining the Unobtainable\" — [link](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable) — Uber/Buffett tender story, 2021 regrets, AI CapEx and moats, the \"miscellaneous bucket\". Video [here](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fpqXvDKhtvQ) (2026-10-14).\n- 2024-12 — TIME — \"Five Lessons From My Mentor\" (op-ed on Bonderman) — [link](https://time.com/7202470/five-lessons-mentor-trust-work-mentorship/).\n- 2023-05 — Milken Global Conference — \"Disrupt or Be Disrupted: Venturing into Tech\" with Levy, Saroya, Craver and Settle — [panel](https://milkeninstitute.org/panel/14592), [transcript](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-92-marc-stad-2023) — three-box screen, 18 structured deals, IPO-cycle rule, PointClickCare.\n- 2022-04 — uAspire First Ones honoree — [video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4umo8Tpxobk) — Bezos restroom pitch, walking out of the Morgan Stanley conference, MercadoLibre/PayPal raise.\n- 2021-05 — Investor's Studio (Clubhouse) with Andy Rachleff — [announcement](https://x.com/arachleff/status/1397567960311472133) — no recording found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Screen:** IGSB's seven rules: moat, free cash flow, great founder, big market, understandable, upside, margin of safety [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable). The 2023 version: great business, great price, known \"darn well\"; otherwise no interest [src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-92-marc-stad-2023).\n- **Concentration:** \"I think diversifying feels like a big risk\"; wait for high conviction, then swing very hard.\n- **Contrarian:** profitability is not the test, unit economics are. Buy public companies in their first year after IPO and early in an IPO cycle. SaaS is no longer the focus; the next Uber, Robinhood or Airbnb is.\n- **Calls:** right on Spotify, Uber, DoorDash (~$6B entry) and MercadoLibre. Wrong, by his own account, on parts of the 2021 book.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [GSB View From The Top transcript](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable): the richest source (deal stories, mistakes, AI view).\n2. [Milken 2023 panel transcript](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-92-marc-stad-2023): the investing screen and IPO-cycle rules.\n3. [uAspire 2022 speech](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4umo8Tpxobk) (from 00:47:20): the \"create your own luck\" and creativity stories.\n4. [TIME op-ed on Bonderman](https://time.com/7202470/five-lessons-mentor-trust-work-mentorship/): the mentor who funded and launched him.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X or Substack presence; public voice is limited to a handful of talks and quotes in deal announcements.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | 2025 $40B round ($8.3B tranche; $2.8B check) | 2025-08 | yes | Blackstone, T. Rowe Price, TPG, others | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/08/01/openai-funding-oversubscribed-early-investors-new-partners-dragoneer/) |\n| Anthropic | Series H ($65B, $965B post) | 2026-05-28 | yes (one of four) | Altimeter, Greenoaks, Sequoia | [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h) |\n| Uber | Tender (~$12B with SoftBank) plus Berkshire convertible | not dated in source | co-lead | SoftBank, Berkshire Hathaway | [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable) |\n| Spotify | Structured deal | 2016 | not stated | | [src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-92-marc-stad-2023) |\n| DoorDash | at ~$6B valuation | not dated | not stated | | [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable) |\n| Facebook | private (IGSB era) | 2010 | not stated | | [src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-92-marc-stad-2023) |\n| Palantir; DeepMind | private (IGSB/Bonderman pool, pre-2012) | pre-2012 | not stated | | [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable) |\n| MercadoLibre | IPO (2007, as intern); later ~$2B structured raise with PayPal | 2007; later undated | | PayPal | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4umo8Tpxobk) |\n| PointClickCare | holding | by 2023 | not stated | | [src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-92-marc-stad-2023) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Uber:** Stad pitched a large tender to Uber's CEO and head of business development and lost it to SoftBank a month later. At 3am he phoned Asian strategics and sovereigns, then GA, Tiger and TPG, and had $6B of indications by 9:30am. A cold email to Buffett's assistant added a Berkshire convertible and lifted the offer to $10B. A 24-hour expiry with a split-if-SoftBank-signs clause ended in a joint ~$12B tender. Masa called Yuri Milner to ask \"Who the hell is this guy Marc?\" [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable).\n- **MercadoLibre 2007:** cold-wrote the founder, met him after his Capital Group meeting, put the full $1M intern allocation into the IPO at $18 and sold near $80 [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable).\n- **Bonderman's $5M:** collected on an IOU from a hotel deal Stad ran for Bonderman at TPG. When Bonderman ran out of liquidity for the pool, he told Stad to raise outside capital, and Dragoneer followed [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Pat Robertson | Dragoneer co-founder/President; on every Form D with Stad | 2012 | [filing](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/161443/PDF/161443.pdf) |\n| David Bonderman (TPG) | Mentor; half of the pre-Dragoneer capital | TPG years (date not given) | [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable) |\n| Tim and Reese (IGSB; surnames unconfirmed) | Recruiters and lifelong mentors | 2007 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4umo8Tpxobk) |\n| Masayoshi Son | Uber rival, now \"dear friend\" | Uber tender | [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable) |\n| Warren Buffett | Uber co-investor via a convertible | Uber tender | [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable) |\n| Marc Lore, Alex Rodriguez | Timberwolves/Lynx co-owners; Stad buying control | 2026 | [src](https://www.axios.com/2026/08/21/minnesota-timberwolves-lynx-stad-lore-arod) |\n| Sarah Friar (OpenAI CFO) | Director on Dragoneer SPACs from Aug 2020 | 2020-08 | [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1827076/000119312521065721/d922828ds1.htm) |\n| Anton Levy, Monti Saroya, David Craver, Dana Settle | Milken 2023 co-panelists (\"very close professional and personal friends\") | 2023 | [src](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-92-marc-stad-2023) |\n\n## Open questions\n- DeepMind: what round and when did the IGSB/Bonderman pool invest, and was it held through the 2014 Google sale?\n- Which 2021 investments he regrets (not named).\n- Date of the Uber tender (it implies the SoftBank 2017-18 deal) and the Bezos-pitched company.\n- Full names of IGSB mentors \"Tim\" and \"Reese\".\n- The Clubhouse Investor's Studio (2021) and the Milken video (login-gated) were not heard.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/marc-stad-obtaining-unobtainable\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fpqXvDKhtvQ\n- https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-92-marc-stad-2023\n- https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-347-marc-stad-2026\n- https://milkeninstitute.org/panel/14592\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4umo8Tpxobk\n- https://time.com/7202470/five-lessons-mentor-trust-work-mentorship/\n- https://x.com/arachleff/status/1397567960311472133\n- https://fortune.com/ranking/40-under-40/2018/marc-stad/\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/161443/PDF/161443.pdf\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1827076/000119312521065721/d922828ds1.htm\n- https://fortune.com/2025/08/01/openai-funding-oversubscribed-early-investors-new-partners-dragoneer/\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h\n- https://www.axios.com/2026/08/21/minnesota-timberwolves-lynx-stad-lore-arod\n", "person:marco-mascorro": "\n# Marco Mascorro\n\n> Field note: `seniority: angel` and `check_capacity: sub-10m` describe him **now**, as an independent. While at a16z (sourced 2024-08 to 2026-03) he was a **Partner** on rounds from $31M to $2B. The $10M+ access belonged to the platform, which he has left.\n\n## One-line take\nHe was a roboticist turned a16z AI-infra partner, named on the lead side of Cursor, Black Forest Labs, Thinking Machines and Deeptune. He has now left a16z to build and do research in RL environments and interpretability. That makes him a technical, deal-adjacent angel and co-investor, not a lead check.\n\n## Crux\n- **Status resolved (was needs-id).** He has left a16z.\n  - METR: \"Previously he was a Partner at Andreessen Horowitz\" and \"He is currently an independent researcher and entrepreneur\" ([src](https://metr.org/team/marco-mascorro)).\n  - His own site, under the heading \"When I was a Partner at a16z, I invested and worked with:\", calls him \"Founder, AI Researcher & Roboticist\" ([src](https://mascobot.com/investing), [src](https://mascobot.com)).\n  - His last a16z byline is the Deeptune Series A on 2026-03-19 ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-deeptune/)), so he left somewhere between 2026-03-19 and 2026-11-10. No departure announcement was found.\n- **Why he won.** He is a builder-investor in the technical layer. Before a16z he co-founded the robotics company Fellow Robots/Fellow AI ([src](https://mascobot.com)). In March 2026 he was still writing evaluation infrastructure himself (Harbor + OSWorld). Ten days later, a16z announced it was leading a Series A in an RL-environment company with him as first author.\n- **What constrains him now.** He no longer has the platform. His $10M+ lead access came from a16z's balance sheet, with Martin Casado and Anjney Midha as senior co-signers on every memo. On his own page he describes his investing in modest terms: \"Occasionally, I like investing in and helping founders.\" ([src](https://mascobot.com/investing))\n- **What he would never do (inferred, not stated).** Evaluate agents with LLM-as-judge. He writes for \"deterministic comparison of file contents, UI state, or application output.\" ([src](https://www.mascobot.com/blog/osworld-harbor.html))\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Built in the category, then backed it ten days later.**\n  - 2026-03-09: he published an OSWorld adapter (412 desktop tasks) and three computer-use agents for the Harbor eval framework, co-authored with Joan Cabezas. He wrote: \"The real goal is generating training signal.\" ([src](https://www.mascobot.com/blog/osworld-harbor.html))\n  - 2026-03-19: a16z announced it was \"leading the Series A in Deeptune\", a company making RL environments for computer use and code. Byline: Mascorro first, Casado second ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-deeptune/)).\n  - The Deeptune memo's thesis is that the next decade \"will be mostly driven by better environments\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-deeptune/)).\n  - Deeptune's own site reports a $43M Series A in March 2026 and says it is joining Mercor (July 2026), about four months after the round ([src](https://www.deeptune.com/)).\n- **Thinking Machines.** He claims it as a seed lead on his own page: \"Thinking Machines: seed lead\" ([src](https://mascobot.com/investing)). a16z led TML's $2B seed at a $12B valuation, which closed 2025-07-14 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/15/mira-muratis-thinking-machines-lab-is-worth-12b-in-seed-round/)). No a16z memo naming him was found, so his involvement rests on his own page only.\n- **Byline position over time.** He moved from supporting name to lead name:\n  - BFL: 2nd of 4, behind Midha.\n  - Cursor: 2nd, behind Bornstein.\n  - WaveForms and Shizuku: 2nd, behind Justine Moore.\n  - Deeptune: 1st, alongside Casado only.\n\n  (Sources: [BFL](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/), [Cursor](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/), [WaveForms](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-waveforms-ai/), [Shizuku](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-shizuku-ai/), [Deeptune](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-deeptune/).) This is a signal, not proof of who sourced each deal.\n- **Preference Model credit conflict (unresolved).**\n  - His site lists Preference Model under his a16z deals as \"seed lead\" ([src](https://mascobot.com/investing)).\n  - a16z's announcement, dated 2026-10-07, is bylined Jennifer Li alone and does not say whether a16z led ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-preference-model/)).\n  - Either he did the deal and left before the announcement, or the credit differs. Both Preference Model and Deeptune build RL environments, the same theme as his own work.\n- **Exits he claims on his own page (single-sourced, not verified):** Cursor \"acquired by SpaceX\", WaveForms \"acquired by Meta\", Embra \"acquired by Notion\", Stack AI \"acquired by Asana\" ([src](https://mascobot.com/investing)).\n- **Personal portfolio sits close to frontier talent.** Under \"Other investments:\" (no firm named) he lists:\n  - Discovery Loop (pre-seed), with the names Jeff Dean, Quoc Le, Sanjay G. and Oriol V.\n  - Mechanize (labelled \"seed lead\"), with Tamay Besiroglu, Ege Erdil and Matthew Barnett.\n  - SF Compute (pre-seed).\n  - Runpod (\"first check\").\n\n  ([src](https://mascobot.com/investing)) The page doesn't say whether the listed names are founders or co-investors.\n- **Current research focus:** \"a roboticist currently researching AI models for interpretability\" ([src](https://mascobot.com)). He has advised METR since April 2025 ([src](https://metr.org/team/marco-mascorro)), which overlapped with his time at a16z.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High as a network and co-investor node. Low as a lead or check source.**\n- The deals that matter to you: Thinking Machines ($2B seed, his claim), Black Forest Labs ($31M seed, a16z first institution in), Deeptune (RL environments, Series A lead), Cursor (Series A), SF Compute (pre-seed, compute), Discovery Loop (pre-seed, Google Brain-adjacent names) and Mechanize.\n- He has no fund now, so he won't anchor a $600M seed. His value is technical diligence on environments, evals and interpretability, and early access to research-team spinouts.\n- He is likely receptive to a co-invest ask on a new lab or an RL-environment company.\n\n## Background\n- **BMW, Munich:** robotics and new vehicle design, including work on the 2014 MINI Cooper series ([src](https://mascobot.com)). Dates not given.\n- **Japan:** AI and robotics research tied to the Fukushima disaster response, and autonomous wheelchair robots ([src](https://metr.org/team/marco-mascorro)).\n- **Singularity University:** AI and Robotics Teaching Fellow, 2012–2014 ([src](https://mascobot.com)).\n- **Fellow Robots / Fellow AI:** co-founded with former NASA astronaut Dan Barry. Launched LoweBot and OSHbot in 2014 ([src](https://mascobot.com)).\n- **YC:** his 2018 blog post describes a conversation with Sam Altman about the UBI pilot \"they were running with UBI during YC\" ([src](https://www.mascobot.com/blog/on-universal-basic-income.html)). His exact YC role is unclear.\n- **a16z:** Partner, AI infrastructure. Bio: \"invests in AI infrastructure companies\" (2024-08 memos) ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/)). Join date not found.\n- **METR:** advisor from April 2025 ([src](https://metr.org/team/marco-mascorro)).\n- **Now:** independent researcher and entrepreneur, by 2026 (same source).\n- **Education:** nothing found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://mascobot.com | bio + investing list; checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://mascobot.com/archives.html | 2 posts (2018-02-26, 2026-03-09) |\n| X | https://x.com/Mascobot | from METR page; fetch blocked (HTTP 402) |\n| LinkedIn | https://linkedin.com/in/marcomascorro/ | from METR page; not fetched |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/Mascobot | open-source pandemic ventilator (239 stars); Harbor fork |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The search budget was exhausted, so this is not a negative finding.\n\n## How they think\n- **Environments are the bottleneck now.** The Deeptune memo says \"the limiting factor is no longer just pretraining data.\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-deeptune/))\n- **Deterministic graders over LLM-as-judge; evals exist to make training signal** ([src](https://www.mascobot.com/blog/osworld-harbor.html)).\n- **Open models for the visual stack.** The BFL memo: \"Visual AI has a supply chain problem.\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/))\n- **Calls that look right:** Cursor and BFL entered early in 2024, and TML was a top-tier lab seed. Deeptune's sale to Mercor after four months reads as a fast but probably modest outcome; terms are unknown.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Integrating Harbor for CUA evaluation](https://www.mascobot.com/blog/osworld-harbor.html) (2026-03-09): his hands-on view of evals and RL.\n2. [Investing in Deeptune](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-deeptune/) (2026-03-19): his only first-author lead memo.\n3. [Investing in Black Forest Labs](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/) (2024-08-01): the open visual-model thesis.\n4. [mascobot.com/investing](https://mascobot.com/investing): his self-reported list, including deals done outside a16z.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X returned HTTP 402.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Black Forest Labs | Seed ($31M) | 2024-08-01 | yes (a16z) | General Catalyst, MätchVC (follow-up); angels Iribe, Ovitz, Tan, Aila, Koltun | [bfl](https://bfl.ai/announcements/24-08-01-bfl), [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/) |\n| Cursor (Anysphere) | Series A ($60M) | 2024-08-22 | his site: \"series A lead\"; cursor.com doesn't say | Thrive Capital, OpenAI, Jeff Dean, Noam Brown | [cursor](https://cursor.com/blog/series-a), [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/) |\n| WaveForms AI | Seed (size not confirmed) | 2024-12-09 | his site: \"seed lead\" | not found | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-waveforms-ai/), [site](https://mascobot.com/investing) |\n| Thinking Machines Lab | Seed ($2B at $12B) | 2025-07-14 | yes (a16z); his involvement from his site only | Nvidia, Accel, ServiceNow, Cisco, AMD, Jane Street | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/15/mira-muratis-thinking-machines-lab-is-worth-12b-in-seed-round/), [site](https://mascobot.com/investing) |\n| Shizuku AI | Seed | 2026-02-09 | yes (a16z) | not found | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-shizuku-ai/) |\n| Deeptune | Series A ($43M) | 2026-03-19 | yes (a16z) | not found | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-deeptune/), [deeptune](https://www.deeptune.com/) |\n| Preference Model | Seed (his label) | 2026 (a16z post 2026-10-07) | his site: \"seed lead\"; a16z post silent | not found | [site](https://mascobot.com/investing), [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-preference-model/) |\n| Mechanize | Seed (his label \"seed lead\") | unknown | per his site; vehicle unattributed | not found | [site](https://mascobot.com/investing) |\n| Discovery Loop | Pre-seed | unknown | no | not found | [site](https://mascobot.com/investing) |\n| SF Compute | Pre-seed | unknown | no | not found | [site](https://mascobot.com/investing) |\n| Runpod | \"first check\" | unknown | unknown | not found | [site](https://mascobot.com/investing) |\n| Third Moment Research | Pre-seed | unknown | no | not found | [site](https://mascobot.com/investing) |\n| Veeda AI | Seed | unknown | no | not found | [site](https://mascobot.com/investing) |\n| Conway Research / Underdog | Seed | unknown | no | not found | [site](https://mascobot.com/investing) |\n| Embra AI | Seed | unknown | no | not found | [site](https://mascobot.com/investing) |\n| Stack AI | Seed | unknown | no | not found | [site](https://mascobot.com/investing) |\n| AirGarage | Seed | unknown | no | not found | [site](https://mascobot.com/investing) |\n| General Proximity Bio | Seed | unknown | no | not found | [site](https://mascobot.com/investing) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Black Forest Labs.** a16z was the first institution in on the priced seed; General Catalyst and MätchVC are named only as follow-ups. Midha is first author on the memo, so he most likely ran the deal, with Mascorro second ([src](https://bfl.ai/announcements/24-08-01-bfl)).\n- **Deeptune.** He published his own computer-use eval tooling on 2026-03-09 and was first author on the a16z lead ten days later. The ordering suggests he came to it through his own technical work, but the intro path is not sourced. Deeptune later joined Mercor (July 2026) ([src](https://www.deeptune.com/)).\n- **Cursor.** He was second author on a four-person memo behind Bornstein. Lead status is self-reported on his site.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Andreessen Horowitz | Partner, AI infra (former) | by 2024-08 to after 2026-03-19 | [src](https://metr.org/team/marco-mascorro) |\n| METR | Advisor | 2025-04 | [src](https://metr.org/team/marco-mascorro) |\n| Martin Casado | Co-author on Cursor, WaveForms, Shizuku, Deeptune | 2024 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-deeptune/) |\n| Anjney Midha | Co-author on BFL (first author) | 2024 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/) |\n| Justine Moore | Co-author on BFL, WaveForms, Shizuku | 2024 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-shizuku-ai/) |\n| Matt Bornstein | Co-author on Cursor (first author) | 2024 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/) |\n| Rajko Radovanovic | Co-author on BFL, Cursor | 2024 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/) |\n| Joan Cabezas | Co-author, Harbor/OSWorld post | 2026 | [src](https://www.mascobot.com/blog/osworld-harbor.html) |\n| Dan Barry | Fellow Robots co-founder | not dated | [src](https://mascobot.com) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What is his exact departure date from a16z, and is there a new company? His site says \"Founder\".\n- Did he originate Thinking Machines, or is he one name among many at a16z?\n- Who led Mechanize's seed, and through which vehicle?\n- Who are the Discovery Loop founders? Jeff Dean, Quoc Le, Sanjay Ghemawat and Oriol Vinyals are named, but their roles are unclear.\n- What was the WaveForms seed size, and what were the Meta acquisition terms?\n- What were the Deeptune–Mercor terms?\n- When did he join a16z, and with what title?\n\n## Sources\n- https://metr.org/team/marco-mascorro\n- https://mascobot.com\n- https://mascobot.com/investing\n- https://mascobot.com/archives.html\n- https://www.mascobot.com/blog/osworld-harbor.html\n- https://www.mascobot.com/blog/on-universal-basic-income.html\n- https://github.com/Mascobot\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-waveforms-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-shizuku-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-deeptune/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-preference-model/\n- https://bfl.ai/announcements/24-08-01-bfl\n- https://cursor.com/blog/series-a\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/15/mira-muratis-thinking-machines-lab-is-worth-12b-in-seed-round/\n- https://www.deeptune.com/\n", "person:mario-gabriele": "\n# Mario Gabriele\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. The web search budget was used up at the start of this task, so this profile rests only on direct fetches of hummingbird.vc, generalist.com and a guest-view LinkedIn page, plus facts already in this repo. A follow-up search pass is needed for Mario's join date, X posts and podcast guest appearances. Sources give no pronouns, so this profile uses \"they\".*\n\n## One-line take\nMario runs the best-known long-form tech publication still owned by an investor. In 2026 they pointed it at frontier-AI labs (Periodic Labs, Radical Numerics, Simile, Generalist AI), and they are now a Partner at Hummingbird, whose only frontier-lab position is Flapping Airplanes.\n\n## Crux\n- **How they win: access, not capital.** Founders sit for long-form interviews before they are famous, and in 2026 The Generalist interviewed lab founders.\n- **They turned the publication into a partner seat.** In 2023 Mario profiled Hummingbird from the outside; by 2026 they are listed as a Partner ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)).\n- **Constraint 1: no confirmed deal authority.** No deal is attributed to them at Hummingbird, and their own vehicle, Generalist Capital, has only one sourced position (Privy).\n- **Constraint 2: the independent-fund route met resistance.** In 2022 at least one fund-of-funds, Nomads, declined Generalist Capital ([src](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)).\n- **What they'd never do: claim to explain founders with a formula.** Their founder-formation series disclaims statistical generalization. That fits Hummingbird's \"outlier person\" thesis rather than a checklist approach.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Met Flapping Airplanes' CEO before the Series A.** Mario wrote on LinkedIn that they recall a sit-down \"with Benjamin Spector at this office in SF earlier this year\" ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/mariogabriele/), Oct 2026). This was before the $1B Series A announced 2026-10-21. It is the only sourced touchpoint between a Hummingbird person and the Flapping deal.\n- **Framed Hummingbird as joining an existing story.** Mario wrote they are \"even happier that Hummingbird is joining the Flappy story\" ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/mariogabriele/)).\n  - Inference: Hummingbird entered at the Series A, and Mario likely helped bring it in.\n  - Not confirmed: this is not attribution. \"Even happier\" may mean they were already pleased for the company, or that they hold a personal stake.\n  - Correction: this supersedes the `needs-id` lead in `absolute-ventures-leads.csv`, which called it a plain repost.\n- **Generalist Capital is a live investing vehicle.** Every Generalist post carries the footer: \"may feature entities in which Generalist Capital, LLC or the author has invested\" ([src](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)). So their coverage overlaps with their own book, but no position is disclosed in any 2026 AI-lab episode. I checked the Periodic Labs, Radical Numerics, Simile and Pete Florence pages, and none has a deal disclosure.\n- **The fund-of-funds rejection is on the record, in their own words.** \"In the spring of 2022, I was introduced to Wouter Gort's team as part of my fundraise for Generalist Capital,\" and \"Nomads declined to invest in Generalist Capital\" ([src](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird), 2023-12-03).\n- **Generalist Capital overlaps with the Hummingbird orbit.** Privy's backers include \"Sequoia Capital, Paradigm, Blue Yard, and Generalist Capital\", alongside Pamir Gelenbe, who is linked to Hummingbird ([src](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)).\n- **Their own takes on AI, from the \"From my notebook\" section of the Periodic Labs piece:**\n  - \"Sci-fi remains an endless source of alpha\"\n  - \"I underestimated the gap between discovery and utility\"\n  - Source: [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/a-chatbot-walks-into-a-laboratory), 2026-09-29.\n- **Compute view.** Mario called memory prices \"a growing tax on anyone trying to build or scale compute\" ([src](https://www.generalist.com/p/generalist-intelligence-july-10), 2026-07-10; paywalled after the opening, so the view may be incomplete).\n- **Founder-formation lens matches Hummingbird's.** The 2026 Saplings series traces instability and loss in founders' early lives (\"these children learned that life could be fickle and cruel\") but says it \"does not attempt to make statistical observations\" ([src](https://www.generalist.com/p/saplings-unstable-ground), 2026-08-13). Mario's 2023 profile reported that Hummingbird treats founder trauma as predictive.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium, and possibly high once confirmed.**\n\nWhy it could be high:\n- Mario is the most visible frontier-AI sourcing node at a firm that wrote a check into a neolab's Series A (Flapping Airplanes) and backs AI compute (Etched, OLIX).\n- Their 2026 guest list is close to my target list: Periodic Labs, Radical Numerics, Simile and Generalist AI.\n\nWhy it is not high yet:\n- No deal is attributed to them.\n- Their check size and authority are unknown.\n- Generalist Capital's sourced holdings (Privy) are not AI.\n\nUseful as:\n- A warm channel into lab founders before they raise.\n- A read on which labs a media-plus-capital investor rates.\n- A possible co-investor at the seed stage, through Hummingbird's concentrated, double-down model.\n\nDeals that matter to me: Flapping Airplanes (Hummingbird holding plus Mario's sourced pre-A meeting). The rest are interview subjects only.\n\n## Background\n- Columbia College, Columbia University, 2008–2012, Summa Cum Laude and Phi Beta Kappa ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/mariogabriele/)). A second education entry is listed for 2014–2016 with no institution named.\n- Before The Generalist, they worked in venture capital and as a startup operator, and briefly as a cook at a Michelin-starred restaurant ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)). Employers and dates were not found.\n- Founded The Generalist (date not found) ([about](https://www.generalist.com/about)).\n- Raised for Generalist Capital in spring 2022 ([src](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)). The fund size and close were not found.\n- Partner at Hummingbird Ventures ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)). Join date not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.generalist.com/ | The repo's Hummingbird profile cites 169k+ subscribers ([firms/hummingbird-ventures.md](../firms/hummingbird-ventures.md)) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.generalist.com/archive | About weekly: Generalist Intelligence briefings, podcasts and the Saplings series (2026) |\n| X | https://x.com/mariogabriele | Fetch blocked (402); not verified |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/mariogabriele/ | \"35K followers\" (guest view, 2026-11-10); posts podcast promos |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | The Generalist (YouTube, Apple, Spotify) | Episodes link from generalist.com |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nThese are episodes Mario hosted. No guest appearances were found, because search was unavailable.\n- **2026-11-03, The Generalist, Simile (Joon Sung Park).** Mario predicts orchestrated, task-specific bots \"will become the default for professional work\" — [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/a-portrait-of-eight-billion-people). The page says Simile raised \"more than $300 million\" at a $2B valuation.\n- **2026-10-20, The Generalist, Pete Florence (Generalist AI).** On robot dexterity — [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/pete-florence-when-robots-improvise)\n- **2026-10-08, The Generalist, Jaan Tallinn.** On AI race dynamics. Tallinn's Metaplanet led Anthropic's first round — [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/jaan-tallinn-would-like-us-to-survive). I left out quotes because the speaker was ambiguous.\n- **2026-09-29, The Generalist, Periodic Labs.** See the \"notebook\" takes above — [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/a-chatbot-walks-into-a-laboratory)\n- **2026-08-04, The Generalist, Eric Nguyen (Radical Numerics).** The page says the company raised $50M to train on biological data — [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/ai-got-good-at-language-now-its-learning)\n- **2026-06-30, The Generalist, Matan Grinberg (Factory).** On token budgets and open-weight models — [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/the-token-budget-problem)\n\n## How they think\n- **Science fiction as a source of investing edge.** Mario expects AI's next leg to be AI-for-science and physical-world labs.\n- **Lags matter.** They admitted underestimating the gap between discovery and usefulness.\n- **Founders matter more than markets.** Their long-form founder biographies (Saplings) mirror Hummingbird's view that the founder is everything.\n- **Compute costs.** Memory and DRAM costs act as a tax on anyone building compute without hyperscaler purchasing power.\n- **Contrarian lean.** Mario frames frontier capability gains as accruing to the best-resourced companies, which they call \"far from ideal\" ([src](https://www.generalist.com/p/a-portrait-of-eight-billion-people)).\n- **Calls right or wrong:** not enough sourced evidence to judge.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [The Best Venture Firm You've Never Heard Of (Hummingbird)](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird), 2023: the firm they later joined, plus the Nomads disclosure.\n2. [Periodic Labs: A chatbot walks into a laboratory](https://www.generalist.com/p/a-chatbot-walks-into-a-laboratory), 2026: their AI-for-science lens and the \"notebook\" takes.\n3. [Saplings: Unstable Ground](https://www.generalist.com/p/saplings-unstable-ground), 2026: their founder-formation lens.\n4. [A portrait of eight billion people (Simile)](https://www.generalist.com/p/a-portrait-of-eight-billion-people), 2026: their view on agents and on frontier concentration.\n5. [RAM Fever](https://www.generalist.com/p/generalist-intelligence-july-10), 2026: their compute-cost view.\n\n## Social engagement\n- On LinkedIn, Mario mostly promotes podcast episodes, such as those with Pete Florence, Joon Sung Park and Scott Morton.\n- They reposted Flapping Airplanes' Series A with personal commentary on Ben Spector ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/mariogabriele/)).\n- X was not verified (fetch blocked).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Privy (via Generalist Capital) | not found | not found (before 2023-12) | not found | Sequoia Capital, Paradigm, BlueYard, Pamir Gelenbe | [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Flapping Airplanes (Hummingbird's position; Mario's role unconfirmed) | Series A ($1B) | 2026-10-21 | no | Index, Lux (co-leads) and others | [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/mariogabriele/) |\n\nNot portfolio: Periodic Labs, Radical Numerics, Simile and Generalist AI are only interview subjects. No investment is disclosed for any of them.\n\n## Deal stories\n**Flapping Airplanes (2026).**\n- Mario says they sat down with CEO Benjamin Spector at the SF office \"earlier this year,\" that is, before the Series A ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/mariogabriele/)).\n- They describe Spector as having unfurled an \"expansive, original and detailed\" exploration of AI on a blackboard.\n- At the 2026-10-21 Series A, co-led by Index and Lux, Hummingbird appeared as a new backer. Mario wrote that Hummingbird is \"joining the Flappy story as part of this round.\"\n- Timing: on this evidence, Hummingbird was not in the seed (the repo's Hummingbird file also found no seed participation), and Mario's meeting came first.\n- Not confirmed: whether Mario sourced or led the ticket.\n\n**Nomads and Generalist Capital (2022).** Mario was introduced to Wouter Gort's team (Nomads) while raising Generalist Capital, and Nomads declined. Mario disclosed this in their own 2023 profile of the firm ([src](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hummingbird Ventures | Partner | not found | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) |\n| Generalist Capital, LLC | Founder/investor (their own vehicle) | 2022 (fundraise) | [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Nomads (Wouter Gort) | Prospective LP; declined | 2022 | [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Benjamin Spector (Flapping Airplanes) | Met in person before the Series A | 2026 | [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/mariogabriele/) |\n| Pamir Gelenbe | Co-investor in Privy | before 2023-12 | [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Liam Fedus, Dogus Cubuk (Periodic Labs) | Podcast guests | 2026-09 | [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/a-chatbot-walks-into-a-laboratory) |\n| Eric Nguyen (Radical Numerics) | Podcast guest | 2026-08 | [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/ai-got-good-at-language-now-its-learning) |\n| Joon Sung Park (Simile) | Podcast guest | 2026-11 | [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/a-portrait-of-eight-billion-people) |\n| Pete Florence (Generalist AI) | Podcast guest | 2026-10 | [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/pete-florence-when-robots-improvise) |\n| Jaan Tallinn | Podcast guest | 2026-10 | [src](https://www.generalist.com/p/jaan-tallinn-would-like-us-to-survive) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did Mario join Hummingbird, and do they have check authority or carry?\n- Did Mario source or champion the Flapping Airplanes ticket? Do they or Generalist Capital hold a personal stake?\n- What are Generalist Capital's size, LPs and holdings beyond Privy? Is it still investing now that Mario is a Hummingbird Partner?\n- Have any 2026 podcast guests (Periodic Labs, Radical Numerics, Simile, Generalist AI) since raised from Hummingbird?\n- What does Mario say on X and on outside podcasts? Search budget was exhausted, so neither was checked.\n- Who is \"Scott Morton\" (LinkedIn podcast promo)? Not identified.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.hummingbird.vc/team\n- https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio\n- https://www.generalist.com/about\n- https://www.generalist.com/archive\n- https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird\n- https://www.generalist.com/p/a-chatbot-walks-into-a-laboratory\n- https://www.generalist.com/p/ai-got-good-at-language-now-its-learning\n- https://www.generalist.com/p/a-portrait-of-eight-billion-people\n- https://www.generalist.com/p/pete-florence-when-robots-improvise\n- https://www.generalist.com/p/jaan-tallinn-would-like-us-to-survive\n- https://www.generalist.com/p/the-token-budget-problem\n- https://www.generalist.com/p/generalist-intelligence-july-10\n- https://www.generalist.com/p/saplings-unstable-ground\n- https://www.generalist.com/p/saplings-the-world-outside\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/mariogabriele/\n", "person:mark-kraynak": "\n# Mark Kraynak\n\n## One-line take\nAcrew's security partner: a long-time Imperva operator who co-led Protect AI's seed with Boldstart in 2022 and whose AI exposure is security-for-AI, not models.\n\n## Crux\nHe wins with security founders because he has built and sold security products for over a decade and brings a repeat co-lead partner in Boldstart. He is constrained to that lane: his AI deals are the security layer around AI (Protect AI, and probably Keycard). He is not an owner of the management company, so fund-level decisions sit with Gouw and Kolodny.\n\n## Differential insight\n- His firm bio lists the deals he \"led or co-led\": early stage Aembit, At-Bay, Casa, Moderne, Nextdata, Nokod Security, \"Protect AI\", Radiant Security, Silverfort; later stage Aqua Security, Cato Networks, Exabeam, Vanta ([src](https://www.acrewcapital.com/team-members/mark-kraynak)).\n- Protect AI seed ($13.5M, 2022-12-15): Acrew and Boldstart co-led and he joined the board with Ed Sim ([src](https://www.helpnetsecurity.com/2022/12/18/protect-ai-funding/)).\n- The pairing repeated at Keycard in 2025: a16z and Boldstart co-led the $8M seed and \"a $30 million Series A round was led by Acrew\" ([src](https://www.securityweek.com/keycard-emerges-from-stealth-mode-with-38-million-in-funding/)). The source does not name the Acrew partner.\n- Not an owner: the adviser's Schedule A lists only the Gouw and Kolodny trusts ([src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/307729/PDF/307729.pdf)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. Relevant where a frontier company needs a security-credible investor, and as the route to Boldstart's Ed Sim. Not a labs or compute investor.\n\n## Background\n- Imperva: nearly 13 years across marketing, product, corporate development and strategy ([src](https://www.acrewcapital.com/team-members/mark-kraynak); dates not given).\n- Aspect Ventures: entrepreneur in residence, then full-time investor from the end of 2017 (same bio).\n- Acrew Capital: founding partner at the 2019 launch ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/10/18/acrew-capital-hits-five-years-raises-700m)).\n- Advisor to Exabeam, Indegy, Kloudless, Trustar and Aqua Security (bio).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.acrewcapital.com/team-members/mark-kraynak | Firm bio |\n| Substack / blog | https://perspectives.acrewcapital.com | Co-author of the Savi and Horizon3 posts (2026) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/raknak | Not read |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/mark-kraynak/ | |\n| GitHub | none found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- RSA Conference speaker profile — [link](https://www.rsaconference.com/experts/mark-kraynak) — not read.\n- No podcast found in this pass.\n\n## How they think\nNothing first-hand was read. From the deals: back security infrastructure where a new platform (cloud, then AI models and agents) creates a new attack surface, and co-lead with a seed specialist.\n\n## Read these first\n- \"Why We Backed Savi\" (2026-07-07, with Lauren Kolodny): [link](https://perspectives.acrewcapital.com/p/why-we-backed-savi-ai-native-protection) (not mined).\n- \"Investing in Horizon3\" (2026-08-03): [link](https://perspectives.acrewcapital.com/p/investing-in-horizon3-hacking-the) (not mined).\n- His firm bio for the deal list.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Protect AI | Seed ($13.5M) | 2022-12-15 | co-led; board | Boldstart (co-lead); Knollwood Capital; Pelion Ventures; Aviso Ventures | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2022/12/15/protect-ai-lands-a-13-5m-investment-to-harden-ai-projects-from-attack) |\n| Protect AI | Series A ($35M) and Series B ($60M) | 2023-07-26; 2024-08-01 | no | see repo rounds ledger | [src](https://www.acrewcapital.com/team-members/mark-kraynak) |\n| Savi Security | Not stated | 2026-07-07 | unknown | not read | [src](https://perspectives.acrewcapital.com/p/why-we-backed-savi-ai-native-protection) |\n| Horizon3.ai | Series E ($250M) | 2026-08-03 | no | see repo rounds ledger | [src](https://perspectives.acrewcapital.com/p/investing-in-horizon3-hacking-the) |\n| Keycard (attribution unconfirmed) | Series A ($30M) | 2025-10-21 | Acrew led | a16z; Boldstart | [src](https://www.securityweek.com/keycard-emerges-from-stealth-mode-with-38-million-in-funding/) |\n| Silverfort, Aembit, At-Bay, Casa, Moderne, Nextdata, Nokod, Radiant Security, Aqua, Cato, Exabeam, Vanta | Led or co-led per bio; rounds not listed | | | | [src](https://www.acrewcapital.com/team-members/mark-kraynak) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Protect AI.** Founders came from AWS's and Oracle's AI teams; Acrew and Boldstart split the seed lead and both took board seats. How he met the founders was not found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ed Sim (Boldstart) | Co-lead and fellow board member at Protect AI; same firms in Keycard | 2022-12 | [src](https://www.helpnetsecurity.com/2022/12/18/protect-ai-funding/) |\n| Theresia Gouw | Colleague since Aspect Ventures | 2017 | [src](https://www.acrewcapital.com/team-members/mark-kraynak) |\n| Lauren Kolodny | Co-author on Savi | 2026 | [src](https://perspectives.acrewcapital.com/p/why-we-backed-savi-ai-native-protection) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is he the Acrew partner on Keycard?\n- How did the Protect AI relationship start?\n- Any podcast or essay with his own views on AI security: none read yet.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.acrewcapital.com/team-members/mark-kraynak\n- https://techcrunch.com/2022/12/15/protect-ai-lands-a-13-5m-investment-to-harden-ai-projects-from-attack\n- https://www.helpnetsecurity.com/2022/12/18/protect-ai-funding/ (via repo ledger and search summary)\n- https://www.securityweek.com/keycard-emerges-from-stealth-mode-with-38-million-in-funding/ (via search summary)\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/307729/PDF/307729.pdf\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/10/18/acrew-capital-hits-five-years-raises-700m\n", "person:mark-xu": "\n# Mark Xu\n\n## One-line take\nMark Xu is a Partner at Index who recently moved from growth investing at Lightspeed. Within months of joining he turned a Harvard friendship and the Prod founder network into Index's co-led seed and $1B A in Flapping Airplanes, and he holds that board seat. On my lens he is the person to know at Index for new labs.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** relationship lead time inside a young founder graph. He knew Asher Spector from Harvard and Ben Spector through Prod before the company existed. Index's multi-stage balance sheet then let him lead the seed and re-lead the A in-house [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/), [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/).\n- **What constrains him:**\n  - He works as a pair with Shardul Shah, the senior partner. Shah co-wrote every post Xu appears on, and Shah alone wrote the Enigma and Simile A posts.\n  - Outside Flapping, his independent sourcing is not shown in any source.\n  - He is neither a founding nor a managing partner. His join date is not published.\n  - His background is growth investing (Wiz, Glean, Grafana), so his early-stage record is about one year old [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/mark-xu/).\n- **What he would not do (inference):** pass up re-leading his own winners. Index's pattern on his deals is \"lead once, then double down\" (Flapping, Simile), which leaves outsiders little later-round allocation.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Join timing is the hidden story.** The Flapping process began \"Not long after Mark joined Index, Shardul was catching up with Chris Ré\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/) (2026-01-28). So he joined before about January 2026. He is also tagged on the 2025-10-28 Fireworks post, but that tag may postdate the deal, so the date cannot be narrowed further. Either way, his first big early-stage lead came soon after he moved from a growth seat.\n- **Two independent paths to the same founders:**\n  - Asher Spector: \"Mark has known Asher since they were students at Harvard\".\n  - Ben Spector: Xu \"built a close relationship with Ben through the Prod community\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/).\n  - Index credits Prod with shaping \"companies like Cursor, Mercor, Etched, Decart, and others\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/). Xu sits in that founder graph.\n- **How he signals conviction.** The seed post quotes a text Xu sent Shah: \"I can physically feel my excitement.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/). This is the only first-person Xu quote I found. It reads as founder-gut conviction, not price discipline.\n- **The board seat went to the junior partner.** Index will \"lead their $1B million Series A, with Mark joining the board\" ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/), 2026-10-21; \"$1B million\" appears that way on the page). Shah did not take the seat, so Index is backing Xu as the lab's long-term relationship owner. Lux co-led the A but goes unmentioned in Index's post.\n- **His thesis on Flapping is data efficiency, not scale.** Index believes \"today's models could be orders of magnitude more data-efficient than they are now\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/).\n- **Hone: tracking talent inside hot AI companies, plus an application-layer thesis.**\n  - Origin: \"a friend introduced us to Moritz Stephan\", who was then at Cognition, about a year before the investment.\n  - Thesis: value goes to \"whoever owns the layer between the model and the enterprise\" ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-owns-business-outcomes-our-investment-in-hone/), 2026-10-08).\n- **Simile: Xu was not first.**\n  - A* co-led the seed [src](https://www.a-star.co/news/simile).\n  - Index then led the $100M A [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/life-the-universe-and-simile-leading-similes-series-a/).\n  - Index re-upped in the $200M B: \"We first led Simile's Series A earlier this year\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/simulating-society-at-scale-our-investment-in-similes-200m-series-b/).\n  - Greenoaks led the B [src](https://www.a-star.co/news/simile).\n- **Podcasts, Substack, X: nothing found.** X (@marklxu1) returned a paywall error. I found no podcast or Substack, and the web search budget ran out before a full sweep (see Open questions).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is the board member on the year's largest new-lab A, Flapping Airplanes ($180M seed, then $1B A at a reported ~$6B) ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/29/flapping-airplanes-and-the-promise-of-research-driven-ai/), [src](https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/)). That is exactly the large seed round where a ~$20M check sits. Hone is AI-native automation in the Serval mould. His Prod and Harvard network is a live channel to young research founders.\n\nWatch-outs:\n- He re-leads in-house, so later-round allocation for outsiders is thin.\n- His book is Shah-anchored, so co-investing likely runs through Shah too.\n- Enigma (security, human-machine interaction) and Instinct (assistant) are less core to my thesis.\n\n## Background\n- Played violin at Juilliard before college [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/mark-xu/). Dates not found.\n- Harvard: studied mathematics, economics and computer science [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/mark-xu/). Graduated cum laude per LinkedIn [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/marklxu/). Class year not found.\n- Lightspeed: growth investor who supported Wiz, Glean and Grafana [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/mark-xu/). Dates not found.\n- Index Ventures: Partner, New York. Focus is early- through late-stage cybersecurity, infrastructure and AI. Join date was not published; the seed post implies he joined before about January 2026 [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/mark-xu/ | Index bio, fetched 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | Index Perspectives posts (co-authored) | Flapping seed (2026-01-28), Flapping A (2026-10-21), Hone (2026-10-08), Ben Spector interview (2026-10-28) |\n| X | https://x.com/marklxu1 | Linked from Index bio; content not retrievable (HTTP 402) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/marklxu/ | Thin public profile, no dates |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-10 — Index Perspectives (video) — \"Catching up with Ben Spector, CEO and co-founder of Flapping Airplanes\" — https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/catching-up-with-ben-spector-ceo-and-co-founder-of-flapping-airplanes/ — Xu is the interviewer. The page has no transcript and the video was not viewable, so none of Xu's own words are recorded here.\n- No external podcasts, panels or talks found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Research labs on efficiency:** backs the idea that AI can be made far more data- and compute-efficient (Flapping) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/).\n- **The application layer owns outcomes:** agents that own a business result beat tools inside human workflows (Hone) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-owns-business-outcomes-our-investment-in-hone/).\n- **Founder-first, gut conviction:** his only quoted line is about physically feeling excitement. Index's bio says he looks for technical founders with deep domain expertise, hustle and customer obsession [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/mark-xu/).\n- **Calls so far:** Flapping went from ~$1.5B to a reported ~$6B in about nine months [src](https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/). Simile went from the A to a $2B B [src](https://www.a-star.co/news/simile). It is too early for outcomes.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Taking Flight: Our Investment in Flapping Airplanes](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/): the deal origin, the Harvard and Prod paths, and his conviction text.\n2. [Toward More Efficient AI: Leading Flapping Airplanes' $1B Series A](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/): the re-lead and his board seat.\n3. [AI That Owns Business Outcomes: Our Investment in Hone](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-owns-business-outcomes-our-investment-in-hone/): his application-layer thesis.\n4. [Catching up with Ben Spector](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/catching-up-with-ben-spector-ceo-and-co-founder-of-flapping-airplanes/): his interview style (video only).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X was not retrievable and the web search budget was exhausted before amplification patterns could be checked.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Flapping Airplanes | Seed $180M | 2026-01-28 | Co-led (Index, GV, Sequoia) | GV, Sequoia | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/), [size](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/29/flapping-airplanes-and-the-promise-of-research-driven-ai/) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Series A $1B (board) | 2026-10-21 | Co-led (Index, Lux) | Lux, Sequoia, GV, Kleiner, NVIDIA and others | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/), [src](https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/) |\n| Simile | Series A $100M | 2026-02-12 | Index led (Shah byline; Xu tagged) | not named | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/life-the-universe-and-simile-leading-similes-series-a/) |\n| Simile | Series B $200M | 2026-07-30 | No (Greenoaks led) | Greenoaks, A* | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/simulating-society-at-scale-our-investment-in-similes-200m-series-b/), [src](https://www.a-star.co/news/simile) |\n| Hone | undisclosed | 2026-10-08 | not stated | Benchmark | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-owns-business-outcomes-our-investment-in-hone/) |\n| Enigma | Seed $71M | 2026-07-27 | Index led (Shah byline; Xu tagged) | Ribbit (Micky Malka) | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/) |\n| Instinct | unknown | unknown | unknown | unknown | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/instinct/) |\n| Fireworks AI | Series C $250M (Xu tagged; Azam led) | 2025-10-28 | Index co-led | Lightspeed, Evantic | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/) |\n| Parallel | unclear which \"Parallel\" | | | | [bio](https://www.indexventures.com/team/mark-xu/) (needs-id) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Flapping Airplanes.** Xu had known Asher Spector since Harvard and Ben Spector through Prod. Shortly after Xu joined Index, Shah met Ben in Chris Ré's office and called Xu right away to rave about him [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/).\n  - Index co-led the $180M seed with GV and Sequoia (2026-01-28).\n  - Index was not the first institutional investor: Sequoia had partnered in 2025, per `companies/flapping-airplanes.md`.\n  - About nine months later, Index re-led the $1B A with Lux and Xu joined the board [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/).\n  - A week later Xu interviewed CEO Ben Spector for Index's channel.\n- **Hone.** A friend introduced Index to Moritz Stephan at Cognition about a year before the post. Index invested alongside Benchmark. Shah and Xu co-wrote the post [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-owns-business-outcomes-our-investment-in-hone/).\n- **Simile.** A* co-led the seed. Index led the $100M A (2026-02-12), then doubled down in the $200M B (2026-07-30) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/simulating-society-at-scale-our-investment-in-similes-200m-series-b/). Xu's personal role in sourcing is not described.\n- **Enigma.** Shah's handshake at 2 a.m. before incorporation. Xu is only tagged [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Asher Spector | Harvard schoolmate; Flapping co-founder | college | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/) |\n| Ben Spector | Close relationship via Prod; Flapping CEO; Xu on his board | pre-2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/) |\n| Shardul Shah | Senior deal partner at Index | ~2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/) |\n| Danny Rimer | Co-team on Instinct | | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/instinct/) |\n| Sahir Azam | Co-listed on Fireworks | 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/) |\n| Juriaan Duizendstraal | Co-tagged on Enigma | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/) |\n| Lightspeed | Former employer (growth) | | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/mark-xu/) |\n| Moritz Stephan | Hone founder (via a friend's intro) | ~2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-owns-business-outcomes-our-investment-in-hone/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact Index join date and Lightspeed tenure, and whether he was promoted to Partner or hired as one.\n- Which \"Parallel\" is on his bio: Parallel Web Systems (Shah) or Index's hospital-admin Parallel (Julia Andre)?\n- His personal role on Simile, Enigma and Fireworks (tagged versus sourced).\n- Instinct round, date and stage. Is Noah Shinn the Reflexion-paper author?\n- X posts and any podcast appearances: the web search budget ran out this turn, so rerun a search for \"Mark Xu\" Index podcast/X.\n- Other Prod alumni he is close to (Etched, Decart, Mercor founders) as forward deal flow.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/mark-xu/\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/marklxu/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/catching-up-with-ben-spector-ceo-and-co-founder-of-flapping-airplanes/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-owns-business-outcomes-our-investment-in-hone/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/hone/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/simile/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/life-the-universe-and-simile-leading-similes-series-a/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/simulating-society-at-scale-our-investment-in-similes-200m-series-b/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/instinct/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/29/flapping-airplanes-and-the-promise-of-research-driven-ai/\n- https://techfundingnews.com/flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-6b-from-index-and-lux-to-make-every-ai-chip-work-harder/\n- https://www.a-star.co/news/simile\n- https://parallel.ai/blog/series-b\n", "person:martin-casado": "\n# Martin Casado\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the a16z GP who decides frontier-AI infra deals at both ends of the size range. In 2026 alone he led seeds into agent infrastructure (Runta, QuiverAI, Shizuku AI) and took the board seat on TypeSafe AI's $870M Series A. He also decides how much autonomy the partners under him get.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He is an ex-founder-technologist: he co-founded Nicira (sold to VMware) and helped pioneer software-defined networking at Stanford ([src](https://a16z.com/author/martin-casado/)). Researchers come to him because of long personal relationships. He has known Fei-Fei Li \"for nearly two decades\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-world-labs/)), backed Diogo Almeida at TypeSafe after years as a fan ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-typesafe-ai/)), and re-backed the Parsec founders at Phaze ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-phaze/)).\n- **What he believes.** His view is \"bitter economics\": AI turns capital into product, so the biggest checks go to the teams that can absorb compute ([src](https://a16z.com/bitter-economics/), 2025-11-19). That is how he can justify a $7.5B-valuation Series A for a model company.\n- **What constrains him.** He is rarely the first check on the marquee deals. The OpenAI Startup Fund led Cursor's seed ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/in-just-4-months-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-raised-another-100m-at-a-2-5b-valuation-led-by-thrive-sources-say)), DCVC was already in TypeSafe ([src](https://typesafe.ai/blog/series-ai)), and NEA appears to have led a World Labs round ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/13/fei-fei-lis-world-labs-comes-out-of-stealth-with-230m-in-funding/)). His edge is leading the first big priced round.\n- **What he would never do.** He calls himself a \"productive\" investor, not a \"financial\" one, and warns against the \"grab bag trap\" of switching philosophies to argue for or against a deal ([src](https://a16z.com/the-evolving-landscape-of-venture-capital-mindsets/), 2026-10-28). He would not reject an AI app on gross margin alone ([src](https://a16z.com/questioning-margins-is-a-boring-cliche/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Partner autonomy is a GP gate.** On Jennifer Li's 2024 promotion: \"Promoting Jennifer to general partner means that much more autonomy in deal making\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/19/andreessen-horowitz-jennifer-li-125-billion-dollar-infrastructure-fund/), 2024-04-19). Read: non-GP partners on Infra need GP sponsorship. When working with Yoko Li or Marco Mascorro, the real decision runs through Casado or Jennifer Li. The tension: in public he frames the GP title as what unlocks autonomy, but on 20VC (2022) he said \"Junior Partners have a lot of pull\" and that he gets credit for deals his team drove ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1FWa0A0k8Ww), ~40:39). Read: a junior Infra partner can champion a deal, but a GP signs it.\n- **World Labs came from a 20-year relationship, not a process.** \"We've known World Labs cofounder Fei-Fei Li for nearly two decades.\" Li came to them with the world-model idea ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-world-labs/), 2024-09-13).\n- **TypeSafe was a long courtship of the founder.** \"We've long been fans of Diogo, the founder and CEO of TypeSafe\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-typesafe-ai/), 2026-10-09). Marc Andreessen and Ben Horowitz co-signed the post, which is unusual and signals a firm-level bet. Casado took the board seat ([src](https://typesafe.ai/blog/series-ai)).\n- **Repeat founders.** On Phaze, written solo: \"I'm very excited to be working with this team again\". a16z had backed Parsec and sat on its board until Unity bought it ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-phaze/), 2026-11-04).\n- **His framework for first checks.** \"nearly all first money in checks are frontier investing\", and he says he is a \"productive\" investor ([src](https://a16z.com/the-evolving-landscape-of-venture-capital-mindsets/), 2026-10-28).\n- **Why AI absorbs money.** AI systems \"become more powerful the more data and money you pour into them\". He also argues AI fits problems \"hard to specify but possible to verify\" ([src](https://a16z.com/bitter-economics/), 2025-11-19).\n- **On margin bears** (co-written with Sarah Wang): \"Being a pessimist makes you sound smart. Being an optimist makes you money.\" ([src](https://a16z.com/questioning-margins-is-a-boring-cliche/), 2025-08-21)\n- **A 2026 theme across three seeds and an A: agents need a whole computer.** Runta (seed, 2026-07-16): \"AI agents just want a computer\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-runta/)). Phaze (2026-11-04) is remote desktops for agents ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-phaze/)). doxxnet (Series A, 2026-10-01) is a private network for people and their agents ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-doxxnet/)). Relace (Series A, 2025-10-08) builds small models for coding agents ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-relace/)). Founders building agent runtimes should start here.\n- **Cursor timing.** TechCrunch corrected its story: \"That round was led by a16z.\" The $60M Series A was at $400M, with Thrive participating. The OpenAI Startup Fund had led the seed, apparently in 2023 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/in-just-4-months-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-raised-another-100m-at-a-2-5b-valuation-led-by-thrive-sources-say)). Casado is quoted as an Anysphere board member: \"We've never seen a company achieve such broad and rapid adoption\" ([src](https://www.maginative.com/article/anysphere-raises-105m-for-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-reaching-2-5b-valuation/), 2025-01-14). His August 2026 post with Wang and Bornstein says Michael Truell sat in a16z's office in June 2024 making the enterprise case, about two months before the A ([src](https://a16z.com/cursor-spacexai-the-fastest-iterating-team-wins/)).\n- **Hardware money.** The $1.1B Machine Age Fund (2026-08-28) will \"invest into all of the computer infrastructure on which AI runs\": chips, memory, networking and power. Casado is a co-author ([src](https://a16z.com/the-machine-age-fund/)). This is now the a16z pool most relevant to AI semis.\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-10)\nDrawn from podcast transcripts (YouTube auto-captions via Rosetta) and one Substack interview. Full catalogue: `data/rows/martin-casado-voice-media.csv` (162 items).\n- **How he picks.** He says judging one company alone is \"underdetermined\". Instead he studies a whole space and picks the best of the cohort: \"if I meet five companies I can probably tell you which one\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1FWa0A0k8Ww), 20VC, 2022-12-06, ~20:16). A second-time founder is close to an automatic yes.\n- **His one sin.** Backing a space that fails is fine. Backing the wrong company in a space that works is not ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v0-7wlTdGck), 20VC, 2025-07-28, ~1:00:34). He says he often waits until the winner is clear: \"often we wait for that reason\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4XgSfhj-LQU), 2025-09-04, ~44:02). This explains why he is rarely the first check.\n- **His seed rule.** He backs the person who did the thing at a big company and is one of about five world experts in a very technical niche ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4XgSfhj-LQU)).\n- **On price.** In 2022 he said that in competitive deals he is \"almost always on the low end\" on price ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1FWa0A0k8Ww), ~17:53). By 2025 he said he had thrown away price and TAM thinking for AI: \"the market is the market\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4XgSfhj-LQU)).\n- **Who really does the deal.** \"Junior Partners have a lot of pull.\" He also says he is often credited for deals his team drove ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1FWa0A0k8Ww), ~40:39 and ~49:44). So the Infra partner who works with a founder day to day may be the real champion.\n- **Space selection comes from Chris Dixon.** Go where smart founders are going, and ignore what VCs and Twitter think ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1FWa0A0k8Ww), ~22:31).\n- **Model economics.** He sees small, unsubsidised model labs as \"wonderful businesses\": ElevenLabs, Midjourney, Black Forest Labs and Ideogram. He sees frontier language models as a high-stakes game where only the leaders keep their capital ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v0-7wlTdGck), ~9:44).\n- **Mistakes he admits.**\n  - No bet in cloud security posture management, which missed Wiz, Lacework and Orca ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1FWa0A0k8Ww), ~1:01:41).\n  - Several bets across data streaming that failed ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v0-7wlTdGck)).\n  - In a16z's early years, junior partners churned every two to three years ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1FWa0A0k8Ww)).\n- **Origin of the a16z tie.** He first met Ben Horowitz by trying to hire him as Nicira's CEO ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1FWa0A0k8Ww), ~45:26). Andreessen and Horowitz then backed Nicira in the 2008 downturn \"before the fund even existed\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hiwGlzUof-4)).\n- **Conflicts.** a16z backs its existing portfolio company over a new AI entrant in the same space, even when the incumbent looks weak ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4XgSfhj-LQU), ~19:49). Founders in a16z-adjacent spaces should expect to be blocked.\n- **Who he rates.**\n  - Ram Shriram (Sherpalo) is his \"most underrated angel\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1FWa0A0k8Ww), ~1:02:47).\n  - Michael Truell is the one founder he would fund in any category ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v0-7wlTdGck), ~1:05:40).\n- **Board load.** He was on 17 boards in 2020 ([src](https://systemsapproach.substack.com/p/a-conversation-with-sdn-pioneer-martin-casado), 2020-10-20).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He decides at a16z on the categories in THESIS.md:\n- **New labs and model companies:** World Labs; TypeSafe AI ($870M Series A at $7.5B).\n- **Researcher-led seeds he led:** QuiverAI, Runta, Shizuku AI, Phota Labs.\n- **Machine Age Fund:** AI chips and compute.\n- **AI-native automation infra:** Relace, Deeptune, Braintrust.\n\n**Caveats:**\n- He leads with large ownership-heavy checks. On mega-rounds you will co-invest beside him, not alongside his lead.\n- He is often not first: OpenAI Startup Fund, DCVC and NEA came before him on his three biggest names. The angle is to be the earlier check that he later marks up, or to bring him researcher-founders he already respects.\n\n## Background\n- Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory, then a PhD and Master's in CS at Stanford, where he helped pioneer software-defined networking ([src](https://a16z.com/author/martin-casado/)).\n- Co-founded Nicira, which VMware acquired. Then ran VMware's networking and security business unit ([src](https://a16z.com/author/martin-casado/)).\n- Joined a16z in 2016. He is now the GP leading the infrastructure practice and sits on many boards ([src](https://a16z.com/author/martin-casado/)). Managed the $1.25B Infra fund per TechCrunch, 2024-04-19 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/19/andreessen-horowitz-jennifer-li-125-billion-dollar-infrastructure-fund/)).\n- Won the ACM Grace Murray Hopper Award ([src](https://a16z.com/author/martin-casado/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/martin-casado/ | Investment posts and essays, several a month in 2026 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found beyond a16z.com | |\n| X | https://x.com/martin_casado | Follower count not retrieved (x.com returned 402 to fetch, 2026-11-10) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/martincasado | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | a16z podcast (frequent guest and co-host) | Many episodes listed on the author page |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNo transcripts were available for the items below. Transcribed items are covered in \"In their own words\" above, and the full catalogue is in `data/rows/martin-casado-voice-media.csv`.\n- 2025-09-04: a16z, \"Is Non-Consensus Investing Overrated?\", with Erik Torenberg and Leo Polovets (Humba Ventures). [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/is-non-consensus-investing-overrated/)\n- Date not retrieved: \"Jack Altman & Martin Casado on the Future of VC\". Per the summary, it covers a16z moving from generalists to specialized platforms. [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/jack-altman-martin-casado-on-the-future-of-vc/)\n- 2026-08-27: \"Inside Cursor: The Anatomy of a Generational Startup\", with Bornstein and Wang. [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/inside-cursor-the-anatomy-of-a-generational-startup/)\n- 2026-08-28: \"The Infrastructure Behind the Machine Age\", with Ben Horowitz and Raghu Raghuram. [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/the-infrastructure-behind-the-machine-age/)\n- Several Fei-Fei Li episodes on world models and spatial intelligence. [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/fei-fei-li-the-race-to-build-world-models-for-ai/)\n- Also: \"Why This Isn't the Dot-Com Bubble\" (WSJ Bold Names) and \"Monopolies vs Oligopolies in AI\". Both are listed on the [author page](https://a16z.com/author/martin-casado/).\n\n## How they think\n- **Bitter economics.** Compute plus data turns capital into product, so AI companies raise more money earlier and burn more ([src](https://a16z.com/bitter-economics/)).\n- **Productive, not financial, investing.** Early-stage tech creates markets, and business models trail the technology ([src](https://a16z.com/the-evolving-landscape-of-venture-capital-mindsets/)).\n- **Contrarian on margins.** Low gross margins in AI apps are usually fixable through routing, pricing tiers and falling inference costs ([src](https://a16z.com/questioning-margins-is-a-boring-cliche/)).\n- **Open-source and AI-policy advocate.** \"Keep the Code Behind AI Open\" in The Economist, and \"Base AI Policy on Evidence, Not Existential Angst\" ([src](https://a16z.com/author/martin-casado/)).\n- **Calls that look right so far:**\n  - Cursor: led the A at $400M (2024). SpaceX later agreed to a $60B all-stock acquisition ([src](https://a16z.com/cursor-spacexai-the-fastest-iterating-team-wins/)).\n  - World Labs: listed backers include Nvidia, AMD and Temasek ([src](https://www.worldlabs.ai/about)).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Bitter Economics](https://a16z.com/bitter-economics/) (2025-11-19): why he will fund huge rounds for AI teams.\n- [The Evolving Landscape of VC Mindsets](https://a16z.com/the-evolving-landscape-of-venture-capital-mindsets/) (2026-10-28): his own frameworks for deciding.\n- [Questioning Margins is a Boring Cliché](https://a16z.com/questioning-margins-is-a-boring-cliche/) (2025-08-21): how he underwrites AI apps.\n- [The Machine Age Fund](https://a16z.com/the-machine-age-fund/) (2026-08-28): the hardware and compute mandate.\n- [Cursor + SpaceXAI](https://a16z.com/cursor-spacexai-the-fastest-iterating-team-wins/) (2026-08-14): their post-mortem on their biggest win.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot retrieved. x.com blocked fetches (HTTP 402) and web search budget was exhausted this run. On the a16z podcast he appears most often with Sarah Wang, Matt Bornstein, Steven Sinofsky, Aaron Levie and Fei-Fei Li ([src](https://a16z.com/author/martin-casado/)).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ideogram | Series A | 2024-02-28 | yes (a16z) | a16z also in seed (per post); co-signer Jennifer Li | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-ideogram/) |\n| Cursor (Anysphere) | Series A, $60M at $400M | 2024-08-22 | yes (a16z) | Thrive, OpenAI, Jeff Dean, Noam Brown; co-signers Bornstein, Mascorro, Radovanovic | [src](https://cursor.com/blog/series-a), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/in-just-4-months-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-raised-another-100m-at-a-2-5b-valuation-led-by-thrive-sources-say) |\n| World Labs | undisclosed; part of $230M across two rounds | 2024-09-13 | unknown | NEA, Radical Ventures; co-signer Sarah Wang | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-world-labs/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/13/fei-fei-lis-world-labs-comes-out-of-stealth-with-230m-in-funding/) |\n| Braintrust | Series A | 2024-10-08 | yes (a16z) | Greylock, Elad Gil | [src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/announcing-series-a), [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-braintrust/) |\n| WaveForms AI | undisclosed | 2024-12-09 | unknown | co-signers Justine Moore, Marco Mascorro | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-waveforms-ai/) |\n| Cursor (Anysphere) | $105M at $2.5B | 2025-01-14 | no (Thrive led) | Thrive, Benchmark; Casado named board member | [src](https://www.maginative.com/article/anysphere-raises-105m-for-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-reaching-2-5b-valuation/) |\n| Phota Labs | Seed | 2025-09-16 | yes (a16z) | co-signers Yoko Li, Jennifer Li | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-phota-labs/) |\n| Relace | Series A, $23M | 2025-10-08 | yes (a16z, board) | co-signers Yoko Li, Guido Appenzeller | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-relace/) |\n| Shizuku AI | Seed | 2026-02-09 | yes (a16z) | co-signers Justine Moore, Marco Mascorro | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-shizuku-ai/) |\n| QuiverAI | Seed | 2026-02-25 | yes (a16z) | co-signers Yoko Li, Guido Appenzeller | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-quiverai/) |\n| Deeptune | Series A | 2026-03-19 | yes (a16z) | co-signer Marco Mascorro | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-deeptune/) |\n| Runta | Seed | 2026-07-16 | yes (a16z) | co-signers Yoko Li, Guido Appenzeller | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-runta/) |\n| doxxnet | Series A | 2026-10-01 | yes (a16z) | co-signer Joel de la Garza | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-doxxnet/) |\n| TypeSafe AI | Series A, $870M at $7.5B | 2026-10-09 | yes (a16z); Casado board | Sequoia, DCVC (existing); co-signers Jennifer Li, Sarah Wang, Marc Andreessen, Ben Horowitz | [src](https://typesafe.ai/blog/series-ai), [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-typesafe-ai/) |\n| Tutor Intelligence | Series B | 2026-11-02 | yes (a16z) | co-signers Raghu Raghuram, Jamie Sullivan | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-tutor-intelligence/) |\n| Phaze | undisclosed | 2026-11-04 | unknown | solo post; founders previously backed at Parsec | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-phaze/) |\n\nAlso on his author page, not researched this run: Resend, Tigris, Distributional ([src](https://a16z.com/author/martin-casado/)).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **World Labs.**\n  - Origin: Fei-Fei Li had advised a16z on AI several times, then came to the firm with the world-model idea ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-world-labs/)).\n  - Timing: TechCrunch says the $230M came in \"two rounds spaced a couple of months apart\". It links an August story whose URL describes an NEA-led $100M round ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/13/fei-fei-lis-world-labs-comes-out-of-stealth-with-230m-in-funding/)). Whether a16z led the first round is unconfirmed.\n- **Cursor.**\n  - First check: the OpenAI Startup Fund led the seed, with Neo, Patrick Collison, Nat Friedman and Arash Ferdowsi ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/in-just-4-months-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-raised-another-100m-at-a-2-5b-valuation-led-by-thrive-sources-say)).\n  - The a16z lead: Truell pitched enterprise adoption from a16z's office in June 2024 ([src](https://a16z.com/cursor-spacexai-the-fastest-iterating-team-wins/)), and a16z led the A in August.\n  - After the A: Thrive led the next round about 4–5 months later, and Casado held the board seat.\n  - Exit: SpaceX agreed to a $60B all-stock deal in June 2026 ([src](https://a16z.com/cursor-spacexai-the-fastest-iterating-team-wins/)).\n- **TypeSafe AI.**\n  - Origin: a long relationship with founder Diogo Almeida, and \"philosophically aligned\" from the first conversation ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-typesafe-ai/)).\n  - Not first in: DCVC was already an investor ([src](https://typesafe.ai/blog/series-ai)).\n  - Why it matters: a $870M Series A is a lab-scale first priced round, the kind of mega-early round THESIS.md targets.\n- **Phaze.** A repeat bet on Benjy Boxer and Chris Dickson after Parsec, which a16z backed until Unity acquired it ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-phaze/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fei-Fei Li (World Labs) | Known for about 20 years; advisor to a16z; portfolio founder | ~2004 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-world-labs/) |\n| Sarah Wang (a16z) | Co-signer on World Labs and TypeSafe; co-author on margins and Cursor posts | 2024 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-world-labs/) |\n| Jennifer Li (a16z) | Fellow Infra GP; Casado framed her GP promotion | 2024 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/19/andreessen-horowitz-jennifer-li-125-billion-dollar-infrastructure-fund/) |\n| Yoko Li, Guido Appenzeller (a16z) | Co-signers on Relace, QuiverAI and Runta | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-runta/) |\n| Marco Mascorro, Matt Bornstein (a16z) | Co-signers on Cursor, Deeptune, Shizuku and WaveForms | 2024 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/) |\n| Thrive Capital | Co-investor and alternating lead on Cursor | 2024 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/in-just-4-months-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-raised-another-100m-at-a-2-5b-valuation-led-by-thrive-sources-say) |\n| Diogo Almeida (TypeSafe AI) | Board member; long-time fan | 2026 | [src](https://typesafe.ai/blog/series-ai) |\n| Benjy Boxer, Chris Dickson (Phaze, ex-Parsec) | Repeat founders | Parsec era | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-phaze/) |\n| Ben Horowitz, Raghu Raghuram, David Ulevitch, David George | Machine Age Fund co-authors | 2026-08 | [src](https://a16z.com/the-machine-age-fund/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did a16z lead the first World Labs round, or did NEA? The Casado post doesn't say.\n- How big are Machine Age Fund checks, and who runs seed chip deals day to day?\n- How did a16z first meet Cursor? The SpaceXAI post shows a June 2024 meeting but not the intro path.\n- When did DCVC get into TypeSafe, and how big was its seed?\n- What are his X habits and who does he amplify? Not retrieved (x.com 402; web search budget exhausted this run).\n- What was his title on joining in 2016? Not confirmed.\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/martin-casado/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-world-labs/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-typesafe-ai/\n- https://typesafe.ai/blog/series-ai\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/\n- https://cursor.com/blog/series-a\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/in-just-4-months-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-raised-another-100m-at-a-2-5b-valuation-led-by-thrive-sources-say\n- https://www.maginative.com/article/anysphere-raises-105m-for-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-reaching-2-5b-valuation/\n- https://a16z.com/cursor-spacexai-the-fastest-iterating-team-wins/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-phaze/\n- https://a16z.com/the-machine-age-fund/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/19/andreessen-horowitz-jennifer-li-125-billion-dollar-infrastructure-fund/\n- https://a16z.com/the-evolving-landscape-of-venture-capital-mindsets/\n- https://a16z.com/bitter-economics/\n- https://a16z.com/questioning-margins-is-a-boring-cliche/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-runta/, investing-in-quiverai/, investing-in-shizuku-ai/, investing-in-phota-labs/, investing-in-relace/, investing-in-deeptune/, investing-in-doxxnet/, investing-in-tutor-intelligence/, investing-in-waveforms-ai/, investing-in-ideogram/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/13/fei-fei-lis-world-labs-comes-out-of-stealth-with-230m-in-funding/\n- https://www.worldlabs.ai/about\n- https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/announcing-series-a\n", "person:martin-mignot": "\n# Martin Mignot\n\n## One-line take\nA 16-year Index veteran (Revolut, Deliveroo) who is early only where they already have a personal relationship with the founder. In AI that means seed-leading AI-native automation (Beside, Build), while on compute (Phaidra) and AI growth rounds (Decagon, Crosby) they came in late or as the second name. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/martin-mignot/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/beside/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** long founder relationships turned into first checks. Deliveroo's CEO says \"Martin was the first investor to believe in us,\" and Bloomberg credits them with driving Index's Revolut stake. On Beside they had known the founder since 2012 and wrote a personal pre-seed check before Index led the seed. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/martin-mignot/) [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/beside/)\n- **What they back in AI:** \"AI- and automation-driven products that improve with use\" (team page, paraphrased) in old industries: small local businesses (Beside), commercial real estate development (Build), data-center cooling (Phaidra). Not research labs.\n- **What constrains them:** they're a generalist with healthcare, fintech and AI on their plate. Where they have no prior relationship (Phaidra, Crosby, Decagon), they enter after others have priced the risk. Index's fund-size discipline (see [danny-rimer](danny-rimer.md)) also caps the lead role on very large rounds.\n- **What they'd never do (inferred, not stated):** be first into a pre-training lab without an existing personal tie to the founders. Nothing in their record shows a lab or chip deal.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Beside was a 2012 relationship, not a 2025 deal.** They met founder Maxime Germain in 2012 while spending a month a year working from the TriBeCa loft of their friend Kenneth Schlenker's startup Gertrude. Germain pitched them the Beside idea in 2023. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/beside/)\n- **Personal money before fund money:** \"I invested personally in Beside's pre-seed round\". Index then led the seed. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/beside/)\n- **Phaidra was a late entry, not a seed bet.** Phaidra's own news page lists a $25M round (2022-07), a Helena-led Series A (2023-04), then Index's reported $12M (2024-07), then a $50M+ B led by Collaborative Fund (2025-10) with Index participating. So Index came in about two years after the first institutional money. Helena's own page says it was the largest investor in a $25M Series A and began in 2022, which conflicts with the 2023-04 date; I left both unreconciled. [src](https://www.phaidra.ai/news) [src](https://www.helena.org/projects/phaidra/) [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/collaborative-fund-leads-phaidras-50m-series-b-to-build-the-ai-factories-of-the-future-302572418.html)\n- **Compute thesis in their own words:** \"Compute is king.\" They frame energy as the cost of scaling compute. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/compute-is-king-our-investment-in-phaidra/) (firm site; not hidden)\n- **Why AI-native automation:** \"Deterministic software breaks down at the edges\" (on Build, a human-plus-agent real estate development firm). [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/automating-the-built-world-our-partnership-with-build/)\n- **Founder filter:** \"The best founders today live and breathe at the frontier\". [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/beside/)\n- **The senior/junior pairing runs both ways.** On Crosby (Jahanvi Sardana's byline) and Decagon (Sofia Dolfe's byline), Mignot is the tagged second name. On Oratomic, Mignot holds the byline and Bastian Hasslinger and Simon Pastor are tagged under them. A company-page listing is therefore not evidence that they sourced the deal. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/investing-in-decagon-rigor-speed-and-a-new-concierge-model-for-customer-experience/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/making-quantum-computing-real-our-investment-in-oratomic/)\n- **Succession:** Bloomberg (via Bloomberg Línea, in Spanish) reports that Danny Rimer named Mignot with Shardul Shah, Nina Achadjian and Jan Hammer as the next generation of leaders. It says Mignot joined in 2010 alongside Hammer and is close to Rimer. [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/)\n\nGap: no podcast transcript, Substack or X posts could be read. X returned an error, and this pass's search budget was exhausted. Everything above comes from the firm site, company sites or press.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Build (seed lead, 2026-06) and Beside (personal pre-seed, then Index-led seed) are AI-native automation at seed, which `THESIS.md` names directly. Mignot is the Index contact for that category in New York. Phaidra is compute-adjacent but was a late round. Anthropic is a company-page listing with no stated round. Oratomic is quantum, not AI, though it is a frontier-hardware co-lead alongside ARCH, Khosla, Spark, GC and Bezos Expeditions [src](https://thequantuminsider.com/2026/10/08/oratomic-secures-775-million-fault-tolerant-quantum-computers). That shows Index will join large deep-tech syndicates. They have no lab, pre-training or AI-chip deal, so this isn't high. Useful as a co-investor on AI-native vertical automation seeds in NYC and for Europe-to-NYC founder flow.\n\n## Background\n- Joined Index Ventures in 2010. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/martin-mignot/)\n- Early-stage leads include Algolia, Swile, Trainline, Deliveroo, Nourish and Personio, and they were one of Revolut's earliest investors (team page). [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/martin-mignot/)\n- 2022: helped establish Index's New York office. Bloomberg Línea names Shardul Shah as co-leading that office. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/martin-mignot/) [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/)\n- French, based in New York (Bloomberg Línea). Forbes Midas List (team page). Founded Happy Streets, a foundation for walkable neighborhoods. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/martin-mignot/)\n- Education and pre-Index jobs: nothing found in fetched sources.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/martin-mignot/ | 32 companies listed (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ | Bylines: Phaidra (2024-07), Beside (2025-11), Build (2026-06), Oratomic (2026-10) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/martinmignot | Linked from team page; not readable (402), follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/martinmignot/ | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2017-12: TechCrunch, \"Index Ventures spells out the ABCs of investing\", a panel with Jan Hammer. Not watched; the page has no transcript. [src](https://techcrunch.com/video/index-ventures-spells-out-the-abcs-of-investing)\n- No AI-era podcast found (search budget exhausted).\n\n## How they think\n- **Compute as the bottleneck, energy as its cost:** \"Scaling compute capacity is critical for AI innovation\" (Phaidra post). [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/compute-is-king-our-investment-in-phaidra/)\n- **Human plus agent firms for non-standard industries:** Build pairs \"human experts with agentic AI\", since buildings aren't standardized the way software needs. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/automating-the-built-world-our-partnership-with-build/)\n- **AI for the \"real economy\":** Beside targets local service businesses that modern software barely touches. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/beside/)\n- **Calls right:** Deliveroo (first investor), Revolut (earliest investor; Bloomberg says the stake could out-return Wiz). [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/) Calls wrong: none sourced.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [AI for the Real Economy: Our Investment in Beside](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/beside/) (2025-11-11): the relationship-first origin story and the personal pre-seed.\n2. [Compute is king: Our investment in Phaidra](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/compute-is-king-our-investment-in-phaidra/) (2024-07-02): their compute and energy thesis.\n3. [Automating the built world: our partnership with Build](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/automating-the-built-world-our-partnership-with-build/) (2026-06-29): the AI-native services firm thesis.\n4. [Making Quantum Computing Real: Oratomic](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/making-quantum-computing-real-our-investment-in-oratomic/) (2026-10-08): a frontier-hardware bet with a Caltech team.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed (X not readable this pass).\n\n## Portfolio\nAI and compute deals only. The full list of 32 is on the team page.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Phaidra (AI data-center cooling) | Round of ~$12M reportedly from Index | 2024-07 | Yes (Index lead per Phaidra news page); Mignot sole byline | Prior: Helena and a DeepMind co-founder (2022-23) | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/compute-is-king-our-investment-in-phaidra/) [Phaidra](https://www.phaidra.ai/news) |\n| Phaidra | Series B ($50M+) | 2025-10-01 | No; Collaborative Fund led | Helena, NVIDIA, Sony Innovation Fund | [PRN](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/collaborative-fund-leads-phaidras-50m-series-b-to-build-the-ai-factories-of-the-future-302572418.html) |\n| Beside (AI OS for local businesses) | Personal pre-seed, then Index-led seed | Before 2025-11 (dates not stated) | Index led the seed; Mignot sole author | Not named | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/beside/) |\n| Beside | Series A ($32M) | 2025-11-11 | Not stated | Not named | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/beside/) |\n| Build (build.inc, agentic real estate development) | Seed | 2026-06-29 | Yes (Index leading); Mignot sole author, no other Index person tagged | Not disclosed | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/automating-the-built-world-our-partnership-with-build/) |\n| Crosby (AI law firm) | Series A ($20M) | 2025-10-08 | Index claims lead (contested by BCV); Jahanvi Sardana byline, Mignot tagged | BCV, Sequoia, Elad Gil, Cooley, Patrick Collison | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/) [Crosby](https://crosby.ai/blog/series-a-and-faster-agreements) |\n| Crosby | Series B ($60M) | 2026-03-31 | Lux and Index per Crosby | Sequoia, Elad Gil, 01 Advisors, BCV | [Crosby](https://crosby.ai/blog/series-b-planting-our-flag) |\n| Decagon | Series D ($250M) | 2026-01-28 | Index co-led; Sofia Dolfe byline, Mignot tagged | Other lead not named in post | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/investing-in-decagon-rigor-speed-and-a-new-concierge-model-for-customer-experience/) |\n| Anthropic | Not stated | Not stated | Not stated | Listed with Nina Achadjian and Vlad Loktev | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/anthropic/) |\n| Mirage | Not stated | Not stated | Not stated | Which Mirage is unconfirmed (company page 404) | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/team/martin-mignot/) |\n| Oratomic (quantum, not AI) | Part of $775M raised since 2026-03 | 2026-10-08 | Index among six named leaders; Mignot byline, Hasslinger and Pastor tagged | ARCH, Spark, Khosla, General Catalyst, Bezos Expeditions | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/making-quantum-computing-real-our-investment-in-oratomic/) [TQI](https://thequantuminsider.com/2026/10/08/oratomic-secures-775-million-fault-tolerant-quantum-computers) |\n\n**Timing summary:** first on Beside (personal pre-seed). No earlier round found for Build, which is not proof Index was first. Late on Phaidra (about two years after first money), Crosby (Sequoia and BCV seeded it a round earlier, per [companies/crosby.md](../companies/crosby.md)) and Decagon (a Series D).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Beside:** they met Germain in 2012 at the Gertrude loft (the startup of Kenneth Schlenker) in TriBeCa and stayed in touch. Germain later built and sold Jour to Index portfolio company Alan for $20M and experimented with LLMs there. In 2023 he told Mignot the Beside idea. Mignot invested personally at pre-seed, Index led the seed, and Beside emerged from stealth with a $32M A. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/beside/)\n- **Build:** CEO James Stirrat-Ellis is an architect who worked at Heatherwick Studio on Changi T5, taught himself to code, and led product at an early agentic AI startup until 2024. The intro path is not stated. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/automating-the-built-world-our-partnership-with-build/) CTO Ben McClusky did multi-agent RL at Imperial and worked at Gorillas. [src](https://build.inc)\n- **Phaidra:** founded by ex-DeepMind Energy researchers Jim Gao and Veda Panneershelvam with Katie Hoffman. The intro path is not stated. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/compute-is-king-our-investment-in-phaidra/)\n- **Crosby:** sourced by Sardana. They knew CEO Ryan Daniels from A.Team and heard of CTO John Sarihan through Ramp. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/)\n- **Oratomic:** the post describes a visit to the team in Pasadena. The intro path is not stated. Founders on Index's page include Dolev Bluvstein, John Preskill, Madelyn Cain, Manuel Endres and Robert Huang. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/oratomic/)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Maxime Germain (Beside) | Long-time friend; personal pre-seed investor | 2012 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/beside/) |\n| Kenneth Schlenker (Gertrude) | \"Close friend\"; Mignot worked from Gertrude's loft | 2012 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/beside/) |\n| Will Shu (Deliveroo) | First investor | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/martin-mignot/) |\n| Danny Rimer (Index) | Close colleague; named Mignot a successor | 2010 | [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/) |\n| Jan Hammer (Index) | Joined Index in the same year (2010) | 2010 | [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/) |\n| Shardul Shah (Index) | NY office colleague (Shah co-leads NY per Bloomberg Línea) | 2022 | [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/) |\n| Jahanvi Sardana (Index) | Junior byline on Crosby; Mignot tagged | 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/) |\n| Sofia Dolfe (Index) | Byline on Decagon; Mignot tagged | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/investing-in-decagon-rigor-speed-and-a-new-concierge-model-for-customer-experience/) |\n| Bastian Hasslinger, Simon Pastor (Index) | Tagged under Mignot's Oratomic byline | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/making-quantum-computing-real-our-investment-in-oratomic/) |\n| Nina Achadjian, Vlad Loktev (Index) | Co-listed on Anthropic | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/anthropic/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which round of Anthropic Index joined, and what role Mignot had.\n- Which Mirage this is (the company page 404'd).\n- Build seed size and co-investors, and the intro path to Stirrat-Ellis.\n- Beside seed date and size, and who led the $32M A.\n- Phaidra 2022 vs 2023 Series A dating (Helena and Phaidra disagree).\n- Any podcast or X content in their own voice. Searches were not possible this pass, so look again for talks on AI-native services and compute.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/martin-mignot/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/beside/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/compute-is-king-our-investment-in-phaidra/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/automating-the-built-world-our-partnership-with-build/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/making-quantum-computing-real-our-investment-in-oratomic/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/contracts-at-the-speed-of-business-our-investment-in-crosby/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/investing-in-decagon-rigor-speed-and-a-new-concierge-model-for-customer-experience/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/anthropic/ ; /companies/phaidra/ ; /companies/build/ ; /companies/beside/ ; /companies/crosby/ ; /companies/decagon/ ; /companies/oratomic/\n- https://www.phaidra.ai/news\n- https://www.helena.org/projects/phaidra/\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/collaborative-fund-leads-phaidras-50m-series-b-to-build-the-ai-factories-of-the-future-302572418.html\n- https://thequantuminsider.com/2026/10/08/oratomic-secures-775-million-fault-tolerant-quantum-computers\n- https://build.inc\n- https://crosby.ai/blog/series-a-and-faster-agreements ; https://crosby.ai/blog/series-b-planting-our-flag\n- https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/\n- https://techcrunch.com/video/index-ventures-spells-out-the-abcs-of-investing\n", "person:martin-rudigier": "\n# Martin Rudigier\n\n## One-line take\nHe alone turns Stanford GSB alumni demand into SPV allocation in hot rounds: 127 SPVs and $308M in four years, including Axiom Math, Rhoda AI, Upscale AI and Starcloud [src](https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?company=GSBackers&type=D&dateb=&owner=include&count=100&action=getcompany).\n\n## Crux\nHis edge is distribution, not selection: he can fill a few million dollars quickly from 2,000+ alumni. He is constrained by the SPV form (no committed capital, no lead cheques), and two SPVs above $10M (You.com I and Axiom Math II) are the ceiling so far. `check_capacity` is sub-10m on that basis.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Stanford's alumni office profiled him and quotes a member: \"Martin has directly changed my life\" (Kari Schrader) [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/alumni/news/catalyst/martin-rudigier-mba-18-ms-19).\n- Valerie Shen is cited as saying he gets allocation in competitive deals alongside Midas List investors (paraphrase in the Stanford profile) [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/alumni/news/catalyst/martin-rudigier-mba-18-ms-19).\n- See [firms/gsbackers.md](../firms/gsbackers.md) for the full SPV table.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. Not a peer investor, but a visible indicator of which AI rounds are open to syndicate money, and a possible route to allocation.\n\n## Background\nStanford MBA 2018 and MS 2019; 13 years between Germany's auto industry and Silicon Valley; coaches business-school applicants [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/alumni/news/catalyst/martin-rudigier-mba-18-ms-19).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://gsbackers.com | placeholder page |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/company/gsbackers | company page; not opened |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found.\n\n## How they think\nNo public writing found.\n\n## Read these first\n- Stanford GSB Catalyst profile (2025-09-13) [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/alumni/news/catalyst/martin-rudigier-mba-18-ms-19).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nSee the SPV table in [firms/gsbackers.md](../firms/gsbackers.md).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Axiom Math | two SPVs, $20.3M | 2026-03 | no | | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2114847/000211484726000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Rhoda AI | Series A, two SPVs, $14.0M | 2025-04 and 2026-03 | no | | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2113344/000211334426000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| GSBackers | founder | 2021/2022 | [src](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/alumni/news/catalyst/martin-rudigier-mba-18-ms-19) |\n| Sydecar | SPV administrator for every deal | 2022-09 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?company=GSBackers&type=D&dateb=&owner=include&count=100&action=getcompany) |\n\n## Open questions\nWho gives him allocation; terms; whether he invests personally.\n\n## Sources\nhttps://www.gsb.stanford.edu/alumni/news/catalyst/martin-rudigier-mba-18-ms-19 ; https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?company=GSBackers&type=D&dateb=&owner=include&count=100&action=getcompany\n", "person:masayoshi-son": "\n# Masayoshi Son\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (strategic sweep, run 2). Companion pages: [SoftBank Group](../firms/softbank-group.md), [Vision Fund 2](../firms/softbank-vision-fund-2.md), [SoftBank AI ledger](../sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md). Row files: `data/rows/masayoshi-son-{deals,rels,insights,leads,tags}.csv`. Rows on his fund co-investment already sit in `data/rows/softbank-map-{insights,rels,lp}.csv` and are not repeated.*\n\n\"Q2 report\" below means SoftBank Group's consolidated financial report for the six months to 2026-09-30 ([PDF](https://group.softbank/media/Project/sbg/sbg/pdf/ir/financials/financial_reports/financial-report_q2fy2026_01_en.pdf)). \"FY2025 report\" means the report for the year to 2026-03-31 ([PDF](https://group.softbank/media/Project/sbg/sbg/pdf/ir/financials/financial_reports/financial-report_q4fy2025_01_en.pdf)).\n\n## One-line take\nThe only person at SoftBank whose yes moves a frontier-lab check: he has put $64.6B into OpenAI in two years and buys chip and robotics companies outright, but he has never been found on a seed round.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He decides alone and at a size nobody matches. Vision Fund 2 has no outside LPs, so there is no investment committee to persuade except him (Q2 report). He committed $30B to OpenAI twice, eleven months apart ([SBG 2025-04-01](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250401), [SBG 2026-02-27](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260227)).\n- **What constrains him.** Cash, not conviction. He sold every NVIDIA share SoftBank owned to pay for OpenAI and said so: \"I was crying to sell Nvidia shares\" ([Yahoo Finance, 2025-12-02](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/dont-want-sell-single-share-002214711.html)). The parent holds itself to a loan-to-value ratio below 25% ([SBG 2026-02-27](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260227)).\n- **What he will not do.** Spread small checks again. A former Vision Fund executive says he missed the labs in 2021-22 because \"he had used all his ammunition on other companies\" (CNBC, [syndicated copy](https://newsio.com/2025/08/11/softbank-founder-son-makes-his-biggest-bet-by-staking-the-japanese-giants-future-on-ai/), 2025-08-11). The AI book is now one position plus acquisitions.\n- **His map.** Four areas: models, semiconductors, AI infrastructure and robots. \"Once you secure the four corners in the middle of the game\", the board turns ([CEO message, 2026-06-24](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/son)). OpenAI holds the model corner.\n\n## What he personally signs, sources or prices\n\n| Pool | His seat | What the sources show |\n|---|---|---|\n| Parent balance sheet | Chairman & CEO; one of five members of the parent's Investment Committee ([firm page](../firms/softbank-group.md), from the SoftBank Group Report 2026) | The board resolves large deals and hands the final terms to him, to CFO Yoshimitsu Goto, or to both. Both: OpenAI 2025 ([SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250401)) and ABB robotics ([SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20251008)). Goto alone: OpenAI 2026 ([SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260227)) |\n| Vision Fund 2 | No title at the manager found. Personal co-investor: his vehicle MASA USA LLC holds 17.25% of the equity of SVF2 LLC (Q2 report) | The fund is 98% the parent's money. OpenAI \"is subject to the co-investment program\" (FY2025 report), so he shares personally in it. He has paid nothing in and owes $3,171M (Q2 report) |\n| LatAm fund | Co-investor, 17.25% of the equity of SLA LLC | Owes $744M; interest valued at zero (Q2 report) |\n| SB Northstar | Holds 33% indirectly | Listed securities and bonds, not venture; cumulative loss ¥703.4B (Q2 report) |\n| Vision Fund 1 | Founder of the fund ([biography](https://group.softbank/en/about/officer/son)) | Outside LPs' money; stopped investing in 2019. No current role in new deals |\n| Stargate | Chairman ([SBG 2025-01-22](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250122)) | SoftBank carries \"financial responsibility\"; OpenAI runs operations |\n\n**Pricing.** He sets price by paying it. The 2025 OpenAI round went in at $260B pre-money and the 2026 money at $730B pre-money (same two releases). He does not negotiate down: on OpenAI he told shareholders it \"takes bravery to invest\" (CNBC via [NBC DFW, 2025-06-27](https://www.nbcdfw.com/news/business/money-report/softbank-ceo-says-hes-all-in-on-openai-reveals-hes-long-wanted-microsofts-spot-as-main-backer/3873283/)).\n\n**Sourcing.** Founder to Son, directly. No source shows a Vision Fund partner bringing him a lab. See \"Deal stories\".\n\n## Differential insight\nEach bullet is mirrored in `data/rows/masayoshi-son-insights.csv` unless marked as an existing row.\n\n- **He is the named decision-maker on OpenAI only while the deal sat on the parent.** In 2025 the investing entity was \"SBG or its subsidiary\" and the final decision \"was delegated to Mr. Masayoshi Son\" and Goto ([SBG 2025-04-01](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250401)). In 2026 the investing entity was Vision Fund 2 from the start and the final terms went to Goto alone ([SBG 2026-02-27](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260227)). Son owns 17.25% of that fund's co-investment equity. That this is why he stepped out is my inference; no filing gives a reason.\n- **OpenAI is a deal he lost once.** Before 2019 Altman asked him for $10B. He says he agreed, but \"eventually, they picked Microsoft\" (CNBC via [NBC DFW, 2025-06-27](https://www.nbcdfw.com/news/business/money-report/softbank-ceo-says-hes-all-in-on-openai-reveals-hes-long-wanted-microsofts-spot-as-main-backer/3873283/)).\n- **His own fund passed on the labs on price, and he overrode it.** Reuters reported in January 2024 that Vision Fund 2 was \"passing on high-profile AI investment opportunities, such as OpenAI and Anthropic\" ([via Yahoo, 2024-01-11](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html)). First OpenAI money followed nine months later.\n- **He pushes for position after the first check.** Eight weeks after the first $500M he \"was persistent in asking for a larger stake\" and got a $1.5B employee tender ([CNBC via NBC Boston, 2024-11-27](https://nbcboston.com/news/business/money-report/openai-gets-new-1-5-billion-investment-from-softbank-allowing-employees-to-sell-shares-in-a-tender-offer/3562735/); existing row in `softbank-ledger-insights.csv`).\n- **The model slot is taken.** \"Our key partner in the AI model area, which governs intelligence, is OpenAI\" ([CEO message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/son)). No other lab is held in any SoftBank pool ([ledger](../sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md)).\n- **But he does not call the market winner-take-all.** He said OpenAI, Anthropic and Google can each grow \"ten-fold, a hundred-fold\" (Bloomberg via [Swissinfo, 2026-06-24](https://www.swissinfo.ch/eng/softbanks-masayoshi-son-scraps-plan-to-retire-in-his-sixties/91638621)).\n- **His payout from the fund is gated.** Nothing is paid to him until the sub-fund's value \"exceeds 130% of the aggregate acquisition cost\" (Q2 report; existing row in `softbank-map-insights.csv`).\n- **No succession.** \"I don’t have time to retire\"; he asked for another 10 or 15 years (same Swissinfo piece).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a co-investor at seed and not an LP.\n- **For a new lab:** he is not the anchor. OpenAI holds that seat and he is \"all in\" on it (NBC DFW piece above). He matters as a later price-setter only if a lab becomes a second pillar, which no source suggests.\n- **For AI chips and compute:** an acquirer. Ampere's lead investors Carlyle and Oracle sold to him for $6.5B in cash ([SBG 2025-03-20](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250320)). A seed in Arm-adjacent silicon has a real exit here.\n- **For robotics and automation:** an acquirer and a late lead. ABB's robotics arm for $5.375B ([SBG 2025-10-08](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20251008)); Skild AI's Series C at over $14B ([ledger](../sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md)).\n- **For neoclouds:** now a competitor through SB Neo ([SBG 2026-07-02](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260702)).\n- **Who to know instead of him:** Kentaro Matsui for physical AI and Vikas J. Parekh for US positions ([firm page](../firms/softbank-group.md), section 6). Son is reached by the founder, once the round is already large.\n\n## Background\n- Born 1957-08-11. Founded Nihon SoftBank in September 1981 and has been chairman and CEO since ([biography](https://group.softbank/en/about/officer/son)).\n- Director of Alibaba from October 2005 (same biography; end date not given there). The Alibaba stake began as a $20M investment in 2000 (CNBC, [syndicated copy](https://newsio.com/2025/08/11/softbank-founder-son-makes-his-biggest-bet-by-staking-the-japanese-giants-future-on-ai/)).\n- April 2006: Chairman, President & CEO of Vodafone K.K., now SoftBank Corp.; Board Director, Founder there since April 2021 (biography).\n- September 2016: Chairman of ARM Holdings after the buyout; Chairman and Director of Arm Holdings plc since August 2023 (biography).\n- 2017: founded the Vision Fund (biography). Rajeev Misra ran the manager ([SBG 2017-05-22](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20170522)).\n- 2022: record Vision Fund losses; he declared \"defense\" mode (CNBC piece above).\n- Holds 1,865,511,164 SoftBank Group shares as of 2026-06-01 (biography). Some are pledged against what he owes the funds: 35,588,400 for Vision Fund 2 and 8,674,000 for LatAm (Q2 report).\n- Education: nothing found in the sources read for this page.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [group.softbank/en/about/officer/son](https://group.softbank/en/about/officer/son) | Official biography, dated 2026-06-24. Loaded 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | [CEO message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/son) | One letter a year in the SoftBank Group Report. Loaded 2026-11-10 |\n| X | @masason | Not checked directly. A search summary of a Reuters piece says his last known posts were in March 2020. Unconfirmed |\n| LinkedIn | none found | |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n| Events | [SoftBank press and webcast pages](https://group.softbank/en/news/press) | He speaks at the June shareholders' meeting, SoftBank World in July and occasional forums. He does not take inbound through any public channel found |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-12-16 — Mar-a-Lago press conference with Donald Trump — [Semafor](https://semafor.com/article/12/17/2024/softbank-ceo-son-pledges-100b-in-us-projects-to-trump) — pledged $100B, double his 2016 figure: \"President Trump is a double-down president\".\n- 2025-02-03 — Tokyo event with Sam Altman — [SBG release](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250203_0) — SoftBank to spend $3B a year on OpenAI products; SB OpenAI Japan joint venture announced.\n- 2025-06-27 — SoftBank Group shareholders' meeting — [CNBC via NBC DFW](https://www.nbcdfw.com/news/business/money-report/softbank-ceo-says-hes-all-in-on-openai-reveals-hes-long-wanted-microsofts-spot-as-main-backer/3873283/) — \"all in\" on OpenAI; the pre-2019 $10B story.\n- 2025-12 — FII Priority Asia, Tokyo — [Yahoo Finance](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/dont-want-sell-single-share-002214711.html) — on selling NVIDIA to fund OpenAI.\n- 2026-06-24 — SoftBank Group shareholders' meeting — [Bloomberg via Swissinfo](https://www.swissinfo.ch/eng/softbanks-masayoshi-son-scraps-plan-to-retire-in-his-sixties/91638621) — bubble talk \"is an insult\"; staying 10 to 15 more years; physical AI as the new theme.\n\nNo long-form podcast or transcript was found. Every quote above is from a company release or a press report of a live event.\n\n## How they think\n- **One thesis, stated for 16 years.** The 2010 \"Next 30-Year Vision\" described a \"brain computer\"; he now says that is superintelligence ([CEO message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/son)).\n- **Own the stack, not a portfolio.** Models (OpenAI), chips (Arm, Ampere), infrastructure (Stargate, DigitalBridge, SB Neo) and robots (ABB). Same letter.\n- **Contrarian view:** there is no AI bubble. \"The AI revolution has only just begun\" (same letter).\n- **He moves his own goalposts.** \"I have decided to update my 50-year life plan\", which had him retiring in his sixties (same letter).\n- **Target:** net asset value of ¥1 quadrillion by 2042, from ¥74 trillion in June 2026 (same letter).\n- **Right:** Alibaba (2000), Arm (2016). **Wrong:** WeWork and other late Vision Fund 1 bets (CNBC piece); selling NVIDIA twice, the second time in 2025 ([Fortune, 2025-11-11](https://fortune.com/2025/11/11/softbank-nvidia-openai-masayoshi-son-sam-altman-investment/)); losing OpenAI to Microsoft in 2019 and letting the fund pass on it again before 2024 (NBC DFW and Reuters pieces).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [CEO message, SoftBank Group Report 2026](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/son). The four-corners map and the ¥1 quadrillion target, in his own words.\n2. [OpenAI investment terms, 2025-04-01](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250401). How a Son deal is structured: staged closings, conditions, syndication, delegation.\n3. [CNBC, \"all in\" on OpenAI](https://www.nbcdfw.com/news/business/money-report/softbank-ceo-says-hes-all-in-on-openai-reveals-hes-long-wanted-microsofts-spot-as-main-backer/3873283/). The lost 2019 deal.\n4. The related-party note in the [Q2 report](https://group.softbank/media/Project/sbg/sbg/pdf/ir/financials/financial_reports/financial-report_q2fy2026_01_en.pdf). The only place his personal stake in the funds is laid out.\n5. [Reuters on Alex Clavel, 2024-01-11](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html). What the fund did when Son was not driving.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing current found. He communicates through shareholder meetings, earnings presentations and company releases. Sam Altman, ABB's Morten Wierod, DigitalBridge's Marc Ganzi and SoftBank Corp.'s Junichi Miyakawa are quoted beside him in the releases cited on this page.\n\n## Portfolio\nDeals he is personally named on. Fund-level deals with no named SoftBank person are in the [ledger](../sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | Tender (about $1.5B at $157B). Entry was eight weeks earlier, about nine years after founding | 2024-11-27 | sole buyer | none | [CNBC via NBC Boston](https://nbcboston.com/news/business/money-report/openai-gets-new-1-5-billion-investment-from-softbank-allowing-employees-to-sell-shares-in-a-tender-offer/3562735/) |\n| Stargate | Strategic (joint venture; chairman) | 2025-01-21 | yes | OpenAI, Oracle, MGX | [SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250122) |\n| Ampere Computing | Strategic (acquisition, $6.5B cash). Quoted | 2025-03-20 | acquirer | sellers: Carlyle, Oracle | [SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250320) |\n| OpenAI | Growth (up to $40B at $260B pre-money). Final terms delegated to him and Goto | 2025-03-31 signed; closed 2025-04 and 2025-12-26 | yes | $11.0B syndicated to co-investors | [SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250401), [SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20251231) |\n| ABB Robotics | Strategic (acquisition, $5.375B). Quoted; final terms delegated to him and Goto. Not closed | 2025-10-08 | acquirer | seller: ABB | [SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20251008) |\n| DigitalBridge | Strategic (acquisition, about $4.0B enterprise value, $16.00 a share). Quoted | 2025-12-29 | acquirer | none | [SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20251229) |\n| OpenAI | Growth ($30B at $730B pre-money, three tranches). Quoted; final terms delegated to Goto alone | 2026-02-27 | co-lead | Amazon, NVIDIA and others ([ledger](../sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md)) | [SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260227) |\n| SB Neo | Strategic (internal joint venture, 49% SoftBank Group). Quoted | 2026-07-02 | no | SoftBank Corp. (51%) | [SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260702) |\n\n**Stage and timing.** Every row is a growth round, a tender, a joint venture or a whole-company purchase. None is a first priced round. No seed or Series A carries his name.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **OpenAI.** Altman asked for $10B before 2019; Son says he agreed; Microsoft won (NBC DFW piece). His fund passed again around 2023 on valuation (Reuters). First money went in during September-October 2024 at $157B, then the tender he pushed for in November. On 2025-03-31 the parent signed for up to $40B, with $10B to be sold on to co-investors ([SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250401)). In October 2025 he sold SoftBank's NVIDIA shares to help pay (Yahoo piece). In February 2026 he added $30B at 2.8 times the price he had set eleven months earlier ([SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260227)). Total: $64.6B for about 13% ([SBG 2026-10-01](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20261001)).\n- **What came with the money.** A commercial contract and a joint venture: SoftBank spends \"$3 billion US annually\" on OpenAI products, and SB OpenAI Japan sells them \"exclusively to major companies in Japan\" ([SBG 2025-02-03](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250203_0)). Then Stargate, which he chairs.\n- **Stargate.** Announced 2025-01-21, five weeks after his $100B pledge beside Trump (Semafor piece; SBG release).\n- **Skild AI.** A search summary of a Reuters report says SoftBank's interest \"stems from\" Son's enthusiasm for robotics. I could not open the article, so this is unconfirmed and has no row.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sam Altman | Counterparty on every OpenAI deal; Stargate partner | before 2019 | [NBC DFW](https://www.nbcdfw.com/news/business/money-report/softbank-ceo-says-hes-all-in-on-openai-reveals-hes-long-wanted-microsofts-spot-as-main-backer/3873283/) |\n| Yoshimitsu Goto | CFO; holds the delegated final terms with him or instead of him | 2025-03 (first seen) | [SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250401) |\n| Jensen Huang | \"I respect Jensen\"; sold the NVIDIA stake anyway | 2017 | [Yahoo Finance](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/dont-want-sell-single-share-002214711.html) |\n| Rene Haas | Arm CEO; SoftBank director; CEO of SoftBank Group International from 2026-04-21 | 2026-04 | [SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260421) |\n| Alok Sama | Former finance chief (to 2016) and president (to 2019); recalls Son pitching the singularity at dinner | to 2019 | [CNBC, syndicated](https://newsio.com/2025/08/11/softbank-founder-son-makes-his-biggest-bet-by-staking-the-japanese-giants-future-on-ai/) |\n| Donald Trump | $50B pledge in 2016, $100B in 2024 | 2016 | [Semafor](https://semafor.com/article/12/17/2024/softbank-ceo-son-pledges-100b-in-us-projects-to-trump) |\n| Taizo Son | Brother; his vehicle bought SoftBank Ventures Asia in 2023 | 2023 | [firm page](../firms/softbank-group.md) |\n| Rajeev Misra | Ran the Vision Fund manager from 2017; left 2024-11 | 2017 | [SBG](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20170522) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Why the 2026 OpenAI delegation went to Goto alone. Related-party handling is my guess.\n- Whether the 2026 OpenAI tranches sit inside the co-investment sub-fund in which he holds 17.25%.\n- The size threshold above which a deal leaves the Investment Committee for the full board.\n- Whether \"Roze\", a reported US-listed AI and robotics vehicle (Bloomberg via Swissinfo, people familiar), exists yet and who runs it.\n- Whether he met the Skild AI or Wayve founders before those rounds. No account found.\n- His X account status and the date he left Alibaba's board.\n- The CNBC long read and the Reuters piece were read through syndicated copies.\n\n## Sources\n- SoftBank biography: [link](https://group.softbank/en/about/officer/son). CEO message 2026: [link](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/son)\n- SoftBank releases: [Vision Fund 2017-05-22](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20170522); [Stargate 2025-01-22](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250122); [Cristal intelligence 2025-02-03](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250203_0); [Ampere 2025-03-20](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250320); [OpenAI 2025-04-01](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20250401); [ABB 2025-10-08](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20251008); [DigitalBridge 2025-12-29](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20251229); [OpenAI completion 2025-12-31](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20251231); [OpenAI follow-on 2026-02-27](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260227); [Rene Haas 2026-04-21](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260421); [SB Neo 2026-07-02](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20260702); [third tranche 2026-10-01](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20261001)\n- SoftBank filings: [Q2 FY2026 report](https://group.softbank/media/Project/sbg/sbg/pdf/ir/financials/financial_reports/financial-report_q2fy2026_01_en.pdf); [FY2025 report](https://group.softbank/media/Project/sbg/sbg/pdf/ir/financials/financial_reports/financial-report_q4fy2025_01_en.pdf) (both read by the SoftBank map task; quotes taken from its rows)\n- CNBC via NBC DFW, 2025-06-27: [link](https://www.nbcdfw.com/news/business/money-report/softbank-ceo-says-hes-all-in-on-openai-reveals-hes-long-wanted-microsofts-spot-as-main-backer/3873283/)\n- CNBC, 2025-08-11, syndicated copy: [link](https://newsio.com/2025/08/11/softbank-founder-son-makes-his-biggest-bet-by-staking-the-japanese-giants-future-on-ai/)\n- CNBC via NBC Boston, 2024-11-27: [link](https://nbcboston.com/news/business/money-report/openai-gets-new-1-5-billion-investment-from-softbank-allowing-employees-to-sell-shares-in-a-tender-offer/3562735/)\n- Fortune, 2025-11-11: [link](https://fortune.com/2025/11/11/softbank-nvidia-openai-masayoshi-son-sam-altman-investment/)\n- Yahoo Finance, 2025-12-02: [link](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/dont-want-sell-single-share-002214711.html)\n- Bloomberg via Swissinfo, 2026-06-24: [link](https://www.swissinfo.ch/eng/softbanks-masayoshi-son-scraps-plan-to-retire-in-his-sixties/91638621)\n- Semafor, 2024-12-18: [link](https://semafor.com/article/12/17/2024/softbank-ceo-son-pledges-100b-in-us-projects-to-trump)\n- Reuters via Yahoo, 2024-01-11: [link](https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/behind-softbank-investing-cleanup-us-110626656.html)\n", "person:mason-murray": "\n# Mason Murray\n\n## One-line take\nThe NEA junior with the most written AI-application theses (agentic internet, AI ads, \"personal intelligence\"), who has been on the team for Twelve Labs' A, ElevenLabs' C, Bluefish's A and Corca's seed, and was made Principal in 2026-02 ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-promotes-arjun-jain-to-partner-and-mason-murray-to-principal)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he writes the category maps NEA's consumer/app partners (Danielle Lay, Luke Pappas, Alex Sharata) publish under, then turns up on the deal. Rick Yang's promotion quote credits him with \"cultivating and strengthening key investment theses\" ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-promotes-arjun-jain-to-partner-and-mason-murray-to-principal)).\n- **Constraint:** he is never the sole named NEA person on a lead. Every deal page or post pairs him with a partner (Luck on Twelve Labs, Sharata on ElevenLabs, Pappas on Corca, Lay on Bluefish), so he sources and co-writes but doesn't hold the board seat.\n- **What he'd never do (by record):** nothing in his written work backs frontier generalist labs. His thesis is that generalist LLM platforms will be forced into ads and commerce, and that the opportunity sits in the rails and tools around that shift.\n\n## Differential insight\n- He argues foundation-model companies are structurally worse businesses than SaaS and will be forced into advertising: \"Foundation model businesses face structurally worse margin profiles than SaaS\" ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/ai-internet-monetization), 2026-01-15, with Danielle Lay and Alexa Grabelle). The post names OpenAds, Polynomic, Kontext and Koah Labs as companies building AI-native ad rails. None are in NEA's portfolio CMS as of 2026-11-11, so treat them as a live pipeline.\n- The Bluefish A (2025-08) came straight out of the Perplexity position: \"Our conviction in this category began more than two years ago\" ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/bluefish-the-ai-marketing-platform-for-the-agentic-era)). NEA led the $20M round, with Salesforce Ventures participating ([src](https://www.foley.com/news/2025/08/foley-represents-nea-as-lead-investor-in-series-a-funding-for-ai-marketing-platform-bluefish/)).\n- Consumer thesis (2025-01): \"the most compelling whitespace lies in self-actualization\" ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/personalized-intelligence)). The post's portfolio map is Perplexity, Wispr, Genmo, Limitless, Backflip, Sakana, Twelve Labs and World Labs.\n- He co-runs the NEA Fellows program for students (2023 post with Hunter Worland and Andrew Schoen) ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/the-nea-fellowship-2023)), which is a pre-founder sourcing funnel.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. He isn't on lab deals as a decision-maker, but he is the NEA principal for AI applications and consumer AI at the A stage (Twelve Labs, Bluefish, Corca), and a useful back door into NEA's app-side IC conversations. His Twelve Labs seat matters: NEA co-led the A with NVentures (2024-06) and the B (2026).\n\n## Background\n- Columbia University, BA in business management and art history ([src](https://www.nea.com/team/mason-murray)).\n- Investment banking, Bank of America Technology, Media & Telecom group (M&A, equity and debt) ([src](https://www.nea.com/team/mason-murray)).\n- First internship: HR at Rocket Lawyer, \"fresh off of a Series C\" (NEA Q&A on [team page](https://www.nea.com/team/mason-murray)).\n- NEA: joined 2022; Senior Associate 2024-06-13 ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-appoints-andrew-schoen-and-blake-wu-to-senior-investing-team-roles-promotes-alex-sharata-to-partner)); Principal 2026-02-03 ([src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-promotes-arjun-jain-to-partner-and-mason-murray-to-principal)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.nea.com/team/mason-murray | NEA team page (no LinkedIn or X listed there) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.nea.com/blog | 6 posts as author or co-author, 2023-01 to 2026-07 |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not listed on NEA page | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search for his name returned only unrelated National Education Association results (checked 2026-11-11).\n\n## How they think\n- The open web is \"compressing\": agents resolve intent at the point of query, so the long tail loses economic value and attention concentrates on high-trust, identity-rich and AI-native surfaces ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/ai-internet-monetization)).\n- He expects big LLM platforms to build their own ad stacks (\"new-age walled gardens\") and sees room for independent ad networks for the long tail of AI apps, by analogy to The Trade Desk and Taboola (same source).\n- Consumer AI will win in \"self-actualization\" categories (curiosity, creativity, fulfilment), where incumbents' data advantages count for least ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/personalized-intelligence)).\n\n## Read these first\n- \"AI ads, agentic interfaces, and the future of internet monetization\" (2026-01-15): his clearest thesis, and it names companies NEA hasn't funded yet ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/ai-internet-monetization)).\n- \"Personalized Intelligence\" (2025-01-14): the consumer AI map ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/personalized-intelligence)).\n- Bluefish Series A post (2025-08-20) ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/bluefish-the-ai-marketing-platform-for-the-agentic-era)).\n- Corca seed post with Luke Pappas (2026-07-09) ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/investing-in-corca-the-ide-for-mathematics)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: no public X or LinkedIn link on the NEA page.\n\n## Portfolio\nDeal rows already in `data/rows/new-enterprise-associates-portfolio-deals.csv`. New firm-level Bluefish lead row in `data/rows/mason-murray-deals.csv`.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Twelve Labs | Series A ($50M) | 2024-06 | NEA co-lead (team) | NVentures | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/twelve-labs-multimodal-ai-investment) |\n| ElevenLabs | Series C | 2025-01-30 | no (post co-author) | a16z, ICONIQ (co-leads) | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/elevenlabs-ais-natural-interface) |\n| Bluefish AI | Series A ($20M) | 2025-08-20 | NEA lead (sole post author) | Salesforce Ventures, Crane, Swift, Bloomberg Beta | [src](https://www.foley.com/news/2025/08/foley-represents-nea-as-lead-investor-in-series-a-funding-for-ai-marketing-platform-bluefish/) |\n| Corca | Seed ($7.8M) | 2026-07-09 | NEA co-lead | NVentures, Daft Capital, Bloomberg Beta | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/investing-in-corca-the-ide-for-mathematics) |\n| Limitless, Centralize, beehiiv, Fizz, Wonder, Glacier, Aigen | various (CMS \"team\") | 2020-2026 | n/a | | [NEA CMS](https://www.nea.com/portfolio) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Bluefish:** NEA's Perplexity A (2023-03) gave it the view that AI answer engines would rewrite discovery. Murray's post cites a 10x revenue rise at Bluefish in six months and says more than 80% of customers are Fortune 500 ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/bluefish-the-ai-marketing-platform-for-the-agentic-era)). Foley's notice calls Salesforce Ventures a co-lead, while the company release says it participated (conflict noted).\n- **Corca:** NEA co-led with NVentures. NEA also co-led Twelve Labs' A with NVentures, which makes NVentures a repeat co-lead partner on his deals ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/investing-in-corca-the-ide-for-mathematics)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| New Enterprise Associates | at: Principal | 2022 | [src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-promotes-arjun-jain-to-partner-and-mason-murray-to-principal) |\n| Danielle Lay | co-author (AI internet monetization; Personalized Intelligence) | 2025 | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/ai-internet-monetization) |\n| Luke Pappas | co-author, Corca | 2026 | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/investing-in-corca-the-ide-for-mathematics) |\n| Alex Sharata | co-author, ElevenLabs | 2025 | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/elevenlabs-ais-natural-interface) |\n| Rick Yang | promoted him (Head of Technology) | 2026 | [src](https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-promotes-arjun-jain-to-partner-and-mason-murray-to-principal) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is a follow-up post on the \"agent-to-agent layer\" (promised in the 2026-01 post) out, and does it name companies?\n- Has NEA backed any of OpenAds, Polynomic, Kontext or Koah Labs since?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.nea.com/team/mason-murray\n- https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-promotes-arjun-jain-to-partner-and-mason-murray-to-principal\n- https://www.nea.com/news/press-releases/nea-appoints-andrew-schoen-and-blake-wu-to-senior-investing-team-roles-promotes-alex-sharata-to-partner\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/ai-internet-monetization\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/personalized-intelligence\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/bluefish-the-ai-marketing-platform-for-the-agentic-era\n- https://www.foley.com/news/2025/08/foley-represents-nea-as-lead-investor-in-series-a-funding-for-ai-marketing-platform-bluefish/\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/investing-in-corca-the-ide-for-mathematics\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/the-nea-fellowship-2023\n", "person:matt-bornstein": "\n# Matt Bornstein\n\n## One-line take\na16z Infra's AI writer-investor, who has been at the firm for 6+ years and was made GP on 2026-01-21. Since then his byline has gone onto three of the largest AI-infra seeds of the cycle: Unconventional AI (co-lead, $475M), Inferact (co-lead, $150M) and Mirendil (co-lead, $200M).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He has spent years writing a16z's canonical AI-stack maps (\"Who Owns the Generative AI Platform?\", \"Emerging Architectures for LLM Applications\", \"Navigating the High Cost of AI Compute\", \"The Modern Data Stack\"). That writing makes him the infra team's public voice to developers. He also ran a16z's open-source AI grants program, which gave him a 2+ year early look at the teams (vLLM) that later became seed deals [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/), [src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/).\n- **What constrains him.** On each mega-seed he is one of two or three a16z bylines, next to Appenzeller, Raghuram, Casado, Levine or Aubakirova. The sources do not show his independent sign-off authority. In 2026 his frontier-lab seeds are all co-leads alongside another multi-stage firm (Lightspeed on Inferact, Kleiner on Mirendil) [src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference), [src](https://mirendil.com/news/announcing-mirendil).\n- **What he would avoid (inferred from his writing).** Betting on model-layer moats. His 2023 thesis was that \"There don't appear, today, to be any systemic moats in generative AI\" [src](https://a16z.com/who-owns-the-generative-ai-platform/). His later deals favor picks-and-shovels and the automated-R&D layer over owning a model company. Mistral is the exception, and Midha was first author on it.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The grant program fed the Inferact deal.** \"the first a16z open source AI grant was made to the vLLM team\" [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/). Bornstein co-authored the grant announcement on 2023-08-30. It named Woosuk Kwon and Zhuohan Li (vLLM), along with Nous Research, Teknium, TheBloke, Jeremy Howard and others [src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/). The Inferact seed (2026-01-22) came about 29 months later. The firm also says \"The first vLLM meetup was hosted in our office\" [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/).\n- **The grants carried no equity.** They were not a SAFE or an investment, and were meant to let developers keep working \"without the pressure to generate financial returns\" [src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/). In effect this was a free option on open-source AI talent. The rest of the grantee list (Nous Research, Teknium, Eric Hartford, oobabooga, Jon Durbin) is a ready-made lead list for future neolab founders.\n- **Inferact thesis.** \"an explicit bet that the future will bring incredible diversity of AI apps\" [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/).\n- **Mirendil thesis: automated AI R&D.** He backs the team because \"they are one of the few teams who have experience and strong priors about how to make the end-to-end system work\" [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirendil/). The a16z post highlights that Harsh Mehta \"built the first version of the Anthropic autoresearch platform\" [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirendil/). Two months earlier, Bornstein and Aubakirova had published the thesis piece behind it: \"Real learning requires compression.\" / \"But retrieval is not learning.\" [src](https://a16z.com/why-we-need-continual-learning/) (2026-04-22). Pattern: **the essay comes first, then the seed.**\n- **The a16z Mirendil post omits Kleiner.** The post says only \"we're leading the seed round\", while the company names a16z and Kleiner Perkins as co-leads [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirendil/), [src](https://mirendil.com/news/announcing-mirendil). Read a16z's \"leading\" language as possibly a co-lead.\n- **Cursor timing.** The 2026 retrospective places Truell \"in our office in June 2024\", two months before the Series A post of 2024-08-22. The retrospective is by Sarah Wang, Bornstein and Casado, and calls the outcome a $60B all-stock acquisition by SpaceX [src](https://a16z.com/cursor-spacexai-the-fastest-iterating-team-wins/), [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/). Casado's promotion post says \"He was lead on Cursor.\" [src](https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/). This conflicts with the repo's Cursor file, which treats Casado as the board and lead person.\n- **Compute-bound worldview (2023).** \"The generative AI boom is compute-bound.\" [src](https://a16z.com/navigating-the-high-cost-of-ai-compute/). He held this view two years before co-leading the Unconventional AI chip seed, where a16z wrote \"real innovation at the hardware layer is necessary\" [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/).\n- **Operator background.** \"Before becoming an investor, Matt started two companies operating as CTO.\" [src](https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.**\n- Since the GP promotion he has co-led three deals in exactly your seed bands:\n  - an AI-chip seed: Unconventional AI, $475M\n  - an inference-infra seed: Inferact, $150M at $800M\n  - a neolab seed: Mirendil, $200M\n- These are the $150M-$600M seeds you'd want a ~$20M slice of. He can steer a16z to a lead, so he decides who gets allocation.\n- His open-source grant network (vLLM, Nous Research, Teknium) is an early-talent pipeline that fits your pre-training and new-lab focus.\n- Caveat: he is a newly minted GP inside a big platform. a16z wants the lead and large ownership on these rounds, so he is a co-investor or allocation source for you, not a peer co-lead.\n\n## Background\n- Studied mathematics at Brown; HBS graduate [src](https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/).\n- Before investing: started two companies as CTO [src](https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/). Company names are not sourced here.\n- The repo roster lead row lists Blumberg Capital and LinkedIn as earlier jobs. That is not re-verified in a primary source here.\n- a16z: joined \"over six years ago\" (as of 2026-01-21), focused on AI/ML and data [src](https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/). His earliest a16z byline found is \"The New Business of AI\" (2020-02-16) [src](https://a16z.com/the-new-business-of-ai-and-how-its-different-from-traditional-software/).\n- Promoted to GP within the infrastructure fund on 2026-01-21 [src](https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/matt-bornstein/ | 45+ bylines, 2020-2026 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found (handle not confirmed; web search budget exhausted) | |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | a16z podcast, guest interviewer (Naveen Rao at NeurIPS; Inferact founders; Scott Chacon) | [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/the-80-year-bet-why-naveen-rao-is-rebuilding-the-computer-from-scratch/) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-12-08: a16z podcast, \"The 80-Year Bet: Why Naveen Rao Is Rebuilding the Computer from Scratch\". Bornstein interviews Rao at NeurIPS on the day of the seed post. No transcript was available [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/the-80-year-bet-why-naveen-rao-is-rebuilding-the-computer-from-scratch/).\n- 2026-01-22: a16z podcast, \"Inferact: Building the Infrastructure That Runs Modern AI\", with Woosuk Kwon and Simon Mo. No transcript [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/inferact-building-the-infrastructure-that-runs-modern-ai/).\n- 2026: \"Rethinking Git for the Age of Coding Agents\" with Scott Chacon (GitButler) [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/rethinking-git-for-the-age-of-coding-agents-with-github-cofounder-scott-chacon/).\n- Undated: \"Inside Cursor: The Anatomy of a Generational Startup\"; \"The Engine Powering Open-Source AI\"; \"What Is an AI Agent?\" [src](https://a16z.com/author/matt-bornstein/). Transcripts were not reviewed, so no quotes are attributed.\n\n## How they think\n- **Value capture:** in 2023 he wrote that infra vendors were \"likely the biggest winners in this market so far\" [src](https://a16z.com/who-owns-the-generative-ai-platform/).\n- **Compute is the bottleneck,** and hardware innovation is needed to beat Nvidia's ecosystem [src](https://a16z.com/navigating-the-high-cost-of-ai-compute/), [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/).\n- **Open source as a talent and market wedge:** Mistral A (2023), vLLM grants (2023), then the Inferact seed (2026) [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/).\n- **Continual learning and automated research are the next frontier** [src](https://a16z.com/why-we-need-continual-learning/), which led to the Mirendil deal.\n- **Developer experience over models:** Replicate as \"the Vercel of machine learning\" [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-replicate/). Cursor praised for not training its own foundation model (as of 2024) [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/).\n- **Calls right:** Cursor (Series A at $400M, later a ~$60B exit), Tabular (sold to Databricks), Replicate (sold to Cloudflare) [src](https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/). **Debatable:** the 2023 \"no systemic moats\" call, given how much value later concentrated in frontier labs.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Who Owns the Generative AI Platform?\" (2023-01-19): his value-capture map [src](https://a16z.com/who-owns-the-generative-ai-platform/)\n2. \"Navigating the High Cost of AI Compute\" (2023-04-27): the compute-bound thesis [src](https://a16z.com/navigating-the-high-cost-of-ai-compute/)\n3. \"Supporting the Open Source AI Community\" (2023-08-30): the grant list that fed later deals [src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/)\n4. \"Why We Need Continual Learning\" (2026-04-22): the pre-Mirendil thesis [src](https://a16z.com/why-we-need-continual-learning/)\n5. \"Investing in Unconventional\" (2025-12-08) [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X handle was confirmed, and the web search budget was exhausted during this run.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Unconventional AI | Seed ($475M) | 2025-12-08 | co-lead (a16z) | other co-lead not named in a16z post; Sequoia (Konstantine Buhler) per Sequoia site | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/) |\n| Inferact (vLLM) | Seed ($150M at $800M) | 2026-01-22 | co-lead with Lightspeed | Sequoia, Altimeter, Redpoint, ZhenFund, Databricks Ventures, others | [src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference) |\n| Mirendil | Seed ($200M) | 2026-06-24 | co-lead with Kleiner Perkins | NVIDIA after seed | [src](https://mirendil.com/news/announcing-mirendil) |\n| GitButler | Series A | 2026-04-08 | yes (a16z) | not named | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gitbutler/) |\n| Hedra | Series A | 2025-05-15 | not stated | not named | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-hedra/) |\n| Cursor | Series A ($60M at $400M) | 2024-08-22 | yes; Casado post says \"He was lead on Cursor\" | Thrive, OpenAI, angels | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/), [src](https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/) |\n| Mistral AI | Series A (EUR385M) | 2023-12-10 | a16z led; Midha first author | Lightspeed, General Catalyst, Salesforce | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/) |\n| Distributional | not stated | 2023-12-14 | not verified | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-distributional/) |\n| Pinecone | Series B ($100M) | 2023-04-27 | yes (a16z) | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-pinecone/) |\n| Coactive | Seed and Series A | 2023-03-21 | not stated | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-coactive/) |\n| Replicate | Series A | 2023-02-21 | yes (a16z) | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-replicate/) |\n| Hex | not stated | 2022-03-22 | not verified | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-hex/) |\n| Tabular | not stated | not found | yes, per Casado (\"lead on Tabular\") | exit to Databricks | [src](https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/) |\n| Tecton | not stated | not found | not stated | exit to Databricks | [src](https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/) |\n| Character.ai | not confirmed | | no Bornstein byline or credit found | | Not confirmed. Not in Casado's list or his author page |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Inferact.** a16z gave its first open-source AI grant to vLLM (announced 2023-08-30) and hosted the first vLLM meetup. Bornstein co-authored the grant post. a16z then co-led the $150M seed with Lightspeed on 2026-01-22 [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/), [src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference). **First in:** yes, about 29 months before the priced round.\n- **Mirendil.** The continual-learning essay with Aubakirova came out on 2026-04-22, and the co-led $200M seed with Kleiner on 2026-06-24 [src](https://a16z.com/why-we-need-continual-learning/), [src](https://mirendil.com/news/announcing-mirendil). a16z was in at inception (no earlier round).\n- **Unconventional AI.** Bornstein interviewed Naveen Rao at NeurIPS on the same day the seed was announced [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/the-80-year-bet-why-naveen-rao-is-rebuilding-the-computer-from-scratch/). Sequoia lists itself as partnering at \"Pre-Seed/Seed\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/unconventional-ai/). Who was first between a16z and Sequoia is unknown.\n- **Cursor.** Truell pitched enterprise adoption in a16z's office in June 2024. a16z led the Series A in August 2024, about 10 months after the OpenAI Startup Fund's $8M seed (2023-10) [src](https://a16z.com/cursor-spacexai-the-fastest-iterating-team-wins/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/10/11/anysphere-raises-8m-from-openai-to-build-an-ai-powered-ide/). **Not first.**\n- **Mistral.** Lightspeed led the seed in 2023-06. a16z led the A six months later, with Midha as lead author and Bornstein second [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/). **Not first.**\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Martin Casado | Infra fund head; announced his GP promotion; co-author on many pieces | c. 2019 | [src](https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/) |\n| Guido Appenzeller | Co-author (compute cost, gen-AI platform, Unconventional) | 2023 | [src](https://a16z.com/navigating-the-high-cost-of-ai-compute/) |\n| Raghu Raghuram | Co-author on Unconventional and Inferact seeds | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/) |\n| Malika Aubakirova | Co-author on continual learning and Mirendil | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirendil/) |\n| Jason Cui | Co-author on Inferact | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/) |\n| Rajko Radovanovic | Co-author on open-source grants, Mistral, Cursor | 2023 | [src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/) |\n| Anjney Midha | Lead author on Mistral A | 2023 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/) |\n| Sarah Wang | Co-author of Cursor retrospective | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/cursor-spacexai-the-fastest-iterating-team-wins/) |\n| Peter Levine | Co-author on Pinecone, GitButler | 2023 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-pinecone/) |\n| Naveen Rao | Founder of Unconventional AI; interviewed by him | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/the-80-year-bet-why-naveen-rao-is-rebuilding-the-computer-from-scratch/) |\n| Woosuk Kwon, Simon Mo | Inferact cofounders; vLLM grantees | 2023 | [src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/) |\n| Kleiner Perkins (Mamoon Hamid) | Mirendil seed co-lead | 2026 | [src](https://mirendil.com/news/announcing-mirendil) |\n| Lightspeed | Inferact seed co-lead | 2026 | [src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who co-led the Unconventional AI $475M seed besides a16z? The a16z post names no other lead. An earlier repo note says press named Lightspeed (not verified here).\n- What was his title on joining a16z (c. 2019), and was there an intermediate partner step before GP?\n- Are any other 2023 open-source grantees (Nous Research, Teknium) now raising, and is a16z first in?\n- Character.ai: is he named on it at all? No primary source found.\n- X handle and podcast transcripts were not reviewed, because the web search budget was exhausted this run.\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/\n- https://a16z.com/author/matt-bornstein/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/\n- https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mirendil/\n- https://mirendil.com/news/announcing-mirendil\n- https://a16z.com/why-we-need-continual-learning/\n- https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/\n- https://a16z.com/cursor-spacexai-the-fastest-iterating-team-wins/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-replicate/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-pinecone/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gitbutler/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-hedra/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-coactive/\n- https://a16z.com/who-owns-the-generative-ai-platform/\n- https://a16z.com/navigating-the-high-cost-of-ai-compute/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/the-80-year-bet-why-naveen-rao-is-rebuilding-the-computer-from-scratch/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/inferact-building-the-infrastructure-that-runs-modern-ai/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/unconventional-ai/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/10/11/anysphere-raises-8m-from-openai-to-build-an-ai-powered-ide/\n", "person:matt-nordby": "\n# Matt Nordby\n\n## One-line take\nNordby is the only person who legally controls Flume Ventures, so they are the real counterparty behind Flume's checks into Positron (seed through Series C), Hydra Host and Parasail. Their Sun Microsystems start explains why Scott McNealy is the public face of the fund.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** access, not technical sourcing. Nordby turned a career as an operator and dealmaker (Sun, Greenplum, Playboy licensing, Lion Capital PE) into a Lake Tahoe fund backed by about 40 wealthy individuals and families. Its pitch to founders is customer introductions from that network. Positron's CEO credits Flume with \"helping us sign partnerships with Fortune 100 companies\" [src](https://www.flume.ventures/).\n- **Control:** only Nordby. ADV Schedule A lists Leykwise, LLC as the sole direct owner (75%+, since 03/2023). Schedule B lists Nordby as Leykwise's 75%+ owner and control person since 10/2016 (individual CRD 7733303) [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/326134/PDF/326134.pdf). They sign every Form D as \"Managing Director of the General Partner of the Issuer\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2150724/000215072426000001/primary_doc.xml).\n- **But there is an investment committee:** Scott Flanders (ex-CEO of Playboy Enterprises 2009-2016, now Fathom Holdings chair) is \"a member of the investment committee of Flume Ventures\" per Fathom's 10-K/A [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1753162/000162828026028585/fthm-20251231.htm). The committee's other members and its voting rules are **unconfirmed**.\n- **Constraints:** a small fund (Fund I $80.5M sold; Entrepreneurs Fund $147M) with LPs who are individuals. The book is concentrated and secondary-heavy (SpaceX and 1Password head the logo wall), so seed checks are follow-the-crowd participations, not leads. No source shows Nordby leading or pricing an AI round.\n- **What they'd never do (inferred):** take institutional fund-of-funds money. The ADV shows 0% FoF and 0% non-US ownership and a $0 minimum, which suggests the fund is built on relationships rather than institutions.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Sun thread.** The Local Bounti proxy says Nordby \"launched his career at Sun Microsystems where he held various leadership positions\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000114036126016379/ny20065853x2_def14a.htm). This explains why Sun co-founder Scott McNealy carries Flume's brand (\"Operating Partner & Investor\") and why the site says the Sun and Workday founders back it, while Nordby alone owns the GP. The repo's \"Flume (Scott McNealy) led\" Hydra Host attribution is the wrong way round.\n- **Their ex-Playboy boss sits on their investment committee.** Nordby was \"President of Playboy Enterprises\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000114036126016379/ny20065853x2_def14a.htm). Scott Flanders was Playboy's CEO from July 2009 to May 2016 and is on Flume's investment committee [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1753162/000162828026028585/fthm-20251231.htm). That Nordby reported to Flanders is an inference from the overlapping titles. Nordby's Playboy dates are **unconfirmed**. Fathom's 2024 10-K/A already named Flanders on the committee, so it dates to at least early 2024 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1753162/000110465924054005/tm2412885d1_10ka.htm).\n- **They cleared their public-board seat the day after the fund closed.** Nordby resigned as Local Bounti's Lead Independent Director effective 2026-06-18 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000162828026044247/locl-20260617.htm). That was the day after they signed the Entrepreneurs Fund Form D/A showing $147M sold to 40 investors, fully subscribed and upsized from a $125M target [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084326/000208432626000001/primary_doc.xml). The 8-K cites no disagreement. That the two events are linked is an inference.\n- **Leykwise predates Flume by seven years.** Nordby has held the LLC that now owns the management company since 10/2016, while still in operating and PE roles [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/326134/PDF/326134.pdf). It looks like a personal investment vehicle. What it held before 2023 is **unconfirmed**.\n- **Still tied to Lion Capital.** In 2026 Nordby \"remains an advisor\" to Lion Capital [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000114036126016379/ny20065853x2_def14a.htm). The Local Bounti seat came through Lion's SPAC, Leo Holdings III, which merged with Local Bounti in November 2021 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000162828022007907/locl-20211231.htm).\n- **The AI-chip founders they appear with:** Flume's About page shows Nordby with Mitesh Agrawal (Positron) and Sid Sheth (d-Matrix) at Khanference [src](https://www.flume.ventures/about). Flume has no announced d-Matrix investment. This shows a relationship, not a role on a deal.\n- **Self-description lags the book.** In the 2026 proxy Flume is still \"focused on investing in enterprise technology companies,\" the same line as the 2023 launch, even though its largest new positions are AI silicon and GPU clouds.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Nordby doesn't lead or price rounds and writes undisclosed checks that are probably under $10M, so they are not a peer for a $20M seed check. Two things make them useful:\n1. **Co-investor in AI compute.** Flume is in Positron from the 2025-02 seed through the 2026-09 $875M Series C, plus Hydra Host (Seed+ 2025, Series A 2026) and Parasail (Series A 2026). If you are looking at Nevada or inference silicon, Nordby is a friendly existing holder with a cap-table view.\n2. **A route to wealthy-individual LPs.** Their LP base (Duffield, McNealy circle, 40 individuals and families, no FoF) is the kind that backs concentrated emerging managers.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** B.A., Auburn University [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000114036126016379/ny20065853x2_def14a.htm). Age 46 as of the April 2026 proxy (same src).\n- **Sun Microsystems:** career start, \"various leadership positions\" (dates **unconfirmed**) (same src).\n- **Greenplum:** executive who helped build the software start-up that EMC bought in 2010 (same src).\n- **Playboy Enterprises:** President, leading global licensing \"to over $1.5 billion in annual revenue\" (dates **unconfirmed**) (same src).\n- **Leykwise, LLC:** owner and managing director since 10/2016 [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/326134/PDF/326134.pdf).\n- **Lion Capital:** Partner, 2019-01 to 2023-04; advisor since [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000114036126016379/ny20065853x2_def14a.htm).\n- **Local Bounti (NYSE: LOCL):** director from the SPAC close in 2021-11 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000162828022007907/locl-20211231.htm). Later Lead Independent Director, Nominating and Governance chair and Compensation Committee member. Resigned effective 2026-06-18 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000162828026044247/locl-20260617.htm).\n- **Flume Ventures:** filed the Fund I Form D on 2023-02-03 ($100M target), first sale 2023-02-17 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1962310/000196231024000001/primary_doc.xml). Launch reported 2023-04-04 [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-04-04/lion-capital-s-nordby-starts-flume-ventures-seeks-100-million).\n- **How they got the seat:** they own it. The firm is self-founded, seeded by Tahoe-area wealthy individuals (Bloomberg named Dave Duffield and Annika Sörenstam) and built on Nordby's Sun-era ties to McNealy.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.flume.ventures/about | Firm site has no bio. Pictured at Khanference and Partner Summit (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Email (firm) | hello@flume.ventures | General inbox on the site footer [src](https://www.flume.ventures/about) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Not checked beyond the firm site; web search was unavailable this run |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/company/flume-ventures/ | Firm page listed in ADV 1.I. The personal profile was not verified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Imran Khan hosts \"The Khanversation\" for Flume |\n| SEC filer | https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?action=getcompany&CIK=0001893900 | Forms 3/4/144 for Local Bounti only (2021-2026) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. They appear in photos at Flume's Partner Summit (with Doug Burgum and Annika Sörenstam) and at Khanference [src](https://www.flume.ventures/about).\n\n## How they think\n- Operator-first: \"we're operators first\", and portfolio companies are \"partners\" [src](https://www.flume.ventures/). Value-add means enterprise customer introductions (Positron, Hydra Host, Rize).\n- Home-state bias: the Nevada Innovation Pledge reinvests a share of profits in the state [src](https://www.flume.ventures/about). Positron (Reno) is a top position.\n- PE-style concentration. The logo wall is ranked \"by invested capital,\" led by SpaceX, Positron, 1Password and GrubMarket [src](https://www.flume.ventures/about).\n\n## Read these first\n- Local Bounti 2026 DEF 14A director bio: the only full career summary in a primary source [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000114036126016379/ny20065853x2_def14a.htm)\n- Flume Form ADV (2026-03-27): ownership chain and LP counts [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/326134/PDF/326134.pdf)\n- Fathom Holdings 10-K/A: names Scott Flanders on Flume's investment committee [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1753162/000162828026028585/fthm-20251231.htm)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. Nordby has no public social presence in any source fetched.\n\n## Portfolio\nNo round announcement names Nordby personally. These are Flume Ventures deals, where Nordby is the sole control person of the GP:\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Positron | Seed ($23.5M) | 2025-02 | no | Valor Equity Partners; Atreides; Resilience Reserve | [src](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/02/12/ai-chip-startup-positron-raises-235-million-seed-round-to-take-on-nvidia) |\n| Positron | Series A ($51.6M) | 2025-07 | no (re-up) | Atreides; DFJ Growth; Valor | [src](https://convergedigest.com/positron-ai-raises-51-6m-series-a-to-scale-custom-inference-silicon/?amp=1) |\n| Positron | Series B ($230M) | 2026-02 | no (re-up) | Arena; Jump; Unless; QIA | [src](https://convergedigest.com/positron-ai-secures-230m-series-b-to-advance-memory-centric-ai-inference/?amp=1) |\n| Positron | Series C ($875M total at $5B post) | 2026-09 | no (re-up) | DFJ Growth; QIA; Arena; 1517; Unless; Banyan | [src](https://www.verdict.co.uk/positron-raises-875m/) |\n| Hydra Host | Seed+ ($8M SAFE; reported) | 2025-02 | reported lead, low confidence | Founders Fund | [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-02-11/hydrahost-raises-8m-at-a-500m-safe-cap-to-bring-gpu-rental-software-to-data-centers) |\n| Hydra Host | Series A ($100M) | 2026-06 | no (existing) | Kindred (lead); NVIDIA; ARK; Founders Fund | [src](https://hydrahost.com/blog/news/hydra-host-raises-100m-series-a/) |\n| Parasail | Series A ($32M) | 2026-04 | no | Touring; Kindred; Samsung NEXT; Banyan | [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/parasail-raises-32m-series-a-to-build-the-supercloud-that-puts-developers-in-control-of-their-ai-302742856.html) |\n\nFirst in: Positron at seed. Flume re-upped in every later round, so it is a holder through four rounds in 19 months. Who at Flume sourced the deal is **unconfirmed**.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Positron:** No source says how Flume got in. Plausible paths: the Nevada tie (Reno, Nevada Innovation Pledge) and Agrawal's presence at Khanference [src](https://www.flume.ventures/about). Both are **unconfirmed** as the intro path.\n- **Hydra Host:** the public credit goes to McNealy (named in the Series A release) and to GP Colin Delea (pictured with the CEO). Nordby approved the check as the GP's control person [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/326134/PDF/326134.pdf). Who sourced it is **unconfirmed**.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Flume Ventures | Founder, Managing Partner, sole control person | 2023-02 | [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/326134/PDF/326134.pdf) |\n| Lion Capital | Partner 2019-01 to 2023-04; advisor since | 2019-01 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000114036126016379/ny20065853x2_def14a.htm) |\n| Scott Flanders | On Flume's investment committee; Playboy CEO 2009-2016 while Nordby was a Playboy President (overlap inferred) | by 2024 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1753162/000162828026028585/fthm-20251231.htm) |\n| Scott McNealy | Flume Operating Partner and Investor; both came out of Sun | 2023 | [src](https://www.theheaviside.com/press-series-a) |\n| Colin Delea | Flume GP; also ex-Lion Capital | 2023 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240912121823/https://flume.ventures/community-2/) |\n| Mitesh Agrawal (Positron) | Pictured together at Khanference | n/a | [src](https://www.flume.ventures/about) |\n| Sid Sheth (d-Matrix) | Pictured together at Khanference | n/a | [src](https://www.flume.ventures/about) |\n| Local Bounti | Director, later Lead Independent Director | 2021-11 to 2026-06 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000162828026044247/locl-20260617.htm) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who else sits on Flume's investment committee, and does Nordby hold a veto?\n- Nordby's Playboy dates: did they report to Flanders?\n- What does Flume Opportunities I ($8M, 2026-08) hold? A Positron Series C SPV would fit the timing (unconfirmed).\n- Check sizes in Positron rounds, and whether Flume ever led or priced an AI round.\n- The Bloomberg launch article (paywalled, 403) may give Nordby's own thesis quotes.\n\n## Sources\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/326134/PDF/326134.pdf\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1962310/000196231023000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1962310/000196231024000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084326/000208432625000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084326/000208432626000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2150724/000215072426000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000114036126016379/ny20065853x2_def14a.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000162828022007907/locl-20211231.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840780/000162828026044247/locl-20260617.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1753162/000162828026028585/fthm-20251231.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1753162/000110465924054005/tm2412885d1_10ka.htm\n- https://www.flume.ventures/ and https://www.flume.ventures/about\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-04-04/lion-capital-s-nordby-starts-flume-ventures-seeks-100-million (headline only; 403)\n- Not checked this run: web search (budget exhausted), web.archive.org (blocked for the fetch tool), Companies House (Lion Capital LLP officer records), LinkedIn.\n", "person:matt-ocko": "\n# Matt Ocko\n\n## One-line take\nCo-founder of DCVC since about 2009, a repeat AI-chip board member (Nervana, Mythic) who has backed all three of Naveen Rao's companies, and unusually candid in public about LP mechanics, his mistakes and the deals he won't do.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He buys \"must-have\" technology at seed or Series A in areas others find scary: agriculture and microbes, launch, counter-drone, AI chips. At that stage he faces only five or six competing firms and gets better entry prices. He then concentrates the opportunity fund on the winners: about $500K to start, about $10M into the one that works [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc).\n- **How DCVC diligences.** About 50 outside technologists (\"Equity Partners\") hold direct carry and do the technical diligence, and he runs the firm with just one co-managing partner, Zachary Bogue, to avoid big-firm politics [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc).\n- **Constraints.** He depends on patient LPs, says he fears long adoption cycles, and filters hard for compute and data advantage.\n- **What he would never do.** He won't back consumer social, undifferentiated SaaS, opiate or tar-sands applications, or anything that sends money to the PRC [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/0e9b36d9/transcript).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Check-size habit (2016).** Small bets across a sector, then concentration on the winner: \"we can give $10 million to instead of $500,000\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc).\n- **Competition (00:28:20).** He says early deep tech is barely contested: \"The best deals in Silicon Valley are not necessarily competitive\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc).\n- **LP mechanics.** 85-90% of DCVC's LPs re-upped across funds, and Funds V and VI (about $300M) closed in 75 days. One prospective LP passed because an unmarked company \"hasn't been marked up by another VC it's worthless\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc). The funds were topped up to $387M in 2017 after very large investors approached DCVC unprompted [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/data-collective-tops-up-previously-closed-fifth-and-sixth-funds-for-a-new-total-of-387-million).\n- **Emerging-manager advice.** Land a $5-10M anchor from an LP with \"reputational stature\", then signal that you are closing. DCVC had written commitments for about 3x its fund [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc).\n- **Mistakes admitted:**\n  - a 1994 wireless and location chip that was too early;\n  - a late-1990s pre-LTE company his \"partners in a larger firm killed\", whose patents later sold for over $500M;\n  - an early-DCVC security deal with 12-13 stealth rivals that he says should have been a loss [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc).\n- **Return bar in writing.** The 2017 Rocket Lab memo states \"early stage investment parameters of 25x to 100x+ returns\" [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/space-is-open-for-business-announcing-dcvcs-investment-in-rocket-lab).\n- **Distributions.** He says DCVC returned about $1B to LPs in the last year and \"closer to $2,000,000,000 of net distributions\" over a couple of years (2025) [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/0e9b36d9/transcript).\n- **Reversal on weapons.** In 2016 he said \"we won't do stuff in in armaments\" (01:06:31) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc). By 2025 he described portfolio company Fortem as \"flocks of hunter killer drones\" [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/0e9b36d9/transcript).\n- **Mythic.** A $70M DoD grant was awarded over a year earlier and \"they still have not gotten that award\". He stresses the company has no Taiwan fab dependence (2025) [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/0e9b36d9/transcript).\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-11)\n- His thesis rests on fear rather than vision: \"in my cowardice I Retreat to investing in what I like to call the musthaves\" (Stanford, 2016) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc).\n- Hard filter: no compute and data \"cheat\", no investment [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc).\n- Founder-risk rule: the bigger danger is a founder's sincere \"Messianic belief\", not fraud [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc).\n- Opportunity-fund rule: \"contractually prohibited from chasing deals\" outside the portfolio (20VC 2016, AI notes page, low confidence) [src](https://www.deciphr.ai/podcast/20vc-data-collectives-matt-ocko-on-why-all-fund-size-models-are-wrong-and-the-lifecycle-of-current-venture-funds-is-inefficient).\n- Early AI view (2016): mass job loss doesn't need AGI (AI notes page, low confidence) [src](https://www.deciphr.ai/podcast/20vc-data-collectives-matt-ocko-on-the-dark-side-of-ai--what-happens-when-34m-people-become-unemployed-overnight).\n- Market view: \"The cool kids sectors exhibit downward spirals\" (2025) [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/0e9b36d9/transcript).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** DCVC led and was the largest investor in Nervana's 2015 Series A, with Ocko as director [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1617559/000161755915000001/primary_doc.xml). It co-led MosaicML's first money in 2020 [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/dcvc-companies-are-at-the-forefront-of-better-generative-ai-databricks-to-acquire-mosaicml-for-1-3b) and was in Unconventional AI's $475M seed [src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/). It has backed Mythic since 2016 and led its $125M round in 2025 [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/mythic-raises-125m-to-break-through-ais-power-wall).\n\nHis comfort with long, contrarian AI-hardware bets matches the owner's lens. The style differs: DCVC enters early and cheap, then concentrates, rather than writing $20M into a mega-seed. As LP-side intel, his candour on anchors, markups and re-ups is directly useful to someone raising.\n\n## Background\n- Started programming at 7 and his first company at 13 (Stanford, 2016) [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc). His first DoD contract came at 14 [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/0e9b36d9/transcript).\n- Yale, physics [src](https://www.dcvc.com/team/matthew-ocko).\n- Founded Da Vinci Systems (email software) and served as VP R&D until it was acquired [src](https://www.dcvc.com/team/matthew-ocko).\n- VC since the early 1990s [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc). Per an aggregator: founding GP at SoftBank Technology Ventures, partner at VantagePoint, venture partner at Sevin Rosen, co-founder and CTO of Teneros, and CIO (or MD) of Archimedes Capital from 2011 [src](https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/MATTHEW-OCKO-A07TM6/).\n- Met Zachary Bogue at a mutual friend's birthday party, co-invested with him, then co-founded Data Collective (DCVC) around 2009 [src](https://www.deciphr.ai/podcast/20vc-data-collectives-matt-ocko-on-why-all-fund-size-models-are-wrong-and-the-lifecycle-of-current-venture-funds-is-inefficient).\n- Angel or early investor in Facebook, Zynga, Uber, AngelList and Zoom [src](https://www.dcvc.com/team/matthew-ocko).\n- Advisor to In-Q-Tel and a long-time member of BENS [src](https://specialops.org/who-we-are/board-and-staff/matthew-ocko/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.dcvc.com/team/matthew-ocko | 53 bylined or co-signed posts, 2017-2026 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes on DCVC News & Insights |\n| X | https://x.com/mattocko | Not checked (X not fetchable) |\n| LinkedIn | linked from DCVC team page | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- **2016-05 · Stanford · \"The Art of Funding Deep Technology\"** ([YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc)). His richest source: LP mechanics, admitted mistakes, ethical passes.\n- **2016-08 · 20VC · fund-size models are wrong** ([episode](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-data-collectives-matt-ocko-on-eb2)). Opportunity-fund rules and $2-7M solo checks.\n- **2016-11 · 20VC · the dark side of AI** ([episode](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-data-collectives-matt-ocko-on-def)). Job losses without AGI.\n- **2017-09 · GIC Insights · on investing in AI and AI hype** ([YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkYKDSY3Szg)). Not mined.\n- **2019-05 · This Week in Startups (Angel S3E3)** ([episode](https://thisweekinstartups.com/episodes/8331c7-dcvc-s-matt-ocko-on-deep-tech-w-algorithmic-edge-difficult-markets-seed-risk-tak)). Covers meeting Elizabeth Holmes and deals he passed on (36:14). Transcript not reachable; not mined.\n- **2021-02 · Azeem Azhar's Exponential View** ([episode](https://globalplayer.com/podcasts/episodes/7DrcU9Y)). AbCellera and computational advantage. Not mined.\n- **2021-06 · SynBioBeta podcast · Star Trek vs Star Wars** ([episode](https://www.synbiobeta.com/listen/what-will-our-technology-future-look-like-star-trek-vs-star-wars)). Not mined.\n- **2025-09 · Bullets & Beans (Jake Chapman)** ([transcript](https://share.transistor.fm/s/0e9b36d9/transcript)). Distributions, Mythic's delayed grant, China.\n- **Undated:** We The Builders E26, SOSV \"Deep Tech OGs\", and Semil Shah's six-part \"Sunday Conversation\". All listed in `data/rows/matt-ocko-voice-media.csv`, not mined.\n\n## How they think\n- **Main thesis.** \"Must-haves\" sold to large enterprises and governments, where compute and data substitute for capex, opex and time. Enter in year 15 of a 30-year adoption arc, not year 1 or year 29 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc).\n- **Contrarian views:**\n  - consumer outcomes are \"stochastic\";\n  - the best early deals are uncontested;\n  - big SaaS rounds are riskier than hard tech [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/0e9b36d9/transcript).\n- **Calls:**\n  - Right: Nervana to Intel, MosaicML to Databricks, Rocket Lab going public.\n  - Wrong, in his own words: the 1994 chip, the pre-LTE company, the crowded security deal [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc).\n- **Shift.** He has moved from excluding armaments (2016) to openly backing autonomous counter-drone systems (2025).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Stanford talk, 2016 ([YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc), [transcript](https://rosetta.to/u/stanford/matt-ocko-the-art-of-funding-deep-technology)). His whole model, LP mechanics and mistakes.\n2. Bullets & Beans, 2025 ([transcript](https://share.transistor.fm/s/0e9b36d9/transcript)). His current defense, AI-chip and China stance, plus distribution figures.\n3. Rocket Lab memo, 2017 ([post](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/space-is-open-for-business-announcing-dcvcs-investment-in-rocket-lab)). The 25-100x return bar.\n4. Mythic $125M post, 2025 ([post](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/mythic-raises-125m-to-break-through-ais-power-wall)). His AI-chip conviction.\n\n## Social engagement\nX (@mattocko) is linked from the DCVC team page but could not be fetched. Nothing verified.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Nervana Systems | Series A ($20.5M) | 2015-06 | yes | DFJ, Allen & Co. | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1617559/000161755915000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Mythic | First check (as Isocline) | 2016 | unknown | | [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/mythic-raises-125m-to-break-through-ais-power-wall) |\n| Rocket Lab | Undisclosed | 2017-03 | unknown | | [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/space-is-open-for-business-announcing-dcvcs-investment-in-rocket-lab) |\n| Capella Space | Series B ($19M) | 2018-09 | co-led | Spark Capital | [src](https://medium.com/@nabeel/capella-space-raises-19m-series-b-satellite-swarms-with-synthetic-aperture-radar-for-continuous-5fe236491b93) |\n| MosaicML | Seed (co-led) | 2020 | co-led | Lux, Playground | [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/dcvc-companies-are-at-the-forefront-of-better-generative-ai-databricks-to-acquire-mosaicml-for-1-3b) |\n| Agility Robotics | Series B ($150M) | 2022-04 | co-led | Playground Global | [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/human-scale-robots-for-global-scale-abundance-resilience-and-dignity) |\n| Mythic | Growth ($125M) | 2025-12 | yes | Honda, Lockheed Martin | [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/mythic-raises-125m-to-break-through-ais-power-wall) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Agriculture microbe company (Stanford 2016; almost certainly Pivot Bio).** Other investors yawned at agriculture plus bacteria, so DCVC had little competition [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc).\n- **Drug-discovery founder (unnamed).** A woman founder whom other investors patronised. DCVC committed because it knew her prior company [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc).\n- **Fusion pitch.** A friend-of-a-friend proposed a fusion device to reach break-even for under $6M. It passed his compute and data test, and he said DCVC was looking at it in 2016 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc).\n- **Fortem.** Ocko says DCVC first invested about a decade before 2026 [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/neutralizing-the-swarm-fortem-cements-its-position-as-the-go-to-for-drone-defense).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Zachary Bogue | DCVC co-founder and co-MP | about 2009 | [src](https://www.deciphr.ai/podcast/20vc-data-collectives-matt-ocko-on-why-all-fund-size-models-are-wrong-and-the-lifecycle-of-current-venture-funds-is-inefficient) |\n| Naveen Rao | Backed Nervana, MosaicML and Unconventional AI | 2015 | [src](https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/dcvc-2025-q3-update) |\n| Nabeel Hyatt (Spark) | Co-led Capella Series B | 2018 | [src](https://medium.com/@nabeel/capella-space-raises-19m-series-b-satellite-swarms-with-synthetic-aperture-radar-for-continuous-5fe236491b93) |\n| Mubadala | DCVC MENA fund partner via Hub71 | 2019 | [src](https://www.hub71.com/latest-news/press-release/mubadala-launches-mena-tech-investment-vehicles-us-150m-fund-of-funds-and-us-100m-direct-fund) |\n| Sapphire Partners | DCVC LP he praises | by 2018 | [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20181129063952/http://sapphireventures.com/about/fund-investments/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **TWiST 2019 (36:14):** which deals did he pass on, and what did he say about meeting Elizabeth Holmes? Transcript not reachable.\n- **Sovereign LPs:** which very large investors joined the 2017 top-up? A pool of nearly $1T suggests sovereigns (Mubadala? GIC, where he spoke in 2017?).\n- **Archimedes Capital:** what is it (from 2011)?\n- **Rocket Lab id:** \"Rocket Lab\" canonicalises to the unrelated `rocket` id. The main session should add `rocket,rocket-lab-usa` to `data/not_duplicates.csv`.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XEc7B9r-Isc (transcript: https://rosetta.to/u/stanford/matt-ocko-the-art-of-funding-deep-technology)\n- https://share.transistor.fm/s/0e9b36d9/transcript\n- https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-data-collectives-matt-ocko-on-eb2\n- https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-data-collectives-matt-ocko-on-def\n- https://www.dcvc.com/team/matthew-ocko\n- https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/space-is-open-for-business-announcing-dcvcs-investment-in-rocket-lab\n- https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/data-collective-tops-up-previously-closed-fifth-and-sixth-funds-for-a-new-total-of-387-million\n- https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/neutralizing-the-swarm-fortem-cements-its-position-as-the-go-to-for-drone-defense\n- https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/MATTHEW-OCKO-A07TM6/ (aggregator)\n- https://specialops.org/who-we-are/board-and-staff/matthew-ocko/\n", "person:matthias-plappert": "\n# Matthias Plappert\n\n## One-line take\nHe was the technical voice behind Factorial's 2024 research essays. He now runs his own ML consultancy and angel-invests in European deep tech, so he is a researcher-angel to track, not a Factorial decision-maker [src](https://matthiasplappert.com/about).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he mattered:** He was an OpenAI researcher on the robot hand that solved a Rubik's Cube and on the Codex paper, then at GitHub on Copilot [src](https://matthiasplappert.com/about). Factorial's bio called him \"one of the authors of the Codex paper\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250215230138/https://factorialfunds.com/team). His Sora essay gave Factorial a widely shared, quantitative inference-demand estimate [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240320142226/https://www.factorialfunds.com/blog/under-the-hood-how-openai-s-sora-model-works).\n- **Constraint:** At Factorial he had no named deals, and Sol Bier owns every decision. He stepped down to Venture Partner by 2025-02 and was gone by 2025-06.\n- **Now:** He runs dfdx labs, applying ML to hard technical problems for companies, and says \"I angel-invest in European deep tech startups.\" His focus is ML plus power markets and renewables [src](https://matthiasplappert.com/about).\n\n## Differential insight\n- Sora essay estimate: \"a peak demand of ~720k Nvidia H100 GPUs for inference\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240320142226/https://www.factorialfunds.com/blog/under-the-hood-how-openai-s-sora-model-works).\n- Llama 3 essay, which he co-wrote with Durk Kingma and the Factorial investing team: over-training small models is rational because \"inference compute is incurred continuously\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240523113601/https://factorialfunds.com/blog/thoughts-on-llama-3).\n- After he left, the next essay (DeepSeek, Feb 2025) ran under the house byline \"Factorial Insights\". The 2026 site has no blog link at all.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. He is ex-OpenAI pretraining-adjacent talent and an angel in European deep tech, energy and robotics. He is useful as a technical reference or co-angel on European research spinouts. Not a capital source.\n\n## Background\n- BSc and MSc in Computer Science, Karlsruhe Institute of Technology (graduated with distinction) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250215230138/https://factorialfunds.com/team).\n- OpenAI: research scientist and team lead (dexterous manipulation; Codex). GitHub: Copilot. Factorial Funds Partner, then Venture Partner (2023–2025). dfdx labs (current).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://matthiasplappert.com | blog restarted 2026-02; about 8 posts through 2026-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://matthiasplappert.com/archive | posts on agents, humanoids, robotics setup |\n| X | none found | Bluesky: matthiasplappert.com; Mastodon: @mplappert |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/matthiasplappert/ | |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/matthiasplappert | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found.\n\n## How they think\n- Scaling plus inference economics: training is a one-off cost and inference scales with users (Sora and Llama 3 essays).\n- 2026 personal blog topics: \"Let's talk about the humanoid robot in the room\" (2026-02-25) and \"Two types of AI agents\" (2026-10-06) [src](https://matthiasplappert.com/archive). Not mined yet.\n\n## Read these first\n- Sora essay (2024-03-15): compute and inference projections.\n- Thoughts on Llama 3 (2024-04-24): the data and over-training argument.\n- matthiasplappert.com/about: his current focus.\n\n## Social engagement\nLow-volume; on Bluesky and Mastodon rather than X.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| none named | | | | | |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Factorial Funds | Partner, then Venture Partner | 2023-09 to 2025-06 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230925170526/http://factorialfunds.com/team) |\n| Durk Kingma | co-author (Llama 3 essay) | 2024 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240523113601/https://factorialfunds.com/blog/thoughts-on-llama-3) |\n| OpenAI, GitHub | former research roles | | [src](https://matthiasplappert.com/about) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which European deep-tech companies has he angel-invested in? Any connection to Fractile (UK)?\n- Why did he step back from Factorial in 2025?\n\n## Sources\n- https://matthiasplappert.com/about\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20250215230138/https://factorialfunds.com/team\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20240320142226/https://www.factorialfunds.com/blog/under-the-hood-how-openai-s-sora-model-works\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20240523113601/https://factorialfunds.com/blog/thoughts-on-llama-3\n", "person:max-gazor": "\n# Max Gazor\n\n## One-line take\nThe CRV partner behind Reflection, Dyna, Airtable and Cribl who left to write $5-30M first cheques into AI labs and chip teams from a ten-LP, $165M fund.\n\n## Crux\nHe wins by committing a large cheque and a board seat before there is a company, then staying on the board for a decade (Airtable from 2015 to its 2026 sale). He is constrained by a single small fund with half its capital pointed at Israel, and by being the only named officer on the vehicle. He will not invest after revenue: his page reports 100% of investments made pre-revenue.\n\n## Differential insight\n- He sells cheque size from a small fund: \"We can write large checks like the biggest funds\" [src](https://www.ynetnews.com/business/article/sk11tpvkebe) (2026-01-05).\n- He positions against multi-stage firms: \"you raised capital from an asset manager, not a venture capitalist.\" [src](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/day-0-striker-venture-partners-max-gazor-oviic/) (2025-10-01).\n- The fund's own page shows 13 inception entries against a pitch of \"10 LPs, 10 investments\" [src](https://www.striker.vc/news) [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio).\n- Elorian is the only Striker-era company on his board list; Ricursive, Inferact and Edison are credited to him by the firm but he lists no board seat there [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor).\nRows: `data/rows/early-striker-venture-partners-insights.csv`.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. Inception cheques of the size I write, into exactly my categories: Ricursive and Tenspresso (AI chips), Reflection and Elorian (labs), Inferact (inference compute).\n\n## Background\nBS EECS UC Berkeley; MS EECS MIT (PhD candidate); MBA Harvard. Corporate development at Cisco and Oracle. CRV for over 14 years, Midas List 2022 to 2026, still a CRV board partner. Founded Striker in 2025; Fund I first sale 2025-09-05 [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084186/000208418625000003/primary_doc.xml).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor | Board list and stats |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/day-0-striker-venture-partners-max-gazor-oviic/ | One launch essay |\n| X | https://x.com/maxgazor | Not read |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/maxgazor/ | Posts deal news (Scanner A, 2026-03) |\n| GitHub | none found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-10 — WSJ Pro — launch interview (\"One Fund, 10 LPs, 10 Investments\") — [link](https://www.wsj.com/articles/new-firm-striker-venture-partners-has-a-simple-formula-one-fund-10-lps-10-investments-5e5ae631) — paywalled, not read\n- 2026-01 — Ynet — Israel launch interview — [link](https://www.ynetnews.com/business/article/sk11tpvkebe) — the pre-revenue style \"is extremely effective at the seed stage\"\n- 2026-04 — WSJ — quoted on cloud stocks and VC returns — not read\nNo podcast with a transcript was found.\n\n## How they think\n- Concentration is the product: ten companies, deep board work, pro rata honoured.\n- Multi-stage platforms are asset managers; founders and LPs both lose from spray.\n- Day-zero entry is the only way to own enough of a lab or chip team before a $500M mark.\n\n## Read these first\n- \"Day 0: Striker Venture Partners\" — the whole thesis in one page.\n- Ynet 2026-01-05 — the only place he and Lamdan are quoted at length.\n- striker.vc/news — what he chooses to claim, in order.\n\n## Social engagement\nFirm account [@strikervp](https://x.com/strikervp) and LinkedIn carry event recaps (NeurIPS with Sequoia and Reflection; Anthropic and Stanford GSB). Personal X not reviewed.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Reflection AI | Inception (CRV); seed $25M | 2024; 2025-03 | Co-led with Sequoia; board | Sequoia; Lightspeed | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor) |\n| Dyna Robotics | Inception (CRV) | 2024-08 | Board | First Round | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor) |\n| Ricursive Intelligence | Inception (Striker) | 2025 | First cheque claimed | Sequoia (seed lead); Lightspeed (A) | [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio) |\n| Inferact | Inception (Striker) | 2025 | First cheque claimed | a16z; Lightspeed | [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio) |\n| Edison Scientific | Inception (Striker) | 2025 | Participant | | [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio) |\n| Elorian AI | Seed $55M | 2026-04-09 | Co-led; board | Menlo; Altimeter; NVIDIA | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor) |\n| Tenspresso | $20M first money (firm-level; partner not named) | 2026-10 | Lead | Altimeter bidding | [src](https://runtimewire.com/article/tenspresso-500-million-valuation-talks-altimeter-striker) |\n| Deductive AI | Inception (CRV) | n/a | Board; sold to Elastic 2026-07 | | [src](https://www.striker.vc/news) |\n| Lepton AI | Pre-revenue (CRV) | n/a | Board; sold to NVIDIA | | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor) |\n| Scanner, Cribl, Airtable, Respond Software, CloudGenix | Pre-revenue (CRV) | Airtable 2015 | Board | | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- Elorian: The Information reported the ex-DeepMind team \"working with Striker\" on 2026-01-10; the $55M seed was announced 2026-04-09 with Menlo and Altimeter as co-leads [src](https://www.striker.vc/news).\n- Tenspresso: Striker's $20M came first; within weeks Altimeter approached at a $500M valuation [src](https://runtimewire.com/article/tenspresso-500-million-valuation-talks-altimeter-striker).\n- No founder-side account of an intro path was found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CRV | Former GP; still board partner | about 2011 | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor) |\n| Brian Zhan | Partner; worked under him at CRV | 2025 | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/brian-zhan) |\n| Matan Lamdan | Partner (Israel, cyber) | 2026-01 | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/r1v9fnf411g) |\n| Sequoia Capital | Repeat co-investor and event co-host | 2025 | [src](https://www.striker.vc/news) |\n| Altimeter Capital | Follows into inception deals | 2026 | [src](https://runtimewire.com/article/tenspresso-500-million-valuation-talks-altimeter-striker) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who are the ten to twelve LPs?\n- Is a Fund II or opportunity vehicle being raised, given 13 entries on the portfolio page?\n- Which partner sourced Tenspresso and Ricursive?\n\n## Sources\nhttps://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor ; https://www.striker.vc/news ; https://www.striker.vc/portfolio ; https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/day-0-striker-venture-partners-max-gazor-oviic/ ; https://www.ynetnews.com/business/article/sk11tpvkebe ; https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/r1v9fnf411g ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084186/000208418625000003/primary_doc.xml ; https://runtimewire.com/article/tenspresso-500-million-valuation-talks-altimeter-striker\n", "person:max-rimpel": "\n# Max Rimpel\n\n## One-line take\nRimpel is GC's most frequent non-MD name on frontier-AI deals. Their strongest personal evidence is the three investment memos they co-bylined: Engram (first round), Spiral (Series A) and Modal (Series C). River AI and Cognition rest only on portfolio-page tags.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win.** They are the working investor on GC's AI infrastructure and research-spinout deals. GC publishes their name as a co-author of the investment case on Engram ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-engram), 2026-06-23), Modal ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-modal), 2026-05-21) and Spiral ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-spiral), 2025-09-11). GC is lead or co-lead on all three. Engram was a first round, which means they were in at inception on a Chris Ré lab spinout.\n- **What constrains them.** On every deal the owner cares about, they appear next to an MD or the CEO. Quentin Clark is on 8 of them, Marc Bhargava on 5 and Hemant Taneja on 3 (GC portfolio pages, see Portfolio). The deals where they are the **only** GC name are smaller and further from the lens: Highlight, Sapien, Accordance, Anagram and Bandana. Independent lead authority on large AI rounds is not shown by any source.\n- **Correction to the premise (River AI).** River AI's own announcement names only Hemant Taneja and Marc Bhargava for GC ([src](https://river.ai/series-seed-series-a-funding)), and GC's River story is a Taneja interview ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/own-your-ai-experience)). Rimpel's River credit is a portfolio-page tag only ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/river-ai)). Treat it as \"on the deal team\", not \"sourced it\".\n- **What they'd never do.** Nothing sourced. They have no public writing beyond co-authored firm memos, and no podcasts were found.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **In at inception on Engram.** The memo, co-bylined Bhargava + Rimpel, says \"We were lucky enough to co-lead the first round of funding with Modern Capital\" ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-engram), 2026-06-23). That makes GC and Modern Capital co-first institutional money in a research lab founded by Dan Biderman, Sabri Eyuboglu, Jessy Lin, Jack Morris and Scott Linderman out of Chris Ré's lab. This is a firm statement in a co-authored memo, not personal speech.\n- **The Engram thesis they put their name to** says \"the constraint holding AI back is memory, not reasoning power\" ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-engram)). The bet is training knowledge into weights instead of using retrieval.\n- **Earliest memo byline:** Spiral, with Quentin Clark: \"That’s why we’re leading their Series A.\" ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-spiral), 2025-09-11).\n- **Modal:** \"General Catalyst is co-leading Modal's Series C.\" ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-modal), 2026-05-21), co-bylined with Clark and Katie Keller.\n- **Windsurf credit belongs to Clark publicly.** TechCrunch quotes \"Quentin Clark, managing director at General Catalyst\" on GC's lead of the $150M Codeium Series C ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/29/github-copilot-competitor-codeium-raises-150m-at-a-1-25b-valuation/), 2024-08-29). Rimpel is only on GC's tag list ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/windsurf)).\n- **Mercor (inference, low confidence).** GC's Mercor page lists Rimpel *first*, ahead of MD Marc Bhargava, \"Backed since 2023\" ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/mercor)). 2023 is the year GC led Mercor's $3.6M seed ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/20/mercor-an-ai-recruiting-startup-founded-by-21-year-olds-raises-100m-at-2b-valuation/)). That makes them a plausible seed sponsor of a company now valued at $10B, but no source states it.\n- **Their own remit, from a first-person GC bio:** \"B2B investment opportunities, now across both New York and Europe\" ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/max-rimpel)). This fits their European AI co-tags (Parloa, Pelico, both with Jeannette zu Fürstenberg).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, with a credit discount.** The deals that matter to me:\n- **Engram:** a frontier memory research lab; first round; Rimpel co-bylined. This is the cleanest early credit.\n- **General Intuition:** a new frontier lab; $133.7M seed co-led by Khosla and GC ([src](https://gamesbeat.com/general-intuition-raises-133-7m-to-build-an-ai-frontier-research-lab-for-the-game-industry/)). The press names no GC person; Rimpel is a co-tag with Taneja and Clark.\n- **River AI:** a new lab; $1.1B seed+A co-led by GC and AMP PBC. Rimpel is tag-only (see Crux).\n- **Modal:** AI compute; Series C co-lead; co-bylined.\n\nIf I'm writing ~$20M into a large lab seed, they are a useful working-level contact at GC: close to the deal flow, with Taneja, Bhargava and Clark as the decision-makers. They are not a standalone sponsor I could anchor on.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in a small village in Germany. High school in the US, university in Berlin, advanced degrees in Paris and Madrid. Stints at intergovernmental institutions and in equity market research in the US. Then Google in London ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/max-rimpel), first-person bio). No dates are given.\n- Index Ventures, title Principal per TheOrg ([aggregator](https://theorg.com/org/general-catalyst/org-chart/max-rimpel)). A WebSearch snippet attributed to MarketScreener gives 2017; it is unverified and the page is login-walled.\n- General Catalyst, New York. TheOrg says Partner, focused on \"all things AI\". 'Since 2021' comes only from the unverified MarketScreener snippet. The earliest \"backed since\" on their GC deals is 2023 (Bandana, Mercor).\n- TheOrg also lists Taxfix as a previous company, with no role or dates.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/max-rimpel | GC bio, 18 portfolio companies (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Co-authored GC memos only (Engram, Modal, Spiral) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/max_rimpel | Linked from GC; X returned 402, so not read |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/maxrimpel/ | Linked from GC; not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The web-search budget ran out this run, so there was no podcast or talk search. Worth a follow-up.\n\n## How they think\nFrom co-signed firm memos, not personal statements:\n- **The data layer is overdue for reinvention because AI, not humans, will be its main consumer** (Spiral memo).\n- **The bottleneck moves from intelligence to execution.** A substrate for GPU workloads wins (Modal memo).\n- **Memory, not reasoning, is the constraint.** Knowledge should live in weights (Engram memo).\n\nTaken together, they back technical founders rebuilding a layer of the AI stack (data format, compute runtime, memory), often from research labs or elite systems teams. Examples: Palantir/Citadel at Spiral, IOI medalists at Modal, Stanford/MIT/Cornell at Engram.\n\nThere are no public calls, right or wrong, beyond the portfolio itself.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Our Investment in Engram](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-engram) (2026-06-23). The most frontier-relevant thesis; a first-round co-lead.\n- [Our Investment in Spiral](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-spiral) (2025-09-11). AI-native data infrastructure; a Series A lead.\n- [Our Investment in Modal](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-modal) (2026-05-21). AI compute as a substrate; a Series C co-lead.\n- [GC bio](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/max-rimpel). Background and the NY + Europe B2B remit.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X returned 402 and LinkedIn was not read.\n\n## Portfolio\nAll 18 companies on their GC page ([src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/max-rimpel)). Co-tags come from each GC portfolio page.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| River AI | Seed + Series A, $1.1B | 2026-08-11 | GC co-lead | AMP PBC (co-lead), NVIDIA, AMD Ventures, YC, Temasek. GC tags: Taneja, Bhargava, Rimpel | [company](https://river.ai/series-seed-series-a-funding), [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/river-ai) |\n| Engram | First round (amount n/d) | 2026-06-23 | GC co-lead | Modern Capital. Memo: Bhargava + Rimpel | [memo](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-engram) |\n| Modal | Series C (amount n/d in memo) | 2026-05-21 | GC co-lead | Memo: Clark, Rimpel, Keller | [memo](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-modal) |\n| Cognition | Series D, >$1B at $26B | 2026-05-27 | GC co-lead | Lux, 8VC. GC tags: Taneja, Bhargava, Rimpel (page says backed since 2025) | [company](https://cognition.com/blog/series-d), [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/cognition) |\n| General Intuition | Seed, $133.7M | 2025-10-16 | GC co-lead | Khosla (co-lead), Raine. GC tags: Taneja, Clark, Rimpel | [GamesBeat](https://gamesbeat.com/general-intuition-raises-133-7m-to-build-an-ai-frontier-research-lab-for-the-game-industry/), [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/general-intuition) |\n| Spiral | Series A (amount n/d) | 2025-09-11 | GC led | Memo: Clark + Rimpel | [memo](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-spiral) |\n| Windsurf (Codeium) | Series C, $150M at $1.25B | 2024-08-29 | GC led | Kleiner Perkins, Greenoaks. Clark quoted. GC tags: Clark, Rimpel, Bhargava | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/29/github-copilot-competitor-codeium-raises-150m-at-a-1-25b-valuation/) |\n| Mercor | Seed, $3.6M; then B and C as existing investor | 2023; 2025-02-20; 2025-10-27 | GC led the seed | Felicis led B and C. GC tags: Rimpel, Bhargava | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/20/mercor-an-ai-recruiting-startup-founded-by-21-year-olds-raises-100m-at-2b-valuation/), [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/mercor) |\n| Lumaril | n/d | 2026 | unknown | GC tags: Clark + Rimpel | [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/lumaril) |\n| Parloa | n/d | 2025 | unknown | GC tags: zu Fürstenberg + Rimpel | [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/parloa) |\n| Pelico | n/d | 2025 | unknown | GC tags: zu Fürstenberg, Bohn, Rimpel | [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/pelico) |\n| Accordance | n/d | 2025 | unknown | Rimpel solo | [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/accordance) |\n| Anagram | n/d | 2025 | unknown | Rimpel solo | [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/anagram) |\n| Highlight | n/d | 2024 | unknown | Rimpel solo | [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/highlight) |\n| Sapien | n/d | 2024 | unknown | Rimpel solo | [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/sapien) |\n| Standard Bots | n/d | 2024 | unknown | GC tags: Clark + Rimpel | [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/standard-bots) |\n| Datavolo | n/d | 2024 | unknown | GC tags: Clark + Rimpel | [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/datavolo) |\n| Bandana | n/d | 2023 | unknown | Rimpel solo | [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/bandana) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Timing: were they first?**\n  - **Yes, as co-first institutional money:** Engram (first round, with Modern Capital), River AI (first institutional round, with AMP PBC), General Intuition (seed, with Khosla) and Mercor (GC led the 2023 seed; Benchmark followed at the 2024 A, per the repo's Mercor round map).\n  - **No:**\n    - Windsurf: Greenoaks led the 2022 Exafunction A and Kleiner the 2023 B before GC's 2024 C.\n    - Cognition: Founders Fund and others were existing investors at the 2026 Series D.\n    - Modal: GC entered at the C.\n- **How they source:** no source describes their sourcing.\n  - The pattern suggests a lot of it runs through GC's senior partners. Clark is on the infrastructure deals; Taneja and Bhargava are on the labs.\n  - Research-lab spinouts (Engram from the Ré lab) suggest academic-network sourcing.\n  - The solo deals (Highlight, Accordance, Sapien, Anagram, Bandana) are probably their own sourcing, but no source confirms that.\n- No intro paths found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Quentin Clark (GC MD) | Co-signer on 8 deals; co-author of the Spiral and Modal memos | 2024 | [Spiral memo](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-spiral) |\n| Marc Bhargava (GC MD) | Co-author of the Engram memo; co-tag on Mercor, River, Cognition, Windsurf | 2023 | [Engram memo](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-engram) |\n| Hemant Taneja (GC CEO) | Co-tag on River AI, Cognition, General Intuition | 2025 | [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/river-ai) |\n| Katie Keller (GC) | Co-author of the Modal memo | 2026 | [Modal memo](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-modal) |\n| Jeannette zu Fürstenberg (GC) | Co-tag on Parloa and Pelico | 2025 | [GC](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/parloa) |\n| Modern Capital | Co-lead with GC on the Engram first round | 2026 | [Engram memo](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-engram) |\n| Index Ventures | Former employer (Principal per TheOrg) | unknown | [TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/general-catalyst/org-chart/max-rimpel) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact GC title and dates: the GC card is blank, TheOrg says Partner, and the MarketScreener \"2021\" is unverified. LinkedIn would settle it.\n- Did they source Mercor's 2023 seed? They are listed ahead of Bhargava, and Bonatsos is also called a Mercor investor.\n- Who is \"Modern Capital\" (the Engram co-lead)?\n- What are Highlight, Accordance, Sapien and Anagram, their solo deals?\n- What did they do at Index (which deals), and what is the Taxfix connection?\n- Podcasts, X posts and talks: not searched this run because the search budget ran out.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/team/max-rimpel\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-engram\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-modal\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-spiral\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/own-your-ai-experience\n- https://river.ai/series-seed-series-a-funding\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/ (river-ai, mercor, general-intuition, cognition, engram, modal, windsurf, spiral, lumaril, parloa, pelico, highlight, accordance, sapien, anagram, bandana, standard-bots, datavolo)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/29/github-copilot-competitor-codeium-raises-150m-at-a-1-25b-valuation/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/20/mercor-an-ai-recruiting-startup-founded-by-21-year-olds-raises-100m-at-2b-valuation/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/mercor-quintuples-valuation-to-10b-with-350m-series-c/\n- https://gamesbeat.com/general-intuition-raises-133-7m-to-build-an-ai-frontier-research-lab-for-the-game-industry/\n- https://cognition.com/blog/series-d\n- https://theorg.com/org/general-catalyst/org-chart/max-rimpel (aggregator)\n- https://www.lumaril.com/\n", "person:mel-demma": "\n# Mel Demma (Melissa Demma)\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (owner pick). Sources: wndr.vc JS bundle (live and Wayback 2026-06-10), archived wndrco.com team page (2026-04-30), Swift Ventures bio page (Wayback, 2023-09 to 2026-02), Differential Ventures team page (Wayback 2023-01-04), EVCA leadership interview (2025-01-07), SEC Form D for Swift Ventures Fund II, press on Dash Bio. Pronouns: Swift, Differential and EVCA all use \"she\".*\n\n**Identity resolved (was `needs-id`).** \"Mel Demma\" on wndr.vc is Melissa Demma, ex-Swift and ex-Differential:\n1. wndr.vc's own team data links \"Mel Demma\" to `linkedin.com/in/melissademma` ([src](https://wndr.vc/team), bundle `assets/index-CuMFcD--.js`, read 2026-11-10).\n2. Swift's bio page is titled \"Melissa Demma\" and calls her \"Mel\" throughout ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250614162910id_/https://www.swift.vc/team/melissa)).\n3. Differential's 2023 bio and Swift's bio share the same unusual details: USC Marshall BS in Business Administration, USC Marshall Partners Board, and the executive team of Destined for X ([Differential](https://web.archive.org/web/20230104022519id_/https://www.differential.vc/team), [Swift](https://web.archive.org/web/20230922172957id_/https://www.swift.vc/team/melissa)). NFX Signal (aggregator) lists WndrCo, Swift and Differential under one profile ([src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/melissa-demma)). The StartupIntros \"Differential associate\" entry links the same LinkedIn URL and is simply stale ([src](https://startupintros.com/people/melissa-demma)).\nConfidence: WndrCo = Swift = \"Mel\" is high (the firms' own pages). Differential = Swift is high but derived (matching bio text plus the aggregator).\n\n## One-line take\nShe's a community-built, enterprise-AI and automation seed investor. She climbed from analyst through four small funds into a seed partner seat at WndrCo, where her checks are small.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** network assembly. At Swift she co-led the Emerging Venture Capitalists Association's San Francisco chapter, running curated dinners, fireside chats with managing partners, and topic happy hours for junior VCs ([EVCA](https://web.archive.org/web/20250113162532id_/https://www.evca.org/blog/leadership-spotlight-san-francisco-chapter-co-lead-mel-demma-of-swift-ventures)). Every bio she has had stresses in-person convening and hands-on help with hiring, ideas and first customers. Her peer network of rising associates and principals is the sourcing edge WndrCo's seed book is buying.\n- **Constraint:** every fund she has worked at is small. Swift Ventures Fund II is $43.4M from 90 investors ([Form D/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1978069/000197806924000001/primary_doc.xml)), and WndrCo's Seed III targets $65M ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2058875/000205984325000002/primary_doc.xml)) for about 25 bets a year ([src](https://wndr.vc/about)). Her checks will be sub-$3M (inference), and the aggregator range is $50K-$2M.\n- **What she'd never do (inference):** anchor a $100M+ lab seed. She has never been at a firm that could, and no public deal names her as a lead.\n- **Track record:** no public deal names her personally as lead or board member. The only deal she cites by name is Dash Bio (Swift participant, 2024). Treat her as an unproven picker with a strong network.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Built an AI screen for public stocks at a seed fund.** She named the Swift AI Index as her proudest recent work: \"a fine-tuned LLM to analyze earnings and regulatory filings\" ([EVCA, 2025-01-07](https://web.archive.org/web/20250113162532id_/https://www.evca.org/blog/leadership-spotlight-san-francisco-chapter-co-lead-mel-demma-of-swift-ventures)). The goal was to find \"true AI companies\" beyond the Magnificent Seven, with AppLovin as her surprise pick. This shows a quantitative, data-screening instinct, consistent with her time at Magerman's Differential (founded by a Renaissance alum).\n- **Contrarian view: AI drug development over AI drug discovery.** She sees a bigger opportunity in \"the development side of biopharma R&D\" ([EVCA](https://web.archive.org/web/20250113162532id_/https://www.evca.org/blog/leadership-spotlight-san-francisco-chapter-co-lead-mel-demma-of-swift-ventures)). Her argument: a clinical-trial capacity bottleneck is coming. Dash Bio, founded by Moderna alums, was the bet.\n- **EVCA was her own on-ramp.** She said EVCA was \"a great resource to me when I was just starting out\" (same source). She now runs the program that helped her, which matters for how she sources.\n- **Thesis drift: female founders, then data/ML, then enterprise AI and automation.** At Shatter Fund she focused on \"female-founded companies\" ([Differential bio](https://web.archive.org/web/20230104022519id_/https://www.differential.vc/team)). Differential is a data-science and ML seed fund. At Swift she covered \"early-stage enterprise AI and automation\" (EVCA).\n- **Swift re-hiring her seat.** Swift's live team page no longer lists her and posts an \"Investment Principal\" opening ([src](https://swift.vc/team), read 2026-11-10). Her Swift bio was still captured on 2026-02-15, and she was not on the WndrCo page of 2026-04-30 but was on wndr.vc by 2026-06-10. The move happened between roughly March and June 2026.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Against THESIS.md: she covers enterprise AI and AI-native automation (the Serval-type category) at seed, and she sits on a seed bench at a firm that also writes growth checks into Harvey, Cursor and ElevenLabs. That makes her a possible co-investor and a source of early automation deal flow, plus an entry point to WndrCo's later-stage SPV machine. She is not a frontier-lab, pre-training or AI-chip investor. Her checks can't match the owner's ~$20M into large seeds, and she has no public lead deals. Deals that matter to the owner: Dash Bio (AI plus robotics for drug development, Swift participant, 2024 seed) is adjacent at best. Worth one relationship call to map her EVCA network of rising junior VCs. That network matters more to the owner than her own book.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Woodside, CA ([Swift bio 2023](https://web.archive.org/web/20230922172957id_/https://www.swift.vc/team/melissa)).\n- USC Marshall School of Business, BS in Business Administration, concentration in entrepreneurship ([Differential bio](https://web.archive.org/web/20230104022519id_/https://www.differential.vc/team)). Year not found. Student roles (Blackstone LaunchPad at USC, Greif Center, EA analytics intern) per aggregator ([NFX](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/melissa-demma)).\n- Venture Analyst at Sway Ventures, San Francisco, and Program Manager of seed-stage investments at its accelerator Nex Cubed (Differential bio). Dates not found.\n- Analyst, Shatter Fund (female-founded companies), per the Differential bio. An aggregator dated it 2020-2021 (search snippet only; the page is now dead).\n- Associate, Differential Ventures, New York. Listed on the team page captured 2023-01-04, not on 2020-05-15 or 2024-04-23 captures ([2023](https://web.archive.org/web/20230104022519id_/https://www.differential.vc/team)).\n- Senior Associate, Swift Ventures. Bio captured 2023-09-22 through 2025-03-26 ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250326170253id_/https://www.swift.vc/team/melissa)). Also \"Head of the Emerging Venture Capital Association in San Francisco\" per the 2023 bio.\n- Principal, Swift Ventures. Bio captured 2025-06-14 ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250614162910id_/https://www.swift.vc/team/melissa)). Still on the Swift team page 2025-11-17 ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20251117021902id_/https://www.swift.vc/team)).\n- Partner, Seed, WndrCo. Not on the wndrco.com team page 2026-04-30 ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260430110940id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team)). Listed on wndr.vc 2026-06-10 ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260610052536id_/https://wndr.vc/team)) and live ([src](https://wndr.vc/team)). No WndrCo bio text is published.\n- Other: USC Marshall Partners Board; executive team of Destined for X, a nonprofit placing underrepresented students at Bay Area tech companies (Swift and EVCA bios).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://wndr.vc/team | Name, title and LinkedIn only; no bio (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | EVCA interview is the only long-form Q&A found |\n| X | nothing found | No handle on wndr.vc, Swift or NFX |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/melissademma/ | Linked from wndr.vc |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-01 — EVCA Leadership Spotlight (written Q&A by Isaac Snitkoff) — [link](https://web.archive.org/web/20250113162532id_/https://www.evca.org/blog/leadership-spotlight-san-francisco-chapter-co-lead-mel-demma-of-swift-ventures) — AI Index, contrarian AI drug-development view, Dash Bio. Also excerpted in the EVCA newsletter of 2025-01-09 ([src](https://evca.substack.com/p/evca-newsletter-january-edition-our)).\n- No podcasts found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** enterprise AI and automation at seed. AI plus robotics in pharma development, not just discovery. Measuring \"real\" AI exposure from filings, hiring and open-source contributions.\n- **Contrarian:** the drug-development bottleneck (clinical-trial capacity) is a bigger AI opportunity than discovery (EVCA 2025).\n- **Calls:** Dash Bio: $6.5M seed in 2024-10, then an $11M follow-on in 2025-07 with Swift again participating ([Ropes & Gray](https://www.ropesgray.com/en/news-and-events/news/2024/10/dash-bio-closes-seed-financing-round), [VCA](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2025072310/dash-bio-secures-11m-to-accelerate-a-new-era-in-bioanalysis----bringing-total-funding-to-17-5m/)). A reported $30M Series A led by Oak HC/FT (2026) is unconfirmed ([src](https://raising.fi/news/dash-bio-series-a-august-2026)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. EVCA Leadership Spotlight (2025-01-07): her only long-form statement of views ([link](https://web.archive.org/web/20250113162532id_/https://www.evca.org/blog/leadership-spotlight-san-francisco-chapter-co-lead-mel-demma-of-swift-ventures)).\n2. Swift bio, 2025 Principal version, for how she pitches her help to founders ([link](https://web.archive.org/web/20250614162910id_/https://www.swift.vc/team/melissa)).\n3. Differential team page (2023) for the early career path ([link](https://web.archive.org/web/20230104022519id_/https://www.differential.vc/team)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public X or Substack. Her visible activity is offline: EVCA SF events and dinners for the tech and venture community (Swift bio 2023).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Dash Bio (at Swift; she cites \"we recently invested\") | Seed ($6.5M) | 2024-10 | no (Freestyle Capital led) | Freestyle Capital, LifeX Ventures | [EVCA](https://web.archive.org/web/20250113162532id_/https://www.evca.org/blog/leadership-spotlight-san-francisco-chapter-co-lead-mel-demma-of-swift-ventures), [Ropes & Gray](https://www.ropesgray.com/en/news-and-events/news/2024/10/dash-bio-closes-seed-financing-round) |\n\nNo WndrCo deal is publicly attributed to her yet (wndr.vc portfolio and bundle checked 2026-11-10). The Swift portfolio she likely worked on during 2023-2026 includes Coval, AgentPhone, MCPJam, Scope, Cognifence, Donobu, Superinterviews and Pickaxe ([src](https://swift.vc/companies)), but no source names her on any of them, so they are not in the deal rows.\n\n## Deal stories\nDash Bio: CEO Dave Johnson previously led data and AI at Moderna. The company automates clinical bioanalysis with robotics, AI and software. Freestyle led the $6.5M seed and Swift participated (2024-10). Mel presented it as the bet behind her drug-development thesis (EVCA 2025). Intro path not found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| WndrCo | Partner, Seed | 2026 (between 04-30 and 06-10) | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| ChenLi Wang, Anthony Saleh | Co-Heads of Seed at WndrCo (her seed leads) | 2026 | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Chonghao Huang | Fellow Partner, Seed at WndrCo | 2026 | [wndr.vc](https://wndr.vc/team) |\n| Swift Ventures (Brett Wilson, John Hughes, GPs) | Senior Associate, then Principal | 2023 to 2026 | [Swift](https://web.archive.org/web/20251117021902id_/https://www.swift.vc/team) |\n| Differential Ventures (Nick Adams, David Magerman, MPs) | Associate | by 2023-01, gone by 2024-04 | [Differential](https://web.archive.org/web/20230104022519id_/https://www.differential.vc/team) |\n| Shatter Fund | Analyst | 2020-2021 (aggregator) | Differential bio |\n| Sway Ventures / Nex Cubed | Venture Analyst; accelerator seed program manager | dates not found | Differential bio |\n| EVCA (Sam Huang, Isaac Snitkoff) | San Francisco chapter co-lead | by 2023 | [EVCA](https://web.archive.org/web/20250113162532id_/https://www.evca.org/blog/leadership-spotlight-san-francisco-chapter-co-lead-mel-demma-of-swift-ventures) |\n| Dash Bio | Swift investment she championed in public | 2024-10 | EVCA |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which WndrCo seed deals has she done since mid-2026? Check new WndrCo seed announcements and her LinkedIn activity.\n- Is she based in New York (NFX) or San Francisco? This matters for which WndrCo office and deal flow she covers.\n- When did she start at Differential? This settles `fast_promotion`.\n- Is \"Sway Ventures\" (Nex Cubed's parent, SF) the same as the \"Sway Capital\" lead in this repo? NFX lists both names. Kept separate.\n- Who introduced her to WndrCo? Possibly via EVCA or via Chonghao Huang (both seed); no source.\n- Was she personally on the Dash Bio deal team, or was it a firm-level \"we\"?\n\n## Sources\n- https://wndr.vc/team (bundle `assets/index-CuMFcD--.js`, read 2026-11-10); https://web.archive.org/web/20260610052536id_/https://wndr.vc/team; https://web.archive.org/web/20260430110940id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team\n- Swift bio: https://web.archive.org/web/20230922172957id_/https://www.swift.vc/team/melissa, .../20250326170253id_/..., .../20250614162910id_/..., .../20260215222940id_/...; team pages https://web.archive.org/web/20240530150024id_/https://www.swift.vc/team, https://web.archive.org/web/20251117021902id_/https://www.swift.vc/team, live https://swift.vc/team and https://swift.vc/companies\n- Differential: https://web.archive.org/web/20230104022519id_/https://www.differential.vc/team\n- EVCA: https://web.archive.org/web/20250113162532id_/https://www.evca.org/blog/leadership-spotlight-san-francisco-chapter-co-lead-mel-demma-of-swift-ventures; https://evca.substack.com/p/evca-newsletter-january-edition-our\n- SEC: Swift Ventures Fund II D/A https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1978069/000197806924000001/primary_doc.xml; WndrCo Seed III D https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2058875/000205984325000002/primary_doc.xml\n- Dash Bio: https://www.ropesgray.com/en/news-and-events/news/2024/10/dash-bio-closes-seed-financing-round; https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2025072310/dash-bio-secures-11m-to-accelerate-a-new-era-in-bioanalysis----bringing-total-funding-to-17-5m/; https://raising.fi/news/dash-bio-series-a-august-2026\n- Aggregators (secondary): https://signal.nfx.com/investors/melissa-demma; https://startupintros.com/people/melissa-demma\n", "person:merritt-hummer": "\n# Merritt Hummer\n\n## One-line take\nShe is the senior half of BCV's NY growth pair with Abby Meyers. In 2026 the pair moved from horizontal SaaS into AI compute growth rounds: they co-led Lumilens' $700M+ Series C and joined Etched's $700M round at $21B, both in August 2026. She also hired and championed Meyers, BCV's fastest promotion.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** she uses Bain Capital's private-equity network as a diligence and distribution machine. She calls CFOs and frontline users across Bain-owned companies, finds the product users already prefer, and then pays up at growth. Her stated rule is that BCV only invests where it can say how it will \"disproportionately support\" the company ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/merritt-hummer/)).\n- **What constrains her:** she is never first. In every AI deal found, BCV entered at Series C or later, or after a16z, Accel or Benchmark had already led. Her published thesis turns that into a virtue: the Legora memo argues second movers win in AI apps ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/investing-in-legora-ai-and-second-mover-advantage/)).\n- **What she'd never do (inferred):** a pre-product research lab seed. Her written criteria are \"no brainer\" metrics and an enabling technology or market tailwind ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/merritt-hummer/)). There is no seed AI deal under her name.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Hummer–Meyers pair is a single unit.** BCV's portfolio CMS lists both as partners on every Hummer deal from 2023 onward: Apollo.io, MaintainX, Whop, Pylon, Legora, Atoms, Lumilens, Etched and Rillet ([src](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22etched%22%5D)). Before 2023 she is the sole partner (SmartRent 2019, Material Bank 2020, Pleo, Socure and Ankorstore 2021, Fidel API and Mercanto 2022).\n- **She wrote the Meyers promotion post.** The CMS `insightsAuthor` on \"Introducing Abby Meyers, Our Newest Partner\" (2025-08-07) points only to Hummer's team record. The post says \"She was hired as soon as we hung up the phone.\" It also credits Meyers with deploying over $190M across six companies in three years ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/)). Hummer is therefore the sponsor behind the associate-to-partner jump the owner flagged.\n- **Five AI growth checks in six months.** CMS record creation dates are:\n  - Legora: 2026-03-10.\n  - Atoms: 2026-07-22.\n  - Lumilens: 2026-09-14. The round was announced 2026-08-06.\n  - Etched and Rillet: both 2026-08-18.\n\n  Before 2026 her AI exposure was Pylon (2025) plus AI layers inside SaaS companies. This is a mandate shift that her bio, which still says \"B2B SaaS ... product-led growth\", has not caught up with ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/merritt-hummer/)).\n- **She co-led a $5.5B AI-networking round.** Lumilens' Series C (more than $700M at $5.51B, 2026-08-06) was co-led by Atreides, Bain Capital Ventures, Meritech, Seligman and Spark ([src](https://lumilens.com/news-insights/lumilens-emerges-with-900m-in-funding)). BCV's record names Hummer and Meyers. This is the closest she has come to the owner's compute thesis, and she led rather than followed.\n- **The second-mover memo is her investment philosophy for AI apps.** The Legora post (2026-03-10, co-authored by Hummer, Meyers and Joshua Xu per CMS) says \"AI weakens the foundations of the first mover playbook.\" The memo also discloses Legora's growth relative to Harvey: it \"started 2025 at roughly 8% of Harvey’s scale and ended the year around 40%\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/investing-in-legora-ai-and-second-mover-advantage/)).\n- **Her diligence method is reference calls on the incumbent's customers.** The Legora memo says \"nearly every Harvey customer we spoke with said they would consider alternatives.\" It also notes that executives see the two products as interchangeable while practitioners do not ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/investing-in-legora-ai-and-second-mover-advantage/)).\n- **Rillet was sourced through Bain Capital's portfolio.** When BCV went to make portfolio intros, \"we were surprised to find how many companies were already customers\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rillet-closes-the-books-on-legacy-erp/)). The deal was a $100M Series C, written up by Hummer and Meyers per CMS.\n- **Legora Series E omission (open).** Legora's 2026-10-21 Series E release ($550M at $8.8B, led by Atreides) lists existing investors without Bain Capital or BCV ([src](https://www.legora.com/newsroom/legora-raises-550-million-series-e-at-8.8-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-growth)). That suggests BCV did not take its pro rata seven months after entering, or was simply not named. This is unconfirmed.\n- **Her biggest realized outcome is a SaaS-to-AI story.** BCV led MaintainX's Series C in 2023 and co-led its $150M Series D with Bessemer ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-re-doubling-down-on-maintainx/)). Autodesk then agreed to buy MaintainX for about $3.6B. BCV's post credits AI for the late growth: \"the AI layer drove compounding growth\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-autodesk-and-maintainx-belong-together/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium (borderline low).**\n- **Against:** she does no seed or lab work. In every AI deal BCV entered at Series C or later, or behind a16z, Accel or Benchmark. Her written criteria (\"no brainer\" metrics) exclude pre-revenue research labs.\n- **For:**\n  1. She now writes growth checks into AI compute (Etched at $21B; the Lumilens co-lead at $5.5B) and AI-native automation (Legora, Rillet, Pylon). That makes her a natural next-round buyer for compute or automation companies the owner backs early, and Rillet/Pylon-style automation is the owner's \"Serval\" type.\n  2. She is the sponsor and partner-of-record pair for Abby Meyers, the fast-promoted name the owner flagged.\n  3. She brings Bain Capital PE distribution, which has real value for AI automation companies selling to CFOs and operators.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** Lumilens (co-lead), Etched, Legora, Rillet, Pylon, Atoms.\n\n## Background\n- Prior employers: New Mountain Capital, Goldman Sachs, Bain & Company. Source is TheOrg (aggregator), with no dates or titles ([src](https://theorg.com/org/bain-capital-ventures/org-chart/merritt-hummer)).\n- Education: not found.\n- At BCV:\n  - Earliest portfolio record is SmartRent, year of investment 2019 ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/merritt-hummer/)).\n  - Bylines on the GoCardless post (2021-01-05, with Matt Harris and others), Ankorstore (2021-05-18, with Allison Xu) and Pleo (2021-07-06, with Keri Gohman) ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-future-of-retail-is-local-co-leading-ankorstores-100m-series-b/)).\n  - Called \"BCV Partner\" on 2023-01-25 ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-2022-update/)) and \"partner\" at the Fund X and Select IV launch on 2023-02-28 ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/)).\n  - The exact promotion date and her pre-partner title were not found.\n- Pronouns: BCV refers to \"her recent Forbes article\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-2022-update/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://baincapitalventures.com/team/merritt-hummer/ | Firm bio with Q&A |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes BCV insight posts (listed below) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/merritthummer | Handle from BCV; fetch blocked (HTTP 402) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/merritt-hummer-aa187712/ | Fetch blocked (HTTP 999) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | BCV founder video series | Interviews Legora, Pylon and Whop CEOs (2026) |\n| Forbes | nothing found | A 2022 Forbes article is referenced by BCV; the author page returned 403 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-03 — BCV video — \"Inside Pylon's Mission to Build the 'Zendesk Killer'\" with Marty Kausas — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VBKSeA0EbWM — BCV summary only; no quotes from her captured.\n- 2026-09 — BCV video — \"Legora Is Building the Place Where Legal Work Happens\" with Max Junestrand, \"BCV partner Merritt Hummer\" interviewing — https://youtu.be/-66gM87qV8M — summary says Legora went from $1M to $100M ARR in under 18 months ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/video-legora-is-building-the-place-where-legal-work-happens/)).\n- 2026-11 — BCV video — Whop with Steven Schwartz — https://youtu.be/S1HJNvio1J4 — summary only.\n- 2022-10 — Money20/20 — moderated an open-finance panel with Fidel API, Pinwheel and Orum ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/q3-bcv-update/)).\n- 2025-09 — co-author of the BCV CRO Advisory Board launch ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/charting-the-future-of-revenue-leadership-introducing-the-bcv-cro-advisory-board/)).\n- Transcripts were not obtained, and the web-search budget was exhausted this session.\n\n## How they think\n- **Market timing over category:** \"a market tailwind is the difference between a good investment and a great investment\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/merritt-hummer/)).\n- **Founders with lived problems:** she prefers insight \"derived from personal experience\" over \"whiteboarding sessions\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/merritt-hummer/)).\n- **Multi-stage edge:** \"Our early stage exposure is a tremendous asset for identifying winners\" (2023-02-28) ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/)).\n- **Contrarian view: second movers win in AI apps** where end users choose. First movers hold where risk committees choose (Legora memo).\n- **2022 call:** a \"reversion to revenue models that are profitable and predictable\" (her Forbes piece, quoted by BCV) ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-2022-update/)).\n- **Calls right:**\n  - MaintainX: led the 2023 Series C, sold to Autodesk for about $3.6B in 2026.\n  - SmartRent: reached IPO.\n  - Ankorstore: co-led the 2021 Series B with Tiger.\n- **Calls wrong:** not found, and outcomes for her 2021 fintech deals were not checked.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Investing in Legora: AI & Second Mover Advantage (2026-03-10): her framework for paying up late in AI apps. https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/investing-in-legora-ai-and-second-mover-advantage/\n2. Her BCV team-page Q&A, covering what she looks for in an investment, a founder and value-add. https://baincapitalventures.com/team/merritt-hummer/\n3. Rillet Closes the Books on Legacy ERP (2026-08-18): Bain Capital network sourcing in practice. https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rillet-closes-the-books-on-legacy-erp/\n4. Introducing Abby Meyers (2025-08-07): how she spots and sponsors talent. https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/\n5. Why Autodesk and MaintainX Belong Together (2026-05-28): the PLG-into-industrials thesis behind her largest exit. https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-autodesk-and-maintainx-belong-together/\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found, because X could not be fetched. Her public output is BCV memos and a 2026 founder-interview video series (Legora, Pylon, Whop), which works as relationship maintenance with portfolio CEOs.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Lumilens (AI optical interconnect) | Series C ($700M+ at $5.51B) | 2026-08-06 | Co-lead | Atreides, Meritech, Seligman, Spark (co-leads); Redpoint, Mayfield, Peak XV, Qualcomm Ventures, Addition and others | [src](https://lumilens.com/news-insights/lumilens-emerges-with-900m-in-funding) |\n| Etched (transformer ASIC) | Growth ($700M at $21B) | 2026-08-18 | No (Jane Street led) | Kleiner, Sequoia, a16z, Thiel, Tiger, Stripes, Primary, Positive Sum, Blackstone | [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one) |\n| Rillet (AI-native ERP) | Series C ($100M) | 2026-08-18 | Not stated | Not stated | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rillet-closes-the-books-on-legacy-erp/) |\n| Atoms (Kalanick physical automation) | Growth | 2026 (CMS 2026-07-22) | No (a16z led, aggregator) | a16z, Uber, Fifth Wall (aggregator) | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/portfolio/atoms/) |\n| Legora (legal AI) | Series D ($550M at $5.55B), \"Bain Capital\" a new backer | 2026-03-10 | No (Accel led) | Benchmark, Bessemer, GC, ICONIQ, Redpoint, Menlo, Alkeon | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/10/legora-reaches-5-55-billion-valuation-as-ai-legaltech-boom-endures/) |\n| Pylon (AI B2B support) | Growth (round not confirmed) | 2025 (post 2025-08-20) | Not stated | a16z (led prior Series A), General Catalyst, YC | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-future-of-b2b-support-is-conversational-and-agentic/) |\n| Whop | Growth | 2024 | Not stated | | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/merritt-hummer/) |\n| MaintainX | Series C (led), Series D $150M (co-led with Bessemer) | 2023 / 2025-07 | Yes | Bessemer | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-re-doubling-down-on-maintainx/) |\n| Apollo.io | Growth | 2023 | Not stated | | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/merritt-hummer/) |\n| Pleo | Series C ($150M) | 2021-07 | Co-lead | | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-co-leads-150m-series-c-round-in-pleo-the-top-spend-management-platform-for-european-smes/) |\n| Ankorstore | Series B ($100M) | 2021-05 | Co-lead | Tiger Global | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-future-of-retail-is-local-co-leading-ankorstores-100m-series-b/) |\n| Properly | Series B ($35M) | 2021-07 | Yes (byline) | | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-leads-35m-series-b-in-properly-canadas-one-stop-shop-for-home-buying-and-selling/) |\n| SmartRent, Material Bank, Socure, Fidel API, Mercanto | various | 2019–2022 | | | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/merritt-hummer/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Legora:** BCV came in at the Series D. Benchmark had led the seed, Redpoint the Series A and ICONIQ the Series B, and Accel led the D. The memo's case was built from interviews with Harvey's customers and Legora's ARR growth relative to Harvey. In her 2026 video, Junestrand told her about the founders moving into a Swedish law firm's offices ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/video-legora-is-building-the-place-where-legal-work-happens/)).\n- **Etched:** BCV was among the last investors in. Primary seeded in 2023 and Etched raised the Series A (2024), Series B (Stripes, 2025-12) and Series C (Sequoia, 2026-07-23) before BCV's first appearance. That came on 2026-08-18 in the $21B round, about 3.5 years after Primary.\n- **Rillet:** BCV offered intros to Bain Capital portfolio CFOs and found many were already customers, which became the conviction signal.\n- **Pylon:** BCV entered after a16z's $17M Series A (2024-08-28) ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/28/pylon-lands-17m-investment-to-build-a-full-service-b2b-customer-service-platform/)). Total raised was $51M by 2026-03 per BCV, so the BCV round was likely about $30M, but this is unconfirmed.\n- **Meyers:** Hummer's post says BCV first met Meyers as an Associate Consultant at Bain & Company and hired her right after a reference call.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Abby Meyers | Co-partner on every deal since 2023; Hummer authored her partner announcement | 2022 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/) |\n| Joshua Xu (BCV) | Co-author of the Legora memo | 2026 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/investing-in-legora-ai-and-second-mover-advantage/) |\n| Allison Xu (BCV) | Co-author on Ankorstore, Properly and GoCardless posts | 2021 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-future-of-retail-is-local-co-leading-ankorstores-100m-series-b/) |\n| Matt Harris (BCV) | Co-author on GoCardless | 2021 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-future-of-open-banking-our-investment-in-gocardless/) |\n| Keri Gohman | Co-author on Pleo | 2021 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-co-leads-150m-series-c-round-in-pleo-the-top-spend-management-platform-for-european-smes/) |\n| Bessemer Venture Partners | Co-lead on MaintainX Series D | 2025 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-re-doubling-down-on-maintainx/) |\n| Atreides, Meritech, Seligman, Spark | Co-leads on Lumilens Series C | 2026 | [src](https://lumilens.com/news-insights/lumilens-emerges-with-900m-in-funding) |\n| Tiger Global | Co-lead on Ankorstore Series B | 2021 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-future-of-retail-is-local-co-leading-ankorstores-100m-series-b/) |\n| Max Junestrand (Legora), Marty Kausas (Pylon), Steven Schwartz (Whop) | Founder video interviews | 2026 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/video-legora-is-building-the-place-where-legal-work-happens/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact promotion date to Partner and her pre-partner title. Education.\n- BCV's round and whether it led in Pylon (2025) and Rillet (Series C 2026). Who led Rillet's C?\n- Did BCV take pro rata in Legora's Series E? It is not listed there.\n- Which round in Atoms? The only source is an aggregator.\n- Who sourced Lumilens and Etched: Hummer, Meyers or a BCV infra partner? BCV's CMS credits only the pair.\n- X activity and the Forbes 2022 article (fetch blocked).\n\n## Sources\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/team/merritt-hummer/\n- BCV CMS (Sanity) portfolio and insights records: https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/investing-in-legora-ai-and-second-mover-advantage/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rillet-closes-the-books-on-legacy-erp/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-future-of-b2b-support-is-conversational-and-agentic/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/introducing-abby-meyers-our-newest-partner/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-re-doubling-down-on-maintainx/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-autodesk-and-maintainx-belong-together/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-2022-update/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/q3-bcv-update/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/video-legora-is-building-the-place-where-legal-work-happens/\n- https://lumilens.com/news-insights/lumilens-emerges-with-900m-in-funding\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/10/legora-reaches-5-55-billion-valuation-as-ai-legaltech-boom-endures/\n- https://www.legora.com/newsroom/legora-raises-550-million-series-e-at-8.8-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-growth\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/28/pylon-lands-17m-investment-to-build-a-full-service-b2b-customer-service-platform/\n- https://theorg.com/org/bain-capital-ventures/org-chart/merritt-hummer (aggregator)\n", "person:mia-krishnamurthy": "\n# Mia Krishnamurthy\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. Web search was limited; her X accounts could not be read (x.com returned 402 and the syndication endpoint was rate limited). Pronouns: Lux's bio and her own CRV page use \"she\".*\n\n## One-line take\nA Partner-titled Lux hire about four years out of Cornell. Before Lux she was a CRV principal on the app layer. No frontier-AI, chip or compute deal carries her name, so she is an early-access option on Lux's $1.5B Fund IX, not a proven sourcer ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy), [src](https://www.sidley.com/en/newslanding/newsannouncements/2026/01/sidley-advises-lux-capital-on-new-vc-fund-lux-ix)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she could win:** she is a founder-operator first. At about 17 she started Nova Apparel NYC, a women's squash apparel brand. It got three top-20 pros into its gear and dressed the U.S. women's team at the Pan Am Games ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260713004403/https://www.crv.com/team/mia-krishnamurthy)). She frames investing as relationship work. Her Cornell honors thesis argued that Silicon Valley's success ran on interpersonal relationships (same src). Lux now gives her a Partner title and a large fund.\n- **What constrains her:** she has no named lead deal anywhere. Lux's announcement describes her CRV role as having \"supported\" early-stage investing across 10+ portfolio companies ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-continues-expansion-with-three-new-partners-across-investing-and-capital-formation)). CRV's company pages did not list her on any company card, either in a July 2026 snapshot or now ([snapshot](https://web.archive.org/web/20260712234055/https://www.crv.com/companies), [src](https://www.crv.com/companies)). Her Lux page lists \"No items found\" under investments ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy)). At Lux she sits beside an established AI pair, Reeves and Isford, who already own lab and compute deals ([firms/lux-capital.md](../firms/lux-capital.md)).\n- **What she'd never do (inference):** lead a research-lab or chip founding round as her first Lux deal. Her stated focus at CRV was \"app layer investing\" ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260713004403/https://www.crv.com/team/mia-krishnamurthy)). Lux's bio widens that to AI apps, infrastructure and the physical world, but no pre-training or semiconductor work is on record.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Her own CRV page, now deleted (it returns 404 since she left), states an app-layer focus:** \"my primary area of expertise and interest is in app layer investing\" ([snapshot 2026-07-13](https://web.archive.org/web/20260713004403/https://www.crv.com/team/mia-krishnamurthy)). Lux's bio adds \"infrastructure\" and \"the physical world\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy)). Read the Lux mandate as broader than her track record.\n- **Sourcing theory, in her words:** her Cornell honors thesis was on \"how Silicon Valley’s technological success was driven by interpersonal relationships\" (same snapshot). Expect her to source through people networks (Dorm Room Fund, GA, CRV alumni), not research papers.\n- **Founder filter:** she wants founders \"especially if they’re underestimated or underrepresented\" (same snapshot). At Dorm Room Fund (2021) she focused on closing the \"gender funding gap\" ([src](https://wp.dormroomfund.com/meet-the-newest-dorm-room-fund-partners/)).\n- **Operator proof point:** \"we dressed the U.S. women’s squash team for the Pan American games\" (same snapshot). Her persistence story: \"I continued building relationships with people in the ecosystem\" after hundreds of people walked past her booth (same snapshot).\n- **Support role, not lead role:** Lux's own wording is that she \"supported the firm's early-stage investing across more than ten portfolio companies\" at CRV ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-continues-expansion-with-three-new-partners-across-investing-and-capital-formation), 2026-09-21).\n- **Two X handles:** Lux links x.com/miakrishh ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy)). Her CRV page linked x.com/miakrishnamurt1 ([snapshot](https://web.archive.org/web/20260713004403/https://www.crv.com/team/mia-krishnamurthy)). Neither could be read.\n- **Aggregator noise:** StartupIntros lists her with a Stanford economics degree, Bain and 500 Global, which contradicts every primary source (Cornell; GA; CRV). Treat that page as a mismatched or merged record ([src](https://startupintros.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy), aggregator).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium (watch).** The firm file rates her \"low for now\" because it judged her on deals on record. I rate her medium because THESIS asks me to hunt recently titled partners at $10M+ funds, and she now has the Lux title and access to Fund IX. On the plus side, she is a recently titled partner at a fund that writes $10M+ checks. Lux led Sakana's $30M founding round, Together's seed and SkyPilot's $20M seed, and co-led Flapping Airplanes' $1B Series A ([firms/lux-capital.md](../firms/lux-capital.md)). That is exactly the segment THESIS says to watch for alpha. On the minus side, there is **no frontier-AI, AI-chip or compute deal she is named on**: not at CRV, not at GA, not at Lux (see Portfolio). Her stated taste is app layer and underrepresented founders. That fits AI-native automation (Serval-type) more than pre-training labs.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** none confirmed. The nearest is Taste Labs. CRV co-led its $18.5M seed with Amplify in June 2026, and the company sells post-training data, RL environments and evals to frontier labs ([src](https://startups.gallery/companies/taste-labs)). But CRV's card names Thais Castello Branco, not her ([src](https://www.crv.com/companies)). Her link to it rests only on a search-result mention of a LinkedIn post, which is unverified.\n- **Flapping Airplanes overlap:** both CRV (her old firm) and Lux (her new one) are in the $1B Series A of 2026-10-21 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/)). No source puts her on either side of that deal. Reeves is Lux's named partner on it.\n- **How to use her:** as a co-investor contact for AI-native app and automation seeds at Lux, and as a junior-partner read on CRV's app-layer pipeline. Don't expect her to bring lab deals.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Connecticut ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy)). She was a nationally ranked junior squash player, ranked 33rd in U19 girls per Squash Radio ([src](https://squashradio.podbean.com/e/25-mia-krishnamurthy-20-yrs-old-founder-shares-her-journey-of-starting-a-women-s-squash-clothing-company), 2021-02-26).\n- **Nova Apparel NYC**, founder. Launched in 2018 per her CRV page; TheOrg dates it 2017–2021 ([snapshot](https://web.archive.org/web/20260713004403/https://www.crv.com/team/mia-krishnamurthy), [src](https://theorg.com/org/crv/org-chart/mia-krishnamurthy) aggregator).\n- **Cornell University**, BA in Sociology per Lux, and a varsity squash player ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy)). DRF says \"sociology and business\" ([src](https://wp.dormroomfund.com/meet-the-newest-dorm-room-fund-partners/)), and Squash Radio says economics.\n- **Venture fellow at Halogen and Ripple**, plus startup jobs in food and beverage, fintech and edtech, all before November 2021 ([src](https://wp.dormroomfund.com/meet-the-newest-dorm-room-fund-partners/)).\n- **Dorm Room Fund**, investment partner. Announced 2021-11-09; TheOrg dates it 2021–2022 ([src](https://wp.dormroomfund.com/meet-the-newest-dorm-room-fund-partners/), [src](https://theorg.com/org/crv/org-chart/mia-krishnamurthy)).\n- **General Atlantic**, technology investment team. Lux confirms the role ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy)); the dates 2023-01 to 2024-10 come from an aggregator ([src](https://theorg.com/org/crv/org-chart/mia-krishnamurthy)).\n- **CRV**, principal per Lux ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy)), from 2024-10 per an aggregator ([src](https://theorg.com/org/crv/org-chart/mia-krishnamurthy)). CRV's own page for her was live on 2026-07-13 and is gone now.\n- **Lux Capital**, Partner, announced 2026-09-21 alongside Sarah Hess (investing) and Mahi Joshi (capital formation) ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-continues-expansion-with-three-new-partners-across-investing-and-capital-formation)).\n- **Promotion story:** she went from the GA analyst-level team (2023) to CRV principal (2024) to Lux Partner (2026-09). That is about 3.7 years from her first investing job to a Partner title, but every step was a lateral move, not an internal promotion. At Lux every non-founder investor carries the \"Partner\" title ([firms/lux-capital.md](../firms/lux-capital.md)), so the title says less than it would at CRV. I marked fast_promotion `unknown`, because the GA and CRV dates come only from an aggregator and the repo rule requires firm, LinkedIn or press dates.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.luxcapital.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy | Lux bio; no investments listed (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | No CRV content posts found under her name (CRV /content index, 2026-11-10) |\n| X | https://x.com/miakrishh (Lux link); https://x.com/miakrishnamurt1 (old CRV link) | Unreadable (402, rate limited); followers unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/mia-krishnamurthy/ | Not fetched (login wall) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2021-02 — Squash Radio — Episode #25, a 20-year-old founder on starting a women's squash clothing company — [link](https://squashradio.podbean.com/e/25-mia-krishnamurthy-20-yrs-old-founder-shares-her-journey-of-starting-a-women-s-squash-clothing-company) — The page has a description only, no transcript. It says Shark Tank inspired her and recommends *Shoe Dog* and *How I Built This*. A quick-fire follow-up ran 2021-03-03 ([link](https://squashradio.podbean.com/e/quick-fire-25-mia-krishnamurthy-20-yrs-old-founder-shares-her-journey-of-starting-nova-a-women-s-squash-clothing-company)).\n- No investing podcast, talk or panel was found. StartupIntros claims she spoke at TechCrunch Disrupt in October 2024, but that profile contradicts primary sources, so the claim is unverified.\n\n## How they think\n- **Relationships drive tech outcomes.** This was her thesis topic, and her own story of building Nova rests on it ([snapshot](https://web.archive.org/web/20260713004403/https://www.crv.com/team/mia-krishnamurthy)).\n- **App layer first** (CRV), now widened to AI apps, infrastructure and the physical world (Lux).\n- **Underestimated founders.** She offers founders psychological support through the early stages (same snapshot).\n- **Calls she got right or wrong:** none on record.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Her CRV team page, archived 2026-07-13 — a first-person essay on failure, Nova and her thesis ([link](https://web.archive.org/web/20260713004403/https://www.crv.com/team/mia-krishnamurthy)).\n2. Lux's hire announcement, 2026-09-21 — frames her as a supporting investor across 10+ CRV companies ([link](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-continues-expansion-with-three-new-partners-across-investing-and-capital-formation)).\n3. Her Lux bio — the new mandate across AI apps, infrastructure and the physical world ([link](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy)).\n4. DRF's \"newest partners\" post, 2021-11-09 — the gender funding gap focus ([link](https://wp.dormroomfund.com/meet-the-newest-dorm-room-fund-partners/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. Neither X account could be read. Next step: read x.com/miakrishh from a logged-in browser.\n\n## Portfolio\nNo deal names her personally. Checked: her Lux page (no items), CRV company cards in the July 2026 snapshot and now (she is on none), CRV content posts (none by her), and searches.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| none confirmed | | | | | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy) |\n\nUnconfirmed CRV-era links: a search summary says her LinkedIn shows posts about CRV co-leading Taste Labs' $18.5M seed with Amplify (2026-06-16) and about Fulcrum's $25M seed plus Series A led by CRV. Reposts aren't attribution. CRV's Taste card names Thais Castello Branco ([src](https://www.crv.com/companies), [src](https://startups.gallery/companies/taste-labs)).\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. No first contact, intro path or deal memo is public.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lux Capital | Partner | 2026-09 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-continues-expansion-with-three-new-partners-across-investing-and-capital-formation) |\n| CRV | Principal (left before 2026-09) | 2024-10 (aggregator) | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy), [src](https://theorg.com/org/crv/org-chart/mia-krishnamurthy) |\n| General Atlantic | Technology investment team | 2023-01 to 2024-10 (aggregator) | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy), [src](https://theorg.com/org/crv/org-chart/mia-krishnamurthy) |\n| Dorm Room Fund | Investment partner (Cornell) | 2021-11 | [src](https://wp.dormroomfund.com/meet-the-newest-dorm-room-fund-partners/) |\n| Halogen; Ripple | Venture fellow | before 2021-11 | [src](https://wp.dormroomfund.com/meet-the-newest-dorm-room-fund-partners/) |\n| Nova Apparel NYC | Founder | 2018 launch | [snapshot](https://web.archive.org/web/20260713004403/https://www.crv.com/team/mia-krishnamurthy) |\n| Sarah Hess; Mahi Joshi | Same Lux hiring class | 2026-09 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-continues-expansion-with-three-new-partners-across-investing-and-capital-formation) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Was she on CRV's Taste Labs or Fulcrum deal team? Check her LinkedIn posts directly.\n- Did she work on CRV's side of the Flapping Airplanes Series A before leaving? No source says so.\n- What is her first Lux deal, and is it an AI app or a physical-world deal? Watch luxcapital.com/news bylines.\n- Read x.com/miakrishh: follower count, who she amplifies, any AI-infra views.\n- Her exact CRV title history (associate → principal?) and departure date.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy (checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-continues-expansion-with-three-new-partners-across-investing-and-capital-formation (2026-09-21)\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20260713004403/https://www.crv.com/team/mia-krishnamurthy (her own CRV page; live URL now 404)\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20260712234055/https://www.crv.com/companies and https://www.crv.com/companies (checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://wp.dormroomfund.com/meet-the-newest-dorm-room-fund-partners/ (2021-11-09)\n- https://squashradio.podbean.com/e/25-mia-krishnamurthy-20-yrs-old-founder-shares-her-journey-of-starting-a-women-s-squash-clothing-company (2021-02-26)\n- https://theorg.com/org/crv/org-chart/mia-krishnamurthy (aggregator)\n- https://startupintros.com/people/mia-krishnamurthy (aggregator; contradicts primary sources)\n- https://startups.gallery/companies/taste-labs (aggregator)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/\n", "person:michael-chen-felicis": "\n# Michael Chen\n\n## One-line take\nScale AI operator turned Applied Compute GTM lead and part-time Felicis venture partner. His angel book of AI companies that went on to raise big rounds makes him a deal-flow node more than a check-writer.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he's an operator inside a frontier-adjacent post-training company (Applied Compute) with a Scale AI pedigree. That gives him early sightlines into AI-native companies that buy training and inference, and he angel-invests in them. Felicis says so directly: he is \"an angel in Mercor, Decagon, Cartesia, Crosby Legal, Design Arena\" [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner).\n- **Constraint:** the venture partner role is part-time. He keeps building \"full time at Applied Compute\" [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner). No Felicis-led deal is attributed to him, and he is not on felicis.com/team as of 2026-11-10 (checked).\n- **What he would not do:** unknown. No interviews, posts or talks were found.\n\n**Identity:** the Felicis post pins him as one person. It is firm-authored, dated, and names his employer, his Scale AI history, Harvard and his angel list, and the headshot's source file is literally named \"michael chen.jpg\". No source outside Felicis independently confirms this specific Michael Chen; see \"What I could not confirm\".\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Venture-partner angel stakes ahead of Felicis leads (timing).** Chen's Corridor angel stake was public on 2026-02-12 [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner). About five weeks later Felicis wrote \"We’ve led Corridor’s Series A\" ($25M, 2026-03-18) [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/proactive-security-for-the-code-gen-era). Another Felicis venture partner, Alex Stamos, was also a Corridor seed angel ([src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html); see `people/nancy-wang.md`). I infer, without proof of causation, that Felicis venture partners' personal cheques act as a pre-lead signal. The same overlap shows up in Mercor, where Felicis led the Series B (2025-02-20) and Series C (2025-10-27) [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-mercor). Chen's entry date in Mercor is unconfirmed.\n- **Angel companies are also Applied Compute customers.** Applied Compute's homepage quotes Mercor's Brendan Foody (\"Applied Compute achieving frontier capabilities on APEX Agents with just 2,000 tasks\") and a Decagon research engineer [src](https://www.appliedcompute.com/). Both are named Chen angel holdings. That is a plausible angel-to-customer loop for a GTM lead (inference; which came first is unconfirmed).\n- **Operator speed claim.** Felicis says he launched Scale AI's audio data business and \"grew to a $100 million run rate in less than six months\" [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner). This is the firm's characterization, not an independent number.\n- **Long-standing tie, formalised.** Aydin Senkut wrote \"We’ve known Michael for years\" [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner). The venture partner title formalised an existing relationship.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He sits close to the post-training and inference layer (Applied Compute, Scale AI) and angel-invests early in AI-native automation and model companies (Decagon, Mercor, Cartesia, Design Arena). That makes him a useful co-investor and a talent and deal-flow node. But he is a part-time operator-angel with personal checks, not someone who can lead or anchor a $10M+ round himself, and nothing ties him to pre-training labs or chips. The deals that matter to me are Cartesia (frontier-lab tag), Design Arena (evals) and Applied Compute's customer network. **On segmenting:** `seniority: partner` reflects the Venture Partner title. The enum's `vp` means the vice-president rung on the associate-to-partner ladder, so I did not use it. `check_capacity: sub-10m` diverges from an earlier lead row that said `10m-plus` without a source. His own investing is personal angel money, and Felicis frames the role as operational support.\n\n## Background\n- Founded his first company at 18 (company not named) [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner).\n- Harvard: Math, Economics and Public Policy (years not stated) [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner).\n- Scale AI: cofounded the international public sector applications business and launched the audio data business (dates and title not stated) [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner).\n- Applied Compute: leads GTM and operations, full-time, as of 2026-02-12 (start date not stated) [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner).\n- Felicis: Venture Partner from 2026-02-12 [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | Not on https://www.felicis.com/team (checked 2026-11-10); the only firm page is the 2026-02-12 announcement post |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Web search budget was exhausted this run; no handle verified |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | Not verified; the name is common |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\nContact path per the post: founders are told to \"reach out to James, Eric, or Michael directly\" [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner). No email is given for Michael.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found (web search was unavailable this run; not checked exhaustively).\n\n## How they think\n- Nothing first-person found. Felicis describes him as an \"empathetic partner to founders\" with operator credibility [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner).\n- Revealed preference from the angel list: AI-native application companies that buy a lot of data, training and inference (Mercor, Decagon, Crosby, Corridor), plus a voice-model lab (Cartesia) and an evals company (Design Arena).\n\n## Read these first\n- Felicis announcement, 2026-02-12: the only primary source on him. https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner\n- Applied Compute homepage (customer quotes that overlap his angel book): https://www.appliedcompute.com/\n- Felicis on Corridor's Series A (the timing overlap): https://www.felicis.com/blog/proactive-security-for-the-code-gen-era\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nAll are personal angel positions disclosed in one post. The post gives no round, date or check size for any of them [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mercor | Undisclosed (personal angel) | before 2026-02-12 | no | Felicis led the Series B (2025-02-20) and Series C (2025-10-27); first in vs Felicis unconfirmed | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner) |\n| Decagon | Undisclosed (personal angel) | before 2026-02-12 | no | not stated | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner) |\n| Cartesia | Undisclosed (personal angel) | before 2026-02-12 | no | not stated | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner) |\n| Crosby (\"Crosby Legal\") | Undisclosed (personal angel) | before 2026-02-12 | no | not stated | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner) |\n| Intelligence (Design Arena) | Undisclosed (personal angel) | before 2026-02-12 | no | In before the $7.9M seed (Index, Conviction, 2026-08-03) | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner) |\n| Corridor (\"Corridor Security\") | Undisclosed (personal angel) | before 2026-02-12 | no | In before the Felicis-led $25M Series A (2026-03-18) | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner) |\n| Others \"still in stealth\" | Undisclosed | | | | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner) |\n\nTiming summary: he was first in, or earlier than the next disclosed round, only where the dates prove it: Design Arena (before its seed) and Corridor (before the Series A). No follow-on or double-down is disclosed for any position.\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. No intro paths or deal narratives are public.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Felicis | Venture Partner | 2026-02-12 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner) |\n| Applied Compute | GTM and operations lead (full-time) | not stated | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner) |\n| Scale AI | Cofounded international public sector applications business; launched audio data | not stated | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner) |\n| Aydin Senkut | Wrote the announcement; \"known Michael for years\" | years before 2026 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner) |\n| James Detweiler, Eric Flaningam | Announced together (GP and Partner promotions) | 2026-02-12 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Entry dates and rounds for each angel position, especially Mercor relative to Felicis's 2025 leads.\n- Whether he brought Corridor or Design Arena to Felicis, and whether Felicis has invested in Design Arena.\n- Scale AI tenure dates and title; when he joined Applied Compute.\n- The name of the company he founded at 18.\n- Any X, LinkedIn or podcast presence. Web search was rate-limited this run, so rerun with search.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner (2026-02-12, primary)\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/proactive-security-for-the-code-gen-era (Corridor Series A)\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-mercor (Felicis-led Mercor Series B)\n- https://www.appliedcompute.com/ (customer quotes, fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.conviction.com/launches/corridor.html (Corridor seed angels, via repo)\n- https://www.felicis.com/team (checked 2026-11-10; not listed)\n", "person:michael-dempsey": "\n# Michael Dempsey\n\n## One-line take\nDempsey reads research papers and cold-emails their authors years before they found companies. He was the first or lead seed money in Wayve (cold email in 2015, led the seed, joined the board) and Runway (led the 2018 seed). He is now openly skeptical of the \"$1B on $4B\" neolab rounds the owner invests in. That makes him an early-warning signal and a source of researcher relationships, not a co-investor in big rounds.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He builds a thesis, then goes to the researchers who could break it, sometimes years before they found anything. For Wayve he emailed Alex Kendall about the SegNet paper in 2015 and led the seed in 2017-18 ([Medium, 2018-05-22](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/scaling-autonomous-vehicles-with-machine-learning-our-investment-in-wayve-4d9bcf127932)). He says the founders of Compound's two largest companies, Runway and Wayve, would not count as \"super legible\" even today ([Delphi, 2026-08-19](https://thedelphipodcast.buzzsprout.com/2609274/episodes/19667419-most-venture-funds-are-playing-momentum-games-michael-dempsey)).\n- **What constrains him:**\n  - **Fund size.** The funds are $55–115M per the Form Ds, so the firm is crowded out of later rounds.\n  - **The research edge is gone, in his own words.** He says it went once ChatGPT could explain any paper.\n  - **Robotics timing.** He was \"eight to ten years too early\" in robotics. When the researchers he had cultivated founded companies, they raised \"$500 million seed rounds\" Compound \"couldn't invest\" in (Delphi).\n  - **Little recent AI.** Fund II (deploying 2021 through end of 2026) is \"a lot of bio, it's a little bit of AI\" (Delphi).\n- **What he'd never do.** Play momentum, or pay up to join consensus neolab rounds. He calls momentum games \"not durable.\" Labs raising \"$1B on $4B\" are, in his view, believed to be \"downside protected\" ([Substack, 2026-03-04](https://mhdempsey.substack.com/p/ai-safety-has-12-months-left)). He sat out most of 2021–22 (Delphi host intro; [2023 letter](https://www.compound.vc/writing/compound-annual-letter-2023)).\n- **The nuance the owner should note.** He does *not* think price at seed predicts loss. He says loss ratios of high-priced seeds are not \"meaningfully different,\" and that outcomes are similar across everything but the bottom quartile of seed valuations (Delphi). He doesn't object to the price of big rounds. He objects to underwriting talent floors, where \"whether or not the investors get hosed\" is undetermined (Delphi).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Wayve came from a 2015 cold email about a paper.** He read SegNet and wrote: \"I sent one of the authors, Alex Kendall, an email congratulating him.\" In 2017 Board Partner Drew Gray mentioned a Cambridge PhD team; Dempsey searched his inbox, bumped the old thread and re-met Kendall and Amar Shah ([Medium, 2018-05-22](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/scaling-autonomous-vehicles-with-machine-learning-our-investment-in-wayve-4d9bcf127932)).\n- **He led Wayve's seed and took a board seat.** \"We led the seed round and I joined the board,\" alongside Gabe Matuschka of Fly Ventures (same post). Unquote dates Compound's lead to May 2018 (per a search snippet; the page itself would not load). Eclipse led the $20M Series A about 18 months later, in November 2019 ([Unquote](https://www.unquote.com/uk/official-record/3017287/eclipse-leads-usd20m-series-a-for-wayve)). This settles the board-seat question the firm file left open.\n- **Pattern-depth before committing.** Before Wayve he had \"seen 20+ AV startups in the prior 2 years.\" Before AI.Reverie (synthetic data) he met \"10+ companies from pure simulation to larger scale world building\" ([Medium, 2018-05-08](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/ai-reverie-f67251b7cf1b)).\n- **Robotics: early, then priced out.** His words: \"they started companies and they raised it like $500 million seed rounds.\" Robotics people he had built relationships with over years left to found companies Compound couldn't afford ([Delphi, ~1:12](https://thedelphipodcast.buzzsprout.com/2609274/episodes/19667419-most-venture-funds-are-playing-momentum-games-michael-dempsey)). His network sits upstream of exactly the rounds the owner writes into.\n- **A stealth robotics lab.** \"We have an investment in a robotics frontier lab that we're super excited by.\" He calls it \"a very weird approach\" (Delphi, 1:14:04). This is probably the \"Robotics Lab Co.\" on the portfolio page (inference).\n- **Price at seed:** \"I don't believe that the loss ratios of high-priced seed investments are meaningfully different\" (Delphi, ~05:05).\n- **Talent floors in neolabs.** He compares neolabs to mobile and self-driving acqui-hires, where teams were taken out at $5–15M a head: \"whether or not the investors get hosed on that is still to be determined\" (Delphi).\n- **The research edge is gone, he admits:** \"It's not like it hasn't decayed, it's largely gone.\" (Delphi, ~15:10).\n- **Portfolio math:** \"one out of every 12 times we make like a pretty good decision.\" He puts an \"incredible\" decision at 1 in 20, and tells LPs the portfolio \"should feel riskier and riskier\" (Delphi, 32:30).\n- **A closed-end long/short public vehicle runs alongside venture:** \"The vehicle's not like an annual, it's a closed-end vehicle.\" It keeps tight stops on shorts. He believes the \"only performance-oriented asset classes in technology investing is early stage venture and public markets\" (Delphi, 43:45–54:27).\n- **Recent mix is mostly bio:** \"bio is where we've invested the most heavily the past three years\" (Delphi, 39:02).\n- **The Equal Odds framework came from Runway's CEO.** He calls it \"a framing I've stolen from Cris at Runway.\" In his own taxonomy, Wayve and Prime Intellect are \"sequencing\" companies ([2025-10-03](https://www.michaeldempsey.me/blog/2025/10/03/sequencing-vs-equal-odds-applied-research/)).\n- **Unverified host claims:**\n  - **Host summary, low confidence.** Personable's show notes say he has \"5X every fund he has ever run,\" wrote \"the first check into Runway ML,\" and makes \"six to eight investments a year\" ([2026-05-30](https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/hbrackensmith/episodes/Managing-Partner-Compound-How-He-Has-5X-Every-Fund-He-Has-Run--Michael-Dempsey-e3jsq9a)). These are the host's words; I didn't hear them verified in his own voice.\n  - **Peel chapter title.** The Peel's chapter list includes \"Leading Runway's Seed in 2018\" ([2025-08-14](https://www.podrocket.ai/podcast/7fb9c410-58cc-496b-9ebe-34dc7129c8d2/episode/533d556b-a10c-4723-a72b-b87b941753a5)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, as an upstream signal and a relationship map, not as a co-investor.** He was first or lead seed money in Wayve (2017–18, board), Runway (2018 seed lead; named on the 2020 Series A) and, at firm level, Prime Intellect (2024 seed) and General Intelligence Co. (2025 pre-seed). That is the owner's category, 1–3 years before the large rounds.\n\n**Limits:**\n- Checks are sub-$10M.\n- His recent deployment is mostly bio.\n- He is publicly skeptical of neolab mega-rounds.\n- He has said the researchers he cultivated went on to raise $500M seeds without him.\n\n**How the owner can use him:**\n- **As a source.** The researchers he has emailed and backed are tomorrow's neolab founders.\n- **As a co-investor ahead of a big round.** His seed is often the round before a large one: Eclipse in Wayve's Series A, Founders Fund and then Radical at Prime Intellect, USV at GIC.\n- **As an honest bear case.** His talent-floor argument is the strongest critique of the owner's strategy.\n\n**Deals that matter:** Wayve, Runway, Prime Intellect, The General Intelligence Company, the stealth \"robotics frontier lab\", Orbital (firm-level).\n\n## Background\n- College: NYU graduate (Personable host notes, 2026-05-30). Studied markets and trading in college ([ILB, 2021-02-09](https://colossus.com/episode/dempsey-investing-in-bleeding-edge-technology/)).\n- **Hedge fund (Crane Partners, per Personable notes).** Worked on long/short, derivatives and cross-border PE, then ran a seed carve-out: \"a little off for 2011, hedge funds doing seed stuff\" (ILB). He led \"a small carve out to do seed-stage investing\" ([Inquisitive VC, 2021-01-27](https://theinquisitivevc.substack.com/p/michael-dempsey-compound-vc)).\n- **CB Insights.** He joined \"pretty early on\" and helped build the data and research team (Inquisitive VC; [bio](https://www.compound.vc/team/michaeldempsey)).\n- **Co-founded an AI animation studio** ([bio](https://www.compound.vc/team/michaeldempsey)); dates not found. Related essay: [Animation Is Eating The World](http://michaeldempsey.me/animation).\n- **Compound since 2016** (Inquisitive VC). Title \"General Partner at Compound Ventures\" on ILB, 2021-02. Now Managing Partner. Signer for the Compound 2026, L.P. ($115M, filed 2026-03-11) and Compound Explorer I, L.P. ($35M, filed 2026-08-10) Form Ds; co-director with David Hirsch on all three Form Ds ([2021](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1839701/000120919121002941/primary_doc.xml), [2026](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2114614/000110465926026411/primary_doc.xml), [Explorer](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2147721/000110465926093414/primary_doc.xml)).\n- Family background in restaurants, which shaped his food-robotics thesis ([Ono post, 2019-08-15](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/robotics-food-and-people-announcing-our-investment-in-ono-food-co-faf17e57a9a8)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.michaeldempsey.me/ | Blog with essays from 2017 onward; \"Select Writing\" list (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://mhdempsey.substack.com | About monthly in 2025-26 (12 posts, 2025-09 to 2026-08); older posts on [Medium](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey) (2017-19) |\n| X | https://x.com/mhdempsey | Not reviewed (X not fetchable); follower count not verified |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/michaelhdempsey | Not reviewed |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-08-19 — The Delphi Podcast (Emerging Manager series, host José Macedo) — \"Most Venture Funds Are Playing Momentum Games\" — [link](https://thedelphipodcast.buzzsprout.com/2609274/episodes/19667419-most-venture-funds-are-playing-momentum-games-michael-dempsey) — full transcript read. Covers price vs loss ratio, the research edge being gone, 1-in-12 decisions, robotics talent lost to $500M seeds, the stealth robotics lab, and the public long/short vehicle.\n- 2026-05-30 — Personable (Harvey Bracken-Smith) — \"How He Has 5X Every Fund He Has Run\" — [link](https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/hbrackensmith/episodes/Managing-Partner-Compound-How-He-Has-5X-Every-Fund-He-Has-Run--Michael-Dempsey-e3jsq9a) — show notes only. They claim the 5X funds, the first Runway check, and six to eight deals a year.\n- 2025-08-14 — The Peel with Turner Novak — \"The Past, Present, and Future of AI, Robotics, VC, Crypto\" — [chapters](https://www.podrocket.ai/podcast/7fb9c410-58cc-496b-9ebe-34dc7129c8d2/episode/533d556b-a10c-4723-a72b-b87b941753a5) — no transcript found. Chapters include \"Leading Runway's Seed in 2018\" (4:09), \"Investing in Wayve in 2016\" (52:31), \"How to approach massive funding rounds\" (1:04:57) and \"Humanoid robots won't work\" (1:49:00).\n- 2025-04-08 (replay) — Money Moves with Neil Devani, with Kesar Varma of Upfront — \"How Mike Dempsey finds 100x+ MOIC Investments\" — [link](https://www.buzzsprout.com/2390643/episodes/16932095-how-mike-dempsey-finds-100x-moic-investments-for-compound-featuring-kesar-varma-from-upfront-ventures) — show notes only. Covers Fund I vs Fund II construction and check sizes, and Runway as a research-sourced example.\n- 2024-02-20 — Turpentine VC E27 — \"Thesis-Driven vs Founder-Driven Investing\" — not reviewed.\n- 2021-02-09 — Invest Like the Best EP.212 — \"Investing in Bleeding Edge Technology\" — [link](https://colossus.com/episode/dempsey-investing-in-bleeding-edge-technology/) — transcript is mostly paywalled. The open part covers the 2011 hedge-fund seed carve-out.\n- Undated — RiskReversal pod (\"Agentic Disruption & The Race For Robotaxi Supremacy\"); Indie (Bryce Roberts); Moth Minds; Scenius Studio ep. 41; Village Global \"Venture Stories\" — not reviewed.\n\n## How they think\n- **Thesis first, then look for founders who \"shatter\" it.** \"Why I generally like to be so thesis-driven… so that I can recognize when a founder/company shatters my thesis\" ([Tia post, 2017-10-10](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/tia-5125413e54af)).\n- **Sequencing vs Equal Odds** ([2025-10-03](https://www.michaeldempsey.me/blog/2025/10/03/sequencing-vs-equal-odds-applied-research/)).\n  - Sequencing companies follow a planned chain of milestones; his examples are Wayve and Prime Intellect.\n  - Equal Odds companies run many fast experiments; his examples are Runway and Orbital.\n  - Being stuck in the middle is \"a death wish.\"\n- **Frontier-model economics** ([2023-07-18](https://www.michaeldempsey.me/blog/2023/07/18/the-dark-forest-of-rd-and-capital-deployment-in-ai/)).\n  - Foundation-model companies have to globalize, opinionize or verticalize.\n  - Many will end up \"lighting $2 on fire to get $1 of revenue perpetually.\"\n  - Outcomes will be \"few do, most don't.\"\n- **Researcher prisoner's dilemma** ([2024-07-16](https://www.michaeldempsey.me/blog/2024/07/16/the-venturification-of-research-capital-the-resulting-prisoners-dilemma/)). Once one lab researcher raises, peers spin out too. Researchers who like lab pace \"probably shouldn't be a founder.\"\n- **Contrarian views:**\n  - Humanoids make sense only where the cost being replaced is a human life, i.e. military or industrial (Delphi). Peel chapter: \"Humanoid robots won't work.\"\n  - He says robotics went consensus \"very quickly\" ([2024-06-20](https://www.michaeldempsey.me/blog/2024/06/20/robotics-fomo-scaling-laws-technology-forecasting/)).\n  - \"The largest venture firms are investment banks now\" ([2026-02-02](https://mhdempsey.substack.com/p/vc-backed-startups-are-low-status)).\n  - His satire \"Dear VCs, Just Give Up\" mocks the idea that a \"$5B fund\" will win every seed ([2026-08-18](https://mhdempsey.substack.com/p/dear-vcs-just-give-up)).\n- **Calls right:** Wayve's end-to-end ML bet (2017-18), Runway (2018), AI as a category in 2016.\n- **Calls wrong (his admission):** robotics was 8–10 years early, and 2023 was \"too disciplined\" ([letter](https://www.compound.vc/writing/compound-annual-letter-2023)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Scaling Autonomous Vehicles with ML — Our Investment in Wayve](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/scaling-autonomous-vehicles-with-machine-learning-our-investment-in-wayve-4d9bcf127932) (2018-05-22): the cold-email-to-board origin story, and his sourcing method in miniature.\n2. [Sequencing vs. Equal Odds](https://www.michaeldempsey.me/blog/2025/10/03/sequencing-vs-equal-odds-applied-research/) (2025-10-03): how he classifies frontier companies, Prime Intellect and Wayve included.\n3. [The Venturification of Research & The Resulting Prisoner's Dilemma](https://www.michaeldempsey.me/blog/2024/07/16/the-venturification-of-research-capital-the-resulting-prisoners-dilemma/) (2024-07-16): his model of the neolab spin-out wave.\n4. [AI Safety Has 12 Months Left](https://mhdempsey.substack.com/p/ai-safety-has-12-months-left) (2026-03-04): the \"$1B on $4B neolab\" critique.\n5. [Delphi Podcast transcript](https://thedelphipodcast.buzzsprout.com/2609274/episodes/19667419-most-venture-funds-are-playing-momentum-games-michael-dempsey) (2026-08-19): his most candid statements on price, edge decay and robotics regret.\n\n## Social engagement\n- **X not reviewed:** it isn't fetchable from this environment.\n- **Essay acknowledgements show his close readers:**\n  - The 2026 \"Low Status\" essay thanks AK (Asad Khaliq), KK (Kevin Kwok), PM (Patrick Mandia) and AW. It links Smac (0xsmac), Chris Dixon and a Founders Fund essay.\n  - The 2023 Dark Forest essay cites Keerthana Gopalakrishnan, Jon Luttig, Vicki Boykis and Nat Friedman.\n- **Amplified by:**\n  - Emerging-manager podcasts: Delphi, Turner Novak, Neil Devani (Necessary Ventures).\n  - Turpentine.\n- **Recurring topics:** venture structure and status, AI lab economics, robotics, bio, crypto, psychedelics and therapy.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Wayve | Seed (~$3.1M) | closed 2017-09 per aggregator; announced 2018-05 | Yes (Compound led; Dempsey joined the board with Fly's Gabe Matuschka) | Fly Ventures, firstminute, Zoubin Ghahramani | [Medium](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/scaling-autonomous-vehicles-with-machine-learning-our-investment-in-wayve-4d9bcf127932); [Unquote](https://www.unquote.com/uk/official-record/3017287/eclipse-leads-usd20m-series-a-for-wayve) |\n| Wayve | Series A $20M (existing investor) | 2019-11 | No (Eclipse led) | Eclipse, Balderton, Fly, firstminute, Pieter Abbeel | [Unquote](https://www.unquote.com/uk/official-record/3017287/eclipse-leads-usd20m-series-a-for-wayve) (press snippet) |\n| Runway | Seed ($2M per aggregator) | 2018 | Yes per Peel chapter title \"Leading Runway's Seed in 2018\"; \"first check\" per Personable host notes | Lux (aggregator) | [Peel](https://www.podrocket.ai/podcast/7fb9c410-58cc-496b-9ebe-34dc7129c8d2/episode/533d556b-a10c-4723-a72b-b87b941753a5); [multiples](https://multiples.vc/private-comps/runway) (aggregator) |\n| Runway | Series A $8.5M | 2020-12-16 | No (Amplify led) | Amplify (Sarah Catanzaro, Sunil Dhaliwal), Lux (Zavain Dar) | [Runway](https://runway.com/news/runway-raises-8-5m-series-a) — names \"Michael Dempsey at Compound Ventures\" |\n| AI.Reverie (synthetic data for ML/robotics) | Undisclosed | 2018-05 | Unknown | Unknown | [Medium, authored by Dempsey](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/ai-reverie-f67251b7cf1b) |\n| Ono Food Co. (food robotics) | Undisclosed | 2019-08 | Unknown | Unknown | [Medium, authored by Dempsey](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/robotics-food-and-people-announcing-our-investment-in-ono-food-co-faf17e57a9a8) |\n| Tia (women's health, non-AI) | Seed | 2017-10 | Unknown | Hunter Walk (Homebrew), Soleio, @soopa | [Medium](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/tia-5125413e54af) |\n| Deepgram | Seed $1.8M (firm-level; Dempsey not named) | 2016-09 | Co-lead with YC (aggregator) | YC, Fenox, Zillionize, Ludlow, 1517 | [multiples](https://multiples.vc/private-comps/deepgram) (aggregator) |\n| Prime Intellect | Seed $5.5M (firm-level; Dempsey cites it as \"our portfolio\") | 2024-04-23 | No (CoinFund, Distributed Global) | Collab+Currency, Protocol Labs, Clem Delangue, Dylan Patel | [The Block](https://www.theblock.co/news/deals/2024-04-23-coinfund-and-distributed-global-lead-5-5-million-seed-round-for-decentralized-ai-firm-prime-intellect-290476); [Dempsey 2025](https://www.michaeldempsey.me/blog/2025/10/03/sequencing-vs-equal-odds-applied-research/) |\n| The General Intelligence Company of NY | Pre-seed $2M (firm-level) | 2025-04 (round in March per digest) | Compound led per secondary digest | Acrew | [AlleyWatch](https://alleywatch.com/2025/04/the-alleywatch-startup-daily-funding-report-4-15-2025/) |\n| Stealth \"robotics frontier lab\" | Unknown | before 2026-08 | Unknown | Unknown | [Delphi 1:14:04](https://thedelphipodcast.buzzsprout.com/2609274/episodes/19667419-most-venture-funds-are-playing-momentum-games-michael-dempsey) |\n\n**Timing vs the next investors:**\n\n| Company | Compound's entry | Next investors in | Gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Wayve | Email 2015; seed led 2017-18 | Eclipse Series A, 2019-11 | ~18 months after the seed was announced |\n| Runway | Seed 2018 | Amplify Series A, 2020-12 | ~2 years |\n| Prime Intellect | Seed 2024-04 | Founders Fund $15M, 2025-02; Radical $130M Series A, 2026-07 (both per firm file); Compound not named in either | ~10 months to Founders Fund |\n| General Intelligence Co. | Pre-seed 2025-03/04 | USV seed, 2025-12 | ~8 months |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Wayve** (source: [Medium, 2018-05-22](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/scaling-autonomous-vehicles-with-machine-learning-our-investment-in-wayve-4d9bcf127932)):\n  1. **2015.** He read the SegNet paper and emailed author Alex Kendall about commercialization. He calls it \"Not my best cold email.\"\n  2. **2017.** Compound board partner Drew Gray (ex-Tesla Autopilot, Uber ATG) told him about a Cambridge PhD team. He found the old email, bumped the thread and met Kendall and Amar Shah.\n  3. **Decision.** He judged their approach the most differentiated of 20+ AV startups he had seen in two years: end-to-end Bayesian deep learning and RL, under $5k of sensors per car.\n  4. **Close.** Compound led the seed, and he joined the board with Gabe Matuschka of Fly Ventures.\n- **Runway.**\n  - **Seed (2018).** He discussed leading Runway's 2018 seed on the Peel, but no transcript was found. Personable's host notes say he wrote \"the first check into Runway ML before anyone knew what it was.\" Runway came out of NYU Tisch ([Runway](https://runway.com/news/runway-raises-8-5m-series-a)), and Dempsey is an NYU graduate (Personable). An NYU link is possible, but no source says how they met.\n  - **Series A (2020).** He participated personally alongside Zavain Dar of Lux.\n  - **Since.** He has adopted CEO Cristóbal Valenzuela's \"Equal Odds\" framing ([2025](https://www.michaeldempsey.me/blog/2025/10/03/sequencing-vs-equal-odds-applied-research/)).\n- **AI.Reverie** ([Medium, 2018-05-08](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/ai-reverie-f67251b7cf1b)). He came to it through an AV-bottleneck thesis: annotation data was the constraint and synthetic data the answer. He met 10+ simulation companies, then Daeil Kim and Paul Walborsky. Compound's 2026 \"Reverie Grants\" share the name (possibly a nod; inference).\n- **Tia** ([Medium, 2017-10-10](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/tia-5125413e54af)). He found Tia after DMing someone on Twitter who was asking about women's-health apps, and was then introduced by John Melas-Kyriazi. This shows his X-to-deal sourcing habit.\n- **Prime Intellect** (firm-level). How the deal came to Compound isn't documented. The firm file's Molecule–Vincent Weisser link remains an inference.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Compound | Managing Partner; Managing Director of the GP entities | 2016 | [Inquisitive VC](https://theinquisitivevc.substack.com/p/michael-dempsey-compound-vc); Form Ds |\n| David Hirsch | Co-director of Compound's GP on the 2021, 2026 and Explorer I Form Ds | by 2021-01 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1839701/000120919121002941/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Alex Kendall (Wayve) | Cold-emailed in 2015; led his seed; board member | 2015 | [Medium](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/scaling-autonomous-vehicles-with-machine-learning-our-investment-in-wayve-4d9bcf127932) |\n| Amar Shah (Wayve co-founder) | Backed at seed | 2017 | same |\n| Drew Gray | Compound board / venture partner; brought him the Wayve team | by 2017 | same |\n| Gabe Matuschka (Fly Ventures) | Fellow Wayve board member at seed | 2017-18 | same |\n| Cristóbal Valenzuela (Runway) | Seed lead and Series A investor; borrowed his \"Equal Odds\" framing | 2018 | [Runway](https://runway.com/news/runway-raises-8-5m-series-a); [blog](https://www.michaeldempsey.me/blog/2025/10/03/sequencing-vs-equal-odds-applied-research/) |\n| Zavain Dar (Lux, later Dimension) | Runway Series A co-investor | 2020-12 | [Runway](https://runway.com/news/runway-raises-8-5m-series-a) |\n| Sarah Catanzaro, Sunil Dhaliwal (Amplify) | Led the Runway Series A he joined | 2020-12 | same |\n| Daeil Kim, Paul Walborsky (AI.Reverie) | Backed | 2018 | [Medium](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/ai-reverie-f67251b7cf1b) |\n| John Melas-Kyriazi | Introduced him to Tia | 2017 | [Medium](https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/tia-5125413e54af) |\n| Hunter Walk (Homebrew) | Tia co-investor | 2017 | same |\n| Smac, Shelby Newsad, Mackenzie Morehead | Compound partners and researcher; he links Smac's writing | — | [Low Status](https://mhdempsey.substack.com/p/vc-backed-startups-are-low-status) |\n| Asad Khaliq, Kevin Kwok, Patrick Mandia | Thanked as readers of his 2026 essay | 2026-02 | same |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which company is the \"robotics frontier lab\" with \"a very weird approach\"? Is it the same as Robotics Lab Co. on the portfolio page?\n- Which robotics researchers did he cultivate who later raised \"$500 million seed rounds\"? Physical Intelligence and Skild are candidates (needs-id; not stated).\n- The Peel transcript: the Runway 2018 seed story (intro path, check size) and \"How to approach massive funding rounds.\"\n- Is the \"5X every fund\" claim (host notes) sourced in his own voice? What is Compound I's DPI?\n- Did he personally lead Prime Intellect and General Intelligence Co., or did another partner?\n- What is Compound Explorer I ($35M, 2026-08) for? Is it tied to the public long/short \"closed-end vehicle\"?\n- When did his title change from GP to Managing Partner?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.compound.vc/team/michaeldempsey ; https://www.michaeldempsey.me/ ; https://mhdempsey.substack.com/archive ; https://medium.com/feed/@mhdempsey (full text of the 2017-19 Medium posts)\n- https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/scaling-autonomous-vehicles-with-machine-learning-our-investment-in-wayve-4d9bcf127932 (2018-05-22)\n- https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/ai-reverie-f67251b7cf1b (2018-05-08); https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/tia-5125413e54af (2017-10-10); https://medium.com/@mhdempsey/robotics-food-and-people-announcing-our-investment-in-ono-food-co-faf17e57a9a8 (2019-08-15)\n- https://thedelphipodcast.buzzsprout.com/2609274/episodes/19667419-most-venture-funds-are-playing-momentum-games-michael-dempsey (2026-08-19, transcript)\n- https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/hbrackensmith/episodes/Managing-Partner-Compound-How-He-Has-5X-Every-Fund-He-Has-Run--Michael-Dempsey-e3jsq9a (2026-05-30, show notes via RSS https://anchor.fm/s/10fa8a22c/podcast/rss)\n- https://www.podrocket.ai/podcast/7fb9c410-58cc-496b-9ebe-34dc7129c8d2/episode/533d556b-a10c-4723-a72b-b87b941753a5 (Peel, 2025-08-14)\n- https://www.buzzsprout.com/2390643/episodes/16932095-how-mike-dempsey-finds-100x-moic-investments-for-compound-featuring-kesar-varma-from-upfront-ventures\n- https://colossus.com/episode/dempsey-investing-in-bleeding-edge-technology/ (2021-02-09)\n- https://theinquisitivevc.substack.com/p/michael-dempsey-compound-vc (2021-01-27)\n- https://www.michaeldempsey.me/blog/2025/10/03/sequencing-vs-equal-odds-applied-research/ ; /2024/07/16/the-venturification-of-research-capital-the-resulting-prisoners-dilemma/ ; /2024/06/20/robotics-fomo-scaling-laws-technology-forecasting/ ; /2023/07/18/the-dark-forest-of-rd-and-capital-deployment-in-ai/\n- https://mhdempsey.substack.com/p/ai-safety-has-12-months-left ; /vc-backed-startups-are-low-status ; /dear-vcs-just-give-up ; /narrative-cycle-entry\n- https://runway.com/news/runway-raises-8-5m-series-a ; https://www.unquote.com/uk/official-record/3017287/eclipse-leads-usd20m-series-a-for-wayve (via search snippet) ; https://www.theblock.co/news/deals/2024-04-23-coinfund-and-distributed-global-lead-5-5-million-seed-round-for-decentralized-ai-firm-prime-intellect-290476 ; https://alleywatch.com/2025/04/the-alleywatch-startup-daily-funding-report-4-15-2025/\n- SEC Form D: CIK 1839701, 2114614, 2147721\n- Aggregators (secondary): https://multiples.vc/private-comps/runway ; https://multiples.vc/private-comps/deepgram ; https://www.seedtable.com/funding-rounds/wayve-ai-seed-2017\n- Firm context: firms/compound.md (not edited)\n", "person:michael-laser": "\n# Michael Laser\n\n## One-line take\nAn Eclipse partner who builds companies and financing structures (credit, project finance) for AI infrastructure rather than sourcing outside deals, worth tracking as a compute-financing node more than as a seed check-writer ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** Laser combines Coatue-era public and private investing with operator experience. At Second Avenue they raised and deployed $1B and scaled the business to more than 250 people. Eclipse uses that mix to incubate companies and to design non-equity financing ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- **Their edge:** Structuring. Eclipse credits them with spotting a pre-IPO financing need at a portfolio company and leading a credit facility to meet it. Their stated aim is financing infrastructure projects so that venture equity goes only to the work that still needs it ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- **What constrains them:** No public deal record. All three companies they are building are in stealth. They have no public X, Substack or podcast footprint that I could find, and their standing comes entirely from Eclipse's own announcement.\n- **What they'd probably avoid:** Using expensive venture equity to fund land, construction or power-to-compute capex that can be debt-financed. This is my inference from the firm post, not their own words.\n\n## Differential insight\nEvery line below comes from one firm-authored primary source. I found no podcast, Substack, X or talk material. Web search was unavailable in this run because the session-wide search budget was used up, so independent coverage was not checked.\n- **How they see openings:** Eclipse says they spot new companies inside the limits of existing ones: \"sees the opportunity for a new company in the constraints of an existing one\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale), 2026-10-28).\n- **Credit facility:** Before a portfolio company's IPO, they \"led the design and execution of a credit facility\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)). The company is not named. Cerebras is Eclipse's 2026 IPO (2026-05-14), but nothing ties this facility to Cerebras. Treat that link as an open question.\n- **Financing philosophy:** \"preserving venture equity for the work that still requires it\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)). This is a capital-stack view of AI infrastructure: debt or project finance for land, construction and power, and equity for the technology risk.\n- **AI-infrastructure scope:** Their focus runs \"from land and construction to the systems that turn power into compute\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- **Company building:** They are \"building two new Eclipse companies alongside their founders and developing a third, all in stealth\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- **Commercial work:** They \"negotiated the commercial partnerships to put them into practice,\" meaning new business models set up with portfolio founders ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:** Eclipse is a calibration \"yes\" in `THESIS.md` because of Cerebras. Laser's stated focus, AI infrastructure from land to power-to-compute, sits next to the AI-chips and compute part of the thesis. As an incubator, they are present at formation, which is the earliest possible entry.\n- **Against:** They have no named frontier-lab, pre-training or chip deal. Their edge is financing structure and company creation inside Eclipse, not backing outside research talent. Eclipse incubations are unlikely to open large co-invest seed allocations to outside investors (inference).\n- **Why it still matters:** For a ~$20M check into a compute-heavy new lab, Laser is the person to know on how to finance the capex side with credit rather than equity.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** None are named yet. Watch for the two or three stealth Eclipse companies as they launch.\n\n## Background\n- **Coatue:** Began investing \"over a decade ago\" at Coatue, across public and private markets ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)). Exact years were not found.\n- **Second Avenue:** Co-founded Second Avenue, which Eclipse describes as a \"vertically integrated real estate investment and technology company.\" From inception through exit they raised and deployed $1B and scaled the business past 250 people ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n  - secondavenue.com, a single-family-rental operator formed in 2017, is likely the same company. Its site does not name founders or an exit, so the match is unconfirmed ([src](https://www.secondavenue.com/about)).\n- **Tennis:** Former professional tennis player ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- **Eclipse:** Joined in 2025 and was promoted to Partner on 2026-10-28, alongside Ryan Gibson ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- **Education:** Nothing found.\n- **Pronouns:** Eclipse uses he/him ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://eclipse.capital/team/michael-laser | Firm profile page linked from the team page ([src](https://eclipse.capital/team)); individual page content not retrieved |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No personal handle on the Eclipse team page |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | Not verified (search unavailable this run) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was unavailable this run, so this is not a confirmed absence.\n\n## How they think\n- **Constraints create companies:** New companies come out of the limits of existing ones, so they incubate next to portfolio founders ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- **Capital-stack discipline:** Infrastructure should be funded with the cheapest suitable capital, keeping venture equity for technical risk ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- **Own versus partner:** Their sense of scale shapes early calls on \"what a company should own, where it should partner\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- **Calls they got right or wrong:** No public record found.\n\n## Read these first\n- [From Formation to Scale](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale) (Eclipse, 2026-10-28): the only substantive source. It covers their promotion, mandate and focus.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. They have no visible personal social presence.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Stealth Eclipse company #1 (unnamed) | Formation / incubation | 2025–2026 | Building with founders | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale) |\n| Stealth Eclipse company #2 (unnamed) | Formation / incubation | 2025–2026 | Building with founders | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale) |\n| Stealth Eclipse company #3 (unnamed) | In development | 2026 | Developing | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale) |\n| Unnamed Eclipse portfolio company | Pre-IPO credit facility | before IPO | Led design and execution | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale) |\n| Second Avenue | Co-founder (operator) | inception to exit | n/a (founder) | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Pre-IPO credit facility:** They identified the financing need themselves and led the facility's design and execution ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)). The company, lenders, size and date are not disclosed.\n- **Deal timing:** Their companies are Eclipse-incubated, so Eclipse is by construction first in. There are no follow-on investors to report yet.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Eclipse | Partner (joined 2025; promoted 2026-10-28) | 2025 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale) |\n| Coatue | Former investor (public and private) | over a decade before 2026 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale) |\n| Second Avenue | Co-founder | not dated | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale) |\n| Ryan Gibson | Promoted to Partner in the same announcement | 2026-10-28 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Credit facility:** Which portfolio company got the pre-IPO facility? Cerebras is the obvious candidate but is unconfirmed. Who were the lenders?\n- **Stealth companies:** What are the two stealth companies? Are they AI-infrastructure plays, such as power or data-center development?\n- **Coatue tenure:** What years, and on which team (public or private, tech or other)?\n- **Second Avenue:** Is it the secondavenue.com SFR company? When did it exit, and to whom? Did Coatue back it?\n- **Public footprint:** Any podcast, talk or X presence? Rerun the search when budget allows.\n\n## Sources\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale (firm post, 2026-10-28)\n- https://eclipse.capital/team (team page listing)\n- https://www.secondavenue.com/about (company site; does not name founders)\n", "person:michael-mcbride": "\n# Michael McBride\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. Sources: GV's team page and McBride's own GV posts, GitLab and Attio press releases, Global Venturing, Fortune, and secondary reports on Guild.ai. X (402 error), Podchaser and Axios (403 errors) could not be read. One podcast appearance was found but has no readable transcript.*\n\n## One-line take\nA GitLab CRO turned GV General Partner. He wins enterprise software deals by diligencing customers the way a sales leader would. He has **no frontier-lab, AI-chip or compute deal on record**, so for the owner he matters as a GV enterprise and agent-infrastructure contact, not a lab co-investor.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** operator credibility with enterprise and open-source founders. Munichiello writes that GitLab's revenue run-rate grew \"more than 100x\" under his go-to-market leadership [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mcbride-gp).\n- **How he hunts:** he talks to customers and then shows up in person. He flew to Lisbon to meet Cribl's CEO [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cribl-enterprise-data), and he met Attio's founders in London [src](https://www.gv.com/news/attio-ai-native-crm).\n- **Check range:** he can lead very large late rounds. His first GV deal was Cribl's $319M Series E, one of the largest investments in GV's history [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cribl-enterprise-data). He can also lead at formation (Guild.ai) [src](https://www.gv.com/news/guild-ai-sharing-intelligence).\n- **Constraint:** his mandate is enterprise software, \"open source and AI-driven companies\" [src](https://www.gv.com/team/michael-mcbride). Within GV, labs and compute are covered by Munichiello, Zeb and Hulme (per `firms/gv.md`).\n- **What he'd never do (inference, unsourced):** back a pre-product research lab with no customers to call. Every sourced deal he has done rests on customer or go-to-market evidence, or on a founder who has already built the tool at scale.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Cribl came from customer back-channels before he even joined GV, then an 11-hour flight.** Before joining GV he heard praise for Cribl from customers and leaders he trusted. He then flew to Lisbon to meet CEO Clint Sharp. Quote: \"he struck me as a leader with a world-changing lens.\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cribl-enterprise-data) Fortune adds that the two spent hours walking around Sintra [src](https://fortune.com/2024/09/20/gv-lead-investor-in-cribl-series-e/).\n- **His diligence test is whether customers call the company a partner.** Quote: \"Not once did they say 'vendor'.\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cribl-enterprise-data)\n- **Conviction shown after the deal.** Quote: \"I have not lost a wink of sleep since we did the investment\" [src](https://fortune.com/2024/09/20/gv-lead-investor-in-cribl-series-e/). Fortune also reports that this was GV's second-largest investment ever, after Uber in 2013.\n- **Attio was outbound, and he began as a skeptic.** Quote: \"I first cautiously reached out to Nick and Alex in 2024\". He explains the caution: \"I had seen similar efforts try and fail before.\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/attio-ai-native-crm) As a former CRO, he is buying a fix for a problem he lived with himself (a CRM).\n- **Guild.ai was a GV formation-stage deal.** Quote: \"partner with the team from company formation and as the lead\" of the Series Seed. [src](https://www.gv.com/news/guild-ai-sharing-intelligence)\n  - Founder James Everingham was previously head of engineering at Instagram (secondary source) [src](https://savedelete.com/article/guild-ai-raises-44m-ai-agent-infrastructure/).\n  - GV then also led the $30M Series A. The two rounds total $44M at a $300M valuation, per the Axios exclusive as relayed by secondary sources [src](https://news.bensbites.co/posts/60150-guildai-which-helps-companies-develop-deploy-and-observe-ai-agents-raised-a-14m-seed-and-30m-series-a-both-led-by-gv-and-is-now-valued-at-300m).\n- **GV fought to lead Mechanical Orchard.** Quote: \"we convinced them to let us lead their $50M Series B\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mechanical-orchard-ai). The post is co-signed with Crystal Huang (2024-08).\n- **Origin of the GV hire: GitLab's board room.** Munichiello met him in 2018, when McBride joined GitLab, a GV portfolio company. Munichiello writes: \"In our first board meeting together, I saw McB's authentic and selfless focus...\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mcbride-gp)\n- **Why he joined GV, in his own words.** Quote: \"They are thoughtful but move at founder speed.\" [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/corporate-venturer/mcbride-joins-gv-as-a-general-partner?format=md)\n- **A long-standing operator creed.** When he joined GitLab in 2019, he said: \"My goal is to make sure that we build long-term partnerships with our customers.\" [src](https://about.gitlab.com/press/releases/2019-02-19-gitlab-announces-new-cmo-cro/) Five years later he praised Cribl in almost the same terms.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.**\n- **No frontier-lab, AI-semiconductor or compute deal is attributed to him.** That holds for his GV profile list (Attio, Cribl, Guild.ai) [src](https://www.gv.com/team/michael-mcbride), his bylines (Cribl, Attio, Guild.ai, Mechanical Orchard) and his press. He is not named on GV's Flapping Airplanes rounds: the NVIDIA, Lux and Index Series A is credited to Munichiello in `firms/gv.md`. He is also not named on Recursive, Thinking Machines, humans&, SambaNova, Lightmatter or Modular.\n- **The deal closest to the lens is Guild.ai.** GV backed it from formation and led both rounds. It is AI-agent infrastructure, a control plane for enterprise agents. It is adjacent to AI-native automation, but it is not a lab.\n- **Why he could still be useful:**\n  - He is a GV GP who leads large rounds and joins boards (Attio [src](https://attio.com/blog/attio-raises-52m-series-b), Cribl [src](https://theorg.com/org/cribl/org-chart/michael-mcbride) (aggregator)).\n  - He could be a go-to-market advisor for AI-native automation companies in your portfolio.\n  - He is a second GV door into enterprise-AI rounds, besides Crystal Huang.\n\n## Background\n- B.S. Mechanical Engineering and MBA, both at Stanford; guest lecturer at Stanford GSB [src](https://www.gv.com/team/michael-mcbride). Graduation years not found.\n- Early career: M&A at an investment bank. Twice a founding executive, at Lionside and Starcode Software [src](https://www.gv.com/team/michael-mcbride). Be, Inc. [src](https://www.gv.com/team/michael-mcbride). DeNA worldwide platform [src](https://about.gitlab.com/press/releases/2019-02-19-gitlab-announces-new-cmo-cro/). No dates found for any of these.\n- Good Technology (product and sales), Meraki (worldwide sales), and Lookout (SVP Worldwide Field Operations, his most recent role before GitLab) [src](https://about.gitlab.com/press/releases/2019-02-19-gitlab-announces-new-cmo-cro/). No dates found.\n- **GitLab CRO:**\n  - Start date: GV and Global Venturing say he joined in 2018 [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mcbride-gp), but GitLab's press release announcing the hire is dated 2019-02-19 [src](https://about.gitlab.com/press/releases/2019-02-19-gitlab-announces-new-cmo-cro/).\n  - Ran sales, customer success, channels and field operations.\n  - Succeeded in 2023; stayed with GitLab until 2023-08-01 per GitLab's SEC exhibit [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1653482/000162828023025064/gitlab-pressrelease07x18x2.htm).\n- **GV General Partner, announced 2023-12-13** (post by Dave Munichiello) [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mcbride-gp). His mandate is early-stage AI and open-source enterprise software [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/corporate-venturer/mcbride-joins-gv-as-a-general-partner?format=md).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.gv.com/team/michael-mcbride | GV bio, read 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | GV news bylines (Cribl, Attio, Guild.ai, Mechanical Orchard) | About one post per deal |\n| X | https://www.twitter.com/mcbmichael | Linked from GV page; could not be read (HTTP 402) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/mikemcb | Linked from GV page; not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2022-04 — Growth Culture (Dedicated.ai) — \"Michael McBride: Moore's Law and the Laws of More\" — https://www.audible.com/es_US/podcast/Michael-McBride-Moores-Law-and-the-Laws-of-More/B09XVH1RNP\n  - Per the episode description, it covers GitLab's move from on-prem to SaaS and how to incentivize teams on non-quota metrics.\n  - The transcript could not be read (Podchaser returned 403), so no quotes were used.\n- No investor-era podcast found (one search, 2026-11-10).\n\n## How they think\n- **The customer is the diligence.** He listens for \"partner\", not \"vendor\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cribl-enterprise-data), and he talks about long-term customer partnerships [src](https://about.gitlab.com/press/releases/2019-02-19-gitlab-announces-new-cmo-cro/).\n- **Open source as the template for AI.**\n  - Guild.ai is pitched as doing for agents what open source did for code. Quote: \"Guild.ai is creating the environment to share intelligence.\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/guild-ai-sharing-intelligence)\n  - The GitLab-to-Cribl pattern matches: Cribl's CEO said Cribl resembles GitLab as a fast-growing enterprise software company [src](https://fortune.com/2024/09/20/gv-lead-investor-in-cribl-series-e/).\n- **Operator-pain theses.** CRM (Attio) is a tool he wished for as a go-to-market leader. Quote: \"Attio felt like the answer I had wished for all along.\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/attio-ai-native-crm)\n- **Calls:** too early to judge. Cribl's E was at $3.5B. Guild.ai was valued at $300M within about four months of its seed (secondary source).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Cribl: enterprise data](https://www.gv.com/news/cribl-enterprise-data), 2024-08-27. The Lisbon trip and the customer-diligence method.\n- [Attio: AI-native CRM](https://www.gv.com/news/attio-ai-native-crm), 2025-08-26. A cautious outbound approach and an operator-pain thesis.\n- [Guild.ai: sharing intelligence](https://www.gv.com/news/guild-ai-sharing-intelligence), 2025-11-19. His only formation-stage AI deal.\n- [Welcoming McBride](https://www.gv.com/news/mcbride-gp), 2023-12-13. How GV knew him.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked. X returned HTTP 402 and LinkedIn was not fetched.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cribl | Series E $319M at $3.5B | 2024-08-27 | Yes (GV led; his first GV deal; board seat per TheOrg, an aggregator) | Not named in the sources | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cribl-enterprise-data), [src](https://fortune.com/2024/09/20/gv-lead-investor-in-cribl-series-e/) |\n| Mechanical Orchard | Series B $50M | 2024-08-06 | Yes (GV) | Co-signed with Crystal Huang; others not named | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mechanical-orchard-ai) |\n| Attio | Series B $52M | 2025-08-26 | Yes (GV); board seat | Redpoint, Balderton, Point Nine, 01A | [src](https://attio.com/blog/attio-raises-52m-series-b), [src](https://www.gv.com/news/attio-ai-native-crm) |\n| Guild.ai | Series Seed (reported $14M) | 2025-11-19 | Yes, from company formation | NFX, Coastal Ventures, Scribble Ventures, Accru, Web Investment Network (secondary); Khosla named by some outlets | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/guild-ai-sharing-intelligence), [src](https://savedelete.com/article/guild-ai-raises-44m-ai-agent-infrastructure/) |\n| Guild.ai | Series A $30M ($300M valuation, $44M total) | ~2026-03 | Yes (GV, secondary) | as above | [src](https://news.bensbites.co/posts/60150-guildai-which-helps-companies-develop-deploy-and-observe-ai-agents-raised-a-14m-seed-and-30m-series-a-both-led-by-gv-and-is-now-valued-at-300m) |\n\n**Frontier AI, AI-chip and compute deals: none found.**\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Cribl (first in? no).**\n  - GV came in at the Series E, with McBride as a new GP less than six months into the job [src](https://fortune.com/2024/09/20/gv-lead-investor-in-cribl-series-e/).\n  - Source path: customer and executive back-channels he had heard before joining GV.\n  - He asked for a call with Clint Sharp, who had only an hour, so McBride flew to Lisbon for the company's event [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cribl-enterprise-data).\n- **Mechanical Orchard (first in? no).**\n  - GV pitched to lead the $50M Series B. Huang and McBride co-signed the post [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mechanical-orchard-ai).\n- **Attio (first in? no).**\n  - Redpoint, Balderton, Point Nine and 01A were existing backers [src](https://attio.com/blog/attio-raises-52m-series-b).\n  - McBride reached out to Nick and Alex (Nicolas Sharp and Alexander Christie) in 2024 and met them in London. GV then led the B in 2025, roughly a year after first contact [src](https://www.gv.com/news/attio-ai-native-crm).\n- **Guild.ai (first in? yes, as institutional lead).**\n  - GV partnered \"from company formation\" and led the seed [src](https://www.gv.com/news/guild-ai-sharing-intelligence).\n  - Signalbase lists a $3.2M pre-seed [src](https://www.trysignalbase.com/news/funding/guildai-raises-30m). That would conflict with GV-at-formation, and the pre-seed investors are unknown.\n  - The intro path to Everingham is not sourced.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Dave Munichiello (GV MP) | GitLab board colleague; wrote his GV hire announcement | 2018 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mcbride-gp) |\n| Crystal Huang (GV GP) | Co-signer on Mechanical Orchard Series B | 2024 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mechanical-orchard-ai) |\n| Clint Sharp (Cribl CEO) | Board/investor relationship; led Series E | 2024 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/cribl-enterprise-data) |\n| Nicolas Sharp, Alexander Christie (Attio) | Attio board member; led Series B | 2024 (first contact) | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/attio-ai-native-crm) |\n| James Everingham (Guild.ai) | Backed from company formation | 2025 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/guild-ai-sharing-intelligence) |\n| GitLab | CRO; GV-backed company | 2018–2023 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/mcbride-gp) |\n| Lookout | SVP Worldwide Field Operations | before 2019 | [src](https://about.gitlab.com/press/releases/2019-02-19-gitlab-announces-new-cmo-cro/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is he on any AI-infrastructure deal not yet announced in 2026? His GV profile lists only three companies.\n- How did Guild.ai come to GV? Was it through Everingham's Instagram or Meta network or through GV's portfolio? And who funded the $3.2M pre-seed listed by Signalbase?\n- What is on his X account? It needs an authenticated read.\n- Can the Growth Culture podcast transcript be obtained for verbatim go-to-market views?\n- Did he join the Cribl board? Only TheOrg (an aggregator) says so.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.gv.com/team/michael-mcbride (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/mcbride-gp (2023-12-13)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/cribl-enterprise-data (2024-08-27)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/mechanical-orchard-ai (2024-08-06)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/attio-ai-native-crm (2025-08-26)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/guild-ai-sharing-intelligence (2025-11-19)\n- https://attio.com/blog/attio-raises-52m-series-b (2025-08)\n- https://fortune.com/2024/09/20/gv-lead-investor-in-cribl-series-e/ (2024-09-20)\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/corporate-venturer/mcbride-joins-gv-as-a-general-partner?format=md (2023-12-14)\n- https://about.gitlab.com/press/releases/2019-02-19-gitlab-announces-new-cmo-cro/ (2019-02-19)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1653482/000162828023025064/gitlab-pressrelease07x18x2.htm (2023)\n- Secondary (Guild.ai rounds): https://savedelete.com/article/guild-ai-raises-44m-ai-agent-infrastructure/ ; https://news.bensbites.co/posts/60150-guildai-which-helps-companies-develop-deploy-and-observe-ai-agents-raised-a-14m-seed-and-30m-series-a-both-led-by-gv-and-is-now-valued-at-300m ; https://www.trysignalbase.com/news/funding/guildai-raises-30m ; https://www.axios.com/pro/enterprise-software-deals/2026/03/03/guildai-khosla-agentic-enterprise-tech-ai (paywalled, not read)\n- Aggregator: https://theorg.com/org/cribl/org-chart/michael-mcbride\n- Podcast: https://www.audible.com/es_US/podcast/Michael-McBride-Moores-Law-and-the-Laws-of-More/B09XVH1RNP\n", "person:michael-stothard": "\n# Michael Stothard\n\n## One-line take\nThe ex-FT journalist at firstminute who covers France and wrote the firm's case for joining Yann LeCun's $1.03B AMI Labs seed.\n\n## Crux\nHe wins on access and narrative: French coverage, a journalist's network, and a fund whose LPs are founders. He is constrained by a seed fund that normally joins $2-3M rounds, so a lab seed is a relationship allocation, not a lead. Thin page: the firm's site blocks fetches and only one essay of his was read.\n\n## Differential insight\n- AMI Labs came through people the fund already backed: \"venture is a people game and these are incredible people.\" [src](https://vcmarketfit.substack.com/p/why-we-backed-yann-lecuns-1b-seed) (2026-03-14). The link was Alex LeBrun's Nabla (pre-seed) and Founders Forum for LeCun.\n- He admits the deal is off-strategy: \"We don't normally do rounds of this size.\" [same source]\n- Normal entry: \"We normally back founders raising $2-3m for the first time.\" [same source]\nRows: `data/rows/early-firstminute-capital-insights.csv`.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. A European seed generalist, but he is the named person on a frontier-lab seed (AMI Labs) and covers French AI, where Mistral and AMI sit.\n\n## Background\nForeign correspondent and bureau chief at the Financial Times; founding editor of Sifted; then firstminute, first reported as principal and now partner [src](https://www.vcsheet.com/who/michael-stothard). Dates not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://firstminute.capital/team/michael-stothard | Blocks automated fetches |\n| Substack / blog | https://vcmarketfit.substack.com/p/why-we-backed-yann-lecuns-1b-seed | Guest essay |\n| X | https://x.com/MStothard | Not read |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/michael-stothard | |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found in this run.\n\n## How they think\n- Backs repeat founders the fund already knows; treats world-model research (JEPA) as the bet behind AMI.\n- Publishes frameworks on AI-enabled services in labour-heavy industries (directory description; essay not read).\n\n## Read these first\n- \"Why we backed Yann LeCun's $1B seed\" — the only primary text read.\n- firstminute's \"AI-enabled services is the next great venture category\" — byline not confirmed.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| AMI Labs | Seed ($1.03B) | 2026-03-10 | No | Cathay Innovation; Greycroft; Hiro Capital; HV Capital | [src](https://vcmarketfit.substack.com/p/why-we-backed-yann-lecuns-1b-seed) |\n\nNo other deal is attributed to him in sources read.\n\n## Deal stories\nAMI Labs: firstminute had backed Alex LeBrun at Nabla's pre-seed and knew Yann LeCun through Founders Forum; cheque size undisclosed [src](https://vcmarketfit.substack.com/p/why-we-backed-yann-lecuns-1b-seed).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| firstminute capital | Partner | not found | [src](https://www.vcsheet.com/who/michael-stothard) |\n| Alex LeBrun | Founder backed twice by the fund (Nabla, AMI) | n/a | [src](https://vcmarketfit.substack.com/p/why-we-backed-yann-lecuns-1b-seed) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did he join and make partner? Which French AI seeds did he source (Mistral)?\n\n## Sources\nhttps://vcmarketfit.substack.com/p/why-we-backed-yann-lecuns-1b-seed ; https://www.vcsheet.com/who/michael-stothard ; https://altss.com/profile/firstminute-capital\n", "person:micky-malka": "\n# Meyer \"Micky\" Malka\n\n## One-line take\nRibbit's founder was the best fintech investor of the 2010s (No. 2 on the 2024 Midas List). He has since called fintech dead and moved Ribbit into AI labs, AI chips and AI-agent infrastructure. He is also a patron LP for very young AI managers (Koko Xs's Nova), so he matters to the owner both as a co-investor and as a model LP.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He gets in early on founders and then backs them with outsized, fast balance sheet when it matters. Ribbit was in Coinbase in 2013. During the GameStop crisis in January 2021 it wired $500M to Robinhood by 9:30am using a term sheet it had drafted nine months earlier [ILB transcript](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/micky-malka-building-ribbit-invest-like-the-best-ep401). Patrick O'Shaughnessy says word of Ribbit spreads \"through founders, not through normal channels\" (same source).\n- **His model:** Run the firm as a company with no brand, \"not a venture firm\" (the name is Ribbit *Capital*). Fund documents allow any asset (he says Ribbit was the only fund in 2012 allowed to buy crypto). He doesn't believe in diversification, and he chooses LPs as selectively as founders (same source).\n- **What constrains him:** Ribbit's sourcing edge was fintech, and he declared that category over (\"fintech 2012 to 2023 ... was dead\"). In AI he mostly shows up after others: the Etched B, the Harmonic B and C, the Cognition D and E, the Crusoe D. His only priced seed check in a lab-like company in the repo is Enigma, as a co-investor with Index.\n- **What he'd never do (inference from his own words):** Spread capital thinly for diversification, or take money from LPs who lead with \"what's your track record.\"\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Calls diversification a lack of conviction.** \"If you don't have conviction, you end up diversifying.\" He uses the same rule to choose LPs: \"it also applies to who do we take money from\" ([ILB EP.401, 2024-12-10, podscripts transcript](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/micky-malka-building-ribbit-invest-like-the-best-ep401)).\n- **Raised Fund I with no track record and turned away LPs who asked for one.** \"I had no investor track record.\" To institutions asking who else was in: \"This is not meant for you.\" He says the first money came from entrepreneurs who knew him (same source). This is the closest primary analogue to the owner's own LP question.\n- **His AI sourcing heuristic is talent flow from crypto to AI.** \"follow the young minds.\" The engineers who \"started with crypto when they were in college are now moving to AI\" (same source). That matches his patronage of Koko Xs's Nova and the Mach/Etched/Vigil network (see Deal stories).\n- **Fintech is dead, and he told his LPs so on a slide.** The 2023 annual meeting showed a tombstone reading \"fintech 2012 to 2023 and it was dead.\" His reason: \"once you're ahead, you have to change the rules of the game\" (same source).\n- **\"The grid\" thesis puts power in the stack.** \"There is no knowledge grid without power.\" Money, data/AI and electricity are converging, and \"AI agents are going to need access to money\" (same source). Ribbit's letters page lists a **Power Letter (2026)** after the Identity (2024) and Token (2025) letters [ribbitcap.com/knowledge](https://ribbitcap.com/knowledge). The PDFs are not publicly linked.\n- **The Robinhood rescue was an SVB overdraft.** He woke SVB CEO Greg Becker and asked \"to overdraft every line that Rivet [Ribbit] has for $500 million.\" The emergency term sheet had been drafted with \"all the terms with brackets ready\" nine months earlier. The mistake he admits: \"We didn't spend enough time understanding the brand\" consequences (same source).\n- **Runs Ribbit as a radically open firm.** \"anybody in Rivet can read anybody else's inbox.\" Every hire gets retroactive economics across all funds. He says the fee model, \"charging fees just to manage capital,\" \"seem[s] completely broken,\" and Ribbit uses a budget system instead (same source).\n- **Koko Xs credits Malka with Nova's founding idea.** \"Micky Malka gave us the line that unlocked it: identity comes before thesis.\" ([Koko Xs Annual Letter 2025](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Annual-Letter-2025_-Craft.pdf)).\n- Note: the ILB quotes come from a third-party machine transcript (it renders Ribbit as \"Rivet/Ribbitt\"). The official Colossus transcript is behind a login [colossus.com](https://colossus.com/episode/building-ribbit/). Confidence medium.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** As an LP he backs a young, concentrated frontier-AI manager (Nova), raised his own first fund on conviction rather than track record, and says outright that he picks LPs for fit. That makes him the profile of individual LP who might back the owner. As an investor, Ribbit is now in:\n- Etched (Series B, 2025-12)\n- Harmonic (B and led C, 2025)\n- Enigma ($71M seed, 2026-07)\n- Mach Industries (co-led $300M C, 2026-06)\n- AIUC (led $40M A, 2026-09)\n- Cognition (D and E, 2026)\n- Crusoe (D, 2024-12)\n- Flapping Airplanes A (personally, 2026-10)\n\nHe is rarely first into labs or chips, but he co-invests in big rounds, which is the owner's lane.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Venezuela during the 1980s maxi-devaluations. As a child he asked the tooth fairy for dollars [ILB transcript](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/micky-malka-building-ribbit-invest-like-the-best-ep401).\n- Economics degree, Universidad Católica Andrés Bello, Caracas [Apollo](https://www.apollo.com/insights-news/pressreleases/2026/10/apollo-appoints-ribbit-capital-founder-meyer-micky-malka-to-board-of-directors-3385005).\n- 1996: opened Venezuela's first Wendy's franchises and met Dave Thomas [Forbes Perú, 2024-06-04](https://forbes.pe/editors-pick/2024-06-04/tres-latinoamericanos-figuran-en-la-lista-midas-de-forbes-2024-entre-los-mejores-inversionistas-de-capital-de-riesgo-del-mundo).\n- Co-founded Heptagon Group (a broker-dealer) and Patagon.com, which Santander bought in 2000 [Apollo](https://www.apollo.com/insights-news/pressreleases/2026/10/apollo-appoints-ribbit-capital-founder-meyer-micky-malka-to-board-of-directors-3385005). Secondary sources give a $750M price [marketscreener](https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/MICKY-MALKA-A0O2AF/).\n- Co-founded Banco Lemon (Brazilian microfinance; sold to Banco do Brasil in 2009) and Bling Nation, which became Lemon, a mobile wallet sold in 2013 [Apollo](https://www.apollo.com/insights-news/pressreleases/2026/10/apollo-appoints-ribbit-capital-founder-meyer-micky-malka-to-board-of-directors-3385005). In 2011 he handed the Lemon CEO role to his co-founder and spent a year traveling before starting Ribbit [ILB transcript](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/micky-malka-building-ribbit-invest-like-the-best-ep401).\n- 2012: founded Ribbit Capital. Fund I raised $100M (Jan 2013), and the firm had $446M by 2015 [Wikipedia, citing VCJ and NYT](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribbit_Capital). Fund V was $420M and Fund VI targeted $420M. A related vehicle, Bullfrog Capital (Malka listed as director), filed for $500M [Crunchbase News, 2020-01-30](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/ribbit-capital-targets-420m-for-its-sixth-flagship-venture-fund/). Regulatory AUM was about $12B in 2024 [Wikipedia citing ADV](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribbit_Capital).\n- No. 2 on the Forbes Midas List 2024 [Forbes Perú](https://forbes.pe/editors-pick/2024-06-04/tres-latinoamericanos-figuran-en-la-lista-midas-de-forbes-2024-entre-los-mejores-inversionistas-de-capital-de-riesgo-del-mundo).\n- Robinhood board member. Named to Apollo's board 2026-10-20, effective 2027-01-01 [Apollo](https://www.apollo.com/insights-news/pressreleases/2026/10/apollo-appoints-ribbit-capital-founder-meyer-micky-malka-to-board-of-directors-3385005).\n- Co-founder of Node, a gallery for digital artists (per the Senra episode description [Podcast Rex](https://podcastrex.com/shows/david-senra/micky-malka-founder-of-ribbit-capital); not fetched, 403).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [ribbitcap.com](https://ribbitcap.com/) | Firm site: no team page, no bio, no photo (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | [ribbitcap.com/knowledge](https://ribbitcap.com/knowledge) | Ribbit's LP letters (Bitcoin 2013 to Power 2026) and \"Ribbit Pulse\" notes are listed, but the PDFs are not publicly linked. No personal Substack found |\n| X | not found | Searched \"@mickymalka\" (2026-11-10): no verified account |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | No profile URL confirmed |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only (see below) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-12-10 — Invest Like the Best EP.401 — \"Building Ribbit\" — [colossus.com](https://colossus.com/episode/building-ribbit/) ([transcript](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/micky-malka-building-ribbit-invest-like-the-best-ep401)) — Covers fintech is dead, the grid, conviction vs diversification, LP selection, the Robinhood rescue, and the move of young engineers from crypto to AI.\n- 2025-07-24 — TBPN — the \"token factory\" thesis, per a digest only (summary; one quote: \"buckle up\") [tbpn-digest](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-07-24/micky-malka-on-ribbit-capitals-token-factory-thesis-fintech-crypto-and-ai-are-converging-into-one-category)\n- 2025 (date unconfirmed) — David Senra — \"Micky Malka, Founder of Ribbit Capital\" — has a chapter \"Token Factories, AI Bankers, and the Future of Money\" [Podcast Rex](https://podcastrex.com/shows/david-senra/micky-malka-founder-of-ribbit-capital) (not transcribed)\n- 2025 — Raoul Pal, The Journey Man — \"AI + Crypto: Building the New Internet of Money\" (AI summary only) [summary](https://bigdegenenergy.github.io/podcast-summaries/episodes/2025/10/02/ai-crypto-building-the-new-internet-of-money-ft-micky-malka)\n\n## How they think\n- **Conviction over diversification**, for founders, for LPs, and in life (\"You don't have more kids to diversify\") [ILB](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/micky-malka-building-ribbit-invest-like-the-best-ep401).\n- **Kill your own category.** He declared fintech dead at its peak so Ribbit could \"fall behind\" and reinvent itself (same).\n- **The grid:** knowledge (AI), money (crypto and stablecoins) and power (electrons) are converging. AI is \"the printing press moment,\" built on 30 years of digitized content. Identity and tokenization come next (same).\n- **Compounders:** Ribbit sorted 3,000 fintechs into about 200 likely 15-year compounders, Berkshire-style. Robinhood is his example (same).\n- **Art as a leading indicator.** He says artists use AI and NFTs ahead of engineers (same).\n- **Calls:** Right on Coinbase and Bitcoin in 2013 [Wikipedia citing FT](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribbit_Capital), and on Robinhood. Missed TransferWise (Wise) [Wikipedia citing NYT 2015](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribbit_Capital). The Ribbit LEAP SPAC found no target and returned $403M in 2022 [Wikipedia citing Bizjournals](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribbit_Capital).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Invest Like the Best EP.401 transcript](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/micky-malka-building-ribbit-invest-like-the-best-ep401): the fullest primary statement of his philosophy.\n2. [Ribbit knowledge page](https://ribbitcap.com/knowledge): titles of the Identity, Token and Power letters show where the thesis is heading (texts not public).\n3. [Koko Xs Annual Letter 2025](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Annual-Letter-2025_-Craft.pdf): Malka as patron of a 20-something AI manager.\n4. [Apollo board release, 2026-10-20](https://www.apollo.com/insights-news/pressreleases/2026/10/apollo-appoints-ribbit-capital-founder-meyer-micky-malka-to-board-of-directors-3385005): his authoritative career history.\n\n## Social engagement\nNo verified personal X or LinkedIn activity found. His public voice comes through long-form podcasts and Ribbit LP letters. Founders and other GPs amplify him: Index's Enigma post calls him \"our friend Micky\" [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/), and Koko credits him in an annual letter.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Coinbase | early (Ribbit) | 2013 | n/a | | [Wikipedia citing FT](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribbit_Capital) |\n| Robinhood | early (Ribbit), then emergency round | 2014 onward; 2021-01 | yes (2021 emergency) | | [ILB transcript](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/micky-malka-building-ribbit-invest-like-the-best-ep401) |\n| Crusoe | Series D ($600M) | 2024-12-12 | no (Founders Fund) | Fidelity, Mubadala, NVIDIA, Valor | [Crusoe](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-series-d-funding) |\n| Harmonic | Series B ($100M) | 2025-07-09 | no (Kleiner) | Paradigm, Sequoia, Index | [Harmonic](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-b/) |\n| Harmonic | Series C ($120M at $1.45B) | 2025-11-25 | yes (Ribbit; Nick Shalek quoted) | Sequoia, Index, Kleiner, Emerson | [Harmonic](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-c/) |\n| Etched | Series B ($500M at $5B) | 2025-12 | no (Stripes) | Thiel, Positive Sum, Jane Street, HRT, Two Sigma, Primary | [Stripes](https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched) |\n| Decagon | Series D ($250M) | 2026-01-28 | no | a16z, Index, Coatue | [Decagon](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-d-announcement) |\n| Cognition | Series D | 2026-05-27 | no (Lux, GC, 8VC) | | [Cognition](https://cognition.com/blog/series-d) |\n| Mach Industries | Series C ($300M at $1.8B) | 2026-06-02 | co-led with Infinite Capital | Bedrock, Sequoia, Khosla | [Cooley](https://www.cooley.com/news/coverage/2026/2026-06-02-mach-industries-raises-$300-million-in-series-c-funding) |\n| Enigma | Seed ($71M) | 2026-07-27 | no (Index) | Index | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/) |\n| Cognition | Series E | 2026-09-08 | no (a16z, Accel) | | [Cognition](https://cognition.com/blog/series-e) |\n| AIUC | Series A ($40M) | 2026-09-15/17 | yes (Ribbit) | First Harmonic, Terrain | [FinTech Global](https://fintech.global/2026/09/17/aiuc-raises-40m-to-police-risk-in-frontier-ai-agents/) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Series A ($1B) personal | 2026-10-21 | no (Index, Lux) | Kravis, Duquesne, Nova SPV, Sequoia | [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/) |\n| Vigil Labs | Seed ($20M) | undated | yes (Ribbit), per aggregator only | Lux, Valor, Nova | [Simplify (aggregator)](https://simplify.jobs/c/Vigil-Labs) |\n| Lighter | Round ($68M) | 2025-11-11 | co-led with Founders Fund | Haun, Robinhood | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2025/11/11/lighter-fundraise-founders-fund-ribbit-capital-haun-ventures-robinhood-vladimir-novakovski/) |\n\nThe Ribbit rows are firm-level. Only Enigma (Index names \"Micky\") and Flapping (personal) put Malka by name on an AI deal.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Robinhood, January 2021.** About nine months before GameStop, Ribbit and Robinhood's finance team (the CFO, named only as \"Jason\" in the transcript) drafted an emergency term sheet with bracketed terms. On the night of the crisis Malka called SVB's Greg Becker, the banker who had given Coinbase its first bank account in 2013 at his request, and asked to overdraw Ribbit's lines. \"by 930 in the morning, we were wiring $500 million to Robin Hood\" [ILB transcript](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/micky-malka-building-ribbit-invest-like-the-best-ep401).\n- **The Koko/Thornton cluster (timing).** Mach founder Ethan Thornton introduced Koko Xs to Etched (see `people/koko-xs.md`), and Malka is a Nova backer [kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/). Ribbit then shows up in the same network after others:\n  - Etched B (2025-12), about 33 months after Primary's March 2023 seed.\n  - Co-led Mach C (2026-06).\n  - Reportedly led the Vigil round after Nova did (aggregator).\n\n  The patron LP and his firm appear to follow the micro manager's founders at later, larger rounds. That is an inference, not a stated arrangement.\n- **AIUC.** NFDG (Nat Friedman) led the $15M seed in July 2025. Ribbit led the $40M A about 14 months later [FinTech Global](https://fintech.global/2026/09/17/aiuc-raises-40m-to-police-risk-in-frontier-ai-agents/). This fits his \"AI agents need money and identity\" thesis (insuring and auditing agents).\n- **Harmonic.** Ribbit was already in the B. Nick Shalek, not Malka, is the named GP on the C: \"Ribbit's renewed investment reflects the tremendous technical achievements\" [Harmonic](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-c/).\n- **Flapping Airplanes A.** He is named personally (\"Meyer 'Micky' Malka\") alongside fellow Nova backers Kravis and Druckenmiller. Nova used a dedicated SPV (see `firms/nova-global.md`).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Koko Xs / Nova Global | Named backer (LP) of Nova; credited with \"identity comes before thesis\" | by 2025-12 | [kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/), [Annual Letter](https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Annual-Letter-2025_-Craft.pdf) |\n| Henry Kravis, Stanley Druckenmiller, Marc Andreessen | Fellow Nova backers; Kravis and Druckenmiller co-participants in the Flapping A | 2026 | [kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/) |\n| Index Ventures (Shardul Shah) | Co-investor, Enigma seed (\"our friend Micky\") | 2026-07 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/) |\n| Vlad Tenev, Baiju Bhatt (Robinhood) | Board member; 2021 emergency financing | 2014 | [ILB](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/micky-malka-building-ribbit-invest-like-the-best-ep401) |\n| Greg Becker (ex-SVB) | Banker; Coinbase's first bank account (2013) and Ribbit's 2021 overdraft | 2013 | same |\n| Brian Armstrong, Fred Ehrsam (Coinbase) | Early backer | 2013 | same |\n| Nick Shalek | Ribbit GP, quoted on the Harmonic C | 2025 | [Harmonic](https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-c/) |\n| Patrick O'Shaughnessy (Positive Sum) | Podcast host; Positive Sum also in Etched | 2024-12 | [colossus.com](https://colossus.com/episode/building-ribbit/) |\n| Marc Rowan (Apollo) | Joins Apollo board 2027-01-01 | 2026-10 | [Apollo](https://www.apollo.com/insights-news/pressreleases/2026/10/apollo-appoints-ribbit-capital-founder-meyer-micky-malka-to-board-of-directors-3385005) |\n| Infinite Capital | Co-lead, Mach C | 2026-06 | [Cooley](https://www.cooley.com/news/coverage/2026/2026-06-02-mach-industries-raises-$300-million-in-series-c-funding) |\n| Ribbit alumni (Salvador Gala at EV3, Ray Chua at Fortwest, Nick Huber at Spice Expeditions) | Spinout firms | 2022+ | [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribbit_Capital) |\n| Ribbit LPs: BBVA, Silicon Valley Bank (early); Sequoia, ICONIQ, David Lawee, Sheryl Sandberg (later); UTIMCO (2025-26) | LPs in Ribbit | various | [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribbit_Capital); UTIMCO per `data/lp_commitments.csv` |\n\n## Open questions\n- What is in the **Power Letter (2026)**? Does Ribbit now invest in compute or energy for AI (Crusoe, Etched point that way)?\n- Which Nova vehicle does Malka back, and is he the single $5M investor in \"Nova Select, LP\"?\n- Does Ribbit systematically follow on in Nova and Koko founders (Vigil, Mach, Etched)? Is there a formal pro rata or co-invest arrangement?\n- Does Malka back other emerging AI managers personally beyond Nova? Nothing found.\n- NeoSpace AI (Brazil) is listed by an aggregator as his latest deal [evalyze](https://www.evalyze.ai/investors/meyer-micky-malka). Not verified.\n- Get the official Colossus and Senra transcripts to upgrade the quote confidence.\n\n## Sources\n- https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/micky-malka-building-ribbit-invest-like-the-best-ep401\n- https://colossus.com/episode/building-ribbit/\n- https://ribbitcap.com/ , https://ribbitcap.com/knowledge\n- https://www.apollo.com/insights-news/pressreleases/2026/10/apollo-appoints-ribbit-capital-founder-meyer-micky-malka-to-board-of-directors-3385005\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribbit_Capital\n- https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/ribbit-capital-targets-420m-for-its-sixth-flagship-venture-fund/\n- https://forbes.pe/editors-pick/2024-06-04/tres-latinoamericanos-figuran-en-la-lista-midas-de-forbes-2024-entre-los-mejores-inversionistas-de-capital-de-riesgo-del-mundo\n- https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-c/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/\n- https://www.cooley.com/news/coverage/2026/2026-06-02-mach-industries-raises-$300-million-in-series-c-funding\n- https://fintech.global/2026/09/17/aiuc-raises-40m-to-police-risk-in-frontier-ai-agents/\n- https://kokoxs.com/ ; https://kokospotlights.me/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Annual-Letter-2025_-Craft.pdf\n- https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/\n- https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched ; https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-series-d-funding ; https://cognition.com/blog/series-d ; https://cognition.com/blog/series-e ; https://decagon.ai/blog/series-d-announcement\n- https://simplify.jobs/c/Vigil-Labs (aggregator) ; https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/MICKY-MALKA-A0O2AF/ (aggregator)\n- https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-07-24/micky-malka-on-ribbit-capitals-token-factory-thesis-fintech-crypto-and-ai-are-converging-into-one-category (summary)\n", "person:mike-gorbinski": "\n# Mike Gorbinski\n\n## One-line take\nThe only named Virtu person on any AI-chip deal: he ran the on-prem Tenstorrent Galaxy evaluation that came before Virtu's Series E check, so he is the way into Virtu for inference and chip founders, not a dealmaker.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** at a trading firm, the engineering evaluation comes before the equity. Virtu was named as a Galaxy production customer on 2026-04-28 [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-enables-ai-at-scale-with-industry-leading-performance). Gorbinski spoke for that deployment on 2026-05-01 [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tt-deploy). Virtu joined the $1.5B Series E on 2026-10-19 [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-announces-series-e).\n- **Constraint:** he does not control the money. It is Virtu's own balance sheet, and no source says who approved the check.\n- **What he buys on:** single-user tokens per second, day-zero access to new open models, and an open stack that outside firms can tune. Hardware that is fast in aggregate but slow for one user would lose him.\n\n## Differential insight\nAll from his own words on the TT-Deploy \"Deploy at Scale\" panel (2026-05-01; video posted 2026-05-04). Quotes come from YouTube auto-captions.\n- **Virtu is an ML shop.** He says most HFT firms \"are really machine learning companies in disguise\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-HTJrLGVIPg) (05:10).\n- **This corrects the press.** He separates \"the trading part, which is where we discover prices\" from \"the utility AI part\". He puts the Tenstorrent boxes on the utility side: coding, mathematical research for quants and operational research [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-HTJrLGVIPg) (05:19). Tenstorrent's release says \"trading and operational automation\" [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-enables-ai-at-scale-with-industry-leading-performance). SiliconANGLE went further and wrote that Virtu uses the Galaxy servers \"to power on-premises AI trading agents\" [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=850987). His own description does not support that.\n- **His buying metric** is \"tokens per second per user\". His reason: a research run of \"hundreds of turns, tens of millions of tokens\" should finish while a quant gets a coffee, not over 8 hours [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-HTJrLGVIPg) (10:33).\n- **Day-zero models.** GLM-5.1 came out in early April 2026 and was \"serving customers in production\" by 2026-05-01: \"you do not want your quants trying to get insights from dumb models\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-HTJrLGVIPg) (06:22, 10:10). Who the \"customers\" are is unclear. They may be internal desks.\n- **Why not NVIDIA.** The open stack lets outside firms help speed up models. He calls this running \"sovereignly\" and names SwarmOne for automated benchmarking [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-HTJrLGVIPg) (05:49, 10:15).\n- **Caption caveat.** The newsroom recap paraphrases him as getting GLM-5.1 \"off the ground quickly\" [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tt-deploy). The captions do not contain \"quickly\" there. Use the video for verbatim quotes.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not an investor and has no carry, so he will never lead a seed. But Virtu writes balance-sheet checks into AI-chip rounds after its engineers have used the hardware, and he is that engineer. For a frontier-AI or inference-hardware company you back, he is the warm route to a trading-firm customer, and maybe later an investor. The deal that matters is Tenstorrent (Galaxy deployment 2026-04, then Series E 2026-10-19).\n\n## Background\n- **Title:** he introduces himself as \"Virtu Special Projects\". The surname appears in the same captions as \"Gerbovsky\", so the transcription is unreliable. The Tenstorrent recap and the video description both spell it \"Gorbinski\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-HTJrLGVIPg) [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tt-deploy). Title unconfirmed.\n- **Not found:** education, prior employers and the date he joined Virtu. Searches for \"Mike Gorbinski\" and \"Michael Gorbinski\" found nothing else. SEC EDGAR full-text search for \"Gorbinski\" returned 0 hits (checked 2026-11-11). No Virtu filing in 2026 mentions Tenstorrent.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | No profile was confirmed in search results (2026-11-11) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | GitHub user search for \"gorbinski\" was attempted; no match was confirmed |\n| Talk | https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-HTJrLGVIPg | TT-Deploy panel, 2026-05-01; his only public appearance found |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-05 — TT-Deploy (Tenstorrent) — \"Deploy at Scale, Customer Panel\", hosted by Amr Elashmawi, with David Bennett (AI&), Alex Nataros (Cirrascale) and Sanchayan Sinha (Turiyam) — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-HTJrLGVIPg — said HFT firms are ML companies in disguise, the hardware runs utility AI rather than trading, and the metric he buys on is tokens per second per user.\n\n## How they think\n- Inference is a research-speed weapon: better maths research than competitors is the edge.\n- He wants open hardware and an open software stack so third parties can tune it. He wants the latest open-weights models on day zero.\n- Calls right or wrong: too early to judge.\n\n## Read these first\n1. The panel video, 05:06–06:27 and 09:49–10:57 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-HTJrLGVIPg)\n2. The TT-Deploy recap [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tt-deploy)\n3. The deployments release that names Virtu [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-enables-ai-at-scale-with-industry-leading-performance)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nHe is not personally named on any deal.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Tenstorrent (Virtu's investment, **not his**) | Series E ($1.5B at $6.2B post) | 2026-10-19 | no (Trace Capital and Innovation X led) | Fidelity; ARK; Morgan Stanley; XTX Markets; Eclipse; others | [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-announces-series-e) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Tenstorrent.** Virtu deployed Galaxy Blackhole servers on-prem. Tenstorrent named it a \"tier-1 market maker\" customer on 2026-04-28. Gorbinski brought GLM-5.1 up on them in April with SwarmOne and presented the results on 2026-05-01. Virtu appears in the Series E investor list on 2026-10-19, which was its first round, so it was not a re-up. Nothing links him to the investment decision. The order of events (customer, then about six months later investor) matches Jane Street with Etched and Jump with Positron.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Virtu Financial | at: Special Projects (unconfirmed) | not found | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-HTJrLGVIPg) |\n| Tenstorrent | customer-side engineer for Virtu's Galaxy deployment | 2026-04 | [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tt-deploy) |\n| SwarmOne | works with them on automated benchmarking | 2026-04 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-HTJrLGVIPg) |\n| Amr Elashmawi (Tenstorrent VP Strategy & BD) | panel host | 2026-05-01 | [src](https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tt-deploy) |\n\n## Open questions\n- His exact title and seniority, and whether \"Special Projects\" means a corporate-development or principal-investments function at Virtu.\n- Who at Virtu approved the Series E check, and how large it was. Check the Q4 2026 10-K for an equity-investment line.\n- Whether Virtu evaluates other inference chips (Etched, Positron, Groq) through the same team.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-HTJrLGVIPg\n- https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tt-deploy\n- https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-enables-ai-at-scale-with-industry-leading-performance\n- https://tenstorrent.com/newsroom/tenstorrent-announces-series-e\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=850987\n- https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Gorbinski%22\n", "person:mike-schroepfer": "\n# Mike Schroepfer\n\n## One-line take\nMeta's CTO for nine years, who founded FAIR and built Meta's data centers, now runs a $250M climate fund. His argument is that power, not chips, now limits compute, and he backs AI-compute companies (Fractile, Panthalassa) as energy bets.\n\n## Crux\nHe wins hard-tech deals on credibility and a buyer's view of supply chains. He commits after front-loaded diligence (\"deep belief\", then \"ride or die\"). He is constrained by his own setup: a climate-mandated $250M fund writing $1–10M checks, so he joins large AI rounds rather than leading them. He controls the firm outright (The HS Trust owns 75%+ of the adviser, per Form ADV), and he self-funded about 22 deals before taking outside money. He would not back AI software with no physical moat or data flywheel (low-confidence summary).\n\n## Differential insight\n- He self-funded first so mistakes were his alone: \"if I’m wrong, it’s on me before I take on any external capital\" ([Shift Key](https://heatmap.news/podcast/shift-key-s3-e56-gigascale-capital-transcript), 2026-06-30).\n- Power is the binding constraint on compute: \"access to power is often the limiting constraint\" (same).\n- He holds Fractile as an energy bet: \"Chips are the easiest place to optimize your power load in a data center.\" (same)\n- He is excited about photonic computing (\"a whole switch to photonic computing\") as the next compute-architecture shift (same).\n- How he diligences: \"I’m going to ask a lot of annoying questions up front\", then \"ride or die\" (same).\n- Control: Form ADV lists him as \"PRESIDENT & SHAREHOLDER OF GIGASCALE MANAGEMENT, INC.\" and as trustee of The HS Trust, which owns 75%+ of the adviser. Fund I is ~14% related-party money, across 61 holders ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/340205/PDF/340205.pdf)).\n- FAIR lineage in deals: Gigascale backed Warp Sciences, whose CTO Manohar Paluri was \"a founding member of FAIR\" ([Robot Report](https://www.therobotreport.com/warp-sciences-emerges-stealth-apply-robotics-drug-research/)). He also put in his own money, alongside the fund, in ex-Meta Amir Frenkel's Noetive ([src](https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. He is not a $20M co-lead, and Gigascale is not an allocator. He matters as (a) an angel and FAIR/Meta-network connector into AI-science and automation founders (Warp, Noetive), (b) an energy-for-compute diligence partner whose name helps in chip, ocean-compute and data-center-power rounds (Fractile, Panthalassa, Arbor), and (c) a possible individual LP or advisor for an AI fund, given his personal wealth and AI background (not evidenced; worth asking).\n\n## Background\n- BS 1997 and MS 1999 in CS, Stanford. Puffin Designs 1997–1999. Partner at Reactivity from 1999-11 (John Lilly's company). Founded CenterRun 2000 (Sequoia-backed), sold to Sun Microsystems 2003-11. CTO of Sun's N1 division ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Schroepfer)).\n- Mozilla VP Engineering 2005-07 to 2008-08. Facebook director of engineering 2008-07. CTO 2013-03 to 2022-03. Founded FAIR in 2013 and ran the Oculus and Instagram acquisitions. Now Senior Fellow advising Meta on AI ([src](https://gigascale.com/team/mike-schroepfer/)).\n- Launched Gigascale 2023-05-02. Founded Additional Ventures with Erin Hoffman. Chairs Carbon to Sea. Founded Outlier Projects ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Schroepfer)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://gigascale.com/team/mike-schroepfer/ | |\n| Substack / blog | https://gigascale.com/blog/ | founder essays |\n| X | @schrep (not verified this run) | Wikipedia cites a Threads @schrep |\n| LinkedIn | not used | |\n| GitHub | — | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-06 — Shift Key (Heatmap) — self-funding, power as the compute bottleneck, Panthalassa's 10 TW wave thesis, photonics — [link](https://heatmap.news/podcast/shift-key-s3-e56-gigascale-capital-transcript)\n- 2026-06 — Crunchbase News interview — \"We're going to need all the compute\", $1–10M checks — [link](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/hard-tech-infrastructure-moat-schroepfer-gigascale/)\n- 2025-06 — Bloomberg Zero — \"energy and AI for acceleration of atoms\" — [link](https://gigascale.com/insights/energy-ai-next-era-deep-tech/)\n- 2025-03 — MCJ Inevitable — bring-your-own power as a competitive advantage; data-flywheel filter (summary) — [link](https://mcj.vc/inevitable-podcast/mike-schroepfer)\n- 2025-03 — Masters of Scale; 2024 Masters of Scale Summit with DJ Patil; Behind the Tech (Kevin Scott); Full Ratchet #470 (DeepSeek); South Park Commons talk (2024-10); NYCW (2024-10). Not mined; see the media rows.\n\n## How they think\n- Cost curves win, not virtue: solar went from 40 GW to 600 GW a year \"because it got cheaper\" ([src](https://gigascale.com/insights/gigascale-fund-i-physical-economy/)).\n- AI makes software cheap, so the moat shifts to infrastructure ([src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/hard-tech-infrastructure-moat-schroepfer-gigascale/), medium confidence).\n- He takes an all-of-the-above energy view: one fission and two fusion investments, Arbor's gas turbine, and fusion as the mid-2030s \"ultimate unlock\" ([src](https://heatmap.news/podcast/shift-key-s3-e56-gigascale-capital-transcript)).\n- He calls doubt about hardware \"stop energy\" ([src](https://gigascale.com/insights/choosing-an-investor-is-a-technical-decision/)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Shift Key transcript (2026-06): the fullest statement of how he invests.\n2. \"Choosing an Investor Is a Technical Decision\" (2026-06): his pitch to founders.\n3. Fund I post (2026-06): the thesis and the portfolio highlights.\n4. Crunchbase News interview (2026-06): checks, compute and the infrastructure-moat argument.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot researched this run (X/Threads not fetched).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Vor Systems | Pre-seed $3M | 2026-03-05 | yes (Gigascale) | Virta | [src](http://pv-magazine-usa.com/2026/03/05/vor-systems-raises-3-million-pre-seed-for-ai-enabled-renewable-energy-deal-making/) |\n| Nyquis | Seed $6.3M | 2026-11-05 | co-led | South Park Commons | [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/energy/articles/power-demand-surges-wildfire-shutoffs-140000070.html) |\n| Solcoa | Seed $75M | 2026-09-24 | no | Bain Capital Ventures | [src](https://secure.businesswire.com/news/home/20260924752270/en/Solcoa-Announces-%2475M-to-Scale-Americas-First-New-Metalmaking-Process-in-80-Years) |\n| Noetive | Seed $41M (personal and fund) | 2026-09-16 | no | Eclipse lead | [src](https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed) |\n| Commonwealth Fusion Systems | Series B2 $863M | 2025-08-28 | no | NVentures, Google | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/08/28/nvidia-google-and-bill-gates-help-commonwealth-fusion-systems-raise-863m) |\n| Terraton | Seed $11.5M | 2025-08-27 | co-led | Lowercarbon | [src](https://eznewswire.com/newsroom/terraton-raises-11-5m-biochar-carbon-removal-emerging-markets) |\nThe full firm book is in `firms/gigascale-capital.md`.\n\n## Deal stories\n- Panthalassa: he describes it as bringing compute to 10 TW of unused Southern Ocean wave power ([src](https://heatmap.news/podcast/shift-key-s3-e56-gigascale-capital-transcript)). Gigascale was in the 2024 round (aggregator) and returned for the $140M Series B led by Peter Thiel.\n- Solcoa: Evaline Tsai backed the founder in high school, before anyone else. Schroepfer was quoted at the $75M seed ([src](https://gigascale.com/insights/choosing-an-investor-is-a-technical-decision/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| John Lilly | Reactivity (1999) and Mozilla colleague; now Gigascale Advising Partner | 1999 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Schroepfer) |\n| Victoria Beasley | Co-founder and partner (ex-Prelude GP) | 2023 | [src](https://gigascale.com/team/victoria-beasley/) |\n| Manohar Paluri | FAIR founding member under Schroepfer; Warp Sciences CTO | 2013 | [src](https://www.therobotreport.com/warp-sciences-emerges-stealth-apply-robotics-drug-research/) |\n| Lowercarbon Capital | Most frequent co-investor (Terraton co-lead) | 2023 | [src](https://eznewswire.com/newsroom/terraton-raises-11-5m-biochar-carbon-removal-emerging-markets) |\n| Meta | Senior Fellow advising on AI | 2022 | [src](https://gigascale.com/team/mike-schroepfer/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who the ~16% funds of funds and the ~31% non-US LPs are. The finance lead (Simran Arora) is the LP interface.\n- Does he angel-invest in frontier labs or AI seeds outside Gigascale? Only Noetive is confirmed.\n- Is Gigascale in Fractile's reported Oct 2026 Series C?\n- The exact wording of his data-flywheel filter (Inevitable page blocked to direct fetch).\n\n## Sources\nhttps://gigascale.com/team/mike-schroepfer/; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Schroepfer; https://heatmap.news/podcast/shift-key-s3-e56-gigascale-capital-transcript; https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/hard-tech-infrastructure-moat-schroepfer-gigascale/; https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/340205/PDF/340205.pdf; https://gigascale.com/insights/energy-ai-next-era-deep-tech/; https://mcj.vc/inevitable-podcast/mike-schroepfer\n", "person:mike-volpi": "\n# Mike Volpi\n\n## One-line take\nThe senior name behind Index's first-generation AI-infra leads (Cohere A, Hebbia A, Scale, Cartesia seed) who now runs Hanabi, a small ex-Index partnership that shows up as a non-lead co-investor in the hottest 2025-26 AI-native rounds (Traversal, Applied Compute, Cognition, Simile).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** A Cisco M&A and infrastructure operator background, plus fifteen years of Index board seats in open-source and data infrastructure (Confluent, Elastic, ClickHouse, Kong, Cockroach) ([wiki](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Volpi)). That gave them a founder and co-investor network that now lets Hanabi get into crowded rounds (Applied Compute, Traversal) as a name-brand small check rather than as a lead.\n- **What constrains them:** Hanabi is never named as lead in any sourced round. The Index-era lead capacity has gone, and their time is split with legacy Index boards (\"conservó sus puestos en el consejo\") and corporate boards (Ferrari, Stanford Health Care).\n- **What they'd never do (inferred):** Run a lead process for a $600M new-lab seed. Their Index pattern was model and infra companies at Series A, not pre-product research labs, and the one frontier-architecture bet on record (Cartesia) was a $22M seed.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Who actually leads at Hanabi.** On Traversal, the founders name all three Hanabi partners together: \"Bryan Offutt, Ishani Thakur & Mike Volpi of Hanabi\" ([src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement)). Offutt is Index's ex-AI-infra originator (Adaptive ML, Lightning), so Volpi is likely the senior name and Offutt and Thakur the deal-doers. This pairing is inferred, not stated.\n- **Left Index in 2023, not 2025.** Bloomberg Linea: \"se retiró de Index en 2023\" ([src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/), 2026-01-08). Forbes instead says \"Volpi is still a partner at Index and oversees his existing portfolio companies\" ([src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/mike-volpi/)). Read it as off day-to-day at Index but still holding legacy boards.\n- **The Scale AI payout funded the succession story.** Bloomberg Linea says they \"lideró la inversión en Scale AI\" (they led the Scale AI investment). The Meta 49% deal reportedly returned about $1.8B to Index, per one person familiar ([src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/)). They also co-designed Index's succession plan with the Rimer brothers (same source).\n- **Architecture contrarian (2024).** On the Cartesia seed they said: \"We believe Cartesia's SSMs can be that new architecture\" ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-next-generation-of-real-time-ai-models-our-investment-in-cartesia/), 2024-12-12). This is a public bet against transformers' dominance, made by an Index partner on a Stanford/Chris Ré lab spinout.\n- **Druckenmiller link.** The Hebbia Series A that Volpi led (2022-09-27) lists \"another Alphabet board member, Stanley Druckenmiller, Kevin Warsh\" among investors ([src](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of)). Druckenmiller writes direct checks into AI companies alongside Volpi-led rounds, not only into Etched.\n- **Hanabi timing.** Applied Compute: Hanabi was in the pre-launch $20M round at a $100M valuation, which was \"effectively a Series A in its size and scope\" ([src](https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/ex-openai-applied-compute-raises-20m), 2025-06-27). Benchmark's Victor Lazarte led, so Hanabi was first-round but not first-mover. At Cognition, Hanabi came in late as a \"new investor\" at a $10.2B valuation ([src](https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents)).\n\n## Update 2026-11-11: a16z video (\"Why AI Infrastructure Will Outlast the Hype Cycle\")\nSource: [a16z on YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LI3lxzHfRrU), 2026-11-11, with Raghu Raghuram and Guido Appenzeller. Transcript: YouTube auto-captions (machine-made). Quotes are kept only where the words are clean. The captions have no speaker labels, so attribution comes from turn flow. Rows are in `data/rows/mike-volpi-yt-insights.csv`.\n\n**He confirms the 2023 exit.** \"Technically speaking, I retired in 2023, and I was supposed to get a yacht\" (54:00). This matches Bloomberg Linea against Forbes. He then started Hanabi because this is \"the most fascinating time to be doing our job\" (54:08). The video says nothing on check sizes, fund size or how Hanabi works.\n\n**How he decides (Cisco-trained):**\n- M&A as a portfolio. Roughly a third of deals were great, a third so-so and a third failed: \"we were not afraid to fail and we did it with high enough frequency\" (15:39). His team did about 70 deals, about half of Cisco's ~$30B revenue. Most were all-stock and almost never more than 4–5% of market cap (16:30, 19:15).\n- Corp dev means cataloguing every company as \"inventory\" against a strategy statement. He ignored inbound sale requests: \"We never tried to pay attention to inbound\" (30:16).\n- On boards: talk a thriving CEO out of selling and help a struggling one sell. For the in-between case, ask whether the founder still has the fire and whether it can 10x (not 2x) from here. \"you have to be pretty cold and objective\" (27:28).\n- He pushes portfolio CEOs to have one or two coffees a week with NVIDIA, Amazon, OpenAI and even direct competitors. He calls it \"an inverse intelligence gathering mechanism\" (30:39).\n\n**Mistakes he owns:** criticised for buying StrataCom over Cascade, plus an optical acquisition that \"went nowhere\" (19:00–19:06; company not identifiable from the captions). Scientific Atlanta (after his M&A tenure; he moved to running a business unit in 2000) \"was a disaster\" (25:09). Cisco's 2000–01 crash: customers double-ordered and Cisco prepaid suppliers, \"we were taking the financial risk on those orders\" (44:33). The quarter missed by ~35–40% and the stock fell about 90% in six months.\n\n**AI-infra views (mostly contrarian):**\n- He expects the buildout to be fully used but a correction to come. The question is \"Whether it will be owned by the same companies or not\" (06:35). Net, \"I'm actually more optimistic about this cycle\" (08:46).\n- He calls NVIDIA's neocloud financing vendor financing in all but name (10:24). He thinks it is futile because scale industries consolidate: \"no matter how much you finance them, the little ones are always weaklings\" (21:04).\n- GPUs are outliving 5-year depreciation: H100s rent for more now than at launch, making them more financeable (12:23; speaker attributed by turn flow).\n- The supply constraint is fab capacity for 2–5 years, so fab owners are \"kingmakers\" (34:18). On demand, model routers and the 5–7x price gap between Anthropic and Kimi tokens will add price sensitivity (34:42).\n- \"the best architecture is the Google TPU architecture\" because it is built as one system (36:34). Cerebras-plus-Arista stacks are a patchwork. More model diversity will mean more chip diversity, and reasoning and KV-cache demands favour memory-rich 3D-stacked designs (40:37). He prefers copper to optics inside the server (43:19).\n- Data-center switches \"have the correct amount of bandwidth and the incorrect architecture\" (49:40). Predictable AI traffic calls for circuit or TDM-style fabrics, not stat-mux. That is an innovator's dilemma for Cisco and Broadcom, and adoption must come through the rack vendors.\n- Unclear from the captions: someone says \"we're investors in Cerebras\" (40:43). The speaker can't be told from the auto-captions (it could be an a16z host), so it isn't attributed to Volpi or Hanabi.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Hanabi is a credible early AI-native co-investor that recurs in Applied Compute (RL for enterprises, ex-OpenAI), Traversal (AI SRE automation, close to the Serval type), Simile and Netic. Volpi personally has a frontier-model pedigree: Cohere board, Scale, Cartesia (SSM architecture) and the Covariant board. But no sourced deal shows them backing a new pre-training lab, Hanabi never leads, and there is no Hanabi presence in AI chips or compute. Deals that matter to you: **Cartesia** (new architecture), **Cohere** (lab), **Applied Compute** and **Traversal** (AI-native automation). Hanabi is a plausible co-investor in your $20M slot on AI-native automation rounds, and a source of Index-network deal flow. They are unlikely to anchor a lab round.\n\n## Background\n- Born 1966-12-13 in Milan; moved to the US in 1984 for Stanford. BS in mechanical engineering, MS in manufacturing systems engineering, and an MBA from Stanford GSB in 1994 ([wiki](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Volpi)).\n- 1989: Hewlett-Packard, optoelectronics ([wiki](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Volpi)).\n- 1994–2007: Cisco. Business development, then led acquisitions from 1996, then chief strategy officer, then co-led the routing and service provider group ([wiki](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Volpi)). Forbes says they were on a Cisco team that acquired more than 70 companies ([src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/mike-volpi/)).\n- 2007: EIR at Sequoia, then CEO of Joost ([wiki](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Volpi)).\n- 2009: joined Index as a partner and co-founded its San Francisco office with Danny Rimer ([wiki](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Volpi); [Robot Brains ep](https://shows.acast.com/the-robot-brains/episodes/mike-volpi-on-why-vcs-are-investing-record-amounts-into-ai-s)).\n- 2023: retired from Index at 56 and kept their boards ([Bloomberg Linea](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/)). This conflicts with Forbes, which still lists them as an Index partner.\n- 2025: launched Hanabi Capital with Bryan Offutt and Ishani Thakur ([Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/mike-volpi/)). The Hanabi team page also lists Shreyan Jain (Partner) and Rachel Hansen (Operating Partner) ([src](https://hanabi.com/team)).\n- Boards: Ferrari (non-executive), Stanford Health Care, ClickHouse, Cohere, Hebbia, Kong, Scale, Wealthfront ([wiki](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Volpi)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://hanabi.com/team | Name and title only; no bio (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/mavolpi | Handle given in the a16z episode description ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LI3lxzHfRrU)); activity not checked |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n| Forbes profile | https://www.forbes.com/profile/mike-volpi/ | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2021-06 — The Robot Brains (host Pieter Abbeel) — \"Mike Volpi on why VCs are investing record amounts into AI startups\" — [link](https://shows.acast.com/the-robot-brains/episodes/mike-volpi-on-why-vcs-are-investing-record-amounts-into-ai-s) — The page describes them as investing mainly in infrastructure, open-source and AI. No transcript found, so no quotes.\n- 2026-11-11 — a16z (YouTube; with Raghu Raghuram and Guido Appenzeller) — \"Mike Volpi on Why AI Infrastructure Will Outlast the Hype Cycle\" (56 min) — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LI3lxzHfRrU) — Mined from YouTube auto-captions on 2026-11-11 (23 insight rows). Topics: AI buildout vs the 1990s fiber boom, GPU useful life, Cisco M&A playbook, exit advice, chips, data-center networking.\n- Nothing else found in this run (the web-search budget ran out mid-task, see Open questions).\n\n## How they think\n- **Infrastructure and open-source first.** Their boards cluster in data and dev infrastructure (Confluent, Elastic, ClickHouse, Kong, Cockroach), and AI is framed as the next infrastructure layer ([wiki](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Volpi)).\n- **Contrarian on architecture.** They publicly backed SSMs over transformers at Cartesia ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-next-generation-of-real-time-ai-models-our-investment-in-cartesia/)).\n- **Enterprise-data framing.** At Hebbia they framed the opportunity as \"a multi-billion dollar problem that has stumped industry for years\" ([src](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of)).\n- **Right call:** Scale AI, which returned about $1.8B to Index via Meta (reported). Cohere (board) is valued at $7B per Forbes.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Cartesia investment post (2024-12-12): their SSM and new-architecture thesis. [link](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-next-generation-of-real-time-ai-models-our-investment-in-cartesia/)\n2. Cohere Series A coverage (2021-09-08): why they took the board seat. [link](https://betakit.com/cohere-raises-40-million-to-make-natural-language-processing-more-accessible/)\n3. Hebbia Series A post (2022-09-27): their lead and the investor roster (Druckenmiller, Thiel, Wang). [link](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of)\n4. Bloomberg Linea on Index succession (2026-01-08): their exit and the Scale payout. [link](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. They have no visible public posting footprint. Their public voice comes through portfolio press quotes and the occasional podcast.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cohere | Series A, $40M | 2021-09-08 | Yes (Index led; Volpi joined board) | Section 32, Radical Ventures, Hinton, Fei-Fei Li, Abbeel, Urtasun | [src](https://betakit.com/cohere-raises-40-million-to-make-natural-language-processing-more-accessible/) |\n| Hebbia | Series A, $30M | 2022-09-27 | Yes (\"led by Mike Volpi\") | Radical, Jerry Yang, Ram Shriram, Druckenmiller, Thiel, Alexandr Wang, Abbeel | [src](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of) |\n| Cartesia | Seed, $22M (total $27M) | 2024-12-12 | Yes (Index led; Volpi quoted, Shardul Shah tagged) | A* Capital, Conviction, General Catalyst, Lightspeed, SV Angel | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-next-generation-of-real-time-ai-models-our-investment-in-cartesia/) |\n| Scale AI | Round not stated | not stated | Led Index's investment | not stated | [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/) |\n| Covariant | Board seat (round not stated) | by 2021 | not stated | | [wiki](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Volpi) |\n| Traversal (Hanabi) | Seed + Series A, $48M combined | 2025-06 (date per repo; page undated) | No (Sequoia and Kleiner led) | NFDG; Hanabi team named: Offutt, Thakur, Volpi | [src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement) |\n| Applied Compute (Hanabi) | $20M at $100M pre-launch | 2025-06-27 | No (Benchmark / Victor Lazarte led) | Sequoia, Conviction, Definition, Zach Frankel | [src](https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/ex-openai-applied-compute-raises-20m) |\n| Applied Compute (Hanabi) | $80M at $1.3B | 2026-04-08 | No (Kleiner led) | Elad Gil, Lux, Greenoaks, Neo | [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/fundraise) |\n| Applied Compute (Hanabi) | $350M at $3.25B | 2026-11-02 | No (Elad Gil led) | Lux, Kleiner, NVIDIA, Blackstone | [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise) |\n| Cognition (Hanabi) | >$400M at $10.2B | 2025-09-08 | No (Founders Fund led); Hanabi a new investor | BCV, D1, Lux, 8VC | [src](https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents) |\n| Netic (Hanabi) | Series A | 2025 (exact date not given) | No (Founders Fund led) | Greylock, Day One, SV Angel | [src](https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm) |\n| Simile (Hanabi) | Series A, $100M | 2026-02-12 (per repo) | No (Index led) | A*, BCV, Karpathy, Fei-Fei Li | [src](https://www.simile.com/blog/the-simulation-company) |\n| Simile (Hanabi) | Series B, >$200M at $2B | 2026-07-30 (per repo) | No (Greenoaks and Index co-led) | BCV, A*, Factory, CVS Health Ventures, Definition | [src](https://www.simile.com/blog/series-b) |\n| Mind Robotics (Hanabi) | unconfirmed | 2026 | unknown | | Wikipedia says so; the cited Robot Report article does not name Hanabi |\n\nThe Hanabi rows name the firm only, except Traversal, where Volpi is personally named.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Cohere (2021):** Index led the $40M A. Volpi took the board seat and said Cohere aimed to \"democratize access to one of the most important technologies of our time\" ([src](https://betakit.com/cohere-raises-40-million-to-make-natural-language-processing-more-accessible/)). Radical Ventures came first as a returning investor, so Index was the first big-fund lead but not the first check.\n- **Hebbia (2022):** Volpi led the $30M A. Pieter Abbeel, Henrique Dubugras and Raquel Urtasun were already in the seed, so Volpi came in after the AI-luminary angels ([src](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of)).\n- **Cartesia (2024):** Index led the seed. Volpi was the quoted partner and Shardul Shah was tagged on the post, which looks like a senior/junior pairing ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-next-generation-of-real-time-ai-models-our-investment-in-cartesia/)).\n- **Traversal (2025):** Hanabi's three partners are named together. Sequoia (Balkansky, Curnin) and Kleiner (Hamid, Naganath) led, with NFDG alongside ([src](https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement)).\n- The intro paths for these deals are not documented in any source read.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bryan Offutt | Hanabi co-founder (ex-Index) | 2025 | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/mike-volpi/) |\n| Ishani Thakur | Hanabi co-founder (ex-Index) | 2025 | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/mike-volpi/) |\n| Shreyan Jain | Hanabi partner | 2025 | [src](https://hanabi.com/team) |\n| Rachel Hansen | Hanabi operating partner | | [src](https://hanabi.com/team) |\n| Danny Rimer | Co-founded Index SF office; co-designed succession plan | 2009 | [wiki](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Volpi), [BL](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/) |\n| Pieter Abbeel | Co-investor (Cohere, Hebbia); Covariant board; podcast host | 2021 | [src](https://shows.acast.com/the-robot-brains/episodes/mike-volpi-on-why-vcs-are-investing-record-amounts-into-ai-s) |\n| Shardul Shah | Index colleague on Cartesia seed | 2024 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-next-generation-of-real-time-ai-models-our-investment-in-cartesia/) |\n| Nina Achadjian | Announced their promotion to Index partner | 2020 | [src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/our-new-partner-nina-achadjian) |\n| Stanley Druckenmiller | Co-investor in Volpi-led Hebbia A | 2022 | [src](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Hanabi's fund size, LPs and check size are not sourced. Is it a pure co-invest vehicle?\n- Index exit date: 2023 (Bloomberg Linea) versus still a partner (Forbes). Volpi said on a16z (2026-11-11) that he retired in 2023, which supports Bloomberg Linea; Forbes likely reflects the legacy boards.\n- Which optical-networking acquisition does Volpi call one that \"went nowhere\" (a16z 19:06)? The auto-captions garble it. Was the \"we're investors in Cerebras\" line (40:43) Volpi or a host?\n- Is Hanabi in Mind Robotics? Wikipedia claims it, but the cited article doesn't confirm.\n- Which Hanabi partner sourced Applied Compute, Cognition, Netic and Simile?\n- Hanabi's relationship with Index: it co-invested in Index-led Simile A and B. Does Hanabi get systematic Index allocations?\n- Any Hanabi exposure to labs or chips (none found).\n- The web-search budget was exhausted early in this run, so there was no podcast transcript or X search. Retry for Volpi interviews from 2025-26.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LI3lxzHfRrU\n- https://hanabi.com/team\n- https://www.forbes.com/profile/mike-volpi/\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Volpi\n- https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/\n- https://betakit.com/cohere-raises-40-million-to-make-natural-language-processing-more-accessible/\n- https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-next-generation-of-real-time-ai-models-our-investment-in-cartesia/\n- https://shows.acast.com/the-robot-brains/episodes/mike-volpi-on-why-vcs-are-investing-record-amounts-into-ai-s\n- https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement\n- https://www.upstartsmedia.com/p/ex-openai-applied-compute-raises-20m\n- https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific\n- https://appliedcompute.com/company/fundraise\n- https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise\n- https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents\n- https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2025/11/13/847406.htm\n- https://www.simile.com/blog/the-simulation-company\n- https://www.simile.com/blog/series-b\n- https://www.therobotreport.com/mind-robotics-raises-400m-scale-ai-powered-robots-in-manufacturing/\n", "person:miles-grimshaw": "\n# Miles Grimshaw\n\n_Upgraded to new template 2026-11-09: added Crux, Differential insight, Fit with my lens, and Dialectic #56 material. Earlier content kept._\n\n## One-line take\nHe is Thrive's \"bottom-up\" investor: he finds deals by looking at what developers and teams actually use, not by top-down market sizing. He pushed Thrive into GitHub at 23, led LangChain's first round at Benchmark, and was the named Thrive partner on Cursor's Jan 2025 round ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/06/benchmarks-view-on-the-ai-race-talking-with-miles-grimshaw/), [src](https://www.maginative.com/article/anysphere-raises-105m-for-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-reaching-2-5b-valuation/)).\n\n## Crux\nHe wins by joining the team before the round exists: year-long courtships (about a year with Cursor's Michael Truell before the Series A; about 1.5 years with Turbopuffer's Simon Eskildsen after a declined term sheet), recruiting help as the currency, and a decision made off \"rough numbers over a dinner\" rather than a spreadsheet ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw)). That is backed by Thrive's always-on, fast process: Turbopuffer was committed within an hour on a Monday night, and Mesh Optical from first meeting to agreement over one long weekend ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw)). The constraint, in his words, is that this does not scale, so they must be \"hyper-selective\" where they can be a real team member ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw)). He would not run a spreadsheet-first process or pretend to domain expertise (\"I don't know\" is, to him, the three most important words for an investor). The live tension: the bottom-up developer-tools investor now says software's main moat, accumulated wall-clock time, has largely been eliminated by AI, while Thrive pushes into AI silicon and photonics with no prior silicon experience ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw)).\n\n## Differential insight\nPrimary source for all of these: Dialectic #56 with Jackson Dahl, published 2026-09-01 (full transcript on the page) ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw)). Rows mirrored in `data/rows/miles-grimshaw-insights.csv`.\n- **Turbopuffer, deal origin.** He heard of it because Cursor used it for semantic search. After one call he self-invited to Ottawa for dinner: \"They told me I was the first person to fly to Ottawa.\" Thrive gave a term sheet the next day, which Eskildsen declined; he had mostly bootstrapped ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw), 12:32-15:51).\n- **Turbopuffer, timing.** About 1.5 years later Eskildsen rang at ~9:30pm ET on a Monday: \"Miles, this is that call.\" Team call at 10:15pm, committed by ~10:30pm ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw), 14:59). His X post on the deal cites \"1.5 yrs of getting to know\" the founder ([src](https://x.com/milesgrimshaw/status/2001673239958086037), 2025-12).\n- **Turbopuffer cap table.** \"Along with Lachy Groom, we're actually the only outside investors.\" ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw), 15:51). Thrive will sit beside a solo GP in a two-investor cap table.\n- **Cursor timing.** \"Our first meeting was probably a year before the first investment\" (the Series A). He says \"I never got a spreadsheet of any finances\"; Thrive then recruited for Cursor \"full-time\" before Truell picked them to lead the Series B ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw), 23:23-24:29). Cursor was passing \"tens of millions in ARR\" with \"zero salespeople\", and had a very small sales team until early 2026 ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw), 34:24).\n- **Gut check.** \"The gut question I always ask myself is: 'Can I imagine recruiting for this?'\" He insists \"We don't make a bet\", calling it a commitment ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw), 1:22:58).\n- **AI silicon, admitted constraint.** \"We have no prior silicon experience on the team.\" Thrive spent about a year learning the AI hardware supply chain and, at recording, was making its \"first investment there\" ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw), 17:43). Which deal that is remains unclear (see Open questions).\n- **Mesh Optical, deal origin.** Three ex-SpaceX Starlink laser-interconnect engineers, introduced the week they left by a founder he \"never backed\" but stayed close to. Friday meeting, Saturday follow-up, Sunday team pitch, agreed by Monday, partnering at incorporation in a field \"we had no business knowing anything about.\" He says he would not have had that confidence five years ago ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw), 1:12:19-1:17:23). The public $50M Series A (Feb 2026) names Philip Clark as the Thrive partner ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=731311)).\n- **Mistake admitted.** On leaving Thrive for Benchmark: \"I made the wrong call in that regard.\" He says an LP framed the return as an act of humility ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw), 1:37:17).\n- **OpenAI timing.** \"after we'd agreed to our investment in OpenAI at 150 billion\", during the Brockman/Schulman turmoil; Kushner flew to the West Coast twice in three days ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw), 1:37:17 answer).\n- **Software moats.** The old moat was \"wall-clock time\"; now \"the wall-clock time has kind of been eliminated\", so software companies need more surface area at lower margins ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw), 57:25).\n- **Stripe price discipline.** Thrive got in early \"at $3 billion or $5 billion\" and stepped in again post-ZIRP \"when everyone else was fearful\" ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw), 17:43).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is a GP at a multi-stage fund, not a micro-fund, but his recent work sits on the THESIS's core: Cursor (AI-native, from Series A), Turbopuffer (AI search infra, early), Chai Discovery (AI-bio, ~$30M seed co-led by Thrive with OpenAI) ([src](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement)), and Thrive's move into AI optics and silicon (Mesh Optical at incorporation) ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw)). Useful to me in three ways: (1) he shares tiny, concentrated cap tables with solo GPs (Lachy Groom at Turbopuffer), so co-investing is plausible; (2) his courtship timeline is a benchmark for how early a large fund shows up; (3) the founder who routed Mesh Optical to him is an unidentified talent node worth finding. Caveats: no sourced personal check size, and his historic core is developer tools and application software, not pre-training labs.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in the U.K. and moved to the U.S. at 12 ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/09/09/benchmarks-newest-partner-on-why-sometimes-you-just-have-to-fire-your-customer/)). He was raised first in England, then Boston, in a blended family of seven children ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- His stepfather was a second-generation Korean immigrant who founded the e-commerce software company ATG in Boston in the 1990s and later started a second company from the family living room ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/09/09/benchmarks-newest-partner-on-why-sometimes-you-just-have-to-fire-your-customer/)). Grimshaw credits his mother and stepfather with his \"Why not? Sure you can\" outlook ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- Yale graduate ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw)).\n- How he got into VC: in college he and friends taught weekend web-development classes in New York. One student, Will Gaybrick (later at Stripe), knew Josh Kushner, and that connection led to the Thrive job ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/09/09/benchmarks-newest-partner-on-why-sometimes-you-just-have-to-fire-your-customer/)). Colossus tells a slightly different version: after college in 2013 he started hanging around Thrive's office to see his friend Gaybrick, then an early Thrive partner. He was later called the firm's \"entrepreneur in residence,\" which was news to him ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- 2013: joined Thrive, \"as one of five partners\" per TechCrunch ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw)). By 2020 he was a general partner there. He helped raise Thrive's fourth and fifth funds and sourced Lattice, Mapbox, Benchling and Airtable ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw)). Thrive's 2024 welcome post says he spent eight years at the firm the first time (the post itself returned 403; this is from the search excerpt) ([src](https://medium.com/@thrivecapital/welcoming-miles-grimshaw-to-thrive-cc381f23f6bc)).\n- December 2020: joined Benchmark at 29 as its fifth general partner. He knew the Benchmark partners from shared boards: Benchling with Eric Vishria, Airtable with Peter Fenton, and Supergreat with Sarah Tavel ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw)). TheOrg dates the Benchmark tenure Feb 2021 to Mar 2024 ([src](https://theorg.com/org/langchain/org-chart/miles-grimshaw)).\n- March 5, 2024: TechCrunch reported he was leaving Benchmark to rejoin Thrive as a general partner, after about three years at Benchmark ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/05/miles-grimshaw-leaves-benchmark-to-re-join-kushners-thrive-capital)). On X he said he was \"elated to re-join the team at Thrive\" and looked forward to keeping on partnering with Benchmark ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/05/miles-grimshaw-leaves-benchmark-to-re-join-kushners-thrive-capital)).\n- Timing context: the move came about two months after the FT reported Thrive was preparing to raise at least $3B ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/05/miles-grimshaw-leaves-benchmark-to-re-join-kushners-thrive-capital)). By Sept 2025 Thrive had closed $5B in new funds and launched Thrive Holdings ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/laboss-podcast)). No source gives his own reason for leaving Benchmark.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://milesgrimshaw.com | Redirects (301) to his X profile; no standalone site content as of 2026-11-09 (checked directly) |\n| Substack / blog | https://substack.com/@milesgrimshaw | Profile with no posts and 7 subscribers. The bio is from the Benchmark era. As of 2026-11-09 ([src](https://substack.com/@milesgrimshaw)) |\n| X | https://x.com/milesgrimshaw | About 14.2K followers (third-party Digg snapshot, about 2026-11-09). His bio lists Cursor, Chai Discovery, Turbopuffer, Socket Security, Revel, LangChain, Benchling, Monzo, Segment and Airtable ([src](https://digg.com/rankings/milesgrimshaw)). X itself could not be fetched (402) |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | Not checked directly |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-09 — Dialectic (Jackson Dahl), #56 — \"Back Down the Mountain\" — https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw — full transcript. Turbopuffer and Cursor courtships, recruiting as gut check, AI silicon learning, Mesh Optical two-day decision, wall-clock-time moat, admits Benchmark move was \"the wrong call\" (see Differential insight). Read 2026-11-09.\n- 2023-09 — The Gradient Podcast (host Daniel Bashir), Ep. 90 — \"Miles Grimshaw: Benchmark, LangChain, and Investing in AI\" — https://thegradientpub.substack.com/p/miles-grimshaw-benchmark-langchain-ai-investing — topics include his investment philosophy, the \"decade of deep learning,\" why Benchmark backed LangChain, GPU shortages and LLM UX, and advice for AI founders ([src](https://thegradientpub.substack.com/p/miles-grimshaw-benchmark-langchain-ai-investing)).\n- 2023-06 — TechCrunch interview (Connie Loizos) — \"Benchmark's view on the AI race\" — https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/06/benchmarks-view-on-the-ai-race-talking-with-miles-grimshaw/ — Benchmark had not invested in foundation models and instead focused on \"the developer layer above that\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/06/benchmarks-view-on-the-ai-race-talking-with-miles-grimshaw/)).\n- 2021-09 — Fortune interview (Lucinda Shen) — \"Benchmark's newest partner on why sometimes, you just have to fire your customer\" — https://fortune.com/2021/09/09/benchmarks-newest-partner-on-why-sometimes-you-just-have-to-fire-your-customer/ — the Benchling \"bad revenue\" lesson (see Deal stories) ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/09/09/benchmarks-newest-partner-on-why-sometimes-you-just-have-to-fire-your-customer/)).\n- 2025-09 — LABOSS Podcast (Alex LaBossiere), #58 — \"Miles Grimshaw\" — https://pod.wave.co/podcast/laboss-podcast — 68 min on trillion-dollar companies, Silicon Valley as an idea, \"business genetics,\" and investing strategy ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/laboss-podcast)). Transcript not reviewed.\n\n## How they think\n- **Bottom-up, usage-first sourcing.** Colossus calls him Thrive's best \"bottom-up\" thinker, in contrast to the \"top-down\" and long-term partners. In his spare time he scrapes company websites, court documents and old investment memos ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). His GitHub thesis was that GitHub was what everyone already used, and that more infrastructure was being turned into code ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). The Cursor thesis followed the same logic: he pointed to how widely developers across Thrive's own portfolio already used it ([src](https://www.maginative.com/article/anysphere-raises-105m-for-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-reaching-2-5b-valuation/)).\n- **Developer tools as a core thesis.** He helped build up Thrive's developer-tools portfolio ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). At Benchmark he invested across application software, developer tools and consumer ([src](https://techcrunch.com/author/miles-grimshaw)).\n- **AI: the developer layer over the model layer (2023).** Benchmark had not found conviction that any single foundation-model company would hold an enduring, outsize share. He cited open source catching up quickly and knowledge diffusing ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/06/benchmarks-view-on-the-ai-race-talking-with-miles-grimshaw/)). How this view sits with Thrive's own model-layer bets since he returned is an open question.\n- **Word of mouth as signal.** On LangChain he stressed its \"remarkable word of mouth\" and said the company did no marketing ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/06/benchmarks-view-on-the-ai-race-talking-with-miles-grimshaw/)).\n- **Focus over revenue.** Revenue from customers who don't fit the long-term product can be a distraction. A small, fast-growing market can be as attractive as a large TAM. Capital is usually not the limiting factor for early companies. Founders should weigh long-term return on equity over the highest headline valuation ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/09/09/benchmarks-newest-partner-on-why-sometimes-you-just-have-to-fire-your-customer/)).\n- **Ignore macro.** Great companies should outshine whatever macro environment exists at entry or exit ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/09/09/benchmarks-newest-partner-on-why-sometimes-you-just-have-to-fire-your-customer/)).\n- **Service over capital; check size follows the company.** Benchmark aims to serve on boards \"for a decade,\" and he says the value is the \"commitment of service,\" not the money. Benchmark also didn't fix check sizes up front ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/06/benchmarks-view-on-the-ai-race-talking-with-miles-grimshaw/)).\n- **Calls right:** GitHub (about 7x in 18 months for Thrive per Colossus) ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). LangChain seed in April 2023, with a reported $1.25B valuation by Oct 2025 ([src](https://www.revenuememo.com/p/langchain-funding)). Cursor: Thrive led successive rounds from Dec 2024 ([src](https://www.maginative.com/article/anysphere-raises-105m-for-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-reaching-2-5b-valuation/), [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/06/05/anysphere-raises-900m-ai-powered-cursor-code-editor)).\n- **Calls wrong / mixed:** nothing sourced. Supergreat and Glide outcomes not checked.\n\n## Read these first\nHe has published almost no long-form writing under his own name. His site redirects to X and his Substack is empty ([src](https://substack.com/@milesgrimshaw)). No early essays on developer tools or open source were found, and the Wayback Machine could not be reached. Best substitutes:\n1. Colossus, \"The New World\" (Oct 2025): the GitHub story and how he works ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n2. TechCrunch, \"Benchmark's view on the AI race\" (Jun 2023): his AI-stack thesis and LangChain ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/06/benchmarks-view-on-the-ai-race-talking-with-miles-grimshaw/)).\n3. Fortune (Sep 2021): the Benchling \"fire your customer\" lesson and his views on TAM and valuation ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/09/09/benchmarks-newest-partner-on-why-sometimes-you-just-have-to-fire-your-customer/)).\n4. The Gradient Podcast Ep. 90 (Sep 2023) ([src](https://thegradientpub.substack.com/p/miles-grimshaw-benchmark-langchain-ai-investing)).\n5. LABOSS Podcast #58 (Sep 2025): the most recent long-form conversation ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/laboss-podcast)).\n\n## Social engagement\nX could not be fetched directly (402). A third-party snapshot shows about 14.2K followers, and his bio lists portfolio companies, including recent ones such as Turbopuffer and Socket Security ([src](https://digg.com/rankings/milesgrimshaw)). His 2024 return was announced on X, both by him and by Josh Kushner, who called him \"a formidable partner to our founders\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/05/miles-grimshaw-leaves-benchmark-to-re-join-kushners-thrive-capital)). Posting cadence, topics, and reply or amplification patterns: nothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n\"Led?\" is the firm-level lead. Whether Grimshaw personally led the deal is in the notes.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Benchling | Investment (Thrive) | 2015 | ? | | [src](https://fortune.com/2021/09/09/benchmarks-newest-partner-on-why-sometimes-you-just-have-to-fire-your-customer/) |\n| Benchling | $7M round | 2016-10 | Thrive led | a16z | [src](https://www.benchling.com/news/benchling-announces-7m-in-new-funding-led-by-thrive-capital) |\n| GitHub | $250M Series B | 2015-07-29 | No (Sequoia led) | a16z, IVP | [src](https://venturebeat.com/business/github-confirms-that-it-has-raised-250m-led-by-sequoia-capital), [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Segment | $27M Series B | 2015-10 | Thrive led | Accel, KPCB | [src](https://startupintros.com/news/segment-series-b-2015-10) — Gaybrick took the board seat; techcrunch.com/author lists Grimshaw as a former Segment director |\n| Lattice | Seed (~$2.8M) | 2016 | Thrive led (per Lattice) | Slack Fund, Khosla, SV Angel, Fuel | [src](https://lattice.com/blog/raising-our-series-a), [src](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2017/08/02/lattice-lets-in-series-a-cash/) |\n| Lattice | $6.4M Series A | 2017-07-31 | Thrive led | | [src](https://lattice.com/blog/raising-our-series-a) |\n| Monzo | £19.5M round | 2017-02-22 | Thrive led (£13M) | Passion Capital, Orange Digital Ventures | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/02/22/confirmed-uk-challenger-bank-monzo-raises-19-5m-with-another-2-5m-in-crowdfunding-planned) |\n| Airtable | $100M Series C | 2018-11-15 | Co-led (Thrive, Benchmark, Coatue) | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=1747109) — TC names Kushner as Thrive's lead; Kushner 2024 credits Grimshaw with Airtable |\n| Mapbox | ? | ? | ? | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw) (sourced; board) |\n| Supergreat | $6.5M Series A (Benchmark) | 2020-12 | Benchmark led | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw) — Tavel's deal; Grimshaw also on board |\n| Glide | $20M Series A | 2022-04 | Benchmark led | | [src](https://www.glideapps.com/series-a), [src](https://startupintros.com/news/glide-apps-series-a-2022-04) |\n| LangChain | $10M seed | 2023-04-04 | Benchmark led (Grimshaw) | | [src](https://www.langchain.com/blog/announcing-our-10m-seed-round-led-by-benchmark), [src](https://techcrunch.com/author/miles-grimshaw) |\n| SphinxBio | ? | ? | ? | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/author/miles-grimshaw) (board, Benchmark era; round not found) |\n| Chai Discovery | ~$30M seed | 2024-09-09 | Co-led (Thrive, OpenAI, Dimension) | Conviction, Neo, Lachy Groom, Amplify | [src](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement) — Grimshaw leading it is (unverified) |\n| Anysphere (Cursor) | $105M Series B | 2025-01-14 | Thrive led | a16z, Benchmark | [src](https://www.maginative.com/article/anysphere-raises-105m-for-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-reaching-2-5b-valuation/) |\n| Anysphere (Cursor) | $900M Series C | 2025-06-05 | Thrive led | | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/06/05/anysphere-raises-900m-ai-powered-cursor-code-editor) |\n| Turbopuffer | Seed | 2025-12-18 | ? | Lachy Groom, Lux | [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-12-18/turbopuffers-simon-eskildsen-on-adding-thrive-capital-reaching-trillions-of-objects-and-building-a-concentrated-cap-table-like-a-team) — Grimshaw's role (unverified) |\n\n## Deal stories\n**GitHub (2015).** Per Colossus (Oct 2025):\n- Grimshaw, then 23, pushed GitHub at an \"11pm investment meeting\" as a strategic crown jewel. GitHub had bootstrapped for years and raised only once, and it wasn't seen as a fast grower.\n- The growth round was outside the normal scope of Thrive's $400M fund, but Thrive put \"7% of Thrive's $400 million fund into the company.\" His pitch to Kushner was simple: everyone uses it.\n- After the wire, GitHub fired its CFO, head of product and head of marketing following a sexual-harassment scandal. LPs feared a zero, and Grimshaw recalls mentors at other firms urging him to sell.\n- Thrive instead sent in Nabil Mallick as effective interim CFO. It then \"invested another $120 million across two tranches at a 25% discount\" and made about 7x in 18 months.\n- Microsoft bought GitHub in 2018 for $7.5B in stock ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- The public round Thrive joined was the $250M Series B led by Sequoia on 2015-07-29 ([src](https://venturebeat.com/business/github-confirms-that-it-has-raised-250m-led-by-sequoia-capital)).\n\n**Benchling (2015 on).** He invested at Thrive in 2015 ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/09/09/benchmarks-newest-partner-on-why-sometimes-you-just-have-to-fire-your-customer/)). Thrive led the Oct 2016 $7M round ([src](https://www.benchling.com/news/benchling-announces-7m-in-new-funding-led-by-thrive-capital)).\n- After the investment, Benchling dropped an early customer whose needs didn't fit its target market, so revenue fell. He tells this as the \"fire your customer\" lesson ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/09/09/benchmarks-newest-partner-on-why-sometimes-you-just-have-to-fire-your-customer/)).\n- By 2020 he sat on Benchling's board with Benchmark's Eric Vishria, and boards like this were how he got to know Benchmark ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw)). A search summary says Benchmark led Benchling's 2018 $14.5M round; that page wasn't fetched, so this is (unverified) ([src](https://www.biospectrumasia.com/news/83/11097/benchling-closes-at-14-5m-in-series-b-round.html)).\n\n**LangChain (2023).** He led Benchmark's $10M seed, LangChain's first financing, announced 2023-04-04, and joined the board ([src](https://www.langchain.com/blog/announcing-our-10m-seed-round-led-by-benchmark), [src](https://techcrunch.com/author/miles-grimshaw)). He cited word-of-mouth growth with no marketing ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/06/benchmarks-view-on-the-ai-race-talking-with-miles-grimshaw/)). Sequoia led the next round ([src](https://venturebeat.com/ai/langchain-lands-25m-round-launches-platform-to-support-entire-llm-application-lifecycle)). Intro path: nothing found.\n\n**Glide (2022).** Benchmark led a $20M Series A and Grimshaw joined the board ([src](https://www.glideapps.com/series-a)). He compared Glide to Shopify for stores and Canva for design ([src](https://startupintros.com/news/glide-apps-series-a-2022-04)).\n\n**Cursor (2024 on, back at Thrive).** Thrive led Anysphere's $105M round in Jan 2025, with a16z and Benchmark participating. Grimshaw was named as the Thrive partner and pointed to widespread Cursor use across Thrive's portfolio ([src](https://www.maginative.com/article/anysphere-raises-105m-for-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-reaching-2-5b-valuation/)). LABOSS credits him with leading Thrive's Cursor investment ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/laboss-podcast)). Benchmark, his old firm, was also on the cap table ([src](https://www.maginative.com/article/anysphere-raises-105m-for-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-reaching-2-5b-valuation/)).\n\n**Turbopuffer (2024-2025, Thrive).** Found via Cursor's usage, one call, dinner in Ottawa, term sheet the next day, declined, about 1.5 years of staying close, then a Monday-night call and same-night commit. Thrive and Lachy Groom are the only outside investors ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw), [src](https://x.com/milesgrimshaw/status/2001673239958086037)).\n\n**Mesh Optical (2025-2026, Thrive).** Introduced by a founder he never backed; agreed over a long weekend at incorporation ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw)). Thrive led a $50M Series A announced 2026-02-17, with Also Capital and Banner VC; SiliconANGLE names Philip Clark as the Thrive partner ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=731311)). An aggregator citing Bloomberg says the FTC cleared a Musk/SpaceX acquisition of Mesh on 2026-06-25, terms undisclosed (aggregator, Bloomberg not fetched; unverified) ([src](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/musk-cleared-to-acquire-mesh-optical-a-spacex-alumni-startup)).\n\n**Cursor, how it started.** First met Truell about a year before Thrive's Series A; rough numbers over dinner, no spreadsheet; recruiting help was the pitch; Truell then chose Thrive to lead the Series B ([src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw)).\n\n**Benchmark exit and board seats.** No source found says what happened to his Benchmark-era seats at LangChain, Glide, SphinxBio and Supergreat after March 2024. TheOrg still lists him on Glide's board ([src](https://theorg.com/org/doji/org-chart/miles-grimshaw), unverified).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Simon Eskildsen (Turbopuffer) | Courted ~1.5 yrs after declined term sheet; investor | ~2024 | [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw) |\n| Lachy Groom | Only other outside investor in Turbopuffer | 2025 | [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw) |\n| Michael Truell (Cursor) | Met ~1 yr before Series A; recruiting partner | ~2023-24 | [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw) |\n| Philip Clark (Thrive) | Colleague; named Thrive partner on Mesh Optical | 2026 | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=731311) |\n| Jack Altman (Benchmark) | Described as a good friend | ? | [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw) |\n| Will Gaybrick | Friend; introduced him to Kushner and Thrive (student in his web-dev class per Fortune) | ~2013 | [src](https://fortune.com/2021/09/09/benchmarks-newest-partner-on-why-sometimes-you-just-have-to-fire-your-customer/), [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Joshua Kushner | Founder of Thrive; his boss and partner across both stints | 2013 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/05/miles-grimshaw-leaves-benchmark-to-re-join-kushners-thrive-capital) |\n| Thrive Capital | Partner then GP (2013–2020); GP again from Mar 2024 | 2013 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/05/miles-grimshaw-leaves-benchmark-to-re-join-kushners-thrive-capital) |\n| Benchmark | General Partner | 2020-12 to 2024-03 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/05/miles-grimshaw-leaves-benchmark-to-re-join-kushners-thrive-capital) |\n| Eric Vishria (Benchmark) | Co-director on Benchling board | by 2020 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw) |\n| Peter Fenton (Benchmark) | Co-director on Airtable board | by 2020 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw) |\n| Sarah Tavel (Benchmark) | Co-director on Supergreat board | 2020 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw) |\n| Nabil Mallick (Thrive) | Thrive colleague; interim CFO at GitHub during the 2015 crisis | 2015 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Benchling | Board director | by 2020 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw) |\n| Airtable | Board director | by 2020 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw) |\n| Lattice | Board director | by 2020 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw) |\n| Mapbox | Board director | by 2020 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw) |\n| Segment, Monzo | Former board director (per Benchmark-era bio) | ? | [src](https://techcrunch.com/author/miles-grimshaw) |\n| Glide | Board director | 2022 | [src](https://www.glideapps.com/series-a) |\n| LangChain (Harrison Chase) | Board director; led first financing | 2023-04 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/author/miles-grimshaw) |\n| SphinxBio | Board director (Benchmark-era bio) | ? | [src](https://techcrunch.com/author/miles-grimshaw) |\n| Anysphere, Doji, Chai Discovery, Revel | Board director (unverified, TheOrg only) | ? | [src](https://theorg.com/org/doji/org-chart/miles-grimshaw) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which AI-silicon deal was Thrive's \"first investment there\" at recording (mid-2026)? Mesh Optical (Feb 2026) seems to predate it, so it is probably an unannounced deal.\n- Who is the founder that introduced Mesh Optical (\"best young people recruiting out there\")? Recorded as a needs-id lead.\n- Did Mesh's reported SpaceX/Musk acquisition close, and at what price? Fetch the Bloomberg piece.\n- Was Thrive's Turbopuffer entry a seed or a later round, and what size? TBPN digest labels it seed (unverified).\n- His personal check size: still nothing sourced.\n- LaBossiere #58 transcript (YouTube https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OWjg27u5rpA) and the \"Why Software Moats Just Disappeared\" video (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MZ14rrkHVbg) not yet reviewed.\n- What happened to the LangChain, Glide, SphinxBio and Supergreat board seats after he left Benchmark in March 2024? Did Benchmark partners take them, or did he keep any?\n- Why he left Benchmark after about three years. No source gives a reason.\n- Is he on Cursor's board? Only TheOrg says so.\n- Did he personally lead Chai Discovery, Turbopuffer, Socket Security, Revel and Doji? Find the round announcements.\n- Which Thrive round did he lead in Airtable? TechCrunch credits Kushner with the Series C.\n- Mapbox round details, and the SphinxBio round at Benchmark.\n- Transcripts of the LABOSS #58 and Gradient #90 episodes, for direct quotes on \"business genetics\" and his thinking on trillion-dollar companies.\n- Early writing: check the Wayback Machine for milesgrimshaw.com from before the X redirect.\n- Thrive's welcome post (Medium, 403): read it in full.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/miles-grimshaw (Dialectic #56 transcript, 2026-09-01; added 2026-11-09)\n- https://x.com/milesgrimshaw/status/2001673239958086037\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=731311\n- https://aiweekly.co/alerts/musk-cleared-to-acquire-mesh-optical-a-spacex-alumni-startup (aggregator)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/05/miles-grimshaw-leaves-benchmark-to-re-join-kushners-thrive-capital\n- https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/11/benchmark-fills-out-its-yes-bench-with-miles-grimshaw\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/06/benchmarks-view-on-the-ai-race-talking-with-miles-grimshaw/\n- https://techcrunch.com/author/miles-grimshaw\n- https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/\n- https://fortune.com/2021/09/09/benchmarks-newest-partner-on-why-sometimes-you-just-have-to-fire-your-customer/\n- https://medium.com/@thrivecapital/welcoming-miles-grimshaw-to-thrive-cc381f23f6bc\n- https://thegradientpub.substack.com/p/miles-grimshaw-benchmark-langchain-ai-investing\n- https://pod.wave.co/podcast/laboss-podcast\n- https://substack.com/@milesgrimshaw\n- https://digg.com/rankings/milesgrimshaw\n- https://theorg.com/org/doji/org-chart/miles-grimshaw\n- https://theorg.com/org/langchain/org-chart/miles-grimshaw\n- https://www.langchain.com/blog/announcing-our-10m-seed-round-led-by-benchmark\n- https://venturebeat.com/ai/langchain-lands-25m-round-launches-platform-to-support-entire-llm-application-lifecycle\n- https://www.revenuememo.com/p/langchain-funding\n- https://www.glideapps.com/series-a\n- https://startupintros.com/news/glide-apps-series-a-2022-04\n- https://venturebeat.com/business/github-confirms-that-it-has-raised-250m-led-by-sequoia-capital\n- https://www.benchling.com/news/benchling-announces-7m-in-new-funding-led-by-thrive-capital\n- https://www.biospectrumasia.com/news/83/11097/benchling-closes-at-14-5m-in-series-b-round.html\n- https://lattice.com/blog/raising-our-series-a\n- https://globalventuring.com/blog/2017/08/02/lattice-lets-in-series-a-cash/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2017/02/22/confirmed-uk-challenger-bank-monzo-raises-19-5m-with-another-2-5m-in-crowdfunding-planned\n- https://techcrunch.com/?p=1747109\n- https://startupintros.com/news/segment-series-b-2015-10\n- https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement\n- https://www.maginative.com/article/anysphere-raises-105m-for-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-reaching-2-5b-valuation/\n- https://siliconangle.com/2025/06/05/anysphere-raises-900m-ai-powered-cursor-code-editor\n- https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-12-18/turbopuffers-simon-eskildsen-on-adding-thrive-capital-reaching-trillions-of-objects-and-building-a-concentrated-cap-table-like-a-team\n", "person:mohamed-sid-siddeek": "\n# Mohamed \"Sid\" Siddeek\n\n## One-line take\nSiddeek runs the only NVIDIA investing pool that has a stated returns mandate, and that pool now writes nine-figure checks into new-lab seeds. But he is never named on a round and does almost no public talking. Follow him as the gatekeeper of NVIDIA's syndicate slot, not as a personal deal-maker.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** Siddeek moved back to NVIDIA in 2021 for a pitch no financial VC can match. In his words, he can offer \"something no other financial VC or corporate VC could and get a seat at the table\" ([GCV Powerlist 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md)).\n- **The constraint.** He shares NVIDIA's investing with corporate development under Vishal Bhagwati, which is purely strategic. NVentures has \"a returns focus as well, which Vishal doesn't\" ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)). The bigger lab checks (Reflection B, OpenAI, Anthropic) appear to go through corporate development rather than through him. GCV also says the unit must avoid looking like a closed loop that funds future NVIDIA customers ([GCV 2026](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-mohamed-siddeek-nventures/?format=md)).\n- **What he would never do.** He has no external LPs, since the money comes off the balance sheet ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)). No source shows him seeking personal attribution or running a public brand.\n\n## Differential insight\nEvery quote below was checked against the raw GCV markdown. All of them come from one interviewer outlet (Global Corporate Venturing), and no podcast, X account, blog or talk was found (see Podcast and talk appearances).\n\n- **His filter is \"any place Nvidia touches\", then investability.** He describes it as two steps: \"It's really any place Nvidia touches\" and \"That's the first filter\". After that comes a second filter on \"which areas are investible\" ([GCV Powerlist 2024, 2024-06](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md)). This confirms the two-filter wording that `firms/nvidia.md` flagged as unverified.\n- **A third criterion: \"zero-billion dollar market\".** After team and technology he weighs market potential, including \"what we call a zero-billion dollar market – something brand new\" ([GCV 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)). The firm file quoted only the team and technology criteria. This is the frame that fits nine-figure seeds into labs with no revenue (Ineffable, Periodic, Inherent).\n- **Why he came back: a unique seat at the table.** He wanted \"a value proposition that's unique to the market\" ([GCV Powerlist 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md)). GCV also paraphrases him: a big incentive to return was working with Jensen Huang again. So what NVentures sells is technical and go-to-market access, not money.\n- **He says the returns mandate sets him apart from corporate development.** \"we have a returns focus as well, which Vishal doesn't\" ([GCV 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)). This is the cleanest primary statement of the split between NVIDIA's two pools.\n- **Team first, and humility counts.** \"humility is something we look for\" ([GCV 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)). He looks at pedigree, experience and \"how deep the bench is\" (same source).\n- **Diligence goes deep on the technology.** \"we spend a lot of time kicking the tyres on that\" ([GCV 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)). He asks whether the platform will hold up over \"the next five or 10 years\".\n- **No AI screening of deals, as of 2024.** NVentures did not use NVIDIA compute to filter deals and relied on traditional, team-led sourcing. Siddeek said that could change (reporter's paraphrase, [GCV 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)).\n- **He moves into new sectors one at a time.** \"But we're approaching it one at a time.\" At the time he named healthcare and manufacturing as done and financial services as a gap ([GCV 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)). By 2026 the unit was in legal AI (Legora), which fits a sector-by-sector expansion.\n- **He contrasts SoftBank's culture with NVIDIA's.** GCV reports he found SoftBank Vision Fund's culture \"very different\" to NVIDIA ([GCV 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)). He gave no detail. This is low-confidence colour, not an insight row.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The deals are high-fit. NVentures shows up in the first priced round of new labs and AI infrastructure: Periodic seed, Ineffable seed (at least $250M), Inherent seed, RadixArk seed, Bedrock seed+A, plus Ricursive A and Reflection A. That makes NVentures one of the most common co-investors in the very rounds the owner wants to join with ~$20M. Three things cap the fit as a relationship:\n- No external LPs, so he is not an LP prospect for the owner.\n- He is never personally named on a round, so warm intros run through the unit and the lead investor, not through him.\n- He is a corporate VP and not a check-writer the owner can syndicate with in the usual way. The NVIDIA slot in a lab seed is usually allocated by the lead (Sequoia, Lightspeed, a16z, Accel, Radical).\n\nMost useful as a signal: if NVentures is in a lab seed, NVIDIA compute access and a possible corporate-development follow-on are in play. Reflection went from an NVentures A check in March 2025 to an NVIDIA-led $2B B seven months later ([ledger](../sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md)).\n\n## Background\n- Education: Yale (BS), Wharton (MBA), engineering studies at Oxford. Research co-author in PNAS ([genesis.ml](https://www.genesis.ml/team/mohamed-sid-siddeek)). The paper itself was not identified.\n- 1999: on NVIDIA's IPO team ([genesis.ml](https://www.genesis.ml/team/mohamed-sid-siddeek)).\n- Early 2000s: investor relations manager at NVIDIA ([GCV Powerlist 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md)).\n- Close to nine years at Mubadala, heading investments for its technology and telecom unit ([GCV Powerlist 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md)). Exact dates not found.\n- Nearly two years at SoftBank Vision Fund, right before 2021 ([GCV 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)).\n- 2021: rejoined NVIDIA as corporate vice president \"after 17 years away\" ([GCV 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md); [GCV Powerlist 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md)).\n- Early 2023: NVentures formally launched as the successor to NVIDIA GPU Ventures, with him as head ([GCV 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)).\n- On the GCV Powerlist in 2024, 2025 and 2026 ([2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md), [2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md), [2026](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-mohamed-siddeek-nventures/?format=md)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.genesis.ml/team/mohamed-sid-siddeek | Genesis Molecular AI team page. His role there is not stated. As of 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked (LinkedIn is not used as a source here) | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-03 — Global Corporate Venturing interview (print) — [link](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md) — covers tech-bio, his criteria, and the split between the returns mandate and corporate development.\n- 2024-06 — GCV Powerlist 2024 profile, with fresh quotes — [link](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md) — covers the two-filter mandate and why he returned to NVIDIA.\n- No podcast, video talk or panel found. One web search for podcasts, interviews and panels on 2026-11-10 surfaced only GCV pages and other people named Sid.\n\n## How they think\n- The thesis is that \"AI will touch every single industry\". He expands into verticals one at a time ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)).\n- **Tech-bio.** He thinks in-silico work raises the odds before wet-lab testing, and NVentures leaned heavily into drug discovery: Relation, Terray, Generate and Genesis ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)).\n- **Evaluation order.** He weighs the team (pedigree, bench depth, humility), then how durable the technology is, then market potential, including zero-billion-dollar markets.\n- **Calls.** Generate Biomedicines IPO'd for $400M on Nasdaq in Feb 2026, and Replicate was acquired ([GCV 2026](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-mohamed-siddeek-nventures/?format=md)). Neither call is attributed to him personally. No admitted mistakes found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [GCV interview, 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md). His longest on-record statement: the criteria, the returns mandate versus corporate development, and tech-bio.\n2. [GCV Powerlist 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md). The two-filter mandate and why he came back.\n3. [GCV Powerlist 2026](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-mohamed-siddeek-nventures/?format=md). Recent deal list, the closed-loop constraint, and the count of more than 125 rounds.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public X or Substack presence is known.\n\n## Portfolio\nThese are NVentures rounds from the [NVIDIA frontier ledger](../sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md) and GCV. **Siddeek is not personally named on any of them.** They are listed because he heads the unit.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ineffable Intelligence | Seed, $1.1B (NVentures at least $250M) | 2026-04-27 | no | Sequoia, Lightspeed (co-leads); Index; Google; DST; UK Sovereign AI | [TNW](https://thenextweb.com/news/ineffable-intelligence-david-silver-sequoia-nvidia-5-billion) |\n| Periodic Labs | Seed, $300M | 2025-09-30 | no | a16z (lead); Felicis; DST; Accel; Bezos; Elad Gil; Eric Schmidt; Jeff Dean | [Maginative](https://www.maginative.com/article/periodic-labs-launches-with-300m-to-build-an-ai-scientist/) |\n| Inherent | Seed, $50M | 2026-05-30 | no | Radical, Index (co-leads); Ex/Ante; Metaplanet | [TNW](https://thenextweb.com/news/inherent-ai-50-million-seed-deepmind-faraday-science) |\n| RadixArk | Seed, $100M | 2026-05-05 | no | Accel (lead); Spark (co-lead); AMD; MediaTek; Databricks | [RadixArk](https://www.radixark.com/blog/radixark-launches-100m-seed) |\n| Reflection AI | Series A, $105M | 2025-03-07 | no | Lightspeed, CRV (co-leads); Sequoia | [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956) |\n| Ricursive Intelligence | Series A, $300M | 2026-01-26 | no | Lightspeed (lead); DST; Felicis; 49 Palms; Radical AI; Sequoia | [AI Journal](https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/) |\n| Bedrock Robotics | Seed + A, $80M | 2025-07-16 | no | Eclipse; 8VC; Two Sigma Ventures; Valor; and others | [PR Newswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/bedrock-robotics-emerges-from-stealth-with-80m-in-funding-for-autonomous-construction-technology-302506159.html) |\n| Bedrock Robotics | Series B, $270M | 2026-02-04 | no | Valor Atreides AI Fund, CapitalG (co-leads); Xora; 8VC; Eclipse | [PR Newswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/bedrock-robotics-raises-270-million-in-series-b-funding-to-accelerate-the-future-of-autonomous-construction-302679014.html) |\n| Neocis | 2024 round (size not given) | 2024 | yes (NVentures led) | not given | [GCV Powerlist 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md) |\n| Relation Therapeutics | 2024 round (size not given) | 2024 | yes (NVentures led) | not given | [GCV Powerlist 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md) |\n| Legora | Series D extension, $50M | 2026 | not stated | not stated | [GCV 2026](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-mohamed-siddeek-nventures/?format=md) |\n| Emerald AI | Seed extension, $22.7M | 2026 | not stated | not stated | [GCV 2026](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-mohamed-siddeek-nventures/?format=md) |\n| Freeform | Series B, $67M | 2026 | not stated | not stated | [GCV 2026](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-mohamed-siddeek-nventures/?format=md) |\n\n**Timing.** In the frontier set NVentures is never the first check:\n- **Periodic:** Felicis committed before incorporation.\n- **Ineffable:** Flying Fish wrote an earlier first check.\n- **Bedrock:** Eclipse formed the company in 2024.\n- **Ricursive:** NVentures came in about 1 to 2 months after a seed led by Sequoia.\n- **Reflection:** NVentures arrived at the Series A.\n\nBut it is reliably in the **first priced round** of new labs (Periodic, Ineffable, Inherent, RadixArk, Bedrock), always alongside a top-tier lead. All timing is per the [ledger](../sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md).\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found that names Siddeek in how any deal happened. The one pattern that is sourced belongs to the unit, not to him: an NVentures check at Reflection's Series A (2025-03, ~$555M valuation), then an NVIDIA-led $2B Series B seven months later at $8B ([SiliconANGLE A](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956), [SiliconANGLE B](https://siliconangle.com/?p=715519)). No source says whether he was involved in the B.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jensen Huang (NVIDIA) | Worked for him 1999 to early 2000s. Cites working with Huang again as a reason to return (GCV paraphrase) | 1999 | [GCV Powerlist 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md) |\n| Vishal Bhagwati (NVIDIA corp dev) | Runs the parallel, purely strategic pool. Siddeek draws the line between them on the returns mandate | 2021+ | [GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md) |\n| John Wesley (NVentures) | Investment director under him at the 2023 relaunch | 2023 | [GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md) |\n| Mubadala | Head of technology and telecom investments, about nine years | pre-2019 (dates not found) | [GCV Powerlist 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md) |\n| SoftBank Vision Fund | Investor, about two years before 2021 | ~2019 | [GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md) |\n| Genesis Molecular AI | Listed on the team page under \"NVentures, NVIDIA\". Role not stated: board, observer or advisor is unclear | not found | [genesis.ml](https://www.genesis.ml/team/mohamed-sid-siddeek) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What is his role at Genesis Molecular AI (board seat or observer)? This is the only company page that lists him personally.\n- Who does he report to: the CFO, Huang, or neither?\n- Was the $250M+ Ineffable check signed off by him, or was it a corporate-development allocation that went through NVentures?\n- Which NVentures partners own the lab deals (Periodic, Inherent, RadixArk, Ineffable)? Nobody is named.\n- Mubadala dates, and which deals there. The Mubadala and Vision Fund network could explain his ties to Gulf capital.\n- The ledger contradicts itself on Inherent. One line says Radical \"co-led the Inherent seed with NVentures\", but the row lists Radical and Index as co-leads and NVentures as not leading.\n\n## Sources\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md (2024-03-22)\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md (2024-06)\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md (2025-06-17)\n- https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-mohamed-siddeek-nventures/?format=md (2026-06-22)\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/emerging-leaders-2024-john-wesley-nventures/?format=md\n- https://www.genesis.ml/team/mohamed-sid-siddeek\n- ../sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md and ../firms/nvidia.md\n- Deal sources linked in the Portfolio table\n", "person:molly-welch": "\n# Molly Welch\n\n## One-line take\nRadical Ventures' San Francisco partner for AI-native applications (Spara, Outset) and physical-world AI (P-1 AI, Generalist, Muon Space). She led P-1 AI's $23M seed, holds its board seat, and hosts Radical's podcast, so she is in the room with the researchers Radical courts. [src](https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/) [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026072916/engineering-ai-startup-p-1-ai-announces-its-series-a-financing-led-by-nea-adding-jeff-immelt-to-the-company-s-board/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** She worked on Google Brain \"collaborating with research leadership on commercial initiatives\" [src](https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/). That background shows in who she backs. P-1 AI combines a DeepMind researcher (Aleksa Gordić) with ex-Airbus CTO Paul Eremenko, and its seed angels include Jeff Dean, Peter Welinder and Zak Stone [src](https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-the-physical-world-with-artificial-general-engineering-intelligence/). She also writes on her own Substack and hosts Radical Talks, which puts her in conversation with researchers such as Jim Fan and Ross Taylor.\n- **What constrains her:** Radical splits deals by partner. Pre-training labs and compute sit mainly with Jordan Jacobs, Rob Toews, David Katz and Aaron Rosenberg, and the growth deals she is named on are co-credited. Generalist is co-credited to Toews, who also gave the quote, and Outset is co-authored with Richa Mehta. Her solo, bylined leads are mid-size checks into applications and engineering AI ($15–30M rounds). She is credited on Periodic Labs and World Labs, but Radical did not lead the Periodic round and Jacobs incubated World Labs, so she rides along on the frontier-lab names rather than originating them (inference from bylines and team pages).\n- **What she would not do (inference):** Back a non-AI company. Lead an ownership-maximizing solo seed into a frontier lab: her own lab exposure came through rounds led by others (a16z on Periodic Labs; Jacobs on World Labs).\n- **Pattern:** She leads the seed and takes the board seat, then lets a bigger firm price the A. NEA led P-1 AI's $50M Series A (2026-07-29) with no Radical named among the A investors, while she stayed on the board. [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026072916/engineering-ai-startup-p-1-ai-announces-its-series-a-financing-led-by-nea-adding-jeff-immelt-to-the-company-s-board/)\n\n## Differential insight\nAll quotes were checked against the raw page text on 2026-11-10.\n- **She says she is on the P-1 AI board; Radical's site does not.** The P-1 Series A release says Immelt \"joins Radical Ventures partner Molly Welch\" and Eremenko on the board. Radical is credited only with the seed. [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026072916/engineering-ai-startup-p-1-ai-announces-its-series-a-financing-led-by-nea-adding-jeff-immelt-to-the-company-s-board/) (2026-07-29)\n- **Her bio was rewritten to stress research ties.** In April 2024 the Radical bio said she \"worked at Google Research in public policy and marketing roles\". By October 2025 it said she worked \"on the Google Brain team, collaborating with research leadership on commercial initiatives.\" [Wayback 2024-04-16](http://web.archive.org/web/20240416012647/https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/) [Wayback 2025-10-12](http://web.archive.org/web/20251012044858/https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/)\n- **Her credited deals changed.** The October 2025 and April 2026 bios name \"Datology, Muon Space, P-1, Solver, Spara, World Labs\". The current bio drops Datology and Solver (Solver is marked acquired) and adds Generalist, Outset and Periodic Labs. Datology is otherwise credited in this repo only to Rob Toews. [Wayback 2026-04-16](http://web.archive.org/web/20260416080407/https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/) [current](https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/)\n- **She expects neolab spin-outs:** \"I anticipate that we’ll see more schisms from the labs in 2026.\" [src](https://mewelch.substack.com/p/ai-in-2026) (2026-02-04)\n- **She thinks bottlenecks move past chips:** \"real-world dependencies will go beyond just chips in 2026\". She lists rare earths, energy and cooling, and hosted a Radical Talks episode with John Maslin of Vulcan Elements. [src](https://mewelch.substack.com/p/ai-in-2026)\n- **She treats data as the moat:** \"owning the reality anchors that ground models\" [src](https://mewelch.substack.com/p/ai-in-2026)\n- **Her one-line thesis for 2026:** \"the bottleneck is no longer raw intelligence, but rather real-world utility\" [src](https://mewelch.substack.com/p/ai-in-2026)\n- **A falsifiable 2025 call:** \"we’ll see the first company sub-100 employees hit $100m in annual revenue.\" [src](https://mewelch.substack.com/p/what-im-watching-in-ai-in-2025) (2025-01-09)\n- **She bet on AI-native systems of record early:** \"AI-native systems of record that are built around LLMs\". Spara and Outset are this thesis applied to GTM and customer research. [src](https://mewelch.substack.com/p/what-im-watching-in-ai-in-2025)\n- **In 2022 she said compute and data were the constraint:** \"the real bottlenecks for generative AI are data and compute\". The same post mentions CentML and an unnamed stealth \"Radical investment\" building synthetic-data foundation models. [src](https://mewelch.substack.com/p/whats-unsolved-in-generative-ai) (2022-12-07)\n- **Spara thesis, in her words:** \"the future of sales isn’t more automation for sellers\". [src](https://pulse2.com/spara-15-million-seed-funding-secured-for-transforming-inbound-sales/) (2025-09-16)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Her named deals are mostly AI-native automation (Spara, Outset, P-1 AI's engineering agent) and physical-world AI (Generalist, Muon Space). These are adjacent to your Serval-style automation interest but are not pre-training labs or chips. Her lab exposure (Periodic Labs' $300M seed; World Labs) came through rounds others led. She matters to you as (1) a co-investor on AI-native automation seeds of $15–25M, where a $20M check could anchor next to her, and (2) a window into Radical's research network through her podcast and Substack. The deals that matter most to you are P-1 AI, Periodic Labs and Spara.\n\n## Background\n- B.A. with distinction, Stanford; MPP, Harvard Kennedy School; MBA with distinction, Harvard Business School (years not found). [src](https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/)\n- During graduate school: AI policy at Lyft; venture investor at Playground Global (dates not found). [src](https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/)\n- Google: the Radical bio says Google Brain team; the 2024 bio said \"Google Research in public policy and marketing roles\"; her Substack says \"In a former life, I worked at Google AI\" (dates not found). [src](https://mewelch.substack.com/about)\n- Radical Ventures: working there by 2022-12-07, when her Substack post referred to \"One recent Radical investment\" and gave molly@radical.vc as her contact [src](https://mewelch.substack.com/p/whats-unsolved-in-generative-ai). The team page showed \"Investor\" (Bay Area) in April–August 2024 and \"Partner\" by 2024-11-03 (Wayback). She is now Partner, San Francisco.\n- Council on Foreign Relations Term Member. [src](https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/ | Firm bio; email molly@radical.vc per her Substack about page |\n| Substack / blog | https://mewelch.substack.com | 9 posts 2022-12 to 2026-02; an annual \"what I'm watching\" post each January/February; Radical republishes some of them (2026-11-10) |\n| X | https://x.com/molwelch | Handle given on her own Substack about page (\"on Twitter @molwelch\"); the profile was not fetched and the follower count is unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/mewelch/ | Linked from her Radical team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Radical Talks (Spotify/Apple/YouTube) | She has hosted most episodes since the September 2025 season relaunch |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-09 — Radical Talks — \"The Curse of Catastrophic Forgetting\" — host, with Rob Toews; season relaunch on continual learning — https://radical.vc/articles/radical-talks-the-curse-of-catastrophic-forgetting-and-the-quest-to-build-ai-systems-that-continuously-learn/\n- 2025-09 — Radical Talks — \"Voice AI's Enterprise Revolution\" — host, with Sanjana Basu and Ribbon CEO Arsham Ghahramani — https://radical.vc/articles/voice-ais-enterprise-revolution-how-natural-conversation-is-transforming-recruiting-and-beyond-2/\n- 2025-10 — Radical Talks — \"Reinforcement Learning's Renaissance\" — co-host with Aaron Rosenberg, guest Ross Taylor (General Reasoning; ex-Meta reasoning lead). They questioned whether coding benchmarks reflect real-world results — https://radical.vc/articles/reinforcement-learnings-renaissance-how-rl-is-reshaping-ais-future/\n- 2026-02 — Radical Talks — Jim Fan (NVIDIA) on robotics and embodied AI, recorded at the AI Founders Masterclass (search results credit Welch as host; the post itself credits \"Radical Talks\") — https://radical.vc/articles/jim-fan-on-the-future-of-robotics-and-embodied-ai/\n- Undated (search snippets only, not verified): Radical Talks episodes with Varun Sivaram (Emerald AI; co-host Rob Toews), Michael Rubenstein (Firsthand) and John Maslin (Vulcan Elements).\n\n## How they think\n- **The bottleneck is \"real-world utility\":** memory, continual learning, context, rights-cleared data and physical inputs, not raw model intelligence (Substack, 2026-02).\n- **Neolabs:** she expects more spin-outs from the big labs and asks whether they can produce breakthroughs and durable businesses. She is open-minded but has not made a bylined bet of her own here.\n- **Physical layer:** she points to rare earths, energy, cooling and real estate as constraints, and to chip diversity for inference (\"NVIDIA’s Groq acquisition is a bellweather here\").\n- **Applications:** \"functional analysts\" as the new vertical SaaS (2024-03), and AI-native systems of record (2025-01). Spara and Outset are the bets that follow from this.\n- **Calls:** the 2025 call that a company with fewer than 100 employees would reach $100M ARR. Rob Toews graded Radical's 2025 predictions, but this one was hers on Substack and its grading was not checked.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"AI in 2026\" (2026-02-04): https://mewelch.substack.com/p/ai-in-2026 (her full map: neolabs, physical bottlenecks, data moats)\n2. \"What I'm watching in AI in 2025\" (2025-01-09): https://mewelch.substack.com/p/what-im-watching-in-ai-in-2025 (AI-native systems of record; the sub-100-employee call)\n3. \"Transforming the Physical World with Artificial General Engineering Intelligence\" (2025-05-05): https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-the-physical-world-with-artificial-general-engineering-intelligence/ (her P-1 AI lead memo)\n4. \"The AI-Powered Sales Revolution\" (2025-10-13): https://radical.vc/articles/the-ai-powered-sales-revolution/ (her Spara lead memo)\n5. \"What's unsolved in generative AI?\" (2022-12-07): https://mewelch.substack.com/p/whats-unsolved-in-generative-ai (her earliest view of the data and compute bottlenecks)\n\n## Social engagement\nHer X account (@molwelch) was not fetched, so its topics, reply patterns and amplifiers are unknown. Radical republishes her Substack (\"Themes in AI to Watch in 2026\", 2026-02-09) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/themes-in-ai-to-watch-in-2026/), and her podcast co-hosts are Rob Toews, Aaron Rosenberg and Sanjana Basu.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| P-1 AI | Seed ($23M) | 2025-04 | yes (Radical led; Welch bylined; on the board) | Village Global, Schematic Ventures, Lerer Hippeau; angels Jeff Dean, Peter Welinder, Bob van Luijt, Zak Stone | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-the-physical-world-with-artificial-general-engineering-intelligence/) [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026072916/engineering-ai-startup-p-1-ai-announces-its-series-a-financing-led-by-nea-adding-jeff-immelt-to-the-company-s-board/) |\n| Spara | Seed ($15M) | 2025-09-16 | co-led with Inspired Capital | XYZ Ventures, FJ Labs, Remarkable Ventures | [src](https://pulse2.com/spara-15-million-seed-funding-secured-for-transforming-inbound-sales/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-ai-powered-sales-revolution/) |\n| Outset | Series B ($30M) | 2025-12 | yes (Radical led; post by Mehta and Welch) | M12; existing 8VC, Y Combinator, Adverb | [src](https://outset.ai/series-b) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/investing-in-outset/) |\n| Periodic Labs | Seed ($300M) | 2025-09-30 | no (a16z led) | Felicis, Accel, Khosla, DST, NVIDIA | [src](https://thelogic.co/briefing/radical-ventures-backs-periodic-labs-in-us300m-round-to-build-ai-scientists/); Welch credited on team page only |\n| Generalist | Series B ($400M at $2B) | 2026-06-04 | yes (Radical led; Toews quoted) | 8VC, USV, Norwest, Hanabi, NVIDIA, Bezos Expeditions | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2026/06/04/generalist-ai-raises-400m-2b-valuation-build-general-intelligence-real-world/); Welch on team page |\n| World Labs | Undisclosed (Radical: seed 2024, co-led $230M 2024; Jacobs incubated) | 2024 | unknown for her | a16z, NEA | [src](https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/) |\n| Muon Space | Undisclosed (Radical's first post 2022-08-15); Series B $56.7M (Activate led) 2024-08; B1 2025-06 | 2022–2025 | no | Activate, Acme, Costanoa, Congruent | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/taking-the-high-ground-our-investment-in-muon-space/) [src](https://pulse2.com/muon-space-end-to-end-space-systems-provider-closes-over-56-million-in-series-b/) |\n| DatologyAI | Seed / Series A (Radical in both) | 2024 | no | Amplify, Felicis | [Wayback 2025-10-12 team page](http://web.archive.org/web/20251012044858/https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/) |\n| Solver | Undisclosed (acquired) | 2023 | unknown | | [Wayback 2025-10-12 team page](http://web.archive.org/web/20251012044858/https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **P-1 AI:** Radical led the $23M seed (April 2025) and Welch wrote the memo. Its angels (Jeff Dean, Zak Stone) are in her Google Brain circle, though no source says they were her introduction. She took the board seat. NEA led the $50M Series A on 2026-07-29 and brought Jeff Immelt on as a director and Lila Tretikov as an observer. Radical is not named among the A investors, so either its pro-rata went unannounced or it did not follow on. That is not confirmed either way.\n- **Spara:** She co-led the $15M seed with Inspired Capital (announced 2025-09-16). A Techleap summary claims an earlier Inspired-led SAFE (2024-02) and a Radical-led seed in 2024-07. If true, Radical committed more than a year before the announcement. That timeline is unverified and conflicts with the launch press. [src](https://finder.techleap.nl/news/feed/spara-raises-15m-to-enhance-ai-sales)\n- **Outset:** Radical entered at the Series B, less than six months after the $17M A, when revenue had grown more than 8x. The deal pairs her application thesis with Mehta's growth seat. Radical was not in earlier rounds.\n- **Muon Space:** Radical's first Muon post is from 2022-08-15, which may be before or near when she joined. Her role in that entry is unknown.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Partner, San Francisco | Partner title by 2024-11; at the firm by 2022-12 | [src](https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/) |\n| P-1 AI | Board director | 2025 | [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026072916/engineering-ai-startup-p-1-ai-announces-its-series-a-financing-led-by-nea-adding-jeff-immelt-to-the-company-s-board/) |\n| Paul Eremenko | Her portfolio CEO (P-1 AI); fellow board member | 2025 | same |\n| Jeff Immelt (NEA) | Fellow P-1 AI board member | 2026-07 | same |\n| Rob Toews | Podcast co-host; shared credit on Generalist | 2025-09 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-talks-the-curse-of-catastrophic-forgetting-and-the-quest-to-build-ai-systems-that-continuously-learn/) |\n| Richa Mehta | Co-author on the Outset Series B lead | 2026-01 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/investing-in-outset/) |\n| Aaron Rosenberg | Podcast co-host (RL episode) | 2025-10 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/reinforcement-learnings-renaissance-how-rl-is-reshaping-ais-future/) |\n| Sanjana Basu | Podcast guest (voice AI episode) | 2025-09 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/voice-ais-enterprise-revolution-how-natural-conversation-is-transforming-recruiting-and-beyond-2/) |\n| Inspired Capital | Co-lead on Spara's seed | 2025-09 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-ai-powered-sales-revolution/) |\n| Google Brain | Former employer | dates not found | [src](https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/) |\n| Playground Global | Former venture investor | during graduate school | same |\n| Lyft | AI policy role | during graduate school | same |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact join date at Radical and her title on joining. LinkedIn was not fetched, and the Wayback team-page history starts only in 2024-04.\n- Did Radical take pro-rata in P-1 AI's NEA-led Series A?\n- Why were Datology and Solver dropped from her bio? Was it a credit dispute, an exit, or just tidying?\n- Her personal role in the World Labs and Periodic Labs entries (sourcing, diligence or allocation).\n- Any neolab or pre-training seed she originates herself, given her \"schisms from the labs\" call.\n- The full text of her continual-learning essay. Its slug was not found, and both URL guesses returned 404.\n- Her X activity. The search budget ran out before X could be checked.\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/ (current); Wayback snapshots 2024-04-16, 2024-07-21, 2024-08-05, 2024-11-03, 2025-01-15, 2025-03-26, 2025-10-12, 2026-04-16\n- https://mewelch.substack.com/about ; /p/ai-in-2026 ; /p/what-im-watching-in-ai-in-2025 ; /p/whats-unsolved-in-generative-ai ; /archive\n- https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-the-physical-world-with-artificial-general-engineering-intelligence/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/the-ai-powered-sales-revolution/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/investing-in-outset/ ; https://outset.ai/series-b\n- https://radical.vc/articles/themes-in-ai-to-watch-in-2026/\n- Radical Talks posts: catastrophic forgetting, voice AI, RL renaissance, Jim Fan (URLs above)\n- https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026072916/engineering-ai-startup-p-1-ai-announces-its-series-a-financing-led-by-nea-adding-jeff-immelt-to-the-company-s-board/\n- https://www.mondaq.com/pressrelease/203968/goodwin-advises-p-1-ai-on-its-%2450-million-series-a-financing\n- https://pulse2.com/spara-15-million-seed-funding-secured-for-transforming-inbound-sales/\n- https://thelogic.co/briefing/radical-ventures-backs-periodic-labs-in-us300m-round-to-build-ai-scientists/\n- https://siliconangle.com/2026/06/04/generalist-ai-raises-400m-2b-valuation-build-general-intelligence-real-world/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/taking-the-high-ground-our-investment-in-muon-space/ ; https://pulse2.com/muon-space-end-to-end-space-systems-provider-closes-over-56-million-in-series-b/\n- Search budget was exhausted on 2026-11-10, so podcast listings for the Sivaram, Rubenstein and Maslin episodes come from search snippets only.\n", "person:morgan-beller": "\n# Morgan Beller\n\n## One-line take\nShe led NFX's seed in Starcloud (orbital AI data centres, $40M to $2.3B in 20 months) and its first EvenUp cheque, and in 2026 left to start an AI-infrastructure, energy and deep-tech seed firm with Ashton Kutcher.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** she commits fast to odd, capital-heavy ideas. Starcloud's seed closed in days against 200+ VCs and she took the board seat ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/11/200-vcs-wanted-to-get-into-lumen-orbits-11m-seed-round/)).\n- **How she thinks about AI:** sell the outcome, not the model. \"The customers don’t care that you’re using AI.\" ([src](https://nfx.com/post/why-nfx-invested-in-evenup)).\n- **Constraint:** her NFX track record now sits in funds she no longer runs, and NFX itself is closing to outside capital. The new firm has no public name, size or first deal yet, and Kutcher's celebrity brand is new for her.\n\n## Differential insight\n- EvenUp was her first NFX deal. NFX met the founders in July 2020, two months before she was announced as GP. Quote from her memo: \"They were the perfect team to change the personal injury space for the better.\" ([src](https://nfx.com/post/why-nfx-invested-in-evenup)). Mirrored in insights.\n- TechCrunch says she \"until recently was a general partner\" at NFX ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/01/ashton-kutcher-leaving-sound-ventures-to-launch-new-vc-firm-with-morgan-beller/)); NFX SPV Form Ds filed in Feb-Mar 2026 still list her as an executive officer ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2109603/000210960326000001/primary_doc.xml)), so the exit falls between March and June 2026.\n- Guy Oseary and Sound GP Effie Epstein will advise the new firm; the WSJ says the split with Sound was over stage, with Kutcher wanting earlier ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/01/ashton-kutcher-leaving-sound-ventures-to-launch-new-vc-firm-with-morgan-beller/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. AI infrastructure and energy at seed is my lane, and a first-time fund from a proven seed lead is where a co-investor can get allocation. Deals that matter: Starcloud (AI compute in orbit), EvenUp (AI-native automation).\n\n## Background\n- a16z partner for nearly three years (dates not stated in sources read).\n- Meta: co-creator of Libra, then head of strategy at Novi.\n- NFX: fourth General Partner, announced 2020-09 ([src](https://nfx.com/post/announcing-new-general-partner-morgan-beller)); led crypto for Fund III (2021).\n- Left NFX by mid-2026; co-founding a firm with Ashton Kutcher.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | not found | new firm has no public site as of 2026-11-11 |\n| Substack / blog | https://nfx.com/library | NFX essays under her byline (may be removed) |\n| X | https://x.com/beller | handle per NFX memo byline; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing mined. Space Ambition ran a written \"Spotlight: Morgan Beller\" ([link](https://spaceambition.substack.com/p/spotlight-morgan-beller-general-partner)), not read.\n\n## How they think\n- Vertical AI should hide the AI: \"EvenUp understands that the AI is not the selling point.\"\n- Data head starts compound into network effects (EvenUp: 300+ firms, 1,000+ demands a month at the Series B).\n- Comfortable with physical, capital-heavy seed bets (Starcloud) alongside crypto and software.\n- Calls right so far: EvenUp (seed to $325M by 2023 and beyond), Starcloud ($40M seed to $2.3B Series A extension, 2026-08).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Why NFX Invested in EvenUp](https://nfx.com/post/why-nfx-invested-in-evenup) (2023-06): her clearest statement on vertical AI.\n- \"The Stack Above the Cloud\" (NFX, 2026-02): listed in the NFX library, not read.\n- [TechCrunch on the Kutcher firm](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/01/ashton-kutcher-leaving-sound-ventures-to-launch-new-vc-firm-with-morgan-beller/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| EvenUp | Seed (then A 2021, B 2023) | 2020-08 | No (seed lead not stated) | SignalFire; later Bessemer, Bain Capital Ventures | [src](https://nfx.com/post/why-nfx-invested-in-evenup) |\n| Starcloud (Lumen Orbit) | Seed $11M at $40M | 2024-12-11 | Yes, board | Fuse.VC, Soma Capital, a16z and Sequoia scout funds | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/11/200-vcs-wanted-to-get-into-lumen-orbits-11m-seed-round/) |\n\nHer crypto deals at NFX (Fund III) were not researched.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Starcloud:** YC S24; TechCrunch reported in October 2024 that the round was competitive, then confirmed $11M at $40M in December. The CEO said 200+ VCs reached out and the company opened a further SAFE at a higher price. NFX led and Beller joined the board. NFX followed in the 2025 extension and the 2026 Series A extension.\n- **EvenUp:** met July 2020; invested at seed; she wrote the public memo three years later at the $50.5M Series B.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NFX | General Partner | 2020-09 to 2026 | [src](https://nfx.com/post/announcing-new-general-partner-morgan-beller) |\n| Ashton Kutcher | Co-founder of new firm | 2026 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/01/ashton-kutcher-leaving-sound-ventures-to-launch-new-vc-firm-with-morgan-beller/) |\n| Guy Oseary, Effie Epstein (Sound Ventures) | Advisers to the new firm | 2026 | same |\n| Starcloud (Philip Johnston) | Board director | 2024-12 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2051381/000108036825000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Andreessen Horowitz | Former partner | n/a | TechCrunch |\n\n## Open questions\n- Name, size and LPs of the Kutcher-Beller firm (one blog says \"Decimal Capital\", ~$500M, citing WSJ; unconfirmed).\n- Does she keep the Starcloud board seat, or does it stay with NFX?\n- Her exact NFX exit date and whether it relates to NFX's decision to stop raising outside funds.\n\n## Sources\n- https://nfx.com/post/why-nfx-invested-in-evenup\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/11/200-vcs-wanted-to-get-into-lumen-orbits-11m-seed-round/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/01/ashton-kutcher-leaving-sound-ventures-to-launch-new-vc-firm-with-morgan-beller/\n- https://nfx.com/post/announcing-new-general-partner-morgan-beller\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2109603/000210960326000001/primary_doc.xml\n", "person:mounisha-anumolu": "\n# Mounisha Anumolu\n\n## One-line take\nA New York GV associate with a byline on a GV-led $27M Series A (Translucent, 2026-03-11). That deal is AI-native automation for hospital finance, not frontier AI. She is worth a watch-list slot, not a meeting yet.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she could win:** GV puts associates' names on lead-deal announcements. She co-wrote the Translucent post with GP Crystal Huang [src](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare). That suggests she worked on the deal, not just sat near it, and it ties her to Huang, the most active early-stage AI-apps GP at GV (see `firms/gv.md`).\n- **What constrains her:** she is an associate, so GV's checks are signed by GPs. Her one credited deal is vertical AI software (healthcare finance), far from labs, pre-training or chips. Her mandate is \"AI bets across the stack and across stages\" [src](https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu), which is broad and doesn't point at a thesis.\n- **What she would never do:** nothing found. She has no public writing, podcasts or X activity that states a view.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Translucent thesis came from operator interviews, not from a model or infrastructure view.** GV says its conviction came from \"dozens of interviews with clinical and finance operators\", who confirmed that healthcare finance is underserved. That is a workflow-diligence style, the opposite of a frontier-talent bet [src](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare) (2026-03-11).\n- **The round was an inside-plus-GV deal.** Translucent says NEA, Virtue and FPV Ventures all came back, and writes \"The preemptive, oversubscribed round comes just months after\" the seed [src](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv). Crystal Huang joined GV from NEA in 2021 [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/huang-and-zeb-to-hang-at-gv/?format=md). **This is my inference, not a sourced intro path:** an NEA-to-Huang connection is a plausible route into the deal. Treat it as an open question.\n- **Only one public credit, and it's a byline, not a board seat.** Her GV profile lists one investment, Translucent AI [src](https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu). GV's other 2026 AI think-pieces I checked are by KJ Sidberry and David Benjamin, not her [src](https://www.gv.com/news/long-live-the-wrapper), [src](https://www.gv.com/news/the-age-of-participatory-software), [src](https://www.gv.com/news/when-agents-become-the-customer-part-1).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Translucent describes itself as an agentic AI platform for healthcare finance [src](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv). That's AI-native automation, the Serval-like category the owner likes, but it's vertical SaaS, not frontier AI. She has no sourced link to labs, pre-training talent or chips. What she is useful for: a junior window into Crystal Huang's AI-apps pipeline at GV, and into New York deal flow. If she shows up on a second AI-native automation deal, raise her lens_fit to high.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** Dartmouth College; studied Economics, Computer Science and Public Policy [src](https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu). Graduation year not found.\n- **Brookings Institution:** research intern on emerging fintech initiatives [src](https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu). Dates not found.\n- **Goldman Sachs:** investment banking analyst in the TMT division, working on enterprise software M&A [src](https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu). Dates not found.\n- **GV:** associate on the investing team, New York [src](https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu). Start date not found. LinkedIn would have it; not confirmed.\n- Her bio also says she is from Connecticut and cares about the NYC startup ecosystem [src](https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu | GV team page (read 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | GV news byline: Translucent (2026-03-11) |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/mounisha-anumolu | Linked from her GV page; not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Diligence by operator interviews:** the Translucent post rests on talks with hospital clinical and finance operators, and on payer and budget pressure (it cites CMS and KFF data on health spending and the 2025 budget reconciliation bill) [src](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare).\n- **Values founder-domain fit:** the post stresses that founder Jack O'Hara has run technology at large provider groups (ChenMed CIO/CPTO, Springfield Clinic CIO, Mount Sinai regional CIO) [src](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare).\n- **Contrarian views, and calls right or wrong:** nothing found. One deal is too little to judge.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Translucent AI: Healthcare's Financial OS](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare): GV's lead memo, co-written with Crystal Huang (2026-03-11). It's the only public piece of her thinking.\n2. [Her GV profile](https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu): her mandate and background.\n3. [Translucent's Series A announcement](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv): the round terms and investors.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. She has no public X account, and I didn't read LinkedIn.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Translucent (Translucent AI) | Series A, $27M | 2026-03-11 | GV led. Her role: co-author of GV's announcement | NEA, Virtue, FPV Ventures (all existing investors) | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare), [src](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv) |\n\nNo other deals found with her name on them.\n\n## Deal stories\n**Translucent Series A ($27M, GV-led, announced 2026-03-11)**\n- **The company:** Translucent builds a financial operating platform for healthcare providers. AI agents track P&L, labor costs and service-line profitability, generate reports and flag staffing and vendor-contract opportunities [src](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare). Customers named include Northwestern Medicine, Duly Health and Care, Wray Hospital and Clinic and Springfield Clinic [src](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv).\n- **Founder:** Jack O'Hara, founder and CEO, previously at ChenMed [src](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv).\n- **Before GV:** a $7M seed announced in August 2025 [src](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv). Who led the seed was not confirmed. NEA, Virtue and FPV Ventures are named as existing investors.\n- **How GV found it:** nothing in either source. GV says its conviction came from dozens of operator interviews [src](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare). Huang's NEA background is a plausible route, but it's unconfirmed (see Differential insight).\n- **Timing:** the Translucent page shows two dates, 2026-02-13 at the top and a 2026-03-11 dateline. GV's post is dated 2026-03-11 [src](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv). That's about 7 months from the seed to a preemptive A.\n- **Who was there:** Crystal Huang is quoted in the company release. Anumolu isn't named in the company release, only on GV's byline [src](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv).\n- **What it's worth now:** no valuation was disclosed, and no later round was found as of 2026-11-10.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| GV | Employer (associate, New York) | start not found | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu) |\n| Crystal Huang (GV GP) | Co-author on the Translucent lead post | 2026-03 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare) |\n| Jack O'Hara (Translucent CEO) | Portfolio founder (GV-led A) | 2026-03 | [src](https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv) |\n| Goldman Sachs | Former employer (TMT IB analyst) | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu) |\n| Brookings Institution | Former research intern (fintech) | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu) |\n| Dartmouth College | Alma mater | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did she join GV, and has she been promoted? Check LinkedIn.\n- Did she source Translucent, or was it Huang's deal (possibly via NEA)? Who led the $7M seed?\n- Is she on any New York AI-native automation deals that GV hasn't announced?\n- Does she write or post anywhere (Dartmouth or NYC tech events)?\n- A broader name search (talks, Dartmouth or NYC events, other deals) hasn't been done yet.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare (2026-03-11)\n- https://www.translucent.ai/blog/translucent-ai-raises-27m-series-a-led-by-gv (2026-03-11 dateline)\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/huang-and-zeb-to-hang-at-gv/?format=md (Huang from NEA, via firms/gv.md)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/long-live-the-wrapper ; https://www.gv.com/news/the-age-of-participatory-software ; https://www.gv.com/news/when-agents-become-the-customer-part-1 (author checks)\n", "person:murali-joshi": "\n# Murali Joshi\n\n## One-line take\nJoshi is a young ICONIQ Growth GP who sits on the Anthropic deal team for all three rounds ICONIQ led or co-led (Series F, G, H). His own AI book (Statsig, DX, Tennr, Outtake) is built by courting founder-led, often capital-light companies for years before ICONIQ writes the check.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He plays a long, relationship-heavy game with founders who don't need money. On DX, ICONIQ describes \"years of flights to Salt Lake City\" and customer introductions before investing in a company that had built without outside capital ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/committing-to-the-future-of-engineering-intelligence-our-investment-in-dx), 2025-03-06). On Statsig, the team joined stand-ups over \"countless visits to their office\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-product-development-why-were-backing-statsig-2), 2025-05-06). Both exited within months to AI-era acquirers: Statsig to OpenAI, DX to Atlassian ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/dx-joins-atlassian-powering-developer-intelligence-for-the-ai-era), 2025-09-18).\n- **What constrains him.** He is a growth investor inside a multi-partner machine. He is never the lone name on the Anthropic posts, which list Divesh Makan, Matt Jacobson and Will Griffith first ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-anthropic)). Matt Jacobson co-signs every Joshi deal post found. ICONIQ met Anthropic in early 2023 but entered only at the $13B Series F in Sep 2025, so this is late-stage capital, not first-in.\n- **What he would never do (inferred).** Back a pre-product seed lab. Every named deal is Series B or later, or a first institutional round into an already-scaled bootstrapped company (DX).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **ICONIQ courted Anthropic for about 2.5 years before entering.** \"Since we first met Anthropic in early 2023\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-anthropic), 2025-09-02). The Series F followed in Sep 2025, and ICONIQ led the $13B round at a $183B post ([Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-series-f-at-usd183b-post-money-valuation)).\n- **The Anthropic Series H \"co-lead\" is real but crowded.** Anthropic's own release lists ICONIQ as one of six co-leads behind four leads (Altimeter, Dragoneer, Greenoaks, Sequoia) on the $65B round at $965B ([src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h), 2026-05-28). ICONIQ's post frames it as co-leading \"for the third consecutive round\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/anthropic-series-h)). Anthropic filed its S-1 on 2026-10-26 ([src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/s1-sec)), so this position is now near liquidity.\n- **DX was a bootstrapped, first-institutional-money deal.** ICONIQ praised DX for \"not taking outside investor capital\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/committing-to-the-future-of-engineering-intelligence-our-investment-in-dx)). ICONIQ first met the team in 2023 at \"a couple dozen employees\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/dx-joins-atlassian-powering-developer-intelligence-for-the-ai-era)), invested in Mar 2025, and called itself \"DX's largest institutional investor\" when Atlassian agreed to buy DX six months later.\n- **Customer intros are the sourcing wedge.** For DX, ICONIQ points to \"many successful customer introductions\" to Global 2000 enterprises and its own portfolio before it invested ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/committing-to-the-future-of-engineering-intelligence-our-investment-in-dx)).\n- **Statsig: lead and named partner.** Statsig's own post says ICONIQ Growth led the $100M Series C at $1.1B, with Joshi leading for ICONIQ and Sequoia and Madrona participating ([src](https://www.statsig.com/blog/statsig-series-c), 2025-05-06). OpenAI later acquired Statsig and made founder Vijaye Raji CTO of Applications. Raji then spoke at ICONIQ Frontier in Oct 2026 ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/videos/openais-vijaye-raji-on-the-architecture-behind-a-1b-ads-business)), which shows the relationship outlived the exit.\n- **Outtake angel syndicate.** ICONIQ led the $40M Series B, and Joshi joined the board. CRV and S32 participated, along with angels Satya Nadella, Nikesh Arora, Bill Ackman, Shyam Sankar, Trae Stephens and Bob McGrew ([src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/outtake-raises-40m-series-b-163000387.html), 2026-01-28).\n- **Pre-GP track record (Forbes 2023).** \"He personally sourced and led $1.04 billion across 27 investments in 10 companies\" ([src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/murali-joshi/)). Harker's alumni news adds Datadog and Procore among his 17 companies ([src](https://news.harker.org/two-alumni-named-to-forbes-30-under-30/), 2023-05-24).\n- **Podcasts, X, talks.** Nothing found in his own voice beyond firm posts and press quotes. He hosted ICONIQ's \"Uncommon Path\" interview with Tennr CEO Trey Holterman (2026-07-14), but no transcript is published ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/videos/the-uncommon-path-trey-holterman-on-building-ai-to-fix-patient-referrals-2)). Note: this turn's web-search budget was exhausted, so podcasts and X were checked only by direct fetches of known URLs.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** On the thesis alone this is a growth investor, which is normally \"low\". He scores higher on two counts:\n1. **The owner's segment.** He was promoted fast (principal in 2023, GP by early 2025 bylines) at a fund that writes growth checks.\n2. **Downstream capital for the owner's companies.** He is on the Anthropic deal team at a firm that led or co-led three straight frontier-lab rounds. That makes him a likely Series B/C partner for AI-native automation companies the owner seeds (Tennr and Outtake are both AI-native automation).\n\nHe is not a co-investor at seed. Deals that matter to the owner: Anthropic F/G/H, Statsig C (OpenAI exit), Outtake B, Tennr C.\n\n## Background\n- Harker School, class of 2012 ([src](https://news.harker.org/two-alumni-named-to-forbes-30-under-30/)).\n- Bachelor's degree from the University of Southern California ([Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/murali-joshi/)).\n- First in his family to attend college in the US ([Harker](https://news.harker.org/two-alumni-named-to-forbes-30-under-30/)).\n- Principal at ICONIQ Growth, Forbes 30 Under 30 VC (2023) ([Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/murali-joshi/)).\n- General Partner on ICONIQ bylines from the 2025-03-06 DX post onward ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/committing-to-the-future-of-engineering-intelligence-our-investment-in-dx)).\n- Join date (around 2018 per an aggregator) and any pre-ICONIQ employer were not confirmed from a primary source.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found (ICONIQ bio URL 404s) | |\n| Substack / blog | ICONIQ Growth insights bylines | co-author on about 6 deal posts, 2025-2026 |\n| X | nothing found | not verified |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | not verified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | ICONIQ \"The Uncommon Path\" video series | hosted Tennr episode, 2026-07-14 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-07 — ICONIQ, The Uncommon Path — \"Trey Holterman on Tennr and eliminating patient care delays\" — [link](https://www.iconiq.com/videos/the-uncommon-path-trey-holterman-on-building-ai-to-fix-patient-referrals-2) — Joshi interviews; the page has only a summary (\"problem obsession\", \"frontier mindset\"), no transcript.\n- No external podcasts found. Search budget was exhausted; see Open questions.\n\n## How they think\n- **Underwriting thesis.** Two ideas recur in his posts: platforms that become infrastructure for engineering teams (Statsig, DX), and trust and security in an agentic internet (Outtake).\n- **Anthropic.** The underwriting stated at the Series H was frontier-model quality ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/anthropic-series-h)). The Series G post emphasized trust: \"transparent, trustworthy, and transformational\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/deepening-our-partnership-with-anthropic-the-claude-moment-for-ai)).\n- **Calls he got right.** DX: invested Mar 2025, Atlassian deal Sep 2025. Statsig: Series C May 2025, OpenAI acquisition later in 2025. Anthropic: entered at $183B, last private mark $965B, S-1 filed Oct 2026.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Committing to the Future of Engineering Intelligence: DX](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/committing-to-the-future-of-engineering-intelligence-our-investment-in-dx): the clearest view of his multi-year courting playbook.\n- [Why We're Backing Statsig](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-product-development-why-were-backing-statsig-2): office visits and stand-ups as diligence.\n- [Partnering with Anthropic](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-anthropic): ICONIQ's 2023-to-2025 path into the lab.\n- [Our Partnership with Outtake](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/building-a-digital-trust-platform-for-an-ai-driven-internet-our-partnership-with-outtake): the agentic-security thesis.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No verified X or LinkedIn activity was reviewed.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Anthropic | Series F ($13B, $183B post) | 2025-09-02 | yes (ICONIQ lead) | Fidelity, Lightspeed (co-leads) | [Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-series-f-at-usd183b-post-money-valuation), [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-anthropic) |\n| Anthropic | Series G ($30B, $380B post) | 2026-02-12 | co-lead | GIC, Coatue (leads); D. E. Shaw, Dragoneer, Founders Fund, MGX (co-leads) | [Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-30-billion-series-g-funding-380-billion-post-money-valuation) |\n| Anthropic | Series H ($65B, $965B post) | 2026-05-28 | co-lead | Altimeter, Dragoneer, Greenoaks, Sequoia (leads); Capital Group, Coatue, D1, GIC, XN (co-leads) | [Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h) |\n| Statsig | Series C ($100M, $1.1B) | 2025-05-06 | yes (Joshi led) | Sequoia, Madrona | [Statsig](https://www.statsig.com/blog/statsig-series-c) |\n| DX | first ICONIQ investment (round not disclosed) | 2025-03-06 | not stated | none (bootstrapped before) | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/committing-to-the-future-of-engineering-intelligence-our-investment-in-dx) |\n| Tennr | Series C | 2025-06-18 | not stated | not disclosed | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/revolutionizing-patient-referrals-with-ai-our-partnership-with-tennr) |\n| Outtake | Series B ($40M) | 2026-01-28 | yes; Joshi joins board | CRV, S32, plus angels | [Yahoo/press](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/outtake-raises-40m-series-b-163000387.html) |\n| Datadog, Procore | pre-2023 involvement (round not specified) | before 2023-05 | n/a | n/a | [Harker](https://news.harker.org/two-alumni-named-to-forbes-30-under-30/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Anthropic.**\n  - First meeting in early 2023 ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-anthropic)).\n  - Entered as lead of the $13B Series F in Sep 2025 ([src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-series-f-at-usd183b-post-money-valuation)).\n  - Co-led the G and H rounds. On public posts the deal team is Makan, Jacobson, Griffith, Joshi, Liang and Fahn, plus Agarwal on F.\n  - Timing: ICONIQ was far from first. Earlier institutional backers (Spark, Menlo, Google, Amazon, Lightspeed) are not sourced in this file; see Open questions.\n- **DX.**\n  - Met in 2023 ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/dx-joins-atlassian-powering-developer-intelligence-for-the-ai-era)).\n  - Years of flights to Salt Lake City and customer intros came before the March 2025 investment.\n  - ICONIQ was the first and largest institutional investor.\n  - Atlassian agreed to buy DX on 2025-09-18 ([Atlassian](https://www.atlassian.com/blog/announcements/atlassian-acquires-dx)). Price not disclosed in the sources read.\n- **Statsig.**\n  - ICONIQ says it watched the team \"throughout the years\" through office visits before leading the C ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-product-development-why-were-backing-statsig-2)).\n  - Sequoia and Madrona were earlier backers who participated ([src](https://www.statsig.com/blog/statsig-series-c)).\n- **Outtake.**\n  - ICONIQ led the Series B, and Joshi took a board seat. How they met is not stated.\n  - The founder (Alex Dhillon, ex-Palantir) later featured in ICONIQ's founder series ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/videos/the-uncommon-path-alex-dhillon-on-building-ai-to-defend-digital-trust)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ICONIQ Growth | Principal (2023) -> General Partner | by 2023 | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/murali-joshi/) |\n| Matt Jacobson | Co-author on every Joshi deal post (Statsig, DX, Tennr, Anthropic) | 2025 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/committing-to-the-future-of-engineering-intelligence-our-investment-in-dx) |\n| Austin Liang | Deal-team junior on DX and Anthropic | 2025 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/anthropic-series-h) |\n| Ryan Koh | Deal-team junior on Statsig and Outtake | 2025 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-product-development-why-were-backing-statsig-2) |\n| Divesh Makan | ICONIQ founder; senior name on Anthropic posts | 2025 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-anthropic) |\n| Amit Agarwal | GP co-signer on Anthropic F and Outtake | 2025 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/building-a-digital-trust-platform-for-an-ai-driven-internet-our-partnership-with-outtake) |\n| Caroline Xie | GP co-signer on Tennr | 2025 | [ICONIQ](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/revolutionizing-patient-referrals-with-ai-our-partnership-with-tennr) |\n| Vijaye Raji | Statsig founder (now OpenAI CTO of Applications); spoke at ICONIQ Frontier | 2025 | [Statsig](https://www.statsig.com/blog/statsig-series-c) |\n| Alex Dhillon | Outtake founder; Joshi on board | 2026 | [press](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/outtake-raises-40m-series-b-163000387.html) |\n| Trey Holterman | Tennr CEO; Joshi interviewed him | 2025 | [ICONIQ video](https://www.iconiq.com/videos/the-uncommon-path-trey-holterman-on-building-ai-to-fix-patient-referrals-2) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact promotion dates. ICONIQ bylines may show current titles retroactively, so the GP date could be later than Mar 2025. Confirm via LinkedIn or an ICONIQ promotion announcement.\n- Join year (around 2018 per an aggregator) and any pre-ICONIQ job.\n- Tennr Series C size and lead. The firm post does not say whether ICONIQ led.\n- His personal role on Anthropic. Is he the day-to-day owner or one of six names?\n- Board seats an aggregator lists (1Password, Drata, Monte Carlo, Orca, Statsig) are unverified.\n- Any podcast or X presence. Re-run search when budget allows.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-anthropic\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/deepening-our-partnership-with-anthropic-the-claude-moment-for-ai\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/anthropic-series-h\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-series-f-at-usd183b-post-money-valuation\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-30-billion-series-g-funding-380-billion-post-money-valuation\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/s1-sec\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-product-development-why-were-backing-statsig-2\n- https://www.statsig.com/blog/statsig-series-c\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/committing-to-the-future-of-engineering-intelligence-our-investment-in-dx\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/dx-joins-atlassian-powering-developer-intelligence-for-the-ai-era\n- https://www.atlassian.com/blog/announcements/atlassian-acquires-dx\n- https://getdx.com/news/iconiq-investment-in-dx/\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/revolutionizing-patient-referrals-with-ai-our-partnership-with-tennr\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/building-a-digital-trust-platform-for-an-ai-driven-internet-our-partnership-with-outtake\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/news/outtake-raises-40m-series-b-163000387.html\n- https://www.iconiq.com/videos/the-uncommon-path-trey-holterman-on-building-ai-to-fix-patient-referrals-2\n- https://www.iconiq.com/videos/the-uncommon-path-alex-dhillon-on-building-ai-to-defend-digital-trust\n- https://www.iconiq.com/videos/openais-vijaye-raji-on-the-architecture-behind-a-1b-ads-business\n- https://www.forbes.com/profile/murali-joshi/\n- https://news.harker.org/two-alumni-named-to-forbes-30-under-30/\n", "person:nabeel-hyatt": "\n# Nabeel Hyatt\n\n## One-line take\nFor about 14 years he was Spark Capital's product-first board partner (Cruise, Discord, Adept, Granola, Wordware, The Bot Company). He now says on his own site that he \"now angel invest[s]\" [src](https://www.nabeelhyatt.com/). He is worth following for his read on AI app founders and his ties to Kyle Vogt and Fraser Kelton, not as a source of $10M+ checks any more.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He judges the product, not the market. In 2016 he said Spark is \"product driven\" while most VCs are market-first and need a market an associate can size. That let Spark lean harder into Oculus, Tumblr and Cruise when rivals hesitated [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt). He builds long relationships before investing. He spent about a year modelling Cruise's first product with Kyle Vogt before passing, then led the A after the pivot. He knew Jason Citron seven years and passed on Discord twice. He met Chris Pedregal (Granola) in 2012 [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt).\n- **What constrains him:** He was one of six investors running a $700M fund (\"kind of broken\"). He did about one good deal a year, 37 Spark investments in over a decade, and won't compete when a rival triples the price [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt) [src](https://www.nabeelhyatt.com/investments). He has now left the GP seat. He was dropped from the Fund IX Form D, Spark's Feb 2026 deck lists him as \"Venture Partner\", and his site says he \"was General Partner\" [src](https://www.nabeelhyatt.com/).\n- **What he'd never do:** Invest without meeting founders in person, pre-empt a round to buy cheap ownership, use SPVs, or fund a $25M round he thinks will harm the company [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt) [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/bb13b138).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He has stepped back to angel investing.** His site reads \"I've invested in early-stage founders for fourteen years and now angel invest.\" It describes Spark in the past tense and lists him on \"a number of startup boards\" [src](https://www.nabeelhyatt.com/).\n- **How Spark sized checks:** \"usually it's not about valuation usually it's about check size\". The ladder was $5M/$10M/$15M/$20M at a fixed ownership target. In a 2025 Series A Spark offered \"10 on 60\" against a founder's ask of $100M [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt) (39:48, 36:19).\n- **Losing to deployment-optimisers:** \"lost two deals in 24 months where literally they trebled the price\". The winning funds also bought common stock [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt) (12:53).\n- **COVID admission:** \"our quality of investing went down on the early stage team\". He wrote about two checks in 18 months [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt) (19:29).\n- **Cruise origin:** after he passed on the Audi highway kit, Vogt \"disappeared for nine months and wouldn't return my emails\". Vogt came back with the L4 pivot and demoed it to Nabeel and about five other investors [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt) (36:19). In 2016 he added that he had built go-to-market spreadsheets for the earlier product, \"I started working on Excel spreadsheets with them\" [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt) (17:41, own transcription).\n- **Wordware:** \"I met with those guys six seven times before we invested\", in one week, against higher term sheets [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt).\n- **Oculus mistake:** his first reaction to Santo Politi's lead was \"My first reaction was like, cynical, right?\" [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt) (11:20).\n- **Under-weight in the last AI wave:** \"as a firm, we didn't go that deep into it\". That covered the deep-learning era, apart from Cruise [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/1ca2d86f) (00:19:01).\n- **Spark partnership mechanics:** \"there's seven early stage partners, flat. There's not a lot of credit.\" [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/06872b41). Also \"We don't do SPVs.\" [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/bb13b138).\n- **Personal filter:** a company must be \"something I would be willing to join if I wasn't a VC\" [src](https://medium.com/@nabeel/fig-fixing-how-games-get-made-669880751b68).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is the right person for AI-native apps and agentic tools (Adept, Granola, Wordware, Conductor, Sauna, Producer.AI) and for Kyle Vogt's robotics (Cruise, The Bot Company). He was on the Anthropic side of Spark (\"we were early investor in anthropic\") [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt). But he now calls himself an angel, so he is not a co-lead for $20M seeds. He is not a pre-training or chips investor, and he says model-layer questions don't change how he judges an AI company [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/3f097087). Main value: introductions to Vogt, Kelton (his de facto successor on Spark's AI-app seat) and the Granola/Descript founder circle.\n\n## Background\n- Started his first tech company at 16 and was in an IPO before 25. He was involved in five startups, including co-founder/product/CEO roles at Ambient Devices (an MIT Media Lab spinout) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-members/nabeel-hyatt) [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt).\n- Studied design at the Maryland Institute College of Art [src](https://www.sparkcapital.com/archive/team-profiles/nabeel-hyatt).\n- Co-founder and CEO of Conduit Labs, sold to Zynga in 2010. GM of Zynga Boston through the IPO [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt).\n- Feb 2012: joined Spark as its first venture partner to open San Francisco [src](https://bijansabet.com/2012/02/10/a-new-member-of-the-spark-team/) [src](https://gamesbeat.com/as-social-games-boom-spark-capital-hires-former-zynga-exec-as-first-venture-partner/). Bijan Sabet, a friend of about 10 years, recruited him [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt). Later General Partner, Early [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-members/nabeel-hyatt).\n- 2026: Venture Partner in the Spark deck. Self-described angel, living in Berkeley and opening Tabletop Library, a board-game space [src](https://www.nabeelhyatt.com/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.nabeelhyatt.com/ | Bio, board list, writing/talks archive (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://nabeel.medium.com/ | Medium 2013-2023; older Tumblr nabeel.tumblr.com (2013-14) |\n| X | https://x.com/nabeel | Long threads on OKRs, layoffs, AI board-game design |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/nabeelhyatt | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://www.hallwaychat.com | Hallway Chat: with Bijan Sabet 2015-2021 (#1-#32), with Fraser Kelton Nov 2023-Mar 2026 (30 eps, transcripts on Transistor) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2016-06 — 20VC — Investing in Oculus and Cruise — https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt — product-first vs market-first firms; the Cruise courtship; Oculus cynicism.\n- 2016-2021 — Hallway Chat #23-#32 with Bijan Sabet (Fred Wilson, Kevin Thau, Brian Watson guests) — https://share.transistor.fm/s/b41782a4 — #30 (2019) on whether VC pattern recognition is worth anything; no transcripts, not mined.\n- 2019 — Venture Stories: Marketplaces with Sarah Tavel — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4HP8embNPE0 — not mined (YouTube blocked).\n- 2019 — LDV Vision Summit — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sVMpuOsE2-I — not mined.\n- 2023-11-10 — Hallway Chat relaunch — https://share.transistor.fm/s/1ca2d86f — Spark under-invested in the last AI wave; rejects \"big tech wins\".\n- 2023-12-05 — Unsolicited Feedback (Reforge) — https://www.reforge.com/podcast/unsolicited-feedback/episode-15 — no transcript.\n- 2023-12-11 — Hallway Chat — https://share.transistor.fm/s/9e031443 — \"At Spark, we like demos\"; how he passes by email.\n- 2025-01-22 — AI & I (Every) — https://every.to/podcast/the-venture-capitalist-who-only-makes-two-bets-a-year — \"Japanese toilets of AI\"; Granola.\n- 2025-02-03 — 20VC E1255 — https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt — check-size ladder, lost deals, COVID self-critique, principal incentives.\n- 2025-06-05 — Hallway Chat — https://share.transistor.fm/s/bb13b138 — against pre-emption; no SPVs.\n- 2025-08-04 — How I AI — https://www.lennysnewsletter.com/p/how-a-vc-and-a-tech-founder-used — building Tabletop Library with Andrew Mason.\n\n## How they think\n- Product over market: you learn more from \"the thing that comes out of their hands\" than from a pitch [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt).\n- Adaptation / evolution / revolution: a lens from the mobile era, reapplied to AI. Incumbents adapt, products like Descript evolve the medium, and new markets are the revolution [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt).\n- Contrarian views: big tech won't dominate AI apps [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/1ca2d86f). Pre-emption mostly benefits the VC [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/bb13b138). Most investor advice is pattern-matching noise [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt). Principal-run firms chase promotions, not exits [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt).\n- Calls: right on Cruise (passed on the first product, led after the pivot; GM paid $1B+ within about a year) [src](https://medium.com/spark-capital/on-cruise-cc51c782d70f). Late to Oculus by his own account. Spark's early team did worse in 2020-21 [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt).\n\n## Read these first\n1. 20VC E1255 (2025): the clearest statement of check size, competition and the incentive critique — https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt\n2. \"On Cruise, the hard things the hard way\" (2016): the deal origin in his words — https://medium.com/spark-capital/on-cruise-cc51c782d70f\n3. Hallway Chat, 2023-11-10: why this AI wave favours startups — https://share.transistor.fm/s/1ca2d86f\n4. Hallway Chat, 2025-06-05: pre-emption and SPVs — https://share.transistor.fm/s/bb13b138\n5. nabeelhyatt.com/investments: his own board list — https://www.nabeelhyatt.com/investments\n\n## Social engagement\nHe posts long-form X threads on company building (OKRs 2021, layoffs 2020, SF civic engagement 2020) and on AI as a creative tool (a 2023 board-game thread) [src](https://www.nabeelhyatt.com/writing). On Hallway Chat he amplifies Sam Lessin's podcast while disagreeing with Lessin's \"seed is dead\" deck [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/1ca2d86f). He cites Fred Wilson's blog as his favourite [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt). Follower counts not checked.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cruise | Series A ($12.5M) | 2015-09 | yes (with Sam Altman) | Sam Altman | [src](https://www.sparkcapital.com/archive/team-profiles/nabeel-hyatt) [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=1211414) |\n| Discord | Series C | 2016 | yes | | [src](https://www.sparkcapital.com/archive/team-profiles/nabeel-hyatt) |\n| Thalmic Labs / North | Series A; Series B participation ($120M, led by Intel and Amazon) | 2013-14; 2016-09 | A: yes | Intel, Amazon, iNovia | [src](https://medium.com/spark-capital/thalmic-labs-raises-120m-9f8eb394a0f7) |\n| Sonder | Series A ($10M) | 2016-09 | yes | | [src](https://medium.com/spark-capital/sonder-e50dcc8d881f) |\n| Fig | Seed | 2015 | yes (incubated in Spark SF) | | [src](https://medium.com/@nabeel/fig-fixing-how-games-get-made-669880751b68) |\n| Smash.gg | Series A | 2017-08 | yes | Accel, Horizons | [src](https://medium.com/@nabeel/smash-gg-building-the-infrastructure-for-esports-7975cc4046fe) |\n| Capella Space | Series A; Series B ($19M) | 2017; 2018-09 | co-led both | DCVC (Matt Ocko) | [src](https://medium.com/@nabeel/capella-space-raises-19m-series-b-satellite-swarms-with-synthetic-aperture-radar-for-continuous-5fe236491b93) |\n| Rare Bits | Series A ($6M) | 2018-04 | yes | First Round Capital | [src](https://medium.com/spark-capital/partnering-with-rare-bits-ed356b834b5f) |\n| AllStripes | Series A | 2020 | yes | | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-members/nabeel-hyatt) |\n| Adept | Undisclosed (Spark co-led) | 2023-03 | co-led | | [src](https://medium.com/spark-capital/spark-capital-co-leads-round-in-adept-the-ai-co-pilot-for-your-computer-6a3fd88f26e5) |\n| Q.ai | Series A | 2023 | co-led | Kleiner Perkins (Mamoon Hamid), GV, Aleph | [src](https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001533701) |\n| Granola | Seed and Series A | 2023-24 | board | | [src](https://www.nabeelhyatt.com/investments) |\n| Profluent | Series A ($35M) | 2024-03 | board (with Fraser Kelton) | | [src](https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/profluent-secures-35m-in-additional-funding-and-key-industry-experts-to-scale-foundational-ai-models-for-biomedicine-and-tackle-first-vertical-in-gene-editing) |\n| The Bot Company | Seed ($150M at $550M) | 2024-05 | co-led with Nat Friedman, Daniel Gross | | [src](https://www.therobotreport.com/the-bot-company-led-by-kyle-vogt-brings-in-another-150m/) |\n| Wordware | Seed | 2024 | yes (per his account of winning it) | | [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt) |\n| Postmates, Descript, Instawork, Zum, Wild Type, Ridwell, Sonera, Sauna, Producer.AI, Conductor | boards (year he lists) | 2014-2025 | | | [src](https://www.nabeelhyatt.com/investments) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Cruise (2014-15):** He first test-drove the Audi-only highway prototype and liked the team but not the product. For about a year he met the founders every couple of months and built Excel go-to-market models for the dealer roll-out, while saying he wasn't investing [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt). After Vogt went quiet for nine months, he emailed to say he was killing the working RP-1 autopilot and asked Nabeel to come by. That meeting led Spark to lead the A, and Nabeel took the board seat [src](https://medium.com/spark-capital/on-cruise-cc51c782d70f) [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt). Spark moved \"over the next couple of weeks\". GM bought Cruise for $1B+ in March 2016.\n- **Oculus (2013):** Santo Politi emailed him about the company. Nabeel was cynical until the demo, and Politi led the deal. Several tier-one firms passed [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt).\n- **Discord (2016):** He knew Jason Citron for seven years as fellow founders and passed twice before leading the C [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt).\n- **Granola (2023-24):** Chris Pedregal was an earlier Spark founder (Socratic). Nabeel was sent to coach him on growth soon after joining Spark. The product changed completely between seed and A [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt).\n- **Wordware (2024):** He met the founders six or seven times in a week, including dinners and morning walks, and won against higher term sheets [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt).\n- **Capella (2016-17):** He met Payam Banazadeh in Sept 2016 on a walk around Mountain View [src](https://medium.com/spark-capital/spark-capella-and-a-world-of-information-d7683ab1564e).\n- **Fig (2015):** Spark seeded Fig and housed it in Spark's SF office for its first months [src](https://medium.com/@nabeel/fig-fixing-how-games-get-made-669880751b68).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Spark Capital | Venture Partner (2012) -> GP, Early -> Venture Partner (2026) | 2012 | [src](https://bijansabet.com/2012/02/10/a-new-member-of-the-spark-team/) |\n| Zynga | GM (Zynga Boston) | 2010-2012 | [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt) |\n| Conduit Labs | Co-founder & CEO | until 2010 | [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt) |\n| Bijan Sabet | Recruiter, mentor, Hallway Chat co-host 2015-2021 | about 2002 | [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt) |\n| Fraser Kelton | Hallway Chat co-host; co-board at Profluent | 2023 | [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/1ca2d86f) |\n| Santo Politi | Spark partner who led Oculus | 2012 | [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt) |\n| Fred Destin | Former Spark partner he tried to imitate (his \"biggest mistake\") | | [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt) |\n| Kyle Vogt | Cruise A lead and board; The Bot Company backer | 2014 | [src](https://medium.com/spark-capital/on-cruise-cc51c782d70f) |\n| Andrew Mason | Descript board; Tabletop Library partner | | [src](https://www.lennysnewsletter.com/p/how-a-vc-and-a-tech-founder-used) |\n| Matt Ocko (DCVC) | Capella Series B co-lead and co-author | 2018 | [src](https://medium.com/@nabeel/capella-space-raises-19m-series-b-satellite-swarms-with-synthetic-aperture-radar-for-continuous-5fe236491b93) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact date he moved from GP to Venture Partner and angel, and whether he still sits on Spark boards (Granola, Wordware, Conductor). Fund IX Form D and the Feb 2026 deck bound it to 2025-26.\n- Is he doing angel checks alongside Fraser Kelton or Kyle Vogt (e.g. later Bot Company rounds)?\n- The pre-2023 Hallway Chat archive (#1-#32 with Bijan, 2015-2021) has no transcripts. #30 (2019, \"Is VC pattern recognition actually valuable\") and #31 (2020, investing during the pandemic) are the best unmined items.\n- Venture Stories with Sarah Tavel (2019) and LDV Vision Summit (2019) are on YouTube but not mined (bot-check block).\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-10)\n- He has stepped back to angel investing: \"now angel invest\", Spark in the past tense, 37 Spark investments in over a decade [src](https://www.nabeelhyatt.com/) [src](https://www.nabeelhyatt.com/investments).\n- Spark's check ladder ($5M to $20M at fixed ownership) and his rule that too much capital hurts companies [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt).\n- Two deals lost in 24 months to funds that tripled the price, plus his admission that early-stage quality fell in 2020-21 [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt).\n- How Cruise, Discord, Granola and Wordware were won through long courtships, passes and in-person intensity [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt) [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt).\n- Spark mechanics: seven flat early partners, no SPVs, against pre-emption, demos over decks [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/06872b41) [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/bb13b138) [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/9e031443).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.nabeelhyatt.com/ ; https://www.nabeelhyatt.com/investments ; https://www.nabeelhyatt.com/writing\n- https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeel-hyatt (transcript: https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/nabeel-hyatt-gp-spark-capital-to-win-in-ai-investors-need-to-change-their-approa)\n- https://thetwentyminutevc.com/nabeelhyatt (audio transcribed locally with Whisper; no public transcript)\n- https://feeds.transistor.fm/hallway-chat and episode transcripts on share.transistor.fm\n- https://medium.com/spark-capital/on-cruise-cc51c782d70f ; https://medium.com/@nabeel/fig-fixing-how-games-get-made-669880751b68 ; https://medium.com/spark-capital/thalmic-labs-raises-120m-9f8eb394a0f7 ; https://medium.com/spark-capital/spark-capella-and-a-world-of-information-d7683ab1564e ; https://medium.com/@nabeel/i-do-not-know-about-other-vcs-but-this-does-not-map-to-how-we-have-related-to-our-lps-at-spark-c012d4e1ecdc\n- https://www.sparkcapital.com/archive/team-profiles/nabeel-hyatt ; https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-members/nabeel-hyatt\n- https://bijansabet.com/2012/02/10/a-new-member-of-the-spark-team/ ; https://gamesbeat.com/as-social-games-boom-spark-capital-hires-former-zynga-exec-as-first-venture-partner/\n- https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001533701 ; https://www.therobotreport.com/the-bot-company-led-by-kyle-vogt-brings-in-another-150m/\n", "person:nabil-mallick": "\n# Nabil Mallick\n\n## One-line take\nThrive's operator-in-chief: the 2015 hire who embedded at GitHub as interim CFO and turned a 1% stake into 10%; now COO, signing the firm's vehicles.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** information edge from embedding. Mallick was dispatched to GitHub in their first nine months at Thrive, ran its budget and analytics as interim CFO, saw growth accelerating, and Thrive then invested $120M more at a 25% discount ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260109021000/https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). That is Thrive's \"embed an operator, then concentrate\" playbook in one person.\n- **Role today:** a firm-builder more than a deal-sourcer. Colossus calls Mallick Thrive's COO and one of \"the three main faces of Thrive\" with Kushner and Zaki (same src). Mallick signs Thrive's vehicle Form Ds as Authorized Signatory (FLB Partners T 2025-05 and 2026-01; Olam T 2026-04) ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2068359/000101297525000231/primary_doc.xml), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2128534/000212853426000004/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **Constraints:** Thrive attributes no deal to any individual (Colossus), so Mallick's named deals surface only through counsel/company releases and board filings. The named book is consumer, commerce and fintech: Skims, Glossier, Studs, Zola, Grailed, Vimeo, Guru, Modern Life.\n- **What they'd never do (by record):** nothing in the record shows Mallick leading a frontier-lab, AI-chip or infra deal.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **GitHub was an averaging-down, not a momentum trade.** Thrive bought at a $2B valuation, then after Mallick's embed invested $120M across two tranches at a 25% discount; Mallick: \"We go from 1% to 10% holders\" ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20260109021000/https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/), 2025-10). Thrive made about 7x in 18 months (same src).\n- **The GitHub role was effectively interim CFO, for nine months, as a first assignment.** Mallick \"flew back and forth between New Jersey and San Francisco\" (same src). The firm-file framing of \"acting CFO\" is approximately right; the source word is \"interim\".\n- **Mallick helped run GitHub's 2018 sale process.** It took three weeks; Microsoft opened at $6.5B all cash and ended at $7.5B all stock (same src).\n- **Operator, not a VC by training.** Before Thrive, Mallick ran Heinz's North America finance and operations for 3G, and quit when Heinz's big bet for the year was a mustard line: \"I saw my career flash before my eyes\" (same src).\n- **SEC filings give Mallick the GP title since May 2015**, the month Thrive hired them ([Vimeo DEF 14A 2022](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837686/000110465922051194/tm2213218d2_def14a.htm); [DEF 14A 2023](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837686/000110465923049624/tm232143d2_def14a.htm)). The press labels \"COO\" and \"Partner\" understate that.\n- **Private board book (from Vimeo proxies):** director at Glossier, Guru, The Browser Company, Studs and Zola; former director at Grailed; former board observer at GitHub and Greenhouse (2023 proxy, same link).\n- **Glossier's 2019 Form D lists Mallick as both \"Executive Officer\" and \"Director\"** of Glossier, for the $100M raise that closed 2019-03-13 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1587141/000158714119000002/primary_doc.xml)). This may be a filing convention or a real interim operating role; not confirmed.\n- **How Thrive got latitude from its first LP:** Mallick credits Princeton's Andy Golden, saying \"we're in business today because of a very consequential decision\" (Colossus, same src).\n- **Mallick resigned from Vimeo's board on 2023-10-18**, with no disagreement, after two and a half years ([8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837686/000110465923110794/tm2328907d1_8k.htm)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** Mallick's named book has no frontier AI, chips or automation; the only AI-adjacent deal is Modern Life (AI tooling for life-insurance advisors, Series A lead 2025). Why still worth knowing: Mallick is one of Thrive's three public faces and runs the firm's vehicles, so Mallick is a relationship node into Thrive (and Thrive Holdings-style operating plays), not a co-investor on lab seeds.\n\n## Background\n- Born in Karachi, Pakistan; moved to New Jersey in elementary school; father built a civil engineering firm of nearly 100 people ([Colossus](https://web.archive.org/web/20260109021000/https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- Undergraduate: New York University, 2005 (aggregator: [MarketScreener](https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/NABIL-MALLICK-A0ISH6/)).\n- Investment banker at Citigroup, then Perella Weinberg Partners (Vimeo proxy; MarketScreener lists Perella through 2008-12).\n- Harvard Business School, where Mallick met Kushner and Zaki (Colossus quotes Nitin Nohria).\n- 3G Capital / H.J. Heinz: one of the first non-Brazilians to move to Pittsburgh after 3G bought Heinz; within three months ran Heinz finance and operations for North America (Colossus).\n- Thrive Capital, May 2015 to present: hired to lead the portfolio-impact team (then a team of one), first assignment GitHub; GP since May 2015 per SEC filings; COO per Colossus 2025-10.\n- Age 39 as of the April 2023 Vimeo proxy.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | thrivecap.com has no team page |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | an aggregator lists x.com/nabilmallick; not verified, not used |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | aggregator-listed URL not verified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| SEC EDGAR | [CIK 0001859305](https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?action=getcompany&CIK=0001859305) | Vimeo Forms 3/4, 2021–2023 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-10 — Colossus (written profile of Kushner/Thrive, not a podcast) — [link](https://web.archive.org/web/20260109021000/https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) — GitHub embed, Heinz, the Princeton seed.\n- No podcast or talk found.\n\n## How they think\n- Businesses are businesses: \"It also helps to know ketchup,\" Mallick said, \"which is just like software\" (Colossus).\n- Concentrate when you have an edge from inside the company (GitHub 1% -> 10%).\n- Distance from Silicon Valley groupthink lets Thrive \"run toward a burning building\" (Colossus paraphrase of Grimshaw and Mallick).\n- Consumer brands win on direct connection: Skims' ability to connect \"on a personal level\" ([Gunderson](https://www.gunder.com/news/thrive-capital-leads-154m-financing-of-skims), 2021-04-09).\n- Right calls: GitHub (about 7x in 18 months). Wrong calls: nothing admitted on record.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Colossus on Kushner and Thrive](https://web.archive.org/web/20260109021000/https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) (2025-10): the GitHub section is Mallick's own account.\n2. [Vimeo 2023 proxy bio](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837686/000110465923049624/tm232143d2_def14a.htm): the only authoritative list of titles and boards.\n3. [Modern Life Series A release](https://modernlife.com/article/modern-life-raises-20-million-series-a-to-empower-advisors-with-ai-powered-life-insurance-platform) (2025-11-19): Mallick's only AI-adjacent quote.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: no verified X, Substack or LinkedIn activity.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| GitHub | Growth (Thrive entry at ~$2B; $20-30M check) | 2015 | no | not stated | [Colossus](https://web.archive.org/web/20260109021000/https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| GitHub | Growth ($120M follow-on in two tranches at a 25% discount) | ~2016 (within a year of entry) | Thrive only | not stated | same |\n| Skims | Series A ($154M) | 2021-04-09 | yes (Thrive) | Imaginary Ventures; Alliance Consumer Growth | [Gunderson](https://www.gunder.com/news/thrive-capital-leads-154m-financing-of-skims) (existing row) |\n| Neon (gaming) | Seed ($14M) | 2024-11 | unclear | Renegade Partners and others | existing row in thrive-capital-fullbook-deals.csv |\n| Modern Life | Series A ($20M) | 2025-11-19 | yes (Thrive) | New York Life Ventures; Northwestern Mutual Future Ventures; Allegis | [Modern Life](https://modernlife.com/article/modern-life-raises-20-million-series-a-to-empower-advisors-with-ai-powered-life-insurance-platform) (existing row; Thrive also led the $15M seed) |\n| Vimeo | board seat after Thrive's Growth ($150M) | director 2021-05 to 2023-10-18 | Thrive led | GIC | [8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837686/000110465923110794/tm2328907d1_8k.htm) |\n| Glossier, Guru, The Browser Company, Studs, Zola | board seats (rounds not attributed to Mallick) | as of 2023-04 | — | — | [2023 proxy](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837686/000110465923049624/tm232143d2_def14a.htm) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **GitHub (2015–2018):** Grimshaw pitched it; Thrive put 7% of its $400M fund in by mid-2015. Then GitHub's CEO fired the CFO and heads of product and marketing after a harassment scandal, and LPs called asking if the stake was going to zero. That same week Thrive hired Mallick and sent them in. Mallick finalized the budget and built analytics, concluded growth was accelerating, and Thrive bought $120M more at 25% below its entry price, reaching ~10% and board-observer seats. Mallick then helped run the 2018 sale to Microsoft ([Colossus](https://web.archive.org/web/20260109021000/https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- **How Mallick joined:** Kushner knew Mallick from HBS, heard they were looking, and met for coffee. Mallick warned they had \"never had an iPhone\" and didn't know what SaaS meant (same src).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Thrive Capital | General Partner; COO (2025) | 2015-05 | [Vimeo proxy](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837686/000110465923049624/tm232143d2_def14a.htm); [Colossus](https://web.archive.org/web/20260109021000/https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Josh Kushner | HBS classmate; hired Mallick; close personal friend (walked home together across the Charles on Friday nights) | HBS | Colossus |\n| Kareem Zaki | HBS-era friend; one of the \"three main faces of Thrive\" with Mallick and Kushner | HBS | Colossus |\n| Miles Grimshaw | GitHub deal partner | 2015 | Colossus |\n| Chris Wanstrath | GitHub founder/CEO; Mallick embedded as interim CFO | 2015 | Colossus |\n| Andy Golden (Princeton) | Mallick credits Golden's seed of Thrive's first institutional fund | ~2010 | Colossus |\n| Vimeo | Director | 2021-05 to 2023-10-18 | [8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837686/000110465923110794/tm2328907d1_8k.htm) |\n| Glossier, Guru, The Browser Company, Studs, Zola | Director | as of 2023-04 | 2023 proxy |\n| Grailed | Former director | ended by 2023-04 | 2023 proxy |\n| GitHub, Greenhouse | Former board observer | — | 2023 proxy |\n| FLB Partners T, L.P.; Olam T, L.P. | Authorized Signatory on Form Ds (Thrive-address vehicles; Kushner as executive officer) | 2025-05 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2068359/000101297525000231/primary_doc.xml); [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2128534/000212853426000004/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What are FLB Partners T and Olam T (2024 and 2026 Thrive-address LPs, 3(c)(7))? Possibly Thrive Holdings or Thrive Eternal vehicles; unconfirmed.\n- Was the Glossier \"Executive Officer\" listing a real interim operating role, like the GitHub embed?\n- Is Mallick involved in Thrive Holdings' AI roll-ups (Shield, Crete)? No source links Mallick to them.\n- Neon (gaming) seed 2024: GamesBeat quote returned 403; Thrive's lead status unconfirmed. Note: the repo tag slug `neon` is non-ai and may refer to the Postgres company, not this gaming company.\n- **Warning for later agents:** aggregator profiles (startupintros, evalyze) carry a false bio (Wharton, Goldman, Battery, Insight, \"promoted 2022\", an Attentive deal). SEC filings and Colossus contradict it; don't ingest it.\n\n## Sources\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20260109021000/https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/ (Colossus, 2025-10; live colossus.com returns 403)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837686/000110465922051194/tm2213218d2_def14a.htm (Vimeo DEF 14A, 2022-04-27)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837686/000110465923049624/tm232143d2_def14a.htm (Vimeo DEF 14A, 2023-04-25)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837686/000110465923110794/tm2328907d1_8k.htm (Vimeo 8-K, 2023-10-23)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1587141/000158714119000002/primary_doc.xml (Glossier Form D, 2019-03-25)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2068359/000101297525000231/primary_doc.xml and https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2068359/000206835926000001/primary_doc.xml (FLB Partners T Form Ds)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2128534/000212853426000004/primary_doc.xml (Olam T Form D)\n- https://www.gunder.com/news/thrive-capital-leads-154m-financing-of-skims\n- https://modernlife.com/article/modern-life-raises-20-million-series-a-to-empower-advisors-with-ai-powered-life-insurance-platform\n- https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/NABIL-MALLICK-A0ISH6/ (aggregator)\n", "person:nadia-cochinwala": "\n# Nadia Cochinwala\n\n## One-line take\nShe is Mamoon Hamid's growth-side deal partner at Kleiner Perkins: five of the six KP posts with her byline are co-signed by Hamid, and two of them are KP leads (Hippocratic AI Series B, $141M; Rogo Series D, $160M). Her compute names (Nexthop AI, Fal, Together.ai) are portfolio-page listings only, not bylines.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** she sits on the KP Select / \"inflection\" side of the firm next to Hamid, the partner who carries the firm's AI book. Since early 2025 she has co-signed the AI-application growth leads (Hippocratic B, Rogo D) and the big growth entries (OpenEvidence, Waymo, Granola, Garner) ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/)). Her TPG Growth, Bridgewater and Evercore training fits growth diligence, and Rogo (AI for bankers) is squarely her old world.\n- **What constrains her:** she is a principal with no sole byline. Every post she co-signs has a partner on it (Hamid on five, Coyne on Garner). Her deals are growth entries after other firms led the early rounds: KP was not first money in any bylined deal.\n- **What she'd never do (inference):** nothing in her record touches pre-training labs or chips at seed. Her stated focus is \"enterprise applications and infrastructure\" ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/)), and her career has been growth and inflection-stage investing ([2023 bio](https://web.archive.org/web/20230529181403/https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Hamid pairing is the real signal.** Hippocratic AI (2025-01-09), OpenEvidence (2025-07-15), Waymo (2026-02-02), Granola (2026-03-25) and Rogo (2026-04-29) all carry \"By Mamoon Hamid ... Nadia Cochinwala\" ([Rogo](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/rogo-the-ai-platform-for-global-finance/), [Granola](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/granola-putting-your-companys-context-to-work/), [Hippocratic](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/hippocratic-ai-series-b/), [Waymo](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/waymo-the-infrastructure-of-autonomy/), [OpenEvidence](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/openevidence-expanding-the-worlds-collective-medical-knowledge/)). The sixth, Garner Health (2026-02-10), is with Josh Coyne ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/garner-health-building-the-front-door-to-healthcare/)). The repo's KP firm file only credited her with the Rogo byline. She has five more.\n- **First bylined lead came about 18 months after joining:** \"We're excited to lead Hippocratic AI's Series B\" (2025-01-09, [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/hippocratic-ai-series-b/)). The post credits customer pull as the decider: \"it was the customer feedback that really moved us.\" The same post shows a relationship-driven origin: \"We've known Munjal Shah for a few decades.\" That is Hamid's network, not hers (inference: she joined in 2023).\n- **Hired as an inflection-stage investor, not early stage.** The 2023 bio says she \"focuses on inflection investments primarily in enterprise software and consumer software\". It also lists Golden Gate Capital as a prior role, which later bios drop ([Wayback 2023-05-29](https://web.archive.org/web/20230529181403/https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/)). By 2026 the focus reads \"enterprise applications and infrastructure\" ([current page](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/)): a drift toward infra, in line with the Nexthop and Fal listings.\n- **Her own framing of investing (2024 KP Q&A):** \"investing feels like the grown up version of debate\". She cites eight years of competitive parliamentary debate, starting at 13 ([Wayback 2024-11-10](https://web.archive.org/web/20241110184638/https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/)). Takeaway: she does breadth-first study of many sectors, which fits a growth generalist.\n- **Granola: the company credits Hamid, not her.** Granola's Series C post says the $125M round at $1.5B was \"led by Danny Rimer at Index Ventures\", with KP as a new investor via Hamid ([src](https://www.granola.ai/blog/series-c)). Her byline is on KP's post only. Read: she is deal team, not the founder-facing name.\n- **Title history from the Wayback Machine:** \"Investor\" on 2024-04-15 and 2024-11-10, then \"Principal\" on 2026-03-23 ([2024](https://web.archive.org/web/20241110184638/https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/), [2026](https://web.archive.org/web/20260323154510/https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/)). No KP announcement of the promotion was found.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** On deals she is low: everything she has signed is growth-stage AI applications (healthcare, finance, meetings) or Waymo, and KP was never first in. The case for following her is your segment rule: a principal at a firm that writes $100M+ growth leads, sitting next to the partner (Hamid) who also runs KP's frontier book (Flapping Airplanes, Mirendil, Etched, per the [KP firm file](../firms/kleiner-perkins.md)). The deals that matter to you on her page are only listings: **Nexthop AI** (AI networking, KP \"Early 2025\"), **Fal** (KP new in the $140M Series D, Dec 2025) and **Together.ai** (KP led the $102.5M Series A, Nov 2023; Hamid's byline). Her personal role in these is unconfirmed. Useful as a co-investor channel into KP Select on large AI rounds, not as an early-stage frontier picker.\n\n## Background\n- Born and raised in the Bay Area. Graduated from UC Berkeley (Haas School of Business per the 2023 bio) and was on Berkeley's Parliamentary Debate team ([Wayback 2023](https://web.archive.org/web/20230529181403/https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/)).\n- Career start: Evercore, advising on \"multi billion dollar acquisitions and financings\" ([current KP page](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/)).\n- Investing roles at Bridgewater Associates and Golden Gate Capital (2023 bio), then TPG Growth (2023 bio). Dates for each not found.\n- Kleiner Perkins from 2023: \"Investor\", then \"Principal\" by March 2026 (see promotion_path).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [KP profile](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/) | Portfolio list of 14 companies; fetched 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | No solo KP posts; six co-bylines |\n| X | [@NadiaCochinwala](https://twitter.com/NadiaCochinwala) | Linked from the KP page; content not retrievable (X returned 402); follower count not found |\n| LinkedIn | [nadiacochinwala](https://www.linkedin.com/in/nadiacochinwala/) | Linked from the KP page; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. The web-search budget for this run was used up, so podcasts and talks were not searched. KP's own podcast pages were not checked.\n\n## How they think\n- **Customer pull over thesis:** on Hippocratic AI, KP had been following the company but \"it was the customer feedback that really moved us\" ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/hippocratic-ai-series-b/)). The Rogo and Granola posts likewise lead with usage: 35,000+ daily Rogo users, and Granola use spreading across companies.\n- **\"System of record\" framing:** Granola is \"the system of record for conversational context\" ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/granola-putting-your-companys-context-to-work/)). Garner is the \"Moneyball database for healthcare providers\" ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/garner-health-building-the-front-door-to-healthcare/)). The theme is data-moat applications.\n- **Vertical AI where generic models fail on precision:** the Rogo post argues generic AI tools fall apart on institutional finance's auditability needs ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/rogo-the-ai-platform-for-global-finance/)).\n- Contrarian views or calls admitted wrong: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Hippocratic AI: Making healthcare more abundant](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/hippocratic-ai-series-b/) (2025-01-09): the first lead she co-signed; shows the customer-pull test.\n2. [Rogo: The AI Platform for Global Finance](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/rogo-the-ai-platform-for-global-finance/) (2026-04-29): the Series D lead, in her own banking domain.\n3. [Garner Health](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/garner-health-building-the-front-door-to-healthcare/) (2026-02-10): the longest post, and the only one without Hamid (co-signed with Coyne).\n4. [Granola](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/granola-putting-your-companys-context-to-work/) (2026-03-25): the context-layer thesis.\n5. [2024 KP Q&A (Wayback)](https://web.archive.org/web/20241110184638/https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/): the debate-to-investing origin story.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot retrievable: X returned 402 and web search was exhausted. Nothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nKP page \"Partnered Since\" listings, plus bylines where they exist. A listing means KP's page puts her on the company; it does not say she sourced or led the deal.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Rogo (AI for finance) | Series D, $160M | 2026-04-29 | yes (KP); co-byline Hamid | Sequoia, Thrive, Khosla, J.P. Morgan Growth Equity Partners, BoxGroup, Mantis VC, Jack Altman | [KP](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/rogo-the-ai-platform-for-global-finance/), [Rogo](https://rogo.com/blog/series-d) |\n| Granola | Series C, $125M at $1.5B | 2026-03-25 | no (Index / Danny Rimer led); co-byline Hamid | Lightspeed, Spark, NFDG (existing) | [KP](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/granola-putting-your-companys-context-to-work/), [Granola](https://www.granola.ai/blog/series-c) |\n| Garner Health | not stated (Growth, 2024 per page) | post 2026-02-10 | not stated; co-byline Coyne | not stated | [KP](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/garner-health-building-the-front-door-to-healthcare/) |\n| Waymo | not stated (Growth, 2026) | post 2026-02-02 | not stated; co-byline Hamid, Oliveira | not stated | [KP](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/waymo-the-infrastructure-of-autonomy/) |\n| OpenEvidence | not stated (Early, 2025 per page) | post 2025-07-15 | not stated; co-byline Hamid, Doerr, Coyne | not stated | [KP](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/openevidence-expanding-the-worlds-collective-medical-knowledge/) |\n| Hippocratic AI | Series B, $141M at $1.64B | 2025-01-09 | yes (KP); co-byline Hamid | not named on the press page | [KP](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/hippocratic-ai-series-b/), [press](https://hippocraticai.com/press/) |\n| Nexthop AI (AI networking) | listed \"Early, 2025\"; Lightspeed's page calls its 2025 entry the Series A | 2025 | not found | Lightspeed (Chahal, Mhatre); 2026 $500M Series B led by Lightspeed, a16z, Altimeter | [KP page](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/), [LSVP](https://lsvp.com/company/nexthop-ai/), [Nexthop B](https://nexthop.ai/news-and-event/nexthop-ai-accelerates-into-hypergrowth-with-oversubscribed-500m-series-b-funding-catapulting-the-companys-valuation-to-4-2-billion/) |\n| Fal | Series D, $140M (KP a new investor) | 2025-12-09 | no lead stated | Sequoia, NVIDIA (new) | [fal](https://blog.fal.ai/our-series-d-scaling-fal/) |\n| Together.ai | Series A, $102.5M | 2023-11-29 | yes (KP), Hamid's byline only | NVIDIA, Emergence, NEA, Prosperity7, Greycroft, 137 Ventures; seed: Lux, Definition and others | [Together](https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a), [KP](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/together-ai/) |\n| Cartesia | listed Early 2024; KP post bylined by Ilya Fushman only | 2025-03-11 post | not found | not found | [KP](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/cartesia-pioneering-real-time-voice-ai/) |\n| Windsurf (Codeium) | listed Early 2024; Series B post bylined by Braswell only | 2024-01-30 post | not found | not found | [KP](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/codeium-series-b/) |\n| Nooks | listed Early 2024 (Hamid/Braswell lead per KP firm file) | 2024 | not found | not found | [KP page](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/) |\n| Databricks | listed Growth 2024 | 2024 | not found | not found | [KP page](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/) |\n| Applied Intuition | listed Early 2018 (before she joined: a legacy listing) | 2018 | not found | not found | [KP page](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/) |\n\n**Timing vs others (was KP first?):**\n- **Rogo:** no. Seed $7M (Feb 2024), Series A $18M from Khosla (Oct 2024), Series B $50M from Thrive, J.P. Morgan and Tiger (Apr 2025), Series C $75M (Jan 2026), then KP's D lead about 3 months later ([src](https://rogo.com/news)).\n- **Hippocratic:** no. KP led the B about 18 months after founding ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/hippocratic-ai-series-b/)).\n- **Granola:** no. Series A $20M in Oct 2024 ([src](https://www.granola.ai/blog)), then KP came in as a new investor in the C about 17 months later.\n- **Together:** no. Lux led the $20M seed in May 2023 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/05/15/together-raises-20m-to-build-open-source-generative-ai-models/)), and KP led the A about 6 months later.\n- **Nexthop:** possibly early. Both KP (\"Early 2025\") and Lightspeed (Series A, 2025) date their entry to 2025, so KP was likely in the first institutional round. Whether KP or Lightspeed led, and the round size, were not confirmed from a primary source this pass.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Hippocratic AI B (Jan 2025):** the relationship came first (\"known Munjal Shah for a few decades\"), KP watched the company, and adoption at 23+ health systems tipped the decision ([src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/hippocratic-ai-series-b/)). The long relationship reads as Hamid's.\n- **Rogo D (Apr 2026):** KP led a $160M D in a crowded cap table (Sequoia, Thrive, Khosla) ([src](https://rogo.com/blog/series-d)). Intro path not found.\n- **How she sources:** not found in any primary source. Every bylined deal is co-signed with a partner, and her pages show no solo sourcing story.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Kleiner Perkins | Investor, then Principal | 2023 | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/) |\n| Mamoon Hamid (KP) | Co-author on 5 of 6 bylines (Hippocratic, OpenEvidence, Waymo, Granola, Rogo) | 2025-01 | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/rogo-the-ai-platform-for-global-finance/) |\n| Josh Coyne (KP) | Co-author (Garner Health, OpenEvidence) | 2025-07 | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/garner-health-building-the-front-door-to-healthcare/) |\n| Lucas Oliveira (KP) | Co-author (Waymo); fellow 2023 principal | 2026-02 | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/waymo-the-infrastructure-of-autonomy/) |\n| John Doerr (KP) | Co-author (OpenEvidence) | 2025-07 | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/openevidence-expanding-the-worlds-collective-medical-knowledge/) |\n| TPG Growth | Former investor | before 2023 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230529181403/https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/) |\n| Bridgewater Associates | Former investing role | before 2023 | same |\n| Golden Gate Capital | Former investing role (2023 bio only) | before 2023 | same |\n| Evercore | Career start (advisory) | not stated | [src](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- The exact Investor-to-Principal date (no Wayback captures between 2024-11 and 2026-03; LinkedIn not fetched).\n- The Nexthop AI 2025 round: size, lead, and KP's role. The computed brief said \"$110M, Lightspeed led\", but no primary source was found this pass.\n- Her actual role on Fal, Together and Nexthop (listings only). Is she the day-to-day on KP's compute-infra names?\n- X activity and any podcast appearances (search budget exhausted this run).\n- Whether she has a board seat or observer role anywhere (none listed).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20230529181403/https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20240415223438/https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20241110184638/https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20260323154510/https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/nadia-cochinwala/\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/rogo-the-ai-platform-for-global-finance/\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/granola-putting-your-companys-context-to-work/\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/hippocratic-ai-series-b/\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/openevidence-expanding-the-worlds-collective-medical-knowledge/\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/waymo-the-infrastructure-of-autonomy/\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/garner-health-building-the-front-door-to-healthcare/\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/together-ai/\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/cartesia-pioneering-real-time-voice-ai/\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/codeium-series-b/\n- https://rogo.com/news ; https://rogo.com/blog/series-d\n- https://www.granola.ai/blog ; https://www.granola.ai/blog/series-c\n- https://hippocraticai.com/press/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/nexthop-ai/\n- https://nexthop.ai/news-and-event/nexthop-ai-accelerates-into-hypergrowth-with-oversubscribed-500m-series-b-funding-catapulting-the-companys-valuation-to-4-2-billion/\n- https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop\n- https://blog.fal.ai/our-series-d-scaling-fal/\n- https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/05/15/together-raises-20m-to-build-open-source-generative-ai-models/ (via repo row)\n", "person:nat-friedman": "\n# Nat Friedman\n\n> Written 2026-11-11 (sweep early-backers-1). The fund is covered in [firms/nfdg.md](../firms/nfdg.md) and his partner in [people/daniel-gross.md](daniel-gross.md); this page is about him as an individual: the vehicles, the full cheque list, and what he has said. Sources: aigrant.com, nat.org, the Dwarkesh transcript, company announcements already in the ledger, and aggregators (labelled). Stratechery interviews are catalogued but paywalled and not mined.\n\n## One-line take\nAn operator who invested through three stacked vehicles: personal angel cheques (2019 to 2023), a $250K accelerator cheque (AI Grant, 92 companies) and a $1.1B fund (NFDG). The accelerator fed the fund, and since July 2025 he has been inside Meta and is not leading new rounds.\n\n## Crux\nHe won because founders wanted the former GitHub CEO on the cap table and because he decided in one meeting. The mechanism that is easy to miss is the ladder: AI Grant put $250K into a company at formation, and NFDG came back to lead the seed or A in the ones that worked (MatX, Pika, Juicebox, Granola, Pulse). The constraint now is Meta: the fund is in run-off under Hersh Desai, and his own time is spoken for. He would not run a consensus process: his site lists distrust of the efficient-market view among his beliefs ([src](https://nat.org/), paraphrase).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **AI Grant is the real seed book.** Four batches, 92 companies, each \"no cap, no discount MFN SAFE\" at $250K ([src](https://aigrant.com/)). Batch 1 includes Perplexity, Cursor, Replicate, Chroma, Dust, Recraft and Vizcom; batch 2 MatX, Pika, ggml, Hume AI, RunPod and Lindy; batch 3 Browserbase, Matic and K-Scale Labs; batch 4 Goodfire, Granola, Juicebox, Inception Labs, Haize Labs and Vapi.\n- **It is his and Gross's money:** the site says they \"have invested $10M in AI Grant\" and that investments are made by AI Grant LLC ([src](https://aigrant.com/)).\n- **Product over research, by design:** \"AI research is abundant, but UX and product innovation is just getting started\" ([src](https://aigrant.com/)).\n- **On proliferation (2023-03):** \"the pro proliferation case is a bit stronger.\" ([Dwarkesh transcript](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/nat-friedman)).\n- **On why builders mattered then:** \"an alien substance has landed on Earth\" (same source).\n- **On alignment:** \"there's no open source technical alignment community.\" (same source).\n- Already in the ledger: he pushed Carmack to take outside money at Keen (\"glad Nat pushed me on it\", [src](https://techcrunch.com/2022/08/19/john-carmack-agi-keen-raises-20-million-from-sequoia-nat-friedman-and-others)) and committed to AIUC after one 90-minute pitch ([src](https://fortune.com/2025/07/23/ai-agent-insurance-startup-aiuc-stealth-15-million-seed-nat-friedman/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh, but historical. He backed exactly what the owner backs (new labs, chips, compute, AI-native automation) at seed with large cheques. He is not a live competitor for new rounds while at Meta. The use now is (1) his book as a map of who was early, (2) AI Grant's batch list as a pre-seed feed, (3) Hersh Desai as the person holding follow-on rights.\n\n## Background\nGrew up in Charlottesville, Virginia; MIT; co-founded Ximian and Xamarin; CEO of GitHub 2018 to 2021 ([src](https://nat.org/)). Launched the AI Grant programme with Daniel Gross in October 2022 ([src](https://stratechery.com/2022/an-interview-with-daniel-gross-and-nat-friedman-about-the-democratization-of-ai/)); an earlier version of AI Grant is not sourced here. Co-founded NFDG in 2023; joined Meta in July 2025 to lead products and applied research at Meta Superintelligence Labs alongside Alexandr Wang ([src](https://fortune.com/2025/06/30/mark-zuckerberg-overhauled-meta-ai-org-superintelligence-alexandr-wang/)). Side projects: the Vesuvius Challenge (scrollprize.org) and PlasticList ([src](https://nat.org/)).\n\n## Vehicles\n| Vehicle | What it is | Cheque | Period | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Personal | Named individually in rounds (W&B 2019, AssemblyAI 2022, Perplexity seed 2022, Keen 2022, Magic A 2023) | not disclosed | 2019 to 2023; a few after (RunPod 2024, Mechanize 2025) | ledger rows |\n| AI Grant LLC | Accelerator funded with $10M from Friedman and Gross; now run by Hersh Desai and Lenny Bogdonoff | $250K uncapped MFN SAFE plus credits | 2022 to 2025 (four batches) | [src](https://aigrant.com/) |\n| NFDG | About $1.1B fund with Daniel Gross; one Form D entity (NFDG Siena, LLC) | $1M to $100M | 2023 to 2025-07; run-off since | [firm page](../firms/nfdg.md) |\n| Andromeda | GPU cluster offered to portfolio companies, later spun out | compute, not cash | 2023 onward | [firm page](../firms/nfdg.md) |\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://nat.org/ | short beliefs list; no portfolio |\n| Substack / blog | none | |\n| X | not checked in this pass | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | not checked | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | recurring guest on Stratechery with Daniel Gross |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-03 — Dwarkesh Podcast — Reading ancient scrolls, open source, and AI — [link](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/nat-friedman) — mined; says over half of code at Copilot users was Copilot-written by then\n- 2022-10 to 2025 — Stratechery — eight interviews with Daniel Gross and Ben Thompson — [index](https://stratechery.com/topic/events/interview/nat-friedman-and-daniel-gross/) — paywalled, not mined; the first (2022-10) was timed to the AI Grant relaunch\n\n## How they think\n- Ambition, speed and small teams; many tech companies are overstaffed ([src](https://nat.org/), paraphrase).\n- Research is abundant, product is scarce (AI Grant).\n- Many models will exist; techniques spread (Dwarkesh, 2023).\n- Calls: early on Copilot, Perplexity, Cursor, ElevenLabs, MatX. Nothing found where he publicly says he passed on a company or regrets a pass.\n\n## Read these first\n- [aigrant.com](https://aigrant.com/): terms and all four batch lists; the best single map of his seed exposure.\n- [Dwarkesh transcript](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/nat-friedman): his AI views in early 2023.\n- [nat.org](https://nat.org/): operating beliefs.\n- [firms/nfdg.md](../firms/nfdg.md): the fund and what happened after Meta.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed in this pass (X not fetched).\n\n## Portfolio\nStage key: **P/S** = in at pre-seed or seed. \"Ledger\" means the row and its source are already in `data/rounds.csv` or `data/deals.csv`.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Vehicle | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Weights & Biases | Series A ($15M); B 2021; co-led $50M 2023 | 2019-05-30 | 2023 only | Daniel Gross (2023) | personal | ledger |\n| AssemblyAI | Series A ($28M); C 2023 | 2022-03-04 | no | | personal | ledger |\n| Chroma **P/S** | Pre-seed; AI Grant batch 1 | 2022-05 | no | Anthony Goldbloom led | AI Grant | ledger; [src](https://aigrant.com/) |\n| Keen Technologies **P/S** | Seed ($20M), board | 2022-08-19 | co-led | Daniel Gross, Sequoia, Collisons, Lutke | personal | ledger |\n| Perplexity **P/S** | Seed (co-led with Elad Gil); AI Grant batch 1; A, B, B-1 | 2022-09 | co-led | Elad Gil | personal + AI Grant | ledger; [src](https://aigrant.com/) |\n| Atomic Semi **P/S** | Seed (reported engaged) | 2023-01 | no | OpenAI Startup Fund, Ehrsam, Ravikant | personal | ledger |\n| Atomic AI | Series A ($35M) | 2023-01-25 | no | Playground | personal | ledger |\n| Magic | Series A; led B 2024; C | 2023-02-06 | B | Daniel Gross | personal then NFDG | ledger |\n| Distyl **P/S** | Seed ($7M); A 2024 | 2023-04 | no | Coatue, Dell | personal | ledger |\n| CoreWeave | Series B | 2023-04-20 | no | Magnetar, NVIDIA | NFDG | ledger |\n| ggml **P/S** | Pre-seed; AI Grant batch 2 | 2023-06 | yes | Daniel Gross | personal + AI Grant | ledger |\n| ElevenLabs | Series A ($19M) | 2023-06-20 | co-led | a16z | NFDG | ledger |\n| Pika **P/S** | Pre-seed and seed; AI Grant batch 2 | 2023 | yes | | AI Grant then NFDG | ledger; [src](https://aigrant.com/) |\n| Cursor **P/S** | Seed ($8M); AI Grant batch 1 | 2023-10-11 | no | OpenAI Startup Fund led | personal + AI Grant | ledger; [src](https://aigrant.com/) |\n| MatX **P/S** | Seed (~$24.9M); AI Grant batch 2; B 2026 | 2023-12 | yes | Daniel Gross | AI Grant then NFDG | ledger; [src](https://aigrant.com/) |\n| Generalist AI **P/S** | Seed | 2024-03 | no | boldstart, NVIDIA (co-leads), Spark | NFDG | ledger |\n| RunPod **P/S** | Seed ($20M); AI Grant batch 2 | 2024-05-08 | no | Intel Capital, Dell | personal + AI Grant | ledger; [src](https://aigrant.com/) |\n| The Bot Company **P/S** | Seed ($150M) | 2024-05-13 | co-led | Daniel Gross, Spark | personal / NFDG | ledger |\n| Suno | Series B | 2024-05-21 | no | Lightspeed | NFDG | ledger |\n| Scale AI | Series F (existing angel) | 2024-05-21 | no | | personal | ledger |\n| Browserbase **P/S** | Seed; AI Grant batch 3 | 2024-06 | no | | AI Grant | ledger |\n| EvolutionaryScale **P/S** | Seed ($142M) | 2024-06-25 | co-led | Lux, Amazon, NVentures | NFDG | ledger |\n| Klarity | Series B ($70M) | 2024-06-24 | yes | | NFDG | ledger |\n| SSI | First round ($1B) | 2024-09-04 | no | a16z, Sequoia, DST | NFDG | ledger |\n| Basis | Series A ($34M) | 2024-12-17 | no | Khosla led | NFDG | ledger |\n| XBOW | Series A (co-led); B 2025 | undated | co-led | | NFDG | ledger |\n| Mechanize **P/S** | Seed | 2025-04-17 | no | | personal | ledger |\n| Granola | Series B ($43M); AI Grant batch 4 | 2025-05 | yes | | AI Grant then NFDG | [firm page](../firms/nfdg.md) |\n| Traversal **P/S** | Seed + A | 2025-06-18 | no | Sequoia, Kleiner | NFDG | ledger |\n| AIUC **P/S** | Seed ($15M) | 2025-07-23 | yes | Emergence, Terrain | NFDG | ledger |\n| Juicebox **P/S** | Seed ($6M); AI Grant batch 4 | before 2025-09 | yes | | AI Grant then NFDG | [firm page](../firms/nfdg.md) |\n| Inception **P/S** | named backer; AI Grant batch 4 | undated | no | | personal + AI Grant | ledger; [src](https://aigrant.com/) |\n| NewLimit, Medra | B / A | 2025 | no | | NFDG | ledger |\n\n**AI Grant batches (all pre-seed/seed, $250K each; [src](https://aigrant.com/)).** Names not already in the table:\n- Batch 1: Replicate, Animato, Lexica.art, Minion.ai, Recraft, Flair, ValueBase, WOMBO, Poly Corp, Sieve, Sameday, Play.ht, Ghostwrite, BuildShip, Birch, Vizcom, Circle Labs, Samaya AI, Secret Weapons, Pixelcut, AniML, Dust, Forefront.\n- Batch 2: Lindy, Freed, Mathpix, Abstract AI, Julius, Hume AI, Cofactory, Induced, Zeta Labs, Common Sense Machines, Guru AI, Curio, Echo Labs, Reality Defender, Andiron AI, Lightpage, Portola, Espresso AI, Jenni, AutogenAI, Listen Labs, Tutor Intelligence, Akool, Coframe, OpusClip, Kino AI.\n- Batch 3: Antimetal, Matic Robots, Omnivore, Momentic, Reworkd AI, Spark AI, K-Scale Labs, Matrices, Didero, Consensus, Method Security, Yuma AI, Abel, Virtual Staging AI, Symphonic Labs, DayDream Dental, Creatify AI, Womp Labs.\n- Batch 4: Goodfire, Silurian, Pulse AI, InspectMind AI, Hamming, Agency, Artificial Analysis, Pursuit, Graylark, Haize Labs, Sync, Rowan, Vapi, Supertrace AI.\n\n**Aggregator-only, not verified** ([Clera](https://www.getclera.com/investors/nat-friedman)): Character.ai, Reka AI, Retool, Shellworks, Distributed Spectrum, Accord, Zoo, Terraform Industries, Dagger, Airplane, Grafbase, Strange Loop Labs, Glide Apps, Strapi, Humanoid. Aggregators count 85 to 145 investments in total and disagree.\n\n## Hit pattern\n- **Seed cheques that became hot:** Perplexity, Cursor, MatX, Pika, Keen, Chroma, RunPod, Replicate, Granola, Goodfire, ElevenLabs (A), SSI.\n- **Front-run then lead:** AI Grant first, NFDG lead later, in MatX, Pika, Juicebox, Granola and Pulse.\n- **Passes or regrets:** nothing found in public.\n\n## Co-investors\n| Who | Pattern | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Daniel Gross | co-lead on nearly everything from 2022 | [daniel-gross](daniel-gross.md) |\n| Elad Gil | co-led Perplexity seed; together in The Bot Company | ledger |\n| Patrick and John Collison | Keen, The Bot Company; John advises NFDG in run-off | ledger; [firm page](../firms/nfdg.md) |\n| Sequoia | Keen seed, SSI, Traversal | ledger |\n| a16z | co-led ElevenLabs A; SSI | ledger |\n| OpenAI Startup Fund | Cursor seed, Atomic Semi | ledger |\n| Spark Capital (Nabeel Hyatt) | The Bot Company, Generalist | ledger |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Keen (2022):** Carmack credits him with pushing the outside raise; he took the only outside board seat (ledger insight).\n- **AIUC (2025):** one 90-minute pitch, weeks before Meta (ledger insight).\n- **MatX (2023):** AI Grant batch 2 company; he and Gross then led the seed and the fund returned in the 2026 Series B (ledger; [src](https://aigrant.com/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Daniel Gross | co-investor, NFDG and AI Grant co-founder; both now at Meta | 2022 | ledger |\n| Hersh Desai | runs AI Grant and NFDG investing | by 2024 | [src](https://aigrant.com/) |\n| Lenny Bogdonoff | co-runs AI Grant | not found | [src](https://aigrant.com/) |\n| Alexandr Wang | co-leads MSL with him | 2025-07 | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/06/30/mark-zuckerberg-overhauled-meta-ai-org-superintelligence-alexandr-wang/) |\n| Jason Warner | GitHub CTO under him; fellow dev-tools angel | 2018 | ledger |\n| Leopold Aschenbrenner | seed backer of Situational Awareness LP | 2024 | ledger (LP row) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is he writing any personal cheques while at Meta? Nothing found after Mechanize (2025-04).\n- Batch dates for AI Grant are not on the site; the rounds rows are undated.\n- Verify the aggregator-only names (Character.ai, Reka, Retool) against company posts.\n- The eight Stratechery interviews are the largest unmined source on how he and Gross reasoned about deals.\n\n## Sources\n- https://nat.org/\n- https://aigrant.com/\n- https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/nat-friedman\n- https://fortune.com/2025/06/30/mark-zuckerberg-overhauled-meta-ai-org-superintelligence-alexandr-wang/\n- https://stratechery.com/topic/events/interview/nat-friedman-and-daniel-gross/\n- https://www.getclera.com/investors/nat-friedman (aggregator)\n- https://www.hershdesai.com/\n- Ledger rows in `data/rounds.csv` and `data/deals.csv` (each with its own source URL)\n", "person:natasha-lim": "\n# Natasha Lim\n\n## One-line take\nNatasha Lim is a junior seat to watch rather than to pitch today. She is a post-MBA Principal in Radical Ventures' SF office who came out of Warburg Pincus growth equity, and no public round names her yet. [src](https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she could matter:** She sits in Radical's SF office, where Toews and Welch work (Rich Kotite is London-based, per Radical Reads 2026-06-30), at the moment Radical added a growth vehicle with a first close of more than US$1B (Breakouts Fund, 2026-09). Her Warburg Pincus and Goldman TMT background (\"growth and late-stage technology companies\") fits growth-round underwriting more than seed sourcing. [src](https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/)\n- **Constraint:** She is a Principal about a year in. Radical credits deals to partners on team pages and on investment-post bylines, and her page lists no portfolio companies. A WordPress API search of radical.vc returns only her team page, so she has no essay or Radical Reads byline either. [src](https://radical.vc/wp-json/wp/v2/search?search=Natasha%20Lim)\n- **What she would not do (inference):** Lead or sign off a round on her own. At Radical that authority sits with partners.\n\n## Differential insight\nNo podcast, X account, Substack, essay or interview by her was found. Everything here comes from the firm site, web-archive metadata and aggregators.\n- **Join date is about Oct 2025, not the July 2026 date the site shows now.** The current page shows datePublished 2026-07-24, which is a site-rebuild artifact. The Wayback capture of 2025-10-09 shows the original page with `datePublished 2025-10-02`. [src](http://web.archive.org/cdx/search/cdx?url=radical.vc/team/natasha-lim*&output=txt) This corrects the firm file's \"join date not found\".\n- **Internship-to-hire path:** Aggregator profiles list a Radical \"summer investor\" role in 2024 focused on AI, during an HBS MBA dated 2023–2025 (TheOrg, unverified). The pattern is summer at Radical, finish the MBA in 2025, then join as Principal by October 2025. [src](https://theorg.com/org/harvard-business-school/org-chart/natasha-lim)\n- **Growth, not research, lineage:** \"worked with growth and late-stage technology companies\" (Warburg Pincus, New York). This is unusual on a team that wins through researcher-founders. [src](https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/)\n- **Southeast Asia network:** She is \"Originally from Singapore.\" Aggregators also list Grab strategy and planning (2018) and a Stripes spring analyst role (2019). The Grab and Stripes ties are aggregator only. [src](https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/)\n- **Alumni-network operator:** Her LinkedIn lists Stern Alumni Council (Stern 10 Vice Chair, 2019-07 to 2024-06), Phillips Exeter Young Alumni Council (2022-11 to present) and NYC co-president of Synergist Network (2019-08 to 2023-07). Those are three standing sourcing networks outside AI research circles. [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/natashalim27/)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low for now, worth watching.** She has no named frontier-AI deal, no public writing to read a thesis from, and no lead authority as a Principal. On the other side, Radical can write $10M+ and growth checks, and principals hired straight out of an MBA into an SF growth-heavy office are a classic early-relationship target before promotion. Re-check in 6–12 months for her first byline or board-observer seat, especially on Breakouts deals (Discovery Loop, Etched, Crusoe). [src](https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/)\n\n## Background\n- Phillips Exeter Academy (she serves on its Young Alumni Council) [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/natashalim27/)\n- NYU Stern, B.S. in Business and Political Economy, summa cum laude (year not found) [src](https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/)\n- Grab, strategy and planning, 2018 (aggregator: TheOrg via search snippet; unverified)\n- Stripes Group, spring analyst, 2019 (aggregator; unverified)\n- Goldman Sachs, TMT investment banking (\"IPOs, M&A, and strategic financings\"), ~2019–2021 (dates aggregator) [src](https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/)\n- Warburg Pincus, New York, investor in growth and late-stage tech, ~2021–2023 (dates aggregator) [src](https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/)\n- Love Bonito, Chief of Staff, 2023 (MarketScreener snippet; behind a login and unverified; may be a pre-MBA or summer role)\n- Harvard Business School MBA with distinction, 2023–2025 (dates aggregator) [src](https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/) [src](https://theorg.com/org/harvard-business-school/org-chart/natasha-lim)\n- Radical Ventures, summer investor (AI), 2024 (aggregator), then Principal in SF from ~2025-10 [src](https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/ | Firm bio; no portfolio list (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | none found | No radical.vc articles under her name (WP search, 2026-11-10) |\n| X | none found | The team page's X link goes to the firm account @radicalvcfund, not hers |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/natashalim27/ | Linked from the team page; posts behind a sign-in wall |\n| GitHub | none found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found (searched 2026-11-10).\n\n## How they think\nNothing primary found. No essays, posts or talks. Inferred only from her career: growth-equity and banking underwriting (Warburg, Goldman TMT) rather than technical or research diligence.\n\n## Read these first\nNothing she has authored was found. For context on the seat she sits in:\n- Radical Breakouts Fund launch (2026-09), the growth mandate her background suits: https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/\n- Her team bio: https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No personal X account was located. LinkedIn activity was not readable without signing in.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| none publicly attributed | | | | | [team page](https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/) lists none; no Radical post or press release names her (checked 2026-11-10) |\n\nFirm-level deals made since she joined, none attributed to her: Decart $300M (2026-05, Radical led), Generalist $400M B (2026-06, led), Prime Intellect $130M A (2026-07, led), Discovery Loop and Veeda seeds (2026-08), Mantic $25M seed (2026-09). See `firms/radical-ventures.md`.\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. No deal publicly credits her.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Principal, San Francisco | ~2025-10 (team page created 2025-10-02) | [src](https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/) |\n| Radical Ventures | Summer investor (AI) | 2024 | [aggregator](https://theorg.com/org/radical-ventures/org-chart/natasha-lim) |\n| Warburg Pincus | Investor, growth and late-stage tech, New York | ~2021–2023 | [src](https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/) |\n| Goldman Sachs | TMT investment banker | ~2019–2021 | [src](https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/) |\n| Harvard Business School | MBA with distinction | 2023–2025 | [src](https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/) |\n| NYU Stern | B.S.; Stern 10 Vice Chair on the alumni council, 2019–2024 | 2019 | [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/natashalim27/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Was her title at join in Oct 2025 Principal, or something lower? The Wayback content fetch was blocked.\n- A search-engine summary claimed her LinkedIn posts discuss Radical leading Decart's round and backing Crusoe and Waabi. This is **unverified** (sign-in wall, no URL or quote), so no deal rows were written. Check it directly on LinkedIn.\n- Which Warburg Pincus deals did she work on (2021–23)? Any AI or infra names?\n- Does she work under the Growth team (Shannon, Mehta) or the SF early-stage partners (Toews, Welch)?\n- Confirm the Grab, Stripes and Love Bonito roles and their dates.\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/natasha-lim/ (fetched 2026-11-10; headshot confirmed with alt=\"Natasha Lim\")\n- https://radical.vc/wp-json/wp/v2/search?search=Natasha%20Lim and https://radical.vc/wp-json/wp/v2/team/403\n- http://web.archive.org/cdx/search/cdx?url=radical.vc/team/natasha-lim* (captures 2025-10-09, 2026-01-14, 2026-04-20; the 2025-10 capture's JSON-LD shows datePublished 2025-10-02)\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/natashalim27/ (public view)\n- https://theorg.com/org/harvard-business-school/org-chart/natasha-lim and https://theorg.com/org/radical-ventures/org-chart/natasha-lim (aggregator; the latter now 404)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/\n", "person:naveen-rao": "\n# Naveen Rao\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the repeat AI-compute founder whose companies are funded by the same small circle of backers each cycle. If you want into his next thing, the way in is to be in his current one: Unconventional AI, which raised a $475M seed that is only a first tranche toward $1B.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he has done the AI-hardware-plus-software cycle twice. Nervana (AI accelerators) sold to Intel in 2016. MosaicML (efficient training) sold to Databricks for about $1.3B in 2023 ([src](https://www.databricks.com/company/newsroom/press-releases/databricks-signs-definitive-agreement-acquire-mosaicml-leading-generative-ai-platform)). That record let him raise a $475M seed at a $4.5B valuation for a company about two months old ([src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/); [Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-12-08/ai-computer-startup-hits-4-5-billion-valuation-in-seed-round)).\n- **What constrains him:** his bet is a new physical computing substrate (analog and dynamical systems), not a better GPU. It depends on energy becoming the binding constraint within about 3 years, and on the field accepting porting at the model layer rather than the operations layer ([All-In transcript](https://podcasts.happyscribe.com/all-in-with-chamath-jason-sacks-friedberg/naveen-rao-4d-computing-ai-s-energy-wall-beating-biology), 2026-09).\n- **What he'd never do again:** sell early. He says he sold Nervana to Intel \"way too early\", and Intel later killed Nervana's training chip in favour of Habana ([Synced](https://syncedreview.com/2020/02/26/intel-ai-gm-naveen-rao-leaves-after-habana-chosen-over-nervana/), 2020-02-26). So expect him to keep Unconventional independent and raise in tranches rather than look for an early exit.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The same backers keep coming back (my synthesis across four announcements; no single page says this).** Nervana's first round in 2014 was led by DFJ, with Steve Jurvetson joining the board ([socaltech](https://socaltech.com/nervana_nabs_3_3m_for_machine_learning_hardware/s-0056585.html), 2014-08-21). Data Collective (now DCVC) led the 2015 round, with Lux, Playground, AME and DFJ in it ([VentureBeat](https://venturebeat.com/big-data/deep-learning-startup-nervana-raises-20-5m), 2015-06-04). MosaicML's $37M round in 2021 was \"led by Lux Capital DCVC, Future Ventures\" and also had Playground and AME ([SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/2021/10/13/naveen-raos-startup-mosaicml-exits-stealth-mission-improve-ml-training/)). Unconventional's 2025 seed lists \"participation from Sequoia, Lux Capital, DCVC, Future Ventures, Jeff Bezos\" ([src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/)). Lux and DCVC have been in all three companies since 2015, and Jurvetson (DFJ, then Future Ventures) in all three since 2014. a16z and Lightspeed are the new money.\n- **Regret about the Intel sale.** \"I think I sold the company way too early to Intel.\" (All-In, 2026-09, auto-generated transcript; [src](https://podcasts.happyscribe.com/all-in-with-chamath-jason-sacks-friedberg/naveen-rao-4d-computing-ai-s-energy-wall-beating-biology))\n- **The seed is a tranche, so the round may still be open.** TechCrunch: \"a first installment toward the goal of up to $1 billion for the round\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/09/unconventional-ai-confirms-its-massive-475m-seed-round), 2025-12-09). In October 2025, a16z had \"agreed to lead\" a $1B raise at $5B ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/03/sources-naveen-raos-new-ai-hardware-startup-targets-5b-valuation-with-backing-from-a16z), 2025-10-03). It closed at $4.5B with Lightspeed as co-lead.\n- **The timeline moved up.** The original goal was \"within 5 years to get to 1,000x power efficiency\"; now \"things have gone faster than we anticipated\" (All-In, auto-transcript). He says the first chip \"sent it to the fab in June\" 2026, from a company that in January \"didn't even have a team\".\n- **Energy is the thesis.** \"we're gonna run out of energy pretty fast in like 3 years\" (All-In, auto-transcript). Elsewhere he says energy is \"About 50% of the cost of serving a token\".\n- **A Mosaic claim worth checking.** He says the Mosaic-era business is \"a quarter of the total revenue of Databricks today\" (All-In, auto-transcript). This is unverified and self-reported.\n- **A speed edge from having no legacy.** \"having no baggage actually I think is a true competitive advantage\" ([Sequoia talk mirror](https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/why-the-brain-computes-1-000-000x-more-efficiently-than-a-gpu-unconventional-ais-naveen-rao), 2026-05-06, auto-captions). He also says: \"This is not 10 years away. This is two, three, four years\".\n- **He hires by cold email to academics.** In 2020 he \"emailed Frankle and Carbin out of the blue\" after reading their model-shrinking work, and that became MosaicML ([MIT News](https://news.mit.edu/2024/mosaicml-helps-nonexperts-build-advanced-generative-ai-models-0621), 2024-06-21). Michael Carbin (MIT) is now an Unconventional cofounder ([Lightspeed](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-unconventional-ai-biology-scale-efficiency-for-the-ai-era/)). Sources disagree on Carbin's MosaicML title: MIT calls him a co-founder, while the company listing calls him a founding advisor.\n- **He buys teams.** Unconventional agreed to acquire NeuReality (Israel, inference silicon; CEO Moshe Tanach), with closing expected in Q4 2026. In his words, \"Bringing NeuReality's team into Unconventional accelerates our roadmap significantly\" ([src](https://unconv.ai/blog/unconventional-ai-to-acquire-neureality/), 2026-10-26).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Unconventional AI is the exact THESIS.md shape: a huge seed for a new AI-compute lab. It is also tranched toward $1B, so later tranches may still have room for a ~$20M check. Even if you never get in, Rao is useful as:\n1. A signal for which firms are inside his circle. Lux (Farshchi), DCVC and Future Ventures (Jurvetson) are the repeat backers. a16z (Bornstein, Appenzeller, Raghuram) and Lightspeed (Chahal, Mhatre, Moore) are the new co-leads.\n2. A source of talent: Hanlin Tang, Jonathan Frankle, Michael Carbin, MeeLan Lee, Sara Achour and the NeuReality team.\n\nHe is not an alpha target on the investor side. He is a founder, and his third-party angel record is thin and unverified.\n\n## Background\n- Duke, BS in EE/CS; Brown, PhD in computational neuroscience (around 2011) ([aiwiki](https://aiwiki.ai/wiki/naveen_rao), secondary).\n- About 10 years as a computer architect before that: \"I was a computer architect for 10 years before that.\" ([Sequoia mirror](https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/why-the-brain-computes-1-000-000x-more-efficiently-than-a-gpu-unconventional-ais-naveen-rao)). Roles included Sun and Kealia ([aiwiki](https://aiwiki.ai/wiki/naveen_rao), secondary).\n- Qualcomm, neuromorphic research, where the Nervana founders met ([VentureBeat](https://venturebeat.com/big-data/deep-learning-startup-nervana-raises-20-5m); [Synced](https://syncedreview.com/2020/02/26/intel-ai-gm-naveen-rao-leaves-after-habana-chosen-over-nervana/)).\n- 2014: cofounded Nervana Systems in San Diego with Amir Khosrowshahi (CTO) and Arjun Bansal ([VentureBeat](https://venturebeat.com/big-data/deep-learning-startup-nervana-raises-20-5m)).\n- 2016-08: Intel acquired Nervana. Reported prices run from about $350M to over $408M ([aiwiki](https://aiwiki.ai/wiki/naveen_rao), secondary).\n- 2016-2020: Intel CVP and GM of the AI Platforms Group. He left after Intel chose Habana (about $2B) over Nervana's NNP-T, and Gadi Singer succeeded him ([Synced](https://syncedreview.com/2020/02/26/intel-ai-gm-naveen-rao-leaves-after-habana-chosen-over-nervana/), 2020-02-26).\n- 2021: MosaicML with Hanlin Tang, emerging from stealth with $37M ([SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/2021/10/13/naveen-raos-startup-mosaicml-exits-stealth-mission-improve-ml-training/), 2021-10-13).\n- 2023-06: Databricks agreed to acquire MosaicML for about $1.3B ([Databricks](https://www.databricks.com/company/newsroom/press-releases/databricks-signs-definitive-agreement-acquire-mosaicml-leading-generative-ai-platform)). He then led AI at Databricks.\n- 2025-09: left Databricks for an advisory role ([Bloomberg Law](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/databricks-ai-chief-to-exit-launch-a-new-computer-startup), 2025-09-12).\n- 2025-12-08: launched Unconventional AI ([src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://unconv.ai/ | Company blog, roughly weekly research posts in 2026 (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://unconv.ai/blog/ | Company posts, e.g. \"Analog is dead, long live analog!\" (2026-04-30) |\n| X | https://x.com/NaveenGRao | Handle per a third-party directory ([src](https://siliconvalleyinvestclub.com/companies/unconventional/team/naveen-rao/)); X blocked our fetch, so not verified directly. He announced his Intel exit on Twitter ([Synced](https://syncedreview.com/2020/02/26/intel-ai-gm-naveen-rao-leaves-after-habana-chosen-over-nervana/)) |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | nothing found |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only, see below |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-12-08: a16z, \"The 80-Year Bet\", hosted by Matt Bornstein at NeurIPS. Theme: \"80 years of digital computing may be the wrong substrate for AI\" ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/the-80-year-bet-why-naveen-rao-is-rebuilding-the-computer-from-scratch/))\n- 2026-02: Sourcery, \"$4.5B Brain-Inspired AI Chip Company\" ([src](https://pocketcasts.com/podcast/sourcery/d2d206a0-d020-013c-5ce9-0acc26574db2/45b-brain-inspired-ai-chip-company/9b449107-67c9-4bc8-91b0-b737f04d4a60)). Not read (403).\n- 2026-05-06: Sequoia Capital talk, \"Why the Brain Computes 1,000,000x More Efficiently Than A GPU\". Covers no baggage as an advantage and tape-outs in months ([mirror](https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/why-the-brain-computes-1-000-000x-more-efficiently-than-a-gpu-unconventional-ais-naveen-rao))\n- 2026-09-21: All-In, \"4D Computing, AI's Energy Wall & Beating Biology\". Covers the regret over the Intel sale, the 3-year energy wall, the June tape-out, and 1,000x efficiency in 3.5 years ([transcript](https://podcasts.happyscribe.com/all-in-with-chamath-jason-sacks-friedberg/naveen-rao-4d-computing-ai-s-energy-wall-beating-biology))\n- 2026-09-22: Primary's The Compute 100, hosted by Brian Schechter and Gaby Lorenzi (already in repo rows; [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/podcast/id1896779326))\n- 2025-10: Lux 3rd AI Summit speaker (per `people/grace-isford.md`; [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-3rd-annual-ai-summit-a-canvas-for-creativity))\n- Older: ARK Invest ep84 on Nervana ([src](https://www.ark-invest.com/podcast/ep84-naveen-rao-nervana-systems)); swyx mixtape on PubMedGPT and small LMs ([src](https://mixtape.swyx.io/episodes/ai-small-language-models-and-training-pubmedgpt-naveen-rao)). Neither was read.\n\n## How they think\n- **Thesis:** compute will be limited by energy, not chips. Shrinking transistors no longer buys efficiency, and current computers are about \"10 billion times away\" from the thermodynamic limit (All-In, auto-transcript). So run the network \"on the physics directly\" ([src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/)).\n- **Contrarian view:** the brain does not do matrix math (\"The brain is dynamic. It does not use matrix math to do compute.\"; Sequoia mirror). Lightspeed's memo frames the bet as a market \"scaling on power and cost, not efficiency\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-unconventional-ai-biology-scale-efficiency-for-the-ai-era/)).\n- **Business model:** sell efficiency per contracted watt: \"we're going to monetize that 1,000x better than existing hardware\" (All-In, auto-transcript).\n- **Calls right:** efficient training as a business (MosaicML to Databricks).\n- **Calls wrong (his own words):** selling Nervana too early. Nervana's training chip was later dropped by Intel.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Introducing Unconventional AI\": the thesis, the round and his $10M check ([src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/))\n2. Lightspeed's memo: why the co-lead bought in, and who the cofounders are ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-unconventional-ai-biology-scale-efficiency-for-the-ai-era/))\n3. All-In transcript: timelines, the energy wall and the Intel regret ([src](https://podcasts.happyscribe.com/all-in-with-chamath-jason-sacks-friedberg/naveen-rao-4d-computing-ai-s-energy-wall-beating-biology))\n4. \"Analog is dead, long live analog!\" (unconv.ai, 2026-04-30) and \"How to improve AI energy efficiency by 1000x\" (2026-05-07) ([index](https://unconv.ai/))\n5. NeuReality acquisition post: how he builds teams through M&A ([src](https://unconv.ai/blog/unconventional-ai-to-acquire-neureality/))\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked directly, because X blocked our fetch. Press quotes his X posts, e.g. the goal of a computer \"as efficient as biology\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/09/unconventional-ai-confirms-its-massive-475m-seed-round)). The people who amplify him that we can see are his investors' media arms: a16z (Bornstein), Sequoia's channel, Primary's Compute 100, and Lux's AI Summit.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Unconventional AI (own company) | Seed $475M at $4.5B; $10M personal | 2025-12-08 | no (founder check) | Lightspeed and a16z (leads); Sequoia, Lux, DCVC, Future Ventures, Jeff Bezos | [src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/) |\n\nNo third-party angel check verified. CB Insights reportedly lists 6 investments for a \"Naveen Rao\", including Topos Bio (seed, 2026-01) and a Cleanlab exit. These came only through search snippets and may refer to a different person, so they are recorded as `needs-id` leads.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Unconventional AI seed.** Bloomberg (2025-09-12) reported he was leaving Databricks, which \"is planning to invest in his new startup\". By 2025-10-03, a16z had agreed to lead a $1B round at $5B, with Lightspeed and Lux participating ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/03/sources-naveen-raos-new-ai-hardware-startup-targets-5b-valuation-with-backing-from-a16z)). It closed on 2025-12-08 at $475M and $4.5B, co-led by Lightspeed and a16z, as the first tranche toward $1B.\n  - **Timing:** a16z is the first firm publicly tied to the round. Lux was named in October too, the repeat backer.\n  - **Sequoia's role (this answers an open question in `people/matt-bornstein.md`):** the company post lists Sequoia as a participant, not a lead ([src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/)).\n  - **Databricks:** Bloomberg Law said it planned to invest, but neither the December post nor TechCrunch lists it, so this conflicts and is unresolved.\n  - **Who led on each side:** the a16z partners were Bornstein, Appenzeller and Raghuram ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/)); Lightspeed's were Chahal, Mhatre and Moore ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-unconventional-ai-biology-scale-efficiency-for-the-ai-era/)).\n- **MosaicML.** Founded after he cold-emailed Frankle and Carbin in 2020 ([MIT News](https://news.mit.edu/2024/mosaicml-helps-nonexperts-build-advanced-generative-ai-models-0621)). Lux led the $37M debut; Shahin Farshchi's House bio credits him with it (see `people/shahin-farshchi.md`).\n- **Nervana.** DFJ led the $3.3M Series A in 2014, with Jurvetson on the board ([socaltech](https://socaltech.com/nervana_nabs_3_3m_for_machine_learning_hardware/s-0056585.html)). Data Collective led the $20.5M round in 2015 ([VentureBeat](https://venturebeat.com/big-data/deep-learning-startup-nervana-raises-20-5m)). Intel acquired it in 2016.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hanlin Tang | Nervana colleague; MosaicML cofounder and CTO | 2016 or earlier | [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/2021/10/13/naveen-raos-startup-mosaicml-exits-stealth-mission-improve-ml-training/) |\n| Michael Carbin (MIT) | MosaicML founding advisor or co-founder (sources differ); Unconventional cofounder | 2020 | [MIT News](https://news.mit.edu/2024/mosaicml-helps-nonexperts-build-advanced-generative-ai-models-0621), [Lightspeed](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-unconventional-ai-biology-scale-efficiency-for-the-ai-era/) |\n| Jonathan Frankle | MosaicML chief scientist | 2020 | [MIT News](https://news.mit.edu/2024/mosaicml-helps-nonexperts-build-advanced-generative-ai-models-0621) |\n| MeeLan Lee, Sara Achour | Unconventional cofounders | 2025 | [Lightspeed](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-unconventional-ai-biology-scale-efficiency-for-the-ai-era/) |\n| Amir Khosrowshahi, Arjun Bansal | Nervana cofounders (from Qualcomm) | 2014 | [VentureBeat](https://venturebeat.com/big-data/deep-learning-startup-nervana-raises-20-5m) |\n| Steve Jurvetson (DFJ, then Future Ventures) | Nervana board (2014); Future Ventures in MosaicML and Unconventional | 2014 | [socaltech](https://socaltech.com/nervana_nabs_3_3m_for_machine_learning_hardware/s-0056585.html), [src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/) |\n| Shahin Farshchi / Lux Capital | Lux in Nervana 2015, led MosaicML 2021, in Unconventional 2025 | 2015 | [VentureBeat](https://venturebeat.com/big-data/deep-learning-startup-nervana-raises-20-5m), [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/2021/10/13/naveen-raos-startup-mosaicml-exits-stealth-mission-improve-ml-training/) |\n| DCVC (Data Collective) | Led Nervana 2015; in MosaicML and Unconventional | 2015 | same |\n| a16z: Bornstein, Appenzeller, Raghuram | Co-led Unconventional seed | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/) |\n| Lightspeed: Chahal, Mhatre, Moore | Co-led Unconventional seed | 2025 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-unconventional-ai-biology-scale-efficiency-for-the-ai-era/) |\n| Sequoia (Konstantine Buhler) | Seed participant; Buhler is partner of record | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/unconventional-ai/) |\n| Jeff Bezos | Seed participant | 2025 | [src](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/) |\n| Databricks | Acquirer of MosaicML; he ran AI there; advisor after 2025-09 | 2023 | [Bloomberg Law](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/databricks-ai-chief-to-exit-launch-a-new-computer-startup) |\n| Moshe Tanach / NeuReality | Acquisition target; team joining | 2026-10 | [src](https://unconv.ai/blog/unconventional-ai-to-acquire-neureality/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is the $1B tranche still open, and who is in the later installments? This decides whether a ~$20M check can get in.\n- Did Databricks actually invest? Bloomberg Law said it planned to; later lists omit it.\n- Is he an LP in any fund (Lux, DCVC, Future Ventures)? Nothing found. No `-lp.csv` was written.\n- Verify his angel portfolio (Topos Bio, Cleanlab) as this Naveen Rao.\n- Read the Sourcery and ARK transcripts for how the Nervana and MosaicML deals came together.\n- NeuReality deal price and NeuReality's investors.\n\n## Sources\n- https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/\n- https://unconv.ai/\n- https://unconv.ai/blog/unconventional-ai-to-acquire-neureality/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-unconventional-ai-biology-scale-efficiency-for-the-ai-era/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/the-80-year-bet-why-naveen-rao-is-rebuilding-the-computer-from-scratch/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/unconventional-ai/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/03/sources-naveen-raos-new-ai-hardware-startup-targets-5b-valuation-with-backing-from-a16z\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/09/unconventional-ai-confirms-its-massive-475m-seed-round\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-12-08/ai-computer-startup-hits-4-5-billion-valuation-in-seed-round\n- https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/databricks-ai-chief-to-exit-launch-a-new-computer-startup\n- https://podcasts.happyscribe.com/all-in-with-chamath-jason-sacks-friedberg/naveen-rao-4d-computing-ai-s-energy-wall-beating-biology\n- https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/why-the-brain-computes-1-000-000x-more-efficiently-than-a-gpu-unconventional-ais-naveen-rao\n- https://www.databricks.com/company/newsroom/press-releases/databricks-signs-definitive-agreement-acquire-mosaicml-leading-generative-ai-platform\n- https://siliconangle.com/2021/10/13/naveen-raos-startup-mosaicml-exits-stealth-mission-improve-ml-training/\n- https://news.mit.edu/2024/mosaicml-helps-nonexperts-build-advanced-generative-ai-models-0621\n- https://socaltech.com/nervana_nabs_3_3m_for_machine_learning_hardware/s-0056585.html\n- https://venturebeat.com/big-data/deep-learning-startup-nervana-raises-20-5m\n- https://syncedreview.com/2020/02/26/intel-ai-gm-naveen-rao-leaves-after-habana-chosen-over-nervana/\n- https://aiwiki.ai/wiki/naveen_rao (secondary)\n- https://siliconvalleyinvestclub.com/companies/unconventional/team/naveen-rao/ (aggregator, X handle only)\n", "person:neil-mehta": "\n# Neil Mehta\n\n## One-line take\nThe person behind Greenoaks' giant single-name AI bets (Scale 2021, Anthropic 2026). He matters to the owner as a later-round buyer and as a quiet individual LP in seed managers, not as a seed peer.\n\n## Crux\n**Why he wins:** extreme concentration and speed. He put 40% of a ~$50M first fund into Coupang [src](https://colossus.com/?p=391499), and he can commit $500M in half an hour (Rippling, SVB weekend) [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419). **What constrains him:** price and a nine-person team covering ~$15B. He came to AI labs late and at peak valuations (Anthropic entry at $380B). **What he'd never do:** spray small checks, or back a company because of a single technical breakthrough. He says he judges labs by \"the laws of great businesses\" [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He was publicly skeptical of model economics in April 2025, then bought Anthropic at the top.** On capex and competition he said \"there seems to be like a pretty fast catch-up,\" and he hedged with \"and by the way, I've been wrong.\" [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419) (~49:00, auto-transcript). About 10 months later Greenoaks entered at the $380B Series G and then led the $965B Series H.\n- **He calls the Musk miss the firm's biggest mistake:** \"the biggest mistake we've ever made\" [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419) (~34:15). Mentors had cast Musk as a micromanager and fast firer. Mehta now treats those traits as positives and never outsources primary diligence.\n- **He commits fast:** \"It took us about 30 minutes to agree to $500 million\" (Rippling, March 2023) [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419).\n- **Pricing rule:** he pays up only with \"differentiated insight on why we are willing to pay that\" [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419) (~54:16).\n- **Kravis backed the first fund:** \"It's like, I'm in.\" That was Kravis committing after one meeting at KKR, per Mehta [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419) (~37:05).\n- **Hidden LP link:** he is an individual LP in Abstract Fund I, Ramtin Naimi's $100M seed fund, among \"another set of individuals\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/).\n- **Team scale:** \"we only have 55 companies across 15 billion of AUM\" [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419). This is why deal credit almost always says \"Greenoaks\" and not a person.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** He writes growth checks into frontier labs at $380B+, not $20M into a $600M seed. Two things matter to the owner: (1) Greenoaks is the likely next-round buyer for AI infra and robotics names, and it re-ups (Sierra, Mytra, Windsurf). (2) Mehta personally LPs into seed managers (Abstract), so he is a possible individual LP for someone like the owner. That link is unconfirmed beyond Abstract.\n\n## Background\n- College: met co-founder Benny Peretz there. The school is not named in the sources [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419).\n- D.E. Shaw: worked on software companies in Los Angeles, then moved to Hong Kong around 2007 for special situations (Chinese and Macau real estate, distressed debt). Left at the end of 2010 [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419).\n- 2012: co-founded Greenoaks with Peretz at age 27 [src](https://colossus.com/?p=391499). Early backers included Henry Kravis and MITIMCo (see firms/greenoaks.md).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.greenoaks.com | Firm home page only; no bio or team page |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No verified account found (search budget exhausted, not exhaustively checked) |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest: [Invest Like the Best EP.419](https://colossus.com/?p=391499), [transcript](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-04-15: Invest Like the Best EP.419, \"Finding Future S&P 500 Companies\" [src](https://colossus.com/?p=391499), [auto-transcript](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419). Highlights: AI skepticism alongside an admission he could be wrong; the Musk mistake; Rippling $500M in 30 minutes; Carvana averaging down from ~$50 to ~$5; Yuri Milner \"broke that mental model\"; Moritz on transparency.\n- 2025-05: Buyers and Builders, \"never outsource DD.\" Known only from a summary; the page returned 403 [src](https://ppacc.player.fm/series/holdco-builders/what-holdco-investors-can-learn-from-neil-mehta-never-outsource-dd).\n\n## How they think\n- **Compounding filter:** keeps an S&P 500 list on his desk and asks which companies will join it (blog summary, low confidence) [src](https://www.antoinebuteau.com/lessons-from-neil-mehta/).\n- **One question for a company's people:** \"are your best days ahead of you or behind you\" [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419).\n- **AI:** judges impact on customers, not benchmarks. Contrarian on model capex in 2025, then bought the leading lab anyway. He was early on AI infra at growth stage (Scale 2021).\n- **Calls:** right on Coupang, Wiz and Carvana (averaged down). Wrong on Musk, and he passed on Carvana during COVID before buying [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419).\n\n## Read these first\n- Invest Like the Best EP.419 transcript, the most complete source on his thinking [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419).\n- Anthropic Series H announcement, his framing of the lab bet [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h).\n- Scale AI Series E post, his 2021 AI-infra thesis [src](https://scale.com/blog/series-e).\n- Colossus profile of Ramtin Naimi, the evidence for his LP role [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public social presence. He surfaces only through podcasts and company press releases.\n\n## Portfolio\nThese are AI deals where Mehta is **personally named**. Nothing found on board seats: no AI board seat surfaced.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Scale AI | Series E ($325M at $7.3B) | 2021-04-13 | co-led | Dragoneer, Tiger Global (co-leads); Coatue, Index, Founders Fund, YC, Wellington, Durable | [src](https://scale.com/blog/series-e) |\n| Anthropic | Series H ($65B, $965B post) | 2026-05-28 | yes (one of 4 leads) | Altimeter, Dragoneer, Sequoia (leads); Coatue, D1, GIC, ICONIQ, Capital Group, XN | [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h) |\n\n**Timing.** At Scale he came in at the E, as a new investor; Index, Founders Fund and YC are listed as existing investors [src](https://scale.com/blog/series-e). At Anthropic he arrived late (not in the Series F; first in at the G in Feb 2026) and doubled down as a lead 3.5 months later.\n\n**Firm deals with no person named** (in data/rows/greenoaks-portfolio-deals.csv; not attributed to Mehta): SSI (B, ~$500M, 2025), Sierra (lead twice, 2024 and 2025), The Bot Company, Mytra, Windsurf/Exafunction, Databricks, Simile, Judgment Labs, Glow, Applied Compute, Mind Robotics and Airbound. I checked the primary announcements for Sierra (2024 and 2025), Simile, Judgment, Applied Compute, Glow and Mytra: none names a Greenoaks person.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Coupang:** 40% of the first fund; about $1B in total across five led rounds over ~10 years; kept buying after the IPO [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419).\n- **Rippling (non-AI):** Parker Conrad called during the SVB collapse, and Greenoaks agreed to $500M in ~30 minutes so payroll cleared that Monday [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419).\n- **Anthropic:** entered at the Series G (Feb 2026, $380B), then co-led the Series H with the words \"We are honored to deepen our partnership\" [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h). A $692M \"Prime Radiant Founders LP\" filed four weeks later may be the co-invest vehicle (unconfirmed; see firms/greenoaks.md).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Benny Peretz | Greenoaks co-founder; met in college | 2012 | [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419) |\n| Henry Kravis | Early Greenoaks investor | 2012 | [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419) |\n| Abstract (Ramtin Naimi) | Mehta is an LP in Fund I | 2018-2019 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Yuri Milner | Influence | | [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419) |\n| Mike Moritz | Influence and adviser | | [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419) |\n| Thrive Capital (Josh Kushner) | Repeat co-investor at firm level (Sierra, Physical Intelligence) | 2024 | see firms/greenoaks.md |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is Prime Radiant Founders LP an Anthropic SPV, and who are its 15 investors?\n- Did Mehta personally lead SSI or Sierra? Press credits only the firm.\n- Which other seed funds does he LP in, beyond Abstract?\n- Does he hold any board seats (Coupang, Rippling, AI names)?\n- The transcript is auto-generated, so quotes should be spot-checked against the audio.\n\n## Sources\n- https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/neil-mehta-finding-future-sp-500-companies-invest-like-the-best-ep419\n- https://colossus.com/?p=391499\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-30-billion-series-g-funding-380-billion-post-money-valuation\n- https://scale.com/blog/series-e\n- https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/\n- https://www.antoinebuteau.com/lessons-from-neil-mehta/ (summary)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/04/bret-taylors-sierra-raises-350m-at-a-10b-valuation\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=675877\n", "person:nico-caprez": "\n# Nico Caprez\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. Almost everything here comes from one paywalled profile by Phoebe Liu in The Information ([original, not read](https://www.theinformation.com/articles/jensen-huangs-son-in-law-became-one-trusted-executives)), as summarised by AI Weekly (2026-10-05) and Dealroom (2026-10-06). Those rows are tagged `source_type: summary`, `confidence: low`. Caprez declined to be interviewed for that profile (Dealroom). The only first-person source is his self-written Milken Global Conference 2026 speaker bio, which Milken says it does not verify. I mapped it to `firm-site` as the closest source type. Sources use \"he\". **Lens note:** the existing lead row from `vishal-bhagwati` rated him `medium`. This profile rates him `low`; see \"Fit with my lens\".*\n\n## One-line take\nCaprez is the one fast-promoted NVIDIA capital deployer with a name on deals. Those deals are credit backstops, GPU-backed loans and land-and-power stakes for the clouds that buy NVIDIA chips, not equity in labs. Follow him to understand how NVIDIA finances its own demand, not as a route into seed rounds.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He sits where NVIDIA's balance sheet meets its biggest customers. He \"manages Nvidia's multibillion-dollar relationships with neoclouds and other data-center operators\" and co-leads the Cloud Partner program with VP Raj Mirpuri ([Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/)). He also has a direct line to the CEO: he married Madison Huang in late September 2026, and AI Weekly reports The Information calling him Huang's \"consigliere\" ([AI Weekly](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/nvidia-promotes-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-to-neocloud-vp)).\n- **What he does.** He turns NVIDIA's credit into chip demand. Dealroom says NVIDIA is increasingly \"acting as lender and backstop for the neoclouds that buy its chips\" ([Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/)).\n- **What constrains him.** First, the counterparties: established neoclouds such as Nebius pushed back, and NVIDIA's AI Compute Partnership is being restructured as a result. Second, NVIDIA's own credit: its CDS spreads have roughly doubled in recent months ([Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/)). Third, the CEO: pricing on big NVIDIA deals reportedly sits with Huang ([firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md)).\n- **What he'd never do (inferred).** There is no source of him leading or joining an equity round in a lab or startup. His mandate is infrastructure supply: clouds, power and land. A seed check is outside it.\n\n## Differential insight\nAll of these rest on one paywalled article read through two summaries, except where marked. Treat them as reported, not confirmed.\n\n- **His first named deal predates his seniority.** Dealroom says he \"helped expand Blackstone's GPU-backed loan facility for CoreWeave\" from $2.3B to $7.5B ([Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/)). CoreWeave announced that $7.5B facility on **2024-05-17** and called it a follow-on to its August 2023 $2.3B facility, also led by Blackstone and Magnetar ([CoreWeave IR](https://investors.coreweave.com/news/news-details/2024/CoreWeave-Secures-7-5-Billion-Debt-Financing-Facility-led-by-Blackstone-and-Magnetar/default.aspx)). Matching the figures to that release is my inference. If it holds, he worked on a landmark GPU-debt deal a few months after joining as a corporate development *manager*. The promotions came after the dealmaking.\n- **The access line.** Brookfield's Sikander Rashid relays what Huang told him: \"If you want something done, talk to Nico.\" ([AI Weekly](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/nvidia-promotes-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-to-neocloud-vp)). The chain is Huang to Rashid to The Information to AI Weekly. It is not a direct Huang quote.\n- **A Brookfield link (my inference).** Rashid is Brookfield's Global Head of AI Infrastructure. Brookfield is one of the six partners in NVIDIA's August 2026 plan for $500B+ of compute financing, alongside Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Goldman Sachs and KKR ([Simpson Thacher, 2026-08-12](https://www.stblaw.com/about-us/news/view/2026/08/12/blackstone-partners-with-nvidia-in-nvidia-s-announced-partnerships-to-establish-platforms-to-finance-over-$500-billion-in-nvidia-compute)). Caprez \"worked on\" that plan ([Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/)). So the person vouching for him is a counterparty on his biggest framework.\n- **Firmus is a revenue-share structure, not an equity check.** In June 2026 he led credit support for about 170,000 Grace Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips \"in exchange for a cut of future cloud revenue\" ([AI Weekly](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/nvidia-promotes-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-to-neocloud-vp)).\n- **The flagship deal hit trouble within four months.** Firmus pulled its A$5.5B IPO on 2026-10-09. The listing price implied US$30.6B, nearly triple the US$10.5B of the August round, and investors balked at escrow terms ([NST/Reuters](https://www.nst.com.my/amp/business/corporate/2026/10/1551088/nvidia-backed-firmus-scraps-us5bil-australian-ipo-poor-demand)). By 2026-10-21, \"Nvidia is in talks to invest hundreds of millions of dollars in Firmus\" ([AI Weekly](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/nvidia-eyes-hundreds-of-millions-in-firmus-after-5b-ipo-pulled), summarising The Information). That is four months from credit backstop to a possible rescue round on his desk. No source names him on the new talks.\n- **His own bio plays down the finance role.** His Milken bio says he leads strategy \"to scale AI infrastructure across cloud providers, enterprises, and strategic partners\", and says corporate development meant \"supporting strategic investments\" ([Milken](https://milkeninstitute.org/events/global-conference-2026/speakers/nico-caprez)). The bio never mentions credit, backstops or financing. The lender role is The Information's framing.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low** (2026-11-10). This departs from the `medium` on the vishal-bhagwati lead row. He signs credit support, loan facilities and land-and-power stakes, not seed or Series A equity, and he is named on **zero equity rounds**. So the \"seed vs later\" question doesn't apply to him: every deal he is named on is infrastructure finance for companies that already exist and already buy chips. The deals that matter to my thesis are only indirect. The Firmus and CoreWeave structures are how NVIDIA lines up compute supply for labs. A new lab that needs a GPU cloud with NVIDIA credit behind it (for example, a $600M seed that buys capacity) may end up dealing with his desk on the compute side. He would not be the one taking the equity. He would rise to **medium** only if a source puts him on a lab or chip equity round, or shows his desk tying compute backstops to lab customers.\n\n## Background\n- Former Swiss pro ski racer; age 35 as of October 2026 ([Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/)).\n- BSc in Banking and Finance, University of Zurich; MBA, London Business School ([Milken](https://milkeninstitute.org/events/global-conference-2026/speakers/nico-caprez)). No dates given.\n- BCG Zurich, early career, advising corporates and governments ([Milken](https://milkeninstitute.org/events/global-conference-2026/speakers/nico-caprez); [Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/)).\n- Investment analyst at PSquared Asset Management (Zurich), covering tech in public and private markets ([Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/); [Milken](https://milkeninstitute.org/events/global-conference-2026/speakers/nico-caprez)). Dates not given.\n- NVIDIA, early 2024: corporate development manager, recruited with help from Madison Huang. Later director (date not sourced). Promoted to VP Global AI Infrastructure Growth in January 2026, skipping senior director ([Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/)). Manager to VP took about two years.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | not searched in depth (budget) |\n| LinkedIn | not located | likely exists; not verified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| Conference bio | [Milken Global Conference 2026](https://milkeninstitute.org/events/global-conference-2026/speakers/nico-caprez) | self-provided bio and headshot |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-05-05 — Milken Institute Global Conference — panel \"The Productivity Shock: Is AI Rewriting the Global Growth Model?\" — [link](https://milkeninstitute.org/events/global-conference-2026/speakers/nico-caprez). Recording not reviewed; nothing quoted from it.\n\n## How they think\n- No first-person views found. He declined The Information's interview, and the Milken panel was not reviewed.\n- His self-description is ecosystem expansion and \"large-scale AI platform deployment\" ([Milken](https://milkeninstitute.org/events/global-conference-2026/speakers/nico-caprez)). He frames the job as growth for NVIDIA's platform, not as investing.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [AI Weekly, 2026-10-05](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/nvidia-promotes-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-to-neocloud-vp): the deal list, the Rashid quote and the \"consigliere\" framing.\n2. [Dealroom, 2026-10-06](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/): career path, CoreWeave figures, Nebius pushback and CDS spreads.\n3. [Milken speaker bio](https://milkeninstitute.org/events/global-conference-2026/speakers/nico-caprez): his own framing of the role.\n4. [The Information original](https://www.theinformation.com/articles/jensen-huangs-son-in-law-became-one-trusted-executives) (paywalled, not read).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Firmus | Debt (credit support for ~170k Grace Blackwell / Vera Rubin chips, possibly >$15B, for a share of future cloud revenue) | 2026-06 | yes (led for NVIDIA) | none named | [AI Weekly](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/nvidia-promotes-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-to-neocloud-vp), [Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/) |\n| CoreWeave | Debt ($7.5B GPU-backed facility, up from $2.3B) | 2024-05-17 (date matched by me) | no (helped) | Blackstone (lead); Magnetar (co-lead); Coatue; Carlyle; CDPQ; DigitalBridge Credit; BlackRock funds; Eldridge; Great Elm | [Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/), [CoreWeave IR](https://investors.coreweave.com/news/news-details/2024/CoreWeave-Secures-7-5-Billion-Debt-Financing-Facility-led-by-Blackstone-and-Magnetar/default.aspx) |\n| Two unnamed US land-and-power developers | Strategic (minority stakes) | summer 2026 | yes (led for NVIDIA) | not named | [AI Weekly](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/nvidia-promotes-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-to-neocloud-vp) (logged as needs-id leads, not deal rows) |\n| $500B+ compute-financing platforms (Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, KKR) | Not a round: MOUs, subject to final agreements | 2026-08 | worked on | six partners | [Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/), [Simpson Thacher](https://www.stblaw.com/about-us/news/view/2026/08/12/blackstone-partners-with-nvidia-in-nvidia-s-announced-partnerships-to-establish-platforms-to-finance-over-$500-billion-in-nvidia-compute) |\n\n**Seed vs later:** every deal above is late-stage infrastructure finance. Firmus had already raised a US$505M strategic round led by Coatue (April 2026), and CoreWeave was a multi-billion-dollar GPU cloud. Caprez is named on no seed or early equity round.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Firmus (June 2026).** NVIDIA provided credit support so Firmus could buy about 170,000 GB/VR chips. In return NVIDIA takes a share of future cloud revenue, and Caprez led it ([AI Weekly](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/nvidia-promotes-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-to-neocloud-vp)). NVIDIA was already a Firmus equity holder from the April and August 2026 rounds ([ledger](../sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md)). Firmus later reserved about half its IPO for existing holders including NVIDIA and Blackstone, pulled the IPO on 2026-10-09, and by 2026-10-21 was in talks for hundreds of millions more from NVIDIA (see Differential insight).\n- **CoreWeave facility.** He is credited with helping; his specific role is not described.\n- Nothing found on intro paths for any deal.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NVIDIA | Corp dev manager -> director -> VP Global AI Infrastructure Growth | early 2024 | [Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/) |\n| Jensen Huang | Son-in-law; reportedly his \"consigliere\" | 2026-09 (marriage) | [AI Weekly](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/nvidia-promotes-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-to-neocloud-vp) |\n| Madison Huang | Spouse (married late September 2026); helped recruit him to NVIDIA | ~2024 | [Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/) |\n| Raj Mirpuri | Co-leads NVIDIA's Cloud Partner program with him | not dated | [Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/) |\n| Sikander Rashid (Brookfield) | Counterparty; relayed Huang's \"talk to Nico\" line | not dated | [AI Weekly](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/nvidia-promotes-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-to-neocloud-vp) |\n| Blackstone | Counterparty: CoreWeave facility; $500B plan | 2024 | [Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/) |\n| Firmus | Led NVIDIA credit support | 2026-06 | [AI Weekly](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/nvidia-promotes-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-to-neocloud-vp) |\n| BCG (Zurich) | Consultant | before NVIDIA | [Milken](https://milkeninstitute.org/events/global-conference-2026/speakers/nico-caprez) |\n| PSquared Asset Management | Investment analyst | before NVIDIA | [Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/) |\n\n**How a founder reaches him.** There is no public channel: no X, blog or podcast, and he declined press. The routes that are sourced all run through infrastructure counterparties: Brookfield (Rashid), Blackstone, and the Cloud Partner program he co-leads with Raj Mirpuri. For a lab founder, the realistic path is through the GPU cloud or the infra financier on their compute deal, or by taking a seat in NVIDIA's Cloud Partner channel. A founder who wants equity should go to NVentures (Siddeek) or corporate development (Bhagwati) instead. That is my inference from the deal mix.\n\n## Open questions\n- Who are the two US land-and-power developers? (logged as `needs-id` leads)\n- Is he involved in the post-IPO Firmus investment talks (October 2026)?\n- When was he promoted from manager to director? Does he report to Vishal Bhagwati or to a sales or cloud line?\n- Is his desk behind the AI Compute Partnership restructuring that followed the Nebius pushback?\n- Does his desk tie compute backstops to lab customers (for example, OpenAI's reported $250B guarantee talks)? No source links him to them.\n- For the main session: `firms/nvidia.md` people table should add him (not written here; out of scope for this task).\n- The Milken panel recording may contain his first public views.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.theinformation.com/articles/jensen-huangs-son-in-law-became-one-trusted-executives (paywalled; not read)\n- https://aiweekly.co/alerts/nvidia-promotes-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-to-neocloud-vp (2026-10-05)\n- https://dealroom.co/news/160271-jensen-huangs-son-in-law-nico-caprez-runs-nvidias-push-to-finance-the-ne/ (2026-10-06)\n- https://milkeninstitute.org/events/global-conference-2026/speakers/nico-caprez\n- https://investors.coreweave.com/news/news-details/2024/CoreWeave-Secures-7-5-Billion-Debt-Financing-Facility-led-by-Blackstone-and-Magnetar/default.aspx (2024-05-17)\n- https://www.stblaw.com/about-us/news/view/2026/08/12/blackstone-partners-with-nvidia-in-nvidia-s-announced-partnerships-to-establish-platforms-to-finance-over-$500-billion-in-nvidia-compute (2026-08-12)\n- https://www.nst.com.my/amp/business/corporate/2026/10/1551088/nvidia-backed-firmus-scraps-us5bil-australian-ipo-poor-demand (2026-10-09)\n- https://aiweekly.co/alerts/nvidia-eyes-hundreds-of-millions-in-firmus-after-5b-ipo-pulled (2026-10-21)\n- ../firms/nvidia.md, ../sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md\n", "person:nikhil-suresh": "\n# Nikhil Suresh\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. Research note: this run's web-search budget was used up before I started (0 searches returned results). Everything here comes from fetching known URLs: striker.vc pages, scalingintelligence.stanford.edu, and company sites. X (HTTP 402) and LinkedIn (HTTP 999) were blocked, so there is no podcast, X or Substack material. Treat this profile as a first pass. The bio uses \"he\" [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh).*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the only non-partner investor at Striker, Max Gazor's $165M inception-stage spinout from CRV. He is a former researcher in a Stanford systems/scaling lab and an early Mercor engineer, which puts him one hop from frontier-lab founders while Striker is backing pre-revenue AI labs such as Ricursive, Elorian and Inferact.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he could win.** He combines researcher credibility (Stanford Scaling Intelligence Lab) with talent-marketplace operating experience (he led search at Mercor, which matches AI researchers to labs). That pairing fits a firm whose pitch is backing founding teams at inception. His bio frames his job as helping founders \"build generational founding teams,\" which is recruiting work. [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh)\n- **What constrains him.** He is a junior \"Investor\" on a five-person team (Gazor, Zhan and Lamdan are Partners, plus Marinelli in ops) [src](https://www.striker.vc/team). He has no public deal attribution: Striker's portfolio page credits no team member [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio). His personal track record consists of angel co-investments with unnamed companies.\n- **What he'd never do.** By firm design, post-revenue or growth deals. Every portfolio company is marked \"At Inception\" [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio), and his own disclosed investments are 100% pre-revenue (firm-site disclosure as of 2025-09-01) [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Mercor search lead → talent-graph access.** His bio says he was \"an early engineer leading Mercor's Search product.\" [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh). Mercor's search product matches experts and researchers to AI labs, so he knows how lab talent gets sourced. This is the non-obvious edge for a junior investor.\n- **Lab affiliation not visible on the lab's own site.** The bio calls him an \"AI researcher in the Scaling Intelligence Lab at Stanford\" [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh). As of 2026-11-10 neither the lab's member list nor its publications list names him (the only Suresh is PhD student Tarun Suresh) [src](https://scalingintelligence.stanford.edu/), [src](https://scalingintelligence.stanford.edu/pubs/). So he was likely a short-term or undergrad/masters researcher, or listed under a different name. Unconfirmed.\n- **The lab-to-portfolio adjacency (inference).** The Scaling Intelligence Lab is run by Azalia Mirhoseini [src](https://scalingintelligence.stanford.edu/). Ricursive Intelligence, linked to Mirhoseini and Anna Goldie, is a 2025 Striker inception deal [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio), [src](https://www.ricursive.com/). Striker credits Ricursive to Max Gazor, not Suresh [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor). Whether Suresh helped make the connection is **not sourced**.\n- **Angel co-investor before Striker.** He has \"co-invested in pre-revenue companies alongside Kleiner Perkins, a16z, and Khosla\" [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh). The companies aren't named, and the sector tag is \"AI & Robotics.\"\n- **Mandate is wider than labs.** He works \"with founders at inception on companies in AI applications,\" plus data/ML systems and next-gen software and hardware infra (paraphrase) [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh). So the AI-native automation part of the thesis is in scope, not only labs.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High (on access, not yet on track record).** Striker is the team that inherited CRV's lab franchise (Reflection, Skild, Dyna, Periodic via Gazor and Zhan) and now partners at inception with frontier-AI teams. The deals that matter to me, all firm-level with Suresh's role unconfirmed:\n- **Ricursive Intelligence** (AI for chip design; partnered 2025). Seed $35M led by Sequoia, then a $300M Series A at a $4B valuation led by Lightspeed [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/16/how-ricursive-intelligence-raised-335m-at-a-4b-valuation-in-4-months/).\n- **Elorian AI** (ex-DeepMind visual-reasoning model lab). Striker is named with Menlo and Altimeter as lead backers; $55M total [src](https://elorian.ai/).\n- **Inferact** (company from vLLM's creators; inference compute). Partnered 2025 [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio), [src](https://inferact.ai/).\n- **Edison Scientific** (partnered 2025) [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio).\n\nAs a co-investor for my ~$20M checks into large seeds: Striker writes $5–30M on day zero (firm-site paraphrase) [src](https://www.striker.vc/), which is the same ticket band. That makes him a natural junior counterpart for syndicate intel and early looks. Seven stealth 2026 inception deals sit on the portfolio page [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio). He is the most reachable person who may know what they are.\n\n## Background\n- Serial founder (companies not named) [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh)\n- AI researcher, Scaling Intelligence Lab, Stanford (dates not given) [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh)\n- Early engineer leading Mercor's Search product; the page lists his title as \"Member of Technical Staff\" (dates not given) [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh)\n- Logos on his page: Mercor, SAIL, Amazon, Stanford, MIT. Degrees and dates aren't stated, so education is unconfirmed [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh)\n- Investor at Striker Venture Partners (firm founded 2025) [src](https://www.striker.vc/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh | Firm bio |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/nikhil_suresh2 | Linked from the Striker bio; the fetch was blocked (402), so follower count is unknown as of 2026-11-10 |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/nikhil-suresh2/ | Linked from the Striker bio; the fetch was blocked |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Search was unavailable this run.\n\n## How they think\n- Inception-only, team-first. This is the firm's line (\"Every generational company starts at inception\"; \"You're all in. Why isn't your VC?\") [src](https://www.striker.vc/), and his bio echoes it with the \"generational founding teams\" framing [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh).\n- His own views are not on record. No X posts or writing could be retrieved.\n- Calls right or wrong: none attributable yet.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Striker bio: the only primary source on him [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh)\n2. Striker portfolio: 12 inception deals, seven of them stealth [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio)\n3. Scaling Intelligence Lab: his research home and its talent pool [src](https://scalingintelligence.stanford.edu/)\n4. `firms/crv.md` in this repo, for the CRV-to-Striker split\n\n## Social engagement\nUnknown. X was not fetchable.\n\n## Portfolio\nAll deals below are Striker firm-level. None is attributed to Suresh in any source.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ricursive Intelligence | Inception (Striker); seed $35M led by Sequoia; A $300M led by Lightspeed | partnered 2025; A ~2026-01 | No (seed lead was Sequoia) | Sequoia; Lightspeed; Nvidia | [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/16/how-ricursive-intelligence-raised-335m-at-a-4b-valuation-in-4-months/) |\n| Elorian AI | Pre-seed/seed ($55M total) | partnered 2026; debut 2026-04-09 (Bloomberg, per company site) | Co-lead backer | Menlo; Altimeter; NVIDIA; 49 Palms; Jeff Dean | [src](https://elorian.ai/) |\n| Inferact | Inception | partnered 2025 | unknown | unknown | [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio) |\n| Edison Scientific | Inception | partnered 2025 | unknown | unknown | [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio) |\n| Bay Security | Inception | partnered 2026 | unknown | unknown | [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio) |\n| Stealth x7 | Inception | partnered 2026 | unknown | unknown | [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio) |\n| Angel (unnamed) | pre-revenue | before 2025-09 | No | Kleiner Perkins; a16z; Khosla | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing sourced. Striker's team pages credit Ricursive, Inferact, Edison and Elorian to Max Gazor [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor) and Elorian also to Brian Zhan [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/brian-zhan). Suresh's role in any deal is unknown.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Striker Venture Partners | Investor | ~2025 | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team) |\n| Max Gazor | Colleague (Founder & Partner) | ~2025 | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team) |\n| Brian Zhan | Colleague (Partner; ex-CRV lab sourcer) | ~2025 | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team) |\n| Matan Lamdan | Colleague (Partner) | ~2025 | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team) |\n| Mercor | Former early engineer, led Search | n/a | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh) |\n| Stanford Scaling Intelligence Lab (Azalia Mirhoseini, PI) | Former researcher; not on the lab's current member list | n/a | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh), [src](https://scalingintelligence.stanford.edu/) |\n| Kleiner Perkins, a16z, Khosla | Angel co-investors (deals unnamed) | before 2025-09 | [src](https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did Suresh source or help with Ricursive, given his tie to the Mirhoseini lab? Ask directly.\n- Which of the seven 2026 stealth inception deals did he bring in?\n- When was he at Mercor, how long, and does he keep ties to Mercor's founders?\n- Is Tarun Suresh (a PhD student in the same lab) related? Do not assume.\n- What are his X handle's content and network? Re-fetch when search budget allows.\n- Which angel deals did he do alongside Kleiner, a16z and Khosla?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.striker.vc/team/nikhil-suresh\n- https://www.striker.vc/team\n- https://www.striker.vc/\n- https://www.striker.vc/portfolio\n- https://www.striker.vc/team/max-gazor\n- https://www.striker.vc/team/brian-zhan\n- https://scalingintelligence.stanford.edu/\n- https://scalingintelligence.stanford.edu/pubs/\n- https://www.ricursive.com/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/16/how-ricursive-intelligence-raised-335m-at-a-4b-valuation-in-4-months/\n- https://elorian.ai/\n- https://inferact.ai/\n", "person:nikita-andersson": "\n# Nikita Andersson\n\nThe owner asked about \"Nikita Andreessen\", who \"did Pathfinders\" and \"Fractile\". The best match is **Nikita Andersson**, a London investor at Hummingbird Ventures. Fractile's Companies House filings list her by name as a personal shareholder, and she posted on X about Pathfinder's Series A on 2026-11-02. The name match (\"Andreessen\" sounds like \"Andersson\") is an inference. The Fractile and Pathfinder links are from primary sources (one filing, one post of hers).\n\n## One-line take\nShe is a London Hummingbird investor who put a personal angel cheque of about £5K into Fractile three months after its seed, years before the $1B Series B and the reported $6.5B round. Her own X post places her at Lovable's 2023 partnering in Stockholm.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** she has early access in the UK and Nordics. She was at Lovable's 2023 founder meeting in Stockholm ([X](https://x.com/nikitaandersso3)). She is on Fractile's cap table personally, from the Seed A extension in October 2024 ([CS01 2025](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/filing-history/MzQ3Njc5ODA3MmFkaXF6a2N4/document?format=pdf&download=0)). She is now posting about investing alongside Benchmark in Tendrils, a UK chip startup.\n- **What constrains her:** she is not a partner. Hummingbird's announcements credit the firm, not individuals ([firm file](../firms/hummingbird-ventures.md)). As of 2026-11-10 she is not on Hummingbird's team page ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)), so which vehicle she invests through is unclear.\n- **What she'd never do (inferred, low confidence):** treat AI as purely commercial. Her essays frame compute and chips as questions of sovereignty and power ([Substack](https://nikitaandersson.substack.com/p/sovereignty-as-an-ideology)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **She is a named Fractile shareholder.** The 2025-08-02 confirmation statement lists \"NIKITA ANDERSSON\" with 278 Seed A Preferred shares. She was not on the 2024-08-02 list ([CS01 2025](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/filing-history/MzQ3Njc5ODA3MmFkaXF6a2N4/document?format=pdf&download=0), [CS01 2024](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/filing-history/MzQzMDk4OTI1MWFkaXF6a2N4/document?format=pdf&download=0)). She still holds them on the 2026-08-02 statement, next to Accel, Founders Fund IX, 01 Advisors 03 and Conviction Fund II ([CS01 2026](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/filing-history/MzUzNzc4MjAwOWFkaXF6a2N4/document?format=pdf&download=0)).\n- **When and how much.** Exactly 278 Seed A Preferred shares were allotted on 2024-10-25, with £18 paid per share. That comes to about £5K, and no other holder has exactly 278 shares, so the allotment is hers by inference ([SH01](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/filing-history/MzQ1NDcxNjE1M2FkaXF6a2N4/document?format=pdf&download=0)). This was a small angel extension three months after the $15M seed was announced in July 2024 ([tech.eu](https://tech.eu/2024/07/26/fractile-secures-15m-funding-for-better-hardware-for-ai-inference/)). It is a personal holding: no Hummingbird vehicle is on the cap table.\n- **Lovable origin.** Her pinned X post (2025-12-18) shows photos with Anton Osika and Fabian Hedin \"after partnering, in Stockholm 2023\" ([X](https://x.com/nikitaandersso3)). Hummingbird's portfolio lists Lovable as Pre-Seed/Seed, 2023 ([portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio)).\n- **Pathfinder.** On 2026-11-02 she quote-posted Pathfinder's Series A, saying she \"couldn't be more excited for @amaankahmad + Pathfinder\" ([X](https://x.com/nikitaandersso3)). Pathfinder is a Phoenix-based fintech for Education Savings Account funds. Its $12M Series A was led by Portage, with Reach Capital, Gradient and Stand Together Ventures Lab ([SaaS News](https://thesaasnews.com/news/pathfinder-raises-12m-series-a)). Its $4M seed (2026-03) was led by Gradient and Reach ([Startup Intros](https://startupintros.com/news/2026-03-18-pathfinder-seed)). **No source names her or Hummingbird as a Pathfinder investor.** It is unconfirmed whether she invested, scouted or advised.\n- **Tendrils.** On 2026-11-10 she posted \"Extremely excited to invest alongside @benchmark\" on Tendrils' $30M round ([X](https://x.com/nikitaandersso3)). The vehicle (personal, Hummingbird or something new) is unconfirmed. UK Sovereign AI also invested in Tendrils in November 2026 ([Sovereign AI](https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-tendrils-to-develop-general-purpose-chips)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. She is an early, personal-money holder in Fractile, the frontier UK inference-chip company. She is now on a second UK chip deal (Tendrils) next to Benchmark, and was at Lovable's earliest round. She is a sourcing node for UK chip and AI-app founders, but she is a junior investor (not a partner) and cannot write $20M checks. She is useful as a scout or co-investor relationship, not as a lead partner.\n\n## Background\n- Oxford: history undergraduate. She wrote her 2018 undergraduate thesis on the history of dyslexia in Britain ([Substack](https://nikitaandersson.substack.com/p/cud-do-betty)). She says she has \"two degrees in History and Education\" ([Substack](https://nikitaandersson.substack.com/p/not-the-regular-way)).\n- Harvard Graduate Teaching Fellow (aggregator: search snippet of TheOrg and Success.ai; pages not fetchable, unconfirmed).\n- Hummingbird Ventures, Investor, London, from April 2021 (aggregator, same sources) ([NFX Signal](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/nikita-andersson), [Startup Intros](https://startupintros.com/people/nikita-andersson)). A LinkedIn search title still says Hummingbird Ventures, but she is **not on hummingbird.vc/team as of 2026-11-10** ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)). Whether she has left is unconfirmed.\n- She writes publicly that she is dyslexic ([Substack](https://nikitaandersson.substack.com/p/not-the-regular-way)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | https://nikitaandersson.substack.com (\"Affinities.\") | 6 posts, 2026-09 to 2026-11 (RSS read 2026-11-10) |\n| X | https://x.com/nikitaandersso3 | about 1.6K followers, 839 posts (2026-11-10); bio \"Lifelong learner\", London |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/nikita-andersson-2a1b77165/ | title Hummingbird Ventures (search snippet) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Two LinkedIn search snippets mention an AI-and-healthcare series and a Hummingbird-sponsored Generative AI x Healthcare hackathon at Imperial College London. The underlying pages were not read, so this is unconfirmed.\n\n## How they think\n- **Sovereignty as power:** her 2026-11-07 essay maps the AI supply chain layer by layer and asks who controls the chokepoints ([Substack](https://nikitaandersson.substack.com/p/sovereignty-as-an-ideology)). This matches her UK chip bets (Fractile, Tendrils).\n- **VCs as historians:** investors reason from selective collective memory about winners. She cites Concept and Credo leading ElevenLabs' first round as the kind of story that shapes what is \"legible to capital\" ([Substack](https://nikitaandersson.substack.com/p/venture-capitalists-as-historians)).\n- **Education:** she writes that she has \"always been extremely passionate about education\" (Pathfinder post, [X](https://x.com/nikitaandersso3)). NFX tags her for Education at Seed and Series A (aggregator).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Sovereignty as an ideology](https://nikitaandersson.substack.com/p/sovereignty-as-an-ideology) (2026-11-07): her frame for AI chips and compute.\n- [Venture Capitalists as Historians?](https://nikitaandersson.substack.com/p/venture-capitalists-as-historians) (2026-10-12): how she thinks about pattern-matching in VC.\n- [Not 'The Regular' Way](https://nikitaandersson.substack.com/p/not-the-regular-way) (2026-09-15): personal background.\n\n## Social engagement\nShe congratulates portfolio and network founders: Lovable (pinned post), Pathfinder (Amaan Ahmad), Scape (Melvin Hagberg) and Tendrils (Nils, @nilscmr). She also posts design-hiring calls for @jackandjill_AI (search snippet) ([X](https://x.com/nikitaandersso3)).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Fractile | Seed (Seed A Preferred extension; 278 shares at £18, about £5K personal) | 2024-10-25 | no | Kindred Capital, NATO Innovation Fund, OSE (seed leads); Pat Gelsinger trust (also new on the 2025 list) | [SH01](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/filing-history/MzQ1NDcxNjE1M2FkaXF6a2N4/document?format=pdf&download=0), [CS01 2025](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/filing-history/MzQ3Njc5ODA3MmFkaXF6a2N4/document?format=pdf&download=0) |\n| Lovable | Pre-seed (via Hummingbird; personal role per her own post) | 2023 | unknown | | [X](https://x.com/nikitaandersso3), [portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio) |\n| Tendrils | Undisclosed ($30M led by Benchmark; vehicle unconfirmed) | 2026-11-10 | no | Benchmark; UK Sovereign AI | [X](https://x.com/nikitaandersso3) |\n| Pathfinder | unconfirmed (post only) | 2026-11-02 | n/a | Portage, Reach, Gradient, Stand Together | [X](https://x.com/nikitaandersso3) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Fractile:** she joined the cap table as an individual in the Seed A extension (2024-10-25). The Patrick and Linda Gelsinger Trust also first appears on the 2025 list. Its allotment date was not matched to a specific SH01; Gelsinger's investment was reported in February 2025 by [DCD](https://datacenterdynamics.com/en/news/former-intel-ceo-pat-gelsinger-invests-in-british-ai-chip-startup-fractile)). The intro path is not found.\n- **Lovable:** her post places her with both co-founders in Stockholm in 2023 \"after partnering\". Her exact role on Hummingbird's deal team is not stated.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hummingbird Ventures | at: Investor (current status unconfirmed) | 2021-04 (aggregator) | [NFX](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/nikita-andersson) |\n| Fractile | personal shareholder, 278 Seed A Preferred | 2024-10-25 | [CS01 2025](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/filing-history/MzQ3Njc5ODA3MmFkaXF6a2N4/document?format=pdf&download=0) |\n| Anton Osika, Fabian Hedin (Lovable) | partnered at the 2023 round | 2023 | [X](https://x.com/nikitaandersso3) |\n| Amaan Ahmad (Pathfinder) | publicly supportive; financial tie unconfirmed | 2026 | [X](https://x.com/nikitaandersso3) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Has she left Hummingbird (she is off the team page), and is Tendrils through a new fund or her own money?\n- Did she invest personally in Pathfinder's seed or Series A? Pathfinder is a US company, so check Form D (EDGAR) or ask.\n- Who introduced her to Fractile's founders (Walter Goodwin)? Was it the Oxford network (OSE was a seed co-lead)?\n- Name check: the owner's \"Andreessen\" is assumed to be \"Andersson\". Confirm with the owner.\n\n## Sources\n- Fractile Ltd (Companies House 14143636): [officers](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/officers), [CS01 2024-08-02](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/filing-history/MzQzMDk4OTI1MWFkaXF6a2N4/document?format=pdf&download=0), [CS01 2025-08-02](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/filing-history/MzQ3Njc5ODA3MmFkaXF6a2N4/document?format=pdf&download=0), [CS01 2026-08-02](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/filing-history/MzUzNzc4MjAwOWFkaXF6a2N4/document?format=pdf&download=0), [SH01 2024-10-25](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/filing-history/MzQ1NDcxNjE1M2FkaXF6a2N4/document?format=pdf&download=0)\n- https://x.com/nikitaandersso3 (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://nikitaandersson.substack.com/feed (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.hummingbird.vc/team ; https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://signal.nfx.com/investors/nikita-andersson ; https://startupintros.com/people/nikita-andersson (aggregators)\n- https://thesaasnews.com/news/pathfinder-raises-12m-series-a ; https://startupintros.com/news/2026-03-18-pathfinder-seed\n- https://www.sovereignai.gov.uk/post/sovereign-ai-invests-in-tendrils-to-develop-general-purpose-chips\n", "person:niko-ciminelli": "\n# Niko Ciminelli\n\n## One-line take\nHe is a KV principal with an Israeli deep-tech operator background and corporate-VC training. Nothing public yet ties him to a named deal. Track him early, but don't assume anything yet.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he might win.** He has frontier-tech pattern recognition from both sides of the table. He left a PhD for a Tel Aviv startup and then co-founded an AI startup with an early Mobileye team member ([src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli)). That gives him a likely path to Israeli founders in deep tech and perception.\n- **What constrains him.** At KV, deal credit and lead authority sit with the partners. His Samsung Next role was corporate VC, and the firm page says only that he \"helped lead\" investments ([src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli)). I could not confirm any deal he was named on, at either firm.\n- **What he'd never do.** Nothing found. There are no public statements from him.\n- **Caveat.** This profile rests on one primary source, the KV team page. The web search budget for this run was used up after one query, so podcasts, X and press could not be searched. Everything else is \"nothing found\", not \"nothing exists\".\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Operator before investor.** He left academia for a startup instead of going straight into VC. KV's page says he \"dropped out of his PhD to work at a startup in Tel Aviv\" ([src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli)). source_type firm-site, so non_obvious is low to medium.\n- **Mobileye lineage.** He \"co-founded an AI-startup alongside one of the early team members of Mobileye\" ([src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli)). This is the most useful lead in the profile: the Mobileye alumni network seeds many Israeli AI and perception companies. The co-founder and the startup are not named (status needs-id).\n- **Corporate-VC apprenticeship.** At Samsung Next he \"helped lead investments in frontier technologies, AI, robotics, and cybersecurity\" ([src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli)). The verb is \"helped lead\", not \"led\", so expect a support role on those deals. Samsung Next's current team page does not list him ([src](https://www.samsungnext.com/team)), which fits with him having left.\n- No podcast, X, Substack or talk material found. His LinkedIn exists ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/nikolas-ciminelli-74a049188/)) but was not read.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** His stated focus (frontier tech, infrastructure, AI, robotics, industrial systems) matches THESIS.md's AI-infra and compute interests, and KV writes $10M+ checks into frontier labs. That is the type of firm I want as a co-investor. It is not high because no deal ties him to labs, chips or AI-native automation yet. Upgrade him if a KV deal names him on an AI-infra or robotics round, or if the Mobileye-linked startup turns out to be relevant.\n\n## Background\n- PhD program (institution and field not found), left early ([src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli))\n- Startup in Tel Aviv (company not named; dates not found) ([src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli))\n- Co-founded an AI startup with an early Mobileye team member (name and dates not found) ([src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli))\n- Samsung Next: helped lead frontier tech, AI, robotics and cybersecurity investments (title and dates not found) ([src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli))\n- Khosla Ventures: Principal (join date not found) ([src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli))\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli | KV team page, checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No personal handle on the KV page |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/nikolas-ciminelli-74a049188/ | Linked from the KV page; not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found (web search was unavailable this run)\n\n## How they think\n- Stated interests: \"frontier technologies, infrastructure, AI, robotics, and industrial systems\" ([src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli))\n- Contrarian views: nothing found\n- Calls right or wrong: nothing found\n\n## Read these first\n- The KV team bio (https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli), which is the only primary source found.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| nothing found | | | | | No KV or Samsung Next deal names him in the sources checked |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Khosla Ventures | Principal | unknown | [src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli) |\n| Samsung Next | Former investor (helped lead investments) | unknown | [src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli) |\n| Unnamed early Mobileye team member | AI startup co-founder | unknown | [src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did he join KV, and what was his Samsung Next title (and does that title imply a fast promotion)? LinkedIn would answer this.\n- What was the Mobileye-linked AI startup, and who was his co-founder?\n- Which Samsung Next deals did he work on (frontier, AI, robotics, cyber)?\n- Is he named on any KV AI-infra or robotics announcement since he joined?\n- Which PhD program did he leave, and in what field?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/niko-ciminelli (KV team page, fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.samsungnext.com/team (fetched 2026-11-10; he is not listed)\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/nikolas-ciminelli-74a049188/ (linked, not read)\n", "person:nina-achadjian": "\n# Nina Achadjian\n\n## One-line take\nIndex's SF partner for \"AI meets atoms\" (robotics, hardware-design software, AI biology): she leads seed-to-C rounds, takes boards, and gets into frontier names like Physical Intelligence and Anthropic, but late and as a new name in big rounds, not at the seed.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** long, patient personal relationships that become deals years later. /dev/agents came from a nine-year friendship that began when she brought David Singleton to Armenia [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-operating-system-for-ai-agents-our-investment-in-devagents/). On top of that, Index's ability to write anything from a $5M seed lead (Tofu) to leading a $400M Series C (Chai Discovery) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/tofu-raises-5m-seed-round-to-put-b2b-marketing-on-autopilot-with-ai/) [src](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design).\n- **How she operates:** she presents herself as a \"business builder\" who prepares heavily for boards, not a cheerleader for founders. She says she sat out much of the 2021 transactional term-sheet market [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/odd-lots/nina-achadjian-on-what-it-takes-to-win-in-vc-right-now).\n- **What holds her back:** in frontier labs she has entered as a new name in later, priced rounds. Index was new at Physical Intelligence's $5.6B Series B, about 20 months after Thrive's seed [src](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/robotics-startup-physical-intelligence-valued-at-5-6-billion) [src](https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/). It led Chai Discovery's C after the OpenAI/Thrive/Dimension seed [src](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement).\n- **What she'd never do:** back thin wrappers on foundation models. She calls that market \"a race to the bottom\" on price [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/odd-lots/nina-achadjian-on-what-it-takes-to-win-in-vc-right-now).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **/dev/agents came from a nine-year personal relationship.** \"I first met David Singleton nine years ago in Yerevan, Armenia.\" She invited him to her first Armenia Tech Summit. They stayed in touch over Formula 1, and she sought his advice on investments before he founded the company. Index then led the $56M seed, with CapitalG co-leading [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-operating-system-for-ai-agents-our-investment-in-devagents/) [src](https://siliconangle.com/2024/11/26/new-startup-named-dev-agents-led-ex-google-meta-tech-leaders-raises-56m-ai-agents).\n- **Her Armenian diaspora network is a real sourcing channel.** Before Index she founded HIVE Ventures, which the firm describes as the first seed fund focused on Armenian entrepreneurs [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-achadjian/). ServiceTitan is her flagship board seat, and her bio quotes CEO Ara Mahdessian [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-achadjian/). Whether that relationship came through the Armenian network is not established by any source checked.\n- **She thinks foundation models are consolidating.** \"dust is settling on and consolidating with these players\" (Odd Lots, Dec 2024) [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/odd-lots/nina-achadjian-on-what-it-takes-to-win-in-vc-right-now). That fits a pattern of backing applied physical-AI and AI-for-hardware companies rather than new text-model labs.\n- **She brings in outside technical experts for diligence.** \"we will often bring in also deeply technical experts\" (Odd Lots) [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/odd-lots/nina-achadjian-on-what-it-takes-to-win-in-vc-right-now). That matters for physics-heavy deals such as Quilter and Revel.\n- **She plans her close in advance.** She describes mapping every step from term sheet to signing ahead of time: \"I mapped that all out ahead of time.\" (Odd Lots) [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/odd-lots/nina-achadjian-on-what-it-takes-to-win-in-vc-right-now).\n- **Her Revel deal moved fast.** \"I first met Scott over a Zoom call in late December.\" Index's Series B post went up on 2026-02-26, roughly two months later [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/great-hardware-deserves-great-software-investing-in-revel/).\n- **She decides on founders quickly.** Chai: \"Within just a few minutes of talking to Josh, I knew this was someone we had to get to know.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/transforming-biology-from-science-to-engineering-our-investment-in-chai-discovery/).\n- **She is being positioned as a successor.** Bloomberg Línea (Jan 2026) lists her among the next generation as Index plans partner succession. This comes from an existing row in danny-rimer-insights [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** She is the Index partner on Physical Intelligence, Anthropic and Chai Discovery, and she led the /dev/agents mega-seed, all core THESIS.md names. But on the frontier labs she came in as a new investor at growth prices (PI Series B at $5.6B), not at the seed. Her own early leads are applied: AI for PCB design (Quilter), hardware test software (Revel), AI industrial design (Vizcom) and AI marketing (Tofu). Quilter and Revel are AI-native automation for hardware, which sits close to the owner's \"AI-native automation\" lane. She is useful to me as:\n1. A likely growth-round buyer for robotics and physical-AI seeds I back.\n2. A route to Index's $50M+ seed-lead capacity (/dev/agents $56M seed).\n3. A diaspora and operator network (Armenia, ex-Stripe via Singleton).\n\nShe is not a source of first-money frontier-lab access.\n\n## Background\n- Raised in the Bay Area as a first-generation Armenian-American; Harvard B.A. in government; Michael C. Rockefeller Scholarship [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-achadjian/) [src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/our-new-partner-nina-achadjian).\n- Worked in a baklava bakery in Istanbul, putting a Wall Street job on hold (dates not stated) [src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/our-new-partner-nina-achadjian).\n- High-yield bond trader at Citi in New York; her Index bio says she traded bonds in New York and London (dates not stated) [src](https://pulse2.com/nina-achadjian-named-partner-at-index-ventures/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-achadjian/).\n- Google, FP&A Lead, where she helped build the go-to-market infrastructure for AdSense sales (dates not stated) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-achadjian/).\n- Founded HIVE Ventures, a seed fund for Armenian entrepreneurs (date not stated) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-achadjian/).\n- Index Ventures from about 2017. Promoted to Partner in February 2020, when Mike Volpi announced it; credited with Index's vertical SaaS thesis, its angel program and the \"Oasis by Index\" seed-fund retreat [src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/our-new-partner-nina-achadjian).\n- Named to the Forbes Midas Brink List in 2022 [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-achadjian/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-achadjian/ | Firm profile listing 24 companies (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ | Writes the Index investment posts (/dev/agents, Vizcom, Quilter, Revel, Chai) |\n| X | nothing found | Not verified; search budget ran out |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | Not verified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-12 — Bloomberg Odd Lots — \"Nina Achadjian on What It Takes to Win in VC Right Now\" — https://www.bloomberg.com/news/audio/2024-12-19/odd-lots-nina-achadjian-on-what-it-takes-to-win-in-vc-podcast — The transcript was read via podscripts, a third-party aggregator [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/odd-lots/nina-achadjian-on-what-it-takes-to-win-in-vc-right-now). She lists sourcing as the first job of a VC. She sat out the 2021 market as too \"transactional\". She sees foundation models consolidating and thin wrappers in a race to the bottom on price.\n- 2026-06 — Bloomberg Tech (Ed Ludlow) — interview on AI moving into manufacturing, robotics and industrial systems, and on the SpaceX IPO — https://grafa.com/en/news/australia/index-ventures-achadjian-on-backing-ex-spacex-workers — only a summary page was available; no quotes.\n- Other appearances: \"Building The Future Through Venture\" (RedCircle), and a vertical SaaS episode on Venture Europe. Seen in search results but not read [src](https://redcircle.com/shows/97b7202f-4d5a-4ae6-825f-12fcf6e3464a/ep/4ada62ca-3ed2-4741-a059-2cdded71306e) [src](https://podm8.com/episodes/venture-europe/nina-achadjian-vertical-saas-1I2ENj2t0D).\n\n## How they think\n- **AI is moving into atoms.** Her posts argue that \"great hardware, whether it's a rocket or a robot, depends on software\" (Revel) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/great-hardware-deserves-great-software-investing-in-revel/). Her portfolio follows that line: Physical Intelligence, Quilter, Vizcom and Revel.\n- **Biology is a large market AI hasn't yet reached.** Her Chai post makes that case and calls Chai \"the latest expression of that thesis\", after Index's earlier bets on Cradle and Causaly [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/transforming-biology-from-science-to-engineering-our-investment-in-chai-discovery/).\n- **Foundation models are consolidating, and thin wrappers get commoditized.** (Odd Lots.)\n- **AI should help human creativity, not replace it.** On Vizcom: \"let's preserve the human creativity\" (Odd Lots).\n- **Calls she got right:** ServiceTitan through its 2024 IPO; /dev/agents at a $500M seed valuation. Whether that one worked out is not known as of the sources checked. Physical Intelligence went from $5.6B to about $11B and then to talks near $20B after Index entered [src](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/artificial-intelligence/ai-robotics-lab-in-talks-to-nab-1-billion-at-11-billion-value).\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"The Operating System for AI Agents: Our investment in /dev/agents\" (2024-11): how a decade-long relationship turned into a $56M seed lead [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-operating-system-for-ai-agents-our-investment-in-devagents/).\n2. \"A New Era for Electronics Design: Our Investment in Quilter\" (2025-10): her AI-for-hardware lead and board seat [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-era-for-electronics-design-our-investment-in-quilter/).\n3. \"Transforming Biology From Science To Engineering: Chai Discovery\" (2026-07): her AI-bio thesis [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/transforming-biology-from-science-to-engineering-our-investment-in-chai-discovery/).\n4. \"Great hardware deserves great software: investing in Revel\" (2026-02) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/great-hardware-deserves-great-software-investing-in-revel/).\n5. The Odd Lots transcript (2024-12) for her operating style [src](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/odd-lots/nina-achadjian-on-what-it-takes-to-win-in-vc-right-now).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X account was verified, and the search budget ran out before one could be checked.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI-relevant deals first. Non-AI names on her profile: ServiceTitan, Shopmonkey, Double, Gong, Motive, Persona, Productboard, CodeSignal, Check, Class Companion, DeepScribe, Loop, Monograph, Seso, Subset, Vooma, Transcend [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-achadjian/).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Physical Intelligence | Series B ($600M at $5.6B) | 2025-11-20 | No. CapitalG led; Index was a new investor | Index team: Achadjian + Eryk Dobrushkin. Round: CapitalG, Lux, Thrive, Bezos, T. Rowe Price (new), Bond, Redpoint, Sequoia, OpenAI | [src](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/robotics-startup-physical-intelligence-valued-at-5-6-billion) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/physical-intelligence/) |\n| Anthropic | not stated | not stated | No lead found | Index team: Achadjian + Martin Mignot + Vlad Loktev | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/anthropic/) |\n| /dev/agents | Seed ($56M, ~$500M valuation) | 2024-11-26 | Yes. Index led, CapitalG co-led | Conviction (Sarah Guo); angels Alexandr Wang, Andrej Karpathy, Nikesh Arora. Index team: Achadjian alone (company page) | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2024/11/26/new-startup-named-dev-agents-led-ex-google-meta-tech-leaders-raises-56m-ai-agents) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-operating-system-for-ai-agents-our-investment-in-devagents/) |\n| Chai Discovery | Series C ($400M at $3.8B) | 2026-07-14 | Yes. Index led per BioSpace; Chai's own page names no lead | Index team: Achadjian + Emil Schaefer. Round: Kleiner Perkins, Sequoia, Dimension | [src](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design) [src](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/series-c) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/chai-discovery/) |\n| Quilter | Series B ($25.5M per Index; $25M per press release) | 2025-10-07 | Yes. Led, with a board seat | Index team: Achadjian + Eryk Dobrushkin. Prior backers: Benchmark, Coatue, Root, Lip-Bu Tan | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-era-for-electronics-design-our-investment-in-quilter/) [src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20251007165399/en) |\n| Revel | Series B ($150M, per headline) | 2026-02-26 | Per the post's headline only; the body doesn't confirm it | Index team: Achadjian + Eryk Dobrushkin (company page). Founder Scott Morton | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/great-hardware-deserves-great-software-investing-in-revel/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/revel/) |\n| Vizcom | not stated | 2024-03-28 | not stated | Index team: Achadjian alone (company page) | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/vizcom/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inside-vizcoms-vision-to-transform-industrial-design-with-ai/) |\n| Tofu | Seed ($5M) | 2023-10-19 | Yes. Index led; the post is bylined \"Index Ventures\", and the Index company page lists Achadjian alone | not named; Index team: Achadjian alone | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/tofu/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/tofu-raises-5m-seed-round-to-put-b2b-marketing-on-autopilot-with-ai/) |\n\n**Senior/junior pairing:** Eryk Dobrushkin is her recurring Index partner on physical-AI deals: PI, Quilter and Revel. Emil Schaefer pairs with her on Chai. Anthropic is shared with Martin Mignot and Vlad Loktev. She is the sole Index name on /dev/agents, Vizcom and Tofu, so those are her solo deals.\n\n**Timing:**\n- **Physical Intelligence:** she was not first. The seed was Thrive-led in 2024-03 (Khosla, Lux, OpenAI, Sequoia), and Index came in 20 months later.\n- **Chai Discovery:** not first. The seed in 2024-09 was co-led by Thrive, OpenAI and Dimension, and the B in 2025-12 by Oak HC/FT and General Catalyst. Index came in at the C [src](https://www.oakhcft.com/news/chai-discovery-announces-130-million-series-b-to-transform-molecular-discovery).\n- **/dev/agents:** first institutional lead at the seed.\n- **Tofu:** seed lead.\n- **Quilter:** came in at the B, after Benchmark and Coatue.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **/dev/agents.**\n  1. She met David Singleton about nine years before the deal, when he came to her first Armenia Tech Summit on a 72-hour trip.\n  2. They stayed in touch, and she sought his advice on trends and investments.\n  3. He spoke at Index's Founder Retreat.\n  4. When he started the agent OS company, Index said yes and led the $56M seed with CapitalG [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-operating-system-for-ai-agents-our-investment-in-devagents/).\n\n  This is the clearest case of her sourcing pattern: a long, non-transactional relationship with a senior operator (Stripe CTO, ex-Google Android).\n- **Revel.** Her first contact with founder Scott was a Zoom call in late December 2025. Index announced the Series B about two months later [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/great-hardware-deserves-great-software-investing-in-revel/).\n- **Chai Discovery.** Her post does not say when she met Joshua Meier. It frames Chai as following Index's earlier AI-bio bets, Cradle and Causaly [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/transforming-biology-from-science-to-engineering-our-investment-in-chai-discovery/).\n- **Quilter.** She led the $25.5M Series B and joined the board. The post gives no intro path [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-era-for-electronics-design-our-investment-in-quilter/).\n- **Physical Intelligence and Anthropic.** No deal story found. Her bio says she serves on the boards of or works with both companies, and does not say which [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-achadjian/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Index Ventures | Partner, SF | ~2017; Partner 2020 | [src](https://indexventures.com/perspectives/our-new-partner-nina-achadjian) |\n| Mike Volpi | Announced her promotion to Partner | 2020 | [src](https://pulse2.com/nina-achadjian-named-partner-at-index-ventures/) |\n| David Singleton (/dev/agents) | Friend; she sought his advice; she led his seed | ~2015 (nine years before 2024) | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-operating-system-for-ai-agents-our-investment-in-devagents/) |\n| Ara Mahdessian (ServiceTitan) | Board member; he gave her bio endorsement | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-achadjian/) |\n| Eryk Dobrushkin | Index co-signer on PI, Quilter, Revel | 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/quilter/) |\n| Emil Schaefer | Index co-signer on Chai | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/chai-discovery/) |\n| Martin Mignot, Vlad Loktev | Index co-team on Anthropic | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/anthropic/) |\n| Sergiy Nesterenko (Quilter) | Board member | 2025-10 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-era-for-electronics-design-our-investment-in-quilter/) |\n| Joshua Meier (Chai) | Investor | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/transforming-biology-from-science-to-engineering-our-investment-in-chai-discovery/) |\n| HIVE Ventures | Founder | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-achadjian/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which Anthropic round did Index join, and when? Does she hold a board or observer role there? Her bio's \"serves on the boards of or works with\" wording does not say.\n- Is she on the Physical Intelligence board? Only a third-party profile says so.\n- Who led the Vizcom round she wrote up in 2024-03?\n- Did Index or she come into Chai Discovery before the C, for example through an insider SPV?\n- Can she be found on X? What did she say about ex-SpaceX founders in the June 2026 Bloomberg Tech interview?\n- Which founder ties came from HIVE Ventures and the Armenia Tech Summit? Possible names: David Singleton, the ServiceTitan founders.\n- Web search budget ran out mid-task. A follow-up sweep should check Tofu's co-investors, Vizcom's round, and her X activity.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-achadjian/\n- https://indexventures.com/perspectives/our-new-partner-nina-achadjian\n- https://pulse2.com/nina-achadjian-named-partner-at-index-ventures/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/the-operating-system-for-ai-agents-our-investment-in-devagents/\n- https://siliconangle.com/2024/11/26/new-startup-named-dev-agents-led-ex-google-meta-tech-leaders-raises-56m-ai-agents\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-era-for-electronics-design-our-investment-in-quilter/\n- https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20251007165399/en\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/transforming-biology-from-science-to-engineering-our-investment-in-chai-discovery/\n- https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/series-c\n- https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design\n- https://www.oakhcft.com/news/chai-discovery-announces-130-million-series-b-to-transform-molecular-discovery\n- https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/great-hardware-deserves-great-software-investing-in-revel/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inside-vizcoms-vision-to-transform-industrial-design-with-ai/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/tofu-raises-5m-seed-round-to-put-b2b-marketing-on-autopilot-with-ai/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/physical-intelligence/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/anthropic/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/chai-discovery/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/quilter/\n- https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/robotics-startup-physical-intelligence-valued-at-5-6-billion\n- https://www.maginative.com/article/physical-intelligence-raises-70m-to-build-ai-powered-robots-for-any-application/\n- https://news.bloomberglaw.com/artificial-intelligence/ai-robotics-lab-in-talks-to-nab-1-billion-at-11-billion-value\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/audio/2024-12-19/odd-lots-nina-achadjian-on-what-it-takes-to-win-in-vc-podcast\n- https://podscripts.co/podcasts/odd-lots/nina-achadjian-on-what-it-takes-to-win-in-vc-right-now (aggregator transcript)\n- https://grafa.com/en/news/australia/index-ventures-achadjian-on-backing-ex-spacex-workers (summary only)\n- https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/\n", "person:nina-gerson": "\n# Nina Gerson\n\n## One-line take\nA growth-trained enterprise, security and AI-infra investor (CapitalG, then Coatue VP to GP) who has moved to Index. No Index deal or frontier-lab deal is on record yet. For this lens she is a \"watch what she does first at Index\" name, not a co-investor to chase now.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** pattern-matching on enterprise software and security at scale. She worked on CrowdStrike and AlphaSense at CapitalG, then on Databricks, Decagon, Cyera, Island and Legion at Coatue ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/company-update/welcoming-jade-lai-and-nina-gerson-to-coatue), [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/)).\n- **What constrains her:** her only bylined lead found is a Series A, Legion Security (July 2025), and she co-signed it under a senior GP, Sri Viswanath ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai)). No public writing, podcasts or X presence were found, and her Index franchise is unproven.\n- **What she would probably not do:** nothing in the sources shows her backing a pre-product research lab, pre-training, or chips. This is an inference from her record, not a stated rule.\n\n## Differential insight\n- She arrived at Coatue in April 2023 as a **Vice President, not a partner**, and the Index bio now calls her a former **General Partner**. That is a VP-to-GP rise inside roughly 2.5 years or less, though the GP date is not public. Coatue's welcome post gives her title on joining as Vice President ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/company-update/welcoming-jade-lai-and-nina-gerson-to-coatue), 2023-04-17). Index: \"Before joining Index, Nina was a General Partner at Coatue\" ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/)).\n- **Senior/junior pairing on Legion:** the Coatue Legion Series A post is bylined Sri Viswanath (GP, ex-Atlassian CTO), then Nina Gerson, then Abhi Srinivas (VP). The thesis line is \"It's automation that earns trust before it takes action.\" ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai), 2025-07-30). Viswanath later left to found Sycamore (see repo lead roster-coatue).\n- **Decagon crossover:** her Index bio lists Decagon among the companies she worked with at Coatue ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/)). Coatue and Index then co-led Decagon's $250M Series D at a $4.5B valuation. Decagon says it was \"led by new investors Coatue Management and Index Ventures\" ([src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-d-announcement), 2026-01-27). Neither firm's named people on that deal include her. Coatue's post is by Lucas Swisher, David Schneider and Abhi Srinivas ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/partnering-with-decagon-a-new-customer-experience)), and Index's named partner is Sofia Dolfe ([src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-d-announcement)). Whether that co-lead helped bring her to Index is **unconfirmed**.\n- She is also not a named author on Coatue's July 2026 Databricks post, which is bylined Laffont, Swisher, Lai, Srinivas and Sun ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/our-partnership-with-databricks), 2026-07-20). So her Databricks credit is supporting work, not a named lead.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** Under `THESIS.md` she is mostly growth-stage enterprise and security, and no frontier-lab, pre-training or chip deal was found. The closest deals are AI-native automation: Decagon (AI customer service, Coatue era, round not attributed to her) and Legion Security (AI security automation, Series A lead, 2025). The upside case is her new Index book: at Index she has early-stage remit for \"founders solving highly technical problems across AI, infrastructure and security\" ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/)). Re-check once her first Index deal is announced.\n\n## Background\n- Washington University in St. Louis, where she was a goalkeeper on the NCAA championship-winning soccer team ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/)).\n- Investment banker at Union Square Advisors (dates not found) ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/)).\n- CapitalG (Alphabet's growth fund), working on AlphaSense, CrowdStrike, Strive Health and Next Insurance (dates not found) ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/company-update/welcoming-jade-lai-and-nina-gerson-to-coatue)).\n- Coatue: joined April 2023 as VP, enterprise, SF ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/company-update/welcoming-jade-lai-and-nina-gerson-to-coatue)), and later became General Partner ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/)).\n- Index Ventures: Partner, SF; join date not found ([src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/ | Index team page, checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | No Index Perspectives posts linked to her as of 2026-11-10 |\n| X | nothing found | Index page links only the firm account |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/ninagerson/ | Linked from the Index bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search budget was exhausted during this run, so this is not an exhaustive check.\n\n## How they think\n- Security has to keep pace with AI: \"security must evolve faster than the threats it faces\" (Legion post, co-authored) ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai)).\n- She favours trust-first automation, meaning agents that earn trust before they act autonomously (same source).\n- Contrarian views and calls she got right or wrong: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Legion: scaling security with AI](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai) (2025-07-30): her only bylined deal memo found.\n2. [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/): her current remit and Coatue-era company list.\n3. [Coatue welcome post](https://www.coatue.com/blog/company-update/welcoming-jade-lai-and-nina-gerson-to-coatue) (2023-04-17): her entry title and CapitalG deals.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No personal X account is linked.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Legion Security (Coatue) | Series A | 2025-07-30 | Yes (Coatue led; co-signed with Sri Viswanath and Abhi Srinivas) | Accel; Picture Capital | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai) |\n| Decagon (Coatue) | round not attributed to her | not stated | not stated | n/a | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/) |\n| Databricks (Coatue) | round not attributed to her | not stated | not stated | n/a | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/) |\n| Cyera (Coatue) | round not attributed to her | not stated | not stated | n/a | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/) |\n| Island (Coatue) | round not attributed to her | not stated | not stated | n/a | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/) |\n| Rippling (Coatue) | round not attributed to her | not stated | not stated | n/a | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/) |\n| CrowdStrike, AlphaSense, Strive Health, Next Insurance (CapitalG) | not stated | not stated | not stated | n/a | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/company-update/welcoming-jade-lai-and-nina-gerson-to-coatue) |\n| Index Ventures deals | none found | | | | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Legion Security (Series A, 2025-07-30):** Coatue led, with Accel and Picture Capital. The byline runs senior GP (Viswanath), then Gerson, then VP (Srinivas). Amount, intro path and timing versus Accel were not found ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai)). Coatue was not first in, since Accel and Picture Capital already backed the company. Earlier rounds were not verified.\n- **Decagon:** listed among her Coatue companies. Coatue came in only at the Series D (Jan 2026, co-led with Index), well after a16z (seed), Accel (A) and Bain Capital Ventures (B). So any Gerson involvement was late-stage, not first-in ([src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-d-announcement)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Index Ventures | Partner | join date not found | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/) |\n| Coatue | VP, later General Partner (alumna) | 2023-04 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/company-update/welcoming-jade-lai-and-nina-gerson-to-coatue) |\n| CapitalG | Investor (alumna) | dates not found | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/) |\n| Union Square Advisors | Investment banker (alumna) | dates not found | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/) |\n| Sri Viswanath | Senior co-author on Legion Series A | 2025 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai) |\n| Abhi Srinivas | Junior co-author on Legion Series A | 2025 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai) |\n| Jade Lai | Joined Coatue in the same announcement | 2023-04 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/company-update/welcoming-jade-lai-and-nina-gerson-to-coatue) |\n| Thomas Laffont | Coatue co-founder who announced her hire | 2023-04 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/company-update/welcoming-jade-lai-and-nina-gerson-to-coatue) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact date she made GP at Coatue, and when she joined Index. LinkedIn is the likeliest source.\n- Did the Coatue and Index co-lead of Decagon (Jan 2026, Swisher and Dolfe) lead to her move?\n- Her first Index deal. Watch for AI-infra or security Series A/B announcements naming her.\n- Which Decagon and Cyera rounds she actually worked on at Coatue.\n- Podcasts or talks: not searched exhaustively, because the web search budget ran out in this run.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/nina-gerson/\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/company-update/welcoming-jade-lai-and-nina-gerson-to-coatue\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/partnering-with-decagon-a-new-customer-experience\n- https://decagon.ai/blog/series-d-announcement\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/our-partnership-with-databricks\n", "person:nnamdi-iregbulem": "\n# Nnamdi Iregbulem\n\n## One-line take\nA quant-minded Lightspeed infra and devtools partner who led the first institutional round in Goodfire (interpretability) and Patronus, and co-led Exa's A. He's worth tracking as the Lightspeed partner closest to \"research-lab-adjacent infra\", not as a frontier-lab check writer.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he is a self-taught programmer who studies technical products from first principles ([src](http://lsvp.com/team-member/nnamdi-iregbulem/)). He also publishes data-heavy essays on venture market structure ([src](https://whoisnnamdi.com/)). Both make him credible with technical founders building tools for other engineers.\n- **His pattern:** lead the first institutional round in small amounts at tooling or research-tooling companies ($3M Patronus seed, $7M Goodfire seed, $17M Exa A), then follow on behind bigger leads (Notable, Menlo, B Capital, Benchmark, a16z), sources below.\n- **What constrains him:** Lightspeed's AI partner bench is crowded, and frontier-lab deals there carry other partners' names (James Alcorn on Thinking Machines, Reflection and Periodic, per `data/rows/roster-lightspeed-venture-partners-leads.csv`). His named deals are all infra, evals, search or interpretability. None is a pre-training lab.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred from his writing, not stated):** treat a headline valuation or token spend as evidence of quality. His 2026 essays argue that private marks and \"tokenmaxxing\" are both misleading proxies ([src](https://whoisnnamdi.com/valuations-need-a-correction/), [src](https://fortune.com/2026/07/29/button-mashing-gaming-tokenmaxxing-nnamdi-iregbulem-lightspeed-ai/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Bottleneck view (2026-07):** he thinks the binding constraint on AI productivity is human review capacity, not compute or tokens. In his words, \"It is human verification bandwidth.\" ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/07/29/button-mashing-gaming-tokenmaxxing-nnamdi-iregbulem-lightspeed-ai/)) The same view underlies two of his bets: Patronus (evals) and Goodfire (interpretability).\n- **Seed market (2026-06):** he says the number of active seed-stage startups is falling and that \"exits now outpace new Seed financings\" ([src](https://whoisnnamdi.com/taking-stock-of-the-seed-stage/)). He also says nearly every newly funded seed company \"has something to do with AI.\" ([src](https://whoisnnamdi.com/taking-stock-of-the-seed-stage/))\n- **Private marks (2026-10):** he calls deal valuations \"a horrible proxy for the broader market.\" ([src](https://whoisnnamdi.com/valuations-need-a-correction/)) His estimates put the median Series A graduate in the top ~3% of seeds today, versus the top 10–15% in the early 2010s (same source). He reads private prices as financing prices of survivors.\n- **Series A (2024-01):** \"Series A does not mean you have product-market fit.\" ([src](https://whoisnnamdi.com/series-a-bust/)) He frames investors as people who \"expect more but believe less\" (same source).\n- **Exa thesis (2024-07):** he co-wrote the Series A memo, which argues \"AI agents don't need to see ads, they need to see results.\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/exa-redesigning-search-for-ai/)) Lightspeed came back for the B and C behind other leads, with three memos in total (see Portfolio).\n- **Pre-Lightspeed record:** he says he sourced and co-led ICONIQ investments in GitLab, Epic Games and ezCater ([src](https://whoisnnamdi.com/about-me/)). That is growth-stage pattern recognition carried into early-stage infra.\n- **Podcasts, X:** I found no transcripts. X (@whoisnnamdi) blocks fetching.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The deal that matters is **Goodfire**: Lightspeed led the $7M seed (Aug 2024) with him as the only named partner and the quoted voice. The company went on to a $150M Series B at $1.25B (Feb 2026), and Lightspeed followed on ([src](https://pulse2.com/goodfire-150-million-series-b-at-1-25-billion-valuation-raised-for-interpretability-ai-lab)). That is a research-lab seed in a frontier-safety category. **Nuance Labs** (2025 seed, a \"real-time human foundation model\") is also new-lab shaped ([src](https://lsvp.com/company/nuance-labs/)). Everything else he is named on is AI infra and devtools: Exa, Unsloth, Patronus, Zo, Snorkel. That is useful as a co-investor and signal, but it is not pre-training, chips or compute. He fits the owner's \"senior at a $10M+ fund\" segment. He is not a fast-promoted principal, since the promotion date is unknown. As a co-investor, he is a natural seed partner for interpretability, evals or agent-infra spinouts from labs.\n\n## Background\n- Yale, Economics (personal site); Lightspeed's page adds computer science ([src](https://whoisnnamdi.com/about-me/), [src](http://lsvp.com/team-member/nnamdi-iregbulem/)). He worked at McKinsey and as a Student Tech while at Yale.\n- J.P. Morgan TMT investment banking, New York, his first job after school (no dates found).\n- ICONIQ Capital, four years of growth investing. He deployed $500M+ and sourced or co-led GitLab, Epic Games, ezCater and Brightwheel ([src](https://whoisnnamdi.com/about-me/), [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/nnamdi-iregbulem/)).\n- Stanford GSB MBA. While there he was a PM at Confluent, worked at GitLab in a product role, and was co-president of the VC Club ([src](https://whoisnnamdi.com/about-me/), [src](http://lsvp.com/team-member/nnamdi-iregbulem/)).\n- Lightspeed, joined 2020 ([src](http://lsvp.com/team-member/nnamdi-iregbulem/)). Forbes 30 Under 30 VC (2021 list, per the Forbes page) ([src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/nnamdi-iregbulem/)). VCJ 40 Under 40 (2022, per his site).\n- **Promotion story:** the entry title in 2020 is not confirmed by any fetched source. He is \"Partner\" on the Goodfire seed release (Aug 2024) ([src](https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/goodfire-raises-7m-to-break-open-the-black-box-of-generative-ai-models)). An earlier aggregator claim of \"Partner since 2020\" (MarketScreener) could not be verified: the MarketScreener page I fetched was for a different person. `fast_promotion: unknown`.\n- Pronouns: Forbes uses \"his\".\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://whoisnnamdi.com/ | Data essays; 3 posts in 2026 (Jun, Jul via Fortune, Oct) |\n| Substack / blog | https://medium.com/@whoisnnamdi | Linked from Lightspeed bio; 403 on fetch |\n| X | https://twitter.com/whoisnnamdi | Not fetchable; follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/nnamdiiregbulem/ | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found this pass. The web-search budget was exhausted, so podcasts were not searched. This is the first follow-up to do.\n- 2026-07: Fortune op-ed, \"Button-mashing never worked in StarCraft. Tokenmaxxing won't work in AI\" ([src](https://fortune.com/2026/07/29/button-mashing-gaming-tokenmaxxing-nnamdi-iregbulem-lightspeed-ai/)). It applies Goodhart's law to token metrics and cites Karpathy and Anthropic.\n\n## How they think\n- **Verification over generation:** build review capacity ahead of output capacity. This connects evals (Patronus) and interpretability (Goodfire).\n- **Selection bias in venture data:** private valuations are \"conditional prices\" ([src](https://whoisnnamdi.com/valuations-need-a-correction/)). The seed pool is shrinking even while AI dollars boom.\n- **Agent-native infrastructure:** agents, not humans, are the new consumers of the web, the thesis behind Exa ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/tripling-down-on-exa-to-build-the-search-engine-for-ai/)).\n- **Software demand is unbounded:** \"Why We Will Never Have Enough Software Developers\" (2020) ([src](https://whoisnnamdi.com/never-enough-developers/)). His stated mission is to raise total software output ([src](https://whoisnnamdi.com/about-me/)).\n- **Calls that look right:** Goodfire (seed Aug 2024, $1.25B by Feb 2026) and Exa (A in 2024, then a16z-led $250M C in 2026).\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Taking Stock of the Seed Stage\" (2026-06): his model of a shrinking seed pool. https://whoisnnamdi.com/taking-stock-of-the-seed-stage/\n2. \"Valuations Need a Correction\" (2026-10): why private marks mislead. https://whoisnnamdi.com/valuations-need-a-correction/\n3. Fortune op-ed on tokenmaxxing (2026-07): his verification-bottleneck thesis. https://fortune.com/2026/07/29/button-mashing-gaming-tokenmaxxing-nnamdi-iregbulem-lightspeed-ai/\n4. Exa Series A memo (2024-07): his agent-search thesis. https://lsvp.com/stories/exa-redesigning-search-for-ai/\n5. \"The Series A Bust\" (2024-01). https://whoisnnamdi.com/series-a-bust/\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed: X blocked fetching. His long-form output is low-cadence and data-heavy, and the Fortune piece shows he engages with Karpathy-style agentic-coding discourse.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Goodfire | Seed $7M | 2024-08-15 | yes (Lightspeed lead; he is quoted) | Menlo (Anthology), South Park Commons, Work-Bench, Juniper, Mythos, Bluebirds, angels | [src](https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/goodfire-raises-7m-to-break-open-the-black-box-of-generative-ai-models) |\n| Goodfire | Series A $50M | 2025-04-17 | no (Menlo led) | Anthropic, B Capital, Work-Bench, Wing, SPC | [src](https://www.goodfire.com/blog/announcing-our-50m-series-a) |\n| Goodfire | Series B $150M @ $1.25B | 2026-02-05 | no (B Capital led) | Juniper, Menlo, SPC, Wing, DFJ Growth, Salesforce Ventures | [src](https://pulse2.com/goodfire-150-million-series-b-at-1-25-billion-valuation-raised-for-interpretability-ai-lab) |\n| Exa | Series A $17M | 2024-07-16 | yes (Lightspeed lead; memo co-author with Guru Chahal) | NVIDIA Ventures, Y Combinator | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/exa-redesigning-search-for-ai/) |\n| Exa | Series B $85M | 2025-09-03 | no (Benchmark / Peter Fenton led) | YC, NVIDIA Ventures | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/search-perfected-for-ai-why-were-doubling-down-on-exa/) |\n| Exa | Series C $250M | 2026-05-20 | no (a16z led) | Benchmark, YC, NVIDIA Ventures | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/tripling-down-on-exa-to-build-the-search-engine-for-ai/) |\n| Patronus AI | Seed $3M | 2023-09-14 | yes (Lightspeed lead; he is the only Lightspeed partner listed) | Factorial Capital; angels Amjad Masad, Gokul Rajaram | [src](https://www.patronus.ai/news/patronus-launch), [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/patronus) |\n| Patronus AI | Series A $17M | 2024-05-22 | no (Notable Capital led) | Datadog, Factorial | [src](https://www.patronus.ai/news/announcing-our-17-million-series-a) |\n| Patronus AI | Series B $50M | 2026-06-25 | no (Greenfield Partners led) | Notable, Datadog, Samsung, Factorial, Gokul Rajaram | [src](https://www.patronus.ai/news/announcing-our-50m-series-b) |\n| Zo Computer | Seed | 2024 | unknown | Decibel, Craft; angels incl. the Collisons, Guillermo Rauch, Clem Delangue | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/zo/), [src](https://www.zo.computer/team) |\n| Nuance Labs | Seed | 2025 | unknown | co-listed partner Guru Chahal | [src](https://lsvp.com/company/nuance-labs/) |\n| Unsloth | round not stated | not stated | unknown | Lightspeed partners James Alcorn, Guru Chahal, Hemant Mohapatra also listed | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/unsloth) |\n| Snorkel AI | Lightspeed entered at seed in 2019, before he joined | 2019 | n/a for him | Lightspeed partners Ravi Mhatre and Raviraj Jain also listed | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/snorkel-ai/) |\n| Pre-AI infra (not verified per round) | Materialize, Redpanda, Matillion, Voltron Data, Ponder, Select Star, Astro, Polar Signals, Tana | | | | [src](http://lsvp.com/team-member/nnamdi-iregbulem/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n**How he sources (my inference, not stated anywhere):** I found no account of a specific intro path. The record shows three habits:\n- **Early first checks.** Three times he wrote the first institutional check at or before public launch: Patronus at its stealth launch in Sep 2023, Goodfire's seed, and Exa's A, where YC was the only earlier named backer. That points to pre-launch access to technical founders.\n- **A standing partner.** He works as a pair with Guru Chahal on AI infra (Exa, Zo, Nuance Labs, Unsloth).\n- **Writing as inbound.** His public data essays and Lightspeed memos likely work as an inbound surface for technical founders.\n\nGoodfire's seed syndicate included South Park Commons, which is a plausible origination path, but this is unverified.\n\n- **Goodfire:** Lightspeed led the seed (Aug 2024). Menlo joined through its Anthology Fund, then took the Series A lead eight months later ([src](https://menlovc.com/?p=18316)). Lightspeed was the first institutional lead. The intro path is not found.\n- **Exa:** Lightspeed led the $17M A in July 2024. YC came earlier. The B came ~14 months later under Benchmark, and the C ~8 months after that under a16z. Lightspeed was a participant in both later rounds, with Iregbulem and Chahal co-authoring each memo. How they met Exa is not stated.\n- **Patronus:** Lightspeed led the $3M seed at the stealth launch (Sep 2023), so it was the first institutional check. Notable Capital led the A eight months later, and Lightspeed followed in the A and the B.\n- **Snorkel:** he inherited the position. Lightspeed's 2019 seed predates his 2020 arrival.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Guru Chahal (Lightspeed) | Co-author on all three Exa memos; co-listed on Zo, Nuance Labs, Unsloth | 2024 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/exa-redesigning-search-for-ai/) |\n| James Alcorn (Lightspeed) | Co-listed on Unsloth | 2025 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/unsloth) |\n| Eric Ho (Goodfire CEO) | Seed lead investor | 2024 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/goodfire) |\n| Menlo Ventures / Deedy Das | Goodfire seed co-investor, then Series A lead | 2024 | [src](https://menlovc.com/?p=18316) |\n| Peter Fenton (Benchmark) | Took the Exa B lead after Lightspeed's A | 2025 | [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/search-perfected-for-ai-why-were-doubling-down-on-exa/) |\n| Factorial Capital | Patronus seed co-investor | 2023 | [src](https://www.patronus.ai/news/patronus-launch) |\n| ICONIQ Capital | Former employer (4 yrs growth) | pre-2020 | [src](https://whoisnnamdi.com/about-me/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What was his entry title in 2020, and when was he named Partner? Check LinkedIn and the Lightspeed 2020 hire post.\n- Who led Zo Computer's and Nuance Labs' seeds, and at what size? Nuance's founders (Fangchang Ma, Edward Zhang, Karren Yang) look like a research team: which lab did they come from?\n- What did Lightspeed write into Unsloth, and when?\n- Is he on the Goodfire board? How did the Goodfire intro happen (South Park Commons?).\n- Find podcast appearances (search budget was exhausted this pass).\n\n## Sources\n- http://lsvp.com/team-member/nnamdi-iregbulem/\n- https://whoisnnamdi.com/ ; /about-me/ ; /taking-stock-of-the-seed-stage/ ; /valuations-need-a-correction/ ; /series-a-bust/ ; /dark-matter/\n- https://fortune.com/2026/07/29/button-mashing-gaming-tokenmaxxing-nnamdi-iregbulem-lightspeed-ai/\n- https://www.forbes.com/profile/nnamdi-iregbulem/\n- https://www.silicon.co.uk/press-release/goodfire-raises-7m-to-break-open-the-black-box-of-generative-ai-models\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/goodfire ; /stories/exa-redesigning-search-for-ai/ ; /stories/search-perfected-for-ai-why-were-doubling-down-on-exa/ ; /stories/tripling-down-on-exa-to-build-the-search-engine-for-ai/ ; /stories/patronus ; /stories/unsloth ; /stories/zo/ ; /stories/snorkel-ai/ ; /company/nuance-labs/\n- https://www.patronus.ai/news/patronus-launch ; /news/announcing-our-17-million-series-a ; /news/announcing-our-50m-series-b\n- https://www.zo.computer/team\n- https://menlovc.com/?p=18316 ; https://www.goodfire.com/blog/announcing-our-50m-series-a ; https://pulse2.com/goodfire-150-million-series-b-at-1-25-billion-valuation-raised-for-interpretability-ai-lab\n", "person:noam-brown": "\n# Noam Brown\n\n## One-line take\nA working frontier researcher who writes few cheques, nearly all into companies whose product depends on models that reason for a long time: 7 named so far, with Cursor's Series A the standout.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why founders want him:** he built the reasoning line of work (poker search, CICERO, o1), so his cheque tells other investors the team's bet on model capability is sound. He is not a lead and no cheque size is public.\n- **Pattern:** he comes in at Series A more often than seed (Magic 2023, Cursor 2024, Basis 2024, Brain Co 2025, Deeptune 2026); Conway (seed, 2026) is the exception.\n- **Constraint:** full-time at OpenAI. Small book, no vehicle, little public talk about investing. Do not expect follow-on capital or board work.\n\n## Differential insight\n- He argues reasoning models were neglected rather than blocked by compute: \"There were various reasons why this research direction was neglected\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/19/openai-research-lead-noam-brown-thinks-ai-reasoning-models-couldve-arrived-decades-ago). That view lines up with what he funds.\n- On evaluation: \"The state of benchmarks in AI is really bad\" and fixing them needs little compute [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/19/openai-research-lead-noam-brown-thinks-ai-reasoning-models-couldve-arrived-decades-ago).\n- CB Insights counts 7 investments and shows two with hidden names: a Series A on 2026-02-23 and a Series B on 2026-02-24 (aggregator) [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/noam-brown). The repo has 7 named, so the ledger may already hold those two under other dates, or two are still missing.\n- The Conway seed (CB Insights date 2026-03-01; ledger has 2026-10-02 from TechCrunch) put him next to a16z, Anthropic, Khosla, Menlo, SV Angel and Patrick Collison (aggregator) [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/noam-brown).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh as a signal, low as a relationship to build: he marks reasoning-heavy and coding-agent companies early, which is my automation lens. Deals that matter: Cursor (A), Magic (A), Basis (A), Conway (seed), Etched (chips).\n\n## Background\nPhD at Carnegie Mellon on imperfect-information games (Libratus, Pluribus); research scientist at Meta FAIR (CICERO, Diplomacy); then OpenAI, working on reasoning models [src](https://noambrown.github.io/). Dates not sourced this pass.\n\n## Vehicle and cheque size\nPersonal. No fund, SPV or scout role found; cheque size not disclosed.\n\n## How founders get to him\nNo direct evidence. The companies are either OpenAI-adjacent (Cursor's Series A included OpenAI's fund; Basis and Conway founders are in the same research circle) or share co-angels with him (Jeff Dean in Cursor and Basis).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://noambrown.github.io/ | Papers and bio |\n| Substack / blog | | |\n| X | https://x.com/polynoamial | Active; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | | not checked |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/noambrown | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-03-19 — Nvidia GTC panel (via TechCrunch) — reasoning could have arrived decades ago — [link](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/19/openai-research-lead-noam-brown-thinks-ai-reasoning-models-couldve-arrived-decades-ago) — mined\n- n/d — Unsupervised Learning (Redpoint) — o1 release and the path to AGI — [link](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/video/openai-researcher-noam-brown-unpacks-the-full-release-of-o1-and-the-path-to-agi/) — not mined\n- n/d — Dwarkesh Podcast — [link](https://dwarkesh.com/p/noam-brown) — not mined\n\n## How they think\n- Search and thinking time are underused; scaling inference-time reasoning is the main lever [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/19/openai-research-lead-noam-brown-thinks-ai-reasoning-models-couldve-arrived-decades-ago).\n- Academia should work on benchmarks, where compute is not the constraint [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/19/openai-research-lead-noam-brown-thinks-ai-reasoning-models-couldve-arrived-decades-ago).\n- Nothing public on how he chooses investments, and no pass or regret found.\n\n## Read these first\n- [TechCrunch on his GTC panel](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/19/openai-research-lead-noam-brown-thinks-ai-reasoning-models-couldve-arrived-decades-ago).\n- [Unsupervised Learning episode](https://www.redpoint.com/content-hub/video/openai-researcher-noam-brown-unpacks-the-full-release-of-o1-and-the-path-to-agi/) (not yet read).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot mined this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n7 named AI companies; 1 at seed. Etched's round is undated in the ledger.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Role | Early (pre-seed/seed)? | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Conway Research | Seed (first round) | 2026-10-02 | angel | yes | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=3174503) |\n| Deeptune | Series A ($43M) | 2026-03-19 | angel | no | [src](https://fortune.com/2026/03/19/andreessen-horowitz-ai-startups-deeptune-series-a) |\n| Brain Co | Series A ($30M) | 2025-09-10 | angel | no | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/noam-brown) |\n| Basis | Series A ($34M) | 2024-12-17 | angel | no | [src](https://www.getbasis.ai/blogs/basis-raises-a-34m-series-a-led-by-khosla-ventures) |\n| Cursor | Series A | 2024-08-22 | participant | no | [src](https://cursor.com/blog/series-a) |\n| Magic | Series A | 2023-02-06 | participant | no | [src](https://magic.dev/blog/series-a) |\n| Etched | Undisclosed (one of four unannounced financings 2024-2026) | n/d | participant | no | [src](https://www.etched.com/) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found on how any single cheque happened.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | Employer | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/19/openai-research-lead-noam-brown-thinks-ai-reasoning-models-couldve-arrived-decades-ago) |\n| Jeff Dean | Co-angel in Cursor Series A and Basis Series A | 2024 | [src](https://cursor.com/blog/series-a) |\n| Andreessen Horowitz | Lead on Cursor A, Deeptune A; in Conway seed | 2024 | [src](https://buttondown.com/AngelRegistry/archive/angel-registry-update-march-20-2026) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Identify the two hidden February 2026 cheques on CB Insights.\n- Any statement from him on investing while employed at OpenAI.\n- Mine the Redpoint and Dwarkesh episodes.\n\n## Sources\nhttps://techcrunch.com/2025/03/19/openai-research-lead-noam-brown-thinks-ai-reasoning-models-couldve-arrived-decades-ago ; https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/noam-brown ; https://buttondown.com/AngelRegistry/archive/angel-registry-update-march-20-2026 ; https://noambrown.github.io/ ; https://latent.space/wiki/noam-brown/\n", "person:nowi-kallen": "\n# Nowi Kallen\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the Salesforce Ventures (SFV) partner who owns the European AI book: BFL, Lovable, ElevenLabs, Legora and Fetcherr. He co-led the one lab-adjacent round where SFV set the price (Black Forest Labs B, Dec 2025). He is useful to the owner as a later-round price-setter and follow-on source, not as a seed co-investor.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He has the CISO and enterprise-buyer network from a decade of security deals. Wiz is the proof: SFV went in at under $5M ARR in May 2021, and Alphabet's $32B purchase has since closed ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-wiz/)). Behind him sits Salesforce's balance sheet, which can join $300M-$550M rounds ([BFL release](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier), [Legora](https://legora.com/newsroom/legora-raises-550-million-series-e-at-8.8-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-growth)).\n- **What constrains him.** He invests corporate money with no outside LPs. Approval sits with SFV President John Somorjai, and no investment committee has been described in any source checked (see [firms/salesforce-ventures](../firms/salesforce-ventures.md)). He enters at Series B and later, and mostly follows: ElevenLabs C, Lovable B/C and Legora D/E were all priced by others.\n- **What he would not do.** Nothing in his book is a pre-product lab seed. His smallest AI check is Covecta's $6.5M round ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-covecta/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Unannounced promotion.** The SFV team page shows him as \"Managing Director\" through 2026-02-05 and as \"Partner\" by 2026-02-14 ([2026-02-05](https://web.archive.org/web/20260205184504/https://salesforceventures.com/people/), [2026-02-14](https://web.archive.org/web/20260214163052/https://salesforceventures.com/people/)). In the same update Emily Zhao moved from Principal to MD, so it reads as a promotion round. No press release was found. The 2026-02-14 bio reads: \"Nowi Kallen is a Partner at Salesforce Ventures.\" ([snapshot](https://web.archive.org/web/20260214165140/https://salesforceventures.com/people/nowi-kallen/))\n- **A security investor relabelled as an AI investor.** In 2023 his focus was \"dev ops, data, cybersecurity, and product-led growth\", and none of the deals he listed were AI ([2023-03 snapshot](https://web.archive.org/web/20230328023122/https://salesforceventures.com/people/nowi-kallen/)). AI first appears in 2025, with ElevenLabs ([2025-06](https://web.archive.org/web/20250620072000/https://salesforceventures.com/people/nowi-kallen/)). The bio did not switch to \"AI & cloud infrastructure, and vertical AI\" until the rewrite between Feb and May 2026 ([2026-05](https://web.archive.org/web/20260513191008/https://salesforceventures.com/people/nowi-kallen/)).\n- **The \"led\" list is curated, not a record of rounds.** The Oct 2025 bio lists Snyk and Oleria as led ([snapshot](https://web.archive.org/web/20251012234530/https://salesforceventures.com/people/nowi-kallen/)). By May 2026 Oleria is gone from the bio entirely and Snyk is gone from the led list. Miro was \"(co-)led\" in 2023 and is \"also invested\" now. ElevenLabs is listed as \"led\", but SFV's Series C was co-led by ICONIQ and a16z ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-elevenlabs/)). At SFV, \"led\" means he owned the deal internally, not that he priced the round.\n- **The strategic tie is commercial, not compute.** On Hopin (Series A, summer 2020) SFV said it helped \"bring Salesforce as a Hopin customer across numerous parts\" of the business and brokered a product-partnership conversation ([SFV 2021-03-04](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/hopin-and-the-coming-of-age-of-european-tech/)). On Wiz, SFV credits its \"tailored introductions\" for Wiz's entry into large US enterprises ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-wiz/)). His 2025-26 AI posts (BFL, Lovable, ElevenLabs, Legora) name no Salesforce commercial tie.\n- **How he gets conviction.** On Wiz, SFV says \"Fortune 100 CISOs told us they'd buy from Wiz sight unseen\" ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-wiz/)). He leans on customer references, not technical diligence.\n- **Overlapping credit.** Principal Alexandra Quie's bio also claims Fetcherr and says she led AutogenAI B, while Kallen sits on both bylines ([Quie](https://salesforceventures.com/people/alexandra-quie/)). Investor Sam Ackah-Yensu co-bylines almost every Kallen AI deal and is the likely day-to-day contact.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a seed partner. For the owner's ~$20M frontier-AI seed checks he matters in three ways:\n1. **A later-round price-setter.** He co-led BFL's $300M B at $3.25B post ([release](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier)) and led Fetcherr's C ([release headline](https://www.accessnewswire.com/newsroom/en/aerospace-and-defense/fetcherr-raises-42m-in-series-c-funding-led-by-salesforce-ventures-1077687)).\n2. **A reliable re-upper.** He followed ElevenLabs from B to C, Lovable from B to C, and Legora from D to E.\n3. **A route to Salesforce as a customer or channel.**\n\nHe is not an LP, and not an acquirer in his own right; acquisitions are Salesforce corp dev, also under Somorjai. The deals that matter for the owner's lens are Black Forest Labs and ElevenLabs.\n\n## Background\n- Investment banking at Barclays (New York and London), advising security and software companies on M&A and IPOs. Dates unconfirmed ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/nowi-kallen/)).\n- Investor at Vitruvian Partners, focused on software. Dates unconfirmed ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/nowi-kallen/)).\n- At Salesforce Ventures by 2020-03-26, his first byline ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/lessons-in-saas-from-the-2008-downturn-what-can-we-expect/)). Managing Director by 2023-03. Partner from Feb 2026 (snapshots above).\n- Has \"lived on three continents\" ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/nowi-kallen/)). Education is not stated in any source checked.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://salesforceventures.com/people/nowi-kallen/ | SFV bio; page loads 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ | Deal posts co-bylined; about 38 posts 2020-2026 |\n| X | https://twitter.com/nkkall | Linked from the SFV bio; activity not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/kallennowi/ | Linked from the SFV bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n**How to reach him.** Through portfolio founders he backed in Europe (BFL's Robin Rombach, Lovable, Legora). Through AMP's Anjney Midha, his BFL co-lead. Or through SFV London Investor Sam Ackah-Yensu, who co-bylines his AI deals.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. Web search was unavailable this session; checked only SFV pages.\n\n## How they think\n- **Great companies can come from anywhere.** He backs technical founders outside the usual hubs ([bio](https://salesforceventures.com/people/nowi-kallen/)), a line he already used in the 2021 Hopin post ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/hopin-and-the-coming-of-age-of-european-tech/)).\n- **Enterprise automation is in its early innings,** with AI an accelerant ([bio](https://salesforceventures.com/people/nowi-kallen/)).\n- **On BFL:** \"we believe BFL will be the source of the next major advance in visual research\" ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-black-forest-labs/)).\n- **Right:** Wiz (entry under $5M ARR, $32B exit). **Wrong:** Hopin (Series A, then the $5.65B Series C he co-bylined in 2021). Hopin's later decline is not covered in sources checked here.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Behind the investment: Wiz](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-wiz/): how he diligenced through CISO references.\n- [Welcome Black Forest Labs](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-black-forest-labs/): his only lab-adjacent round lead.\n- [Hopin and the coming of age of European tech](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/hopin-and-the-coming-of-age-of-european-tech/): his Europe thesis, and how the Salesforce customer tie worked.\n- [Lovable's CFO on choosing velocity](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/lovables-cfo-on-choosing-velocity-over-speed/): confirms the Series C re-up.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked; X was not fetched.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Hopin | Series A | 2020 summer | unknown | | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/hopin-and-the-coming-of-age-of-european-tech/) |\n| Wiz | Series B (then C/D/E) | 2021-05 | bio: led | | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-wiz/) |\n| AutogenAI | Series B | 2023-12-14 | SFV co-led (Quie claims) | Spark Capital | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-autogenai/) |\n| Sixfold | Series A | 2024-06-05 | yes | Scale VP, Bessemer | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-sixfold/) |\n| PointFive | Undisclosed | 2024-11-12 | bio: led | Index, Entree | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-pointfive/) |\n| ElevenLabs | Series C ($180M) | 2025-01-30 | no (re-up from B) | ICONIQ, a16z, NEA, Sequoia | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-elevenlabs/) |\n| Protex AI | Series B | 2025-02-04 | not stated | Hedosophia, Notion | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-protex-ai/) |\n| Chainguard | Series D ($356M) | 2025-04-23 | not stated | IVP, Kleiner Perkins | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-chainguard/) |\n| Covecta | Undisclosed ($6.5M) | 2025-09-16 | not stated | | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-covecta/) |\n| Fetcherr | Series C ($42M) | 2025-09-25 | yes (headline; quote unverified) | Battery, Left Lane | [release](https://www.accessnewswire.com/newsroom/en/aerospace-and-defense/fetcherr-raises-42m-in-series-c-funding-led-by-salesforce-ventures-1077687) |\n| Black Forest Labs | Series B ($300M, $3.25B post) | 2025-12-01 | co-lead with AMP | a16z, Northzone, Creandum, Temasek | [release](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier) |\n| Lovable | Series B ($330M) | 2025-12-18 | no | CapitalG, Menlo | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-lovable/) |\n| Legora | Series D | 2026-03-10 | no | Accel, Benchmark | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-operating-system-for-modern-legal-work/) |\n| Upwind | Series B ($250M) | 2026-03-18 | not stated | | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-next-generation-of-cloud-security/) |\n| Lovable | Series C | 2026-08 (post date) | no | | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/lovables-cfo-on-choosing-velocity-over-speed/) |\n| Legora | Series E ($550M, $8.8B) | 2026-10-21 | no | Atreides (lead) | [Legora](https://legora.com/newsroom/legora-raises-550-million-series-e-at-8.8-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-growth) |\n\nAlso named in his bio, with no round detail: Avalor (acquired by Zscaler), Laminar (acquired by Rubrik), Snyk, Aiven, Miro, Monday.com, Contentful, Gong, Oleria, Odigos.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Black Forest Labs B.** SFV co-led with AMP's Anjney Midha. The release says a16z had led a previously unannounced Series A. Kallen is quoted (as MD): BFL sits at \"the intersection of breakthrough research and commercial excellence\" ([release](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier)). No Salesforce product tie is stated.\n- **Wiz.** The team met founders Assaf Rappaport and Ami Luttwak in 2021. Diligence rested on CISO and former-employee references, and the round moved fast. SFV says its introductions opened US enterprises for Wiz ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-wiz/)). The intro path to the founders is not stated.\n- **Hopin.** He co-bylined with Alex Kayyal. SFV invested in the Series A in summer 2020 and helped make Salesforce a Hopin customer ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/hopin-and-the-coming-of-age-of-european-tech/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Salesforce Ventures | Partner (MD until Feb 2026) | 2020-03 (first byline) | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/nowi-kallen/) |\n| Vitruvian Partners | former investor | unconfirmed | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/nowi-kallen/) |\n| Barclays | former banker | unconfirmed | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/nowi-kallen/) |\n| Anjney Midha (AMP) | BFL B co-lead | 2025-12 | [release](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier) |\n| Sam Ackah-Yensu (SFV Investor) | co-author on most AI deals | 2024-11 | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-black-forest-labs/) |\n| Kartik Gupta (SFV Principal) | security deals (Wiz, PointFive, Upwind) | 2024-11 | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-next-generation-of-cloud-security/) |\n| Alex Kayyal | Hopin co-author | 2020 | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/hopin-and-the-coming-of-age-of-european-tech/) |\n| John Somorjai (SFV President) | approver (inferred; no IC described) | | [firms/salesforce-ventures](../firms/salesforce-ventures.md) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who prices inside SFV: does Kallen hold any sign-off authority, and what is the threshold above which Somorjai or Salesforce approval is needed?\n- Did SFV invest in ElevenLabs at Series B? SFV says so, but public B coverage does not name SFV.\n- Fetcherr C: re-verify the Kallen quote (the release page returned 403). Also, who actually ran the deal, Kallen or Quie?\n- Is he on any board (Fetcherr, Sixfold, BFL)? Not stated anywhere checked.\n- Dates for Vitruvian and Barclays, and education.\n\n## Sources\n- https://salesforceventures.com/people/nowi-kallen/ (and WP API `wp-json/wp/v2/people?slug=nowi-kallen`)\n- Wayback snapshots of the bio: 20230328023122, 20240620153134, 20250620072000, 20251012234530, 20260214165140, 20260513191008\n- Wayback snapshots of the team page: 20251001040402, 20260205184504, 20260214163052\n- SFV posts: welcome-black-forest-labs, welcome-lovable, welcome-elevenlabs, welcome-chainguard, welcome-pointfive, behind-the-investment-wiz, congratulations-wiz, hopin-and-the-coming-of-age-of-european-tech, the-operating-system-for-modern-legal-work, the-next-generation-of-cloud-security, lovables-cfo-on-choosing-velocity-over-speed, welcome-covecta, welcome-protex-ai, welcome-sixfold, welcome-autogenai\n- https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier\n- https://legora.com/newsroom/legora-raises-550-million-series-e-at-8.8-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-growth\n- https://www.accessnewswire.com/newsroom/en/aerospace-and-defense/fetcherr-raises-42m-in-series-c-funding-led-by-salesforce-ventures-1077687 (403 on 2026-11-10)\n", "person:ofir-ehrlich": "\n# Ofir Ehrlich\n\n## One-line take\nA repeat Israeli infrastructure founder who puts small personal cheques into the seed rounds of the next Israeli AI-security and data companies, usually in a bloc with the Wiz founders [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/euno-raises-23m-series-a-n47-ai-agent-context-platform).\n\n## Crux\nFounders want him as a reference customer and operator: he sold CloudEndure to AWS and took Eon to a unicorn in under a year, so his name tells US investors the product sells in the US. He never leads; every named cheque sits behind Sequoia, Greenoaks, Lightspeed or N47. The constraint is time and size: he is a full-time CEO writing small cheques. This pass found little first-hand material, so treat the page as thin.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Euno's Series A names \"Eon chief executive Ofir Ehrlich\" beside Wiz's Yinon Costica, Cyera's Yotam Segev and Tavily's Rotem Weiss [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/euno-raises-23m-series-a-n47-ai-agent-context-platform).\n- His test of a company: \"build something once and sell it many times\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/8dwwqmx15).\n- What US investors once told him about his Israeli customers: \"your customer base carries no weight for us.\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/8dwwqmx15)\n- \"Persistence is often more powerful than both the idea and the business plan.\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/8dwwqmx15)\n- Grove Ventures, where he sits on the Dev Council, says he has backed more than 25 startups [src](https://grovevc.com/team/devcouncil-ofir-ehrlich).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nLow. He is an Israeli security and infrastructure angel, not a frontier-AI talent investor. He matters only as a signal in Israeli AI-security seeds (Irregular, AIR, NewCore) and as a node next to Sequoia's Dean Meyer and Shaun Maguire.\n\n## Background\n- Co-founded AcceloWeb (acquired by Limelight Networks) and CloudEndure (acquired by AWS), VP R&D at both [src](https://grovevc.com/team/devcouncil-ofir-ehrlich).\n- Co-founded Eon in 2024 with Ron Kimchi and Gonen Stein; Sequoia partnered in 2024 (Shaun Maguire, Dean Meyer) [src](https://sequoiacap.com/founder/ofir-ehrlich).\n- Eon rounds: $20M seed led by Sequoia with Vine Ventures, Meron Capital and Eight Roads [src](https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-israeli-cloud-backup-co-eon-raises-77m-1001491019); later rounds to a reported $4B valuation in Dec 2025 (search summary of ynet, not fetched).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.eon.io/ | company site |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | not found | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\nVehicle: personal. No fund, syndicate or Form D found. Aggregator claims of a venture-partner role at Eight Roads and an advisor role at QP Ventures are unverified.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-03 — CTech Growth+ — \"The real goal of a startup is to build something once and sell it many times\" — [link](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/8dwwqmx15) — sales repeatability, hiring commercial talent early; mined\n- date not found — CTech — How Eon became a $1.4B unicorn in less than a year — [link](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rydjrl3sjl) — not read\n\nNothing found where he talks about angel investing or AI.\n\n## How they think\n- Repeatable sales over marquee customers [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/8dwwqmx15).\n- Hire \"someone with strong business orientation from day one\" [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/8dwwqmx15).\n- Passes or regrets as an investor: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n- The CTech Growth+ interview [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/8dwwqmx15).\n- Sequoia's Eon post, for how the seed came together [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI cheques with a source. \"Early\" marks seed entry. No cheque sizes are disclosed.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Lead / co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Atero (early) | Seed ($12M) | 2024 | no | - | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rkdw11antlx) |\n| Irregular | Series A (second round, ~$50M of $80M total) | 2025-09 | no | Sequoia; Redpoint; Assaf Rappaport | [src](https://www.newswire.com/news/irregular-raises-80-million-to-set-the-security-standards-for-frontier-ai) |\n| NewCore (early) | Seed (expanded, $66M total) | 2026-06 | no | - | [src](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/newcore-raises-66m-seed/) |\n| Harmony (early) | Second seed ($34M) | 2026-07 | no | Lightspeed; Fin Capital; Rappaport (aggregator only) | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/ofir-ehrlich) |\n| AIR (early) | Second seed ($40M) | 2026-09-01 | no | Greenoaks; Sequoia; Yinon Costica | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=3159543) |\n| Euno | Series A ($23M) | 2026-09-09 | no | N47 led; 10D; Costica; Segev; Weiss | [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/euno-raises-23m-series-a-n47-ai-agent-context-platform) |\n\nAggregator-only, not verified (mostly security, some not AI): Daylight Security, Velocity, Waltz, Apono, Finaloop, IVIX, Speedata, Sentra, Reco, Hopper Security, IO River, Prime Security, BlinkOps [src](https://startupim.com/investor/ofir-ehrlich) [src](https://www.startuphub.ai/investors/ofir-ehrlich). One aggregator also lists Engram Labs, but Engram's launch release does not name him [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026062313/engram-launches-with-98m-to-build-ai-that-actually-knows-your-organization/).\n\n**Hit pattern:** Irregular (reported $1.5B talks), AIR and NewCore are the hot ones; in each he is one of several founder-angels and Sequoia or Greenoaks is the institutional lead.\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing first-hand found on any angel deal. On his own company: Dean Meyer, then at Vine Ventures, turned a planned one-on-one lunch with Ehrlich into a meeting with Shaun Maguire in November 2023, and Sequoia led Eon's seed about a week later (see [dean-meyer](dean-meyer.md)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sequoia (Shaun Maguire, Dean Meyer) | Eon seed lead; he co-invests in their Israeli deals (Irregular, AIR) | 2024 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/founder/ofir-ehrlich) |\n| Vine Ventures | Eon seed investor | 2024 | [src](https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-israeli-cloud-backup-co-eon-raises-77m-1001491019) |\n| Yinon Costica (Wiz) | co-angel in Euno and AIR | 2026 | [src](https://thenextweb.com/news/euno-raises-23m-series-a-n47-ai-agent-context-platform) |\n| Assaf Rappaport (Wiz) | co-angel in Irregular | 2025 | [src](https://www.newswire.com/news/irregular-raises-80-million-to-set-the-security-standards-for-frontier-ai) |\n| Grove Ventures | Dev Council member | not stated | [src](https://grovevc.com/team/devcouncil-ofir-ehrlich) |\n\n## Open questions\n- A verified list of the 25-plus cheques; primary sources exist for only six AI companies.\n- Whether he invests through any vehicle, and his typical cheque.\n- Any interview on why he angel invests. The web-search budget ran out before this could be searched properly.\n\n## Sources\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/euno-raises-23m-series-a-n47-ai-agent-context-platform\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/8dwwqmx15\n- https://grovevc.com/team/devcouncil-ofir-ehrlich\n- https://sequoiacap.com/founder/ofir-ehrlich\n- https://startupim.com/investor/ofir-ehrlich ; https://www.startuphub.ai/investors/ofir-ehrlich ; https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/ofir-ehrlich (aggregators)\n", "person:olivier-pomel": "\n# Olivier Pomel\n\n## One-line take\nDatadog's CEO angels with his own money into AI-infra and dev-tool companies that later get Datadog corporate checks or get acquired, so his name on a cap table is an early signal of strategic interest.\n\n## Crux\nAn engineer-CEO (Ecole Centrale CS, VLC co-author) who writes small personal checks alongside top leads (Greylock, Index, FirstMark). He never leads. The corp dev and ventures group (Bharat Sajnani) reports to him, so he is the final approver of Datadog's strategic checks and acquisitions [src](https://builtin.com/job/corporate-development-lead/6878158). His personal and corporate money are separate, but they land in the same companies.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Braintrust: he was in the first round, listed among existing investors in the seed post: \"Olivier Pomel, CEO of Datadog\" [src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round). Datadog corporate then came into the Series A (see firms/datadog.md). Personal money went in first.\n- Daytona: the order flips. Datadog corporate was in the A, then Pomel personally joined the $60M Series B at $1B: \"Olivier Pomel (Datadog), Adam D'Angelo (Quora)\" [src](https://www.daytona.io/dotfiles/daytona-raises-60m-series-b-to-build-the-agent-cloud).\n- Adaptive ML: angel in the Index-led $20M seed, \"many other individuals such as Xavier Niel, Olivier Pomel\" [src](https://www.adaptive-ml.com/post/adaptive-raises-seed); Datadog acquired the company in June 2026 with no priced round in between (firms/datadog.md).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. He is not a lead or a fund, so he is not competition. He is a strategic co-investor, and his presence signals a likely Datadog acquirer or corporate check. Useful for frontier-AI infra seeds (evals, agent runtimes, post-training) where Datadog is a natural buyer. Key deals: Adaptive ML, Braintrust, Daytona.\n\n## Background\n- MS in Computer Science, Ecole Centrale Paris; an original author of VLC [src](https://www.datadoghq.com/about/leadership/).\n- Software engineering at IBM Research and several internet startups (dates unconfirmed) [src](https://www.datadoghq.com/about/leadership/).\n- VP Technology at Wireless Generation, which grew to about 100 engineers before News Corp bought it (dates unconfirmed) [src](https://www.datadoghq.com/about/leadership/).\n- Co-founded Datadog in 2010 and has been CEO since; the seat is a founder's seat.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.datadoghq.com/about/leadership/ | Official bio, verified 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | unconfirmed | not verified |\n| LinkedIn | unconfirmed | not verified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\nHow to reach them: there is no public inbound channel. Go through the lead investors he co-invests with (Index's Hannah Seal and Bastian Hasslinger, Greylock's Jerry Chen) or through Datadog Ventures (Bharat Sajnani). This is inferred from the co-investor lists and has not been confirmed.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNot researched this pass.\n\n## How they think\n- Backs technical infra founders close to Datadog's surface: evals, sandboxes, model customization, observability.\n- Also backs the French founder network: Hugging Face, Dataiku, nexos.ai, Ankar (see data/rounds.csv).\n\n## Read these first\nnothing found this pass.\n\n## Social engagement\nnothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Braintrust | Pre-seed (label unconfirmed; the post only says \"last round\") | 2023-10 (about 6 weeks before 2023-12-13) | no | Greylock led the next seed | [src](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round) |\n| Adaptive ML | Seed ($20M) | 2024 (post dated 2024-09-01; repo has 2024-03-11, unconfirmed) | no | Index (lead), ICONIQ, Xavier Niel | [src](https://www.adaptive-ml.com/post/adaptive-raises-seed) |\n| Daytona | Series B ($60M at $1B) | 2026-10-20 | no | FirstMark Growth (lead), Adam D'Angelo, Brendan Foody | [src](https://www.daytona.io/dotfiles/daytona-raises-60m-series-b-to-build-the-agent-cloud) |\n| Others in data/rounds.csv | Hugging Face B, Dataiku E/F, Mem0, nexos.ai, Niteshift, Entire, Fundamental, RadixArk (aggregator only) | | no | | see data/rounds.csv |\n\n## Deal stories\nAdaptive ML: angel in the seed, then Datadog acquired the company on 2026-06-30. No source shows whether he recused himself from the acquisition decision.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Datadog | CEO & Co-founder | 2010 | [src](https://www.datadoghq.com/about/leadership/) |\n| Bharat Sajnani | Head of Datadog corp dev / ventures; reports to Pomel | | [src](https://builtin.com/job/corporate-development-lead/6878158) |\n| Alexis Le-Quoc | Datadog co-founder; co-angel in Niteshift | | [src](https://niteshift.dev/blog/introducing-niteshift) |\n\n## Open questions\n- His check sizes and whether he uses a personal vehicle (a family office or LLC); nothing found.\n- How Datadog handles conflicts when it acquires a company he angel'd (Adaptive ML).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.datadoghq.com/about/leadership/\n- https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/seed-round\n- https://www.adaptive-ml.com/post/adaptive-raises-seed\n- https://www.daytona.io/dotfiles/daytona-raises-60m-series-b-to-build-the-agent-cloud\n- https://builtin.com/job/corporate-development-lead/6878158\n", "person:pablo-lubroth": "\n# Pablo Lubroth\n\nPronouns: \"he\", per his Decoding Bio bio ([src](https://www.decodingbio.com/about)).\n\n## One-line take\nHe was the junior hire behind Hummingbird's biotech push, and the only Hummingbird junior with a public deal list (Enveda, Waypoint Bio, Kaleidoscope), though only an aggregator ties those deals to him. He then left investing to operate at Enveda, Hummingbird's biotech winner that is now reported at $2B. He matters to you mainly for his AI-for-science access through Decoding Science (DeepMind's Pushmeet Kohli, Stanford's James Zou), not for capital.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he won:** Hummingbird had no biotech expertise. Firat Ileri's plan was to \"hire some junior people who can help us get there, and we pick the founders\" ([Generalist, 2023-12-03](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)). His own bio says he \"built and led the biotech practice at Hummingbird Ventures\" and \"led investments across therapeutics, tools and software\" ([Decoding Bio](https://www.decodingbio.com/about)). My inference: he was the domain filter that the founder-picking partners lacked.\n- **What constrained him:** at Hummingbird, a junior reportedly becomes partner only after returning a fund ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird); the author hedges with \"If true\"). Biotech holds take a long time to return capital. No Hummingbird title for him was ever published, and he is not on the current team page ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team), checked 2026-11-10).\n- **What he did instead:** he moved to operate at Enveda, a Hummingbird biotech company (2019 entry per [portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio)), as VP Special Projects. He still invests in \"AI x Science-related companies as a Venture Partner\" ([Decoding Bio](https://www.decodingbio.com/about)). Which firm that is: not found.\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** write large checks. The aggregator range is $500K–$5M ([startupintros](https://startupintros.com/people/pablo-lubroth)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The role was created by Ileri's biotech bet.** Ileri: \"I'm not a biotech expert, but I'm sure we can hire some junior people\" ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird), 2023-12-03). The Generalist doesn't name Lubroth. The link to him comes from his own \"built and led the biotech practice\" line ([Decoding Bio](https://www.decodingbio.com/about)). The join between the two is my inference.\n- **Why he left (partly inference):** the Generalist reports a rule, hedged with \"If true\", that a junior becomes partner only \"if they returned a fund with their investments\" ([src](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)). It also says Hummingbird juniors get asked \"What companies did you invest in?\" when they job-hunt (same source). An operator role at the firm's biggest biotech winner is one way around that bar (inference).\n- **Enveda's founder on Hummingbird diligence**, the deal he later joined: \"They don't change their mind based on how hot the deal is.\" (Viswa Colluru, [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)). Colluru also describes a partner meeting where \"the entire hour was filled with behavioral questions\" (same source).\n- **His current sourcing channel is AI-for-science media.** In 2025 he started Decoding Science (Substack created 2025-05-21, copyright \"Pablo Lubroth\"; [archive API](https://decodingscience.substack.com/api/v1/archive?sort=new&limit=12)). He interviewed Pushmeet Kohli of Google DeepMind ([2026-05-01](https://decodingscience.substack.com/p/the-three-forms-of-scientific-intelligence)), writing that he \"was keen to understand how he and his team select those problems\". He also interviewed James Zou of Stanford ([2026-09-08](https://decodingbio.substack.com/p/a-conversation-with-james-zou-scientific)). This mirrors Mario Gabriele's media-to-deal-flow funnel at Hummingbird (inference).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** He is bio-focused, now an operator, and writes sub-$5M checks (aggregator), so he is not a co-lead for $20M checks into frontier-lab seeds. Two things keep him above skip:\n1. **AI-for-science access.** His interviews with DeepMind's science unit lead and Stanford's AI for Science Lab, plus his Venture Partner mandate in \"AI x Science\", give him a front-row view of scientific-agent and virtual-lab founders. He is a scout-type contact, not capital.\n2. **Hummingbird bio deals with AI angles:** Enveda (AI plus natural-product drug discovery, ~$2B per WSJ headline) and Waypoint Bio (computer vision and generative AI for cell-therapy design, per [waypointbio.com](https://www.waypointbio.com/)). Neither is in your core lens.\n\n## Background\n- Studied at University College London and the University of Cambridge. He started in neuroscience and pharmacology, then moved to biochemical and genetic engineering ([Decoding Bio](https://www.decodingbio.com/about)). Degrees and years: not found.\n- Corporate development at a genome-editing company; he also incubated companies in biomanufacturing, genome editing and computational biology (same source). TheOrg's logo strip lists Horizon Discovery and Future Planet Capital among prior employers ([TheOrg, aggregator, \"Unverified\"](https://theorg.com/org/basecamp-research/org-chart/pablo-lubroth)). Dates: not found.\n- Hummingbird Ventures: built and led the biotech practice ([Decoding Bio](https://www.decodingbio.com/about)). Start and end dates: not found. Inference: he was likely there by 2019–2022, when the Enveda (2019), Waypoint (2021) and Kaleidoscope (2022) entries were made ([portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio)).\n- Now VP of Special Projects at Enveda, and Venture Partner investing in AI x Science ([Decoding Bio](https://www.decodingbio.com/about)). Start date: not found.\n- Co-founder and editor of Decoding Bio, with Amee Kapadia, Morgan Cheatham, Patrick Malone and Ketan Yerneni ([src](https://www.decodingbio.com/about)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.decodingbio.com/about | Decoding Bio team bio, checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://decodingscience.substack.com/ ; https://decodingbio.substack.com/ | Decoding Science issues every two weeks plus long interviews (Kohli 2026-05-01, Zou 2026-09-08). Subscriber counts not checked |\n| X | https://twitter.com/pablolubroth | linked from Decoding Bio; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/pablolubroth/ | linked from Decoding Bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found (he hosts written interviews) | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found as a guest. The web search budget ran out, so this is provisional.\n- 2026-05-01 — Decoding Science (host, written live interview) — \"The Three Forms of Scientific Intelligence: A Conversation with DeepMind's Pushmeet Kohli\" — [link](https://decodingscience.substack.com/p/the-three-forms-of-scientific-intelligence) — covers Kohli's four-condition problem-selection framework and why DeepMind's science unit takes on about two new problems a year.\n- 2026-09-08 — Decoding Bio / Decoding Science (host) — \"A Conversation with James Zou\" — [link](https://decodingbio.substack.com/p/a-conversation-with-james-zou-scientific) — covers analogical reasoning for scientific creativity, Virtual Labs, and the changing moats between small biotechs and pharma.\n\n## How they think\n- **Themes:** AI moving from scientific tool to collaborator; how labs select problems; virtual cells and scaling laws in biology (from his interview agendas, linked above).\n- **Contrarian views:** nothing found in his own words.\n- **Calls right or wrong:** Enveda, which he has been tied to as an investor (aggregator only), is now a unicorn. Coverage: $130M ([Bloomberg 2024-11-21](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-11-21/ai-drug-discovery-startup-enveda-raises-130-million)), a $150M Series D at unicorn status ([Axios 2025-09-04](https://www.axios.com/pro/biotech-deals/2025/09/04/enveda-raises-150m-unicorn-status)), and a WSJ headline \"AI Biotech Enveda Doubles in Value to $2 Billion\" (2026-09-23, as listed on [enveda.com/news](https://www.enveda.com/news); the WSJ page itself was not fetched). His personal role in that deal is unconfirmed.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Decoding Bio bio](https://www.decodingbio.com/about): his only first-party career summary.\n2. [Kohli interview](https://decodingscience.substack.com/p/the-three-forms-of-scientific-intelligence): what he asks frontier AI-for-science leaders.\n3. [Zou interview](https://decodingbio.substack.com/p/a-conversation-with-james-zou-scientific): his take on agentic science and biotech moats.\n4. [The Generalist on Hummingbird](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird): the firm-side context for why his role existed.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked; X was not fetched. Substack bylines show him co-writing Decoding Science issues 012–017 (2026-01 to 2026-04) with Finn Gottert, \"Dispersion Limits\", Hiya Jain and Carolina Monck ([archive](https://decodingscience.substack.com/api/v1/archive?sort=new&limit=12)).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Enveda | $55M (aggregator says 2022) | 2022 per aggregator; TechCrunch dates a $55M Series B2 to 2024-06-14 | unknown | B2: Microsoft, The Nature Conservancy, Premji Invest, Lingotto, Kinnevik, True Ventures, FPV, Level, Jazz ([TC](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/14/enveda-raises-55m-to-combine-ancient-remedies-with-ai-for-drug-discovery/)); Hummingbird not named in TC | [startupintros (aggregator)](https://startupintros.com/people/pablo-lubroth); Hummingbird entry 2019 \"Early\" per [portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio) |\n| Waypoint Bio | $15M | 2024 (aggregator) | unknown | not found; site shows a \"hummingbird\" logo file among General Catalyst, Mitsui, Alexandria ([site](https://www.waypointbio.com/)) | [startupintros (aggregator)](https://startupintros.com/people/pablo-lubroth); Hummingbird entry 2021 Pre-Seed/Seed per [portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio) |\n| Kaleidoscope | $6M seed | 2022-09-28 (Business Wire URL date) | Hummingbird co-led with Dimension (firm level) | Dimension, Caffeinated, SV Angel, Hawktail, Gunderson, angels ([AlleyWatch](https://www.alleywatch.com/2022/10/kaleidoscope-scientific-research-team-collaboration-platform-bogdan-knezevic/)) | [startupintros (aggregator)](https://startupintros.com/people/pablo-lubroth); [Business Wire](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20220928005019/en/Kaleidoscope-Raises-6M-Seed-Financing-Co-Led-by-Hummingbird-Ventures-and-Dimension) |\n| Basecamp Research | board member (no round) | not found | n/a | n/a | [TheOrg (aggregator, \"Unverified\")](https://theorg.com/org/basecamp-research/org-chart/pablo-lubroth); Hummingbird entry 2020 Pre-Seed/Seed per [portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio) |\n| Cradle Bio | angel | not found | no | not found | [startupintros (aggregator)](https://startupintros.com/people/pablo-lubroth) |\n| Bioptimus | angel | not found | no | not found | [startupintros (aggregator)](https://startupintros.com/people/pablo-lubroth) |\n\nNo primary source (press release, company page or firm page) names him on any of these deals. The Kaleidoscope release, AlleyWatch, the TechCrunch Enveda piece, and the Waypoint and Kaleidoscope sites all omit him.\n\n## Deal stories\n**Enveda (standout deal).**\n- **How he found it, and when:** nothing found. Hummingbird lists Enveda as a 2019 \"Early\" entry ([portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio)). The Generalist lists Enveda among the biotech names added after BillionToOne (2019), the deal Ileri personally sourced ([src](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)). No source says who at Hummingbird sourced Enveda.\n- **Who else was there:** Colluru (CEO) describes a Hummingbird partner meeting of provocative behavioral questions (same source). Other investors named in the 2024 B2: Microsoft, The Nature Conservancy, Premji Invest, Lingotto, Kinnevik, True Ventures, FPV, Level and Jazz ([TC](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/14/enveda-raises-55m-to-combine-ancient-remedies-with-ai-for-drug-discovery/)).\n- **The aggregator's \"$55M, 2022\" vs the record:** it matches the size of the 2024 B2 but not its date. The enveda.com news page lists nothing from 2022. Treat the 2022 date as aggregator-only and unconfirmed.\n- **Worth now:** $230M raised by mid-2024 (TC). Then $130M in 2024-11 (Bloomberg), $150M Series D at unicorn status in 2025-09 (Axios), and a WSJ headline of a $2B valuation in 2026-09 (via [enveda.com/news](https://www.enveda.com/news)).\n- **After:** he joined Enveda as VP Special Projects ([Decoding Bio](https://www.decodingbio.com/about)). Date not found.\n\n**Kaleidoscope.** A $6M seed co-led by Hummingbird and Dimension, announced 2022-09-28 ([Business Wire](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20220928005019/en/Kaleidoscope-Raises-6M-Seed-Financing-Co-Led-by-Hummingbird-Ventures-and-Dimension), [AlleyWatch](https://www.alleywatch.com/2022/10/kaleidoscope-scientific-research-team-collaboration-platform-bogdan-knezevic/)). Founders: Bogdan Knezevic (CEO), Ahmed Elnaiem and David Yen ([kaleidoscope.bio](https://www.kaleidoscope.bio/company)). The company is now positioned as R&D orchestration for biopharma, CROs and AI agents. Intro path: not found.\n\n**Waypoint Bio.** Hummingbird's entry was 2021 Pre-Seed/Seed per its portfolio page. The aggregator's $15M (2024) is therefore likely a later round, not his entry check. Founders: Xinchen Wang and David Phizicky ([team](https://www.waypointbio.com/team)). Intro path: not found.\n\n**How he sources (now):** through media. He co-founded Decoding Bio and started Decoding Science in 2025, which lets him interview frontier AI-for-science leaders (Kohli, Zou). Sourcing at Hummingbird: nothing specific to him found. The firm targets 20–30 companies a week per investor ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hummingbird Ventures | former investor; built and led the biotech practice | not found | [Decoding Bio](https://www.decodingbio.com/about) |\n| Firat Ileri | MP whose biotech push created the junior biotech role (inference) | not found | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Enveda (Viswa Colluru, CEO) | VP Special Projects | not found | [Decoding Bio](https://www.decodingbio.com/about) |\n| Basecamp Research | board member (aggregator, unverified) | not found | [TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/basecamp-research/org-chart/pablo-lubroth) |\n| Decoding Bio co-founders (Amee Kapadia, Morgan Cheatham, Patrick Malone, Ketan Yerneni) | co-founder | not found | [Decoding Bio](https://www.decodingbio.com/about) |\n| Pushmeet Kohli (Google DeepMind) | interviewee | 2026-05 | [Decoding Science](https://decodingscience.substack.com/p/the-three-forms-of-scientific-intelligence) |\n| James Zou (Stanford) | interviewee | 2026-09 | [Decoding Bio](https://decodingbio.substack.com/p/a-conversation-with-james-zou-scientific) |\n| Dimension | co-lead with Hummingbird on Kaleidoscope (firm level) | 2022-09 | [AlleyWatch](https://www.alleywatch.com/2022/10/kaleidoscope-scientific-research-team-collaboration-platform-bogdan-knezevic/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which firm is he a Venture Partner at? Does he write AI-for-science seed checks there?\n- When did he join and leave Hummingbird, and what was his title?\n- Did he source Enveda, or did he only lead later rounds? Is \"$55M, 2022\" a misdated 2024 B2?\n- Did he personally lead the Kaleidoscope co-lead (2022)?\n- Has any Decoding Science interviewee (for example from Zou's Virtual Lab) since raised from him or his firm?\n- Note on research limits: WebSearch was exhausted this turn, so podcast appearances, X activity and the \"none found\" attributions are provisional.\n\n## Sources\n- https://startupintros.com/people/pablo-lubroth (aggregator)\n- https://theorg.com/org/basecamp-research/org-chart/pablo-lubroth (aggregator)\n- https://www.decodingbio.com/about\n- https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird\n- https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio\n- https://www.hummingbird.vc/team\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/14/enveda-raises-55m-to-combine-ancient-remedies-with-ai-for-drug-discovery/\n- https://www.enveda.com/news\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-11-21/ai-drug-discovery-startup-enveda-raises-130-million\n- https://www.axios.com/pro/biotech-deals/2025/09/04/enveda-raises-150m-unicorn-status\n- https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20220928005019/en/Kaleidoscope-Raises-6M-Seed-Financing-Co-Led-by-Hummingbird-Ventures-and-Dimension\n- https://www.alleywatch.com/2022/10/kaleidoscope-scientific-research-team-collaboration-platform-bogdan-knezevic/\n- https://www.kaleidoscope.bio/company\n- https://www.waypointbio.com/ ; https://www.waypointbio.com/team\n- https://decodingscience.substack.com/p/the-three-forms-of-scientific-intelligence\n- https://decodingbio.substack.com/p/a-conversation-with-james-zou-scientific\n", "person:parasvil-patel": "\n# Parasvil Patel\n\n## One-line take\nPatel is the partner at Radical who leads Series A and B rounds in applied AI for physical industries (agriculture, climate, satellites, industrial design, training-data tooling), not frontier labs. He is useful to you as a Radical co-investor and board contact, not as an early source of frontier-lab deals.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He was one of Radical's first investment-team hires after Fund 2 launched in 2019, and he helped build the team's process and the go-to-market work for the Velocity program [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/). He made Partner in about two years. His deals are Series A leads that come with board seats (ClimateAi, V7, Enriched Ag). He came from KKR and BCG, so he brings operating and value-creation skills rather than research credentials.\n- **What constrains him:** He is not a researcher, and none of his named deals is a lab or a compute company. At Radical, the researcher-founder and frontier deals go to Jacobs, Toews, Rosenberg and Katz (see [firms/radical-ventures.md](../firms/radical-ventures.md)). After the 2022 run (Pixxel, Enriched Ag, V7) and Graft in 2023, the only new deal publicly tied to him is Vizcom in 2025.\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** Lead a pre-product mega-seed for a research lab. His pattern is to come in once there is a product and an industrial customer, and to help with operations from the board.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He is a Series A/B entrant, not a first-check investor.** On Vizcom, Radical came in third: Unusual led the ~$5M seed (2023) and Index led the $20M A (2024). Radical then led the $27M B in 2025, with Index, Unusual and Basis Set coming back. Patel and Mehta wrote the investment post. The thesis: product design is \"stubbornly stuck in the past\" and Vizcom wants to be \"the Figma for the physical world\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/building-the-future-of-industrial-design/) [src](https://vizcom.ai/blog/vizcom-raises-series-a-funding).\n- **His moat view is data network effects, not models.** The Vizcom post bets on a \"powerful moat through data network effects\" from models tuned to each customer. It also argues that the valuable apps \"go beyond automating tasks\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/building-the-future-of-industrial-design/). The V7 quote follows the same line: V7 is \"well-positioned to become the industry-standard for managing data\" [src](https://www.v7labs.com/news/v7-raises-33m-series-a).\n- **Climate-adaptation thread (2021–22).** ClimateAi (led the $12M A, board), Pixxel (led the $25M A, at the time India's largest spacetech raise) and Enriched Ag (co-led the $9M seed with Future Ventures, board). For ClimateAi his pitch was that \"traditional forecasting is highly inaccurate beyond two-week timeframes\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/our-investment-in-climateai/) [src](https://thelogic.co/briefing/torontos-radical-ventures-leads-us12m-series-a-in-climateai/) [src](https://payloadspace.com/pixxel-series-a/).\n- **Public stage with CentML's CEO two months before Radical's CentML post (relationship, not deal credit).** On 2023-04-24 Patel ran a U of T fireside, \"Starting a Company as an AI Researcher,\" with Gennady Pekhimenko, already CentML's CEO [src](https://web.cs.toronto.edu/news-events/events/mscac-talks-april-2023-parasvil-patel-gennady-pekhimenko). Radical's CentML seed post came out in 2023-06 and credits David Katz. No source puts Patel on the CentML deal.\n- **People-management writing as a Principal.** In a May 2020 BetaKit op-ed he told founders to \"start the discussion by being vulnerable and sharing what they are feeling.\" It shows the operator, coaching-style board role he plays [src](https://betakit.com/not-fine-and-thats-ok-a-model-for-managing-teams-through-crisis/).\n- **Graft may have exited into another Radical company (unverified).** Radical led Graft's $10M seed in July 2023, with GV participating, and Patel's team page names him on it [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/07/26/graft-is-building-an-ai-development-platform-for-the-masses/amp/). Aggregators say You.com, another Radical portfolio company, acquired Graft's team in October 2025. Not confirmed from a primary source.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** None of his deals is in your core lens (pre-training, new labs, chips, mega-seeds). The closest are V7 (training-data infrastructure; co-led the $33M A with Temasek; board) and Graft (AI platform). He matters because he is a partner who can lead a $10M+ round at a firm that is now raising a $1B+ Breakouts fund, and because he sits on applied-AI boards where you might co-invest. Cohere is on his team page, but no source shows his role there.\n\n## Background\n- B.Tech. in Electrical Engineering, IIT Bombay [src](https://radical.vc/team/parasvil-patel/)\n- BCG consultant in India and the US [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/)\n- MBA, Harvard Business School (Baker Scholar). At HBS he ran business operations for an early-stage cloud-infrastructure startup [src](https://radical.vc/team/parasvil-patel/)\n- KKR, New York: diligence and portfolio value creation across growth equity and PE. MarketScreener (aggregator) gives Principal, 2015 to 2019-01 [src](https://be.marketscreener.com/insider/PARASVIL-PATEL-A2RMDJ/)\n- Radical Ventures, investment team \"soon after\" Fund 2 launched; the US$350M fund was announced 2019-05-07 [src](https://www.investpsp.com/en/news/radical-ventures-launches-usd-350-million-vc-fund-focused-on-artificial-intelligence). Aggregator start date 2019-07 [src](https://be.marketscreener.com/insider/PARASVIL-PATEL-A2RMDJ/). His BetaKit byline said Principal in 2020-05 [src](https://betakit.com/author/parasvil/). Promoted to Partner on 2021-09-20, alongside CFO Blair Bernholtz, in the same post that announced Rob Toews' hire [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/) [src](https://betakit.com/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-partner-in-san-francisco/)\n- Pronouns: the Radical team page uses \"he.\"\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/parasvil-patel/ | Team bio; names Cohere, ClimateAi, Enriched Ag, Graft, Pixxel, V7 Labs (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://radical.vc/articles/building-the-future-of-industrial-design/ | Radical investment posts (ClimateAi 2021, Vizcom 2025); one BetaKit op-ed (2020-05) https://betakit.com/author/parasvil/ |\n| X | nothing found | No personal handle found; the team page links only @radicalvcfund |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/parasvil/ | Linked from the radical.vc team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Not a host; Radical Talks is hosted by Molly Welch |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-07 — AI in NYC Show — \"AI Innovator's Podcast: Parasvil Patel from Radical Ventures.\" Show notes say he covered Radical's culture and where long-term AI moats will form, and that he argued for or against the host's \"5 Contrarian AI Theses For Early Stage Investors.\" No transcript available, so no quotes [src](https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/ai-in-nyc-show/episode/ai-innovators-podcast-parasvil-patel-from-radical-ventures-28000)\n- 2023-04-24 — U of T MScAC Talks — \"Starting a Company as an AI Researcher,\" with Gennady Pekhimenko (CentML) [src](https://web.cs.toronto.edu/news-events/events/mscac-talks-april-2023-parasvil-patel-gennady-pekhimenko)\n- 2023 — U of T MScAC Talks — \"Entrepreneurship and innovation at the intersection of AI and climate,\" with ClimateAi co-founder Max Evans [src](https://web.cs.toronto.edu/news-events/events/entrepreneurship-and-innovation-at-the-intersection-of-ai-and-climate-mscac-talks)\n- 2022-10-24 — U of T Mechanical and Industrial Engineering talk [src](https://mie.utoronto.ca/?p=22449)\n- Speaker bios at the Rosenblatt fintech summit, Kennedy Human Rights and Underscore VC events (no content found) [src](https://rblt.com/fintech-summit-biography/parasvil-patel)\n\n## How they think\n- Applied AI wins on workflow and proprietary data loops, not model novelty (Vizcom, V7).\n- Physical and industrial sectors that are behind software are the opportunity: agriculture, climate risk, earth observation, industrial design.\n- Calls: Pixxel and V7 are still active. Graft is reportedly acquired (unverified). ClimateAi and Enriched Ag no longer appear on Radical's current portfolio page (see the firm file), so their outcomes are unclear.\n\n## Read these first\n- Vizcom investment post (2025-11, with Richa Mehta): his clearest current thesis on moats in applied AI. https://radical.vc/articles/building-the-future-of-industrial-design/\n- ClimateAi investment post (2021-07): the climate-adaptation thesis. https://radical.vc/articles/our-investment-in-climateai/\n- BetaKit op-ed (2020-05): how he coaches founders and teams. https://betakit.com/not-fine-and-thats-ok-a-model-for-managing-teams-through-crisis/\n\n## Social engagement\nNo personal X account found. His public presence is Radical posts, U of T student talks and conference speaker bios.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cohere | Seed (founding round; Patel's role not stated) | 2019 | Radical yes | — | [src](https://radical.vc/team/parasvil-patel/) |\n| ClimateAi | Series A ($12M) | 2021-07-21 | yes (board) | FootPrint Coalition; Finistere; Neotribe; AMECloud; ThirdStream | [src](https://thelogic.co/briefing/torontos-radical-ventures-leads-us12m-series-a-in-climateai/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/our-investment-in-climateai/) |\n| Pixxel | Series A ($25M) | 2022-03-28 | yes | Seraphim; Lightspeed Partners; Blume; Sparta; Jordan Noone | [src](https://payloadspace.com/pixxel-series-a/) |\n| V7 | Series A ($33M) | 2022-11-28 | co-led with Temasek (board) | Air Street; Amadeus; Partech; Chollet, Vinyals, Vaswani (angels) | [src](https://www.v7labs.com/news/v7-raises-33m-series-a) |\n| Enriched Ag | Seed ($9M) | 2022-11 | co-led with Future Ventures (board) | — | [src](https://www.accessnewswire.com/newsroom/en/agriculture/enriched-ag-launches-with-9m-seed-funding-to-help-unlock-the-value-and-resilience-of-gra-725033) |\n| Graft | Seed ($10M) | 2023-07-26 | Radical yes | GV | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/07/26/graft-is-building-an-ai-development-platform-for-the-masses/amp/) |\n| Vizcom | Series B ($27M) | 2025-10-29 (announced 2025-11-03) | yes | Index; Unusual; Basis Set | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/building-the-future-of-industrial-design/) |\n\n**Timing:** Radical was first in at Cohere and Enriched Ag (co-lead). At ClimateAi, Pixxel and V7 it was the first institutional lead at Series A, after seed funds. At Vizcom it came in third. No double-down is documented for any of his own deals.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **V7:** Radical co-led the A with Temasek on top of the existing investors (Air Street, Amadeus, Partech). Patel took the board seat [src](https://www.v7labs.com/news/v7-raises-33m-series-a).\n- **ClimateAi:** Radical led the A, and FootPrint Coalition (Robert Downey Jr.) followed on. Patel wrote the post and is named on the board [src](https://radical.vc/articles/our-investment-in-climateai/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/).\n- **Vizcom:** Radical made its first entry as lead of the B, after Unusual's seed and Index's A. Patel co-wrote the post with Richa Mehta (growth co-lead), which points to growth-team involvement [src](https://radical.vc/articles/building-the-future-of-industrial-design/).\n- Intro paths: none found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Partner | 2021-09-20 (on the team since 2019) | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/) |\n| Richa Mehta | Co-author and co-deal lead on Vizcom | 2025 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/building-the-future-of-industrial-design/) |\n| Blair Bernholtz | Promoted to Partner in the same announcement | 2021-09 | same |\n| Jordan Jacobs | Reports to (TheOrg, aggregator); wrote the promotion post | 2019 | [src](https://theorg.com/org/radical-ventures/org-chart/parasvil-patel) |\n| Gennady Pekhimenko (CentML) | Fireside co-speaker | 2023-04 | [src](https://web.cs.toronto.edu/news-events/events/mscac-talks-april-2023-parasvil-patel-gennady-pekhimenko) |\n| ClimateAi, V7, Enriched Ag | Board member | 2021 / 2022 / 2022 | sources above |\n| KKR | Former (PE/growth diligence) | until 2019 | [src](https://radical.vc/team/parasvil-patel/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- His exact role on Cohere: seed sourcing, diligence or board observer?\n- Is he involved in any Breakouts Fund or 2026 deals? None are public.\n- The AI in NYC episode (2023-07) has no transcript yet. It may contain his contrarian theses.\n- Confirm the Graft to You.com acquisition, and what happened to ClimateAi and Enriched Ag.\n- Is his day-to-day base Toronto or New York?\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/parasvil-patel/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/\n- https://betakit.com/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-partner-in-san-francisco/\n- https://betakit.com/author/parasvil/ ; https://betakit.com/not-fine-and-thats-ok-a-model-for-managing-teams-through-crisis/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/building-the-future-of-industrial-design/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/our-investment-in-climateai/\n- https://thelogic.co/briefing/torontos-radical-ventures-leads-us12m-series-a-in-climateai/\n- https://payloadspace.com/pixxel-series-a/\n- https://www.v7labs.com/news/v7-raises-33m-series-a\n- https://www.accessnewswire.com/newsroom/en/agriculture/enriched-ag-launches-with-9m-seed-funding-to-help-unlock-the-value-and-resilience-of-gra-725033\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/07/26/graft-is-building-an-ai-development-platform-for-the-masses/amp/\n- https://vizcom.ai/blog/vizcom-raises-series-a-funding\n- https://www.investpsp.com/en/news/radical-ventures-launches-usd-350-million-vc-fund-focused-on-artificial-intelligence\n- https://web.cs.toronto.edu/news-events/events/mscac-talks-april-2023-parasvil-patel-gennady-pekhimenko\n- https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/ai-in-nyc-show/episode/ai-innovators-podcast-parasvil-patel-from-radical-ventures-28000\n- https://be.marketscreener.com/insider/PARASVIL-PATEL-A2RMDJ/ (aggregator)\n- https://theorg.com/org/radical-ventures/org-chart/parasvil-patel (aggregator)\n", "person:pascal-cagni": "\n# Pascal Cagni\n\n## One-line take\nAn operator with French state standing whose small fund gets into rounds like AMI Labs and Graphcore on access and European distribution, not cheque size.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** founders want the person who took Apple's EMEIA revenue from about $1B to about $40B and who can open European customers and government doors. He holds a presidential appointment as chairman of Business France ([bio](https://c4v.com/people/team/pascal-cagni/)).\n- **What constrains him:** fund size. Tickets are EUR2-5M, and Fund III's first close was EUR40M against a EUR100M target.\n- **What he'd never do:** build a large platform firm. The firm's line is that venture \"doesn't scale\".\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Fund III's first two deals were not Series As.** They were Bedrock Robotics' $270M Series B and AMI Labs' $1.03B seed, both very large rounds where C4 is a minor participant ([Bedrock](https://c4v.com/news/c4-ventures-iii-makes-it-first-investment-in-bedrock-robotics/), [AMI](https://c4v.com/news/c4-ventures-backs-yann-lecuns-ami-labs-in-a-landmark-1b-round/)).\n- **On AMI Labs:** \"AMI Labs embodies scientific ambition at the highest level\" (same announcement). He frames it as a European sovereignty bet.\n- **State role:** his bio says he was \"appointed by French President Emmanuel Macron\" as chairman of Business France and ambassador for international investment, since 2017.\n- **Graphcore:** Nigel Toon credits C4 as \"among the earliest investors to spot the potential in Graphcore\" ([page](https://c4v.com/companies/graphcore/)).\n- **He was an angel in Nest (January 2012)** before the fund existed ([page](https://c4v.com/companies/nest/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He backs AI chips and compute early by European standards (Graphcore 2016, Arago 2025) and is in a frontier-lab seed, but as a small follow-on cheque. Useful as a French co-investor and for European customer access for a lab or chip company.\n\n## Background\nFrom his C4 bio ([src](https://c4v.com/people/team/pascal-cagni/)) and the firm timeline ([src](https://c4v.com/about/who-we-are/)).\n- Booz Allen in California; Compaq from 1988, where he met Eric Boustouller.\n- Packard Bell from 1995, Europe general manager.\n- Apple, VP and GM EMEIA, 2000 to 2012, hired by Steve Jobs.\n- C4 Industries (2004) for personal minority investments; C4 Ventures from 2014 with Boris Bakech.\n- Chairman of Business France since 2017.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://c4v.com/people/team/pascal-cagni/ | Bio and companies backed |\n| Substack / blog | https://c4v.com/news/ | Firm news; quotes in deal posts |\n| X | not checked | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2020-06 — TechCrunch interview on Fund II — [link](https://techcrunch.com/2020/06/03/c4-ventures-raises-88m-fund-for-for-post-series-a-startups-in-a-post-covid19-world/) — said the firm would raise and deploy through COVID \"as planned\"\n- 2024 — PrivateEquityVox podcast after IPEM Paris — listed on [C4 news](https://c4v.com/news/); not opened\n\n## How they think\n- He calls AI the biggest technology shift since the Internet and says Fund III is \"doubling down on this moment\" ([Fund III release](https://c4v.com/news/c4-ventures-launches-e100m-fund-iii-to-back-europes-next-generation-of-ai-and-deep-tech-leaders/)).\n- Hardware is a strength, not a risk: the firm counts five hardware unicorns.\n- Europe can build global companies; C4 also helps US and Israeli companies enter Europe.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Fund III release](https://c4v.com/news/c4-ventures-launches-e100m-fund-iii-to-back-europes-next-generation-of-ai-and-deep-tech-leaders/).\n2. [AMI Labs announcement](https://c4v.com/news/c4-ventures-backs-yann-lecuns-ami-labs-in-a-landmark-1b-round/).\n3. [Who we are](https://c4v.com/about/who-we-are/): the firm timeline.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| AMI Labs | Seed ($1.03B) | 2026-03 | no | see ledger | [announcement](https://c4v.com/news/c4-ventures-backs-yann-lecuns-ami-labs-in-a-landmark-1b-round/) |\n| Graphcore | Series A ($30M) | 2016-10 | no | Bosch, Amadeus, Draper Esprit, Foundation, Pitango, Samsung | [page](https://c4v.com/companies/graphcore/) |\n| DriveNets | Series A ($110M) | 2019-03 | no | see ledger | [page](https://c4v.com/companies/drivenets/) |\n| Formlabs | not stated | 2016-08 | not stated | | [page](https://c4v.com/companies/formlabs/) |\n| Via | not stated | 2016-05 | not stated | | [page](https://c4v.com/companies/via/) |\n| Riskified | not stated | 2017-06 | not stated | | [page](https://c4v.com/companies/riskified/) |\n| Nest (angel) | not stated | 2012-01 | no | | [page](https://c4v.com/companies/nest/) |\n| Netatmo (angel) | not stated | 2013-05 | no | | [page](https://c4v.com/companies/netatmo/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **AMI Labs:** C4 joined the round through Fund III; the path in is not documented. See firm page.\n- **Graphcore:** in at the 2016 Series A and in each round through the Series D (ledger).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Boris Bakech | his analyst at Apple, then co-founder | 2011 | [who we are](https://c4v.com/about/who-we-are/) |\n| Eric Boustouller | colleague at Compaq, now partner | 1988 | [who we are](https://c4v.com/about/who-we-are/) |\n| Emmanuel Macron | appointed him chairman of Business France | 2017 | [bio](https://c4v.com/people/team/pascal-cagni/) |\n| Nigel Toon | Graphcore CEO he backed | 2016 | [page](https://c4v.com/companies/graphcore/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- How C4 got its AMI Labs allocation and how large it is.\n- His own interviews in French press (not searched in depth).\n\n## Sources\nLinked inline.\n", "person:pat-grady": "\n# Pat Grady\n\n## One-line take\nSequoia's co-steward and the public voice of its \"AI apps / service-as-a-software\" thesis: they rarely back labs or chips himself, but they decide firm capital and they pick AI-native automation (Harvey, OpenEvidence, Serval, Rogo) early. Their seed deals come almost entirely from their own SaaS network (HubSpot, Drift, Outreach, ex-Sequoia partners).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** Two assets. (1) A 19-year SaaS growth book (HubSpot 2011, ServiceNow 2009, Okta, Qualtrics, Snowflake, Zoom) produced an operator alumni network that now founds AI-native apps and comes back to them for seed: Day.ai (HubSpot CPO), Agency (Drift founder), Paid (Outreach founder), Blockit (ex-Sequoia partner) ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/pat-grady/)). (2) A thesis platform co-written with Sonya Huang: the GPT-3, Act Two, Act o1 and \"2026: This is AGI\" essays plus the Training Data podcast, which they host ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/2026-this-is-agi/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-daniel-nadler/)).\n- **What constrains him:** They are co-steward of a firm that just cut back to seven GPs and closed Arc and Scouts ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)). Two of their own seeds (Day.ai, Agency) came via Arc, so that funnel is gone ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-day-ai-customer-obsession-productized/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/agency/)). On AI their own publicly stated view puts the application layer ahead of models for venture returns ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ais-act-o1/)).\n- **What they'd never do (on the evidence):** Be the first check into a pre-training lab or AI chip company. No lab, chip or compute seed carries their name. OpenAI (2021 share sale) and Hugging Face (2022 Series C, Lux led) were late, non-lead entries ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/openai/), [src](https://huggingface.co/blog/series-c)). Trajectory (post-training tooling, Series A led 2026-11) is the closest they get to the research stack ([src](https://trajectory.ai/field-notes/introducing-the-trajectory-platform)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **They think the app layer, not models, is where VC returns are.** In Act o1 they and Huang call the application layer \"The most interesting layer for venture capital.\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ais-act-o1/), 2024-10-09). This is the opposite of the owner's lab-first lens and explains their deal list.\n- **They publicly logged their own forecasting mistakes.** Act Two admits they expected the separation of model and app layers and durable data moats, and both were wrong: \"The moats are in the customers, not the data.\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ai-act-two/), 2023-09-20).\n- **Long-horizon agents are their single bet.** \"If there's one exponential curve to bet on, it's the performance of long-horizon agents.\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/2026-this-is-agi/), 2026-01-14). The essay names XBOW, Harvey, OpenEvidence, Day AI, Juicebox and Ricursive, nearly all Sequoia companies.\n- **They ask founders whether foundation models are commoditizing.** On Training Data: \"Broad domain, general purpose foundation models, they are commoditizing. Yes or no?\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-daniel-nadler/)). Nadler's answer: frontier models stay distinct but business value sits in apps.\n- **Blockit is a bet on a former Sequoia partner.** Sequoia \"invested in Kais in 2019 as a partner\"; Grady wrote \"This was his calling, and we were delighted to see him pursue it.\" They led the $5M seed and forecasts $1Bn+ revenue ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/the-most-precious-resource/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/22/former-sequoia-partners-new-startup-uses-ai-to-negotiate-your-calendar-for-you/), 2026-01-22). Sequoia's company page dates the partnership to 2024, about 18 months before the announced seed ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/blockit-ai/)).\n- **Day.ai was sourced inside HubSpot.** The Sequoia post (Team Sequoia byline; Grady is the only partner on the company page) says the author met both founders at HubSpot and visited O'Donnell when Day.ai was about a week old (May 2023), then led the seed via Arc: \"But we knew Christopher and Michael were a team we could believe in\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-day-ai-customer-obsession-productized/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/day-ai/)). Grady was on HubSpot from 2011 ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/pat-grady/)); Grady introduced O'Donnell on Training Data as \"the man who built that business at HubSpot\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-christopher-odonnell/)).\n- **Same pattern with Paid and Agency.** Paid's EUR10M first round was \"led by EQT and Sequoia\" for Outreach founder Manny Medina; Grady is the only Sequoia partner on it ([src](https://paid.ai/blog/paid-raises-10m-to-build-the-business), 2025-03-25; [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/paid/)). Agency is Drift founder Elias Torres' AI customer-success company, partnered through Arc 2024 with Julien Bek ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/founder/elias-torres/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/agency/)). Drift was a Grady deal from 2017 ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/pat-grady/)).\n- **First institutional money in OpenEvidence.** Grady's memo says Sequoia led the Series A as first institutional investor and \"the engine of OpenEvidence's growth is trust.\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-openevidence-a-life-saving-healthcare-revolution/), 2025-02-19). Co-signer: James Flynn ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/openevidence/)).\n- **Rogo's Series C was a Sequoia lead but Halligan was the voice.** The company post names Sequoia as lead and quotes Brian Halligan, not Grady ([src](https://rogo.com/blog/scaling-rogo-to-build-the-future-of-investment-banking-our-75m-series-c-and-european-expansion), 2026-01-28). Grady, Halligan and Flynn are all on the Rogo page ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/rogo/)). Pattern: on AI-native vertical apps, Grady pairs with HubSpot co-founder Halligan (Harvey, Rogo, Serval) ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/brian-halligan/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Not a lab or chip investor: his AI book is application layer and growth, and they have said in writing that apps are the best layer for venture. Why it is not low:\n- They are co-steward, so they sign off on Sequoia's mega-seeds and growth checks in labs.\n- They are directly on AI-native automation, which the owner likes: Serval (Series B preempt 2025, co-author of the memo), Rogo, Harvey, Day.ai, Agency, Paid ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-serval-empowering-it-for-ai-enterprise-automation/)).\n- Trajectory (post-training tooling; team from Gemini 3, GPT-4o and Meta Superintelligence; $45M Series A at $300M, Sequoia led, NVIDIA in) is the deal that matters most here ([src](https://trajectory.ai/field-notes/introducing-the-trajectory-platform)).\n- Co-investment angle for the owner: their seeds are small and network-sourced ($5M Blockit, EUR10M Paid), so they are not a competitor for $20M slices of $600M lab seeds; they are a possible later-round partner for AI-native automation companies.\n\n## Background\n- High-school construction jobs, then inside sales after college ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/pat-grady/)). Education: nothing found from a primary source.\n- At Sequoia by 2007: Jive Software (2007) is their earliest listed deal. The Sequoia page links an X post titled \"15 Years at Sequoia\", which implies 2007, but the post could not be retrieved (X returned 402) ([src](https://twitter.com/gradypb/status/1500853901901197314), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/pat-grady/)).\n- Growth-stage deals: ServiceNow (2009), OpenDNS (2009), Sunrun (2010), HubSpot (2011), Medallia (2011), Okta (2012), Qualtrics (2012), Sumo Logic (2014), Zoom (2016), Drift (2017), Snowflake (2018), Amplitude (2018) ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/pat-grady/)).\n- Co-steward with Alfred Lin; one of seven owner-GPs named in Nov 2026 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/pat-grady/ | Bio, deal list, articles (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | Sequoia articles (with Sonya Huang) | e.g. https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/2026-this-is-agi/ ; roughly one AI thesis essay a year |\n| X | https://x.com/gradypb | Handle from Sequoia page link; follower count not checked (fetch blocked) |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Training Data, https://www.sequoiacap.com/series/training-data | Hosts episodes (Daniel Nadler, Christopher O'Donnell, Dust) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-07 — 20VC — Sequoia investment process with Pat Grady — https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/pat-grady — Founder framework (early vs late), team/traction/TAM ranking, lessons from Leone, Botha, Lin. Show notes only; no transcript read.\n- 2025 — Upfront Summit — Adapting to Change in Venture Capital and AI — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fEE5sfroDcw — not transcribed here.\n- Uncapped (Jack Altman) Ep. 36 — Pat Grady & Alfred Lin on the Tactics of Great Venture Investing — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fq33ylz7IMc — no transcript retrieved.\n- 2026-01 — TBPN — The Era of AI Agents — https://x.com/tbpn/status/2012303522474377326 — not retrieved (X blocked).\n- Training Data host — OpenEvidence (Nadler): asks whether general models are commoditizing — https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-daniel-nadler/\n- Training Data host — Day.ai (O'Donnell), recorded in Boston — https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-christopher-odonnell/\n- Training Data co-host with Konstantine Buhler — Dust — \"Short-term pessimist, long-term optimist.\" — https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-dust/\n- 2022-07 — CNBC Closing Bell — tough times build strong companies — https://www.cnbc.com/video/2022/07/08/tough-times-like-today-help-build-strong-companies-says-sequoia-capitals-grady.html\n\n## How they think\n- **Apps over models for VC returns.** Act o1: \"service-as-a-software\", the target market is services spend, not software spend ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ais-act-o1/)).\n- **Agents sold as labor.** 2026 essay: \"Soon you'll be able to hire an agent\"; founders should sell outcomes, not seats ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/2026-this-is-agi/)). Paid (agent billing) is tthey pick-and-shovels version of the same view ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/paid/)).\n- **Moats = customers and workflows,** not data ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ai-act-two/)).\n- **Repeat leads as signal.** Calls Sequoia's three Harvey leads \"the ultimate sign of conviction\" ([src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/03/25/legal-ai-startup-harvey-raises-200-million-at-11-billion-valuation.html)).\n- **Calls admitted wrong:** Act One timing (expected a decade), GPU supply, model/app layer separation, data moats ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ai-act-two/)).\n- **Calls right:** legal (\"ultimate text-in, text-out business\", Harvey A in 2023 at $21M, now $11B) ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-harvey-putting-llms-to-work/), [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/03/25/legal-ai-startup-harvey-raises-200-million-at-11-billion-valuation.html)); OpenEvidence first institutional.\n\n## Read these first\n1. 2026: This is AGI (with Sonya Huang, 2026-01) — long-horizon agents as the one curve to bet on. https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/2026-this-is-agi/\n2. Generative AI's Act o1 (2024-10) — inference-time compute and \"service-as-a-software\"; apps are the VC layer. https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ais-act-o1/\n3. Generative AI's Act Two (2023-09) — self-graded predictions and where moats moved. https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ai-act-two/\n4. Partnering with Harvey (2023-04, with Charlie Curnin) — why legal first. https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-harvey-putting-llms-to-work/\n5. The Most Precious Resource (2026-01) — Blockit; shows their network-seed pattern. https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/the-most-precious-resource/\n\n## Social engagement\nX handle @gradypb; posts could not be retrieved (X returned 402). Their public AI output is mostly Sequoia essays co-authored with Sonya Huang and Training Data episodes co-hosted with Huang, Buhler and Lauren Reeder (Paid episode) ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/paid/)). Amplification patterns: nothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI deals only; full list on [his Sequoia page](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/pat-grady/).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | 2021 share sale (Sequoia \"Growth, 2021\") | 2021 | no | Tiger Global; Sequoia team Lin, Huang, Flynn | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/openai/) |\n| Hugging Face | Series C $100M | 2022-05-09 | no (Lux led) | Lux, Coatue, Addition, SV Angel, AIX; Sequoia team Cahn, Huang | [src](https://huggingface.co/blog/series-c), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/hugging-face/) |\n| Harvey | Series A $21M | 2023-04-26 | yes (first priced institutional lead) | co-signer Charlie Curnin | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-harvey-putting-llms-to-work/) |\n| Harvey | Series D $300M at $3B | 2025-02-12 | yes (Sequoia; Grady inferred) | | [src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-d) |\n| Harvey | Growth $200M at $11B | 2026-03-25 | co-lead with GIC | Grady named | [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/03/25/legal-ai-startup-harvey-raises-200-million-at-11-billion-valuation.html) |\n| Day.ai | Seed (amount n/d) | 2023 (post 2024-06-20) | yes, first institutional, via Arc | sole Sequoia partner | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-day-ai-customer-obsession-productized/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/day-ai/) |\n| Agency | Arc 2024 (pre-seed/seed) | 2024 | unknown | Julien Bek | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/agency/) |\n| Blockit AI | Seed $5M | announced 2026-01-22 (partnered 2024) | yes | sole Sequoia partner | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/22/former-sequoia-partners-new-startup-uses-ai-to-negotiate-your-calendar-for-you/) |\n| OpenEvidence | Series A (~$1B valuation per the CNBC headline linked on Sequoia's company page; article itself not retrieved) | 2025-02-19 | yes, first institutional | James Flynn | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-openevidence-a-life-saving-healthcare-revolution/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/openevidence/) |\n| Paid | First round EUR10M | 2025-03-25 | co-lead with EQT | GTM Fund, Exceptional Capital | [src](https://paid.ai/blog/paid-raises-10m-to-build-the-business) |\n| Serval | Series B $75M at $1B (preempt) | 2025-12-11 | yes | memo co-authors Anas Biad, Brian Halligan | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-serval-empowering-it-for-ai-enterprise-automation/) |\n| Rogo | Series C $75M | 2026-01-28 | yes (Sequoia) | Henry Kravis, Wells Fargo, Thrive, Khosla, Tiger, J.P. Morgan; Sequoia team Halligan, Flynn | [src](https://rogo.com/blog/scaling-rogo-to-build-the-future-of-investment-banking-our-75m-series-c-and-european-expansion) |\n| Chai Discovery | Series C $400M at $3.8B | 2026-07-14 | co-lead | Index, Kleiner, Dimension; Sonali Singh, James Flynn | [src](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/chai-discovery/) |\n| Instinct | Growth (round not confirmed) | 2026 | unknown | Abhishek Malani | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/instinct/) |\n| Trajectory | Series A $45M at $300M | 2026-11-02 | yes (Sequoia) | NVIDIA, Bessemer, BCV; Lauren Reeder | [src](https://trajectory.ai/field-notes/introducing-the-trajectory-platform), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/trajectory/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Harvey:** OpenAI Startup Fund and angels seeded it; Sequoia (Grady + Curnin) took the first priced lead five months later at Series A, then led D and co-led the Mar 2026 round ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-harvey-putting-llms-to-work/), [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/03/25/legal-ai-startup-harvey-raises-200-million-at-11-billion-valuation.html)). Intro path not found.\n- **Day.ai:** HubSpot relationship; visited the founder when the company was about a week old (May 2023); seed lead through Arc ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-day-ai-customer-obsession-productized/)).\n- **Blockit:** Kais Khimji was a Sequoia partner (from 2019, six years) who first pitched the time-optimisation idea internally, then left to found it; Grady led the $5M seed ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/the-most-precious-resource/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/22/former-sequoia-partners-new-startup-uses-ai-to-negotiate-your-calendar-for-you/)).\n- **OpenEvidence:** first institutional money at Series A; how they met Nadler is not stated in any source read ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-openevidence-a-life-saving-healthcare-revolution/)).\n- **Instinct:** Noah Shinn (Reflexion lead author, ex-Sierra) ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/partnering-with-instinct)). Earlier $100M came from a group including Conviction and Greenoaks; Index and Benchmark co-led the ~$250M round in Aug 2026 ([src](https://siliconangle.com/2026/08/27/consumer-focused-ai-assistant-startup-instinct-reportedly-raising-250m/)); Coatue co-led the Sep 2026 Series C ([src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/partnering-with-instinct)). Which round Sequoia (Grady + Abhishek Malani) joined is not confirmed; Sequoia was not first in.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Alfred Lin | Co-steward of Sequoia | c. 2025 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/) |\n| Sonya Huang | Co-author of all four generative-AI essays; co-listed on OpenAI, Hugging Face | 2022 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/2026-this-is-agi/) |\n| Brian Halligan | HubSpot co-founder (Grady's 2011 deal), now Sequoia partner; pairs on Harvey, Rogo, Serval | 2011 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/brian-halligan/) |\n| Charlie Curnin | Co-author, Harvey Series A | 2023 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-harvey-putting-llms-to-work/) |\n| James Flynn | Co-partner on OpenEvidence, Rogo, Chai, OpenAI | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/openevidence/) |\n| Lauren Reeder | Co-partner on Trajectory; Training Data co-host (Paid) | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/trajectory/) |\n| Abhishek Malani | Co-partner on Instinct | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/instinct/) |\n| Sonali Singh | Co-partner on Chai Discovery | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/chai-discovery/) |\n| Julien Bek | Co-partner on Agency | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/agency/) |\n| Anas Biad | Co-author, Serval Series B | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-serval-empowering-it-for-ai-enterprise-automation/) |\n| Kais Khimji | Former Sequoia partner; Grady-led Blockit seed | 2019 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/the-most-precious-resource/) |\n| Christopher O'Donnell | Ex-HubSpot CPO; Day.ai founder | HubSpot era | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-day-ai-customer-obsession-productized/) |\n| Manny Medina | Outreach founder; Paid founder | 2025 | [src](https://paid.ai/blog/paid-raises-10m-to-build-the-business) |\n| Elias Torres | Drift founder (Grady 2017 deal); Agency founder | 2017 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/founder/elias-torres/) |\n| Konstantine Buhler | Training Data co-host (Dust) | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-dust/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Rogo timing: Sequoia's page says first partnered Growth, 2025, but the Apr 2025 Series B post names only Thrive, J.P. Morgan and Tiger, and the first Sequoia-named round is the Jan 2026 Series C lead. When did Sequoia actually enter? ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/rogo/), [src](https://rogo.com/news))\n- Which Instinct round did Sequoia join, at what price, and did Grady or Malani drive it?\n- Did Sequoia participate in Paid's $21M seed (Lightspeed-led)? The post does not name Sequoia ([src](https://paid.ai/blog/paid-raises-21-6-million-seed-round)).\n- Uncapped Ep. 36 and 20VC transcripts: likely hold check-size and stewardship rules of thumb; not retrieved. Web search budget was exhausted during this run.\n- Was Grady involved in Sequoia's lab mega-seeds (Ineffable, Reflection, Ricursive) as steward, even though not listed?\n- Education and pre-Sequoia employer: nothing found from a primary source.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/pat-grady/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/2026-this-is-agi/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ais-act-o1/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ai-act-two/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-harvey-putting-llms-to-work/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-openevidence-a-life-saving-healthcare-revolution/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/the-most-precious-resource/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-day-ai-customer-obsession-productized/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-serval-empowering-it-for-ai-enterprise-automation/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-daniel-nadler/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-christopher-odonnell/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-dust/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/openai/ , /hugging-face/ , /openevidence/ , /rogo/ , /blockit-ai/ , /day-ai/ , /paid/ , /agency/ , /instinct/ , /trajectory/ , /chai-discovery/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/brian-halligan/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/founder/elias-torres/\n- https://huggingface.co/blog/series-c\n- https://rogo.com/blog/scaling-rogo-to-build-the-future-of-investment-banking-our-75m-series-c-and-european-expansion\n- https://paid.ai/blog/paid-raises-10m-to-build-the-business\n- https://paid.ai/blog/paid-raises-21-6-million-seed-round\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/22/former-sequoia-partners-new-startup-uses-ai-to-negotiate-your-calendar-for-you/\n- https://trajectory.ai/field-notes/introducing-the-trajectory-platform\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/partnering-with-instinct\n- https://siliconangle.com/2026/08/27/consumer-focused-ai-assistant-startup-instinct-reportedly-raising-250m/\n- https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2026/03/25/legal-ai-startup-harvey-raises-200-million-at-11-billion-valuation.html\n- https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-d\n- https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/pat-grady\n- https://twitter.com/gradypb/status/1500853901901197314\n", "person:patrick-collison": "\n# Patrick Collison\n\n*Research note (2026-11-11): no primary list of his cheques exists. He has no investing page, and I found no interview where he discusses angel investing, passes or regrets. The cheque list below is assembled from company announcements already in the ledger plus aggregators, which are labelled. Entity registry lists this id with kind `firm`; it should be `person`.*\n\n## One-line take\nStripe's CEO is a name on the cap table of Cursor, Cognition, MatX, The Bot Company and about twenty other AI companies, almost always as a small personal cheque behind a tier-one lead.\n\n## Crux\nFounders want him for the signal and for Stripe-grade operating advice, not for capital: he has no fund, no staff and no stated cheque size. He mostly backs people he already knows (John Carmack, Kyle Vogt, Mitchell Hashimoto, the Cursor and Cognition teams) and usually arrives at Series A or B behind Founders Fund, a16z, Index or Elad Gil. He is constrained by time and by running Stripe, so he does not lead, price or take boards in AI (his one board outside Stripe-adjacent work, Pacific Fusion, is not AI).\n\n## Differential insight\n- His template for deploying money is a tiny fast team: \"Fast Grants was three beloved squirrels in a trench coat.\" (Dwarkesh Podcast, 2024-02-21, 00:37:18) [src](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/patrick-collison)\n- People before projects: \"Figure out who Moncef is and go hire Moncef.\" (same episode, 00:37:46)\n- On AI he stays openly uncertain: \"Everyone has to be highly perplexed\" (same episode, 00:34:34)\n- Cursor's CEO Michael Truell interviewed him on Cursor's own podcast in Feb 2026; a16z's page labels him \"an investor in Anysphere\" [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/patrick-collison-on-stripes-early-choices-smalltalk-and-what-comes-after-coding/) (summary page, no transcript).\n- He is an LP in Situational Awareness, which then led Fluidstack's $830M Series A at $7.5B [src](https://siliconangle.com/2026/07/21/ai-data-center-builder-fluidstack-raises-830m-7-5b-valuation/). His compute exposure is indirect, through Aschenbrenner.\n- My synthesis from the cheque list: only 6 of about 22 AI cheques are pre-seed or seed. He front-runs nobody; he confirms.\nRows: `data/rows/early-patrick-collison-insights.csv`.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. Not a competitor for allocation and not a lead, but a useful signal: when he appears in a seed (Radical Numerics pre-seed, Keen, Mechanize, The Bot Company, Superlogical, Conway Research) the founder is usually a known repeat builder. He co-invests with Elad Gil, Nat Friedman and Daniel Gross.\n\n## Background\nCo-founded Stripe with John Collison in 2010. Co-founded Fast Grants (2020, with Tyler Cowen) and Arc Institute (2021). Joined Meta's board in April 2025 (secondary: Hustle Fund) [src](https://hustlefund.vc/post/patrick-and-john-collison-investments-how-the-stripe-founders-became-infrastructure-investors). Site: [src](https://patrickcollison.com/about).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://patrickcollison.com/ | Essays and lists (Fast, Questions, Labs, Progress); nothing on investing |\n| Substack / blog | none | |\n| X | https://x.com/patrickc | Not reviewed in this run |\n| LinkedIn | none used | |\n| GitHub | not checked | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none (John Collison hosts Cheeky Pint) | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-02 — Dwarkesh Podcast — Craft, Beauty, and the Future of Payments — [link](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/patrick-collison) — Fast Grants and Arc as funding models; uncertainty on AI\n- 2026-02 — Cursor podcast (reposted by a16z) — Stripe's early choices and what comes after coding — [link](https://a16z.com/podcast/patrick-collison-on-stripes-early-choices-smalltalk-and-what-comes-after-coding/) — not transcribed; covers whether AI shows up in productivity data\n- 2022-11 — Money20/20 with Vinod Khosla — in ledger, not mined\n- Noahpinion interview — [link](https://noahpinion.substack.com/p/interview-patrick-collison-co-founder) — not read\n\n## How they think\n- Speed and small teams beat process (Fast, Fast Grants).\n- Progress studies: fund scientists directly and let them choose the work (Arc).\n- No public statement found on angel investing, passes or regrets.\n\n## Read these first\n- Dwarkesh Podcast 2024-02-21: the fullest record of how he funds things.\n- patrickcollison.com/fast: his reference list of fast projects.\n- patrickcollison.com/questions: what he is curious about.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed in this run.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI cheques found: about 22 (ledger had 17 in rounds plus 2 in deals only). P = pre-seed or seed entry.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors / lead | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Numerics (P) | Pre-seed | n/a | No | earliest named backer | ledger [src](https://fortune.com/2026/06/15/exclusive-ai-dna-radical-numerics-eric-nguyen-biology-biodefense-drug-discovery/) |\n| Keen Technologies (P) | Seed ($20M) | 2022-08-19 | No | Nat Friedman and Daniel Gross co-led; Sequoia | ledger |\n| The Bot Company (P) | Seed ($150M at $550M) | 2024-05-13 | No | | ledger |\n| Mechanize (P) | Seed | 2025-04-17 | No | | ledger |\n| Superlogical (P) | Seed | 2026-07-29 | No | Notable Capital | ledger |\n| Conway Research (P) | Seed (first round) | 2026-10-02 | No | | ledger |\n| AssemblyAI | Series A ($28M) | 2022-03-04 | No | | ledger |\n| Cognition | Series A ($21M) | 2024-03-12 | No | Founders Fund led; John Collison; Elad Gil | ledger |\n| Cursor (Anysphere) | Series A ($60M at $400M) | 2024-08 | No | a16z led; Thrive | ledger; still listed as investor at the 2024-12 B [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/in-just-4-months-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-raised-another-100m-at-a-2-5b-valuation-led-by-thrive-sources-say) |\n| Fundamental Research Labs | Series A ($33M) | 2025-08-01 | No | Prosus led | ledger |\n| Brain Co | Series A ($30M) | 2025-09 | No | Elad Gil and Affinity co-led | [src](https://forbes.es/empresas/795587/como-la-nueva-startup-de-jared-kushner-esta-ayudando-a-las-empresas-a-integrar-la-ia-con-exito/) |\n| Crosby | Series A ($20M) | 2025-10-08 | No | Index; BCV; Sequoia; Elad Gil | ledger |\n| Antithesis | Series A ($105M) | 2025-12-03 | No | Jane Street led | aggregator only [src](https://neuronfeed.com/investors/patrick-collison) |\n| Browserbase | Series B | 2025-06-17 | No | | ledger |\n| Osmo | Series B ($70M) | 2026-02-04 | No | | [src](https://www.intelligentcio.com/north-america/2026/02/04/osmo-raises-us70m-series-b-funding-to-scale-ai-powered-olfaction-technology/) |\n| MatX | Series B | 2026-02-24 | No | | ledger |\n| Tasklet | Series A ($20M) | 2026-04-01 | No | | aggregator only [src](https://neuronfeed.com/investors/patrick-collison) |\n| Daytona | Series B ($60M at $1B) | 2026-10-20 | No | | ledger |\n| Inception; NewLimit; Zo Computer (Substrate) | Undisclosed | n/a | No | | ledger |\n| Fluidstack | Series A ($830M) | 2026-07-20 | No | Indirect: Situational Awareness led; one aggregator also lists him personally | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2026/07/21/ai-data-center-builder-fluidstack-raises-830m-7-5b-valuation/) |\n\nNon-AI seen in passing: Rivan Industries (2025-05), Centivax (Series A 2026-03), Entrepreneurs First (Series D 2026-03), Pacific Fusion (board), BioAge (SAFE). Monaco's 2026 Series A lists John, not Patrick.\n\nHit pattern: Cursor (A at $400M; Calcalist reports a later SpaceX stock deal near $60B [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rym9imnnmg)), Cognition (A), MatX, The Bot Company. Publicly passed or regretted: nothing found.\n\n## Deal stories\nNo first-hand account of any AI cheque was found. The closest relationship evidence is outside AI: he recruited Will Gaybrick from Stripe's cap table and vouched for Shaun Maguire to Sequoia (both in ledger).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Stripe | Co-founder & CEO | 2010 | [src](https://patrickcollison.com/about) |\n| John Collison | Brother; frequent co-investor (aggregator: 3 shared AI deals) | n/a | [src](https://neuronfeed.com/investors/patrick-collison) |\n| Elad Gil | Stripe angel at eight people; co-investor in Crosby, Brain Co, Cognition | n/a | [src](https://crosby.ai/blog/series-a-and-faster-agreements) |\n| Nat Friedman; Daniel Gross | Co-investors (Keen; aggregator counts 2 shared each) | 2022 | [src](https://neuronfeed.com/investors/patrick-collison) |\n| Situational Awareness (Leopold Aschenbrenner) | LP / seed backer | 2024 | ledger |\n| Michael Truell | Investor in Anysphere | 2024-08 | [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/patrick-collison-on-stripes-early-choices-smalltalk-and-what-comes-after-coding/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Cheque size and whether he invests through an entity.\n- Whether Antithesis, Tasklet and Fluidstack are real personal cheques (aggregator only).\n- What he says about AI on the 2026 Cursor podcast (needs audio).\n\n## Sources\nhttps://www.dwarkesh.com/p/patrick-collison ; https://patrickcollison.com/ ; https://a16z.com/podcast/patrick-collison-on-stripes-early-choices-smalltalk-and-what-comes-after-coding/ ; https://neuronfeed.com/investors/patrick-collison (aggregator) ; https://hustlefund.vc/post/patrick-and-john-collison-investments-how-the-stripe-founders-became-infrastructure-investors (secondary) ; https://siliconangle.com/2026/07/21/ai-data-center-builder-fluidstack-raises-830m-7-5b-valuation/ ; ledger rows in data/rounds.csv and data/deals.csv\n", "person:patrick-oshaughnessy": "\n# Patrick O'Shaughnessy\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the person who personally wrote the conviction check in Etched after \"every major investor in the Valley passed.\" He made the bet in 2023 against the experts he called, co-led the $120M A with Primary, and has re-upped about five times through the $21B round. His podcast is both his sourcing channel and his megaphone ([EP.480](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/etched-building-ai-hardware-to-make-inference-faster-and-cheaper-invest-like-the-best-ep480), [Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he underwrites founders and market size, not technology. He then keeps adding capital as the gap between what he sees privately and what the market believes stays open. In his words that gap is \"perception gap equals opportunity\" ([EP.480](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/etched-building-ai-hardware-to-make-inference-faster-and-cheaper-invest-like-the-best-ep480), ~01:11). Invest Like the Best and Colossus give him long, trust-building time with founders before and after he invests.\n- **What constrains him:** no technical diligence edge. He says \"I'm not a math expert or a semiconductor expert\" and admits that if he had known how hard it was, \"I probably wouldn't have done it\" (same source, ~01:08–01:09). His fund sizes are mid-sized: Fund III had sold $184.75M against a $400M target (2024-12). Fund IV ($400M) and a $100M Conviction Fund were filed 2026-08 with $0 sold yet ([Fund III](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2048473/000172348924000003/primary_doc.xml), [Fund IV](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149092/000123191926000870/primary_doc.xml), [Conviction](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149093/000123191926000906/primary_doc.xml)). Big follow-ons have needed SPVs (for example the $42.55M \"EAI Holdings\" SPV, 2026-03; the company is not named in the filing) ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2111757/000123191926000251/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **What he'd never do:** whiplash founders, or treat the job as anything but backing people. He says \"I don't want to whiplash people\", and \"my main job is to back people with LP dollars, lots of them\" ([EP.455](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/patrick-oshaughnessy-creating-on-principle-invest-like-the-best-ep455), 01:38:50 and 01:41:33).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He called experts before investing in Etched, they told him no, and he invested anyway.** On EP.480 he recalls calling experts while \"considering investing in the company\" and that he \"didn't know a lot about chips at the time.\" He relays their verdict as \"two 21-year-olds are like not going to do this\" ([EP.480](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/etched-building-ai-hardware-to-make-inference-faster-and-cheaper-invest-like-the-best-ep480), ~03:29). This is the concrete mechanism behind \"damn the base rate.\"\n- **The decision was made in 2023, not 2024.** He tells Uberti that \"what I felt about you was the majority of the reason why we made the bet when we did in 2023\" (same source, ~01:08). The A was only announced on 2024-06-25 ([Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware)). The founders place the $103M soft-commit board moment in the same late-2023 window (founders speaking, ~01:05). That makes EP.356 (2023-12-12) **contemporaneous with his commitment, not six months before it**, which corrects the \"podcast first, investment six months later\" framing in `firms/positive-sum.md`. See Open questions.\n- **Underwriting rule from Etched:** \"you kind of have to damn the base rate\" and \"I didn't know what I didn't know\" (EP.480, ~01:08). He also says the bet \"was much more of believed in the concept of this market potentially being huge\" (same).\n- **Biggest first check, then repeated re-ups:** it was \"the largest by a lot first check that I had written\". He says \"I think I've invested, I don't know, five or so times\" (the auto-transcript renders Etched as \"enched\"), and that \"stakes are getting bigger and bigger\" (EP.480, ~01:08 and ~01:10). He also calls himself a long-time big investor: \"I'm a big edge investor. I've been involved for a long time.\" (~01:07; \"edge\" is the transcript's rendering of \"Etched\").\n- **Edge framing:** \"That perception gap equals opportunity\" (EP.480, ~01:11). Etched was the textbook case: consensus said no in 2023 and it was priced at $21B by 2026-08.\n- **How he thinks about his own role:** \"you're mostly or largely backing the person and the team\" ([EP.455](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/patrick-oshaughnessy-creating-on-principle-invest-like-the-best-ep455), 01:41:33). He says that \"when I see undiscovered talent, it is my obligation\" to champion it (~15:45).\n- **Self-admitted flaw:** \"I can tend to whiplash around a lot and change my opinion.\" (EP.455, 01:34:42). The \"don't whiplash people\" rule is a correction he imposes on himself.\n- **GP-commit pressure before the exit:** he describes needing to \"make a GP commit to my own fund\", saying \"this is before I sold my business\", in the context of Sam Hinkie raising his first fund (EP.455, ~01:37). That dates the early Positive Sum funds to before the OSAM sale (Fund I Form D 2020-10-14; Franklin deal 2021-09-30).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is the clearest example in the repo of a non-technical, founder-conviction investor taking a large position in an AI-chip company at the A while the majors passed, then concentrating into it. The deals that matter:\n- **Etched:** A co-lead (2024-06, committed 2023), about five investments, still named in the $700M at $21B round (2026-08-18) ([Etched](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one)).\n- **Cognition:** existing investor by the $26B Series D (2026-05-27) ([Cognition](https://cognition.com/blog/series-d)).\n- **Physical Intelligence, Flapping Airplanes, Modal:** on the firm portfolio page, with entry rounds not found ([portfolio](https://www.positivesum.com/portfolio)).\n\nWhat it means for you: he is a natural co-investor in conviction rounds where technical diligence is done by others (Uberti says trading firms audited Etched down to the RTL). With Fund IV plus the Conviction Fund, he can now lead or anchor $10M+ tickets, but probably not $20M+ into a $600M lab seed from one vehicle without an SPV (inference from Form D sizes). He is also an access channel: founders he hosts on ILTB get distribution, and that makes him a desirable co-lead for founders.\n\n## Background\n- CEO of O'Shaughnessy Asset Management (OSAM). Franklin Templeton agreed to acquire OSAM, including the Canvas custom-indexing platform ($6.4B AUM, Canvas $1.8B), announced 2021-09-30 ([ThinkAdvisor](https://thinkadvisor.com/2021/09/30/franklin-templeton-acquiring-oshaughnessy-asset-management), [WealthManagement](https://www.wealthmanagement.com/investment-news/franklin-templeton-to-acquire-osam-and-custom-index-platform-canvas)). On EP.455 he says he \"didn't know anything about software\" before building Canvas ([EP.455](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/patrick-oshaughnessy-creating-on-principle-invest-like-the-best-ep455)).\n- Co-founded Positive Sum with Sam Cates. The first fund Form D was filed 2020-10-14 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1825089/000182508920000001/primary_doc.xml), [PRNewswire 2022](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/patrick-oshaughnessys-venture-capital-firm-positive-sum-invests-20m-in-investment-technology-platform-tegus-301635047.html)).\n- Host of Invest Like the Best and founder of Colossus, a long-form profile publication. Its editor is Jeremy Stern ([EP.455](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/patrick-oshaughnessy-creating-on-principle-invest-like-the-best-ep455)).\n- Education: nothing found in the sources fetched.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.positivesum.com | Firm site; team page has no titles or photos (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://colossus.com | Colossus long-form profiles |\n| X | https://x.com/patrick_oshag | Follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Invest Like the Best (Colossus) | Weekly-ish; EP.480 dated 2026-06-30 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-12 — Invest Like the Best (host) — EP.356 Gavin Uberti, \"Real-Time AI & The Future of AI Hardware\" — [podscripts](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/gavin-uberti-real-time-ai-the-future-of-ai-hardware-invest-like-the-best-ep356) — relays \"One of your early investors told me\" (01:46), so he was in the Etched investor network in the same window as his 2023 commitment.\n- 2026-01 — David Senra conversation, cross-posted as ILTB EP.455 \"Creating on Principle\" (guest) — [podscripts](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/patrick-oshaughnessy-creating-on-principle-invest-like-the-best-ep455) — backing people with LP dollars, whiplash, GP commit, Sam Hinkie's fund.\n- 2026-06 — Invest Like the Best (host) — EP.480 Etched, with Uberti and Wachen — [podscripts](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/etched-building-ai-hardware-to-make-inference-faster-and-cheaper-invest-like-the-best-ep480) — negative expert calls, the 2023 bet, about five investments, perception gap. It aired the day TechCrunch disclosed the $5B Stripes round and sourced the \"majors passed\" story to the episode ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/)).\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** bet on people first (\"you're mostly or largely backing the person and the team\"). Prefer founder-led companies: \"founder-led companies are so much... more interesting\" (EP.455, 01:47:57). Market size over technical certainty.\n- **Contrarian:** consensus rejection is information, because a gap between outside perception and inside evidence is the opportunity (EP.480). This rhymes with Ramtin Naimi's point that Etched's thin early conversion was not a bad-founder signal, until \"Sequoia blessed him with a $10 billion valuation\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4), as recorded in `people/ramtin-naimi.md`).\n- **Calls right:** Etched (committed 2023, A announced 2024-06, $21B by 2026-08). Cognition held into the $26B D.\n- **Calls wrong / admitted:** he says he would probably not have invested had he understood the difficulty. He calls himself \"quite bad on that dimension for a long time\" (EP.455, 01:43:01; the topic is not clear from the fetch).\n\n## Read these first\n1. EP.480 (2026-06-30): his own account of the Etched bet, the expert calls and the re-ups.\n2. EP.356 (2023-12-12): Uberti one-on-one, recorded during the window when he was committing.\n3. EP.455 (2026-01-20): his operating principles as a GP (people, LP dollars, no whiplash).\n4. [Primary on the Etched A](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware): the co-lead's account, naming him.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot researched this pass (X not indexed by search). The visible pattern is that the podcast amplifies portfolio founders (Etched twice; sponsor reads include portfolio companies Rogo, Vanta and Ridgeline on EP.455).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Etched | Series A $120M (committed 2023 per EP.480) | 2024-06-25 | co-lead | Primary (co-lead), Hummingbird, Two Sigma, Skybox, Fundomo, Oceans | [Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware) |\n| Etched | about five investments in total, individual tranches not itemized | 2023–2026 | no (after A) | Stripes, Sequoia, Jane Street leads of later rounds | [EP.480](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/etched-building-ai-hardware-to-make-inference-faster-and-cheaper-invest-like-the-best-ep480) |\n| Etched | $700M at $21B | 2026-08-18 | no | Jane Street (lead), Kleiner, Sequoia, a16z, Stripes, Primary | [Etched](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one) |\n| Cognition | existing investor at Series D ($1B+ at $26B); entry round not found | 2026-05-27 | no | Lux, General Catalyst, 8VC (leads); Founders Fund; Elad Gil | [Cognition](https://cognition.com/blog/series-d) |\n| Physical Intelligence | round not found (an aggregator lists a 2025-12-05 date, unverified) | — | unknown | — | [portfolio](https://www.positivesum.com/portfolio) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | round not found; not named in the $180M seed lists seen | — | unknown | — | [portfolio](https://www.positivesum.com/portfolio) |\n| Modal | round not found | — | unknown | — | [portfolio](https://www.positivesum.com/portfolio) |\n| Tegus | $20M; he took a board observer seat | 2022-09-28 | yes | — | [PRNewswire](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/patrick-oshaughnessys-venture-capital-firm-positive-sum-invests-20m-in-investment-technology-platform-tegus-301635047.html) |\n\nAttribution caveat: Cognition, Physical Intelligence, Flapping Airplanes and Modal are firm-level holdings. No source names him personally on them.\n\n## Deal stories\n**Etched (the main story).**\n- *Was he first?* No. Primary's Brian Schechter led the ~$5.4M seed at about $34M in 2023-03 ([Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond)). He was the **first non-seed institutional conviction**: he committed in 2023 (\"the bet when we did in 2023\") while the majors were passing.\n- *Sequence and gaps:* seed 2023-03 → his commitment in 2023 (inside the late-2023 soft-commit window) → A announced 2024-06-25 ($120M, co-lead with Primary; valuation undisclosed) → Stripes $500M at $5B post (2025-12, disclosed 2026-06-30) → Sequoia $300M at $10.3B (2026-07-23) → Jane Street $700M at $21B (2026-08-18) ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/), [Etched](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one)). The gap from his commitment to the next new lead (Stripes) was roughly two years.\n- *How he decided:* he called experts and they said young founders could not build a chip company. He bet on Uberti personally and on market size. He did not do technical diligence himself; per Uberti, the trading-firm investors did that later.\n- *Price:* the A price is not disclosed anywhere found. As co-lead he co-set it with Primary.\n- *First contact:* not found. EP.356 shows he was already talking to an unnamed early Etched investor by 2023-12.\n- *After:* he re-upped about five times as stakes grew, and EP.480 (aired the day the $5B round was disclosed) shaped the public \"every major investor passed\" narrative ahead of Sequoia's $10.3B C.\n\n**Tegus (2022):** $20M growth investment plus a board observer seat; Tegus was later acquired by AlphaSense ([PRNewswire](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/patrick-oshaughnessys-venture-capital-firm-positive-sum-invests-20m-in-investment-technology-platform-tegus-301635047.html)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Positive Sum | Co-founder & CEO | 2020 | [PRNewswire](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/patrick-oshaughnessys-venture-capital-firm-positive-sum-invests-20m-in-investment-technology-platform-tegus-301635047.html) |\n| Gavin Uberti (Etched) | Investor; ILTB host twice | 2023 | [EP.480](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/etched-building-ai-hardware-to-make-inference-faster-and-cheaper-invest-like-the-best-ep480) |\n| Rob Wachen (Etched) | Investor; ILTB guest | 2023 | same |\n| Brian Schechter (Primary) | Etched A co-lead | 2024-06 | [Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware) |\n| Sam Cates | Positive Sum co-founder | 2020 | [PRNewswire](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/patrick-oshaughnessys-venture-capital-firm-positive-sum-invests-20m-in-investment-technology-platform-tegus-301635047.html) |\n| Jeremy Stern | Colossus editor he recruited | unknown | [EP.455](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/patrick-oshaughnessy-creating-on-principle-invest-like-the-best-ep455) |\n| Sam Hinkie | He discussed his fund (Hinkie raising first fund) | unknown | EP.455 ~01:37 |\n| Franklin Templeton | Acquirer of his prior firm OSAM | 2021-09-30 | [ThinkAdvisor](https://thinkadvisor.com/2021/09/30/franklin-templeton-acquiring-oshaughnessy-asset-management) |\n| David Senra | Interviewer on EP.455 | 2026-01 | EP.455 |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Timing correction to propagate (main session):** EP.480 says the Etched bet was made \"in 2023\". `firms/positive-sum.md` and `patterns/co-investor-gangs.md` frame EP.356 (2023-12-12) as about six months *before* the investment. The better reading is that the commitment happened in late 2023 and was announced 2024-06-25. The exact month of his commitment is still unknown.\n- Etched A price and his check size. The EAI Holdings SPV ($42.55M, 2026-03) may or may not be Etched.\n- Entry rounds for Cognition, Physical Intelligence, Flapping Airplanes and Modal, and whether he personally led any of them.\n- How he first met Uberti, and who the \"early investor\" he quoted on EP.356 was.\n- What the $100M Conviction Fund is for (follow-ons into winners like Etched?), and who the Fund IV LPs are.\n- Whether \"I think I was quite bad on that dimension\" (EP.455) refers to investing.\n\n## Sources\n- https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/etched-building-ai-hardware-to-make-inference-faster-and-cheaper-invest-like-the-best-ep480 (auto-transcript; \"Etched\" is rendered \"edge\"/\"enched\"; timestamps are segment-level and approximate)\n- https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/gavin-uberti-real-time-ai-the-future-of-ai-hardware-invest-like-the-best-ep356\n- https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/patrick-oshaughnessy-creating-on-principle-invest-like-the-best-ep455\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware ; https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/\n- https://cognition.com/blog/series-d\n- https://www.positivesum.com/portfolio ; https://www.positivesum.com/team\n- SEC Form Ds: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1825089/000182508920000001/primary_doc.xml , https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2048473/000172348924000003/primary_doc.xml , https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2111757/000123191926000251/primary_doc.xml , https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149092/000123191926000870/primary_doc.xml , https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149093/000123191926000906/primary_doc.xml\n- https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/patrick-oshaughnessys-venture-capital-firm-positive-sum-invests-20m-in-investment-technology-platform-tegus-301635047.html\n- https://thinkadvisor.com/2021/09/30/franklin-templeton-acquiring-oshaughnessy-asset-management ; https://www.wealthmanagement.com/investment-news/franklin-templeton-to-acquire-osam-and-custom-index-platform-canvas\n- Aggregator (secondary, unverified): parsers.vc entry for Physical Intelligence (via search snippet)\n", "person:paul-drews": "\n# Paul Drews\n\n## One-line take\nThe senior signatory on Salesforce's Anthropic position, now about 45% of Salesforce's strategic portfolio. He is the person who speaks for the Generative AI Fund, but the frontier-lab lead credit sits with his MD, Emily Zhao, and SFV enters labs late as a participant, never at the seed.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he sells distribution, not compute or price. His Anthropic and Together AI pitches rest on Salesforce AI Research diligence, Fortune 500 CIO introductions (Innovation Forums, Demo Days) and Salesforce as a buyer ([SFV Anthropic F](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/anthropics-13b-series-f/), [Behind the Investment](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-anthropic/)).\n- **What constrains him:** he invests one pool, the Salesforce balance sheet. The \"Generative AI Fund\" ($250M 2023-03, $500M 2023-06, $1B 2024-09) is a label, with no outside LPs (see [firms/salesforce-ventures.md](../firms/salesforce-ventures.md)). John Somorjai (President, and chief corporate development and investments officer) sits above him. No investment committee is described in any source, and **his reporting line is unconfirmed**. Deals have to fit Salesforce's enterprise customer story: responsible AI, ROI, trust ([SFV 2025-10-16](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-deploys-over-850m-of-its-1b-ai-fund-to-back-the-ai-revolution/)).\n- **What he would not do (inferred from the record):** lead or price a lab seed. On frontier labs SFV participates from the first big priced round (Anthropic C, 2023-05) and then re-ups every round.\n\n## What he personally signs, sources or prices\n- **Signs (byline or quote):** every Anthropic post from C (2023-05) to G (2026-02), the Runway C-extension, the Generative AI Fund doubling (2023-06) and the $850M-deployed release (2025-10), plus the Together AI A2 quote (2024-03).\n- **Sources:** by his own account the Anthropic thesis started with the SFV team and Salesforce AI Research (Silvio Savarese) in summer 2022. The first meeting with Dario Amodei was in early 2023 ([Behind the Investment](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-anthropic/)). Who made the introduction is not stated.\n- **Prices:** no sourced case of Drews setting a price on an AI deal. The SFV leads on his list are Axion B (SFV led, [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-axion-2/)), Mintlify B (co-led with a16z, [Mintlify](https://mintlify.com/blog/series-b)) and, in 2021, Vivun C (an SFV-led $75M round, per his SFV page post list). In the Anthropic rounds SFV followed Spark, Lightspeed, ICONIQ, Fidelity and GIC.\n- **Board:** his SFV page says he is \"currently a board observer or actively involved with\" Airbyte, Anthropic, Astronomer, Databricks, Lyra, Runway, Starburst, Vercel, Vivun and VNDLY ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/paul-drews/)). The wording is a disjunction, so **an Anthropic observer seat is unconfirmed**.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He co-signs; Zhao is credited as lead.** His bio claims involvement, not leads: \"currently a board observer or actively involved with our investments\" ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/paul-drews/)). Emily Zhao's page says she \"led several investments in the fund, including Anthropic, Hugging Face, Together AI, fal, Runway, Cohere\" ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/emily-zhao/)). For a founder, the working contact on a lab or infra deal is Zhao. Drews is the escalation and the voice of the fund.\n- **His public thesis cut against the position that now dominates the book.** On Together AI's A2 (2024-03) he said \"companies are moving towards an open-source approach in AI adoption\" ([Together AI](https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a2)). By 2026-07-31 the book was about 45% Anthropic, a closed model ([10-Q](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1108524/000110852626000190/crm-20260731.htm), via firm file), and Salesforce \"expects to spend around $300mn on Anthropic tokens this year\" ([TNW 2026-08-27](https://thenextweb.com/news/salesforce-anthropic-claudeforce-partnership)).\n- **He admits the 2023 entry was not obvious.** He writes that \"betting on a research-heavy startup at a meaningful valuation was not obvious\". The tiebreaker was testing Constitutional AI with Fortune 500 CIOs, who saw it as \"a key selling point\" ([Behind the Investment, 2026-02-12](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-anthropic/)).\n- **The value-add is customer introductions.** SFV says its Innovation Forums and Demo Days led to Anthropic customer relationships with Accenture, Barclays and Coinbase ([SFV Anthropic F](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/anthropics-13b-series-f/)). Anthropic's CFO Krishna Rao is quoted saying SFV \"helped Anthropic scale faster\" ([SFV 2025-10-16](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-deploys-over-850m-of-its-1b-ai-fund-to-back-the-ai-revolution/)).\n- **House check band, in his essay:** \"from <$5M for a seed round to $50M+ for a growth-stage business\", leading \"from seed to growth\" ([The Long Game, 2024-11-20](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-long-game-how-salesforce-ventures-is-reimagining-vc/)). The frontier-lab record is participation only.\n\n## The strategic tie (what comes with his money)\n- **To the company:** Salesforce as a customer and channel. Early Claude ran through Slack, and Claude was integrated into Einstein 1 Studio ([SFV Anthropic F](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/anthropics-13b-series-f/)). Resolve AI became a paying Salesforce customer around the SFV round (firm file). Founders also get Fortune 500 CIO introductions and Dreamforce stage time.\n- **To the parent:** in Oct 2025 Claude became \"a preferred model\" for Agentforce in regulated industries, and Salesforce is deploying \"Claude Code across its global engineering organization\" ([Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/salesforce-anthropic-expanded-partnership)). In Aug 2026 Claudeforce made Claude the default reasoning model across Agentforce and Slack ([TNW](https://thenextweb.com/news/salesforce-anthropic-claudeforce-partnership)).\n- No exclusivity, compute deal, tranche or customer-commitment term attached to an SFV check was found.\n\n## AI book vs. Salesforce's products (deals he is named on)\n| Company | Relation to Salesforce |\n|---|---|\n| Anthropic | Supplier (default model in Agentforce and Slack). Also a competitor for enterprise agent spend |\n| Runway | None directly (creative video) |\n| Writer | Competitor-adjacent to Agentforce content and marketing (inference) |\n| Recall.ai | Infrastructure for meeting data; plausible supplier to Slack and Agentforce (inference) |\n| Dyna Robotics | None (robotics) |\n| Vercel, Supabase, Mintlify | Developer infrastructure; overlaps Agentforce Vibes and Heroku only loosely (inference) |\n| Axion | AI observability for customer quality. Adjacent to Service Cloud (inference) |\n| Resolve AI | Supplier: Salesforce is a paying customer |\n| Databricks | Partner and competitor in data (Data Cloud) (inference) |\n| Together AI | Open-model inference supplier |\n\n## For the owner\n- **Role:** a later-round participant and distribution partner for frontier labs. He is not a seed co-investor. SFV never entered a lab at the seed, and the 2025-26 mega-seeds (Thinking Machines, Periodic, Ineffable and others) have no SFV row (firm file). A strategic acquirer path exists through Somorjai's M&A side (Convergence, Qualified, Airkit), not through Drews personally. He is not an LP.\n- **Who to know:** Emily Zhao for frontier and infrastructure deals, with Drews as the senior sign-off. A ~$20M seed check from the owner would sit ahead of SFV. The useful pitch to Drews is \"your next Anthropic\", meaning a Series A or B slot with the Salesforce channel.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He has growth-scale check capacity and owns the most important corporate tie to a frontier lab, but he enters at the C or later and does not do seeds. He matters to the owner as a later-round buyer and as a route into Salesforce distribution for portfolio companies.\n\n## Background\n- BS in Economics, Duke. MBA, Wharton ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/paul-drews/)).\n- Vice President, Battery Ventures: Collibra, JFrog, Opsgenie, StreamSets. Dates unconfirmed ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/paul-drews/)).\n- Partner, Tenaya Capital: led Concord and StreamSets (acquired by Software AG). Dates unconfirmed ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/paul-drews/)).\n- Salesforce Ventures. Join date unconfirmed. The earliest byline found is 2021-04 (firm file). He was Managing Partner by 2023-06-12 ([Salesforce newsroom](https://www.salesforce.com/uk/news/stories/generative-ai-investing/)) and is listed as Managing Partner in the 15-year report ([PDF, 2024-11](https://salesforceventures.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/15-Years-of-Salesforce-Ventures.pdf)). Matt Garratt, a prior SFV Managing Partner, left 2021-06 (firm file). Whether Drews took that seat then is unconfirmed.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://salesforceventures.com/people/paul-drews/ | SFV bio; its \"Get in Touch\" link points to pdrews@salesforce.com (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://salesforceventures.com/people/paul-drews/ | SFV Perspectives bylines (deal posts, \"The Long Game\") |\n| X | https://twitter.com/pdrews | Linked from SFV bio; activity not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/pauldrews/ | Linked from SFV bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024 — Salesforce Ventures Elevate CEO summit, leadership panel write-up ([SFV page list](https://salesforceventures.com/people/paul-drews/)).\n- 2024-09 — Dreamforce 2024, pictured with Anthropic's Kate Jensen, Claudine Emeott and Together AI's Vipul Ved Prakash ([SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-anthropic/)).\n- No podcast transcripts found. Web search was unavailable in this run.\n\n## How they think\n- AI is \"the most profound platform shift of our time\" ([SFV 2025-10-16](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-deploys-over-850m-of-its-1b-ai-fund-to-back-the-ai-revolution/)).\n- Value accrues first at the model layer. \"Enterprise revenue is hard to earn, but far more durable\" (Behind the Investment).\n- Patient capital plus platform: the corporate VC should bring its whole team and network, not just attend board meetings (The Long Game).\n- He got the Anthropic call right. In 2024 he made an open-source call on Together AI. Its outcome is mixed, since the closed lab came to dominate the book.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Behind the Investment: Anthropic (2026-02-12)](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-anthropic/): how the 2023 entry happened.\n- [The Long Game (2024-11-20)](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-long-game-how-salesforce-ventures-is-reimagining-vc/): the house model and check bands.\n- [Anthropic's $13B Series F (2025-09)](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/anthropics-13b-series-f/): what SFV delivers to a lab.\n- [Together AI A2 quote (2024-03)](https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a2): the open-source view.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked. X and LinkedIn were not retrieved, and search was unavailable in this run.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Databricks | Series G ($1B at $28B) | 2021-02 | no | Franklin Templeton (lead), CPP, Fidelity, AWS, CapitalG, Microsoft | [PRN](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/databricks-raises-1-billion-series-g-investment-at-28-billion-valuation-301219270.html); bio involvement |\n| Vercel | Series C | 2021-06 | unknown | — | [SFV byline Drews](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-invests-in-vercel/) |\n| Anthropic | Series C ($450M) | 2023-05 | no (Spark led) | Google, Zoom, Sound, Menlo | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-anthropic/) |\n| Runway | Series C extension ($141M) | 2023-06 | no | Google, NVIDIA | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-runway/) |\n| Together AI | Series A2 ($106M) | 2024-03 | yes (SFV) | Coatue, Lux, Kleiner, Emergence, NEA | [Together](https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a2) (quoted; Zhao credited as lead) |\n| Writer | Series C ($200M at $1.9B) | 2024-11 | no | Premji, Radical, ICONIQ (co-leads) | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-writer/) |\n| Anthropic | Series E ($3.5B) | 2025-03 | no | Lightspeed (lead) | [Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-raises-series-e-at-usd61-5b-post-money-valuation); SFV says it backed every round. **Drews not named on E (unconfirmed personal credit)** |\n| Anthropic | Series F ($13B at $183B) | 2025-09 | no | ICONIQ, Fidelity, Lightspeed | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/anthropics-13b-series-f/) |\n| Recall.ai | Undisclosed | 2025-09 | unknown | Bessemer, HubSpot Ventures, Ridge, YC | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-recall-ai/) |\n| Dyna Robotics | Series A ($120M) | 2025-09 | no | CRV, First Round | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-dyna-robotics/) |\n| Vercel | Series F ($300M at $9.3B) | 2025-09 | no | Accel, GIC (co-leads) | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/deepening-our-commitment-vercel/) |\n| Axion | Series B ($37M) | 2025-12 | yes (SFV) | Bessemer, Inspired, Amplo, Schneider Electric | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-axion-2/) |\n| Anthropic | Series G ($30B at $380B) | 2026-02 | no | GIC | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-anthropic/) |\n| Mintlify | Series B ($45M at $500M) | 2026-04 | co-led with a16z | Bain Capital Ventures, YC | [Mintlify](https://mintlify.com/blog/series-b) |\n| Resolve AI | Series A ($40M) | 2026-04 | unknown | — | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/autonomous-production-with-ai/) |\n| Supabase | Series F ($500M at $10.5B) | 2026-06 | no (GIC led) | Accel, YC, Craft, Felicis, Peak XV, Coatue | [Orrick](https://www.orrick.com/en/News/2026/06/Supabase-Raises-500-Million-at-10-5-Billion-Valuation-to-Accelerate-Lead-in-Agentic-Infrastructure) |\n\nThe SFV rows for Anthropic C/E/F/G, Runway, Vercel, Supabase and Databricks are in `data/rows/salesforce-ventures-portfolio-deals.csv`, and Together AI is in `data/deals.csv`. The rows new in this batch are in `data/rows/paul-drews-deals.csv`.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Anthropic (2022-23).** The team started researching after DALL-E 2 (summer 2022), and ChatGPT confirmed the thesis. Working with Silvio Savarese's research team, SFV concluded that value accrues first at the model layer. Drews and Zhao first met Dario Amodei in San Francisco in early 2023, were skeptical, and were won over by the roadmap and Constitutional AI. They tested Constitutional AI with Fortune 500 CIOs before investing in the C. SFV re-upped in D through G ([Behind the Investment](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-anthropic/)).\n- **Together AI A2 (2024-03).** SFV led. Drews gave the press quote and Zhao is credited as lead ([Together](https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a2), [SFV Zhao](https://salesforceventures.com/people/emily-zhao/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Salesforce Ventures | Managing Partner | by 2023-06 (join unconfirmed) | [Salesforce](https://www.salesforce.com/uk/news/stories/generative-ai-investing/) |\n| Tenaya Capital | Partner (former) | unconfirmed | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/paul-drews/) |\n| Battery Ventures | Vice President (former) | unconfirmed | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/paul-drews/) |\n| Emily Zhao | Co-signatory on Anthropic and Runway; she is credited as lead | 2023 | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/emily-zhao/) |\n| John Somorjai | SFV President; reporting line unconfirmed | — | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-long-game-how-salesforce-ventures-is-reimagining-vc/) |\n| Silvio Savarese | Thesis partner (Salesforce AI Research) | 2022 | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-anthropic/) |\n| Pascha Hao, Zak Kokosa, Nathan Ho | Junior co-authors on his 2024-26 deals | 2024 | [SFV](https://salesforceventures.com/people/paul-drews/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- His start date at SFV and the date of his promotion to Managing Partner. Did he take Garratt's seat in 2021?\n- Whether he holds the Anthropic board observer seat.\n- Who approves an SFV check: Somorjai alone, or a committee?\n- Per-round check sizes in Anthropic.\n\n## Sources\n- https://salesforceventures.com/people/paul-drews/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/people/emily-zhao/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/behind-the-investment-anthropic/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/anthropics-13b-series-f/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-long-game-how-salesforce-ventures-is-reimagining-vc/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/salesforce-ventures-deploys-over-850m-of-its-1b-ai-fund-to-back-the-ai-revolution/\n- https://www.salesforce.com/uk/news/stories/generative-ai-investing/\n- https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a2\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/salesforce-anthropic-claudeforce-partnership\n- https://www.anthropic.com/news/salesforce-anthropic-expanded-partnership\n- https://salesforceventures.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/15-Years-of-Salesforce-Ventures.pdf\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1108524/000110852626000190/crm-20260731.htm (via firms/salesforce-ventures.md)\n", "person:pete-sonsini": "\n# Pete Sonsini\n\n*Written 2026-11-11 (voice-2 sweep) from his own writing and interviews (NEA blog 2014-2020, TechCrunch 2013-2025, SiliconANGLE 2015, Laude memos 2024-2025, Kellogg Magazine 2026). YouTube transcripts could not be fetched (YouTube rate-limited and bot-checked every route), so the video interviews are catalogued but not mined.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is an enterprise-infrastructure GP whose best deals came from following one researcher-founder (Ion Stoica) through three companies. He has now built a small fund, Laude, around that pattern: research founders, researcher LPs and infrastructure bets.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he stays loyal to research founders across companies, and he invests below the hype layer. He backed Ion Stoica at Conviva, then Databricks, then Anyscale, and Laude later backed Stoica's Arena ([src](https://nea.com/blog/anyscale-blog), [src](https://laude.vc/news/lmarena-turning-research-driven-evaluation-into-ai-infrastructure)). He also backed Alex Salazar at Stormpath and again at Arcade ([src](https://laude.vc/news/arcade-dev-unlocking-ais-full-potential-in-the-enterprise)). He says his edge is \"the lower levels, the infrastructure\" rather than the shiny agent layer ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/18/arcade-raises-12m-from-perplexity-co-founders-new-fund-to-make-ai-agents-less-awful/)). Before venture he ran OEM alliances at VMware, which still shapes how he coaches CEOs ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/biz-dev-can-make-or-break-your-startup-10-rules-for-getting-it-right)).\n- **What constrains him:** Laude's fund is small at $150M ([src](https://laude.vc/news/from-breakthrough-research-to-breakout-companies)). He leads small seeds (Tensormesh $4.5M) and joins mega-seeds such as Arena's $100M as a participant ([src](https://laude.vc/news/tensormesh-cutting-cost-ai-inference-10x), [src](https://laude.vc/news/lmarena-turning-research-driven-evaluation-into-ai-infrastructure)). His career thesis is enterprise and data infrastructure, so pure frontier pre-training labs are outside his record so far.\n- **What he'd never do (from his writing):** chase a wave without a clear path to it. He asks \"when is that gonna happen\" before backing a vision ([src](https://siliconangle.com/2015/10/12/vc-reveals-who-gets-funded-vmworld/)). He also tells portfolio companies to avoid single strategic investors ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/biz-dev-can-make-or-break-your-startup-10-rules-for-getting-it-right)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **His go/no-go test is how much sleep a deal will cost him.** \"how much sleep am I likely to lose over this one?\" He said Databricks was the rare deal that felt easy, because he already knew the founder ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/databricks-makes-venture-seem-easy), 2014-06-30; unbylined first-person NEA post, attributed through its Conviva and MapR references).\n- **He accepts losses as part of the job.** \"if you don’t lose any money, you aren’t taking enough risks\" ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/databricks-makes-venture-seem-easy), 2014-06-30).\n- **He held the Perplexity deal together during the Bing scare.** Perplexity agreed terms with NEA on a Friday, and the term sheet had a 30-day diligence clause. When Bing's chat UI leaked, the founders feared NEA would back out. Sonsini called the next day: \"We trust you, you’ll figure it out.\" Srinivas calls the round a seed; NEA and the company call it the Series A ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/how-perplexity-ai-is-pioneering-the-future-of-search), undated Q&A, likely 2023).\n- **He invested in Databricks before it had software revenue.** \"I invested in Databricks before they had any software revenue.\" Ghodsi says the company nearly failed in 2015 and that Sonsini \"stuck out his neck\" ([src](https://www.kellogg.northwestern.edu/magazine/departments/summer-2026/pete-sonsini/), Summer 2026).\n- **He was already watching Anyscale's co-founders as future founders.** At the 2020 Series B he wrote: \"I already find myself wondering what they might do next\" (Nishihara and Moritz) ([src](https://nea.com/blog/anyscale-blog), 2020-10-21).\n- **He pattern-matches infra deals to VMware.** \"The promise of Anyscale reminds me of what made VMware so special.\" The analogy is a developer-first wedge that then goes into production ([src](https://nea.com/blog/anyscale-blog)).\n- **He is contrarian on strategic capital.** \"Avoid Taking an Investment, Unless It’s from a Consortium.\" His reason is that one strategic gets signalling and first-look rights ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/biz-dev-can-make-or-break-your-startup-10-rules-for-getting-it-right), 2017-01-12).\n- **His open-source diligence rule is adoption first.** \"focus first on adoption, then on revenue\". He calls GitHub stars a vanity metric and says NEA tends to \"know it when we see\" it and move fast ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/02/05/where-top-vcs-are-investing-in-open-source-and-dev-tools-part-2-of-2/), 2020-02-05).\n- **Laude's LP base is its sourcing engine.** \"We have limited partners that are researchers\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/18/arcade-raises-12m-from-perplexity-co-founders-new-fund-to-make-ai-agents-less-awful/)). Fund I has 50+ computer scientists, \"one in three of which is a unicorn founder\" ([src](https://laude.vc/news/from-breakthrough-research-to-breakout-companies)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, with a caveat.** He sits on the Berkeley research-founder pipeline (Sky/RISE lab spinouts: Anyscale, Arena, Tensormesh) and co-runs a fund whose LPs include Jeff Dean, Dave Patterson, Ion Stoica, Ali Ghodsi and Aravind Srinivas. That makes him a good co-investor and source for AI infra and inference seeds. The caveat is that his record is enterprise infrastructure and dev tools, not frontier pre-training labs. With a $150M fund he cannot anchor $100M+ seeds, so he is a co-investor in those rounds, not a competitor. Deals that matter to the owner: Perplexity A (2023), Anyscale B (2020), Arena seed (2025), Tensormesh seed (2025), Arcade (2025).\n\n## Background\n- 1996: Kellogg MBA ([src](https://www.kellogg.northwestern.edu/magazine/departments/summer-2026/pete-sonsini/)).\n- Before VMware: product manager at HP, and a sales role ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/biz-dev-can-make-or-break-your-startup-10-rules-for-getting-it-right)).\n- About 2001-2005: senior director of strategic alliances at VMware. He ran OEM and alliances for Diane Greene and structured the IBM deal ([src](https://www.kellogg.northwestern.edu/magazine/departments/summer-2026/pete-sonsini/), [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/biz-dev-can-make-or-break-your-startup-10-rules-for-getting-it-right)).\n- 2005-2024: NEA, head of software investing ([src](https://www.kellogg.northwestern.edu/magazine/departments/summer-2026/pete-sonsini/)). He was co-head of the enterprise software practice by 2013 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2013/04/26/ask-a-vc-neas-pete-sonsini-on-the-next-disruptive-startup-in-the-enterprise/)) and is named General Partner in 2015 and 2020 releases. Exact exit date not found.\n- 2024-12: co-founded Laude Ventures with Andy Konwinski and Andrew Krioukov ([src](https://laude.vc/news/from-breakthrough-research-to-breakout-companies)).\n- Family: his father is Larry Sonsini, founder of Wilson Sonsini (vcsheet profile, aggregator: [src](https://www.vcsheet.com/who/pete-sonsini)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://laude.vc/team | Laude team page (checked 2026-11-11) |\n| Substack / blog | https://laude.vc/news ; https://www.nea.com/blog | Laude deal memos (2024-); older NEA posts are mostly unbylined |\n| X | https://x.com/psonsini | Handle per NEA blog footers and vcsheet; posts not reviewed (X not fetchable) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/petesonsini/ | per vcsheet |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | No podcast guest appearances found; his spoken record is mostly theCUBE and TechCrunch video |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2011-08: theCUBE, VMworld 2011 VC panel with Charles Curran (Valhalla). https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nvTfwOUUqaM. Not mined (transcript blocked).\n- 2013-04: TechCrunch Ask A VC on which layer of the cloud stack to build in. https://techcrunch.com/2013/04/26/ask-a-vc-neas-pete-sonsini-on-the-next-disruptive-startup-in-the-enterprise/. Video not mined.\n- 2014-08: theCUBE, VMworld 2014 with Jerry Chen (Greylock). https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L7d10v2P6FU. Not mined.\n- 2015-04: #VCchat Episode 1 CrowdChat, \"Ask VC Pete Anything\". https://siliconangle.com/2015/04/06/launching-vcchat-inaugural-crowdchat-with-nea-pete-sonsini/\n- 2015-08/09: theCUBE, VMworld 2015. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D53pg3oaJGc. Mined through the SiliconANGLE write-up: he looks for when a wave arrives and the path to it ([src](https://siliconangle.com/2015/10/12/vc-reveals-who-gets-funded-vmworld/)).\n- 2015: GSV Hall of Fame tribute to Dick Kramlich. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4ovQUMwcUDQ\n- Undated: CNBC interview with Perplexity board members Cack Wilhelm and Pete Sonsini. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xPc_mQ3cWEo. Not mined.\n- 2025-04-16: UIUC fireside chat with Andy Konwinski, \"My Journey From Research to Startup\". https://calendars.illinois.edu/detail/7604/33517862. No recording found.\n- 2025-06: theCUBE at Databricks Data+AI Summit 2025, on leaving NEA for Laude. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c_Zw1qyG_MI. Not mined.\n- 2026: Kellogg video \"From Research to Returns\". https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1kF_hL3OUmc. Not mined.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** real-time data processing as the key to big data (2014); open source eating the whole stack, including apps (2020); now research spinouts in AI infrastructure, such as inference caching, evals and agent auth (2025).\n- **Contrarian views:** \"Big Data\" was a dead buzzword by 2015 even though he kept backing the layer beneath it ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/data-performance-management-unlocking-the-enterprises-greatest-asset), unbylined). He avoids single strategic investors. He treats losses as proof of enough risk-taking.\n- **Calls he got right:** Databricks before revenue (2014); Anyscale (2020); Perplexity Series A (2023). He named Stormpath as a 2014 example of the next cloud wave and backed its founder again a decade later ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2014/05/25/how-cloud-startups-are-changing-the-face-of-innovation/)).\n- **Calls he got wrong:** none admitted in what I read.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Databricks makes venture seem easy\" (2014): the sleep test, his attitude to losses and the Stoica chain. https://www.nea.com/blog/databricks-makes-venture-seem-easy\n2. \"Biz Dev Can Make (or Break) Your Startup\" (2017): his VMware/IBM operator playbook and his rule on strategic capital. https://www.nea.com/blog/biz-dev-can-make-or-break-your-startup-10-rules-for-getting-it-right\n3. Anyscale Series B memo (2020): the serial-founder thesis and the VMware analogy. https://nea.com/blog/anyscale-blog\n4. Laude Fund I letter (2024): the research-founder thesis and the LP base. https://laude.vc/news/from-breakthrough-research-to-breakout-companies\n5. Arcade memo (2025): his repeat-founder sourcing. https://laude.vc/news/arcade-dev-unlocking-ais-full-potential-in-the-enterprise\n\n## Social engagement\nX handle @psonsini is listed in NEA's 2020 posts. I could not read it, because X isn't fetchable here. No pattern of public commentary found; he publishes mainly as deal memos.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Conviva | Early (one of his first NEA deals) | ~2006 (undated) | unknown | | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/databricks-makes-venture-seem-easy) |\n| MapR | NEA investment | undated | unknown | | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/databricks-makes-venture-seem-easy) |\n| GuideSpark | NEA investment | 2014-03 | yes | | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/the-last-mile-of-enterprise-applications) |\n| Databricks | Series B ($33M) | 2014-06 | yes | Andreessen Horowitz | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/databricks-makes-venture-seem-easy) |\n| Stormpath | investor and board member | undated | unknown | | [src](https://laude.vc/news/arcade-dev-unlocking-ais-full-potential-in-the-enterprise) |\n| StreamSets | NEA investment (unbylined post) | 2015-09 | unknown | | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/data-performance-management-unlocking-the-enterprises-greatest-asset) |\n| Anyscale | Series A (participant) / Series B ($40M) | 2019-12 / 2020-10 | no / yes | a16z, Intel Capital, Foundation Capital | [src](https://nea.com/blog/anyscale-blog) |\n| Plasmic | early stage | by 2020-02 | unknown | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/02/05/where-top-vcs-are-investing-in-open-source-and-dev-tools-part-2-of-2/) |\n| Perplexity | Series A (~$25.6M) | 2023-03 | yes | Elad Gil, Nat Friedman, Databricks Ventures | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/our-investment-in-perplexity-ai-answer-engines-and-the-end-of-traditional-search) |\n| Arcade | Seed ($12M, Laude) | 2025-03 | yes | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/18/arcade-raises-12m-from-perplexity-co-founders-new-fund-to-make-ai-agents-less-awful/) |\n| Arena | Seed ($100M) | 2025-05 | no | a16z and UC Investments (leads), Lightspeed, Felicis, Kleiner Perkins, The House Fund | [src](https://laude.vc/news/lmarena-turning-research-driven-evaluation-into-ai-infrastructure) |\n| Tensormesh | Seed ($4.5M) | 2025-10 | yes | | [src](https://laude.vc/news/tensormesh-cutting-cost-ai-inference-10x) |\n\nEarlier boards per TechCrunch 2013: Xensource (acquired by Citrix), Teracent (acquired by Google), Engine Yard, Eucalyptus ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2013/04/26/ask-a-vc-neas-pete-sonsini-on-the-next-disruptive-startup-in-the-enterprise/)).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Databricks (2014):** Conviva, co-founded by Ion Stoica, was one of his first NEA deals. MapR convinced him that real-time processing was the bigger opportunity, so leading Databricks' $33M Series B felt \"as natural as breathing\" ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/databricks-makes-venture-seem-easy)). Databricks had no software revenue and the raise was hard. Ghodsi says it almost failed in 2015 and credits Sonsini for backing them ([src](https://www.kellogg.northwestern.edu/magazine/departments/summer-2026/pete-sonsini/)).\n- **Anyscale (2019-2020):** NEA invested in the A, which a16z led, then Sonsini led the $40M B. He called it \"the next chapter\" of the Stoica partnership ([src](https://nea.com/blog/anyscale-blog)).\n- **Perplexity (2023):** the term sheet carried a 30-day diligence clause. After the Bing chat leak, Sonsini called the founders to reassure them ([src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/how-perplexity-ai-is-pioneering-the-future-of-search)). Andy Konwinski was Perplexity's president at the time, which seeded the later Laude partnership.\n- **Arcade (2025):** he heard that Salazar, his Stormpath founder, was starting again and reached out. It was Laude's first public check ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/18/arcade-raises-12m-from-perplexity-co-founders-new-fund-to-make-ai-agents-less-awful/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ion Stoica | Backed Conviva, Databricks, Anyscale; co-investor in Arena | ~2006 | [src](https://nea.com/blog/anyscale-blog) |\n| Andy Konwinski | Laude co-founder and co-GP; Perplexity president during the NEA round | 2023 | [src](https://laude.vc/news/from-breakthrough-research-to-breakout-companies) |\n| Andrew Krioukov | Laude co-founder and partner | 2024 | [src](https://laude.vc/news/from-breakthrough-research-to-breakout-companies) |\n| Ali Ghodsi | Databricks board (2014); Laude LP | 2014 | [src](https://www.kellogg.northwestern.edu/magazine/departments/summer-2026/pete-sonsini/) |\n| Aravind Srinivas | Led Perplexity A; Laude LP | 2023 | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/how-perplexity-ai-is-pioneering-the-future-of-search) |\n| Alex Salazar | Stormpath investor and board member; Arcade lead | pre-2017 | [src](https://laude.vc/news/arcade-dev-unlocking-ais-full-potential-in-the-enterprise) |\n| Raghu Raghuram | VMware-era colleague; vouches for him in Kellogg profile | VMware era | [src](https://www.kellogg.northwestern.edu/magazine/departments/summer-2026/pete-sonsini/) |\n| Jeff Dean, Dave Patterson | Laude Fund I LPs | 2024 | [src](https://laude.vc/news/from-breakthrough-research-to-breakout-companies) |\n| New Enterprise Associates | GP, head of software/enterprise (former) | 2005-2024 | [src](https://www.kellogg.northwestern.edu/magazine/departments/summer-2026/pete-sonsini/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Transcripts of theCUBE 2025 (NEA to Laude), the CNBC Perplexity board interview and VMworld 2014/2015. All are blocked from this environment, so retry with a working YouTube transcript route.\n- Exact NEA exit date, and whether he kept a Venture Advisor title (aggregator only).\n- Laude check sizes per deal, and whether Laude will lead anything above about $12M.\n- Is he still on the Perplexity board? The CNBC video title says so; other sources don't confirm.\n- Authorship of unbylined NEA posts (StreamSets 2015, Open for Business 2014, Perplexity 2023).\n- The 2015 #VCchat CrowdChat archive may hold written Q&A.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/databricks-makes-venture-seem-easy\n- https://nea.com/blog/anyscale-blog\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/biz-dev-can-make-or-break-your-startup-10-rules-for-getting-it-right\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/data-performance-management-unlocking-the-enterprises-greatest-asset\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/our-investment-in-perplexity-ai-answer-engines-and-the-end-of-traditional-search\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/the-last-mile-of-enterprise-applications\n- https://techcrunch.com/2013/04/26/ask-a-vc-neas-pete-sonsini-on-the-next-disruptive-startup-in-the-enterprise/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2014/05/25/how-cloud-startups-are-changing-the-face-of-innovation/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2020/02/05/where-top-vcs-are-investing-in-open-source-and-dev-tools-part-2-of-2/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/18/arcade-raises-12m-from-perplexity-co-founders-new-fund-to-make-ai-agents-less-awful/\n- https://siliconangle.com/2015/10/12/vc-reveals-who-gets-funded-vmworld/\n- https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/how-perplexity-ai-is-pioneering-the-future-of-search\n- https://laude.vc/news/from-breakthrough-research-to-breakout-companies\n- https://laude.vc/news/arcade-dev-unlocking-ais-full-potential-in-the-enterprise\n- https://laude.vc/news/lmarena-turning-research-driven-evaluation-into-ai-infrastructure\n- https://laude.vc/news/tensormesh-cutting-cost-ai-inference-10x\n- https://www.kellogg.northwestern.edu/magazine/departments/summer-2026/pete-sonsini/\n- https://www.vcsheet.com/who/pete-sonsini (aggregator)\n", "person:peter-devlin": "\n# Peter Devlin\n\n## One-line take\nHe is Hummingbird's only investor with a stated deep-tech remit and a physics/AI-research background, and he joined as the firm moved into AI chips and compute (Etched 2024; OLIX, Watney, MicroAGI 2026). No source yet ties him by name to any deal, so he is a person to watch, not yet someone with a track record ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team), [portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he could win:** he is a technical screener inside a founder-first, two-key firm that does not run checklists. His record covers Oxford physics, an AI-for-particle-physics paper with a US DOE national lab, and feasibility diligence on climate and energy tech for angels and PE ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)). That fits underwriting photonics (OLIX), chip fabrication (Atum Works) and data-center robotics (Watney).\n- **Constraint:** he has no decision rights. Every Hummingbird deal needs both Firat Ileri and Barend Van den Brande ([Generalist 2023-12-03](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird), as recorded in [firms/hummingbird-ventures.md](../firms/hummingbird-ventures.md)). The Generalist also reports, unconfirmed, that junior investors only make partner after returning a fund (same source). So he sources and diligences, and the MPs sign.\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** underwrite by credential or by consensus. The firm says it does not use pedigree screening ([site](https://www.hummingbird.vc/)). Nothing he has said himself has been found.\n- **The honest negative:** no company announcement, press piece, post or podcast found names Peter Devlin on any deal. The links to Etched, OLIX, Watney and MicroAGI come from title and timing only.\n\n## Differential insight\nSearch budget for this run was used up after the first query. Only the firm bio and portfolio page could be fetched directly. No podcast, Substack, X or talk was found or reachable. Each bullet is from the firm site, so none of them is highly non-obvious.\n- **Remit stated in his own firm's words:** \"to focus on the most transformative deep tech startups\" ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team), read 2026-11-10).\n- **Diligence muscle:** before Hummingbird he consulted for angels and private equity \"on the feasibility of emerging climate and energy technologies\" ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)). It reads like a technical go/no-go role, which a hardware-heavy deal flow needs. That would make Thaleron (mechanical grid storage, 2023) and Fuse Energy (2026) natural adjacent deals, but neither is attributed to him.\n- **Research credential:** he \"authored a paper on AI modeling of particle physics\" with a DOE national lab ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)). The paper, the lab and his coauthors were not identified (logged as `needs-id`).\n- **Timing (inference from two firm pages):** \"Peter joined Hummingbird in 2024\" ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)). The portfolio dates Etched and Truffle to 2024, Atum Works to 2025, and OLIX, Watney and MicroAGI to 2026 ([portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio)). Hummingbird's deep-tech ramp lines up with his tenure. Before that, the firm's deep-tech names were space and robotics (Starpath, Salient Motion, Karman+, Monumental) rather than compute.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High on remit, unverified on agency.**\n- **Remit fit:** the deals in his stated area are exactly my thesis sectors. They are AI chips and compute (Etched; OLIX, photonic chips with no HBM; Atum Works, 3D nanofabrication for chips; Truffle, local inference hardware), AI-native automation (Watney, robots for AI data-center cabling) and a physical-AI lab (MicroAGI) ([portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio)).\n- **Agency:** unconfirmed. If he sourced OLIX before its Feb 2026 $220M round, he got into a UK AI-chip company before it hit a $3.3B valuation (Aug 2026 Series B led by Fundomo) ([tech.eu](https://tech.eu/2026/08/03/uk-chip-startup-olix-raises-ps312m-at-ps33bn-valuation/)). That would make him a high-value European compute scout.\n- **Why he matters to me:** he is a likely co-investor and diligence partner on European deep-tech and compute seeds. He also comes from the Entrepreneur First EIR pipeline, which is a pre-team talent funnel.\n\n## Background\n| Period | Role | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| not dated | BA Physics, University of Oxford | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) |\n| not dated | Authored a paper on AI modeling of particle physics with a US DOE national lab (lab not named) | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) |\n| not dated | Entrepreneur-in-Residence, Entrepreneur First | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) |\n| not dated (after EF, before 2024) | Consultant to angels and PE on climate and energy tech feasibility | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) |\n| 2024 to now | Investor, Hummingbird Ventures (deep tech) | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) |\n\nPronouns: the firm bio uses \"he\".\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.hummingbird.vc/team | Firm bio; read 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | x.com/peterdevlin is not confirmed as his (fetch blocked); not linked from firm page |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/peter-devlin/ | Linked from firm page; fetch blocked (HTTP 999) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was unavailable for this run, so this is not a confirmed absence.\n\n## How they think\n- **Main theses (from remit only):** \"transformative\" deep tech. The portfolio in that remit centres on removing AI-compute bottlenecks: memory bandwidth (OLIX), chip fabrication (Atum Works), data-center labour (Watney) and inference at the edge (Truffle) ([portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio)). This is inference from the portfolio, not his stated view.\n- **Contrarian views:** nothing found.\n- **Calls right or wrong:** nothing attributable.\n\n## Read these first\n- Hummingbird team bio: https://www.hummingbird.vc/team (the only primary source on him).\n- The Generalist's Hummingbird profile, for the firm's decision mechanics he works within: https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird\n- The OLIX Series B coverage, for the deal his remit most likely touched: https://tech.eu/2026/08/03/uk-chip-startup-olix-raises-ps312m-at-ps33bn-valuation/\n- His DOE particle-physics paper: not yet identified.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X account confirmed, and LinkedIn is not readable without login.\n\n## Portfolio\nNothing is attributed to him personally. The firm's portfolio rows in his remit (firm-level, not personal) are below. `data/rows/peter-devlin-deals.csv` is left header-only on purpose.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Etched (firm) | Early | 2024 | unknown | not stated | [portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio) |\n| Truffle (firm) | Pre-Seed/Seed | 2024 | unknown | not stated | [portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio) |\n| Atum Works (firm) | Pre-Seed/Seed | 2025 | unknown | not stated | [portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio) |\n| OLIX (firm) | Early | 2026 | unknown | Series B (2026-08-03): Fundomo (lead), Arm, HRT, UK Sovereign AI fund, Reed Hastings | [portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio), [tech.eu](https://tech.eu/2026/08/03/uk-chip-startup-olix-raises-ps312m-at-ps33bn-valuation/) |\n| Watney (firm) | Early | 2026 | unknown | not found | [portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio) |\n| MicroAGI (firm) | Early | 2026 | unknown | not found | [portfolio](https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. The tech.eu article on OLIX does not mention Hummingbird. The OLIX, MicroAGI, Thaleron and Karman+ homepages name no investors from Hummingbird.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hummingbird Ventures | Investor, deep tech | 2024 | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) |\n| Entrepreneur First | Former Entrepreneur-in-Residence | not dated | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) |\n| University of Oxford | BA Physics | not dated | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) |\n| US DOE national lab (unnamed) | Paper on AI modeling of particle physics | not dated | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team) |\n| Firat Ileri, Barend Van den Brande | Two-key deal approvers at his firm | 2024 | [team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team), [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which deals is he on? Check Watney and MicroAGI funding announcements and OLIX's Feb 2026 round press for investor quotes, and check his LinkedIn activity.\n- Which paper and which DOE lab? Look on arXiv or INSPIRE-HEP for \"P. Devlin\" with an Oxford affiliation. Confirm the Oxford link before attaching it, because the name is common.\n- Was his EF stint with a specific cohort or founder (possible pre-team relationships with OLIX's James Dacombe or Watney's founders)?\n- Did he work on Etched, or did Etched predate his arrival in 2024?\n- Location: London or US?\n- Does he have an X handle or write anywhere?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.hummingbird.vc/team (bio, raw HTML read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.hummingbird.vc/portfolio (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.hummingbird.vc/\n- https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird (2023-12-03; via firms/hummingbird-ventures.md)\n- https://tech.eu/2026/08/03/uk-chip-startup-olix-raises-ps312m-at-ps33bn-valuation/\n- https://olix.com/ ; https://www.microagi.ai/ ; https://thaleron.com/ ; https://www.karmanplus.com/ (none name him)\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/peter-devlin/ (blocked)\n- Web search budget was used up for this run, so no searches ran. Coverage is limited to direct fetches.\n", "person:peter-levine": "\n# Peter Levine\n\n> **Data warning (2026-11-11):** the id `peter-levine` is also used in `data/relationships.csv` and `data/leads.csv` for a *different* Peter Levine, Brown University's incoming deputy CIO (ex-New Holland). This profile covers only the a16z GP. The main session needs to split the ids before merging.\n\n## One-line take\nHe is a former operator (Veritas, XenSource) who became a16z's main enterprise and infra GP of the 2010s. He is a reliable judge of developer-led go-to-market (GTM). By his own account he stopped actively sourcing in 2020, so for frontier AI he mainly matters as a route to Matt Bornstein and Martin Casado.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he knows how developer products turn into revenue. His own early-stage experience is running sales and an open-source company, and in his posts he keeps picking bottom-up, viral developer tools by matching them to GitHub: GitHub \"growing at 100% with no sales organization\" ([src](https://a16z.com/microsoft-buys-github/)), then DigitalOcean, Netlify and Figma. Founders take him on as the GTM board member: he says he brings \"go-to-market experience with bottom-up sales execution\" to Figma ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-figma-the-decade-of-design/)).\n- **Constraint:** in Sept 2020 he wrote that he \"will not actively be making new investments at a16z\" while remaining a GP of existing funds ([src](https://a16z.com/a-transition-to-my-new-normal/), 2020-09-04). Lead posts carrying his name kept appearing after that (Replay 2021, Trunk 2021, Graphite 2022, Pinecone 2023, GitButler 2026). The recent ones are co-signed with Matt Bornstein, so how much he does himself is unclear.\n- **What he'd never do:** no source states a hard rule. His writing argues against copying the Red Hat support model ([src](https://a16z.com/why-there-will-never-be-another-red-hat-the-economics-of-open-source/)) and against locking in a GTM motion early ([src](https://a16z.com/market-annealing-getting-to-10m-arr-in-very-early-markets/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The 2020 step-back:** \"I will not actively be making new investments at a16z\" ([src](https://a16z.com/a-transition-to-my-new-normal/), 2020-09-04). His current a16z page doesn't say this. The byline on that post calls him an \"advisor ... focuses on enterprise investing\", while the author page says general partner ([src](https://a16z.com/author/peter-levine/)).\n- **GitHub, Jan 2012:** the founder dinner was \"set up for me to convince them to let us invest\" ([src](https://a16z.com/microsoft-buys-github/), 2018-06-04). a16z was the one asking to get in.\n- **A 14-year loop:** GitHub co-founder Scott Chacon is now CEO of GitButler. a16z led its Series A in April 2026 and calls Chacon \"someone we at a16z have worked with for over a decade\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gitbutler/), 2026-04-08).\n- **Admitted bias on DigitalOcean:** before the 2014 first meeting he thought \"DigitalOcean couldn’t possibly make it\", and the numbers changed his mind. He later chaired the board through the 2021 IPO ([src](https://a16z.com/digitalocean-ipo/), 2021-03-24).\n- **Candid board style:** he told the incoming CEO there were 20 levers to an IPO, and admits \"I had no clue what the levers were!\" ([src](https://a16z.com/digitalocean-ipo/)).\n- **He watches a category for years before investing:** on Figma, \"When I first heard about Figma five years ago\" (that is, about 2015, five years before the 2020 investment) ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-figma-the-decade-of-design/)). On CAD, \"I have been keeping an eye out since I arrived at a16z\", until Onshape in 2015 ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/cad-emerges-from-the-stone-age-to-finally-join-the-mobile-era/)).\n- **A named rule, the \"Forrest Gump Rule of Investing\":** take away something important today and imagine what replaces it ([src](https://a16z.com/the-end-of-cloud-computing/), talk Dec 2016). The 2016 edge thesis (\"a drone is a data center with wings\") came a few months before he led the Shield AI Series A in March 2017. The link between the two is our inference.\n- **An early-2020 AI-infra view that aged badly:** \"Compute power is essentially unlimited\", so data is the bottleneck. That was the Labelbox thesis ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-labelbox/), 2020-02-04).\n- **Admitted mistake at XenSource:** \"At XenSource, we had inaccurate numbers at one point\". Download counts included unfinished downloads ([src](https://a16z.com/open-source-from-community-to-commercialization/), 2019-10-04).\n- **On open-source governance:** OpenStack failed because \"there was, in my mind, no leadership of that project\" ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/a16z-podcast-free-software-and-open-source-business/), 2019-10-21).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** He is a senior GP who said he stopped making new investments in 2020. His portfolio is developer tools and infra (GitHub, DigitalOcean, Figma, Netlify, PlanetScale, Graphite, GitButler), not labs or chips. The deals that do touch the thesis are the Shield AI Series A (2017, AI robotics), Labelbox (2020, AI training data) and the $100M Pinecone Series B (2023). The more useful people are the colleagues who co-sign his recent deals, **Matt Bornstein** and **Martin Casado**. He is not a likely co-investor on a $600M lab seed.\n\n## Background\n- BS in engineering, Boston University (1983). Software engineer on MIT's Project Athena while at MIT Sloan, 1988-89 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_J._Levine), secondary).\n- Veritas Software, about 11 years, from engineer to EVP ([src](https://a16z.com/author/peter-levine/)).\n- General partner at Mayfield Fund, 2002-05 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_J._Levine), secondary; Mayfield GP role confirmed on [a16z bio](https://a16z.com/author/peter-levine/)).\n- President and CEO of XenSource from Feb 2006; Citrix acquired it in 2007 for about $500M. He then ran Citrix's Data Center and Cloud division ([src](https://a16z.com/author/peter-levine/)).\n- Joined a16z in 2011 ([src](https://a16z.com/author/peter-levine/)). Taught at Stanford GSB (mentioned in his 2020 post, [src](https://a16z.com/a-transition-to-my-new-normal/)). The a16z bio lists him as adjunct professor at Dartmouth Tuck.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/peter-levine/ | About 85 items, 2013-2026; output fell sharply after 2020 |\n| Substack / blog | http://peter.a16z.com | Old personal blog; now redirects to the a16z author page (checked 2026-11-11) |\n| X | https://x.com/PeterL4e | Linked from the a16z author page; not read |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/peter-levine-681386172 | From the a16z author page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | a16z Podcast | Hosted or co-hosted about 30 episodes, 2014-2020 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2014-06 to 2016: a16z Podcast. A run of datacenter, storage, open-source and SaaS episodes with founders (Ben Uretsky, Chris Wanstrath, Florian Leibert, Haoyuan Li). Full list in `data/rows/peter-levine-voice-media.csv`.\n- 2015-07: UCTV talk, \"Peter Levine Partner Andreessen Horowitz\" ([video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HWM2si13jNg)). Not mined.\n- 2016-12: a16z Summit talk, \"The End of Cloud Computing\" ([transcript](https://a16z.com/the-end-of-cloud-computing/), [video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4QTAtFaIiyc)). Edge computing plus ML, the Forrest Gump Rule.\n- 2017-02: Software Engineering Daily, \"The End of Cloud Computing\" ([src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/2017/02/03/the-end-of-cloud-computing-with-peter-levine/)).\n- 2017-10: a16z Podcast, \"A New Lab Rises\", with Ion Stoica on Berkeley's RISELab ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/a16z-podcast-a-new-lab-rises/)). No transcript reached.\n- 2018-09: \"All Things Sales! 16 Mini-Lessons\", a video series plus 15 posts ([src](https://a16z.com/all-things-sales-16-mini-lessons-for-startup-founders/)).\n- 2019-01: a16z Podcast with Mark Leslie, his old Veritas CEO ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/a16z-podcast-stories-and-lessons-in-enterprise-sales/)). No transcript reached.\n- 2019-02: a16z Podcast, \"Product-Market Sales Fit\", with Jyoti Bansal ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/a16z-podcast-product-market-sales-fit-what-comes-first/)). Pricing: \"charge more than you think\".\n- 2019-03: Ingram Micro Cloud Summit X keynote on edge computing ([video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=74tKg06Jfpo)).\n- 2019-10: a16z panel with Ali Ghodsi and Armon Dadgar on open-source business ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/a16z-podcast-free-software-and-open-source-business/)).\n- 2020: two-part a16z episode with Alex Honnold on risk, plus \"The Open Source CIO\" with Red Hat's Mike Kelly.\n- 2025-04: Boston University Wadsworth lecture ([write-up](https://insights.bu.edu/2025-wadsworth-family-distinguished-lecture-in-business-leadership-technology-innovation-peter-levine/); no direct quotes).\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** bottom-up developer adoption turns into enterprise revenue in layers ([src](https://a16z.com/open-source-from-community-to-commercialization/)). Open-source companies need project-community fit, then product-market fit, then value-market fit. Computing swings between centralised and distributed, and ML drives it to the edge ([src](https://a16z.com/the-end-of-cloud-computing/)).\n- **Contrarian views:** the Red Hat support model was a business failure for everyone but Red Hat (2014). Category creation is \"somewhat of a false narrative\", and \"many markets never become pull-based\" (2023, with Casado, [src](https://a16z.com/market-annealing-getting-to-10m-arr-in-very-early-markets/)). Founder sales can carry a company to about $4M ARR.\n- **Calls right:** GitHub (2012, sold to Microsoft for $7.5B in 2018), DigitalOcean (IPO 2021), Figma (IPO 2025 per Wikipedia), and edge ML / autonomy before Shield AI. **Called badly:** \"Compute power is essentially unlimited\" (Feb 2020).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [A Transition to My New Normal](https://a16z.com/a-transition-to-my-new-normal/) (2020): the step-back that reframes every later deal credit.\n2. [Microsoft Buys GitHub](https://a16z.com/microsoft-buys-github/) (2018): the 2012 deal origin and his GitHub pattern.\n3. [The End of Cloud Computing](https://a16z.com/the-end-of-cloud-computing/) (2016): his forecasting method and the edge/ML thesis.\n4. [Market Annealing](https://a16z.com/market-annealing-getting-to-10m-arr-in-very-early-markets/) (2023, with Casado): how he coaches GTM in markets that don't exist yet.\n5. [Open Source: From Community to Commercialization](https://a16z.com/open-source-from-community-to-commercialization/) (2019): the three-pillar open-source framework and the XenSource lessons.\n\n## Social engagement\nX account @PeterL4e is linked from the a16z author page ([src](https://a16z.com/author/peter-levine/)). It was not read, so topics and amplifiers are unknown. Since 2020, Martin Casado, Matt Bornstein and Satish Talluri have been his most frequent co-authors at a16z.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| GitHub | Undisclosed (first board meeting 2012) | 2012 | unknown (board seat) | | [src](https://a16z.com/microsoft-buys-github/) |\n| DigitalOcean | Series B | 2014 | unknown; later board chair | | [src](https://a16z.com/digitalocean-ipo/) |\n| Arimo (Adatao) | Series A ($13M) | 2014-08 | yes (board) | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/big-data-meets-big-compute/) |\n| Tachyon Nexus (Alluxio) | Undisclosed | 2015-03 | board | | [src](https://a16z.com/tachyon-a-file-and-storage-system-for-the-future-of-computing/) |\n| Onshape | Undisclosed | 2015-09 | unknown | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/cad-emerges-from-the-stone-age-to-finally-join-the-mobile-era/) |\n| Shield AI | Series A ($10.5M) | 2017-03 | yes (board) | | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1675769/000167576917000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Netlify | Undisclosed (post-seed) | 2017-08 | unknown | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/netlify/) |\n| People.ai | Series B | 2018-10 | unknown | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/people-ai/) |\n| PlanetScale | Undisclosed (company described as pre-Series A) | 2019-05 | unknown | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/planetscale/) |\n| Apollo GraphQL | Undisclosed | 2019-06 | unknown | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/apollo-graphql/) |\n| Labelbox | Series B ($25M) | 2020-02 | yes | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-labelbox/) |\n| Figma | Undisclosed (2020) | 2020-04 | unknown | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-figma-the-decade-of-design/) |\n| Graphite | Series A ($20M) | 2022-05 | yes | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2022/05/03/how-graphite-turned-a-side-project-into-a-20m-series-a) |\n| Lumos | Series A | 2022-05 | yes | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-lumos/) |\n| Pinecone | Series B ($100M at $750M post) | 2023-04 | yes (board) | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-pinecone/) |\n| GitButler | Series A | 2026-04 | yes (with Bornstein) | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gitbutler/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **GitHub (2012):** the first meeting was a January 2012 dinner with Wanstrath, Preston-Werner, Hyett and Chacon. It was meant as a16z's pitch to them. He says the company had revenue and cash flow but no org charts, plans or sales function ([src](https://a16z.com/microsoft-buys-github/)).\n- **DigitalOcean (2014):** he arrived sceptical of cloud hosting, and the numbers and the founders' developer focus won him over. He led the B and was later board chair after the IPO ([src](https://a16z.com/digitalocean-ipo/)).\n- **Netlify (2017):** he was convinced within one meeting, at 75,000 developers growing about 200 a day \"on seed funding alone\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/netlify/)).\n- **Figma (2020):** he followed the founders from about 2015 and joined in 2020 as the bottom-up GTM adviser ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-figma-the-decade-of-design/)).\n- **GitButler (2026):** a16z led the Series A in the company of Scott Chacon, whom Levine met at the 2012 GitHub dinner ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gitbutler/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Andreessen Horowitz | General Partner | 2011 | [src](https://a16z.com/author/peter-levine/) |\n| Mayfield | General Partner | 2002-2005 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_J._Levine) |\n| XenSource / Citrix | CEO, then SVP/GM | 2006-2011 | [src](https://a16z.com/author/peter-levine/) |\n| Veritas Software | Engineer to EVP | 1990-2001 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_J._Levine) |\n| Mark Leslie | Former Veritas boss; 2019 podcast | 1990 | [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/a16z-podcast-stories-and-lessons-in-enterprise-sales/) |\n| Scott Chacon | GitHub (2012) to GitButler (2026) | 2012 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gitbutler/) |\n| Matt Bornstein | Co-author on Pinecone and GitButler | 2023 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-pinecone/) |\n| Martin Casado | Co-author, Market Annealing; podcast co-host | 2016 | [src](https://a16z.com/market-annealing-getting-to-10m-arr-in-very-early-markets/) |\n| Ben and Moisey Uretsky | DigitalOcean founders; board chair | 2014 | [src](https://a16z.com/digitalocean-ipo/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Id collision:** `peter-levine` is also used for Brown's incoming deputy CIO. The main session needs to give that person a separate id (for example `peter-levine-brown`) and move the Brown rels and leads rows to it.\n- How hands-on is he on the 2021-2026 leads he is bylined on, given the 2020 step-back? Is Matt Bornstein the real deal owner on Pinecone and GitButler?\n- Is he still on the Shield AI and Pinecone boards? Pinecone's 2023 Form D lists him as a director.\n- The transcripts of \"Stories and Lessons in Enterprise Sales\" (with Mark Leslie), \"A New Lab Rises\" (Ion Stoica) and the 2015 UCTV talk were not reachable. They may hold Veritas and XenSource deal stories.\n- Did he invest in Anyscale or Databricks through his RISELab/AMPLab ties? The Tachyon post calls Databricks and Mesosphere \"our investments\".\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/peter-levine/\n- https://a16z.com/a-transition-to-my-new-normal/\n- https://a16z.com/microsoft-buys-github/\n- https://a16z.com/digitalocean-ipo/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-figma-the-decade-of-design/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/cad-emerges-from-the-stone-age-to-finally-join-the-mobile-era/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/netlify/\n- https://a16z.com/the-end-of-cloud-computing/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/a16z-podcast-free-software-and-open-source-business/\n- https://a16z.com/open-source-from-community-to-commercialization/\n- https://a16z.com/why-there-will-never-be-another-red-hat-the-economics-of-open-source/\n- https://a16z.com/market-annealing-getting-to-10m-arr-in-very-early-markets/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/a16z-podcast-product-market-sales-fit-what-comes-first/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-labelbox/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-pinecone/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gitbutler/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/people-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/tachyon-a-file-and-storage-system-for-the-future-of-computing/\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_J._Levine (secondary)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1675769/000167576917000002/primary_doc.xml\n- https://techcrunch.com/2022/05/03/how-graphite-turned-a-side-project-into-a-20m-series-a\n- https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/2017/02/03/the-end-of-cloud-computing-with-peter-levine/\n- https://insights.bu.edu/2025-wadsworth-family-distinguished-lecture-in-business-leadership-technology-innovation-peter-levine/\n", "person:peter-singlehurst": "\n# Peter Singlehurst\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (strategics-2 sweep). The firm-level view is in `firms/baillie-gifford.md`. This page covers what he personally signs and says. Two points here correct the firm page: the Tekever 2024 lead, and the reasons to doubt the Crusoe guess.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe runs the private-company book at the Edinburgh partnership that is the patient, IPO-bridging buyer of frontier-AI winners. He is also the public voice explaining why Schiehallion paid up for Anthropic and why it is \"quite discriminating\" about the rest of AI ([Schiehallion page](https://bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/funds/schiehallion-fund)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins allocation:** he offers founders a holder that sits between VC and the public market. When Baillie Gifford invests privately, it is \"the first touchpoint\" with an institution that can become the largest public shareholder (he cites Spotify, where Baillie Gifford is the largest holder after Daniel Ek, and Wise) ([Synthesis forum, Oct 2025](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/)). He pitches governance, not control: he calls control investing \"inappropriate for these companies\" and runs board-building forums ahead of IPO (same).\n- **What constrains him:** (1) **The fund is fully deployed.** Schiehallion \"reached full deployment for the first time in its history\" in FY to January 2026, so every new deal is paid for by selling something ([annual report, 2026-04-07](https://www.investegate.co.uk/announcement/rns/the-schiehallion-fund-limited-npv--mntn/schiehallion-fund-annual-financial-report-/9509143)). About $575M has been realised and recycled since 2019, half of it from SpaceX and Bending Spoons ([Q3 2026 update](https://bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/funds/schiehallion-fund)). (2) **The hurdle:** \"a plausible path to making five times our investments over five years\", and they invest in \"around 1 percent\" of what they see (same). (3) **He doesn't set his own marks.** A valuations group independent of the investment team, advised by S&P Global, sets them, and no management fee is charged on cash (annual report).\n- **What he'd never do:** take control, or invest before product-market fit. He defines his universe as companies doing \"a couple of $100m in revenue\" and growing about 70% (Synthesis forum).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He leads first institutional rounds when the company is outside the consensus.** On Tekever he said \"We first invested in 2024, leading the company's first institutional funding round alongside the NATO Innovation Fund\" ([Q3 2026 update](https://bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/funds/schiehallion-fund)). That was the EUR 70M Series B of 2024-11-20, which press says Baillie Gifford led ([SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=678515)). Schiehallion then co-led the $580M Series D at $6.4B in September 2026, and Tekever is now a top-five holding. This contradicts the \"enters at Series C\" framing used elsewhere in this repo.\n- **The October 2026 \"crucial partner in the buildout of AI infrastructure\" is probably not Crusoe's Series F.** He said they are \"currently finalising\" an \"off-market transaction that we came to through our network\", and \"we'll tell you more about that next time\" (Q3 2026 update, produced in October 2026). Crusoe's Series F had already named Baillie Gifford publicly on 2026-09-17 ([Crusoe](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-f-funding)). A deal that was already public isn't one you promise to reveal next quarter, and \"off-market\" suggests a secondary or a privately negotiated primary. **Unconfirmed either way.** The Q4 2026 update, the Schiehallion monthly factsheet or the January 2027 annual report will name it.\n- **Anthropic sizing:** Schiehallion's net purchases of Anthropic in FY to January 2026 were exactly US$25.0M, marked at $45.96M (2.6% of NAV) at 2026-01-31 ([annual report](https://www.investegate.co.uk/announcement/rns/the-schiehallion-fund-limited-npv--mntn/schiehallion-fund-annual-financial-report-/9509143)). Baillie Gifford's marketing director told the AIC the cost through May 2026 was $46M ([AIC](https://www.theaic.co.uk/aic/news/press-releases/investment-trust-investors-to-achieve-strong-returns-on-anthropic-ipo)). That implies about $21M more around the Series H (derived). His explanation for the gain: run rate went from about $5B to about $65B, \"more than tenfold in the space of a year\" (Q3 2026 update).\n- **The liquidity point he makes in private:** \"If we wanted, we could sell our shares in ByteDance, back to ByteDance\", and he declines because he thinks the shares are underpriced. SpaceX was trimmed through company tenders (Synthesis forum).\n- **How he funded the AI entry:** in the year he bought Anthropic, Schiehallion fully sold Airbnb, Oscar Health and Warby Parker, sold Masterclass in a secondary, and trimmed SpaceX and Cohesity through secondaries. That money paid for 8 new deals (Anthropic, Anduril, Revolut, Rippling, Avanci, Clear Street, Cellares, RedNote) (annual report).\n\n*Attribution note:* the Synthesis forum quotes come from an unlabelled caption file. They are assigned to Singlehurst from the spoken handoffs (\"I'll turn it over to Peter\" and \"I'm going to pass over to Rob now\"), so confidence is medium.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low as a co-investor, medium as a relationship.** He won't be in a $600M seed. His book starts where product-market fit is proven, and he says AI \"valuations are elevated\". He matters to the owner in three ways. (1) He is the later-round buyer who holds through IPO for a lab the owner seeds, once it has revenue. (2) Tekever shows he will lead the first institutional round of a non-consensus company found through his network, so a hardware-heavy or defence-adjacent AI company could get him earlier than Series C. (3) He decides where Schiehallion's IPO proceeds go next, and in October 2026 that was AI infrastructure.\n\n## Background\n- Durham University; joined Baillie Gifford \"straight out of\" university in 2010 ([20VC, 2022-07-14](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/peter-singlehurst)). Exact degree not found.\n- Baillie Gifford teams: Credit, UK Equities and Global Discovery, then Long Term Global Growth from 2014, where he became \"the first investment manager at Baillie Gifford to work exclusively on private company research\" ([bio](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/global/existing-investors/insights/ic-video/2024-q2-schiehallion-manager-update-10046711/)).\n- Manager of The Schiehallion Fund since its 2019 launch. Partner from May 2022 ([bio](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/global/existing-investors/insights/ic-video/2024-q2-schiehallion-manager-update-10046711/); [partner announcement](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/1kihreff/20220120143946_new_partners_announcement.pdf)). Head of Private Companies (date not found).\n- 20VC credits him with backing Wise, Grammarly and Zymergen ([20VC](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/peter-singlehurst)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [Schiehallion fund page](https://bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/funds/schiehallion-fund) | Quarterly manager videos with transcripts; the Q3 2026 update is his (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | The firm bio page links to LinkedIn, but no profile URL was confirmed |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n| Email (firm inbound) | privateopportunities@bailliegifford.com | Firm-level deal inbound ([bailliegifford.com](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/individual-investors/about-us/private-companies/)) |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** Over 80% of deals come through Baillie Gifford's own network ([bailliegifford.com](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/individual-investors/about-us/private-companies/)). The routes that show up in his deals are other growth investors and defence or sovereign co-investors (NATO Innovation Fund on Tekever), plus the public-equity teams' company relationships. A warm intro through a Baillie Gifford public-team portfolio founder, or through Robert Natzler (co-manager), beats the inbound address.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-10 — Schiehallion Q3 update (video, 7 min) — [fund page](https://bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/funds/schiehallion-fund) — Anthropic tenfold, Tekever first-round lead, the off-market AI-infra deal, AI valuations elevated.\n- 2026-05 — \"Schiehallion: finding growth before the market\" (43 min) — [fund page](https://bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/funds/schiehallion-fund) — not transcribed in this pass.\n- 2026-03 — \"Private Companies: our philosophy\" (4 min) — same page.\n- 2025-10 — Synthesis forum, \"Navigating the $14tn private market\", with Natzler and Brian Kelly — [page](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/) — about 1,000 companies met a year and about 10 deals; governance over control; ByteDance buyback liquidity.\n- 2024-04 — Schiehallion manager update with Natzler — [transcript PDF](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/xzflnyl5/20251218115053_baillie-gifford-webinar-schiehallion-manager-update-transcript-april-2024.pdf). The team's 2024 AI view \"biases away from the middle of that stack\": chips and apps, not model labs. The speaker for that section is probably Natzler.\n- 2022-07-14 — 20VC with Harry Stebbings — [episode](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/peter-singlehurst) — portfolio construction, GP/LP misalignment, his biggest miss and hit. Transcript not retrieved.\n\n## How they think\n- Growth gives venture-like asymmetry with lower loss rates. Compounding 60% growth over five years gives about 10x revenue, which can \"absorb\" multiple compression and dilution (Synthesis forum; growth-math segment).\n- Private markets misprice growth because there are no quant arbitrageurs. He compares Stripe with Adyen and Bending Spoons with Constellation Software (Synthesis forum).\n- On AI: the firm held back for years, then bought Anthropic once margins and Claude Code proved the business. He now says the firm is \"quite discriminating around AI\" (Q3 2026).\n- **Calls:** Anthropic (about 2x cost within months and now 8.3% of Schiehallion). Tekever (EUR 70M Series B in 2024, then $6.4B in 2026). **Miss:** the 2021 vintage, when Schiehallion's share price fell 48.6% and 55.6% in the years to September 2022 and 2023 (performance table, fund page).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Q3 2026 Schiehallion update transcript (fund page): the AI-infrastructure deal, Tekever, recycling.\n2. Synthesis forum Oct 2025: his full case for private growth and his access model.\n3. Schiehallion annual report FY to 2026-01-31: deal list, Anthropic sizing, fee and valuation mechanics.\n4. 20VC 2022 episode: portfolio construction and loss rates.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public X or Substack. He speaks only through Baillie Gifford videos and podcasts.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Anthropic | Series F ($13B at $183B post) — Schiehallion $25M | 2025-09-02 (first invested Aug 2025) | no | ICONIQ, Fidelity, Lightspeed | [annual report](https://www.investegate.co.uk/announcement/rns/the-schiehallion-fund-limited-npv--mntn/schiehallion-fund-annual-financial-report-/9509143) |\n| Anthropic | Series H ($65B at $965B post) — about $21M (derived) | 2026-05-28 | no | Altimeter, Dragoneer, Greenoaks, Sequoia | [AIC](https://www.theaic.co.uk/aic/news/press-releases/investment-trust-investors-to-achieve-strong-returns-on-anthropic-ipo) |\n| Tekever | Series B (EUR 70M; \"first institutional round\") | 2024-11-20 | **yes** | NATO Innovation Fund, NSSIF, Crescent Cove | [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=678515) |\n| Tekever | Series D ($580M at $6.4B) | 2026-09-23 | co-lead with UC Investments | Crescent Cove, Ventura | [inforcapital](https://inforcapital.com/news/tekever-raises-580-million-series-d-at-64-billion-valuation-led-by-uc-investments-and-baillie-gifford/) |\n| Grammarly | Growth ($200M at $13B) | 2021-11-17 | BG and BlackRock (lead status varies by outlet) | BlackRock | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2021/11/17/grammarly-raises-200m-at-a-13b-valuation-to-make-you-an-even-better-writer-through-ai/), [20VC](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/peter-singlehurst) |\n| Undisclosed AI-infrastructure \"crucial partner\" | off-market (likely secondary), finalising | 2026-10 | unknown | — | [Q3 2026](https://bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/funds/schiehallion-fund) |\n| Anduril (new Schiehallion holding) | round unconfirmed | FY to 2026-01 | no | — | [annual report](https://www.investegate.co.uk/announcement/rns/the-schiehallion-fund-limited-npv--mntn/schiehallion-fund-annual-financial-report-/9509143) |\n\nAlso in Schiehallion by role (non-AI or not his named deal): Databricks, Wayve, Bending Spoons, SpaceX, ByteDance, Revolut, Wise, Stripe. Board seats: none found.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Anthropic:** the firm's relationship began at the 2024 FTX-estate stake sale, according to Natzler, who tells that story ([transcript](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf)). Singlehurst's role was manager sign-off on the Schiehallion allocation. Schiehallion put in a $25M first check in the Series F, a follow-on around the Series H, and owned 8.3% of NAV by 2026-10-31.\n- **Tekever:** sourced outside VC. Schiehallion led the 2024 first institutional round with the NATO Innovation Fund, saw the company win a British Army programme worth up to GBP 400M, and co-led again at $6.4B (Q3 2026 update).\n- **AI-infra deal (Oct 2026):** reached \"through our network\", off-market, and paid for with SpaceX and Bending Spoons IPO proceeds. Unnamed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Robert Natzler | Schiehallion co-manager; co-signs reports | 2019 | [annual report](https://www.investegate.co.uk/announcement/rns/the-schiehallion-fund-limited-npv--mntn/schiehallion-fund-annual-financial-report-/9509143) |\n| Brian Kelly | Private Companies colleague; co-presented at Synthesis 2025 | 2025 | [Synthesis](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/) |\n| NATO Innovation Fund | Co-investor in the Tekever Series B | 2024-11 | [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=678515) |\n| Ricardo Mendes (Tekever CEO) | Portfolio founder; filmed conversation for Schiehallion | 2024 | [fund page](https://bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/funds/schiehallion-fund) |\n\n## Open questions\n- The name of the October 2026 off-market AI-infrastructure deal, and whether it is a secondary. Crusoe looks less likely given the timing.\n- His personal role on the Anthropic decision versus Natzler's (Natzler tells the story).\n- Contents of the 20VC episode (biggest miss and hit) and the 43-minute May 2026 webinar: no transcript retrieved.\n- Year he became Head of Private Companies.\n- Anduril entry round and size.\n\n## Sources\n- https://bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/funds/schiehallion-fund\n- https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/global/existing-investors/insights/ic-video/2024-q2-schiehallion-manager-update-10046711/\n- https://www.investegate.co.uk/announcement/rns/the-schiehallion-fund-limited-npv--mntn/schiehallion-fund-annual-financial-report-/9509143\n- https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/\n- https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/xzflnyl5/20251218115053_baillie-gifford-webinar-schiehallion-manager-update-transcript-april-2024.pdf\n- https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/peter-singlehurst\n- https://www.theaic.co.uk/aic/news/press-releases/investment-trust-investors-to-achieve-strong-returns-on-anthropic-ipo\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=678515\n- https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-f-funding\n- https://inforcapital.com/news/tekever-raises-580-million-series-d-at-64-billion-valuation-led-by-uc-investments-and-baillie-gifford/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2021/11/17/grammarly-raises-200m-at-a-13b-valuation-to-make-you-an-even-better-writer-through-ai/\n- https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/1kihreff/20220120143946_new_partners_announcement.pdf\n", "person:peter-specht": "\n# Peter Specht\n\n## One-line take\nCreandum's London AI partner: the person on Black Forest Labs, Rillet (seed co-lead to $1B in two years), Conduct, Maisa and Jack & Jill.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he is the German-speaking partner in a firm with the Lovable and Spotify brand, so DACH research founders (BFL) and European operators building in the US (Rillet's ex-N26 CEO) land with him.\n- **How he picks:** incumbents whose customised legacy data blocks AI. On NetSuite: \"it frankly sucks and is ripe for disruption\" ([src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-rillet)). He says he has \"a soft spot for founders with spikes\" ([src](https://creandum.com/team/peter-specht)).\n- **Constraint:** seed and Series A cheques from a European fund; in BFL he is a participant, not the lead.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Creandum never wrote a BFL memo, but his team page carries a photo captioned \"With Robin, Andreas and Patrick from BFL\" ([src](https://creandum.com/team/peter-specht)); a conference bio lists BFL among deals he led for the firm ([src](https://www.bitsandpretzels.com/speaker/peter-specht-119573), via search snippet).\n- He hired Hanel Baveja from USV into the London office (\"When Peter offered me a role\") ([src](https://creandum.com/team/hanel-baveja)) and wrote the 2022 post welcoming Jakob Stein back, so he is also the firm's talent builder.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. BFL is a frontier lab entered at Series A; Rillet, Conduct and Maisa are AI-native automation at seed. He is the Creandum partner to know.\n\n## Background\n- University of St. Gallen and ESADE.\n- Rocket Internet's Lazada in Bangkok (early team).\n- Creandum: promoted to Partner 2021-06; based in London (opened the London office with Baveja's hire in 2023).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://creandum.com/team/peter-specht | |\n| Substack / blog | https://creandum.com/stories | memos under his byline |\n| X | https://twitter.com/specht_p | follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/pspecht/ | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-12 — EUVC E671 — AI Recruiting Agents, a $20M Seed & the New GTM Playbook (with Jack & Jill's Matthew Wilson) — [link](https://podwise.ai/episodes/6456858) — not transcribed.\n- 2023-06 — Riding Unicorns — [link](https://open.spotify.com/episode/2PgWVdyxFQsifpo6vo2Ckh) — not transcribed.\n- Bits & Pretzels and SaaStr Europe speaker (dates not checked).\n\n## How they think\n- AI needs clean data; legacy systems with years of customisation cannot deliver it, so rebuild the system of record (Rillet).\n- Enterprise AI stalls at pilot because of trust; traceable \"agentic process automation\" fixes that (Maisa).\n- Backs founders with one extreme strength over all-rounders.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Backing Rillet](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-rillet) (2024-07)\n- [APA > RPA (Maisa)](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-maisa) (2025-08)\n- [Backing Jack & Jill](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-jack-and-jill) (2025-10)\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI deals at Creandum.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Rillet | Seed $13.5M | 2024-07-29 | Co-lead | First Round Capital | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-rillet) |\n| Black Forest Labs | Series A (unannounced) | 2024-11/2025 | No | a16z and others per ledger | [src](https://creandum.com/team/peter-specht) |\n| Maisa | Seed $25M | 2025-08-28 | not confirmed | Forgepoint, NFX | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-maisa) |\n| Conduct | Seed EUR11.2M | 2025-09-18 | Yes | n/a | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-conduct) |\n| Jack & Jill | Seed $20M | 2025-10-16 | not confirmed | n/a | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-jack-and-jill) |\n\nEarlier non-AI: Factorial, CarOnSale (conference bio).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Rillet:** co-led the combined pre-seed and seed with First Round in July 2024; Creandum followed in the A (2025-05) and the $100M C at $1B (2026-08).\n- **Black Forest Labs:** entry was never announced by either side; it showed up as an existing investor when BFL disclosed its earlier Series A. How he met Rombach's team is not public.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Creandum | Partner | 2021-06 (partner) | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/creandum-promotes-peter-specht-to-partner) |\n| BFL founders (Robin, Andreas, Patrick) | Investor | 2024/25 | [src](https://creandum.com/team/peter-specht) |\n| Hanel Baveja | Hired her into London | 2023-01 | [src](https://creandum.com/team/hanel-baveja) |\n| First Round Capital | Seed co-lead on Rillet | 2024-07 | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-rillet) |\n| Matthew Wilson (Jack & Jill, ex-Omnipresent) | Investor; joint podcast | 2025 | [src](https://creandum.com/stories/backing-jack-and-jill) |\n\n## Open questions\n- How and when Creandum got into BFL, and the cheque size.\n- His Creandum start date and pre-partner titles.\n- Whether he sits on the Rillet and Conduct boards.\n\n## Sources\n- https://creandum.com/team/peter-specht\n- https://creandum.com/stories/backing-rillet , /backing-maisa , /backing-jack-and-jill , /backing-conduct , /creandum-promotes-peter-specht-to-partner\n- https://www.bitsandpretzels.com/speaker/peter-specht-119573\n", "person:peter-xiaotong-zhou": "\n# Peter (Xiaotong) Zhou\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the controlling owner of a $346M adviser that writes $10-20M first checks into compute and bio, and he has almost no public voice, so filings are the only way to read him ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf); pronoun per [Opalesque, 2020-11-09](https://www.opalesque.com/682439/Opalesque_Roundup_Hedge_fund_industry_witnesses_two_remarkable243.html)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he controls the platform. ADV Schedule A/B lists him on the adviser from 07/2020, as CEO and a 75%+ owner of the management LP, and as an equal 25-50% member of the GP LLC alongside Huber and Hochberg, who joined in 05/2022 ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf)). He signs every fund Form D and every 13F ([EDGAR full-text](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Peter%20Zhou%22%20Triatomic)). A decade of Coatue public and private books (2010-2020, [site](https://triatomic.ai)) gives him a crossover investor's comfort with leading capital-heavy hardware rounds at $10-20M.\n- **What constrains him:** the core fund is about $297M gross ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf)), so his lead deals are mid-size A/B rounds, not the first check. In iPronics, Amadeus led the 2022 seed before him; in Elegen, a16z, 8VC and others were already on the cap table.\n- **Public voice:** none found. Huber does the podcasts and is the named lead on Coworker and Zapata. Zhou is quoted only in two portfolio press releases (Elegen, iPronics). You can't read him from content; you have to meet him.\n- **What he'd never do (inference from portfolio):** sub-$5M app-layer AI seeds. Every deal he is personally named on is hardware or biology infrastructure.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The hedge fund was public-markets-first, and the compute thesis showed up in the 13F before the venture cluster.** At 2023-06-30 the adviser's 13F, signed by \"Xiaotong (Peter) Zhou\", held $97.4M including NVIDIA, AMD (shares and calls), TSMC, ACM Research and Aehr Test Systems next to a large China-internet book (Alibaba, KE Holdings, PDD, Kanzhun, Gaotu) ([13F Q2 2023](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852813/000117266123003043/infotable.xml)). The book fell to $78.5M at 2023-12-31 and $3.4M at 2024-06-30 ([Q4 2023](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852813/000117266124000845/infotable.xml), [Q2 2024](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852813/000117266124003349/infotable.xml)). The 13F identifies the signer as \"Xiaotong (Peter) Zhou\" with title \"General Partner\".\n- **Hongkou was a private, pre-IPO investor in an AI drug-discovery company in 2021.** Hongkou Capital Master Fund LP signed Exscientia's shareholders' agreement dated 2021-08-10, with Zhou signing as \"acting by its sole member\" and COO John Larre as witness, alongside Farallon and Laurion ([F-1 ex.10.1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1865408/000110465921114491/tm2119783d6_ex10-1.htm)). It also signed the 2021-10-04 IPO registration rights agreement ([SC 13D ex.99.3](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1865408/000119312521300074/d153466dex993.htm)). The repo previously knew Exscientia only as a 13F public holding.\n- **He launched with a reported $300M in commitments.** Opalesque reported in 2020-11 that he \"has been launching his own new fund\" ([Opalesque, 2020-11-09](https://www.opalesque.com/682439/Opalesque_Roundup_Hedge_fund_industry_witnesses_two_remarkable243.html)). The first 13F (Q4 2021) showed about $163M (see firms/triatomic.md), so either commitments were not all drawn or AUM fell (unresolved).\n- **Coworker (AI agent) names him on its Form D, though press credits Huber.** Village Platforms, Inc., the legal entity behind Coworker.ai ([coworker.ai footer](https://coworker.ai)), filed a Form D on 2025-05-20 listing \"Xiaotong Zhou\" as a related person with the Executive Officer and Director boxes checked: $16,372,053 sold to 30 investors, first sale 2025-03-31 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2067496/000206749625000001/primary_doc.xml)). Press says the $13M seed was led by Jeff Huber of Triatomic ([pulse2, 2025-05-21](https://pulse2.com/coworker-ai-13-million-raised-for-ai-agent-research-company/amp/)). Which partner holds the board seat is unresolved; the filing points to Zhou.\n- **iPronics thesis in his words:** \"Network fabrics operating entirely at the speed of light are essential\" ([TechNews180, 2025-01-29](https://technews180.com/funding-news/ipronics-raises-e20m-in-series-a-funding-investors-include-triatomic-capital/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, with a caveat.** He controls a firm that writes the owner's check size (\"$10-20M initial checks\", [site](https://triatomic.ai)) and personally led iPronics, a photonic networking company for AI clusters, about 20 months before NVIDIA joined its $125M Series B (secondary source). The firm's compute cluster (MatX, d-Matrix, Gimlet, Efficient, Volantis) is relevant, but no partner is named on those deals. His personally attributed deals skew to biology (Elegen) and he is not first money in compute. Best use: a co-lead or syndicate partner on $50-150M hardware and networking rounds, and a crossover-minded investor who can hold through to public markets (Exscientia, Zapata).\n\n## Background\n- Harvard, Economics, 2007 ([site](https://triatomic.ai)).\n- Analyst, Corporate Private Equity, Blackstone, US and Asia transactions ([site](https://triatomic.ai)). Dates not found.\n- Coatue Management, 2010-2020, Senior Managing Director over public and private tech, healthcare and consumer investments ([site](https://triatomic.ai)).\n- Hongkou Capital: on ADV Schedule A from 07/2020 ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf)); launch reported 2020-11 ([Opalesque](https://www.opalesque.com/682439/Opalesque_Roundup_Hedge_fund_industry_witnesses_two_remarkable243.html)); first Form D 2021-03-31 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852813/000090571821000481/primary_doc.xml)).\n- Renamed Triatomic Management LP 2022-02-14 ([EDGAR](https://data.sec.gov/submissions/CIK0001852813.json)); Huber and Hochberg joined the GP LLC 05/2022 (ADV).\n- Board of Trustees, Cancer Research Institute ([site](https://triatomic.ai)); aggregator says since 2015-11 and also a Seventh Regiment Armory Conservancy director since 2018-11 ([MarketScreener](https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/PETER-ZHOU-A12JPI/), aggregator).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai) | Team bio; loads (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | search 2026-11-10 found no account or posts |\n| LinkedIn | [linkedin.com/in/peterzhou](https://www.linkedin.com/in/peterzhou/) | linked from triatomic.ai; not fetched (LinkedIn blocks) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| SEC filings | [EDGAR full-text \"Xiaotong Zhou\"](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Xiaotong%20Zhou%22) | the best primary source on him |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found (searched 2026-11-10). Jeff Huber is the firm's public voice ([Insightful Investor #76](https://getpodcast.com/podcast/insightful-investor/76-jeff-huber-innovation-google-grail-triatomic_87a80abc40)).\n\n## How they think\n- **Photonic interconnect as the next bottleneck in the AI compute stack:** \"advance photonics adoption in data centres and AI clusters\" ([TechNews180](https://technews180.com/funding-news/ipronics-raises-e20m-in-series-a-funding-investors-include-triatomic-capital/)). The firm later joined Volantis (photonic memory interconnect), consistent with this view (firms/triatomic.md).\n- **Bio infrastructure picks-and-shovels:** Elegen is \"innovative and differentiated from other DNA synthesis providers\" ([BioSpace](https://www.biospace.com/elegen-raises-35-million-in-series-b-to-accelerate-expansion-into-clinical-manufacturing)).\n- **Crossover reflexes:** long/short with index puts and options in the hedge-fund era (13Fs), pre-IPO private rounds (Exscientia), and a public-company financing (Zapata, led by Huber).\n- Calls right or wrong: not assessable from public sources.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Form ADV Part 1 (2026-03-31)](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf): ownership, vehicles and AUM.\n- [13F, Q2 2023](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852813/000117266123003043/infotable.xml): the public book at its last scale, with the compute names.\n- [iPronics Series A release](https://technews180.com/funding-news/ipronics-raises-e20m-in-series-a-funding-investors-include-triatomic-capital/): his only compute-thesis quote.\n- [Elegen Series B release](https://www.biospace.com/elegen-raises-35-million-in-series-b-to-accelerate-expansion-into-clinical-manufacturing): his board seat and bio thesis.\n- No writing of his own was found.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X account or LinkedIn posts surfaced in search (2026-11-10).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Exscientia (Hongkou Master Fund) | Pre-IPO preferred; shareholders' agreement party | 2021-08-10 | No | Farallon, Laurion and other signatories | [F-1 ex.10.1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1865408/000110465921114491/tm2119783d6_ex10-1.htm) |\n| Elegen | Series B $35M | 2024-05 | Yes (joins board) | GSK, John Ballantyne, a16z Bio+Health, KdT, 8VC, Digitalis, ACVC, Alix, AME Cloud, Agilent | [BioSpace](https://www.biospace.com/elegen-raises-35-million-in-series-b-to-accelerate-expansion-into-clinical-manufacturing) |\n| iPronics | Series A EUR 20M | 2025-01-29 | Yes | Fine Structure (Fidelity), Bosch Ventures, Amadeus, Criteria Venture Tech | [TechNews180](https://technews180.com/funding-news/ipronics-raises-e20m-in-series-a-funding-investors-include-triatomic-capital/) |\n| Coworker (Village Platforms) | Seed $13M ($16.4M Form D) | 2025-03-31 first sale; announced 2025-05-21 | Triatomic led (press names Huber); Zhou on Form D as director | Abstract, Operator Collective, Eniac, K2 Access Fund | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2067496/000206749625000001/primary_doc.xml); [pulse2](https://pulse2.com/coworker-ai-13-million-raised-for-ai-agent-research-company/amp/) |\n| iPronics | Series B $125M (follow-on) | 2026-09-03 | No (Maverick Silicon, Light Street) | NVIDIA, Bosch, Catalight, EIC Fund, Fine Structure, Amadeus | [raising.fi](https://raising.fi/news/ipronics-series-b-september-2026) (secondary; ipronics.com/news 404) |\n| Firm investments, partner not named | MatX B, d-Matrix C (co-lead), Gimlet A/B, Efficient B, Volantis A, Axiom A, Chalk A | 2025-2026 | see firm file | see firm file | firms/triatomic.md |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **iPronics (timing):** Amadeus Capital led the EUR 3.7M seed in 2022-07 ([datacentre.solutions](https://datacentre.solutions/news/63921/ipronics-raises-37-million-to-accelerate-the-adoption-of-programmable-photonic-chips)). Zhou's Triatomic led the EUR 20M Series A about 30 months later (2025-01). NVIDIA came in at the $125M Series B in 2026-09, about 20 months after Zhou (secondary). The intro path was not found.\n- **Elegen:** he led the B and took the board seat; the round added GSK and Aldevron co-founder John Ballantyne as strategics ([BioSpace](https://www.biospace.com/elegen-raises-35-million-in-series-b-to-accelerate-expansion-into-clinical-manufacturing)). Not first: a16z, 8VC and KdT were existing investors.\n- **Coworker:** pre-seed backers included Soma Capital ([pulse2](https://pulse2.com/coworker-ai-13-million-raised-for-ai-agent-research-company/amp/)), where Triatomic principal Willem van den Bosch previously worked (firms/triatomic.md). A Soma-to-Triatomic sourcing path is plausible but unconfirmed (inference).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Triatomic Management LP | CEO; 75%+ owner of management LP | 07/2020 | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf) |\n| Jeff Huber | Co-GP; equal GP LLC member | 05/2022 | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf) |\n| Steve Hochberg | Co-GP; equal GP LLC member | 05/2022 | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf) |\n| John Larre | COO at Hongkou; witnessed his Exscientia signature | 2021 | [F-1 ex.10.1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1865408/000110465921114491/tm2119783d6_ex10-1.htm) |\n| Coatue Management | Senior Managing Director | 2010-2020 | [site](https://triatomic.ai) |\n| Blackstone | Analyst, Corporate PE | dates not found | [site](https://triatomic.ai) |\n| Elegen | Board member | 2024-05 | [BioSpace](https://www.biospace.com/elegen-raises-35-million-in-series-b-to-accelerate-expansion-into-clinical-manufacturing) |\n| Coworker (Village Platforms) | Listed as director on Form D | 2025 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2067496/000206749625000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Cancer Research Institute | Board of Trustees | 2015-11 (aggregator) | [site](https://triatomic.ai) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who holds the Coworker board seat: Zhou (Form D) or Huber (press)?\n- Did Hongkou's reported $300M in commitments (2020) shrink to $163M by end-2021 through losses or undrawn commitments?\n- Which partner sourced MatX, d-Matrix, Gimlet, Efficient and Volantis? Does Zhou sit on the iPronics board?\n- \"Xiaotong Zhou\" appears as an individual holder in Roivant's 2021 registration rights agreement ([10-K ex.10.1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1635088/000119312522183362/d307489dex101.htm)). It is unconfirmed that this is the same person (recorded as a needs-id lead).\n- Is a Fund II being raised? None filed as of 2026-11-10 (firms/triatomic.md).\n\n## Sources\n- https://triatomic.ai (bio; fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/312408/PDF/312408.pdf\n- https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Xiaotong%20Zhou%22 and ?q=%22Peter%20Zhou%22%20Triatomic\n- 13Fs: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852813/000117266123003043/infotable.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852813/000117266124000845/infotable.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852813/000117266124003349/infotable.xml\n- Exscientia: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1865408/000110465921114491/tm2119783d6_ex10-1.htm ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1865408/000119312521300074/d153466dex993.htm\n- Coworker: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2067496/000206749625000001/primary_doc.xml ; https://coworker.ai ; https://pulse2.com/coworker-ai-13-million-raised-for-ai-agent-research-company/amp/\n- https://www.biospace.com/elegen-raises-35-million-in-series-b-to-accelerate-expansion-into-clinical-manufacturing\n- https://technews180.com/funding-news/ipronics-raises-e20m-in-series-a-funding-investors-include-triatomic-capital/\n- https://datacentre.solutions/news/63921/ipronics-raises-37-million-to-accelerate-the-adoption-of-programmable-photonic-chips\n- https://raising.fi/news/ipronics-series-b-september-2026 (secondary)\n- https://www.opalesque.com/682439/Opalesque_Roundup_Hedge_fund_industry_witnesses_two_remarkable243.html\n- https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/PETER-ZHOU-A12JPI/ (aggregator)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1635088/000119312522183362/d307489dex101.htm (Roivant; identity unconfirmed)\n\n**Not confirmed:** iPronics Series B (secondary only); Coworker board seat holder; Roivant identity; Blackstone dates; any podcast, X or writing by Zhou.\n", "person:phil-inagaki": "\n# Phil Inagaki\n\n## One-line take\nPhil Inagaki is a physicist and former deep-tech founder who decides how Temasek's early-stage AI and deep-tech money is spent through Xora. He leads $50M+ seeds and co-leads $200M Series As in AI networking and simulation, and he still runs Xora-built companies himself at formation.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he is an operator who invests. He spent about 15 years as a deep-tech founder and CEO, starting with Polyera in 2005 ([src](https://xora.vc/team/phil-inagaki)). At Xora he incubates companies himself and serves as interim CEO before handing over, as he did at Amperesand ([src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2661926)). Temasek as anchor LP lets him write big early checks: the $53M Hang Ten seed and a co-lead of Upscale AI's $200M A.\n- **What constrains him:** Singapore. He says openly that he can't scale hiring there quickly, so his companies move to the US for talent ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/21/amperesand-targets-data-centers-as-the-next-big-customer-for-its-solid-state-transformers)). Xora's 2026 hire of a Silicon Valley GP, Eric Rosenblum, follows from that.\n- **What he'd never do:** back cheap, low-margin hardware, judging by what he says. He wants high-ASP infrastructure units, a path to the first $100M of revenue, and $1B run-rate headroom ([src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2661926)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- His revenue screen for an incubated hardware company: \"a type of company which could quickly [reach] the first $100 million in revenue\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2661926), 2024-02-07).\n- He bets on the semiconductor cost curve beating legacy hardware: \"Nothing advances in performance and cost like semiconductor tech.\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2661926))\n- He admits the customer told him something he hadn't expected about footprint: \"I've been surprised, they bring it up more than I thought\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2661926)).\n- He names Singapore's talent ceiling himself: \"We have some in Singapore, but we won't be able to scale that quickly.\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/21/amperesand-targets-data-centers-as-the-next-big-customer-for-its-solid-state-transformers), 2025-01-21)\n- Amperesand's Series A was already under way in January 2025: \"We've just kicked off a Series A, and it's moving really quickly\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/21/amperesand-targets-data-centers-as-the-next-big-customer-for-its-solid-state-transformers)).\n- His partner-hiring rule is builder first, then investor. He said Rosenblum \"proved that he can hunt and win as an investor\" ([src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push), 2026-04-08).\n- His Vinci thesis is simulation \"without requiring customer data\", with co-design later expanding the EDA market ([src](https://pulse2.com/vinci-46-million-funding/), 2025-12-06).\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-11)\nHis public voice is thin and comes almost entirely through reporters. We found no podcast, YouTube talk, Substack or X account. The material is two TechCrunch interviews about Amperesand, the 2015 Polyera Wove launch cycle, and canned quotes in funding releases.\n- **Decision rule:** a quick path to the first $100M of revenue, with room for a $1B run rate that doesn't dent the market ([src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2661926)).\n- **Thesis engine:** semiconductor cost curves replace iron-and-oil incumbents that \"are very hard to scale in manufacturing\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2661926)).\n- **Constraint:** Singapore can't supply hardware talent at scale, so the US is \"where there's amazing talent that we can capture\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/21/amperesand-targets-data-centers-as-the-next-big-customer-for-its-solid-state-transformers)).\n- **Founder era:** at Polyera he called today's devices \"rigid bricks\" ([src](https://csmonitor.com/Technology/2015/0817/New-startup-promises-world-s-first-truly-flexible-touch-screen-display), citing CNN Money). He pitched Wove as a new on-body display platform ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2015/10/16/meet-wove-an-e-ink-touchscreen-computer-that-wraps-around-your-arm/)). As an investor he has moved from consumer devices to high-ASP infrastructure.\n- **AI infra view:** \"AI workloads are outpacing the capabilities of traditional networks\" (Upscale AI) ([src](https://intelcapital.com/from-100m-seed-to-unicorn-in-months-upscale-ai-closes-oversubscribed-200m-series-a-to-build-the-first-pure-play-ai-networking-company/)). He argues enterprise AI is held back by deployment, not models (Hang Ten, reported speech) ([src](https://www.dealstreetasia.com/stories/temasek-xora-funding-hang-ten-495406/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Xora is Temasek's early-stage AI and deep-tech vehicle and writes large early checks into AI infrastructure:\n- led Vinci's $36M Series A, then co-led its $250M B ([src](https://pulse2.com/vinci-46-million-funding/))\n- joined Upscale AI's $100M+ seed and co-led its $200M A ([src](https://intelcapital.com/from-100m-seed-to-unicorn-in-months-upscale-ai-closes-oversubscribed-200m-series-a-to-build-the-first-pure-play-ai-networking-company/))\n- led Hang Ten's $53M seed ([src](https://www.dealstreetasia.com/stories/temasek-xora-funding-hang-ten-495406/))\n\nHe is a natural co-investor in large AI-chip and infra seeds. He is not an LP for outside managers: Xora is Temasek's own captive vehicle.\n\n## Background\n- Princeton, A.B. in Physics, magna cum laude; 12 US patents ([src](https://xora.vc/team/phil-inagaki)).\n- 2005: co-founded Polyera, a flexible-electronics spinout from Northwestern, and was its CEO. Polyera launched the Wove band in 2015 ([src](https://csmonitor.com/Technology/2015/0817/New-startup-promises-world-s-first-truly-flexible-touch-screen-display); founding year per [MarketScreener](https://ca.marketscreener.com/insider/PHILIPPE-INAGAKI-A1GJMQ/), aggregator).\n- Flexterra (Polyera successor): chairman/president per MarketScreener (aggregator, unconfirmed).\n- Voxel8 CEO 2016-17 per MarketScreener only. This conflicts with 3dprint.com, which names other CEOs, so treat it as unconfirmed.\n- Operating Partner at The Engine, MIT's tough-tech fund, before Xora ([src](https://events.eco-business.com/speakers/phil-inagaki)).\n- Managing Director at Xora by January 2022 ([src](https://events.eco-business.com/speakers/phil-inagaki)). Managing Partner & CIO since 2024 ([src](https://xora.vc/team/phil-inagaki); start date per MarketScreener, aggregator).\n- 2023: co-founded Amperesand with Xora and was its interim CEO at the 2024 seed. He is now board chair there, and also board chair at Aether Fuels ([src](https://xora.vc/team/phil-inagaki)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://xora.vc/team/phil-inagaki | Firm bio (2026-11-11) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No X account listed on the Xora page |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/philinagaki/ | Reposts Xora deals (not read; login wall) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2015-08 — CNN Money, via the Christian Science Monitor — Polyera Wove launch — [link](https://csmonitor.com/Technology/2015/0817/New-startup-promises-world-s-first-truly-flexible-touch-screen-display) — called today's devices \"rigid bricks\"\n- 2015-10 — TechCrunch interview — Wove — [link](https://techcrunch.com/2015/10/16/meet-wove-an-e-ink-touchscreen-computer-that-wraps-around-your-arm/) — pitched a new on-body display platform\n- 2022-01 — Eco-Business / Temasek Foundation — launch of The Liveability Challenge 2022 (speaker) — [link](https://events.eco-business.com/speakers/phil-inagaki) — no recording found\n- 2024-02 — TechCrunch interview — Amperesand seed — [link](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2661926) — his $100M-revenue and $1B-headroom rule\n- 2025-01 — TechCrunch interview — Amperesand and data centers — [link](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/21/amperesand-targets-data-centers-as-the-next-big-customer-for-its-solid-state-transformers) — the US-talent reasoning\n- No podcasts or YouTube talks found.\n\n## How they think\n- Main theses: semiconductor substitution for legacy infrastructure (SiC transformers, photonics, AI networking), and AI held back by the physical bottlenecks of power and networks.\n- Contrarian view: he pushes hardware companies built in Singapore to move to the US quickly instead of scaling them locally.\n- Calls: Celestial AI was sold to Marvell. Upscale AI went from seed to a valuation above $1B within months. Vinci was marked up about tenfold from A to B. Polyera's Wove did not reach mass market.\n\n## Read these first\n- [TechCrunch, 2024-02-07](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2661926): his clearest statement of how he sizes a company.\n- [TechCrunch, 2025-01-21](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/21/amperesand-targets-data-centers-as-the-next-big-customer-for-its-solid-state-transformers): how he incubates a company, hands it over, and moves it to the US.\n- [Xora Rosenblum release, 2026-04-08](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push): what he looks for in a partner.\n\n## Social engagement\nNo public X presence found. On LinkedIn he mostly reposts Xora funding news (seen in search snippets only, not read).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Amperesand | Seed ($12.5M) | 2024-02 | co-lead (Xora) | Material Impact (co-lead); TDK Ventures; Foothill Ventures | [src](https://compoundsemiconductor.net/article/118628/Amperesand_raises_1245M_to_transform_power_grids) |\n| Vinci | Series A ($36M) | 2025-12 | lead (Xora) | Eclipse (seed lead) | [src](https://pulse2.com/vinci-46-million-funding/) |\n| Upscale AI | Series A ($200M) | 2026-01-21 | co-lead (Xora) | Tiger Global; Premji Invest; Mayfield; Intel Capital | [src](https://intelcapital.com/from-100m-seed-to-unicorn-in-months-upscale-ai-closes-oversubscribed-200m-series-a-to-build-the-first-pure-play-ai-networking-company/) |\n| Hang Ten Systems | Seed ($53M) | 2026-09-17 | lead (Xora) | Mayfield; Aramco Ventures | [src](https://www.dealstreetasia.com/stories/temasek-xora-funding-hang-ten-495406/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Amperesand:** Xora took silicon-carbide transformer technology from NTU and built the company in-house. Inagaki was interim CEO through the $12.5M seed, then handed over to Gary Lawrence (CEO), with Brian Dow and Tommy Joyner, both ex-Tesla/Generac, as CPO and CTO. The company opened a US office to hire ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/21/amperesand-targets-data-centers-as-the-next-big-customer-for-its-solid-state-transformers)). Its first market was EV fast charging; it then moved to AI data centers.\n- **Vinci:** Eclipse led the seed and Xora led the A. Ten months later Xora and Temasek co-led the $250M B ([src](https://pulse2.com/vinci-46-million-funding/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Temasek | Anchor LP and parent of Xora | | [src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push) |\n| Eric Rosenblum | Xora GP; Inagaki hired him for the US push | 2026-04 | [src](https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push) |\n| The Engine | Former Operating Partner | | [src](https://events.eco-business.com/speakers/phil-inagaki) |\n| Gary Lawrence | Successor as Amperesand CEO | 2024-25 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/21/amperesand-targets-data-centers-as-the-next-big-customer-for-its-solid-state-transformers) |\n| Vishal Sikka | Founder of Hang Ten, which Xora backed with a $53M seed | 2026-09 | [src](https://www.dealstreetasia.com/stories/temasek-xora-funding-hang-ten-495406/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Dates at The Engine, and whether he was at Voxel8 at all (MarketScreener only).\n- What \"Base Capital\" is (TheOrg lists him as co-founder and executive chairman).\n- Xora's fund size and check bands. Who decided the Upscale AI and Hang Ten checks: Inagaki, or a partner such as Artem Smirnov?\n- Whether a recording exists of the Liveability Challenge 2022 launch or any SWITCH talk.\n- The repo has two Xora ids (`xora` and `xora-innovation`). This file uses `xora`, matching the existing rels rows.\n\n## Sources\n- https://xora.vc/team/phil-inagaki\n- https://techcrunch.com/?p=2661926\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/21/amperesand-targets-data-centers-as-the-next-big-customer-for-its-solid-state-transformers\n- https://techcrunch.com/2015/10/16/meet-wove-an-e-ink-touchscreen-computer-that-wraps-around-your-arm/\n- https://csmonitor.com/Technology/2015/0817/New-startup-promises-world-s-first-truly-flexible-touch-screen-display\n- https://events.eco-business.com/speakers/phil-inagaki\n- https://pulse2.com/vinci-46-million-funding/\n- https://intelcapital.com/from-100m-seed-to-unicorn-in-months-upscale-ai-closes-oversubscribed-200m-series-a-to-build-the-first-pure-play-ai-networking-company/\n- https://briefglance.com/articles/xora-taps-veteran-investor-rosenblum-for-us-ai-and-deep-tech-push\n- https://www.dealstreetasia.com/stories/temasek-xora-funding-hang-ten-495406/\n- https://compoundsemiconductor.net/article/118628/Amperesand_raises_1245M_to_transform_power_grids\n- https://ca.marketscreener.com/insider/PHILIPPE-INAGAKI-A1GJMQ/ (aggregator)\n- https://theorg.com/org/xora-innovation/org-chart/phil-inagaki (aggregator)\n", "person:phil-shin": "\n# Phil Shin\n\n## One-line take\nThe working dealmaker who sits on both sides of Databricks' wallet, M&A and venture, so a Databricks Ventures cheque he works on is the earliest public signal that Databricks may one day buy the company.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** he joined in early 2022 as only the second corporate development hire ([src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-phil-shin/?format=md)) and says he now oversees \"our M&A and venture investing efforts\" ([src](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2)). Databricks' invest-then-acquire pattern (Arcion at 2x DV entry; Tecton; Neon) runs through that one desk ([src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-phil-shin/?format=md)).\n- **What constrains him:** he does not price or lead. DV joins alongside \"a lead financial investor who sets the terms\" ([src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/information-technology/databricks-ventures-building-ecosystem/?format=md)), takes no board seats and asks for \"fairly minimal strategic rights\" ([src](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2)). CEO Ali Ghodsi signs off on every investment ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund)), and since April 2026 Philip Kirk is SVP of corp dev and ventures above the team ([src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/kirk-joins-databricks-corporate-development-head/?format=md)).\n- **What he'd never do:** lead a seed on terms, take a board seat, or back something Databricks product and engineering won't validate (\"Our product and engineering teams validate every investment\", [src](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2)).\n- **What he personally does:** sources and runs process. He says he has led 15+ investments since 2022, but only one public deal names him (LlamaIndex, 2025). He does not sign; Ghodsi approves; the lead VC prices.\n\n## Differential insight\n- He was at Salesforce corp dev while Salesforce invested in Databricks, so he knew Databricks from the investor side before he joined: \"during which time the latter invested in Databricks\" ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-phil-shin/?format=md)).\n- Second corp dev hire at Databricks: \"Shin was, in fact, only its second corporate development hire\" ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-phil-shin/?format=md)). He has seen nearly every Databricks deal since 2022, MosaicML and Tabular era included; his named role on them is unconfirmed.\n- Picks deals with Databricks partner data on joint customers: \"partnerships data give us an unfair advantage\" ([BRV 2024-12-17](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2)). DV's edge is seeing which ecosystem startups Databricks customers already use, which a financial VC can't see.\n- Inbound-driven: \"tons of dealflow with minimal sourcing efforts\" ([BRV](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2)). The way in is a Databricks partnership, not a cold pitch.\n- \"Leading 15+ investments\" ([BRV](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2)), yet public credit goes to Andrew Ferguson or the unit. He does the work behind the scenes.\n- Theses he names: \"the rise of compound AI systems and AI agents\" and LLM-driven data tooling ([BRV](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He isn't a co-lead for a $20M frontier seed. DV follows, writes modest cheques and sits on the Databricks platform layer (inference engines, retrieval, agent frameworks, data tooling), not pre-training labs. The exceptions are big inference seeds where DV joined behind a16z/Lightspeed/Accel (Inferact $150M seed 2026-01, RadixArk $100M seed 2026-05, [DV ledger](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference)). Shin isn't named on either. Why he's worth knowing anyway: (1) for any infra or automation company in my book that sells into Databricks customers, he is the person who can turn a DV cheque into a partnership and later an acquisition (exit path, not price-setter); (2) he sees Databricks acquisition interest earlier than anyone outside. Classify as: **strategic co-investor plus acquirer contact**, not a price-setter or LP.\n\n## Background\n- Investment banking at Morgan Stanley (analyst; dates not disclosed) ([src](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2); [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-phil-shin/?format=md)).\n- Strategic planning, Disney Parks & Resorts (dates not disclosed) ([src](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2)).\n- Led strategic finance at an unnamed digital health startup ([src](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2)).\n- MBA, Harvard Business School ([src](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2)). Years 2017-2019 per an aggregator profile (village.ai); unconfirmed.\n- Salesforce M&A team, Director of corporate development, \"nearly four years\" to 2022 ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-phil-shin/?format=md)). Jan 2018 to Jan 2022 per an aggregator; unconfirmed.\n- Databricks, early 2022: Director, corp dev and ventures (title per GCV, June 2024); Senior Director by December 2024 ([BRV](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2)); \"Corporate Development Sr. Director\" on the Databricks blog ([src](https://www.databricks.com/blog/author/phil-shin)). The Director-to-Senior Director step took about 2.5 years from joining. It isn't associate-to-partner, so fast_promotion stays unknown.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.databricks.com/blog/author/phil-shin | Databricks author page; one post (LlamaIndex, 2025-05-01); as of 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/shinphilip | Linked from the Databricks author page; not opened (login wall) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** the route he describes is through Databricks itself. Get into the Databricks partner program and build joint-customer traction, which is what his partnerships data surfaces ([BRV](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2)). Product/engineering validation (e.g. co-founder Patrick Wendell co-signed LlamaIndex) is the other gate. For a co-investor, the lead VC on a round DV joins deals with him or Ferguson directly; DV slots in behind the lead's terms.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-12-17 — BR Venture Fund (Cornell student fund) — written Q&A \"Insights from Phil Shin, Senior Director @ Databricks Ventures\" — https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2 — passive investor/active partner; partner data as edge; 15+ investments.\n- 2024-06-24 — GCV Powerlist 2024 (profile, not a talk) — https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-phil-shin/?format=md\n\n## How they think\n- Strategic fit first: category leaders that align with the Databricks ecosystem and can create \"mutual value\" ([BRV](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2)).\n- Passive investor, active partner: no boards, minimal rights; pays founders back with prioritized partnerships and go-to-market ([BRV](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2)).\n- AI view: compound AI systems, agents, LLMs that simplify work for data engineers and analysts ([BRV](https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2)).\n- Calls right/wrong: Arcion returned 2x in about 18 months via Databricks' own acquisition ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-phil-shin/?format=md)); whether Shin worked on Arcion is unconfirmed.\n\n## Read these first\n1. BRV Q&A (2024-12-17), the only place he speaks at length — https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2\n2. GCV Powerlist 2024 entry: second hire, Salesforce-to-Databricks path, Q1 2024 deals — https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-phil-shin/?format=md\n3. LlamaIndex investment post (his only byline) — https://www.databricks.com/blog/databricks-invests-llamaindex-advance-knowledge-agents-over-enterprise-data\n4. Firm context: [firms/databricks-ventures.md](../firms/databricks-ventures.md)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public X or Substack; he isn't quoted in DV press releases (those quote Ferguson).\n\n## Portfolio\nOnly LlamaIndex names Shin personally. The other rows are unit deals from his tenure, credited to Databricks Ventures, not to him.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| LlamaIndex (Shin named, co-byline) | Undisclosed | 2025-05-01 | no | not disclosed | [src](https://www.databricks.com/blog/databricks-invests-llamaindex-advance-knowledge-agents-over-enterprise-data) |\n| Glean (unit; cited in Shin's Powerlist entry) | Series D ($200M+ at $2.2B) | 2024-02 | no | Kleiner Perkins, Lightspeed (co-leads); Sequoia; Coatue; Citi; Workday Ventures | [src](https://www.glean.com/blog/glean-series-d) |\n| Mistral AI (unit) | Series A participation with partnership | 2024-03-14 | no | a16z (Series A lead) | [src](https://www.solutions-numeriques.com/ia-generative-databricks-investit-dans-la-startup-francaise-mistral-ai) |\n| Perplexity (unit) | Series B ($73.6M at $520M) | 2024-01-04 | no | IVP (lead); NEA; NVIDIA; Jeff Bezos | [src](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-raises-series-b-funding-round) |\n| Arcion (unit; acquired by Databricks 2023) | Undisclosed | ~2022 | no | not disclosed | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-phil-shin/?format=md) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **LlamaIndex (2025-05-01):** the post names co-founder Patrick Wendell, Denny Lee (product/devrel), Andrew Ferguson and Shin. That fits the stated process: product and engineering validate, ventures executes. The stated aim was to deepen the relationship and speed up knowledge agents over enterprise data ([src](https://www.databricks.com/blog/databricks-invests-llamaindex-advance-knowledge-agents-over-enterprise-data)). No amount or round was disclosed.\n- **Q1 2024 burst:** eight deals in one quarter (Glean, Mistral, Perplexity among them), six in startups under three years old ([GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-phil-shin/?format=md)). Shin was Director then; his role on each is unconfirmed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Andrew Ferguson (VP, head of DV) | Works alongside; co-byline on LlamaIndex | 2022 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-phil-shin/?format=md) |\n| Philip Kirk (SVP corp dev and ventures) | Likely reports into him (inferred from titles; unconfirmed) | 2026-04 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/kirk-joins-databricks-corporate-development-head/?format=md) |\n| Ali Ghodsi (CEO) | Approves every DV investment | 2022 | [src](https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund) |\n| Patrick Wendell (co-founder) | Co-signer on LlamaIndex | 2025 | [src](https://www.databricks.com/blog/databricks-invests-llamaindex-advance-knowledge-agents-over-enterprise-data) |\n| Salesforce corp dev | Former employer (~4 years); Salesforce invested in Databricks during his tenure | to 2022 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-phil-shin/?format=md) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which of the 15+ investments he led? Only LlamaIndex is public. The unnamed deals likely include Q1 2024 names and 2025 data/AI tooling.\n- Was he on the Arcion, Tecton, Neon or Tabular acquisitions? The M&A side is where his edge would show.\n- His role after Philip Kirk arrived in April 2026: still Senior Director, or promoted or narrowed?\n- Did he work on the Inferact or RadixArk inference seeds (2026)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-phil-shin/?format=md\n- https://www.brventurefund.com/4896-2\n- https://www.databricks.com/blog/author/phil-shin\n- https://www.databricks.com/blog/databricks-invests-llamaindex-advance-knowledge-agents-over-enterprise-data\n- https://www.glean.com/blog/glean-series-d\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/information-technology/databricks-ventures-building-ecosystem/?format=md\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/kirk-joins-databricks-corporate-development-head/?format=md\n- https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund\n- https://www.solutions-numeriques.com/ia-generative-databricks-investit-dans-la-startup-francaise-mistral-ai\n- https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-raises-series-b-funding-round\n- Aggregator, unconfirmed: https://village.ai/person/phil-shin-NjI5NTU4MzA0 (returned 403 on fetch; dates from search snippet only)\n", "person:philip-clark": "\n# Philip Clark\n\n## One-line take\nA young Thrive partner who sits on the firm's most important AI, security and hard-tech deals (OpenAI, Cursor, Wiz, Physical Intelligence, Nudge, Mesh) and argues that the biggest companies of the next decade may be physical, not software-only [src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-inside-thrive-capital).\n\n## Crux\n*(Added 2026-11-09 from the full YouTube auto-caption transcript of the Sourcery episode, uploaded 2025-11-24 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=niosgDC-QHU).)*\n\nClark wins by courting founders for about a year before any money moves. He \"simulates\" being a partner well before Thrive is on the cap table: he met Cursor on pivot day in late 2022 and invested in May 2024, and he worked on Wiz from 2022 until early 2024 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=niosgDC-QHU). When conviction arrives, Thrive's concentration lets him put a lot of capital behind it. His physicist and chip-Substack background makes him Thrive's \"hardware guy,\" and he says the hardware opportunity set is the largest in 20 years [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=niosgDC-QHU). He is constrained by Thrive's concentration model, which allows only a small number of \"n of 1\" founders at large check sizes, and by working under Kushner (Kushner plus Clark on Wiz). He says he'd never invest on momentum, meaning a deal where one bad quarter would shake his conviction, and that Thrive doesn't define itself against other investors (\"you play against yourself\") [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=niosgDC-QHU).\n\n## Differential insight\nAll quotes are from the YouTube auto-captions of the Sourcery episode [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=niosgDC-QHU), so captions may mis-transcribe. Mirrored in `data/rows/philip-clark-insights.csv`.\n- **Mesh seed was early 2025, about a year before the public round.** \"we led the seed for that company at the beginning of this year\" (05:34). The episode was recorded around late Oct 2025 (host: \"It's apparently almost November\"), which implies an early-2025 seed. Bloomberg's Thrive-led $50M round is dated Feb 2026 [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-02-17/spacex-alumni-raise-50-million-for-data-center-optical-tech). That gap is my inference from the recording date.\n- **Cursor entry metrics.** At Thrive's first investment (May 2024), Cursor had \"a couple tens of thousands of users\" and \"low single digits million ARR\" (06:28).\n- **OpenAI was an uncontested bet.** \"we were one of two uh firms that gave the company a term sheet\" at around $29B, which he calls \"an investment that required belief\" rather than a competitive win (40:39). This corrects the Podchemy framing that it closed before ChatGPT launched: he only says the price was high for a company \"chat hadn't even launched for,\" and that wording is ambiguous.\n- **Diligence by acting like a partner first.** \"trying to simulate acting like partners well ahead of actually being partners\" (13:02).\n- **His favorite enterprise metric.** Deal size relative to sales speed. Wiz had the \"very best ratio of deal size to speed\" he had seen, closing six-figure deals in weeks (15:48).\n- **Talent rule.** Labs win by hiring people who \"could be great versus who are already great.\" Young AI-native researchers \"hold the model weights in their brains,\" and he puts the Sora team's median age in the low 20s (44:21). He cites Alec Radford as the archetype.\n- **Hardware compounding.** Scaled hardware companies accelerate because \"the bigger you get the bigger markets you can access\" (25:45).\n- **The Wiz trip was about signaling, not competition.** \"you play against yourself. You don't play against others.\" (14:38)\n- **Anti-momentum rule.** Avoid deals where \"if the numbers changed one quarter or one year\" your conviction would change (54:53).\n- **A lab Thrive is not in.** He names \"periodic,\" a new materials-science AI company, saying \"we are not an investor\" (56:56). I map this to Periodic Labs (inference).\n- **His earliest AI diligence screen.** \"How much research DNA should your team have?\" [src](https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/ais-eigenquestions) (2022-12-18).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** At Thrive he has led or worked on exactly the seed and early bets I care about:\n- Physical Intelligence: Thrive led the $70M seed [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=676543).\n- Mesh Optical: AI data-center interconnect; Thrive led the seed in early 2025 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=niosgDC-QHU).\n- OpenAI: Thrive was one of two term sheets in the ~$29B round [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=niosgDC-QHU).\n- Cursor: invested pre-scale [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=niosgDC-QHU).\n\nHis screens line up closely with my frontier-talent lens: \"research DNA\" and \"could be great\" researchers. Thrive writes very large concentrated checks, so in a $600M new-lab seed he is a likely co-investor or lead, and a good source of deal flow and founder references. He is not an LP prospect. Caveat: no personal check size or ownership targets are sourced.\n\n## Background\n- Age 28 as of Sept 2025, per Fortune [src](https://fortune.com/2025/09/03/thrive-capitals-philip-clark-is-promoted-to-partner).\n- As a child he wanted to be a physicist and CEO of Lockheed Martin [src](https://fortune.com/2025/09/03/thrive-capitals-philip-clark-is-promoted-to-partner).\n- Stanford: degrees in Computer Science and Management Science & Engineering [src](https://techcrunch.com/author/philip-clark). He started in physics, decided the route to impact was too long, and switched to CS [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088).\n- Department of Defense work, and research for Condoleezza Rice and H.R. McMaster (no dates given) [src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-inside-thrive-capital).\n- Bridgewater Associates: \"led systematic geopolitical analysis\" per Sourcery [src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-inside-thrive-capital); TechCrunch says he \"previously invested at Bridgewater\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/08/15/meet-the-first-batch-of-vcs-set-to-judge-startup-battlefield-200-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2025). Podchemy's summary describes the move as from \"quantitative trading\" into venture [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088). Dates not found.\n- 8VC: Principal per TheOrg (no dates) [src](https://theorg.com/org/thrive-capital/person/philip-clark); Fortune confirms he came to Thrive \"from 8VC in 2022\" [src](https://fortune.com/2025/09/03/thrive-capitals-philip-clark-is-promoted-to-partner).\n- Thrive Capital: joined May 2022 [src](https://theorg.com/org/thrive-capital/person/philip-clark); promoted to Partner, reported Sept 3, 2025 [src](https://fortune.com/2025/09/03/thrive-capitals-philip-clark-is-promoted-to-partner). Colossus (Oct 2025) calls him \"Thrive's most recent partner\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/).\n- Advises the Hoover Institution on emerging technologies [src](https://techcrunch.com/author/philip-clark). No Hoover page for him was found.\n- **How he got to Thrive:** He wrote a semiconductor Substack in 2021–22 about reshoring advanced chip manufacturing, and it caught Josh Kushner's attention [src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-inside-thrive-capital). Kushner was looking at reshoring chip manufacturing himself. A former colleague introduced them. The project was too capital-intensive for Thrive, but when Clark later moved toward venture he asked Kushner for advice, and that turned into a job offer [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088). **I could not find the chip Substack itself** (see Open questions).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | [Futurist fieldnotes](https://philipjclark.substack.com/) | 5 posts, Dec 2022 to Mar 2024. Publication created 2022-12-18, after he joined Thrive, so it is not the 2021–22 chip Substack. Inactive since Mar 2024 (checked 2026-11-09) [src](https://philipjclark.substack.com/archive) |\n| X | [@PJClark](https://x.com/PJClark) | Handle confirmed by Molly O'Shea's post [src](https://x.com/MollySOShea/status/1993827686695793041). Follower count not retrieved (X returned 402 on 2026-11-09) |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| Other | [TechCrunch author page](https://techcrunch.com/author/philip-clark) | Bio only, no articles listed [src](https://techcrunch.com/author/philip-clark) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-11 — Sourcery (Molly O'Shea) — \"BREAKING: Inside Thrive Capital\" — [link](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-inside-thrive-capital) — Covers the chip-Substack origin story, the Cursor and Wiz deal stories, \"hardware + AI,\" concentration, and OpenAI's talent density. Episode notes with timestamps: [Podchemy](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088).\n- 2025-11-24 — Sourcery on YouTube — \"Inside Thrive Capital: Investing in OpenAI, Wiz, Cursor, Nudge, Physical Intelligence\" — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=niosgDC-QHU) — Full auto-caption transcript read 2026-11-09. Recorded around late Oct 2025. Also covers Isomorphic Labs (Thrive says it is an investor), Periodic (Thrive is not), Michael Dell's \"speed chess\" line, and Miles Grimshaw's \"paranoid but patient.\"\n- 2026-02 — Sourcery — \"BREAKING: Inside Thrive Capital (Again)\" — [link](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-inside-thrive-capital-again) — The write-up largely overlaps the Nov 2025 episode (same deals and theses, updated valuations), so it may be a re-release.\n- 2025 — TechCrunch Disrupt 2025 — Startup Battlefield 200 judge, listed as \"Investor, Thrive Capital\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/08/15/meet-the-first-batch-of-vcs-set-to-judge-startup-battlefield-200-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2025).\n\n## How they think\n- **Counterfactual companies.** Thrive's bar is that the world would look materially different if the company didn't exist, and that the founder is counterfactually important, meaning nobody else would get the same result with the same idea [src](https://fortune.com/2025/09/03/thrive-capitals-philip-clark-is-promoted-to-partner).\n- **The \"alien test.\"** If you described to aliens how the world works, the company should appear on that list [src](https://fortune.com/2025/09/03/thrive-capitals-philip-clark-is-promoted-to-partner).\n- **Hardware + AI.** Sensor costs are collapsing (he cites LiDAR going from tens of thousands of dollars per unit to a few hundred), and with AI and a new generation of engineers, hardware can scale faster and be more defensible than pure software. Hardware is harder to build but very durable once it scales (SpaceX, Anduril, Tesla) [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088). He thinks the biggest companies of the next decade may be physical [src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-inside-thrive-capital).\n- **AI is broader than knowledge work.** He says people are \"way too narrow\" about AI's impact, and he does not think there is an AI bubble. He uses a dot-com analogy: weaker companies get funded, but so does the next Amazon [src](https://fortune.com/2025/09/03/thrive-capitals-philip-clark-is-promoted-to-partner).\n- **Concentration over \"spray and pray.\"** He cites Carta data on roughly 20-company portfolios [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088). Venture has \"the worst beta and the best alpha,\" and he expects hardware to look the same [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088).\n- **Wanting it most.** He repeats Kushner's line that \"the people who win deals are the ones who want to win them most\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/).\n- **Judge the swing, not the ball (Dalio).** He evaluates decision quality rather than short-term outcomes [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088). This fits the Bridgewater background.\n- **Founders over ideas.** Ideas change and founders are the constant. Cursor is his proof case: he met them on the day they abandoned their first idea [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088).\n- **OpenAI's moat is talent density plus paradigm shifts** (pre-training, then SFT/RLHF, then RL) [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088).\n- **AI augments rather than substitutes.** He has not seen a portfolio company lay off engineers because of AI tools [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088).\n- **Writing-era theses (2022–24, Futurist fieldnotes):**\n  - AI rewards \"maximalist, consumer-like, and weird\" startups and punishes incrementalism. Copilots are transitional and autopilots win. Software will have to serve machine identities as well as humans [src](https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/the-end-of-incrementalism-how-ai).\n  - LLMs will force a sixth B2B business model, moving pricing from seats/inputs to outcomes (SLA pricing, output pricing, \"agent renting\"), and buyers will track \"return on software spend\" [src](https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/beyond-saas).\n  - Open source is a business tactic, not a strategy. Open-source infra companies didn't grow faster than closed peers, and the developer-led GTM playbook transfers poorly to LLMs [src](https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/can-open-source-be-too-open).\n- **Calls that aged well:**\n  - In Nov 2023 he argued for outcome-based and \"agent renting\" pricing [src](https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/beyond-saas). The \"post-SaaS\" theme returns in the 2025 podcast [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088).\n  - In Aug 2023 he predicted \"autopilots\" over copilots [src](https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/the-end-of-incrementalism-how-ai). Cursor, which he tracked from its pivot, is a case in point.\n  - No clearly wrong calls found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [The end of incrementalism: how AI will reward maximalist start-ups](https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/the-end-of-incrementalism-how-ai) (2023-08-30): the clearest statement of his taste. AI looks like the early consumer internet, not the enterprise-efficiency era, so back weird, ambitious companies.\n2. [Beyond SaaS](https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/beyond-saas) (2023-11-12): five past software business models and why LLMs bring a sixth, priced on outcomes.\n3. [AI's eigenquestions](https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/ais-eigenquestions) (2022-12-18): seven diligence questions for startups built on foundation models (UX leap, system of record, moat, data flywheel, research DNA, build vs. buy, whether to use an FM at all). Effectively his early AI diligence checklist.\n4. [Can open source be too open?](https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/can-open-source-be-too-open) (2023-09-17): data-driven skepticism of open source as a growth strategy, applied to open LLM companies.\n5. Sourcery episode notes [Podchemy](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088): the best single source for his current hardware thesis and deal stories.\n- Also: [The Next Blackstone](https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/the-next-blackstone) (2024-03-31). Only an editor's note was visible; he says he found a new dataset to validate his hypotheses and would update the piece. The body was not readable when checked.\n\n## Social engagement\n- Little sourced. The main signal is Molly O'Shea (Sourcery) amplifying him on X, summarizing \"3 big takeaways\" on AI drawn from OpenAI, Cursor and Physical Intelligence and his semiconductor background [src](https://x.com/MollySOShea/status/1993827686695793041).\n- Posting cadence, topics, and who he replies to were not retrieved (X blocked).\n\n## Portfolio\nThese are Thrive deals he is reported to have worked on. They are firm investments, not personal checks.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Anduril | not specified; he \"began working on\" Thrive's investment in 2022 | 2022 | unknown | | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/09/03/thrive-capitals-philip-clark-is-promoted-to-partner) |\n| OpenAI | Thrive's investments, incl. what Fortune calls the largest private tech deal ever; round not specified per Clark | 2023– | unknown for his role | | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/09/03/thrive-capitals-philip-clark-is-promoted-to-partner) |\n| OpenAI (Thrive round, context) | $6.6B at $157B | 2024-10 | Thrive led | | [src](https://benefitsandpensionsmonitor.com/news/industry-news/openai-secures-157bn-valuation-with-new-funding-round-led-by-thrive-capital/388893) |\n| Wiz | $1B at $12B | 2024-05 | Co-led (a16z, Lightspeed, Thrive) | Greylock, Wellington; existing: Cyberstarts, Greenoaks, Index, Salesforce Ventures, Sequoia | [src](https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001478169) |\n| Physical Intelligence | $70M seed | 2024-03 | Thrive led | | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=676543) |\n| Physical Intelligence | $400M | 2024-11 | not stated | Jeff Bezos, OpenAI, Lux Capital | [src](https://www.streetinsider.com/Reuters/Robot+AI+startup+Physical+Intelligence+raises+%24400+million+from+Bezos%2C+OpenAI/23921622.html) |\n| Physical Intelligence | $600M Series B at $5.6B | 2025-11 | No (CapitalG led) | Lux, Thrive, Jeff Bezos | [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-11-20/robotics-startup-physical-intelligence-valued-at-5-6-billion-in-new-funding) |\n| Cursor (Anysphere) | Thrive on cap table per Clark | 2024-05 | | | [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088) |\n| Cursor (Anysphere) | ~$100–105M Series B at ~$2.5–2.6B | 2024-12 | Thrive led | a16z | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/in-just-4-months-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-raised-another-100m-at-a-2-5b-valuation-led-by-thrive-sources-say) |\n| Cursor (Anysphere) | $900M at ~$10B | 2025-06 | Thrive led | | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/06/05/anysphere-raises-900m-ai-powered-cursor-code-editor) |\n| Nudge | $100M Series A | 2025-07 | Co-led (Thrive, Greenoaks) | angels | [src](https://news.bitcoin.com/coinbase-co-founder-fred-ehrsam-raises-100m-for-brain-interface-startup/) |\n| Mesh Optical Technologies | $50M Series A (Clark calls it a seed lead) | 2026-02 | Thrive led | Also Capital, Banner VC (per Dealroom) | [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-02-17/spacex-alumni-raise-50-million-for-data-center-optical-tech) |\n| Neuralink | not specified | | unknown | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/author/philip-clark) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Cursor: met them on pivot day, then a year of courtship.**\n  - Clark first met Cursor co-founder \"Michael\" (first name only in the source) in late 2022. Michael was building an AI tool for mechanical engineers and decided to pivot that same day, so their first conversation was mostly about pivot options [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088).\n  - He followed Michael and Aman as they moved to SF and merged with another team (adding two more co-founders, Arvid and one the summary transcribes as \"Swale\"), and became an early user. He called trying the product a \"magical one way door moment\" [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088).\n  - He says he and partner \"Miles\" pursued the deal for about a year and joined the cap table in May 2024 [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088).\n  - Timing vs. other investors: a16z led the ~$60M Series A in Aug 2024; TechCrunch corrected an earlier report that Thrive co-led it. Thrive then led the Dec 2024 Series B [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/in-just-4-months-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-raised-another-100m-at-a-2-5b-valuation-led-by-thrive-sources-say). Thrive's Miles Grimshaw joined Cursor's board as part of Thrive's investment [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-04-22/andreessen-thrive-poised-for-windfall-from-spacex-s-cursor-bid).\n  - Fortune adds that Clark helped Cursor hire after Thrive's 2024 investment [src](https://fortune.com/2025/09/03/thrive-capitals-philip-clark-is-promoted-to-partner).\n- **Wiz: a year of relationship, then a wartime flight.**\n  - Thrive sent Clark to Tel Aviv as part of a push beyond NY and SF. He met CEO Assaf Rappaport in 2022; the first call was a three-way Zoom across New York, Tel Aviv and Tokyo [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088).\n  - His read was that Wiz was software infrastructure rather than a classic security tool, with about half of its users being developers [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088).\n  - After about a year, Rappaport shared updated numbers in late 2023 and Clark and Kushner decided to invest [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088).\n  - Colossus places a call with Rappaport about two months after the Nov 2023 OpenAI board episode. Clark and Kushner flew to Israel 48 hours later via Paris and Cyprus because Israeli airspace was closed, went straight from the 7pm arrival into a four-hour meeting with the founders, and agreed on a deal [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/). Clark's explanation was that there were \"no other venture investors in Tel Aviv\" then [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/).\n  - Accounts differ on details: the podcast says 24 hours and a four-hour dinner with a handshake that night [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088).\n  - Outcome: Colossus says Thrive led Wiz's final two rounds and ended with a ~$1B stake before Google's $32B acquisition [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/). The announced May 2024 round lists a16z, Lightspeed and Thrive as co-leads [src](https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001478169).\n- **OpenAI: the pre-launch GPT-4 demo.**\n  - He says Thrive saw a GPT-4 demo in Nov 2022, which he called one of the most astounding technologies he had seen. Only two firms offered term sheets, and the original investment memo did not mention ChatGPT [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088).\n  - The summary's claim that Thrive invested at $29B \"before ChatGPT launched\" conflicts with public reporting that dates the $29B round to 2023 (unverified, needs the transcript).\n  - Fortune credits him with working on Thrive's OpenAI investments [src](https://fortune.com/2025/09/03/thrive-capitals-philip-clark-is-promoted-to-partner).\n  - Colossus's OpenAI section does not name him [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/).\n- **Nudge: an \"n of 1\" founder story.** Founded by an early Neuralink leader (Jeremy) and Coinbase co-founder Fred Ehrsam. Clark frames it as \"writing\" to the brain with non-invasive ultrasound, where Neuralink \"reads,\" and aims first at depression and addiction [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088). Thrive and Greenoaks led the $100M Series A in July 2025 [src](https://news.bitcoin.com/coinbase-co-founder-fred-ehrsam-raises-100m-for-brain-interface-startup/).\n- **Mesh Optical: SpaceX alumni, data center optics.** Founded by engineers from SpaceX's Starlink laser-link team, building optical transceivers for AI data centers. Clark describes it as a seed he led [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088). Bloomberg reports a $50M round led by Thrive in Feb 2026 [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-02-17/spacex-alumni-raise-50-million-for-data-center-optical-tech). The FTC later cleared Musk to acquire Mesh [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/26/ftc-gives-musk-the-ok-to-acquire-spacex-alumni-startup-mesh/).\n- **Anduril: his first defense deal at Thrive.** He began working on Thrive's Anduril investment in 2022, his first year at the firm. Fortune presents it as his alternative route into defense, after the childhood Lockheed dream [src](https://fortune.com/2025/09/03/thrive-capitals-philip-clark-is-promoted-to-partner). No round details found.\n- **Physical Intelligence:** Thrive led the March 2024 seed [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=676543). No story of Clark's specific role was found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Joshua Kushner (Thrive founder) | Recruited him after reading his chip Substack. Flew with him to Tel Aviv to close Wiz | 2022 | [src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-inside-thrive-capital), [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Thrive Capital | Investor (May 2022), Partner (Sept 2025) | 2022-05 | [src](https://theorg.com/org/thrive-capital/person/philip-clark), [src](https://fortune.com/2025/09/03/thrive-capitals-philip-clark-is-promoted-to-partner) |\n| \"Miles\" (Thrive partner; likely Miles Grimshaw, unverified mapping) | Co-pursued Cursor for about a year | ~2023 | [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088) |\n| \"Kareem\" (Thrive partner; surname not given) | Cited for the founder-alignment framing of concentration | | [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088) |\n| 8VC | Former employer (Principal per TheOrg) | before 2022 | [src](https://theorg.com/org/thrive-capital/person/philip-clark), [src](https://fortune.com/2025/09/03/thrive-capitals-philip-clark-is-promoted-to-partner) |\n| Bridgewater Associates | Former employer (investing / systematic geopolitical analysis) | | [src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-inside-thrive-capital) |\n| Hoover Institution | Advisor on emerging technologies | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/author/philip-clark) |\n| Condoleezza Rice, H.R. McMaster | Did research for them | | [src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-inside-thrive-capital) |\n| Assaf Rappaport (Wiz CEO) | Founder relationship from 2022, closed in Tel Aviv | 2022 | [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088) |\n| Cursor co-founders \"Michael\" and \"Aman\" (first names only in source) | Founder relationship from pivot day | late 2022 | [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088) |\n| Fred Ehrsam (Nudge) | Founder of portfolio company Nudge | 2025 | [src](https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088) |\n| Molly O'Shea (Sourcery) | Interviewed him twice and amplifies him on X | 2025-11 | [src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-inside-thrive-capital) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Which chip Substack got him hired?** Sourcery and Podchemy say a 2021–22 Substack on chip reshoring, but neither names it. Futurist fieldnotes (philipjclark.substack.com) was created Dec 2022, after he joined Thrive, and has no chip posts. I checked one candidate and ruled it out: Semi-Literate (semiliterate.substack.com, 2021–23 chip policy). Its 2021 about page identifies the author as John VerWey [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20210730041736/https://semiliterate.substack.com/about). Next steps: ask him, check X posts from 2021–22, or check whether posts were deleted or moved.\n- What was his first Thrive deal, and was he on the Anduril deal as an associate or did he drive it? Which Anduril round?\n- What exactly is his Neuralink role (TechCrunch lists it in his portfolio)? Is he on any boards?\n- OpenAI timing conflict: the podcast says they invested before ChatGPT launched at $29B, while public reports date the $29B round to 2023. Get the transcript.\n- Wiz trip timing: about 2 months after Nov 2023 per Colossus, which implies roughly Jan 2024 (inference, not stated). Did Thrive invest in Wiz before the May 2024 round?\n- (2026-11-09) Mesh founders \"Travis\" and \"Cameron\" (first names only in the transcript): get their surnames. Pin down the exact date of the early-2025 Mesh seed and its size.\n- (2026-11-09) OpenAI: the transcript resolves \"one of two term sheets\" but not whether Thrive's ~$29B commitment predates ChatGPT's launch (Nov 30, 2022).\n- Confirm \"Kareem\" is Kareem Zaki, and \"Miles\" is Miles Grimshaw.\n- Bridgewater, DoD and 8VC dates; LinkedIn not retrieved.\n- X follower count and posting pattern (blocked).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=niosgDC-QHU (auto-caption transcript, read 2026-11-09)\n- https://fortune.com/2025/09/03/thrive-capitals-philip-clark-is-promoted-to-partner\n- https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/\n- https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-inside-thrive-capital\n- https://www.sourcery.vc/p/breaking-inside-thrive-capital-again\n- https://www.podchemy.com/notes/inside-thrive-capital-investing-in-openai-wiz-cursor-nudge-physical-intelligence-45704758088\n- https://techcrunch.com/author/philip-clark\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/08/15/meet-the-first-batch-of-vcs-set-to-judge-startup-battlefield-200-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2025\n- https://theorg.com/org/thrive-capital/person/philip-clark\n- https://x.com/MollySOShea/status/1993827686695793041\n- https://philipjclark.substack.com/archive\n- https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/ais-eigenquestions\n- https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/the-end-of-incrementalism-how-ai\n- https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/can-open-source-be-too-open\n- https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/beyond-saas\n- https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/the-next-blackstone\n- http://web.archive.org/web/20210730041736/https://semiliterate.substack.com/about\n- https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001478169\n- https://benefitsandpensionsmonitor.com/news/industry-news/openai-secures-157bn-valuation-with-new-funding-round-led-by-thrive-capital/388893\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=676543\n- https://www.streetinsider.com/Reuters/Robot+AI+startup+Physical+Intelligence+raises+%24400+million+from+Bezos%2C+OpenAI/23921622.html\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-11-20/robotics-startup-physical-intelligence-valued-at-5-6-billion-in-new-funding\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/in-just-4-months-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-raised-another-100m-at-a-2-5b-valuation-led-by-thrive-sources-say\n- https://siliconangle.com/2025/06/05/anysphere-raises-900m-ai-powered-cursor-code-editor\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-04-22/andreessen-thrive-poised-for-windfall-from-spacex-s-cursor-bid\n- https://news.bitcoin.com/coinbase-co-founder-fred-ehrsam-raises-100m-for-brain-interface-startup/\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-02-17/spacex-alumni-raise-50-million-for-data-center-optical-tech\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/26/ftc-gives-musk-the-ok-to-acquire-spacex-alumni-startup-mesh/\n", "person:philip-kirk": "\n# Philip Kirk\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (strategics-2 sweep). Seniority is recorded as `vp` because the enum has no SVP; his actual title is SVP. \"Phil Kirk\" (Venture Atlanta 2019) is the same person and should be aliased to `philip-kirk`.*\n\n## One-line take\nKirk is the most senior dealmaker at Databricks for buying companies and for venture investing. He has done corporate M&A for nearly 20 years: Cisco from 2007 to 2020, ServiceNow from 2020 to 2026, and Databricks since April 2026. If Databricks ever buys an AI-infra company the owner backed, he is the person who runs the deal. He is not the person who writes the small Databricks Ventures checks. ([GCV 2026-04-13](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/kirk-joins-databricks-corporate-development-head/?format=md))\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He handles the full range of corporate deals. At Cisco he \"leads all acquisitions and investments related to cloud, analytics, big data, and enterprise software\" and executed the $3.7B AppDynamics acquisition, a deal Cisco signed in January 2017 days before AppDynamics' planned IPO ([Venture Atlanta 2019](https://www.ventureatlanta.org/cisco-phil-kirk/); price and timing per [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AppDynamics)). At ServiceNow he \"led strategy, venture investments and acquisitions\" ([GCV 2026-04-08](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/servicenow-investment-team-shrinks-kirk/?format=md)). Databricks hired him to \"oversee corporate development and ventures\" ([GCV 2026-04-13](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/kirk-joins-databricks-corporate-development-head/?format=md)).\n- **What he personally signs, sources or prices:** none of his deals at Databricks have been made public. He is most likely to own acquisitions, which require board approval according to the corp-dev job ad cited in [firms/databricks-ventures.md](../firms/databricks-ventures.md). Small DV checks still carry Andrew Ferguson's name and Ali Ghodsi's sign-off ([Fortune 2024](https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund)). His reporting line and whether he sits on DV's approval thread are **unconfirmed**.\n- **Constraint:** he works with Databricks' strategic money, not a financial fund. DV follows a lead and \"does not lead rounds\". Every deal has to make strategic sense for the lakehouse and agent platform, so he will never set the price on a frontier-lab seed.\n- **What he'd never do:** lead a lab seed at a $1B+ valuation for financial return. In his whole public record, the only lab bet was ServiceNow joining Thinking Machines' $2B seed as a follower, and the CFO announced that one, not Kirk.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He has executed a multi-billion-dollar acquisition.** The Venture Atlanta bio credits him with \"executing the $3.7N acquisition of AppDynamics\" (the N is a typo on the page for B) ([Venture Atlanta 2019-08-13](https://www.ventureatlanta.org/cisco-phil-kirk/)). GCV's coverage of his moves does not mention this. Cisco paid about 2x AppDynamics' planned IPO valuation to buy it before it listed, which shows he can negotiate a pre-IPO takeout.\n- **He started in VC.** Before Cisco he was an associate at DTEC Venture Capital and worked on Microsoft's corporate strategy team ([Venture Atlanta](https://www.ventureatlanta.org/cisco-phil-kirk/)). It is unclear which firm DTEC is (needs-id).\n- **He has taken observer seats.** At Cisco he was \"a board observer at two of its portfolio companies\", Moogsoft and Puppet Labs ([GCV 2020-08-03](https://globalventuring.com/kirk-leaves-cisco-for-servicenow/?format=md)). Databricks Ventures says it takes no board seats, so it is an open question whether Kirk changes that.\n- **Databricks' M&A pace since he arrived.** Since April 2026 Databricks has agreed to buy Panther Labs (June, [Reuters via The Star](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/06/16/databricks-strikes-deal-to-buy-panther-labs-in-cybersecurity-push)), bought Electric (August, [Databricks blog](https://www.databricks.com/blog/electric-joins-databricks-bring-wasm-postgres-ai-agent-sandboxes)) and bought Row Zero (September, after a $10M Series A). Ghodsi: \"We intend to do many more acquisitions like this in the future\" ([SaaSRise, citing TechCrunch](https://www.saasrise.com/deals/databricks-buys-row-zero-and-is-scouting-for-more-startups-to-acquire-0cf62ecf-edbc-447c-934d-7ef5822b8bd5)). **No source names Kirk on any of these.** They happened during his tenure, and attributing them to him is unconfirmed.\n- **His old and new employers back the same companies.** ServiceNow and Databricks Ventures were both in LangChain's Series B (2025-10, [LangChain](https://www.langchain.com/blog/series-b)) and OpenRouter's Series B (2026-05). The OpenRouter post lists \"ServiceNow, MongoDB, Snowflake and Databricks\" as strategics ([OpenRouter](https://openrouter.ai/blog/announcements/series-b/)). Kirk has seen both strategic seats on these cap tables.\n- **The talent swap between ServiceNow and Databricks runs both ways.** Victor Chang moved from Databricks to ServiceNow Ventures in 2023 (see [firms/servicenow-ventures.md](../firms/servicenow-ventures.md)), and Kirk moved the other way in 2026.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Kirk will not co-invest in a $600M lab seed, and Databricks Ventures does not price rounds. He matters to the owner as an **acquirer and exit path** for AI-infra, inference and agent-data companies: Databricks has bought Tecton, Neon, Electric and Row Zero and plans more. He also links to the strategics around frontier labs, because ServiceNow (Thinking Machines seed, Mistral B) and Databricks (Inferact and RadixArk seeds, Unconventional AI, Mistral) both bought into them during his time. The deals that matter to the owner (Thinking Machines Lab, Inferact, RadixArk) are company-level links. None is personally attributed to him.\n\n## Background\n- Before Cisco: strategy consultant in internet and e-commerce, associate at DTEC Venture Capital, and Microsoft Corporate Strategy team. Dates are not given ([Venture Atlanta](https://www.ventureatlanta.org/cisco-phil-kirk/)).\n- Education: an aggregator lists a University of Chicago undergraduate degree and a Wharton MBA. **Unconfirmed**, because the MarketScreener page blocked access (search snippet only).\n- **Cisco, 2007 to 2020-08:** Senior Director of Investments in 2019 per [Venture Atlanta](https://www.ventureatlanta.org/cisco-phil-kirk/). GCV says he spent 13 years in \"the same role\" as VP of corporate development ([GCV 2020](https://globalventuring.com/kirk-leaves-cisco-for-servicenow/?format=md)). The sources disagree on his title. He covered cloud, big data, analytics, infrastructure software and data centres. Named deals: Platfora (bought by Workday in 2016), Piston Cloud Computing, and AppDynamics (acquisition). He held observer seats at Moogsoft and Puppet.\n- **ServiceNow, 2020-08 to 2026-04:** hired as VP corporate development ([GCV 2020-08-03](https://globalventuring.com/kirk-leaves-cisco-for-servicenow/?format=md)). He was SVP corporate business development by May 2023 ([GCV 2023-05-26](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/fundraising/servicenow-hikes-cvc-plans-to-1bn/?format=md)) and left in April 2026 ([GCV 2026-04-08](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/servicenow-investment-team-shrinks-kirk/?format=md)).\n- **Databricks, 2026-04 to now:** SVP corporate development and ventures ([GCV 2026-04-13](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/kirk-joins-databricks-corporate-development-head/?format=md)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | No Databricks author page or team bio found as of 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | Search snippets reference a profile; URL not confirmed |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n**How to reach him:** there is no public channel. Realistic routes:\n- (1) Andrew Ferguson or Phil Shin at Databricks Ventures, who sit on his team.\n- (2) Co-investors from shared cap tables: CapitalG on OpenRouter, IVP on LangChain.\n- (3) The Databricks VC Network funds (a16z, NEA, Menlo, Laude and others; see the firm page).\n- (4) GCV events. He has spoken on conference corporate-development panels (Venture Atlanta 2019).\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2019-10 (announced 2019-08-13) — Venture Atlanta 2019 — corporate development sessions, roundtables and 1:1s with growth-stage companies — [ventureatlanta.org](https://www.ventureatlanta.org/cisco-phil-kirk/). No transcript.\n- No podcasts found.\n\n## How they think\n- Investing is a way to bring innovation onto the parent's platform: \"Net-new innovation is the only way forward\", seeking founders who \"bring greater value to the ServiceNow platform\" ([GCV 2023-05-26](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/fundraising/servicenow-hikes-cvc-plans-to-1bn/?format=md)).\n- He judges AI bets by whether they drive adoption on the platform: \"8Flow shares our vision to accelerate adoption of agentic AI in enterprises\" ([Newswire 2025-04-10](https://www.newswire.com/news/8flow-secures-10m-series-a-with-strategic-investment-from-servicenow-22553910)).\n- His public record has no contrarian views and no admitted calls, right or wrong.\n\n## Read these first\n- [GCV 2026-04-13: Kirk joins Databricks](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/kirk-joins-databricks-corporate-development-head/?format=md): his remit at Databricks.\n- [Venture Atlanta 2019 bio](https://www.ventureatlanta.org/cisco-phil-kirk/): the only detailed bio, covering the AppDynamics deal and his early career.\n- [GCV 2023-05-26: ServiceNow's $1B CVC commitment](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/fundraising/servicenow-hikes-cvc-plans-to-1bn/?format=md): his one on-record statement of philosophy.\n- [firms/databricks-ventures.md](../firms/databricks-ventures.md): the vehicle and team he now oversees.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public X or Substack, and his only quotes are in press releases.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 8Flow | Series A ($10M), for ServiceNow | 2025-04-10 | no | Caffeinated Capital (lead), Okta | [Newswire](https://www.newswire.com/news/8flow-secures-10m-series-a-with-strategic-investment-from-servicenow-22553910) (quoted) |\n| Thinking Machines Lab | Seed ($2B at $12B), for ServiceNow | 2025-07 | no | a16z (lead), NVIDIA, Accel, Cisco, AMD | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/kirk-joins-databricks-corporate-development-head/?format=md) (tenure only; CFO announced it, so his role is unconfirmed) |\n| Genesys | Strategic ($750M preferred), for ServiceNow | 2025-09 | co-lead with Salesforce | Salesforce | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/kirk-joins-databricks-corporate-development-head/?format=md) (tenure only; his role is unconfirmed) |\n| AppDynamics | Acquisition by Cisco ($3.7B) | 2017-01 | yes (acquirer) | — | [Venture Atlanta](https://www.ventureatlanta.org/cisco-phil-kirk/) |\n| Platfora | Undisclosed, for Cisco | not dated | no | — | [GCV 2020](https://globalventuring.com/kirk-leaves-cisco-for-servicenow/?format=md) |\n| Piston Cloud Computing | Undisclosed, for Cisco | not dated | no | — | [GCV 2020](https://globalventuring.com/kirk-leaves-cisco-for-servicenow/?format=md) |\n| Databricks (any) | none attributed yet | since 2026-04 | — | — | — |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **AppDynamics (2017):** Cisco bought the company days before its planned IPO for $3.7B ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AppDynamics)), and Kirk is credited with executing the deal ([Venture Atlanta](https://www.ventureatlanta.org/cisco-phil-kirk/)). It is the template for a pre-IPO takeout. The intro path and his exact role on the deal team are not disclosed.\n- **Thinking Machines (2025):** CFO Gina Mastantuono announced ServiceNow's seed participation on LinkedIn ([diginomica](https://diginomica.com/node/29074)). GCV lists it among ServiceNow's investments during Kirk's tenure. Who sourced it is not disclosed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Databricks Ventures | SVP corp dev and ventures | 2026-04 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/kirk-joins-databricks-corporate-development-head/?format=md) |\n| ServiceNow Ventures / corp BD | VP then SVP | 2020-08 to 2026-04 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/servicenow-investment-team-shrinks-kirk/?format=md) |\n| Cisco Investments / corp dev | Senior Director of Investments / VP | 2007 to 2020-08 | [Venture Atlanta](https://www.ventureatlanta.org/cisco-phil-kirk/) |\n| Andrew Ferguson | Heads DV, on Kirk's team | 2026-04 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/kirk-joins-databricks-corporate-development-head/?format=md) |\n| Amanda Vinson | Former ServiceNow corp BD colleague | until 2026-04 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/servicenow-investment-team-shrinks-kirk/?format=md) |\n| Vishal Kumar Gupta (ServiceNow; not the BVP Vishal Gupta) | Former ServiceNow corp dev and ventures VP | until 2025-10 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/servicenow-investment-team-shrinks-kirk/?format=md) |\n| Moogsoft, Puppet Labs | Board observer for Cisco | until 2020-08 | [GCV 2020](https://globalventuring.com/kirk-leaves-cisco-for-servicenow/?format=md) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who he reports to (Ghodsi or the CFO), and whether he now sits on DV's approval thread in place of or alongside Ghodsi.\n- Whether he ran Panther Labs, Electric or Row Zero. The Quotient AI and Antimatter/SiftD.ai deals in March 2026 came before his start.\n- His Cisco title: the 2019 Venture Atlanta bio says Senior Director of Investments; GCV in 2020 says VP. Also the exact month he became SVP at ServiceNow (between 2020-08 and 2023-05).\n- His personal role on ServiceNow's Thinking Machines and Genesys deals.\n- Education (aggregator only). The identity of DTEC Venture Capital.\n- A headshot. None was found on a Databricks page.\n\n## Sources\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/kirk-joins-databricks-corporate-development-head/?format=md\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/servicenow-investment-team-shrinks-kirk/?format=md\n- https://globalventuring.com/kirk-leaves-cisco-for-servicenow/?format=md\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/fundraising/servicenow-hikes-cvc-plans-to-1bn/?format=md\n- https://www.ventureatlanta.org/cisco-phil-kirk/\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AppDynamics\n- https://www.newswire.com/news/8flow-secures-10m-series-a-with-strategic-investment-from-servicenow-22553910\n- https://openrouter.ai/blog/announcements/series-b/\n- https://www.langchain.com/blog/series-b\n- https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/06/16/databricks-strikes-deal-to-buy-panther-labs-in-cybersecurity-push\n- https://www.databricks.com/blog/electric-joins-databricks-bring-wasm-postgres-ai-agent-sandboxes\n- https://www.saasrise.com/deals/databricks-buys-row-zero-and-is-scouting-for-more-startups-to-acquire-0cf62ecf-edbc-447c-934d-7ef5822b8bd5\n- https://diginomica.com/node/29074\n- https://fortune.com/2024/05/21/exclusive-databricks-expanding-ai-investments-second-vc-fund\n", "person:pieter-abbeel": "\n# Pieter Abbeel\n\n## One-line take\nBerkeley robot-learning professor whose former students founded OpenAI (Schulman), Perplexity, Skild, Physical Intelligence, Reflection, Ideogram and others; he writes small personal angel checks into frontier-AI rounds (Perplexity seed, Cohere A, Etched, Engram, Config) and now runs Amazon's frontier model research team, so he is a sourcing node more than a capital source [src](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/brief_bio.html) [src](https://fortune.com/2025/12/17/amazon-ceo-andy-jassy-announces-departure-of-ai-exec-rohit-prasad-in-leadership-shakeup/).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He gets early access to talent. His bio says his students co-founded over a dozen AI companies, including OpenAI, Perplexity, Skild, Physical Intelligence, Reflection, EvolutionaryScale, Ideogram, Genmo, xdof and Inception [src](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/brief_bio.html). Founders also list him as a credential. Etched lists him as \"Pieter Abbeel (Covariant)\" in its angel wall [src](https://www.etched.com/), and Engram names him alongside Karpathy [src](https://dealroom.co/news/135964-engram-emerges-from-stealth-with-98m-to-build-a-learned-memory-layer-for/).\n- **How he invests:** He never leads. In every round found, he is a named angel inside a round someone else leads: Index (Cohere, Hebbia), Gil/Friedman (Perplexity), Eclipse (Wayve), a16z/Index (Ideogram), Samsung (Config), and GC/KP/Sequoia (Engram).\n- **What constrains him:** Since 2025-12-17 he has had a full-time job inside a frontier lab: he leads Amazon's frontier model research team [src](https://fortune.com/2025/12/17/amazon-ceo-andy-jassy-announces-departure-of-ai-exec-rohit-prasad-in-leadership-shakeup/). He is also no longer listed on the AIX Ventures team page [src](https://aixventures.com/team). He still wrote personal checks after the Amazon appointment (Config 2026-05, Engram 2026-06).\n- **What he would never do:** Lead, or price a round. Nothing found shows him taking a board seat or writing a check above aggregator-reported angel size.\n- **How he converts his network:** Selectively. Of the student-founded companies on his bio, he is confirmed on the cap table of only two (Perplexity, Ideogram). He is not named in the funding announcements of the others that I checked: Skild, Physical Intelligence, Reflection, xdof and Inception. Genmo lists him as an advisor, not an investor [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/genmos-open-source-genai-model-aims-to-power-the-future-of-video).\n\n## Differential insight\nMirrored in `data/rows/pieter-abbeel-insights.csv`. Note: **no primary source was found where he discusses how he picks or sizes angel checks**. He hosts The Robot Brains podcast, but transcripts were not retrievable, so his investing philosophy is undocumented (see Open Questions).\n- **He kept angel investing after joining Amazon's frontier team.** TechCrunch on Config's $27M seed (2026-05-11), led by Samsung Venture Investment, names \"angel investor Pieter Abbeel (co-founder of Covariant and a UC Berkeley professor)\" [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/11/koreas-biggest-manufacturers-back-config-the-tsmc-of-robot-data/). Engram's $98M round (reported 2026-06-25, $600M post) names \"AI/robotics pioneer Pieter Abbeel\" next to Karpathy and Wiz CEO Assaf Rappaport [src](https://dealroom.co/news/135964-engram-emerges-from-stealth-with-98m-to-build-a-learned-memory-layer-for/). Both came after his 2025-12-17 Amazon appointment.\n- **He has quietly left the AIX Ventures roster.** AIX's launch post said \"Fund I Investing Partners include Pieter Abbeel, Anthony Goldbloom, Chris Manning, and myself\" [src](https://www.aixventures.com/newsroom/introducing-aix-ventures). The current team page lists Socher, Goldbloom, Manning, Ramadurai, Crance and two operations staff, with no Abbeel [src](https://aixventures.com/team). Aggregators still show him as \"Investment Partner at AIX Ventures (2021-present)\" [src](https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/PIETER-ABBEEL-A20LXR/), so they are stale.\n- **It is unclear whether his checks are personal or AIX money.** Perplexity's post credits \"Pieter Abbeel (UC Berkeley, AIX Ventures)\" in the seed [src](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/announcing-our-series-a-funding-round-and-mobile-app-launch). AIX as a firm also appears in Ideogram's seed [src](https://finsmes.com/2023/08/ideogram-raises-usd16-5m-in-seed-funding.html), so a 2021–2024 deal tagged to him may have been AIX money.\n- **He co-invests repeatedly beside Index / Mike Volpi.**\n  - Index led Cohere's Series A (2021), with Abbeel among the AI angels [src](https://betakit.com/cohere-raises-40-million-to-make-natural-language-processing-more-accessible/).\n  - \"The round was led by Mike Volpi\" at Hebbia (2022), where Abbeel was in the seed [src](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of).\n  - Index led Covariant's 2021 Series C [src](https://globalventuring.com/university/covariant-collects-80m-in-series-c-funding).\n  - Index co-led Ideogram's seed [src](https://finsmes.com/2023/08/ideogram-raises-usd16-5m-in-seed-funding.html).\n  - Volpi sits on the Covariant board and was a guest on Abbeel's own podcast (Robot Brains S1E14, 2021-06-30) [src](https://shows.acast.com/the-robot-brains/episodes/mike-volpi-on-why-vcs-are-investing-record-amounts-into-ai-s).\n- **He belongs to a recurring \"AI luminary\" angel bench.**\n  - Cohere's A named \"Geoffrey Hinton, Fei-Fei Li, Pieter Abbeel, and Raquel Urtasun\" [src](https://betakit.com/cohere-raises-40-million-to-make-natural-language-processing-more-accessible/).\n  - Urtasun reappears with him in Hebbia [src](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of) and Ideogram [src](https://finsmes.com/2023/08/ideogram-raises-usd16-5m-in-seed-funding.html).\n  - Karpathy appears with him in Perplexity, Ideogram, Etched and Engram.\n  - Hinton and Fei-Fei Li appear with him again on Etched [src](https://www.etched.com/).\n- **Hebbia was a seed check, not the Series A.** Hebbia says \"Pieter Abbeel, Henrique Dubugras, and Raquel Urtasun joined the seed as well\" [src](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of). The repo's `data/rounds.csv` currently files him under the Series A.\n- **He backs and vouches for his own students.** On Srinivas: \"And now we are seeing him do the same thing with Perplexity\" [src](https://engineering.berkeley.edu/news/2025/03/berkeley-alum-wants-to-make-the-planet-smarter/). Srinivas did his PhD under Abbeel [src](https://engineering.berkeley.edu/news/2025/03/berkeley-alum-wants-to-make-the-planet-smarter/).\n- **His view on foundation models.** On training data: \"Yes, but the key is not just large. It's very diverse.\" (2023-06-15) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/15/pieter-abbeel-and-ken-goldberg-on-generative-ai-applications/). This fits his 2026 checks into Config (robot data) and Engram (a memory layer).\n- **W&B came through research use.** W&B's own post describes him as a \"Berkeley roboticist and entrepreneur who saw our product through our collaboration with OpenAI.\" In other words, he saw the product as a user before he invested [src](https://wandb.ai/wandb_fc/articles/reports/W-B-Raises-15-Million-for-Deep-Learning-Experiment-Tracking--Vmlldzo1NDY3MDAx). This quote is from a search snippet; the page did not render.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** As a co-investor or capital source he is weak: he never leads, writes small checks (aggregator range $500K–$2M [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/pieter-abbeel)), and is now an Amazon executive. As a **talent and sourcing node** he is among the highest-value people in frontier AI:\n- **The Berkeley robot-learning lab is a founder factory.** His students founded OpenAI, Perplexity, Skild, Physical Intelligence, Reflection, Ideogram, xdof and Inception [src](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/brief_bio.html).\n- **His Amazon seat matters.** He sees which pre-training researchers Amazon's frontier team competes with to hire.\n\nDeals that matter to me:\n- **Etched** (AI chips, angel)\n- **Engram** ($98M first round for a new research company, $600M post; the \"big seed for a new lab\" pattern)\n- **Perplexity** (first round)\n- **Cohere** (Series A)\n- **Config** (robot data)\n- **Ideogram** (student company)\n\nUse him for warm intros to the next Berkeley/BAIR spinouts, not for allocation.\n\n## Background\n- PhD at Stanford under Andrew Ng (per Wikipedia, secondary) [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pieter_Abbeel); professor of AI and robotics at UC Berkeley [src](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/brief_bio.html).\n- Early researcher at OpenAI (VentureBeat via search; not fetched) [src](https://venturebeat.com/ai/top-ai-execs-including-richard-socher-launch-aix-ventures).\n- Co-founded Gradescope (AI grading) and Covariant (robotics foundation models). Covariant began as Embodied Intelligence, which raised a $7M seed [src](https://eecs.berkeley.edu/news/embodied-intelligence-raises-7m-seed-round/) [src](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/brief_bio.html).\n- Covariant raised from Index (Series C 2021; $80M) and Radical/Index (2023 $75M extension, with AIX Ventures participating) [src](https://globalventuring.com/university/covariant-collects-80m-in-series-c-funding) [src](https://thelogic.co/briefing/radical-ventures-co-leads-us75m-investment-in-robotics-firm-covariant/).\n- AIX Ventures Fund I investing partner. The fund was $50M, pre-seed/seed, alongside Socher, Goldbloom and Manning [src](https://www.aixventures.com/newsroom/introducing-aix-ventures). AIX raised a $202M Fund II (announced Feb 13) [src](https://www.aixventures.com/newsroom/second-fund-of-202m). That post does not name partners.\n- 2024-08: joined Amazon with Covariant co-founders Peter Chen and Rocky Duan. Amazon took a non-exclusive license to Covariant's models; terms were not disclosed [src](https://www.geekwire.com/2024/amazon-hires-covariant-founders-inks-licensing-deal-with-robotics-ai-startup-in-latest-reverse-acquihire-deal/).\n- 2025-12-17: Jassy named him to lead Amazon's frontier model research team in the AGI org under Peter DeSantis. He continues to work with robotics [src](https://fortune.com/2025/12/17/amazon-ceo-andy-jassy-announces-departure-of-ai-exec-rohit-prasad-in-leadership-shakeup/) [src](https://www.theregister.com/2025/12/17/jassy_taps_peter_desantis_to_run_agi/).\n- Awards: ACM Prize in Computing 2021 [src](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/brief_bio.html).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/ | Bio page lists student-founded companies [src](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/brief_bio.html) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing verified (X not fetchable) | Follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | nothing verified | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | The Robot Brains Podcast | Launched spring 2021 [src](https://eecs.berkeley.edu/news/robots-ai-and-podcasting-qa-pieter-abbeel/); acast feed [src](https://shows.acast.com/the-robot-brains/about) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2021-06-30 — The Robot Brains (host) — S1E14 with Mike Volpi (Index) on why VCs are pouring money into AI — [src](https://shows.acast.com/the-robot-brains/episodes/mike-volpi-on-why-vcs-are-investing-record-amounts-into-ai-s) — Volpi sits on the Covariant board. No transcript found.\n- 2021-10-07 — Gradient Dissent (Lukas Biewald, W&B) — \"Robotics, Startups, and Robotics Startups\" — [src](https://wandb.ai/site/resources/podcast/episodes/gradient-dissent-pieter-abbeel) — chapters cover commercialising Gradescope (36:23), what he looks for in co-founders, and the Covariant story (44:35). Show-notes summary only, no transcript.\n- 2021-09 — SuperDataScience SDS 503 — deep RL for robotics — [src](https://www.superdatascience.com/podcast/sds-503-deep-reinforcement-learning-for-robotics) — the transcript PDF was not retrievable.\n- 2023-06-15 — TechCrunch, with Ken Goldberg — generative AI applications — [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/15/pieter-abbeel-and-ken-goldberg-on-generative-ai-applications/) — \"It's all about chasing the long tail of edge cases.\"\n- 2024-04-19 — The Robot Brains (host) — with Richard Socher on search and AI — [src](https://www.aixventures.com/newsroom/tag/Pieter+Abbeel) — no transcript found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Foundation models need diverse data, not only more data.** The quotes are in the 2023 TechCrunch interview [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/15/pieter-abbeel-and-ken-goldberg-on-generative-ai-applications/). His 2026 checks (Config robot data, Engram memory layer) are consistent with this, but that link is my inference.\n- **He backs researcher-founders he has seen do research.** His praise of Srinivas combines \"vision\" and \"execution\" [src](https://engineering.berkeley.edu/news/2025/03/berkeley-alum-wants-to-make-the-planet-smarter/).\n- **Calls he got right:**\n  - Perplexity: seed in Sept 2022, a month after founding (per existing repo insight) [src](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/announcing-our-series-a-funding-round-and-mobile-app-launch).\n  - Cohere: Series A 2021 [src](https://betakit.com/cohere-raises-40-million-to-make-natural-language-processing-more-accessible/).\n  - W&B: an early angel [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/weights-and-biases-raises-45m-series-b-to-expand-beyond-experiment-tracking-for-machine-learning-practitioners-everywhere/).\n- **Calls that went wrong:** Covariant's licensing payment from Amazon was reported as well below its 2023 $625M valuation (Wikipedia mirror, secondary) [src](https://wiki.krisyotam.com/content/wikipedia_en_all_nopic_2026-03/Covariant_(company)). Graphcore is listed as an exit on 2024-07-11 (aggregator) [src](https://www.angelbacked.co/investor/pieter-abbeel).\n- **Contrarian views:** nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n- His brief bio, with the list of student-founded companies, which is effectively his sourcing map [src](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/brief_bio.html).\n- TechCrunch 2023 with Ken Goldberg: his foundation-model worldview [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/15/pieter-abbeel-and-ken-goldberg-on-generative-ai-applications/).\n- Berkeley Engineering 2025-03 on Srinivas: how he judges a student-founder [src](https://engineering.berkeley.edu/news/2025/03/berkeley-alum-wants-to-make-the-planet-smarter/).\n- Fortune 2025-12-17: his current Amazon mandate [src](https://fortune.com/2025/12/17/amazon-ceo-andy-jassy-announces-departure-of-ai-exec-rohit-prasad-in-leadership-shakeup/).\n- Gradient Dissent 2021: entrepreneurship and co-founder selection (audio only) [src](https://wandb.ai/site/resources/podcast/episodes/gradient-dissent-pieter-abbeel).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found (X not fetchable). His public voice runs through The Robot Brains, whose guests include his portfolio founders and partners (Biewald, Delangue, Socher, Volpi) [src](https://www.aixventures.com/newsroom/tag/Pieter+Abbeel).\n\n## Portfolio\n\"Led?\" is \"no\" everywhere: no source shows him leading. Rows marked *(aggregator)* are unconfirmed by a company or press source.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Etched | Undisclosed (angel wall) | not disclosed | no | Sequoia, a16z, Thiel, Jane Street; angels Hinton, Karpathy, Druckenmiller, Fei-Fei Li, Tri Dao | [src](https://www.etched.com/) |\n| Engram | $98M first round (seed per some sources, Series A per CB Insights) | 2026-06-25 | no | General Catalyst, Kleiner Perkins, Sequoia, Amplify; angels Karpathy, Assaf Rappaport | [src](https://dealroom.co/news/135964-engram-emerges-from-stealth-with-98m-to-build-a-learned-memory-layer-for/) |\n| Config | $27M Seed | 2026-05-11 | no | Samsung Venture Investment (lead), ZER01NE, LG Tech Ventures, SKT America, Kakao Ventures | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/11/koreas-biggest-manufacturers-back-config-the-tsmc-of-robot-data/) |\n| Grounded Superintelligence *(aggregator, needs-id)* | Angel | 2025-08-07 | unknown | unknown | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/pieter-abbeel) |\n| Ideogram | $16.5M Seed | 2023-08-22 | no | a16z + Index (co-leads), AIX Ventures, Golden, Two Small Fish; angels Karpathy, Jeff Dean, Urtasun, Sarah Guo | [src](https://finsmes.com/2023/08/ideogram-raises-usd16-5m-in-seed-funding.html) |\n| Perplexity | $3.1M Seed | 2022-09 | no | Elad Gil + Nat Friedman (co-leads); LeCun, Karpathy, Vaswani, Masad, Delangue | [src](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/announcing-our-series-a-funding-round-and-mobile-app-launch) |\n| Hebbia | Seed (named in Series A post) | announced 2022-09-27 | no | Series A led by Mike Volpi / Index; Dubugras, Urtasun in seed | [src](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of) |\n| Wayve | $200M Series B | 2022-01-18 | no | Eclipse (lead), D1, Baillie Gifford, Microsoft, Virgin, Ocado; angels LeCun, Branson | [src](https://wayve.ai/blog/wayve-announces-usd200-million-in-funding-to-accelerate-the-development-of-av2-0-the-next-wave-of) |\n| Cohere | $40M Series A | 2021-09-07 | no | Index (lead), Section 32, Radical; Hinton, Fei-Fei Li, Urtasun | [src](https://betakit.com/cohere-raises-40-million-to-make-natural-language-processing-more-accessible/) |\n| Cohere *(aggregator)* | Series B | 2022-02-15 | no | not verified | [src](https://shizune.co/investors/profile/pieter-abbeel) |\n| Weights & Biases | Seed (angel) | ~2018 | no | Trinity (seed lead); angels Socher, Nat Friedman | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/weights-and-biases-raises-45m-series-b-to-expand-beyond-experiment-tracking-for-machine-learning-practitioners-everywhere/) |\n| Bloom AI *(aggregator; via AIX)* | $1.1M Seed | 2022-03-07 | no | AIX Ventures (Socher + Abbeel named) | [src](https://vcnewsdaily.com/bloom/venture-capital-funding/rtgxmrkhdt) |\n| Ambience Healthcare *(aggregator)* | $30M Series A | 2022-04-25 | no | AIX portfolio company | [src](https://shizune.co/investors/profile/pieter-abbeel) |\n| Gantry *(aggregator)* | $23.9M Series A | 2022-06-07 | no | CEO Josh Tobin was his PhD student | [src](https://shizune.co/investors/profile/pieter-abbeel) |\n| ZenML *(aggregator)* | $2.7M Seed | 2021-12-14 | no | not verified | [src](https://shizune.co/investors/profile/pieter-abbeel) |\n| Prompt AI *(aggregator)* | $5M Seed | 2023-09-28 | no | AIX Ventures + Abstract (co-leads) | [src](https://shizune.co/investors/profile/pieter-abbeel) |\n| Graphcore *(aggregator)* | unknown | exit 2024-07-11 | no | | [src](https://www.angelbacked.co/investor/pieter-abbeel) |\n| Covariant | Founder | 2017– | n/a | Amplify, Index, Radical, AIX | [src](https://globalventuring.com/university/covariant-collects-80m-in-series-c-funding) |\n\n**Firm co-signers (senior/junior pairing):** this doesn't map well to an angel. The nearest equivalent is the AIX pattern:\n- Bloom AI (2022): the announcement names Socher and Abbeel as AIX investing partners [src](https://vcnewsdaily.com/bloom/venture-capital-funding/rtgxmrkhdt).\n- W&B: Socher and Abbeel are both early angels [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/weights-and-biases-raises-45m-series-b-to-expand-beyond-experiment-tracking-for-machine-learning-practitioners-everywhere/).\n- Ideogram: AIX invested as a firm alongside his personal name [src](https://finsmes.com/2023/08/ideogram-raises-usd16-5m-in-seed-funding.html).\n- No junior partner is named alongside him on any deal: nothing found.\n\n**Timing (was he first?):**\n- **Perplexity:** in the first priced round (Sept 2022). It was co-led by Gil/Friedman, so he was in the first round but no source shows he committed first.\n- **Hebbia:** seed, ahead of Index's Series A.\n- **Cohere:** Series A, after Radical's earlier money.\n- **Wayve:** Series B, so late.\n- **Etched:** round undisclosed, so timing can't be established.\n- **Engram and Config:** the first announced institutional rounds, so he was in at the start.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Perplexity.** Srinivas did his PhD in Abbeel's lab and the two co-taught a 2020 course [src](https://engineering.berkeley.edu/news/2025/03/berkeley-alum-wants-to-make-the-planet-smarter/). Abbeel joined the $3.1M seed a month after founding [src](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/announcing-our-series-a-funding-round-and-mobile-app-launch). The intro path is presumably the advisor relationship, but no source states it.\n- **W&B.** W&B describes him as having seen the product through its collaboration with OpenAI before investing [src](https://wandb.ai/wandb_fc/articles/reports/W-B-Raises-15-Million-for-Deep-Learning-Experiment-Tracking--Vmlldzo1NDY3MDAx).\n- **Config.** The Seoul/San Jose robot-data company has no Berkeley founders (CEO Minjoon Seo is ex-Meta and TwelveLabs). Abbeel was the sole named US angel in a round of Korean strategics [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/11/koreas-biggest-manufacturers-back-config-the-tsmc-of-robot-data/).\n- **Engram.** Its founders are researchers from Stanford, Berkeley and Cornell, including Chris Ré and Scott Linderman [src](https://dealroom.co/news/135964-engram-emerges-from-stealth-with-98m-to-build-a-learned-memory-layer-for/). No intro path was found.\n- **Etched.** Abbeel is on the angel wall. No round, date or intro was found [src](https://www.etched.com/).\n- **Owner's question: does Druckenmiller get direct Etched exposure?** Etched's angel wall lists \"Stanley Druckenmiller (Duquesne)\" as a named angel alongside Abbeel [src](https://www.etched.com/). That indicates a direct position, at least as listed. Druckenmiller and Abbeel also co-appear on Hebbia [src](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Aravind Srinivas (Perplexity) | Former PhD student; seed angel | PhD completed 2021 per wiki; seed 2022-09 | [src](https://engineering.berkeley.edu/news/2025/03/berkeley-alum-wants-to-make-the-planet-smarter/) |\n| John Schulman (OpenAI) | Former student, OpenAI co-founder | | [src](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/brief_bio.html) |\n| Deepak Pathak (Skild) | Former student | | [src](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/brief_bio.html) |\n| Chelsea Finn, Sergey Levine (Physical Intelligence) | Former students | | [src](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/brief_bio.html) |\n| Misha Laskin (Reflection) | Former student | | [src](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/brief_bio.html) |\n| Jonathan Ho (Ideogram) | Former student; seed angel | 2023-08 | [src](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/brief_bio.html) |\n| Josh Tobin (Gantry) | Former PhD student | | [src](https://www.insiderapps.com/news/gantry-launches-mission-assist-data-scientists-keeping-ai-models-fresh) |\n| Philipp Wu, Fred Shentu (xdof) | Former students | | [src](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/brief_bio.html) |\n| Peter Chen, Rocky Duan | Covariant co-founders; joined Amazon with him | 2024-08 | [src](https://www.geekwire.com/2024/amazon-hires-covariant-founders-inks-licensing-deal-with-robotics-ai-startup-in-latest-reverse-acquihire-deal/) |\n| Richard Socher (AIX Ventures) | AIX Fund I co-partner; W&B co-angel | 2021 | [src](https://www.aixventures.com/newsroom/introducing-aix-ventures) |\n| Mike Volpi (Index) | Covariant board; led Cohere A and Hebbia A beside him; podcast guest | 2021 | [src](https://shows.acast.com/the-robot-brains/episodes/mike-volpi-on-why-vcs-are-investing-record-amounts-into-ai-s) |\n| Raquel Urtasun | Repeat co-angel (Cohere, Hebbia, Ideogram) | 2021 | [src](https://betakit.com/cohere-raises-40-million-to-make-natural-language-processing-more-accessible/) |\n| Andrej Karpathy | Repeat co-angel (Perplexity, Ideogram, Etched, Engram) | 2022 | [src](https://dealroom.co/news/135964-engram-emerges-from-stealth-with-98m-to-build-a-learned-memory-layer-for/) |\n| Peter DeSantis / Andy Jassy (Amazon) | Reports into DeSantis's AI org | 2025-12-17 | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/12/17/amazon-ceo-andy-jassy-announces-departure-of-ai-exec-rohit-prasad-in-leadership-shakeup/) |\n| Ken Goldberg (Berkeley) | Colleague; joint TechCrunch interview | 2023-06 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/15/pieter-abbeel-and-ken-goldberg-on-generative-ai-applications/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is \"Grounded Superintelligence\" (CB Insights, angel, 2025-08-07) a real new lab? I couldn't confirm it (search budget ran out). If it is a lab, it would be the highest-fit item in his portfolio.\n- Personal vs AIX: which 2021–2024 checks were his own money? Did he formally leave AIX, and when?\n- Does Amazon's conflict-of-interest policy limit his angel checks into labs that compete with Amazon's frontier team? Engram (2026-06) suggests not, or at least not yet.\n- Did he invest in Skild, Physical Intelligence, Reflection, xdof or Inception? None of their funding announcements found name him. Cap-table checks needed.\n- When and in which round did he enter Etched? Was Etched his intro, or did it come via Hinton or another co-angel?\n- His own words on check size, pass reasons and sourcing are missing. Robot Brains and Gradient Dissent transcripts would be the place to look.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.etched.com/\n- https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/brief_bio.html\n- https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~pabbeel/\n- https://fortune.com/2025/12/17/amazon-ceo-andy-jassy-announces-departure-of-ai-exec-rohit-prasad-in-leadership-shakeup/\n- https://www.theregister.com/2025/12/17/jassy_taps_peter_desantis_to_run_agi/\n- https://www.geekwire.com/2024/amazon-hires-covariant-founders-inks-licensing-deal-with-robotics-ai-startup-in-latest-reverse-acquihire-deal/\n- https://www.aixventures.com/newsroom/introducing-aix-ventures\n- https://aixventures.com/team\n- https://www.aixventures.com/newsroom/second-fund-of-202m\n- https://www.aixventures.com/newsroom/tag/Pieter+Abbeel\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/11/koreas-biggest-manufacturers-back-config-the-tsmc-of-robot-data/\n- https://dealroom.co/news/135964-engram-emerges-from-stealth-with-98m-to-build-a-learned-memory-layer-for/\n- https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/announcing-our-series-a-funding-round-and-mobile-app-launch\n- https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of\n- https://betakit.com/cohere-raises-40-million-to-make-natural-language-processing-more-accessible/\n- https://wayve.ai/blog/wayve-announces-usd200-million-in-funding-to-accelerate-the-development-of-av2-0-the-next-wave-of\n- https://finsmes.com/2023/08/ideogram-raises-usd16-5m-in-seed-funding.html\n- https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/weights-and-biases-raises-45m-series-b-to-expand-beyond-experiment-tracking-for-machine-learning-practitioners-everywhere/\n- https://wandb.ai/wandb_fc/articles/reports/W-B-Raises-15-Million-for-Deep-Learning-Experiment-Tracking--Vmlldzo1NDY3MDAx\n- https://engineering.berkeley.edu/news/2025/03/berkeley-alum-wants-to-make-the-planet-smarter/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/15/pieter-abbeel-and-ken-goldberg-on-generative-ai-applications/\n- https://shows.acast.com/the-robot-brains/episodes/mike-volpi-on-why-vcs-are-investing-record-amounts-into-ai-s\n- https://wandb.ai/site/resources/podcast/episodes/gradient-dissent-pieter-abbeel\n- https://eecs.berkeley.edu/news/robots-ai-and-podcasting-qa-pieter-abbeel/\n- https://globalventuring.com/university/covariant-collects-80m-in-series-c-funding\n- https://thelogic.co/briefing/radical-ventures-co-leads-us75m-investment-in-robotics-firm-covariant/\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/genmos-open-source-genai-model-aims-to-power-the-future-of-video\n- https://www.insiderapps.com/news/gantry-launches-mission-assist-data-scientists-keeping-ai-models-fresh\n- Aggregators (secondary): https://signal.nfx.com/investors/pieter-abbeel, https://shizune.co/investors/profile/pieter-abbeel, https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/pieter-abbeel, https://www.angelbacked.co/investor/pieter-abbeel, https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/PIETER-ABBEEL-A20LXR/, https://vcnewsdaily.com/bloom/venture-capital-funding/rtgxmrkhdt\n", "person:pradeep-sindhu": "\n# Pradeep Sindhu\n\n## One-line take\nThe networking-silicon architect whose angel name sits on the cap tables of Cerebras, Sagence AI and Astrus, and who as of May 2026 is formally attached to Mayfield, which makes him a door into both Khosla's and Mayfield's AI-silicon deal flow.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He has founded two silicon/systems companies from a blank sheet: Juniper (router ASICs, 1996) and Fungible (the DPU, 2015/16). Few angels have that credibility with chip founders. His name on a seed round tells later investors a systems architect has looked at the design. He is in the same small technologist-angel syndicate as Andy Bechtolsheim, Atiq Raza and Lip-Bu Tan, and that group shows up together on AI-chip cap tables ([Cerebras company page](https://www.cerebras.ai/company), [eeNews 2021-06-07](https://www.eenewseurope.com/en/analog-inference-startup-raises-10-6-million/)).\n- **What constrains him.** He writes angel-sized checks. No check amount is disclosed anywhere. He has spent most of his life as an operator, and Microsoft employment (CVP Silicon as of Nov 2024) probably limits how visible and how large his outside bets can be. His second company, Fungible, raised $300M+ ([FinSMEs/press](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/fungible-closes-200-million-series-c-financing-led-by-softbank-vision-fund-300875437.html)) and was sold for a reported ~$190M ([Blocks & Files](https://blocksandfiles.com/2022/12/13/fungible-sold-to-microsoft-for-190-million/)). His record as a company builder is therefore one huge win (Juniper) and one acqui-hire-like exit.\n- **What he'd never do.** Back a \"SmartNIC\"-style, me-too architecture. He has publicly dismissed bolting general-purpose cores onto a NIC as \"not innovation\" ([Grey Beards transcript](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/grey-beards-on-storage/105-greybeards-talk-new-datacenter-architecture-with-pradeep-sindhu-ceo-co-founder-fungible)). He wants architectural first principles.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Mayfield made him a Venture Advisor in May 2026.** Mayfield's own post names his focus as semiconductors, systems and \"scaling the foundational infrastructure powering the AI era\" ([Mayfield, 2026-05](https://www.mayfield.com/pradeep-sindhu-joins-mayfield-as-venture-advisor/)). This is new and not in any aggregator profile. It is the first formal investing role he has held.\n- **The Mayfield relationship goes back 16 years.** Navin Chaddha (Mayfield): \"I first met Pradeep at the World Economic Forum at Davos in January 2010.\" Later in 2010 Juniper bought Mayfield's portfolio company Ankeena. Sindhu pitched the Fungible idea in 2015. In 2016 the founders presented a hand-written deck on an iPad, and Mayfield co-led the Series A ([Chaddha, 2019-06-27, Mayfield post now redirecting to LinkedIn Pulse](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/celebrating-latest-milestone-our-decade-long-pradeep-sindhu-chaddha/)).\n- **Khosla network, three times.** Vinod Khosla incubated Juniper at Kleiner Perkins in 1995–96 ([Khosla Ventures](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/vinod-khosla); [Kleiner case study](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/case-study/juniper-networks)). Khosla Ventures led the seeds of Analog Inference/Sagence (2018) and Astrus (2025), and Sindhu is an angel in both ([Private Capital Journal 2025-09-02](https://privatecapitaljournal.com/astrus-secures-us-8m-seed-financing/)). His recent chip angel deals look like KV-sourced allocations.\n- **Same angel trio on two AI-chip cap tables.** Bechtolsheim, Raza and Sindhu are named together on Analog Inference (\"investors and advisors\", [eeNews 2021-06-07](https://www.eenewseurope.com/en/analog-inference-startup-raises-10-6-million/)). The same three are on Cerebras's investor list ([Cerebras](https://www.cerebras.ai/company)). Cerebras went public in May 2026 ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-mints-two-billionaires-sets-stage-for-potential-ai-wave.html)), so this syndicate now has a liquid AI-chip win.\n- **Lip-Bu Tan link.** Tan (Walden Riverwood) co-led Fungible's Series A and joined its board in Feb 2017 ([GlobeNewswire 2017-02-07](https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2017/02/07/1052337/0/en/Fungible-Inc-Launches-with-32-5-Million-in-Financing-and-a-Plan-to-Revolutionize-Cloud-Data-Centers.html)). Tan is also a Cerebras investor and is now Intel's CEO ([Cerebras](https://www.cerebras.ai/company)).\n- **Redline Capital link.** Redline Capital led Innovium's Series C in Mar 2017, the round in which Sindhu joined Innovium's advisory board. Redline also co-led Fungible's Series B ([Innovium release](https://aeccafe.com/nbc/articles/1/1492727/Innovium-Raises-$38.3M-Oversubscribed-Series-C-Funding-Announces-Advisory-Board); [Wikipedia: Fungible](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fungible_Inc.)).\n- **His AI view: interconnect is the problem.** In an Aug 2025 @Scale keynote, billed as \"Keynote from Microsoft\", he argued that AI's hyperparallelism makes interconnect the central problem. He favoured flat, high-radix topologies and a simplified Ethernet stack with receiver-based congestion control ([podwise summary](https://podwise.ai/episodes/4976538)). This comes from a summary, not a transcript, so confidence is low.\n- **IIT Kanpur philanthropy.** The Pradeep Sindhu Foundation funds an endowed chair at IIT Kanpur. Rajshekhar Gannavarpu holds it for 2025–28 ([IITK DoRA](https://iitk.ac.in/dora/chair/10/pradeep-sindhu-chair)). This is a possible route into IIT-alumni chip founders.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** AI semis and compute are core to the thesis. Sindhu is not a check-writer at the $20M scale. He is a co-investor and diligence signal at the very earliest stage of AI-chip companies: Cerebras (early angel), Sagence AI (seed-era angel/advisor), Astrus (2025 seed). He brings a new Mayfield pipeline as well. The deals that matter to me:\n- Cerebras: angel, round undisclosed; IPO May 2026.\n- Sagence AI: seed-era angel/advisor alongside Khosla's lead.\n- Astrus: Sep 2025 $8M seed led by Khosla.\n\nHe is most useful as (a) a technical reference on interconnect and accelerator architecture, (b) a co-investor whose presence signals that KV and Mayfield are interested, and (c) a source of early chip founders, especially ex-Juniper, ex-Fungible and Microsoft silicon alumni.\n\n## Background\n- **1953-09-04:** Born in Varanasi, India ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pradeep_Sindhu)).\n- **Education:** B.Tech EE, IIT Kanpur, 1974. M.S. EE, University of Hawaiʻi, 1976. PhD CS, Carnegie Mellon, 1982, under Bob Sproull ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pradeep_Sindhu)).\n- **Xerox PARC:** About 11 years at the Computer Science Lab, working on VLSI tools and high-speed interconnects for shared-memory multiprocessors. That work influenced Sun's SPARCcenter 2000 ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pradeep_Sindhu)).\n- **1995:** Introduced to Vinod Khosla. **Feb 1996:** Founded Juniper Networks with Dennis Ferguson and Bjorn Liencres. Founding CEO and Chairman, later Vice Chairman, CTO and Chief Scientist. Juniper IPO'd on 1999-06-25 ([Kleiner](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/case-study/juniper-networks); [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pradeep_Sindhu)).\n- **Feb 2017:** Stepped back from Juniper's vice chairman role to technical adviser to focus on Fungible ([SDxCentral](https://www.sdxcentral.com/articles/news/juniper-cto-pradeep-sindhu-stepping-aside-to-focus-on-his-startup/2017/02/); [Juniper 8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1043604/000119312517031856/d530517d8k.htm)).\n- **Fungible:** Co-founded with Bertrand Serlet. He says it was founded in 2015 ([Grey Beards](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/grey-beards-on-storage/105-greybeards-talk-new-datacenter-architecture-with-pradeep-sindhu-ceo-co-founder-fungible)). Funding: Series A, $32.5M, Feb 2017 (Mayfield, Walden Riverwood, Battery; Juniper participating). Series B led by Samsung Catalyst and Redline. Series C, $200M, Jun 2019, led by SoftBank Vision Fund ([GlobeNewswire](https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2017/02/07/1052337/0/en/Fungible-Inc-Launches-with-32-5-Million-in-Financing-and-a-Plan-to-Revolutionize-Cloud-Data-Centers.html); [PRNewswire](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/fungible-closes-200-million-series-c-financing-led-by-softbank-vision-fund-300875437.html)). Became Chief Development Officer when Eric Hayes became CEO in 2021 ([Wikipedia: Fungible](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fungible_Inc.)).\n- **Jan 2023:** Microsoft confirmed the Fungible acquisition. The reported price was about $190M; ITPro called it a \"fire sale\" ([TechCrunch 2023-01-09](https://techcrunch.com/2023/01/09/microsoft-acquires-fungible-a-maker-of-data-processing-units-to-bolster-azure/); [ITPro](https://itpro.com/business-strategy/mergers-and-acquisitions/369836/microsoft-fungible-fire-sale-dpu-technology)).\n- **Microsoft:** \"Corporate VP Silicon\" per [Blocks & Files 2024-11-20](https://blocksandfiles.com/2024/11/20/microsoft-boosts-azure-infrastructure-with-fungible-derived-dpu/). [TMTPost 2024-11-21](https://en.tmtpost.com/post/7348136) calls him \"CVP for silicon\". The Fungible-derived Azure Boost DPU was announced at Ignite in Nov 2024. He was billed as a \"current Microsoft leader\" on the @Scale Podcast in Oct 2025 ([Apple Podcasts](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/scale-podcast-with-founder-of-juniper-networks-dr/id1818211959?i=1000729560151)). Whether the role continues after May 2026 is unconfirmed.\n- **May 2026:** Venture Advisor at Mayfield, focused on AI and enterprise ([Mayfield team page](https://www.mayfield.com/team/pradeep-sindhu/)).\n- Member of the National Academy of Engineering; 200+ patents ([Mayfield](https://www.mayfield.com/pradeep-sindhu-joins-mayfield-as-venture-advisor/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.mayfield.com/team/pradeep-sindhu/ | Mayfield Venture Advisor page (May 2026); no personal site found |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No verified account found |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | Not linked from Mayfield page; not checked |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only (see below) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- **2025-10** — @Scale Podcast, Ep. 3, host Francois Richard (Meta). Topics: PARC, Sun and Juniper lessons; why CPUs struggle; AI compared with the internet; advice on hardware startups — [Apple Podcasts](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/scale-podcast-with-founder-of-juniper-networks-dr/id1818211959?i=1000729560151). Description only; no transcript checked.\n- **2025-08** — @Scale: Networking (Santa Clara), \"Keynote from Microsoft\". AI interconnect fundamentals; flat high-radix fabrics; simplified Ethernet transport — [podwise summary](https://podwise.ai/episodes/4976538). Summary only.\n- **2021-01** — SiliconANGLE interview on the DPU. He said \"A SmartNIC is not a DPU\" and \"CPUs are not good at executing these data-centric computations\" — [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=536114).\n- **2020-08** — Grey Beards on Storage, Ep. 105, \"New datacenter architecture\". He said x86 architecture \"has reached the end of its useful life\" for data-centric work — [podscripts transcript](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/grey-beards-on-storage/105-greybeards-talk-new-datacenter-architecture-with-pradeep-sindhu-ceo-co-founder-fungible).\n- **2020-08** — Hot Interconnects 2020 keynote panel (Fungible era) — listed in [HPC-announce program](https://lists.mcs.anl.gov/pipermail/hpc-announce/2020-August/007192.html); no transcript checked.\n\n## How they think\n- **Data-centric computing.** In his framing, \"all work arrives at a server in the form of packets on a wire\" ([Grey Beards](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/grey-beards-on-storage/105-greybeards-talk-new-datacenter-architecture-with-pradeep-sindhu-ceo-co-founder-fungible)). General-purpose CPUs waste transistors on that work, and a purpose-built processor such as the DPU is the answer.\n- **Contrarian on SmartNICs and Arm offload.** He has said moving data-path work to Arm cores is \"not going to run more efficiently on an ARM CPU\" ([Grey Beards](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/grey-beards-on-storage/105-greybeards-talk-new-datacenter-architecture-with-pradeep-sindhu-ceo-co-founder-fungible)).\n- **For AI, the network is the computer.** In the 2025 keynote, per the summary, he treats hyperparallel training and inference as an interconnect problem and favours flat, high-radix Ethernet-based fabrics over proprietary scale-up stacks.\n- **Calls right:** Juniper's router ASICs, a ~$7B outcome for Kleiner on ~$3M per [Wikipedia: Khosla](https://www.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vinod_Khosla). The DPU category, now standard across hyperscalers, including Microsoft's own Azure Boost DPU.\n- **Calls wrong or early:** Fungible as an independent company. It was sold for less than the capital it raised, which suggests the DPU thesis was right but the standalone merchant-silicon business was not.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Mayfield: Pradeep Sindhu joins as Venture Advisor (May 2026)](https://www.mayfield.com/pradeep-sindhu-joins-mayfield-as-venture-advisor/): his current investing posture and focus areas.\n2. [Grey Beards Ep. 105 transcript (2020)](https://podscripts.co/podcasts/grey-beards-on-storage/105-greybeards-talk-new-datacenter-architecture-with-pradeep-sindhu-ceo-co-founder-fungible): the longest first-person statement of his architecture worldview.\n3. [SiliconANGLE DPU interview (2021)](https://siliconangle.com/?p=536114): a crisp version of why SmartNICs are not DPUs.\n4. [Chaddha on the Fungible origin (2019)](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/celebrating-latest-milestone-our-decade-long-pradeep-sindhu-chaddha/): how the Mayfield relationship was built.\n5. [@Scale Podcast Ep. 3 (2025)](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/scale-podcast-with-founder-of-juniper-networks-dr/id1818211959?i=1000729560151): his most recent long-form take on AI compared with the internet.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No active X or Substack presence was located. His public output is conference keynotes and podcast guest spots.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cerebras | Angel (round not disclosed) | undated (pre-Dec 2016 likely) | no | Bechtolsheim, Raza, Lip-Bu Tan, Altman, Sutskever, McKeown, Weber, Perlmutter | [Cerebras](https://www.cerebras.ai/company) |\n| Sagence AI (ex-Analog Inference) | Seed-era angel + advisor (entry round not specified) | by 2021-06 | no | Khosla Ventures (lead), Andy Bechtolsheim, Atiq Raza | [eeNews 2021-06-07](https://www.eenewseurope.com/en/analog-inference-startup-raises-10-6-million/) |\n| Astrus | Seed ($8M; second seed) | 2025-09-02 | no | Khosla Ventures (lead), 1517 Fund, Drive Capital, Alumni Ventures | [Private Capital Journal](https://privatecapitaljournal.com/astrus-secures-us-8m-seed-financing/) |\n| Innovium | Advisor (named with Series C, $38.3M) | 2017-03-14 | no | Redline Capital (lead) | [Innovium release](https://aeccafe.com/nbc/articles/1/1492727/Innovium-Raises-$38.3M-Oversubscribed-Series-C-Funding-Announces-Advisory-Board) |\n| Fungible | Founder | 2015/16 | n/a | Mayfield, Walden Riverwood, Battery, Juniper, Samsung Catalyst, Redline, SoftBank VF, Norwest | [GlobeNewswire](https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2017/02/07/1052337/0/en/Fungible-Inc-Launches-with-32-5-Million-in-Financing-and-a-Plan-to-Revolutionize-Cloud-Data-Centers.html) |\n| Datera | Undisclosed | — | — | — | aggregator, unconfirmed ([RoundFunded](https://www.roundfunded.com/en/investor/pradeep-sindhu-222508-1)) |\n| Huddl.ai | Undisclosed | — | — | — | aggregator, unconfirmed ([RoundFunded](https://www.roundfunded.com/en/investor/pradeep-sindhu-222508-1)) |\n| ShiftRight | Undisclosed | — | — | — | aggregator, unconfirmed ([RoundFunded](https://www.roundfunded.com/en/investor/pradeep-sindhu-222508-1)) |\n| Avicena | Advisor (claimed) | — | — | — | TheOrg \"Unverified\" only ([TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/avicena-tech/org-chart/pradeep-sindhu)); targeted search found no confirmation |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Fungible / Mayfield.** He met Navin Chaddha at Davos in January 2010. Later that year Juniper acquired Mayfield's Ankeena. In 2015 Sindhu proposed a \"data-centric era\" startup to be spun out of Juniper and brought in Bertrand Serlet (ex-Apple). In 2016 they pitched Mayfield with a hand-written iPad deck. Mayfield co-led the $32.5M Series A with Walden Riverwood (Lip-Bu Tan) and Battery, announced in Feb 2017. Chaddha and Tan took board seats ([Chaddha](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/celebrating-latest-milestone-our-decade-long-pradeep-sindhu-chaddha/); [GlobeNewswire](https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2017/02/07/1052337/0/en/Fungible-Inc-Launches-with-32-5-Million-in-Financing-and-a-Plan-to-Revolutionize-Cloud-Data-Centers.html)). The company was sold to Microsoft in Jan 2023, and Mayfield hired him as Venture Advisor in May 2026.\n- **Juniper / Khosla.** He was introduced to Vinod Khosla in 1995 while at PARC. Kleiner incubated Juniper, and it launched in Feb 1996 ([Kleiner](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/case-study/juniper-networks)). The repo's KV deal row notes \"Juniper, a Vinod incubation\".\n- **Sagence AI and Astrus.** No first-contact story was found. Both are KV-led seeds, which points to the Khosla relationship as the likely intro path (inference, not sourced).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Vinod Khosla / Khosla Ventures | Incubated Juniper at Kleiner; KV-led seeds Sindhu joined (Sagence, Astrus) | 1995 | [Kleiner](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/case-study/juniper-networks); [PCJ](https://privatecapitaljournal.com/astrus-secures-us-8m-seed-financing/) |\n| Navin Chaddha / Mayfield | Fungible Series A co-lead and board member for ~10 yrs; now Mayfield Venture Advisor | 2010 | [Mayfield](https://www.mayfield.com/pradeep-sindhu-joins-mayfield-as-venture-advisor/) |\n| Lip-Bu Tan (Walden Riverwood; Intel CEO) | Fungible board (Series A co-lead); co-investor in Cerebras | 2017 | [GlobeNewswire](https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2017/02/07/1052337/0/en/Fungible-Inc-Launches-with-32-5-Million-in-Financing-and-a-Plan-to-Revolutionize-Cloud-Data-Centers.html); [Cerebras](https://www.cerebras.ai/company) |\n| Andy Bechtolsheim | Co-angel in Cerebras and Analog Inference/Sagence | by 2021 | [eeNews](https://www.eenewseurope.com/en/analog-inference-startup-raises-10-6-million/) |\n| Atiq Raza | Co-angel in Cerebras and Analog Inference/Sagence | by 2021 | [eeNews](https://www.eenewseurope.com/en/analog-inference-startup-raises-10-6-million/) |\n| Bertrand Serlet | Fungible co-founder | 2015 | [Chaddha](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/celebrating-latest-milestone-our-decade-long-pradeep-sindhu-chaddha/) |\n| Dharmesh Thakker / Battery Ventures | Fungible board observer (Series A co-lead) | 2017 | [GlobeNewswire](https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2017/02/07/1052337/0/en/Fungible-Inc-Launches-with-32-5-Million-in-Financing-and-a-Plan-to-Revolutionize-Cloud-Data-Centers.html) |\n| Redline Capital | Led Innovium Series C (where he joined advisory board); co-led Fungible Series B | 2017 | [Innovium](https://aeccafe.com/nbc/articles/1/1492727/Innovium-Raises-$38.3M-Oversubscribed-Series-C-Funding-Announces-Advisory-Board) |\n| Vishal Sarin (Sagence AI founder/CEO) | Investor/advisor | by 2021 | [eeNews](https://www.eenewseurope.com/en/analog-inference-startup-raises-10-6-million/) |\n| Zeyi Wang, Bradley J. Moon (Astrus) | Investor | 2025 | [PCJ](https://privatecapitaljournal.com/astrus-secures-us-8m-seed-financing/) |\n\n**How a founder or investor gets to him.**\n1. The cleanest formal route is now Mayfield: Navin Chaddha or the Mayfield AI team. His mandate there is explicitly about advising founders on semiconductors and systems.\n2. Khosla Ventures. KV has brought him into at least two chip seeds.\n3. The Bechtolsheim/Raza technologist-angel circle.\n4. IIT Kanpur alumni channels, through his foundation's endowed chair.\n\n## Open questions\n- Is he still at Microsoft after the May 2026 Mayfield role? The Mayfield bio describes Microsoft only as Fungible's acquirer.\n- Check sizes: none disclosed for Cerebras, Sagence or Astrus.\n- Did he join Astrus's later round(s)? An unverified search snippet cites a 2026 Fasken notice of a Series A, which could not be fetched (403).\n- Are the Datera, Huddl.ai, ShiftRight and Avicena links real? They are aggregator- or TheOrg-only.\n- Which Mayfield AI-silicon deals since May 2026 has he touched?\n- Is there a transcript of the Aug 2025 @Scale keynote, for verbatim AI-interconnect quotes?\n\n## Sources\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pradeep_Sindhu\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fungible_Inc.\n- https://www.mayfield.com/pradeep-sindhu-joins-mayfield-as-venture-advisor/\n- https://www.mayfield.com/team/pradeep-sindhu/\n- https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/celebrating-latest-milestone-our-decade-long-pradeep-sindhu-chaddha/\n- https://blocksandfiles.com/2024/11/20/microsoft-boosts-azure-infrastructure-with-fungible-derived-dpu/\n- https://en.tmtpost.com/post/7348136\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/scale-podcast-with-founder-of-juniper-networks-dr/id1818211959?i=1000729560151\n- https://podwise.ai/episodes/4976538\n- https://podscripts.co/podcasts/grey-beards-on-storage/105-greybeards-talk-new-datacenter-architecture-with-pradeep-sindhu-ceo-co-founder-fungible\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=536114\n- https://www.eenewseurope.com/en/analog-inference-startup-raises-10-6-million/\n- https://www.cerebras.ai/company\n- https://privatecapitaljournal.com/astrus-secures-us-8m-seed-financing/\n- https://aeccafe.com/nbc/articles/1/1492727/Innovium-Raises-$38.3M-Oversubscribed-Series-C-Funding-Announces-Advisory-Board\n- https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2017/02/07/1052337/0/en/Fungible-Inc-Launches-with-32-5-Million-in-Financing-and-a-Plan-to-Revolutionize-Cloud-Data-Centers.html\n- https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/fungible-closes-200-million-series-c-financing-led-by-softbank-vision-fund-300875437.html\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/01/09/microsoft-acquires-fungible-a-maker-of-data-processing-units-to-bolster-azure/\n- https://blocksandfiles.com/2022/12/13/fungible-sold-to-microsoft-for-190-million/\n- https://itpro.com/business-strategy/mergers-and-acquisitions/369836/microsoft-fungible-fire-sale-dpu-technology\n- https://www.kleinerperkins.com/case-study/juniper-networks\n- https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/vinod-khosla\n- https://www.sdxcentral.com/articles/news/juniper-cto-pradeep-sindhu-stepping-aside-to-focus-on-his-startup/2017/02/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1043604/000119312517031856/d530517d8k.htm\n- https://iitk.ac.in/dora/chair/10/pradeep-sindhu-chair\n- https://www.roundfunded.com/en/investor/pradeep-sindhu-222508-1 (aggregator)\n- https://theorg.com/org/avicena-tech/org-chart/pradeep-sindhu (aggregator, unverified)\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-mints-two-billionaires-sets-stage-for-potential-ai-wave.html\n", "person:raghu-raghuram": "\n# Raghu Raghuram\n\n## One-line take\nThe ex-VMware CEO who joined a16z as a Managing Partner in 2025 and now co-signs nearly every a16z compute, inference and robotics deal (Unconventional AI, Inferact, Nexthop, Netris, Etched, Volta, Gimlet, Eka, Tutor). Too senior to be the owner's peer, but he is the a16z signature you need on a large compute or lab round, and his junior co-signers (Shangda Xu, Yoko Li, Stephenie Zhang) are the alpha.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He is an operator with 20 years running a data-center software company (VMware, 2003 to the Broadcom sale), so he can underwrite compute, networking and infra businesses at both seed (Unconventional, Inferact) and growth (Gimlet, Nexthop, Etched) from one seat. He sits on two pools of capital at once: he co-wrote the post announcing a16z Growth's $8.5B fifth fund ([src](https://a16z.com/expanding-the-a16z-growth-fund-and-platform/), 2026-08-31) and is a named author on the $1.1B Machine Age Fund for AI compute infrastructure ([src](https://a16z.com/the-machine-age-fund/), 2026-08-28).\n- **What constrains him:** He is a co-signer more than a solo lead. On every AI deal found, the memo has 2 to 4 authors. On the seed deals another GP fronts the byline (Bornstein on Unconventional and Inferact; Appenzeller led Netris and took the board seat per the [Netris release](https://netris.io/news/press-release/andreessen-horowitz-leads-netris-series-a-to-accelerate-adoption-of-gpu-network-automation-and-multi-tenancy-across-ai-cloud-operators-worldwide/)). He appears first on Volta, Eka, Tutor, Nexthop, Gimlet and the Etched essay (my reading of byline order, which may be firm convention, not a sourced fact). He also carries firm-management work (global expansion, the Japan office), which competes with deal time.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred, not stated):** Back a general-purpose chip or a \"me-too\" GPU cloud. Every compute memo argues the opposite: specialised silicon for inference (Etched, Unconventional), heterogeneous inference (Gimlet), networking as the bottleneck (Nexthop, Netris), and clouds built for startups rather than hyperscaler customers (Volta).\n\n## Differential insight\n*Update 2026-11-10 (second pass): photo URL re-checked (HTTP 200); round labels normalised; Nicira link and board-seat finding added. The web search budget ran out during this pass, so board seats and podcast transcripts were checked only against a16z and company pages.*\n- **The Casado link goes back to 2012, not 2025.** VMware announced it would buy Martin Casado's Nicira for $1.26B on 2012-07-23 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicira)). Raghuram had been at VMware since 2003, and his own a16z episode covers the Nicira deal and calls AI \"the biggest infrastructure reset since virtualization\" ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/raghu-raghuram-ai-robotics-and-the-rebirth-of-infrastructure/), 2025-10-27; page summary, not a transcript). The likeliest route into a16z is Casado, his VMware colleague of about four years. No source confirms that Casado recruited him or who reported to whom.\n- **He holds no board seat on any AI deal found.** That covers all 11 AI deals (12 rounds, counting Etched twice). Where a board seat is named, another GP holds it: Appenzeller on Netris ([src](https://netris.io/news/press-release/andreessen-horowitz-leads-netris-series-a-to-accelerate-adoption-of-gpu-network-automation-and-multi-tenancy-across-ai-cloud-operators-worldwide/)). He works as the senior sponsor across the infra and growth books, not as a board-level partner of record.\n- **Join date bounded by his own words.** In a 2026-06-04 post he wrote \"Since joining a16z last year, I've seen first-hand\" ([src](https://a16z.com/helping-our-portfolio-companies-expand-globally/)), so he joined in 2025. The earliest a16z appearance found is a 2025-08-08 podcast with Martin Casado and Cisco's Jeetu Patel, already titled Managing Partner ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/how-to-build-a-successful-company-in-an-era-of-disruption/)).\n- **Inference is his biggest thesis, in his own essay on Etched** (with Sarah Wang, 2026-07-23): \"Inference is the COGS of intelligence.\" and \"Tokens per watt is the real currency of inference.\" He also argued \"the general-purpose GPU wouldn't be the endgame of that market\" ([src](https://a16z.com/how-to-win-the-largest-market-in-ai/)).\n- **Etched timing.** The essay went up the same day Etched announced a $300M round at $10.3B led by Sequoia, with a16z as a participant ([src](https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference), 2026-07-23). Twenty-six days later a16z was again in the $700M round at $21B led by Jane Street ([src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one), 2026-08-18). a16z was a follower here, not first; tying him to these rounds is my inference from the essay byline.\n- **Inferact (vLLM) was a long-run open-source relationship before it was a seed.** The memo (with Bornstein and Jason Cui) says a16z made \"the first a16z open source AI grant\" to the vLLM team, hosted the first vLLM meetup, and has backed the project \"since 2023\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/), 2026-01-22). a16z then co-led the $150M seed at $800M with Lightspeed ([src](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference)). This is the clearest case of a16z being first, about two years ahead of the round.\n- **Neocloud for startups and neolabs.** Volta memo (with Shangda Xu): \"Volta is building the neocloud for Little Tech.\" and the reason: \"Most startups don't have financeable balance sheets: they're funded 18 months at a time.\" Volta builds \"for AI-native companies first and foremost\" including \"emerging neolabs\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-volta/), 2026-08-04). The lead group was crowded: Volta's launch release names Azora, a16z, Altimeter and NVIDIA as leaders of the seed and Series A combined, at a $2.4B valuation, alongside a $10B AI-factory deal with an unnamed AI lab ([src](https://volta.com/news/volta-launches-the-utility-of-compute/)).\n- **Power is the binding constraint.** Gimlet memo: \"We are running out of watts.\" and \"The future of inference is heterogeneous\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gimlet/), 2026-09-04). a16z led Gimlet's $300M Series B about 5.5 months after Menlo led the Series A ([src](https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-b)), so a16z was not first.\n- **Nexthop: a16z did not lead, despite the memo framing.** a16z's post calls the firm a major investor, but Nexthop's own release says Lightspeed led the $500M Series B at $4.2B, with a16z and Altimeter participating ([src](https://nexthop.ai/news-and-event/nexthop-ai-accelerates-into-hypergrowth-with-oversubscribed-500m-series-b-funding-catapulting-the-companys-valuation-to-4-2-billion/), 2026-03-10). Memo line: \"networking is the bottleneck between GPU capacity and GPU output\" ([src](https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop)). The founder, Anshul Sadana, spent 15+ years at Arista, a former data-center peer of VMware's (no personal link sourced).\n- **Netscape roots show up in robotics memos.** Tutor Intelligence memo (with Casado and Jamie Sullivan) quotes former Netscape CEO Jim Barksdale: \"the main thing is to keep the main thing the main thing.\" It also bets that \"product-focused teams will win in this market.\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-tutor-intelligence/), 2026-11-02). His a16z bio lists Netscape among his early product jobs ([src](https://a16z.com/author/raghu-raghuram/)).\n- **Robotics as a compute-scaling bet.** Eka memo (with Yoko Li): \"robot experience can increasingly scale with compute rather than human labor.\" a16z led the Series A ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-eka-robotics/), 2026-10-20).\n- **Hardware is the team's identity.** Machine Age Fund post: \"Hardware is in our team DNA, and it's great to get back to our roots.\" The post credits Raghuram and Casado with \"multiple decades in the data center space\" and says Shangda Xu and David George \"have led investments across the AI infrastructure stack\" ([src](https://a16z.com/the-machine-age-fund/), 2026-08-28). Read: Xu is the hands-on lead on the AI infra book.\n- **Global-expansion owner.** He writes that home-market-first expansion advice is \"no longer sufficient\", and that \"markets like Japan are hard\" while the UK is \"easy\" ([src](https://a16z.com/helping-our-portfolio-companies-expand-globally/), 2026-06-04). His X account announced a16z's Japan office under Hitoshi Yoshida (around 2026-10-11, relative timestamp on the [a16z author page](https://a16z.com/author/raghu-raghuram/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High on what he backs; low on accessibility as a peer.**\n- **For:** By the THESIS.md rubric he is high. He co-signed a $475M AI-chip seed (Unconventional AI, Naveen Rao), the vLLM inference seed (Inferact), and Series A rounds in robotics (Eka) and neoclouds (Volta). The Machine Age Fund ($1.1B) is a dedicated compute pool, so he is the a16z sponsor most likely to anchor a big compute or new-lab seed next to a $20M check from you.\n- **Against:** He is a Managing Partner, above the principal and newly promoted partner tier you are hunting. Many of his deals are growth-stage follow-ons where a16z was not first (Etched, Nexthop, Gimlet).\n- **Practical use:** Use him as the senior sign-off to map a16z compute decisions, and work through the juniors on his bylines: Shangda Xu (Volta, Nexthop, Gimlet, Vals), Yoko Li (Eka, Vals), Stephenie Zhang (Temporal, Gimlet) and Jamie Sullivan (Tutor). Volta is directly useful to you as a compute source for neolabs.\n\n## Background\n- MSc Physics, IIT Madras; MS Electrical Engineering, IIT Bombay; MBA, Wharton ([src](https://a16z.com/author/raghu-raghuram/)).\n- Product management at Netscape, AOL and Bang Networks (dates not found) ([src](https://a16z.com/author/raghu-raghuram/)).\n- Joined VMware in 2003 while it was still a startup ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/12/vmware-new-ceo-raghu-raghuram-pat-gelsinger/)). Roles included VP of Products, GM of the Software-Defined Datacenter business, and COO ([src](https://a16z.com/author/raghu-raghuram/)).\n- Named VMware CEO on 2021-05-12, succeeding Pat Gelsinger, and took over in June 2021 ([src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/12/vmware-new-ceo-raghu-raghuram-pat-gelsinger/)). Led the Dell spinout and the independent public company until Broadcom's $69B acquisition ([src](https://a16z.com/author/raghu-raghuram/)). The closing date was not fetched.\n- Joined a16z in 2025 as Managing Partner and GP on Growth and Infrastructure ([src](https://a16z.com/helping-our-portfolio-companies-expand-globally/)). A lateral senior hire, so there is no promotion path inside a16z. Note that Martin Casado, his a16z co-author, left VMware for a16z in April 2016 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VMware)), a likely link but no source says Casado recruited him.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/raghu-raghuram/ | Author page with all memos and podcasts (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop | a16z newsletter carries some memos |\n| X | https://x.com/RaghuRaghuram | Mostly reposts deal memos and firm news; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/raghuraghuram/ | Blocked to fetch; URL unverified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNo transcripts were available on any of these pages, so no verbatim quotes are taken from them.\n- 2025-08-08 — a16z podcast — How to Build a Successful Company in an Era of Disruption (with Martin Casado, Jeetu Patel) — https://a16z.com/podcast/how-to-build-a-successful-company-in-an-era-of-disruption/ — platform shifts from virtualization to AI (page summary only)\n- 2025-10-27 — a16z podcast — Raghu Raghuram: AI, Robotics, and the Rebirth of Infrastructure (Ben Horowitz, Casado, David George) — https://a16z.com/podcast/raghu-raghuram-ai-robotics-and-the-rebirth-of-infrastructure/ — no transcript\n- Undated — Building the Real-World Infrastructure for AI, with Google, Cisco & a16z (with Amin Vahdat, Jeetu Patel) — https://a16z.com/podcast/building-the-real-world-infrastructure-for-ai-with-google-cisco-a16z/ — not fetched\n- 2026-07-03 — a16z Goes Global: Why American Tech Must Lead the World (Horowitz, Anne Neuberger, Jen Kha) — https://a16z.com/podcast/a16z-goes-global-why-american-tech-must-lead-the-world/ — no transcript\n- 2026-08-28 — The Infrastructure Behind the Machine Age (Horowitz, Casado, Erik Torenberg) — https://a16z.com/podcast/the-infrastructure-behind-the-machine-age/ — summary only: the AI bottleneck is moving below the model layer\n\n## How they think\n- **Inference becomes the largest compute market** and moves off general-purpose GPUs, the way graphics and networking did (Etched essay).\n- **Power and networking are the bottlenecks**, not FLOPs (Gimlet, Nexthop, Netris).\n- **Startups need their own compute supply chain**, because they can't sign hyperscaler-style long-term contracts (Volta).\n- **Robotics data scales with deployment and compute** (Eka, Tutor).\n- **Growth companies must go multi-product, multi-channel, multi-geography at once** (Growth Fund V post, with David George).\n- **Calls so far:** Etched went from $10.3B to $21B in under a month after his essay. Temporal raised a $550M Series E at $12.55B after a16z led the Series D (his X repost, about 2026-09-18, relative timestamp). These are early marks, not outcomes.\n\n## Read these first\n1. How to Win the Largest Market in AI (with Sarah Wang, 2026-07-23) — his full inference thesis: https://a16z.com/how-to-win-the-largest-market-in-ai/\n2. The Machine Age Fund (2026-08-28) — the $1.1B compute fund and who runs what: https://a16z.com/the-machine-age-fund/\n3. Investing in Volta (2026-08-04) — neoclouds for startups and neolabs: https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-volta/\n4. Investing in Gimlet (2026-09-04) — power-limited heterogeneous inference: https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gimlet/\n5. Helping Our Portfolio Companies Expand Globally (2026-06-04) — his firm-building role: https://a16z.com/helping-our-portfolio-companies-expand-globally/\n\n## Social engagement\nFrom the three posts on his a16z author page (relative timestamps only, read 2026-11-10): reposts of his own memos with founder handles (Eka: @pulkitology, @haarnoja), firm news (Japan office opening with Governor Koike), and portfolio wins (Temporal Series E repost). No sign of original long-form posting. Amplification network not checked.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Unconventional AI | Seed ($475M) | 2025-12-08 | co-lead | Lightspeed (seed investor); Sequoia claims pre-seed/seed 2025 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/), [LSVP](https://lsvp.com/company/unconventional-ai/) |\n| Inferact (vLLM) | Seed ($150M at $800M) | 2026-01-22 | co-lead | Lightspeed (co-lead); Sequoia; Altimeter; Redpoint; ZhenFund; Databricks Ventures | [Inferact](https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference) |\n| Temporal | Series D | 2026-02-17 | yes | nothing named in post | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-temporal/) |\n| Nexthop AI | Series B ($500M at $4.2B) | 2026-03-10 | no | Lightspeed (lead); Altimeter; existing investors | [Nexthop](https://nexthop.ai/news-and-event/nexthop-ai-accelerates-into-hypergrowth-with-oversubscribed-500m-series-b-funding-catapulting-the-companys-valuation-to-4-2-billion/) |\n| Netris | Series A ($15M) | 2026-06-25 | yes (Appenzeller on board) | not named | [Netris](https://netris.io/news/press-release/andreessen-horowitz-leads-netris-series-a-to-accelerate-adoption-of-gpu-network-automation-and-multi-tenancy-across-ai-cloud-operators-worldwide/) |\n| Etched | Growth ($300M at $10.3B; round not named) | 2026-07-23 | no | Sequoia (lead); Jane Street; Diffusion; Argo; SK hynix | [Etched](https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference) (attribution to him inferred from essay) |\n| Volta | Seed + Series A ($2.4B val.) | 2026-08-04 | co-lead | Azora; Altimeter; NVIDIA (co-leads); Michael Dell family office; Matter Venture Partners | [Volta](https://volta.com/news/volta-launches-the-utility-of-compute/) |\n| Vals AI | Series A ($40M at $400M) | 2026-08-13 | yes | 8VC; Pear VC; Bloomberg Beta; HRT Ventures; Next Ladder | [Vals](https://www.vals.ai/blogs/series-a) |\n| Etched | Growth ($700M at $21B; round not named) | 2026-08-18 | no | Jane Street (lead); Sequoia; Kleiner Perkins; Thiel; Tiger; BCV; Stripes; Blackstone | [Etched](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one) (attribution inferred) |\n| Gimlet Labs | Series B ($300M) | 2026-09-04 | yes | Menlo; Sapphire; Eclipse; Factory; Arm; M12; Samsung; Tiger; XTX | [Gimlet](https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-b) |\n| Eka Robotics | Series A | 2026-10-20 | yes | not named | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-eka-robotics/) |\n| Tutor Intelligence | Series B ($75M) | 2026-11-02 | yes | not named | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-tutor-intelligence/), [Tutor](https://tutorintelligence.com/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Inferact:** a16z built the relationship through open-source grants and community events from 2023, then co-led the seed with Lightspeed in January 2026 ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/)). No source says whether Raghuram personally sourced it; Bornstein is first author.\n- **Etched:** The thesis essay and the Sequoia-led round came out the same day. a16z then followed into Jane Street's round a month later ([src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one)). How a16z first entered Etched was not found.\n- **Volta:** Founders Ricard Boada and Sofia Gumuzio built Brookfield's AI infrastructure platform. Volta absorbed the Genesis Cloud team ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-volta/)). Intro path not found.\n- Intro paths for the other deals: nothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Andreessen Horowitz | Managing Partner; GP Growth and Infra | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/helping-our-portfolio-companies-expand-globally/) |\n| VMware | Employee 2003 -> COO -> CEO (2021) | 2003 | [src](https://fortune.com/2021/05/12/vmware-new-ceo-raghu-raghuram-pat-gelsinger/) |\n| Shangda Xu | Co-author: Volta, Nexthop, Gimlet, Vals | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-volta/) |\n| Sarah Wang | Co-author: Etched essay, Temporal, Gimlet | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/how-to-win-the-largest-market-in-ai/) |\n| Martin Casado | VMware colleagues after VMware bought Casado's Nicira (2012); now co-author: Tutor, Machine Age Fund | 2012 | [src](https://a16z.com/the-machine-age-fund/) |\n| Guido Appenzeller | Co-author: Unconventional, Nexthop, Netris | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-netris/) |\n| Matt Bornstein | Co-author: Unconventional, Inferact | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/) |\n| Yoko Li | Co-author: Eka, Vals | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-eka-robotics/) |\n| Stephenie Zhang | Co-author: Temporal, Gimlet | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-temporal/) |\n| Jamie Sullivan | Co-author: Tutor Intelligence | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-tutor-intelligence/) |\n| Jason Cui | Co-author: Inferact, Netris | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-netris/) |\n| David George | Co-author: Growth Fund V post | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/expanding-the-a16z-growth-fund-and-platform/) |\n| Anne Neuberger; Jen Kha | Global expansion work | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/helping-our-portfolio-companies-expand-globally/) |\n| Hitoshi Yoshida | Head of a16z Japan (announced by Raghuram on X) | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/author/raghu-raghuram/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact a16z start date and whether Casado recruited him.\n- Does he hold any board seats? None was found on 11 AI deals (Appenzeller holds Netris). Check the Eka, Volta and Tutor filings or company team pages.\n- Did Casado report to him at VMware after Nicira, and did Casado bring him to a16z?\n- Who led Unconventional's seed with a16z (Lightspeed is listed as a seed investor without lead status), and did Sequoia's pre-seed come first?\n- Volta's lead list: the volta.com homepage says a16z and Altimeter co-led; the launch release names four leaders.\n- Gimlet and Vals: is he the partner of record or a sponsor for Xu?\n- The 2025-10-27 podcast likely has his own account of why he joined; get the YouTube transcript.\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/raghu-raghuram/\n- https://a16z.com/helping-our-portfolio-companies-expand-globally/\n- https://a16z.com/how-to-win-the-largest-market-in-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/the-machine-age-fund/\n- https://a16z.com/expanding-the-a16z-growth-fund-and-platform/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-unconventional/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-temporal/\n- https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-netris/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-volta/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-vals/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gimlet/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-eka-robotics/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-tutor-intelligence/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/how-to-build-a-successful-company-in-an-era-of-disruption/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/raghu-raghuram-ai-robotics-and-the-rebirth-of-infrastructure/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/the-infrastructure-behind-the-machine-age/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/a16z-goes-global-why-american-tech-must-lead-the-world/\n- https://fortune.com/2021/05/12/vmware-new-ceo-raghu-raghuram-pat-gelsinger/\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VMware\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicira\n- https://inferact.ai/news/introducing-inferact-building-the-future-of-ai-inference\n- https://nexthop.ai/news-and-event/nexthop-ai-accelerates-into-hypergrowth-with-oversubscribed-500m-series-b-funding-catapulting-the-companys-valuation-to-4-2-billion/\n- https://netris.io/news/press-release/andreessen-horowitz-leads-netris-series-a-to-accelerate-adoption-of-gpu-network-automation-and-multi-tenancy-across-ai-cloud-operators-worldwide/\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one\n- https://volta.com/ and https://volta.com/news/volta-launches-the-utility-of-compute/\n- https://www.vals.ai/blogs/series-a\n- https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-b\n- https://tutorintelligence.com/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/unconventional-ai/\n", "person:rajko-radovanovic": "\n# Rajko Radovanovic\n\n## One-line take\nHe was the junior constant on a16z's 2023-24 frontier-model run (Mistral A, Luma B, Black Forest Labs seed, Cursor A), and he co-built a16z's open-source AI grant program, which turned out to be a sourcing funnel: its first grant went to the vLLM team that a16z later backed as Inferact.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins (or won).** He sat on two engines at once. One was Anjney Midha's frontier-lab deal team (Mistral, Luma, BFL) ([Mistral](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/); [Luma](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-luma-ai/); [BFL](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/)). The other was Matt Bornstein's developer and open-source track (Emerging Architectures, Cursor, the grants) ([src](https://a16z.com/emerging-architectures-for-llm-applications/); [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/)). The firm bio says he \"incubated and helps run\" the Open Source AI Grant Program ([src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/), author schema). That program put a16z money and goodwill in front of the vLLM, LMSys/SGLang, Nous and Axolotl maintainers years before they became companies.\n- **What constrains him.** He was never the named lead. Bornstein is credited as \"lead on Cursor\" ([src](https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/), 2026-01-21), and Midha is first author on the three lab deals. His $10M+ access came from the a16z platform. If he has left (see below), that access may have gone with the platform, as it did for Midha and Marco Mascorro in this repo.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred, not stated).** Back a closed-only model thesis. Every post he co-signed argues for openness: \"AI should be open\" (Mistral), BFL's \"permissive licenses\", and grants explicitly \"not an investment or SAFE note\".\n- **Status is the open question.** He is absent from the full ~575-name a16z roster ([team page](https://a16z.com/team/), checked 2026-11-10), his author archive renders a generic page (as Mascorro's does, and Mascorro has left), and his last a16z byline was 2025-06-26. His GitHub profile still reads \"AI/Infra at Andreessen Horowitz\", company \"a16z\", and was updated 2026-11-02 ([src](https://api.github.com/users/rajko-rad)). Self-reported bios go stale, so this stays needs-id.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Update 2026-11-10: present at entry, absent from every re-up.** a16z followed on in Mistral (Series B 2024-06, C 2025-09, D 2026-09), Cursor (B 2025-01, C 2025-06, D 2025-11), Luma (Series C 2025-11-19, Humain-led, [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/11/19/luma-ai-raises-900-million-in-funding-led-by-saudi-ai-firm-humain.html)) and BFL (Series B 2025-12-01). His name appears on none of them; Midha and Casado carry the follow-ons (repo `data/rounds.csv`). He doubled down only in the sense that the firm did. The relationship owner was someone else.\n- **Luma is his most concentrated byline.** Only two authors (Midha and him), with \"We're excited to lead Luma AI's Series B round\" and \"work even closer with this world-class team\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-luma-ai/), 2024-01-09). \"Even closer\" suggests a relationship before the B. TechCrunch, by contrast, describes a16z only as \"participation\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/01/09/luma-raises-43m-to-build-ai-that-crafts-3d-models/)), so the lead claim rests on a16z's own post.\n- **The grant program fed the deal flow (inference from two a16z sources).** He co-authored the first grants post (2023-08-30), whose recipients include Woosuk Kwon and Zhuohan Li of vLLM ([src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/)). The 2026-01-22 Inferact post says \"the first a16z open source AI grant was made to the vLLM team\" and that a16z is leading Inferact's seed ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/)). His name is not on the Inferact byline (Bornstein, Jason Cui, Raghu Raghuram), so whether he had a hand in the deal is unknown.\n- **Repeat grantees became an a16z pattern.** The LMSys team (Lianmin Zheng, Wei-Lin Chiang, Ying Sheng) got a grant in the 2023-12-13 batch, described there as behind \"Chatbot Arena\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcing-our-latest-open-source-ai-grants/)). Zheng and Sheng got a second grant for SGLang in the 2025-06-26 batch, which he also co-authored ([src](https://a16z.com/advancing-open-source-ai-through-benchmarks-and-bold-experimentation/)). a16z (Midha) was a \"founding\" investor in LMArena's seed on 2025-05-28 ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-lmarena-the-reliability-layer-for-ai/)). That post says only that it \"began as a Berkeley research project\" and does not tie it to LMSys or the grant. The link is my inference.\n- **Grant terms were a deliberate no-strings wedge.** Grants are \"not an investment or SAFE note\" and let recipients work \"without the pressure to generate financial returns\" ([src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/), 2023-08-30). By the third batch (2025-06-26), the target was work \"outside of the major labs\": hackers, benchmark builders (SWE-bench, ARC Prize) and agent artists (Truth Terminal, Pliny, Janus) ([src](https://a16z.com/advancing-open-source-ai-through-benchmarks-and-bold-experimentation/)).\n- **His early framing of LLM apps.** The June 2023 piece he co-wrote with Bornstein, which became the \"LLM app stack\" diagram many people copied, says retrieval \"reduces an AI problem to a data engineering problem\" and that \"agents don't really work yet\" ([src](https://a16z.com/emerging-architectures-for-llm-applications/), 2023-06-20).\n- **Hands-on tinkerer.** His public GitHub forks include WizardLM (2023-06), a reverse-engineered Ideogram API wrapper (2023-09), gpt-migrate and gpt-engineer ([src](https://api.github.com/users/rajko-rad/repos)). He tries the tools himself, not just the decks.\n- **Bylined vs bio-claimed deals differ.** His bio also claims Udio, World Labs and Braintrust ([src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/), author schema), but a16z's posts on World Labs (Casado, Sarah Wang) and Braintrust (Casado) don't carry his name, and no a16z Udio post exists in the sitemap. Treat those as team-member involvement, not lead credit.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, with a status caveat.** He worked on exactly the deals I care about: the Mistral Series A (frontier lab, a16z-led EUR385M), Black Forest Labs (a16z led the $31M seed, the first institution in) and Cursor (a16z's Series A, a $60M round). The grant program is the most transferable edge for my lens: it gets you to pre-company research and infra talent (vLLM, SGLang, Nous) before there's a round. As a principal-type partner rather than a GP, he fits the \"alpha in principals\" thesis. The caveat: if he has left a16z, his check capacity depends on where he went (AMP PBC is the obvious hypothesis, given Midha and the BFL overlap, but nothing sources it).\n\n## Background\n- Harvard, degree in Economics and Computer Science; Teaching Fellow for Advanced Mathematical Optimization ([src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/), a16z author schema). Years not given.\n- Boston Consulting Group: \"enterprise technology and consulting\" (same source). Dates not given.\n- NEA: \"venture investor\", working with Perplexity AI, Sentry, Metabase, Weaviate and TimescaleDB (same source). Title and dates are unsourced; the task context notes an aggregator also lists NEA.\n- a16z infra team: the author headshot was uploaded 2023-04 (from the image URL path; an inference about join timing, not a date the firm published). First byline 2023-06-20. Last byline 2025-06-26 ([sitemap sweep of 522 a16z posts](https://a16z.com/sitemap_index.xml), 2026-11-10).\n- Public GitHub forks include a Serbian-language app for registering to vote abroad (2026-09) ([src](https://api.github.com/users/rajko-rad/repos)). That suggests Serbian roots; not otherwise sourced.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | [a16z author page](https://a16z.com/author/rajko-radovanovic/) | Now renders a generic page (2026-11-10); posts reachable individually |\n| X | [@rajko_rad](https://x.com/rajko_rad) | Handle from GitHub profile; content not fetched |\n| LinkedIn | nothing fetched | |\n| GitHub | [rajko-rad](https://github.com/rajko-rad) | 63 followers, 8 public repos (all forks), profile updated 2026-11-02 |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. A scan of 613 a16z podcast pages had no mention of him (2026-11-10). Web search budget was exhausted, so outside podcasts were not checked.\n\n## How they think\n- **Open models as infrastructure.** He co-signed \"AI should be open\" and \"Communities build cheaper, faster, and safer software infrastructure together\" (Mistral, 2023-12). On BFL: \"Visual AI has a supply chain problem\", and BFL is \"focusing exclusively on the first mile\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/)).\n- **Product craft over model access in apps.** On Cursor: \"Cursor, distinctly among AI coding tools, has simply gotten it right\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/)).\n- **Generative media as the next mainstream modality.** On Luma: \"generative 3D models are on the cusp of their own breakthrough\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-luma-ai/)).\n- **Calls right.** Cursor (bought at a $400M valuation; ~$60B SpaceX exit per the repo's matt-bornstein rows), Mistral, BFL. **Calls wrong:** none found. His 2023 claim that \"agents don't really work yet\" was right at the time.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Emerging Architectures for LLM Applications](https://a16z.com/emerging-architectures-for-llm-applications/) (2023-06-20): his technical worldview.\n2. [Supporting the Open Source AI Community](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/) (2023-08-30): the grant-program design.\n3. [Investing in Black Forest Labs](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/) (2024-08-01): the frontier-lab seed thesis.\n4. [Advancing open source AI through benchmarks](https://a16z.com/advancing-open-source-ai-through-benchmarks-and-bold-experimentation/) (2025-06-26): his last byline; shows where the grant net was cast.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing fetched from X (search budget exhausted). GitHub shows he forks and tries LLM tools (WizardLM, gpt-engineer, uv).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mistral AI | Series A (EUR385M) | 2023-12-11 | yes (a16z) | Lightspeed; General Catalyst; Salesforce; BNP Paribas; Elad Gil; Conviction | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/); [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2023/12/11/mistral-ai-a-paris-based-openai-rival-closed-its-415-million-funding-round/) |\n| Luma AI | Series B ($43M) | 2024-01-09 | yes (a16z \"excited to lead\") | not named (prior: Amplify-led A 2023-03) | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-luma-ai/); [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/01/09/luma-raises-43m-to-build-ai-that-crafts-3d-models/) |\n| Black Forest Labs | Seed ($31M) | 2024-08-01 | yes (a16z) | General Catalyst, MätchVC (follow-ups); angels Brendan Iribe, Garry Tan, Timo Aila, Vladlen Koltun, Michael Ovitz | [BFL](https://bfl.ai/announcements/24-08-01-bfl); [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/) |\n| Cursor (Anysphere) | Series A ($60M at $400M) | 2024-08-22 | yes (a16z; Bornstein credited as lead) | Thrive Capital; OpenAI; Jeff Dean; Noam Brown | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/); [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/in-just-4-months-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-raised-another-100m-at-a-2-5b-valuation-led-by-thrive-sources-say) |\n| Udio | Undisclosed | unknown | unknown | | bio only ([src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/)) |\n| World Labs | Seed ($230M across two rounds, first 2024-04) | 2024-09-13 | unknown | NEA; Radical | bio only; a16z post by Casado/Wang ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-world-labs/)) |\n| Braintrust | Series A ($36M) | 2024-10-08 | yes (a16z, Casado) | Greylock; Elad Gil | bio only; a16z post by Casado ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-braintrust/)); [company](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/announcing-series-a) |\n| vLLM (grant, not equity) | Strategic (Open Source AI Grant) | 2023-08-30 | n/a | | [src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/) |\n\n**Timing (were they first?)**\n- **BFL: yes.** a16z led the $31M seed on launch day, and the company calls GC and MätchVC \"follow-up\" investors ([src](https://bfl.ai/announcements/24-08-01-bfl)). It is the cleanest first-in on his record.\n- **Mistral: no.** Lightspeed led the EUR105M seed in 2023-06, about six months earlier ([src](https://siliconangle.com/2023/06/13/paris-based-startup-mistral-ai-raises-113m-seed-funding/)).\n- **Cursor: no.** The repo's matt-bornstein rows record an earlier OpenAI Startup Fund-led seed (2023-10), about 10 months before a16z. Not re-verified here.\n- **Luma: no.** Amplify Partners partnered in 2021 and led the $20M Series A in 2023-03 with NVentures and General Catalyst ([Radiance Fields, secondary](https://radiancefields.com/luma-ai-announces-20m-series-a-round)), about 10 months before a16z's Series B.\n- **BFL round label.** The a16z post calls the $31M round a seed; BFL's Dec 2025 Series B release refers to an a16z-led \"previously unannounced Series A\" ([src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier)). Both labels are recorded.\n- **Cursor lead attribution.** a16z credits Bornstein as internal lead ([src](https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/)); press and repo rounds rows tie the board-facing role to Casado. These are compatible.\n- **Did he double down? No, personally.** See the update bullet under Differential insight.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **BFL:** The memo has four authors (Midha, Mascorro, Radovanovic, Moore). Midha is first author and later co-led BFL's Series B from AMP PBC (2025-12-01), per the repo's anjney-midha rows. Radovanovic's role beyond the byline is unknown.\n- **Cursor:** Casado's 2026 promotion post credits Bornstein as lead ([src](https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/)). Radovanovic is the third of four co-signers.\n- **Inferact (vLLM):** a16z's relationship started with the first grant in 2023, which he co-ran, and reached a seed lead on 2026-01-22. He is not on that byline.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Anjney Midha | Co-author on Mistral, Luma, BFL | 2023-12 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/) |\n| Matt Bornstein | Co-author on LLM architectures, all three grant batches, Mistral, Cursor | 2023-06 | [src](https://a16z.com/emerging-architectures-for-llm-applications/) |\n| Marco Mascorro | Co-author on BFL, Cursor | 2024-08 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/) |\n| Martin Casado | Co-author on Cursor (infra GP) | 2024-08 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/) |\n| Justine Moore | Co-author on BFL | 2024-08 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/) |\n| NEA | Former employer | unknown | [src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/) |\n| Boston Consulting Group | Former employer | unknown | same |\n| vLLM team (Woosuk Kwon, Zhuohan Li) | Grant recipients in his first batch | 2023-08 | [src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/) |\n| LMSys / SGLang (Lianmin Zheng, Ying Sheng) | Grant recipients twice | 2023-12 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcing-our-latest-open-source-ai-grants/) |\n| Nous Research | Grant recipient, first batch | 2023-08 | [src](https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Update 2026-11-10: GitHub API re-checked live (still \"AI/Infra at Andreessen Horowitz\"); X profile fetch blocked (HTTP 402); web search budget exhausted this run, so LinkedIn and outside podcasts remain unchecked. LP links: nothing found.\n- Is he still at a16z? He's off the roster with no byline since 2025-06-26, but GitHub still says a16z. If he left, where did he go: AMP PBC (Midha), a lab, or a fund of his own? Check LinkedIn and X @rajko_rad.\n- What was his role in the Inferact seed and the LMArena seed, given his grant-program ties to both teams?\n- When did he join NEA and a16z, under what titles, and was \"investing partner\" a promotion inside a16z?\n- What was his role on Udio, World Labs and Braintrust (claimed in his bio, absent from the bylines)?\n- Did any other grantees (Nous Research, Axolotl, SkyPilot, Ostris, Open WebUI) later raise from a16z?\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/supporting-the-open-source-ai-community/ (2023-08-30; author bio in page schema)\n- https://a16z.com/announcing-our-latest-open-source-ai-grants/ (2023-12-13)\n- https://a16z.com/advancing-open-source-ai-through-benchmarks-and-bold-experimentation/ (2025-06-26)\n- https://a16z.com/emerging-architectures-for-llm-applications/ (2023-06-20)\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mistral/ (2023-12-11)\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-luma-ai/ (2024-01-09)\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-black-forest-labs/ (2024-08-01)\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-cursor/ (2024-08-22)\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-inferact/ (2026-01-22)\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-lmarena-the-reliability-layer-for-ai/ (2025-05-28)\n- https://a16z.com/matt-bornstein/ (2026-01-21)\n- https://a16z.com/team/ (checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://bfl.ai/announcements/24-08-01-bfl\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/12/11/mistral-ai-a-paris-based-openai-rival-closed-its-415-million-funding-round/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/01/09/luma-raises-43m-to-build-ai-that-crafts-3d-models/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/in-just-4-months-ai-coding-assistant-cursor-raised-another-100m-at-a-2-5b-valuation-led-by-thrive-sources-say\n- https://radiancefields.com/luma-ai-announces-20m-series-a-round (secondary)\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2025/11/19/luma-ai-raises-900-million-in-funding-led-by-saudi-ai-firm-humain.html\n- https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier\n- https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/announcing-series-a\n- https://siliconangle.com/2023/06/13/paris-based-startup-mistral-ai-raises-113m-seed-funding/\n- https://api.github.com/users/rajko-rad and https://api.github.com/users/rajko-rad/repos\n", "person:rak-garg": "\n# Rak Garg\n\n## One-line take\nRak Garg went from BCV Associate to Partner in about 2.6 years. He built BCV Labs, the firm's Palo Alto incubator, and is on its two most frontier-relevant bets: poolside (seed, then the ~$500M Series B) and Oratomic (fault-tolerant quantum, 2026). In 2026 his stated mandate moved quietly from \"AI incubations\" toward defense and \"national importance\".\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he runs an incubation machine that gets BCV in before the first financing. BCV Labs says it shapes ideas with founders \"before any funds are raised\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/our-first-year-in-review-bcv-labs/)). Contextual AI and Echelon AI were both incubated there, and BCV led Contextual's $20M seed ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/), [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-new-echelon-of-it-implementations-and-ai-powered-services/)).\n- **Second edge:** a dense Atlassian-alumni and security-buyer network. Prophet Security and Index both trace to people he worked with at Atlassian (see Deal stories).\n- **What constrains him:** his own portfolio is mostly enterprise AI applications and security, such as Norm Ai, Prophet, Straiker, Nebulock and Cogent. These are not labs. On the one lab in his portfolio, poolside, BCV followed in at the seed and only took a lead role at the Series B. He shares credit on every BCV CMS record with another partner (Enrique Salem, Aaref Hilaly or Slater Stich).\n- **What he'd never do:** back a founder with a \"butts in seats\" hiring mindset. In his words, every such startup \"has failed\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The 2026 mandate shift.** BCV's CMS team record (updated 2026-09-16) now gives his short bio as founders \"at the frontier of cyber-security, space, defense, industrial capacity, and other areas of national importance\". The SEO description on the same record still says \"infrastructure software, cybersecurity and AI incubations\" ([src](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22team%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22rak-garg%22%5D%7Bshort_bio%2CseoMetadata%2C_updatedAt%7D)). His 2026 deals fit the new mandate: Mind Robotics (industrial robotics) and Oratomic (fault-tolerant quantum).\n- **He passed on leading a seed and expects to pay up for the A.** On a team BCV had \"known for years,\" he wrote: \"In the end, we passed on leading the seed.\" He added that BCV \"may end up paying a premium for the A round\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/), 2025-12-18). The company is not named.\n- **An early hardware call (2023-02).** He predicted \"New hardware architectures emerge for foundation models.\" He singled out model parallelism that maps layers \"on a large on-chip SRAM array\", which is the Cerebras and Groq design ([src](https://rakgarg.substack.com/p/five-predictions-for-ai-in-2023), mirrored at [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/foundation-models-and-five-predictions-for-ai-in-2023/)). He has no chip deal on record.\n- **Contextual AI was decided fast.** BCV met Douwe Kiela and Amanpreet Singh \"early this year\" (2023), through their RAG research that it had seen \"on the desks of CTOs\". The post says: \"Within days, we'd agreed to partner and lead their company's seed round\". The founders then worked out of BCV Labs ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/), 2023-06-07). BCV's CMS now tags Contextual AI as \"Acquired\". The acquirer was not found.\n- **Talent thesis for labs (2024-10).** He argues that money alone no longer moves researchers and that \"mission alignment\" is the new axis, \"especially true for post-training talent.\" He co-wrote this with Daniel LaBruna ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-next-phase-of-competition-in-ai/)).\n- **How he got into venture.** He came to BCV through Kevin Zhang, then a BCV Partner, whom he calls \"a long-time mentor\". A mutual friend introduced them over product-led growth. He joined in June 2021 ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/get-to-know-a-bcv-associate-rak-garg/)).\n- **Echelon was an incubation built from portfolio alumni.** Echelon's founders met at Moveworks, \"where our partner Enrique led the Series A\". BCV \"invited them to incubate Echelon with us at BCV Labs\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-new-echelon-of-it-implementations-and-ai-powered-services/)). This shows BCV Labs recycling talent from its own portfolio companies.\n- **His Substack has been dormant since 2023-12.** \"Rak's Facts\" last posted \"Identity Crisis: The Biggest Prize in Security\" on 2023-12-18 ([src](https://rakgarg.substack.com/api/v1/posts?limit=20)). Since then his writing has moved to BCV posts co-written with other partners.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, with a caveat.**\n- **For:** he is a fast-promoted partner at a firm that can write growth checks and led poolside's ~$500M Series B ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/09/12/ai-startup-poolside-3-billion-valuation-bain-capital-ventures/)). He runs an incubator that gets in before round one. He also wrote early on SRAM and model-parallel chip architectures and on post-training talent. Oratomic is a 2026 frontier-hardware bet whose team page lists John Preskill, Manuel Endres, Dolev Bluvstein and Hsin-Yuan Huang ([src](https://www.oratomic.com/)).\n- **Caveat:** most of his deal count is enterprise AI applications and security, not labs. On poolside, BCV was not first; it was \"an existing investor\" from the seed era ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/09/12/ai-startup-poolside-3-billion-valuation-bain-capital-ventures/)). His 2026 bio points toward defense and industrial work rather than pre-training.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** poolside, Oratomic, Contextual AI, Mind Robotics, Krea, and Echelon AI (AI-native services automation, close to the Serval pattern).\n- **Use him as:** a co-investor on large frontier seeds and a source of BCV Labs incubation deal flow.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Sunnyvale. His father started at DEC as a support engineer. As a teenager he spent his time on game modding, Kali, IRC and Phrack ([src](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22team%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22rak-garg%22%5D%7Bshort_bio%2CseoMetadata%2C_updatedAt%7D)). This is the \"about\" field of the BCV CMS team record.\n- UCLA, computer science ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/get-to-know-a-bcv-associate-rak-garg/)). He took a leave in his junior year to be a product manager at Redfin around its IPO (same source).\n- Atlassian: built and led security products ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/rak-garg/)). He led security products there in 2017, during the Skyhigh Networks partnership ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/prophet-security-is-using-ai-to-cut-mean-time-to-response-by-10x-for-security-operations-teams/)).\n- BCV, joined June 2021 as an Associate ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/get-to-know-a-bcv-associate-rak-garg/), 2021-11-08).\n- Founded BCV Labs, announced 2023-11-16 ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-labs-is-an-ai-incubator-and-technical-community-in-silicon-valley/)).\n- Promoted to Partner on 2024-01-10, after \"just about three years\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rak-garg-promoted-to-partner/)). Any intermediate title such as Vice President or Principal was not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://baincapitalventures.com/team/rak-garg/ | Firm bio; email rgarg@baincapital.com is listed publicly |\n| Substack / blog | https://rakgarg.substack.com | \"Rak's Facts\"; 11 posts 2021-01 to 2023-12; dormant since |\n| X | https://twitter.com/rak_garg | Handle from BCV CMS; fetch blocked (HTTP 402), follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/rakgarg/ | From BCV CMS; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Web-search budget exhausted this session |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-03 — BCV video — Cogent: \"Building autonomous AI defenders for enterprise security\" — https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/video-cogent-building-autonomous-ai-defenders-for-enterprise-security/\n- 2026-10 — BCV video — Echelon: building an AI workforce for enterprise IT. He and Rahul Kayala frame Echelon as a possible \"$100 billion transformation company\" — https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/video-echelon-is-building-an-ai-workforce-for-enterprise-it/\n- External podcasts: nothing found. The web-search budget ran out before podcast search.\n\n## How they think\n- **Inference is memory-bound.** Co-written with Daniel LaBruna (2024-10-24): \"inference is almost always memory bound\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/what-s-next-in-ai-inference/)).\n- **Competition axes.** Once benchmarks level off, AI competition moves to compute, talent, data and distribution. He sees organic alignment data as the next moat ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-next-phase-of-competition-in-ai/)).\n- **AI cyberwar.** His 2026 prediction is that adversaries will use AI against semiconductors, energy and rare-earths firms, which he calls \"a clarion call\" for offensive and defensive security products ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/)).\n- **Founder taste.** He favours founders from \"unlikely backgrounds\" with \"chips on their shoulders\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/rak-garg/)).\n- **Calls:**\n  - Right: the 2023 SRAM and model-parallel hardware prediction, early RAG (Contextual AI), and poolside before its $3B valuation.\n  - Self-flagged possible miss: the unnamed 2025 seed he passed on.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Five Predictions for AI in 2023\" (2023-02-01). His early hardware and LLM-middleware calls — https://rakgarg.substack.com/p/five-predictions-for-ai-in-2023\n2. \"The Next Phase of Competition in AI\" (2024-10-16). His views on talent, mission alignment and alignment data — https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-next-phase-of-competition-in-ai/\n3. \"Bringing Context to Enterprise AI\" (2023-06-07). How an incubated lead happens — https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/\n4. BCV 2025 year-end Q&A (2025-12-18). The seed he passed on and his hiring heuristic — https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/\n5. \"BCV Labs is an AI incubator\" (2023-11-16). The incubator's terms (\"small pre-seeds to larger, priced seed rounds\") — https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-labs-is-an-ai-incubator-and-technical-community-in-silicon-valley/\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: X could not be fetched (HTTP 402). His co-authors on BCV posts are a proxy for his circle: Enrique Salem, Aaref Hilaly, Saanya Ojha, Slater Stich, Daniel LaBruna and William Eleazer.\n\n## Portfolio\nAll deals are from BCV's CMS records naming him as partner ([src](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26references%28%22ndF450ZgZJsxSk7ku186I5%22%29%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Ename%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Eslug.current%2C_createdAt%7D)), plus the deal posts cited.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| poolside (AI lab, coding) | Seed (BCV CMS) | 2023 | Not by BCV; lead not found in sources reached | Co-partners Enrique Salem, Saanya Ojha | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/poolside-is-scaling-a-team-to-help-billions-code-and-realize-agi/) |\n| poolside | Series B (~$500M, $3B valuation) | 2024-10-02 | BCV \"in talks to lead\" as an existing investor; Bloomberg headline says \"with Bain, DST\" | DST Global, eBay (per Wikipedia citing ZDNet) | [src](https://fortune.com/2024/09/12/ai-startup-poolside-3-billion-valuation-bain-capital-ventures/) |\n| Contextual AI | Seed ($20M) | 2023-06-07 | Yes (BCV led; incubated at BCV Labs) | Lightspeed, Greycroft, Elad Gil, Lip-Bu Tan, Sarah Guo; co-partner Aaref Hilaly | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/) |\n| Unstructured | Seed, then Series A | seed before 2023-07; A 2023-07-27 | Seed partner; A participant | Madrona, Shield, M12, LangChain, Weaviate; co-partner Enrique Salem | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/how-unstructured-is-powering-the-llm-data-stack/) |\n| Oratomic (fault-tolerant quantum) | Early (BCV CMS) | 2026 | Unknown | Co-partner Slater Stich | [src](https://www.oratomic.com/) |\n| Mind Robotics (industrial robotics) | Early (BCV CMS) | 2026 | Unknown | Co-partner Ajay Agarwal; Rivian is \"first manufacturing partner\" | [src](https://www.mindrobotics.com/) |\n| Krea | Series B (BCV CMS \"Growth\") | 2025-04-07 | BCV named for the B | Co-partner Aaref Hilaly; a16z led the 2023 A | [src](https://www.krea.ai/blog/new-krea) |\n| Echelon AI | Seed (incubated at BCV Labs) | 2025-10-09 | First in (incubation) | Co-partner Aaref Hilaly | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-new-echelon-of-it-implementations-and-ai-powered-services/) |\n| Structify | Seed | 2025 | Yes (BCV led) | Co-partner Slater Stich | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/structify-makes-building-custom-datasets-easy-with-ai/) |\n| Prophet Security | Seed ($11M) | 2024-04-23 | Yes | Security angels; co-partner Enrique Salem | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/prophet-security-is-using-ai-to-cut-mean-time-to-response-by-10x-for-security-operations-teams/) |\n| Norm Ai | Series A ($27M) | 2024-06-25 | No (participant) | Coatue, Blackstone, Citi, New York Life, TIAA; co-partners Aaref Hilaly, Matt Harris | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/norm-ai-is-using-ai-to-clean-up-the-sludge-of-regulatory-compliance/) |\n| Index | Seed | 2025-02-11 | Unknown | Founder is his ex-Atlassian colleague Simon Kubica | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/index-brings-product-planning-into-the-ai-age/) |\n| Nebulock | Seed | 2025-07-29 | Unknown | Co-partner Enrique Salem | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/proactive-by-default-how-nebulock-rethinks-threat-hunting/) |\n| Cogent Security | Series A | 2026-02-18 | Unknown (\"partnering with them\") | Co-partner Enrique Salem | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-more-cogent-approach-to-vulnerability-management/) |\n| Straiker (AI security) | Early (BCV CMS) | 2024 | Unknown | Co-partner Enrique Salem | [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26references%28%22ndF450ZgZJsxSk7ku186I5%22%29%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Ename%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Eslug.current%2C_createdAt%7D) |\n| VISO Trust (acquired by Protecht) | Early | 2021 | Unknown | Co-partner Enrique Salem | [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26references%28%22ndF450ZgZJsxSk7ku186I5%22%29%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Ename%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Eslug.current%2C_createdAt%7D) |\n\nThe promotion post also credits him with board-level work at Cleanlab and Momento ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rak-garg-promoted-to-partner/)).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Contextual AI (2023).**\n  - BCV first noticed the founders' RAG and hallucination research on CTOs' desks and met them in early 2023.\n  - It agreed to lead the $20M seed \"within days\".\n  - The team then worked \"shoulder-to-shoulder\" in BCV Labs, Palo Alto ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/)).\n- **poolside.** BCV was in at the seed, which Fortune reports as $126M in total. By 2024-09 it was \"an existing investor\" in talks to lead the ~$500M round at a $3B valuation ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/09/12/ai-startup-poolside-3-billion-valuation-bain-capital-ventures/)). BCV's post says it saw product-market fit \"in our early conversations\" with Jason Warner and Eiso Kant ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/poolside-is-scaling-a-team-to-help-billions-code-and-realize-agi/)).\n- **Prophet Security (2024).**\n  - Rak led security products at Atlassian when it partnered with Skyhigh Networks in 2017, where Kamal Shah led products.\n  - BCV later led Prophet's $11M seed ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/prophet-security-is-using-ai-to-cut-mean-time-to-response-by-10x-for-security-operations-teams/)).\n  - The source says Shah is a co-founder.\n- **Index (2025).** \"I worked with Simon at Atlassian, where both of us led product teams\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/index-brings-product-planning-into-the-ai-age/)).\n- **Echelon AI (2025).** The founders came out of Moveworks, an Enrique Salem Series A. BCV met them \"at the earliest stages\" and incubated them at BCV Labs ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-new-echelon-of-it-implementations-and-ai-powered-services/)).\n- **Krea (2025).** He and Aaref Hilaly met the founders on bean bags in their Pacific Heights office-home, then several more times that week ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-powerful-new-platform-for-kreatives/)). BCV came in at the Series B, after HF0, A*, Gradient, Pebblebed and the a16z-led A ([src](https://www.krea.ai/blog/new-krea)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bain Capital Ventures | Associate, then Partner; founded BCV Labs | 2021-06 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/get-to-know-a-bcv-associate-rak-garg/) |\n| Kevin Zhang | Mentor; his route into BCV | before 2021 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/get-to-know-a-bcv-associate-rak-garg/) |\n| Enrique Salem | Most frequent co-partner (poolside, Unstructured, Prophet, Straiker, Nebulock, Cogent, VISO) and Atlassian board link | 2021 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/get-to-know-a-bcv-associate-rak-garg/) |\n| Aaref Hilaly | Co-partner (Contextual AI, Krea, Norm Ai, Echelon, Index) | 2023 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/) |\n| Slater Stich | Co-partner (Oratomic, Structify); BCV Labs co-author | 2023 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-labs-is-an-ai-incubator-and-technical-community-in-silicon-valley/) |\n| Saanya Ojha | Co-partner (poolside) | 2023 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/poolside-is-scaling-a-team-to-help-billions-code-and-realize-agi/) |\n| Daniel LaBruna | Co-author of AI inference and competition research posts | 2024 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/what-s-next-in-ai-inference/) |\n| William Eleazer | BCV Labs co-lead | 2024 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/our-first-year-in-review-bcv-labs/) |\n| Douwe Kiela, Amanpreet Singh | Founders of Contextual AI; incubated at Labs | 2023 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/) |\n| Jason Warner, Eiso Kant | Founders of poolside | 2023 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/poolside-is-scaling-a-team-to-help-billions-code-and-realize-agi/) |\n| Kamal Shah | Prophet Security; known since Atlassian–Skyhigh in 2017 | 2017 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/prophet-security-is-using-ai-to-cut-mean-time-to-response-by-10x-for-security-operations-teams/) |\n| Simon Kubica | Index founder; Atlassian colleague | Atlassian era | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/index-brings-product-planning-into-the-ai-age/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who led poolside's seed, and what was BCV's check?\n- Did BCV formally lead or co-lead the 2024-10 Series B, and was he the lead partner?\n- Who acquired Contextual AI (BCV CMS tags it \"Acquired\"), and when?\n- Oratomic and Mind Robotics: round size, lead and date. Was BCV first?\n- Which 2025 seed did he pass on? It was a team BCV had \"known for years\".\n- What were the six BCV Labs incubations in year one?\n- Was he Vice President or Principal between 2021 and 2024?\n- Podcasts and X activity were not reachable this session (web-search budget exhausted).\n\n## Sources\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/team/rak-garg/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/get-to-know-a-bcv-associate-rak-garg/ (2021-11-08)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/rak-garg-promoted-to-partner/ (2024-01-10)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-labs-is-an-ai-incubator-and-technical-community-in-silicon-valley/ (2023-11-16)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/our-first-year-in-review-bcv-labs/ (2024-12-22)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/ (2023-06-07)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/poolside-is-scaling-a-team-to-help-billions-code-and-realize-agi/ (2024-11-21)\n- https://fortune.com/2024/09/12/ai-startup-poolside-3-billion-valuation-bain-capital-ventures/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/how-unstructured-is-powering-the-llm-data-stack/ (2023-07-27)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/prophet-security-is-using-ai-to-cut-mean-time-to-response-by-10x-for-security-operations-teams/ (2024-04-23)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/norm-ai-is-using-ai-to-clean-up-the-sludge-of-regulatory-compliance/ (2024-06-25)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/index-brings-product-planning-into-the-ai-age/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-powerful-new-platform-for-kreatives/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/proactive-by-default-how-nebulock-rethinks-threat-hunting/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-new-echelon-of-it-implementations-and-ai-powered-services/ (2025-10-09)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/a-more-cogent-approach-to-vulnerability-management/ (2026-02-18)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/ (2025-12-18)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-next-phase-of-competition-in-ai/ (2024-10-16)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/what-s-next-in-ai-inference/ (2024-10-24)\n- https://rakgarg.substack.com/p/five-predictions-for-ai-in-2023 (2023-02-01)\n- https://rakgarg.substack.com/api/v1/posts?limit=20 (archive listing)\n- https://www.krea.ai/blog/new-krea\n- https://www.oratomic.com/\n- https://www.mindrobotics.com/\n- BCV Sanity CMS (team and portfolio records), query URLs inline above\n", "person:ramtin-naimi": "\n# Ramtin Naimi\n\n## One-line take\nA college-skipping ex-hedge-fund trader who went through bankruptcy, then used AngelList SPVs (Ripple, Solana, Rippling) and a management-company sale to Andreessen, Druckenmiller, Ackman and others to build Abstract into a multi-fund seed firm ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n\n## Crux\nHe wins by working the founder's *next* round rather than by picking alone: find founders before they raise (a LinkedIn scraper flagged ~7,000 tier-1-profile people when they became founders [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)), give an early or preemptive term sheet, then run the Series A so tier-1 firms act as his distribution, not his rivals. His constraint has always been capital, not access. He says he was \"never allocation constrained\" and calls raising funds too small his biggest mistake ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4), 2026-11-06). His sizing logic is the opposite of rigid ownership discipline: 5% beside a tier-1 firm's 15% in Fund I, then 15% of a whole fund into xAI's formation round. What he avoids: putting most of a fund into the obvious consensus names at peak prices, which he calls the classic way to end up bottom-quartile.\n\n## Differential insight\n*Added 2026-11-09 from Invest Like the Best EP.499 \"Trillion-Dollar Goalposts\" (2026-11-06). Quotes are from YouTube's English auto-captions, timestamps per the YouTube video ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4); episode page [src](https://colossus.com/episode/trillion-dollar-goalposts/)).*\n- **xAI was a fund-level bet:** about 15% of the 2023-vintage fund went into xAI's formation round: \"15% of our 2023 vintage fund into the formation round of XAI\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) 00:42).\n- **Biggest mistake = fund size:** \"we've always raised our funds too small\". He says every fund could have taken ~2x the capital at the same returns and let Abstract lead more often ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) 00:44).\n- **Capital-constrained, not access-constrained:** \"we were never allocation constrained. We were capital constrained.\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) 00:43).\n- **$800M latest fund; LP sentiment flipped:** in 2018 LPs said $100M was too big for a Fund I; in 2026 \"I got LPs commending me on my discipline for staying so small\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) 00:43).\n- **Proactive > reactive:** \"being proactive allows you to be a top 5% manager\". Reactive picking caps you at top quartile ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) 00:28).\n- **Timing (Conduit):** \"we we seeded a company like 9 months ago called Conduit\". That was a non-invasive BCI seed (~early 2026) before BCI was hot; its next round drew 13 term sheets ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) 00:28–00:29).\n- **Few term sheets can be a buy signal:** after many meetings, a thin conversion is \"probably not because everybody thought it was actually a bad founder\". Example: Etched, until \"Sequoia blessed him with a $10 billion valuation\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) 00:30–00:32).\n- **Neolab risk is debt, not equity:** labs \"need debt in excess of 5 to 10 x of the equity\". He met a neolab raising $1B for one training run; Abstract routinely sources compute for portfolio companies because large Neo Clouds skip sub-$200M deals ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) 00:22–00:25).\n- **LP liquidity is SpaceX-shaped:** LPs hold \"30 to 50% of their AUM in like three to four companies\"; without SpaceX-heavy funds they have had little liquidity, which tightens the LP market ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) 00:13).\n- **Market read (Nov 2026):** opportunity 10/10, froth \"6.5 to 7\" vs. 2021 at 10. Most renegotiated term sheets since 2021 in the last 6–8 weeks; mega-funds deploying ~3x faster than planned; some leads committing before their funds close ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) 00:08–00:17, 00:46).\n- **Fund I economics:** \"I was doing all the work and only getting 5%\". He then led deals while leaving tier-1 Series A firms ~10% ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He takes the same non-rigid stance on ownership that I do, and he concentrates when conviction is high: ~15% of a fund into xAI's formation round ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4)). He seeds frontier-research-style companies early (Conduit, BCI with scaling-law data) and talks fluently about neolab, compute and GPU financing. Deals that matter to me: xAI, Conduit, plus Series B leads in AI-native automation (Dust, Doctronic). He also names Physical Intelligence, Applied Compute, NewLimit and Chai Discovery as bellwethers ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4)); his ownership in those is not stated. At $800M, Abstract is a likely co-investor in large seeds and a source of introductions for founders before they raise. Caveat: critics have called Abstract a \"signal chaser\" behind tier-1 firms, which he partly concedes about the early years ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n\n## Background\n- Born November 1990 to parents who emigrated from Tehran; moved to the SF area at 12 and grew up in Tiburon ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- Began trading at 13 with $2,000 borrowed from his parents ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)); TechCrunch puts his start at 15 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand)). Interned for a Tiburon fund manager at 16 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand)).\n- 2008: earned several hundred thousand dollars on options on leveraged bank ETFs around the Lehman collapse; skipped college, got a Series 65 ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- Hedge fund (Opes Cura per TechCrunch): sources conflict. Colossus says launched January 2009 at 19 with $3M from 45 investors, ~59% annualized over three years, wound down in 2013 ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). TechCrunch says launched 2012 at 22 with $600K, peaking at $18M ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand)).\n- 2014: self-funded Lenders Exchange (secondary market for P2P loans); it failed as marketplace lending collapsed; ~$300K credit-card debt led to a Chapter 13 filing, cleared in about two years ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- Investment intern at Artis Ventures ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand)); then a part-intern/part-associate role at Core Innovation Capital under Arjan Schütte ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- Founded Abstract Ventures in January 2016 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand)), at age 25 per the firm ([src](https://www.abstract.com/people/)). August 2016–June 2017, while still at Core, invested in 47 seed companies via SPVs or as an angel, at one point ~one-third of AngelList volume ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- 2017: sold 20% of the Abstract management company to nine investors for $10M rather than take a job at another firm; owned 100% again by end-2024 ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.abstract.com/people/ | Firm bio only (2026-11-09) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No verified handle surfaced (2026-11-09) |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | Not confirmed |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-07 — Invest Like the Best (Patrick O'Shaughnessy) — EP.435 \"Building Abstract\" — https://colossus.com/?p=398983 — covers art collecting with mentors like Michael Ovitz, art-market status dynamics vs. venture power laws, bankruptcy and starting from scratch, competing for deals, and brand in VC ([src](https://colossus.com/?p=398983)).\n- 2026-11 — Invest Like the Best — EP.499 \"Trillion-Dollar Goalposts\" (2026-11-06) — https://colossus.com/episode/trillion-dollar-goalposts/ / https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4 — xAI was ~15% of the 2023 fund; latest fund is $800M; funds were always too small; proactive vs. reactive returns; neolab debt; froth 6.5–7/10 ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4)).\n\n## How they think\n- **Risk tolerance formed early:** says he became comfortable with risk \"at way too early of an age\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand)).\n- **Win by serving the founder's next round:** preemptive term sheets plus running the founder's fundraise (Krea: ~15 meetings in two days, $60M offer turned into a $135M a16z deal; Partiful: six term sheets, price 67% above the first verbal offer) ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- **Align with founders:** gave referring founders a share of SPV upside and pledged 5% of returns to founder-chosen charities ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand)).\n- **Art ↔ venture analogy:** treats art-market status and power-law dynamics as a lens on venture ([src](https://colossus.com/?p=398983)). Collects Christopher Wool, George Condo, Mark Grotjahn, Larry Bell ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- **Right calls:** Ripple and Solana each later topped $100B in coin market cap; Rippling reached ~$17B valuation; SPVs returned just under $100M ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- **Wrong calls:** Lenders Exchange and the resulting bankruptcy ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). 2017 bets included Juicero ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand)); its outcome is not covered by these sources.\n\n## Read these first\nNo writing by Naimi himself found. Best pieces about him:\n- \"The Man With the Hot Hand,\" Colossus, July 2025 — the definitive profile (bankruptcy, management-company deal, fund history, deal stories) ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- Invest Like the Best EP.435, 2025-07-29 — his own telling ([src](https://colossus.com/?p=398983)).\n- TechCrunch, 2017-04-05 — the SPV model and philanthropy pledge at age 26 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: no active public X, Substack, or LinkedIn presence confirmed as of 2026-11-09.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ripple | First AngelList SPV ($470K in four hours) | 2016–2017 | SPV syndicate | — | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Solana | Early (SPV era) | — | — | — | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Rippling | Early (SPV era) | — | — | — | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Brave | Early-stage | by 2017 | — | Founders Fund (backer) | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand) |\n| Starsky Robotics | Early-stage | by 2017 | — | — | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand) |\n| Juicero | Later-stage | by 2017 | — | — | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand) |\n| Clay, Cherry, Material Security, Newfront | Early rounds (SPV era) | 2016–2017 | — | — | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Hebbia | Preemptive term sheet; second-largest check | 2020-10 | No (Index won lead per Colossus) | Index Ventures | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Partiful | Series A process | 2022-10 | — | six term sheets | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Krea | a16z round at $135M valuation | 2023-11 | No | Andreessen Horowitz | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| xAI | Formation round (~15% of 2023-vintage fund) | — | No | — | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) |\n| Conduit (BCI) | Seed | ~2026-02 (\"9 months ago\" on 2026-11-06) | Not stated | — | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) |\n| Socket | Series B | — | Yes | — | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) |\n| Doctronic | Series B | — | Yes | — | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) |\n| Dust | Series B | — | Yes | — | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) |\n| Crusoe | Growth (passive) | — | No | — | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) |\n| Polymarket | Series B (\"big investor\") | — | Not stated | — | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4) |\n\nFirm-level deals without a named partner (e.g. Applied Labs seed, Abstract led, 2025-01-27 [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/01/27/applied-labs-raises-4-2m-ai-powered-business-automation-platform)) are in `firms/abstract.md` and the deals CSV under `abstract`.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Ripple (first SPV):** raised $470K in four hours on AngelList; he earned 15% carry on syndicated SPVs ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- **Hebbia (Oct 2020):** offered a preemptive term sheet; Index Ventures won the lead and he took the second-largest check, then wrote bigger checks in later rounds ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). Conflict: aggregators describe Oct 2020 as a ~$1M pre-seed led by Floodgate, and Hebbia's own blog says Index led the $30M Series A in 2022 ([src](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of)).\n- **Partiful (Oct 2022):** after a verbal Series A offer, he arranged meetings with senior partners elsewhere; six term sheets followed and the final price was 67% above the first offer ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- **Krea (Nov 2023):** after a viral launch he set up ~15 investor meetings in two days; a $60M preemptive offer became an a16z deal at $135M, signed Thanksgiving morning ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- **Management-company raise (2017):** intro chain ran Cyan Banister → Kevin Hartz → Keith Rabois and Chris Dixon → Marc Andreessen → Michael Ovitz → Ackman, Warsh, Druckenmiller, Sacks ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n- **Fund I close:** final $2M from an Israeli family office on Christmas Eve 2019, meeting the one-year deadline from first close ([src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Abstract | Founder & GP | 2016 | [src](https://www.abstract.com/people/) |\n| Stuart Peterson (Artis Ventures) | Mentor (cold email), strategic advisor; owns Abstract's office building | pre-2017 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand), [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Gil Penchina | Mentor (cold email), strategic advisor | pre-2017 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand) |\n| Arjan Schütte / Core Innovation Capital | Gave him a part-intern/associate role post-bankruptcy | mid-2010s | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Michael Ovitz | Art mentor; management-company investor | 2017 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Marc Andreessen, Chris Dixon, David Sacks, Keith Rabois, Kevin Hartz, Bill Ackman, Stanley Druckenmiller, Kevin Warsh | Management-company investors (20% for $10M, seven-year sunset) | 2017–2024 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Cyan Banister | Started intro chain to Kevin Hartz | 2017 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Joshua Kushner (Thrive Capital) | LP in Abstract Fund I | 2018–2019 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Matt Cohler, Neil Mehta, Chase Coleman, Santo Politi, Jerry Yang, Frederic Kerrest | LPs in Abstract Fund I | 2018–2019 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Alex Davidov | Hired from Core as founding GP | 2017 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Andrei Kozyrev | Hired as GP via Sequoia connection; ex-Thrive EIR | 2024 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| David Kwon | Hired as GP via Coatue connection | — | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| CSC Upshot / AngelList | Frequent SPV co-investor / platform | 2016–2017 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand) |\n| Andreessen Horowitz | Recurring later-round partner (Krea) | 2023 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Index Ventures | Won Hebbia lead over his preempt | 2020 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Hedge fund timeline: 2009/$3M (Colossus) vs. 2012/$600K, peak $18M (TechCrunch).\n- Hebbia round dating: Colossus \"Oct 2020, Index won lead\" vs. Floodgate-led 2020 pre-seed and Index-led 2022 Series A.\n- AUM: $1.8B (Colossus, July 2025) vs. $3.5B (abstract.com, Nov 2026). What drove the jump — new fund, markups (e.g. xAI/SpaceX listed \"IPO, 2026\")?\n- Fund numbering and any 2026 fund; no Form D for a 2025–2026 main fund found.\n- Rebrand timing from \"Abstract Ventures\" to \"Abstract\".\n- Will Thomsen's status (Partner on Crunchbase, absent from firm page).\n- Whether Abstract was in Physical Intelligence's Thrive-led seed round specifically.\n- His X/LinkedIn handles.\n- (2026-11-09) Does Abstract hold Physical Intelligence, Applied Compute, NewLimit or Chai Discovery? EP.499 names them only as bellwethers.\n- (2026-11-09) $800M fund: name, close date, Form D. Does the $3.5B AUM on abstract.com include it?\n- (2026-11-09) xAI formation-round check in dollars (15% of the \"2023 vintage fund\", which Colossus implies is the $300M raise).\n- (2026-11-09) EP.499 quotes come from YouTube auto-captions; check against the Colossus transcript (paywalled) if exact wording matters.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.abstract.com/people/\n- https://www.abstract.com/companies-list/\n- https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/\n- https://colossus.com/?p=398983\n- https://techcrunch.com/2017/04/05/at-ramtin-naimis-abstract-ventures-early-stage-investing-and-philanthropy-go-hand-in-hand\n- https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of\n- https://siliconangle.com/2025/01/27/applied-labs-raises-4-2m-ai-powered-business-automation-platform\n- https://colossus.com/episode/trillion-dollar-goalposts/\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r95bEutZUN4 (English auto-captions, retrieved 2026-11-09)\n", "person:ravi-mhatre": "\n# Ravi Mhatre\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the person who signs off on Lightspeed's US frontier-lab and frontier-hardware checks. He is listed on Anthropic, Thinking Machines, Ineffable, Unconventional AI, Periodic Labs, Ricursive, Skild, Mithril, Helion, Pacific Fusion and Neuralink, so a Lightspeed co-investor in a mega-seed is almost always dealing with him ([lsvp bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/ravi-mhatre/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He co-founded Lightspeed in 2000 ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lightspeed_Venture_Partners), secondary) and has about 25 years of enterprise relationships. Those relationships turn into repeat-founder deals. Glean is the cleanest example: Lightspeed had known Arvind Jain \"for nearly 20 years\", through Riverbed and then Rubrik, before co-leading Glean's 2019 Series A ([lsvp 2021](https://lsvp.com/stories/glean-bringing-search-to-the-workplace/), [lsvp 2024](https://lsvp.com/stories/glean-is-the-ai-powered-work-assistant-enterprises-desperately-need/)).\n- **The 2023 reset.** In April 2023 he and Bejul Somaia told partners worldwide to clear their calendars, \"Even board meetings.\" The trigger was that Lightspeed had no early OpenAI stake and had missed ElevenLabs and Hugging Face ([Newcomer, 2026-10-08](https://www.newcomer.co/p/after-missing-ais-early-days-lightspeed)). Since then the firm's frontier bets carry his name. Lightspeed first engaged with Anthropic in January 2023 ([lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-announces-lead-investment-in-anthropics-3-5b-series-e-financing/)), invested at the Series D in 2024 ([lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/anthropic/)), and led the Series E.\n- **What constrains him.** On labs he is almost never first. Anthropic came in at the D. On Thinking Machines a16z led the seed ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/15/mira-muratis-thinking-machines-lab-is-worth-12b-in-seed-round/)). On Ineffable, Sequoia was first reported as sole lead and Lightspeed became co-lead only at close (see Deal stories). His edge is conviction and fund size: Opportunity Fund III is $3.3B and Select VI is $1.8B ([press release, 2025-12-15](https://newshub.medianet.com.au/2025/12/lightspeed-closes-over-9b-in-new-funds-aimed-at-backing-category-definers-worldwide/133641)). Proprietary access to pre-founding researchers is not where he wins.\n- **Correction to the task premise: Mistral is not his deal.** Lightspeed's Mistral page (seed, 2023) lists Paul Murphy, Antoine Moyroud and James Ephrati, not Mhatre ([lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/mistralai/)). European AI routes through Moyroud and Murphy. Mhatre's name appears on Europe only where the deal is very large or defense-related: Ineffable (with Moyroud) and Helsing ([lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/helsing/)).\n- **What he'd never do.** He would not back an app built for today's model. His founder filter is \"thinking in future tense\", which means designing for where model capability will be in six months (Lightwork, 2026-06-15; see Differential insight). No source states a hard \"never\".\n\n## Differential insight\nThe Lightwork quotes come from a machine transcription (Whisper base.en) of the episode audio, which I pulled from the show's RSS feed. Only short, unambiguous phrases are quoted. Timestamps are episode time.\n- **\"Future tense\" is borrowed from Dario Amodei.** He says he looks for founders \"who really are thinking in future tense\" and \"I sort of borrowed that phrase\" from Anthropic's CEO ([Lightwork ep., 2026-06-15, ~42:05](https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/generative-now--ai-builde/episodes/The-SpaceX-IPO--Apple-Rebuilds-Siri--Anthropic-Releases-Fable-5-e3kqhr5)). Lab proximity is shaping his app-layer filter: build for \"where the intelligence is going to be in six months\" (~42:46).\n- **The \"tipping point\" model.** Capability crosses a threshold and adoption goes vertical. His examples are Waymo and Claude Code. On agentic coding at scale, before it worked, you were viewed as \"not just contrarian but outright wrong\" (~47:26). He frames the investing job as spotting what moves \"from seeming impossible\" to inevitable within six to twelve months.\n- **The enterprise market for intelligence may be bigger than consumer.** He says the enterprise market \"may actually be significantly larger\" than consumer (~51:28). Models that lack trust infrastructure \"they'll get in for the prototype\" but not production (~51:41). This matches the Anthropic memo: \"The ultimate value to be unlocked with AI is rooted in the trust people have in the model\" ([lsvp, 2025-03-03](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-announces-lead-investment-in-anthropics-3-5b-series-e-financing/)).\n- **The 2023 reset meeting.** This one is not in his own words; it is reported narration. Somaia ran the meeting for seven hours, and partners in Europe and Israel \"stayed on past midnight.\" The question was \"what would it mean to act as though that were true?\" ([Newcomer / Tom Dotan, 2026-10-08](https://www.newcomer.co/p/after-missing-ais-early-days-lightspeed)). The rest of the piece is paywalled. The subhead says the meeting set Lightspeed on a course that \"includes PE-style roll-ups\".\n- **Anthropic timing.** Lightspeed engaged \"in early 2023, when the company had fewer than 100 employees, no public product, and was pre-revenue\" ([lsvp memo](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-announces-lead-investment-in-anthropics-3-5b-series-e-financing/)). The first check came about a year later, at the Series D ([lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/anthropic/)). First contact was months before the April 2023 reset.\n- **Lab thesis.** \"the biggest value from AI's development will be unlocked at the frontier\" and \"Intelligence *is* the product\". He is first author of the memo, with Guru Chahal and Sebastian Duesterhoeft ([lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-announces-lead-investment-in-anthropics-3-5b-series-e-financing/)).\n- **Ineffable.** He co-signed \"We have not seen this paradigm pitched anywhere else\" with Raviraj Jain and Moyroud ([lsvp, 2026-04-27](https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/)).\n- **Risk posture.** \"I do not allow the fear of failure to hold me, Lightspeed, or our founders back\" ([lsvp bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/ravi-mhatre/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is the gatekeeper for the Lightspeed checks that come into the mega-seeds you want to be in:\n- Ineffable Intelligence ($1.1B seed, co-lead, 2026)\n- Unconventional AI ($475M seed, co-lead with a16z, 2025; [unconv.ai](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/))\n- Thinking Machines (seed, 2025)\n- Periodic Labs (2025)\n- Ricursive Intelligence (AI for chip design, Series A lead, 2026)\n- Mithril (AI cloud)\n- Nexthop AI (AI networking)\n\nHe is not a seed-first lab hunter. In practice he is a co-lead or a co-investor right behind the first lead. If you are in a lab before a large round, Lightspeed with Mhatre is a likely second or third name.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford: BS in electrical engineering, BA in economics, MBA ([lsvp bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/ravi-mhatre/)).\n- Before investing he was a software engineer at a biotech instrument maker, a consultant at Booz Allen, and worked at Silicon Graphics ([lsvp bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/ravi-mhatre/)). The founders came from Weiss, Peck & Greer ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lightspeed_Venture_Partners), secondary). One podcast directory says he was at Bessemer; that is unverified ([podbean](https://www.podbean.com/ew/dir-eckzs-bf3c9a0)).\n- 2000: co-founded Lightspeed with Barry Eggers, Christopher Schaepe and Peter Nieh ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lightspeed_Venture_Partners)).\n- Midas List in 2017, 2018, 2019, 2021, 2025 and 2026 ([lsvp bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/ravi-mhatre/)).\n- Current board role: Stanford GSB Trust advisory board. Former boards: WAVC and TiE Silicon Valley ([lsvp bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/ravi-mhatre/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [lsvp.com/team-member/ravi-mhatre](https://lsvp.com/team-member/ravi-mhatre/) | Firm bio and a list of about 90 companies (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | [medium.com/@ravi_lsvp](https://medium.com/@ravi_lsvp) | Linked from the firm bio, but the feed shows recipe posts (2023-12 to 2024-02), no investing writing. His investing writing is on lsvp.com/stories |\n| X | [x.com/ravi_lsvp](https://x.com/ravi_lsvp) | Handle as linked from the firm bio; activity not checked |\n| LinkedIn | [linkedin.com/in/ravimhatre](https://www.linkedin.com/in/ravimhatre) | From the firm bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found; guest on Lightspeed's [Lightwork](https://anchor.fm/s/113c83c8c/podcast/rss) | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-06-15 — Lightwork (Lightspeed) — the \"Fable 5\" episode, with a guest interview at 40:45 — [link](https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/generative-now--ai-builde/episodes/The-SpaceX-IPO--Apple-Rebuilds-Siri--Anthropic-Releases-Fable-5-e3kqhr5) — \"future tense\" founders, the tipping-point model, and enterprise trust as the moat.\n- 2025-03-05 — Bloomberg TV — Europe's AI talent and Anthropic — [link](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/videos/2025-03-05/europe-has-strong-technological-talent-and-artificial-intelligence-companies-lightspeed-co-founder) — page blocked (403); content not verified.\n- 2023 — Dead Cat (Newcomer) — \"Venture Capital Gravitational Physics\" — [link](https://www.everand.com/podcast/634492400/Venture-Capital-Gravitational-Physics-with-Ravi-Mhatre) — recorded around the SVB collapse; not transcribed.\n- 2020-11 — Stanford Entrepreneurial Thought Leaders — \"The Language of Technology\" — [link](https://www.everand.com/podcast/483831425/Ravi-Mhatre-Lightspeed-Venture-Partners-The-Language-of-Technology) — on careers in VC; not transcribed.\n\n## How they think\n- **Value sits at the frontier.** Intelligence is the product, so own the model layer (Anthropic) and the physical bottlenecks under it. His page includes Helion and Pacific Fusion (energy), Unconventional AI, Ricursive and Nexthop (compute), and Mithril (cloud).\n- **Exponentials with tipping points.** Founders should design for model capability six to twelve months out.\n- **Enterprise trust is the moat.** This means access control, explainability and being \"enterprise grade\" from day one (Lightwork, ~51:00).\n- **A call he got right:** Glean, from the 2019 A onward, through a long relationship with its founder. **A call he admits was missed:** the firm's early-AI misses (OpenAI, ElevenLabs, Hugging Face) led to the 2023 reset he co-called ([Newcomer](https://www.newcomer.co/p/after-missing-ais-early-days-lightspeed)).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Lightspeed's Anthropic Series E memo](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-announces-lead-investment-in-anthropics-3-5b-series-e-financing/) (2025-03-03): his frontier thesis in his own memo.\n- [Move 37: Ineffable Intelligence](https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/) (2026-04-27): why he backed the RL-from-experience lab.\n- [Glean: Bringing search to the workplace](https://lsvp.com/stories/glean-bringing-search-to-the-workplace/) (2021-09-15): the repeat-founder playbook.\n- [Investing in Helion](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-helion-abundant-intelligence-requires-abundant-energy/) (2025-01-28, with Raviraj Jain): energy as the AI bottleneck.\n- [After Missing AI's Early Days, Lightspeed Remade Itself](https://www.newcomer.co/p/after-missing-ais-early-days-lightspeed) (2026-10-08, paywalled): the 2023 reset.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing verified. X was not checked, and Medium shows non-investing content. Lightspeed's own channels (Lightwork, lsvp.com/stories) carry his voice.\n\n## Portfolio\nLightspeed entry year and stage come from each lsvp.com company page, which lists the partners named.\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Anthropic | Series D (entry) | 2024 | no | Guru Chahal, Sebastian Duesterhoeft (LSVP) | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/anthropic/) |\n| Anthropic | Series E $3.5B, $61.5B post | 2025-03-03 | yes, largest investor | memo with Chahal, Duesterhoeft | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-announces-lead-investment-in-anthropics-3-5b-series-e-financing/) |\n| Anthropic | Series F $13B | 2025-09 | co-lead (ICONIQ led) | Fidelity | [The Australian via HM1](https://www.heartsandmindsinvestments.com.au/insights/inside-lightspeeds-anthropic-bet-that-woke-silicon-valley-from-its-slumber) (secondary) |\n| Thinking Machines Lab | Seed | 2025 | no (a16z led) | Lisa Han, James Alcorn (LSVP) | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/thinking-machines/), [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/15/mira-muratis-thinking-machines-lab-is-worth-12b-in-seed-round/) |\n| Unconventional AI | Seed $475M, $4.5B | 2025-12-08 | co-lead with a16z | Sequoia, Lux, DCVC, Future Ventures, Jeff Bezos; LSVP Chahal, Moore | [unconv.ai](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/), [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/unconventional-ai/) |\n| Ineffable Intelligence | Seed $1.1B, $5.1B post | 2026-04-27 | co-lead with Sequoia | Raviraj Jain, Antoine Moyroud (LSVP) | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/) |\n| Periodic Labs | \"Series A\" per lsvp | 2025 | unknown | James Alcorn (LSVP) | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/periodic-labs/) |\n| Ricursive Intelligence | Series A $300M, $4B post | 2026-01-26 | yes | Chahal, Jonah Cader (LSVP); DST, NVentures, Sequoia | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/ricursive-intelligence/) |\n| Skild AI | Series A | 2024 | unknown | Raviraj Jain, Prathik Hosalli | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/skild-ai/) |\n| Mithril | Series A | 2024 | unknown | Raviraj Jain | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/mithril/) |\n| Nexthop AI | Series A $110M | 2025-03-25 | yes | Guru Chahal (memo author) | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/nexthop-ai/) |\n| Fireworks AI | Series C $250M | 2025-10-28 | co-lead | Index, Evantic; Chahal, Duesterhoeft (LSVP) | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/fireworks-ai/) |\n| Augment | Series B | 2024 | unknown | Guru Chahal | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/augment/) |\n| Cartesia | Series A | 2024 | no (Kleiner led) | Raviraj Jain | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/cartesia/) |\n| Arena | Series A | 2025 | no | James Alcorn, Bucky Moore | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/arena/) |\n| Helion | Series F $425M | 2025-01-28 | not stated | Raviraj Jain | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/helion/) |\n| Pacific Fusion | n/s | 2024 | unknown | Raviraj Jain | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/pacific-fusion/) |\n| Neuralink | n/s | 2025 | unknown | Shelley Chu, Brenton Fargnoli, Jonathan MacQuitty | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/neuralink/) |\n| Helsing | Series C | 2024 | unknown | Jain, Alexander Schmitt, Hosalli | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/helsing/) |\n| Anduril | Series E | 2022 | unknown | Jain, Will Kohler, Hosalli | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/anduril/) |\n| Castelion | Series A | 2024 | unknown | Jain, Hosalli | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/castelion/) |\n| Science Corporation | Series C | 2026 | not stated | Jonah Cader, Paul Murphy | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/science-corporation/) |\n| Glean | Series A $15.3M | 2019 | co-lead | Kleiner (Mamoon Hamid); James Ephrati | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/glean-is-the-ai-powered-work-assistant-enterprises-desperately-need/) |\n| Glean | Series D | 2024-02-27 | co-lead | Kleiner | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/glean-is-the-ai-powered-work-assistant-enterprises-desperately-need/) |\n| Snorkel AI | Seed (entry) and Series B lead | 2019 / 2021 | B: yes | Raviraj Jain (board with Mhatre) | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/snorkel-ai-unlocking-ai-for-enterprises/) |\n| Typeface | Series A | 2022 | unknown | Raviraj Jain | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/typeface/) |\n| Cylake | $245M financing | 2026-09-08 | yes | Guru Chahal | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-cylake/) |\n| Rubrik, Nutanix, AppDynamics, MuleSoft, Zscaler, ThoughtSpot, Offchain Labs | various (legacy) | | | | [lsvp bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/ravi-mhatre/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Anthropic.** First contact was in January 2023, when the company was pre-product and pre-revenue. The first check was the Series D (2024). Lightspeed then led the Series E as the largest investor (2025-03) and co-led the F (2025-09) ([lsvp memo](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-announces-lead-investment-in-anthropics-3-5b-series-e-financing/), [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/anthropic/), [The Australian via HM1](https://www.heartsandmindsinvestments.com.au/insights/inside-lightspeeds-anthropic-bet-that-woke-silicon-valley-from-its-slumber)). Lightspeed partner Issac Kim is the one quoted in press: \"We made a contrarian bet that this was different\" (same source). **Were they first? No.** Anthropic was founded in 2021, and Lightspeed's first check came about three years later. Mhatre's own framing on Lightwork makes the Claude Code / Opus 4.5 tipping point the proof case.\n- **Ineffable Intelligence.** FT-sourced reporting in February 2026 had Sequoia as sole lead. By the 2026-04-27 close, Lightspeed was co-lead at $5.1B post ([SiliconANGLE Feb](https://siliconangle.com/?p=731373), [SiliconANGLE Apr](https://siliconangle.com/?p=742691)). This was a first institutional round, but Lightspeed did not originate it.\n- **Unconventional AI.** A co-lead of the first round with a16z ([unconv.ai](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/)). Of his lab and compute deals, this is the closest to \"first in\".\n- **Glean.** This was a relationship deal. Lightspeed backed Arvind Jain at Riverbed and then Rubrik, co-led the 2019 A, and kept leading or co-leading through the D ([lsvp 2024](https://lsvp.com/stories/glean-is-the-ai-powered-work-assistant-enterprises-desperately-need/)).\n- **Snorkel AI.** Lightspeed knew the team \"when it was still just a research project at Stanford University\", invested at the 2019 seed, and led the Series B. Mhatre and Raviraj Jain joined the board ([lsvp 2021](https://lsvp.com/stories/snorkel-ai-unlocking-ai-for-enterprises/)). This is his earliest Stanford-lab-to-company AI bet (Chris Ré is a co-founder).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bejul Somaia | Co-leader of Lightspeed; co-called the 2023 AI reset | 2023 (as co-leaders) | [Newcomer](https://www.newcomer.co/p/after-missing-ais-early-days-lightspeed) |\n| Barry Eggers, Christopher Schaepe, Peter Nieh | Lightspeed co-founders | 2000 | [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lightspeed_Venture_Partners) |\n| Dario Amodei | Anthropic CEO; source of his \"future tense\" founder frame | 2023 | [Lightwork](https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/generative-now--ai-builde/episodes/The-SpaceX-IPO--Apple-Rebuilds-Siri--Anthropic-Releases-Fable-5-e3kqhr5) |\n| Guru Chahal | Co-signer on Anthropic, Unconventional, Ricursive, Cylake, Fireworks | 2019 | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-investors-gaurav-gupta-and-guru-chahal-to-lightspeed/) |\n| Raviraj Jain | Co-signer on Ineffable, Helion, Skild, Mithril, Pacific Fusion; Snorkel board | 2019 | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/snorkel-ai-unlocking-ai-for-enterprises/) |\n| Antoine Moyroud | Co-author on the Ineffable seed co-lead | 2026 | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/) |\n| Sebastian Duesterhoeft | Co-author of the Anthropic Series E memo | 2025 | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-announces-lead-investment-in-anthropics-3-5b-series-e-financing/) |\n| James Alcorn | Co-listed on Thinking Machines, Periodic Labs, Arena | 2025 | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/periodic-labs/) |\n| Issac Kim | Lightspeed partner quoted on the Anthropic bet | 2025 | [The Australian via HM1](https://www.heartsandmindsinvestments.com.au/insights/inside-lightspeeds-anthropic-bet-that-woke-silicon-valley-from-its-slumber) |\n| Arvind Jain | Founder backed three times (Riverbed, Rubrik, Glean) | about 2005 | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/glean-bringing-search-to-the-workplace/) |\n| Sonya Huang / Sequoia | Co-investor across Glean, Fireworks, Ineffable | 2022 | [repo pattern](../patterns/co-investor-gangs.md) |\n| Mamoon Hamid / Kleiner | Glean Series A co-lead | 2019 | [repo pattern](../patterns/co-investor-gangs.md) |\n| Naveen Rao | Unconventional AI founder; Lightspeed co-led the seed | 2025 | [unconv.ai](https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/) |\n| David Silver | Ineffable founder | 2026 | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **LP role.** No evidence that he is a personal LP in any outside fund. A web search for LP or angel links found only aggregator angel entries (Zeel, Lookery, TutorVista), so I wrote no lp.csv.\n- **The paywalled Newcomer piece.** It likely holds the Anthropic sourcing path and the \"PE-style roll-ups\" detail. Worth buying.\n- **Anthropic check size.** The $1B figure for the Series E or F appears only in aggregators.\n- **Bloomberg 2025 interview on Europe.** Blocked, so not verified.\n- **Periodic Labs.** Lightspeed labels its entry a \"Series A\". Check against press, which describes a seed.\n- **Whether he still personally sources labs, or signs as senior partner on deals Chahal, Jain or Alcorn originate.** The deal-team pattern can't tell these apart.\n\n## Sources\n- https://lsvp.com/team-member/ravi-mhatre/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-announces-lead-investment-in-anthropics-3-5b-series-e-financing/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/glean-bringing-search-to-the-workplace/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/glean-is-the-ai-powered-work-assistant-enterprises-desperately-need/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/snorkel-ai-unlocking-ai-for-enterprises/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-helion-abundant-intelligence-requires-abundant-energy/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-cylake/\n- https://lsvp.com/company/ pages: anthropic, thinking-machines, periodic-labs, skild-ai, mithril, pacific-fusion, neuralink, helsing, castelion, anduril, ineffable-intelligence, unconventional-ai, ricursive-intelligence, nexthop-ai, fireworks-ai, augment, cartesia, arena, helion, science-corporation, glean, snorkel-ai, typeface, mistralai\n- https://www.newcomer.co/p/after-missing-ais-early-days-lightspeed\n- https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/generative-now--ai-builde/episodes/The-SpaceX-IPO--Apple-Rebuilds-Siri--Anthropic-Releases-Fable-5-e3kqhr5 (audio transcribed locally)\n- https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/\n- https://newshub.medianet.com.au/2025/12/lightspeed-closes-over-9b-in-new-funds-aimed-at-backing-category-definers-worldwide/133641\n- https://www.heartsandmindsinvestments.com.au/insights/inside-lightspeeds-anthropic-bet-that-woke-silicon-valley-from-its-slumber\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lightspeed_Venture_Partners (secondary)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/15/mira-muratis-thinking-machines-lab-is-worth-12b-in-seed-round/\n", "person:raviraj-jain": "\n# Raviraj Jain\n\n*Updated 2026-11-10: the deal list now covers the full lsvp.com portfolio, and the crux is rewritten. An earlier pass read him as \"never first into labs\". The Skild, Snorkel, Fiddler and Dexterity records contradict that.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the partner who has worked next to co-founder Ravi Mhatre on almost every Lightspeed robotics, ML-infra and lab deal since 2017. He is the Lightspeed name quoted on Skild AI's $300M Series A and the board member at Reflection AI. In 2025–26 he started leading large AI-infra seeds himself: Sycamore ($65M), Luel ($31.2M) and Sphinx.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins: he is the Mhatre-paired operator.**\n  - lsvp.com pairs him with Mhatre on his ML and robotics deals going back to his first year: Dexterity (seed 2017), Fiddler (seed 2018, led), Snorkel AI (seed 2019, led), Osmos (seed 2019), Aurora (B 2019), Tonkean (A 2020, led) and Typeface (A 2022). The pairing continues on Mithril, Skild, Cartesia, Helsing, Ineffable and Covenant ([Snorkel](https://lsvp.com/company/snorkel-ai/), [Fiddler](https://lsvp.com/company/fiddler/), [Dexterity](https://lsvp.com/company/dexterity/), [Skild](https://lsvp.com/company/skild-ai/), [Covenant](https://lsvp.com/company/covenant/)).\n  - So the frontier deals are not borrowed from the MP. They are a nine-year two-person partnership, with Jain as the public face on several of them. Skild's own release quotes Jain, not Mhatre ([src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a)).\n- **He goes in early and keeps re-upping.**\n  - Skild: Lightspeed co-led the seed (aggregator), co-led the $300M A with Jain quoted, then \"doubled down\" at the C ([A](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a), [C](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/series-c)).\n  - Nirvana: seed, A and B led, then the D (\"Quadrupling-Down\").\n  - EvenUp: the Series E memo renews \"conviction ... for a third time\".\n  - Reflection: co-led the A, then re-upped at the B.\n- **What constrains him.** On the pure-LLM labs, Sequoia got there first. It co-led Reflection's seed before Lightspeed co-led the A ([src](https://aimmediahouse.com/ai-startups/google-deepmind-veterans-raise-130m-to-build-ai-that-codes-without-humans)). The FT reported Sequoia as sole lead on Ineffable before Lightspeed showed up as co-lead at close ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=731373)). On the largest checks he sits inside a Mhatre-led deal team; he is not a solo decision-maker.\n- **What he'd never do.** No source says. Inferred from his posts and bio: he would not back a founder without product depth (his bio says founders are \"the first de-facto product managers\").\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Mhatre pairing dates to 2017, not the AI boom.** Every robotics and ML-infra deal on his lsvp page from 2017 to 2022 lists Mhatre next to him: Dexterity, Fiddler, Snorkel, Osmos, Aurora, Tonkean and Typeface. The firm's \"frontier\" bets (Skild, Mithril, Cartesia, Ineffable) are the same duo running the same playbook at a larger size. This is drawn from lsvp company pages ([Snorkel](https://lsvp.com/company/snorkel-ai/), [Typeface](https://lsvp.com/company/typeface/)) and is an inference from deal-team listings, not a stated fact.\n- **Skild: he is the voice, and Lightspeed was in from the seed.** Skild's release quotes him: \"we believe they're a one-of-a-kind company\" ([src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a), 2024-07-09). Lightspeed is the first name in a lead group with Coatue, SoftBank and Bezos. A secondary aggregator says Lightspeed and Sequoia co-led the $14.5M seed in 2023 ([aggregator](https://websets.exa.ai/websets/directory/skild-funding)).\n- **Sycamore came from an investor-turned-founder and his board network.** Founder Sri Viswanath came from \"the investing side at Coatue\" ([Sycamore](https://sycamore.so/blog/company/building-the-ai-native-enterprise-os/)), and Coatue co-led the $65M seed with Lightspeed.\n  - In the Lightspeed interview, Jain appears to say he has known Sri \"for many years as a fellow investor\" and that they worked together on boards for about three to four years. The source is an auto-transcript with unmarked speakers, so the attribution is inferred ([Rosetta](https://rosetta.to/u/lightspeedvp/how-ai-can-make-every-employee-a-builder-raviraj-jain-sycamore-labs), 2026-03-30).\n  - Skild co-founders Deepak Pathak and Abhinav Gupta are angels in the seed, alongside Bob McGrew, Jerry Tworek, Sachin Katti, François Chollet and Lip-Bu Tan. His own portfolio founders are co-investing in his new seeds ([src](https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed)).\n- **His seed sizes have moved up.** His 2025–26 seeds are large: Sycamore $65M, co-lead (2026-03-30), and Luel $31.2M, lead (2026-05-15). Ineffable's $1.1B seed (co-lead, 2026-04-27) is the extreme case. This is the same mega-seed band that THESIS.md describes.\n- **He frames labs as needing data.** On Luel he wrote \"The data wall the labs are hitting is real.\" ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-luel-the-marketplace-for-multimodal-ai-training-data/))\n- **Composio sourcing.** Lightspeed knew the founders \"over a period of several years\". The founders were IIT Bombay roommates, and Jain is also IIT Bombay ([post](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-composio-building-the-backbone-of-ai-agent-intelligence/)). The shared alma mater is a fact; the post does not name it as the intro path.\n- **EvenUp timing.** Lightspeed met the founders in March 2022 and joined only at the Series C (August 2023), then re-upped twice ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-evenup-the-ai-platform-shaping-the-future-of-legal-services/), [src](https://lsvp.com/stories/evenup-and-the-future-of-legal-ai-faster-smarter-fairer/)). He waited 17 months before the first check.\n- **Resolve AI.** He is a co-author of the memo. The memo gives no round, but TechCrunch reports a $125M Series A at $1B led by Lightspeed ([TC](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/04/ai-sre-resolve-ai-confirms-125m-raise-unicorn-valuation/)). Office visit: \"When we visited their office on a Sunday, 40 people were working\" ([post](https://lsvp.com/stories/building-the-ai-for-production-our-investment-in-resolve-ai/)).\n- **Post Money podcast (2026-05-21, 46 min).** *Corrected 2026-11-10:* Substack publishes a full transcript. Quotes from it are in \"In their own words\" below ([src](https://postmoneypodcast.substack.com/p/investing-in-civilization-scale-problems)).\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-10)\n*Added 2026-11-10. Sources: his Medium archive (2018–2022), a 49-minute 2019 interview, CNBC-TV18 (2023), IITBAA (2024), the Post Money transcript (2026) and Lightwork (2026). The rows are in `data/rows/raviraj-jain-voice-insights.csv`.*\n- **A three-part test for neolabs.** He says product-market-fit heuristics are \"not relevant as it relates to Neolabs\".\n  - (1) The founder needs several major model breakthroughs behind them. Pedigree is \"necessary\" but \"not sufficient\".\n  - (2) They need a \"following\": the ability to recruit when top researchers have too many options.\n  - (3) The lab's research must be \"authentic\" to the founder's past work.\n  - He also avoids labs \"in the direct shooting range\" of the frontier labs. He prefers orthogonal architectures or other layers, such as continual learning ([src](https://postmoneypodcast.substack.com/p/investing-in-civilization-scale-problems), 7:32–14:44).\n- **His case for neolabs is a pharma analogy.** Frontier labs are \"on the clock\" shipping models, so large research directions stay unexplored for upstarts ([src](https://postmoneypodcast.substack.com/p/investing-in-civilization-scale-problems), 8:00).\n- **Skild timing, in his words.** \"Lightspeed was the first investor\" (the transcript renders it \"Skilled\"). He says Lightspeed led the seed and he has been on the board for about three years. Lightspeed's own memo says the seed was co-led with Sequoia ([podcast](https://postmoneypodcast.substack.com/p/investing-in-civilization-scale-problems) 22:03; [memo](https://lsvp.com/stories/skild-is-bringing-generative-ai-to-the-real-world/)).\n  - The deal came from a thesis, not inbound. After time in language models, the team asked what \"intelligence in the real world\" needs. He also built robots as an IIT freshman.\n- **His seat, by his own description.** He is purely an early-stage enterprise and frontier investor. Lightspeed is usually \"the first institutional investor\", and he is often the first board member ([src](https://postmoneypodcast.substack.com/p/investing-in-civilization-scale-problems), 5:31).\n  - In 2024 he said he meets about 600 companies a year and invests in two or three ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XxT_E9t1kuk), 1:01).\n- **Diligence habits.**\n  - In one frontier space, Lightspeed spent \"a year diligencing the space\" before its first check ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yq4JaCQCOM4), 8:01).\n  - In 2026 he said Claude is \"a core part of how I do diligence\", working through data rooms ([src](https://postmoneypodcast.substack.com/p/investing-in-civilization-scale-problems), 41:12).\n  - Before Aurora, Lightspeed met more than 100 AV startups ([src](https://medium.com/lightspeed-venture-partners/driving-innovation-aurora-goes-public-5605378ea4db), 2021).\n- **Founder rules.**\n  - 2019: \"gradient of your life is more important than\" where you are. He backs a steep rate of improvement ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x1ZohcoNvf8), 46:02).\n  - 2026: three frontier archetypes, namely the prodigy, the breakthrough scientist and the more-ambitious repeat founder ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yq4JaCQCOM4), 9:01).\n- **Constraint.** He backs long-horizon bets such as fusion only with a path to exits inside a 10–12 year fund life ([src](https://postmoneypodcast.substack.com/p/investing-in-civilization-scale-problems), 31:44).\n- **Deal origins from the older archive.**\n  - Dexterity: he met Samir Menon in November 2017 and \"immediately decided to lead the seed round\" ([src](https://medium.com/lightspeed-venture-partners/dexterity-ushering-a-new-era-of-intelligent-robots-3b80cbf57167)).\n  - Snorkel: Lightspeed tracked it from the Stanford research project, seeded it, then led the B ([src](https://medium.com/lightspeed-venture-partners/snorkel-ai-unlocking-ai-for-enterprises-edc1e74c8f17)).\n  - Nirvana: a personal-network deal. \"I've known Rushil for many years\" ([src](https://medium.com/lightspeed-venture-partners/nirvana-insurance-data-driven-insurance-for-commercial-fleets-and-the-connected-supply-chain-f9cec10d24b5)).\n- **How he got into venture.** He was not looking. Lightspeed's Nakul Mandan recruited him, and he joined in January 2017. He calls himself an introvert who builds a few deep, non-transactional ties ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x1ZohcoNvf8), 28:00–34:00; the auto-captions garble Mandan's name).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** THESIS.md defines high as backing frontier AI, labs or AI-native automation at seed or A, and he does that repeatedly:\n- **Labs and frontier models:** Ineffable Intelligence (seed co-lead, $1.1B), Reflection AI (A co-lead and board) and Cartesia (deal team).\n- **Compute and infrastructure:** Mithril (A 2024), Luel (seed lead), Composio (A lead) and Snorkel (seed lead 2019).\n- **Robotics:** Skild AI (seed and A), Dexterity (seed 2017).\n- **AI-native automation:** Sycamore (seed co-lead), Distyl (A lead), Resolve (A), Tonkean, Omnifold and Ketryx.\n\nCaveats:\n- He is a senior partner, not a hidden rising name.\n- On the labs, Sequoia usually got there first, so he is a co-lead partner, not an early-signal source.\n- **Practical use for you:** he is the most likely Lightspeed co-lead counterpart on a $20M slot in a $50M+ AI-infra seed (the Sycamore and Luel pattern), and a route into Skild and Reflection.\n\n## Background\n- Bachelor's in engineering, IIT Bombay; MBA, Harvard Business School ([bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/raviraj-jain/)). Dates not found.\n- Before investing he was an entrepreneur, product lead and business analyst in the US and India. He was product lead at Fundbox and product manager at LinkedIn ([bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/raviraj-jain/)). Dates for each role not found.\n- Joined Lightspeed in 2017, with \"Partner since 2017\" per the bio. His earliest lsvp deals (Dexterity seed, Reliable Robotics A) are dated 2017. No promotion post found.\n- Pronouns: the firm bio uses he/him.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://lsvp.com/team-member/raviraj-jain/ | 40 portfolio companies, 8 stories (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://medium.com/@ravirajjain_3536 | Linked from the lsvp bio. Read via its RSS feed on 2026-11-10: 9 posts from 2018-06 to 2022-02 (Tonkean, Dexterity x2, Reliable Robotics, Snorkel, Osmos, Aurora, Nirvana, IIT conference). Nothing since; his memos moved to lsvp.com/stories |\n| X | https://twitter.com/ravirajjain | Linked from the lsvp bio; x.com returned 402, so the follower count is unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/ravirajjain | Linked from the lsvp bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://rosetta.to/u/lightspeedvp/how-ai-can-make-every-employee-a-builder-raviraj-jain-sycamore-labs | He was interviewer on Lightspeed's \"The Investment Memo\" (Sycamore episode, 2026-03-30); not a regular host |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- **2026-05-21, Post Money (host Nilanjana Bhowmik):** \"Investing in Civilization Scale problems at Lightspeed\" ([link](https://postmoneypodcast.substack.com/p/investing-in-civilization-scale-problems)). The show notes cover self-learning systems, humanoids arriving sooner than expected, fusion, energy as AI's bottleneck, and AI changing VC workflows. *Corrected 2026-11-10:* a full transcript is on the Substack page and is mined above. There is also a [YouTube version](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=krHjLQcbIks).\n- **2026-03-30, Lightspeed \"The Investment Memo\":** \"How AI Can Make Every Employee a Builder\" with Sri Viswanath of Sycamore ([link](https://rosetta.to/u/lightspeedvp/how-ai-can-make-every-employee-a-builder-raviraj-jain-sycamore-labs)). It covers his years-long investor relationship with Sri and why AI coding tools don't solve the enterprise problem. The auto-transcript has no speaker labels.\n- **2026-08-11, Lightwork (Lightspeed, host Claire Zau):** \"Raviraj Jain on the Six Frontiers Defining the Next Century\" ([YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yq4JaCQCOM4), [audio](https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/generative-now--ai-builde/episodes/Raviraj-Jain-on-What-Frontier-Investing-Actually-Means--Lightwork-e3n8gqj)). It covers frontier versus diffusion, three founder archetypes, a year of diligence before a first frontier check, and his \"pet idea\": a world model of human biology. *Located and mined 2026-11-10.*\n- **2024-10-25, IITBAA Leadership Conference interview** ([YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XxT_E9t1kuk)): 600 companies a year, 2–3 investments; given the size of Lightspeed's funds, he looks for founders aiming far beyond a $1B company (the auto-captions garble the exact figure).\n- **2024-10-08, Founders' Creative clip on robotics** ([YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XkVXmMlm-yM)): billions of robots in 50 years.\n- **2023-07-01, CNBC-TV18 Young Turks \"Voices from the Valley\"** ([YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wL8wI95upaA)): in the downturn, founder quality at seed rose.\n- **2019-06/07, Stilt \"Immigrants\" interview series** ([full 49 min](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x1ZohcoNvf8) plus five short clips): his IIT startups, the HBS financing, LinkedIn and Fundbox, his recruitment to Lightspeed, and founder rules.\n- **2018-06, IIT Bay Area Leadership Conference AI panel**, per his own [post](https://medium.com/@ravirajjain_3536/learnings-from-iit-bay-area-leadership-conference-78e3c6b41583). No recording found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Product-centric founders.** He looks for founders who think like PMs ([bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/raviraj-jain/)).\n- **Physical and frontier intelligence:** robotics foundation models (Skild, Dexterity), RL-first labs (Reflection, Ineffable), fusion and defense (Helion, Pacific Fusion, Helsing, Anduril, Covenant). The Post Money show notes frame this as \"civilization scale\" problems.\n- **Picks and shovels for labs and agents:** training data (Luel, Snorkel), agent tooling (Composio), compute (Mithril), and agent security in the enterprise (Sycamore).\n- **Vertical AI rebuilding legacy industries:** \"rebuilding data-rich, process-constrained industries from the ground up\" ([Nirvana](https://lsvp.com/stories/quadrupling-down-in-nirvana-ai-native-insurance-for-the-modern-economy/)). Examples are insurance (Nirvana/Mandl), legal (EvenUp), medical coding (Fathom) and engineering design (Eagle; AI role not stated by lsvp).\n- **Calls that went right:**\n  - Skild: $1.5B at the A to more than $14B at the C ([C](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/series-c)).\n  - Reflection: about $555M at the A to $8B at the B ([B](https://reflection.ai/blog/building-frontier-open-intelligencence)).\n  - Snorkel: seed lead in 2019.\n\n  Calls that went wrong: no investment mistake has been admitted on the record. As a founder, he says four or five startups after HelloIntern \"didn't work out\", and BookMyCab (2009) was too early for India's 2009 smartphone base ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x1ZohcoNvf8), 11:02, 2019).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Skild AI $300M Series A release](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a): his quote, and Lightspeed at the top of the lead group.\n2. [Open Intelligence for Everyone: Reflection AI](https://lsvp.com/stories/towards-superintelligence-reflection-ai/): the A co-lead memo with Alcorn, updated August 2026.\n3. [Our Investment in Luel](https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-luel-the-marketplace-for-multimodal-ai-training-data/): the data wall for labs.\n4. [Quadrupling-Down in Nirvana](https://lsvp.com/stories/quadrupling-down-in-nirvana-ai-native-insurance-for-the-modern-economy/): his seed-to-D re-up pattern.\n5. [Post Money transcript](https://postmoneypodcast.substack.com/p/investing-in-civilization-scale-problems) (2026): his neolab underwriting rules in full.\n6. [Stilt interview](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x1ZohcoNvf8) (2019): the only long-form pre-2024 audio, covering how he decides and how he got into venture.\n\n## Social engagement\nX: still not retrievable (402); no threads found. Medium: 9 posts from 2018 to 2022, read via RSS 2026-11-10. The posts are deal memos co-signed with Mhatre, Eggers, Janmohamed and Vaswani, plus one personal post on the IIT network.\n\n## Portfolio\nThese are the AI and deep-tech deals where lsvp.com, a company release or press names him. \"Led?\" refers to Lightspeed's role.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ineffable Intelligence | Seed ($1.1B at $5.1B) | 2026-04-27 | co-lead | Sequoia (co-lead); Nvidia; Google; DST; Index; Mhatre and Moyroud on the deal team | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/), [press](https://siliconangle.com/?p=742691) |\n| Reflection AI | Series A ($105M; $130M with the seed) | 2025-03-07 | co-lead; Jain on the board | CRV (co-lead); Sequoia and CRV led the earlier seed; Alcorn | [AIM](https://aimmediahouse.com/ai-startups/google-deepmind-veterans-raise-130m-to-build-ai-that-codes-without-humans), [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/towards-superintelligence-reflection-ai/) |\n| Reflection AI | Series B ($2B at $8B) | 2025-10-09 | no (Nvidia led) | Sequoia; CRV; DST | [company](https://reflection.ai/blog/building-frontier-open-intelligencence) |\n| Skild AI | Seed ($14.5M) | 2023 | co-lead (aggregator) | Sequoia | [aggregator](https://websets.exa.ai/websets/directory/skild-funding); Jain not named for this round |\n| Skild AI | Series A ($300M at $1.5B) | 2024-07-09 | co-lead; Jain quoted | Coatue, SoftBank, Bezos (co-leads); Felicis; Sequoia; Menlo; GC; CRV | [Skild](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a), [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/skild-ai/) |\n| Skild AI | Series C ($1.4B, >$14B) | 2026-01-14 | no; \"doubled down\" | — | [Skild](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/series-c) |\n| Sycamore | Seed ($65M) | 2026-03-30 | co-lead; Jain quoted | Coatue (co-lead); 8VC; Abstract; Dell Tech Capital; angels including the Skild founders | [release](https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed) |\n| Luel | Seed ($31.2M) | 2026-05-15 | lead | General Catalyst; SV Angel; Lisa Han | [post](https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-luel-the-marketplace-for-multimodal-ai-training-data/) |\n| Sphinx | Seed | 2025 | not stated | Bucky Moore and Amber Yang on the deal team | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/sphinx/) |\n| Resolve AI | Series A ($125M at $1B) | 2026-02-04 | lead (press) | Duesterhoeft and Conniff co-authored | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/04/ai-sre-resolve-ai-confirms-125m-raise-unicorn-valuation/), [post](https://lsvp.com/stories/building-the-ai-for-production-our-investment-in-resolve-ai/) |\n| Composio | Series A ($25M) | 2025-07-22 | lead; sole-author memo | Lightspeed India (Mohapatra) | [post](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-composio-building-the-backbone-of-ai-agent-intelligence/) |\n| Distyl | Series A ($20M) | 2024-11-19 | lead | Khosla; Coatue; Dell Tech Capital; Nat Friedman; Lisa Han | [Distyl](https://www.distyl.ai/blog/news/distyl-secures-20m-from-lightspeed) |\n| Distyl | Series B ($175M at $1.8B) | 2025-09-23 | co-lead (secondary) | — | [PRN](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/distyl-ai-raises-175-million-at-1-8-billion-valuation-to-help-global-enterprises-become-ai-native-302564270.html) |\n| Mithril (ex-Foundry) | Series A | 2024 | co-lead (unconfirmed) | Sequoia; Mhatre | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/mithril/) |\n| Cartesia | Seed ($22M new) | 2024-12-12 | participant | **Conflict:** lsvp labels this \"Series A 2024\", but Cartesia's own A ($64M) was led by Kleiner on 2025-03-11 | [Cartesia seed](https://cartesia.ai/blog/seed), [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/cartesia/) |\n| Snorkel AI | Seed | 2019 | lead | Mhatre; Iregbulem | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/snorkel-ai/) |\n| Fiddler | Seed | 2018 | lead | Mhatre; Semil Shah | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/fiddler/) |\n| Dexterity | Seed | 2017 | not stated | Mhatre | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/dexterity/) |\n| Reliable Robotics | Series A | 2017 | lead | sole Lightspeed partner | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/reliable-robotics/) |\n| Aurora | Series B | 2019 | not stated | Mhatre | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/aurora/) |\n| Tonkean | Series A | 2020 | lead | Mhatre; Arif Janmohamed | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/tonkean/) |\n| Typeface | Series A | 2022 | not stated | Mhatre | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/typeface/) |\n| Helsing | Series C | 2024 | lead | Mhatre; Schmitt; Hosalli | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/helsing/) |\n| Nirvana (now Mandl) | Seed, then A and B led, D joined | 2020 seed; D post 2025-12-18 | lead (seed, A, B) | Shaurya Aggarwal | [post](https://lsvp.com/stories/quadrupling-down-in-nirvana-ai-native-insurance-for-the-modern-economy/), [Mandl](https://lsvp.com/company/mandl/) |\n| EvenUp | Series C, then two re-ups through the E | 2023-08 (C); E memo 2025-10-07 | not stated | Duesterhoeft | [post](https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-evenup-the-ai-platform-shaping-the-future-of-legal-services/) |\n| Ketryx | Series A | 2023 | not stated | Guru Chahal; Janmohamed | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/ketryx/) |\n| Omnifold | Series A | 2025 | not stated | Chahal; Janmohamed | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/omnifold/) |\n| Fathom | Series B | 2022 | not stated | Imanbayev; Kohler | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/fathom/) |\n| Keychain | Undisclosed | 2024 | not stated | Alex Taussig | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/keychain/) |\n| Eagle | Seed | 2026 | lead | Isaac Kim; Desai; Zhuo (Lightspeed growth PE team) | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/eagle/) |\n| Osmos | Seed | 2019 | not stated | Mhatre; Vaswani | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/osmos/) |\n| P0 Security | Seed | 2023 | lead | sole Lightspeed partner | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/p0-security/) |\n| Tessell | Series A | 2023 | not stated | sole Lightspeed partner | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/company/tessell/) |\n| Character.AI | Undisclosed | 2023 | no | Lisa Han; Faraz Fatemi | repo roster row; the lsvp page returned 404 this session |\n\nHe is also named on these non-AI or defense and energy companies: Anduril (E 2022), Covenant (2026, with Mhatre and Hosalli), Castelion, Pacific Fusion, Helion (F 2025, a new investor), Materialize, FalconX, Wintermute and Rocksalt ([bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/raviraj-jain/)).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Skild AI.**\n  - A secondary aggregator says Lightspeed co-led the 2023 seed with Sequoia.\n  - At the $300M A (2024-07-09, $1.5B), Lightspeed is listed first of four leads, and Jain is the quoted partner.\n  - Lightspeed doubled down at the $1.4B C (2026-01).\n  - Two years later the Skild founders show up as angels in Jain's Sycamore seed.\n- **Reflection AI.**\n  - Sequoia and CRV led the seed. Lightspeed and CRV co-led the $105M A, announced 2025-03-07, and Jain joined the board ([AIM](https://aimmediahouse.com/ai-startups/google-deepmind-veterans-raise-130m-to-build-ai-that-codes-without-humans)).\n  - Lightspeed re-upped in the $2B B led by Nvidia.\n  - Who sourced the deal, Jain or Alcorn, is not stated.\n- **Ineffable Intelligence.**\n  - The FT had Sequoia as sole lead in February 2026 ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=731373)). At close, Lightspeed was co-lead ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=742691)).\n  - Jain's attribution is firm-site only: he co-wrote the post with Mhatre and Moyroud. Press names no Lightspeed partner.\n- **Sycamore.** Coatue alum Sri Viswanath and Jain had years of investor and board overlap before the seed, per the inferred transcript attribution. Coatue and Lightspeed co-led the $65M ([release](https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed)).\n- **Nirvana and Mandl.**\n  - He met Rushil Goel in 2020, when it was \"just him and a vision\", then led the seed, A and B, and joined the D.\n  - Nirvana now sits under Mandl, alongside Vega and Cooper. lsvp lists Mandl as founded 2026, yet shows the 2020 seed against it; the page does not explain the gap ([Mandl](https://lsvp.com/company/mandl/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lightspeed Venture Partners | Partner, Enterprise, Menlo Park | 2017 | [bio](https://lsvp.com/team-member/raviraj-jain/) |\n| Ravi Mhatre | Co-deal-team on about 15 deals, from Dexterity (2017) to Covenant (2026) | 2017 | [Dexterity](https://lsvp.com/company/dexterity/), [Covenant](https://lsvp.com/company/covenant/) |\n| James Alcorn | Co-author and co-deal-team on Reflection | 2025 | [lsvp](https://lsvp.com/stories/towards-superintelligence-reflection-ai/) |\n| Antoine Moyroud | Co-author on Ineffable | 2026 | [post](https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/) |\n| Lisa Han | Co-lead on the Luel and Distyl memos | 2024 | [Luel](https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-luel-the-marketplace-for-multimodal-ai-training-data/) |\n| Sebastian Duesterhoeft | Co-author on EvenUp and Resolve | 2023 | [EvenUp](https://lsvp.com/stories/evenup-and-the-future-of-legal-ai-faster-smarter-fairer/) |\n| Arif Janmohamed (venture partner) | On Tonkean, Ketryx and Omnifold | 2020 | [Tonkean](https://lsvp.com/company/tonkean/) |\n| Sri Viswanath (Sycamore; ex-Coatue) | Years-long investor and board relationship; seed co-lead | about 2022 | [Rosetta](https://rosetta.to/u/lightspeedvp/how-ai-can-make-every-employee-a-builder-raviraj-jain-sycamore-labs) (inferred attribution) |\n| Coatue | Co-lead on Skild A and Sycamore seed; co-investor on Distyl | 2024 | [Skild](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a) |\n| Deepak Pathak and Abhinav Gupta (Skild) | Portfolio founders; angels in his Sycamore seed | 2024 | [Sycamore](https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed) |\n| Misha Laskin and Ioannis Antonoglou (Reflection) | Board member | 2025 | [AIM](https://aimmediahouse.com/ai-startups/google-deepmind-veterans-raise-130m-to-build-ai-that-codes-without-humans) |\n| Sequoia Capital | Got to Reflection and Ineffable ahead of Lightspeed; Skild seed co-lead | 2023 | [AIM](https://aimmediahouse.com/ai-startups/google-deepmind-veterans-raise-130m-to-build-ai-that-codes-without-humans) |\n| Rushil Goel (Nirvana/Mandl) | Seed lead investor | 2020 | [post](https://lsvp.com/stories/quadrupling-down-in-nirvana-ai-native-insurance-for-the-modern-economy/) |\n| Soham Ganatra and Karan Vaidya (Composio) | A lead; IIT Bombay overlap | multi-year | [post](https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-composio-building-the-backbone-of-ai-agent-intelligence/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Skild seed: *answered 2026-11-10.* Jain says Lightspeed was the first investor and led the seed ([Post Money](https://postmoneypodcast.substack.com/p/investing-in-civilization-scale-problems), 22:03). The lsvp memo, written by Jain and Mhatre, says it was co-led with Sequoia ([memo](https://lsvp.com/stories/skild-is-bringing-generative-ai-to-the-real-world/)). Still open: who wrote the first term sheet.\n- Who originated Reflection: Jain or Alcorn? Skild: Jain describes a thesis-led search in the first person plural (\"we ended up finding\"); he does not say which partner found it.\n- *Answered 2026-11-10:* both the Post Money and Lightwork transcripts were found and mined. Still open: a clean, speaker-labelled transcript of the Sycamore Investment Memo episode (YouTube blocked caption download), and the 2024-03-21 Fortune piece where he described \"two camps\" on an issue (article not located).\n- Cartesia: lsvp's \"Series A 2024\" label against Cartesia's own record.\n- Covenant round and size (2026); Sphinx seed size and lead.\n- His X output (x.com blocked). Medium is done: 9 posts from 2018 to 2022.\n- Whether he is an LP in any fund: nothing found.\n\n## Sources\n- https://lsvp.com/team-member/raviraj-jain/\n- https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a ; https://www.skild.ai/blogs/series-c ; https://websets.exa.ai/websets/directory/skild-funding (aggregator)\n- https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed ; https://sycamore.so/blog/company/building-the-ai-native-enterprise-os/\n- https://rosetta.to/u/lightspeedvp/how-ai-can-make-every-employee-a-builder-raviraj-jain-sycamore-labs\n- https://postmoneypodcast.substack.com/p/investing-in-civilization-scale-problems\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/move-37-ten-years-on-partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/towards-superintelligence-reflection-ai/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-composio-building-the-backbone-of-ai-agent-intelligence/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-luel-the-marketplace-for-multimodal-ai-training-data/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/building-the-ai-for-production-our-investment-in-resolve-ai/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/04/ai-sre-resolve-ai-confirms-125m-raise-unicorn-valuation/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-evenup-the-ai-platform-shaping-the-future-of-legal-services/ ; https://lsvp.com/stories/evenup-and-the-future-of-legal-ai-faster-smarter-fairer/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/quadrupling-down-in-nirvana-ai-native-insurance-for-the-modern-economy/\n- https://www.distyl.ai/blog/news/distyl-secures-20m-from-lightspeed\n- lsvp company pages: sphinx, mandl, covenant, skild-ai, cartesia, mithril, typeface, snorkel-ai, fiddler, osmos, tonkean, dexterity, reliable-robotics, keychain, ketryx, omnifold, fathom, eagle, tessell, aurora, helsing, anduril, p0-security\n- https://aimmediahouse.com/ai-startups/google-deepmind-veterans-raise-130m-to-build-ai-that-codes-without-humans\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=742691 ; https://siliconangle.com/?p=731373\n- https://reflection.ai/blog/building-frontier-open-intelligencence\n- https://cartesia.ai/blog/seed ; https://cartesia.ai/blog/series-a\n", "person:ray-tonsing": "\n# Raymond Tonsing\n\n## One-line take\nA one-owner firm that leads seed and Series A rounds and then keeps buying through SPVs; relevant to me for Saronic, Baseten, Human Intelligence and Sea12.\n\n## Crux\nHe wins by committing early and staying in every round, and by being the only decision-maker. The constraint is the same thing: one owner, a second GP since 2020, and almost no public voice. See [firms/caffeinated-capital.md](../firms/caffeinated-capital.md) for the fund structure.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Sole owner: ADV Schedule A lists only \"TONSING, RAYMOND, MICHAEL\", member since 01/2012, 75%+ [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/165964/PDF/165964.pdf).\n- On Saronic: \"one of the most timely and ambitious companies we have ever partnered with\" [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/naval-maritime-autonomy-company-raises-55-million-in-series-a-fundraise-301951085.html).\n- Fund I raised $7.86M of a $15M target from 29 investors (Form D, 2013) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1577242/000095013813000260/primary_doc.xml).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. Generalist with a defense and industrial tilt; the AI deals are few but early (Baseten Series A 2022, Human Intelligence seed and A, Sea12 A 2026).\n\n## Background\nReal-estate investor before venture; founded Caffeinated in 2009 per press; wrote one of the first cheques into Affirm in 2012; Partner at Akkadian Ventures 2010-2013 and University of Colorado Boulder BA per NFX Signal (aggregator) [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/raymond-tonsing) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/22/raymond-tonsings-caffeinated-capital-seeks-400m-for-fifth-fund/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.caffeinated.com/ | Firm site |\n| Substack / blog | | none found |\n| X | | not checked |\n| LinkedIn | | not checked |\n| GitHub | | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. He was not quoted in the 2025 TechCrunch fund story.\n\n## How they think\nFrom the firm site only: \"the luxury of long-term thinking\" and \"take genuine early-stage risk alongside you\" [src](https://www.caffeinated.com/).\n\n## Read these first\n- [TechCrunch, 2025-01-22](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/22/raymond-tonsings-caffeinated-capital-seeks-400m-for-fifth-fund/).\n- [Caffeinated's ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/165964/PDF/165964.pdf): the fund and SPV list is the best description of how he invests.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot mined.\n\n## Portfolio\nSee the firm page. Personally named: Saronic Series A (lead, 2023-10-10) [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/naval-maritime-autonomy-company-raises-55-million-in-series-a-fundraise-301951085.html); Baseten Series A (participant, 2022-04-26) [src](https://www.baseten.co/blog/announcing-our-series-a/). Board roles at Saronic, Onebrief and Twenty are aggregator-only [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/raymond-tonsing).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Saronic Technologies | Series A ($55M) | 2023-10-10 | yes | 8VC, a16z, Lightspeed, Point72 Ventures | [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/naval-maritime-autonomy-company-raises-55-million-in-series-a-fundraise-301951085.html) |\n| Baseten | Series A | 2022-04-26 | no | Greylock (lead), South Park Commons, Lachy Groom | [src](https://www.baseten.co/blog/announcing-our-series-a/) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found on how he met the Saronic or Baseten founders.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Varun Gupta | Second GP at Caffeinated | 2020 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/22/raymond-tonsings-caffeinated-capital-seeks-400m-for-fifth-fund/) |\n| Nick Marwell | Runs the Caffeinated scout fund | | [src](https://www.caffeinated.com/) |\n| Avichal Garg | Related person on Entrepreneurs Fund I | 2016 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1675710/000167571016000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n## Open questions\nAny interview or podcast; how Saronic and Baseten were sourced.\n\n## Sources\nhttps://techcrunch.com/2025/01/22/raymond-tonsings-caffeinated-capital-seeks-400m-for-fifth-fund/ ; https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/165964/PDF/165964.pdf ; https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/naval-maritime-autonomy-company-raises-55-million-in-series-a-fundraise-301951085.html ; https://www.caffeinated.com/ ; https://signal.nfx.com/investors/raymond-tonsing\n", "person:rayfe-gaspar-asaoka": "\n# Rayfe Gaspar-Asaoka\n\n## One-line take\nKnown for: Ex-Canaan frontier-tech partner, now co-running Geodesic's Alliance Fund; named on Smack (frontier AI lab, co-lead) and Mueon (AI-infra seed).\n\n## Crux\nAn electrical engineer (USC BS, Stanford MS, chip design and signal processing) who built Canaan's frontier-tech practice and now co-leads deep-tech and national-security deals for Geodesic's Alliance Fund [src](https://geodesiccap.com/about/). He writes $5-10M lead or co-lead checks and expects the lead partner to sit on the board [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/geodesic-alliance-fund-founder-faq/). He is limited by fund size: no evidence he has led a round above roughly $30M. His edge is the US-Japan angle, which matters in Indo-Pacific defense deals.\n\n## Differential insight\n- He is one of only two founder contacts the Alliance Fund publishes. The other is Tom Gillespie: \"please reach out to us: tgillespie@geodesiccap.com or rayfe@geodesiccap.com\" [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/geodesic-alliance-fund-founder-faq/)\n- He was promoted to Partner at Canaan in January 2021 for building out \"Rayfe with frontier tech\" [src](http://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.canaan.com/latest/canaan-promotes-rayfe-gaspar-asaoka-and-laura-chau-to-partner). That is about 5-6 years after joining in 2015 [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20250616230916/https://www.canaan.com/team/rayfe-gaspar-asaoka).\n- Smack thesis: \"what stood out was how clearly they understood the problem\" [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-smack-building-an-ai-frontier-lab-for-national-security/). Smack's training with Japanese forces fit Geodesic's US-Japan mission.\n- In 2021 he argued that tiny edge ML, not bigger GPUs, was the frontier: \"These tiny low-power devices at the “edge” are the biggest frontier\" [src](http://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.canaan.com/latest/rayfe-gaspar-asaoka-the-biggest-opportunity-in-machine-learning-is-tiny-announcing-our-series-a-investment-in-edge-impulse)\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. He is at the right deals (Smack frontier-lab co-lead, Mueon AI-infra seed), but his checks are $5-10M, so he is a co-investor or syndicate partner, not a lead who can match a $20M check. He is useful for warm access to defense AI and Japan-linked AI-infra deals.\n\n## Background\n- USC BS EE (graduated in 3 years), Stanford MS EE [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20250616230916/https://www.canaan.com/team/rayfe-gaspar-asaoka)\n- Electrical engineer at Raytheon, then management consultant at Keystone Strategy (dates unconfirmed) [src](https://geodesiccap.com/about/)\n- Canaan, 2015-~2025; Partner from 2021-01-21 [src](http://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.canaan.com/latest/canaan-promotes-rayfe-gaspar-asaoka-and-laura-chau-to-partner); Forbes 30 Under 30 [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20250616230916/https://www.canaan.com/team/rayfe-gaspar-asaoka); Kauffman Fellow [src](https://geodesiccap.com/about/)\n- Partner at Geodesic Alliance Fund (start date unconfirmed; the Alliance Fund FAQ is dated 2025-10-16) [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/geodesic-alliance-fund-founder-faq/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://geodesiccap.com/about/ | team page, 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://geodesiccap.com/insight/ | co-authored investment posts |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/rayfe/ | linked from the Geodesic team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| Email | rayfe@geodesiccap.com | published for founders [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/geodesic-alliance-fund-founder-faq/) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing verified in this pass.\n\n## How they think\n- Teams first: \"Our conviction to invest came from the depth and credibility of the team\" (Mueon) [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-mueon-building-data-center-scale-ai-infrastructure/)\n- Interested in physical limits on compute: power, thermal and memory-compute integration (Mueon); edge ML (Edge Impulse)\n- Defense AI: values operator founders who have lived the problem (Smack)\n\n## Read these first\n- Why we invested in Smack (2026) [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-smack-building-an-ai-frontier-lab-for-national-security/)\n- Why we invested in Mueon (2025) [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-mueon-building-data-center-scale-ai-infrastructure/)\n- Edge Impulse Series A post (2021) [src](http://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.canaan.com/latest/rayfe-gaspar-asaoka-the-biggest-opportunity-in-machine-learning-is-tiny-announcing-our-series-a-investment-in-edge-impulse)\n- Alliance Fund Founder FAQ [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/geodesic-alliance-fund-founder-faq/)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found in this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Smack Technologies | Series A ($32M seed+A combined) | 2026-03-02 | co-lead | Costanoa (co-lead), Point72, Felicis, First In, Scribble | [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-smack-building-an-ai-frontier-lab-for-national-security/) |\n| Mueon | Seed ($15.5M) | 2025-09-17 | no | Intel Capital (lead), Oregon Venture Fund | [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-mueon-building-data-center-scale-ai-infrastructure/) |\n| Edge Impulse (Canaan) | Series A | 2021-05-19 | unconfirmed | | [src](http://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.canaan.com/latest/rayfe-gaspar-asaoka-the-biggest-opportunity-in-machine-learning-is-tiny-announcing-our-series-a-investment-in-edge-impulse) |\n| Canaan book: Apex.AI, Enterpret, Fireflies.ai, HeyMilo, Kepler Communications, Tropic | rounds unconfirmed | | | | [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20250616230916/https://www.canaan.com/team/rayfe-gaspar-asaoka) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- Smack: co-led with Tom Gillespie through the Alliance Fund. This was the first institutional round on record (seed and A announced together). Board seat unconfirmed [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-smack-building-an-ai-frontier-lab-for-national-security/).\n- Mueon: Geodesic joined the stealth-exit seed led by Intel Capital. Co-authored with Neb Mela [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-mueon-building-data-center-scale-ai-infrastructure/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| tom-gillespie | Alliance Fund partner; Smack co-author | 2026 | [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-smack-building-an-ai-frontier-lab-for-national-security/) |\n| neb-mela | Mueon co-author | 2025 | [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-mueon-building-data-center-scale-ai-infrastructure/) |\n| laura-chau | promoted to Canaan Partner the same day | 2021-01-21 | [src](http://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.canaan.com/latest/canaan-promotes-rayfe-gaspar-asaoka-and-laura-chau-to-partner) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact Geodesic start date, and whether he sits on Smack's board\n- His title at Canaan in 2015 (associate?), which would settle fast_promotion\n- Whether he kept any Canaan board seats after leaving\n\n## Sources\n- https://geodesiccap.com/about/\n- http://web.archive.org/web/20250616230916/https://www.canaan.com/team/rayfe-gaspar-asaoka\n- http://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.canaan.com/latest/canaan-promotes-rayfe-gaspar-asaoka-and-laura-chau-to-partner\n- https://geodesiccap.com/insight/geodesic-alliance-fund-founder-faq/\n- https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-smack-building-an-ai-frontier-lab-for-national-security/\n- https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-mueon-building-data-center-scale-ai-infrastructure/\n- http://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.canaan.com/latest/rayfe-gaspar-asaoka-the-biggest-opportunity-in-machine-learning-is-tiny-announcing-our-series-a-investment-in-edge-impulse\n", "person:reid-hoffman": "\n# Reid Hoffman\n\n## One-line take\nThe original network route into frontier AI. He was OpenAI's first donor and its founding angel, co-founded Inflection with Mustafa Suleyman, and is Greylock's AI-lab partner. In his own words, his deals come from a closed circle of trusted introducers, not inbound.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** access through people. His AI path ran from quarterly dinners with Elon Musk, to Demis Hassabis and Mustafa Suleyman, to Sam Altman. He sources almost only through trusted introductions and weights each introducer by domain ([src](https://conversationswithtyler.com/episodes/reid-hoffman/)).\n- **What constrains him:** conflicts. He left OpenAI's board in March 2023 because Greylock portfolio companies kept asking him for API help ([src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/this-kicked-off-with-a-dinner-with)). He sat on Microsoft's board until it announced he would not stand again in 2026 ([src](https://pulse2.com/reid-hoffman-to-leave-microsoft-board-after-nearly-a-decade-to-focus-on-ai-biopharma-venture/)). Greylock as a fund would not do OpenAI's 2019 LP round with no business plan, so he did it personally through his foundation.\n- **What he would not do:** hardware and robotics outside constrained settings (\"bits is where... we anchor our work at Greylock\"), and thin wrappers on someone else's model ([src](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/fireside-chat-with-reid-hoffman-on)).\n\n## Differential insight\nIn their own words (2026-11-10). Mirrored in `data/rows/reid-hoffman-voice-insights.csv`.\n- **Greylock passed on OpenAI's first equity round.** Hoffman brought the 2019 capped-profit LP to Greylock. The firm declined because of its duty to LPs, and he says OpenAI had \"no business plan... no revenue model\". He went in as \"the founding investor as an angel\" through a program-related investment from his foundation, after first making a straight grant ([src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/this-kicked-off-with-a-dinner-with), 2023-03-07).\n- **Check and co-investor.** \"So I put them 50\" (units unstated). He thinks Khosla was \"the only venture fund investing in that round\" ([src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/this-kicked-off-with-a-dinner-with)).\n- **OpenAI board exit, his version.** Portfolio requests for API access made him think \"I might have a real conflict of interest here\". Once off the board, he asked Altman to look at \"unblocking, my portfolio company's thing\" ([src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/this-kicked-off-with-a-dinner-with)). Semafor reported he was privately unhappy and that Altman pushed for the exit ([src](https://www.semafor.com/article/11/19/2023/reid-hoffman-was-privately-unhappy-about-leaving-openais-board)).\n- **His price for joining OpenAI.** He asked Musk to stop saying \"robocalypse\" ([src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/this-kicked-off-with-a-dinner-with)).\n- **Inflection origin.** He first offered Suleyman \"I'll be chair of your board and I'll invest\". Suleyman asked him to co-found instead, at about a day a week ([src](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/fireside-chat-with-reid-hoffman-on), 2023-01-18). The two met \"at a pub in London\" more than a decade earlier, and Greylock hired Suleyman as a venture partner before Inflection was public ([src](https://greylock.com/blog/welcome-mustafa-suleyman/), 2022-01-20).\n- **On lab capital.** \"a billion dollars is really not that much money\". He named compute, talent and data as the real constraints, and called large models an \"Oligopoly\" that mirrors cloud ([src](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/fireside-chat-with-reid-hoffman-on)).\n- **Sourcing and diligence.** \"I almost never meet with an entrepreneur that doesn't come from an introduction\". \"I would rather do reference checks than interviews\". His key question to a reference is the person's greatest weakness ([src](https://conversationswithtyler.com/episodes/reid-hoffman/), 2020-01-15).\n- **Founder test.** \"Would I give the person the money as an investor and then walk away\" for five years ([src](https://www.possible.fm/podcasts/riffs007/), 2024-07-31).\n- **Backing competitors in AI.** \"I think the increasing competition within these is inevitable.\" His rule is to disclose conflicts and never pass information between portfolio companies ([src](https://www.possible.fm/podcasts/riffs060/), 2026-10-28).\n- **Anti-portfolio.** He missed Stripe and passed on SpaceX after a Musk pitch. This comes only from show notes; the transcript is unread ([src](https://thisweekinstartups.com/episodes/816d42-reid-hoffman-on-the-joby-aviation-spac-lessons-from-early-airbnb-facebook-bets-a), 2021-02-25, 26:50 and 49:15).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. He was first in at OpenAI (grant in 2015, then founding angel in the 2019 LP round) and co-founded and chairs Inflection, a $225M seed incubated at Greylock ([src](https://siliconangle.com/2022/05/13/newly-launched-ai-startup-inflection-ai-raises-225m-funding/)). He also backed Adept with Saam Motamedi ([src](https://greylock.com/blog/the-intelligent-future/)) and co-founded Manas AI (AI drug discovery). His view that a billion dollars is not much for a lab matches the owner's large-seed thesis. His caution on robotics and hardware is the main gap with the thesis.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford BS in symbolic systems; master's in philosophy from Oxford ([src](https://greylock.com/team/reid-hoffman/)).\n- PayPal executive vice president and founding board member; co-founded LinkedIn in 2003 ([src](https://greylock.com/team/reid-hoffman/)).\n- Joined Greylock in 2009 ([src](https://greylock.com/team/reid-hoffman/)).\n- Microsoft director from 2017 after the LinkedIn acquisition. In June 2026 he told the board he will not stand for re-election and will serve until the annual meeting ([src](https://pulse2.com/reid-hoffman-to-leave-microsoft-board-after-nearly-a-decade-to-focus-on-ai-biopharma-venture/)).\n- OpenAI board for several years; left 2023-03 ([src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/this-kicked-off-with-a-dinner-with)).\n- Advises Reinvent Capital, reported 2019 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2019/01/22/report-zynga-founder-mark-pincus-is-raising-up-to-700-million-for-an-investment-fund)). Chairman of Village Global ([src](https://www.villageglobal.com/team)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.reidhoffman.org/ | Speeches (Perugia 2024, Bologna 2023) |\n| Substack / blog | https://reidhoffman.substack.com/ | 18 posts 2026-04 to 2026-11 (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/reidhoffman | Not read in this pass |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/reidhoffman | Not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://www.possible.fm/podcasts/ ; Masters of Scale ; Greymatter (Greylock) | Possible publishes weekly, with transcripts on possible.fm |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nThe full catalogue is in `data/rows/reid-hoffman-voice-media.csv` (283 items, 2015-2026). The mined ones:\n- 2020-01 — Conversations with Tyler — Systems, Levers, and Quixotic Quests — https://conversationswithtyler.com/episodes/reid-hoffman/ — sources only through trusted introductions; prefers references to interviews.\n- 2022-01 — Greymatter — Mustafa Suleyman, A Pioneer in Applied AI — https://greylock.com/blog/welcome-mustafa-suleyman/ — the London pub origin of the relationship.\n- 2022-09 — Greymatter — The Intelligent Future (with Saam Motamedi) — https://greylock.com/blog/the-intelligent-future/ — names Inflection and Adept as his deals; \"a rare talent set\".\n- 2023-01 — Elad Gil fireside — https://blog.eladgil.com/p/fireside-chat-with-reid-hoffman-on — Inflection origin; models will be an oligopoly; $1B \"not that much money\".\n- 2023-03 — Newcomer — https://www.newcomer.co/p/this-kicked-off-with-a-dinner-with — OpenAI grant, then angel; Greylock pass; Khosla; board exit.\n- 2023-05 — Greymatter — The AI Friend Zone — https://greylock.com/blog/inflectionai-the-ai-friend-zone/ — why Inflection is a PBC.\n- 2023-06 — Conversations with Tyler — Possibilities of AI — https://conversationswithtyler.com/episodes/reid-hoffman-2/ — open source is \"less safe\"; Instagram had 13 staff when Greylock funded it.\n- 2024-07 / 2025-07 / 2025-10 / 2026-10 — Possible riffs 007, 028, 035, 060 — founder test, team sizing, board trust, backing competitors.\n\n## How they think\n- Scale compute is the driver of progress; expects new mechanisms beyond transformers ([src](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/fireside-chat-with-reid-hoffman-on)).\n- An app built on a model needs network effects, enterprise integration or a deep stack. \"It can't just be, I'm a lightweight front end\" ([src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/this-kicked-off-with-a-dinner-with)).\n- Contrarian: closed frontier models with open access are safer than open source ([src](https://conversationswithtyler.com/episodes/reid-hoffman-2/)).\n- Calls: early to OpenAI (right); his 2023 view that labs would be an oligopoly fits how the market formed. On Inflection, the team largely moved to Microsoft in 2024 (Microsoft licensed the technology) ([src](https://pulse2.com/reid-hoffman-to-leave-microsoft-board-after-nearly-a-decade-to-focus-on-ai-biopharma-venture/)).\n\n## Read these first\n- Newcomer 2023: the OpenAI funding sequence and board exit in his words.\n- Elad Gil fireside 2023: Inflection origin and his frontier-model economics.\n- Conversations with Tyler 2020: how he sources and checks founders.\n- Possible riffs 060 (2026): his current rules on conflicts in AI portfolios.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot mined in this pass. X and LinkedIn posts were not read.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | Nonprofit grant, then LP initial round (via foundation) | 2015; 2019 | no (angel) | Khosla Ventures | [src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/this-kicked-off-with-a-dinner-with) |\n| Inflection AI | Seed ($225M) | 2022-05 | co-founder; Greylock incubated | Microsoft; Bill Gates; Eric Schmidt | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2022/05/13/newly-launched-ai-startup-inflection-ai-raises-225m-funding/) |\n| Adept | Series A ($65M, Greylock and Addition co-led) | 2022-04 | Greylock co-led | Addition | [src](https://greylock.com/blog/a-human-centric-path-to-agi/) |\n| Aurora | Series A | 2018-02 | co-led | Index Ventures | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2019/01/08/report-self-driving-car-startup-aurora-is-raising-capital-at-a-2b-valuation/) |\n| Nauto | Series B | unknown | unknown | | [src](https://greylock.com/portfolio/nauto/) |\n| Tome | board seat (round not captured) | unknown | | | [src](https://greylock.com/blog/inflectionai-the-ai-friend-zone/) |\n| Airbnb | Greylock investment; board observer | unknown | | | [src](https://greylock.com/team/reid-hoffman/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **OpenAI.** It began with dinners with Musk and conversations with Altman. He made a straight grant first. When the capped-profit LP was created he brought it to Greylock, which passed (no business plan, duty to LPs). He then invested personally through a program-related investment from his foundation, saying \"So I put them 50\". Khosla was in the same round ([src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/this-kicked-off-with-a-dinner-with)).\n- **Inflection.** He offered Suleyman to chair and invest; Suleyman asked him to co-found at about a day a week. Suleyman had joined Greylock as a venture partner in January 2022 ([src](https://greylock.com/blog/welcome-mustafa-suleyman/)) and the company was incubated there ([src](https://blog.eladgil.com/p/fireside-chat-with-reid-hoffman-on)).\n- **Airbnb.** By about minute three of the founders' pitch he knew he wanted to invest ([src](https://www.possible.fm/podcasts/riffs035/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Greylock | Partner | 2009 | [src](https://greylock.com/team/reid-hoffman/) |\n| Village Global | Chairman; LP | unknown | [src](https://www.villageglobal.com/team) |\n| Reinvent Capital | Adviser | 2019 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2019/01/22/report-zynga-founder-mark-pincus-is-raising-up-to-700-million-for-an-investment-fund) |\n| Mustafa Suleyman | Inflection co-founder; Greylock colleague | met over a decade before 2022 | [src](https://greylock.com/blog/welcome-mustafa-suleyman/) |\n| Sam Altman | OpenAI donor, angel and board member until 2023-03 | 2015 | [src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/this-kicked-off-with-a-dinner-with) |\n| Elon Musk | quarterly dinners that started his AI focus | about 2013 | [src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/this-kicked-off-with-a-dinner-with) |\n| Saam Motamedi | Greylock co-investor on Adept | 2022 | [src](https://greylock.com/blog/the-intelligent-future/) |\n| Vinod Khosla / Khosla Ventures | co-investor in the OpenAI LP round | 2019 | [src](https://www.newcomer.co/p/this-kicked-off-with-a-dinner-with) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact size and date of his foundation's OpenAI LP check (\"50\": $50M?). Is the foundation the \"Aphorism Foundation\" already in leads?\n- TWiST 2021 (Stripe miss, SpaceX pass, the Clubhouse investment) and 20VC 2020 (Airbnb and the Stripe pass) need transcripts.\n- Greymatter 2019 episodes with Sam Altman and Greg Brockman on scaling AGI (early OpenAI thinking) have no transcript found yet.\n- Manas AI round sizes and Greylock's role.\n- When did he become Village Global chairman?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.newcomer.co/p/this-kicked-off-with-a-dinner-with\n- https://blog.eladgil.com/p/fireside-chat-with-reid-hoffman-on\n- https://conversationswithtyler.com/episodes/reid-hoffman/\n- https://conversationswithtyler.com/episodes/reid-hoffman-2/\n- https://greylock.com/blog/the-intelligent-future/\n- https://greylock.com/blog/welcome-mustafa-suleyman/\n- https://greylock.com/blog/inflectionai-the-ai-friend-zone/\n- https://greylock.com/blog/a-human-centric-path-to-agi/\n- https://greylock.com/team/reid-hoffman/\n- https://www.possible.fm/podcasts/riffs007/\n- https://www.possible.fm/podcasts/riffs028/\n- https://www.possible.fm/podcasts/riffs035/\n- https://www.possible.fm/podcasts/riffs060/\n- https://thisweekinstartups.com/episodes/816d42-reid-hoffman-on-the-joby-aviation-spac-lessons-from-early-airbnb-facebook-bets-a\n- https://www.semafor.com/article/11/19/2023/reid-hoffman-was-privately-unhappy-about-leaving-openais-board\n- https://pulse2.com/reid-hoffman-to-leave-microsoft-board-after-nearly-a-decade-to-focus-on-ai-biopharma-venture/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2019/01/22/report-zynga-founder-mark-pincus-is-raising-up-to-700-million-for-an-investment-fund\n- https://www.villageglobal.com/team\n- https://siliconangle.com/2022/05/13/newly-launched-ai-startup-inflection-ai-raises-225m-funding/\n", "person:remus-brett": "\n# Remus Brett\n\n## One-line take\nLocalGlobe/Phoenix Court GP whose visible work is UK fintech boards, not AI.\n\n## Crux\nBrett is a board-level GP at LocalGlobe (Phoenix Court). Companies House shows a fintech and insurtech board book: Cuvva, Yonder, Yapily. No press release or round announcement names him on any deal. No AI round in data/rounds.csv carries his name. The money is LocalGlobe fund money with outside LPs. The Klein family (Saul and Robin Klein, listed under Investment and Board) approves. How he personally votes is unconfirmed.\n\n## Differential insight\n- The board seats run through the firm: Cuvva and Yonder records give \"Phoenix Court, 2, Brill Place, London\" as his address ([Companies House](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/BSlJ1sKzWwKe29E5rYXa-dj82OM/appointments)).\n- He is an operator turned investor. He was a Finalta Enterprises director from 2004 to 2016 and joined his first VC-style board (Osper) in October 2016 ([Companies House](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/BSlJ1sKzWwKe29E5rYXa-dj82OM/appointments), [second record](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/RjkaRRnPdE1waUIOeb0kMlAU8eY/appointments)). That he founded Finalta is unconfirmed.\n- His only post-2021 seat that could be AI is Translucent Ltd (director since 2022-07-18). What Translucent does is unconfirmed ([Companies House](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/RjkaRRnPdE1waUIOeb0kMlAU8eY/appointments)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nLow. He sits on no frontier-AI deal and shows no seed-AI pattern. He is useful only as a LocalGlobe board contact on fintech.\n\n## Background\n- Finalta Enterprises Ltd: director 2004-01-15 to 2016-06-02 and secretary 2011-2015 (Companies House). Finalta was dissolved. Whether he founded it, and whether McKinsey bought it, is unconfirmed.\n- Board seats (Companies House, DOB Apr 1973 on both records):\n  - Osper: 2016-2018\n  - Lively Minds (a charity): 2019-2026\n  - Cuvva: 2019 to present\n  - Zamna: 2019-2024\n  - Laka: 2020-2021\n  - Yapily: 2020 to present\n  - Cable Technology: 2020-2021\n  - Translucent: 2022 to present\n  - Yonder: 2023 to present\n- Phoenix Court team page: \"Remus Brett, Investment\" ([team](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team)). When he got the GP title is unconfirmed.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team | Team listing only, 2026-11-10 |\n| Companies House | https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/BSlJ1sKzWwKe29E5rYXa-dj82OM/appointments | Main appointments record |\n| LinkedIn | | not checked |\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cuvva | Undisclosed (board from 2019-08) | 2019 | unconfirmed | | [CH](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/BSlJ1sKzWwKe29E5rYXa-dj82OM/appointments) |\n| Zamna | Undisclosed (board 2019-10 to 2024-05) | 2019 | unconfirmed | | [CH](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/BSlJ1sKzWwKe29E5rYXa-dj82OM/appointments) |\n| Laka | Undisclosed (board 2020-02 to 2021-12) | 2020 | unconfirmed | | [CH](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/BSlJ1sKzWwKe29E5rYXa-dj82OM/appointments) |\n| Yapily | Undisclosed (board from 2020-03) | 2020 | unconfirmed | | [CH](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/RjkaRRnPdE1waUIOeb0kMlAU8eY/appointments) |\n| Translucent | Undisclosed (board from 2022-07) | 2022 | unconfirmed | | [CH](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/RjkaRRnPdE1waUIOeb0kMlAU8eY/appointments) |\n| Yonder | Undisclosed (board from 2023-03) | 2023 | unconfirmed | | [CH](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/BSlJ1sKzWwKe29E5rYXa-dj82OM/appointments) |\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| LocalGlobe / Phoenix Court | GP, Investment | unconfirmed | [team](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team) |\n\n## How to reach them\nNo public email or social handle was found. The warmest routes are the founders of his board companies (Cuvva, Yonder, Yapily) or the Phoenix Court office at 2 Brill Place.\n\n## Open questions\n- What does Translucent Ltd do, and is it an AI company?\n- When did he join LocalGlobe, and when did he become GP?\n- Did he found Finalta?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/BSlJ1sKzWwKe29E5rYXa-dj82OM/appointments\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/RjkaRRnPdE1waUIOeb0kMlAU8eY/appointments\n", "person:rich-kotite": "\n# Rich Kotite\n\n## One-line take\nRich Kotite is Radical's newest London partner. He co-wrote \"The Rise of the NeoLab\", which is close to a written version of this lens, and he openly asks NeoLab founders to email him. That makes him an easy way into Radical's London frontier-lab deal flow before he is well known. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he could win:** He is paired with Aaron Rosenberg, DeepMind's former Head of Strategy and Operations, in London, where Radical says DeepMind has produced more NeoLab founders than any other lab. Kotite adds the market mapping and the operator and growth-equity rigour (Bain, Google BizOps, Plaid, Accel) to Rosenberg's researcher network. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/5-more-ai-predictions-for-the-year-2030/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/)\n- **What constrains him:** He is new to Radical. The only Radical deal he is named on is Mantic, where Rosenberg took the board seat and Kotite is an observer. That suggests he is still second chair on frontier-lab deals [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/). Before Radical, his named deals were European B2B and SaaS rounds (security, payments, clinical trials, voice AI), not research labs.\n- **How he thinks about risk:** He underwrites NeoLabs as a power-law bet. He treats compute access, rather than capital, as the binding constraint, and he worries that acquihire exits pay investors little.\n- **What he'd likely never do (inference):** Back a NeoLab without a differentiated research bet *and* a commercial wedge. His essay lists both as requirements for the bull case.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The bottleneck is compute, not capital:** \"the real constraint is no longer capital\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/) (2026-06-21). Labs that lock in multi-year compute can beat better-funded rivals.\n- **He underwrites NeoLabs as a power law:** \"the one that does may justify the entire portfolio\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/).\n- **The base-case exit can leave investors with nothing:** he flags \"reverse-acquihires which may exclude or limit capital flowing to the investors\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/). This is a direct warning for anyone writing large seed checks.\n- **The buyer pool is shrinking:** \"Many natural homes already have taken reservations, so to speak\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/). In other words, most hyperscalers have already paired with a lab.\n- **Where founders come from:** DeepMind has produced more than 40 NeoLab founders, \"more than the other three frontier labs combined\". PhD teams now \"raise without ever having worked in industry\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/).\n- **He positions Radical against the generalists:** Radical is \"one of the few AI-native firms operating at a comparable level\" to Sequoia, Lightspeed and a16z in model-provider rounds [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/).\n- **He sources by open call:** \"please reach out to rich@radical.vc and aaronr@radical.vc\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/).\n- **He is based in London, not SF:** Radical names \"Rich Kotite, previously at Accel and Google\" as based in London [src](https://radical.vc/articles/5-more-ai-predictions-for-the-year-2030/) (2026-06-30).\n- **The Mantic setup:** Rosenberg took the Mantic board seat and Kotite \"joins as a board observer\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/) (2026-09-28).\n- **At Accel he led seed rounds:** his Polar post announces \"leading the company’s $10M seed round\" [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-polar-the-future-of-global-payments-for-ai-native-developers) (2025-06-18).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** The NeoLab essay maps exactly the deals this lens targets: new labs, large first rounds, compute as the constraint, and DeepMind alumni. Radical can lead $25M seeds and co-lead $50–90M seeds (Inherent, Veeda). That makes Kotite a natural co-investor counterpart for $20M checks into large lab seeds. He is also more reachable than Jacobs or Toews, and he asked publicly for inbound. The deals that matter are Mantic (an AI forecasting company from a DeepMind evals lead) and the London DeepMind-alumni pipeline he shares with Rosenberg (Inherent, Latent Labs, Orbital Industries, and companies \"soon to be announced\") [src](https://radical.vc/articles/5-more-ai-predictions-for-the-year-2030/). One caution: his own essay says the base-case exit (an acquihire) can cut investors out, so he is likely to push for strong protective terms.\n\n## Background\n- BA from UC Berkeley, double major in Economics and Operations Research & Management Science; MBA with Distinction from INSEAD (years not stated) [src](https://radical.vc/team/rich-kotite/).\n- Consultant at Bain & Company, then Google's central BizOps group, advising on Alphabet's strategic product and go-to-market initiatives, then an early member of Plaid's international expansion team, leading partnerships and business development. No dates are published [src](https://radical.vc/team/rich-kotite/).\n- Vice President at Accel, London. He is named on Accel posts from 2024-02-29 (Filigran) to 2025-06-25 (Synthflow), and is no longer listed on Accel's team page as of 2026-11-10 [src](https://www.accel.com/news/democratizing-threat-intelligence-our-investment-in-filigran) [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-synthflow-transforming-enterprise-communications-with-voice-ai) [src](https://www.accel.com/team). An earlier lead note said he joined Accel in 2022, but that is unsourced and not confirmed here.\n- Partner at Radical Ventures, London. He is first named on 2026-06-21 (the NeoLab essay). Radical's team-page records were all re-created on 2026-07-24 in a site migration, so they don't show a join date [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/) [src](https://radical.vc/wp-json/wp/v2/team/406).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/rich-kotite/ | Radical team bio; email rich@radical.vc given in the NeoLab essay [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/) |\n| Substack / blog | https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/ | Writes through Radical's AI Perspectives and Radical Reads; 2 bylined pieces as of 2026-11-10 |\n| X | not verified | Team page links only the firm's X account; no personal handle found (web search budget ran out) |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | Not confirmed; the team page links only the company page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Radical Talks (guest co-host) | Mantic episode, Oct 2026 [src](https://radical.vc/articles/beyond-the-scoreboard-how-mantic-turns-forecasts-into-decisions/) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-10 — Radical Talks — Mantic episode with Toby Shevlane and Ben Day (co-hosted with Rosenberg) — https://radical.vc/articles/beyond-the-scoreboard-how-mantic-turns-forecasts-into-decisions/. Radical's write-up quotes only the founders, and no transcript was read, so nothing verbatim from Kotite is recorded.\n\n## How they think\n- **Main thesis:** NeoLabs are a new shape of company. There are 40+ labs that have raised $40B+ since 2024, they specialise by paradigm (world models, recursive self-improvement, RL, continual learning, diffusion, energy-based models), and OpenAI, Cohere and Anthropic are the \"existence proofs\" investors cite [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/).\n- **Risk framework:** He lists five risks. Distillation and open weights; capital intensity; a moving target (Magic and Poolside versus Codex and Claude Code); a shallow buyer pool; and the \"commercial chasm\" between revenue and entry valuations [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/).\n- **Contrarian view:** NVIDIA, not any VC, is the most active investor in model providers, because compute is often well over half of a NeoLab's spend [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/).\n- **Calls:** The Mantic bet (2026-09) is that AI forecasting beats humans and sells to hedge funds. Mantic outscored all 676 humans in the Metaculus Cup before the round [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/). It is too early to say whether the call was right.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"The Rise of the NeoLab\" (2026-06-21, with Rosenberg): the full market map and bull, base and bear framework. https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/\n2. \"The Era of Superhuman Forecasting\" (2026-09-28): why Radical led Mantic. https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/\n3. \"Beyond the Scoreboard\" (2026-10-16): podcast write-up on Mantic's economics. https://radical.vc/articles/beyond-the-scoreboard-how-mantic-turns-forecasts-into-decisions/\n4. Accel, \"Our Investment in Polar\" (2025-06-18): his seed-lead writing style at Accel. https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-polar-the-future-of-global-payments-for-ai-native-developers\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No personal X or LinkedIn activity was verified, and the web search budget ran out before social accounts could be checked. Radical Reads amplifies his work, and his only co-authors on record are Rosenberg at Radical and Andrei Brasoveanu, Luca Bocchio and Matt Robinson at Accel.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mantic (Radical; board observer) | Seed ($25M) | 2026-09-28 | yes (Radical) | Balderton; Thinking Machines; DRW | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/) |\n| Synthflow AI (Accel; co-author) | Series A | 2025-06-25 | yes (Accel) | not stated in the post | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-synthflow-transforming-enterprise-communications-with-voice-ai) |\n| Polar (Accel; co-author) | Seed ($10M) | 2025-06-18 | yes (Accel) | angels incl. Steven Tey, Koen Bok, Anton Osika | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-polar-the-future-of-global-payments-for-ai-native-developers) |\n| PhaseV (Accel; co-author) | Series A ($50M) | 2025-05-13 | co-led (Accel with Insight Partners) | Insight Partners; Exor Ventures; LionBird; Viola | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/supercharging-ai-for-clinical-development-our-investment-in-phasev) |\n| Filigran (Accel; co-author) | Series A | 2024-02-29 | yes (Accel) | not stated in the post | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/democratizing-threat-intelligence-our-investment-in-filigran) |\n| Dash0 (Accel) | Undisclosed (Accel investment announced 2024-11-05) | 2024-11-05 | unknown | — | Listed in his Radical bio [src](https://radical.vc/team/rich-kotite/); the Accel post is bylined by Harry Nelis only [src](https://www.accel.com/news/simplifying-observability-for-developers-everywhere-our-investment-in-dash0) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Mantic (2026):** Founders Toby Shevlane (ex-Google DeepMind senior research scientist who co-led frontier-capability evaluations) and Ben Day met at Cambridge. Radical led the $25M seed alongside Balderton, Thinking Machines and DRW. Rosenberg took the board seat and Kotite became board observer [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/). Mantic's DeepMind origin fits the London alumni pipeline that Kotite and Rosenberg describe. Whether Radical was the first institutional money in is not established: no earlier Mantic round was found.\n- **Timing:** Radical's Mantic post came three months after the NeoLab essay, which asked founders to email Kotite and Rosenberg. No source says Mantic came in through that call.\n- **Accel (2024–25):** He was second author on London early-stage leads with Andrei Brasoveanu (Filigran A, Polar seed), Luca Bocchio (Synthflow A) and Matt Robinson (PhaseV A). In each case he appears as the junior co-author.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Partner (London) | 2026-06 (earliest dated mention) | [src](https://radical.vc/team/rich-kotite/) |\n| Aaron Rosenberg | Co-author (NeoLab essay, Mantic post); Mantic board/observer pair; both London-based | 2026-06 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/) |\n| Mantic | Board observer | 2026-09 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/) |\n| Accel | VP, London | by 2024-02 to 2025 or later | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/democratizing-threat-intelligence-our-investment-in-filigran) |\n| Andrei Brasoveanu (Accel) | Co-author (Filigran, Polar) | 2024 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-polar-the-future-of-global-payments-for-ai-native-developers) |\n| Luca Bocchio (Accel) | Co-author (Synthflow) | 2025 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-synthflow-transforming-enterprise-communications-with-voice-ai) |\n| Matt Robinson (Accel) | Co-author (PhaseV) | 2025 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/supercharging-ai-for-clinical-development-our-investment-in-phasev) |\n| Toby Shevlane, Ben Day | Founders of Mantic (portfolio) | 2026 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/) |\n| Plaid; Google; Bain & Company | Former employers | before Accel | [src](https://radical.vc/team/rich-kotite/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact Radical join date. It is between 2025-06 (last Accel post) and 2026-06-21. Was he hired as Partner directly, which would mean a lateral VP-to-Partner jump?\n- Which of the London deals \"soon to be announced\" (as of 2026-06-30) is he sourcing personally?\n- Personal X and LinkedIn handles. Not verified because the web search budget ran out.\n- Did Mantic raise before the $25M seed, and who was first in?\n- `people/natasha-lim.md` places Kotite in SF, but Radical's own post says London. That file should be corrected by its owner.\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/rich-kotite/ (bio, photo; checked 2026-11-10, image returns HTTP 200)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/ (2026-06-21)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/radical-reads-the-rise-of-the-neolab/ (2026-06-22)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/5-more-ai-predictions-for-the-year-2030/ (2026-06-30; London location)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting/ (2026-09-28)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/beyond-the-scoreboard-how-mantic-turns-forecasts-into-decisions/ (2026-10-16)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/the-era-of-superhuman-forecasting-is-here/ (2026-10-19)\n- https://radical.vc/wp-json/wp/v2/team/406 (team record metadata)\n- Accel posts: Filigran (2024-02-29), Dash0 (2024-11-05), PhaseV (2025-05-13), Polar (2025-06-18), Synthflow (2025-06-25); https://www.accel.com/team\n", "person:richa-mehta": "\n# Richa Mehta\n\n## One-line take\nA growth investor who learned the trade on ICONIQ's 2021-22 SaaS and security leads (1Password, Drata, Ramp, Hightouch), now one of two partners running Radical Ventures' new $1B+ growth vehicle, which is already in Etched and Crusoe. [src](https://radical.vc/team/richa-mehta/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** Radical has a decade of first-in AI seed relationships (Cohere, Covariant and others) but historically could not follow on through hyper-growth rounds. The Breakouts fund exists to fix that, and Mehta brings ICONIQ-style growth underwriting to it. Her edge is access to Radical's own breakouts, not open-market sourcing. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/)\n- **Constraint:** She has no publicly named AI deal yet, at ICONIQ or at Radical. Her named record is SaaS, security and fintech. She shares the growth seat with Ryan Shannon, who has the AI track record (Cohere, Writer, Hebbia, Twelve Labs), so her personal decision weight is unclear. [src](https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/)\n- **What she would not do (inferred):** Seed or pre-product checks. The growth strategy is built for later rounds, and Radical's seed work sits with partners like Rich Kotite and Jordan Jacobs. This is an inference, not something she has said.\n\n## Differential insight\nFew primary sources exist. No podcast, X account, Substack or authored memo was found (web search budget ran out mid-task; see Open questions). Everything below comes from firm pages and company announcements.\n- **Radical's own gap is her mandate.** The Breakouts launch says Radical could not always \"back companies through later rounds of hyper-growth.\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/) (2026-09-21)\n- **Fund size gives her real growth capacity:** \"well over US$1 billion committed to the new strategy in the first closing.\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/)\n- **First Breakouts positions are AI compute:** \"Breakouts is investing in 3 companies: Discovery Loop, Etched and Crusoe.\" Her name is not attached to any of them. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/)\n- **She co-leads, not solo:** Ryan Shannon's page says he \"helps to lead the firm's Growth Strategy\", the same wording as hers. [src](https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/)\n- **ICONIQ pattern of repeat leads:** ICONIQ led Drata's Series B (2021-11) and then co-led the Series C with GGV, which \"teamed up to co-lead this round together\" (2022-12). Mehta is listed on Drata but not named in either announcement. [src](https://drata.com/blog/drata-series-b) [src](https://drata.com/blog/drata-series-c)\n- **Hightouch went ICONIQ-only in its Series B:** \"We are lucky to partner with ICONIQ for this round\" (2021-11-17). Doug Pepper took the board seat. [src](https://hightouch.com/blog/series-b)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** She is not a seed investor and has no sourced frontier-AI deal. But she is the profile you described: an ex-principal from a growth fund who now holds a partner seat with $1B+ of growth capital at a frontier-AI firm (Etched, Crusoe), and that capital is aimed at Radical's own seed companies. She matters as a likely co-investor or follow-on source after a large lab seed, not as an early co-lead. Raise to high if she is named on Etched, Crusoe or Discovery Loop.\n\n## Background\n- University of Pennsylvania, summa cum laude: B.S. Economics (Wharton) and B.A. International Studies. Born in Mumbai, raised in Tokyo. Graduation year not stated. [src](https://radical.vc/team/richa-mehta/)\n- General Atlantic, technology investing team, New York. [src](https://radical.vc/team/richa-mehta/) An aggregator places this at 2017-20 as an analyst; not re-verified here.\n- ICONIQ Capital, Principal. Dates not found. [src](https://radical.vc/team/richa-mehta/)\n- Radical Ventures, Partner, helps lead the Growth Strategy. Join date not found. The firm's CMS shows every team page created on 2026-07-24 during a site rebuild, so that date is not a start date. [src](https://radical.vc/wp-json/wp/v2/team?search=Richa&_fields=id,date,modified,link,slug)\n- The upstream claim that she joined \"from about 2024\" could not be confirmed.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/richa-mehta/ | Firm bio, checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | No Radical post mentions her (WP API search for \"Mehta\", 2026-11-10) |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/richa-mehta-ai-investor/ | Linked from the Radical bio; the handle signals an AI rebrand |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search budget ran out before podcast searches could run.\n\n## How they think\n- No public statements found. Her ICONIQ list (security, compliance, fintech, data infrastructure, edtech) suggests a classic growth-SaaS underwriting style: revenue-quality, category-leader bets. This is inferred from the portfolio, not from anything she has said.\n- Calls right or wrong: nothing sourced.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Radical Breakouts Fund launch: the mandate she runs. https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/\n2. Her Radical bio: the only primary source on her record. https://radical.vc/team/richa-mehta/\n3. Ryan Shannon's bio: her growth co-lead and the AI side of the strategy. https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nThe Radical bio lists her ICONIQ companies. No company announcement found names her personally. None of them is an AI-native company (Hightouch is the closest, as data infrastructure).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Drata | Series B ($100M, ~$1B val) | 2021-11-08 | ICONIQ led | Alkeon; Salesforce Ventures | [src](https://drata.com/blog/drata-series-b) |\n| Drata | Series C ($200M) | 2022-12-07 | ICONIQ co-led | GGV (co-lead); Alkeon; Cowboy; Salesforce Ventures; SVCI | [src](https://drata.com/blog/drata-series-c) |\n| Hightouch | Series B ($40M) | 2021-11-17 | ICONIQ only investor named | none named; Doug Pepper joined board | [src](https://hightouch.com/blog/series-b) |\n| 1Password | round not confirmed | unknown | unknown | | [src](https://radical.vc/team/richa-mehta/) |\n| Ramp | round not confirmed | unknown | unknown | | [src](https://radical.vc/team/richa-mehta/) |\n| Aurora Solar, Automattic, Fireblocks, Incident IQ, Pepper, Virtru | rounds not confirmed | unknown | unknown | | [src](https://radical.vc/team/richa-mehta/) |\n| Radical Breakouts: Discovery Loop, Etched, Crusoe | not stated | by 2026-09-21 | unknown | | Fund-level only; Mehta not named [src](https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/) |\n\n**AI deals she is personally named on: none found.** She was not first in on any sourced deal. ICONIQ came in at Series B or later in every case confirmed here.\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing sourced at the deal level. All that is confirmed: ICONIQ led Drata's B (2021-11) and then co-led the C (2022-12), about 13 months apart. ICONIQ was the sole partner Hightouch named on its B, with Doug Pepper on the board. Mehta's personal role in either is not documented.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Partner, helps lead the Growth Strategy | unknown | [src](https://radical.vc/team/richa-mehta/) |\n| Ryan Shannon | Co-leads Radical's Growth Strategy | unknown | [src](https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/) |\n| ICONIQ Capital / ICONIQ Growth | Former Principal | unknown | [src](https://radical.vc/team/richa-mehta/) |\n| General Atlantic | Former technology investing team, NYC | unknown | [src](https://radical.vc/team/richa-mehta/) |\n| Doug Pepper | ICONIQ board member at Hightouch (overlapping portfolio) | 2021 | [src](https://hightouch.com/blog/series-b) |\n| Rich Kotite | Radical partner colleague (ex-Accel VP, early-stage AI) | unknown | [src](https://radical.vc/team/rich-kotite/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Radical join date and ICONIQ tenure. LinkedIn would settle both. The \"about 2024\" claim upstream is unconfirmed.\n- Is she named on the Etched, Crusoe or Discovery Loop Breakouts checks? Etched and Crusoe press may name Radical partners.\n- Any podcast, conference panel or LinkedIn posts. Searches were not run because the shared web-search budget was used up; rerun on a follow-up.\n- Exact ICONIQ rounds for 1Password (the 2022 Series C ICONIQ-led round is widely reported but not verified here) and Ramp.\n- How Breakouts checks are split between Shannon and Mehta.\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/richa-mehta/\n- https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/\n- https://radical.vc/team/rich-kotite/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/\n- https://radical.vc/wp-json/wp/v2/team?search=Richa&_fields=id,date,modified,link,slug\n- https://drata.com/blog/drata-series-b\n- https://drata.com/blog/drata-series-c\n- https://hightouch.com/blog/series-b\n", "person:rishi-modi": "\n# Rishi Modi\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. This profile is mostly a null result. GV's team page is the only primary source on him. Searched: general web, gv.com (domain-restricted), podcasts/Substack/X, and his prior employers. LinkedIn returned HTTP 999. GV's /news index was read back to 2026-04-20 and not paged further. A gv.com-only search turned up no byline, but its coverage of gv.com is thin, so older posts count as \"not checked\". The GV bio uses \"he\" and \"his\" (\"where he studied\"), so this file does too.*\n\n## One-line take\nA GV associate in San Francisco since 2024, covering AI apps and infrastructure \"from seed to growth\" [src](https://www.gv.com/team/rishi-modi). He has no public deal credit, writing or talks, so he belongs on a watch list, not a call list.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he could win:** GV puts associates' names on deal posts. His NY peer Mounisha Anumolu co-wrote the Translucent Series A post with GP Crystal Huang [src](https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare). He hasn't had a byline like that yet. If one appears, it will name his deal partner and his first credited deal.\n- **What shapes him (sourced, my read):** before GV, he worked almost entirely on corporate-venture and fund-platform teams: SVB Capital, Dell Tech Capital, Citi Ventures, and incubation at Menlo Ventures. Add one go-to-market job (product marketing at CyCognito) and J.P. Morgan tech banking [src](https://www.gv.com/team/rishi-modi). He has no research, pre-training or chip-engineering background. That suggests an apps/infra deal-process investor more than a technical-talent sourcer.\n- **What constrains him:** he is an associate, so GV partners sign the checks. His remit is broad (AI apps and infra, seed to growth) and doesn't point at a thesis [src](https://www.gv.com/team/rishi-modi). In GV's frontier-AI deals the named partners are Munichiello (Flapping, Modular), Hulme (Recursive) and Zeb (Thinking Machines), never an associate (see `firms/gv.md`).\n- **What he'd never do:** nothing found. He has stated no public view.\n\n## Differential insight\nNothing found. He has no podcast, Substack, X account, talk or GV-authored post. GV's team page links only the firm's X account (@gvteam), not a personal one [src](https://www.gv.com/team/rishi-modi). The one secondary trace is a LinkedIn activity snippet in search results: he reposted GV's Sierra Series E co-lead. That says something about GV, not about him, so it gets no insight row. With no verbatim primary quote, `data/rows/rishi-modi-insights.csv` is header-only.\n\nThis does fill one gap in the firm file. `firms/gv.md` said only \"no deals found\". This profile adds the CVC-heavy career path (above) and notes that a domain-restricted search turned up no gv.com post with his byline. That search is not exhaustive.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.**\n- THESIS.md looks for principals and recently promoted partners at $10M+ funds who are doing hot deals before they're famous. He is a two-year associate with no sourced deal credit.\n- I found no frontier-AI, AI-chip or compute deal that names him: none for Flapping Airplanes, Recursive, Thinking Machines, Modular, SambaNova or Lightmatter.\n- The most relevant GV frontier deal is the Flapping Airplanes Series A. Index and Lux co-led it, and NVIDIA and GV participated [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/). GV's own post on that deal is by Dave Munichiello [src](https://www.gv.com/news/why-we-invested-in-flapping-airplanes). Nothing ties Modi to it.\n- Revisit if he gets a byline or a promotion to principal.\n- **check_capacity note:** set to `10m-plus` because GV writes $10M+ checks and to match `people/mounisha-anumolu.md`. He does not personally sign checks.\n\n## Background\nAll of the following comes from GV's bio; the page gives no dates for the pre-GV roles [src](https://www.gv.com/team/rishi-modi).\n- UC Berkeley, business and data science.\n- Pre-GV roles:\n  - Menlo Ventures (helped incubate companies)\n  - CyCognito (product marketing; a security startup)\n  - SVB Capital\n  - Dell Tech Capital\n  - Citi Ventures\n  - J.P. Morgan (tech investment banking analyst, the job right before GV)\n- Joined GV in 2024.\n- Grew up in the Midwest; Chicago sports fan.\n- Secondary (RocketReach, aggregator): a Menlo Ventures \"Venture Intern\" role, 2020–2022 [src](https://rocketreach.co/rishi-modi-email_50097641).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.gv.com/team/rishi-modi | GV team bio, read 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | GV page links only @gvteam |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/rishi-modi | Linked from GV page; fetch blocked (HTTP 999) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found as of 2026-11-10.\n\n## How they think\nNothing found. He has no public writing or talks. His only stated remit is AI applications and infrastructure from seed to growth [src](https://www.gv.com/team/rishi-modi).\n\n## Read these first\nNothing of his to read. The GV bio is the only primary source [src](https://www.gv.com/team/rishi-modi).\n\n## Social engagement\nNo personal X account was found. A LinkedIn snippet in search results shows a repost of GV's Sierra Series E co-lead announcement. That is secondary and unverified, since LinkedIn blocked the fetch.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| none found | | | | | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/rishi-modi) (no investments list on his page) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| GV | Associate, SF | 2024 | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/rishi-modi) |\n| J.P. Morgan | Tech IB analyst (prior) | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/rishi-modi) |\n| Menlo Ventures | Helped incubate companies (prior) | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/rishi-modi) |\n| CyCognito | Product marketing (prior) | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/rishi-modi) |\n| SVB Capital, Dell Tech Capital, Citi Ventures | Prior roles | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/rishi-modi) |\n| Mounisha Anumolu | GV associate peer (NY), same AI remit | 2024+ | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which GV partner does he work under? Huang, Zeb and Sakach are the likely AI-apps candidates. Not sourced.\n- Has he worked on any GV AI-infra deal since 2024? The LinkedIn experience section and GV's pre-2026-04 posts could not be read.\n- Menlo incubation: which companies, and was it tied to Menlo's Anthology Fund? Unknown.\n- **Namesake:** a different Rishi Modi (Carlyle/TCW, Stanford MBA, Michigan Ross) shows up in search. Do not merge them. That person is logged as a `needs-id` lead.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.gv.com/team/rishi-modi (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.gv.com/news (index read 2026-11-10; back to 2026-04-20)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/the-end-of-uniform-computing ; https://www.gv.com/news/when-agents-become-the-customer-part-1 (both by Sidberry and Benjamin; Modi not named)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/translucent-ai-healthcare ; https://www.gv.com/team/mounisha-anumolu\n- https://www.gv.com/news/why-we-invested-in-flapping-airplanes\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/\n- https://rocketreach.co/rishi-modi-email_50097641 (aggregator, secondary)\n- https://uk.marketscreener.com/insider/RISHI-MODI-A13SR5/ (namesake)\n", "person:rob-keith": "\n# Rob Keith\n\n**known_for:** Salesforce Ventures partner since 2014. First byline on the Cohere C, Hugging Face D and Together A2 rounds; now leads defense and mission-critical AI deals.\n\n## One-line take\nKeith is the longest-serving deal partner on Salesforce Ventures' first generative-AI wave: first byline on Cohere C, the Hugging Face D lead (now a $12.9B NVIDIA exit) and the Together A2 lead. Since 2024 his work has moved, with Kevin Wu, to defense and mission-critical AI software, and he co-led a $30M seed into a model lab (Fundamental).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He is a former banker who sits on the Salesforce balance sheet ([bio](https://salesforceventures.com/people/rob-keith/)), so he can pre-empt and lead mid-to-late rounds with no fund-cycle pressure. He sells Salesforce as customer and channel. On Linear the parent became a customer before SFV invested ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-agent-first-developer-stack/)). Second Front's CEO says SFV approached before any raise was planned ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/second-front-ceo-tyler-sweatt-on-innovating-in-govtech-alongside-salesforce-ventures/)). When he wants a deal he shows up in person: Paris for Hugging Face ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/congratulations-hugging-face/)).\n- **What constrains him.** It is a corporate pool, so the thesis must read as enterprise trust and fit Salesforce. On model labs he screens for go-to-market maturity over raw research ([Cohere post](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-cohere/)). He mostly follows or leads B-and-later rounds. He is not a sole lead at the first priced round of a frontier lab.\n- **What he'd likely never do.** Lead a pure-research, pre-product mega-seed for an AGI lab. His one seed in a model company (Fundamental) is enterprise-first: a tabular model with an AWS go-to-market deal ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-structured-data/)). This is inferred, not stated.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Hugging Face was won in person.** He met the team over the July 4 2023 holiday and flew to Paris weeks later: \"we were on a plane to Paris to win the deal in person\" ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/congratulations-hugging-face/), 2026-09-03). The exit came three years later: an NVIDIA definitive agreement at $12.9B against the $4.5B Series D. That is about 2.9x on headline value; preferences and SFV's check size are unconfirmed.\n- **Linear came through a tender, not a round.** SFV invested \"through its employee tender offer\" ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-agent-first-developer-stack/), 2026-08-26). This follows \"Salesforce became a customer earlier this year\". The pattern is customer first, then secondary, which is why no 2026 Linear round shows up in public data.\n- **A seed co-lead in a model lab.** Fundamental: \"excited to have co-led their Seed and to be participating in their Series A\" ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-structured-data/)). The seed was $30M and the A was $225M, led by Oak HC/FT ([AWS release](https://press.aboutamazon.com/aws/2026/2/fundamental-announces-255m-in-funding-and-publicly-launches-its-most-powerful-large-tabular-model-ltm)). Seed date and SFV check size are unconfirmed.\n- **Fast when convinced.** On Code Metal, \"By the time we met Peter Morales last November\", SFV went on to lead the $125M B at $1.25B post about three months later. RTX Ventures was in the same round ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/verified-code-for-mission-critical-systems/)).\n- **Slow-watch on AI infra.** He met fal \"in early 2024\" and bought into the C 18 months later as a non-lead ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-fal/)). He met Together's Vipul Prakash in summer 2023 and led the A2 in March 2024 ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-together-ai/)).\n- **The portfolio as diligence network.** For Nominal C he called \"the dozen customers ... including Forterra, Muon Space\", one of which is his own Forterra deal ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/hardware-learned-to-move-fast-its-data-infrastructure-didnt/), 2026-11-09).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a frontier-lab seed peer. He is a later-round price-setter or follower with a balance sheet, and occasionally a seed co-lead when the lab is enterprise-first (Fundamental). Use him as:\n- **A co-investor or next-round lead** for an enterprise-facing model or AI-infra company at B and later. He led Hugging Face D, Together A2 and Code Metal B.\n- **A route to Salesforce as customer.** Linear shows the parent buying before SFV invests.\n- **Not an LP.** SFV has no fund-of-funds program.\n\nDeals that matter to me: Fundamental seed co-lead, Hugging Face D lead and exit, Together A2 lead, Cohere C and D-II, and Code Metal B lead.\n\n## Background\n- University of Southern California; from San Diego ([bio](https://salesforceventures.com/people/rob-keith/)).\n- Morgan Stanley Technology Banking Group: M&A and capital raising for Veeva, Splunk, eBay and Spotify. Dates not stated (unconfirmed).\n- Joined Salesforce Ventures in 2014, covering early to growth stage in vertical SaaS, security and AI/ML.\n- 2019: moved to Sydney to help launch SFV's Australian and APAC arm. 2021: returned to the US.\n- His bio says he helped lead nCino, DocuSign, Highspot, MuleSoft, Forter, Airwallex, GO1, Drata, AppOmni and Protect AI.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://salesforceventures.com/people/rob-keith/ | Bio and full byline list, checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Email | rob@salesforceventures.com | Published by him [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/elevate-2024-scaling-generative-ai-across-the-enterprise/) and in [Second Front Q&A](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/second-front-ceo-tyler-sweatt-on-innovating-in-govtech-alongside-salesforce-ventures/) |\n| Substack / blog | SFV Perspectives bylines (above) | About 1-2 posts a month in 2025-26 |\n| X | nothing found | not verified |\n| LinkedIn | not verified (fetch blocked, HTTP 999) | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Hosts SFV Elevate CEO-summit panels |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-05 — SFV Elevate (CEO summit) — \"Scaling Generative AI Across the Enterprise\": moderated Cris Valenzuela (Runway), Vipul Ved Prakash (Together AI) and Richard Socher (You.com) ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/elevate-2024-scaling-generative-ai-across-the-enterprise/)). The write-up stresses open source for cost and moving from proof of concept into production.\n- 2025-02 — SFV founder event with Edward Norton (Zeck) and Godard Abel (G2), co-written with Paul Drews ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-new-enterprise-software-playbook-lessons-from-zeck-and-g2/)).\n- No external podcasts found. Web search was unavailable this run.\n\n## How they think\n- **Open source as infrastructure.** He backed Hugging Face and Together. His exit post says community was the moat and infrastructure was the business ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/congratulations-hugging-face/)).\n- **Enterprise trust.** He wants model companies with deployment control (Cohere's VPC/on-prem pitch) and security for AI (Protect AI, Endor Labs).\n- **Verification and mission-critical systems.** Code Metal (formally verified code), Nominal (hardware test data), Forterra and Onebrief (defense).\n- **Calls that worked:** Protect AI, entered 2023-07 and acquired by Palo Alto Networks in 2025-04 ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/congratulations-protect-ai/)). Hugging Face, acquired by NVIDIA in 2026-09.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Congratulations, Hugging Face!](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/congratulations-hugging-face/): how the D was won, and his thesis on the exit.\n2. [Welcome, Cohere!](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-cohere/): his filter for model labs (go-to-market maturity, cloud-agnostic).\n3. [The Agent-First Developer Stack](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-agent-first-developer-stack/): the Linear tender, and the parent as customer.\n4. [Verified Code for Mission-Critical Systems](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/verified-code-for-mission-critical-systems/): the Code Metal lead and his current focus.\n5. [Bringing AI to Structured Data](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-structured-data/): the Fundamental seed co-lead.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no verified X account, and web search was unavailable this run.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI and adjacent deals he is bylined on. \"Led?\" refers to SFV's role. The bylines are the only attribution signal; he is listed first on most.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cohere | Series C ($270M) | 2023-06 | no (Inovia lead) | NVIDIA, Oracle, Index | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-cohere/) |\n| Protect AI | Undisclosed | 2023-07 | helped lead (bio) | Evolution Equity, boldstart, Acrew | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-protect-ai/) |\n| Hugging Face | Series D ($235M at $4.5B) | 2023-08 | yes | Google, Amazon, NVIDIA, Intel, AMD | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-hugging-face/) |\n| Together AI | Series A2 ($106M) | 2024-03 | yes | Coatue, Lux, Kleiner | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-together-ai/) |\n| Endor Labs | Series B ($93M) | 2025-04 | no | DFJ Growth, Lightspeed, Coatue | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-endor-labs/) |\n| fal | Series C ($125M) | 2025-07 | no (Meritech) | a16z, Notable, Bessemer | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-fal/) |\n| Cohere | Series D-II ($500M at $6.8B) | 2025-08 | no (Radical, Inovia) | | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/strengthening-our-commitment-to-cohere/) |\n| Laminar | Seed ($7.2M) | 2025-08 | yes (SFV); his role unconfirmed | Crane | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/sre-ai-ceo-rajsekhar-kadiyala-on-bringing-ai-to-devops-why-move-fast-break-things-doesnt-work/) |\n| Centralize | Undisclosed | 2025-10 | unknown | Y Combinator | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-centralize/) |\n| Forterra | Series C ($238M) | 2025-11 | unknown | | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-forterra/) |\n| Onebrief | Series D | 2026-01 | unknown | | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-onebrief/) |\n| LiveKit | Series C ($100M at $1B) | 2026-01 | no (Index) | Altimeter, Redpoint | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-livekit/) |\n| Fundamental | Seed ($30M) | before 2026-02 (unconfirmed) | co-led | | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-structured-data/) |\n| Fundamental | Series A ($225M) | 2026-02 | no (Oak HC/FT) | Valor, Battery, Hetz | [src](https://press.aboutamazon.com/aws/2026/2/fundamental-announces-255m-in-funding-and-publicly-launches-its-most-powerful-large-tabular-model-ltm) |\n| Code Metal | Series B ($125M at $1.25B) | 2026-02 | yes | Accel, B Capital, Shield, Smith Point, RTX Ventures | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/verified-code-for-mission-critical-systems/) |\n| Linear | Tender (employee) | 2026-08 | unknown | | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/the-agent-first-developer-stack/) |\n| Nominal | Series C | 2026-11 | no (Lightspeed, Insight) | Founders Fund, Sequoia, Lux, GC | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/hardware-learned-to-move-fast-its-data-infrastructure-didnt/) |\n\nNon-AI deals on his page: Drata B 2021, Blockdaemon 2022, Second Front C 2024 (SFV led), OneVest B 2025 (SFV led), Altruist F 2025 (Vanguard exit 2026-08).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Hugging Face D (2023).** Met the team July 4, flew to Paris, and SFV led the round. The NVIDIA agreement at $12.9B followed in Sept 2026 ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/congratulations-hugging-face/)).\n- **Together A2 (2024).** Met Prakash in summer 2023 and led the extension eight months later. He then put Prakash on his Elevate panel ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-together-ai/)).\n- **Second Front C (2024).** SFV approached unprompted, after deep prior work, and the CEO raised outside his plan ([src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/second-front-ceo-tyler-sweatt-on-innovating-in-govtech-alongside-salesforce-ventures/)).\n- **Code Metal B (2026).** Dozens of references reached him before he met the CEO in Nov 2025; he led the round in Feb 2026.\n- **Linear (2026).** Salesforce adopted Linear internally, then SFV bought in through the employee tender.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Salesforce Ventures | Partner | 2014 | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/people/rob-keith/) |\n| Morgan Stanley | Tech banker (pre-2014, unconfirmed dates) | | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/people/rob-keith/) |\n| Emily Zhao | Co-bylined on lab and infra deals | 2023 | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-cohere/) |\n| Kevin Wu (SFV) | Defense and security deal partner | 2024 | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-second-front/) |\n| Dom Pusateri, Caroline Fiegel | Seed-stage co-bylines (Fundamental, Laminar, Centralize, Linear) | 2025 | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/welcome-centralize/) |\n| Vipul Ved Prakash (Together AI) | Led his A2; panel guest | 2023 | [src](https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/elevate-2024-scaling-generative-ai-across-the-enterprise/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When was he promoted to Partner? Is there an investment-committee sign-off above him (Paul Drews, John Somorjai)?\n- What was the date of the Fundamental seed, and who co-led it?\n- What were SFV's check sizes for Hugging Face D and Code Metal B? What does Salesforce book on the NVIDIA–Hugging Face close (10-Q gain line)?\n- Does he hold any board seats? None are stated on his page.\n\n## Sources\n- https://salesforceventures.com/people/rob-keith/\n- https://salesforceventures.com/perspectives/ (each post linked above)\n- https://press.aboutamazon.com/aws/2026/2/fundamental-announces-255m-in-funding-and-publicly-launches-its-most-powerful-large-tabular-model-ltm\n- Web search was unavailable this run (budget used up); all facts come from direct fetches of primary pages.\n", "person:rob-toews": "\n# Rob Toews\n\n## One-line take\nRadical's first and still lead Bay Area partner, Toews is a public AI forecaster (annual Forbes predictions, self-graded) whose predictions often show up later as deals. Among other bets they were Radical's founding investor in Reka, led Yutori's and Emerald AI's seeds, and sit on Emerald's board.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** They are the Bay Area face of an AI-only firm whose founders built Toronto's AI scene (Hinton, Fei-Fei Li). They can bring luminary angels into their rounds: Radical introduced Fei-Fei Li to Twelve Labs and she invested [src](https://radical.vc/articles/ctrlf-for-video-search-our-investment-in-twelve-labs/), and Jeff Dean and Fei-Fei Li joined their Yutori seed [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/yutori-launches-stealth-15m-seed-funding-build-consumer-ai-assistants). The Forbes column (yearly \"10 AI predictions\" since end of 2020) is a sourcing magnet and a public record of their theses [src](https://hgcapital.com/insights/orbit-podcast/predicting-ai-rob-toews-scorecard-from-the-frontier).\n- **What constrains them:** Radical's lead partners share deals; most of their named companies are co-credited (Shannon on Hebbia, Writer and Delphina; Welch on Generalist; Katz on Crusoe; Basu on Nabla). In several big rounds they followed someone else's lead (Hebbia A by Index, DatologyAI A by Felicis, Ricursive A by Lightspeed). They are not the pre-company incubator; that is Jordan Jacobs' role (World Labs).\n- **What they'd never do (inference):** Non-AI deals at Radical. Their only non-AI-looking deal, Muon Space, sits under their \"AI data centers in space\" thesis [src](https://radical.vc/articles/10-ai-predictions-for-2025/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Predictions become deals.** In Dec 2024 they wrote \"Web agents will go mainstream, becoming the next major killer application in consumer AI.\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/10-ai-predictions-for-2025/) Three months later they led Yutori's $15M seed, a web-agent assistant from ex-Meta FAIR researchers [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/yutori-launches-stealth-15m-seed-funding-build-consumer-ai-assistants). Read their December column as their pipeline for the next year.\n- **Compute bull who doubts GPU accounting.** On Hg's Orbit podcast (2026) they questioned \"whether it's still justifiable for these companies to use five-plus-year depreciation schedules\", while they back Crusoe and sit on Emerald AI's board [src](https://hgcapital.com/insights/orbit-podcast/predicting-ai-rob-toews-scorecard-from-the-frontier).\n- **Expects today's training to look wasteful:** \"egregiously resource intensive, the way we did AI in 2026\" (same source). That fits DatologyAI (data efficiency) and their continual-learning essays [src](https://radical.vc/articles/rob-toews-on-overcoming-ais-catastrophic-forgetting/).\n- **Against the \"SaaS is dead\" trade:** \"I think those stocks have been oversold.\" (Hg Orbit, 2026). They co-led Writer's Series C, an enterprise-app bet.\n- **Closed models win, incumbents have distribution (Dec 2023):** \"I think the best models will continue to be closed source.\" and \"the Microsoft’s of the world have a really strong advantage\" [src](https://paulineyang.substack.com/p/ai-2023-recap-2024-predictions-with). That is consistent with Reka (an independent closed-model lab sold to enterprises) and with net-new-workflow apps rather than features.\n- **2026 calls on X:** \"Anthropic will go public. OpenAI will not.\" and \"Details of SSI’s research and technology will leak to the public.\" [src](https://x.com/_RobToews/status/2003945222494138389)\n- **Emerald board is a real director seat.** Emerald AI's Aug 2026 Form D names only three directors: Varun Sivaram, Toews and Specks, while NVIDIA's NVentures is only an observer [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2047797/000204779726000004/xslFormDX01/primary_doc.xml) (via [people/christina-buchanan.md](christina-buchanan.md)).\n- **Laredo Labs disappeared from their bio.** An earlier Radical bio listed Laredo Labs (AI codegen; Radical co-led an $8.5M seed with Horizons, Dec 2023) [src](https://radical.vc/?p=945) [src](https://bestofai.com/article/laredo-wants-to-use-gen-ai-to-automate-dev-work-techcrunch). The current team page drops it [src](https://radical.vc/team/rob-toews/). That could mean a wind-down or reassignment (not verified).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** They back model labs and frontier-adjacent infrastructure at seed and A (Reka founding seed; Ricursive Intelligence, the AlphaChip founders' AI-for-chips lab; Generalist robotics foundation models; DatologyAI; Emerald AI for AI-energy). They also lead AI-native automation and apps (Yutori, Writer, Hebbia, Delphina). Radical's growth vehicles mean they can carry positions to large rounds, which makes them a natural co-investor for a $20M check into a large seed (Generalist $400M B, Ricursive $300M A). Deals that matter most to me: Reka, Ricursive, Generalist, Yutori, Emerald AI, DatologyAI. Caveat: they are a lateral senior partner, not the \"rising junior\" alpha profile.\n\n## Background\n- Undergraduate degree from Stanford; JD from Harvard Law School; MBA from Harvard Business School [src](https://radical.vc/team/rob-toews/).\n- Started at Bain & Company in Silicon Valley; staffer in the Obama White House on autonomous-vehicle policy; strategy at Zoox (later acquired by Amazon) [src](https://radical.vc/team/rob-toews/). Dates not found.\n- Highland Capital Partners: Principal leading the firm's AI investing; backed Domino Data Lab, Eko, Idelic and StackRox (acquired by Red Hat) [src](https://radical.vc/team/rob-toews/). An aggregator dates this May 2019 to Sep 2021 [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/rob-toews).\n- Joined Radical Ventures as Partner in Sept 2021, its first investment partner in the Bay Area (announced 2021-09-20 by Jordan Jacobs) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/rob-toews/ | Radical team page, checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.forbes.com/sites/robtoews/ | Forbes AI column; annual 10 predictions every December plus a self-grading piece (e.g. [2025-12-14](https://www.forbes.com/sites/robtoews/2025/12/14/grading-our-2025-ai-predictions-how-did-we-do/), [2025-12-22](https://www.forbes.com/sites/robtoews/2025/12/22/10-ai-predictions-for-2026/), [2026-06-21](https://www.forbes.com/sites/robtoews/2026/06/21/5-more-ai-predictions-for-the-year-2030/)). Reposted on radical.vc |\n| X | https://x.com/_RobToews | Posts their prediction threads; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/robtoews/ | Linked from Radical team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Regular guest on Radical Talks (host Molly Welch) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026 — Hg Orbit (host Jonathan Wulkan) — \"Predicting AI: Rob Toews' scorecard from the frontier\" — [link](https://hgcapital.com/insights/orbit-podcast/predicting-ai-rob-toews-scorecard-from-the-frontier) — SaaS oversold; doubts five-plus-year GPU depreciation; deep-tech renaissance (chips, robotics, lithography, space) could challenge NVIDIA and ASML.\n- 2025-08 — Radical Talks — \"Rob Toews on Overcoming AI's Catastrophic Forgetting\" — [link](https://radical.vc/articles/rob-toews-on-overcoming-ais-catastrophic-forgetting/) — continual learning as a future moat (summary from episode page only).\n- Radical Talks with Varun Sivaram and Molly Welch on what holds back AI infrastructure — [listing](https://rephonic.com/podcasts/radical-talks) (date not confirmed).\n- 2023-12 — Cross Validated (Pauline Yang) — AI 2023 recap and 2024 predictions — [link](https://paulineyang.substack.com/p/ai-2023-recap-2024-predictions-with) — closed models stay ahead; incumbents have distribution; startups should build net-new workflows.\n- 2022-12 — Prateek Joshi's Infinite ML podcast — investing in AI startups, LLMs and robotics — [listing](https://podknife.com/episodes/investing-in-ai-startups-large-language-models-robotics-future-of-generative-ai-rob-toews-partner-at-radical-vc).\n- Speaker at TED AI, SALT and HumanX [src](https://www.ted.com/speakers/rob_toews) [src](https://www.humanx.co/speakers/rob-toews).\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** multimodal AI is the future (Twelve Labs) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/ctrlf-for-video-search-our-investment-in-twelve-labs/); scaling laws spreading to robotics and biology (Generalist, Nabla Bio); web agents as the consumer killer app (Yutori); AI data centers in space and AI energy (Muon Space, Emerald AI); labs \"moving up the stack\" into applications [src](https://radical.vc/articles/10-ai-predictions-for-2025/).\n- **Contrarian views:** SaaS stocks oversold; GPU depreciation schedules too generous; 2026-era AI will look wasteful in hindsight (all Hg Orbit 2026).\n- **Calls right or wrong:** Radical reports 6 of their 10 2024 predictions came true (Chief AI Officers, transformer alternatives, Microsoft–OpenAI tension) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/10-ai-predictions-for-2025/). 2025's \"Trump and Musk will have a messy falling-out\" came true in public. The open 2026 call, \"Anthropic will go public. OpenAI will not.\", is not graded here.\n\n## Read these first\n1. 10 AI Predictions For 2026 (Forbes, 2025-12-22): https://www.forbes.com/sites/robtoews/2025/12/22/10-ai-predictions-for-2026/ — their current pipeline of theses.\n2. Grading Our 2025 AI Predictions (Forbes, 2025-12-14): https://www.forbes.com/sites/robtoews/2025/12/14/grading-our-2025-ai-predictions-how-did-we-do/ — where they admit misses.\n3. 10 AI predictions for 2025 (radical.vc, 2024-12-23): https://radical.vc/articles/10-ai-predictions-for-2025/ — the web-agent call that preceded Yutori.\n4. \"The Gaping Hole In Today's AI Capabilities\" (continual learning), summarized at https://radical.vc/the-gaping-hole-in-todays-ai-capabilities/\n5. Hg Orbit transcript (2026): https://hgcapital.com/insights/orbit-podcast/predicting-ai-rob-toews-scorecard-from-the-frontier\n\n## Social engagement\nThey mostly post their yearly prediction threads on X [src](https://x.com/_RobToews/status/2003945222494138389) and Radical repackages their Forbes columns in Radical Reads. Who they reply to and who amplifies them was not checked (X not crawled).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Reka | Seed (founding) | 2022 | yes (board) | | [src](https://www.reka.ai/news/announcing-our-60m-funding-to-build-generative-models-and-advance-ai-research) |\n| Reka | Series A ($58M) | 2023-06 | co-led | DST Global, Snowflake Ventures, Nat Friedman | [src](https://nordic9.com/news/reka-came-out-of-stealth-with-a-58-million-round-led-by-dst-global-partners-joined-by-radical-vc/) |\n| Twelve Labs | Seed ($5M) | 2022-03 | no | Index Ventures, Fei-Fei Li | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/ctrlf-for-video-search-our-investment-in-twelve-labs/) |\n| Twelve Labs | Seed extension ($12M) | 2022-12 | yes | | [src](https://bigdatawire.com/this-just-in/twelve-labs-raises-12m-seed-extension-and-announces-partnership-with-oracle) |\n| Hebbia | Series A ($30M) | 2022-09 | no | Index (lead), Thiel, Druckenmiller | [src](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of) |\n| Unlearn | Undisclosed | 2022 | unknown | | [src](https://radical.vc/portfolio/unlearn/) |\n| Delphina | Undisclosed | 2023-12 | unknown | | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/automating-traditional-data-science-why-we-invested-in-delphina/) |\n| Laredo Labs | Seed ($8.5M) | 2023-12 | co-led | Horizons Ventures | [src](https://bestofai.com/article/laredo-wants-to-use-gen-ai-to-automate-dev-work-techcrunch) (aggregator of TechCrunch) |\n| DatologyAI | Seed ($11.65M) | 2024-02 | no | Amplify (lead), Conviction | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2024/02/22/datologyai-raises-11-65m-automate-data-curation-efficient-ai-training) |\n| DatologyAI | Series A ($46M) | 2024-05 | no | Felicis (lead), Amplify | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2024/05/08/datologyai-raises-46m-streamline-ai-model-training-data-diets/) |\n| Nabla Bio | Series A | 2024-05 | yes | | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/why-we-led-nabla-bios-series-a/) |\n| Writer | Series C ($200M at $1.9B) | 2024-11 | co-led | Premji, ICONIQ Growth, Salesforce Ventures | [src](https://businesswire.com/news/home/20241112482564/en) |\n| Yutori | Seed ($15M) | 2025-03 | yes | Felicis, Elad Gil, Sarah Guo, Jeff Dean, Fei-Fei Li | [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/yutori-launches-stealth-15m-seed-funding-build-consumer-ai-assistants) |\n| Emerald AI | Seed | 2025-07 | yes (board) | NVIDIA, Jeff Dean, John Doerr | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/reimagining-data-centers-as-energy-assets/) |\n| Crusoe | Series E ($1.375B) | 2025-10 | no | | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/building-ai-factories-radicals-investment-in-crusoe/) |\n| Ricursive Intelligence | Seed ($35M, Sequoia led) | 2025-12 | no | Sequoia | inferred from portfolio year; [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/startup-ai-lab-ricursive-seriesa-unicorn/) |\n| Ricursive Intelligence | Series A ($300M at $4B) | 2026-01 | no | Lightspeed (lead), DST, NVentures, Sequoia | [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/startup-ai-lab-ricursive-seriesa-unicorn/) |\n| Generalist | Series B ($400M at $2B) | 2026-06 | yes (Radical; shared with Welch) | 8VC, USV, NVIDIA, Bezos Expeditions | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2026/06/04/generalist-ai-raises-400m-2b-valuation-build-general-intelligence-real-world/) |\n| Muon Space | Undisclosed | n/a | unknown | | [src](https://radical.vc/team/rob-toews/) |\n| Highland deals: Domino Data Lab, Eko, Idelic, StackRox | Undisclosed | 2019–21 | unknown | | [src](https://radical.vc/team/rob-toews/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Reka (first in, then doubled down):** Radical was the founding investor (Reka's own words) with Toews credited and a board seat. Radical then co-led the $58M Series A with DST about a year later [src](https://www.reka.ai/news/announcing-our-60m-funding-to-build-generative-models-and-advance-ai-research) [src](https://nordic9.com/news/reka-came-out-of-stealth-with-a-58-million-round-led-by-dst-global-partners-joined-by-radical-vc/).\n- **Twelve Labs (followed, then led):** They joined Index's $5M seed in Mar 2022 and Radical brought Fei-Fei Li in. Nine months later Radical led the $12M seed extension [src](https://radical.vc/articles/ctrlf-for-video-search-our-investment-in-twelve-labs/) [src](https://bigdatawire.com/this-just-in/twelve-labs-raises-12m-seed-extension-and-announces-partnership-with-oracle).\n- **Yutori (thesis first, then lead):** They predicted web agents in Dec 2024 and led Yutori's $15M stealth-exit seed in Mar 2025. Their quote: \"Yutori's vision is to build a 'chief of staff for everyone'\" [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/yutori-launches-stealth-15m-seed-funding-build-consumer-ai-assistants).\n- **Emerald AI:** Radical led the seed \"with major participation from Nvidia\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/reimagining-data-centers-as-energy-assets/). Toews is a Form D director as of Aug 2026.\n- **Crusoe:** A late-stage entry at Series E, written up with David Katz (Radical's compute practice). Toews was not first in [src](https://radical.vc/articles/building-ai-factories-radicals-investment-in-crusoe/).\n- **Ricursive:** Sequoia's Stephanie Zhan led the seed and doubled down in the A. Radical appears as a participant; early coverage misnamed it \"Radical AI\" until Crunchbase corrected it [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/startup-ai-lab-ricursive-seriesa-unicorn/).\n- Intro paths for any deal: nothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Partner, leads Bay Area office | 2021-09 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/) |\n| Jordan Jacobs | Hired them (announced the move) | 2021-09 | same |\n| Highland Capital Partners | Former Principal, led AI | 2019-05 to 2021-09 (aggregator) | [src](https://radical.vc/team/rob-toews/) |\n| Zoox | Former strategy | n/a | [src](https://radical.vc/team/rob-toews/) |\n| Emerald AI / Varun Sivaram | Board director | 2025 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2047797/000204779726000004/xslFormDX01/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Reka / Dani Yogatama | Founding investor, board | 2022 | [src](https://www.reka.ai/news/announcing-our-60m-funding-to-build-generative-models-and-advance-ai-research) |\n| Writer | Board director (aggregator only) | 2024-11 | [src](https://d1kn7cmwp1llb6.cloudfront.net/investors/rob-toews) |\n| David Katz | Co-author on Crusoe | 2025-10 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/building-ai-factories-radicals-investment-in-crusoe/) |\n| Molly Welch | Co-credited on Generalist; hosts their podcast episodes | 2025 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/rob-toews-on-overcoming-ais-catastrophic-forgetting/) |\n| Fei-Fei Li | Brought into Twelve Labs seed; co-angel in Yutori | 2022 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/ctrlf-for-video-search-our-investment-in-twelve-labs/) |\n| Felicis | Recurring co-investor (Yutori, DatologyAI, Ricursive) | 2024 | [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/yutori-launches-stealth-15m-seed-funding-build-consumer-ai-assistants) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is the Writer board seat real? Only an aggregator says so.\n- Did Radical join Ricursive's Dec 2025 seed or enter at the A?\n- Why was Laredo Labs dropped from their bio?\n- Which deals are the \"others still in stealth\" on their team page?\n- Exact dates for the White House, Zoox and Bain roles (LinkedIn not fetched).\n- Their 2025 grading column: which misses they owned (Forbes returned 403).\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/rob-toews/\n- https://radical.vc/?p=945 (earlier bio)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/10-ai-predictions-for-2025/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/ctrlf-for-video-search-our-investment-in-twelve-labs/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/reimagining-data-centers-as-energy-assets/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/building-ai-factories-radicals-investment-in-crusoe/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/why-we-led-nabla-bios-series-a/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/automating-traditional-data-science-why-we-invested-in-delphina/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/rob-toews-on-overcoming-ais-catastrophic-forgetting/\n- https://hgcapital.com/insights/orbit-podcast/predicting-ai-rob-toews-scorecard-from-the-frontier\n- https://paulineyang.substack.com/p/ai-2023-recap-2024-predictions-with\n- https://x.com/_RobToews/status/2003945222494138389\n- https://www.forbes.com/sites/robtoews/ (403 on fetch; URLs from search)\n- https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/yutori-launches-stealth-15m-seed-funding-build-consumer-ai-assistants\n- https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/startup-ai-lab-ricursive-seriesa-unicorn/\n- https://bestofai.com/article/laredo-wants-to-use-gen-ai-to-automate-dev-work-techcrunch\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2047797/000204779726000004/xslFormDX01/primary_doc.xml\n- https://signal.nfx.com/investors/rob-toews (aggregator)\n- https://d1kn7cmwp1llb6.cloudfront.net/investors/rob-toews (aggregator)\n- Deal sources cited in the Portfolio table\n", "person:robert-natzler": "\n# Robert Natzler\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (strategics-2 sweep). Built from five Baillie Gifford transcripts in which he speaks (Apr 2024, May 2025, Oct 2025, Nov 2025, Jul 2026), his firm bio and press. Firm context, the vehicle table and BG's whole AI book are in `firms/baillie-gifford.md`.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the working half of the two-person team that runs Baillie Gifford's private book. He sits on the four-person IC for the GP/LP funds, co-manages Schiehallion and tells the Anthropic and Tekever deals first-hand. For a seed investor he is the person who buys your winners at Series C and later, not a co-investor.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** Baillie Gifford's pitch is that it is neither a VC (a forced seller at IPO) nor a crossover (a flipper). It comes in \"typically at a Series C round\" and funds \"several more rounds\" before the IPO, then brings in the public teams, who sometimes cornerstone ([Nov 2025 transcript](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf)). Natzler is the person who says this to founders and runs the pipeline. A colleague introduced him as having \"together helped found the private company business with Peter\" Singlehurst ([Oct 2025](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/)).\n- **What constrains him:** his hurdle is a \"better than one in 20 chance of a five-times outcome over five years\" ([Nov 2025](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf)). He does not control trust allocations: each trust's board decides. The team is about 10 people and converts about 1% of the roughly 1,000 companies it meets a year into about 10 investments ([Jul 2026](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2026-quarterly-strategy-recordings-q2-schiehallion-fund-10064099/)).\n- **What he'd never do:** take control, or back pre-revenue ideas. In his words, these are \"not very early-stage concepts being pitched by a great team with a deck\" ([Nov 2025](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf)). He held off on AI labs for years because they had \"the organisational maturity of an early-stage company\".\n\n## Differential insight\n- **BG strung Anthropic along for about three years.** It met the company during the FTX stake \"fire sale\" and kept asking for more time: \"we just kept on saying, we want another six months\" ([Oct 2025](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/)). He priced the Series F entry at \"about 20 times annual recurring revenue\" (about $180B enterprise value). He sized the market from US coding salaries, citing \"almost $500bn a year\" from US government data, and was reassured by Anthropic's gross margin at a time when coding startups were \"reselling Anthropic at a loss\" ([Nov 2025](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf)).\n- **He publicly reversed his own AI-stack thesis.** In April 2024 he said the team \"biases away from the middle of that stack and towards the ends\" (chips and applications) ([Apr 2024 transcript](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/xzflnyl5/20251218115053_baillie-gifford-webinar-schiehallion-manager-update-transcript-april-2024.pdf)). In May 2025 Singlehurst named Tenstorrent (chips) and Runway (application) as Schiehallion's only two US new investments, and Natzler said they were \"mostly staying away from simple pure play AI laboratories\" ([May 2025](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q2-schiehallion-fund-finding-opportunity-amid-uncertainty-10054767/)). Three months later they bought Anthropic, the purest middle-of-stack company there is.\n- **Tekever was won with a counter-offer to a buyout.** Warburg Pincus offered about $1B to buy Tekever outright. BG \"counter-offered with a 500m US dollar term sheet\" for a minority stake on the road to IPO and settled at $640M, \"roughly 15 times price earnings\". It brought in NATO as co-lead, and NATO's technical diligence lead, who had run the US Reaper drone programme, later joined Tekever ([Oct 2025](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/)). This is BG's template for a rare lead: an off-market name where the founder wants independence. Press dates the round to 2024-11 (EUR 70M, with the NATO Innovation Fund) ([Bloomberg Law](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/drone-maker-tekever-raises-70-million-from-spacex-backer-nato)).\n- **The IC is four people, and nobody is paid on their own deals.** The team is \"remunerated by what our clients eat\", meaning total portfolio return plus a portion of carry. Whoever sources a company gets \"first dibs\" on working on it. Ideas a sourcer can't staff go to \"those four faces up at the top\", the IC, who reassign them ([Oct 2025](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/)). This is why BG holdings don't become orphans when someone leaves.\n- **Fund III terms.** It is BG's first off-the-shelf 10-year GP/LP fund, charging \"a 1 in 10 fee with the carry ratcheting up\". The target was $800M, and more than half was raised at first close in October 2025 ([Oct 2025](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/)). Fund I (2019) was evergreen. Fund II (2021) had an unusual 15-year term.\n- **Admitted mistake:** \"we deployed more capital in 2021 than obviously I wish we had\" ([Nov 2025](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf)).\n- **Skin in the game (2024):** asked whether the managers own Schiehallion shares, Singlehurst said a substantial part of his wealth was in it. Natzler: \"I can't say the same. That's something that I'm hoping to change.\" ([Apr 2024](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/xzflnyl5/20251218115053_baillie-gifford-webinar-schiehallion-manager-update-transcript-april-2024.pdf)).\n- **Anthropic IPO timing:** in July 2026 he said Anthropic \"has also confidentially filed its IPO paperwork\". Schiehallion's public share rose to about 40% after the SpaceX and Bending Spoons listings, and he said the fund has \"realised and reinvested almost $400 million\" since 2019 ([Jul 2026](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2026-quarterly-strategy-recordings-q2-schiehallion-fund-10064099/)).\n- **China pricing:** he bought RedNote at \"a little over 20 times price-earnings\" because forced American selling made Chinese assets cheap ([Nov 2025](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf)). In 2024 he said the only others leaning into China were \"the sovereign funds from the Gulf area\" ([Apr 2024](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/xzflnyl5/20251218115053_baillie-gifford-webinar-schiehallion-manager-update-transcript-april-2024.pdf)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low as a co-investor, medium as a relationship.** He will never be in a $600M seed: BG enters at Series C with about $200M revenue. He matters to the owner in three ways:\n1. **Buyer of the owner's winners.** He is the later-round price-setter and holder through IPO for frontier-AI companies that reach scale (Anthropic F/G/H; chips via Graphcore and Tenstorrent). BG pays up for quality (\"eye-watering\", 20x ARR on Anthropic) but takes months to years to commit.\n2. **Off-market leads.** When a founder wants independence over a buyout, BG will lead and price (Tekever, Hadrian co-lead). That is relevant to AI-native hardware or defence companies in the owner's book at Series C.\n3. **LP angle:** BG's GP/LP fund series raises from institutions such as Kentucky TRS and is an allocator to growth, not to seed managers. Not an LP prospect.\nDeals that matter: Anthropic Series F (2025), Graphcore (2020), Tekever (2024), Tenstorrent Series D (2024, Schiehallion; by role).\n\n## Background\n- MA in Philosophy, Politics and Economics, University of Oxford, graduated 2014 ([BG bio](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-disruption-week-webinars-2025-disruption-week-private-companies-getting-ahead-of-the-crowd-10058507/)).\n- Joined Baillie Gifford in 2015 and worked in Emerging Markets, UK and Long Term Global Growth equities. Private companies full time from 2018. Played \"a leading role in designing our training program for new joiners\" ([BG bio](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-disruption-week-webinars-2025-disruption-week-private-companies-getting-ahead-of-the-crowd-10058507/)).\n- Earlier, per an aggregator (unconfirmed): development economist for an African solar co-operative (Accra), co-founder of CABAL Student Investments (funded student theatre), and a Clifford Chance internship ([TheOrg, aggregator](https://theorg.com/org/baillie-gifford/org-chart/robert-natzler)).\n- Graphcore: a search-index copy of his team-page bio says he \"led research\" on Graphcore, Tanium, Sana Biotechnology, Pet Circle and Loft (see firms/baillie-gifford.md). The live page loads dynamically and could not be re-fetched on 2026-11-10, so this is **unconfirmed this pass**.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/funds/schiehallion-fund/ | Fund page with bio and a list of his webinars, roughly quarterly (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | TheOrg mirrors a LinkedIn-style history (aggregator) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest on BG Disruption Week and Schiehallion webinars |\n| Inbound | privateopportunities@bailliegifford.com | BG's stated private-deal inbox ([bailliegifford.com](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/individual-investors/about-us/private-companies/)) |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** BG says over 80% of deals come from its own network, so a warm intro beats the inbox. The routes are (a) one of BG's 130 public investors, who meet him at a quarterly \"human architecture\" session ([Oct 2025](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/)); (b) an existing BG portfolio CEO, through BG's IPO-mentoring forums; (c) any analyst on the roughly 10-person team, since whoever sources a deal gets \"first dibs\" and the IC sees it. He speaks at Schiehallion AGMs and webinars every spring and in the quarterly updates.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-07: BG Schiehallion Q2 update. SpaceX IPO, Bending Spoons listing, Anthropic confidential IPO filing, capital recycling. [link](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2026-quarterly-strategy-recordings-q2-schiehallion-fund-10064099/)\n- 2026-05: Schiehallion Manager Insights. NAV +33% to 2026-01, 53 holdings, 62% of the fund in profitable companies, FTSE 250 entry. [link](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2026-q2-manager-insights-shiehallion-10062597/)\n- 2025-11: BG Disruption Week, \"Private companies: getting ahead of the crowd\". Anthropic, Revolut, Mottu, RedNote. [transcript](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf)\n- 2025-10: \"Navigating the $14tn private market\" (with Singlehurst and Brian Kelly). Fund history, team design, Tekever, Anthropic. [link](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/)\n- 2025-05: Schiehallion annual webinar. \"Mostly staying away from simple pure play AI laboratories\". [link](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q2-schiehallion-fund-finding-opportunity-amid-uncertainty-10054767/)\n- 2024-04: Schiehallion manager update. AI stack \"ends\" thesis, China, secondary-market sales. [transcript](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/xzflnyl5/20251218115053_baillie-gifford-webinar-schiehallion-manager-update-transcript-april-2024.pdf)\n- 2022-03: Schiehallion Annual Manager Update, billed as \"Deputy Manager\". [link](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2022-q1-the-schiehallion-fund-annual-manager-update-10008226/)\n- 2021-05: Schiehallion Manager Insight, billed as \"investment manager of The Schiehallion Fund\". [link](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2021-q2-schiehallion-manager-insights-may-2021-int-we-0290/)\n\n## How they think\n- **Customer set is destiny.** \"Your customer set, in a sense, is your destiny\". He saw OpenAI as a consumer and entertainment product and Anthropic as an enterprise and coding product ([Nov 2025](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf)).\n- **Revenue that \"lands on your lap\" is not growth-stage maturity.** Labs that go from $0 to $500M in six months on other people's R&D budgets are early-stage organisations with late-stage numbers.\n- **Two kinds of deals:** \"beachfront real estate\" that the BG brand gets him into (Revolut), and off-the-beaten-path names found through the public teams (Mottu, Tekever).\n- **Clusters matter more in AI.** He says generative AI caused \"re-clustering\" back to San Francisco ([Nov 2025](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf)).\n- **Calls:** he was right to sit out late 2022, by his own account. He says the AI-lab wait was right, but the Series F price was far above where BG could have entered in 2023. He reversed his \"ends of the stack\" view in 2025.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Nov 2025 Disruption Week transcript](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf): the Anthropic thesis in his words.\n2. [Oct 2025 \"Navigating the $14tn private market\"](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/): how the team, IC, pay and funds work; the Tekever story.\n3. [Apr 2024 Schiehallion update](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/xzflnyl5/20251218115053_baillie-gifford-webinar-schiehallion-manager-update-transcript-april-2024.pdf): the \"ends of the AI stack\" thesis he later dropped.\n4. [Jul 2026 Q2 update](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2026-quarterly-strategy-recordings-q2-schiehallion-fund-10064099/): what BG does with IPO liquidity.\n\n## Social engagement\nNo personal social presence found. He speaks only through BG channels.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Anthropic | Series F ($13B at $183B post) | 2025-09 | no | ICONIQ (lead), Fidelity, Lightspeed | [transcript](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf) |\n| Tekever | Series B (EUR 70M; BG says entry valuation $640M) | 2024-11 | yes (co-lead with NATO Innovation Fund) | NATO Innovation Fund, NSSIF, Crescent Cove, Iberis | [Oct 2025](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/), [Bloomberg Law](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/drone-maker-tekever-raises-70-million-from-spacex-backer-nato) |\n| Tenstorrent | Series D ($693M at $2B pre) | 2024-12 | no | Samsung Securities, AFW Partners (leads), Fidelity, Bezos Expeditions, LG, Hyundai | [insideHPC](https://insidehpc.com/2024/12/tenstorrent-closes-693m-series-d-round), by role (Schiehallion new holding, [May 2025](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q2-schiehallion-fund-finding-opportunity-amid-uncertainty-10054767/)) |\n| Graphcore | Series D extension (BG's earliest round in repo; his round not stated) | 2020-02 | no | not stated | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/graphcore-grows-series-d-to-350m); bio credit unconfirmed this pass |\n| Revolut, Mottu, RedNote (non-AI) | rounds not stated | 2024-2025 | unknown | | [transcript](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Anthropic.** First contact came at the FTX-estate stake sale, \"back when they were a research lab\". Over about three years BG watched Anthropic build KPIs and forecasting and kept deferring. Anthropic was \"delighted\" when BG finally came in at Series F. The key worry was \"whether they were going to be naïve\" as safety-first founders, and BG \"saw them make good business decisions time and time again\" ([Nov 2025](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf); [Oct 2025](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/)). BG followed into the G and H rounds (firms/baillie-gifford.md).\n- **Tekever.** See Differential insight: a counter-offer to Warburg's buyout, $500M opening, $640M close, NATO as co-lead. Growth went from high double digits to low triple digits after NATO introduced Tekever to member militaries ([Oct 2025](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/)). BG then co-led the 2026-09 Series D (firms/baillie-gifford.md).\n- **Mottu.** Found through BG's public emerging-markets investors, not VC networks. The thesis was that trackable bikes turn bad credit into good credit ([Nov 2025](https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Baillie Gifford | at: Investment Manager; a leader of the Private Companies Team | 2015 | [bio](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-disruption-week-webinars-2025-disruption-week-private-companies-getting-ahead-of-the-crowd-10058507/) |\n| The Schiehallion Fund | at: investment manager (2021), Deputy Manager (by 2022-03), now co-manager and decision maker | 2021 | [2021](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2021-q2-schiehallion-manager-insights-may-2021-int-we-0290/), [2022](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2022-q1-the-schiehallion-fund-annual-manager-update-10008226/) |\n| Baillie Gifford Private Companies GP/LP fund series (Fund II 2021, Fund III 2025) | at: investment committee member | date not found | [bio](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-disruption-week-webinars-2025-disruption-week-private-companies-getting-ahead-of-the-crowd-10058507/) |\n| Peter Singlehurst | Co-built the private company business; manager to his deputy and co-manager role | 2018 | [Oct 2025](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/) |\n| NATO Innovation Fund | Co-lead on Tekever | 2024 | [Oct 2025](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/) |\n| Warburg Pincus | Outbid on Tekever (BG's minority offer beat a $1B buyout) | 2024 | same |\n\n## Open questions\n- The names of the other two members of the four-person IC (presumably Singlehurst plus two others). Not stated.\n- Which Graphcore round he researched, and whether he was on the 2024 SoftBank sale. BG sold its Graphcore shares on the acquisition ([May 2025](https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q2-schiehallion-fund-finding-opportunity-amid-uncertainty-10054767/)).\n- Runway: Singlehurst counts it among Schiehallion's new investments in the year to 2025-01-31, which is before the Series D on 2025-04-03 that the repo records. Schiehallion's entry round is unconfirmed.\n- Who at BG sourced Tenstorrent and Runway (it is not clear it was Natzler).\n- Exact date of the deputy-to-co-manager change.\n\n## Sources\n- https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/wolc5wyt/20251119143030_disruption-week-private-companies.pdf\n- https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-disruption-week-webinars-2025-disruption-week-private-companies-getting-ahead-of-the-crowd-10058507/\n- https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q4-synthesis-navigating-the-private-market-10059140/\n- https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2025-q2-schiehallion-fund-finding-opportunity-amid-uncertainty-10054767/\n- https://media.bailliegifford.com/mws/xzflnyl5/20251218115053_baillie-gifford-webinar-schiehallion-manager-update-transcript-april-2024.pdf\n- https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2026-quarterly-strategy-recordings-q2-schiehallion-fund-10064099/\n- https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2026-q2-manager-insights-shiehallion-10062597/\n- https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2022-q1-the-schiehallion-fund-annual-manager-update-10008226/\n- https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/insights/ic-video/2021-q2-schiehallion-manager-insights-may-2021-int-we-0290/\n- https://www.bailliegifford.com/en/uk/intermediaries/funds/schiehallion-fund/\n- https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/drone-maker-tekever-raises-70-million-from-spacex-backer-nato\n- https://insidehpc.com/2024/12/tenstorrent-closes-693m-series-d-round\n- https://globalventuring.com/graphcore-grows-series-d-to-350m\n- https://theorg.com/org/baillie-gifford/org-chart/robert-natzler (aggregator)\n", "person:robin-rombach": "\n# Robin Rombach\n\n## One-line take\nA frontier-model founder who lends his name and his network to a Factorial Capital microfund. The value is his deal flow and his opinion of founders, not his checks.\n\n## Crux\nRombach is a full-time founder (Black Forest Labs), not a professional investor. Factorial Capital runs \"individual partnerships with exceptional technical founders\", and Rombach's is one of two named ones (the other is Clem Delangue's HuggingFund). The likely split is that Rombach sources deals and judges the technical team, while Matt Hartman's Factorial runs the vehicle. That split is inferred and unconfirmed. Whose money it is: Factorial Capital's vehicle. No Form D for a separate \"Robin x Factorial\" entity was found, and whether Rombach has his own capital in it is unconfirmed. Who approves: unconfirmed. The site names only Hartman as contact.\n\n## Differential insight\n- The partnership started from a cap-table tie: \"Matt and Robin met when Factorial invested in Black Forest Labs.\" ([factorialcap.com/robin](https://factorialcap.com/robin)). BFL's own seed announcement (2024-08-01) does not name Factorial. It names a16z (lead), angels, General Catalyst and MätchVC ([bfl.ai](https://bfl.ai/announcements/24-08-01-bfl)). So Factorial's BFL stake was never announced; its round and timing are unconfirmed.\n- The microfund page went live around 2025-08-19. That date is the upload timestamp in its image filename (`robinpenguin_1755610318231.png`), found in the site JS bundle ([bundle](https://factorialcap.com/assets/index-BHVOjRGD.js)). The image was made with FLUX.\n- Check size: \"We write ~$500k checks but are flexible\" and \"happy to be the only $250k to help you quit your job\" ([page](https://factorialcap.com/robin)).\n- No deal is publicly named as a Robin x Factorial deal. Checked: the page itself, Factorial's site bundle, the BFL announcement, and the repo's Factorial rows. The Wayback Machine has no snapshot of /robin.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** This is not a co-lead for a $20M check: the microfund is about $500k a deal. Rombach matters as a **source** and a **reference**. He sees image and video model researchers in the European and Stability/Ommer-lab diaspora before anyone else does. Relevant deals: fal and Prior Labs (personal angel), and BFL itself.\n\n## Background\n- Lead author of \"High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models\", the paper behind Stable Diffusion, written with Blattmann, Lorenz, Esser and Ommer ([arXiv 2112.10752](https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.10752)).\n- Co-founded Black Forest Labs, which launched on 2024-08-01 with a $31M seed led by a16z ([bfl.ai](https://bfl.ai/announcements/24-08-01-bfl)).\n- Investment Partner on Robin x Factorial since about 2025-08 ([factorialcap.com/robin](https://factorialcap.com/robin)). He got the seat through Factorial's BFL investment.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Microfund page | https://factorialcap.com/robin | live 2026-11-10 |\n| Company | https://bfl.ai | co-founder |\n| Contact route | mailto:matt@factorialcap.com | the only email on Factorial's site, for Hartman; there is no direct Rombach contact |\n| X / LinkedIn | unconfirmed | not verified this pass |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found that I verified in this pass.\n\n## How they think\n- From the microfund page: the focus is \"engineering-led teams building AI-native companies\". That covers open-source AI infrastructure, training specialist models, and new uses of models ([page](https://factorialcap.com/robin)).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Latent Diffusion paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.10752): the research that earned Rombach the network.\n- [Robin x Factorial page](https://factorialcap.com/robin): the microfund's terms.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing verified in this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| fal | Seed or Series A (unclear) | 2024-09-18 | no | a16z, Kindred | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/18/fal-ai-which-hosts-media-generating-ai-models-raises-23m-from-a16z-and-others/) |\n| Prior Labs | Pre-seed/seed | 2025 | no | — | [Prior Labs about](https://www.priorlabs.ai/about) |\n\nBoth are personal angel listings. Neither source says they went through Factorial.\n\n## Deal stories\n- fal hosts FLUX models commercially. The angel check lines up with a distribution partner (inference: the companies are linked, and the reason for the check is not sourced).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Matt Hartman (Factorial) | microfund partner | 2025-08 | [page](https://factorialcap.com/robin) |\n| Black Forest Labs | co-founder | 2024 | [bfl.ai](https://bfl.ai/announcements/24-08-01-bfl) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When and in what round Factorial invested in BFL.\n- Whether Rombach puts in his own capital or carry, and whether he has a veto.\n- Which Factorial deals since 2025-08 Rombach sourced.\n\n## Sources\n- https://factorialcap.com/robin ; https://factorialcap.com/assets/index-BHVOjRGD.js\n- https://bfl.ai/announcements/24-08-01-bfl\n- https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.10752\n- https://www.priorlabs.ai/about\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/18/fal-ai-which-hosts-media-generating-ai-models-raises-23m-from-a16z-and-others/\n", "person:roni-hiranand": "\n# Roni Hiranand\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. Sources: his gv.com profile and two GV posts he wrote or co-wrote, company press releases, and trade press quoting him. No podcasts, talks or Substack were found. X (@RoniHiranand) returned HTTP 402 and is not indexed by search, so no posts were read. LinkedIn was not read. Pronoun \"he\" comes from GV's own bio [src](https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand).*\n\n## One-line take\nGV's London principal for European \"hard\" frontier tech (satellites, glasses-free XR, AI reliability, AI-data-center power). He is quoted as the GV voice on four leads or co-leads from 2024 to 2025. He has **no** frontier-lab, AI-chip or compute deal, but his book sits in rounds next to Lux, NVIDIA, Founders Fund and Bessemer.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he finds capital-efficient European hardware or infrastructure teams early and lets GV write the first big institutional check.\n  - Distance: GV led the €10M seed about three months after a $2.7M Finnish pre-seed [src](https://siliconcanals.com/distance-technologies-secures-e10m).\n  - Climate X: GV led the $18M Series A [src](https://climate-x.com/articles/press-releases/series-a).\n  - Phoebe: GV co-led the $17M seed with Cherry [src](https://newsio.com/2025/08/20/ai-immune-system-phoebe-lands-backing-from-google-arm).\n- **His filter, in his own writing:** unit economics in hardware (\"profitable in its first year of sales\"), vertical integration forced by scarcity, and **proprietary data** as the durable moat once models commoditise [src](https://www.gv.com/news/endurosat-democratising-orbit).\n- **Constraint:** as a principal, the GV leads he is quoted on top out around $18M. On the one breakout (EnduroSat), GV came in as a *new* investor in the Oct-2025 ~$104M round, after Founders Fund had led the May-2025 round. GV was not first, and there was no named lead [src](https://www.satellitetoday.com/finance/2025/10/30/endurosat-raises-another-100m-with-backing-from-google-ventures/), [src](https://therecursive.com/endurosat-104m-funding-expand-satellite-production/).\n- **What he'd likely never do (inference):** back a pure-model lab. Nothing in his record or writing points at model building. His stated thesis is that value accrues to data and physical infrastructure *around* models.\n\n## Differential insight\nEvery bullet is mirrored in `data/rows/roni-hiranand-insights.csv`.\n- **The data moat comes before the model moat.** In the EnduroSat post he argues that as ML models spread, value moves to \"the durable advantage shifts to the proprietary data used to train them\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/endurosat-democratising-orbit) (2025-11-13). He frames space hardware as a *training-data acquisition* play. That's the bridge from his \"space\" deal to AI.\n- **Hardware judged on software metrics.** He highlights that \"EnduroSat was profitable in its first year of sales\" and has software-like margins on every line [src](https://www.gv.com/news/endurosat-democratising-orbit).\n- **Scarcity as an advantage.** He writes that the team, lacking early VC, \"was forced to build nearly every component in-house\", and he treats that as the moat [src](https://www.gv.com/news/endurosat-democratising-orbit). This is a contrarian read: under-funding early is a positive signal.\n- **Competitors as customers** is a proof point he looks for: \"their customers now include their direct competitors\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/endurosat-democratising-orbit).\n- **AI reliability gap (Phoebe thesis):** \"AI has transformed how code is written, but software reliability has not kept pace.\" [src](https://newsio.com/2025/08/20/ai-immune-system-phoebe-lands-backing-from-google-arm) (2025-08-20). This is his clearest AI-native-automation thesis.\n- **Speed as a diligence signal (Distance):** \"We've been impressed with how fast the team has moved\" [src](https://siliconcanals.com/distance-technologies-secures-e10m) (2024-09). GV led the seed about three months after the pre-seed.\n- **Founder-domain fit (Climate X):** he credits the founders' \"deep expertise in corporate risk management\" [src](https://climate-x.com/articles/press-releases/series-a) (2024-06-26).\n- **AI energy as a continuum from marketing to AI.** In the Scope3 post, co-written with GP Erik Nordlander, they \"see a continuum of needs and services\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/scope3-ai-sustainable) (2024-10-10). It's an early (2024) GV bet on AI-compute energy measurement.\n- **Title conflict:** SatelliteToday quotes him as \"Partner at GV\" on EnduroSat (2025-10-30) [src](https://www.satellitetoday.com/finance/2025/10/30/endurosat-raises-another-100m-with-backing-from-google-ventures/). GV's own page still says Principal on 2026-11-10 [src](https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand). Treat it as a press error until confirmed.\n- **Hidden Asia network:** before GV he invested at Ant Financial's VC arm (Bace Capital) and at Hong Kong's Vectr Ventures [src](https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand). HKU alumni named him to Forbes 30 Under 30 Asia 2021, Finance & VC [src](https://www.alumni.hku.hk/hku-young-alumni-selected-as-forbes-30-under-30-asia/). An HK-to-London path is unusual among GV London investors.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is a rising principal at a $13B single-LP fund that can write $10M+ checks, and he does early European frontier-hardware leads. But **none of his deals are frontier labs, AI chips or compute**. The deals that matter to you:\n- **Verse** (AI-data-center power software). Series B of $54M, 2026-06-18, led by Bessemer, with **GV and NVIDIA** participating. The software is being integrated into NVIDIA's DSX AI Factory design [src](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/verse-enterprises-raises-54m-series-b/). GV's earlier $20.5M Series A (2024-05-22) was led by GV GP **Andy Wheeler**, not Hiranand [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/verse-secures-20-5-million-in-series-a-funding-led-by-gv-to-help-organizations-reduce-electricity-costs--emissions-302152878.html). Verse appears on Hiranand's GV investment list [src](https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand). He likely inherited coverage (inference).\n- **Phoebe** (AI agents that keep software running, ex-Stripe Europe founders). GV co-led the $17M seed with Cherry, 2025-08 [src](https://newsio.com/2025/08/20/ai-immune-system-phoebe-lands-backing-from-google-arm). This is AI-native automation, the Serval-adjacent category you like.\n- **EnduroSat.** GV + **Lux** + Riot came in Oct 2025 (~$104M) [src](https://www.satellitetoday.com/finance/2025/10/30/endurosat-raises-another-100m-with-backing-from-google-ventures/). GV followed into the $205M round at >$1B in Oct 2026, co-led by Riot and Atreides [src](https://vestbee.com/insights/articles/enduro-sat-lands-205-m). It is not AI, but it is a unicorn on his book, which puts him in the same rounds as Lux.\n\nHe is useful as a co-investor contact for European AI-infra and AI-energy seeds, and as the GV London route into those rounds. He is not a lab or chip partner: route those through Munichiello (see `firms/gv.md`).\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Hong Kong. BA in History, University of Hong Kong. Guest lecturer on food science at HKU [src](https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand).\n- Vectr Ventures (Hong Kong): early-stage investor [src](https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand). An aggregator lists him as analyst from Jan 2015 [src](https://startupintros.com/people/roni-hiranand) (secondary; dates unverified). Crunchbase lists him as Associate at Vectr [src](https://www.crunchbase.com/person/roni-hiranand) (secondary).\n- Bace Capital (Ant Financial's VC arm): investor, early stage [src](https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand). Dates not found.\n- Forbes 30 Under 30 Asia 2021, Finance & VC [src](https://www.alumni.hku.hk/hku-young-alumni-selected-as-forbes-30-under-30-asia/).\n- GV, London: Principal. His earliest dated GV quote is June 2024 (Climate X) [src](https://climate-x.com/articles/press-releases/series-a). Join date not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand | GV bio and investment list, read 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | GV news posts: https://www.gv.com/news/endurosat-democratising-orbit ; https://www.gv.com/news/scope3-ai-sustainable | 2 posts (2024-10, 2025-11) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/RoniHiranand (linked from GV page) | Could not read (HTTP 402); followers unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://hk.linkedin.com/in/roni-hiranand-7a9ba937 | Not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. One search for podcasts and interviews returned nothing that names him.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:**\n  - Space as a horizontal, mass-manufactured data layer.\n  - Proprietary data as the post-commoditisation AI moat.\n  - AI reliability infrastructure (\"software immune system\").\n  - AI-compute energy (Scope3, Verse).\n  - Headset-free XR for auto, aero and defense (Distance).\n- **Contrarian view:** early capital scarcity builds the moat, in hardware as much as anywhere [src](https://www.gv.com/news/endurosat-democratising-orbit).\n- **Calls:**\n  - EnduroSat went from GV's entry in Oct 2025 to >$1B in Oct 2026 [src](https://vestbee.com/insights/articles/enduro-sat-lands-205-m). It looks right so far.\n  - In Nov 2025 he wrote that EnduroSat was on track to be the largest satellite maker outside Starlink \"by early next year\". Not verified.\n\n## Read these first\n1. EnduroSat: Democratising Access to Orbit (2025-11-13). His full investment logic: unit economics, vertical integration, the data moat. https://www.gv.com/news/endurosat-democratising-orbit\n2. Scope3: A New Way to Make AI Sustainable and Efficient (2024-10-10, with Nordlander). An early AI-energy thesis. https://www.gv.com/news/scope3-ai-sustainable\n3. Phoebe seed coverage (2025-08). His AI-reliability quote. https://newsio.com/2025/08/20/ai-immune-system-phoebe-lands-backing-from-google-arm\n4. Climate X Series A release (2024-06-26). What he looks for in founders. https://climate-x.com/articles/press-releases/series-a\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X could not be read and he has no indexed posts.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Phoebe (AI software reliability) | Seed $17M | 2025-08-20 | Co-led (GV + Cherry); he is the quoted GV voice | Cherry Ventures | [src](https://newsio.com/2025/08/20/ai-immune-system-phoebe-lands-backing-from-google-arm) |\n| Verse (AI-data-center energy software) | Series B $54M | 2026-06-18 | No (Bessemer led) | Bessemer, NVIDIA, Norrsken VC | [src](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/verse-enterprises-raises-54m-series-b/) |\n| Verse | Series A $20.5M | 2024-05-22 | GV led, via **Andy Wheeler**, not Hiranand | Coatue, CIV, MCJ Collective | [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/verse-secures-20-5-million-in-series-a-funding-led-by-gv-to-help-organizations-reduce-electricity-costs--emissions-302152878.html) |\n| EnduroSat (satellites) | ~$104M (label unclear) | 2025-10-30 | Not stated; GV a new investor; he is quoted | Riot Ventures, Lux Capital, EIC Fund, Shrug Capital | [src](https://www.satellitetoday.com/finance/2025/10/30/endurosat-raises-another-100m-with-backing-from-google-ventures/) |\n| EnduroSat | $205M at >$1B | 2026-10 | No (Riot + Atreides co-led) | EIC Fund, Founders Fund, Lux, House, Omnes, Giant, Endeavor Catalyst | [src](https://vestbee.com/insights/articles/enduro-sat-lands-205-m) |\n| Distance Technologies (glasses-free XR) | Seed €10M | 2024-09-24 | Yes (GV led; he is quoted) | FOV Ventures, Maki.vc | [src](https://siliconcanals.com/distance-technologies-secures-e10m) |\n| Climate X (climate-risk data) | Series A $18M | 2024-06-26 | Yes (GV led; he is quoted) | Pale Blue Dot, CommerzVentures, A/O, Blue Wire, PT1, Unconventional, WTI | [src](https://climate-x.com/articles/press-releases/series-a) |\n| Scope3 (ad and AI emissions) | Latest round (amount not in post) | 2024-10-10 | Yes (GV led; post by Nordlander + Hiranand) | not named | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/scope3-ai-sustainable) |\n| Again (CO2-to-chemicals) | Series A $43M | 2024-07 | Co-led (GV + HV Capital); his role is from the profile list only | HV Capital, EIFO, ACME, Atlantic Labs | [src](https://www.kompas.vc/news/why-we-invested-in-again), [src](https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand) |\n| Comstruct (construction procurement) | €12.5M (Series A per aggregators) | 2025-02 | Co-led (GV + 20VC); profile list only; also on Luna Schmid's list | 20VC, Booom, Puzzle Ventures | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/06/comstruct-a-platform-to-digitize-the-construction-industry-raises-135-million/), [src](https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand) |\n| SideQuest (VR app store) | Series A $12M | 2022-10 | GV led (M.G. Siegler took the board); his role is from the profile list only | not checked | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2022/10/03/googles-gv-backs-sidequest-an-unofficial-meta-quest-app-store), [src](https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand) |\n\n**Frontier-AI / AI-chip / compute deals:** none.\n- The closest are Verse (AI-data-center power; follow-on next to NVIDIA, not first, not led), Phoebe (AI agents, co-led first institutional round) and Scope3 (AI energy measurement).\n- On none of them was he first money in a lab or chip company.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Distance:** Maki.vc and FOV co-led a $2.7M pre-seed in June 2024 [src](https://en.ain.ua/2024/06/14/distance-technologies-pre-seed-investment). GV led the €10M seed about three months later, with both pre-seed leads following. Hiranand's quote stresses the team's speed [src](https://siliconcanals.com/distance-technologies-secures-e10m). GV was the first big-fund money in, not the first money overall.\n- **EnduroSat:**\n  - Before GV: CEECAT led €20M in Feb 2023, then Founders Fund led €43M in May 2025 [src](https://therecursive.com/endurosat-104m-funding-expand-satellite-production/).\n  - GV entered in Oct 2025, alongside Lux and Riot, about five months after Founders Fund [src](https://www.satellitetoday.com/finance/2025/10/30/endurosat-raises-another-100m-with-backing-from-google-ventures/).\n  - His own post two weeks later makes the case [src](https://www.gv.com/news/endurosat-democratising-orbit).\n  - Within a year the company passed $1B [src](https://vestbee.com/insights/articles/enduro-sat-lands-205-m).\n- **Phoebe:**\n  - Founded 2023 by Matt Henderson and James Summerfield, former CEO and CIO of Stripe Europe [src](https://newsio.com/2025/08/20/ai-immune-system-phoebe-lands-backing-from-google-arm).\n  - The GV/Cherry seed was announced with the public launch.\n  - Intro path not found.\n- **Verse:**\n  - Andy Wheeler led GV's Series A in 2024 [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/verse-secures-20-5-million-in-series-a-funding-led-by-gv-to-help-organizations-reduce-electricity-costs--emissions-302152878.html).\n  - The company now sits on Hiranand's list [src](https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand).\n  - In 2026 Verse repositioned toward AI-data-center power and brought in NVIDIA at the Series B [src](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/verse-enterprises-raises-54m-series-b/).\n  - Whether Hiranand took over the board or observer role is unconfirmed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| GV | Principal, London | by 2024-06 | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand) |\n| Erik Nordlander (GV GP) | Co-author on the Scope3 lead | 2024-10 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/scope3-ai-sustainable) |\n| Luna Schmid (GV Partner, London) | Both list Comstruct | 2025 | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/luna-schmid) |\n| Andy Wheeler (GV GP at the time) | Led Verse Series A, now on Hiranand's list | 2024-05 | [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/verse-secures-20-5-million-in-series-a-funding-led-by-gv-to-help-organizations-reduce-electricity-costs--emissions-302152878.html) |\n| Cherry Ventures | Co-lead, Phoebe seed | 2025-08 | [src](https://newsio.com/2025/08/20/ai-immune-system-phoebe-lands-backing-from-google-arm) |\n| Lux Capital, Riot Ventures | Co-investors in EnduroSat | 2025-10 | [src](https://www.satellitetoday.com/finance/2025/10/30/endurosat-raises-another-100m-with-backing-from-google-ventures/) |\n| NVIDIA, Bessemer | Co-investors in Verse Series B | 2026-06 | [src](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/verse-enterprises-raises-54m-series-b/) |\n| Raycho Raychev (EnduroSat CEO) | Portfolio founder | 2025 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/endurosat-democratising-orbit) |\n| Matt Henderson, James Summerfield (Phoebe) | Portfolio founders | 2025 | [src](https://newsio.com/2025/08/20/ai-immune-system-phoebe-lands-backing-from-google-arm) |\n| Urho Konttori (Distance CEO) | Portfolio founder | 2024 | [src](https://siliconcanals.com/distance-technologies-secures-e10m) |\n| Kamil Kluza, Lukky Ahmed (Climate X) | Portfolio founders | 2024 | [src](https://climate-x.com/articles/press-releases/series-a) |\n| Bace Capital (Ant Financial) | Former employer | dates unknown | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand) |\n| Vectr Ventures | Former employer | 2015 (aggregator) | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand) |\n\n## Open questions\n- His GV join date and any promotion. Is the SatelliteToday \"Partner\" title real or an error? Check LinkedIn.\n- Is he the board member or observer on Verse after Wheeler, and did he drive GV's Series B pro-rata next to NVIDIA?\n- Intro path into Phoebe (Stripe network? Cherry?).\n- What his X feed covers. It couldn't be fetched.\n- Which deals he did at Bace Capital and Vectr.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.gv.com/team/roni-hiranand (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/endurosat-democratising-orbit (2025-11-13)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/scope3-ai-sustainable (2024-10-10)\n- https://climate-x.com/articles/press-releases/series-a (2024-06-26)\n- https://siliconcanals.com/distance-technologies-secures-e10m (2024-09-27)\n- https://en.ain.ua/2024/06/14/distance-technologies-pre-seed-investment\n- https://newsio.com/2025/08/20/ai-immune-system-phoebe-lands-backing-from-google-arm\n- https://www.uktech.news/funding/uk-tech-funding-roundup-this-weeks-deals-from-phoebe-to-evaluatelocate-20250822\n- https://www.satellitetoday.com/finance/2025/10/30/endurosat-raises-another-100m-with-backing-from-google-ventures/\n- https://therecursive.com/endurosat-104m-funding-expand-satellite-production/\n- https://vestbee.com/insights/articles/enduro-sat-lands-205-m\n- https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/verse-secures-20-5-million-in-series-a-funding-led-by-gv-to-help-organizations-reduce-electricity-costs--emissions-302152878.html\n- https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/verse-enterprises-raises-54m-series-b/ ; https://raising.fi/news/verse-series-b-june-2026\n- https://www.kompas.vc/news/why-we-invested-in-again\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/06/comstruct-a-platform-to-digitize-the-construction-industry-raises-135-million/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2022/10/03/googles-gv-backs-sidequest-an-unofficial-meta-quest-app-store\n- https://www.alumni.hku.hk/hku-young-alumni-selected-as-forbes-30-under-30-asia/\n- Aggregators (secondary): https://startupintros.com/people/roni-hiranand ; https://www.crunchbase.com/person/roni-hiranand ; https://www.evalyze.ai/investors/roni-hiranand\n", "person:rosa-jahankhah": "\n# Rosa Jahankhah\n\n## One-line take\nShe is a very new, technical associate who joined in mid/late 2026 to build Triatomic's internal AI and diligence tooling, not to lead deals. She's worth knowing as a technical back channel into how a $10–20M-check deep-tech fund screens compute and agentic companies. She isn't yet a deal-maker to follow.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she matters:** her edge is technical, not network. The firm bio says she \"builds the platforms and AI systems\". Her research background is RL (NSF-funded), game theory and formal verification at Stanford, plus a quant research internship at Citadel ([site](https://triatomic.ai)). At a 3-GP firm with no public research output, she is the person building its research-first machinery (\"We form views through original research\", [site](https://triatomic.ai)).\n- **What constrains her:** she is the most junior investor on the team and arrived very recently. She is not on the team page in the 2026-06-13 Wayback snapshot ([Wayback](https://web.archive.org/web/20260613033859/https://triatomic.ai/)) but is there on 2026-11-10 ([site](https://triatomic.ai)). No deal names her. GPs Zhou, Huber and Hochberg are the named leads on every attributed Triatomic deal (see `firms/triatomic.md`).\n- **What she wouldn't do (inferred):** source or lead a round on her own at this stage. Her role is platform and AI systems, not a sourcing mandate.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **She's new, and part of a 2026 hiring wave.** The 2026-06-13 snapshot of the team page lists 6 of today's 9 names. Jahankhah, Lauren Reyes and CFO Dhruv Bavaria were all added after that date ([Wayback 2026-06-13](https://web.archive.org/web/20260613033859/https://triatomic.ai/) vs [live site](https://triatomic.ai)). Bavaria's late arrival may explain why the March 2026 ADV names a different CFO (Sauerhoff), which `firms/triatomic.md` flags as unresolved.\n- **She is prototyping diligence tooling in public.** Her repo `tech-stack-visualizer` (first commits 2026-09-22) is a 3D map of a company's tech stack. The README says to \"model your own stack (or a company's, for due-diligence notes)\" ([GitHub](https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah/Rosa-Jahankhah-Portfolio/tree/main/tech-stack-visualizer)).\n- **The stack model added an agentic layer in September 2026.** A 2026-09-23 commit is titled \"Replace Products layer with a separate Agentic Pipeline\". The layers are now memory, planning, tool use and execution ([commits](https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah/Rosa-Jahankhah-Portfolio/commits/main)). This lines up with the firm's \"Automated Economy\" theme and its Coworker position (inference).\n- **She builds with AI coding agents.** PRs merge from `claude/3d-tech-stack-visual-...` branches, and commits in a sibling repo carry \"Co-authored-by: Cursor\" ([commits](https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah/Rosa-Jahankhah-Portfolio/commits/main); [history-hub commits](https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah/history-hub/commits/main)).\n- **Her RL work is at the fundamentals level.** In 2026-05 she published an epsilon-greedy bandit \"from scratch\", described as \"Sutton & Barto Chapter 2\" ([GitHub](https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah/RL-n-armed-bandit)). That's a textbook reproduction, not research output. No papers under her name turned up on arXiv (author search returned 0).\n- **Identification basis:** GitHub `Rosa-Jahankhah` has a bio describing her as a \"Statistics and Computer Science student at UC Berkeley\" ([GitHub](https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah)). That matches the firm bio's degree, the rare surname and the \"rosajahan\" LinkedIn slug. Medium-high confidence that it's the same person.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low** (the lead row said medium; downgraded). She has no attributed deals, no public investing views, no network into frontier labs or compute founders, and no promotion history. Her formal-verification and RL background is thematically adjacent to Axiom Math, a formal-reasoning company where Triatomic was in the 2026-03 Series A (aggregator-sourced, see `firms/triatomic.md`). Nothing connects her to that deal. Her practical value to you is as a way to understand how Triatomic diligences compute and agentic stacks, and as an early-career technical contact to revisit in 2–3 years. Triatomic's check size (10m-plus) makes the firm a peer co-investor, but she is not the decision-maker.\n\n## Background\n- **UC Berkeley, BA in Statistics and Computer Science** ([site](https://triatomic.ai)). Graduation year not stated. Her GitHub has Stat 153 time-series homework uploaded 2025-10-04 ([repo](https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah/Rosa-Jahankhah-Portfolio/tree/main/Time-Series-Project)), which suggests she was an undergrad until about 2025–26 (inference, unconfirmed).\n- **Stanford research** in reinforcement learning (NSF-funded), game theory and formal verification ([site](https://triatomic.ai)). The lab, PI and dates weren't found.\n- **Citadel**, Quant Researcher intern ([site](https://triatomic.ai)). Dates not found.\n- **Triatomic Capital**, Associate. First appears on the team page between 2026-06-13 and 2026-11-10 ([Wayback](https://web.archive.org/web/20260613033859/https://triatomic.ai/); [site](https://triatomic.ai)). Exact start date not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [triatomic.ai team bio](https://triatomic.ai) | Verified 2026-11-10. No headshot on the page |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Not searched beyond name queries; no handle surfaced |\n| LinkedIn | [linkedin.com/in/rosajahan](https://www.linkedin.com/in/rosajahan/) | Linked from the firm bio. Not loaded (HTTP 999 block), so unverified directly |\n| GitHub | [github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah](https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah) | Verified via API 2026-11-10: 4 public repos, 0 followers, account created 2024-11-01, last push 2026-09-23 |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Name searches on the web and arXiv returned no talks, papers or interviews.\n\n## How they think\n- **Main theses:** there is no public writing. Her repos show interest in stack-level decomposition of companies (infra → data → backend → product → users), plus a separate agentic layer (memory, planning, tool use, execution) ([stackData.ts](https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah/Rosa-Jahankhah-Portfolio/blob/main/tech-stack-visualizer/src/data/stackData.ts)).\n- **Contrarian views:** nothing found.\n- **Calls they got right or wrong:** none attributable.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Triatomic team bio](https://triatomic.ai): the only official statement of her role and background.\n2. [tech-stack-visualizer README](https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah/Rosa-Jahankhah-Portfolio/tree/main/tech-stack-visualizer): a due-diligence-oriented stack mapping tool, with a roadmap.\n3. [Portfolio commit log](https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah/Rosa-Jahankhah-Portfolio/commits/main): shows the September 2026 move to model an agentic pipeline.\n4. [RL-n-armed-bandit](https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah/RL-n-armed-bandit): her RL fundamentals.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. She has no public X or Substack presence, and her GitHub has 0 followers.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| nothing found | | | | | No deal publicly names her. For firm-level deals, see `firms/triatomic.md` |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. No company announcement, board listing or press names her on a deal.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Triatomic Capital | Associate | 2026 (added to site after 2026-06-13) | [site](https://triatomic.ai); [Wayback](https://web.archive.org/web/20260613033859/https://triatomic.ai/) |\n| Citadel | Former Quant Researcher intern | dates not found | [site](https://triatomic.ai) |\n| Stanford (RL / game theory / formal verification research) | Former researcher | dates not found | [site](https://triatomic.ai) |\n| UC Berkeley | Alumna (BA Statistics and CS) | year not found | [site](https://triatomic.ai) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Her exact start date, and whether she joined straight out of Berkeley (class of 2026?).\n- Which Stanford lab and PI she worked with. A formal-verification group would be a lead into the Axiom and Lean ecosystem.\n- Whether the firm's \"AI systems\" include a deal-screening or portfolio-research agent, and whether the tech-stack visualizer is used in Triatomic diligence.\n- Whether she has any role in the compute cluster (Gimlet B, Efficient B, Volantis A, all 2026 rounds after her probable arrival).\n- Whether the late-2026 additions (Bavaria, Reyes, Jahankhah) signal a Fund II build-out. No Private II Form D has been found yet (`firms/triatomic.md`).\n\n## Sources\n- https://triatomic.ai (team bio; fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20260613033859/https://triatomic.ai/ (team page snapshot, 6 of 9 current names; no Jahankhah)\n- https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah (profile; API checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah/Rosa-Jahankhah-Portfolio (tech-stack-visualizer, Stat 153 HW, commit log)\n- https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah/RL-n-armed-bandit\n- https://github.com/Rosa-Jahankhah/history-hub (personal Next.js project; not firm-related)\n- arXiv author query \"Jahankhah\": 0 results (2026-11-10)\n- Datanyze lists her as an Associate at Triatomic (aggregator, search snippet only): https://www.datanyze.com/companies/triatomic-capital/1320641947\n", "person:ryan-beiermeister": "\n# Ryan Beiermeister\n\n## One-line take\nA new Founders Fund partner (July 2026) who built Palantir's Gotham product and ran product policy at OpenAI. Worth following as an open inbound door at FF for \"regulated frontier\" AI and agent companies, but she has no sourced deals yet and her OpenAI network comes from policy and safety, not research.\n\n## Crux\nSources use she/her ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/16/founders-fund-hires-former-openai-exec-ryan-beiermeister-and-not-because-of-her-mafia-skills/)). Her edge is 14 years building product in hard, regulated settings: Palantir defense systems, Meta election and integrity work, and OpenAI safety systems ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/rbeiermeister_today-i-joined-founders-fund-as-a-partner-share-7483229315074023424-U-wz/)). On top of that she has a Palantir-era bond of at least ten years with Trae Stephens ([src](https://dealroom.co/news/139657-ex-openai-policy-exec-ryan-beiermeister-joins-founders-fund-as-partner/)). Her stated filter is categories \"where product engineering is hardest\", which points to applied AI in defense, energy, biotech and government-adjacent agents rather than pre-training labs.\n\nThree things constrain her:\n- She is a first-time investor with no track record.\n- Her mandate is spread across six sectors.\n- She left OpenAI in a disputed firing. It was reportedly tied to her opposing ChatGPT's \"adult mode\", and she denies the discrimination allegation ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/10/openai-policy-exec-who-opposed-chatbots-adult-mode-reportedly-fired-on-discrimination-claim/)). That may make her OpenAI ties less warm than \"ex-OpenAI network\" implies.\n\nInference, not sourced: she is unlikely to lead a giant pre-product research-lab seed on her own at FF, because that is outside her background and the firm's lab book sits with other partners.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Stated mandate is broad, and AI is one lane.** She lists \"AI infrastructure and agentic systems, defense, energy, climate, biotech\" ([src](https://dealroom.co/news/139657-ex-openai-policy-exec-ryan-beiermeister-joins-founders-fund-as-partner/), quoting her LinkedIn post, 2026-07).\n- **Her filter is how hard the build is.** She says winners are \"being built in the categories where product engineering is hardest\" ([src](https://dealroom.co/news/139657-ex-openai-policy-exec-ryan-beiermeister-joins-founders-fund-as-partner/)).\n- **She is open to cold outreach from unconventional founders.** She writes \"especially if you don't fit the standard mold\", and says her inbox is open ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/rbeiermeister_today-i-joined-founders-fund-as-a-partner-share-7483229315074023424-U-wz/), 2026-07-13).\n- **A scout-style mandate.** She wants to \"widen the aperture\" of what FF sees ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/rbeiermeister_today-i-joined-founders-fund-as-a-partner-share-7483229315074023424-U-wz/)).\n- **The Palantir tie to Stephens.** \"Beiermeister has known Founders Fund partner Trae Stephens for at least a decade\" ([src](https://dealroom.co/news/139657-ex-openai-policy-exec-ryan-beiermeister-joins-founders-fund-as-partner/)).\n- **A long courtship, not a cold hire.** An FF spokesperson said she has been \"friendly with our team for years\". She was a standout on FF's \"Mafia\" YouTube show alongside Sam Altman, Palmer Luckey, Dylan Field, Ryan Petersen and Trae Stephens ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/16/founders-fund-hires-former-openai-exec-ryan-beiermeister-and-not-because-of-her-mafia-skills/); show: [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EDCwQe7P8T0)).\n- **The OpenAI exit.** She reportedly opposed the planned ChatGPT \"adult mode\" and was then fired after a colleague's discrimination accusation. She says: \"The allegation that I discriminated against anyone is absolutely false.\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/10/openai-policy-exec-who-opposed-chatbots-adult-mode-reportedly-fired-on-discrimination-claim/), 2026-02-10). OpenAI reportedly dropped adult mode in March 2026 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/16/founders-fund-hires-former-openai-exec-ryan-beiermeister-and-not-because-of-her-mafia-skills/)).\n- **A technical product background, not only policy.** She is a named co-inventor on a Palantir patent, \"Systems and methods for identifying and categorizing electronic documents through machine learning\". Its priority date is 2015-12-11 and it was granted 2016-12-06 ([src](https://patents.google.com/patent/US9514414B1/en)). Her LinkedIn lists four more Palantir-era patents ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/rbeiermeister)).\n- **Deal timing:** nothing found. No investment has been attributed to her as of 2026-11-09, so there are no first-in or follow-on data yet.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:**\n  - She is a decision-maker at a firm that does write early AI checks.\n  - She openly asks for AI infrastructure and agent founders, and agent companies overlap with my AI-native automation interest (Serval-type).\n  - Her Palantir and Stephens links make her a natural sponsor for agentic automation sold into government, defense or other regulated buyers.\n- **Against:**\n  - She has no deals yet.\n  - She has no sourced pre-training, research-lab or AI-chip angle.\n  - Her OpenAI network is from policy and safety and ended in a contentious exit.\n  - Founders Fund's centre of gravity is growth (see `firms/founders-fund.md`).\n- **Deals that matter to me:** none yet.\n\nWatch her first two or three deals. If one is an applied agent or AI-infrastructure seed, she becomes a co-investor contact for automation deals. If the deals are defense or energy, downgrade her to low.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** BA in political science from Northwestern University, summa cum laude ([src](https://foundersfund.com/team/ryan-beiermeister/)).\n- **Debate:** Copeland Award 2012 for the nation's top-ranked two-person policy debate team, and Top Speaker at the 2012 National Debate Tournament ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/rbeiermeister); [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/ryan-beiermeister/)).\n- **Palantir:** more than seven years ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/10/openai-policy-exec-who-opposed-chatbots-adult-mode-reportedly-fired-on-discrimination-claim/)). She rose to VP and led development of Gotham ([src](https://foundersfund.com/team/ryan-beiermeister/)), and patent filings run from 2015 to 2016 ([src](https://patents.google.com/patent/US9514414B1/en)). Exact dates are not sourced.\n- **Meta:** about four years on the product team ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/10/openai-policy-exec-who-opposed-chatbots-adult-mode-reportedly-fired-on-discrimination-claim/)), as Product Director for election integrity and social issues ([src](https://foundersfund.com/team/ryan-beiermeister/)).\n- **OpenAI:** VP of Product Policy for about two years ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/16/founders-fund-hires-former-openai-exec-ryan-beiermeister-and-not-because-of-her-mafia-skills/)). She was fired in early 2026: TechCrunch (2026-02-10) says January ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/10/openai-policy-exec-who-opposed-chatbots-adult-mode-reportedly-fired-on-discrimination-claim/)), while Dealroom says February ([src](https://dealroom.co/news/139657-ex-openai-policy-exec-ryan-beiermeister-joins-founders-fund-as-partner/)).\n- **Founders Fund:** Partner, announced in a LinkedIn post the week of 2026-07-13 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/16/founders-fund-hires-former-openai-exec-ryan-beiermeister-and-not-because-of-her-mafia-skills/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://foundersfund.com/team/ryan-beiermeister/ | FF bio; the Twitter link on it is blank (2026-11-09) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | FF bio has an empty Twitter link |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/rbeiermeister | Main public channel; follower count not captured |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026 — Founders Fund YouTube — \"Can Tech Legends Find the Liar? (Mafia Episode 1)\" — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EDCwQe7P8T0 — a social-deduction game, not an investing talk. Per TechCrunch, she and Altman each implied the other would be the killer ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/16/founders-fund-hires-former-openai-exec-ryan-beiermeister-and-not-because-of-her-mafia-skills/)).\n- No investing podcasts or talks found. Web search was unavailable during this run (see Open questions).\n\n## How they think\n- **Hard, regulated, high-stakes products are where value accrues.** She names \"the regulated frontier\" as a category ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/16/founders-fund-hires-former-openai-exec-ryan-beiermeister-and-not-because-of-her-mafia-skills/)).\n- **Unconventional founders are welcome.** She takes an open-inbox approach ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/rbeiermeister_today-i-joined-founders-fund-as-a-partner-share-7483229315074023424-U-wz/)).\n- **Contrarian view:** at OpenAI she pushed against a revenue-friendly consumer feature (adult mode) on safety grounds, and OpenAI later dropped it ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/16/founders-fund-hires-former-openai-exec-ryan-beiermeister-and-not-because-of-her-mafia-skills/)).\n- **Calls right or wrong:** nothing found as an investor.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Her LinkedIn announcement of joining FF, which sets out her mandate and filter: https://www.linkedin.com/posts/rbeiermeister_today-i-joined-founders-fund-as-a-partner-share-7483229315074023424-U-wz/\n2. TechCrunch on the hire, covering the Stephens tie, Mafia and how FF frames her: https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/16/founders-fund-hires-former-openai-exec-ryan-beiermeister-and-not-because-of-her-mafia-skills/\n3. TechCrunch on her OpenAI exit, the context for her OpenAI network: https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/10/openai-policy-exec-who-opposed-chatbots-adult-mode-reportedly-fired-on-discrimination-claim/\n4. Her FF bio: https://foundersfund.com/team/ryan-beiermeister/\n\n## Social engagement\n- LinkedIn is the only active channel found.\n- Her FF announcement drew endorsements from Samidh Chakrabarti, Henry Tsai, Alex Beyer and Amanda LeHaf. It also drew congratulations from Leigh Marie Braswell, Dustin Ho, Vidita Subbarao, Nat Meurer and Steve Yazicioglu ([src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/rbeiermeister_today-i-joined-founders-fund-as-a-partner-share-7483229315074023424-U-wz/)). These are recorded as needs-id leads.\n- I couldn't check X activity.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| nothing found | | | | | No deals attributed as of 2026-11-09 |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Founders Fund | Partner | 2026-07 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/16/founders-fund-hires-former-openai-exec-ryan-beiermeister-and-not-because-of-her-mafia-skills/) |\n| Trae Stephens | Palantir colleague; known each other at least a decade | about 2016 or earlier | [src](https://dealroom.co/news/139657-ex-openai-policy-exec-ryan-beiermeister-joins-founders-fund-as-partner/) |\n| Mike Solana (FF CMO) | Host of the Mafia show she played on | 2026 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/16/founders-fund-hires-former-openai-exec-ryan-beiermeister-and-not-because-of-her-mafia-skills/) |\n| Sam Altman / OpenAI | Former employer (VP Product Policy); Mafia co-player | about 2024–2026 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/16/founders-fund-hires-former-openai-exec-ryan-beiermeister-and-not-because-of-her-mafia-skills/) |\n| Meta | Product Director, about four years | | [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/ryan-beiermeister/) |\n| Palantir (co-inventors J. Rosswog, D. Erenrich, C. Bills and others) | VP; patent co-inventor | priority 2015-12 | [src](https://patents.google.com/patent/US9514414B1/en) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **First deals.** Which companies has she sponsored since July 2026? Check FF announcements and her LinkedIn. Web search was exhausted during this run.\n- **Podcast or Substack voice.** Has she done any investing podcast since joining FF? None was found, and search was unavailable.\n- **Leigh Marie Braswell.** Is the person who congratulated her the early-stage AI investor of that name? This would be a high-fit link.\n- **Her OpenAI network.** Which ex-OpenAI people (safety, policy, integrity) are now founders she could back? Is her relationship with OpenAI leadership workable after the firing?\n- **Exit date.** Did she leave in January or February 2026? The sources conflict.\n- **Exact dates** at Palantir and Meta.\n- **Pairing with Stephens.** Does she co-source with Stephens on defense AI? Watch for a shared first deal.\n\n## Sources\n- https://dealroom.co/news/139657-ex-openai-policy-exec-ryan-beiermeister-joins-founders-fund-as-partner/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/16/founders-fund-hires-former-openai-exec-ryan-beiermeister-and-not-because-of-her-mafia-skills/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/10/openai-policy-exec-who-opposed-chatbots-adult-mode-reportedly-fired-on-discrimination-claim/\n- https://foundersfund.com/team/ryan-beiermeister/\n- https://www.linkedin.com/posts/rbeiermeister_today-i-joined-founders-fund-as-a-partner-share-7483229315074023424-U-wz/\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/rbeiermeister\n- https://patents.google.com/patent/US9514414B1/en\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EDCwQe7P8T0\n", "person:ryan-gibson": "\n# Ryan Gibson\n\n*Updated 2026-11-10: added the 2025 Bedrock launch round, the Noetive angel syndicate and CEO, the Bedrock intro path, and a co-authored contrarian view. Eclipse's posts refer to him as \"he\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).*\n\n## One-line take\nGibson is Eclipse's company manufacturer. He forms physical-economy and AI-automation companies before they exist (Bedrock Robotics, Noetive), so on Eclipse-built companies he is in before any round is priced ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He wins on domain immersion before incorporation. He sources, recruits and vets technical co-founders himself, and opens doors through an operator network. For Bedrock that meant 50+ construction-industry conversations, many through his Kojo contacts, before the company was formed ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)). Eclipse says the practice has built nearly 20 companies during his tenure, about 20% of them unicorns, collectively worth over $5B. These figures are firm-reported ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- **He is the scoper, not the sourcer.** On Bedrock, the founders came in through other partners. Boris Sofman connected with Aidan Madigan-Curtis, and Kevin Peterson had worked with Seth Winterroth. Gibson's job was scoping the company and opening the industry ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock)). On Noetive, the technical lead was Amir Frenkel, Eclipse's Chief AI Officer, who became Noetive's CEO ([src](https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed)).\n- **What constrains him.** The work does not scale. Eclipse says it takes on only a few builds a year ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)). His network comes from construction tech and data-center hardware, so his edge is in construction, logistics, energy and manufacturing. It is unproven in frontier-model or compute companies.\n- **What he would not do.** This is inferred, not stated: he would not back a company from the outside in a contested round. Every sourced deal is an Eclipse formation where the firm is in before the founders incorporate. He and Lior Susan also reject top-down enterprise selling into industrial companies (see below).\n\n## Differential insight\nNo podcast, Substack, X post or talk by Gibson was found. This session had no web search left, and earlier direct fetches found nothing. Everything below comes from Eclipse's blog (firm-site), company releases and press. It is primary-ish, but not his own unscripted voice.\n\n- **Co-authored contrarian view on GTM.** In the Noetive post he co-signed with Susan: \"can’t become the central nervous system of an industrial business with a top-down approach\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy), 2026-09-16). Read this as bottom-up deployment through design partners in plants, not CIO-led enterprise sales.\n- **Bedrock intro path was not his.** The post says \"Boris connected with Aidan as he began exploring what to build next\". Separately, Peterson had previously worked with Winterroth ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock)). Gibson's credited asset was this: \"Ryan's background as a construction-tech founder was especially valuable at this stage.\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock))\n- **Rolodex as diligence.** \"That meant more than 50 conversations before the company was formed\". This included a day on site with a major US general contractor, and outreach to a Bay Area developer that became Bedrock's first test site ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale), 2026-10-28).\n- **Portfolio-founder angel loop.** Bedrock CEO Boris Sofman is an individual investor in Noetive's seed, Gibson's next published build. Others in that seed are Meta CTO Andrew Bosworth, Ahmad Al-Dahle (Airbnb CTO, ex-Meta GenAI VP), Mike Schroepfer (Gigascale), Matt Rogers (Incite), Yaser Sheikh, Zach Frenkel (ex-Founders Fund), and Decart AI co-founders Dean Leitersdorf, Moshe Shalev and Orian Leitersdorf ([src](https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed), 2026-09-16). Most of the angels are Meta alumni, which tracks Frenkel's decade at Meta. The syndicate looks assembled around the CEO's network, with Eclipse founders (Sofman) recycled in.\n- **He recruits co-founders himself.** The firm says: \"He sources, recruits, and vets technical co-founders himself\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- **Builds are rationed.** \"Eclipse only takes on a few such builds each year.\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale))\n- **Stated rules of thumb**, as quoted by the firm: \"Immerse yourself in the industry with the people who know it best.\" and \"Build the right product, align incentives with the customer’s risk\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- **Field diligence elsewhere.** He toured battery factories in northern Sweden with Madigan-Curtis for Peak Energy. For Mytra, he drove for hours with the founder to spend a day inside a large logistics company ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)). His formal role in those companies (formed, led or board) is not stated.\n- **Bedrock timing arc.** Eclipse was first, then 8VC, then crossovers within about 24 months:\n  - **2024.** Formed with Eclipse, pre-incorporation ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock)). It had a prototype within six months.\n  - **2025-07-16.** $80M launch round. Eclipse and 8VC are the only investors named ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/16/ex-waymo-engineers-launch-bedrock-robotics-with-80m-to-automate-construction/)). The lead is not stated.\n  - **2026-02.** $270M at about $1.75B. The lead is not named ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock)).\n  - **2026-10-26.** $600M Series C at $4.1B, co-led by CapitalG and Emergence. USIT, Bessemer and Temasek were new. 8VC, Georgian, Valor, Xora, Eclipse, Perry Creek and Nvidia were existing ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/)).\n  - **Read.** 8VC (also a Kojo backer per [usekojo.com](https://www.usekojo.com/about)) was the first outside firm in, about a year after formation.\n- **Noetive seed: built in-house, then syndicated.** The post says the company was built \"from the ground up\" with Frenkel, and Eclipse led the $41M seed. Craft, Westly Group, Swish, Factory, Incite, Gigascale, Operator Partners and Liquid 2 followed ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy), 2026-09-16). Frenkel joined Eclipse on 2026-04-28 ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one)). That is about 4.5 months from the CAIO hire to a $41M public seed.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium overall. High on the AI-native automation slice only.**\n- **Noetive (high).** Frontier-AI operational intelligence for manufacturing and logistics, \"driven by frontier AI research\" per the post ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy)). It is the closest thing in his record to the Serval-type AI-native automation in THESIS. The $41M seed took nine outside funds, so a ~$20M-type co-invest seat on his next build is plausible.\n- **Bedrock Robotics (high as AI automation, not frontier AI).** Autonomy for heavy machinery. At $4.1B the entry window has closed ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/)), but its cap table is a map of who follows Eclipse formations.\n- **Indirect frontier-AI nodes.** The Noetive angel list connects him to Decart AI's founders and to Mike Schroepfer's Gigascale ([src](https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed)). Both are closer to the core of my lens than he is.\n- **No exposure to the core of the lens.** I found nothing on pre-training research, new labs or AI semiconductors and compute. Eclipse's compute wins (Cerebras, Oxide) belong to other partners ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era), [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/built-for-the-compute-ownership-era)).\n- **Practical value.** He is the person to know to get allocation in Eclipse-formed seeds before they syndicate.\n\n## Background\n- Engineer by training. He worked on complex devices including Microsoft HoloLens, and on data-center hardware at Google and Facebook. Dates not given ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- Co-founded Kojo, a construction-tech company, entering the industry as an outsider who \"had to earn his place in the construction industry\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)). Kojo's site lists 8VC, Tiger Global, Battery, BoxGroup and Abstract as backers, but does not name founders ([src](https://www.usekojo.com/about)). Dates not found.\n- Joined Eclipse in 2022 as a Venture Partner and formed Venture Equity. Promoted to Partner on 2026-10-28 alongside Michael Laser ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- An aggregator lists investment roles at Construct Capital and Fifty Years, and a BS in aerospace engineering from the University of Maryland ([src](https://startupintros.com/people/ryan-gibson)). This is unverified and possibly a different Ryan Gibson, because it does not obviously match Eclipse's bio. Treat it as needs-id.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://eclipse.capital/team/ryan-gibson | Firm profile; the directory fetch showed title \"Partner\" only (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://eclipse.capital/blog | Co-author of the Noetive post (2026-09-16); no personal blog found |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryanjgibson/ | Aggregator only ([src](https://startupintros.com/people/ryan-gibson)); not verified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. Web search was unavailable in this session and the previous one. Aidan Madigan-Curtis's Crux \"Critical Capital\" episode (2026-04-28) does not mention him ([src](https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis)).\n\n## How they think\n- **Industry immersion first.** He scopes a company through dozens of customer and site conversations before incorporation ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- **Align with customer risk.** He builds the right product, ties incentives to the customer's risk, and is honest about timelines ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- **Bottom-up, not top-down, in industrial AI.** This is co-authored with Susan ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy)).\n- **Recruit founders to a problem.** He finds the problem, then recruits and vets technical co-founders himself ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n- **Calls that worked.** Bedrock went from 2024 formation to a $4.1B valuation by 2026-10 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/)).\n- **Admitted mistakes:** nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"From Formation to Scale\" (2026-10-28). His promotion post and the only description of how he works ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n2. \"Operational Intelligence for the Physical Economy\" (2026-09-16). His co-authored Noetive thesis, including the top-down critique ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy)).\n3. \"Breaking Ground with Bedrock\" (2026-04-30). The formation story and intro path ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock)).\n4. Noetive launch release (2026-09-16). The angel syndicate behind his latest build ([src](https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X account was identified.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bedrock Robotics | Formation (Venture Equity, pre-incorporation) | 2024 | Co-formed with Aidan Madigan-Curtis | None at formation | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock) |\n| Bedrock Robotics | Launch round, $80M | 2025-07-16 | Lead not stated | 8VC | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/16/ex-waymo-engineers-launch-bedrock-robotics-with-80m-to-automate-construction/) |\n| Bedrock Robotics | $270M at ~$1.75B | 2026-02 | Not stated | Not named | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock) |\n| Bedrock Robotics | Series C, $600M at $4.1B | 2026-10-26 | No. CapitalG and Emergence co-led | 8VC, Georgian, Valor, Xora, Perry Creek, Nvidia, USIT, Bessemer, Temasek | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/) |\n| Noetive | Seed, $41M (in-house build) | 2026-09-16 | Eclipse led; Gibson co-authored the post with Lior Susan | Craft, Westly Group, Swish, Factory, Incite, Gigascale, Operator Partners, Liquid 2; angels including Boris Sofman, Andrew Bosworth, Mike Schroepfer and the Decart co-founders | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy), [src](https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed) |\n| Peak Energy | Involvement (factory diligence); round and role not stated | Not given | Not stated | Xora led the 2024 Series A per the company site | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale), [src](https://peakenergy.com) |\n| Mytra | Involvement (customer immersion with the founder); round and role not stated | Not given | Not stated | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale) |\n\nThe promotion post also shows logos for Jetson, Vinci, Reframe, Remedy, Prime Minute and Augmenta, without stating Gibson's role. They are logged as leads, not deals ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Bedrock Robotics.**\n  - *Founders and intro path.* Boris Sofman (ex-Waymo, Uber Freight) connected with Madigan-Curtis while exploring what to build next. Kevin Peterson (ex-Waymo, Caterpillar autonomy) had worked with Winterroth.\n  - *Formation.* Gibson and Madigan-Curtis spent months with the founders on problem definition and go-to-market. Gibson opened construction doors from his Kojo days. Tom Eliaz and Ajay Gummalla joined as co-founders, and Bedrock formed in 2024. It had a prototype within six months and came out of stealth in 2025 ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock), [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale)).\n  - *Rounds.* $80M with Eclipse and 8VC (2025-07) ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/16/ex-waymo-engineers-launch-bedrock-robotics-with-80m-to-automate-construction/)). $270M (2026-02, ~$1.75B). A $600M Series C (2026-10-26, $4.1B) ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/)).\n  - *Later.* Sofman later angel-invested in Noetive ([src](https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed)).\n- **Noetive.**\n  - *Origin.* Amir Frenkel, nearly a decade as a Meta VP, joined Eclipse as Chief AI Officer on 2026-04-28 to build a company with Eclipse Venture Equity ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one)). Gibson and Susan write that they built Noetive \"from the ground up\" with him ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy)).\n  - *Launch.* Frenkel is CEO and co-founder. Steuben Foods is a design partner. The $41M Eclipse-led seed was announced 2026-09-16 ([src](https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Eclipse | Venture Partner, then Partner (2026-10-28); formed Venture Equity | 2022 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale) |\n| Aidan Madigan-Curtis | Co-formed Bedrock; joint Peak Energy diligence | 2024 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock) |\n| Lior Susan | Co-author of the Noetive post | 2026 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy) |\n| Amir Frenkel | Built Noetive together; Frenkel is its CEO | 2026 | [src](https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed) |\n| Seth Winterroth | Peterson's prior Eclipse tie; Bedrock deal team | 2024 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock) |\n| Boris Sofman | Bedrock co-founder he co-formed with; later a Noetive angel | 2024 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock), [src](https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed) |\n| Kevin Peterson, Tom Eliaz, Ajay Gummalla | Bedrock co-founders | 2024 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock) |\n| 8VC | Co-investor in Bedrock's $80M launch round; Kojo backer | 2025 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/16/ex-waymo-engineers-launch-bedrock-robotics-with-80m-to-automate-construction/) |\n| Michael Laser | Promoted to Partner the same day | 2026-10-28 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale) |\n| Kojo | Co-founder | Dates not found | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who led Bedrock's $80M 2025 round and its 2026-02 $270M round? Did Eclipse lead either?\n- Which of the \"nearly 20\" Venture Equity companies are the unicorns, besides Bedrock?\n- Does the aggregator's Construct Capital, Fifty Years and UMD background belong to the same person?\n- What was his role in Jetson, Vinci, Reframe, Remedy, Prime Minute and Augmenta?\n- Are there podcasts or talks? Re-run search when budget allows: Kojo-era construction-tech podcasts, Eclipse \"Industrial Scaling\" events.\n- Did the Decart co-founders' Noetive angel checks lead to an Eclipse relationship with Decart?\n- Will Venture Equity ever build in AI compute or model companies?\n\n## Sources\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/from-formation-to-scale (firm-site, 2026-10-28)\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock (firm-site, 2026-04-30)\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/operational-intelligence-for-the-physical-economy (firm-site, 2026-09-16)\n- https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed (company release, 2026-09-16)\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/day-one (firm-site, 2026-04-28)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/16/ex-waymo-engineers-launch-bedrock-robotics-with-80m-to-automate-construction/ (press, 2025-07-16)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/ (press, 2026-10-26)\n- https://bedrockrobotics.com/news (company news page)\n- https://peakenergy.com (company site)\n- https://www.usekojo.com/about (company site)\n- https://startupintros.com/people/ryan-gibson (aggregator, secondary)\n- https://www.crux.com/critical-capital/physical-ai-aidan-madigan-curtis (podcast; no mention of Gibson)\n", "person:ryan-kim": "\n# Ryan Kim\n\n## One-line take\nHe is a young BCV partner, about two years at the firm by its own site. He is the only BCV partner named on Flapping Airplanes ($1B Series A, 2026-10-21) and on Corridor (a $25M seed he led). Most of his other deals are AI-native services seeds co-signed with Christina Melas-Kyriazi ([CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5Breferences(%22448b36c8-82d0-413c-9daa-8f16c697017e%22)%5D%7B_type%2Ctitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Elast_name%7D)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He sits at the junction of two BCV motions:\n  - **AI-native services seeds**, where BCV leads and is often first. Crosby was incubated through a BCV EIR. He also led the Adaptive and Corridor seeds.\n  - **Allocation into hot research-team rounds**: Simile at the A, Flapping Airplanes at the A.\n\n  His memo voice is thesis-heavy and history-minded:\n  - Baumol's cost disease and the 1880s electric-motor story (Adaptive)\n  - \"regulatory arbitrage\" (his bio)\n  - the \"diffusion gap\" (Corridor)\n\n  ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/curing-the-cost-disease-our-investment-in-adaptive-innovations/), [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/ryan-kim/), [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/protecting-the-american-dream-our-investment-in-corridor/))\n- **What constrains him.** On the frontier-lab side he is a late, non-lead allocator.\n  - Flapping: BCV came in at the $1B A, about 9 months after Index, GV and Sequoia co-led the seed ([companies/flapping-airplanes.md](../companies/flapping-airplanes.md)).\n  - Simile: BCV entered at the Index-led A, after A* co-led the seed ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-human-layer-of-ai-why-we-re-continuing-to-invest-in-simile/), [src](https://www.a-star.co/news/simile)).\n  - He has no lab or semiconductor seed of his own on record.\n  - Five of his seven CMS records carry a co-signer, mostly Melas-Kyriazi. Only Flapping and Corridor are solo, so his independent authority is recent.\n- **What he'd never do (by inference from the memos).** Back a copilot sold into an incumbent workflow. His services memos argue the opposite: become the provider. \"you cannot re-pour concrete from the outside\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/curing-the-cost-disease-our-investment-in-adaptive-innovations/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Sole BCV name on a frontier lab.** BCV's CMS lists exactly one partner on Flapping Airplanes: Kim. The tagline is \"Improving AI efficiency with radical new techniques\", tagged Early, Infrastructure, 2026. It is the only infrastructure company on his page ([CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22flapping-airplanes%22%5D%7Btitle%2Cabout%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Elast_name%7D), record created 2026-10-22). BCV published no Flapping memo (none of 350 CMS insights mention it).\n- **Crosby was a BCV incubation; BCV was there before Sequoia priced it.**\n  - \"We have been fortunate to work with Crosby since inception\". Ryan Daniels was a BCV EIR, and \"Once we introduced him to his co-founder John Sarihan\" the company formed ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/operational-efficiency-isn-t-enough-ai-products-should-help-you-close-deals/), 2025-06-17; co-authored with Melas-Kyriazi and Miasnik).\n  - BCV's memo says it was \"participating with Sequoia Capital\" in the $5.8M seed, while Sequoia's account calls BCV a co-lead ([alfred-lin rows](../data/rows/alfred-lin-deals.csv)).\n- **A third party credits Crosby to him personally.** A 2026-07-29 Crosby profile calls him \"a partner at Bain Capital Ventures who incubated Crosby\". This is also the earliest public use of the partner title found. In the same profile he describes CEO Daniels as \"fantastic at\" \"creating reality distortion fields,\" and CTO Sarihan as all \"fast clock speeds and quiet competence.\" He was interviewed alongside Lux's Grace Isford, who led the Series B ([src](https://www.new-ontologies.com/posts/Crosby)). BCV then led Crosby's Series A: \"excited to lead their Series A\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crosby-is-redefining-legal-work-with-ai-powered-contract-automation/), 2025-10-08).\n- **Crosby diligence story.** Daniels did field research in India on legal-process-outsourcing workers. The co-founders ran an \"AI-vs-human duel\", which cut initial review time from 24 to 12 hours ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crosby-is-redefining-legal-work-with-ai-powered-contract-automation/), CMS 2025-10-08).\n- **Adaptive: a seed lead, then a double-down.** \"we led the $10M seed round in Adaptive Innovations\", then BCV doubled down in the $50M Series A. The founders first tried selling software to home-health agencies, and the agencies \"did not treat any more patients\". So they became the provider ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/curing-the-cost-disease-our-investment-in-adaptive-innovations/), 2026-06-02).\n- **The Adaptive memo states his services thesis outright.** \"the cure looks less like a better tool but a different kind of company\". He names Crosby and Reserv as prior bets on this thesis (same src).\n- **Corridor: his solo seed lead.** \"lead their $25m seed financing\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/protecting-the-american-dream-our-investment-in-corridor/), 2026-09-22; sole author).\n  - The diligence detail is the kind that comes from channel calls: \"some of the biggest firms have stopped paying their brokers on small accounts\".\n  - Two of the founders, Eric and Jackson, came from Scale AI.\n  - **Name clash:** this Corridor (corridoradvisors.com, benefits brokerage) is **not** the Conviction/Felicis AI-security Corridor in `bella-garcia-camargo` rows.\n- **Tomo: a consumer-AI thesis.**\n  - \"Many AI products are substitutable.\" The moat is the accumulated user relationship.\n  - \"tens of thousands of paying subscribers\" in just over three months.\n\n  The founders met at Retool ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/tomo-believes-in-you/), 2026-06-24; co-authored with Melas-Kyriazi).\n- **Policy lens from his own bio.** \"I've kept a close eye on policy proposals for potential regulatory arbitrage.\" He also says he built a company in college \"leveraging healthcare price transparency laws\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/ryan-kim/)). The CMS logo for his 2.5-year prior job is filed as \"turquoise2.png\", which points to Turquoise Health (price transparency). That identification is my inference; his role there is unconfirmed.\n- **Interfaces sourcing circuit.** He co-hosted the \"Intent: Proactive & Predictive Interfaces\" panel with Amanda Huang and ThirdLayer. Speakers came from Simile, ThirdLayer, Interaction and Tomo, two of which are his portfolio companies ([src](https://luma.com/vs9pfati), ~May 2026).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.**\n- **Why high:** he is a newly titled partner at a fund that writes $10M+ and growth checks, which is exactly the owner's \"principals and recently promoted partners\" segment. He is the named BCV person on Flapping Airplanes (a new lab) and on Simile (a Stanford research spinout). He also leads AI-native automation seeds (Crosby, Adaptive, Corridor) that resemble the Serval pattern in THESIS.md.\n- **Against:** on labs he has been a follow-on allocator, not first. No pre-training, chip or compute seed carries his name. The useful angle is co-investment: he is a likely BCV door for $20M-scale allocations into new-lab rounds, and a source of AI-native services seed deal flow.\n\n## Background\n- Harvard, 4 years (BCV site logo; years and degree not stated) ([CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22team%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22ryan-kim%22%5D%7B%22exp%22%3ApastExperience%5B%5D%7Byears%2C%22logo%22%3AcompanyLogo.asset-%3EoriginalFilename%7D%7D)).\n- Built a healthcare price-transparency company in college ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/ryan-kim/)). The 2.5-year prior role's logo is \"turquoise2.png\" (likely Turquoise Health; unconfirmed).\n- At BCV for about 2 years per the site; the team record was created 2025-06-05. The site title is Partner (SEO meta \"Partner | Bain Capital Ventures\"), while the bio still says \"investor\".\n- Family immigrated from Korea, per the bio's flag emoji and text ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/ryan-kim/)).\n- Pronouns: \"He\", per the BCV bio.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://baincapitalventures.com/team/ryan-kim/ | Partner; 7 portfolio records (as of 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | BCV insights | 6 memos co-authored or authored, 2025-06 to 2026-09 |\n| X | https://x.com/rkhkimx | Linked from BCV CMS; not readable (402) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryan-k-kim/ | Linked from BCV CMS; an earlier pass found it resolving to an unrelated profile, so unverified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- ~2026-05: BCV x ThirdLayer, \"Intent: Proactive & Predictive Interfaces\" (SF), co-host ([src](https://luma.com/vs9pfati)). He was a host, not a quoted speaker.\n- No podcasts found. The web-search budget ran out this pass, so the search was not exhaustive.\n\n## How they think\n- **AI-native services over tools.** Become the provider (Adaptive), the law firm (Crosby) or the broker (Corridor). Charge per outcome, not per hour.\n- **Revenue enablement beats efficiency.** \"AI Products Should Help You Close Deals\" (2025-06 memo title).\n- **Diffusion gap and regulatory arbitrage.** He looks for sectors where models are good enough but the industry's cost structure blocks adoption (small-group health benefits, home health).\n- **Consumer AI moats come from accumulated personal context** (Tomo).\n- **Calls so far:**\n  - Simile went from the A (2026-02) to a $2B Series B (2026-07).\n  - Flapping was priced at a reported $6B at the A.\n  - Too early to judge the seeds.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Curing the Cost Disease (Adaptive)](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/curing-the-cost-disease-our-investment-in-adaptive-innovations/): his fullest thesis piece.\n2. [Protecting the American Dream (Corridor)](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/protecting-the-american-dream-our-investment-in-corridor/): his solo-authored seed lead.\n3. [Operational Efficiency Isn't Enough (Crosby)](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/operational-efficiency-isn-t-enough-ai-products-should-help-you-close-deals/): the EIR incubation story.\n4. [Tomo Believes in You](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/tomo-believes-in-you/): his consumer-AI moat view.\n5. [The Human Layer of AI (Simile)](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-human-layer-of-ai-why-we-re-continuing-to-invest-in-simile/): the research-spinout follow-on.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. His X account (@rkhkimx) could not be read in this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Crosby | Seed ($5.8M) | 2025-06-17 | Co-lead per Sequoia; BCV says \"participating with\" | Sequoia (Alfred Lin). BCV co-signers Melas-Kyriazi, Miasnik | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/operational-efficiency-isn-t-enough-ai-products-should-help-you-close-deals/) |\n| Crosby | Series A | 2025-10-08 | Yes (\"excited to lead their Series A\") | Lux's Grace Isford later led the Series B (2025) | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crosby-is-redefining-legal-work-with-ai-powered-contract-automation/), [src](https://www.new-ontologies.com/posts/Crosby) |\n| Adaptive Innovations (home health) | Seed ($10M) | 2025 (CMS year) | Yes | BCV co-signer Melas-Kyriazi | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/curing-the-cost-disease-our-investment-in-adaptive-innovations/) |\n| Adaptive Innovations | Series A ($50M) | 2026-06-02 | Not stated (\"doubling down\") | none named | same |\n| Simile | Series A ($100M) | 2026-02-12 | No (Index led) | BCV co-signers Hilaly, Co | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-human-layer-of-ai-why-we-re-continuing-to-invest-in-simile/), [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/life-the-universe-and-simile-leading-similes-series-a/) |\n| Simile | Series B (>$200M at $2B) | 2026-07-30 | No (Greenoaks, Index) | A*, Hanabi, CVS Health Ventures and others | same |\n| Tomo | Seed | 2026 (memo 2026-06-24) | Not stated | BCV co-signer Melas-Kyriazi | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/tomo-believes-in-you/) |\n| Andera | Series A ($37M) | 2026-07-20 | No (Lightspeed led) | A* (seed lead 2024-10). BCV co-signer Melas-Kyriazi | [A*](https://www.a-star.co/news/andera), [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22andera%22%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Elast_name%7D) |\n| Corridor (benefits brokerage) | Seed ($25M) | 2026-09-22 | Yes (sole BCV signer) | none named | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/protecting-the-american-dream-our-investment-in-corridor/) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Series A ($1B, ~$6B reported) | 2026-10-21 | No (Index and Lux co-led) | Sequoia, GV, Kleiner, NVIDIA, A*, Jump, XTX and others. Sole BCV signer | [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22flapping-airplanes%22%5D%7Btitle%2Cabout%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Elast_name%7D), [X](https://x.com/flappyairplanes/status/2112958916871225736) |\n\n**Timing, deal by deal:**\n- **First in:** Crosby (BCV-incubated; led the A), Adaptive (seed lead), Corridor (seed lead).\n- **Not first:**\n  - Simile: A* co-led the seed in 2025; BCV joined at the A.\n  - Andera: A* led the seed in 2024-10; BCV joined at the A about 21 months later.\n  - Flapping Airplanes: Index, GV and Sequoia co-led the seed (announced 2026-01-28); BCV joined at the A about 9 months later.\n- **Unknown:** Tomo.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Crosby.** Ryan Daniels was a BCV EIR in New York. BCV introduced him to John Sarihan (ex-Ramp). BCV then co-funded the $5.8M seed with Sequoia ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/operational-efficiency-isn-t-enough-ai-products-should-help-you-close-deals/)). Kim's personal role versus Melas-Kyriazi's and Miasnik's is not separated in the sources.\n- **Adaptive.** BCV led the $10M seed, then followed in the $50M A. The memo cites more than 100k visits, 500+ referral partners and a 4.9% readmission rate against roughly 13% for the industry ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/curing-the-cost-disease-our-investment-in-adaptive-innovations/)).\n- **Flapping Airplanes.** How BCV got the allocation is unknown. The firm file notes a possible Harvard overlap between Kim and Asher Spector (my inference; class years unknown). Index's Mark Xu also knew Asher from Harvard ([firms/bain-capital-ventures.md](../firms/bain-capital-ventures.md)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Christina Melas-Kyriazi (BCV) | Co-signer on Crosby, Adaptive, Tomo and Andera; co-author of 4 memos | 2025 | [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5Breferences(%22448b36c8-82d0-413c-9daa-8f16c697017e%22)%5D%7B_type%2Ctitle%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Elast_name%7D) |\n| Ron Miasnik (BCV) | Co-signer on Crosby | 2025 | same |\n| Aaref Hilaly, Alysaa Co (BCV) | Co-signers on Simile | 2026 | same |\n| Amanda Huang (BCV) | Co-host of the Intent interfaces panel | 2026 | [src](https://luma.com/vs9pfati) |\n| Ryan Daniels, John Sarihan | Crosby founders (Daniels was a BCV EIR) | 2024-25 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/operational-efficiency-isn-t-enough-ai-products-should-help-you-close-deals/) |\n| Ben and Asher Spector, Aidan Smith | Flapping Airplanes founders | 2026 | [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22flapping-airplanes%22%5D%7Btitle%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Elast_name%7D) |\n| Joon Sung Park, Percy Liang, Michael Bernstein | Simile founders | 2026 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-human-layer-of-ai-why-we-re-continuing-to-invest-in-simile/) |\n| Justin Quan, Raymond Chen | Tomo founders | 2026 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/tomo-believes-in-you/) |\n| Grace Isford (Lux) | Crosby Series B lead; interviewed alongside him | 2025 | [src](https://www.new-ontologies.com/posts/Crosby) |\n| Sequoia (Alfred Lin) | Crosby seed co-investor | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-crosby-a-law-firm-at-the-speed-of-ai/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- (Update 2026-11-10: this profile replaces an earlier draft; prior row files were merged rather than discarded.)\n- His entry title and promotion date. No BCV \"newest partner\" post exists for him, unlike Abby Meyers, Ron Miasnik and Alysaa Co. LinkedIn is unverified. If he joined around 2024 straight from an operating role and is Partner by 2026, that would be very fast.\n- How the Flapping allocation was sourced. Was it the Harvard network, Index, or BCV Labs (Slater Stich)?\n- Whether the \"turquoise\" logo is Turquoise Health, and what his role was there.\n- The Tomo round size and whether BCV led.\n- Corridor's founders' full names and any co-investors.\n- The web-search budget was exhausted this pass. Podcasts, X posts and press quotes were not searched.\n\n## Sources\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/team/ryan-kim/ and BCV Sanity CMS queries linked above\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/operational-efficiency-isn-t-enough-ai-products-should-help-you-close-deals/ (2025-06-17)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/crosby-is-redefining-legal-work-with-ai-powered-contract-automation/ (2025-10-08)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/curing-the-cost-disease-our-investment-in-adaptive-innovations/ (2026-06-02)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/tomo-believes-in-you/ (2026-06-24)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-human-layer-of-ai-why-we-re-continuing-to-invest-in-simile/ (2026-07-30)\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/protecting-the-american-dream-our-investment-in-corridor/ (2026-09-22)\n- https://luma.com/vs9pfati\n- https://www.new-ontologies.com/posts/Crosby (2026-07-29)\n- https://www.a-star.co/news/andera ; https://www.a-star.co/news/simile\n- https://x.com/flappyairplanes/status/2112958916871225736 ; https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/year-old-ai-lab-flapping-airplanes-raises-1b-at-a-6b-valuation/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/life-the-universe-and-simile-leading-similes-series-a/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-crosby-a-law-firm-at-the-speed-of-ai/\n", "person:ryan-koh": "\n# Ryan Koh\n\n## One-line take\nAn ex-banker principal at a growth firm with large checks who, according to Business Insider, is pushing ICONIQ to invest earlier. He is a junior name on three ICONIQ-led AI-era software rounds: TinyFish A, Statsig C and Outtake B.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He sits inside a growth platform that leads $40M-$100M rounds, and he is the person driving it to write earlier checks. Business Insider describes him as leading ICONIQ's early-stage efforts across its venture and growth funds, with more than $800M deployed ([src](https://briefly-prod.herokuapp.com/anchor/Venture/story/hes-a-rising-star-in-vc-heres-where-iconiqs-ryan-koh-says-the-next-great-investors-will-come-from), 2026-02-05, BI syndicated).\n- **What constrains him.** On every deal found, a General Partner is the lead name and board member: Murali Joshi on Statsig and Outtake, Amit Agarwal on TinyFish. Koh appears as a second or third byline ([src](https://www.statsig.com/blog/statsig-series-c); [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/outtake-raises-40m-series-b-163000387.html)). No board seat or solo lead is sourced.\n- **What he'd never do.** Nothing sourced. The pattern is ICONIQ-style long courtship and leading a priced round. There is no evidence of first-check seed or frontier-lab mega-seeds.\n\n## Differential insight\n- How he got into VC: at a closing dinner, as a young Qatalyst-era banker on a sale to Cisco, he watched founders and backers trade stories. \"That was the moment I realized, 'Hey, this is a part I want to contribute to'\" ([src](https://briefly-prod.herokuapp.com/anchor/Venture/story/hes-a-rising-star-in-vc-heres-where-iconiqs-ryan-koh-says-the-next-great-investors-will-come-from), 2026-02-05). This comes from BI's profile, via a syndication page, so it is press, not primary.\n- Diligence style on Statsig, from a firm post that Koh co-signed: \"After countless visits to their office—immersing ourselves in daily stand-ups\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-product-development-why-were-backing-statsig-2), 2025-05-06). They courted the company over years and backed it with Sequoia already on the cap table. OpenAI acquired Statsig within months.\n- TinyFish thesis, from a post Koh co-signed: \"We believe the next generation of iconic companies will emerge from this AI-driven wave\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-enterprise-web-agents-our-partnership-with-tinyfish), 2025-08-20). The same post says ICONIQ first met the team in February (year not stated), so the gap from first meeting to a led Series A was months.\n- Outtake thesis, from a post Koh co-signed: \"Outtake is building the digital trust platform for this new era\" ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/building-a-digital-trust-platform-for-an-ai-driven-internet-our-partnership-with-outtake), 2026-01-28). This is security for an agentic internet.\n- Title mismatch: ICONIQ's own bylines list him as \"Investor\" through Jan 2026, while BI and TheOrg say Principal. The promotion is probably recent, or bylines use a generic title. Not confirmed.\n- No podcast, Substack, X account or talk by Koh was found. The web-search budget ran out mid-task, so this is not exhaustive.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is an AI-native automation investor (TinyFish enterprise web agents, Outtake agentic trust) at a firm that can write growth checks, and he is the internal champion for going earlier. That makes him a likely Series A/B lead for the owner's AI-automation seeds, and a principal-level relationship worth building before he is promoted. He is not a frontier-lab, pre-training or chips investor, and no seed or mega-seed activity is sourced. Deals that matter: TinyFish A (AI web agents, ICONIQ led), Outtake B (ICONIQ led), Statsig C (OpenAI exit).\n\n## Background\n- Investment banker before VC. BI says he was a young banker on a company sale to Cisco ([src](https://briefly-prod.herokuapp.com/anchor/Venture/story/hes-a-rising-star-in-vc-heres-where-iconiqs-ryan-koh-says-the-next-great-investors-will-come-from)). TheOrg lists Qatalyst Partners and Uber as prior employers, without dates ([src](https://theorg.com/org/iconiq-capital/org-chart/ryan-koh), aggregator).\n- ICONIQ Growth: \"focusing on ICONIQ Growth since 2020,\" per TheOrg (aggregator) ([src](https://theorg.com/org/iconiq-capital/org-chart/ryan-koh)).\n- Principal; Business Insider 2026 Rising Stars of VC, Feb 2026 ([src](https://briefly-prod.herokuapp.com/anchor/Venture/story/hes-a-rising-star-in-vc-heres-where-iconiqs-ryan-koh-says-the-next-great-investors-will-come-from)).\n- Forbes 30 Under 30 2026: claimed in the roster lead (ICONIQ 2025 review), but not confirmed. The Forbes list page did not render.\n- Education: nothing found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://theorg.com/org/iconiq-capital/org-chart/ryan-koh | aggregator; ICONIQ team page URL 404s |\n| Substack / blog | ICONIQ insights bylines | co-author on TinyFish, Statsig, Outtake posts |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. The search budget ran out, so this is unverified.\n\n## How they think\n- Main theses: AI-native workflow automation (enterprise web agents), developer and product infrastructure (Statsig), and trust and security for an AI-driven internet (Outtake). All three come from co-signed firm posts.\n- Contrarian view: pushing a growth firm down-stage ([src](https://briefly-prod.herokuapp.com/anchor/Venture/story/hes-a-rising-star-in-vc-heres-where-iconiqs-ryan-koh-says-the-next-great-investors-will-come-from)).\n- Calls right: Statsig C in May 2025, then the OpenAI acquisition the same year ([src](https://www.statsig.com/blog/statsig-series-c)).\n\n## Read these first\n- [TinyFish partnership](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-enterprise-web-agents-our-partnership-with-tinyfish): the enterprise web-agent thesis.\n- [Why we're backing Statsig](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-product-development-why-were-backing-statsig-2): diligence by office visits and stand-ups.\n- [Outtake partnership](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/building-a-digital-trust-platform-for-an-ai-driven-internet-our-partnership-with-outtake): agentic trust and security.\n- [BI Rising Stars profile (syndicated)](https://briefly-prod.herokuapp.com/anchor/Venture/story/hes-a-rising-star-in-vc-heres-where-iconiqs-ryan-koh-says-the-next-great-investors-will-come-from): his origin story and the early-stage push.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| TinyFish | Series A ($47M per roster row; the firm post gives no size) | 2025-08-20 | ICONIQ led; Koh is third byline after GP Amit Agarwal | not named in firm post | [src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-enterprise-web-agents-our-partnership-with-tinyfish) |\n| Statsig | Series C, $100M at $1.1B | 2025-05-06 | ICONIQ led; Joshi named lead, Koh co-signed | Sequoia, Madrona | [src](https://www.statsig.com/blog/statsig-series-c) |\n| Outtake | Series B, $40M | 2026-01-28 | ICONIQ led; Joshi on board, Koh co-signed | CRV, S32; angels incl. Satya Nadella, Bob McGrew, Shyam Sankar | [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/outtake-raises-40m-series-b-163000387.html) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **TinyFish.** ICONIQ first met the team in February (year unstated) and led the Series A by Aug 2025. ICONIQ was the first named institutional lead in a priced round we can see. Seed investors are not found in the firm post. Agarwal is the board-level partner ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-enterprise-web-agents-our-partnership-with-tinyfish)).\n- **Statsig.** ICONIQ was not first. Sequoia first partnered at pre-seed/seed in 2021 ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/lauren-reeder/)), about 4 years before ICONIQ's C. ICONIQ courted the company \"throughout the years\" before leading ([src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-product-development-why-were-backing-statsig-2)).\n- **Outtake.** ICONIQ was not first. CRV led a $20M Series A earlier ([src](https://www.crv.com/team/murat-bicer), date not on page). ICONIQ led the B in Jan 2026 ([src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/outtake-raises-40m-series-b-163000387.html)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Murali Joshi (ICONIQ GP) | co-signer; Joshi leads, Koh on deal team (Statsig, Outtake) | 2025 | [src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-product-development-why-were-backing-statsig-2) |\n| Amit Agarwal (ICONIQ GP) | co-signer on TinyFish and Outtake | 2025 | [src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-enterprise-web-agents-our-partnership-with-tinyfish) |\n| Kelly Zheng (ICONIQ) | peer co-signer on TinyFish and Outtake | 2025 | [src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/building-a-digital-trust-platform-for-an-ai-driven-internet-our-partnership-with-outtake) |\n| Brooke Chang (ICONIQ) | peer co-signer on Statsig | 2025 | [src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-product-development-why-were-backing-statsig-2) |\n| Matt Jacobson (ICONIQ partner) | co-signer on Statsig | 2025 | [src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-product-development-why-were-backing-statsig-2) |\n| Vijaye Raji (Statsig founder, now at OpenAI) | portfolio founder | 2025 | [src](https://www.statsig.com/blog/statsig-series-c) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact promotion date to Principal (Investor -> Principal), and whether he holds any board seat.\n- Which early-stage (seed/A) checks the early-stage push has made beyond TinyFish. His $800M+ deployed figure needs a deal list.\n- Forbes 30U30 2026 confirmation; education; X or LinkedIn handle; any podcast.\n- How the TinyFish relationship originated (who introduced them, and the year of the February meeting).\n- How he sources: no primary statement found. The inferred pattern is long-running founder courtship, as with Statsig.\n\n## Sources\n- https://briefly-prod.herokuapp.com/anchor/Venture/story/hes-a-rising-star-in-vc-heres-where-iconiqs-ryan-koh-says-the-next-great-investors-will-come-from\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-enterprise-web-agents-our-partnership-with-tinyfish\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/powering-the-future-of-product-development-why-were-backing-statsig-2\n- https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/building-a-digital-trust-platform-for-an-ai-driven-internet-our-partnership-with-outtake\n- https://www.statsig.com/blog/statsig-series-c\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/news/outtake-raises-40m-series-b-163000387.html\n- https://theorg.com/org/iconiq-capital/org-chart/ryan-koh (aggregator)\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/lauren-reeder/\n- https://www.crv.com/team/murat-bicer\n", "person:ryan-shannon": "\n# Ryan Shannon\n\n## One-line take\nRadical's ex-TPG partner who helps run its growth strategy. His own deals are AI-native services and enterprise apps (OffDeal, twice led), and his Substack says frontier models are \"big tech's game to lose\". That cuts against his firm's 2026 neolab bets, so he is the person at Radical who will push hardest on the application side and on growth follow-ons [src](https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/) [src](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/what-are-the-implications-for-startups).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He brings PE-style underwriting to AI. His essays are spreadsheet arguments about burn, dilution, ARR per FTE and procurement budgets, and he publishes a thesis first and then announces the deal that fits it. The August 2024 piece on AI helping small businesses was followed in September 2024 by OffDeal, \"our first core bet within this theme\" [src](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/the-future-of-m-and-a). His growth role sits on top of Radical's early book: the companies he is named on (Cohere, Writer, Twelve Labs, Hebbia, V7) are mostly ones where Radical kept investing at later stages [src](https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/).\n- **What constrains him:** Only one deal (OffDeal) is clearly his own lead. Radical's founding Cohere, Covariant and Promise Robotics checks (2019–2020) were made before he joined full-time in September 2021, so his credit on those is for later involvement, not origination [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/) [src](https://radical.vc/portfolio/covariant/). No board seats are sourced.\n- **What he'd never do (inference from his writing):** fund a company in the middle of his \"barbell\", that is, one with neither deep research nor extreme shipping speed. He calls that zone \"Death Valley\" [src](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/the-death-valley-of-building-in-ai). As of February 2025 he would not back a new lab trying to push the frontier against Google, OpenAI and Anthropic [src](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/what-are-the-implications-for-startups).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He is publicly skeptical of new frontier labs, unlike his firm.** In February 2025 he wrote that pushing the frontier is \"big tech's game to lose\", and that the real players are \"Google, OpenAI / Microsoft, and Anthropic / Amazon. That's it\". In 2026 Radical co-led Discovery Loop, backed Periodic Labs and published \"The Rise of the NeoLab\", none of which he is named on [src](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/what-are-the-implications-for-startups) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-rise-of-the-neolab/).\n- **He says enterprises happily pay for forward-deployed engineers but balk at outcome pricing.** A Radical portfolio company (unnamed) with a product \"an order of magnitude better\" still faces six-month sales cycles. On the forward-deployed engineers he writes: \"they are way, way more expensive!\" He calls services-heavy AI companies \"the most mispriced thing\" in the market (September 2026) [src](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/the-legibility-tax).\n- **\"Selling work, not SaaS\" was his OffDeal thesis from day one.** The September 2024 post says OffDeal is \"explicitly NOT selling software\". He argues AI fixes both reasons services never reached venture scale: margins and the inability to hire fast enough [src](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/the-future-of-m-and-a).\n- **OffDeal timing.** OffDeal was in YC's Winter 2024 batch. Radical led the $4.7M seed (TechCrunch, 2024-09-12), then led the $12M Series A, announced 2025-07-29, \"less than 10 months after\" the seed. YC, Rebel Fund and Centre Street Partners joined the A. The A's angels include execs from Firsthand, another Shannon-credited company [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/12/offdeal-wants-to-help-small-businesses-find-big-exits-with-ai-agents/) [src](https://offdeal.io/news/offdeal-series-a-funding-radical-ventures-2025).\n- **His 2023 valuation math for foundation-model startups.** In March 2023 he said such companies spend $1M to $50M+ a year on training at seed and Series A. On his worked example the model company raises at $42M pre versus $12M for a normal startup: \"a bizarre outcome for what is clearly a less capital efficient business\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/funding-ai-unpacking-high-valuations/). He also cites an unsourced figure of about $40M for GPT-4 training (\"we have heard\"), which reads as an insider number at the time.\n- **He leans on Hebbia's founder.** The Legibility Tax essay builds on George Sivulka's argument that individual productivity gains did not make any firm 10x more valuable. Hebbia is a company he is credited on [src](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/the-legibility-tax).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is a growth-capable partner at a firm that writes $10M+ and growth checks, which matches the segment the owner looks for. His own thesis, though, is AI-native services and vertical apps, which fits the owner's interest in AI-native automation (OffDeal is a Serval-like \"sell the work\" bet). It cuts against the owner's core thesis of new labs and pre-training: he wrote in 2025 that only Google, OpenAI and Anthropic can push the frontier. The deals that matter to you are OffDeal (AI-native services, led twice) and Writer and Cohere (growth-stage enterprise model companies he is credited on). For co-investing in big lab seeds, talk to Jacobs, Rosenberg, Kotite or Toews instead. Approach Shannon for AI-native automation companies raising a Series A or B.\n\n## Background\n- HBA, Ivey Business School at Western University (Ivey Scholar); MBA, Harvard Business School. Years not found [src](https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/)\n- Private equity investor at TPG Capital in San Francisco, working on leveraged buyouts, corporate carve-outs and take-privates of North American businesses. Dates not found [src](https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/)\n- Joined Radical in September 2021 \"as a full-time Investment Team member\". The \"full-time\" wording may mean he was involved earlier, for example during his MBA; that is not verified [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/)\n- Referred to as \"Radical Partner Ryan Shannon\" by 2025-08-11 [src](https://radical.vc/articles/building-the-first-ai-native-investment-bank/). The team page now says Partner and that he \"helps to lead the firm's Growth Strategy\" [src](https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/ | Team page (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://ryanshannon.substack.com | \"Entry Level Investing\". 20 posts in the RSS feed from 2023-10 to 2026-09; roughly monthly in 2024, every 4–6 months since. Linked from Radical's OffDeal post |\n| X | not found | The team page links only @radicalvcfund. No personal handle verified (search budget was used up) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryan-shannon-1b3a7884/ | Linked from the radical.vc team page |\n| GitHub | not found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. Web search was unavailable for this task, so podcasts were not checked; worth a follow-up.\n\n## How they think\n- **The barbell (2026):** winners are either \"Luminaries\" (deep research such as World Labs, Cohere and Anthropic) or \"Sprinters\" (shipping speed and last-mile delivery such as Harvey, ElevenLabs and OffDeal). Everything in between is Death Valley. If you are \"not growing 200% YoY (at least)\", another round may not happen [src](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/the-death-valley-of-building-in-ai).\n- **Last-mile delivery beats model performance (2025-11):** labs build the interstate highways and startups build the local roads. He thinks the \"GPT-wrapper\" worry is overblown for verticals where errors are costly (legal, finance, healthcare) and correct for \"good enough\" categories such as marketing copy [src](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/forget-gpt-wrappers-the-future-of).\n- **Legibility Tax (2026-09):** AI adoption outside coding is slow because there is no cheap way to verify the output and because organisations have no way to hold anyone accountable for machine errors. The lag \"IS the transformation\" [src](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/the-legibility-tax).\n- **Full-stack AI services:** OffDeal is a full-stack company in the YC sense, and he expects more in law, accounting, consulting and insurance [src](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/doubling-down-on-full-stack-ai-our).\n- **Calls:** After DeepSeek (2025-02) he said the market \"massively overreacted\": buy Nvidia if you liked it before, and only the big labs will move the frontier. The first call held up. The second sits awkwardly next to Radical's 2026 neolab investments, which are outside his own deals.\n- **On boards:** Radical encourages boards from the seed stage, and early VCs almost never want to replace the CEO [src](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/why-do-startups-have-boards-of-directors).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [The Legibility Tax](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/the-legibility-tax) (2026-09): his clearest view on enterprise AI adoption and why services and forward-deployed engineers are mispriced.\n2. [The \"Death Valley\" of Building in AI](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/the-death-valley-of-building-in-ai) (2026-03): the barbell screen he uses on companies.\n3. [What are the implications for startups in a post-DeepSeek world?](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/what-are-the-implications-for-startups) (2025-02): his \"only three labs\" view.\n4. [The Future of M&A](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/the-future-of-m-and-a) (2024-09): the \"sell work, not SaaS\" thesis behind OffDeal.\n5. [Funding AI: Unpacking High Valuations](https://radical.vc/articles/funding-ai-unpacking-high-valuations/) (2023-03): how he priced foundation-model startups.\n\n## Social engagement\nNo personal X account was verified. He distributes his writing through Substack and Radical Reads, the firm newsletter, which republished his OffDeal pieces. In his essays he engages with Sam Altman's comments on David Senra's podcast, Jason Furman's GDP arithmetic, George Sivulka (Hebbia) and YC's \"full-stack AI\" framing.\n\n## Portfolio\nDeals he is credited on come from his team page; the round details come from Radical's portfolio data unless noted.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OffDeal | Seed ($4.7M) | 2024-09-12 | yes (Radical led; Shannon bylined the post) | YC (W24 batch, earlier) | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/12/offdeal-wants-to-help-small-businesses-find-big-exits-with-ai-agents/), [Radical](https://radical.vc/articles/the-worlds-first-ai-native-investment-bank/) |\n| OffDeal | Series A ($12M) | 2025-07-29 | yes (double down) | Y Combinator; Rebel Fund; Centre Street Partners; angels incl. Firsthand, Cognition, Rogo execs | [OffDeal](https://offdeal.io/news/offdeal-series-a-funding-radical-ventures-2025) |\n| Firsthand | Seed ($6.65M) | 2024-02-21 | Radical led (David Katz quoted, not Shannon) | not named | [VentureBeat](https://venturebeat.com/ai/exclusive-adtech-pioneers-launch-ai-startup-to-empower-publishers-and-brands-at-crucial-moment/), [Radical](https://radical.vc/portfolio/firsthand/) |\n| Cohere | Founding seed 2019 (before he joined); A 2021; B 2022; Series D $500M co-led 2025-08 | 2019–2025 | firm led seed, co-led D | Index, Tiger, Inovia | [team page](https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/), [Torys](https://www.torys.com/en/work/2025/08/4e762eb5-37a5-4e34-8245-c611c4f323e1) |\n| Writer | Series C ($200M at $1.9B) | 2024-11-12 | co-led (Toews board) | Premji, ICONIQ Growth | [BusinessWire](https://businesswire.com/news/home/20241112482564/en) |\n| Twelve Labs | Seed 2022; extension led 2022-12; A 2024; B 2026 | 2022–2026 | led the extension | Index, Fei-Fei Li | [Radical](https://radical.vc/articles/ctrlf-for-video-search-our-investment-in-twelve-labs/) |\n| Hebbia | Series A ($30M) | 2022-09-27 | no (Index led) | Index; Peter Thiel; Stanley Druckenmiller | [Hebbia](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of) |\n| V7 | Series A ($33M) | 2022-11-29 | co-led (with Temasek; Patel) | Air Street, Amadeus, Partech | [V7](https://www.v7labs.com/news/v7-raises-33m-series-a) |\n| Delphina | Undisclosed | 2023-12-13 | unknown (Toews post) | | [Radical](https://radical.vc/articles/automating-traditional-data-science-why-we-invested-in-delphina/) |\n| Covariant | Undisclosed | 2020 (before he joined full-time) | unknown | | [Radical](https://radical.vc/portfolio/covariant/) |\n| Promise Robotics | Undisclosed | 2020 (before he joined full-time) | unknown | | [Radical](https://radical.vc/portfolio/promise-robotics/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **OffDeal:** OffDeal (founders Ori Eldarov, an ex-investment banker, and Alston Lin, an ex-Meta engineer) went through YC W24. Shannon's August 2024 Substack set out a thesis that AI would help small businesses, and Radical led the $4.7M seed in September 2024, which he called the \"first core bet within this theme\". With more than 30 sell-side mandates launched in 2025, Radical \"doubled down\" and led the $12M Series A in July 2025, bringing total funding to $17M. Radical was first among institutional VCs after YC and led both priced rounds. The intro path is not sourced; the YC batch is the likely route (inference) [src](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/the-future-of-m-and-a) [src](https://offdeal.io/news/offdeal-series-a-funding-radical-ventures-2025).\n- **Growth-strategy deals (inference):** Growth Fund I's first sale was on 2024-06-28. The Writer Series C co-lead (2024-11) and the Cohere co-led round (2025-08, through the RV C1 SPV) fall in that growth fund's window, and Shannon is credited on both. That fits his growth role, but no source names him as a lead on either [src](https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Partner; helps lead Growth Strategy | 2021-09 | [src](https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/) |\n| TPG Capital | Former PE investor (San Francisco) | before 2021-09 | [src](https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/) |\n| Richa Mehta | Fellow Radical partner who co-leads Growth | n/a | [src](https://radical.vc/team/richa-mehta/) |\n| Ori Eldarov / Alston Lin (OffDeal) | Investor; led seed and Series A | 2024-09 | [src](https://offdeal.io/news/offdeal-series-a-funding-radical-ventures-2025) |\n| George Sivulka (Hebbia) | Portfolio founder whose essay he builds on | 2022 | [src](https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/the-legibility-tax) |\n| Y Combinator | Co-investor in OffDeal | 2024 | [src](https://offdeal.io/news/offdeal-series-a-funding-radical-ventures-2025) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact date of the promotion to Partner, and his title from 2021 to 2024 (LinkedIn could not be read; the Wayback Machine was not used).\n- Was he involved with Radical before September 2021, given the \"full-time\" wording and his credit on 2019–2020 deals?\n- Which portfolio company is the \"order of magnitude better\" product with six-month sales cycles in the Legibility Tax essay (Writer, Hebbia or Cohere?).\n- Board seats: none sourced, including at OffDeal.\n- Podcasts and personal X were not checked because the search budget was used up.\n- Does he dissent internally on the neolab deals, or has his view changed since February 2025?\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/ryan-shannon/ (checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ventures-expands-leadership-team-adds-first-investment-partner-in-sf-bay-area/ (2021-09-20)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/funding-ai-unpacking-high-valuations/ (2023-03-19)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/the-worlds-first-ai-native-investment-bank/ (2024-09-30)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/building-the-first-ai-native-investment-bank/ (2025-08-11)\n- https://ryanshannon.substack.com/feed (20 posts, 2023-10 to 2026-09), especially the posts linked above\n- https://offdeal.io/news/offdeal-series-a-funding-radical-ventures-2025 (2025-07-29)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/12/offdeal-wants-to-help-small-businesses-find-big-exits-with-ai-agents/\n- https://venturebeat.com/ai/exclusive-adtech-pioneers-launch-ai-startup-to-empower-publishers-and-brands-at-crucial-moment/ (2024-02-21)\n- Radical portfolio pages for Firsthand, Promise Robotics, Covariant, Writer, Hebbia, V7 and Delphina\n- Round data from data/rows/radical-ventures-portfolio-deals.csv\n", "person:ryan-shmeizer": "\n# Ryan Shmeizer\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. The shared web-search budget was used up when this task started, so the profile rests on direct fetches of his HOF bio, NFX Signal (aggregator), every GreenPoint Partners news post, fal's blog, and what the repo already has on HOF, fal and Flapping Airplanes. LinkedIn (HTTP 999) and X (HTTP 402, handle @ryanshmeizer per NFX) could not be read, so LinkedIn-derived lines come from the earlier HOF-capital pass and could not be re-verified. His HOF bio uses \"he\".*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is a recently hired HOF partner who covers the part of HOF's book closest to the thesis: AI infrastructure and physical-world tech, near fal, Flapping Airplanes, Crusoe and the Compute Infrastructure vehicle. But no source names him on any HOF deal, so treat him as a watch name until one does.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he could win:** He has the most varied venture training at HOF among the non-founders. He spent 2011-2020 at Insight (growth), Spark (early) and Thrive (Affirm, Slack, Grammarly), then co-built GreenPoint's venture arm \"from inception to over $1 billion in AUM\" [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/ryan-shmeizer/). His GreenPoint model was to win deals by being a commercial bridge: he says the firm \"acted as a conduit between our portfolio companies and senior real asset stakeholders\" [src](https://www.greenpointpartners.com/news-posts/greenpoint-announces-global-platform-investing-at-the-intersection-of-real-assets-technology-and-sustainability). That model matches HOF's own pitch to founders, which is LP-network access (see `firms/hof-capital.md`).\n- **What constrains him:** At HOF the frontier-AI calls (Anthropic, Crusoe, Extropic, SpaceX/xAI) are attributed to the co-founders Hisham Elhaddad and Fady Yacoub on their own bios (see `firms/hof-capital.md`). His only visible link to HOF's AI-compute deals is that he reshared firm posts. His own track record as a lead is in proptech and real-asset software, not AI.\n- **What he'd likely never do (inference):** lead a pre-training lab's mega-seed on his own. Nothing in his record touches labs, model research or chips. His pattern is enterprise software sold into big incumbent industries.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The \"AI infra\" partner is new to HOF and has no HOF deal attribution.** His bio frames his focus as \"AI infrastructure, enterprise software, and technologies reshaping the physical world\" [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/ryan-shmeizer/). The bio names no HOF deals at all. Compare Tahseen Rashid, whose bio says he \"sourced and led\" five deals (see `firms/hof-capital.md`).\n- **The fal and Flapping links are amplification, not attribution.** The posts he reshared are HOF's company posts: \"Excited for HOF Capital to partner with fal!\" and HOF is \"proud to back\" the Flapping founders [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryan-shmeizer-0543a12a/) (guest view captured in the earlier HOF pass, relative dates, not re-verified 2026-11-10). No HOF person is named in either round's announcements in this repo. The other HOF people are just less visible online, so his reshares are not evidence that he did the deals.\n- **His one sourced venture vehicle was a $134M real-asset tech fund backed by strategics.** GreenPoint's Technology Fund I closed at $134M, about $200M with co-invest, on 2022-05-18. PSP Investments was the lead LP, with Ivanhoé Cambridge, Goodman Group, Lendlease, Greystar, Charter Hall and others [src](https://www.greenpointpartners.com/news-posts/greenpoint-announces-global-platform-investing-at-the-intersection-of-real-assets-technology-and-sustainability). PSP called itself \"the lead investor in their Tech Fund\" in the same release. He has raised from operator-LPs who buy the products the portfolio sells. That is a sourcing and diligence edge for physical-world infrastructure such as data centres and power.\n- **He has called his first exit himself.** As GP he pointed to \"our first exit with the acquisition of Envizi by IBM\" [src](https://www.greenpointpartners.com/news-posts/greenpoint-announces-global-platform-investing-at-the-intersection-of-real-assets-technology-and-sustainability) (2022-05-18). Envizi is ESG and energy data software, so this is a small strategic exit, not a power-law outcome.\n- **He left as GreenPoint pivoted away from venture (inference from dates).** NFX dates his GP role 2020-2025 [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/ryan-shmeizer). GreenPoint's 2025-09-17 announcement of over $1B in \"inaugural fund series\" commitments quotes only founder Chris Green and describes a real-assets fund (Lysara, Outpost) [src](https://www.greenpointpartners.com/news-posts/greenpoint-closes-inaugural-fund-series-with-over-1-billion-in-equity-commitments). Its current team page does not list him, and no tech-venture investments appear on its investments page [src](https://www.greenpointpartners.com/team), [src](https://www.greenpointpartners.com/investments). The \"$1 billion in AUM\" in his HOF bio most likely counts this real-asset capital. That last point is my inference, not sourced.\n- **NFX shows a small personal check range.** It lists \"Sweet Spot $25K\", a range of $10K-$50K, 3 investments on record and a \"Current Fund Size $300M\" [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/ryan-shmeizer) (aggregator, may be stale). Its sector ranks are SMB Software, Education and Space at seed and Series A, with no AI. The record is likely out of date relative to the HOF mandate.\n- **He has a South Africa link.** He has a University of Cape Town BBusSc in quantitative finance as well as the Wharton degree (NFX) [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/ryan-shmeizer). HOF's bio says he \"grew up in South Africa\" [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/ryan-shmeizer/). This matters little for the thesis, but it is a possible bridge to non-US LPs. HOF's own LP base is global, across 37 countries per its partners page.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium, as a watch name.** For: he sits on HOF's AI infrastructure and physical-world desk, the area next to fal (inference compute), Flapping Airplanes (a $1B Series A research lab), Crusoe, Extropic and HOF's Compute Infrastructure vehicle. HOF writes into the large late rounds of frontier names, so he is a possible co-investor or follow-on source for a $20M-check seed investor. Against: no sourced HOF deal, no AI-native lead in his own history (proptech, ESG data, logistics software at GreenPoint; growth-era fintech and SaaS at Thrive), and the firm's frontier-AI access runs through Elhaddad and Yacoub. Reassess as soon as any HOF announcement names him. The deals that would matter: fal Series E/F, Flapping Airplanes Series A, and any HOF compute-vehicle deal.\n\n## Background\n- Wharton, BS Economics, magna cum laude (HOF bio; degree detail per NFX) [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/ryan-shmeizer/), [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/ryan-shmeizer).\n- University of Cape Town, Bachelor of Business Science, Quantitative Finance (NFX, aggregator) [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/ryan-shmeizer).\n- 2011-2014: Insight Venture Partners, investment team. The HOF bio confirms the 2011 start [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/ryan-shmeizer/). End date per NFX [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/ryan-shmeizer).\n- 2014-2018: Spark Capital, investor (NFX) [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/ryan-shmeizer).\n- 2018-2020: Thrive Capital, investor. Worked on Affirm, Slack and Grammarly per the HOF bio [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/ryan-shmeizer/). Dates per NFX.\n- 2020-2025: GreenPoint Partners, General Partner and Head of Venture Investing. GP title confirmed 2022-05-18 [src](https://www.greenpointpartners.com/news-posts/greenpoint-announces-global-platform-investing-at-the-intersection-of-real-assets-technology-and-sustainability). Dates per NFX.\n- 2025 or 2026 (not found): HOF Capital, Partner [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/ryan-shmeizer/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://hofcapital.com/team/ryan-shmeizer/ | HOF bio, read 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://www.twitter.com/ryanshmeizer | Handle per NFX; HTTP 402, not read |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryan-shmeizer-0543a12a/ | Main public channel; reshares HOF deal posts; HTTP 999 on 2026-11-10 |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| Other | https://signal.nfx.com/investors/ryan-shmeizer | Aggregator; AngelList https://www.angel.co/u/ryan-shmeizer and Crunchbase https://www.crunchbase.com/person/ryan-shmeizer linked from it, not read |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. No search budget was left to look for any.\n\n## How they think\n- **Software sold into incumbent physical industries.** The GreenPoint fund backed tech \"underpinning the digital transformation of multitrillion-dollar real asset industries\", with the investors acting as the customers [src](https://www.greenpointpartners.com/news-posts/greenpoint-announces-global-platform-investing-at-the-intersection-of-real-assets-technology-and-sustainability). His HOF focus on \"technologies reshaping the physical world\" continues that view [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/ryan-shmeizer/).\n- **AI business models.** The earlier HOF pass recorded a LinkedIn post of his on AI pricing moving to outcome-based models, prompted by Cognition's raise [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryan-shmeizer-0543a12a/) (paraphrase of a guest view, no verbatim text captured).\n- **Calls right or wrong:** Envizi to IBM was an early exit (2022). The outcomes of Built Technologies, OpenSpace, Snapdocs and Relay Payments were not checked. No HOF calls can be attributed to him.\n\n## Read these first\n1. HOF bio: his mandate and career arc [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/ryan-shmeizer/).\n2. GreenPoint's 2022-05-18 Tech Fund I close: his only on-record quote, the LP list and the portfolio [src](https://www.greenpointpartners.com/news-posts/greenpoint-announces-global-platform-investing-at-the-intersection-of-real-assets-technology-and-sustainability).\n3. `firms/hof-capital.md`: who did what at HOF, and why the frontier-AI deals trace to the co-founders.\n4. `companies/flapping-airplanes.md` and `companies/fal.md`: the two rounds he amplified.\n\n## Social engagement\nOn LinkedIn he reshares HOF's company announcements: fal, Flapping Airplanes, and Elhaddad's post about HOF's \"early investments in Anthropic and SpaceXAI\" [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryan-shmeizer-0543a12a/) (per the earlier HOF pass). He also posts on AI pricing. X not read. Who amplifies him: not found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| fal (HOF) | Series E and F ($1.65B combined per the HOF post he reshared) | ~2026-11 (relative LinkedIn date) | No (HOF participant; his personal role not stated) | not found; prior rounds Sequoia, Kleiner, a16z, Meritech | [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryan-shmeizer-0543a12a/) |\n| Flapping Airplanes (HOF) | Series A $1B (reported ~$6B) | 2026-10-21 | No (HOF one of ~20 participants; his role not stated) | Index, Lux (co-leads), Sequoia, GV, Kleiner, NVIDIA | [src](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/) |\n| Envizi (GreenPoint) | not stated; exited to IBM | exit by 2022-05 | not stated | not found | [src](https://www.greenpointpartners.com/news-posts/greenpoint-announces-global-platform-investing-at-the-intersection-of-real-assets-technology-and-sustainability) |\n| Built Technologies, OpenSpace, ility, Fetch Package, Snapdocs, Relay Payments (GreenPoint) | not stated | by 2022-05 | not stated (fund-level; he was GP and Head of Venture) | not found | [src](https://www.greenpointpartners.com/news-posts/greenpoint-announces-global-platform-investing-at-the-intersection-of-real-assets-technology-and-sustainability) |\n| Affirm, Slack, Grammarly (Thrive) | not stated | 2018-2020 (his Thrive tenure per NFX) | No (\"worked on\") | not found | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/ryan-shmeizer/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n**Standout (unattributed): HOF in fal's Series E/F and Flapping Airplanes' Series A, 2026.**\n- *What is known:* HOF entered Flapping Airplanes at the $1B Series A on 2026-10-21. It was one of about 20 non-lead participants behind co-leads Index and Lux, alongside Sequoia, GV, Kleiner, NVIDIA and others. It was not in the seed [src](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/). The reported valuation is about $6B (see `companies/flapping-airplanes.md`). For fal, the HOF post he reshared puts the Series E and F at $1.65B combined [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryan-shmeizer-0543a12a/). fal's blog had no Series E/F post as of 2026-11-10; its last funding post is the Dec 2025 Series D at $4.5B [src](https://blog.fal.ai/). The E/F valuation was not found. Forbes reported talks at about $8B in March 2026 (see `companies/fal.md`).\n- *How HOF found them / who did it:* not found. Flapping's founder story runs through Index's Mark Xu and Shardul Shah, and no HOF person appears in it (see `firms/hof-capital.md`). HOF's frontier-AI access is documented through Elhaddad (Anthropic via the FTX-estate secondary, Crusoe) and Yacoub (Extropic), so either is a more likely owner than Shmeizer on the evidence. HOF's Strategic Opportunities Fund II made its first sale nine weeks before the Flapping A, which shows only that capital was available, not who sourced the deal.\n- *His part:* resharing HOF's posts (\"proud to back\"; \"Excited for HOF Capital to partner with fal!\"). That is amplification, not attribution. **Not counted as his deal.**\n\n**GreenPoint Technology Fund I (2020-2022).** He co-built the venture arm with founder Chris Green and closed $134M, about $200M with co-invest. PSP Investments was the lead LP, with real-asset owners such as Ivanhoé Cambridge, Goodman, Lendlease and Greystar as LPs. By May 2022 the fund had 16 companies and one exit, Envizi to IBM [src](https://www.greenpointpartners.com/news-posts/greenpoint-announces-global-platform-investing-at-the-intersection-of-real-assets-technology-and-sustainability). Built Technologies' CEO credited GreenPoint with opening \"channels to the decision makers\" at large real-estate firms (same source). That is the sourcing and value-add model he describes.\n\n**How he sources:** On the record, through strategic-LP access. Companies that sell into big asset owners are drawn by the chance to reach those owners through the fund's LPs (GreenPoint), and at HOF the equivalent is HOF's global LP network. He has no sourced founder-intro stories or first-check origin stories.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| HOF Capital | Partner | not found (after GreenPoint, 2025 per NFX end date) | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/ryan-shmeizer/) |\n| Hisham Elhaddad, Fady Yacoub | HOF co-founders; own the firm's frontier-AI deal attributions | not found | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/hisham-elhaddad/), [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/fady-yacoub/) |\n| GreenPoint Partners / Chris Green | GP and Head of Venture; Green is founder and CEO | 2020-2025 (NFX) | [src](https://www.greenpointpartners.com/news-posts/greenpoint-announces-global-platform-investing-at-the-intersection-of-real-assets-technology-and-sustainability) |\n| Eric Boothe, Rick Michaux, John Forrest | GreenPoint operating partners alongside him (ex-JLL) | 2022 | [src](https://www.greenpointpartners.com/news-posts/greenpoint-announces-global-platform-investing-at-the-intersection-of-real-assets-technology-and-sustainability) |\n| PSP Investments | Lead LP of his GreenPoint Tech Fund I | 2022 | same |\n| Thrive Capital | Investor | 2018-2020 (NFX) | [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/ryan-shmeizer) |\n| Spark Capital | Investor | 2014-2018 (NFX) | [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/ryan-shmeizer) |\n| Insight Venture Partners | Investment team | 2011-2014 | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/ryan-shmeizer/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did he join HOF, and which vehicle(s) does he work on (Compute Infrastructure? SOF II)?\n- Is he the deal partner on fal E/F or Flapping A, or did Elhaddad or Yacoub do them? Check fal's E/F announcement once it is published, and HOF's LinkedIn posts.\n- What is the valuation of fal's E/F, and who led it?\n- Who were his personal deals at Spark (2014-2018)? None are named in any source read.\n- Did GreenPoint wind down its tech-venture strategy when he left? Who manages the Tech Fund I portfolio now?\n- Read his X account (@ryanshmeizer) and LinkedIn posts for verbatim views on AI infra and pricing.\n\n## Sources\n- https://hofcapital.com/team/ryan-shmeizer/ (bio and headshot, read 2026-11-10)\n- https://signal.nfx.com/investors/ryan-shmeizer (aggregator, read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.greenpointpartners.com/news-posts/greenpoint-announces-global-platform-investing-at-the-intersection-of-real-assets-technology-and-sustainability (2022-05-18)\n- https://www.greenpointpartners.com/news-posts/greenpoint-closes-inaugural-fund-series-with-over-1-billion-in-equity-commitments (2025-09-17)\n- https://www.greenpointpartners.com/team, https://www.greenpointpartners.com/investments (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryan-shmeizer-0543a12a/ (via earlier HOF pass; HTTP 999 today)\n- https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/ (Flapping Series A)\n- https://blog.fal.ai/ (no E/F post as of 2026-11-10)\n- firms/hof-capital.md, companies/fal.md, companies/flapping-airplanes.md\n", "person:saam-motamedi": "\n# Saam Motamedi\n\n*Created 2026-11-10 from a voice sweep: his Greylock memos (2019-2026), Greymatter transcripts, the 20VC episode (2024-07-15) and TechCrunch coverage. Deal rows for him already in `data/deals.csv` are summarized under Portfolio. Pronouns: none stated in the sources read, so this file uses \"they\".*\n\n## One-line take\nGreylock's AI and enterprise general partner, made GP at 26 after three years. They co-led Adept's first round, led the Snorkel, Braintrust, LlamaIndex and Resolve seeds, and were named on Greylock's first and largest-ever check, Anthropic's Series F.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win.** They start with people, not companies. They say about 80% of Greylock investments are with people met before the company existed, and they once spent 90 days with founders in Greylock's offices before writing a $6.5M check ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)). Abnormal (2018) and Tome started inside Greylock ([memo](https://greylock.com/blog/our-investment-in-abnormal-security/), [Greymatter 2023](https://greylock.com/blog/ais-transformative-power/)).\n- **What constrains them.** Partner time: one or two new investments a year, with \"our constraint is our time\" ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)). Ownership: they target 20-25%+ and are \"more ownership sensitive than price sensitive\" (same source).\n- **What they'd never do.** They won't back a hot AI app that fails a retention and defensibility filter, even one that went from 0 to $20M in six months. They won't sell specialist infrastructure into a buyer's core competency, which they call \"a more niche opportunity\" ([Greymatter 2022](https://greylock.com/blog/the-intelligent-future/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- Ownership comes before price. On core early positions they target 20-25%+, but say \"we're not as religious about that as we once were\" because Greylock now gives founders more capital up front ([20VC 2024-07-15](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)).\n- Check sizes: their last two new checks (12 months to mid-2024) were \"6.5 million\" and \"$5 million\". The $5M went into the Braintrust seed ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)).\n- Large seeds can carry less risk: Greylock led Upwind's seed with \"the round size was $26 million\" because the founders had already sold a company to NetApp ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)).\n- On seed price: \"I really don't believe you should be passing on price in those ranges\" (a $20-40M post) ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)).\n- ElevenLabs pass: \"we got a tiny check offered to us at like 100 million price\" and declined it, since a ~$200K check seemed pointless ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)).\n- AgentSync lesson: Elad Gil offered them the seed and they hesitated. Their rule since: \"when someone really great brings you into a round don't ask twice\" ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)).\n- Biggest mistakes are seed passes where \"we've met someone truly iconic and we did not like the idea\" ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)).\n- On incubation, despite owning the Abnormal story: \"broadly speaking I don't think incubations work\" ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)).\n- Braintrust: \"I texted my team I was like we are immediately investing in this man\" ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)). They had known Ankur Goyal \"for nearly 8 years\" ([memo](https://greylock.com/blog/infrastructure-for-ais-new-era/)).\n- In March 2023 they said \"there's likely sub 10 companies\" at the foundation-model layer, naming OpenAI, Anthropic, Adept, Inflection and Google ([Greymatter](https://greylock.com/blog/ais-transformative-power/)). Greylock's first Anthropic check came at the Series F, \"the largest investment in the firm's history\" ([TechCrunch 2026-07-15](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/15/why-greylock-capped-its-new-fund-at-1-5b-when-it-says-it-could-have-raised-more/)).\n- Changed their mind on focused models: \"start with a better focused model and then very quickly move up the stack\" ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)).\n- The VC whose portfolio they'd most swap for is Gili Raanan (Cyberstarts). The auto-transcript garbles the name, so this is identified from context ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** They are among the most active early AI partners at a $1.5B fund (Fund 18) that can lead $10M-$35M rounds: Resolve's $35M seed, Antioch's $32M A and Adept's $65M A (co-led with Addition). Adept, Anthropic, Snorkel, Braintrust, LlamaIndex, Modular and Predibase are the deals that matter here.\n\nAs a co-investor they are a mixed fit with the owner's style. Greylock wants 20-25% and leads; it is not a passive syndicate partner. Their stated openness to large seeds into proven teams does match the owner's thesis. The Elad Gil and Cyberstarts relationships show they value being brought into rounds by strong peers.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford, where they say their AI work began ([Greylock bio](https://greylock.com/team/saam-motamedi/)). Degree and year not confirmed.\n- Founded Guru Labs, an ML fintech startup, and worked in product management at RelateIQ, which Salesforce acquired ([TechCrunch 2022-03-09](https://techcrunch.com/2022/03/09/hear-how-snorkel-ai-pitched-and-won-over-greylock-on-techcrunch-live/)).\n- Joined Greylock in 2016 ([TechCrunch 2022-03-14](https://techcrunch.com/2022/03/14/snorkel-ai-pitch-raise-techcrunch-live/)).\n- Named general partner in 2019 at age 26, Greylock's youngest partner to date ([Greylock firm news](https://greylock.com/firm-news/saam-motamedi-is-greylocks-newest-general-partner/), [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2022/03/09/hear-how-snorkel-ai-pitched-and-won-over-greylock-on-techcrunch-live/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://greylock.com/team/saam-motamedi/ | Bio essay \"Selecting the right preconditions\"; article index at https://greylock.com/blog/saam-motamedi/articles/ (24 items, 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://greylock.com/blog/saam-motamedi/articles/ | Investment memos roughly 2-3 a year since 2019 |\n| X | https://twitter.com/saammotamedi | Not crawled; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/saammotamedi | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://rss.buzzsprout.com/1926433.rss | Enterprise AI Innovators (fka Enterprise Software Innovators), co-hosted with Evan Reiser since 2022-01; ~57 of 73 episodes name them as host. Also Greymatter episodes 2020-2024 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nFull catalogue in `data/rows/saam-motamedi-voice-media.csv` (100 items).\n- 2020-07 — Greymatter — The Virtual VC (with Reid Hoffman) — [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/reid-hoffman-and-saam-motamedi-the-virtual-vc/id1089013200?i=1000485199179) — not mined.\n- 2020-07 — TechCrunch Early Stage — customer advisory boards — [link](https://techcrunch.com/2020/07/24/building-your-startups-customer-advisory-board/) — 3-6 CAB customers with small advisory grants; a monthly cadence plus quarterly customer days.\n- 2021-04 — Hacker Valley Studio #129 — [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/episode-129-seeing-the-value-in-everything-with/id1471881997?i=1000515314027) — not mined.\n- 2022-03 — TechCrunch Live with Snorkel AI — [link](https://techcrunch.com/2022/03/14/snorkel-ai-pitch-raise-techcrunch-live/) — at seed and A they look for the story and the team.\n- 2022-09 — Greymatter — Introducing the Intelligent Future — [transcript](https://greylock.com/blog/the-intelligent-future/) — build-vs-buy lens; actuation as the next wave.\n- 2023-03 — Greymatter — AI's Transformative Power — [transcript](https://greylock.com/blog/ais-transformative-power/) — sub-10 foundation-model companies; Tome incubation.\n- 2024-07 — 20VC — [episode](https://thetwentyminutevc.libsyn.com/20vc-why-we-are-in-a-bubble-now-is-frothier-than-2021-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-series-a-why-seed-pricing-is-rational-large-seed-rounds-have-less-risk-why-many-ai-apps-have-bs-revenue-are-not-sustainable-with-saam-motamedi-greylock) — the richest source; see Differential insight.\n- 2024-11 — Swish Ventures podcast, with Amiram Shachar of Upwind — [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/greylocks-saam-motamedi-upwinds-amiram-shachar-the/id1762300491?i=1000676836133) — not mined.\n- 2025-12 — Uncapped #37 with Jack Altman — [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/uncapped-37-saam-motamedi-from-greylock-partners/id1801867202?i=1000741507314) — only AI summaries were reachable, so nothing was mined.\n- 2025-12 and 2026-07 — TBPN guest segments — not mined.\n\n## How they think\n- **Main theses.**\n  - AI moves \"large swaths of spending\" from labor to software ([bio](https://greylock.com/team/saam-motamedi/)).\n  - Co-pilots will spread through every function.\n  - Models that act are the next quantum leap; this was the Adept thesis ([memo](https://greylock.com/blog/a-human-centric-path-to-agi/)).\n  - Every tech wave needs a new infrastructure stack: evals (Braintrust) and data frameworks (LlamaIndex).\n- **Contrarian views.**\n  - In mid-2024 they called AI investing a bubble \"frothier than 2021\".\n  - They doubt the Series B vintage as a whole will perform.\n  - Seed pricing is rational for scarce, iconic teams.\n  - Incubation broadly fails.\n  - Investors can help companies find product-market fit ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)).\n- **Calls right or wrong.**\n  - Right: the 2023 sub-10 lab thesis, and the Adept/actuation thesis (direction right; Adept itself went to Amazon in 2024).\n  - Wrong, by their own account: the ElevenLabs pass and the AgentSync hesitation. They also had avoided focused-model companies.\n\n## Read these first\n1. 20VC (2024-07-15) — ownership, check sizes, passes and mistakes in their own words. [Transcript](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)\n2. AI's Transformative Power (2023-03-30) — their three-layer map of AI value and the sub-10 lab view. [Transcript](https://greylock.com/blog/ais-transformative-power/)\n3. A Human-Centric Path to AGI (2022-04-26) — the Adept memo. [Memo](https://greylock.com/blog/a-human-centric-path-to-agi/)\n4. Infrastructure for AI's New Era (2023-12-13) — the Braintrust memo and co-investor list. [Memo](https://greylock.com/blog/infrastructure-for-ais-new-era/)\n5. Our Investment in Abnormal AI (2019-11-19) — the incubation template. [Memo](https://greylock.com/blog/our-investment-in-abnormal-security/)\n\n## Social engagement\nNot crawled this sweep. One sampled post congratulates Jack Altman on launching Uncapped and calls him \"a dear friend\" ([X, 2025-03-13](https://twitter.com/saammotamedi/status/1900218958705549648)). On 20VC they name Elad Gil and Gili Raanan as the outside VCs they most respect.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Abnormal AI | Series A ($24M; incubated 2018) | 2019-11 | yes | | [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/our-investment-in-abnormal-security/) |\n| Snorkel AI | Seed ($3.3M per TechCrunch), then Series A | 2019 | co-led seed, led A | GV | [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/snorkel-making-ai-practical-investment/) |\n| Cresta | Series A | 2020-02 | yes | | [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/experts-on-day-one-our-investment-in-cresta/) |\n| Apiiro | Series A ($35M) | 2020-10 | | | [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/reinventing-secure-development-lifecycle/) |\n| Predibase | Seed | 2021 | yes | | [blog](https://greylock.com/blog/congratulations-predibase-and-rubrik/) |\n| Adept | Series A ($65M) | 2022-04 | co-led | Addition | [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/a-human-centric-path-to-agi/) |\n| Opal Security | Seed and Series A | 2022-06 | | | [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/scalable-least-privilege/) |\n| Modular | Seed ($30M) | 2022 | no | GV (lead) | [GV](https://www.gv.com/news/modular-qualcomm-inference) |\n| Orb | Investment | 2023-03 | | | [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/the-modern-pricing-platform/) |\n| LlamaIndex | Seed | 2023-06 | yes | | [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/the-data-framework-for-llms/) |\n| Braintrust | Seed ($5.1M round; $5M Greylock check) | 2023-12 | yes | Elad Gil; Alana Goyal (Basecase) | [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/infrastructure-for-ais-new-era/) |\n| Upwind | Seed ($26M round) | | yes | Cyberstarts | [20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser) |\n| Resolve AI | Seed ($35M) | 2024-10 | yes | Unusual Ventures | [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/introducing-resolve/) |\n| Cogent Security | Seed ($11M) | 2025-07 | yes | | [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/introducing-cogent-ai-agents-for-vulnerability-management/) |\n| Fable Security | Seed | 2025-07 | | | [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/introducing-fable-ai-native-human-risk-management/) |\n| Anthropic | Series F ($13B at $183B) | 2025-09 | no | ICONIQ (lead) | [Greylock](https://greylock.com/portfolio/anthropic/) |\n| Antioch | Series A ($32M) | 2026-09 | yes | A* | [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/introducing-antioch-the-simulation-platform-for-physical-ai/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Abnormal AI.** Initiated at Greylock's offices in 2018 with Evan Reiser and Sanjay Jeyakumar, who came from TellApart (a Greylock company). Rajiv Gupta, whom Greylock had backed twice before, joined the board. Greylock led the $24M A ([memo](https://greylock.com/blog/our-investment-in-abnormal-security/)). Reiser and Motamedi have co-hosted a CIO podcast since 2022.\n- **Snorkel AI.** Spun out of the Stanford AI Lab and worked out of Greylock's offices. Greylock co-led the seed with GV in spring 2019 and led the A in fall 2019, about six months later ([memo](https://greylock.com/blog/snorkel-making-ai-practical-investment/)).\n- **Braintrust.** They had known Ankur Goyal for about 8 years. After the first meeting they texted the team \"we are immediately investing\", and Greylock led the $5.1M seed with a $5M check. Elad Gil helped initiate the company ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser), [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/infrastructure-for-ais-new-era/)).\n- **Upwind.** The founders had built and sold their previous company to NetApp. Greylock led a seed round of $26M alongside Cyberstarts ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)).\n- **ElevenLabs (pass).** Offered a ~$200K allocation at about a $100M price; declined ([20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Greylock | Joined 2016; general partner since 2019 | 2016 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2022/03/14/snorkel-ai-pitch-raise-techcrunch-live/) |\n| Reid Hoffman | Partner; co-hosted Intelligent Future and Greymatter episodes | 2020 | [Greymatter](https://greylock.com/blog/the-intelligent-future/) |\n| Asheem Chandna | Partner on the Abnormal incubation and Anthropic | 2018 | [20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser) |\n| Evan Reiser | Abnormal founder; podcast co-host | 2018 | [feed](https://rss.buzzsprout.com/1926433.rss) |\n| Elad Gil | Braintrust co-investor; offered them the AgentSync seed | 2023 | [20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser) |\n| Gili Raanan (Cyberstarts) | Upwind co-investor; the VC whose portfolio they'd most swap for | | [20VC](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser) |\n| Ankur Goyal | Known about 8 years before the Braintrust seed | ~2015 | [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/infrastructure-for-ais-new-era/) |\n| Jack Altman | Close friend (Uncapped guest) | | [X](https://twitter.com/saammotamedi/status/1900218958705549648) |\n| Dave Munichiello (GV) | Snorkel seed co-lead; Modular syndicate | 2019 | [memo](https://greylock.com/blog/snorkel-making-ai-practical-investment/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Uncapped #37 (2025-12): get a real transcript. Summaries mention an \"inputs\" performance document and a \"zero market risk\" rule, but these are unverified.\n- Which company got the $6.5M check after 90 days in Greylock's offices (2023-24)? Possibly Resolve or Cogent.\n- The X archive and the Intelligent Future summit videos on YouTube were not catalogued.\n- Swish Ventures and Hacker Valley episodes have not been mined.\n\n## Sources\n- https://greylock.com/team/saam-motamedi/\n- https://greylock.com/blog/saam-motamedi/articles/\n- https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/saam-motamedi-why-series-b-won-t-make-money-why-1m-arr-is-a-bs-milestone-for-ser\n- https://greylock.com/blog/the-intelligent-future/\n- https://greylock.com/blog/ais-transformative-power/\n- https://greylock.com/blog/our-investment-in-abnormal-security/\n- https://greylock.com/blog/snorkel-making-ai-practical-investment/\n- https://greylock.com/blog/a-human-centric-path-to-agi/\n- https://greylock.com/blog/the-data-framework-for-llms/\n- https://greylock.com/blog/infrastructure-for-ais-new-era/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2022/03/09/hear-how-snorkel-ai-pitched-and-won-over-greylock-on-techcrunch-live/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2022/03/14/snorkel-ai-pitch-raise-techcrunch-live/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/15/why-greylock-capped-its-new-fund-at-1-5b-when-it-says-it-could-have-raised-more/\n- https://greylock.com/firm-news/saam-motamedi-is-greylocks-newest-general-partner/\n- https://rss.buzzsprout.com/1926433.rss\n- https://feeds.megaphone.fm/GRL5808166404\n", "person:saanya-ojha": "\n# Saanya Ojha\n\n## One-line take\nA BCV growth partner who covers security and data-infra at Series B and later, and who writes one of the sharper weekly public reads on frontier-AI capital markets (GPU credit, Anthropic's S-1, lab services JVs). Worth following as an analyst of AI capital. Not a frontier-lab check-writer.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** a growth-equity investor's view of public markets. That comes from Goldman hedge-fund investing and a Coatue partnership ([src](https://saanyaojha.substack.com/about); [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/)). They also have an enterprise-buyer feedback loop: they co-chair BCV's CTO and CISO advisory boards ([src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions)). The result is conviction growth checks in security and infra that sell to cautious enterprises: Cape, Halcyon, Armadin, HYCU.\n- **What constrains them:** their mandate is Series B and later ([src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-aditya-modak-and-akhil-aggarwal-our-new-growth-associates)), and they say they walk away when valuations detach. Their one frontier-lab tie (poolside) is a CMS coverage tag only, with Rak Garg and Enrique Salem as the visible partners.\n- **What they'd never do:** chase a priced-to-perfection round. Their \"hardest call\" of 2025 was passing on companies they liked because of valuation, and they say \"Discipline compounds.\" ([src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions)). They also say a startup \"can't launch as a platform\" and should start with a narrow use case.\n- **The gap is the story:** they invest in growth-stage security and infra, but write mostly about frontier-AI economics, to 8,000+ subscribers ([src](https://saanyaojha.substack.com/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **BCV's \"partner\" tags mean current coverage, not who sourced the deal.** BCV's portfolio CMS tags Ojha on Redis (`year_of_investment` 2012) and HYCU (2021). Both investments predate their arrival at BCV, which was by 2023-02-28 ([CMS query](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26references(*%5Bslug.current%3D%3D%22saanya-ojha%22%5D._id)%5D.title); [fund release](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/)). The CMS also dates Astronomer to 2025, while BCV's own post says \"We first invested in Astronomer back in 2019\" ([src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/from-airflow-to-dataops-astronomer-is-building-the-data-control-plane-for-the-enterprise)). So a CMS partner tag at BCV is not evidence of origination. This affects how much credit for poolside, and for other firm-mapped deals in this repo, goes to the tagged partners.\n- **The poolside credit is thin.** Ojha is in the CMS `partner` array for poolside, alongside Rak Garg and Enrique Salem, and the round is tagged Seed 2023 ([CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22poolside%22%5D)). But the rendered portfolio table names only Garg and Salem ([src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/portfolio/poolside/)). Ojha's own team page leaves poolside out of their listed investments ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/saanya-ojha/)), and they appear only in an unlabeled avatar strip on the poolside post ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/poolside-is-scaling-a-team-to-help-billions-code-and-realize-agi/)). The growth-stage $500M round in 2024-10, reported as BCV-led ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/09/12/ai-startup-poolside-3-billion-valuation-bain-capital-ventures/)), would fit a growth partner. No source names them on it.\n- **They read neoclouds as a credit story.** In \"Guppy-Level Death, Investment-Grade Debt\" (2026-10-28) they predict \"guppy-level death and consolidation\" across the 200+ neoclouds. They contrast CoreWeave's widening bond yields with Lambda's 6.78% contract-backed financing, and argue that lenders now underwrite the investment-grade customer, not the GPU ([src](https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/guppy-level-death-investment-grade)). This matters directly for AI-compute bets.\n- **They doubt how diversified AI capital really is.** They say much of the apparent spread traces back to a few hyperscalers: \"You can't slice the same steak six different ways and call it diversification.\" ([src](https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/guppy-level-death-investment-grade))\n- **They are bullish on frontier-lab unit economics.** On Anthropic's S-1 (2026-10-26) they write that customers pay \"the way they pay for electricity.\" They call it possibly \"one of the most efficient scalings in the history of capitalism\" ([src](https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/anthropic-and-the-mother-of-all-s)).\n- **They expect the labs to take over the services layer.** On the OpenAI and Anthropic services JVs (2026-05-06): \"The next monetization frontier is operational.\" They argue whoever owns deployment gets \"a privileged feedback loop\", which squeezes systems integrators ([src](https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/openai-anthropic-and-the-war-of-the)). The implication: AI-native automation startups face lab-owned competition in deployment.\n- **Safety as a competitive axis (2025-12-22).** They predicted \"at least one meaningful model-misuse event\" in 2026. They also expect safety to become \"the primary competitive axis\" ([src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/our-predictions-for-security-in-2026)). Their Mythos piece (2026-04-08) follows that call: labs \"self-regulate under competitive pressure\" ([src](https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/the-mythos-of-anthropic)).\n- **Enterprise AI is a change-management problem.** \"Right now, 90% of enterprise AI is built on duct tape and vibes.\" ([src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/sexy-tech-boring-problems)). On the MIT finding that 95% of pilots fail, they say the market \"confused growing pains for terminal illness\" ([src](https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/the-95-problem-ai-isnt-overhyped)).\n- **Contracts and procurement:** \"One-year contracts only. No one's committing long-term.\" and \"Indemnity is king.\" ([src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/selling-to-skeptics-what-enterprise-buyers-want-today)). This is their read on how enterprise buyers are behaving, sourced from the CISO and CTO boards.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**medium.** The two references conflict, so here is the reasoning:\n- THESIS.md says \"mostly growth or late stage\" is **low**. Ojha's mandate is Series B+ security and infra, and they have no seed or Series A frontier-lab, chip or AI-native-automation lead.\n- The owner's 2026-11-10 request widens coverage to \"anybody who can write more than 10 million rounds or ... growth rounds\", including principals and fast risers. Ojha clears the check-size bar: the Cape $100M Series C was a BCV co-lead ([src](https://www.cape.co/blog/announcing-our-series-c)), and the Halcyon $40M Series B was BCV-led ([src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/halcyon-is-ai-powered-cybersecurity-company-dedicated-to-stopping-ransomware)).\n- They do **not** pass the \"quick promotion\" filter. They came to BCV as a partner, laterally from a Coatue partnership.\n- Why still medium: (1) they are the growth-side partner at a firm whose early team (Garg, Salem, Meyers, Stich) backs labs, so they are a likely growth follow-on buyer for frontier deals the owner seeds. (2) Their Substack is a free, weekly, primary-source read on AI compute credit, lab economics and hyperscaler capex.\n- Deals that matter to the owner: **poolside** (tag only), **Armadin** (AI-era offensive security, 2026), **Halcyon** (AI-driven ransomware defense), **Astronomer** (data and AI orchestration). Cape is their biggest check, but it isn't AI.\n\n## Background\n| When | What | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| ~4 yrs (dates not given) | Wharton (BCV timeline logo; degree not stated) | [BCV CMS pastExperience](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5Bslug.current%3D%3D%22saanya-ojha%22%5D%5B0%5D%7BpastExperience%7D) |\n| ~4 yrs | Hedge-fund investor, Goldman Sachs | [Substack about](https://saanyaojha.substack.com/about); [fund release](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/) |\n| ~4 yrs | Partner, Coatue (growth) | same |\n| Added between ~2022-02 and 2023-02-28 | Partner, BCV. Named among four partners \"added over the past year\" in the 2023-02-28 $1.9B fund release | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/) |\n| 2024-12 on | Co-chairs BCV's CTO advisory board (with Enrique Salem) and CISO advisory board | [src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/bain-capital-ventures-launches-cto-advisory-board); [src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions) |\n\nFrom India; has worked in New York, Hong Kong and Singapore ([src](https://saanyaojha.substack.com/about)). The exact year of each move is not confirmed: LinkedIn was not fetched, and the CMS gives durations only.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://baincapitalventures.com/team/saanya-ojha/ | BCV team page |\n| Substack / blog | https://saanyaojha.substack.com/ | \"The Change Constant\"; 8,000+ subscribers; several posts a week plus a Weekly Wrap Sheet (as of 2026-11-10) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/SaanyaOjha | not fetched |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/saanya-ojha-99167386/ | not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The web-search budget ran out before podcast and talk searches could be run.\n\n## How they think\n- **Enterprise adoption is gated by the organisation, not the model.** \"Models are advancing far more quickly than enterprise operating disciplines\" ([src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-hard-truth-about-enterprise-ai-adoption-and-how-leaders-get-it-right), 2026-02-17).\n- **Agents need reliability infrastructure.** They agree with Karpathy that 2025 was not the year of agents, and see the opportunity in the \"invisible layer\" that adds boundaries, context and monitoring ([src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions)).\n- **Multi-model world:** \"the one-model-to-rule-them-all era is over\", with Google and open source catching up (same source).\n- **Compute finance is fragile.** Stranded compute comes from price, not idle chips, and GPU depreciation outruns loan tenors ([src](https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/guppy-level-death-investment-grade)).\n- **Contrarian:** safety will become a market differentiator, not a compliance cost. Price discipline is an edge in a hot market.\n- **Calls to track:** the \"meaningful model-misuse event\" in 2026 (they later wrote up Claude Mythos's withheld release, which partly bears it out), and the neocloud shake-out.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Guppy-Level Death, Investment-Grade Debt\" (2026-10-28): AI compute as a credit market. https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/guppy-level-death-investment-grade\n2. \"Anthropic and the Mother of All S-1s\" (2026-10-26): frontier-lab unit economics. https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/anthropic-and-the-mother-of-all-s\n3. \"OpenAI, Anthropic, and the War of the JVs\" (2026-05-06): labs moving into deployment services. https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/openai-anthropic-and-the-war-of-the\n4. BCV \"VC Insights 2025\" Q&A: their pass discipline and 2026 agent view. https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions\n5. \"Selling to Skeptics\" (2025-05-07): enterprise procurement rules. https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/selling-to-skeptics-what-enterprise-buyers-want-today\n\n## Social engagement\nX was not fetched. On Substack the topics are big-tech earnings (Alphabet, Meta, Salesforce), frontier-lab economics (Anthropic, OpenAI), compute finance, and research explainers. For example, they covered the MIT CSAIL \"Sparks of Weight-Space Programming\" paper by Takehi, Teoh, Zhang and Khattab ([src](https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/sparks-of-weight-space-programming), 2026-11-04). Who amplifies them was not found.\n\n## Portfolio\n\"Partner tag\" means BCV's CMS `partner` field, which tracks coverage rather than origination (see Differential insight).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| poolside (AI coding / foundation-model lab) | Seed (CMS stage; $126M seed per Fortune) | 2023 | unknown | not found | [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22poolside%22%5D) partner tag with Rak Garg and Enrique Salem; not on Ojha's team page |\n| poolside | Series B, $500M at ~$3B | 2024-10-02 | BCV reported as lead (Fortune: \"in talks to lead\") | DST Global, eBay | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/09/12/ai-startup-poolside-3-billion-valuation-bain-capital-ventures/); [blog](https://poolside.ai/blog/announcing-our-500-million-fundraise-to-make-progress-towards-agi). Ojha not named |\n| Halcyon (AI anti-ransomware) | Series B, $40M | 2023-12-19 | yes (BCV) | not named | [BCV post](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/halcyon-is-ai-powered-cybersecurity-company-dedicated-to-stopping-ransomware); partner tag with Salem ([src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/portfolio/halcyon/)). Post names Shafic Mackie and Jeff Williams, not Ojha |\n| Astronomer (data and AI orchestration) | Series D, $93M | 2025-05-01 | yes (BCV) | Salesforce Ventures; Insight, Meritech, Venrock | [press release](https://www.astronomer.io/press-releases/astronomer-secures-93-million-series-d-funding/). Quote is from Salem; Ojha is tagged on the BCV post ([src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/from-airflow-to-dataops-astronomer-is-building-the-data-control-plane-for-the-enterprise)). BCV first in 2019 |\n| Armadin (\"ultimate cybersecurity attacker\") | Growth (CMS) | 2026 | unknown | not found | [BCV](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/portfolio/armadin/); partner tag with Salem |\n| Cape (privacy-first MVNO; not AI) | Series C, $100M at $900M | 2026-03-19 | co-lead (BCV and IVP, both new) | 01 Advisors, 137 Ventures, Definition, Fifth Down; prior A*, a16z, Costanoa | [Cape](https://www.cape.co/blog/announcing-our-series-c); [BCV](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/cape-getting-to-the-core-of-connectivity) |\n| HYCU (data protection) | Growth (CMS) | 2021 (predates Ojha) | unknown | not found | CMS partner tag with Salem |\n| Redis | Early (CMS) | 2012 (predates Ojha) | unknown | not found | CMS partner tag with Salem |\n| Swish (10-minute meals, India) | Early (CMS) | 2026 | unknown | not found | [BCV](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/swish-rewiring-the-home-kitchen-for-india-s-on-demand-future); with Ajay Agarwal |\n| Pronto (home cleaning, India) | Seed (CMS) | 2025 | unknown | not found | [BCV](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/portfolio/pronto/); with Ajay Agarwal |\n\n**Timing:** they were not first in on any AI deal found. BCV was already in Astronomer (2019) and poolside (2023 seed) before the growth rounds. Halcyon's Series B was BCV-led, but earlier investors were not checked. In Cape, BCV came in at the C after A*, a16z and Costanoa.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Cape:** BCV and IVP co-led the $100M Series C as new investors at a $900M valuation ([src](https://www.cape.co/blog/announcing-our-series-c)). BCV's post credits founder John Doyle's Palantir government-scaling background and Cape's rebuild of the telecom core ([src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/cape-getting-to-the-core-of-connectivity)). The intro path was not found.\n- **Halcyon:** BCV led the $40M Series B. A Bain PE extern helped on the deal, and BCV operating partner Jeff Williams became Halcyon's CRO, an operator-placement pattern ([src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/halcyon-is-ai-powered-cybersecurity-company-dedicated-to-stopping-ransomware)).\n- **Astronomer:** BCV re-upped with a $93M Series D lead in 2025 after first backing the company in 2019 ([src](https://www.astronomer.io/press-releases/astronomer-secures-93-million-series-d-funding/)). Salem is the public face; Ojha is co-tagged.\n- **Sourcing pattern (inferred, medium confidence):** nearly every deal is co-tagged with Enrique Salem, and the India consumer seeds with Ajay Agarwal. The CTO and CISO advisory boards they co-chair are a likely source of buyer diligence and deal flow, though no deal is explicitly attributed to them.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bain Capital Ventures | Partner, Growth team | by 2023-02-28 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/) |\n| Coatue | Former partner | before BCV | same |\n| Goldman Sachs | Former hedge-fund investor | before Coatue | [src](https://saanyaojha.substack.com/about) |\n| Enrique Salem | Co-partner on Halcyon, Astronomer, Armadin, Cape, HYCU, Redis, poolside; co-chair of the CTO board | 2023 | [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26references(*%5Bslug.current%3D%3D%22saanya-ojha%22%5D._id)%5D.title) |\n| Rak Garg | Co-tagged on poolside | 2023 | CMS |\n| Ajay Agarwal | Co-partner on Swish and Pronto (India) | 2025 | [src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/portfolio/pronto/) |\n| Matt Harris | Co-author of growth-associates hiring post | 2025-08 | [src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-aditya-modak-and-akhil-aggarwal-our-new-growth-associates) |\n| Akhil Aggarwal, Aditya Modak | Growth associates; Aggarwal covers infra and security (Ojha's lane) | 2025-08 | same |\n| Slater Stich | Co-tagged Growth-team member on the Astronomer post | 2025-05 | [src](https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/from-airflow-to-dataops-astronomer-is-building-the-data-control-plane-for-the-enterprise) |\n| John Doyle (Cape CEO) | Portfolio founder | 2026-03 | [src](https://www.cape.co/blog/announcing-our-series-c) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is Ojha named on poolside's 2024 $500M round, or on the reported 2025 Nvidia-led round? Do they hold a poolside board seat?\n- Armadin's round size, date and co-investors (armadin.com returned 403).\n- Board seats at Cape, Halcyon or Armadin.\n- Exact Coatue and Goldman years and the BCV start date (LinkedIn).\n- Did any Coatue-era deals carry AI exposure? Their Coatue portfolio was not found.\n- Podcasts and talks: none found because the search budget ran out.\n\n## Sources\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/team/saanya-ojha/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/\n- https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production (BCV CMS queries above)\n- https://www.baincapitalventures.com/portfolio/poolside/ ; /portfolio/halcyon/ ; /portfolio/armadin/ ; /portfolio/pronto/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/poolside-is-scaling-a-team-to-help-billions-code-and-realize-agi/\n- https://fortune.com/2024/09/12/ai-startup-poolside-3-billion-valuation-bain-capital-ventures/\n- https://poolside.ai/blog/announcing-our-500-million-fundraise-to-make-progress-towards-agi\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poolside_AI (secondary; cites the Bloomberg 2024-10-02 report)\n- https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/halcyon-is-ai-powered-cybersecurity-company-dedicated-to-stopping-ransomware\n- https://www.astronomer.io/press-releases/astronomer-secures-93-million-series-d-funding/\n- https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/from-airflow-to-dataops-astronomer-is-building-the-data-control-plane-for-the-enterprise\n- https://www.cape.co/blog/announcing-our-series-c ; https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/cape-getting-to-the-core-of-connectivity\n- https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/swish-rewiring-the-home-kitchen-for-india-s-on-demand-future\n- https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions\n- https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/our-predictions-for-security-in-2026\n- https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/sexy-tech-boring-problems\n- https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/selling-to-skeptics-what-enterprise-buyers-want-today\n- https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-hard-truth-about-enterprise-ai-adoption-and-how-leaders-get-it-right\n- https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-aditya-modak-and-akhil-aggarwal-our-new-growth-associates\n- https://www.baincapitalventures.com/insight/bain-capital-ventures-launches-cto-advisory-board\n- https://saanyaojha.substack.com/ ; /about ; /archive\n- https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/guppy-level-death-investment-grade\n- https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/anthropic-and-the-mother-of-all-s\n- https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/openai-anthropic-and-the-war-of-the\n- https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/the-mythos-of-anthropic\n- https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/the-95-problem-ai-isnt-overhyped\n- https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/why-sam-wants-your-ram\n- https://saanyaojha.substack.com/p/sparks-of-weight-space-programming\n", "person:saaya-pal": "\n# Saaya Pal\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. The WebSearch budget was exhausted before this task, so everything here comes from direct fetches: jumpcap.com (live and Wayback Machine copies), standardkernel.com, vcaonline.com and SEC EDGAR. Treat gaps as \"not searched\", not \"absent\". She published as **Saaya Nath** until about late 2024: the team-page URL moved from `saaya-nath` (2024-08) to `saaya-nath-pal` (2024-11) to `saaya-pal` (2025-01) [src](http://web.archive.org/cdx/search/cdx?url=jumpcap.com/team*&output=txt&filter=original:.*saaya.*). Her X handle is still @saayanath [src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/saaya-pal/).*\n\n## One-line take\nShe is the person behind Jump Capital's AI-infrastructure and AI-security money. She is the named Partner in the company's own release on the $20M Standard Kernel GPU-kernel seed that Jump led [src](https://standardkernel.com/blog/announcing-our-seed-round-is-kernel-generation-solved/), and she is a byline on every Jump post for a security or AI-infra deal since 2025 (Realm, Above, Standard Kernel, Pluto) [src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/saaya-pal/).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** she writes the thesis in public first and then leads the company that matches it. In March 2026 (going into RSA) and August 2026 (after Black Hat), Jump wrote that it wanted endpoint runtime security. In October it led Pluto's $33M A with the line \"Pluto is the company we were describing\" [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-pluto-security/). Founders who match a published Jump thesis have a direct way in.\n- **What she underwrites:** distribution and buyer clarity as much as technology. She told VCJ that distribution \"is becoming an increasingly important differentiator\" [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/when-everyone-can-build-who-wins-the-changing-rules-of-cybersecurity-investing/). She also argues that a point solution pitching \"what twenty other vendors are\" won't get a call back [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/ai-is-reshaping-cybersecurity-capital-is-following/).\n- **Constraint:** Jump Capital Fund 8 is $350M, with checks of \"$1 to $4 million and $8 to $15 million\" [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/jump-capital-fund-8-three-shifts-shaping-ai/). Yet she is named on leads of a $20M seed and a $33M A. So Jump's lead check is probably a slice of the rounds she fronts, and the rest of each round goes to co-investors. That is my inference from the two facts; no source gives Jump's dollar amount in either round.\n- **What she'd never do (inferred):** fund pre-training labs or chips. Her hunting list is the software layer on top of hardware: inference routing, edge inference, kernels, agent security [src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/saaya-pal/). Standard Kernel is pitched as \"a software problem\", not hardware [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **She rebuilt her coverage area; the archive shows it, the live site doesn't.** At her 2023 promotion her focus was the creator economy and future of work, and her flagship lead was Koji [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/congratulations-to-our-new-partners-saaya-nath-and-tarun-gupta/). The August 2024 archived page has her hunting \"AI Governance & Secure AI\", \"Data Observability\" and \"LLM Ops\" [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20240812133122/https://jumpcap.com/team-members/saaya-nath/). By 2026 the list is agent security, semantic DLP, inference routing, edge inference and cyber-physical security [src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/saaya-pal/). She is a recently converted AI-infra partner, not a career infra investor.\n- **Round \"stacking\" in cyber.** She says Above Security came out of stealth with $50M after about six months of revenue, which a few years ago \"might have looked more like a Series B story\" [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/ai-is-reshaping-cybersecurity-capital-is-following/). The trade-off as she puts it: \"The bigger rounds buy you the scope to actually build a platform\" [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/ai-is-reshaping-cybersecurity-capital-is-following/).\n- **First wave exited, second wave bought.** Jump backed LayerX and Prompt Security, the browser/prompt-layer AI-security companies; Akamai and SentinelOne bought them [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-pluto-security/). Both logos are on her team page [src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/saaya-pal/). Pluto is her bet that control moves from the browser to the endpoint. She is re-buying the same theme one layer down after the exits.\n- **Enforcement as the diligence signal.** \"Every Pluto customer runs enforcement.\" [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-pluto-security/). Most security tools stay in monitor mode, so she treats customers switching on blocking as proof of product trust.\n- **Her stated value-add (archive only, 2023).** On the Female VC Lab podcast she pitched \"the true value of having an operationally focused partner at the table\" [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20230320155826/https://jumpcap.com/insights/saaya-nath-appears-on-female-vc-lab-podcast).\n- **Standard Kernel's cap table puts her next to heavyweight technical angels.** The release names Jeff Dean, Jonathan Frankle, Michael Carbin, Sachin Katti, David M. Siegel and Walden Yan, plus CoreWeave and Ericsson Ventures [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026031110/standard-kernel-raises-20m-seed-round-to-let-ai-rewrite-the-software-that-runs-ai/). She is the only investor-side person quoted from the lead. General Catalyst published its own Standard Kernel investment post dated 2025-09-15 [src](https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-standard-kernel). So GC may have been in before Jump's March 2026 lead (unconfirmed which round GC joined).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** She is a real decision-maker: she is named as Partner on a lead, and she does $20M+ AI-infra seeds. Standard Kernel (AI writing GPU kernels from the KernelBench authors) is squarely in my lens. The rest of her book is AI security and AI-enabled SaaS rather than labs, chips or pre-training. Useful as (a) a co-lead partner for kernel, inference and compute-software seeds where Jump's $8–15M leaves room for a $20M check, and (b) a source of deal flow from Israeli security founders (Unit 8200 teams such as Pluto) [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-pluto-security/). Not a lab investor.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up around founders; her father \"had been a founder most of my life\" [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20240812133122/https://jumpcap.com/team-members/saaya-nath/).\n- Degree in Computer Engineering (school not stated), then \"various practices at BCG\" (title and dates not stated) [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20240812133122/https://jumpcap.com/team-members/saaya-nath/). She also mentions being a startup employee (company not named) [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20240812133122/https://jumpcap.com/team-members/saaya-nath/).\n- The Jump co-founders first met her in 2019 [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/congratulations-to-our-new-partners-saaya-nath-and-tarun-gupta/). Her joining title and date are unconfirmed.\n- 2021–2022: creator-economy thesis writer (\"The Creative Age\" series, 2021-08) [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/creator-economy-pt-2/); led Koji's $20M Series B (post 2022-01-27) [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/an-ecosystem-for-everyone-why-we-invested-in-koji/). Named in Insider's creator-economy leaders list (per Jump; the Insider link was not fetched) [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/congratulations-to-our-new-partners-saaya-nath-and-tarun-gupta/).\n- **2023-01-03: promoted to Partner**, alongside Tarun Gupta [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/congratulations-to-our-new-partners-saaya-nath-and-tarun-gupta/).\n- 2024–2026: moved into AI governance/security, then AI infrastructure (see Differential insight).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://jumpcap.com/team-members/saaya-pal/ | Team page with \"Currently Hunting\" list; checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://jumpcap.com/insights/ | Writes on the Jump blog: deal posts, RSA and Black Hat recaps, press recaps |\n| X | https://twitter.com/saayanath | Linked from the team page; not fetched; follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/saaya-pal-12ba82a3/ | Linked from the team page; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Jump runs \"The Jump Off Point\" podcast; her hosting role is not confirmed [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/the-jump-off-point/id1554150085) |\n\n**How to reach her:** the team page has an email button. The Ternary post ends \"reach out to me\" [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/battling-cloudflation-with-innovation-why-we-invested-in-ternary/). The practical route for a founder is to pitch against her published \"Currently Hunting\" list or a Jump RSA or Black Hat recap. For a co-investor, she works deals with Aqil Pasha (VP) and Jason Felger (Partner).\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-02 — Female VC Lab (Barbara Bickham, Trailyn VC) — her path to Jump and future-of-work focus [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20230320155826/https://jumpcap.com/insights/saaya-nath-appears-on-female-vc-lab-podcast)\n- 2026-08 — S&P Global Market Intelligence interview (Eleennae Ayson) on cyber capital concentration [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/ai-is-reshaping-cybersecurity-capital-is-following/)\n- 2026-11 — Venture Capital Journal Nov/Dec cover story (David Bogoslaw) on AI agents and security investing [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/when-everyone-can-build-who-wins-the-changing-rules-of-cybersecurity-investing/)\n\n## How they think\n- Every security layer gets rebuilt \"for a user that doesn't behave like a user\" (agents) [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/ai-is-reshaping-cybersecurity-capital-is-following/).\n- Identity moves from \"who has access\" to what an actor does with it; the control point is runtime [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/ai-is-reshaping-cybersecurity-capital-is-following/).\n- AI infra: recapture stranded GPU capital through software rather than adding hardware [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/).\n- Timing: on post-quantum, compliance pulls regulated buyers first and broad demand comes later [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/when-everyone-can-build-who-wins-the-changing-rules-of-cybersecurity-investing/).\n- Calls: LayerX and Prompt exited (to Akamai and SentinelOne); Workera was acquired by Pearson 2026-09-29 per jumpcap.com/news (from an existing repo row; not re-fetched).\n\n## Read these first\n- Why We Invested in Pluto Security (2026-10-14) — her clearest \"thesis first, then lead\" post [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-pluto-security/)\n- Why we invested in Standard Kernel (2026-03-20, with Aqil Pasha) — her compute-layer view [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/)\n- AI Is Reshaping Cybersecurity. Capital Is Following. (2026-08-20) — round stacking and platform pressure [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/ai-is-reshaping-cybersecurity-capital-is-following/)\n- When Everyone Can Build, Who Wins? (2026-11-08) — distribution as the moat [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/when-everyone-can-build-who-wins-the-changing-rules-of-cybersecurity-investing/)\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked (X and LinkedIn were not fetched).\n\n## Portfolio\n\"Led\" is as the Jump post states it. Logo-only means the company appears only as a logo on her team page, with no round or role sourced.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Pluto Security | Series A ($33M) | 2026-10-14 (post) | yes (Jump) — co-byline Jason Felger | not stated | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-pluto-security/) |\n| Above Security | Undisclosed (emerged with $50M stacked) | 2026-03-31 (post) | not stated — co-byline Aqil Pasha | not stated | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-above-security/) |\n| Standard Kernel | Seed ($20M) | 2026-03-11 | yes (Jump); she is the quoted Partner | General Catalyst; Felicis; Cowboy; Link; Essence VC; CoreWeave Ventures; Ericsson Ventures; angels | [src](https://standardkernel.com/blog/announcing-our-seed-round-is-kernel-generation-solved/) |\n| Realm Security | Seed (post refers to \"the seed stage\") | 2025-10-08 (post) | not stated — co-byline Aqil Pasha | not stated | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-realm-security-fixing-the-broken-security-data-layer/) |\n| AskElephant | Seed ($6M) | 2025-05-19 (post) | yes (sole byline) | High Alpha; Tandem; SaaS Ventures | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-askelephant-the-operating-system-for-modern-gtm-teams/) |\n| TrueFoundry | Series A (round from Intel Capital post) | 2025-02 | no; logo-only for her | Intel Capital (lead); Peak XV; Eniac | [src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/saaya-pal/) |\n| LayerX | Undisclosed | not found | logo-only; acquired by Akamai | | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-pluto-security/) |\n| Prompt Security | Undisclosed | not found | logo-only; acquired by SentinelOne | | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-pluto-security/) |\n| Ternary | Series A ($12M) | 2023-10-25 (post) | yes (sole byline) | not stated | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/battling-cloudflation-with-innovation-why-we-invested-in-ternary/) |\n| Workera | Series B ($23.5M) | 2023-03-21 (post) | yes (co-byline Mike McMahon) | not stated | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/making-skills-intelligence-more-intelligent-why-we-invested-in-workera/) |\n| Koji | Series B ($20M) | 2022-01-27 (post) | yes | not stated | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/an-ecosystem-for-everyone-why-we-invested-in-koji/) |\n| New Era ADR | Undisclosed | not found | yes (per promotion post) | | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/congratulations-to-our-new-partners-saaya-nath-and-tarun-gupta/) |\n| Obsess, Osano | Undisclosed | before 2023 | \"integral roles\" | | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/congratulations-to-our-new-partners-saaya-nath-and-tarun-gupta/) |\n| Mobly | Undisclosed | 2025-01-23 (post) | not stated (sole byline) | | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-unlocking-the-full-potential-of-in-person-events/) |\n| MindStudio, Symbl.ai, Deeto, Snoots | Undisclosed | not found | logo-only | | [src](https://jumpcap.com/team-members/saaya-pal/) |\n\nThe promotion post also credits her on Recurate and Indico (pre-2023, \"integral roles\") [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/congratulations-to-our-new-partners-saaya-nath-and-tarun-gupta/).\n\n**First in or re-up?** For Standard Kernel, General Catalyst's 2025-09-15 post suggests GC was in before Jump's March 2026 lead (unconfirmed). Pluto's A is Jump's first round in the company as far as the post shows. No re-up by her was found in any AI deal.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Pluto Security:** Jump wrote the thesis publicly in March (pre-RSA) and August (post-Black Hat) 2026, then led the A in October. On CEO Shahar Bahat: \"What stood out from our first call was how precisely she sees the problem\" [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-pluto-security/).\n- **Above Security:** CISO conversations ranked insider risk a \"top-two concern\". Jump says it saw Aviv Nahum's clarity \"from our first conversation\" [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-above-security/).\n- **AskElephant:** first conviction came from a demo of a meeting-prep workflow. She cites 100% quarter-over-quarter growth [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-askelephant-the-operating-system-for-modern-gtm-teams/).\n- Intro paths: not found for any deal.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jump Capital | Partner (promoted 2023-01-03) | met founders 2019 | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/congratulations-to-our-new-partners-saaya-nath-and-tarun-gupta/) |\n| BCG | former employee (practices, title unknown) | dates unknown | [src](http://web.archive.org/web/20240812133122/https://jumpcap.com/team-members/saaya-nath/) |\n| Aqil Pasha | co-byline on Realm, Above, Standard Kernel | 2025 | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/) |\n| Jason Felger | co-byline on Pluto; RSA and Black Hat recaps | 2026 | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-pluto-security/) |\n| Mike McMahon | co-byline on Workera; co-signed her promotion | 2019 | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/making-skills-intelligence-more-intelligent-why-we-invested-in-workera/) |\n| Anne Ouyang, Chris Rinard | Standard Kernel founders | 2026 | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/) |\n| Shahar Bahat | Pluto Security CEO | 2026 | [src](https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-pluto-security/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Board or observer seats: no post, release or filing read names one for her. Standard Kernel, Pluto and Above board composition was not found.\n- Who approves at Jump Capital: the JCDP-8 Form D lists Chitnis, McMahon and Jump Trading co-founders DiSomma and Gurinas (plus Matthew Hinerfeld) as executive officers, not her [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2142408/000090514826003233/primary_doc.xml). Whether Jump Trading principals sit on the investment committee is unconfirmed.\n- Jump's actual dollar amount in the Standard Kernel and Pluto rounds.\n- Her joining title and date (2019?), and the school behind her Computer Engineering degree.\n\n## Sources\n- https://jumpcap.com/team-members/saaya-pal/\n- http://web.archive.org/web/20240812133122/https://jumpcap.com/team-members/saaya-nath/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/congratulations-to-our-new-partners-saaya-nath-and-tarun-gupta/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel/\n- https://standardkernel.com/blog/announcing-our-seed-round-is-kernel-generation-solved/\n- https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026031110/standard-kernel-raises-20m-seed-round-to-let-ai-rewrite-the-software-that-runs-ai/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-pluto-security/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-above-security/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-realm-security-fixing-the-broken-security-data-layer/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-askelephant-the-operating-system-for-modern-gtm-teams/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/battling-cloudflation-with-innovation-why-we-invested-in-ternary/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/making-skills-intelligence-more-intelligent-why-we-invested-in-workera/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/ai-is-reshaping-cybersecurity-capital-is-following/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/when-everyone-can-build-who-wins-the-changing-rules-of-cybersecurity-investing/\n- https://jumpcap.com/insights/jump-capital-fund-8-three-shifts-shaping-ai/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2142408/000090514826003233/primary_doc.xml\n- http://web.archive.org/web/20230320155826/https://jumpcap.com/insights/saaya-nath-appears-on-female-vc-lab-podcast\n- https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-standard-kernel\n", "person:sabrina-hahn": "\n# Sabrina Hahn\n\n## One-line take\nShe is the clearest example in the repo of a small, non-institutional manager who gets allocations in the hottest neolab and AI-infra rounds: Flapping Airplanes, Mirendil, Periodic, fal and Together. Sequoia and Insight are LPs in her fund [src](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** access, not price-setting. She sits in rounds others lead and price. Her Midas Seed record (#23, headline deal fal) was built through SH Fund, a solo fund she has run since 2022 [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/), [src](https://www.foundevo.com/top-seed-stage-investors/) (aggregator). Her LPs include Sequoia, which co-led Flapping's seed and led Sable's A, both deals she is in [src](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/), [src](https://www.finanznachrichten.de/nachrichten-2026-07/69050088-sable-raises-dollar-45m-to-build-the-first-ai-employee-that-can-click-see-and-explain-200.htm).\n- **Constraint:** the fund is small and newly institutionalized. Both 2026 vehicles report $0 sold and an indefinite offering at filing [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2140744/000090514826003382/primary_doc.xml). In every deal found she is a participant, never a lead.\n- **What she'd never do (inference):** lead or price a large round. Nothing in any source shows her doing so.\n- **Structure:** in 2026 she turned the solo fund into a sibling partnership. Her brother Evan, an ex-Insight MD, now signs the fund paperwork (see Differential insight).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Absolute is her solo fund continued, with the same phone number.** SH Fund's 2022 Form D and both 2026 AP vehicle Form Ds give one contact number, \"(917) 300-8234\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2140744/000090514826003382/primary_doc.xml), [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1912867/000092542122000004/primary_doc.xml). The address moved from a PO box in Alpine, NJ to 2 East 28th St, New York.\n- **Evan signs now.** Sabrina signed SH Fund's 2022 filing herself. Both 2026 filings are signed by \"Evan Hahn Manager of the General Partner of the Issuer\", with both siblings listed as managers [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149542/000090514826003539/primary_doc.xml). My inference: Evan, with his Insight background, runs the institutional side and Sabrina runs access.\n- **Two vehicles, only one discloses a fee.** AP Capital I says the GP is \"entitled to a management fee payable by the Issuer\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2140744/000090514826003382/primary_doc.xml). AP Align I discloses no fee [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149542/000090514826003539/primary_doc.xml). Inference: Align is a no-fee or co-invest sidecar, the kind of vehicle an LP like Sequoia or Insight might use for direct exposure. Unconfirmed.\n- **She is almost never named by the companies.** I read the funding posts for Serval C, Vals A, Exa (launch, A, B, C), Cartesia seed and A, fal E/F, Nudge A and Mirendil's $200M round. None names her. The only release naming her is Sable's, as \"Sabrina and Evan Hahn\", for a company \"founded by a group of Harvard graduates\" [src](https://www.finanznachrichten.de/nachrichten-2026-07/69050088-sable-raises-dollar-45m-to-build-the-first-ai-employee-that-can-click-see-and-explain-200.htm). She is a Harvard graduate [src](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/). Inference: Harvard is one sourcing channel.\n- **Flapping timing.** Forbes listed her holding Flapping, \"valued at $1.5 billion after a 2026 seed round\", on 2026-05-27, about five months before the 2026-10-21 Series A [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/). So the A was a follow-on. Whether she entered in the seed itself, at the seed price, is **not confirmed**. Forbes gives the company's valuation, not her entry.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Her named book overlaps the THESIS almost exactly:\n- Neolabs: Flapping Airplanes, Mirendil, Periodic\n- Compute and inference: Together, fal\n- AI-native automation: Serval and Sable\n\nShe is useful to me in three ways:\n1. As a co-investor signal in hot neolab rounds.\n2. As a model for how a small fund with big-GP LPs (Sequoia, Insight) gets allocations in $200M–$1B rounds it doesn't lead.\n3. As a possible tail co-investor in large seeds where I write ~$20M.\n\nShe is not a partner for sharing lead risk. Deals that matter to me: Flapping (seed holding plus Series A), Mirendil, Periodic, Serval, Sable.\n\n## Background\n- Horace Mann School. Harvard B.A. in Art History, minor in Economics, Ernst Kitzinger Prize [src](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/). Graduation year not found.\n- Director at Pace Gallery, the \"youngest Director in the gallery's history\" [src](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/). Dates not found.\n- Forbes 30 Under 30 Art & Style alum (year not stated) [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/). Age 34 per Forbes as of 2026-05.\n- Author of the children's series ABCs of Art, 123s of Art and others. ABCs of Art was a Vanity Fair best book of 2019 [src](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/), [src](https://www.vanityfair.com/style/2019/12/the-best-books-of-2019-editors-picks).\n- Angel investor in tech (dates not found), then SH Fund, LP in 2022 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1912867/000092542122000003/primary_doc.xml), then Absolute Partners in 2026 with Evan Hahn [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sabrinahahn.com/ | Bio, portfolio logo list, books (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | https://x.com/sabrinahahn | \"Venture Investor \\| Midas Seed List\"; 276 followers; joined 2009 (fxtwitter API, 2026-11-10) |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | no link on her site |\n| GitHub | none found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n| Forbes profile | https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/ | updated 2026-05-27 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The web-search budget was exhausted this session, so I could not run a search for interviews. Her site links no talks.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses (from the portfolio, not from her own words):** neolabs, inference and compute, AI-native automation, plus a few consumer and non-AI bets (Underdog, Nudge) [src](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/).\n- **Contrarian views:** none on record. No essays, posts or interviews found.\n- **Calls right or wrong:**\n  - fal and Together through SH Fund, which Forbes credits for the Midas listing [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/).\n  - Gamma, PostHog and Hightouch are now valued above $1B [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/).\n  - An aggregator describes SH Fund as a Web3 fund (see firms/absolute-ventures.md). If that's right, her 2022 thesis drifted to AI.\n\n## Read these first\n- Her site's About and Investments sections: the only self-description [src](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/).\n- Forbes profile: the deals with valuations and the SH Fund-to-Absolute story [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/).\n- The AP Capital I Form D, for the structure and Evan's role [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2140744/000090514826003382/primary_doc.xml).\n- Sable's Series A release, the one deal naming her [src](https://www.finanznachrichten.de/nachrichten-2026-07/69050088-sable-raises-dollar-45m-to-build-the-first-ai-employee-that-can-click-see-and-explain-200.htm).\n\n## Social engagement\nShe posts little; the account has 276 followers. Her bio flags the Midas listing. An aggregator lists crypto-heavy followers (Chris Dixon, Fred Ehrsam, Katie Haun, Arianna Simpson, Alexis Ohanian) [src](https://twitterscore.io/twitter/sabrinahahn/) (aggregator). I did not read her timeline.\n\n## Portfolio\nDeal count: her site names 15 companies \"and others\". Forbes adds Doppel, PostHog and Hightouch. Sable, QuiverAI and Nozomio come from other sources. That's about 20 names in total, which doesn't reconcile with the \"17 deals\" count in the task context. Rows are in `data/rows/sabrina-hahn-deals.csv`.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Flapping Airplanes | Held before the A (entry round unconfirmed; company's seed was $180M, Jan 2026) | by 2026-05-27 | no | Index, GV, Sequoia (seed co-leads) | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Series A ($1B), follow-on, listed as \"Absolute Ventures\" (medium confidence) | 2026-10-21 | no | Index, Lux (co-leads), Sequoia, GV, Kleiner, CRV, NVIDIA | [X](https://x.com/flappyairplanes/status/2112958916871225736) |\n| Sable | Series A ($45M), with Evan Hahn | 2026-07-16 | no | Sequoia, 8VC (co-leads), BoxGroup, SV Angel, Valor Atreides | [release](https://www.finanznachrichten.de/nachrichten-2026-07/69050088-sable-raises-dollar-45m-to-build-the-first-ai-employee-that-can-click-see-and-explain-200.htm) |\n| fal | Undisclosed (SH Fund; Midas headline deal) | n/a | unknown | | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/) |\n| Together AI | Undisclosed (SH Fund) | n/a | unknown | | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/) |\n| Periodic Labs | Undisclosed | n/a | unknown | | [site](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/) |\n| Mirendil | Undisclosed (not named in the $200M round, 2026-06-26) | n/a | unknown | | [site](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/) |\n| Serval | Undisclosed (not named in the Series C post) | n/a | unknown | | [site](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/) |\n| Cartesia, Sesame, Exa, Engram, Vals AI, Gamma, Nudge, Underdog | Undisclosed | n/a | unknown | | [site](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/) |\n| Doppel, PostHog, Hightouch | Undisclosed | n/a | unknown | | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/) |\n| QuiverAI | Seed ($8.3M), aggregator only | 2026-02-25 | no | a16z (lead) | [CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/company/quiverai/financials) |\n| Nozomio | Seed, from an earlier search snippet; page not read (HTTP 429) | 2025 | no | CRV, BoxGroup, LocalGlobe | [blog](https://trynia.ai/blog/announcing-nozomio-seed-round) |\n\n**First in?** No evidence that she was first money anywhere. **Doubled down?** Only on Flapping: a holding by May 2026, then the October Series A.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Flapping Airplanes:**\n  - Seed: $180M in Jan 2026, co-led by Index, GV and Sequoia.\n  - Forbes shows her holding it by 2026-05-27, then she joins the $1B Series A on 2026-10-21 under the name \"Absolute Ventures\" [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/), [src](https://x.com/flappyairplanes/status/2112958916871225736).\n  - Intro path: not found. Candidates are Sequoia (an LP in her fund and a seed co-lead) and Harvard (co-founder Asher Spector is a Harvard alum). Both unconfirmed.\n- **Sable:** both siblings invested personally or through the fund in the $45M A, led by Shaun Maguire for Sequoia and Joe Lonsdale for 8VC [src](https://www.finanznachrichten.de/nachrichten-2026-07/69050088-sable-raises-dollar-45m-to-build-the-first-ai-employee-that-can-click-see-and-explain-200.htm). Sable's founders are Harvard graduates. Intro path: not found.\n- Other deals: no deal story found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Absolute Partners (absolute-ventures) | at: Founder and Managing Partner | 2026 | [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/) |\n| SH Fund, LP (sh-fund) | at: Managing Director of the GP (sole) | 2022-02 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1912867/000092542122000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Pace Gallery | at: Director (former) | dates not found | [site](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/) |\n| Evan Hahn | Brother; co-founder; signs the AP Form Ds | 2026 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2140744/000090514826003382/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Sequoia Capital | LP in her fund (vehicle not named) | n/a | [site](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/) |\n| Insight Partners | LP in her fund (vehicle not named); Evan is a former MD there | n/a | [site](https://www.sabrinahahn.com/), [Insight](https://www.insightpartners.com/team/evan-hahn/) |\n| Shaun Maguire, Joe Lonsdale | Co-investors on Sable (they led) | 2026-07 | [release](https://www.finanznachrichten.de/nachrichten-2026-07/69050088-sable-raises-dollar-45m-to-build-the-first-ai-employee-that-can-click-see-and-explain-200.htm) |\n\n**LP status:** she is not known to be an LP in anyone else's fund. Sequoia and Insight are LPs in hers; those rows already exist in `data/lp_commitments.csv` under their own slugs, so I wrote no `-lp.csv`.\n\n## Open questions\n- What is AP Align I? A no-fee sidecar or co-invest vehicle, and for which LPs?\n- Which fund do Sequoia and Insight back: SH Fund (2022) or AP Capital I (2026)?\n- Her entry round and price in Flapping, Mirendil and Periodic. Did she get in at or near the seed price?\n- What is SH Fund's size? Was it really a Web3 fund (aggregator claim)?\n- How did she get into the fal and Together rounds?\n- Pace Gallery tenure dates, and the start of her angel investing.\n- Any podcast or interview. None found; search was budget-limited.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sabrinahahn.com/ (checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/ (updated 2026-05-27)\n- https://www.foundevo.com/top-seed-stage-investors/ (aggregator)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1912867/000092542122000003/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1912867/000092542122000004/primary_doc.xml (SH Fund Form D and D/A, 2022-02)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2140744/000090514826003382/primary_doc.xml (AP Capital I, 2026-07-29)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149542/000090514826003539/primary_doc.xml (AP Align I, 2026-08-10)\n- https://x.com/flappyairplanes/status/2112958916871225736 (2026-10-21)\n- https://www.finanznachrichten.de/nachrichten-2026-07/69050088-sable-raises-dollar-45m-to-build-the-first-ai-employee-that-can-click-see-and-explain-200.htm (2026-07-16)\n- https://techfundingnews.com/ex-anthropic-researchers-raise-200m-just-weeks-after-quitting-to-build-ai-that-creates-better-ai/ (Mirendil)\n- https://www.serval.com/blog/we-raised-150m-to-make-everyone-a-builder ; https://www.vals.ai/blogs/series-a ; https://cartesia.ai/blog/seed ; https://nudge.com/blog/series-a ; https://exa.ai/blog/announcing-series-c ; https://fal.ai/blog/series-e-f (checked; she is not named)\n- https://www.insightpartners.com/team/evan-hahn/\n- https://www.cbinsights.com/company/quiverai/financials (aggregator)\n- https://twitterscore.io/twitter/sabrinahahn/ (aggregator)\n", "person:sahir-azam": "\n# Sahir Azam\n\n## One-line take\nA former MongoDB CPO who joined Index in September 2025 and now runs the firm's inference and real-time infrastructure growth deals (Fireworks C and D, LiveKit C). They sit close to the compute layer, but they come in at Series C at a $1B+ valuation, not at the lab or chip seed.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** operator credibility with infrastructure founders. They ran product at MongoDB, where the post says they drove Atlas [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/welcoming-sahir-azam-to-index/). They bring relationships formed as a buyer and partner of AI-infra companies into Index. Their marquee deal, Fireworks, began as a MongoDB Ventures strategic check in the 2024 Series B. They then re-upped as an Index co-lead in the 2025 C ($4B) and again in the 2026 D ($17.5B) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/) [src](https://fireworks.ai/news/fireworks-raises-1-5-billion-series-d).\n- **Constraint:** they are new to venture (about 14 months in) and say they want to grow into a generalist [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/sahir-azam/). Their bylined leads are growth rounds. Their two early-stage names (the Conduct A and the Ando seed) are co-signed: Conduct with Bastian Hasslinger, and Ando was bylined by Sofia Dolfe [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/).\n- **Would never (inferred, low confidence):** take model or research risk. Their stated thesis is that value moves away from foundation models toward the infrastructure layer, so a pre-training lab seed runs against their own framing [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Fireworks was a relationship carried from a corporate fund to Index.** They met Lin Qiao at MongoDB: \"I first met Lin two years ago when I was at MongoDB.\" They then backed the company through MongoDB's fund: \"I was fortunate to invest in Fireworks through the MongoDB Venture Fund.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/). MongoDB Ventures is listed in the $52M Series B led by Sequoia (2024-07-11) [src](https://fireworks.ai/blog/fireworks-series-b-funding-sequoia). So Index's C co-lead, made about seven weeks after they joined, was sourced through their own MongoDB network.\n- **Thesis: model commoditization pushes value to infrastructure.** \"As foundation models commoditize, value will increasingly accrue to the infra layer\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/). Their Fireworks framing: \"We believe inference is the new runtime\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/).\n- **LiveKit came through Index's network, not a lab.** \"Last year, through friends of Index, I got connected with LiveKit founders\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/). They admit the first read undersold it: \"I didn't fully grasp the scope of their vision\" until a sit-down.\n- **Ando: second-opinion role on a seed.** Dolfe's post: \"my partner Sahir met her separately and came away just as impressed\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/). Index co-led the seed with Emergence, after Accel led the pre-seed [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-ando-modern-messaging-for-teams-and-agents).\n- **Temporal is inherited, not originated.** Index's Temporal post dates to 2022-02-16 and has no byline, about 3.5 years before Azam joined, but Azam is now the only Index person on the company page [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/everything-is-a-workflow-our-investment-in-temporal/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/temporal/). They hosted a talk with Temporal CEO Maxim Fateev (\"Finding Stability Among Uncertainty\") [src](https://youtu.be/4q8xTqALe9I).\n- **Mentor link:** MongoDB CEO Dev Ittycheria is quoted in the welcome post praising their \"deep domain knowledge across data, AI, and security\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/welcoming-sahir-azam-to-index/). Ittycheria is a likely referral source.\n- No podcast transcript, Substack or X posts were retrieved (the X profile returned a paywall error). All insight above comes from firm-site posts.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium**, at the boundary with low. Most of their bylined AI money is growth (THESIS: low), but they co-led an AI-native automation Series A (Conduct) and a seed (Ando) in the last 15 months, and they are connected to high-fit names (THESIS: medium). Azam's bylined AI book is Series C or later at $1B+ valuations: Fireworks C at $4B and D at $17.5B, LiveKit C at $1B. They have no lab, pre-training or chip deal. Where they matter:\n- **Fireworks** (inference compute layer). They are the Index person on the board-level relationship. Useful for diligence on inference demand (ARR went from $280M to over $1B between the C and the D [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/candid-with-index-in-conversation-with-fireworks-lin-qiao/)) and as a possible later-stage buyer of positions in compute companies.\n- **Conduct** (AI-native enterprise automation, Series A) is the closest to a Serval-type deal, but Index came in after Creandum, Lucid and Booom (per the Hasslinger profile).\n- Their thesis that value flows away from foundation models is the opposite of a new-lab bet. Don't expect them to co-lead a $600M lab seed.\n\n## Background\n- Go-to-market and product roles at BladeLogic/BMC, VMware and Sumo Logic. Dates not found [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/sahir-azam/).\n- MongoDB for \"nearly a decade\" (per the bio summary), ending as Chief Product Officer. The welcome post credits them with driving Atlas [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/sahir-azam/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/welcoming-sahir-azam-to-index/).\n- Invested via the MongoDB Venture Fund, including in the Fireworks Series B in 2024 [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/).\n- Joined Index as a Partner in New York, announced 2025-09-11 in a post by Shardul Shah [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/welcoming-sahir-azam-to-index/).\n- Education: nothing found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/sahir-azam/ | Index bio; email s.azam@indexventures.com is listed |\n| Substack / blog | Index Perspectives (see Read these first) | About 4 bylined posts, 2025-10 to 2026-07 |\n| X | https://x.com/sahirazam (unverified; fetch returned 402) | follower count not retrieved |\n| LinkedIn | https://linkedin.com/in/sahirazam | linked from the Index bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Hosts \"Candid with Index\" style interviews (Lin Qiao video, 2026-07) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-07 — Index \"Candid with Index\" — conversation with Fireworks CEO Lin Qiao (video only, no transcript) — https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/candid-with-index-in-conversation-with-fireworks-lin-qiao/\n- Date not found — \"Finding Stability Among Uncertainty | Maxim Fateev (Temporal) with Sahir Azam (Index Ventures)\" — https://youtu.be/4q8xTqALe9I — no transcript retrieved\n- 2025-09 (per the page title) — The Information TV, \"Salesforce & Microsoft's AI Sales Challenges, AI's Impact on Product & Sales Teams\" — https://www.youtube.com/live/qa8cefO6g2I?t=1416 — linked from the Index bio; content not retrieved\n\n## How they think\n- Inference is becoming its own runtime layer. Teams want control over models, not just API calls, and most don't want to become \"model ops experts\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/).\n- Foundation models commoditize and value moves to infrastructure. Voice agents in call centers are the first big real-time workload [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/).\n- Enterprise-specific business logic (SAP, Oracle, Salesforce customizations) is a moat that agents need. This is the opposite of horizontal enterprise AI [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/).\n- Right so far: Fireworks went from $4B (C) to $17.5B (D) in about nine months. Too early to judge the others.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Inference is the new runtime: our investment in Fireworks\" (2025-10-28). The origin story and the inference thesis. https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/\n2. \"See, Hear, Speak: Investing in LiveKit\" (2026-01-22). The infrastructure-over-models thesis and the intro path. https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/\n3. \"Conduct: a world where corporates move at startup speed\" (2026-06-17, with Hasslinger). Their only early-stage byline. https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/\n4. \"Welcoming Sahir Azam to Index\" (2025-09-11). Shah's and Ittycheria's framing. https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/welcoming-sahir-azam-to-index/\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: the X profile could not be fetched (HTTP 402), and no posts were retrieved.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Fireworks AI | Series B $52M (via MongoDB Ventures, before Index) | 2024-07-11 | no (Sequoia led) | Sequoia; NVIDIA; AMD | [src](https://fireworks.ai/blog/fireworks-series-b-funding-sequoia) |\n| Fireworks AI | Series C $250M at $4B | 2025-10-28 | co-lead (Index); Azam byline. Shardul Shah and Mark Xu tagged | Lightspeed and Evantic (co-leads); Sequoia | [src](https://fireworks.ai/news/fireworks-ai-raises-250m-series-c) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/) |\n| Fireworks AI | Series D $1.505B at $17.5B | 2026-07-16 | co-lead (Index); Azam interviewer on the post | Atreides; TCV (co-leads); Lightspeed; Evantic; NVIDIA and others | [src](https://fireworks.ai/news/fireworks-raises-1-5-billion-series-d) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/candid-with-index-in-conversation-with-fireworks-lin-qiao/) |\n| LiveKit | Series C $100M at $1B | 2026-01-22 | yes (Index lead); Azam byline and board seat. Eryk Dobrushkin and Sofia Dolfe tagged | Salesforce Ventures; Hanabi Capital; Altimeter; Redpoint | [src](https://livekit.com/blog/livekit-series-c/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/) |\n| Conduct | Series A $60M | 2026-06-17 | co-lead with ICONIQ; Azam and Bastian Hasslinger byline. Emil Schaefer and Hannah Seal tagged | ICONIQ; (SAP, Creandum per other profiles) | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/) |\n| Ando | Seed (amount undisclosed) | 2026-09-24 | co-lead (Index with Emergence); Sofia Dolfe byline, Azam tagged and met the founder separately | Emergence; Accel (pre-seed lead) | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/) [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-ando-modern-messaging-for-teams-and-agents) |\n| Temporal | Index position from 2022 (inherited; round not stated in the Index post) | 2022-02-16 (Index post) | not attributable to Azam | not found | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/temporal/) |\n\n**Timing:** Azam was not first on any of these. On Fireworks, Benchmark and Sequoia preceded them, and their own MongoDB check came in the B. LiveKit had Redpoint and Altimeter. Conduct had Creandum and others. Ando had Accel at pre-seed.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Fireworks:** they met Lin Qiao at MongoDB while looking for AI thought leaders, and invested via the MongoDB Venture Fund (the Series B, 2024-07). After joining Index (2025-09), they co-led the Series C seven weeks later, then the D (2026-07). Over that span ARR grew from $280M to over $1B [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/candid-with-index-in-conversation-with-fireworks-lin-qiao/).\n- **LiveKit:** introduced in 2025 \"through friends of Index\" to Russ d'Sa and David Zhao. They led the C and took a board seat [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/).\n- **Ando:** Sofia Dolfe led the relationship with founder Sara Du. Azam met her separately as a second-partner check [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/).\n- **Conduct:** how Index met the founders is not disclosed. Azam is the senior co-signer with Hasslinger [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lin Qiao (Fireworks) | Met at MongoDB; investor via MongoDB Ventures, then Index | ~2023 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/) |\n| Dev Ittycheria (MongoDB CEO) | Former boss and mentor; endorsed the Index hire | before 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/welcoming-sahir-azam-to-index/) |\n| Shardul Shah (Index) | Wrote the welcome post; tagged on Fireworks | 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/welcoming-sahir-azam-to-index/) |\n| Mark Xu (Index) | Tagged on Fireworks C | 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/) |\n| Eryk Dobrushkin (Index) | Junior tag on LiveKit C | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/livekit/) |\n| Sofia Dolfe (Index) | Tagged on LiveKit; Azam tagged on her Ando seed | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/) |\n| Bastian Hasslinger (Index) | Co-author on Conduct A | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/) |\n| Russ d'Sa and David Zhao (LiveKit) | Board member | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/) |\n| Maxim Fateev (Temporal) | Hosted a talk with them; Index company contact | date not found | [src](https://youtu.be/4q8xTqALe9I) |\n| Atreides, TCV, Lightspeed, Evantic, ICONIQ, Emergence | Co-lead firms | 2025–2026 | see Portfolio |\n\n## Open questions\n- Does Azam hold the Fireworks board seat for Index? Not confirmed.\n- Exact Temporal round and whether Azam took over a board or observer role from an earlier Index partner.\n- Their personal MongoDB Ventures portfolio beyond Fireworks (other AI-infra strategic checks may have followed them to Index).\n- Transcripts of The Information TV and the Fateev video, for verbatim views.\n- X activity and follower count.\n- Note: the web search budget for this run was exhausted, so findings rely only on directly fetched pages.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/sahir-azam/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/welcoming-sahir-azam-to-index/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/candid-with-index-in-conversation-with-fireworks-lin-qiao/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/conduct-a-world-where-corporates-move-at-startup-speed/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/everything-is-a-workflow-our-investment-in-temporal/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/temporal/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/livekit/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/conduct/\n- https://fireworks.ai/blog/fireworks-series-b-funding-sequoia\n- https://fireworks.ai/news/fireworks-ai-raises-250m-series-c\n- https://fireworks.ai/news/fireworks-raises-1-5-billion-series-d\n- https://livekit.com/blog/livekit-series-c/\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-ando-modern-messaging-for-teams-and-agents\n- https://youtu.be/4q8xTqALe9I\n- https://www.youtube.com/live/qa8cefO6g2I\n", "person:salim-teja": "\n# Salim Teja\n\n## One-line take\nTeja is not a frontier-AI deal lead. They run Velocity, the platform team that does Radical's company-building work (recruiting, compute, cloud deals, policy and press), and the firm's own Velocity case study credits that team with incubation work that matches Cohere. [src](https://radical.vc/velocity/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they matter:** Velocity is the mechanism behind Radical's main edge: forming companies with researchers before they incorporate. The Velocity page's anonymous case study (a company \"co-founded by ex-Google Brain researchers\" including a Transformer co-author, which matches Cohere's founding team; the match is my inference) says \"Radical was the sole investor upon incorporation.\" It also says the team \"Scouted employee number one\" and \"Negotiated the company's first major cloud provider deal\". [src](https://radical.vc/velocity/)\n- **Background:** They spent more than 25 years building the Canadian ecosystem: MaRS Venture Services, Brightspark Ventures, Canada's Semiconductor Council. That makes them the firm's link to Canadian institutions and policy, not to Bay Area labs. [src](https://radical.vc/team/salim-teja/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/pride-in-our-technology/)\n- **What constrains them:** They are a platform partner, not on the investment team that leads deals. Their two board seats are in Canadian robotics and biotech companies Radical first backed in 2020, and they are not personally named on any frontier-lab, chip or AI-automation round.\n- **What they'd never do (inference):** lead a big competitive round in a Bay Area lab. That job belongs to Jacobs, Toews, Welch, Katz and the growth partners.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Velocity case study looks like Cohere's founding.** Radical's Velocity page describes, without naming the company, a firm co-founded by ex-Google Brain researchers including a Transformer co-author: \"Radical was the sole investor upon incorporation.\" Cohere co-founder Aidan Gomez is a Transformer co-author and was at Google Brain, so this reads as Velocity's own account of Cohere's incubation (my inference). The case study also credits Velocity with coordinating angel money from \"Turing Award winners and AI pioneers\". [src](https://radical.vc/velocity/)\n- **Semiconductor policy before Radical built a compute practice.** In June 2021, Radical quoted Teja as a \"founding member of Canada's Semiconductor Council\". They argued Canada should specialize in machine-learning, edge and quantum chips, and that a national chip policy was still needed. That came years before Katz's Compute practice and the CentML, Untether and Etched bets. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/pride-in-our-technology/)\n- **Physical-AI thesis on Promise Robotics (2021):** \"Automation brings scale and efficiency to the construction process\" [src](https://news.promiserobotics.com/psp-investments-radical-ventures-to-invest-in-promise-robotics/). They doubled down in the 2023 Series A: \"Promise Robotics is now poised to transform how buildings get made.\" [src](https://betakit.com/promise-robotics-nets-20-8-million-cad-series-a-to-speed-up-home-building-with-robots/)\n- **They interview founders.** They are Radical's founder-journey interviewer: they asked Raquel Urtasun what she \"was not prepared for\" after running Waabi for 18 months [src](https://radical.vc/articles/raquel-urtasun-on-entrepreneurship-in-uncertain-times/), and they moderated the 2023 AI Founders Masterclass panel on building AI products for \"researchers thinking about the journey to entrepreneurship\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ai-founders-building-ai-products/). That fits Velocity's job of turning researchers into founders.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low** as a deal source. They are not named on any frontier-lab, AI-chip, compute or AI-automation round, and their board seats are in Canadian construction robotics (Promise Robotics) and bioprinting (Aspect Biosystems). **Medium as an access node:** if you co-invest with Radical in a researcher-led lab, Velocity is the team that handles recruiting, cloud deals and policy. They are also a Canadian-institution connection (MaRS, Semiconductor Council, PSP co-investment). The deals that matter to you at Radical sit with Jacobs, Toews, Welch, Katz and Rosenberg.\n\n## Background\n- Ivey Business School, Western University (dates not given) [src](https://radical.vc/team/salim-teja/)\n- Co-founded MobShop Inc., a San Francisco e-commerce company backed by GE Capital, Visa International, Mayfield Fund and Marc Andreessen (dates not given; dot-com era by inference) [src](https://radical.vc/team/salim-teja/)\n- Partner at Brightspark Ventures, an early-stage Canadian VC (dates not given) [src](https://radical.vc/team/salim-teja/)\n- Leadership roles at Indigo Books & Music and CX Digital (dates not given) [src](https://radical.vc/team/salim-teja/)\n- President, Venture Services at MaRS Discovery District, Toronto (dates not given) [src](https://radical.vc/team/salim-teja/)\n- Radical Ventures Partner by 2021-03-17 [src](https://news.promiserobotics.com/psp-investments-radical-ventures-to-invest-in-promise-robotics/). Their join date was not found on Radical's site; LinkedIn was blocked (HTTP 999).\n- Founding member of Canada's Semiconductor Council (as of 2021-06) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/pride-in-our-technology/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/salim-teja/ | Team bio; checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | No bylined Radical essays; the radical.vc search for \"Salim\" returns 4 items (bio, Masterclass recap, Urtasun interview, 2021 chips piece), checked 2026-11-10 |\n| X | nothing found | The team page links only the firm account, https://twitter.com/radicalvcfund |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/salim-teja-847a524/ | Linked from the radical.vc team page; contents not readable (HTTP 999) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Interviewer in Radical's written series (Urtasun 2022) and Masterclass moderator (2023) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-10 — Radical AI Founders Masterclass — \"Building AI Products\" panel (moderator) — https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ai-founders-building-ai-products/ — asked how AI product development differs from non-AI software and how teams define product-market fit\n- 2022-11 — Radical \"AI Pioneers\" written interview — \"Raquel Urtasun on Entrepreneurship in Uncertain Times\" — https://radical.vc/articles/raquel-urtasun-on-entrepreneurship-in-uncertain-times/ — questions about what founders aren't ready for and the \"roller coaster\" of founding\n- No audio podcast appearances found (web search budget was exhausted; only direct fetches were possible)\n\n## How they think\n- Canada should specialize in AI hardware (ML, edge and quantum chips) and needs a national chip policy (2021) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/pride-in-our-technology/)\n- AI and robotics can industrialize construction (Promise Robotics, 2021 and 2023)\n- Researchers need hands-on company-building help (recruiting, cloud deals, policy, press), which is how Velocity works [src](https://radical.vc/velocity/)\n- Contrarian views: nothing found\n- Calls: Promise Robotics signed a multi-year D.R. Horton agreement in October 2026 [src](https://news.promiserobotics.com/), and Aspect signed a Novo Nordisk partnership \"worth over US$2.6 billion\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-new-era-of-life-sciences-aspect-biosystems/). Both boards are progressing, but neither has exited.\n\n## Read these first\n- https://radical.vc/velocity/ — what their team actually does, including the anonymized incubation case study that appears to be Cohere\n- https://radical.vc/articles/pride-in-our-technology/ — their 2021 chips-policy view\n- https://radical.vc/articles/raquel-urtasun-on-entrepreneurship-in-uncertain-times/ — their founder-journey interview style\n- https://betakit.com/promise-robotics-nets-20-8-million-cad-series-a-to-speed-up-home-building-with-robots/ — the Promise Robotics Series A, where they are the quoted Radical partner\n\n## Social engagement\nNo personal X account found. The firm account is @radicalvcfund. Their LinkedIn could not be read.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Promise Robotics | Strategic (Radical's first investment; amount undisclosed) | 2021-03-17 | unknown | PSP Investments | [src](https://news.promiserobotics.com/psp-investments-radical-ventures-to-invest-in-promise-robotics/) (Teja quoted; portfolio page says investment year 2020 [src](https://radical.vc/portfolio/promise-robotics/)) |\n| Promise Robotics | Series A (C$20.8M; C$34M raised to date) | 2023-10-31 | no (Horizons Ventures led) | PSP, United Brotherhood of Carpenters, Relay Ventures, Alate Partners | [src](https://betakit.com/promise-robotics-nets-20-8-million-cad-series-a-to-speed-up-home-building-with-robots/) (Teja quoted) |\n| Aspect Biosystems | Series A (US$20M; Radical led) | 2020-01-09 | firm led; Teja not named | Pangaea, Pallasite, Rhino | [src](https://www.aspectbiosystems.com/news-resources/aspect-biosystems-announces-us-20-million-series-a-financing) (Jacobs quoted; Teja's board seat is from the team page) |\n| Aspect Biosystems | Series B (US$115M) | 2025-01-08 | no (Dimension led) | Novo Nordisk, InBC, Pallasite, Pangaea, Rhino, T1D Fund | [src](https://www.aspectbiosystems.com/news-resources/aspect-biosystems-raises-us-115-million-series-b-financing-to-advance-bioprinted-tissue-therapeutics) (Teja not named) |\n\nBoard seats: Aspect Biosystems and Promise Robotics [src](https://radical.vc/team/salim-teja/). They are not personally named on any frontier-lab, AI-chip or AI-automation round.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Promise Robotics:** Radical's portfolio page dates the investment to 2020. It was announced in March 2021 alongside PSP Investments, one of Radical's own LPs and later a Breakouts anchor, with Teja as the quoted partner. Radical followed on in the Horizons-led Series A in October 2023, and Teja was quoted again. The intro path was not found. They are on the board. [src](https://news.promiserobotics.com/psp-investments-radical-ventures-to-invest-in-promise-robotics/) [src](https://betakit.com/promise-robotics-nets-20-8-million-cad-series-a-to-speed-up-home-building-with-robots/)\n- **Aspect Biosystems:** Radical led the US$20M Series A in January 2020, with Jacobs quoted, then followed on in Dimension's US$115M Series B (2025). Teja holds the board seat, but no source shows when they took it. [src](https://www.aspectbiosystems.com/news-resources/aspect-biosystems-announces-us-20-million-series-a-financing)\n- **Velocity incubation, apparently Cohere:** According to the Velocity page, Radical was the sole investor at incorporation; the team coordinated angel money from AI pioneers, scouted the first employee and negotiated the first major cloud deal. Matching it to Cohere and to Teja's personal involvement is inference. [src](https://radical.vc/velocity/)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Partner, Head of Velocity Team | by 2021-03 | [src](https://radical.vc/team/salim-teja/) [src](https://news.promiserobotics.com/psp-investments-radical-ventures-to-invest-in-promise-robotics/) |\n| Promise Robotics | Board member | not found | [src](https://radical.vc/team/salim-teja/) |\n| Aspect Biosystems | Board member | not found | [src](https://radical.vc/team/salim-teja/) |\n| MaRS Discovery District | Former President, Venture Services | dates n/a | [src](https://radical.vc/team/salim-teja/) |\n| Brightspark Ventures | Former Partner | dates n/a | [src](https://radical.vc/team/salim-teja/) |\n| Canada's Semiconductor Council | Founding member | by 2021-06 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/pride-in-our-technology/) |\n| Raquel Urtasun (Waabi) | Interviewed her for Radical | 2022-11 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/raquel-urtasun-on-entrepreneurship-in-uncertain-times/) |\n| Paige Dickie | Velocity Team colleague (VP) | not found | [src](https://radical.vc/team/paige-dickie/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did they join Radical? It must be 2021-03 or earlier; Radical's founding (2017) or the Fund II era (2019) are both possible.\n- Was Velocity's case study Cohere, and did Teja personally source Cohere's first hire or its Google Cloud deal?\n- The firm file lists Teja as a related person on Radical's Form D filings, but the four I checked (Fund II International, Growth Fund I, Fund IV, Breakouts Fund I) do not list them. Check the RV C1 Coinvest and Opportunity Fund I filings.\n- Does Velocity's compute work (the Compute Cohort credits) run under Teja or under Katz's Compute practice?\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/salim-teja/\n- https://radical.vc/velocity/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/pride-in-our-technology/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/raquel-urtasun-on-entrepreneurship-in-uncertain-times/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/radical-ai-founders-building-ai-products/\n- https://radical.vc/portfolio/promise-robotics/ ; https://radical.vc/portfolio/aspect-biosystems/\n- https://news.promiserobotics.com/psp-investments-radical-ventures-to-invest-in-promise-robotics/\n- https://betakit.com/promise-robotics-nets-20-8-million-cad-series-a-to-speed-up-home-building-with-robots/\n- https://www.aspectbiosystems.com/news-resources/aspect-biosystems-announces-us-20-million-series-a-financing\n- https://www.aspectbiosystems.com/news-resources/aspect-biosystems-raises-us-115-million-series-b-financing-to-advance-bioprinted-tissue-therapeutics\n- SEC Form D filings: CIK 1981717, 2024166, 2065455, 2152767 (Teja not listed)\n- Note: the web search budget was exhausted, so this profile uses direct page fetches only.\n", "person:sam-teller": "\n# Sam Teller\n\n## One-line take\nFormer chief of staff to Elon Musk (2014–2019), then Valor Equity Partners, who joined Thrive Capital as a Partner in 2025. Worth watching as an operator who knows how Musk's companies are run from the inside. Little of his Thrive work is public so far.\n\n## Crux\nTeller is a channel, not a thesis-driven picker. As Musk's chief of staff he was the conduit between Musk and the OpenAI founders during the 2017 for-profit negotiations. 2026 trial exhibits show him relaying Musk's offer of a Tesla board seat to Altman [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/12/openai-trial-updates-sam-altman-set-to-testify-in-musk-suit.html). Shivon Zilis testified that she fed him information on what was happening inside OpenAI [src](https://abc7news.com/live-updates/elon-musk-sam-altman-live-updates-trial-enters-2nd-week/19036397/entry/19058213/). His edge is trust and access inside the Musk network (Tesla, SpaceX, Valor, the people Musk's office hired), and now Thrive's capital. His constraint is the flip side: he has no public writing, no podcasts and no attributed investing thesis. Over roughly six years of investing (Valor, angel, Thrive), no deal is publicly credited to him as lead. No source says what he would never do. His 2012 interview stresses backing teams that have already built something, not ideas [src](https://socaltech.com/interview_with_sam_teller_launchpadla/s-0043420.html). _(Added 2026-11-10.)_ A second constraint is structural. Thrive insists \"no individual name gets attributed to any deal\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/), so his Thrive-era record may never become visible. His access also predates OpenAI: he sat in on Musk's 2017–2018 meetings with PIF governor Yasir al-Rumayyan [src](https://www.clickorlando.com/business/2023/02/01/ex-aide-says-elon-musk-had-handshake-deal-for-tesla-buyout/).\n\n## Differential insight\n(Added 2026-11-09.) Each bullet is mirrored in `data/rows/sam-teller-insights.csv`.\n- **He carried Musk's Tesla board offer to Altman (Nov 2017).** In an exhibit shown at the Musk v. Altman trial, Teller wrote that Musk \"remains very open to the idea of you joining the Tesla board\" (CNBC live blog, 2026-05-12) [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/12/openai-trial-updates-sam-altman-set-to-testify-in-musk-suit.html). He also wrote that Musk hoped Altman, Brockman and Sutskever would help build Tesla's AI team (same source, paraphrased). This puts him inside the OpenAI founding-era negotiations, which no firm page or profile mentions.\n- **He was an information channel on OpenAI.** Zilis testified she was \"sometimes providing information to Musk and Teller about what was happening at the company\" (ABC7, 2026-05-07) [src](https://abc7news.com/live-updates/elon-musk-sam-altman-live-updates-trial-enters-2nd-week/19036397/entry/19058213/). CNBC reports that her August 2017 messages to Teller referenced a \"funding freeze\" [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/07/open-ai-trial-shivon-zilis-musk-altman-tesla-board.html). He was cc'd on Musk's 2017-08-28 email to Zilis (\"Please encourage them to go start a company\") [src](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=42155833) (secondary: Hacker News comment quoting the released email archive).\n- **His testimony did not help Musk's donations claim.** TechCrunch lists \"his chief of staff Sam Teller\" among witnesses who described no specific restrictions on Musk's donations (2026-05-14) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/what-the-jury-will-actually-decide-in-the-case-of-elon-musk-vs-sam-altman/). The jury saw a short video deposition from him on 2026-05-12 [src](https://www.nbcsandiego.com/news/california/open-ai-trial-live-updates-tuesday/4022639/).\n- **He hired the Musk-office staffer who now gets a small LA fund into xAI and SpaceX.** Forbes (John Hyatt, Apr 2025) says Teller hired Elissa Butterfield into Musk's office. She later joined Island Green Capital Management (LA, about $88M, founded 2023 by Ateet Ahluwalia), and Forbes credits her with its xAI (May 2024 round) and SpaceX (SPV, Jan 2025) positions. The source is the Spanish syndication, quoted in Spanish: \"quien fue jefe de gabinete de Musk entre 2014 y 2019\" [src](https://www.forbesargentina.com/millonarios/de-asistente-elon-musk-inversora-spacex-historia-elissa-butterfield-n71483). This is the Musk-alumni access pattern that Teller himself embodies.\n- **Valor tenure: three and a half years** (Forbes, via the same syndication): \"donde trabajó como socio durante tres años y medio\" [src](https://www.forbesargentina.com/millonarios/de-asistente-elon-musk-inversora-spacex-historia-elissa-butterfield-n71483). This is more consistent with a 2020–mid-2023 span than with the aggregator dates.\n- **Launchpad LA terms and rules of thumb (2012, his only on-record investing interview).** Program terms were \"$50,000 for 6 percent in common stock, with no strings attached\" [src](https://socaltech.com/interview_with_sam_teller_launchpadla/s-0043420.html). These were 2012 program terms, not his current check, and the program later doubled to $100K per company (Jan 2013) [src](https://socaltech.com/launchpadla_opens_spring_applications_bumps_investment_dollars/s-0047037.html). On selection he said \"We're not investing in ideas and trying to turn them into companies,\" and that referrals matter: \"A strong referral from someone in the network will help\" [src](https://socaltech.com/interview_with_sam_teller_launchpadla/s-0043420.html). He also said he joined Mark Suster to raise \"a small fund\" for the program the year before [src](https://socaltech.com/interview_with_sam_teller_launchpadla/s-0043420.html).\n- **(Added 2026-11-10) In the room with Saudi PIF.** In 2023 testimony he recalled al-Rumayyan saying \"Don't worry about it, we've got a lot of money\" about the cost of the 2018 take-private (AP, 2023-02-01) [src](https://www.clickorlando.com/business/2023/02/01/ex-aide-says-elon-musk-had-handshake-deal-for-tesla-buyout/). This is a first-hand, if dated, tie to one of the largest sovereign allocators in AI compute.\n- **(Added 2026-11-10) What the job actually was.** He testified that Musk \"is the type of CEO who likes to absorb his communications\" and compared himself to an air traffic controller. He was triaging Musk's communications, not gatekeeping them [src](https://www.clickorlando.com/business/2023/02/01/ex-aide-says-elon-musk-had-handshake-deal-for-tesla-buyout/).\n- **(Added 2026-11-10) More 2012 rules of thumb:** \"We don't get excited about derivative businesses\", and he urged founders to \"try and find a technical co-founder\" (raw page text verified) [src](https://www.socaltech.com/interview_with_sam_teller_launchpadla/s-0043420.html).\n- **(Added 2026-11-10) The user-supplied link, nova.nexus, has no link to Teller.** The site describes Nova as \"a global talent investing firm\" (San Francisco, X @novaholdings) and names no team or investors [src](https://www.nova.nexus/). The \"Nova Network\" in his angel portfolio (CB Insights, $1.75M angel, 2024-03-14) resolves to itsnova.com, an LA marketplace that connects brands with creative talent [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/company/nova-network/financials) [src](https://itsnova.com). They appear to be different companies, so nova.nexus is logged as a `needs-id` lead, not as a relationship.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He sits inside Thrive, one of the core frontier-AI allocators (OpenAI and others), and inside the Musk/xAI network. He also has first-hand history with OpenAI's founding-era negotiations (see above). Neither of those makes him a frontier-AI investor on the record, though. No AI, lab, chip or automation deal is attributed to him. His sourced angel deals are media and consumer: ODF, FazeWorld and Nova Network. Value to me is as a relationship node into Musk-world deal flow (SpaceX/xAI secondaries, ex-Tesla/SpaceX founders) and into Thrive, not as a co-investor with a visible thesis. Upgrade to high if a Thrive-era AI or hard-tech deal is attributed to him.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** BA, Harvard University. Only an aggregator lists this [src](https://startupintros.com/people/sam-teller). One report says he and Josh Kushner were undergrads together (unverified; see Relationships).\n- **Charlie (LA media company):** co-founder, before Launchpad LA [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Launchpad_LA). Aggregators date it to 2010 (unverified) [src](https://pipeseed.com/investor/sam-teller-979084).\n- **Launchpad LA (seed accelerator/fund):** took over leadership starting with the third class [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Launchpad_LA). The program wound down in 2014 when he became Musk's chief of staff [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Launchpad_LA).\n- **Elon Musk's office, 2014–2019:** described as Musk's chief of staff, working closely with him from 2014 to 2019 [src](https://www.clickorlando.com/business/2023/02/01/ex-aide-says-elon-musk-had-handshake-deal-for-tesla-buyout/) (AP, 2023-02-01). Aggregators give the title as \"Director, Office of the CEO\" at Tesla and SpaceX [src](https://startupintros.com/people/sam-teller). One aggregator says The Boring Company was added in 2017 (unverified) [src](https://startupintros.com/people/sam-teller). In trial testimony he compared the job to an air traffic controller and said he left in 2019 because \"I was pretty tired and it was time to do something else\" [src](https://www.clickorlando.com/business/2023/02/01/ex-aide-says-elon-musk-had-handshake-deal-for-tesla-buyout/).\n- **Valor Equity Partners:** Partner. He left in 2023, per Dan Primack (Axios) on 2025-04-28 [src](https://x.com/danprimack/status/1916857068901765566). Aggregators give Partner 2020–2022 and Venture Partner 2022–2023. Those dates are unverified [src](https://www.signalhire.com/profiles/sam-teller%27s-email/10047880).\n- **2023–2025 gap:** no source found for what he did between Valor and Thrive, such as founding a company or fund. He made angel investments in this period (see Portfolio).\n- **Thrive Capital, 2025–present:** Dan Primack posted \"Sam Teller is joining Thrive Capital\" on 2025-04-28. The date comes from the post ID; the post page itself was paywalled and its wording comes from a search snippet [src](https://x.com/danprimack/status/1916857068901765566). Colossus (Oct 2025, Issue 05) lists him as a Thrive Partner and one of the people interviewed for its Kushner/Thrive profile [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/). Aggregators give a March 2025 start (unverified) [src](https://www.signalhire.com/profiles/sam-teller%27s-email/10047880). Thrive's own site has no team page, so the firm has not confirmed his role or start date.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/samteller | Unverified handle. The page could not be loaded (402), so follower count and posting cadence are unknown as of 2026-11-09 |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/steller | Cited by an aggregator [src](https://www.dataroomhq.ai/partner/sam-teller/). Not loaded |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| Product Hunt | https://www.producthunt.com/@samteller | Bio describes him as a Valor investor and ex-SpaceX/Tesla (stale; seen in a search snippet) [src](https://www.producthunt.com/@samteller) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2012-06 — SoCal Tech interview (text Q&A, not a podcast) on Launchpad LA terms and selection [src](https://socaltech.com/interview_with_sam_teller_launchpadla/s-0043420.html). See Differential insight.\n- 2026-05-12 — Video deposition played to the jury in Musk v. Altman [src](https://www.nbcsandiego.com/news/california/open-ai-trial-live-updates-tuesday/4022639/).\n\nNo podcasts or YouTube talks found as of 2026-11-09. The only public record of him speaking at length is his testimony in the Tesla \"funding secured\" shareholder trial, in which Musk's lawyers called him as a witness:\n- 2023-02 — Tesla \"funding secured\" shareholder trial (testimony, not a talk). He testified that he believed Musk and Saudi PIF governor Yasir al-Rumayyan \"made a handshake deal\" on a 2018 take-private [src](https://www.teslarati.com/elon-musk-handshake-deal-saudi-pif-funding-secured-tweet/). He said the PIF meetings began in January 2017 [src](https://www.teslarati.com/elon-musk-handshake-deal-saudi-pif-funding-secured-tweet/). He also said he sometimes had to soften Musk's blunt emails [src](https://www.clickorlando.com/business/2023/02/01/ex-aide-says-elon-musk-had-handshake-deal-for-tesla-buyout/).\n\n## How they think\nNothing found. He has no public writing, podcasts or interviews on investing. Aggregators tag him with pre-seed and seed focus in SMB software, govtech, education, entertainment, fashion and social commerce [src](https://www.dataroomhq.ai/partner/sam-teller/), but these look auto-generated and are not his stated theses. His trial testimony describes Musk as a CEO who \"likes to absorb his communications\" without others filtering them [src](https://www.clickorlando.com/business/2023/02/01/ex-aide-says-elon-musk-had-handshake-deal-for-tesla-buyout/). That shows his view of Musk's management style, not an investing thesis.\n\n## Read these first\nNothing found by him. Two pieces about him:\n- AP report on his trial testimony (2023-02-01): the best public account of what his role for Musk involved [src](https://www.clickorlando.com/business/2023/02/01/ex-aide-says-elon-musk-had-handshake-deal-for-tesla-buyout/)\n- Colossus, \"The New World: Joshua Kushner, Thrive Capital, and the American dream\" (Oct 2025): names him as a Thrive Partner and source, though the summary I retrieved contains no quote from him [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. The X account could not be checked.\n\n## Portfolio\nAngel investments from an aggregator (CB Insights lists 10 investments; 5 are visible and 2 of those hide the company name). No Thrive-era deals are publicly attributed to him.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ODF (On Deck) | Series A | 2021-03-25 | No (angel) | Founders Fund, Elad Gil, Dylan Field, others | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/sam-teller) |\n| FazeWorld | Seed | 2023-12 | No (angel) | Jeremy Zimmer, Scott Belsky, Jordan Fudge, Steal The Moon Ventures | [src](https://www.axios.com/2023/12/05/fazeworld-studio-tiktok-shows) |\n| Nova Network | Angel | 2024-03-14 | No (angel) | Humberto Leon, Troy Carter | [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/sam-teller) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Elon Musk | Chief of staff / Office of the CEO | 2014–2019 | [src](https://www.clickorlando.com/business/2023/02/01/ex-aide-says-elon-musk-had-handshake-deal-for-tesla-buyout/) |\n| Tesla, SpaceX | Office of the CEO (aggregator title) | 2014–2019 | [src](https://startupintros.com/people/sam-teller) |\n| Launchpad LA | Led the accelerator from its third class until it wound down in 2014 | until 2014 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Launchpad_LA) |\n| Mark Suster | Co-founder of Launchpad LA, the program Teller later led. Direct working relationship not confirmed | — | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Launchpad_LA) |\n| Valor Equity Partners | Partner; left 2023 | until 2023 | [src](https://x.com/danprimack/status/1916857068901765566) |\n| Thrive Capital | Partner | 2025 | [src](https://x.com/danprimack/status/1916857068901765566) |\n| Sam Altman | Teller relayed Musk's Tesla-board offer to Altman (trial exhibit, Nov 2017) | 2017 | [src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/12/openai-trial-updates-sam-altman-set-to-testify-in-musk-suit.html) |\n| Shivon Zilis | Fed Teller and Musk information on OpenAI, 2017–2018 (her trial testimony) | 2017 | [src](https://abc7news.com/live-updates/elon-musk-sam-altman-live-updates-trial-enters-2nd-week/19036397/entry/19058213/) |\n| Elissa Butterfield | Teller hired her into Musk's office; she now runs Musk-company deals at Island Green | ~2016 | [src](https://www.forbesargentina.com/millonarios/de-asistente-elon-musk-inversora-spacex-historia-elissa-butterfield-n71483) |\n| Joshua Kushner | Colleague at Thrive. Axios Pro Rata reportedly said they have known each other since Harvard (unverified; Axios item not loaded, only a search snippet) | 2025 at Thrive | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **OpenAI-era role (added 2026-11-09):** read the full trial exhibits and the OpenAI email archive (LessWrong compilation could not be loaded) for every message Teller authored in 2017–2018.\n- **Thrive scope:** any Thrive AI or hard-tech deal with Teller on it? Does he source SpaceX/xAI or ex-Musk-company founders for Thrive?\n- **Antonio Gracias:** no source found tying Teller personally to Gracias beyond both being at Valor. Did Gracias hire him out of Musk's office? Is the Valor role a Musk-network hire?\n- **Exact Thrive start:** aggregators say March 2025 and Primack posted on 2025-04-28. What is his scope at Thrive: a stage, a sector, or a platform/portfolio role? Is he tied to Thrive's 2026 move into sports [src](https://www.axios.com/2026/04/27/joshua-kushner-sports-giants-thrive-eternal) (no link to Teller found)?\n- **2023–2025:** what did he do between Valor and Thrive?\n- **Aggregator conflation:** profiles mix him with other people or out-of-date data. Examples are \"Managing Partner, Launchpad Venture Group\" (likely a separate angel group), \"Partner, ORKKA SOFT\", and conflicting locations. FabFitFun co-founder appears only in directory listings, and mainstream coverage names three other founders, so it is left out of this profile.\n- **Board seat, The Elysian Theater (from 2024):** listed only by Village (page returned 403), so unverified.\n- **Harvard overlap with Josh Kushner:** find the Axios Pro Rata 2025-04-28 item to confirm.\n- **Two hidden 2021 seed deals on CB Insights:** which companies?\n- **nova.nexus (user-supplied, 2026-11-10):** who runs it? F4 Fund names a co-founder (search snippet only; the page returned 403). Is there any link to Teller? None found. Also, is it related to Nova Network (itsnova.com)? It looks unrelated.\n- **Isaacson's *Elon Musk* (2023):** reportedly mentions Teller (for example, around the 2018 Thai cave mini-sub). Not checked against the book.\n\n## Sources\n- https://builtin.com/articles/four-la-tech-giants-you-must-know (2013-06-29; added 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.tesmanian.com/blogs/tesmanian-blog/tesla-s-former-chief-of-staff-said-he-had-a-sense-that-pif-and-elon-musk-made-a-handshake-deal-to-proceed-with-taking-tesla-private-in-2018 (2023-02-01; added 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.nova.nexus/ (user-supplied; no Teller link found; checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://itsnova.com (Nova Network's site per CB Insights; checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.cbinsights.com/company/nova-network/financials (aggregator; added 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/12/openai-trial-updates-sam-altman-set-to-testify-in-musk-suit.html (2026-05-12)\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/07/open-ai-trial-shivon-zilis-musk-altman-tesla-board.html (2026-05-07)\n- https://abc7news.com/live-updates/elon-musk-sam-altman-live-updates-trial-enters-2nd-week/19036397/entry/19058213/ (2026-05-07)\n- https://www.nbcsandiego.com/news/california/open-ai-trial-live-updates-tuesday/4022639/ (2026-05-12)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/what-the-jury-will-actually-decide-in-the-case-of-elon-musk-vs-sam-altman/ (2026-05-14)\n- https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=42155833 (2024-11; secondary, quotes released email)\n- https://www.forbesargentina.com/millonarios/de-asistente-elon-musk-inversora-spacex-historia-elissa-butterfield-n71483 (2025-04-30; Forbes syndication of John Hyatt's article)\n- https://socaltech.com/interview_with_sam_teller_launchpadla/s-0043420.html (2012-06-20; raw text verified)\n- https://socaltech.com/launchpadla_opens_spring_applications_bumps_investment_dollars/s-0047037.html (2013-01-04)\n- https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/ (Oct 2025)\n- https://x.com/danprimack/status/1916857068901765566 (2025-04-28; paywalled, content from search snippet)\n- https://www.clickorlando.com/business/2023/02/01/ex-aide-says-elon-musk-had-handshake-deal-for-tesla-buyout/ (AP, 2023-02-01)\n- https://www.teslarati.com/elon-musk-handshake-deal-saudi-pif-funding-secured-tweet/ (2023-02-02)\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Launchpad_LA\n- https://www.axios.com/2023/12/05/fazeworld-studio-tiktok-shows (2023-12-05)\n- https://www.axios.com/2026/04/27/joshua-kushner-sports-giants-thrive-eternal\n- https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/sam-teller (aggregator)\n- https://startupintros.com/people/sam-teller (aggregator)\n- https://www.signalhire.com/profiles/sam-teller%27s-email/10047880 (aggregator)\n- https://www.dataroomhq.ai/partner/sam-teller/ (aggregator)\n- https://pipeseed.com/investor/sam-teller-979084 (aggregator)\n- https://village.ai/person/sam-teller-NDI5Nzg0MTc (403, not read)\n", "person:sameer-dholakia": "\n# Sameer Dholakia\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the operator-turned-growth-partner who pushed Bessemer into Anthropic over its lowest-ever IC vote, and he now argues openly that capital is a moat and that small seed funds cannot win in AI ([20VC](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He brings 25-plus years of enterprise-software operating and an operator's network. Fireworks is the clearest case: its new president George Hu had worked alongside him for 18 months after the Twilio-SendGrid deal, and that hire was his reason to invest ([20VC 1:03:10-1:04:28](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)). The Anthropic allocation came through Byron Deeter's friendship with Menlo's Matt Murphy ([20VC 5:21](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **How he decides.** He prices on next-12-months revenue only, thinks 10-15x NTM is fine for hypergrowth, and favours sticky enterprise revenue over prosumer revenue. He wants a 5x or better on every growth deal ([20VC 17:17, 18:11, 40:02](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **What constrains him.** He writes growth checks from a platform fund ($4B growth in BVP's $5.75B raise), so ownership and seed pricing are not his game. By his own account he is still \"four and a half years\" into venture ([20VC 15:43](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **What he'd never do.** He would not run a mid-sized generalist fund (\"if you're in between, good luck\"), and he would not underwrite more than about 12 months forward ([20VC 31:43, 17:35](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Anthropic intro path.** Menlo's Matt Murphy phoned Byron Deeter to build the Series D syndicate, and Deeter pulled Dholakia in: \"Matt called up his buddy Byron and said\" ([20VC 5:21](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **Anthropic entry price.** He recalls it as \"I think it was 15 billion pre, 18 billion post,\" at about $100M ARR (~150x) ([20VC 5:08, 16:46](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)). Our deals table only has BVP's undisclosed \"prior investment,\" so this fills the round.\n- **The IC fight.** Anthropic \"was the lowest voted deal in the history of the firm\" (per Deeter). It went through on BVP's \"empowerment\" value ([20VC 7:41](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **Regret.** \"I just wish I wish we had written a bigger check\" ([20VC 8:35](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **OpenAI pass.** He passed right after ChatGPT: \"that open AI uh opportunity that I also passed on\". OpenAI's 2023 revenue ramp is what got him to Anthropic ([20VC 14:13](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **Concentration.** BVP's Century II growth fund put \"20% into one name the name was anthropic\". Typical growth bets are $200-400M per company ([20VC 37:05-37:50](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **Seed view.** \"I don't know how you would have a small seed fund and be successful\". Seed used to be $3-5M on $20-40M and is now \"factors higher\" ([20VC 28:48-30:08](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **Mistral.** In October 2026 he said BVP is \"investing in Mistral right now,\" alongside Sarvam, on a sovereign-model thesis ([20VC 1:05:13](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **Changed mind.** He underrated \"how big the open token economy is going to be\". That is why BVP leans into fal and Fireworks ([20VC 1:14:43](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **Entry multiples he names.**\n  - Abridge at ~$5M ARR for ~$700-800M.\n  - Legora led at $1.8B on ~$20M ARR.\n  - Fieldguide at sub-$10M ARR for under $200M.\n\n  Source: [20VC 16:38, 40:37](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8).\n- **Pre-BVP backer.** Bain Capital Ventures' SendGrid check was a bet on him personally. Ajay Agarwal, a 22-year friend from Trilogy, said: \"The moment Sameer took the job, we were writing the check\" ([SaaStr 2017](https://www.saastr.com/css-speaker-series-005-sameer-dholakiasendgrid-ajay-agarwalbain-capital-ventures-video-transcript/)).\n- **Operator mistake he admits.** He followed board advice to \"focus\": \"we didn't get act two started until too late\" ([SaaStr 218, 2019](https://www.saastr.com/saastr-podcast-218-twilio-founder-jeff-lawson-sendgrid-ceo-sameer-dholakia-on-why-developer-first-is-a-maturation-in-the-supply-chain-of-software)).\n- **2008 VMLogix near-death.** An acquisition collapsed after Lehman, and he turned it into a prepaid royalty OEM deal: \"We can't get married now. So let's get engaged.\" ([Powderkeg 2017](https://powderkeg.com/proven-business-leadership-habits-ceo-sendgrid)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is a growth investor, but he is a named BVP partner on frontier labs and AI infrastructure:\n- Anthropic: Series D, Series E and Series G\n- Fireworks AI: Series D\n- fal: Series F lead\n- Mistral: being invested in, per 20VC\n- Sarvam\n- doubleAI: Series A\n- Trajectory: seed\n\nHe is also on the AI-native automation companies the owner likes: Legora, EvenUp, Fieldguide and EliseAI. He is not a co-lead for $20M seed checks. He is the person who brings BVP growth money in behind a lab at the next round, and his view that \"capital is a moat\" means he will back the lab with the biggest war chest. He is a useful contact for follow-on rounds and for growth syndicates on labs. He is a poor fit as an LP or as a seed peer.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford BA and MA; Harvard MBA ([BVP bio](https://www.bvp.com/team/sameer-dholakia)). Graduated in 1995 ([20VC 2:07](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- 1995: Trilogy University class. He met Ajay Agarwal there ([SaaStr 2017](https://www.saastr.com/css-speaker-series-005-sameer-dholakiasendgrid-ajay-agarwalbain-capital-ventures-video-transcript/)).\n- VMLogix CEO. The company was acquired by Citrix ([BVP bio](https://www.bvp.com/team/sameer-dholakia)). Citrix GVP and GM of the Cloud Platforms Group for about four years until 2014.\n- 2014-09: SendGrid CEO. The company was at about $30M of trailing revenue ([PR Newswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/sendgrid-appoints-sameer-dholakia-as-new-ceo-277609291.html), [SaaStr 2019](https://www.saastr.com/the-sendgrid-journey-scaling-from-growth)). NYSE IPO in 2017; acquired by Twilio in early 2019 for about $3B. He left Twilio in 2020 ([BVP Atlas](https://www.bvp.com/atlas/six-ceo-lessons-to-help-teenage-startups-thrives)).\n- 2020-2022: sabbatical and advisory work. Jasper's GPT-3 demo in winter 2021 was his \"magic\" moment ([20VC 3:20](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- 2022-05-09: joined Bessemer as Partner and co-lead of the growth practice and the Century fund with Elliott Robinson ([BVP](https://www.bvp.com/news/bessemer-venture-partners-welcomes-sameer-dholakia-as-partner-and-co-lead-of-the-growth-investment-practice)). BVP had backed SendGrid since before he arrived.\n- Board chair of the Menlo School ([BVP bio](https://www.bvp.com/team/sameer-dholakia)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.bvp.com/team/sameer-dholakia | BVP bio |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.bvp.com/atlas | Co-author on Atlas pieces and BVP \"why we invested\" memos (about 25 since 2022) |\n| X | nothing found | BVP page links only @BessemerVP |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/sameerdholakia/ | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2017-03 — Powderkeg #21 — leadership habits — https://powderkeg.com/proven-business-leadership-habits-ceo-sendgrid — the 2008 VMLogix \"engaged, not married\" OEM deal; Mark Templeton as mentor.\n- 2017-07 — SaaStr speaker series with Ajay Agarwal — https://www.saastr.com/css-speaker-series-005-sameer-dholakiasendgrid-ajay-agarwalbain-capital-ventures-video-transcript/ — BCV wrote its check the day he took the job; \"profitability is a choice.\"\n- 2018-02 — Stanford Entrepreneurial Thought Leaders, \"Focus On People\" — https://stvp.stanford.edu/podcasts/focus-on-people — transcript PDF returned 404, not mined.\n- 2019-02 — This Week in Startups E901 — https://thisweekinstartups.com/episodes/7dde56-e901-twilio-sendgrid-sameer-dholakia-led-30m-to-3b-acquisition-w-marketing-produ — not mined; no transcript text was reachable.\n- 2019-03 — SaaStr 218 with Jeff Lawson — the act-two-too-late admission and \"I wish I had just gone faster.\"\n- 2019 — SaaStr Annual with Anna Khan (BVP) — https://www.saastr.com/the-sendgrid-journey-scaling-from-growth — turned down Twilio before the IPO because \"we couldn't make the math work\"; one exec hire per quarter.\n- 2023-05 — Bloomberg Technology on BVP's $1B AI commitment; SaaStr 664 ($10M to $100M).\n- 2025-10 — Give First (Brad Feld) Ep. 102 — not mined.\n- 2026-10-19 — 20VC with Harry Stebbings — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8 — his richest source: Anthropic origin, OpenAI pass, fund construction, Mistral.\n\n## How they think\n- **Cloud redux.** Frontier models are capital-intensive like hyperscalers, so the layer consolidates to a handful of labs with hundreds of apps on top ([20VC 6:00](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8); [BVP 2024](https://www.bvp.com/news/why-we-invested-in-anthropic)).\n- **Capital is a moat.** It is \"necessary but not sufficient\": a lab needs the biggest war chest because compute is scarce ([20VC 23:52-25:52](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **Barbell venture.** Firms will be either giant platforms or extreme specialists ([20VC 30:33](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **Open and closed.** Predicting the open versus closed token mix \"is irrelevant\" at this size of market ([20VC 9:46](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **Calls he concedes he got wrong.**\n  - OpenAI: he passed.\n  - Anthropic: he wanted a bigger check.\n  - He underestimated the frontier TAM \"by factors.\"\n  - Stripe was BVP's miss.\n- **Bold calls on record.**\n  - Waymo is a trillion-dollar business.\n  - Fireworks is a 20x from about $15B.\n  - IPOs unlock in late 2027 and 2028.\n  - Robotics is at the \"2021 GenAI\" moment.\n\n## Read these first\n- [20VC, 2026-10-19](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8): the full playbook in his own words.\n- [Why we backed Anthropic, 2024-10-22](https://www.bvp.com/news/why-we-invested-in-anthropic): the cloud-level TAM thesis, with him as first author.\n- [The SendGrid Journey, SaaStr 2019](https://www.saastr.com/the-sendgrid-journey-scaling-from-growth): how he runs M&A and exec hiring.\n- [A CEO's tactical guide to profitable growth, 2023](https://www.bvp.com/atlas/a-ceo-s-tactical-guide-to-driving-profitable-growth): the SendGrid -30% to breakeven case.\n- [Crossing the Rubicon, 2023-05-09](https://www.bvp.com/news/crossing-the-rubicon): BVP's $1B AI commitment, co-signed with Talia Goldberg.\n\n## Social engagement\nNo personal X account was found; the BVP bio links only to the firm's account. His public voice is BVP memos, Atlas pieces and podcasts. Harry Stebbings has hosted him twice (SaaStr 218 in 2019 and 20VC in 2026). Talia Goldberg seeded a question for the 20VC interview ([20VC 43:12](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Anthropic | Series D (~$15B pre / $18B post, his recollection) | 2024 | no | Menlo (lead), Lightspeed | [20VC 5:08](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8) |\n| Anthropic | Series E ($3.5B at $61.5B) | 2025-03 | no | Lightspeed (lead) | [BVP](https://www.bvp.com/news/3-5-billion-more-for-anthropic) |\n| Legora | Series C ($150M at $1.8B) | 2025-10 | yes | | [BVP](https://www.bvp.com/news/building-legoras-collaborative-ai-platform-for-legal-workflow-automation) |\n| EvenUp | Series B / Series E | 2023-06 / 2025-10 | yes / yes | Bain Capital Ventures | [BVP](https://www.bvp.com/news/evenup-providing-equal-access-to-justice-with-ai), [BVP](https://www.bvp.com/news/tripling-down-on-evenup-to-redefine-justice-with-ai) |\n| Fieldguide | Series B ($30M) | 2024-03 | yes | | [BVP](https://www.bvp.com/news/fieldguide-transforming-the-200-billion-audit-and-advisory-industry-with-ai) |\n| Fireworks AI | Series D ($1.5B at $17.5B) | 2026-07 | no | Atreides, Index, TCV | [BVP](https://www.bvp.com/news/fireworks-the-inference-layer-for-the-open-model-era) |\n| Waymo | Growth (nearly $200M from BVP) | 2026-02 | no | Sequoia, Dragoneer, DST | [BVP](https://www.bvp.com/news/waymo-drives-the-next-chapter-of-autonomous-mobility), [20VC 1:15:29](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8) |\n| EliseAI | Growth ($350M; BVP nearly $200M) | 2026-09 | co-led | | [BVP](https://www.bvp.com/news/eliseai-deepens-vertical-operations-in-housing-and-healthcare) |\n| Bedrock Robotics | Series C | 2026-10 | no | CapitalG, Emergence | [BVP](https://www.bvp.com/news/bedrock-robotics-deploying-autonomous-equipment-for-the-built-world) |\n| fal | Series F | 2026-11 | yes | | [BVP](https://www.bvp.com/news/investing-in-fal-the-generative-media-company) |\n| Mistral AI | in progress (\"investing right now\") | 2026-10 | unknown | | [20VC 1:05:13](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8) |\n\nThe full deal list (doubleAI, Trajectory, Abridge, Sarvam, Jasper, DeepL, Valence, Upwind) is in `data/deals.csv`.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Anthropic (Series D).**\n  - Matt Murphy led for Menlo and called his old friend Byron Deeter to build the syndicate. Deeter brought Dholakia ([20VC 5:21](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n  - The thesis was \"cloud redux.\" AWS was the hyperscaler without a strong in-house model (Nova), and its Anthropic tie gave compute and distribution.\n  - The IC vote was the lowest in BVP history at the time, but the partner pair had conviction.\n  - BVP followed in the Series E and G, and Anthropic grew to about 20% of Century II ([20VC 7:41, 37:50](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **Fireworks AI.** George Hu, ex-Twilio and Dholakia's colleague in Jeff Lawson's leadership team, joined CEO Lin Qiao as president a month or two before BVP invested. On their first call Hu asked him how BVP would add value ([20VC 1:02:36-1:04:16](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **Jasper.** Dave Rogenmoser demoed Jasper on GPT-3 to him in winter 2021, while he was advising ([20VC 3:28](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)). BVP joined Jasper's $125M Series A in October 2022, five months after he arrived ([BVP](https://www.bvp.com/news/how-jasper-is-making-ai-the-future-of-content-creation)). That sequence suggests he brought it in, but no source says so.\n- **SendGrid and Twilio.** SendGrid turned down Twilio before its IPO because \"we couldn't make the math work.\" Jeff Lawson came back 6-9 months after the IPO ([SaaStr 2019](https://www.saastr.com/the-sendgrid-journey-scaling-from-growth)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Byron Deeter (BVP) | Anthropic co-lead and IC champion; has known him since the start of SendGrid | 2014 | [BVP](https://www.bvp.com/news/bessemer-venture-partners-welcomes-sameer-dholakia-as-partner-and-co-lead-of-the-growth-investment-practice), [20VC](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8) |\n| Elliott Robinson (BVP) | Co-leads growth and the Century fund | 2022 | [BVP](https://www.bvp.com/news/bessemer-venture-partners-welcomes-sameer-dholakia-as-partner-and-co-lead-of-the-growth-investment-practice) |\n| Matt Murphy (Menlo) | Brought BVP into the Anthropic Series D via Deeter | 2024 | [20VC](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8) |\n| George Hu (Fireworks) | Ex-Twilio colleague; his move to Fireworks triggered the investment | 2019 | [20VC](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8) |\n| Ajay Agarwal (Bain Capital Ventures) | 22-year friend from Trilogy; SendGrid board member | 1995 | [SaaStr 2017](https://www.saastr.com/css-speaker-series-005-sameer-dholakiasendgrid-ajay-agarwalbain-capital-ventures-video-transcript/) |\n| Ravi Mhatre (Lightspeed) | His dream BVP hire | | [20VC](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8) |\n| Jeff Lawson (Twilio) | Acquirer and former boss | 2018 | [SaaStr 2019](https://www.saastr.com/the-sendgrid-journey-scaling-from-growth) |\n| Dave Rogenmoser (Jasper) | Advisee whose demo got him into AI | 2021 | [20VC](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8) |\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-11)\n- **Check sizing and concentration.**\n  - Growth bets run $200-400M per company.\n  - Century II has about 20% in Anthropic.\n  - He wants at least 5x on every growth deal.\n\n  Source: [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8).\n- **Pricing rule.** He uses next-12-months revenue only, and 10-15x NTM is \"not crazy\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **Seed.** He does not see how a small seed fund can succeed at today's AI prices ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8)).\n- **Misses.**\n  - OpenAI after ChatGPT.\n  - Not sizing up Anthropic.\n  - Stripe, for BVP.\n\n  Source: [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8).\n- **Operator lessons.** SendGrid started its second product too late because of board \"focus\" advice ([src](https://www.saastr.com/saastr-podcast-218-twilio-founder-jeff-lawson-sendgrid-ceo-sameer-dholakia-on-why-developer-first-is-a-maturation-in-the-supply-chain-of-software)). He rejected Twilio before the IPO on price ([src](https://www.saastr.com/the-sendgrid-journey-scaling-from-growth)).\n\n## Open questions\n- Is BVP's Mistral investment closed, and which round is it in (he said \"investing right now\" on 2026-10-19)?\n- Did he personally source BVP's October 2022 Jasper Series A, after the 2021 demo?\n- Who was the 2008 VMLogix OEM counterparty? Was it Citrix, which bought VMLogix in 2010?\n- Give First (2025), TWiST E901 (2019) and the Stanford ETL talk (2018) are not mined. No transcripts were reachable.\n- Do the two Bloomberg Tech hits in 2026 have verbatim lines on the Anthropic and government dispute?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-r2_5LboWL8 (20VC, 2026-10-19; auto-caption transcript)\n- https://www.bvp.com/team/sameer-dholakia\n- https://www.bvp.com/news/bessemer-venture-partners-welcomes-sameer-dholakia-as-partner-and-co-lead-of-the-growth-investment-practice\n- https://www.bvp.com/news/why-we-invested-in-anthropic\n- https://www.bvp.com/news/crossing-the-rubicon\n- https://www.bvp.com/atlas/a-ceo-s-tactical-guide-to-driving-profitable-growth\n- https://www.bvp.com/atlas/six-ceo-lessons-to-help-teenage-startups-thrives\n- https://www.saastr.com/css-speaker-series-005-sameer-dholakiasendgrid-ajay-agarwalbain-capital-ventures-video-transcript/\n- https://www.saastr.com/the-sendgrid-journey-scaling-from-growth\n- https://www.saastr.com/saastr-podcast-218-twilio-founder-jeff-lawson-sendgrid-ceo-sameer-dholakia-on-why-developer-first-is-a-maturation-in-the-supply-chain-of-software\n- https://powderkeg.com/proven-business-leadership-habits-ceo-sendgrid\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/sendgrid-appoints-sameer-dholakia-as-new-ceo-277609291.html\n- Full media inventory: data/rows/sameer-dholakia-voice-media.csv\n", "person:sameer-gandhi": "\n# Sameer Gandhi\n\n## One-line take\nGandhi is a 25-year, two-firm (Sequoia, then Accel) partner who wins by tracking founders for years and then putting large, concentrated checks behind them. That habit now points at physical AI (Mind Robotics board, 2026) and late-stage frontier names (Anthropic, Perplexity).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he sticks with founders across companies and across firms. He led Dropbox's initial seed at Sequoia, stayed on it at Accel through the IPO ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20161102211254/http://www.accel.com:80/team/sameer-gandhi), [src](https://www.accel.com/news/the-dropbox-ipo)), and has backed Dean Drako, Wayne Jackson and Marc Lore at more than one company each ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/pioneering-the-video-surveillance-industrys-movement-to-the-cloud-our-investment-in-eagle-eye-networks), [src](https://www.accel.com/news/the-power-of-persistence), [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/wonder-marc-lore)).\n- **How he deploys:** he forms the thesis first, then hunts for a team ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/crwd-solving-the-hard-problems-first)), and once in, he concentrates. At CrowdStrike, Accel took $25M of a $30M round and then led or co-led every later round through the IPO ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240623233407/https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/FINAL2-2021-09-01-CrowdStrike-Accel-Memo-redacted-1.pdf), [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/live-at-accel-during-the-rsa-conference-crowdstrikes-george-kurtz-on-the-security-arms-race)).\n- **Constraint:** he is a senior, board-heavy partner (CrowdStrike, Freshworks, Mind Robotics), and most of his recorded thinking is about security and SaaS, not research labs. On frontier AI he shows up at large rounds (Anthropic 2025, Perplexity 2025, Mind Robotics $500M A), not at pre-seed.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred from his own words):** take the hybrid shortcut. He recalls agreeing with Kurtz to keep CrowdStrike cloud-only even when that cost customers ([src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/live-at-accel-during-the-rsa-conference-crowdstrikes-george-kurtz-on-the-security-arms-race)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **CrowdStrike check size, from the 2013 IC memo** (published by 20VC in 2021): \"Accel to speak for $25 of $30m round\", at \"$143m pre, all primary\". The deal page is headed \"A 30 Day Hunting License\" ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240623233407/https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/FINAL2-2021-09-01-CrowdStrike-Accel-Memo-redacted-1.pdf)).\n- **Accel was not first in:** \"Kurtz raised $25m from Warburg and began selling in Q3 2012\" (same memo, p.2). Bottoms-up market sizing was still an open item after the deal was pursued: \"Market size analysis to run (bottoms up)\" (p.6).\n- **How the CrowdStrike deal began:** \"we hounded George incessantly to even meet with us\". Kurtz arrived 30 minutes late with dilated eyes, and the one-hour meeting ran two hours ([src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/live-at-accel-during-the-rsa-conference-crowdstrikes-george-kurtz-on-the-security-arms-race), 01:52).\n- **Price was contested:** \"You would've changed the valuation that I invested at.\" Kurtz says they \"went back and forth\" on valuation ([src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/live-at-accel-during-the-rsa-conference-crowdstrikes-george-kurtz-on-the-security-arms-race), 21:39).\n- **Admitted mistake (Flipkart, 2008):** Accel assumed about $500M to break even: \"we were definitely an order of magnitude off!\" They still wrote an $800K seed ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/flipkart-realizing-the-india-opportunity)).\n- **Dropbox at Sequoia:** he decided \"after 30 minutes of a quick back and forth conversation\", in a category he called \"a wasteland of failed products and companies\" ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/the-dropbox-ipo)). His archived Accel bio credits him with \"Dropbox (initial seed)\" at Sequoia ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20161102211254/http://www.accel.com:80/team/sameer-gandhi)).\n- **Tracking talent for years:** \"We first met Alex back in 2011 when he was working on his PhD\". He advised Karpenko's first company (Luma, sold to Instagram), then backed Rylo when it formed in 2015 ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/introducing-rylo)).\n- **Deals built around a person:** \"Accel would invest and join the board and he would join as CEO\". This was Sonatype in 2010, built around Wayne Jackson, whom he knew from his 2004 Sourcefire investment ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/the-power-of-persistence)).\n- **Physical AI thesis (2026):** \"The blocker is data.\" He argues Rivian's factories give Mind Robotics the deployment data that closes the training loop ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/mind-robotics-bringing-ai-to-the-physical-world)).\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-11)\nThe strongest points from mining his memos and Spotlight On transcripts (rows in `data/rows/sameer-gandhi-voice-insights.csv`):\n- He concentrates once he is in. He took 83% of CrowdStrike's 2013 round, then led or co-led every round to the IPO ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240623233407/https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/FINAL2-2021-09-01-CrowdStrike-Accel-Memo-redacted-1.pdf), [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/live-at-accel-during-the-rsa-conference-crowdstrikes-george-kurtz-on-the-security-arms-race)).\n- He forms the thesis first and chases the team second, even when the founder will not meet investors ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/crwd-solving-the-hard-problems-first)).\n- He openly misjudged Flipkart's capital needs by about 10x ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/flipkart-realizing-the-india-opportunity)).\n- He re-ups with founders across companies (Drako, Jackson, Lore, Karpenko) and across firms (Dropbox, from Sequoia to Accel).\n- His current frontier thesis is data-bound physical AI with vertical integration ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/mind-robotics-bringing-ai-to-the-physical-world)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is the senior Accel name on the firm's largest frontier and physical-AI bets: Anthropic (first invested 2025, with Arun Mathew) ([src](https://www.accel.com/companies/anthropic)), Perplexity (2025; Accel reported as lead of the ~$14B round) ([src](https://www.accel.com/companies/perplexity)), and the Mind Robotics $500M Series A, co-led with a16z, where he holds the board seat ([src](https://siliconangle.com/2026/03/11/rivians-industrial-automation-spinoff-mind-robotics-secures-500m-funding/)). His habit of very large single-firm checks ($25M of $30M in 2013) fits the owner's $20M-into-big-seed style, making him a natural co-investor on mega-seeds for new labs. Still, he works at growth and late stage, and his public voice is mostly security and SaaS, so he is not an early-research-talent scout. The rising juniors on his recent deals (Ben Quazzo, Josh Fang) may be the higher-fit follow-ups.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Maryland ([src](https://www.accel.com/team/sameer-gandhi)).\n- MIT: BS and MS in electrical engineering, plus an MS in computer science. Stanford GSB: MBA ([src](https://www.crowdstrike.com/en-gb/about-us/board-of-directors/sameer-gandhi/)).\n- Worked at Oracle and then at Broadview International, a tech M&A bank (dates not given) ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20141029044356/http://www.accel.com:80/people/sameer-gandhi)).\n- Partner at Sequoia Capital for close to 10 years ([src](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/sameer-gandhi)). There he led Barracuda Networks, Dropbox, eHarmony, Gracenote, Sourcefire and Trulia ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20141029044356/http://www.accel.com:80/people/sameer-gandhi)). Start year 1998 is from an aggregator only and is unconfirmed.\n- Joined Accel in 2008 ([src](https://www.accel.com/team/sameer-gandhi)).\n- Board member and former President of the Western Association of Venture Capitalists. Named multiple times to the Forbes Midas List ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20141029044356/http://www.accel.com:80/people/sameer-gandhi)).\n- Sits on the CrowdStrike and Freshworks boards ([src](https://www.crowdstrike.com/en-gb/about-us/board-of-directors/sameer-gandhi/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.accel.com/team/sameer-gandhi | Accel team page with linked memos |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.accel.com/news | Accel \"Noteworthies\" memos (2017-2026). No personal blog found |\n| X | https://twitter.com/skg_accel | Linked from Accel page; posts not reviewed (no access) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/skgandhi/ | Linked from Accel page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/wonder-marc-lore | Hosts some Accel \"Spotlight On\" episodes (2024-2025) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2013-03: YourStory YSTV, on India, Accel's US growth fund and big data ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nyUhyBgGYE)). Not mined (no transcript).\n- 2013-04: theCUBE at the Accel Partners Symposium, with Dropbox's Aditya Agarwal ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sWLhPC16r_o)). Not mined.\n- 2018-06: FactorDaily Outliers #61, on lessons from Dropbox, Spotify and DJI. He calls founders \"the spiritual core\" and stresses the founder-to-CEO shift ([src](https://podcast.factordaily.com/outliers-61-sameer-gandhi-shares-lessons-from-the-startup-journeys-of-dropbox-spotify-and-dji/)).\n- 2021-06: eComm Connect keynote with Marc Lore, moderated by The Economist's Ludwig Siegele ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/seven-takeaways-from-ecomm-connect)).\n- 2021-09: 20VC \"The CrowdStrike Memo\", on leading internal rounds, telling Kurtz to shop his term sheet, and market sizing ([src](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/sameer-gandhi)). No transcript found; the memo itself was mined.\n- 2024-04: Spotlight On S2E5, CrowdStrike's George Kurtz ([src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/crowdstrike-george-kurtz)).\n- 2024-06: Spotlight On S2E13, Wonder's Marc Lore ([src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/wonder-marc-lore)).\n- 2025-06: Spotlight On S3E14, live at RSA with Kurtz. Covers the origin story, the valuation joke and cloud-only discipline ([src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/live-at-accel-during-the-rsa-conference-crowdstrikes-george-kurtz-on-the-security-arms-race)).\n- 2025-07: Spotlight On S3E17, Freshworks CEO Dennis Woodside, on founder succession ([src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/freshworks-ceo-dennis-woodside-on-translating-bold-visions-into-operational-excellence)).\n\n## How they think\n- **Founder first, market second.** He backed Dropbox after 30 minutes in a dead category, and Flipkart while misjudging its capital needs by about 10x.\n- **Thesis-led hunting.** In 2013 he decided antivirus was obsolete, then canvassed for a team ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/crwd-solving-the-hard-problems-first)).\n- **Concentrate in winners.** At CrowdStrike, Accel led or co-led every round and also bought in the IPO.\n- **Solve the hard problem first.** \"no quick fixes to hard problems if you want to take the long view\" ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/crwd-solving-the-hard-problems-first)). He prefers native platforms to stitched acquisitions.\n- **Succession realism.** \"the people that got you to a certain place aren't always the people\" ([src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/freshworks-ceo-dennis-woodside-on-translating-bold-visions-into-operational-excellence), 28:25).\n- **Contrarian calls that were right:** cloud-and-ML endpoint security (2013), a Chennai SaaS company (2011 seed), and \"all you can eat\" streaming (Spotify 2011).\n- **Calls that were wrong:** Flipkart's capital need ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/flipkart-realizing-the-india-opportunity)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. The CrowdStrike IC memo (Aug 2013): real check size, price and diligence list ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240623233407/https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/FINAL2-2021-09-01-CrowdStrike-Accel-Memo-redacted-1.pdf)).\n2. \"Flipkart: Realizing the India Opportunity\" (2018): seed size and the admitted 10x misjudgment ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/flipkart-realizing-the-india-opportunity)).\n3. \"The Dropbox IPO\" (2018): his 2007 seed decision ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/the-dropbox-ipo)).\n4. Spotlight On, live at RSA (2025): the origin story and board dynamics in his own voice ([src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/live-at-accel-during-the-rsa-conference-crowdstrikes-george-kurtz-on-the-security-arms-race)).\n5. \"Mind Robotics: Bringing AI to the Physical World\" (2026): his current AI thesis ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/mind-robotics-bringing-ai-to-the-physical-world)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. His X account (@skg_accel) is linked from Accel but could not be read, and his public voice runs through Accel memos and Accel-hosted podcasts.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Dropbox | Seed (at Sequoia) | 2007 | yes | | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20161102211254/http://www.accel.com:80/team/sameer-gandhi) |\n| Sourcefire | Undisclosed (at Sequoia) | 2004 | unknown | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/the-power-of-persistence) |\n| Flipkart | Seed ($800K) | 2008 | yes (Accel) | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/flipkart-realizing-the-india-opportunity) |\n| Sonatype | Undisclosed | 2010 | unknown | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/the-power-of-persistence) |\n| Spotify | Undisclosed (Accel entry) | 2011 | unknown | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/spotifys-direct-listing) |\n| Freshworks | Seed | 2011 | yes (Accel, first investor) | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/freshworks-chennai-a-decade-of-aspirations-and-accomplishments) |\n| Plex Systems | Undisclosed | 2012 | unknown | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/plex-made-in-detroit) |\n| CrowdStrike | Series B ($30M at $143M pre; Accel $25M) | 2013-08 | yes | Warburg Pincus (existing) | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240623233407/https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/FINAL2-2021-09-01-CrowdStrike-Accel-Memo-redacted-1.pdf) |\n| Rylo | Seed (formation) | 2015 | unknown | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/introducing-rylo) |\n| Eagle Eye Networks | Undisclosed | 2020-10 | unknown | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/pioneering-the-video-surveillance-industrys-movement-to-the-cloud-our-investment-in-eagle-eye-networks) |\n| Nimble Robotics | Series A ($50M) | 2021-03 | no | | [src](https://www.accel.com/companies/nimble-robotics) |\n| Helsing | Series C (EUR450M) | 2024-07 | no | General Catalyst (lead), Lightspeed, Plural | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2024/07/11/defense-technology-startup-helsing-raises-e450m-reported-e5b-valuation) |\n| Anthropic | Undisclosed (Accel entry) | 2025 | no | | [src](https://www.accel.com/companies/anthropic) |\n| Perplexity | Series E ($14B) | 2025 | reported lead (Accel) | | [src](https://www.accel.com/companies/perplexity) |\n| Mind Robotics | Series A ($500M) | 2026-03 | co-led | Andreessen Horowitz | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2026/03/11/rivians-industrial-automation-spinoff-mind-robotics-secures-500m-funding/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **CrowdStrike (2013).** By early 2013 Accel had decided signature-based antivirus was finished and went looking for a team. It heard of a secretive Irvine company, and Kurtz would not meet investors ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/crwd-solving-the-hard-problems-first)). Accel \"hounded\" him, flew down, and he turned up 30 minutes late with dilated eyes. The meeting ran two hours ([src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/live-at-accel-during-the-rsa-conference-crowdstrikes-george-kurtz-on-the-security-arms-race), 01:52). The memo shows $30M at $143M pre, with Accel taking $25M and Warburg as the prior $25M investor ([src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240623233407/https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/FINAL2-2021-09-01-CrowdStrike-Accel-Memo-redacted-1.pdf)). Accel then led or co-led every round and bought in the IPO. On 20VC, Gandhi said he told Kurtz to shop his term sheet; that is from the show notes only, and no transcript was found ([src](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/sameer-gandhi)).\n- **Dropbox (2007).** He met Houston and Ferdowsi fresh out of Y Combinator. The demo synced a photo from the first iPhone to a Mac and a Windows laptop, and he decided within 30 minutes ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/the-dropbox-ipo)). He led the seed at Sequoia, then moved to Accel in 2008, the year of the Series A.\n- **Flipkart (2008).** The seed came from Accel India's new $65M fund. Accel assumed about $500M would be needed to break even and was about 10x low ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/flipkart-realizing-the-india-opportunity)).\n- **Sonatype (2010).** Wayne Jackson, his Sourcefire CEO, brought the company to Accel. The financing was conditioned on Jackson becoming CEO ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/the-power-of-persistence)).\n- **Eagle Eye Networks (2020).** It came through Dean Drako, whose Barracuda Networks Gandhi first backed at Sequoia ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/pioneering-the-video-surveillance-industrys-movement-to-the-cloud-our-investment-in-eagle-eye-networks)).\n- **Mind Robotics (2026).** Accel co-led the $500M Series A of RJ Scaringe's Rivian spinout, and Gandhi joined the board ([src](https://www.accel.com/news/mind-robotics-bringing-ai-to-the-physical-world)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sequoia Capital | Partner (close to 10 years) | to 2008 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20141029044356/http://www.accel.com:80/people/sameer-gandhi) |\n| Accel | Partner | 2008 | [src](https://www.accel.com/team/sameer-gandhi) |\n| George Kurtz | CrowdStrike investor and board member | 2013 | [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/live-at-accel-during-the-rsa-conference-crowdstrikes-george-kurtz-on-the-security-arms-race) |\n| Drew Houston / Arash Ferdowsi | Seed investor at Sequoia, then Accel | 2007 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/the-dropbox-ipo) |\n| Dean Drako | Barracuda (Sequoia), then Eagle Eye (Accel) | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/pioneering-the-video-surveillance-industrys-movement-to-the-cloud-our-investment-in-eagle-eye-networks) |\n| Wayne Jackson | Sourcefire (2004), then Sonatype (2010) | 2004 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/the-power-of-persistence) |\n| Marc Lore | Backed Diapers.com, Jet and Wonder | | [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/wonder-marc-lore) |\n| Alex Karpenko | Tracked from Stanford PhD; Rylo | 2011 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/introducing-rylo) |\n| RJ Scaringe | Mind Robotics board | 2026 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/mind-robotics-bringing-ai-to-the-physical-world) |\n| Arun Mathew, Ben Quazzo, Josh Fang | Accel co-authors on Mind Robotics; Mathew also on Anthropic | 2025 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/mind-robotics-bringing-ai-to-the-physical-world) |\n| Sonali De Rycker | Co-led Spotify for Accel | 2011 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/spotifys-direct-listing) |\n| Shekhar Kirani, Subrata Mitra | Accel India co-leads on Freshworks and Flipkart | 2008 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/flipkart-realizing-the-india-opportunity) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Get a transcript of the 20VC CrowdStrike Memo episode (2021): the \"go shop your term sheet\" story, services-to-product ratio and market sizing.\n- Who is the \"Nate\" at the first Kurtz meeting (possibly Nate Niparko)?\n- Gandhi's exact role in Anthropic and Perplexity: which rounds, and who sourced them.\n- Sequoia start year (1998 per an aggregator) and his Sequoia-era deal list beyond the archived bio.\n- Whether his X account has any substantive threads.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.accel.com/team/sameer-gandhi\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20141029044356/http://www.accel.com:80/people/sameer-gandhi\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20161102211254/http://www.accel.com:80/team/sameer-gandhi\n- https://www.crowdstrike.com/en-gb/about-us/board-of-directors/sameer-gandhi/\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20240623233407/https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/FINAL2-2021-09-01-CrowdStrike-Accel-Memo-redacted-1.pdf\n- https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/sameer-gandhi\n- https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/live-at-accel-during-the-rsa-conference-crowdstrikes-george-kurtz-on-the-security-arms-race\n- https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/freshworks-ceo-dennis-woodside-on-translating-bold-visions-into-operational-excellence\n- https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/wonder-marc-lore\n- https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/crowdstrike-george-kurtz\n- https://www.accel.com/news/the-dropbox-ipo\n- https://www.accel.com/news/spotifys-direct-listing\n- https://www.accel.com/news/flipkart-realizing-the-india-opportunity\n- https://www.accel.com/news/crwd-solving-the-hard-problems-first\n- https://www.accel.com/news/introducing-rylo\n- https://www.accel.com/news/the-power-of-persistence\n- https://www.accel.com/news/pioneering-the-video-surveillance-industrys-movement-to-the-cloud-our-investment-in-eagle-eye-networks\n- https://www.accel.com/news/freshworks-chennai-a-decade-of-aspirations-and-accomplishments\n- https://www.accel.com/news/plex-made-in-detroit\n- https://www.accel.com/news/mind-robotics-bringing-ai-to-the-physical-world\n- https://www.accel.com/news/seven-takeaways-from-ecomm-connect\n- https://podcast.factordaily.com/outliers-61-sameer-gandhi-shares-lessons-from-the-startup-journeys-of-dropbox-spotify-and-dji/\n- https://siliconangle.com/2026/03/11/rivians-industrial-automation-spinoff-mind-robotics-secures-500m-funding/\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nyUhyBgGYE\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sWLhPC16r_o\n", "person:samir-kaul": "\n# Samir Kaul\n\n## One-line take\nKaul is the KV co-founder who argued for the 2019 OpenAI cheque, the firm's largest first cheque at the time. Across six years of interviews they describe one playbook: incubate, take technical risk instead of market risk, take big ownership, and recycle founders. That playbook explains how KV prices frontier-lab seeds like Discovery Loop.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** technical credibility (genomics researcher, Flagship company-builder, founding CEO) plus a 20-year advisor network. KV incubates when no company exists, and its incubations carry \"disproportionate ownership\". Kaul says those incubations become the one or two fund returners each fund needs [src](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/how-one-vc-navigates-todays-volatility).\n- **What constrains them:** price discipline. Kaul admits KV has lost AI deals because it would not match valuations [src](https://fullratchet.net/392-cutting-the-1st-and-largest-check-into-openai-assessing-generative-ai-moats-monopolies-and-market-opportunities-and-why-now-is-the-right-time-for-climatetech-samir-kaul/). They also say they are too slow to give up on companies.\n- **What they'd never do:** take market or adoption risk, make a \"3x investment\", follow on at plain pro rata, or compete for $100M therapeutics Series A rounds against Arch and Flagship [src](https://www.genengnews.com/gen-edge/inside-vc-samir-kaul-of-khosla-ventures-on-evaluating-and-managing-risk-in-2023/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **OpenAI was a size and structure exception.** Kaul calls it \"the largest first check, initial foray into a company that we've ever made\". They add that it had \"more of an unconventional structure at the time\" [src](https://fullratchet.net/392-cutting-the-1st-and-largest-check-into-openai-assessing-generative-ai-moats-monopolies-and-market-opportunities-and-why-now-is-the-right-time-for-climatetech-samir-kaul/) (00:08:48, 00:09:22). The host's $50M figure was not confirmed.\n- **The OpenAI thesis included hiring future founders.** KV expected OpenAI engineers who left to come to KV for funding, and to act as diligence experts in the meantime: \"we'd get a great network of people that once they leave\" (00:38:22). It is the Square-alumni playbook (DoorDash, Faire, Jackie Reses's neobank) applied to a lab [src](https://fullratchet.net/392-cutting-the-1st-and-largest-check-into-openai-assessing-generative-ai-moats-monopolies-and-market-opportunities-and-why-now-is-the-right-time-for-climatetech-samir-kaul/).\n- **Relationship before the deal:** \"we've known Sam Altman for nearly two decades\" (2023) [src](https://fullratchet.net/392-cutting-the-1st-and-largest-check-into-openai-assessing-generative-ai-moats-monopolies-and-market-opportunities-and-why-now-is-the-right-time-for-climatetech-samir-kaul/).\n- **Investment committee mechanics:** \"So we have a no veto policy.\" The four MDs score deals from 1 to 4, and one strong sponsor is enough [src](https://fullratchet.net/392-cutting-the-1st-and-largest-check-into-openai-assessing-generative-ai-moats-monopolies-and-market-opportunities-and-why-now-is-the-right-time-for-climatetech-samir-kaul/). On 20VC, explaining the no-veto rule, they suggest the company that would otherwise have been blamed on a veto was Airbnb, which implies KV passed on it. This is my reading of an ambiguous passage: \"And guess what that company was? It was Airbnb.\" [src](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/the-twenty-minute-vc-with-samir-kaul)\n- **Follow-ons:** \"I think pro-rata is a total cop-out.\" Every follow-on is either well above pro rata or close to zero [src](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/the-twenty-minute-vc-with-samir-kaul).\n- **Ownership at entry:** \"We bought 40-50% of Rocket Lab for $5 million\". Impossible was 50% for $3M; Square was \"$8-9 million down for 20% of the company\". Until OpenAI, the largest first cheque at the highest entry price was $20-25M into Stripe at $1B [src](https://www.genengnews.com/gen-edge/inside-vc-samir-kaul-of-khosla-ventures-on-evaluating-and-managing-risk-in-2023/).\n- **Repeat founders get large first cheques:** \"$10 million the first time I met him\" (Gilad Almogy). They have since done three companies together, including Ultima [src](https://www.genengnews.com/gen-edge/inside-vc-samir-kaul-of-khosla-ventures-on-evaluating-and-managing-risk-in-2023/). The same pattern shows in Rhoda AI (Jagdeep Singh, previously QuantumScape).\n- **AI dev-cloud bets:** \"we incubated with one of our partners, Nikita, called Neon\", plus Replit. Kaul also expected new inference-chip companies (2023) [src](https://fullratchet.net/392-cutting-the-1st-and-largest-check-into-openai-assessing-generative-ai-moats-monopolies-and-market-opportunities-and-why-now-is-the-right-time-for-climatetech-samir-kaul/).\n- **Painful pass, Pinterest Series A:** KV had a term sheet in but lost to a bid about $10M higher on the pre-money and held its price. Kaul's lesson is that entry price shouldn't block a deal you love. The good pass was a Theranos-style company that would not allow diligence: \"You run for the hills.\" [src](https://fullratchet.net/investor-stories-311-why-i-passed-biederman-kaul-susan/) (00:03:00; quotes from my own whisper transcription of the audio).\n- **Rule for juniors:** \"don't do a three x investment\" [src](https://fullratchet.net/392-cutting-the-1st-and-largest-check-into-openai-assessing-generative-ai-moats-monopolies-and-market-opportunities-and-why-now-is-the-right-time-for-climatetech-samir-kaul/) (00:42:35).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Kaul is above the junior cut but personally sponsors frontier-AI and AI-automation deals at seed and lead stage:\n- co-led the first round of Discovery Loop (Jeff Dean, Sanjay Ghemawat, Oriol Vinyals, Quoc Le) in 2026 [src](https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop)\n- co-led the Moonvalley seed in 2024 [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-company-moonvalley-raises-70-million-in-funding-to-develop-future-of-generative-media-302307953.html)\n- led the Norm AI Series C in 2026 [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/norm-ai-raises-120-million-at-a-1-2-billion-valuation-led-by-khosla-ventures-to-deliver-the-full-stack-model-for-legal-ai-302819152.html)\n- Rhoda AI, KV-incubated [src](https://dealroom.co/news/126719-rhoda-ai-exits-stealth-as-a-1-7b-unicorn-with-450m-to-train-robots-on-vi/)\n- was KV's public voice on OpenAI [src](https://fortune.com/2023/02/02/openais-first-vc-backer-khosla-ventures-weighs-in-on-the-future-of-generative-a-i/)\n\nTheir price discipline and taste for heavy ownership make KV a tough co-investor on crowded lab rounds. Their technical-risk-first framing fits new labs well.\n\n## Background\n- Undergraduate at the University of Michigan, class of 1995 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TAJ169LlhH8). Graduate degrees: Maryland (1997) and HBS (2002) per aggregator [src](https://in.marketscreener.com/insider/SAMIR-KAUL-A05S71/relations/) (secondary). Kaul says they left business school in 2002 [src](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/the-twenty-minute-vc-with-samir-kaul).\n- Led the Arabidopsis Genome Initiative at Craig Venter's TIGR. Seven peer-reviewed genomics papers [src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/samir-kaul).\n- Flagship Ventures, 2002 to about 2005, after an internship with Noubar Afeyan. They helped start four companies: Helicos (IPO), Codon Devices (founding CEO), and others. Vinod Khosla invested in three of them [src](https://fullratchet.net/392-cutting-the-1st-and-largest-check-into-openai-assessing-generative-ai-moats-monopolies-and-market-opportunities-and-why-now-is-the-right-time-for-climatetech-samir-kaul/). Tenure conflicts: 20VC says four years, the KV page says five.\n- Co-founded Khosla Ventures with Vinod Khosla and David Weiden. The NJ memo says 2004 [src](https://nj.gov/treasury/doinvest/pdf/AlternativeInvestments/PrivateEquity/Khosla.pdf); Kaul on 20VC says \"towards the end of 2005\" [src](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/the-twenty-minute-vc-with-samir-kaul). The NJ memo lists Kaul as responsible for healthcare.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/samir-kaul | KV bio and list of led deals |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes rarely; one Nature Food paper (2021) |\n| X | nothing found | No confirmed handle (checked 2026-11-11) |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2020-01 — 20VC — pro rata, technical vs market risk — [link](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/the-twenty-minute-vc-with-samir-kaul) — $1-2M seed option-value cheques; pro rata is a cop-out; Impossible started in their office.\n- 2022-02 — Sand Hill Road (NBC) — Impossible Investments — [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/impossible-investments-samir-kaul/id1448400925) — not yet mined.\n- 2022-06 — Instigators of Change (KV) — How one VC navigates today's volatility — [link](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/how-one-vc-navigates-todays-volatility) — incubations as fund returners; the Ultima to Mojo Vision exercise.\n- 2023-02 — GEN Edge Q&A — [link](https://www.genengnews.com/gen-edge/inside-vc-samir-kaul-of-khosla-ventures-on-evaluating-and-managing-risk-in-2023/) — entry ownership figures for Square, Stripe, Rocket Lab, Impossible and DoorDash.\n- 2023-07 — The Full Ratchet #392 — OpenAI cheque — [link](https://fullratchet.net/392-cutting-the-1st-and-largest-check-into-openai-assessing-generative-ai-moats-monopolies-and-market-opportunities-and-why-now-is-the-right-time-for-climatetech-samir-kaul/) — the richest source: OpenAI, no-veto rule, risk matrix, Neon and Replit.\n- 2024-02 — Venture Unlocked #128 — [link](https://ventureunlocked.substack.com/p/khosla-ventures-samir-kaul-on-building) — firm building; not yet mined.\n- 2026-08 — Bloomberg Tech — Discovery Loop — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3LnTUtEeZMc).\n- 2026-11 — MTS; High Flyers #291 — recent, not yet mined.\n- The full catalogue is in `data/rows/samir-kaul-voice-media.csv`.\n\n## How they think\n- **Technical risk instead of market risk.** Kaul treats delay as a known cost: $250-300K per engineer per year. Products must be a \"no-brainer\" at equal cost and equal performance [src](https://fullratchet.net/392-cutting-the-1st-and-largest-check-into-openai-assessing-generative-ai-moats-monopolies-and-market-opportunities-and-why-now-is-the-right-time-for-climatetech-samir-kaul/).\n- **Asymmetry:** downside is 1x. If more than half your deals succeed, you aren't taking enough risk. Consensus deals have already lost their alpha.\n- **Risk matrix:** retire the two or three binary risks before hiring sales. A \"gene pool matrix\" maps which industries to hire talent from.\n- **The \"what becomes possible next\" exercise:** about five years into a bet, ask what companies the bet makes possible and start them [src](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/how-one-vc-navigates-todays-volatility).\n- **Contrarian:** in 2023 they said frontier AI is not winner-take-all; they skipped crypto and quantum \"fads\".\n- **Calls:** right on Impossible, Square, Guardant and OpenAI. Wrong in the cleantech bust, when KV became the \"funder of last resort\" [src](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/the-twenty-minute-vc-with-samir-kaul).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Full Ratchet #392 transcript (2023): OpenAI cheque, IC mechanics, AI bets.\n2. 20VC transcript (2020): pro rata, seed option value, the cleantech failure.\n3. GEN Edge Q&A (2023): concrete entry cheques and ownership.\n4. Instigators of Change transcript (2022): the incubation and fund-returner logic.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No active X account was found. Their public voice comes through podcasts, KV's own channel (CEO-summit fireside chats with Jeff Weiner, Keith Rabois, Chris Britt and Margaret Heffernan) and Bloomberg TV hits on new deals.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Discovery Loop | Seed (first round) | 2026-08 | co-lead | Radical Ventures; Lightspeed; Kleiner Perkins | [src](https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop) |\n| Norm AI | Series C ($120M at $1.2B) | 2026-07 | yes | Coatue | [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/norm-ai-raises-120-million-at-a-1-2-billion-valuation-led-by-khosla-ventures-to-deliver-the-full-stack-model-for-legal-ai-302819152.html) |\n| Rhoda AI | Series A ($450M at $1.7B) | 2026-03 | unclear | Temasek; Premji Invest; Mayfield | [src](https://rhoda.ai/news/press-release) |\n| Reevo | Series A | 2025-11 | co-led | Kleiner Perkins | [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-11-05/khosla-ventures-and-kleiner-perkins-back-ai-startup-reevo) |\n| Moonvalley | Seed ($70M) | 2024-11 | co-led | General Catalyst; Bessemer | [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-company-moonvalley-raises-70-million-in-funding-to-develop-future-of-generative-media-302307953.html) |\n| Ultima Genomics | first cheque $10M | undated | yes | | [src](https://www.genengnews.com/gen-edge/inside-vc-samir-kaul-of-khosla-ventures-on-evaluating-and-managing-risk-in-2023/) |\n| Impossible Foods | Seed ($3M for ~50%) | ~2011 | yes | | [src](https://www.genengnews.com/gen-edge/inside-vc-samir-kaul-of-khosla-ventures-on-evaluating-and-managing-risk-in-2023/) |\n| Guardant, Nutanix, Oscar, QuantumScape, Granular, Varda, Mariana Minerals, Primer, Mainspring | led (per KV page) | various | yes | | [src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/samir-kaul) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **OpenAI (2019):** KV's largest first cheque at the time, with an unconventional structure. The decision rested on a roughly 20-year relationship with Altman and a talent-network thesis. Fortune reports Vinod Khosla led it; Kaul says KV does not credit deals to individuals [src](https://fortune.com/2023/02/02/openais-first-vc-backer-khosla-ventures-weighs-in-on-the-future-of-generative-a-i/).\n- **Impossible Foods:** Pat Brown worked out of Kaul's office on day one. KV put in the first $3M for about 50%. Kaul credits their wife with keeping them in sustainability after the cleantech bust [src](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/the-twenty-minute-vc-with-samir-kaul).\n- **Square (2008/09):** a ~$30M Series A when the company was \"Jack and Jim and a PowerPoint\". It later produced the DoorDash first cheque (Tony Xu, $1-1.5M for ~10%) [src](https://www.genengnews.com/gen-edge/inside-vc-samir-kaul-of-khosla-ventures-on-evaluating-and-managing-risk-in-2023/).\n- **Neon:** incubated inside KV with partner Nikita Shamgunov [src](https://fullratchet.net/392-cutting-the-1st-and-largest-check-into-openai-assessing-generative-ai-moats-monopolies-and-market-opportunities-and-why-now-is-the-right-time-for-climatetech-samir-kaul/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Vinod Khosla | KV co-founder; backed three of Kaul's Flagship companies | 2002 | [src](https://fullratchet.net/392-cutting-the-1st-and-largest-check-into-openai-assessing-generative-ai-moats-monopolies-and-market-opportunities-and-why-now-is-the-right-time-for-climatetech-samir-kaul/) |\n| David Weiden | KV co-founder | 2004/05 | [src](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/the-twenty-minute-vc-with-samir-kaul) |\n| Noubar Afeyan / Flagship | first boss, mentor | 2002 | [src](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/the-twenty-minute-vc-with-samir-kaul) |\n| Steve Quake | co-founded Helicos plus three companies | 2002 | [src](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/the-twenty-minute-vc-with-samir-kaul) |\n| Sam Altman | known ~20 years (said 2023) | ~2003 | [src](https://fullratchet.net/392-cutting-the-1st-and-largest-check-into-openai-assessing-generative-ai-moats-monopolies-and-market-opportunities-and-why-now-is-the-right-time-for-climatetech-samir-kaul/) |\n| Gilad Almogy | three companies including Ultima | | [src](https://www.genengnews.com/gen-edge/inside-vc-samir-kaul-of-khosla-ventures-on-evaluating-and-managing-risk-in-2023/) |\n| Craig Venter | early mentor at TIGR (attended Kaul's wedding) | 1990s | [src](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/how-one-vc-navigates-todays-volatility) |\n| Keith Rabois | former and returning KV partner; joint KV talk 2023 | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EpmVb1rvnBA) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact OpenAI cheque size and structure (the host's $50M is unconfirmed).\n- Is the Airbnb reference on 20VC a real KV pass, and who sponsored it?\n- The Full Ratchet \"Post Mortems\", \"Lessons Learned\" and \"Strange & Unusual\" segments (2023), plus Venture Unlocked and Sand Hill Road, are not transcribed yet. They probably name specific failures. \"Why I Passed\" is done: the Pinterest pass.\n- The 2026 appearances (MTS, High Flyers #291 on \"losing millions\", TBPN) may cover how Discovery Loop was sourced.\n- How Kaul and Strohband split AI deals inside KV (the NJ memo names Strohband as the AI MD).\n\n## Sources\n- https://khoslaventures.com/posts/the-twenty-minute-vc-with-samir-kaul\n- https://khoslaventures.com/posts/how-one-vc-navigates-todays-volatility\n- https://fullratchet.net/392-cutting-the-1st-and-largest-check-into-openai-assessing-generative-ai-moats-monopolies-and-market-opportunities-and-why-now-is-the-right-time-for-climatetech-samir-kaul/\n- https://www.genengnews.com/gen-edge/inside-vc-samir-kaul-of-khosla-ventures-on-evaluating-and-managing-risk-in-2023/\n- https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/samir-kaul\n- https://nj.gov/treasury/doinvest/pdf/AlternativeInvestments/PrivateEquity/Khosla.pdf\n- https://fortune.com/2023/02/02/openais-first-vc-backer-khosla-ventures-weighs-in-on-the-future-of-generative-a-i/\n- https://www.discoveryloop.com/blog/announcing-discovery-loop\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TAJ169LlhH8\n", "person:sandesh-patnam": "\n# Sandesh Patnam\n\n## One-line take\nThe decision-maker for an evergreen balance sheet who comes into Series A rounds of AI-chip and vertical-AI companies when he already knows the founder.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he can promise never to need an exit, and he underwrites private AI-hardware companies against the public comparables he also manages. His bio has him \"running both the private and listed equities investing practice in the US\" ([bio](https://www.premjiinvest.com/team/sandesh-patnam)).\n- **What constrains him:** a small team and a habit of waiting. Premji watched robotics for years before Rhoda, and tracked inference companies for 21 months before Baseten.\n- **What he'd never do:** fund a competitor of an existing holding (firm policy, stated in the Baseten post).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Repeat founders are the sourcing engine.** \"Upscale AI's founders previously architected Innovium, and we backed them then\" ([post, 2026-06-22](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/premji-invest-upscale-ai-partnership)). The same pair, Rajiv Khemani and Barun Kar, founded Auradine/Velaura, also a Premji holding.\n- **Hippocratic was a relationship bet.** He had known Munjal Shah \"for nearly 2 decades now\" and sat with the team \"when they were whiteboarding different ideas nearly 12 months back\" ([post, 2024-03-21](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/our-conviction-in-hippocratic-ai)).\n- **He admits the firm's former view on robotics.** \"robotics sat at the bottom of our list\" ([Rhoda post](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/goodbye-vlas-why-we-led-rhodas-series-a)).\n- **No fund clock.** \"We answer to a foundation built to exist in perpetuity\" ([2026-01-15](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/the-long-game-building-the-future-of-premji-invest)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High** as a co-investor in large seed and Series A rounds of AI-chip and compute companies: Upscale AI, Velaura, Tensordyne, CScale, Rhoda. He is not a first-cheque seed investor, but he comes in at A with size and stays.\n\n## Background\nFrom his bio ([src](https://www.premjiinvest.com/team/sandesh-patnam)); no dates are given.\n- Engineering and business roles at Cirrus Logic, Integrated Device Technologies, Silicon Graphics, Malleable Technologies.\n- TeleSoft Partners: led systems and components investing (Aarohi, Validity Sensors).\n- Bay Partners: General Partner, IT infrastructure and SaaS (Enphase Energy, ParAccel; involved in Lending Club, MuleSoft, Dynatrace, Guidewire).\n- Seligman Technology Group at Ameriprise: senior equity analyst, software and IT infrastructure.\n- Premji Invest: joined to open the US office; now Managing Partner.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.premjiinvest.com/team/sandesh-patnam | Bio, board seats, checked 2026-11-11 |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/the-long-game-building-the-future-of-premji-invest | Posts under his byline on the firm blog, a few per quarter |\n| X | not found | |\n| LinkedIn | linked from his bio page | not opened |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-05 — 20VC — The Downstream Effects of SpaceX, OpenAI, and Anthropic Soaking Up $3T in the Public Market — [repost](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/the-downstream-effects-of-spacex-openai-and-anthropic-soaking-up-3t-in-the-public-market-who-is-netscape-and-who-is-google-in-the-ai-era-and-the-impact-of-private-credit-redemption-requests-on-pe-and-vc-sandesh-patnam) — not mined\n- 2026-07 — Premji video — Robotics isn't having its ChatGPT moment just yet — [link](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/robotics-isnt-having-its-chatgpt-moment-just-yet) — not mined\n- 2026-03 — Premji video — Why I take founders on a 3-mile hike before writing a check — [link](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/why-i-take-founders-on-a-3-mile-hike-before-writing-a-check) — not mined\n- 2026-01 — Premji video — How Great Investors Spot Real Moats in AI — [link](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/how-great-investors-spot-real-moats-in-ai) — not mined\n- 2025-09 — Premji video — The Anti-Exit Strategy — [link](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/the-anti-exit-strategy-premji-invests-approach-to-evergreen-capital) — not mined\n\n## How they think\n- Vertical AI needs its own data and model work; thin layers on model providers are avoided.\n- Semis and networking for AI are a core theme, reached through founders he has backed before.\n- He calls the team \"truth-seekers\" who stay unconvinced longer than others (Jan 2026 post).\n- Call he revised: robotics, from the bottom of the list to leading Rhoda's A (per Premji).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [The Long Game](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/the-long-game-building-the-future-of-premji-invest): the evergreen structure and how he hires.\n2. [Goodbye VLAs](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/goodbye-vlas-why-we-led-rhodas-series-a): why he changed his mind on robotics.\n3. [Upscale AI partnership](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/premji-invest-upscale-ai-partnership): the repeat-founder pattern.\n4. [Our conviction in Hippocratic AI](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/our-conviction-in-hippocratic-ai): the relationship route into a hot A.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found on X. His public output is the firm blog and video series.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Hippocratic AI | Series A ($53M) | 2024-03 | co-led | General Catalyst, a16z | [post](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/our-conviction-in-hippocratic-ai) |\n| Upscale AI | Series A; A-1 ($190M) | 2026-01; 2026-06 | co-led; led | Tiger Global, Xora, Mayfield, NVIDIA | [post](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/premji-invest-upscale-ai-partnership) |\n| Rhoda | Series A ($450M) | 2026-03 | led per Premji | Khosla, Mayfield, Temasek | [post](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/goodbye-vlas-why-we-led-rhodas-series-a) |\n| Pramaana Labs | Seed ($27M) | 2026-06 | no | Khosla (lead), Accel | [post](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/formal-verification-just-left-the-lab-why-we-invested-in-pramaana-labs) |\n| CScale | not named | 2026-09 | not stated | not stated | [post](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/why-we-invested-in-cscale-for-reliable-scale-up-optics) |\n| Writer | Series C ($200M) | 2024-11 | co-led | Radical, ICONIQ | [bio](https://www.premjiinvest.com/team/sandesh-patnam) |\n| Innovium, Cohesity, DataStax, Ikigai, Anaplan, Coupa, Looker | earlier Premji deals he led | not stated | led | not stated | [bio](https://www.premjiinvest.com/team/sandesh-patnam) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Hippocratic AI:** see above; relationship first, a year of whiteboarding, then a co-lead at the A.\n- **Upscale AI:** backed the founders at Innovium (sold to Marvell), co-led the A, then led the A-1 five months later.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Munjal Shah | founder he has known about two decades | not stated | [post](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/our-conviction-in-hippocratic-ai) |\n| Rajiv Khemani, Barun Kar | backed at Innovium, Auradine/Velaura, Upscale AI | not stated | [post](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/premji-invest-upscale-ai-partnership) |\n| Khosla Ventures, Mayfield | regular co-investors | not stated | [post](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/goodbye-vlas-why-we-led-rhodas-series-a) |\n| Jake Epstein, Vedant Agrawal | his early-stage leads | 2026 | [post](https://www.premjiinvest.com/blog/the-long-game-building-the-future-of-premji-invest) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When he joined Premji, and his cheque ceiling at Series A.\n- What he said on 20VC about cheque size and public-market crowding (not mined).\n\n## Sources\nPremji Invest bio and posts linked above.\n", "person:sangeen-zeb": "\n# Sangeen Zeb\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. The shared WebSearch budget was used up before this profile started, so it relies on direct fetches only: his GV profile, his two GV posts, Global Venturing, company blogs, Wikipedia, CNBC, TechCrunch, Yahoo Finance and existing repo files. I found no podcasts, X or LinkedIn posts. Treat gaps as \"not checked\", not \"doesn't exist\".*\n\n## One-line take\nHe has the strongest AI deal record at GV below Managing Partner. He led OpenEvidence's Series B and joined its board, co-led Sierra's Series E, sits on the Harvey and Vercel boards, and has Thinking Machines Lab on his list. He became GP about 18 months after joining GV. He is the GV person to know for growth allocations to AI apps and frontier labs, not for seeds ([src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb), [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sangeen-zeb-general-partner-gv/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He is a growth-equity investor (Summit, Centerview, Founders Circle) running GV's AI-application book. He can put GV's balance sheet behind category leaders once they break out. At OpenEvidence he combined that with GV's in-house physicians, who were already daily users, and closed the deal in one day ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/openevidence-ai-doctors)).\n- **What constrains him.** His entries come after product-market fit:\n  - OpenEvidence: entered at the Series B, after Sequoia's $75M Series A at $1B ([src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/01/21/openevidence-chatgpt-for-doctors-doubles-valuation-to-12-billion.html)).\n  - Harvey: GV led the Series C ([src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-c)).\n  - Sierra: co-led the Series E ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/sierra-ai-giving-the-enterprise-a-voice)).\n  - Thinking Machines Lab: GV joined the a16z-led $2B seed as a participant ([src](https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/accel-reportedly-talks-lead-1b-193629728.html)).\n  - I found no sourced case of him being first institutional money in an AI company.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred, unsourced).** Lead a pre-product research lab at formation. GV's lab leads (Flapping, Recursive) went to Managing Partners Munichiello and Hulme (see `firms/gv.md`).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **OpenEvidence was won by GV's physicians, not by a cold pitch.** GV's life-sciences team, who are doctors, heard about the product from colleagues at medical institutions and started using it. Krishna Yeshwant used it daily. Six GV people spent a full day with Daniel Nadler and offered to invest that evening. GV had also backed Nadler's first company, Kensho, in 2013. Zeb's own framing of the product: \"You don't pay, you just start using it.\" ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/openevidence-ai-doctors), 2025-07-15)\n- **Board seat at OpenEvidence.** Zeb wrote that he is \"excited to join the board\" ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/openevidence-ai-doctors)).\n- **His growth claim, made in 2025:** \"This is the fastest-growing healthcare application ever built\" ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/openevidence-ai-doctors)). Marks since then back it: $3.5B at GV's entry in July 2025, then $6B (Oct 2025), $12B (Jan 2026) and $15B (Sep 2026) ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenEvidence)).\n- **The Thinking Machines ticket was a seed follow, not a lead.** GV was in the $2B seed at $12B: \"joined by Nvidia, GV, Lightspeed, and Conviction Partners\" ([src](https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/accel-reportedly-talks-lead-1b-193629728.html), 2026-09-04). The company is on Zeb's GV list ([src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb)). No source names him as the person who ran it.\n- **SSI appears in his bio prose but not on his investment list.** That could mean an undisclosed position or a stale bio; it is unconfirmed ([src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb)).\n- **He was hired for fintech and enterprise, not AI.** Global Venturing describes him as joining \"as a partner specialising in enterprise software and financial technology\" ([src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sangeen-zeb-general-partner-gv/), 2023-01-19). The AI title came later, as GV's AI-app book grew.\n- **Correction to the brief.** He is not the only non-MP at GV with a frontier lab on their list. Partner Elena Sakach has humans&, which calls itself a frontier AI lab. He is the only *General Partner* with one ([src](https://www.gv.com/team/elena-sakach), [src](https://humansand.ai/)).\n- **Harvey: GV is missing from the latest round.** The Sep 2026 $550M round at $15.5B lists Sequoia, Kleiner, a16z, Coatue and others as existing investors, but not GV. Zeb's bio still lists the Harvey board seat ([src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-dollar550m-at-a-dollar155b-valuation-to-help-legal-teams-own-their-intelligence), [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb)). It's unclear whether GV did not take pro rata or simply wasn't listed.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **Against:** almost all of his book is growth-stage AI applications: OpenEvidence B, Harvey C, Sierra E, Hebbia B. THESIS.md treats that as low.\n- **For:**\n  - He controls GV's ticket into frontier-lab mega-rounds. Thinking Machines Lab is on his list, and GV was in its $2B seed at $12B, a $600M-scale \"seed\" of exactly the type in THESIS.md ([src](https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/accel-reportedly-talks-lead-1b-193629728.html)).\n  - SSI is named in his bio.\n  - If I'm writing ~$20M into a large lab seed, he is the likely GV co-investor or follow-on capital.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** Thinking Machines Lab, SSI (unconfirmed), and OpenEvidence as a model of how GV uses in-house domain experts to pre-diligence a deal.\n\n## Background\n- **Summit Partners:** started his investing career there (dates not sourced) ([src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb)).\n- **Centerview Capital Technology:** more than five years, senior associate then principal ([src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sangeen-zeb-general-partner-gv/)).\n- **Founders Circle Capital (growth equity):** partner for more than two years. Helped lead investments in Databricks, Robinhood, Confluent, Segment, UiPath and Cohesity ([src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sangeen-zeb-general-partner-gv/), [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb)).\n- **GV, partner:** joined about 18 months before January 2023, so roughly mid-2021, focused on enterprise software and fintech ([src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sangeen-zeb-general-partner-gv/)).\n- **GV, General Partner:** promoted 2023-01-18. The article is dated 2023-01-19 and says \"yesterday\" ([src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sangeen-zeb-general-partner-gv/)).\n- **Education:** nothing found.\n- **Promotion speed:** fast_promotion is `no` under the repo definition (associate to partner in about 4 years). He spent 5+ years at Centerview before becoming a partner elsewhere. The fast step was partner to GP at GV in about 18 months.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb | GV profile, read 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | GV news posts by him: [OpenEvidence](https://www.gv.com/news/openevidence-ai-doctors) (2025-07-15), [Sierra](https://www.gv.com/news/sierra-ai-giving-the-enterprise-a-voice) (2026-05-04) | Writes the announcement post for deals he leads |\n| X | nothing found | The GV profile links only to @gvteam |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/sangeen-zeb-891b413/ | Linked from his GV profile; not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. Search budget was exhausted, so this is not checked rather than confirmed absent.\n\n## How they think\n- **Product pull over sales.** In the OpenEvidence post he stresses free, viral use by physicians: \"You don't pay, you just start using it.\" ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/openevidence-ai-doctors))\n- **Enterprise agents as a category.** The Sierra post opens: \"The era of the enterprise agent is officially here\" ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/sierra-ai-giving-the-enterprise-a-voice)).\n- **Calls right so far:**\n  - OpenEvidence: about 4.3x from the $3.5B entry mark to $15B in 14 months ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenEvidence)).\n  - Harvey: $1.5B at GV's Series C lead to $15.5B ([src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-c), [src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-dollar550m-at-a-dollar155b-valuation-to-help-legal-teams-own-their-intelligence)). That he personally ran the Harvey C is inferred from his board seat; no source names him.\n- **Contrarian views:** nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [OpenEvidence: AI for doctors](https://www.gv.com/news/openevidence-ai-doctors) (2025-07-15): the best record of how GV's experts pre-diligence a deal and how fast he closed.\n2. [Sierra: Giving the Enterprise a Voice](https://www.gv.com/news/sierra-ai-giving-the-enterprise-a-voice) (2026-05-04): his Series E co-lead thesis on enterprise agents.\n3. [Global Venturing on his GP promotion](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sangeen-zeb-general-partner-gv/) (2023-01-19): career path and his early GV deals (Chronosphere, Courier, Lacework, Medallion).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. I found no personal X account, and the GV profile links only to the firm account.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenEvidence | Series B, $210M at $3.5B | 2025-07-15 | Yes (GV and Kleiner Perkins co-led per Wikipedia/Modern Healthcare). Zeb on board | Kleiner Perkins, Coatue, Conviction, Thrive | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/openevidence-ai-doctors), [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenEvidence) |\n| Sierra | Series E (size not stated) | 2026-05-04 | Co-led | Other co-lead not named | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/sierra-ai-giving-the-enterprise-a-voice) |\n| Harvey | Series C, $100M at $1.5B (GV lead; Zeb not named). Zeb on board | 2024-07-23 | GV led | OpenAI, Kleiner Perkins, Sequoia, Elad Gil, SV Angel | [src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-c), [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb) |\n| Harvey | Series D $300M at $3B; Series E $300M at $5B (GV participant) | 2025-02-12; 2025-06-23 | No | See companies/harvey.md | [src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-d), [src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-e) |\n| Thinking Machines Lab | Seed, $2B at $12B (GV participant) | 2025-07 | No (a16z led) | Nvidia, Lightspeed, Conviction | [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/accel-reportedly-talks-lead-1b-193629728.html), [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb) |\n| Hebbia | Series B, $130M at ~$700M (GV participant) | 2024-07 | No (a16z led) | Index Ventures, Peter Thiel | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/07/09/ai-startup-hebbia-rased-130m-at-a-700m-valuation-on-13-million-of-profitable-revenue/), [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb) |\n| Vercel | Round not confirmed. Zeb on board | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb) |\n| Safe Superintelligence | Named in bio prose, not on list. Unconfirmed | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb) |\n| Chronosphere (exited) | Round not stated (company raised $115M Jan 2023) | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb), [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sangeen-zeb-general-partner-gv/) |\n| GitLab (exited) | Round not stated | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb) |\n| Medallion | Round not stated | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb) |\n| Fabric | Round not stated | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb) |\n| dope.security | Round not stated | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb) |\n| Courier | Round not stated | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sangeen-zeb-general-partner-gv/) |\n| Lacework | Round not stated | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sangeen-zeb-general-partner-gv/) |\n| Databricks, Robinhood, Confluent, Segment, UiPath, Cohesity | At Founders Circle (\"helped lead\") | before 2021 | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb) |\n\n## Deal stories\n### OpenEvidence (standout deal)\n- **Before GV.** Daniel Nadler founded OpenEvidence in 2022 with Harvard ML researcher Zack Ziegler ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenEvidence)). Sequoia led a $75M round at $1B in February 2025 ([src](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/02/19/ai-startup-openevidence-secures-sequoia-funding-1-billion-valuation.html)), so GV was not first.\n- **How GV found it.** Two paths ran in parallel ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/openevidence-ai-doctors)):\n  - Zeb had met Nadler. When is not stated.\n  - GV's physician-heavy life-sciences team heard about the product from colleagues at medical institutions and began using it. Krishna Yeshwant (a physician, Managing Partner) used it daily.\n  - GV also had history with Nadler: it backed his first company, Kensho, in 2013.\n- **The close.** Six GV people spent a full day with Nadler and offered to invest by the end of that evening ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/openevidence-ai-doctors)).\n- **The round.** $210M Series B at $3.5B, co-led by GV and Kleiner Perkins, with Coatue, Conviction and Thrive participating ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenEvidence), citing Modern Healthcare). GV's post was published 2025-07-15, Zeb wrote it, and he joined the board ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/openevidence-ai-doctors)).\n- **Since then:**\n  - $200M at $6B, Oct 2025 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenEvidence)).\n  - $250M at $12B led by Thrive and DST, Jan 2026 ([src](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/01/21/openevidence-chatgpt-for-doctors-doubles-valuation-to-12-billion.html)).\n  - $250M at $15B, Sep 2026 ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenEvidence)).\n- **What it's worth now.** About 4.3x the entry valuation on paper in about 14 months. GV's ticket size and ownership are not disclosed.\n\n### Sierra\n- GV co-led the Series E, and Zeb wrote the post dated 2026-05-04: \"we are co-leading Sierra's Series E financing\" ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/sierra-ai-giving-the-enterprise-a-voice)).\n- Round size, valuation and the other lead were not found.\n- Sierra says it passed $400M+ ARR in October 2026 ([src](https://sierra.ai/blog/our-customers-ambition-is-accelerating-our-growth)).\n- Whether GV held earlier positions in Sierra is not found.\n\n### Harvey\n- GV led the $100M Series C at $1.5B on 2024-07-23 ([src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-c)), then participated in the D and E (see `companies/harvey.md`).\n- No source names Zeb on the round. His Harvey board seat is the link ([src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb)).\n\n### Thinking Machines Lab\n- GV participated in the a16z-led $2B seed at $12B ([src](https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/accel-reportedly-talks-lead-1b-193629728.html)).\n- The deal is on Zeb's list, but how GV got its allocation is not found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| GV | General Partner (AI) | partner ~2021, GP 2023-01-18 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sangeen-zeb-general-partner-gv/) |\n| Founders Circle Capital | Partner (2+ yrs) | before 2021 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sangeen-zeb-general-partner-gv/) |\n| Centerview Capital Technology | Senior associate, then principal (5+ yrs) | n/a | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sangeen-zeb-general-partner-gv/) |\n| Summit Partners | First investing job | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb) |\n| Daniel Nadler (OpenEvidence) | Board member at his company | 2025-07 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/openevidence-ai-doctors) |\n| Krishna Yeshwant (GV) | Colleague; daily OpenEvidence user who helped diligence | 2025 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/openevidence-ai-doctors) |\n| Kleiner Perkins | Co-lead on OpenEvidence Series B | 2025-07 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenEvidence) |\n| Harvey | Board member | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb) |\n| Vercel | Board member | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb) |\n| Bret Taylor, Clay Bavor (Sierra) | Investor (Series E co-lead) | 2026-05 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/sierra-ai-giving-the-enterprise-a-voice) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who co-led Sierra's Series E with GV, and at what size and valuation? Did GV hold Sierra before the E?\n- Is SSI a real GV position? It is in his bio prose but not on his list.\n- Did Zeb personally run the Thinking Machines allocation? Will GV take part in the reported Accel-led round at $40B+?\n- Why is GV missing from Harvey's Sep 2026 investor list, given his board seat?\n- Which Vercel round did GV enter, and when did Zeb join that board?\n- When and how did Zeb first meet Nadler: through Kensho, or independently?\n- Are there podcasts or talks? None were checked, because search was exhausted.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.gv.com/team/sangeen-zeb\n- https://www.gv.com/news/openevidence-ai-doctors\n- https://www.gv.com/news/sierra-ai-giving-the-enterprise-a-voice\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sangeen-zeb-general-partner-gv/\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenEvidence\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2026/01/21/openevidence-chatgpt-for-doctors-doubles-valuation-to-12-billion.html\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2025/02/19/ai-startup-openevidence-secures-sequoia-funding-1-billion-valuation.html (cited via Wikipedia, not fetched)\n- https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-c\n- https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-dollar550m-at-a-dollar155b-valuation-to-help-legal-teams-own-their-intelligence\n- https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-d and https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-e (via companies/harvey.md, not re-fetched)\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/accel-reportedly-talks-lead-1b-193629728.html\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/07/09/ai-startup-hebbia-rased-130m-at-a-700m-valuation-on-13-million-of-profitable-revenue/\n- https://sierra.ai/blog/our-customers-ambition-is-accelerating-our-growth\n- https://www.gv.com/team/elena-sakach, https://humansand.ai/\n", "person:sanjana-basu": "\n# Sanjana Basu\n\n## One-line take\nShe is Radical's longest-tenured non-founder investor and its applied-AI seed partner. She leads small, first-institutional checks into voice-AI and AI-native services companies (Ribbon, Attuned Intelligence, General Magic), and she often knows the founder in public before the company exists. [src](https://radical.vc/team/sanjana-basu/) [src](https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/voice-ai-agents-evolution-to-emerging)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** She has been at Radical for seven years and has a long public paper trail on a few theses: healthcare AI (2020–22), AI for science (2024–25), voice agents (2024–26) and tabular/causal foundation models (2026). She writes about a space, names the founders in it, and then leads their seed. The clearest case is Attuned Intelligence. Her August 2024 voice-AI essay credited Domenic Donato as \"CTO of Assembly AI building something new\" about 14 months before Radical co-led his $13M seed. [src](https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/voice-ai-agents-evolution-to-emerging) [src](https://hitconsultant.net/2025/10/07/attuned-intelligence-emerges-from-stealth-with-13m-to-automate-hospital-call-centers-with-supervised-ai)\n- **What constrains her:** Every check she is publicly named on is under $10M of round size, or a $13M co-lead. Radical's large frontier deals (Cohere, World Labs, Discovery Loop, Prime Intellect) belong to Jacobs, Toews, Rosenberg and Katz. Science deals she writes about, Nabla Bio and Orbital, carry other partners' bylines: Toews on Nabla Bio [src](https://radical.vc/articles/why-we-led-nabla-bios-series-a/). She also splits foundational and applied explicitly. For foundational tech, investors want \"the top five to ten people on earth\"; for applied AI, she evaluates a founder's \"slope\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/arsham-ghahramani-on-understanding-the-venture-mindset/). That is a summary of her view by Radical editorial staff, not a direct quote.\n- **What she would not do:** Fund non-AI businesses (\"you should be building an AI business\"). She also will not decide in an exploding window: \"We're not just going to throw money anywhere.\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/arsham-ghahramani-on-understanding-the-venture-mindset/)\n\n## Differential insight\n- **She named the Attuned founder about 14 months before the deal.** Her August 2024 essay adapted a voice-agent market map from Domenic Donato, \"ex-DeepMind research engineer & CTO of Assembly AI building something new\". Donato is now co-founder and CEO of Attuned Intelligence, whose $13M seed Radical co-led with Threshold in October 2025. [src](https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/voice-ai-agents-evolution-to-emerging) [src](https://radical.vc/portfolio/attuned-intelligence/)\n- **She is contrarian on consumer voice:** \"Consumer is much harder than enterprise from a voice perspective.\" She bets on enterprise voice agents first (recruiting, hospital call centers, insurance servicing). [src](https://radical.vc/articles/voice-ais-enterprise-revolution-how-natural-conversation-is-transforming-recruiting-and-beyond-2/)\n- **She sees voice-only as transitional:** \"single-modality Voice AI Agents are a stepping stone to seamlessly integrated multimodal agents\". She says she is already meeting home digital-companion companies. [src](https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/voice-ai-agents-evolution-to-emerging)\n- **On talent, she thinks the next winners may not be lab spinouts:** \"The next iconic companies may not come from labs.\" She co-wrote this with Radical's Senior Director of Talent Meg Sahni (2025-07). It is notable from a firm whose brand is researcher-founders. [src](https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/the-new-ai-talent-wars-what-matters) [src](https://radical.vc/team/meg-sahni/)\n- **Her 2026 thesis is tabular and causal foundation models,** co-written with U of T professor Rahul G. Krishnan, a CausalPFN author she interviewed in 2022. She names Prior Labs (TabPFN), Fundamental ($255M stealth emergence) and Kumo, and argues hyperscalers with proprietary tables are the most likely leaders. Watch this for a possible Krishnan spinout or a Radical tabular-model deal. [src](https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/tabular-foundation-models-enterprise) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/de-mystifying-healthcare-ai-with-rahul-krishnan/)\n- **How she decides:** She dislikes compressed processes: \"they have a term sheet and I have 12 hours to decide\". Radical's write-up says she believes the best decisions happen \"over a period of time, not at a point in time\". [src](https://radical.vc/articles/arsham-ghahramani-on-understanding-the-venture-mindset/)\n- **Her General Magic thesis is to wrap legacy systems, not replace them:** \"not by rebuilding from scratch, but by bridging the gap\". That is the same \"reasoning layer over systems of record\" framing as AI-native automation plays like Serval. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/investing-in-general-magic/)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** On the plus side, she is the Radical partner closest to your AI-native automation interest: General Magic (insurance agents), Ribbon (voice recruiter) and Attuned (hospital call-center voice agent) are all \"agent on top of legacy workflow\" companies, like Serval. She leads at pre-seed and seed and is often the first institutional money. On the minus side, her checks are small ($2.5M–$13M rounds), she is not named on Radical's frontier-lab or compute deals, and her healthcare-era portfolio (PocketHealth, Synex, Lisa Health) is non-frontier. Use her as a co-investor or deal-flow source for applied voice and agent seeds. Her tabular/causal foundation-model thesis (Prior Labs, Fundamental, Krishnan's CausalPFN) is the one place she touches new-lab territory.\n\n## Background\n- Originally from Mumbai. BA in Economics & International Relations, Tufts. MBA, IIM Bangalore. [src](https://radical.vc/team/sanjana-basu/)\n- Investment banking at Barclays, then venture investing at Tata (\"India's largest industrial and technology group\") in deep tech, AI and consumer tech. Dates not stated. [src](https://radical.vc/team/sanjana-basu/)\n- Says she started working on AI in 2016. Joined Radical at its 2019 inception and moved to Toronto for it. [src](https://radical.vc/team/sanjana-basu/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/from-ai-researcher-to-entrepreneur-with-charles-onu/)\n- Titled \"Investor\" through at least 2024-04-29 and \"Partner\" by 2025-01-13. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/from-ai-researcher-to-entrepreneur-with-charles-onu/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/investing-in-the-exponential-ai-in-sciences/). One event bio calls her an \"Investment Associate\" (undated, secondary) [src](https://datanatives.io/mentor/sanjanabasu/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/sanjana-basu/ | Team page; checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/ | \"Signal from the Frontier\"; about 19 posts, roughly 2 a year since 2023; latest 2026-04-01 (RSS checked 2026-11-10) |\n| X | nothing found | Team page links only the firm account (https://twitter.com/radicalvcfund); no personal handle verified |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/sanjana-basu/ | Linked from the radical.vc team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Radical Talks (Radical's podcast) | Guest-host or interviewer on Masterclass episodes (2025-12, 2026-02) |\n| Writing (firm) | https://radical.vc/ai-perspectives/ | About 14 Radical Reads bylines, 2020–2026 |\n| Email (published) | sanjana@radical.vc | Published on her 2021 market-map post [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-first-canadian-ai-and-healthcare-market-map/) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-02 — Radical Talks (Masterclass edition) — \"Arsham Ghahramani on Understanding the Venture Mindset\" — https://radical.vc/articles/arsham-ghahramani-on-understanding-the-venture-mindset/ — the 12-hour term-sheet complaint; a strict AI-thesis filter on inbound.\n- 2025-12 — Radical Talks (Masterclass edition) — Joelle Pineau (Cohere Chief AI Officer) on research-to-deployment — https://radical.vc/articles/joelle-pineau-on-turning-ai-research-into-real-world-impact/ — she interviews.\n- 2025-11 — Radical AI Founders Masterclass — \"Understanding Venture Capital\" session — https://radical.vc/articles/inside-the-2025-radical-ai-founders-masterclass/\n- 2025-09 — Radical Talks — \"Voice AI's Enterprise Revolution\" with Ribbon CEO, hosted by Molly Welch — https://radical.vc/articles/voice-ais-enterprise-revolution-how-natural-conversation-is-transforming-recruiting-and-beyond-2/ — consumer voice is harder than enterprise; the \"speech Turing test\".\n- 2025 — Founder Friendly Venture Capital podcast — \"Investing in Healthcare AI\" — https://www.podchaser.com/podcasts/founder-friendly-venture-capit-1054840/episodes/investing-in-healthcare-ai-san-189966321 — page returned 403; content not verified.\n- Undated (referenced 2024-04) — TVO's The Agenda with Steve Paikin, on Ontario as an AI hub — https://radical.vc/articles/from-ai-researcher-to-entrepreneur-with-charles-onu/\n- 2022-05 — MaRS Impact Health investor roundtable — https://radical.vc/articles/healthcare-the-top-funded-sector-in-ai/\n- 2021-06 — FLIK fireside with Michelle Kwok — https://radical.vc/articles/for-founders-by-founders/ — grit, founder-market fit, adaptability, moats.\n\n## How they think\n- **Voice is the interface:** \"Voice AI will become a primary means of interacting with machines\" (Ribbon lead post, 2025-04) [src](https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/ribbon-and-the-voice-ai-opportunity). Enterprise comes first; consumer has to pass a \"speech Turing test\".\n- **AI-native services wrap legacy systems.** Use a reasoning layer over existing systems of record rather than rip-and-replace (General Magic) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/investing-in-general-magic/).\n- **AI for science:** scarce dual-domain talent, pharma appetite, and \"trillion-dollar industries waiting to be created\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/investing-in-the-exponential-ai-in-sciences/).\n- **Buying behavior:** AI tools spread bottom-up as \"10x PLG\" [src](https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/is-ai-reshaping-buying-behavior).\n- **Tabular/causal FMs** will turn predictive modeling into a utility, and causal meta-learning moves companies from prediction to intervention [src](https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/tabular-foundation-models-enterprise).\n- **Calls:** Her 2020–22 \"Wave 2\" healthcare-AI thesis was early. Her 2024 voice-agent call held up: the enterprise recruiting and call-center deals she described were funded in 2025.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Voice AI Agents: Evolution to Emerging Applications\" (2024-08). Her voice map, which names Donato a year before Attuned. https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/voice-ai-agents-evolution-to-emerging\n2. \"Tabular Foundation Models: Enterprise AI's Next Wave\" (2026-04). Her newest thesis, co-written with Rahul G. Krishnan. https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/tabular-foundation-models-enterprise\n3. \"Investing in General Magic\" (2026-03). Her most recent lead and her AI-native-services framing. https://radical.vc/articles/investing-in-general-magic/\n4. \"The New AI Talent Wars\" (2025-07, with Meg Sahni). Her bet on product builders over lab superstars. https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/the-new-ai-talent-wars-what-matters\n5. Ribbon masterclass write-up (2026-02). How she decides and what she rejects. https://radical.vc/articles/arsham-ghahramani-on-understanding-the-venture-mindset/\n\n## Social engagement\nNo personal X account was found, and LinkedIn activity was not checked because it requires login. Her public output is the Substack, Radical Reads bylines, and Radical Talks and Masterclass interviews. She repeatedly amplifies Ribbon CEO Arsham Ghahramani: lead post, podcast, Masterclass panel and Masterclass podcast.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ubenwa | Pre-seed ($2.5M) | 2022-07-25 | Radical led; her bylined post | AIX Ventures, Yoshua Bengio, Hugo Larochelle, Marc Bellemare | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2022/07/25/can-a-babys-cry-identify-neurological-disorders-ubenwa-says-yes/), [Radical](https://radical.vc/articles/diagnosing-a-babys-cry-with-ai/) |\n| Lisa Health (Midday) | Seed ($2.5M) | 2022-07 | Radical led; her bylined post; exited (team page) | not named | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-midday-the-ai-powered-menopause-app-backed-by-mayo-clinic/) |\n| Signal 1 | Undisclosed | 2022 (portfolio year) | unknown; co-founded by Radical's Tomi Poutanen | — | [src](https://radical.vc/portfolio/signal-1/) |\n| Orbital Industries (ex-Orbital Materials) | Series A ($16M) | 2024-02-21 | Radical led (she is credited on team page; the Radical post was reportedly by Aaron Rosenberg, not verified) | Toyota Ventures, Fly Ventures, Compound | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2667769), [team](https://radical.vc/team/sanjana-basu/) |\n| Orbital Industries | Series B ($50M) | 2026-05-28 | No (Plural led); Radical re-upped | NVentures, Compound, Fly | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2026/05/28/exclusive-orbital-industries-raises-50-million-series-b-funding-round-ai-to-discover-exotic-new-materials/) |\n| Nabla Bio | Series A | 2024-05-28 | Radical led (post by Rob Toews; she is credited on team page) | not named | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/why-we-led-nabla-bios-series-a/) |\n| Ribbon | Seed ($8.2M; one co-investor calls it Series A) | 2025-03-31 | Yes, \"Radical Ventures' lead investment\" (her post) | Social Leverage, Cadenza Ventures, GD1 | [Torys](https://www.torys.com/work/2025/03/61993aea-898c-4cc0-9967-62353d814411), [her post](https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/ribbon-and-the-voice-ai-opportunity) |\n| Attuned Intelligence | Seed ($13M) | 2025-10-07 | Co-led with Threshold Ventures | — | [HIT Consultant](https://hitconsultant.net/2025/10/07/attuned-intelligence-emerges-from-stealth-with-13m-to-automate-hospital-call-centers-with-supervised-ai) |\n| General Magic | Seed ($7.2M) | 2026-03-02 | Yes; her bylined post | a16z Speedrun; angels from OpenAI and Figma; Aidan Gomez | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/investing-in-general-magic/) |\n| PocketHealth | Undisclosed | 2020 (portfolio year) | unknown | — | [src](https://radical.vc/portfolio/pocket-health/) |\n| Synex Medical | Undisclosed | 2020 (portfolio year) | unknown | — | [src](https://radical.vc/portfolio/synex/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Attuned Intelligence (first in, ahead of stealth exit):** In August 2024 she credited Domenic Donato's voice-agent taxonomy while he was \"building something new\". In October 2025 Radical co-led his $13M seed with Threshold as Attuned came out of stealth. That points to a relationship formed through her thesis writing, not an inbound pitch. The intro path is not stated. [src](https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/voice-ai-agents-evolution-to-emerging) [src](https://hitconsultant.net/2025/10/07/attuned-intelligence-emerges-from-stealth-with-13m-to-automate-hospital-call-centers-with-supervised-ai)\n- **Ribbon (led round, then heavy platform use):** Radical led the $8.2M round in March 2025. The founders are Arsham Ghahramani (Amazon recommendation systems, AI PhD) and Dave Vu. Ghahramani later appeared with her on Radical Talks (2025-09), on the Masterclass stage (2025-11) and on a Masterclass podcast (2026-02). [src](https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/ribbon-and-the-voice-ai-opportunity) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/arsham-ghahramani-on-understanding-the-venture-mindset/)\n- **Ubenwa (first institutional lead):** A Mila spinout advised by and backed by Yoshua Bengio. Radical led the $2.5M pre-seed in July 2022. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/diagnosing-a-babys-cry-with-ai/)\n- **General Magic:** She led the $7.2M seed alongside a16z Speedrun, with Cohere CEO Aidan Gomez as an angel, which shows the Radical portfolio network being used for angel syndication. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/investing-in-general-magic/)\n- **Orbital:** Radical was not in the 2023 seed (Fly, Compound). It led the $16M A in February 2024 and followed in the 2026 B. That fits a \"second-money\" entry into science companies. Her personal role is unclear.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | at: Partner (Investor until at least 2024-04) | 2019 | [src](https://radical.vc/team/sanjana-basu/) |\n| Domenic Donato (Attuned Intelligence) | cited his work in 2024; Radical co-led his seed | 2024-08 | [src](https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/voice-ai-agents-evolution-to-emerging) |\n| Arsham Ghahramani (Ribbon) | led investment; repeat podcast and stage partner | 2025-03 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/arsham-ghahramani-on-understanding-the-venture-mindset/) |\n| Rahul G. Krishnan (U of T, Vector) | interviewed him 2022; co-wrote 2026 essay | 2022-07 | [src](https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/tabular-foundation-models-enterprise) |\n| Meg Sahni (Radical talent) | co-author | 2025-07 | [src](https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/the-new-ai-talent-wars-what-matters) |\n| Fei-Fei Li | invited her to Stanford HAI 2022 conference | 2022-04 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/accountable-ai-foundation-models-and-embodied-ai-stanford-hai-2022-spring-conference/) |\n| Charles Onu (Ubenwa) | led his pre-seed | 2022-07 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/diagnosing-a-babys-cry-with-ai/) |\n| Threshold Ventures | co-lead (Attuned) | 2025-10 | [src](https://hitconsultant.net/2025/10/07/attuned-intelligence-emerges-from-stealth-with-13m-to-automate-hospital-call-centers-with-supervised-ai) |\n| a16z Speedrun | co-investor (General Magic) | 2026-03 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/investing-in-general-magic/) |\n| Joelle Pineau (Cohere) | interviewed at Masterclass | 2025-12 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/joelle-pineau-on-turning-ai-research-into-real-world-impact/) |\n| Tata (venture arm), Barclays | prior employers | dates not found | [src](https://radical.vc/team/sanjana-basu/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact promotion date to Partner (between 2024-04 and 2025-01). LinkedIn would confirm.\n- Board seats: none are disclosed. Does she sit on the Ribbon, Attuned or General Magic boards?\n- Her personal role on Nabla Bio and Orbital (lead partner, or support for Toews and Rosenberg?).\n- Is Rahul G. Krishnan spinning out a causal or tabular foundation-model company with Radical? Is Radical looking at Prior Labs or Fundamental?\n- Whether the \"Investing in Healthcare AI\" podcast (Founder Friendly VC) has a transcript.\n- Who introduced Donato, and whether Radical gave him pre-company support.\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/sanjana-basu/ (checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/feed (all posts; checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/investing-in-general-magic/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/arsham-ghahramani-on-understanding-the-venture-mindset/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/voice-ais-enterprise-revolution-how-natural-conversation-is-transforming-recruiting-and-beyond-2/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/joelle-pineau-on-turning-ai-research-into-real-world-impact/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/inside-the-2025-radical-ai-founders-masterclass/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/investing-in-the-exponential-ai-in-sciences/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/diagnosing-a-babys-cry-with-ai/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-midday-the-ai-powered-menopause-app-backed-by-mayo-clinic/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/healthcare-ai-enters-the-mainstream/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/for-founders-by-founders/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/from-ai-researcher-to-entrepreneur-with-charles-onu/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/de-mystifying-healthcare-ai-with-rahul-krishnan/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/the-first-canadian-ai-and-healthcare-market-map/\n- https://radical.vc/team/meg-sahni/\n- https://radical.vc/portfolio/ (company pages: attuned-intelligence, ribbon, general-magic, signal-1, pocket-health, synex, orbital-materials, nabla-bio)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2022/07/25/can-a-babys-cry-identify-neurological-disorders-ubenwa-says-yes/\n- https://techcrunch.com/?p=2667769 (Orbital Series A, 2024-02-21)\n- https://www.torys.com/work/2025/03/61993aea-898c-4cc0-9967-62353d814411\n- https://hitconsultant.net/2025/10/07/attuned-intelligence-emerges-from-stealth-with-13m-to-automate-hospital-call-centers-with-supervised-ai\n- https://fortune.com/2026/05/28/exclusive-orbital-industries-raises-50-million-series-b-funding-round-ai-to-discover-exotic-new-materials/\n- https://h2i.utoronto.ca/?p=11068 (secondary bio: \"Investor... since 2019\")\n- https://datanatives.io/mentor/sanjanabasu/ (secondary, undated)\n", "person:sarah-guo": "\n# Sarah Guo\n\n## One-line take\nShe commits in the first meeting, often before a round exists, then keeps adding to winners all the way to growth. Baseten (seed 2019 through Series F 2026) and Harvey (personal seed check, then four re-ups) are the clearest early-AI double-downs on record [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:**\n  - **Long-dated founder options.** She told Baseten's CEO in 2014 that his idea was bad but to call her if he did something else. Five years later she co-led his seed [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n  - **She decides in the room.** Harvey got a yes in the first meeting [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n  - **She is a router.** She introduced Baseten's CEO to Jensen Huang before Nvidia put $150M into Baseten's Series E [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/). The OpenAI Startup Fund sent her Harvey.\n- **Constraints:**\n  - She never owned the front of the race. She missed OpenAI and Anthropic because \"The fund was too small, and they were too big.\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/)\n  - Her lab positions (Mistral A, Thinking Machines seed) are participations, not leads (see Portfolio).\n  - She admits she was \"uncommitted\" to backing researcher-founders at first [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- **What she'd never do:** run a crypto fund. She says she \"was never going to do a crypto fund\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/), despite a visibly crypto-heavy last year at Greylock (see Differential insight).\n\n## Differential insight\nPrimary podcast transcripts and X could not be fetched this sweep because the web-search budget ran out. The quotes below come from the Colossus long-form profile (2026-07-19), which I checked against the article's raw HTML. They are verbatim quotes from Guo and the people around her.\n\n- **Baseten was a five-year founder option.** As a Greylock principal she turned down Srivastava's ML-for-healthcare idea in 2014 but asked him to come back: \"This idea is bad. But if you ever do something else, please call me.\" He pivoted, sold that company and returned with Baseten in 2019. She co-led the seed even though she rated MLOps \"not a great category from that vintage.\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/)\n- **She brokered Nvidia's entry into her own company.** \"It was Guo who introduced Srivastava to Huang.\" Nvidia then put $150M into Baseten's $300M Series E (Jan 2026) [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/). Huang's line: \"Nvidia loves investing with her.\"\n- **She admits her researcher-founder brand came late.** \"I was actually uncommitted that we should back a lot of researchers\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/). Her Mistral check was a people bet: \"Mistral wasn't even a technical belief.\"\n- **She sized early winners too small.** She and Reddy \"sized the positions, in her word, wimpily\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/). The Expansion I vehicle ($149M sold, 2025) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2056437/000205643725000001/primary_doc.xml) looks like the structural fix (my inference).\n- **She blocks rival investors by occupying the founders' time.** She was introduced late to Remotion, which OpenAI later bought, and spent a whole weekend with its founders: \"It's like filibustering.\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/)\n- **Her last year at Greylock was a crypto near-miss.**\n  - She helped run Greylock's crypto effort and changed her avatar to a CryptoPunk-style zombie.\n  - She launched the Fungible Times podcast in Feb 2022.\n  - She \"helped a younger partner lead Greylock's $70 million Series B into 0x Labs\" and shared the board seat [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/). The ZeroEx Holdings Form D (2022-04, $67.95M sold) names her [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1828694/000182869422000001/primary_doc.xml).\n  - Conviction launched as AI-only five weeks before FTX failed.\n- **She nearly left venture in 2016.** She \"nearly left to co-found an AI language-learning company with Andrew Ng\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- **Her office is a sourcing funnel for research talent.** Karpathy, Transformer co-authors Niki Parmar and Ashish Vaswani, and Copilot architect Alex Graveley have all worked from Conviction's desks. The house rule for tenants: \"If you hire eight people, or you raise more than $20 million,\" leave [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- **Some board seats only show up in SEC filings.** EDGAR full-text search for \"Sarah Guo\" returns 20 Form Ds [src](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Sarah%20Guo%22&forms=D). Besides the Conviction vehicles, they show director seats at:\n  - Baseten [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1865393/000186539326000004/primary_doc.xml)\n  - Watney [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2071023/000123191925000064/primary_doc.xml)\n  - Seek AI (2023 and 2024) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1911589/000191158924000001/primary_doc.xml)\n  - Cleo Labs (2019–2021) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1723292/000172329221000001/primary_doc.xml)\n  - Two companies I could not identify: Mysteries, Inc. (Seattle, Dec 2021, $18M sold) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1777464/000177746421000001/primary_doc.xml) and Stodge Inc. (Scottsdale, Feb 2021, $47M sold) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1848312/000184831221000001/primary_doc.xml).\n\n  Seek AI is a board seat that doesn't appear on Conviction's portfolio page.\n- **She is skeptical of the sectors behind her winners:** \"I think health tech still sucks, man,\" said while backing OpenEvidence at $3.5B [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.**\n- **Frontier labs:** she is in Mistral (Series A 2023) and Thinking Machines (seed 2025), but as a participant, never a lead. So she is a co-investor, not a competitor, for the $20M-into-mega-seed slot.\n- **AI infra and compute:** Baseten seed-to-F is the best-documented early AI-infra double-down in the repo.\n- **AI-native automation:** Sierra (pre-public-round check), Cognition A, Sola seed lead, /dev/agents seed.\n- **Pace-setter:** her Expansion and Select vehicles let her pay up late. She committed to OpenEvidence at $3.5B within a week [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/), so she is a pace-setter in hot rounds.\n- **As an LP:** her own LP base is about 50 founders and operators [src](https://www.conviction.com/launching-with-conviction.html). That suggests she values operator LPs, but nothing shows her backing other managers.\n\n## Background\n- Phillips Academy; University of Pennsylvania and Wharton, with BA, BS, MBA and MA \"in 5 years\" (Wikipedia, secondary) [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarah_Guo).\n- **2012:** Goldman Sachs in San Francisco, IPO and growth advisory (Workday IPO; Nvidia during the Starboard activist period). She left after one year of a two-year program [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- **2013:** joined Greylock, recruited by Aneel Bhusri. She was a principal by 2014.\n  - Helped lead Figma's Series A.\n  - Worked on Musical.ly.\n  - Became the youngest-ever GP at age 28 [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n  - Greylock-era board seats per Wikipedia (secondary): Awake Security (Arista), Skyhigh (McAfee), Demisto (Palo Alto Networks), Sqreen (Datadog), Common Room, and \"Multi (OpenAI)\" [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarah_Guo).\n- **2016:** nearly co-founded a startup with Andrew Ng.\n- **June 2022:** left Greylock and kept her board seats [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- **Oct 2022:** launched Conviction. Fund I sold $101M to 70 investors [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1941234/000194123422000001/primary_doc.xml), followed by Fund II ($230M) and Expansion I. The firm now manages \"nearly a billion dollars\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.conviction.com | Firm site; launch memos at /launches/; \"Plausible Schemes\" idea list at /startups.html |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes as Conviction (launch memos) |\n| X | https://x.com/saranormous | Linked from conviction.com team list (checked 2026-11-10); follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | No link found on firm or own pages |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | No Priors: [Spotify](https://open.spotify.com/show/0O65xhqvGVhpgdIrrdlEYk), [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCB0YUUvXzVr3SAVFolNN1nw) | Co-hosted with Elad Gil since about 6 months into Conviction (2023); weekly-ish |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nThese come only from Colossus. I fetched no transcripts this sweep [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- **2022-02:** launched Fungible Times, a crypto podcast, with two Greylock colleagues.\n- **2023:** launched No Priors with Elad Gil. The first guest was Noam Brown.\n- **2026-03:** hosted the three-hour pregame for Jensen Huang's GTC keynote, with Gavin Baker and Alfred Lin.\n- **2026 (spring):** No Priors episodes with Karpathy (about 1M views), Satya Nadella, Mark Zuckerberg and Lip-Bu Tan.\n\n## How they think\n- **AI as a general technology, crypto as a financial one.** She chose AI because \"AI is a general technology\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- **Apps still matter in a lab-dominated world.** The labs will be huge, but someone still has to wedge agents into courts, clinics and service queues. That is the Harvey, Sierra and OpenEvidence pattern [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- **Bet on people over category.** Baseten (weak category) and Mistral (\"wasn't even a technical belief\") show it.\n- **Contrarian on lab concentration.** She warns that investors heavy in lab positions blur the line \"between conviction and being dishonest with yourself\" [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- **Right:** Baseten 2019, Harvey 2022, Sierra and Cognition in Conviction's first year. Colossus says Conviction backed 6 of the 21 AI-native companies above $10B with more than $100M run-rate.\n- **Wrong, by her own account:** position sizing, and an initial reluctance to back researchers.\n\n## Read these first\n- Colossus, \"Sarah Guo\" (2026-07-19). The only deep primary-quote source so far [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- Launching With Conviction (2022): the \"Software 3.0\" thesis and the operator-LP base [src](https://www.conviction.com/launching-with-conviction.html).\n- Sola launch memo (Reddy and Guo): how a seed she led was argued [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/sola.html).\n- Latent launch memo [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/latent.html).\n- Plausible Schemes idea list [src](https://www.conviction.com/startups.html).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked this sweep, because X could not be fetched. Colossus describes her pre-2022 avatar change and crypto podcast, and No Priors as her main public channel.\n\n## Portfolio\nThese are her personal AI deals: rounds she led, sits on the board of, wrote personally, or where a source names her. The full list with notes is in `data/rows/sarah-guo-deals.csv`. Firm-level Conviction deals with no partner named (for example Cartesia, Chai, Applied Compute, HeyGen, Instinct, Trajectory, Fractile, Serval) stay in `data/rows/conviction-portfolio-deals.csv`.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Baseten | Seed (Greylock) | 2019 | co-led | n/a | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Baseten | Series A (Greylock) | 2022 | yes | n/a | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Baseten | Series E ($300M) | 2026-01 | no (every-round re-up) | NVIDIA ($150M) | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Baseten | Series F ($1.5B), director | 2026-06 | co-led | n/a | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1865393/000186539326000004/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Harvey | Seed ($5M, personal check) | 2022-11 | no | OpenAI Startup Fund (lead), Elad Gil | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Harvey | Series D / E / F / Growth re-ups | 2025-02 to 2026-09 | no | Sequoia, Kleiner, Coatue, a16z | [src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-d) |\n| Seek AI | Seed ($3M), director | 2023-05 | unknown | n/a | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1911589/000191158923000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Seek AI | Follow-on ($2M) | 2024-08 | unknown | n/a | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1911589/000191158924000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Contextual AI | Seed (angel) | 2023-06 | no | BCV (lead), Lightspeed, Elad Gil | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/) |\n| Ideogram | Seed (angel) | 2023-08 | no | a16z, Index (co-leads), Karpathy, Jeff Dean | [src](https://finsmes.com/2023/08/ideogram-raises-usd16-5m-in-seed-funding.html) |\n| Sierra | Early check (before the first public round) | 2023 | no | n/a | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Mistral AI | Series A | 2023-12 | no | a16z (lead), Lightspeed, NVIDIA | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/12/11/mistral-ai-a-paris-based-openai-rival-closed-its-415-million-funding-round/) |\n| Cognition | Series A | 2024-03 | no | Founders Fund (lead), Collisons, Elad Gil | [src](https://cognition.com/blog/introducing-devin) |\n| Voyage AI | Pre-A, then Series A; board per Wikipedia | 2024-10 | no | CRV (lead), Wing VC | [src](https://blog.voyageai.com/2024/10/03/series-a-funding/) |\n| /dev/agents | Seed ($56M) | 2024-11 | no | Index, CapitalG (co-leads) | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2024/11/26/new-startup-named-dev-agents-led-ex-google-meta-tech-leaders-raises-56m-ai-agents) |\n| Yutori | Seed (angel) | 2025-03 | no | Radical (lead), Felicis, Elad Gil | [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/yutori-launches-stealth-15m-seed-funding-build-consumer-ai-assistants) |\n| Watney Robotics | Seed ($21.6M), director | 2025-04 | unknown | n/a | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2071023/000123191925000064/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Watney Robotics | Series A ($80M) | 2026-09 | no | n/a | [src](https://dealroom.co/news/151986-data-centre-robot-startup-watney-raises-80m-series-a/) |\n| OpenEvidence | Series B ($210M at $3.5B) | 2025-07 | no | GV, Kleiner (co-leads) | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/openevidence-ai-doctors) |\n| Thinking Machines Lab | Seed ($2B), firm-level | 2025-07 | no | a16z (lead), NVIDIA | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/06/20/mira-muratis-thinking-machines-lab-closes-on-2b-at-10b-valuation/) |\n| Sola | Seed ($3.5M) | n.d. | yes | n/a | [src](https://www.sola-solutions.com/series-a-announcement) |\n| Latent Health | Launch investment (memo co-author) | n.d. | unknown | n/a | [src](https://www.conviction.com/launches/latent.html) |\n| Design Arena (The Intelligence Company) | Seed ($7.9M) | 2026-08 | no | n/a | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/03/designarena-creators-raise-7-9-million-to-bring-taste-to-ai-models/) |\n\nTiming:\n- **First in:** Baseten (2019 seed co-lead, three years before ChatGPT), Harvey (first-meeting yes, alongside OpenAI's fund), Sierra (before the Sequoia/Benchmark $110M round), Sola (seed lead).\n- **Doubled down:** Baseten (every round), Harvey (5 rounds), Watney (seed then A), Seek AI (two Form Ds).\n- **Late but fast:** OpenEvidence at $3.5B.\n- **Pattern:** after her seed leads, a multi-stage firm usually takes the A. Sola went to a16z [src](https://www.sola-solutions.com/series-a-announcement).\n\nNon-AI personal positions: Figma A (Greylock), Cleo Labs board, 0x Labs B (shared board seat), Remotion/Multi, Awake, Demisto, Sqreen, Common Room and Skyhigh. They are listed in Background and Differential insight, not in the deal rows.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Baseten:**\n  - She met Tuhin Srivastava in 2014 and told him the idea was bad but to call back.\n  - She co-led the 2019 seed and led the 2022 A.\n  - In late 2022, between Greylock and Conviction, she flew to New York for a four-hour recruiting lunch with an engineer at Carbone.\n  - She introduced Srivastava to Huang. Nvidia put $150M into the Series E.\n  - She co-led the $1.5B Series F and sits on the board alongside Will Reed and Jay Simons [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1865393/000186539326000004/primary_doc.xml).\n- **Harvey:**\n  - Ian Hathaway (OpenAI Startup Fund) sent the founders to her.\n  - She said yes in the first Zoom, which had no slides.\n  - She wrote the check personally because Fund I was still raising, then folded it into the fund.\n  - She found Harvey's first client, A&O Shearman, and re-upped in D, E, F and the $15.5B round [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- **Remotion:** introduced late, she spent the whole weekend with the founders so they could not meet anyone else. Pat Grady drove her; it was their anniversary [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n- **OpenEvidence:** one lunch with Daniel Nadler, then a commitment at $3.5B within a week. It raised at $12B six months later [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Pat Grady (Sequoia) | Spouse; stated deal wall; both in Harvey | n/a | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Elad Gil | No Priors co-host; co-angel (Harvey, Contextual AI, Yutori) | 2023 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Mike Vernal | Conviction GP, recruited over 18 months | 2025-01 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Pranav Reddy | First hire | 2022 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Tuhin Srivastava (Baseten) | Investor since 2019, met 2014 | 2014 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Jensen Huang / NVIDIA | Co-investor; GTC pregame host | n/a | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Ian Hathaway (OpenAI Startup Fund) | Harvey referral | 2022 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Bret Taylor (Sierra) | Calls her for talent and market reads | 2023 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Andrej Karpathy | Office tenant; No Priors guest | to 2026-05 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Will Reed, Jay Simons | Baseten co-directors | 2026 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1865393/000186539326000004/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Aneel Bhusri | Recruited her to Greylock | 2013 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Asheem Chandna | Former Greylock colleague; still sore about her exit | 2013 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n| Andrew Ng | Near co-founder | 2016 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **No Priors and X:** read primary transcripts and posts for check-size rules and passes. None were read this sweep because the search budget was exhausted.\n- **Unidentified board seats:** what are Mysteries, Inc. (Seattle) and Stodge Inc. (Scottsdale)?\n- **CBT1, LLC:** which company is behind the Oct 2026 Guo/Vernal SPV?\n- **Baseten timeline:** dates and sizes for Series B–D, and who co-led the 2019 seed.\n- **Voyage AI:** which round Conviction first entered, and whether the board seat (Wikipedia) is a Conviction seat.\n- **Multi:** confirm it is the renamed Remotion before minting a company.\n- **LP:** is she an LP anywhere? I found no evidence. I checked EDGAR full-text search, Colossus and the launch post. No `-lp.csv` was written.\n- **fast_promotion:** whether she went from Greylock analyst/associate to GP in four years or less. There is no sourced date for her GP promotion, only her age.\n\n## Sources\n- https://colossus.com/article/sarah-guo-conviction/ (2026-07-19; quotes checked against raw HTML)\n- https://www.conviction.com/ and https://www.conviction.com/launching-with-conviction.html (fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarah_Guo (secondary)\n- EDGAR full-text search: https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Sarah%20Guo%22&forms=D, plus the Form Ds linked above\n- https://blog.voyageai.com/2024/10/03/series-a-funding/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/31/guos-conviction-partners-adds-mike-vernal-as-gp-raises-230m-fund\n- The company announcements linked in the Portfolio table\n", "person:sarah-wang": "\n# Sarah Wang\n\n## One-line take\nShe is the a16z Growth GP who puts growth-fund money into brand-new frontier labs: a16z led the $2B Thinking Machines Lab seed, and she is the growth half of a long \"tag team\" with Martin Casado that also covers World Labs, Cursor, Exa, TypeSafe and Gimlet Labs.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** a16z Growth gives her a balance sheet that can fund labs whose day-one needs are, in her words, \"growth scale\". She also sits close to Casado's infra team, so she reaches founders before the round is priced. One example: Cursor's CEO pitched the team in a16z's office in June 2024, before the August 2024 Series A ([src](https://a16z.com/cursor-spacexai-the-fastest-iterating-team-wins/)).\n- **How she picks:** she backs a \"very specific thesis\" about each lab's founder and team, not the whole category ([src](https://www.latent.space/p/a16z)). She has said a16z is \"not invested in all of them\".\n- **What constrains her:** she can't own many of these deals alone. Most lab and infra posts are co-signed by Casado, Jennifer Li or the firm's founders (Mind Robotics is the solo exception), and many of the bio deals have no byline from her at all.\n- **What she wouldn't do:** in February 2026 she was publicly skeptical of robotics valuations. A month later she wrote the post on Mind Robotics, a founder-driven exception (RJ Scaringe of Rivian), so the skepticism looks aimed at the category, not at proven founders.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Lab rounds are founder bets that happen to need growth-scale money.** On Latent Space (2026-02-19) she called these rounds hybrid: \"it's still a tech founder bet, which obviously is inherently early stage.\" ([src](https://www.latent.space/p/a16z), ~00:01:20, auto-transcript)\n- **Compute-for-equity is part of the job.** Labs negotiate compute contracts worth hundreds of millions at six months old. She asked, \"do you get equity for the compute?\" ([src](https://www.latent.space/p/a16z), ~00:02:40, auto-transcript)\n- **a16z is selective on labs:** \"we're not invested in all of them\" (~00:44:56). Each bet rests on \"a very specific thesis\" ([src](https://www.latent.space/p/a16z)). She cited Ilya Sutskever's record at SSI as one such thesis.\n- **She doubled down on Thinking Machines after its co-founder turmoil:** \"we're more bullish about thinking than, um, you know, even before.\" (~00:49:10, auto-transcript) ([src](https://www.latent.space/p/a16z))\n- **Robotics skepticism, then Mind Robotics:** in February 2026 she said robotics funding \"feels like it's already. Taking that for granted\" (~00:20:20, the ChatGPT moment). On 2026-03-11 she was sole author of \"Investing in Mind Robotics\" ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mind-robotics/)).\n- **Specialists can win against the labs:** \"There's clear value. To specialization.\" She used ElevenLabs as the example ([src](https://www.latent.space/p/a16z), ~00:46:00).\n- **Cursor timing:** \"sitting in our office in June 2024 telling us confidently\" (Michael Truell). That was two months before the Series A a16z joined on 2024-08-22 ([src](https://a16z.com/cursor-spacexai-the-fastest-iterating-team-wins/), [src](https://cursor.com/blog/series-a)).\n- **She treats margin bears as noise:** \"Being a pessimist makes you sound smart. Being an optimist makes you money\" (with Casado, 2025-08-21) ([src](https://a16z.com/questioning-margins-is-a-boring-cliche/)).\n- **Pricing view for AI apps:** \"Token pricing pulls the customer conversation toward a cost curve that keeps falling.\" (with Tugce Erten, 2026-08-27) ([src](https://a16z.com/you-are-not-a-model-dont-price-per-token/))\n- **Character.AI thesis (2023):** \"major technology platform shifts start with the consumer\". She led the Series A and took the board seat ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-character-ai/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** She is a growth GP, but what matters for this lens is that a16z writes very large seeds into brand-new labs and she is on those deals. That is the owner's own play (about $20M into $600M+ seeds), done at a much larger size.\n- **Thinking Machines Lab:** a16z led the $2B seed at a $12B valuation ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/15/mira-muratis-thinking-machines-lab-is-worth-12b-in-seed-round/)). The bio names her on the deal, but no byline confirms her role.\n- **SSI:** a16z was in the $1B round in September 2024 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/04/ilya-sutskevers-startup-safe-super-intelligence-raises-1b/)). Her bio says she works on the growth stakes.\n- **World Labs:** she co-signed the September 2024 post with Casado.\n- **Gimlet Labs (multi-silicon inference cloud, 2026-09-04):** this is AI compute, and it fits the chips-and-compute part of the thesis.\n- **Also relevant:** TypeSafe (a lab-like Series A a16z led) and Exa.\n- **Caveat:** she is a co-investor at most, and a16z's check sizes crowd out small checks. She is useful as a signal of which labs a16z will fund next and as a co-investor on mega-seeds.\n\n## Background\n- Harvard undergraduate (Phi Beta Kappa); Stanford GSB MBA (Siebel Scholar, Arjay Miller Scholar) ([src](https://a16z.com/author/sarah-wang/)). Years not given.\n- Boston Consulting Group, then Morgan Stanley ([src](https://a16z.com/author/sarah-wang/)). Dates not found.\n- Vice President at TA Associates (growth equity), where she sat on the ZoomInfo board ([src](https://a16z.com/author/sarah-wang/)).\n- Joined a16z in 2019: \"Before joining a16z in 2019\" ([src](https://a16z.com/author/sarah-wang/)).\n- **GP date not found.** a16z re-renders the current author bio on old posts, so the \"general partner\" label on her 2021 Crossbeam post ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-crossbeam/)) doesn't prove she was a GP in 2021. Don't infer a promotion date from bylines.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/sarah-wang/ | Bio and roughly 50 bylines (2021–2026) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found (writes on a16z.com) | |\n| X | https://x.com/SarahDingWang | Handle from firm bio; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none; frequent guest on a16z podcast | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-02-19: Latent Space, \"Bitter Lessons in Venture vs Growth\" (with Casado) ([link](https://www.latent.space/p/a16z)). Covered lab rounds as hybrid deals, compute-for-equity, selectivity on labs, robotics skepticism and Thinking Machines.\n- a16z podcast, \"Where Value Will Accrue in AI: Martin Casado & Sarah Wang\" ([link](https://a16z.com/podcast/where-value-will-accrue-in-ai-martin-casado-sarah-wang/)). Date and transcript not checked.\n- a16z, \"From Vibe Coding to Vibe Researching\" with OpenAI's Mark Chen and Jakub Pachocki, with Anjney Midha ([author page](https://a16z.com/author/sarah-wang/)). Date not checked.\n- a16z, \"Building Search for AI Agents with Exa CEO Will Bryk\" ([author page](https://a16z.com/author/sarah-wang/)).\n\n## How they think\n- Big lab rounds are early-stage founder bets that need growth capital, so growth funds belong in them.\n- In AI, capability comes first and revenue follows (Latent Space, ~00:46).\n- Specialists such as ElevenLabs and Exa can beat the general-purpose labs in their own niche.\n- Low gross margins at AI apps are a passing phase, not disqualifying ([src](https://a16z.com/questioning-margins-is-a-boring-cliche/)).\n- AI apps should price on outcomes or credits, not tokens ([src](https://a16z.com/you-are-not-a-model-dont-price-per-token/)).\n- Calls that went right: Cursor. a16z was in the Series A (2024-08); SpaceX agreed to buy Cursor in a $60B all-stock deal in June 2026 ([src](https://a16z.com/cursor-spacexai-the-fastest-iterating-team-wins/)). Character.AI outcome: not checked in this pass.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Latent Space: Bitter Lessons in Venture vs Growth](https://www.latent.space/p/a16z) (2026-02): how she thinks about lab mega-rounds.\n- [Cursor + SpaceXAI: the fastest iterating team wins](https://a16z.com/cursor-spacexai-the-fastest-iterating-team-wins/) (2026-08): a deal retrospective.\n- [Questioning Margins is a Boring Cliche](https://a16z.com/questioning-margins-is-a-boring-cliche/) (2025-08)\n- [Investing in Character.AI](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-character-ai/) (2023-03): her consumer-first platform thesis.\n- [Investing in Gimlet](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gimlet/) (2026-09): her view on heterogeneous inference compute.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked; X was not fetched in this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Thinking Machines Lab | Seed ($2B at $12B) | 2025-07 | a16z led (her role: bio only) | Nvidia, Accel, ServiceNow, Cisco, AMD, Jane Street | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/15/mira-muratis-thinking-machines-lab-is-worth-12b-in-seed-round/) |\n| SSI | ~$1B round (~$5B val. per Reuters) | 2024-09-04 | no | NFDG, Sequoia, DST, SV Angel | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/04/ilya-sutskevers-startup-safe-super-intelligence-raises-1b/) |\n| OpenAI | growth stake | not found | no | — | [bio](https://a16z.com/author/sarah-wang/) |\n| World Labs | undisclosed | 2024-09-13 | not stated | — | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-world-labs/) |\n| Cursor | Series A ($60M) | 2024-08-22 | not stated | Thrive, OpenAI, angels | [src](https://cursor.com/blog/series-a) |\n| Cursor | Series B ($105M) | 2025-01-16 | not stated | Thrive, Benchmark | [src](https://cursor.com/blog/series-b) |\n| Decagon | Seed ($5M; a16z lead) / later rounds | 2024-06 | a16z led seed (her role: bio only) | Accel (led A), A*, Elad Gil | [src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-a) |\n| Exa | Series C ($250M at $2.2B) | 2026-05-20 | yes | — | [src](https://exa.ai/blog/announcing-series-c) |\n| Mind Robotics | undisclosed | 2026-03-11 | not stated | — | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mind-robotics/) |\n| TypeSafe AI | Series A | 2026-10-09 | yes | — | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-typesafe-ai/) |\n| Gimlet Labs | undisclosed | 2026-09-04 | not stated | — | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gimlet/) |\n| Temporal | Series D | 2026-02-17 | yes | — | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-temporal/) |\n| Gamma | undisclosed | 2025-11-10 | not stated | — | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gamma/) |\n| Character.AI | Series A (board seat) | 2023-03-23 | yes | — | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-character-ai/) |\n| Wiz | undisclosed (co-led) | 2024-05-07 | co-led | — | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-wiz/) |\n| Hex | Series B | 2022-03-22 | yes (Growth Fund) | Databricks, Snowflake | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-hex/) |\n| Sourcegraph | Series D | 2021-07-13 | yes (Growth Fund) | — | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sourcegraph/) |\n| Crossbeam | undisclosed | 2021-10-26 | not stated | — | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-crossbeam/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Thinking Machines Lab:** a16z led the $2B seed in July 2025, which made it the first institutional lead ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/15/mira-muratis-thinking-machines-lab-is-worth-12b-in-seed-round/)). After the lab's co-founder turmoil she said a16z was more bullish than before (Latent Space, February 2026).\n- **Cursor:** Michael Truell pitched the enterprise case in a16z's office in June 2024. a16z was named in the $60M Series A in August 2024, but the lead is not stated ([src](https://cursor.com/blog/series-a)). It was in the Series B in January 2025. SpaceX's $60B all-stock purchase was confirmed in June 2026. Wang, Bornstein and Casado wrote the retrospective.\n- **World Labs:** relationship-first. a16z had known Fei-Fei Li for \"nearly two decades\", and she approached the firm the year before the September 2024 post ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-world-labs/)).\n- **Decagon:** a16z led the $5M seed and Accel led the $30M Series A (announced June 2024) ([src](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-a)), so a16z was first in. Who at a16z led the seed is not sourced. Her solo post \"The internet ruined customer service. AI could save it.\" (date not checked) is the related thesis piece.\n- **Exa:** a16z led the Series C in May 2026. It was not first in; Benchmark led the Series B in September 2025 (see `data/rows/jason-cui-deals.csv`).\n- **TypeSafe:** a16z led (\"We've long been fans of Diogo\"), and Casado took the board seat.\n- **Mind Robotics:** she was sole author. She thanks Sullivan, George, Raghuram, Price-Wright, Zietek and Appenzeller, which suggests a growth-led deal with cross-team diligence.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Martin Casado | \"hybrid tag team\" on AI infra; co-author on World Labs, TypeSafe, Cursor, margins | ~2019 (\"Seven, seven years ago now\") | [src](https://www.latent.space/p/a16z) |\n| Jennifer Li | Co-author: Exa, TypeSafe, Crossbeam, Data 50 | 2021 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-crossbeam/) |\n| Stephenie Zhang | Co-author: Exa, Temporal, Gimlet, Gamma | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/) |\n| Raghu Raghuram | Co-author: Gimlet, Temporal | 2026 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gimlet/) |\n| Shangda Xu | Co-author: Gimlet, enterprise-AI CIO surveys | 2023 | [src](https://a16z.com/author/sarah-wang/) |\n| Tugce Erten | Co-author: AI pricing pieces | 2024 | [src](https://a16z.com/you-are-not-a-model-dont-price-per-token/) |\n| Matt Bornstein | Co-author: Hex, Cursor retrospective | 2022 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-hex/) |\n| David George | Co-author: growth metrics; thanked on Mind Robotics | 2021 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mind-robotics/) |\n| Jason Cui | Co-author: Exa, Search Wars | 2025 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/) |\n| Character.AI | Board member (2023) | 2023-03 | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-character-ai/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- GP promotion date. Bylines can't settle it; LinkedIn or an a16z promotions post would.\n- Her actual role on Thinking Machines, SSI and OpenAI, and whether she holds a TML board or observer seat.\n- Round size and leads for Mind Robotics and Gimlet Labs.\n- Who at a16z led the Decagon seed, and whether she joined at a later round.\n- Who \"John\" is in her Latent Space remark about Thinking Machines (likely John Schulman, but the transcript gives only the first name).\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/sarah-wang/\n- https://www.latent.space/p/a16z\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/ais-capital-flywheel-models-money-and-the-future-of-power/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-character-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mind-robotics/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-world-labs/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-typesafe-ai/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gimlet/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-temporal/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gamma/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-wiz/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-hex/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-sourcegraph/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-crossbeam/\n- https://a16z.com/cursor-spacexai-the-fastest-iterating-team-wins/\n- https://a16z.com/questioning-margins-is-a-boring-cliche/\n- https://a16z.com/you-are-not-a-model-dont-price-per-token/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/15/mira-muratis-thinking-machines-lab-is-worth-12b-in-seed-round/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/04/ilya-sutskevers-startup-safe-super-intelligence-raises-1b/\n- https://cursor.com/blog/series-a\n- https://cursor.com/blog/series-b\n- https://decagon.ai/blog/series-a\n- https://exa.ai/blog/announcing-series-c\n- Note: WebSearch budget was exhausted this run; findings come from WebFetch of known URLs only.\n", "person:sarah-zeng": "\n# Sarah Zeng\n\n## One-line take\nAn operator who works inside AI infrastructure (early Character AI, now Braintrust's chief of staff) and runs a one-person fund of under $6M. Through operator access they got small allocations in Flapping Airplanes, Etched, Architect Labs, Trajectory and Crosby, which makes them a sourcing and signal node, not a co-lead [src](https://www.snowyleopard.vc/), [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2135458/000213545826000001/primary_doc.xml).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** their deal flow comes from their day job. Braintrust, the AI evals and observability company where Zeng is chief of staff, sits inside many AI teams' build stacks. Zeng invests in some of the same companies Braintrust buys from or backs.\n  - Braintrust is a Crosby customer. Zeng was the customer reference who described moving all of Braintrust's contracts to Crosby, and Snowy Leopard holds Crosby [src](https://www.new-ontologies.com/posts/Crosby).\n  - Braintrust (the company) is listed as a Trajectory backer, and Snowy Leopard also lists Trajectory [src](https://trajectory.ai), [src](https://www.snowyleopard.vc/).\n- **Network layers:** an earlier investing career (firm unnamed), then a seat among Character AI's first 50 employees [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/ie/podcast/inside-braintrust-scaling-a-category-defining-ai/id1840326068?i=1000752779565).\n- **What constrains them:**\n  - **Size:** Fund I targets $6M and had sold $2.48M to 18 LPs at filing [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2135458/000213545826000001/primary_doc.xml).\n  - **Time:** Zeng operates full time, so investing is part-time.\n  - **Access, not ownership:** checks into rounds like Flapping's $1B Series A are signal and access, with no meaningful ownership.\n- **What they'd never do (on the evidence):** lead or price a round. No source shows a lead. The firm's list mixes \"Fund & Angel\" checks [src](https://www.snowyleopard.vc/).\n\n## Differential insight\nThese bullets come from the anonymous Zero Shot Substack. Its author is unnamed, but they describe themselves in a 2025-08-08 post as Braintrust's chief of staff and as an angel and former institutional investor. That matches Zeng's documented role [src](https://www.new-ontologies.com/posts/Crosby). I treat the attribution as probable, not confirmed.\n- **They were sceptical of pre-training moats in early 2025, then backed a new lab in 2026.** In Feb 2025 the author wrote that pre-training a model was a moat in 2021 and that this has since changed: \"In 2021, pre-training your own large language model provided a significant competitive advantage.\" [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-primer-on-ai-agents).\n  - Twenty months later Snowy Leopard sits in Flapping Airplanes' Series A [src](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/).\n  - The likely reconciliation (my inference) is that they back labs whose pitch is efficiency, not the scale race.\n- **Data is the moat:** \"I think the key point for AI companies will continue to be data.\" [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-primer-on-ai-agents) (2025-02-17).\n- **Vertical search needs operations on the ground:** \"Acquiring this data may require on-the-ground operations.\" The post frames Mercor as a vertical-search company [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-new-opportunity-for-search) (2025-05-20).\n  - This is the same services-plus-software shape as Crosby, an AI-native legal firm, which they later held.\n- **Infrastructure thesis by analogy to Datadog:** \"we're at the start of a wave similar to companies like Datadog enabled\". The post also predicts \"the most successful companies to be platform-agnostic\" [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/the-infrastructure-behind-ai-development) (2025-08-08).\n  - This explains the tilt toward infrastructure and compute (Etched, Architect Labs, Trajectory).\n- **Timing of the Braintrust move (inference from the Substack bios):**\n  - On 2025-02-17 and 2025-05-20 the bio reads \"I lead biz ops & finance at an AI startup\" [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-new-opportunity-for-search).\n  - By 2025-08-08 it reads \"I'm Chief of Staff at Braintrust\" [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/the-infrastructure-behind-ai-development).\n  - So they joined Braintrust around mid-2025. The earlier \"AI startup\" was probably Character AI (unconfirmed).\n- **The customer-to-investor path is documented:** Crosby's features gave Braintrust \"the confidence to migrate all of Braintrust's contracts over\" [src](https://www.new-ontologies.com/posts/Crosby) (2026-07-29).\n- **Shared angel, possible channel (inference):** Jeff Dean is a listed backer of both Trajectory [src](https://trajectory.ai) and Architect Labs [src](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/06/18/architect-labs-raises-24-million-to-take-on-broadcom-marvell-custom-chip-business). He is also an unverified Flapping Airplanes angel per an aggregator. No source links Dean and Zeng directly.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **The portfolio is close to dead-centre on THESIS.md:**\n  - a new lab (Flapping Airplanes)\n  - AI inference chips (Etched)\n  - AI for custom-chip design (Architect Labs)\n  - a post-training and continual-learning platform (Trajectory)\n  - AI-native services, the Serval-like automation pattern (Crosby)\n- **But not a co-lead or LP fit:** the whole fund is less than one of my ~$20M checks.\n- **The value is as a scout and signal:**\n  - Zeng sees which AI-infra startups real AI teams buy from, through Braintrust's customer base.\n  - They hold ex-Character AI talent relationships.\n  - They got into a pre-hype chip-design seed (Architect Labs).\n- **Worth a relationship for deal flow:** seed-stage AI infra and chips, and early hires out of consumer-AI labs.\n\n## Background\n- Former institutional investor; the firm is not named in any source found [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-primer-on-ai-agents).\n- Moved \"from investing to operating\"; among the first 50 employees at Character AI [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/ie/podcast/inside-braintrust-scaling-a-category-defining-ai/id1840326068?i=1000752779565). Dates not found.\n- Through at least 2025-05: led biz ops and finance at an unnamed AI startup, per the Substack bio [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-new-opportunity-for-search).\n- By 2025-08-08: Chief of Staff at Braintrust [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/the-infrastructure-behind-ai-development).\n- 2026: Snowy Leopard Ventures Fund I, LP.\n  - First sale 2026-05-20; Form D filed 2026-05-28 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2135458/000213545826000001/primary_doc.xml).\n  - The Form D signer is \"Yuanjun Zeng\", probably Zeng's legal name (unconfirmed).\n- Education: an X-bio snippet reportedly mentions applied math at Harvard. Not verified; X returned 402.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.snowyleopard.vc/ | Portfolio list plus contact sarah@snowyleopard.vc (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://thezeroshot.substack.com/ | Zero Shot. Anonymous; attributed by role match. 3 posts (2025-02, 2025-05, 2025-08), dormant since |\n| X | https://x.com/sarahzengy | Not fetchable (402); the handle comes from the firm profile's search snippet |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/sarah-zeng99 | 2K followers, 500+ connections (guest view, 2026-11-10) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-03 — Early Access — \"Inside Braintrust: Scaling a Category-Defining AI Evals Platform (with Sarah Zeng)\" (26 min) — [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/ie/podcast/inside-braintrust-scaling-a-category-defining-ai/id1840326068?i=1000752779565).\n  - Chapters cover the move from investing to startups, joining Character AI, the move to Braintrust, and \"Growth in Consumer AI vs. Infrastructure AI\".\n  - No transcript was available, so nothing they said is quoted here. The host is not named on the page.\n\n## How they think\n- **Data is the durable moat for AI companies.** In Feb 2025 they said pre-training your own model is no longer one [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-primer-on-ai-agents).\n- **Vertical, data-heavy businesses that need ground operations can beat horizontal incumbents** [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-new-opportunity-for-search).\n- **AI evals and observability will be a Datadog-scale category, and winners will be platform-agnostic** [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/the-infrastructure-behind-ai-development). The post is also self-interested: it promotes their employer.\n- **Agents will take a long time to reach broad adoption.** They expect build-vs-buy to start fragmented and then converge [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-primer-on-ai-agents).\n- Calls right or wrong: too early to judge.\n  - The pre-training-moat scepticism sits awkwardly with the Flapping Airplanes position. This is unresolved.\n  - Mercor (named in the vertical-search post) went on to scale, but Zeng holds no recorded position in it.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"A Primer on AI Agents\" (2025-02-17). The data-moat view and the claim that pre-training is no longer an advantage [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-primer-on-ai-agents).\n2. \"A new opportunity for search\" (2025-05-20). The vertical-search, data-flywheel thesis [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-new-opportunity-for-search).\n3. \"The Infrastructure Behind AI Development\" (2025-08-08). The AI-evals-as-Datadog thesis [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/the-infrastructure-behind-ai-development).\n4. New Ontologies' Crosby piece (2026-07-29). It shows the customer-to-investor path [src](https://www.new-ontologies.com/posts/Crosby).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: X could not be fetched (402) and LinkedIn is behind a login. The Substack invites founders in vertical search to reach out, which suggests it doubles as a sourcing channel [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-new-opportunity-for-search).\n\n## Portfolio\nFund and angel checks are mixed on the firm site, which lists every company below [src](https://www.snowyleopard.vc/). No round, date or check size is confirmed for Zeng personally.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Flapping Airplanes | Series A ($1B; reported $6B valuation). Not in the 2026-01-28 seed | 2026-10-21 | no | Index, Lux (co-leads), Sequoia, GV, Kleiner, NVIDIA | [src](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/) |\n| Etched | unknown | unknown | no | Sequoia, Jane Street (later rounds) | [src](https://www.snowyleopard.vc/) |\n| Architect Labs | probably the $24M seed (the only round found) | 2026-06-18 | no | Kindred (lead), TQ, Race Capital, Together Fund, Jeff Dean | [src](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/06/18/architect-labs-raises-24-million-to-take-on-broadcom-marvell-custom-chip-business) |\n| Trajectory | unknown | unknown | no | Sequoia, Conviction, NVIDIA, Braintrust, Modal, Hugging Face, Fei-Fei Li, Jeff Dean | [src](https://trajectory.ai) |\n| Crosby | unknown (PitchBook snippet: minority) | unknown | no | Sequoia, Lux, Index, Elad Gil, BCV | [src](https://www.new-ontologies.com/posts/Crosby) |\n| Liquid (liquid.trade) | unknown | unknown | no | Paradigm | [src](https://www.snowyleopard.vc/) |\n| AIR Security | unknown | unknown | no | unknown | [src](https://www.snowyleopard.vc/) |\n| Orbit (orbit.engineering) | unknown | unknown | no | unknown | [src](https://www.snowyleopard.vc/) |\n| Altis | unknown | unknown | no | unknown | [src](https://www.snowyleopard.vc/) |\n\n**Timing:** for no company is there a source showing Zeng in first.\n- **Flapping Airplanes:** a late, small follow-on. Index, GV and Sequoia co-led the seed about nine months before the Series A [src](https://alleywatch.com/2026/10/the-weekly-notable-startup-funding-report-10-26-26/).\n- **Architect Labs:** possibly in at the seed, alongside Kindred.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Crosby:**\n  - Braintrust became a Crosby customer and migrated all of its contracts to the firm.\n  - Zeng was interviewed as the customer, alongside lead investors Grace Isford (Lux) and Ryan Kim (BCV) [src](https://www.new-ontologies.com/posts/Crosby).\n  - Snowy Leopard lists Crosby. Whether the investment came before or after the customer relationship is unknown.\n- **Trajectory:** Braintrust (the company) is a listed backer [src](https://trajectory.ai). A shared channel is likely, but no intro is sourced.\n- **Flapping Airplanes, Etched, Architect Labs:** no intro path found.\n  - Possible Harvard (Etched, Flapping's Asher Spector) and Jeff Dean overlaps are inference only.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Snowy Leopard Ventures | Founder; only named person | 2026 (Fund I) | [src](https://www.snowyleopard.vc/) |\n| Yuanjun Zeng | Form D GP manager; probably the same person (needs-id) | 2026-05 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2135458/000213545826000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Braintrust | Chief of Staff | by 2025-08 | [src](https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/the-infrastructure-behind-ai-development) |\n| Character AI | Early employee (first 50) | unknown | [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/ie/podcast/inside-braintrust-scaling-a-category-defining-ai/id1840326068?i=1000752779565) |\n| Crosby | Customer reference (via Braintrust) and investor | by 2026-07 | [src](https://www.new-ontologies.com/posts/Crosby) |\n| Grace Isford (Lux), Ryan Kim (BCV) | Fellow interviewees on Crosby | 2026-07 | [src](https://www.new-ontologies.com/posts/Crosby) |\n| Early Access podcast (host unnamed) | Guest | 2026-03-02 | [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/ie/podcast/inside-braintrust-scaling-a-category-defining-ai/id1840326068?i=1000752779565) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which institutional firm did they work at before operating? Which \"AI startup\" did they run biz ops and finance for in early 2025: Character AI or another?\n- Is Yuanjun Zeng the same person (legal name)?\n- Flapping Airplanes: fund or personal SPV? Who made the intro, and what was the check size?\n- Etched: when did they enter? Was it a pre-fund angel check?\n- Who are the 18 Fund I LPs? Are Braintrust colleagues or the ex-Character AI cohort among them?\n- The podcast audio (26 min) is untranscribed. The \"Consumer AI vs. Infrastructure AI\" segment probably holds their thesis in their own words.\n- X (@sarahzengy) is unread.\n- Web search budget was exhausted during this run; a follow-up search pass is needed.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.snowyleopard.vc/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2135458/000213545826000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.new-ontologies.com/posts/Crosby\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/ie/podcast/inside-braintrust-scaling-a-category-defining-ai/id1840326068?i=1000752779565\n- https://thezeroshot.substack.com/\n- https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-primer-on-ai-agents\n- https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-new-opportunity-for-search\n- https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/the-infrastructure-behind-ai-development\n- https://trajectory.ai\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/sarah-zeng99\n- https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/\n- https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/06/18/architect-labs-raises-24-million-to-take-on-broadcom-marvell-custom-chip-business\n- https://alleywatch.com/2026/10/the-weekly-notable-startup-funding-report-10-26-26/\n- firms/snowy-leopard.md (prior profile; contains further sources)\n", "person:scott-wilson": "\n# Scott Wilson\n\n## One-line take\nWorth following as the clearest voice for the \"endowment as concentrated co-investor\" model. He uses GPs as a deal funnel into $50–200M directs, and his look-through book holds SpaceX, Cerebras and Anthropic.\n\n## Crux\nWilson wins by underwriting companies, not managers. The team is all generalists, spends 80–90% of its time with companies, starts from a trusted GP's top picks, and concentrates hard when it agrees. By his own account, directs are the whole difference between peer-average and top-of-peer returns. Constraints: a ~5% payout pins privates near 50%, there is a 1%-of-portfolio (~$200M) conviction bar, and board approval kicks in at about 4% at cost. He would never pay 100x revenue, buy a mega-platform's add-on funds to keep access, or seed-pick from scratch (\"we're not good super early stage investors\").\n\n## Differential insight\nAll quotes from the 20VC episode aired 2026-11-08 [src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026); timestamps are approximate.\n- Put in redemptions for \"over half the portfolio in the first week\" (~06:00), then exited all 36–37 hedge funds and turned over more than 75% of privates via secondaries over about 18 months.\n- Funds alone would have been average: \"we would've been right at the average of our peer group\" (~12:00).\n- Sourcing starts \"on second or third base\" from a trusted GP's conviction (~12:45). Partners are sorted into \"high signal and, and lower signal\" (~13:00).\n- Proactive model: he asks a GP \"who are the five companies in your portfolio\" they are most excited about, then meets them (~24:30). Inbound SPVs mean \"a huge negative selection bias\" (~25:00).\n- Sizing: \"if we don't have enough conviction to do 1% of the portfolio,\" don't do it, and that is now about $200M (~19:00).\n- Governance: notify the board above 2–3% at cost, and \"I think it's 4%, we need their approval\" (~14:00).\n- Per-fund concentration limits are \"mostly artificial\" (~14:15).\n- Hit rate: \"probably more like 0.1% of those investments than 1%\" (~16:30).\n- Anti-mega-fund: \"our model doesn't really work with the mega funds\" (~20:15). In none over $10B (~23:00).\n- Prefers a manager who raises $1B every 18 months to $10B every 3 years (~37:00).\n- Sequoia evergreen: \"I'm sure it was a mistake if you look at the returns\" (~33:45).\n- Biggest mistake: not adding to winners because of position size or frame-of-reference bias, \"it's cost us billions of dollars\" (~43:45).\n- Missed a late-2020 secondary sale by waiting for Q4 write-ups: \"we were just essentially being greedy\" (~45:00).\n- AI views: thinks \"probably half of these companies don't exist\" in 10 years (~28:45). Also: \"for most of our tasks... we don't really need a frontier model\" (~30:00). Still holds Anthropic exposure via partners.\n- Top manager: Lee Fixel / Addition, \"probably the most talented investor in our portfolio\" (~52:00). Wishes they were in \"benchmark probably\" (~51:00).\n- Cerebras: hedged and trimmed before distribution, still holds some. Got it \"through a fund called Eclipse\" (~51:00).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** The 1% (~$200M) per-idea rule and about one new fund a year make WashU a poor LP for an early-stage frontier-AI manager. Wilson rarely does Seed or Series A. The only opening he describes is a ~$20M check into a \"very small fund\" that gives a route to a bigger direct check in an underlying company. His value to me is as a downstream co-investor for labs and compute companies, and as a map of which GPs top endowments rate (Addition, Eclipse, Benchmark, Founders Fund). Deals that matter: Cerebras (via Eclipse), Anthropic (via unnamed partners), SpaceX.\n\n## Background\n- Grinnell College, B.A. Economics and Mathematics (class of '98); attended the University of Chicago Financial Mathematics graduate program; CFA [src](https://endowment.wustl.edu/people/scott-l-wilson/) [src](https://grinnell.edu/news/special-campus-memo-scott-wilson).\n- Equity research analyst, then trading and quant roles in rates, equity and FX derivatives at Bank of America Securities (Tokyo, Chicago, London); Head of Rates Option Trading at Barclays Capital Japan [src](https://endowment.wustl.edu/people/scott-l-wilson/).\n- Grinnell College: rejoined in 2010 as Director of Investments; CIO from 2014-01-01. Hired by David Clay (per 20VC) [src](https://grinnell.edu/news/special-campus-memo-scott-wilson).\n- WashU CIO since late 2017. Grinnell's memo gave a December 1 start; WashU's announcement was in September 2017 [src](https://source.washu.edu/2017/09/scott-wilson-appointed-chief-investment-officer-washington-university/). In his words: started at \"just under 8 billion,\" now \"just under $20 billion\"; \"$17 billion of profits\" and about $5B paid to the university over nine years (~42:00).\n- TIFF TAS Advisory Board [src](https://www.tiff.org/?p=4719).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://endowment.wustl.edu/people/scott-l-wilson/ | WUIMC bio |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/scott-wilson-a6864415/ | Linked from the 20VC newsletter |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-11 — 20VC — \"Scott Wilson, CIO @ WashU\" — https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026 — the 1% rule, board thresholds, Sequoia evergreen, wishes in Benchmark, Addition as top pick.\n- 2020-10 (re-aired 2025 summer series) — Capital Allocators (Ted Seides) — \"CIO Greatest Hits: Endowments\" — https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/capital-allocators-inside-the-institutional-investment-industry/episode/cio-greatest-hits-endowments-scott-wilson-washu-90200 — position-focused diligence and co-investing (show notes only; not reviewed in full).\n\n## How they think\n- Publics and privates differ only in mark-to-market frequency. With no payout he would hold 70–80% in privates. Privates compounded low-to-mid 20s vs publics at about 10% over the decade (~08:00–10:00).\n- Diversification benefits stop after 20–30 names. The target is half the portfolio in the top 50 look-through positions (~18:15).\n- \"price is what you pay, value is what you get\" (~47:45). Buys cheaper non-US assets (Europe, China) with similar KPIs.\n- Cyclicals: sees a coming shakeout in AI and semis / memory, and calls them historically \"not great businesses\" (~40:00).\n- Calls admitted wrong: Sequoia evergreen exit (returns), not adding to winners, the 2020 secondary timing, and two manufacturing directs.\n- Called right: holding SpaceX through continuation vehicles; trimming and hedging Cerebras before distribution.\n\n## Read these first\n- 20VC transcript (2026-11-08) — the fullest statement of the model: https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026\n- Bloomberg Línea on the SpaceX stake and FY2026 results — https://www.bloomberglinea.com.br/negocios/como-um-aporte-na-spacex-ha-uma-decada-rendeu-us-2-bi-a-uma-universidade-dos-eua/\n- Capital Allocators episode (2020) — the earlier version of the position-focused approach.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public X or Substack identified.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| SpaceX | early stake ~$50M (pre-Wilson) + continuation vehicles | ~2016–2017; held through IPO (2026) | no | Vy Capital | [Bloomberg Línea](https://www.bloomberglinea.com.br/negocios/como-um-aporte-na-spacex-ha-uma-decada-rendeu-us-2-bi-a-uma-universidade-dos-eua/); [20VC](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n| Cerebras | via Eclipse fund (plus possible directs) | unknown | no | Eclipse | [20VC](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n| Anthropic | look-through via \"a few of our partners\" | unknown | no | unnamed | [20VC](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n| Nubank | private, then distributed | held ~5 years post-IPO | unknown | unknown | [20VC](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **SpaceX:** Wilson says it predates him [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com.br/negocios/como-um-aporte-na-spacex-ha-uma-decada-rendeu-us-2-bi-a-uma-universidade-dos-eua/) and credits \"Lee and the board, and Adam.\" The team resisted many liquidity chances and used several continuation vehicles to hold through the IPO. The position was once more than 20% of the portfolio (~13:00). After the IPO they \"take some chips\" over several years (~32:00).\n- **Cerebras:** sourced through Eclipse (Lior Susan). It was a very big position at the IPO, and they hedged and trimmed before distribution (~38:15).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| David Clay | Hired Wilson at Grinnell; mentor | 2010 | [20VC ~05:00](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n| Lee Fixel (Addition) | \"Mutual friend\" with Harry; credited for SpaceX; Wilson's top-rated manager | unknown | [20VC ~13:00, ~52:00](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n| Lior Susan (Eclipse) | GP; source of Cerebras | unknown | [20VC ~51:00](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n| Andrew Feldman (Cerebras) | Portfolio CEO they \"like\" | unknown | [20VC ~38:15](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n| Erik Lundberg (Michigan) | Peer CIO he respects (\"Eric\") | n/a | [20VC ~52:30](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n| Seth Alexander (MITIMCo) | Peer CIO he respects | n/a | [20VC ~52:30](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n| \"Robert\" (needs-id) | Peer CIO he respects | n/a | [20VC ~52:30](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n| Jane Dietze / Joshua Kennedy (Brown) | Peer CIOs he respects | n/a | [20VC ~52:30](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n| Sequoia Capital | Former manager; stepped back at evergreen | n/a | [20VC ~32:30](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026) |\n| TIFF | TAS Advisory Board | unknown | [TIFF](https://www.tiff.org/?p=4719) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who is \"Adam\"? Who are the Anthropic-exposure partners?\n- Which fund was added last year, and which next year?\n- Is there any WashU allocation to Founders Fund today?\n- Identify \"Robert\" among the peer CIOs.\n\n## Sources\n- https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026\n- https://endowment.wustl.edu/people/scott-l-wilson/\n- https://grinnell.edu/news/special-campus-memo-scott-wilson\n- https://source.washu.edu/2017/09/scott-wilson-appointed-chief-investment-officer-washington-university/\n- https://www.tiff.org/?p=4719\n- https://www.bloomberglinea.com.br/negocios/como-um-aporte-na-spacex-ha-uma-decada-rendeu-us-2-bi-a-uma-universidade-dos-eua/\n- https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/capital-allocators-inside-the-institutional-investment-industry/episode/cio-greatest-hits-endowments-scott-wilson-washu-90200\n", "person:sean-liu": "\n# Sean Liu\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the Founders Fund partner quoted on the deal itself when the firm led Crusoe's $600M Series D (AI compute), and he came from a hard-tech CFO seat, so he may be the firm's capital-intensive infrastructure operator. But his public footprint is nearly empty and the sources do not show he sourced the deal.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He comes from the operator side of a capital-intensive scale-up. As Solugen's CFO he \"led the finance, strategy, and operations functions\" through its move from pilot to first commercial plant. Before that he worked on the investing side at Silver Lake, Vy Capital and SoftBank ([src](https://foundersfund.com/team/sean-liu/)). That mix suits judging AI data-center buildouts, where financing structure matters as much as the technology.\n- **What constrains him.** The one sourced AI deal is an inside-led follow-on. Founders Fund already held Crusoe from the 2021 Series B and the 2022 Series C. It then led the D in 2024-12, about 9 months after his team page went up (2024-03-18) ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-series-b), [src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-millions-in-new-capital), [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/sean-liu/)). The relationship predates him, and he shared the release with Peter Thiel. So the evidence fits \"owned a late, large follow-on\" better than \"first-in at seed\".\n- **What he'd never do.** Nothing found. No public writing or interviews were located that state what he rules out.\n\n## Differential insight\nThin. No podcast, Substack, X or talk by him was found (web search budget was exhausted mid-run; see Open questions). Everything below comes from press releases and the firm site, so none of it is high non-obvious.\n- **Timing: he arrived after the position existed.** Founders Fund was a non-lead participant in Crusoe's B (2021-04) and C (2022-04), then \"The investment was led by Founders Fund\" for the D (2024-12-12) ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-series-d-funding)). After that it went back to participant: Valor and Mubadala co-led the E, and Atreides, Mubadala and Valor co-led the F, with Founders Fund named only among \"new and existing investors\" ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-f-funding)). So Founders Fund stepped up once, at $2.8B, and did not defend the lead at $10B+ or $30.9B.\n- **How he pitches the thesis.** His quote stresses vertical integration and unit economics: \"By innovating at every layer of the stack\" Crusoe delivers \"unmatched price-performance, speed-to-market\" ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-series-d-funding)). Thiel's quote on the same release is the slogan (\"we don't go big enough\"). Liu's is the operating case. The release does not name the deal partner, so read this as an inference, not proof that Liu ran the deal.\n- **Generic people-first line.** \"We invest in smart people solving difficult problems\" ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-series-d-funding)). This is a stock line and carries low signal.\n- **Operator credential.** The Founders Fund bio says he \"supported the company’s scale up from initial pilot to its first commercial facility\" at Solugen ([src](https://foundersfund.com/team/sean-liu/)). Getting a first commercial plant built is the same kind of problem as financing a first gigawatt campus.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** I'm downgrading the `high` in the founders-fund lead row as of 2026-11-10. THESIS.md reserves high for backing frontier AI, chips or compute *at seed or Series A*. The only AI deal attributable to Liu is a Series D growth lead in a company his firm already owned. He matters to me as:\n- a likely Founders Fund counterpart for compute and data-center infrastructure (relevant if a lab I back needs capacity, or if I see an AI-compute seed), and\n- a capital-markets-minded partner at a firm that does write early lab checks.\nI found no seed, Series A, lab or chip deal of his. If one turns up, move him to high.\n\n## Background\n- BS, International Economics, Georgetown University. Date not stated ([src](https://foundersfund.com/team/sean-liu/)).\n- Investment and operating roles in Asia and the US at Silver Lake, Google, Vy Capital and SoftBank. Dates and order not stated ([src](https://foundersfund.com/team/sean-liu/)).\n- CFO, Solugen (bio-based chemicals). Led finance, strategy and operations from pilot to first commercial facility. Dates not stated ([src](https://foundersfund.com/team/sean-liu/)).\n- Partner, Founders Fund. His team page was published 2024-03-18 and last modified 2025-07-17, per the page metadata. That is a proxy for the join date, not a stated start date ([src](https://foundersfund.com/team/sean-liu/)).\n- Still listed as Partner on the Founders Fund team page as of 2026-11-10 ([src](https://foundersfund.com/team/sean-liu/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://foundersfund.com/team/sean-liu/ | Firm bio; as of 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | The Founders Fund page has a Twitter link with an empty username; no handle verified (2026-11-10) |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | Not verified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was exhausted for this run, so this is a coverage gap, not proof he has no appearances.\n\n## How they think\n- **Vertical integration plus cost curve.** In public he frames AI infrastructure as a full-stack, price-performance and speed-to-market problem ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-series-d-funding)).\n- **Capital-intensive hard tech.** Both sourced chapters (Solugen's plant scale-up and Crusoe's data centers) involve physical buildouts financed with large capital stacks ([src](https://foundersfund.com/team/sean-liu/)). That is an inference from two data points.\n- Contrarian views: nothing found.\n- Calls right or wrong: Crusoe's valuation went from $2.8B at the D (2024-12) to $30.9B post at the F (2026-09) ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-series-d-funding), [src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-f-funding)). On paper the D lead looks right. Founders Fund did not lead later rounds.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Crusoe Series D release, his only sourced public statement on a deal: https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-series-d-funding\n2. Founders Fund bio, his operator and investor background: https://foundersfund.com/team/sean-liu/\n3. `companies/crusoe.md` in this repo, the full round map showing where Founders Fund sat in each round.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No verified X handle.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Crusoe | Series D, $600M at $2.8B | 2024-12-12 | Firm led; Liu quoted as Partner (deal partner not stated) | Fidelity; Long Journey Ventures; Mubadala; NVIDIA; Ribbit Capital; Valor Equity Partners | [src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-series-d-funding) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Crusoe Series D (2024-12-12).** Founders Fund had been a non-lead investor since the 2021 Series B, which Valor led ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-series-b)), and returned for the 2022 Series C, which G2VP led ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-millions-in-new-capital)). It led the $600M D, with Thiel and Liu both quoted ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-series-d-funding)). The intro path, internal deal ownership and check size are not public in what I found. Afterwards Founders Fund was only a participant in the E (2025-10) ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-e-funding)) and F (2026-09) ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-f-funding)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Founders Fund | Partner | 2024 (team page posted 2024-03-18) | [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/sean-liu/) |\n| Peter Thiel | Co-quoted Founders Fund partner on the Crusoe D | 2024-12-12 | [src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-series-d-funding) |\n| Crusoe (Chase Lochmiller, CEO) | Investor; firm led the D | 2024-12-12 | [src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-series-d-funding) |\n| Solugen | Former CFO | dates not stated | [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/sean-liu/) |\n| Silver Lake; Google; Vy Capital; SoftBank | Former investment and operating roles | dates not stated | [src](https://foundersfund.com/team/sean-liu/) |\n| Valor Equity Partners (Antonio Gracias) | Co-investor on the Crusoe D (Valor led B, co-led E and F) | 2024-12-12 | [src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-series-d-funding) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Was Liu the internal deal partner on the Crusoe D, or did Thiel or someone else own it? Check a Founders Fund or Crusoe podcast, or Lochmiller interviews.\n- Exact start date at Founders Fund, and Solugen CFO tenure dates. Solugen's newsroom index shows nothing on him.\n- Any seed or Series A, lab or chip deals since 2024? Check Founders Fund announcements with \"Sean Liu\" for AI infra, energy and data centers.\n- Verify an X or LinkedIn handle before recording any follower counts.\n- Vy Capital (a prior employer) is worth checking: what he did there and when, and whether it connects to any AI deals (not researched).\n- Web search hit the per-turn budget during this run. Rerun discovery for podcasts, talks and X.\n\n## Sources\n- https://foundersfund.com/team/sean-liu/\n- https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-series-d-funding\n- https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-series-b\n- https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-closes-millions-in-new-capital\n- https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-e-funding\n- https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-f-funding\n- https://solugen.com/newsroom (checked; no mention found)\n", "person:serge-lemonde": "\n# Serge Lemonde\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. The task lead said \"single unverified source (needs-id)\". That status is resolved: NVIDIA's own blog has an author page for him with the same title, \"senior director of NVIDIA's Inception program worldwide\", and three posts from 2021 to 2023 ([NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/sergelemonde/)). His identity and role are now verified from a first-party source. Both bios use \"he\". \"Senior director\" maps to seniority `vp` as the closest allowed value (same call as [clifford-platt](clifford-platt.md)). `check_capacity: sub-10m` is the closest allowed value too. He writes no equity checks at all.*\n\n**Known for:** the long-tenured NVIDIA insider (since 2000) who, by his 2025 bio, architected NVIDIA's free, no-equity startup program (Inception) around 2016 and has run it worldwide as senior director since. He is the program's operating owner, not an investor.\n\n## One-line take\nNot a check-writer. He is the operating owner of the program that NVIDIA uses to meet almost every AI startup before NVentures or corporate development does, so he is a door into NVIDIA, not into capital.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** Inception is NVIDIA's widest-funnel startup relationship. In 2021 he wrote that its 8,500+ members were \"about two-thirds of the total number of AI startups worldwide\" ([NVIDIA blog 2021-08-02](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/inception-8k-members/)), and the program passed 10,000 members in 110 countries by March 2022 ([NVIDIA blog 2022-03-23](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/inception-expands-beyond-10k-with-omniverse-climate-startups/)). He has owned that funnel since it was designed, per the RAISE bio.\n- **Constraint:** Inception has no equity and no capital. Its benefits are credits, discounts, DGX leasing, training and \"matchmaking with investors\" ([NVIDIA blog 2023-11-17](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/scaleway-european-startups-inception/)). Equity decisions sit in NVentures (Siddeek) and corporate development (Bhagwati) ([firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md)). No source links him to any investment decision.\n- **Hierarchy (inferred):** Howard Wright joined from AWS in 2024 as VP of Inception ([NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/howardwright/)). That puts a VP above a senior director who built the program. VC Alliance relationships are run by Sydney Sykes ([sources/nvidia-check-writers.md](../sources/nvidia-check-writers.md)). Lemonde's remit is the global program operation. That is inferred from titles; no org chart was found.\n- **What he'd never do:** take equity or price a round. Inception is defined by \"startups never have to give up equity to join\" ([NVIDIA blog 2021-08-02](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/inception-8k-members/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He is described as the program's architect, not just a manager.** The RAISE bio says he became sales director \"right before architecting the NVIDIA for startups program\" \"nearly 9 years ago\" ([RAISE 2025](https://tickets.raisesummit.com/2025/speaker/1769247/serge-lemonde)). That puts it around 2016, matching Inception's launch era. This sits next to the repo's existing framing that Jeff Herbst was \"Head of NVIDIA GPU Ventures and Inception\" until 2021 ([sources/nvidia-check-writers.md](../sources/nvidia-check-writers.md)). The likely reading (inferred) is that Herbst was the executive owner and Lemonde was the builder and operator who stayed on after Herbst left. Neither source says this directly.\n- **His two bios disagree on his early career.** NVIDIA's blog says he joined in 2000 \"following a career that included\" the sales director role for cloud service providers and a marketing director role at a French systems integrator ([NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/sergelemonde/)). The 2025 RAISE bio instead says he \"joined NVIDIA in 2000, as Marketing director for EMEA\" and then became the CSP sales director *inside* NVIDIA ([RAISE 2025](https://tickets.raisesummit.com/2025/speaker/1769247/serge-lemonde)). The self-supplied conference bio is more recent. If it is right, he is a 25-year NVIDIA lifer who moved from European CSP sales straight into designing the startup program. That would mean Inception was designed by a cloud-provider channel seller, which fits the Scaleway credits deal below.\n- **Inception doubles as NVIDIA's market-intelligence census.** He used Inception's own member data to map the global AI startup landscape by sector and country (\"How do we know this?\", [NVIDIA blog 2021-08-02](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/inception-8k-members/)). It reported $60B+ of cumulative member funding at the time. Whoever runs the program sees a near-complete startup census, which is the data NVentures-style sourcing would want. That use is inferred; no source says NVentures uses it.\n- **The European sovereign-cloud credits channel is his.** He authored the 2023 announcement of Scaleway (iliad Group) cloud credits on its H100 cluster for Inception members, naming Hugging Face as an early user ([NVIDIA blog 2023-11-17](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/scaleway-european-startups-inception/)). This is the compute-access route for European seed labs, and it is a CSP partnership, the same channel his RAISE bio says he sold into.\n- No podcasts, X account, interviews or GCV coverage were found (search 2026-11-10). The 2025 RAISE Summit listing is his only conference appearance found, and no session title was listed.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low** (as of 2026-11-10). He is not an investor, writes no checks, is named on no round, and Inception takes no equity. Instrumental value only: (1) a portfolio-support door for compute credits, GPU access and NVIDIA technical engagement for companies I back, especially European seed labs, given the Scaleway channel; (2) a possible warm path inward to NVentures, which is the pool that writes into lab seeds (Ineffable, RadixArk, Inherent per [firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md)). No source links him to any of those deals.\n\n## Background\n- Education: Stanford Graduate School of Business alumnus (program and year not given); bachelor's degree in electronics and industrial solutions ([NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/sergelemonde/)).\n- Pre-2000 (NVIDIA blog version): sales director for cloud service providers in Southern Europe and the Middle East, and marketing director at a systems integrator in France ([NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/sergelemonde/)).\n- 2000: joined NVIDIA. RAISE version: Marketing Director EMEA, then Sales Director for CSPs, Southern Europe and Middle East ([RAISE 2025](https://tickets.raisesummit.com/2025/speaker/1769247/serge-lemonde)).\n- ~2016: architected the NVIDIA startup program, dated as \"nearly 9 years ago\" in the 2025 bio ([RAISE 2025](https://tickets.raisesummit.com/2025/speaker/1769247/serge-lemonde)).\n- 2021 to 2025: Senior Director, Inception (worldwide), the title on NVIDIA blog posts dated 2021-08-02 to 2023-11-17 and on the 2025 RAISE listing.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | |\n| Substack / blog | [NVIDIA blog author page](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/sergelemonde/) | 3 posts, 2021-08 to 2023-11; none since (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not located via search | not verified |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025 — RAISE Summit 2025 — speaker listing, no session title shown — [link](https://tickets.raisesummit.com/2025/speaker/1769247/serge-lemonde) — the bio frames his remit as working with \"investors and VCs around the world\".\n- No podcasts or recorded talks found.\n\n## How they think\n- Writing frames Inception as a census and lens: \"Startups are the single best lens into the future of modern AI\" ([NVIDIA blog 2021-08-02](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/inception-8k-members/)).\n- Program design rests on no fees and no equity, with value delivered through partner credits (Scaleway), discounts (NVIDIA AI Enterprise), DGX leasing and DLI training ([2022 post](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/inception-expands-beyond-10k-with-omniverse-climate-startups/), [2023 post](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/scaleway-european-startups-inception/)).\n- No contrarian views or calls on record.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Better Than 8K Resolution (2021-08-02)](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/inception-8k-members/): Inception member data as a global AI startup census.\n2. [NVIDIA and Scaleway (2023-11-17)](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/scaleway-european-startups-inception/): the European sovereign-cloud credits route for Inception startups.\n3. [Inception new benefits (2022-03-23)](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/inception-expands-beyond-10k-with-omniverse-climate-startups/): what the program actually gives, including DGX leasing for US members.\n4. [RAISE 2025 bio](https://tickets.raisesummit.com/2025/speaker/1769247/serge-lemonde): the \"architect\" claim and the career timeline.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public social presence located.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| none | n/a | n/a | n/a | n/a | Inception takes no equity ([NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/inception-8k-members/)); no round names him |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. He is named on no funding round. The closest thing to a deal is the Scaleway partnership (2023-11-17), a compute-credits partnership, not an investment ([NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/scaleway-european-startups-inception/)).\n\n**How a founder reaches him:** apply to NVIDIA Inception (free; [nvidia.com/startups](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/)). For investor routing, the Inception VC Alliance is run by Sydney Sykes ([sources/nvidia-check-writers.md](../sources/nvidia-check-writers.md)). Direct contact: European AI conferences (RAISE 2025), or NVIDIA's EMEA CSP partners such as Scaleway. There is no public email or social handle.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NVIDIA | Employee; Senior Director, Inception (worldwide) | 2000 (Inception from ~2016) | [RAISE 2025](https://tickets.raisesummit.com/2025/speaker/1769247/serge-lemonde), [NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/sergelemonde/) |\n| Howard Wright | VP of Inception since 2024; likely his superior (inferred from titles) | 2024 | [NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/howardwright/) |\n| Jeff Herbst | Former head of GPU Ventures and Inception until 2021; overlapping leadership (inferred) | ~2016–2021 | [sources/nvidia-check-writers.md](../sources/nvidia-check-writers.md) |\n| Scaleway (iliad Group) | Partner; he announced its Inception credits program | 2023-11 | [NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/scaleway-european-startups-inception/) |\n| Hugging Face | Inception member named in his Scaleway post | 2023-11 | [NVIDIA blog](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/scaleway-european-startups-inception/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which early-career account is right: did the CSP sales director role come before or after he joined NVIDIA in 2000? (The two bios conflict.)\n- Reporting line to Howard Wright, and whether Inception deal flow is formally routed to NVentures.\n- Whether he is still at NVIDIA after 2025 (no post since 2023-11).\n- Where he is based (likely Europe, not confirmed).\n\n## Sources\n- NVIDIA blog author page: https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/sergelemonde/\n- RAISE Summit 2025 speaker listing: https://tickets.raisesummit.com/2025/speaker/1769247/serge-lemonde\n- NVIDIA blog 2021-08-02: https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/inception-8k-members/\n- NVIDIA blog 2022-03-23: https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/inception-expands-beyond-10k-with-omniverse-climate-startups/\n- NVIDIA blog 2023-11-17: https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/scaleway-european-startups-inception/\n- Howard Wright author page: https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/howardwright/\n- Repo: firms/nvidia.md, sources/nvidia-check-writers.md\n", "person:seth-winterroth": "\n# Seth Winterroth\n\n## One-line take\nHe is Eclipse's longest-tenured non-founder deal partner on robotics, autonomy and compute. His standout record is Oxide: Eclipse led its seed and Series A in 2019 and its $445M Series D in 2026-10, and he wrote both stories. He is not the Cerebras partner.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He holds positions for a long time and sits on many boards. MarketScreener (aggregator) lists about eight director seats.\n  - He started in robotics early. Kiva was his origin story, and 6 River Systems was his first Eclipse deal.\n  - He stays in his best positions through very large later rounds: Oxide from seed to D over seven years, Wayve A and B, True Anomaly A through D.\n  - His network is prior colleagues in robotics. Kevin Peterson's route into Eclipse's Bedrock Robotics ran through him.\n- **Constraint.** The Eclipse mandate is the physical economy. That excludes pure-software frontier labs, so his AI exposure comes through hardware.\n  - Compute hardware: Oxide, plus a board seat at the photonic-quantum company Quantum Source (aggregator).\n  - Embodied AI: Wayve, Foxglove, Bedrock.\n- **What he'd never do.** Commercialize before the system design works. He warns founders against exactly that ([src](https://foxglove.dev/blog/spotlight-seth-winterroth-on-the-joys-and-challenges-of-working-with-robotics-startups)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He is not the Cerebras partner.** The task framing linked him to Eclipse's Cerebras lineage, but the long Cerebras origin post never names him. That post credits Pierre Lamond with the origin and the TSMC introduction, and Lior Susan with the G42 and OpenAI introductions ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era)). His compute deal is Oxide. Eclipse led Oxide's seed and Series A in 2019, and he writes \"we led their seed and Series A rounds\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/built-for-the-compute-ownership-era), 2026-10-09).\n- **The Oxide Series D thesis is \"compute ownership\" for AI.** He frames the $445M lead as a bet that AI users want to run on hardware they own: they \"don't want to pour their most valuable data into infrastructure someone else controls\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/built-for-the-compute-ownership-era)). The post claims revenue grew nearly 80x from 2025 to 2026. Atreides and AMD Ventures came in new, alongside existing investors USIT, Riot Ventures and Jane Street.\n- **He was not first into Wayve.** He met Alex Kendall during Wayve's \"proof of concept phase\", about a year before a 2019 test ride. Eclipse then led the late-2019 Series A and the $200M Series B in 2022 ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/wayve-s-road-to-industrial-scale)). Another profile in this repo cites a 2017 Wayve seed with Compound in it (seedtable, aggregator), so Eclipse was the first Series A lead, not the first money in.\n- **His network is colleagues, not chip-industry elders.** On Bedrock Robotics, Eclipse writes that \"Kevin had previously worked with Eclipse Partner Seth Winterroth\" ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock)). Boris Sofman came to the deal separately, through Aidan Madigan-Curtis. The post doesn't say where Winterroth and Peterson worked together.\n- **His origin story is Kiva.** He calls Amazon's purchase of Kiva \"the acquisition that launched a thousand robotics ventures\" ([src](https://foxglove.dev/blog/spotlight-seth-winterroth-on-the-joys-and-challenges-of-working-with-robotics-startups), 2023-01-08). The Boost VC show notes say 6 River Systems, a Kiva-gap warehouse-robotics bet, was his first Eclipse investment. Shopify bought 6 River in 2019, at $450M or $500M depending on the source ([src](https://www.boost.vc/bvc/2023/5/22/adam-draper-interviews-seth-winterroth-of-eclipse-ventures), publisher summary).\n- **His founder advice: go narrow, spend time with customers, don't commercialize early.**\n  - \"I see companies trying to commercialize too early.\"\n  - \"Focus on the highest-value applications possible.\"\n  - \"So many companies just don't spend enough time in the customers' environment.\"\n  - All three are from the Foxglove interview ([src](https://foxglove.dev/blog/spotlight-seth-winterroth-on-the-joys-and-challenges-of-working-with-robotics-startups)).\n- **He bets on manufacturing.** \"I think manufacturing is going to experience the biggest upside\" (same source). He links that to cheaper, better hardware components.\n- **Process (publisher summary, not his verbatim words).** The Boost VC notes describe a market-first method: start with customer pain points, then find an \"n-of-1\" specialist. They also describe Eclipse's Venture Equity program as \"engineer storm vs. wait for lightning to strike\" ([src](https://www.boost.vc/bvc/2023/5/22/adam-draper-interviews-seth-winterroth-of-eclipse-ventures)). Low confidence.\n- **A WashU link that is not an LP link.** The Takeoff podcast host met him when he visited WashU for a career fair ([src](https://thetakeoff.substack.com/p/seth-winterroth-partner-at-eclipse), 2021-04-15). WashU is a confirmed Eclipse LP (see `firms/eclipse.md`). Nothing ties him to that LP relationship.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is a check-writer at a fund that can lead $10M+ seeds and $400M+ growth rounds, and Eclipse is the THESIS archetype. But his own book is mostly robotics, autonomy, space and medical devices, not labs or AI chips.\n\nThe deals that matter to me:\n- **Oxide**, owned AI compute from seed through D.\n- **Quantum Source**, photonic quantum computing. Eclipse led the $50M Series A in 2024-09 ([src](https://www.qs-labs.com/post/n-quantum-source-raises-50-million-series-a-funding-to-make-scalable-useful-quantum-computing-a-reality)). His board seat is aggregator-only.\n- **Wayve**, an embodied-AI lab.\n\nHe is not the person for a pre-training lab. For Eclipse AI chips, see Greg Reichow (Efficient Computer) and Lior Susan (Cerebras).\n\n## Background\n- Claremont McKenna College, class of 2012. CMC's innovation center billed a 2019 talk as \"Seth Winterroth '12\" ([src](https://cie.cmc.edu/calendar/2019-04-10/)).\n- A year of travel after graduating, then GE, then GE Ventures, then Eclipse ([src](https://thetakeoff.substack.com/p/seth-winterroth-partner-at-eclipse)).\n- At GE Ventures he invested in robotics, additive manufacturing and industrial IoT ([src](https://techcrunch.com/author/seth-winterroth/)). Aggregator dates for the associate role: 2013-05 to 2015-05 ([src](https://me.sh/profile/seth-winterroth), aggregator).\n- He joined Eclipse at its 2015 founding: the aggregator lists his Eclipse start as 2015-04 ([src](https://marketscreener.com/insider/SETH-WINTERROTH-A2F90E), aggregator), and the firm dates its own inception to 2015 ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/wayve-s-road-to-industrial-scale)). Partner per the Eclipse team page ([src](https://eclipse.capital/team/seth-winterroth)). The Boost VC notes (2023) credit him with about nine years in VC.\n- **Correction for the merge.** `patterns/2026-11-10-who-did-the-crazy-deals.md` says \"Partner since 2015\". The Wayve post it cites dates Eclipse's founding, not his title. His partner-promotion date is unsourced.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [eclipse.capital/team/seth-winterroth](https://eclipse.capital/team/seth-winterroth) | Firm team page; bylined posts on [eclipse.capital/blog](https://eclipse.capital/blog) are the best record of his deals |\n| Substack / blog | [TechCrunch author page](https://techcrunch.com/author/seth-winterroth/) | Guest contributor; article list not retrievable (2026-11-10) |\n| X | not found | No personal account verified; firm account is [x.com/EclipseVentures](https://x.com/EclipseVentures) |\n| LinkedIn | not found | No verified URL; firm page [linkedin.com/company/eclipse-vc](https://www.linkedin.com/company/eclipse-vc/) |\n| GitHub | not found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only (see below) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- undated — Industrial Evolution — with Eclipse partner Kushagra Vaid ([src](https://directory.libsyn.com/episode/index/show/industrialevolution/id/20433581)). Not listened to; search-snippet description only.\n- 2023-05 — Boost VC Deep Tech Series Ep. 2 (also syndicated as \"Can't Be Done\") — with Adam Draper — [link](https://www.boost.vc/bvc/2023/5/22/adam-draper-interviews-seth-winterroth-of-eclipse-ventures). Show notes cover the market-first thesis and 6 River Systems as his first Eclipse deal. His advice to his younger self: don't rush to build a track record (summary).\n- 2023-01 — Foxglove blog spotlight interview (written Q&A by Esther Weon) — [link](https://foxglove.dev/blog/spotlight-seth-winterroth-on-the-joys-and-challenges-of-working-with-robotics-startups). Kiva origin, premature commercialization, manufacturing upside.\n- 2021-04 — The Takeoff Podcast (Lukas Steinbock) — [link](https://thetakeoff.substack.com/p/seth-winterroth-partner-at-eclipse). Career path, mentors, mental health.\n- 2019-04 — Claremont McKenna CIE talk, \"How to Break Into Venture Capital\" — [link](https://cie.cmc.edu/calendar/2019-04-10/).\n- A search snippet mentioned an NVIDIA AI Podcast appearance about robotics. It was not confirmed, so treat it as an open question.\n\n## How they think\n- **Robotics is gated by economics and design maturity, not demand.** He notes that \"hardware elements have gotten higher in performance and lower in cost\" (Foxglove).\n- **Market first, then the team.** Start from customer pain, find the n-of-1 technologist, then add engineers who know how to scale (Boost summary).\n- **Compute sovereignty.** AI pushes enterprises back toward owning their infrastructure. This is the Oxide Series D thesis.\n- **Defense and space.** He believed \"it was imperative for the U.S. to achieve space superiority\" when Eclipse led True Anomaly's Series A ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-next-frontier-of-the-space-economy)).\n- **Calls he got right.**\n  - 6 River (sold to Shopify).\n  - Oxide, held from 2019 through the $445M D.\n  - Wayve, led the A in 2019 before SoftBank's $1.05B C in 2024.\n  - True Anomaly, led the A in 2023 and co-led the $650M D in 2026.\n- **Calls he got wrong.** None sourced.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Built for the compute-ownership era](https://eclipse.capital/blog/built-for-the-compute-ownership-era) (2026-10). Oxide Series D and his AI-infra thesis.\n2. [Wayve's road to industrial scale](https://eclipse.capital/blog/wayve-s-road-to-industrial-scale) (2026-04). How he met Kendall and Eclipse's A and B leads.\n3. [Foxglove spotlight](https://foxglove.dev/blog/spotlight-seth-winterroth-on-the-joys-and-challenges-of-working-with-robotics-startups) (2023-01). His only long first-person Q&A.\n4. [The next frontier of the space economy](https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-next-frontier-of-the-space-economy) (2026-04). True Anomaly Series D.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No verified personal X or LinkedIn presence; his public output is Eclipse blog bylines.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Oxide | Seed and Series A | 2019 | yes (Eclipse) | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/built-for-the-compute-ownership-era) |\n| Oxide | Series D $445M | 2026-10-09 | yes (Eclipse) | USIT, Riot Ventures, Jane Street (existing); Atreides, AMD Ventures (new) | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/built-for-the-compute-ownership-era) |\n| Wayve | Series A | late 2019 | yes | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/wayve-s-road-to-industrial-scale) |\n| Wayve | Series B $200M | 2022 | yes | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/wayve-s-road-to-industrial-scale) |\n| True Anomaly | Series A | 2023 | yes | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-next-frontier-of-the-space-economy) |\n| True Anomaly | Series D $650M | 2026-04-28 | co-led | | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-next-frontier-of-the-space-economy) |\n| Quantum Source | Series A $50M | 2024-09 | yes (Eclipse; his board seat aggregator-only) | Standard Investments, Level VC, Canon Equity, Pitango First, Grove Ventures, 10D, Dell Technologies Capital | [src](https://www.qs-labs.com/post/n-quantum-source-raises-50-million-series-a-funding-to-make-scalable-useful-quantum-computing-a-reality) |\n| 6 River Systems | first Eclipse deal (round not stated) | ~2015-16 | not stated | | [src](https://www.boost.vc/bvc/2023/5/22/adam-draper-interviews-seth-winterroth-of-eclipse-ventures) (summary) |\n| Foxglove | Series B $40M (Eclipse led the 2022 A) | 2025-11 | no | | `data/deals.csv` (eclipse.capital/blog/The-Robotics-Accelerator) |\n| ForSight Robotics | Series B $125M | 2025-06 | yes | | `data/deals.csv` (eclipse.capital blog) |\n| Ursa Major | Series E $100M | 2025-11 | yes | co-bylined with Lior Susan | `data/deals.csv` |\n| Humble | Seed $24M | 2026-04-21 | yes | co-bylined with Jiten Behl | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/a-fresh-approach-to-autonomous-trucking) |\n| Third Wave Automation, Mytra, RideCo, Invicta Medical | board seats | 2018–2022 | n/a | | [src](https://marketscreener.com/insider/SETH-WINTERROTH-A2F90E) (aggregator) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Oxide.** Eclipse began working with Oxide in 2019, \"right when they launched\", and led the seed and Series A.\n  - Eclipse backed the Series B but did not lead it.\n  - Oxide shipped its first rack in 2023.\n  - Eclipse's growth fund then led the $445M Series D in 2026-10, citing nearly 80x revenue growth from 2025 to 2026 and data-center capex passing $1T ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/built-for-the-compute-ownership-era)).\n  - The founders are Bryan Cantrill and Steve Tuck.\n- **Wayve.** He met Alex Kendall while Wayve was still at proof of concept. About a year later he took a 2019 test ride, and Eclipse led the late-2019 Series A, then the 2022 $200M Series B.\n  - SoftBank led the $1.05B Series C in 2024, with Nvidia and Microsoft.\n  - Nissan, Mercedes-Benz, Stellantis, AMD, Arm and Qualcomm joined later ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/wayve-s-road-to-industrial-scale)).\n- **Bedrock Robotics.** One of two intro paths ran through him: Kevin Peterson had previously worked with him. Boris Sofman came through Madigan-Curtis ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock)).\n- **6 River Systems.** Per the Boost show notes, Kiva's sale to Amazon left a market gap that 6 River filled, and that gap is why he picked it.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bryan Cantrill, Steve Tuck (Oxide) | backer and author of the Oxide posts | 2019 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/built-for-the-compute-ownership-era) |\n| Alex Kendall (Wayve) | early backer; board seat (aggregator) | ~2018 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/wayve-s-road-to-industrial-scale) |\n| Kevin Peterson (Bedrock Robotics) | prior working tie; intro path to Eclipse | before 2024 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock) |\n| Even Rogers, Kyle Zakrzewski (True Anomaly) | led A, co-led D | 2023 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-next-frontier-of-the-space-economy) |\n| Lior Susan (Eclipse) | co-byline on Ursa Major | 2025 | `data/deals.csv` |\n| Jiten Behl (Eclipse) | co-byline on Humble | 2026 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/a-fresh-approach-to-autonomous-trucking) |\n| Kushagra Vaid (Eclipse) | co-guest, Industrial Evolution podcast | | [src](https://directory.libsyn.com/episode/index/show/industrialevolution/id/20433581) |\n| Adam Draper (Boost VC) | podcast host | 2023 | [src](https://www.boost.vc/bvc/2023/5/22/adam-draper-interviews-seth-winterroth-of-eclipse-ventures) |\n| GE Ventures | former employer | 2013–2015 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/author/seth-winterroth/) |\n| Claremont McKenna CIE | alumnus; advisory board (search snippet, unverified) | 2012 | [src](https://cie.cmc.edu/calendar/2019-04-10/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is he on the Oxide board, or only the author of its posts? Which Eclipse fund holds Oxide?\n- Where did he and Kevin Peterson work together? (Peterson co-founded Marble; this is not sourced.)\n- When was he promoted to partner? This decides `fast_promotion`.\n- Is there an NVIDIA AI Podcast episode with him? A snippet suggested one in 2024 but it was not confirmed.\n- What is his role at Quantum Source? It is the only quantum or compute board seat besides Oxide, and the seat is aggregator-only.\n- No LP or personal-fund commitments found.\n\n## Sources\n- https://eclipse.capital/team/seth-winterroth\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/built-for-the-compute-ownership-era\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/wayve-s-road-to-industrial-scale\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/the-next-frontier-of-the-space-economy\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era\n- https://eclipse.capital/blog/a-fresh-approach-to-autonomous-trucking\n- https://foxglove.dev/blog/spotlight-seth-winterroth-on-the-joys-and-challenges-of-working-with-robotics-startups\n- https://www.boost.vc/bvc/2023/5/22/adam-draper-interviews-seth-winterroth-of-eclipse-ventures\n- https://thetakeoff.substack.com/p/seth-winterroth-partner-at-eclipse\n- https://techcrunch.com/author/seth-winterroth/\n- https://cie.cmc.edu/calendar/2019-04-10/\n- https://www.qs-labs.com/post/n-quantum-source-raises-50-million-series-a-funding-to-make-scalable-useful-quantum-computing-a-reality\n- https://marketscreener.com/insider/SETH-WINTERROTH-A2F90E (aggregator)\n- https://me.sh/profile/seth-winterroth (aggregator)\n- https://directory.libsyn.com/episode/index/show/industrialevolution/id/20433581\n", "person:shahin-farshchi": "\n# Shahin Farshchi, PhD\n\n## One-line take\nHe is Lux's chip engineer-turned-GP who keeps backing the same AI-compute founders across cycles. He was a later investor in Nervana in 2015, led MosaicML when Nervana's founder Naveen Rao started it in 2021, and co-led Cognichip's $33M seed in 2025. That makes him a likely co-investor, and a source of founders, for any AI-chip or compute-efficiency seed.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He has an EE PhD (brain-signal chips at UCLA), co-founded a company from his PhD work, and has 20 years in semis. He builds companies with founders rather than only reacting to rounds. His witness bio says he \"led Lux's role in the formation\" of Flex Logix and Aeva ([src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf)).\n- **How he compounds:** He keeps backing the same chip lineage. Lux was in Nervana, then led MosaicML for the Nervana team, and Databricks bought MosaicML in 2023.\n- **What constrains him:** He is a hardware generalist spread across AI, chips, autonomy and space (Zoox, Planet, Relativity, Astranis, Impulse, Varda). Lux's founding-round AI-lab leads (Sakana, Together) are credited to other partners. In 2014 he said he avoids sharing-economy plays ([src](https://strictlyvc.com/?p=3761)).\n- **What he'd never do:** Back an app-layer marketplace with no engineering moat.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He sourced from university labs.** In 2014 he said \"My first deals were all university spin-outs.\" and \"I basically spent all my time walking up and down the halls\" of universities ([src](https://strictlyvc.com/?p=3761), StrictlyVC, 2014-07-28). He started as a principal working \"out of my car\" before Lux had a West Coast office.\n- **His 2014 check discipline.** He said \"we set aside $10 million to $15 million per company\", and that the biggest first check was \"in the higher single digits\" ([src](https://strictlyvc.com/?p=3761)). His more recent AI-chip seeds are $33M to $37M rounds that Lux led or co-led, so Lux's sizing has scaled up a lot.\n- **He was first into Flex Logix.** In July 2014 he said \"I just invested in a stealth company that's playing into this challenge\", meaning software-programmable silicon ([src](https://strictlyvc.com/?p=3761)). By October 2015 Lux was described as Flex Logix's \"founding investors Lux Capital\", and Eclipse led the next round ([src](https://venturebeat.com/technology/flex-logix-raises-7-4m-to-commercialize-its-clever-flexible-chip-designs)). His witness bio credits him with Lux's role in forming Flex Logix ([src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf)). Linking the 2014 \"stealth company\" to Flex Logix is my inference: Flex Logix was founded March 2014 per [eeNews](https://www.eenewseurope.com/en/fpga-fabric-startup-raises-5-millon).\n- **Lux was second into Nervana, not first.** DFJ led Nervana's $3.3M round in Aug 2014 ([src](https://socaltech.com/nervana_nabs_3_3m_for_machine_learning_hardware/s-0056585.html), search excerpt). Lux came in on the $20.5M round led by Data Collective (DCVC) on 2015-06-04, about 10 months later ([src](https://venturebeat.com/ai/deep-learning-startup-nervana-raises-20-5m)). Intel bought Nervana in Aug 2016 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2016/08/09/intel-buys-deep-learning-startup-nervana-systems-for-a-reported-350-million)).\n- **His Nervana bet repeated through founder loyalty.** Six years later Lux led MosaicML's $37M debut round ([src](https://siliconangle.com/2021/10/13/naveen-raos-startup-mosaicml-exits-stealth-mission-improve-ml-training/), 2021-10-13). MosaicML was started by Nervana alumni Naveen Rao and Hanlin Tang, with DCVC again alongside. His House bio says he led Lux's MosaicML investment: \"MosaicML, which is bringing the most powerful AI models\" to the enterprise ([src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf)). Databricks acquired MosaicML in 2023 ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/mosaicml)). My inference is that this is why Lux's Databricks card (2023) lists him ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/databricks)).\n- **He wrote the AI-for-chip-design thesis before the deal.** An Aug 2024 Lux/Securities essay by Danny Crichton, written \"with\" Farshchi and associate Dario Soatto, argues that design labor, not fabs, is the bottleneck. It favors \"founders with deliberate and clever strategies for gaining an early foothold\" against Cadence and Synopsys ([src](https://www.readsecurities.com/p/the-looming-labor-crisis-in-chip-design), 2024-08-03). Lux's Cognichip card dates the investment to 2024 ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/cognichip)). Lux and Mayfield co-led the $33M seed announced 2025-05-15, where he said \"Cognichip is introducing a new scaling factor for semiconductors: intelligence.\" ([src](https://markets.financialcontent.com/stocks/article/bizwire-2025-5-15-cognichip-launches-out-of-stealth-with-33m-in-seed-funding-to-deliver-artificial-chip-intelligence-aci)). These words are his, from a company press release.\n- **He was early on neuromorphic and analog chips.** In 2016 he wrote that AI favors noise-tolerant analog compute: \"Indeed, our brains are incredibly noisy systems that work just fine.\" He also named the main drawback: \"The biggest weakness of analog is that it's rigid\" ([src](https://spectrum.ieee.org/analog-and-neuromorphic-chips-will-rule-robotic-age), IEEE Spectrum, 2016-10-17). This frames how he would judge today's analog and in-memory AI-chip seeds.\n- **He has recently talked up younger chip founders.** The Primary \"Compute 100\" episode (Jul 22, likely 2026) covers \"the case for younger founders in chips\" and a shrinking set of venture-scale outcomes. This is from the episode description only. I did not hear the audio or see a transcript ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/podcast/id1896779326)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is the AI-semis and compute person at a firm that writes $10M+ checks and co-leads $30M+ seeds (Cognichip $33M, MosaicML $37M). Lux Fund IX is $1.5B ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/podcast/id1896779326)). The deals that matter to me:\n- Cognichip, co-led seed in 2025\n- MosaicML, led in 2021\n- Nervana, 2015\n- Flex Logix, founding investor in 2014\n\nHe is also a natural co-investor for a ~$20M check in an AI-chip or compute-efficiency seed, and a route to Naveen Rao's network. Rao's later lab venture is worth checking separately; I have no source in this pass. He is not a rising-star alpha target: he is a long-tenured GP with a slow 8-year principal-to-partner path. His frontier-lab exposure (Sakana, Physical Intelligence, Together, Hugging Face) is listed in his Lux bio portfolio, but those leads are credited to other Lux partners where cards say anything.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** BS in EECS from UC Berkeley. PhD at UCLA, where he developed neural-interface (brain-signal) chip technology ([src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf), [src](https://strictlyvc.com/?p=3761)).\n- **Operator years:** He developed hybrid electric vehicles at General Motors, worked as a software developer at Silicon Valley startups, and researched semiconductor manufacturing techniques ([src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf)).\n- **2004:** Co-founded Vista Integrated Systems, which built wireless vital-sign monitors from his PhD work ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/shahin-farshchi), [src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf)).\n- **2006:** Joined Lux as a principal ([src](https://strictlyvc.com/?p=3761)).\n- **~June 2014:** Promoted to partner; StrictlyVC on 2014-07-28 said \"last month\" ([src](https://strictlyvc.com/?p=3761)).\n- **By June 2023:** Titled General Partner in House testimony materials ([src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.luxcapital.com/people/shahin-farshchi | Lux bio, checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://spectrum.ieee.org/u/shahin-farshchi | 3 IEEE Spectrum op-eds, 2016–2017. Lux's Securities/Riskgaming is Crichton's, not his |\n| X | https://x.com/farshchi | @farshchi; page returned 402, so follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/shahinf | from Lux bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- **2026-07-22 (year inferred):** Primary, *The Compute 100*, \"Shahin Farshchi, General Partner at Lux Capital\" ([link](https://podcasts.apple.com/podcast/id1896779326)). Topics: the semis cycle, younger founders in chips, and fewer venture-scale outcomes. Description only; no transcript found.\n- **2026:** *We The Builders*, a conversation at Hill & Valley Forum 2026 ([link](https://pocketcasts.com/podcast/we-the-builders/966d5ca0-c0ae-013e-bbc1-0acc26574db2/in-conversation-with-shahin-farshchi-at-lux-capital-7b-fund-accelerating-scifi/96137b19-3419-40fc-9cc9-9e8f81d871e3)). The page returned 403, so content was not checked.\n- **2025-03:** Blubrry interview on his background and Lux in hard tech ([link](https://blubrry.com/alitest/143751398)). Search excerpt only.\n- **2024-09:** Riskgaming with Danny Crichton, \"AI is spiking chip design costs – can it solve them too?\" ([link](https://metacast.app/podcast/riskgaming/3C63w3AJ/ai-is-spiking-chip-design-costs-can-it-solve-them-too/2UngPNgZ)). Page returned 403; no transcript found.\n- **2024-03-20:** *Venture with Grace*, \"Future Building\" ([link](https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/venture-with-grace/episode/future-building-investment-insights-with-lux-capitals-shahin-farshchi-58022)). Not transcribed; the show notes say Lux's founders invited him to partner in 2006.\n- **2023-06-22:** Witness at a House hearing (Science, Space and Technology; witness bio PDF) ([link](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf)). Topic not confirmed.\n- **2015-05:** O'Reilly webinar on how hardware startups build value ([link](https://oreilly.com/pub/e/3335)). Search excerpt only.\n- **2020:** *Business of AI* podcast ([link](https://businessofai.libsyn.com/2020/02)). Not reviewed.\n\n## How they think\n- **Physics-level moats.** He backs chips, sensors and vehicles where the engineering is the moat, and avoids \"rent-your couch\" marketplaces ([src](https://strictlyvc.com/?p=3761)).\n- **AI changes the compute substrate.**\n  - 2016: he argued neural nets tolerate noise, which favors analog and neuromorphic hardware ([src](https://spectrum.ieee.org/analog-and-neuromorphic-chips-will-rule-robotic-age)).\n  - 2014–2016: he backed programmable silicon (Flex Logix) and an ASIC for neural nets (Nervana).\n  - 2024–2025: he moved to AI that designs the chips (Cognichip).\n- **Design labor is the semis bottleneck.** He frames AI design automation as the way to democratize custom silicon (Aug 2024 essay he co-shaped, [src](https://www.readsecurities.com/p/the-looming-labor-crisis-in-chip-design)).\n- **Calls that paid off:** Nervana went to Intel within about 14 months of Lux's round. MosaicML went to Databricks (reported $1.3B) within about 20 months of its debut. Zoox went to Amazon.\n- **Calls that went badly:** None of his own admissions were found. Intel later shut down Nervana development in favor of Habana (Jan 2020, per [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nervana_Systems)), which was a loss for Intel, not for Lux.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [StrictlyVC, \"The PhD VC\" (2014)](https://strictlyvc.com/?p=3761): how he sourced from university halls, his early check sizes, and what he avoids.\n2. [IEEE Spectrum, analog and neuromorphic chips (2016)](https://spectrum.ieee.org/analog-and-neuromorphic-chips-will-rule-robotic-age): his own AI-hardware thesis.\n3. [Securities, \"The looming labor crisis in chip design\" (2024)](https://www.readsecurities.com/p/the-looming-labor-crisis-in-chip-design): the AI-for-EDA thesis behind Cognichip. Crichton wrote it with his input.\n4. [House witness bio (2023)](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf): the most explicit list of deals he \"led\" and companies he helped form.\n5. [IEEE Spectrum, driverless startups (2017)](https://spectrum.ieee.org/driverless-tech-startups-are-driving-past-a-trilliondollar-opportunity): his autonomy thesis from the Zoox era.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: x.com/farshchi returned 402, so posts and engagement could not be read. Lux bios link @farshchi.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cognichip (AI for chip design) | Seed, $33M (Lux investment dated 2024 on card) | 2025-05-15 | Co-led (Lux + Mayfield) | Mayfield, FPV, Candou Ventures. Seligman led a $60M follow-on in 2026-04 | [src](https://markets.financialcontent.com/stocks/article/bizwire-2025-5-15-cognichip-launches-out-of-stealth-with-33m-in-seed-funding-to-deliver-artificial-chip-intelligence-aci), [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=696697), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/cognichip) |\n| MosaicML | Debut round, $37M | 2021-10-13 | Lux led (DCVC named alongside); his bio says he led Lux's investment | DCVC, Future Ventures, Playground Global, AME, Correlation, E14 | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2021/10/13/naveen-raos-startup-mosaicml-exits-stealth-mission-improve-ml-training/), [src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf) |\n| Databricks | Lux investment (likely via MosaicML acquisition; my inference) | 2023 | no | — | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/databricks) |\n| Nervana Systems (AI ASIC) | $20.5M round | 2015-06-04 | No (Data Collective led) | DCVC, DFJ, Allen & Co., AME Cloud, Playground, CME Group, Fuel, Omidyar | [src](https://venturebeat.com/ai/deep-learning-startup-nervana-raises-20-5m), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/shahin-farshchi) |\n| Flex Logix (embedded FPGA) | Founding investor; then $7.4M round | 2014 / 2015-10-26 | Founding: Lux. 2015: Eclipse led | Tate Family Trust, Eclipse | [src](https://venturebeat.com/technology/flex-logix-raises-7-4m-to-commercialize-its-clever-flexible-chip-designs), [src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf) |\n| Zoox | Lux investment he led | — | yes (Lux lead per bio) | — | [src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf) |\n| Planet | Lux investment he led | — | yes (per bio) | — | [src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf) |\n| Relativity Space | Lux investment he led | — | yes (per bio) | — | [src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf) |\n| Astranis | Lux investment he led | — | yes (per bio) | — | [src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf) |\n| Aeva | Company formation | — | Lux formation role (per bio) | — | [src](https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf) |\n| Molecular Imprints | Lux portfolio he described as his | sold to Canon, early 2014 | unknown | — | [src](https://strictlyvc.com/?p=3761) |\n| Varda | Lux investment | 2020 | unknown | — | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/varda) |\n| Impulse Space | Lux investment | — | unknown | — | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies) |\n| Sakana, Physical Intelligence, Together, Hugging Face, Cognition, Runway, Anduril, Applied Intuition, Chronosphere, Eikon, Auris | Listed in his Lux bio portfolio; his personal role is not stated. The Together card credits Reeves, Isford and Jiang | — | unknown | — | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/shahin-farshchi), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/together) |\n| Silicon Clocks (to Silicon Labs), SiBeam (to Lattice) | Listed as acquired in his bio | — | unknown | — | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/shahin-farshchi) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Nervana → MosaicML → Databricks:**\n  - DFJ (Steve Jurvetson) led Nervana in Aug 2014. Lux joined the DCVC-led $20.5M round in June 2015. Intel bought Nervana in Aug 2016.\n  - In 2021, Nervana alumni Rao and Tang started MosaicML. Lux led the $37M debut, with DCVC alongside again, and his House bio credits him with leading it. Databricks acquired MosaicML in 2023.\n  - The pattern is one founder lineage, a 2015 ticket and a 2021 lead, with the same co-investor (DCVC) both times. The exact intro path for MosaicML is not sourced.\n- **Flex Logix:** In July 2014 he described a fresh stealth investment in software-programmable silicon. Lux was the founding investor, and Eclipse led the next round in Oct 2015. His bio says he led Lux's role in forming the company.\n- **Cognichip:**\n  - Aug 2024: Lux published the AI-for-chip-design thesis with his input.\n  - 2024: Lux invested, per its card.\n  - May 2025: Lux and Mayfield co-led the $33M seed. CEO Faraj Aalaei previously ran Aquantia, which Marvell bought in 2019.\n  - Apr 2026: a $60M follow-on reported as led by Seligman Ventures ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=737108)).\n  - How the founder intro happened was not found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lux Capital | Principal → Partner → GP | 2006 | [src](https://strictlyvc.com/?p=3761) |\n| Naveen Rao (Nervana, MosaicML) | Backed him twice: Lux in Nervana 2015, then Lux led MosaicML 2021 | 2015 | [src](https://venturebeat.com/ai/deep-learning-startup-nervana-raises-20-5m), [src](https://siliconangle.com/2021/10/13/naveen-raos-startup-mosaicml-exits-stealth-mission-improve-ml-training/) |\n| DCVC (Data Collective) | Co-investor in Nervana (DCVC led) and MosaicML | 2015 | same as above |\n| Mayfield | Co-lead on Cognichip seed | 2025 | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=696697) |\n| Faraj Aalaei (Cognichip CEO) | Portfolio founder | 2024 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/cognichip) |\n| Eclipse Ventures | Co-investor in Flex Logix (Eclipse led the 2015 round) | 2015 | [src](https://venturebeat.com/technology/flex-logix-raises-7-4m-to-commercialize-its-clever-flexible-chip-designs) |\n| Geoff Tate (Flex Logix CEO) | Portfolio founder | 2014 | [src](https://venturebeat.com/technology/flex-logix-raises-7-4m-to-commercialize-its-clever-flexible-chip-designs) |\n| Danny Crichton, Dario Soatto (Lux) | Co-wrote the chip-design labor thesis | 2024 | [src](https://www.readsecurities.com/p/the-looming-labor-crisis-in-chip-design) |\n| Brian Schechter, Gaby Lorenzi (Primary) | Hosted him on *The Compute 100* | 2026 | [src](https://podcasts.apple.com/podcast/id1896779326) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is he in Naveen Rao's post-Databricks venture? That would be a direct frontier-AI or chip-lab lead. Unchecked.\n- What is his actual role, if any, in Sakana, Physical Intelligence, Cognition and Runway, which his bio lists?\n- I need a *Compute 100* transcript to get verbatim lines on younger chip founders and the \"shrinking\" set of outcomes.\n- Which of the nine AI-for-EDA startups named in the Aug 2024 essay did Lux meet or pass on? These were VoltAI, Silimate, Chipstack, Cadstrom, Silogy, Motivo, Quilter, Mitai and CircuitMind.\n- What did he say on Riskgaming (Sept 2024) and at Hill & Valley 2026? Both pages returned 403.\n- What was the House hearing topic on 2023-06-22?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/people/shahin-farshchi\n- https://www.congress.gov/118/meeting/house/116078/witnesses/HHRG-118-SY00-Bio-FarshciS-20230622.pdf\n- https://strictlyvc.com/?p=3761\n- https://spectrum.ieee.org/u/shahin-farshchi\n- https://spectrum.ieee.org/analog-and-neuromorphic-chips-will-rule-robotic-age\n- https://www.readsecurities.com/p/the-looming-labor-crisis-in-chip-design\n- https://markets.financialcontent.com/stocks/article/bizwire-2025-5-15-cognichip-launches-out-of-stealth-with-33m-in-seed-funding-to-deliver-artificial-chip-intelligence-aci\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=696697\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=737108\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/cognichip\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/mosaicml\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/databricks\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/together\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/varda\n- https://siliconangle.com/2021/10/13/naveen-raos-startup-mosaicml-exits-stealth-mission-improve-ml-training/\n- https://venturebeat.com/ai/deep-learning-startup-nervana-raises-20-5m\n- https://socaltech.com/nervana_nabs_3_3m_for_machine_learning_hardware/s-0056585.html (search excerpt)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2016/08/09/intel-buys-deep-learning-startup-nervana-systems-for-a-reported-350-million\n- https://venturebeat.com/technology/flex-logix-raises-7-4m-to-commercialize-its-clever-flexible-chip-designs\n- https://www.eenewseurope.com/en/fpga-fabric-startup-raises-5-millon\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/podcast/id1896779326\n- https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/venture-with-grace/episode/future-building-investment-insights-with-lux-capitals-shahin-farshchi-58022\n- https://metacast.app/podcast/riskgaming/3C63w3AJ/ai-is-spiking-chip-design-costs-can-it-solve-them-too/2UngPNgZ\n", "person:shangda-xu": "\n# Shangda Xu\n\n## One-line take\nThe junior partner on a16z Growth who shows up, beside Raghu Raghuram, on nearly every 2026 a16z compute and AI-hardware deal (Nexthop, Volta, Gimlet) plus the eval company Vals. The most direct way into a16z's growth-stage AI infrastructure decisions below GP level.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** Xu sits at the junction of two a16z power centres. He co-wrote the enterprise-AI research franchise with Sarah Wang (2023 to 2026 CIO surveys and model-share reports) and co-sign the infrastructure and compute deals with Managing Partner Raghu Raghuram. Every 2026 deal byline we found pairs him with Raghuram. That gives him a GP sponsor and a data asset (the CIO surveys) to use in competitive growth processes.\n- **Constraint:** This is growth capital. In all four named deals, a16z came in after someone else had already backed the company: Menlo led Gimlet's Series A, Lightspeed led the Nexthop Series B, and 8VC, Pear and Bloomberg Beta were already in Vals. Volta is the exception: a16z co-led its Series A, though Volta is a project-finance-heavy neocloud, not a seed-stage lab. Xu's personal decision rights versus Raghuram's are unknown.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** Lead a pre-product seed. His bylines and his 2022 essay frame deals around valuation, dilution and capital structure, not team-only bets. This is inferred from the deals and essays, not something he has said.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Every 2026 deal is a Raghuram co-sign.** Nexthop, Volta, Vals and Gimlet all list Raghu Raghuram as a co-author ([Nexthop](https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop), [Volta](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-volta/), [Vals](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-vals/), [Gimlet](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gimlet/)). Volta is a two-person byline: \"Raghu Raghuram and Shangda Xu\". Read: Xu is the working partner on the managing partner's compute book.\n- **Compute thesis is power-bound, not chip-bound.** Gimlet memo: \"We are running out of watts.\" and \"It must be heterogeneous.\" ([a16z, 2026-09-04](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gimlet/)). The thesis is multi-silicon inference orchestration (GPUs, CPUs, accelerators) within a fixed power envelope.\n- **Networking is the bottleneck.** Nexthop memo: \"networking is the bottleneck between GPU capacity and GPU output.\" ([a16z, 2026-03-10](https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop)).\n- **Neocloud for startups and neolabs.** Volta memo: \"Volta is building the neocloud for Little Tech.\" and \"Most startups don't have financeable balance sheets: they're funded 18 months at a time.\" ([a16z, 2026-08-04](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-volta/)). The memo highlights Ricard Boada and Sofia Gumuzio, who built Brookfield's AI infrastructure platform. For the owner, this is a capacity source for new labs.\n- **Evals as independent scorekeeper.** Vals memo: \"Every market eventually needs an independent scorekeeper.\" ([a16z, 2026-08-13](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-vals/)).\n- **Model layer is not commoditizing (2025 survey).** \"the enterprise model layer has not become commoditized.\" and \"Switching costs are rising as AI tackles more complex tasks.\" ([a16z, 2025-06-10](https://a16z.com/ai-enterprise-2025/)).\n- **Model share call (2026-01-30).** \"Anthropic and Gemini are steadily gaining at OpenAI's expense.\" ([a16z](https://a16z.com/leaders-gainers-and-unexpected-winners-in-the-enterprise-ai-arms-race/)).\n- **Deal-structure realist (2022, first byline).** Co-wrote with David George and Alex Immerman: \"Structured clauses and deals, despite being called “dirty,” aren’t innately good or bad.\" ([a16z, 2022-09-22](https://a16z.com/funding-when-capital-isnt-cheap/)). This is also the earliest dated evidence of Xu at a16z.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Topically this is high-fit: AI compute, networking, inference silicon orchestration, neoclouds and evals. Stage-wise it is low: all named deals are Series A at $400M or more, Series B, or growth, and a16z was never the first institutional investor except as co-lead of Volta's Series A. The people to know are Xu and Raghuram as **follow-on capital and a compute-capacity channel** (Volta) for any lab or chip company the owner seeds. Deals that matter here: Gimlet Labs (Series B lead), Nexthop AI (Series B participant), Volta (Series A co-lead with Altimeter), Vals (Series A lead).\n\n## Background\n- Harvard, summa cum laude; grew up in Connecticut ([a16z bio](https://a16z.com/author/shangda-xu/)). Graduation year not found.\n- Investor at Warburg Pincus covering later-stage software, tech-enabled services and industrials ([a16z bio](https://a16z.com/author/shangda-xu/)). Dates not found.\n- a16z Growth team: earliest dated byline is 2022-09-22 ([Funding When Capital Isn't Cheap](https://a16z.com/funding-when-capital-isnt-cheap/)). A 2022 join date also appears on aggregators (unverified). His title at join is not found; we could not confirm the date of promotion to Partner.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://a16z.com/author/shangda-xu/ | Firm author page, 9 pieces listed (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop | a16z newsletter bylines |\n| X | https://x.com/shangdaxu | Mostly retweets of a16z colleagues (Raghuram, Stephenie Zhang); follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/shangdaxu | Linked from firm page; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. On the 2026-11-10 re-check the web-search budget was again used up, so podcasts were not searched. This needs a follow-up.\n\n## How they think\n- **Physical-layer bottlenecks drive AI infra returns:** power (\"running out of watts\"), networking and the balance sheet (startup compute financing). Each 2026 deal targets one of these.\n- **Heterogeneous inference:** \"Inference is no longer a single workload.\" ([Gimlet memo](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gimlet/)).\n- **Enterprise model market stays contested:** he argues against commoditization (2025) and tracked OpenAI's share eroding to Anthropic and Gemini (2026).\n- **Calls:** the 2024 survey said the enterprise revenue opportunity would be \"multiples larger\" ([a16z, 2024-03-21](https://a16z.com/generative-ai-enterprise-2024/)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Investing in Gimlet](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gimlet/): the power-constrained, multi-silicon inference thesis.\n2. [Investing in Volta](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-volta/): the compute-financing gap for startups and neolabs.\n3. [Investing in Nexthop AI](https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop): AI networking as the GPU-output bottleneck.\n4. [How 100 Enterprise CIOs Are Building and Buying Gen AI in 2025](https://a16z.com/ai-enterprise-2025/): the survey data asset.\n5. [Funding When Capital Isn't Cheap](https://a16z.com/funding-when-capital-isnt-cheap/): how they think about price versus structure.\n\n## Social engagement\nX activity on the firm page is retweets of @steph_zhang, @zainasgar and @RaghuRaghuram (a16z Japan launch) ([a16z author page](https://a16z.com/author/shangda-xu/), relative dates only). It is a low-signal amplification account.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Nexthop AI | Series B, $500M, $4.2B post | 2026-03-10 | No. Lightspeed led; a16z was a \"major investor\" | Lightspeed (lead), Altimeter, existing investors | [Nexthop PR](https://nexthop.ai/news-and-event/nexthop-ai-accelerates-into-hypergrowth-with-oversubscribed-500m-series-b-funding-catapulting-the-companys-valuation-to-4-2-billion/), [a16z](https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop) |\n| Volta | Series A (amount not disclosed) | 2026-08-04 | Yes, co-lead | Altimeter (co-lead); NVIDIA, Dell, Matter Venture Partners, Azora shown as partners/investors | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-volta/), [volta.com](https://volta.com/) |\n| Vals AI | Series A, $40M at $400M | 2026-08-13 | Yes (a16z led) | 8VC, Pear VC, Bloomberg Beta (existing); HRT Ventures, Next Ladder Ventures (new) | [Vals](https://www.vals.ai/blogs/series-a), [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-vals/) |\n| Gimlet Labs | Series B, $300M | 2026-09-04 | Yes (a16z led) | Sapphire, Menlo, 645, Arm, Eclipse, Emergence, Factory, HRT, M12, OnePrime, Prosperity7, QuantumLight, Samsung Ventures, Tiger Global, Triatomic, Wing, XTX Markets | [Gimlet](https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-b), [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gimlet/) |\n\nCompany sources name only the firm. Xu's role comes from the a16z memo bylines.\n\n**Completeness check (2026-11-10):** the a16z author page lists exactly four investment memos: Gimlet, Vals, Volta and Nexthop ([a16z](https://a16z.com/author/shangda-xu/)). The other five pieces are research. We found no board seat, and none appears on the bio. Any deals from 2022 to 2025, before his first memo, are unattributed, not absent: a16z Growth deals from those years named only GPs. We could not search further, because the web-search budget was used up.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Gimlet Labs:** Menlo Ventures led an $80M Series A on 2026-03-23 with Eclipse, Factory, Prosperity7 and Triatomic ([Gimlet blog](https://gimletlabs.ai/blog)). a16z led the $300M Series B on 2026-09-04, about 5.5 months later ([Gimlet](https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-b)). a16z was not first: Factory and Eclipse were in before the A. The memo co-signers are Raghuram, Sarah Wang and Stephenie Zhang.\n- **Nexthop AI:** Founded by Anshul Sadana (15+ years at Arista, per the a16z memo). Lightspeed led the $500M Series B; a16z's memo calls itself a \"major investor\" ([a16z](https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop)). The company's release says a16z was \"joining as a major investor\", which points to a16z first coming in at the Series B, not earlier ([Nexthop PR](https://nexthop.ai/news-and-event/nexthop-ai-accelerates-into-hypergrowth-with-oversubscribed-500m-series-b-funding-catapulting-the-companys-valuation-to-4-2-billion/)). The a16z memo uses no \"again\" or follow-on wording either. The release quotes Raghuram, not Xu, as a16z's voice. Co-signers: Raghuram and Guido Appenzeller.\n- **Volta:** a16z co-led the Series A with Altimeter (2026-08-04). Only Raghuram and Xu signed the memo. Volta pairs cloud ops with project finance. An Azora-sponsored $5B AI infrastructure program and senior debt sit alongside the equity ([volta.com](https://volta.com/)). We found no earlier institutional round, so a16z may be among the first institutional investors.\n- **Vals AI:** a16z led a $40M Series A at a $400M valuation (2026-08-13). 8VC, Pear VC and Bloomberg Beta were already in ([Vals](https://www.vals.ai/blogs/series-a)). Co-signers: Jennifer Li, Yoko Li and Raghuram. Founders: Rayan Krishnan and Langston Nashold.\n- Intro paths and sourcing for all four deals: nothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Raghu Raghuram (a16z MP) | Co-signer on all four 2026 deals; sole co-author on Volta | 2026 | [Volta](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-volta/) |\n| Sarah Wang (a16z GP) | Long-running research co-author (2023 to 2026); Gimlet co-signer | 2023 | [Next Token](https://a16z.com/the-next-token-of-progress-4-unlocks-on-the-generative-ai-horizon/) |\n| Justin Kahl (a16z) | CIO survey and model-share co-author | 2025 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/ai-enterprise-2025/) |\n| Tugce Erten (a16z) | 2025 CIO survey co-author | 2025 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/ai-enterprise-2025/) |\n| Guido Appenzeller (a16z) | Nexthop co-signer | 2026 | [a16z](https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop) |\n| Jennifer Li, Yoko Li (a16z) | Vals co-signers | 2026 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-vals/) |\n| Stephenie Zhang (a16z) | Gimlet co-signer; Xu retweets her | 2026 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gimlet/) |\n| David George, Alex Immerman (a16z Growth) | 2022 essay co-authors | 2022 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/funding-when-capital-isnt-cheap/) |\n| Altimeter | Co-lead on Volta Series A; co-investor in Nexthop Series B | 2026 | [volta.com](https://volta.com/) |\n| Warburg Pincus | Prior employer | dates not found | [a16z bio](https://a16z.com/author/shangda-xu/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Date of promotion to Partner and title at join. LinkedIn and an archived a16z bio would settle it; neither could be fetched.\n- Volta Series A size, and whether Volta had a prior institutional round.\n- Nexthop: the release's \"joining\" wording suggests a16z's first entry was the Series B. Check a Form D or the earlier-round release to confirm.\n- Who sourced Gimlet and Volta: Xu or Raghuram?\n- Podcast or talk appearances: not searched, because the search budget was used up.\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/shangda-xu/\n- https://www.a16z.news/p/investing-in-nexthop\n- https://nexthop.ai/news-and-event/nexthop-ai-accelerates-into-hypergrowth-with-oversubscribed-500m-series-b-funding-catapulting-the-companys-valuation-to-4-2-billion/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-volta/\n- https://volta.com/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-vals/\n- https://www.vals.ai/blogs/series-a\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-gimlet/\n- https://gimletlabs.ai/blog\n- https://gimletlabs.ai/blog/announcing-series-b\n- https://a16z.com/generative-ai-enterprise-2024/\n- https://a16z.com/ai-enterprise-2025/\n- https://a16z.com/leaders-gainers-and-unexpected-winners-in-the-enterprise-ai-arms-race/\n- https://a16z.com/the-next-token-of-progress-4-unlocks-on-the-generative-ai-horizon/\n- https://a16z.com/funding-when-capital-isnt-cheap/\n", "person:shardul-shah": "\n# Shardul Shah\n\n## One-line take\nIndex's senior New York partner who has quietly become the firm's US frontier-AI lead: he authored or co-authored Index's posts on Flapping Airplanes, Parallel Web Systems, Simile, Enigma and Hone. He underwrites the person before the product, and that is how he gets in early with Stanford-lab founders.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He commits to people before there is a company to diligence. For Parallel, Index's 2024 memo had almost nothing on product or market (\"We actually weren't sure we knew any of that.\") [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/parallels-100m-series-a/). For Enigma, he shook hands at 2am before incorporation and before any talk of price [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/). He brings the security playbook from Wiz (Index's largest outside position) to AI.\n- **Where his edge comes from:** One Stanford lab. Chris Ré co-founded Cartesia, an Index-led seed with Shah as the only Index name on the company page, and Shah met Flapping Airplanes' Ben Spector in Ré's office [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/cartesia/), [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/). His security book comes from long relationships with Israeli repeat founders: Assaf Rappaport, Lior Div and Zohar Alon.\n- **Constraints:** He is rarely the first institution into the company. Sequoia was in Flapping before Index, A* co-led Simile's seed, and Greylock, CRV and Spark held 7AI's seed. At the next round he often cedes the lead: Kleiner led Cartesia's A and Sequoia led Parallel's B. On new labs Index syndicates rather than leading alone. Since 2025 his frontier-AI work runs as a pair with the more junior Mark Xu, who took the Flapping board seat.\n- **What he'd never do (stated):** speed-date founders, or decide on market size. The team page says \"Index doesn't believe in speed dating.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/shardul-shah/), and on 20VC he says \"TAM is a trap.\" [src](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-indexs-shardul-shah-on-why-market-size-is-a-trap--biggest-lessons-on-pricing-from-leading-rounds-in-wiz--datadog--why-benchmarks--averages-in-vc-are-bs--how-index-makes-decisions-and-why-growth--early-are-the-same-investing-style).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Parallel memo was about people, not product.** Index first invested in Parag Agrawal's team in 2024 with a memo that said little about the business: \"We actually weren't sure we knew any of that.\" Instead it had \"pages and pages of notes about Parag and his team\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/parallels-100m-series-a/) (2025-11-12). Index was in about a year before the public $30M round was announced in August 2025 [src](https://builtin.com/articles/parallel-web-systems-raises-30m-ai-web-search-20250818).\n- **Enigma: a pre-incorporation handshake, no price talk.** They shook hands at 2am outside the McKittrick Hotel after a full day together, and \"We hadn't spent a single second talking numbers\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/) (2026-07-27). The result was a $71M Index-led seed with Ribbit.\n- **The Flapping origin was an accident in Chris Ré's office.** Ben Spector walked into Ré's office while Shah was there, the two \"took an impromptu walk,\" and Shah called Xu right after: \"Ben Spector is a sensation.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/) (2026-01-28). Xu had a separate path through Harvard and Prod, so the deal came from two independent relationships converging.\n- **The Ré lab is a repeat source.** Cartesia was \"Founded by a group of researchers from the Stanford AI Lab\" and names \"their former professor Chris Ré\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-next-generation-of-real-time-ai-models-our-investment-in-cartesia/). Index's company page lists Ré as a founder and Shah as the only Index name [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/cartesia/). Note: the Cartesia post is bylined \"Index Ventures,\" and the Index partner quoted in it is Mike Volpi.\n- **The 7AI lead was a decade-long relationship.** Shah writes of Lior Div \"as our relationship has grown over the past decade.\" He also writes: \"I was reminded of that when we spent two and a half days together at a recent Index event.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/securitys-agentic-era-starts-here-our-investment-in-7ai/) (2025-12-04). Index's own events act as a deal-closing venue.\n- **He is a product of Index's apprenticeship culture.** He started \"as an intern at Index,\" and co-founder David Rimer gave him a Douglas Adams book [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/life-the-universe-and-simile-leading-similes-series-a/). On 20VC he credits \"the importance of mentorship and apprenticeship\" (auto-generated transcript) [src](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-indexs-shardul-shah-on-why-market-size-is-a-trap--biggest-lessons-on-pricing-from-leading-rounds-in-wiz--datadog--why-benchmarks--averages-in-vc-are-bs--how-index-makes-decisions-and-why-growth--early-are-the-same-investing-style/segment/389.947). He now runs the same model with Mark Xu (Flapping, Simile, Hone, Enigma) and Juriaan Duizendstraal (Enigma, 7AI, NewCore).\n- **His power-law framing (20VC, 2024-09):** \"We are in the business of finding fund returners.\" He also tells partners \"don't be cute on price\" (auto-generated transcript) [src](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-indexs-shardul-shah-on-why-market-size-is-a-trap--biggest-lessons-on-pricing-from-leading-rounds-in-wiz--datadog--why-benchmarks--averages-in-vc-are-bs--how-index-makes-decisions-and-why-growth--early-are-the-same-investing-style). This explains why he will pay up for a $180M seed or a $1B A.\n- **Wiz (summary only, low confidence):** a TBPN digest says a term sheet followed the first Rappaport meeting within 72 hours, and that Index sat in the Wiz seed alongside Doug Leone and an investor the digest spells \"Gilly Renan\" (likely Cyberstarts' Gili Raanan, needs-id) [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-03-11/shardul-shah-on-wiz-acquisition-closing-and-index-ventures-journey). Newcomer's March 2026 episode is reportedly titled around wiring money \"before knowing what they were building\" (title only, episode not read) [src](https://app.podcastguru.io/podcast/1582647825/episode/shardul-shah-i-wired-the-money-before-knowing-what-they-were-building-e2d47e2f759aa3261f7219d9ec73d9c3).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is the Index partner on several of the deals that matter most to the owner: the Flapping Airplanes seed ($180M) and $1B Series A (a new data-efficiency lab), Cartesia (an SSM architecture lab out of Ré's group), Parallel Web Systems (AI web infrastructure, where he holds the board seat), and Hone (an AI-native automation company that \"owns entire outcomes,\" close to the Serval type). He writes $100M+ Series A checks in AI. He is a good co-investor for a $20M check into a large lab seed, and Index syndicates rather than taking whole rounds. Calibration note: an earlier lead row (from danny-rimer) marked him `low` (\"security not labs\"). That view is outdated. Since 2024 his named AI book outweighs his security book for this lens, though about half his recent leads (7AI, NewCore, Enigma-adjacent Wiz alumni) are still security. Not a junior-alpha target: he is a senior, Midas-level partner, so his junior pair Mark Xu is the rising-star angle.\n\n## Background\n- University of Chicago, then Summit Partners [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/shardul-shah/).\n- Intern at Index (year not stated) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/life-the-universe-and-simile-leading-similes-series-a/). At Index since 2008 [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/shardul-shah/).\n- Early investments named on his profile: Datadog, Wiz, Simile, Parallel. Other work: Adallom, Duo Security, Signal Sciences, Nginx, Coalition and others. Forbes Midas List several times [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/shardul-shah/).\n- Co-opened Index's New York office (post co-authored with Martin Mignot, listed on his team page) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/shardul-shah/).\n- Bloomberg (Jan 2026) coverage of Index succession names him among the next generation (repo row from danny-rimer) [src](https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/shardul-shah/ | Index team page; follower counts not checked |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes Index Perspectives posts (Flapping, Parallel, Simile, Enigma, 7AI, NewCore, Hone, Wiz) |\n| X | https://twitter.com/shardul_shah | Linked from team page; activity not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/shardul-shah-3589062/ | Linked from team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-09 — 20VC — \"Why market size is a trap… lessons on pricing from Wiz, Datadog\" — [audioscrape](https://audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-indexs-shardul-shah-on-why-market-size-is-a-trap--biggest-lessons-on-pricing-from-leading-rounds-in-wiz--datadog--why-benchmarks--averages-in-vc-are-bs--how-index-makes-decisions-and-why-growth--early-are-the-same-investing-style) — \"TAM is a trap.\"; \"don't be cute on price\"; says AI is a tailwind for existing markets and also opens new ones (segment 2717s). Auto-generated transcript, only fragments read.\n- 2026-03-11 — TBPN — Wiz acquisition closing — [digest](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-03-11/shardul-shah-on-wiz-acquisition-closing-and-index-ventures-journey) — summary only: 72-hour term sheet, and Wiz at the intersection of cloud, security and AI spend.\n- 2026-03 — Newcomer Pod — \"I wired the money before knowing what they were building\" — [listing](https://app.podcastguru.io/podcast/1582647825/episode/shardul-shah-i-wired-the-money-before-knowing-what-they-were-building-e2d47e2f759aa3261f7219d9ec73d9c3) — not read (listing 404 on fetch).\n- 2026-09-11 — Technovation with Peter High — \"Why the Founder Is the Investment Thesis\" — [podwise](https://podwise.ai/episodes/8894063) — summary only: high-agency founders, machine-to-machine security, and his own use of AI agents. No verbatim quotes available.\n- 2023-03 — \"Podcast 374\" on cloud and security, Wiz GTM, and Datadog/Coalition lessons — seen only in a search listing, not verified.\n\n## How they think\n- **The founder is the thesis.** His profile principles are founder-centric. The Parallel memo and the Enigma handshake show it in practice.\n- **Market size is not a filter.** \"TAM is a trap.\" The best founders expand their markets (20VC).\n- **Pay up for power-law outcomes.** \"don't be cute on price\"; finding fund returners matters more than entry price (20VC).\n- **AI theses he has written down:** data efficiency as a frontier-lab bet (Flapping), the web as live context for agents (Parallel: \"Where today's models try to memorize the world, Parallel helps them understand it.\"), simulation of human behavior (Simile), and agents that own outcomes rather than act as tools (Hone).\n- **Calls right:** Wiz (Google deal) and Datadog. Parallel went from Index's 2024 first check to a $2B valuation by April 2026 [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/29/parallel-web-systems-hits-2b-valuation-five-months-after-its-last-big-raise/). No admitted mistakes found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Parallel's $100M Series A](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/parallels-100m-series-a/): the \"unconventional memo,\" his clearest statement of person-first underwriting.\n2. [Taking flight: Flapping Airplanes](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/): how a lab deal actually starts for him (Ré's office).\n3. [Enigma emerges from stealth](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/): the pre-incorporation, no-numbers handshake.\n4. [Leading Simile's Series A](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/life-the-universe-and-simile-leading-similes-series-a/): his origin story at Index and his taste for ambitious science.\n5. [Hone](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-owns-business-outcomes-our-investment-in-hone/): his AI-native automation thesis.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X activity was not checked (web search budget exhausted this run).\n\n## Portfolio\nAI deals only. Security deals are listed in Relationships and Open questions.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Twelve Labs | Index investment (round not stated) | 2022-03-16 | Not stated on Index post | n/s | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/unlocking-the-power-of-video-with-twelve-labs/). **Attribution disputed:** the post is by Kelly Toole; Shah is now the only Index name on the [company page](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/twelve-labs/) |\n| Augment | Series B $227M ($977M post) | 2024-04-24 | No (participant; lead not stated) | Sutter Hill, Innovation Endeavors, Lightspeed, Meritech | [src](https://www.augmentcode.com/blog/augment-inc-raises-227-million). Shah quoted as investor |\n| Parallel Web Systems | First check (round not named; later reported as part of $30M) | 2024 (announced 2025-08) | n/s | Khosla, First Round, Terrain | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/parallels-100m-series-a/), [src](https://builtin.com/articles/parallel-web-systems-raises-30m-ai-web-search-20250818) |\n| Cartesia | Seed $22M (total $27M) | 2024-12-12 | Yes (Index led) | A* Capital, Conviction, General Catalyst, Lightspeed, SV Angel | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-next-generation-of-real-time-ai-models-our-investment-in-cartesia/). Shah tagged; the post quotes Volpi |\n| Cartesia | Series A $64M | 2025-03-11 | No (Kleiner Perkins led) | Kleiner, Lightspeed, A*, Factory, Greycroft | [src](https://fortune.com/2025/03/11/exclusive-cartesia-voice-ai-startup-raises-64-million-series-a/) (from repo row) |\n| Fireworks AI | Series C $250M ($4B) | 2025-10-28 | Co-lead (Index), but Sahir Azam wrote the post | n/s in post | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/). Shah tagged only |\n| Parallel Web Systems | Series A $100M ($740M post) | 2025-11-12 | Co-led with Kleiner Perkins; Shah on board | Kleiner, Spark, Khosla, First Round, Terrain | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/parallels-100m-series-a/) |\n| 7AI | Series A $130M | 2025-12-04 | Yes (Index led); Shah joins board | Blackstone Innovations, Greylock, CRV, Spark | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/securitys-agentic-era-starts-here-our-investment-in-7ai/) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Seed $180M | 2026-01-28 | Co-led (with GV, Sequoia) | GV, Sequoia | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/) |\n| Simile | Series A $100M | 2026-02-12 | Yes (Index led) | n/s | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/life-the-universe-and-simile-leading-similes-series-a/) |\n| Parallel Web Systems | Series B $100M ($2B) | 2026-04-28 | No (Sequoia led) | Sequoia, Kleiner, Khosla | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/29/parallel-web-systems-hits-2b-valuation-five-months-after-its-last-big-raise/) |\n| NewCore (AI-era identity security) | First round $66M | 2026-06-15 | Not stated in fetch | Cyberstarts, Evolution Equity | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/rebuilding-identity-for-the-agentic-era-our-investment-in-newcore/) |\n| Enigma | Seed $71M | 2026-07-27 | Yes (Index led) | Ribbit Capital | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/) |\n| Simile | Series B $200M | 2026-07-30 | Not stated (Index \"doubling down\") | n/s | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/simulating-society-at-scale-our-investment-in-similes-200m-series-b/) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Series A $1B | 2026-10-21 | Co-led (Index; Lux per other sources); Xu board | Lux, Sequoia, GV, Kleiner, NVIDIA | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/) |\n| Hone | Undisclosed | 2026-10-08 | Not stated | Benchmark | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-owns-business-outcomes-our-investment-in-hone/) |\n| Thread AI | not checked | n/s | n/s | n/s | listed on [team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/shardul-shah/) |\n\n**Senior/junior pairing (who co-signed from Index):**\n- Flapping seed and A: Shah + Mark Xu (co-bylined; Xu on the board).\n- Simile A: Shah byline, Xu tagged. Simile B: both tagged.\n- Hone: Shah + Xu co-bylined.\n- Enigma: Shah byline; Xu and Juriaan Duizendstraal tagged.\n- 7AI: Shah byline and board; Xu and Duizendstraal tagged.\n- NewCore: Shah byline; Duizendstraal tagged.\n- Cartesia: Shah tagged; Mike Volpi quoted.\n- Fireworks: Sahir Azam byline; Shah and Xu tagged.\n- Parallel A: Shah byline; no one else tagged.\n\n**Timing (first in?):**\n- Enigma: likely first (handshake before incorporation; not confirmed).\n- Parallel: early. Index's first check was in 2024, a year before the public raise; whether Khosla came first is unknown.\n- Cartesia: not first. About $5M was raised before Index's $22M seed, and Kleiner led the A three months later.\n- Flapping: not first. Sequoia partnered in 2025, before launch (see firms/index-ventures.md).\n- Simile: not first. A* co-led the seed.\n- 7AI: not first. Greylock, CRV and Spark held the $36M seed.\n- Fireworks: late (Series C).\n- Augment: late (Series B).\n- Hone: Index met Moritz Stephan about a year before the round, while he was still at Cognition.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Flapping Airplanes:** Shah was catching up with Chris Ré when Ben Spector walked in. They took a walk, and Shah called Xu right after. Xu already knew Asher Spector from Harvard. Index co-led the $180M seed with GV and Sequoia, then co-led the $1B A nine months later, with Xu taking the board seat [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/), [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/).\n- **Parallel Web Systems:** Index backed Parag Agrawal's team in 2024 on a memo that was mostly notes about the people. It co-led the $100M A with Kleiner in November 2025 (Shah on the board). Sequoia led the B five months later at $2B [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/parallels-100m-series-a/).\n- **Enigma:** Shah spent a day in New York with Jonathan Jacobi and Gal Niv, from a sunrise walk in Central Park to a magic show, and shook hands at 2am. Price never came up, and the company was not yet incorporated. The founders have worked with people close to Index (Wiz and Dazz alumni). Index led the $71M seed and welcomed Ribbit's Micky Malka [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/).\n- **7AI:** Shah has known Lior Div for about a decade, and they spent two and a half days together at an Index event before the deal. Index led a $130M A described as \"the largest cybersecurity Series A in history,\" and Shah joined the board [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/securitys-agentic-era-starts-here-our-investment-in-7ai/).\n- **Hone:** A friend introduced Index to Moritz Stephan, then at Cognition, about a year before the round. Index later met co-founders Oliver Brady and Carlo Kobe, and partnered with Benchmark [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-owns-business-outcomes-our-investment-in-hone/).\n- **Cartesia:** Index led the $22M seed into Stanford AI Lab researchers (Karan Goel, Albert Gu and others) and their former professor Chris Ré. Kleiner, where ex-Index GP Ilya Fushman works, led the A [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-next-generation-of-real-time-ai-models-our-investment-in-cartesia/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Mark Xu (Index) | Junior deal partner on Flapping, Simile, Hone, Enigma, 7AI, Fireworks | 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/) |\n| Juriaan Duizendstraal (Index) | Co-tagged on Israeli AI/security deals (Enigma, 7AI, NewCore) | 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/rebuilding-identity-for-the-agentic-era-our-investment-in-newcore/) |\n| Mike Volpi (Index) | Quoted Index partner on the Cartesia seed Shah is tagged on | 2024 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-next-generation-of-real-time-ai-models-our-investment-in-cartesia/) |\n| Sahir Azam (Index) | Lead author on Fireworks; Shah tagged | 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/) |\n| David Rimer (Index co-founder) | Mentor from Shah's intern days | pre-2008 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/life-the-universe-and-simile-leading-similes-series-a/) |\n| Martin Mignot (Index) | Co-opened the NY office | n/s | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/shardul-shah/) |\n| Chris Ré (Stanford) | Catches up with him; source of Flapping and Cartesia | n/s | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/) |\n| Ben Spector (Flapping) | Met in Ré's office | 2025 | same |\n| Parag Agrawal (Parallel) | Backed in 2024; board member | 2024 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/parallels-100m-series-a/) |\n| Lior Div (7AI) | About a decade's relationship; board | ~2015 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/securitys-agentic-era-starts-here-our-investment-in-7ai/) |\n| Zohar Alon (NewCore, ex-Dome9) | Index relationship from the Dome9 days | Dome9 era | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/rebuilding-identity-for-the-agentic-era-our-investment-in-newcore/) |\n| Assaf Rappaport (Wiz) | Backed at Adallom and Wiz | 2010s | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/shardul-shah/) |\n| Kleiner Perkins | Co-led Parallel A; led Cartesia A | 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/parallels-100m-series-a/) |\n| Sequoia | Co-led Flapping seed; led Parallel B | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/) |\n| Ribbit Capital (Micky Malka) | Co-investor in Enigma seed | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/) |\n| Benchmark | Co-investor in Hone | 2026 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-owns-business-outcomes-our-investment-in-hone/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What did Index's first Parallel check look like: amount, round label, and was Index ahead of Khosla?\n- Who actually sourced Twelve Labs (Kelly Toole's byline vs Erin Price-Wright per BI vs Shah on the current company page)?\n- Who else from Chris Ré's lab is Shah tracking? Ré is a serial founder (Cartesia and others), so his lab could feed the next Index lab deal.\n- Thread AI: round, date and role.\n- The Newcomer and Technovation transcripts: do they contain check-size or ownership rules, or AI passes?\n- Wiz seed co-investors (Doug Leone, Gili Raanan per a summary digest) need primary confirmation.\n- Did Index or Shah participate in Hone's earlier round, and what was the round label?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/shardul-shah/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/taking-flight-our-investment-in-flapping-airplanes/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/parallels-100m-series-a/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/enigma-emerges-from-stealth-our-investment-in-jj-and-gal/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/life-the-universe-and-simile-leading-similes-series-a/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/simulating-society-at-scale-our-investment-in-similes-200m-series-b/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ai-that-owns-business-outcomes-our-investment-in-hone/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/securitys-agentic-era-starts-here-our-investment-in-7ai/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/rebuilding-identity-for-the-agentic-era-our-investment-in-newcore/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-next-generation-of-real-time-ai-models-our-investment-in-cartesia/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/cartesia/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/twelve-labs/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/augment/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/unlocking-the-power-of-video-with-twelve-labs/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inference-is-the-new-runtime-our-investment-in-fireworks/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/wiz-defining-a-new-kind-of-company/\n- https://www.augmentcode.com/blog/augment-inc-raises-227-million\n- https://builtin.com/articles/parallel-web-systems-raises-30m-ai-web-search-20250818\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/29/parallel-web-systems-hits-2b-valuation-five-months-after-its-last-big-raise/\n- https://fortune.com/2025/03/11/exclusive-cartesia-voice-ai-startup-raises-64-million-series-a/\n- https://audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-indexs-shardul-shah-on-why-market-size-is-a-trap--biggest-lessons-on-pricing-from-leading-rounds-in-wiz--datadog--why-benchmarks--averages-in-vc-are-bs--how-index-makes-decisions-and-why-growth--early-are-the-same-investing-style\n- https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-03-11/shardul-shah-on-wiz-acquisition-closing-and-index-ventures-journey (summary)\n- https://podwise.ai/episodes/8894063 (summary)\n- https://app.podcastguru.io/podcast/1582647825/episode/shardul-shah-i-wired-the-money-before-knowing-what-they-were-building-e2d47e2f759aa3261f7219d9ec73d9c3 (listing only)\n- https://www.bloomberglinea.com/mercados/index-ventures-el-silencioso-hacedor-de-reyes-de-silicon-valley-busca-sucesores/\n", "person:shaun-maguire": "\n# Shaun Maguire\n\n## One-line take\nMaguire is Sequoia's senior partner of record on frontier-lab and deep-tech bets at formation: Mithril (AI compute), Decart, Irregular, Corma, Sable, Engram and Harmonic. They also carry the firm's Musk positions (SpaceX, X, xAI, Neuralink, Boring), so they sit at both ends of what I care about: $10-100M lab seeds and the mega-rounds those labs graduate into ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/roelof-botha-explains-why-sequoia-supports-shaun-maguire-after-coo-quit/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win.** They are a physicist-founder (Caltech PhD, co-founded Escape Dynamics and Qadium/Expanse, which Palo Alto Networks bought for over $1B) with personal ties to Patrick Collison and the Musk orbit ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shaun_Maguire), aggregator; [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/11/sequoia-bets-on-silence/)). Technical founders treat them as a peer: Factory's founder cold-emailed them as \"a fellow former hep-th physicist\" and got a reply in under an hour ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-factory-autonomous-ai-for-all/)).\n- **How they work.** They are the senior name, paired with a junior or regional partner who owns the local relationship: Dean Meyer in Israel, Liam Corrigan on Valar, Andrew Reed on SSI and Harmonic, and Sonya Huang and James Flynn on Corma. Their filter is founder intensity over pedigree. Their bio says they want someone \"irrationally motivated\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/)).\n- **What constrains them.** Sequoia's scout program fed them founders years early (Mithril). The 2026 memo shut Arc and Scouts, so that option-on-founders pipeline is gone ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)). Their public political posting (the July 2025 Mamdani post, a December 2025 Brown shooting misidentification) brought founder petitions and press. Sequoia's managing partner defended them, saying the firm needs \"spiky\" people, and conceded \"does it come with trade-offs? Yes, it does\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/roelof-botha-explains-why-sequoia-supports-shaun-maguire-after-coo-quit/)). Some founders will self-select out of a Maguire-led cap table.\n- **What they'd never do.** No sourced statement. Their record is all hard tech (labs, compute, security, defense, nuclear, space). I found no consumer-social or thin AI wrapper deal on their page. That is my read, not their statement.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Scout program worked as a multi-year option on Mithril's founder.** Andrew Reed met Jared Quincy Davis in 2021 and introduced them to Maguire. Maguire wrote that \"we immediately decided to make Jared a Sequoia Scout\". Sequoia then led the seed of Mithril (an AI compute/GPU cloud, founded Sept 2022) as its first money and co-led the Series A ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/), 2024-03-21).\n- **Cold inbound works with them if you share their physics background.** Factory's Matan Grinberg cold-emailed them in March 2023. Maguire: \"I wrote back in less than an hour.\" A walk followed a few days later ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-factory-autonomous-ai-for-all/), 2023-11-02).\n- **The Israel pipeline runs through Dean Meyer.** In November 2023, Meyer turned a planned one-on-one lunch with Eon's Ofir Ehrlich into a three-way meeting with Maguire: \"Dean had invited Ofir to join what was originally planned as a one-on-one lunch\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/), 2024-10-01). Sequoia later hired Meyer. The same pair co-wrote the Decart (2024) and Irregular (2025) posts.\n- **The hiring path was a Stripe reference.** Their bio says Sequoia \"did a reference call with my friend Patrick Collison\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/)). TechCrunch traces the friendship to a 2015 Founders Fund event and says they helped GV get into Stripe in their first week there ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/11/sequoia-bets-on-silence/)). Bridge, the stablecoin startup Stripe bought for $1.1B, was their deal (same source).\n- **Their founder test is weekend behaviour.** The bio asks \"what do people do on their weekends?\" and says \"The best founders are just so obsessed with what they're doing\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/)).\n- **The Corma thesis is that the lab must own its model.** Sequoia led Corma's seed (2026-08-10) on the claim that \"Corma's vertical integration and 'sovereign AI' approach will win\". This rhymes with Sonya Huang's \"not your weights\" line ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-corma-closing-the-defensive-cybersecurity-gap/)).\n- **Sequoia stayed on Sable after the seed.** Sequoia was \"thrilled to lead Sable's seed round and co-lead its Series A\". Sable's four Harvard founders came out of post-training and RL research ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sable-closing-the-diffusion-gap/), 2026-07-16).\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-10)\nMined from six interviews and talks (2019-2025), using auto-captions or the published transcript. Each item has a timestamped row in `data/rows/shaun-maguire-voice-insights.csv`. The full catalogue of 58 items is in `data/rows/shaun-maguire-voice-media.csv`.\n- **Sequoia decides unanimously, and anyone can veto.** In 2024 Maguire said every partner, \"even a 22-year old analyst has veto rights\", and that an analyst did veto a deal about five years earlier ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VaEX_bjwj8w), 25:46). A bigger check means \"20 reference calls instead of five\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VaEX_bjwj8w), 29:47).\n- **The \"iconic founder\" override is rationed.** At the Monday meeting, a GP can argue \"back this founder even though the idea isn't formed\" about \"once every five years\". Being wrong is close to fireable. Seed is looser ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VaEX_bjwj8w), 45:12).\n- **The bar for skipping the seed.** To go straight to a large Series A with Sequoia, \"you basically have to be second time founders with a strong unique insight\", with Wiz as the model. Young elite-unit founders get a seed instead ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VaEX_bjwj8w), 29:01). This matters for anyone pricing $20M+ first rounds alongside them.\n- **They rate the reference, not just the founder.** Their chess-Elo rule: only a much stronger player can rate a strong one. So a Noam Shazeer or Ilya Sutskever calling someone an outlier counts for far more than a Stanford undergrad saying it. Sequoia bans vague words like \"spiky\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jcx3pKnvT8I), 72:04). For foundation-model and robotics companies, the thing to underwrite is \"just how good are the researchers\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jcx3pKnvT8I), 70:58).\n- **They dislike sourcing.** \"I hate the phase of sourcing new companies\". Their strengths are diligence, winning and board work ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VaEX_bjwj8w), 40:05). This explains the partner-originates, Maguire-closes pattern in the deal table (Dean Meyer, Andrew Reed).\n- **Defense was a why-now call, not a standing thesis.** Despite having founded a defense company, they turned down most defense pitches before the Ukraine war. In 2024 they expected defense to stop being a focus within about three years ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VaEX_bjwj8w), 24:29; 50:37).\n- **Hiring path, first-hand.** Expanse \"never pitched Sequoia\" because Sequoia had a competing company. Patrick Collison emailed Mike Moritz and Michael Abramson to say Sequoia should hire them ([src](https://heritageproject.caltech.edu/interviews-updates/shaun-maguire)). GV's Stripe check came in Maguire's first week, decided in one meeting with no diligence ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OV1oexfsBxg), 35:44).\n- **Diligence habits.** They allow about 10-15 calls for a Series A, spent where the risk is ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OV1oexfsBxg), 29:50). For SpaceX they \"talked to like 10 of their engineers\" without asking the company ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OV1oexfsBxg), 36:27). They use Caltech physicists as expert diligence ([src](https://heritageproject.caltech.edu/interviews-updates/shaun-maguire)).\n- **Funnel and bar.** In 2021: about 5,000 decks, about 500 meetings and 4-5 investments a year. An intro from a Sequoia founder is the main filter ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OV1oexfsBxg), 14:05). Sequoia's target outcome rose from $10B+ to \"closer to 20 billion\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VaEX_bjwj8w), 49:17).\n- **Mistakes admitted.** On X at $44B: \"I wish we paid a lower price, obviously\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9IrW9yuWKy8), 57:22). The trap they warn about is backing a company because you can see how you would run it ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OV1oexfsBxg), 42:44). They say 20-30% of their bets are \"widget\" companies backed for the founder ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jcx3pKnvT8I), 57:37).\n- **Early on ML, talent-flow heuristic.** In Sept 2022 they said ML was the fastest-moving field and they were reading more of it than quantum ([src](https://heritageproject.caltech.edu/interviews-updates/shaun-maguire)). Their 2022 rule was to follow where IMO gold medalists go ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lfx3fVsp564), 43:41).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Maguire leads frontier-lab seeds of exactly the kind I want into: Corma (frontier cyber-defense lab, seed 2026), Decart (real-time generative model lab, seed 2024), Irregular (frontier AI security lab, $80M co-led with Redpoint, 2025), Engram (context-trained models, 2025) and Sable (2025). They also took the first AI compute check in Mithril (2022) and led Harmonic's $75M Series A (2024). For me the deals that matter are Corma, Engram, Decart, Mithril and Irregular. Most are Israel-heavy and paired with Dean Meyer, so Meyer is the practical way in. Caveats: on SSI and xAI, Maguire joined big syndicates rather than originating them, and their public controversies are a real reputational factor for any co-investor.\n\n## Background\n- Stanford (attended, dates not found) and a Caltech physics PhD (Heritage Project lists 2018), in quantum gravity per their own bio ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shaun_Maguire), aggregator; [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/)).\n- They say \"I did horribly in high school\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/)). Botha says they dropped out of high school ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/roelof-botha-explains-why-sequoia-supports-shaun-maguire-after-coo-quit/)).\n- 2010: co-founded Escape Dynamics with Caltech scientists. DARPA civilian contractor work included a deployment to Afghanistan (undated). 2012: co-founded Qadium/Expanse, which Palo Alto Networks acquired in 2020 for over $1B. All from Wikipedia citing The Information, BI and WSJ ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shaun_Maguire), aggregator).\n- The bio says \"I've started five companies.\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/)).\n- 2016-2019 at GV, then Sequoia from 2019 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/11/sequoia-bets-on-silence/)). Named one of seven GPs in Nov 2026 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/ | firm bio and portfolio list |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | writes Sequoia partnership posts (Mithril, Factory, Decart, Eon, Irregular, Corma, Sable) |\n| X | https://x.com/shaunmmaguire | very active and political; follower count not fetched (X blocked fetch, 2026-11-10) |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none as a regular host. Hosted the Neuralink (DJ Seo) session at Sequoia AI Ascent 2026 | https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=by7Y9KoErTw |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nFull catalogue (58 items, 2014-2026) in `data/rows/shaun-maguire-voice-media.csv` (2026-11-10). Highlights:\n- Seven Questions with Shaun Maguire, Sequoia newsletter, 2019-11-06 ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/newsletter/2019-11-06-shaun-maguire)). Mined.\n- MIT VCPE Club talk, 2021-04-28 ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OV1oexfsBxg)). Mined.\n- Crypto podcasts, 2022: The Scoop (04-06), Chain Reaction (05-01), Logan Bartlett EP22 (06-24, mined), Unchained Ep. 367 (06-28, mined), EthCC[5] with Pat Grady (07-20).\n- Caltech Heritage Project oral history, recorded 2022-09-23, published 2023-01-04 ([src](https://heritageproject.caltech.edu/interviews-updates/shaun-maguire)). Mined.\n- Pomp Podcast #1263, 2023-10-17 (DARPA, bitcoin).\n- Sheva VC podcast with Omri Casspi, 2024-08-11 ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VaEX_bjwj8w)). Mined; the best item on Sequoia's internal process.\n- 20VC E1189, 2024-08-12 ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9IrW9yuWKy8)). Mined.\n- Uncapped #1 with Jack Altman, 2025-03-13 ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jcx3pKnvT8I)). Mined.\n- 2025-26: TBPN (several), Upfront Summit, Georgetown, Limitless/Bankless, Relentless, Idea Dealer, Sourcery (x2), CNBC and Bloomberg SpaceX/Neros segments, AI Ascent 2026 (host).\n\n## How they think\n- **Founder over market.** \"We look for founders with grit, distance traveled, and irrational motivation\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/)).\n- **Technical peerage.** Founders in their deals are often physicists or elite researchers. They called Decart \"one of the most technically gifted teams we've ever worked with\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-decart-the-future-of-ai-generated-experiences/)). On Corma: \"Alon is personally one of the most elite hackers in the world\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-corma-closing-the-defensive-cybersecurity-gap/)).\n- **Security as the AGI picks-and-shovels.** Irregular, Corma and Eon, plus Expanse before investing. On Irregular: \"an indispensable partner in the race toward AGI\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-irregular-ahead-of-the-curve/)).\n- **Hard tech across the stack.** Energy for AI (Valar Atomics), space (SpaceX, Reflect Orbital) and defense (Mach Industries, Neros, Kela) ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/)).\n- **Calls right or wrong.** Mithril, Factory, Decart and Irregular all raised follow-ons. Valuations are not sourced here.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Partnering with Mithril: AI Compute, On Demand\" (2024-03-21). The Scout-to-seed path and their founder criteria. https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/\n2. \"Partnering with Factory\" (2023-11-02). The cold email answered in under an hour. https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-factory-autonomous-ai-for-all/\n3. \"Partnering with Corma\" (2026-08-10). The thesis for a frontier lab in one domain. https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-corma-closing-the-defensive-cybersecurity-gap/\n4. Sequoia bio page. Their own story, the weekend test, \"irrationally motivated.\" https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/\n5. TechCrunch on Botha's defence (2025-10-27). How the firm weighs their deal flow against reputational cost. https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/roelof-botha-explains-why-sequoia-supports-shaun-maguire-after-coo-quit/\n\n## Social engagement\nX (@shaunmmaguire) is heavily political: Israel, US politics, Musk. The July 2025 Mamdani post drew a petition from about 900 tech figures and a counter-letter of support (Wikipedia citing CNBC and WSJ, aggregator; [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shaun_Maguire)). Who they reply to or amplify was not analyzed because X could not be fetched.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI and compute-relevant deals first, then other notable ones. Dates are the Sequoia article publication dates or the \"Partnered\" year.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mithril (AI compute / GPU cloud) | Seed; then Series A | founded 2022-09; post 2024-03-21 | Led seed (Sequoia's first money); co-led A | Sequoia: Andrew Reed (intro). External: not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/) |\n| Factory (autonomous SWE agents) | not stated | 2023 (post 2023-11-02) | not stated | Sequoia: Maguire alone | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-factory-autonomous-ai-for-all/) |\n| Decart (real-time generative models) | Seed (the post also says \"lead Series A investor\"; conflict) | 2024-10-31 | Yes | Sequoia: Dean Meyer. External: not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-decart-the-future-of-ai-generated-experiences/) |\n| Harmonic (math superintelligence) | Series A, $75M | 2024-09-23 | Yes | Index (significant), GreatPoint, Era Funds, Blossom, DST partners, Elefund, Nikesh Arora, Jared Leto. Sequoia: Andrew Reed | [src](https://harmonic.fun/news/series-a/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/harmonic/) |\n| Safe Superintelligence | $1B+ at ~$5B | 2024-09-04 | No (among investors) | NFDG, a16z, DST, SV Angel. Sequoia: Andrew Reed | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/04/ilya-sutskevers-startup-safe-super-intelligence-raises-1b/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/safe-superintelligence/) |\n| xAI | Series B $6B; Series C $6B | 2024-05-26; 2024-12-23 | No (participant) | Valor, Vy, a16z, Fidelity, Kingdom Holding | [src](https://x.ai/news/series-b), [src](https://x.ai/news/series-c) |\n| Irregular (frontier AI security lab, ex-Pattern Labs) | $80M at $450M | 2025-09-17 | Co-led | Redpoint (co-lead), Assaf Rappaport. Sequoia: Dean Meyer | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/17/irregular-raises-80-million-to-secure-frontier-ai-models/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-irregular-ahead-of-the-curve/) |\n| Engram (context-trained models) | not stated (site lists Early and Pre-Seed/Seed) | 2025 | not stated | Sequoia: Maguire alone. No partnership post | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/engram/) |\n| Sable AI (AI employees) | Seed; Series A | 2025 (post 2026-07-16) | Led seed; co-led A | Sequoia: Maguire alone | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sable-closing-the-diffusion-gap/) |\n| Corma (frontier cyber-defense lab) | Seed | 2026-08-10 | Yes | Sequoia: Sonya Huang, James Flynn, Carl Eschenbach | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-corma-closing-the-defensive-cybersecurity-gap/) |\n| Valar Atomics (nuclear for AI gigasites) | not stated (\"Growth\" on site) | 2026 | not stated | Sequoia: Liam Corrigan | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/valar-atomics/) |\n| Neuralink | Growth | 2025 | not stated | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/) |\n| Eon (cloud backup) | Seed, then Series B | seed after 2023-11 lunch; post 2024-10-01 | Led seed | Sequoia: Dean Meyer | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/) |\n| Kela (defense) | Seed | 2024 | Yes (per Dean Meyer rows) | Sequoia: David Cahn, Dean Meyer | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-kela-modern-defense-for-israel-and-western-allies/) |\n| Other (non-AI): SpaceX 2019, X 2022, Bridge 2023, Mach Industries 2023, Neros 2023, Reflect Orbital 2024, Semgrep 2017, LayerZero 2022, Monad 2020, Watershed 2020 | various | | | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Mithril.** Andrew Reed met Jared Quincy Davis in 2021 and introduced them to Maguire, citing shared intellectual interests. Sequoia made Davis a Scout. When Davis founded Mithril in Sept 2022, Sequoia led the seed as its first investment and later co-led the A ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/)). The company page's meta description says \"Foundry\", so this may be the former Foundry. Not confirmed.\n- **Factory.** Matan Grinberg cold-emailed in March 2023 as a fellow high-energy-physics theorist. Maguire replied in under an hour and they walked a few days later. Once the founders settled on the idea, they signed a term sheet, incorporated and started building within a day. The post doesn't name the term-sheet investor ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-factory-autonomous-ai-for-all/)).\n- **Eon.** In November 2023 in Tel Aviv, Dean Meyer brought Maguire into a lunch with Ofir Ehrlich, and Sequoia led the seed. Meyer then joined Sequoia ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/)).\n- **Decart.** The first contact was \"our first walk with Dean in the Bay Area\", followed by a meeting with the full team in Tel Aviv. \"Dean\" could be founder Dean Leitersdorf or partner Dean Meyer; the summary didn't make it clear. No intro path is given ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-decart-the-future-of-ai-generated-experiences/)).\n- **Irregular.** In an early meeting, Dan Lahav pulled up a test environment showing an AI agent doing what he had described. Sequoia and Redpoint then co-led $80M at a $450M valuation ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-irregular-ahead-of-the-curve/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/17/irregular-raises-80-million-to-secure-frontier-ai-models/)).\n- **xAI and SSI (not first).** On xAI, Sequoia's access came via the Musk and X relationship, and it participated in the B and C (see `companies/xai.md`). SSI's first round centered on NFDG (Daniel Gross and Nat Friedman), with Sequoia one of five named investors ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/04/ilya-sutskevers-startup-safe-super-intelligence-raises-1b/)).\n\n### Timing: were they first?\n| Company | First money? | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mithril | Yes (led seed; founder was a Sequoia Scout first) | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/) |\n| Decart | Led the seed. Earlier money not found | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-decart-the-future-of-ai-generated-experiences/) |\n| Eon | Led the seed | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/) |\n| Corma | Led the seed | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-corma-closing-the-defensive-cybersecurity-gap/) |\n| Sable | Led the seed | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sable-closing-the-diffusion-gap/) |\n| Harmonic | Not first. Led the Series A, founded 2023. No seed round found | [src](https://harmonic.fun/news/series-a/) |\n| Irregular | Unknown. Co-led $80M; company founded 2023 as Pattern Labs | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/17/irregular-raises-80-million-to-secure-frontier-ai-models/) |\n| SSI, xAI | Not first. Joined syndicates | above |\n\n### Senior/junior pairing\n| Co-partner | Deals with Maguire | Pattern |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dean Meyer | Eon, Decart, Irregular, Kela | Israel sourcing; Meyer brings founders in, Maguire is the senior name |\n| Andrew Reed | SSI, Harmonic; intro to Mithril's founder | peer GP; Reed brings the network |\n| Liam Corrigan | Valar Atomics | 2026 \"rising star\" hire on energy/hardware |\n| Sonya Huang, James Flynn, Carl Eschenbach | Corma | cross-team lab seed |\n| David Cahn, Dean Meyer | Kela | defense |\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Patrick Collison (Stripe) | friend; gave the reference call for the Sequoia hire | 2015 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/11/sequoia-bets-on-silence/) |\n| Dean Meyer (Sequoia) | co-partner; Israel deal source | 2023 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/) |\n| Andrew Reed (Sequoia) | co-partner; introduced Jared Quincy Davis | 2021 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/) |\n| Jared Quincy Davis (Mithril) | Scout, then seed founder | 2021 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/) |\n| Matan Grinberg (Factory) | founder; cold email | 2023-03 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-factory-autonomous-ai-for-all/) |\n| Elon Musk | carries Sequoia's SpaceX, X, xAI, Neuralink and Boring positions | 2019 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/roelof-botha-explains-why-sequoia-supports-shaun-maguire-after-coo-quit/) |\n| Roelof Botha (Sequoia) | public defender after the 2025 controversy | 2025 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/roelof-botha-explains-why-sequoia-supports-shaun-maguire-after-coo-quit/) |\n| Redpoint Ventures | co-lead on Irregular | 2025 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/17/irregular-raises-80-million-to-secure-frontier-ai-models/) |\n| Index Ventures | co-investor on Harmonic A | 2024 | [src](https://harmonic.fun/news/series-a/) |\n| Sonya Huang, James Flynn (Sequoia) | co-partners on Corma | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/corma/) |\n| Liam Corrigan (Sequoia) | co-partner on Valar | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/valar-atomics/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Not yet mined (2026-11-10): Pomp #1263 (2023), EthCC 2022, CNBC 2022, Limitless 2025 (transcript on podscripts), Relentless 2025, TBPN Wiz/Valar segments, Sourcery 2026, and the X archive (X blocks fetch).\n- Which deal was the unannounced 'about 40 term sheets' deal Maguire fought for with Omri Casspi (Sheva VC, 2024)?\n- Decart: was Sequoia's 2024 round a seed or a Series A? The post says both. Size and co-investors are unknown.\n- Engram: round, size, co-investors, and how Maguire met the founders (Stanford/Columbia-adjacent researchers Biderman, Lin, Morris, Eyuboglu, Linderman).\n- Corma: round size, co-investors, how Alon Pluda was sourced (Israel/Meyer?).\n- Was Mithril previously called Foundry? Only the page metadata hints at it.\n- Factory: who wrote the first term sheet, and did Sequoia lead?\n- Valar Atomics round terms.\n- Do the Scouts and Arc closures reduce Maguire's early access, or does their personal network substitute?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-factory-autonomous-ai-for-all/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-decart-the-future-of-ai-generated-experiences/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-irregular-ahead-of-the-curve/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-corma-closing-the-defensive-cybersecurity-gap/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sable-closing-the-diffusion-gap/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-kela-modern-defense-for-israel-and-western-allies/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/engram/, /corma/, /harmonic/, /safe-superintelligence/, /valar-atomics/, /irregular/, /decart/, /mithril/, /factory/, /sable-ai/\n- https://harmonic.fun/news/series-a/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/09/04/ilya-sutskevers-startup-safe-super-intelligence-raises-1b/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/17/irregular-raises-80-million-to-secure-frontier-ai-models/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/11/sequoia-bets-on-silence/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/roelof-botha-explains-why-sequoia-supports-shaun-maguire-after-coo-quit/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/\n- https://x.ai/news/series-b, https://x.ai/news/series-c\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shaun_Maguire (aggregator)\n", "person:shayle-kann": "\n# Shayle Kann\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. Sources use \"he\". Web search was unavailable for this run (budget exhausted), so everything below comes from direct fetches: EIP's team page, Emerald AI's site, Latitude Media episode transcripts, Nitricity's site, and SEC EDGAR (Form D, 6-K, FWP, C-AR). Gaps are marked.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the energy-side investor most often in the room on AI's power bottleneck, and his weekly podcast doubles as his sourcing funnel. Emerald AI went from Catalyst guest to EIP lead in about seven months.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** about 15 years as the market analyst everyone quoted (GTM Research), plus a weekly show (Catalyst) where founders and operators come to him. Together these give him diligence depth and deal access that most climate investors lack.\n- **The capital behind him:** EIP's corporate and utility LP base, for example TransAlta's $25M commitment to Frontier Fund 1 \"to identify, pilot, commercialize\" portfolio tech ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1144800/000127956922000731/ex991.htm)). It gives his companies customers and pilots.\n- **What constrains him:**\n  - The Frontier mandate is deep decarbonization ([src](https://www.energyimpactpartners.com/team/)).\n  - The funds are mid-sized: Fund I raised $402M ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1842065/000184206522000001/primary_doc.xml)) and Fund II has raised $236M so far ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2081052/000208105226000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **What he'd never do (inferred, not stated):** back a model lab or a chip company on its own. AI enters his book only through energy, as with Emerald AI.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The podcast is the pipeline.**\n  - Aug 2025: he hosts Varun Sivaram, calling him \"my old friend, Varun Sivaram\" ([src](https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/catalyst-the-mechanics-of-data-center-flexibility/)).\n  - 2026-03-31: EIP leads Emerald's $25M round.\n  - 2026-04-09: Sivaram is back on the show, and Kann opens with \"Anyway, long story short, I invested in Emerald.\" ([src](https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/catalyst-the-rise-of-flexible-data-centers/)).\n  - In between, Kann says he \"went on my own journey on this concept\" for about a year. His conclusion: \"From first principles, data centers should be flexible assets.\" (same src).\n- **Contrarian on AI power:** \"I think people are undercounting the grid and utilities.\" He expects more grid capacity added in two years than in any year in decades, against the \"everything behind the meter\" narrative ([src](https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/catalyst-fuel-cells-geopolitics-and-the-solar-growth-curve/), 2026-09-17).\n- **Data-center limits won't slow frontier AI:** \"that is not going to be what slows down the pace of frontier AI\" (same src). He thinks regulation is the likelier brake.\n- **Admitted miss:** on the bipartisan local backlash against data centers, \"But that came faster and has become more serious than I expected.\" (same src).\n- **Early and repeat in Nitricity.**\n  - A contemporaneous Form D (filed 2021-08-18, $6.0M raised) already lists him as Director ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1804951/000180495121000002/primary_doc.xml)). That is over a year before the Khosla/Fine Structure-led $20M Series A of 2022-10-18 ([src](https://www.nitricity.co/news/nitricity-raises-20-million-series-a)).\n  - EIP followed on in the $50M Series B of 2025-09-10 ([src](https://www.nitricity.co/news/nitricity-raises-50m-to-go-global-with-unique-tech-transforming-almond-waste-into-organic-fertilizer)).\n- **Fund mechanics from filings:**\n  - Frontier Fund I first closed 2021-01-11. It had $53.0M from 15 investors by Jan 2021 and $402.3M from 50 by Jul 2022. Kann is listed as \"Member of the General Partner and Investment Committee Member\" ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1842065/000184206522000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - Frontier Fund II (an onshore LP plus Luxembourg vehicles, with a Japan offering page at [src](https://www.energyimpactpartners.com/japan/)) first sold 2025-10-06. It had $236.3M from 29 investors by 2026-07-27.\n  - Fund II's Form D names only Kobler, Feldman and Wool as officers and lists no IC members, so Kann's absence there tells us nothing about his role ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2081052/000208105226000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **Analyst-era footprint:** issuer filings quoted him, for example SolarCity's 2014 solar-bond FWP calls him \"the vice-president of Greentech Media\" ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1408356/000119312514437860/d803869dfwp.htm)). Powur's crowdfunding reports cite him as \"Vice President of Research at Greentech Media\" ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1639406/000167025419000283/document_14.txt)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium**, up from the low rating in the original lead row.\n- **For:** he led a priced round in an AI-infra company the repo tags `ai-infra-compute` (Emerald AI, now a $1.05B Series A company). He can lead $10M-plus rounds. He has real insight into the power side of the compute bottleneck, which matters to anyone backing labs that need gigawatts.\n- **Against:** his book is climate deeptech (Form Energy, Electric Hydrogen, Sublime, Nitricity, Koloma). There is no evidence of lab, pre-training or chip deals, and his LPs are utilities and industrials.\n- **How to use him:** as a co-investor or diligence call on AI-energy companies (data-center power, grid interconnect, on-site generation), and as a channel. Catalyst reaches the energy side of the AI buildout.\n- **Not useful for:** as an LP or as a lead on a lab seed.\n\n## Background\n- Pomona College, cum laude, Phi Beta Kappa. He was a U.S. Fulbright Scholar in Australia ([src](https://www.energyimpactpartners.com/team/)). Dates not found.\n- Worked in regulatory policy at the California Public Utilities Commission and on carbon markets at Conservation Services Group (later acquired by ClearResult) ([src](https://www.energyimpactpartners.com/team/)). Dates not found.\n- Built and ran GTM Research, Greentech Media's market-intelligence arm, through and after Verisk's 2016 acquisition of GTM ([src](https://www.energyimpactpartners.com/team/)). Quoted as VP of Greentech Media in Dec 2014 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1408356/000119312514437860/d803869dfwp.htm)). Exact start and end dates unconfirmed; the old GTM article URL now redirects to Wood Mackenzie.\n- At Energy Impact Partners by Jan 2021 at the latest, as GP and IC member of Frontier Fund I ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1842065/000184206521000001/primary_doc.xml)). His EIP headshot sits in a 2018/03 upload folder, which hints at an earlier association but proves nothing. Join date unconfirmed.\n- Now Managing Partner, Frontier ([src](https://www.energyimpactpartners.com/team/)) and a member of Azolla Ventures' Investor Advisory Board (same src).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.energyimpactpartners.com/team/ | EIP team page with bio (verified 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | none checked beyond EIP and Latitude |\n| X | not verified | handle not confirmed this run (no search) |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | not fetched |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://www.latitudemedia.com/podcasts/catalyst/ | \"Catalyst with Shayle Kann\", weekly on Thursdays (verified 2026-11-10); older episodes are also listed at https://www.canarymedia.com/podcasts/catalyst-with-shayle-kann |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-08-28: Catalyst, \"The mechanics of data center flexibility\", with Varun Sivaram ([link](https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/catalyst-the-mechanics-of-data-center-flexibility/)). Kann calls Sivaram an old friend, and Sivaram calls NVIDIA \"our biggest investor\". This was before EIP invested.\n- 2026-04-09: Catalyst, \"The rise of flexible data centers\", with Sivaram ([link](https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/catalyst-the-rise-of-flexible-data-centers/)). Kann discloses the EIP-led investment on air and calls them \"partners in crime\".\n- 2026-09-17: Catalyst, \"Fuel cells, geopolitics, and the solar growth curve\", an AMA-style episode ([link](https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/catalyst-fuel-cells-geopolitics-and-the-solar-growth-curve/)). Covers the data-center backlash, why Bloom Energy \"was in the right place at the right time\", and his grid hot take.\n- Oct–Nov 2026: Catalyst episodes on gas turbines, methane from data centers, gas supply for AI, and nuclear fuel ([list](https://www.latitudemedia.com/podcasts/catalyst/)).\n\n## How they think\n- Reasons from first principles about energy physics and economics, then looks for the coordination problem that blocks adoption. On Emerald: the physics says yes, aligning the players is the hard part ([src](https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/catalyst-the-rise-of-flexible-data-centers/)).\n- Bullish on the grid and utilities scaling capacity within about two years, bearish on the idea that everything moves behind the meter ([src](https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/catalyst-fuel-cells-geopolitics-and-the-solar-growth-curve/)).\n- Sees AI as the demand driver of the current energy cycle, not a constraint on AI itself. He does not think data-center limits will slow frontier AI (same src).\n- Calls he got wrong: he underestimated how fast and how bipartisan the data-center backlash would be (same src).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Catalyst 2026-04-09 transcript: how and why he invested in Emerald AI ([link](https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/catalyst-the-rise-of-flexible-data-centers/)).\n2. Catalyst 2026-09-17 AMA: his own views on AI power, the grid and solar, plus his self-assessment ([link](https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/catalyst-fuel-cells-geopolitics-and-the-solar-growth-curve/)).\n3. Catalyst 2025-08-28 with Sivaram: the pre-investment diligence conversation in public ([link](https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/catalyst-the-mechanics-of-data-center-flexibility/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked this run; X and LinkedIn were not fetched. On Catalyst he relies on recurring guests and long-time friends (Sivaram; Andy Lubershane is referenced as a recurring conversation partner in the 2026-09-17 episode).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Emerald AI | Strategic ($25M; total $68M) | 2026-03-31 | yes (EIP) | NVentures, Radical, Lowercarbon, Salesforce, Samsung, Siemens, GE Vernova, Eaton, IQT, Amplo | [src](https://www.emeraldai.co/blog/sharing-our-strategic-expansion-round-emerald-ai-raises-25-million-to-transform-ai-data-centers-into-flexible-power-grid-assets) |\n| Nitricity | Seed ($6.0M per Form D) | 2021-08 | unknown | not stated | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1804951/000180495121000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Nitricity | Series A ($20M) | 2022-10-18 | no | Khosla, Fine Structure (co-leads), Lowercarbon, MCJ | [src](https://www.nitricity.co/news/nitricity-raises-20-million-series-a) |\n| Nitricity | Series B ($50M) | 2025-09-10 | no | World Fund, Khosla (co-leads) | [src](https://www.nitricity.co/news/nitricity-raises-50m-to-go-global-with-unique-tech-transforming-almond-waste-into-organic-fertilizer) |\n| Electric Hydrogen | Seed through Series C ($0.75M, $23.9M, $139.3M, $380.6M per Form D) | 2020-12 to 2023-04 | unknown | not stated in filings | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1997980/000199798023000004/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Koloma | Undisclosed ($245.7M equity per Form D; board seat from 2023-06) | 2023-12 | unknown | not stated | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1881912/000188191224000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Form Energy | not identified | | | | [EIP bio](https://www.energyimpactpartners.com/team/) |\n| Sublime Systems | not identified | | | | [EIP bio](https://www.energyimpactpartners.com/team/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Emerald AI.**\n  - Radical incubated the company and led its $24.5M seed in Jul 2025. NVentures came in at the seed, and Lowercarbon led an $18M extension in Nov 2025 (repo rows).\n  - Kann hosted Sivaram, an \"old friend\", on Catalyst in Aug 2025, after the seed and before EIP had invested.\n  - Over the following months he studied data-center flexibility and concluded it is right from first principles. EIP then led the $25M strategic round on 2026-03-31, its first check about nine months after the seed. Kann took an observer seat ([src](https://www.emeraldai.co/company)).\n  - Emerald raised a $150M Series A at $1.05B in Aug 2026, co-led by DCVC and Energize. EIP's participation in that round is **not confirmed**.\n- **Nitricity.** Kann was a director by the Aug 2021 raise. EIP participated (did not lead) in the Series A and followed again in the Series B, so he stayed involved over about four years. Who led the 2021 round is not stated.\n- **Electric Hydrogen.** Kann is named director on all four Form Ds, which cover rounds from Dec 2020 ($750K, 3 investors) to Apr 2023 ($380.6M). All four were filed together in Oct 2023, so the filings do not prove he held a seat at inception.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Energy Impact Partners | Managing Partner, Frontier; GP and IC member of Frontier Fund I | by 2021-01 | [team](https://www.energyimpactpartners.com/team/), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1842065/000184206521000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Hans Kobler | EIP founder; Managing Member of both Frontier GPs | by 2021-01 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2081052/000208105226000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Varun Sivaram (Emerald AI) | \"old friend\"; portfolio CEO; podcast guest | 2025-08 or earlier | [src](https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/catalyst-the-mechanics-of-data-center-flexibility/) |\n| Rob Toews, David Katz (Radical); Zachary Bogue (DCVC); Clay Dumas (Lowercarbon); Christina Buchanan (NVentures) | Emerald AI board and observer peers | 2026 | [src](https://www.emeraldai.co/company) |\n| Khosla Ventures (Rajesh Swaminathan) | Nitricity co-investor; fellow external director | 2022 | [src](https://www.nitricity.co/about) |\n| Latitude Media (Stephen Lacey, exec editor) | Produces Catalyst | Catalyst episodes on Canary Media list from 2023 | [src](https://www.latitudemedia.com/podcasts/catalyst/) |\n| Azolla Ventures | Investor Advisory Board | not stated | [src](https://www.energyimpactpartners.com/team/) |\n| TransAlta | LP ($25M) in Frontier Fund 1 | 2022 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1144800/000127956922000731/ex991.htm) |\n\n## Open questions\n- His exact EIP start date and title history (GTM exit year; senior advisor before full-time?).\n- Did EIP follow into Emerald's $150M Series A (Aug 2026)?\n- Did EIP invest in Koloma, and in which round? The board seat is filing-confirmed but the check is not.\n- Round-level detail for Form Energy and Sublime Systems.\n- Fund II's final size, and whether Kann sits on the Fund II IC (the Form D format doesn't list IC members).\n- Any non-energy AI deals at EIP that he sponsors. ASI, a \"frontier AI company\" for critical infrastructure, is in EIP's portfolio ([src](https://www.energyimpactpartners.com/_portfolio/)), but the sponsoring partner is unknown.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.energyimpactpartners.com/team/\n- https://www.energyimpactpartners.com/japan/\n- https://www.energyimpactpartners.com/_portfolio/\n- https://www.emeraldai.co/company\n- https://www.emeraldai.co/blog/sharing-our-strategic-expansion-round-emerald-ai-raises-25-million-to-transform-ai-data-centers-into-flexible-power-grid-assets\n- https://www.fortune.com/2026/03/31/emerald-ai-nvidia-fast-pass-data-center-grid-connects/\n- https://www.dcvc.com/news-insights/dcvc-co-leads-emerald-ais-150-million-series-a-round-to-transform-data-centers-into-intelligent-grid-responsive-assets\n- https://www.latitudemedia.com/podcasts/catalyst/\n- https://www.latitudemedia.com/about/\n- https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/catalyst-the-mechanics-of-data-center-flexibility/\n- https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/catalyst-the-rise-of-flexible-data-centers/\n- https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/catalyst-fuel-cells-geopolitics-and-the-solar-growth-curve/\n- https://www.canarymedia.com/podcasts/catalyst-with-shayle-kann\n- https://www.nitricity.co/about\n- https://www.nitricity.co/news/nitricity-raises-20-million-series-a\n- https://www.nitricity.co/news/nitricity-raises-50m-to-go-global-with-unique-tech-transforming-almond-waste-into-organic-fertilizer\n- https://eh2.com/news/\n- SEC Form D: EIP Deep Decarbonization Frontier Fund I https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1842065/000184206521000001/primary_doc.xml and https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1842065/000184206522000001/primary_doc.xml\n- SEC Form D: EIP Frontier Fund II https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2081052/000101297525000597/primary_doc.xml and https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2081052/000208105226000001/primary_doc.xml\n- SEC Form D: Nitricity https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1804951/000180495121000002/primary_doc.xml, https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1804951/000180495122000001/primary_doc.xml, https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1804951/000180495125000004/primary_doc.xml\n- SEC Form D: Electric Hydrogen https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1997980/000199798023000001/primary_doc.xml through 000199798023000004\n- SEC Form D: Koloma https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1881912/000188191223000002/primary_doc.xml, https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1881912/000188191224000001/primary_doc.xml, https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1881912/000188191226000004/primary_doc.xml\n- TransAlta 6-K exhibit https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1144800/000127956922000731/ex991.htm\n- SolarCity FWP https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1408356/000119312514437860/d803869dfwp.htm\n- Powur C-AR https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1639406/000167025419000283/document_14.txt\n", "person:shelby-newsad": "\n# Shelby Newsad\n\nPronouns: the firm bio uses \"they/them\" ([src](https://www.compound.vc/team/shelbyn)); some aggregators and a podcast page use \"she\". This file uses \"they\".\n\n## One-line take\nShelby is Compound's bio partner. They are a Cambridge plant-sciences PhD who writes the clearest public map of where AI meets wet-lab biology: autonomous labs, next-generation CROs, bio \"neolabs\", and selling bio data to frontier labs. They are a useful early-warning feed for bio-AI companies, but not a co-investor at the owner's check size.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** they write the thesis before the deal and publish it, which pulls in technical founders. Their bio claims more than 20 active bio and health theses ([src](https://www.compound.vc/team/shelbyn)). They also run sourcing formats that are new for the firm: Compound Spinout Sessions, which match academics with would-be founders (first session August 2026), and an X-first network. That network is how Jason Kelly of Ginkgo became a Compound venture partner ([src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/welcoming-jason-kelly-as-a-venture-partner-at-compound)).\n- **What constrains them:** Compound's small funds ($73M Fund II; $115M Compound 2026 Form D, per `firms/compound.md`) and a bio portfolio where the firm rarely credits a partner by name. The only deal publicly attributed to them is Anagen/HairDAO, a consumer hair-loss DeSci company co-written with Smac. They have no named frontier-AI or AI-chip deal.\n- **What they'd never do:** sell picks-and-shovels data as the business. They warn bio companies that selling data to frontier labs can mean being \"hooked on a revenue stream that's not core\" ([src](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/the-sirens-of-data-sales)). They also distrust model-only moats (\"models have fleeting moats\") and push founders to \"build the hardest version of the company\" ([src](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/predicting-protein-dynamics-moats)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Bullish on bio neolabs, on the record.** In the May 2026 predictions they write \"Bio neolabs will become very large companies.\" They cite Isomorphic's $2.2bn raise from Thrive. Their bear case is that frontier labs acquihire the best ML-bio teams, with \"Meta-level comp packages to thwart competition and upstarts\" ([src](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/biggest-questions-in-biotech), 2026-05-13).\n- **Next-gen CROs as the venture-scale autonomous-science play.** They write that \"built from self-driving labs, could become venture scale businesses\" and that \"a first principles, microfluidics native system for automating science is likely the unlock\" ([src](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/commercializing-autonomous-science), 2025-02-06). The May 2026 predictions repeat the CRO call.\n- **A data-sales playbook with frontier labs as customers.** One option they list for bio startups is to \"Work as a bio Mercor for the foundation labs\" ([src](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/tech-bio-automation-ai-stream-of), 2026-04-06). Four months later they warn that \"most bio companies aren't poised to sell to the frontier labs\" and that \"it might destroy your main company\" ([src](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/the-sirens-of-data-sales), 2026-08-03). Their reasoning: lab bio budgets will tighten once the frontier labs go public.\n- **Anagen deal timing.** Compound invested years after first meeting the founders: \"We met the Anagen founders years ago,\" and the firm was \"lucky to partner with Anagen in early 2025.\" The public \"why we invested\" post came only on 2026-06-17 ([src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/emergent-science-and-funding-coordination)). The post also says Compound \"may buy or sell $HAIR token and $MANE stock,\" which signals that the firm trades the liquid public comparable, Veradermics.\n- **How the Jason Kelly recruit happened.** \"We initially connected with Jason on X.\" The relationship then deepened through an Idea Factory event they ran together ([src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/welcoming-jason-kelly-as-a-venture-partner-at-compound), 2026-08-19). Their X presence works as a sourcing channel, not just a broadcast channel.\n- **Disclosures in the DeSci playbook (co-written with Smac).** Its footnotes read \"Compound is an investor in MoleculeDAO\" and \"...in HairDAO.\" The playbook says \"the only two long-term moats we've observed in crypto today are liquidity & brand\" ([src](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/the-decentralized-science-playbook), 2025-10-23).\n- **Contrarian on asset-centric biotech.** The bear case they flag: \"Misguided early-stage biotech investors over-rotated on assets to prove platforms\" ([src](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/biggest-questions-in-biotech)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**medium.**\n- **Against:** sub-10m checks (aggregator $100K–$3M) at a sub-$150M firm. No named AI-lab, AI-chip or AI-native automation deal. Their only attributed deal is consumer-bio/DeSci (Anagen).\n- **For:** they are one of the few investors writing in public, with falsifiable predictions, about **bio neolabs**, **autonomous-science CROs** and **frontier-lab data demand**. Those categories sit right next to the owner's frontier-AI-for-science interests, such as Periodic Labs, Chai and Isomorphic. Their new Spinout Sessions are a first-look channel into academic AI/bio/materials spinouts.\n- **Use:** read Biocene as a scout. Ask them which bio neolab or self-driving-lab CRO they'd back if the round were too big for Compound. That is a natural handoff to a $20M check.\n- **Firm-level bio-AI holdings that matter, with no partner credited:** Achira ($33M seed, 2025, Dimension-led), CellVoyant (seed, 2024, aggregator), Pheiron (seed $4.22M, 2025-06-20, [CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/company/pheiron/financials), aggregator), Spaero Bio, Briefly Bio, Red Queen Bio. Whether Shelby led any of them is unconfirmed.\n\n## Background\n- PhD in plant sciences, University of Cambridge ([firm bio](https://www.compound.vc/team/shelbyn)). A search snippet also shows an MPhil in Bioscience Enterprise from Cambridge (aggregator, unverified). Dates not found.\n- Researcher in neuroscience at the NIH and in drug discovery at the Helmholtz Institute; dates not given ([firm bio](https://www.compound.vc/team/shelbyn)).\n- Pre-Compound venture: Hummingbird Ventures (analyst, per a search-result snippet only) and Ahren Innovation Capital (listed on [TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/compound/org-chart/dr-shelby), no title or dates). Unverified.\n- Compound: joined July 2022 ([TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/compound/org-chart/dr-shelby), aggregator); Investor 2022 → Partner 2024 (aggregator, now 404); Partner on the current firm page.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.compound.vc/team/shelbyn | Firm bio; shelby@compound.vc listed in posts |\n| Substack / blog | https://shelbyann.substack.com/ (\"Biocene\") | About 1 post a month in 2026 (Mar, Apr, May, Jun, Jul, Aug, Oct); subscriber count not shown |\n| X | https://twitter.com/shelbynewsad | Couldn't fetch (xcancel 451); follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/shelbynewsad | not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Date unknown (Feb 3, year not shown) — Grow Everything — \"Putting Money Where Your Microbe Is. Investing in Biotech.\" — https://www.messaginglab.com/groweverything/blog-post-title-four-yyyd9 — Show notes only (summary): NYC biotech, IndieBio's effect on New York, consumer-bio risk, fermentation. No transcript reviewed.\n- SynBioBeta speaker page exists (https://synbiobeta.com/speakers/shelby-newsad, now 404). Session not confirmed.\n\n## How they think\n- **Main theses:** autonomous / self-driving labs (Gen-4 bio-AI) built microfluidics-first; next-gen CROs serving neolabs, frontier labs and garage biologists; bio neolabs becoming large companies; consumer-first biotech (\"much closer to consumers vs. mediated through doctors\"); human challenge trials as cheap efficacy data; DeSci token financing as a capital and recruiting tool.\n- **Contrarian views:** selling data to frontier labs is a Siren for most bio companies; model moats are fleeting; early-stage biotech over-rotated on single assets; China will not become the global biotech hub.\n- **Calls:** they bet on DeSci (Molecule, HairDAO/Anagen) while it was niche. The neolab and CRO predictions (2026-05) are still open. No public misses found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Biggest questions in biotech](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/biggest-questions-in-biotech) (2026-05-13): bull and bear forks plus falsifiable predictions on neolabs, CROs and China.\n2. [Commercializing Autonomous Science](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/commercializing-autonomous-science) (2025-02-06): the self-driving-lab business-model map.\n3. [The Sirens of Data Sales](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/the-sirens-of-data-sales) (2026-08-03): when bio startups should, and shouldn't, sell to frontier labs.\n4. [Predicting protein dynamics, moats and commercialization](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/predicting-protein-dynamics-moats) (2024-12-18): data plus compute, and why models aren't moats.\n5. [Investing in Anagen](https://www.compound.vc/writing/emergent-science-and-funding-coordination) (2026-06-17): their only public \"why we invested\" post.\n\n## Social engagement\nX couldn't be fetched. Per the vcsheet summary (aggregator), they post about alternative regulatory pathways, autonomous science and AI plus wet-lab work, consumer biotech, TechBio brand challenges, and immunology, vaccines and oncology. Substack posts thank Michael Dempsey, Mackenzie Morehead, Collin Spencer, Sidharth Sirdeshmukh and \"Eryney\" for input. Who amplifies them was not checked.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Anagen / HairDAO ($HAIR) | not disclosed (equity and/or token) | early 2025 (post 2026-06-17) | unknown | not disclosed | [compound.vc](https://www.compound.vc/writing/emergent-science-and-funding-coordination) (co-authored with Smac) |\n| MoleculeDAO | firm-level disclosure in a post they co-wrote; not attributed to them | pre-2024 | unknown | — | [DeSci playbook](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/the-decentralized-science-playbook) |\n\nNo AI, AI-chip or robotics deal is publicly attributed to Shelby. Timing against other investors couldn't be set for Anagen because neither the round nor the co-investors are disclosed.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Anagen/HairDAO:** Compound met the founders \"years ago,\" when they were presenting hair-loss research as HairDAO. The founders came out of crypto. The appeal was community-sourced experiments plus token incentives and fast iteration. Compound invested in early 2025 and published the thesis in June 2026, framed against Veradermics' (~$4bn) valuation ([src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/emergent-science-and-funding-coordination)).\n- **Jason Kelly (venture partner, 2026-08-19):** they connected on X over autonomous science, then ran an Idea Factory event together. Kelly stays almost full time at Ginkgo ([src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/welcoming-jason-kelly-as-a-venture-partner-at-compound)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Compound | Partner, bio & health | 2022 | [firm](https://www.compound.vc/team/shelbyn) |\n| Smac (Compound) | Co-author (DeSci playbook; Anagen post) | 2025 | [src](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/the-decentralized-science-playbook) |\n| Jason Kelly (Ginkgo) | Recruited as Compound venture partner; met on X | 2026 | [src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/welcoming-jason-kelly-as-a-venture-partner-at-compound) |\n| Michael Dempsey (Compound) | MP; reviews their drafts | 2022 | [src](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/the-sirens-of-data-sales) |\n| Mackenzie Morehead (Compound) | Colleague; cross-linked research | — | [src](https://shelbyann.substack.com/p/commercializing-autonomous-science) |\n| Anagen / HairDAO | Investor (Compound) | early 2025 | [src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/emergent-science-and-funding-coordination) |\n| Hummingbird Ventures | Prior role (analyst, snippet only) | — | search snippet, unverified |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which of Achira, CellVoyant, Pheiron, Spaero, Briefly Bio, Red Queen Bio and \"Chemical Synthesis Co.\" did Shelby source or lead?\n- Exact promotion date. The 2024 Partner date comes only from a now-404 aggregator; TheOrg says Partner since 2022.\n- Hummingbird / Ahren titles and dates.\n- Whether Compound Explorer I ($35M, 2026-08) is tied to Spinout Sessions or incubations.\n- The Anagen round size and form (equity vs. $HAIR tokens).\n- X follower count; who in frontier labs engages with them.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.compound.vc/team/shelbyn ; https://www.compound.vc/about ; https://www.compound.vc/writing\n- https://www.compound.vc/writing/emergent-science-and-funding-coordination\n- https://www.compound.vc/writing/welcoming-jason-kelly-as-a-venture-partner-at-compound\n- https://shelbyann.substack.com/archive and posts: commercializing-autonomous-science, predicting-protein-dynamics-moats, the-decentralized-science-playbook, tech-bio-automation-ai-stream-of, biggest-questions-in-biotech, compound-spinout-sessions, the-sirens-of-data-sales\n- https://www.messaginglab.com/groweverything/blog-post-title-four-yyyd9 (show notes)\n- Aggregators: https://theorg.com/org/compound/org-chart/dr-shelby ; https://startupintros.com/people/shelby-newsad ; https://www.vcsheet.com/who/shelby-newsad ; https://the-network-search.vercel.app/profile/shelby-newsad-2970d7cd (404 on 2026-11-10) ; https://www.cbinsights.com/company/pheiron/financials\n- Note: startupintros attaches \"Compound Seed 2018 / Series A 2019 / Series B 2022\" news to Shelby. Those rounds belong to Compound Labs (the DeFi protocol), not to Shelby, so they were ignored.\n", "person:siranush-babakhanova": "\n# Siranush Babakhanova\n\nAlso files as Siranush Nobile and Siranush Babakhanova Nobile. The repo has a duplicate id `siranush-nobile` for the same person: Companies House registers \"BABAKHANOVA NOBILE, Siranush\" and Fund III's Form D names \"Siranush Babakhanova Nobile\" ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2145369/000214536926000002/primary_doc.xml)). All rows here use `siranush-babakhanova`.\n\n## One-line take\nShe is the quieter co-founder of Fundomo, a roughly $1B SPV-heavy frontier-compute GP, and is about 28. She is also the partner on OLIX's board, which makes her the one to watch on Fundomo's chip book.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** she is half of a two-person GP that gets allocations in the hottest compute and new-lab rounds (Etched, Flapping Airplanes, OLIX, Tenstorrent, Mercor, Sakana) and then syndicates each one through a dedicated SPV. She is a named GP manager on all 25 Fundomo Form Ds that full-text search returns ([EDGAR FTS](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Babakhanova%22)).\n- **The fact that changes the picture:** OLIX's board seat is hers, not Corey Nobile's. She was appointed director of OLIX Computing on 2026-07-06 ([Companies House](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/15585099/officers)). tech.eu reports that Fundomo led OLIX's $312M Series B ([src](https://tech.eu/2026/08/03/uk-chip-startup-olix-raises-ps312m-at-ps33bn-valuation/)). This is the only deal where a filing ties her personally, separately from Nobile.\n- **Constraint:** she has no public voice. I found no podcast, post or interview, and every vehicle is signed by Nobile. Outside OLIX, credit is joint, and her individual sourcing cannot be separated from his.\n- **What she'd never do (inference):** build a public brand. Fundomo's whole style is to stay invisible and show up only in filings.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **OLIX board seat, timed to the round.** She was appointed director on 2026-07-06, \"Appointed on 6 July 2026\" ([Companies House](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/15585099/officers)). That is 17 days after the OLX-002 SPV's first sale (2026-06-19, $82.1M from 70 investors) ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149938/000214993826000001/primary_doc.xml)) and four weeks before the B was announced on 2026-08-03. OLIX is her only UK appointment ([officer page](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/UyaDEgAHi4y4Q2i6H9qt4lNt_uU/appointments)).\n- **Who led the B is disputed.** tech.eu writes \"The round was led by New York VC Fundomo\" ([src](https://tech.eu/2026/08/03/uk-chip-startup-olix-raises-ps312m-at-ps33bn-valuation/)). OLIX's own release says only that the round \"included Fundomo, Arm and Hudson River Trading\" ([src](https://olix.com/news/company-raises-series-b)).\n- **Co-controls the Standard Nuclear position.** The IPO prospectus says \"Corey Nobile and Siranush Babakhanova are the managers of Fundomo SN -001 GP\" (and of SN-002 GP) ([424B4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086716/000121390026078747/ea0276071-13.htm)). Corey's Form 3 calls him sole member of those GPs ([Form 3](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084264/000208426426000005/primary_doc.xml)). Member and manager are different roles, so both filings can be accurate. Even so, firms/fundomo.md credits control to Corey alone, and the prospectus shows she shares it.\n- **Age.** Companies House gives her birth year as 1998, \"Date of birth August 1998\" ([src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/15585099/officers)). She has been named on Fundomo GP filings since she was about 25.\n- **Neurotech roots, a possible sourcing angle.** At MIT she \"designed tools for deep brain imaging and in situ proteomics in Boyden lab\" ([KHS](https://knight-hennessy.stanford.edu/people/siranush-babakhanova)). OLIX's founder James Dacombe also runs CoMind, a brain-monitoring company ([tech.eu](https://tech.eu/2026/08/03/uk-chip-startup-olix-raises-ps312m-at-ps33bn-valuation/)). Unconfirmed: I found nothing showing this overlap produced the OLIX deal.\n- **An unmapped vehicle.** \"Who Is John Galt, LP\" sold $28.26M to 8 investors (first sale 2025-08-12, $100K minimum). She and Nobile manage its GP. It does not follow the two-letter prefix convention, and ET001's Form D was filed under its accession prefix ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084166/000208416625000001/primary_doc.xml)). The underlying company is unknown.\n- **Shift to private-equity classification.** Fund III (2026-08-11) is filed as a \"Private Equity Fund\" with an indefinite offering size, unlike the venture-fund classification on every earlier vehicle ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2145369/000214536926000002/primary_doc.xml)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** She co-runs a small, early-stage GP that writes into the largest frontier-compute rounds: Flapping Airplanes seed ($180M) and Series A ($1B), and OLIX Series B ($312M, reportedly led). It can deploy $10M+ per name through SPVs (ME-003 $118.5M, OLX-002 $82.1M, TT-001 $31M) ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2139243/000213924326000001/primary_doc.xml)). She is young, technical (MIT physics, neurotech tools), and holds a board seat on a $3.3B AI-chip company. That makes her a natural co-investor and peer for $20M checks into big seeds. Watch-outs: they rarely lead, there is no public thesis, and with a two-person GP her separate judgment is hard to isolate. Deals that matter most: OLIX, Flapping Airplanes, Etched, Tenstorrent.\n\n## Background\n- From Yerevan, Armenia; nationality American and Armenian per Companies House ([src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/15585099/officers)).\n- First Armenian woman to win each of six International Olympiad awards, per KHS ([src](https://knight-hennessy.stanford.edu/people/siranush-babakhanova)).\n- MIT BS Physics, 2020. Research in Ed Boyden's lab (deep brain imaging, in situ proteomics) and NASA simulation work. Co-founded the Xapiens human-augmentation initiative and was president of the MIT Armenian Society ([KHS](https://knight-hennessy.stanford.edu/people/siranush-babakhanova); [MIT Physics](https://physics.mit.edu/news/siranush-babakhanova-and-michal-gala-named-2020-knight-hennessy-scholars/)).\n- 2020 Knight-Hennessy Scholar, Stanford PhD in Biophysics. The KHS page labels the program \"PhD in Biophysics (Discontinued)\", and whether she completed it is unconfirmed. NSF GRFP, Interact and ADVANCE fellow; Hertz Fellowship finalist ([src](https://knight-hennessy.stanford.edu/people/siranush-babakhanova)). The firm file cites LinkedIn for Stanford 2020–2025 (not re-verified here).\n- 2023: co-founded Fundomo with her husband Corey Nobile ([about.me](https://about.me/coreynobile)). Named managing member of the GP on the Fund I Form D (first sale 2023-12-01) ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2023492/000202349224000001/primary_doc.xml)). The couple also fund math and physics research ([about.me](https://about.me/coreynobile)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | none found | fundomo.com names no people ([src](https://www.fundomo.com)) |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | unconfirmed | no verified handle; x.com/siranushb not verified (x.com returns 200 for any handle) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/siranushb/ | cited by firm file; not fetched this pass (login wall) |\n| GitHub | none found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n| Registry | [Companies House officer page](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/UyaDEgAHi4y4Q2i6H9qt4lNt_uU/appointments) | 1 appointment (OLIX), checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Profile | [Knight-Hennessy](https://knight-hennessy.stanford.edu/people/siranush-babakhanova) | bio and headshot |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Checked: EDGAR full-text search, Companies House, KHS, about.me, fundomo.com and the Tutor Intelligence letter (which names Corey, not her). Web search was rate-limited this pass, so a broader search of podcasts and X is still pending.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** nothing in her own words. Fundomo's tagline is \"State-scale systems\" ([src](https://www.fundomo.com)). Her KHS statement says she aspires to apply physical and computational sciences to read out, manipulate and augment biological systems ([src](https://knight-hennessy.stanford.edu/people/siranush-babakhanova)). The book leans toward compute efficiency: OLIX's optical processor, Etched's ASIC, Tenstorrent, and Flapping Airplanes' work on chip efficiency.\n- **Contrarian views:** none found.\n- **Calls right or wrong:** Etched ET001 marked about 1.8x by StepStone (cost $8.22M, fair value $15.06M at 2026-06-30) ([N-PORT](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1918642/000119312526376298/ea0303531-04_nportex.htm)). OLIX's valuation roughly tripled between its Feb 2026 Series A and the Aug 2026 Series B ([tech.eu](https://tech.eu/2026/08/03/uk-chip-startup-olix-raises-ps312m-at-ps33bn-valuation/)). Standard Nuclear went from a Dec 2024 SAFE to a July 2026 IPO ([424B4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086716/000121390026078747/ea0276071-13.htm)).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Knight-Hennessy profile](https://knight-hennessy.stanford.edu/people/siranush-babakhanova): the only first-person-adjacent statement of her interests.\n- [OLIX Companies House officers](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/15585099/officers): her board seat and her fellow directors.\n- [Standard Nuclear 424B4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086716/000121390026078747/ea0276071-13.htm): names her as SN GP manager.\n- [OLIX Series B release](https://olix.com/news/company-raises-series-b) and [tech.eu](https://tech.eu/2026/08/03/uk-chip-startup-olix-raises-ps312m-at-ps33bn-valuation/).\n- `firms/fundomo.md`: vehicle map and LPs.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public social activity was verified.\n\n## Portfolio\nEvery row below is a Fundomo vehicle whose Form D names her as a GP manager. Prefix-to-company mappings marked * are inferred (SN = Standard Nuclear is confirmed by the prospectus).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OLIX | Series B ($312M at $3.3B). OX-001* $3.85M (2026-03-30); OLX-002 $82.1M (2026-06-19); she is a director from 2026-07-06 | 2026-08-03 | Led per tech.eu; company says \"included\" | Arm, Hudson River Trading, Hummingbird, Plural, Creandum, Phoenix Court, Reed Hastings | [tech.eu](https://tech.eu/2026/08/03/uk-chip-startup-olix-raises-ps312m-at-ps33bn-valuation/), [OLX-002](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149938/000214993826000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Flapping Airplanes* | Seed ($180M) via FA-001: $10.3M, 3 investors | first sale 2026-01-23; announced 2026-01-28 | no | Index, GV, Sequoia | [FA-001](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2124227/000212422726000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Flapping Airplanes* | Series A ($1B) via FA-002: $24.85M, 17 investors (about a 2.4x re-up) | first sale 2026-08-10; announced 2026-10-21 | no | Index, Lux, Sequoia, GV, Kleiner, NVIDIA | [FA-002](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2158561/000215856126000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Etched | ET001: $40.3M, 10 investors | 2025-06-05 | no | | [ET001](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084294/000208416625000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Etched | ET-002: $15.1M, 11 investors | 2025-12-31 | no | | [ET-002](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2124223/000212422326000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Standard Nuclear | Series A via SN-001 ($5.49M) and A-2 via SN-002 ($10.34M); >5% holder at the July 2026 IPO | 2025-08-21 / 2026-01-31 | no | Decisive Point, Welara, Washington Harbour | [424B4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086716/000121390026078747/ea0276071-13.htm) |\n| Mercor* | ME-002 $44.5M; ME-003 $118.5M | 2025-11-12 / 2026-08-10 | no | | [ME-002](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2124232/000212423226000001/primary_doc.xml), [ME-003](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2158633/000215863326000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Tenstorrent* | TT-001 $31.0M; TT-002 $12.5M | 2026-03-30 / 2026-07-03 | no | | [TT-001](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2139243/000213924326000001/primary_doc.xml), [TT-002](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2149959/000214995926000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Sakana AI* | Series B via SA-001: $10.3M, first sale 2026-02-01 (about 11 weeks after the announcement) | 2025-11-17 | no | MUFG, Khosla, Lux, NEA, In-Q-Tel | [SA-001](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2124230/000212423026000001/primary_doc.xml), [Sakana](https://sakana.ai/series-b/) |\n| Unknown | \"Who Is John Galt, LP\": $28.26M, 8 investors | 2025-08-12 | | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084166/000208416625000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Smaller SPVs | GH-001 $1.55M, FL-001 $3.13M, GL-005 $0.52M, BN-001 $0.35M (companies unmapped) | 2026 | | | EDGAR FTS |\n\nCommingled funds she co-manages: Fund I ($42.98M, 62 investors), Fund II ($87.37M, 74), Flagship ($381M, 132), Morningstar ($41.3M, 44), Fund III (nothing sold yet) ([Flagship](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2068182/000206818226000002/primary_doc.xml)).\n\n**Timing:** she was never the first money into any of these. Fundomo came in at the seed of Flapping Airplanes (co-led by Index, GV and Sequoia), and at OLIX only at the B, after Hummingbird's A. Its pattern is to double down. Flapping Airplanes went from $10.3M to $24.85M. Etched has four vehicles (2024–2025). Mercor grew from $5.7M to $44.5M to $118.5M. At OLIX, a $3.85M vehicle was followed three months later by an $82.1M vehicle and a board seat.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **OLIX:** Fundomo first appears through OX-001 ($3.85M, 2026-03-30), about six weeks after OLIX's $220M Feb 2026 round. Next came OLX-002 ($82.1M from 70 investors, 2026-06-19), then her directorship (2026-07-06) and the B announcement (2026-08-03) ([Companies House](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/15585099/officers); [OX-001](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2124231/000212423126000001/primary_doc.xml)). StepStone took $13.5M of OLX-002 ([N-PORT](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1918642/000119312526376298/ea0303531-04_nportex.htm)). Why she rather than Nobile took the seat, and who introduced Fundomo, are not known.\n- **Flapping Airplanes:** possible MIT/Stanford overlap with the Spector founders; no source confirms it (see firms/fundomo.md).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Corey Nobile | Spouse; Fundomo co-founder | 2023 (firm) | [about.me](https://about.me/coreynobile) |\n| Fundomo | at: Co-founder, Managing Member of the GP | 2023 | [Fund I Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2023492/000202349224000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| OLIX (James Dacombe, CEO) | Board director | 2026-07-06 | [Companies House](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/15585099/officers) |\n| Fellow OLIX directors: Saul Klein (2025-06), Jonathan Heiliger (2025-06), Taavet Hinrikus (2025-10), Firat Ileri (2026-02), Nick McKeown (2026-02) | Co-directors | 2026 | same |\n| Ed Boyden (MIT) | Former lab (undergraduate researcher) | to 2020 | [KHS](https://knight-hennessy.stanford.edu/people/siranush-babakhanova) |\n| StepStone, USVC | LPs in vehicles she co-manages | 2025–2026 | [N-PORT](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1918642/000119312526376298/ea0303531-04_nportex.htm) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Why does she, not Nobile, hold the OLIX seat? Is she Fundomo's lead on chips?\n- Did Fundomo lead the OLIX B (tech.eu) or only participate (company release)?\n- What company sits in \"Who Is John Galt, LP\" ($28.3M)?\n- Did she complete the Stanford PhD? Did her Stanford years overlap with the Spectors?\n- Merge the duplicate id `siranush-nobile` into `siranush-babakhanova`.\n- Is she an LP anywhere? I checked EDGAR full-text search for her name and found only Fundomo GP roles. No LP link was found, so no -lp.csv was written.\n\n## Sources\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/15585099/officers (checked 2026-11-10)\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/UyaDEgAHi4y4Q2i6H9qt4lNt_uU/appointments\n- https://knight-hennessy.stanford.edu/people/siranush-babakhanova\n- https://about.me/coreynobile ; https://www.fundomo.com\n- https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Babakhanova%22 ; https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Siranush%20Nobile%22 (25 Fundomo Form Ds parsed 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086716/000121390026078747/ea0276071-13.htm (Standard Nuclear 424B4)\n- https://olix.com/news/company-raises-series-b ; https://tech.eu/2026/08/03/uk-chip-startup-olix-raises-ps312m-at-ps33bn-valuation/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1918642/000119312526376298/ea0303531-04_nportex.htm (StepStone N-PORT)\n- https://sakana.ai/series-b/\n", "person:slater-stich": "\n# Slater Stich\n\n## One-line take\nHe is BCV's Bay Area partner for infrastructure and data tools. He is also the partner of record on BCV's frontier-lab bets (Periodic Labs seed, Black Forest Labs Series B, Oratomic), and in 2026 he co-signed the firm's $1.6B fund announcement. That makes him one of the people setting BCV's AI agenda, though his title stays \"Partner.\"\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He shows conviction through long technical essays. On the day BCV's Periodic Labs record was created (2025-09-30), his memo \"We Must Know, We Will Know\" worked through the history of high-temperature superconductors, named four bottlenecks (lab throughput, data loss, literature review, limits of simulation), set out the lab that would fix them, and ended \"And you'd call the company Periodic\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/we-must-know-we-will-know/)). He builds credibility with researchers by interviewing the authors of landmark papers, for example his \"History of Diffusion\" series with Jascha Sohl-Dickstein, Yang Song and Sander Dieleman ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/diffusion-without-tears/)).\n- **What constrains him:** His stated mandate is \"infrastructure, with a focus on tools for data teams\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/slater-stich/)). On both frontier-lab deals BCV followed: a16z led Periodic, and AMP and Salesforce Ventures co-led BFL's Series B. On Oratomic, BCV does not appear in the press investor list at all. He leads seeds in developer and data tools (Polars, Structify) and follows on labs.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** Back a tool he hasn't used. His own account of his first deal, Replicate, is that using the product told him about the founders ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/slater-stich/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The memo was the announcement, and it priced the round in advance.** \"We Must Know, We Will Know\" says the right AI-for-materials lab would \"raise hundreds of millions of dollars for GPUs\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/we-must-know-we-will-know/)). In BCV's CMS, the post (created 2025-09-30T16:23Z) and the Periodic portfolio record (created 2025-09-30T20:56Z) appeared the same day ([src](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26%22tXj82aAr3VYUsa9AwjV9XS%22%20in%20partner%5B%5D._ref%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Ename%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Eslug.current%2C_createdAt%7D)).\n- **He underwrites talent pedigree explicitly.** The memo describes the ideal hires as \"maybe the people who invented the attention mechanism\" or members of the original ChatGPT team ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/we-must-know-we-will-know/)).\n- **He diligences by using the product.** On Replicate he wrote: \"Using the tool helped me understand the decisions Ben and Andreas had made\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/slater-stich/)). It was most likely pre-BCV. BCV's 2023 release calls him *formerly* founder of Activation Fund ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/)), and Replicate's Series B names no BCV ([src](https://replicate.com/blog/series-b)). BCV's CMS also has no Replicate record ([src](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22replicate%22%5D)). That is consistent with a pre-BCV deal but doesn't prove it. Whether the money went through Activation Fund is unconfirmed. Replicate was acquired by Cloudflare, announced 2025-11-17 ([src](https://replicate.com/blog/replicate-cloudflare)).\n- **His view of AI is labour automation, not communication.** On his personal blog, in \"Automate or Die\" (2024-11), he writes \"We are poor in intelligence,\" and argues that AI is closer to farm mechanization than to the Internet ([src](https://www.slater.works/posts/automate-or-die/)).\n- **On timelines he is a \"build it to know it\" person.** His Manhattan Project essay (2023-08-09) concludes that the only way to predict technology \"is to build things\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/what-we-can-actually-learn-about-ai-from-the-manhattan-project/)).\n- **His weight inside BCV is rising without a title change.** He is one of three co-authors, with Kevin Zhang and Christina Melas-Kyriazi, of \"Capital's Duty to the Future,\" the post announcing BCV's new $1.6B fund for early and growth deals (2026-09-16) ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/capital-s-duty-to-the-future/)). He also wrote Amanda Huang's promotion post to Principal (2026-02-03) ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-amanda-huang-our-newest-principal/)).\n- **Gap between BCV's CMS and the press on Oratomic.** BCV's CMS lists Oratomic (Early, 2026; partners Rak Garg and Slater Stich; record created 2026-07-13). The Quantum Insider's 2026-10-08 report names six leads for the $775M raised and does not mention BCV ([src](https://thequantuminsider.com/2026/10/08/oratomic-secures-775-million-fault-tolerant-quantum-computers)). The same thing happens on Periodic, where BCV is not on the company's backer list. On mega-rounds, BCV under Stich takes unlisted, non-lead allocations.\n- **He sources through roundtables as well as essays.** In 2023 he and Saanya Ojha ran a code-gen roundtable with engineering leaders. Their takeaway was \"context loading works better than fine-tuning, at least for today\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/3-takeaways-from-our-code-gen-roundtable-with-ai-engineers/)).\n- **Activation Fund.** BCV's 2023-02-28 release calls him \"formerly founder of Activation Fund and a data scientist for Square\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/)). The activation.fund site carries agency-template filler (client counts, project budgets). Its only useful signal is the handles @slaterstich, @jakeklamka and @ericcolson in its header, so I treat the co-runners as needs-id ([src](https://www.activation.fund/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High, with caveats.**\n- **For:** He is the BCV partner of record on Periodic Labs' $300M a16z-led seed. That is exactly the mega-seed format where a ~$20M check fits, and the memo shows he will underwrite \"hundreds of millions\" for a new lab. He is also on Black Forest Labs (frontier image models, Series B) and Oratomic (fault-tolerant quantum, compute-adjacent). His writing (\"Automate or Die,\" the Fund XI post on post-AGI capital) matches the AI-native automation thesis.\n- **Against:** His stated mandate is data and infra tooling. On every frontier-lab deal BCV was neither first nor the lead, so he is a co-investor on labs, not an originator. For me that makes him a co-investment peer and a read on which labs BCV will join, rather than a source of first-money deal flow.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** Periodic Labs, Black Forest Labs, Oratomic, Replicate (pre-BCV, acquired by Cloudflare).\n\n## Background\nDurations come from BCV's CMS career timeline, which gives years but no start dates ([src](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22team%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22slater-stich%22%5D)):\n- Princeton (4 yrs). Degree not stated.\n- UC Berkeley (1 yr). Program not stated.\n- Elasticbox (1 yr)\n- Square (2 yrs), as a data scientist per [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/)\n- Stanford GSB (2 yrs)\n- Valor (3 yrs). Logo \"valor\"; probably Valor Equity Partners (unconfirmed).\n- Activation Fund (2 yrs), founder. Its tagline is \"Data teams deserve better tools\" ([src](https://www.activation.fund/)).\n- BCV (3 yrs as of the CMS record, created 2024-08-22). He was named among partners added \"over the last year\" in the 2023-02-28 release, so he joined around 2022 as Partner ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/)).\n- He grew up in New Smyrna Beach, Florida, and surfs ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/slater-stich/)).\n- **Promotion:** BCV hired him as Partner; there is no internal climb. fast_promotion is `no`. The signal is rising influence since 2023 (fund co-author, mentor of a Principal), not a title change.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.slater.works/ | Personal blog with 19 posts (history, math, AI); posts undated except \"Automate or Die\" (2024-11). Checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://baincapitalventures.com/team/slater-stich/ | BCV insights: about 10 posts, 2023 to 2026 |\n| X | https://twitter.com/slaterstich | Not fetched; follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/slater-stich-4658a849/ | Not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | \"History of Diffusion\" interview series (YouTube) | https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VpYNlHIHT7o (Sohl-Dickstein), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ud6z5SkjoZI (Yang Song) |\n| Email | sstich@baincapital.com | Listed on BCV CMS |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-01 — BCV — \"History of Diffusion\" interviews with Jascha Sohl-Dickstein, Yang Song and Sander Dieleman, with a companion primer ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/diffusion-without-tears/)). He hosts the series rather than appearing as a guest. Transcripts not reviewed.\n- 2023-06 — BCV roundtable (written up, not recorded) — code generation with engineering leaders, co-run with Saanya Ojha ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/3-takeaways-from-our-code-gen-roundtable-with-ai-engineers/)).\n- No guest podcast appearances found. Web search was unavailable this run, so coverage is incomplete.\n\n## How they think\n- **Instruments drive discovery.** Galileo had the telescope; we have AI. He asks which \"obvious\" bottlenecks AI removes ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/we-must-know-we-will-know/)).\n- **Intelligence is the scarce input.** AI automates labour, as mechanization did ([src](https://www.slater.works/posts/automate-or-die/)).\n- **Timelines are unknowable across scientific breakthroughs.** How confident you are about AGI should depend on how many discoveries remain; you learn by building ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/what-we-can-actually-learn-about-ai-from-the-manhattan-project/)).\n- **Infrastructure is still early.** \"A lot of software infra feels way harder than it needs to be\" ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/slater-stich/)).\n- **Contrarian:** in a post-scarcity world, people will compete for status. \"The final frontier is not space — it's status\" ([src](https://www.slater.works/posts/post-scarcity/)).\n- **Call that played out:** Replicate, his first investment, went from ML versioning to running open models and was acquired by Cloudflare in 2025.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [We Must Know, We Will Know](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/we-must-know-we-will-know/) (2025-09-30). The Periodic thesis, and how he turns history into a company spec.\n2. [Automate or Die](https://www.slater.works/posts/automate-or-die/) (2024-11). His core AI worldview.\n3. [The Most Valuable Takeaways for AI From the Manhattan Project](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/what-we-can-actually-learn-about-ai-from-the-manhattan-project/) (2023-08-09). On timelines and building.\n4. [Diffusion Without Tears](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/diffusion-without-tears/) (2025-01). How he earns researcher trust.\n5. [Capital's Duty to the Future](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/capital-s-duty-to-the-future/) (2026-09-16). BCV Fund XI; shows his voice at firm level.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X was not fetchable, and web search was unavailable this run.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Replicate | First investment he made (round unknown; pre-BCV) | unknown | unknown | Later: a16z led the $40M Series B (2023-12-05) with NVentures, Heavybit, Sequoia, YC | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/slater-stich/), [src](https://replicate.com/blog/series-b) |\n| Polars | Seed (~$4M) | announced 2023-08-03 | yes (BCV) | none named | [src](https://pola.rs/posts/company-announcement/) |\n| Northflank | Early | 2024 (CMS) | unknown | unknown | [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26%22tXj82aAr3VYUsa9AwjV9XS%22%20in%20partner%5B%5D._ref%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Ename%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Eslug.current%2C_createdAt%7D) |\n| Structify | Seed | 2025-04-30 | yes (BCV); co-partner Rak Garg | unknown | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/structify-makes-building-custom-datasets-easy-with-ai/) |\n| Astronomer (not his deal) | Growth | 2025 | n/a. He co-wrote the BCV post; the CMS partners of record are Enrique Salem and Saanya Ojha | unknown | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/from-airflow-to-dataops-astronomer-is-building-the-data-control-plane-for-the-enterprise/) |\n| Periodic Labs | Seed ($300M) | 2025-09-30 | no (a16z led) | a16z, Felicis, DST, NVentures, Accel | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/we-must-know-we-will-know/), [src](https://periodic.com/) |\n| Black Forest Labs | Series B ($300M, $3.25B post) | 2025-12-01 | no (AMP and Salesforce Ventures co-led) | Temasek, Air Street, Lux, GC, NVIDIA and others | [src](https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier) |\n| Oratomic | Early | 2026 (CMS record 2026-07-13) | no evidence of a lead; co-partner Rak Garg | Leads per press: ARCH, Spark, Khosla, Index, GC, Bezos Expeditions | [src](https://thequantuminsider.com/2026/10/08/oratomic-secures-775-million-fault-tolerant-quantum-computers) |\n\n**Was he first?** On Polars and Structify, BCV led the seed (first institutional money is likely but unconfirmed). On Periodic, BFL and Oratomic, no: BCV joined rounds led by others. On Replicate, it was his first investment ever, but whether he was Replicate's first investor is unsourced.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Replicate:** He had tried many ML versioning tools and found them clunky. Replicate's tool was \"effortless,\" and using it convinced him about founders Ben Firshman and Andreas Jansson ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/slater-stich/)).\n- **Periodic Labs:** He published the thesis memo the same day the BCV record was created. Amanda Huang (then an associate, later Principal) \"played a key role,\" in his words ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-amanda-huang-our-newest-principal/)). The intro path is unknown.\n- **Structify:** BCV led the seed. His published founder interview covers how Ronak Gandhi and Alex Reichenbach met on their first day of college, and how Alex Goldstein came for three months and stayed ([src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/structify-makes-building-custom-datasets-easy-with-ai/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bain Capital Ventures | Partner | ~2022 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/) |\n| Amanda Huang | His principal; he wrote her promotion post | 2024 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-amanda-huang-our-newest-principal/) |\n| Rak Garg | Co-partner on Structify and Oratomic | 2025 | [CMS](https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26%22tXj82aAr3VYUsa9AwjV9XS%22%20in%20partner%5B%5D._ref%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Ename%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Eslug.current%2C_createdAt%7D) |\n| Saanya Ojha | Co-author on the code-gen roundtable (2023) and the Astronomer post (2025) | 2023 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/3-takeaways-from-our-code-gen-roundtable-with-ai-engineers/) |\n| Kevin Zhang, Christina Melas-Kyriazi | Co-authors of the Fund XI announcement | 2026-09 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/capital-s-duty-to-the-future/) |\n| Jascha Sohl-Dickstein, Yang Song, Sander Dieleman | Interviewees in the History of Diffusion series | 2025-01 | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/diffusion-without-tears/) |\n| Ben Firshman, Andreas Jansson (Replicate) | Founders of his first investment | unknown | [src](https://baincapitalventures.com/team/slater-stich/) |\n| @jakeklamka, @ericcolson | Handles on the Activation Fund site (needs-id) | ~2020–21 | [src](https://www.activation.fund/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Was he Replicate's first investor? What round, and through Activation Fund or personally?\n- Who was in Activation Fund, and who were its LPs?\n- Oratomic: what did BCV invest, and in which round? BCV is absent from the press investor list.\n- Periodic: how big was BCV's allocation, and what was the intro path?\n- Is the Princeton or Berkeley time a math PhD? His blog's heavy math suggests it, but nothing confirms it.\n- What does his X feed say? It was not fetched this run.\n\n## Sources\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/team/slater-stich/\n- BCV CMS team record: https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22team%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22slater-stich%22%5D\n- BCV CMS portfolio-by-partner query: https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26%22tXj82aAr3VYUsa9AwjV9XS%22%20in%20partner%5B%5D._ref%5D%7Btitle%2Cyear_of_investment%2C%22stage%22%3Astage%5B%5D-%3Ename%2C%22partners%22%3Apartner%5B%5D-%3Eslug.current%2C_createdAt%7D\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/we-must-know-we-will-know/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/capital-s-duty-to-the-future/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/meet-amanda-huang-our-newest-principal/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bcv-launches-new-fund-x-select-iv-funds/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/what-we-can-actually-learn-about-ai-from-the-manhattan-project/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/diffusion-without-tears/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/3-takeaways-from-our-code-gen-roundtable-with-ai-engineers/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/structify-makes-building-custom-datasets-easy-with-ai/\n- https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/from-airflow-to-dataops-astronomer-is-building-the-data-control-plane-for-the-enterprise/\n- https://www.slater.works/posts/automate-or-die/ , https://www.slater.works/posts/post-scarcity/\n- https://pola.rs/posts/company-announcement/\n- https://natlawreview.com/press-releases/update-black-forest-labs-announces-series-b-investment-accelerate-frontier\n- https://thequantuminsider.com/2026/10/08/oratomic-secures-775-million-fault-tolerant-quantum-computers\n- https://replicate.com/blog/series-b , https://replicate.com/blog/replicate-cloudflare\n- https://www.activation.fund/\n", "person:smac": "\n# Smac\n\n## One-line take\nCompound's pseudonymous crypto partner thinks like a public-markets fundamental investor applied to tokens and onchain market structure. Smac is named on no AI, AI-chip, robotics or bio-AI deal; their only attributed deal is the Anagen/HairDAO DeSci investment, which they co-wrote up with Shelby Newsad. Watch Smac for crypto-market-structure views, not for frontier-AI deal flow.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** they run Compound's liquid and early-stage crypto sleeve with a fundamentals and valuation lens that most crypto VCs don't have. Their essays use dividend-yield and leader-premium frameworks on perp DEX tokens ([src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/if-youre-not-first-youre-maybe-potentially), 2026-04-02). Their reach comes from being a known anon in crypto Twitter and podcast circles, through their own show Smac Talk and guest spots ([Apple](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/id1794010439); [Liquid Culture](https://share.transistor.fm/s/f97134cb)).\n- **Constraint:** Compound's funds are small ($73M Fund II; $115M Compound 2026 Form D, per [firms/compound.md](../firms/compound.md)), so Smac writes sub-$10M checks. The real name isn't public, which limits LP-facing and board-level visibility. No deal is publicly attributed to Smac alone.\n- **What they'd never do:** pay a follower's discount just because it looks cheap next to the leader. They argue that in nascent markets the leader's premium is often deserved ([src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/if-youre-not-first-youre-maybe-potentially)). They are also skeptical of CEX-first token launches that are \"optimized for tokens with a half-life of weeks\" ([src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/from-backrooms-to-builder-codes), 2025-09-03).\n- **Identity:** real name not given anywhere found (needs-id). The firm bio is only a joke line ([src](https://www.compound.vc/team/smac)). Pronouns are not given by the firm; some summaries say \"he\", but no primary source confirms it, so this file uses \"they\".\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Anagen is the firm's DeSci thesis, written as a crypto coordination bet.** Smac and Shelby Newsad co-wrote it: \"crypto’s unique global coordination properties will continue to push into emergent verticals\" ([Compound](https://www.compound.vc/writing/emergent-science-and-funding-coordination), 2026-06-17; investment made early 2025). The post publishes about 1.5 years after the check. The round and co-investors are undisclosed.\n- **The DeSci angle is a data play:** the post names \"Global coordination to solve cold start data collection problems\" as a crypto strength ([src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/emergent-science-and-funding-coordination)). The same token-incentivized data-collection logic underlies DePIN (Helium and Hivemapper are cited in the post).\n- **Valuation-first, unusual for a crypto VC:** \"Alpha is paid out of other people’s errors or misjudgments.\" ([src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/a-trillion-times-larger), 2026-08-20). The essay critiques the AI-era consensus of \"Don’t bother with valuation work\".\n- **Their view of AI labs is binary:** labs are either \"enormous capex sinks that turn into the most valuable economic infrastructure\" or money pits, and \"AI is even more of a Rorschach test\" ([src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/let-the-bubble-wash-over-you), 2026-05-21). This is a public-markets observer's view, not an AI-deal view.\n- **Rule of thumb on tails:** \"you can't really rationally price an investment without probability-weighting the tail outcomes\" ([src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/if-youre-not-first-youre-maybe-potentially)). They apply it to Hyperliquid versus Lighter.\n- **The portfolio and the podcast overlap.** Kinetiq, a Hyperliquid liquid-staking protocol, appears in Compound's crypto portfolio ([src](https://www.compound.vc/portfolio2)). Its co-founder Omnia was a Smac Talk guest on Mar 3 ([Apple](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/id1794010439)). It is an inference that Smac sourced or covers Kinetiq; no source names them on the deal.\n- **Sourcing runs through the crypto-native anon network:** Smac Talk guests are mostly pseudonymous traders and founders (Cheshire, Nexus, Peach, Gwart, Molesy), plus Zee Prime Capital and Theia Research ([Apple](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/id1794010439)). The show calls itself \"hosted by an anon crypto VC about crypto, markets, technology\" ([podstatus](https://podstatus.com/podcasts/smac-talk-305899)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**low.** Smac is a crypto and liquid-token specialist. They are named on no AI, AI-chip, robotics or bio-AI deal. The nearest things are firm-level positions with no partner named: Prime Intellect, which Compound files under AI rather than crypto, and \"Local Compute Co.\", a stealth company listed in Compound's **crypto** section ([src](https://www.compound.vc/portfolio2)). If Local Compute Co. is decentralized compute, Smac is the most likely owner (inference). That is the one question worth asking them. Fund size makes them sub-$10M, not a peer for $20M checks. Useful as a read on crypto x AI market structure and as a route to Hyperliquid-ecosystem founders, not as a frontier-AI co-investor.\n\n## Background\nNothing found on education or prior jobs. Earliest dated Compound affiliation found: 2024-06-05, \"Smac (investor @ Compound VC)\" ([Liquid Culture](https://share.transistor.fm/s/f97134cb)). Now listed as Partner ([about](https://www.compound.vc/about)). Their essays suggest a public-markets, long/short fundamental background; they describe a bias toward that style ([src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/let-the-bubble-wash-over-you)). The career history is unconfirmed.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.compound.vc/team/smac | firm bio; joke one-liner only (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://0xsmac.substack.com | \"Semi-Lucid Thoughts\"; 12 posts May 2025 – Aug 2026, roughly monthly |\n| X | https://x.com/0xsmac | follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | none (placeholder link on firm page) | pseudonymous |\n| GitHub | not found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Smac Talk — https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/id1794010439 | 10 episodes, Jan 2025 – Nov 2026 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-06 — Liquid Culture (Ben Roy and Natalie Mullins) — crypto culture from early Bitcoin to NFT mania, with Jonathan Moore (TCG Crypto) — [link](https://share.transistor.fm/s/f97134cb) — not reviewed beyond the description.\n- Date not found — Decypher — \"How to build an edge as a crypto trader ft 0xsmac\" — [link](https://www.buzzsprout.com/2380082/episodes/16647390-how-to-build-an-edge-as-a-crypto-trader-ft-0xsmac) — no transcript.\n- Host, Smac Talk: Felipe (Theia Research), 2025-01-30; Matti and Luffi (Zee Prime Capital; DeSci and a robotics tease), 2025-02-12; Gwart, 2025-03-18; Peach, 2025-07-02; Annanay (QFEX, equity perps), Jan 5; Nexus, Feb 16; Omnia (Kinetiq), Mar 3; Cheshire, Mar 25; Molesy, Jun 7; Alex (Duality Research), about 2026-11-05 ([Apple](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/id1794010439)). These are episode descriptions only; no transcripts were reviewed.\n- \"Chain Venturer: 0xSmac of Compound VC\" interview (Launchy newsletter): the URL returned 404, so it wasn't reviewed ([url](https://launchy.beehiiv.com/p/chain-venturer-0xsmac-compound-vc)).\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** markets have moved from a stratiform regime to a \"convective chain\" one, with rolling narrative cells (AI, robotics, quantum, crypto) ([src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/let-the-bubble-wash-over-you)). They favor fundamentals over the multiple ([src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/a-trillion-times-larger)). Leaders deserve a premium in nascent markets ([src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/if-youre-not-first-youre-maybe-potentially)). Onchain capital formation should replace CEX listings ([src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/from-backrooms-to-builder-codes), co-written with krane of asula labs). Crypto coordination applies to science funding ([src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/emergent-science-and-funding-coordination)).\n- **Contrarian:** they reject the \"buy the dip\" reflex as untested for their generation of investors ([src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/when-the-iron-turns-to-silk), 2025-05-01). They warn that momentum positioning in physical-world AI winners is fragile ([src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/a-trillion-times-larger)).\n- **Calls:** no scored calls found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [If You're Not First, You're (Maybe?…) Last](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/if-youre-not-first-youre-maybe-potentially) — the leader-premium and tail-pricing framework, applied to perp DEXs.\n2. [From Backrooms to Builder Codes](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/from-backrooms-to-builder-codes) — their blueprint for onchain token capital formation.\n3. [Emergent science and funding coordination](https://www.compound.vc/writing/emergent-science-and-funding-coordination) — the Anagen/HairDAO DeSci thesis (with Shelby Newsad).\n4. [A Trillion Times Larger](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/a-trillion-times-larger) — fundamentals vs. multiple; AI-era mispricing.\n5. [Let The Bubble Wash Over You](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/let-the-bubble-wash-over-you) — the narrative-cycle market regime; their AI-lab capex view.\n\n## Social engagement\nThe essays thank @mhdempsey, @rajivpoc, @kratikl_ and @thiccyth0t for feedback ([src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/from-backrooms-to-builder-codes)). They link Dempsey's narrative-cycle piece ([src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/let-the-bubble-wash-over-you)). Topics: perp DEXs (Hyperliquid, Lighter), token market structure, prediction markets, macro. X activity was not reviewed directly.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Anagen (HairDAO) | not disclosed | early 2025 | not disclosed | not disclosed | [Compound](https://www.compound.vc/writing/emergent-science-and-funding-coordination) (Smac co-author) |\n| *Firm-level, Smac not named:* The Clearing Company | Seed $15M | 2025-08-27 | No (USV led) | Coinbase Ventures, Haun, Variant, Asylum, Cursor Capital, Earl Grey, Rubik | [The Block](https://www.theblock.co/news/deals/2025-08-27-former-polymarket-exec-raises-15-million-from-coinbase-and-usv-for-rival-prediction-platform-368519) |\n| *Firm-level, Smac not named:* Kinetiq, Local Compute Co., Delpho, Manifest, Threshold, Arbitrum, Livepeer, The Graph, Stacks, Molecule, PartyDAO | unknown | unknown | unknown | — | [portfolio](https://www.compound.vc/portfolio2) |\n\nAI, AI-chip, robotics and bio-AI deals naming Smac: **none found.**\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Anagen/HairDAO (early 2025).** HairDAO pools hair-loss \"internet experts\" and gives them token incentives. Anagen is its science-based telehealth company. Compound framed it as crypto coordination solving cold-start data collection for new treatments ([src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/emergent-science-and-funding-coordination)). The thesis spans bio (Newsad) and crypto (Smac). The intro path and timing relative to other investors were not found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Shelby Newsad (Compound) | co-author of the Anagen investment post | 2025 | [src](https://www.compound.vc/writing/emergent-science-and-funding-coordination) |\n| Michael Dempsey (Compound) | MP; thanked as a reviewer; Smac links his work | — | [src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/from-backrooms-to-builder-codes) |\n| krane (asula labs) | co-author of \"From Backrooms to Builder Codes\" | 2025-09 | [src](https://0xsmac.substack.com/p/from-backrooms-to-builder-codes) |\n| Ben Roy, Natalie Mullins (Liquid Culture) | \"close friends of the pod\" | 2024-06 | [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/f97134cb) |\n| Jonathan Moore (TCG Crypto) | co-guest on Liquid Culture | 2024-06 | [src](https://share.transistor.fm/s/f97134cb) |\n| Omnia (Kinetiq) | Smac Talk guest; Kinetiq is in Compound's portfolio | 2026-03 | [Apple](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/id1794010439); [portfolio](https://www.compound.vc/portfolio2) |\n| Felipe (Theia Research); Matti and Luffi (Zee Prime Capital) | Smac Talk guests | 2025-01/02 | [Apple](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/id1794010439) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Real identity, prior career, and join and promotion dates at Compound.\n- What \"Local Compute Co.\" (crypto section) is, and whether Smac owns it. This is the only possible crypto-x-compute link to the owner's thesis.\n- Whether Smac sourced Kinetiq or The Clearing Company.\n- The Anagen round size and co-investors.\n- The Launchy \"Chain Venturer\" interview (404) and the Decypher transcript.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.compound.vc/team/smac ; https://www.compound.vc/about ; https://www.compound.vc/portfolio2 ; https://www.compound.vc/writing\n- https://www.compound.vc/writing/emergent-science-and-funding-coordination\n- https://www.compound.vc/writing/compound-annual-letter-2023\n- https://0xsmac.substack.com/archive and the posts linked above\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/id1794010439 ; https://podstatus.com/podcasts/smac-talk-305899\n- https://share.transistor.fm/s/f97134cb ; https://www.buzzsprout.com/2380082/episodes/16647390-how-to-build-an-edge-as-a-crypto-trader-ft-0xsmac\n- https://www.theblock.co/news/deals/2025-08-27-former-polymarket-exec-raises-15-million-from-coinbase-and-usv-for-rival-prediction-platform-368519\n- Not reviewed (404): https://launchy.beehiiv.com/p/chain-venturer-0xsmac-compound-vc\n", "person:sofia-dolfe": "\n# Sofia Dolfe\n\n## One-line take\nAn Index investor who moved from London to San Francisco. Her AI record is enterprise AI apps: she co-led Decagon's $250M Series D, led Ando's seed (agent-native messaging), was in Cradle (AI protein design) from its seed, and is tagged on LiveKit. She is worth knowing as a route into Index for AI-native automation, not for labs or compute. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/sofia-dolfe/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** She judges founders on how they operate, from live sessions and dinners rather than pitch decks.\n  - She was won over by Decagon's Jesse Zhang running a working session on live cases instead of pitching [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/investing-in-decagon-rigor-speed-and-a-new-concierge-model-for-customer-experience/).\n  - She met Ando's Sara Du at a founder dinner Index hosted [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/).\n  - She works on both sides of Index: seed (Ando, and Cradle from weeks after incorporation) and growth (Decagon D).\n- **What constrains her:**\n  - On the AI deals that matter most she was not first. Index entered Decagon at the Series D, after A*'s seed, Accel's A and BCV's B.\n  - Her named portfolio is mostly consumer, gaming and fintech: Dream Games, Bigger Games, RevenueCat, Atlar, Printify, Rohlik [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/sofia-dolfe/).\n  - She has no named pre-training, lab or AI-chip deals.\n- **What she'd never do:** No sourced statement found. Her posts suggest she won't back an AI product that bolts an assistant onto an existing workflow. On Ando she wrote that doing so \"adds another cook into an already crowded kitchen\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Decagon first contact (Index came late):** She met CEO Jesse Zhang in person in summer 2025 in Decagon's SF office: \"I first met Jesse Zhang in person last summer\". Zhang came with sales leaders and live cases \"Instead of the usual pitch deck\". The D was announced 2026-01-28, so about six months from that meeting to a co-led $250M round. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/investing-in-decagon-rigor-speed-and-a-new-concierge-model-for-customer-experience/)\n- **Decagon timing context:**\n  - Seed: A* backed the team before there was a product idea. Hartz had been an angel in Ashwin Sreenivas's earlier company, Helia [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/).\n  - Series A: Accel, 2024-06 [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-series-a-in-decagon-bringing-human-like-ai-to-enterprise-customer-operations).\n  - Series B: BCV led $65M, 2024-10 [src](https://cooley.com/news/coverage/2024/2024-10-15-decagon-raises-65-million-series-b).\n  - Series D: Index co-led with Coatue, which says it led \"alongside our friends at Index\" [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/partnering-with-decagon-a-new-customer-experience).\n\n  Index was a growth entrant, about 19 months after Accel's A.\n- **What she underwrote at Decagon:** The founders' \"clock speed\". She also noted they \"didn't start with a predetermined product idea\", interviewing hundreds of operators instead. This matches A*'s account of the seed. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/investing-in-decagon-rigor-speed-and-a-new-concierge-model-for-customer-experience/)\n- **Ando origin:** She sat next to Sara Du at an Index-hosted SF founder dinner in summer 2025. Sahir Azam met Du separately, and after one coffee \"we knew we wanted to invest\". Index led the seed (post dated 2026-09-24). Index's own dinners are a sourcing channel for her. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/)\n- **Ando thesis, an agent-native view:** AI-native tools optimise for agents and treat the human interface \"as an afterthought\". She bets that the messaging layer itself has to be rebuilt with agents as first-class participants. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/)\n- **Cradle, in first:** Index met Stef van Grieken in November 2021, \"just weeks after they'd incorporated\". It closed the seed by the end of that month, led the Series A the next year, and joined IVP's $73M Series B in 2024-11. The post is co-signed by Dolfe and Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/catching-up-with-cradles-co-founder-stef-van-grieke/) Cradle's B announcement thanks \"Carlos & Sofia at Index Ventures\". [src](https://www.cradle.bio/blog/series-b)\n- **Gaming thesis (non-AI, explains her prior):** \"Games aren't just great products, they're great businesses.\" (2023-04-05) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/game-on-short-term-slowdown-long-term-play/) She also wrote Index's Bigger Games Series A post (2025-06-16), tagged with Stephane Kurgan. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/fun-is-a-feeling-building-emotion-engines-for-the-next-wave-of-mobile-gaming/)\n- **Earlier remit:** Her 2019 Forbes profile places her in London, covering the Nordics and pan-European consumer (PropTech, HealthTech, FemTech). Her move into US enterprise AI is recent. [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sofia-dolfe/)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **Relevant:**\n  - Decagon is AI-native automation (customer service), the Serval end of THESIS.md, but she came in at the Series D as a growth co-lead.\n  - Ando is an agent-native seed she personally sourced and led. It is the clearest early-stage AI signal in her record.\n  - Cradle (AI protein design) shows she will back a technical AI team weeks after incorporation, though bio is outside the core lens.\n- **Not relevant:** No pre-training, new-lab, chip or compute deals. Most of her board list is consumer, gaming and fintech.\n- **Use:** A warm route into Index SF for AI-native automation companies. She is a possible co-investor or follow-on source for application-layer seeds. She is not a decision maker for frontier-lab seeds. Index's frontier-lab posts, linked from her own Decagon post, carry other partners' bylines. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/investing-in-decagon-rigor-speed-and-a-new-concierge-model-for-customer-experience/)\n\n## Background\n- M.Sc., London School of Economics [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/sofia-dolfe/)\n- Worked at the European Union Delegation to the OECD, and in commercial due diligence at Parthenon-EY (London, Paris) [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sofia-dolfe/)\n- Investor, EQT Ventures, Stockholm [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/sofia-dolfe/)\n- Joined Index in April 2018 (London), covering Nordic and European consumer [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sofia-dolfe/); now based in San Francisco [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/sofia-dolfe/)\n- Forbes 30 Under 30 Europe, Finance, 2019 [src](https://www.forbes.com/profile/sofia-dolfe/); Forbes Midas Brink List 2025 [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/sofia-dolfe/)\n- Former soccer player [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/sofia-dolfe/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/sofia-dolfe/ | Index bio; Perspectives listing at /perspectives/team-member/sofia-dolfe/ (5 pages) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes on Index Perspectives |\n| X | https://twitter.com/sofiadolfe | Linked from Index bio; couldn't fetch (402), follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/sofia-dolfe-53556954 | Linked from Index bio |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. The web-search budget for this run was used up, so I could only fetch known URLs and didn't search for podcasts or talks. See Open questions.\n\n## How they think\n- **Founder over idea:** She praises Decagon's founders for starting without a fixed product idea and doing hundreds of operator interviews. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/investing-in-decagon-rigor-speed-and-a-new-concierge-model-for-customer-experience/)\n- **Agent-native rather than AI bolted on:** For Ando, the human interface still decides who wins: \"something people want to open every day\". [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/)\n- **Large incumbent spend as the market proof:** She cites Salesforce Service Cloud revenue for Decagon and Microsoft Teams revenue for Ando.\n- **Gaming:** She sees original titles from capital-efficient mobile studios as durable, high-margin businesses. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/game-on-short-term-slowdown-long-term-play/)\n- **Calls:** Cradle from weeks after incorporation through the IVP-led B looks right so far. It's too early to judge Decagon or Ando.\n\n## Read these first\n- \"Investing in Decagon: Rigor, Speed, and a New Concierge Model for Customer Experience\" (2026-01-28): how she diligences a growth AI-apps deal. [link](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/investing-in-decagon-rigor-speed-and-a-new-concierge-model-for-customer-experience/)\n- \"Ando: Building the Interface Layer for Human and Agent Teams\" (2026-09-24): her own seed sourcing and her agent-native thesis. [link](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/)\n- \"Catching up with Cradle's co-founder Stef van Grieken\" (2024-11-26): timing from seed to B. [link](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/catching-up-with-cradles-co-founder-stef-van-grieke/)\n- \"Game on: short-term slowdown, long-term play\" (2023-04-05): her gaming prior. [link](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/game-on-short-term-slowdown-long-term-play/)\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X couldn't be fetched in this run.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Decagon | Series D, $250M | 2026-01-28 | Co-lead (Index with Coatue). Dolfe byline; Martin Mignot tagged and on the company page | Coatue (co-lead); prior investors | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/investing-in-decagon-rigor-speed-and-a-new-concierge-model-for-customer-experience/), [Coatue](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/partnering-with-decagon-a-new-customer-experience) |\n| Ando | Seed (size not disclosed) | 2026-09-24 | Yes (Index led). Dolfe byline; Sahir Azam tagged | Not named | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/) |\n| LiveKit | Series C (size not disclosed) | 2026-01-22 | Index led, but Sahir Azam led and took the board seat; Dolfe and Eryk Dobrushkin tagged | Not named | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/) |\n| Cradle | Seed | 2021-11 | Index closed the seed; lead status not stated | Not named | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/catching-up-with-cradles-co-founder-stef-van-grieke/) |\n| Cradle | Series A | Date unconfirmed. The Index post says \"the next year\" after the 2021 seed; an earlier repo source says $24M, 2023-11 | Yes (Index led) | Not named | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/catching-up-with-cradles-co-founder-stef-van-grieke/) |\n| Cradle | Series B, $73M | 2024-11-26 | No (IVP led) | IVP; Kindred Capital | [Cradle](https://www.cradle.bio/blog/series-b) |\n| Bigger Games (non-AI) | Series A, $25M | 2025-06-16 | Dolfe byline; Index's role in the A not stated | Stephane Kurgan tagged | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/fun-is-a-feeling-building-emotion-engines-for-the-next-wave-of-mobile-gaming/) |\n| Dream Games, RevenueCat, Atlar, Printify, Common Room, Rohlik, KRY/LIVI, Otrium, Matera, Vinyl Equity, BIT ODD (mostly non-AI) | not stated | | | | [Index bio](https://www.indexventures.com/team/sofia-dolfe/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Decagon:** First in-person meeting in summer 2025 at Decagon's SF office. Zhang brought two senior sales leaders and live cases instead of a pitch deck. Dolfe wrote the post when Index co-led the $250M D with Coatue. Mignot is the second Index name, on the post and on the company page. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/investing-in-decagon-rigor-speed-and-a-new-concierge-model-for-customer-experience/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/decagon/)\n- **Ando:** Seated next to founder Sara Du at an Index founder dinner in SF in summer 2025. Sahir Azam met Du independently. After a joint coffee they decided to invest, and Index led the seed (2026-09). Du dropped out of Harvard at 19 to found Alloy. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/)\n- **Cradle:** Index met CEO Stef van Grieken in 2021-11, weeks after incorporation, and closed the seed within the month. The Dolfe and Gonzalez-Cadenas pairing carried through to the B. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/catching-up-with-cradles-co-founder-stef-van-grieke/)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Martin Mignot (Index) | Second Index name on Decagon (tagged on Dolfe's post; on company page) | 2026-01 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/decagon/) |\n| Sahir Azam (Index) | Co-sourced and co-signed the Ando seed; Azam led LiveKit C with Dolfe tagged | 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/) |\n| Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas (Index) | Co-author on Cradle; shared Index seat | 2021-11 | [src](https://www.cradle.bio/blog/series-b) |\n| Eryk Dobrushkin (Index) | Co-tagged on LiveKit C | 2026-01 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/) |\n| Stephane Kurgan (Index) | Co-tagged on Bigger Games A | 2025-06 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/fun-is-a-feeling-building-emotion-engines-for-the-next-wave-of-mobile-gaming/) |\n| Jesse Zhang, Ashwin Sreenivas (Decagon) | Portfolio founders | 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/investing-in-decagon-rigor-speed-and-a-new-concierge-model-for-customer-experience/) |\n| Sara Du (Ando) | Portfolio founder; met at Index dinner | 2025 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/) |\n| Stef van Grieken (Cradle) | Portfolio founder | 2021-11 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/catching-up-with-cradles-co-founder-stef-van-grieke/) |\n| Coatue | Co-lead on Decagon D | 2026-01 | [src](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/partnering-with-decagon-a-new-customer-experience) |\n| EQT Ventures | Former employer | before 2018-04 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/sofia-dolfe/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Her formal title and the date she was promoted to partner, if she has been. Index's bio gives no title.\n- When she moved from London to SF, and whether that move coincided with the shift to US enterprise AI.\n- Ando seed size and co-investors.\n- Who personally led Cradle's Series A. Another repo profile cites biopharma-reporter, but that URL now returns 410.\n- Whether Index looked at Decagon before the D, for example during the Series C, which this run didn't find.\n- Podcasts, talks and X posts. The search budget ran out in this run, so a follow-up sweep is needed.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/sofia-dolfe/\n- https://www.forbes.com/profile/sofia-dolfe/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/investing-in-decagon-rigor-speed-and-a-new-concierge-model-for-customer-experience/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/decagon/\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/partnering-with-decagon-a-new-customer-experience\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-series-a-in-decagon-bringing-human-like-ai-to-enterprise-customer-operations\n- https://cooley.com/news/coverage/2024/2024-10-15-decagon-raises-65-million-series-b\n- https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ando-building-the-interface-layer-for-human-and-agent-teams/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/ando/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/see-hear-speak-investing-in-livekit-and-the-future-of-realtime-applications/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/catching-up-with-cradles-co-founder-stef-van-grieke/\n- https://www.cradle.bio/blog/series-b\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/game-on-short-term-slowdown-long-term-play/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/fun-is-a-feeling-building-emotion-engines-for-the-next-wave-of-mobile-gaming/\n", "person:sol-bier": "\n# Sol Bier\n\n## One-line take\nHe controls a ~$1B adviser that mostly moves non-US money into US frontier names. In May 2026 he made his first visible lead and board seat, in Fractile, an AI inference-chip company. That makes him a person to call for co-lead capacity in big AI-hardware rounds [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/319107/PDF/319107.pdf) [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/officers).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He can fill a large single-name allocation fast, using offshore and strategic capital. 68% of the $967M RAUM is from non-US clients. Several vehicles are 100% non-US, with $1M–$2M minimums and one to 15 investors [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/319107/PDF/319107.pdf). Fund II was anchored by Hyundai and Kia [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2025052024/factorial-funds-launches-second-fund-with-200-million/). In 2024 he wrapped this in a research brand, with OpenAI co-founder Durk Kingma and ex-OpenAI researcher Matthias Plappert on the masthead.\n- **What constrains him:** The firm is one person. He is the sole owner of every advisory entity, and there are 6 employees, 3 of them in investment roles. The research bench has gone: Kingma left by 2025-02 and Plappert by 2025-06. About half of RAUM sits in SX-named vehicles, likely SpaceX, so the book is concentrated. The early book (2021–2023) was mostly late-stage SPVs and secondaries (Robinhood, Kraken, GitLab), not first checks.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** Run a classic diversified seed fund. Every vehicle is concentrated, and the SPVs carry deal-by-deal management fees (Athena Form D: \"customary management fees\").\n\n## Differential insight\n- He became a **Fractile director on 2026-05-15**, two days after the Series B announcement. Two earlier directors resigned the same day (Sam Harman and Patrick Schneider Sikorsky) [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/officers). Quote: \"Appointed on 15 May 2026\". No press story named any Factorial individual on the deal.\n- **Athena II likely funded his Fractile check (inference).** Athena II's Form D (2026-04-30) said the first sale was \"yet to occur\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2083721/000208372126000001/primary_doc.xml). StepStone bought $15.8M of it the next day [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1918642/000119312526376298/ea0303531-04_nportex.htm), and the Fractile B was announced 12 days later. The latest N-PORT (2026-06-30) still carries it at cost, so the Series C markup can't yet confirm it.\n- **Continuation-style liquidity early.** On 2025-12-10 he filed Factorial Frontier LP, with Jefferies as placement agent [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2099499/000101297525000890/primary_doc.xml). The same day, Lexington's evergreen fund bought Frontier and Fund II interests as \"secondary investments\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2008602/000094040026008787/LMQ0301LM123125.htm). That was about seven months after Fund II's first close.\n- **Retail origins.** The first vehicle (2021) went through the Assure SPV platform with a $5,000 minimum and 48 investors [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1853055/000185305521000001/primary_doc.xml).\n- **Personal consumer-AI check.** He was named personally in Arcade's Series A: \"Sol Bier of Factorial Funds participating\" [src](https://pulse2.com/arcade-25-million-raised-for-ai-physical-product-marketplace).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High as a co-investor, not as an LP.** He can co-lead $200M+ rounds in AI silicon (Fractile) and holds positions in Anthropic, xAI, Mistral, Thinking Machines and Physical Intelligence. He is a natural partner to call when filling a large frontier seed or Series A. He also competes for late allocation via SPVs. He is not an LP prospect: his capital is foreign strategic and fund-of-funds money routed into his own vehicles.\n\n## Background\n- Early founding engineering team at Cruise Automation (YC W14), before GM bought it in 2016. Holds 7 patents in AI, ML and robotics; BS in Physics from USC [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230925170526/http://factorialfunds.com/team). Aggregators also list him as a former venture partner at \"Synapse Capital Management\" (unverified).\n- ADV dates his control of the Factorial adviser to 01/2020. The firm says it was founded in 2021. SEC registration: CRD 319107, file 801-134037.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://factorialfunds.com/team | no bio on the 2026 site |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | none found under his name; firm account @factorialfunds | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/sol-bier-7b81b040/ | |\n| GitHub | none found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Searches for Sol Bier interviews and podcasts returned nothing relevant (2026-11-11).\n\n## How they think\n- The firm essays argued that inference compute would dominate training. The Sora piece projected about 720k H100s of inference demand; the Llama 3 piece said \"inference compute is incurred continuously\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240523113601/https://factorialfunds.com/blog/thoughts-on-llama-3). Two years later he co-led Fractile, an inference chip company.\n- On DeepSeek: compute demand does not fall with efficiency, and \"whoever has more compute wins\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250324064009/https://factorialfunds.com/blog/20-things-you-should-know-about-deepseek). None of these essays carry his byline.\n\n## Read these first\n- Form ADV (2026-03-31): the whole business in numbers [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/319107/PDF/319107.pdf)\n- Sora essay (2024-03) and Llama 3 essay (2024-04): the firm's inference thesis\n- Fractile officers list on Companies House\n\n## Social engagement\nNo personal social presence found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Fractile | Series B ($220M) + board | 2026-05-13 | co-lead | Accel, Founders Fund, Conviction, Gigascale, Felicis, 8VC | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=754866) [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/officers) |\n| Arcade (Naficy) | Series A ($25M) | 2025-03-28 | no | Canaan, Forerunner (leads) | [src](https://pulse2.com/arcade-25-million-raised-for-ai-physical-product-marketplace) |\n| Firm-level book (Anthropic, xAI, SpaceX, Mistral, Perplexity, Sakana, Thinking Machines, Physical Intelligence, Ayar Labs, Bot Co, Harbor Compute) | see firms/factorial-funds.md | | | | |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Fractile:** Fractile is a UK inference-chip company. Factorial co-led its $220M B with Accel and Founders Fund, Bier joined the board, and Factorial returned in the ~$720M C at roughly $7B six months later [src](https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/ai-chip-company-fractile-reveals-investors-behind-720m-series-c/). The intro path is unknown.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Factorial Funds | Founding Partner, sole owner | 2020-01 | [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/319107/PDF/319107.pdf) |\n| Fractile (Walter Goodwin) | board director | 2026-05-15 | [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/officers) |\n| Durk Kingma, Matthias Plappert, Rich Miner | former executive partners and research bench | 2023–2025 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20240224060523/https://factorialfunds.com/team) |\n| Hyundai Motor Company / Kia | Fund II anchor LPs | 2025 | [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2025052024/factorial-funds-launches-second-fund-with-200-million/) |\n| StepStone, Lexington | fund-of-funds buyers of his vehicles | 2025-12 / 2026-05 | N-PORTs |\n| Cruise (Kyle Vogt) | early engineering team | pre-2016 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230925170526/http://factorialfunds.com/team) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What do Athena I (July 2025), Athena III (September 2026) and the DCRD, DX, BF, DS and RR series hold?\n- Is The Bot Company (Kyle Vogt) allocation a Cruise-alumni relationship deal?\n- Who sourced Fractile: Bier himself, Khayrattee, or the newer partners Carmichael and Nelson?\n- Which non-US LPs make up the 68%?\n\n## Sources\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/319107/PDF/319107.pdf\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14143636/officers\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2083721/000208372126000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1918642/000119312526376298/ea0303531-04_nportex.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2099499/000101297525000890/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2008602/000094040026008787/LMQ0301LM123125.htm\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20230925170526/http://factorialfunds.com/team\n- https://pulse2.com/arcade-25-million-raised-for-ai-physical-product-marketplace\n", "person:sonali-singh": "\n# Sonali Singh\n\n> Pronoun note: the visible Sequoia bio is written in the first person. The page's own meta description uses \"she/her\" (\"where she partners with founders…\") [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/). This profile uses she/her.\n\n## One-line take\nA new Sequoia growth partner who is already listed on four of the firm's hottest 2025–26 AI deals (Serval, Parallel, Chai, TypeSafe), always next to a senior partner. Watch her to see where Sequoia's growth team is placing AI bets [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/).\n\n## Crux\nA former Morgan Stanley banker who was new to VC as of Jan 2026 (\"I recently entered the world of venture capital\") [src](https://singhso.substack.com/p/holding-water-an-intro-to-things), now working as the second or third name next to senior Sequoia growth partners (Pat Grady, Sonya Huang, Anas Biad, Andrew Reed) on fast-moving AI rounds [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/). Her advantage is access and the firm's platform, not her own sourcing. No source shows her originating or leading a deal, and every listed company is a growth-stage or late-priced round ($1B to $7.5B valuations) [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/serval/), [src](https://pulse2.com/typesafe-raises-870-million-series-a-at-7-5-billion-valuation/). Her constraint: no check authority or own check size is public, and her writing is joint work with Huang and Grady, so her personal view is hard to separate from theirs. What she would not do, as far as the sources show: nothing found. She has stated no exclusions.\n\n## Differential insight\n(Added 2026-11-09 from new primary-source research. Mirrored in `data/rows/sonali-singh-insights.csv`.)\n- **Her X handle is confirmed.** The Aug 2026 \"Own Your Intelligence: A How-To Guide\" X article has the byline \"By @sonyatweetybird, @gradypb, and @w1tness1ngh\" [src](https://x.com/sonyatweetybird/status/2089390902162895350). The account @w1tness1ngh is named \"Sonali Singh\", with the bio \"i work @sequoia\" and location San Francisco. It had 342 followers, and the account was created 2024-11-15 (fetched 2026-11-09 via the fxtwitter API) [src](https://api.fxtwitter.com/w1tness1ngh). The timeline itself could not be read because X's syndication endpoint was rate-limited.\n- **Co-authored \"own the weights\" thesis.** The article she co-wrote argues: \"Not your weights, not your product.\" [src](https://x.com/sonyatweetybird/status/2089390902162895350) It says open-weight models (Kimi K3, GLM 5.2) now start \"close to the frontier\" and that the post-training stack (Fireworks, Mercor) has matured. It also says owning intelligence is \"not a mandate nor a recommendation to get off the labs.\" This is a bet on post-training and data vendors, and on app companies building model teams, rather than on frontier labs alone.\n- **A small model team is enough.** The same article holds up Harvey as an example: \"Small, de novo teams go very far here\". It cites Harvey doing research \"with a team of just seven people\" [src](https://x.com/sonyatweetybird/status/2089390902162895350). The article also says buyers now break ties on published research and benchmarks.\n- **TypeSafe: usage pull, not price discipline.** Sequoia took a Series A behind a16z at a reported $7.5B, about three weeks after the $40M DCVC seed. The memo she co-wrote justifies this with \"the type of developer pull we haven't seen since GPT-3\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/typesafe-the-jevolution/), [src](https://dealroom.co/news/151032-typesafe-exits-stealth-with-40m-seed-to-build-ai-for-software-not-people/). Timing gap: DCVC was first at seed, and Sequoia came in as a follower within weeks at a much higher price.\n- **Bet on task selection over scale.** The TypeSafe memo backs founder Diogo Almeida, who worked on InstructGPT and RLHF at OpenAI. It endorses his \"bitterest lesson\" that \"picking the right task to solve matters even more than scale\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/typesafe-the-jevolution/). This is a frontier-lab spinout thesis that does not depend on having the most compute.\n- **Diligence on AI-bio: talent mix first.** On the Chai episode, her first question was about hiring, saying \"you need to assemble a kind of quadrilingual group of people\", \"an Avengers squad\" of chemists, biologists and AI people [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/chai-discoverys-bitter-lesson-drug-design-is-another-scaling-problem).\n- **A founder corrected her framing on air.** She called biology \"one of those inherently not so verifiable domains\". Joshua Meier replied that he thinks it is \"one of the more verifiable domains\", because wet-lab readouts are objective, just slower [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/chai-discoverys-bitter-lesson-drug-design-is-another-scaling-problem). This is useful for calibration: she asks the generalist investor's question and lets the technical founder correct it. She also said Sequoia knows \"very little about biology\", in the phrase \"people like us who know very little about biology\" (same source).\n- **Her own views, away from Sequoia (Substack, Jan 2026).** She is skeptical of hype like \"data centers in space\". She found space mining the idea with the most traction among deep-tech people, and argued it is \"just a math problem\" as launch costs fall. She named Starcloud, Varda, Interlune and AstroForge [src](https://singhso.substack.com/p/schadenfreude-and-fremdschamen-things). She also cited Sarah Guo (\"optimal amount of cringe is not zero\") and Andrew Reed on posting in public (same source).\n- Not found: a personal check size, deals passed on, mistakes admitted, or how any of her deals started (intro paths). Her own X timeline could not be read.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** She works at growth stage: every deal she is on is Series A at $7.5B or later [src](https://pulse2.com/typesafe-raises-870-million-series-a-at-7-5-billion-valuation/). She is never the seed or first check, so she won't be in rounds next to my ~$20M seed checks. She matters as a signal and a connector. She sits on Serval, which is my named example of AI-native automation [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/serval/), and on TypeSafe, a spinout from OpenAI research [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/typesafe-the-jevolution/). She also co-wrote Sequoia's thesis that app companies will own post-trained open weights [src](https://x.com/sonyatweetybird/status/2089390902162895350). That points toward post-training infrastructure (Fireworks, Mercor) and toward app companies building research teams of about seven people, which is relevant to pre-training and post-training talent. The deals that matter to me: Serval (high), TypeSafe (high), Chai Discovery (medium; scaling-law AI-bio), Parallel (medium). The higher-value people to follow are the seed-stage investors who were first into these companies: DCVC for TypeSafe, and Thrive's Miles Grimshaw for Chai.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in a small town in Connecticut. Her parents, both PhDs, emigrated from India on two scholarships [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/).\n- College: studied finance, creative writing and math, and found she liked teaching. The Sequoia page does not name the school [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/). A third-party aggregator (RocketReach) reportedly lists Wharton, Bocconi and Choate, but the page returned 403, so this is (unverified) and left out of the rows.\n- Started her career at Morgan Stanley. Her role, group and dates are not given [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/).\n- Moved to Silicon Valley \"to be in the epicenter of technology\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/).\n- Sequoia Capital, partner on the growth team [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/) (meta description). **No source gives her join date.** Inferred range, labeled as such: her earliest listed company, Serval, is marked \"Partnered 2025\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/serval/), and in Jan 2026 she wrote that she had \"recently entered the world of venture capital\" [src](https://singhso.substack.com/p/holding-water-an-intro-to-things). That points to roughly 2025 (unverified).\n- Personal interests listed by Sequoia: movies, wine, jazz, Frank O'Hara, physics [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/ | Sequoia profile, checked 2026-11-09 [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/) |\n| Substack / blog | https://singhso.substack.com | \"Sonali's Substack\" (\"Things I've Learned Lately\"). Two posts, 2026-01-25 and 2026-01-27, none since (checked 2026-11-09) [src](https://singhso.substack.com/api/v1/archive?sort=new&limit=20). Linked to her by roundfunded [src](https://www.roundfunded.com/en/investor/sonali-singh). The content matches her Sequoia bio (SF, new to VC, physics, wine) |\n| X | https://x.com/w1tness1ngh | **Confirmed 2026-11-09**: byline of the Aug 2026 Sequoia X article [src](https://x.com/sonyatweetybird/status/2089390902162895350). Display name \"Sonali Singh\", bio \"i work @sequoia\", 342 followers, 219 posts, created 2024-11-15 (as of 2026-11-09) [src](https://api.fxtwitter.com/w1tness1ngh). First attributed by ai4s.fyi [src](http://ai4s.fyi/people/sonali-singh) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/sonalisingh0/ | Linked from her Sequoia page [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/). Not fetched (login wall, HTTP 999) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Training Data (Sequoia), as a guest co-host for one episode | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/chai-discoverys-bitter-lesson-drug-design-is-another-scaling-problem) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-08 (a third-party listing gives 2026-08-04 [src](https://www.teahose.com/podcast/Training%20Data/Chai%20Discovery's%20Bitter%20Lesson_%20Drug%20Design%20Is%20Another%20Scaling%20Problem). The Sequoia page HTML carries Nov 2026 timestamps that look like build dates, so the release date is unconfirmed). Training Data, \"Chai Discovery's Bitter Lesson: Drug Design Is Another Scaling Problem\", co-hosted with Pat Grady. Guests: Joshua Meier and Matthew McPartlon [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/chai-discoverys-bitter-lesson-drug-design-is-another-scaling-problem). Her questions covered:\n  - hiring a \"quadrilingual\" team of chemists, biologists and AI people\n  - how Chai verifies model progress when biology is \"not so verifiable\"\n  - faster work on existing targets versus opening up undruggable ones\n  - competition now that AI protein design is crowded\n  [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/chai-discoverys-bitter-lesson-drug-design-is-another-scaling-problem). (The teahose listing names a \"Josh Birkley\", which looks like an error; Sequoia's transcript says Joshua Meier.)\n- No other podcasts, talks or panels found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Growth-stage AI.** She is on the growth team [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/), and three of her four listed companies are tagged \"Growth\" by Sequoia. TypeSafe is tagged \"Early\" even though Sequoia came in at an $870M Series A [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/), [src](https://pulse2.com/typesafe-raises-870-million-series-a-at-7-5-billion-valuation/).\n- **Founder filter.** She looks for \"ambitious, high-agency, zeroth-principle thinkers\" and ties this to her immigrant parents. She is interested in both moonshots and businesses that change daily life [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/).\n- **Cheap intelligence and Jevons paradox (TypeSafe piece, with Sonya Huang).** When classification becomes nearly free, teams stop rationing it and many new use cases open up. The piece calls Jev a fast \"System 1\" model next to \"System 2\" LLMs, and calls it \"Prod, not God\" infrastructure for runtime decisions [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/typesafe-the-jevolution/).\n- **Own your model.** In an X thread dated 2026-08-17, co-written with Sonya Huang and Pat Grady and posted from Huang's account, they argued that app companies should stop defaulting to frontier APIs. They named cost, speed, proprietary data and control as reasons to own the model. Known only from secondary coverage [src](https://aiinsiders.net/article/sequoia-tells-founders-rent-frontier-ai-but-own-where-it).\n- **Drug design as an engineering and scaling problem.** She framed this through the Chai episode. These are mostly the founders' views, which she drew out in her questions [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/chai-discoverys-bitter-lesson-drug-design-is-another-scaling-problem).\n- **Personal writing.** On her Substack she wants a system for learning in an age of information overload, worries about creeping anti-intellectualism, and writes about language, infinity (Hilbert's hotel) and \"data centers in space\" hype [src](https://singhso.substack.com/p/holding-water-an-intro-to-things), [src](https://singhso.substack.com/p/schadenfreude-and-fremdschamen-things).\n- Calls right or wrong: too early to judge. Every position dates from late 2025 or 2026.\n\n## Read these first\n1. \"Partnering with TypeSafe: The Jevolution\" (with Sonya Huang, 2026-10-10). Her one Sequoia investment memo, about cheap classification models and Jevons paradox [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/typesafe-the-jevolution/).\n2. Training Data: Chai Discovery episode (with Pat Grady). Shows the questions she asks a frontier AI-bio company [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/chai-discoverys-bitter-lesson-drug-design-is-another-scaling-problem).\n2b. \"Own Your Intelligence: A How-To Guide\" (X article, with Sonya Huang and Pat Grady, 2026-08-17). Sequoia's case for app companies owning post-trained open weights [src](https://x.com/sonyatweetybird/status/2089390902162895350).\n3. \"Holding Water: An Intro to Things I've Learned Lately\" (2026-01-25). Her own account of being new to VC and how she plans to learn [src](https://singhso.substack.com/p/holding-water-an-intro-to-things).\n4. Sequoia bio. Her founder filter and background in her own words [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/).\n\n## Social engagement\n(Update 2026-11-09) The X account @w1tness1ngh is confirmed as hers (see Differential insight), but its posts could not be read (rate-limited). Before this update: nothing found on her own accounts. X and LinkedIn could not be checked. The one known X activity is the Aug 2026 co-written thread posted from Sonya Huang's account [src](https://aiinsiders.net/article/sequoia-tells-founders-rent-frontier-ai-but-own-where-it). Her Substack's intro post had about 25 likes at fetch time [src](https://singhso.substack.com/p/holding-water-an-intro-to-things), and the second post had 28 reactions [src](https://singhso.substack.com/api/v1/archive?sort=new&limit=20) (checked 2026-11-09).\n\n## Portfolio\nSequoia's company pages list her as a partner on each company below. No round announcement names her personally, so the round facts are Sequoia's, not hers.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Serval | Series B, $75M at $1B | 2025-12-11 | Sequoia led (Anas Biad quoted) | Redpoint, Meritech, First Round, General Catalyst, Evantic | [round](https://www.eznewswire.com/newsroom/serval-series-b-funding-75m-sequoia-1b-valuation); [her link](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/serval/) |\n| Serval | Series C, $150M at $3B | 2026-10-29 | Sequoia led | Elad Gil, Evantic, First Round, General Catalyst, Redpoint; new: Conviction, Headline | [round](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/serval-raises-150m-series-c/); [her link](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/serval/) |\n| Parallel Web Systems | Series B, $100M at $2B | 2026-04-29 | Sequoia led | Kleiner Perkins, Index Ventures, Khosla, First Round, Spark, Terrain | [round](https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/29/parallel-web-systems-hits-2b-valuation-five-months-after-its-last-big-raise/); [her link](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/parallel-web-systems/) |\n| Chai Discovery | Series C, $400M at $3.8B | 2026-07-14 | No. Index Ventures led; Sequoia participated alongside Kleiner Perkins and Dimension | Index, Kleiner Perkins, Dimension, Bain Capital Ventures, Battery, Baillie Gifford, BDT & MSD, Sapphire, Avra; existing: Thrive Capital, OpenAI, Oak HC/FT, Menlo, General Catalyst, Glade Brook, Avenir, Lachy Groom, Yosemite | [round](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design); [her link](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/chai-discovery/) |\n| TypeSafe AI | Series A, $870M at $7.5B | 2026-10 (reported Oct 9–11; Sequoia post dated 2026-10-10) | No. a16z led (\"behind our friends at a16z\") | a16z, DCVC, angels | [round](https://pulse2.com/typesafe-raises-870-million-series-a-at-7-5-billion-valuation/); [her memo](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/typesafe-the-jevolution/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **TypeSafe AI.** Sequoia joined the Series A behind a16z, about three weeks after TypeSafe came out of stealth with a $40M seed led by DCVC [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/typesafe-the-jevolution/), [src](https://dealroom.co/news/151032-typesafe-exits-stealth-with-40m-seed-to-build-ai-for-software-not-people/). The memo she co-wrote cites the pull from developers. Jev became the fastest-adopted model in Vercel AI Gateway history within 24 hours of its 2026-09-15 launch, and Sequoia portfolio companies were \"racing\" to put it into production. The memo says developer pull like this had not been seen \"since GPT-3\" [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/typesafe-the-jevolution/). Intro path: nothing found.\n- **Chai Discovery, and how it links to Thrive.** Thrive Capital was in at the seed (~$30M, 2024-09-09, with OpenAI and Dimension), which this repo records under Thrive's Miles Grimshaw [src](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement). Sequoia came in about two years later at the 2026-07-14 Series C, where Thrive and OpenAI took part as existing investors [src](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design). Sequoia's Chai partners are Pat Grady, Sonali Singh and James Flynn [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/chai-discovery/). Pat Grady is the Sequoia person quoted in the release [src](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design). So Thrive was the early backer and Sequoia the later growth entrant, and the two now share Chai's cap table.\n- **Parallel Web Systems.** Sequoia led the Series B five months after the Kleiner/Index Series A [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/29/parallel-web-systems-hits-2b-valuation-five-months-after-its-last-big-raise/). Andrew Reed took the board seat [src](https://www.the-sourcecode.com/startups/parag-agrawal-parallel-web-systems-100m-series-b-sequoia-2bn-valuation). Her role beyond being listed as a partner: nothing found.\n- **Serval.** Sequoia led back-to-back rounds, the Series B (Dec 2025) and the Series C (Oct 2026) [src](https://www.eznewswire.com/newsroom/serval-series-b-funding-75m-sequoia-1b-valuation), [src](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/serval-raises-150m-series-c/). Anas Biad is the public lead partner. He compared the thesis to Sequoia's ServiceNow bet [src](https://fourweekmba.com/ai-sequoia-leads-150m-for-servicenow-rival-serval-at-3b/). Sequoia's own \"Partnering with Serval\" post is by Biad, Pat Grady and Brian Halligan, not Singh [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/serval/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sequoia Capital | Partner, growth team | ~2025 (inferred, unverified) | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/) |\n| Morgan Stanley | Started her career there (role and dates unknown) | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/) |\n| Pat Grady (Sequoia) | Co-partner on Chai Discovery; co-host of the Chai Training Data episode; co-author of the Aug 2026 X thread | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/chai-discovery/), [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/chai-discoverys-bitter-lesson-drug-design-is-another-scaling-problem) |\n| Sonya Huang (Sequoia) | Co-partner on TypeSafe; co-author of \"The Jevolution\"; co-author of the Aug 2026 X thread | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/typesafe-ai/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/typesafe-the-jevolution/) |\n| James Flynn (Sequoia) | Co-partner on Chai Discovery | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/chai-discovery/) |\n| Anas Biad (Sequoia) | Co-partner on Serval (Biad is the public lead) | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/serval/) |\n| Brian Halligan (Sequoia) | Co-partner on Serval | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/serval/) |\n| Andrew Reed (Sequoia) | Co-partner on Parallel (Reed holds the board seat) | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/parallel-web-systems/) |\n| Abhishek Malani (Sequoia) | Co-partner on Parallel | 2026 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/parallel-web-systems/) |\n| Thrive Capital | Co-investor on Chai Discovery (Thrive from seed; Sequoia from Series C) | 2026-07 | [src](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design) |\n| Andreessen Horowitz | Co-investor on TypeSafe (a16z led; Sequoia followed) | 2026-10 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/typesafe-the-jevolution/) |\n| Index Ventures, Kleiner Perkins | Co-investors on both Chai (Series C) and Parallel (Series B) | 2026 | [src](https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/29/parallel-web-systems-hits-2b-valuation-five-months-after-its-last-big-raise/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When exactly did she join Sequoia, and what did she do between Morgan Stanley and Sequoia? Check her LinkedIn (sonalisingh0).\n- Education: confirm the reported Wharton/Bocconi/Choate details from a primary source.\n- ~~Is x.com/w1tness1ngh actually her account?~~ Confirmed 2026-11-09 via the article byline. Next: read her timeline (replies to and amplification of David Cahn, Andrew Reed and the Chai founders) once X is not rate-limited.\n- The X account was created 2024-11-15 and its bio says she works at Sequoia, yet in Jan 2026 she wrote that she had \"recently entered\" VC. Did she join Sequoia in late 2024 or 2025?\n- What is her day-to-day role on each deal (sourcing vs. diligence support)? She is never the named lead.\n- Why does Sequoia tag TypeSafe \"Early\" when it entered at a $7.5B Series A? Is she also doing early-stage work?\n- Release date of the Chai Training Data episode (Aug 2026 vs. later).\n- Thrive overlap: does Thrive's Miles Grimshaw sit on Chai's board alongside any Sequoia seat? Did Thrive take part in Parallel, Serval or TypeSafe?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/sonali-singh/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/serval/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/chai-discovery/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/parallel-web-systems/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/typesafe-ai/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/typesafe-the-jevolution/\n- https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/chai-discoverys-bitter-lesson-drug-design-is-another-scaling-problem\n- https://www.teahose.com/podcast/Training%20Data/Chai%20Discovery's%20Bitter%20Lesson_%20Drug%20Design%20Is%20Another%20Scaling%20Problem\n- https://singhso.substack.com/p/holding-water-an-intro-to-things\n- https://singhso.substack.com/p/schadenfreude-and-fremdschamen-things\n- https://singhso.substack.com/api/v1/archive?sort=new&limit=20\n- https://www.roundfunded.com/en/investor/sonali-singh\n- http://ai4s.fyi/people/sonali-singh\n- https://aiinsiders.net/article/sequoia-tells-founders-rent-frontier-ai-but-own-where-it\n- https://www.eznewswire.com/newsroom/serval-series-b-funding-75m-sequoia-1b-valuation\n- https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/serval-raises-150m-series-c/\n- https://fourweekmba.com/ai-sequoia-leads-150m-for-servicenow-rival-serval-at-3b/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/29/parallel-web-systems-hits-2b-valuation-five-months-after-its-last-big-raise/\n- https://www.the-sourcecode.com/startups/parag-agrawal-parallel-web-systems-100m-series-b-sequoia-2bn-valuation\n- https://biospace.com/press-releases/chai-discovery-announces-400m-series-c-to-advance-ai-driven-molecular-design\n- https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/series-c\n- https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement\n- https://pulse2.com/typesafe-raises-870-million-series-a-at-7-5-billion-valuation/\n- https://dealroom.co/news/151032-typesafe-exits-stealth-with-40m-seed-to-build-ai-for-software-not-people/\n- https://x.com/sonyatweetybird/status/2089390902162895350 (added 2026-11-09)\n- https://api.fxtwitter.com/w1tness1ngh (added 2026-11-09)\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bMMv0bZzONg (Sonya Huang's Own Your Intelligence talk; Singh not a speaker; added 2026-11-09)\n", "person:sonya-huang": "\n# Sonya Huang\n\n## One-line take\nShe co-writes (with Pat Grady) Sequoia's public AI theses, from \"Generative AI: A Creative New World\" in 2022 to \"2026: This is AGI.\" She co-hosts the Training Data podcast. She is the Sequoia partner on LangChain, Glean, fal, Etched, Zed, and the Ineffable Intelligence and Standard Intelligence lab bets ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/)). Her yearly essays are the clearest public record of how one top firm's AI view changed, and she says plainly where earlier calls were wrong.\n\n## Crux\nShe wins on access to research founders, not on price. She is Sequoia's public AI voice: the yearly essays, AI Ascent keynotes and the Training Data podcast. That platform doubles as a relationship channel with top researchers. David Silver was the first guest she emailed when the show started, about two years before Sequoia co-led his $1.1B seed ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/david-silver-and-lasse-espeholt-of-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-age-of-experience)). Her filter is \"hard to build\": she is openly hostile to thin wrappers and \"vibe revenue\" ([src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-05-09/sequoias-sonya-huang-on-ai-application-layer-winners-humanoid-robotics-timeline-and-enterprise-ai-battles/transcript)). Since mid-2026 she has argued that companies must own their own models (\"not your weights, your products\") ([src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-08-13/sonya-huang-on-ais-not-your-weights-not-your-product-moment-and-why-application-companies-are-building-their-own-labs/transcript)). The constraint is that she sits on a growth team at a mega-fund. Her deals are big, priced rounds she co-leads with other large firms (Lightspeed, Spark), and her public essays put her calls on record, so reversals are visible. Act o1 (Oct 2024) said to avoid models and infra. She has since co-led two labs and an inference-chip round.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Podcast as sourcing channel.** She says of Silver: \"the first person I called — emailed — to come on Training Data\". The show launched 2024-06; Sequoia co-led Ineffable's seed in 2026-04 ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/david-silver-and-lasse-espeholt-of-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-age-of-experience)).\n- **Sequoia backed an AlphaGo alum before Silver.** On the same episode she says: \"We backed Ioannis [Antonoglou], who was on your original AlphaGo team\" ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/david-silver-and-lasse-espeholt-of-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-age-of-experience)). Sequoia co-led Reflection AI's $25M seed with CRV ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956)). That makes Ineffable Sequoia's second RL-from-DeepMind bet, not its first. Whether Huang was the partner on Reflection is unconfirmed.\n- **How Ineffable was won.** Alfred Lin and Huang flew to London to meet Silver in person ([src](https://thenextweb.com/news/ineffable-intelligence-david-silver-sequoia-nvidia-5-billion)). Sequoia's own post calls it \"genuinely contrarian\" ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-era-of-experience)).\n- **Backs very young research founders at Series A.** Of Standard Intelligence: \"Both founders are wise beyond their years (21 and 20 respectively)\". The founders met at the Atlas Fellowship in 2022. Sequoia led the round with Spark Capital ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/standard-intelligence-training-general-intelligence-in-pixel-space/)).\n- **Ownership-of-intelligence thesis (Aug 2026).** \"I'm on the board of Fireworks.\" She argues app companies now need to own their models: \"you need to intentionally own it\" ([src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-08-13/sonya-huang-on-ais-not-your-weights-not-your-product-moment-and-why-application-companies-are-building-their-own-labs/transcript)). Her example of how small this can be is Harvey: \"their entire research team is seven people\" ([src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-08-13/sonya-huang-on-ais-not-your-weights-not-your-product-moment-and-why-application-companies-are-building-their-own-labs/transcript)). This is the clearest stated update from Act o1's \"avoid models\" stance. She frames it as app companies building labs, not as a reversal.\n- **Says Sequoia backs research labs (Jan 2026).** On TBPN, a speaker inferred to be Huang (the transcript is unlabelled) says \"we are absolutely backing research labs\" and names a \"Recursive Intelligence\" chip-design company. That is likely Ricursive Intelligence, whose $35M seed (Dec 2025) Sequoia led with partner Stephanie Zhan ([src](https://convergedigest.com/ricursive-intelligence-aims-to-accelerate-semiconductor-design/), [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-01-16/sequoias-pat-grady-and-sonya-huang-declare-agi-has-arrived-and-issue-a-call-to-arms-for-founders/transcript)). Low confidence on who is speaking.\n- **Rules of thumb (May 2025, TBPN):** \"companies are raising on like what I'd call Vibe revenue right now\". \"There is there's too much venture capital already.\" She values companies \"selling into a services replacement, not a tools replacement\" ([src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-05-09/sequoias-sonya-huang-on-ai-application-layer-winners-humanoid-robotics-timeline-and-enterprise-ai-battles/transcript)).\n- **Admitted flip on robotics:** \"I was personally like a robot bear for the longest time\" ([src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-05-09/sequoias-sonya-huang-on-ai-application-layer-winners-humanoid-robotics-timeline-and-enterprise-ai-battles/transcript)).\n- **Early-career mistake (2019):** \"I was trying to win every argument and getting nowhere.\" Her rule of thumb: \"Just because something hasn't worked before doesn't mean it won't this time around\" ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/seven-questions-with-sonya-huang)).\n- Check sizes, deals passed on, and who introduced her to any founder: nothing found.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** She is a Sequoia lead or co-lead on exactly the rounds THESIS.md describes. Ineffable Intelligence was a $1.1B seed for a new RL lab. Standard Intelligence's Series A backed pixel-space pre-training. Etched's Series C backed inference silicon, and fal and Fireworks are inference infrastructure ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/)). Sequoia's other lab bets are adjacent: Reflection AI ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956)) and Ricursive (Stephanie Zhan). A $20M check could sit alongside her in a mega-seed like Ineffable's, which had several co-investors (Lightspeed, Nvidia, Google, Index). She is not a source of small, high-ownership deals. Her deals that matter to me: Ineffable, Standard Intelligence, Etched, fal, Fireworks, and the Harvey/OpenEvidence-style \"own your model\" app companies she now champions. On AI-native automation, she argues services replacement is the big market (\"services is the new software\") ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/this-is-agi-sequoia-ai-ascent-2026-keynote)).\n\n## Background\n- Born in Mountain View and raised in Silicon Valley. Her parents are immigrant engineers in the semiconductor industry. She says she learned to code in third grade ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/)).\n- In college she trained computer-vision neural networks on brain-scan and astrophysics data ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/)).\n- AB in Economics, summa cum laude, Princeton (HubSpot INBOUND speaker bio, [src](https://unbound.hubspot.com/speakers/recZt9MyM5ZU878pY)). Aggregators add minors in computer science and statistics/ML (unverified, [src](https://startupintros.com/people/sonya-huang)).\n- Goldman Sachs, then TPG Capital (private equity) ([src](https://unbound.hubspot.com/speakers/recZt9MyM5ZU878pY)). Her Sequoia bio also lists Goldman and private equity ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/)). Third-party dates are Goldman investment-banking analyst 2013–2014 and TPG associate 2014–2018 (unverified, [src](https://yespress.io/sonya-huang)).\n- Joined Sequoia in 2018 ([src](https://unbound.hubspot.com/speakers/recZt9MyM5ZU878pY)). Some aggregators say September 2018 (unverified, [src](https://theorg.com/org/langchain/org-chart/sonya-huang)). She is now a Partner on Sequoia's growth team, focused on AI ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/)). Some third-party profiles call her \"General Partner.\" Sequoia's own page says \"Partner.\"\n- Her first companies on the Sequoia page are growth-stage consumer and SaaS: Glossier and Gong (2019), then dbt Labs (2020) ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/)). From 2021–2022 she shifts to AI: OpenAI (2021), then Hugging Face, Glean and Fireworks (2022) ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/ | Sequoia profile with portfolio and articles; checked 2026-11-09 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes on sequoiacap.com (Perspectives and partnership posts) instead ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/)) |\n| X | https://x.com/sonyatweetybird | Bio \"Partner @Sequoia\". Long threads include the Gen AI market map (Oct 2022) and the AI Ascent 2025 recap ([src](https://threadreaderapp.com/user/sonyatweetybird)). Follower count: nothing found |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | Not checked directly |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Training Data (Sequoia) — https://sequoiacap.com/series/training-data/ | Co-hosted with Pat Grady ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/)). Launched 2024-06-05 ([src](https://metacast.app/podcast/training-data/Zs9F3aEo/introducing-training-data/YYO5Y04L)). 114 episodes, 4.2/5 from 43 ratings, about weekly; as of 2026-11-09 ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/training-data/id1750736528)) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-03-26 — Sequoia AI Ascent 2024 — opening remarks with Pat Grady and Konstantine Buhler — https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/the-ai-opportunity-sequoia-capital-s-ai-ascent-2024-opening-remarks — Put gen-AI revenue at about $3B. Said roughly $50B of Nvidia GPU spend was not yet earning it back (\"the math isn't mathing yet\"). Noted funding was going to models, not apps, the reverse of what she expected. Predicted copilots giving way to agents, better reasoning via inference-time compute, and compute shifting from pre-training to inference ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/the-ai-opportunity-sequoia-capital-s-ai-ascent-2024-opening-remarks)).\n- 2024-06-05 — Training Data — \"Introducing Training Data\" (launch) ([src](https://metacast.app/podcast/training-data/Zs9F3aEo/introducing-training-data/YYO5Y04L)).\n- 2025-05-07 — Sequoia AI Ascent 2025 keynote (\"AI's Trillion-Dollar Opportunity\") with Grady and Buhler — https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/ai-s-trillion-dollar-opportunity-sequoia-ai-ascent-2025-keynote — ChatGPT engagement (DAU/MAU) now near Reddit's. Coding reached PMF after Claude 3.5 Sonnet, and 2024 was voice's \"Her moment.\" Pre-training gains are slowing, so progress now comes from reasoning, tool use and scaffolding. Predicted agent-first companies and RL-tuned vertical agents. Asked whether taste becomes the scarce asset in an \"abundance era\" ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/ai-s-trillion-dollar-opportunity-sequoia-ai-ascent-2025-keynote)).\n- 2025 or 2026 (date unverified) — Training Data — \"Context Engineering Our Way to Long-Horizon Agents: LangChain's Harrison Chase\" — her own portfolio founder ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/series/training-data/)).\n- 2025 or 2026 (date unverified) — Training Data — \"Why IDEs Won't Die in the Age of AI Coding: Zed Founder Nathan Sobo\" and \"The Rise of Generative Media: fal's Bet on Video, Infrastructure, and Speed\": both her portfolio companies ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/series/training-data/)). Her role as host on these two is unverified.\n- 2026-08-25 — Training Data — \"Parallel's Parag Agrawal: Building a New Web for AI Agents\" (with Andrew Reed) ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/training-data/id1750736528)).\n- 2026-09-15 — Training Data — \"Box's Aaron Levie: On Reinventing Yourself in the AI Age and Enterprise Diffusion\" ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/training-data/id1750736528)).\n- 2026-10-06 — Training Data — \"Google's AI Infrastructure Chief, Amin Vahdat, on the Physics & Economics of Frontier AI\" ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/training-data/id1750736528)).\n- 2026-10-13 — Training Data — \"David Silver and Lasse Espeholt of Ineffable Intelligence: A Superlearner for the Age of Experience\": founders of a company she co-led ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/training-data/id1750736528)).\n- 2026-10-20 — Training Data — \"Inside the Robot Data Factory: Mecka's Josh Gao\" ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/training-data/id1750736528)).\n- 2025-05-09 — TBPN (guest) — interview on app-layer winners, humanoid robotics, \"vibe revenue\" ([src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-05-09/sequoias-sonya-huang-on-ai-application-layer-winners-humanoid-robotics-timeline-and-enterprise-ai-battles/transcript)).\n- 2026-01-16 — TBPN (guest, with Pat Grady) — on \"This is AGI\"; \"talkers to doers\"; Sequoia backing research labs (speaker labels missing from transcript) ([src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-01-16/sequoias-pat-grady-and-sonya-huang-declare-agi-has-arrived-and-issue-a-call-to-arms-for-founders/transcript)).\n- 2026-04-30 — Sequoia AI Ascent 2026 keynote \"This is AGI\" (with Grady, Buhler) — her section on agents: \"services is the new software,\" async agents, \"dark factories\" ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/this-is-agi-sequoia-ai-ascent-2026-keynote)).\n- 2026-08-13 — TBPN (guest) — \"not your weights, your products\"; Fireworks board; app companies building their own research labs ([src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-08-13/sonya-huang-on-ais-not-your-weights-not-your-product-moment-and-why-application-companies-are-building-their-own-labs/transcript)).\n- Other episodes worth a look (hosts and dates not checked): OpenAI Sora 2 team, OpenAI IMO gold team, Nvidia CTO Michael Kagan, Rich Sutton and Khurram Javed on continual learning, Dylan Patel of SemiAnalysis ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/series/training-data/)).\n\n## How they think\nThe arc is four co-written essays with Pat Grady, one per AI phase, plus the AI Ascent keynotes. She openly grades her past calls.\n\n**1. Sept 2022 — \"Generative AI: A Creative New World\"** ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ai-a-creative-new-world/)). Published 2022-09-19, two months before ChatGPT. Co-written with GPT-3.\n- Generative AI could push the marginal cost of creative and knowledge work toward zero. It could make workers at least 10% more efficient, worth trillions.\n- Moats would come from a user-engagement → data → model-performance flywheel.\n- Expected a split: platform/model companies below, killer apps above.\n- Said the near term would look like the early iPhone App Store: gimmicky apps, unclear business models.\n- A follow-up X thread (2022-10-17) launched Sequoia's living Gen AI market map ([src](https://threadreaderapp.com/user/sonyatweetybird)).\n\n**2. Sept 2023 — \"Generative AI's Act Two\"** ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ai-act-two/)). Published 2023-09-20. A public scorecard on the 2022 piece.\n- *Admitted wrong:* Progress came faster than expected. GPUs, not demand, were the bottleneck. The app/model split didn't happen, and the winners were vertically integrated. Incumbents (Google, Adobe) responded fast. Data flywheels were weak moats: \"The moats are in the customers, not the data.\"\n- *Claimed right:* Gen AI went mainstream, and ChatGPT was the killer app (100M MAU in about six weeks).\n- New problem: retention. Median gen-AI DAU/MAU was about 14% vs. 60–65% for top consumer apps. The shift is from \"technology-out\" to \"customer-back,\" end-to-end products.\n\n**3. Mar 2024 — AI Ascent.** She worried that capital was going to models, not apps, and that GPU spend far exceeded revenue. She predicted agents and inference-time reasoning. Both happened within the year ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/the-ai-opportunity-sequoia-capital-s-ai-ascent-2024-opening-remarks)). (Note: \"AI's $600B Question,\" June 2024, is by her colleague David Cahn, not Huang ([src](https://alpha-maven.com/news/venture-capital/ais-600b-question-sequoia-capital)).)\n\n**4. Oct 2024 — \"Generative AI's Act o1\"** ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ais-act-o1/)). Published 2024-10-09.\n- Reasoning (\"System 2\") through inference-time compute is a new scaling law. Clusters shift toward \"inference clouds.\"\n- The model layer has consolidated around big-tech pairs, so next-token prediction becomes cheap.\n- Value goes to application companies with \"cognitive architectures.\"\n- \"Service-as-software\": selling outcomes, not seats.\n- Reversed an earlier view that incumbent SaaS would be fine (\"no longer so sure\").\n- Said they were wary of infrastructure and models, and saw the application layer as the best place for venture returns.\n\n**5. May 2025 — AI Ascent.** Coding was the breakout category. Value sits at the app layer (she credited Grady). Agents go from fragile to robust via evals or end-to-end RL ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/ai-s-trillion-dollar-opportunity-sequoia-ai-ascent-2025-keynote)).\n\n**6. Jan 2026 — \"2026: This is AGI\"** ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/2026-this-is-agi/)). Published 2026-01-14.\n- A practical definition: \"AGI is the ability to figure things out. That's it.\"\n- Long-horizon agents are \"functionally AGI,\" and 2026 is their breakout year.\n- Extrapolates METR task-length doubling (about every seven months): day-long expert tasks by 2028, year-long by 2034.\n- Products shift from \"talkers\" to \"doers.\"\n\n**What held up**\n- The agents and reasoning calls in Mar 2024, ahead of o1 in Sept 2024.\n- Inference demand: her fal and Etched bets follow from it.\n- Coding as the first category to tip.\n- The retention worry in Act Two was right in 2023. By 2025 she reported ChatGPT engagement near Reddit's, so the problem eased for the leader ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/ai-s-trillion-dollar-opportunity-sequoia-ai-ascent-2025-keynote)).\n\n**What didn't**\n- The 2022 data-flywheel moat thesis and the app/model split, both retracted in 2023.\n\n**Unresolved tension (the most interesting thing here)**\nAct o1 (Oct 2024) said to avoid models and infrastructure and favor apps. But her named deals since then have moved down the stack:\n- fal Series D, inference (Dec 2025)\n- Ineffable Intelligence, $1.1B seed for an RL superintelligence lab (Apr 2026)\n- Standard Intelligence, video-pretrained computer-use models (Apr 2026)\n- Etched Series C, inference silicon (Jul 2026)\n\nAnthropic also appears on her page from 2026, and Sequoia joined its round that month ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/18/sequoia-to-invest-in-anthropic-breaking-vc-taboo-on-backing-rivals-ft)). Read alongside \"This is AGI,\" the view seems to have moved from \"models are commoditizing\" to \"new learning paradigms (RL and experience, pixels, continual learning) and inference hardware are fresh frontiers.\" That reading is an inference. No source shows her stating the reversal.\n\n**Contrarian streaks**\n- She called gen-AI revenue a SaaS-beating ramp early (>$1B by Sept 2023) ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ai-act-two/)) while the same firm published David Cahn's \"$600B question\" skepticism ([src](https://alpha-maven.com/news/venture-capital/ais-600b-question-sequoia-capital)).\n- She backs an IDE built from scratch (Zed) against VS Code forks ([src](https://business.mammothtimes.com/mammothtimes/article/bizwire-2025-8-20-zed-raises-32m-series-b-led-by-sequoia-to-scale-collaborative-ai-coding-vision)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. **\"Generative AI's Act Two\" (2023-09-20)**: the best single piece. It grades the 2022 thesis line by line and introduces \"customer-back\" and the retention critique ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ai-act-two/)).\n2. **\"Generative AI's Act o1\" (2024-10-09)**: reasoning and inference-time scaling, \"service-as-software,\" and the apps-over-models investing stance ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ais-act-o1/)).\n3. **\"2026: This is AGI\" (2026-01-14)**: her current frame. Long-horizon agents, the METR extrapolation, and \"doers\" ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/2026-this-is-agi/)).\n4. **\"Generative AI: A Creative New World\" (2022-09-19)**: the starting point. Read it after Act Two to see what changed ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ai-a-creative-new-world/)).\n5. **AI Ascent 2024 opening remarks (2024-03-26)**: her own section, with dated predictions you can check ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/the-ai-opportunity-sequoia-capital-s-ai-ascent-2024-opening-remarks)).\n\n## Social engagement\n- X (@sonyatweetybird). Her known long threads are firm-amplifying: the Gen AI market map (2022-10-17) and an AI Ascent 2025 recap (2025-05-07) covering Jensen Huang, Sam Altman and Jeff Dean ([src](https://threadreaderapp.com/user/sonyatweetybird)). Reply and repost patterns not checked; follower count not found.\n- Main amplifier is Sequoia: co-steward of AI Ascent and co-host of Training Data with Pat Grady ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/)). Podcast guests often come from her portfolio (LangChain, Zed, fal, Ineffable) ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/series/training-data/)).\n\n## Portfolio\n\"First partnered\" years come from Sequoia's attribution on her page ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/)). Rounds are sourced separately. Nubank (2013) and Armis (2015) appear under \"Enduring companies\" on her page but predate her 2018 arrival, so they are left out as her deals.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Glossier | nothing found (first partnered 2019) | 2019 | unverified | | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/) |\n| Gong | nothing found (first partnered 2019) | 2019 | unverified | | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/) |\n| dbt Labs | nothing found (first partnered 2020) | 2020 | unverified | | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/) |\n| Streamlit | Series B, $35M | 2021-04 | Sequoia led; Huang on board | GGV Capital, Gradient Ventures | [src](https://devops.com/streamlit-transforms-how-data-scientists-share-data-raises-35-million-in-funding-led-by-sequoia/) |\n| Apollo | nothing found (first partnered 2021) | 2021 | unverified | | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/) |\n| OpenAI | ~$300M share sale at $27–29B | 2023-04-28 | no (Sequoia participant; her page says first partnered 2021) | a16z, Thrive Capital, K2 Global, Founders Fund | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/04/28/openai-funding-valuation-chatgpt/embed/) |\n| Hugging Face | Series C, $100M at ~$2B | 2022-05-09 | no (Lux led; Sequoia's first investment) | Lux, Coatue, Addition, Betaworks, AIX Ventures | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=579586) |\n| Glean | Series C, $100M at $1B | 2022-05-18 | yes (Sequoia; she is a byline author) | Kleiner Perkins, Lightspeed (co-leads per Sequoia); General Catalyst, Slack Fund | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-glean-organizing-knowledge-work/) |\n| LangChain | Series A, $25M | 2024-02-15 (announced); Sequoia's 2025 retrospective says \"early 2023\" | yes | not named in the Sequoia post | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-langchain-the-llm-application-framework/) |\n| Fireworks AI | Series B, $52M | 2024-07-11 | Sequoia led; her role from her page (first partnered 2022) | NVIDIA, AMD, MongoDB | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2024/07/11/fireworks-ai-raises-52m-led-sequoia-522b-valuation) |\n| Magic | $320M | 2024-08-29 | no (lead disputed; her page: first partnered 2024) | Eric Schmidt, Jane Street, Atlassian, CapitalG, Elad Gil, Nat Friedman, Daniel Gross | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/29/generative-ai-coding-startup-magic-lands-320m-investment-from-eric-schmidt-atlassian-and-others/) |\n| Mercury | Series C, $300M at $3.5B | 2025-03-26 | yes (Sequoia's first check; her page lists 2025) | Coatue, CRV, a16z, Spark, Marathon | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/26/fintech-mercury-lands-300m-in-sequoia-led-series-c-doubles-valuation-to-3-5b) |\n| Zed | Series B, $32M | 2025-08-20 | yes (her page says first partnered 2024, gap unexplained) | Redpoint, Root, Matchstick, V1.VC (per deal database) | [src](https://business.mammothtimes.com/mammothtimes/article/bizwire-2025-8-20-zed-raises-32m-series-b-led-by-sequoia-to-scale-collaborative-ai-coding-vision) |\n| fal | Series D, $140M at $4.5B | 2025-12-09 | yes (she co-authored with James Flynn) | Kleiner Perkins, NVentures, Alkeon; existing a16z, Bessemer | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-fal-the-generative-media-company/), [src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/fal-nabs-140m-fresh-funding-222133234.html) |\n| Anthropic | participation in ~$25B round at $350B (FT report) | 2026-01 | no (GIC and Coatue led); Huang's personal role unverified beyond her page listing | GIC, Coatue | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/18/sequoia-to-invest-in-anthropic-breaking-vc-taboo-on-backing-rivals-ft) |\n| Ineffable Intelligence | Seed, $1.1B at $5.1B post | 2026-04-27 | co-lead (Sequoia with Lightspeed; Sequoia post with Alfred Lin) | Lightspeed, Nvidia, Google, Index | [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-04-27/sequoia-and-nvidia-back-ex-deepmind-researcher-at-5-1-billion-value) |\n| Standard Intelligence | Series A | 2026-04-30 | co-lead | Spark Capital (co-lead) | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/standard-intelligence-training-general-intelligence-in-pixel-space/) |\n| Etched | Series C, $300M at $10B pre | 2026-07-23 | yes (with Abhishek Malani) | Jane Street, a16z, Diffusion, SK Hynix | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/) |\n| Corma, Preview, TypeSafe AI | nothing found (first partnered 2026) | 2026 | unverified | | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/) |\n| Keen Technologies (John Carmack) | nothing found | unverified | unverified | | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/) |\n\n**Cross-firm overlap with Thrive Capital**\n- **OpenAI:** both firms were in the Apr 2023 ~$300M share sale ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/04/28/openai-funding-valuation-chatgpt/embed/)). Thrive then led the ~$86B employee tender ([src](https://www.maginative.com/article/thrive-capital-to-lead-purchase-of-openai-tender-offer-at-80-billion-valuation/)).\n- **Cursor:** no Sequoia or Huang involvement found. A Training Data episode covered how Cursor trained Composer on Fireworks, which is her portfolio company ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/series/training-data/)).\n- **Benchmark → Sequoia handoff at LangChain:** Benchmark (Miles Grimshaw, now Thrive) led the Apr 2023 seed and Sequoia/Huang led the Series A ([src](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/open-source-agentic-startup-langchain-221246959.html)). See `people/miles-grimshaw.md`.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **LangChain:** Harrison Chase started it as a nights-and-weekends project while at Robust Intelligence, a Sequoia portfolio company, then recruited colleague Ankush Gola ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-langchain-the-llm-application-framework/)). The Sequoia-portfolio link is the likely discovery path, but the intro is not documented. Her Oct 2025 retrospective says that at the Series A LangChain had 2,000+ open-source contributors and was used by 50,000+ LLM apps ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/langchain-from-agent-0-to-1-to-agentic-engineering)). Its date (\"early 2023\") conflicts with the Feb 2024 announcement. A board seat is listed only by TheOrg (unverified, [src](https://theorg.com/org/langchain/org-chart/sonya-huang)).\n- **Glean (2022):** a \"why now?\" case. SaaS sprawl plus better language models made out-of-the-box enterprise search possible. Usage was strong: 45% DAU/MAU and 40–60% MAU/employees. Founder Arvind Jain (ex-Google, Rubrik co-founder) was central ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-glean-organizing-knowledge-work/)).\n- **Mercury (2025):** Sequoia's first check into Mercury, eight years after founding, as lead of the Series C ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/26/fintech-mercury-lands-300m-in-sequoia-led-series-c-doubles-valuation-to-3-5b)).\n- **Etched (2026):** the stated reasons were the team's ability to ship successive generations of inference hardware, its first-gen chip tape-out at TSMC, and a cluster bring-up in 40 days ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/)).\n- **Ineffable Intelligence:** FT first reported Sequoia leading about $1B at about $4B in Feb 2026. It closed in Apr 2026 at $1.1B / $5.1B with Lightspeed as co-lead ([src](https://thenextweb.com/news/david-silver-is-chasing-superhuman-intelligence-with-a-1bn-seed), [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-04-27/sequoia-and-nvidia-back-ex-deepmind-researcher-at-5-1-billion-value)). She later hosted the founders on Training Data (2026-10-13) ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/training-data/id1750736528)).\n- **Standard Intelligence (2026-04-30):** Sequoia led the Series A with Spark Capital (\"Miko and Yasmin\"). The founders, Galen Mead and Devansh Pandey, were 21 and 20, and met as teenagers at the Atlas Fellowship in 2022 ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/standard-intelligence-training-general-intelligence-in-pixel-space/)). How Sequoia found them: nothing found.\n- **Ineffable, first contact (added 2026-11-09):** Lin and Huang flew to London to meet Silver ([src](https://thenextweb.com/news/ineffable-intelligence-david-silver-sequoia-nvidia-5-billion)). Her podcast outreach to Silver predates the deal ([src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/david-silver-and-lasse-espeholt-of-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-age-of-experience)).\n- First-contact and intro-path details for other deals: nothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sequoia Capital | Partner (growth, AI) | 2018 | [src](https://unbound.hubspot.com/speakers/recZt9MyM5ZU878pY) |\n| Pat Grady (Sequoia) | Co-author of all four gen-AI essays; Training Data co-host; AI Ascent co-presenter | 2022-09 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ai-a-creative-new-world/) |\n| Konstantine Buhler (Sequoia) | AI Ascent keynote co-presenter | 2024-03 | [src](https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/the-ai-opportunity-sequoia-capital-s-ai-ascent-2024-opening-remarks) |\n| James Flynn (Sequoia) | Co-author on fal and Zed partnership posts | 2025 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/) |\n| Abhishek Malani (Sequoia) | Co-author on Etched post | 2026-07 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/) |\n| Alfred Lin (Sequoia) | Co-author on Ineffable Intelligence post | 2026 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/) |\n| Isaiah Boone (Sequoia) | Co-author on Mercury post | 2025 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/) |\n| Bill Coughran (Sequoia) | Co-author on Glean post | 2022-05 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-glean-organizing-knowledge-work/) |\n| Harrison Chase (LangChain) | Portfolio founder; Series A lead | 2024-02 (announced) | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-langchain-the-llm-application-framework/) |\n| Streamlit | Board member | 2021-04 | [src](https://devops.com/streamlit-transforms-how-data-scientists-share-data-raises-35-million-in-funding-led-by-sequoia/) |\n| David Silver (Ineffable Intelligence) | Portfolio founder; seed co-lead | 2026-04 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/) |\n| Spark Capital | Co-lead, Standard Intelligence Series A; co-investor, Mercury Series C | 2025-03 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/standard-intelligence-training-general-intelligence-in-pixel-space/) |\n| Thrive Capital | Co-investor in OpenAI (Apr 2023 share sale) | 2023-04 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2023/04/28/openai-funding-valuation-chatgpt/embed/) |\n| Kleiner Perkins | Co-investor in Glean Series C and fal Series D | 2022-05 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-glean-organizing-knowledge-work/) |\n| Fireworks AI | Board member (her own words) | 2026-08 or earlier | [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-08-13/sonya-huang-on-ais-not-your-weights-not-your-product-moment-and-why-application-companies-are-building-their-own-labs/transcript) |\n| Ioannis Antonoglou (Reflection AI) | Sequoia-backed founder, cited by her as \"we backed\" | 2025-03 (seed announced) | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/david-silver-and-lasse-espeholt-of-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-age-of-experience) |\n| Lightspeed | Co-investor in Glean Series C; co-lead of Ineffable seed | 2022-05 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-glean-organizing-knowledge-work/) |\n\nSonali Singh (Sequoia) is a likely colleague, but no co-authored piece, deal or episode linking them was found.\n\n## Open questions\n- Did she personally put Sequoia into OpenAI in 2021 (her page says \"first partnered 2021\"), and in which round?\n- LangChain Series A date: early 2023 (her retrospective) vs. Feb 2024 (announcement). Probably closed in 2023 and announced in 2024; needs confirmation.\n- Zed: what was the 2024 Sequoia involvement before the Aug 2025 Series B?\n- Is she the Sequoia partner on the Jan 2026 Anthropic round, and what was the check size?\n- Board seats beyond Streamlit (LangChain per TheOrg, Glean, fal, Etched?).\n- Has she publicly reconciled Act o1's \"avoid models and infra\" with her 2025–26 lab and inference-hardware deals?\n- X follower count and reply patterns. Training Data episode-level host credits for the 2024–2025 back catalog.\n- Glossier, Gong, dbt Labs, Apollo: which rounds and with whom?\n- Was she the Sequoia partner on Reflection AI's seed (with CRV)? She said \"we backed Ioannis\" on the Ineffable episode.\n- Did Silver ever appear on Training Data before founding Ineffable, or did he decline her 2024 invite?\n- The Jan 2026 TBPN \"absolutely backing research labs\" line: confirm the speaker against the video.\n- Fireworks board: when did she join it (her page says first partnered 2022)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://sequoiacap.com/people/sonya-huang/\n- https://unbound.hubspot.com/speakers/recZt9MyM5ZU878pY\n- https://startupintros.com/people/sonya-huang\n- https://yespress.io/sonya-huang\n- https://theorg.com/org/langchain/org-chart/sonya-huang\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ai-a-creative-new-world/\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ai-act-two/\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/generative-ais-act-o1/\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/2026-this-is-agi/\n- https://sequoiacap.com/series/training-data/\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/training-data/id1750736528\n- https://metacast.app/podcast/training-data/Zs9F3aEo/introducing-training-data/YYO5Y04L\n- https://threadreaderapp.com/user/sonyatweetybird\n- https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/the-ai-opportunity-sequoia-capital-s-ai-ascent-2024-opening-remarks\n- https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/ai-s-trillion-dollar-opportunity-sequoia-ai-ascent-2025-keynote\n- https://alpha-maven.com/news/venture-capital/ais-600b-question-sequoia-capital\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-langchain-the-llm-application-framework/\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/langchain-from-agent-0-to-1-to-agentic-engineering\n- https://venturebeat.com/ai/langchain-lands-25m-round-launches-platform-to-support-entire-llm-application-lifecycle\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/news/open-source-agentic-startup-langchain-221246959.html\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-glean-organizing-knowledge-work/\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-fal-the-generative-media-company/\n- https://finance.yahoo.com/news/fal-nabs-140m-fresh-funding-222133234.html\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-etched-building-the-inference-machine/\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/standard-intelligence-training-general-intelligence-in-pixel-space/\n- https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-04-27/sequoia-and-nvidia-back-ex-deepmind-researcher-at-5-1-billion-value\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/david-silver-is-chasing-superhuman-intelligence-with-a-1bn-seed\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/26/fintech-mercury-lands-300m-in-sequoia-led-series-c-doubles-valuation-to-3-5b\n- https://business.mammothtimes.com/mammothtimes/article/bizwire-2025-8-20-zed-raises-32m-series-b-led-by-sequoia-to-scale-collaborative-ai-coding-vision\n- https://siliconangle.com/2024/07/11/fireworks-ai-raises-52m-led-sequoia-522b-valuation\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/29/generative-ai-coding-startup-magic-lands-320m-investment-from-eric-schmidt-atlassian-and-others/\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=579586\n- https://devops.com/streamlit-transforms-how-data-scientists-share-data-raises-35-million-in-funding-led-by-sequoia/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/04/28/openai-funding-valuation-chatgpt/embed/\n- https://www.maginative.com/article/thrive-capital-to-lead-purchase-of-openai-tender-offer-at-80-billion-valuation/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/18/sequoia-to-invest-in-anthropic-breaking-vc-taboo-on-backing-rivals-ft\n- https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-05-09/sequoias-sonya-huang-on-ai-application-layer-winners-humanoid-robotics-timeline-and-enterprise-ai-battles/transcript\n- https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-08-13/sonya-huang-on-ais-not-your-weights-not-your-product-moment-and-why-application-companies-are-building-their-own-labs/transcript\n- https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-01-16/sequoias-pat-grady-and-sonya-huang-declare-agi-has-arrived-and-issue-a-call-to-arms-for-founders/transcript\n- https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/david-silver-and-lasse-espeholt-of-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-age-of-experience\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/ineffable-intelligence-david-silver-sequoia-nvidia-5-billion\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/standard-intelligence-training-general-intelligence-in-pixel-space/\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/seven-questions-with-sonya-huang\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-era-of-experience\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956\n- https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/this-is-agi-sequoia-ai-ascent-2026-keynote\n- https://sequoiacap.com/founder/david-silver\n- https://convergedigest.com/ricursive-intelligence-aims-to-accelerate-semiconductor-design/\n", "person:spencer-powers": "\n# Spencer Powers\n\n## One-line take\nDRW's Head of Special Investments [src](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-technologies-names-kit-merker-as-ceo). He co-founded the photonic-chip company Lightelligence and holds board and officer seats at DRW's compute company, Silicon Data. For photonics or compute-market founders he is the most direct person at DRW.\n\n## Crux\n- **What makes him win:** he is a hardware co-founder (Lightelligence, MIT optical AI chips) now sitting in the seat of a proprietary trading firm that invests its own balance sheet. DRW has no outside LPs (see [firms/drw-venture-capital.md](../firms/drw-venture-capital.md)).\n- **How the seat works:** it is a control and governance seat, not a VC check-writer's.\n  - He chairs Plainsight's board and speaks for it when the CEO changes [src](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-announces-jonathan-simkins-as-new-ceo-succeeding-kit-merker).\n  - He has been a director of Silicon Data's legal entity since the seed. He is also an executive officer there [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml).\n- **Constraint:** he works inside DRW's structure, where Don Wilson co-sits on the Silicon Data board. Final approval likely rests with Wilson (inference, unconfirmed).\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** probably not a passive minority co-investor in a crowded frontier-lab seed. His named deals are DRW-incubated or DRW-controlled companies.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **On Silicon Data's board from the seed, not just the A.** The March 2025 seed Form D for Silicon Derivatives Inc. ($4,724,994 sold, 12 investors) lists him as a director at DRW's address: \"Spencer Powers 540 W. Madison, Suite 2500 Chicago\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml).\n  - By the August 2026 A ($30,499,940, 16 investors, Valor Atreides lead) he is \"Executive Officer\" and director [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml).\n  - This corrects an earlier repo row that dated the seat to 2026-07.\n  - The $4.72M seed figure matches Silicon Data's announced $4.7M seed. That strengthens the Silicon Derivatives = Silicon Data match, which is still an inference.\n- **Plainsight looks DRW-owned.** Plainsight Technologies Inc. was incorporated in 2024. Its Form D shows $12M sold to **one** investor, with Powers as sole director at DRW's Chicago address [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2027185/000202718524000001/primary_doc.xml).\n  - He is \"Chairman of the Board at Plainsight Technologies, and Head of Special Investments\" [src](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-technologies-names-kit-merker-as-ceo).\n  - Reading: a DRW recapitalization or full ownership of the ex-Sixgill computer-vision business. The single investor is not named, so this is unconfirmed.\n- **A shared back office.** Sure People's Form D (HR analytics; $5M from one investor; Powers a director) lists phone \"3128364073\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2046502/000204650224000001/primary_doc.xml). Silicon Derivatives' Form Ds list the same number.\n  - One office, likely DRW special investments (unconfirmed), handles filings for both. Other single-investor holdings under the same desk may exist.\n- **He runs CEO succession.** On replacing Plainsight's CEO in December 2025 he said: \"Kit accomplished what the company needed during a pivotal time for Plainsight\" [src](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-announces-jonathan-simkins-as-new-ceo-succeeding-kit-merker).\n- **How he got the chip background:** \"I was an MBA student at MIT in 2015 when I met Yichen\" [src](https://physicsworld.com/a/optical-chipmaker-focuses-on-high-performance-computing/). Yichen Shen is Lightelligence's founder-CEO.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:** he is a real, named decision-maker at a balance-sheet investor that co-led Silicon Data's seed and has backed GPU supply (Crusoe, Vast.ai per the firm file). Photonics and compute-market founders have a natural ally in him. DRW's money has no LP ownership constraints, which suits the owner's dislike of rigid-ownership LPs.\n- **Against:**\n  - None of his named deals is a frontier-lab or a $20M-style seed.\n  - His seat is closer to an owner or operator than a co-investor. The 2025 seed was $4.7M, Plainsight $12M from one investor, Sure People $5M.\n  - Seniority maps loosely: \"Head of\" at a trading firm is set here to `partner`, which is an approximation.\n- **Deals that matter:** Silicon Data Seed (2025) and Series A (2026).\n\n## Background\n- BA in Economics, Boston College (per search snippets of LinkedIn/TheOrg; unconfirmed).\n- Roles at IDEO, Mercer, Liberty Mutual and Ropes & Gray appear as logos on TheOrg [src](https://theorg.com/org/drw/org-chart/spencer-powers). Aggregator; titles and dates unknown.\n- Co-founded ArtLifting (benefit corporation for artists) in 2013. Director 2013 to 2022-03 per MarketScreener [src](https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/SPENCER-POWERS-A1WWYO/).\n- MIT Sloan MBA '16 [src](https://physics.mit.edu/news/accelerating-ai-at-the-speed-of-light).\n- Co-founded Lux Labs, an MIT spinout per a TheOrg search snippet. Unconfirmed, and not to be confused with Lux Capital's Lux Labs.\n- Co-founded Lightelligence (founded 2017) with Yichen Shen and Marin Soljačić. Sources call him a co-founder and board member [src](https://physics.mit.edu/news/accelerating-ai-at-the-speed-of-light), [src](https://physicsworld.com/a/optical-chipmaker-focuses-on-high-performance-computing/). Title \"President\" and tenure 2018-04 to 2023-08 come from TheOrg via search snippet (aggregator, unconfirmed).\n- Moved to DRW as Head of Special Investments. Start 2023-08 per TheOrg (aggregator). Confirmed in the title by Plainsight's 2024-01-24 release.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | No DRW team page names him (drw.com/global-investments checked 2026-11-11) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/scp1 | Surfaced in search for \"Spencer Powers Lightelligence\"; identity match unconfirmed (one snippet showed a Seattle profile) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| Aggregator | https://theorg.com/org/drw/org-chart/spencer-powers | TheOrg, unverified profile, no photo |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2022-04 — Physics World Optics and Photonics Briefing — Lightelligence interview (co-founder voice on Lightelligence's origin) — https://physicsworld.com/a/optical-chipmaker-focuses-on-high-performance-computing/\n- No DRW-era talks or podcasts found.\n\n## How they think\n- Frames enterprise AI as an adoption problem: Plainsight serves companies \"underserved because of the complexity of consuming AI\" [src](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-technologies-names-kit-merker-as-ceo).\n- Hands-on governance: picks and replaces CEOs at companies he chairs (Merker 2024, Simkins 2025).\n- No public contrarian views found.\n\n## Read these first\n- Silicon Derivatives Form Ds, [2025](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml) and [2026](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml): his seat on Silicon Data from the seed onward.\n- [Plainsight 2024 CEO release](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-technologies-names-kit-merker-as-ceo): his only substantive on-record quotes.\n- [Physics World 2022](https://physicsworld.com/a/optical-chipmaker-focuses-on-high-performance-computing/): Lightelligence origin.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: no public X, Substack or podcast presence.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Silicon Data (Silicon Derivatives Inc.) | Seed ($4.7M); director | 2025-03-20 | DRW co-led with Jump | Jump Trading Group; Woodside AI; Wintermute Ventures; Sancus; SoGal | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Silicon Data | Series A ($30.5M); re-up, executive officer and director | 2026-08 (first sale 2026-07-17) | no (Valor Atreides AI Fund led) | CME Group; F-Prime; Samsung; VanEck | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Plainsight | Undisclosed ($12M, single investor); sole director, chairman | 2024-05-31 | unknown (investor unnamed) | none listed | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2027185/000202718524000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Sure People (not AI-core) | Undisclosed ($5M, single investor); director | 2024-11-08 | unknown | none listed | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2046502/000204650224000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Silicon Data:**\n  - DRW incubated it, per the firm file. Powers and Don Wilson were both directors from the March 2025 seed.\n  - Valor's Vivek Pattipati joined the board with the 2026 A, and Powers added an officer role.\n  - First in: yes (seed). Re-up: yes (A).\n  - Intro path and pricing role not disclosed.\n- **Plainsight:**\n  - Plainsight is the former Sixgill. Aggregators list DRW Venture Capital as an earlier investor (unconfirmed).\n  - The 2024 restructuring into Plainsight Technologies Inc. had a single $12M investor and Powers as sole director. He chaired the board through two CEO changes.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DRW | Head of Special Investments | 2023-08 (aggregator) / by 2024-01 | [Plainsight](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-technologies-names-kit-merker-as-ceo) |\n| Don Wilson | Co-director, Silicon Derivatives | 2025-03 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Rongnan Li | CEO of Silicon Derivatives; co-director | 2025-03 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Vivek Pattipati (Valor) | Co-director | 2026 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Yichen Shen, Marin Soljačić | Lightelligence co-founders | 2015 (met) / 2017 | [Physics World](https://physicsworld.com/a/optical-chipmaker-focuses-on-high-performance-computing/) |\n| Kit Merker, Jonathan Simkins | Plainsight CEOs hired under his chairmanship | 2024, 2025 | [2024](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-technologies-names-kit-merker-as-ceo), [2025](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-announces-jonathan-simkins-as-new-ceo-succeeding-kit-merker) |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** no public channel was found.\n- **Best route:** through Silicon Data (CEO Rongnan Li, who may be Carmen Li; not confirmed) or Valor's Vivek Pattipati, both co-directors.\n- **For photonics founders:** the MIT and Lightelligence network (Yichen Shen).\n- **Mail:** the DRW address, 540 W. Madison, Suite 2500, Chicago.\n\n## Open questions\n- Is Rongnan Li the same person as Silicon Data's CEO Carmen Li? No source links the names.\n- Who is the single investor in Plainsight ($12M) and Sure People ($5M)? Is DRW behind Sure People?\n- Does Special Investments sit under DRW Global Investments, which describes \"longer-term, strategic investments\" [src](https://www.drw.com/global-investments)? Unconfirmed.\n- Does he still hold Lightelligence equity or a board seat? MarketScreener shows no end date. If so, that is a conflict and also a lens: DRW and photonics.\n- Who approves his checks? Likely Don Wilson (inference).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2027185/000202718524000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2046502/000204650224000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-technologies-names-kit-merker-as-ceo\n- https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-announces-jonathan-simkins-as-new-ceo-succeeding-kit-merker\n- https://physicsworld.com/a/optical-chipmaker-focuses-on-high-performance-computing/\n- https://physics.mit.edu/news/accelerating-ai-at-the-speed-of-light\n- https://theorg.com/org/drw/org-chart/spencer-powers (aggregator)\n- https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/SPENCER-POWERS-A1WWYO/ (aggregator)\n- https://www.drw.com/global-investments\n", "person:sriram-viswanathan": "\n# Sriram Viswanathan\n\n## One-line take\nThe Celesta partner on its AI-compute boards (Velaura AI, Tensordyne, Aurascape), who keeps backing the same founder, Rajiv Khemani, from one company to the next [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan).\n\n## Crux\nHe wins with chip founders who already know him from Intel and from earlier Celesta companies, and he stays on the board through several rounds. He is constrained by fund size and LP pressure for distributions: Fund V had $102M a year in [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1987549/000198754925000002/primary_doc.xml), and he says \"DPI is the new IRR\" [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/). He would not chase a hot software round; his deals are hardware and infrastructure.\n\n## Differential insight\n- \"DPI is the new IRR\", and \"Everyone wants DPI\": his read of deep-tech LPs in 2026 [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/).\n- \"there are fewer good companies to go after\" and \"It's a take-all kind of market\" [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/).\n- What he asks of companies now: \"Show me revenue growth. Show me profitability growth.\" [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/)\n- He is a related person on Celesta Capital V; Lip-Bu Tan is not [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1987549/000198754925000002/primary_doc.xml).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. AI chips and compute are on thesis and he enters early (Auradine A, Recogni A), but he is a senior MP at a small fund, not a rising junior, and he does not do labs. Useful as a co-investor signal in AI silicon: Velaura AI, Upscale AI, Tensordyne.\n\n## Background\n- Nearly 20 years at Intel: VP in the Intel Architecture Group, VP at Intel Capital, GM of incubation and innovation; dates not stated [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan).\n- Founded IndusAge Partners, a US-India cross-border venture firm [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan).\n- MBA, UCLA Anderson (1997); degrees from the American College and the Indian Institute of Science [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan).\n- Advisory board, India Semiconductor Mission [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan | |\n| Substack / blog | Celesta Substack (not read) | |\n| X | https://x.com/1sriram | linked from his Celesta page; followers not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/onesriram/ | linked from his Celesta page |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/techsurge-deep-tech-podcast/id1755173852 | TechSurge: Deep Tech Podcast |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-02 — Crunchbase News — interview on deep tech and secondaries — [link](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/) — DPI over IRR; mined\n- ongoing — TechSurge (host) — guests include Sanjay Mehrotra and Stacy Rasgon — [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/techsurge-deep-tech-podcast/id1755173852) — not mined\n\n## How they think\n- Deep tech is winner-take-all with fewer fundable companies, so concentrate on known founders [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/).\n- AI is the hottest area but he also points to biotech and \"pragmatic\" semiconductors [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/).\n- Stated mistakes or passes: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n- The Crunchbase News interview [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/).\n- His Celesta page for the board list [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed (X not read this pass).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Velaura AI (Auradine) | Series A ($81M) | 2023-05 | co-led; board | Mayfield; DCVC | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan) |\n| Tensordyne (Recogni) | Series B ($48.9M) | 2021-02 | led | - | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan) |\n| Tensordyne (Recogni) | Series C ($102M) | 2024-02 | co-led; board | GreatPoint | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan) |\n| Aurascape | Seed ($12.8M) | 2024-09 | board; Mayfield led | Mayfield | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan) |\n| Movandi; Platform9; Baffle | board seats | not stated | - | - | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan) |\n\nRound details are from the repo ledger; his page confirms the board seats only.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Khemani chain:** Celesta's portfolio page dates Innovium to 2015, Velaura (Auradine) to 2022, Aurascape to 2024 and Upscale AI to 2025; the last three share founders, and Viswanathan sits on two of the boards [src](https://www.celesta.vc/portfolio) [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan). No first-hand account of the intro was found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Rajiv Khemani | repeat founder (Auradine, Aurascape, Upscale AI) | 2022 | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/portfolio) |\n| Mayfield (Navin Chaddha) | co-lead partner on the Khemani companies | 2023 | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/portfolio) |\n| Lip-Bu Tan; Michael Marks; Nicholas Brathwaite | co-founding managing partners | 2018 or earlier | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1746798/000174679820000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Intel Capital | former employer | - | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When he joined WRVI, and whether he personally sourced Upscale AI.\n- TechSurge episodes where he states views on AI chips have not been read.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan\n- https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/\n- https://www.celesta.vc/portfolio\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1987549/000198754925000002/primary_doc.xml\n", "person:stanley-druckenmiller": "\n# Stanley Druckenmiller\n\n## One-line take\nA macro billionaire whose private AI book (Etched, Perplexity, Standard Intelligence, Flapping Airplanes, Q.ANT, Reflexivity) gets allocated to him through relationships and his LP stakes. He doesn't source or lead, so he is useful to the owner as a signal of who the \"kids\" network routes into, and as a possible LP, not as a lead investor to follow.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins allocation:** His name is a cap-table asset. Founders list him as an \"angel & advisor\" ([si.inc](https://si.inc/posts/a/)), and Etched puts him on its public angel wall next to Karpathy and Hinton ([etched.com](https://www.etched.com/)). He openly says he outsources AI judgment to younger people. On Morgan Stanley's *Hard Lessons* (early 2026), he said he has \"young superstars in my firm,\" and that in venture \"we've always looked at ... where the kids are going\" (transcribed by [A Letter a Day #320](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and)).\n- **The pattern in the deals:** He is late and never leads. The only company where a source calls him the **first** investor is Toggle AI (now Reflexivity), and its co-founder Jan Szilagyi used to work for him at Duquesne ([CNBC 2023-06-06](https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/06/a-harvard-econ-phd-whiz-teaching-generative-ai-to-invest-for-everyone.html)). Everywhere else he arrives as a listed participant after institutional leads have priced the round:\n  - Perplexity: a \"new investor\" in the April 2024 B1, about 19 months after the 2022 seed ([perplexity.ai](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-launches-enterprise-pro)).\n  - Etched: an unannounced 2024–26 financing, not the 2023 seed ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/)).\n  - Standard Intelligence and Flapping Airplanes: the Series A in both.\n- **What constrains him:** Bandwidth on the private side, which just got worse. Per Barron's, \"Warsh runs the firm's private-equity investments, according to Druckenmiller\" ([Barron's via itiger, 2026-02-02](https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848)). Kevin Warsh was nominated Fed chair on 2026-01-30 and confirmed in May 2026. He filed to stop consulting for Duquesne and to divest Juggernaut ([CBS](https://www.cbsnews.com/amp/news/kevin-warsh-net-worth-fed-chair-nominee/)). The person who ran Duquesne's private investing has left, and no successor is named in any source I found.\n- **Public-market discipline over loyalty:** He does trim AI winners and says so on air. He cut Nvidia because it \"tripled in a year.\" By early 2026 he said AI was \"not driving the engine anymore\" and called the cycle \"Maybe 8th inning\" ([NBC/CNBC](https://www.nbcconnecticut.com/news/business/money-report/stanley-druckenmiller-cut-his-nvidia-stake-in-late-march-says-ai-may-be-a-bit-overhyped-short-term/3284318/); [A Letter a Day #320](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and)).\n- **What he would never do (inference):** Underwrite a lab on his own technical judgment, or take a board seat. His own word for his edge on AI is \"luck,\" and he credits his \"young partner.\"\n\n## Differential insight\n- **His venture heuristic is talent flow, not technology.** \"that's something we've always looked at in venture is where the kids are going.\" On the same transcript, his 2008–09 Palantir purchase was \"a cool company back then that all the kids wanted to go to.\" ([Morgan Stanley *Hard Lessons*, transcribed by A Letter a Day #320, 2026-03-03](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and)). The transcript is third-party (medium confidence).\n- **The Nvidia call came from an unnamed young partner with a Palo Alto AI network.** CNBC: \"My young partner was early with Nvidia.\" \"I didn't even know how to spell it.\" ([CNBC Squawk Box transcript, 2024-05-07](https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html)). *Hard Lessons* adds that the partner \"had in people from his AI network in Palo Alto\" and that the signal was engineers moving \"from crypto to AI\" ([A Letter a Day #320](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and); [In Good Company excerpt](https://acquirersmultiple.com/2024/11/stanley-druckenmiller-why-im-bullish-and-cautious-on-ai-investment)). No source names the partner, and it is not Warsh.\n- **He was first money in an AI company only once, and its founder was his own alum.** CNBC calls him \"Toggle's first investor.\" Szilagyi: \"Without him, I'm not sure Giuseppe and I would've really made the leap\" ([CNBC 2023-06-06](https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/06/a-harvard-econ-phd-whiz-teaching-generative-ai-to-invest-for-everyone.html)). He followed on in the $30M Series B led by Greycroft and Interactive Brokers ([HedgeFundAlpha, 2024-10](https://hedgefundalpha.com/news/reflexivity-raises-30-million-series-b-funding/)).\n- **The Druckenmiller + Warsh pairing is the private-side \"senior/junior\" team (inference from two co-occurrences).**\n  - Barron's says Warsh ran private equity at Duquesne ([src](https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848)).\n  - Both men are named together on Hebbia's 2022 round (\"Stanley Druckenmiller, Kevin Warsh, Julius Genachowski\") ([Hebbia blog](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of)).\n  - Both bought into Abstract's management company in 2017 ([Colossus](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n  - Abstract's Ramtin Naimi took the second-largest check in Hebbia's early round (same source).\n  - Possible path: the Abstract relationship brought both men into Hebbia. That is inference only. Warsh's 2026 ethics filing lists a Hebbia stake. It is his personal holding, not a disclosed Duquesne position ([CBS](https://www.cbsnews.com/amp/news/kevin-warsh-net-worth-fed-chair-nominee/)).\n- **His first exposure to Flapping Airplanes may have come through an LP stake, not his own sourcing.**\n  - Nova Global, Koko Xs's firm, names him as a backer: \"backed by folks like Marc Andreessen, Henry Kravis, Stan Druckenmiller, Micky Malka\" ([kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/)).\n  - Nova is listed in Flapping's seed, but only by an aggregator ([iq.wiki](https://iq.wiki/wiki/flapping-airplanes)). It also ran an SPV, \"Nova Flappy, LP,\" for the A ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2146901/000123191926000844/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - Duquesne Family Office appears directly as an individual backer in the A ([company LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/)).\n- **He co-founded an AI pathology company.** \"we had started this company that basically looks for pathology in prostate cancer,\" and the machines \"could do it as well or better\" ([A Letter a Day #320](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and)). The company is not named. Paige (an MSK spin-out with a prostate product) is a candidate only, and Paige's Series A coverage names Breyer, not him ([Becker's](https://www.beckershospitalreview.com/artificial-intelligence/paige-ai-launches-with-25m-in-funding-data-license-agreement-with-memorial-sloan-kettering.html)).\n- **Photonic AI compute through the family office.** Duquesne Family Office joined the second close of Q.ANT's Series A, which brought total funding to $80M ([Tech.eu, 2025-10-30](https://tech.eu/2025/10/30/qant-secures-additional-investment-from-duquesne-family-office-bringing-total-funding-to-80m/)). It is the deal most squarely in the owner's thesis, and it wasn't in the brief.\n- **He reads the cycle as late.** By early 2026 he called AI trading \"disturbingly heated\" and said \"Definitely not early,\" \"Maybe 8th inning\" ([A Letter a Day #320](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and)). Yet he kept writing private lab checks (Standard Intelligence in April 2026, Flapping in October 2026). He rotates his public book but keeps adding small private options.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**medium.**\n- **For:** He is on the cap tables of the frontier-AI companies the owner cares about: Etched (AI chips), Standard Intelligence (new lab, Series A), Flapping Airplanes (new lab, $1B A), Q.ANT (photonic AI processors), and Perplexity. He is also an LP in Nova, a concentrated talent-first emerging manager, and was a management-company investor in Abstract. That is exactly the \"LP who backs young concentrated managers\" profile THESIS rates high.\n- **Against:** He doesn't source, lead, price, or take board seats in any AI deal found, and check sizes are never disclosed. As a co-investor signal he lags the leads. His private-side operator, Warsh, has left for the Fed.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** Etched, Standard Intelligence A, Flapping Airplanes A, Q.ANT A, plus the Nova LP stake.\n- **Would he back someone like me?** Plausibly, as an individual LP. The Nova evidence is a single firm-site line, and the Abstract stake was a management-company purchase reached through an Ovitz/Andreessen intro chain ([Colossus](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). Both suggest he enters through trusted introducers, not through cold outreach.\n\n## Background\n- Worked at Soros Fund Management; Scott Bessent worked with him there from 1991 to 2000 ([Barron's via itiger, 2026-02-02](https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848)).\n- Duquesne Capital's record: an average annual return of about 30% with no losing year over roughly three decades, per secondary coverage ([Hustle Fund](https://www.hustlefund.vc/post/angel-squad-stanley-druckenmiller-investments-the-macro-master-who-generated-30-annual-returns-without-a-single-down-year)).\n- Now runs Duquesne Family Office, which manages his personal fortune. Its listed equity book is about $4B ([Barron's via itiger](https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848)).\n- Private capital sits partly in Juggernaut Fund LP, described as \"the private partnership through which Stanley Druckenmiller manages his personal fortune\" ([MarketWatch via itiger, 2026-05-07](https://www.itiger.com/news/2633141932)).\n- I didn't fetch a primary source for education or specific founding years. Open question.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found (Duquesne has no public site found) | 13F filings are the only regular disclosure |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No personal account found |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Frequent guest: CNBC Squawk Box, Bloomberg, Sohn, In Good Company, Morgan Stanley Hard Lessons |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-05 — Sohn Investment Conference — AI is real and could be as impactful as the internet (secondary reporting only, [Trusted Insight](https://www.thetrustedinsight.com/investment-news/stan-druckenmiller-david-tepper-lead-family-offices-betting-on-ai-20230516697/)).\n- 2023-06 — Bloomberg Invest — \"unlike crypto, I think AI is real\"; \"It could be as transformative as the Internet\"; AI names had \"dominated the long portfolio for five or six months\" ([Acquirer's Multiple excerpt](https://acquirersmultiple.com/?p=38566)).\n- 2024-05-07 — CNBC Squawk Box — the young partner's Nvidia call; \"AI might be a little overhyped now but under-hyped long term\" ([CNBC transcript](https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html)).\n- 2024-11 — In Good Company with Nicolai Tangen (Norges Bank) — analysts spotted Stanford and MIT engineers moving from crypto to AI; Nvidia was \"picks and shovels\"; the timing was \"just total luck\" ([excerpt](https://acquirersmultiple.com/2024/11/stanley-druckenmiller-why-im-bullish-and-cautious-on-ai-investment); [episode](https://shows.acast.com/in-good-company-with-nicolai-tangen/episodes/highlights-stan-druckenmiller)).\n- 2025-01 — CNBC — AI adopters that cut costs will outperform; expects AGI by 2035 (secondary: [X clip post](https://x.com/timothyhliu5/status/1882242636846928227)).\n- 2026-02/03 — Morgan Stanley *Hard Lessons* with Iliana Bouzali — \"where the kids are going,\" Palantir 2008–09, the prostate-pathology company he co-started, and AI as \"8th inning\" ([transcription](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and); [MS page](https://www.morganstanley.com/insights/videos/hard-lessons/duquesne-stan-druckenmiller-iliana-bouzali), timed out when fetched).\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** AI \"could rhyme with the internet\": overhyped short term, underhyped long term ([CNBC 2024](https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html)). He started with \"picks and shovels\" (Nvidia), then moved to infrastructure bottlenecks (memory, Broadcom, TSMC) and later rotated out (Q2 2026 13F: exited Micron, Intel, Broadcom) ([24/7 Wall St](https://247wallst.com/investing/2026/08/20/billionaire-stanley-druckenmiller-dumped-micron-intel-and-broadcom-here-are-the-ai-infrastructure-stocks-he-bought-instead/)).\n- **Contrarian views:** He says anyone predicting AI job losses \"with conviction suffers from arrogance\" ([A Letter a Day #320](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and)).\n- **Calls right:** Nvidia bought in fall 2022, before ChatGPT ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html)); Palantir privately in 2008–09.\n- **Calls wrong (self-admitted):** Selling all his Nvidia, which he called a \"big mistake\" (secondary: [NBC Chicago](https://nbcchicago.com/news/business/money-report/stanley-druckenmiller-says-hes-licking-my-wounds-from-selling-nvidia-too-soon/3576080)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Morgan Stanley *Hard Lessons* transcript (A Letter a Day #320): his venture heuristic, the young-partner AI story, and his current AI caution. https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and\n2. CNBC Squawk Box transcript, 2024-05-07: the Nvidia origin story in his own words. https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html\n3. CNBC on Toggle AI (2023-06-06): the one AI company where he was first money, and why. https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/06/a-harvard-econ-phd-whiz-teaching-generative-ai-to-invest-for-everyone.html\n4. Barron's, \"The Druckenmiller Connection\" (2026-02-02): Warsh's private-equity role at Duquesne. https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no personal social account, and his public voice is TV, conference and podcast interviews.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Palantir | private (undisclosed) | 2008–09 (his account) | no evidence | not named | [Hard Lessons transcript](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and) |\n| Toggle AI / Reflexivity | first investor (pre-seed or seed, undated) | before 2023-06 | first money; lead not stated | not named | [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/06/a-harvard-econ-phd-whiz-teaching-generative-ai-to-invest-for-everyone.html) |\n| Reflexivity | Series B, $30M | 2024-10-28 | no (Greycroft, Interactive Brokers) | Greg Coffey, General Catalyst, SoftBank LatAm | [HedgeFundAlpha](https://hedgefundalpha.com/news/reflexivity-raises-30-million-series-b-funding/) |\n| Hebbia | Series A, $30M (blog also says some \"joined the seed\") | 2022-09-27 | no (Index, Mike Volpi) | Radical, Kevin Warsh, Jerry Yang, Ram Shriram, Alexandr Wang, Pieter Abbeel, others | [Hebbia blog](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of) |\n| Perplexity | Series B1, $62.7M at ~$1.04B | 2024-04-23 | no (Daniel Gross) | Garry Tan, Dylan Field, Brad Gerstner, Jeff Bezos, NVIDIA, Karpathy, IVP, NEA | [Perplexity](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-launches-enterprise-pro) |\n| Etched | undisclosed (one of four unannounced financings 2024–26) | by 2026-06-30 | no | Thiel; Ribbit, Jump, HRT, Two Sigma, Stripes on cap table | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/), [etched.com](https://www.etched.com/) |\n| Q.ANT (photonic AI processors) | Series A second close (total $80M) | 2025-10-30 | no (Cherry, UVC, imec.xpand existing leads); goldi.ai labels Duquesne \"Lead\" (aggregator conflict) | L-Bank, TRUMPF, Verve, others | [Tech.eu](https://tech.eu/2025/10/30/qant-secures-additional-investment-from-duquesne-family-office-bringing-total-funding-to-80m/) |\n| Standard Intelligence | Series A, $75M | 2026-04-30 | no (Sequoia, Spark) | Karpathy, Milan Kovac | [si.inc](https://si.inc/posts/a/) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Series A, $1B (via Duquesne Family Office) | 2026-10-21 | no (Index, Lux) | Kravis, Malka, Steven Cohen, Nova Global (SPV) | [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/) |\n| Precision Neuroscience (BCI) | Series C / Series D | 2024-12 / 2026-09 | participant | Pershing Square, B Capital (D) | aggregator only: [goldi.ai](https://goldi.ai/intelligence/vc/duquesne-family-office), [Tracxn](https://tracxn.com/d/single-family-office/duquesne-family-office/__Xt4unrgbtvrRfmvJIBn9r4N29E_yLyiTH51ZVzPQNxI) |\n| Unnamed AI prostate-pathology company | co-founder (\"we had started\") | undated | n/a | not named | [Hard Lessons transcript](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and) |\n| **LP / GP-stake** Nova Global (Koko Xs) | LP / backer | by 2026 | n/a | Andreessen, Kravis, Malka | [kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/) |\n| **GP-stake** Abstract Ventures management company | 20% for $10M round (his share undisclosed) | 2017 | no | Andreessen, Dixon, Sacks, Rabois, Hartz, Ackman, Warsh, Ovitz | [Colossus](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n\nExcluded: Iambic Therapeutics. The October 2026 IPO drew a non-binding indication of interest that Duquesne and ARK shared ([Renaissance Capital](https://www.renaissancecapital.com/IPO-Center/News/122239/small-molecule-biotech-iambic-therapeutics-priced-ipo-at-16-the-midpoint)). That is public-market, not venture.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Toggle / Reflexivity:** Jan Szilagyi joined Duquesne right after his economics PhD and spent most of his career there. He then co-founded Toggle with Giuseppe Sette, with Druckenmiller as first investor ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/06/a-harvard-econ-phd-whiz-teaching-generative-ai-to-invest-for-everyone.html)). This is the alum-spinout path, the only \"first in\" case found.\n- **Nvidia (public, but it explains his AI sourcing):** In fall 2022 a young partner, who had brought in people from an AI network in Palo Alto, told him to buy Nvidia. He bought \"a pretty good chunk,\" and ChatGPT launched weeks later ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html); [In Good Company excerpt](https://acquirersmultiple.com/2024/11/stanley-druckenmiller-why-im-bullish-and-cautious-on-ai-investment)).\n- **Abstract (2017):** Naimi's intro chain ran Cyan Banister → Kevin Hartz → Rabois and Dixon → Andreessen → Ovitz → Ackman, Warsh, Druckenmiller, Sacks ([Colossus](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). It shows that his GP-side bets come through elite intro chains.\n- **Flapping Airplanes A (2026-10):** Duquesne appears among the A's individual backers alongside two other Nova backers, Kravis and Malka. Nova ran a dedicated SPV. Whether Nova brought him in or he brought Nova in is unknown (see `firms/nova-global.md`).\n- **Etched:** I couldn't find when he entered or how. The Etched seed was Primary-led in 2023-03. He is tied only to the \"four unannounced financings\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Kevin Warsh | Duquesne partner/adviser; ran private equity; co-investor in Hebbia and Abstract; left to become Fed chair in 2026 | 2011 | [Barron's via itiger](https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848), [CBS](https://www.cbsnews.com/amp/news/kevin-warsh-net-worth-fed-chair-nominee/) |\n| Unnamed \"young partner\" (Duquesne) | Brought the Palo Alto AI network and the Nvidia call | 2022 | [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html) |\n| Jan Szilagyi | Former Duquesne employee; Druckenmiller was first investor in his company | pre-2018 | [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/06/a-harvard-econ-phd-whiz-teaching-generative-ai-to-invest-for-everyone.html) |\n| Koko Xs / Nova Global | LP / backer | by 2026 | [kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/) |\n| Ramtin Naimi / Abstract | Management-company investor | 2017 | [Colossus](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Scott Bessent | Soros Fund Management colleague | 1991–2000 | [Barron's via itiger](https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848) |\n| Henry Kravis, Micky Malka | Fellow Nova backers and co-participants in the Flapping A | 2026 | [kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/), [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/) |\n| Andrej Karpathy | Co-angel at Etched, Standard Intelligence and Perplexity | 2024– | [si.inc](https://si.inc/posts/a/), [etched.com](https://www.etched.com/), [Perplexity](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-launches-enterprise-pro) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who is the \"young partner\" with the Palo Alto AI network? They likely route the private AI deals too. Does that partner now run private investing after Warsh's exit?\n- Which Etched round did he enter, at what price, and through whom (Ribbit/Malka? Nova? Thiel?)?\n- Hebbia: did he join the seed or the Series A? The blog folds both into one paragraph, and `companies/hebbia.md` records Series A.\n- Which company is the prostate-pathology business he \"started\"? Paige is a candidate, unconfirmed.\n- Was the Flapping exposure Duquesne direct, through Nova's SPV, or both?\n- Did Duquesne lead any Q.ANT tranche (goldi.ai says \"Lead\" in 2025-01), or only join the second close (Tech.eu)?\n- Is he an LP in other emerging AI managers besides Nova and the Abstract management company?\n- Check sizes: none disclosed anywhere.\n\n## Sources\n- https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and\n- https://www.morganstanley.com/insights/videos/hard-lessons/duquesne-stan-druckenmiller-iliana-bouzali (timed out)\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html\n- https://www.nbcconnecticut.com/news/business/money-report/stanley-druckenmiller-cut-his-nvidia-stake-in-late-march-says-ai-may-be-a-bit-overhyped-short-term/3284318/\n- https://acquirersmultiple.com/2024/11/stanley-druckenmiller-why-im-bullish-and-cautious-on-ai-investment\n- https://acquirersmultiple.com/?p=38566\n- https://acquirersmultiple.com/?p=43973\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/06/a-harvard-econ-phd-whiz-teaching-generative-ai-to-invest-for-everyone.html\n- https://hedgefundalpha.com/news/reflexivity-raises-30-million-series-b-funding/\n- https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of\n- https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-launches-enterprise-pro\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/\n- https://www.etched.com/\n- https://si.inc/posts/a/\n- https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/\n- https://tech.eu/2025/10/30/qant-secures-additional-investment-from-duquesne-family-office-bringing-total-funding-to-80m/\n- https://goldi.ai/intelligence/vc/duquesne-family-office (aggregator)\n- https://tracxn.com/d/single-family-office/duquesne-family-office/__Xt4unrgbtvrRfmvJIBn9r4N29E_yLyiTH51ZVzPQNxI (aggregator)\n- https://kokoxs.com/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2146901/000123191926000844/primary_doc.xml\n- https://iq.wiki/wiki/flapping-airplanes (aggregator)\n- https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/\n- https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848\n- https://www.itiger.com/news/2633141932\n- https://www.cbsnews.com/amp/news/kevin-warsh-net-worth-fed-chair-nominee/\n- https://www.renaissancecapital.com/IPO-Center/News/122239/small-molecule-biotech-iambic-therapeutics-priced-ipo-at-16-the-midpoint\n- https://247wallst.com/investing/2026/08/20/billionaire-stanley-druckenmiller-dumped-micron-intel-and-broadcom-here-are-the-ai-infrastructure-stocks-he-bought-instead/\n- https://nbcchicago.com/news/business/money-report/stanley-druckenmiller-says-hes-licking-my-wounds-from-selling-nvidia-too-soon/3576080\n- https://www.beckershospitalreview.com/artificial-intelligence/paige-ai-launches-with-25m-in-funding-data-license-agreement-with-memorial-sloan-kettering.html\n- https://www.hustlefund.vc/post/angel-squad-stanley-druckenmiller-investments-the-macro-master-who-generated-30-annual-returns-without-a-single-down-year\n", "person:stefan-dyckerhoff": "\n# Stefan Dyckerhoff\n\n## One-line take\nThe Sutter Hill MD who quietly builds the AI data-center networking and interconnect stack (Astera Labs, Enfabrica, Celero, nEye, Aria Networks), often by incubating the company and sometimes by running it himself.\n\n## Crux\nAn ex-Juniper systems executive (employee #33) who invests the Sutter Hill way: incubate in-house, put a partner in the CEO seat if needed, then lead or overfund every follow-on. He ran SiFive from inception to Aug 2017 [src](https://www.sifive.com/press/sifive-appoints-naveed-sherwani-as-ceo) and Lacework from Nov 2017 [src](https://www.globalsecuritymag.fr/Lacework-Introduces-New-Chief,20171107,74972.html) without leaving the firm. His edge is technical depth in networking and silicon, plus a network of chip operators (Apstra, Inphi/Marvell, ClariPhy). His constraint is a low public profile: Sutter Hill rarely does press, so almost all his on-record words are press-release quotes. He would not be a passive minority investor in a lab-style mega-seed. His pattern is owning the company from creation.\n\n## Differential insight\n- He was **SiFive's founding CEO**, holding \"the top spot at the company since its inception\" until Aug 2017 [src](https://www.sifive.com/press/sifive-appoints-naveed-sherwani-as-ceo). Most deal databases only record Sutter Hill as SiFive's first investor.\n- 2016 thesis on custom silicon: \"We want to radically drive down human involvement in design costs\". He argued processor IP is overvalued and the SoC implementation is the hard part [src](https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/).\n- Hidden link: \"deeply involved with P.A. Semi prior to the Apple acquisition\" [src](https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/).\n- Timing read in 2016: a \"definite uptick in developer registration activity\" for RISC-V after SoftBank bought ARM [src](https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/).\n- Check-size habit: in Atmosic's $72M round (2022) Sutter Hill chose to \"double down with a super pro-rata investment\" [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/51273/atmosic-funding.html). It led Atmosic's B, the 2022 round and the 2025 D [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2025030303/atmosic-technologies-secures-40m-funding-led-by-sutter-hill-ventures/).\n- Decision rule (Aria, 2026): conviction is \"grounded in the technology, the momentum, and above all, the founders\". Customers were \"already ordering\" before the $125M round [src](https://datacentrenews.uk/story/aria-launches-deep-networking-platform-with-usd-125-million).\n- Technical bet (nEye, 2026): away from \"complex mechanical assemblies to a foundry-compatible wafer-scale process\". He sizes optical circuit switching at $3B+ within three years [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026041401/neye-ai-secures-80-million-series-c-to-scale-optical-circuit-switching-for-ai-infrastructure/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is the Sutter Hill partner closest to AI semiconductors and compute: Astera Labs (board since Nov 2019, before the AI boom), Enfabrica (board since 2020 per Astera IR bio, secondary), Celero (seed and A, 2024), nEye (led C, 2026) and Aria Networks (led $125M first round, 2026). Many of these rounds sit at the $8M-$125M scale where a $20M co-investment is realistic. Repeat co-investors Atreides, Valor and CapitalG show where the follow-on capital comes from.\n\n## Background\n- Duke (BS), Stanford (MS) [src](https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/profile/stefan-dyckerhoff/).\n- Juniper Networks from 1997 as employee #33 on the founding engineering team. Rose to EVP of the Platform Systems Division [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=681511).\n- Lacework's 2017 release also says he led Cisco's enterprise security and routing business before Juniper [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/lacework-names-stefan-dyckerhoff-chief-executive-officer-300550869.html). The dates conflict with TechCrunch (see Open questions).\n- Joined Sutter Hill as Managing Director in Oct 2012, alongside Sam Pullara [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=681511).\n- SiFive founding CEO, 2015 to Aug 2017 [src](https://www.sifive.com/press/sifive-appoints-naveed-sherwani-as-ceo).\n- Lacework President and CEO from Nov 2017. Dan Hubbard took over in 2019 [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/security-industry-veteran-dan-hubbard-named-ceo-of-lacework-300862324.html).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found (Sutter Hill site has no bios) | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found; one 2018 Infosecurity Magazine column [src](https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/opinions/questions-ceo-moving-cloud/) | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://linkedin.com/in/stefandyckerhoff | not checked |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2016-10: SemiWiki interview (SiFive strategy) [src](https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/). Customization thesis, design-cost automation, China deprioritised.\n- 2019-12: RISC-V Summit talk \"How RISC-V made the Quick Jump from Academia to Industry... a Venture Capital perspective\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CfFMLG1hF6U). Not mined: YouTube blocked transcript access and the slides link on riscv.org returns 404.\n- 2026-04: Aria Networks launch video [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LjMWrTDkKrA). His appearance is unconfirmed.\n- No podcasts found.\n\n## How they think\n- Compute that benefits from customization beats general-purpose IP. Automate the design labor [src](https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/).\n- Platform shifts break the adjacent layer: cloud broke security (Lacework), and AI clusters break networking and optics (Celero, nEye, Aria) [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/lacework-names-stefan-dyckerhoff-chief-executive-officer-300550869.html).\n- Funds winners hard after the first check: super pro-rata in Atmosic, and kept investing in Astera's C and D.\n- Calls right: Astera Labs (backed in 2019-2020, IPO 2024). Lacework rose high, then sold for far less than its peak (not covered here).\n\n## Read these first\n1. SemiWiki 2016 interview: the only long-form record of his thinking [src](https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/).\n2. Infosecurity Magazine column (2018): his operator stance [src](https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/opinions/questions-ceo-moving-cloud/).\n3. Aria Networks launch coverage (2026): his current rule for leading a big round [src](https://datacentrenews.uk/story/aria-launches-deep-networking-platform-with-usd-125-million).\n4. nEye Series C release (2026) [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026041401/neye-ai-secures-80-million-series-c-to-scale-optical-circuit-switching-for-ai-infrastructure/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No active X or Substack presence was located.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| SiFive | Series A ($5M) | 2015-09 | yes | | [src](https://seedtable.com/companies/sifive/funding-rounds) (aggregator) |\n| SiFive | Series C ($50.6M) | 2018-04 | co-lead | Spark Capital | [src](https://www.sifive.com/posts/2018/04/02/sifive-secures-50-million-funding-to-advance-risc-v-based-semiconductors/) |\n| Lacework | Series B ($24M) | 2018-08 | Sutter Hill led | Liberty Global Ventures, AME Cloud Ventures | [src](https://zingy-fr.netlify.app/fr/press-release/lacework-closes-24-million-series-b-financing) |\n| Atmosic | Series B ($28.5M) | 2019-12 | Sutter Hill led | Clear Ventures, Walden | [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/47306/atmosic-funding.html) |\n| Astera Labs | Series B (board Nov 2019) | 2020-04 | | Intel Capital, Avigdor Willenz | [src](https://dev.aiwire.net/2020/04/22/astera-labs-closes-series-b-funding-announces-manufacturing-partnerships/) |\n| Astera Labs | Series C ($50M at $950M) | 2021-10 | no (Fidelity) | Atreides, Valor | [src](https://www.businesswirechina.com/en/news/48219.html) |\n| Atmosic | Undisclosed ($72M, super pro-rata) | 2022-01 | Sutter Hill led | | [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/51273/atmosic-funding.html) |\n| Celero Communications | Seed + Series A ($40M) | 2024 | Sutter Hill led | Valor, Atreides, Maverick Silicon | [src](https://pulse2.com/celero-communications-140-million-raised-to-expand-ai-infrastructure-connectivity) |\n| Atmosic | Series D ($40M) | 2025-03 | Sutter Hill led | Clear Ventures | [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2025030303/atmosic-technologies-secures-40m-funding-led-by-sutter-hill-ventures/) |\n| Aria Networks | Series A ($125M) | 2026-04 | yes | Atreides, Valor, Eclipse | [src](https://datacentrenews.uk/story/aria-launches-deep-networking-platform-with-usd-125-million) |\n| nEye | Series C ($80M) | 2026-04 | yes | CapitalG, M12, Socratic Partners | [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026041401/neye-ai-secures-80-million-series-c-to-scale-optical-circuit-switching-for-ai-infrastructure/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **SiFive**: Sutter Hill funded the Berkeley RISC-V team, and Dyckerhoff served as CEO from inception. The first win was a 2-month custom-core project for Microsemi's end customer [src](https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/). He handed over to Naveed Sherwani in Aug 2017 and kept the board seat [src](https://www.sifive.com/press/sifive-appoints-naveed-sherwani-as-ceo).\n- **Lacework**: incubated at Sutter Hill. He became CEO in Nov 2017 while remaining MD [src](https://www.globalsecuritymag.fr/Lacework-Introduces-New-Chief,20171107,74972.html). Sutter Hill then led the $24M B at about 20 production customers [src](https://zingy-fr.netlify.app/fr/press-release/lacework-closes-24-million-series-b-financing).\n- **Aria Networks**: founders Mansour Karam and Subhachandra Chandra became Sutter Hill EIRs after the Apstra exit to Juniper. Sutter Hill then led the $125M first round, and Dyckerhoff joined the board with Atreides' Gavin Baker [src](https://www.implicator.ai/aria-networks-sells-the-same-chips-as-arista-its-125-million-bet-is-the-software/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sutter Hill Ventures | Managing Director; 10-25% owner of adviser | 2012 | [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/162959/PDF/162959.pdf) |\n| Sam Pullara | Joined Sutter Hill as MD the same day | 2012 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=681511) |\n| Gavin Baker (Atreides) | Co-board member at Aria; Atreides also co-invests in Astera and Celero | 2026 | [src](https://datacentrenews.uk/story/aria-launches-deep-networking-platform-with-usd-125-million) |\n| Rajeev Madhavan (Clear Ventures) | Co-investor and co-board at Atmosic | 2019 | [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/51273/atmosic-funding.html) |\n| Mansour Karam | Sutter Hill EIR, then Aria founder | 2021+ | [src](https://www.implicator.ai/aria-networks-sells-the-same-chips-as-arista-its-125-million-bet-is-the-software/) |\n| Krste Asanovic / SiFive founders | Ran SiFive as CEO with them | 2015 | [src](https://www.sifive.com/press/sifive-appoints-naveed-sherwani-as-ceo) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Cisco stint: the Lacework release puts it before Juniper, but TechCrunch says he joined Juniper from Stanford in 1997. When did he work at Cisco?\n- What did he say in the 2019 RISC-V Summit VC talk? The video exists but no transcript was reachable, and the slides URL is dead.\n- An eWeek 2018 interview reportedly has his Lacework customer count and competitor view, but it sits behind Cloudflare and was not read.\n- Enfabrica board role (from 2020 per Astera IR, secondary): did he lead its Series A?\n- Did he sit on ThousandEyes, Vera or Benu boards? These rest only on aggregator listings.\n\n## Sources\n- https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/\n- https://www.sifive.com/press/sifive-appoints-naveed-sherwani-as-ceo\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/lacework-names-stefan-dyckerhoff-chief-executive-officer-300550869.html\n- https://www.globalsecuritymag.fr/Lacework-Introduces-New-Chief,20171107,74972.html\n- https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/opinions/questions-ceo-moving-cloud/\n- https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/profile/stefan-dyckerhoff/\n- https://zingy-fr.netlify.app/fr/press-release/lacework-closes-24-million-series-b-financing\n- https://techcrunch.com/?p=681511\n- https://us.design-reuse.com/news/47306/atmosic-funding.html\n- https://us.design-reuse.com/news/51273/atmosic-funding.html\n- https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2025030303/atmosic-technologies-secures-40m-funding-led-by-sutter-hill-ventures/\n- https://dev.aiwire.net/2020/04/22/astera-labs-closes-series-b-funding-announces-manufacturing-partnerships/\n- https://www.businesswirechina.com/en/news/48219.html\n- https://pulse2.com/celero-communications-140-million-raised-to-expand-ai-infrastructure-connectivity\n- https://datacentrenews.uk/story/aria-launches-deep-networking-platform-with-usd-125-million\n- https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026041401/neye-ai-secures-80-million-series-c-to-scale-optical-circuit-switching-for-ai-infrastructure/\n- https://www.implicator.ai/aria-networks-sells-the-same-chips-as-arista-its-125-million-bet-is-the-software/\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/162959/PDF/162959.pdf\n", "person:stephane-kurgan": "\n# Stephane Kurgan\n\n## One-line take\nAn operator venture partner at Index London (ex-COO of King, the Candy Crush maker). They add a senior name to applied-AI rounds that a full partner runs day to day, here Scope (industrial inspection AI, $20M, 2026) and Arondite (defence AI, $12M seed, 2025). They are not a route into labs, pre-training or compute. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** Operating credibility at scale. The Index bio credits them with helping oversee King's IPO and its later sale to Activision Blizzard, after earlier roles across sales, product, finance and management, plus McKinsey. Founders who want scaling help get a former COO on the board. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n- **Constraint:** The title is Venture Partner. On both AI deals they co-sign with Bastian Hasslinger, an Index partner who is on every European AI-native automation deal in that cluster. That suggests Hasslinger runs these deals and Kurgan is the senior operator voice (an inference from the bylines, not stated by Index). [Scope](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/) [Arondite](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/)\n- **What they back:** Applied AI in physical or regulated industries, where the founders learned the trade by hand, and consumer gaming (Dream Games, Bigger Games), which fits the King background. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n- **What they'd never do (inferred):** Nothing on record suggests they would lead a pre-product research lab or chip company. No such deal is named on their page.\n\n## Differential insight\nTheir public footprint is thin. There is no X account on the Index page, and I found no podcast or talk in this pass because the web-search budget was used up (see Open questions). Everything below comes from the two Index Perspectives they co-signed and the trade press on those deals.\n- **Founder archetype (Scope, with Hasslinger):** \"There's a type of founder we keep coming back to at Index.\" The passage describes founders obsessed with a problem they found themselves. Scope's founders cold-called inspection firms and went on inspections. [BusinessCloud, 2026-05-20](https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/)\n- **Trajectory over metrics:** The Scope post says \"that trajectory, more than any single metric, is what convinced us\". It is an explicit statement that they bet on the founders' rate of improvement, not on traction numbers. [Index, 2026-05-19](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/)\n- **Index tracked Scope before investing:** The post says \"we've had a front-row seat to their evolution\". Index watched the company over time and was not the first money. [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/)\n- **Timing (Scope): late, not first.** Susa Ventures and Entrepreneurs First were the earlier backers. CEO Jonny Low said they \"have doubled down in every round\". Index came in at the $20M round (£15M, which press calls a Series A). [BusinessCloud](https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/)\n- **Timing (Arondite): known early, but second in.** Index says \"We've known Arondite since inception\". Concept Ventures still led an unannounced $2.25M pre-seed in 2024, before Index put about $10M into the $12.25M seed. [Index, 2025-05-01](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/) [Calibre Defence, 2025-05-06](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/)\n- **Industrial-data thesis:** Scope is pitched as \"a live intelligence layer for physical infrastructure\". The AI workflows save inspectors time first, then build a proprietary dataset on how assets degrade. [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** Their AI deals are applied, not frontier:\n- Scope: AI-native automation for industrial inspection, closest to the Serval end of THESIS.md, but a Series A with Index arriving after Susa Ventures and EF.\n- Arondite: defence AI and human-machine teaming.\n\nThey have no pre-training, new-lab or AI-chip deals. Hasslinger and Hannah Seal (both profiled) are the better Index London contacts for AI-native automation. Kurgan matters mainly as a co-signer who validates operator-heavy, physical-world AI companies, and possibly as a gaming and consumer connection given the King background.\n\n## Background\n- BSc Economics, Université Libre de Bruxelles; diploma in International Relations, Johns Hopkins SAIS; MBA, INSEAD. Dates are not on the firm page. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n- McKinsey & Co. consultant; then sales, product, finance and senior-management roles at European tech companies. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n- COO of King Digital Entertainment, which made Candy Crush Saga. The bio says they helped oversee the IPO and the later acquisition by Activision Blizzard. Dates are not given on the page. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n- Joined Index Ventures as Venture Partner in 2020. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/ | Index team page, 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Only co-authored Index Perspectives |\n| X | nothing found | Index page links only the firm account |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/stephane-kurgan-149181 | Linked from the Index page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found in this pass. Web search was unavailable (budget exhausted), so this is unverified, not a confirmed absence.\n\n## How they think\n- **Founders who earned the insight by hand.** Scope's founders cold-called and joined inspections, and Arondite's CEO came from the British Army, Palantir and Helsing. [Scope](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/) [Calibre](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/)\n- **Trajectory over metrics** (Scope post).\n- **Purpose-driven leadership:** the Index bio stresses a strong belief in it. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n- **Calls right or wrong:** nothing sourced beyond the deals themselves.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Our investment in Scope](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/): their founder screen and the industrial-AI data thesis.\n2. [Arondite raises $12M](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/): the defence-AI rationale, co-signed with Seal and Hasslinger.\n3. [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/): the operator background and full portfolio list.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. They have no public X presence linked from Index.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Scope (industrial inspection AI) | $20M (£15M), press calls it Series A | 2026-05-19 | Index led; Kurgan co-signed with Bastian Hasslinger | Susa Ventures and Entrepreneurs First were earlier backers (\"doubled down in every round\"). No A co-investors named | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/) [BusinessCloud](https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/) |\n| Arondite (defence AI) | Seed, $12.25M (Index about $10M) | 2025-05-01 | Index led (per Calibre); co-signed with Hannah Seal and Bastian Hasslinger | Concept Ventures led the earlier $2.25M pre-seed (2024) | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/) [Calibre](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/) |\n| Dream Games | not stated | not stated | not stated | | [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/) |\n| Bigger Games | not stated | not stated | not stated | | [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/) |\n| Birdie (healthcare) | not stated | not stated | not stated | | [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/) |\n| Codat (fintech) | not stated | not stated | not stated | | [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/) |\n| Fonoa (fintech) | not stated | not stated | not stated | | [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Scope:** Founded by Jonny Low (CEO) and Jakob Cassiman. Index followed them for some time (\"front-row seat\") before leading the $20M round. Susa Ventures and EF were first and re-upped each round. Index was not first. [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/) [BusinessCloud](https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/)\n- **Arondite:** Will Blyth (CEO; ex-British Army, Palantir, Helsing) and Rob Underhill (CTO; ex-BAE Systems lead engineer, founder of Garrison Technologies). Index knew them from founding, but Concept Ventures led the 2024 pre-seed. Index then wrote about $10M of the $12.25M seed. The intro path is not stated. [Calibre](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/) [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bastian Hasslinger (Index) | Co-signer on Scope and Arondite | 2025 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/) |\n| Hannah Seal (Index) | Co-signer on Arondite | 2025 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/) |\n| King Digital Entertainment | Former COO | before 2020 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/) |\n| Jonny Low, Jakob Cassiman (Scope) | Portfolio founders | 2026 | [BusinessCloud](https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/) |\n| Will Blyth, Rob Underhill (Arondite) | Portfolio founders | 2025 | [Calibre](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/) |\n| Susa Ventures; Entrepreneurs First | Earlier backers of Scope | before 2026-05 | [BusinessCloud](https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/) |\n| Concept Ventures | Led Arondite pre-seed before Index | 2024 | [Calibre](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Any podcast, talk or interview, especially on King, operator-to-VC or AI in gaming? Web search was exhausted in this run, so this needs a follow-up pass.\n- Are they on the boards of Scope or Arondite, or is Hasslinger?\n- Did their King network source Dream Games or Bigger Games? Was Index a King investor? Neither is checked here.\n- Is Scope CEO Jonny Low an alumnus of the AI lab Conjecture? This is unconfirmed (flagged in the Hasslinger profile).\n- Dates of the King COO tenure. LinkedIn would have them.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/\n- https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/\n- https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/\n", "person:stephanie-zhan": "\n# Stephanie Zhan\n\n*Research note (2026-11-10): this run's web-search budget was used up before it started, so everything here comes from fetching known URLs (Sequoia pages and articles, TechCrunch, company blogs, press already in the repo). Her X announcement of the new firm returned HTTP 402, so the firm's name, size and team are still unknown. **Disambiguation:** a different investor, Brian Zhan (CRV, now Striker Venture Partners), sits on several of the same cap tables (Reflection, Skild, Ricursive). Repo notes that say \"Gazor and Zhan\", \"Zhan's same-month partnering\" or \"CRV partnered (Zhan)\" refer to Brian Zhan, not Stephanie.*\n\n## One-line take\nShe was Sequoia's seed partner on the first institutional rounds of three frontier-type labs: Skild AI (robotics foundation model, seed 2023), Reflection AI (DeepMind RL founders, seed 2024) and Ricursive Intelligence (AlphaChip founders, first round 2025). In October 2026 she left to launch her own firm ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins.** She commits at formation and commits fast. At Skild she met the two CMU professors on a Thursday and Sequoia was their partner by the following Tuesday ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/)). At Ricursive, Sequoia led \"their very first round from the formation\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ricursive-intelligence-a-premier-frontier-lab-pioneering-ai-for-chip-design/)). At Reflection, Sequoia calls itself the \"first and long-term partner\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-reflection-toward-superintelligence-with-autonomous-coding/)). Each of these seeds was followed within months by a $105M-$300M round from Lightspeed and others at a 3-5x+ step-up.\n- **Her filter**, as her own deal posts put it: researcher-founders who already built the canonical system in their field (AlphaGo/Gemini RL, AlphaChip, CMU robot learning), pitched as a \"frontier AI lab\" for a category, plus a compute-bottleneck story (\"chip design is the compute bottleneck\").\n- **Constraint.** The edge was partly Sequoia's balance sheet and brand (Arc, follow-on re-ups in Skild's A, Reflection's B and Ricursive's A). Her new firm's size is unknown. If it's a concentrated fund, she can't anchor $300M+ lab rounds alone and will look more like Striker than Sequoia.\n- **What she'd never do (inferred).** Nothing in her posts suggests late entry. Every AI deal she's named on is a seed or first round. Treat this as a pattern, not a stated rule.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Five-day seed decision on Skild.** She writes \"I first met Deepak and Abhinav on a Thursday afternoon\" and \"we were partners by Tuesday.\" This was during their initial seed raise in 2023, but the partnership was only made public in July 2024 alongside the $300M Series A ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/skild-ai/)). Sequoia then followed on as a participant in that A, which Lightspeed, Coatue, SoftBank and Bezos led ([src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a)).\n- **Ricursive: first round \"from the formation\", but Striker also claims inception.** Her post says Sequoia was \"leading their very first round from the formation of Ricursive Intelligence\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ricursive-intelligence-a-premier-frontier-lab-pioneering-ai-for-chip-design/), 2025-12-02). Striker's portfolio page also lists Ricursive as \"Partnered 2025, At Inception\" ([src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio)). So two firms claim the founding round, and it's unknown whether Striker was in Sequoia's $35M round or a separate one. The timing: a $35M seed at $750M (announced 2025-12-03) ([src](https://convergedigest.com/ricursive-intelligence-aims-to-accelerate-semiconductor-design/)), then a Lightspeed-led $300M A at $4B on 2026-01-26, under two months later, with Sequoia re-upping ([src](https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/)).\n- **Compute thesis in her own words.** \"Compute is the most valuable resource in the AI world we live in today\" and \"chip design is the compute bottleneck\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ricursive-intelligence-a-premier-frontier-lab-pioneering-ai-for-chip-design/)). This is the AI-chips leg of THESIS.md, stated by the lead investor.\n- **Reflection: senior/junior pairing with Charlie Curnin, first money before the launch.** Sequoia's company page names both Zhan and Curnin, with Founded, Partnered and Arc all dated 2024 ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/)). The $25M seed (co-led with CRV) was only announced on 2025-03-07, the same day as the $105M A that CRV and Lightspeed co-led at about $555M ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956)). Curnin is also no longer on Sequoia's team page ([people/charlie-curnin.md](charlie-curnin.md)), which raises the question of whether Curnin joined her new firm.\n- **Robotics call made in July 2024.** \"A GPT-3 moment is coming to the world of robotics\" ([src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a)). Skild announced a $1.4B Series C led by SoftBank at over $14B in January 2026 ([src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs)).\n- **Tavus: she tried the product the night she met the founders.** Sequoia led the seed in late 2021, while the product was in alpha ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-tavus-ai-personalized-videos/)).\n- **The podcast came after the deal, not before it.** She co-hosted Sequoia's Training Data podcast with Sonya Huang, including an episode with Reflection's Misha Laskin, a founder Sequoia had already seeded ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-misha-laskin/)). Huang used the same show to build a relationship before a deal (David Silver, then Ineffable; see [sonya-huang](sonya-huang.md)). Zhan used it to amplify a portfolio company. No sourcing-by-podcast example of hers was found.\n- **Co-investor overlap with Brian Zhan's firms.** Not a relationship claim, just shared cap tables: CRV co-led the Reflection seed with Sequoia ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956)), CRV was in Skild's A ([src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a)), and Striker claims inception at Ricursive ([src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio)).\n- Not found: check sizes, who introduced her to any founder, deals she passed on, mistakes admitted, or the new firm's name, size or LPs.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Her named AI deals are the THESIS.md categories at the entry point THESIS.md cares about. Reflection AI is a new lab (seed 2024, now valued in the billions). Ricursive Intelligence is AI for chip design, a compute bottleneck (first round, 2025). Skild AI is a robotics foundation-model lab (seed 2023). As a newly independent manager she is both a **co-investor** (likely to be in the founding round of the next ex-DeepMind or ex-Google research team) and a **comparable** for an owner who writes ~$20M into large seeds. The key unknown is fund size: it decides whether she leads lab seeds or needs partners for them. Lower-fit parts of her record: Tavus (AI video, applied), Mach Industries (defense, with Shaun Maguire), and Linear, Middesk and Evy (non-AI SaaS, low/skip).\n\n## Background\n- Studied Computer Science at Stanford (degree year not listed). Grew up across Asia and learned to code building simple video games ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/)).\n- Joined Sequoia in 2015 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)). Became a Partner on the seed/early team; promotion dates not found ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/)).\n- Prior jobs before Sequoia: nothing found.\n- Announced in October 2026 that she is launching her own firm. TechCrunch says she \"has meanwhile left the firm entirely\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)). Sequoia's site still showed her page as of 2026-11-10.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/ | Legacy Sequoia bio; still live 2026-11-10. New firm site not found |\n| Substack / blog | Sequoia \"Partnering with...\" posts (Skild, Reflection, Ricursive, Tavus) | Deal announcement posts under her byline |\n| X | https://x.com/stephzhan | Firm announcement post: https://x.com/stephzhan/status/2113303172894867859 (fetch returned 402; follower count not checked) |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Training Data (Sequoia), co-host with Sonya Huang | e.g. https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-misha-laskin/ |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Training Data: \"Reflection AI's Misha Laskin on the AlphaGo Moment for LLMs\". Co-hosted with Sonya Huang; a portfolio founder ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-misha-laskin/)). The page metadata date (2026-11-09) is a site artifact; original air date not confirmed.\n- Training Data: \"Jim Fan on Nvidia's Embodied AI Lab...\". Co-hosted with Sonya Huang ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-jim-fan/)). No substantive investing statements were retrieved.\n- Talks (e.g. AI Ascent): nothing retrieved.\n\n## How they think\n- **Category-defining labs from the field's original builders.** Her Ricursive goal is \"to build the frontier AI lab defining this category\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ricursive-intelligence-a-premier-frontier-lab-pioneering-ai-for-chip-design/)).\n- **Compute scarcity as thesis**, applied to chip design ([same src]).\n- **Robotics as the next \"GPT-3 moment\"**, called in July 2024 ([src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a)).\n- **Founders first.** Her bio stresses ambition, agency and good character, and backing companies \"from idea to IPO and beyond\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/)).\n- **Calls she got right.** On step-ups since her entry: Skild seed (2023) to a Series C at over $14B (Jan 2026) ([src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs)). Reflection seed (2024) to a $2B B at $8B (Oct 2025) ([src](https://reflection.ai/blog/building-frontier-open-intelligencence)). Ricursive at $750M to $4B in under two months ([src](https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/)). Wrong calls: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Partnering with Ricursive Intelligence](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ricursive-intelligence-a-premier-frontier-lab-pioneering-ai-for-chip-design/) (2025-12-02): her compute and chip-design thesis, and the \"first round from formation\" claim.\n2. [Partnering with Skild](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/) (2024-07-09): the Thursday-to-Tuesday seed decision.\n3. [Partnering with Reflection](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-reflection-toward-superintelligence-with-autonomous-coding/) (2025-03-07, with Charlie Curnin): first-partner framing.\n4. [Training Data with Misha Laskin](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-misha-laskin/): how she talks with a lab founder.\n5. [TechCrunch on Sequoia's contraction](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/) (2026-11-04): context for her exit.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing retrieved. X was not fetchable (402).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ricursive Intelligence | Seed $35M at $750M (first round from formation) | 2025 (announced 2025-12-03) | Yes (Sequoia led) | None named in the announcement; Striker claims \"At Inception\" 2025 separately | [src](https://convergedigest.com/ricursive-intelligence-aims-to-accelerate-semiconductor-design/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ricursive-intelligence-a-premier-frontier-lab-pioneering-ai-for-chip-design/) |\n| Ricursive Intelligence | Series A $300M at $4B (follow-on) | 2026-01-26 | No (Lightspeed led) | DST Global, NVentures, Felicis, 49 Palms, Radical AI | [src](https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/) |\n| Reflection AI | Seed $25M | Partnered 2024 (Arc 2024); announced 2025-03-07 | Co-led with CRV | CRV; Sequoia co-partner Charlie Curnin | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/) |\n| Reflection AI | Series B $2B at $8B (Sequoia re-up) | 2025-10-09 | No (Nvidia led) | Lightspeed, CRV, DST, others; her personal role in the B not stated | [src](https://reflection.ai/blog/building-frontier-open-intelligencence) |\n| Skild AI | Seed (amount undisclosed) | 2023 (made public 2024-07-09) | unknown | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/skild-ai/) |\n| Skild AI | Series A $300M at $1.5B (follow-on) | 2024-07-09 | No (Lightspeed, Coatue, SoftBank, Bezos led) | Felicis, Menlo, General Catalyst, CRV, SV Angel, CMU, Amazon | [src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a) |\n| Tavus | Seed | 2021 | Yes (\"to lead their seed round\") | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-tavus-ai-personalized-videos/) |\n| Mach Industries | Pre-seed/seed (defense) | 2023 | unknown | Sequoia co-partner Shaun Maguire | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/) |\n| Linear | Pre-seed/seed (non-AI) | 2019 | unknown | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/) |\n| Middesk | Pre-seed/seed (non-AI) | 2019 | unknown | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/) |\n| Evy | Early (non-AI) | 2021 | unknown | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Skild AI (2023).** She met Deepak Pathak and Abhinav Gupta, both CMU Robotics Institute faculty, while they were raising their initial seed. She met them on a Thursday and Sequoia was their partner by the following Tuesday. Sequoia said it would join the board. The intro path is not stated ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/)).\n- **Reflection AI (2024).** Founded, partnered and in Arc all in 2024, with Zhan and Charlie Curnin as the Sequoia partners. The $25M seed was co-led with CRV and kept quiet until the March 2025 launch ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/), [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956)). How Sequoia met Laskin and Antonoglou is not stated. Huang separately says on-air \"We backed Ioannis\" ([see sonya-huang](sonya-huang.md)).\n- **Ricursive Intelligence (2025).** Founded and partnered in 2025. Sequoia led the founding round, and Zhan is the only Sequoia partner listed ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/ricursive-intelligence/)). Felicis says it knew co-founder Azalia Mirhoseini for years through its sponsorship of her Stanford lab ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/ricursive-intelligence-a-step-change-on-the-path-to-agi)). Zhan's intro path is not stated.\n- **Tavus (2021).** She tried the alpha product the night after meeting Hassaan Raza and Quinn Favret, and Sequoia led the seed ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-tavus-ai-personalized-videos/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sequoia Capital | Investor, then Partner on the seed/early team; left Oct 2026 | 2015-2026 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/) |\n| Charlie Curnin | Sequoia co-partner on Reflection AI | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/) |\n| Shaun Maguire | Sequoia co-partner on Mach Industries | 2023 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/) |\n| Sonya Huang | Training Data podcast co-host | unknown | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-misha-laskin/) |\n| Misha Laskin, Ioannis Antonoglou | Seed investor (Reflection AI) | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/) |\n| Anna Goldie, Azalia Mirhoseini | Led first round (Ricursive Intelligence) | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/ricursive-intelligence/) |\n| Deepak Pathak, Abhinav Gupta | Seed investor (Skild AI); Sequoia board seat stated | 2023 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/) |\n| Hassaan Raza, Quinn Favret | Led seed (Tavus) | 2021 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/tavus/) |\n| CRV | Seed co-lead on Reflection AI | 2024 | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956) |\n| Lightspeed Venture Partners | Next-round lead after her seeds (Reflection A, Skild A, Ricursive A) | 2024-2026 | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956), [src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a), [src](https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/) |\n| Striker Venture Partners | Also claims inception at Ricursive (same-round status unknown) | 2025 | [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio) |\n\n## Open questions\n1. **New firm's name, fund size, LPs and team.** Did Charlie Curnin join? The X post is at https://x.com/stephzhan/status/2113303172894867859 and couldn't be fetched.\n2. Was Striker in Sequoia's $35M Ricursive round, or did it come in separately? Who was actually first?\n3. Did Sequoia lead Skild's 2023 seed, and how big was it? Who else was in it?\n4. How did she meet Laskin and Antonoglou, the CMU founders, and Goldie and Mirhoseini: through Arc, Stanford, or a DeepMind or Google network?\n5. Will her new firm write into Reflection, Skild or Ricursive follow-ons, and will she keep any Sequoia board seats?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/\n- https://x.com/stephzhan/status/2113303172894867859 (not fetchable, 402)\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ricursive-intelligence-a-premier-frontier-lab-pioneering-ai-for-chip-design/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-reflection-toward-superintelligence-with-autonomous-coding/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-tavus-ai-personalized-videos/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/ricursive-intelligence/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/skild-ai/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/tavus/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-misha-laskin/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-jim-fan/\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956\n- https://convergedigest.com/ricursive-intelligence-aims-to-accelerate-semiconductor-design/\n- https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/\n- https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a\n- https://www.skild.ai/blogs\n- https://www.striker.vc/portfolio\n- https://reflection.ai/blog/building-frontier-open-intelligencence\n", "person:steve-bowsher": "\n# Steve Bowsher\n\n## One-line take\nHe has run In-Q-Tel's investing since 2006 and approves its deals, so his view of what agencies want explains why IQT shows up in a given AI or chip seed.\n\n## Crux\nHe is a former venture GP who now invests without a return target: \"we're not investing to try and drive financial returns\" [src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel). He wins access because IQT is not a rival for ownership, and he is constrained by agency demand and by a model built around pilots, not pricing. `check_capacity` is set to sub-10m as an assumption; no cheque size was found.\n\n## Differential insight\n- IQT's scorecard: \"we measure ourselves on that pilot rate and adoption rate\" [src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel).\n- On what he found most interesting in 2024: \"pretty interesting, but I think bio is, is even more interesting\" (generative AI versus AI for biology) [src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel).\n- Sourcing: \"top-tier venture capital firms who we've had successful co-investments with\" [src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nLow. Government strategic capital, not a co-investor peer; useful as a signal of agency demand in chips and compute seeds.\n\n## Background\nHarvard (magna cum laude); Stanford MBA; early employee at two startups; helped launch Destination E*TRADE; General Partner at InterWest Partners for eight years; at IQT led investment strategy from 2006 as Managing General Partner and EVP; now CEO [src](https://www.iqt.org/about/our-team).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.iqt.org/about/our-team | bio on the team page |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-09 — Tech'ed Up — Venture Capital Meets Mission Critical — https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel — transcript read; quotes above\n- NatSec Tech — Episode 86 — https://natsec-tech.simplecast.com/episodes/episode-86-steve-bowsher-on-how-in-q-tel-is-spurring-innovation — not read\n- All Quiet on the Second Front — Episode 53 — not read\n- Authority Magazine — 5 Things I Need To See Before Making A VC Investment — not read (fetch blocked)\n\n## How they think\n- Strategic, not financial: impact on agencies is the goal [src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel).\n- Pilots first: \"the heart of what we do is to, uh, facilitate the piloting of technology\" [src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel).\n\n## Read these first\n- The Tech'ed Up transcript [src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Palantir, Anduril, Databricks, MongoDB, Anaconda, Cloudera, Cleversafe | named in his IQT bio; rounds not stated | | no | | [src](https://www.iqt.org/about/our-team) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| In-Q-Tel | CEO; led investing | 2006 | [src](https://www.iqt.org/about/our-team) |\n| InterWest Partners | former General Partner | until about 2006 | [src](https://www.iqt.org/about/our-team) |\n| Dan Gwak | his chief investment officer | | [src](https://www.iqt.org/about/our-team) |\n\n## Open questions\nDate he became CEO; which AI deals he personally sponsored.\n\n## Sources\nhttps://www.iqt.org/about/our-team ; https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel\n", "person:steve-hochberg": "\n# Steve Hochberg\n\n## One-line take\nHochberg is the healthcare-structuring GP at Triatomic. The only deal his name is publicly on is Chemify (AI-driven chemistry), and SEC filings show he kept two other investing roles through the Triatomic years. Read him as a board and capital-structure operator and a door into New York healthcare capital, not as someone sourcing AI compute.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** about three decades of healthcare company-building and structured deals.\n  - A 2020 Deerfield SPAC prospectus credits him with \"over 65 transactions completed, representing approximately $1 billion in capital\" ([DFP S-1, 2020-02-21](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1799191/000104746920000999/a2240771zs-1.htm)).\n  - He led the Continuum–Mount Sinai hospital merger and is still a Mount Sinai Vice Chairman.\n  - That gives him board credibility and a crossover-style feel for structure: SPACs, BDCs, structured equity.\n- **Constraint:** his attention is split. His own filed bio lists four roles running in parallel during the Triatomic years:\n  - Triatomic co-GP since 2022.\n  - Deerfield Operating Partner from 2022 to 2025.\n  - Ascent Biomedical Ventures co-founder and manager since 2004.\n  - SLR Investment Corp. independent director since 2007, re-nominated in 2026 for a term to 2029 ([SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)).\n  - His network and his edge are in healthcare and medtech, not AI systems.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** lead an AI-application seed on research-talent grounds. His one public lead is a science-platform Series A with an existing institutional backer, which fits a structured, de-risked style.\n\n## Differential insight\n1. **\"Ex-Deerfield\" is wrong on his own SEC filing.**\n   - His 2026 SLR proxy bio reads \"Operating Partner of Deerfield Management ... from 2022 to 2025\" ([SLR DEF 14A, 2026-10-07](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)).\n   - So he was still at Deerfield when he became a Triatomic GP (05/2022), when he led Chemify (2023-08) and when he became Managing Partner (09/2023).\n   - The Triatomic site bio gives Deerfield as 2013–2022 only ([triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai)).\n   - The same proxy still describes him as Ascent Biomedical Ventures \"Co-Founder and Manager ... since 2004\", which makes a third active investing seat.\n2. **Triatomic's hedge-fund predecessor held his SPACs before he joined.**\n   - He was President and CEO of two Deerfield SPACs.\n     - DFP Healthcare Acquisitions Corp. later traded as TOI (The Oncology Institute). The S-1 says: \"Steven Hochberg has been our President and Chief Executive Officer since our formation\" ([DFP S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1799191/000104746920000999/a2240771zs-1.htm)).\n     - Deerfield Healthcare Technology Acquisitions Corp. later traded as CMAX (CareMax). He signed its 10-K certification ([10-K ex.31.1, 2021-03-04](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1813914/000110465921031739/tm2039179d1_ex31-1.htm)).\n   - Peter Zhou's Hongkou Capital, the adviser that became Triatomic, filed a Q4 2021 13F (filed 2022-02-14) showing 876,464 CareMax Class A shares (about $6.7M), 65,021 CareMax warrants and 400,000 TOI shares (about $3.9M) ([13F infotable](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852813/000117266122000517/infotable.xml)).\n   - Hochberg joined Triatomic's GP LLC three months later, in 05/2022.\n   - Inference: the Zhou–Hochberg relationship probably began as Zhou backing Hochberg's de-SPACs (as a PIPE or open-market investor, not established). No filing says how they met.\n3. **He also sat on the first Deerfield SPAC.** DFB Healthcare Acquisitions Corp. (which became AdaptHealth, ticker AHCO) filed his consent as a director nominee ([DFB S-1/A exhibit list, 2018-02-12](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1725255/000104746918000757/a2234511zs-1a.htm)). That puts him on all three of Deerfield's healthcare SPACs from 2018 to 2020.\n4. **His own exposure to his longest board seat is tiny.** He has been an SLR director since 2007 but holds 2,742 shares, in the $10,001–$50,000 range ([SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)).\n5. **His early-stage filter, in his own words (2011, as LSAN co-founder):** \"We are looking for unmet clinical needs.\" He described the angel network's role as \"bridging the gap between the napkin stage and the clinical testing stage\" ([NYAS, 2011-03-01](https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945)).\n6. **On Chemify he took the lead personally but wasn't first money.** He said \"We are excited to be leading the series A in Chemify\" ([Univ. of Glasgow, 2023-08](https://www.gla.ac.uk/news/archiveofnews/2023/august/headline_990451_en.html)). BlueYard was already an investor. In the 2025 Series B, Triatomic is listed only as an existing backer, and the board seats went to Wing (Satpathy) and Insight (Aceves) ([Insight Partners, 2025-10-21](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/chemify-raises-more-than-50-million-in-oversubscribed-series-b-to-drive-global-expansion-of-digital-chemistry-and-discovery/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The firm itself is high fit: it writes \"$10–20M initial checks\" into compute (MatX, d-Matrix, Gimlet, Efficient, Volantis, iPronics). Hochberg is personally named only on Chemify, which is AI for chemistry, not pre-training or chips, and his edge is healthcare and structured capital. He is useful to the owner as:\n- a channel into Deerfield, Mount Sinai and New York healthcare money for any AI-bio deal;\n- the person to ask about how Triatomic structures its co-invest and SPV stack.\n\nHe is not the partner to pitch a frontier lab or chip seed to. Peter Zhou (iPronics, Elegen) and Jeff Huber are better entry points for that.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** B.S. in Business Administration from the University of Michigan; MBA from Harvard Business School ([triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai)). Age 64 per the 2026 proxy ([SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)).\n- **1991:** co-founded Physicians' Online, his first company ([vcsheet](https://www.vcsheet.com/who/steve-hochberg), aggregator; the site says \"early '90s\"). He has founded or co-founded more than 30 companies per the Triatomic site, or 12 per a 2011 NYAS piece ([NYAS](https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945)).\n- **2004–present:** co-founder and manager of Ascent Biomedical Ventures, an early-stage biomedical VC ([SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)). He also co-founded the Life Sciences Angel Network and was a NYAS Board Governor ([NYAS, 2011](https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945)).\n- **2007–present:** director of SLR (Solar Capital) Investment Corp. He was also on the board of SLR Senior Investment Corp. from 2011 to April 2022 ([SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)).\n- **2011–2013:** Chairman of Continuum Health Partners until its merger with Mount Sinai. He is now Vice Chairman of the Mount Sinai Health System ([SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)).\n- **2013–2021:** partner in Deerfield's private transactions group, working on structured transactions ([DFP S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1799191/000104746920000999/a2240771zs-1.htm); proxy).\n- **2018–2021:** Deerfield SPACs, as above. Director nominee at DFB (AdaptHealth); President and CEO of DFP (TOI) and of Deerfield Healthcare Technology Acquisitions (CareMax).\n- **2022–2025:** Deerfield Operating Partner ([SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)).\n- **2022–present:** co-founder and co-GP of Triatomic. He has been a 25–50% member of Triatomic Management GP LLC since 05/2022 and Managing Partner since 09/2023 (Form ADV, per [firms/triatomic.md](../firms/triatomic.md)).\n- **Other boards, per an aggregator ([marketscreener](https://ca.marketscreener.com/insider/STEVE-HOCHBERG-A0CRKD/), not verified):** Crosstrees Medical, Synecor, Heart Repair Technologies, Motus GI; Executive Chairman of Hearme in 30 (2022).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai) | Team bio; no headshot (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Watch for name collisions: Steve Hochberg of Elliott Wave International and Michael Hochberg (photonics founder) are different people |\n| LinkedIn | [steve-hochberg-15b1b622b](https://www.linkedin.com/in/steve-hochberg-15b1b622b/) | Linked from triatomic.ai |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n| SEC filings | [SLR Investment Corp. proxies (CIK 1418076)](https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?action=getcompany&CIK=1418076&type=DEF+14A) | The best dated bio, refreshed every year |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2011-03 — NYAS blog Q&A, \"The Need for Entrepreneurs in Advancing Science\" (by Adam Ludwig) — [link](https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945) — the LSAN filter: \"unmet clinical needs\", early-stage funding before institutions come in.\n- No podcasts or talks found from the Triatomic era (one targeted search, 2026-11-10).\n\n## How they think\n- **Clinical need first, then structure.** He funds early biomedical companies against unmet needs ([NYAS 2011](https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945)), then brings in structured and public-market capital through SPACs, BDCs and roll-ups. The DFP S-1 credits him with \"rollups of companies within the services and the medtech sectors\".\n- **Platform chemistry as healthcare infrastructure.** The Chemify Series A was pitched as digital chemistry for partners \"where molecular design and manufacturing is a critical bottleneck\" (Glasgow release; second fragment was not verified as contiguous).\n- **Calls:** how the CareMax, TOI and AdaptHealth de-SPACs performed has not been checked here (open question). Pronoun note: SEC filings say \"Mr. Hochberg\" and \"his\", so this file uses \"he\".\n\n## Read these first\n1. [SLR Investment Corp. DEF 14A (2026-10-07)](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm): his own dated career summary, including the overlapping Deerfield and Ascent roles.\n2. [DFP Healthcare Acquisitions S-1 (2020-02-21)](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1799191/000104746920000999/a2240771zs-1.htm): track-record numbers (65+ deals, about $1B) and how he pitches himself.\n3. [Univ. of Glasgow Chemify release (2023-08)](https://www.gla.ac.uk/news/archiveofnews/2023/august/headline_990451_en.html): his only public quote on a Triatomic deal.\n4. [NYAS 2011](https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945): his early-stage filter, in his own words.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public X, Substack or podcast presence.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Chemify | Series A $43M (£36M total) | 2023-08 | Yes (personally quoted) | Horizon Ventures, Rocketship, Possible, Alix, Eos, UK Innovation Accelerator, BlueYard (existing) | [Glasgow](https://www.gla.ac.uk/news/archiveofnews/2023/august/headline_990451_en.html) |\n| Chemify | Series B >$50M (firm-level; Hochberg not named) | 2025-10-21 | No (Wing + Insight) | 8VC, BlueYard, Rockspring, Eos | [Insight](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/chemify-raises-more-than-50-million-in-oversubscribed-series-b-to-drive-global-expansion-of-digital-chemistry-and-discovery/) |\n| DFP Healthcare Acquisitions (became The Oncology Institute) | SPAC sponsor-side; President and CEO | 2020 | n/a | Deerfield | [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1799191/000104746920000999/a2240771zs-1.htm) |\n| Deerfield Healthcare Technology Acquisitions (became CareMax) | SPAC sponsor-side; CEO (10-K signer) | 2020–21 | n/a | Deerfield | [10-K cert.](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1813914/000110465921031739/tm2039179d1_ex31-1.htm) |\n| DFB Healthcare Acquisitions (became AdaptHealth) | Director nominee | 2018 | n/a | Deerfield | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1725255/000104746918000757/a2234511zs-1a.htm) |\n\n**AI and compute deals, and timing.** Chemify is his only named AI-adjacent deal. He was not first in: BlueYard was an existing investor before the Series A. No partner is named on Triatomic's compute deals (MatX, d-Matrix, Gimlet, Efficient, Chalk, Volantis), so none is attributed to him here.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Chemify A (2023-08):** Hochberg is the quoted lead. The release describes him as \"from Triatomic Capital\", and the \"About\" section calls him \"former Deerfield Management\", which contradicts the proxy's Operating Partner 2022–2025. The intro path is unknown. Horizon Ventures (APAC) also came in on the Series A. By the Series B, Triatomic had gone from lead to existing participant without a board seat, as far as public sources show.\n- **The Hongkou–Hochberg link (inference from filings):** Hongkou held CareMax and TOI at the end of 2021. Hochberg ran both SPACs, and Triatomic took him on as an equal GP owner in 05/2022. Whether Zhou was a PIPE investor in those de-SPACs isn't established; the PIPE subscriber lists in the S-4s were not checked.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Peter Zhou (peter-zhou) | Triatomic co-GP; Hongkou (Zhou) held Hochberg's de-SPACs before he joined | 2021 (13F) / 2022 (GP) | [13F](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852813/000117266122000517/infotable.xml); ADV |\n| Jeff Huber (jeff-huber) | Triatomic co-GP; equal GP LLC member | 2022-05 | ADV via firms/triatomic.md |\n| Deerfield Management | Partner, then Operating Partner | 2013–2025 | [SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm) |\n| Ascent Biomedical Ventures | Co-founder and manager | 2004 | same |\n| SLR Capital Partners (Michael S. Gross, Bruce Spohler) | Independent director, SLR Investment Corp. | 2007 | same |\n| Mount Sinai Health System | Vice Chairman (ex-Continuum chair) | 2013 | same |\n| Lee Cronin / Chemify | Led the Series A | 2023-08 | [Glasgow](https://www.gla.ac.uk/news/archiveofnews/2023/august/headline_990451_en.html) |\n| Life Sciences Angel Network | Co-founder | before 2011 | [NYAS](https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Was Hongkou or Zhou a PIPE investor in the CareMax or TOI de-SPACs? Check the S-4 subscription agreements (DHTA–CareMax 2021; DFP–TOI 2021).\n- Did he really leave Deerfield in 2025, and does Ascent Biomedical Ventures still invest? Ascent's activity has not been verified.\n- Does he hold a Chemify board or observer seat?\n- Which Triatomic deals besides Chemify did he source? Edison Scientific (AI for science) and Elegen are possible candidates given his bio background, but nothing is sourced.\n- Photo: no permitted source. triatomic.ai has no headshots, and the Deerfield team page (deerfield.com/team/steven-hochberg) now returns 404.\n\n## Sources\n- https://triatomic.ai (bio; fetched 2026-11-10)\n- SLR Investment Corp. DEF 14A, 2026-10-07: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm\n- DFP Healthcare Acquisitions S-1, 2020-02-21: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1799191/000104746920000999/a2240771zs-1.htm\n- Deerfield Healthcare Technology Acquisitions 10-K ex.31.1, 2021-03-04: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1813914/000110465921031739/tm2039179d1_ex31-1.htm\n- DFB Healthcare Acquisitions S-1/A, 2018-02-12: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1725255/000104746918000757/a2234511zs-1a.htm\n- Hongkou Capital 13F infotable (Q4 2021): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852813/000117266122000517/infotable.xml\n- Univ. of Glasgow Chemify Series A: https://www.gla.ac.uk/news/archiveofnews/2023/august/headline_990451_en.html\n- Insight Partners Chemify Series B: https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/chemify-raises-more-than-50-million-in-oversubscribed-series-b-to-drive-global-expansion-of-digital-chemistry-and-discovery/\n- NYAS 2011: https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945\n- Aggregators (secondary): https://www.vcsheet.com/who/steve-hochberg ; https://ca.marketscreener.com/insider/STEVE-HOCHBERG-A0CRKD/\n", "person:steve-jang": "\n# Steve Jang\n\n*Created 2026-11-11 from his own words: about 55 public items catalogued in `data/rows/steve-jang-voice-media.csv`, 7 of them read in full. Sources do not state his pronouns, so this file uses \"they\". YouTube quotes come from auto-captions, so names in them are sometimes garbled (\"Kanye\" for Kanyi, \"a lad Gill\" for Elad Gil).*\n\n## One-line take\nAn operator-turned-seed-lead with ten $1B+ companies who now leads AI infra, generative-media and robotics rounds of up to $100M. They source from alumni networks (ex-Uber, ex-Coinbase), introduce portfolio companies to frontier labs early, and quietly back small emerging funds as an LP.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** relationship-led sourcing and patience. They tracked Humane's founders for months through ex-Apple friends before getting a meeting [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg). About 20% of Kindred's portfolio founders were ex-Uber or ex-Coinbase in 2023 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg). After investing they work hands-on, then introduce the company to the right partner (Humane to Sam Altman while OpenAI was a nonprofit).\n- **What constrains them:** Kindred has only two partners. They don't do biotech at seed (\"biotechnology aside which we don't invest in\") [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg). LPs gave little weight to their angel record when Kindred raised Fund I [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg).\n- **What they'd never do:** fix a check size by stage (\"The idea of fixing a set amount to a stage\") [src](https://mercury.com/blog/kindred-ventures), or skip a sector just because it is unfamiliar (\"the most boring thing I can think of\") [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Humane origin (2019).** Two close ex-Apple friends tipped them off. They tracked the founders for months, then \"finally I got an introduction from one of my friends.\" They cut a Kindred offsite short to take Kanyi Maqubela to the demo. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) (49:56)\n- **Lab introduction.** Kindred introduced Humane to Sam Altman \"at that time open AI was very much a nonprofit research organization.\" Jang says Altman later invested personally. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) (51:25)\n- **They back small funds as an LP.** Jang is an LP in Scalar Capital (Linda Xie), Paradigm (Matt Huang, Fred Ehrsam) and Rackhouse (Kevin Novak, ex-Uber head of ML): \"it's not sort of a highly calculated Financial exercise.\" In 2023 they also backed a former intern's small AI fund (manager not named). [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) (34:33, 40:08)\n- **Who helped raise Fund I.** Rob Hayes and Josh Kopelman \"introduced us to a lot of LPS gave us a lot of advice.\" Chris Dixon and Marc Andreessen also helped, and Jang calls Andreessen \"a great LP\" of Kindred (auto-caption source, medium confidence). [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) (44:02, 1:06:01)\n- **Follow-on behaviour.** For conviction bets they lead the seed, then invest super pro-rata \"or even co-leading follow on rounds.\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) (52:25)\n- **Uber was a hard seed raise.** \"there were several people who I won't name but uh declined.\" Travis Kalanick was still only an angel when Jang asked him about joining full-time. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g) (14:54, 15:23; recorded July 2020)\n- **Timing as a fourth pillar.** Fred Wilson weighs market, product and team; Jang adds \"the fourth thing that um I offered was that timing is super important.\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g) (25:03)\n- **Admitted mistake.** \"I started reinvesting everything I had into other people's startups, which I don't recommend.\" [src](https://mercury.com/blog/kindred-ventures) (2021)\n- **Diligence by reading code.** \"You can go into their github or API documentation.\" They prefer a demo to a deck. [src](https://mercury.com/blog/kindred-ventures)\n- **Blue Bottle without a deck.** True Ventures made the introduction, and Jang told the founders \"send it to me but I'm in.\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) (1:01:18)\n- **Coinbase.** Kindred invested in 2014 and bought bitcoin at the same time; \"it was a non-consensus industry sector.\" They sold nothing at the 2021 direct listing. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KnWFhrhXlhE) (1:18)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Jang personally leads Kindred's frontier-AI deals: fal's Series A (and through the D), PlayAI's $21M seed (board seat; Meta acquired it in 2025), Causal Labs, Sophont, Overworld, Kotoba, Hydra Host's $100M A, Parasail's $32M A (co-lead) and Dream Labs' $90M seed (board seat). See the Portfolio table below. The LP side also fits: Jang personally backs small, concentrated managers they have worked with, such as Kevin Novak's Rackhouse. That makes them a plausible LP or co-investor for a concentrated AI fund. One caution is that most Kindred checks are still seed-sized.\n\n## Background\n- Studied English literature at UC Berkeley and built websites as an undergraduate in the 1990s [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg).\n- Co-founder or founding team member at imeem (acquired by MySpace), SoundTracking (acquired by Rhapsody) and Bitski (acquired by Phantom) [src](https://kindredventures.com/about/steve-jang/).\n- Adviser to and angel in Uber from its founding in 2009 [src](https://kindredventures.com/about/steve-jang/). Made about 50 angel investments of $25K-$250K (Coinbase, Blue Bottle, Postmates, Zymergen) [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/steve-jang-kanyi-maqubela-form-or-fund-as-kindred-ventures/).\n- Started Kindred in 2014 as a solo angel vehicle [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ut0SrT2tfrs). Formalised it with Kanyi Maqubela as Fund I in 2019 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1765533/000176553319000002/primary_doc.xml). On the Forbes Midas List in 2023 and 2024 [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/steve-jang-named-to-2024-midas-list/).\n- Family is originally from Korea with roots in Japan. They helped Uber, Coinbase and Blue Bottle expand into Korea and Japan [src](https://tim.blog/2023/12/01/steve-jang-live-from-south-korea-transcript/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://kindredventures.com/about/steve-jang/ | Bio; deal memos with founder Q&As at /announcement |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes through Kindred announcements and research |\n| X | https://x.com/stevejang | Handle per Kindred firm file; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/stevejang1/ | Listed on TWiT profile |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nThe full list (55 items, 2017-2026) is in `data/rows/steve-jang-voice-media.csv`. The ones that matter most:\n- 2017-03 — Slush Tokyo 2017 panel with Aaron Batalion and Josh Felser — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e0Q9GzQzBhE) — their angel thesis then: consumer hardware only with a subscription model; in Japan and Korea the Series A gap was their main worry.\n- 2020-08 — Village Global Venture Stories (Erik Torenberg) — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g) — Uber's side-project origin, the timing pillar, the \"rabbit hole\" approach.\n- 2021-03 — Mercury Series Tea (with Kanyi Maqubela) — [link](https://mercury.com/blog/kindred-ventures) — lead checks, concentration, no fixed check size, reading GitHub during diligence.\n- 2021-04 — CNBC on the Coinbase listing — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KnWFhrhXlhE) — invested in 2014 and did not sell.\n- 2023-10 — Smart Venture / Venture with Grace #140 — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) — Humane, the OpenAI introduction, their LP positions, Fund I LP intros. The richest single source.\n- 2023-11 — Tim Ferriss #707, live from Seoul — [transcript](https://tim.blog/2023/12/01/steve-jang-live-from-south-korea-transcript/) — Korean culture; Korean VC began as a government-backed media industry.\n- 2024-03 — The Dave Chang Show, \"So You Wanna Be a Seed-Stage Investor?\" — [link](https://www.theringer.com/podcasts/the-dave-chang-show/2024/03/04/so-you-wanna-be-a-seed-stage-investor-with-steve-jang) — not mined.\n- 2025-2026 — Bloomberg, CNBC, NYSE, Upfront Summit (Aravind Srinivas in 2025, Burkay Gur in 2026), HumanX, Startup Grind with Nuro — not mined.\n\n## How they think\n- **Timing is the fourth pillar,** alongside market, product and team [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g).\n- **Non-consensus \"moonshots\"** that open a new category beat crowded lanes like marketplaces and SaaS (\"investing in companies that seem very very non-consensus\") [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg).\n- **Emotion matters at seed:** before a product exists, \"it has to be emotional emotional investing\" on top of the analysis [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g).\n- **Open-source models (2023):** closed models are the best platform today, but open models allow \"that many flowers blooming type of variation\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg).\n- **Calls that worked:** Uber (2009), Coinbase (2014), Humane's early AI-device thesis (2019; the product later failed and HP bought its assets), and fal (Series A at $80M, through the Series D).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Smart Venture #140 (2023)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg): the Humane deal, LP positions, Fund I raise, advisers.\n- [Mercury Series Tea (2021)](https://mercury.com/blog/kindred-ventures): a human-edited transcript of how Kindred decides and sizes checks.\n- [Village Global (2020)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g): Uber's origin, timing, beginner's mind.\n- [TechCrunch on Kindred's formation model (2019)](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/steve-jang-kanyi-maqubela-form-or-fund-as-kindred-ventures/): formation plus seed; target 10-20% by the Series A.\n- [Kindred's $355M funds post (2026)](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/kindred-ventures-announces-our-new-355m-set-of-funds/): the current AI thesis.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot analysed this run (X was not fetched). Their public voice runs mostly through Kindred announcements, TV hits (CNBC, Bloomberg, Fox Business, NYSE) and conference stages.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Uber | Seed / angel (adviser) | 2009-2010 | no | First Round (Rob Hayes) | [src](https://kindredventures.com/about/steve-jang/) |\n| Coinbase | Early (angel/Kindred) | 2014 | unknown | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KnWFhrhXlhE) |\n| Blue Bottle Coffee | Angel | before 2014 | no | True Ventures | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) |\n| Humane | Seed (Kindred-led) | 2019 | yes | Sam Altman later | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) |\n| PlayAI | Seed ($21M combined) | 2024-06 | yes | Race Capital; 500 Global; YC | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/playai-x-meta-superintelligence-lab/) |\n| Aww | Seed ($6M) | 2024-07 | yes | | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/our-investment-in-aww-a-virtual-human-company/) |\n| fal | Series A ($14M) | 2024-09 | yes | a16z; First Round | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/our-investment-in-fal-ai-inference-platform-for-generative-media/) |\n| Causal Labs | Seed ($6M) | 2025-03 | yes | | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/causal-labs-pioneering-ai-driven-physics-models-to-address-causal-intelligence/) |\n| Sophont | Seed ($9.22M combined) | 2025-09 | yes | Jeff Dean; Upfront | [src](https://sophont.med/blog/seed_announcement) |\n| Scorecard | Seed ($3.75M) | 2025-09 | yes | | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/scorecard-solving-ai-agent-evaluation-at-scale/) |\n| Dedalus Labs | Seed ($11M) | 2025-10 | co-led | | [src](https://pubt.io/view/04FE6C24A787599A474AEFD975A3234B8E3FD6FA) |\n| Overworld | Pre-seed ($4.5M) | 2026-01 | yes | | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/overworld-building-real-time-world-models/) |\n| Parasail | Series A ($32M) | 2026-04 | co-led | | [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026041505/parasail-raises-32m-series-a-to-build-the-supercloud-that-puts-developers-in-control-of-their-ai/) |\n| Hydra Host | Series A ($100M) | 2026-06 | yes | NVIDIA; ARK; Magnetar | [src](https://hydrahost.com/blog/news/hydra-host-raises-100m-series-a/) |\n| Kotoba | Seed ($10M) | 2026-06 | yes | | [src](https://gamesbeat.com/kotoba-technologies-raises-10m-for-real-time-voice-ai-platform-in-east-asia/) |\n| Dream Labs Robotics | Seed ($90M) | 2026-10 | no (board) | B Capital (lead); SV Angel; Thomas Wolf | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/dream-labs-building-the-brain-for-everyday-robots/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Uber (2009):** Garrett Camp's side project. Jang wrote Camp a check [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/steve-jang-kanyi-maqubela-form-or-fund-as-kindred-ventures/) and, as an adviser, helped get a contractor-built iOS/Android prototype made. Several seed investors passed [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g).\n- **Humane (2019):** months of tracking through ex-Apple friends, a friend's introduction, a demo that made them cut an offsite short, a Kindred-led seed, then an introduction to Sam Altman at OpenAI [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg).\n- **Blue Bottle:** Jang was a daily customer at the Hayes Valley garage. True Ventures introduced the founders, and Jang committed before seeing a deck, then helped with the Japan and Korea launches [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg).\n- **Bitski:** started as Riff, a video app. Kindred helped pivot it into crypto developer tooling [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/steve-jang-kanyi-maqubela-form-or-fund-as-kindred-ventures/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Kanyi Maqubela | Co-founder and MP at Kindred; co-investor before Kindred while at Collaborative Fund | before 2019 | [src](https://mercury.com/blog/kindred-ventures) |\n| Garrett Camp | Close friend; Jang advised Uber from its start | 2009 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g) |\n| Rob Hayes / Josh Kopelman (First Round) | Introduced LPs to Kindred Fund I | 2018-19 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) |\n| Marc Andreessen | Kindred LP per Jang (auto-caption) | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) |\n| Om Malik / Jon Callaghan (True Ventures) | Investors and board members in Jang's startup; advisers | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) |\n| Scalar Capital; Paradigm; Rackhouse | Jang is an LP | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) |\n| Sam Altman | Jang introduced Humane to him | 2019-20 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) |\n| Erik Torenberg (Village Global) | Frequent co-investor | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who is the former intern whose small AI-focused fund Jang backed in 2023? (He worked at Upstart and then a UK firm.)\n- Is Marc Andreessen an LP in a Kindred fund, and which one? The only source so far is an auto-caption.\n- Not yet mined: The Dave Chang Show (2024), the Web3 Gen Z podcast (mirror.xyz returned 403), Generative Now (2025), the 2025-2026 conference talks (Upfront with Srinivas, HumanX, the Josh Constine fireside) and their X posts.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg (Smart Venture #140, 2023-10-05)\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g (Village Global, recorded July 2020)\n- https://mercury.com/blog/kindred-ventures (Series Tea, 2021-03-29)\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e0Q9GzQzBhE (Slush Tokyo 2017, uploaded 2018)\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KnWFhrhXlhE (CNBC, 2021-04-14)\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ut0SrT2tfrs (Forbes, 2023-06-09)\n- https://tim.blog/2023/12/01/steve-jang-live-from-south-korea-transcript/\n- https://kindredventures.com/about/steve-jang/\n- https://kindredventures.com/announcement/steve-jang-kanyi-maqubela-form-or-fund-as-kindred-ventures/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1765533/000176553319000002/primary_doc.xml\n- `data/deals.csv` and `data/rounds.csv` rows for steve-jang\n", "person:steve-loughlin": "\n# Steve Loughlin\n\n## One-line take\nLoughlin is an Accel early-stage partner and former RelateIQ founder. His book is mostly enterprise SaaS and security, but he co-led both the seed and the A of Bob McGrew's Arda. That frontier-adjacent deal came from a relationship of more than twenty years, not from a research-world position. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he is a founder-turned-partner, and founders seek him out. Jason Boehmig went looking for him because people called RelateIQ their favourite place to work. [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/)\n- **His network:** he keeps backing people he has already worked with or known for years. He wrote the first check into Airkit, started by his RelateIQ COO and CTO [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit). He incubated Clockwise, started by RelateIQ platform engineers [src](https://www.accel.com/news/delivering-time-with-clockwise). He had known the Vorlon team for years [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-series-a-in-vorlon-detect-and-remediate-api-security-threats), and McGrew for 20+ years.\n- **How he decides:** he looks for founders who were the customer for the problem they are solving, and then tries hard to break the thesis before committing (AssemblyAI). [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai)\n- **Constraint:** his centre of gravity is horizontal enterprise software (sales tooling, data reliability, security). Research-lab deals reach him through his personal network, not through a sourcing engine.\n\n## Differential insight\n- AssemblyAI nearly died at the paper screen. He had Covid, and the deck looked like \"another voice-to-text company\". He forwarded it to Sara Ittelson, about two weeks into her partnership, and she pulled him back in. [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai) (12:34)\n- His diligence ritual: after meeting Dylan Fox, the two partners called him daily for six to ten days, saying \"we got to find something wrong with this\". [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai) (14:07)\n- He framed the AssemblyAI A as a pre-ChatGPT contrarian bet: \"an AI company at that point was non-obvious\". [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai) (16:03)\n- The Ironclad A turned on a slide the founder underrated: each new legal user added about nine users from other departments. Loughlin's words: \"for every legal user Ironclad added, they added nine other users\". He signed the deal from the hospital on the day his child was born. [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/)\n- Before ChatGPT, AI came up at every Ironclad board meeting: \"every board meeting we talked about should we do something with AI\". [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt) (06:21)\n- He says generative AI makes repricing away from seats \"not just aspirational, it's also existential\" for software companies. [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt) (20:43)\n- He met Barr Moses in 2018, before she had chosen an idea, and told her to go for it. Her recollection: \"this investor believes in me even more than I believe in myself\". [src](https://www.accel.com/news/secrets-to-scaling-with-monte-carlos-barr-moses)\n- Hidden network link: as a Stanford GSB classmate, he introduced Jared Weinstein (later Thrive) to Joe Lonsdale and Shyam Sankar, and Weinstein went on to Palantir. Weinstein: \"a guy named Steve Laughlin, who is now an Accel partner\". [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein) (1:11:36)\n- Arda already had Anduril as a customer at its stealth launch: \"Anduril Industries already relies on Arda’s technology\". [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda)\n- The CHAOS Industries post he wrote announces \"Accel’s $145 million investment in\" a defense company founded in 2022. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-chaos-bringing-groundbreaking-new-technologies-to-national-defense)\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-10)\nMined from two Spotlight On transcripts he hosted (2023), the TechCrunch Extra Crunch Live recap (2021) and 13 Accel memos he wrote (2016–2026). The audio-only SaaStr (2017) and DealMakers (2019) episodes are not yet transcribed.\n- **First-check identity:** in 2016 he wrote that he wants to be there at the moment a founder sets an audacious goal, with an AI/ML-plus-data thesis from day one. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/relateiq-founder-steve-loughlin-on-joining-accel-as-an-investor)\n- **Founder-market rule:** \"The bar is you, you would've been a customer.\" He treats customer meetings as a weak proxy for understanding the problem. [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai) (18:00)\n- **Lived-pain deals:** his Scratchpad, Vivun, Clockwise and Airkit theses each start from a pain he had as RelateIQ CEO. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/accels-seed-in-scratchpad-the-first-workspace-for-revenue-teams) [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-series-a-in-vivun-the-worlds-first-platform-for-presales)\n- **Board style:** he agrees the plan with the founder up front so he can push against it. With Boehmig, a weekly meeting the founder could cancel became biweekly, then async. [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/)\n- **AI cycle view (Oct 2023):** \"we're definitely in a hype cycle\". He expects an App Store-style burst of experiments with a few durable winners. [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai) (29:31, 34:21)\n- **Platform shifts:** \"the biggest mistake people can make is not engaging in it\". [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt) (28:32)\n- **Seed-then-A habit:** Stairwell (met Nov 2019, seed 2020, A 2021), Monte Carlo (seed and A), Vorlon (seed 2022, A 2024), Clockwise (incubated, seed, A co-lead with Greylock), Arda (seed and A together). [src](https://www.accel.com/news/an-inside-out-approach-to-security-threats-our-continued-investment-in-stairwell) [src](https://www.accel.com/news/launching-the-data-reliability-category-with-monte-carlo)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The deals that matter for the thesis:\n- **Arda:** co-led the seed and A of a lab-adjacent company founded by McGrew, ex-OpenAI chief research officer. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda) The seed was reported at about $70M on a $700M valuation (WSJ, via secondary sources in `data/rounds.csv`).\n- **AssemblyAI:** speech foundation models, Series A and C lead. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-continued-investment-in-assemblyai)\n- **CHAOS Industries:** defense hardware with AI. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-chaos-bringing-groundbreaking-new-technologies-to-national-defense)\n\nThe rest of the book is enterprise SaaS and security: Ironclad, Monte Carlo, Vivun, Scratchpad, Productiv, Stairwell, Vorlon. He is a useful co-lead on big seeds for ex-lab founders he knows personally, but he does not originate frontier-research deals. The McGrew/Palantir/Stanford network is the reason to know him.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Portland, Oregon; Stanford graduate. [src](https://www.accel.com/team/steve-loughlin) Stanford GSB classmate of Jared Weinstein. [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein)\n- President and CEO of Affinity Circles; advised or invested in Palantir, Addepar and Roam Analytics. [src](https://cloud.substack.com/p/saastr-130-accels-steve-loughlin-142)\n- Brief entrepreneur-in-residence at Morgenthaler Ventures in early 2011. [src](https://venturebeat.com/ai/relateiq-thinks-it-can-beat-salesforce-with-better-big-data-and-40m)\n- Co-founded RelateIQ with Adam Evans in July 2011. Salesforce bought it in 2014 for close to $400M. [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SalesforceIQ) Accel led RelateIQ's Series A. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit)\n- Stayed inside Salesforce after the deal (\"RelateIQ and then Salesforce\"). [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-series-a-in-vivun-the-worlds-first-platform-for-presales)\n- Joined Accel as a Partner in September 2016. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/relateiq-founder-steve-loughlin-on-joining-accel-as-an-investor)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.accel.com/team/steve-loughlin | Accel team page; 18-19 portfolio logos (2026-11) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.accel.com/news | Accel posts he authored, about 1-2 a year (16 found, 2016–2026) |\n| X | nothing found | No verified handle linked from Accel or press |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/steveloughlin/ | Linked from the Accel team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://www.accel.com/news/podcasts | Hosted 3 Spotlight On episodes (AssemblyAI, Ironclad, Monte Carlo), 2023-2024 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2017-06 — SaaStr — Ep. 130, founding RelateIQ, Benioff lessons, market choice — https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/saastr-130-accels-steve-loughlin-on-founding-relateiq/id1089973241?i=1000386727793 — no transcript found; not mined\n- 2019-09 — DealMakers (Alejandro Cremades) — selling RelateIQ, investing in AI from a billion-dollar fund — https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/dealmakers/episode/steve-loughlin-on-selling-his-business-to-salesforce-for-390m-and-now-investing-from-a-billion-dollar-fund-in-ai-startups-15429 — not mined\n- 2021-02 — TechCrunch Extra Crunch Live, with Jason Boehmig — https://techcrunch.com/video/steve-loughlin-accel-jason-boehmig-ironclad/ — Ironclad A origin, the nine-users slide, board cadence, pricing\n- 2023-09 — Startup Grind, with Barr Moses, on building a category with customers — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_BILhYiM57g — not mined (captions not retrievable)\n- 2023-10 — Accel Spotlight On S1E1, AssemblyAI (co-host with Sara Ittelson) — https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai — how the deal nearly got missed; hype-cycle view\n- 2023-12 — Accel Spotlight On S1E8, Ironclad's Cai GoGwilt — https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt — AI on every board agenda; pricing is existential\n- 2024-05 — Accel Spotlight On S2E9, Monte Carlo's Barr Moses — https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/monte-carlos-barr-moses-on-creating-a-playbook-for/id1710032122?i=1000656541603 — no transcript; not mined\n\n## How they think\n- Main theses: data, AI/ML and new interfaces will change how work gets done (2016). [src](https://www.accel.com/news/relateiq-founder-steve-loughlin-on-joining-accel-as-an-investor) He looks for horizontal \"core object\" collaboration plays (Ironclad's contract) and observability-style categories (Monte Carlo as \"APM for data\"). [src](https://www.accel.com/news/launching-the-data-reliability-category-with-monte-carlo)\n- Contrarian views: an AI company was a non-obvious bet in 2022. Incumbents with data and workflow can win generative AI if they jump in. Seat pricing is dying. Fear of unintended consequences should not slow innovation. [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt)\n- Calls right: AssemblyAI before ChatGPT; Monte Carlo pre-company (2018); Ironclad as horizontal software rather than legal tech. Calls wrong or open: Airkit, Clockwise and Scratchpad have not visibly broken out; nothing found on admitted mistakes.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Spotlight On: AssemblyAI transcript](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai): how a deal he nearly passed on was won, and his diligence ritual.\n- [Spotlight On: Ironclad transcript](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt): his view of generative AI as a board-level and pricing issue.\n- [TechCrunch ECL recap](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/): the Ironclad A origin and his board mechanics.\n- [Our seed in Airkit](https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit): his habit of re-backing his own team, in his own words.\n- [Our investment in Arda](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda): the McGrew relationship and the Anduril link.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: no verified X account. His public output is Accel portfolio posts and portfolio-founder podcasts.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Arda | Seed + Series A | 2026 | co-led | co-lead not named in Loughlin's post; Founders Fund reported on the seed (WSJ via data/rounds.csv) | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda) |\n| CHAOS Industries | Accel $145M investment | 2024-11 | not stated | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-chaos-bringing-groundbreaking-new-technologies-to-national-defense) |\n| Vorlon | Seed; Series A | 2022; 2024-04 | A announced by Accel | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-series-a-in-vorlon-detect-and-remediate-api-security-threats) |\n| AssemblyAI | Series A; Series C | 2022; 2023-11 | yes (both) | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-continued-investment-in-assemblyai) |\n| Stairwell | Seed; Series A | 2020; 2021-09 | led seed; co-led A | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/an-inside-out-approach-to-security-threats-our-continued-investment-in-stairwell) |\n| Scratchpad | Seed | 2020 | yes | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/accels-seed-in-scratchpad-the-first-workspace-for-revenue-teams) |\n| Vivun | Series A; follow-on | 2020-10; 2021-02 | led A | Menlo, Salesforce Ventures, Atlassian Ventures (follow-on) | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-continued-partnership-with-vivun-empowering-presales) |\n| Airkit | Seed (first check) | 2020-10 announced | yes | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit) |\n| Monte Carlo | Seed; Series A | by 2020-09 | yes (both) | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/launching-the-data-reliability-category-with-monte-carlo) |\n| Clockwise | Incubation; Seed; Series A | by 2019-06 | led seed; co-led A | Greylock (John Lilly) | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/delivering-time-with-clockwise) |\n| Productiv | Series A | 2019-04 | yes | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/congratulations-productiv-pioneering-the-concept-of-application-engagement) |\n| Ironclad | Series A ($8M) | 2017 | yes; board | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/) |\n| Also on Accel page: Ascend.io, Centaur Labs, Nines, Poggio, Split, VoiceOps | | | | | [src](https://www.accel.com/team/steve-loughlin) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **AssemblyAI (2022):** the deck arrived while he had Covid and looked generic. He handed it to newly arrived partner Sara Ittelson, who was excited. He met Dylan Fox a day or two later, and the two partners talked to Fox daily for 6-10 days trying to find a flaw before leading the A. Accel led the C in 2023. [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai)\n- **Ironclad (2017):** Boehmig reached him through a trusted reference. Loughlin and Vas Natarajan expected a legal-tech pitch. The \"contract as horizontal collaboration object\" reframe and the nine-users slide converted him, and he led the $8M A and signed from the hospital. [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/) [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt)\n- **Monte Carlo (2018-2020):** he met Barr Moses while she was still interviewing about 50 founders, before she had chosen an idea, and encouraged her to start. Accel led the seed and A. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/secrets-to-scaling-with-monte-carlos-barr-moses)\n- **Airkit (2020):** his RelateIQ COO Steve Ehikian and CTO Adam Evans pitched him, and he asked to write the first check. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit)\n- **Clockwise (2019):** its founders were RelateIQ core-platform engineers. Accel incubated it, led the seed and co-led the A with Greylock. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/delivering-time-with-clockwise)\n- **Arda (2026):** he has known Bob McGrew for 20+ years, and Anduril was already a user at launch. Accel co-led the seed and A together, $230M in total. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bob McGrew | known 20+ years; co-led Arda seed and A | by 2006 (\"more than two decades\" as of 2026) | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda) |\n| Adam Evans | RelateIQ co-founder; Airkit first check | 2011 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit) |\n| Steve Ehikian | RelateIQ COO; Airkit first check | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit) |\n| Jared Weinstein | Stanford GSB classmate; introduced him to Lonsdale and Sankar | Stanford years | [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein) |\n| Sara Ittelson | Accel partner; AssemblyAI co-sourcing | 2022 | [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai) |\n| Vas Natarajan | Accel partner; Ironclad first meeting | 2017 | [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt) |\n| Jason Boehmig | Ironclad CEO; board seat since Series A | 2017 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/) |\n| John Lilly (Greylock) | Clockwise Series A co-lead | 2019 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/delivering-time-with-clockwise) |\n| Kendall Collins | ex-Salesforce exec who advised the RelateIQ integration; brought in as Accel EIR | 2019 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/welcome-kendall-collins-as-accel-executive-in-residence) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Transcribe SaaStr 130 (2017) and DealMakers (2019): these are the only long-form solo interviews and likely hold RelateIQ fundraising stories, the Benioff relationship and early Accel check sizes.\n- How does he know McGrew? Both were at Stanford around the same time, and Loughlin advised Palantir, where McGrew worked. The link is unconfirmed.\n- Who were the 2026 Arda seed co-lead and the A co-lead? Founders Fund and Khosla are reported in existing rows.\n- The lead `steve-laughlin` (needs-id, surfaced by jared-weinstein) is this person; it should be merged as an alias.\n- No admitted mistakes or passes were found in his public words.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.accel.com/team/steve-loughlin\n- https://www.accel.com/news/relateiq-founder-steve-loughlin-on-joining-accel-as-an-investor\n- https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai\n- https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt\n- https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/\n- https://techcrunch.com/video/steve-loughlin-accel-jason-boehmig-ironclad/\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-chaos-bringing-groundbreaking-new-technologies-to-national-defense\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-series-a-in-vorlon-detect-and-remediate-api-security-threats\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-continued-investment-in-assemblyai\n- https://www.accel.com/news/an-inside-out-approach-to-security-threats-our-continued-investment-in-stairwell\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-continued-partnership-with-vivun-empowering-presales\n- https://www.accel.com/news/accels-seed-in-scratchpad-the-first-workspace-for-revenue-teams\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-series-a-in-vivun-the-worlds-first-platform-for-presales\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit\n- https://www.accel.com/news/launching-the-data-reliability-category-with-monte-carlo\n- https://www.accel.com/news/delivering-time-with-clockwise\n- https://www.accel.com/news/congratulations-productiv-pioneering-the-concept-of-application-engagement\n- https://www.accel.com/news/welcome-kendall-collins-as-accel-executive-in-residence\n- https://www.accel.com/news/secrets-to-scaling-with-monte-carlos-barr-moses\n- https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein\n- https://cloud.substack.com/p/saastr-130-accels-steve-loughlin-142\n- https://venturebeat.com/ai/relateiq-thinks-it-can-beat-salesforce-with-better-big-data-and-40m\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SalesforceIQ\n", "person:sujay-jaswa": "\n# Sujay Jaswa\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (owner pick; new profile). Pronouns: Kauffman Fellows and Bessemer use \"he\". Main primary sources: The Logan Bartlett Show EP 144 (YouTube, uploaded 2025-05-24; I read the auto-captions, which don't label speakers, so lines are attributed from context), the Bessemer \"Wish I Knew\" transcript (2022-11-14), the Aura Consolidated Group S-1/A (2026-09-02), the Kauffman Fellows bio, and SEC filings (Intersections 13D, Dropbox 424B4, StubHub S-1 exhibit, Vy Global Growth S-1 exhibit, WndrCo and Aura/Point Wild Form Ds).*\n\n## One-line take\nJaswa runs the money at WndrCo. He is a Constellation-Software-style holding-company builder whose venture arm (only since 2020) buys late into generational companies run by friends from his network (Databricks, Figma, Cursor, Harvey). Watch him as a growth co-investor and a node in the Dropbox network, not as a frontier-lab seed lead.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** network plus partner. He was Dropbox's first business executive (2010-2015). He calls that alumni group the source of \"one, two, three\" of the top execs at most next-generation companies, OpenAI and Figma included ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY), ~00:56:52). Katzenberg brings the access: Jaswa says \"without Jeffrey, we're just a bunch of people with reasonable resumes\" (~00:21:07).\n- **How the book works:** the build side is the safe base. About $300M bought 28-80% stakes in 10-11 companies doing ~$1.1B revenue in 2025 (~00:13:49). That lets the venture side aim only for outliers: 5-7 shots a year, each needing a 10-15x path (~00:54:19, ~00:54:49). Late-stage AI fits that test: enter at ~$10B if the founder can plausibly build $100B+.\n- **Constraint:** deal count (\"we just don't do that many deals... that's our biggest constraint\", ~00:18:52). Also self-awareness: they won't incubate in AI because \"we're not the smartest people in the world at it\" (~01:14:19).\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** lead a large seed into a new frontier lab. The firm builds only where it thinks it is the most qualified operator (consumer security, PE-starved categories), and none of his named deals are seed-stage AI.\n- **Revises the firm file:** `firms/wndrco.md` infers $5-15M lead checks from Alembic, Orby and Creatify. Jaswa's own numbers (~$150-200M a year over 5-7 venture deals plus a build) point to much larger average venture checks for his late-stage names. Those seed-strategy co-leads likely sit with the seed team, not with him. Flagged for the merge.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Self-declared biggest mistake:** no venture arm from 2016 to 2020, just as Dropbox alumni were founding and staffing the next wave. \"the biggest mistake was that we didn't do venture from 2016 to 2020\" ([YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY), 00:55:52; the speaker is likely Katzenberg). The S-1/A dates WndrCo Capital Management to 2020, which matches ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm)).\n- **Databricks timing:** Katzenberg says Jaswa's greatest regret is that he \"had the chance to invest in him at 500 million\", then at $1B and $2B, before WndrCo came in late (00:56:22). Jaswa calls Ali Ghodsi \"a really good friend of mine\" (00:54:19).\n- **Late-stage underwriting rule, in his words:** \"can we get a 10 or 15x on something?\" (00:54:19). He sizes Figma against $100B and Databricks against \"half a trillion\".\n- **He is a big personal LP.** Dropbox liquidity started around 2011, and after that \"I invested in dozens and dozens and dozens of venture funds\". Fifteen years on he says he is still waiting for principal back (00:49:44). No fund is named.\n- **Origin of his own wealth:** in 2009 he put about a third of his net worth into a \"Facebook secondary at 8 billion\". He says that funded the move from NEA to Dropbox. The path was his McKinsey best friend Matt Cohler (00:52:19). SpaceX came via John Hering of Vy Capital, who syndicated it to friends \"at 10 billion\" (00:53:49).\n- **2022 regret:** in the drawdown WndrCo looked at three deals priced at 150-200x ARR and passed. He now thinks \"in retrospect we probably should have done them all\" (01:02:31).\n- **The founding model was Constellation Software, not venture.** After Dropbox he set out to buy freemium/prosumer software and \"build the next Constellation Software that way\" ([Bessemer](https://www.bvp.com/wish-i-knew/jeffrey-katzenberg-and-sujay-jaswa), 2022-11-14). A Spruce House Capital founder had handed him *The Outsiders* ([YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY), ~00:05:41).\n- **Build-side hunting rule:** \"when an industry has gotten private equity\", because PE owners cut long-term R&D (01:16:50). Aura is the case: a ~$50M market-cap identity-protection company (Intersections) bought for about $95M all-in (~01:21:24).\n- **Process:** \"it's sentiment consensus\" (00:27:35, likely Katzenberg), not a vote and not a single-partner call. Jaswa swings between \"greatest idea\" and \"stupid idea\" several times a day, per Katzenberg (00:00:00).\n- **Early capital behind WndrCo (filing):** in 2018 WndrCo, LLC's board of managers included C. Ashton Newhall of Greenspring Associates (now StepStone) and Jamie McJunkin of Madrone Capital Partners ([13D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1095277/000119312518325662/d635534dsc13d.htm)). That points to both as early LPs, which fits StepStone later holding a WndrCo SPV.\n- **Hidden personal links (filings):** his revocable trust is a StubHub investor and co-signed its investor rights agreement next to Naveen Rao and Dara Khosrowshahi ([exhibit 4.3](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1337634/000119312525060140/d225849dex43.htm)). In 2020 he consented to be a director nominee of Vy Capital's SPAC, Vy Global Growth ([consent](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920105181/tm2029689d3_ex99-5.htm)). He sold 433,333 of his 2,148,289 Dropbox shares in the 2018 IPO ([424B4](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1467623/000119312518092764/d553522d424b4.htm)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a frontier-lab or seed-AI investor. His AI deals are Databricks, Cursor and Harvey (all late, at $8B+ for Harvey) and Abridge (Series E). He openly says WndrCo won't incubate in AI. Three things make him worth knowing anyway:\n1. **Personal LP in \"dozens\" of venture funds.** He is the kind of individual who backs managers, though 15 years of waiting on principal may have cooled him.\n2. **The Dropbox network** (Houston, Ferdowsi, ChenLi Wang, Ghodsi as a close friend) feeds AI talent into OpenAI, Figma and Databricks. He can be a reference or co-investor for later rounds of your AI-native companies.\n3. **Vy Capital and Naveen Rao ties** sit close to frontier compute.\n\nNot a lead for $600M lab seeds; a possible growth-round follow-on for an AI-native automation company once it has PMF.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Silicon Valley. Father Raj Jaswa co-founded the chipset maker Opti (1988) and TiE ([Bessemer](https://www.bvp.com/wish-i-knew/jeffrey-katzenberg-and-sujay-jaswa)). A brother was a Bessemer analyst (same source). Age 46 per the S-1/A.\n- Princeton AB Economics (certificate in finance); Harvard MBA ([Kauffman](https://www.kauffmanfellows.org/fellows/sujay-jaswa)). Princeton is where he met Jeffrey Nykun.\n- McKinsey, Silicon Valley office (where he met Matt Cohler). Then Cisco Corporate Business Development (video and broadband deals), then a year at Cisco portfolio company CinemaNow, before business school ([Kauffman](https://www.kauffmanfellows.org/fellows/sujay-jaswa)).\n- 2005: interim CEO of YFly.com, a celebrity entertainment site; he recapitalized and relaunched it (same source).\n- 2008-2010: Principal at NEA, consumer and IT; Kauffman Fellow Class 13, mentored by Mark Perry and Patrick Chung. Brought in and co-managed NEA's lead Series A deals in Playdom (later Disney), BeachMint, Huddler, Edit Ring and 1Bog, and NEA's Series C in Causes ([Kauffman](https://www.kauffmanfellows.org/fellows/sujay-jaswa)). Also involved in Workday, Cloudflare and Playdom ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm)).\n- 2010-2015: Dropbox Business Founder and VP Business (2010-2014), then CFO (2014-2015). Raised the 2011 ($4B) and 2014 ($10B) rounds ([YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY), ~00:57:23). Grew the business team from 2 to 500+ ([20VC](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/jeffrey-katzenberg-and-sujay-jaswa)).\n- 2015-2016: individual investor and advisor to JP Morgan ([Kauffman](https://www.kauffmanfellows.org/fellows/sujay-jaswa)).\n- 2016: co-founded WndrCo with Katzenberg and Ann Daly. Diego Berdakin made the intro after a poker game with Katzenberg ([Bessemer](https://www.bvp.com/wish-i-knew/jeffrey-katzenberg-and-sujay-jaswa)). Some secondary bios say 2017; WndrCo, LLC's first sale was 2017-01-25.\n- 2020: co-founded WndrCo Capital Management, the venture arm ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm)).\n- Stanford GSB lecturer on startups since 2014.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://wndr.vc/team | bio modal; checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | no verified account located |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/sujayjaswa/ | linked from wndr.vc |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-05 — The Logan Bartlett Show — EP 144 with Katzenberg — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY — biggest-mistake admission, Databricks regret, 10-15x late-stage rule, personal LP in dozens of funds, deal volume and dollars, Aura origin.\n- 2022-11-22 — 20VC #952 with Katzenberg — https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/jeffrey-katzenberg-and-sujay-jaswa — show notes only: argues operating experience goes stale fast; Alfred Lin taught him a question about letting people go.\n- 2022-11-14 — Bessemer \"Wish I Knew\" with Katzenberg (host Ethan Kurzweil) — https://www.bvp.com/wish-i-knew/jeffrey-katzenberg-and-sujay-jaswa — Constellation model, Quibi losses as a small share of the fund, bundling thesis for consumer security.\n- 2021-07-22 — Joe Lonsdale: American Optimist, S1 Ep 7 — https://blog.joelonsdale.com/p/sujay-jaswa-billion-dollar-wins-and-2c8 — show notes only: Dropbox, Aura, Quibi; argues housing scarcity is Silicon Valley's biggest problem.\n- Undated — Kauffman Fellows \"Talent Environments\" — not read (page blocked).\n\n## How they think\n- **Holding company first, venture second.** He studied hands-on holding companies (LVMH, Constellation; Buffett \"less so\") and runs WndrCo as \"founder chairman\" next to a founder CEO (~00:14:29, ~00:15:52).\n- **Power law even at late stage.** Only one or two of the 5-15 companies worth $5B+ created each year need to be theirs (\"get at least one or two of the five to 15 every year\", 00:48:12).\n- **Anti-cynicism:** \"being cynical in our world about excess and bubbles is a silly\" game (00:48:43). He dates AI at roughly the \"Google getting started\" point after ChatGPT's Netscape moment (~00:47:42).\n- **Valuation:** usually ignored for special companies, except in rare dispersion moments like 2022. He now thinks even that exception cost them (01:02:31).\n- **Talent:** Dropbox hired for raw talent over experience and gave stars bigger jobs fast (00:58:25, ~00:59:59). Calls that the root of the Dropbox network.\n- **Calls:** right on Facebook (2009, $8B), SpaceX (~$10B) and Aura (take-private of a shrinking public company). Wrong on Databricks timing, on skipping venture 2016-2020, and on Quibi (a small fund loss, by his account).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Logan Bartlett EP 144 (2025) — the most candid source on economics and mistakes.\n2. Bessemer \"Wish I Knew\" transcript (2022) — origin of the holding-company model and the consumer-security thesis.\n3. Aura Consolidated Group S-1/A (2026) — his bio, deal attributions, 23% Aura position, board roles.\n4. Kauffman Fellows bio — pre-Dropbox deal record at NEA.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No active X or Substack located; his public voice is joint podcast appearances with Katzenberg.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Databricks | Undisclosed (late; passed at $500M-$2B earlier) | — | led WndrCo's position | — | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm), [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY) |\n| Cursor | Undisclosed | — | led WndrCo's position | — | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm) |\n| Harvey | Series F ($160M at $8B); Growth ($550M at $15.5B) | 2025-12-04; 2026-09-09 | participant | EQT, Sequoia, Kleiner, Conviction | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/04/legal-ai-startup-harvey-confirms-8b-valuation/), [Harvey](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-dollar550m-at-a-dollar155b-valuation-to-help-legal-teams-own-their-intelligence) |\n| Abridge | Series E ($300M) | 2025-06-27 | participant | a16z, Khosla | [WndrCo post](https://web.archive.org/web/2025id_/https://www.wndrco.com/news/transforming-the-future-of-healthcare-our-investment-in-abridge) |\n| Meter | Undisclosed (WndrCo since 2021) | — | led WndrCo's position | — | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm) |\n| Figma | Undisclosed (\"last Figma round\", ~$10B; inference) | — | — | — | [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY) |\n| Waymo, SpaceX, Yassir, Quince, WndrHLTH, 1Password | Undisclosed | — | led WndrCo's position | — | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm) |\n| Aura (Intersections take-private) | Growth ($189.7M Form D) | 2019-02 | yes | General Catalyst, iSubscribed | [13D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1095277/000119312518325662/d635534dsc13d.htm), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1767658/000176765819000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Pilot, Rally, The Infatuation/Zagat | Undisclosed | — | led WndrCo's position | — | [20VC](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/jeffrey-katzenberg-and-sujay-jaswa) |\n| Personal: Facebook (secondary at $8B, 2009), SpaceX (~$10B), Guardant Health, StubHub | — | — | — | — | [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY), [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm), [StubHub](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1337634/000119312525060140/d225849dex43.htm) |\n| NEA era: Playdom, BeachMint, Huddler (lead A); Causes (C); Workday, Cloudflare | — | 2008-2010 | — | — | [Kauffman](https://www.kauffmanfellows.org/fellows/sujay-jaswa) |\n\nBoards: founding chairman of Aura, Point Wild, Super Unlimited and Twingate (S-1/A); wndr.vc adds WndrHLTH. He sits on Aura's audit and remuneration committees and is a Point Wild director (Form D 2026-01-07, $150M sold to 6 investors). WndrCo entities he co-manages hold 52,774,879 Aura shares (23%).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Aura (2018-2019):** a WndrCo associate kept pushing Intersections Inc. It was a ~$140M-revenue, shrinking identity-protection company trading near $50M in Chantilly, Virginia. Trevor Oelschig of General Catalyst introduced Jaswa to Hari Ravichandran, who had the same idea. A month later Ravichandran said yes. All-in cost was about $95M including equity. Almost all the old staff turned over. The growth business became Aura and the cash-flow business became Pango, now Point Wild ([YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY), ~01:15:19-01:23:25; [13D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1095277/000119312518325662/d635534dsc13d.htm)).\n- **Facebook (2009):** a secondary at $8B via McKinsey friend Matt Cohler; about a third of his net worth then (~00:52:19).\n- **SpaceX:** a friends group run by John Hering (Vy Capital) at ~$10B; at the time Starlink sounded far-fetched (~00:53:49).\n- **Databricks:** passed at $500M, $1B and $2B despite being close to Ghodsi; bought later (00:56:22).\n- **Joining Dropbox (2010):** after meeting Drew Houston, his first call was to ChenLi Wang at NEA. The NEA joke was that \"real work has to be done\" (~00:21:56).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jeffrey Katzenberg | co-founder; \"like a second dad\" | 2016 | [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY) ~00:32:16 |\n| Diego Berdakin | made the Katzenberg intro | 2016 | [Bessemer](https://www.bvp.com/wish-i-knew/jeffrey-katzenberg-and-sujay-jaswa) |\n| Anthony Saleh | partner; close friend of Katzenberg's son, helped connect them; met Jaswa via Ben Horowitz | — | [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY) ~00:25:31, ~00:29:08 |\n| ChenLi Wang | NEA colleague; Dropbox; WndrCo partner | 2008 | [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY) ~00:21:28 |\n| Jeffrey Nykun | Princeton friend; WndrCo NY | college | [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY) ~00:22:58 |\n| Justin Wexler | promoted from the 2017 associate class to partner | 2017 | [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY) ~00:26:01 |\n| Drew Houston | best friend; officiated each other's weddings | 2010 | [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY) ~00:58:56 |\n| Arash Ferdowsi | Dropbox co-founder | 2010 | same, ~00:57:54 |\n| Ali Ghodsi | close friend; Databricks | — | same, ~00:54:19 |\n| Hari Ravichandran / Trevor Oelschig (General Catalyst) | Aura partner / introducer | 2018 | same, ~01:15:19 |\n| Matt Cohler | McKinsey best friend | early 2000s | same, ~00:52:49 |\n| John Hering / Vy Capital | SpaceX syndicate; Vy SPAC nominee | 2020 (SPAC) | same; [Vy consent](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920105181/tm2029689d3_ex99-5.htm) |\n| Mark Perry, Patrick Chung, Scott Sandell (NEA) | mentors / colleagues | 2008 | [Kauffman](https://www.kauffmanfellows.org/fellows/sujay-jaswa) |\n| Ethan Kurzweil | old friend | — | [Bessemer](https://www.bvp.com/wish-i-knew/jeffrey-katzenberg-and-sujay-jaswa) |\n| Greenspring (Newhall), Madrone (McJunkin) | fellow managers of WndrCo, LLC, 2018 | 2017-2018 | [13D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1095277/000119312518325662/d635534dsc13d.htm) |\n| Joe Lonsdale / 8VC | podcast host; 8VC is an Aura holder | 2021 | [podcast](https://blog.joelonsdale.com/p/sujay-jaswa-billion-dollar-wins-and-2c8), [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which venture funds is he personally an LP in? He says \"dozens\" from about 2011-2012 vintages. Fund 990-PFs or GP announcements won't show individuals, so the route is direct contact or LPAC lists.\n- Round and date for Cursor and Databricks: not disclosed, and WndrCo is absent from public round announcements I checked.\n- Which three 2022 deals did WndrCo pass on? The likely candidates were AI/infra names priced at 150-200x ARR.\n- Is \"Tom Kulie\" (former WndrCo associate, now at Sequoia per Jaswa) a real name? The auto-captions may have garbled it.\n- Did Greenspring and Madrone commit to WndrCo, LLC, or did they hold manager seats for another reason? The 2017 Form D shows a $25M minimum and 18 investors.\n- Holdings ATM LP (2026, via Morgan Stanley) as a Harvey SPV is still an inference from the firm file; nothing found this session confirms it.\n- Did the Vy Global Growth SPAC (2020) ever close a deal, and is Jaswa an LP in Vy funds?\n\n## Sources\n- The Logan Bartlett Show EP 144, YouTube auto-captions: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e3zyrMYXUdY (uploaded 2025-05-24); chapters: https://brapodd.se/podd/the-logan-bartlett-show/avsnitt/ep-144-jeffrey-katzenberg-sujay-jaswa-wndrco-on-what-startup-founders-can-steal-from-hollywood\n- Bessemer Wish I Knew (2022-11-14): https://www.bvp.com/wish-i-knew/jeffrey-katzenberg-and-sujay-jaswa\n- 20VC #952 show notes (2022-11-22): https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/jeffrey-katzenberg-and-sujay-jaswa\n- American Optimist S1E7 notes (2021-07-22): https://blog.joelonsdale.com/p/sujay-jaswa-billion-dollar-wins-and-2c8\n- Kauffman Fellows: https://www.kauffmanfellows.org/fellows/sujay-jaswa\n- wndr.vc team bio (JS bundle, read 2026-11-10): https://wndr.vc/team\n- Aura Consolidated Group S-1/A (2026-09-02): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2055301/000162828026060183/aura-sx1a.htm\n- Intersections SC 13D (2018-11-13): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1095277/000119312518325662/d635534dsc13d.htm\n- WC SACD Holdings Form D (2019): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1767658/000176765819000001/primary_doc.xml ; Point Wild Form D (2026): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1767658/000176765826000001/primary_doc.xml\n- Dropbox 424B4 (2018): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1467623/000119312518092764/d553522d424b4.htm\n- StubHub S-1 exhibit 4.3 (2025): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1337634/000119312525060140/d225849dex43.htm\n- Vy Global Growth S-1 exhibit 99.5 (2020): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920105181/tm2029689d3_ex99-5.htm\n- Harvey and Abridge round sources as linked in the Portfolio table\n", "person:sumer-juneja": "\n# Sumer Juneja\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. The vehicle is in [softbank-vision-fund-2.md](../firms/softbank-vision-fund-2.md), the parent and its other pools in [softbank-group.md](../firms/softbank-group.md), and the deal ledger in [softbank-ai-ledger.md](../sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md).*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the managing partner above SoftBank Vision Fund 2's earliest-stage AI checks, but directors and partners under him work the deals and take the board seats. Treat him as the approving sponsor for India and EMEA, not as the person who finds or prices an AI round.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** a captive pool and a regional monopoly inside it. Vision Fund 2 is 98% SoftBank Group's own money ([firm file](../firms/softbank-vision-fund-2.md)), and he heads two of its regions. He was hired in 2018 to open India and argued for smaller, earlier checks than the fund's norm: \"Juneja pushed for smaller, earlier bets reflecting India's GDP reality\" ([ClickZ, 2025-06-04](https://clickz.com/how-europe-and-india-are-building-a-more-equitable-tech-future/272191/)).\n- **What he personally does:** sponsors and supervises. On all four AI positions on his page, the named working investor is someone else (table below). No release found quotes him on an AI round.\n- **What constrains him:** the fund is about half OpenAI by cost and deploys little else. The fund's FY2025 letter names only four new companies besides OpenAI, and two of them (Cast AI, Emergent) are his ([Clavel message](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/svf)). Who approves his checks (investment committee, Clavel, Son) is not disclosed; unconfirmed.\n- **What he'd never do:** a seed. His earliest AI entry is a Series B co-lead. He has no stated commercial tie to offer either: nothing on compute, distribution or customer commitments is disclosed for any of his AI companies.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The Emergent deal was worked from Mumbai by Sarthak Misra.** The fund's own feature says \"Misra and his team talked to nearly two dozen Emergent users\" before the Series B ([Vision Fund, 2026](https://visionfund.com/insights/emergent-ai-coding-for-everyone)). Juneja is not mentioned in it.\n- **Board seats go to his directors.** Tractable's Series E release: Nahoko Hoshino, \"Investment Director for SoftBank Investment Advisers will join the Tractable board\" ([Insight Partners, 2023-07-18](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/tractable-ai-raises-65m-in-series-e-funding-led-by-softbank-vision-fund-2-investment-to-power-next-generation-ai-for-instant-visual-assessments-within-the-automotive-and-property-ecosystems/)). Cast AI's release quotes \"Tim Yap, Investment Director at SoftBank Investment Advisers\" ([Cast AI, 2025-04-30](https://cast.ai/press-release/cast-ai-closes-108m-series-c-round/)).\n- **They re-upped in Emergent but did not set the next price.** Six months after co-leading the B at about $300M, the Series C at $1.5B was led by Creaegis: \"Earlier backers Khosla Ventures, SoftBank's Vision Fund 2, Lightspeed, and Y Combinator returned\" ([The Next Web, 2026-07-15](https://thenextweb.com/news/emergent-130m-series-c-vibe-coding-unicorn)).\n- **His AI screen is vertical, not model-first.** \"Is this entrepreneur building a real vertical outcome?\" ([ClickZ, 2025-06-04](https://clickz.com/how-europe-and-india-are-building-a-more-equitable-tech-future/272191/)). In 2023 he said SoftBank would not \"invest in a company that misses the AI wave\" ([Entrepreneur India, citing Bloomberg, 2023-08-28](https://www.entrepreneur.com/en-in/news-and-trends/softbank-plans-ai-tour-to-boost-technology-adoption-report/458125)).\n- **Structured terms are a feature in India.** On his own page he says India has \"more scope for competitive dealmaking with more flexible capital structures\" than Europe ([Vision Fund](https://visionfund.com/team/sumer-juneja)).\n- **His process is slow.** Lenskart's founder, on stage with him: \"we took, what, six months to align on what we want to do\" ([Vision Fund, Slush 2023](https://visionfund.com/insights/scaling-across-borders)).\n- **Why India is run locally.** \"You can't deploy capital in India from a boardroom in Mayfair\" ([ClickZ, 2025-06-04](https://clickz.com/how-europe-and-india-are-building-a-more-equitable-tech-future/272191/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** He writes Series B and later checks from a captive growth fund, in India and EMEA, into application and tooling companies. For a ~$20M check into a frontier-AI seed he is neither a co-investor nor an LP.\n\nWhat he is: **a later-round co-lead** for companies based in India or Europe, two or more rounds after the seed. Emergent shows the pattern: SVF2 co-led the B three to four months after the A, then followed in the C. If a seed company of mine is India- or Europe-based and growing fast, his group is a plausible Series B co-lead.\n\n**Who to know:** not Juneja first. For India, Sarthak Misra (Partner, Mumbai), who worked Emergent. For Europe, Nahoko Hoshino (poolside, Tractable) and Tim Yap (Cast AI), both Directors in London. Juneja is the sign-off above them.\n\nThe one deal that matters to my lens is **poolside** (a model builder). SoftBank's entry round there is unconfirmed.\n\n## Background\n- BSc in politics and economics, London School of Economics ([Vision Fund](https://visionfund.com/team/sumer-juneja)). Dates not found.\n- Goldman Sachs: M&A analyst, then a founding member of its Asian Special Situations Group in India ([GCV, 2018-11-23](https://globalventuring.com/juneja-jumps-to-softbank-investment-role/?format=md)). Dates not found.\n- Norwest Venture Partners: partner from 2009 to 2018, India growth equity and venture. Deals included Swiggy and Quikr (same GCV source).\n- SoftBank: hired 2018-11 to lead the India investment team (same GCV source, citing the Economic Times).\n- Managing Partner, 2021-11, one of four promoted; credited with nearly $3B invested in India ([GCV, 2021-11-23](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sbia-scores-four-managing-partners/?format=md)).\n- Europe added and move to London, 2022-08, when Yanni Pipilis and Munish Varma left with Rajeev Misra ([GCV, 2022-08-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/juneja-expands-role-softbank/?format=md)).\n- Has lived in London, Hong Kong and Mumbai ([Vision Fund](https://visionfund.com/team/sumer-juneja)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [visionfund.com/team/sumer-juneja](https://visionfund.com/team/sumer-juneja) | Loads (checked 2026-11-10). Bio, Q&A, 20 portfolio companies |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | none found | Not searched exhaustively; unconfirmed |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | No profile URL confirmed from a primary source |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n**How to reach him.** No public inbound channel found. The routes that sources support:\n- Through his team: Sarthak Misra (India), Nahoko Hoshino and Tim Yap (EMEA). They are the people named on the AI deals.\n- Through co-leads on his rounds: Khosla Ventures (Emergent), G2 Venture Partners (Cast AI), Insight Partners and Georgian (Tractable).\n- At events where he speaks: Slush (2023) and SXSW London (2025).\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-11 — Slush — \"Scaling across borders\", with Peyush Bansal of Lenskart — [transcript](https://visionfund.com/insights/scaling-across-borders) — says he has been on the Lenskart board since the 2019 investment; Bansal describes six months of alignment before signing.\n- 2025-06 — SXSW London — session written up by [ClickZ](https://clickz.com/how-europe-and-india-are-building-a-more-equitable-tech-future/272191/) — Europe should fund \"the next Revoluts and UIPaths\"; AI judged by vertical outcome.\n- 2025-12 — Mint interview, \"If you have a deal, we have the money, says SoftBank\". Seen only as a search snippet of a paywalled reprint; **unconfirmed**, not quoted here.\n\n## How they think\n- **Local presence over macro story.** India looks good from afar; the judgement is in the detail (ClickZ).\n- **Smaller and earlier than the house style, in India.** His own argument inside SoftBank (ClickZ). Still Series B at the earliest in AI.\n- **AI as a filter on every company.** Technical team and an industry where AI improves the model are \"must-haves\" for a new check (Entrepreneur India, 2023).\n- **Founder focus over market size.** On Lenskart: the team's single mission mattered more than the model ([Slush transcript](https://visionfund.com/insights/scaling-across-borders)).\n- **Calls:** Lenskart and Meesho are on his page and were reported as listed in 2025 (search snippet of the Mint piece; unconfirmed). No admitted mistake found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Vision Fund profile and Q&A](https://visionfund.com/team/sumer-juneja) — his own framing of India versus Europe, and the full portfolio list.\n2. [Slush 2023 transcript](https://visionfund.com/insights/scaling-across-borders) — the only long-form record of how he talks to a founder.\n3. [ClickZ on his SXSW London session](https://clickz.com/how-europe-and-india-are-building-a-more-equitable-tech-future/272191/) — his AI and stage views.\n4. [Emergent feature](https://visionfund.com/insights/emergent-ai-coding-for-everyone) — shows who in his group did the work.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No personal X or Substack located.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI positions (all Vision Fund 2, per the fund's pages). \"Named\" is who the release or fund feature names for SoftBank.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Named for SoftBank | Relation to SoftBank's business | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Tractable | Series E ($65M) | 2023-07-18 | lead | Insight Partners, Georgian | Nahoko Hoshino (quote, board) | none stated | [Insight](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/tractable-ai-raises-65m-in-series-e-funding-led-by-softbank-vision-fund-2-investment-to-power-next-generation-ai-for-instant-visual-assessments-within-the-automotive-and-property-ecosystems/) |\n| Cast AI | Series C ($108M) | 2025-04-30 | co-lead | G2 Venture Partners (co-lead), Aglaé | Tim Yap (quote) | none stated; cuts GPU and cloud cost | [Cast AI](https://cast.ai/press-release/cast-ai-closes-108m-series-c-round/) |\n| Emergent | Series B ($70M, ~$300M reported) | 2026-01-20 | co-lead | Khosla (co-lead), Prosus, Lightspeed, Together, YC | Sarthak Misra (diligence) | none stated | [Vision Fund](https://visionfund.com/insights/emergent-ai-coding-for-everyone) |\n| Emergent | Series C ($130M at $1.5B) | 2026-07-15 | no (followed) | Creaegis (lead), Claypond, Sentinel Global, Khosla, Lightspeed, YC | not stated | none stated | [TNW](https://thenextweb.com/news/emergent-130m-series-c-vibe-coding-unicorn) |\n| poolside | Undisclosed (round not identified) | not found | unknown | not stated | Nahoko Hoshino on page team | competes with OpenAI's coding products (my reading, not sourced) | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/poolside) |\n\nOther companies on his page (not AI-first; rounds not researched here): AutoStore, Contentsquare, Emeritus, Enpal, Icertis, Job&Talent, Lenskart, Meesho, Metsera, OfBusiness, Perk, Rimac, Swiggy, Vestiaire Collective ([Vision Fund](https://visionfund.com/team/sumer-juneja)).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Emergent (2026).** Launched its product in June 2025. SVF2 co-led the Series B with Khosla in January 2026. Misra's team interviewed about two dozen customers first ([Vision Fund](https://visionfund.com/insights/emergent-ai-coding-for-everyone)). Six months later SVF2 followed in a Series C led by Creaegis at five times the price ([TNW](https://thenextweb.com/news/emergent-130m-series-c-vibe-coding-unicorn)). No board seat or commercial tie is stated.\n- **Lenskart (2019).** Six months of alignment before the round; Juneja has sat on the board since ([Slush transcript](https://visionfund.com/insights/scaling-across-borders)).\n- **poolside.** Listed under SVF2 with Hoshino and Juneja. No round announcement found names SoftBank; poolside's own 2024 fundraise page does not mention it. Entry unconfirmed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sarthak Misra | Partner, Mumbai; worked Emergent; shares five page teams | 2026-01 (Emergent) | [Vision Fund](https://visionfund.com/insights/emergent-ai-coding-for-everyone) |\n| Nahoko Hoshino | Director, EMEA; Tractable board; poolside | 2023-07 | [Insight](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/tractable-ai-raises-65m-in-series-e-funding-led-by-softbank-vision-fund-2-investment-to-power-next-generation-ai-for-instant-visual-assessments-within-the-automotive-and-property-ecosystems/) |\n| Tim Yap | Director, EMEA; Cast AI, Enpal, Perk | 2025-04 | [Cast AI](https://cast.ai/press-release/cast-ai-closes-108m-series-c-round/) |\n| Alex Clavel | CEO of SBIA and SBGA; reporting line presumed, not stated | n/a | [firm file](../firms/softbank-group.md) |\n| Rajeev Misra | Former SBIA CEO; his memo gave Juneja Europe | 2022-08 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/juneja-expands-role-softbank/?format=md) |\n| Norwest Venture Partners | Former firm, partner 2009 to 2018 | 2009 | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/juneja-jumps-to-softbank-investment-role/?format=md) |\n| Peyush Bansal (Lenskart) | Board member since 2019 | 2019 | [Slush transcript](https://visionfund.com/insights/scaling-across-borders) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which poolside round SoftBank entered, and at what size.\n- Who approves his checks, and up to what size he can sign alone.\n- Whether SVF2 took a board or observer seat at Emergent or Cast AI.\n- SVF2's dollar share of each round; none is disclosed.\n- The 2025-12 Mint and Moneycontrol interviews (paywalled; not read).\n\n## Sources\n- Vision Fund: [profile](https://visionfund.com/team/sumer-juneja); [Emergent feature](https://visionfund.com/insights/emergent-ai-coding-for-everyone); [Slush 2023 transcript](https://visionfund.com/insights/scaling-across-borders); portfolio pages for [Emergent](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/emergent), [Cast AI](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/castai), [poolside](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/poolside), [Tractable](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/tractable)\n- [Tractable Series E release, via Insight Partners, 2023-07-18](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/tractable-ai-raises-65m-in-series-e-funding-led-by-softbank-vision-fund-2-investment-to-power-next-generation-ai-for-instant-visual-assessments-within-the-automotive-and-property-ecosystems/)\n- [Cast AI Series C release, 2025-04-30](https://cast.ai/press-release/cast-ai-closes-108m-series-c-round/); [Reuters via The Star](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2025/04/30/cast-ai-secures-108-million-funding-to-expand-cloud-automation)\n- [The Next Web on Emergent's Series C, 2026-07-15](https://thenextweb.com/news/emergent-130m-series-c-vibe-coding-unicorn) (read through a fetch summary; quote wording medium confidence)\n- [ClickZ, 2025-06-04](https://clickz.com/how-europe-and-india-are-building-a-more-equitable-tech-future/272191/)\n- [Entrepreneur India citing Bloomberg, 2023-08-28](https://www.entrepreneur.com/en-in/news-and-trends/softbank-plans-ai-tour-to-boost-technology-adoption-report/458125)\n- Global Corporate Venturing (secondary, citing the Economic Times and Bloomberg): [2018-11-23](https://globalventuring.com/juneja-jumps-to-softbank-investment-role/?format=md), [2021-11-23](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sbia-scores-four-managing-partners/?format=md), [2022-08-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/juneja-expands-role-softbank/?format=md)\n- [SVF message in SoftBank Group Report 2026](https://group.softbank/en/ir/financials/annual_reports/2026/message/svf)\n", "person:susana-rojas": "\n# Susana Rojas\n\n## One-line take\nA New York junior investor at Index who works under Martin Mignot on AI applications for professional services (DraftWise) and digital health, and is now co-listed on Mirage; useful as a window into Index's NYC application book, not into labs or compute.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** They sit on Martin Mignot's NYC deal team and co-sign his application deals (DraftWise, Mirage, Allara are all listed with Mignot on Index company pages) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/draftwise/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/captions/). Their written thesis is pragmatic workflow automation in law and accounting, not model research [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/automating-the-back-office-how-ai-is-transforming-professional-services/).\n- **Constraint:** No deal found where they are the named lead; on DraftWise only Mignot is quoted and Rojas is tagged [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/draftwise-raises-20m-to-bring-ai-powered-drafting-negotiating-software-to-legal-teams/). On Mirage they were added after the July 2024 Series C, which Damir Becirovic led for Index [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/captions-raises-60m-in-series-c-funding-to-invest-in-generative-video-research/).\n- **What they'd never do (inference):** Nothing in their writing or portfolio points to pre-training labs, chips or compute. Their stated focus is \"AI, healthcare, and application software in NYC\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/susana-rojas/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- With Hannah Seal, they argue near-term value is in simple automation, not frontier capability: \"Simplification and automation can deliver 95% of what AI can offer right now.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/automating-the-back-office-how-ai-is-transforming-professional-services/) (2024-09-12)\n- Same piece flags adoption friction as the real bottleneck: \"We're asking people to change a lot.\" (quoted within the post; speaker attribution inside the article not verified) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/automating-the-back-office-how-ai-is-transforming-professional-services/)\n- Sourcing method in their own writing is alumni-network mapping: with Mignot they \"went searching for alumni founders\" from Oscar, Flatiron, Ro and Thirty Madison [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inside-new-yorks-thriving-digital-health-alumni-network/) (2025-03-27)\n- Timing note: Mirage (ex-Captions) Series C (2024-07-09) names only Becirovic for Index; the company page now lists Mignot and Rojas, which suggests the board seat was handed over (inference; no handover announcement found) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/captions/).\n- No podcast, X account or talk transcript found. Firm page shows them on stage at ViVE 2026 (digital health conference) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/susana-rojas/). Web search budget was exhausted during this run, so external sources were not swept.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** Their AI work is application layer (legal drafting, back-office automation) plus a seat on Mirage, a video app that describes itself as using \"frontier AI research\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/captions/). Index entered Mirage at Series C behind Kleiner, Sequoia and a16z, so it isn't an early lab bet. DraftWise sits loosely near AI-native automation, but it's a legal SaaS Series A. The value to me is as a junior Index contact in NYC and as a read on where Mignot's team is heading. No labs, pre-training or compute.\n\n## Background\n- Originally from Colombia [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/susana-rojas/)\n- B.S. Finance and Business Analytics, The Wharton School, summa cum laude (year not stated) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/susana-rojas/)\n- Investment banker, Goldman Sachs (dates not stated) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/susana-rojas/)\n- Investor, Picus Capital (dates not stated) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/susana-rojas/)\n- Joined Index Ventures in 2023, New York [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/susana-rojas/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/susana-rojas/ | Firm bio, 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes on Index Perspectives (3 bylined/co-bylined pieces) |\n| X | nothing found | Firm page links only @indexventures |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/susana-rojas/ | Linked from firm page; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026 — ViVE Conference 2026 — on stage (photo caption on firm page) — https://www.indexventures.com/team/susana-rojas/ — no transcript found\n\n## How they think\n- AI in professional services pays off through workflow-fit automation of simple, low-risk tasks first [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/automating-the-back-office-how-ai-is-transforming-professional-services/)\n- Human judgment stays central; tools must fit existing workflows [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/automating-the-back-office-how-ai-is-transforming-professional-services/)\n- NYC digital health has a third wave driven by alumni of Oscar, Flatiron, Ro and Thirty Madison [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inside-new-yorks-thriving-digital-health-alumni-network/)\n- Calls right or wrong: nothing found\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Automating the Back Office: How AI is Transforming Professional Services](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/automating-the-back-office-how-ai-is-transforming-professional-services/) (with Hannah Seal, 2024-09-12): their AI thesis.\n2. [Inside New York's Thriving Digital Health Alumni Network](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inside-new-yorks-thriving-digital-health-alumni-network/) (with Martin Mignot, 2025-03-27): how they map founders.\n3. [How Allara is bridging the gap in women's hormonal healthcare](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/how-allara-is-bridging-the-gap-in-womens-hormonal-healthcare/): healthcare deal write-up (not read in full).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No personal X account is linked from the firm page.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| DraftWise (legal AI) | Series A ($20M) | 2024-03-14 | Index led; Mignot is the quoted partner, Rojas tagged | none named | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/draftwise-raises-20m-to-bring-ai-powered-drafting-negotiating-software-to-legal-teams/) |\n| Mirage (ex-Captions; AI video) | Series C ($60M, $500M valuation) Index entry | 2024-07-09 | Index led via Damir Becirovic; Rojas not named in the round post, listed on company page later with Mignot | Kleiner Perkins; a16z; Sequoia; Adobe Ventures; HubSpot Ventures; Jared Leto | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/captions-raises-60m-in-series-c-funding-to-invest-in-generative-video-research/) |\n| Allara (healthcare, not AI) | not stated | not stated | not stated | Mignot co-listed (per firm pages) | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/susana-rojas/) |\n\nTiming: on Mirage, Index came in after Kleiner, Sequoia and a16z (all described as returning investors), so it was not first. On DraftWise, the post names no prior investors, so first-check status is unknown.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **DraftWise:** Index led the $20M Series A in March 2024. Mignot gave the rationale (\"perfect complement of AI expertise, legal experience, and customer obsession\"). How Index met the founders (James Ding, Emre Ozen, Ozan Yalti) isn't stated [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/draftwise-raises-20m-to-bring-ai-powered-drafting-negotiating-software-to-legal-teams/) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/draftwise/).\n- **Mirage:** Becirovic led the Series C and interviewed CEO Gaurav Misra at Startup Grind (2024-11-04) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/captions-gaurav-misra-talks-leveraging-ai-opportunities-with-indexs-damir-becirovic/). The current company page lists Mignot and Rojas, not Becirovic [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/captions/). Why it changed isn't documented.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Index Ventures | Investor, New York | 2023 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/susana-rojas/) |\n| Martin Mignot | Senior partner co-listed on DraftWise, Mirage, Allara; co-author | 2024 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/draftwise/) |\n| Hannah Seal | Co-author of the AI professional-services thesis | 2024-09 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/automating-the-back-office-how-ai-is-transforming-professional-services/) |\n| Damir Becirovic | Led Mirage Series C that Rojas later appears on | 2024-07 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/captions-raises-60m-in-series-c-funding-to-invest-in-generative-video-research/) |\n| Picus Capital | Former employer (investor) | before 2023 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/susana-rojas/) |\n| Goldman Sachs | Former employer (investment banking) | before Picus | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/susana-rojas/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact title and any promotion (LinkedIn not fetched; firm page gives none).\n- Did they work on the DraftWise Series A at sourcing stage, or join after? When did DraftWise raise next?\n- Why did Mirage coverage move from Becirovic to Mignot and Rojas? Is Becirovic still at Index?\n- Do they cover any AI deals not on their firm page (e.g. Wordsmith, named in their post)?\n- No podcast, X or talk transcript found. A re-run with search budget should check ViVE 2026 and Wharton/Colombian-founder events.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/susana-rojas/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/draftwise/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/captions/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/draftwise-raises-20m-to-bring-ai-powered-drafting-negotiating-software-to-legal-teams/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/captions-raises-60m-in-series-c-funding-to-invest-in-generative-video-research/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/captions-gaurav-misra-talks-leveraging-ai-opportunities-with-indexs-damir-becirovic/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/automating-the-back-office-how-ai-is-transforming-professional-services/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/inside-new-yorks-thriving-digital-health-alumni-network/\n", "person:sydney-sykes": "\n# Sydney Sykes\n\nknown_for: Runs NVIDIA's VC Alliance, the company's formal desk for venture funds; she routes funds to Inception and deal flow, but writes no NVIDIA checks. She is an ex-Lightspeed consumer partner who came up through the Scout program.\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 (hop 1 from [firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md)). Pronouns: Lightspeed's team page and TechCrunch both use she/her. Seniority `partner` reflects her Lightspeed title (2022-2024). At NVIDIA she holds a \"Global Head\" title with no investment authority, so `check_capacity: sub-10m` means \"no checks\", not \"small checks\". The NVIDIA start and promotion dates come only from LinkedIn-derived aggregators. Her title differs between NVIDIA's page and TechCrunch's.*\n\n## One-line take\nShe is the one named NVIDIA person whose job is relationships with VC funds. For a new early-stage investor, she is the practical door into NVIDIA's VC Alliance and Inception deal flow. She is not a door to NVentures or corporate-development equity.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she matters:** NVIDIA's investing runs through three pools: NVentures (Siddeek), corporate development (Bhagwati, with Huang reportedly setting prices) and Inception, which takes no equity ([firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md)). Sykes sits in the third. The VC Alliance gives member funds access to Inception's portfolio benefits, \"curated deal flow\" from more than 30,000 startups, founder intros, pitch events and closed-door investor gatherings ([NVIDIA VC Alliance](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/venture-capital)). She is the person who decides how funds plug into that.\n- **What she personally signs or sources:** no equity. Neither the Alliance nor Inception takes equity ([nvidia.com/startups](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/)). No round in the repo ledger names her ([sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md](../sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md), [sources/nvidia-check-writers.md](../sources/nvidia-check-writers.md)). What she controls is fund relationships and founder visibility: which funds join, which portfolio companies get referred, and which founders and VCs meet NVIDIA people. The Alliance application has a \"Portfolio Referral\" section ([src](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/venture-capital)). She can make a company visible to NVIDIA. She cannot price or sign a check.\n- **Constraint:** by her own podcast framing, NVIDIA's corporate VC filters for strategic relevance first. TechCrunch's episode notes say strategic alignment matters more than \"a slick pitch deck\" (producer notes, not her words, [TechCrunch Build Mode 2026-07-23](https://techcrunch.com/podcast/nvidias-sydney-sykes-on-winning-corporate-venture-capital-deals/)). The equity decisions sit with NVentures and corporate development.\n- **What she'd never do:** commit NVIDIA capital. The role has no balance-sheet authority on any source checked.\n- **Background mismatch, inferred:** her investing record is consumer and DTC (Dolls Kill operator; Lightspeed Consumer; Archive and Apothekary scout checks). She was hired to run fund relationships, not to make AI-technical calls.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **How she reached the Lightspeed partnership:** through the Scout program, not an associate track. Lightspeed wrote that she \"first came to our attention when she joined our Scouts program\" ([Lightspeed 2022-04-06](https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-the-newest-members-of-the-lightspeed-consumer-team-sydney-sykes-and-faraz-fatemi/)). Her scout check into Archive turned into a Lightspeed portfolio company (same source).\n- **NVIDIA frames the Alliance as investor-first:** her line on the VC Alliance page is \"Investors today aren't just financing innovation, they're architecting the AI-first economy\" ([NVIDIA](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/venture-capital)).\n- **How she meets people:** in person, not online. She called her \"internet presence is honestly kind of weak\" and says she meets people at small dinners and events ([Lightspeed Scout spotlight 2021-07-21](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-scout-spotlight-sydney-sykes/)).\n- **Access is her long-running cause:** she named two levers, \"the warm intro, and how you help people of color build a track record\" ([GV 2020-11-18](https://gv.com/news/sydney-sykes-championing-diversity)). At Stanford she described BLCK VC's goal as fixing \"the lack of capital in the hands of Black investors\" ([Stanford Daily 2021-01-25](https://stanforddaily.com/2021/01/25/qa-blck-vc-co-founder-sydney-sykes-mba-22-on-venture-capitals-representation-problem/)).\n- **Her consumer investing filter:** \"I like companies that are driven by what their customers want first\" ([Lightspeed 2021](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-scout-spotlight-sydney-sykes/)).\n- **Possible fast rise at NVIDIA (aggregator only):** Manager from 2024-10, then Global Head from 2025-05, about seven months ([icebreaker](https://app.icebreaker.xyz/profiles/linkedin-sydney-sykes-8700907a)). Not confirmed by NVIDIA.\n- **Lightspeed overlap (inference):** Lightspeed is one of NVIDIA's most frequent co-investors in lab rounds in this repo ([patterns/co-investor-gangs.md](../patterns/co-investor-gangs.md)). Her former partners are therefore among the funds the Alliance serves most. This is an inference from the repo pattern; no source says it.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** She cannot write or influence a $20M lab seed check, and she has no AI deal record. Her value is access. (1) NVIDIA is a near-certain co-investor in the large lab seeds the owner targets ([firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md)), and she is the formal intro point for a fund into NVIDIA. (2) The Alliance gives deal-flow visibility into Inception's 30,000+ startups, plus pitch events. (3) The Alliance accepts family offices and \"corporate venture arms\" as well as institutional VCs, but asks for an active website and a track record of relevant investments ([src](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/venture-capital)). The owner would apply with their own vehicle; she is the person to know once they are in. Deals that matter to me: none attributed to her.\n\n## Background\n- Harvard University, Psychology and Economics; led Harvard Undergraduate Women in Business ([Lightspeed Lighthouse](https://lighthouse.lsvp.com/people/sydney-sykes)).\n- Went into VC straight from college and cold-emailed her way in ([GV 2020](https://gv.com/news/sydney-sykes-championing-diversity)). Early-stage investor at NEA; dates not given ([Lightspeed 2022](https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-the-newest-members-of-the-lightspeed-consumer-team-sydney-sykes-and-faraz-fatemi/), [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/podcast/nvidias-sydney-sykes-on-winning-corporate-venture-capital-deals/)).\n- Co-founded BLCK VC, a network of Black venture investors, and was co-CEO for about five years. Lightspeed says she grew it to \"a multi-million-dollar budget\" ([Lightspeed 2022](https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-the-newest-members-of-the-lightspeed-consumer-team-sydney-sykes-and-faraz-fatemi/)). Aggregators give the founding as 2018 with Frederik Groce, and later a role as board co-chair from 2024-10 (aggregator, unverified).\n- Dolls Kill: growth, forecasting and strategy for the retailer's own brands ([Lighthouse](https://lighthouse.lsvp.com/people/sydney-sykes)).\n- Stanford GSB, MBA class of 2022 ([Stanford Daily](https://stanforddaily.com/2021/01/25/qa-blck-vc-co-founder-sydney-sykes-mba-22-on-venture-capitals-representation-problem/)).\n- Lightspeed Scout, then Partner in the Consumer practice from 2022-04 ([Lightspeed 2022-04-06](https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-the-newest-members-of-the-lightspeed-consumer-team-sydney-sykes-and-faraz-fatemi/)). Aggregators put her exit at 2024-09.\n- NVIDIA: VC Alliance Manager from 2024-10, Global Head from 2025-05 (aggregator). NVIDIA's own page confirms the current title ([src](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/venture-capital)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [NVIDIA VC Alliance](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/venture-capital) | Quoted as Global Head; apply at programs.nvidia.com/vcalliance (about 10 min form, up to 10 business days to review) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Self-described \"kind of weak\" internet presence (2021) |\n| LinkedIn | [linkedin.com/in/sydney-l-sykes](https://www.linkedin.com/in/sydney-l-sykes) | Linked by TechCrunch (2026-09-18); not opened |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-10 (Oct 13-15) — TechCrunch Disrupt 2026, Builders Stage — \"The Open vs. Closed AI Debate Is Just Getting Started\", with NVIDIA's Nader Khalil — [TechCrunch 2026-09-18](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/18/open-or-closed-ai-nvidias-nader-khalil-and-sydney-sykes-take-on-one-of-the-decisions-shaping-next-gen-startups-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026/) — no recording or quotes retrieved.\n- 2026-07-23 — TechCrunch Build Mode (host Isabelle Johannessen, 41 min) — getting into NVIDIA's orbit through Inception, corporate vs traditional VC, building a cap table, why VCs should watch energy and data-center infrastructure — [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/podcast/nvidias-sydney-sykes-on-winning-corporate-venture-capital-deals/) — producer notes only, no transcript retrieved.\n- 2023-04-20 — DTC POD #271 \"VC & The Future of Commerce\" — as Lightspeed partner; AI as an enabling tool for brands — [audioscrape](https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/dtc-pod-how-the-best-brands-are-built/episode/271-vc-the-future-of-commerce-6476) — not transcribed.\n- 2023 — SXSW speaker as Lightspeed Consumer partner — [SXSW](https://schedule.sxsw.com/2023/speakers/2081111).\n\n## How they think\n- Strategic fit before pitch polish when raising from a corporate VC (episode notes, summary only).\n- Watches energy and data-center infrastructure as the next area for VCs (episode notes, summary only).\n- Consumer instinct: customer-led companies and overlooked \"misfit\" customers ([Lightspeed 2021](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-scout-spotlight-sydney-sykes/)).\n- Access in venture: the warm intro and building a track record are the levers ([GV 2020](https://gv.com/news/sydney-sykes-championing-diversity)).\n- No calls she got right or wrong are on record.\n\n## Read these first\n- [NVIDIA VC Alliance page](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/venture-capital): what she runs, the benefits and who is eligible.\n- [TechCrunch Build Mode, 2026-07-23](https://techcrunch.com/podcast/nvidias-sydney-sykes-on-winning-corporate-venture-capital-deals/): her only long-form NVIDIA-era interview (audio).\n- [Lightspeed partner announcement, 2022-04-06](https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-the-newest-members-of-the-lightspeed-consumer-team-sydney-sykes-and-faraz-fatemi/): the Scout-to-Partner path.\n- [Lightspeed Scout spotlight, 2021-07-21](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-scout-spotlight-sydney-sykes/): how she sources and picks.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found on X or Substack. By her own account she works through in-person dinners and events (2021).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Archive | Undisclosed (Lightspeed Scout check) | before 2022-04 | no | Lightspeed later invested | [Lightspeed 2022](https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-the-newest-members-of-the-lightspeed-consumer-team-sydney-sykes-and-faraz-fatemi/) |\n| Apothekary | Undisclosed (scout or personal check; vehicle not stated) | before 2021-07-21 | no | not stated | [Lightspeed 2021](https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-scout-spotlight-sydney-sykes/) |\n| NVIDIA (2024-) | none: no equity | | | | [nvidia.com/startups](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/) |\n\nNo Lightspeed partner-era deal (2022-2024) naming her was found. Lightspeed's page refers to unannounced deals without naming them. Seed vs later: both of the attributed checks were early-stage scout checks; the exact round is undisclosed.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Archive:** she backed it as a Lightspeed Scout, and the company then became a Lightspeed portfolio company. Lightspeed cites this in her partner announcement ([src](https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-the-newest-members-of-the-lightspeed-consumer-team-sydney-sykes-and-faraz-fatemi/)). The scout check was her audition for the partner role.\n- **How a founder reaches her:** (1) join NVIDIA Inception, which is free and takes no equity, or be referred by a VC Alliance member fund through its portfolio-referral section ([src](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/venture-capital)); (2) Alliance pitch events and closed-door gatherings (same source); (3) TechCrunch-circuit events such as Disrupt; (4) a warm intro through BLCK VC or Lightspeed alumni. Her own preference is small dinners and events (2021). For a fund (the owner): apply to the Alliance, then build the relationship with her directly.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NVIDIA | VC Alliance Manager, then Global Head of VC Alliance Partnerships | 2024-10 (aggregator) | [NVIDIA](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/venture-capital), [icebreaker](https://app.icebreaker.xyz/profiles/linkedin-sydney-sykes-8700907a) |\n| Lightspeed Venture Partners | Scout, then Partner (Consumer) | Partner 2022-04 to about 2024-09 | [Lightspeed](https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-the-newest-members-of-the-lightspeed-consumer-team-sydney-sykes-and-faraz-fatemi/) |\n| Nicole Quinn, Alex Taussig | Authored her Lightspeed partner announcement (Consumer team) | 2022 | [Lightspeed](https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-the-newest-members-of-the-lightspeed-consumer-team-sydney-sykes-and-faraz-fatemi/) |\n| NEA | Early-stage investor | before Lightspeed | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/podcast/nvidias-sydney-sykes-on-winning-corporate-venture-capital-deals/) |\n| BLCK VC | Co-founder; co-CEO for about five years | about 2018 (aggregator) | [Lightspeed](https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-the-newest-members-of-the-lightspeed-consumer-team-sydney-sykes-and-faraz-fatemi/) |\n| Frederik Groce | BLCK VC co-founder (aggregator directory only) | 2018 | [incubatorlist](https://incubatorlist.com/sydney-sykes) |\n| Dolls Kill | Growth and strategy for its own brands | before Lightspeed | [Lighthouse](https://lighthouse.lsvp.com/people/sydney-sykes) |\n| Nader Khalil (NVIDIA) | Disrupt 2026 co-speaker | 2026-10 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/18/open-or-closed-ai-nvidias-nader-khalil-and-sydney-sykes-take-on-one-of-the-decisions-shaping-next-gen-startups-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Reporting line: does the VC Alliance sit under Howard Wright (VP, Inception), or elsewhere? Not stated in any source.\n- Does the Alliance send anything to NVentures? Is a portfolio referral a path to an NVentures check? Not stated.\n- Build Mode transcript: the only real NVIDIA-era quotes are likely in the audio. Not retrieved.\n- Which Lightspeed consumer deals, if any, she led in 2022-2024.\n- Confirm the NVIDIA start (2024-10) and promotion (2025-05) dates against a primary source.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/venture-capital\n- https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/startups/\n- https://techcrunch.com/podcast/nvidias-sydney-sykes-on-winning-corporate-venture-capital-deals/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/18/open-or-closed-ai-nvidias-nader-khalil-and-sydney-sykes-take-on-one-of-the-decisions-shaping-next-gen-startups-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026/\n- https://lighthouse.lsvp.com/people/sydney-sykes\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/meet-the-newest-members-of-the-lightspeed-consumer-team-sydney-sykes-and-faraz-fatemi/\n- https://lsvp.com/stories/lightspeed-scout-spotlight-sydney-sykes/\n- https://gv.com/news/sydney-sykes-championing-diversity\n- https://stanforddaily.com/2021/01/25/qa-blck-vc-co-founder-sydney-sykes-mba-22-on-venture-capitals-representation-problem/\n- https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/dtc-pod-how-the-best-brands-are-built/episode/271-vc-the-future-of-commerce-6476\n- https://schedule.sxsw.com/2023/speakers/2081111\n- https://app.icebreaker.xyz/profiles/linkedin-sydney-sykes-8700907a (aggregator)\n- https://incubatorlist.com/sydney-sykes (aggregator)\n- ../firms/nvidia.md, ../sources/nvidia-check-writers.md, ../sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md\n", "person:tahseen-rashid": "\n# Tahseen Rashid\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. The web-search budget for this run was already used up, so this profile rests on direct fetches: his HOF bio, the company sites and blogs of TextQL, Jota, Textla and Codesphere, Brazilian press linked from Jota's own news page, and two aggregators (VC Sheet, InforCapital). VC Sheet spells his first name \"Tasheen\", so search both spellings.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the only HOF person below partner whom the firm's own site credits with sourcing and leading deals. The best-documented one is Jota, a Brazilian AI financial agent on WhatsApp. HOF backed it from the pre-seed, followed on in the Maya-led seed (Jan 2025), and again in the Haun-led $30M Series A at a $185M post-money valuation (Jun 2026).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** an international, emerging-markets sourcing angle that HOF's partners don't cover. He was a Venture Fellow at NXTP in Argentina and worked on growth at Kormo, a Google Next Billion Users jobs app in Asia [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/). His one fully documented deal, Jota, is a Brazilian company that HOF got into before the seed [src](https://braziljournal.com/jota-e-avaliada-em-us-180-milhoes-em-rodada-com-haun-ventures/).\n- **What constrains him:** small checks inside a firm whose money and brand go to late-stage AI (Anthropic, OpenAI, xAI, Crusoe; see `firms/hof-capital.md`). An aggregator puts his checks at about $500K [src](https://www.vcsheet.com/who/tasheen-rashid). He participates in rounds rather than pricing them: in both of Jota's priced rounds HOF followed (Maya led the seed, Haun led the A).\n- **What he'd likely never do (inference):** write a lab-sized or chip-sized check. His stated filter is software businesses with aggregation dynamics, not capital-intensive research.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **HOF was in Jota from the pre-seed, so it was one of the earliest institutional names.** Brazil Journal says HOF and Alter Global \"já eram investidoras do Jota desde o pre-seed\" (were already investors since the pre-seed) [src](https://braziljournal.com/jota-e-avaliada-em-us-180-milhoes-em-rodada-com-haun-ventures/) (2026-06-29). Combined with the HOF bio, which says he \"sourced and led\" Jota [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/), this makes Jota his earliest-entry deal on record.\n- **The firm's attribution for TextQL is not backed by TextQL's own announcements.** His bio says \"Tahseen sourced and led HOF Capital's investments in TextQL, Jota, Textla, Atlas, Codesphere\" [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/). But TextQL's seed post (2024-01-24) names Neo and DCM as leads plus a long list of funds and angels, ending \"and others\", without HOF [src](https://textql.com/blog/fundraising). TextQL's $17M post (2026-04-17) names only Blackstone Innovations Investments [src](https://textql.com/blog/textql-raises-17m-blackstone). Only an aggregator lists HOF in that round [src](https://inforcapital.com/companies/textql/). So HOF's TextQL entry round is undated and, as far as primary sources go, unnamed.\n- **His selection filter, in his firm bio:** he has an interest in \"infinite games and software businesses that leverage Aggregation Theory\" [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/). This is firm-site copy, not his own words on a podcast.\n- **Cold outreach works (aggregator claim, not his words).** VC Sheet says he keeps \"direct messages open and a dedicated deck submission path\" [src](https://www.vcsheet.com/who/tasheen-rashid).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The argument:\n- **For:** Jota is an AI-native automation company. It is an agent that runs a small business's banking, payments and reminders inside WhatsApp, which is close to the Serval-style automation category in `THESIS.md`. TextQL is an AI data-analyst agent that runs inside the customer's own cloud. He found Jota before its seed and kept HOF in through a top-tier US lead (Haun) at a $185M post, which shows early taste in agent products.\n- **Against:** none of his named deals is a lab, a pre-training team, a chip or compute. Codesphere is a cloud/dev platform, Textla is SMS messaging software, and Atlas is unidentified. His ~$500K checks [src](https://www.vcsheet.com/who/tasheen-rashid) mean he is not a co-investor at my $20M size.\n- **Use:** a deal-flow contact for AI-agent companies, especially in Latin America, and a view into which HOF early-stage deals get follow-on capital from the firm's big funds. The deals that matter to me: Jota and TextQL.\n\n## Background\n- Colby College, BA in Mathematics and Economics (year not stated) [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/).\n- Country ambassador for Snapchat (dates not stated) [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/).\n- Venture Fellow, NXTP Ventures, Argentina (dates not stated) [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/).\n- Growth team, Kormo (Google Next Billion Users jobs platform), which he helped scale to millions of users across Asia (dates not stated) [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/).\n- Market research for Tiger Global, analysing early-stage opportunities (dates not stated) [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/).\n- HOF Capital, Principal; join date and earlier titles not found. VC Sheet labels him \"Investor / Analyst\", which may be stale [src](https://www.vcsheet.com/who/tasheen-rashid).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/ | Firm bio, read 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Search budget exhausted; not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/tahseen-rashid/ | Linked from VC Sheet [src](https://www.vcsheet.com/who/tasheen-rashid); guest view returned nothing on 2026-11-10 |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found (search budget exhausted before this could be checked).\n\n## How they think\n- **Aggregation Theory software:** the bio's stated interest is software businesses with aggregation dynamics, played as \"infinite games\" [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/). Jota fits: it aggregates a small business's accounts through Open Finance into a single WhatsApp agent [src](https://revistapegn.globo.com/startups/noticia/2026/06/jota-assistente-financeiro-para-empreendedores-capta-r-150-milhoes-em-serie-a.ghtml).\n- **AI/ML enterprise software plus international markets** are his two stated focus areas [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/).\n- **Calls right so far:** Jota went from pre-seed to a $185M post-money Series A in about two years (company founded about two years before the A, per Brazil Journal) [src](https://braziljournal.com/jota-e-avaliada-em-us-180-milhoes-em-rodada-com-haun-ventures/). TextQL drew Blackstone's early-stage arm as anchor after hands-on testing [src](https://textql.com/blog/textql-raises-17m-blackstone). No misses on record.\n\n## Read these first\nNothing of his own found. Closest proxies:\n- His HOF bio, for his stated filter and the deal list [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/).\n- Brazil Journal on the Jota Series A, for how HOF stayed in from the pre-seed [src](https://braziljournal.com/jota-e-avaliada-em-us-180-milhoes-em-rodada-com-haun-ventures/).\n- TextQL's \"Ten Year Thesis\" and $17M posts, for what he bought into [src](https://textql.com/blog/textql-raises-17m-blackstone).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. LinkedIn was unreachable as a guest and no X account was found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Jota | Pre-seed (HOF entry) | before Jan 2025 (not dated) | bio says he \"sourced and led\" HOF's investment; round lead not stated | Alter Global | [src](https://braziljournal.com/jota-e-avaliada-em-us-180-milhoes-em-rodada-com-haun-ventures/), [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/) |\n| Jota | Seed, US$8.9M (R$60M per PEGN) | 2025-01-23 | No (MAYA Capital led) | BigBets, Alter Global, Bogari Capital, Norte Ventures, angels incl. Fersen and Mateus Lambranho, Mauro D'Ancona, Dennis Wang, Pedro Fiuza | [src](https://braziljournal.com/ex-pagbank-lanca-a-jota-que-facilita-as-financas-do-mei-no-whatsapp/), [src](https://revistapegn.globo.com/startups/noticia/2025/01/fintech-jota-focada-em-pequenos-negocios-capta-us-89-milhoes-em-rodada-seed-liderada-pela-maya-capital.ghtml) |\n| Jota | Series A, US$30M (R$150M), US$185M post | 2026-06-29 | No (Haun Ventures led) | Alter Global, Greyhound Capital (new), undisclosed investors | [src](https://braziljournal.com/jota-e-avaliada-em-us-180-milhoes-em-rodada-com-haun-ventures/), [src](https://revistapegn.globo.com/startups/noticia/2026/06/jota-assistente-financeiro-para-empreendedores-capta-r-150-milhoes-em-serie-a.ghtml) |\n| TextQL | HOF entry round not found (not named in the Jan 2024 seed post) | not found | bio says he \"sourced and led\" | n/a | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/), [src](https://textql.com/blog/fundraising) |\n| TextQL | Strategic, $17M (aggregator places HOF in it) | 2026-04 (company post 2026-04-17) | No (Blackstone Innovations Investments anchored) | Neo, DCM, Unshackled, Dropbox, Allison Pickens, Varsha Rao (aggregator list) | [src](https://inforcapital.com/companies/textql/), [src](https://textql.com/blog/textql-raises-17m-blackstone) |\n| Codesphere | not found (company says >$39M total funding) | not found | bio says he \"sourced and led\" | not found | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/), [src](https://codesphere.com/company) |\n| Textla | not found | not found | bio says he \"sourced and led\" | not found | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/) |\n| Atlas (unidentified) | not found | not found | bio says he \"sourced and led\" | not found | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/) |\n| \"several other companies in stealth\" | not disclosed | n/a | bio | n/a | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n\n### Jota (standout)\n- **What it is:** a digital account and AI agent for Brazilian micro and small businesses that runs in WhatsApp and its own app. Users pay by text, audio or photo, collect payments by QR code, and ask the agent for revenue reports and reminders. It makes money from FalaTap, a tap-to-pay feature launched around May 2026 [src](https://braziljournal.com/jota-e-avaliada-em-us-180-milhoes-em-rodada-com-haun-ventures/).\n- **Founder:** Davi Holanda, from Ceará. He spent six years at PagBank leading more than 15 products, including its entry into card machines. After the IPO (2018) he co-founded Bankly, a banking-as-a-service company sold to Méliuz and then Banco BV [src](https://braziljournal.com/jota-e-avaliada-em-us-180-milhoes-em-rodada-com-haun-ventures/).\n- **How HOF found it and when:** no source describes the intro. HOF's bio says Rashid \"sourced and led\" the investment [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/), and Brazil Journal says HOF and Alter Global were investors from the pre-seed [src](https://braziljournal.com/jota-e-avaliada-em-us-180-milhoes-em-rodada-com-haun-ventures/). The pre-seed date and amount were not found. His NXTP (Argentina) fellowship is the obvious Latin America network link, but that is my inference; no source ties it to Jota.\n- **Seed (announced 2025-01-23):** US$8.9M led by MAYA Capital (Mônica Saggioro and Lara Lemann). HOF, BigBets, Alter Global, Bogari Capital, Norte Ventures and angels participated [src](https://braziljournal.com/ex-pagbank-lanca-a-jota-que-facilita-as-financas-do-mei-no-whatsapp/), [src](https://revistapegn.globo.com/startups/noticia/2025/01/fintech-jota-focada-em-pequenos-negocios-capta-us-89-milhoes-em-rodada-seed-liderada-pela-maya-capital.ghtml).\n- **Series A (2026-06-29):** US$30M led by Haun Ventures, its first Brazilian investment, at a US$185M post-money valuation. HOF and Alter Global followed on and Greyhound Capital entered [src](https://braziljournal.com/jota-e-avaliada-em-us-180-milhoes-em-rodada-com-haun-ventures/). At the time Jota had about 300K customers and R$3.5B in annualized transaction volume [src](https://revistapegn.globo.com/startups/noticia/2026/06/jota-assistente-financeiro-para-empreendedores-capta-r-150-milhoes-em-serie-a.ghtml). The company targeted R$50M ARR by December 2026 [src](https://braziljournal.com/jota-e-avaliada-em-us-180-milhoes-em-rodada-com-haun-ventures/).\n- **What it's worth now:** last reported mark is US$185M post-money (Jun 2026). Jota's homepage now says it raised R$200M from investors of \"OpenAI, Claude e Bugatti\" (a nod to HOF's portfolio). The investor logos alt text lists Haun, HOF, Greyhound Capital, Alter, Maya Capital and Commit [src](https://jota.ai). HOF's ownership and check sizes were not found.\n\n### TextQL\n- **What it is:** an AI data-analyst agent with its own warehouse that runs inside the customer's private cloud or on-prem. It was co-founded in 2022 by Ethan Ding and Mark Hay (aggregator) [src](https://inforcapital.com/companies/textql/). Amazon and Dropbox use it in production [src](https://textql.com/blog/textql-raises-17m-blackstone).\n- **Seed, US$4.1M (2024-01-24):** led by Neo and DCM, with Unshackled, Indicator, FirstHand, Page One, Worklife and angels including Tristan Handy. The list ends \"and others\" and does not name HOF [src](https://textql.com/blog/fundraising).\n- **Strategic, US$17M (2026-04-17):** anchored by Blackstone Innovations Investments after hands-on testing. Blackstone's CTO John Stecher is quoted on time-to-value [src](https://textql.com/blog/textql-raises-17m-blackstone). An aggregator lists HOF as a co-investor [src](https://inforcapital.com/companies/textql/).\n- **Gap:** when and in which round HOF entered is not found. Rashid's \"sourced and led\" credit is the firm's claim only.\n\n### Codesphere, Textla, Atlas\n- **Codesphere:** a German (Karlsruhe and Munich) cloud and sovereign-infrastructure platform reporting more than $39M in total funding [src](https://codesphere.com/company). HOF's round was not found.\n- **Textla:** a US business SMS platform claiming more than 2B messages sent and 21K businesses [src](https://textla.com). Funding and HOF's round were not found.\n- **Atlas:** too generic a name to identify without a source. Recorded as a needs-id lead.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| HOF Capital | Principal | not found | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/) |\n| Tiger Global | Former market research (early-stage opportunities) | not found | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/) |\n| NXTP Ventures | Former Venture Fellow (Argentina) | not found | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/) |\n| Kormo | Former growth team | not found | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/) |\n| Davi Holanda (Jota) | Portfolio founder; HOF backed from pre-seed | pre-2025 | [src](https://braziljournal.com/jota-e-avaliada-em-us-180-milhoes-em-rodada-com-haun-ventures/) |\n| Alter Global | Co-investor in Jota from pre-seed through Series A | pre-2025 | [src](https://braziljournal.com/jota-e-avaliada-em-us-180-milhoes-em-rodada-com-haun-ventures/) |\n| MAYA Capital | Led Jota seed, HOF participated | 2025-01 | [src](https://braziljournal.com/ex-pagbank-lanca-a-jota-que-facilita-as-financas-do-mei-no-whatsapp/) |\n| Haun Ventures | Led Jota Series A, HOF participated | 2026-06 | [src](https://braziljournal.com/jota-e-avaliada-em-us-180-milhoes-em-rodada-com-haun-ventures/) |\n| Ethan Ding (TextQL) | Portfolio founder | not found | [src](https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Jota pre-seed: date, size, lead, and how Rashid met Davi Holanda. Was it through NXTP or the Latin America network?\n- TextQL: which round HOF entered and when, given that the seed post doesn't name HOF. Was it an unannounced extension?\n- Identify \"Atlas\", and find HOF's rounds in Textla and Codesphere.\n- Join date at HOF and promotion to Principal (from LinkedIn, which needs a logged-in view).\n- Do the HOF growth funds follow on into his seed deals? Jota shows HOF following through the A, but the check sizes are unknown.\n- Any podcast, X or writing by him. None checked because the search budget was exhausted.\n\n## Sources\n- https://hofcapital.com/team/tahseen-rashid/ (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.vcsheet.com/who/tasheen-rashid (aggregator)\n- https://inforcapital.com/companies/textql/ (aggregator)\n- https://textql.com/blog/fundraising (2024-01-24)\n- https://textql.com/blog/textql-raises-17m-blackstone (2026-04-17)\n- https://braziljournal.com/jota-e-avaliada-em-us-180-milhoes-em-rodada-com-haun-ventures/ (2026-06-29)\n- https://revistapegn.globo.com/startups/noticia/2026/06/jota-assistente-financeiro-para-empreendedores-capta-r-150-milhoes-em-serie-a.ghtml (2026-06-29)\n- https://braziljournal.com/ex-pagbank-lanca-a-jota-que-facilita-as-financas-do-mei-no-whatsapp/ (Jan 2025)\n- https://revistapegn.globo.com/startups/noticia/2025/01/fintech-jota-focada-em-pequenos-negocios-capta-us-89-milhoes-em-rodada-seed-liderada-pela-maya-capital.ghtml (2025-01-24)\n- https://jota.ai (homepage, read 2026-11-10)\n- https://codesphere.com/company\n- https://textla.com\n", "person:teddy-citrin": "\n# Teddy (Theodore) Citrin\n\n## One-line take\nThe one Definition partner who publicly puts his name on deals, and the person to reach for its frontier-AI co-invest book (Cognition, Together, Modal, Valthos).\n\n## Crux\nCitrin runs a small two-partner firm with an $80M Fund I. Most of its capital sits in single-deal SPVs, which co-founder Weston Reynolds manages [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/321942/PDF/321942.pdf). He wins allocations in rounds led by others, mostly Lux-anchored ones, and only rarely leads (Firefly A 2026, tldraw A 2025 with Lux). He is the brand and the deal voice. Reynolds signs the SPV filings.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The only partner who claims deals.** His own X posts claim three: Ramp C (2021), Valthos seed (2025) and Cognition E (2026). Reynolds and Mahayni claim none.\n  - Cognition: \"Immensely proud to continue partnering closely with @ScottWu46\" [src](https://x.com/tcitrin/status/2097444281799643366). \"Continue\" points to a re-up: Definition was already in Cognition before the E.\n- **Valthos is a hidden position.** Definition is missing from every public Valthos investor list (OpenAI Startup Fund, Lux, Founders Fund) [src](https://www.pymnts.com/?p=3164828). Only his post shows it: \"Proud to partner with @kath_mcmahon and the @ValthosTech team\" [src](https://x.com/tcitrin/status/1981736414178918785).\n- **He signs the leads.** When Definition led Firefly's $20M Series A, he was the only investor quoted [src](https://pulse2.com/firefly-raises-20-million-series-a/).\n- **His bio says \"Founder of Definition\", singular** [src](https://x.com/tcitrin). Reynolds is a co-founder and controls the SPV manager [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1891366/000189136621000001/primary_doc.xml).\n- **Reposts are not deal credit.** He reposts the funding news of Together, Modal, Phonic, TerraFirma and Flapping Airplanes, but no source names him on those deals [src](https://x.com/tcitrin/status/1892620975209943056).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Definition holds positions in Together AI, Modal, Cognition, Applied Compute, Flapping Airplanes and Valthos, and Citrin is the visible decision-maker on that book.\n- Definition rarely leads, so treat it as a co-investor that brings SPV capital, not a seed lead to follow.\n- Firefly is the test of whether Citrin now leads AI rounds.\n\n## Background\n- Analyst at Starwood Capital, then product at eBay (NFX, aggregator) [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/teddy-citrin).\n- Greycroft:\n  - On the team page by 2019-10. Listed as Board Partner in 2021-12 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20211202154204/https://www.greycroft.com/team).\n  - His Principal title comes from NFX (aggregator) [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/teddy-citrin).\n- Founded Definition in 2021; the management company was formed 2021-07. He is managing member of the GP on the Definition I Form D [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1911690/000191169022000001/primary_doc.xml).\n- He got the seat by founding the firm. Its launch post went out 2024-02 [src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/teddycitrin_introducing-definition-activity-7166054088604913664-i1ZP).\n- Education: unconfirmed.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://definitioncap.com | linked from X bio |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | https://x.com/tcitrin | ~2.6k followers, ~200 posts; quote-posts portfolio rounds (2026-11-10) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/teddycitrin | URL inferred from his post URLs; unconfirmed |\n| GitHub | none found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNone found. No podcast search was run in this pass.\n\n## How they think\n- Concentrated backing of founders across rounds: Cognition is a re-up, and Together's seed through Series B were all claimed by the firm.\n- Leads physical-AI and hardware-adjacent deals. In his Firefly quote he says \"advances in AI and hardware\" make new safety systems possible [src](https://pulse2.com/firefly-raises-20-million-series-a/).\n\n## Read these first\n- Introducing Definition (Fund I launch, 2024-02) [src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/teddycitrin_introducing-definition-activity-7166054088604913664-i1ZP)\n- His Firefly quote [src](https://pulse2.com/firefly-raises-20-million-series-a/)\n\n## Social engagement\n- Mostly reposts of portfolio funding news (Together, Modal, Phonic, TerraFirma, Flapping, Cognition) and of OpenAI's Orchestra Bio acquisition.\n- Also amplified Hone, founded by a former Cognition chief of staff.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Firefly | Series A $20M | 2026-10-20 | yes (Definition) | not checked | [src](https://pulse2.com/firefly-raises-20-million-series-a/) |\n| Cognition | Series E | 2026-09-08 | no | a16z, Accel, Founders Fund, GC | [src](https://x.com/tcitrin/status/2097444281799643366) |\n| Valthos | Seed $30M | 2025-10-24 | no | OpenAI Startup Fund, Lux, Founders Fund | [src](https://x.com/tcitrin/status/1981736414178918785) |\n| Ramp | Series C $300M | 2021-08-24 | no | Founders Fund (lead) | [src](https://x.com/tcitrin/status/1430180709566717960) |\n| Tapcart (Greycroft) | Seed $4M | 2019 | Greycroft led | | [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/teddy-citrin) |\n| Cardless (Greycroft) | Seed | unconfirmed | | | [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/teddy-citrin) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Ramp C (2021-08-24).** Came one month after the management company formed and before the first Opportunity SPV Form D (2021-11-01). Ramp may be the deal the SPV model started with (inference; vehicle unconfirmed).\n- **Valthos (2025).** A Lux incubation, so access probably came through Lux (inference).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Weston Reynolds | Definition co-founder; runs the SPV manager | 2021 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1891366/000189136621000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Thabet Mahayni | Definition principal/partner; ex-Greycroft colleague | 2022 | [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/thabet-mahayni) |\n| Lux Capital | Co-investor in most AI deals | | [src](https://tldraw.dev/blog/announcing-tldraw-series-a) |\n| Scott Wu (Cognition) | Portfolio CEO | | [src](https://x.com/tcitrin/status/2097444281799643366) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which vehicle (fund or SPV) held Ramp and Valthos, and how big were the checks?\n- When did Definition first enter Cognition?\n- Education and start year at Greycroft.\n\n## Sources\n- https://x.com/tcitrin (via api.fxtwitter.com, 2026-11-10)\n- https://pulse2.com/firefly-raises-20-million-series-a/\n- https://signal.nfx.com/investors/teddy-citrin\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20211202154204/https://www.greycroft.com/team\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1911690/000191169022000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/321942/PDF/321942.pdf\n- https://www.pymnts.com/?p=3164828\n", "person:tess-van-stekelenburg": "\n# Tess van Stekelenburg\n\n## One-line take\nTess is a Lux partner who is also a named co-founder of Valthos, Lux Labs' AI biodefense de novo, which raised a $30M seed with the OpenAI Startup Fund and Founders Fund. Their real edge is building companies and recruiting researchers through the EVOLVED AI-bio hackathon, not writing outside checks ([src](https://decrypt.co/345959/ai-biodefense-startup-valthos-launches-30-million-openai-backing), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/valthos)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** Tess is a computational neuroscientist (Oxford and UCL) who led AI methods research before investing ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg)). At Lux they work as a company builder more than a check-writer. Lux's own Valthos page lists them under **Founders**, next to CEO Kathleen McMahon, not under partners ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/valthos), fetched 2026-11-10). Decrypt says Valthos was \"co-founded last November\" by McMahon and Tess, which puts the founding around Nov 2024 ([src](https://decrypt.co/345959/ai-biodefense-startup-valthos-launches-30-million-openai-backing)).\n- **How they source:** EVOLVED, an annual AI-for-biology hackathon they run. It drew 200+ ML researchers, software engineers and computational biologists. Their 2024 write-up was co-authored with NVIDIA's Vega Shah, and teams got early access to ESM3 plus about $300K in compute and APIs from Modal, Together, LatchBio, AWS and NVIDIA ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design)). By 2025 it had become \"Evolved Technology, Inc.\", with Nebius H100s and a showcase at NVIDIA HQ ([src](https://nebius.com/events/evolved-hackathon-global-sprint), [src](https://labcritics.com/?p=13112)).\n- **What constrains them:** Every non-founder at Lux holds the title \"Partner\", so the title says nothing about seniority or check authority ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people)). Tess has **no Lux investment announcement** bearing their name: their \"Selected Investments\" section is empty ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg)). Their book is AI-for-bio, aerospace and \"atoms\". It is not pre-training, chips or general labs.\n- **What they'd never do (inference):** treat computational biology as a near-term drug-asset play. Their own essay argues that ML methods barely matter today to getting a drug to an IND filing, and still calls for funding method R&D anyway ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Tess is a Valthos co-founder, not just the Lux partner on the deal.** Lux's Valthos company card lists \"Founders: Kathleen McMahon, Tess van Stekelenburg\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/valthos)). Decrypt: \"co-founded last November by Kathleen McMahon ... and Tess van Stekelenburg\" ([src](https://decrypt.co/345959/ai-biodefense-startup-valthos-launches-30-million-openai-backing)). Catenaa says the same ([src](https://catenaa.com/industries/technology/valthos-ai-biodefense-startup-launches-with-30m-funding)). **Tension:** Lux's Q3 2025 LP letter credits only McMahon, saying Valthos \"was co-founded with Kathleen McMahon\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report)). Lux's partner-facing copy does not credit Tess. The firm file credited Valthos to Wolfe and Reeves via cards, which misses this.\n- **The aggregator's ESM3 \"investment\" looks like a misread.** What the primary source shows is a partnership: EVOLVED offered \"Early access to EvolutionaryScale's ESM3 (98B-parameter protein model)\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design)). Lux did co-lead EvolutionaryScale's $142M seed (2024-06-25) with Nat Friedman and Daniel Gross, and NVentures and Amazon participated. But Fortune names no Lux individual ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/25/meta-ai-mafia-evolutionaryscale-llm-biology-seed-round-142-million/)), and Lux's EvolutionaryScale page does not list Tess (fetched 2026-11-10). Their role in the round is unconfirmed.\n- **The thesis in their own numbers:** binder design went from ~15,000 designs per 10 hits (2022) to a 46% hit rate (2024): \"That's the equivalent of testing 22 binders to get 10 hits.\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design), 2025-03-07).\n- **Contrarian on bio \"platforms\":** they concede that getting an asset to an IND \"requires very little of this\" (computational methods). They still argue for funding foundational method work, by analogy to ChatGPT and Physical Intelligence ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design)).\n- **Talent-flow thesis:** they expect an \"influx of software engineering talent\" into biology now that models are approaching experimental accuracy. That explains why their funnel is a hackathon for engineers rather than a biotech pitch day ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design)).\n- **The panel is the network.** At Lux's Oct 2025 AI Summit, Tess moderated the \"living canvas\" bio panel with McMahon (Valthos), Sam Sinai (Dyno Therapeutics) and Kenny Workman (LatchBio). LatchBio was also an EVOLVED compute sponsor, and the EVOLVED essay names Dyno as a Lux portfolio company ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-3rd-annual-ai-summit-a-canvas-for-creativity)). The byline is unresolved: the news index listed Tess as a co-author, but the post page says \"Unum Lux\".\n- **NVIDIA thread:** their EVOLVED write-up was co-authored by NVIDIA PMM lead Vega Shah, the 2025 edition showcases at NVIDIA HQ, and NVentures sits in EvolutionaryScale's seed alongside Lux ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design), [src](https://nebius.com/events/evolved-hackathon-global-sprint), [src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/25/meta-ai-mafia-evolutionaryscale-llm-biology-seed-round-142-million/)). No GV link to Tess was found.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Tess sits adjacent to frontier AI (AI-for-science, bio foundation models, AI biodefense), not inside pre-training, chips or general labs. The deal that matters is **Valthos**: frontier AI systems for biodefense, a $30M seed from the OpenAI Startup Fund, Lux and Founders Fund, announced 2025-10-24. Tess co-founded it ([src](https://catenaa.com/industries/technology/valthos-ai-biodefense-startup-launches-with-30m-funding), [src](https://decrypt.co/345959/ai-biodefense-startup-valthos-launches-30-million-openai-backing)). They are useful to me in two ways:\n1. as the Lux contact for any **bio foundation-model lab** seed (ESM-style, Evo/Arc lineage), where Lux has already co-led a $142M seed (EvolutionaryScale);\n2. as a **talent-sourcing channel**, because EVOLVED surfaces ML engineers entering biology before they found companies.\nThey are not the Lux contact for compute or chips (that is Reeves or Farshchi) or for general labs (Isford or Reeves). Their check authority is unconfirmed.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up across Europe, originally from the Netherlands. Master's across computational neuroscience and biological sciences at Oxford and UCL ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg)).\n- Research engineer at UCL's Gatsby Computational Neuroscience Unit (aggregator, [src](https://whisperlist.draftboard.com/vc/10842922-6e76-45e3-9c11-d46929d768dd/tess-van-valthos)). Computational neuroscience research lead at UCL, Jan 2016 to Jan 2018 (aggregator, [src](https://startupintros.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg)).\n- Led AI methods research in computational neuroscience at Oxford ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg)). The aggregator dates this Sep 2019 to Aug 2020 ([src](https://startupintros.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg)).\n- Co-founded a software startup; the name was not found ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg)).\n- Hummingbird Ventures investment team, Jan 2020 to Jan 2022 (aggregator, [src](https://startupintros.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg)). No Hummingbird deal credit found.\n- Lux Capital since Mar 2023 (aggregator, [src](https://startupintros.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg)). Now \"Partner\" on the firm site ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg)). Their title on arrival and any internal promotion date were not found.\n- Co-founded Valthos around Nov 2024 ([src](https://decrypt.co/345959/ai-biodefense-startup-valthos-launches-30-million-openai-backing)). It came out of stealth 2025-10-24.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.luxcapital.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg | Firm bio; the X and LinkedIn buttons point to \"#\" (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design | Lux news posts; only one confirmed byline |\n| X | https://x.com/velvetatom | Aggregator-only handle, unverified; the fetch returned 402 |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/tess-van-stekelenburg/ | Per aggregator; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Runs the EVOLVED hackathon instead |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-10 — Lux 3rd Annual AI Summit — moderated the \"living canvas\" AI x bio panel with McMahon, Sinai and Workman — [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-3rd-annual-ai-summit-a-canvas-for-creativity). The recap paraphrases the panel (AlphaFold saving \"billions of years\", tuning \"five to seven dials at once\") without saying who said what.\n- No podcast appearances found. One web search for podcasts and interviews returned only namesakes.\n\n## How they think\n- **Exponential, not single-leap, progress in comp bio.** Structure prediction is moving into functional design, and binder hit rates rose about 10^3 in two years ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design)).\n- **Contrarian:** today's computational platforms add little to IND-stage drug development, and the \"platform\" idea is out of favour. Tess still argues for funding method development and expects AI-native discovery workflows \"built from scratch\".\n- **Engineers are the input.** Give software engineers data, base models and compute that only big biotech platforms have, and companies follow.\n- **Dual-use awareness:** Valthos's premise is that AI lowers the barrier to bioweapons. Lux's letter frames it as moving from state actors to \"a few independent individuals\" ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report)).\n- Calls right or wrong: too early to judge. ESM3 and EvolutionaryScale, which they worked with via EVOLVED, did raise a large seed.\n\n## Read these first\n1. EVOLVED 2024: \"Code, Compile, Cure\" (2025-03-07). Their full thesis on AI for biology and the talent influx ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design)).\n2. Lux Q3 2025 report, Valthos section. How Lux frames the de novo it built with Tess ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report)).\n3. Lux 3rd Annual AI Summit recap. Shows who sits in their network ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-3rd-annual-ai-summit-a-canvas-for-creativity)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing verifiable found. The X handle @velvetatom comes only from an aggregator, and x.com blocked the fetch. Grace Isford amplified the Valthos launch on X, calling it a Lux \"incubation\" (as rendered by [PYMNTS](https://www.pymnts.com/?p=3164828)).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Valthos (AI biodefense; Lux Labs de novo) | Co-founder. Lux incubation, then a $30M seed | Founded ~2024-11; seed announced 2025-10-24 | Tess is a founder, not the lead investor. The OpenAI Startup Fund is reported as lead (search summary only) | OpenAI Startup Fund, Founders Fund, Lux | [src](https://decrypt.co/345959/ai-biodefense-startup-valthos-launches-30-million-openai-backing), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/valthos) |\n| EvolutionaryScale (ESM3) | $142M seed (Lux co-led at firm level) | 2024-06-25 | Lux yes; Tess's role unconfirmed (aggregator only) | Nat Friedman, Daniel Gross, Amazon, NVentures | [src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/25/meta-ai-mafia-evolutionaryscale-llm-biology-seed-round-142-million/), [aggregator](https://startupintros.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg) |\n| Varda (space manufacturing) | not stated | Lux investment 2020, before Tess joined | Lux card names Farshchi, not Tess | — | [aggregator](https://startupintros.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg), [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/varda) |\n| LatchBio | not stated | — | Tess's role unconfirmed (aggregator only); LatchBio was an EVOLVED sponsor | — | [aggregator](https://startupintros.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Valthos:** a Lux Labs de novo. Lux says it \"was co-founded with Kathleen McMahon\", former head of life sciences at Palantir ([src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report)). Decrypt and Lux's own company card name Tess as the other co-founder. One low-quality source adds a third founder, Victor Mao (unconfirmed) ([src](https://theccpress.com/valthos-ai-biodefense-secures-30m)). It came out of stealth on 2025-10-24 with $30M from the OpenAI Startup Fund, Lux and Founders Fund ([src](https://catenaa.com/industries/technology/valthos-ai-biodefense-startup-launches-with-30m-funding)). **Timing:** Lux was first money by construction, since it incubated the company for about 11 months before the priced round. OpenAI and Founders Fund came in at the seed. How McMahon and Tess met is not sourced.\n- **EvolutionaryScale and EVOLVED:** EVOLVED (fall 2024) had early access to ESM3 three to six months after Lux co-led the seed. This suggests Tess runs the post-investment ecosystem for Lux's bio-AI positions (inference).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Kathleen McMahon (Valthos CEO) | Valthos co-founder | ~2024-11 | [src](https://decrypt.co/345959/ai-biodefense-startup-valthos-launches-30-million-openai-backing) |\n| Vega Shah (NVIDIA) | Co-author of the EVOLVED 2024 post | 2025-03 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design) |\n| Grace Isford (Lux) | Colleague; publicly announced Valthos; Summit co-host | 2023 | [src](https://www.pymnts.com/?p=3164828) |\n| Sam Sinai (Dyno), Kenny Workman (LatchBio) | Panelists Tess moderated at the Lux AI Summit | 2025-10 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-3rd-annual-ai-summit-a-canvas-for-creativity) |\n| Christoph Krettler (Enveda), Kim Tran (Princeton), Matthew Nemeth (Lux) | EVOLVED co-organizers | 2024 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design) |\n| EvolutionaryScale | Provided ESM3 early access for EVOLVED | 2024 | [src](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design) |\n| Hummingbird Ventures | Former investment team | 2020-01 to 2022-01 | [aggregator](https://startupintros.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Does Tess still hold a Valthos operating role, or a board seat? Are they still investing day to day at Lux?\n- Who led Valthos's seed? Only search summaries say the OpenAI Startup Fund led it.\n- Tess's role, if any, on EvolutionaryScale's seed and on any Arc Institute (Evo) spinout.\n- What Hummingbird deals they worked on (2020–2022), and the name of the software startup they co-founded.\n- Is the X account @velvetatom theirs, and what do they post?\n- EVOLVED alumni who went on to found companies. These are the best leads for the owner.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg (fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/valthos (Founders field, fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design (2025-03-07)\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-capitals-3rd-annual-ai-summit-a-canvas-for-creativity (2025-10-28)\n- https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-quarter-3-2025-report\n- https://decrypt.co/345959/ai-biodefense-startup-valthos-launches-30-million-openai-backing\n- https://catenaa.com/industries/technology/valthos-ai-biodefense-startup-launches-with-30m-funding (2025-10-29)\n- https://www.pymnts.com/?p=3164828\n- https://theccpress.com/valthos-ai-biodefense-secures-30m (low quality)\n- https://www.tectonicdefense.com/icymi-valthos-emerges-from-stealth/\n- https://fortune.com/2024/06/25/meta-ai-mafia-evolutionaryscale-llm-biology-seed-round-142-million/\n- https://nebius.com/events/evolved-hackathon-global-sprint ; https://labcritics.com/?p=13112\n- Aggregators: https://startupintros.com/people/tess-van-stekelenburg ; https://whisperlist.draftboard.com/vc/10842922-6e76-45e3-9c11-d46929d768dd/tess-van-valthos\n", "person:thomas-joshi": "\n# Thomas Joshi\n\n## One-line take\nNEA's only investor with a research byline, a DSPy co-author who joined in 2025, is listed first on NEA's neolab memo, and has worked on Factory, Namespace and the CuspAI $450M B. He is the front door for any research lab pitching NEA ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/the-ai-neolab-wild-west)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** labs can talk shop with him. He is a co-author on the 2023 DSPy paper (Khattab, Zaharia, Potts et al.) ([src](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03714v1)), and his NEA Q&A gets into KV-cache attention sinks and evaluation design ([src](https://www.nea.com/team/thomas-joshi)).\n- **Constraint:** he is an Associate. Board seats on his deals sit with Tretikov, Faulkner (until 2026-05), Jacobson or Schoen, and his name always appears alongside them.\n- **What he'd never do (by record):** argue for scale-only generalists. The neolab memo he leads says the marginal pre-training dollar buys less capability than the one before it.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He wrote DSPy while working at a private-equity firm, not a lab.** The DSPy paper lists him as \"Thomas T. Joshi\", affiliation \"Calera Capital\" (a PE firm), alongside Stanford, Berkeley, Microsoft and Two Sigma authors ([src](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03714v1), 2023-10). NEA's bio describes him only as having \"AI Researcher, AI Engineering, and finance\" roles.\n- His stated obsession is sovereign AI infrastructure: \"Every day I think about three things particularly as it relates to Sovereign AI\" (compute, data, energy) ([src](https://www.nea.com/team/thomas-joshi)). That fits NEA's later Valarian lead (European sovereign infrastructure, 2026-07) and the Sakana sovereign-Japan thesis.\n- His view of where value is missing: \"treat evaluation, task design, and human-in-the-loop feedback as its own new layer\" ([src](https://www.nea.com/team/thomas-joshi)). This is the \"verifier\" layer of the neolab memo in earlier form.\n- Neolab memo line most likely his (it is the most research-specific): \"Whoever owns the environment owns the data flywheel\" ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/the-ai-neolab-wild-west), 2026-06-17, authors Joshi, Faulkner, Tretikov, Schoen).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh. If you seed a domain lab (materials, code, science) at $100-600M, Joshi is the person at NEA who screens it for the A or B. The CuspAI path, NEA-led A in 2025-09 then a $450M B co-lead with Kleiner Perkins in 2026-07, is the template ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/from-algorithms-to-atoms-part-ii-doubling-down-on-cuspai)).\n\n## Background\n- Columbia University, B.Sc. in Artificial Intelligence ([src](https://www.nea.com/team/thomas-joshi)).\n- First job: AI Engineer building infrastructure for language and classification models in conversational AI for healthcare (team page Q&A).\n- Calera Capital (affiliation on the DSPy paper, 2023-10) ([src](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03714v1)). His exact role there is not confirmed.\n- NEA: Associate on the Technology Investing Team from 2025 ([src](https://www.nea.com/team/thomas-joshi)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.nea.com/team/thomas-joshi | |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.nea.com/blog | co-author of 3 NEA posts in 2026 |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/thomas-joshi/ | listed on NEA page |\n| GitHub | DSPy contributor (paper co-author) | https://github.com/stanfordnlp/dspy |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found.\n\n## How they think\n- Pre-training returns are falling, open weights are catching up, and RL-first training puts value in owning the environment and the verifier, not the cluster ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/the-ai-neolab-wild-west)).\n- AI has \"a working recipe for capability\". The unsolved half is utility: defining the right problems and owning evaluation ([src](https://www.nea.com/team/thomas-joshi)).\n- Infrastructure focus: compute, data and energy for sovereign AI, plus inference optimization (team page).\n\n## Read these first\n- \"The AI Neolab Wild West\" (2026-06-17), lead author ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/the-ai-neolab-wild-west)).\n- \"From Algorithms to Atoms Part II: Doubling Down on CuspAI\" (2026-07-23) ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/from-algorithms-to-atoms-part-ii-doubling-down-on-cuspai)).\n- \"Namespace: CI/CD is dead, agents need computers\" (2026-03-23) ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/namespace-cicd-is-dead-agents-need-computers)).\n- DSPy paper (2023-10) ([src](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03714v1)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\nRows already in `data/rows/new-enterprise-associates-portfolio-deals.csv` (Factory B, Namespace A, CuspAI B). The CMS also lists him on Anyscale and Metabase, but those pre-date his 2025 arrival.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Factory | Series B ($50M at $300M) | 2025-09-25 | NEA lead (CMS team) | Sequoia, J.P. Morgan, NVIDIA | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/factory-the-platform-for-agent-native-development) |\n| Namespace | Series A | 2026-03-23 | NEA lead (post co-author) | | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/namespace-cicd-is-dead-agents-need-computers) |\n| CuspAI | Series B ($450M) | 2026-07-23 | NEA co-lead (post co-author) | Kleiner Perkins (co-lead), Bezos Expeditions | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/from-algorithms-to-atoms-part-ii-doubling-down-on-cuspai) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **CuspAI B:** NEA calls it \"one of the highest-conviction follow-on investments we've made\", about 10 months after leading the A ([src](https://www.nea.com/blog/from-algorithms-to-atoms-part-ii-doubling-down-on-cuspai)). Authors: Tretikov, Chopin, Schoen, Joshi, de Fressenel, a mix of the US neolab team and London.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| New Enterprise Associates | at: Associate | 2025 | [src](https://www.nea.com/team/thomas-joshi) |\n| Calera Capital | at: (role unconfirmed; DSPy paper affiliation) | 2023 | [src](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03714v1) |\n| Omar Khattab | DSPy co-author | 2023 | [src](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03714v1) |\n| Matei Zaharia | DSPy co-author (Databricks co-founder; NEA led Databricks' B) | 2023 | [src](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03714v1) |\n| Lila Tretikov | neolab team lead partner | 2025 | [src](https://www.nea.com/blog/the-ai-neolab-wild-west) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What did he do at Calera Capital, and when did he leave?\n- Did his DSPy link to Zaharia (Databricks) or Khattab feed any NEA deal flow?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.nea.com/team/thomas-joshi\n- https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03714v1\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/the-ai-neolab-wild-west\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/from-algorithms-to-atoms-part-ii-doubling-down-on-cuspai\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/namespace-cicd-is-dead-agents-need-computers\n- https://www.nea.com/blog/factory-the-platform-for-agent-native-development\n", "person:thomas-wolf": "\n# Thomas Wolf\n\n## One-line take\nA Hugging Face co-founder who says they have made about 100 AI angel investments and runs a Factorial microfund. They are an early signal on open-weights, small-model, robotics and AI-for-science founders, and now have Nvidia-deal liquidity behind those checks.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** they are the scientific face of the open-model ecosystem (Transformers, BLOOM/BigScience, FineWeb, the Ultra-Scale and Smol playbooks, LeRobot). Researchers who build on Hugging Face come to them first. They say they treat each angel company like one of Hugging Face's internal moonshots: a small team with freedom and light steering [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/3061136/).\n- **What constrains them:** the checks are small. Factorial first checks are $500k-1m, and a deal needs Matt Hartman's agreement [src](https://factorialcap.com/). Their day job is now inside Nvidia, so they act as a signal and a source of deals, not as a lead.\n- **What they'd never do:** back \"very thin wrappers\" on subsidised closed APIs [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/1171821/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- Scale of the book: \"about one hundred investment in AI\". That is far more than the 8 deals tracked in this repo [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/3061136/) (Benevolent Disruptors, 2025-12-08 per the show's transistor.fm feed, 2:54; transcript via ceointerviews).\n- First filter: \"Is it something you actually think is bringing like net positive to society\". Team quality comes second [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/3061136/) (21:14).\n- Why they invest at all: \"counterbalance a little bit the concentration of power that's happening in AI\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/3061136/) (19:37).\n- On European founders: \"I try to push them to be a bit more American, to be honest.\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/3061136/) (25:19)\n- Stated investing rule: \"you don't want to invest only in these very thin wrappers\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/1171821/) (MAD Podcast, 2026-08-07, 44:24).\n- They think closed-model pricing won't last: \"the closest model may be in a way subsidized right now\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/1171821/) (37:31).\n- They see Western open-weights labs as investable again: \"there is also now a range of promising companies in the West.\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/1171821/) (32:53)\n- Open weights as a recruiting weapon: a lab that stopped open-sourcing \"saw an immediate backslash, I think mostly on hiring.\" [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-thomas-wolf)\n- \"So the frontier is quite thin at the moment in a way\" [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-thomas-wolf). A year later they put open models at \"less than one year behind\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/1897988/) (31:12).\n- A change of view: in Dec 2025 they called the AI pause \"not the right approach\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/3061136/). By Aug 2026, after an OpenAI model attacked Hugging Face, they had signed a slowdown letter: \"I'm not in the camp of we need to rush really quickly right now.\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/1171821/) (52:26)\n- Deal flow from LeRobot: \"A lot of investors have actually bought the SO-100 arm\", and many startups build on it [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-thomas-wolf).\n- Hugging Face passed $100M in revenue in April 2026, and \"the main source of revenue right now is around storage.\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/1897988/) (8:42)\n- Hugging Face's pivot: \"We had to go back to our early VC and convince them.\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/1039426/) (Due Diligence, 5:04). Matt Hartman of Betaworks was the first investor [src](https://factorialcap.com/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** They are an angel and microfund partner, not a $20M-check peer. Two things lift them above low:\n1. Their angel positions sit in frontier and new labs: Humans& seed ($480M) [src](https://humansand.ai/), Grounded Superintelligence [src](https://www.gsi.company/), Kotoba [src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250805128670/en/Kotoba-Technologies-Raises-USD-11.83-Million-in-Seed-2-Funding-to-Accelerate-Commercialization-of-AI-powered-Simultaneous-Interpretation), Prior Labs [src](https://www.priorlabs.ai/about), RadixArk (aggregator only) [src](https://buttondown.com/AngelRegistry/archive/angel-registry-update-may-6-2026/), Runta [src](https://runta.com/blog/runta-the-execution-layer-for-agents/), ZeroEntropy [src](https://www.zeroentropy.dev/articles/zeroentropy-raises-4-2m-seed-round-to-make-ai-retrieval-truly-intelligent) and Dedalus Labs [src](https://pubt.io/view/04FE6C24A787599A474AEFD975A3234B8E3FD6FA).\n2. They are a gatekeeper to Hugging Face pre-training and post-training talent (FineWeb, SmolLM, Open-R1, LeRobot) that is likely to spin out after the Nvidia deal.\n\nUse them for co-invest signal and talent intros.\n\n## Background\n- Ecole Polytechnique, then a PhD in statistical/quantum physics (Sorbonne / ESPCI) [src](https://thomwolf.io/).\n- About five years as a European patent attorney at Cabinet Plasseraud. They wrote patents for startups and say \"A lot of my ideas were just not good ideas.\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/548709/) [src](https://thomwolf.io/)\n- Moved into ML after 2015. Julien Chaumond started Hugging Face in New York and invited them to build the science team [src](https://snorkel.ai/the-scientist-behind-hugging-face). They met Clem Delangue at an entrepreneur co-founder meeting [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/548709/).\n- Co-founder and CSO of Hugging Face since 2016. Sequoia has backed Hugging Face since 2022 [src](https://sequoiacap.com/founder/thomas-wolf). Hugging Face combined with (was acquired by) Nvidia in 2026; the price was stated by the MTS host, not by Wolf, and is not confirmed here [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/2990242/).\n- On the Factorial Capital partner list by 2025-08-26 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250826141835/https://factorialcap.com/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://thomwolf.io/ | essays (Einstein AI model, DeepSeek notes, Low Tech AI) |\n| Substack / blog | https://thomwolf.substack.com/ | 3 posts, 2025-12 to 2026-11; older Medium posts 2017-2019 |\n| X | https://x.com/Thom_Wolf | active; not mined this pass |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/thomwolf | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none; interviewed MiniMax's Olive Song at AI Engineer 2026 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5Cxe5dv2Xlw) | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n59 items are catalogued in `data/rows/thomas-wolf-voice-media.csv`. The most useful:\n- 2021-02 — Snorkel ScienceTalks — Productionizing ML Research — [link](https://snorkel.ai/the-scientist-behind-hugging-face) — admits they thought \"only the model was important\"\n- 2022-03 — TWIML #564 — BigScience and embodied learning — [link](https://twimlai.com/podcast/twimlai/big-science-embodied-learning-hugging-face-thomas-wolf/) — not mined (no transcript)\n- 2025-03 — STARTS Podcast — [link](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/548709/) — openness and risk are separate axes\n- 2025-09 — Sequoia Training Data — [link](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-thomas-wolf) — thin frontier; open weights help hiring; LeRobot deal flow\n- 2025-12 — Benevolent Disruptors Ep. 5 — [link](https://share.transistor.fm/s/3105e1a3) — ~100 angel investments, how they pick founders\n- 2026-08 — The MAD Podcast (Matt Turck) — [link](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/1171821/) — OpenAI-model intrusion; no thin wrappers\n- 2026-09 — This Week in Startups E2339 — [link](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/1897988/) — $100M revenue, storage-led\n\n## How they think\n- **Open models are about control.** The question is \"who has the hand on the switch\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/1171821/). Openness and risk are separate axes, and they are glad the very frontier stays mostly closed [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/548709/).\n- **Contrarian on AI for science.** \"We're currently building very obedient students, not revolutionaries.\" [src](https://thomwolf.io/blog/scientific-ai.html) At NeurIPS 2025 they said they were mostly scouting AI for science [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/567027/).\n- **Against the arms-race framing.** \"open-source knows no border.\" [src](https://thomwolf.io/blog/deepseek-sanctions.html) (rebutting Dario Amodei, 2025-01-31)\n- **Calls:** they bet early (2019) on open checkpoints driving library adoption: \"Sharing checkpoints made the library grow.\" [src](https://snorkel.ai/the-scientist-behind-hugging-face) They called Chinese labs open-source champions before the Western revival.\n\n## Read these first\n1. The Einstein AI model: why LLMs won't compress a century of science [src](https://thomwolf.io/blog/scientific-ai.html)\n2. Some notes on \"DeepSeek and export control\": their open-model geopolitics [src](https://thomwolf.io/blog/deepseek-sanctions.html)\n3. Benevolent Disruptors Ep. 5: their only long discussion of angel investing found so far [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/3061136/)\n4. MAD Podcast 2026: closed-model subsidies, investing rule, safety shift [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/1171821/)\n\n## Social engagement\nNot mined this pass. Their X (@Thom_Wolf) and Hugging Face posts focus on robotics (LeRobot, Reachy Mini), Open-R1 and open model releases [src](https://huggingface.co/thomwolf/activity/posts).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Humans& | Seed ($480M) | 2026-01-20 | no | | [src](https://humansand.ai/) |\n| Grounded Superintelligence | Seed (angel) | 2025-07 | no | | [src](https://www.gsi.company/) |\n| Kotoba | Seed 2 ($11.83M) | 2025-06-30 | no | | [src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250805128670/en/Kotoba-Technologies-Raises-USD-11.83-Million-in-Seed-2-Funding-to-Accelerate-Commercialization-of-AI-powered-Simultaneous-Interpretation) |\n| Prior Labs | angel (round undisclosed) | 2025 | no | | [src](https://www.priorlabs.ai/about) |\n| ZeroEntropy | Seed ($4.2M) | 2025-07-09 | no | | [src](https://www.zeroentropy.dev/articles/zeroentropy-raises-4-2m-seed-round-to-make-ai-retrieval-truly-intelligent) |\n| Dedalus Labs | Seed ($11M) | 2025-10-15 | no | | [src](https://pubt.io/view/04FE6C24A787599A474AEFD975A3234B8E3FD6FA) |\n| RadixArk | Seed ($100M) | 2026-05-05 | no | aggregator only | [src](https://buttondown.com/AngelRegistry/archive/angel-registry-update-may-6-2026/) |\n| Runta | Seed ($20M) | 2026-07-16 | no | | [src](https://runta.com/blog/runta-the-execution-layer-for-agents/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Hugging Face pivot:** the early VC (Matt Hartman of Betaworks) had to be convinced to back the move from the teen chatbot to open-source libraries [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/1039426/).\n- **Pollen Robotics:** Hugging Face's first hardware acquisition, which followed LeRobot's success [src](https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-thomas-wolf).\n- **Nvidia:** \"Working with Nvidia, who is amazingly aligned with us\" [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/2990242/) (they also cite shared robotics work). The deal mechanics were not discussed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hugging Face | Co-founder, CSO | 2016 | [src](https://thomwolf.io/) |\n| Factorial Capital / Matt Hartman | Investment partner; Hartman co-approves each deal | 2025-08 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250826141835/https://factorialcap.com/) |\n| Julien Chaumond | Co-founder; recruited Wolf | ~2016 | [src](https://snorkel.ai/the-scientist-behind-hugging-face) |\n| Clem Delangue | Co-founder; met at a co-founder meeting | ~2016 | [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/548709/) |\n| Julien Launay | Co-keynote PyData 2023 | 2023 | [src](https://amsterdam2023.pydata.org/cfp/speaker/F9SCH3) |\n| Robin Rombach | Slush 2025 co-panel; fellow Factorial partner | 2025 | [src](https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/437377/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which of the ~100 angel investments are frontier labs? Only 8 are tracked here.\n- Typical check size: no public source found.\n- Is the Thomas Wolf on Fractile's CS01 and the CuspAI filings the same person? Not confirmed; treat as unconfirmed.\n- Pre-2024 audio (TWIML 2022, Chai Time 2020, Radio Davos 2023, TED AI Show 2024) has not been transcribed or mined.\n- What role do they have inside Nvidia after the deal, and does it limit angel activity?\n\n## Sources\n- https://thomwolf.io/ ; https://thomwolf.substack.com/\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jBq7-A_FtMg (Benevolent Disruptors; transcript https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/3061136/)\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FU9A481E2W8 (MAD Podcast; transcript https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/1171821/)\n- https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-thomas-wolf\n- https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/1039426/ ; https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/548709/\n- https://snorkel.ai/the-scientist-behind-hugging-face\n- https://thomwolf.io/blog/deepseek-sanctions.html ; https://thomwolf.io/blog/scientific-ai.html\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6748oCo7Z_M ; https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t4gnwNXR_cM ; https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mmpW36WdFbI ; https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ggRcL-hyN0Y\n- https://factorialcap.com/ ; https://web.archive.org/web/20250826141835/https://factorialcap.com/\n", "person:tobi-coker": "\n# Tobi Coker\n\n## One-line take\nThey are a Felicis Partner who writes the firm's AI-infrastructure theses and leads Series A rounds in that layer (Automat, Evertune). Their closest deal to my lens is Datology, a pre-training data curation company where they co-authored the Series A post. The Exa deal is a lesson in patience: Felicis only got in at the Series C, more than two years after the first email.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** They work thesis-first and publish the map before the deal. They wrote essays on unstructured data (2023), APIs (2024), context engineering (2025) and the multi-model stack (2026), and ran a 23-person survey of engineering leaders ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-ai-stack-is-half-built)). Felicis portfolio companies (MotherDuck, Exa, Deep Infra, Runway) show up inside those essays as proof points. They also keep relationships warm for years: Exa took about 2.5 years from first email to investment ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/exa-series-c-search-in-the-age-of-agents)).\n- **What constrains them:**\n  - Every sourced lead is a Series A in infra or applied AI. None is a seed lab.\n  - On the frontier-adjacent deals they co-sign with GPs: Peter Deng on Exa and Evertune, Viviana Faga and Astasia Myers on Datology and MotherDuck. Their solo lead authority is visible only on Automat and on the Dylibso seed.\n  - They were not first into Exa. a16z led Exa's $250M Series C ([src](https://exa.ai/blog/series-c)).\n- **What they would never do (inferred from their writing, not stated):**\n  - Bet on fully autonomous coding agents arriving soon. They argued in 2024 that end-to-end agentic development is further out than most investors believe ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/software-devs-ai-agents)).\n  - Pick one model winner. They argue that building across many models is what scales ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/we-re-living-in-a-multi-model-world)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Exa first contact.** Tobi says \"our first email, which went out on March 12, 2024\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/exa-series-c-search-in-the-age-of-agents), 2026-10-05). Felicis invested only at the Series C. Their framing: \"the best founder relationships develop over time\".\n- **How they found Exa: as a user, during bio diligence.** \"I was looking at a stem-cell company\" when Google Scholar failed them. They then used Exa's research search product, back when the company was moving from its old name, Metaphor, to Exa ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/exa-series-c-search-in-the-age-of-agents)). Their health and bio background fed directly into an AI-infra sourcing edge.\n- **Felicis's Exa post came about 4.5 months after the round was announced.** Exa announced \"$250M at a $2.2B valuation led by a16z\" on 2026-05-20 ([src](https://exa.ai/blog/series-c)). Felicis's interview post is dated 2026-10-05 ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/exa-series-c-search-in-the-age-of-agents)). Exa's post does not name Felicis. Whether Felicis was in the May close or a later extension is unconfirmed.\n- **Survey-driven theses.** \"we surveyed twenty-three engineering leaders anonymously across the AI-native cohort\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-ai-stack-is-half-built), 2026-05-07). They say the thesis behind the post \"led to our investment in Deep Infra\". Felicis was a participant, not a lead, in Deep Infra's $107M Series B, which 500 Global and Georges Harik co-led ([src](https://deepinfra.com/series-b), 2026-05-04).\n- **Contrarian on the post-training bottleneck:** \"The bottleneck on post-training isn't compute. It's measurement.\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-ai-stack-is-half-built))\n- **Contrarian on model lock-in:** \"Switching from Anthropic to OpenAI for a specific workload is an afternoon.\" (co-authored with Feyza Haskaraman, [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/we-re-living-in-a-multi-model-world), 2026-06-15)\n- **Contrarian on coding agents (2024):** \"We are further out from end-to-end agentic software development than most investors believe\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/software-devs-ai-agents), 2024-05-06)\n- **Next thesis: physical-AI data infrastructure.** \"Someone will build the Snowflake of the physical world.\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-physical-world-has-no-data-stack), 2026-09-16)\n  - The post maps Foxglove, Rerun and Roboto and announces no deal, so this is likely an open hunt.\n  - It thanks Eric Deng, Jacob Zietek and Vishnu Mano for input. These are possible scouts or sources (unconfirmed).\n- **Serval was in their 2024 IT-automation market map.** The post lists Serval among the ITSM startups and announces no Felicis deal ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/ai-it-qa-incident-response), 2024-11-19, co-authored with Astasia Myers).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:**\n  - Datology: pre-training data curation, Series A, co-authored ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/datology-series-a)). This sits squarely in my pre-training interest.\n  - Exa: search infrastructure for agents.\n  - Automat (led): AI-native enterprise automation in the Serval mould ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/automat-reinventing-automation-for-ease-reliability-and-adaptability)).\n  - They mapped Serval's category early.\n  - They are a fast-promoted partner at a fund that can write $10M+ Series A checks, which is exactly the segment I said has alpha.\n- **Against:** No seed lab deal, no AI chips and no $100M+ seeds are personally attributed to them. Felicis's lab deals (Periodic Labs, Humans&, Ricursive) carry other partners' bylines in this repo's sources. On Exa they were late and not the lead.\n- **Use:** A co-investor or scout at Series A in data and agent infrastructure, and a thesis feed on where infra gaps are, such as the physical-world data stack and post-training evaluation.\n\n## Background\n- B.S. in Biology, Georgetown University, with minors in Economics and Computer Science ([src](https://www.felicis.com/team/tobi-coker)).\n- Morgan Stanley Investment Banking and Capital Markets, covering healthcare and biotech ([src](https://www.felicis.com/team/tobi-coker)). Dates not sourced.\n- Investor at The Production Board (TPB), where they helped found and seed six life sciences and human health companies ([src](https://www.felicis.com/team/tobi-coker)). Dates not sourced.\n- Felicis:\n  - Senior Associate (Mar 2021), Deal Partner (2022), Partner (Sep 2024), per village.ai, an aggregator I could not fetch. These dates are unconfirmed. A Felicis 2026-02-12 promotions post covers others (Eric Flaningam, James Detweiler), not Tobi ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner)).\n  - Felicis bylines now show \"Partner\" even on 2023 posts, so bylines can't date the promotion.\n- Pronouns: the Felicis bio uses \"he\", but this file uses \"they\" as a conservative default.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.felicis.com/team/tobi-coker | Bio, 10 featured companies, blog index |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.felicis.com/blog/archive | About 19+ bylined Felicis posts, 2023 to 2026; roughly monthly in 2025 and 2026 |\n| X | https://twitter.com/tecoker | Linked from the Felicis page; not reviewed (follower count not sourced) |\n| LinkedIn | http://www.linkedin.com/in/tobi-coker | Linked from the Felicis page; not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Hosts the Felicis interview with Will Bryk (Exa) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-10 — Felicis blog/video interview — \"Search In the Age of Agents\" with Will Bryk (Exa) — https://www.felicis.com/blog/exa-series-c-search-in-the-age-of-agents — covers the first-email date (2024-03-12), the stem-cell research use case, and a question on Exa Connect publisher pricing.\n- No external podcasts found. Web search was unavailable this run (budget exhausted), so coverage is incomplete.\n\n## How they think\n- **AI infra is behind deployment:** \"AI is the first software wave to reach mass deployment before its infrastructure was built.\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-ai-stack-is-half-built))\n- **Data quality as the lever:** \"Businesses can't have an AI strategy without a data strategy.\" (Datology post, [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/datology-series-a))\n- **Agent infra as ops:** \"Think of it as DevOps for cognition.\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/context-engineering), 2025-07-23)\n- **Model layer:** \"The model layer is a commodity and a premium good at the same time\" ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-ai-stack-is-half-built))\n- **Calls so far:**\n  - Right: the context-engineering and agent-infra thesis (mid-2025) came before Exa's run to a $2.2B valuation, and Serval was in their market map a year before its $1B Series B.\n  - Possibly early or wrong: the 2024 \"coding agents are further out\" call, given Cursor-era adoption. This is my judgement, not a sourced verdict.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [The AI Stack Is Half-Built](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-ai-stack-is-half-built) (2026-05-07): survey of 23 engineering leaders. Shows what they think is missing (cost tooling, post-training evaluation).\n2. [Search In the Age of Agents](https://www.felicis.com/blog/exa-series-c-search-in-the-age-of-agents) (2026-10-05): how they source, through patience and use of the product.\n3. [We're living in a multi-model world](https://www.felicis.com/blog/we-re-living-in-a-multi-model-world) (2026-06-15): their market map of what sits beyond the frontier-lab duopoly.\n4. [Data curation is the missing piece of the AI strategy](https://www.felicis.com/blog/datology-series-a) (2024-05-08): their pre-training data thesis.\n5. [The Physical World Has No Data Stack](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-physical-world-has-no-data-stack) (2026-09-16): their current hunting ground.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed. The X account @tecoker is linked from Felicis, but search budget was exhausted and X can't be fetched directly. Nothing sourced.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Exa | Series C ($250M at $2.2B) | Felicis post 2026-10-05; round announced 2026-05-20 | no (a16z led) | Peter Deng (Felicis co-author) | [Felicis](https://www.felicis.com/blog/exa-series-c-search-in-the-age-of-agents), [Exa](https://exa.ai/blog/series-c) |\n| Automat | Series A | 2025-11-19 | yes (Felicis led; sole author) | not stated | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/automat-reinventing-automation-for-ease-reliability-and-adaptability) |\n| Evertune | Series A | 2025-08-13 | yes (Felicis led and took a board seat; board member not named) | Peter Deng (co-author) | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-evertune) |\n| Datology | Series A | 2024-05-08 | not stated on Felicis page | Viviana Faga, Astasia Myers (co-authors) | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/datology-series-a) |\n| Metaplane | Series A | 2024-03-05 | not stated | not stated | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/metaplane-series-a) |\n| MotherDuck | Series B | 2023-09-20 | yes (Felicis led) | a16z, Redpoint, Madrona, Amplify, Altimeter; Viviana Faga co-author | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/motherduck-series-b) |\n| Dylibso | Seed | 2023-03-24 | yes (Felicis led; sole author) | not stated | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/dylibso-series-seed) |\n| Deep Infra | Series B ($107M) | 2026-05-04 | no (500 Global and Georges Harik co-led) | A.Capital, Crescent Cove, NVIDIA, Peak6, Samsung Next, Supermicro, Upper90 | [src](https://deepinfra.com/series-b); Tobi links the deal to their thesis ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-ai-stack-is-half-built)). The Felicis portfolio page credits Aydin Senkut's posts, so Tobi's personal role is unclear. |\n| Runway | Series C ($50M, Felicis led per portfolio page) | 2022-12 | firm led; Tobi's role unconfirmed | | [Felicis portfolio](https://www.felicis.com/portfolio/runway); listed as a featured company on [team page](https://www.felicis.com/team/tobi-coker) |\n| Aaru | Series A (Felicis portfolio stage) | not sourced | unknown | | [team page](https://www.felicis.com/team/tobi-coker), [portfolio](https://www.felicis.com/portfolio) |\n| Stainless | not sourced | not sourced | unknown | | [team page](https://www.felicis.com/team/tobi-coker) |\n| Cellino, Maze | not sourced | | unknown | | [team page](https://www.felicis.com/team/tobi-coker); bio deals, posts \"The Promise of Personalized Stem Cells\" and \"Enter the Maze\" |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Exa:**\n  - First email 2024-03-12, around the time the company was moving from Metaphor to Exa.\n  - Tobi was a user first: they used Exa's research search while doing diligence on a stem-cell company.\n  - Felicis did not invest in the Series A or B. It got in at the Series C, which a16z led ([src](https://exa.ai/blog/series-c)), about 2.5 years after first contact.\n  - Peter Deng co-signed. Peter says the team first met Will Bryk in 2024 ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/exa-series-c-search-in-the-age-of-agents)).\n- **Automat:** Felicis led the Series A. Founders Lucas Ochoa and Gautam Bose met at Carnegie Mellon and worked together at Google Creative Lab. The thesis is that text-to-action from robotics plus VLMs and LLMs replaces brittle RPA ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/automat-reinventing-automation-for-ease-reliability-and-adaptability)). How Tobi met them is not stated.\n- **Evertune:** Felicis led the Series A and joined the board, co-signed with Peter Deng. CEO Brian Stempeck helped grow The Trade Desk from 8 people to IPO ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-evertune)). The intro path is not stated.\n- **Datology:** Series A co-authored with Faga and Myers. The thesis: \"better data equates to better performance\" for pre-training efficiency ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/datology-series-a)). The post cites the MotherDuck investment, which suggests a continuous data-infra thesis line from 2023. Lead status is not stated on the Felicis page.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Peter Deng (Felicis GP) | Co-author on Exa and Evertune | 2025 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-evertune) |\n| Viviana Faga (Felicis GP) | Co-author on Datology and MotherDuck | 2023 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/motherduck-series-b) |\n| Astasia Myers (Felicis Senior Venture Partner) | Co-author on Datology and IT/QA thesis | 2024 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/ai-it-qa-incident-response) |\n| Feyza Haskaraman (Felicis Partner) | Co-author, multi-model thesis | 2026 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/we-re-living-in-a-multi-model-world) |\n| Sundeep Peechu (Felicis MP) | Co-author, healthcare admin thesis | 2025 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/healthcare-ai-admin) |\n| Will Bryk (Exa CEO) | Courted from 2024-03-12, invested at Series C | 2024 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/exa-series-c-search-in-the-age-of-agents) |\n| Brian Stempeck (Evertune CEO) | Felicis led Series A and took a board seat | 2025 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-evertune) |\n| Lucas Ochoa, Gautam Bose (Automat) | Led Series A | 2025 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/automat-reinventing-automation-for-ease-reliability-and-adaptability) |\n| Ari Morcos, Bogdan Gaza, Matthew Leavitt (Datology) | Series A investor | 2024 | [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/datology-series-a) |\n| The Production Board | Former employer | dates not sourced | [src](https://www.felicis.com/team/tobi-coker) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Was Felicis in the May 2026 Exa close or a later extension? What was the check size?\n- Did Felicis lead Datology's Series A? The Felicis page doesn't say, and I couldn't confirm press this run.\n- Tobi's personal role on Runway, Aaru and Stainless (listed as featured companies but no byline found). Who sits on the Evertune board?\n- Confirm the promotion dates (Mar 2021, 2022, Sep 2024) from LinkedIn or a Felicis post.\n- Has the physical-AI data-stack thesis produced a deal yet (Foxglove, Rerun, Roboto, or a new company)?\n- Who are Eric Deng, Jacob Zietek and Vishnu Mano (thanked in the physical-AI post)? They may be scouts or operators.\n- Review @tecoker on X for founder-level signal.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.felicis.com/team/tobi-coker\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/archive\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/exa-series-c-search-in-the-age-of-agents\n- https://exa.ai/blog/series-c\n- https://www.felicis.com/portfolio/exa\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/datology-series-a\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/automat-reinventing-automation-for-ease-reliability-and-adaptability\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/investing-in-evertune\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/motherduck-series-b\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/metaplane-series-a\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/dylibso-series-seed\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-ai-stack-is-half-built\n- https://deepinfra.com/series-b\n- https://www.felicis.com/portfolio/deepInfra\n- https://www.felicis.com/portfolio/runway\n- https://www.felicis.com/portfolio\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/we-re-living-in-a-multi-model-world\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-physical-world-has-no-data-stack\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/context-engineering\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/software-devs-ai-agents\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/ai-it-qa-incident-response\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/healthcare-ai-admin\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/ais-future-depends-on-managing-apis\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/the-unstructured-data-problem\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner\n- village.ai (aggregator; blocked to direct fetch; promotion dates only)\n", "person:tobias-citron": "\n# Tobias Citron\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 by the Etched price-setters pass. Primary's own site uses \"he\" for Citron, so this file does too. Sources are his three bylined Primary posts, his team page, Calcalist, and two podcast episode descriptions. I made local machine transcripts (whisper tiny.en) of both of his podcast episodes, Wharton Tech Toks (2024) and Venture with Grace (2026).*\n\n## One-line take\nOn Etched, Citron mattered only as the second name on Primary's chip lane. The real story is **Atero**: Citron bylined the post on it. He led a $12M seed in a GPU-memory startup in 2024, and Crusoe bought it about 13 months after founding for roughly $150M. That makes him a newly promoted Primary partner with one fast, sourced compute win, who now runs the cyber practice. On Etched he was the formal second on the chips lane (\"I cover the bottom three with Brian\"), but he was never its champion or price-setter.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He works thesis-first. Primary goes \"down a rabbit hole\" on a technical bottleneck, publishes about it, and lets the writing pull in founders. Atero came from a newsletter reader re-introducing a founder Citron had already met ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-atero--and-why-crusoe-agreed)). Eos came from a market Primary was about to incubate itself ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/identity-security-for-the-agentic-era)). He also has a Tel Aviv network. Atero and its co-lead lool are Israeli, and his cyber post pitches New York as home to \"the largest Israeli community outside of Israel\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/investing-in-the-reinvention-of-cybersecurity)).\n- **What constrains him:** He runs a seed lane at a seed firm, with about two new deals a year: \"I will make maybe two investments a year\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/investing-in-the-reinvention-of-cybersecurity)). He was promoted into **cybersecurity**, not compute. Compute belongs to Schechter and Lorenzi. Both of his exits so far (Atero and Eos) were early sales of about $150M or undisclosed, not compounding outcomes.\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** Lead a growth round or set the price on a late-stage chip round. He also wouldn't back cyber visibility or dashboard plays: \"VCs have over-funded visibility\" (same source).\n- **On Etched specifically:** No post or release names Citron's role. Every Etched post is bylined Schechter, and Schechter \"has led these investments and sits on the Board\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round)). But in the Wharton Tech Toks episode (recorded around June 2024, published 2024-11-14), Citron says he covers chips, foundation models and AI middleware jointly with Schechter: \"I cover the bottom three with Brian\" ([audio, ~16:00](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/primary-venture-partners-funding-deeptech-innovation/id1538885021?i=1000676980531); machine transcript). So he was the formal second partner on Primary's AI-chip lane at the time of Etched's Series A. He was not the champion, the board member or the price-setter. In January 2026 he described himself as \"involved in\" an AI chips business (Etched, which the machine transcript renders as \"X\") but \"invested in\" Atero ([Venture with Grace, ~25:11](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/tobias-citron-primary-vc-partner-on-cyber-ai-infrastructure/id1729243199?i=1000745654041)). The co-listing runs both ways: Schechter is on Citron's Atero card too.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Atero was Citron's deal, and Primary led it.** His bylined post says \"We led the seed round in Atero just over a year ago\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-atero--and-why-crusoe-agreed), page dated 2025-10-22). The repo's Schechter and Primary files credit Atero to Schechter with the round unknown. Founder signal: Atero CEO Alon Yariv's testimonial on Citron's page says \"his belief in us was pivotal to scaling with ambition\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/tobias-citron)).\n- **Price and timing of Atero:** \"$12 million in a previously undisclosed seed round\", led by Primary and co-led by lool Ventures. Founded July 2024; Crusoe's purchase was \"estimated at around $150 million in cash and stock\" ([Calcalist, 2025-08-21](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rkdw11antlx)). The price is a press estimate. Crusoe did not disclose terms.\n- **How Atero was sourced.** Primary published two posts on AI workload orchestration (March and May 2024). Then \"A reader of our newsletter re-introduced us to Alon Yariv\". The underlying intro path ran through Roy Katznelson: \"Roy introduced us to Alon originally and then re-introduced us\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-atero--and-why-crusoe-agreed)).\n- **Eos was nearly an incubation.** \"we were considering incubating a business in this space\". Operator-in-Residence Doug Steinberg did the diligence. Primary seeded Eos in summer 2025 and AppViewX bought it about nine months later ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/identity-security-for-the-agentic-era), 2026-03-19).\n- **Pace:** \"I will make maybe two investments a year\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/investing-in-the-reinvention-of-cybersecurity)).\n- **Dated title step:** In November 2024 he was \"a principal at Primary Venture Partners focused on data / developer tools\", AI and cyber ([Wharton Tech Toks description](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/primary-venture-partners-funding-deeptech-innovation/id1538885021?i=1000676980531)). His own post then says \"my promotion to Partner is being announced to the outside world\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/investing-in-the-reinvention-of-cybersecurity)).\n- **Date caveat on the promotion post:** The page date is 2026-02-10, the same date as the Fund V post and the compute thesis post, so it may reflect a site relaunch. The text refers to an Anthropic disclosure \"Just last week\", which suggests it was written earlier. A host description on 2026-01-18 already calls him Partner. Treat late 2025 to early 2026 as the promotion window.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Atero is squarely in the AI compute bucket: a $12M led seed, roughly a 13-month exit to a top AI cloud, and a Tel Aviv GPU-memory team. Citron is a real person to know for Israeli GPU and infrastructure deal flow. But Primary has pointed him at cybersecurity, he makes about two deals a year, and he can't write or anchor a $20M check. He played no sourced part in Etched's pricing. Deals that matter to me: **Atero** (led), **Cake AI** (2021 seed, AI infrastructure), and Etched (on the card only).\n\n## Background\n- Princeton, AB in Public Policy; Wharton MBA ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/tobias-citron)). Dates not found.\n- Worked on a New York startup in his early 20s, and met Primary at 25, before business school ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/investing-in-the-reinvention-of-cybersecurity)).\n- Helped build Radicle Labs, a private-markets research firm spun out of Prehype ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/tobias-citron)). A podcast host's description adds \"founding GM\", Deloitte strategy consulting, Citi investment banking, and a Rough Draft Ventures fellowship ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/tobias-citron-primary-vc-partner-on-cyber-ai-infrastructure/id1729243199?i=1000745654041)). These are secondary and undated.\n- **His own account** ([Wharton Tech Toks](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/primary-venture-partners-funding-deeptech-innovation/id1538885021?i=1000676980531); machine transcript):\n  - **Radicle Labs (late 2016 / early 2017):** joined at Prehype on the founding team. Radicle was trying to be a PitchBook/CB Insights competitor, with Dow Jones as a strategic investor and a team of 12–15.\n  - **Primary internship:** met co-founder Ben Sun before business school and interned for Sun before and during his first year at Wharton.\n  - **Second year at Wharton:** worked for Schechter.\n  - **Joining full-time:** joined Primary after graduating.\n  - **First months:** spent the first six months on Schechter's Plural incubation, which explains the 2020 Plural card.\n  - **Specialty:** since then he has specialized in data infrastructure, developer tools, DevOps and cyber, splitting time about 50/50 between cyber and everything else.\n  - **Tenure:** \"a little bit over three years\" at Primary in a mid-2024 recording, so he joined about 2021.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.primary.vc/team/tobias-citron | Bio, cards, testimonial (2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | Primary articles, bylined: Atero (2025-10-22), cyber/promotion (2026-02-10), Eos (2026-03-19) | Primary newsletter carried the 2024 orchestration posts (not located) |\n| X | nothing found | Not listed on Primary's page |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/tobias-citron-65500b78/ | Not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-11-14 — Wharton Tech Toks — \"Primary Venture Partners: Funding Deeptech Innovation\" — [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/primary-venture-partners-funding-deeptech-innovation/id1538885021?i=1000676980531). Per the description: where value accrues in AI, AI's effect on cyber, and how he broke into VC. Title \"principal\" at the time.\n- 2026-01-18 — Venture with Grace — \"Tobias Citron, Primary VC Partner on Cyber & AI Infrastructure\" (about 60 min) — [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/tobias-citron-primary-vc-partner-on-cyber-ai-infrastructure/id1729243199?i=1000745654041). Description topics: the refragmentation of cyber in the AI era, the Anthropic breach, and early signals of breakout developer platforms. The host description lists Etched, Cake, Stanza and SparkAI as his companies, but SparkAI is on Schechter's card at Primary, so treat the list as loose.\n- **Wharton Tech Toks transcript.** I made a local whisper tiny.en machine transcript, so quotes may carry small errors. The recording date is inferred as about June 2024 from his reference to the Apple and OpenAI deal \"over the last whatever week or two\". What stood out:\n  - **Primary's rules:** \"we really only lead, we're a concentrated fund\". It makes 12–15 investments a year, about 4 of them incubations. The average check is $3–4M and the range $1–7M (~01:49–02:14).\n  - **His coverage:** the AI stack as compute, models, middleware and apps. \"I cover the bottom three with Brian\" (~16:00).\n  - **Atero before the deal:** he mentions a GPU-optimization company \"we've been talking to for a bit now\" that is neither hardware nor middleware (~15:45). That is almost certainly Atero, before Primary led the seed.\n  - **AI value:** most of it goes to incumbents, but \"the pie\" leaves room for generational startups. He cites Coatue's mobile-shift analysis (~18:00–20:15).\n  - **Career advice:** be the first choice for a niche, not the third-best generalist (~33:00+).\n- **Venture with Grace transcript** (2026-01-18; local whisper tiny.en machine transcript). What stood out:\n  - **Etched vs Atero, in his own words:** \"I'm involved in a business called X, which is an AI chips business\", then \"I invested in a business called [A]terro\" (~25:11). The transcript renders Etched as \"X\". He says \"involved in\" Etched but \"invested in\" Atero, which matches the written record.\n  - **Next compute-adjacent idea:** a cloud-security layer for GPU clouds, because CSPMs like Wiz \"weren't built as much for the GPU stack\" (~25:42).\n  - **Primary's numbers:** entry checks average $4–5M, \"up to like $10 million\" for \"really big seed rounds\" (~39:10). He expects 6–7 cyber investments from Fund V. Primary leads or co-leads, and its first check is always pre-seed or seed.\n  - **Sourcing:** in Israel he deliberately co-leads seeds with local funds that want a US partner, and names Team8 (~33:47–34:53). In New York he draws on operators at Wiz, Cyera and Axonius (~57:30).\n  - **Founders:** \"I'm a huge believer in the AI native young founder\" (~30:22). Both of his 2025 cyber deals had repeat-operator founders.\n  - **Origin of his lanes:** his Radicle data experience led him to data infrastructure first, and cyber followed through shared CIO buyers (~04:00–04:55).\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** AI-era cyber risk is \"existential\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/tobias-citron)). The posture-management and dashboard era overbuilt visibility, so the next winners automate remediation (Teleskope, Eos). Infrastructure cost is the wedge in AI: orchestration tools (Kubernetes, Slurm) weren't built for AI workloads, and memory-efficient GPU orchestration saves money ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-atero--and-why-crusoe-agreed)).\n- **New York and Israel:** He pitches New York as the cyber hub, citing Wiz, Cyera and Axonius moving their headquarters there ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/investing-in-the-reinvention-of-cybersecurity)).\n- **Calls right:** Atero (seed 2024, sold to Crusoe 2025) and Eos (seed 2025, sold 2026). Both were quick sales. Whether a faster, smaller exit is a feature of his style or a limit is open.\n- **Price:** I found nothing on how he sets or negotiates price.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Why we invested in Atero, and why Crusoe agreed](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-atero--and-why-crusoe-agreed): his clearest deal-origin story, the compute one.\n2. [Investing in the reinvention of cybersecurity](https://www.primary.vc/articles/investing-in-the-reinvention-of-cybersecurity): the promotion post, with his pace, thesis and hiring.\n3. [Eos: identity security for the agentic era](https://www.primary.vc/articles/identity-security-for-the-agentic-era): incubation-adjacent seeding.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. Primary lists no X handle for him, and I did not fetch LinkedIn.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Atero (GPU memory / orchestration; acquired by Crusoe ~2025-08) | Seed, $12M | 2024 | Yes, Primary led (lool co-led); Citron bylined | lool, MVP Ventures, 91 VC, Encoded, Garuda, angels incl. Assaf Rappaport, Thomas Dohmke | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-atero--and-why-crusoe-agreed), [Calcalist](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rkdw11antlx) |\n| Eos (agent identity; acquired by AppViewX 2026-03) | Seed | summer 2025 | Primary seeded it, lead not stated; he is the only partner on the card | not stated | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/identity-security-for-the-agentic-era) |\n| Etched | Seed (card: 2023) | 2023-03 | Primary led; **Schechter** named as the lead partner and board member; Citron card only | Schechter | [card](https://www.primary.vc/team/tobias-citron), [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round) |\n| Teleskope | Series A, $25M (card: 2023) | announced 2025-01-06 | Not stated | M13, Lerer Hippeau | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/data-security-rebuilt-for-companies-deploying-ai) |\n| Plural | Seed (card: 2020); $6M seed announced 2025-09-30 | 2020 / 2025 | Primary led the $6M seed (Schechter byline) | Company Ventures, Capital One Ventures | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/ai-for-devops-at-scale) |\n| Cake AI | Seed | 2021 | not stated | Schechter | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/tobias-citron) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Atero (2024):**\n  - **Thesis first.** In spring 2024, Primary was down a \"rabbit hole\" on AI workload orchestration and wrote two posts on it in March and May 2024.\n  - **The founder came back.** Primary had met Alon Yariv at his previous startup through Roy Katznelson. A newsletter reader re-introduced Yariv when he started Atero, and Katznelson also re-introduced him.\n  - **Terms and team.** Primary led a $12M seed, co-led with lool. Yariv built a team that included Omer Landau, Ben Chess and Eyal Salomon.\n  - **Exit.** Crusoe bought Atero to optimize its inference platform, about 13 months after founding, for an estimated ~$150M in cash and stock. The 25-person team became Crusoe's Israel R&D center ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-atero--and-why-crusoe-agreed), [Calcalist](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rkdw11antlx)).\n  - **Who was first.** Primary was the first institutional lead. No earlier round is reported.\n- **Etched (2023):** Schechter's deal by every primary source.\n  - Seed of $5.4M at ~$34M in March 2023 ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=662004)). Primary also co-led the A, and Stripes led the B at a $5B post about 33 months after the seed. Sequoia then led the C and Jane Street the $700M round (see firms/primary-venture-partners.md).\n  - Citron's card listing is the only link. Whether he was the second voting partner on the investment committee, did diligence, or was simply listed as the infrastructure-pod partner is **not sourced**.\n- **Eos (2025):** Primary researched agent identity for months and almost incubated a company there. It met Archit Lohokare and Kash Ivaturi (ex-CyberArk and Idaptive) in summer 2025, seeded them, and AppViewX bought the company in March 2026 ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/identity-security-for-the-agentic-era)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Brian Schechter (Primary) | Co-listed on Etched, Atero, Cake, Teleskope and Plural cards; infrastructure pod | 2020 | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/tobias-citron) |\n| Alon Yariv (Atero, now Crusoe) | Backed as CEO; knew him from his previous startup | before 2024 | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-atero--and-why-crusoe-agreed) |\n| Roy Katznelson | Made the intro to Yariv twice | — | same |\n| lool Ventures | Atero seed co-lead | 2024 | [src](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rkdw11antlx) |\n| Crusoe | Bought Atero | 2025-08 | same |\n| Doug Steinberg (Primary OIR) | Did the research behind Eos | 2025 | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/identity-security-for-the-agentic-era) |\n| Radicle Labs / Prehype | Helped build it before Primary | — | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/tobias-citron) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Etched second-partner question:** Partly answered. Citron says he co-covered chips and models with Schechter (mid-2024). Still unknown is whether he did diligence or sat on the IC for the March 2023 seed, when he had been at Primary for about two years. The Venture with Grace host lists Etched among \"his\" companies.\n- **Transcript quality:** Both episodes were machine-transcribed with whisper tiny.en (a small model). Re-run with a larger model before quoting him outside this repo.\n- **GPU-cloud security:** Has Citron since seeded a company in the GPU-cloud security space he described in January 2026?\n- **How he decides on price:** nothing found. Primary leads or co-leads every deal. Its checks ran $1–7M in 2024 ([audio](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/primary-venture-partners-funding-deeptech-innovation/id1538885021?i=1000676980531)) and \"up to like $10 million\" by 2026 ([audio](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/tobias-citron-primary-vc-partner-on-cyber-ai-infrastructure/id1729243199?i=1000745654041)), so it sets or co-sets the price on every first check. No source shows Citron's own pricing rule.\n- Atero: Citron's check size and ownership, the seed price, and confirmation of the $150M figure beyond Calcalist. Also, who wrote Primary's March and May 2024 orchestration newsletter posts?\n- Entry title and join date at Primary. LinkedIn was not fetched, and the \"since 2021\" claim is from a host description.\n- Who is Roy Katznelson (role or firm)?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.primary.vc/team/tobias-citron (photo checked: HTTP 200 image/avif, 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-atero--and-why-crusoe-agreed (2025-10-22)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/investing-in-the-reinvention-of-cybersecurity (2026-02-10)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/identity-security-for-the-agentic-era (2026-03-19)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round (2026-06-30)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/data-security-rebuilt-for-companies-deploying-ai (2025-01-06)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/ai-for-devops-at-scale (2025-09-30)\n- https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/rkdw11antlx (2025-08-21)\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=662004 (2024-06-25)\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/primary-venture-partners-funding-deeptech-innovation/id1538885021?i=1000676980531 (2024-11-14)\n- https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/tobias-citron-primary-vc-partner-on-cyber-ai-infrastructure/id1729243199?i=1000745654041 (2026-01-18)\n", "person:tom-gillespie": "\n# Tom Gillespie\n\n## One-line take\nThe only early-stage check-writer at a Japan-backed growth fund, bringing two decades of In-Q-Tel defense dealflow to seed-to-B deep tech.\n\n## Crux\nGillespie runs the Geodesic Alliance Fund (space, maritime, energy, autonomy), which leads or co-leads $5-10M checks from seed to Series B [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/geodesic-alliance-fund-founder-faq/). His edge is an In-Q-Tel network built over ~20 years [src](https://geodesiccap.com/about/). The constraint: checks are too small to anchor a $20M seed, and the thesis is defense/dual-use, so frontier-AI exposure is narrow (one named AI deal: Smack).\n\n## Differential insight\n- Founders are told to email him directly: \"tgillespie@geodesiccap.com\" [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/geodesic-alliance-fund-founder-faq/).\n- Smack thesis cited \"side-by-side with Japanese forces\" — Indo-Pacific relevance, which matches Geodesic's Japanese corporate/bank capital base (per firms/geodesic-capital.md) [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-smack-building-an-ai-frontier-lab-for-national-security/).\n- Board role is soft-promised: \"a lead partner from the Alliance Fund will play an active role\" [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/geodesic-alliance-fund-founder-faq/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. A useful co-lead partner for a defense-flavoured frontier-AI seed (Smack), with a direct inbound channel, but sub-$10M checks and a defense-only remit mean he won't price a $20M general frontier-lab seed.\n\n## Background\n- Earlier: international privatization work and U.S. Congress staff roles (telecom, space, finance, foreign affairs); dates unconfirmed [src](https://geodesiccap.com/about/).\n- Booz Allen Hamilton strategy consultant (aerospace, financial services, national security clients); dates unconfirmed [src](https://geodesiccap.com/about/).\n- In-Q-Tel ~20 years: \"as a Managing Partner on their Investment Team\" and Investment Lead for Deep Tech; 20+ space ventures plus robotics, materials, power, autonomy [src](https://geodesiccap.com/about/). Exact years unconfirmed.\n- Geodesic Capital, Partner & Head of Alliance Fund, 2025 (fund FAQ published 2025-10-16) [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/geodesic-alliance-fund-founder-faq/).\n- A.B. Economics, Stanford; MBA Finance, Wharton [src](https://geodesiccap.com/about/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://geodesiccap.com/about/ | Firm bio, verified 2026-11-10 |\n| Email | tgillespie@geodesiccap.com | Published in Alliance FAQ [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/geodesic-alliance-fund-founder-faq/) |\n| X | unconfirmed | not checked (web search budget exhausted) |\n| LinkedIn | unconfirmed | not checked |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- None verified this pass (web search unavailable).\n\n## How they think\n- Defense problems first: Smack chosen for \"one of the hardest problems in defense\" and founders' clarity on the problem [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-smack-building-an-ai-frontier-lab-for-national-security/).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Smack Technologies | Series A ($32M) | 2026-03 | co-lead (Geodesic Alliance) | Costanoa (co-lead), Point72 Ventures, Felicis, First In, Scribble | [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-smack-building-an-ai-frontier-lab-for-national-security/) |\n\nIn-Q-Tel deals (20+ space ventures) are not individually attributed by any source checked.\n\n## Deal stories\n- Smack: Geodesic's post is co-bylined by Gaspar-Asaoka and Gillespie, dated 2026-03-02; first-in at Series A for Geodesic (no prior Geodesic Smack round found); board seat unconfirmed [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-smack-building-an-ai-frontier-lab-for-national-security/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Rayfe Gaspar-Asaoka | Alliance Fund co-partner; Smack co-author | 2026 | [src](https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-smack-building-an-ai-frontier-lab-for-national-security/) |\n| In-Q-Tel | Former Managing Partner | ~20 yrs to 2025 | [src](https://geodesiccap.com/about/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact In-Q-Tel years and which IQT space/AI deals he personally led.\n- Whether he took the Smack board seat.\n- Alliance Fund size and LPs (FAQ only says Geodesic ~$1B AUM).\n\n## Sources\n- https://geodesiccap.com/about/\n- https://geodesiccap.com/insight/geodesic-alliance-fund-founder-faq/\n- https://geodesiccap.com/insight/why-we-invested-in-smack-building-an-ai-frontier-lab-for-national-security/\n", "person:tom-hulme": "\n# Tom Hulme\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. The primary sources are two auto-generated podcast transcripts (20VC, 2024-05-08; TBPN, 2026-03-19), read through a fetch tool. Quotes keep the transcripts' errors (\"acrue\", \"Tessle\"). Neither transcript has timestamps. X and LinkedIn were not read. Pronouns: \"they\" throughout.*\n\n## One-line take\nGV's London MP. In 2024 they called foundation models a commoditizing power-station business. By 2026 they had co-led a $650M+ round into a four-month-old lab, Recursive, and named the Google DeepMind alumni wave as Europe's edge.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** a 12-year London network (GV Europe founding partner, 2014) that gets them **inception-round, first-money positions with proven or repeat founders.** Examples:\n  - Q.ai: GV co-led the 2022 inception seed, and Apple bought the company in 2026 [src](https://gv.com/news/q-ai-joins-apple).\n  - Tessl: GV co-led the 2024 inception round with boldstart [src](https://boldstart.vc/news/tessl-ai-native-software-development-welcome-to-boldstart/). Its founder Guy Podjarny previously built Snyk, which is also on Hulme's list.\n- **Their frontier-lab view changed over two years:**\n  - In 2024 they described funding a model as \"investing a few hundred million into a power station\". They expected value to go to incumbents with data and distribution, and said they'd struggle to invest in OpenAI at $90B [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/tom-hulme-lessons-from-a-24x-angel-track-record-275x-on-robinhood-making-billion).\n  - In 2026 they said \"80 or 90% of what we're doing is AI\" and pointed to the London DeepMind spinout wave [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-03-19/gvs-tom-hulme-35-of-the-worlds-ai-researchers-are-in-europe-and-80-90-of-gvs-deals-are-ai/transcript). In May 2026 they co-led Recursive [src](https://www.gv.com/news/recursive-superintelligence-self-improving-ai).\n  - I found no source that explains the change. A plausible read is that they distinguish commodity scaling labs from RL and self-improvement bets. That is my inference.\n- **What constrains them:**\n  - GV has one LP, Alphabet, and Hulme shares the frontier-lab lane with Munichiello (Flapping, Modular). Hulme has **no AI-chip or compute deal** on any source.\n  - They co-lead rather than lead alone in large lab rounds (Recursive with Greycroft [src](https://tech.eu/2026/05/13/recursive-superintelligence-emerges-from-stealth-with-650m-raise/)).\n- **What they'd never do (inferred):** take diligence on trust from a big co-investor's name. They call that their angel-era mistake: \"Outsource discipline and due diligence to them\" [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/tom-hulme-lessons-from-a-24x-angel-track-record-275x-on-robinhood-making-billion).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The power-station call (2024).** They compared building a foundation model to \"investing a few hundred million into a power station\". They argued \"the value migrates to whoever's got data and distribution\" and called generative AI \"it's a sustaining Innovation\" [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/tom-hulme-lessons-from-a-24x-angel-track-record-275x-on-robinhood-making-billion) (20VC, 2024-05-08). Two years later they co-led a $4.65B lab round. The firm file does not have this tension.\n- **The pivot marker (2026-03):** \"80 or 90% of what we're doing is AI\" [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-03-19/gvs-tom-hulme-35-of-the-worlds-ai-researchers-are-in-europe-and-80-90-of-gvs-deals-are-ai/transcript).\n- **The sourcing map is the DeepMind diaspora.**\n  - They say \"35% of the world's AI researchers or masters programs are actually in Europe\".\n  - They name Recursive, David Silver's Ineffable and AMI Labs, adding \"these are all coming out of GDM.\"\n  - They credit Hassabis, who \"insisted on keeping a base here\", and say \"we have an equivalent of the sort of PayPal mafia happening.\" [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-03-19/gvs-tom-hulme-35-of-the-worlds-ai-researchers-are-in-europe-and-80-90-of-gvs-deals-are-ai/transcript)\n  - The transcript does not say GV invested in Ineffable or AMI Labs.\n- **Recursive's two nodes.** They describe \"reinforcement learning companies like Recursive Super Intelligence\". In the auto-transcript, Rocktäschel (\"Tim Rock Tasho\") is placed in London and Socher's team on the West Coast [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-03-19/gvs-tom-hulme-35-of-the-worlds-ai-researchers-are-in-europe-and-80-90-of-gvs-deals-are-ai/transcript). That suggests the London end (Rocktäschel, UCL and DeepMind) is Hulme's route in. This is my inference; no source states the intro.\n- **First money at Q.ai.** Hulme said \"we led the seed and did the series A with our friends at Kleiner\" [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-03-19/gvs-tom-hulme-35-of-the-worlds-ai-researchers-are-in-europe-and-80-90-of-gvs-deals-are-ai/transcript). GV's own post says it co-led with Aleph [src](https://gv.com/news/q-ai-joins-apple).\n- **Value moves to devices.** They argue that if models overshoot what users need, \"a lot of the value will actually acrue to the physical devices\". They give Nothing as the example [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-03-19/gvs-tom-hulme-35-of-the-worlds-ai-researchers-are-in-europe-and-80-90-of-gvs-deals-are-ai/transcript). Q.ai (silent voice interfaces) fits the same thesis.\n- **Repeat founders.** On Podjarny, \"who has now done Tessle.\" They say most of GV's European founders are repeat founders [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-03-19/gvs-tom-hulme-35-of-the-worlds-ai-researchers-are-in-europe-and-80-90-of-gvs-deals-are-ai/transcript).\n- **VC orthodoxy.** \"a lot of VC strategy is a lagging indicator\". To new angels: \"the amount you actually in invest is almost irrelevant\" [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/tom-hulme-lessons-from-a-24x-angel-track-record-275x-on-robinhood-making-billion).\n- **Angel track record (summary, low confidence).** The fetch tool's paraphrase of the 20VC transcript gives roughly 27 pre-2015 angel companies, about 4.5x DPI and about 24-25x TVPI. GoCardless was the best. Two momentum bets (later acquired by Fab and Jawbone) went to zero [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/tom-hulme-lessons-from-a-24x-angel-track-record-275x-on-robinhood-making-billion). The figures are not verbatim.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:**\n  - Recursive is a frontier lab that Hulme co-led at $650M+ in its first announced round. That is the mega-seed pattern you write $20M into.\n  - They have two sourced first-money credits (Q.ai, Tessl).\n  - SolveAI (Series A lead) is AI-native automation.\n  - They have a clear map of the London lab wave (Ineffable, AMI, Recursive).\n- **Against:**\n  - No AI-chip or compute deals.\n  - Single-LP corporate arm, so never an LP for you.\n  - On record in 2024 as skeptical of lab valuations.\n- **On \"NVIDIA + GV + Lux\":**\n  - Hulme's only NVIDIA overlap is Recursive, where NVIDIA and AMD participated [src](https://tech.eu/2026/05/13/recursive-superintelligence-emerges-from-stealth-with-650m-raise/).\n  - Lux does not appear anywhere in Hulme's book. The GV + Lux + NVIDIA round is Flapping Airplanes' A, which is Munichiello's deal (see `firms/gv.md`).\n- **Deals that matter to you:** Recursive (and the London RL/self-improvement founders behind it), SolveAI and Tessl.\n- **Why know them:** they are the GV door for London DeepMind spinouts.\n\n## Background\n- Bachelor's in physics (first class), University of Bristol. MBA, Harvard Business School (Baker Scholar). Honorary doctorate in Design, University of the Arts London [src](https://www.gv.com/team/tom-hulme).\n- Before GV:\n  - Taught at a rural school in Tanzania.\n  - Founded an engineering startup and Magnom (magnetic filters).\n  - Managing director of Marcos, the British sports-car maker.\n  - Design director at IDEO, where they ran IDEO's startup efforts and founded OpenIDEO [src](https://www.gv.com/team/tom-hulme).\n  - None of these roles is dated on the source.\n- Angel investor in London before 2015. TechCrunch called their hiring with Peter Read a \"serious coup\" (2014-07-10) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2014/07/10/google-ventures-in-europe-and-the-missing-fifth-man/).\n- Joined GV as a founding partner of the European team in 2014, with Eze Vidra, Peter Read and Avid Larizadeh [src](https://techcrunch.com/2014/07/10/google-ventures-in-europe-and-the-missing-fifth-man/). Avid Larizadeh Duggan left in early 2018, leaving \"one partner remaining\" (the visible text does not name them) [src](https://www.itpro.com/business-strategy/30273/yet-another-partner-leaves-the-european-arm-of-google-ventures).\n- Now Managing Partner and Head of Europe. **No dated promotion announcement found.** Aggregators still list \"General Partner\" (secondary).\n- Advises the UK government and the MoD; Reservist; MBE (2025) [src](https://www.gv.com/team/tom-hulme).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.gv.com/team/tom-hulme | GV bio and investment list, read 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | GV news bylines: https://www.gv.com/news/recursive-superintelligence-self-improving-ai, https://gv.com/news/q-ai-joins-apple | No personal blog found |\n| X | http://twitter.com/thulme | Linked from GV page; not read; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/thulme/ | Not read |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Has a TED speaker page [src](https://www.ted.com/speakers/tom_hulme) (not read) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-03 — TBPN — on GV's AI share and the European lab wave — [transcript](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-03-19/gvs-tom-hulme-35-of-the-worlds-ai-researchers-are-in-europe-and-80-90-of-gvs-deals-are-ai/transcript):\n  - \"80 or 90% of what we're doing is AI\"\n  - DeepMind spinouts as a \"PayPal mafia\"\n  - Q.ai: seed and Apple exit\n  - Devices capture value\n- 2024-05 — 20VC (Harry Stebbings) — \"Why investing in foundation models is like investing in power stations…\" — [transcript](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/tom-hulme-lessons-from-a-24x-angel-track-record-275x-on-robinhood-making-billion):\n  - Power-station analogy; value goes to data and distribution owners\n  - Generative AI as a sustaining innovation\n  - Angel record; outsourcing diligence as a mistake\n- 2019 — TechCrunch Disrupt Berlin (speaker) [src](https://techcrunch.com/2019/09/03/tom-hulme-from-gv-is-joining-us-at-disrupt-berlin) — not reviewed.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:**\n  - Europe's AI research talent density, with DeepMind as the seeding institution.\n  - Repeat founders.\n  - Interfaces disappearing: \"The history of computing is a history of friction.\" [src](https://gv.com/news/q-ai-joins-apple)\n  - Value moving to devices.\n  - Self-improving AI. GV's post on Recursive says \"AI is code, and now AI can code.\" [src](https://www.gv.com/news/recursive-superintelligence-self-improving-ai)\n- **Contrarian views:**\n  - Generative AI as a sustaining innovation that favors incumbents (2024).\n  - Most VC strategy is a lagging indicator.\n  - Check size matters little for new angels.\n- **Calls they got right:**\n  - Q.ai: inception seed in 2022, Apple acquisition in 2026, reported ~$1.5-2B [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/29/apple-buys-israeli-startup-q-ai-as-the-ai-race-heats-up).\n  - GoCardless as an angel.\n- **Open call:** \"foundation models = power stations\" (2024) versus co-leading Recursive (2026). They have not reconciled the two in anything found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. 20VC transcript (2024-05-08): the 2024 baseline on labs and incumbents.\n2. TBPN transcript (2026-03-19): the 2026 view on Europe and the DeepMind diaspora.\n3. GV, \"Recursive Superintelligence: Why Self-Improving AI is the Next Frontier\" (2026-05-13): their lab thesis [src](https://www.gv.com/news/recursive-superintelligence-self-improving-ai).\n4. GV, \"Making Technology Disappear: The promise of Q.ai as they join Apple\" (2026-01-29): the interface and devices thesis [src](https://gv.com/news/q-ai-joins-apple).\n\n## Social engagement\nnothing found (X @thulme not read this run).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Recursive | Early round, $650M+ at $4.65B | 2026-05-13 | Co-led (with Greycroft per tech.eu; GV post names no co-lead) | NVIDIA, AMD | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/recursive-superintelligence-self-improving-ai), [src](https://tech.eu/2026/05/13/recursive-superintelligence-emerges-from-stealth-with-650m-raise/) |\n| SolveAI | Series A, $45M (~£33M) | 2026-02-25 | Yes | Accel led the earlier $5M pre-seed, so GV was **not first** | [src](https://www.startupmag.co.uk/funding/solveai-2026-series-a-funding) |\n| Q.ai | Inception seed | 2022 | Co-led with Aleph (**first money**) | Aleph | [src](https://gv.com/news/q-ai-joins-apple) |\n| Q.ai | Series A | 2023 | No (Kleiner and Spark led per Globes) | Kleiner Perkins, Spark, Aleph | [src](https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001533701) |\n| Tessl | Inception, $25M | 2024-04 (disclosed 2024-11-14) | Co-led with boldstart (**first money**) | boldstart | [src](https://boldstart.vc/news/tessl-ai-native-software-development-welcome-to-boldstart/) |\n| Tessl | Series A, $100M | 2024-11-14 | No (Index led) | Index, Accel, boldstart | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/11/14/tessl-raises-125m-at-at-500m-valuation-to-build-ai-that-writes-and-maintains-code/) |\n| Genesis | not stated | n/a | n/a | Entity not identified | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/tom-hulme) |\n| Snyk, Stripe, Monzo, Nothing, Multiverse, Scandit, nPlan, Biomodal, Blockchain, Gravity Sketch, Lapse, Simply | profile list | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/tom-hulme) |\n| Exited: Autolus, Blue Vision Labs, Carmera, Currencycloud, GoCardless, Lemonade, Vaccitech, Q.ai | profile list | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/tom-hulme) |\n\n**AI chips and compute:** none found under Hulme's name. At GV, that lane belongs to Munichiello (Modular, SambaNova) and Nordlander (Lightmatter); see `firms/gv.md`.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Recursive (2026):**\n  - The company was \"set up just months ago\" when it emerged with $650M+ at $4.65B. It has offices in London and SF and fewer than 30 staff [src](https://tech.eu/2026/05/13/recursive-superintelligence-emerges-from-stealth-with-650m-raise/).\n  - GV was likely in the first institutional round, but no source says whether there was an earlier seed.\n  - Intro path not found. Rocktäschel's London base is the likely link (my inference).\n- **Q.ai (2022-2026):**\n  - GV co-led the inception seed with Aleph and followed on in the 2023 A. GV was on the board throughout, per Hulme on TBPN.\n  - Founder Aviad Maizels had previously sold PrimeSense to Apple.\n  - Apple acquired Q.ai on 2026-01-29 [src](https://gv.com/news/q-ai-joins-apple), [src](https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001533701).\n- **Tessl (2024):**\n  - GV and boldstart co-led a $25M inception round (boldstart's first check went in in April). The round stayed undisclosed until a $100M A led by Index [src](https://boldstart.vc/news/tessl-ai-native-software-development-welcome-to-boldstart/).\n  - This is a repeat bet on the Snyk founder.\n- **SolveAI (2026):** GV led a $45M A when the company was about eight months old [src](https://news.lavx.hu/article/solveai-raises-50m-in-eight-months-london-startup-s-enterprise-coding-ambitions) (secondary, not opened). That followed Accel's $5M pre-seed. Hulme was GV's quoted voice [src](https://www.startupmag.co.uk/funding/solveai-2026-series-a-funding).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Richard Socher, Tim Rocktäschel | Recursive co-founders; Hulme co-led and wrote GV's post | 2026 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/recursive-superintelligence-self-improving-ai) |\n| Greycroft | Co-lead on Recursive | 2026 | [src](https://tech.eu/2026/05/13/recursive-superintelligence-emerges-from-stealth-with-650m-raise/) |\n| Guy Podjarny | Backed Snyk and Tessl | 2024 (Tessl) | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/tom-hulme) |\n| boldstart ventures | Tessl inception co-lead | 2024 | [src](https://boldstart.vc/news/tessl-ai-native-software-development-welcome-to-boldstart/) |\n| Aviad Maizels | Q.ai seed co-lead, board | 2022 | [src](https://gv.com/news/q-ai-joins-apple) |\n| Aleph (Eden Shochat) | Q.ai seed co-lead | 2022 | [src](https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001533701) |\n| Kleiner Perkins, Spark | Q.ai Series A co-investors | 2023 | [src](https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001533701) |\n| Accel, Index | SolveAI pre-seed lead; Tessl A lead and co-investor | 2024–2026 | [src](https://www.startupmag.co.uk/funding/solveai-2026-series-a-funding) |\n| Dave Munichiello | Fellow GV MP; co-lead of global tech | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/tom-hulme) |\n| Peter Read, Eze Vidra, Avid Larizadeh | GV Europe founding partners | 2014 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2014/07/10/google-ventures-in-europe-and-the-missing-fifth-man/) |\n| Carl Pei (Nothing) | Portfolio founder | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/tom-hulme) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Did GV, through Hulme, invest in Ineffable or AMI Labs? Hulme named both as GDM spinouts.\n- What did Recursive raise before May 2026, if anything, and who introduced GV? Which Greycroft partner co-led?\n- What is the date of the promotion from GP to MP?\n- Which \"Genesis\" is on the list?\n- What do their X posts (@thulme) say about the change from the 2024 power-station view to backing Recursive?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.gv.com/team/tom-hulme (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/recursive-superintelligence-self-improving-ai (2026-05-13)\n- https://gv.com/news/q-ai-joins-apple (2026-01-29)\n- https://tech.eu/2026/05/13/recursive-superintelligence-emerges-from-stealth-with-650m-raise/\n- https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-03-19/gvs-tom-hulme-35-of-the-worlds-ai-researchers-are-in-europe-and-80-90-of-gvs-deals-are-ai/transcript (auto-generated)\n- https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/tom-hulme-lessons-from-a-24x-angel-track-record-275x-on-robinhood-making-billion (auto-generated, 2024-05-08)\n- https://boldstart.vc/news/tessl-ai-native-software-development-welcome-to-boldstart/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/11/14/tessl-raises-125m-at-at-500m-valuation-to-build-ai-that-writes-and-maintains-code/\n- https://en.globes.co.il/en/article-1001533701 ; https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/29/apple-buys-israeli-startup-q-ai-as-the-ai-race-heats-up\n- https://www.startupmag.co.uk/funding/solveai-2026-series-a-funding\n- https://techcrunch.com/2014/07/10/google-ventures-in-europe-and-the-missing-fifth-man/ ; https://www.itpro.com/business-strategy/30273/yet-another-partner-leaves-the-european-arm-of-google-ventures\n", "person:tom-lambert": "\n# Tom Lambert\n\n## One-line take\nListed on the Phoenix Court investment team, but no public source ties them to a single deal, so treat as a placeholder until a deal surfaces.\n\n## Crux\nThe only confirmed fact is a listing on the Phoenix Court team page under the \"Investment\" function. [src](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team) The \"Partner\" title comes from the earlier LocalGlobe people pass and is not visible in the page text read on 2026-11-10; unconfirmed. LocalGlobe money comes from numbered funds with outside LPs (e.g. \"LocalGlobe XII LP\"); Companies House shows Saul and Robin Klein as the controlling persons of Phoenix Court Group. [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/12156857/persons-with-significant-control) Who approves deals (IC membership) is not published.\n\n## Differential insight\n- No first-hand quotes, deals or board seats found. What I checked is listed under Open questions.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nLow for now: no named AI deal, no sourced check size, promotion date unknown. LocalGlobe's own frontier-AI rounds (Mistral, CuspAI, OLIX, MicroAGI, ZML; see firms/localglobe.md) carry no person attribution to Tom.\n\n## Background\nUnconfirmed. No education or prior-employer source found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team | Team listing, \"Investment\", read 2026-11-10 |\n| Firm contact | https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/contact | Firm-level only; 2 Brill Place, London NW1 1DX |\n| X | https://x.com/phxcrt | Firm account, not personal |\n\nPersonal X/LinkedIn not verified.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| None found | | | | | |\n\n## Open questions\n- What I checked on 2026-11-10: the Phoenix Court team page (listed, function \"Investment\", no bio); phoenixcourt.vc/team/tom-lambert (404); localglobe.vc/team (redirects to a 404); Companies House officer search for \"Thomas Lambert\" (20 hits on page 1, none tied to LocalGlobe/Phoenix Court addresses, so not linked). The web search budget for this run was used up, so press, podcasts and portfolio announcements were NOT searched. Rerun with search for \"Tom Lambert\" + LocalGlobe / Latitude.\n- Promotion date and prior role.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/12156857/persons-with-significant-control\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/search/officers?q=Thomas+Lambert\n", "person:tomi-poutanen": "\n# Tomi Poutanen\n\n## One-line take\nFollow him as the technical half of the Jacobs–Poutanen pair behind Radical, Layer 6 and the Vector Institute, not as an active dealmaker. Since April 2022 his day job has been CEO of Signal 1, a Radical-backed hospital AI company. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-hospitals-with-ai/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** He is a career ML engineer who built search ranking at Yahoo! and Bing, then Layer 6's deep-learning recommender. He co-wrote the Pan-Canadian AI Strategy with Jordan Jacobs and co-founded Vector with Hinton. That network is what Radical's founder access runs on. Toronto Life called Jacobs and Poutanen \"the hub\" of Toronto AI. [src](https://radical.vc/team/tomi-poutanen/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/ais-outsized-opportunity/)\n- **What constrains him:** He is an operator-CEO first. Signal 1 has been his full-time job since 2022, and he is personally named on only two AI investments (BenchSci and Untether AI). [src](https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-hospitals-with-ai/)\n- **What he'd never do (inference from his own words):** Back an \"incrementally better\" product. He says a company needs a \"massive step change improvement\" to change behaviour. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/engineer-to-entrepreneur-building-ai-products-that-scale-with-tomi-poutanen/)\n- **Founder-GP pattern:** Radical's earliest position in BenchSci began as personal angel checks from Jacobs and Poutanen in 2016. Fund I then used their pro-rata rights in 2017. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/key-questions-ai-founders-must-ask-themselves/)\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The firm doubled down on its founders' own angel bets.** Jacobs and Poutanen backed BenchSci in 2016 \"as very early angels\", and Radical Fund I later invested through their pro-rata rights. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/key-questions-ai-founders-must-ask-themselves/) (2022-01-31)\n- **Step change over incremental gains:** \"being incrementally better is not relevant when you are trying to build a business.\" He learned this from an undergraduate compression startup at Waterloo whose product was only about 10% better than rivals. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/engineer-to-entrepreneur-building-ai-products-that-scale-with-tomi-poutanen/) (2022-06-28)\n- **Breakthroughs are few and small:** \"there are 200 lines that have really mattered\". He says his four breakthroughs could each be written \"in 50 lines of code.\" This is how an engineer judges technical moats. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/engineer-to-entrepreneur-building-ai-products-that-scale-with-tomi-poutanen/)\n- **Microsoft origin story:** As a co-op on the Windows SDK team, his compression work cut Windows 95 from 13 floppies to 11. He calls it \"an innovation worth $100 million to Microsoft\". He says Azure still uses the algorithm. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/engineer-to-entrepreneur-building-ai-products-that-scale-with-tomi-poutanen/)\n- **Signal 1 pivot:** At its April 2022 launch Signal 1 was built around CHARTWatch, a deterioration-risk model from St. Michael's / Unity Health. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-hospitals-with-ai/) By 2026 it sells governance and orchestration for hospitals running 100+ AI tools. Mount Sinai adopted it in June 2026 and NewYork-Presbyterian in August 2026. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/5-more-ai-predictions-for-the-year-2030/) [src](https://signal1.ai/)\n- **CDL roots:** Daniel Mulet says Poutanen was a founding mentor at the Creative Destruction Lab and pushed for its first ML-only accelerator in 2015. That 25-company cohort was the template for building a \"critical mass\" network. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/tracking-investment-in-canadian-ai-startups/)\n- **Chip view (paraphrase only):** In 2021 Radical's editorial summarised him as saying AI-chip success rests on complementary software. No verbatim quote was found. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/pride-in-our-technology/)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** He co-founded a high-fit firm, but today he is a healthcare-AI CEO and not the person who sources frontier-lab or compute deals at Radical. That is Jacobs, Toews, Katz and Rosenberg. The deal that matters to you is Untether AI (AI chips; 2019 Series A board seat; later acquired). He is useful as a reference into Toronto research talent (Hinton, Vector, Layer 6 alumni) and as a view on how Radical's founders seed the firm's book with their own angel positions. As an LP or co-investor contact he is not a channel; for Radical itself, go through Jacobs.\n\n## Background\n- University of Waterloo undergraduate. He ran a data-compression software business that paid his tuition and did a co-op at Microsoft on the Windows SDK team. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/engineer-to-entrepreneur-building-ai-products-that-scale-with-tomi-poutanen/)\n- MSc Computer Engineering and MBA, University of Toronto. [src](https://radical.vc/team/tomi-poutanen/)\n- Early career in Silicon Valley bringing ML into web search, first at Yahoo! Search and then at Bing. He founded four AI companies with exits to Microsoft, Yahoo! and TD (the 2022 article says five companies). Dates were not found. [src](https://radical.vc/team/tomi-poutanen/)\n- 2011: co-founded Milq with Jordan Jacobs and Don MacKinnon, a playlisting app with a deep-learning recommender. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/ai-founders-master-class/)\n- 2016: the AI was spun out of Milq as Layer 6 AI. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/ai-founders-master-class/)\n- 2017: co-founded the Vector Institute (launched 2017-03-30) with Jacobs, Hinton, Ed Clark and University of Toronto professors. Co-wrote the Pan-Canadian AI Strategy. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/leading-with-talent/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/fdas-action-plan-for-regulating-ai-ml/)\n- 2017: co-founded Radical Ventures. [src](https://radical.vc/team/tomi-poutanen/)\n- 2018: TD Bank Group acquired Layer 6, and he became TD's Chief AI Officer and SVP. Co-authored Canada's first responsible-AI guidelines for financial services. [src](https://radical.vc/team/tomi-poutanen/)\n- 2022-04: left TD to become CEO of Signal 1, which raised a $10M seed from Radical, Inovia Capital, TD Bank and Geoffrey Hinton. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-hospitals-with-ai/) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/healthcare-the-top-funded-sector-in-ai/)\n- 2023: on Maclean's top-ten list for healthcare innovations. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/funding-ai-unpacking-high-valuations/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/tomi-poutanen/ | Radical team bio, checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-hospitals-with-ai/ | Occasional Radical Reads bylines (2022). No personal blog found |\n| X | not found | Checked radical.vc/team 2026-11-10; it links only the firm account @radicalvcfund. No personal handle verified |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/tomipoutanen/ | Linked from his radical.vc team page (2026-11-10); followers not checked |\n| GitHub | not found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | Interviewer for Radical's AI Founders Master Class (Richard Socher, 2022) [src](https://radical.vc/articles/richard-socher-on-building-ai-products/) |\n| Company | https://signal1.ai/ | Signal 1, where he is CEO |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2022-06 — Radical, \"Engineer to Entrepreneur\" — [link](https://radical.vc/articles/engineer-to-entrepreneur-building-ai-products-that-scale-with-tomi-poutanen/) — Step change beats incremental improvement; the small number of lines of code that really mattered.\n- 2022-10 — AI Founders Master Class — he interviewed Richard Socher (You.com) — [link](https://radical.vc/articles/richard-socher-on-building-ai-products/) — His questions centred on why researchers become founders.\n- No external podcasts were found. The search budget ran out before podcast directories could be checked.\n\n## How they think\n- **Step-change products:** Incumbents only lose to products that are far better, not slightly better. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/engineer-to-entrepreneur-building-ai-products-that-scale-with-tomi-poutanen/)\n- **Healthcare as a wedge:** In healthcare \"quality and costs are inversely related\", so better care saves hospitals money. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-hospitals-with-ai/)\n- **Ecosystem builder:** Through CDL, Vector, the national strategy and Radical, he builds a critical mass of talent in one city. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/tracking-investment-in-canadian-ai-startups/)\n- **Calls right:** He backed Toronto AI infrastructure early (Vector, Layer 6 sold to TD, BenchSci). **Calls unclear:** the outcome of the Untether AI acquisition was not checked.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Engineer to Entrepreneur](https://radical.vc/articles/engineer-to-entrepreneur-building-ai-products-that-scale-with-tomi-poutanen/) (2022): his operating philosophy in his own words.\n2. [Transforming Hospitals with AI](https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-hospitals-with-ai/) (2022): why he left TD for Signal 1.\n3. [Key Questions AI Founders Must Ask Themselves](https://radical.vc/articles/key-questions-ai-founders-must-ask-themselves/) (2022): the BenchSci angel-to-fund story.\n4. [AI Founders Master Class](https://radical.vc/articles/ai-founders-master-class/) (Jacobs, 2022): the Milq to Layer 6 origin.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No personal X account was verified, and LinkedIn activity was not checked.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| BenchSci | Undisclosed (personal angel, with Jordan Jacobs) | 2016 | no (angel) | Jordan Jacobs | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/key-questions-ai-founders-must-ask-themselves/) |\n| BenchSci | Undisclosed (Radical Fund I via founders' pro-rata) | 2017 | unknown | | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/key-questions-ai-founders-must-ask-themselves/) |\n| Untether AI | Series A ($20M); he joined the board | 2019-05 (announced 2019-11-05) | co-led (per the earlier firm sweep) | Intel Capital | [src](https://businesswire.com/news/home/20191105005309/en/Untether-AI-Announces-20-Million-Series-Appoints) |\n| Signal 1 (as founder/CEO, not an investment) | Seed ($10M) | 2022-04 | n/a | Radical, Inovia Capital, TD Bank, Geoffrey Hinton | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-hospitals-with-ai/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **BenchSci (first in, then doubled down):** Jacobs and Poutanen invested personally in 2016, before Radical's first fund existed. In 2017 Fund I used their pro-rata rights. By January 2022 BenchSci had raised a $50M Series C and was used by 16 of the top 20 pharma companies. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/key-questions-ai-founders-must-ask-themselves/)\n- **Untether AI:** Radical co-invested in the $20M Series A with Intel Capital in 2019 and he took a board seat. Radical then led a $7M extension in July 2020. Radical's portfolio page now marks Untether \"Acquired\"; the buyer was not checked. [src](https://radical.vc/portfolio/untether-ai/) [src](https://www.osler.com/en/expertise/deals-cases/untether-ai-radical-ventures)\n- **Signal 1:** The company launched in April 2022 with Unity Health Toronto and Layer 6 as strategic partners and Dr. Muhammad Mamdani as clinical co-founder. Its first product was CHARTWatch, in use at St. Michael's since October 2020. [src](https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-hospitals-with-ai/)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Co-founder, Partner | 2017 | [src](https://radical.vc/team/tomi-poutanen/) |\n| Jordan Jacobs | Co-founder of Milq, Layer 6, Vector and Radical | 2011 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/ai-founders-master-class/) |\n| Geoffrey Hinton | Vector co-founder; Signal 1 seed investor | 2017 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/leading-with-talent/) |\n| Ed Clark | Vector co-founder; Radical partner | 2017 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/leading-with-talent/) |\n| Don MacKinnon | Milq co-founder | 2011 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/ai-founders-master-class/) |\n| Signal 1 | Co-founder and CEO | 2022-04 | [src](https://radical.vc/portfolio/signal-1/) |\n| Mara Lederman | Signal 1 co-founder and COO | 2022 | [src](https://radical.vc/portfolio/signal-1/) |\n| Muhammad Mamdani | Signal 1 co-founder (Unity Health) | 2022 | [src](https://radical.vc/portfolio/signal-1/) |\n| TD Bank Group | Chief AI Officer & SVP after Layer 6 acquisition | 2018 | [src](https://radical.vc/team/tomi-poutanen/) |\n| Inovia Capital | Co-investor in Signal 1 seed | 2022 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-hospitals-with-ai/) |\n| Creative Destruction Lab | Founding mentor | 2015 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/tracking-investment-in-canadian-ai-startups/) |\n| Untether AI | Board member | 2019 | [src](https://businesswire.com/news/home/20191105005309/en/Untether-AI-Announces-20-Million-Series-Appoints) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Untether AI:** The BusinessWire release returned 403 on 2026-11-10, so the \"co-led\" label and the board seat are carried over from the earlier firm sweep without being re-checked. Who bought Untether, and at what price?\n- Is he still on Radical's investment committee? From 2022 he has been signed as a Signal 1 full-timer.\n- Signal 1's later rounds (after the seed) and whether Radical's growth fund followed on.\n- Personal X handle, if any. Names and dates of the four earlier exits (Microsoft, Yahoo!, TD).\n- Whether he is personally named on other early Radical deals (Fund I 2017–2018: Waabi, Cohere?). No source names him.\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/tomi-poutanen/ (fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://radical.vc/portfolio/signal-1/ ; https://radical.vc/portfolio/untether-ai/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/engineer-to-entrepreneur-building-ai-products-that-scale-with-tomi-poutanen/ (2022-06-28)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/transforming-hospitals-with-ai/ (2022-05-02)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/key-questions-ai-founders-must-ask-themselves/ (2022-01-31)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/ai-founders-master-class/ (2022-10-11)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/leading-with-talent/ (2022-03-27)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/tracking-investment-in-canadian-ai-startups/ (2020-12-21)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/ais-outsized-opportunity/ (2021-11-22)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/pride-in-our-technology/ (2021-06-28)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/5-more-ai-predictions-for-the-year-2030/ (2026-06-30)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/healthcare-the-top-funded-sector-in-ai/ (2022-05-24)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/funding-ai-unpacking-high-valuations/ (2023-03-19)\n- https://radical.vc/articles/richard-socher-on-building-ai-products/ (2022-10-31)\n- https://signal1.ai/\n- https://businesswire.com/news/home/20191105005309/en/Untether-AI-Announces-20-Million-Series-Appoints (403 on re-check)\n- https://www.osler.com/en/expertise/deals-cases/untether-ai-radical-ventures\n- Web search budget was exhausted this turn; no Forbes, podcast-directory or press searches were run.\n", "person:vamsi-duvvuri": "\n# Vamsi Duvvuri\n\n## One-line take\nVy Capital's only long-tenured investor besides Tamas turns out to run his own emerging-markets fund family, Dharana Capital. Its first fund was a Vy-managed vehicle (2021) and its second ($197.9M raised, 2025) uses Vy's Cayman directors and filer account. In October 2026 he led his first visible AI deal, Clueso's $10M Series A ([src](https://www.clueso.io/blog/series-a), [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1895879/000094562121000440/primary_doc.xml), [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2076241/000206073925000003/primary_doc.xml)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he has held Vy's India book for a decade. He was board observer at Zomato, Urban Company and Upgrade by 2020 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm)). He kept those founder relationships into his own vehicle: Dharana lists Zomato (exited), Urban Company, and two of Deepinder Goyal's new companies, LAT Aerospace and Temple ([src](https://dharana.com/), [src](https://www.lat.com/)).\n- **Constraint:** Dharana is small: about four people, two of them investors (TheOrg, aggregator) ([src](https://theorg.com/org/dharana-capital)). Fund II is $197.9M from 32 investors ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2076241/000206073925000003/primary_doc.xml)). That fits India growth secondaries and $10M Series A leads, not $20M+ frontier-lab seeds.\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** compete with Tamas on Musk-orbit and US frontier deals. No source puts him on Cerebras, xAI, SpaceX or Neuralink.\n- **Whose money:** the Dharana funds have outside LPs: Fund II has 32 investors with a $2.5M minimum ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2076241/000206073925000003/primary_doc.xml)). The 2021 Vy Dharana EM Technology Fund was managed by Vy Capital Management Company, with a Vy-controlled GP ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1895879/000094562121000440/primary_doc.xml)). Who approves Dharana deals (Duvvuri alone, or a Vy committee) is **unconfirmed**.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Dharana is a Vy-born fund family, not a clean spinout.** The 2021 Form D for \"Vy Dharana EM Technology Fund\" is filed \"C/O VY CAPITAL MGMT CO. LTD\" and names Vy Capital Management as manager. The 2025 Dharana Fund II Form D drops the Vy name but uses the same Cayman GP directors as Vy Fund III, Vy Nous and VY Cerebrum (Bashforth and Bain). It was filed under the EDGAR filer account of Vy Space III, L.P. (CIK 2060739) ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1895879/000094562121000440/primary_doc.xml), [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2076241/000206073925000003/primary_doc.xml), [src](https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?action=getcompany&CIK=2060739&type=&dateb=&owner=include&count=40)). Whether Duvvuri still holds a Vy title is **unconfirmed**.\n- **Fund II size and timing:** $250M offering, $197,875,000 sold to 32 investors, first sale 2025-07-16, $2.5M minimum. The issuer contact is a +91 (India) phone number ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2076241/000206073925000003/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **The Clueso \"lead\" was mostly insider money.** Clueso says \"more than 80% of the capital came from investors who had already backed\" it at seed ([src](https://www.clueso.io/blog/series-a)). So either Dharana's lead check was under about $2M of the $10M, or Dharana was an undisclosed earlier backer. The seed was announced as f7 Ventures plus YC, with no Dharana ([src](https://www.clueso.io/blog/announcing-our-seed-funding)). Which one is true is **unconfirmed**.\n- **He re-upped on Urban Company with his own vehicle.** Vy was already an \"existing investor\" by the 2018 Series D ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2018/11/30/urbanclap-50-million/)) and joined the E (2019) and F (2021) ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2019/08/01/urbanclap-indias-largest-home-services-startup-raises-75m/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/06/01/home-services-platform-urban-company-raises-255-million-at-2-1-billion-valuation/)). Then Dharana Capital bought $50M in a July 2024 secondary ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_Company)). That was a year before Urban Company's IPO (the IPO date is from memory, unconfirmed here).\n- **The Goyal thread:** he was Vy's Zomato observer, and Dharana's site now lists Goyal's LAT Aerospace (\"Founded by Deepinder Goyal and Surobhi Das\") and Temple ([src](https://dharana.com/), [src](https://www.lat.com/)). Round, date and size for both are **not found**.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** He is an India and emerging-markets growth and Series A investor with a roughly $200M fund. The only AI deal he is personally named on is an application-layer Series A (Clueso, AI video, 2026-10). He is useful as (a) the person who knows Vy's internal machinery outside the Musk book, (b) an India co-investor for an AI company with Indian founders or an Indian market, and (c) a route into the Goyal ecosystem (LAT Aerospace, Temple). He is not a $20M frontier-seed partner.\n\n## Background\n- BTech in Computer Science and Engineering, IIT Kanpur. MBA, IIM Ahmedabad (years not given) ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm)).\n- Equity research analyst at Religare Capital before 2013 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm)).\n- Joined Vy Capital in 2013, the year Vy was founded. By the 2020 VYGG prospectus he was Managing Director and head of EM investments ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm)). How he got the seat (an intro to Tamas or Szeszkowski) is not found.\n- 2021: Vy files the Form D for \"Vy Dharana EM Technology Fund\" ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1895879/000094562121000440/primary_doc.xml)). By 2024-07, \"Dharana Capital\" is investing under its own name ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_Company)). By 2026 he is styled Founder and Managing Partner ([src](https://www.clueso.io/blog/series-a)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://dharana.com/ | Dharana Capital: contact@dharana.com, Dubai and Bengaluru; no team page (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | not checked by search (web search unavailable this run) |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | TheOrg profile (aggregator): https://theorg.com/org/dharana-capital/org-chart/vamsi-duvvuri |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. Web search was unavailable for this run, so this is not a confirmed absence.\n\n## How they think\n- Dharana's stated thesis is \"We back missionary founders building durable businesses\" ([src](https://dharana.com/)).\n- On Clueso he praised \"customer obsession and speed of execution\" ([src](https://www.clueso.io/blog/series-a)).\n- Pattern: he holds founders across companies and rounds (Urban Company from at least 2018 to 2024; Goyal from Zomato to LAT and Temple). He buys secondaries in winners he already knows.\n\n## Read these first\n- The Clueso Series A post, which carries his only public quote found: https://www.clueso.io/blog/series-a\n- The 2020 VYGG prospectus bio: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm\n- The Dharana Fund II Form D: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2076241/000206073925000003/primary_doc.xml\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Clueso (AI video) | Series A ($10M), Dharana | 2026-10-13 | yes (Dharana); Duvvuri quoted | Y Combinator, f7 Ventures, Founders Future, Neotribe Ventures | [src](https://www.clueso.io/blog/series-a) |\n| Urban Company | Secondary ($50M), Dharana | 2024-07 | sole buyer named | none | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_Company) |\n| Urban Company | Vy: existing by Series D; participated in D, E and F | 2018-11 / 2019-08 / 2021-06 | no | Steadview, Tiger Global, Prosus, Dragoneer, Wellington | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2018/11/30/urbanclap-50-million/) |\n| Zomato, Upgrade, Urban Company | Vy board observer (round not given) | by 2020-10 | not found | | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm) |\n| LAT Aerospace, Temple, Vayudh, Lentra, MyGate, NoBroker, Petpooja, Vyapar, ITILITE, Zopper, Beyond Appliances | Dharana portfolio logos (round, date and size not found) | not found | not found | | [src](https://dharana.com/) |\n\nHis personal role in the Vy Urban Company rounds is not stated. He was observer there by 2020.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Clueso (2026):** Dharana led the $10M Series A. Over 80% of the capital came from seed-stage backers (see Differential insight). The intro path is not found. Clueso's founders say they raise through portfolio-company and angel intros, never cold emails ([src](https://www.clueso.io/blog/how-we-raised-our-1-4m-seed-round)).\n- **Urban Company (2018-2024):** Vy was an existing investor by Nov 2018 and Duvvuri was its observer. Dharana then bought a $50M block in a secondary in July 2024.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Vy Capital | Managing Director, head of EM | 2013 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm) |\n| Dharana Capital | Founder and Managing Partner | by 2024 (inferred) | [src](https://www.clueso.io/blog/series-a) |\n| Religare Capital | equity research analyst | before 2013 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm) |\n| Deepinder Goyal | backed him at Zomato (Vy observer), then LAT Aerospace and Temple (Dharana logos) | by 2020 | [src](https://dharana.com/) |\n| Alexander Tamas | colleague; Vy co-founder | 2013 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Is he still a Vy MD, or did Dharana separate fully? Does Vy (Tamas) sit on the Dharana investment committee, or invest as an LP?\n- Dharana's check in Clueso, and whether it held an earlier Clueso position.\n- Round, date and size for LAT Aerospace, Temple, Vayudh (drones), Lentra and the rest of the logos.\n- Who \"PS\" on the Dharana investment team is (TheOrg shows only initials).\n- Did he sell Urban Company shares in the 2025 IPO, through a VYC SPV or Dharana?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1822877/000110465920111326/tm2029689-13_424b4.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1895879/000094562121000440/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2076241/000206073925000003/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?action=getcompany&CIK=2060739&type=&dateb=&owner=include&count=40\n- https://www.clueso.io/blog/series-a\n- https://www.clueso.io/blog/announcing-our-seed-funding\n- https://www.clueso.io/blog/how-we-raised-our-1-4m-seed-round\n- https://dharana.com/\n- https://www.lat.com/\n- https://theorg.com/org/dharana-capital (aggregator)\n- https://theorg.com/org/dharana-capital/org-chart/vamsi-duvvuri (aggregator)\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_Company\n- https://techcrunch.com/2018/11/30/urbanclap-50-million/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2019/08/01/urbanclap-indias-largest-home-services-startup-raises-75m/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2021/06/01/home-services-platform-urban-company-raises-255-million-at-2-1-billion-valuation/\n", "person:vega-shah": "\n# Vega Shah\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. Sources use \"she\" (NVIDIA author bio). This replaces the 'NVIDIA PMM lead' description in `people/tess-van-stekelenburg.md` and the lead row from that profile. The PMM title was correct until 2025. The NVentures platform role is newer.*\n\n## One-line take\nShah is NVentures's portfolio-to-NVIDIA connector, not a check-writer. If you co-invest with NVentures, she is the person who turns the NVIDIA logo on the cap table into engineering time. But she has held the role only since about November 2025, and no public source shows her doing it for a named company yet.\n\n## Crux\n- **What she does.** NVIDIA's own bio says she \"manages the firm's investment portfolio\". It also says she focuses on \"driving technical and ecosystem integration between NVIDIA and portfolio companies\" ([NVIDIA blog author page, JP mirror of the English bio](https://blogs.nvidia.co.jp/blog/author/vegashah/); [EN](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/vegashah)). Her portfolio scope covers frontier models, techbio, robotics, AI infrastructure, materials discovery, energy, quantum and healthcare (same page).\n- **Why it matters.** Siddeek sells NVentures as \"something no other financial VC or corporate VC could\" offer ([GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md)). Shah's role is where that promise gets delivered after the check. It is the only post-investment role at NVentures that NVIDIA names publicly ([sources/nvidia-check-writers.md](../sources/nvidia-check-writers.md)).\n- **What constrains her.** She does not personally sign, source or price anything. No round names her, and no source puts her on an investment committee. Her leverage is internal: knowing which NVIDIA product team (BioNeMo, NIM, Isaac, DGX Cloud) a portfolio company should talk to. That is an inference from her product-marketing background.\n- **What she'd never do.** Lead a round. Her career runs scientist, then product, then product marketing, then platform. There is no investing role on record.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The NVentures role is recent, and her 2025 bylines predate it.** In December 2024 she was introduced as \"Product Marketing Manager Lead for Healthcare & Life Sciences, NVIDIA\" ([Bright Spots in Healthcare, 2024-12-24](https://healthpodcastlibrary.com/legacy/episodes/bright-spots-in-healthcare/how-ai-is-reshaping-healthcare-an-interview-with-nvidias-vega-shah/)). In March 2025 Lux's EVOLVED post credited her as \"NVIDIA PMM Lead\" ([Lux, 2025-03-07](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design)). A people-search aggregator dates \"NVentures Portfolio Manager\" to Nov 2025 ([village.ai, aggregator, returned 403 on direct fetch; seen in a search snippet](https://village.ai/person/vega-shah-MTc1MTMwMzMx)). Her NVIDIA author page is updated over time, so the platform-lead bio now shows on her April and June 2025 posts too. **Correction to the task brief and to `sources/nvidia-check-writers.md`:** those 2025 posts are not NVentures portfolio posts. One features BrainStorm Therapeutics, an Inception member with no NVentures mention ([2025-04-22](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/brain-disease-ai-lab-in-the-loop/)). The other covers an IQVIA corporate partnership ([2025-06-11](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/iqvia-ai-agents-clinical-research/)). \"By 2025-04 (first byline)\" therefore dates her blogging, not the platform role.\n- **A healthcare GTM operator moved into the venture arm.** She \"leads go-to-market strategies for Healthcare & Life Sciences Solutions at NVIDIA\" (podcast guest bio, 2024-12). Before NVIDIA she worked on cloud virtual-screening tools at Dotmatics and drug-development product strategy at Benchling ([NVIDIA developer blog, CN mirror](https://developer.nvidia.cn/blog/author/vega-shah)). NVentures appears to staff platform roles from NVIDIA's vertical go-to-market teams rather than hiring outside VC platform people. This is one data point, so treat it as inference.\n- **She feeds the AI-bio pipeline from the community side.** The podcast bio says \"She is founder of Techbio Transformers\", a community of biology-and-AI operators. An aggregator lists Renee Ng as co-founder from 2025-01 (village.ai, search snippet only). She co-authored Lux's EVOLVED 2024 write-up. BrainStorm Therapeutics got a special mention at EVOLVED. Six weeks later her NVIDIA blog post featured BrainStorm, which \"is a member of NVIDIA Inception\" ([Lux](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design), [NVIDIA](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/brain-disease-ai-lab-in-the-loop/)). That traces a path from hackathon to Inception to NVIDIA blog. It is a pattern across two sources, not a stated process.\n- **The scope now reaches past healthcare.** Her current bio lists \"frontier models\" first and adds quantum and energy ([author page](https://blogs.nvidia.co.jp/blog/author/vegashah/)). The task brief named three areas (frontier models, healthcare, robotics). The bio lists eight.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium, as a post-close lever, not as a co-investor.** She writes no checks, so she is not a syndicate partner for a $20M seed position. Her value to the owner is narrower: when NVentures is in a lab seed the owner joins (Ineffable, Periodic, Inherent, RadixArk; see [firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md)), she is the named person who connects the company to NVIDIA engineering and ecosystem teams. That is the practical half of an NVentures check. Her depth is techbio, so she is most useful for AI-for-science labs (Periodic, Inherent, Ineffable's RL-for-science angle) and least proven on pure frontier-model labs. She has no LP or ownership relevance.\n\n## Background\n- BS in biology, UC Berkeley; PhD in genomics, University of Washington ([NVIDIA author page](https://blogs.nvidia.co.jp/blog/author/vegashah/)). Years not found.\n- Benchling: drug-development product strategy; dates not found ([NVIDIA developer blog, CN](https://developer.nvidia.cn/blog/author/vega-shah)).\n- Dotmatics: product manager, cloud-based virtual-screening tools; dates not found (same source).\n- NVIDIA: led product marketing for Healthcare & Life Sciences. First dated byline 2024-09-26 (Montai NIM spotlight, developer blog). Titled PMM lead on 2024-10-22 (HLTH recording) and 2025-03-07 (Lux). Sources above.\n- NVentures platform lead / portfolio manager: since Nov 2025 per aggregator. NVIDIA has not announced a start date.\n- Co-founded Techbio Transformers, a community (podcast bio, 2024-12).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/vegashah | NVIDIA blog author page, current bio. Two corporate-blog posts (2025-04, 2025-06) |\n| Substack / blog | https://developer.nvidia.cn/blog/author/vega-shah | NVIDIA developer-blog posts 2024-09 to 2025-12 (CN mirror; English path returned 404) |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked (not used as a source) | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| Bluesky | dr-alphalyrae.bsky.social | Attributed to her only by a search snippet. Account deactivated as of 2026-11-10 (public API check) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-12 (recorded 2024-10-22 at HLTH) — Bright Spots in Healthcare — \"How AI is reshaping healthcare: an interview with NVIDIA's Vega Shah\" — [link](https://healthpodcastlibrary.com/legacy/episodes/bright-spots-in-healthcare/how-ai-is-reshaping-healthcare-an-interview-with-nvidias-vega-shah/) — no transcript. The description covers generative AI in patient care and adoption challenges. It predates the NVentures role.\n- BIO convention speaker listing as PMM Lead, Healthcare & Life Sciences ([convention.bio.org](https://convention.bio.org/speakers/vega-shah); returned 404 on fetch, seen in a search snippet only; year not confirmed).\n- No NVentures-era talk, panel or podcast found.\n\n## How they think\n- Her interests per her bio: areas \"where AI translates into real-world systems\", including biology and robotics ([author page](https://blogs.nvidia.co.jp/blog/author/vegashah/)).\n- Her developer-blog topics trace NVIDIA's healthcare stack from NIM microservices (2024) through research agents (AI-Q blueprint, 2025-06) to RL training of scientific agents (2025-12) ([developer blog CN](https://developer.nvidia.cn/blog/author/vega-shah)). The last fits the AI-scientist labs NVentures backs (Periodic, Inherent).\n- Contrarian views and calls: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n- NVIDIA author bio: the clearest statement of the platform role ([link](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/vegashah)).\n- \"How to train scientific agents with reinforcement learning\", 2025-12-15, NVIDIA developer blog (CN mirror listing: [link](https://developer.nvidia.cn/blog/author/vega-shah)). Her most recent technical piece, and closest to the frontier-lab portfolio.\n- Lux EVOLVED 2024 write-up, co-authored with Tess van Stekelenburg ([link](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design)).\n- BrainStorm Therapeutics \"lab in the loop\" post ([link](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/brain-disease-ai-lab-in-the-loop/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing usable. No X account was found, and the Bluesky account attributed to her is deactivated.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| none as investor | | | | | She is not named on any round. She supports the NVentures portfolio as a whole; see [firms/nvidia.md](../firms/nvidia.md) and [people/mohamed-sid-siddeek.md](mohamed-sid-siddeek.md) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNone. No source shows her originating, sourcing or signing a deal. The closest thing is the BrainStorm Therapeutics path: EVOLVED hackathon mention (Lux post, 2025-03), then Inception member, then her NVIDIA blog feature (2025-04). That is ecosystem promotion, not an investment.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NVIDIA (NVentures) | Platform lead / portfolio manager | 2025-11 (aggregator) | [author page](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/vegashah) |\n| NVIDIA (Healthcare & Life Sciences) | PMM lead, go-to-market | by 2024-09 (first byline) to ~2025 | [podcast](https://healthpodcastlibrary.com/legacy/episodes/bright-spots-in-healthcare/how-ai-is-reshaping-healthcare-an-interview-with-nvidias-vega-shah/) |\n| Mohamed \"Sid\" Siddeek | Head of NVentures; her unit head (reporting line not stated) | 2025-11 | [sources/nvidia-check-writers.md](../sources/nvidia-check-writers.md) |\n| Tess van Stekelenburg (Lux) | Co-author of the EVOLVED 2024 post | 2025-03 | [Lux](https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design) |\n| Techbio Transformers | Founder (podcast bio) | by 2024-12 | [podcast](https://healthpodcastlibrary.com/legacy/episodes/bright-spots-in-healthcare/how-ai-is-reshaping-healthcare-an-interview-with-nvidias-vega-shah/) |\n| Dotmatics; Benchling | Former product roles | before NVIDIA | [developer blog CN](https://developer.nvidia.cn/blog/author/vega-shah) |\n| BrainStorm Therapeutics | Featured in her NVIDIA blog post (Inception member) | 2025-04 | [NVIDIA](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/brain-disease-ai-lab-in-the-loop/) |\n\n## How a founder reaches her\n- **Best path:** after an NVentures check, through the NVentures deal lead or Siddeek. The platform role is described as portfolio-facing, so she is not an inbound sourcing channel.\n- **Before a check, in techbio:** the Techbio Transformers community and AI-bio hackathons such as Lux's EVOLVED, where she has a documented presence, or NVIDIA Inception.\n- **Lux's Tess van Stekelenburg** is a documented co-author tie, which makes a warm intro plausible for AI-bio founders.\n- No public email or X handle found.\n\n## Open questions\n- Is the start date of the NVentures role (Nov 2025, aggregator) right, and does she report to Siddeek?\n- Has she published any NVentures-era portfolio post (2026)? None turned up on her author pages.\n- Who else does platform work at NVentures? Does it scale with nine-figure lab checks?\n- Techbio Transformers: what is her founding role relative to Renee Ng, and is she still active?\n\n## Sources\n- NVIDIA blog author page (EN): https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/author/vegashah\n- NVIDIA blog author page (JP mirror, verbatim bio): https://blogs.nvidia.co.jp/blog/author/vegashah/\n- NVIDIA developer blog author page (CN mirror): https://developer.nvidia.cn/blog/author/vega-shah\n- BrainStorm post (2025-04-22): https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/brain-disease-ai-lab-in-the-loop/\n- IQVIA post (2025-06-11): https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/iqvia-ai-agents-clinical-research/\n- Bright Spots in Healthcare podcast (2024-12-24): https://healthpodcastlibrary.com/legacy/episodes/bright-spots-in-healthcare/how-ai-is-reshaping-healthcare-an-interview-with-nvidias-vega-shah/\n- Lux EVOLVED 2024 (2025-03-07): https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design\n- village.ai (aggregator, search snippet only): https://village.ai/person/vega-shah-MTc1MTMwMzMx\n- GCV Siddeek Powerlist 2024: https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-mohamed-siddeek/?format=md\n", "person:victor-lazarte": "\n# Victor Lazarte\n\n## One-line take\nWildlife Studios co-founder turned Benchmark GP who led three fast-marking-up AI Series As in 2024 (HeyGen, Mercor, Decart), then left in July 2025 to raise a reported $200M debut fund. Worth following as a co-investor and as a signal of which AI companies are getting real usage.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He is an operator who built a scaled consumer company (Wildlife Studios, founded 2011; Benchmark led its Dec 2019 round at ~US$1.3B) ([src](https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wildlife_Studios)). He backs AI companies only once usage shows up, then pays up fast and leads. He was not first in on any of his three named deals:\n  - **Decart:** Sequoia led the $21M seed (just over $100M valuation, Oct 31 2024). Lazarte led the $32M A above $500M on Dec 19 2024, less than two months later ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/decart-adds-another-32m-at-a-500m-valuation/)).\n  - **Mercor:** General Catalyst led the 2023 seed, and Benchmark came in at the $32M A at $250M ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/20/mercor-an-ai-recruiting-startup-founded-by-21-year-olds-raises-100m-at-2b-valuation/)).\n  - **HeyGen:** He led the A at $500M post-money after the company was profitable and at more than $35M ARR ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/)).\n- **What constrains him:** The fund is small (reported $200M) and new, so leading $30M–$60M As alone means very few deals or co-leads. The firm name and strategy are not public in anything we could read. He has done nothing found in semis, compute or pre-training.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred, not stated):** Spray small checks across many bets. His own framing is concentration (\"very few things that matter\") ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/)). The 20VC episode title calls portfolio construction \"BS\" (episode page, [src](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/victor-lazarte)).\n\n## Differential insight\nNote: the web-search budget ran out during this run, and no podcast or X transcript was available. These quotes come from press interviews, so most are only medium non-obvious.\n- **Selective at Benchmark:** He looked at hundreds of companies over about 11 months before making his first deal, HeyGen. On the job: \"It's about doing very few things that matter.\" ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/), 2024-06-24)\n- **Skeptical of AI value capture in 2024:** \"there are very few companies that are actually creating value\" ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/))\n- **Unsure where AI value accrues:** \"But in that transformation, where's the value going to accrue?\" ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/))\n- **Timing pattern, a fast follower at a step-up:** In Decart he led an A about 5x the seed price within seven weeks of Sequoia's seed, describing the company as \"makes AI generation not only more efficient, but also more accessible\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/decart-adds-another-32m-at-a-500m-valuation/), 2024-12-19). Decart's CEO calls it \"a fully vertically integrated AI research lab\" (same source).\n- **Exit framing:** He left Benchmark to \"build a new investment practice\". This is his X wording as relayed by TechCrunch; we did not read the post itself ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/24/vc-victor-lazarte-is-leaving-benchmark-to-launch-his-own-firm/), 2025-07-24).\n- **Origin of the Benchmark relationship:** Benchmark led Wildlife Studios' Dec 2019 unicorn round ([src](https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wildlife_Studios)). That makes him a portfolio founder whom the firm recruited as a GP, an inference from the sequence. No quote was found.\n- **Angel in Applied Compute:** He is named as an individual backer of Applied Compute's first $80M (Oct 2025), after he left Benchmark ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:**\n  - He leads AI rounds early-ish and with conviction.\n  - Decart (self-described vertically integrated AI research lab) and Mercor (expert data for labs) are close to the frontier-lab ecosystem.\n  - Applied Compute (angel) is a high-fit name.\n  - A ~$200M fund that concentrates makes a plausible co-investor in large AI seeds.\n- **Against:**\n  - His record is Series A at a step-up, not first-in on research teams.\n  - Nothing found in pre-training, semis or compute.\n  - HeyGen is app layer.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** Decart, Mercor, Applied Compute and (unconfirmed) Flapping Airplanes. Flapping Airplanes was listed only by the aggregator iq.wiki ([src](https://iq.wiki/wiki/flapping-airplanes)).\n\n## Background\n- **2011:** Co-founded Wildlife Studios (Brazil mobile games) with Arthur Lazarte and Michael Mac-Vicar ([src](https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wildlife_Studios)). Fortune says he co-founded it with his brother and was CEO until about a year before June 2024 ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/)).\n- **2019-12:** Benchmark led Wildlife's round at ~US$1.3B ([src](https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wildlife_Studios)). TechCrunch cites an estimated $3B valuation in 2020 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/24/vc-victor-lazarte-is-leaving-benchmark-to-launch-his-own-firm/)).\n- **~2023 to 2025-07:** General partner at Benchmark, for about two years ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/24/vc-victor-lazarte-is-leaving-benchmark-to-launch-his-own-firm/)).\n- **2025-11:** Raised a $200M debut fund. This comes from the WSJ headline as cited on Wikipedia; we did not read the article ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benchmark_(venture_capital_firm)), [WSJ](https://www.wsj.com/articles/ex-benchmark-partner-victor-lazarte-collects-200-million-for-debut-fund-1fdd2379)).\n- **Education:** nothing found.\n\n## Update 2026-11-10: the new firm is almost certainly \"Diffusion\" (SEC Form Ds)\nWeb search was exhausted in this pass, and WSJ and Reuters could not be fetched. Everything below comes from SEC EDGAR full-text search for \"Victor Lazarte\", portfolio-company posts and Wikipedia.\n- **VL Fund I, L.P. (first vehicle):** This is a Delaware VC fund. Lazarte is the only named individual, as \"Managing Director of the General Partner\" of VL Fund GP I, LLC. First sale was 2025-09-08, with **$45M sold to 3 investors** and the offering fully sold. He signed it on 2025-09-22 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086666/000123191925000224/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - This does **not** match the ~$200M WSJ reported on 2025-11-21. We could not confirm whether VL Fund I was a first close, a sidecar, or something else, or how it relates to the WSJ figure. EDGAR shows only this one filing for the entity.\n- **Diffusion ONE, LP (main fund):**\n  - **Structure:** Delaware VC fund, General Partner Diffusion ONE GP, LLC, address 2261 Market St, San Francisco.\n  - **GP team:** two Managing Directors of the GP, **Kristian Fredrickson** (who signs the filings) and **Victor Lazarte**.\n  - **Initial filing (2026-06-22):** a $1B target ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2141438/000214143826000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - **Amendment (2026-10-09):** first sale 2026-06-23, **$1.252B sold to 113 investors**, filed under 3(c)(7) (qualified purchasers) ([Form D/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2141438/000123191926001442/primary_doc.xml)). No placement agent was listed.\n- **Diffusion V2, LP and Diffusion V3, LP:** Both have the same GP (Diffusion Umbrella GP LLC) and the same two MDs.\n  - **V2:** $40M from 16 investors, first sale 2026-08-14 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2148945/000214894526000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - **V3:** $70M from 9 investors, first sale 2026-10-13 ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2157328/000215732826000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - The small investor counts and fully-sold amounts look like single-deal SPVs or co-invest vehicles. That is an inference: the filings do not name the targets.\n- **Is \"Diffusion\" in AI round posts the same firm?** It is the leading hypothesis, **not confirmed**. Since mid-2026, AI rounds have named an investor called \"Diffusion\":\n  - co-lead of **Harvey's $550M at $15.5B** with Lightspeed, as a new investor ([Harvey](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-dollar550m-at-a-dollar155b-valuation-to-help-legal-teams-own-their-intelligence), 2026-09-09). Wikipedia, citing Reuters, calls it \"Diffusion Capital Partners\" ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvey_(software)))\n  - Etched's $300M Series C ([src](https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference), 2026-07-23) and $700M Growth ([src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one), 2026-08-18)\n  - Cognition's Series E ([src](https://cognition.com/blog/series-e), 2026-09-08)\n  - Applied Compute's $350M at $3.25B ([src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise), 2026-11-02)\n\n  The timing fits: the first Etched deal came one month after Diffusion ONE's first sale, and V2's first sale was four days before Etched's $700M round. The network fits: Lazarte led Applied Compute's seed, and Fredrickson was a Harvey angel whose Verified Capital is an existing Harvey investor. The missing link is a primary source that puts Lazarte or Fredrickson on a Diffusion-branded investment. Diffusion Capital Ltd (diffusion.capital), a crypto investment office, is a different firm.\n- **What changed in the read on him:** He is no longer a ~$200M Series A investor. With Fredrickson he co-runs a **>$1.25B growth-style vehicle** that co-leads $500M+ rounds at $10B+ valuations (Harvey) and buys into AI chips (Etched) and agents (Cognition). The check-capacity view moves from Series A to growth.\n- **Benchmark tenure, from a primary source:** He is listed as a related person, with Fenton, Hu, Puttagunta, Tavel and Vishria, on Benchmark Partners 1, L.P. ($425M sold to 39 investors, first sale 2024-07-02) ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2028211/000202821124000001/primary_doc.xml)). He is also on Benchmark Partners Founders' Fund 1 ($165M offering) ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2028199/000202819924000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **Co-founder Kristian (\"Kris\") Fredrickson:**\n  - Managing Member of the GP of **Verified Capital Fund I, LP** ($175M offering, 2025-04-29) ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2041499/000204149925000002/primary_doc.xml)) and of **Verified V1 LLC** ($25M, 6 investors, 2026-06) ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2138406/000213840626000001/primary_doc.xml))\n  - Former director of Pill Club Holdings ([Form D 2021](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1719865/000171986521000002/primary_doc.xml))\n  - Named angel in Harvey's Series E as \"Kris Fredrickson\" ([src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-series-e), 2025-06-23)\n  - Verified Capital appears in Juicebox's Series B ([src](https://juicebox.ai/blog/series-b)). We assume Kris and Kristian are the same person from the name and the Harvey overlap.\n- **LPs:** none named. The Form Ds give counts only (3, 113, 16 and 9 investors). No pension, endowment or fund-of-funds disclosure was found, and the WSJ article remains unread.\n\n## Update 2026-11-10 (later): Diffusion identity confirmed; firm set to Diffusion\n- **Confirmed by TechCrunch:** TechCrunch names \"Victor Lazarte and Kris Fredrickson, two former Benchmark VCs\" as raising $1.252B for Diffusion ONE. It credits that fund with the Harvey $550M co-lead (which it calls Series H) and with Etched's Series C ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/27/from-8vc-to-benchmark-heres-whats-hiding-in-october-sec-filings/), 2026-10-27). The round-post \"Diffusion\" is therefore his firm, at least for Harvey and Etched C.\n- **Forbes on the raise:** Forbes reported in May 2026 that the pair was raising ~$800M. The article is paywalled, so we have the headline only ([src](https://www.forbes.com/sites/iainmartin/2026/05/26/former-benchmark-investors-lazarte-and-fredrickson-aim-big-with-800-million-ai-fund/)).\n- **Bloomberg's framing:** Bloomberg's Harvey report, as relayed by aiweekly, credits Fredrickson as co-founder and does not name Lazarte ([src](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/harvey-raises-550m-at-156b-co-led-by-lightspeed-and-diffusion)).\n- **Frontmatter:** `firm` is now set to Diffusion. See [firms/diffusion.md](../firms/diffusion.md) and [people/kris-fredrickson.md](kris-fredrickson.md).\n- **Still unconfirmed:** whether Cognition and Applied Compute are Diffusion ONE credits (they match by name only), and which partner leads each deal.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | New firm site not identified |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/victorlazarte (handle unverified; fetch blocked) | Announced the Benchmark exit on X per [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/24/vc-victor-lazarte-is-leaving-benchmark-to-launch-his-own-firm/) |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-04 — 20VC — the episode covers why portfolio construction is BS, why SaaS spreadsheet investing is dead, and China as a stabilising force — https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/victor-lazarte. The show notes list founder traits (17:30), SaaS investing (35:30), China vs US AI (38:59), portfolio construction (44:38) and a Duolingo bull case ([src](https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/the-twenty-minute-vc-20vc-venture-capital--startup-funding--the-pitch/episode/20vc-benchmarks-victor-lazarte-on-why-portfolio-construction-is-bs-why-saas-spreadsheet-investing-is-dead--why-china-is-a-stabilising-force-for-the-us--three-traits-all-the-best-founders-have--the-lie-all-big-tech-companies-have-been-telling-2)). **No transcript obtained**, so nothing he said is quoted here.\n\n## How they think\n- **Concentration over coverage:** He makes very few high-leverage choices ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/)). The 20VC title suggests he rejects portfolio-construction orthodoxy (topic only).\n- **Operator bias as a known risk:** He told Fortune that ex-operators project their own vision onto pitches. His partners pushed back when he was excited about a small market ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/)).\n- **Demand proof before paying up:** In 2024 he backed AI companies with real usage and fast revenue (HeyGen profitable, $1M to $35M+ ARR) ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/)).\n- **Calls so far:**\n  - Mercor went from $250M (A) to $2B in Feb 2025 to $10B in Oct 2025 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/20/mercor-an-ai-recruiting-startup-founded-by-21-year-olds-raises-100m-at-2b-valuation/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/mercor-quintuples-valuation-to-10b-with-350m-series-c/)).\n  - Decart went from $500M+ to $3.1B in Aug 2025 ([src](https://fortune.com/2025/08/07/exclusive-decart-raises-100-million-at-a-3-1-billion-valuation-chasing-the-future-of-real-time-creative-ai/)), then raised $300M led by Radical in May 2026 ([src](https://www.decart.ai/)).\n  - Both look right so far, on paper.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Fortune, \"transitioning from operator to VC\" (2024-06-24): his only long on-record interview found ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/)).\n2. 20VC episode (Apr 2025): listen for portfolio construction and the China views. No transcript yet ([src](https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/victor-lazarte)).\n3. TechCrunch, Decart Series A (2024-12-19): shows his timing pattern ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/decart-adds-another-32m-at-a-500m-valuation/)).\n4. TechCrunch, departure (2025-07-24) ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/24/vc-victor-lazarte-is-leaving-benchmark-to-launch-his-own-firm/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X was not fetchable (HTTP 402).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| HeyGen | Series A $60M at $500M post | 2024-06 | Yes (Benchmark); board | Thrive, Bond, Conviction, Dylan Field, Elad Gil, SV Angel | [src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/) |\n| Mercor | Series A $32M at $250M | 2024 | Benchmark backed it; \"led\" not confirmed by a quality source | General Catalyst (seed lead 2023) | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/20/mercor-an-ai-recruiting-startup-founded-by-21-year-olds-raises-100m-at-2b-valuation/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/24/vc-victor-lazarte-is-leaving-benchmark-to-launch-his-own-firm/) |\n| Decart | Series A $32M, >$500M post | 2024-12-19 | Yes (Lazarte led) | Sequoia, Zeev Ventures | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/decart-adds-another-32m-at-a-500m-valuation/) |\n| Applied Compute | Early funding ($80M total disclosed) | by 2025-10-29 | No (angel) | Benchmark, Sequoia, Lux, Hanabi, Neo, Definition, Elad Gil, Omri Casspi | [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Seed ($180M) | 2026-01 | No | Index, GV, Sequoia | Aggregator only, unconfirmed ([src](https://iq.wiki/wiki/flapping-airplanes)) |\n| Etched (via Diffusion; firm identity inferred) | Series C ($300M at $10.3B) | 2026-07-23 | No (Sequoia led) | a16z, Jane Street, Argo, SK Hynix | [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference) |\n| Etched (via Diffusion; inferred) | Growth ($700M at $21B) | 2026-08-18 | No (Jane Street led) | Kleiner, Sequoia, a16z, Thiel, Tiger, Blackstone | [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one) |\n| Cognition (via Diffusion; inferred) | Series E ($2B+ at $48B) | 2026-09-08 | No (a16z, Accel led) | Founders Fund, GC, Avenir | [src](https://cognition.com/blog/series-e) |\n| Harvey (via Diffusion; inferred) | Growth ($550M at $15.5B) | 2026-09-09 | Yes, co-lead with Lightspeed | Sequoia, Kleiner, a16z, Coatue, GIC, Verified Capital | [src](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-dollar550m-at-a-dollar155b-valuation-to-help-legal-teams-own-their-intelligence) |\n| Applied Compute (via Diffusion; inferred) | Growth ($350M at $3.25B) | 2026-11-02 | No (Elad Gil led) | Kleiner, Lux, Hanabi, NVIDIA, Blackstone | [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **HeyGen (first Benchmark deal):** He came after about 11 months and hundreds of company reviews. The company was already profitable with $35M+ ARR. He led the round and joined the board ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/)). The intro path was not found.\n- **Decart:** Sequoia (Shaun Maguire, Dean Meyer byline) led the seed on 2024-10-31 ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-decart-the-future-of-ai-generated-experiences/)). Lazarte led the A less than two months later at about 5x the price. Sequoia and Zeev followed on ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/decart-adds-another-32m-at-a-500m-valuation/)). Next was the $100M at $3.1B (Aug 2025), in which Benchmark, Sequoia and Zeev participated and Aleph was new ([src](https://fortune.com/2025/08/07/exclusive-decart-raises-100-million-at-a-3-1-billion-valuation-chasing-the-future-of-real-time-creative-ai/)).\n- **Mercor:** General Catalyst led a $3.6M seed in 2023. Benchmark did the $32M A at $250M. Felicis led the B at $2B ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/20/mercor-an-ai-recruiting-startup-founded-by-21-year-olds-raises-100m-at-2b-valuation/)) and the C at $10B ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/mercor-quintuples-valuation-to-10b-with-350m-series-c/)). The intro path was not found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Diffusion (Diffusion ONE GP) | Managing Director of the GP, with Kris Fredrickson | 2026-06 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2141438/000123191926001442/primary_doc.xml) |\n| VL Fund I (VL Fund GP I) | Managing Director of the GP | 2025-09 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086666/000123191925000224/primary_doc.xml) |\n| [Kris Fredrickson](kris-fredrickson.md) | Co-MD at Diffusion; Verified Capital GP | 2026-06 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2141438/000214143826000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Benchmark | GP; left 2025-07 | ~2023 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/24/vc-victor-lazarte-is-leaving-benchmark-to-launch-his-own-firm/) |\n| Benchmark | Investor in his company (led Wildlife round) | 2019-12 | [src](https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wildlife_Studios) |\n| Arthur Lazarte | Wildlife co-founder (Fortune says he co-founded with \"his brother\"; not confirmed that this is Arthur) | 2011 | [src](https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wildlife_Studios) |\n| Michael Mac-Vicar | Wildlife co-founder | 2011 | [src](https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wildlife_Studios) |\n| HeyGen | Board director | 2024-06 | [src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/) |\n| Sequoia (Shaun Maguire) | Seed lead ahead of him in Decart | 2024-10 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-decart-the-future-of-ai-generated-experiences/) |\n| General Catalyst | Seed lead ahead of Benchmark in Mercor | 2023 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/20/mercor-an-ai-recruiting-startup-founded-by-21-year-olds-raises-100m-at-2b-valuation/) |\n| Thrive, Bond, Conviction, Elad Gil | HeyGen A co-investors | 2024-06 | [src](https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- What is the new firm's name, and who are its LPs and its check size? WSJ (2025-11-21) is still unread. **Partly answered 2026-11-10:** the SEC filings show VL Fund I ($45M, 2025) and Diffusion ONE ($1.252B, 2026), the latter co-run with Kris Fredrickson. Still open: LP names and whether VL Fund I is the WSJ \"$200M\" fund. The Diffusion identity was answered by TechCrunch on 2026-10-27.\n- Is he on Decart's or Mercor's board, and did he personally lead Mercor's A?\n- Is he really in Flapping Airplanes? Only iq.wiki lists him.\n- What did he say on 20VC about portfolio construction and China? We need the transcript.\n- Has the new fund led any AI-lab or compute seeds since Nov 2025? The search budget ran out before we could check.\n- Note: the brief says the fund is \"at Benchmark\". The sources say it is his own firm, separate from Benchmark.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2086666/000123191925000224/primary_doc.xml (VL Fund I Form D)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2141438/000214143826000001/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2141438/000123191926001442/primary_doc.xml (Diffusion ONE Form D, D/A)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2148945/000214894526000001/primary_doc.xml ; https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2157328/000215732826000001/primary_doc.xml (Diffusion V2, V3)\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2028211/000202821124000001/primary_doc.xml (Benchmark Partners 1)\n- https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-raises-dollar550m-at-a-dollar155b-valuation-to-help-legal-teams-own-their-intelligence\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/accelerating-inference ; https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one\n- https://cognition.com/blog/series-e ; https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/24/vc-victor-lazarte-is-leaving-benchmark-to-launch-his-own-firm/\n- https://fortune.com/2024/06/24/benchmark-victor-lazarte-transitioning-from-operator-to-vc/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/19/decart-adds-another-32m-at-a-500m-valuation/\n- https://fortune.com/2025/08/07/exclusive-decart-raises-100-million-at-a-3-1-billion-valuation-chasing-the-future-of-real-time-creative-ai/\n- https://www.decart.ai/about ; https://www.decart.ai/newsroom ; https://www.decart.ai/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-decart-the-future-of-ai-generated-experiences/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/20/mercor-an-ai-recruiting-startup-founded-by-21-year-olds-raises-100m-at-2b-valuation/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/mercor-quintuples-valuation-to-10b-with-350m-series-c/\n- https://www.heygen.com/article/announcing-our-series-a\n- https://appliedcompute.com/company/its-time-to-get-specific\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benchmark_(venture_capital_firm)\n- https://www.wsj.com/articles/ex-benchmark-partner-victor-lazarte-collects-200-million-for-debut-fund-1fdd2379 (headline only, via Wikipedia)\n- https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wildlife_Studios\n- https://www.thetwentyminutevc.com/victor-lazarte\n- https://iq.wiki/wiki/flapping-airplanes (aggregator)\n", "person:vidu-shanmugarajah": "\n# Vidu Shanmugarajah\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. GV's own pages use \"he\". The main primary source is the Scaling Europe interview (YouTube, 2026-01-30). Quotes from it come from YouTube auto-captions, so they keep the captions' stutters and misspellings (\"Cynthia\" for Synthesia). The Riding Unicorns episode (2024-11-13) has no transcript, and its YouTube copy is \"unavailable\", so only its show notes were used. No X account was found (GV's page links only @gvteam). LinkedIn was not read.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is GV's European AI-applications GP. He turned a 2021 Synthesia Series B into GV's lead of the $200M Series E in 2026, sits on the boards of the Lawhive and Buena AI-automation companies, and by his own record has no frontier-lab, AI-chip or compute deal.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he compounds conviction by holding positions over years and doubling down. He calls developing \"unreasonable conviction\" the holy grail of the job, and says what earns it is watching a team execute over several years ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ)).\n  - Synthesia: GV joined at the 2021 Series B and led the Series E in 2026 ([src](https://gv.com/news/interview-with-synthesia-ceo-victor-riparbelli)).\n  - Lawhive: GV led the 2024 seed, co-led the Series A and followed in the Series B ([src](https://gv.com/news/lawhive-ceo-pierre-proner-interview)).\n  - The Synthesia CEO's endorsement in GV's announcement: \"I know he'll always go to bat for us.\" ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/vidu-shanmugarajah-general-partner))\n- **What he backs:** applied AI with enterprise-grade economics. On Synthesia, what impressed him was founders who were \"completely unromantic\" about where the AI actually works ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ)). His Buena post states the AI-native automation thesis outright: automate labor-intensive industries \"to achieve software margins\" ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/buena-property-management)).\n- **What constrains him:** he is a single European GP inside a single-LP corporate fund. His deals are application-layer (video, legal, hiring, property, security, NLP tooling), and none are labs or silicon. He says GV is happy with low AI gross margins but wants a path to better economics ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ)). GV's London frontier-lab deal (Recursive) sits with Tom Hulme ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/recursive-superintelligence-self-improving-ai)).\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** back a founder who seems more interested in the success than in the problem. He tests for this with a \"murder detective\" line of questioning on detail ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ)). He also says he would not pin Europe's opportunity to one layer, model or application ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ)).\n\n## Differential insight\nNone of these appear in GV's press releases. Quotes are verbatim from auto-captions unless the line says otherwise.\n- **Conviction is earned over time.** He says \"the holy grail of our job as VCs is to develop unreasonable conviction\" and that it deepens after watching a company execute for years. That is why he doubled down from the B to the E ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ), ~00:00).\n- **The Synthesia insight that sold him.** The founders found that \"the best use of the AI wasn't where you were trying to replace video\". The real target was text. He says this came from hundreds of hours with customers, and he remembers Victor Riparbelli explaining it \"vividly\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ), ~00:04).\n- **What he means by a tier-one team.** His phrase is a tier-one AI team that is \"completely unromantic about how you deliver that value in the real world\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ), ~00:05).\n- **Metrics he cites at Series E (2026-01).** Synthesia \"now has over 140% net retention\" and serves over 90% of the Fortune 100. He says the ingredients were visible at the Series B, before the numbers were ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ), ~00:04).\n- **His order of screening.** Size of opportunity comes first: could this become a $100B+ company? Unit economics come second, and he is fine with companies \"operating at lower gross margins today as is normal in AI\" if there is a path to better ones ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ), ~00:08–00:09).\n- **A contrarian read on Europe.** Because the biggest customers are in the US, European founders go global with product-led growth from day one. He calls the distance \"arguably a feature and not a bug\" and names Synthesia, ElevenLabs and Metaview as examples ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ), ~00:12–00:13).\n- **He wants decades-long patience.** His reason: \"you get the biggest gains of compounding in years 10 to 20\". He uses NVIDIA as the example of a 30-year \"overnight success\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ), ~00:15).\n- **His diligence test.** He borrows Marc Andreessen's \"murder detective\" idea and probes for depth of detail. A thin story, like a thin alibi, falls apart ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ), ~00:16).\n- **What unifies his founders.** \"They're in it for the love of the game\". He also stresses founder–company fit: \"an exceptional founder for one company may not be the right founder\" for another ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ), ~00:18–00:19).\n- **He wrote GV's Buena thesis.** It amounts to AI-native automation: \"take labor-intensive industries and automate them with AI to achieve software margins\" ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/buena-property-management), 2025-07-23).\n- **How he picked deepset (2022).** He was struck by the founders' bet that they could \"build a bridge between research and industry\" through open-source NLP ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/deepset-natural-language)).\n- **A self-description that matches the transcript.** \"I'm massively curious and want to understand things from first principles.\" ([src](https://www.gv.com/team/vidu-shanmugarajah)) He uses first-principles framing in the interview too: pre-AI software handled structured data, and LLMs open up unstructured data and media ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ), ~00:09).\n- **He uses AI for GV content.** His 2025 Synthesia lessons post says it was \"vibe-scribed\" by Gemini from a transcript ([src](https://gv.com/news/synthesia-secrets-product-market-fit)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **No frontier-lab, AI-chip or compute deal is credited to him.** His GV list is Synthesia, deepset, Lawhive, Metaview, Buena, Push Security, Genie AI, Resistant AI, Scandit and several exited or biotech names ([src](https://www.gv.com/team/vidu-shanmugarajah)). In GV London, frontier labs belong to Tom Hulme (Recursive).\n- **Where he matches the lens is AI-native automation**, the Serval-like category:\n  - Buena (AI property management, with software margins as the thesis).\n  - Lawhive (AI plus human lawyers for consumer legal work).\n  - Metaview (vertical AI for hiring).\n- **Synthesia is a European AI company with its own research team** (co-founders include Prof. Matt Niessner and Prof. Lourdes Agapito ([src](https://gv.com/case-study/synthesia))). NVIDIA's NVentures sits beside GV on its cap table ([src](https://www.synthesia.io/post/series-e-200-million-4-billion-valuation-future-work)).\n- **Useful to you as:**\n  - A co-investor or follow-on source for European AI applications.\n  - A route to Synthesia's founders. They are frontier-adjacent European AI operators and possible future lab founders or angels.\n  - Not a source for entry into frontier labs.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** law degree, London School of Economics ([src](https://www.gv.com/team/vidu-shanmugarajah)).\n- **Career before GV** ([src](https://www.gv.com/team/vidu-shanmugarajah)):\n  - Linklaters, corporate law (M&A, restructuring, financing).\n  - Grafton Capital (enterprise software investments).\n  - HgCapital (tech buyouts and growth equity).\n  - Founded an online platform giving startups and small businesses access to legal services.\n- **Dates for the pre-GV moves:** not found.\n- **GV:** joined in 2014, shortly after GV opened its European office, \"initially as a lawyer\". He then moved into investing ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/vidu-shanmugarajah-general-partner)).\n- **Titles:**\n  - Partner on Riding Unicorns, 2024-11 ([src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1620178/episodes/16040444-vidu-shanmugarajah-partner-gv)).\n  - General Partner, announced 2025-09-25 by Tom Hulme ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/vidu-shanmugarajah-general-partner)).\n- **Promotion pace:** about 11 years from joining GV to GP, and the starting role was legal rather than investing. So fast_promotion is no. The exact date of his move into investing was not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.gv.com/team/vidu-shanmugarajah | GV bio, investments list, press list (read 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | GV news posts under his byline | ~1–4 posts/yr; 4 founder interviews on 2026-03-18 |\n| X | none found | GV page links only @gvteam |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/vidu-shanmugarajah/ | from GV page; not read |\n| GitHub | none found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-01-30: Scaling Europe (host Seb Johnson). \"Backing Synthesia from Series B to a $200m Series E\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ)). What stood out: the \"unreasonable conviction\" framing, the replace-text-not-video insight, 140%+ net retention, the Europe PLG \"feature not a bug\" view, the murder-detective diligence test, and the compounding-in-years-10-to-20 point.\n- 2024-11-13: Riding Unicorns S8E6 (hosts James Pringle, Hector Mason). \"Vidu Shanmugarajah, Partner @ GV\" ([src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1620178/episodes/16040444-vidu-shanmugarajah-partner-gv)). Show notes only: his path from law to PE to founder to GV, GV's Alphabet model, standout founders, AI and diversity. No transcript was available, and the YouTube copy (DCmgjBt-V1g) is unavailable.\n- 2026-04-16: Forbes video on Synthesia, listed on his GV page ([src](https://www.forbes.com/sites/alexyork/2026/04/16/video-meet-the-4-billion-startup-remaking-corporate-training-with-ai-avatars/)). Not read.\n\n## How they think\n- **Opportunity size first, economics second.** He tolerates low AI gross margins for now ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ)).\n- **AI's expansion:** AI extends software from structured data into unstructured data and media, which widens the opportunity set ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ)).\n- **Europe:** founder-led and horizontal, not tied to one layer. Distance from US customers pushes companies into global product-led growth ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ)).\n- **Founders:** he looks for patience over decades, love of the problem, and founder–company fit ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ)).\n- **Vertical AI:**\n  - Metaview's Series B post is titled \"The Power of Vertical AI in Hiring\" ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/metaview-ai-hiring)).\n  - Buena's is AI-automated property management ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/buena-property-management)).\n  - Push Security's 2023 thesis: \"SaaS exploits will become the primary way to compromise\" companies ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/push-security-enterprise-saas-security)).\n- **Calls so far:**\n  - **Right:** Synthesia, from a 2021 Series B to $4B in 2026 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/26/synthesia-hits-4b-valuation-lets-employees-cash-in/)).\n  - **Right:** Lawhive, from a £9.5M seed (2024) to a $60M Series B (2026) ([src](https://techstartups.com/2026/02/05/lawhive-raises-60m-series-b-funding-to-expand-across-35-us-states/)).\n  - **No misses found** in public sources.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Scaling Europe interview, 2026-01-30 ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ)). The only long-form primary source with his own reasoning.\n2. \"Buena: The AI Powered Platform That's Reshaping Property Management\", 2025-07-23 ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/buena-property-management)). His AI-automation thesis.\n3. \"Synthesia's $2B+ Secrets\", 2025-04-03 ([src](https://gv.com/news/synthesia-secrets-product-market-fit)). Victor Riparbelli's lessons on avoiding AI hype, as he chose to frame them.\n4. \"Metaview: The Power of Vertical AI in Hiring\", 2025-06-25 ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/metaview-ai-hiring)).\n5. \"Deepset: Moving Open Source NLP Forward\", 2022-04-28 ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/deepset-natural-language)). His pre-ChatGPT LLM-tooling bet.\n\n## Social engagement\n- No personal X account was found.\n- Public output is GV-site posts and founder Q&As. On 2026-03-18 he published four interviews at once: Synthesia, Lawhive, Buena and OMass ([src](https://www.gv.com/team/vidu-shanmugarajah)).\n- He is amplified mainly through portfolio press (Synthesia, Lawhive). Who he replies to or amplifies: not checked.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Synthesia | Series B ($50M); GV's entry | 2021-12-08 | Lead structure not stated (GV named alongside Kleiner Perkins) | Kleiner Perkins | [src](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2021/12/08/ai-video-avatar-platform-synthesia-raises-50-million-in-venture-capital), [src](https://gv.com/news/interview-with-synthesia-ceo-victor-riparbelli) |\n| Synthesia | Series E ($200M at $4B) | 2026-01-26 | Yes (GV led) | Evantic, Hedosophia, NVentures, Accel, Kleiner Perkins, NEA, PSP Growth, Air Street, FirstMark, MMC | [src](https://www.synthesia.io/post/series-e-200-million-4-billion-valuation-future-work) |\n| deepset | Series A ($14M) | 2022-04-28 | Yes | not named in GV post | [src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20220428005187/en), [src](https://www.gv.com/news/deepset-natural-language) |\n| deepset | Series B ($30M) | 2023-08-09 | No (Balderton led; GV existing investor) | Balderton | [src](https://www.deepset.ai/blog/funding-announcement-balderton-capital) |\n| Push Security | Series A ($15M) | 2023-04-03 | Yes (co-authored with Karim Faris) | not named | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/push-security-enterprise-saas-security) |\n| Lawhive | Seed (£9.5M) | 2024-04-15 | Yes | Episode 1 | [src](https://www.uktechnews.info/2024/04/15/lawhive-secures-9-5-million-seed-investment-led-by-gv/) |\n| Lawhive | Series A ($40M) | 2024-12-05 | Co-led with TQ Ventures | Balderton, Jigsaw, Episode 1 | [src](https://tech.eu/2024/12/05/uk-legaltech-lawhive-secures-40m-series-a-for-us-market-entry/) |\n| Lawhive | Series B ($60M) | 2026-02-05 | No (Mitch Rales led) | TQ Ventures, Balderton, Jigsaw, Anton Levy, LTS | [src](https://techstartups.com/2026/02/05/lawhive-raises-60m-series-b-funding-to-expand-across-35-us-states/) |\n| Metaview | Series B ($35M) | 2025-06-25 | Yes | not named | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/metaview-ai-hiring) |\n| Buena | Series A | 2024 (per GV); Forbes headline \"$58 Million From Google Ventures\" 2025-07-25 | Yes | not found | [src](https://gv.com/news/din-bisevac-interview-buena-property-management), [src](https://www.forbes.com/sites/zoyahasan/2025/07/25/how-this-26-year-old-rescued-a-failing-real-estate-startup-and-landed-58-million-from-google-ventures/) |\n| Genie AI, Resistant AI, Scandit, OMass, Evox, Oxford Science Enterprises; exits Context.ai, Currencycloud, Vaccitech, Wonderbly | profile list | n/a | n/a | n/a | [src](https://www.gv.com/team/vidu-shanmugarajah) |\n\n**Frontier AI, AI chips and compute: none found.** The closest names:\n- **Synthesia:** a generative-video model company. GV was not first in. FirstMark and MMC were earlier backers and are listed as existing investors at the E ([src](https://www.synthesia.io/post/series-e-200-million-4-billion-valuation-future-work)). NVentures is also on the cap table; the date it entered was not confirmed.\n- **deepset:** open-source LLM/NLP tooling (Haystack). He led the 2022 Series A before ChatGPT launched.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Synthesia:**\n  - **Entry:** GV first invested at the Series B in 2021 ([src](https://gv.com/news/interview-with-synthesia-ceo-victor-riparbelli)), a deal \"pre-ChatGPT\" by his own description ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ)).\n  - **Why he committed:** the founders could take a new, buggy technology and find an enterprise use. Their use was corporate training, replacing text rather than video ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ)).\n  - **Role since:** he is a board observer ([src](https://gv.com/news/interview-with-synthesia-ceo-victor-riparbelli)).\n  - **Doubling down:** GV led the $200M Series E on 2026-01-26. Synthesia also ran an employee secondary through Nasdaq at the same $4B valuation ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/26/synthesia-hits-4b-valuation-lets-employees-cash-in/)).\n  - **Intro path:** not found.\n- **Lawhive:** GV led the £9.5M seed in April 2024 ([src](https://www.uktechnews.info/2024/04/15/lawhive-secures-9-5-million-seed-investment-led-by-gv/)) and co-led the $40M A eight months later ([src](https://tech.eu/2024/12/05/uk-legaltech-lawhive-secures-40m-series-a-for-us-market-entry/)). He sits on the board ([src](https://gv.com/news/lawhive-ceo-pierre-proner-interview)). This fits his background in legal-services access (he founded a legal-services platform himself). The link between the two is my inference.\n- **Buena:** GV led the Series A in 2024. He is on the board ([src](https://gv.com/news/din-bisevac-interview-buena-property-management)). Forbes frames the founder as having rescued a failing startup (headline only; the article was not readable).\n- **deepset:** he led the $14M Series A in 2022 ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/deepset-natural-language)). The firm file records it as GV's first German deal.\n- **Push Security:** co-led internally with Karim Faris, Series A 2023 ([src](https://www.gv.com/news/push-security-enterprise-saas-security)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Tom Hulme (GV MP, Head of Europe) | Wrote his GP-promotion post; London leadership | 2014 (GV Europe) | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/vidu-shanmugarajah-general-partner) |\n| Karim Faris (GV GP) | Co-author / co-deal on Push Security | 2023 | [src](https://www.gv.com/news/push-security-enterprise-saas-security) |\n| Victor Riparbelli (Synthesia CEO) | Board observer; backer since Series B | 2021 | [src](https://gv.com/news/interview-with-synthesia-ceo-victor-riparbelli) |\n| Steffen Tjerrild (Synthesia co-founder) | Named with Riparbelli as the team he backed | 2021 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ) |\n| Pierre Proner (Lawhive CEO) | Board member | 2024 | [src](https://gv.com/news/lawhive-ceo-pierre-proner-interview) |\n| Din Bisevac (Buena CEO) | Board member | 2024 | [src](https://gv.com/news/din-bisevac-interview-buena-property-management) |\n| TQ Ventures | Co-led Lawhive Series A | 2024 | [src](https://tech.eu/2024/12/05/uk-legaltech-lawhive-secures-40m-series-a-for-us-market-entry/) |\n| Kleiner Perkins | Co-investor at Synthesia Series B | 2021 | [src](https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2021/12/08/ai-video-avatar-platform-synthesia-raises-50-million-in-venture-capital) |\n| NVentures (NVIDIA) | Co-investor at Synthesia (existing investor at E) | by 2026-01 | [src](https://www.synthesia.io/post/series-e-200-million-4-billion-valuation-future-work) |\n| Balderton Capital | Led deepset B (GV followed); co-investor in Lawhive | 2023 | [src](https://www.deepset.ai/blog/funding-announcement-balderton-capital) |\n| Episode 1 Ventures | Co-investor in Lawhive seed | 2024 | [src](https://www.uktechnews.info/2024/04/15/lawhive-secures-9-5-million-seed-investment-led-by-gv/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who led the Synthesia Series B, and how did GV get the deal: did FirstMark, MMC or Kleiner make the intro? When did NVentures enter Synthesia?\n- When did he move from GV legal into an investing title, and what were his titles before Partner?\n- The Riding Unicorns episode needs a transcript; the audio may cover his own startup and the deals he passed on.\n- Has he looked at any European neolab (Recursive, or labs out of DeepMind London), or does all frontier-lab flow go to Hulme?\n- Genie AI and Resistant AI: round and year not checked.\n- Buena: the Series A year (2024 per GV) conflicts with the Forbes $58M story dated 2025-07.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.gv.com/team/vidu-shanmugarajah (read 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/vidu-shanmugarajah-general-partner (2025-09-25)\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ (Scaling Europe, 2026-01-30; auto-captions)\n- https://www.buzzsprout.com/1620178/episodes/16040444-vidu-shanmugarajah-partner-gv (Riding Unicorns, 2024-11-13; show notes only)\n- https://www.synthesia.io/post/series-e-200-million-4-billion-valuation-future-work (2026-01-26)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/26/synthesia-hits-4b-valuation-lets-employees-cash-in/\n- https://gv.com/news/interview-with-synthesia-ceo-victor-riparbelli (2026-03-18)\n- https://gv.com/case-study/synthesia\n- https://gv.com/news/synthesia-secrets-product-market-fit (2025-04-03)\n- https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2021/12/08/ai-video-avatar-platform-synthesia-raises-50-million-in-venture-capital\n- https://www.gv.com/news/deepset-natural-language (2022-04-28); https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20220428005187/en ; https://www.deepset.ai/blog/funding-announcement-balderton-capital\n- https://www.gv.com/news/push-security-enterprise-saas-security (2023-04-03)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/metaview-ai-hiring (2025-06-25)\n- https://www.gv.com/news/buena-property-management (2025-07-23); https://gv.com/news/din-bisevac-interview-buena-property-management (2026-03-18)\n- https://www.uktechnews.info/2024/04/15/lawhive-secures-9-5-million-seed-investment-led-by-gv/ ; https://tech.eu/2024/12/05/uk-legaltech-lawhive-secures-40m-series-a-for-us-market-entry/ ; https://techstartups.com/2026/02/05/lawhive-raises-60m-series-b-funding-to-expand-across-35-us-states/ ; https://gv.com/news/lawhive-ceo-pierre-proner-interview\n- https://www.gv.com/news/recursive-superintelligence-self-improving-ai\n", "person:vijay-pande": "\n# Vijay Pande\n\n## One-line take\nPande was the scientist who talked Andreessen Horowitz into biology, and he ran the bio practice until it managed about $4B. He now runs VZVC, a two-person firm with no associates that relies on agents and plans about five concentrated bets a year ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/29/were-not-doing-30-bets-a-year-vijay-pande-on-betting-small-after-running-4-billion-at-a16z/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he combines academic credibility with founder pipelines. Folding@home came out of his Stanford lab, Genesis was spun out of it, and Daphne Koller (insitro) was a Stanford colleague. He also had a thesis early: in 2016 he said \"ML + engineered biology\" would come to look like software in 2005, and that call aged well ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ISXqEBVuKeQ)).\n- **What he screens for:**\n  - founders who are \"full stack\" in both biology and computer science\n  - platforms that can also generate drug assets\n  - go-to-market treated as being as hard as the science\n- **What constrains him now:** VZVC is two people and the fund is still being raised. A source told STAT the fund could be up to $400M ([src](https://www.statnews.com/2026/01/13/andreessen-horowitz-alum-vijay-pande-plans-new-vc-firm/)). He says founders \"make room\" for VZVC, so it does not compete in hot A/B rounds.\n- **What he would not do:**\n  - single-asset biotech, which is how he reads a16z's original \"no healthcare\" rule\n  - companies that bolt off-the-shelf ML onto genomics\n  - spray-and-pray volume (\"not doing 30 bets a year\")\n\n## Differential insight\n- In 2016, a few months into the a16z bio fund, his founder filter was that the winners are teams that \"go full stack in the biology and the computer science\". Teams that take genomics and ML \"out of the box\" would only do OK ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ISXqEBVuKeQ), 11:59 and 12:31).\n- His 2016 screen was a cost-curve argument. He wanted businesses whose markets sit on Eroom's-law curves (drugs and care getting more expensive) but whose own costs \"take advantage of the Moore's Law curves\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ISXqEBVuKeQ), 16:17).\n- He reads Marc and Ben's founding \"no healthcare\" rule narrowly: \"what they really meant was a single asset, style, life sciences\" ([src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/SED1645-SED1645_a16z_Bio.txt), 0:05:10).\n- Workload at a16z: \"I've got maybe 25, 30 investments that I'm shepherding\", inside a Bio + Health team of about 50 ([src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/SED1645-SED1645_a16z_Bio.txt), 0:01:39 and 0:03:27).\n- Deal origin: \"we actually led their series a several years ago\". This was BioAge, and a16z kept re-investing ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9r7bSjK6aWs), 51:26). Portfolio company Q Bio flagged his own pre-diabetes ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9r7bSjK6aWs), 47:28).\n- In a late-2024 bust for platform biotechs, he said \"platform companies that can survive now and build now\" will be the winners. He added that \"the best platforms generate a bunch of assets too\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pTm_ANV1Ksk), 42:58 and 43:19).\n- **Timing:** his October 2024 essay argued \"A 20-person team powered by AI could perform at the level\" of a 200-person one ([src](https://vijaypande.substack.com/p/the-economics-of-building-ai-first)). By August 2026, VZVC had dropped plans to hire associates \"with the agents that we've built up\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/29/were-not-doing-30-bets-a-year-vijay-pande-on-betting-small-after-running-4-billion-at-a16z/)).\n- He compares adding a company at a typical fund to \"adding a Facebook friend\"; at VZVC it is \"more like … wanting to have another child\". He names Antonio Gracias at Valor, and Thrive's concentrated portfolio, as models ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/29/were-not-doing-30-bets-a-year-vijay-pande-on-betting-small-after-running-4-billion-at-a16z/)).\n- Admitted mistake: \"it really always comes back to go-to-market\". He says he took time to learn that GTM is at least as hard as the technology ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/29/were-not-doing-30-bets-a-year-vijay-pande-on-betting-small-after-running-4-billion-at-a16z/)).\n- As a new fund manager in 2026, he publicly urges readers to \"root for the crash that torches my own asset class\" in AI ([src](https://vijaypande.substack.com/p/let-it-crash-how-to-steer-what-comes)).\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-11)\nThis is the first pass over the archive: about 200 catalogued items, and 10 of them mined. The strongest points:\n- His founder filter has been stable for a decade:\n  - full-stack bio x CS founders in 2016 ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ISXqEBVuKeQ))\n  - \"engineering mindset\" companies in 2018 ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=la8P4Bm5A_0))\n  - one person combining biology, ML and GTM in 2024 ([src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/SED1645-SED1645_a16z_Bio.txt))\n- What changed is the GTM weighting. He now calls GTM his main learned lesson ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/29/were-not-doing-30-bets-a-year-vijay-pande-on-betting-small-after-running-4-billion-at-a16z/)).\n- VZVC puts his October 2024 \"AI-first company\" essay into practice: two investors, agents instead of associates, and about five bets a year ([src](https://vijaypande.substack.com/p/the-economics-of-building-ai-first), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/29/were-not-doing-30-bets-a-year-vijay-pande-on-betting-small-after-running-4-billion-at-a16z/)).\n- He is contrarian on the cycle in both directions:\n  - backing platforms in the 2024 bust ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pTm_ANV1Ksk))\n  - rooting for an AI crash in 2026 ([src](https://vijaypande.substack.com/p/let-it-crash-how-to-steer-what-comes))\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** His focus is AI-bio and healthcare AI, not pre-training labs, chips or compute, so the deals themselves are only adjacent. Two things are thesis-relevant:\n- VZVC is a concentrated, AI-native fund modelled on Valor and Thrive, built around about five bets a year. That is a structure close to what the owner runs.\n- He has a pipeline of academic founders from Stanford AI x chemistry (Genesis, insitro).\n\nThe deals that matter:\n- **Genesis Molecular AI:** he led the seed; it is a spinout of his lab (per existing lead row).\n- **insitro:** a16z led the Series B ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-insitro/)).\n- **Inceptive:** he was the sole outside director on its 2023 Form D ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1993343/000199334323000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **Asimov:** described in his 2018 talk as an a16z investment ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=la8P4Bm5A_0)).\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** physics undergraduate at Princeton, then a physics PhD at MIT (1992-1995). He spent time at Berkeley between MIT and Stanford ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9r7bSjK6aWs), 1:00 to 13:00).\n- **High school:** first employee at Naughty Dog, alongside founders Andy Gavin and Jason Rubin ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9r7bSjK6aWs), 0:57).\n- **Stanford, 1999-2015:**\n  - chemistry, structural biology and computer science\n  - chair of biophysics and Henry Dreyfus Professor of Chemistry\n  - founded Folding@home in 2000, which set a Guinness petaflop record in 2007\n  - sources: [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9r7bSjK6aWs), [src](https://vijaypande.substack.com/p/a-personal-update)\n- **Startups:** co-founded Globavir Biosciences, which worked on drugs for dengue and Ebola ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9r7bSjK6aWs), 35:00).\n- **a16z:**\n  - 2014: professor-in-residence\n  - 2015: founded the Bio + Health fund and became GP\n  - 2017-12: Bio Fund II ($450M) ([src](https://a16z.com/bio-fund-ii/))\n  - grew the practice to about $4B\n  - 2025-06-09: stepped away ([src](https://vijaypande.substack.com/p/a-personal-update), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/29/were-not-doing-30-bets-a-year-vijay-pande-on-betting-small-after-running-4-billion-at-a16z/))\n- **VZVC:** co-founded in 2025 with Zack Werner ([src](https://www.statnews.com/2026/01/13/andreessen-horowitz-alum-vijay-pande-plans-new-vc-firm/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found for VZVC site | |\n| Substack / blog | https://vijaypande.substack.com | 38 posts. A burst in Oct-Nov 2024, then weekly from Jun 2026 (\"Renaissance Papers\", essays on AI and society). Checked 2026-11-11 |\n| X | https://x.com/vijaypande | Handle per a16z podcast notes; not fetched |\n| LinkedIn | nothing checked | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Bio Eats World / Raising Health (a16z), 2020-2025 | https://raisinghealth.simplecast.com |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2016-03: Vator Splash Health talk, \"Bio 2.0\" ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ISXqEBVuKeQ)). Key points: full-stack founders, Eroom vs Moore, and \"bio today is software in 2005\".\n- 2016-10: a16z Podcast with Freenome's Gabriel Otte, on the Freenome thesis ([src](https://a16z.simplecast.com/episodes/a16z-podcast-on-the-genomics-of-disease-from-science-to-business-oPXtdsNI)). Not mined.\n- 2018-05: TiE Inflect talk ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=la8P4Bm5A_0)). Covers his portfolio (Freenome, Cardiogram, Asimov, Omada, PatientPing, BioAge) and the shift from empiricism to engineering.\n- 2018-05: a16z Podcast, \"Shifting Risk Mindsets, From Tech to Bio\", with Jorge Conde ([src](https://a16z.simplecast.com/episodes/a16z-podcast-shifting-risk-mindsets-from-tech-to-bio-mCB0i6Tp)). Not mined; the a16z page no longer has a transcript.\n- 2021-05: POD OF JAKE #64 ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9r7bSjK6aWs)). Covers how BioAge's Series A was led, Q Bio, his FDA view and the Naughty Dog origin.\n- 2023-10: Raising Health with Evan Feinberg on Genesis, a spinout of his lab ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/supercharging-discovery-with-evan-feinberg/)). Not mined.\n- 2024-02: Software Engineering Daily ([src](https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/SED1645-SED1645_a16z_Bio.txt)). Covers his 25-30 investments, a 50-person team and how he weights GTM.\n- 2024-08: NEJM AI Grand Rounds ([src](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/from-petri-dishes-to-pitch-decks-cultivating-health/id1657518313?i=1000666121654)). Not mined.\n- 2024-12: Translating Proteomics (Nautilus) with Matt McIlwain ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pTm_ANV1Ksk)). Covers platforms vs assets in the bust.\n- 2025-10: Endpoints Post-Hoc Live ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=17UlmTKWfyI)). Not mined.\n- 2026-08: TechCrunch/StrictlyVC Q&A on VZVC ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/29/were-not-doing-30-bets-a-year-vijay-pande-on-betting-small-after-running-4-billion-at-a16z/)).\n- The full catalogue is in `data/rows/vijay-pande-voice-media.csv`: 132 podcast episodes, 15 YouTube videos and 38 Substack posts.\n\n## How they think\n- **Biology is moving from empiricism to engineering.** He uses the \"bridge\" analogy: nobody tests a bridge by sending mice over it ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=la8P4Bm5A_0)).\n- **AI-first companies should be built natively, not retrofitted.** They gain more equity per employee and avoid the drag of a large org ([src](https://vijaypande.substack.com/p/the-economics-of-building-ai-first)).\n- **Data is the bottleneck in bio AI, not models.** He expects open-source bio foundation models (\"atlases\") to matter the way open LLMs did ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/29/were-not-doing-30-bets-a-year-vijay-pande-on-betting-small-after-running-4-billion-at-a16z/)).\n- **Contrarian views:**\n  - payers and real-world evidence should carry more of the efficacy burden than the FDA ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9r7bSjK6aWs))\n  - he roots for an AI crash ([src](https://vijaypande.substack.com/p/let-it-crash-how-to-steer-what-comes))\n- **Calls that came true:**\n  - ML and AI drug design going mainstream, which was \"heretical\" in 2015 ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9r7bSjK6aWs))\n  - the insitro and Genesis outcomes\n- **Mistake:** he learned late how much GTM matters.\n\n## Read these first\n- [When Software Eats Bio (2015)](https://a16z.com/when-software-eats-bio/): the founding a16z bio thesis.\n- [Vator Splash 2016 talk](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ISXqEBVuKeQ): his clearest early statement of how he screens companies.\n- [Biology is Eating the World: A Manifesto (2019)](https://a16z.com/biology-is-eating-the-world-a-manifesto/): the mid-a16z worldview, co-written with Conde and Yoo.\n- [The Economics of Building AI-First Companies (2024)](https://vijaypande.substack.com/p/the-economics-of-building-ai-first): the blueprint for VZVC's operating model.\n- [TechCrunch Q&A (2026)](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/29/were-not-doing-30-bets-a-year-vijay-pande-on-betting-small-after-running-4-billion-at-a16z/): VZVC's pace, its models and the GTM lesson.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing checked on X in this pass. In 2021 he named Twitter (@vijaypande) and Clubhouse as his main channels ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9r7bSjK6aWs)). The Substack is his active channel in 2026.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Freenome | Undisclosed (investing post) | 2016-06 | unknown | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/freenome/) |\n| Cardiogram | Undisclosed (investing post) | 2016-10 | unknown | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/cardiogram/) |\n| BioAge Labs | Series A | 2017-07 | yes (\"we actually led their series a\") | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/bioage/), [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9r7bSjK6aWs) |\n| insitro | Series B | 2020-05 | yes | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-insitro/) |\n| Scribe Therapeutics | Undisclosed | 2020-10 | unknown | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-scribe-therapeutics/) |\n| Function Health | Undisclosed | 2024-06 | unknown | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-function-health/) |\n| Slingshot AI | Undisclosed (board) | 2025-01 | unknown | | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-slingshot-ai/) |\n| Asimov | Undisclosed (named as investment) | by 2018-05 | unknown | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=la8P4Bm5A_0) |\n| Q Bio | Undisclosed (portfolio co) | by 2021 | unknown | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9r7bSjK6aWs) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **BioAge (2017):** the memo builds the case from Wyss-Coray's parabiosis results to \"age itself as an indication\". Blood biomarkers could shorten aging trials. a16z led the Series A and kept re-investing ([src](https://a16z.com/announcement/bioage/), [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9r7bSjK6aWs)).\n- **Genesis:** a spinout of Pande's own Stanford lab. Evan Feinberg did his lab work there ([src](https://a16z.com/podcast/supercharging-discovery-with-evan-feinberg/)).\n- **Q Bio:** he used the product himself, and it caught his pre-diabetes ([src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9r7bSjK6aWs)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Andreessen Horowitz | Professor-in-residence, then founding GP of Bio + Health (left 2025-06) | 2014 | [src](https://vijaypande.substack.com/p/a-personal-update) |\n| VZVC | Co-founder | 2025 | [src](https://www.statnews.com/2026/01/13/andreessen-horowitz-alum-vijay-pande-plans-new-vc-firm/) |\n| Zack Werner | VZVC co-founder | 2025 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/29/were-not-doing-30-bets-a-year-vijay-pande-on-betting-small-after-running-4-billion-at-a16z/) |\n| Jorge Conde, Julie Yoo, Vineeta Agarwala | Bio + Health partners he says he built the team with | | [src](https://vijaypande.substack.com/p/a-personal-update) |\n| Evan Feinberg | Former lab member; Genesis founder | | [src](https://a16z.com/podcast/supercharging-discovery-with-evan-feinberg/) |\n| Antonio Gracias (Valor), Thrive Capital | Named models for VZVC | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/29/were-not-doing-30-bets-a-year-vijay-pande-on-betting-small-after-running-4-billion-at-a16z/) |\n| Matt McIlwain (Madrona) | Co-panelist; shared platform thesis | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pTm_ANV1Ksk) |\n\n## Open questions\n- VZVC: fund size actually closed, its LPs, check size, and the company being incubated with a founder he has known for 20 years (per TechCrunch's question).\n- Who is Zack Werner, and what is his track record?\n- The unmined 2018 risk-mindset episode, the 2016 Freenome episode and the 2023 Genesis episode. Their transcripts are gone from a16z.com, so they need audio transcription.\n- NEJM AI Grand Rounds (2024) and Endpoints (2025) are likely to have post-a16z reflections.\n\n## Sources\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/29/were-not-doing-30-bets-a-year-vijay-pande-on-betting-small-after-running-4-billion-at-a16z/\n- https://www.statnews.com/2026/01/13/andreessen-horowitz-alum-vijay-pande-plans-new-vc-firm/\n- https://vijaypande.substack.com/p/a-personal-update\n- https://vijaypande.substack.com/p/the-economics-of-building-ai-first\n- https://vijaypande.substack.com/p/let-it-crash-how-to-steer-what-comes\n- https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/SED1645-SED1645_a16z_Bio.txt\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ISXqEBVuKeQ\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=la8P4Bm5A_0\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9r7bSjK6aWs\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pTm_ANV1Ksk\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/bioage/\n- https://a16z.com/podcast/supercharging-discovery-with-evan-feinberg/\n", "person:vikas-parekh": "\n# Vikas J. Parekh\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. The pools of money, the investment committee and the OpenAI mechanics are in [firms/softbank-group.md](../firms/softbank-group.md) and [firms/softbank-vision-fund-2.md](../firms/softbank-vision-fund-2.md). The round-by-round ledger is in [sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md](../sources/softbank-ai-ledger.md). The id is `vikas-parekh`, the one already used in the SoftBank row files; the fund site spells the name with the middle initial.*\n\nknown_for: SoftBank's Americas managing partner for chips, compute and robotics; senior name on the fund-site OpenAI team; fronted the Graphcore purchase and Symbotic.\n\n## One-line take\nThe person inside SoftBank who covers US chips, compute, robotics and the OpenAI position, and who speaks for the parent when it buys a company. He sponsors deals; he does not sign them.\n\n## Crux\nParekh is a KKR-trained electrical engineer who made managing partner on control work: GCV credits him with the WeWork restructuring and the Arm buyout, not with venture rounds ([GCV 2021-11-23](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sbia-scores-four-managing-partners/?format=md)). His edge is that he sits in two seats. The fund site calls him Managing Partner, Americas, at SoftBank Investment Advisers; a 2026 SEC filing calls him a Managing Partner at SoftBank Group International, the parent unit ([Twenty One Capital 10-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070457/000121390026037460/ea0280065-10k_twenty.htm)). So he can bring a fund check, a parent acquisition or a SoftBank customer contract to the same company. His constraint is that none of it is his to approve. The fund's manager entity makes the final decisions, and the large AI checks are Masayoshi Son's. He does not do seeds: the earliest entry on his list is a Series D.\n\n## The six method questions, for this person\n\n**1. Which pool he invests from.** Three, depending on the deal.\n- *Vision Fund 2* (the parent's money plus Son's co-investment; see the fund page). The fund site lists him on 21 company pages labelled SVF2, including OpenAI, SambaNova, Labelbox, Agility Robotics, Helion and Symbotic ([his team page](https://visionfund.com/team/vikas-j-parekh); each company page was fetched on 2026-11-10).\n- *Vision Fund 1* (outside LPs). Eight pages: Slack, Cohesity, Nauto, Petuum, Cruise, OSIsoft, Zomato and the Arm stake. Five of those are marked fully divested.\n- *The parent balance sheet.* He is the quoted SoftBank person on two parent deals that are not fund investments: the Graphcore acquisition ([Graphcore, 2024-07-11](https://www.graphcore.ai/posts/graphcore-joins-softbank-group-to-build-next-generation-of-ai-compute)) and the GreenBox joint venture with Symbotic ([SoftBank Group, 2023-07-24](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20230724)).\n\n**2. What he signs.** Nothing on his own, on the evidence. Symbotic's proxy says SB Global Advisers, the manager, is \"exclusively responsible for making final decisions\" on what Vision Fund 2 buys, structures, finances and sells ([Symbotic proxy, 2026-01-16](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837240/000119312526015381/sym-20260116.htm)). Alex Clavel runs that manager. Parent deals go to the SoftBank Group investment committee and Son (firm page). No source gives Parekh a personal check limit. His role is to source, diligence, hold the board seat and present.\n\n**3. The strategic tie he attaches.** Symbotic is the worked example. SoftBank took it public through its own SPAC in 2021, then in 2023 formed GreenBox (SoftBank 65%, Symbotic 35%), which committed to buy about $7.5B of Symbotic systems over six years. In return SoftBank received warrants for up to 2% of Symbotic at $41.9719 a share, vesting as GreenBox pays ([SoftBank Group](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20230724)). Parekh was the SoftBank voice on both steps and holds the board seat. The money came with a customer, and the parent got equity for being that customer. SambaNova shows the lighter version: in 2026 SoftBank Corp. was reported as the first customer for its SN50 chip ([Bloomberg Government headline](https://news.bgov.com/private-equity/intel-backed-sambanova-raises-cash-touts-softbank-chip-contract); body not read).\n\n**4. Deal flow and terms.** He enters late and at the lead: Series D at SambaNova and Labelbox, Series F at Helion, a SPAC at Symbotic, an outright purchase at Graphcore. No seed or Series A appears on any page that lists him. Structure is common: SPAC, joint venture, warrants, acquisition. Graphcore shows the downside for earlier holders: TechCrunch reported the price was undisclosed and that former employees' stock was wiped out ([TechCrunch, 2024-07-11](https://techcrunch.com/2024/07/11/softbank-acquires-uk-ai-chipmaker-graphcore)).\n\n**5. His AI book against SoftBank's own products.**\n\n| Company | Pool | How it relates to SoftBank |\n|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | SVF2 | Partner and customer: Stargate, SB OpenAI Japan (firm page) |\n| SambaNova | SVF2 | Supplier: SoftBank Corp. reported as first SN50 customer. Also a competitor to Graphcore and Ampere, which the parent owns |\n| Graphcore | Parent (subsidiary) | Owned outright since 2024-07 |\n| Arm | Parent (majority-owned); SVF1 stake divested | Core holding; he is credited with the 2016 buyout |\n| Labelbox | SVF2 | None found |\n| Agility Robotics | SVF2 | Overlaps the parent's robotics push (ABB robotics purchase, Skild); no contract found |\n| Symbotic | SVF2, SB Northstar and a parent vehicle | Supplier to the GreenBox/Exol joint venture, which SoftBank controls |\n| Helion | SVF2 | Power for AI data centers; Sam Altman is a major backer. No contract found |\n\n**6. For the owner.** A later-round price-setter and an acquirer. He is not a seed co-investor and not an LP. He is the person to know at SoftBank for chips, compute and robotics, for one reason: he is the route by which a company gets SoftBank as a customer or a buyer.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The fund-site team is the current owner, not the person who did the deal.** The site lists Parekh and Amit Lubovsky on SambaNova. The 2021 Series D release quotes someone else: \"Deep Nishar, Senior Managing Partner at SoftBank Investment Advisers\" ([SambaNova](https://sambanova.ai/press/series-d)). Nishar has since left. So part of Parekh's chip book is inherited, and \"listed on the page\" cannot be read as \"led the round\". The same caution applies to Labelbox, where the company post names no SoftBank person ([Labelbox](https://labelbox.com/blog/announcing-our-series-d/)).\n- **He holds a parent title that the fund site does not show.** \"He is a Managing Partner at SoftBank Group International\" ([Twenty One Capital 10-K, 2026-03-31](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070457/000121390026037460/ea0280065-10k_twenty.htm)). That explains why a fund partner is quoted on parent acquisitions. SoftBank Group International is the unit Rene Haas has led since 2026-04 (firm page).\n- **His remit, in a filing:** he \"leads investments and initiatives in AI, robotics, automation, and next-generation infrastructure technologies\" (same 10-K). The fund site still describes him as covering automation and enterprise software ([team page](https://visionfund.com/team/vikas-j-parekh)).\n- **His board seats are SoftBank's, on a string.** At Twenty One Capital he and Jared Roscoe each signed a letter of resignation \"which resignation would become effective immediately upon request by SoftBank\". SoftBank used it on 2026-05-19, the day Tether bought its whole stake ([8-K, 2026-05-20](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070457/000121390026059270/ea0291071-8k_twenty.htm)).\n- **At Symbotic he takes nothing personally.** \"Mr. Parekh has declined to receive any compensation for his services\", and the ownership table shows no shares for him ([proxy](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837240/000119312526015381/sym-20260116.htm)).\n- **On chips he ties the thesis to AGI:** \"Next generation semi-conductors and compute systems are essential in the AGI journey\" ([Graphcore release](https://www.graphcore.ai/posts/graphcore-joins-softbank-group-to-build-next-generation-of-ai-compute)).\n- **Stargate.** An AP photo shows him and Crusoe CEO Chase Lochmiller as they \"attend a press conference at the Stargate artificial intelligence data center campus\" in Abilene in 2025 ([North State Journal, AP caption](https://nsjonline.com/article/2026/04/microsoft-takes-over-texas-ai-data-center-expansion-as-openai-backs-away/microsoft-openai/)). This is the only public trace found of him on the OpenAI and Stargate work. No SoftBank release, and no press report read, names him on an OpenAI tranche.\n- **Skild AI and Graphcore are not on the fund site.** The site has no page for either, so no fund team is listed for them. The firm page's guess that he owns US robotics positions is still unconfirmed for Skild.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Against a $20M check into frontier-AI seeds he is two or more rounds late and writes ten to a thousand times the size. He matters in three ways:\n- **As the later price-setter** for chip, compute and robotics companies in my portfolio. Agility Robotics, SambaNova and Helion show the entry point: growth rounds with the business already built.\n- **As the acquirer's front man.** Graphcore is the precedent. A struggling AI-chip company was bought by the parent, and he was the quoted name. For a compute seed, he is a plausible exit conversation.\n- **As the route to SoftBank as a customer.** Symbotic's $7.5B order and SambaNova's SN50 contract are the pattern.\nHe is not a route to OpenAI allocation; that is Son's. He is not an LP prospect.\n\n## Background\n- Georgia Institute of Technology: BS in electrical engineering and MS in electrical and computer engineering. Harvard Business School MBA, George F. Baker Scholar ([WeWork release, 2022-12-02](https://www.stocktitan.net/news/WE/we-work-announces-new-soft-bank-appointment-vikas-j-parekh-to-board-i7ktor05pwat.html)). Dates not found.\n- Boston Consulting Group, then private equity at KKR. Dates not found (Symbotic proxy).\n- Joined SoftBank in March 2016 (Symbotic proxy). That is before Vision Fund 1's first close in 2017 and four months before SoftBank's offer for Arm.\n- Promoted to managing partner in November 2021 with Sumer Juneja, Lydia Jett and Dennis Chang (GCV, citing Bloomberg). The proxy loosely says he has been a managing partner \"since March 2016\"; GCV calls him a partner before the promotion. He was about 39.\n- Age 43 as of 2026-01-16 (Symbotic proxy).\n- Boards: Symbotic since June 2022 (proxy); WeWork from 2022-11-30 (release; end date June 2023 per an aggregator, unconfirmed); Twenty One Capital from December 2025 to 2026-05-19 (filings); AutoStore (named in the Twenty One 10-K; chairman per aggregators only).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [visionfund.com/team/vikas-j-parekh](https://visionfund.com/team/vikas-j-parekh) | Bio, a short Q&A and six of his portfolio companies. Checked 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No account confirmed |\n| LinkedIn | not confirmed | Crunchbase links one but the page would not load (403). Not guessed |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| Board listing | [ir.symbotic.com/board-member/vikas-j-parekh](https://ir.symbotic.com/board-member/vikas-j-parekh) | Returned 403 to a direct fetch; the proxy carries the same bio |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him.** No public channel works. The sourced paths are: (1) his own team, who appear next to him on the fund site: Karol Niewiadomski and Kiran Kazmi (OpenAI), Angela Du (Agility Robotics, Helion), Amit Lubovsky (SambaNova, Claroty) and Alex Fortmuller (Symbotic); (2) CEOs of companies where he is on the page or the board, such as Symbotic, SambaNova, Labelbox, Agility Robotics, Helion, Redis, Cohesity and Mapbox; (3) Crusoe's Chase Lochmiller, who shared the Abilene stage with him.\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025 (the Abilene media event was 2025-09-23) — Stargate press conference, Abilene, Texas — [AP photo caption](https://nsjonline.com/article/2026/04/microsoft-takes-over-texas-ai-data-center-expansion-as-openai-backs-away/microsoft-openai/) — pictured with Crusoe's CEO. No remarks found.\n- No podcast, conference talk or interview found.\n\n## How they think\n- **Iteration over vision.** \"We want to see a commitment to iteration.\" He says cheaper experimentation is speeding up innovation across sectors ([team page Q&A](https://visionfund.com/team/vikas-j-parekh), undated).\n- **Decisions as distributions.** He says \"all decisions have a distribution of outcomes\", so build in risk and move on (same Q&A).\n- **Software plus data** was his stated enterprise thesis: mapping, communications and manufacturing (same Q&A). The portfolio matches: Mapbox, Slack, Sendbird, OSIsoft.\n- **Compute as the AGI bottleneck** (Graphcore quote above).\n- **Calls.** Right: Symbotic (SoftBank still holds 45.4M shares and the joint venture). Wrong or painful: WeWork, where he joined the board in late 2022 before its 2023 bankruptcy; Graphcore needed a rescue rather than delivering a return. Cruise, Petuum and Peak are marked fully divested on the fund site.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Twenty One Capital 10-K bio](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070457/000121390026037460/ea0280065-10k_twenty.htm) — his current remit and the parent title.\n2. [SoftBank and Symbotic GreenBox release](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20230724) — the structure he fronts: joint venture, order book, warrants.\n3. [Graphcore joins SoftBank](https://www.graphcore.ai/posts/graphcore-joins-softbank-group-to-build-next-generation-of-ai-compute) — his one statement on chips and AGI.\n4. [Team page Q&A](https://visionfund.com/team/vikas-j-parekh) — how he says he judges founders.\n5. [GCV on the 2021 promotions](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sbia-scores-four-managing-partners/?format=md) — what he was promoted for.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No X account, no posts, no bylines beyond the firm-site Q&A and a Mapbox feature linked from his page.\n\n## Portfolio\n\"Page team\" means he is listed on the Vision Fund site's page for that company. It does not show that he led the round (see Differential insight).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | Growth; tranche not stated on the page. SVF2 has bought from the $157B round (2024) to the 2026 tranches | 2024-2026 | Page team (senior of three) | see ledger | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai) |\n| Graphcore | Strategic (acquisition by SoftBank Group; price undisclosed) | 2024-07-11 | Quoted for SoftBank | none | [Graphcore](https://www.graphcore.ai/posts/graphcore-joins-softbank-group-to-build-next-generation-of-ai-compute) |\n| SambaNova | Series D ($676M at $5.1B), SVF2 lead | 2021-04-13 | Page team; Deep Nishar quoted at entry | Temasek, GIC, BlackRock, Intel Capital, GV | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/sambanova), [release](https://sambanova.ai/press/series-d) |\n| Labelbox | Series D ($110M), SVF2 lead | 2022-01-06 | Page team (sole name) | Snowpoint, Databricks Ventures, B Capital, a16z | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/labelbox), [Labelbox](https://labelbox.com/blog/announcing-our-series-d/) |\n| Helion | Series F ($425M), SVF2 a new investor; lead not stated | 2025-01-28 | Page team with Angela Du | Lightspeed, Sam Altman, Mithril, Capricorn, Good Ventures, Nucor | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/helion), [Helion](https://www.helionenergy.com/blog/announcing-helions-425m-series-f) |\n| Agility Robotics | Growth ($400M reported, WP Global lead; unconfirmed) | 2025-04 (reported) | Page team with Angela Du | WP Global and others | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/agility-robotics), [Verdict](https://www.verdict.co.uk/agility-robotics-reportedly-nearing-400m-funding-round/) |\n| Symbotic | IPO (SPAC merger with SVF Investment Corp. 3, $5.5B) | 2021-12-12 announced; closed 2022-06 | Quoted; board since 2022-06 | Walmart | [Robotics & Automation News](https://roboticsandautomationnews.com/2021/12/13/supply-chain-automation-startup-symbotic-valued-at-5-5-billion-after-725-million-softbank-investment/47612), [proxy](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837240/000119312526015381/sym-20260116.htm) |\n| Symbotic (GreenBox, now Exol) | Strategic (joint venture, SoftBank 65%; $7.5B order; warrants) | 2023-07-24 | Quoted for SoftBank Group | none | [SoftBank Group](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20230724) |\n| Arm | Strategic (take-private by SoftBank Group, about $32B) | 2016-07 | Credited by GCV; role unconfirmed | none | [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sbia-scores-four-managing-partners/?format=md) |\n| Vianai, Petuum, Nauto, Cruise | AI positions; rounds not checked here | 2017-2021 | Page team | | fund pages: [Vianai](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/vianai-systems), [Petuum](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/petuum), [Nauto](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/nauto), [Cruise](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/cruise) |\n| Slack, Cohesity, OSIsoft, Zomato, WeWork, Mapbox, Redis, MinIO, Pantheon, Sendbird, 6sense, Attentive, Claroty, Clarify Health, Dewpoint, Esusu, Peak, Zipline | Non-AI or older positions; rounds not checked here | 2017-2022 | Page team | | [team page](https://visionfund.com/team/vikas-j-parekh) and each company page |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Symbotic, in two steps.** In December 2021 SoftBank's SPAC agreed to merge with Symbotic at $5.5B; Parekh was quoted that \"Symbotic is harnessing the power of AI\" ([Robotics & Automation News](https://roboticsandautomationnews.com/2021/12/13/supply-chain-automation-startup-symbotic-valued-at-5-5-billion-after-725-million-softbank-investment/47612)). He joined the board at closing in June 2022. In July 2023 the parent and Symbotic formed GreenBox with $100M of starting capital and the $7.5B order. In December 2025 GreenBox began trading as Exol (proxy). SoftBank's shares sit in three vehicles: Vision Fund 2's SPAC holding company (20.0M), SB Northstar (2.0M) and a parent vehicle (17.8M) per the proxy's footnote; the table total is 45.4M.\n- **Graphcore.** Bought by SoftBank Group in July 2024 after regulatory approvals were already in place. Parekh, with his fund title, gave the SoftBank quote. First contact and price are not public.\n- **OpenAI.** Nothing public on his part. The fund site lists three people; SoftBank's releases name only Son.\n- **SambaNova.** Entered under Deep Nishar in 2021. Parekh is the listed owner now, during the period in which SoftBank Corp. became a chip customer.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| SoftBank Investment Advisers | Managing Partner, Americas | 2016-03; MP 2021-11 | [proxy](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837240/000119312526015381/sym-20260116.htm), [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sbia-scores-four-managing-partners/?format=md) |\n| SoftBank Group (SoftBank Group International) | Managing Partner (parent-side title) | by 2026-03 | [10-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070457/000121390026037460/ea0280065-10k_twenty.htm) |\n| KKR; Boston Consulting Group | Former employers | before 2016 | proxy |\n| Karol Niewiadomski, Kiran Kazmi | His OpenAI team on the fund site | not stated | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai) |\n| Angela Du | Partner under him on Agility Robotics and Helion | not stated | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/agility-robotics) |\n| Amit Lubovsky | Director under him on SambaNova and Claroty | not stated | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/sambanova) |\n| Alex Fortmuller | Director under him on Symbotic | not stated | [fund page](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/symbotic) |\n| Deep Nishar | Predecessor on SambaNova | 2021 | [SambaNova](https://sambanova.ai/press/series-d) |\n| Rick Cohen (Symbotic chair and CEO) | Fellow director; counterparty on the SPAC and GreenBox | 2021 | proxy |\n| Jared Roscoe | Fellow SoftBank designee at Twenty One Capital | 2025-12 | [8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070457/000121390026059270/ea0291071-8k_twenty.htm) |\n| Chase Lochmiller (Crusoe) | Shared the Stargate Abilene press conference | 2025 | [AP caption](https://nsjonline.com/article/2026/04/microsoft-takes-over-texas-ai-data-center-expansion-as-openai-backs-away/microsoft-openai/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which OpenAI tranches he worked on, and whether he or Niewiadomski runs the position day to day. Nothing public.\n- When he took the SoftBank Group International title, and whether he reports to Rene Haas, Alex Clavel or both.\n- Whether he is on Skild AI, Ampere or the Stargate financing. None of the three is on the fund site, and no source names him.\n- His role in the 2016 Arm buyout. GCV's phrase is the only source.\n- Whether SoftBank led Helion's Series F, and which Agility Robotics round the fund joined.\n- A confirmed LinkedIn URL and location.\n- AutoStore: the chairman title and date come from aggregators only. The fund site lists Sumer Juneja on that position.\n\n## Sources\n- [Vision Fund team page](https://visionfund.com/team/vikas-j-parekh) and company pages for [OpenAI](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/openai), [SambaNova](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/sambanova), [Labelbox](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/labelbox), [Agility Robotics](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/agility-robotics), [Helion](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/helion), [Symbotic](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/symbotic), [Arm](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/arm), [AutoStore](https://visionfund.com/portfolio/autostore); [team list](https://visionfund.com/team)\n- [Symbotic definitive proxy, 2026-01-16](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1837240/000119312526015381/sym-20260116.htm)\n- [Twenty One Capital 10-K, 2026-03-31](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070457/000121390026037460/ea0280065-10k_twenty.htm); [8-K, 2026-05-20](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070457/000121390026059270/ea0291071-8k_twenty.htm)\n- [SoftBank Group, GreenBox release, 2023-07-24](https://group.softbank/en/news/press/20230724)\n- [Graphcore release, 2024-07-11](https://www.graphcore.ai/posts/graphcore-joins-softbank-group-to-build-next-generation-of-ai-compute); [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/07/11/softbank-acquires-uk-ai-chipmaker-graphcore)\n- [SambaNova Series D release](https://sambanova.ai/press/series-d); [Bloomberg Government headline on the SoftBank chip contract](https://news.bgov.com/private-equity/intel-backed-sambanova-raises-cash-touts-softbank-chip-contract)\n- [Labelbox Series D](https://labelbox.com/blog/announcing-our-series-d/); [Helion Series F](https://www.helionenergy.com/blog/announcing-helions-425m-series-f); [SiliconANGLE on Helion](https://siliconangle.com/2025/01/28/fusion-power-startup-helion-raises-425m-funding)\n- [Verdict on Agility Robotics, 2025-04-02](https://www.verdict.co.uk/agility-robotics-reportedly-nearing-400m-funding-round/)\n- [GCV, 2021-11-23](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/sbia-scores-four-managing-partners/?format=md)\n- [WeWork board release via Stock Titan, 2022-12-02](https://www.stocktitan.net/news/WE/we-work-announces-new-soft-bank-appointment-vikas-j-parekh-to-board-i7ktor05pwat.html)\n- [Robotics & Automation News, 2021-12-13](https://roboticsandautomationnews.com/2021/12/13/supply-chain-automation-startup-symbotic-valued-at-5-5-billion-after-725-million-softbank-investment/47612)\n- [North State Journal, AP photo caption](https://nsjonline.com/article/2026/04/microsoft-takes-over-texas-ai-data-center-expansion-as-openai-backs-away/microsoft-openai/)\n- Aggregators, labelled where used: MarketScreener (AutoStore chair, WeWork end date), Crunchbase (location).\n", "person:vin-sachidananda": "\n# Vin Sachidananda\n\n*Written 2026-11-10. Sources: Radical team page, the 2025-10-19 Radical interview, his 2026-07-06 essay \"The Future of AI Inference\", Etched's own posts, Primary's Series A post, and repo files `companies/etched.md`, `people/gavin-uberti.md` and `data/rows/roundmap-inception-rounds.csv` (not edited). Pronouns: Radical's interview uses \"he\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/vin-sachidananda-radicals-newest-partner-on-the-ai-opportunity/). The web search budget ran out at the start of this pass, so X, LinkedIn, podcasts and Google Scholar could not be checked. Everything below comes from direct page fetches.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the Radical partner who brings the inference-chip and compute thesis. He backed Etched at Two Sigma in 2024, then wrote Radical's 2026 Etched memo, which names its rivals and calls them far from shipping [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-future-of-ai-inference-2/).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** He is a technical lateral hire. He spent about a decade as an AI researcher (Stanford EE PhD and MS) and as an engineer at Google, Apple, Amazon and two acquired startups [src](https://radical.vc/team/vin-sachidananda/). That background lets him write hardware and architecture theses most VCs can't, and it gives Radical, a seed-heavy firm, a compute and growth-stage partner just as its growth and Breakouts money arrived.\n- **What constrains him:** He is new (since October 2025) and joined a partnership whose famous deals belong to Jacobs, Toews, Welch and Rosenberg. No Radical round is yet announced with him as lead or board member. His one named Radical deal, Etched, is a late entry at $5B or more that came through the firm's growth and Breakouts vehicles, not a first-in seed.\n- **What he'd never do (inference from his writing):** He would not bet against transformers on generality grounds. He thinks the only reason to leave them is efficiency [src](https://radical.vc/articles/vin-sachidananda-radicals-newest-partner-on-the-ai-opportunity/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He carried one deal across two firms.** Two Sigma was in Etched's $120M Series A (2024-06-25) [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware), and his team page credits him with Etched at Two Sigma [src](https://radical.vc/team/vin-sachidananda/). After he joined Radical, Radical was named an Etched backer on 2026-06-30 [src](https://thelogic.co/briefing/radical-ventures-gomez-hinton-back-etched-to-build-hardware-to-run-ai/). He then wrote the investment essay: \"Etched has charted the fastest path to gigawatt-scale production\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-future-of-ai-inference-2/). Etched is one of the first three Breakouts Fund positions [src](https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/). It is likely he brought the deal to Radical, but no source says so (inference).\n- **He names competitors in a firm memo.** \"MatX and Fractile remain a long way from delivering hardware to the market.\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-future-of-ai-inference-2/). VCs rarely put this in writing. It also tells MatX and Fractile founders that Radical is effectively conflicted out.\n- **Inference is a physical shortage:** \"The bottleneck is physical, with power, memory, and wafers constrained\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-future-of-ai-inference-2/).\n- **Diffusion LMs are his early-research bet:** \"An area that is still early but shows promise is discrete diffusion models.\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/vin-sachidananda-radicals-newest-partner-on-the-ai-opportunity/). This matches his Inception Labs deal at Two Sigma. Two Sigma is **not** in Inception's public $50M Menlo-led seed list (TechCrunch, 2025-11-06, in `data/rows/roundmap-inception-rounds.csv`), so his Inception entry was in an unannounced round.\n- **He is a transformer pragmatist:** \"a reason to move away from Transformers, it would be around efficiency\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/vin-sachidananda-radicals-newest-partner-on-the-ai-opportunity/). That view supports a transformer-only ASIC like Etched.\n- **He says it's not a bubble (Oct 2025):** \"I don't think we are in a bubble.\" His capex argument is that AI is \"an existential risk for many of these software businesses\" [src](https://radical.vc/articles/vin-sachidananda-radicals-newest-partner-on-the-ai-opportunity/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is a Radical partner on AI chips and compute and frontier architecture (diffusion LMs), and Radical can lead $10M+ seeds and $100M+ growth rounds. The deals that matter to you:\n- **Etched.** He saw it at the A, at Two Sigma, which helps for co-investing in compute names.\n- **Inception Labs.** A diffusion-LLM lab, close to \"new labs\".\n- **His inference map** (MatX and Fractile are where he sees the field).\n\nHe is a good counterpart for $20M checks into large compute or new-lab seeds where Radical co-leads. Caveat: he has no Radical lead deal on record yet, so his pull inside Radical is unproven.\n\n## Background\n- BS, Mechanical Engineering, University of Maryland. MS and PhD, Electrical Engineering, Stanford [src](https://radical.vc/team/vin-sachidananda/). Radical says he \"worked on the foundational AI models\" there [src](https://radical.vc/articles/vin-sachidananda-radicals-newest-partner-on-the-ai-opportunity/). He has published at NeurIPS and ICLR (same interview). No years are given.\n- He worked in applied research at Google, Apple and Amazon and was a founding engineer at two startups that were later acquired. No dates or names are given (same sources).\n- Partner at Two Sigma Ventures, where he led AI and deeptech investing. Dates not found [src](https://radical.vc/team/vin-sachidananda/). His Two Sigma Ventures bio URL now redirects to a Two Sigma 404.\n- He joined Radical as a Partner, announced 2025-10-19 [src](https://radical.vc/articles/vin-sachidananda-radicals-newest-partner-on-the-ai-opportunity/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://radical.vc/team/vin-sachidananda/ | Team page (checked 2026-11-10). No personal site found |\n| Substack / blog | https://radical.vc/articles/the-future-of-ai-inference-2/ | Radical essays: one byline found (2026-07-06) |\n| X | not verified | Team page links only to @radicalvcfund. Search budget was exhausted |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | Team page links only to Radical's company page |\n| GitHub | not found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-10 — Radical Editorial Q&A (written, not audio) — \"Vin Sachidananda: Radical's Newest Partner on the AI Opportunity\" — [link](https://radical.vc/articles/vin-sachidananda-radicals-newest-partner-on-the-ai-opportunity/). He says it's not a bubble, transformers stay, and he is watching diffusion LMs and drug-discovery foundation models.\n- No podcasts or talks were found. Search was not possible this pass.\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** Inference is a market limited by physical supply. A memory wall caps decode speed, and purpose-built chips win [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-future-of-ai-inference-2/). He also likes foundation models for drug discovery, citing AlphaFold [src](https://radical.vc/articles/vin-sachidananda-radicals-newest-partner-on-the-ai-opportunity/).\n- **Contrarian views:** not a bubble (Oct 2025); transformers are fine for generality; diffusion LMs are underrated.\n- **Calls:** Etched went from a $120M A (2024) to $5B (B) to $21B (Jane Street-led $700M, 2026-08-18) [src](https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one). The A-era call was right. Radical's 2026 entry price is unknown.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [The Future of AI Inference](https://radical.vc/articles/the-future-of-ai-inference-2/) (2026-07-06): his compute thesis and the Etched memo, with rivals named.\n2. [Radical's Newest Partner on the AI Opportunity](https://radical.vc/articles/vin-sachidananda-radicals-newest-partner-on-the-ai-opportunity/) (2025-10-19): his views on architecture, the bubble, diffusion and biology.\n3. [Team page](https://radical.vc/team/vin-sachidananda/): his Two Sigma portfolio.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No personal X or LinkedIn could be verified this pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Etched (at Radical) | Growth (Radical entry; round not stated; listed 2026-06-30 next to the $500M Stripes-led B at $5B) | 2026 | no | Stripes, Jane Street, Two Sigma, Thiel, VentureTech Alliance | [Logic](https://thelogic.co/briefing/radical-ventures-gomez-hinton-back-etched-to-build-hardware-to-run-ai/), [essay](https://radical.vc/articles/the-future-of-ai-inference-2/) |\n| Etched (at Two Sigma) | Series A ($120M; Primary and Positive Sum co-led) | 2024-06-25 | no | Primary, Positive Sum, Hummingbird, Thiel | [Primary](https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware); round mapping is inference |\n| Inception Labs (at Two Sigma) | Undisclosed (not the public $50M seed) | not found | unknown | | [team page](https://radical.vc/team/vin-sachidananda/) |\n| Zeromatter (at Two Sigma) | Undisclosed | not found | unknown | | same |\n| SDF (at Two Sigma; acquired by dbt) | Undisclosed | not found | unknown | | same |\n| Iambic Therapeutics (at Two Sigma) | Undisclosed | not found | unknown | | same |\n| Cerby (at Two Sigma) | Undisclosed | not found | unknown | | same |\n| Distributional (at Two Sigma) | Undisclosed | not found | unknown | | same |\n| Objective (at Two Sigma; acquired by Upwork) | Undisclosed | not found | unknown | | same |\n\nNo board seats were found. No Radical round names him as lead.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Etched: timing.** Primary seeded it (2023-03). The A came on 2024-06-25, with Two Sigma in it, during his Two Sigma tenure. He left Two Sigma for Radical around October 2025. Two Sigma stayed in the December 2025 B without him (`companies/etched.md`). Radical surfaced as a backer on 2026-06-30, he published the memo on 2026-07-06, and Jane Street led $700M at $21B on 2026-08-18. So he was early (Series A) at his old firm, while Radical came in late. It is the same person doubling down through a new vehicle (Breakouts) rather than Radical being first in.\n- How he sourced Etched at Two Sigma is not documented. Two Sigma is a quant firm, and Uberti has said HFT people were the first to take Etched seriously (`people/gavin-uberti.md`). That fits, but it is unconfirmed.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Radical Ventures | Partner | 2025-10-19 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/vin-sachidananda-radicals-newest-partner-on-the-ai-opportunity/) |\n| Two Sigma Ventures | former Partner, AI/deeptech lead | dates not found | [src](https://radical.vc/team/vin-sachidananda/) |\n| Etched (Gavin Uberti, Chris Zhu, Robert Wachen) | investor at two firms | 2024 | [src](https://radical.vc/articles/the-future-of-ai-inference-2/) |\n| Inception Labs | investor (at Two Sigma) | not found | [src](https://radical.vc/team/vin-sachidananda/) |\n| Stanford EE | PhD, MS | not found | same |\n\n## Open questions\n- What was his Radical start date, and who at Radical recruited him (Katz's compute practice, or growth)?\n- Did he source Radical's Etched entry? Which round, and at what price?\n- Which Inception round did Two Sigma join (pre-seed?), and who led it?\n- What is Zeromatter's business? Did he take board seats at Two Sigma?\n- His X and LinkedIn handles, his PhD advisor and thesis topic, and his Two Sigma start date.\n\n## Sources\n- https://radical.vc/team/vin-sachidananda/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/vin-sachidananda-radicals-newest-partner-on-the-ai-opportunity/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/the-future-of-ai-inference-2/\n- https://radical.vc/articles/introducing-radical-breakouts-fund/\n- https://radical.vc/portfolio/etched/\n- https://thelogic.co/briefing/radical-ventures-gomez-hinton-back-etched-to-build-hardware-to-run-ai/\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/etcheds-series-a-to-revolutionize-ai-hardware\n- https://www.etched.com/progress/from-zero-to-one\n- Repo: companies/etched.md, people/gavin-uberti.md, data/rows/roundmap-inception-rounds.csv\n\n## Update 2026-11-10 (people pass)\n\n*Added 2026-11-10. New sources: the archived TSV profile ([2025-07-08 capture](https://web.archive.org/web/20250708231929/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/profile/vin-sachidananda/)) and TSV's two co-bylined posts, read via curl of dated Wayback captures. Web search was exhausted for this run, so this pass did not cover X, LinkedIn, Scholar, podcasts or Form D. Frontmatter is corrected: location, promotion_path, fast_promotion (now `yes`) and known_for. The rest of the page above is from the earlier pass.*\n\n**Corrections to the page above**\n- **Tenure.** \"Vin joined Two Sigma Ventures in 2023\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250708231929/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/profile/vin-sachidananda/). Archived rosters show Investor (2023-12, 2024-06), then Principal (2024-11, 2025-02), then Partner (2025-06) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250609222741/https://twosigmaventures.com/our-team/). He went from Investor to Partner in about two years, then left for Radical in 2025-10. Seniority now `partner`; fast_promotion `yes`.\n- **His TSV mandate was generalist.** The TSV bio describes \"early-stage investments across a variety of verticals\": AI, B2B SaaS, infrastructure and developer tools. It does not mention chips, compute or deeptech. \"Led AI and Deeptech investing\" appears only on the later Radical page [src](https://radical.vc/team/vin-sachidananda/). The compute/inference identity is Radical-era.\n- **Where he is.** \"Vin splits his time between New York and San Francisco\" (TSV bio, 2025).\n- **Who he worked for before VC.** He was a founding engineer at Celect (MIT spin-out, acquired by Nike) and Clockwork Systems (Stanford spin-out). He built AI products at Google, Apple and Amazon, was a cloud-computing PM at NASA, and has taught ML and entrepreneurship (TSV bio). The bio describes his Stanford research as NLP.\n\n**Deals he is personally named on (a byline or his own page), stage and timing**\n| Company | Round | Date | How he is named | Confirmed? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Objective (AI enterprise search; later acquired by Upwork) | Undisclosed | 2023-10-18 | Co-byline with Frances Schwiep on TSV's announcement [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20251117213227/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/objective-inc-a-new-era-of-outcome-oriented-enterprise-search/) | yes, named |\n| Distributional (AI testing; now Talaria Scientific) | Seed ($11M, a16z lead) | 2023-12-14 | Company post names Two Sigma as a firm only [src](https://distributional.com/blog/we-raised-11m-for-better-ai-testing); the deal is on his Radical page | his role on this round is **unconfirmed** |\n| Distributional | Series A ($19M) | 2024-10-08 | Co-byline with Schwiep on TSV's post [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20251117221333/https://twosigmaventures.com/blog/article/distributional-empowering-trustworthy-ai-in-the-enterprise/) | named. The post does not say TSV led; that claim comes only from press (**unconfirmed**). This was a re-up after the seed |\n| Etched | Series A ($120M) at TSV, then Radical's 2026 entry | 2024-06-25 / 2026 | Listed under \"Select Investments\" on his TSV page and on his Radical page; he wrote Radical's 2026 essay | at TSV, **unconfirmed** at round level. No TSV Etched post exists |\n| Zeromatter (physics-based simulation for autonomy and sensing, [site](https://www.zeromatter.com/)) | Undisclosed | not found | TSV \"Select Investments\", Radical page | **unconfirmed** |\n| Inception Labs, Iambic, SDF, Cerby | Undisclosed | not found | Radical page only | **unconfirmed** |\n\n**What he personally sources, signs or prices.** On both named deals he is the second byline after Frances Schwiep, the senior applied-AI partner. The Distributional post says TSV \"wrote a thesis in 2021\" on AI testing, two years before he joined. So the thesis was the firm's, and probably Schwiep's (inference). There is no evidence he priced or led a round himself at TSV. He held no board seat that I could find. At Radical, Etched is the only deal tied to him, through his essay. Partner pages at Deviation, the TSV spin-out, now show Etched as \"managed by\" Colin Beirne. That reflects who holds the position now, not who originated it (see `firms/two-sigma-ventures.md`).\n\n**Whose money and who approves.** At TSV he invested Two Sigma Ventures funds, which are venture LPs whose GP is affiliated with Two Sigma. At Radical, the early funds and the Growth/Breakouts vehicles have outside LPs, and Jacobs signs the filings (`firms/radical-ventures.md`). No source says what approval he holds personally at either firm.\n\n**How to reach him.** He is in New York and San Francisco. He has no verified personal X or LinkedIn (search was not possible this pass). The best route is a warm intro through Etched (Uberti), Inception or Distributional/Talaria (Scott Clark), or a reply to his Radical inference essay. Pitches that fit his stated interests are inference hardware, diffusion LMs and foundation models for drug discovery.\n\n**Fit (updated).** Still **high** for compute and new-architecture deals. But the TSV record shows he has been a partner for only about five months at TSV and about a year at Radical. He also has no confirmed lead or board seat. Treat him as a strong technical sponsor whose internal pull at Radical is unproven, not as a deal-maker who can underwrite a $20M check on his own.\n\n**Open (added):** Who led Distributional's Series A? Did he take any TSV board or observer seat? What do Clockwork Systems and Celect actually do, and when was he there? Who is his PhD advisor?\n", "person:vince-hankes": "\n# Vince Hankes\n\n## One-line take\nThe Thrive partner who led the firm's OpenAI investment ([src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/vince-hankes)). Worth following because he is the clearest public voice on Thrive's playbook: qualitative conviction first, then very large concentrated checks ([src](https://podcast.app/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital-e427758797), [src](https://www.startuphub.ai/news/betting-on-bold-thrive-capitals-vince-hankes-on-ai-and-venture-investing)).\n\n## Crux\nHe wins by being the finance-minded half of a small, concentrated growth team that underwrites a handful of \"generational\" founder-led companies and then keeps buying, often when it looks expensive or broken (OpenAI at ~$29B, Stripe ~$2B, Carvana after a 90%+ fall) ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital)). His stated model: the ideal Thrive growth fund is about 10 companies, because ~75 companies reached $100B+ in the last decade and most enterprise value is created in a company's second and third decades ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital)). The constraint is the flip side: being \"all in\" on few names implies a commitment to them, which limits what else Thrive can back ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital)). He would not size a bet off precise TAM/moat analysis, and would not stay on the fence: big checks need \"almost dogmatic conviction\" ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital), [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/inside-scoop-how-and-why-thrive-capital-invested-in-openai)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Portfolio construction rule.** Thrive's target growth fund is roughly ten companies: \"an ideal growth fund for us is 10 companies\" (Uncapped #27, 10:36) ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital)).\n- **Why late-but-concentrated works.** He cites LP-meeting data that the $100B+ club is now big enough to pick from: \"75 companies in the last decade that have reached 100 billion\" (11:11) ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital)).\n- **Compounding thesis.** Most value accrues late: \"enterprise value that get created are in the second or third decade\" (13:04) ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital)).\n- **Conflicts as a constraint.** Concentration doubles as a promise to portfolio founders: \"we're not doing a lot, we implicitly are making kind of a commitment\" (33:12) ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital)).\n- **Carvana origin and sizing.** Thrive first looked at Carvana while he was at Tiger (per transcript summary); a close friend was the one spending time on it, and after the 90%+ fall \"we ended up doubling the number of shares\" (26:46, 29:32) ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital)).\n- **OpenAI timing: the demo was not exclusive.** On the investor demo (he says it included GPT-4): \"we were on that call like many other investors.\" Thrive's edge was acting on it, not access; Colossus says Thrive's was the only term sheet ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/vince-hankes-why-we-put-300m-into-openai-sam-altman-s-pitch-lessons-from-josh-ku), [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- **Robotics, and Physical Intelligence.** He frames robotics as an open timing question: \"are we in 2015 self driving or are we on the precipice\" and says \"We're investing in physical intelligence\" (50:28) ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital)).\n- **AI-native automation is founder-first.** On Rogo: \"Gabe, I think he's 12 out of 10.\" and \"Starts with a person.\" (48:24) ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital)).\n- **Canva miss, in his words.** At Tiger he flew to Sydney but pattern-matched to enterprise SaaS: \"we missed what was so special about the company\" (20VC E1009, captions) ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/vince-hankes-why-we-put-300m-into-openai-sam-altman-s-pitch-lessons-from-josh-ku)). He also admits an unnamed loss \"because of an externality that was outside of all control\" ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/vince-hankes-why-we-put-300m-into-openai-sam-altman-s-pitch-lessons-from-josh-ku)).\n- **Who he'd back as an LP.** Asked for one seed manager, he named Nat Friedman: \"I would be certainly interested in putting money in his fund\" (while saying he doesn't know him personally) ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/vince-hankes-why-we-put-300m-into-openai-sam-altman-s-pitch-lessons-from-josh-ku)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is a growth-stage partner, not a seed investor, and Thrive's checks run from ~$130M to ~$2B ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/), [src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital)), far above my ~$20M. But he sits on the deals that matter to me: OpenAI (led Thrive's 2023 entry), Isomorphic Labs (board; Thrive led $600M and $2.1B rounds), Physical Intelligence (Thrive investing, per him), and Rogo (financial AI, [src](https://financialit.net/node/65151)) and Console (board) ([src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/vince-hankes), [src](https://podcast.app/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital-e427758797), [src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital)). Useful as (a) a co-investor in very large lab seeds where Thrive anchors and others fill, and (b) a signal of which frontier companies Thrive will later concentrate into. Thrive raised a $10B fund (Thrive X) in Feb 2026 ([src](https://www.advisorperspectives.com/articles/2026/02/17/josh-kushners-thrive-capital-raises-10-billion)), so check sizes will likely stay large (inference).\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Mount Clemens, Michigan, outside Detroit, the son of two hairdressers. Learned business as a golf caddy ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- University of Michigan, finance / BBA at Ross School of Business ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/), [src](https://startupintros.com/people/vincent-hankes)). Graduation year: (unverified).\n- Goldman Sachs, before Tiger ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). An AI summary of his 20VC episode says he worked on large telecom/tech deals (AT&T, Verizon, Dell–EMC) for about 18 months, then got staffed on Flipkart, which pulled him toward private-company investing ([src](https://www.deciphr.ai/podcast/20vc-the-openai-memo-why-invest-is-it-too-late-to-catch-openai-are-openais-models-truly-defensible-does-the-value-in-ai-accrue-to-incumbemts-or-startups-application-layerinfrastructure-what-happens-with-regulation-with-vince-hankes--thrive)). This comes from a machine summary, so treat it as (unverified) until checked against the audio.\n- Tiger Global, investor, 2017–2019 ([src](https://startupintros.com/people/vincent-hankes)). The 20VC bio says he learned venture there from Lee Fixel ([src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/vince-hankes)).\n- Thrive Capital, 2019–present, now Partner ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). He joined just after Thrive raised a $1B fund ([src](https://www.startuphub.ai/news/betting-on-bold-thrive-capitals-vince-hankes-on-ai-and-venture-investing)).\n- Colossus casts him as the quantitative, finance-minded complement to Josh Kushner, and names him and Kelly Sims as Thrive's \"lead partners\" ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | No personal site found |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/vhankes | Handle from a third-party directory ([src](https://startupintros.com/people/vincent-hankes)). Follower count and cadence not checked (X not fetchable) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/vincent-hankes/ | Per third-party directory ([src](https://startupintros.com/people/vincent-hankes)) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-05 — 20VC — \"The OpenAI Memo: Why Invest?\" — https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-the-openai-memo-why-invest-is-d7b (published 2023-05-03) — The OpenAI investment story, defending the ~$29B price, whether models are defensible, and value capture by incumbents vs. startups. He also gives his biggest miss: Canva at Tiger ([src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-the-openai-memo-why-invest-is-d7b), [src](https://www.deciphr.ai/podcast/20vc-the-openai-memo-why-invest-is-it-too-late-to-catch-openai-are-openais-models-truly-defensible-does-the-value-in-ai-accrue-to-incumbemts-or-startups-application-layerinfrastructure-what-happens-with-regulation-with-vince-hankes--thrive)). A video version is titled \"Why We Put $300M into OpenAI; Sam Altman's Pitch; Lessons from Josh Kushner | E1009\" ([src](https://www.letsplayindex.com/video/vince-hankes-why-we-put-300m-into-openai-sam-altmans-pitch-6E47mhq-YS4)).\n- 2023 — Slush (Helsinki) — \"Building with OpenAI: ChatGPT, agents and the path towards AGI,\" on stage with OpenAI's Romain Huet. Covered GPT-4 Turbo, agents, and open vs. closed source ([src](https://wudpecker.io/blog/building-with-openai-chatgpt-agents-and-the-path-towards-agi-slush-2023-summary)).\n- 2025-10 — Uncapped with Jack Altman #27 — https://podcast.app/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital-e427758797 (released 2025-10-08, about 57 min) — Chapters include \"Qualitative, then quantitative,\" \"Writing massive checks,\" \"Buying Carvana at the bottom,\" \"Managing conflicts,\" \"Scale and timing of robotics,\" and \"OpenAI vs everything else\" ([src](https://podcast.app/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital-e427758797)). Business Insider picked up his line that every Big Tech company has \"a bazooka pointed at\" OpenAI ([src](https://www.aol.com/articles/thrive-capital-partner-says-every-063930431.html)). Full machine transcript at [src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital). Added 2026-11-09 from the transcript: Thrive put \"almost $20 million\" into Instagram (04:50); he calls life sciences the thing he is most bullish on that the market hasn't acknowledged (38:12); he cites Druckenmiller on Nvidia capturing AI profits (41:06) and Bill Gurley (43:01); he names Cursor and Physical Intelligence as investments and Rogo's Gabe as a standout founder.\n\n## How they think\n- **Size the outcome by analogy, not by TAM.** On OpenAI he said investors fixated on precise TAM and moats would find \"50 reasons you can say no.\" Thrive compared the technology instead to the scaling of the iPhone, AWS, and Google, and asked whether the world would be unimaginable without it ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/inside-scoop-how-and-why-thrive-capital-invested-in-openai)).\n- **Admit the price is high, and underwrite to the upper tail.** He conceded \"the price here was high\" at ~$29B. The bet only works with \"upper echelon\" outcomes, and the logic was that once success became obvious, OpenAI would be \"priced up very quickly\" ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/why-we-invested-300m-in-openai-vince-hankes-of-thrive-capital)).\n- **The moat is the ecosystem, not the model.** In 2023 he argued OpenAI's defensibility comes from its user and developer ecosystem (plugins, user growth) rather than model quality alone. He said value will accrue \"mostly to the application layer.\" He framed the incumbent-vs-startup question as whether incumbents acquire innovation before startups acquire distribution ([src](https://www.deciphr.ai/podcast/20vc-the-openai-memo-why-invest-is-it-too-late-to-catch-openai-are-openais-models-truly-defensible-does-the-value-in-ai-accrue-to-incumbemts-or-startups-application-layerinfrastructure-what-happens-with-regulation-with-vince-hankes--thrive)) (AI summary, wording unverified).\n- **OpenAI vs. Big Tech.** His 2025 mental model: OpenAI is cornered, and every Big Tech company wants to stop a new Big Tech company from emerging. He still argues OpenAI is the best risk-adjusted return, so Thrive should invest more when it gets the chance ([src](https://www.aol.com/articles/thrive-capital-partner-says-every-063930431.html)).\n- **Qualitative first, then quantitative; concentration over coverage.** Thrive spent about 18 months getting to know Isomorphic before investing. He says billion-dollar checks need \"almost dogmatic conviction\" (Carvana). He finds it easier to back a company already on a path to $100–200B than to pick a breakout from thousands of startups. Concentration implies a commitment to the companies backed ([src](https://www.startuphub.ai/news/betting-on-bold-thrive-capitals-vince-hankes-on-ai-and-venture-investing)).\n- **Team decisions.** He calls Thrive \"a very team oriented firm\" that will \"disagree and commit,\" and says no one person decides ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/why-we-invested-300m-in-openai-vince-hankes-of-thrive-capital)).\n- **Independence of thought.** On Stripe 2023: \"We're never out there talking our own book.\" He links the Stripe deal's contrarian nerve to what Thrive did with OpenAI later that year ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- **Regulation.** In 2023 he expected AI regulation to come and to be necessary, and he wanted builders involved in writing it ([src](https://www.deciphr.ai/podcast/20vc-the-openai-memo-why-invest-is-it-too-late-to-catch-openai-are-openais-models-truly-defensible-does-the-value-in-ai-accrue-to-incumbemts-or-startups-application-layerinfrastructure-what-happens-with-regulation-with-vince-hankes--thrive)) (AI summary).\n- **Calls right / wrong.**\n  - Right: OpenAI at ~$29B (2023), which reached $300B by March 2025 ([src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/03/31/openai-bags-40b-funding-increasing-post-money-valuation-300b)) and $500B by October 2025 ([src](https://www.thestar.com.my/business/business-news/2025/10/02/openai-hits-us500bil-valuation-after-share-sale-to-softbank-others-source-says)).\n  - Wrong (self-described): at Tiger he passed on Canva after meeting the team in Sydney, because he forced it into a SaaS framework when it should have been valued like a consumer subscription business ([src](https://www.deciphr.ai/podcast/20vc-the-openai-memo-why-invest-is-it-too-late-to-catch-openai-are-openais-models-truly-defensible-does-the-value-in-ai-accrue-to-incumbemts-or-startups-application-layerinfrastructure-what-happens-with-regulation-with-vince-hankes--thrive)) (AI summary).\n\n## Read these first\n- 20VC, \"The OpenAI Memo\" (May 2023): the deal rationale in his own words ([src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-the-openai-memo-why-invest-is-d7b)).\n- Uncapped #27 with Jack Altman (Oct 2025): the Thrive playbook on concentration, conflicts, Carvana, and robotics ([src](https://podcast.app/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital-e427758797)).\n- Colossus, \"The New World\" by Jeremy Stern (Oct 2025): the most detailed account of Thrive's OpenAI history, plus Hankes on Stripe ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- Business Insider write-up of the \"bazooka\" comment (Oct 2025) ([src](https://www.aol.com/articles/thrive-capital-partner-says-every-063930431.html)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He is publicly visible mostly through podcasts and press, not posting. His X handle is @vhankes ([src](https://startupintros.com/people/vincent-hankes)), but activity and follower count could not be checked.\n\n## Portfolio\n\"Led?\" means Thrive led or co-led the round. Hankes is personally credited as lead only on OpenAI, Melio, and Airplane.dev ([src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/vince-hankes)), and with Kushner on Stripe ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | ~$300M secondary at $27–29B (Thrive ~$130M) | 2023-04 (term sheet spring 2022 per Colossus) | Yes (Thrive per Fortune) | Sequoia, a16z, K2 Global, Founders Fund | [src](https://lifeboat.com/blog/2023/04/openai-closes-300m-share-sale-at-27b-29b-valuation), [src](https://fortune.com/longform/josh-kushner-net-worth-thrive-capital-investments/) |\n| OpenAI | Employee tender at $86B | Agreed 2023-10, closed 2024-02 | Yes | — | [src](https://www.cnbc.com/amp/2023/11/30/openai-tender-offer-on-track-despite-leadership-fracas-sources.html), [src](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/esg/openai-deal-lets-employees-sell-shares-at-86-billion-valuation) |\n| OpenAI | $6.6B at $157B (Thrive ~$1.25B per WSJ) | 2024-10 | Yes | Microsoft, Nvidia, SoftBank, Khosla, Altimeter, Fidelity, MGX, Tiger Global | [src](https://fortune.com/2024/10/02/openai-officially-raises-6-6-billion-funding-deal-157-billion-valuation-sam-altman-thrive-capital/) |\n| OpenAI | $40B at $300B | 2025-03 | No (SoftBank led) | SoftBank, Microsoft, Coatue, Altimeter | [src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/03/31/openai-bags-40b-funding-increasing-post-money-valuation-300b) |\n| OpenAI | ~$6.6B employee secondary at $500B | 2025-10 | Unknown | SoftBank, Dragoneer, MGX, T. Rowe Price | [src](https://www.thestar.com.my/business/business-news/2025/10/02/openai-hits-us500bil-valuation-after-share-sale-to-softbank-others-source-says) |\n| Stripe | ~$7B round (per Colossus) | 2023 | Yes (anchored) | — | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Melio | Series D, $250M at $4B | 2021-09 | Co-led with General Catalyst | Tiger Global, Accel, Bessemer, Coatue, Corner, Latitude | [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/melio-raises-250m-to-fuel-expansion-of-its-b2b-payments-platform-and-forge-new-partnerships-tripling-valuation-to-4b-301376128.html) |\n| Airplane | Series B, $32M | 2022-09 | Yes | Benchmark, Calvin French-Owen, Neha Narkhede, Nat Friedman | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2391512) |\n| Airtable | Series D, $185M at ~$2.585B | 2020-09 | Yes | Benchmark, Coatue, Caffeinated, CRV, D1 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/09/14/airtable-raises-185m-and-launches-new-low-code-and-automation-features/embed/) |\n| Lattice | Series F, ~$3B valuation | 2022-01 | Co-led | Elad Gil, Tiger Global, Dragoneer | [src](https://www.cooley.com/news/coverage/2022/2022-01-31-lattice-triples-valuation-to-3-billion-with-series-f-round) |\n| Benchling | Series D, $50M | 2020-05 | No (Alkeon led) | Spark, Lux, ICONIQ, Benchmark, Menlo | [src](https://www.benchling.com/news/benchling-raises-50m-series-d-to-accelerate-biotech-revolution) |\n| Isomorphic Labs | First external round, $600M | 2025-03 | Yes | GV, Alphabet | [src](https://isomorphiclabs.com/articles/isomorphic-labs-announces-600m-external-investment-round) |\n| Isomorphic Labs | Series B, $2.1B | 2026-05 | Yes | Alphabet, GV, MGX, Temasek, CapitalG, UK Sovereign AI Fund | [src](https://www.gunder.com/en/news-insights/client-news/thrive-capital-leads-isomorphic-labs-dollar21-billion-series-b) |\n\nBoard seats:\n- 2023 bio: Airtable, Benchling, Lattice, Melio ([src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/vince-hankes)).\n- Oct 2025 bio: Airtable, Benchling, Console, Isomorphic, Lattice, Rogo ([src](https://podcast.app/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital-e427758797)). Melio has dropped off this list.\n- The Uncapped bio also says he worked on Thrive's SpaceX, Databricks, and Stripe investments ([src](https://podcast.app/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital-e427758797)).\n- Lattice and Benchling were Thrive companies before he joined (Thrive led Lattice's 2017 Series A ([src](https://globalventuring.com/blog/2017/08/02/lattice-lets-in-series-a-cash/)) and Benchling's 2016 $7M round ([src](https://www.benchling.com/news/benchling-announces-7m-in-new-funding-led-by-thrive-capital))). So those board seats are likely inherited or follow-on coverage, not original sourcing (inference).\n\n## Deal stories\n**OpenAI: how Thrive got in**\n- **Early 2022.** Thrive met Sam Altman about a new round and got a preview of GPT-3 ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). Fortune dates a Kushner–Altman meeting to March 2022, at a closed-door event in Arizona ([src](https://fortune.com/longform/josh-kushner-net-worth-thrive-capital-investments/)).\n- **What Hankes noticed first.** In May 2023 he said Thrive started focusing on OpenAI \"maybe 18 months ago.\" The trigger was seeing many startups ship marketing and blog-writing tools that were \"thin user experiences\" on top of OpenAI's model ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/inside-scoop-how-and-why-thrive-capital-invested-in-openai)).\n- **The demo call.** OpenAI ran semi-closed demos for investors. After one call, Hankes and Kushner looked at each other, convinced the technology would change how companies work. Thrive then dug into financials, products, and customers ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/inside-scoop-how-and-why-thrive-capital-invested-in-openai)). Note: Hankes (via an AI summary) mentions GPT-4, while Colossus says GPT-3. The demo may have shown both, or one account is off.\n- **Term sheet.** Colossus says Thrive submitted a term sheet in spring 2022: $130M from its main fund at a $29B valuation. It was the only term sheet OpenAI received for that round ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). Fortune instead describes Thrive leading a ~$130M round at $29B in early 2023, when OpenAI's revenue run-rate was about $50M ([src](https://fortune.com/longform/josh-kushner-net-worth-thrive-capital-investments/)).\n- **Closing.** Public reporting places the close in April 2023, as a ~$300M share sale at $27–29B that also included Sequoia, a16z, K2 Global, and Founders Fund ([src](https://lifeboat.com/blog/2023/04/openai-closes-300m-share-sale-at-27b-29b-valuation)). Microsoft's separate investment closed in January 2023 ([src](https://lifeboat.com/blog/2023/04/openai-closes-300m-share-sale-at-27b-29b-valuation)).\n- **Timing.** So Thrive committed before ChatGPT launched (Nov 2022) and was alone at the term-sheet stage. The other VCs arrived in the same 2023 closing ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). The Colossus and Fortune dates aren't fully reconciled; a likely reading is a 2022 term sheet that closed in 2023 (inference).\n\n**Doubling down (2023)**\n- **August 2023:** Thrive agreed to anchor $400M of a $500M employee tender ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- **October 2023:** Thrive agreed to lead a tender at an $86B valuation ([src](https://fortune.com/longform/josh-kushner-net-worth-thrive-capital-investments/)).\n- **By Nov 17, 2023:** Thrive had committed around $700M to OpenAI ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n\n**November 2023 board crisis**\n- **Nov 17, 2023:** The board fired Altman and removed Greg Brockman as chair ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- **Within the hour:** COO Brad Lightcap called Kushner, and the two talked around the clock for five days ([src](https://fortune.com/longform/josh-kushner-net-worth-thrive-capital-investments/)).\n- **Thrive's partners:** They ran a continuous video call for five days. Colossus says the lead partners (Hankes and Kelly Sims) phoned Kushner when the news broke ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- **Leverage:** Colossus, relying mostly on open sources, says Thrive concluded the board's case was baseless. It reports that Thrive pressed the board via Microsoft ties, other investors, the press, and preparation of lawsuits. Its leverage was the pending tender ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- **Resolution:** Altman was reinstated on Nov 22 ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)). The tender went ahead, pushed to January ([src](https://www.cnbc.com/amp/2023/11/30/openai-tender-offer-on-track-despite-leadership-fracas-sources.html)), and completed in February 2024 ([src](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/esg/openai-deal-lets-employees-sell-shares-at-86-billion-valuation)).\n- **Hankes's personal role:** Beyond being on that call, no source details it.\n\n**Leading the 2024 round and after**\n- **October 2024:** Thrive confirmed it led the $6.6B round at $157B ([src](https://fortune.com/2024/10/02/openai-officially-raises-6-6-billion-funding-deal-157-billion-valuation-sam-altman-thrive-capital/)). It reportedly also got an option to invest another $1B ([src](https://www.neowin.net/news/openai-is-now-valued-at-157-billion-as-it-finishes-its-66-billion-funding-round/)). Colossus describes this as $1B at $150B, with an exclusive right to $1B more through January 2026 ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- **2025:** Thrive took part in SoftBank's $40B round in March ([src](https://siliconangle.com/2025/03/31/openai-bags-40b-funding-increasing-post-money-valuation-300b)) and bought in the $500B secondary in October ([src](https://www.thestar.com.my/business/business-news/2025/10/02/openai-hits-us500bil-valuation-after-share-sale-to-softbank-others-source-says)).\n\n**Stripe (2023)**\n- Kushner flew to Paris to meet John Collison and brought Hankes ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- Thrive anchored the round and had to pitch other investors to fill it. Hankes calls it one of Thrive's \"formative investments\" and says Collison was \"founder mode before founder mode\" ([src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/)).\n- He dates Thrive's first Stripe investment to almost ten years before this ~$2B investment ([src](https://www.startuphub.ai/news/betting-on-bold-thrive-capitals-vince-hankes-on-ai-and-venture-investing)).\n\n**Isomorphic Labs**\n- Thrive spent about 18 months getting to know Isomorphic before investing ([src](https://www.startuphub.ai/news/betting-on-bold-thrive-capitals-vince-hankes-on-ai-and-venture-investing)).\n- It then led the $600M first external round in March 2025 ([src](https://isomorphiclabs.com/articles/isomorphic-labs-announces-600m-external-investment-round)) and the $2.1B Series B in May 2026 ([src](https://www.gunder.com/en/news-insights/client-news/thrive-capital-leads-isomorphic-labs-dollar21-billion-series-b)).\n- Hankes is on the board ([src](https://podcast.app/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital-e427758797)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Thrive Capital | Partner | 2019 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Tiger Global | Investor | 2017–2019 | [src](https://startupintros.com/people/vincent-hankes) |\n| Goldman Sachs | Prior role (title/dates unverified) | — | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Lee Fixel | Mentor at Tiger Global | 2017 | [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/vince-hankes) |\n| Josh Kushner | Colleague; together on OpenAI demo call and Stripe trip | 2019 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/), [src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/inside-scoop-how-and-why-thrive-capital-invested-in-openai) |\n| Kelly Sims | Fellow Thrive lead partner | — | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| John Collison (Stripe) | Met in Paris on 2023 round | 2023 | [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Romain Huet (OpenAI) | Shared Slush 2023 stage | 2023 | [src](https://wudpecker.io/blog/building-with-openai-chatgpt-agents-and-the-path-towards-agi-slush-2023-summary) |\n| Airtable, Benchling, Lattice | Board | by 2023 | [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/vince-hankes) |\n| Melio | Board (listed 2023, not 2025) | by 2023 | [src](https://thetwentyminutevc.com/vince-hankes) |\n| Console, Isomorphic Labs, Rogo | Board | by 2025-10 | [src](https://podcast.app/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital-e427758797) |\n| Harry Stebbings (20VC) | Podcast guest | 2023-05 | [src](https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-the-openai-memo-why-invest-is-d7b) |\n| Jack Altman (Uncapped) | Podcast guest | 2025-10 | [src](https://podcast.app/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital-e427758797) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Reconcile the OpenAI timeline: Colossus's spring 2022 term sheet vs. Fortune's early 2023 round vs. the April 2023 close. Was there one round or two?\n- Did Thrive's ~$130M go in as primary or secondary?\n- Which model did the demo call show: GPT-3 (Colossus) or GPT-4? Update 2026-11-09: the 20VC captions have him describing a closed demo that included GPT-4, attended by many investors ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/vince-hankes-why-we-put-300m-into-openai-sam-altman-s-pitch-lessons-from-josh-ku)). Colossus's GPT-3 may refer to an earlier preview.\n- How did Thrive get into Physical Intelligence, at what round, and who led it internally? He names \"Locky and Carol\" (likely Lachy Groom and Karol Hausman; unconfirmed) ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital)).\n- Which investment did he lose money on \"because of an externality\"? Not named ([src](https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/vince-hankes-why-we-put-300m-into-openai-sam-altman-s-pitch-lessons-from-josh-ku)).\n- Who is the \"close friend\" who worked the Carvana position? Not named in the transcript ([src](https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital)).\n- Console: an aggregator says he led its June 2025 seed ([src](https://startupintros.com/news/console-seed-2025-06-02)); another lists Thrive and DST leading the Sept 2025 Series A ([src](https://startupintros.com/news/console-series-a-2025-09)). Confirm from primary sources.\n- What did Hankes personally do during the Nov 2023 crisis, beyond being on Thrive's partner call?\n- Did Hankes lead the 2024 $157B round, or did Kushner?\n- Exact board start dates. Third-party directories hint at Lattice (Nov 2019), Benchling and Airtable (observer), Melio (observer, Oct 2021), and Airplane (Jan 2022), but these are unconfirmed.\n- Who led Thrive's Rogo and Console deals, and when.\n- Get full transcripts of 20VC E1009 and Uncapped #27, especially the Carvana, robotics, and \"OpenAI vs everything else\" sections, to replace the AI summaries.\n- Melio and Airplane exit outcomes: not researched here.\n\n## Sources\n- https://pod.wave.co/podcast/uncapped-with-jack-altman/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital (Uncapped #27 machine transcript, added 2026-11-09)\n- https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/vince-hankes-why-we-put-300m-into-openai-sam-altman-s-pitch-lessons-from-josh-ku (20VC E1009 captions, added 2026-11-09)\n- https://rosetta.to/person/vince-hankes\n- https://www.advisorperspectives.com/articles/2026/02/17/josh-kushners-thrive-capital-raises-10-billion\n- https://startupintros.com/news/console-seed-2025-06-02 (aggregator)\n- https://startupintros.com/news/console-series-a-2025-09 (aggregator)\n- https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-the-openai-memo-why-invest-is-d7b\n- https://thetwentyminutevc.com/vince-hankes\n- https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/inside-scoop-how-and-why-thrive-capital-invested-in-openai\n- https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/why-we-invested-300m-in-openai-vince-hankes-of-thrive-capital\n- https://www.deciphr.ai/podcast/20vc-the-openai-memo-why-invest-is-it-too-late-to-catch-openai-are-openais-models-truly-defensible-does-the-value-in-ai-accrue-to-incumbemts-or-startups-application-layerinfrastructure-what-happens-with-regulation-with-vince-hankes--thrive\n- https://www.letsplayindex.com/video/vince-hankes-why-we-put-300m-into-openai-sam-altmans-pitch-6E47mhq-YS4\n- https://podcast.app/uncapped-27-vince-hankes-from-thrive-capital-e427758797\n- https://podwise.ai/episodes/5393709\n- https://www.startuphub.ai/news/betting-on-bold-thrive-capitals-vince-hankes-on-ai-and-venture-investing\n- https://www.aol.com/articles/thrive-capital-partner-says-every-063930431.html\n- https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/\n- https://fortune.com/longform/josh-kushner-net-worth-thrive-capital-investments/\n- https://startupintros.com/people/vincent-hankes\n- https://wudpecker.io/blog/building-with-openai-chatgpt-agents-and-the-path-towards-agi-slush-2023-summary\n- https://lifeboat.com/blog/2023/04/openai-closes-300m-share-sale-at-27b-29b-valuation\n- https://www.cnbc.com/amp/2023/11/30/openai-tender-offer-on-track-despite-leadership-fracas-sources.html\n- https://news.bloomberglaw.com/esg/openai-deal-lets-employees-sell-shares-at-86-billion-valuation\n- https://fortune.com/2024/10/02/openai-officially-raises-6-6-billion-funding-deal-157-billion-valuation-sam-altman-thrive-capital/\n- https://www.neowin.net/news/openai-is-now-valued-at-157-billion-as-it-finishes-its-66-billion-funding-round/\n- https://siliconangle.com/2025/03/31/openai-bags-40b-funding-increasing-post-money-valuation-300b\n- https://www.thestar.com.my/business/business-news/2025/10/02/openai-hits-us500bil-valuation-after-share-sale-to-softbank-others-source-says\n- https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/melio-raises-250m-to-fuel-expansion-of-its-b2b-payments-platform-and-forge-new-partnerships-tripling-valuation-to-4b-301376128.html\n- https://techcrunch.com/?p=2391512\n- https://techcrunch.com/2020/09/14/airtable-raises-185m-and-launches-new-low-code-and-automation-features/embed/\n- https://www.cooley.com/news/coverage/2022/2022-01-31-lattice-triples-valuation-to-3-billion-with-series-f-round\n- https://globalventuring.com/blog/2017/08/02/lattice-lets-in-series-a-cash/\n- https://www.benchling.com/news/benchling-raises-50m-series-d-to-accelerate-biotech-revolution\n- https://www.benchling.com/news/benchling-announces-7m-in-new-funding-led-by-thrive-capital\n- https://isomorphiclabs.com/articles/isomorphic-labs-announces-600m-external-investment-round\n- https://www.gunder.com/en/news-insights/client-news/thrive-capital-leads-isomorphic-labs-dollar21-billion-series-b\n", "person:vinod-khosla": "\n# Vinod Khosla\n\n## One-line take\nHe was the first VC into OpenAI. He still personally seeds left-field labs with small proof-of-concept cheques, then leads the next round himself, which makes him the most direct frontier-lab co-investor at KV.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he moves first on contrarian technical bets and then doubles down fast. Examples: OpenAI in 2019 ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2023/02/02/openais-first-vc-backer-khosla-ventures-weighs-in-on-the-future-of-generative-a-i/)), a $2M cheque to Symbolica before anyone else ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/04/09/vinod-khosla-former-tesla-autopilot-engineer-ai-models/)), and the seed and A at Scaled Cognition. His brand as \"first VC in OpenAI\" also pulls in founders.\n- **What constrains him:** KV fund cheques top out around $30M, so he uses SPVs for anything bigger ([NJ memo](https://nj.gov/treasury/doinvest/pdf/AlternativeInvestments/PrivateEquity/Khosla.pdf)). KV also refuses to credit deals to individuals, so his personal list is undercounted. He has now publicly attacked a KV portfolio company his own partner sits on (Factory), which shows his loyalties can override firm consensus ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/30/factory-ceo-just-accused-his-vc-board-advisor-of-spying-for-cognition/)).\n- **What he'd never do:** slow down AI for safety-regulation reasons. He calls China the real risk ([essay](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/ai-dystopia-or-utopia)). He also wouldn't avoid a bet because it is likely to fail.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Diligence by researcher migration.** He admits he doesn't follow Symbolica's category theory. Instead he watches where smart researchers go: \"when these really smart people gravitate to an idea, it's an important idea\" ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/04/09/vinod-khosla-former-tesla-autopilot-engineer-ai-models/), 2024-04-09).\n- **He stages his entry.** He gave Symbolica $2M in 2023 to prove the idea, then led the $31M A about a year later, once the founder delivered. He picks outsiders on purpose: \"We love people coming from left field\" ([Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/04/09/vinod-khosla-former-tesla-autopilot-engineer-ai-models/)).\n- **He backed sovereign models before it was consensus.** Sarvam's A in 2023-12 and Sakana's seed in 2024-01 came in the same quarter, then KV co-led Sakana's ~$200M A eight months later. His line: \"Most sovereign nations will want their own native foundational models\" ([Sakana](https://sakana.ai/seed-round/); [Sarvam](https://sarvam.ai/blogs/announcing-series-a)).\n- **Architecture over scaling.** On Scaled Cognition he said \"you can't just incrementally improve your way to zero hallucination\" ([company](https://scaledcognition.com/series-a), 2026-06-25). He led its seed and A and sits on the board.\n- **He sided against his own partner in public.** On 2026-09-30 he called Factory, where KV led the C and Rabois sits on the board, a \"struggling second tier competitor\" while defending Cognition, another KV company ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/30/factory-ceo-just-accused-his-vc-board-advisor-of-spying-for-cognition/)).\n- **An AI-chip bet from 2018.** KV led the Analog Inference (now Sagence) seed and holds two board seats, and Vinod \"is now acting as an advisor\" ([Alumni Ventures](https://www.av.vc/blog/analog-inference-lower-power-higher-performance)). It is an inference-efficiency bet made years before the LLM wave.\n- **He is a power-law realist.** \"Most AI ventures will end in financial loss.\" ([essay](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/ai-dystopia-or-utopia), 2024-09-20).\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-10)\nI mined 10 of the 151 public items catalogued in `data/rows/vinod-khosla-voice-media.csv`, mostly older talks and podcasts with transcripts. The row-level detail is in `data/rows/vinod-khosla-voice-insights.csv`. YouTube quotes come from auto-captions.\n- **The OpenAI cheque was an apology to LPs.** It is \"the only investment in which I've ever sent an apology letter to our LPs\". It was twice his previous largest initial cheque in 40 years ([Minus One, 31:02](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shMMW_RhztA), 2025-04-02).\n- **How the OpenAI deal happened.** In 2018 Musk didn't meet his funding commitments, and Altman called Vinod. KV decided in 2018 and the deal closed in early 2019. Vinod says he saw only Google and Baidu as rival teams. In his words, \"Elon backing off funding OpenAI was fortunate\" ([Uncapped, 31:23](https://youtu.be/KZ9cYDeum4U), 2025-07-01).\n- **Check-size ladder.** He probes a new category with about ten ~$1M cheques (\"we'll make 10 1 million dollar investments\"). Seeds like Joywell run $5-10M. He adds SPVs on top of fund cheques to scale winners such as Commonwealth Fusion and Impossible ([Moonfare, 54:26 and 34:58](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R0BGdEPuKQI), 2021-09-17).\n- **No consensus and no IRR.** One partner's conviction is enough: \"we in our firm don't require consensus or even two people to agree\" ([Minus One, 33:09](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shMMW_RhztA)). IRR math is banned at decision time ([20VC transcript](https://www.khoslaventures.com/posts/the-twenty-minute-vc-with-vinod-khosla), 2020-02-24).\n- **Price discipline is not his lever.** He calls the difference between 10% and 12% dilution \"trivial compared to the 500% swing\" from choosing the right path ([Uncapped, 1:14:30](https://youtu.be/KZ9cYDeum4U)). This suits a co-investor who doesn't run rigid ownership rules.\n- **Mistakes he admits.** He over-concentrated in climate in 2007-08. When follow-on money died around 2011, in his words \"the funds look terrible\" for years ([Moonfare, 37:57](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R0BGdEPuKQI)). Biofuels were \"spectacular failures\", although three of four battery companies were expected to make money ([Udacity transcript](https://www.khoslaventures.com/posts/udacity-thought-leader-series-how-startups-entrepreneurs-are-disrupting-industries), 2019-11-11).\n- **Juniper was incubated, not just backed.** He called Pradeep Sindhu and they spent six months on the plan. His first $3M cheque returned about 2,500x ([Minus One, 27:01](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shMMW_RhztA)). Per The Generalist's summary, Andy Bechtolsheim introduced him to both Juniper and Google ([src](https://thegeneralist.substack.com/p/vinod-khosla-3), 2024-08-13).\n- **Boards and votes.** He has \"never ever voted against an entrepreneur\" ([Bloomberg Studio 1.0, 9:55](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uJboXgDoE1Y), 2016-10-31). He won't vote even on acquisitions. He left Square's board before the IPO but kept challenge dinners with Dorsey and quarterly sessions with Levchin. He rejects the Founders Fund view that top founders need no help ([Uncapped, 1:08:18](https://youtu.be/KZ9cYDeum4U)).\n- **SVB, 2023-03-12.** KV bridged payroll with personal no-terms loans at its own borrowing cost, \"not using our LP's capital\". It covered companies where it was lead or a major investor ([X](https://twitter.com/vkhosla/status/1634742543534673920); [X](https://twitter.com/vkhosla/status/1634737634907127810)).\n- **LP channel.** In 2021 he pitched KV's first opportunities fund and its SPVs to Moonfare's feeder members, who were already in KV Fund VII ([Moonfare, 56:02](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R0BGdEPuKQI)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He personally backs new frontier labs at seed and A: Symbolica, Scaled Cognition, Sakana, Sarvam and Moonvalley. He also backs AI chips (Sagence) and agent infrastructure (Parallel), and he leads oversized rounds. He co-leads with one other brand (GC, NEA, Lux), which leaves room for a $20M co-investor. The deals that matter for me:\n- Scaled Cognition (seed and A, board)\n- Symbolica (first cheque, then the A)\n- Parallel (board)\n- Sakana (seed, then co-led A)\n- Moonvalley (co-led seed)\n- Sagence (chips)\n\nKV runs no rigid ownership screen at seed. Moonvalley and General Intuition show it co-leading big rounds next to another lead.\n\n## Background\n- B.Tech in Electrical Engineering, IIT Delhi. Master's in Biomedical Engineering, Carnegie Mellon. MBA, Stanford (rejected twice before admission). [src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/vinod-khosla)\n- Co-founded Daisy Systems, then was founding CEO of Sun Microsystems (1982). [src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/vinod-khosla)\n- At Kleiner Perkins he helped incubate Juniper Networks (1995-96). [src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/vinod-khosla)\n- Founded Khosla Ventures in 2004 with Samir Kaul and David Weiden. [src](https://nj.gov/treasury/doinvest/pdf/AlternativeInvestments/PrivateEquity/Khosla.pdf)\n- Ranked No. 1 on the Forbes Midas List in 2001 and again in 2026. Named to the TIME100 AI list in 2024. Giving Pledge signatory. [src](https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/vinod-khosla)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/vinod-khosla | Firm bio, verified 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | https://khoslaventures.com/posts/ai-dystopia-or-utopia | Long essays on khoslaventures.com/posts; no personal Substack found |\n| X | https://twitter.com/vkhosla | Linked from his firm page; active (long threads on AI, hiring, tax); follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | Firm page links only the KV company page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-08-19: Agentic AI Summit, fireside chat with Dawn Song. Per the page summary, he predicts AI will replace keyboards by 2030 and already does most valuable jobs. Only a summary was available, no transcript ([src](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/agentic-ai-summit-fireside-chat-with-vinod-khosla)).\n- 2024-04: The Generalist, \"Letters to a Young Founder\" (Mario Gabriele, four parts) on tenacity, the 2500x Juniper return and founder mode. I only saw the summary ([src](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/the-generalist-letters-to-a-young-founder-vinod-khosla)).\n- \"Where is AI taking us?\" with Sam Altman is listed on the KV site with no URL.\n\n## How they think\n- **AI amplifies the mind the way steam amplified muscle.** He expects most knowledge and manual jobs to be automatable within about 25 years, and says \"Virtually every kind of expertise will be near free\" ([essay](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/ai-dystopia-or-utopia)).\n- **Sovereign foundation models are inevitable,** so he backs national labs (Sarvam, Sakana).\n- **Architecture bets over scale-only bets:** Symbolica (category theory), Scaled Cognition (hallucination-free agents), Sagence (analog inference).\n- **Failure is the price of the outliers.** He backs founders \"who aren’t afraid of the high probability of failure\" ([2018 essay](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/reinventing-societal-infrastructure-with-technology)).\n- **Right call:** OpenAI in 2019. **Contested call:** publicly backing Cognition against Factory (2026). The outcome is unknown.\n\n## Read these first\n- [AI: Dystopia or Utopia?](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/ai-dystopia-or-utopia) (2024-09-20): his full AI worldview, covering jobs, China and the power law.\n- [Reinventing Societal Infrastructure with Technology](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/reinventing-societal-infrastructure-with-technology) (2018-04-22): his bet-selection philosophy.\n- [Fortune on Symbolica](https://fortune.com/2024/04/09/vinod-khosla-former-tesla-autopilot-engineer-ai-models/) (2024-04-09): the clearest view of how he underwrites a new lab.\n- [The Generalist: Letters to a Young Founder](https://khoslaventures.com/posts/the-generalist-letters-to-a-young-founder-vinod-khosla) (2024-04): biography and founder-mode views.\n\n## Social engagement\nThe KV site surfaces his X threads on several topics ([KV home](https://www.khoslaventures.com/)):\n- AI as an amplifier of human intellect (a 25-part thread)\n- AI and economically valuable work\n- Auto-formalization as an emerging area\n- Capital-gains tax in the AI era\n- Hiring CEOs\n- Foreign talent\n- Persistence versus obstinacy\n\nHe amplifies Keith Rabois, including posts on Rabois's 2013 arrival and on team building. In 2026-09 he engaged combatively on X in the Factory/Cognition dispute. Who amplifies him wasn't checked, because X wasn't fetchable.\n\n## Portfolio\nAI deals where he is personally named: led, board, quoted or listed. Firm-only KV deals are in `data/rows/khosla-ventures-portfolio-deals.csv`.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | Undisclosed (OpenAI LP initial round; first VC) | 2019 | Vinod reported as lead of KV cheque | Reid Hoffman foundation | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2023/02/02/openais-first-vc-backer-khosla-ventures-weighs-in-on-the-future-of-generative-a-i/) |\n| OpenAI | Growth (KV OAI SPV $405M) | 2024-10 | no | n/a | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2039800/000203980024000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Sagence AI | Seed ($4.5M, as Analog Inference) | 2018-03 | yes (KV); Vinod advisor | Andy Bechtolsheim; Atiq Raza | [AV](https://www.av.vc/blog/analog-inference-lower-power-higher-performance) |\n| Sagence AI | Series A ($10.6M) | n/a | yes (KV) | Alumni Ventures | [AV](https://www.av.vc/blog/analog-inference-lower-power-higher-performance) |\n| Sagence AI | Series B | 2023-05 | n/a | n/a | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/11/18/sagence-is-building-analog-chips-to-run-ai/) |\n| Scaled Cognition | Seed ($21M) | 2023 | yes; board | n/a | [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/2026/06/25/scaled-cognition-nabs-100m-automate-high-stakes-customer-interactions) |\n| Scaled Cognition | Series A ($100M, ~$750M val) | 2026-06-25 | yes | Genesys | [company](https://scaledcognition.com/series-a) |\n| Symbolica AI | Seed ($2M proof-of-concept) | 2023 | yes (first investor) | n/a | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/04/09/vinod-khosla-former-tesla-autopilot-engineer-ai-models/) |\n| Symbolica AI | Series A ($31M) | 2024-04-09 | yes | General Catalyst; Day One; Abstract; Buckley | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/04/09/vinod-khosla-former-tesla-autopilot-engineer-ai-models/) |\n| Sarvam AI | Series A ($41M) | 2023-12-07 | no (Lightspeed led); quoted | Peak XV | [Sarvam](https://sarvam.ai/blogs/announcing-series-a) |\n| Sakana AI | Seed ($30M) | 2024-01-16 | no (Lux led); quoted | NTT; Sony; KDDI | [Sakana](https://sakana.ai/seed-round/) |\n| Sakana AI | Series A (~$200M) | 2024-09-04 | co-led | NEA; Lux; NVIDIA | [Sakana](https://sakana.ai/series-a/) |\n| Parallel Web Systems | Seed ($30M) | 2024-01 | not disclosed; board | Index; First Round; Terrain | [Parallel](https://parallel.ai/blog/introducing-parallel) |\n| Parallel Web Systems | Series A ($100M at $740M) | 2025-11-13 | no | Kleiner; Index (co-leads) | [Parallel](https://parallel.ai/blog/series-a) |\n| Parallel Web Systems | Series B ($100M at $2B) | 2026-04-29 | no | n/a | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/29/parallel-web-systems-hits-2b-valuation-five-months-after-its-last-big-raise/) |\n| Moonvalley | Seed ($70M) | 2024-11-18 | co-led (with Kaul) | General Catalyst; Bessemer | [PR Newswire](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-company-moonvalley-raises-70-million-in-funding-to-develop-future-of-generative-media-302307953.html) |\n| Basis | Series A ($34M) | 2024-12-17 | no (Rabois led for KV); Vinod listed | Khosla Ventures | [Basis](https://www.getbasis.ai/blogs/basis-raises-a-34m-series-a-led-by-khosla-ventures) |\n| Monumental | Series B ($32M) | 2026-07-15 | yes; quoted | Plural; Hummingbird | [TNW](https://thenextweb.com/news/monumental-32m-khosla-bricklaying-robots-uk-us) |\n\n**Timing:**\n- First in: OpenAI (first VC), Symbolica (first investor), Sagence (seed lead) and Scaled Cognition (seed lead).\n- Doubled down: Scaled Cognition, Symbolica, Sakana, Sagence and OpenAI.\n- Late entrant: Monumental, about 2.5 years after Plural.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **OpenAI (2019).** Vinod is reported to have led KV's cheque into the new capped-profit OpenAI LP, alongside Reid Hoffman's foundation. Kaul called it \"a large bet for us\" and said KV doesn't attribute deals to individuals. KV re-upped in 2024 through a dedicated $405M SPV.\n- **Symbolica.** A $2M cheque in 2023 to George Morgan, an ex-Tesla Autopilot engineer, to prove the idea. When Morgan delivered, KV led the $31M A in 2024-04 with GC.\n- **Factory vs Cognition (2026-09).** After Cognition hired Factory's ex-board adviser Chris Degnan as CRO, Factory's CEO accused him of spying. Vinod attacked Factory on X while Rabois defended it. It is a rare public split inside a firm.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Samir Kaul, David Weiden | KV co-founders; Kaul co-named on Moonvalley | 2004 | [NJ memo](https://nj.gov/treasury/doinvest/pdf/AlternativeInvestments/PrivateEquity/Khosla.pdf) |\n| Keith Rabois | KV MD; opposite sides on Factory | 2013 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/30/factory-ceo-just-accused-his-vc-board-advisor-of-spying-for-cognition/) |\n| Mamoon Hamid, Shardul Shah, Josh Kopelman | Co-directors at Parallel | 2024-25 | [Parallel](https://parallel.ai/blog/series-a) |\n| George Morgan (Symbolica) | First backer | 2023 | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2024/04/09/vinod-khosla-former-tesla-autopilot-engineer-ai-models/) |\n| Sam Altman | First VC backer of OpenAI | 2019 | [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2023/02/02/openais-first-vc-backer-khosla-ventures-weighs-in-on-the-future-of-generative-a-i/) |\n| General Catalyst (Hemant Taneja) | Repeat co-lead (Moonvalley; also Symbolica A, General Intuition seed at firm level) | 2024 | [PR Newswire](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-company-moonvalley-raises-70-million-in-funding-to-develop-future-of-generative-media-302307953.html) |\n| Andy Bechtolsheim | Sun co-founder; co-investor in Sagence seed | 1982 | [Caplight, aggregator](https://www.caplight.com/company/analog-inference) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **General Intuition seed.** One tracker credits Vinod as lead, but the firm page credits Nicole Fraenkel. Unresolved.\n- **Cognition.** Is Vinod the KV partner on the deal, or on its board? His public defence suggests a personal tie, but nothing confirms it.\n- **Other KV labs.** Is he personally on Physical Intelligence, Periodic Labs, PrismML or Fastino? Press names only KV.\n- **KV Excelsior L.P.** It is $1B with 2 investors (2023), and KV Moonshot LLC is $800M (2026). Is either a vehicle for Vinod's family capital or a single LP?\n- **Personal LP positions.** I found no evidence that he is an LP in other funds, so there is no LP row file.\n- **Unmined archive (updated 2026-11-10).** Of 151 catalogued items, 10 have been mined. Still unread: the 2023 All-In Summit, Kara Swisher (2023), Turpentine VC (2024), The Information (2024), Uncapped #40 with Rabois (2026) and the Newcomer pod (2026). YouTube captions were bot-blocked for some videos. Generalist parts 1-3 are mostly paywalled.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.khoslaventures.com/team/vinod-khosla\n- https://www.khoslaventures.com/\n- https://khoslaventures.com/posts/ai-dystopia-or-utopia\n- https://khoslaventures.com/posts/reinventing-societal-infrastructure-with-technology\n- https://khoslaventures.com/posts/agentic-ai-summit-fireside-chat-with-vinod-khosla\n- https://khoslaventures.com/posts/the-generalist-letters-to-a-young-founder-vinod-khosla\n- https://fortune.com/2023/02/02/openais-first-vc-backer-khosla-ventures-weighs-in-on-the-future-of-generative-a-i/\n- https://fortune.com/2024/04/09/vinod-khosla-former-tesla-autopilot-engineer-ai-models/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/30/factory-ceo-just-accused-his-vc-board-advisor-of-spying-for-cognition/\n- https://scaledcognition.com/series-a\n- https://siliconangle.com/2026/06/25/scaled-cognition-nabs-100m-automate-high-stakes-customer-interactions\n- https://parallel.ai/blog/series-a ; https://parallel.ai/blog/introducing-parallel\n- https://sakana.ai/seed-round/ ; https://sakana.ai/series-a/\n- https://sarvam.ai/blogs/announcing-series-a\n- https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/ai-company-moonvalley-raises-70-million-in-funding-to-develop-future-of-generative-media-302307953.html\n- https://www.av.vc/blog/analog-inference-lower-power-higher-performance\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/monumental-32m-khosla-bricklaying-robots-uk-us\n- https://www.getbasis.ai/blogs/basis-raises-a-34m-series-a-led-by-khosla-ventures\n- https://nj.gov/treasury/doinvest/pdf/AlternativeInvestments/PrivateEquity/Khosla.pdf\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2039800/000203980024000001/primary_doc.xml\n", "person:vishal-bhagwati": "\n# Vishal Bhagwati\n\n## One-line take\nHe runs the NVIDIA pool that writes $1B+ strategic checks into labs. He has no public voice, and The Information reports that Huang, not Bhagwati, often sets the price. Track him as an org-chart fact, not as an investor whose judgement you can read.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why the pool wins:** it pays in NVIDIA's balance sheet and gets paid back in gigawatts. Siddeek describes the corp-dev mandate as platform-first. \"They focus on enhancing Nvidia's platform,\" through acquisitions, partnerships and investments ([GCV 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)).\n- **What makes it different from NVentures:** returns are not the objective. Siddeek: \"we have a returns focus as well, which Vishal doesn't\" (same source). This is the only on-record characterisation of Bhagwati's mandate found anywhere.\n- **What constrains him:** the CEO. The Information, via Dealroom, says corp dev \"is led by Vishal Bhagwati (ex-HPE, Oracle), but Huang often sets prices himself\", and \"Huang negotiates big deals himself\" ([Dealroom, 2026-10-07](https://dealroom.co/news/info-i81usb-where-nvidias-100-billion-dealmaking-juggernaut-will-go-next/)). His authority on price is capped from above.\n- **What the pool would never do:** take a price-disciplined, ownership-driven seed position. Every deal attributed to the pool is a post-seed strategic tranche or an M&A or license-and-hire structure. Inferred from the deal list below and in firms/nvidia.md.\n\n## Differential insight\n**Headline finding: no primary-source voice exists.** Searches for his name with NVIDIA, with conference and panel terms, and with deal and acquisition terms (Cray, Arm, Mellanox, Run:ai, OpenAI, Anthropic, xAI) found no podcast, talk, X account, byline or quote from Bhagwati himself (searched 2026-11-10). The firm file's gap can't be filled from his own words. Everything below comes from other people describing him.\n\n- **Mandate defined by a peer, not by him.** NVentures head Siddeek: \"but we have a returns focus as well, which Vishal doesn't\" ([GCV 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md)). This is the cleanest public split between NVIDIA's two investing pools.\n- **Huang sets prices.** \"Huang often sets prices himself\" ([Dealroom summary of The Information, 2026-10-07](https://dealroom.co/news/info-i81usb-where-nvidias-100-billion-dealmaking-juggernaut-will-go-next/)). For a founder, the real counterparty on a big NVIDIA check is Huang. Bhagwati's team executes.\n- **The pipeline (all from the same Dealroom summary of The Information, 2026-10-07; deal-talk details rest on unnamed sources):**\n  - Perplexity: Aravind Srinivas suggested NVIDIA buy Perplexity outright. The two then \"discussed a license-and-hire deal worth at least $20B\" before they \"settled on a partnership with a roughly $3B investment at a $35B pre-money valuation\".\n  - Hugging Face: \"Huang pushed to close Hugging Face fast at $12.9B\", after Salesforce and others circled. Per the page's linked item, it was signed 2026-09-02 and is expected to close in 1H 2027.\n  - Figure: NVIDIA \"has discussed investing another $1B in Figure\", which is raising at about $38B pre-money.\n  - OpenRouter: NVIDIA showed interest in July but \"never made a formal offer before Stripe won with an $8B bid\".\n  - Poolside: listed only as \"Poolside license-and-hire\", with no terms.\n  \n  The pattern: the corp-dev pool now runs a menu of outright buys, license-and-hire deals and minority stakes. Huang's speed decides the outcome.\n- **The HPE exit was not a clean step.** On 2019-11-04 HPE announced he would move from SVP corporate development to CFO of the newly created HPC & MCS unit, \"reporting to Robbiati\" (Tarek Robbiati, HPE CFO) ([HPE release via MarketScreener](https://www.marketscreener.com/quote/stock/HEWLETT-PACKARD-ENTERPRIS-24843838/news/Hewlett-Packard-Enterprise-Announces-Executive-Changes-34096063/)). Sonalee Parekh took over corp dev and IR. TheOrg (aggregator, low confidence) has him at NVIDIA from 2020-01. If both are right, he spent at most about two months in the divisional CFO role before joining NVIDIA. HPE closed its $1.3B Cray acquisition in 2019. That HPC unit is the likely home of Cray, but the release fetched does not mention Cray, so treat the Cray link as unconfirmed.\n- **He builds a team.** Clifford Platt was promoted in 2023 \"under corporate development chief Vishal Bhagwati\" to senior director, corporate development (investments), and left in July 2025 ([GCV 2025-07-14](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md)). GCV names the deals that year (Cohere, Poolside, Crusoe, OpenAI and xAI) but credits neither man with any of them.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** His pool writes growth-stage strategic tranches ($1B+) priced on compute commitments, and you will not sit beside it at seed. The useful calibration is the contrast: in lab mega-seeds (Ineffable, Periodic, RadixArk, Inherent), the NVIDIA pool that shows up is NVentures under Siddeek, not Bhagwati's. Bhagwati's pool matters in two ways. It sets the later-round floor for labs you back: a lab that gets a gigawatt-tied check from his pool gets an anchor buyer and a re-rate. It is also a possible exit or license-and-hire buyer, as with Perplexity, Hugging Face and Poolside. Deals that matter to you: Thinking Machines (2026-03 strategic), Reflection AI Series B (NVIDIA-led), Anthropic (2025-11), Mistral and Figure.\n\n## Background\n- **Oracle:** M&A role, title and dates not found ([TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/nvidia/org-chart/vishal-bhagwati) lists Oracle as a prior employer; Dealroom says \"ex-HPE, Oracle\").\n- **HPE:** SVP Corporate Development until November 2019. On 2019-11-04 HPE announced him as CFO of the new HPC & MCS unit, reporting to CFO Tarek Robbiati ([MarketScreener/HPE](https://www.marketscreener.com/quote/stock/HEWLETT-PACKARD-ENTERPRIS-24843838/news/Hewlett-Packard-Enterprise-Announces-Executive-Changes-34096063/)).\n- **NVIDIA:** Head of Corporate Development, since 2020-01 per TheOrg (aggregator). Corroborated as head by GCV (2024) and The Information (2026).\n- Education: not found.\n- Reporting line at NVIDIA: not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | none found | |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | none found | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked (not retrievable) | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n| Aggregator | [TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/nvidia/org-chart/vishal-bhagwati) | title only; \"not in the org chart\" |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found (searched 2026-11-10).\n\n## How they think\n- No first-person statements found. The mandate as others describe it: platform enhancement through acquisitions, partnerships and investments, with no returns target (Siddeek, GCV 2024).\n- In practice the pool trades equity for supply relationships: OpenAI's money is staged per gigawatt, and Anthropic and Thinking Machines carry 1GW commitments (see firms/nvidia.md).\n- Contrarian views and calls: nothing attributable to him.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [GCV interview with Siddeek, 2024-03-22](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md): the two-pool split and the \"returns focus\" line.\n2. [Dealroom summary of The Information, 2026-10-07](https://dealroom.co/news/info-i81usb-where-nvidias-100-billion-dealmaking-juggernaut-will-go-next/): Huang sets prices, plus the Perplexity, Hugging Face, Figure and OpenRouter pipeline.\n3. [GCV on Clifford Platt, 2025-07-14](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md): Bhagwati's team structure.\n4. [HPE executive changes, 2019-11-04](https://www.marketscreener.com/quote/stock/HEWLETT-PACKARD-ENTERPRIS-24843838/news/Hewlett-Packard-Enterprise-Announces-Executive-Changes-34096063/): his last HPE move.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public social presence located.\n\n## Portfolio\nHe is not personally named on any round. The rows below are deals the sources attribute to the corp-dev pool, or that fall to it by role. They are attributed by pool, not by person.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Figure AI | nine-figure round (pre-GCV 2024-03 interview) | before 2024-03-22 | no | not in source | [GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md) (named as a corp-dev deal) |\n| Mistral AI | nine-figure round (Series A, 2023-12, per ledger) | 2023-12-11 | no | a16z (lead); Lightspeed; General Catalyst | [GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md); [ledger src](https://siecledigital.fr/2023/12/11/mistral-ai-boucle-un-tour-de-table-record-et-devient-une-licorne/) |\n| OpenAI | 2024 round | 2024-10-02 | no | Thrive (lead); Microsoft; SoftBank | [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2024/10/02/openai-raises-at-157-billion-valuation-microsoft-nvidia-join-round.html); by role |\n| xAI | Series C | 2024-12-23 | no | a16z; Sequoia; Valor; AMD | [xAI](https://x.ai/news/series-c); by role |\n| Anthropic | Strategic (up to $10B) | 2025-11-18 | no lead named | Microsoft | [Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/microsoft-nvidia-anthropic-announce-strategic-partnerships); by role |\n| Reflection AI | Series B ($2B) | 2025-10-09 | yes (per SiliconANGLE citing NYT) | Lightspeed; Sequoia; CRV; DST | [SiliconANGLE](https://siliconangle.com/?p=715519); by role |\n| Thinking Machines Lab | Strategic + 1GW compute | 2026-03-10 | no lead named | none named | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/10/thinking-machines-lab-inks-massive-compute-deal-with-nvidia); by role |\n| OpenAI | 2026 $122B round (NVIDIA $30B) | 2026-03-31 | no (co-leads a16z, SoftBank et al.) | a16z; D. E. Shaw; MGX; SoftBank | [Bloomberg Law](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/tech-and-telecom-law/openai-valued-at-852-billion-after-closing-122-billion-round); by role |\n| Hugging Face | Acquisition ($12.9B, signed) | 2026-09-02 (signed) | n/a | n/a | [Dealroom/The Information](https://dealroom.co/news/info-i81usb-where-nvidias-100-billion-dealmaking-juggernaut-will-go-next/); M&A falls to corp dev by mandate |\n| Perplexity | Partnership, ~$3B at $35B pre | 2026 (reported) | n/a | n/a | [Dealroom/The Information](https://dealroom.co/news/info-i81usb-where-nvidias-100-billion-dealmaking-juggernaut-will-go-next/) |\n\n**Timing:** the pool is never first. Anthropic came ~54 months after its first institutional money, OpenAI ~5 years after Microsoft, Mistral ~6 months after the Lightspeed seed, and Thinking Machines' strategic deal ~8 months after the a16z seed (sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md). Reflection B is the only lead, and it came ~7 months after NVentures' Series A check. This is the clearest evidence of handoff: NVentures enters, then corp dev scales the position.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Perplexity (2026):** per The Information, the company proposed the deal (Srinivas suggested a full acquisition). Talks went through a license-and-hire worth at least $20B before landing on a ~$3B minority investment at $35B pre. Bhagwati's own role isn't described.\n- **Hugging Face (2026):** Huang drove speed once Salesforce and others showed interest. The deal was signed at $12.9B, more than 80x the $150M annualized revenue (Dealroom/The Information).\n- **OpenRouter (2026):** NVIDIA showed interest in July but made no formal offer, and Stripe bought it at $8B. A rare reported miss for the pool.\n- No intro paths for any lab deal are documented.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NVIDIA | Head of Corporate Development | 2020-01 (aggregator) | [TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/nvidia/org-chart/vishal-bhagwati); [GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md) |\n| Jensen Huang | CEO; sets prices on big deals | 2020 | [Dealroom/The Information](https://dealroom.co/news/info-i81usb-where-nvidias-100-billion-dealmaking-juggernaut-will-go-next/) |\n| Mohamed \"Sid\" Siddeek | Peer: head of NVentures, the returns-focused pool | 2021 | [GCV 2024](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md) |\n| Clifford Platt | Promoted under him to senior director, investments (2023); left 2025-07 | 2023 | [GCV 2025](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md) |\n| Tarek Robbiati | HPE CFO; he was to report to Robbiati as HPC & MCS CFO | 2019-11 | [HPE via MarketScreener](https://www.marketscreener.com/quote/stock/HEWLETT-PACKARD-ENTERPRIS-24843838/news/Hewlett-Packard-Enterprise-Announces-Executive-Changes-34096063/) |\n| Sonalee Parekh | Succeeded him as HPE SVP corp dev and IR | 2019-11 | same |\n| HPE, Oracle | Prior employers | to 2019 | [TheOrg](https://theorg.com/org/nvidia/org-chart/vishal-bhagwati) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Reporting line at NVIDIA (Huang or the CFO, Kress) and his formal title (VP or SVP).\n- Did he take the HPE HPC & MCS CFO seat at all? Did he run the Cray acquisition at HPE?\n- Oracle tenure, title and dates.\n- Was he on NVIDIA's Arm (2020–22) and Run:ai and Lepton deals? These would date his M&A record at NVIDIA.\n- Where did Clifford Platt go, and who replaced him? A reachable mid-level contact in the pool.\n- Who on his team runs lab deals day to day? None are named.\n\n## Sources\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md\n- https://dealroom.co/news/info-i81usb-where-nvidias-100-billion-dealmaking-juggernaut-will-go-next/\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/clifford-platt-leaves-nvidia/?format=md\n- https://www.marketscreener.com/quote/stock/HEWLETT-PACKARD-ENTERPRIS-24843838/news/Hewlett-Packard-Enterprise-Announces-Executive-Changes-34096063/\n- https://theorg.com/org/nvidia/org-chart/vishal-bhagwati (aggregator)\n- firms/nvidia.md, sources/nvidia-frontier-ledger.md (repo)\n", "person:vivek-pattipati": "\n# Vivek K. Pattipati\n\n*Research note (2026-11-10): web search was exhausted for this run, so everything below comes from direct fetches: his Valor bio, SEC EDGAR full-text search for \"Pattipati\" (84 hits, 2015–2026), the Form D XML for each issuer, BitGo's 2025 10-K, Sonder's 2022 proxy, and the Zeitview and Biobot sites. I found no podcast, Substack, X post or press quote from him. That is the biggest gap.*\n\n## One-line take\nGracias is the face of Valor's AI-compute deals, but Pattipati holds the board seat: he has sat on Crusoe's board on every Form D from the 2022 Series C through the 2026 Series F ([2022](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467422000001/primary_doc.xml), [2026](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467426000002/primary_doc.xml)).\n\n## Crux\n- **What he does.** He is a private-equity-trained structurer. His bio describes the job as \"investment prospect generation, due diligence of potential investments, and investment structuring and execution\" ([bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/vivek-k-pattipati)).\n- **Why he wins.** He takes and keeps board seats across growth rounds. Examples:\n  - Crusoe: on the board through five financings (Form Ds 2022-04 to 2026-09).\n  - BitGo: director since 2019 and now lead independent director of the listed company ([10-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1740604/000174060426000020/btgo-form10xk.htm)).\n- **What constrains him.** Gracias is on every IC and is the Musk relationship. Pattipati sat on the ICs of Funds III–VI and VOF ([bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/vivek-k-pattipati)), but he is not a promoter on Fund VII, Valor Atreides AI I or Valor Compute Infrastructure (filings below). So the newest and biggest AI pools are not formally his.\n- **What he would never do (inferred, unconfirmed).** Write a sub-$10M, lead-less seed cheque. Every seat he holds is in a priced round of $13M or more.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He holds the Crusoe seat, not Gracias.**\n  - He is a director on every Crusoe Form D: the Series C (first sale 2022-04-08), a non-equity raise (2023-11-22), the Series D (2024-11-06), the Series E (2025-10-08), a non-equity raise (2026-07-17) and the Series F (2026-08-31) ([C](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467422000001/primary_doc.xml), [D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467424000003/primary_doc.xml), [E](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467425000004/primary_doc.xml), [F](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467426000002/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - Gracias is the one quoted in the press releases. The Form D quote is \"<lastName>Pattipati</lastName>\".\n- **A new seat that is not on his bio: Silicon Derivatives Inc.**\n  - It is a Hartsdale, NY company, incorporated in 2024.\n  - Its 2025-03 Form D ($4.7M, 12 investors) lists founders Rongnan Li and Spencer Powers plus Donald Wilson Jr. ([2025](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - Its 2026-08 Form D ($30.5M sold of $35M, 16 investors, first sale 2026-07-17) adds Pattipati as a director ([2026](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - Wilson's listed address is \"540 W. Madison, Suite 2500\" in Chicago. That is very likely DRW founder Don Wilson, but the filing does not say so (unconfirmed).\n  - **What the company does is unconfirmed.** Its website returned no content, and web search was exhausted. Do not infer AI chips from the name.\n- **The fund-filing drop-off is narrower than reported.**\n  - He is a promoter on Fund VI through its 2024-03 amendment ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1907694/000190769424000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - He is the only name removed from that list on Fund VII ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2082328/000208232826000002/primary_doc.xml)). The other five are the three principals, the CFO and the CLO.\n  - He is also absent from Opportunity Fund II, Valor Atreides AI I and VCI.\n  - But he is still an \"Executive Officer, Promoter\" on **CV Consortio Fund II L.P.** (Valor's Chicago address, filed 2026-08-13, no sales yet) ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2126869/000212686926000002/primary_doc.xml)), as he was on CV Consortio Fund I in 2023 ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1971232/000196883423000004/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - What CV Consortio invests in is not disclosed. Whether the Fund VII omission means a role change is **unconfirmed**.\n- **Lead independent director of BitGo (ticker BTGO).** The 10-K lists him at age 39 on the compensation and nominating committees and as \"Lead independent director\" ([10-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1740604/000174060426000020/btgo-form10xk.htm)). His Form 3 was filed 2026-01-21 at the IPO.\n- **He gives up a seat at listing when Valor's role ends.** He sat on Sonder's board \"through November 2021\", when Sonder went public by SPAC ([proxy](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1819395/000181939522000027/a2022proxystatement.htm)).\n- **Two other AI-adjacent seats confirmed in filings:**\n  - Zeitview (legal name DroneBase, Inc.): director on a $130M Form D (first sale 2026-01-27, 37 investors) alongside Upfront's Mark Suster ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1676896/000167689626000003/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - Biobot Analytics: director on a $13.45M Form D (2025-05-23). Biobot is not on his bio ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1809780/000180978025000005/primary_doc.xml)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **Against:** he is a growth-stage board and structuring partner, not a seed picker. Valor's AI money is a $3.3B wealth-channel fund plus the joint Atreides vehicle, and he is not a promoter on either.\n- **For:** he is the operating contact on Crusoe, the most relevant AI-compute position for this owner. He also sits on Silicon Derivatives, whose second raise (~$30M) is in the cheque range the owner writes into. That one is worth identifying.\n\nDeals that matter:\n- Crusoe: Series C to F.\n- Silicon Derivatives (business unconfirmed).\n- K Health (AI primary care, 2026).\n- Zeitview (AI inspection, 2026).\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** B.A. in Economics and Statistics from the University of Chicago (Phi Beta Kappa), and an MBA from Harvard Business School as a Baker Scholar ([bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/vivek-k-pattipati)).\n- **Goldman Sachs:** Investment Banking Analyst in the Financial Sponsors Group ([bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/vivek-k-pattipati)).\n- **Madison Dearborn Partners:** Associate, then Vice President, then Director ([BitGo 10-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1740604/000174060426000020/btgo-form10xk.htm)). While at MDP he was already a director of Lanai Holdings I, the Patterson Medical buyout vehicle, alongside MDP's Nicholas Alexos and Timothy Sullivan (2015 Form D, [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1652389/000165238915000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n- **Valor:** joined in September 2017 ([10-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1740604/000174060426000020/btgo-form10xk.htm)), which the bio gives as 2017. By 2019 he was a promoter on Valor funds (M33 IV, Siren Ventures I, Fund V) and a director of BitGo and Sonder.\n- **How he got the Crusoe seat:** Valor led the Series B in April 2021 ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-series-b)), and Pattipati appears as director on Crusoe's first Form D a year later. The seat was most likely taken at the B (unconfirmed).\n- **Age:** 39 per the 10-K.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.valorep.com/team/vivek-k-pattipati | Valor bio (verified 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | web search exhausted; not checked |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| SEC filer | https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?action=getcompany&CIK=0002082317 | Section 16 filer as BitGo director (CIK 2082317) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found (web search exhausted this run).\n\n## How they think\n- From his bio: his job is prospect generation, diligence and structuring, and he holds 13 named board or observer seats spread across fintech/crypto (BitGo, Unchained, Lightning Labs, Republic), insurance (Coalition, Nirvana, AgentSync) and AI/compute (Crusoe, K Health, Zeitview, CompScience) ([bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/vivek-k-pattipati)).\n- From the filings: he stays on boards through repeated rounds (Crusoe ×6, BitGo 2019–2026 and through the IPO).\n- No first-person statements were found.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Valor bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/vivek-k-pattipati): his role and board list.\n- [BitGo 10-K, directors section](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1740604/000174060426000020/btgo-form10xk.htm): career dates and the lead-independent-director role.\n- [Crusoe Series F Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467426000002/primary_doc.xml): the current Crusoe board.\n- [Silicon Derivatives Form D (2026)](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml): his newest seat.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n\"Led?\" is Valor's role, from the firm's rows.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Crusoe | Series C ($307.9M sold) | first sale 2022-04-08 | firm in round; lead unconfirmed | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467422000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Crusoe | Undisclosed (non-equity, $192.4M) | 2023-11-22 | unknown | | [Form D/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467424000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Crusoe | Series D ($685.7M sold) | 2024-11-06 | no (Founders Fund led) | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467424000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Crusoe | Series E ($1.03B sold) | 2025-10-08 | Valor co-led | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467425000004/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Crusoe | Undisclosed (non-equity, $595.9M) | 2026-07-17 | unknown | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467426000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Crusoe | Series F ($3.1B sold) | 2026-08-31 | Valor co-led | Atreides, Mubadala Capital | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467426000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| K Health | Undisclosed ($58.9M sold) | 2026-06-05 | unknown | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1689694/000168969426000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Zeitview | Undisclosed ($130.0M) | 2026-01-27 | unknown | Upfront (Suster on board) | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1676896/000167689626000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Silicon Derivatives | Undisclosed ($30.5M of $35M) | 2026-07-17 | unknown | Don Wilson Jr. on board | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Biobot Analytics | Undisclosed ($13.45M) | 2025-05-23 | unknown | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1809780/000180978025000005/primary_doc.xml) |\n| CompScience | board or observer per bio; no filing names him | | | | [bio](https://www.valorep.com/team/vivek-k-pattipati) |\n| BitGo (non-AI) | director since 2019; IPO 2026-01 | | | | [10-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1740604/000174060426000020/btgo-form10xk.htm) |\n| Republic / OpenDeal (non-AI) | director on the 2021 Form D | 2021-10 | | | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1672732/000167273221000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Sonder (non-AI) | director until 11/2021 | | | | [proxy](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1819395/000181939522000027/a2022proxystatement.htm) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Crusoe:**\n  - Valor led the 2021 Series B with Gracias quoted ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-series-b)).\n  - Pattipati appears as director from the first Form D (2022) and has stayed through the F.\n  - A DRW link runs alongside: DRW VC was in Crusoe's B ([src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-series-b)), and a Donald Wilson Jr. with a Chicago office sits next to Pattipati on the Silicon Derivatives board. Whether that is a working relationship is unconfirmed.\n- **K Health:** he is absent from the 2024-07 Form D and present on the 2026-08 one ([2024](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1689694/000168969424000001/primary_doc.xml)), so the seat is new.\n- No intro stories were found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Antonio Gracias | Valor founder; co-promoter on Funds V–VI and CV Consortio | 2017 | [Fund VI Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1907694/000190769424000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Chase Lochmiller | Crusoe CEO; fellow director | 2022 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467422000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Mark Suster | Fellow Zeitview director | 2026 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1676896/000167689626000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Donald Wilson Jr. (likely DRW's Don Wilson, unconfirmed) | Fellow Silicon Derivatives director | 2026 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Mike Belshe | BitGo CEO; Pattipati is lead independent director | 2019 | [10-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1740604/000174060426000020/btgo-form10xk.htm) |\n| Madison Dearborn Partners | Former employer (Associate to Director) | until 09/2017 | [10-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1740604/000174060426000020/btgo-form10xk.htm) |\n\n**How to reach him:** through Valor's Chicago office (320 N. Sangamon), or through a fellow director: Lochmiller at Crusoe, Suster at Zeitview or Belshe at BitGo. A founder pitching growth compute should expect Pattipati to run the diligence and structuring and Gracias to make the call (inferred from the bio and IC lists).\n\n## Open questions\n- What does Silicon Derivatives do? Did Valor lead its 2026 raise?\n- What is CV Consortio Fund I/II, and why is he a promoter there but not on Fund VII?\n- Does he sit on Valor Atreides AI or VCI investment decisions informally?\n- Is the CompScience seat a board seat or an observer seat?\n- What is his role on Biobot?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.valorep.com/team/vivek-k-pattipati\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1740604/000174060426000020/btgo-form10xk.htm\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1819395/000181939522000027/a2022proxystatement.htm\n- Crusoe Form Ds: [2022](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467422000001/primary_doc.xml), [2023](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467423000001/primary_doc.xml), [2024-02](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467424000001/primary_doc.xml), [2024-11](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467424000003/primary_doc.xml), [2025](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467425000004/primary_doc.xml), [2026-07](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467426000001/primary_doc.xml), [2026-09](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1924674/000192467426000002/primary_doc.xml)\n- K Health Form Ds: [2024](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1689694/000168969424000001/primary_doc.xml), [2026](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1689694/000168969426000002/primary_doc.xml)\n- Zeitview/DroneBase: [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1676896/000167689626000003/primary_doc.xml), [about page](https://www.zeitview.com/about)\n- Silicon Derivatives: [2025](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml), [2026](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml)\n- Biobot: [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1809780/000180978025000005/primary_doc.xml), [site](https://biobot.io)\n- Valor fund Form Ds: [VI](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1907694/000190769424000001/primary_doc.xml), [VII](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2082328/000208232826000002/primary_doc.xml), [Opp II](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2066557/000206655726000001/primary_doc.xml), [Valor Atreides AI I](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2033590/000101297526000109/primary_doc.xml), [VCI](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2074951/000101297526000462/primary_doc.xml), [CV Consortio](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1971232/000196883423000004/primary_doc.xml), [CV Consortio II](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2126869/000212686926000002/primary_doc.xml), [M33 IV](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1773883/000177388319000001/primary_doc.xml)\n- Lanai Holdings I (MDP era): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1652389/000165238915000001/primary_doc.xml\n- EDGAR full-text search for \"Pattipati\": https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Pattipati%22\n", "person:vlad-loktev": "\n# Vlad Loktev\n\n## One-line take\nA part-time Index venture partner and consumer operator (ex-Airbnb, now also a Roblox executive). Their one deal close to the frontier is the Intelligence (Design Arena) model-eval seed, which makes them a read on lab-adjacent deal flow at Index SF, not someone you would meet on a new-lab mega-seed.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** they bring consumer product and marketplace credibility from about 10 years at Airbnb, where they grew the host side and later ran the core business [src](https://fortune.com/2023/11/06/former-airbnb-executive-joins-index-ventures-vlad-loktev/), and they bet on the founder above all (\"it all comes back to Blake\") [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/eyes-that-save-lives-brinc-drones-raises-75m-for-public-safety/).\n- **Where they sit on AI deals:** never the only name. They are the junior co-author next to Danny Rimer on Daydream [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-the-fashion-industry-our-investment-in-daydream/), the senior co-author next to Eryk Dobrushkin on Intelligence [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/), and one of three names on Index's Anthropic page, with Nina Achadjian and Martin Mignot [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/anthropic/). Their only sole-authored posts are non-AI: BRINC and Kindred.\n- **Constraint:** capacity. They hold an operating role as Roblox's Chief Creator Ecosystem Officer alongside the Index seat [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/vlad-loktev/), and have bylined four deal posts in about three years. When they joined, they had not yet led a deal (\"I will absolutely lead my first deal\") [src](https://fortune.com/2023/11/06/former-airbnb-executive-joins-index-ventures-vlad-loktev/).\n- **Would never (inferred):** solo-lead a pre-training lab or chip seed. Nothing in their record touches research-heavy technical diligence.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Joined without having led a deal:** at joining they said \"I will absolutely lead my first deal\" [src](https://fortune.com/2023/11/06/former-airbnb-executive-joins-index-ventures-vlad-loktev/) (Fortune, 2023-11-06). So their AI credits after that date are team deals, not a track record of their own.\n- **Intelligence came in through network pull, not outbound:** three people independently said \"you should meet Grace\" within 24 hours. The team met her that morning and met both founders that evening at Nina Achadjian's apartment [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/) (2026-08-03).\n- **Their AI thesis is about proving grounds, not capability:** \"The constraint isn't intelligence or raw capability.\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/) The post argues models need to be tested under real conditions: \"we must take off the training wheels.\"\n- **Long-tail founder tracking:** BRINC began in 2021, when Index met Blake Resnick as a teenager who had built a fusion reactor. Index wrote a first check that year and came back in the $75M round in 2025: \"we knew right away he was special\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/eyes-that-save-lives-brinc-drones-raises-75m-for-public-safety/). Kindred was mentioned to them in passing about two years before the deal [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-way-to-see-the-world-our-investment-in-kindred/).\n- **Intelligence already had operator-angels on the cap table:** Felicis venture partner Michael Chen lists Design Arena among their angel investments [src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner). This suggests Index was not the first money into the company.\n- Not verified this run: X (the fetch returned HTTP 402), podcasts and Substack. The web-search budget for this run was exhausted.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** Several existing lead rows tag them high; this profile downgrades that. Nothing in their record is pre-training, chips or a new lab. The deal that matters is Intelligence (Design Arena), an eval layer the post says Google, OpenAI, Thinking Machines Lab, xAI and Meta use, so it gives a view into what labs buy. Anthropic is a listing only, with no round or role. Daydream is AI-native consumer commerce. Treat them as a source of lab-adjacent and consumer-AI deal flow at Index SF, not as a co-investor on $20M-into-$600M lab seeds.\n\n## Background\n- BS, Carnegie Mellon; MBA, Harvard Business School [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/vlad-loktev/)\n- Derivatives trading, Morgan Stanley; founded a social survey company; built games at Zynga [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/vlad-loktev/)\n- Airbnb, about 10 years: first growth PM, then VP of Product and GM of core hosting. Helped move bookings to mostly instant book, and ran the core business at Brian Chesky's request [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/vlad-loktev/) [src](https://fortune.com/2023/11/06/former-airbnb-executive-joins-index-ventures-vlad-loktev/)\n- Index Ventures, announced 2023-11-06 [src](https://fortune.com/2023/11/06/former-airbnb-executive-joins-index-ventures-vlad-loktev/)\n- Roblox, Chief Creator Ecosystem Officer, concurrent with Index; start date not found [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/vlad-loktev/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/vlad-loktev/ | Index profile |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/team-member/vlad-loktev/ | Index Perspectives; about 4 bylined deal posts, 2024-2026 |\n| X | https://twitter.com/vladimirloktev | Linked from Index profile; fetch returned 402, follower count not verified |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/vladimirloktev/ | Linked from Index profile |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-05-14: Startup Grind Conference. They interviewed Daydream's Julie Bornstein about AI in shopping and search. The Index page carries the video, with no direct Loktev quotes in the text [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/daydreams-julie-bornstein-and-vlad-loktev-on-how-ai-is-transforming-shopping-and-search/)\n- Other podcasts: none found. Search budget exhausted this run.\n\n## How they think\n- **AI needs real-world proving grounds.** The bottleneck is testing under real conditions, not raw capability (Intelligence post, 2026-08) [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/).\n- **AI will rebuild consumer discovery**, replacing search-based shopping: \"Gone are the days of search-based discovery\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-the-fashion-industry-our-investment-in-daydream/).\n- **Founder-first.** On Daydream they praised \"a chip on her shoulder\" [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/julie-bornsteins-mission-to-reinvent-shopping/); on BRINC, \"it all comes back to Blake\".\n- Calls right or wrong: too early to judge. Intelligence's post-seed traction ($5M to $60M ARR in six months) is reported in Index's own post.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Proving AI in the Real World: Our Investment in Intelligence (2026-08-03). Their only frontier-adjacent thesis [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/)\n2. Eyes That Save Lives: BRINC Drones Raises $75M (2025-04-08). Shows how they track a founder from a teenager to a $75M round [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/eyes-that-save-lives-brinc-drones-raises-75m-for-public-safety/)\n3. Fortune Term Sheet exclusive (2023-11-06). Why they joined, and the admission that they had not yet led a deal [src](https://fortune.com/2023/11/06/former-airbnb-executive-joins-index-ventures-vlad-loktev/)\n4. A New Way to See the World: Our Investment in Kindred (2026-02-03). Their sourcing pattern [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-way-to-see-the-world-our-investment-in-kindred/)\n\n## Social engagement\nNot verified. The X profile could not be fetched (HTTP 402), and the search budget was exhausted this run.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Intelligence (Design Arena) | Seed ($7.9M) | 2026-08-03 | not stated | Eryk Dobrushkin (Index co-author); Nina Achadjian hosted the founder meeting; other firms not named. Michael Chen (Felicis VP) lists an angel position | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/) |\n| Anthropic | not stated (listing only) | not found | no evidence | Index team: Nina Achadjian, Martin Mignot | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/anthropic/) |\n| Daydream | Index post; round not stated | 2024-06-20 | not stated | Danny Rimer (Index co-author, senior) | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-the-fashion-industry-our-investment-in-daydream/) |\n| Daydream | \"$50 million in seed funding\" from Index and others (as reported by Index) | reported 2025-12-03 | not stated | others not named | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/julie-bornsteins-mission-to-reinvent-shopping/) |\n| BRINC | $75M round (Index re-up; first check 2021) | 2025-04-08 | not stated | Motorola Solutions (strategic alliance) | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/eyes-that-save-lives-brinc-drones-raises-75m-for-public-safety/) |\n| Kindred | not stated | 2026-02-03 | not stated | not named; Georgia Stevenson tagged on post | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-way-to-see-the-world-our-investment-in-kindred/) |\n| Linear | not stated (listing only) | not found | no evidence | | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/vlad-loktev/) |\n\nTiming: there is no evidence they were first in or led any AI deal. Index's 2021 BRINC check predates their arrival (2023-11), so the original BRINC deal was not theirs. On Intelligence, an angel (Michael Chen) was already in.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Intelligence (Design Arena), 2026-08:** in one day, three people independently told Index to meet Grace Li. Loktev and Dobrushkin met her that morning and met Li and Kamryn Ohly that evening at Nina Achadjian's apartment. The founders are former Harvard CS students who built a game engine at a hackathon and applied to YC with it. Design Arena then grew to 5.5M users. Index joined the $7.9M seed [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/).\n- **Kindred, 2026-02:** Blake Armstrong mentioned the company in passing about two years earlier. Loktev met cofounder Justine Palefsky at a Jackson Square coffee shop, then spent a full day at the office with Palefsky and Tasneem Amina [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-way-to-see-the-world-our-investment-in-kindred/).\n- **BRINC, 2025-04:** Index met Blake Resnick in 2021 (teenager, garage fusion reactor), invested that year, and backed the $75M round alongside a Motorola Solutions alliance [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/eyes-that-save-lives-brinc-drones-raises-75m-for-public-safety/).\n- **Daydream, 2024-06:** Index had followed Julie Bornstein (ex-Stitch Fix COO) for years. The post is co-written with Danny Rimer [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-the-fashion-industry-our-investment-in-daydream/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Eryk Dobrushkin (Index) | Co-author on Intelligence seed; Loktev the senior name | 2026-08 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/) |\n| Nina Achadjian (Index) | Hosted Intelligence founder meeting; co-listed on Anthropic | 2026-08 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/anthropic/) |\n| Danny Rimer (Index) | Co-author on Daydream; Rimer the senior name | 2024-06 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-the-fashion-industry-our-investment-in-daydream/) |\n| Martin Mignot (Index) | Co-listed on Anthropic | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/companies/anthropic/) |\n| Georgia Stevenson (Index) | Tagged on Kindred post | 2026-02 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-way-to-see-the-world-our-investment-in-kindred/) |\n| Brian Chesky (Airbnb) | Former CEO; asked Loktev to run core business | Airbnb era | [src](https://fortune.com/2023/11/06/former-airbnb-executive-joins-index-ventures-vlad-loktev/) |\n| Roblox | Chief Creator Ecosystem Officer (concurrent) | not stated | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/vlad-loktev/) |\n| Blake Armstrong | Mentioned Kindred to Loktev | about 2024 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-way-to-see-the-world-our-investment-in-kindred/) |\n| Grace Li, Kamryn Ohly | Seed investor in their company (Intelligence) | 2026-08 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/) |\n| Julie Bornstein | Investor in Daydream; interviewed her at Startup Grind | 2024-06 | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/daydreams-julie-bornstein-and-vlad-loktev-on-how-ai-is-transforming-shopping-and-search/) |\n| Blake Resnick | Investor in BRINC (Index first in 2021) | 2025-04 (Loktev post) | [src](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/eyes-that-save-lives-brinc-drones-raises-75m-for-public-safety/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Index's Anthropic entry: which round and year, and what was Loktev's role? Was it Roblox-adjacent or a consumer-AI angle?\n- Did Index lead the Intelligence $7.9M seed? Who were the other investors, and who were the three referrers?\n- Did Index lead Daydream's $50M seed?\n- When did the Roblox role start, and how much time does it leave for Index?\n- Title: \"Partner\" (Fortune 2023) or \"Venture Partner\" (Index site)?\n- X activity and any podcasts. Not checked because the search budget was exhausted.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/vlad-loktev/\n- https://fortune.com/2023/11/06/former-airbnb-executive-joins-index-ventures-vlad-loktev/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/proving-ai-in-the-real-world-our-investment-in-the-intelligence-company/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/bringing-ai-to-the-fashion-industry-our-investment-in-daydream/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/julie-bornsteins-mission-to-reinvent-shopping/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/daydreams-julie-bornstein-and-vlad-loktev-on-how-ai-is-transforming-shopping-and-search/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/eyes-that-save-lives-brinc-drones-raises-75m-for-public-safety/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/a-new-way-to-see-the-world-our-investment-in-kindred/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/companies/anthropic/\n- https://www.felicis.com/blog/expanding-our-investing-team-with-two-partner-promotions-and-a-new-venture-partner (secondary, via repo lead row)\n", "person:weston-reynolds": "\n# Weston Reynolds\n\n## One-line take\nReynolds is the person whose name sits on every Definition SPV. He controls the co-invest LP base that funds the firm's large AI checks, but no source names him on any deal.\n\n## Crux\n- Definition has two founders and two roles.\n  - Teddy Citrin is managing member of the fund GPs and the public face on deals.\n  - Reynolds is managing member of the SPV manager, Definition Opportunity Management LLC.\n- About $465M of the ~$792M regulatory AUM sits in single-deal vehicles with 1-30 outside investors each [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/321942/PDF/321942.pdf). Those vehicles are why a ~$80M Fund I manager can show up in rounds at Cognition, Applied Compute and Flapping Airplanes.\n- What he personally sources, signs or prices is **unconfirmed**. The Form Ds are legal signatures, made by an attorney-in-fact. They are not evidence that he did the deal work.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **A second, unbranded vehicle family.** Since April 2026, Reynolds has also been the related person on \"Telos\" LLCs, which carry no Definition name.\n  - Telos Artifex raised three tranches from 1-2 investors each: $5.08M (first sale 2026-04-22), $3.55M (2026-08) and $15.77M (first sale 2026-10-29). That is about $24.4M in total [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2136482/000213648226000001/primary_doc.xml), [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2136482/000213648226000003/primary_doc.xml).\n  - Telos Astra raised $7.1M from 2 investors (first sale 2026-09-01) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2153772/000215377226000001/primary_doc.xml).\n  - Each filing lists him as \"Manager of Definition Opportunity Management LLC, its Manager\".\n  - Inference, unconfirmed: this looks like a separately managed account for one large LP. The $15.77M tranche starts days before Applied Compute's $350M growth round (2026-11-02), where Definition is a named investor [src](https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise). No filing names the company.\n- **He signs by proxy.** The filings read \"Weston Reynolds by his attorney in fact Elle Christian\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2153772/000215377226000001/primary_doc.xml). The SPV paperwork runs through operations staff.\n- **Scale of the program.** EDGAR full-text search returns 49 Form D filings naming him: Definition Opportunity I-XI, Definition II-A to II-F, Telos Artifex and Telos Astra. The first was Opportunity IA ($3.29M from 11 investors), filed 2021-11-01 [src](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Weston%20Reynolds%22&forms=D), [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1891366/000189136621000001/primary_doc.xml).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- He is not a deal-picking partner you can be co-sourced by.\n- He is the gatekeeper of the capital that lets Definition write $10M+ tickets into frontier-AI rounds:\n  - Cognition: Series A 2024, re-ups through Series E 2026\n  - Applied Compute: seed 2025, growth 2026\n  - Flapping Airplanes: Series A 2026\n- For a $20M-check investor, he matters as a co-invest counterpart and as a view into who the SPV LPs are. He is not a source of seed deal flow.\n\n## Background\n- **Greycroft:** associate, then senior associate, 2014-2020 (aggregator dates) [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/weston-reynolds). His Greycroft team-page URL was captured by archive.org on 2015-01-02 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20150102233301/http://greycroft.com:80/team/weston-reynolds).\n- **Education:** Stanford GSB MBA and UVA BA (aggregator; not confirmed against a primary source) [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/weston-reynolds).\n- **Definition:** co-founded with Teddy Citrin, a fellow Greycroft alum, in 2021.\n  - The management company was formed mid-2021.\n  - The first SPV Form D was filed 2021-11-01 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1891366/000189136621000001/primary_doc.xml).\n  - How the seat was split: Citrin took the fund GPs and Reynolds the SPV manager (from the Form D related-person roles).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://definitioncap.com | No team page (2026-11-10) |\n| X | https://x.com/Weston_Reynolds | About 11 posts, dormant; bio \"Founder @ Definition\" |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | |\n| SEC | https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Weston%20Reynolds%22&forms=D | 49 Form D filings |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNone found.\n\n## How they think\nNothing found: no writing, talks or deal posts.\n\n## Read these first\nNothing found. The Form D filings linked above are the only primary record.\n\n## Social engagement\nX is dormant (about 11 posts); no deal posts or amplification pattern found.\n\n## Portfolio\nNo deals where a source names him personally. For the firm's deals, see [firms/definition.md](../firms/definition.md).\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found: no source describes how any Definition deal reached him.\n\n## How to reach him\n- **Founders:** go through Citrin, who is public on X, or through Lux partners. Lux anchors most Definition AI rounds.\n- **Co-investors and LPs:** Reynolds is the natural counterpart for SPV allocation. The firm address on every filing is 134 West 4th St, Unit A, New York.\n- No public email or office hours found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Definition | Founder & GP; manager of the SPV manager | 2021 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1891366/000189136621000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Greycroft | Associate → Senior Associate | 2014-2020 | [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/weston-reynolds) |\n| Teddy Citrin | Co-founder; Greycroft colleague | 2014 or earlier | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20211202154204/https://www.greycroft.com/team) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who is the Telos LP, and which companies do Telos Artifex and Telos Astra hold?\n- Does Reynolds sit on any investment committee vote, or only run capital formation?\n- Greycroft deals he worked on: the archive was offline on 2026-11-10, so this is unchecked.\n\n## Checked, nothing found\n- Portfolio announcements: Together, Modal, Cognition, Applied Compute, Phonic, TerraFirma, tldraw, Firefly and Flapping Airplanes name no Reynolds quote.\n- definitioncap.com has no team page.\n- His X has no deal posts.\n- No podcasts or talks found.\n- Wayback Greycroft pages: the archive returned \"Temporarily Offline\" on 2026-11-10.\n\n## Sources\n- https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%22Weston%20Reynolds%22&forms=D\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1891366/000189136621000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2136482/000213648226000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2136482/000213648226000003/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2153772/000215377226000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/321942/PDF/321942.pdf\n- https://signal.nfx.com/investors/weston-reynolds\n- https://appliedcompute.com/company/100m-run-rate-fundraise\n", "person:will-gaybrick": "\n# Will Gaybrick\n\n## One-line take\nThe Thrive partner who wrote Thrive's first big check ($30M into Stripe, 2014), then left to run finance and later product at Stripe. Today he is a small-check angel whose recent bets sit in AI infrastructure and AI agents. He is worth following as a signal and an intro path into Stripe and Thrive, not as a co-investor.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** an engineer-lawyer-investor hybrid that founders trust. Patrick Collison recruited him from the cap table by text. The Collisons had known him \"a couple of years\" as an investor before that ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)).\n- **Network:** his angel cheques land in rounds next to OpenAI, Thrive, Lightspeed and 20VC (Chai seed 2024, Substrate Labs seed 2024, ArchAstro pre-seed 2026). That suggests founders seek him for Stripe-scale product and go-to-market judgment.\n- **Constraint:** he is a full-time Stripe executive running product engineering, partnerships, risk and go-to-market operations ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)). His checks are $10K-$100K (aggregator: [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/will-gaybrick)), he has no fund, and no LP position has been found.\n- **What he'd never do (by his own account):** go back to full-time VC. He calls himself \"an accidental VC\" ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)). He also says his default plan before Stripe was to start a company.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He was hired into Thrive as its CTO, not as an investor.** Kushner cold-called him while he was still in school and working as an engineer at a Thrive portfolio company. Gaybrick: \"he reached out because he wanted a CTO for the fund.\" He built Thrive's first CRM, then moved to doing deals as Thrive grew from a ~$5M fund to \"a few billion\" ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and), The Peel, 2025-11-13). This resolves the `needs-id` \"Will Gabric\" lead from the Hankes episode.\n- **Thrive's earliest money, in his words:** \"It was Josh and Jared, a little bit of individuals money.\" He adds that some General Catalyst partners were involved ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)). This matches, as a first-hand account, the General Catalyst/Joel Cutler LP row already on the Thrive firm page.\n- **The Stripe CFO offer came by text.** Patrick asked \"do you want to come be CFO?\" Gaybrick replied it \"seems like a really bad idea\" and was told the Collisons thought he could do it ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)). He also admits he probably wasn't the right CFO to take Stripe public.\n- **His B2B screen, with his own deal as the counter-example.** He looks for huge, universal markets where buyers care about picking the best product. He names his own Thrive deal Greenhouse as universal \"but maybe not core\" ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)).\n- **How Stripe's 2014 underwriting played out:** \"Stripe was ahead of my projections on volume\" but slightly below on gross margin, i.e. take rate net of network fees ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)).\n- **Contrarian view:** \"it is great to be a low margin business.\" His reason is that low margins keep a company obsessed with customers ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)).\n- **Hiring and diligence rule:** \"the most important input into hiring is back channels and references\" ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)).\n- **The Grimshaw link, corrected.** Gaybrick ran Hack Yale, an unofficial coding course, while at Yale Law. He says Miles Grimshaw \"was one of my TAs\" ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)). The repo's Grimshaw profile currently says Grimshaw was a student in the class (per Fortune).\n- **Segment board seat during the handover.** On 2015-10-09, TechCrunch described him as \"CFO of Stripe and G.P. at Thrive Capital\" in the same line, as he joined Segment's board on a Thrive-led Series B ([src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=1221699)). So he took a Thrive board seat the same day his Stripe CFO move was reported ([src](https://www.pymnts.com/news/2015/stripe-snags-will-gaybrick-as-first-cfo/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is not a frontier-AI co-investor. He writes ~$50K angel checks and runs no fund. He is high value as a signal and a node:\n- His recent angel deals are AI. Chai Discovery seed (2024, alongside OpenAI and Thrive), Substrate Labs seed (2024, AI-deployment API by ex-Stripe founders) and ArchAstro pre-seed (2026, cross-company AI agents for software integration) all sit in the owner's AI-automation and AI-infra lanes.\n- He is a likely magnet for ex-Stripe founders (Substrate Labs and ArchAstro both have Stripe alumni on the founding teams). Stripe alumni are a known source of AI-automation founders.\n- He is a direct line to Kushner and Grimshaw at Thrive, and to the Collisons.\n\nHe would not lead or anchor a $600M lab seed. As an angel name on a cap table, he signals that the Stripe and Thrive networks are involved.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** A.B. in theoretical mathematics, cum laude, Harvard College. J.D., Yale Law School, where he won the William K. S. Wang prize in corporate law ([src](https://launchpad.la/?p=2794)). He was on The Facebook \"when it was Harvard only\" ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)).\n- **2007, Blackstone:** analyst for about 18 months ([src](https://launchpad.la/?p=2794)).\n- **Engineering roles:**\n  - Data scientist and backend engineer at Hunch, which eBay acquired in 2011 ([src](https://launchpad.la/?p=2794)).\n  - Software engineer at Jumo ([src](https://www.pymnts.com/news/2015/stripe-snags-will-gaybrick-as-first-cfo/)).\n  - Aggregator profiles also list Milewise ([src](https://vcdir.com/angels/bff1f5f7-bfc7-45c0-bd53-759cef2fd58e)).\n  - Which of these was the Thrive portfolio company where Kushner found him is not stated.\n- **At Yale Law (dates not given):** ran Hack Yale, an unofficial coding course. A third of Yale undergrads applied in a week ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)).\n- **2011-07 to 2015-10, Thrive Capital:** joined as the fund's CTO, then General Partner ([src](https://launchpad.la/?p=2794), [src](https://www.pymnts.com/news/2015/stripe-snags-will-gaybrick-as-first-cfo/)). In Oct 2014 TechCrunch named him among Thrive's partners \"all in their 20s\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2014/10/06/thrive-400m)).\n- **2015-10, Stripe:** first CFO, joining a finance team of about five ([src](https://www.pymnts.com/news/2015/stripe-snags-will-gaybrick-as-first-cfo/), [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)).\n- **2018-12:** CFO to chief product officer and head of payments ([src](https://www.businessinsider.in/the-cfo-of-20-billion-stripe-is-now-its-chief-product-officer-and-he-says-his-mission-is-to-shield-customers-from-the-highly-complex-and-esoteric-global-financial-system/articleshow/66960152.cms); text read via a Dec 2018 [repost](https://medialandingpage.blogspot.com/2018/12/20-billion-startup-stripe-has-given-its.html)).\n- **2023-02:** promoted to president of product and business ([src](https://www.americanbanker.com/articles/stripe-cfo-suryadevara-to-depart-firm-to-handle-family-matters)).\n- **Current title:** President, Technology and Business. He explains the rename on The Peel (2025-11) ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)), and Stripe's Sessions keynote page (2026-04-29) uses the same title ([src](https://stripe.com/sessions/2026/opening-remarks-and-product)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | No personal site found |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | not verified | No handle confirmed this pass |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | Not fetched |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | Guest only, see below |\n| Stripe Sessions 2026 keynote | https://stripe.com/sessions/2026/opening-remarks-and-product | Verified 2026-11-10; keynote 2026-04-29 |\n| Signal NFX angel profile | https://signal.nfx.com/investors/will-gaybrick | Verified 2026-11-10 (aggregator) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-11 — The Peel with Turner Novak — \"Inside Stripe: Stablecoins, AI, and (not) Going Public\" — https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and — full transcript. Covers how he joined Thrive's ~$5M fund as CTO (1:08:23), the low-margin thesis (1:12:30), the Stripe CFO text (1:16:30), and Stripe's payments foundation model and AI products.\n- 2024-09 — The Logan Bartlett Show EP 118 — \"Will Gaybrick (President of Product and Business, Stripe) on Capital Allocation, Org Design, AI, and Global Growth\" — https://theloganbartlettshow.simplecast.com/episodes/ep-118-will-gaybrick-president-stripe-on-capital-allocation-org-design-ai-and-global-growth-oTfz6lmf — transcript not retrieved (403s).\n- 2026-04-29 — Stripe Sessions opening and product keynote — https://stripe.com/sessions/2026/opening-remarks-and-product\n- 2018-09 — Bloomberg TV, as Stripe CFO, on larger companies moving to Stripe — https://www.bloomberg.com/news/videos/2018-09-26/larger-companies-are-turning-to-stripe-cfo-says-video (not watched)\n\n## How they think\n- **B2B market screen:** he wants an enormous market, a product everyone needs, and buyers who agonize over which one to pick. He backed Stripe because developers cared about speed and quality, and merchants were starting to as well ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)).\n- **Low margins are a feature:** they force customer obsession, and every new product is accretive. He is bullish on stablecoins as a margin lever for marketplaces (same src).\n- **Sales is reality:** he urges product leaders to get close to go-to-market. He says sales-team design is like a chipset you can change only once a year (same src).\n- **Hiring:** back channels and track record matter more than interviews (same src).\n- **AI:** build versus buy depends on how strategic a capability is. Stripe built an internal coding-agent tool (\"Minions\") and shipped a payments foundation model in mid-2025 (same src).\n- **Calls:** Stripe at $3.5B in 2014 was right; Stripe's 2026 tender valued it at ~$159B (secondary, per search; not re-verified). Maple (Thrive led the seed while he was a partner) shut down in 2017 and was sold to Deliveroo ([src](https://venturebeat.com/technology/food-delivery-startup-maple-shuts-down-and-sells-to-deliveroo-after-raising-29-million)).\n\n## Read these first\n- The Peel transcript (2025-11) — his only long first-person account of Thrive's early days and the Stripe move. https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and\n- TechCrunch, \"Thrive Capital Bet Big ($30 Million, To Be Exact) On Stripe\" (2014-12-04) — his signature deal. https://techcrunch.com/2014/12/04/thrive-capital-bet-big-30-million-to-be-exact-on-stripe/\n- PRNewswire, Justworks Series A (2014-10-10) — his own quote as a Thrive GP. https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/thrive-capital-and-index-ventures-invest-in-justworks-next-generation-peo-platform-278775451.html\n- Logan Bartlett EP 118 (2024-09) — capital allocation and org design (transcript not retrieved).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No public X or Substack presence was confirmed. His public voice is podcasts and Stripe keynotes.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Justworks | Series A ($6M) | 2014-10-10 | Thrive led; Gaybrick joined board | Index Ventures | [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/thrive-capital-and-index-ventures-invest-in-justworks-next-generation-peo-platform-278775451.html) (row already in `thrive-capital-fullbook-deals.csv`) |\n| Stripe | Series C ($70M at $3.5B); Thrive check $30M | 2014-12-04 | Yes, Gaybrick led for Thrive | not named | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2014/12/04/thrive-capital-bet-big-30-million-to-be-exact-on-stripe/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2477794) |\n| Greenhouse | Series C ($35M) | 2015-08-25 | Thrive led; Gaybrick quoted (per search excerpt of release) | Benchmark, Social+Capital, Groupe Arnault | [src](https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2015/08/25/1224369/0/en/Greenhouse-Raises-35-Million-in-Latest-Round-of-Funding.html) (403; firm row exists) |\n| Segment | Series B ($27M) | 2015-10-09 | Thrive led; Gaybrick joined board | Accel, KPCB, Jon Winkelried | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=1221699) |\n| Maple | Seed ($4M), Thrive led | 2014 | Thrive led; his role unclear (an aggregator lists him as co-founder) | — | [src](https://fortune.com/2015/03/20/food-startup-maple-funding), [src](https://vcdir.com/angels/bff1f5f7-bfc7-45c0-bd53-759cef2fd58e) |\n| Substrate Labs (row company `Substrate.run`, slug substrate-run, to avoid the chip company `substrate`) | Seed ($8M) | 2024-06-20 | No (Lightspeed led), angel | South Park Commons, Craft, Guillermo Rauch, Immad Akhund | [src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20240620159165/en) (403; per search excerpt), [src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/will-gaybrick) |\n| Chai Discovery | Seed (~$30M) | 2024-09-09 | No (Thrive, OpenAI, Dimension co-led), angel | Greg Brockman, Fred Ehrsam, Kevin and Julia Hartz | [src](https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement) |\n| ArchAstro | Pre-seed ($6.2M) | 2026-06-09 | No, angel | 20VC, Kyber Knight, Claire Hughes Johnson, Deb Liu, Vijaye Raji | [src](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/archastro-raises-6-2m-pre-seed/) |\n\nCB Insights shows 8 angel investments: hidden rounds dated 2023-11-06 (seed), 2021-09-13 (Series D) and 2021-08-25 (seed II). Signal NFX shows 18. Neither lists the companies without a login ([src](https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/will-gaybrick), [src](https://signal.nfx.com/investors/will-gaybrick)).\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Stripe (2014):** Gaybrick led the $70M round and wrote a $30M check. TechCrunch called it by far Thrive's largest single investment, from a firm whose last fund was $400M ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2014/12/04/thrive-capital-bet-big-30-million-to-be-exact-on-stripe/)). His thesis: a huge, universal market, developers who care about quality, and the founders themselves ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)). About ten months later Patrick Collison texted him the CFO offer ([src](https://www.pymnts.com/news/2015/stripe-snags-will-gaybrick-as-first-cfo/)). The deal is the root of Thrive's later ~$1.8B Stripe position in 2023 (see the Thrive firm page).\n- **Justworks (2014):** Thrive led the $6M Series A and Gaybrick took the board seat. He said \"There's a lot of buzz in the HR space\" ([src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/thrive-capital-and-index-ventures-invest-in-justworks-next-generation-peo-platform-278775451.html)). Thrive's Jared Weinstein later held the Justworks board seat from 2015 (aggregator: [src](https://it.marketscreener.com/insider/JARED-WEINSTEIN-A2MBJ9)). That fits Gaybrick handing it off when he left.\n- **Thrive hire (2011):** Kushner cold-called him while he was still in school, through his engineering job at a portfolio company ([src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Thrive Capital | Fund CTO, then General Partner (2011-07 to 2015-10) | 2011 | [src](https://launchpad.la/?p=2794), [src](https://www.pymnts.com/news/2015/stripe-snags-will-gaybrick-as-first-cfo/) |\n| Josh Kushner | Recruited him by cold call for the fund CTO role | 2011 | [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and) |\n| Jared Weinstein (Thrive) | Early Thrive colleague (\"Josh and Jared\") | 2011 | [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and) |\n| Miles Grimshaw | His Hack Yale TA; brought Grimshaw around Thrive in 2013 | ~2012 | [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and), [src](https://colossus.com/article/joshua-kushner-thrive-new-world/) |\n| Patrick and John Collison | Investor, then executive; recruited as CFO by Patrick's text | 2014 | [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and) |\n| Stripe | CFO 2015, CPO 2018, President 2023 to now | 2015-10 | [src](https://www.pymnts.com/news/2015/stripe-snags-will-gaybrick-as-first-cfo/) |\n| Claire Hughes Johnson | Ex-Stripe COO; helped brief The Peel host; co-angel in ArchAstro | — | [src](https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and), [src](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/archastro-raises-6-2m-pre-seed/) |\n| Isaac Oates (Justworks) | Board member as Thrive GP | 2014-10 | [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/thrive-capital-and-index-ventures-invest-in-justworks-next-generation-peo-platform-278775451.html) |\n| Segment | Board member | 2015-10 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=1221699) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Was he really a Maple co-founder while a Thrive GP? Only an aggregator says so. Fortune names Merkl and Navle as founders.\n- Was he on Oscar's board? A Foundersuite profile claims it (403, not read). Oscar's proxies list Kushner only.\n- Which Thrive portfolio company employed him when Kushner called (Hunch or Jumo)?\n- The hidden 2021 and 2023 angel deals on CB Insights.\n- Is he an LP in any fund, for example Thrive? Not found.\n- Who is \"Big Rose,\" his Hack Yale co-teacher? This is a transcription artifact, recorded as a needs-id lead.\n- Logan Bartlett EP 118 transcript (blocked).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.thespl.it/p/inside-stripe-stablecoins-ai-and (The Peel, 2025-11-13; full transcript via Substack API)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2014/12/04/thrive-capital-bet-big-30-million-to-be-exact-on-stripe/\n- https://techcrunch.com/?p=2477794 (2023-01-31; \"Series C\" label)\n- https://techcrunch.com/2022/02/17/new-yorks-thrive-capital-closes-its-eighth-fund-with-a-whopping-3-billion\n- https://techcrunch.com/2014/10/06/thrive-400m\n- https://techcrunch.com/?p=1221699 (Segment, 2015-10-09)\n- https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/thrive-capital-and-index-ventures-invest-in-justworks-next-generation-peo-platform-278775451.html (upgrade over the MarketScreener source on the existing Justworks row)\n- https://www.pymnts.com/news/2015/stripe-snags-will-gaybrick-as-first-cfo/\n- https://www.cnbc.com/will-gaybrick-stripe/\n- https://www.americanbanker.com/articles/stripe-cfo-suryadevara-to-depart-firm-to-handle-family-matters\n- https://medialandingpage.blogspot.com/2018/12/20-billion-startup-stripe-has-given-its.html (repost of Business Insider, Dec 2018)\n- https://stripe.com/sessions/2026/opening-remarks-and-product\n- https://launchpad.la/?p=2794 (~2013 mentor bio)\n- https://www.chaidiscovery.com/news/seed-announcement\n- https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/archastro-raises-6-2m-pre-seed/\n- https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/will-gaybrick, https://signal.nfx.com/investors/will-gaybrick, https://vcdir.com/angels/bff1f5f7-bfc7-45c0-bd53-759cef2fd58e (aggregators)\n- https://fortune.com/2015/03/20/food-startup-maple-funding, https://venturebeat.com/technology/food-delivery-startup-maple-shuts-down-and-sells-to-deliveroo-after-raising-29-million\n", "person:will-reed": "\n# Will Reed\n\n## One-line take\nSpark's growth GP behind Baseten, Abridge and Scale. He has run one infrastructure rule since 2019, \"painful for everyone but strategic for no one\", and in 2026 he led a $1B-valuation Series A in AI data-center power (TAR).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** Courtships that last years, then a growth-sized lead. He met Benchling's CEO in 2015 and invested in 2020 [src](https://medium.com/@willreed_21/welcome-benchling-4712aeb27937). He knew Scale for about two years before its 2019 round [src](https://medium.com/spark-capital/welcome-scale-aa3acd946688). He met Baseten while it was still a no-code product, and Spark had been \"hoping\" for years to get into Mercury [src](https://x.com/willreed/status/1904922332801769794). He also uses Spark's portfolio as a sourcing and diligence engine: Dooly came from raving users inside portfolio companies, and Baseten from portfolio companies all hitting the same inference wall [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SYNEqUGMGFQ).\n- **Constraint:** He is a Series B/C growth investor (bio), so he arrives after the seed. At Baseten, Greylock led the A before Spark came in.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** Bet on owning a capability the customer can't outsource, or join the \"incumbents win AI\" consensus. He says he watched that view fail in 2024–25 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SYNEqUGMGFQ).\n\n## Differential insight\n- Baseten origin: Tuhin Srivastava ended their first Zoom by saying \"just so you know, I wouldn't invest in us right now\". Five or six months later, over coffee, he pitched the inference pivot [yt 0:43](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SYNEqUGMGFQ).\n- His infra rule, carried over from pre-AI IT-infra deals: back problems that are \"painful for everyone but strategic for no one\" [yt 6:38](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SYNEqUGMGFQ). He wrote the same thesis about Scale in 2019: \"outsource an ops-intensive part of their development cycle\" [memo](https://medium.com/spark-capital/welcome-scale-aa3acd946688).\n- A 2019 call: \"access to training data — not better algorithms — will determine the winners\" [memo](https://medium.com/spark-capital/welcome-scale-aa3acd946688).\n- On the circa-2024 VC consensus: \"the cool VC thing to say was you know AI is an enabling technology\". AI-native startups won instead [yt 7:58](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SYNEqUGMGFQ).\n- Real AI apps are \"stringing together, you know, dozens of models\", so the open-vs-closed debate misses the point [yt 4:07](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SYNEqUGMGFQ).\n- Dooly sourcing: \"We first heard about @DoolyHQ from *raving* users inside the Spark portfolio\" [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1395358514671853568).\n- TAR (Sep 2026, Spark led the $120M A at $1B): power is \"the main bottleneck to scaling compute\" [press](https://pulse2.com/tar-raises-120-million-series-a-at-1-billion-valuation-to-build-off-grid-power-for-ai-data-centers/).\n- 2020-01-30: a burst of congratulations tagged Reed together with Yasmin Razavi [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1222947096409640968). The Anthropic S-1 dates Razavi's GP title to March 2020, so this was probably a joint GP promotion. Not confirmed for Reed.\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-11)\n- The rule is consistent across six years: Scale in 2019 and Baseten in 2025 rest on the same \"outsource the non-core, ops-heavy AI step to a best-in-class vendor\" thesis [memo](https://medium.com/spark-capital/welcome-scale-aa3acd946688), [yt](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SYNEqUGMGFQ).\n- He says the inference market is \"*unbelievably* early\" even with Baseten leading. He names Abridge, OpenEvidence and Clay as Baseten customers, so his portfolio companies feed each other [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1963997459484496243).\n- On fintech: Mercury's market \"would be won and lost on product, not marketing\" [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1904922331618959757). At growth stage he weights the team most: \"the most important 'product' a founder can build is their team\" [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1904936843592032691).\n- He frames COVID as a pull-forward rather than a new thesis: \"we continue to invest behind the same trends\" (CNBC, 2020) [yt 3:31](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FsPg3x53LXk). Axis Security got the same framing [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1374064922548572160).\n- He is not a podcast guest. Across Apple Podcasts, YouTube, Spark's site and his own site, the only long-form audio or video found is a 9-minute Baseten interview (2025) and a 5-minute CNBC segment (2020). His voice lives in Spark Medium memos (2018–2020) and X threads (@willreed, formerly @willreed_21).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He writes growth checks (Series B/C), not seeds into new labs. What raises the fit: the Baseten B and C co-leads, early Scale conviction (2019), Abridge (Spark-led B, 2023) and now TAR, AI data-center power at a $1B Series A ([press](https://pulse2.com/tar-raises-120-million-series-a-at-1-billion-valuation-to-build-off-grid-power-for-ai-data-centers/)). That makes him a natural follow-on buyer for AI-infra companies the owner seeds. What lowers it: Underdog, Mercury, Headway, SmileDirectClub and SmartRent are generalist growth bets.\n\n## Background\n- Amherst College, BA History [bio](https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-members/will-reed).\n- High-yield credit, then leveraged buyouts, across tech, healthcare, financial services and energy. No source names the employers [bio](https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-members/will-reed).\n- Joined Spark in 2015 as the first growth fund started, moving to SF less than a week before meeting Benchling's CEO [src](https://medium.com/@willreed_21/welcome-benchling-4712aeb27937), [yt 0:11](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SYNEqUGMGFQ).\n- General Partner, Growth (date not confirmed; see Differential insight). Forbes 2024 Midas Brink list [press](https://www.forbes.com/sites/truebridge/2024/06/04/venture-capitals-up-and-comers-the-2024-midas-brink-list/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.willreed.fyi/ | Bio plus portfolio logos (Abridge, Baseten, Scale, Discord, Headway, Handshake, Mercury, Cardless, Formation Bio, Bird, Underdog, Teamshares) |\n| Substack / blog | https://medium.com/@willreed_21 | 6 memos, 2018-10 to 2020-10; dormant since |\n| X | https://x.com/willreed | Formerly @willreed_21. Mostly portfolio threads; about 740 archived tweets 2016–2025 |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2020-12 — CNBC (Shepard Smith) / Squawk Pod — MessageBird in the $1B+ club — [yt](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FsPg3x53LXk), [podcast](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/boeing-737-max-return-the-%241b-club-messagebird/id1480890290?i=1000503813907) — COVID pulled digital adoption forward; MessageBird as the consolidator.\n- 2025-05 — Baseten YouTube — \"Building in the age of AI\" with Tuhin Srivastava — [yt](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SYNEqUGMGFQ) — Baseten origin, the \"painful for everyone\" rule, multi-model apps, AI natives beating incumbents.\n- 2025-09 — ELC Annual (SF) — speaker; session not named — [src](https://sfelc.com/speaker/will-reed).\n\n## How they think\n- AI infra: back the universally painful, non-strategic layer that customers outsource (data labelling in 2019, inference in 2022–25, power in 2026).\n- Data over algorithms (2019 Scale memo).\n- AI-native startups beat incumbents in the first wave, but the enterprise will \"turn on\" later.\n- Consolidation theses: one global omnichannel vendor wins (MessageBird, 2020).\n- Calls: Scale and Baseten were early and right. SmileDirectClub (2018 memo) was a consumer-health bet; outcome not tracked here.\n\n## Read these first\n1. Welcome, Scale! (2019): the data-over-algorithms thesis [memo](https://medium.com/spark-capital/welcome-scale-aa3acd946688).\n2. Building in the age of AI (2025): the Baseten origin and his infra rule [yt](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SYNEqUGMGFQ).\n3. Welcome, Benchling! (2020): how a five-year courtship works [memo](https://medium.com/@willreed_21/welcome-benchling-4712aeb27937).\n4. Baseten X thread (2025-09-05): inference as core infra [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1963997456452243467).\n5. Welcome, Instabase! (2019): why RPA is constrictive [memo](https://medium.com/spark-capital/welcome-instabase-751b373d8c6d).\n\n## Social engagement\nHe posts portfolio milestones in multi-part threads, plus cycling, mountain biking and his dog. He is friendly with Sequoia's Andrew Reed (\"proud to call both these guys friends\" [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1321544150567718912)) and retweets Anthropic, Baseten, Abridge and Benchling heavily (archive count).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| SmileDirectClub | Undisclosed | 2018-10 | unknown | CD&R; Kleiner Perkins | [memo](https://medium.com/spark-capital/welcome-smiledirectclub-90f68c88f0d) |\n| Scale AI | Undisclosed | 2019-08 | no | Founders Fund; Thrive; Coatue; Index; Accel | [memo](https://medium.com/spark-capital/welcome-scale-aa3acd946688) |\n| Instabase | Undisclosed | 2019-10 | unknown | Index; Greylock; NEA; a16z | [memo](https://medium.com/spark-capital/welcome-instabase-751b373d8c6d) |\n| SmartRent | Undisclosed | 2020-05 | unknown | Bain Capital Ventures; RET; Fifth Wall; EIP; Alexa Fund | [memo](https://medium.com/@willreed_21/welcome-smartrent-73441434d7f6) |\n| Benchling | Undisclosed | 2020-05 | unknown | Alkeon; Thrive; Benchmark; Lux; ICONIQ | [memo](https://medium.com/@willreed_21/welcome-benchling-4712aeb27937) |\n| MessageBird | Series C | 2020-10 | yes (board) | | [yt](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FsPg3x53LXk) |\n| Axis Security | Series C ($50M) | 2021-03 | yes | Canaan; Ten Eleven; Cyberstarts | [release](https://www.newswire.ca/news-releases/axis-security-accelerates-growth-with-series-c-funding-led-by-spark-capital-raises-100-million-in-first-year-with-disruptive-platform-that-transforms-secure-remote-access-819105445.html) |\n| Clarify Health | Undisclosed (board) | 2021-03 | unknown | Insight Partners | [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1371896616026656771) |\n| Handshake | Series E ($80M) | 2021-05 | co-led | Lightspeed | [TC](https://techcrunch.com/2021/05/12/handshake-raises-80m-at-a-1-5b-valuation-as-its-diversity-focused-recruitment-network-for-grads-passes-18m-users/) |\n| Dooly | Series B ($80M) | 2021-05 | yes | Greenspring; Tiger; Lachy Groom | [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1395358519918948354) |\n| Headway | Series C ($125M) | 2023-10 | yes (Spark) | Thrive; Accel; a16z | [src](https://www.gunder.com/en/news-insights/client-news/spark-capital-led-125m-series-c-financing-of-headway) |\n| Abridge | Series B ($30M) | 2023-10 | yes | Bessemer; CVS Health Ventures | [BW](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20231026850279/en/Abridge-raises-30M-to-Accelerate-Adoption-of-its-Proven-Generative-AI-Solution-across-U.S.-Healthcare-Systems) |\n| Baseten | Series B ($40M) | 2024-03 | co-led | IVP | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=647917) |\n| Mercury | Series C | 2025-03 | unknown | Sequoia | [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1904922329937027528) |\n| Underdog | Series C | 2025-03 | yes (Spark) | | [BW](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250326332418/en/Spark-Capital-Leads-Underdog-Series-C-Pushing-Valuation-Over-$1.2-Billion) |\n| TAR | Series A ($120M at $1B) | 2026-09 | yes (Spark) | | [press](https://pulse2.com/tar-raises-120-million-series-a-at-1-billion-valuation-to-build-off-grid-power-for-ai-data-centers/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Baseten:** First Zoom about three years before May 2025 (circa 2022). The no-code product had $1M+ ARR, and Tuhin said not to invest yet. Five or six months later Tuhin pitched the inference pivot over coffee in SF. Spark's portfolio companies all had the same inference problem, early customers vouched, and Spark \"took the plunge\" [yt](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SYNEqUGMGFQ).\n- **Benchling:** Met Saji Wickramasekara in Reed's first week in SF (2015) and invested in 2020 [memo](https://medium.com/@willreed_21/welcome-benchling-4712aeb27937).\n- **Dooly:** Sourced from portfolio sales reps who said they would quit or pay out of pocket to keep it [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1395358514671853568).\n- **Handshake:** Spark led the B in 2016 (Megan Quinn's seat). Reed co-led the E five years later [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1392540416210137089).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Spark Capital | General Partner, Growth | 2015 | [bio](https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-members/will-reed) |\n| Tuhin Srivastava (Baseten) | investor; Baseten co-director with Sarah Guo | ~2022 | [yt](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SYNEqUGMGFQ) |\n| Megan Quinn | former Spark colleague, \"mentor\" | 2015–2020 | [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1250841290566057984) |\n| Andrew Reed (Sequoia) | friend | 2019 | [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1321544150567718912) |\n| Yasmin Razavi | Spark growth co-GP | 2017 | [x](https://x.com/willreed/status/1222947096409640968) |\n| IVP | repeat co-lead (Baseten B, C) | 2024 | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=647917) |\n| Alexandr Wang (Scale) | investor since 2019, known since ~2017 | 2017 | [memo](https://medium.com/spark-capital/welcome-scale-aa3acd946688) |\n\n## Open questions\n- His exact GP promotion date (likely early 2020, alongside Razavi).\n- Is he on the TAR board, and who sourced TAR?\n- Did Spark follow on in Abridge's 2024–25 mega-rounds?\n- Who were his credit and LBO employers before 2015?\n- What was his ELC Annual 2025 session?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-members/will-reed\n- https://www.willreed.fyi/\n- https://medium.com/@willreed_21 (feed: 6 memos)\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SYNEqUGMGFQ\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FsPg3x53LXk\n- https://x.com/willreed (archived tweets via the Wayback CDX)\n- https://pulse2.com/tar-raises-120-million-series-a-at-1-billion-valuation-to-build-off-grid-power-for-ai-data-centers/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm (Razavi GP date)\n- https://sfelc.com/speaker/will-reed\n", "person:willem-van-den-bosch": "\n# Willem van den Bosch\n\n*Created 2026-11-10 at hop depth 1 from `firms/triatomic.md`.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the only non-partner investor with a Principal title at a fund that writes $10–20M first checks into AI compute, which makes him the owner's most likely working-level contact at Triatomic. No deal is publicly attributed to him, so follow him for access, not as a deal signal.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** Triatomic is small (about $346M regulatory AUM) and partner-heavy. Its team page lists three GPs, one Principal (him) and one Associate ([triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai)). Below the GPs, he is the investor most likely to run diligence and syndicate calls on the compute cluster: MatX, d-Matrix, Gimlet, Efficient, Volantis and iPronics (see `firms/triatomic.md`).\n- **What he brings:** A quantitative, operating-finance background rather than research or engineering. At a Los Angeles long/short fund, black-and-white Capital, he used alternative data across consumer goods, trucking and logistics, mobile gaming and internet. At DoorDash he did driver-supply and logistics modeling before the IPO ([Soma bio, archived](http://web.archive.org/web/20221201043541/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/)). Nothing found shows a technical background in chips or ML research.\n- **Constraints:** He has no public deal attribution, no public writing and no X presence. Public Triatomic announcements name the GPs (Zhou, Huber, Hochberg), never him, so his personal sourcing record can't be checked. He was hired directly as Principal (2024), and no promotion has been announced.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred from the firm):** sub-$5M apps-layer seeds. Triatomic's entry checks are $10–20M.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The two employers describe his Soma role differently.** Soma's own page said he \"joined Soma Capital in 2021 and focuses on growth-stage investments\" ([Soma, archived 2022-12-01](http://web.archive.org/web/20221201043541/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/)). Triatomic's bio says he \"helped lead early stage investments in a broad array of early stage businesses\" ([triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai)). Same job, different framing. The live Soma page now returns 404, so only the archive shows the original. His Soma title there was \"Soma Investor\".\n- **The site rebuild that came with his arrival was built in a Webflow project named after him.** Through 2024-09 triatomic.ai was a Squarespace site ([archived 2024-09-03](http://web.archive.org/web/20240903125232/https://triatomic.ai/)). By 2024-10-08 it had moved to Webflow, with a stylesheet called `willems-fantastic-site-2d4a47.webflow.63ebd7429.min.css`, and he appeared on the team list as \"Principal\" ([archived 2024-10-08](http://web.archive.org/web/20241008150812/https://triatomic.ai/)). That fits his building the firm's site in his first weeks, but this is inference from a filename. The current site is a Next.js build.\n- **Hired in as Principal, not promoted to it.** His dedicated Triatomic bio page is first archived 2024-11-03 with the title \"Principal\" and the same text as today ([archived bio](http://web.archive.org/web/20241103135522/https://www.triatomic.ai/team/willem-van-den-bosch)). The old Squarespace /team page listed only the three GPs as late as 2024-09-05 ([archived](http://web.archive.org/web/20240905210032/https://www.triatomic.ai/team)), so its omission of him proves nothing about his start date. Aggregators put his Soma exit in 2024 ([Zonebourse](https://www.zonebourse.com/insider/WILLEM-VAN-DEN-BOSCH-A3PBGT/), aggregator).\n- **A Soma-to-Triatomic pipeline, in people and in a deal.** Triatomic's site-listed CFO, Dhruv Bavaria, is also ex-Soma ([triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai)). Coworker, an AI-agent company on Triatomic's portfolio page, raised its pre-seed from Soma Capital, Focal VC, Mischief and Karman. Its $13M seed (announced 2025-05-21) was led by Jeff Huber of Triatomic ([pulse2](https://pulse2.com/coworker-ai-13-million-raised-for-ai-agent-research-company/amp/); [release via Observer-Reporter](https://stocks.observer-reporter.com/observerreporter/article/abnewswire-2025-5-23-coworker-secures-13m-to-launch-first-ai-teammate-with-built-in-organizational-memory)). No source names him on Coworker. That he carried the deal from Soma to Triatomic is a plausible hypothesis, not a finding.\n- **The hedge-fund alt-data background** (\"used alternative data to inform investment decisions\"; [Soma, archived](http://web.archive.org/web/20221201043541/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/)) is the only concrete clue to how he works: a data and modeling diligence style rather than a technical one.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The firm is high fit: $10–20M first checks, plus d-Matrix, MatX, Gimlet, Efficient, Volantis and iPronics. He is the practical contact for co-investing in large compute and hardware rounds, and for asking how Triatomic sizes its co-invest vehicle. He is not a first-money or talent signal: no attributed deals, no research background, and a background in consumer, logistics and fintech. The deal that matters to the owner is **Coworker** (AI-native automation, the same category as Serval). It is the one Triatomic deal where his previous firm (Soma) was an earlier investor. Ask him directly whether he sourced it.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** UCLA, double major in Economics and Political Science, \"concentrating on methods and modeling\" ([Soma, archived](http://web.archive.org/web/20221201043541/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/)). Year not found.\n- **black-and-white Capital, Los Angeles:** a long/short equity hedge fund. He used alternative data across consumer, trucking and logistics, mobile gaming and internet ([Soma, archived](http://web.archive.org/web/20221201043541/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/)). Dates not found.\n- **DoorDash, Finance & Strategy, Associate:** before the IPO (DoorDash listed in December 2020). He worked on driver supply, logistics and analytics, scaling regional driver-acquisition modeling ([Soma, archived](http://web.archive.org/web/20221201043541/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/)). Dates not found.\n- **Soma Capital, investor, 2021 to 2024:** Soma's bio says he joined in 2021, and his member page was published 2021-08-23 (page metadata, [archived](http://web.archive.org/web/20211209143620/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/)). Aggregators give \"Investment Partner, 2021 to 2024\" and an end date written \"01/07/2024\", which could be 1 July or 7 January ([Zonebourse](https://www.zonebourse.com/insider/WILLEM-VAN-DEN-BOSCH-A3PBGT/), aggregator).\n- **Triatomic Capital, Principal, 2024 to present:** on the site by 2024-10-08 ([archived](http://web.archive.org/web/20241008150812/https://triatomic.ai/)). The bio says \"8 cumulative years of combined operating and investing experience\" ([triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai)). That text hasn't changed since 2024-11.\n- Pronouns: both the Soma and Triatomic bios use \"he\".\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai) (bio page: [/team/willem-van-den-bosch](https://www.triatomic.ai/team/willem-van-den-bosch), archived) | Firm bio. No headshot on the current site. The old Soma page (live 404) had one, available only in the archive ([archived Soma](http://web.archive.org/web/20221201043541/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/)), so `photo:` is left blank |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No account found (2026-11-10) |\n| LinkedIn | [linkedin.com/in/wvdb1996](https://www.linkedin.com/in/wvdb1996/) | Linked from the firm site. The logged-out view shows no dates |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found.\n\n## How they think\nNothing in his own words was found: no podcast, X posts or writing. The only clues are second-hand.\n- The Soma bio and the Triatomic bio describe his Soma focus differently (growth-stage versus early stage) (see Differential insight).\n- IncubatorList says he evaluates AI companies' architecture technically and builds founder relationships over years ([IncubatorList](https://incubatorlist.com/willem-van-den-bosch)). This is an aggregator, it reads as generated text, and it has low confidence. It is not in the insight rows.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Soma Capital bio, archived 2022-12-01](http://web.archive.org/web/20221201043541/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/): the most detailed account of his pre-venture work.\n2. [Triatomic team page](https://triatomic.ai): his current bio, and the firm's $10–20M check and theme statements.\n3. [Coworker $13M seed announcement](https://pulse2.com/coworker-ai-13-million-raised-for-ai-agent-research-company/amp/): the Soma pre-seed and Triatomic seed link.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public X account and no public LinkedIn post text that could be retrieved.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| nothing attributable | | | | | No announcement or filing names him on a deal. Unverified aggregator and search claims are under Open questions |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found. The closest is Coworker. Soma Capital (his employer 2021–24) was in the pre-seed, and Triatomic (his employer from 2024) led the $13M seed, which was announced 2025-05-21 with Jeff Huber named as lead ([pulse2](https://pulse2.com/coworker-ai-13-million-raised-for-ai-agent-research-company/amp/)). His own role isn't documented.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Triatomic Capital | Principal | 2024 (on site by 2024-10-08) | [archived](http://web.archive.org/web/20241008150812/https://triatomic.ai/) |\n| Soma Capital | Investor (\"Soma Investor\") | 2021 to 2024 | [Soma, archived](http://web.archive.org/web/20221201043541/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/); end year from [Zonebourse](https://www.zonebourse.com/insider/WILLEM-VAN-DEN-BOSCH-A3PBGT/) (aggregator) |\n| DoorDash | Associate, Finance & Strategy (pre-IPO) | dates not found | [Soma, archived](http://web.archive.org/web/20221201043541/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/) |\n| black-and-white Capital | Analyst role using alternative data at a long/short fund | dates not found | [Soma, archived](http://web.archive.org/web/20221201043541/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/) |\n| Dhruv Bavaria | Fellow ex-Soma colleague, now Triatomic CFO per site | | [triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai) |\n| Jeff Huber, Peter Zhou, Steve Hochberg | Triatomic GPs | 2024 | [triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai) |\n\n## Open questions\n- **Unverified deal claims, all from search-engine summaries of pages I couldn't read:**\n  - A search summary said LinkedIn posts by him announced Triatomic's lead of Coworker's $13M seed and support of Chalk's $50M Series A. The posts could not be retrieved.\n  - A search summary said NFX Signal lists one investment for him: Fasset, Series A, April 2022, $22M (Soma era). The Signal page hides deals behind a sign-in.\n  - None of these are in the deals rows.\n- Did he source Coworker from his Soma network? Ask him directly.\n- Which compute deals (Gimlet A/B, Efficient B, Volantis A, MatX B) did he run diligence on?\n- Exact start date at Triatomic, and his Soma exit date (July or January 2024).\n- Dates at DoorDash and black-and-white Capital, and his graduation year.\n- Is the archived Soma headshot ([image, archived](http://web.archive.org/web/20211209143620im_/https://www.somacap.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/IMG_2171-1-scaled.jpg)) acceptable as a photo source? It is from a former employer and the live URL is dead.\n\n## Sources\n- https://triatomic.ai (bio, team; fetched 2026-11-10)\n- http://web.archive.org/web/20241008150812/https://triatomic.ai/ (first Webflow snapshot; team list; stylesheet name)\n- http://web.archive.org/web/20240903125232/https://triatomic.ai/ and http://web.archive.org/web/20240905210032/https://www.triatomic.ai/team (Squarespace era, GP-only team)\n- http://web.archive.org/web/20241103135522/https://www.triatomic.ai/team/willem-van-den-bosch\n- http://web.archive.org/web/20221201043541/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/ and http://web.archive.org/web/20211209143620/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/\n- https://pulse2.com/coworker-ai-13-million-raised-for-ai-agent-research-company/amp/ ; https://stocks.observer-reporter.com/observerreporter/article/abnewswire-2025-5-23-coworker-secures-13m-to-launch-first-ai-teammate-with-built-in-organizational-memory\n- Aggregators: https://signal.nfx.com/investors/willem-van-den-bosch ; https://incubatorlist.com/willem-van-den-bosch ; https://www.zonebourse.com/insider/WILLEM-VAN-DEN-BOSCH-A3PBGT/\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/wvdb1996/ (logged-out view)\n", "person:wouter-gort": "\n# Wouter Gort\n\n*Created 2026-11-10. The Generalist uses \"he\" for Gort, so this file does too. Public footprint is very thin: no X, Substack, podcast or firm-page bio found (checks listed under \"Where to find them\"). Most of what is known comes from one long Generalist profile (2023-12-03), a NOAH conference fund book, and aggregators. Nomads' own facts live in `lps/nomads.md`; this file covers the person.*\n\n## One-line take\nGort is the gatekeeper of Nomads, the one fund of funds visibly anchoring tiny frontier-AI-talent managers (Koko Xs's $17.5M Fund I), and he got there from fintech growth equity, not from AI or deep tech.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** a growth-investor's network (Atomico, then Greyhound alongside Pogos Saiadian on Revolut, Brex, N26, Marqeta and Ualá) plus close friendships with Hummingbird's managing partners, which is how Nomads came to sit inside Hummingbird in 2021 ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)).\n- **How he decides:** an interview about how a GP makes decisions and who they are, not a track-record checklist. His own test question: \"How do you get to the best decisions as quickly as possible?\" ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)).\n- **Constraint:** size. Nomads looks for funds that can return 10x and excludes managers above $250M AUM ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)).\n- **What he'd never do (again):** back prestige funds for the aura. Nomads' first fund held Tiger, Addition and Northzone; he moved it to insurgent funds only from 2021 ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The \"ex-Atomico MD\" label is disputed by a contemporaneous source.** The Generalist (2023, retrospective) says he \"served as a Managing Director at Atomico\" from 2011 to 2014 ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)). Atomico's own listing in the NOAH London fund book gives \"Principal, Europe, Value Creation\": \"Wouter is responsible for helping portfolio companies with business development in Continental Europe\" and he \"manages the Open Office outreach programme\" ([NOAH15 fund book](https://noah-conference.relayto.com/e/noah15-london-fund-book-osaevbaj/moEN9ydf52)). Crunchbase's snippet also says principal, 2011 to June 2014 (aggregator, [Crunchbase](https://www.crunchbase.com/person/wouter-gort), 403 on fetch; search snippet only). Read: his Atomico seat was a portfolio-support and outreach role, not a deal-leading partner seat.\n- **His investing record is fintech growth, not AI.** At Greyhound, \"he and Saiadan backed fintech winners Revolut, Brex, Ualá, N26, and Marqeta\" ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)). No source ties him personally to an AI, chip or deep-tech deal. His frontier-AI exposure comes only through the managers Nomads picks.\n- **Nomads' anchor in Koko Xs bought a track record made before the fund existed.** Koko's Etched check was a personal pre-Nova check from about $140K of savings ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc)); the Mercor entry is 2023 seed-era (repo: `people/koko-xs.md`); Fund I's Form D was filed 2024-08-15 ([SEC](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2030439/000203043924000001/primary_doc.xml)). The Flapping Airplanes exposure is via a later Nova SPV. So the \"Nomads look-through to Etched, Mercor, Flapping\" framing in `lps/nomads.md` is not supported; the anchor was a bet on the person (inference from dates).\n- **He went to fund investing after leaving growth equity, first with his own money.** \"In 2021, Gort stepped back from Greyhound and began investing his own money,\" and he was \"increasingly drawn to investing in funds rather than directly into startups\" ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)). Which funds he backed personally is not disclosed.\n- **Nomads joined Hummingbird through friendship, not hiring.** Gort \"built friendships with other Europe-focused investors, including Barend Van den Brande and Firat Ileri,\" and the two sides \"decided to join forces\" ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)).\n- **GPs say his questions are unusual.** Mario Gabriele, whom Nomads declined in 2022: \"Gort and his colleagues asked questions I received from no other LP\" ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)). Gabriele is now a Hummingbird partner ([team](https://www.hummingbird.vc/team)).\n- **Saiadian was likely an Atomico colleague first.** The Generalist calls Saiadan (spelled Saiadian on LinkedIn) a \"former colleague\"; an aggregator lists Saiadian as VP at Atomico 2013-2014, then Greyhound partner from 2015 (aggregator, [MarketScreener](https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/POGOS-SAIADIAN-A13MEX/), search snippet). So Greyhound was spun out of the Atomico network.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** As an LP for a fund writing ~$20M checks into $600M seeds: low, because of the $250M AUM cap ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)). As a person to know: medium-high. He decides which small frontier-AI-talent managers get anchor capital (Koko Xs Fund I; Compound), so his pipeline is an early map of emerging AI GPs and a source of co-invest. He backs people before track records, and he accepts GPs whose worldview differs from Hummingbird's. Deals that matter: Koko Xsu Venture Co Fund I (anchor, 2024), Compound (LP by 2023), Fifty Years Deep (LP, filing).\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** MSc Business Administration, RSM Erasmus University Rotterdam (aggregator, MarketScreener search snippet; may conflate two MarketScreener IDs, A34JUW and A0IX3J).\n- **Before Atomico:** an aggregator lists a principal/officer role at a Dutch firm 2002/2003-2008 (unconfirmed, MarketScreener snippet).\n- **Atomico, 2011-2014:** Principal, Europe, Value Creation ([NOAH15](https://noah-conference.relayto.com/e/noah15-london-fund-book-osaevbaj/moEN9ydf52)); \"Managing Director\" per [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird). One conference bio says \"since 2010\" (search snippet of nextconf.eu, page unreachable).\n- **The Big Search BV (2014) / Gort Management:** founder, per aggregators (MarketScreener snippet; [Crunchbase](https://www.crunchbase.com/person/wouter-gort) snippet). Not verified.\n- **Greyhound Capital (London growth equity, founded 2015 per aggregator):** partner with Pogos Saiadian; backed Revolut, Brex, Ualá, N26, Marqeta ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)). Dates conflict across aggregators (2014-06 to 2018-08 vs 2017-2021).\n- **2021:** stepped back from Greyhound, invested personally, then joined forces with Hummingbird to lead Nomads ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)); aggregator lists Hummingbird Partners NV from 2021-05-31.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://nomads.vc | Nomads site; tagline only, no team named (checked 2026-11-10). Not on https://www.hummingbird.vc/team (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | not found | Nomads' public writing is by Yilancioglu ([Postcards from Istanbul](https://postcardsfromistanbul.substack.com/p/our-learnings-on-venture-funds)) |\n| X | not found | Searched 2026-11-10; no account surfaced |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | Profile exists per Crunchbase snippet, URL not confirmed (LinkedIn blocks fetch) |\n| GitHub | not found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n| Profile (secondary) | https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird | Best source on him, 2023-12-03 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found. Nomads' public voice is Jordan Nel (Swimming with Allocators 2023-12; How I Invest E59 2024-04; Delphi 2026-07) and Yavuzhan Yilancioglu (Substack). See `lps/nomads.md`.\n- 2015 (approx.) — NOAH London fund book — listed as Atomico Principal, Europe, Value Creation ([NOAH15](https://noah-conference.relayto.com/e/noah15-london-fund-book-osaevbaj/moEN9ydf52)).\n\n## How they think\n- **Funds that can 10x, so small funds.** Size is the first gate ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)).\n- **Decision speed and conviction.** His diligence asks how a GP gets to the best decisions fast and whether they can act on them ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)).\n- **Contrarian view:** prestige funds give \"aura\" but not returns for a fund of funds; he walked away from Tiger, Addition and Northzone after Fund I ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)).\n- **Calls right:** the 2021 pivot away from Tiger and growth fund exposure before the 2022 drawdown (timing inference); anchoring Koko Xs in 2024 before Nova's frontier-AI deals (Flapping, Midas) became visible.\n\n## Read these first\n1. The Generalist, \"The Best Venture Firm You've Never Heard Of\" (2023-12-03), the Nomads section: https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird\n2. Yilancioglu, \"Our learnings on venture funds\" (2024-01-10), the team's codified view: https://postcardsfromistanbul.substack.com/p/our-learnings-on-venture-funds\n3. TechCrunch on Koko Xs (2024-12-13), the anchor that matters for frontier AI: https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc\n\n## Social engagement\nNo public social presence found. He is low-profile; colleagues (Nel, Yilancioglu) do the public talking.\n\n## Portfolio\nFund-level (as Nomads lead; rows in `data/rows/nomads-lp.csv`) and earlier direct deals.\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Koko Xsu Venture Co Fund I (fund) | Anchor LP, $17.5M fund | 2024 | anchor | Lux Capital, Josh Wolfe, Marc Andreessen, Chris Dixon, Kevin Hartz | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc) |\n| Compound (fund; vehicle not named) | LP | by 2023 | n/a | n/a | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Fifty Years Deep, LP (fund) | LP | not disclosed | n/a | n/a | [SEC 13D/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070849/000110465926124070/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Revolut | Greyhound era, round not named | not dated | not stated | Pogos Saiadian (Greyhound) | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Brex | Greyhound era, round not named | not dated | not stated | Saiadian | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| N26 | Greyhound era, round not named | not dated | not stated | Saiadian | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Marqeta | Greyhound era, round not named | not dated | not stated | Saiadian | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Ualá | Greyhound era, round not named | not dated | not stated | Saiadian | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Nomads joining Hummingbird (2021):** after stepping back from Greyhound, Gort was investing in funds personally; Hummingbird was firming up interest in a fund-of-funds; given his close relationship with its managing partners (Van den Brande, Ileri), they combined ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)).\n- **Generalist Capital pass (spring 2022):** Gabriele was introduced to \"Wouter Gort's team\", went through interview-style diligence, and was declined ([Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird)). He later joined Hummingbird as a partner.\n- **Koko Xs anchor (2024):** intro path not found. Nomads anchored the $17.5M fund of a 21-year-old whose best positions (Etched, Mercor) were personal or pre-fund checks ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Nomads | Leads | 2021 | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Barend Van den Brande | Friend; Hummingbird founder and Nomads founder | before 2021 | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Firat Ileri | Friend; Hummingbird MP | before 2021 | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Pogos Saiadian | Former colleague; Greyhound partner | Atomico era | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Atomico | Principal (or MD), 2011-2014 | 2011 | [NOAH15](https://noah-conference.relayto.com/e/noah15-london-fund-book-osaevbaj/moEN9ydf52) |\n| Greyhound Capital | Partner (dates disputed) | mid-2010s | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Jordan Nel; Yavuzhan Yilancioglu | Nomads team (research; investments) | by 2023 | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Mario Gabriele | Declined his fund in 2022; now a Hummingbird colleague | 2022 | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Michael Dempsey (Compound) | Portfolio GP | by 2023 | [Generalist](https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird) |\n| Koko Xs (Nova Global) | Portfolio GP (anchor) | 2024 | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc) |\n\n## Open questions\n- His exact Atomico title (Principal vs MD) and whether he led any Atomico deals.\n- Greyhound dates and whether he co-founded it; which rounds of Revolut and Brex he did.\n- Which funds he backed personally in 2021 before Nomads.\n- Nomads ticket sizes; whether Nomads backs Nova Global (Koko's successor) or other frontier-AI emerging managers in Nomads II.\n- Who introduced Koko Xs to Nomads.\n- Whether the $250M AUM cap still holds after Hummingbird's larger 2026 raise.\n\n## Sources\n- The Generalist, \"The Best Venture Firm You've Never Heard Of\" (2023-12-03): https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird\n- NOAH15 London fund book, Atomico team: https://noah-conference.relayto.com/e/noah15-london-fund-book-osaevbaj/moEN9ydf52\n- TechCrunch on Koko Xs (2024-12-13): https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc\n- Koko Xsu Venture Co Fund I Form D (2024-08-15): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2030439/000203043924000001/primary_doc.xml\n- BillionToOne 13D/A (2026-11-09): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070849/000110465926124070/primary_doc.xml\n- Hummingbird team page (checked 2026-11-10): https://www.hummingbird.vc/team\n- Aggregators (search snippets; pages blocked): Crunchbase https://www.crunchbase.com/person/wouter-gort ; MarketScreener https://marketscreener.com/insider/WOUTER-GORT-A34JUW/experience ; MarketScreener (Saiadian) https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/POGOS-SAIADIAN-A13MEX/ ; Gilion (Greyhound) https://vc-mapping.gilion.com/vc-firms/greyhound-capital\n", "person:yasmin-razavi": "\n# Yasmin Razavi\n\n## One-line take\nShe is the only investor on Anthropic's board, and she goes early on deep-technical companies (MatX, Standard Intelligence) from a growth fund. That makes her the Spark partner most likely to co-lead or buy into a frontier lab or AI-chip company right after the owner's seed. She entered the 2026 Forbes Midas List at #13, the highest new entry [press](https://www.forbes.com.tr/para-yatirim/forbes-midas-listesi-2026-nin-en-iyi-girisim-sermayesi-yatirimcilari).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins:** She makes a few concentrated, contrarian, team-based bets and then backs them with repeat follow-ons. She led Anthropic's Series C when most VCs had passed, wrote two long memos to get the partnership to agree, and then kept Spark in all six later rounds through Series H, about $777M in total [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm). She deliberately rejects gut-feel investing and favours highly technical, non-performer founders ([archived bio, 2020](https://web.archive.org/web/20200924103350/https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-profiles/yasmin-razavi)).\n- **What constrains her:** She can't approve a deal alone. Each Spark growth GP entity (IV, V, VI) is run by five or six managing members and decides by majority vote [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm). Her fund's mandate is $25M-$100M Series B/C checks, and Anthropic board work (two committees, through an IPO) takes a lot of her time.\n- **What she'd never do (inferred):** Spray and pray. She uses that phrase herself to describe what Anthropic was not [press](https://www.forbes.com/sites/iainmartin/2026/05/27/investing-superstar-yasmin-razavi-turned-a-75-million-check-into-a-3-billion-ai-windfall/).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Her board seat came before the announced round.** The S-1 says she has been a director \"since March 2023\" ([S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm)). The Series C financing ran March-June 2023 and was announced on 2023-05-23 ([src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-series-c)). Spark had effectively won the round by March; earlier reports of a ~$300M Spark-led raise two months before the announcement fit this timing ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=617716)).\n- **She can't approve deals alone.** Spark's growth GPs \"make investment and voting decisions based on a majority vote\" ([S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm)). That is why the two internal memos were needed.\n- **Every Spark growth fund holds Anthropic.** The $440M Series H check (May-June 2026, $589.0095/share) went in partly through \"Spark Capital Growth Fund Holdings 2026, LLC\". The members of that vehicle are Growth IV, Founders IV, V, Founders V and VI ([S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm)). The full per-round list is under Portfolio.\n- **Her governance role is deeper than a typical investor seat.** She was elected by the Senior Voting Preferred holders. She counts as Nasdaq-independent and sits on the compensation committee and on the nominating and governance committee. She took no director pay or equity awards for 2025. After the IPO she remains one of three Class A/F-elected directors, alongside Dario and Daniela Amodei ([S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm)).\n- **Her thesis was the team.** She says \"this was the team that built GPT-3\" ([Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/sites/iainmartin/2026/05/27/investing-superstar-yasmin-razavi-turned-a-75-million-check-into-a-3-billion-ai-windfall/); forbes.com returned 403, so the text was read via a syndicated mirror).\n- **She shaped strategy at board level.** Daniela Amodei recalls Razavi telling the founders that coding \"is gonna be a huge market and you can absolutely own\" it, before Claude Code ([SF Standard](https://sfstandard.com/2026/10/28/anthropic-ipo-bay-area-insiders/)). This is Daniela's recollection, not Razavi's own quote.\n- **She is anti-gut.** She says \"gut driven investing often favors a certain type of entrepreneur\" and wants to avoid that bias ([archived Spark bio, 2020](https://web.archive.org/web/20200924103350/https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-profiles/yasmin-razavi)).\n- **She sources cold.** She found Deel by DMing CEO Alex Bouaziz on Twitter, then ran about six weeks of Zoom calls without a pitch deck. Spark led the $30M Series B four months after a16z's A ([secondary](https://www.thetrustedinsight.com/investment-news/remote-working-startup-deel-raised-30m-without-a-pitch-deck-20201003686/), summary only).\n- **The gap to other investors.** Menlo's Matt Murphy joined her Series C as a participant and later said \"I wish we had broken some rules during our Series C\" ([via BI, aggregator](https://www.kucoin.com/news/flash/spark-capital-s-75m-anthropic-bet-now-worth-7b)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Her named deals include a frontier lab (Anthropic, Series C lead and board), an AI-chip company (MatX, Series A lead and board), a pixel-space pre-training lab (Standard Intelligence, Series A co-lead with Sequoia) and AI compute (Crusoe, Series E). **Caveat:** she is a growth GP who writes $25M-$100M and up to $440M. She won't compete with the owner for a $20M seed allocation. Her role is more like the **next-round buyer or an A/B co-lead** for the owner's seed positions, as shown by MatX's A less than a year after the Friedman/Gross seed. Her Standard Intelligence A came ~17 months after Lachy Groom's seed (see `patterns/co-investor-gangs.md`).\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Tehran. On a school trip to visit a brother in Canada she chose not to return ([Forbes, via forbes.it](https://forbes.it/2026/06/03/yasmin-razavi-ha-trasformato-un-assegno-da-75-milioni-di-dollari-in-un-guadagno-per-lai-da-3-miliardi-di-dollari/)). Age 37 per the S-1 (2026-10).\n- BASc in Industrial Engineering (Operations Research), University of Toronto. MBA, Harvard Business School ([S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm); [2018 bio](https://web.archive.org/web/20180906010701/http://www.sparkcapital.com:80/team/yasmin-razavi)).\n- McKinsey & Company consultant, 2012-08 to 2014-08 ([S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm)).\n- Index Ventures investor in London, 2014-08 to 2015-08, working on consumer and gaming deals. Per Forbes, she was hired despite lacking the gaming and Nordic experience the role asked for. Her former boss there was Ben Holmes ([S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm); [Forbes, via forbes.it](https://forbes.it/2026/06/03/yasmin-razavi-ha-trasformato-un-assegno-da-75-milioni-di-dollari-in-un-guadagno-per-lai-da-3-miliardi-di-dollari/)).\n- Snap: product manager on the monetization stack according to Spark's bios. Forbes calls it an HBS-era internship. The S-1 lists neither, so this conflict is unresolved ([2018 bio](https://web.archive.org/web/20180906010701/http://www.sparkcapital.com:80/team/yasmin-razavi)).\n- Spark Capital: joined the San Francisco growth team in July 2017 and became General Partner in March 2020 ([S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm)). The 2018 and 2019 bios show no title ([2018](https://web.archive.org/web/20180906010701/http://www.sparkcapital.com:80/team/yasmin-razavi)), so her entry seniority is unknown. **fast_promotion is unknown:** it was ~2y8m to GP, but the entry title is not disclosed. She is a related person on the Growth IV Form D (2021) and on the Growth VI Form D (2026) ([filing](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2123891/000212389126000002/primary_doc.xml)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-members/yasmin-razavi | Spark bio (fetched 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | https://x.com/YasminRazavi | Linked from her Spark bio. Loads (HTTP 200, 2026-11-10); posts not readable without login |\n| LinkedIn | not verified | Not linked from the Spark page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2020-09: TechCrunch Disrupt, \"Getting to the Growth Stage\" panel with Elliott Robinson (Bessemer). How early founders should approach growth money ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2020/09/14/get-to-the-growth-stage-at-techcrunch-disrupt-this-week/)). She said every metric comes down to unit economics ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/06/16/yasmin-razavi-of-spark-capital-will-sit-in-judgment-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2021s-startup-battlefield/)).\n- 2021-06: TechCrunch Disrupt 2021, Startup Battlefield judge ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/06/16/yasmin-razavi-of-spark-capital-will-sit-in-judgment-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2021s-startup-battlefield/)).\n- 2026-05-27: Forbes Midas interview by Iain Martin ([src](https://www.forbes.com/sites/iainmartin/2026/05/27/investing-superstar-yasmin-razavi-turned-a-75-million-check-into-a-3-billion-ai-windfall/)). An Anghami \"episode\" with the same title is an audio reading of this article, not an interview.\n- No long-form podcast found. She keeps a low public profile.\n\n## How they think\n- **Team over traction for labs:** she backed the GPT-3 team before Anthropic had meaningful revenue. Her verdict on Dario Amodei: \"Dario never misses\" ([via BI](https://dnyuz.com/?p=312451)).\n- **Data, narrative and market:** she rejects gut calls and wants to understand at a granular level why a company does what it does ([2020 bio](https://web.archive.org/web/20200924103350/https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-profiles/yasmin-razavi)). For growth deals, unit economics and payback on growth spend matter most ([TC 2021](https://techcrunch.com/2021/06/16/yasmin-razavi-of-spark-capital-will-sit-in-judgment-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2021s-startup-battlefield/)).\n- **Contrarian view:** she wants to avoid the bias towards charismatic \"performer\" founders.\n- **Big repeatable waves:** fintech (Marqeta, Deel), remote work (Deel), then AI. Deel's CEO says she makes unconventional, high-conviction bets ([Forbes, via forbes.com.tr](https://www.forbes.com.tr/yapay-zeka/yasmin-razavi-75-milyon-dolarlik-yatirimini-3-milyar-dolarlik-kazanca-donusturdu)).\n- **Calls:** Anthropic (Spark's ~$75M is estimated at $3B+ per Forbes and ~$7B per a WSJ-cited report) and Deel (backed at ~$200M, now $17B) went right. Marqeta (backed at $1.9B, IPO at $15B, now ~$1.6B market cap) shows the risk at growth entry prices ([Forbes, via forbes.it](https://forbes.it/2026/06/03/yasmin-razavi-ha-trasformato-un-assegno-da-75-milioni-di-dollari-in-un-guadagno-per-lai-da-3-miliardi-di-dollari/)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. The Forbes Midas profile (2026-05-27): the Anthropic memos, the leap of faith, her background ([link](https://www.forbes.com/sites/iainmartin/2026/05/27/investing-superstar-yasmin-razavi-turned-a-75-million-check-into-a-3-billion-ai-windfall/)).\n2. The Anthropic S-1, under Management and Related Person Transactions: her dates, committees, and every Spark check ([link](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm)).\n3. Her archived 2020 Spark bio: the anti-gut, technical-founder philosophy in her own words ([link](https://web.archive.org/web/20200924103350/https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-profiles/yasmin-razavi)).\n4. SF Standard on Anthropic's insiders: Daniela Amodei on the coding push ([link](https://sfstandard.com/2026/10/28/anthropic-ipo-bay-area-insiders/)).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing substantive found. Her X account exists but its posts could not be read without login, and no podcasts or Substack were found. She mostly speaks through Forbes and conference panels.\n\n## Portfolio\nOnly deals where a source names her personally. Rows are in `data/rows/yasmin-razavi-deals.csv`.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Anthropic | Series C ($450M; Spark $75.0M at $11.2261/share); board | 2023-05 (board since 2023-03) | yes | Google, Salesforce Ventures, Sound Ventures, Zoom Ventures, Menlo | [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm), [src](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-series-c) |\n| Anthropic | Series D (Spark $75.0M) | 2024-01 to 05 | no | | [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm) |\n| Anthropic | Series E (Spark $100.0M) | 2025-01 to 03 | no | | [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm) |\n| Anthropic | Series F (Spark $62.0M) | 2025-08 to 09 | no | | [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm) |\n| Anthropic | Series G (Spark $25.0M) | 2026-01 to 02 | no | | [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm) |\n| Anthropic | Series H (Spark $440.0M) | 2026-05 to 06 | no | | [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm) |\n| MatX | Series A (~$80M reported); board | 2024-11 | yes | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2024/11/22/ai-chip-startup-matx-founded-by-google-alums-raises-series-a-at-300m-valuation-sources-say/), [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2004726/000200472624000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| MatX | Series B ($500M) | 2026-02 | no (insider) | | [src](https://matx.com/research/series_b) |\n| Standard Intelligence | Series A ($75M) | 2026-04-30 | co-lead | Sequoia (Sonya Huang); with Spark's Mikowai Ashwill | [src](https://si.inc/posts/a/) |\n| Cambridge Aerospace (non-AI) | Series B ($200M at $1.3B) | 2026-04 | co-lead | Elad Gil & Co | [secondary](https://nordic9.com/news/cambridge-aerospace-raised-200m-series-b-at-1/), [bio](https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-members/yasmin-razavi) |\n| Crusoe | Series E ($1.375B) (her first Spark round not confirmed) | 2025-10 | no | Valor, Mubadala (co-leads) | [src](https://www.cooley.com/news/coverage/2025/2025-10-24-crusoe-announces-$1-375-billion-oversubscribed-series-e), Forbes |\n| Deel | Series B ($30M); board | 2020-09 | yes | | [src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20200909005149/en/) |\n| Ada Support | Series C ($130M at $1.2B) | 2021-05 | yes (Spark) | Tiger Global | [src](https://betakit.com/ada-secures-130-million-claims-1-2-billion-valuation/); her directorship is aggregator-only |\n| Marqeta | 2019 round at ~$1.9B | 2019 | unknown | | [TC bio](https://techcrunch.com/author/yasmin-razavi), Forbes |\n| Rapyd, Niantic, Capitolis, Earnin | Undisclosed | | unknown | | [TC bio](https://techcrunch.com/author/yasmin-razavi) |\n| Kalshi | Undisclosed | | unknown | | [SF Standard](https://sfstandard.com/2026/10/28/anthropic-ipo-bay-area-insiders/) |\n| Unannounced silicon-lithography startup | n/a | | | | Forbes (named only as unannounced) |\n\n**Checked and NOT hers:**\n- **Cruise** (Series A, 2015) and **Discord** (Series C, 2016) appear on Spark's site, but both predate her July 2017 start. Cruise is Nabeel Hyatt's.\n- **Granola**: Spark is an existing investor, but no partner is named.\n- **Adept**: Spark took part in the Series B (2023), but no partner is named.\n\n## Deal stories\n**Anthropic (2023).**\n- *Context:* In 2021-22 Anthropic raised about $1.1B mostly from individuals (Moskovitz, Bankman-Fried). Daniela Amodei says AI then was \"not viewed as this supersexy, exciting thing\" to invest in ([Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/sites/iainmartin/2026/05/27/investing-superstar-yasmin-razavi-turned-a-75-million-check-into-a-3-billion-ai-windfall/)). One secondary account says Anjney Midha pitched it to 22 VCs in 2021 and 21 passed ([36Kr](https://eu.36kr.com/en/p/4055383600600193)).\n- *First contact:* **not found.** No source says who introduced her or when they first met. A WSJ-cited summary says she spent months on industry research and discussion before leading ([aggregator](https://www.kucoin.com/news/flash/spark-capital-s-75m-anthropic-bet-now-worth-7b)).\n- *Inside Spark:* she wrote two long memos to win over her partners. It was Spark's biggest check at the time, and she calls it a leap of faith on the GPT-3 team ([Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/sites/iainmartin/2026/05/27/investing-superstar-yasmin-razavi-turned-a-75-million-check-into-a-3-billion-ai-windfall/)).\n- *Timing:* the C-1 shares were sold March-June 2023 at $11.2261/share, and she joined the board in March 2023. Google (strategic) and Menlo (Matt Murphy) came in the same round as participants. Spark was the first institutional VC to lead ([S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm); [BI via dnyuz](https://dnyuz.com/?p=312451)).\n- *After:* Spark followed on in D, E, F, G and H, putting in ~$777M across six rounds. Its first $75M check is estimated at $3B+ (Forbes, May 2026) or ~$7B (WSJ-cited, July 2026).\n- *Valuation conflict:* the post-money valuation is reported as $4.1B, $4.5B or $5B depending on the outlet.\n\n**Deel (2020).** Per a secondary summary (primary text not retrieved, low confidence), she cold-DMed Alex Bouaziz on Twitter and ran ~6 weeks of Zoom calls. Spark led the $30M Series B, she joined the board, and the round came four months after a16z's $14M Series A ([src](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20200909005149/en/); [secondary](https://www.thetrustedinsight.com/investment-news/remote-working-startup-deel-raised-30m-without-a-pitch-deck-20201003686/)).\n\n**MatX (2024).** Spark led the Series A less than a year after the Friedman/Gross seed. She first appears as a director on the Nov 2024 Form D, not the Dec 2023 one, so Spark took the board seat with the A ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2004726/000200472624000001/primary_doc.xml)).\n\n**Standard Intelligence (2026).** She co-led the $75M A with Sequoia's Sonya Huang, staffed jointly with Spark early-stage GP Mikowai Ashwill. That was a growth-plus-early pairing on one A ([src](https://si.inc/posts/a/)). Lachy Groom led the seed; he was not a co-lead here.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Spark Capital | Joined 2017-07; GP since 2020-03; managing member of the GPs of Growth IV, V and VI | 2017 | [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm) |\n| Index Ventures | Investor, London | 2014-08 to 2015-08 | [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm) |\n| McKinsey & Company | Consultant | 2012-08 to 2014-08 | [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm) |\n| Dario and Daniela Amodei | Co-directors at Anthropic | 2023-03 | [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm) |\n| Reed Hastings, Vas Narasimhan, Chris Liddell | Co-directors; with Narasimhan on the compensation committee, with Hastings and Daniela Amodei on the nominating and governance committee | 2026 | [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm) |\n| Mikowai Ashwill (Spark) | Co-partner on Standard Intelligence | 2026-04 | [src](https://si.inc/posts/a/) |\n| Sonya Huang (Sequoia) | Co-lead on Standard Intelligence | 2026-04 | [src](https://sequoiacap.com/article/standard-intelligence-training-general-intelligence-in-pixel-space/) |\n| Reiner Pope (MatX) | Board | 2024-11 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2004726/000200472624000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Alex Bouaziz (Deel) | Board; cold-DM origin | 2020-09 | [src](https://www.pymnts.com/?p=890816) |\n| Ben Holmes (ex-Index) | Former boss | 2014 | Forbes |\n| Elad Gil & Co | Co-lead, Cambridge Aerospace B | 2026-04 | [secondary](https://nordic9.com/news/cambridge-aerospace-raised-200m-series-b-at-1/) |\n| LACERS, Orange County ERS, Massachusetts PRIM | LPs in her growth funds (IV/V; VI) | 2021+ | [Dealroom](https://dealroom.co/news/168846-anthropics-ipo-windfall-reaches-us-public-pensions-through-spark-capital/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- How did she first meet Anthropic, and who introduced her (Midha? Moskovitz's network?). The WSJ piece from July 2026 may say; it was not retrieved.\n- What was her entry title at Spark in 2017 (associate or principal)? That would decide `fast_promotion`.\n- Which silicon-lithography startup is it (unannounced, per Forbes)? It is high lens-fit.\n- What was Spark's entry round in Crusoe, and is it hers personally?\n- Read her X posts once logged in.\n- LACERS's Q4 2025 report shows Growth IV at 2.29x / 43.6% IRR. Pull the LP rows for the Spark firm file.\n\n## Sources\n- Anthropic S-1, filed 2026-10-26: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm\n- Forbes, Iain Martin, 2026-05-27: https://www.forbes.com/sites/iainmartin/2026/05/27/investing-superstar-yasmin-razavi-turned-a-75-million-check-into-a-3-billion-ai-windfall/ (403 when fetched; read via syndicated mirror and the forbes.it, forbes.com.tr and forbes.com.br editions)\n- Business Insider via DNYUZ: https://dnyuz.com/?p=312451\n- SF Standard: https://sfstandard.com/2026/10/28/anthropic-ipo-bay-area-insiders/\n- Spark bios: https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-members/yasmin-razavi; Wayback 2018 https://web.archive.org/web/20180906010701/http://www.sparkcapital.com:80/team/yasmin-razavi; 2020 https://web.archive.org/web/20200924103350/https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-profiles/yasmin-razavi\n- TechCrunch: https://techcrunch.com/author/yasmin-razavi; https://techcrunch.com/2021/06/16/yasmin-razavi-of-spark-capital-will-sit-in-judgment-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2021s-startup-battlefield/\n- Anthropic Series C: https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-series-c\n- MatX: https://techcrunch.com/2024/11/22/ai-chip-startup-matx-founded-by-google-alums-raises-series-a-at-300m-valuation-sources-say/, https://matx.com/research/series_b, Form D filings\n- Standard Intelligence: https://si.inc/posts/a/\n- Deel: https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20200909005149/en/, https://www.pymnts.com/?p=890816\n- Dealroom (pension LPs): https://dealroom.co/news/168846-anthropics-ipo-windfall-reaches-us-public-pensions-through-spark-capital/\n- Aggregators (labelled): KuCoin/WSJ summary, 36Kr, MarketScreener, nordic9, Forbes Türkiye Midas list\n", "person:yoko-li": "\n# Yoko Li\n\n## One-line take\nThe a16z Infra partner most likely to have already used your product: she found Resend through its open-source library, builds public AI experiments (AI-town, TetrisBench) and turns them into theses that precede her deals in agents, coding models and code-native visual AI.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins.** She is a practising engineer-investor: ex-AppDynamics engineer, founding engineer and PM at Transposit, head of product for Terraform Cloud at HashiCorp ([a16z bio](https://a16z.com/author/yoko-li/)). Her a16z output is mostly hands-on build posts and open-source projects (AI-town at 10.7k GitHub stars, created 2023-07-16, [GitHub API](https://api.github.com/repos/a16z-infra/ai-town)). Developers meet her through the tools before they meet her as an investor.\n- **What constrains her.** She has never been the sole name on an a16z deal post. Every byline pairs her with a GP: Martin Casado (Inngest, Resend, Phota, Relace, QuiverAI, Runta), Jennifer Li (Upstash, Mintlify, Stainless, Reducto, Vals) or Raghu Raghuram (Vals, Eka) (sources in Portfolio). Casado has said non-GP partners need GP sponsorship for autonomy ([martin-casado profile](martin-casado.md)). Boards: only Resend and Relace; she is an observer at Mintlify, Clerk, Phota Labs and Inngest ([a16z bio](https://a16z.com/author/yoko-li/)).\n- **What she would never do (inferred).** Back a product she cannot reason about as a developer. Every deal she is named on is a developer surface, an agent runtime or a model whose output is code or code-like (SVG, diffs, API SDKs). No frontier-lab, consumer or vertical-SaaS bylines found.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Sourcing by using the product.** On Resend: \"we first discovered Resend by using their open source project react-email\" ([a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-resend/), 2024-12-04). a16z then led the $18M Series A and joined the board; she holds that seat per her bio ([Resend](https://resend.com/blog/series-a); [bio](https://a16z.com/author/yoko-li/)).\n- **Her first a16z lead is Inngest, not Mintlify.** Inngest's own post titled \"Inngest raises $6.1M led by a16z\" credits Martin Casado and Yoko Li as leads ([Inngest](https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-funding-from-a16z), 2024-01-30). That is seven months before the Mintlify Series A, and it is missing from the a16z firm page roster in this repo. a16z did not lead again: Altimeter led Inngest's $21M Series A with a16z only continuing ([Inngest](https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-inngest-series-a), 2025-09-16).\n- **Essays come after the deal and act as portfolio marketing, but they show the thesis.** \"The Next Frontier of Visual AI Is Code\" (2026-06-02) argues models are \"generating the source code behind\" visuals and uses Quiver for a logo example ([a16z](https://a16z.com/the-next-frontier-of-visual-ai-is-code/)), three months after she led QuiverAI's seed (2026-02-25, [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-quiverai/)). Expect her next deals in code-native creative tools (HTML/CSS, Lottie, Blender scripts are named).\n- **Her own experiment encodes her view on models.** TetrisBench: models played badly choosing moves, but \"What they are very good at, though, is writing code\" ([a16z](https://a16z.com/i-built-tetrisbench-where-llms-compete-at-playing-tetris-heres-what-i-found/), 2026-02-23). That is the same week as the QuiverAI seed, and it explains why Relace (models for coding agents) and QuiverAI (SVG model) are her first-byline deals.\n- **Convergence and verifiers are her 2026 frame for agents.** \"A loop is not yet a direction\" ([a16z](https://a16z.com/knowing-when-to-stop-the-art-of-making-a-loop-converge/), 2026-08-06). She argues agent loops need an external verifier and a cost-aware stopping rule; this lines up with Vals (evals, 2026-08-13) and Runta (agent execution, 2026-07-16).\n- **Guillermo Rauch is the recurring co-investor in her devtool deals.** He is an angel in Resend ([Resend](https://resend.com/blog/series-a)), a follow-on investor in Inngest's 2024 round and its Series A ([Inngest](https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-funding-from-a16z); [Inngest A](https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-inngest-series-a)) and a new investor in Clerk's Series A ([Clerk](https://clerk.com/blog/series-a)). The Vercel ecosystem looks like a sourcing channel (my inference).\n- **Late entries, not only first checks.** Reducto: First Round led the seed and Benchmark the Series A before a16z led the $75M Series B, six months after the A ([Reducto A](https://reducto.ai/blog/reducto-series-a-funding), 2025-04-25; [Reducto B](https://reducto.ai/blog/reducto-series-b-funding), 2025-10-14). Vals: 8VC and Bloomberg Beta led the seed before a16z led the A ([Vals](https://www.vals.ai/blogs/series-a)). Relace: YC W23, then a16z led the A ([Relace](https://www.relace.ai/blog/series-a-announcement)). True first institutional check only at Phota Labs, QuiverAI and Runta (a16z-led seeds).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** She is early (seed and Series A) and AI-native, which fits, but her lane is AI dev tooling and agent infrastructure, not frontier labs or AI chips. Deals that matter to me: **QuiverAI** and **Phota Labs** (small model companies, a16z-led seeds), **Relace** (models for coding agents, board), **Runta** ($20M a16z-led seed, the size of round I write into) and **Eka Robotics** (robot learning, Series A). She is useful as a scout: her public build posts preview the next category a16z Infra will lead. a16z usually leads these rounds, so co-investment room for a $20M check is limited.\n\n## Background\n- Rice University (LinkedIn; degree and years not listed, https://www.linkedin.com/in/yokoli/). ACM SPLASH poster (HJ-Viz), October 2014 (LinkedIn publications).\n- Engineer at AppDynamics; founding engineer and PM at Transposit; head of product, Terraform Cloud, HashiCorp ([a16z bio](https://a16z.com/author/yoko-li/)). Dates not published.\n- Partner, a16z Infra, covering developer tools, infrastructure, AI and creative tools ([a16z bio](https://a16z.com/author/yoko-li/)). Join date not found; earliest repo-visible a16z artifacts are 2023 (photo upload path, AI-town repo created 2023-07-16).\n- Open-source maintainer (AI-town, AI-tamago, companion-app, local-ai-stack) and cartoonist who explains technical concepts in comics ([a16z bio](https://a16z.com/author/yoko-li/)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://yoko.dev | Listed as blog on GitHub profile (2026-11-10); a16z author page https://a16z.com/author/yoko-li/ |\n| Substack / blog | https://a16z.com/author/yoko-li/ | a16z posts roughly monthly in 2026 |\n| X | https://x.com/stuffyokodraws | Handle confirmed by a16z bio and GitHub profile; follower count not loadable (X returned 402) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/yokoli/ | Sparse; no titles or dates |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/ykhli | 487 followers (GitHub API, 2026-11-10) |\n| Podcasts (host) | a16z podcast feed | Co-hosts a16z AI/devtool episodes (see below) |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nTitles from the a16z author page ([src](https://a16z.com/author/yoko-li/)); no transcripts read this run.\n- 2026 — a16z — Building AI for Creators: Luma and Phota Labs\n- 2026-06 — a16z — AI, Design, and the Power of Open Models (with Ideogram's Mohammad Norouzi and Justine Moore) — https://a16z.com/podcast/ai-design-and-the-power-of-open-models/\n- 2025 — a16z — How Mintlify Is Rebuilding Documentation for Coding Agents; The $3 Trillion AI Coding Opportunity; Google DeepMind Developers: How Nano Banana Was Made\n- 2025 — a16z — MCP Co-Creator on the Next Wave of LLM Innovation; A Deep Dive Into MCP and the Future of AI Tooling; What Is an AI Agent? (two episodes)\n\n## How they think\n- Code is the universal output format for models: visuals as code (SVG, HTML), games as scoring functions, docs for coding agents.\n- Agents need verifiers, precise edits and stopping rules; \"done\" has to be defined and priced up front.\n- Developer experience and craft as signals: she praises founders for craftsmanship and tenacity through pivots (Mintlify, Resend posts).\n- Calls so far: Reducto entered at $75M B (~$600M post per repo rounds data); Vals A at $400M; Inngest followed by Altimeter-led A; Clerk later rounds led by Madrona and CRV with a16z only following.\n\n## Read these first\n1. The Next Frontier of Visual AI Is Code (2026-06-02) — her code-native creative thesis; QuiverAI context.\n2. Knowing When to Stop: The Art of Making a Loop Converge (2026-08-06) — agent-loop framework behind Vals and Runta.\n3. I Built TetrisBench (2026-02-23) — the clearest look at how she evaluates models by building.\n4. Investing in Resend (2024-12-04) — shows the \"used it first\" sourcing pattern.\n5. Emerging Developer Patterns for the AI Era (2025) — her map of agent-era devtools (title only checked).\n\n## Social engagement\nPosts as @stuffyokodraws, mixing cartoons and build demos (a16z bio). Could not load X this run (HTTP 402), so reply and amplification patterns are not verified. Company posts tag her by first name with X links (Resend thanks \"Yoko, Jennifer, and Martin\").\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Upstash | Undisclosed | 2024-02-14 | not stated | co-signer Jennifer Li | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-upstash/) |\n| Inngest | Seed ($6.1M) | 2024-01-30 | yes, named lead with Casado; board observer | GGV, Afore, Guillermo Rauch | [Inngest](https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-funding-from-a16z) |\n| Inngest | Series A ($21M) | 2025-09-16 | no (Altimeter led); a16z continuing | Altimeter, Notable, Afore, Rauch | [Inngest](https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-inngest-series-a) |\n| Mintlify | Series A ($18M) | 2024-09-03 | yes (a16z, board); she is observer | Bain Capital Ventures, YC | [Mintlify](https://mintlify.com/blog/series-a); [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mintlify/) |\n| Resend | Series A ($18M) | 2024-12-04 | yes; she holds board seat | YC, Basecase, Rauch, Grinich, Rowghani | [Resend](https://resend.com/blog/series-a); [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-resend/) |\n| Stainless | Series A ($25M) | 2024-12-10 | yes, led by Jennifer Li; Yoko co-signer | Sequoia, The General Partnership, Felicis, Zapier, MongoDB Ventures | [Stainless](https://www.stainless.com/blog/stainless-series-a) |\n| Phota Labs | Seed | 2025-09-16 | yes; first byline; observer | Casado, Jennifer Li co-sign | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-phota-labs/) |\n| Relace | Series A ($23M) | 2025-10-08 | yes; first byline; she holds board seat | Matrix, YC | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-relace/); [Relace](https://www.relace.ai/blog/series-a-announcement) |\n| Reducto | Series B ($75M) | 2025-10-14 | yes (a16z) | Benchmark, First Round, BoxGroup, YC | [Reducto](https://reducto.ai/blog/reducto-series-b-funding); [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-reducto/) |\n| Keycard | Undisclosed ($38M total announced) | 2025-10-21 | not stated | co-signers Lackey, de la Garza, Aubakirova | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-keycard/); [Keycard](https://www.keycard.ai/blog) |\n| QuiverAI | Seed | 2026-02-25 | yes; first byline | Appenzeller, Casado co-sign | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-quiverai/) |\n| Runta | Seed ($20M) | 2026-07-16 | yes (Casado led) | Ghodsi, Fei-Fei Li, Dean, Wolf, Shriram (angels) | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-runta/) |\n| Vals AI | Series A ($40M at $400M) | 2026-08-13 | yes (a16z) | 8VC, Pear, Bloomberg Beta | [Vals](https://www.vals.ai/blogs/series-a); [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-vals/) |\n| Eka Robotics | Series A | 2026-10-20 | yes (a16z) | Raghuram co-sign | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-eka-robotics/) |\n| Clerk | Undisclosed (a16z existing before Madrona-led A) | not found | no; she is board observer | Madrona, CRV led later rounds | [Clerk A](https://clerk.com/blog/series-a); [Clerk B](https://clerk.com/blog/series-b) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Resend.** Found as users of react-email; a16z led the $18M A and joined the board, with Yoko in the seat ([a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-resend/); [bio](https://a16z.com/author/yoko-li/)).\n- **Inngest.** Casado and Yoko led a $6.1M round on 2024-01-30 with GGV, Afore and Rauch following on; she took an observer seat. a16z ceded the Series A lead to Altimeter in 2025.\n- **QuiverAI.** First byline; the post says founder Joan Rodríguez's hands-on StarVector and RLRF work impressed them; intro path not stated ([a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-quiverai/)).\n- **Runta.** Casado's deal: he has known CEO Guanlan for nearly a decade (Cloudflare, Kong). Yoko is second on the byline ([a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-runta/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Martin Casado (a16z) | Co-lead/co-signer: Inngest, Resend, Phota, Relace, QuiverAI, Runta | 2024 | [Inngest](https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-funding-from-a16z) |\n| Jennifer Li (a16z) | Co-signer: Upstash, Mintlify, Stainless, Reducto, Vals, Resend, Phota | 2024 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-upstash/) |\n| Guido Appenzeller (a16z) | Co-signer: Relace, QuiverAI, Runta | 2025 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-relace/) |\n| Raghu Raghuram (a16z) | Co-signer: Vals, Eka | 2026 | [a16z](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-eka-robotics/) |\n| Guillermo Rauch | Co-investor in Resend, Inngest, Clerk | 2024 | [Resend](https://resend.com/blog/series-a) |\n| Resend; Relace | Board member | 2024; 2025 | [bio](https://a16z.com/author/yoko-li/) |\n| Mintlify; Clerk; Phota Labs; Inngest | Board observer | — | [bio](https://a16z.com/author/yoko-li/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did she join a16z and when was she made partner? No announcement found.\n- How did a16z enter Clerk (round and date) and was she on that deal?\n- Keycard round names and lead (a16z post and company listing do not say).\n- Is she an angel or LP anywhere? Nothing found (search budget exhausted this run).\n- Eka Robotics round size and co-investors.\n\n## Sources\n- https://a16z.com/author/yoko-li/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-resend/ ; https://resend.com/blog/series-a\n- https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-funding-from-a16z ; https://www.inngest.com/blog/announcing-inngest-series-a\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-mintlify/ ; https://mintlify.com/blog/series-a\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-stainless/ ; https://www.stainless.com/blog/stainless-series-a\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-upstash/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-phota-labs/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-relace/ ; https://www.relace.ai/blog/series-a-announcement\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-reducto/ ; https://reducto.ai/blog/reducto-series-b-funding ; https://reducto.ai/blog/reducto-series-a-funding\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-keycard/ ; https://www.keycard.ai/blog\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-quiverai/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-runta/ ; https://runta.com/blog/runta-the-execution-layer-for-agents/\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-vals/ ; https://www.vals.ai/blogs/series-a\n- https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-eka-robotics/\n- https://clerk.com/blog/series-a ; https://clerk.com/blog/series-b\n- https://a16z.com/the-next-frontier-of-visual-ai-is-code/\n- https://a16z.com/knowing-when-to-stop-the-art-of-making-a-loop-converge/\n- https://a16z.com/i-built-tetrisbench-where-llms-compete-at-playing-tetris-heres-what-i-found/\n- https://api.github.com/users/ykhli ; https://api.github.com/repos/a16z-infra/ai-town\n- https://www.linkedin.com/in/yokoli/\n", "person:zach-fredericks": "\n# Zach Fredericks\n\n*Written 2026-11-10 by the Etched price-setters pass. Sources: his team page and eleven posts on primary.vc, Primary's portfolio cards, and the Heavy Hitters podcast listing. I used two web searches and read no podcast transcripts. Primary's bio uses \"he\".*\n\n**Dates on primary.vc are unreliable.** Unrelated posts share the same \"Published\" stamps. Four carry February 10, 2026 (the day Fund V launched with the new site), and three carry September 30, 2025, including a post about the 2020–21 pandemic supply chain. These look like stamps from a site migration, not publish dates. Only the following dates are used as real here:\n- the flexibility post, 2026-07-22\n- the GETs post, 2026-05-15\n- the critical-minerals post, 2025-05-05\n- the interoperability post, 2024-11-05\n- the powered-land post, 2026-11-06\n\n## One-line take\nFredericks is Primary's industrials principal. He has turned a supply-chain seed practice into the firm's public voice on the power bottleneck in the AI buildout. He argues the binding constraint has moved from chips to energy, then to coordination, and now to community permission. He has **no role in Etched or in any chip, compute or lab deal**.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** He is an ex-operator who led product at Loadsmart from seed through Series D ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/zach-fredericks)). He reads the AI buildout as a supply-chain optimization problem. The flexibility post says it plainly: \"That’s our background, so it’s what we know\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-energy-flexibility-stack-how-data-centers-that-bend-get-power-faster), 2026-07-22).\n- **How he works:** He writes a newsletter backed by expert calls, sharpens a thesis in public, and publishes a list of startup archetypes before the deals exist.\n- **Constraint:** He is a principal at a seed and pre-seed firm whose energy lane only opened in 2025: \"In 2025, we’re expanding our focus to include energy\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/critical-mineral-exploration-and-ai)). His bio says \"He gravitates toward asset-light models\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/zach-fredericks)). I found no energy or data-center-power deal on any card yet.\n- **What he'd never do:** Back an asset-heavy power developer for venture returns. In his words, \"most will earn infrastructure returns\", and the venture outcomes go to the software layer ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-energy-flexibility-stack-how-data-centers-that-bend-get-power-faster)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **He draws the line against Primary's own compute practice.** He calls chip and algorithm efficiency work important, done by \"many smart individuals (including our colleagues)\". He then says the constraint his team focuses on is \"the broader system-level energy relationship\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-energy-intelligence-hypothesis), date unreliable). Read with \"We think the frontier of constraint has moved\" (same post), this means Primary is running two separate bets on the same buildout: Schechter and Lorenzi on silicon (Etched), and Fredericks on the electrons. The colleague reference is most likely to Schechter's compute practice, though that is an inference.\n- **He reframes the power shortage as a coordination failure.** He writes: \"we view the energy constraint as a failure of coordination\". He also claims there is \"enough capacity on the grid already to meet the projected data center electricity demand\" over five years, if loads can curtail ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/grid-enhancing-technologies-and-the-time-to-power-economy), 2026-05-15). This is contrarian against the \"build more generation\" consensus.\n- **Where he says venture returns sit in grid tech.** The control and orchestration layer offers \"the most attractive risk–return profile for venture\" (same source).\n- **His selection test for flexibility startups:** \"Do they own the dispatch decision or merely advise it?\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-energy-flexibility-stack-how-data-centers-that-bend-get-power-faster), 2026-07-22).\n- **His sourcing network shows in the turbine post** ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-gas-turbine-bottleneck-reshaping-energy-infrastructure-ex8qe), date unreliable). The post quotes people who \"told us\":\n  - a Texas midstream CFO, who called turbine pricing \"cartel pricing\"\n  - \"an executive responsible for the data center buildout at Microsoft\"\n  - a hyperscaler chief strategy officer\n  - a contact near the DOE's new venture fund\n- **Two hidden details from that post:**\n  - The DOE contact said the administration was \"considering whether to pressure allies with outstanding GE turbine orders\" to release slots to US buyers.\n  - The hyperscaler CSO is \"signing these LOIs freely, while assuming most will never reach operation\". In other words, nuclear and geothermal PPAs are options, not commitments.\n- **He is open to a heavy-industry bet in principle.** On building an \"Anduril for turbines\": \"(though we’d entertain it)\" (same source).\n- **His view on the powered-land market:** \"Permission is now the constraint du jour of the AI buildout.\" The smartest infrastructure investors he knows \"think half of that, at best, actually shows up\" of the forecast capacity. He calls today's market \"mostly land flippers holding options on dirt\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/powered-land-people-actually-want), 2026-11-06).\n- **On Primary's industrials investing before AI:** a 2024 podcast listing says he explained a business-model shift to \"sell work not SaaS\". That phrase is from the host's episode description, not a transcript ([src](https://anchor.fm/heavyhittersindustrial), 2024-09-23).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He matters as the best map Primary has of the power side of the compute buildout. He names the companies in that stack (Emerald AI, Phaidra, Lumora, Shatterdome) and the archetypes he wants (a flexible interconnection platform, a behind-the-meter load controller, curtailment insurance). He is not a chip, lab or pre-training investor, though.\n\nHis cards are all supply chain, industrials, mining or ocean. The closest one to THESIS.md's \"AI-native automation\" clause is Forgepoint, a 2026 seed building \"AI workers for back office departments\" for manufacturers ([src](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization)). Caspian (AI trade compliance) and the trades AI-procurement seed are close behind. He is a seed-check principal, so he is not a co-investor for $20M checks into large seeds. Use him for leads on data-center power and AI-native industrial automation, and as an early read on where Primary's energy lane will deploy.\n\n## Background\n- Founded a plumbing equipment manufacturer in India. Date not stated ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/zach-fredericks)).\n- Worked in BlackRock's private equity group (same source). Aggregator snippets say Analyst at BlackRock Private Equity Partners and a BA in International Relations from Brown. Both are unverified, from a search summary of TheOrg/MarketScreener.\n- Led product at Loadsmart \"from seed through Series D\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/zach-fredericks)). Loadsmart's Series D was $200M in February 2022 ([press](https://www.spglobal.com/market-intelligence/en/news-insights/articles/2022/2/loadsmart-raises-200m-in-series-d-round-68687655)). He was \"at a logistics technology company\" during the pandemic ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-energy-flexibility-stack-how-data-centers-that-bend-get-power-faster)).\n- While operating, founded the Supply Chain Collective, a syndicate backing early supply-chain tech ([src](https://www.primary.vc/team/zach-fredericks)).\n- Joined Primary at a date not found. He was there by 2024-09-23, when the Heavy Hitters episode listed him as with Primary ([src](https://anchor.fm/heavyhittersindustrial)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.primary.vc/team/zach-fredericks | Team page with his thesis statement and cards |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.primary.vc/insights | Writes Primary's industrials and energy newsletter. Eleven posts linked from his page |\n| X | https://x.com/zfredericks | Linked from the team page. Not readable without login, so follower count unknown |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/zach-fredericks/ | Linked from the team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-09-23: Heavy Hitters: The Digital Industrial Podcast (host Ty Findley), ep. 93, \"Zach Fredericks, Primary Venture Partners - Road Ahead for Supply Chain\", about 38 minutes ([src](https://anchor.fm/heavyhittersindustrial), [Spotify](https://open.spotify.com/episode/5Y3DAZ2AA0oNBtnwMS2AYF)). The description covers interoperability, AI in logistics, and \"sell work not SaaS\". I read the description only, not a transcript.\n- Undated: The Future of Supply Chain (Dynamo Ventures podcast), ep. 196, \"Surprises and opportunities in supply chain investing with Zach Fredericks of Primary Venture Partners\" ([listing](https://he.player.fm/series/the-future-of-supply-chain-a-dynamo-ventures-podcast/ep-196-surprises-and-opportunities-in-supply-chain-investing-with-zach-fredericks-of-primary-venture-partners)). The page was blocked by Cloudflare and not read.\n\n## How they think\n- **The constraint keeps moving, and he follows it:**\n  - The energy-intelligence hypothesis: chips can't out-run system-level losses. He points to the PUE plateau at about 1.6, the \"Bridge-Plant Trap\" of simple-cycle turbines, and transformer backlogs.\n  - Turbine scarcity.\n  - GETs: coordination, not steel. \"a traditional grid interconnection takes eight years\" versus 18 months for a GPU cluster.\n  - The flexibility stack: \"Flexibility is how you buy speed\".\n  - Powered land: permission is the constraint.\n  - Sources: [EIH](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-energy-intelligence-hypothesis), [turbines](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-gas-turbine-bottleneck-reshaping-energy-infrastructure-ex8qe), [GETs](https://www.primary.vc/articles/grid-enhancing-technologies-and-the-time-to-power-economy), [flex](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-energy-flexibility-stack-how-data-centers-that-bend-get-power-faster), [land](https://www.primary.vc/articles/powered-land-people-actually-want).\n- **The 2020 supply chain as a template for the 2026 energy chain:** \"“Redundancy” was the term we all used in 2020, and it’s “flexibility” now\". Lyric is his example of an optimizer that won the last cycle ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-energy-flexibility-stack-how-data-centers-that-bend-get-power-faster)).\n- **AI-native by default:** \"It should go without saying that this whole stack has to be AI-native.\" (same source).\n- **Asset-light seed bets inside hard sectors.** In mining he favours \"AI survey-exploration hybrids and HaaS companies\" and avoids pure software, which he says faces TAM limits ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/critical-mineral-exploration-and-ai), 2025-05-05).\n- **Contrarian calls:**\n  - The buildout will take \"much longer than people think\" ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/powered-land-people-actually-want)).\n  - Gas is no longer the fast path. Its timelines look like SMR timelines ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-gas-turbine-bottleneck-reshaping-energy-infrastructure-ex8qe)).\n- **Track record:** too early to judge. His energy calls are 2026 essays with no deals attached yet.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [The Energy Flexibility Stack](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-energy-flexibility-stack-how-data-centers-that-bend-get-power-faster) (2026-07-22). His market map and his selection test (\"own the dispatch decision\").\n2. [Grid-Enhancing Technologies and the Time-to-Power Economy](https://www.primary.vc/articles/grid-enhancing-technologies-and-the-time-to-power-economy) (2026-05-15). The coordination thesis and five startup archetypes.\n3. [Powered land people actually want](https://www.primary.vc/articles/powered-land-people-actually-want) (2026-11-06). Permission as the new bottleneck.\n4. [The gas turbine bottleneck](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-gas-turbine-bottleneck-reshaping-energy-infrastructure-ex8qe). The best view of his expert-call network.\n5. [The Energy-Intelligence Hypothesis](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-energy-intelligence-hypothesis). Where he separates his lane from the chip lane.\n\n## Social engagement\nI couldn't read X (@zfredericks) without logging in, so I found nothing on whom he amplifies. His writing cites SemiAnalysis, Epoch AI, Duke's Nicholas Institute and Cleanview as data sources ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/powered-land-people-actually-want), [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-energy-flexibility-stack-how-data-centers-that-bend-get-power-faster)).\n\n## Portfolio\nCards list the Primary investors currently assigned to each company. A card date earlier than his arrival (for example Bedrock in 2020, while he was at Loadsmart) means he took over stewardship later, not that he sourced the deal.\n\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bedrock Ocean (seafloor AUV mapping) | Card: Series A, 2020. Primary \"co-leading their seed round\". Followed on in the $25M round | 2020 (card) | Primary co-led seed. Not lead on the $25M round | Card co-lists Brad Svrluga. $25M round co-led by Costanoa, Harmony Partners, Katapult. Also Northzone, R7, Eniac, Quiet Capital | [card](https://www.primary.vc/team/zach-fredericks), [post](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-future-of-ocean-intelligence) |\n| Lyric (AI supply-chain modeling) | Card: Series B. Primary led the seed and joined the $44M B | 2023 (card) | Primary led seed. B led by others (Insight Partners, VMG Catalyst, Permanent Capital named) | Card co-lists Cassie Young | [card](https://www.primary.vc/team/zach-fredericks), [post](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-lyric--again) |\n| Trades AI-procurement company, founders Dave Widger and Darion Miller (probably Ply; name needs-id) | Seed | 2023 | not stated | Card co-lists Jason Shuman | [card](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization) |\n| Cordage (US–Mexico supply-chain software) | Series A | 2024 | not stated | Card co-lists Brad Svrluga | [card](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization) |\n| Caspian (AI trade compliance and duty recovery) | Seed | 2024 | not stated | Card co-lists Emily Man | [card](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization) |\n| ExploreTech (AI critical-mineral exploration) | Pre-seed | 2025 | not stated (he is the only Primary name) | — | [card](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization), [post](https://www.primary.vc/articles/rebuilding-minings-risk-model-with-ai) |\n| Forgepoint (AI back-office workers for US manufacturers) | Seed | 2026 | not stated | Card co-lists Brad Svrluga | [card](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization) |\n\n**AI-chip, compute and frontier-AI deals: none.** He is not named on Etched in any round: the seed lead (2023-03), the Series A co-lead (2024-06-25), the Stripes B (2025-12), the Sequoia C (2026-07-23) or the Jane Street-led $700M (2026-08-18). I checked:\n- Primary's Etched posts ([seed/thesis](https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond), [every round](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round))\n- the [compute call for startups](https://www.primary.vc/articles/primarys-call-for-compute-startups)\n- the Fund V post\n- every card on his page\n\nThe Etched card names Schechter and Citron ([src](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization)). The energy flexibility post names Lyric as \"in our own portfolio\" but names no portfolio company in data-center power.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **ExploreTech (pre-seed, 2025):** This is the clearest deal of his own: he is the sole Primary name on the card. The minerals newsletter came first and laid out the thesis ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/critical-mineral-exploration-and-ai), 2025-05-05). The deal post followed and credits founders who are Stanford Mineral-X alumni with field experience at Rio Tinto, Freeport and Glencore ([src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/rebuilding-minings-risk-model-with-ai)). Thesis first, deal second: the same order as Schechter's Etched story. Intro path not found.\n- **Bedrock and Lyric:** These were inherited or shared. Primary's seed positions predate any evidence he was at the firm, and his name appears on the follow-on posts and cards.\n- **Data-center power:** No deal yet. The flexibility and powered-land posts read as a public call for founders.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Primary Venture Partners | Principal, Industrials | join date not found | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/zach-fredericks) |\n| Brad Svrluga (Primary MP, co-CIO) | Co-listed on Bedrock, Cordage and Forgepoint. Co-author on the critical-minerals post | — | [src](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization), [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/critical-mineral-exploration-and-ai) |\n| Cassie Young (Primary partner) | Co-listed on Lyric | — | [src](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization) |\n| Jason Shuman (Primary partner) | Co-listed on the trades AI-procurement seed | — | [src](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization) |\n| Emily Man (Primary partner) | Co-listed on Caspian | — | [src](https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization) |\n| Wyatt Bramhall, Devon Pasieka | Industrials team (Incubation Associate; Associate). Bramhall co-bylined on the interoperability post | — | [src](https://www.primary.vc/people), [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-solving-for-interoperability-is-a-huge-challenge--and-opportunity--in-supply-chain) |\n| Brian Schechter, Gaby Lorenzi (Primary compute) | Separate lane on the same buildout. Not co-listed on any deal. He refers to chip-efficiency colleagues | — | [src](https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-energy-intelligence-hypothesis) |\n| Loadsmart | Former product leader, seed through Series D | before Primary | [src](https://www.primary.vc/team/zach-fredericks) |\n| BlackRock private equity | Former | before Loadsmart | same |\n| Supply Chain Collective | Founder (syndicate) | while operating | same |\n| Ty Findley (Heavy Hitters host) | Podcast guest | 2024-09 | [src](https://anchor.fm/heavyhittersindustrial) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When did he join Primary, and with what title? Has he been promoted? Primary hasn't announced it, and LinkedIn couldn't be read.\n- Pricing: I found no stated valuation, ownership or check-size discipline. His only filters are selection tests (\"own the dispatch decision\") and an asset-light preference. How these translate into price is unknown.\n- Has Primary made any data-center power or flexibility investment yet, possibly in stealth? Is he the deal lead or does a partner sponsor it?\n- Does he work with Schechter on overlap areas? Primary's compute call includes \"power delivery, from the grid to a transistor\", which sits right on his border.\n- Who is the \"contact close to the Department of Energy's new venture fund\"? Which hyperscaler CSO?\n- The Dynamo Ventures ep. 196 and Heavy Hitters ep. 93 transcripts: read them for verbatim deal stories.\n- Confirm the name of the trades AI-procurement company (probably Ply).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.primary.vc/team/zach-fredericks (team page, cards, links; headshot checked to load 2026-11-10)\n- https://www.primary.vc/people\n- https://www.primary.vc/portfolio/specialization (pages 1–5)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-energy-flexibility-stack-how-data-centers-that-bend-get-power-faster (2026-07-22)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/powered-land-people-actually-want (2026-11-06)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/grid-enhancing-technologies-and-the-time-to-power-economy (2026-05-15)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-energy-intelligence-hypothesis (stamp 2026-02-10, unreliable)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-gas-turbine-bottleneck-reshaping-energy-infrastructure-ex8qe (stamp 2026-02-10, unreliable)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/critical-mineral-exploration-and-ai (2025-05-05)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/rebuilding-minings-risk-model-with-ai (stamp unreliable)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-future-of-ocean-intelligence (stamp unreliable)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-lyric--again (Cassie Young; stamp unreliable)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-solving-for-interoperability-is-a-huge-challenge--and-opportunity--in-supply-chain (2024-11-05)\n- https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond ; https://www.primary.vc/articles/why-we-invested-in-etched-in-every-round ; https://www.primary.vc/articles/primarys-call-for-compute-startups ; https://www.primary.vc/articles/scaling-for-the-ai-era-fund-v (checked for any Fredericks role; none)\n- https://anchor.fm/heavyhittersindustrial (ep. 93 listing, 2024-09-23) ; https://open.spotify.com/episode/5Y3DAZ2AA0oNBtnwMS2AYF\n- https://he.player.fm/series/the-future-of-supply-chain-a-dynamo-ventures-podcast/ep-196-surprises-and-opportunities-in-supply-chain-investing-with-zach-fredericks-of-primary-venture-partners (listing only)\n- https://theorg.com/org/primary-vc/org-chart/zach-fredericks (aggregator)\n- https://www.spglobal.com/market-intelligence/en/news-insights/articles/2022/2/loadsmart-raises-200m-in-series-d-round-68687655 (press)\n", "person:ziv-reichert": "\n# Ziv Reichert\n\n## One-line take\nBorn 1993, they run LocalGlobe's investment team. The firm credits them with Mistral, Gamma and Granola, but press never names them on those rounds.\n\n**known_for:** Runs Phoenix Court/LocalGlobe's investment team; firm credits them with Mistral, Gamma, Granola, MicroAGI; sits on Wonder Studios' board.\n\n## Crux\nThey are the most senior investment person below Saul and Robin Klein. Their bio says they \"oversees the investment team, investment strategy, and technology\" [src](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team). LocalGlobe usually joins other investors' rounds instead of leading them, so its founders' announcements name the lead's partner, not them. That is why the knowledge base had no deals for them. The only deal a primary source ties them to is a board seat at Wonder Studios. Final approval still sits with the Klein-controlled Phoenix Court Group [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/12156857/persons-with-significant-control). Whether they sit on an investment committee is unconfirmed.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **The firm credits them with deals, but only one filing names them.** The firm's list for them is \"Key investments: Model ML, Granola, microagi, Gamma, Circle, Mistral, Wonder Studios, Frontier Computing\" [src](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team). No press release for those rounds names them. Treat the attribution as the firm's own claim.\n- **Wonder Studios: probably in first, and on the board even though Atomico led.** Companies House shows \"Appointment of Ziv Reichert as a director, effective 7 Jan 2026\" at Rabbit Hole Labs Ltd (16002564), using the LocalGlobe / Phoenix Court service address [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/16002564/officers). Rabbit Hole Labs is Wonder's legal entity: its directors Xavier Collins and Justin Hackney match Wonder's CEO and co-founder in press [src](https://uktech.news/ai/ai-film-studio-wonder-lands-9m-investment-20251023). The 2026-10 confirmation statement shows LocalGlobe XIII, L.P. holding 242,603 Seed 1 preferred shares, against Blackbird's 94,674, plus 51,230 Seed 2 preferred. Atomico VI holds 216,971 Seed 2 [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/16002564/filing-history). So LocalGlobe was the largest Seed 1 holder and probably came in first. It then re-upped in Atomico's $12M seed, and Ziv took the board seat afterwards. Whether LocalGlobe led Seed 1 is unconfirmed.\n- **The Kleins trust them outside the fund too.** They were a founding director of Players United Ltd (14593685) from 2023-01 to 2024-12. Robin Klein was its PSC when it was incorporated, then Improbable Worlds, and Saul Klein was also a director [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14593685/filing-history).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** They are the person to call at LocalGlobe on a European frontier-AI seed. LocalGlobe often co-invests in big seeds, such as Mistral's seed and MicroAGI's $55M seed, but rarely leads them. That fits a $20M check partner who wants a London seed fund alongside. The deals that matter here are Mistral, MicroAGI, Wonder Studios and Frontier Computing (identity unconfirmed). They can probably shape LocalGlobe's allocation, but a $10M+ commitment likely needs Klein sign-off (unconfirmed).\n\n## Background\n- Born January 1993; British (Companies House) [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14593685/officers).\n- Previous roles: Fat Llama, Bringg and Blue Wire Capital. The firm describes \"over a decade as an investor and as a startup operator\" [src](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team). Titles and dates were not found.\n- Director of Warchild Associates Ltd from 2013 (now dissolved) [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/emWim8bJOyzSNEJzTVXRwNowM9s/appointments).\n- Using the Phoenix Court address by 2023-01 [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14593685/officers). Their LocalGlobe start date and how they got the seat are unconfirmed.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team | Firm bio, 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | medium.com/@zivreichert returned 403 |\n| X | https://twitter.com/zivreichert | Linked from firm page; not readable (402) |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/zivreichert/ | Linked from firm page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found. Web search was exhausted in this run.\n\n## How they think\nNothing first-hand found. Their bio's mention of technology suggests they also own Phoenix Court's internal tooling (inference).\n\n## Read these first\nNothing found.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked; X was not readable.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Wonder Studios | Seed ($12M) re-up; board from 2026-01-07 | 2025-10 | no (Atomico) | Atomico, Blackbird, Adobe Ventures, Upside | [CH](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/16002564/officers), [press](https://uktech.news/ai/ai-film-studio-wonder-lands-9m-investment-20251023) |\n| Wonder Studios | Pre-seed / Seed 1 (LocalGlobe XIII largest holder) | before 2025-10 | unconfirmed | Blackbird | [CH](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/16002564/filing-history) |\n| Mistral, Gamma, Granola, MicroAGI, Model ML, Circle, Frontier Computing | firm-attributed; rounds not tied to them | | | | [firm](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team) |\n\n## Deal stories\nWonder Studios: LocalGlobe XIII and Blackbird hold the Seed 1 preferred shares. Atomico (Ben Blume) led the $12M Seed 2 in 2025-10. Ziv joined the board in 2026-01, at the same time as Blume [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/16002564/filing-history). We don't know how the first contact happened.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| LocalGlobe / Phoenix Court | Managing Partner, Investment | unconfirmed | [src](https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team) |\n| Saul Klein, Robin Klein | Co-directors in a Klein-incubated company (Players United) | 2023 | [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14593685/filing-history) |\n| Ben Blume (Atomico) | Fellow Wonder Studios board member | 2026-01 | [src](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/16002564/officers) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which Mistral round does the firm credit them with, and were they involved at the 2023 seed?\n- What are \"Frontier Computing\" and \"Circle\"?\n- When did they become Managing Partner, and what were they promoted from?\n- Do they sit on the investment committee?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.phoenixcourt.vc/team\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/16002564/officers\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/16002564/filing-history\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14593685/officers\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/14593685/filing-history\n- https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/emWim8bJOyzSNEJzTVXRwNowM9s/appointments\n- https://uktech.news/ai/ai-film-studio-wonder-lands-9m-investment-20251023\n", "person:ziyaad-khayrattee": "\n# Ziyaad Khayrattee\n\n## One-line take\nHe is the crossover-fund growth underwriter Bier brought in after the research bench left. He is the most senior investor at Factorial after Bier, but no deal names him personally [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250619131153/https://factorialfunds.com/team).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he matters:** He \"co-headed the D. E. Shaw Group's private venture and growth investing arm\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250619131153/https://factorialfunds.com/team). That is hedge-fund-style growth underwriting, which fits Factorial's large single-name opportunity vehicles.\n- **Constraint:** He has no ownership in the adviser and is not a control person on the ADV; Sol Bier is the sole owner [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/319107/PDF/319107.pdf). No deal is publicly attributed to him.\n\n## Differential insight\n- He appeared on the team page between 2024-10 and 2025-02-15. By 2025-06 he was listed under \"Executive Team\", alongside Bier and Rich Miner [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250215230138/https://factorialfunds.com/team) [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250619131153/https://factorialfunds.com/team). His arrival coincides with Fund II's first close (2025-05) and the move to leading rounds.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. He is a growth investor, not a seed one. He is relevant as a likely diligence lead on Factorial's large AI allocations and co-leads (possibly Fractile; unconfirmed).\n\n## Background\n- BA in finance and liberal arts (Plan II Honors), UT Austin; sits on the Plan II Board of Visitors [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250619131153/https://factorialfunds.com/team).\n- M&A banking at J.P. Morgan and Evercore; technology investor at Summit Partners; co-head of private venture and growth at D. E. Shaw; Partner at Factorial Funds (2025–).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://factorialfunds.com/team | |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | none found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/ziyaadkhayrattee/ | |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNothing found.\n\n## How they think\nNothing public found.\n\n## Read these first\nNothing public found.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| none named personally | | | | | |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Factorial Funds | Partner | 2025-02 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250215230138/https://factorialfunds.com/team) |\n| D. E. Shaw | former co-head, private venture and growth | | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250619131153/https://factorialfunds.com/team) |\n| Summit Partners | former technology investor | | same |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which D. E. Shaw private deals did he run (D. E. Shaw backed AI names at growth)?\n- Did he lead diligence on Fractile?\n\n## Sources\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20250619131153/https://factorialfunds.com/team\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20250215230138/https://factorialfunds.com/team\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/319107/PDF/319107.pdf\n", "firm:137-ventures": "\n# 137 Ventures\n\n## One-line take\nA San Francisco firm built on **lending to and buying from founders and early employees** of companies that stay private for a long time. It holds more than 1% of SpaceX ([Bloomberg](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/spacex-investor-137-raises-700-million-ahead-of-musk-led-ipo)) and now increasingly writes primary checks into AI, defense and industrial companies (Cognition, Hadrian, Physical Intelligence, Armada, Together AI, Crusoe, Applied Intuition). **Two founders control it:** Justin Fishner-Wolfson owns 50–75% of the adviser and Alex Jacobson owns 25–50% ([ADV Schedule A](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/162351/PDF/162351.pdf)). It is not a seed investor. The lens fit is medium, for two reasons: it is a large follow-on and co-lead source in AI hardware and defense rounds, and its brochure discloses a **fund-of-funds program for emerging managers** ([ADV Part 2A](https://files.adviserinfo.sec.gov/IAPD/Content/Common/crd_iapd_Brochure.aspx?BRCHR_VRSN_ID=1037196)).\n\n## People pass 2026-11-10\n**Whose money.** It is outside money in pooled funds and per-company SPVs, with a large GP and insider stake. It does not come from a parent balance sheet. The ADV filed 2026-04-15 lists 42 private funds, all advised by 137 Ventures Management, LLC ([ADV 7.B.(1)](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/162351/PDF/162351.pdf)):\n- **Flagship funds.** 137 Ventures II–VII have gross asset values of $454M to $830M each. Each has 49–111 beneficial owners, of which 4–38% are fund-of-funds. Related persons own 7–24% of each fund. Fund VII ($454M GAV, 62 owners) has the highest fund-of-funds share at 38%.\n- **SPVs.** A family of code-named \"137 Holdings\" SPVs holds most of the money. 137 Holdings SX alone has **$3.65B GAV from 99 owners, 40% owned by 137 insiders**. SX through SXXI add roughly $3.1B more. Reading \"SX\" as SpaceX is my inference and is unconfirmed.\n- **Two AI-coded SPVs.** **137 Holdings AI II, LLC** ($254.8M, 37 owners) and **137 Holdings AI, LLC** ($47.3M, 27 owners). Which company \"AI\" refers to is **unconfirmed**. It may be Applied Intuition.\n- **Evergreen vehicles.** Two evergreen vehicles are 100% owned by related persons: 137 Ventures Evergreen LLC ($22.4M) and LP ($15.0M). A vehicle called **Fishner-Wolfson Investments, LLC** ($53.3M) has 19 owners, 17% of them related persons.\n\n**Who approves.** Schedule A lists **Justin Fishner-Wolfson** (Managing Member since 06/2011, ownership code D, 50–75%) and **S. Alexander (Stuart Alexander) Jacobson** (Managing Member since 06/2011, code C, 25–50%) as the only owners above 5%. Fishner-Wolfson signed every fund Form D from 2011 to 2022 as managing member of the GP. Since the 2025 Fund VII Form D, the signatory has been CFO **Andrew Hansen** ([Form D VII](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2065745/000147083125000014/primary_doc.xml)). The other control persons listed, all below 5%, are Hansen (CFO/COO, 01/2013), **Nicholas Procaccini** (Partner, 04/2019), **Christian Garrett** (Partner, 01/2025), Alexandra Andorfer (GC/CCO, 01/2026) and **James Pardee** (Partner, 03/2026).\n\n**Promotions.** The ADV shows a clean promotion ladder: Procaccini made partner in 04/2019, Garrett in 01/2025 and Pardee in 03/2026. The April 2026 fund announcement confirms Pardee's promotion ([src](https://pulse2.com/137-ventures-700-million-raised-across-two-new-funds-as-aum-surpasses-15-billion/)). Garrett joined in 2019 from Caerus Investment Advisors ([bio](https://137ventures.com/team/christian-garrett)), so he went from joining to owner-partner in about 5.5 years.\n\n**Who did the AI deals.** Sources name a 137 person on **only one** of the five AI rounds in the repo. Alex Jacobson is the \"founding advisor to Armada\" ([bio](https://137ventures.com/team/s-alexander-jacobson)), and he discussed Armada's edge-compute model on TBPN (summary only, [src](https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-08-06/137-ventures-alex-jacobson-on-why-spacex-style-private-liquidity-beats-public-markets-for-employees)). Fishner-Wolfson is the firm's public voice on **Cognition** and **Hadrian** ([Sourcery, 2026-06](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/new-how-137-ventures-built-a-1-spacex)) and is quoted on the **Impulse Space** Series D that 137 co-led ([Via Satellite](https://www.satellitetoday.com/finance/2026/06/02/impulse-space-raises-another-massive-funding-round-in-500m-series-d/)). For Applied Intuition (Series F), Crusoe (Series E) and Together AI (Series A), the announcements list 137 with no person named. I checked the Applied Intuition, Crusoe and Together AI posts, the Armada Series A release, the Hadrian TechCrunch story, 10 fund Form Ds, the ADV and brochure, all 23 current bios, and archived bios for 12 former staff.\n\n## People\n\"Deals personally named on\" counts only deals where a source names the person.\n\n| Photo | Name | Role | Joined | Deals personally named on | Profile |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6899a5586c63433ee793d4be/6899a5586c63433ee793d4f3_Justin%20Fishner-Wolfson.webp) | Justin Fishner-Wolfson | Founder & Managing Partner; 50–75% owner; ex-Founders Fund investment team (monitored the 2008 SpaceX deal) | 2011-06 [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/162351/PDF/162351.pdf) | Impulse Space Series D ($500M, co-lead, 2026-06), quoted [src](https://www.satellitetoday.com/finance/2026/06/02/impulse-space-raises-another-massive-funding-round-in-500m-series-d/). Gives 137's Cognition thesis and Hadrian position on Sourcery (2026-06), but is not named as the deal lead [src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/new-how-137-ventures-built-a-1-spacex). SpaceX (decade-plus position; Bloomberg interview 2026-04) | lead `justin-fishner-wolfson` |\n| ![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6899a5586c63433ee793d4be/6899a5586c63433ee793d4f6_S.%20Alexander%20Jacobson.webp) | S. Alexander (Alex) Jacobson | Co-founder & Investment Partner; 25–50% owner; ex-Founders Fund EIR who led SpaceX diligence | 2011-06 [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/162351/PDF/162351.pdf) | **Armada**: founding advisor [bio](https://137ventures.com/team/s-alexander-jacobson); 137 was in the Series A (2023-12). That he ran 137's check is unconfirmed. Personal: founding investor in ImmuneBridge; advisory board of Strategy Capital (Hamilton Helmer) | lead `alex-jacobson` |\n| ![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6899a5586c63433ee793d4be/6899a5586c63433ee793d4f4_Christian%20Garrett.webp) | Christian Garrett | Investment Partner (ADV partner since 01/2025); co-founder of the Hill & Valley Forum; ex-Caerus Investment Advisors; VCJ Rising Star 2020 | 2019 [bio](https://137ventures.com/team/christian-garrett) | None found. Pictured with the founders in the NYT/Getty coverage; his likely deal areas are defense and policy, which is unconfirmed | lead `christian-garrett` |\n| ![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6899a5586c63433ee793d4be/6899a5586c63433ee793d4fa_James%20Pardee.webp) | James Pardee | Investment Partner; **promoted to Partner 03/2026**; ex-Raymond James internet equity research | join date not found [bio](https://137ventures.com/team/james-pardee) | None found | lead `james-pardee` |\n| ![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6899a5586c63433ee793d4be/6899a5586c63433ee793d4fc_Nicholas%20Procaccini.webp) | Nicholas (Nick) Procaccini | Investment Partner (ADV partner since 04/2019); CFA; ex-UNC Management Company (endowment) | before 2019-04 (exact date not found) [bio](https://137ventures.com/team/nicholas-procaccini) | None found | lead `nicholas-procaccini` |\n| ![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6899a5586c63433ee793d4be/6a3258410c2c4374e36a538b_AndrewHansen_137Ventures.webp) | Andrew Hansen | Partner, CFO & COO; signs the Fund VII Form D; ex-Founders Fund operations ($50M to $1.5B AUM) | 2013-01 [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/162351/PDF/162351.pdf) | None (operations) | lead `andrew-hansen` |\n| ![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6899a5586c63433ee793d4be/68a70c951972c65a5033d6a0_Henry-Hiemstra.webp) | Henry Hiemstra | Associate; ex-FT Partners | about 2025-08 (headshot upload date; unconfirmed) [bio](https://137ventures.com/team/henry-hiemstra) | None found | lead `henry-hiemstra` |\n| ![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6899a5586c63433ee793d4be/6a0ec0bf8a52bf133f23f1de_Henry%20Pace.jpeg) | Henry Pace | Sr. Analyst; ex-Cantor Fitzgerald (software/AI M&A), FT Partners | about 2026-05 (unconfirmed) [bio](https://137ventures.com/team/henry-pace) | None found | lead `henry-pace` |\n| ![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6899a5586c63433ee793d4be/69e73d9ac5060cad496c5422_piero-vasquez.png) | Piero Vasquez | Sr. Analyst; ex-J.P. Morgan, Jefferies tech banking | about 2026-04 (unconfirmed) [bio](https://137ventures.com/team/piero-vasquez) | None found | lead `piero-vasquez` |\n| none | Lindsay Searer | Head of Fund Strategies (LP side); ex-Pathstone/Crestone alternatives, CFA | 2026 [src](https://pulse2.com/137-ventures-700-million-raised-across-two-new-funds-as-aum-surpasses-15-billion/) | None (likely runs the emerging-manager fund-of-funds program; unconfirmed) | lead `lindsay-searer` |\n| none | Ashley Steinberg | Head of Strategic Initiatives (portfolio public policy); ex-head of legal, The Boring Company | 2025-12 to 2026-04 [src](https://137ventures.com/team/ashley-steinberg) | None | lead `ashley-steinberg` |\n| none | Alexandra Andorfer | General Counsel & CCO | on site by 2025-06; CCO 01/2026 [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/162351/PDF/162351.pdf) | None | lead `alexandra-andorfer` (skip) |\n| none | Andrew (Andy) Laszlo | **Former** Investment Partner; ex-VantagePoint partner, Intel Capital director | on site 2014-03; last seen 2025-03; gone from the 2026 page [archive](http://web.archive.org/web/20250320115711/https://www.137ventures.com/team/andrew-laszlo) | None found | lead `andrew-laszlo` |\n| none | Alex Lurie | **Former** Director of Legal & Business Affairs; \"leads 137's transactions\" and fund structuring | seen 2022–2024 [archive](http://web.archive.org/web/20240524000222/https://www.137ventures.com/team/alex-lurie) | None found | lead `alex-lurie` |\n| none | Brian Jue | **Former** Senior Associate; ex-Checkr finance, J.P. Morgan | seen 2022–2024 [archive](http://web.archive.org/web/20240524005746/https://www.137ventures.com/team/brian-jue) | None found | lead `brian-jue` |\n| none | Andrew Schreder | **Former** Senior Analyst; ex-Credit Suisse | seen 2025-04 [archive](http://web.archive.org/web/20250429165139/https://www.137ventures.com/team/andrew-schreder) | None found | lead `andrew-schreder` |\n| none | Shaunak Kulkarni | **Former** Analyst; diligence and deal structuring | seen 2022-08 [archive](http://web.archive.org/web/20220818004556/https://137ventures.com/teammember/shaunak-kulkarni/) | None found | lead `shaunak-kulkarni` |\n\nThe current team page lists operations staff I did not profile: Koby Aliphios, Sebastian Ferus, Josh Goodwine, Megan Larkin, Mason Windatt (fund accounting); Sarah Mitchell, Jessica Watanapun (legal/compliance); Dorothy Sumption (IR); Kelsey Knorr, Neva Lew, Michelle Tunley (EAs) ([src](https://137ventures.com/team)). Archived former names I could not read bios for include Ching Wu, Kathy H. Chan and Alda Leu-Dennis ([CDX](https://web.archive.org/cdx/search/cdx?url=137ventures.com/team/*)).\n\n**Pattern.** Every junior investment hire on the current page came from investment banking or equity research: FT Partners, Cantor, J.P. Morgan, Jefferies, Credit Suisse and Raymond James. None came from operating or research roles. That fits a shop that underwrites cap tables, liquidation preferences and loan collateral more than technical bets.\n\n## Thesis and style\n- **Core product:** \"secured non-recourse loans to equity holders in private companies\", plus installment-sale contracts, direct financings, secondaries and tender participation ([ADV Part 2A, Item 8](https://files.adviserinfo.sec.gov/IAPD/Content/Common/crd_iapd_Brochure.aspx?BRCHR_VRSN_ID=1037196)). The firm holds four California Financing Law lender licenses ([site](https://137ventures.com/)).\n- **What they underwrite:** revenue growth, **cap-table structure, liquidation preferences, secondary liquidity** and management ([ADV Part 2A](https://files.adviserinfo.sec.gov/IAPD/Content/Common/crd_iapd_Brochure.aspx?BRCHR_VRSN_ID=1037196)).\n- **Sourcing:** founders and executives, plus lawyers, tax accountants and \"wealth managers that service early employees\". In other words, the people who see equity-rich employees before anyone else does (same source).\n- **Ownership:** Fishner-Wolfson rejects ownership targets (\"ownership is an overvalued heuristic\", [Venture Unlocked 2021](https://ventureunlocked.substack.com/p/137-ventures-justin-fishner-wolfson)) and favours follow-on concentration. Hadrian is \"four, maybe five checks\" deep ([Sourcery](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/new-how-137-ventures-built-a-1-spacex)).\n- **AI stance:** 137 **passed on the foundation-model labs** because it could not tell which model would win. It backed Cognition as the model-agnostic layer instead (same source). This is the opposite of the owner's lab-seed thesis.\n- **Fees and minimums:** management fee 0–2.5% of commitments; minimum investment $10K to $20M ([ADV Part 2A](https://files.adviserinfo.sec.gov/IAPD/Content/Common/crd_iapd_Brochure.aspx?BRCHR_VRSN_ID=1037196)).\n- **What a company gets:** a long-hold shareholder that keeps adding, liquidity for employees, and since 2025–26 a public-policy function. Ashley Steinberg helps portfolio companies with federal, state and local policy ([bio](https://137ventures.com/team/ashley-steinberg)). Garrett's Hill & Valley Forum provides the Washington network ([bio](https://137ventures.com/team/christian-garrett)).\n- **How to reach them:** as a founder in defense or AI, through Christian Garrett and the Hill & Valley Forum. As an employee seeking liquidity, through the wealth-manager and lawyer channel. As an LP or emerging manager, through Lindsay Searer (fund strategies). These routes are inferred from bios and the brochure.\n\n## Notable bets\n| Company | First round | Date | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| SpaceX | Secondary/structured (about two dozen investments since) | 2011 (some sources say 2010) | [Sourcery](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/new-how-137-ventures-built-a-1-spacex) |\n| Hadrian | Series A ($90M), participant; then C; **co-lead** of the $1.37B round | 2022-03-31 | [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2022/03/31/machining-start-up-hadrian-raises-90-million-from-andreesen-lux.html), [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/06/defense-tech-hadrian-raises-1-37b-at-8b-valuation/) |\n| Together AI | Series A ($102.5M), participant | 2023-11-29 | [src](https://www.together.ai/blog/series-a) |\n| Armada | Series A ($55M+), participant; Jacobson founding advisor | 2023-12-11 | [src](https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/armada-raises-over-55-million-in-funding/) |\n| Applied Intuition | Series F + tender ($600M at $15B), new investor | 2025-06-17 | [src](https://www.appliedintuition.com/news/series-f) |\n| Crusoe | Series E ($1.375B), participant | 2025-10-24 | [src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-e-funding) |\n| Cognition | Existing investor by Series D; also in Series E | 2026-05-27 | [src](https://cognition.com/blog/series-d) |\n| Physical Intelligence | Round not found; named as a recent investment | by 2026-04-30 | [src](https://pulse2.com/137-ventures-700-million-raised-across-two-new-funds-as-aum-surpasses-15-billion/) |\n| Impulse Space | Series D ($500M), **co-lead** with BANNER VC | 2026-06-02 | [src](https://www.satellitetoday.com/finance/2026/06/02/impulse-space-raises-another-massive-funding-round-in-500m-series-d/) |\n| Anduril, Palantir, Brex, Ramp, Gusto, Figma, Varda, Uber, Airbnb, Flexport | various | n/a | [Sourcery](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/new-how-137-ventures-built-a-1-spacex) |\n\n## Fund history (Form D and ADV)\n| Vehicle | Form D | Size signal | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 137 Ventures, LP (Fund I) | first sale 2011-06-03 | $34.6M sold to 9 investors, $1M minimum; GAV now $51.7M, 37% related persons | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1522779/000152277911000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| 137 Ventures II + 137 Affiliates II | first sale 2013-09-12 | $137.95M sold to 107 investors; GAV now $596.8M | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1588377/000158837714000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| 137 Ventures III | filed 2015-06 | $200M target; GAV now $830.1M | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1646335/000164633515000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| 137 Ventures IV | filed 2018-02 | $200M target; GAV now $764.5M | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1728146/000172814618000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| 137 Ventures V / V-A | filed 2020-05 / 2021-07 | V GAV $478.0M; V-A $57.0M (2 owners) | [Form D V](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1802793/000180279320000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| 137 Ventures VI | filed 2022-06 | GAV $617.2M | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1930254/000193025422000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| 137 Ventures VII | filed 2025-05 (Hansen signs) | GAV $453.8M, 62 owners | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2065745/000147083125000014/primary_doc.xml) |\n| 137 Opportunity Fund, LP | (no Form D found under that name) | GAV $292.6M, 36 owners | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/162351/PDF/162351.pdf) |\n| 137 Direct Fund, LP | none found | GAV $163.5M, 100 owners | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/162351/PDF/162351.pdf) |\n\nThe April 2026 \">$700M across two new funds\" announcement does not name the funds. They are probably Fund VII and the Opportunity fund, since IEQ Capital set up feeders into exactly those two in 2025 (below). That pairing is unconfirmed.\n\n## Advisors, LPs, and connected capital\n| LP | Type | Fund / vintage | How known | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| IEQ Capital (via iCapital PE GP feeder) | wealth manager / RIA feeder | 137 Ventures Fund VII: $26.0M from 25 investors, first sale 2025-07-01 | filing (Form D) | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070917/000207091725000005/primary_doc.xml) |\n| IEQ Capital (via iCapital PE GP feeder) | wealth manager / RIA feeder | 137 Ventures Opportunity: $42.5M from 52 investors, first sale 2025-05-12 | filing (Form D) | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2067687/000206768725000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| 137 insiders (related persons) | GP / individuals | 7–40% of most vehicles; 100% of the Evergreen vehicles | filing (ADV Q14) | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/162351/PDF/162351.pdf) |\n| Unnamed fund-of-funds | fund-of-funds | 4–38% of flagship funds; 98–100% of 137 Holdings CI, CII and Co-Investment Fund | filing (ADV Q15) | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/162351/PDF/162351.pdf) |\n\nNo institutional LP is named anywhere I checked: 10 Form Ds, the 2026-04-15 ADV, the 2026-03-31 brochure, TechCrunch and the press release. Fishner-Wolfson told Sourcery that institutional LPs were skeptical early ([src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/new-how-137-ventures-built-a-1-spacex)). Capital Allocators interviewed allocator Nat Fraser (Agility) about 137 in 2021, which suggests Agility is an LP; that is **unconfirmed** ([src](https://www.capitalallocators.com/?p=40463)).\n\n## Content\n- Sourcery (Molly O'Shea): Fishner-Wolfson (2026-06), Garrett (\"Winning the AI Race & Reindustrialization\") and a 2025-03 liquidity episode ([src](https://www.sourcery.vc/p/new-how-137-ventures-built-a-1-spacex)).\n- Venture Unlocked, 2021-08 ([src](https://ventureunlocked.substack.com/p/137-ventures-justin-fishner-wolfson)).\n- TBPN: Fishner-Wolfson (2025-05) and Jacobson (2025-08), summaries only.\n- Bloomberg TV, 2026-04-30.\n- No firm blog.\n\n## Sources\n- Form ADV Part 1, 137 Ventures Management LLC (filed 2026-04-15): https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/162351/PDF/162351.pdf\n- ADV Part 2A brochure (2026-03-31): https://files.adviserinfo.sec.gov/IAPD/Content/Common/crd_iapd_Brochure.aspx?BRCHR_VRSN_ID=1037196\n- Team page and bios: https://137ventures.com/team\n- Form Ds: EDGAR CIKs 1522779, 1588377, 1646335, 1728146, 1802793, 1870661, 1930254, 2065745, 2070917, 2067687\n- Sourcery, \"How 137 Ventures Built a 1%+ SpaceX Position\" (2026-06): https://www.sourcery.vc/p/new-how-137-ventures-built-a-1-spacex\n- Pulse 2.0 on the $700M close (2026-04-30): https://pulse2.com/137-ventures-700-million-raised-across-two-new-funds-as-aum-surpasses-15-billion/\n- TechCrunch (2026-04-30): https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/30/spacex-backer-137-ventures-raises-700m-for-two-growth-stage-funds/\n- Archived team pages: web.archive.org (137ventures.com/team/*)\n", "firm:1517-fund": "\n# 1517 Fund\n\n## One-line take\n1517 is a small pre-seed fund run by the two people who built the Thiel Fellowship. Peter Thiel and about 30 wealthy individuals and families (Omidyar, Tallinn, the Coors and Hunt families, Berggruen) are LPs [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250830224702/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2016-12-01/thiel-s-1517-fund-is-stalking-the-next-zuckerberg). The money is outside LPs', not Thiel's balance sheet. Danielle Strachman and Michael Gibson are the only GP managers on every filing, so they approve. The edge is talent-tracking: $1K grants and a 6,000-person community find dropouts and Thiel Fellows years before they raise. That got them into Luminar, Lambda, Loom, Deepgram and, in AI chips, Positron (Series A, B and C) and Astrus (a pre-seed co-lead with Khosla). Checks are $50K-$1M, plus deal-by-deal SPVs for follow-ons. For a $20M seed writer they are an early-signal source and co-investor, not a competitor.\n\n## People pass 2026-11-10\n\n### Whose money, who decides\n- **Whose money:** pooled funds with outside LPs, mostly wealthy individuals and families. Bloomberg (2016-12): \"Thiel bit, as did about 30 other investors\", naming Pierre Omidyar, Jaan Tallinn, the Coors family, Texas's Hunt family and Nicolas Berggruen. They put $20M into the first fund [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250830224702/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2016-12-01/thiel-s-1517-fund-is-stalking-the-next-zuckerberg). Scott Alexander's review of Gibson's memoir says Thiel \"invested $4 million\" [src](https://www.astralcodexten.com/p/book-review-paper-belt-on-fire) (a review of the book, not the book itself). There is no parent balance sheet. Fund I charged 2% and 20% carry [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250830224702/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2016-12-01/thiel-s-1517-fund-is-stalking-the-next-zuckerberg).\n- **Who approves:** Danielle Strachman and Michael Gibson. They are the only related persons on every 1517 Form D (Funds I, II, IV, IV-A and SPVs A-V), as Managers or Managing Members of the GP. They alternate as signer [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2068201/000206820125000001/primary_doc.xml) [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2146260/000214626026000001/primary_doc.xml). Partners Slayback and Arnett do not appear on any filing. Whether they have carry or an investment-committee vote is **unconfirmed**.\n- **Fund vehicles (Form D, primary):**\n\n| Vehicle | GP entity | First sale | Sold / target | Investors | Filing |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1517 Fund I, L.P. | 1517 Fund I GP, LLC (Oakland) | 2015-08-14 | $6.4M / indefinite (at first filing; Bloomberg says $20M by 2016-12) | 12 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1651759/000165175915000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| 1517 Fund II, L.P. | 1517 Fund II GP, LLC | 2019-03-25 | $18.95M / $40M target (2020-06-16) | 49 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1772980/000177298020000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| 1517 Fund III | none found | none found | **No Form D on EDGAR** under any \"1517\" entity (company browse, entity search and full-text search for \"1517 Fund III\" all return nothing) | n/a | n/a |\n| 1517 Fund IV, L.P. | GP (Telluride) | 2025-05-12 | $25.5M (2025-05), then $41.8M (2026-05), then $43.13M (2026-09-03); indefinite | 25, then 43, then 85 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2068201/000206820126000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| 1517 Fund IV-A, L.P. | parallel vehicle | not yet (2026-05-22) | $0 | 0 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2068202/000206820226000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| SPV A / B / C (Fund I GP) | 1517 Fund I GP | 2016-12 / 2018-01 / 2018-07 | $3.05M / $3.01M / $3.41M (all filed late, 2020-11/12) | 11 / 9 / 7 | [A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1692127/000169212720000001/primary_doc.xml) [B](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1833319/000183331920000001/primary_doc.xml) [C](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1833336/000183333620000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| SPV D / E / F (Fund II GP) | 1517 Fund II GP | 2019-06 / 2019-09 / 2021-03 | $3.65M / $1.0M / $6.1M | 17 / 7 / 26 | [D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1833463/000183346320000001/primary_doc.xml) [E](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1833581/000183358120000001/primary_doc.xml) [F](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1873265/000187326521000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| SPV I | Telluride | 2021-02 | $3.1M | 21 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1908226/000156761922003271/primary_doc.xml) |\n| SPV S / U | Telluride | not yet (2026-03 / 2026-07) | $0 at filing | 0 | [S](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2098715/000209871526000001/primary_doc.xml) [U](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2138316/000213831626000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| SPV V | Telluride | 2026-08-18 | $9.96M | 13 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2146260/000214626026000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n\nThe SPV filings **never name the portfolio company**. SPV letters G-H, J-R and T have no Form D found. A \"1517 Fund I Sidecar LP\" (2015, $290K, 6 investors) is on EDGAR, but its GP is Tec-Rx Ventures LLC, signed by Steven Hogan at a Santa Clara address, with no Strachman or Gibson [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1658654/000165865415000001/primary_doc.xml). Treat it as an unrelated name collision (**unconfirmed**).\n\n### People\n| Name | Role | Joined | Personally named on (company, round, year) | Profile |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Danielle Strachman (danielle-strachman) | General Partner, Co-Founder; GP manager and Form D signer | 2015 (Fund I Form D signer) | **Loom, Pre-seed (1517 lead), c.2015-16**: Loom's CEO names \"Danielle and Michael\" as the pre-seed lead who wired money before docs were signed [src](https://www.1517fund.com/post/1517-medici-project). **Deepgram, pre-company grant (AWS credits plus $1K), 2016** [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250830224702/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2016-12-01/thiel-s-1517-fund-is-stalking-the-next-zuckerberg). Assessed Luminar's Austin Russell for the Thiel Fellowship (fellowship-era, not a 1517 check) [src](https://www.1517fund.com/post/luminar-goes-public). Every 1517 Form D | Thiel Fellowship founding team 2010-2015 (design and operations); founded Innovations Academy, a San Diego K-8 charter school [src](https://www.1517fund.com/). Pronoun \"she\" per firm site |\n| Michael Gibson (michael-gibson) | General Partner, Co-Founder; GP manager; signs Fund II, Fund IV amendments and SPV S/U/V | 2015 | **Loom, Pre-seed (lead)** and **Deepgram grant, 2016**, same sources as above. Every 1517 Form D | Ex-VP for grants at the Thiel Foundation and principal at Thiel Capital; helped run the Fellowship [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20160312094906/http://www.1517fund.com:80/team). Author of *Paper Belt on Fire* [src](https://www.astralcodexten.com/p/book-review-paper-belt-on-fire). Pronoun \"his\" per [src](https://www.1517fund.com/post/luminar-goes-public) |\n| Zak Slayback (zak-slayback) | Partner (Principal until early 2022) | 2017 (aggregator) [src](https://at.marketscreener.com/insider/ZAK-SLAYBACK-A3IK4S); Principal on archived pages 2020-09 to 2022-01-17; Partner by 2022-05-22 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20220522195835/https://www.1517fund.com/team/zakslayback) | **None found.** No round announcement fetched names him. Author of 1517's \"What Pre-Seed Means in 2021\" [src](https://www.1517fund.com/post/pre-seed-2021). The Symba CEO thanks \"Danielle, Michael, Zak\" generally, not on a round [src](https://www.1517fund.com/) | Left Penn in 2014; early team at Praxis. 2022 focus: biotech, deep tech, founders taking on incumbents, SaaS/CPG [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20221203090545/https://www.1517fund.com/). Email published on the site. Substack zakslayback.substack.com |\n| Nick Arnett (nick-arnett) | Partner (Principal until 2023) | Principal by 2020-06-20 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20200620185526/https://www.1517fund.com/team/nickarnett); Partner heading by 2023-05-20 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20230520074758/https://www.1517fund.com/) | **None found** | Ex-Thiel Fellowship community lead; founded WayPaver Foundation (lunar settlement); never attended university [src](https://www.1517fund.com/). The firm's own post calls him \"quiet but deadly\" and low-profile [src](https://www.1517fund.com/post/full-circle-playing-the-long-game) |\n| Haylee Johnson (haylee-johnson) | Operations Partner (Chief of Staff in 2022-24 snapshots) | by 2022-12 [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20221203090545/https://www.1517fund.com/) | None (operations and community, not investing) | [src](https://www.1517fund.com/) |\n\nNone of the AI-chip and compute rounds (Positron A, B and C; Astrus pre-seed, seed and seed 2; Hydra Host seed; Lambda A and C) names a 1517 individual in any source fetched. Positron's investors page lists \"1517 Fund\" as a logo only, and Positron's board has no 1517 seat [src](https://www.positron.ai/about).\n\n### How 1517 got into Positron (what is and isn't known)\n- **Known:** 1517 is listed among Positron investors [src](https://www.positron.ai/about). It is a named participant in the Series A ($51.6M, 2025-07-28) [src](https://convergedigest.com/positron-ai-raises-51-6m-series-a-to-scale-custom-inference-silicon/?amp=1), an existing investor in the Series B (2026-02-04), and in the Series C (2026-09-10) (per `data/rounds.csv`).\n- **Network ties, both sourced:** co-founder and chairman Thomas Sohmers is \"a Thiel Fellow\". CEO Mitesh Agrawal was previously COO at Lambda [src](https://convergedigest.com/positron-ai-raises-51-6m-series-a-to-scale-custom-inference-silicon/?amp=1). 1517 was an existing Lambda investor before Lambda's 2021 Series A, and Lambda's founder says \"1517 believed in me first\" [src](https://lambda.ai/blog/lambda-raises-24-5-to-build-a-gpu-cloud-and-deep-learning-infrastructure) [src](https://www.1517fund.com/).\n- **Unconfirmed:** whether 1517 was in Positron's 2025-02 seed ($23.5M); which tie brought them in; which partner did it; whether SPV V ($9.96M, first sale 2026-08-18) was a Positron Series C vehicle. The filing names no company, so do not infer it.\n\n## Thesis and style\n- Backs \"dropouts, renegade students, and deep tech scientists\" from idea stage through seed. First checks run from $50K at idea/R&D stage to $1M for deep-tech seeds, averaging a $400K pre-seed. It will lead pre-seeds up to $1M [src](https://www.1517fund.com/). Breaks the no-degree rule for \"deep tech plays (we make a few deep tech investments every year)\" [src](https://www.1517fund.com/post/thesis).\n- Sourcing is talent-tracking: Medici $1K-minimum grants (200+ a year), a 6,000+ community, and hackathon and campus tours. In 2016 Strachman called this \"a search cost\" [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250830224702/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2016-12-01/thiel-s-1517-fund-is-stalking-the-next-zuckerberg). \"We tell our LPs that we're pros in 'talent identification'\" [src](https://www.1517fund.com/post/thesis).\n- Says it will stay small: no associates, not \"doubling the size of our fund every time\", \"preseed forever!\" [src](https://www.1517fund.com/post/moving-ahead). Follow-ons go through SPVs: \"15+ SPVs for Outstanding Investments\" [src](https://www.1517fund.com/).\n- The site said \"Our funds are currently closed\" in 2026-11, while Fund IV was still adding investors through 2026-09 [src](https://www.1517fund.com/).\n- **How a founder or co-investor reaches them:** the site's form (they reply within two weeks if in-thesis), info@1517fund.com, or Slayback directly (address on the site; say where you found it) [src](https://www.1517fund.com/).\n\n## Notable bets\n| Company | First round | Date | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Luminar | Undisclosed (1517's first IPO, 2020 SPAC at $3.4B; ACX review says about $200M gain) | entry unconfirmed | [src](https://www.1517fund.com/post/luminar-goes-public) |\n| Loom | Pre-seed (1517 led) | c.2015-16 (unconfirmed) | [src](https://www.1517fund.com/post/1517-medici-project) |\n| Deepgram | Grant to founder (2016), then Seed $1.8M (aggregator) | 2016 | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250830224702/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2016-12-01/thiel-s-1517-fund-is-stalking-the-next-zuckerberg) [src](https://multiples.vc/private-comps/deepgram) |\n| Lambda | Before Series A (existing investor at the 2021-07 Series A); also Series C 2024 | before 2021 | [src](https://lambda.ai/blog/lambda-raises-24-5-to-build-a-gpu-cloud-and-deep-learning-infrastructure) |\n| Atom Computing (neutral-atom quantum) | Entry round unconfirmed (on 1517's select portfolio) | unconfirmed | [src](https://www.1517fund.com/) |\n| Hydra Host | Seed $10M (Founders Fund led) | 2022-04-06 | [src](https://refreshmiami.com/news/hydra-host-closes-10m-seed-round-led-by-founders-fund-to-scale-resilient-secure-cross-cloud-infrastructure/) |\n| Astrus (AI analog chip layout) | Pre-seed co-lead with Khosla, $100K each | 2023-02 | [src](https://betakit.com/with-new-funding-astrus-has-secured-3-6-million-cad-to-date-for-its-ai-microchip-design-offering/) |\n| Positron (AI inference chips) | Series A participant (earlier entry unconfirmed); also B and C | 2025-07-28 | [src](https://convergedigest.com/positron-ai-raises-51-6m-series-a-to-scale-custom-inference-silicon/?amp=1) |\n| HeyPCB, Xona Space Systems, Rainmaker, nTopology, Fountain, Fossa, Cents, Symba, Stryx, JobPixel, Upkeep | logos and announcement posts; rounds mostly not stated | 2020-2026 | [src](https://www.1517fund.com/) [src](https://www.1517fund.com/post) |\n\n## Advisors, LPs, and connected capital\n| LP | Type | Fund / vintage | How known | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Peter Thiel | individual | Fund I (2015-16); about $4M per the ACX review of Gibson's book | press: Bloomberg 2016; summary: ACX review | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250830224702/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2016-12-01/thiel-s-1517-fund-is-stalking-the-next-zuckerberg) [src](https://www.astralcodexten.com/p/book-review-paper-belt-on-fire) |\n| Pierre Omidyar | individual | Fund I | press | [src](https://web.archive.org/web/20250830224702/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2016-12-01/thiel-s-1517-fund-is-stalking-the-next-zuckerberg) |\n| Jaan Tallinn | individual | Fund I | press | same |\n| Coors family | family office | Fund I | press | same |\n| Hunt family (Texas) | family office | Fund I | press | same |\n| Nicolas Berggruen | individual | Fund I | press (he calls 1517 \"a learning factory\") | same |\n\nBloomberg says about 30 investors put in $20M by 2016-12. The Fund I Form D (2015-08) shows $6.4M from 12 investors and was never amended, so both may be true at different dates. Fund II had 49 investors, Fund IV 85 (Form D); no LP names are disclosed for them. Checked: all 1517 Form Ds, the firm site and blog, and the archived 2016 team page. LP rows were not written to a `-lp.csv` file because this task named no such file; the table above is the record.\n\n## Content\n- Michael Gibson, *Paper Belt on Fire* (memoir and manifesto on the Thiel Fellowship and founding 1517). Review: [src](https://www.astralcodexten.com/p/book-review-paper-belt-on-fire)\n- Firm blog posts on thesis, pre-seed definitions, valuation caps, onboarding and Medici [src](https://www.1517fund.com/post); podcasts page [src](https://www.1517fund.com/podcasts); newsletter \"Letters from the Edge\"; Slayback's Substack.\n- Podcast appearances surfaced in search but not listened to here (Strachman: Intelligent Investing, Epsilon Theory's Intentional Investor; Gibson: Going Deep with Aaron, The DeSci Podcast). The ACX review is secondary.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.1517fund.com/ (team, check sizes, metrics, portfolio, founder quotes; fetched 2026-11-10)\n- https://web.archive.org/web/20250830224702/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2016-12-01/thiel-s-1517-fund-is-stalking-the-next-zuckerberg\n- SEC Form Ds: CIKs 1651759, 1772980, 2068201, 2068202, 1692127, 1833319, 1833336, 1833463, 1833581, 1873265, 1908226, 2098715, 2138316, 2146260 (and 1658654, unrelated GP)\n- Archived team pages: https://web.archive.org/web/20160312094906/http://www.1517fund.com:80/team ; .../20200921055628/.../team/zakslayback ; .../20220117220640/... ; .../20220522195835/... ; .../20200620185526/.../team/nickarnett ; .../20221203090545/https://www.1517fund.com/ ; .../20230520074758/https://www.1517fund.com/\n- https://www.positron.ai/about ; https://convergedigest.com/positron-ai-raises-51-6m-series-a-to-scale-custom-inference-silicon/?amp=1 ; https://lambda.ai/blog/lambda-raises-24-5-to-build-a-gpu-cloud-and-deep-learning-infrastructure ; https://betakit.com/with-new-funding-astrus-has-secured-3-6-million-cad-to-date-for-its-ai-microchip-design-offering/ ; https://www.eenewseurope.com/en/ai-eda-startup-astrus-raises-seed-funding ; https://refreshmiami.com/news/hydra-host-closes-10m-seed-round-led-by-founders-fund-to-scale-resilient-secure-cross-cloud-infrastructure/\n- https://www.astralcodexten.com/p/book-review-paper-belt-on-fire\n", "firm:1789-capital": "\n# 1789 Capital\n\n## One-line take\nA politically branded crossover vehicle controlled by **Omeed Malik and Christopher Buskirk**. It buys allocations in late-stage AI rounds (Cerebras G, Reflection B, Groq E, Skild C, Crusoe E/F, Ayar Labs E) through code-named per-deal SPVs, funded largely by **a handful of non-US holders**. **No source names any 1789 person on any of its AI deals.** The deal-facing bench is ex-Citi and Farvahar bankers, not venture investors. For my lens it is a co-investor and price-taker, not a seed partner ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/335007/PDF/335007.pdf), [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2091024/000121390026005884/ea0261111-02.htm)).\n\n## People pass 2026-11-10\n**Whose money.** It is outside money in pooled LPs, not a parent balance sheet. Related persons own only 0% to 63% of each vehicle, and 8% of Fund I ([ADV 7.B.(1) Q14](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/335007/PDF/335007.pdf)). The money is concentrated:\n- **Fund I:** $890M gross assets from 98 beneficial owners, 46% non-US.\n- **1789 Parallel Fund I:** $345M from **4 owners, 93% non-US**.\n- **1789 Pacific Alliance US Growth Equity Fund:** $382M from **3 owners, 55% non-US**.\n- **1789 Pacific Alliance Special Situations Fund:** $200M from 2 owners.\n- **Odyssey II:** $46.6M from 4 owners, 99% non-US.\n\nAll figures are from the ADV filed 2026-08-11. Item 5.F(3) reports $0 of AUM from non-US *clients*. That is consistent with the above, because the clients are the Delaware funds while the Q16 percentages describe their *beneficial owners*. The counterparty behind \"Pacific Alliance\" is not identified anywhere I could read (needs-id).\n\n**Who approves.** ADV Schedule A lists Malik as President and Secretary (since 01/2023) and Buskirk as Vice President (since 01/2023) as the only individual control persons. Each holds through an LLC: Knights Court LLC (Malik) and Telperion LLC (Buskirk). Both LLCs sit on the board of managers of 1789 Capital Holdings LLC, which owns the adviser ([ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/335007/PDF/335007.pdf)). Every one of the 44 Form Ds names Malik and Buskirk (or the Fund I GP they manage), and Malik signs nearly all of them ([EDGAR](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%221789%20Capital%22&forms=D)). **Donald Trump Jr. is not an owner or control person.** The ADV lists him only as an advisory affiliate with a civil-judicial disclosure (the New York case). **Rebekah Mercer** was a managing member of the GP on the 2023 and June 2024 Form Ds ([2023](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1965234/000196523423000001/primary_doc.xml), [2024](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2025959/000202595924000001/primary_doc.xml)). She is gone from every filing from January 2025 on.\n\n**Who does the AI deals.** No source names one. Here is what I checked: the Cerebras Series G release, the Skild Series C post, the Crusoe Series E release, the Eclipse VulcanForms post, Fortune on Last Country, the Colombier III S-1/A, 44 Form Ds, the ADV, all 14 team bios, and EDGAR full-text searches for each partner's name. None ties a 1789 person to an AI round. The most plausible owners from title and bio, all **unconfirmed**:\n- Paul Abrahimzadeh, origination. He was Citi Co-Head of ECM North America until April 2025 and covered AI and semis.\n- Joe Voboril, the only \"Head of Research\".\n- Malik and Buskirk, as approvers.\n\n## People\n\"Deals personally named on\" lists only deals where a source names the person. \"None found\" means I checked the bio, the S-1/A and EDGAR.\n\n| Photo | Name | Role | Joined | Deals personally named on | Profile |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ![](https://1789capital.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/omeed-malik-headshot.webp) | Omeed Malik | Co-Founder & President; control person; also Farvahar founder/CEO and Colombier III Chair/CEO; Fannie Mae director | 2023-01 [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/335007/PDF/335007.pdf) | PublicSquare (Colombier I SPAC merger, 2023); GrabAGun (Colombier II SPAC merger, 2025) [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2091024/000121390026005884/ea0261111-02.htm). Fortune names him as founder in the Last Country seed coverage (2023), not as deal lead [src](https://fortune.com/2023/10/17/tucker-carlson-media-company-funding-1789-15-million-seed-capital/). **No AI deal** | lead `omeed-malik` |\n| ![](https://1789capital.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Christopher-Buskirk-Headshot_1789_BW.webp) | Christopher (Chris) Buskirk | Co-Founder & CIO; control person; publisher of American Greatness; also MD at Atlas Capital Partners | 2023-01 [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2091024/000121390026005884/ea0261111-02.htm) | Director of Colombier II during the GrabAGun merger (2025) [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2091024/000121390026005884/ea0261111-02.htm). **No AI deal** | lead `christopher-buskirk` |\n| ![](https://1789capital.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/don-trump-jr-headshot.webp) | Donald Trump Jr. | Senior Partner (Partner from 2024-11); advisory affiliate, not an owner; strategic advisor to Polymarket and Kalshi | 2024-11 [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2091024/000121390026005884/ea0261111-02.htm) | None found. Advises Polymarket, where 1789 invested 2025-08 (Wikipedia, secondary) [src](https://1789capital.io/team/don-trump-jr/) | lead `donald-trump-jr` |\n| ![](https://1789capital.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/paul-abrahimzadeh-headshot.webp) | Paul Abrahimzadeh | Senior Partner: origination, structuring, capital raising; President of Colombier III; runs Farvahar banking | 2025-04 [src](https://1789capital.io/team/paul-abrahimzadeh/) | None found. Ran Citi ECM; Citi was joint placement agent on the Cerebras G (link unconfirmed) | lead `paul-abrahimzadeh` |\n| ![](https://1789capital.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/jordan-cohen-headshot.webp) | Jordan Cohen | Senior Partner & COO; signs SPV Form Ds | 2023-01 [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2091024/000121390026005884/ea0261111-02.htm) | Officer of Colombier II (GrabAGun) and adviser to Colombier I (PublicSquare). No AI deal | lead `jordan-cohen` |\n| ![](https://1789capital.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/joe-voboril-headshot.webp) | Joe Voboril | Partner & **Head of Research**; Farvahar co-founder; CFO of Colombier III | 2023-01 [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2091024/000121390026005884/ea0261111-02.htm) | Officer of Colombier I and II. No AI deal named | lead `joe-voboril` |\n| ![](https://1789capital.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/andy-nasser-headshot.webp) | Andy (Andrew) Nasser | Partner; CIO of Colombier III; ex-M. Klein/Churchill SPAC sourcing | since inception [src](https://1789capital.io/team/andy-nasser/) | None found | lead `andy-nasser` |\n| ![](https://1789capital.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/mark-moskowitz-headshot.webp) | Mark Moskowitz | Partner; ex-Citi | 2025-06 [src](https://1789capital.io/team/mark-moskowitz/) | None found | lead `mark-moskowitz` |\n| ![](https://1789capital.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/emanuel-zareh-headshot.webp) | Emanuel Zareh | Partner; ex-Commerce presidential appointee (AI export EO, Middle East diplomacy); ex-Emles family office | 2025-11 [src](https://1789capital.io/team/emanuel-zareh/) | None found | lead `emanuel-zareh` |\n| ![](https://1789capital.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/dane-fraser-headshot.webp) | Dane Fraser | Partner & Co-Head of IR; ex-Balyasny MD | 2026-05 [src](https://1789capital.io/team/dane-fraser/) | None (LP-facing) | lead `dane-fraser` |\n| ![](https://1789capital.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/leo-valentine-headshot.webp) | Leo Valentine | Finance & Co-Head of IR | not found [src](https://1789capital.io/team/) | None | lead `leo-valentine` (skip) |\n| ![](https://1789capital.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chris-ward-willis-headshot.webp) | Christopher Ward-Willis | VP: investments, IR, ops; ex-Citi healthcare IB | 2025-09 [src](https://1789capital.io/team/christopher-ward-willis/) | None found | lead `christopher-ward-willis` |\n| ![](https://1789capital.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/steve-jeffries-headshot.webp) | Steve Jeffries | VP: capital raising, legal, finance; ex-Kirkland fund formation | 2025-11 [src](https://1789capital.io/team/steve-jeffries/) | None | lead `steve-jeffries` (skip) |\n| ![](https://1789capital.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/zach-tucker-headshot.webp) | Zach Tucker | VP: investments, IR, ops; ex-Mizuho/Greenhill TMT | 2026-05 [src](https://1789capital.io/team/zach-tucker/) | None found | lead `zach-tucker` |\n| none | Rebekah Mercer | Co-founder; GP managing member (2023 to June 2024 Form Ds) | 2023-01 | None found | lead `rebekah-mercer` |\n| none | Norman Hans | Chief Compliance Officer | 2025-04 [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/335007/PDF/335007.pdf) | None | lead `norman-hans` (skip) |\n\nNon-investing staff on the team page: Sarah Armstrong (Chief of Staff), Kellyann Perdie and Kate Balser (Executive Assistants) [src](https://1789capital.io/team/). Blake Masters was an early adviser per Wikipedia and is a Colombier III director nominee [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2091024/000121390026005884/ea0261111-02.htm).\n\n**Pattern worth knowing:** Malik, Voboril, Abrahimzadeh, Zareh and Fraser are all Colgate graduates (team bios). Every partner except Buskirk and Trump Jr. is also a Farvahar Partners partner. The 1789 team, the Farvahar broker-dealer and the Colombier SPAC team are effectively one group of people.\n\n## Thesis and style\n- Brand: \"patriotic capitalism\", anti-ESG and anti-DEI screens, per Malik in Wikipedia's summary [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1789_Capital).\n- How the AI exposure is built: per-deal LPs with code names, all under the Fund I GP (Form D list below). Inception II raised $100.5M from 81 investors and Lakehouse III raised $89.7M from 46. Most others raised $2M to $60M from 1 to 58 investors. **Which SPV holds which company is not disclosed.** With 44 vehicles and about ten AI rounds in 14 months, first-sale dates that fall near round announcements prove nothing. Some vehicles are feeders: Treehouse is 57% fund-of-funds and Treehouse II 100%. Any SPV-to-company mapping is unconfirmed.\n- Role in rounds: participant at Series E and later, except VulcanForms Series D, which it co-led with Eclipse ([src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/Putting-Pedal-to-Metal)), and the Last Country seed (2023), its first deal ([src](https://fortune.com/2023/10/17/tucker-carlson-media-company-funding-1789-15-million-seed-capital/)).\n- What a company gets: political and retail-investor credibility. The Colombier III S-1/A pitches the team's ability to \"credentialize\" a company within its ecosystem ([S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2091024/000121390026005884/ea0261111-02.htm)). FT (May 2026, via Wikipedia) reports portfolio companies won more than $735M in federal contracts and loans, including a $620M Pentagon loan to Vulcan Elements ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1789_Capital)).\n- How to reach them: as a founder, through Abrahimzadeh (origination) or Farvahar. As an LP, through Dane Fraser and Leo Valentine (IR). Both routes are inferred from bios.\n\n**Musk-orbit overlap.** Wikipedia (citing a 2025-07-15 Bloomberg headline) says 1789 is in \"almost all\" Musk companies: xAI, SpaceX, X and Neuralink ([src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1789_Capital)). **No primary source gives a 1789 round or amount in xAI or SpaceX.** The repo has no 1789 xAI row, and I wrote none. Only X has a dated round attached (Wikipedia cites the 2025-03 X raise at $32B). In repo data, **Valor** shares four rounds with 1789: Cerebras G, Crusoe E (Valor co-lead), Hadrian C and D, and Base Power C. **Vy Capital shares none.** Vy's Cerebras position dates from 2017 ([Vy page](vy-capital.md)), and the Vy and 1789 overlap in xAI and SpaceX rests only on the secondary claim above.\n\n## Notable bets\n| Company | First round | Date | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Last Country (Tucker Carlson Network) | Seed ($15M), first deal | 2023-10-17 | [src](https://fortune.com/2023/10/17/tucker-carlson-media-company-funding-1789-15-million-seed-capital/) |\n| Hadrian | Series C ($260M), participant | 2025-07-17 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/17/hadrian-raises-260m-to-build-out-automated-factories-for-space-and-defense-parts/) |\n| Polymarket | Undisclosed (strategic) | 2025-08-26 | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polymarket) (secondary) |\n| Groq | Series E ($750M) | 2025-09-17 | [src](https://www.storagenewsletter.com/2025/09/25/groq-raises-750-million-as-inference-demand-surges/) |\n| Cerebras | Series G ($1.1B), new investor | 2025-09-30 | [src](https://www.cerebras.ai/press-release/series-g) |\n| Reflection AI | Series B ($2B at $8B) | 2025-10-09 | [src](https://reflection.ai/blog/building-frontier-open-intelligencence) |\n| Base Power | Series C ($1B) | 2025-10-08 | [src](https://houston.innovationmap.com/base-power-company-2674182537.html) |\n| Crusoe | Series E; re-up in Series F | 2025-10-24 | [src](https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-series-e-funding) |\n| Skild AI | Series C (~$1.4B) | 2026-01-14 | [src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/series-c) |\n| VulcanForms | Series D ($220M), **co-lead** | 2026-01-30 | [src](https://eclipse.capital/blog/Putting-Pedal-to-Metal) |\n| Ayar Labs | Series E ($500M) | 2026-03-03 | [src](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/ayar-labs-closes-500m-series-e-accelerates-volume-production-of-co-packaged-optics/) |\n| xAI, SpaceX, X, Neuralink, Anduril, Perplexity, PsiQuantum | unconfirmed rounds | n/a | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1789_Capital) (secondary only) |\n\n## Advisors, LPs, and connected capital\n| LP | Type | Fund / vintage | How known | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Unnamed: 4 beneficial owners, 93% non-US | unknown | 1789 Parallel Fund I ($345M GAV) | filing (ADV 2026-08-11) | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/335007/PDF/335007.pdf) |\n| \"Pacific Alliance\" partner (unidentified): 3 owners, 55% non-US | unknown | 1789 Pacific Alliance US Growth Equity Fund ($382M); Special Situations Fund ($200M, 2 owners) | filing | [ADV](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/335007/PDF/335007.pdf) |\n| Unnamed: 98 owners, 46% non-US, 1% fund-of-funds | mixed | 1789 Capital Fund I ($890M GAV; Form D sold $270.5M to 71 investors by 2025-10) | filing | [Form D/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1965234/000196523425000002/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Rebekah Mercer | individual (co-founder; LP status unconfirmed) | Fund I GP managing member 2023 to 2024 | filing | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1965234/000196523423000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Marc Andreessen, Charlie Kirk, Clay Travis | individuals, \"early backers\" (LP status unconfirmed) | n/a | press via Wikipedia | [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1789_Capital) |\n\nNo LP is named in any filing. I checked 44 Form Ds (EDGAR full-text search, 2023-02 to 2026-11-06), the 2026-08-11 ADV and the Colombier III S-1/A. The fund-of-funds share is 100% for Treehouse II and 57% for Treehouse, so some SPVs are fed by other pooled vehicles.\n\n**Form D ledger (sold amounts):** Fund I $270.5M/71 investors; Inception II $100.5M/81 (first sale 2026-04-27); Lakehouse III $89.7M/46 (2026-03-27); Inception III $64.5M/106 (2026-10-30); Blaze $59.7M/23 (2026-04-17); Odyssey II $22.8M/3 (2025-02-07); Artemis $20.6M/5 (2026-10-01); Patriot $19.7M/14 (2025-02-24); Odyssey $17.5M/30 (2024-12-12); RO $13.7M/55 (2024-10-10); Treehouse $11.8M/3 (2025-09-30); Dawn $10.2M/6 (2026-08-13); BH $9.7M/28 (2024-06-07); Nirvana II $5.4M/1; Matrix $2.0M/2; Co-Invest I $2.2M/7. Fund II-A and II-B (filed 2026-09-08) and Cobra and Cobra II (2026-11-03) have no sales yet [EDGAR](https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%221789%20Capital%22&forms=D).\n\n## Content\nNo firm blog; the portfolio page lists no companies ([src](https://1789capital.io/portfolio/)). Public statements come through Malik and Trump Jr. interviews in press.\n\n## Sources\n- Form ADV, 1789 Capital Management (amendment 2026-08-11): https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/335007/PDF/335007.pdf\n- Colombier Acquisition Corp. III S-1/A (2026-01-21): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2091024/000121390026005884/ea0261111-02.htm\n- 1789 Form Ds: https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%221789%20Capital%22&forms=D\n- Team page and bios: https://1789capital.io/team/\n- Wikipedia (secondary; summarizes Reuters, Bloomberg, FT, NYT): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1789_Capital\n- Blocked or unreadable this run: Reuters, Bloomberg, Business Insider, FT, Financial Express, Axios, web.archive.org. Web search budget was exhausted.\n\n## Gulf-pipe pass 2026-11-11\n**\"93% non-US\" is not by itself evidence of Gulf money.** The 2026-08-11 ADV shows that 1789 Parallel Fund I ($345M) has 4 beneficial owners, 93% non-US. No filing or press source names any of them, or any specific Gulf LP in any 1789 vehicle. Checked: the ADV; 44 Form Ds; the Raskin letter of 2026-08-26 (it names no foreign investor) [letter](https://democrats-judiciary.house.gov/sites/evo-subsites/democrats-judiciary.house.gov/files/evo-media-document/2026-08-26-raskin-to-malik-buskirk-trump-jr-1789-capital-re-insider-access.pdf); ION/Mergermarket 2025-07-17 (no LPs) [src](https://ionanalytics.com/insights/mergermarket/1789-capital-targets-breakout-returns-from-late-stage-us-innovation-partner/); and Bloomberg-derived coverage of the $3B Fund II raise ($2B \"from existing investors\", unnamed) [src](https://www.rootdata.com/news/768145).\n- **The GP has said it raised Gulf money, without numbers.** The FT (Spanish syndication, 2025-09-04) reports that 1789 \"había recaudado millones de dólares en la región\" (\"had raised millions of dollars in the region\"). This is per Omeed Malik, who declined to give figures. The same piece puts Trump Jr. at the Qatar Economic Forum in May 2025, meeting the Qatari PM and the Emir [DF/FT](https://www.df.cl/internacional/ft/como-los-trump-se-ganaron-el-golfo-la-lucrativa-jugada-de-los-negocios-de). So a Gulf LP base exists by the GP's own account, but no investor is named and the share of the 93%-non-US Parallel Fund is unknown. \"Millions\" fits a minor Gulf share, not a sovereign anchor (inference). Aggregators (Tracxn) list QIA as a frequent co-investor; that is deal overlap, not an LP link. Partner Emanuel Zareh's bio cites Middle East diplomacy work.\n- \"Pacific Alliance\": a third vehicle, \"1789-Pacific Alliance US Growth Equity Fund II, LP\", filed in Oct 2026 (aggregator) [src](https://altstreet.investments/md/edgar/2159900). The name points to an Asia-Pacific partner, not a Gulf one. The counterparty is still unidentified (needs-id).\n- Status: Gulf money is in 1789 by Malik's own account, but it is unsized and unnamed. The 93% non-US concentration is real; its nationality is unknown.\n"}, "__n": 2};
